if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-583"]="(of Clement’s Inn, Teacher of Drawing)\n\nThis is the story of what a Woman’s patience can endure, and what a Man’s resolution can achieve.\n\nIf the machinery of the Law could be depended on to fathom every case of suspicion, and to conduct every process of inquiry, with moderate assistance only from the lubricating influences of oil of gold, the events which fill these pages might have claimed their share of the public attention in a Court of Justice.\n\nBut the Law is still, in certain inevitable cases, the pre-engaged servant of the long purse; and the story is left to be told, for the first time, in this place. As the Judge might once have heard it, so the Reader shall hear it now. No circumstance of importance, from the beginning to the end of the disclosure, shall be related on hearsay evidence. When the writer of these introductory lines (Walter Hartright by name) happens to be more closely connected than others with the incidents to be recorded, he will describe them in his own person. When his experience fails, he will retire from the position of narrator; and his task will be continued, from the point at which he has left it off, by other persons who can speak to the circumstances under notice from their own knowledge, just as clearly and positively as he has spoken before them.\n\nThus, the story here presented will be told by more than one pen, as the story of an offence against the laws is told in Court by more than one witness—with the same object, in both cases, to present the truth always in its most direct and most intelligible aspect; and to trace the course of one complete series of events, by making the persons who have been most closely connected with them, at each successive stage, relate their own experience, word for word.\n\nLet Walter Hartright, teacher of drawing, aged twenty-eight years, be heard first.\n\n# II\n\nIt was the last day of July. The long hot summer was drawing to a close; and we, the weary pilgrims of the London pavement, were beginning to think of the cloud-shadows on the corn-fields, and the autumn breezes on the sea-shore.\n\nFor my own poor part, the fading summer left me out of health, out of spirits, and, if the truth must be told, out of money as well. During the past year I had not managed my professional resources as carefully as usual; and my extravagance now limited me to the prospect of spending the autumn economically between my mother’s cottage at Hampstead and my own chambers in town.\n\nThe evening, I remember, was still and cloudy; the London air was at its heaviest; the distant hum of the street-traffic was at its faintest; the small pulse of the life within me, and the great heart of the city around me, seemed to be sinking in unison, languidly and more languidly, with the sinking sun. I roused myself from the book which I was dreaming over rather than reading, and left my chambers to meet the cool night air in the suburbs. It was one of the two evenings in every week which I was accustomed to spend with my mother and my sister. So I turned my steps northward in the direction of Hampstead.\n\nEvents which I have yet to relate make it necessary to mention in this place that my father had been dead some years at the period of which I am now writing; and that my sister Sarah and I were the sole survivors of a family of five children. My father was a drawing-master before me. His exertions had made him highly successful in his profession; and his affectionate anxiety to provide for the future of those who were dependent on his labours had impelled him, from the time of his marriage, to devote to the insuring of his life a much larger portion of his income than most men consider it necessary to set aside for that purpose. Thanks to his admirable prudence and self-denial my mother and sister were left, after his death, as independent of the world as they had been during his lifetime. I succeeded to his connection, and had every reason to feel grateful for the prospect that awaited me at my starting in life.\n\nThe quiet twilight was still trembling on the topmost ridges of the heath; and the view of London below me had sunk into a black gulf in the shadow of the cloudy night, when I stood before the gate of my mother’s cottage. I had hardly rung the bell before the house door was opened violently; my worthy Italian friend, Professor Pesca, appeared in the servant’s place; and darted out joyously to receive me, with a shrill foreign parody on an English cheer.\n\nOn his own account, and, I must be allowed to add, on mine also, the Professor merits the honour of a formal introduction. Accident has made him the starting-point of the strange family story which it is the purpose of these pages to unfold.\n\nI had first become acquainted with my Italian friend by meeting him at certain great houses where he taught his own language and I taught drawing. All I then knew of the history of his life was, that he had once held a situation in the University of Padua; that he had left Italy for political reasons (the nature of which he uniformly declined to mention to any one); and that he had been for many years respectably established in London as a teacher of languages.\n\nWithout being actually a dwarf—for he was perfectly well proportioned from head to foot—Pesca was, I think, the smallest human being I ever saw out of a show-room. Remarkable anywhere, by his personal appearance, he was still further distinguished among the rank and file of mankind by the harmless eccentricity of his character. The ruling idea of his life appeared to be, that he was bound to show his gratitude to the country which had afforded him an asylum and a means of subsistence by doing his utmost to turn himself into an Englishman. Not content with paying the nation in general the compliment of invariably carrying an umbrella, and invariably wearing gaiters and a white hat, the Professor further aspired to become an Englishman in his habits and amusements, as well as in his personal appearance. Finding us distinguished, as a nation, by our love of athletic exercises, the little man, in the innocence of his heart, devoted himself impromptu to all our English sports and pastimes whenever he had the opportunity of joining them; firmly persuaded that he could adopt our national amusements of the field by an effort of will precisely as he had adopted our national gaiters and our national white hat.\n\nI had seen him risk his limbs blindly at a fox-hunt and in a cricket-field; and soon afterwards I saw him risk his life, just as blindly, in the sea at Brighton.\n\nWe had met there accidentally, and were bathing together. If we had been engaged in any exercise peculiar to my own nation I should, of course, have looked after Pesca carefully; but as foreigners are generally quite as well able to take care of themselves in the water as Englishmen, it never occurred to me that the art of swimming might merely add one more to the list of manly exercises which the Professor believed that he could learn impromptu. Soon after we had both struck out from shore, I stopped, finding my friend did not gain on me, and turned round to look for him. To my horror and amazement, I saw nothing between me and the beach but two little white arms which struggled for an instant above the surface of the water, and then disappeared from view. When I dived for him, the poor little man was lying quietly coiled up at the bottom, in a hollow of shingle, looking by many degrees smaller than I had ever seen him look before. During the few minutes that elapsed while I was taking him in, the air revived him, and he ascended the steps of the machine with my assistance. With the partial recovery of his animation came the return of his wonderful delusion on the subject of swimming. As soon as his chattering teeth would let him speak, he smiled vacantly, and said he thought it must have been the Cramp.\n\nWhen he had thoroughly recovered himself, and had joined me on the beach, his warm Southern nature broke through all artificial English restraints in a moment. He overwhelmed me with the wildest expressions of affection—exclaimed passionately, in his exaggerated Italian way, that he would hold his life henceforth at my disposal—and declared that he should never be happy again until he had found an opportunity of proving his gratitude by rendering me some service which I might remember, on my side, to the end of my days.\n\nI did my best to stop the torrent of his tears and protestations by persisting in treating the whole adventure as a good subject for a joke; and succeeded at last, as I imagined, in lessening Pesca’s overwhelming sense of obligation to me. Little did I think then—little did I think afterwards when our pleasant holiday had drawn to an end—that the opportunity of serving me for which my grateful companion so ardently longed was soon to come; that he was eagerly to seize it on the instant; and that by so doing he was to turn the whole current of my existence into a new channel, and to alter me to myself almost past recognition.\n\nYet so it was. If I had not dived for Professor Pesca when he lay under water on his shingle bed, I should in all human probability never have been connected with the story which these pages will relate—I should never, perhaps, have heard even the name of the woman who has lived in all my thoughts, who has possessed herself of all my energies, who has become the one guiding influence that now directs the purpose of my life.\n\n# III\n\nPesca’s face and manner, on the evening when we confronted each other at my mother’s gate, were more than sufficient to inform me that something extraordinary had happened. It was quite useless, however, to ask him for an immediate explanation. I could only conjecture, while he was dragging me in by both hands, that (knowing my habits) he had come to the cottage to make sure of meeting me that night, and that he had some news to tell of an unusually agreeable kind.\n\nWe both bounced into the parlour in a highly abrupt and undignified manner. My mother sat by the open window laughing and fanning herself. Pesca was one of her especial favourites and his wildest eccentricities were always pardonable in her eyes. Poor dear soul! from the first moment when she found out that the little Professor was deeply and gratefully attached to her son, she opened her heart to him unreservedly, and took all his puzzling foreign peculiarities for granted, without so much as attempting to understand any one of them.\n\nMy sister Sarah, with all the advantages of youth, was, strangely enough, less pliable. She did full justice to Pesca’s excellent qualities of heart; but she could not accept him implicitly, as my mother accepted him, for my sake. Her insular notions of propriety rose in perpetual revolt against Pesca’s constitutional contempt for appearances; and she was always more or less undisguisedly astonished at her mother’s familiarity with the eccentric little foreigner. I have observed, not only in my sister’s case, but in the instances of others, that we of the young generation are nothing like so hearty and so impulsive as some of our elders. I constantly see old people flushed and excited by the prospect of some anticipated pleasure which altogether fails to ruffle the tranquillity of their serene grandchildren. Are we, I wonder, quite such genuine boys and girls now as our seniors were in their time? Has the great advance in education taken rather too long a stride; and are we in these modern days, just the least trifle in the world too well brought up?\n\nWithout attempting to answer those questions decisively, I may at least record that I never saw my mother and my sister together in Pesca’s society, without finding my mother much the younger woman of the two. On this occasion, for example, while the old lady was laughing heartily over the boyish manner in which we tumbled into the parlour, Sarah was perturbedly picking up the broken pieces of a teacup, which the Professor had knocked off the table in his precipitate advance to meet me at the door.\n\n“I don’t know what would have happened, Walter,” said my mother, “if you had delayed much longer. Pesca has been half mad with impatience, and I have been half mad with curiosity. The Professor has brought some wonderful news with him, in which he says you are concerned; and he has cruelly refused to give us the smallest hint of it till his friend Walter appeared.”\n\n“Very provoking: it spoils the Set,” murmured Sarah to herself, mournfully absorbed over the ruins of the broken cup.\n\nWhile these words were being spoken, Pesca, happily and fussily unconscious of the irreparable wrong which the crockery had suffered at his hands, was dragging a large arm-chair to the opposite end of the room, so as to command us all three, in the character of a public speaker addressing an audience. Having turned the chair with its back towards us, he jumped into it on his knees, and excitedly addressed his small congregation of three from an impromptu pulpit.\n\n“Now, my good dears,” began Pesca (who always said “good dears” when he meant “worthy friends”), “listen to me. The time has come—I recite my good news—I speak at last.”\n\n“Hear, hear!” said my mother, humouring the joke.\n\n“The next thing he will break, mamma,” whispered Sarah, “will be the back of the best arm-chair.”\n\n“I go back into my life, and I address myself to the noblest of created beings,” continued Pesca, vehemently apostrophising my unworthy self over the top rail of the chair. “Who found me dead at the bottom of the sea (through Cramp); and who pulled me up to the top; and what did I say when I got into my own life and my own clothes again?”\n\n“Much more than was at all necessary,” I answered as doggedly as possible; for the least encouragement in connection with this subject invariably let loose the Professor’s emotions in a flood of tears.\n\n“I said,” persisted Pesca, “that my life belonged to my dear friend, Walter, for the rest of my days—and so it does. I said that I should never be happy again till I had found the opportunity of doing a good Something for Walter—and I have never been contented with myself till this most blessed day. Now,” cried the enthusiastic little man at the top of his voice, “the overflowing happiness bursts out of me at every pore of my skin, like a perspiration; for on my faith, and soul, and honour, the something is done at last, and the only word to say now is—Right-all-right!”\n\nIt may be necessary to explain here that Pesca prided himself on being a perfect Englishman in his language, as well as in his dress, manners, and amusements. Having picked up a few of our most familiar colloquial expressions, he scattered them about over his conversation whenever they happened to occur to him, turning them, in his high relish for their sound and his general ignorance of their sense, into compound words and repetitions of his own, and always running them into each other, as if they consisted of one long syllable.\n\n“Among the fine London Houses where I teach the language of my native country,” said the Professor, rushing into his long-deferred explanation without another word of preface, “there is one, mighty fine, in the big place called Portland. You all know where that is? Yes, yes—course-of-course. The fine house, my good dears, has got inside it a fine family. A Mamma, fair and fat; three young Misses, fair and fat; two young Misters, fair and fat; and a Papa, the fairest and the fattest of all, who is a mighty merchant, up to his eyes in gold—a fine man once, but seeing that he has got a naked head and two chins, fine no longer at the present time. Now mind! I teach the sublime Dante to the young Misses, and ah!—my-soul-bless-my-soul!—it is not in human language to say how the sublime Dante puzzles the pretty heads of all three! No matter—all in good time—and the more lessons the better for me. Now mind! Imagine to yourselves that I am teaching the young Misses to-day, as usual. We are all four of us down together in the Hell of Dante. At the Seventh Circle—but no matter for that: all the Circles are alike to the three young Misses, fair and fat,—at the Seventh Circle, nevertheless, my pupils are sticking fast; and I, to set them going again, recite, explain, and blow myself up red-hot with useless enthusiasm, when—a creak of boots in the passage outside, and in comes the golden Papa, the mighty merchant with the naked head and the two chins.—Ha! my good dears, I am closer than you think for to the business, now. Have you been patient so far? or have you said to yourselves, ‘Deuce-what-the-deuce! Pesca is long-winded to-night?’”\n\nWe declared that we were deeply interested. The Professor went on:\n\n“In his hand, the golden Papa has a letter; and after he has made his excuse for disturbing us in our Infernal Region with the common mortal Business of the house, he addresses himself to the three young Misses, and begins, as you English begin everything in this blessed world that you have to say, with a great O. ‘O, my dears,’ says the mighty merchant, ‘I have got here a letter from my friend, Mr.——‘(the name has slipped out of my mind; but no matter; we shall come back to that; yes, yes—right-all-right). So the Papa says, ‘I have got a letter from my friend, the Mister; and he wants a recommend from me, of a drawing-master, to go down to his house in the country.’ My-soul-bless-my-soul! when I heard the golden Papa say those words, if I had been big enough to reach up to him, I should have put my arms round his neck, and pressed him to my bosom in a long and grateful hug! As it was, I only bounced upon my chair. My seat was on thorns, and my soul was on fire to speak but I held my tongue, and let Papa go on. ‘Perhaps you know,’ says this good man of money, twiddling his friend’s letter this way and that, in his golden fingers and thumbs, ‘perhaps you know, my dears, of a drawing-master that I can recommend?’ The three young Misses all look at each other, and then say (with the indispensable great O to begin) “O, dear no, Papa! But here is Mr. Pesca——’ At the mention of myself I can hold no longer—the thought of you, my good dears, mounts like blood to my head—I start from my seat, as if a spike had grown up from the ground through the bottom of my chair—I address myself to the mighty merchant, and I say (English phrase) ‘Dear sir, I have the man! The first and foremost drawing-master of the world! Recommend him by the post to-night, and send him off, bag and baggage (English phrase again—ha!), send him off, bag and baggage, by the train to-morrow!’ ‘Stop, stop,’ says Papa; ‘is he a foreigner, or an Englishman?’ ‘English to the bone of his back,’ I answer. ‘Respectable?’ says Papa. ‘Sir,’ I say (for this last question of his outrages me, and I have done being familiar with him—) ‘Sir! the immortal fire of genius burns in this Englishman’s bosom, and, what is more, his father had it before him!’ ‘Never mind,’ says the golden barbarian of a Papa, ‘never mind about his genius, Mr. Pesca. We don’t want genius in this country, unless it is accompanied by respectability—and then we are very glad to have it, very glad indeed. Can your friend produce testimonials—letters that speak to his character?’ I wave my hand negligently. ‘Letters?’ I say. ‘Ha! my-soul-bless-my-soul! I should think so, indeed! Volumes of letters and portfolios of testimonials, if you like!’ ‘One or two will do,’ says this man of phlegm and money. ‘Let him send them to me, with his name and address. And—stop, stop, Mr. Pesca—before you go to your friend, you had better take a note.’ ‘Bank-note!’ I say, indignantly. ‘No bank-note, if you please, till my brave Englishman has earned it first.’ ‘Bank-note!’ says Papa, in a great surprise, ‘who talked of bank-note? I mean a note of the terms—a memorandum of what he is expected to do. Go on with your lesson, Mr. Pesca, and I will give you the necessary extract from my friend’s letter.’ Down sits the man of merchandise and money to his pen, ink, and paper; and down I go once again into the Hell of Dante, with my three young Misses after me. In ten minutes’ time the note is written, and the boots of Papa are creaking themselves away in the passage outside. From that moment, on my faith, and soul, and honour, I know nothing more! The glorious thought that I have caught my opportunity at last, and that my grateful service for my dearest friend in the world is as good as done already, flies up into my head and makes me drunk. How I pull my young Misses and myself out of our Infernal Region again, how my other business is done afterwards, how my little bit of dinner slides itself down my throat, I know no more than a man in the moon. Enough for me, that here I am, with the mighty merchant’s note in my hand, as large as life, as hot as fire, and as happy as a king! Ha! ha! ha! right-right-right-all-right!” Here the Professor waved the memorandum of terms over his head, and ended his long and voluble narrative with his shrill Italian parody on an English cheer.\n\nMy mother rose the moment he had done, with flushed cheeks and brightened eyes. She caught the little man warmly by both hands.\n\n“My dear, good Pesca,” she said, “I never doubted your true affection for Walter—but I am more than ever persuaded of it now!”\n\n“I am sure we are very much obliged to Professor Pesca, for Walter’s sake,” added Sarah. She half rose, while she spoke, as if to approach the arm-chair, in her turn; but, observing that Pesca was rapturously kissing my mother’s hands, looked serious, and resumed her seat. “If the familiar little man treats my mother in that way, how will he treat ME?” Faces sometimes tell truth; and that was unquestionably the thought in Sarah’s mind, as she sat down again.\n\nAlthough I myself was gratefully sensible of the kindness of Pesca’s motives, my spirits were hardly so much elevated as they ought to have been by the prospect of future employment now placed before me. When the Professor had quite done with my mother’s hand, and when I had warmly thanked him for his interference on my behalf, I asked to be allowed to look at the note of terms which his respectable patron had drawn up for my inspection.\n\nPesca handed me the paper, with a triumphant flourish of the hand.\n\n“Read!” said the little man majestically. “I promise you my friend, the writing of the golden Papa speaks with a tongue of trumpets for itself.”\n\nThe note of terms was plain, straightforward, and comprehensive, at any rate. It informed me,\n\nFirst, That Frederick Fairlie, Esquire, of Limmeridge House, Cumberland, wanted to engage the services of a thoroughly competent drawing-master, for a period of four months certain.\n\nSecondly, That the duties which the master was expected to perform would be of a twofold kind. He was to superintend the instruction of two young ladies in the art of painting in water-colours; and he was to devote his leisure time, afterwards, to the business of repairing and mounting a valuable collection of drawings, which had been suffered to fall into a condition of total neglect.\n\nThirdly, That the terms offered to the person who should undertake and properly perform these duties were four guineas a week; that he was to reside at Limmeridge House; and that he was to be treated there on the footing of a gentleman.\n\nFourthly, and lastly, That no person need think of applying for this situation unless he could furnish the most unexceptionable references to character and abilities. The references were to be sent to Mr. Fairlie’s friend in London, who was empowered to conclude all necessary arrangements. These instructions were followed by the name and address of Pesca’s employer in Portland Place—and there the note, or memorandum, ended.\n\nThe prospect which this offer of an engagement held out was certainly an attractive one. The employment was likely to be both easy and agreeable; it was proposed to me at the autumn time of the year when I was least occupied; and the terms, judging by my personal experience in my profession, were surprisingly liberal. I knew this; I knew that I ought to consider myself very fortunate if I succeeded in securing the offered employment—and yet, no sooner had I read the memorandum than I felt an inexplicable unwillingness within me to stir in the matter. I had never in the whole of my previous experience found my duty and my inclination so painfully and so unaccountably at variance as I found them now.\n\n“Oh, Walter, your father never had such a chance as this!” said my mother, when she had read the note of terms and had handed it back to me.\n\n“Such distinguished people to know,” remarked Sarah, straightening herself in the chair; “and on such gratifying terms of equality too!”\n\n“Yes, yes; the terms, in every sense, are tempting enough,” I replied impatiently. “But before I send in my testimonials, I should like a little time to consider——”\n\n“Consider!” exclaimed my mother. “Why, Walter, what is the matter with you?”\n\n“Consider!” echoed my sister. “What a very extraordinary thing to say, under the circumstances!”\n\n“Consider!” chimed in the Professor. “What is there to consider about? Answer me this! Have you not been complaining of your health, and have you not been longing for what you call a smack of the country breeze? Well! there in your hand is the paper that offers you perpetual choking mouthfuls of country breeze for four months’ time. Is it not so? Ha! Again—you want money. Well! Is four golden guineas a week nothing? My-soul-bless-my-soul! only give it to me—and my boots shall creak like the golden Papa’s, with a sense of the overpowering richness of the man who walks in them! Four guineas a week, and, more than that, the charming society of two young misses! and, more than that, your bed, your breakfast, your dinner, your gorging English teas and lunches and drinks of foaming beer, all for nothing—why, Walter, my dear good friend—deuce-what-the-deuce!—for the first time in my life I have not eyes enough in my head to look, and wonder at you!”\n\nNeither my mother’s evident astonishment at my behaviour, nor Pesca’s fervid enumeration of the advantages offered to me by the new employment, had any effect in shaking my unreasonable disinclination to go to Limmeridge House. After starting all the petty objections that I could think of to going to Cumberland, and after hearing them answered, one after another, to my own complete discomfiture, I tried to set up a last obstacle by asking what was to become of my pupils in London while I was teaching Mr. Fairlie’s young ladies to sketch from nature. The obvious answer to this was, that the greater part of them would be away on their autumn travels, and that the few who remained at home might be confided to the care of one of my brother drawing-masters, whose pupils I had once taken off his hands under similar circumstances. My sister reminded me that this gentleman had expressly placed his services at my disposal, during the present season, in case I wished to leave town; my mother seriously appealed to me not to let an idle caprice stand in the way of my own interests and my own health; and Pesca piteously entreated that I would not wound him to the heart by rejecting the first grateful offer of service that he had been able to make to the friend who had saved his life.\n\nThe evident sincerity and affection which inspired these remonstrances would have influenced any man with an atom of good feeling in his composition. Though I could not conquer my own unaccountable perversity, I had at least virtue enough to be heartily ashamed of it, and to end the discussion pleasantly by giving way, and promising to do all that was wanted of me.\n\nThe rest of the evening passed merrily enough in humorous anticipations of my coming life with the two young ladies in Cumberland. Pesca, inspired by our national grog, which appeared to get into his head, in the most marvellous manner, five minutes after it had gone down his throat, asserted his claims to be considered a complete Englishman by making a series of speeches in rapid succession, proposing my mother’s health, my sister’s health, my health, and the healths, in mass, of Mr. Fairlie and the two young Misses, pathetically returning thanks himself, immediately afterwards, for the whole party. “A secret, Walter,” said my little friend confidentially, as we walked home together. “I am flushed by the recollection of my own eloquence. My soul bursts itself with ambition. One of these days I go into your noble Parliament. It is the dream of my whole life to be Honourable Pesca, M.P.!”\n\nThe next morning I sent my testimonials to the Professor’s employer in Portland Place. Three days passed, and I concluded, with secret satisfaction, that my papers had not been found sufficiently explicit. On the fourth day, however, an answer came. It announced that Mr. Fairlie accepted my services, and requested me to start for Cumberland immediately. All the necessary instructions for my journey were carefully and clearly added in a postscript.\n\nI made my arrangements, unwillingly enough, for leaving London early the next day. Towards evening Pesca looked in, on his way to a dinner-party, to bid me good-bye.\n\n“I shall dry my tears in your absence,” said the Professor gaily, “with this glorious thought. It is my auspicious hand that has given the first push to your fortune in the world. Go, my friend! When your sun shines in Cumberland (English proverb), in the name of heaven make your hay. Marry one of the two young Misses; become Honourable Hartright, M.P.; and when you are on the top of the ladder remember that Pesca, at the bottom, has done it all!”\n\nI tried to laugh with my little friend over his parting jest, but my spirits were not to be commanded. Something jarred in me almost painfully while he was speaking his light farewell words.\n\nWhen I was left alone again nothing remained to be done but to walk to the Hampstead cottage and bid my mother and Sarah good-bye.\n\n# IV\n\nThe heat had been painfully oppressive all day, and it was now a close and sultry night.\n\nMy mother and sister had spoken so many last words, and had begged me to wait another five minutes so many times, that it was nearly midnight when the servant locked the garden-gate behind me. I walked forward a few paces on the shortest way back to London, then stopped and hesitated.\n\nThe moon was full and broad in the dark blue starless sky, and the broken ground of the heath looked wild enough in the mysterious light to be hundreds of miles away from the great city that lay beneath it. The idea of descending any sooner than I could help into the heat and gloom of London repelled me. The prospect of going to bed in my airless chambers, and the prospect of gradual suffocation, seemed, in my present restless frame of mind and body, to be one and the same thing. I determined to stroll home in the purer air by the most roundabout way I could take; to follow the white winding paths across the lonely heath; and to approach London through its most open suburb by striking into the Finchley Road, and so getting back, in the cool of the new morning, by the western side of the Regent’s Park.\n\nI wound my way down slowly over the heath, enjoying the divine stillness of the scene, and admiring the soft alternations of light and shade as they followed each other over the broken ground on every side of me. So long as I was proceeding through this first and prettiest part of my night walk my mind remained passively open to the impressions produced by the view; and I thought but little on any subject—indeed, so far as my own sensations were concerned, I can hardly say that I thought at all.\n\nBut when I had left the heath and had turned into the by-road, where there was less to see, the ideas naturally engendered by the approaching change in my habits and occupations gradually drew more and more of my attention exclusively to themselves. By the time I had arrived at the end of the road I had become completely absorbed in my own fanciful visions of Limmeridge House, of Mr. Fairlie, and of the two ladies whose practice in the art of water-colour painting I was so soon to superintend.\n\nI had now arrived at that particular point of my walk where four roads met—the road to Hampstead, along which I had returned, the road to Finchley, the road to West End, and the road back to London. I had mechanically turned in this latter direction, and was strolling along the lonely high-road—idly wondering, I remember, what the Cumberland young ladies would look like—when, in one moment, every drop of blood in my body was brought to a stop by the touch of a hand laid lightly and suddenly on my shoulder from behind me.\n\nI turned on the instant, with my fingers tightening round the handle of my stick.\n\nThere, in the middle of the broad bright high-road—there, as if it had that moment sprung out of the earth or dropped from the heaven—stood the figure of a solitary Woman, dressed from head to foot in white garments, her face bent in grave inquiry on mine, her hand pointing to the dark cloud over London, as I faced her.\n\nI was far too seriously startled by the suddenness with which this extraordinary apparition stood before me, in the dead of night and in that lonely place, to ask what she wanted. The strange woman spoke first.\n\n“Is that the road to London?” she said.\n\nI looked attentively at her, as she put that singular question to me. It was then nearly one o’clock. All I could discern distinctly by the moonlight was a colourless, youthful face, meagre and sharp to look at about the cheeks and chin; large, grave, wistfully attentive eyes; nervous, uncertain lips; and light hair of a pale, brownish-yellow hue. There was nothing wild, nothing immodest in her manner: it was quiet and self-controlled, a little melancholy and a little touched by suspicion; not exactly the manner of a lady, and, at the same time, not the manner of a woman in the humblest rank of life. The voice, little as I had yet heard of it, had something curiously still and mechanical in its tones, and the utterance was remarkably rapid. She held a small bag in her hand: and her dress—bonnet, shawl, and gown all of white—was, so far as I could guess, certainly not composed of very delicate or very expensive materials. Her figure was slight, and rather above the average height—her gait and actions free from the slightest approach to extravagance. This was all that I could observe of her in the dim light and under the perplexingly strange circumstances of our meeting. What sort of a woman she was, and how she came to be out alone in the high-road, an hour after midnight, I altogether failed to guess. The one thing of which I felt certain was, that the grossest of mankind could not have misconstrued her motive in speaking, even at that suspiciously late hour and in that suspiciously lonely place.\n\n“Did you hear me?” she said, still quietly and rapidly, and without the least fretfulness or impatience. “I asked if that was the way to London.”\n\n“Yes,” I replied, “that is the way: it leads to St. John’s Wood and the Regent’s Park. You must excuse my not answering you before. I was rather startled by your sudden appearance in the road; and I am, even now, quite unable to account for it.”\n\n“You don’t suspect me of doing anything wrong, do you? I have done nothing wrong. I have met with an accident—I am very unfortunate in being here alone so late. Why do you suspect me of doing wrong?”\n\nShe spoke with unnecessary earnestness and agitation, and shrank back from me several paces. I did my best to reassure her.\n\n“Pray don’t suppose that I have any idea of suspecting you,” I said, “or any other wish than to be of assistance to you, if I can. I only wondered at your appearance in the road, because it seemed to me to be empty the instant before I saw you.”\n\nShe turned, and pointed back to a place at the junction of the road to London and the road to Hampstead, where there was a gap in the hedge.\n\n“I heard you coming,” she said, “and hid there to see what sort of man you were, before I risked speaking. I doubted and feared about it till you passed; and then I was obliged to steal after you, and touch you.”\n\nSteal after me and touch me? Why not call to me? Strange, to say the least of it.\n\n“May I trust you?” she asked. “You don’t think the worse of me because I have met with an accident?” She stopped in confusion; shifted her bag from one hand to the other; and sighed bitterly.\n\nThe loneliness and helplessness of the woman touched me. The natural impulse to assist her and to spare her got the better of the judgment, the caution, the worldly tact, which an older, wiser, and colder man might have summoned to help him in this strange emergency.\n\n“You may trust me for any harmless purpose,” I said. “If it troubles you to explain your strange situation to me, don’t think of returning to the subject again. I have no right to ask you for any explanations. Tell me how I can help you; and if I can, I will.”\n\n“You are very kind, and I am very, very thankful to have met you.” The first touch of womanly tenderness that I had heard from her trembled in her voice as she said the words; but no tears glistened in those large, wistfully attentive eyes of hers, which were still fixed on me. “I have only been in London once before,” she went on, more and more rapidly, “and I know nothing about that side of it, yonder. Can I get a fly, or a carriage of any kind? Is it too late? I don’t know. If you could show me where to get a fly—and if you will only promise not to interfere with me, and to let me leave you, when and how I please—I have a friend in London who will be glad to receive me—I want nothing else—will you promise?”\n\nShe looked anxiously up and down the road; shifted her bag again from one hand to the other; repeated the words, “Will you promise?” and looked hard in my face, with a pleading fear and confusion that it troubled me to see.\n\nWhat could I do? Here was a stranger utterly and helplessly at my mercy—and that stranger a forlorn woman. No house was near; no one was passing whom I could consult; and no earthly right existed on my part to give me a power of control over her, even if I had known how to exercise it. I trace these lines, self-distrustfully, with the shadows of after-events darkening the very paper I write on; and still I say, what could I do?\n\nWhat I did do, was to try and gain time by questioning her. “Are you sure that your friend in London will receive you at such a late hour as this?” I said.\n\n“Quite sure. Only say you will let me leave you when and how I please—only say you won’t interfere with me. Will you promise?”\n\nAs she repeated the words for the third time, she came close to me and laid her hand, with a sudden gentle stealthiness, on my bosom—a thin hand; a cold hand (when I removed it with mine) even on that sultry night. Remember that I was young; remember that the hand which touched me was a woman’s.\n\n“Will you promise?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nOne word! The little familiar word that is on everybody’s lips, every hour in the day. Oh me! and I tremble, now, when I write it.\n\nWe set our faces towards London, and walked on together in the first still hour of the new day—I, and this woman, whose name, whose character, whose story, whose objects in life, whose very presence by my side, at that moment, were fathomless mysteries to me. It was like a dream. Was I Walter Hartright? Was this the well-known, uneventful road, where holiday people strolled on Sundays? Had I really left, little more than an hour since, the quiet, decent, conventionally domestic atmosphere of my mother’s cottage? I was too bewildered—too conscious also of a vague sense of something like self-reproach—to speak to my strange companion for some minutes. It was her voice again that first broke the silence between us.\n\n“I want to ask you something,” she said suddenly. “Do you know many people in London?”\n\n“Yes, a great many.”\n\n“Many men of rank and title?” There was an unmistakable tone of suspicion in the strange question. I hesitated about answering it.\n\n“Some,” I said, after a moment’s silence.\n\n“Many”—she came to a full stop, and looked me searchingly in the face—“many men of the rank of Baronet?”\n\nToo much astonished to reply, I questioned her in my turn.\n\n“Why do you ask?”\n\n“Because I hope, for my own sake, there is one Baronet that you don’t know.”\n\n“Will you tell me his name?”\n\n“I can’t—I daren’t—I forget myself when I mention it.” She spoke loudly and almost fiercely, raised her clenched hand in the air, and shook it passionately; then, on a sudden, controlled herself again, and added, in tones lowered to a whisper “Tell me which of them YOU know.”\n\nI could hardly refuse to humour her in such a trifle, and I mentioned three names. Two, the names of fathers of families whose daughters I taught; one, the name of a bachelor who had once taken me a cruise in his yacht, to make sketches for him.\n\n“Ah! you DON’T know him,” she said, with a sigh of relief. “Are you a man of rank and title yourself?”\n\n“Far from it. I am only a drawing-master.”\n\nAs the reply passed my lips—a little bitterly, perhaps—she took my arm with the abruptness which characterised all her actions.\n\n“Not a man of rank and title,” she repeated to herself. “Thank God! I may trust HIM.”\n\nI had hitherto contrived to master my curiosity out of consideration for my companion; but it got the better of me now.\n\n“I am afraid you have serious reason to complain of some man of rank and title?” I said. “I am afraid the baronet, whose name you are unwilling to mention to me, has done you some grievous wrong? Is he the cause of your being out here at this strange time of night?”\n\n“Don’t ask me: don’t make me talk of it,” she answered. “I’m not fit now. I have been cruelly used and cruelly wronged. You will be kinder than ever, if you will walk on fast, and not speak to me. I sadly want to quiet myself, if I can.”\n\nWe moved forward again at a quick pace; and for half an hour, at least, not a word passed on either side. From time to time, being forbidden to make any more inquiries, I stole a look at her face. It was always the same; the lips close shut, the brow frowning, the eyes looking straight forward, eagerly and yet absently. We had reached the first houses, and were close on the new Wesleyan college, before her set features relaxed and she spoke once more.\n\n“Do you live in London?” she said.\n\n“Yes.” As I answered, it struck me that she might have formed some intention of appealing to me for assistance or advice, and that I ought to spare her a possible disappointment by warning her of my approaching absence from home. So I added, “But to-morrow I shall be away from London for some time. I am going into the country.”\n\n“Where?” she asked. “North or south?”\n\n“North—to Cumberland.”\n\n“Cumberland!” she repeated the word tenderly. “Ah! I wish I was going there too. I was once happy in Cumberland.”\n\nI tried again to lift the veil that hung between this woman and me.\n\n“Perhaps you were born,” I said, “in the beautiful Lake country.”\n\n“No,” she answered. “I was born in Hampshire; but I once went to school for a little while in Cumberland. Lakes? I don’t remember any lakes. It’s Limmeridge village, and Limmeridge House, I should like to see again.”\n\nIt was my turn now to stop suddenly. In the excited state of my curiosity, at that moment, the chance reference to Mr. Fairlie’s place of residence, on the lips of my strange companion, staggered me with astonishment.\n\n“Did you hear anybody calling after us?” she asked, looking up and down the road affrightedly, the instant I stopped.\n\n“No, no. I was only struck by the name of Limmeridge House. I heard it mentioned by some Cumberland people a few days since.”\n\n“Ah! not my people. Mrs. Fairlie is dead; and her husband is dead; and their little girl may be married and gone away by this time. I can’t say who lives at Limmeridge now. If any more are left there of that name, I only know I love them for Mrs. Fairlie’s sake.”\n\nShe seemed about to say more; but while she was speaking, we came within view of the turnpike, at the top of the Avenue Road. Her hand tightened round my arm, and she looked anxiously at the gate before us.\n\n“Is the turnpike man looking out?” she asked.\n\nHe was not looking out; no one else was near the place when we passed through the gate. The sight of the gas-lamps and houses seemed to agitate her, and to make her impatient.\n\n“This is London,” she said. “Do you see any carriage I can get? I am tired and frightened. I want to shut myself in and be driven away.”\n\nI explained to her that we must walk a little further to get to a cab-stand, unless we were fortunate enough to meet with an empty vehicle; and then tried to resume the subject of Cumberland. It was useless. That idea of shutting herself in, and being driven away, had now got full possession of her mind. She could think and talk of nothing else.\n\nWe had hardly proceeded a third of the way down the Avenue Road when I saw a cab draw up at a house a few doors below us, on the opposite side of the way. A gentleman got out and let himself in at the garden door. I hailed the cab, as the driver mounted the box again. When we crossed the road, my companion’s impatience increased to such an extent that she almost forced me to run.\n\n“It’s so late,” she said. “I am only in a hurry because it’s so late.”\n\n“I can’t take you, sir, if you’re not going towards Tottenham Court Road,” said the driver civilly, when I opened the cab door. “My horse is dead beat, and I can’t get him no further than the stable.”\n\n“Yes, yes. That will do for me. I’m going that way—I’m going that way.” She spoke with breathless eagerness, and pressed by me into the cab.\n\nI had assured myself that the man was sober as well as civil before I let her enter the vehicle. And now, when she was seated inside, I entreated her to let me see her set down safely at her destination.\n\n“No, no, no,” she said vehemently. “I’m quite safe, and quite happy now. If you are a gentleman, remember your promise. Let him drive on till I stop him. Thank you—oh! thank you, thank you!”\n\nMy hand was on the cab door. She caught it in hers, kissed it, and pushed it away. The cab drove off at the same moment—I started into the road, with some vague idea of stopping it again, I hardly knew why—hesitated from dread of frightening and distressing her—called, at last, but not loudly enough to attract the driver’s attention. The sound of the wheels grew fainter in the distance—the cab melted into the black shadows on the road—the woman in white was gone.\n\nTen minutes or more had passed. I was still on the same side of the way; now mechanically walking forward a few paces; now stopping again absently. At one moment I found myself doubting the reality of my own adventure; at another I was perplexed and distressed by an uneasy sense of having done wrong, which yet left me confusedly ignorant of how I could have done right. I hardly knew where I was going, or what I meant to do next; I was conscious of nothing but the confusion of my own thoughts, when I was abruptly recalled to myself—awakened, I might almost say—by the sound of rapidly approaching wheels close behind me.\n\nI was on the dark side of the road, in the thick shadow of some garden trees, when I stopped to look round. On the opposite and lighter side of the way, a short distance below me, a policeman was strolling along in the direction of the Regent’s Park.\n\nThe carriage passed me—an open chaise driven by two men.\n\n“Stop!” cried one. “There’s a policeman. Let’s ask him.”\n\nThe horse was instantly pulled up, a few yards beyond the dark place where I stood.\n\n“Policeman!” cried the first speaker. “Have you seen a woman pass this way?”\n\n“What sort of woman, sir?”\n\n“A woman in a lavender-coloured gown——”\n\n“No, no,” interposed the second man. “The clothes we gave her were found on her bed. She must have gone away in the clothes she wore when she came to us. In white, policeman. A woman in white.”\n\n“I haven’t seen her, sir.”\n\n“If you or any of your men meet with the woman, stop her, and send her in careful keeping to that address. I’ll pay all expenses, and a fair reward into the bargain.”\n\nThe policeman looked at the card that was handed down to him.\n\n“Why are we to stop her, sir? What has she done?”\n\n“Done! She has escaped from my Asylum. Don’t forget; a woman in white. Drive on.”\n\n“She has escaped from my Asylum!”\n\nI cannot say with truth that the terrible inference which those words suggested flashed upon me like a new revelation. Some of the strange questions put to me by the woman in white, after my ill-considered promise to leave her free to act as she pleased, had suggested the conclusion either that she was naturally flighty and unsettled, or that some recent shock of terror had disturbed the balance of her faculties. But the idea of absolute insanity which we all associate with the very name of an Asylum, had, I can honestly declare, never occurred to me, in connection with her. I had seen nothing, in her language or her actions, to justify it at the time; and even with the new light thrown on her by the words which the stranger had addressed to the policeman, I could see nothing to justify it now.\n\nWhat had I done? Assisted the victim of the most horrible of all false imprisonments to escape; or cast loose on the wide world of London an unfortunate creature, whose actions it was my duty, and every man’s duty, mercifully to control? I turned sick at heart when the question occurred to me, and when I felt self-reproachfully that it was asked too late.\n\nIn the disturbed state of my mind, it was useless to think of going to bed, when I at last got back to my chambers in Clement’s Inn. Before many hours elapsed it would be necessary to start on my journey to Cumberland. I sat down and tried, first to sketch, then to read—but the woman in white got between me and my pencil, between me and my book. Had the forlorn creature come to any harm? That was my first thought, though I shrank selfishly from confronting it. Other thoughts followed, on which it was less harrowing to dwell. Where had she stopped the cab? What had become of her now? Had she been traced and captured by the men in the chaise? Or was she still capable of controlling her own actions; and were we two following our widely parted roads towards one point in the mysterious future, at which we were to meet once more?\n\nIt was a relief when the hour came to lock my door, to bid farewell to London pursuits, London pupils, and London friends, and to be in movement again towards new interests and a new life. Even the bustle and confusion at the railway terminus, so wearisome and bewildering at other times, roused me and did me good.\n\nMy travelling instructions directed me to go to Carlisle, and then to diverge by a branch railway which ran in the direction of the coast. As a misfortune to begin with, our engine broke down between Lancaster and Carlisle. The delay occasioned by this accident caused me to be too late for the branch train, by which I was to have gone on immediately. I had to wait some hours; and when a later train finally deposited me at the nearest station to Limmeridge House, it was past ten, and the night was so dark that I could hardly see my way to the pony-chaise which Mr. Fairlie had ordered to be in waiting for me.\n\nThe driver was evidently discomposed by the lateness of my arrival. He was in that state of highly respectful sulkiness which is peculiar to English servants. We drove away slowly through the darkness in perfect silence. The roads were bad, and the dense obscurity of the night increased the difficulty of getting over the ground quickly. It was, by my watch, nearly an hour and a half from the time of our leaving the station before I heard the sound of the sea in the distance, and the crunch of our wheels on a smooth gravel drive. We had passed one gate before entering the drive, and we passed another before we drew up at the house. I was received by a solemn man-servant out of livery, was informed that the family had retired for the night, and was then led into a large and lofty room where my supper was awaiting me, in a forlorn manner, at one extremity of a lonesome mahogany wilderness of dining-table.\n\nI was too tired and out of spirits to eat or drink much, especially with the solemn servant waiting on me as elaborately as if a small dinner party had arrived at the house instead of a solitary man. In a quarter of an hour I was ready to be taken up to my bedchamber. The solemn servant conducted me into a prettily furnished room—said, “Breakfast at nine o’clock, sir”—looked all round him to see that everything was in its proper place, and noiselessly withdrew.\n\n“What shall I see in my dreams to-night?” I thought to myself, as I put out the candle; “the woman in white? or the unknown inhabitants of this Cumberland mansion?” It was a strange sensation to be sleeping in the house, like a friend of the family, and yet not to know one of the inmates, even by sight!\n\n# VI\n\nWhen I rose the next morning and drew up my blind, the sea opened before me joyously under the broad August sunlight, and the distant coast of Scotland fringed the horizon with its lines of melting blue.\n\nThe view was such a surprise, and such a change to me, after my weary London experience of brick and mortar landscape, that I seemed to burst into a new life and a new set of thoughts the moment I looked at it. A confused sensation of having suddenly lost my familiarity with the past, without acquiring any additional clearness of idea in reference to the present or the future, took possession of my mind. Circumstances that were but a few days old faded back in my memory, as if they had happened months and months since. Pesca’s quaint announcement of the means by which he had procured me my present employment; the farewell evening I had passed with my mother and sister; even my mysterious adventure on the way home from Hampstead—had all become like events which might have occurred at some former epoch of my existence. Although the woman in white was still in my mind, the image of her seemed to have grown dull and faint already.\n\nA little before nine o’clock, I descended to the ground-floor of the house. The solemn man-servant of the night before met me wandering among the passages, and compassionately showed me the way to the breakfast-room.\n\nMy first glance round me, as the man opened the door, disclosed a well-furnished breakfast-table, standing in the middle of a long room, with many windows in it. I looked from the table to the window farthest from me, and saw a lady standing at it, with her back turned towards me. The instant my eyes rested on her, I was struck by the rare beauty of her form, and by the unaffected grace of her attitude. Her figure was tall, yet not too tall; comely and well-developed, yet not fat; her head set on her shoulders with an easy, pliant firmness; her waist, perfection in the eyes of a man, for it occupied its natural place, it filled out its natural circle, it was visibly and delightfully undeformed by stays. She had not heard my entrance into the room; and I allowed myself the luxury of admiring her for a few moments, before I moved one of the chairs near me, as the least embarrassing means of attracting her attention. She turned towards me immediately. The easy elegance of every movement of her limbs and body as soon as she began to advance from the far end of the room, set me in a flutter of expectation to see her face clearly. She left the window—and I said to myself, The lady is dark. She moved forward a few steps—and I said to myself, The lady is young. She approached nearer—and I said to myself (with a sense of surprise which words fail me to express), The lady is ugly!\n\nNever was the old conventional maxim, that Nature cannot err, more flatly contradicted—never was the fair promise of a lovely figure more strangely and startlingly belied by the face and head that crowned it. The lady’s complexion was almost swarthy, and the dark down on her upper lip was almost a moustache. She had a large, firm, masculine mouth and jaw; prominent, piercing, resolute brown eyes; and thick, coal-black hair, growing unusually low down on her forehead. Her expression—bright, frank, and intelligent—appeared, while she was silent, to be altogether wanting in those feminine attractions of gentleness and pliability, without which the beauty of the handsomest woman alive is beauty incomplete. To see such a face as this set on shoulders that a sculptor would have longed to model—to be charmed by the modest graces of action through which the symmetrical limbs betrayed their beauty when they moved, and then to be almost repelled by the masculine form and masculine look of the features in which the perfectly shaped figure ended—was to feel a sensation oddly akin to the helpless discomfort familiar to us all in sleep, when we recognise yet cannot reconcile the anomalies and contradictions of a dream.\n\n“Mr. Hartright?” said the lady interrogatively, her dark face lighting up with a smile, and softening and growing womanly the moment she began to speak. “We resigned all hope of you last night, and went to bed as usual. Accept my apologies for our apparent want of attention; and allow me to introduce myself as one of your pupils. Shall we shake hands? I suppose we must come to it sooner or later—and why not sooner?”\n\nThese odd words of welcome were spoken in a clear, ringing, pleasant voice. The offered hand—rather large, but beautifully formed—was given to me with the easy, unaffected self-reliance of a highly-bred woman. We sat down together at the breakfast-table in as cordial and customary a manner as if we had known each other for years, and had met at Limmeridge House to talk over old times by previous appointment.\n\n“I hope you come here good-humouredly determined to make the best of your position,” continued the lady. “You will have to begin this morning by putting up with no other company at breakfast than mine. My sister is in her own room, nursing that essentially feminine malady, a slight headache; and her old governess, Mrs. Vesey, is charitably attending on her with restorative tea. My uncle, Mr. Fairlie, never joins us at any of our meals: he is an invalid, and keeps bachelor state in his own apartments. There is nobody else in the house but me. Two young ladies have been staying here, but they went away yesterday, in despair; and no wonder. All through their visit (in consequence of Mr. Fairlie’s invalid condition) we produced no such convenience in the house as a flirtable, danceable, small-talkable creature of the male sex; and the consequence was, we did nothing but quarrel, especially at dinner-time. How can you expect four women to dine together alone every day, and not quarrel? We are such fools, we can’t entertain each other at table. You see I don’t think much of my own sex, Mr. Hartright—which will you have, tea or coffee?—no woman does think much of her own sex, although few of them confess it as freely as I do. Dear me, you look puzzled. Why? Are you wondering what you will have for breakfast? or are you surprised at my careless way of talking? In the first case, I advise you, as a friend, to have nothing to do with that cold ham at your elbow, and to wait till the omelette comes in. In the second case, I will give you some tea to compose your spirits, and do all a woman can (which is very little, by-the-bye) to hold my tongue.”\n\nShe handed me my cup of tea, laughing gaily. Her light flow of talk, and her lively familiarity of manner with a total stranger, were accompanied by an unaffected naturalness and an easy inborn confidence in herself and her position, which would have secured her the respect of the most audacious man breathing. While it was impossible to be formal and reserved in her company, it was more than impossible to take the faintest vestige of a liberty with her, even in thought. I felt this instinctively, even while I caught the infection of her own bright gaiety of spirits—even while I did my best to answer her in her own frank, lively way.\n\n“Yes, yes,” she said, when I had suggested the only explanation I could offer, to account for my perplexed looks, “I understand. You are such a perfect stranger in the house, that you are puzzled by my familiar references to the worthy inhabitants. Natural enough: I ought to have thought of it before. At any rate, I can set it right now. Suppose I begin with myself, so as to get done with that part of the subject as soon as possible? My name is Marian Halcombe; and I am as inaccurate as women usually are, in calling Mr. Fairlie my uncle, and Miss Fairlie my sister. My mother was twice married: the first time to Mr. Halcombe, my father; the second time to Mr. Fairlie, my half-sister’s father. Except that we are both orphans, we are in every respect as unlike each other as possible. My father was a poor man, and Miss Fairlie’s father was a rich man. I have got nothing, and she has a fortune. I am dark and ugly, and she is fair and pretty. Everybody thinks me crabbed and odd (with perfect justice); and everybody thinks her sweet-tempered and charming (with more justice still). In short, she is an angel; and I am—— Try some of that marmalade, Mr. Hartright, and finish the sentence, in the name of female propriety, for yourself. What am I to tell you about Mr. Fairlie? Upon my honour, I hardly know. He is sure to send for you after breakfast, and you can study him for yourself. In the meantime, I may inform you, first, that he is the late Mr. Fairlie’s younger brother; secondly, that he is a single man; and thirdly, that he is Miss Fairlie’s guardian. I won’t live without her, and she can’t live without me; and that is how I come to be at Limmeridge House. My sister and I are honestly fond of each other; which, you will say, is perfectly unaccountable, under the circumstances, and I quite agree with you—but so it is. You must please both of us, Mr. Hartright, or please neither of us: and, what is still more trying, you will be thrown entirely upon our society. Mrs. Vesey is an excellent person, who possesses all the cardinal virtues, and counts for nothing; and Mr. Fairlie is too great an invalid to be a companion for anybody. I don’t know what is the matter with him, and the doctors don’t know what is the matter with him, and he doesn’t know himself what is the matter with him. We all say it’s on the nerves, and we none of us know what we mean when we say it. However, I advise you to humour his little peculiarities, when you see him to-day. Admire his collection of coins, prints, and water-colour drawings, and you will win his heart. Upon my word, if you can be contented with a quiet country life, I don’t see why you should not get on very well here. From breakfast to lunch, Mr. Fairlie’s drawings will occupy you. After lunch, Miss Fairlie and I shoulder our sketch-books, and go out to misrepresent Nature, under your directions. Drawing is her favourite whim, mind, not mine. Women can’t draw—their minds are too flighty, and their eyes are too inattentive. No matter—my sister likes it; so I waste paint and spoil paper, for her sake, as composedly as any woman in England. As for the evenings, I think we can help you through them. Miss Fairlie plays delightfully. For my own poor part, I don’t know one note of music from the other; but I can match you at chess, backgammon, ecarte, and (with the inevitable female drawbacks) even at billiards as well. What do you think of the programme? Can you reconcile yourself to our quiet, regular life? or do you mean to be restless, and secretly thirst for change and adventure, in the humdrum atmosphere of Limmeridge House?”\n\nShe had run on thus far, in her gracefully bantering way, with no other interruptions on my part than the unimportant replies which politeness required of me. The turn of the expression, however, in her last question, or rather the one chance word, “adventure,” lightly as it fell from her lips, recalled my thoughts to my meeting with the woman in white, and urged me to discover the connection which the stranger’s own reference to Mrs. Fairlie informed me must once have existed between the nameless fugitive from the Asylum, and the former mistress of Limmeridge House.\n\n“Even if I were the most restless of mankind,” I said, “I should be in no danger of thirsting after adventures for some time to come. The very night before I arrived at this house, I met with an adventure; and the wonder and excitement of it, I can assure you, Miss Halcombe, will last me for the whole term of my stay in Cumberland, if not for a much longer period.”\n\n“You don’t say so, Mr. Hartright! May I hear it?”\n\n“You have a claim to hear it. The chief person in the adventure was a total stranger to me, and may perhaps be a total stranger to you; but she certainly mentioned the name of the late Mrs. Fairlie in terms of the sincerest gratitude and regard.”\n\n“Mentioned my mother’s name! You interest me indescribably. Pray go on.”\n\nI at once related the circumstances under which I had met the woman in white, exactly as they had occurred; and I repeated what she had said to me about Mrs. Fairlie and Limmeridge House, word for word.\n\nMiss Halcombe’s bright resolute eyes looked eagerly into mine, from the beginning of the narrative to the end. Her face expressed vivid interest and astonishment, but nothing more. She was evidently as far from knowing of any clue to the mystery as I was myself.\n\n“Are you quite sure of those words referring to my mother?” she asked.\n\n“Quite sure,” I replied. “Whoever she may be, the woman was once at school in the village of Limmeridge, was treated with especial kindness by Mrs. Fairlie, and, in grateful remembrance of that kindness, feels an affectionate interest in all surviving members of the family. She knew that Mrs. Fairlie and her husband were both dead; and she spoke of Miss Fairlie as if they had known each other when they were children.”\n\n“You said, I think, that she denied belonging to this place?”\n\n“Yes, she told me she came from Hampshire.”\n\n“And you entirely failed to find out her name?”\n\n“Entirely.”\n\n“Very strange. I think you were quite justified, Mr. Hartright, in giving the poor creature her liberty, for she seems to have done nothing in your presence to show herself unfit to enjoy it. But I wish you had been a little more resolute about finding out her name. We must really clear up this mystery, in some way. You had better not speak of it yet to Mr. Fairlie, or to my sister. They are both of them, I am certain, quite as ignorant of who the woman is, and of what her past history in connection with us can be, as I am myself. But they are also, in widely different ways, rather nervous and sensitive; and you would only fidget one and alarm the other to no purpose. As for myself, I am all aflame with curiosity, and I devote my whole energies to the business of discovery from this moment. When my mother came here, after her second marriage, she certainly established the village school just as it exists at the present time. But the old teachers are all dead, or gone elsewhere; and no enlightenment is to be hoped for from that quarter. The only other alternative I can think of——”\n\nAt this point we were interrupted by the entrance of the servant, with a message from Mr. Fairlie, intimating that he would be glad to see me, as soon as I had done breakfast.\n\n“Wait in the hall,” said Miss Halcombe, answering the servant for me, in her quick, ready way. “Mr. Hartright will come out directly. I was about to say,” she went on, addressing me again, “that my sister and I have a large collection of my mother’s letters, addressed to my father and to hers. In the absence of any other means of getting information, I will pass the morning in looking over my mother’s correspondence with Mr. Fairlie. He was fond of London, and was constantly away from his country home; and she was accustomed, at such times, to write and report to him how things went on at Limmeridge. Her letters are full of references to the school in which she took so strong an interest; and I think it more than likely that I may have discovered something when we meet again. The luncheon hour is two, Mr. Hartright. I shall have the pleasure of introducing you to my sister by that time, and we will occupy the afternoon in driving round the neighbourhood and showing you all our pet points of view. Till two o’clock, then, farewell.”\n\nShe nodded to me with the lively grace, the delightful refinement of familiarity, which characterised all that she did and all that she said; and disappeared by a door at the lower end of the room. As soon as she had left me, I turned my steps towards the hall, and followed the servant, on my way, for the first time, to the presence of Mr. Fairlie.\n\n# VII\n\nMy conductor led me upstairs into a passage which took us back to the bedchamber in which I had slept during the past night; and opening the door next to it, begged me to look in.\n\n“I have my master’s orders to show you your own sitting-room, sir,” said the man, “and to inquire if you approve of the situation and the light.”\n\nI must have been hard to please, indeed, if I had not approved of the room, and of everything about it. The bow-window looked out on the same lovely view which I had admired, in the morning, from my bedroom. The furniture was the perfection of luxury and beauty; the table in the centre was bright with gaily bound books, elegant conveniences for writing, and beautiful flowers; the second table, near the window, was covered with all the necessary materials for mounting water-colour drawings, and had a little easel attached to it, which I could expand or fold up at will; the walls were hung with gaily tinted chintz; and the floor was spread with Indian matting in maize-colour and red. It was the prettiest and most luxurious little sitting-room I had ever seen; and I admired it with the warmest enthusiasm.\n\nThe solemn servant was far too highly trained to betray the slightest satisfaction. He bowed with icy deference when my terms of eulogy were all exhausted, and silently opened the door for me to go out into the passage again.\n\nWe turned a corner, and entered a long second passage, ascended a short flight of stairs at the end, crossed a small circular upper hall, and stopped in front of a door covered with dark baize. The servant opened this door, and led me on a few yards to a second; opened that also, and disclosed two curtains of pale sea-green silk hanging before us; raised one of them noiselessly; softly uttered the words, “Mr. Hartright,” and left me.\n\nI found myself in a large, lofty room, with a magnificent carved ceiling, and with a carpet over the floor, so thick and soft that it felt like piles of velvet under my feet. One side of the room was occupied by a long book-case of some rare inlaid wood that was quite new to me. It was not more than six feet high, and the top was adorned with statuettes in marble, ranged at regular distances one from the other. On the opposite side stood two antique cabinets; and between them, and above them, hung a picture of the Virgin and Child, protected by glass, and bearing Raphael’s name on the gilt tablet at the bottom of the frame. On my right hand and on my left, as I stood inside the door, were chiffoniers and little stands in buhl and marquetterie, loaded with figures in Dresden china, with rare vases, ivory ornaments, and toys and curiosities that sparkled at all points with gold, silver, and precious stones. At the lower end of the room, opposite to me, the windows were concealed and the sunlight was tempered by large blinds of the same pale sea-green colour as the curtains over the door. The light thus produced was deliciously soft, mysterious, and subdued; it fell equally upon all the objects in the room; it helped to intensify the deep silence, and the air of profound seclusion that possessed the place; and it surrounded, with an appropriate halo of repose, the solitary figure of the master of the house, leaning back, listlessly composed, in a large easy-chair, with a reading-easel fastened on one of its arms, and a little table on the other.\n\nIf a man’s personal appearance, when he is out of his dressing-room, and when he has passed forty, can be accepted as a safe guide to his time of life—which is more than doubtful—Mr. Fairlie’s age, when I saw him, might have been reasonably computed at over fifty and under sixty years. His beardless face was thin, worn, and transparently pale, but not wrinkled; his nose was high and hooked; his eyes were of a dim greyish blue, large, prominent, and rather red round the rims of the eyelids; his hair was scanty, soft to look at, and of that light sandy colour which is the last to disclose its own changes towards grey. He was dressed in a dark frock-coat, of some substance much thinner than cloth, and in waistcoat and trousers of spotless white. His feet were effeminately small, and were clad in buff-coloured silk stockings, and little womanish bronze-leather slippers. Two rings adorned his white delicate hands, the value of which even my inexperienced observation detected to be all but priceless. Upon the whole, he had a frail, languidly-fretful, over-refined look—something singularly and unpleasantly delicate in its association with a man, and, at the same time, something which could by no possibility have looked natural and appropriate if it had been transferred to the personal appearance of a woman. My morning’s experience of Miss Halcombe had predisposed me to be pleased with everybody in the house; but my sympathies shut themselves up resolutely at the first sight of Mr. Fairlie.\n\nOn approaching nearer to him, I discovered that he was not so entirely without occupation as I had at first supposed. Placed amid the other rare and beautiful objects on a large round table near him, was a dwarf cabinet in ebony and silver, containing coins of all shapes and sizes, set out in little drawers lined with dark purple velvet. One of these drawers lay on the small table attached to his chair; and near it were some tiny jeweller’s brushes, a wash-leather “stump,” and a little bottle of liquid, all waiting to be used in various ways for the removal of any accidental impurities which might be discovered on the coins. His frail white fingers were listlessly toying with something which looked, to my uninstructed eyes, like a dirty pewter medal with ragged edges, when I advanced within a respectful distance of his chair, and stopped to make my bow.\n\n“So glad to possess you at Limmeridge, Mr. Hartright,” he said in a querulous, croaking voice, which combined, in anything but an agreeable manner, a discordantly high tone with a drowsily languid utterance. “Pray sit down. And don’t trouble yourself to move the chair, please. In the wretched state of my nerves, movement of any kind is exquisitely painful to me. Have you seen your studio? Will it do?”\n\n“I have just come from seeing the room, Mr. Fairlie; and I assure you——”\n\nHe stopped me in the middle of the sentence, by closing his eyes, and holding up one of his white hands imploringly. I paused in astonishment; and the croaking voice honoured me with this explanation—\n\n“Pray excuse me. But could you contrive to speak in a lower key? In the wretched state of my nerves, loud sound of any kind is indescribable torture to me. You will pardon an invalid? I only say to you what the lamentable state of my health obliges me to say to everybody. Yes. And you really like the room?”\n\n“I could wish for nothing prettier and nothing more comfortable,” I answered, dropping my voice, and beginning to discover already that Mr. Fairlie’s selfish affectation and Mr. Fairlie’s wretched nerves meant one and the same thing.\n\n“So glad. You will find your position here, Mr. Hartright, properly recognised. There is none of the horrid English barbarity of feeling about the social position of an artist in this house. So much of my early life has been passed abroad, that I have quite cast my insular skin in that respect. I wish I could say the same of the gentry—detestable word, but I suppose I must use it—of the gentry in the neighbourhood. They are sad Goths in Art, Mr. Hartright. People, I do assure you, who would have opened their eyes in astonishment, if they had seen Charles the Fifth pick up Titian’s brush for him. Do you mind putting this tray of coins back in the cabinet, and giving me the next one to it? In the wretched state of my nerves, exertion of any kind is unspeakably disagreeable to me. Yes. Thank you.”\n\nAs a practical commentary on the liberal social theory which he had just favoured me by illustrating, Mr. Fairlie’s cool request rather amused me. I put back one drawer and gave him the other, with all possible politeness. He began trifling with the new set of coins and the little brushes immediately; languidly looking at them and admiring them all the time he was speaking to me.\n\n“A thousand thanks and a thousand excuses. Do you like coins? Yes. So glad we have another taste in common besides our taste for Art. Now, about the pecuniary arrangements between us—do tell me—are they satisfactory?”\n\n“Most satisfactory, Mr. Fairlie.”\n\n“So glad. And—what next? Ah! I remember. Yes. In reference to the consideration which you are good enough to accept for giving me the benefit of your accomplishments in art, my steward will wait on you at the end of the first week, to ascertain your wishes. And—what next? Curious, is it not? I had a great deal more to say: and I appear to have quite forgotten it. Do you mind touching the bell? In that corner. Yes. Thank you.”\n\nI rang; and a new servant noiselessly made his appearance—a foreigner, with a set smile and perfectly brushed hair—a valet every inch of him.\n\n“Louis,” said Mr. Fairlie, dreamily dusting the tips of his fingers with one of the tiny brushes for the coins, “I made some entries in my tablettes this morning. Find my tablettes. A thousand pardons, Mr. Hartright, I’m afraid I bore you.”\n\nAs he wearily closed his eyes again, before I could answer, and as he did most assuredly bore me, I sat silent, and looked up at the Madonna and Child by Raphael. In the meantime, the valet left the room, and returned shortly with a little ivory book. Mr. Fairlie, after first relieving himself by a gentle sigh, let the book drop open with one hand, and held up the tiny brush with the other, as a sign to the servant to wait for further orders.\n\n“Yes. Just so!” said Mr. Fairlie, consulting the tablettes. “Louis, take down that portfolio.” He pointed, as he spoke, to several portfolios placed near the window, on mahogany stands. “No. Not the one with the green back—that contains my Rembrandt etchings, Mr. Hartright. Do you like etchings? Yes? So glad we have another taste in common. The portfolio with the red back, Louis. Don’t drop it! You have no idea of the tortures I should suffer, Mr. Hartright, if Louis dropped that portfolio. Is it safe on the chair? Do YOU think it safe, Mr. Hartright? Yes? So glad. Will you oblige me by looking at the drawings, if you really think they are quite safe. Louis, go away. What an ass you are. Don’t you see me holding the tablettes? Do you suppose I want to hold them? Then why not relieve me of the tablettes without being told? A thousand pardons, Mr. Hartright; servants are such asses, are they not? Do tell me—what do you think of the drawings? They have come from a sale in a shocking state—I thought they smelt of horrid dealers’ and brokers’ fingers when I looked at them last. CAN you undertake them?”\n\nAlthough my nerves were not delicate enough to detect the odour of plebeian fingers which had offended Mr. Fairlie’s nostrils, my taste was sufficiently educated to enable me to appreciate the value of the drawings, while I turned them over. They were, for the most part, really fine specimens of English water-colour art; and they had deserved much better treatment at the hands of their former possessor than they appeared to have received.\n\n“The drawings,” I answered, “require careful straining and mounting; and, in my opinion, they are well worth——”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” interposed Mr. Fairlie. “Do you mind my closing my eyes while you speak? Even this light is too much for them. Yes?”\n\n“I was about to say that the drawings are well worth all the time and trouble——”\n\nMr. Fairlie suddenly opened his eyes again, and rolled them with an expression of helpless alarm in the direction of the window.\n\n“I entreat you to excuse me, Mr. Hartright,” he said in a feeble flutter. “But surely I hear some horrid children in the garden—my private garden—below?”\n\n“I can’t say, Mr. Fairlie. I heard nothing myself.”\n\n“Oblige me—you have been so very good in humouring my poor nerves—oblige me by lifting up a corner of the blind. Don’t let the sun in on me, Mr. Hartright! Have you got the blind up? Yes? Then will you be so very kind as to look into the garden and make quite sure?”\n\nI complied with this new request. The garden was carefully walled in, all round. Not a human creature, large or small, appeared in any part of the sacred seclusion. I reported that gratifying fact to Mr. Fairlie.\n\n“A thousand thanks. My fancy, I suppose. There are no children, thank Heaven, in the house; but the servants (persons born without nerves) will encourage the children from the village. Such brats—oh, dear me, such brats! Shall I confess it, Mr. Hartright?—I sadly want a reform in the construction of children. Nature’s only idea seems to be to make them machines for the production of incessant noise. Surely our delightful Raffaello’s conception is infinitely preferable?”\n\nHe pointed to the picture of the Madonna, the upper part of which represented the conventional cherubs of Italian Art, celestially provided with sitting accommodation for their chins, on balloons of buff-coloured cloud.\n\n“Quite a model family!” said Mr. Fairlie, leering at the cherubs. “Such nice round faces, and such nice soft wings, and—nothing else. No dirty little legs to run about on, and no noisy little lungs to scream with. How immeasurably superior to the existing construction! I will close my eyes again, if you will allow me. And you really can manage the drawings? So glad. Is there anything else to settle? if there is, I think I have forgotten it. Shall we ring for Louis again?”\n\nBeing, by this time, quite as anxious, on my side, as Mr. Fairlie evidently was on his, to bring the interview to a speedy conclusion, I thought I would try to render the summoning of the servant unnecessary, by offering the requisite suggestion on my own responsibility.\n\n“The only point, Mr. Fairlie, that remains to be discussed,” I said, “refers, I think, to the instruction in sketching which I am engaged to communicate to the two young ladies.”\n\n“Ah! just so,” said Mr. Fairlie. “I wish I felt strong enough to go into that part of the arrangement—but I don’t. The ladies who profit by your kind services, Mr. Hartright, must settle, and decide, and so on, for themselves. My niece is fond of your charming art. She knows just enough about it to be conscious of her own sad defects. Please take pains with her. Yes. Is there anything else? No. We quite understand each other—don’t we? I have no right to detain you any longer from your delightful pursuit—have I? So pleasant to have settled everything—such a sensible relief to have done business. Do you mind ringing for Louis to carry the portfolio to your own room?”\n\n“I will carry it there myself, Mr. Fairlie, if you will allow me.”\n\n“Will you really? Are you strong enough? How nice to be so strong! Are you sure you won’t drop it? So glad to possess you at Limmeridge, Mr. Hartright. I am such a sufferer that I hardly dare hope to enjoy much of your society. Would you mind taking great pains not to let the doors bang, and not to drop the portfolio? Thank you. Gently with the curtains, please—the slightest noise from them goes through me like a knife. Yes. GOOD morning!”\n\nWhen the sea-green curtains were closed, and when the two baize doors were shut behind me, I stopped for a moment in the little circular hall beyond, and drew a long, luxurious breath of relief. It was like coming to the surface of the water after deep diving, to find myself once more on the outside of Mr. Fairlie’s room.\n\nAs soon as I was comfortably established for the morning in my pretty little studio, the first resolution at which I arrived was to turn my steps no more in the direction of the apartments occupied by the master of the house, except in the very improbable event of his honouring me with a special invitation to pay him another visit. Having settled this satisfactory plan of future conduct in reference to Mr. Fairlie, I soon recovered the serenity of temper of which my employer’s haughty familiarity and impudent politeness had, for the moment, deprived me. The remaining hours of the morning passed away pleasantly enough, in looking over the drawings, arranging them in sets, trimming their ragged edges, and accomplishing the other necessary preparations in anticipation of the business of mounting them. I ought, perhaps, to have made more progress than this; but, as the luncheon-time drew near, I grew restless and unsettled, and felt unable to fix my attention on work, even though that work was only of the humble manual kind.\n\nAt two o’clock I descended again to the breakfast-room, a little anxiously. Expectations of some interest were connected with my approaching reappearance in that part of the house. My introduction to Miss Fairlie was now close at hand; and, if Miss Halcombe’s search through her mother’s letters had produced the result which she anticipated, the time had come for clearing up the mystery of the woman in white.\n\n# VIII\n\nWhen I entered the room, I found Miss Halcombe and an elderly lady seated at the luncheon-table.\n\nThe elderly lady, when I was presented to her, proved to be Miss Fairlie’s former governess, Mrs. Vesey, who had been briefly described to me by my lively companion at the breakfast-table, as possessed of “all the cardinal virtues, and counting for nothing.” I can do little more than offer my humble testimony to the truthfulness of Miss Halcombe’s sketch of the old lady’s character. Mrs. Vesey looked the personification of human composure and female amiability. A calm enjoyment of a calm existence beamed in drowsy smiles on her plump, placid face. Some of us rush through life, and some of us saunter through life. Mrs. Vesey SAT through life. Sat in the house, early and late; sat in the garden; sat in unexpected window-seats in passages; sat (on a camp-stool) when her friends tried to take her out walking; sat before she looked at anything, before she talked of anything, before she answered Yes, or No, to the commonest question—always with the same serene smile on her lips, the same vacantly-attentive turn of the head, the same snugly-comfortable position of her hands and arms, under every possible change of domestic circumstances. A mild, a compliant, an unutterably tranquil and harmless old lady, who never by any chance suggested the idea that she had been actually alive since the hour of her birth. Nature has so much to do in this world, and is engaged in generating such a vast variety of co-existent productions, that she must surely be now and then too flurried and confused to distinguish between the different processes that she is carrying on at the same time. Starting from this point of view, it will always remain my private persuasion that Nature was absorbed in making cabbages when Mrs. Vesey was born, and that the good lady suffered the consequences of a vegetable preoccupation in the mind of the Mother of us all.\n\n“Now, Mrs. Vesey,” said Miss Halcombe, looking brighter, sharper, and readier than ever, by contrast with the undemonstrative old lady at her side, “what will you have? A cutlet?”\n\nMrs. Vesey crossed her dimpled hands on the edge of the table, smiled placidly, and said, “Yes, dear.”\n\n“What is that opposite Mr. Hartright? Boiled chicken, is it not? I thought you liked boiled chicken better than cutlet, Mrs. Vesey?”\n\nMrs. Vesey took her dimpled hands off the edge of the table and crossed them on her lap instead; nodded contemplatively at the boiled chicken, and said, “Yes, dear.”\n\n“Well, but which will you have, to-day? Shall Mr. Hartright give you some chicken? or shall I give you some cutlet?”\n\nMrs. Vesey put one of her dimpled hands back again on the edge of the table; hesitated drowsily, and said, “Which you please, dear.”\n\n“Mercy on me! it’s a question for your taste, my good lady, not for mine. Suppose you have a little of both? and suppose you begin with the chicken, because Mr. Hartright looks devoured by anxiety to carve for you.”\n\nMrs. Vesey put the other dimpled hand back on the edge of the table; brightened dimly one moment; went out again the next; bowed obediently, and said, “If you please, sir.”\n\nSurely a mild, a compliant, an unutterably tranquil and harmless old lady! But enough, perhaps, for the present, of Mrs. Vesey.\n\nAll this time, there were no signs of Miss Fairlie. We finished our luncheon; and still she never appeared. Miss Halcombe, whose quick eye nothing escaped, noticed the looks that I cast, from time to time, in the direction of the door.\n\n“I understand you, Mr. Hartright,” she said; “you are wondering what has become of your other pupil. She has been downstairs, and has got over her headache; but has not sufficiently recovered her appetite to join us at lunch. If you will put yourself under my charge, I think I can undertake to find her somewhere in the garden.”\n\nShe took up a parasol lying on a chair near her, and led the way out, by a long window at the bottom of the room, which opened on to the lawn. It is almost unnecessary to say that we left Mrs. Vesey still seated at the table, with her dimpled hands still crossed on the edge of it; apparently settled in that position for the rest of the afternoon.\n\nAs we crossed the lawn, Miss Halcombe looked at me significantly, and shook her head.\n\n“That mysterious adventure of yours,” she said, “still remains involved in its own appropriate midnight darkness. I have been all the morning looking over my mother’s letters, and I have made no discoveries yet. However, don’t despair, Mr. Hartright. This is a matter of curiosity; and you have got a woman for your ally. Under such conditions success is certain, sooner or later. The letters are not exhausted. I have three packets still left, and you may confidently rely on my spending the whole evening over them.”\n\nHere, then, was one of my anticipations of the morning still unfulfilled. I began to wonder, next, whether my introduction to Miss Fairlie would disappoint the expectations that I had been forming of her since breakfast-time.\n\n“And how did you get on with Mr. Fairlie?” inquired Miss Halcombe, as we left the lawn and turned into a shrubbery. “Was he particularly nervous this morning? Never mind considering about your answer, Mr. Hartright. The mere fact of your being obliged to consider is enough for me. I see in your face that he WAS particularly nervous; and, as I am amiably unwilling to throw you into the same condition, I ask no more.”\n\nWe turned off into a winding path while she was speaking, and approached a pretty summer-house, built of wood, in the form of a miniature Swiss chalet. The one room of the summer-house, as we ascended the steps of the door, was occupied by a young lady. She was standing near a rustic table, looking out at the inland view of moor and hill presented by a gap in the trees, and absently turning over the leaves of a little sketch-book that lay at her side. This was Miss Fairlie.\n\nHow can I describe her? How can I separate her from my own sensations, and from all that has happened in the later time? How can I see her again as she looked when my eyes first rested on her—as she should look, now, to the eyes that are about to see her in these pages?\n\nThe water-colour drawing that I made of Laura Fairlie, at an after period, in the place and attitude in which I first saw her, lies on my desk while I write. I look at it, and there dawns upon me brightly, from the dark greenish-brown background of the summer-house, a light, youthful figure, clothed in a simple muslin dress, the pattern of it formed by broad alternate stripes of delicate blue and white. A scarf of the same material sits crisply and closely round her shoulders, and a little straw hat of the natural colour, plainly and sparingly trimmed with ribbon to match the gown, covers her head, and throws its soft pearly shadow over the upper part of her face. Her hair is of so faint and pale a brown—not flaxen, and yet almost as light; not golden, and yet almost as glossy—that it nearly melts, here and there, into the shadow of the hat. It is plainly parted and drawn back over her ears, and the line of it ripples naturally as it crosses her forehead. The eyebrows are rather darker than the hair; and the eyes are of that soft, limpid, turquoise blue, so often sung by the poets, so seldom seen in real life. Lovely eyes in colour, lovely eyes in form—large and tender and quietly thoughtful—but beautiful above all things in the clear truthfulness of look that dwells in their inmost depths, and shines through all their changes of expression with the light of a purer and a better world. The charm—most gently and yet most distinctly expressed—which they shed over the whole face, so covers and transforms its little natural human blemishes elsewhere, that it is difficult to estimate the relative merits and defects of the other features. It is hard to see that the lower part of the face is too delicately refined away towards the chin to be in full and fair proportion with the upper part; that the nose, in escaping the aquiline bend (always hard and cruel in a woman, no matter how abstractedly perfect it may be), has erred a little in the other extreme, and has missed the ideal straightness of line; and that the sweet, sensitive lips are subject to a slight nervous contraction, when she smiles, which draws them upward a little at one corner, towards the cheek. It might be possible to note these blemishes in another woman’s face but it is not easy to dwell on them in hers, so subtly are they connected with all that is individual and characteristic in her expression, and so closely does the expression depend for its full play and life, in every other feature, on the moving impulse of the eyes.\n\nDoes my poor portrait of her, my fond, patient labour of long and happy days, show me these things? Ah, how few of them are in the dim mechanical drawing, and how many in the mind with which I regard it! A fair, delicate girl, in a pretty light dress, trifling with the leaves of a sketch-book, while she looks up from it with truthful, innocent blue eyes—that is all the drawing can say; all, perhaps, that even the deeper reach of thought and pen can say in their language, either. The woman who first gives life, light, and form to our shadowy conceptions of beauty, fills a void in our spiritual nature that has remained unknown to us till she appeared. Sympathies that lie too deep for words, too deep almost for thoughts, are touched, at such times, by other charms than those which the senses feel and which the resources of expression can realise. The mystery which underlies the beauty of women is never raised above the reach of all expression until it has claimed kindred with the deeper mystery in our own souls. Then, and then only, has it passed beyond the narrow region on which light falls, in this world, from the pencil and the pen.\n\nThink of her as you thought of the first woman who quickened the pulses within you that the rest of her sex had no art to stir. Let the kind, candid blue eyes meet yours, as they met mine, with the one matchless look which we both remember so well. Let her voice speak the music that you once loved best, attuned as sweetly to your ear as to mine. Let her footstep, as she comes and goes, in these pages, be like that other footstep to whose airy fall your own heart once beat time. Take her as the visionary nursling of your own fancy; and she will grow upon you, all the more clearly, as the living woman who dwells in mine.\n\nAmong the sensations that crowded on me, when my eyes first looked upon her—familiar sensations which we all know, which spring to life in most of our hearts, die again in so many, and renew their bright existence in so few—there was one that troubled and perplexed me: one that seemed strangely inconsistent and unaccountably out of place in Miss Fairlie’s presence.\n\nMingling with the vivid impression produced by the charm of her fair face and head, her sweet expression, and her winning simplicity of manner, was another impression, which, in a shadowy way, suggested to me the idea of something wanting. At one time it seemed like something wanting in HER: at another, like something wanting in myself, which hindered me from understanding her as I ought. The impression was always strongest in the most contradictory manner, when she looked at me; or, in other words, when I was most conscious of the harmony and charm of her face, and yet, at the same time, most troubled by the sense of an incompleteness which it was impossible to discover. Something wanting, something wanting—and where it was, and what it was, I could not say.\n\nThe effect of this curious caprice of fancy (as I thought it then) was not of a nature to set me at my ease, during a first interview with Miss Fairlie. The few kind words of welcome which she spoke found me hardly self-possessed enough to thank her in the customary phrases of reply. Observing my hesitation, and no doubt attributing it, naturally enough, to some momentary shyness on my part, Miss Halcombe took the business of talking, as easily and readily as usual, into her own hands.\n\n“Look there, Mr. Hartright,” she said, pointing to the sketch-book on the table, and to the little delicate wandering hand that was still trifling with it. “Surely you will acknowledge that your model pupil is found at last? The moment she hears that you are in the house, she seizes her inestimable sketch-book, looks universal Nature straight in the face, and longs to begin!”\n\nMiss Fairlie laughed with a ready good-humour, which broke out as brightly as if it had been part of the sunshine above us, over her lovely face.\n\n“I must not take credit to myself where no credit is due,” she said, her clear, truthful blue eyes looking alternately at Miss Halcombe and at me. “Fond as I am of drawing, I am so conscious of my own ignorance that I am more afraid than anxious to begin. Now I know you are here, Mr. Hartright, I find myself looking over my sketches, as I used to look over my lessons when I was a little girl, and when I was sadly afraid that I should turn out not fit to be heard.”\n\nShe made the confession very prettily and simply, and, with quaint, childish earnestness, drew the sketch-book away close to her own side of the table. Miss Halcombe cut the knot of the little embarrassment forthwith, in her resolute, downright way.\n\n“Good, bad, or indifferent,” she said, “the pupil’s sketches must pass through the fiery ordeal of the master’s judgment—and there’s an end of it. Suppose we take them with us in the carriage, Laura, and let Mr. Hartright see them, for the first time, under circumstances of perpetual jolting and interruption? If we can only confuse him all through the drive, between Nature as it is, when he looks up at the view, and Nature as it is not when he looks down again at our sketch-books, we shall drive him into the last desperate refuge of paying us compliments, and shall slip through his professional fingers with our pet feathers of vanity all unruffled.”\n\n“I hope Mr. Hartright will pay ME no compliments,” said Miss Fairlie, as we all left the summer-house.\n\n“May I venture to inquire why you express that hope?” I asked.\n\n“Because I shall believe all that you say to me,” she answered simply.\n\nIn those few words she unconsciously gave me the key to her whole character: to that generous trust in others which, in her nature, grew innocently out of the sense of her own truth. I only knew it intuitively then. I know it by experience now.\n\nWe merely waited to rouse good Mrs. Vesey from the place which she still occupied at the deserted luncheon-table, before we entered the open carriage for our promised drive. The old lady and Miss Halcombe occupied the back seat, and Miss Fairlie and I sat together in front, with the sketch-book open between us, fairly exhibited at last to my professional eyes. All serious criticism on the drawings, even if I had been disposed to volunteer it, was rendered impossible by Miss Halcombe’s lively resolution to see nothing but the ridiculous side of the Fine Arts, as practised by herself, her sister, and ladies in general. I can remember the conversation that passed far more easily than the sketches that I mechanically looked over. That part of the talk, especially, in which Miss Fairlie took any share, is still as vividly impressed on my memory as if I had heard it only a few hours ago.\n\nYes! let me acknowledge that on this first day I let the charm of her presence lure me from the recollection of myself and my position. The most trifling of the questions that she put to me, on the subject of using her pencil and mixing her colours; the slightest alterations of expression in the lovely eyes that looked into mine with such an earnest desire to learn all that I could teach, and to discover all that I could show, attracted more of my attention than the finest view we passed through, or the grandest changes of light and shade, as they flowed into each other over the waving moorland and the level beach. At any time, and under any circumstances of human interest, is it not strange to see how little real hold the objects of the natural world amid which we live can gain on our hearts and minds? We go to Nature for comfort in trouble, and sympathy in joy, only in books. Admiration of those beauties of the inanimate world, which modern poetry so largely and so eloquently describes, is not, even in the best of us, one of the original instincts of our nature. As children, we none of us possess it. No uninstructed man or woman possesses it. Those whose lives are most exclusively passed amid the ever-changing wonders of sea and land are also those who are most universally insensible to every aspect of Nature not directly associated with the human interest of their calling. Our capacity of appreciating the beauties of the earth we live on is, in truth, one of the civilised accomplishments which we all learn as an Art; and, more, that very capacity is rarely practised by any of us except when our minds are most indolent and most unoccupied. How much share have the attractions of Nature ever had in the pleasurable or painful interests and emotions of ourselves or our friends? What space do they ever occupy in the thousand little narratives of personal experience which pass every day by word of mouth from one of us to the other? All that our minds can compass, all that our hearts can learn, can be accomplished with equal certainty, equal profit, and equal satisfaction to ourselves, in the poorest as in the richest prospect that the face of the earth can show. There is surely a reason for this want of inborn sympathy between the creature and the creation around it, a reason which may perhaps be found in the widely-differing destinies of man and his earthly sphere. The grandest mountain prospect that the eye can range over is appointed to annihilation. The smallest human interest that the pure heart can feel is appointed to immortality.\n\nWe had been out nearly three hours, when the carriage again passed through the gates of Limmeridge House.\n\nOn our way back I had let the ladies settle for themselves the first point of view which they were to sketch, under my instructions, on the afternoon of the next day. When they withdrew to dress for dinner, and when I was alone again in my little sitting-room, my spirits seemed to leave me on a sudden. I felt ill at ease and dissatisfied with myself, I hardly knew why. Perhaps I was now conscious for the first time of having enjoyed our drive too much in the character of a guest, and too little in the character of a drawing-master. Perhaps that strange sense of something wanting, either in Miss Fairlie or in myself, which had perplexed me when I was first introduced to her, haunted me still. Anyhow, it was a relief to my spirits when the dinner-hour called me out of my solitude, and took me back to the society of the ladies of the house.\n\nI was struck, on entering the drawing-room, by the curious contrast, rather in material than in colour, of the dresses which they now wore. While Mrs. Vesey and Miss Halcombe were richly clad (each in the manner most becoming to her age), the first in silver-grey, and the second in that delicate primrose-yellow colour which matches so well with a dark complexion and black hair, Miss Fairlie was unpretendingly and almost poorly dressed in plain white muslin. It was spotlessly pure: it was beautifully put on; but still it was the sort of dress which the wife or daughter of a poor man might have worn, and it made her, so far as externals went, look less affluent in circumstances than her own governess. At a later period, when I learnt to know more of Miss Fairlie’s character, I discovered that this curious contrast, on the wrong side, was due to her natural delicacy of feeling and natural intensity of aversion to the slightest personal display of her own wealth. Neither Mrs. Vesey nor Miss Halcombe could ever induce her to let the advantage in dress desert the two ladies who were poor, to lean to the side of the one lady who was rich.\n\nWhen the dinner was over we returned together to the drawing-room. Although Mr. Fairlie (emulating the magnificent condescension of the monarch who had picked up Titian’s brush for him) had instructed his butler to consult my wishes in relation to the wine that I might prefer after dinner, I was resolute enough to resist the temptation of sitting in solitary grandeur among bottles of my own choosing, and sensible enough to ask the ladies’ permission to leave the table with them habitually, on the civilised foreign plan, during the period of my residence at Limmeridge House.\n\nThe drawing-room, to which we had now withdrawn for the rest of the evening, was on the ground-floor, and was of the same shape and size as the breakfast-room. Large glass doors at the lower end opened on to a terrace, beautifully ornamented along its whole length with a profusion of flowers. The soft, hazy twilight was just shading leaf and blossom alike into harmony with its own sober hues as we entered the room, and the sweet evening scent of the flowers met us with its fragrant welcome through the open glass doors. Good Mrs. Vesey (always the first of the party to sit down) took possession of an arm-chair in a corner, and dozed off comfortably to sleep. At my request Miss Fairlie placed herself at the piano. As I followed her to a seat near the instrument, I saw Miss Halcombe retire into a recess of one of the side windows, to proceed with the search through her mother’s letters by the last quiet rays of the evening light.\n\nHow vividly that peaceful home-picture of the drawing-room comes back to me while I write! From the place where I sat I could see Miss Halcombe’s graceful figure, half of it in soft light, half in mysterious shadow, bending intently over the letters in her lap; while, nearer to me, the fair profile of the player at the piano was just delicately defined against the faintly-deepening background of the inner wall of the room. Outside, on the terrace, the clustering flowers and long grasses and creepers waved so gently in the light evening air, that the sound of their rustling never reached us. The sky was without a cloud, and the dawning mystery of moonlight began to tremble already in the region of the eastern heaven. The sense of peace and seclusion soothed all thought and feeling into a rapt, unearthly repose; and the balmy quiet, that deepened ever with the deepening light, seemed to hover over us with a gentler influence still, when there stole upon it from the piano the heavenly tenderness of the music of Mozart. It was an evening of sights and sounds never to forget.\n\nWe all sat silent in the places we had chosen—Mrs. Vesey still sleeping, Miss Fairlie still playing, Miss Halcombe still reading—till the light failed us. By this time the moon had stolen round to the terrace, and soft, mysterious rays of light were slanting already across the lower end of the room. The change from the twilight obscurity was so beautiful that we banished the lamps, by common consent, when the servant brought them in, and kept the large room unlighted, except by the glimmer of the two candles at the piano.\n\nFor half an hour more the music still went on. After that the beauty of the moonlight view on the terrace tempted Miss Fairlie out to look at it, and I followed her. When the candles at the piano had been lighted Miss Halcombe had changed her place, so as to continue her examination of the letters by their assistance. We left her, on a low chair, at one side of the instrument, so absorbed over her reading that she did not seem to notice when we moved.\n\nWe had been out on the terrace together, just in front of the glass doors, hardly so long as five minutes, I should think; and Miss Fairlie was, by my advice, just tying her white handkerchief over her head as a precaution against the night air—when I heard Miss Halcombe’s voice—low, eager, and altered from its natural lively tone—pronounce my name.\n\n“Mr. Hartright,” she said, “will you come here for a minute? I want to speak to you.”\n\nI entered the room again immediately. The piano stood about half-way down along the inner wall. On the side of the instrument farthest from the terrace Miss Halcombe was sitting with the letters scattered on her lap, and with one in her hand selected from them, and held close to the candle. On the side nearest to the terrace there stood a low ottoman, on which I took my place. In this position I was not far from the glass doors, and I could see Miss Fairlie plainly, as she passed and repassed the opening on to the terrace, walking slowly from end to end of it in the full radiance of the moon.\n\n“I want you to listen while I read the concluding passages in this letter,” said Miss Halcombe. “Tell me if you think they throw any light upon your strange adventure on the road to London. The letter is addressed by my mother to her second husband, Mr. Fairlie, and the date refers to a period of between eleven and twelve years since. At that time Mr. and Mrs. Fairlie, and my half-sister Laura, had been living for years in this house; and I was away from them completing my education at a school in Paris.”\n\nShe looked and spoke earnestly, and, as I thought, a little uneasily as well. At the moment when she raised the letter to the candle before beginning to read it, Miss Fairlie passed us on the terrace, looked in for a moment, and seeing that we were engaged, slowly walked on.\n\nMiss Halcombe began to read as follows:—\n\n“‘You will be tired, my dear Philip, of hearing perpetually about my schools and my scholars. Lay the blame, pray, on the dull uniformity of life at Limmeridge, and not on me. Besides, this time I have something really interesting to tell you about a new scholar.\n\n“‘You know old Mrs. Kempe at the village shop. Well, after years of ailing, the doctor has at last given her up, and she is dying slowly day by day. Her only living relation, a sister, arrived last week to take care of her. This sister comes all the way from Hampshire—her name is Mrs. Catherick. Four days ago Mrs. Catherick came here to see me, and brought her only child with her, a sweet little girl about a year older than our darling Laura——’”\n\nAs the last sentence fell from the reader’s lips, Miss Fairlie passed us on the terrace once more. She was softly singing to herself one of the melodies which she had been playing earlier in the evening. Miss Halcombe waited till she had passed out of sight again, and then went on with the letter—\n\n“‘Mrs. Catherick is a decent, well-behaved, respectable woman; middle-aged, and with the remains of having been moderately, only moderately, nice-looking. There is something in her manner and in her appearance, however, which I can’t make out. She is reserved about herself to the point of downright secrecy, and there is a look in her face—I can’t describe it—which suggests to me that she has something on her mind. She is altogether what you would call a walking mystery. Her errand at Limmeridge House, however, was simple enough. When she left Hampshire to nurse her sister, Mrs. Kempe, through her last illness, she had been obliged to bring her daughter with her, through having no one at home to take care of the little girl. Mrs. Kempe may die in a week’s time, or may linger on for months; and Mrs. Catherick’s object was to ask me to let her daughter, Anne, have the benefit of attending my school, subject to the condition of her being removed from it to go home again with her mother, after Mrs. Kempe’s death. I consented at once, and when Laura and I went out for our walk, we took the little girl (who is just eleven years old) to the school that very day.’”\n\nOnce more Miss Fairlie’s figure, bright and soft in its snowy muslin dress—her face prettily framed by the white folds of the handkerchief which she had tied under her chin—passed by us in the moonlight. Once more Miss Halcombe waited till she was out of sight, and then went on—\n\n“‘I have taken a violent fancy, Philip, to my new scholar, for a reason which I mean to keep till the last for the sake of surprising you. Her mother having told me as little about the child as she told me of herself, I was left to discover (which I did on the first day when we tried her at lessons) that the poor little thing’s intellect is not developed as it ought to be at her age. Seeing this I had her up to the house the next day, and privately arranged with the doctor to come and watch her and question her, and tell me what he thought. His opinion is that she will grow out of it. But he says her careful bringing-up at school is a matter of great importance just now, because her unusual slowness in acquiring ideas implies an unusual tenacity in keeping them, when they are once received into her mind. Now, my love, you must not imagine, in your off-hand way, that I have been attaching myself to an idiot. This poor little Anne Catherick is a sweet, affectionate, grateful girl, and says the quaintest, prettiest things (as you shall judge by an instance), in the most oddly sudden, surprised, half-frightened way. Although she is dressed very neatly, her clothes show a sad want of taste in colour and pattern. So I arranged, yesterday, that some of our darling Laura’s old white frocks and white hats should be altered for Anne Catherick, explaining to her that little girls of her complexion looked neater and better all in white than in anything else. She hesitated and seemed puzzled for a minute, then flushed up, and appeared to understand. Her little hand clasped mine suddenly. She kissed it, Philip, and said (oh, so earnestly!), “I will always wear white as long as I live. It will help me to remember you, ma’am, and to think that I am pleasing you still, when I go away and see you no more.” This is only one specimen of the quaint things she says so prettily. Poor little soul! She shall have a stock of white frocks, made with good deep tucks, to let out for her as she grows——’”\n\nMiss Halcombe paused, and looked at me across the piano.\n\n“Did the forlorn woman whom you met in the high-road seem young?” she asked. “Young enough to be two- or three-and-twenty?”\n\n“Yes, Miss Halcombe, as young as that.”\n\n“And she was strangely dressed, from head to foot, all in white?”\n\n“All in white.”\n\nWhile the answer was passing my lips Miss Fairlie glided into view on the terrace for the third time. Instead of proceeding on her walk, she stopped, with her back turned towards us, and, leaning on the balustrade of the terrace, looked down into the garden beyond. My eyes fixed upon the white gleam of her muslin gown and head-dress in the moonlight, and a sensation, for which I can find no name—a sensation that quickened my pulse, and raised a fluttering at my heart—began to steal over me.\n\n“All in white?” Miss Halcombe repeated. “The most important sentences in the letter, Mr. Hartright, are those at the end, which I will read to you immediately. But I can’t help dwelling a little upon the coincidence of the white costume of the woman you met, and the white frocks which produced that strange answer from my mother’s little scholar. The doctor may have been wrong when he discovered the child’s defects of intellect, and predicted that she would ‘grow out of them.’ She may never have grown out of them, and the old grateful fancy about dressing in white, which was a serious feeling to the girl, may be a serious feeling to the woman still.”\n\nI said a few words in answer—I hardly know what. All my attention was concentrated on the white gleam of Miss Fairlie’s muslin dress.\n\n“Listen to the last sentences of the letter,” said Miss Halcombe. “I think they will surprise you.”\n\nAs she raised the letter to the light of the candle, Miss Fairlie turned from the balustrade, looked doubtfully up and down the terrace, advanced a step towards the glass doors, and then stopped, facing us.\n\nMeanwhile Miss Halcombe read me the last sentences to which she had referred—\n\n“‘And now, my love, seeing that I am at the end of my paper, now for the real reason, the surprising reason, for my fondness for little Anne Catherick. My dear Philip, although she is not half so pretty, she is, nevertheless, by one of those extraordinary caprices of accidental resemblance which one sometimes sees, the living likeness, in her hair, her complexion, the colour of her eyes, and the shape of her face——’”\n\nI started up from the ottoman before Miss Halcombe could pronounce the next words. A thrill of the same feeling which ran through me when the touch was laid upon my shoulder on the lonely high-road chilled me again.\n\nThere stood Miss Fairlie, a white figure, alone in the moonlight; in her attitude, in the turn of her head, in her complexion, in the shape of her face, the living image, at that distance and under those circumstances, of the woman in white! The doubt which had troubled my mind for hours and hours past flashed into conviction in an instant. That “something wanting” was my own recognition of the ominous likeness between the fugitive from the asylum and my pupil at Limmeridge House.\n\n“You see it!” said Miss Halcombe. She dropped the useless letter, and her eyes flashed as they met mine. “You see it now, as my mother saw it eleven years since!”\n\n“I see it—more unwillingly than I can say. To associate that forlorn, friendless, lost woman, even by an accidental likeness only, with Miss Fairlie, seems like casting a shadow on the future of the bright creature who stands looking at us now. Let me lose the impression again as soon as possible. Call her in, out of the dreary moonlight—pray call her in!”\n\n“Mr. Hartright, you surprise me. Whatever women may be, I thought that men, in the nineteenth century, were above superstition.”\n\n“Pray call her in!”\n\n“Hush, hush! She is coming of her own accord. Say nothing in her presence. Let this discovery of the likeness be kept a secret between you and me. Come in, Laura, come in, and wake Mrs. Vesey with the piano. Mr. Hartright is petitioning for some more music, and he wants it, this time, of the lightest and liveliest kind.”\n\nSo ended my eventful first day at Limmeridge House.\n\nMiss Halcombe and I kept our secret. After the discovery of the likeness no fresh light seemed destined to break over the mystery of the woman in white. At the first safe opportunity Miss Halcombe cautiously led her half-sister to speak of their mother, of old times, and of Anne Catherick. Miss Fairlie’s recollections of the little scholar at Limmeridge were, however, only of the most vague and general kind. She remembered the likeness between herself and her mother’s favourite pupil, as something which had been supposed to exist in past times; but she did not refer to the gift of the white dresses, or to the singular form of words in which the child had artlessly expressed her gratitude for them. She remembered that Anne had remained at Limmeridge for a few months only, and had then left it to go back to her home in Hampshire; but she could not say whether the mother and daughter had ever returned, or had ever been heard of afterwards. No further search, on Miss Halcombe’s part, through the few letters of Mrs. Fairlie’s writing which she had left unread, assisted in clearing up the uncertainties still left to perplex us. We had identified the unhappy woman whom I had met in the night-time with Anne Catherick—we had made some advance, at least, towards connecting the probably defective condition of the poor creature’s intellect with the peculiarity of her being dressed all in white, and with the continuance, in her maturer years, of her childish gratitude towards Mrs. Fairlie—and there, so far as we knew at that time, our discoveries had ended.\n\nThe days passed on, the weeks passed on, and the track of the golden autumn wound its bright way visibly through the green summer of the trees. Peaceful, fast-flowing, happy time! my story glides by you now as swiftly as you once glided by me. Of all the treasures of enjoyment that you poured so freely into my heart, how much is left me that has purpose and value enough to be written on this page? Nothing but the saddest of all confessions that a man can make—the confession of his own folly.\n\nThe secret which that confession discloses should be told with little effort, for it has indirectly escaped me already. The poor weak words, which have failed to describe Miss Fairlie, have succeeded in betraying the sensations she awakened in me. It is so with us all. Our words are giants when they do us an injury, and dwarfs when they do us a service.\n\nI loved her.\n\nAh! how well I know all the sadness and all the mockery that is contained in those three words. I can sigh over my mournful confession with the tenderest woman who reads it and pities me. I can laugh at it as bitterly as the hardest man who tosses it from him in contempt. I loved her! Feel for me, or despise me, I confess it with the same immovable resolution to own the truth.\n\nWas there no excuse for me? There was some excuse to be found, surely, in the conditions under which my term of hired service was passed at Limmeridge House.\n\nMy morning hours succeeded each other calmly in the quiet and seclusion of my own room. I had just work enough to do, in mounting my employer’s drawings, to keep my hands and eyes pleasurably employed, while my mind was left free to enjoy the dangerous luxury of its own unbridled thoughts. A perilous solitude, for it lasted long enough to enervate, not long enough to fortify me. A perilous solitude, for it was followed by afternoons and evenings spent, day after day and week after week alone in the society of two women, one of whom possessed all the accomplishments of grace, wit, and high-breeding, the other all the charms of beauty, gentleness, and simple truth, that can purify and subdue the heart of man. Not a day passed, in that dangerous intimacy of teacher and pupil, in which my hand was not close to Miss Fairlie’s; my cheek, as we bent together over her sketch-book, almost touching hers. The more attentively she watched every movement of my brush, the more closely I was breathing the perfume of her hair, and the warm fragrance of her breath. It was part of my service to live in the very light of her eyes—at one time to be bending over her, so close to her bosom as to tremble at the thought of touching it; at another, to feel her bending over me, bending so close to see what I was about, that her voice sank low when she spoke to me, and her ribbons brushed my cheek in the wind before she could draw them back.\n\nThe evenings which followed the sketching excursions of the afternoon varied, rather than checked, these innocent, these inevitable familiarities. My natural fondness for the music which she played with such tender feeling, such delicate womanly taste, and her natural enjoyment of giving me back, by the practice of her art, the pleasure which I had offered to her by the practice of mine, only wove another tie which drew us closer and closer to one another. The accidents of conversation; the simple habits which regulated even such a little thing as the position of our places at table; the play of Miss Halcombe’s ever-ready raillery, always directed against my anxiety as teacher, while it sparkled over her enthusiasm as pupil; the harmless expression of poor Mrs. Vesey’s drowsy approval, which connected Miss Fairlie and me as two model young people who never disturbed her—every one of these trifles, and many more, combined to fold us together in the same domestic atmosphere, and to lead us both insensibly to the same hopeless end.\n\nI should have remembered my position, and have put myself secretly on my guard. I did so, but not till it was too late. All the discretion, all the experience, which had availed me with other women, and secured me against other temptations, failed me with her. It had been my profession, for years past, to be in this close contact with young girls of all ages, and of all orders of beauty. I had accepted the position as part of my calling in life; I had trained myself to leave all the sympathies natural to my age in my employer’s outer hall, as coolly as I left my umbrella there before I went upstairs. I had long since learnt to understand, composedly and as a matter of course, that my situation in life was considered a guarantee against any of my female pupils feeling more than the most ordinary interest in me, and that I was admitted among beautiful and captivating women much as a harmless domestic animal is admitted among them. This guardian experience I had gained early; this guardian experience had sternly and strictly guided me straight along my own poor narrow path, without once letting me stray aside, to the right hand or to the left. And now I and my trusty talisman were parted for the first time. Yes, my hardly-earned self-control was as completely lost to me as if I had never possessed it; lost to me, as it is lost every day to other men, in other critical situations, where women are concerned. I know, now, that I should have questioned myself from the first. I should have asked why any room in the house was better than home to me when she entered it, and barren as a desert when she went out again—why I always noticed and remembered the little changes in her dress that I had noticed and remembered in no other woman’s before—why I saw her, heard her, and touched her (when we shook hands at night and morning) as I had never seen, heard, and touched any other woman in my life? I should have looked into my own heart, and found this new growth springing up there, and plucked it out while it was young. Why was this easiest, simplest work of self-culture always too much for me? The explanation has been written already in the three words that were many enough, and plain enough, for my confession. I loved her.\n\nThe days passed, the weeks passed; it was approaching the third month of my stay in Cumberland. The delicious monotony of life in our calm seclusion flowed on with me, like a smooth stream with a swimmer who glides down the current. All memory of the past, all thought of the future, all sense of the falseness and hopelessness of my own position, lay hushed within me into deceitful rest. Lulled by the Syren-song that my own heart sung to me, with eyes shut to all sight, and ears closed to all sound of danger, I drifted nearer and nearer to the fatal rocks. The warning that aroused me at last, and startled me into sudden, self-accusing consciousness of my own weakness, was the plainest, the truest, the kindest of all warnings, for it came silently from HER.\n\nWe had parted one night as usual. No word had fallen from my lips, at that time or at any time before it, that could betray me, or startle her into sudden knowledge of the truth. But when we met again in the morning, a change had come over her—a change that told me all.\n\nI shrank then—I shrink still—from invading the innermost sanctuary of her heart, and laying it open to others, as I have laid open my own. Let it be enough to say that the time when she first surprised my secret was, I firmly believe, the time when she first surprised her own, and the time, also, when she changed towards me in the interval of one night. Her nature, too truthful to deceive others, was too noble to deceive itself. When the doubt that I had hushed asleep first laid its weary weight on her heart, the true face owned all, and said, in its own frank, simple language—I am sorry for him; I am sorry for myself.\n\nIt said this, and more, which I could not then interpret. I understood but too well the change in her manner, to greater kindness and quicker readiness in interpreting all my wishes, before others—to constraint and sadness, and nervous anxiety to absorb herself in the first occupation she could seize on, whenever we happened to be left together alone. I understood why the sweet sensitive lips smiled so rarely and so restrainedly now, and why the clear blue eyes looked at me, sometimes with the pity of an angel, sometimes with the innocent perplexity of a child. But the change meant more than this. There was a coldness in her hand, there was an unnatural immobility in her face, there was in all her movements the mute expression of constant fear and clinging self-reproach. The sensations that I could trace to herself and to me, the unacknowledged sensations that we were feeling in common, were not these. There were certain elements of the change in her that were still secretly drawing us together, and others that were, as secretly, beginning to drive us apart.\n\nIn my doubt and perplexity, in my vague suspicion of something hidden which I was left to find by my own unaided efforts, I examined Miss Halcombe’s looks and manner for enlightenment. Living in such intimacy as ours, no serious alteration could take place in any one of us which did not sympathetically affect the others. The change in Miss Fairlie was reflected in her half-sister. Although not a word escaped Miss Halcombe which hinted at an altered state of feeling towards myself, her penetrating eyes had contracted a new habit of always watching me. Sometimes the look was like suppressed anger, sometimes like suppressed dread, sometimes like neither—like nothing, in short, which I could understand. A week elapsed, leaving us all three still in this position of secret constraint towards one another. My situation, aggravated by the sense of my own miserable weakness and forgetfulness of myself, now too late awakened in me, was becoming intolerable. I felt that I must cast off the oppression under which I was living, at once and for ever—yet how to act for the best, or what to say first, was more than I could tell.\n\nFrom this position of helplessness and humiliation I was rescued by Miss Halcombe. Her lips told me the bitter, the necessary, the unexpected truth; her hearty kindness sustained me under the shock of hearing it; her sense and courage turned to its right use an event which threatened the worst that could happen, to me and to others, in Limmeridge House.\n\n# X\n\nIt was on a Thursday in the week, and nearly at the end of the third month of my sojourn in Cumberland.\n\nIn the morning, when I went down into the breakfast-room at the usual hour, Miss Halcombe, for the first time since I had known her, was absent from her customary place at the table.\n\nMiss Fairlie was out on the lawn. She bowed to me, but did not come in. Not a word had dropped from my lips, or from hers, that could unsettle either of us—and yet the same unacknowledged sense of embarrassment made us shrink alike from meeting one another alone. She waited on the lawn, and I waited in the breakfast-room, till Mrs. Vesey or Miss Halcombe came in. How quickly I should have joined her: how readily we should have shaken hands, and glided into our customary talk, only a fortnight ago.\n\nIn a few minutes Miss Halcombe entered. She had a preoccupied look, and she made her apologies for being late rather absently.\n\n“I have been detained,” she said, “by a consultation with Mr. Fairlie on a domestic matter which he wished to speak to me about.”\n\nMiss Fairlie came in from the garden, and the usual morning greeting passed between us. Her hand struck colder to mine than ever. She did not look at me, and she was very pale. Even Mrs. Vesey noticed it when she entered the room a moment after.\n\n“I suppose it is the change in the wind,” said the old lady. “The winter is coming—ah, my love, the winter is coming soon!”\n\nIn her heart and in mine it had come already!\n\nOur morning meal—once so full of pleasant good-humoured discussion of the plans for the day—was short and silent. Miss Fairlie seemed to feel the oppression of the long pauses in the conversation, and looked appealingly to her sister to fill them up. Miss Halcombe, after once or twice hesitating and checking herself, in a most uncharacteristic manner, spoke at last.\n\n“I have seen your uncle this morning, Laura,” she said. “He thinks the purple room is the one that ought to be got ready, and he confirms what I told you. Monday is the day—not Tuesday.”\n\nWhile these words were being spoken Miss Fairlie looked down at the table beneath her. Her fingers moved nervously among the crumbs that were scattered on the cloth. The paleness on her cheeks spread to her lips, and the lips themselves trembled visibly. I was not the only person present who noticed this. Miss Halcombe saw it, too, and at once set us the example of rising from table.\n\nMrs. Vesey and Miss Fairlie left the room together. The kind sorrowful blue eyes looked at me, for a moment, with the prescient sadness of a coming and a long farewell. I felt the answering pang in my own heart—the pang that told me I must lose her soon, and love her the more unchangeably for the loss.\n\nI turned towards the garden when the door had closed on her. Miss Halcombe was standing with her hat in her hand, and her shawl over her arm, by the large window that led out to the lawn, and was looking at me attentively.\n\n“Have you any leisure time to spare,” she asked, “before you begin to work in your own room?”\n\n“Certainly, Miss Halcombe. I have always time at your service.”\n\n“I want to say a word to you in private, Mr. Hartright. Get your hat and come out into the garden. We are not likely to be disturbed there at this hour in the morning.”\n\nAs we stepped out on to the lawn, one of the under-gardeners—a mere lad—passed us on his way to the house, with a letter in his hand. Miss Halcombe stopped him.\n\n“Is that letter for me?” she asked.\n\n“Nay, miss; it’s just said to be for Miss Fairlie,” answered the lad, holding out the letter as he spoke.\n\nMiss Halcombe took it from him and looked at the address.\n\n“A strange handwriting,” she said to herself. “Who can Laura’s correspondent be? Where did you get this?” she continued, addressing the gardener.\n\n“Well, miss,” said the lad, “I just got it from a woman.”\n\n“What woman?”\n\n“A woman well stricken in age.”\n\n“Oh, an old woman. Any one you knew?”\n\n“I canna’ tak’ it on mysel’ to say that she was other than a stranger to me.”\n\n“Which way did she go?”\n\n“That gate,” said the under-gardener, turning with great deliberation towards the south, and embracing the whole of that part of England with one comprehensive sweep of his arm.\n\n“Curious,” said Miss Halcombe; “I suppose it must be a begging-letter. There,” she added, handing the letter back to the lad, “take it to the house, and give it to one of the servants. And now, Mr. Hartright, if you have no objection, let us walk this way.”\n\nShe led me across the lawn, along the same path by which I had followed her on the day after my arrival at Limmeridge.\n\nAt the little summer-house, in which Laura Fairlie and I had first seen each other, she stopped, and broke the silence which she had steadily maintained while we were walking together.\n\n“What I have to say to you I can say here.”\n\nWith those words she entered the summer-house, took one of the chairs at the little round table inside, and signed to me to take the other. I suspected what was coming when she spoke to me in the breakfast-room; I felt certain of it now.\n\n“Mr. Hartright,” she said, “I am going to begin by making a frank avowal to you. I am going to say—without phrase-making, which I detest, or paying compliments, which I heartily despise—that I have come, in the course of your residence with us, to feel a strong friendly regard for you. I was predisposed in your favour when you first told me of your conduct towards that unhappy woman whom you met under such remarkable circumstances. Your management of the affair might not have been prudent, but it showed the self-control, the delicacy, and the compassion of a man who was naturally a gentleman. It made me expect good things from you, and you have not disappointed my expectations.”\n\nShe paused—but held up her hand at the same time, as a sign that she awaited no answer from me before she proceeded. When I entered the summer-house, no thought was in me of the woman in white. But now, Miss Halcombe’s own words had put the memory of my adventure back in my mind. It remained there throughout the interview—remained, and not without a result.\n\n“As your friend,” she proceeded, “I am going to tell you, at once, in my own plain, blunt, downright language, that I have discovered your secret—without help or hint, mind, from any one else. Mr. Hartright, you have thoughtlessly allowed yourself to form an attachment—a serious and devoted attachment I am afraid—to my sister Laura. I don’t put you to the pain of confessing it in so many words, because I see and know that you are too honest to deny it. I don’t even blame you—I pity you for opening your heart to a hopeless affection. You have not attempted to take any underhand advantage—you have not spoken to my sister in secret. You are guilty of weakness and want of attention to your own best interests, but of nothing worse. If you had acted, in any single respect, less delicately and less modestly, I should have told you to leave the house without an instant’s notice, or an instant’s consultation of anybody. As it is, I blame the misfortune of your years and your position—I don’t blame YOU. Shake hands—I have given you pain; I am going to give you more, but there is no help for it—shake hands with your friend, Marian Halcombe, first.”\n\nThe sudden kindness—the warm, high-minded, fearless sympathy which met me on such mercifully equal terms, which appealed with such delicate and generous abruptness straight to my heart, my honour, and my courage, overcame me in an instant. I tried to look at her when she took my hand, but my eyes were dim. I tried to thank her, but my voice failed me.\n\n“Listen to me,” she said, considerately avoiding all notice of my loss of self-control. “Listen to me, and let us get it over at once. It is a real true relief to me that I am not obliged, in what I have now to say, to enter into the question—the hard and cruel question as I think it—of social inequalities. Circumstances which will try you to the quick, spare me the ungracious necessity of paining a man who has lived in friendly intimacy under the same roof with myself by any humiliating reference to matters of rank and station. You must leave Limmeridge House, Mr. Hartright, before more harm is done. It is my duty to say that to you; and it would be equally my duty to say it, under precisely the same serious necessity, if you were the representative of the oldest and wealthiest family in England. You must leave us, not because you are a teacher of drawing——”\n\nShe waited a moment, turned her face full on me, and reaching across the table, laid her hand firmly on my arm.\n\n“Not because you are a teacher of drawing,” she repeated, “but because Laura Fairlie is engaged to be married.”\n\nThe last word went like a bullet to my heart. My arm lost all sensation of the hand that grasped it. I never moved and never spoke. The sharp autumn breeze that scattered the dead leaves at our feet came as cold to me, on a sudden, as if my own mad hopes were dead leaves too, whirled away by the wind like the rest. Hopes! Betrothed, or not betrothed, she was equally far from me. Would other men have remembered that in my place? Not if they had loved her as I did.\n\nThe pang passed, and nothing but the dull numbing pain of it remained. I felt Miss Halcombe’s hand again, tightening its hold on my arm—I raised my head and looked at her. Her large black eyes were rooted on me, watching the white change on my face, which I felt, and which she saw.\n\n“Crush it!” she said. “Here, where you first saw her, crush it! Don’t shrink under it like a woman. Tear it out; trample it under foot like a man!”\n\nThe suppressed vehemence with which she spoke, the strength which her will—concentrated in the look she fixed on me, and in the hold on my arm that she had not yet relinquished—communicated to mine, steadied me. We both waited for a minute in silence. At the end of that time I had justified her generous faith in my manhood—I had, outwardly at least, recovered my self-control.\n\n“Are you yourself again?”\n\n“Enough myself, Miss Halcombe, to ask your pardon and hers. Enough myself to be guided by your advice, and to prove my gratitude in that way, if I can prove it in no other.”\n\n“You have proved it already,” she answered, “by those words. Mr. Hartright, concealment is at an end between us. I cannot affect to hide from you what my sister has unconsciously shown to me. You must leave us for her sake, as well as for your own. Your presence here, your necessary intimacy with us, harmless as it has been, God knows, in all other respects, has unsteadied her and made her wretched. I, who love her better than my own life—I, who have learnt to believe in that pure, noble, innocent nature as I believe in my religion—know but too well the secret misery of self-reproach that she has been suffering since the first shadow of a feeling disloyal to her marriage engagement entered her heart in spite of her. I don’t say—it would be useless to attempt to say it after what has happened—that her engagement has ever had a strong hold on her affections. It is an engagement of honour, not of love; her father sanctioned it on his deathbed, two years since; she herself neither welcomed it nor shrank from it—she was content to make it. Till you came here she was in the position of hundreds of other women, who marry men without being greatly attracted to them or greatly repelled by them, and who learn to love them (when they don’t learn to hate!) after marriage, instead of before. I hope more earnestly than words can say—and you should have the self-sacrificing courage to hope too—that the new thoughts and feelings which have disturbed the old calmness and the old content have not taken root too deeply to be ever removed. Your absence (if I had less belief in your honour, and your courage, and your sense, I should not trust to them as I am trusting now) your absence will help my efforts, and time will help us all three. It is something to know that my first confidence in you was not all misplaced. It is something to know that you will not be less honest, less manly, less considerate towards the pupil whose relation to yourself you have had the misfortune to forget, than towards the stranger and the outcast whose appeal to you was not made in vain.”\n\nAgain the chance reference to the woman in white! Was there no possibility of speaking of Miss Fairlie and of me without raising the memory of Anne Catherick, and setting her between us like a fatality that it was hopeless to avoid?\n\n“Tell me what apology I can make to Mr. Fairlie for breaking my engagement,” I said. “Tell me when to go after that apology is accepted. I promise implicit obedience to you and to your advice.”\n\n“Time is every way of importance,” she answered. “You heard me refer this morning to Monday next, and to the necessity of setting the purple room in order. The visitor whom we expect on Monday——”\n\nI could not wait for her to be more explicit. Knowing what I knew now, the memory of Miss Fairlie’s look and manner at the breakfast-table told me that the expected visitor at Limmeridge House was her future husband. I tried to force it back; but something rose within me at that moment stronger than my own will, and I interrupted Miss Halcombe.\n\n“Let me go to-day,” I said bitterly. “The sooner the better.”\n\n“No, not to-day,” she replied. “The only reason you can assign to Mr. Fairlie for your departure, before the end of your engagement, must be that an unforeseen necessity compels you to ask his permission to return at once to London. You must wait till to-morrow to tell him that, at the time when the post comes in, because he will then understand the sudden change in your plans, by associating it with the arrival of a letter from London. It is miserable and sickening to descend to deceit, even of the most harmless kind—but I know Mr. Fairlie, and if you once excite his suspicions that you are trifling with him, he will refuse to release you. Speak to him on Friday morning: occupy yourself afterwards (for the sake of your own interests with your employer) in leaving your unfinished work in as little confusion as possible, and quit this place on Saturday. It will be time enough then, Mr. Hartright, for you, and for all of us.”\n\nBefore I could assure her that she might depend on my acting in the strictest accordance with her wishes, we were both startled by advancing footsteps in the shrubbery. Some one was coming from the house to seek for us! I felt the blood rush into my cheeks and then leave them again. Could the third person who was fast approaching us, at such a time and under such circumstances, be Miss Fairlie?\n\nIt was a relief—so sadly, so hopelessly was my position towards her changed already—it was absolutely a relief to me, when the person who had disturbed us appeared at the entrance of the summer-house, and proved to be only Miss Fairlie’s maid.\n\n“Could I speak to you for a moment, miss?” said the girl, in rather a flurried, unsettled manner.\n\nMiss Halcombe descended the steps into the shrubbery, and walked aside a few paces with the maid.\n\nLeft by myself, my mind reverted, with a sense of forlorn wretchedness which it is not in any words that I can find to describe, to my approaching return to the solitude and the despair of my lonely London home. Thoughts of my kind old mother, and of my sister, who had rejoiced with her so innocently over my prospects in Cumberland—thoughts whose long banishment from my heart it was now my shame and my reproach to realise for the first time—came back to me with the loving mournfulness of old, neglected friends. My mother and my sister, what would they feel when I returned to them from my broken engagement, with the confession of my miserable secret—they who had parted from me so hopefully on that last happy night in the Hampstead cottage!\n\nAnne Catherick again! Even the memory of the farewell evening with my mother and my sister could not return to me now unconnected with that other memory of the moonlight walk back to London. What did it mean? Were that woman and I to meet once more? It was possible, at the least. Did she know that I lived in London? Yes; I had told her so, either before or after that strange question of hers, when she had asked me so distrustfully if I knew many men of the rank of Baronet. Either before or after—my mind was not calm enough, then, to remember which.\n\nA few minutes elapsed before Miss Halcombe dismissed the maid and came back to me. She, too, looked flurried and unsettled now.\n\n“We have arranged all that is necessary, Mr. Hartright,” she said. “We have understood each other, as friends should, and we may go back at once to the house. To tell you the truth, I am uneasy about Laura. She has sent to say she wants to see me directly, and the maid reports that her mistress is apparently very much agitated by a letter that she has received this morning—the same letter, no doubt, which I sent on to the house before we came here.”\n\nWe retraced our steps together hastily along the shrubbery path. Although Miss Halcombe had ended all that she thought it necessary to say on her side, I had not ended all that I wanted to say on mine. From the moment when I had discovered that the expected visitor at Limmeridge was Miss Fairlie’s future husband, I had felt a bitter curiosity, a burning envious eagerness, to know who he was. It was possible that a future opportunity of putting the question might not easily offer, so I risked asking it on our way back to the house.\n\n“Now that you are kind enough to tell me we have understood each other, Miss Halcombe,” I said, “now that you are sure of my gratitude for your forbearance and my obedience to your wishes, may I venture to ask who”—(I hesitated—I had forced myself to think of him, but it was harder still to speak of him, as her promised husband)—“who the gentleman engaged to Miss Fairlie is?”\n\nHer mind was evidently occupied with the message she had received from her sister. She answered in a hasty, absent way—\n\n“A gentleman of large property in Hampshire.”\n\nHampshire! Anne Catherick’s native place. Again, and yet again, the woman in white. There WAS a fatality in it.\n\n“And his name?” I said, as quietly and indifferently as I could.\n\n“Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nSIR—Sir Percival! Anne Catherick’s question—that suspicious question about the men of the rank of Baronet whom I might happen to know—had hardly been dismissed from my mind by Miss Halcombe’s return to me in the summer-house, before it was recalled again by her own answer. I stopped suddenly, and looked at her.\n\n“Sir Percival Glyde,” she repeated, imagining that I had not heard her former reply.\n\n“Knight, or Baronet?” I asked, with an agitation that I could hide no longer.\n\nShe paused for a moment, and then answered, rather coldly—\n\n“Baronet, of course.”\n\n# XI\n\nNot a word more was said, on either side, as we walked back to the house. Miss Halcombe hastened immediately to her sister’s room, and I withdrew to my studio to set in order all of Mr. Fairlie’s drawings that I had not yet mounted and restored before I resigned them to the care of other hands. Thoughts that I had hitherto restrained, thoughts that made my position harder than ever to endure, crowded on me now that I was alone.\n\nShe was engaged to be married, and her future husband was Sir Percival Glyde. A man of the rank of Baronet, and the owner of property in Hampshire.\n\nThere were hundreds of baronets in England, and dozens of landowners in Hampshire. Judging by the ordinary rules of evidence, I had not the shadow of a reason, thus far, for connecting Sir Percival Glyde with the suspicious words of inquiry that had been spoken to me by the woman in white. And yet, I did connect him with them. Was it because he had now become associated in my mind with Miss Fairlie, Miss Fairlie being, in her turn, associated with Anne Catherick, since the night when I had discovered the ominous likeness between them? Had the events of the morning so unnerved me already that I was at the mercy of any delusion which common chances and common coincidences might suggest to my imagination? Impossible to say. I could only feel that what had passed between Miss Halcombe and myself, on our way from the summer-house, had affected me very strangely. The foreboding of some undiscoverable danger lying hid from us all in the darkness of the future was strong on me. The doubt whether I was not linked already to a chain of events which even my approaching departure from Cumberland would be powerless to snap asunder—the doubt whether we any of us saw the end as the end would really be—gathered more and more darkly over my mind. Poignant as it was, the sense of suffering caused by the miserable end of my brief, presumptuous love seemed to be blunted and deadened by the still stronger sense of something obscurely impending, something invisibly threatening, that Time was holding over our heads.\n\nI had been engaged with the drawings little more than half an hour, when there was a knock at the door. It opened, on my answering; and, to my surprise, Miss Halcombe entered the room.\n\nHer manner was angry and agitated. She caught up a chair for herself before I could give her one, and sat down in it, close at my side.\n\n“Mr. Hartright,” she said, “I had hoped that all painful subjects of conversation were exhausted between us, for to-day at least. But it is not to be so. There is some underhand villainy at work to frighten my sister about her approaching marriage. You saw me send the gardener on to the house, with a letter addressed, in a strange handwriting, to Miss Fairlie?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“The letter is an anonymous letter—a vile attempt to injure Sir Percival Glyde in my sister’s estimation. It has so agitated and alarmed her that I have had the greatest possible difficulty in composing her spirits sufficiently to allow me to leave her room and come here. I know this is a family matter on which I ought not to consult you, and in which you can feel no concern or interest——”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe. I feel the strongest possible concern and interest in anything that affects Miss Fairlie’s happiness or yours.”\n\n“I am glad to hear you say so. You are the only person in the house, or out of it, who can advise me. Mr. Fairlie, in his state of health and with his horror of difficulties and mysteries of all kinds, is not to be thought of. The clergyman is a good, weak man, who knows nothing out of the routine of his duties; and our neighbours are just the sort of comfortable, jog-trot acquaintances whom one cannot disturb in times of trouble and danger. What I want to know is this: ought I at once to take such steps as I can to discover the writer of the letter? or ought I to wait, and apply to Mr. Fairlie’s legal adviser to-morrow? It is a question—perhaps a very important one—of gaining or losing a day. Tell me what you think, Mr. Hartright. If necessity had not already obliged me to take you into my confidence under very delicate circumstances, even my helpless situation would, perhaps, be no excuse for me. But as things are I cannot surely be wrong, after all that has passed between us, in forgetting that you are a friend of only three months’ standing.”\n\nShe gave me the letter. It began abruptly, without any preliminary form of address, as follows—\n\n“Do you believe in dreams? I hope, for your own sake, that you do. See what Scripture says about dreams and their fulfilment (Genesis xl. 8, xli. 25; Daniel iv. 18-25), and take the warning I send you before it is too late.\n\n“Last night I dreamed about you, Miss Fairlie. I dreamed that I was standing inside the communion rails of a church—I on one side of the altar-table, and the clergyman, with his surplice and his prayer-book, on the other.\n\n“After a time there walked towards us, down the aisle of the church, a man and a woman, coming to be married. You were the woman. You looked so pretty and innocent in your beautiful white silk dress, and your long white lace veil, that my heart felt for you, and the tears came into my eyes.\n\n“They were tears of pity, young lady, that heaven blesses and instead of falling from my eyes like the everyday tears that we all of us shed, they turned into two rays of light which slanted nearer and nearer to the man standing at the altar with you, till they touched his breast. The two rays sprang in arches like two rainbows between me and him. I looked along them, and I saw down into his inmost heart.\n\n“The outside of the man you were marrying was fair enough to see. He was neither tall nor short—he was a little below the middle size. A light, active, high-spirited man—about five-and-forty years old, to look at. He had a pale face, and was bald over the forehead, but had dark hair on the rest of his head. His beard was shaven on his chin, but was let to grow, of a fine rich brown, on his cheeks and his upper lip. His eyes were brown too, and very bright; his nose straight and handsome and delicate enough to have done for a woman’s. His hands the same. He was troubled from time to time with a dry hacking cough, and when he put up his white right hand to his mouth, he showed the red scar of an old wound across the back of it. Have I dreamt of the right man? You know best, Miss Fairlie; and you can say if I was deceived or not. Read next, what I saw beneath the outside—I entreat you, read, and profit.\n\n“I looked along the two rays of light, and I saw down into his inmost heart. It was black as night, and on it were written, in the red flaming letters which are the handwriting of the fallen angel, ‘Without pity and without remorse. He has strewn with misery the paths of others, and he will live to strew with misery the path of this woman by his side.’ I read that, and then the rays of light shifted and pointed over his shoulder; and there, behind him, stood a fiend laughing. And the rays of light shifted once more, and pointed over your shoulder; and there behind you, stood an angel weeping. And the rays of light shifted for the third time, and pointed straight between you and that man. They widened and widened, thrusting you both asunder, one from the other. And the clergyman looked for the marriage-service in vain: it was gone out of the book, and he shut up the leaves, and put it from him in despair. And I woke with my eyes full of tears and my heart beating—for I believe in dreams.\n\n“Believe too, Miss Fairlie—I beg of you, for your own sake, believe as I do. Joseph and Daniel, and others in Scripture, believed in dreams. Inquire into the past life of that man with the scar on his hand, before you say the words that make you his miserable wife. I don’t give you this warning on my account, but on yours. I have an interest in your well-being that will live as long as I draw breath. Your mother’s daughter has a tender place in my heart—for your mother was my first, my best, my only friend.”\n\nThere the extraordinary letter ended, without signature of any sort.\n\nThe handwriting afforded no prospect of a clue. It was traced on ruled lines, in the cramped, conventional, copy-book character technically termed “small hand.” It was feeble and faint, and defaced by blots, but had otherwise nothing to distinguish it.\n\n“That is not an illiterate letter,” said Miss Halcombe, “and at the same time, it is surely too incoherent to be the letter of an educated person in the higher ranks of life. The reference to the bridal dress and veil, and other little expressions, seem to point to it as the production of some woman. What do you think, Mr. Hartright?”\n\n“I think so too. It seems to me to be not only the letter of a woman, but of a woman whose mind must be——”\n\n“Deranged?” suggested Miss Halcombe. “It struck me in that light too.”\n\nI did not answer. While I was speaking, my eyes rested on the last sentence of the letter: “Your mother’s daughter has a tender place in my heart—for your mother was my first, my best, my only friend.” Those words and the doubt which had just escaped me as to the sanity of the writer of the letter, acting together on my mind, suggested an idea, which I was literally afraid to express openly, or even to encourage secretly. I began to doubt whether my own faculties were not in danger of losing their balance. It seemed almost like a monomania to be tracing back everything strange that happened, everything unexpected that was said, always to the same hidden source and the same sinister influence. I resolved, this time, in defence of my own courage and my own sense, to come to no decision that plain fact did not warrant, and to turn my back resolutely on everything that tempted me in the shape of surmise.\n\n“If we have any chance of tracing the person who has written this,” I said, returning the letter to Miss Halcombe, “there can be no harm in seizing our opportunity the moment it offers. I think we ought to speak to the gardener again about the elderly woman who gave him the letter, and then to continue our inquiries in the village. But first let me ask a question. You mentioned just now the alternative of consulting Mr. Fairlie’s legal adviser to-morrow. Is there no possibility of communicating with him earlier? Why not to-day?”\n\n“I can only explain,” replied Miss Halcombe, “by entering into certain particulars, connected with my sister’s marriage-engagement, which I did not think it necessary or desirable to mention to you this morning. One of Sir Percival Glyde’s objects in coming here on Monday, is to fix the period of his marriage, which has hitherto been left quite unsettled. He is anxious that the event should take place before the end of the year.”\n\n“Does Miss Fairlie know of that wish?” I asked eagerly.\n\n“She has no suspicion of it, and after what has happened, I shall not take the responsibility upon myself of enlightening her. Sir Percival has only mentioned his views to Mr. Fairlie, who has told me himself that he is ready and anxious, as Laura’s guardian, to forward them. He has written to London, to the family solicitor, Mr. Gilmore. Mr. Gilmore happens to be away in Glasgow on business, and he has replied by proposing to stop at Limmeridge House on his way back to town. He will arrive to-morrow, and will stay with us a few days, so as to allow Sir Percival time to plead his own cause. If he succeeds, Mr. Gilmore will then return to London, taking with him his instructions for my sister’s marriage-settlement. You understand now, Mr. Hartright, why I speak of waiting to take legal advice until to-morrow? Mr. Gilmore is the old and tried friend of two generations of Fairlies, and we can trust him, as we could trust no one else.”\n\nThe marriage-settlement! The mere hearing of those two words stung me with a jealous despair that was poison to my higher and better instincts. I began to think—it is hard to confess this, but I must suppress nothing from beginning to end of the terrible story that I now stand committed to reveal—I began to think, with a hateful eagerness of hope, of the vague charges against Sir Percival Glyde which the anonymous letter contained. What if those wild accusations rested on a foundation of truth? What if their truth could be proved before the fatal words of consent were spoken, and the marriage-settlement was drawn? I have tried to think since, that the feeling which then animated me began and ended in pure devotion to Miss Fairlie’s interests, but I have never succeeded in deceiving myself into believing it, and I must not now attempt to deceive others. The feeling began and ended in reckless, vindictive, hopeless hatred of the man who was to marry her.\n\n“If we are to find out anything,” I said, speaking under the new influence which was now directing me, “we had better not let another minute slip by us unemployed. I can only suggest, once more, the propriety of questioning the gardener a second time, and of inquiring in the village immediately afterwards.”\n\n“I think I may be of help to you in both cases,” said Miss Halcombe, rising. “Let us go, Mr. Hartright, at once, and do the best we can together.”\n\nI had the door in my hand to open it for her—but I stopped, on a sudden, to ask an important question before we set forth.\n\n“One of the paragraphs of the anonymous letter,” I said, “contains some sentences of minute personal description. Sir Percival Glyde’s name is not mentioned, I know—but does that description at all resemble him?”\n\n“Accurately—even in stating his age to be forty-five——”\n\nForty-five; and she was not yet twenty-one! Men of his age married wives of her age every day—and experience had shown those marriages to be often the happiest ones. I knew that—and yet even the mention of his age, when I contrasted it with hers, added to my blind hatred and distrust of him.\n\n“Accurately,” Miss Halcombe continued, “even to the scar on his right hand, which is the scar of a wound that he received years since when he was travelling in Italy. There can be no doubt that every peculiarity of his personal appearance is thoroughly well known to the writer of the letter.”\n\n“Even a cough that he is troubled with is mentioned, if I remember right?”\n\n“Yes, and mentioned correctly. He treats it lightly himself, though it sometimes makes his friends anxious about him.”\n\n“I suppose no whispers have ever been heard against his character?”\n\n“Mr. Hartright! I hope you are not unjust enough to let that infamous letter influence you?”\n\nI felt the blood rush into my cheeks, for I knew that it HAD influenced me.\n\n“I hope not,” I answered confusedly. “Perhaps I had no right to ask the question.”\n\n“I am not sorry you asked it,” she said, “for it enables me to do justice to Sir Percival’s reputation. Not a whisper, Mr. Hartright, has ever reached me, or my family, against him. He has fought successfully two contested elections, and has come out of the ordeal unscathed. A man who can do that, in England, is a man whose character is established.”\n\nI opened the door for her in silence, and followed her out. She had not convinced me. If the recording angel had come down from heaven to confirm her, and had opened his book to my mortal eyes, the recording angel would not have convinced me.\n\nWe found the gardener at work as usual. No amount of questioning could extract a single answer of any importance from the lad’s impenetrable stupidity. The woman who had given him the letter was an elderly woman; she had not spoken a word to him, and she had gone away towards the south in a great hurry. That was all the gardener could tell us.\n\nThe village lay southward of the house. So to the village we went next.\n\n# XII\n\nOur inquiries at Limmeridge were patiently pursued in all directions, and among all sorts and conditions of people. But nothing came of them. Three of the villagers did certainly assure us that they had seen the woman, but as they were quite unable to describe her, and quite incapable of agreeing about the exact direction in which she was proceeding when they last saw her, these three bright exceptions to the general rule of total ignorance afforded no more real assistance to us than the mass of their unhelpful and unobservant neighbours.\n\nThe course of our useless investigations brought us, in time, to the end of the village at which the schools established by Mrs. Fairlie were situated. As we passed the side of the building appropriated to the use of the boys, I suggested the propriety of making a last inquiry of the schoolmaster, whom we might presume to be, in virtue of his office, the most intelligent man in the place.\n\n“I am afraid the schoolmaster must have been occupied with his scholars,” said Miss Halcombe, “just at the time when the woman passed through the village and returned again. However, we can but try.”\n\nWe entered the playground enclosure, and walked by the schoolroom window to get round to the door, which was situated at the back of the building. I stopped for a moment at the window and looked in.\n\nThe schoolmaster was sitting at his high desk, with his back to me, apparently haranguing the pupils, who were all gathered together in front of him, with one exception. The one exception was a sturdy white-headed boy, standing apart from all the rest on a stool in a corner—a forlorn little Crusoe, isolated in his own desert island of solitary penal disgrace.\n\nThe door, when we got round to it, was ajar, and the school-master’s voice reached us plainly, as we both stopped for a minute under the porch.\n\n“Now, boys,” said the voice, “mind what I tell you. If I hear another word spoken about ghosts in this school, it will be the worse for all of you. There are no such things as ghosts, and therefore any boy who believes in ghosts believes in what can’t possibly be; and a boy who belongs to Limmeridge School, and believes in what can’t possibly be, sets up his back against reason and discipline, and must be punished accordingly. You all see Jacob Postlethwaite standing up on the stool there in disgrace. He has been punished, not because he said he saw a ghost last night, but because he is too impudent and too obstinate to listen to reason, and because he persists in saying he saw the ghost after I have told him that no such thing can possibly be. If nothing else will do, I mean to cane the ghost out of Jacob Postlethwaite, and if the thing spreads among any of the rest of you, I mean to go a step farther, and cane the ghost out of the whole school.”\n\n“We seem to have chosen an awkward moment for our visit,” said Miss Halcombe, pushing open the door at the end of the schoolmaster’s address, and leading the way in.\n\nOur appearance produced a strong sensation among the boys. They appeared to think that we had arrived for the express purpose of seeing Jacob Postlethwaite caned.\n\n“Go home all of you to dinner,” said the schoolmaster, “except Jacob. Jacob must stop where he is; and the ghost may bring him his dinner, if the ghost pleases.”\n\nJacob’s fortitude deserted him at the double disappearance of his schoolfellows and his prospect of dinner. He took his hands out of his pockets, looked hard at his knuckles, raised them with great deliberation to his eyes, and when they got there, ground them round and round slowly, accompanying the action by short spasms of sniffing, which followed each other at regular intervals—the nasal minute guns of juvenile distress.\n\n“We came here to ask you a question, Mr. Dempster,” said Miss Halcombe, addressing the schoolmaster; “and we little expected to find you occupied in exorcising a ghost. What does it all mean? What has really happened?”\n\n“That wicked boy has been frightening the whole school, Miss Halcombe, by declaring that he saw a ghost yesterday evening,” answered the master; “and he still persists in his absurd story, in spite of all that I can say to him.”\n\n“Most extraordinary,” said Miss Halcombe, “I should not have thought it possible that any of the boys had imagination enough to see a ghost. This is a new accession indeed to the hard labour of forming the youthful mind at Limmeridge, and I heartily wish you well through it, Mr. Dempster. In the meantime, let me explain why you see me here, and what it is I want.”\n\nShe then put the same question to the schoolmaster which we had asked already of almost every one else in the village. It was met by the same discouraging answer. Mr. Dempster had not set eyes on the stranger of whom we were in search.\n\n“We may as well return to the house, Mr. Hartright,” said Miss Halcombe; “the information we want is evidently not to be found.”\n\nShe had bowed to Mr. Dempster, and was about to leave the schoolroom, when the forlorn position of Jacob Postlethwaite, piteously sniffing on the stool of penitence, attracted her attention as she passed him, and made her stop good-humouredly to speak a word to the little prisoner before she opened the door.\n\n“You foolish boy,” she said, “why don’t you beg Mr. Dempster’s pardon, and hold your tongue about the ghost?”\n\n“Eh!—but I saw t’ ghaist,” persisted Jacob Postlethwaite, with a stare of terror and a burst of tears.\n\n“Stuff and nonsense! You saw nothing of the kind. Ghost indeed! What ghost——”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe,” interposed the schoolmaster a little uneasily—“but I think you had better not question the boy. The obstinate folly of his story is beyond all belief; and you might lead him into ignorantly——”\n\n“Ignorantly what?” inquired Miss Halcombe sharply.\n\n“Ignorantly shocking your feelings,” said Mr. Dempster, looking very much discomposed.\n\n“Upon my word, Mr. Dempster, you pay my feelings a great compliment in thinking them weak enough to be shocked by such an urchin as that!” She turned with an air of satirical defiance to little Jacob, and began to question him directly. “Come!” she said, “I mean to know all about this. You naughty boy, when did you see the ghost?”\n\n“Yestere’en, at the gloaming,” replied Jacob.\n\n“Oh! you saw it yesterday evening, in the twilight? And what was it like?”\n\n“Arl in white—as a ghaist should be,” answered the ghost-seer, with a confidence beyond his years.\n\n“And where was it?”\n\n“Away yander, in t’ kirkyard—where a ghaist ought to be.”\n\n“As a ‘ghaist’ should be—where a ‘ghaist’ ought to be—why, you little fool, you talk as if the manners and customs of ghosts had been familiar to you from your infancy! You have got your story at your fingers’ ends, at any rate. I suppose I shall hear next that you can actually tell me whose ghost it was?”\n\n“Eh! but I just can,” replied Jacob, nodding his head with an air of gloomy triumph.\n\nMr. Dempster had already tried several times to speak while Miss Halcombe was examining his pupil, and he now interposed resolutely enough to make himself heard.\n\n“Excuse me, Miss Halcombe,” he said, “if I venture to say that you are only encouraging the boy by asking him these questions.”\n\n“I will merely ask one more, Mr. Dempster, and then I shall be quite satisfied. Well,” she continued, turning to the boy, “and whose ghost was it?”\n\n“T’ ghaist of Mistress Fairlie,” answered Jacob in a whisper.\n\nThe effect which this extraordinary reply produced on Miss Halcombe fully justified the anxiety which the schoolmaster had shown to prevent her from hearing it. Her face crimsoned with indignation—she turned upon little Jacob with an angry suddenness which terrified him into a fresh burst of tears—opened her lips to speak to him—then controlled herself, and addressed the master instead of the boy.\n\n“It is useless,” she said, “to hold such a child as that responsible for what he says. I have little doubt that the idea has been put into his head by others. If there are people in this village, Mr. Dempster, who have forgotten the respect and gratitude due from every soul in it to my mother’s memory, I will find them out, and if I have any influence with Mr. Fairlie, they shall suffer for it.”\n\n“I hope—indeed, I am sure, Miss Halcombe—that you are mistaken,” said the schoolmaster. “The matter begins and ends with the boy’s own perversity and folly. He saw, or thought he saw, a woman in white, yesterday evening, as he was passing the churchyard; and the figure, real or fancied, was standing by the marble cross, which he and every one else in Limmeridge knows to be the monument over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave. These two circumstances are surely sufficient to have suggested to the boy himself the answer which has so naturally shocked you?”\n\nAlthough Miss Halcombe did not seem to be convinced, she evidently felt that the schoolmaster’s statement of the case was too sensible to be openly combated. She merely replied by thanking him for his attention, and by promising to see him again when her doubts were satisfied. This said, she bowed, and led the way out of the schoolroom.\n\nThroughout the whole of this strange scene I had stood apart, listening attentively, and drawing my own conclusions. As soon as we were alone again, Miss Halcombe asked me if I had formed any opinion on what I had heard.\n\n“A very strong opinion,” I answered; “the boy’s story, as I believe, has a foundation in fact. I confess I am anxious to see the monument over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, and to examine the ground about it.”\n\n“You shall see the grave.”\n\nShe paused after making that reply, and reflected a little as we walked on. “What has happened in the schoolroom,” she resumed, “has so completely distracted my attention from the subject of the letter, that I feel a little bewildered when I try to return to it. Must we give up all idea of making any further inquiries, and wait to place the thing in Mr. Gilmore’s hands to-morrow?”\n\n“By no means, Miss Halcombe. What has happened in the schoolroom encourages me to persevere in the investigation.”\n\n“Why does it encourage you?”\n\n“Because it strengthens a suspicion I felt when you gave me the letter to read.”\n\n“I suppose you had your reasons, Mr. Hartright, for concealing that suspicion from me till this moment?”\n\n“I was afraid to encourage it in myself. I thought it was utterly preposterous—I distrusted it as the result of some perversity in my own imagination. But I can do so no longer. Not only the boy’s own answers to your questions, but even a chance expression that dropped from the schoolmaster’s lips in explaining his story, have forced the idea back into my mind. Events may yet prove that idea to be a delusion, Miss Halcombe; but the belief is strong in me, at this moment, that the fancied ghost in the churchyard, and the writer of the anonymous letter, are one and the same person.”\n\nShe stopped, turned pale, and looked me eagerly in the face.\n\n“What person?”\n\n“The schoolmaster unconsciously told you. When he spoke of the figure that the boy saw in the churchyard he called it ‘a woman in white.’”\n\n“Not Anne Catherick?”\n\n“Yes, Anne Catherick.”\n\nShe put her hand through my arm and leaned on it heavily.\n\n“I don’t know why,” she said in low tones, “but there is something in this suspicion of yours that seems to startle and unnerve me. I feel——” She stopped, and tried to laugh it off. “Mr. Hartright,” she went on, “I will show you the grave, and then go back at once to the house. I had better not leave Laura too long alone. I had better go back and sit with her.”\n\nWe were close to the churchyard when she spoke. The church, a dreary building of grey stone, was situated in a little valley, so as to be sheltered from the bleak winds blowing over the moorland all round it. The burial-ground advanced, from the side of the church, a little way up the slope of the hill. It was surrounded by a rough, low stone wall, and was bare and open to the sky, except at one extremity, where a brook trickled down the stony hill-side, and a clump of dwarf trees threw their narrow shadows over the short, meagre grass. Just beyond the brook and the trees, and not far from one of the three stone stiles which afforded entrance, at various points, to the churchyard, rose the white marble cross that distinguished Mrs. Fairlie’s grave from the humbler monuments scattered about it.\n\n“I need go no farther with you,” said Miss Halcombe, pointing to the grave. “You will let me know if you find anything to confirm the idea you have just mentioned to me. Let us meet again at the house.”\n\nShe left me. I descended at once to the churchyard, and crossed the stile which led directly to Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.\n\nThe grass about it was too short, and the ground too hard, to show any marks of footsteps. Disappointed thus far, I next looked attentively at the cross, and at the square block of marble below it, on which the inscription was cut.\n\nThe natural whiteness of the cross was a little clouded, here and there, by weather stains, and rather more than one half of the square block beneath it, on the side which bore the inscription, was in the same condition. The other half, however, attracted my attention at once by its singular freedom from stain or impurity of any kind. I looked closer, and saw that it had been cleaned—recently cleaned, in a downward direction from top to bottom. The boundary line between the part that had been cleaned and the part that had not was traceable wherever the inscription left a blank space of marble—sharply traceable as a line that had been produced by artificial means. Who had begun the cleansing of the marble, and who had left it unfinished?\n\nI looked about me, wondering how the question was to be solved. No sign of a habitation could be discerned from the point at which I was standing—the burial-ground was left in the lonely possession of the dead. I returned to the church, and walked round it till I came to the back of the building; then crossed the boundary wall beyond, by another of the stone stiles, and found myself at the head of a path leading down into a deserted stone quarry. Against one side of the quarry a little two-room cottage was built, and just outside the door an old woman was engaged in washing.\n\nI walked up to her, and entered into conversation about the church and burial-ground. She was ready enough to talk, and almost the first words she said informed me that her husband filled the two offices of clerk and sexton. I said a few words next in praise of Mrs. Fairlie’s monument. The old woman shook her head, and told me I had not seen it at its best. It was her husband’s business to look after it, but he had been so ailing and weak for months and months past, that he had hardly been able to crawl into church on Sundays to do his duty, and the monument had been neglected in consequence. He was getting a little better now, and in a week or ten days’ time he hoped to be strong enough to set to work and clean it.\n\nThis information—extracted from a long rambling answer in the broadest Cumberland dialect—told me all that I most wanted to know. I gave the poor woman a trifle, and returned at once to Limmeridge House.\n\nThe partial cleansing of the monument had evidently been accomplished by a strange hand. Connecting what I had discovered, thus far, with what I had suspected after hearing the story of the ghost seen at twilight, I wanted nothing more to confirm my resolution to watch Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, in secret, that evening, returning to it at sunset, and waiting within sight of it till the night fell. The work of cleansing the monument had been left unfinished, and the person by whom it had been begun might return to complete it.\n\nOn getting back to the house I informed Miss Halcombe of what I intended to do. She looked surprised and uneasy while I was explaining my purpose, but she made no positive objection to the execution of it. She only said, “I hope it may end well.”\n\nJust as she was leaving me again, I stopped her to inquire, as calmly as I could, after Miss Fairlie’s health. She was in better spirits, and Miss Halcombe hoped she might be induced to take a little walking exercise while the afternoon sun lasted.\n\nI returned to my own room to resume setting the drawings in order. It was necessary to do this, and doubly necessary to keep my mind employed on anything that would help to distract my attention from myself, and from the hopeless future that lay before me. From time to time I paused in my work to look out of window and watch the sky as the sun sank nearer and nearer to the horizon. On one of those occasions I saw a figure on the broad gravel walk under my window. It was Miss Fairlie.\n\nI had not seen her since the morning, and I had hardly spoken to her then. Another day at Limmeridge was all that remained to me, and after that day my eyes might never look on her again. This thought was enough to hold me at the window. I had sufficient consideration for her to arrange the blind so that she might not see me if she looked up, but I had no strength to resist the temptation of letting my eyes, at least, follow her as far as they could on her walk.\n\nShe was dressed in a brown cloak, with a plain black silk gown under it. On her head was the same simple straw hat which she had worn on the morning when we first met. A veil was attached to it now which hid her face from me. By her side trotted a little Italian greyhound, the pet companion of all her walks, smartly dressed in a scarlet cloth wrapper, to keep the sharp air from his delicate skin. She did not seem to notice the dog. She walked straight forward, with her head drooping a little, and her arms folded in her cloak. The dead leaves, which had whirled in the wind before me when I had heard of her marriage engagement in the morning, whirled in the wind before her, and rose and fell and scattered themselves at her feet as she walked on in the pale waning sunlight. The dog shivered and trembled, and pressed against her dress impatiently for notice and encouragement. But she never heeded him. She walked on, farther and farther away from me, with the dead leaves whirling about her on the path—walked on, till my aching eyes could see her no more, and I was left alone again with my own heavy heart.\n\nIn another hour’s time I had done my work, and the sunset was at hand. I got my hat and coat in the hall, and slipped out of the house without meeting any one.\n\nThe clouds were wild in the western heaven, and the wind blew chill from the sea. Far as the shore was, the sound of the surf swept over the intervening moorland, and beat drearily in my ears when I entered the churchyard. Not a living creature was in sight. The place looked lonelier than ever as I chose my position, and waited and watched, with my eyes on the white cross that rose over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.\n\n# XIII\n\nThe exposed situation of the churchyard had obliged me to be cautious in choosing the position that I was to occupy.\n\nThe main entrance to the church was on the side next to the burial-ground, and the door was screened by a porch walled in on either side. After some little hesitation, caused by natural reluctance to conceal myself, indispensable as that concealment was to the object in view, I had resolved on entering the porch. A loophole window was pierced in each of its side walls. Through one of these windows I could see Mrs. Fairlie’s grave. The other looked towards the stone quarry in which the sexton’s cottage was built. Before me, fronting the porch entrance, was a patch of bare burial-ground, a line of low stone wall, and a strip of lonely brown hill, with the sunset clouds sailing heavily over it before the strong, steady wind. No living creature was visible or audible—no bird flew by me, no dog barked from the sexton’s cottage. The pauses in the dull beating of the surf were filled up by the dreary rustling of the dwarf trees near the grave, and the cold faint bubble of the brook over its stony bed. A dreary scene and a dreary hour. My spirits sank fast as I counted out the minutes of the evening in my hiding-place under the church porch.\n\nIt was not twilight yet—the light of the setting sun still lingered in the heavens, and little more than the first half-hour of my solitary watch had elapsed—when I heard footsteps and a voice. The footsteps were approaching from the other side of the church, and the voice was a woman’s.\n\n“Don’t you fret, my dear, about the letter,” said the voice. “I gave it to the lad quite safe, and the lad he took it from me without a word. He went his way and I went mine, and not a living soul followed me afterwards—that I’ll warrant.”\n\nThese words strung up my attention to a pitch of expectation that was almost painful. There was a pause of silence, but the footsteps still advanced. In another moment two persons, both women, passed within my range of view from the porch window. They were walking straight towards the grave; and therefore they had their backs turned towards me.\n\nOne of the women was dressed in a bonnet and shawl. The other wore a long travelling-cloak of a dark-blue colour, with the hood drawn over her head. A few inches of her gown were visible below the cloak. My heart beat fast as I noted the colour—it was white.\n\nAfter advancing about half-way between the church and the grave they stopped, and the woman in the cloak turned her head towards her companion. But her side face, which a bonnet might now have allowed me to see, was hidden by the heavy, projecting edge of the hood.\n\n“Mind you keep that comfortable warm cloak on,” said the same voice which I had already heard—the voice of the woman in the shawl. “Mrs. Todd is right about your looking too particular, yesterday, all in white. I’ll walk about a little while you’re here, churchyards being not at all in my way, whatever they may be in yours. Finish what you want to do before I come back, and let us be sure and get home again before night.”\n\nWith those words she turned about, and retracing her steps, advanced with her face towards me. It was the face of an elderly woman, brown, rugged, and healthy, with nothing dishonest or suspicious in the look of it. Close to the church she stopped to pull her shawl closer round her.\n\n“Queer,” she said to herself, “always queer, with her whims and her ways, ever since I can remember her. Harmless, though—as harmless, poor soul, as a little child.”\n\nShe sighed—looked about the burial-ground nervously—shook her head, as if the dreary prospect by no means pleased her, and disappeared round the corner of the church.\n\nI doubted for a moment whether I ought to follow and speak to her or not. My intense anxiety to find myself face to face with her companion helped me to decide in the negative. I could ensure seeing the woman in the shawl by waiting near the churchyard until she came back—although it seemed more than doubtful whether she could give me the information of which I was in search. The person who had delivered the letter was of little consequence. The person who had written it was the one centre of interest, and the one source of information, and that person I now felt convinced was before me in the churchyard.\n\nWhile these ideas were passing through my mind I saw the woman in the cloak approach close to the grave, and stand looking at it for a little while. She then glanced all round her, and taking a white linen cloth or handkerchief from under her cloak, turned aside towards the brook. The little stream ran into the churchyard under a tiny archway in the bottom of the wall, and ran out again, after a winding course of a few dozen yards, under a similar opening. She dipped the cloth in the water, and returned to the grave. I saw her kiss the white cross, then kneel down before the inscription, and apply her wet cloth to the cleansing of it.\n\nAfter considering how I could show myself with the least possible chance of frightening her, I resolved to cross the wall before me, to skirt round it outside, and to enter the churchyard again by the stile near the grave, in order that she might see me as I approached. She was so absorbed over her employment that she did not hear me coming until I had stepped over the stile. Then she looked up, started to her feet with a faint cry, and stood facing me in speechless and motionless terror.\n\n“Don’t be frightened,” I said. “Surely you remember me?”\n\nI stopped while I spoke—then advanced a few steps gently—then stopped again—and so approached by little and little till I was close to her. If there had been any doubt still left in my mind, it must have been now set at rest. There, speaking affrightedly for itself—there was the same face confronting me over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave which had first looked into mine on the high-road by night.\n\n“You remember me?” I said. “We met very late, and I helped you to find the way to London. Surely you have not forgotten that?”\n\nHer features relaxed, and she drew a heavy breath of relief. I saw the new life of recognition stirring slowly under the death-like stillness which fear had set on her face.\n\n“Don’t attempt to speak to me just yet,” I went on. “Take time to recover yourself—take time to feel quite certain that I am a friend.”\n\n“You are very kind to me,” she murmured. “As kind now as you were then.”\n\nShe stopped, and I kept silence on my side. I was not granting time for composure to her only, I was gaining time also for myself. Under the wan wild evening light, that woman and I were met together again, a grave between us, the dead about us, the lonesome hills closing us round on every side. The time, the place, the circumstances under which we now stood face to face in the evening stillness of that dreary valley—the lifelong interests which might hang suspended on the next chance words that passed between us—the sense that, for aught I knew to the contrary, the whole future of Laura Fairlie’s life might be determined, for good or for evil, by my winning or losing the confidence of the forlorn creature who stood trembling by her mother’s grave—all threatened to shake the steadiness and the self-control on which every inch of the progress I might yet make now depended. I tried hard, as I felt this, to possess myself of all my resources; I did my utmost to turn the few moments for reflection to the best account.\n\n“Are you calmer now?” I said, as soon as I thought it time to speak again. “Can you talk to me without feeling frightened, and without forgetting that I am a friend?”\n\n“How did you come here?” she asked, without noticing what I had just said to her.\n\n“Don’t you remember my telling you, when we last met, that I was going to Cumberland? I have been in Cumberland ever since—I have been staying all the time at Limmeridge House.”\n\n“At Limmeridge House!” Her pale face brightened as she repeated the words, her wandering eyes fixed on me with a sudden interest. “Ah, how happy you must have been!” she said, looking at me eagerly, without a shadow of its former distrust left in her expression.\n\nI took advantage of her newly-aroused confidence in me to observe her face, with an attention and a curiosity which I had hitherto restrained myself from showing, for caution’s sake. I looked at her, with my mind full of that other lovely face which had so ominously recalled her to my memory on the terrace by moonlight. I had seen Anne Catherick’s likeness in Miss Fairlie. I now saw Miss Fairlie’s likeness in Anne Catherick—saw it all the more clearly because the points of dissimilarity between the two were presented to me as well as the points of resemblance. In the general outline of the countenance and general proportion of the features—in the colour of the hair and in the little nervous uncertainty about the lips—in the height and size of the figure, and the carriage of the head and body, the likeness appeared even more startling than I had ever felt it to be yet. But there the resemblance ended, and the dissimilarity, in details, began. The delicate beauty of Miss Fairlie’s complexion, the transparent clearness of her eyes, the smooth purity of her skin, the tender bloom of colour on her lips, were all missing from the worn weary face that was now turned towards mine. Although I hated myself even for thinking such a thing, still, while I looked at the woman before me, the idea would force itself into my mind that one sad change, in the future, was all that was wanting to make the likeness complete, which I now saw to be so imperfect in detail. If ever sorrow and suffering set their profaning marks on the youth and beauty of Miss Fairlie’s face, then, and then only, Anne Catherick and she would be the twin-sisters of chance resemblance, the living reflections of one another.\n\nI shuddered at the thought. There was something horrible in the blind unreasoning distrust of the future which the mere passage of it through my mind seemed to imply. It was a welcome interruption to be roused by feeling Anne Catherick’s hand laid on my shoulder. The touch was as stealthy and as sudden as that other touch which had petrified me from head to foot on the night when we first met.\n\n“You are looking at me, and you are thinking of something,” she said, with her strange breathless rapidity of utterance. “What is it?”\n\n“Nothing extraordinary,” I answered. “I was only wondering how you came here.”\n\n“I came with a friend who is very good to me. I have only been here two days.”\n\n“And you found your way to this place yesterday?”\n\n“How do you know that?”\n\n“I only guessed it.”\n\nShe turned from me, and knelt down before the inscription once more.\n\n“Where should I go if not here?” she said. “The friend who was better than a mother to me is the only friend I have to visit at Limmeridge. Oh, it makes my heart ache to see a stain on her tomb! It ought to be kept white as snow, for her sake. I was tempted to begin cleaning it yesterday, and I can’t help coming back to go on with it to-day. Is there anything wrong in that? I hope not. Surely nothing can be wrong that I do for Mrs. Fairlie’s sake?”\n\nThe old grateful sense of her benefactress’s kindness was evidently the ruling idea still in the poor creature’s mind—the narrow mind which had but too plainly opened to no other lasting impression since that first impression of her younger and happier days. I saw that my best chance of winning her confidence lay in encouraging her to proceed with the artless employment which she had come into the burial-ground to pursue. She resumed it at once, on my telling her she might do so, touching the hard marble as tenderly as if it had been a sentient thing, and whispering the words of the inscription to herself, over and over again, as if the lost days of her girlhood had returned and she was patiently learning her lesson once more at Mrs. Fairlie’s knees.\n\n“Should you wonder very much,” I said, preparing the way as cautiously as I could for the questions that were to come, “if I owned that it is a satisfaction to me, as well as a surprise, to see you here? I felt very uneasy about you after you left me in the cab.”\n\nShe looked up quickly and suspiciously.\n\n“Uneasy,” she repeated. “Why?”\n\n“A strange thing happened after we parted that night. Two men overtook me in a chaise. They did not see where I was standing, but they stopped near me, and spoke to a policeman on the other side of the way.”\n\nShe instantly suspended her employment. The hand holding the damp cloth with which she had been cleaning the inscription dropped to her side. The other hand grasped the marble cross at the head of the grave. Her face turned towards me slowly, with the blank look of terror set rigidly on it once more. I went on at all hazards—it was too late now to draw back.\n\n“The two men spoke to the policeman,” I said, “and asked him if he had seen you. He had not seen you; and then one of the men spoke again, and said you had escaped from his Asylum.”\n\nShe sprang to her feet as if my last words had set the pursuers on her track.\n\n“Stop! and hear the end,” I cried. “Stop! and you shall know how I befriended you. A word from me would have told the men which way you had gone—and I never spoke that word. I helped your escape—I made it safe and certain. Think, try to think. Try to understand what I tell you.”\n\nMy manner seemed to influence her more than my words. She made an effort to grasp the new idea. Her hands shifted the damp cloth hesitatingly from one to the other, exactly as they had shifted the little travelling-bag on the night when I first saw her. Slowly the purpose of my words seemed to force its way through the confusion and agitation of her mind. Slowly her features relaxed, and her eyes looked at me with their expression gaining in curiosity what it was fast losing in fear.\n\n“YOU don’t think I ought to be back in the Asylum, do you?” she said.\n\n“Certainly not. I am glad you escaped from it—I am glad I helped you.”\n\n“Yes, yes, you did help me indeed; you helped me at the hard part,” she went on a little vacantly. “It was easy to escape, or I should not have got away. They never suspected me as they suspected the others. I was so quiet, and so obedient, and so easily frightened. The finding London was the hard part, and there you helped me. Did I thank you at the time? I thank you now very kindly.”\n\n“Was the Asylum far from where you met me? Come! show that you believe me to be your friend, and tell me where it was.”\n\nShe mentioned the place—a private Asylum, as its situation informed me; a private Asylum not very far from the spot where I had seen her—and then, with evident suspicion of the use to which I might put her answer, anxiously repeated her former inquiry, “You don’t think I ought to be taken back, do you?”\n\n“Once again, I am glad you escaped—I am glad you prospered well after you left me,” I answered. “You said you had a friend in London to go to. Did you find the friend?”\n\n“Yes. It was very late, but there was a girl up at needle-work in the house, and she helped me to rouse Mrs. Clements. Mrs. Clements is my friend. A good, kind woman, but not like Mrs. Fairlie. Ah no, nobody is like Mrs. Fairlie!”\n\n“Is Mrs. Clements an old friend of yours? Have you known her a long time?”\n\n“Yes, she was a neighbour of ours once, at home, in Hampshire, and liked me, and took care of me when I was a little girl. Years ago, when she went away from us, she wrote down in my Prayer-book for me where she was going to live in London, and she said, ‘If you are ever in trouble, Anne, come to me. I have no husband alive to say me nay, and no children to look after, and I will take care of you.’ Kind words, were they not? I suppose I remember them because they were kind. It’s little enough I remember besides—little enough, little enough!”\n\n“Had you no father or mother to take care of you?”\n\n“Father?—I never saw him—I never heard mother speak of him. Father? Ah, dear! he is dead, I suppose.”\n\n“And your mother?”\n\n“I don’t get on well with her. We are a trouble and a fear to each other.”\n\nA trouble and a fear to each other! At those words the suspicion crossed my mind, for the first time, that her mother might be the person who had placed her under restraint.\n\n“Don’t ask me about mother,” she went on. “I’d rather talk of Mrs. Clements. Mrs. Clements is like you, she doesn’t think that I ought to be back in the Asylum, and she is as glad as you are that I escaped from it. She cried over my misfortune, and said it must be kept secret from everybody.”\n\nHer “misfortune.” In what sense was she using that word? In a sense which might explain her motive in writing the anonymous letter? In a sense which might show it to be the too common and too customary motive that has led many a woman to interpose anonymous hindrances to the marriage of the man who has ruined her? I resolved to attempt the clearing up of this doubt before more words passed between us on either side.\n\n“What misfortune?” I asked.\n\n“The misfortune of my being shut up,” she answered, with every appearance of feeling surprised at my question. “What other misfortune could there be?”\n\nI determined to persist, as delicately and forbearingly as possible. It was of very great importance that I should be absolutely sure of every step in the investigation which I now gained in advance.\n\n“There is another misfortune,” I said, “to which a woman may be liable, and by which she may suffer lifelong sorrow and shame.”\n\n“What is it?” she asked eagerly.\n\n“The misfortune of believing too innocently in her own virtue, and in the faith and honour of the man she loves,” I answered.\n\nShe looked up at me with the artless bewilderment of a child. Not the slightest confusion or change of colour—not the faintest trace of any secret consciousness of shame struggling to the surface appeared in her face—that face which betrayed every other emotion with such transparent clearness. No words that ever were spoken could have assured me, as her look and manner now assured me, that the motive which I had assigned for her writing the letter and sending it to Miss Fairlie was plainly and distinctly the wrong one. That doubt, at any rate, was now set at rest; but the very removal of it opened a new prospect of uncertainty. The letter, as I knew from positive testimony, pointed at Sir Percival Glyde, though it did not name him. She must have had some strong motive, originating in some deep sense of injury, for secretly denouncing him to Miss Fairlie in such terms as she had employed, and that motive was unquestionably not to be traced to the loss of her innocence and her character. Whatever wrong he might have inflicted on her was not of that nature. Of what nature could it be?\n\n“I don’t understand you,” she said, after evidently trying hard, and trying in vain, to discover the meaning of the words I had last said to her.\n\n“Never mind,” I answered. “Let us go on with what we were talking about. Tell me how long you stayed with Mrs. Clements in London, and how you came here.”\n\n“How long?” she repeated. “I stayed with Mrs. Clements till we both came to this place, two days ago.”\n\n“You are living in the village, then?” I said. “It is strange I should not have heard of you, though you have only been here two days.”\n\n“No, no, not in the village. Three miles away at a farm. Do you know the farm? They call it Todd’s Corner.”\n\nI remembered the place perfectly—we had often passed by it in our drives. It was one of the oldest farms in the neighbourhood, situated in a solitary, sheltered spot, inland at the junction of two hills.\n\n“They are relations of Mrs. Clements at Todd’s Corner,” she went on, “and they had often asked her to go and see them. She said she would go, and take me with her, for the quiet and the fresh air. It was very kind, was it not? I would have gone anywhere to be quiet, and safe, and out of the way. But when I heard that Todd’s Corner was near Limmeridge—oh! I was so happy I would have walked all the way barefoot to get there, and see the schools and the village and Limmeridge House again. They are very good people at Todd’s Corner. I hope I shall stay there a long time. There is only one thing I don’t like about them, and don’t like about Mrs. Clements——”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“They will tease me about dressing all in white—they say it looks so particular. How do they know? Mrs. Fairlie knew best. Mrs. Fairlie would never have made me wear this ugly blue cloak! Ah! she was fond of white in her lifetime, and here is white stone about her grave—and I am making it whiter for her sake. She often wore white herself, and she always dressed her little daughter in white. Is Miss Fairlie well and happy? Does she wear white now, as she used when she was a girl?”\n\nHer voice sank when she put the questions about Miss Fairlie, and she turned her head farther and farther away from me. I thought I detected, in the alteration of her manner, an uneasy consciousness of the risk she had run in sending the anonymous letter, and I instantly determined so to frame my answer as to surprise her into owning it.\n\n“Miss Fairlie was not very well or very happy this morning,” I said.\n\nShe murmured a few words, but they were spoken so confusedly, and in such a low tone, that I could not even guess at what they meant.\n\n“Did you ask me why Miss Fairlie was neither well nor happy this morning?” I continued.\n\n“No,” she said quickly and eagerly—“oh no, I never asked that.”\n\n“I will tell you without your asking,” I went on. “Miss Fairlie has received your letter.”\n\nShe had been down on her knees for some little time past, carefully removing the last weather-stains left about the inscription while we were speaking together. The first sentence of the words I had just addressed to her made her pause in her occupation, and turn slowly without rising from her knees, so as to face me. The second sentence literally petrified her. The cloth she had been holding dropped from her hands—her lips fell apart—all the little colour that there was naturally in her face left it in an instant.\n\n“How do you know?” she said faintly. “Who showed it to you?” The blood rushed back into her face—rushed overwhelmingly, as the sense rushed upon her mind that her own words had betrayed her. She struck her hands together in despair. “I never wrote it,” she gasped affrightedly; “I know nothing about it!”\n\n“Yes,” I said, “you wrote it, and you know about it. It was wrong to send such a letter, it was wrong to frighten Miss Fairlie. If you had anything to say that it was right and necessary for her to hear, you should have gone yourself to Limmeridge House—you should have spoken to the young lady with your own lips.”\n\nShe crouched down over the flat stone of the grave, till her face was hidden on it, and made no reply.\n\n“Miss Fairlie will be as good and kind to you as her mother was, if you mean well,” I went on. “Miss Fairlie will keep your secret, and not let you come to any harm. Will you see her to-morrow at the farm? Will you meet her in the garden at Limmeridge House?”\n\n“Oh, if I could die, and be hidden and at rest with YOU!” Her lips murmured the words close on the grave-stone, murmured them in tones of passionate endearment, to the dead remains beneath. “You know how I love your child, for your sake! Oh, Mrs. Fairlie! Mrs. Fairlie! tell me how to save her. Be my darling and my mother once more, and tell me what to do for the best.”\n\nI heard her lips kissing the stone—I saw her hands beating on it passionately. The sound and the sight deeply affected me. I stooped down, and took the poor helpless hands tenderly in mine, and tried to soothe her.\n\nIt was useless. She snatched her hands from me, and never moved her face from the stone. Seeing the urgent necessity of quieting her at any hazard and by any means, I appealed to the only anxiety that she appeared to feel, in connection with me and with my opinion of her—the anxiety to convince me of her fitness to be mistress of her own actions.\n\n“Come, come,” I said gently. “Try to compose yourself, or you will make me alter my opinion of you. Don’t let me think that the person who put you in the Asylum might have had some excuse——”\n\nThe next words died away on my lips. The instant I risked that chance reference to the person who had put her in the Asylum she sprang up on her knees. A most extraordinary and startling change passed over her. Her face, at all ordinary times so touching to look at, in its nervous sensitiveness, weakness, and uncertainty, became suddenly darkened by an expression of maniacally intense hatred and fear, which communicated a wild, unnatural force to every feature. Her eyes dilated in the dim evening light, like the eyes of a wild animal. She caught up the cloth that had fallen at her side, as if it had been a living creature that she could kill, and crushed it in both her hands with such convulsive strength, that the few drops of moisture left in it trickled down on the stone beneath her.\n\n“Talk of something else,” she said, whispering through her teeth. “I shall lose myself if you talk of that.”\n\nEvery vestige of the gentler thoughts which had filled her mind hardly a minute since seemed to be swept from it now. It was evident that the impression left by Mrs. Fairlie’s kindness was not, as I had supposed, the only strong impression on her memory. With the grateful remembrance of her school-days at Limmeridge, there existed the vindictive remembrance of the wrong inflicted on her by her confinement in the Asylum. Who had done that wrong? Could it really be her mother?\n\nIt was hard to give up pursuing the inquiry to that final point, but I forced myself to abandon all idea of continuing it. Seeing her as I saw her now, it would have been cruel to think of anything but the necessity and the humanity of restoring her composure.\n\n“I will talk of nothing to distress you,” I said soothingly.\n\n“You want something,” she answered sharply and suspiciously. “Don’t look at me like that. Speak to me—tell me what you want.”\n\n“I only want you to quiet yourself, and when you are calmer, to think over what I have said.”\n\n“Said?” She paused—twisted the cloth in her hands, backwards and forwards, and whispered to herself, “What is it he said?” She turned again towards me, and shook her head impatiently. “Why don’t you help me?” she asked, with angry suddenness.\n\n“Yes, yes,” I said, “I will help you, and you will soon remember. I ask you to see Miss Fairlie to-morrow and to tell her the truth about the letter.”\n\n“Ah! Miss Fairlie—Fairlie—Fairlie——”\n\nThe mere utterance of the loved familiar name seemed to quiet her. Her face softened and grew like itself again.\n\n“You need have no fear of Miss Fairlie,” I continued, “and no fear of getting into trouble through the letter. She knows so much about it already, that you will have no difficulty in telling her all. There can be little necessity for concealment where there is hardly anything left to conceal. You mention no names in the letter; but Miss Fairlie knows that the person you write of is Sir Percival Glyde——”\n\nThe instant I pronounced that name she started to her feet, and a scream burst from her that rang through the churchyard, and made my heart leap in me with the terror of it. The dark deformity of the expression which had just left her face lowered on it once more, with doubled and trebled intensity. The shriek at the name, the reiterated look of hatred and fear that instantly followed, told all. Not even a last doubt now remained. Her mother was guiltless of imprisoning her in the Asylum. A man had shut her up—and that man was Sir Percival Glyde.\n\nThe scream had reached other ears than mine. On one side I heard the door of the sexton’s cottage open; on the other I heard the voice of her companion, the woman in the shawl, the woman whom she had spoken of as Mrs. Clements.\n\n“I’m coming! I’m coming!” cried the voice from behind the clump of dwarf trees.\n\nIn a moment more Mrs. Clements hurried into view.\n\n“Who are you?” she cried, facing me resolutely as she set her foot on the stile. “How dare you frighten a poor helpless woman like that?”\n\nShe was at Anne Catherick’s side, and had put one arm around her, before I could answer. “What is it, my dear?” she said. “What has he done to you?”\n\n“Nothing,” the poor creature answered. “Nothing. I’m only frightened.”\n\nMrs. Clements turned on me with a fearless indignation, for which I respected her.\n\n“I should be heartily ashamed of myself if I deserved that angry look,” I said. “But I do not deserve it. I have unfortunately startled her without intending it. This is not the first time she has seen me. Ask her yourself, and she will tell you that I am incapable of willingly harming her or any woman.”\n\nI spoke distinctly, so that Anne Catherick might hear and understand me, and I saw that the words and their meaning had reached her.\n\n“Yes, yes,” she said—“he was good to me once—he helped me——” She whispered the rest into her friend’s ear.\n\n“Strange, indeed!” said Mrs. Clements, with a look of perplexity. “It makes all the difference, though. I’m sorry I spoke so rough to you, sir; but you must own that appearances looked suspicious to a stranger. It’s more my fault than yours, for humouring her whims, and letting her be alone in such a place as this. Come, my dear—come home now.”\n\nI thought the good woman looked a little uneasy at the prospect of the walk back, and I offered to go with them until they were both within sight of home. Mrs. Clements thanked me civilly, and declined. She said they were sure to meet some of the farm-labourers as soon as they got to the moor.\n\n“Try to forgive me,” I said, when Anne Catherick took her friend’s arm to go away. Innocent as I had been of any intention to terrify and agitate her, my heart smote me as I looked at the poor, pale, frightened face.\n\n“I will try,” she answered. “But you know too much—I’m afraid you’ll always frighten me now.”\n\nMrs. Clements glanced at me, and shook her head pityingly.\n\n“Good-night, sir,” she said. “You couldn’t help it, I know; but I wish it was me you had frightened, and not her.”\n\nThey moved away a few steps. I thought they had left me, but Anne suddenly stopped, and separated herself from her friend.\n\n“Wait a little,” she said. “I must say good-bye.”\n\nShe returned to the grave, rested both hands tenderly on the marble cross, and kissed it.\n\n“I’m better now,” she sighed, looking up at me quietly. “I forgive you.”\n\nShe joined her companion again, and they left the burial-ground. I saw them stop near the church and speak to the sexton’s wife, who had come from the cottage, and had waited, watching us from a distance. Then they went on again up the path that led to the moor. I looked after Anne Catherick as she disappeared, till all trace of her had faded in the twilight—looked as anxiously and sorrowfully as if that was the last I was to see in this weary world of the woman in white.\n\n# XIV\n\nHalf an hour later I was back at the house, and was informing Miss Halcombe of all that had happened.\n\nShe listened to me from beginning to end with a steady, silent attention, which, in a woman of her temperament and disposition, was the strongest proof that could be offered of the serious manner in which my narrative affected her.\n\n“My mind misgives me,” was all she said when I had done. “My mind misgives me sadly about the future.”\n\n“The future may depend,” I suggested, “on the use we make of the present. It is not improbable that Anne Catherick may speak more readily and unreservedly to a woman than she has spoken to me. If Miss Fairlie——”\n\n“Not to be thought of for a moment,” interposed Miss Halcombe, in her most decided manner.\n\n“Let me suggest, then,” I continued, “that you should see Anne Catherick yourself, and do all you can to win her confidence. For my own part, I shrink from the idea of alarming the poor creature a second time, as I have most unhappily alarmed her already. Do you see any objection to accompanying me to the farmhouse to-morrow?”\n\n“None whatever. I will go anywhere and do anything to serve Laura’s interests. What did you say the place was called?”\n\n“You must know it well. It is called Todd’s Corner.”\n\n“Certainly. Todd’s Corner is one of Mr. Fairlie’s farms. Our dairymaid here is the farmer’s second daughter. She goes backwards and forwards constantly between this house and her father’s farm, and she may have heard or seen something which it may be useful to us to know. Shall I ascertain, at once, if the girl is downstairs?”\n\nShe rang the bell, and sent the servant with his message. He returned, and announced that the dairymaid was then at the farm. She had not been there for the last three days, and the housekeeper had given her leave to go home for an hour or two that evening.\n\n“I can speak to her to-morrow,” said Miss Halcombe, when the servant had left the room again. “In the meantime, let me thoroughly understand the object to be gained by my interview with Anne Catherick. Is there no doubt in your mind that the person who confined her in the Asylum was Sir Percival Glyde?”\n\n“There is not the shadow of a doubt. The only mystery that remains is the mystery of his MOTIVE. Looking to the great difference between his station in life and hers, which seems to preclude all idea of the most distant relationship between them, it is of the last importance—even assuming that she really required to be placed under restraint—to know why HE should have been the person to assume the serious responsibility of shutting her up——”\n\n“In a private Asylum, I think you said?”\n\n“Yes, in a private Asylum, where a sum of money, which no poor person could afford to give, must have been paid for her maintenance as a patient.”\n\n“I see where the doubt lies, Mr. Hartright, and I promise you that it shall be set at rest, whether Anne Catherick assists us to-morrow or not. Sir Percival Glyde shall not be long in this house without satisfying Mr. Gilmore, and satisfying me. My sister’s future is my dearest care in life, and I have influence enough over her to give me some power, where her marriage is concerned, in the disposal of it.”\n\nWe parted for the night.\n\nAfter breakfast the next morning, an obstacle, which the events of the evening before had put out of my memory, interposed to prevent our proceeding immediately to the farm. This was my last day at Limmeridge House, and it was necessary, as soon as the post came in, to follow Miss Halcombe’s advice, and to ask Mr. Fairlie’s permission to shorten my engagement by a month, in consideration of an unforeseen necessity for my return to London.\n\nFortunately for the probability of this excuse, so far as appearances were concerned, the post brought me two letters from London friends that morning. I took them away at once to my own room, and sent the servant with a message to Mr. Fairlie, requesting to know when I could see him on a matter of business.\n\nI awaited the man’s return, free from the slightest feeling of anxiety about the manner in which his master might receive my application. With Mr. Fairlie’s leave or without it, I must go. The consciousness of having now taken the first step on the dreary journey which was henceforth to separate my life from Miss Fairlie’s seemed to have blunted my sensibility to every consideration connected with myself. I had done with my poor man’s touchy pride—I had done with all my little artist vanities. No insolence of Mr. Fairlie’s, if he chose to be insolent, could wound me now.\n\nThe servant returned with a message for which I was not unprepared. Mr. Fairlie regretted that the state of his health, on that particular morning, was such as to preclude all hope of his having the pleasure of receiving me. He begged, therefore, that I would accept his apologies, and kindly communicate what I had to say in the form of a letter. Similar messages to this had reached me, at various intervals, during my three months’ residence in the house. Throughout the whole of that period Mr. Fairlie had been rejoiced to “possess” me, but had never been well enough to see me for a second time. The servant took every fresh batch of drawings that I mounted and restored back to his master with my “respects,” and returned empty-handed with Mr. Fairlie’s “kind compliments,” “best thanks,” and “sincere regrets” that the state of his health still obliged him to remain a solitary prisoner in his own room. A more satisfactory arrangement to both sides could not possibly have been adopted. It would be hard to say which of us, under the circumstances, felt the most grateful sense of obligation to Mr. Fairlie’s accommodating nerves.\n\nI sat down at once to write the letter, expressing myself in it as civilly, as clearly, and as briefly as possible. Mr. Fairlie did not hurry his reply. Nearly an hour elapsed before the answer was placed in my hands. It was written with beautiful regularity and neatness of character, in violet-coloured ink, on note-paper as smooth as ivory and almost as thick as cardboard, and it addressed me in these terms—\n\n“Mr. Fairlie’s compliments to Mr. Hartright. Mr. Fairlie is more surprised and disappointed than he can say (in the present state of his health) by Mr. Hartright’s application. Mr. Fairlie is not a man of business, but he has consulted his steward, who is, and that person confirms Mr. Fairlie’s opinion that Mr. Hartright’s request to be allowed to break his engagement cannot be justified by any necessity whatever, excepting perhaps a case of life and death. If the highly-appreciative feeling towards Art and its professors, which it is the consolation and happiness of Mr. Fairlie’s suffering existence to cultivate, could be easily shaken, Mr. Hartright’s present proceeding would have shaken it. It has not done so—except in the instance of Mr. Hartright himself.\n\n“Having stated his opinion—so far, that is to say, as acute nervous suffering will allow him to state anything—Mr. Fairlie has nothing to add but the expression of his decision, in reference to the highly irregular application that has been made to him. Perfect repose of body and mind being to the last degree important in his case, Mr. Fairlie will not suffer Mr. Hartright to disturb that repose by remaining in the house under circumstances of an essentially irritating nature to both sides. Accordingly, Mr. Fairlie waives his right of refusal, purely with a view to the preservation of his own tranquillity—and informs Mr. Hartright that he may go.”\n\nI folded the letter up, and put it away with my other papers. The time had been when I should have resented it as an insult—I accepted it now as a written release from my engagement. It was off my mind, it was almost out of my memory, when I went downstairs to the breakfast-room, and informed Miss Halcombe that I was ready to walk with her to the farm.\n\n“Has Mr. Fairlie given you a satisfactory answer?” she asked as we left the house.\n\n“He has allowed me to go, Miss Halcombe.”\n\nShe looked up at me quickly, and then, for the first time since I had known her, took my arm of her own accord. No words could have expressed so delicately that she understood how the permission to leave my employment had been granted, and that she gave me her sympathy, not as my superior, but as my friend. I had not felt the man’s insolent letter, but I felt deeply the woman’s atoning kindness.\n\nOn our way to the farm we arranged that Miss Halcombe was to enter the house alone, and that I was to wait outside, within call. We adopted this mode of proceeding from an apprehension that my presence, after what had happened in the churchyard the evening before, might have the effect of renewing Anne Catherick’s nervous dread, and of rendering her additionally distrustful of the advances of a lady who was a stranger to her. Miss Halcombe left me, with the intention of speaking, in the first instance, to the farmer’s wife (of whose friendly readiness to help her in any way she was well assured), while I waited for her in the near neighbourhood of the house.\n\nI had fully expected to be left alone for some time. To my surprise, however, little more than five minutes had elapsed before Miss Halcombe returned.\n\n“Does Anne Catherick refuse to see you?” I asked in astonishment.\n\n“Anne Catherick is gone,” replied Miss Halcombe.\n\n“Gone?”\n\n“Gone with Mrs. Clements. They both left the farm at eight o’clock this morning.”\n\nI could say nothing—I could only feel that our last chance of discovery had gone with them.\n\n“All that Mrs. Todd knows about her guests, I know,” Miss Halcombe went on, “and it leaves me, as it leaves her, in the dark. They both came back safe last night, after they left you, and they passed the first part of the evening with Mr. Todd’s family as usual. Just before supper-time, however, Anne Catherick startled them all by being suddenly seized with faintness. She had had a similar attack, of a less alarming kind, on the day she arrived at the farm; and Mrs. Todd had connected it, on that occasion, with something she was reading at the time in our local newspaper, which lay on the farm table, and which she had taken up only a minute or two before.”\n\n“Does Mrs. Todd know what particular passage in the newspaper affected her in that way?” I inquired.\n\n“No,” replied Miss Halcombe. “She had looked it over, and had seen nothing in it to agitate any one. I asked leave, however, to look it over in my turn, and at the very first page I opened I found that the editor had enriched his small stock of news by drawing upon our family affairs, and had published my sister’s marriage engagement, among his other announcements, copied from the London papers, of Marriages in High Life. I concluded at once that this was the paragraph which had so strangely affected Anne Catherick, and I thought I saw in it, also, the origin of the letter which she sent to our house the next day.”\n\n“There can be no doubt in either case. But what did you hear about her second attack of faintness yesterday evening?”\n\n“Nothing. The cause of it is a complete mystery. There was no stranger in the room. The only visitor was our dairymaid, who, as I told you, is one of Mr. Todd’s daughters, and the only conversation was the usual gossip about local affairs. They heard her cry out, and saw her turn deadly pale, without the slightest apparent reason. Mrs. Todd and Mrs. Clements took her upstairs, and Mrs. Clements remained with her. They were heard talking together until long after the usual bedtime, and early this morning Mrs. Clements took Mrs. Todd aside, and amazed her beyond all power of expression by saying that they must go. The only explanation Mrs. Todd could extract from her guest was, that something had happened, which was not the fault of any one at the farmhouse, but which was serious enough to make Anne Catherick resolve to leave Limmeridge immediately. It was quite useless to press Mrs. Clements to be more explicit. She only shook her head, and said that, for Anne’s sake, she must beg and pray that no one would question her. All she could repeat, with every appearance of being seriously agitated herself, was that Anne must go, that she must go with her, and that the destination to which they might both betake themselves must be kept a secret from everybody. I spare you the recital of Mrs. Todd’s hospitable remonstrances and refusals. It ended in her driving them both to the nearest station, more than three hours since. She tried hard on the way to get them to speak more plainly, but without success; and she set them down outside the station-door, so hurt and offended by the unceremonious abruptness of their departure and their unfriendly reluctance to place the least confidence in her, that she drove away in anger, without so much as stopping to bid them good-bye. That is exactly what has taken place. Search your own memory, Mr. Hartright, and tell me if anything happened in the burial-ground yesterday evening which can at all account for the extraordinary departure of those two women this morning.”\n\n“I should like to account first, Miss Halcombe, for the sudden change in Anne Catherick which alarmed them at the farmhouse, hours after she and I had parted, and when time enough had elapsed to quiet any violent agitation that I might have been unfortunate enough to cause. Did you inquire particularly about the gossip which was going on in the room when she turned faint?”\n\n“Yes. But Mrs. Todd’s household affairs seem to have divided her attention that evening with the talk in the farmhouse parlour. She could only tell me that it was ‘just the news,’—meaning, I suppose, that they all talked as usual about each other.”\n\n“The dairymaid’s memory may be better than her mother’s,” I said. “It may be as well for you to speak to the girl, Miss Halcombe, as soon as we get back.”\n\nMy suggestion was acted on the moment we returned to the house. Miss Halcombe led me round to the servants’ offices, and we found the girl in the dairy, with her sleeves tucked up to her shoulders, cleaning a large milk-pan and singing blithely over her work.\n\n“I have brought this gentleman to see your dairy, Hannah,” said Miss Halcombe. “It is one of the sights of the house, and it always does you credit.”\n\nThe girl blushed and curtseyed, and said shyly that she hoped she always did her best to keep things neat and clean.\n\n“We have just come from your father’s,” Miss Halcombe continued. “You were there yesterday evening, I hear, and you found visitors at the house?”\n\n“Yes, miss.”\n\n“One of them was taken faint and ill, I am told. I suppose nothing was said or done to frighten her? You were not talking of anything very terrible, were you?”\n\n“Oh no, miss!” said the girl, laughing. “We were only talking of the news.”\n\n“Your sisters told you the news at Todd’s Corner, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes, miss.”\n\n“And you told them the news at Limmeridge House?”\n\n“Yes, miss. And I’m quite sure nothing was said to frighten the poor thing, for I was talking when she was taken ill. It gave me quite a turn, miss, to see it, never having been taken faint myself.”\n\nBefore any more questions could be put to her, she was called away to receive a basket of eggs at the dairy door. As she left us I whispered to Miss Halcombe—\n\n“Ask her if she happened to mention, last night, that visitors were expected at Limmeridge House.”\n\nMiss Halcombe showed me, by a look, that she understood, and put the question as soon as the dairymaid returned to us.\n\n“Oh yes, miss, I mentioned that,” said the girl simply. “The company coming, and the accident to the brindled cow, was all the news I had to take to the farm.”\n\n“Did you mention names? Did you tell them that Sir Percival Glyde was expected on Monday?”\n\n“Yes, miss—I told them Sir Percival Glyde was coming. I hope there was no harm in it—I hope I didn’t do wrong.”\n\n“Oh no, no harm. Come, Mr. Hartright, Hannah will begin to think us in the way, if we interrupt her any longer over her work.”\n\nWe stopped and looked at one another the moment we were alone again.\n\n“Is there any doubt in your mind, NOW, Miss Halcombe?”\n\n“Sir Percival Glyde shall remove that doubt, Mr. Hartright—or Laura Fairlie shall never be his wife.”\n\n# XV\n\nAs we walked round to the front of the house a fly from the railway approached us along the drive. Miss Halcombe waited on the door-steps until the fly drew up, and then advanced to shake hands with an old gentleman, who got out briskly the moment the steps were let down. Mr. Gilmore had arrived.\n\nI looked at him, when we were introduced to each other, with an interest and a curiosity which I could hardly conceal. This old man was to remain at Limmeridge House after I had left it, he was to hear Sir Percival Glyde’s explanation, and was to give Miss Halcombe the assistance of his experience in forming her judgment; he was to wait until the question of the marriage was set at rest; and his hand, if that question were decided in the affirmative, was to draw the settlement which bound Miss Fairlie irrevocably to her engagement. Even then, when I knew nothing by comparison with what I know now, I looked at the family lawyer with an interest which I had never felt before in the presence of any man breathing who was a total stranger to me.\n\nIn external appearance Mr. Gilmore was the exact opposite of the conventional idea of an old lawyer. His complexion was florid—his white hair was worn rather long and kept carefully brushed—his black coat, waistcoat, and trousers fitted him with perfect neatness—his white cravat was carefully tied, and his lavender-coloured kid gloves might have adorned the hands of a fashionable clergyman, without fear and without reproach. His manners were pleasantly marked by the formal grace and refinement of the old school of politeness, quickened by the invigorating sharpness and readiness of a man whose business in life obliges him always to keep his faculties in good working order. A sanguine constitution and fair prospects to begin with—a long subsequent career of creditable and comfortable prosperity—a cheerful, diligent, widely-respected old age—such were the general impressions I derived from my introduction to Mr. Gilmore, and it is but fair to him to add, that the knowledge I gained by later and better experience only tended to confirm them.\n\nI left the old gentleman and Miss Halcombe to enter the house together, and to talk of family matters undisturbed by the restraint of a stranger’s presence. They crossed the hall on their way to the drawing-room, and I descended the steps again to wander about the garden alone.\n\nMy hours were numbered at Limmeridge House—my departure the next morning was irrevocably settled—my share in the investigation which the anonymous letter had rendered necessary was at an end. No harm could be done to any one but myself if I let my heart loose again, for the little time that was left me, from the cold cruelty of restraint which necessity had forced me to inflict upon it, and took my farewell of the scenes which were associated with the brief dream-time of my happiness and my love.\n\nI turned instinctively to the walk beneath my study-window, where I had seen her the evening before with her little dog, and followed the path which her dear feet had trodden so often, till I came to the wicket gate that led into her rose garden. The winter bareness spread drearily over it now. The flowers that she had taught me to distinguish by their names, the flowers that I had taught her to paint from, were gone, and the tiny white paths that led between the beds were damp and green already. I went on to the avenue of trees, where we had breathed together the warm fragrance of August evenings, where we had admired together the myriad combinations of shade and sunlight that dappled the ground at our feet. The leaves fell about me from the groaning branches, and the earthy decay in the atmosphere chilled me to the bones. A little farther on, and I was out of the grounds, and following the lane that wound gently upward to the nearest hills. The old felled tree by the wayside, on which we had sat to rest, was sodden with rain, and the tuft of ferns and grasses which I had drawn for her, nestling under the rough stone wall in front of us, had turned to a pool of water, stagnating round an island of draggled weeds. I gained the summit of the hill, and looked at the view which we had so often admired in the happier time. It was cold and barren—it was no longer the view that I remembered. The sunshine of her presence was far from me—the charm of her voice no longer murmured in my ear. She had talked to me, on the spot from which I now looked down, of her father, who was her last surviving parent—had told me how fond of each other they had been, and how sadly she missed him still when she entered certain rooms in the house, and when she took up forgotten occupations and amusements with which he had been associated. Was the view that I had seen, while listening to those words, the view that I saw now, standing on the hill-top by myself? I turned and left it—I wound my way back again, over the moor, and round the sandhills, down to the beach. There was the white rage of the surf, and the multitudinous glory of the leaping waves—but where was the place on which she had once drawn idle figures with her parasol in the sand—the place where we had sat together, while she talked to me about myself and my home, while she asked me a woman’s minutely observant questions about my mother and my sister, and innocently wondered whether I should ever leave my lonely chambers and have a wife and a house of my own? Wind and wave had long since smoothed out the trace of her which she had left in those marks on the sand. I looked over the wide monotony of the sea-side prospect, and the place in which we two had idled away the sunny hours was as lost to me as if I had never known it, as strange to me as if I stood already on a foreign shore.\n\nThe empty silence of the beach struck cold to my heart. I returned to the house and the garden, where traces were left to speak of her at every turn.\n\nOn the west terrace walk I met Mr. Gilmore. He was evidently in search of me, for he quickened his pace when we caught sight of each other. The state of my spirits little fitted me for the society of a stranger; but the meeting was inevitable, and I resigned myself to make the best of it.\n\n“You are the very person I wanted to see,” said the old gentleman. “I had two words to say to you, my dear sir; and if you have no objection I will avail myself of the present opportunity. To put it plainly, Miss Halcombe and I have been talking over family affairs—affairs which are the cause of my being here—and in the course of our conversation she was naturally led to tell me of this unpleasant matter connected with the anonymous letter, and of the share which you have most creditably and properly taken in the proceedings so far. That share, I quite understand, gives you an interest which you might not otherwise have felt, in knowing that the future management of the investigation which you have begun will be placed in safe hands. My dear sir, make yourself quite easy on that point—it will be placed in MY hands.”\n\n“You are, in every way, Mr. Gilmore, much fitter to advise and to act in the matter than I am. Is it an indiscretion on my part to ask if you have decided yet on a course of proceeding?”\n\n“So far as it is possible to decide, Mr. Hartright, I have decided. I mean to send a copy of the letter, accompanied by a statement of the circumstances, to Sir Percival Glyde’s solicitor in London, with whom I have some acquaintance. The letter itself I shall keep here to show to Sir Percival as soon as he arrives. The tracing of the two women I have already provided for, by sending one of Mr. Fairlie’s servants—a confidential person—to the station to make inquiries. The man has his money and his directions, and he will follow the women in the event of his finding any clue. This is all that can be done until Sir Percival comes on Monday. I have no doubt myself that every explanation which can be expected from a gentleman and a man of honour, he will readily give. Sir Percival stands very high, sir—an eminent position, a reputation above suspicion—I feel quite easy about results—quite easy, I am rejoiced to assure you. Things of this sort happen constantly in my experience. Anonymous letters—unfortunate woman—sad state of society. I don’t deny that there are peculiar complications in this case; but the case itself is, most unhappily, common—common.”\n\n“I am afraid, Mr. Gilmore, I have the misfortune to differ from you in the view I take of the case.”\n\n“Just so, my dear sir—just so. I am an old man, and I take the practical view. You are a young man, and you take the romantic view. Let us not dispute about our views. I live professionally in an atmosphere of disputation, Mr. Hartright, and I am only too glad to escape from it, as I am escaping here. We will wait for events—yes, yes, yes—we will wait for events. Charming place this. Good shooting? Probably not, none of Mr. Fairlie’s land is preserved, I think. Charming place, though, and delightful people. You draw and paint, I hear, Mr. Hartright? Enviable accomplishment. What style?”\n\nWe dropped into general conversation, or rather, Mr. Gilmore talked and I listened. My attention was far from him, and from the topics on which he discoursed so fluently. The solitary walk of the last two hours had wrought its effect on me—it had set the idea in my mind of hastening my departure from Limmeridge House. Why should I prolong the hard trial of saying farewell by one unnecessary minute? What further service was required of me by any one? There was no useful purpose to be served by my stay in Cumberland—there was no restriction of time in the permission to leave which my employer had granted to me. Why not end it there and then?\n\nI determined to end it. There were some hours of daylight still left—there was no reason why my journey back to London should not begin on that afternoon. I made the first civil excuse that occurred to me for leaving Mr. Gilmore, and returned at once to the house.\n\nOn my way up to my own room I met Miss Halcombe on the stairs. She saw, by the hurry of my movements and the change in my manner, that I had some new purpose in view, and asked what had happened.\n\nI told her the reasons which induced me to think of hastening my departure, exactly as I have told them here.\n\n“No, no,” she said, earnestly and kindly, “leave us like a friend—break bread with us once more. Stay here and dine, stay here and help us to spend our last evening with you as happily, as like our first evenings, as we can. It is my invitation—Mrs. Vesey’s invitation——” she hesitated a little, and then added, “Laura’s invitation as well.”\n\nI promised to remain. God knows I had no wish to leave even the shadow of a sorrowful impression with any one of them.\n\nMy own room was the best place for me till the dinner bell rang. I waited there till it was time to go downstairs.\n\nI had not spoken to Miss Fairlie—I had not even seen her—all that day. The first meeting with her, when I entered the drawing-room, was a hard trial to her self-control and to mine. She, too, had done her best to make our last evening renew the golden bygone time—the time that could never come again. She had put on the dress which I used to admire more than any other that she possessed—a dark blue silk, trimmed quaintly and prettily with old-fashioned lace; she came forward to meet me with her former readiness—she gave me her hand with the frank, innocent good-will of happier days. The cold fingers that trembled round mine—the pale cheeks with a bright red spot burning in the midst of them—the faint smile that struggled to live on her lips and died away from them while I looked at it, told me at what sacrifice of herself her outward composure was maintained. My heart could take her no closer to me, or I should have loved her then as I had never loved her yet.\n\nMr. Gilmore was a great assistance to us. He was in high good-humour, and he led the conversation with unflagging spirit. Miss Halcombe seconded him resolutely, and I did all I could to follow her example. The kind blue eyes, whose slightest changes of expression I had learnt to interpret so well, looked at me appealingly when we first sat down to table. Help my sister—the sweet anxious face seemed to say—help my sister, and you will help me.\n\nWe got through the dinner, to all outward appearance at least, happily enough. When the ladies had risen from table, and Mr. Gilmore and I were left alone in the dining-room, a new interest presented itself to occupy our attention, and to give me an opportunity of quieting myself by a few minutes of needful and welcome silence. The servant who had been despatched to trace Anne Catherick and Mrs. Clements returned with his report, and was shown into the dining-room immediately.\n\n“Well,” said Mr. Gilmore, “what have you found out?”\n\n“I have found out, sir,” answered the man, “that both the women took tickets at our station here for Carlisle.”\n\n“You went to Carlisle, of course, when you heard that?”\n\n“I did, sir, but I am sorry to say I could find no further trace of them.”\n\n“You inquired at the railway?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“And at the different inns?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“And you left the statement I wrote for you at the police station?”\n\n“I did, sir.”\n\n“Well, my friend, you have done all you could, and I have done all I could, and there the matter must rest till further notice. We have played our trump cards, Mr. Hartright,” continued the old gentleman when the servant had withdrawn. “For the present, at least, the women have outmanoeuvred us, and our only resource now is to wait till Sir Percival Glyde comes here on Monday next. Won’t you fill your glass again? Good bottle of port, that—sound, substantial, old wine. I have got better in my own cellar, though.”\n\nWe returned to the drawing-room—the room in which the happiest evenings of my life had been passed—the room which, after this last night, I was never to see again. Its aspect was altered since the days had shortened and the weather had grown cold. The glass doors on the terrace side were closed, and hidden by thick curtains. Instead of the soft twilight obscurity, in which we used to sit, the bright radiant glow of lamplight now dazzled my eyes. All was changed—indoors and out all was changed.\n\nMiss Halcombe and Mr. Gilmore sat down together at the card-table—Mrs. Vesey took her customary chair. There was no restraint on the disposal of THEIR evening, and I felt the restraint on the disposal of mine all the more painfully from observing it. I saw Miss Fairlie lingering near the music-stand. The time had been when I might have joined her there. I waited irresolutely—I knew neither where to go nor what to do next. She cast one quick glance at me, took a piece of music suddenly from the stand, and came towards me of her own accord.\n\n“Shall I play some of those little melodies of Mozart’s which you used to like so much?” she asked, opening the music nervously, and looking down at it while she spoke.\n\nBefore I could thank her she hastened to the piano. The chair near it, which I had always been accustomed to occupy, stood empty. She struck a few chords—then glanced round at me—then looked back again at her music.\n\n“Won’t you take your old place?” she said, speaking very abruptly and in very low tones.\n\n“I may take it on the last night,” I answered.\n\nShe did not reply—she kept her attention riveted on the music—music which she knew by memory, which she had played over and over again, in former times, without the book. I only knew that she had heard me, I only knew that she was aware of my being close to her, by seeing the red spot on the cheek that was nearest to me fade out, and the face grow pale all over.\n\n“I am very sorry you are going,” she said, her voice almost sinking to a whisper, her eyes looking more and more intently at the music, her fingers flying over the keys of the piano with a strange feverish energy which I had never noticed in her before.\n\n“I shall remember those kind words, Miss Fairlie, long after to-morrow has come and gone.”\n\nThe paleness grew whiter on her face, and she turned it farther away from me.\n\n“Don’t speak of to-morrow,” she said. “Let the music speak to us of to-night, in a happier language than ours.”\n\nHer lips trembled—a faint sigh fluttered from them, which she tried vainly to suppress. Her fingers wavered on the piano—she struck a false note, confused herself in trying to set it right, and dropped her hands angrily on her lap. Miss Halcombe and Mr. Gilmore looked up in astonishment from the card-table at which they were playing. Even Mrs. Vesey, dozing in her chair, woke at the sudden cessation of the music, and inquired what had happened.\n\n“You play at whist, Mr. Hartright?” asked Miss Halcombe, with her eyes directed significantly at the place I occupied.\n\nI knew what she meant—I knew she was right, and I rose at once to go to the card-table. As I left the piano Miss Fairlie turned a page of the music, and touched the keys again with a surer hand.\n\n“I WILL play it,” she said, striking the notes almost passionately. “I WILL play it on the last night.”\n\n“Come, Mrs. Vesey,” said Miss Halcombe, “Mr. Gilmore and I are tired of ecarte—come and be Mr. Hartright’s partner at whist.”\n\nThe old lawyer smiled satirically. His had been the winning hand, and he had just turned up a king. He evidently attributed Miss Halcombe’s abrupt change in the card-table arrangements to a lady’s inability to play the losing game.\n\nThe rest of the evening passed without a word or a look from her. She kept her place at the piano, and I kept mine at the card-table. She played unintermittingly—played as if the music was her only refuge from herself. Sometimes her fingers touched the notes with a lingering fondness—a soft, plaintive, dying tenderness, unutterably beautiful and mournful to hear; sometimes they faltered and failed her, or hurried over the instrument mechanically, as if their task was a burden to them. But still, change and waver as they might in the expression they imparted to the music, their resolution to play never faltered. She only rose from the piano when we all rose to say good night.\n\nMrs. Vesey was the nearest to the door, and the first to shake hands with me.\n\n“I shall not see you again, Mr. Hartright,” said the old lady. “I am truly sorry you are going away. You have been very kind and attentive, and an old woman like me feels kindness and attention. I wish you happy, sir—I wish you a kind good-bye.”\n\nMr. Gilmore came next.\n\n“I hope we shall have a future opportunity of bettering our acquaintance, Mr. Hartright. You quite understand about that little matter of business being safe in my hands? Yes, yes, of course. Bless me, how cold it is! Don’t let me keep you at the door. Bon voyage, my dear sir—bon voyage, as the French say.”\n\nMiss Halcombe followed.\n\n“Half-past seven to-morrow morning,” she said—then added in a whisper, “I have heard and seen more than you think. Your conduct to-night has made me your friend for life.”\n\nMiss Fairlie came last. I could not trust myself to look at her when I took her hand, and when I thought of the next morning.\n\n“My departure must be a very early one,” I said. “I shall be gone, Miss Fairlie, before you——”\n\n“No, no,” she interposed hastily, “not before I am out of my room. I shall be down to breakfast with Marian. I am not so ungrateful, not so forgetful of the past three months——”\n\nHer voice failed her, her hand closed gently round mine—then dropped it suddenly. Before I could say “Good-night” she was gone.\n\nThe end comes fast to meet me—comes inevitably, as the light of the last morning came at Limmeridge House.\n\nIt was barely half-past seven when I went downstairs, but I found them both at the breakfast-table waiting for me. In the chill air, in the dim light, in the gloomy morning silence of the house, we three sat down together, and tried to eat, tried to talk. The struggle to preserve appearances was hopeless and useless, and I rose to end it.\n\nAs I held out my hand, as Miss Halcombe, who was nearest to me, took it, Miss Fairlie turned away suddenly and hurried from the room.\n\n“Better so,” said Miss Halcombe, when the door had closed—“better so, for you and for her.”\n\nI waited a moment before I could speak—it was hard to lose her, without a parting word or a parting look. I controlled myself—I tried to take leave of Miss Halcombe in fitting terms; but all the farewell words I would fain have spoken dwindled to one sentence.\n\n“Have I deserved that you should write to me?” was all I could say.\n\n“You have nobly deserved everything that I can do for you, as long as we both live. Whatever the end is you shall know it.”\n\n“And if I can ever be of help again, at any future time, long after the memory of my presumption and my folly is forgotten . . .”\n\nI could add no more. My voice faltered, my eyes moistened in spite of me.\n\nShe caught me by both hands—she pressed them with the strong, steady grasp of a man—her dark eyes glittered—her brown complexion flushed deep—the force and energy of her face glowed and grew beautiful with the pure inner light of her generosity and her pity.\n\n“I will trust you—if ever the time comes I will trust you as my friend and HER friend, as my brother and HER brother.” She stopped, drew me nearer to her—the fearless, noble creature—touched my forehead, sister-like, with her lips, and called me by my Christian name. “God bless you, Walter!” she said. “Wait here alone and compose yourself—I had better not stay for both our sakes—I had better see you go from the balcony upstairs.”\n\nShe left the room. I turned away towards the window, where nothing faced me but the lonely autumn landscape—I turned away to master myself, before I too left the room in my turn, and left it for ever.\n\nA minute passed—it could hardly have been more—when I heard the door open again softly, and the rustling of a woman’s dress on the carpet moved towards me. My heart beat violently as I turned round. Miss Fairlie was approaching me from the farther end of the room.\n\nShe stopped and hesitated when our eyes met, and when she saw that we were alone. Then, with that courage which women lose so often in the small emergency, and so seldom in the great, she came on nearer to me, strangely pale and strangely quiet, drawing one hand after her along the table by which she walked, and holding something at her side in the other, which was hidden by the folds of her dress.\n\n“I only went into the drawing-room,” she said, “to look for this. It may remind you of your visit here, and of the friends you leave behind you. You told me I had improved very much when I did it, and I thought you might like——”\n\nShe turned her head away, and offered me a little sketch, drawn throughout by her own pencil, of the summer-house in which we had first met. The paper trembled in her hand as she held it out to me—trembled in mine as I took it from her.\n\nI was afraid to say what I felt—I only answered, “It shall never leave me—all my life long it shall be the treasure that I prize most. I am very grateful for it—very grateful to you, for not letting me go away without bidding you good-bye.”\n\n“Oh!” she said innocently, “how could I let you go, after we have passed so many happy days together!”\n\n“Those days may never return, Miss Fairlie—my way of life and yours are very far apart. But if a time should come, when the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength will give you a moment’s happiness, or spare you a moment’s sorrow, will you try to remember the poor drawing-master who has taught you? Miss Halcombe has promised to trust me—will you promise too?”\n\nThe farewell sadness in the kind blue eyes shone dimly through her gathering tears.\n\n“I promise it,” she said in broken tones. “Oh, don’t look at me like that! I promise it with all my heart.”\n\nI ventured a little nearer to her, and held out my hand.\n\n“You have many friends who love you, Miss Fairlie. Your happy future is the dear object of many hopes. May I say, at parting, that it is the dear object of MY hopes too?”\n\nThe tears flowed fast down her cheeks. She rested one trembling hand on the table to steady herself while she gave me the other. I took it in mine—I held it fast. My head drooped over it, my tears fell on it, my lips pressed it—not in love; oh, not in love, at that last moment, but in the agony and the self-abandonment of despair.\n\n“For God’s sake, leave me!” she said faintly.\n\nThe confession of her heart’s secret burst from her in those pleading words. I had no right to hear them, no right to answer them—they were the words that banished me, in the name of her sacred weakness, from the room. It was all over. I dropped her hand, I said no more. The blinding tears shut her out from my eyes, and I dashed them away to look at her for the last time. One look as she sank into a chair, as her arms fell on the table, as her fair head dropped on them wearily. One farewell look, and the door had closed upon her—the great gulf of separation had opened between us—the image of Laura Fairlie was a memory of the past already.\n\nThe End of Hartright’s Narrative.\n\n(of Chancery Lane, Solicitor)\n\n# I\n\nI write these lines at the request of my friend, Mr. Walter Hartright. They are intended to convey a description of certain events which seriously affected Miss Fairlie’s interests, and which took place after the period of Mr. Hartright’s departure from Limmeridge House.\n\nThere is no need for me to say whether my own opinion does or does not sanction the disclosure of the remarkable family story, of which my narrative forms an important component part. Mr. Hartright has taken that responsibility on himself, and circumstances yet to be related will show that he has amply earned the right to do so, if he chooses to exercise it. The plan he has adopted for presenting the story to others, in the most truthful and most vivid manner, requires that it should be told, at each successive stage in the march of events, by the persons who were directly concerned in those events at the time of their occurrence. My appearance here, as narrator, is the necessary consequence of this arrangement. I was present during the sojourn of Sir Percival Glyde in Cumberland, and was personally concerned in one important result of his short residence under Mr. Fairlie’s roof. It is my duty, therefore, to add these new links to the chain of events, and to take up the chain itself at the point where, for the present only, Mr. Hartright has dropped it.\n\nI arrived at Limmeridge House on Friday the second of November.\n\nMy object was to remain at Mr. Fairlie’s until the arrival of Sir Percival Glyde. If that event led to the appointment of any given day for Sir Percival’s union with Miss Fairlie, I was to take the necessary instructions back with me to London, and to occupy myself in drawing the lady’s marriage-settlement.\n\nOn the Friday I was not favoured by Mr. Fairlie with an interview. He had been, or had fancied himself to be, an invalid for years past, and he was not well enough to receive me. Miss Halcombe was the first member of the family whom I saw. She met me at the house door, and introduced me to Mr. Hartright, who had been staying at Limmeridge for some time past.\n\nI did not see Miss Fairlie until later in the day, at dinner-time. She was not looking well, and I was sorry to observe it. She is a sweet lovable girl, as amiable and attentive to every one about her as her excellent mother used to be—though, personally speaking, she takes after her father. Mrs. Fairlie had dark eyes and hair, and her elder daughter, Miss Halcombe, strongly reminds me of her. Miss Fairlie played to us in the evening—not so well as usual, I thought. We had a rubber at whist, a mere profanation, so far as play was concerned, of that noble game. I had been favourably impressed by Mr. Hartright on our first introduction to one another, but I soon discovered that he was not free from the social failings incidental to his age. There are three things that none of the young men of the present generation can do. They can’t sit over their wine, they can’t play at whist, and they can’t pay a lady a compliment. Mr. Hartright was no exception to the general rule. Otherwise, even in those early days and on that short acquaintance, he struck me as being a modest and gentlemanlike young man.\n\nSo the Friday passed. I say nothing about the more serious matters which engaged my attention on that day—the anonymous letter to Miss Fairlie, the measures I thought it right to adopt when the matter was mentioned to me, and the conviction I entertained that every possible explanation of the circumstances would be readily afforded by Sir Percival Glyde, having all been fully noticed, as I understand, in the narrative which precedes this.\n\nOn the Saturday Mr. Hartright had left before I got down to breakfast. Miss Fairlie kept her room all day, and Miss Halcombe appeared to me to be out of spirits. The house was not what it used to be in the time of Mr. and Mrs. Philip Fairlie. I took a walk by myself in the forenoon, and looked about at some of the places which I first saw when I was staying at Limmeridge to transact family business, more than thirty years since. They were not what they used to be either.\n\nAt two o’clock Mr. Fairlie sent to say he was well enough to see me. HE had not altered, at any rate, since I first knew him. His talk was to the same purpose as usual—all about himself and his ailments, his wonderful coins, and his matchless Rembrandt etchings. The moment I tried to speak of the business that had brought me to his house, he shut his eyes and said I “upset” him. I persisted in upsetting him by returning again and again to the subject. All I could ascertain was that he looked on his niece’s marriage as a settled thing, that her father had sanctioned it, that he sanctioned it himself, that it was a desirable marriage, and that he should be personally rejoiced when the worry of it was over. As to the settlements, if I would consult his niece, and afterwards dive as deeply as I pleased into my own knowledge of the family affairs, and get everything ready, and limit his share in the business, as guardian, to saying Yes, at the right moment—why, of course he would meet my views, and everybody else’s views, with infinite pleasure. In the meantime, there I saw him, a helpless sufferer, confined to his room. Did I think he looked as if he wanted teasing? No. Then why tease him?\n\nI might, perhaps, have been a little astonished at this extraordinary absence of all self-assertion on Mr. Fairlie’s part, in the character of guardian, if my knowledge of the family affairs had not been sufficient to remind me that he was a single man, and that he had nothing more than a life-interest in the Limmeridge property. As matters stood, therefore, I was neither surprised nor disappointed at the result of the interview. Mr. Fairlie had simply justified my expectations—and there was an end of it.\n\nSunday was a dull day, out of doors and in. A letter arrived for me from Sir Percival Glyde’s solicitor, acknowledging the receipt of my copy of the anonymous letter and my accompanying statement of the case. Miss Fairlie joined us in the afternoon, looking pale and depressed, and altogether unlike herself. I had some talk with her, and ventured on a delicate allusion to Sir Percival. She listened and said nothing. All other subjects she pursued willingly, but this subject she allowed to drop. I began to doubt whether she might not be repenting of her engagement—just as young ladies often do, when repentance comes too late.\n\nOn Monday Sir Percival Glyde arrived.\n\nI found him to be a most prepossessing man, so far as manners and appearance were concerned. He looked rather older than I had expected, his head being bald over the forehead, and his face somewhat marked and worn, but his movements were as active and his spirits as high as a young man’s. His meeting with Miss Halcombe was delightfully hearty and unaffected, and his reception of me, upon my being presented to him, was so easy and pleasant that we got on together like old friends. Miss Fairlie was not with us when he arrived, but she entered the room about ten minutes afterwards. Sir Percival rose and paid his compliments with perfect grace. His evident concern on seeing the change for the worse in the young lady’s looks was expressed with a mixture of tenderness and respect, with an unassuming delicacy of tone, voice, and manner, which did equal credit to his good breeding and his good sense. I was rather surprised, under these circumstances, to see that Miss Fairlie continued to be constrained and uneasy in his presence, and that she took the first opportunity of leaving the room again. Sir Percival neither noticed the restraint in her reception of him, nor her sudden withdrawal from our society. He had not obtruded his attentions on her while she was present, and he did not embarrass Miss Halcombe by any allusion to her departure when she was gone. His tact and taste were never at fault on this or on any other occasion while I was in his company at Limmeridge House.\n\nAs soon as Miss Fairlie had left the room he spared us all embarrassment on the subject of the anonymous letter, by adverting to it of his own accord. He had stopped in London on his way from Hampshire, had seen his solicitor, had read the documents forwarded by me, and had travelled on to Cumberland, anxious to satisfy our minds by the speediest and the fullest explanation that words could convey. On hearing him express himself to this effect, I offered him the original letter, which I had kept for his inspection. He thanked me, and declined to look at it, saying that he had seen the copy, and that he was quite willing to leave the original in our hands.\n\nThe statement itself, on which he immediately entered, was as simple and satisfactory as I had all along anticipated it would be.\n\nMrs. Catherick, he informed us, had in past years laid him under some obligations for faithful services rendered to his family connections and to himself. She had been doubly unfortunate in being married to a husband who had deserted her, and in having an only child whose mental faculties had been in a disturbed condition from a very early age. Although her marriage had removed her to a part of Hampshire far distant from the neighbourhood in which Sir Percival’s property was situated, he had taken care not to lose sight of her—his friendly feeling towards the poor woman, in consideration of her past services, having been greatly strengthened by his admiration of the patience and courage with which she supported her calamities. In course of time the symptoms of mental affliction in her unhappy daughter increased to such a serious extent, as to make it a matter of necessity to place her under proper medical care. Mrs. Catherick herself recognised this necessity, but she also felt the prejudice common to persons occupying her respectable station, against allowing her child to be admitted, as a pauper, into a public Asylum. Sir Percival had respected this prejudice, as he respected honest independence of feeling in any rank of life, and had resolved to mark his grateful sense of Mrs. Catherick’s early attachment to the interests of himself and his family, by defraying the expense of her daughter’s maintenance in a trustworthy private Asylum. To her mother’s regret, and to his own regret, the unfortunate creature had discovered the share which circumstances had induced him to take in placing her under restraint, and had conceived the most intense hatred and distrust of him in consequence. To that hatred and distrust—which had expressed itself in various ways in the Asylum—the anonymous letter, written after her escape, was plainly attributable. If Miss Halcombe’s or Mr. Gilmore’s recollection of the document did not confirm that view, or if they wished for any additional particulars about the Asylum (the address of which he mentioned, as well as the names and addresses of the two doctors on whose certificates the patient was admitted), he was ready to answer any question and to clear up any uncertainty. He had done his duty to the unhappy young woman, by instructing his solicitor to spare no expense in tracing her, and in restoring her once more to medical care, and he was now only anxious to do his duty towards Miss Fairlie and towards her family, in the same plain, straightforward way.\n\nI was the first to speak in answer to this appeal. My own course was plain to me. It is the great beauty of the Law that it can dispute any human statement, made under any circumstances, and reduced to any form. If I had felt professionally called upon to set up a case against Sir Percival Glyde, on the strength of his own explanation, I could have done so beyond all doubt. But my duty did not lie in this direction—my function was of the purely judicial kind. I was to weigh the explanation we had just heard, to allow all due force to the high reputation of the gentleman who offered it, and to decide honestly whether the probabilities, on Sir Percival’s own showing, were plainly with him, or plainly against him. My own conviction was that they were plainly with him, and I accordingly declared that his explanation was, to my mind, unquestionably a satisfactory one.\n\nMiss Halcombe, after looking at me very earnestly, said a few words, on her side, to the same effect—with a certain hesitation of manner, however, which the circumstances did not seem to me to warrant. I am unable to say, positively, whether Sir Percival noticed this or not. My opinion is that he did, seeing that he pointedly resumed the subject, although he might now, with all propriety, have allowed it to drop.\n\n“If my plain statement of facts had only been addressed to Mr. Gilmore,” he said, “I should consider any further reference to this unhappy matter as unnecessary. I may fairly expect Mr. Gilmore, as a gentleman, to believe me on my word, and when he has done me that justice, all discussion of the subject between us has come to an end. But my position with a lady is not the same. I owe to her—what I would concede to no man alive—a PROOF of the truth of my assertion. You cannot ask for that proof, Miss Halcombe, and it is therefore my duty to you, and still more to Miss Fairlie, to offer it. May I beg that you will write at once to the mother of this unfortunate woman—to Mrs. Catherick—to ask for her testimony in support of the explanation which I have just offered to you.”\n\nI saw Miss Halcombe change colour, and look a little uneasy. Sir Percival’s suggestion, politely as it was expressed, appeared to her, as it appeared to me, to point very delicately at the hesitation which her manner had betrayed a moment or two since.\n\n“I hope, Sir Percival, you don’t do me the injustice to suppose that I distrust you,” she said quickly.\n\n“Certainly not, Miss Halcombe. I make my proposal purely as an act of attention to YOU. Will you excuse my obstinacy if I still venture to press it?”\n\nHe walked to the writing-table as he spoke, drew a chair to it, and opened the paper case.\n\n“Let me beg you to write the note,” he said, “as a favour to ME. It need not occupy you more than a few minutes. You have only to ask Mrs. Catherick two questions. First, if her daughter was placed in the Asylum with her knowledge and approval. Secondly, if the share I took in the matter was such as to merit the expression of her gratitude towards myself? Mr. Gilmore’s mind is at ease on this unpleasant subject, and your mind is at ease—pray set my mind at ease also by writing the note.”\n\n“You oblige me to grant your request, Sir Percival, when I would much rather refuse it.”\n\nWith those words Miss Halcombe rose from her place and went to the writing-table. Sir Percival thanked her, handed her a pen, and then walked away towards the fireplace. Miss Fairlie’s little Italian greyhound was lying on the rug. He held out his hand, and called to the dog good-humouredly.\n\n“Come, Nina,” he said, “we remember each other, don’t we?”\n\nThe little beast, cowardly and cross-grained, as pet-dogs usually are, looked up at him sharply, shrank away from his outstretched hand, whined, shivered, and hid itself under a sofa. It was scarcely possible that he could have been put out by such a trifle as a dog’s reception of him, but I observed, nevertheless, that he walked away towards the window very suddenly. Perhaps his temper is irritable at times. If so, I can sympathise with him. My temper is irritable at times too.\n\nMiss Halcombe was not long in writing the note. When it was done she rose from the writing-table, and handed the open sheet of paper to Sir Percival. He bowed, took it from her, folded it up immediately without looking at the contents, sealed it, wrote the address, and handed it back to her in silence. I never saw anything more gracefully and more becomingly done in my life.\n\n“You insist on my posting this letter, Sir Percival?” said Miss Halcombe.\n\n“I beg you will post it,” he answered. “And now that it is written and sealed up, allow me to ask one or two last questions about the unhappy woman to whom it refers. I have read the communication which Mr. Gilmore kindly addressed to my solicitor, describing the circumstances under which the writer of the anonymous letter was identified. But there are certain points to which that statement does not refer. Did Anne Catherick see Miss Fairlie?”\n\n“Certainly not,” replied Miss Halcombe.\n\n“Did she see you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“She saw nobody from the house then, except a certain Mr. Hartright, who accidentally met with her in the churchyard here?”\n\n“Nobody else.”\n\n“Mr. Hartright was employed at Limmeridge as a drawing-master, I believe? Is he a member of one of the Water-Colour Societies?”\n\n“I believe he is,” answered Miss Halcombe.\n\nHe paused for a moment, as if he was thinking over the last answer, and then added—\n\n“Did you find out where Anne Catherick was living, when she was in this neighbourhood?”\n\n“Yes. At a farm on the moor, called Todd’s Corner.”\n\n“It is a duty we all owe to the poor creature herself to trace her,” continued Sir Percival. “She may have said something at Todd’s Corner which may help us to find her. I will go there and make inquiries on the chance. In the meantime, as I cannot prevail on myself to discuss this painful subject with Miss Fairlie, may I beg, Miss Halcombe, that you will kindly undertake to give her the necessary explanation, deferring it of course until you have received the reply to that note.”\n\nMiss Halcombe promised to comply with his request. He thanked her, nodded pleasantly, and left us, to go and establish himself in his own room. As he opened the door the cross-grained greyhound poked out her sharp muzzle from under the sofa, and barked and snapped at him.\n\n“A good morning’s work, Miss Halcombe,” I said, as soon as we were alone. “Here is an anxious day well ended already.”\n\n“Yes,” she answered; “no doubt. I am very glad your mind is satisfied.”\n\n“My mind! Surely, with that note in your hand, your mind is at ease too?”\n\n“Oh yes—how can it be otherwise? I know the thing could not be,” she went on, speaking more to herself than to me; “but I almost wish Walter Hartright had stayed here long enough to be present at the explanation, and to hear the proposal to me to write this note.”\n\nI was a little surprised—perhaps a little piqued also—by these last words.\n\n“Events, it is true, connected Mr. Hartright very remarkably with the affair of the letter,” I said; “and I readily admit that he conducted himself, all things considered, with great delicacy and discretion. But I am quite at a loss to understand what useful influence his presence could have exercised in relation to the effect of Sir Percival’s statement on your mind or mine.”\n\n“It was only a fancy,” she said absently. “There is no need to discuss it, Mr. Gilmore. Your experience ought to be, and is, the best guide I can desire.”\n\nI did not altogether like her thrusting the whole responsibility, in this marked manner, on my shoulders. If Mr. Fairlie had done it, I should not have been surprised. But resolute, clear-minded Miss Halcombe was the very last person in the world whom I should have expected to find shrinking from the expression of an opinion of her own.\n\n“If any doubts still trouble you,” I said, “why not mention them to me at once? Tell me plainly, have you any reason to distrust Sir Percival Glyde?”\n\n“None whatever.”\n\n“Do you see anything improbable, or contradictory, in his explanation?”\n\n“How can I say I do, after the proof he has offered me of the truth of it? Can there be better testimony in his favour, Mr. Gilmore, than the testimony of the woman’s mother?”\n\n“None better. If the answer to your note of inquiry proves to be satisfactory, I for one cannot see what more any friend of Sir Percival’s can possibly expect from him.”\n\n“Then we will post the note,” she said, rising to leave the room, “and dismiss all further reference to the subject until the answer arrives. Don’t attach any weight to my hesitation. I can give no better reason for it than that I have been over-anxious about Laura lately—and anxiety, Mr. Gilmore, unsettles the strongest of us.”\n\nShe left me abruptly, her naturally firm voice faltering as she spoke those last words. A sensitive, vehement, passionate nature—a woman of ten thousand in these trivial, superficial times. I had known her from her earliest years—I had seen her tested, as she grew up, in more than one trying family crisis, and my long experience made me attach an importance to her hesitation under the circumstances here detailed, which I should certainly not have felt in the case of another woman. I could see no cause for any uneasiness or any doubt, but she had made me a little uneasy, and a little doubtful, nevertheless. In my youth, I should have chafed and fretted under the irritation of my own unreasonable state of mind. In my age, I knew better, and went out philosophically to walk it off.\n\nWe all met again at dinner-time.\n\nSir Percival was in such boisterous high spirits that I hardly recognised him as the same man whose quiet tact, refinement, and good sense had impressed me so strongly at the interview of the morning. The only trace of his former self that I could detect reappeared, every now and then, in his manner towards Miss Fairlie. A look or a word from her suspended his loudest laugh, checked his gayest flow of talk, and rendered him all attention to her, and to no one else at table, in an instant. Although he never openly tried to draw her into the conversation, he never lost the slightest chance she gave him of letting her drift into it by accident, and of saying the words to her, under those favourable circumstances, which a man with less tact and delicacy would have pointedly addressed to her the moment they occurred to him. Rather to my surprise, Miss Fairlie appeared to be sensible of his attentions without being moved by them. She was a little confused from time to time when he looked at her, or spoke to her; but she never warmed towards him. Rank, fortune, good breeding, good looks, the respect of a gentleman, and the devotion of a lover were all humbly placed at her feet, and, so far as appearances went, were all offered in vain.\n\nOn the next day, the Tuesday, Sir Percival went in the morning (taking one of the servants with him as a guide) to Todd’s Corner. His inquiries, as I afterwards heard, led to no results. On his return he had an interview with Mr. Fairlie, and in the afternoon he and Miss Halcombe rode out together. Nothing else happened worthy of record. The evening passed as usual. There was no change in Sir Percival, and no change in Miss Fairlie.\n\nThe Wednesday’s post brought with it an event—the reply from Mrs. Catherick. I took a copy of the document, which I have preserved, and which I may as well present in this place. It ran as follows—\n\n“MADAM,—I beg to acknowledge the receipt of your letter, inquiring whether my daughter, Anne, was placed under medical superintendence with my knowledge and approval, and whether the share taken in the matter by Sir Percival Glyde was such as to merit the expression of my gratitude towards that gentleman. Be pleased to accept my answer in the affirmative to both those questions, and believe me to remain, your obedient servant,\n\n“JANE ANNE CATHERICK.”\n\nShort, sharp, and to the point; in form rather a business-like letter for a woman to write—in substance as plain a confirmation as could be desired of Sir Percival Glyde’s statement. This was my opinion, and with certain minor reservations, Miss Halcombe’s opinion also. Sir Percival, when the letter was shown to him, did not appear to be struck by the sharp, short tone of it. He told us that Mrs. Catherick was a woman of few words, a clear-headed, straightforward, unimaginative person, who wrote briefly and plainly, just as she spoke.\n\nThe next duty to be accomplished, now that the answer had been received, was to acquaint Miss Fairlie with Sir Percival’s explanation. Miss Halcombe had undertaken to do this, and had left the room to go to her sister, when she suddenly returned again, and sat down by the easy-chair in which I was reading the newspaper. Sir Percival had gone out a minute before to look at the stables, and no one was in the room but ourselves.\n\n“I suppose we have really and truly done all we can?” she said, turning and twisting Mrs. Catherick’s letter in her hand.\n\n“If we are friends of Sir Percival’s, who know him and trust him, we have done all, and more than all, that is necessary,” I answered, a little annoyed by this return of her hesitation. “But if we are enemies who suspect him——”\n\n“That alternative is not even to be thought of,” she interposed. “We are Sir Percival’s friends, and if generosity and forbearance can add to our regard for him, we ought to be Sir Percival’s admirers as well. You know that he saw Mr. Fairlie yesterday, and that he afterwards went out with me.”\n\n“Yes. I saw you riding away together.”\n\n“We began the ride by talking about Anne Catherick, and about the singular manner in which Mr. Hartright met with her. But we soon dropped that subject, and Sir Percival spoke next, in the most unselfish terms, of his engagement with Laura. He said he had observed that she was out of spirits, and he was willing, if not informed to the contrary, to attribute to that cause the alteration in her manner towards him during his present visit. If, however, there was any more serious reason for the change, he would entreat that no constraint might be placed on her inclinations either by Mr. Fairlie or by me. All he asked, in that case, was that she would recall to mind, for the last time, what the circumstances were under which the engagement between them was made, and what his conduct had been from the beginning of the courtship to the present time. If, after due reflection on those two subjects, she seriously desired that he should withdraw his pretensions to the honour of becoming her husband—and if she would tell him so plainly with her own lips—he would sacrifice himself by leaving her perfectly free to withdraw from the engagement.”\n\n“No man could say more than that, Miss Halcombe. As to my experience, few men in his situation would have said as much.”\n\nShe paused after I had spoken those words, and looked at me with a singular expression of perplexity and distress.\n\n“I accuse nobody, and I suspect nothing,” she broke out abruptly. “But I cannot and will not accept the responsibility of persuading Laura to this marriage.”\n\n“That is exactly the course which Sir Percival Glyde has himself requested you to take,” I replied in astonishment. “He has begged you not to force her inclinations.”\n\n“And he indirectly obliges me to force them, if I give her his message.”\n\n“How can that possibly be?”\n\n“Consult your own knowledge of Laura, Mr. Gilmore. If I tell her to reflect on the circumstances of her engagement, I at once appeal to two of the strongest feelings in her nature—to her love for her father’s memory, and to her strict regard for truth. You know that she never broke a promise in her life—you know that she entered on this engagement at the beginning of her father’s fatal illness, and that he spoke hopefully and happily of her marriage to Sir Percival Glyde on his deathbed.”\n\nI own that I was a little shocked at this view of the case.\n\n“Surely,” I said, “you don’t mean to infer that when Sir Percival spoke to you yesterday he speculated on such a result as you have just mentioned?”\n\nHer frank, fearless face answered for her before she spoke.\n\n“Do you think I would remain an instant in the company of any man whom I suspected of such baseness as that?” she asked angrily.\n\nI liked to feel her hearty indignation flash out on me in that way. We see so much malice and so little indignation in my profession.\n\n“In that case,” I said, “excuse me if I tell you, in our legal phrase, that you are travelling out of the record. Whatever the consequences may be, Sir Percival has a right to expect that your sister should carefully consider her engagement from every reasonable point of view before she claims her release from it. If that unlucky letter has prejudiced her against him, go at once, and tell her that he has cleared himself in your eyes and in mine. What objection can she urge against him after that? What excuse can she possibly have for changing her mind about a man whom she had virtually accepted for her husband more than two years ago?”\n\n“In the eyes of law and reason, Mr. Gilmore, no excuse, I daresay. If she still hesitates, and if I still hesitate, you must attribute our strange conduct, if you like, to caprice in both cases, and we must bear the imputation as well as we can.”\n\nWith those words she suddenly rose and left me. When a sensible woman has a serious question put to her, and evades it by a flippant answer, it is a sure sign, in ninety-nine cases out of a hundred, that she has something to conceal. I returned to the perusal of the newspaper, strongly suspecting that Miss Halcombe and Miss Fairlie had a secret between them which they were keeping from Sir Percival, and keeping from me. I thought this hard on both of us, especially on Sir Percival.\n\nMy doubts—or to speak more correctly, my convictions—were confirmed by Miss Halcombe’s language and manner when I saw her again later in the day. She was suspiciously brief and reserved in telling me the result of her interview with her sister. Miss Fairlie, it appeared, had listened quietly while the affair of the letter was placed before her in the right point of view, but when Miss Halcombe next proceeded to say that the object of Sir Percival’s visit at Limmeridge was to prevail on her to let a day be fixed for the marriage she checked all further reference to the subject by begging for time. If Sir Percival would consent to spare her for the present, she would undertake to give him his final answer before the end of the year. She pleaded for this delay with such anxiety and agitation, that Miss Halcombe had promised to use her influence, if necessary, to obtain it, and there, at Miss Fairlie’s earnest entreaty, all further discussion of the marriage question had ended.\n\nThe purely temporary arrangement thus proposed might have been convenient enough to the young lady, but it proved somewhat embarrassing to the writer of these lines. That morning’s post had brought a letter from my partner, which obliged me to return to town the next day by the afternoon train. It was extremely probable that I should find no second opportunity of presenting myself at Limmeridge House during the remainder of the year. In that case, supposing Miss Fairlie ultimately decided on holding to her engagement, my necessary personal communication with her, before I drew her settlement, would become something like a downright impossibility, and we should be obliged to commit to writing questions which ought always to be discussed on both sides by word of mouth. I said nothing about this difficulty until Sir Percival had been consulted on the subject of the desired delay. He was too gallant a gentleman not to grant the request immediately. When Miss Halcombe informed me of this I told her that I must absolutely speak to her sister before I left Limmeridge, and it was, therefore, arranged that I should see Miss Fairlie in her own sitting-room the next morning. She did not come down to dinner, or join us in the evening. Indisposition was the excuse, and I thought Sir Percival looked, as well he might, a little annoyed when he heard of it.\n\nThe next morning, as soon as breakfast was over, I went up to Miss Fairlie’s sitting-room. The poor girl looked so pale and sad, and came forward to welcome me so readily and prettily, that the resolution to lecture her on her caprice and indecision, which I had been forming all the way upstairs, failed me on the spot. I led her back to the chair from which she had risen, and placed myself opposite to her. Her cross-grained pet greyhound was in the room, and I fully expected a barking and snapping reception. Strange to say, the whimsical little brute falsified my expectations by jumping into my lap and poking its sharp muzzle familiarly into my hand the moment I sat down.\n\n“You used often to sit on my knee when you were a child, my dear,” I said, “and now your little dog seems determined to succeed you in the vacant throne. Is that pretty drawing your doing?”\n\nI pointed to a little album which lay on the table by her side and which she had evidently been looking over when I came in. The page that lay open had a small water-colour landscape very neatly mounted on it. This was the drawing which had suggested my question—an idle question enough—but how could I begin to talk of business to her the moment I opened my lips?\n\n“No,” she said, looking away from the drawing rather confusedly, “it is not my doing.”\n\nHer fingers had a restless habit, which I remembered in her as a child, of always playing with the first thing that came to hand whenever any one was talking to her. On this occasion they wandered to the album, and toyed absently about the margin of the little water-colour drawing. The expression of melancholy deepened on her face. She did not look at the drawing, or look at me. Her eyes moved uneasily from object to object in the room, betraying plainly that she suspected what my purpose was in coming to speak to her. Seeing that, I thought it best to get to the purpose with as little delay as possible.\n\n“One of the errands, my dear, which brings me here is to bid you good-bye,” I began. “I must get back to London to-day: and, before I leave, I want to have a word with you on the subject of your own affairs.”\n\n“I am very sorry you are going, Mr. Gilmore,” she said, looking at me kindly. “It is like the happy old times to have you here.”\n\n“I hope I may be able to come back and recall those pleasant memories once more,” I continued; “but as there is some uncertainty about the future, I must take my opportunity when I can get it, and speak to you now. I am your old lawyer and your old friend, and I may remind you, I am sure, without offence, of the possibility of your marrying Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nShe took her hand off the little album as suddenly as if it had turned hot and burnt her. Her fingers twined together nervously in her lap, her eyes looked down again at the floor, and an expression of constraint settled on her face which looked almost like an expression of pain.\n\n“Is it absolutely necessary to speak of my marriage engagement?” she asked in low tones.\n\n“It is necessary to refer to it,” I answered, “but not to dwell on it. Let us merely say that you may marry, or that you may not marry. In the first case, I must be prepared, beforehand, to draw your settlement, and I ought not to do that without, as a matter of politeness, first consulting you. This may be my only chance of hearing what your wishes are. Let us, therefore, suppose the case of your marrying, and let me inform you, in as few words as possible, what your position is now, and what you may make it, if you please, in the future.”\n\nI explained to her the object of a marriage-settlement, and then told her exactly what her prospects were—in the first place, on her coming of age, and in the second place, on the decease of her uncle—marking the distinction between the property in which she had a life-interest only, and the property which was left at her own control. She listened attentively, with the constrained expression still on her face, and her hands still nervously clasped together in her lap.\n\n“And now,” I said in conclusion, “tell me if you can think of any condition which, in the case we have supposed, you would wish me to make for you—subject, of course, to your guardian’s approval, as you are not yet of age.”\n\nShe moved uneasily in her chair, then looked in my face on a sudden very earnestly.\n\n“If it does happen,” she began faintly, “if I am——”\n\n“If you are married,” I added, helping her out.\n\n“Don’t let him part me from Marian,” she cried, with a sudden outbreak of energy. “Oh, Mr. Gilmore, pray make it law that Marian is to live with me!”\n\nUnder other circumstances I might, perhaps, have been amused at this essentially feminine interpretation of my question, and of the long explanation which had preceded it. But her looks and tones, when she spoke, were of a kind to make me more than serious—they distressed me. Her words, few as they were, betrayed a desperate clinging to the past which boded ill for the future.\n\n“Your having Marian Halcombe to live with you can easily be settled by private arrangement,” I said. “You hardly understood my question, I think. It referred to your own property—to the disposal of your money. Supposing you were to make a will when you come of age, who would you like the money to go to?”\n\n“Marian has been mother and sister both to me,” said the good, affectionate girl, her pretty blue eyes glistening while she spoke. “May I leave it to Marian, Mr. Gilmore?”\n\n“Certainly, my love,” I answered. “But remember what a large sum it is. Would you like it all to go to Miss Halcombe?”\n\nShe hesitated; her colour came and went, and her hand stole back again to the little album.\n\n“Not all of it,” she said. “There is some one else besides Marian——”\n\nShe stopped; her colour heightened, and the fingers of the hand that rested upon the album beat gently on the margin of the drawing, as if her memory had set them going mechanically with the remembrance of a favourite tune.\n\n“You mean some other member of the family besides Miss Halcombe?” I suggested, seeing her at a loss to proceed.\n\nThe heightening colour spread to her forehead and her neck, and the nervous fingers suddenly clasped themselves fast round the edge of the book.\n\n“There is some one else,” she said, not noticing my last words, though she had evidently heard them; “there is some one else who might like a little keepsake if—if I might leave it. There would be no harm if I should die first——”\n\nShe paused again. The colour that had spread over her cheeks suddenly, as suddenly left them. The hand on the album resigned its hold, trembled a little, and moved the book away from her. She looked at me for an instant—then turned her head aside in the chair. Her handkerchief fell to the floor as she changed her position, and she hurriedly hid her face from me in her hands.\n\nSad! To remember her, as I did, the liveliest, happiest child that ever laughed the day through, and to see her now, in the flower of her age and her beauty, so broken and so brought down as this!\n\nIn the distress that she caused me I forgot the years that had passed, and the change they had made in our position towards one another. I moved my chair close to her, and picked up her handkerchief from the carpet, and drew her hands from her face gently. “Don’t cry, my love,” I said, and dried the tears that were gathering in her eyes with my own hand, as if she had been the little Laura Fairlie of ten long years ago.\n\nIt was the best way I could have taken to compose her. She laid her head on my shoulder, and smiled faintly through her tears.\n\n“I am very sorry for forgetting myself,” she said artlessly. “I have not been well—I have felt sadly weak and nervous lately, and I often cry without reason when I am alone. I am better now—I can answer you as I ought, Mr. Gilmore, I can indeed.”\n\n“No, no, my dear,” I replied, “we will consider the subject as done with for the present. You have said enough to sanction my taking the best possible care of your interests, and we can settle details at another opportunity. Let us have done with business now, and talk of something else.”\n\nI led her at once into speaking on other topics. In ten minutes’ time she was in better spirits, and I rose to take my leave.\n\n“Come here again,” she said earnestly. “I will try to be worthier of your kind feeling for me and for my interests if you will only come again.”\n\nStill clinging to the past—that past which I represented to her, in my way, as Miss Halcombe did in hers! It troubled me sorely to see her looking back, at the beginning of her career, just as I look back at the end of mine.\n\n“If I do come again, I hope I shall find you better,” I said; “better and happier. God bless you, my dear!”\n\nShe only answered by putting up her cheek to me to be kissed. Even lawyers have hearts, and mine ached a little as I took leave of her.\n\nThe whole interview between us had hardly lasted more than half an hour—she had not breathed a word, in my presence, to explain the mystery of her evident distress and dismay at the prospect of her marriage, and yet she had contrived to win me over to her side of the question, I neither knew how nor why. I had entered the room, feeling that Sir Percival Glyde had fair reason to complain of the manner in which she was treating him. I left it, secretly hoping that matters might end in her taking him at his word and claiming her release. A man of my age and experience ought to have known better than to vacillate in this unreasonable manner. I can make no excuse for myself; I can only tell the truth, and say—so it was.\n\nThe hour for my departure was now drawing near. I sent to Mr. Fairlie to say that I would wait on him to take leave if he liked, but that he must excuse my being rather in a hurry. He sent a message back, written in pencil on a slip of paper: “Kind love and best wishes, dear Gilmore. Hurry of any kind is inexpressibly injurious to me. Pray take care of yourself. Good-bye.”\n\nJust before I left I saw Miss Halcombe for a moment alone.\n\n“Have you said all you wanted to Laura?” she asked.\n\n“Yes,” I replied. “She is very weak and nervous—I am glad she has you to take care of her.”\n\nMiss Halcombe’s sharp eyes studied my face attentively.\n\n“You are altering your opinion about Laura,” she said. “You are readier to make allowances for her than you were yesterday.”\n\nNo sensible man ever engages, unprepared, in a fencing match of words with a woman. I only answered—\n\n“Let me know what happens. I will do nothing till I hear from you.”\n\nShe still looked hard in my face. “I wish it was all over, and well over, Mr. Gilmore—and so do you.” With those words she left me.\n\nSir Percival most politely insisted on seeing me to the carriage door.\n\n“If you are ever in my neighbourhood,” he said, “pray don’t forget that I am sincerely anxious to improve our acquaintance. The tried and trusted old friend of this family will be always a welcome visitor in any house of mine.”\n\nA really irresistible man—courteous, considerate, delightfully free from pride—a gentleman, every inch of him. As I drove away to the station I felt as if I could cheerfully do anything to promote the interests of Sir Percival Glyde—anything in the world, except drawing the marriage settlement of his wife.\n\n# III\n\nA week passed, after my return to London, without the receipt of any communication from Miss Halcombe.\n\nOn the eighth day a letter in her handwriting was placed among the other letters on my table.\n\nIt announced that Sir Percival Glyde had been definitely accepted, and that the marriage was to take place, as he had originally desired, before the end of the year. In all probability the ceremony would be performed during the last fortnight in December. Miss Fairlie’s twenty-first birthday was late in March. She would, therefore, by this arrangement, become Sir Percival’s wife about three months before she was of age.\n\nI ought not to have been surprised, I ought not to have been sorry, but I was surprised and sorry, nevertheless. Some little disappointment, caused by the unsatisfactory shortness of Miss Halcombe’s letter, mingled itself with these feelings, and contributed its share towards upsetting my serenity for the day. In six lines my correspondent announced the proposed marriage—in three more, she told me that Sir Percival had left Cumberland to return to his house in Hampshire, and in two concluding sentences she informed me, first, that Laura was sadly in want of change and cheerful society; secondly, that she had resolved to try the effect of some such change forthwith, by taking her sister away with her on a visit to certain old friends in Yorkshire. There the letter ended, without a word to explain what the circumstances were which had decided Miss Fairlie to accept Sir Percival Glyde in one short week from the time when I had last seen her.\n\nAt a later period the cause of this sudden determination was fully explained to me. It is not my business to relate it imperfectly, on hearsay evidence. The circumstances came within the personal experience of Miss Halcombe, and when her narrative succeeds mine, she will describe them in every particular exactly as they happened. In the meantime, the plain duty for me to perform—before I, in my turn, lay down my pen and withdraw from the story—is to relate the one remaining event connected with Miss Fairlie’s proposed marriage in which I was concerned, namely, the drawing of the settlement.\n\nIt is impossible to refer intelligibly to this document without first entering into certain particulars in relation to the bride’s pecuniary affairs. I will try to make my explanation briefly and plainly, and to keep it free from professional obscurities and technicalities. The matter is of the utmost importance. I warn all readers of these lines that Miss Fairlie’s inheritance is a very serious part of Miss Fairlie’s story, and that Mr. Gilmore’s experience, in this particular, must be their experience also, if they wish to understand the narratives which are yet to come.\n\nMiss Fairlie’s expectations, then, were of a twofold kind, comprising her possible inheritance of real property, or land, when her uncle died, and her absolute inheritance of personal property, or money, when she came of age.\n\nLet us take the land first.\n\nIn the time of Miss Fairlie’s paternal grandfather (whom we will call Mr. Fairlie, the elder) the entailed succession to the Limmeridge estate stood thus—\n\nMr. Fairlie, the elder, died and left three sons, Philip, Frederick, and Arthur. As eldest son, Philip succeeded to the estate, if he died without leaving a son, the property went to the second brother, Frederick; and if Frederick died also without leaving a son, the property went to the third brother, Arthur.\n\nAs events turned out, Mr. Philip Fairlie died leaving an only daughter, the Laura of this story, and the estate, in consequence, went, in course of law, to the second brother, Frederick, a single man. The third brother, Arthur, had died many years before the decease of Philip, leaving a son and a daughter. The son, at the age of eighteen, was drowned at Oxford. His death left Laura, the daughter of Mr. Philip Fairlie, presumptive heiress to the estate, with every chance of succeeding to it, in the ordinary course of nature, on her uncle Frederick’s death, if the said Frederick died without leaving male issue.\n\nExcept in the event, then, of Mr. Frederick Fairlie’s marrying and leaving an heir (the two very last things in the world that he was likely to do), his niece, Laura, would have the property on his death, possessing, it must be remembered, nothing more than a life-interest in it. If she died single, or died childless, the estate would revert to her cousin, Magdalen, the daughter of Mr. Arthur Fairlie. If she married, with a proper settlement—or, in other words, with the settlement I meant to make for her—the income from the estate (a good three thousand a year) would, during her lifetime, be at her own disposal. If she died before her husband, he would naturally expect to be left in the enjoyment of the income, for HIS lifetime. If she had a son, that son would be the heir, to the exclusion of her cousin Magdalen. Thus, Sir Percival’s prospects in marrying Miss Fairlie (so far as his wife’s expectations from real property were concerned) promised him these two advantages, on Mr. Frederick Fairlie’s death: First, the use of three thousand a year (by his wife’s permission, while she lived, and in his own right, on her death, if he survived her); and, secondly, the inheritance of Limmeridge for his son, if he had one.\n\nSo much for the landed property, and for the disposal of the income from it, on the occasion of Miss Fairlie’s marriage. Thus far, no difficulty or difference of opinion on the lady’s settlement was at all likely to arise between Sir Percival’s lawyer and myself.\n\nThe personal estate, or, in other words, the money to which Miss Fairlie would become entitled on reaching the age of twenty-one years, is the next point to consider.\n\nThis part of her inheritance was, in itself, a comfortable little fortune. It was derived under her father’s will, and it amounted to the sum of twenty thousand pounds. Besides this, she had a life-interest in ten thousand pounds more, which latter amount was to go, on her decease, to her aunt Eleanor, her father’s only sister. It will greatly assist in setting the family affairs before the reader in the clearest possible light, if I stop here for a moment, to explain why the aunt had been kept waiting for her legacy until the death of the niece.\n\nMr. Philip Fairlie had lived on excellent terms with his sister Eleanor, as long as she remained a single woman. But when her marriage took place, somewhat late in life, and when that marriage united her to an Italian gentleman named Fosco, or, rather, to an Italian nobleman—seeing that he rejoiced in the title of Count—Mr. Fairlie disapproved of her conduct so strongly that he ceased to hold any communication with her, and even went the length of striking her name out of his will. The other members of the family all thought this serious manifestation of resentment at his sister’s marriage more or less unreasonable. Count Fosco, though not a rich man, was not a penniless adventurer either. He had a small but sufficient income of his own. He had lived many years in England, and he held an excellent position in society. These recommendations, however, availed nothing with Mr. Fairlie. In many of his opinions he was an Englishman of the old school, and he hated a foreigner simply and solely because he was a foreigner. The utmost that he could be prevailed on to do, in after years—mainly at Miss Fairlie’s intercession—was to restore his sister’s name to its former place in his will, but to keep her waiting for her legacy by giving the income of the money to his daughter for life, and the money itself, if her aunt died before her, to her cousin Magdalen. Considering the relative ages of the two ladies, the aunt’s chance, in the ordinary course of nature, of receiving the ten thousand pounds, was thus rendered doubtful in the extreme; and Madame Fosco resented her brother’s treatment of her as unjustly as usual in such cases, by refusing to see her niece, and declining to believe that Miss Fairlie’s intercession had ever been exerted to restore her name to Mr. Fairlie’s will.\n\nSuch was the history of the ten thousand pounds. Here again no difficulty could arise with Sir Percival’s legal adviser. The income would be at the wife’s disposal, and the principal would go to her aunt or her cousin on her death.\n\nAll preliminary explanations being now cleared out of the way, I come at last to the real knot of the case—to the twenty thousand pounds.\n\nThis sum was absolutely Miss Fairlie’s own on her completing her twenty-first year, and the whole future disposition of it depended, in the first instance, on the conditions I could obtain for her in her marriage-settlement. The other clauses contained in that document were of a formal kind, and need not be recited here. But the clause relating to the money is too important to be passed over. A few lines will be sufficient to give the necessary abstract of it.\n\nMy stipulation in regard to the twenty thousand pounds was simply this: The whole amount was to be settled so as to give the income to the lady for her life—afterwards to Sir Percival for his life—and the principal to the children of the marriage. In default of issue, the principal was to be disposed of as the lady might by her will direct, for which purpose I reserved to her the right of making a will. The effect of these conditions may be thus summed up. If Lady Glyde died without leaving children, her half-sister Miss Halcombe, and any other relatives or friends whom she might be anxious to benefit, would, on her husband’s death, divide among them such shares of her money as she desired them to have. If, on the other hand, she died leaving children, then their interest, naturally and necessarily, superseded all other interests whatsoever. This was the clause—and no one who reads it can fail, I think, to agree with me that it meted out equal justice to all parties.\n\nWe shall see how my proposals were met on the husband’s side.\n\nAt the time when Miss Halcombe’s letter reached me I was even more busily occupied than usual. But I contrived to make leisure for the settlement. I had drawn it, and had sent it for approval to Sir Percival’s solicitor, in less than a week from the time when Miss Halcombe had informed me of the proposed marriage.\n\nAfter a lapse of two days the document was returned to me, with notes and remarks of the baronet’s lawyer. His objections, in general, proved to be of the most trifling and technical kind, until he came to the clause relating to the twenty thousand pounds. Against this there were double lines drawn in red ink, and the following note was appended to them—\n\n“Not admissible. The PRINCIPAL to go to Sir Percival Glyde, in the event of his surviving Lady Glyde, and there being no issue.”\n\nThat is to say, not one farthing of the twenty thousand pounds was to go to Miss Halcombe, or to any other relative or friend of Lady Glyde’s. The whole sum, if she left no children, was to slip into the pockets of her husband.\n\nThe answer I wrote to this audacious proposal was as short and sharp as I could make it. “My dear sir. Miss Fairlie’s settlement. I maintain the clause to which you object, exactly as it stands. Yours truly.” The rejoinder came back in a quarter of an hour. “My dear sir. Miss Fairlie’s settlement. I maintain the red ink to which you object, exactly as it stands. Yours truly.” In the detestable slang of the day, we were now both “at a deadlock,” and nothing was left for it but to refer to our clients on either side.\n\nAs matters stood, my client—Miss Fairlie not having yet completed her twenty-first year—Mr. Frederick Fairlie, was her guardian. I wrote by that day’s post, and put the case before him exactly as it stood, not only urging every argument I could think of to induce him to maintain the clause as I had drawn it, but stating to him plainly the mercenary motive which was at the bottom of the opposition to my settlement of the twenty thousand pounds. The knowledge of Sir Percival’s affairs which I had necessarily gained when the provisions of the deed on HIS side were submitted in due course to my examination, had but too plainly informed me that the debts on his estate were enormous, and that his income, though nominally a large one, was virtually, for a man in his position, next to nothing. The want of ready money was the practical necessity of Sir Percival’s existence, and his lawyer’s note on the clause in the settlement was nothing but the frankly selfish expression of it.\n\nMr. Fairlie’s answer reached me by return of post, and proved to be wandering and irrelevant in the extreme. Turned into plain English, it practically expressed itself to this effect: “Would dear Gilmore be so very obliging as not to worry his friend and client about such a trifle as a remote contingency? Was it likely that a young woman of twenty-one would die before a man of forty five, and die without children? On the other hand, in such a miserable world as this, was it possible to over-estimate the value of peace and quietness? If those two heavenly blessings were offered in exchange for such an earthly trifle as a remote chance of twenty thousand pounds, was it not a fair bargain? Surely, yes. Then why not make it?”\n\nI threw the letter away in disgust. Just as it had fluttered to the ground, there was a knock at my door, and Sir Percival’s solicitor, Mr. Merriman, was shown in. There are many varieties of sharp practitioners in this world, but I think the hardest of all to deal with are the men who overreach you under the disguise of inveterate good-humour. A fat, well fed, smiling, friendly man of business is of all parties to a bargain the most hopeless to deal with. Mr. Merriman was one of this class.\n\n“And how is good Mr. Gilmore?” he began, all in a glow with the warmth of his own amiability. “Glad to see you, sir, in such excellent health. I was passing your door, and I thought I would look in in case you might have something to say to me. Do—now pray do let us settle this little difference of ours by word of mouth, if we can! Have you heard from your client yet?”\n\n“Yes. Have you heard from yours?”\n\n“My dear, good sir! I wish I had heard from him to any purpose—I wish, with all my heart, the responsibility was off my shoulders; but he is obstinate—or let me rather say, resolute—and he won’t take it off. ‘Merriman, I leave details to you. Do what you think right for my interests, and consider me as having personally withdrawn from the business until it is all over.’ Those were Sir Percival’s words a fortnight ago, and all I can get him to do now is to repeat them. I am not a hard man, Mr. Gilmore, as you know. Personally and privately, I do assure you, I should like to sponge out that note of mine at this very moment. But if Sir Percival won’t go into the matter, if Sir Percival will blindly leave all his interests in my sole care, what course can I possibly take except the course of asserting them? My hands are bound—don’t you see, my dear sir?—my hands are bound.”\n\n“You maintain your note on the clause, then, to the letter?” I said.\n\n“Yes—deuce take it! I have no other alternative.” He walked to the fireplace and warmed himself, humming the fag end of a tune in a rich convivial bass voice. “What does your side say?” he went on; “now pray tell me—what does your side say?”\n\nI was ashamed to tell him. I attempted to gain time—nay, I did worse. My legal instincts got the better of me, and I even tried to bargain.\n\n“Twenty thousand pounds is rather a large sum to be given up by the lady’s friends at two days’ notice,” I said.\n\n“Very true,” replied Mr. Merriman, looking down thoughtfully at his boots. “Properly put, sir—most properly put!”\n\n“A compromise, recognising the interests of the lady’s family as well as the interests of the husband, might not perhaps have frightened my client quite so much,” I went on. “Come, come! this contingency resolves itself into a matter of bargaining after all. What is the least you will take?”\n\n“The least we will take,” said Mr. Merriman, “is nineteen-thousand- nine-hundred-and-ninety-nine-pounds-nineteen-shillings-and-elevenpence- three-farthings. Ha! ha! ha! Excuse me, Mr. Gilmore. I must have my little joke.”\n\n“Little enough,” I remarked. “The joke is just worth the odd farthing it was made for.”\n\nMr. Merriman was delighted. He laughed over my retort till the room rang again. I was not half so good-humoured on my side; I came back to business, and closed the interview.\n\n“This is Friday,” I said. “Give us till Tuesday next for our final answer.”\n\n“By all means,” replied Mr. Merriman. “Longer, my dear sir, if you like.” He took up his hat to go, and then addressed me again. “By the way,” he said, “your clients in Cumberland have not heard anything more of the woman who wrote the anonymous letter, have they?”\n\n“Nothing more,” I answered. “Have you found no trace of her?”\n\n“Not yet,” said my legal friend. “But we don’t despair. Sir Percival has his suspicions that Somebody is keeping her in hiding, and we are having that Somebody watched.”\n\n“You mean the old woman who was with her in Cumberland,” I said.\n\n“Quite another party, sir,” answered Mr. Merriman. “We don’t happen to have laid hands on the old woman yet. Our Somebody is a man. We have got him close under our eye here in London, and we strongly suspect he had something to do with helping her in the first instance to escape from the Asylum. Sir Percival wanted to question him at once, but I said, ‘No. Questioning him will only put him on his guard—watch him, and wait.’ We shall see what happens. A dangerous woman to be at large, Mr. Gilmore; nobody knows what she may do next. I wish you good-morning, sir. On Tuesday next I shall hope for the pleasure of hearing from you.” He smiled amiably and went out.\n\nMy mind had been rather absent during the latter part of the conversation with my legal friend. I was so anxious about the matter of the settlement that I had little attention to give to any other subject, and the moment I was left alone again I began to think over what my next proceeding ought to be.\n\nIn the case of any other client I should have acted on my instructions, however personally distasteful to me, and have given up the point about the twenty thousand pounds on the spot. But I could not act with this business-like indifference towards Miss Fairlie. I had an honest feeling of affection and admiration for her—I remembered gratefully that her father had been the kindest patron and friend to me that ever man had—I had felt towards her while I was drawing the settlement as I might have felt, if I had not been an old bachelor, towards a daughter of my own, and I was determined to spare no personal sacrifice in her service and where her interests were concerned. Writing a second time to Mr. Fairlie was not to be thought of—it would only be giving him a second opportunity of slipping through my fingers. Seeing him and personally remonstrating with him might possibly be of more use. The next day was Saturday. I determined to take a return ticket and jolt my old bones down to Cumberland, on the chance of persuading him to adopt the just, the independent, and the honourable course. It was a poor chance enough, no doubt, but when I had tried it my conscience would be at ease. I should then have done all that a man in my position could do to serve the interests of my old friend’s only child.\n\nThe weather on Saturday was beautiful, a west wind and a bright sun. Having felt latterly a return of that fulness and oppression of the head, against which my doctor warned me so seriously more than two years since, I resolved to take the opportunity of getting a little extra exercise by sending my bag on before me and walking to the terminus in Euston Square. As I came out into Holborn a gentleman walking by rapidly stopped and spoke to me. It was Mr. Walter Hartright.\n\nIf he had not been the first to greet me I should certainly have passed him. He was so changed that I hardly knew him again. His face looked pale and haggard—his manner was hurried and uncertain—and his dress, which I remembered as neat and gentlemanlike when I saw him at Limmeridge, was so slovenly now that I should really have been ashamed of the appearance of it on one of my own clerks.\n\n“Have you been long back from Cumberland?” he asked. “I heard from Miss Halcombe lately. I am aware that Sir Percival Glyde’s explanation has been considered satisfactory. Will the marriage take place soon? Do you happen to know, Mr. Gilmore?”\n\nHe spoke so fast, and crowded his questions together so strangely and confusedly, that I could hardly follow him. However accidentally intimate he might have been with the family at Limmeridge, I could not see that he had any right to expect information on their private affairs, and I determined to drop him, as easily as might be, on the subject of Miss Fairlie’s marriage.\n\n“Time will show, Mr. Hartright,” I said—“time will show. I dare say if we look out for the marriage in the papers we shall not be far wrong. Excuse my noticing it, but I am sorry to see you not looking so well as you were when we last met.”\n\nA momentary nervous contraction quivered about his lips and eyes, and made me half reproach myself for having answered him in such a significantly guarded manner.\n\n“I had no right to ask about her marriage,” he said bitterly. “I must wait to see it in the newspapers like other people. Yes,”—he went on before I could make any apologies—“I have not been well lately. I am going to another country to try a change of scene and occupation. Miss Halcombe has kindly assisted me with her influence, and my testimonials have been found satisfactory. It is a long distance off, but I don’t care where I go, what the climate is, or how long I am away.” He looked about him while he said this at the throng of strangers passing us by on either side, in a strange, suspicious manner, as if he thought that some of them might be watching us.\n\n“I wish you well through it, and safe back again,” I said, and then added, so as not to keep him altogether at arm’s length on the subject of the Fairlies, “I am going down to Limmeridge to-day on business. Miss Halcombe and Miss Fairlie are away just now on a visit to some friends in Yorkshire.”\n\nHis eyes brightened, and he seemed about to say something in answer, but the same momentary nervous spasm crossed his face again. He took my hand, pressed it hard, and disappeared among the crowd without saying another word. Though he was little more than a stranger to me, I waited for a moment, looking after him almost with a feeling of regret. I had gained in my profession sufficient experience of young men to know what the outward signs and tokens were of their beginning to go wrong, and when I resumed my walk to the railway I am sorry to say I felt more than doubtful about Mr. Hartright’s future.\n\n# IV\n\nLeaving by an early train, I got to Limmeridge in time for dinner. The house was oppressively empty and dull. I had expected that good Mrs. Vesey would have been company for me in the absence of the young ladies, but she was confined to her room by a cold. The servants were so surprised at seeing me that they hurried and bustled absurdly, and made all sorts of annoying mistakes. Even the butler, who was old enough to have known better, brought me a bottle of port that was chilled. The reports of Mr. Fairlie’s health were just as usual, and when I sent up a message to announce my arrival, I was told that he would be delighted to see me the next morning but that the sudden news of my appearance had prostrated him with palpitations for the rest of the evening. The wind howled dismally all night, and strange cracking and groaning noises sounded here, there, and everywhere in the empty house. I slept as wretchedly as possible, and got up in a mighty bad humour to breakfast by myself the next morning.\n\nAt ten o’clock I was conducted to Mr. Fairlie’s apartments. He was in his usual room, his usual chair, and his usual aggravating state of mind and body. When I went in, his valet was standing before him, holding up for inspection a heavy volume of etchings, as long and as broad as my office writing-desk. The miserable foreigner grinned in the most abject manner, and looked ready to drop with fatigue, while his master composedly turned over the etchings, and brought their hidden beauties to light with the help of a magnifying glass.\n\n“You very best of good old friends,” said Mr. Fairlie, leaning back lazily before he could look at me, “are you QUITE well? How nice of you to come here and see me in my solitude. Dear Gilmore!”\n\nI had expected that the valet would be dismissed when I appeared, but nothing of the sort happened. There he stood, in front of his master’s chair, trembling under the weight of the etchings, and there Mr. Fairlie sat, serenely twirling the magnifying glass between his white fingers and thumbs.\n\n“I have come to speak to you on a very important matter,” I said, “and you will therefore excuse me, if I suggest that we had better be alone.”\n\nThe unfortunate valet looked at me gratefully. Mr. Fairlie faintly repeated my last three words, “better be alone,” with every appearance of the utmost possible astonishment.\n\nI was in no humour for trifling, and I resolved to make him understand what I meant.\n\n“Oblige me by giving that man permission to withdraw,” I said, pointing to the valet.\n\nMr. Fairlie arched his eyebrows and pursed up his lips in sarcastic surprise.\n\n“Man?” he repeated. “You provoking old Gilmore, what can you possibly mean by calling him a man? He’s nothing of the sort. He might have been a man half an hour ago, before I wanted my etchings, and he may be a man half an hour hence, when I don’t want them any longer. At present he is simply a portfolio stand. Why object, Gilmore, to a portfolio stand?”\n\n“I DO object. For the third time, Mr. Fairlie, I beg that we may be alone.”\n\nMy tone and manner left him no alternative but to comply with my request. He looked at the servant, and pointed peevishly to a chair at his side.\n\n“Put down the etchings and go away,” he said. “Don’t upset me by losing my place. Have you, or have you not, lost my place? Are you sure you have not? And have you put my hand-bell quite within my reach? Yes? Then why the devil don’t you go?”\n\nThe valet went out. Mr. Fairlie twisted himself round in his chair, polished the magnifying glass with his delicate cambric handkerchief, and indulged himself with a sidelong inspection of the open volume of etchings. It was not easy to keep my temper under these circumstances, but I did keep it.\n\n“I have come here at great personal inconvenience,” I said, “to serve the interests of your niece and your family, and I think I have established some slight claim to be favoured with your attention in return.”\n\n“Don’t bully me!” exclaimed Mr. Fairlie, falling back helplessly in the chair, and closing his eyes. “Please don’t bully me. I’m not strong enough.”\n\nI was determined not to let him provoke me, for Laura Fairlie’s sake.\n\n“My object,” I went on, “is to entreat you to reconsider your letter, and not to force me to abandon the just rights of your niece, and of all who belong to her. Let me state the case to you once more, and for the last time.”\n\nMr. Fairlie shook his head and sighed piteously.\n\n“This is heartless of you, Gilmore—very heartless,” he said. “Never mind, go on.”\n\nI put all the points to him carefully—I set the matter before him in every conceivable light. He lay back in the chair the whole time I was speaking with his eyes closed. When I had done he opened them indolently, took his silver smelling-bottle from the table, and sniffed at it with an air of gentle relish.\n\n“Good Gilmore!” he said between the sniffs, “how very nice this is of you! How you reconcile one to human nature!”\n\n“Give me a plain answer to a plain question, Mr. Fairlie. I tell you again, Sir Percival Glyde has no shadow of a claim to expect more than the income of the money. The money itself if your niece has no children, ought to be under her control, and to return to her family. If you stand firm, Sir Percival must give way—he must give way, I tell you, or he exposes himself to the base imputation of marrying Miss Fairlie entirely from mercenary motives.”\n\nMr. Fairlie shook the silver smelling-bottle at me playfully.\n\n“You dear old Gilmore, how you do hate rank and family, don’t you? How you detest Glyde because he happens to be a baronet. What a Radical you are—oh, dear me, what a Radical you are!”\n\nA Radical!!! I could put up with a good deal of provocation, but, after holding the soundest Conservative principles all my life, I could NOT put up with being called a Radical. My blood boiled at it—I started out of my chair—I was speechless with Indignation.\n\n“Don’t shake the room!” cried Mr. Fairlie—“for Heaven’s sake don’t shake the room! Worthiest of all possible Gilmores, I meant no offence. My own views are so extremely liberal that I think I am a Radical myself. Yes. We are a pair of Radicals. Please don’t be angry. I can’t quarrel—I haven’t stamina enough. Shall we drop the subject? Yes. Come and look at these sweet etchings. Do let me teach you to understand the heavenly pearliness of these lines. Do now, there’s a good Gilmore!”\n\nWhile he was maundering on in this way I was, fortunately for my own self-respect, returning to my senses. When I spoke again I was composed enough to treat his impertinence with the silent contempt that it deserved.\n\n“You are entirely wrong, sir,” I said, “in supposing that I speak from any prejudice against Sir Percival Glyde. I may regret that he has so unreservedly resigned himself in this matter to his lawyer’s direction as to make any appeal to himself impossible, but I am not prejudiced against him. What I have said would equally apply to any other man in his situation, high or low. The principle I maintain is a recognised principle. If you were to apply at the nearest town here, to the first respectable solicitor you could find, he would tell you as a stranger what I tell you as a friend. He would inform you that it is against all rule to abandon the lady’s money entirely to the man she marries. He would decline, on grounds of common legal caution, to give the husband, under any circumstances whatever, an interest of twenty thousand pounds in his wife’s death.”\n\n“Would he really, Gilmore?” said Mr. Fairlie. “If he said anything half so horrid, I do assure you I should tinkle my bell for Louis, and have him sent out of the house immediately.”\n\n“You shall not irritate me, Mr. Fairlie—for your niece’s sake and for her father’s sake, you shall not irritate me. You shall take the whole responsibility of this discreditable settlement on your own shoulders before I leave the room.”\n\n“Don’t!—now please don’t!” said Mr. Fairlie. “Think how precious your time is, Gilmore, and don’t throw it away. I would dispute with you if I could, but I can’t—I haven’t stamina enough. You want to upset me, to upset yourself, to upset Glyde, and to upset Laura; and—oh, dear me!—all for the sake of the very last thing in the world that is likely to happen. No, dear friend—in the interests of peace and quietness, positively No!”\n\n“I am to understand, then, that you hold by the determination expressed in your letter?”\n\n“Yes, please. So glad we understand each other at last. Sit down again—do!”\n\nI walked at once to the door, and Mr. Fairlie resignedly “tinkled” his hand-bell. Before I left the room I turned round and addressed him for the last time.\n\n“Whatever happens in the future, sir,” I said, “remember that my plain duty of warning you has been performed. As the faithful friend and servant of your family, I tell you, at parting, that no daughter of mine should be married to any man alive under such a settlement as you are forcing me to make for Miss Fairlie.”\n\nThe door opened behind me, and the valet stood waiting on the threshold.\n\n“Louis,” said Mr. Fairlie, “show Mr. Gilmore out, and then come back and hold up my etchings for me again. Make them give you a good lunch downstairs. Do, Gilmore, make my idle beasts of servants give you a good lunch!”\n\nI was too much disgusted to reply—I turned on my heel, and left him in silence. There was an up train at two o’clock in the afternoon, and by that train I returned to London.\n\nOn the Tuesday I sent in the altered settlement, which practically disinherited the very persons whom Miss Fairlie’s own lips had informed me she was most anxious to benefit. I had no choice. Another lawyer would have drawn up the deed if I had refused to undertake it.\n\nMy task is done. My personal share in the events of the family story extends no farther than the point which I have just reached. Other pens than mine will describe the strange circumstances which are now shortly to follow. Seriously and sorrowfully I close this brief record. Seriously and sorrowfully I repeat here the parting words that I spoke at Limmeridge House:—No daughter of mine should have been married to any man alive under such a settlement as I was compelled to make for Laura Fairlie.\n\nThe End of Mr. Gilmore’s Narrative.\n\n(in Extracts from her Diary)\n\n[1] The passages omitted, here and elsewhere, in Miss Halcombe’s Diary are only those which bear no reference to Miss Fairlie or to any of the persons with whom she is associated in these pages.\n\nThis morning Mr. Gilmore left us.\n\nHis interview with Laura had evidently grieved and surprised him more than he liked to confess. I felt afraid, from his look and manner when we parted, that she might have inadvertently betrayed to him the real secret of her depression and my anxiety. This doubt grew on me so, after he had gone, that I declined riding out with Sir Percival, and went up to Laura’s room instead.\n\nI have been sadly distrustful of myself, in this difficult and lamentable matter, ever since I found out my own ignorance of the strength of Laura’s unhappy attachment. I ought to have known that the delicacy and forbearance and sense of honour which drew me to poor Hartright, and made me so sincerely admire and respect him, were just the qualities to appeal most irresistibly to Laura’s natural sensitiveness and natural generosity of nature. And yet, until she opened her heart to me of her own accord, I had no suspicion that this new feeling had taken root so deeply. I once thought time and care might remove it. I now fear that it will remain with her and alter her for life. The discovery that I have committed such an error in judgment as this makes me hesitate about everything else. I hesitate about Sir Percival, in the face of the plainest proofs. I hesitate even in speaking to Laura. On this very morning I doubted, with my hand on the door, whether I should ask her the questions I had come to put, or not.\n\nWhen I went into her room I found her walking up and down in great impatience. She looked flushed and excited, and she came forward at once, and spoke to me before I could open my lips.\n\n“I wanted you,” she said. “Come and sit down on the sofa with me. Marian! I can bear this no longer—I must and will end it.”\n\nThere was too much colour in her cheeks, too much energy in her manner, too much firmness in her voice. The little book of Hartright’s drawings—the fatal book that she will dream over whenever she is alone—was in one of her hands. I began by gently and firmly taking it from her, and putting it out of sight on a side-table.\n\n“Tell me quietly, my darling, what you wish to do,” I said. “Has Mr. Gilmore been advising you?”\n\nShe shook her head. “No, not in what I am thinking of now. He was very kind and good to me, Marian, and I am ashamed to say I distressed him by crying. I am miserably helpless—I can’t control myself. For my own sake, and for all our sakes, I must have courage enough to end it.”\n\n“Do you mean courage enough to claim your release?” I asked.\n\n“No,” she said simply. “Courage, dear, to tell the truth.”\n\nShe put her arms round my neck, and rested her head quietly on my bosom. On the opposite wall hung the miniature portrait of her father. I bent over her, and saw that she was looking at it while her head lay on my breast.\n\n“I can never claim my release from my engagement,” she went on. “Whatever way it ends it must end wretchedly for me. All I can do, Marian, is not to add the remembrance that I have broken my promise and forgotten my father’s dying words, to make that wretchedness worse.”\n\n“What is it you propose, then?” I asked.\n\n“To tell Sir Percival Glyde the truth with my own lips,” she answered, “and to let him release me, if he will, not because I ask him, but because he knows all.”\n\n“What do you mean, Laura, by ‘all’? Sir Percival will know enough (he has told me so himself) if he knows that the engagement is opposed to your own wishes.”\n\n“Can I tell him that, when the engagement was made for me by my father, with my own consent? I should have kept my promise, not happily, I am afraid, but still contentedly—” she stopped, turned her face to me, and laid her cheek close against mine—“I should have kept my engagement, Marian, if another love had not grown up in my heart, which was not there when I first promised to be Sir Percival’s wife.”\n\n“Laura! you will never lower yourself by making a confession to him?”\n\n“I shall lower myself, indeed, if I gain my release by hiding from him what he has a right to know.”\n\n“He has not the shadow of a right to know it!”\n\n“Wrong, Marian, wrong! I ought to deceive no one—least of all the man to whom my father gave me, and to whom I gave myself.” She put her lips to mine, and kissed me. “My own love,” she said softly, “you are so much too fond of me, and so much too proud of me, that you forget, in my case, what you would remember in your own. Better that Sir Percival should doubt my motives, and misjudge my conduct if he will, than that I should be first false to him in thought, and then mean enough to serve my own interests by hiding the falsehood.”\n\nI held her away from me in astonishment. For the first time in our lives we had changed places—the resolution was all on her side, the hesitation all on mine. I looked into the pale, quiet, resigned young face—I saw the pure, innocent heart, in the loving eyes that looked back at me—and the poor worldly cautions and objections that rose to my lips dwindled and died away in their own emptiness. I hung my head in silence. In her place the despicably small pride which makes so many women deceitful would have been my pride, and would have made me deceitful too.\n\n“Don’t be angry with me, Marian,” she said, mistaking my silence.\n\nI only answered by drawing her close to me again. I was afraid of crying if I spoke. My tears do not flow so easily as they ought—they come almost like men’s tears, with sobs that seem to tear me in pieces, and that frighten every one about me.\n\n“I have thought of this, love, for many days,” she went on, twining and twisting my hair with that childish restlessness in her fingers, which poor Mrs. Vesey still tries so patiently and so vainly to cure her of—“I have thought of it very seriously, and I can be sure of my courage when my own conscience tells me I am right. Let me speak to him to-morrow—in your presence, Marian. I will say nothing that is wrong, nothing that you or I need be ashamed of—but, oh, it will ease my heart so to end this miserable concealment! Only let me know and feel that I have no deception to answer for on my side, and then, when he has heard what I have to say, let him act towards me as he will.”\n\nShe sighed, and put her head back in its old position on my bosom. Sad misgivings about what the end would be weighed upon my mind, but still distrusting myself, I told her that I would do as she wished. She thanked me, and we passed gradually into talking of other things.\n\nAt dinner she joined us again, and was more easy and more herself with Sir Percival than I have seen her yet. In the evening she went to the piano, choosing new music of the dexterous, tuneless, florid kind. The lovely old melodies of Mozart, which poor Hartright was so fond of, she has never played since he left. The book is no longer in the music-stand. She took the volume away herself, so that nobody might find it out and ask her to play from it.\n\nI had no opportunity of discovering whether her purpose of the morning had changed or not, until she wished Sir Percival good-night—and then her own words informed me that it was unaltered. She said, very quietly, that she wished to speak to him after breakfast, and that he would find her in her sitting-room with me. He changed colour at those words, and I felt his hand trembling a little when it came to my turn to take it. The event of the next morning would decide his future life, and he evidently knew it.\n\nI went in, as usual, through the door between our two bedrooms, to bid Laura good-night before she went to sleep. In stooping over her to kiss her I saw the little book of Hartright’s drawings half hidden under her pillow, just in the place where she used to hide her favourite toys when she was a child. I could not find it in my heart to say anything, but I pointed to the book and shook my head. She reached both hands up to my cheeks, and drew my face down to hers till our lips met.\n\n“Leave it there to-night,” she whispered; “to-morrow may be cruel, and may make me say good-bye to it for ever.”\n\n9th.—The first event of the morning was not of a kind to raise my spirits—a letter arrived for me from poor Walter Hartright. It is the answer to mine describing the manner in which Sir Percival cleared himself of the suspicions raised by Anne Catherick’s letter. He writes shortly and bitterly about Sir Percival’s explanations, only saying that he has no right to offer an opinion on the conduct of those who are above him. This is sad, but his occasional references to himself grieve me still more. He says that the effort to return to his old habits and pursuits grows harder instead of easier to him every day and he implores me, if I have any interest, to exert it to get him employment that will necessitate his absence from England, and take him among new scenes and new people. I have been made all the readier to comply with this request by a passage at the end of his letter, which has almost alarmed me.\n\nAfter mentioning that he has neither seen nor heard anything of Anne Catherick, he suddenly breaks off, and hints in the most abrupt, mysterious manner, that he has been perpetually watched and followed by strange men ever since he returned to London. He acknowledges that he cannot prove this extraordinary suspicion by fixing on any particular persons, but he declares that the suspicion itself is present to him night and day. This has frightened me, because it looks as if his one fixed idea about Laura was becoming too much for his mind. I will write immediately to some of my mother’s influential old friends in London, and press his claims on their notice. Change of scene and change of occupation may really be the salvation of him at this crisis in his life.\n\nGreatly to my relief, Sir Percival sent an apology for not joining us at breakfast. He had taken an early cup of coffee in his own room, and he was still engaged there in writing letters. At eleven o’clock, if that hour was convenient, he would do himself the honour of waiting on Miss Fairlie and Miss Halcombe.\n\nMy eyes were on Laura’s face while the message was being delivered. I had found her unaccountably quiet and composed on going into her room in the morning, and so she remained all through breakfast. Even when we were sitting together on the sofa in her room, waiting for Sir Percival, she still preserved her self-control.\n\n“Don’t be afraid of me, Marian,” was all she said; “I may forget myself with an old friend like Mr. Gilmore, or with a dear sister like you, but I will not forget myself with Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nI looked at her, and listened to her in silent surprise. Through all the years of our close intimacy this passive force in her character had been hidden from me—hidden even from herself, till love found it, and suffering called it forth.\n\nAs the clock on the mantelpiece struck eleven Sir Percival knocked at the door and came in. There was suppressed anxiety and agitation in every line of his face. The dry, sharp cough, which teases him at most times, seemed to be troubling him more incessantly than ever. He sat down opposite to us at the table, and Laura remained by me. I looked attentively at them both, and he was the palest of the two.\n\nHe said a few unimportant words, with a visible effort to preserve his customary ease of manner. But his voice was not to be steadied, and the restless uneasiness in his eyes was not to be concealed. He must have felt this himself, for he stopped in the middle of a sentence, and gave up even the attempt to hide his embarrassment any longer.\n\nThere was just one moment of dead silence before Laura addressed him.\n\n“I wish to speak to you, Sir Percival,” she said, “on a subject that is very important to us both. My sister is here, because her presence helps me and gives me confidence. She has not suggested one word of what I am going to say—I speak from my own thoughts, not from hers. I am sure you will be kind enough to understand that before I go any farther?”\n\nSir Percival bowed. She had proceeded thus far, with perfect outward tranquillity and perfect propriety of manner. She looked at him, and he looked at her. They seemed, at the outset, at least, resolved to understand one another plainly.\n\n“I have heard from Marian,” she went on, “that I have only to claim my release from our engagement to obtain that release from you. It was forbearing and generous on your part, Sir Percival, to send me such a message. It is only doing you justice to say that I am grateful for the offer, and I hope and believe that it is only doing myself justice to tell you that I decline to accept it.”\n\nHis attentive face relaxed a little. But I saw one of his feet, softly, quietly, incessantly beating on the carpet under the table, and I felt that he was secretly as anxious as ever.\n\n“I have not forgotten,” she said, “that you asked my father’s permission before you honoured me with a proposal of marriage. Perhaps you have not forgotten either what I said when I consented to our engagement? I ventured to tell you that my father’s influence and advice had mainly decided me to give you my promise. I was guided by my father, because I had always found him the truest of all advisers, the best and fondest of all protectors and friends. I have lost him now—I have only his memory to love, but my faith in that dear dead friend has never been shaken. I believe at this moment, as truly as I ever believed, that he knew what was best, and that his hopes and wishes ought to be my hopes and wishes too.”\n\nHer voice trembled for the first time. Her restless fingers stole their way into my lap, and held fast by one of my hands. There was another moment of silence, and then Sir Percival spoke.\n\n“May I ask,” he said, “if I have ever proved myself unworthy of the trust which it has been hitherto my greatest honour and greatest happiness to possess?”\n\n“I have found nothing in your conduct to blame,” she answered. “You have always treated me with the same delicacy and the same forbearance. You have deserved my trust, and, what is of far more importance in my estimation, you have deserved my father’s trust, out of which mine grew. You have given me no excuse, even if I had wanted to find one, for asking to be released from my pledge. What I have said so far has been spoken with the wish to acknowledge my whole obligation to you. My regard for that obligation, my regard for my father’s memory, and my regard for my own promise, all forbid me to set the example, on my side, of withdrawing from our present position. The breaking of our engagement must be entirely your wish and your act, Sir Percival—not mine.”\n\nThe uneasy beating of his foot suddenly stopped, and he leaned forward eagerly across the table.\n\n“My act?” he said. “What reason can there be on my side for withdrawing?”\n\nI heard her breath quickening—I felt her hand growing cold. In spite of what she had said to me when we were alone, I began to be afraid of her. I was wrong.\n\n“A reason that it is very hard to tell you,” she answered. “There is a change in me, Sir Percival—a change which is serious enough to justify you, to yourself and to me, in breaking off our engagement.”\n\nHis face turned so pale again that even his lips lost their colour. He raised the arm which lay on the table, turned a little away in his chair, and supported his head on his hand, so that his profile only was presented to us.\n\n“What change?” he asked. The tone in which he put the question jarred on me—there was something painfully suppressed in it.\n\nShe sighed heavily, and leaned towards me a little, so as to rest her shoulder against mine. I felt her trembling, and tried to spare her by speaking myself. She stopped me by a warning pressure of her hand, and then addressed Sir Percival one more, but this time without looking at him.\n\n“I have heard,” she said, “and I believe it, that the fondest and truest of all affections is the affection which a woman ought to bear to her husband. When our engagement began that affection was mine to give, if I could, and yours to win, if you could. Will you pardon me, and spare me, Sir Percival, if I acknowledge that it is not so any longer?”\n\nA few tears gathered in her eyes, and dropped over her cheeks slowly as she paused and waited for his answer. He did not utter a word. At the beginning of her reply he had moved the hand on which his head rested, so that it hid his face. I saw nothing but the upper part of his figure at the table. Not a muscle of him moved. The fingers of the hand which supported his head were dented deep in his hair. They might have expressed hidden anger or hidden grief—it was hard to say which—there was no significant trembling in them. There was nothing, absolutely nothing, to tell the secret of his thoughts at that moment—the moment which was the crisis of his life and the crisis of hers.\n\nI was determined to make him declare himself, for Laura’s sake.\n\n“Sir Percival!” I interposed sharply, “have you nothing to say when my sister has said so much? More, in my opinion,” I added, my unlucky temper getting the better of me, “than any man alive, in your position, has a right to hear from her.”\n\nThat last rash sentence opened a way for him by which to escape me if he chose, and he instantly took advantage of it.\n\n“Pardon me, Miss Halcombe,” he said, still keeping his hand over his face, “pardon me if I remind you that I have claimed no such right.”\n\nThe few plain words which would have brought him back to the point from which he had wandered were just on my lips, when Laura checked me by speaking again.\n\n“I hope I have not made my painful acknowledgment in vain,” she continued. “I hope it has secured me your entire confidence in what I have still to say?”\n\n“Pray be assured of it.” He made that brief reply warmly, dropping his hand on the table while he spoke, and turning towards us again. Whatever outward change had passed over him was gone now. His face was eager and expectant—it expressed nothing but the most intense anxiety to hear her next words.\n\n“I wish you to understand that I have not spoken from any selfish motive,” she said. “If you leave me, Sir Percival, after what you have just heard, you do not leave me to marry another man, you only allow me to remain a single woman for the rest of my life. My fault towards you has begun and ended in my own thoughts. It can never go any farther. No word has passed—” She hesitated, in doubt about the expression she should use next, hesitated in a momentary confusion which it was very sad and very painful to see. “No word has passed,” she patiently and resolutely resumed, “between myself and the person to whom I am now referring for the first and last time in your presence of my feelings towards him, or of his feelings towards me—no word ever can pass—neither he nor I are likely, in this world, to meet again. I earnestly beg you to spare me from saying any more, and to believe me, on my word, in what I have just told you. It is the truth, Sir Percival—the truth which I think my promised husband has a claim to hear, at any sacrifice of my own feelings. I trust to his generosity to pardon me, and to his honour to keep my secret.”\n\n“Both those trusts are sacred to me,” he said, “and both shall be sacredly kept.”\n\nAfter answering in those terms he paused, and looked at her as if he was waiting to hear more.\n\n“I have said all I wish to say,” she added quietly—“I have said more than enough to justify you in withdrawing from your engagement.”\n\n“You have said more than enough,” he answered, “to make it the dearest object of my life to KEEP the engagement.” With those words he rose from his chair, and advanced a few steps towards the place where she was sitting.\n\nShe started violently, and a faint cry of surprise escaped her. Every word she had spoken had innocently betrayed her purity and truth to a man who thoroughly understood the priceless value of a pure and true woman. Her own noble conduct had been the hidden enemy, throughout, of all the hopes she had trusted to it. I had dreaded this from the first. I would have prevented it, if she had allowed me the smallest chance of doing so. I even waited and watched now, when the harm was done, for a word from Sir Percival that would give me the opportunity of putting him in the wrong.\n\n“You have left it to ME, Miss Fairlie, to resign you,” he continued. “I am not heartless enough to resign a woman who has just shown herself to be the noblest of her sex.”\n\nHe spoke with such warmth and feeling, with such passionate enthusiasm, and yet with such perfect delicacy, that she raised her head, flushed up a little, and looked at him with sudden animation and spirit.\n\n“No!” she said firmly. “The most wretched of her sex, if she must give herself in marriage when she cannot give her love.”\n\n“May she not give it in the future,” he asked, “if the one object of her husband’s life is to deserve it?”\n\n“Never!” she answered. “If you still persist in maintaining our engagement, I may be your true and faithful wife, Sir Percival—your loving wife, if I know my own heart, never!”\n\nShe looked so irresistibly beautiful as she said those brave words that no man alive could have steeled his heart against her. I tried hard to feel that Sir Percival was to blame, and to say so, but my womanhood would pity him, in spite of myself.\n\n“I gratefully accept your faith and truth,” he said. “The least that you can offer is more to me than the utmost that I could hope for from any other woman in the world.”\n\nHer left hand still held mine, but her right hand hung listlessly at her side. He raised it gently to his lips—touched it with them, rather than kissed it—bowed to me—and then, with perfect delicacy and discretion, silently quitted the room.\n\nShe neither moved nor said a word when he was gone—she sat by me, cold and still, with her eyes fixed on the ground. I saw it was hopeless and useless to speak, and I only put my arm round her, and held her to me in silence. We remained together so for what seemed a long and weary time—so long and so weary, that I grew uneasy and spoke to her softly, in the hope of producing a change.\n\nThe sound of my voice seemed to startle her into consciousness. She suddenly drew herself away from me and rose to her feet.\n\n“I must submit, Marian, as well as I can,” she said. “My new life has its hard duties, and one of them begins to-day.”\n\nAs she spoke she went to a side-table near the window, on which her sketching materials were placed, gathered them together carefully, and put them in a drawer of her cabinet. She locked the drawer and brought the key to me.\n\n“I must part from everything that reminds me of him,” she said. “Keep the key wherever you please—I shall never want it again.”\n\nBefore I could say a word she had turned away to her book-case, and had taken from it the album that contained Walter Hartright’s drawings. She hesitated for a moment, holding the little volume fondly in her hands—then lifted it to her lips and kissed it.\n\n“Oh, Laura! Laura!” I said, not angrily, not reprovingly—with nothing but sorrow in my voice, and nothing but sorrow in my heart.\n\n“It is the last time, Marian,” she pleaded. “I am bidding it good-bye for ever.”\n\nShe laid the book on the table and drew out the comb that fastened her hair. It fell, in its matchless beauty, over her back and shoulders, and dropped round her, far below her waist. She separated one long, thin lock from the rest, cut it off, and pinned it carefully, in the form of a circle, on the first blank page of the album. The moment it was fastened she closed the volume hurriedly, and placed it in my hands.\n\n“You write to him and he writes to you,” she said. “While I am alive, if he asks after me always tell him I am well, and never say I am unhappy. Don’t distress him, Marian, for my sake, don’t distress him. If I die first, promise you will give him this little book of his drawings, with my hair in it. There can be no harm, when I am gone, in telling him that I put it there with my own hands. And say—oh, Marian, say for me, then, what I can never say for myself—say I loved him!”\n\nShe flung her arms round my neck, and whispered the last words in my ear with a passionate delight in uttering them which it almost broke my heart to hear. All the long restraint she had imposed on herself gave way in that first last outburst of tenderness. She broke from me with hysterical vehemence, and threw herself on the sofa in a paroxysm of sobs and tears that shook her from head to foot.\n\nI tried vainly to soothe her and reason with her—she was past being soothed, and past being reasoned with. It was the sad, sudden end for us two of this memorable day. When the fit had worn itself out she was too exhausted to speak. She slumbered towards the afternoon, and I put away the book of drawings so that she might not see it when she woke. My face was calm, whatever my heart might be, when she opened her eyes again and looked at me. We said no more to each other about the distressing interview of the morning. Sir Percival’s name was not mentioned. Walter Hartright was not alluded to again by either of us for the remainder of the day.\n\n10th.—Finding that she was composed and like herself this morning, I returned to the painful subject of yesterday, for the sole purpose of imploring her to let me speak to Sir Percival and Mr. Fairlie, more plainly and strongly than she could speak to either of them herself, about this lamentable marriage. She interposed, gently but firmly, in the middle of my remonstrances.\n\n“I left yesterday to decide,” she said; “and yesterday HAS decided. It is too late to go back.”\n\nSir Percival spoke to me this afternoon about what had passed in Laura’s room. He assured me that the unparalleled trust she had placed in him had awakened such an answering conviction of her innocence and integrity in his mind, that he was guiltless of having felt even a moment’s unworthy jealousy, either at the time when he was in her presence, or afterwards when he had withdrawn from it. Deeply as he lamented the unfortunate attachment which had hindered the progress he might otherwise have made in her esteem and regard, he firmly believed that it had remained unacknowledged in the past, and that it would remain, under all changes of circumstance which it was possible to contemplate, unacknowledged in the future. This was his absolute conviction; and the strongest proof he could give of it was the assurance, which he now offered, that he felt no curiosity to know whether the attachment was of recent date or not, or who had been the object of it. His implicit confidence in Miss Fairlie made him satisfied with what she had thought fit to say to him, and he was honestly innocent of the slightest feeling of anxiety to hear more.\n\nHe waited after saying those words and looked at me. I was so conscious of my unreasonable prejudice against him—so conscious of an unworthy suspicion that he might be speculating on my impulsively answering the very questions which he had just described himself as resolved not to ask—that I evaded all reference to this part of the subject with something like a feeling of confusion on my own part. At the same time I was resolved not to lose even the smallest opportunity of trying to plead Laura’s cause, and I told him boldly that I regretted his generosity had not carried him one step farther, and induced him to withdraw from the engagement altogether.\n\nHere, again, he disarmed me by not attempting to defend himself. He would merely beg me to remember the difference there was between his allowing Miss Fairlie to give him up, which was a matter of submission only, and his forcing himself to give up Miss Fairlie, which was, in other words, asking him to be the suicide of his own hopes. Her conduct of the day before had so strengthened the unchangeable love and admiration of two long years, that all active contention against those feelings, on his part, was henceforth entirely out of his power. I must think him weak, selfish, unfeeling towards the very woman whom he idolised, and he must bow to my opinion as resignedly as he could—only putting it to me, at the same time, whether her future as a single woman, pining under an unhappily placed attachment which she could never acknowledge, could be said to promise her a much brighter prospect than her future as the wife of a man who worshipped the very ground she walked on? In the last case there was hope from time, however slight it might be—in the first case, on her own showing, there was no hope at all.\n\nI answered him—more because my tongue is a woman’s, and must answer, than because I had anything convincing to say. It was only too plain that the course Laura had adopted the day before had offered him the advantage if he chose to take it—and that he HAD chosen to take it. I felt this at the time, and I feel it just as strongly now, while I write these lines, in my own room. The one hope left is that his motives really spring, as he says they do, from the irresistible strength of his attachment to Laura.\n\nBefore I close my diary for to-night I must record that I wrote to-day, in poor Hartright’s interest, to two of my mother’s old friends in London—both men of influence and position. If they can do anything for him, I am quite sure they will. Except Laura, I never was more anxious about any one than I am now about Walter. All that has happened since he left us has only increased my strong regard and sympathy for him. I hope I am doing right in trying to help him to employment abroad—I hope, most earnestly and anxiously, that it will end well.\n\n11th.—Sir Percival had an interview with Mr. Fairlie, and I was sent for to join them.\n\nI found Mr. Fairlie greatly relieved at the prospect of the “family worry” (as he was pleased to describe his niece’s marriage) being settled at last. So far, I did not feel called on to say anything to him about my own opinion, but when he proceeded, in his most aggravatingly languid manner, to suggest that the time for the marriage had better be settled next, in accordance with Sir Percival’s wishes, I enjoyed the satisfaction of assailing Mr. Fairlie’s nerves with as strong a protest against hurrying Laura’s decision as I could put into words. Sir Percival immediately assured me that he felt the force of my objection, and begged me to believe that the proposal had not been made in consequence of any interference on his part. Mr. Fairlie leaned back in his chair, closed his eyes, said we both of us did honour to human nature, and then repeated his suggestion as coolly as if neither Sir Percival nor I had said a word in opposition to it. It ended in my flatly declining to mention the subject to Laura, unless she first approached it of her own accord. I left the room at once after making that declaration. Sir Percival looked seriously embarrassed and distressed, Mr. Fairlie stretched out his lazy legs on his velvet footstool, and said, “Dear Marian! how I envy you your robust nervous system! Don’t bang the door!”\n\nOn going to Laura’s room I found that she had asked for me, and that Mrs. Vesey had informed her that I was with Mr. Fairlie. She inquired at once what I had been wanted for, and I told her all that had passed, without attempting to conceal the vexation and annoyance that I really felt. Her answer surprised and distressed me inexpressibly—it was the very last reply that I should have expected her to make.\n\n“My uncle is right,” she said. “I have caused trouble and anxiety enough to you, and to all about me. Let me cause no more, Marian—let Sir Percival decide.”\n\nI remonstrated warmly, but nothing that I could say moved her.\n\n“I am held to my engagement,” she replied; “I have broken with my old life. The evil day will not come the less surely because I put it off. No, Marian! once again my uncle is right. I have caused trouble enough and anxiety enough, and I will cause no more.”\n\nShe used to be pliability itself, but she was now inflexibly passive in her resignation—I might almost say in her despair. Dearly as I love her, I should have been less pained if she had been violently agitated—it was so shockingly unlike her natural character to see her as cold and insensible as I saw her now.\n\n12th.—Sir Percival put some questions to me at breakfast about Laura, which left me no choice but to tell him what she had said.\n\nWhile we were talking she herself came down and joined us. She was just as unnaturally composed in Sir Percival’s presence as she had been in mine. When breakfast was over he had an opportunity of saying a few words to her privately, in a recess of one of the windows. They were not more than two or three minutes together, and on their separating she left the room with Mrs. Vesey, while Sir Percival came to me. He said he had entreated her to favour him by maintaining her privilege of fixing the time for the marriage at her own will and pleasure. In reply she had merely expressed her acknowledgments, and had desired him to mention what his wishes were to Miss Halcombe.\n\nI have no patience to write more. In this instance, as in every other, Sir Percival has carried his point with the utmost possible credit to himself, in spite of everything that I can say or do. His wishes are now, what they were, of course, when he first came here; and Laura having resigned herself to the one inevitable sacrifice of the marriage, remains as coldly hopeless and enduring as ever. In parting with the little occupations and relics that reminded her of Hartright, she seems to have parted with all her tenderness and all her impressibility. It is only three o’clock in the afternoon while I write these lines, and Sir Percival has left us already, in the happy hurry of a bridegroom, to prepare for the bride’s reception at his house in Hampshire. Unless some extraordinary event happens to prevent it they will be married exactly at the time when he wished to be married—before the end of the year. My very fingers burn as I write it!\n\n13th.—A sleepless night, through uneasiness about Laura. Towards the morning I came to a resolution to try what change of scene would do to rouse her. She cannot surely remain in her present torpor of insensibility, if I take her away from Limmeridge and surround her with the pleasant faces of old friends? After some consideration I decided on writing to the Arnolds, in Yorkshire. They are simple, kind-hearted, hospitable people, and she has known them from her childhood. When I had put the letter in the post-bag I told her what I had done. It would have been a relief to me if she had shown the spirit to resist and object. But no—she only said, “I will go anywhere with you, Marian. I dare say you are right—I dare say the change will do me good.”\n\n14th.—I wrote to Mr. Gilmore, informing him that there was really a prospect of this miserable marriage taking place, and also mentioning my idea of trying what change of scene would do for Laura. I had no heart to go into particulars. Time enough for them when we get nearer to the end of the year.\n\n15th.—Three letters for me. The first, from the Arnolds, full of delight at the prospect of seeing Laura and me. The second, from one of the gentlemen to whom I wrote on Walter Hartright’s behalf, informing me that he has been fortunate enough to find an opportunity of complying with my request. The third, from Walter himself, thanking me, poor fellow, in the warmest terms, for giving him an opportunity of leaving his home, his country, and his friends. A private expedition to make excavations among the ruined cities of Central America is, it seems, about to sail from Liverpool. The draughtsman who had been already appointed to accompany it has lost heart, and withdrawn at the eleventh hour, and Walter is to fill his place. He is to be engaged for six months certain, from the time of the landing in Honduras, and for a year afterwards, if the excavations are successful, and if the funds hold out. His letter ends with a promise to write me a farewell line when they are all on board ship, and when the pilot leaves them. I can only hope and pray earnestly that he and I are both acting in this matter for the best. It seems such a serious step for him to take, that the mere contemplation of it startles me. And yet, in his unhappy position, how can I expect him or wish him to remain at home?\n\n16th.—The carriage is at the door. Laura and I set out on our visit to the Arnolds to-day.\n\n# POLESDEAN LODGE, YORKSHIRE.\n\n23rd.—A week in these new scenes and among these kind-hearted people has done her some good, though not so much as I had hoped. I have resolved to prolong our stay for another week at least. It is useless to go back to Limmeridge till there is an absolute necessity for our return.\n\n24th.—Sad news by this morning’s post. The expedition to Central America sailed on the twenty-first. We have parted with a true man—we have lost a faithful friend. Water Hartright has left England.\n\n25th.—Sad news yesterday—ominous news to-day. Sir Percival Glyde has written to Mr. Fairlie, and Mr. Fairlie has written to Laura and me, to recall us to Limmeridge immediately.\n\nWhat can this mean? Has the day for the marriage been fixed in our absence?\n\n# LIMMERIDGE HOUSE.\n\nNovember 27th.—My forebodings are realised. The marriage is fixed for the twenty-second of December.\n\nThe day after we left for Polesdean Lodge Sir Percival wrote, it seems, to Mr. Fairlie, to say that the necessary repairs and alterations in his house in Hampshire would occupy a much longer time in completion than he had originally anticipated. The proper estimates were to be submitted to him as soon as possible, and it would greatly facilitate his entering into definite arrangements with the workpeople, if he could be informed of the exact period at which the wedding ceremony might be expected to take place. He could then make all his calculations in reference to time, besides writing the necessary apologies to friends who had been engaged to visit him that winter, and who could not, of course, be received when the house was in the hands of the workmen.\n\nTo this letter Mr. Fairlie had replied by requesting Sir Percival himself to suggest a day for the marriage, subject to Miss Fairlie’s approval, which her guardian willingly undertook to do his best to obtain. Sir Percival wrote back by the next post, and proposed (in accordance with his own views and wishes from the first?) the latter part of December—perhaps the twenty-second, or twenty-fourth, or any other day that the lady and her guardian might prefer. The lady not being at hand to speak for herself, her guardian had decided, in her absence, on the earliest day mentioned—the twenty-second of December, and had written to recall us to Limmeridge in consequence.\n\nAfter explaining these particulars to me at a private interview yesterday, Mr. Fairlie suggested, in his most amiable manner, that I should open the necessary negotiations to-day. Feeling that resistance was useless, unless I could first obtain Laura’s authority to make it, I consented to speak to her, but declared, at the same time, that I would on no consideration undertake to gain her consent to Sir Percival’s wishes. Mr. Fairlie complimented me on my “excellent conscience,” much as he would have complimented me, if he had been out walking, on my “excellent constitution,” and seemed perfectly satisfied, so far, with having simply shifted one more family responsibility from his own shoulders to mine.\n\nThis morning I spoke to Laura as I had promised. The composure—I may almost say, the insensibility—which she has so strangely and so resolutely maintained ever since Sir Percival left us, was not proof against the shock of the news I had to tell her. She turned pale and trembled violently.\n\n“Not so soon!” she pleaded. “Oh, Marian, not so soon!”\n\nThe slightest hint she could give was enough for me. I rose to leave the room, and fight her battle for her at once with Mr. Fairlie.\n\nJust as my hand was on the door, she caught fast hold of my dress and stopped me.\n\n“Let me go!” I said. “My tongue burns to tell your uncle that he and Sir Percival are not to have it all their own way.”\n\nShe sighed bitterly, and still held my dress.\n\n“No!” she said faintly. “Too late, Marian, too late!”\n\n“Not a minute too late,” I retorted. “The question of time is OUR question—and trust me, Laura, to take a woman’s full advantage of it.”\n\nI unclasped her hand from my gown while I spoke; but she slipped both her arms round my waist at the same moment, and held me more effectually than ever.\n\n“It will only involve us in more trouble and more confusion,” she said. “It will set you and my uncle at variance, and bring Sir Percival here again with fresh causes of complaint—”\n\n“So much the better!” I cried out passionately. “Who cares for his causes of complaint? Are you to break your heart to set his mind at ease? No man under heaven deserves these sacrifices from us women. Men! They are the enemies of our innocence and our peace—they drag us away from our parents’ love and our sisters’ friendship—they take us body and soul to themselves, and fasten our helpless lives to theirs as they chain up a dog to his kennel. And what does the best of them give us in return? Let me go, Laura—I’m mad when I think of it!”\n\nThe tears—miserable, weak, women’s tears of vexation and rage—started to my eyes. She smiled sadly, and put her handkerchief over my face to hide for me the betrayal of my own weakness—the weakness of all others which she knew that I most despised.\n\n“Oh, Marian!” she said. “You crying! Think what you would say to me, if the places were changed, and if those tears were mine. All your love and courage and devotion will not alter what must happen, sooner or later. Let my uncle have his way. Let us have no more troubles and heart-burnings that any sacrifice of mine can prevent. Say you will live with me, Marian, when I am married—and say no more.”\n\nBut I did say more. I forced back the contemptible tears that were no relief to ME, and that only distressed HER, and reasoned and pleaded as calmly as I could. It was of no avail. She made me twice repeat the promise to live with her when she was married, and then suddenly asked a question which turned my sorrow and my sympathy for her into a new direction.\n\n“While we were at Polesdean,” she said, “you had a letter, Marian——”\n\nHer altered tone—the abrupt manner in which she looked away from me and hid her face on my shoulder—the hesitation which silenced her before she had completed her question, all told me, but too plainly, to whom the half-expressed inquiry pointed.\n\n“I thought, Laura, that you and I were never to refer to him again,” I said gently.\n\n“You had a letter from him?” she persisted.\n\n“Yes,” I replied, “if you must know it.”\n\n“Do you mean to write to him again?”\n\nI hesitated. I had been afraid to tell her of his absence from England, or of the manner in which my exertions to serve his new hopes and projects had connected me with his departure. What answer could I make? He was gone where no letters could reach him for months, perhaps for years, to come.\n\n“Suppose I do mean to write to him again,” I said at last. “What then, Laura?”\n\nHer cheek grew burning hot against my neck, and her arms trembled and tightened round me.\n\n“Don’t tell him about THE TWENTY-SECOND,” she whispered. “Promise, Marian—pray promise you will not even mention my name to him when you write next.”\n\nI gave the promise. No words can say how sorrowfully I gave it. She instantly took her arm from my waist, walked away to the window, and stood looking out with her back to me. After a moment she spoke once more, but without turning round, without allowing me to catch the smallest glimpse of her face.\n\n“Are you going to my uncle’s room?” she asked. “Will you say that I consent to whatever arrangement he may think best? Never mind leaving me, Marian. I shall be better alone for a little while.”\n\nI went out. If, as soon as I got into the passage, I could have transported Mr. Fairlie and Sir Percival Glyde to the uttermost ends of the earth by lifting one of my fingers, that finger would have been raised without an instant’s hesitation. For once my unhappy temper now stood my friend. I should have broken down altogether and burst into a violent fit of crying, if my tears had not been all burnt up in the heat of my anger. As it was, I dashed into Mr. Fairlie’s room—called to him as harshly as possible, “Laura consents to the twenty-second”—and dashed out again without waiting for a word of answer. I banged the door after me, and I hope I shattered Mr. Fairlie’s nervous system for the rest of the day.\n\n28th.—This morning I read poor Hartright’s farewell letter over again, a doubt having crossed my mind since yesterday, whether I am acting wisely in concealing the fact of his departure from Laura.\n\nOn reflection, I still think I am right. The allusions in his letter to the preparations made for the expedition to Central America, all show that the leaders of it know it to be dangerous. If the discovery of this makes me uneasy, what would it make HER? It is bad enough to feel that his departure has deprived us of the friend of all others to whose devotion we could trust in the hour of need, if ever that hour comes and finds us helpless; but it is far worse to know that he has gone from us to face the perils of a bad climate, a wild country, and a disturbed population. Surely it would be a cruel candour to tell Laura this, without a pressing and a positive necessity for it?\n\nI almost doubt whether I ought not to go a step farther, and burn the letter at once, for fear of its one day falling into wrong hands. It not only refers to Laura in terms which ought to remain a secret for ever between the writer and me, but it reiterates his suspicion—so obstinate, so unaccountable, and so alarming—that he has been secretly watched since he left Limmeridge. He declares that he saw the faces of the two strange men who followed him about the streets of London, watching him among the crowd which gathered at Liverpool to see the expedition embark, and he positively asserts that he heard the name of Anne Catherick pronounced behind him as he got into the boat. His own words are, “These events have a meaning, these events must lead to a result. The mystery of Anne Catherick is NOT cleared up yet. She may never cross my path again, but if ever she crosses yours, make better use of the opportunity, Miss Halcombe, than I made of it. I speak on strong conviction—I entreat you to remember what I say.” These are his own expressions. There is no danger of my forgetting them—my memory is only too ready to dwell on any words of Hartright’s that refer to Anne Catherick. But there is danger in my keeping the letter. The merest accident might place it at the mercy of strangers. I may fall ill—I may die. Better to burn it at once, and have one anxiety the less.\n\nIt is burnt. The ashes of his farewell letter—the last he may ever write to me—lie in a few black fragments on the hearth. Is this the sad end to all that sad story? Oh, not the end—surely, surely not the end already!\n\n29th.—The preparations for the marriage have begun. The dressmaker has come to receive her orders. Laura is perfectly impassive, perfectly careless about the question of all others in which a woman’s personal interests are most closely bound up. She has left it all to the dressmaker and to me. If poor Hartright had been the baronet, and the husband of her father’s choice, how differently she would have behaved! How anxious and capricious she would have been, and what a hard task the best of dressmakers would have found it to please her!\n\n30th.—We hear every day from Sir Percival. The last news is that the alterations in his house will occupy from four to six months before they can be properly completed. If painters, paperhangers, and upholsterers could make happiness as well as splendour, I should be interested about their proceedings in Laura’s future home. As it is, the only part of Sir Percival’s last letter which does not leave me as it found me, perfectly indifferent to all his plans and projects, is the part which refers to the wedding tour. He proposes, as Laura is delicate, and as the winter threatens to be unusually severe, to take her to Rome, and to remain in Italy until the early part of next summer. If this plan should not be approved, he is equally ready, although he has no establishment of his own in town, to spend the season in London, in the most suitable furnished house that can be obtained for the purpose.\n\nPutting myself and my own feelings entirely out of the question (which it is my duty to do, and which I have done), I, for one, have no doubt of the propriety of adopting the first of these proposals. In either case a separation between Laura and me is inevitable. It will be a longer separation, in the event of their going abroad, than it would be in the event of their remaining in London—but we must set against this disadvantage the benefit to Laura, on the other side, of passing the winter in a mild climate, and more than that, the immense assistance in raising her spirits, and reconciling her to her new existence, which the mere wonder and excitement of travelling for the first time in her life in the most interesting country in the world, must surely afford. She is not of a disposition to find resources in the conventional gaieties and excitements of London. They would only make the first oppression of this lamentable marriage fall the heavier on her. I dread the beginning of her new life more than words can tell, but I see some hope for her if she travels—none if she remains at home.\n\nIt is strange to look back at this latest entry in my journal, and to find that I am writing of the marriage and the parting with Laura, as people write of a settled thing. It seems so cold and so unfeeling to be looking at the future already in this cruelly composed way. But what other way is possible, now that the time is drawing so near? Before another month is over our heads she will be HIS Laura instead of mine! HIS Laura! I am as little able to realise the idea which those two words convey—my mind feels almost as dulled and stunned by it—as if writing of her marriage were like writing of her death.\n\nDecember 1st.—A sad, sad day—a day that I have no heart to describe at any length. After weakly putting it off last night, I was obliged to speak to her this morning of Sir Percival’s proposal about the wedding tour.\n\nIn the full conviction that I should be with her wherever she went, the poor child—for a child she is still in many things—was almost happy at the prospect of seeing the wonders of Florence and Rome and Naples. It nearly broke my heart to dispel her delusion, and to bring her face to face with the hard truth. I was obliged to tell her that no man tolerates a rival—not even a woman rival—in his wife’s affections, when he first marries, whatever he may do afterwards. I was obliged to warn her that my chance of living with her permanently under her own roof, depended entirely on my not arousing Sir Percival’s jealousy and distrust by standing between them at the beginning of their marriage, in the position of the chosen depositary of his wife’s closest secrets. Drop by drop I poured the profaning bitterness of this world’s wisdom into that pure heart and that innocent mind, while every higher and better feeling within me recoiled from my miserable task. It is over now. She has learnt her hard, her inevitable lesson. The simple illusions of her girlhood are gone, and my hand has stripped them off. Better mine than his—that is all my consolation—better mine than his.\n\nSo the first proposal is the proposal accepted. They are to go to Italy, and I am to arrange, with Sir Percival’s permission, for meeting them and staying with them when they return to England. In other words, I am to ask a personal favour, for the first time in my life, and to ask it of the man of all others to whom I least desire to owe a serious obligation of any kind. Well! I think I could do even more than that, for Laura’s sake.\n\n2nd.—On looking back, I find myself always referring to Sir Percival in disparaging terms. In the turn affairs have now taken, I must and will root out my prejudice against him. I cannot think how it first got into my mind. It certainly never existed in former times.\n\nIs it Laura’s reluctance to become his wife that has set me against him? Have Hartright’s perfectly intelligible prejudices infected me without my suspecting their influence? Does that letter of Anne Catherick’s still leave a lurking distrust in my mind, in spite of Sir Percival’s explanation, and of the proof in my possession of the truth of it? I cannot account for the state of my own feelings; the one thing I am certain of is, that it is my duty—doubly my duty now—not to wrong Sir Percival by unjustly distrusting him. If it has got to be a habit with me always to write of him in the same unfavourable manner, I must and will break myself of this unworthy tendency, even though the effort should force me to close the pages of my journal till the marriage is over! I am seriously dissatisfied with myself—I will write no more to-day.\n\nDecember 16th.—A whole fortnight has passed, and I have not once opened these pages. I have been long enough away from my journal to come back to it with a healthier and better mind, I hope, so far as Sir Percival is concerned.\n\nThere is not much to record of the past two weeks. The dresses are almost all finished, and the new travelling trunks have been sent here from London. Poor dear Laura hardly leaves me for a moment all day, and last night, when neither of us could sleep, she came and crept into my bed to talk to me there. “I shall lose you so soon, Marian,” she said; “I must make the most of you while I can.”\n\nThey are to be married at Limmeridge Church, and thank Heaven, not one of the neighbours is to be invited to the ceremony. The only visitor will be our old friend, Mr. Arnold, who is to come from Polesdean to give Laura away, her uncle being far too delicate to trust himself outside the door in such inclement weather as we now have. If I were not determined, from this day forth, to see nothing but the bright side of our prospects, the melancholy absence of any male relative of Laura’s, at the most important moment of her life, would make me very gloomy and very distrustful of the future. But I have done with gloom and distrust—that is to say, I have done with writing about either the one or the other in this journal.\n\nSir Percival is to arrive to-morrow. He offered, in case we wished to treat him on terms of rigid etiquette, to write and ask our clergyman to grant him the hospitality of the rectory, during the short period of his sojourn at Limmeridge, before the marriage. Under the circumstances, neither Mr. Fairlie nor I thought it at all necessary for us to trouble ourselves about attending to trifling forms and ceremonies. In our wild moorland country, and in this great lonely house, we may well claim to be beyond the reach of the trivial conventionalities which hamper people in other places. I wrote to Sir Percival to thank him for his polite offer, and to beg that he would occupy his old rooms, just as usual, at Limmeridge House.\n\n17th.—He arrived to-day, looking, as I thought, a little worn and anxious, but still talking and laughing like a man in the best possible spirits. He brought with him some really beautiful presents in jewellery, which Laura received with her best grace, and, outwardly at least, with perfect self-possession. The only sign I can detect of the struggle it must cost her to preserve appearances at this trying time, expresses itself in a sudden unwillingness, on her part, ever to be left alone. Instead of retreating to her own room, as usual, she seems to dread going there. When I went upstairs to-day, after lunch, to put on my bonnet for a walk, she volunteered to join me, and again, before dinner, she threw the door open between our two rooms, so that we might talk to each other while we were dressing. “Keep me always doing something,” she said; “keep me always in company with somebody. Don’t let me think—that is all I ask now, Marian—don’t let me think.”\n\nThis sad change in her only increases her attractions for Sir Percival. He interprets it, I can see, to his own advantage. There is a feverish flush in her cheeks, a feverish brightness in her eyes, which he welcomes as the return of her beauty and the recovery of her spirits. She talked to-day at dinner with a gaiety and carelessness so false, so shockingly out of her character, that I secretly longed to silence her and take her away. Sir Percival’s delight and surprise appeared to be beyond all expression. The anxiety which I had noticed on his face when he arrived totally disappeared from it, and he looked, even to my eyes, a good ten years younger than he really is.\n\nThere can be no doubt—though some strange perversity prevents me from seeing it myself—there can be no doubt that Laura’s future husband is a very handsome man. Regular features form a personal advantage to begin with—and he has them. Bright brown eyes, either in man or woman, are a great attraction—and he has them. Even baldness, when it is only baldness over the forehead (as in his case), is rather becoming than not in a man, for it heightens the head and adds to the intelligence of the face. Grace and ease of movement, untiring animation of manner, ready, pliant, conversational powers—all these are unquestionable merits, and all these he certainly possesses. Surely Mr. Gilmore, ignorant as he is of Laura’s secret, was not to blame for feeling surprised that she should repent of her marriage engagement? Any one else in his place would have shared our good old friend’s opinion. If I were asked, at this moment, to say plainly what defects I have discovered in Sir Percival, I could only point out two. One, his incessant restlessness and excitability—which may be caused, naturally enough, by unusual energy of character. The other, his short, sharp, ill-tempered manner of speaking to the servants—which may be only a bad habit after all. No, I cannot dispute it, and I will not dispute it—Sir Percival is a very handsome and a very agreeable man. There! I have written it down at last, and I am glad it’s over.\n\n18th.—Feeling weary and depressed this morning, I left Laura with Mrs. Vesey, and went out alone for one of my brisk midday walks, which I have discontinued too much of late. I took the dry airy road over the moor that leads to Todd’s Corner. After having been out half an hour, I was excessively surprised to see Sir Percival approaching me from the direction of the farm. He was walking rapidly, swinging his stick, his head erect as usual, and his shooting jacket flying open in the wind. When we met he did not wait for me to ask any questions—he told me at once that he had been to the farm to inquire if Mr. or Mrs. Todd had received any tidings, since his last visit to Limmeridge, of Anne Catherick.\n\n“You found, of course, that they had heard nothing?” I said.\n\n“Nothing whatever,” he replied. “I begin to be seriously afraid that we have lost her. Do you happen to know,” he continued, looking me in the face very attentively “if the artist—Mr. Hartright—is in a position to give us any further information?”\n\n“He has neither heard of her, nor seen her, since he left Cumberland,” I answered.\n\n“Very sad,” said Sir Percival, speaking like a man who was disappointed, and yet, oddly enough, looking at the same time like a man who was relieved. “It is impossible to say what misfortunes may not have happened to the miserable creature. I am inexpressibly annoyed at the failure of all my efforts to restore her to the care and protection which she so urgently needs.”\n\nThis time he really looked annoyed. I said a few sympathising words, and we then talked of other subjects on our way back to the house. Surely my chance meeting with him on the moor has disclosed another favourable trait in his character? Surely it was singularly considerate and unselfish of him to think of Anne Catherick on the eve of his marriage, and to go all the way to Todd’s Corner to make inquiries about her, when he might have passed the time so much more agreeably in Laura’s society? Considering that he can only have acted from motives of pure charity, his conduct, under the circumstances, shows unusual good feeling and deserves extraordinary praise. Well! I give him extraordinary praise—and there’s an end of it.\n\n19th.—More discoveries in the inexhaustible mine of Sir Percival’s virtues.\n\nTo-day I approached the subject of my proposed sojourn under his wife’s roof when he brings her back to England. I had hardly dropped my first hint in this direction before he caught me warmly by the hand, and said I had made the very offer to him which he had been, on his side, most anxious to make to me. I was the companion of all others whom he most sincerely longed to secure for his wife, and he begged me to believe that I had conferred a lasting favour on him by making the proposal to live with Laura after her marriage, exactly as I had always lived with her before it.\n\nWhen I had thanked him in her name and mine for his considerate kindness to both of us, we passed next to the subject of his wedding tour, and began to talk of the English society in Rome to which Laura was to be introduced. He ran over the names of several friends whom he expected to meet abroad this winter. They were all English, as well as I can remember, with one exception. The one exception was Count Fosco.\n\nThe mention of the Count’s name, and the discovery that he and his wife are likely to meet the bride and bridegroom on the continent, puts Laura’s marriage, for the first time, in a distinctly favourable light. It is likely to be the means of healing a family feud. Hitherto Madame Fosco has chosen to forget her obligations as Laura’s aunt out of sheer spite against the late Mr. Fairlie for his conduct in the affair of the legacy. Now however, she can persist in this course of conduct no longer. Sir Percival and Count Fosco are old and fast friends, and their wives will have no choice but to meet on civil terms. Madame Fosco in her maiden days was one of the most impertinent women I ever met with—capricious, exacting, and vain to the last degree of absurdity. If her husband has succeeded in bringing her to her senses, he deserves the gratitude of every member of the family, and he may have mine to begin with.\n\nI am becoming anxious to know the Count. He is the most intimate friend of Laura’s husband, and in that capacity he excites my strongest interest. Neither Laura nor I have ever seen him. All I know of him is that his accidental presence, years ago, on the steps of the Trinita del Monte at Rome, assisted Sir Percival’s escape from robbery and assassination at the critical moment when he was wounded in the hand, and might the next instant have been wounded in the heart. I remember also that, at the time of the late Mr. Fairlie’s absurd objections to his sister’s marriage, the Count wrote him a very temperate and sensible letter on the subject, which, I am ashamed to say, remained unanswered. This is all I know of Sir Percival’s friend. I wonder if he will ever come to England? I wonder if I shall like him?\n\nMy pen is running away into mere speculation. Let me return to sober matter of fact. It is certain that Sir Percival’s reception of my venturesome proposal to live with his wife was more than kind, it was almost affectionate. I am sure Laura’s husband will have no reason to complain of me if I can only go on as I have begun. I have already declared him to be handsome, agreeable, full of good feeling towards the unfortunate and full of affectionate kindness towards me. Really, I hardly know myself again, in my new character of Sir Percival’s warmest friend.\n\n20th.—I hate Sir Percival! I flatly deny his good looks. I consider him to be eminently ill-tempered and disagreeable, and totally wanting in kindness and good feeling. Last night the cards for the married couple were sent home. Laura opened the packet and saw her future name in print for the first time. Sir Percival looked over her shoulder familiarly at the new card which had already transformed Miss Fairlie into Lady Glyde—smiled with the most odious self-complacency, and whispered something in her ear. I don’t know what it was—Laura has refused to tell me—but I saw her face turn to such a deadly whiteness that I thought she would have fainted. He took no notice of the change—he seemed to be barbarously unconscious that he had said anything to pain her. All my old feelings of hostility towards him revived on the instant, and all the hours that have passed since have done nothing to dissipate them. I am more unreasonable and more unjust than ever. In three words—how glibly my pen writes them!—in three words, I hate him.\n\n21st.—Have the anxieties of this anxious time shaken me a little, at last? I have been writing, for the last few days, in a tone of levity which, Heaven knows, is far enough from my heart, and which it has rather shocked me to discover on looking back at the entries in my journal.\n\nPerhaps I may have caught the feverish excitement of Laura’s spirits for the last week. If so, the fit has already passed away from me, and has left me in a very strange state of mind. A persistent idea has been forcing itself on my attention, ever since last night, that something will yet happen to prevent the marriage. What has produced this singular fancy? Is it the indirect result of my apprehensions for Laura’s future? Or has it been unconsciously suggested to me by the increasing restlessness and irritability which I have certainly observed in Sir Percival’s manner as the wedding-day draws nearer and nearer? Impossible to say. I know that I have the idea—surely the wildest idea, under the circumstances, that ever entered a woman’s head?—but try as I may, I cannot trace it back to its source.\n\nThis last day has been all confusion and wretchedness. How can I write about it?—and yet, I must write. Anything is better than brooding over my own gloomy thoughts.\n\nKind Mrs. Vesey, whom we have all too much overlooked and forgotten of late, innocently caused us a sad morning to begin with. She has been, for months past, secretly making a warm Shetland shawl for her dear pupil—a most beautiful and surprising piece of work to be done by a woman at her age and with her habits. The gift was presented this morning, and poor warm-hearted Laura completely broke down when the shawl was put proudly on her shoulders by the loving old friend and guardian of her motherless childhood. I was hardly allowed time to quiet them both, or even to dry my own eyes, when I was sent for by Mr. Fairlie, to be favoured with a long recital of his arrangements for the preservation of his own tranquillity on the wedding-day.\n\n“Dear Laura” was to receive his present—a shabby ring, with her affectionate uncle’s hair for an ornament, instead of a precious stone, and with a heartless French inscription inside, about congenial sentiments and eternal friendship—“dear Laura” was to receive this tender tribute from my hands immediately, so that she might have plenty of time to recover from the agitation produced by the gift before she appeared in Mr. Fairlie’s presence. “Dear Laura” was to pay him a little visit that evening, and to be kind enough not to make a scene. “Dear Laura” was to pay him another little visit in her wedding-dress the next morning, and to be kind enough, again, not to make a scene. “Dear Laura” was to look in once more, for the third time, before going away, but without harrowing his feelings by saying WHEN she was going away, and without tears—“in the name of pity, in the name of everything, dear Marian, that is most affectionate and most domestic, and most delightfully and charmingly self-composed, WITHOUT TEARS!” I was so exasperated by this miserable selfish trifling, at such a time, that I should certainly have shocked Mr. Fairlie by some of the hardest and rudest truths he has ever heard in his life, if the arrival of Mr. Arnold from Polesdean had not called me away to new duties downstairs.\n\nThe rest of the day is indescribable. I believe no one in the house really knew how it passed. The confusion of small events, all huddled together one on the other, bewildered everybody. There were dresses sent home that had been forgotten—there were trunks to be packed and unpacked and packed again—there were presents from friends far and near, friends high and low. We were all needlessly hurried, all nervously expectant of the morrow. Sir Percival, especially, was too restless now to remain five minutes together in the same place. That short, sharp cough of his troubled him more than ever. He was in and out of doors all day long, and he seemed to grow so inquisitive on a sudden, that he questioned the very strangers who came on small errands to the house. Add to all this, the one perpetual thought in Laura’s mind and mine, that we were to part the next day, and the haunting dread, unexpressed by either of us, and yet ever present to both, that this deplorable marriage might prove to be the one fatal error of her life and the one hopeless sorrow of mine. For the first time in all the years of our close and happy intercourse we almost avoided looking each other in the face, and we refrained, by common consent, from speaking together in private through the whole evening. I can dwell on it no longer. Whatever future sorrows may be in store for me, I shall always look back on this twenty-first of December as the most comfortless and most miserable day of my life.\n\nI am writing these lines in the solitude of my own room, long after midnight, having just come back from a stolen look at Laura in her pretty little white bed—the bed she has occupied since the days of her girlhood.\n\nThere she lay, unconscious that I was looking at her—quiet, more quiet than I had dared to hope, but not sleeping. The glimmer of the night-light showed me that her eyes were only partially closed—the traces of tears glistened between her eyelids. My little keepsake—only a brooch—lay on the table at her bedside, with her prayer-book, and the miniature portrait of her father which she takes with her wherever she goes. I waited a moment, looking at her from behind her pillow, as she lay beneath me, with one arm and hand resting on the white coverlid, so still, so quietly breathing, that the frill on her night-dress never moved—I waited, looking at her, as I have seen her thousands of times, as I shall never see her again—and then stole back to my room. My own love! with all your wealth, and all your beauty, how friendless you are! The one man who would give his heart’s life to serve you is far away, tossing, this stormy night, on the awful sea. Who else is left to you? No father, no brother—no living creature but the helpless, useless woman who writes these sad lines, and watches by you for the morning, in sorrow that she cannot compose, in doubt that she cannot conquer. Oh, what a trust is to be placed in that man’s hands to-morrow! If ever he forgets it—if ever he injures a hair of her head!——\n\nTHE TWENTY-SECOND OF DECEMBER. Seven o’clock. A wild, unsettled morning. She has just risen—better and calmer, now that the time has come, than she was yesterday.\n\nTen o’clock. She is dressed. We have kissed each other—we have promised each other not to lose courage. I am away for a moment in my own room. In the whirl and confusion of my thoughts, I can detect that strange fancy of some hindrance happening to stop the marriage still hanging about my mind. Is it hanging about HIS mind too? I see him from the window, moving hither and thither uneasily among the carriages at the door.—How can I write such folly! The marriage is a certainty. In less than half an hour we start for the church.\n\nEleven o’clock. It is all over. They are married.\n\nThree o’clock. They are gone! I am blind with crying—I can write no more——\n\n[The First Epoch of the Story closes here.]\n\n# BLACKWATER PARK, HAMPSHIRE.\n\nJune 11th, 1850.—Six months to look back on—six long, lonely months since Laura and I last saw each other!\n\nHow many days have I still to wait? Only one! To-morrow, the twelfth, the travellers return to England. I can hardly realise my own happiness—I can hardly believe that the next four-and-twenty hours will complete the last day of separation between Laura and me.\n\nShe and her husband have been in Italy all the winter, and afterwards in the Tyrol. They come back, accompanied by Count Fosco and his wife, who propose to settle somewhere in the neighbourhood of London, and who have engaged to stay at Blackwater Park for the summer months before deciding on a place of residence. So long as Laura returns, no matter who returns with her. Sir Percival may fill the house from floor to ceiling, if he likes, on condition that his wife and I inhabit it together.\n\nMeanwhile, here I am, established at Blackwater Park, “the ancient and interesting seat” (as the county history obligingly informs me) “of Sir Percival Glyde, Bart.,” and the future abiding-place (as I may now venture to add on my account) of plain Marian Halcombe, spinster, now settled in a snug little sitting-room, with a cup of tea by her side, and all her earthly possessions ranged round her in three boxes and a bag.\n\nI left Limmeridge yesterday, having received Laura’s delightful letter from Paris the day before. I had been previously uncertain whether I was to meet them in London or in Hampshire, but this last letter informed me that Sir Percival proposed to land at Southampton, and to travel straight on to his country-house. He has spent so much money abroad that he has none left to defray the expenses of living in London for the remainder of the season, and he is economically resolved to pass the summer and autumn quietly at Blackwater. Laura has had more than enough of excitement and change of scene, and is pleased at the prospect of country tranquillity and retirement which her husband’s prudence provides for her. As for me, I am ready to be happy anywhere in her society. We are all, therefore, well contented in our various ways, to begin with.\n\nLast night I slept in London, and was delayed there so long to-day by various calls and commissions, that I did not reach Blackwater this evening till after dusk.\n\nJudging by my vague impressions of the place thus far, it is the exact opposite of Limmeridge.\n\nThe house is situated on a dead flat, and seems to be shut in—almost suffocated, to my north-country notions, by trees. I have seen nobody but the man-servant who opened the door to me, and the housekeeper, a very civil person, who showed me the way to my own room, and got me my tea. I have a nice little boudoir and bedroom, at the end of a long passage on the first floor. The servants and some of the spare rooms are on the second floor, and all the living rooms are on the ground floor. I have not seen one of them yet, and I know nothing about the house, except that one wing of it is said to be five hundred years old, that it had a moat round it once, and that it gets its name of Blackwater from a lake in the park.\n\nEleven o’clock has just struck, in a ghostly and solemn manner, from a turret over the centre of the house, which I saw when I came in. A large dog has been woke, apparently by the sound of the bell, and is howling and yawning drearily, somewhere round a corner. I hear echoing footsteps in the passages below, and the iron thumping of bolts and bars at the house door. The servants are evidently going to bed. Shall I follow their example?\n\nNo, I am not half sleepy enough. Sleepy, did I say? I feel as if I should never close my eyes again. The bare anticipation of seeing that dear face, and hearing that well-known voice to-morrow, keeps me in a perpetual fever of excitement. If I only had the privileges of a man, I would order out Sir Percival’s best horse instantly, and tear away on a night-gallop, eastward, to meet the rising sun—a long, hard, heavy, ceaseless gallop of hours and hours, like the famous highwayman’s ride to York. Being, however, nothing but a woman, condemned to patience, propriety, and petticoats for life, I must respect the house-keeper’s opinions, and try to compose myself in some feeble and feminine way.\n\nReading is out of the question—I can’t fix my attention on books. Let me try if I can write myself into sleepiness and fatigue. My journal has been very much neglected of late. What can I recall—standing, as I now do, on the threshold of a new life—of persons and events, of chances and changes, during the past six months—the long, weary, empty interval since Laura’s wedding-day?\n\nWalter Hartright is uppermost in my memory, and he passes first in the shadowy procession of my absent friends. I received a few lines from him, after the landing of the expedition in Honduras, written more cheerfully and hopefully than he has written yet. A month or six weeks later I saw an extract from an American newspaper, describing the departure of the adventurers on their inland journey. They were last seen entering a wild primeval forest, each man with his rifle on his shoulder and his baggage at his back. Since that time, civilisation has lost all trace of them. Not a line more have I received from Walter, not a fragment of news from the expedition has appeared in any of the public journals.\n\nThe same dense, disheartening obscurity hangs over the fate and fortunes of Anne Catherick, and her companion, Mrs. Clements. Nothing whatever has been heard of either of them. Whether they are in the country or out of it, whether they are living or dead, no one knows. Even Sir Percival’s solicitor has lost all hope, and has ordered the useless search after the fugitives to be finally given up.\n\nOur good old friend Mr. Gilmore has met with a sad check in his active professional career. Early in the spring we were alarmed by hearing that he had been found insensible at his desk, and that the seizure was pronounced to be an apoplectic fit. He had been long complaining of fulness and oppression in the head, and his doctor had warned him of the consequences that would follow his persistency in continuing to work, early and late, as if he were still a young man. The result now is that he has been positively ordered to keep out of his office for a year to come, at least, and to seek repose of body and relief of mind by altogether changing his usual mode of life. The business is left, accordingly, to be carried on by his partner, and he is himself, at this moment, away in Germany, visiting some relations who are settled there in mercantile pursuits. Thus another true friend and trustworthy adviser is lost to us—lost, I earnestly hope and trust, for a time only.\n\nPoor Mrs. Vesey travelled with me as far as London. It was impossible to abandon her to solitude at Limmeridge after Laura and I had both left the house, and we have arranged that she is to live with an unmarried younger sister of hers, who keeps a school at Clapham. She is to come here this autumn to visit her pupil—I might almost say her adopted child. I saw the good old lady safe to her destination, and left her in the care of her relative, quietly happy at the prospect of seeing Laura again in a few months’ time.\n\nAs for Mr. Fairlie, I believe I am guilty of no injustice if I describe him as being unutterably relieved by having the house clear of us women. The idea of his missing his niece is simply preposterous—he used to let months pass in the old times without attempting to see her—and in my case and Mrs. Vesey’s, I take leave to consider his telling us both that he was half heart-broken at our departure, to be equivalent to a confession that he was secretly rejoiced to get rid of us. His last caprice has led him to keep two photographers incessantly employed in producing sun-pictures of all the treasures and curiosities in his possession. One complete copy of the collection of the photographs is to be presented to the Mechanics’ Institution of Carlisle, mounted on the finest cardboard, with ostentatious red-letter inscriptions underneath, “Madonna and Child by Raphael. In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire.” “Copper coin of the period of Tiglath Pileser. In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire.” “Unique Rembrandt etching. Known all over Europe as THE SMUDGE, from a printer’s blot in the corner which exists in no other copy. Valued at three hundred guineas. In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esq.” Dozens of photographs of this sort, and all inscribed in this manner, were completed before I left Cumberland, and hundreds more remain to be done. With this new interest to occupy him, Mr. Fairlie will be a happy man for months and months to come, and the two unfortunate photographers will share the social martyrdom which he has hitherto inflicted on his valet alone.\n\nSo much for the persons and events which hold the foremost place in my memory. What next of the one person who holds the foremost place in my heart? Laura has been present to my thoughts all the while I have been writing these lines. What can I recall of her during the past six months, before I close my journal for the night?\n\nI have only her letters to guide me, and on the most important of all the questions which our correspondence can discuss, every one of those letters leaves me in the dark.\n\nDoes he treat her kindly? Is she happier now than she was when I parted with her on the wedding-day? All my letters have contained these two inquiries, put more or less directly, now in one form, and now in another, and all, on that point only, have remained without reply, or have been answered as if my questions merely related to the state of her health. She informs me, over and over again, that she is perfectly well—that travelling agrees with her—that she is getting through the winter, for the first time in her life, without catching cold—but not a word can I find anywhere which tells me plainly that she is reconciled to her marriage, and that she can now look back to the twenty-second of December without any bitter feelings of repentance and regret. The name of her husband is only mentioned in her letters, as she might mention the name of a friend who was travelling with them, and who had undertaken to make all the arrangements for the journey. “Sir Percival” has settled that we leave on such a day—“Sir Percival” has decided that we travel by such a road. Sometimes she writes “Percival” only, but very seldom—in nine cases out of ten she gives him his title.\n\nI cannot find that his habits and opinions have changed and coloured hers in any single particular. The usual moral transformation which is insensibly wrought in a young, fresh, sensitive woman by her marriage, seems never to have taken place in Laura. She writes of her own thoughts and impressions, amid all the wonders she has seen, exactly as she might have written to some one else, if I had been travelling with her instead of her husband. I see no betrayal anywhere of sympathy of any kind existing between them. Even when she wanders from the subject of her travels, and occupies herself with the prospects that await her in England, her speculations are busied with her future as my sister, and persistently neglect to notice her future as Sir Percival’s wife. In all this there is no undertone of complaint to warn me that she is absolutely unhappy in her married life. The impression I have derived from our correspondence does not, thank God, lead me to any such distressing conclusion as that. I only see a sad torpor, an unchangeable indifference, when I turn my mind from her in the old character of a sister, and look at her, through the medium of her letters, in the new character of a wife. In other words, it is always Laura Fairlie who has been writing to me for the last six months, and never Lady Glyde.\n\nThe strange silence which she maintains on the subject of her husband’s character and conduct, she preserves with almost equal resolution in the few references which her later letters contain to the name of her husband’s bosom friend, Count Fosco.\n\nFor some unexplained reason the Count and his wife appear to have changed their plans abruptly, at the end of last autumn, and to have gone to Vienna instead of going to Rome, at which latter place Sir Percival had expected to find them when he left England. They only quitted Vienna in the spring, and travelled as far as the Tyrol to meet the bride and bridegroom on their homeward journey. Laura writes readily enough about the meeting with Madame Fosco, and assures me that she has found her aunt so much changed for the better—so much quieter, and so much more sensible as a wife than she was as a single woman—that I shall hardly know her again when I see her here. But on the subject of Count Fosco (who interests me infinitely more than his wife), Laura is provokingly circumspect and silent. She only says that he puzzles her, and that she will not tell me what her impression of him is until I have seen him, and formed my own opinion first.\n\nThis, to my mind, looks ill for the Count. Laura has preserved, far more perfectly than most people do in later life, the child’s subtle faculty of knowing a friend by instinct, and if I am right in assuming that her first impression of Count Fosco has not been favourable, I for one am in some danger of doubting and distrusting that illustrious foreigner before I have so much as set eyes on him. But, patience, patience—this uncertainty, and many uncertainties more, cannot last much longer. To-morrow will see all my doubts in a fair way of being cleared up, sooner or later.\n\nTwelve o’clock has struck, and I have just come back to close these pages, after looking out at my open window.\n\nIt is a still, sultry, moonless night. The stars are dull and few. The trees that shut out the view on all sides look dimly black and solid in the distance, like a great wall of rock. I hear the croaking of frogs, faint and far off, and the echoes of the great clock hum in the airless calm long after the strokes have ceased. I wonder how Blackwater Park will look in the daytime? I don’t altogether like it by night.\n\n12th.—A day of investigations and discoveries—a more interesting day, for many reasons, than I had ventured to anticipate.\n\nI began my sight-seeing, of course, with the house.\n\nThe main body of the building is of the time of that highly-overrated woman, Queen Elizabeth. On the ground floor there are two hugely long galleries, with low ceilings lying parallel with each other, and rendered additionally dark and dismal by hideous family portraits—every one of which I should like to burn. The rooms on the floor above the two galleries are kept in tolerable repair, but are very seldom used. The civil housekeeper, who acted as my guide, offered to show me over them, but considerately added that she feared I should find them rather out of order. My respect for the integrity of my own petticoats and stockings infinitely exceeds my respect for all the Elizabethan bedrooms in the kingdom, so I positively declined exploring the upper regions of dust and dirt at the risk of soiling my nice clean clothes. The housekeeper said, “I am quite of your opinion, miss,” and appeared to think me the most sensible woman she had met with for a long time past.\n\nSo much, then, for the main building. Two wings are added at either end of it. The half-ruined wing on the left (as you approach the house) was once a place of residence standing by itself, and was built in the fourteenth century. One of Sir Percival’s maternal ancestors—I don’t remember, and don’t care which—tacked on the main building, at right angles to it, in the aforesaid Queen Elizabeth’s time. The housekeeper told me that the architecture of “the old wing,” both outside and inside, was considered remarkably fine by good judges. On further investigation I discovered that good judges could only exercise their abilities on Sir Percival’s piece of antiquity by previously dismissing from their minds all fear of damp, darkness, and rats. Under these circumstances, I unhesitatingly acknowledged myself to be no judge at all, and suggested that we should treat “the old wing” precisely as we had previously treated the Elizabethan bedrooms. Once more the housekeeper said, “I am quite of your opinion, miss,” and once more she looked at me with undisguised admiration of my extraordinary common-sense.\n\nWe went next to the wing on the right, which was built, by way of completing the wonderful architectural jumble at Blackwater Park, in the time of George the Second.\n\nThis is the habitable part of the house, which has been repaired and redecorated inside on Laura’s account. My two rooms, and all the good bedrooms besides, are on the first floor, and the basement contains a drawing-room, a dining-room, a morning-room, a library, and a pretty little boudoir for Laura, all very nicely ornamented in the bright modern way, and all very elegantly furnished with the delightful modern luxuries. None of the rooms are anything like so large and airy as our rooms at Limmeridge, but they all look pleasant to live in. I was terribly afraid, from what I had heard of Blackwater Park, of fatiguing antique chairs, and dismal stained glass, and musty, frouzy hangings, and all the barbarous lumber which people born without a sense of comfort accumulate about them, in defiance of the consideration due to the convenience of their friends. It is an inexpressible relief to find that the nineteenth century has invaded this strange future home of mine, and has swept the dirty “good old times” out of the way of our daily life.\n\nI dawdled away the morning—part of the time in the rooms downstairs, and part out of doors in the great square which is formed by the three sides of the house, and by the lofty iron railings and gates which protect it in front. A large circular fish-pond with stone sides, and an allegorical leaden monster in the middle, occupies the centre of the square. The pond itself is full of gold and silver fish, and is encircled by a broad belt of the softest turf I ever walked on. I loitered here on the shady side pleasantly enough till luncheon-time, and after that took my broad straw hat and wandered out alone in the warm lovely sunlight to explore the grounds.\n\nDaylight confirmed the impression which I had felt the night before, of there being too many trees at Blackwater. The house is stifled by them. They are, for the most part, young, and planted far too thickly. I suspect there must have been a ruinous cutting down of timber all over the estate before Sir Percival’s time, and an angry anxiety on the part of the next possessor to fill up all the gaps as thickly and rapidly as possible. After looking about me in front of the house, I observed a flower-garden on my left hand, and walked towards it to see what I could discover in that direction.\n\nOn a nearer view the garden proved to be small and poor and ill kept. I left it behind me, opened a little gate in a ring fence, and found myself in a plantation of fir-trees.\n\nA pretty winding path, artificially made, led me on among the trees, and my north-country experience soon informed me that I was approaching sandy, heathy ground. After a walk of more than half a mile, I should think, among the firs, the path took a sharp turn—the trees abruptly ceased to appear on either side of me, and I found myself standing suddenly on the margin of a vast open space, and looking down at the Blackwater lake from which the house takes its name.\n\nThe ground, shelving away below me, was all sand, with a few little heathy hillocks to break the monotony of it in certain places. The lake itself had evidently once flowed to the spot on which I stood, and had been gradually wasted and dried up to less than a third of its former size. I saw its still, stagnant waters, a quarter of a mile away from me in the hollow, separated into pools and ponds by twining reeds and rushes, and little knolls of earth. On the farther bank from me the trees rose thickly again, and shut out the view, and cast their black shadows on the sluggish, shallow water. As I walked down to the lake, I saw that the ground on its farther side was damp and marshy, overgrown with rank grass and dismal willows. The water, which was clear enough on the open sandy side, where the sun shone, looked black and poisonous opposite to me, where it lay deeper under the shade of the spongy banks, and the rank overhanging thickets and tangled trees. The frogs were croaking, and the rats were slipping in and out of the shadowy water, like live shadows themselves, as I got nearer to the marshy side of the lake. I saw here, lying half in and half out of the water, the rotten wreck of an old overturned boat, with a sickly spot of sunlight glimmering through a gap in the trees on its dry surface, and a snake basking in the midst of the spot, fantastically coiled and treacherously still. Far and near the view suggested the same dreary impressions of solitude and decay, and the glorious brightness of the summer sky overhead seemed only to deepen and harden the gloom and barrenness of the wilderness on which it shone. I turned and retraced my steps to the high heathy ground, directing them a little aside from my former path towards a shabby old wooden shed, which stood on the outer skirt of the fir plantation, and which had hitherto been too unimportant to share my notice with the wide, wild prospect of the lake.\n\nOn approaching the shed I found that it had once been a boat-house, and that an attempt had apparently been made to convert it afterwards into a sort of rude arbour, by placing inside it a firwood seat, a few stools, and a table. I entered the place, and sat down for a little while to rest and get my breath again.\n\nI had not been in the boat-house more than a minute when it struck me that the sound of my own quick breathing was very strangely echoed by something beneath me. I listened intently for a moment, and heard a low, thick, sobbing breath that seemed to come from the ground under the seat which I was occupying. My nerves are not easily shaken by trifles, but on this occasion I started to my feet in a fright—called out—received no answer—summoned back my recreant courage, and looked under the seat.\n\nThere, crouched up in the farthest corner, lay the forlorn cause of my terror, in the shape of a poor little dog—a black and white spaniel. The creature moaned feebly when I looked at it and called to it, but never stirred. I moved away the seat and looked closer. The poor little dog’s eyes were glazing fast, and there were spots of blood on its glossy white side. The misery of a weak, helpless, dumb creature is surely one of the saddest of all the mournful sights which this world can show. I lifted the poor dog in my arms as gently as I could, and contrived a sort of make-shift hammock for him to lie in, by gathering up the front of my dress all round him. In this way I took the creature, as painlessly as possible, and as fast as possible, back to the house.\n\nFinding no one in the hall I went up at once to my own sitting-room, made a bed for the dog with one of my old shawls, and rang the bell. The largest and fattest of all possible house-maids answered it, in a state of cheerful stupidity which would have provoked the patience of a saint. The girl’s fat, shapeless face actually stretched into a broad grin at the sight of the wounded creature on the floor.\n\n“What do you see there to laugh at?” I asked, as angrily as if she had been a servant of my own. “Do you know whose dog it is?”\n\n“No, miss, that I certainly don’t.” She stooped, and looked down at the spaniel’s injured side—brightened suddenly with the irradiation of a new idea—and pointing to the wound with a chuckle of satisfaction, said, “That’s Baxter’s doings, that is.”\n\nI was so exasperated that I could have boxed her ears. “Baxter?” I said. “Who is the brute you call Baxter?”\n\nThe girl grinned again more cheerfully than ever. “Bless you, miss! Baxter’s the keeper, and when he finds strange dogs hunting about, he takes and shoots ’em. It’s keeper’s dooty, miss. I think that dog will die. Here’s where he’s been shot, ain’t it? That’s Baxter’s doings, that is. Baxter’s doings, miss, and Baxter’s dooty.”\n\nI was almost wicked enough to wish that Baxter had shot the housemaid instead of the dog. Seeing that it was quite useless to expect this densely impenetrable personage to give me any help in relieving the suffering creature at our feet, I told her to request the housekeeper’s attendance with my compliments. She went out exactly as she had come in, grinning from ear to ear. As the door closed on her she said to herself softly, “It’s Baxter’s doings and Baxter’s dooty—that’s what it is.”\n\nThe housekeeper, a person of some education and intelligence, thoughtfully brought upstairs with her some milk and some warm water. The instant she saw the dog on the floor she started and changed colour.\n\n“Why, Lord bless me,” cried the housekeeper, “that must be Mrs. Catherick’s dog!”\n\n“Whose?” I asked, in the utmost astonishment.\n\n“Mrs. Catherick’s. You seem to know Mrs. Catherick, Miss Halcombe?”\n\n“Not personally, but I have heard of her. Does she live here? Has she had any news of her daughter?”\n\n“No, Miss Halcombe, she came here to ask for news.”\n\n“When?”\n\n“Only yesterday. She said some one had reported that a stranger answering to the description of her daughter had been seen in our neighbourhood. No such report has reached us here, and no such report was known in the village, when I sent to make inquiries there on Mrs. Catherick’s account. She certainly brought this poor little dog with her when she came, and I saw it trot out after her when she went away. I suppose the creature strayed into the plantations, and got shot. Where did you find it, Miss Halcombe?”\n\n“In the old shed that looks out on the lake.”\n\n“Ah, yes, that is the plantation side, and the poor thing dragged itself, I suppose, to the nearest shelter, as dogs will, to die. If you can moisten its lips with the milk, Miss Halcombe, I will wash the clotted hair from the wound. I am very much afraid it is too late to do any good. However, we can but try.”\n\nMrs. Catherick! The name still rang in my ears, as if the housekeeper had only that moment surprised me by uttering it. While we were attending to the dog, the words of Walter Hartright’s caution to me returned to my memory: “If ever Anne Catherick crosses your path, make better use of the opportunity, Miss Halcombe, than I made of it.” The finding of the wounded spaniel had led me already to the discovery of Mrs. Catherick’s visit to Blackwater Park, and that event might lead in its turn, to something more. I determined to make the most of the chance which was now offered to me, and to gain as much information as I could.\n\n“Did you say that Mrs. Catherick lived anywhere in this neighbourhood?” I asked.\n\n“Oh dear, no,” said the housekeeper. “She lives at Welmingham, quite at the other end of the county—five-and-twenty miles off, at least.”\n\n“I suppose you have known Mrs. Catherick for some years?”\n\n“On the contrary, Miss Halcombe, I never saw her before she came here yesterday. I had heard of her, of course, because I had heard of Sir Percival’s kindness in putting her daughter under medical care. Mrs. Catherick is rather a strange person in her manners, but extremely respectable-looking. She seemed sorely put out when she found that there was no foundation—none, at least, that any of us could discover—for the report of her daughter having been seen in this neighbourhood.”\n\n“I am rather interested about Mrs. Catherick,” I went on, continuing the conversation as long as possible. “I wish I had arrived here soon enough to see her yesterday. Did she stay for any length of time?”\n\n“Yes,” said the housekeeper, “she stayed for some time; and I think she would have remained longer, if I had not been called away to speak to a strange gentleman—a gentleman who came to ask when Sir Percival was expected back. Mrs. Catherick got up and left at once, when she heard the maid tell me what the visitor’s errand was. She said to me, at parting, that there was no need to tell Sir Percival of her coming here. I thought that rather an odd remark to make, especially to a person in my responsible situation.”\n\nI thought it an odd remark too. Sir Percival had certainly led me to believe, at Limmeridge, that the most perfect confidence existed between himself and Mrs. Catherick. If that was the case, why should she be anxious to have her visit at Blackwater Park kept a secret from him?\n\n“Probably,” I said, seeing that the housekeeper expected me to give my opinion on Mrs. Catherick’s parting words, “probably she thought the announcement of her visit might vex Sir Percival to no purpose, by reminding him that her lost daughter was not found yet. Did she talk much on that subject?”\n\n“Very little,” replied the housekeeper. “She talked principally of Sir Percival, and asked a great many questions about where he had been travelling, and what sort of lady his new wife was. She seemed to be more soured and put out than distressed, by failing to find any traces of her daughter in these parts. ‘I give her up,’ were the last words she said that I can remember; ‘I give her up, ma’am, for lost.’ And from that she passed at once to her questions about Lady Glyde, wanting to know if she was a handsome, amiable lady, comely and healthy and young——Ah, dear! I thought how it would end. Look, Miss Halcombe, the poor thing is out of its misery at last!”\n\nThe dog was dead. It had given a faint, sobbing cry, it had suffered an instant’s convulsion of the limbs, just as those last words, “comely and healthy and young,” dropped from the housekeeper’s lips. The change had happened with startling suddenness—in one moment the creature lay lifeless under our hands.\n\nEight o’clock. I have just returned from dining downstairs, in solitary state. The sunset is burning redly on the wilderness of trees that I see from my window, and I am poring over my journal again, to calm my impatience for the return of the travellers. They ought to have arrived, by my calculations, before this. How still and lonely the house is in the drowsy evening quiet! Oh me! how many minutes more before I hear the carriage wheels and run downstairs to find myself in Laura’s arms?\n\nThe poor little dog! I wish my first day at Blackwater Park had not been associated with death, though it is only the death of a stray animal.\n\nWelmingham—I see, on looking back through these private pages of mine, that Welmingham is the name of the place where Mrs. Catherick lives. Her note is still in my possession, the note in answer to that letter about her unhappy daughter which Sir Percival obliged me to write. One of these days, when I can find a safe opportunity, I will take the note with me by way of introduction, and try what I can make of Mrs. Catherick at a personal interview. I don’t understand her wishing to conceal her visit to this place from Sir Percival’s knowledge, and I don’t feel half so sure, as the housekeeper seems to do, that her daughter Anne is not in the neighbourhood after all. What would Walter Hartright have said in this emergency? Poor, dear Hartright! I am beginning to feel the want of his honest advice and his willing help already.\n\nSurely I heard something. Was it a bustle of footsteps below stairs? Yes! I hear the horses’ feet—I hear the rolling wheels——\n\n# II\n\nJune 15th.—The confusion of their arrival has had time to subside. Two days have elapsed since the return of the travellers, and that interval has sufficed to put the new machinery of our lives at Blackwater Park in fair working order. I may now return to my journal, with some little chance of being able to continue the entries in it as collectedly as usual.\n\nI think I must begin by putting down an odd remark which has suggested itself to me since Laura came back.\n\nWhen two members of a family or two intimate friends are separated, and one goes abroad and one remains at home, the return of the relative or friend who has been travelling always seems to place the relative or friend who has been staying at home at a painful disadvantage when the two first meet. The sudden encounter of the new thoughts and new habits eagerly gained in the one case, with the old thoughts and old habits passively preserved in the other, seems at first to part the sympathies of the most loving relatives and the fondest friends, and to set a sudden strangeness, unexpected by both and uncontrollable by both, between them on either side. After the first happiness of my meeting with Laura was over, after we had sat down together hand in hand to recover breath enough and calmness enough to talk, I felt this strangeness instantly, and I could see that she felt it too. It has partially worn away, now that we have fallen back into most of our old habits, and it will probably disappear before long. But it has certainly had an influence over the first impressions that I have formed of her, now that we are living together again—for which reason only I have thought fit to mention it here.\n\nShe has found me unaltered, but I have found her changed.\n\nChanged in person, and in one respect changed in character. I cannot absolutely say that she is less beautiful than she used to be—I can only say that she is less beautiful to me.\n\nOthers, who do not look at her with my eyes and my recollections, would probably think her improved. There is more colour and more decision and roundness of outline in her face than there used to be, and her figure seems more firmly set and more sure and easy in all its movements than it was in her maiden days. But I miss something when I look at her—something that once belonged to the happy, innocent life of Laura Fairlie, and that I cannot find in Lady Glyde. There was in the old times a freshness, a softness, an ever-varying and yet ever-remaining tenderness of beauty in her face, the charm of which it is not possible to express in words, or, as poor Hartright used often to say, in painting either. This is gone. I thought I saw the faint reflection of it for a moment when she turned pale under the agitation of our sudden meeting on the evening of her return, but it has never reappeared since. None of her letters had prepared me for a personal change in her. On the contrary, they had led me to expect that her marriage had left her, in appearance at least, quite unaltered. Perhaps I read her letters wrongly in the past, and am now reading her face wrongly in the present? No matter! Whether her beauty has gained or whether it has lost in the last six months, the separation either way has made her own dear self more precious to me than ever, and that is one good result of her marriage, at any rate!\n\nThe second change, the change that I have observed in her character, has not surprised me, because I was prepared for it in this case by the tone of her letters. Now that she is at home again, I find her just as unwilling to enter into any details on the subject of her married life as I had previously found her all through the time of our separation, when we could only communicate with each other by writing. At the first approach I made to the forbidden topic she put her hand on my lips with a look and gesture which touchingly, almost painfully, recalled to my memory the days of her girlhood and the happy bygone time when there were no secrets between us.\n\n“Whenever you and I are together, Marian,” she said, “we shall both be happier and easier with one another, if we accept my married life for what it is, and say and think as little about it as possible. I would tell you everything, darling, about myself,” she went on, nervously buckling and unbuckling the ribbon round my waist, “if my confidences could only end there. But they could not—they would lead me into confidences about my husband too; and now I am married, I think I had better avoid them, for his sake, and for your sake, and for mine. I don’t say that they would distress you, or distress me—I wouldn’t have you think that for the world. But—I want to be so happy, now I have got you back again, and I want you to be so happy too——” She broke off abruptly, and looked round the room, my own sitting-room, in which we were talking. “Ah!” she cried, clapping her hands with a bright smile of recognition, “another old friend found already! Your book-case, Marian—your dear-little-shabby-old-satin-wood book-case—how glad I am you brought it with you from Limmeridge! And the horrid heavy man’s umbrella, that you always would walk out with when it rained! And first and foremost of all, your own dear, dark, clever, gipsy-face, looking at me just as usual! It is so like home again to be here. How can we make it more like home still? I will put my father’s portrait in your room instead of in mine—and I will keep all my little treasures from Limmeridge here—and we will pass hours and hours every day with these four friendly walls round us. Oh, Marian!” she said, suddenly seating herself on a footstool at my knees, and looking up earnestly in my face, “promise you will never marry, and leave me. It is selfish to say so, but you are so much better off as a single woman—unless—unless you are very fond of your husband—but you won’t be very fond of anybody but me, will you?” She stopped again, crossed my hands on my lap, and laid her face on them. “Have you been writing many letters, and receiving many letters lately?” she asked, in low, suddenly-altered tones. I understood what the question meant, but I thought it my duty not to encourage her by meeting her half way. “Have you heard from him?” she went on, coaxing me to forgive the more direct appeal on which she now ventured, by kissing my hands, upon which her face still rested. “Is he well and happy, and getting on in his profession? Has he recovered himself—and forgotten me?”\n\nShe should not have asked those questions. She should have remembered her own resolution, on the morning when Sir Percival held her to her marriage engagement, and when she resigned the book of Hartright’s drawings into my hands for ever. But, ah me! where is the faultless human creature who can persevere in a good resolution, without sometimes failing and falling back? Where is the woman who has ever really torn from her heart the image that has been once fixed in it by a true love? Books tell us that such unearthly creatures have existed—but what does our own experience say in answer to books?\n\nI made no attempt to remonstrate with her: perhaps, because I sincerely appreciated the fearless candour which let me see, what other women in her position might have had reasons for concealing even from their dearest friends—perhaps, because I felt, in my own heart and conscience, that in her place I should have asked the same questions and had the same thoughts. All I could honestly do was to reply that I had not written to him or heard from him lately, and then to turn the conversation to less dangerous topics.\n\nThere has been much to sadden me in our interview—my first confidential interview with her since her return. The change which her marriage has produced in our relations towards each other, by placing a forbidden subject between us, for the first time in our lives; the melancholy conviction of the dearth of all warmth of feeling, of all close sympathy, between her husband and herself, which her own unwilling words now force on my mind; the distressing discovery that the influence of that ill-fated attachment still remains (no matter how innocently, how harmlessly) rooted as deeply as ever in her heart—all these are disclosures to sadden any woman who loves her as dearly, and feels for her as acutely, as I do.\n\nThere is only one consolation to set against them—a consolation that ought to comfort me, and that does comfort me. All the graces and gentleness of her character—all the frank affection of her nature—all the sweet, simple, womanly charms which used to make her the darling and delight of every one who approached her, have come back to me with herself. Of my other impressions I am sometimes a little inclined to doubt. Of this last, best, happiest of all impressions, I grow more and more certain every hour in the day.\n\nLet me turn, now, from her to her travelling companions. Her husband must engage my attention first. What have I observed in Sir Percival, since his return, to improve my opinion of him?\n\nI can hardly say. Small vexations and annoyances seem to have beset him since he came back, and no man, under those circumstances, is ever presented at his best. He looks, as I think, thinner than he was when he left England. His wearisome cough and his comfortless restlessness have certainly increased. His manner—at least his manner towards me—is much more abrupt than it used to be. He greeted me, on the evening of his return, with little or nothing of the ceremony and civility of former times—no polite speeches of welcome—no appearance of extraordinary gratification at seeing me—nothing but a short shake of the hand, and a sharp “How-d’ye-do, Miss Halcombe—glad to see you again.” He seemed to accept me as one of the necessary fixtures of Blackwater Park, to be satisfied at finding me established in my proper place, and then to pass me over altogether.\n\nMost men show something of their disposition in their own houses, which they have concealed elsewhere, and Sir Percival has already displayed a mania for order and regularity, which is quite a new revelation of him, so far as my previous knowledge of his character is concerned. If I take a book from the library and leave it on the table, he follows me and puts it back again. If I rise from a chair, and let it remain where I have been sitting, he carefully restores it to its proper place against the wall. He picks up stray flower-blossoms from the carpet, and mutters to himself as discontentedly as if they were hot cinders burning holes in it, and he storms at the servants if there is a crease in the tablecloth, or a knife missing from its place at the dinner-table, as fiercely as if they had personally insulted him.\n\nI have already referred to the small annoyances which appear to have troubled him since his return. Much of the alteration for the worse which I have noticed in him may be due to these. I try to persuade myself that it is so, because I am anxious not to be disheartened already about the future. It is certainly trying to any man’s temper to be met by a vexation the moment he sets foot in his own house again, after a long absence, and this annoying circumstance did really happen to Sir Percival in my presence.\n\nOn the evening of their arrival the housekeeper followed me into the hall to receive her master and mistress and their guests. The instant he saw her, Sir Percival asked if any one had called lately. The housekeeper mentioned to him, in reply, what she had previously mentioned to me, the visit of the strange gentleman to make inquiries about the time of her master’s return. He asked immediately for the gentleman’s name. No name had been left. The gentleman’s business? No business had been mentioned. What was the gentleman like? The housekeeper tried to describe him, but failed to distinguish the nameless visitor by any personal peculiarity which her master could recognise. Sir Percival frowned, stamped angrily on the floor, and walked on into the house, taking no notice of anybody. Why he should have been so discomposed by a trifle I cannot say—but he was seriously discomposed, beyond all doubt.\n\nUpon the whole, it will be best, perhaps, if I abstain from forming a decisive opinion of his manners, language, and conduct in his own house, until time has enabled him to shake off the anxieties, whatever they may be, which now evidently troubled his mind in secret. I will turn over to a new page, and my pen shall let Laura’s husband alone for the present.\n\nThe two guests—the Count and Countess Fosco—come next in my catalogue. I will dispose of the Countess first, so as to have done with the woman as soon as possible.\n\nLaura was certainly not chargeable with any exaggeration, in writing me word that I should hardly recognise her aunt again when we met. Never before have I beheld such a change produced in a woman by her marriage as has been produced in Madame Fosco.\n\nAs Eleanor Fairlie (aged seven-and-thirty), she was always talking pretentious nonsense, and always worrying the unfortunate men with every small exaction which a vain and foolish woman can impose on long-suffering male humanity. As Madame Fosco (aged three-and-forty), she sits for hours together without saying a word, frozen up in the strangest manner in herself. The hideously ridiculous love-locks which used to hang on either side of her face are now replaced by stiff little rows of very short curls, of the sort one sees in old-fashioned wigs. A plain, matronly cap covers her head, and makes her look, for the first time in her life since I remember her, like a decent woman. Nobody (putting her husband out of the question, of course) now sees in her, what everybody once saw—I mean the structure of the female skeleton, in the upper regions of the collar-bones and the shoulder-blades. Clad in quiet black or grey gowns, made high round the throat—dresses that she would have laughed at, or screamed at, as the whim of the moment inclined her, in her maiden days—she sits speechless in corners; her dry white hands (so dry that the pores of her skin look chalky) incessantly engaged, either in monotonous embroidery work or in rolling up endless cigarettes for the Count’s own particular smoking. On the few occasions when her cold blue eyes are off her work, they are generally turned on her husband, with the look of mute submissive inquiry which we are all familiar with in the eyes of a faithful dog. The only approach to an inward thaw which I have yet detected under her outer covering of icy constraint, has betrayed itself, once or twice, in the form of a suppressed tigerish jealousy of any woman in the house (the maids included) to whom the Count speaks, or on whom he looks with anything approaching to special interest or attention. Except in this one particular, she is always, morning, noon, and night, indoors and out, fair weather or foul, as cold as a statue, and as impenetrable as the stone out of which it is cut. For the common purposes of society the extraordinary change thus produced in her is, beyond all doubt, a change for the better, seeing that it has transformed her into a civil, silent, unobtrusive woman, who is never in the way. How far she is really reformed or deteriorated in her secret self, is another question. I have once or twice seen sudden changes of expression on her pinched lips, and heard sudden inflexions of tone in her calm voice, which have led me to suspect that her present state of suppression may have sealed up something dangerous in her nature, which used to evaporate harmlessly in the freedom of her former life. It is quite possible that I may be altogether wrong in this idea. My own impression, however, is, that I am right. Time will show.\n\nAnd the magician who has wrought this wonderful transformation—the foreign husband who has tamed this once wayward English woman till her own relations hardly know her again—the Count himself? What of the Count?\n\nThis in two words: He looks like a man who could tame anything. If he had married a tigress, instead of a woman, he would have tamed the tigress. If he had married me, I should have made his cigarettes, as his wife does—I should have held my tongue when he looked at me, as she holds hers.\n\nI am almost afraid to confess it, even to these secret pages. The man has interested me, has attracted me, has forced me to like him. In two short days he has made his way straight into my favourable estimation, and how he has worked the miracle is more than I can tell.\n\nIt absolutely startles me, now he is in my mind, to find how plainly I see him!—how much more plainly than I see Sir Percival, or Mr. Fairlie, or Walter Hartright, or any other absent person of whom I think, with the one exception of Laura herself! I can hear his voice, as if he was speaking at this moment. I know what his conversation was yesterday, as well as if I was hearing it now. How am I to describe him? There are peculiarities in his personal appearance, his habits, and his amusements, which I should blame in the boldest terms, or ridicule in the most merciless manner, if I had seen them in another man. What is it that makes me unable to blame them, or to ridicule them in HIM?\n\nFor example, he is immensely fat. Before this time I have always especially disliked corpulent humanity. I have always maintained that the popular notion of connecting excessive grossness of size and excessive good-humour as inseparable allies was equivalent to declaring, either that no people but amiable people ever get fat, or that the accidental addition of so many pounds of flesh has a directly favourable influence over the disposition of the person on whose body they accumulate. I have invariably combated both these absurd assertions by quoting examples of fat people who were as mean, vicious, and cruel as the leanest and the worst of their neighbours. I have asked whether Henry the Eighth was an amiable character? Whether Pope Alexander the Sixth was a good man? Whether Mr. Murderer and Mrs. Murderess Manning were not both unusually stout people? Whether hired nurses, proverbially as cruel a set of women as are to be found in all England, were not, for the most part, also as fat a set of women as are to be found in all England?—and so on, through dozens of other examples, modern and ancient, native and foreign, high and low. Holding these strong opinions on the subject with might and main as I do at this moment, here, nevertheless, is Count Fosco, as fat as Henry the Eighth himself, established in my favour, at one day’s notice, without let or hindrance from his own odious corpulence. Marvellous indeed!\n\nIs it his face that has recommended him?\n\nIt may be his face. He is a most remarkable likeness, on a large scale, of the great Napoleon. His features have Napoleon’s magnificent regularity—his expression recalls the grandly calm, immovable power of the Great Soldier’s face. This striking resemblance certainly impressed me, to begin with; but there is something in him besides the resemblance, which has impressed me more. I think the influence I am now trying to find is in his eyes. They are the most unfathomable grey eyes I ever saw, and they have at times a cold, clear, beautiful, irresistible glitter in them which forces me to look at him, and yet causes me sensations, when I do look, which I would rather not feel. Other parts of his face and head have their strange peculiarities. His complexion, for instance, has a singular sallow-fairness, so much at variance with the dark-brown colour of his hair, that I suspect the hair of being a wig, and his face, closely shaven all over, is smoother and freer from all marks and wrinkles than mine, though (according to Sir Percival’s account of him) he is close on sixty years of age. But these are not the prominent personal characteristics which distinguish him, to my mind, from all the other men I have ever seen. The marked peculiarity which singles him out from the rank and file of humanity lies entirely, so far as I can tell at present, in the extraordinary expression and extraordinary power of his eyes.\n\nHis manner and his command of our language may also have assisted him, in some degree, to establish himself in my good opinion. He has that quiet deference, that look of pleased, attentive interest in listening to a woman, and that secret gentleness in his voice in speaking to a woman, which, say what we may, we can none of us resist. Here, too, his unusual command of the English language necessarily helps him. I had often heard of the extraordinary aptitude which many Italians show in mastering our strong, hard, Northern speech; but, until I saw Count Fosco, I had never supposed it possible that any foreigner could have spoken English as he speaks it. There are times when it is almost impossible to detect by his accent that he is not a countryman of our own, and as for fluency, there are very few born Englishmen who can talk with as few stoppages and repetitions as the Count. He may construct his sentences more or less in the foreign way, but I have never yet heard him use a wrong expression, or hesitate for a moment in his choice of a word.\n\nAll the smallest characteristics of this strange man have something strikingly original and perplexingly contradictory in them. Fat as he is and old as he is, his movements are astonishingly light and easy. He is as noiseless in a room as any of us women, and more than that, with all his look of unmistakable mental firmness and power, he is as nervously sensitive as the weakest of us. He starts at chance noises as inveterately as Laura herself. He winced and shuddered yesterday, when Sir Percival beat one of the spaniels, so that I felt ashamed of my own want of tenderness and sensibility by comparison with the Count.\n\nThe relation of this last incident reminds me of one of his most curious peculiarities, which I have not yet mentioned—his extraordinary fondness for pet animals.\n\nSome of these he has left on the Continent, but he has brought with him to this house a cockatoo, two canary-birds, and a whole family of white mice. He attends to all the necessities of these strange favourites himself, and he has taught the creatures to be surprisingly fond of him and familiar with him. The cockatoo, a most vicious and treacherous bird towards every one else, absolutely seems to love him. When he lets it out of its cage, it hops on to his knee, and claws its way up his great big body, and rubs its top-knot against his sallow double chin in the most caressing manner imaginable. He has only to set the doors of the canaries’ cages open, and to call them, and the pretty little cleverly trained creatures perch fearlessly on his hand, mount his fat outstretched fingers one by one, when he tells them to “go upstairs,” and sing together as if they would burst their throats with delight when they get to the top finger. His white mice live in a little pagoda of gaily-painted wirework, designed and made by himself. They are almost as tame as the canaries, and they are perpetually let out like the canaries. They crawl all over him, popping in and out of his waistcoat, and sitting in couples, white as snow, on his capacious shoulders. He seems to be even fonder of his mice than of his other pets, smiles at them, and kisses them, and calls them by all sorts of endearing names. If it be possible to suppose an Englishman with any taste for such childish interests and amusements as these, that Englishman would certainly feel rather ashamed of them, and would be anxious to apologise for them, in the company of grown-up people. But the Count, apparently, sees nothing ridiculous in the amazing contrast between his colossal self and his frail little pets. He would blandly kiss his white mice and twitter to his canary-birds amid an assembly of English fox-hunters, and would only pity them as barbarians when they were all laughing their loudest at him.\n\nIt seems hardly credible while I am writing it down, but it is certainly true, that this same man, who has all the fondness of an old maid for his cockatoo, and all the small dexterities of an organ-boy in managing his white mice, can talk, when anything happens to rouse him, with a daring independence of thought, a knowledge of books in every language, and an experience of society in half the capitals of Europe, which would make him the prominent personage of any assembly in the civilised world. This trainer of canary-birds, this architect of a pagoda for white mice, is (as Sir Percival himself has told me) one of the first experimental chemists living, and has discovered, among other wonderful inventions, a means of petrifying the body after death, so as to preserve it, as hard as marble, to the end of time. This fat, indolent, elderly man, whose nerves are so finely strung that he starts at chance noises, and winces when he sees a house-spaniel get a whipping, went into the stable-yard on the morning after his arrival, and put his hand on the head of a chained bloodhound—a beast so savage that the very groom who feeds him keeps out of his reach. His wife and I were present, and I shall not forget the scene that followed, short as it was.\n\n“Mind that dog, sir,” said the groom; “he flies at everybody!” “He does that, my friend,” replied the Count quietly, “because everybody is afraid of him. Let us see if he flies at me.” And he laid his plump, yellow-white fingers, on which the canary-birds had been perching ten minutes before, upon the formidable brute’s head, and looked him straight in the eyes. “You big dogs are all cowards,” he said, addressing the animal contemptuously, with his face and the dog’s within an inch of each other. “You would kill a poor cat, you infernal coward. You would fly at a starving beggar, you infernal coward. Anything that you can surprise unawares—anything that is afraid of your big body, and your wicked white teeth, and your slobbering, bloodthirsty mouth, is the thing you like to fly at. You could throttle me at this moment, you mean, miserable bully, and you daren’t so much as look me in the face, because I’m not afraid of you. Will you think better of it, and try your teeth in my fat neck? Bah! not you!” He turned away, laughing at the astonishment of the men in the yard, and the dog crept back meekly to his kennel. “Ah! my nice waistcoat!” he said pathetically. “I am sorry I came here. Some of that brute’s slobber has got on my pretty clean waistcoat.” Those words express another of his incomprehensible oddities. He is as fond of fine clothes as the veriest fool in existence, and has appeared in four magnificent waistcoats already—all of light garish colours, and all immensely large even for him—in the two days of his residence at Blackwater Park.\n\nHis tact and cleverness in small things are quite as noticeable as the singular inconsistencies in his character, and the childish triviality of his ordinary tastes and pursuits.\n\nI can see already that he means to live on excellent terms with all of us during the period of his sojourn in this place. He has evidently discovered that Laura secretly dislikes him (she confessed as much to me when I pressed her on the subject)—but he has also found out that she is extravagantly fond of flowers. Whenever she wants a nosegay he has got one to give her, gathered and arranged by himself, and greatly to my amusement, he is always cunningly provided with a duplicate, composed of exactly the same flowers, grouped in exactly the same way, to appease his icily jealous wife before she can so much as think herself aggrieved. His management of the Countess (in public) is a sight to see. He bows to her, he habitually addresses her as “my angel,” he carries his canaries to pay her little visits on his fingers and to sing to her, he kisses her hand when she gives him his cigarettes; he presents her with sugar-plums in return, which he puts into her mouth playfully, from a box in his pocket. The rod of iron with which he rules her never appears in company—it is a private rod, and is always kept upstairs.\n\nHis method of recommending himself to me is entirely different. He flatters my vanity by talking to me as seriously and sensibly as if I was a man. Yes! I can find him out when I am away from him—I know he flatters my vanity, when I think of him up here in my own room—and yet, when I go downstairs, and get into his company again, he will blind me again, and I shall be flattered again, just as if I had never found him out at all! He can manage me as he manages his wife and Laura, as he managed the bloodhound in the stable-yard, as he manages Sir Percival himself, every hour in the day. “My good Percival! how I like your rough English humour!”—“My good Percival! how I enjoy your solid English sense!” He puts the rudest remarks Sir Percival can make on his effeminate tastes and amusements quietly away from him in that manner—always calling the baronet by his Christian name, smiling at him with the calmest superiority, patting him on the shoulder, and bearing with him benignantly, as a good-humoured father bears with a wayward son.\n\nThe interest which I really cannot help feeling in this strangely original man has led me to question Sir Percival about his past life.\n\nSir Percival either knows little, or will tell me little, about it. He and the Count first met many years ago, at Rome, under the dangerous circumstances to which I have alluded elsewhere. Since that time they have been perpetually together in London, in Paris, and in Vienna—but never in Italy again; the Count having, oddly enough, not crossed the frontiers of his native country for years past. Perhaps he has been made the victim of some political persecution? At all events, he seems to be patriotically anxious not to lose sight of any of his own countrymen who may happen to be in England. On the evening of his arrival he asked how far we were from the nearest town, and whether we knew of any Italian gentlemen who might happen to be settled there. He is certainly in correspondence with people on the Continent, for his letters have all sorts of odd stamps on them, and I saw one for him this morning, waiting in his place at the breakfast-table, with a huge, official-looking seal on it. Perhaps he is in correspondence with his government? And yet, that is hardly to be reconciled either with my other idea that he may be a political exile.\n\nHow much I seem to have written about Count Fosco! And what does it all amount to?—as poor, dear Mr. Gilmore would ask, in his impenetrable business-like way I can only repeat that I do assuredly feel, even on this short acquaintance, a strange, half-willing, half-unwilling liking for the Count. He seems to have established over me the same sort of ascendency which he has evidently gained over Sir Percival. Free, and even rude, as he may occasionally be in his manner towards his fat friend, Sir Percival is nevertheless afraid, as I can plainly see, of giving any serious offence to the Count. I wonder whether I am afraid too? I certainly never saw a man, in all my experience, whom I should be so sorry to have for an enemy. Is this because I like him, or because I am afraid of him? Chi sa?—as Count Fosco might say in his own language. Who knows?\n\nJune 16th.—Something to chronicle to-day besides my own ideas and impressions. A visitor has arrived—quite unknown to Laura and to me, and apparently quite unexpected by Sir Percival.\n\nWe were all at lunch, in the room with the new French windows that open into the verandah, and the Count (who devours pastry as I have never yet seen it devoured by any human beings but girls at boarding-schools) had just amused us by asking gravely for his fourth tart—when the servant entered to announce the visitor.\n\n“Mr. Merriman has just come, Sir Percival, and wishes to see you immediately.”\n\nSir Percival started, and looked at the man with an expression of angry alarm.\n\n“Mr. Merriman!” he repeated, as if he thought his own ears must have deceived him.\n\n“Yes, Sir Percival—Mr. Merriman, from London.”\n\n“Where is he?”\n\n“In the library, Sir Percival.”\n\nHe left the table the instant the last answer was given, and hurried out of the room without saying a word to any of us.\n\n“Who is Mr. Merriman?” asked Laura, appealing to me.\n\n“I have not the least idea,” was all I could say in reply.\n\nThe Count had finished his fourth tart, and had gone to a side-table to look after his vicious cockatoo. He turned round to us with the bird perched on his shoulder.\n\n“Mr. Merriman is Sir Percival’s solicitor,” he said quietly.\n\nSir Percival’s solicitor. It was a perfectly straightforward answer to Laura’s question, and yet, under the circumstances, it was not satisfactory. If Mr. Merriman had been specially sent for by his client, there would have been nothing very wonderful in his leaving town to obey the summons. But when a lawyer travels from London to Hampshire without being sent for, and when his arrival at a gentleman’s house seriously startles the gentleman himself, it may be safely taken for granted that the legal visitor is the bearer of some very important and very unexpected news—news which may be either very good or very bad, but which cannot, in either case, be of the common everyday kind.\n\nLaura and I sat silent at the table for a quarter of an hour or more, wondering uneasily what had happened, and waiting for the chance of Sir Percival’s speedy return. There were no signs of his return, and we rose to leave the room.\n\nThe Count, attentive as usual, advanced from the corner in which he had been feeding his cockatoo, with the bird still perched on his shoulder, and opened the door for us. Laura and Madame Fosco went out first. Just as I was on the point of following them he made a sign with his hand, and spoke to me, before I passed him, in the oddest manner.\n\n“Yes,” he said, quietly answering the unexpressed idea at that moment in my mind, as if I had plainly confided it to him in so many words—“yes, Miss Halcombe, something HAS happened.”\n\nI was on the point of answering, “I never said so,” but the vicious cockatoo ruffled his clipped wings and gave a screech that set all my nerves on edge in an instant, and made me only too glad to get out of the room.\n\nI joined Laura at the foot of the stairs. The thought in her mind was the same as the thought in mine, which Count Fosco had surprised, and when she spoke her words were almost the echo of his. She, too, said to me secretly that she was afraid something had happened.\n\n# III\n\nJune 16th.—I have a few lines more to add to this day’s entry before I go to bed to-night.\n\nAbout two hours after Sir Percival rose from the luncheon-table to receive his solicitor, Mr. Merriman, in the library, I left my room alone to take a walk in the plantations. Just as I was at the end of the landing the library door opened and the two gentlemen came out. Thinking it best not to disturb them by appearing on the stairs, I resolved to defer going down till they had crossed the hall. Although they spoke to each other in guarded tones, their words were pronounced with sufficient distinctness of utterance to reach my ears.\n\n“Make your mind easy, Sir Percival,” I heard the lawyer say; “it all rests with Lady Glyde.”\n\nI had turned to go back to my own room for a minute or two, but the sound of Laura’s name on the lips of a stranger stopped me instantly. I daresay it was very wrong and very discreditable to listen, but where is the woman, in the whole range of our sex, who can regulate her actions by the abstract principles of honour, when those principles point one way, and when her affections, and the interests which grow out of them, point the other?\n\nI listened—and under similar circumstances I would listen again—yes! with my ear at the keyhole, if I could not possibly manage it in any other way.\n\n“You quite understand, Sir Percival,” the lawyer went on. “Lady Glyde is to sign her name in the presence of a witness—or of two witnesses, if you wish to be particularly careful—and is then to put her finger on the seal and say, ‘I deliver this as my act and deed.’ If that is done in a week’s time the arrangement will be perfectly successful, and the anxiety will be all over. If not——”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘if not’?” asked Sir Percival angrily. “If the thing must be done it SHALL be done. I promise you that, Merriman.”\n\n“Just so, Sir Percival—just so; but there are two alternatives in all transactions, and we lawyers like to look both of them in the face boldly. If through any extraordinary circumstance the arrangement should not be made, I think I may be able to get the parties to accept bills at three months. But how the money is to be raised when the bills fall due——”\n\n“Damn the bills! The money is only to be got in one way, and in that way, I tell you again, it SHALL be got. Take a glass of wine, Merriman, before you go.”\n\n“Much obliged, Sir Percival; I have not a moment to lose if I am to catch the up-train. You will let me know as soon as the arrangement is complete? and you will not forget the caution I recommended——”\n\n“Of course I won’t. There’s the dog-cart at the door for you. My groom will get you to the station in no time. Benjamin, drive like mad! Jump in. If Mr. Merriman misses the train you lose your place. Hold fast, Merriman, and if you are upset trust to the devil to save his own.” With that parting benediction the baronet turned about and walked back to the library.\n\nI had not heard much, but the little that had reached my ears was enough to make me feel uneasy. The “something” that “had happened” was but too plainly a serious money embarrassment, and Sir Percival’s relief from it depended upon Laura. The prospect of seeing her involved in her husband’s secret difficulties filled me with dismay, exaggerated, no doubt, by my ignorance of business and my settled distrust of Sir Percival. Instead of going out, as I proposed, I went back immediately to Laura’s room to tell her what I had heard.\n\nShe received my bad news so composedly as to surprise me. She evidently knows more of her husband’s character and her husband’s embarrassments than I have suspected up to this time.\n\n“I feared as much,” she said, “when I heard of that strange gentleman who called, and declined to leave his name.”\n\n“Who do you think the gentleman was, then?” I asked.\n\n“Some person who has heavy claims on Sir Percival,” she answered, “and who has been the cause of Mr. Merriman’s visit here to-day.”\n\n“Do you know anything about those claims?”\n\n“No, I know no particulars.”\n\n“You will sign nothing, Laura, without first looking at it?”\n\n“Certainly not, Marian. Whatever I can harmlessly and honestly do to help him I will do—for the sake of making your life and mine, love, as easy and as happy as possible. But I will do nothing ignorantly, which we might, one day, have reason to feel ashamed of. Let us say no more about it now. You have got your hat on—suppose we go and dream away the afternoon in the grounds?”\n\nOn leaving the house we directed our steps to the nearest shade.\n\nAs we passed an open space among the trees in front of the house, there was Count Fosco, slowly walking backwards and forwards on the grass, sunning himself in the full blaze of the hot June afternoon. He had a broad straw hat on, with a violet-coloured ribbon round it. A blue blouse, with profuse white fancy-work over the bosom, covered his prodigious body, and was girt about the place where his waist might once have been with a broad scarlet leather belt. Nankeen trousers, displaying more white fancy-work over the ankles, and purple morocco slippers, adorned his lower extremities. He was singing Figaro’s famous song in the Barber of Seville, with that crisply fluent vocalisation which is never heard from any other than an Italian throat, accompanying himself on the concertina, which he played with ecstatic throwings-up of his arms, and graceful twistings and turnings of his head, like a fat St. Cecilia masquerading in male attire. “Figaro qua! Figaro la! Figaro su! Figaro giu!” sang the Count, jauntily tossing up the concertina at arm’s length, and bowing to us, on one side of the instrument, with the airy grace and elegance of Figaro himself at twenty years of age.\n\n“Take my word for it, Laura, that man knows something of Sir Percival’s embarrassments,” I said, as we returned the Count’s salutation from a safe distance.\n\n“What makes you think that?” she asked.\n\n“How should he have known, otherwise, that Mr. Merriman was Sir Percival’s solicitor?” I rejoined. “Besides, when I followed you out of the luncheon-room, he told me, without a single word of inquiry on my part, that something had happened. Depend upon it, he knows more than we do.”\n\n“Don’t ask him any questions if he does. Don’t take him into our confidence!”\n\n“You seem to dislike him, Laura, in a very determined manner. What has he said or done to justify you?”\n\n“Nothing, Marian. On the contrary, he was all kindness and attention on our journey home, and he several times checked Sir Percival’s outbreaks of temper, in the most considerate manner towards me. Perhaps I dislike him because he has so much more power over my husband than I have. Perhaps it hurts my pride to be under any obligations to his interference. All I know is, that I DO dislike him.”\n\nThe rest of the day and evening passed quietly enough. The Count and I played at chess. For the first two games he politely allowed me to conquer him, and then, when he saw that I had found him out, begged my pardon, and at the third game checkmated me in ten minutes. Sir Percival never once referred, all through the evening, to the lawyer’s visit. But either that event, or something else, had produced a singular alteration for the better in him. He was as polite and agreeable to all of us, as he used to be in the days of his probation at Limmeridge, and he was so amazingly attentive and kind to his wife, that even icy Madame Fosco was roused into looking at him with a grave surprise. What does this mean? I think I can guess—I am afraid Laura can guess—and I am sure Count Fosco knows. I caught Sir Percival looking at him for approval more than once in the course of the evening.\n\nJune 17th.—A day of events. I most fervently hope I may not have to add, a day of disasters as well.\n\nSir Percival was as silent at breakfast as he had been the evening before, on the subject of the mysterious “arrangement” (as the lawyer called it) which is hanging over our heads. An hour afterwards, however, he suddenly entered the morning-room, where his wife and I were waiting, with our hats on, for Madame Fosco to join us, and inquired for the Count.\n\n“We expect to see him here directly,” I said.\n\n“The fact is,” Sir Percival went on, walking nervously about the room, “I want Fosco and his wife in the library, for a mere business formality, and I want you there, Laura, for a minute too.” He stopped, and appeared to notice, for the first time, that we were in our walking costume. “Have you just come in?” he asked, “or were you just going out?”\n\n“We were all thinking of going to the lake this morning,” said Laura. “But if you have any other arrangement to propose——”\n\n“No, no,” he answered hastily. “My arrangement can wait. After lunch will do as well for it as after breakfast. All going to the lake, eh? A good idea. Let’s have an idle morning—I’ll be one of the party.”\n\nThere was no mistaking his manner, even if it had been possible to mistake the uncharacteristic readiness which his words expressed, to submit his own plans and projects to the convenience of others. He was evidently relieved at finding any excuse for delaying the business formality in the library, to which his own words had referred. My heart sank within me as I drew the inevitable inference.\n\nThe Count and his wife joined us at that moment. The lady had her husband’s embroidered tobacco-pouch, and her store of paper in her hand, for the manufacture of the eternal cigarettes. The gentleman, dressed, as usual, in his blouse and straw hat, carried the gay little pagoda-cage, with his darling white mice in it, and smiled on them, and on us, with a bland amiability which it was impossible to resist.\n\n“With your kind permission,” said the Count, “I will take my small family here—my poor-little-harmless-pretty-Mouseys, out for an airing along with us. There are dogs about the house, and shall I leave my forlorn white children at the mercies of the dogs? Ah, never!”\n\nHe chirruped paternally at his small white children through the bars of the pagoda, and we all left the house for the lake.\n\nIn the plantation Sir Percival strayed away from us. It seems to be part of his restless disposition always to separate himself from his companions on these occasions, and always to occupy himself when he is alone in cutting new walking-sticks for his own use. The mere act of cutting and lopping at hazard appears to please him. He has filled the house with walking-sticks of his own making, not one of which he ever takes up for a second time. When they have been once used his interest in them is all exhausted, and he thinks of nothing but going on and making more.\n\nAt the old boat-house he joined us again. I will put down the conversation that ensued when we were all settled in our places exactly as it passed. It is an important conversation, so far as I am concerned, for it has seriously disposed me to distrust the influence which Count Fosco has exercised over my thoughts and feelings, and to resist it for the future as resolutely as I can.\n\nThe boat-house was large enough to hold us all, but Sir Percival remained outside trimming the last new stick with his pocket-axe. We three women found plenty of room on the large seat. Laura took her work, and Madame Fosco began her cigarettes. I, as usual, had nothing to do. My hands always were, and always will be, as awkward as a man’s. The Count good-humouredly took a stool many sizes too small for him, and balanced himself on it with his back against the side of the shed, which creaked and groaned under his weight. He put the pagoda-cage on his lap, and let out the mice to crawl over him as usual. They are pretty, innocent-looking little creatures, but the sight of them creeping about a man’s body is for some reason not pleasant to me. It excites a strange responsive creeping in my own nerves, and suggests hideous ideas of men dying in prison with the crawling creatures of the dungeon preying on them undisturbed.\n\nThe morning was windy and cloudy, and the rapid alternations of shadow and sunlight over the waste of the lake made the view look doubly wild, weird, and gloomy.\n\n“Some people call that picturesque,” said Sir Percival, pointing over the wide prospect with his half-finished walking-stick. “I call it a blot on a gentleman’s property. In my great-grandfather’s time the lake flowed to this place. Look at it now! It is not four feet deep anywhere, and it is all puddles and pools. I wish I could afford to drain it, and plant it all over. My bailiff (a superstitious idiot) says he is quite sure the lake has a curse on it, like the Dead Sea. What do you think, Fosco? It looks just the place for a murder, doesn’t it?”\n\n“My good Percival,” remonstrated the Count. “What is your solid English sense thinking of? The water is too shallow to hide the body, and there is sand everywhere to print off the murderer’s footsteps. It is, upon the whole, the very worst place for a murder that I ever set my eyes on.”\n\n“Humbug!” said Sir Percival, cutting away fiercely at his stick. “You know what I mean. The dreary scenery, the lonely situation. If you choose to understand me, you can—if you don’t choose, I am not going to trouble myself to explain my meaning.”\n\n“And why not,” asked the Count, “when your meaning can be explained by anybody in two words? If a fool was going to commit a murder, your lake is the first place he would choose for it. If a wise man was going to commit a murder, your lake is the last place he would choose for it. Is that your meaning? If it is, there is your explanation for you ready made. Take it, Percival, with your good Fosco’s blessing.”\n\nLaura looked at the Count with her dislike for him appearing a little too plainly in her face. He was so busy with his mice that he did not notice her.\n\n“I am sorry to hear the lake-view connected with anything so horrible as the idea of murder,” she said. “And if Count Fosco must divide murderers into classes, I think he has been very unfortunate in his choice of expressions. To describe them as fools only seems like treating them with an indulgence to which they have no claim. And to describe them as wise men sounds to me like a downright contradiction in terms. I have always heard that truly wise men are truly good men, and have a horror of crime.”\n\n“My dear lady,” said the Count, “those are admirable sentiments, and I have seen them stated at the tops of copy-books.” He lifted one of the white mice in the palm of his hand, and spoke to it in his whimsical way. “My pretty little smooth white rascal,” he said, “here is a moral lesson for you. A truly wise mouse is a truly good mouse. Mention that, if you please, to your companions, and never gnaw at the bars of your cage again as long as you live.”\n\n“It is easy to turn everything into ridicule,” said Laura resolutely; “but you will not find it quite so easy, Count Fosco, to give me an instance of a wise man who has been a great criminal.”\n\nThe Count shrugged his huge shoulders, and smiled on Laura in the friendliest manner.\n\n“Most true!” he said. “The fool’s crime is the crime that is found out, and the wise man’s crime is the crime that is NOT found out. If I could give you an instance, it would not be the instance of a wise man. Dear Lady Glyde, your sound English common sense has been too much for me. It is checkmate for me this time, Miss Halcombe—ha?”\n\n“Stand to your guns, Laura,” sneered Sir Percival, who had been listening in his place at the door. “Tell him next, that crimes cause their own detection. There’s another bit of copy-book morality for you, Fosco. Crimes cause their own detection. What infernal humbug!”\n\n“I believe it to be true,” said Laura quietly.\n\nSir Percival burst out laughing, so violently, so outrageously, that he quite startled us all—the Count more than any of us.\n\n“I believe it too,” I said, coming to Laura’s rescue.\n\nSir Percival, who had been unaccountably amused at his wife’s remark, was just as unaccountably irritated by mine. He struck the new stick savagely on the sand, and walked away from us.\n\n“Poor dear Percival!” cried Count Fosco, looking after him gaily, “he is the victim of English spleen. But, my dear Miss Halcombe, my dear Lady Glyde, do you really believe that crimes cause their own detection? And you, my angel,” he continued, turning to his wife, who had not uttered a word yet, “do you think so too?”\n\n“I wait to be instructed,” replied the Countess, in tones of freezing reproof, intended for Laura and me, “before I venture on giving my opinion in the presence of well-informed men.”\n\n“Do you, indeed?” I said. “I remember the time, Countess, when you advocated the Rights of Women, and freedom of female opinion was one of them.”\n\n“What is your view of the subject, Count?” asked Madame Fosco, calmly proceeding with her cigarettes, and not taking the least notice of me.\n\nThe Count stroked one of his white mice reflectively with his chubby little finger before he answered.\n\n“It is truly wonderful,” he said, “how easily Society can console itself for the worst of its shortcomings with a little bit of clap-trap. The machinery it has set up for the detection of crime is miserably ineffective—and yet only invent a moral epigram, saying that it works well, and you blind everybody to its blunders from that moment. Crimes cause their own detection, do they? And murder will out (another moral epigram), will it? Ask Coroners who sit at inquests in large towns if that is true, Lady Glyde. Ask secretaries of life-assurance companies if that is true, Miss Halcombe. Read your own public journals. In the few cases that get into the newspapers, are there not instances of slain bodies found, and no murderers ever discovered? Multiply the cases that are reported by the cases that are NOT reported, and the bodies that are found by the bodies that are NOT found, and what conclusion do you come to? This. That there are foolish criminals who are discovered, and wise criminals who escape. The hiding of a crime, or the detection of a crime, what is it? A trial of skill between the police on one side, and the individual on the other. When the criminal is a brutal, ignorant fool, the police in nine cases out of ten win. When the criminal is a resolute, educated, highly-intelligent man, the police in nine cases out of ten lose. If the police win, you generally hear all about it. If the police lose, you generally hear nothing. And on this tottering foundation you build up your comfortable moral maxim that Crime causes its own detection! Yes—all the crime you know of. And what of the rest?”\n\n“Devilish true, and very well put,” cried a voice at the entrance of the boat-house. Sir Percival had recovered his equanimity, and had come back while we were listening to the Count.\n\n“Some of it may be true,” I said, “and all of it may be very well put. But I don’t see why Count Fosco should celebrate the victory of the criminal over Society with so much exultation, or why you, Sir Percival, should applaud him so loudly for doing it.”\n\n“Do you hear that, Fosco?” asked Sir Percival. “Take my advice, and make your peace with your audience. Tell them virtue’s a fine thing—they like that, I can promise you.”\n\nThe Count laughed inwardly and silently, and two of the white mice in his waistcoat, alarmed by the internal convulsion going on beneath them, darted out in a violent hurry, and scrambled into their cage again.\n\n“The ladies, my good Percival, shall tell me about virtue,” he said. “They are better authorities than I am, for they know what virtue is, and I don’t.”\n\n“You hear him?” said Sir Percival. “Isn’t it awful?”\n\n“It is true,” said the Count quietly. “I am a citizen of the world, and I have met, in my time, with so many different sorts of virtue, that I am puzzled, in my old age, to say which is the right sort and which is the wrong. Here, in England, there is one virtue. And there, in China, there is another virtue. And John Englishman says my virtue is the genuine virtue. And John Chinaman says my virtue is the genuine virtue. And I say Yes to one, or No to the other, and am just as much bewildered about it in the case of John with the top-boots as I am in the case of John with the pigtail. Ah, nice little Mousey! come, kiss me. What is your own private notion of a virtuous man, my pret-pret-pretty? A man who keeps you warm, and gives you plenty to eat. And a good notion, too, for it is intelligible, at the least.”\n\n“Stay a minute, Count,” I interposed. “Accepting your illustration, surely we have one unquestionable virtue in England which is wanting in China. The Chinese authorities kill thousands of innocent people on the most frivolous pretexts. We in England are free from all guilt of that kind—we commit no such dreadful crime—we abhor reckless bloodshed with all our hearts.”\n\n“Quite right, Marian,” said Laura. “Well thought of, and well expressed.”\n\n“Pray allow the Count to proceed,” said Madame Fosco, with stern civility. “You will find, young ladies, that HE never speaks without having excellent reasons for all that he says.”\n\n“Thank you, my angel,” replied the Count. “Have a bon-bon?” He took out of his pocket a pretty little inlaid box, and placed it open on the table. “Chocolat a la Vanille,” cried the impenetrable man, cheerfully rattling the sweetmeats in the box, and bowing all round. “Offered by Fosco as an act of homage to the charming society.”\n\n“Be good enough to go on, Count,” said his wife, with a spiteful reference to myself. “Oblige me by answering Miss Halcombe.”\n\n“Miss Halcombe is unanswerable,” replied the polite Italian; “that is to say, so far as she goes. Yes! I agree with her. John Bull does abhor the crimes of John Chinaman. He is the quickest old gentleman at finding out faults that are his neighbours’, and the slowest old gentleman at finding out the faults that are his own, who exists on the face of creation. Is he so very much better in this way than the people whom he condemns in their way? English Society, Miss Halcombe, is as often the accomplice as it is the enemy of crime. Yes! yes! Crime is in this country what crime is in other countries—a good friend to a man and to those about him as often as it is an enemy. A great rascal provides for his wife and family. The worse he is the more he makes them the objects for your sympathy. He often provides also for himself. A profligate spendthrift who is always borrowing money will get more from his friends than the rigidly honest man who only borrows of them once, under pressure of the direst want. In the one case the friends will not be at all surprised, and they will give. In the other case they will be very much surprised, and they will hesitate. Is the prison that Mr. Scoundrel lives in at the end of his career a more uncomfortable place than the workhouse that Mr. Honesty lives in at the end of his career? When John-Howard-Philanthropist wants to relieve misery he goes to find it in prisons, where crime is wretched—not in huts and hovels, where virtue is wretched too. Who is the English poet who has won the most universal sympathy—who makes the easiest of all subjects for pathetic writing and pathetic painting? That nice young person who began life with a forgery, and ended it by a suicide—your dear, romantic, interesting Chatterton. Which gets on best, do you think, of two poor starving dressmakers—the woman who resists temptation and is honest, or the woman who falls under temptation and steals? You all know that the stealing is the making of that second woman’s fortune—it advertises her from length to breadth of good-humoured, charitable England—and she is relieved, as the breaker of a commandment, when she would have been left to starve, as the keeper of it. Come here, my jolly little Mouse! Hey! presto! pass! I transform you, for the time being, into a respectable lady. Stop there, in the palm of my great big hand, my dear, and listen. You marry the poor man whom you love, Mouse, and one half your friends pity, and the other half blame you. And now, on the contrary, you sell yourself for gold to a man you don’t care for, and all your friends rejoice over you, and a minister of public worship sanctions the base horror of the vilest of all human bargains, and smiles and smirks afterwards at your table, if you are polite enough to ask him to breakfast. Hey! presto! pass! Be a mouse again, and squeak. If you continue to be a lady much longer, I shall have you telling me that Society abhors crime—and then, Mouse, I shall doubt if your own eyes and ears are really of any use to you. Ah! I am a bad man, Lady Glyde, am I not? I say what other people only think, and when all the rest of the world is in a conspiracy to accept the mask for the true face, mine is the rash hand that tears off the plump pasteboard, and shows the bare bones beneath. I will get up on my big elephant’s legs, before I do myself any more harm in your amiable estimations—I will get up and take a little airy walk of my own. Dear ladies, as your excellent Sheridan said, I go—and leave my character behind me.”\n\nHe got up, put the cage on the table, and paused for a moment to count the mice in it. “One, two, three, four——Ha!” he cried, with a look of horror, “where, in the name of Heaven, is the fifth—the youngest, the whitest, the most amiable of all—my Benjamin of mice!”\n\nNeither Laura nor I were in any favorable disposition to be amused. The Count’s glib cynicism had revealed a new aspect of his nature from which we both recoiled. But it was impossible to resist the comical distress of so very large a man at the loss of so very small a mouse. We laughed in spite of ourselves; and when Madame Fosco rose to set the example of leaving the boat-house empty, so that her husband might search it to its remotest corners, we rose also to follow her out.\n\nBefore we had taken three steps, the Count’s quick eye discovered the lost mouse under the seat that we had been occupying. He pulled aside the bench, took the little animal up in his hand, and then suddenly stopped, on his knees, looking intently at a particular place on the ground just beneath him.\n\nWhen he rose to his feet again, his hand shook so that he could hardly put the mouse back in the cage, and his face was of a faint livid yellow hue all over.\n\n“Percival!” he said, in a whisper. “Percival! come here.”\n\nSir Percival had paid no attention to any of us for the last ten minutes. He had been entirely absorbed in writing figures on the sand, and then rubbing them out again with the point of his stick.\n\n“What’s the matter now?” he asked, lounging carelessly into the boat-house.\n\n“Do you see nothing there?” said the Count, catching him nervously by the collar with one hand, and pointing with the other to the place near which he had found the mouse.\n\n“I see plenty of dry sand,” answered Sir Percival, “and a spot of dirt in the middle of it.”\n\n“Not dirt,” whispered the Count, fastening the other hand suddenly on Sir Percival’s collar, and shaking it in his agitation. “Blood.”\n\nLaura was near enough to hear the last word, softly as he whispered it. She turned to me with a look of terror.\n\n“Nonsense, my dear,” I said. “There is no need to be alarmed. It is only the blood of a poor little stray dog.”\n\nEverybody was astonished, and everybody’s eyes were fixed on me inquiringly.\n\n“How do you know that?” asked Sir Percival, speaking first.\n\n“I found the dog here, dying, on the day when you all returned from abroad,” I replied. “The poor creature had strayed into the plantation, and had been shot by your keeper.”\n\n“Whose dog was it?” inquired Sir Percival. “Not one of mine?”\n\n“Did you try to save the poor thing?” asked Laura earnestly. “Surely you tried to save it, Marian?”\n\n“Yes,” I said, “the housekeeper and I both did our best—but the dog was mortally wounded, and he died under our hands.”\n\n“Whose dog was it?” persisted Sir Percival, repeating his question a little irritably. “One of mine?”\n\n“No, not one of yours.”\n\n“Whose then? Did the housekeeper know?”\n\nThe housekeeper’s report of Mrs. Catherick’s desire to conceal her visit to Blackwater Park from Sir Percival’s knowledge recurred to my memory the moment he put that last question, and I half doubted the discretion of answering it; but in my anxiety to quiet the general alarm, I had thoughtlessly advanced too far to draw back, except at the risk of exciting suspicion, which might only make matters worse. There was nothing for it but to answer at once, without reference to results.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “The housekeeper knew. She told me it was Mrs. Catherick’s dog.”\n\nSir Percival had hitherto remained at the inner end of the boat-house with Count Fosco, while I spoke to him from the door. But the instant Mrs. Catherick’s name passed my lips he pushed by the Count roughly, and placed himself face to face with me under the open daylight.\n\n“How came the housekeeper to know it was Mrs. Catherick’s dog?” he asked, fixing his eyes on mine with a frowning interest and attention, which half angered, half startled me.\n\n“She knew it,” I said quietly, “because Mrs. Catherick brought the dog with her.”\n\n“Brought it with her? Where did she bring it with her?”\n\n“To this house.”\n\n“What the devil did Mrs. Catherick want at this house?”\n\nThe manner in which he put the question was even more offensive than the language in which he expressed it. I marked my sense of his want of common politeness by silently turning away from him.\n\nJust as I moved the Count’s persuasive hand was laid on his shoulder, and the Count’s mellifluous voice interposed to quiet him.\n\n“My dear Percival!—gently—gently!”\n\nSir Percival looked round in his angriest manner. The Count only smiled and repeated the soothing application.\n\n“Gently, my good friend—gently!”\n\nSir Percival hesitated, followed me a few steps, and, to my great surprise, offered me an apology.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe,” he said. “I have been out of order lately, and I am afraid I am a little irritable. But I should like to know what Mrs. Catherick could possibly want here. When did she come? Was the housekeeper the only person who saw her?”\n\n“The only person,” I answered, “so far as I know.”\n\nThe Count interposed again.\n\n“In that case why not question the housekeeper?” he said. “Why not go, Percival, to the fountain-head of information at once?”\n\n“Quite right!” said Sir Percival. “Of course the housekeeper is the first person to question. Excessively stupid of me not to see it myself.” With those words he instantly left us to return to the house.\n\nThe motive of the Count’s interference, which had puzzled me at first, betrayed itself when Sir Percival’s back was turned. He had a host of questions to put to me about Mrs. Catherick, and the cause of her visit to Blackwater Park, which he could scarcely have asked in his friend’s presence. I made my answers as short as I civilly could, for I had already determined to check the least approach to any exchanging of confidences between Count Fosco and myself. Laura, however, unconsciously helped him to extract all my information, by making inquiries herself, which left me no alternative but to reply to her, or to appear in the very unenviable and very false character of a depositary of Sir Percival’s secrets. The end of it was, that, in about ten minutes’ time, the Count knew as much as I know of Mrs. Catherick, and of the events which have so strangely connected us with her daughter, Anne, from the time when Hartright met with her to this day.\n\nThe effect of my information on him was, in one respect, curious enough.\n\nIntimately as he knows Sir Percival, and closely as he appears to be associated with Sir Percival’s private affairs in general, he is certainly as far as I am from knowing anything of the true story of Anne Catherick. The unsolved mystery in connection with this unhappy woman is now rendered doubly suspicious, in my eyes, by the absolute conviction which I feel, that the clue to it has been hidden by Sir Percival from the most intimate friend he has in the world. It was impossible to mistake the eager curiosity of the Count’s look and manner while he drank in greedily every word that fell from my lips. There are many kinds of curiosity, I know—but there is no misinterpreting the curiosity of blank surprise: if I ever saw it in my life I saw it in the Count’s face.\n\nWhile the questions and answers were going on, we had all been strolling quietly back through the plantation. As soon as we reached the house the first object that we saw in front of it was Sir Percival’s dog-cart, with the horse put to and the groom waiting by it in his stable-jacket. If these unexpected appearances were to be trusted, the examination of the house-keeper had produced important results already.\n\n“A fine horse, my friend,” said the Count, addressing the groom with the most engaging familiarity of manner, “You are going to drive out?”\n\n“I am not going, sir,” replied the man, looking at his stable-jacket, and evidently wondering whether the foreign gentleman took it for his livery. “My master drives himself.”\n\n“Aha!” said the Count, “does he indeed? I wonder he gives himself the trouble when he has got you to drive for him. Is he going to fatigue that nice, shining, pretty horse by taking him very far to-day?”\n\n“I don’t know, sir,” answered the man. “The horse is a mare, if you please, sir. She’s the highest-couraged thing we’ve got in the stables. Her name’s Brown Molly, sir, and she’ll go till she drops. Sir Percival usually takes Isaac of York for the short distances.”\n\n“And your shining courageous Brown Molly for the long?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Logical inference, Miss Halcombe,” continued the Count, wheeling round briskly, and addressing me. “Sir Percival is going a long distance to-day.”\n\nI made no reply. I had my own inferences to draw, from what I knew through the housekeeper and from what I saw before me, and I did not choose to share them with Count Fosco.\n\nWhen Sir Percival was in Cumberland (I thought to myself), he walked away a long distance, on Anne’s account, to question the family at Todd’s Corner. Now he is in Hampshire, is he going to drive away a long distance, on Anne’s account again, to question Mrs. Catherick at Welmingham?\n\nWe all entered the house. As we crossed the hall Sir Percival came out from the library to meet us. He looked hurried and pale and anxious—but for all that, he was in his most polite mood when he spoke to us.\n\n“I am sorry to say I am obliged to leave you,” he began—“a long drive—a matter that I can’t very well put off. I shall be back in good time to-morrow—but before I go I should like that little business-formality, which I spoke of this morning, to be settled. Laura, will you come into the library? It won’t take a minute—a mere formality. Countess, may I trouble you also? I want you and the Countess, Fosco, to be witnesses to a signature—nothing more. Come in at once and get it over.”\n\nHe held the library door open until they had passed in, followed them, and shut it softly.\n\nI remained, for a moment afterwards, standing alone in the hall, with my heart beating fast and my mind misgiving me sadly. Then I went on to the staircase, and ascended slowly to my own room.\n\n# IV\n\nJune 17th.—Just as my hand was on the door of my room, I heard Sir Percival’s voice calling to me from below.\n\n“I must beg you to come downstairs again,” he said. “It is Fosco’s fault, Miss Halcombe, not mine. He has started some nonsensical objection to his wife being one of the witnesses, and has obliged me to ask you to join us in the library.”\n\nI entered the room immediately with Sir Percival. Laura was waiting by the writing-table, twisting and turning her garden hat uneasily in her hands. Madame Fosco sat near her, in an arm-chair, imperturbably admiring her husband, who stood by himself at the other end of the library, picking off the dead leaves from the flowers in the window.\n\nThe moment I appeared the Count advanced to meet me, and to offer his explanations.\n\n“A thousand pardons, Miss Halcombe,” he said. “You know the character which is given to my countrymen by the English? We Italians are all wily and suspicious by nature, in the estimation of the good John Bull. Set me down, if you please, as being no better than the rest of my race. I am a wily Italian and a suspicious Italian. You have thought so yourself, dear lady, have you not? Well! it is part of my wiliness and part of my suspicion to object to Madame Fosco being a witness to Lady Glyde’s signature, when I am also a witness myself.”\n\n“There is not the shadow of a reason for his objection,” interposed Sir Percival. “I have explained to him that the law of England allows Madame Fosco to witness a signature as well as her husband.”\n\n“I admit it,” resumed the Count. “The law of England says, Yes, but the conscience of Fosco says, No.” He spread out his fat fingers on the bosom of his blouse, and bowed solemnly, as if he wished to introduce his conscience to us all, in the character of an illustrious addition to the society. “What this document which Lady Glyde is about to sign may be,” he continued, “I neither know nor desire to know. I only say this, circumstances may happen in the future which may oblige Percival, or his representatives, to appeal to the two witnesses, in which case it is certainly desirable that those witnesses should represent two opinions which are perfectly independent the one of the other. This cannot be if my wife signs as well as myself, because we have but one opinion between us, and that opinion is mine. I will not have it cast in my teeth, at some future day, that Madame Fosco acted under my coercion, and was, in plain fact, no witness at all. I speak in Percival’s interest, when I propose that my name shall appear (as the nearest friend of the husband), and your name, Miss Halcombe (as the nearest friend of the wife). I am a Jesuit, if you please to think so—a splitter of straws—a man of trifles and crochets and scruples—but you will humour me, I hope, in merciful consideration for my suspicious Italian character, and my uneasy Italian conscience.” He bowed again, stepped back a few paces, and withdrew his conscience from our society as politely as he had introduced it.\n\nThe Count’s scruples might have been honourable and reasonable enough, but there was something in his manner of expressing them which increased my unwillingness to be concerned in the business of the signature. No consideration of less importance than my consideration for Laura would have induced me to consent to be a witness at all. One look, however, at her anxious face decided me to risk anything rather than desert her.\n\n“I will readily remain in the room,” I said. “And if I find no reason for starting any small scruples on my side, you may rely on me as a witness.”\n\nSir Percival looked at me sharply, as if he was about to say something. But at the same moment, Madame Fosco attracted his attention by rising from her chair. She had caught her husband’s eye, and had evidently received her orders to leave the room.\n\n“You needn’t go,” said Sir Percival.\n\nMadame Fosco looked for her orders again, got them again, said she would prefer leaving us to our business, and resolutely walked out. The Count lit a cigarette, went back to the flowers in the window, and puffed little jets of smoke at the leaves, in a state of the deepest anxiety about killing the insects.\n\nMeanwhile Sir Percival unlocked a cupboard beneath one of the book-cases, and produced from it a piece of parchment, folded longwise, many times over. He placed it on the table, opened the last fold only, and kept his hand on the rest. The last fold displayed a strip of blank parchment with little wafers stuck on it at certain places. Every line of the writing was hidden in the part which he still held folded up under his hand. Laura and I looked at each other. Her face was pale, but it showed no indecision and no fear.\n\nSir Percival dipped a pen in ink, and handed it to his wife. “Sign your name there,” he said, pointing to the place. “You and Fosco are to sign afterwards, Miss Halcombe, opposite those two wafers. Come here, Fosco! witnessing a signature is not to be done by mooning out of window and smoking into the flowers.”\n\nThe Count threw away his cigarette, and joined us at the table, with his hands carelessly thrust into the scarlet belt of his blouse, and his eyes steadily fixed on Sir Percival’s face. Laura, who was on the other side of her husband, with the pen in her hand, looked at him too. He stood between them holding the folded parchment down firmly on the table, and glancing across at me, as I sat opposite to him, with such a sinister mixture of suspicion and embarrassment on his face that he looked more like a prisoner at the bar than a gentleman in his own house.\n\n“Sign there,” he repeated, turning suddenly on Laura, and pointing once more to the place on the parchment.\n\n“What is it I am to sign?” she asked quietly.\n\n“I have no time to explain,” he answered. “The dog-cart is at the door, and I must go directly. Besides, if I had time, you wouldn’t understand. It is a purely formal document, full of legal technicalities, and all that sort of thing. Come! come! sign your name, and let us have done as soon as possible.”\n\n“I ought surely to know what I am signing, Sir Percival, before I write my name?”\n\n“Nonsense! What have women to do with business? I tell you again, you can’t understand it.”\n\n“At any rate, let me try to understand it. Whenever Mr. Gilmore had any business for me to do, he always explained it first, and I always understood him.”\n\n“I dare say he did. He was your servant, and was obliged to explain. I am your husband, and am NOT obliged. How much longer do you mean to keep me here? I tell you again, there is no time for reading anything—the dog-cart is waiting at the door. Once for all, will you sign or will you not?”\n\nShe still had the pen in her hand, but she made no approach to signing her name with it.\n\n“If my signature pledges me to anything,” she said, “surely I have some claim to know what that pledge is?”\n\nHe lifted up the parchment, and struck it angrily on the table.\n\n“Speak out!” he said. “You were always famous for telling the truth. Never mind Miss Halcombe, never mind Fosco—say, in plain terms, you distrust me.”\n\nThe Count took one of his hands out of his belt and laid it on Sir Percival’s shoulder. Sir Percival shook it off irritably. The Count put it on again with unruffled composure.\n\n“Control your unfortunate temper, Percival,” he said, “Lady Glyde is right.”\n\n“Right!” cried Sir Percival. “A wife right in distrusting her husband!”\n\n“It is unjust and cruel to accuse me of distrusting you,” said Laura. “Ask Marian if I am not justified in wanting to know what this writing requires of me before I sign it.”\n\n“I won’t have any appeals made to Miss Halcombe,” retorted Sir Percival. “Miss Halcombe has nothing to do with the matter.”\n\nI had not spoken hitherto, and I would much rather not have spoken now. But the expression of distress in Laura’s face when she turned it towards me, and the insolent injustice of her husband’s conduct, left me no other alternative than to give my opinion, for her sake, as soon as I was asked for it.\n\n“Excuse me, Sir Percival,” I said—“but as one of the witnesses to the signature, I venture to think that I HAVE something to do with the matter. Laura’s objection seems to me a perfectly fair one, and speaking for myself only, I cannot assume the responsibility of witnessing her signature, unless she first understands what the writing is which you wish her to sign.”\n\n“A cool declaration, upon my soul!” cried Sir Percival. “The next time you invite yourself to a man’s house, Miss Halcombe, I recommend you not to repay his hospitality by taking his wife’s side against him in a matter that doesn’t concern you.”\n\nI started to my feet as suddenly as if he had struck me. If I had been a man, I would have knocked him down on the threshold of his own door, and have left his house, never on any earthly consideration to enter it again. But I was only a woman—and I loved his wife so dearly!\n\nThank God, that faithful love helped me, and I sat down again without saying a word. SHE knew what I had suffered and what I had suppressed. She ran round to me, with the tears streaming from her eyes. “Oh, Marian!” she whispered softly. “If my mother had been alive, she could have done no more for me!”\n\n“Come back and sign!” cried Sir Percival from the other side of the table.\n\n“Shall I?” she asked in my ear; “I will, if you tell me.”\n\n“No,” I answered. “The right and the truth are with you—sign nothing, unless you have read it first.”\n\n“Come back and sign!” he reiterated, in his loudest and angriest tones.\n\nThe Count, who had watched Laura and me with a close and silent attention, interposed for the second time.\n\n“Percival!” he said. “I remember that I am in the presence of ladies. Be good enough, if you please, to remember it too.”\n\nSir Percival turned on him speechless with passion. The Count’s firm hand slowly tightened its grasp on his shoulder, and the Count’s steady voice quietly repeated, “Be good enough, if you please, to remember it too.”\n\nThey both looked at each other. Sir Percival slowly drew his shoulder from under the Count’s hand, slowly turned his face away from the Count’s eyes, doggedly looked down for a little while at the parchment on the table, and then spoke, with the sullen submission of a tamed animal, rather than the becoming resignation of a convinced man.\n\n“I don’t want to offend anybody,” he said, “but my wife’s obstinacy is enough to try the patience of a saint. I have told her this is merely a formal document—and what more can she want? You may say what you please, but it is no part of a woman’s duty to set her husband at defiance. Once more, Lady Glyde, and for the last time, will you sign or will you not?”\n\nLaura returned to his side of the table, and took up the pen again.\n\n“I will sign with pleasure,” she said, “if you will only treat me as a responsible being. I care little what sacrifice is required of me, if it will affect no one else, and lead to no ill results—”\n\n“Who talked of a sacrifice being required of you?” he broke in, with a half-suppressed return of his former violence.\n\n“I only meant,” she resumed, “that I would refuse no concession which I could honourably make. If I have a scruple about signing my name to an engagement of which I know nothing, why should you visit it on me so severely? It is rather hard, I think, to treat Count Fosco’s scruples so much more indulgently than you have treated mine.”\n\nThis unfortunate, yet most natural, reference to the Count’s extraordinary power over her husband, indirect as it was, set Sir Percival’s smouldering temper on fire again in an instant.\n\n“Scruples!” he repeated. “YOUR scruples! It is rather late in the day for you to be scrupulous. I should have thought you had got over all weakness of that sort, when you made a virtue of necessity by marrying me.”\n\nThe instant he spoke those words, Laura threw down the pen—looked at him with an expression in her eyes which, throughout all my experience of her, I had never seen in them before, and turned her back on him in dead silence.\n\nThis strong expression of the most open and the most bitter contempt was so entirely unlike herself, so utterly out of her character, that it silenced us all. There was something hidden, beyond a doubt, under the mere surface-brutality of the words which her husband had just addressed to her. There was some lurking insult beneath them, of which I was wholly ignorant, but which had left the mark of its profanation so plainly on her face that even a stranger might have seen it.\n\nThe Count, who was no stranger, saw it as distinctly as I did. When I left my chair to join Laura, I heard him whisper under his breath to Sir Percival, “You idiot!”\n\nLaura walked before me to the door as I advanced, and at the same time her husband spoke to her once more.\n\n“You positively refuse, then, to give me your signature?” he said, in the altered tone of a man who was conscious that he had let his own licence of language seriously injure him.\n\n“After what you have just said to me,” she replied firmly, “I refuse my signature until I have read every line in that parchment from the first word to the last. Come away, Marian, we have remained here long enough.”\n\n“One moment!” interposed the Count before Sir Percival could speak again—“one moment, Lady Glyde, I implore you!”\n\nLaura would have left the room without noticing him, but I stopped her.\n\n“Don’t make an enemy of the Count!” I whispered. “Whatever you do, don’t make an enemy of the Count!”\n\nShe yielded to me. I closed the door again, and we stood near it waiting. Sir Percival sat down at the table, with his elbow on the folded parchment, and his head resting on his clenched fist. The Count stood between us—master of the dreadful position in which we were placed, as he was master of everything else.\n\n“Lady Glyde,” he said, with a gentleness which seemed to address itself to our forlorn situation instead of to ourselves, “pray pardon me if I venture to offer one suggestion, and pray believe that I speak out of my profound respect and my friendly regard for the mistress of this house.” He turned sharply towards Sir Percival. “Is it absolutely necessary,” he asked, “that this thing here, under your elbow, should be signed to-day?”\n\n“It is necessary to my plans and wishes,” returned the other sulkily. “But that consideration, as you may have noticed, has no influence with Lady Glyde.”\n\n“Answer my plain question plainly. Can the business of the signature be put off till to-morrow—Yes or No?”\n\n“Yes, if you will have it so.”\n\n“Then what are you wasting your time for here? Let the signature wait till to-morrow—let it wait till you come back.”\n\nSir Percival looked up with a frown and an oath.\n\n“You are taking a tone with me that I don’t like,” he said. “A tone I won’t bear from any man.”\n\n“I am advising you for your good,” returned the Count, with a smile of quiet contempt. “Give yourself time—give Lady Glyde time. Have you forgotten that your dog-cart is waiting at the door? My tone surprises you—ha? I dare say it does—it is the tone of a man who can keep his temper. How many doses of good advice have I given you in my time? More than you can count. Have I ever been wrong? I defy you to quote me an instance of it. Go! take your drive. The matter of the signature can wait till to-morrow. Let it wait—and renew it when you come back.”\n\nSir Percival hesitated and looked at his watch. His anxiety about the secret journey which he was to take that day, revived by the Count’s words, was now evidently disputing possession of his mind with his anxiety to obtain Laura’s signature. e considered for a little while, and then got up from his chair.\n\n“It is easy to argue me down,” he said, “when I have no time to answer you. I will take your advice, Fosco—not because I want it, or believe in it, but because I can’t stop here any longer.” He paused, and looked round darkly at his wife. “If you don’t give me your signature when I come back to-morrow—!” The rest was lost in the noise of his opening the book-case cupboard again, and locking up the parchment once more. He took his hat and gloves off the table, and made for the door. Laura and I drew back to let him pass. “Remember to-morrow!” he said to his wife, and went out.\n\nWe waited to give him time to cross the hall and drive away. The Count approached us while we were standing near the door.\n\n“You have just seen Percival at his worst, Miss Halcombe,” he said. “As his old friend, I am sorry for him and ashamed of him. As his old friend, I promise you that he shall not break out to-morrow in the same disgraceful manner in which he has broken out to-day.”\n\nLaura had taken my arm while he was speaking and she pressed it significantly when he had done. It would have been a hard trial to any woman to stand by and see the office of apologist for her husband’s misconduct quietly assumed by his male friend in her own house—and it was a trial to HER. I thanked the Count civilly, and let her out. Yes! I thanked him: for I felt already, with a sense of inexpressible helplessness and humiliation, that it was either his interest or his caprice to make sure of my continuing to reside at Blackwater Park, and I knew after Sir Percival’s conduct to me, that without the support of the Count’s influence, I could not hope to remain there. His influence, the influence of all others that I dreaded most, was actually the one tie which now held me to Laura in the hour of her utmost need!\n\nWe heard the wheels of the dog-cart crashing on the gravel of the drive as we came into the hall. Sir Percival had started on his journey.\n\n“Where is he going to, Marian?” Laura whispered. “Every fresh thing he does seems to terrify me about the future. Have you any suspicions?”\n\nAfter what she had undergone that morning, I was unwilling to tell her my suspicions.\n\n“How should I know his secrets?” I said evasively.\n\n“I wonder if the housekeeper knows?” she persisted.\n\n“Certainly not,” I replied. “She must be quite as ignorant as we are.”\n\nLaura shook her head doubtfully.\n\n“Did you not hear from the housekeeper that there was a report of Anne Catherick having been seen in this neighbourhood? Don’t you think he may have gone away to look for her?”\n\n“I would rather compose myself, Laura, by not thinking about it at all, and after what has happened, you had better follow my example. Come into my room, and rest and quiet yourself a little.”\n\nWe sat down together close to the window, and let the fragrant summer air breathe over our faces.\n\n“I am ashamed to look at you, Marian,” she said, “after what you submitted to downstairs, for my sake. Oh, my own love, I am almost heartbroken when I think of it! But I will try to make it up to you—I will indeed!”\n\n“Hush! hush!” I replied; “don’t talk so. What is the trifling mortification of my pride compared to the dreadful sacrifice of your happiness?”\n\n“You heard what he said to me?” she went on quickly and vehemently. “You heard the words—but you don’t know what they meant—you don’t know why I threw down the pen and turned my back on him.” She rose in sudden agitation, and walked about the room. “I have kept many things from your knowledge, Marian, for fear of distressing you, and making you unhappy at the outset of our new lives. You don’t know how he has used me. And yet you ought to know, for you saw how he used me to-day. You heard him sneer at my presuming to be scrupulous—you heard him say I had made a virtue of necessity in marrying him.” She sat down again, her face flushed deeply, and her hands twisted and twined together in her lap. “I can’t tell you about it now,” she said; “I shall burst out crying if I tell you now—later, Marian, when I am more sure of myself. My poor head aches, darling—aches, aches, aches. Where is your smelling-bottle? Let me talk to you about yourself. I wish I had given him my signature, for your sake. Shall I give it to him to-morrow? I would rather compromise myself than compromise you. After your taking my part against him, he will lay all the blame on you if I refuse again. What shall we do? Oh, for a friend to help us and advise us!—a friend we could really trust!”\n\nShe sighed bitterly. I saw in her face that she was thinking of Hartright—saw it the more plainly because her last words set me thinking of him too. In six months only from her marriage we wanted the faithful service he had offered to us in his farewell words. How little I once thought that we should ever want it at all!\n\n“We must do what we can to help ourselves,” I said. “Let us try to talk it over calmly, Laura—let us do all in our power to decide for the best.”\n\nPutting what she knew of her husband’s embarrassments and what I had heard of his conversation with the lawyer together, we arrived necessarily at the conclusion that the parchment in the library had been drawn up for the purpose of borrowing money, and that Laura’s signature was absolutely necessary to fit it for the attainment of Sir Percival’s object.\n\nThe second question, concerning the nature of the legal contract by which the money was to be obtained, and the degree of personal responsibility to which Laura might subject herself if she signed it in the dark, involved considerations which lay far beyond any knowledge and experience that either of us possessed. My own convictions led me to believe that the hidden contents of the parchment concealed a transaction of the meanest and the most fraudulent kind.\n\nI had not formed this conclusion in consequence of Sir Percival’s refusal to show the writing or to explain it, for that refusal might well have proceeded from his obstinate disposition and his domineering temper alone. My sole motive for distrusting his honesty sprang from the change which I had observed in his language and his manners at Blackwater Park, a change which convinced me that he had been acting a part throughout the whole period of his probation at Limmeridge House. His elaborate delicacy, his ceremonious politeness which harmonised so agreeably with Mr. Gilmore’s old-fashioned notions, his modesty with Laura, his candour with me, his moderation with Mr. Fairlie—all these were the artifices of a mean, cunning, and brutal man, who had dropped his disguise when his practised duplicity had gained its end, and had openly shown himself in the library on that very day. I say nothing of the grief which this discovery caused me on Laura’s account, for it is not to be expressed by any words of mine. I only refer to it at all, because it decided me to oppose her signing the parchment, whatever the consequences might be, unless she was first made acquainted with the contents.\n\nUnder these circumstances, the one chance for us when to-morrow came was to be provided with an objection to giving the signature, which might rest on sufficiently firm commercial or legal grounds to shake Sir Percival’s resolution, and to make him suspect that we two women understood the laws and obligations of business as well as himself.\n\nAfter some pondering, I determined to write to the only honest man within reach whom we could trust to help us discreetly in our forlorn situation. That man was Mr. Gilmore’s partner, Mr. Kyrle, who conducted the business now that our old friend had been obliged to withdraw from it, and to leave London on account of his health. I explained to Laura that I had Mr. Gilmore’s own authority for placing implicit confidence in his partner’s integrity, discretion, and accurate knowledge of all her affairs, and with her full approval I sat down at once to write the letter, I began by stating our position to Mr. Kyrle exactly as it was, and then asked for his advice in return, expressed in plain, downright terms which he could comprehend without any danger of misinterpretations and mistakes. My letter was as short as I could possibly make it, and was, I hope, unencumbered by needless apologies and needless details.\n\nJust as I was about to put the address on the envelope an obstacle was discovered by Laura, which in the effort and preoccupation of writing had escaped my mind altogether.\n\n“How are we to get the answer in time?” she asked. “Your letter will not be delivered in London before to-morrow morning, and the post will not bring the reply here till the morning after.”\n\nThe only way of overcoming this difficulty was to have the answer brought to us from the lawyer’s office by a special messenger. I wrote a postscript to that effect, begging that the messenger might be despatched with the reply by the eleven o’clock morning train, which would bring him to our station at twenty minutes past one, and so enable him to reach Blackwater Park by two o’clock at the latest. He was to be directed to ask for me, to answer no questions addressed to him by any one else, and to deliver his letter into no hands but mine.\n\n“In case Sir Percival should come back to-morrow before two o’clock,” I said to Laura, “the wisest plan for you to adopt is to be out in the grounds all the morning with your book or your work, and not to appear at the house till the messenger has had time to arrive with the letter. I will wait here for him all the morning, to guard against any misadventures or mistakes. By following this arrangement I hope and believe we shall avoid being taken by surprise. Let us go down to the drawing-room now. We may excite suspicion if we remain shut up together too long.”\n\n“Suspicion?” she repeated. “Whose suspicion can we excite, now that Sir Percival has left the house? Do you mean Count Fosco?”\n\n“Perhaps I do, Laura.”\n\n“You are beginning to dislike him as much as I do, Marian.”\n\n“No, not to dislike him. Dislike is always more or less associated with contempt—I can see nothing in the Count to despise.”\n\n“You are not afraid of him, are you?”\n\n“Perhaps I am—a little.”\n\n“Afraid of him, after his interference in our favour to-day!”\n\n“Yes. I am more afraid of his interference than I am of Sir Percival’s violence. Remember what I said to you in the library. Whatever you do, Laura, don’t make an enemy of the Count!”\n\nWe went downstairs. Laura entered the drawing-room, while I proceeded across the hall, with my letter in my hand, to put it into the post-bag, which hung against the wall opposite to me.\n\nThe house door was open, and as I crossed past it, I saw Count Fosco and his wife standing talking together on the steps outside, with their faces turned towards me.\n\nThe Countess came into the hall rather hastily, and asked if I had leisure enough for five minutes’ private conversation. Feeling a little surprised by such an appeal from such a person, I put my letter into the bag, and replied that I was quite at her disposal. She took my arm with unaccustomed friendliness and familiarity, and instead of leading me into an empty room, drew me out with her to the belt of turf which surrounded the large fish-pond.\n\nAs we passed the Count on the steps he bowed and smiled, and then went at once into the house, pushing the hall door to after him, but not actually closing it.\n\nThe Countess walked me gently round the fish-pond. I expected to be made the depositary of some extraordinary confidence, and I was astonished to find that Madame Fosco’s communication for my private ear was nothing more than a polite assurance of her sympathy for me, after what had happened in the library. Her husband had told her of all that had passed, and of the insolent manner in which Sir Percival had spoken to me. This information had so shocked and distressed her, on my account and on Laura’s, that she had made up her mind, if anything of the sort happened again, to mark her sense of Sir Percival’s outrageous conduct by leaving the house. The Count had approved of her idea, and she now hoped that I approved of it too.\n\nI thought this a very strange proceeding on the part of such a remarkably reserved woman as Madame Fosco, especially after the interchange of sharp speeches which had passed between us during the conversation in the boat-house on that very morning. However, it was my plain duty to meet a polite and friendly advance on the part of one of my elders with a polite and friendly reply. I answered the Countess accordingly in her own tone, and then, thinking we had said all that was necessary on either side, made an attempt to get back to the house.\n\nBut Madame Fosco seemed resolved not to part with me, and to my unspeakable amazement, resolved also to talk. Hitherto the most silent of women, she now persecuted me with fluent conventionalities on the subject of married life, on the subject of Sir Percival and Laura, on the subject of her own happiness, on the subject of the late Mr. Fairlie’s conduct to her in the matter of her legacy, and on half a dozen other subjects besides, until she had detained me walking round and round the fish-pond for more than half an hour, and had quite wearied me out. Whether she discovered this or not, I cannot say, but she stopped as abruptly as she had begun—looked towards the house door, resumed her icy manner in a moment, and dropped my arm of her own accord before I could think of an excuse for accomplishing my own release from her.\n\nAs I pushed open the door and entered the hall, I found myself suddenly face to face with the Count again. He was just putting a letter into the post-bag.\n\nAfter he had dropped it in and had closed the bag, he asked me where I had left Madame Fosco. I told him, and he went out at the hall door immediately to join his wife. His manner when he spoke to me was so unusually quiet and subdued that I turned and looked after him, wondering if he were ill or out of spirits.\n\nWhy my next proceeding was to go straight up to the post-bag and take out my own letter and look at it again, with a vague distrust on me, and why the looking at it for the second time instantly suggested the idea to my mind of sealing the envelope for its greater security—are mysteries which are either too deep or too shallow for me to fathom. Women, as everybody knows, constantly act on impulses which they cannot explain even to themselves, and I can only suppose that one of those impulses was the hidden cause of my unaccountable conduct on this occasion.\n\nWhatever influence animated me, I found cause to congratulate myself on having obeyed it as soon as I prepared to seal the letter in my own room. I had originally closed the envelope in the usual way by moistening the adhesive point and pressing it on the paper beneath, and when I now tried it with my finger, after a lapse of full three-quarters of an hour, the envelope opened on the instant, without sticking or tearing. Perhaps I had fastened it insufficiently? Perhaps there might have been some defect in the adhesive gum?\n\nOr, perhaps——No! it is quite revolting enough to feel that third conjecture stirring in my mind. I would rather not see it confronting me in plain black and white.\n\nI almost dread to-morrow—so much depends on my discretion and self-control. There are two precautions, at all events, which I am sure not to forget. I must be careful to keep up friendly appearances with the Count, and I must be well on my guard when the messenger from the office comes here with the answer to my letter.\n\n# V\n\nJune 17th.—When the dinner hour brought us together again, Count Fosco was in his usual excellent spirits. He exerted himself to interest and amuse us, as if he was determined to efface from our memories all recollection of what had passed in the library that afternoon. Lively descriptions of his adventures in travelling, amusing anecdotes of remarkable people whom he had met with abroad, quaint comparisons between the social customs of various nations, illustrated by examples drawn from men and women indiscriminately all over Europe, humorous confessions of the innocent follies of his own early life, when he ruled the fashions of a second-rate Italian town, and wrote preposterous romances on the French model for a second-rate Italian newspaper—all flowed in succession so easily and so gaily from his lips, and all addressed our various curiosities and various interests so directly and so delicately, that Laura and I listened to him with as much attention and, inconsistent as it may seem, with as much admiration also, as Madame Fosco herself. Women can resist a man’s love, a man’s fame, a man’s personal appearance, and a man’s money, but they cannot resist a man’s tongue when he knows how to talk to them.\n\nAfter dinner, while the favourable impression which he had produced on us was still vivid in our minds, the Count modestly withdrew to read in the library.\n\nLaura proposed a stroll in the grounds to enjoy the close of the long evening. It was necessary in common politeness to ask Madame Fosco to join us, but this time she had apparently received her orders beforehand, and she begged we would kindly excuse her. “The Count will probably want a fresh supply of cigarettes,” she remarked by way of apology, “and nobody can make them to his satisfaction but myself.” Her cold blue eyes almost warmed as she spoke the words—she looked actually proud of being the officiating medium through which her lord and master composed himself with tobacco-smoke!\n\nLaura and I went out together alone.\n\nIt was a misty, heavy evening. There was a sense of blight in the air; the flowers were drooping in the garden, and the ground was parched and dewless. The western heaven, as we saw it over the quiet trees, was of a pale yellow hue, and the sun was setting faintly in a haze. Coming rain seemed near—it would fall probably with the fall of night.\n\n“Which way shall we go?” I asked\n\n“Towards the lake, Marian, if you like,” she answered.\n\n“You seem unaccountably fond, Laura, of that dismal lake.”\n\n“No, not of the lake but of the scenery about it. The sand and heath and the fir-trees are the only objects I can discover, in all this large place, to remind me of Limmeridge. But we will walk in some other direction if you prefer it.”\n\n“I have no favourite walks at Blackwater Park, my love. One is the same as another to me. Let us go to the lake—we may find it cooler in the open space than we find it here.”\n\nWe walked through the shadowy plantation in silence. The heaviness in the evening air oppressed us both, and when we reached the boat-house we were glad to sit down and rest inside.\n\nA white fog hung low over the lake. The dense brown line of the trees on the opposite bank appeared above it, like a dwarf forest floating in the sky. The sandy ground, shelving downward from where we sat, was lost mysteriously in the outward layers of the fog. The silence was horrible. No rustling of the leaves—no bird’s note in the wood—no cry of water-fowl from the pools of the hidden lake. Even the croaking of the frogs had ceased to-night.\n\n“It is very desolate and gloomy,” said Laura. “But we can be more alone here than anywhere else.”\n\nShe spoke quietly and looked at the wilderness of sand and mist with steady, thoughtful eyes. I could see that her mind was too much occupied to feel the dreary impressions from without which had fastened themselves already on mine.\n\n“I promised, Marian, to tell you the truth about my married life, instead of leaving you any longer to guess it for yourself,” she began. “That secret is the first I have ever had from you, love, and I am determined it shall be the last. I was silent, as you know, for your sake—and perhaps a little for my own sake as well. It is very hard for a woman to confess that the man to whom she has given her whole life is the man of all others who cares least for the gift. If you were married yourself, Marian—and especially if you were happily married—you would feel for me as no single woman CAN feel, however kind and true she may be.”\n\nWhat answer could I make? I could only take her hand and look at her with my whole heart as well as my eyes would let me.\n\n“How often,” she went on, “I have heard you laughing over what you used to call your ‘poverty!’ how often you have made me mock-speeches of congratulation on my wealth! Oh, Marian, never laugh again. Thank God for your poverty—it has made you your own mistress, and has saved you from the lot that has fallen on ME.”\n\nA sad beginning on the lips of a young wife!—sad in its quiet plain-spoken truth. The few days we had all passed together at Blackwater Park had been many enough to show me—to show any one—what her husband had married her for.\n\n“You shall not be distressed,” she said, “by hearing how soon my disappointments and my trials began—or even by knowing what they were. It is bad enough to have them on my memory. If I tell you how he received the first and last attempt at remonstrance that I ever made, you will know how he has always treated me, as well as if I had described it in so many words. It was one day at Rome when we had ridden out together to the tomb of Cecilia Metella. The sky was calm and lovely, and the grand old ruin looked beautiful, and the remembrance that a husband’s love had raised it in the old time to a wife’s memory, made me feel more tenderly and more anxiously towards my husband than I had ever felt yet. ‘Would you build such a tomb for ME, Percival?’ I asked him. ‘You said you loved me dearly before we were married, and yet, since that time——’ I could get no farther. Marian! he was not even looking at me! I pulled down my veil, thinking it best not to let him see that the tears were in my eyes. I fancied he had not paid any attention to me, but he had. He said, ‘Come away,’ and laughed to himself as he helped me on to my horse. He mounted his own horse and laughed again as we rode away. ‘If I do build you a tomb,’ he said, ‘it will be done with your own money. I wonder whether Cecilia Metella had a fortune and paid for hers.’ I made no reply—how could I, when I was crying behind my veil? ‘Ah, you light-complexioned women are all sulky,’ he said. ‘What do you want? compliments and soft speeches? Well! I’m in a good humour this morning. Consider the compliments paid and the speeches said.’ Men little know when they say hard things to us how well we remember them, and how much harm they do us. It would have been better for me if I had gone on crying, but his contempt dried up my tears and hardened my heart. From that time, Marian, I never checked myself again in thinking of Walter Hartright. I let the memory of those happy days, when we were so fond of each other in secret, come back and comfort me. What else had I to look to for consolation? If we had been together you would have helped me to better things. I know it was wrong, darling, but tell me if I was wrong without any excuse.”\n\nI was obliged to turn my face from her. “Don’t ask me!” I said. “Have I suffered as you have suffered? What right have I to decide?”\n\n“I used to think of him,” she pursued, dropping her voice and moving closer to me, “I used to think of him when Percival left me alone at night to go among the Opera people. I used to fancy what I might have been if it had pleased God to bless me with poverty, and if I had been his wife. I used to see myself in my neat cheap gown, sitting at home and waiting for him while he was earning our bread—sitting at home and working for him and loving him all the better because I had to work for him—seeing him come in tired and taking off his hat and coat for him, and, Marian, pleasing him with little dishes at dinner that I had learnt to make for his sake. Oh! I hope he is never lonely enough and sad enough to think of me and see me as I have thought of HIM and see HIM!”\n\nAs she said those melancholy words, all the lost tenderness returned to her voice, and all the lost beauty trembled back into her face. Her eyes rested as lovingly on the blighted, solitary, ill-omened view before us, as if they saw the friendly hills of Cumberland in the dim and threatening sky.\n\n“Don’t speak of Walter any more,” I said, as soon as I could control myself. “Oh, Laura, spare us both the wretchedness of talking of him now!”\n\nShe roused herself, and looked at me tenderly.\n\n“I would rather be silent about him for ever,” she answered, “than cause you a moment’s pain.”\n\n“It is in your interests,” I pleaded; “it is for your sake that I speak. If your husband heard you——”\n\n“It would not surprise him if he did hear me.”\n\nShe made that strange reply with a weary calmness and coldness. The change in her manner, when she gave the answer, startled me almost as much as the answer itself.\n\n“Not surprise him!” I repeated. “Laura! remember what you are saying—you frighten me!”\n\n“It is true,” she said; “it is what I wanted to tell you to-day, when we were talking in your room. My only secret when I opened my heart to him at Limmeridge was a harmless secret, Marian—you said so yourself. The name was all I kept from him, and he has discovered it.”\n\nI heard her, but I could say nothing. Her last words had killed the little hope that still lived in me.\n\n“It happened at Rome,” she went on, as wearily calm and cold as ever. “We were at a little party given to the English by some friends of Sir Percival’s—Mr. and Mrs. Markland. Mrs. Markland had the reputation of sketching very beautifully, and some of the guests prevailed on her to show us her drawings. We all admired them, but something I said attracted her attention particularly to me. ‘Surely you draw yourself?’ she asked. ‘I used to draw a little once,’ I answered, ‘but I have given it up.’ ‘If you have once drawn,’ she said, ‘you may take to it again one of these days, and if you do, I wish you would let me recommend you a master.’ I said nothing—you know why, Marian—and tried to change the conversation. But Mrs. Markland persisted. ‘I have had all sorts of teachers,’ she went on, ‘but the best of all, the most intelligent and the most attentive, was a Mr. Hartright. If you ever take up your drawing again, do try him as a master. He is a young man—modest and gentlemanlike—I am sure you will like him.’ Think of those words being spoken to me publicly, in the presence of strangers—strangers who had been invited to meet the bride and bridegroom! I did all I could to control myself—I said nothing, and looked down close at the drawings. When I ventured to raise my head again, my eyes and my husband’s eyes met, and I knew, by his look, that my face had betrayed me. ‘We will see about Mr. Hartright,’ he said, looking at me all the time, ‘when we get back to England. I agree with you, Mrs. Markland—I think Lady Glyde is sure to like him.’ He laid an emphasis on the last words which made my cheeks burn, and set my heart beating as if it would stifle me. Nothing more was said. We came away early. He was silent in the carriage driving back to the hotel. He helped me out, and followed me upstairs as usual. But the moment we were in the drawing-room, he locked the door, pushed me down into a chair, and stood over me with his hands on my shoulders. ‘Ever since that morning when you made your audacious confession to me at Limmeridge,’ he said, ‘I have wanted to find out the man, and I found him in your face to-night. Your drawing-master was the man, and his name is Hartright. You shall repent it, and he shall repent it, to the last hour of your lives. Now go to bed and dream of him if you like, with the marks of my horsewhip on his shoulders.’ Whenever he is angry with me now he refers to what I acknowledged to him in your presence with a sneer or a threat. I have no power to prevent him from putting his own horrible construction on the confidence I placed in him. I have no influence to make him believe me, or to keep him silent. You looked surprised to-day when you heard him tell me that I had made a virtue of necessity in marrying him. You will not be surprised again when you hear him repeat it, the next time he is out of temper——Oh, Marian! don’t! don’t! you hurt me!”\n\nI had caught her in my arms, and the sting and torment of my remorse had closed them round her like a vice. Yes! my remorse. The white despair of Walter’s face, when my cruel words struck him to the heart in the summer-house at Limmeridge, rose before me in mute, unendurable reproach. My hand had pointed the way which led the man my sister loved, step by step, far from his country and his friends. Between those two young hearts I had stood, to sunder them for ever, the one from the other, and his life and her life lay wasted before me alike in witness of the deed. I had done this, and done it for Sir Percival Glyde.\n\nFor Sir Percival Glyde.\n\nI heard her speaking, and I knew by the tone of her voice that she was comforting me—I, who deserved nothing but the reproach of her silence! How long it was before I mastered the absorbing misery of my own thoughts, I cannot tell. I was first conscious that she was kissing me, and then my eyes seemed to wake on a sudden to their sense of outward things, and I knew that I was looking mechanically straight before me at the prospect of the lake.\n\n“It is late,” I heard her whisper. “It will be dark in the plantation.” She shook my arm and repeated, “Marian! it will be dark in the plantation.”\n\n“Give me a minute longer,” I said—“a minute, to get better in.”\n\nI was afraid to trust myself to look at her yet, and I kept my eyes fixed on the view.\n\nIt WAS late. The dense brown line of trees in the sky had faded in the gathering darkness to the faint resemblance of a long wreath of smoke. The mist over the lake below had stealthily enlarged, and advanced on us. The silence was as breathless as ever, but the horror of it had gone, and the solemn mystery of its stillness was all that remained.\n\n“We are far from the house,” she whispered. “Let us go back.”\n\nShe stopped suddenly, and turned her face from me towards the entrance of the boat-house.\n\n“Marian!” she said, trembling violently. “Do you see nothing? Look!”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Down there, below us.”\n\nShe pointed. My eyes followed her hand, and I saw it too.\n\nA living figure was moving over the waste of heath in the distance. It crossed our range of view from the boat-house, and passed darkly along the outer edge of the mist. It stopped far off, in front of us—waited—and passed on; moving slowly, with the white cloud of mist behind it and above it—slowly, slowly, till it glided by the edge of the boat-house, and we saw it no more.\n\nWe were both unnerved by what had passed between us that evening. Some minutes elapsed before Laura would venture into the plantation, and before I could make up my mind to lead her back to the house.\n\n“Was it a man or a woman?” she asked in a whisper, as we moved at last into the dark dampness of the outer air.\n\n“I am not certain.”\n\n“Which do you think?”\n\n“It looked like a woman.”\n\n“I was afraid it was a man in a long cloak.”\n\n“It may be a man. In this dim light it is not possible to be certain.”\n\n“Wait, Marian! I’m frightened—I don’t see the path. Suppose the figure should follow us?”\n\n“Not at all likely, Laura. There is really nothing to be alarmed about. The shores of the lake are not far from the village, and they are free to any one to walk on by day or night. It is only wonderful we have seen no living creature there before.”\n\nWe were now in the plantation. It was very dark—so dark, that we found some difficulty in keeping the path. I gave Laura my arm, and we walked as fast as we could on our way back.\n\nBefore we were half-way through she stopped, and forced me to stop with her. She was listening.\n\n“Hush,” she whispered. “I hear something behind us.”\n\n“Dead leaves,” I said to cheer her, “or a twig blown off the trees.”\n\n“It is summer time, Marian, and there is not a breath of wind. Listen!”\n\nI heard the sound too—a sound like a light footstep following us.\n\n“No matter who it is, or what it is,” I said, “let us walk on. In another minute, if there is anything to alarm us, we shall be near enough to the house to be heard.”\n\nWe went on quickly—so quickly, that Laura was breathless by the time we were nearly through the plantation, and within sight of the lighted windows.\n\nI waited a moment to give her breathing-time. Just as we were about to proceed she stopped me again, and signed to me with her hand to listen once more. We both heard distinctly a long, heavy sigh behind us, in the black depths of the trees.\n\n“Who’s there?” I called out.\n\nThere was no answer.\n\n“Who’s there?” I repeated.\n\nAn instant of silence followed, and then we heard the light fall of the footsteps again, fainter and fainter—sinking away into the darkness—sinking, sinking, sinking—till they were lost in the silence.\n\nWe hurried out from the trees to the open lawn beyond, crossed it rapidly; and without another word passing between us, reached the house.\n\nIn the light of the hall-lamp Laura looked at me, with white cheeks and startled eyes.\n\n“I am half dead with fear,” she said. “Who could it have been?”\n\n“We will try to guess to-morrow,” I replied. “In the meantime say nothing to any one of what we have heard and seen.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because silence is safe, and we have need of safety in this house.”\n\nI sent Laura upstairs immediately, waited a minute to take off my hat and put my hair smooth, and then went at once to make my first investigations in the library, on pretence of searching for a book.\n\nThere sat the Count, filling out the largest easy-chair in the house, smoking and reading calmly, with his feet on an ottoman, his cravat across his knees, and his shirt collar wide open. And there sat Madame Fosco, like a quiet child, on a stool by his side, making cigarettes. Neither husband nor wife could, by any possibility, have been out late that evening, and have just got back to the house in a hurry. I felt that my object in visiting the library was answered the moment I set eyes on them.\n\nCount Fosco rose in polite confusion and tied his cravat on when I entered the room.\n\n“Pray don’t let me disturb you,” I said. “I have only come here to get a book.”\n\n“All unfortunate men of my size suffer from the heat,” said the Count, refreshing himself gravely with a large green fan. “I wish I could change places with my excellent wife. She is as cool at this moment as a fish in the pond outside.”\n\nThe Countess allowed herself to thaw under the influence of her husband’s quaint comparison. “I am never warm, Miss Halcombe,” she remarked, with the modest air of a woman who was confessing to one of her own merits.\n\n“Have you and Lady Glyde been out this evening?” asked the Count, while I was taking a book from the shelves to preserve appearances.\n\n“Yes, we went out to get a little air.”\n\n“May I ask in what direction?”\n\n“In the direction of the lake—as far as the boat-house.”\n\n“Aha? As far as the boat-house?”\n\nUnder other circumstances I might have resented his curiosity. But to-night I hailed it as another proof that neither he nor his wife were connected with the mysterious appearance at the lake.\n\n“No more adventures, I suppose, this evening?” he went on. “No more discoveries, like your discovery of the wounded dog?”\n\nHe fixed his unfathomable grey eyes on me, with that cold, clear, irresistible glitter in them which always forces me to look at him, and always makes me uneasy while I do look. An unutterable suspicion that his mind is prying into mine overcomes me at these times, and it overcame me now.\n\n“No,” I said shortly; “no adventures—no discoveries.”\n\nI tried to look away from him and leave the room. Strange as it seems, I hardly think I should have succeeded in the attempt if Madame Fosco had not helped me by causing him to move and look away first.\n\n“Count, you are keeping Miss Halcombe standing,” she said.\n\nThe moment he turned round to get me a chair, I seized my opportunity—thanked him—made my excuses—and slipped out.\n\nAn hour later, when Laura’s maid happened to be in her mistress’s room, I took occasion to refer to the closeness of the night, with a view to ascertaining next how the servants had been passing their time.\n\n“Have you been suffering much from the heat downstairs?” I asked.\n\n“No, miss,” said the girl, “we have not felt it to speak of.”\n\n“You have been out in the woods then, I suppose?”\n\n“Some of us thought of going, miss. But cook said she should take her chair into the cool court-yard, outside the kitchen door, and on second thoughts, all the rest of us took our chairs out there too.”\n\nThe housekeeper was now the only person who remained to be accounted for.\n\n“Is Mrs. Michelson gone to bed yet?” I inquired.\n\n“I should think not, miss,” said the girl, smiling. “Mrs. Michelson is more likely to be getting up just now than going to bed.”\n\n“Why? What do you mean? Has Mrs. Michelson been taking to her bed in the daytime?”\n\n“No, miss, not exactly, but the next thing to it. She’s been asleep all the evening on the sofa in her own room.”\n\nPutting together what I observed for myself in the library, and what I have just heard from Laura’s maid, one conclusion seems inevitable. The figure we saw at the lake was not the figure of Madame Fosco, of her husband, or of any of the servants. The footsteps we heard behind us were not the footsteps of any one belonging to the house.\n\nWho could it have been?\n\nIt seems useless to inquire. I cannot even decide whether the figure was a man’s or a woman’s. I can only say that I think it was a woman’s.\n\n# VI\n\nJune 18th.—The misery of self-reproach which I suffered yesterday evening, on hearing what Laura told me in the boat-house, returned in the loneliness of the night, and kept me waking and wretched for hours.\n\nI lighted my candle at last, and searched through my old journals to see what my share in the fatal error of her marriage had really been, and what I might have once done to save her from it. The result soothed me a little for it showed that, however blindly and ignorantly I acted, I acted for the best. Crying generally does me harm; but it was not so last night—I think it relieved me. I rose this morning with a settled resolution and a quiet mind. Nothing Sir Percival can say or do shall ever irritate me again, or make me forget for one moment that I am staying here in defiance of mortifications, insults, and threats, for Laura’s service and for Laura’s sake.\n\nThe speculations in which we might have indulged this morning, on the subject of the figure at the lake and the footsteps in the plantation, have been all suspended by a trifling accident which has caused Laura great regret. She has lost the little brooch I gave her for a keepsake on the day before her marriage. As she wore it when we went out yesterday evening we can only suppose that it must have dropped from her dress, either in the boat-house or on our way back. The servants have been sent to search, and have returned unsuccessful. And now Laura herself has gone to look for it. Whether she finds it or not the loss will help to excuse her absence from the house, if Sir Percival returns before the letter from Mr. Gilmore’s partner is placed in my hands.\n\nOne o’clock has just struck. I am considering whether I had better wait here for the arrival of the messenger from London, or slip away quietly, and watch for him outside the lodge gate.\n\nMy suspicion of everybody and everything in this house inclines me to think that the second plan may be the best. The Count is safe in the breakfast-room. I heard him, through the door, as I ran upstairs ten minutes since, exercising his canary-birds at their tricks:—“Come out on my little finger, my pret-pret-pretties! Come out, and hop upstairs! One, two, three—and up! Three, two, one—and down! One, two, three—twit-twit-twit-tweet!” The birds burst into their usual ecstasy of singing, and the Count chirruped and whistled at them in return, as if he was a bird himself. My room door is open, and I can hear the shrill singing and whistling at this very moment. If I am really to slip out without being observed, now is my time.\n\nFOUR O’CLOCK. The three hours that have passed since I made my last entry have turned the whole march of events at Blackwater Park in a new direction. Whether for good or for evil, I cannot and dare not decide.\n\nLet me get back first to the place at which I left off, or I shall lose myself in the confusion of my own thoughts.\n\nI went out, as I had proposed, to meet the messenger with my letter from London at the lodge gate. On the stairs I saw no one. In the hall I heard the Count still exercising his birds. But on crossing the quadrangle outside, I passed Madame Fosco, walking by herself in her favourite circle, round and round the great fish-pond. I at once slackened my pace, so as to avoid all appearance of being in a hurry, and even went the length, for caution’s sake, of inquiring if she thought of going out before lunch. She smiled at me in the friendliest manner—said she preferred remaining near the house, nodded pleasantly, and re-entered the hall. I looked back, and saw that she had closed the door before I had opened the wicket by the side of the carriage gates.\n\nIn less than a quarter of an hour I reached the lodge.\n\nThe lane outside took a sudden turn to the left, ran on straight for a hundred yards or so, and then took another sharp turn to the right to join the high-road. Between these two turns, hidden from the lodge on one side, and from the way to the station on the other, I waited, walking backwards and forwards. High hedges were on either side of me, and for twenty minutes, by my watch, I neither saw nor heard anything. At the end of that time the sound of a carriage caught my ear, and I was met, as I advanced towards the second turning, by a fly from the railway. I made a sign to the driver to stop. As he obeyed me a respectable-looking man put his head out of the window to see what was the matter.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” I said, “but am I right in supposing that you are going to Blackwater Park?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am.”\n\n“With a letter for any one?”\n\n“With a letter for Miss Halcombe, ma’am.”\n\n“You may give me the letter. I am Miss Halcombe.”\n\nThe man touched his hat, got out of the fly immediately, and gave me the letter.\n\nI opened it at once and read these lines. I copy them here, thinking it best to destroy the original for caution’s sake.\n\n“DEAR MADAM,—Your letter received this morning has caused me very great anxiety. I will reply to it as briefly and plainly as possible.\n\n“My careful consideration of the statement made by yourself, and my knowledge of Lady Glyde’s position, as defined in the settlement, lead me, I regret to say, to the conclusion that a loan of the trust money to Sir Percival (or, in other words, a loan of some portion of the twenty thousand pounds of Lady Glyde’s fortune) is in contemplation, and that she is made a party to the deed, in order to secure her approval of a flagrant breach of trust, and to have her signature produced against her if she should complain hereafter. It is impossible, on any other supposition, to account, situated as she is, for her execution to a deed of any kind being wanted at all.\n\n“In the event of Lady Glyde’s signing such a document, as I am compelled to suppose the deed in question to be, her trustees would be at liberty to advance money to Sir Percival out of her twenty thousand pounds. If the amount so lent should not be paid back, and if Lady Glyde should have children, their fortune will then be diminished by the sum, large or small, so advanced. In plainer terms still, the transaction, for anything that Lady Glyde knows to the contrary, may be a fraud upon her unborn children.\n\n“Under these serious circumstances, I would recommend Lady Glyde to assign as a reason for withholding her signature, that she wishes the deed to be first submitted to myself, as her family solicitor (in the absence of my partner, Mr. Gilmore). No reasonable objection can be made to taking this course—for, if the transaction is an honourable one, there will necessarily be no difficulty in my giving my approval.\n\n“Sincerely assuring you of my readiness to afford any additional help or advice that may be wanted, I beg to remain, Madam, your faithful servant,\n\n“WILLIAM KYRLE.”\n\nI read this kind and sensible letter very thankfully. It supplied Laura with a reason for objecting to the signature which was unanswerable, and which we could both of us understand. The messenger waited near me while I was reading to receive his directions when I had done.\n\n“Will you be good enough to say that I understand the letter, and that I am very much obliged?” I said. “There is no other reply necessary at present.”\n\nExactly at the moment when I was speaking those words, holding the letter open in my hand, Count Fosco turned the corner of the lane from the high-road, and stood before me as if he had sprung up out of the earth.\n\nThe suddenness of his appearance, in the very last place under heaven in which I should have expected to see him, took me completely by surprise. The messenger wished me good-morning, and got into the fly again. I could not say a word to him—I was not even able to return his bow. The conviction that I was discovered—and by that man, of all others—absolutely petrified me.\n\n“Are you going back to the house, Miss Halcombe?” he inquired, without showing the least surprise on his side, and without even looking after the fly, which drove off while he was speaking to me.\n\nI collected myself sufficiently to make a sign in the affirmative.\n\n“I am going back too,” he said. “Pray allow me the pleasure of accompanying you. Will you take my arm? You look surprised at seeing me!”\n\nI took his arm. The first of my scattered senses that came back was the sense that warned me to sacrifice anything rather than make an enemy of him.\n\n“You look surprised at seeing me!” he repeated in his quietly pertinacious way.\n\n“I thought, Count, I heard you with your birds in the breakfast-room,” I answered, as quietly and firmly as I could.\n\n“Surely. But my little feathered children, dear lady, are only too like other children. They have their days of perversity, and this morning was one of them. My wife came in as I was putting them back in their cage, and said she had left you going out alone for a walk. You told her so, did you not?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Well, Miss Halcombe, the pleasure of accompanying you was too great a temptation for me to resist. At my age there is no harm in confessing so much as that, is there? I seized my hat, and set off to offer myself as your escort. Even so fat an old man as Fosco is surely better than no escort at all? I took the wrong path—I came back in despair, and here I am, arrived (may I say it?) at the height of my wishes.”\n\nHe talked on in this complimentary strain with a fluency which left me no exertion to make beyond the effort of maintaining my composure. He never referred in the most distant manner to what he had seen in the lane, or to the letter which I still had in my hand. This ominous discretion helped to convince me that he must have surprised, by the most dishonourable means, the secret of my application in Laura’s interest to the lawyer; and that, having now assured himself of the private manner in which I had received the answer, he had discovered enough to suit his purposes, and was only bent on trying to quiet the suspicions which he knew he must have aroused in my mind. I was wise enough, under these circumstances, not to attempt to deceive him by plausible explanations, and woman enough, notwithstanding my dread of him, to feel as if my hand was tainted by resting on his arm.\n\nOn the drive in front of the house we met the dog-cart being taken round to the stables. Sir Percival had just returned. He came out to meet us at the house-door. Whatever other results his journey might have had, it had not ended in softening his savage temper.\n\n“Oh! here are two of you come back,” he said, with a lowering face. “What is the meaning of the house being deserted in this way? Where is Lady Glyde?”\n\nI told him of the loss of the brooch, and said that Laura had gone into the plantation to look for it.\n\n“Brooch or no brooch,” he growled sulkily, “I recommend her not to forget her appointment in the library this afternoon. I shall expect to see her in half an hour.”\n\nI took my hand from the Count’s arm, and slowly ascended the steps. He honoured me with one of his magnificent bows, and then addressed himself gaily to the scowling master of the house.\n\n“Tell me, Percival,” he said, “have you had a pleasant drive? And has your pretty shining Brown Molly come back at all tired?”\n\n“Brown Molly be hanged—and the drive too! I want my lunch.”\n\n“And I want five minutes’ talk with you, Percival, first,” returned the Count. “Five minutes’ talk, my friend, here on the grass.”\n\n“What about?”\n\n“About business that very much concerns you.”\n\nI lingered long enough in passing through the hall-door to hear this question and answer, and to see Sir Percival thrust his hands into his pockets in sullen hesitation.\n\n“If you want to badger me with any more of your infernal scruples,” he said, “I for one won’t hear them. I want my lunch.”\n\n“Come out here and speak to me,” repeated the Count, still perfectly uninfluenced by the rudest speech that his friend could make to him.\n\nSir Percival descended the steps. The Count took him by the arm, and walked him away gently. The “business,” I was sure, referred to the question of the signature. They were speaking of Laura and of me beyond a doubt. I felt heart-sick and faint with anxiety. It might be of the last importance to both of us to know what they were saying to each other at that moment, and not one word of it could by any possibility reach my ears.\n\nI walked about the house, from room to room, with the lawyer’s letter in my bosom (I was afraid by this time even to trust it under lock and key), till the oppression of my suspense half maddened me. There were no signs of Laura’s return, and I thought of going out to look for her. But my strength was so exhausted by the trials and anxieties of the morning that the heat of the day quite overpowered me, and after an attempt to get to the door I was obliged to return to the drawing-room and lie down on the nearest sofa to recover.\n\nI was just composing myself when the door opened softly and the Count looked in.\n\n“A thousand pardons, Miss Halcombe,” he said; “I only venture to disturb you because I am the bearer of good news. Percival—who is capricious in everything, as you know—has seen fit to alter his mind at the last moment, and the business of the signature is put off for the present. A great relief to all of us, Miss Halcombe, as I see with pleasure in your face. Pray present my best respects and felicitations, when you mention this pleasant change of circumstances to Lady Glyde.”\n\nHe left me before I had recovered my astonishment. There could be no doubt that this extraordinary alteration of purpose in the matter of the signature was due to his influence, and that his discovery of my application to London yesterday, and of my having received an answer to it to-day, had offered him the means of interfering with certain success.\n\nI felt these impressions, but my mind seemed to share the exhaustion of my body, and I was in no condition to dwell on them with any useful reference to the doubtful present or the threatening future. I tried a second time to run out and find Laura, but my head was giddy and my knees trembled under me. There was no choice but to give it up again and return to the sofa, sorely against my will.\n\nThe quiet in the house, and the low murmuring hum of summer insects outside the open window, soothed me. My eyes closed of themselves, and I passed gradually into a strange condition, which was not waking—for I knew nothing of what was going on about me, and not sleeping—for I was conscious of my own repose. In this state my fevered mind broke loose from me, while my weary body was at rest, and in a trance, or day-dream of my fancy—I know not what to call it—I saw Walter Hartright. I had not thought of him since I rose that morning—Laura had not said one word to me either directly or indirectly referring to him—and yet I saw him now as plainly as if the past time had returned, and we were both together again at Limmeridge House.\n\nHe appeared to me as one among many other men, none of whose faces I could plainly discern. They were all lying on the steps of an immense ruined temple. Colossal tropical trees—with rank creepers twining endlessly about their trunks, and hideous stone idols glimmering and grinning at intervals behind leaves and stalks and branches—surrounded the temple and shut out the sky, and threw a dismal shadow over the forlorn band of men on the steps. White exhalations twisted and curled up stealthily from the ground, approached the men in wreaths like smoke, touched them, and stretched them out dead, one by one, in the places where they lay. An agony of pity and fear for Walter loosened my tongue, and I implored him to escape. “Come back, come back!” I said. “Remember your promise to HER and to ME. Come back to us before the Pestilence reaches you and lays you dead like the rest!”\n\nHe looked at me with an unearthly quiet in his face. “Wait,” he said, “I shall come back. The night when I met the lost Woman on the highway was the night which set my life apart to be the instrument of a Design that is yet unseen. Here, lost in the wilderness, or there, welcomed back in the land of my birth, I am still walking on the dark road which leads me, and you, and the sister of your love and mine, to the unknown Retribution and the inevitable End. Wait and look. The Pestilence which touches the rest will pass ME.”\n\nI saw him again. He was still in the forest, and the numbers of his lost companions had dwindled to very few. The temple was gone, and the idols were gone—and in their place the figures of dark, dwarfish men lurked murderously among the trees, with bows in their hands, and arrows fitted to the string. Once more I feared for Walter, and cried out to warn him. Once more he turned to me, with the immovable quiet in his face.\n\n“Another step,” he said, “on the dark road. Wait and look. The arrows that strike the rest will spare me.”\n\nI saw him for the third time in a wrecked ship, stranded on a wild, sandy shore. The overloaded boats were making away from him for the land, and he alone was left to sink with the ship. I cried to him to hail the hindmost boat, and to make a last effort for his life. The quiet face looked at me in return, and the unmoved voice gave me back the changeless reply. “Another step on the journey. Wait and look. The Sea which drowns the rest will spare me.”\n\nI saw him for the last time. He was kneeling by a tomb of white marble, and the shadow of a veiled woman rose out of the grave beneath and waited by his side. The unearthly quiet of his face had changed to an unearthly sorrow. But the terrible certainty of his words remained the same. “Darker and darker,” he said; “farther and farther yet. Death takes the good, the beautiful, and the young—and spares me. The Pestilence that wastes, the Arrow that strikes, the Sea that drowns, the Grave that closes over Love and Hope, are steps of my journey, and take me nearer and nearer to the End.”\n\nMy heart sank under a dread beyond words, under a grief beyond tears. The darkness closed round the pilgrim at the marble tomb—closed round the veiled woman from the grave—closed round the dreamer who looked on them. I saw and heard no more.\n\nI was aroused by a hand laid on my shoulder. It was Laura’s.\n\nShe had dropped on her knees by the side of the sofa. Her face was flushed and agitated, and her eyes met mine in a wild bewildered manner. I started the instant I saw her.\n\n“What has happened?” I asked. “What has frightened you?”\n\nShe looked round at the half-open door, put her lips close to my ear, and answered in a whisper—\n\n“Marian!—the figure at the lake—the footsteps last night—I’ve just seen her! I’ve just spoken to her!”\n\n“Who, for Heaven’s sake?”\n\n“Anne Catherick.”\n\nI was so startled by the disturbance in Laura’s face and manner, and so dismayed by the first waking impressions of my dream, that I was not fit to bear the revelation which burst upon me when that name passed her lips. I could only stand rooted to the floor, looking at her in breathless silence.\n\nShe was too much absorbed by what had happened to notice the effect which her reply had produced on me. “I have seen Anne Catherick! I have spoken to Anne Catherick!” she repeated as if I had not heard her. “Oh, Marian, I have such things to tell you! Come away—we may be interrupted here—come at once into my room.”\n\nWith those eager words she caught me by the hand, and led me through the library, to the end room on the ground floor, which had been fitted up for her own especial use. No third person, except her maid, could have any excuse for surprising us here. She pushed me in before her, locked the door, and drew the chintz curtains that hung over the inside.\n\nThe strange, stunned feeling which had taken possession of me still remained. But a growing conviction that the complications which had long threatened to gather about her, and to gather about me, had suddenly closed fast round us both, was now beginning to penetrate my mind. I could not express it in words—I could hardly even realise it dimly in my own thoughts. “Anne Catherick!” I whispered to myself, with useless, helpless reiteration—“Anne Catherick!”\n\nLaura drew me to the nearest seat, an ottoman in the middle of the room. “Look!” she said, “look here!”—and pointed to the bosom of her dress.\n\nI saw, for the first time, that the lost brooch was pinned in its place again. There was something real in the sight of it, something real in the touching of it afterwards, which seemed to steady the whirl and confusion in my thoughts, and to help me to compose myself.\n\n“Where did you find your brooch?” The first words I could say to her were the words which put that trivial question at that important moment.\n\n“SHE found it, Marian.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“On the floor of the boat-house. Oh, how shall I begin—how shall I tell you about it! She talked to me so strangely—she looked so fearfully ill—she left me so suddenly!”\n\nHer voice rose as the tumult of her recollections pressed upon her mind. The inveterate distrust which weighs, night and day, on my spirits in this house, instantly roused me to warn her—just as the sight of the brooch had roused me to question her, the moment before.\n\n“Speak low,” I said. “The window is open, and the garden path runs beneath it. Begin at the beginning, Laura. Tell me, word for word, what passed between that woman and you.”\n\n“Shall I close the window?”\n\n“No, only speak low—only remember that Anne Catherick is a dangerous subject under your husband’s roof. Where did you first see her?”\n\n“At the boat-house, Marian. I went out, as you know, to find my brooch, and I walked along the path through the plantation, looking down on the ground carefully at every step. In that way I got on, after a long time, to the boat-house, and as soon as I was inside it, I went on my knees to hunt over the floor. I was still searching with my back to the doorway, when I heard a soft, strange voice behind me say, ‘Miss Fairlie.’”\n\n“Miss Fairlie!”\n\n“Yes, my old name—the dear, familiar name that I thought I had parted from for ever. I started up—not frightened, the voice was too kind and gentle to frighten anybody—but very much surprised. There, looking at me from the doorway, stood a woman, whose face I never remembered to have seen before—”\n\n“How was she dressed?”\n\n“She had a neat, pretty white gown on, and over it a poor worn thin dark shawl. Her bonnet was of brown straw, as poor and worn as the shawl. I was struck by the difference between her gown and the rest of her dress, and she saw that I noticed it. ‘Don’t look at my bonnet and shawl,’ she said, speaking in a quick, breathless, sudden way; ‘if I mustn’t wear white, I don’t care what I wear. Look at my gown as much as you please—I’m not ashamed of that.’ Very strange, was it not? Before I could say anything to soothe her, she held out one of her hands, and I saw my brooch in it. I was so pleased and so grateful, that I went quite close to her to say what I really felt. ‘Are you thankful enough to do me one little kindness?’ she asked. ‘Yes, indeed,’ I answered, ‘any kindness in my power I shall be glad to show you.’ ‘Then let me pin your brooch on for you, now I have found it.’ Her request was so unexpected, Marian, and she made it with such extraordinary eagerness, that I drew back a step or two, not well knowing what to do. ‘Ah!’ she said, ‘your mother would have let me pin on the brooch.’ There was something in her voice and her look, as well as in her mentioning my mother in that reproachful manner, which made me ashamed of my distrust. I took her hand with the brooch in it, and put it up gently on the bosom of my dress. ‘You knew my mother?’ I said. ‘Was it very long ago? have I ever seen you before?’ Her hands were busy fastening the brooch: she stopped and pressed them against my breast. ‘You don’t remember a fine spring day at Limmeridge,’ she said, ‘and your mother walking down the path that led to the school, with a little girl on each side of her? I have had nothing else to think of since, and I remember it. You were one of the little girls, and I was the other. Pretty, clever Miss Fairlie, and poor dazed Anne Catherick were nearer to each other then than they are now!’”\n\n“Did you remember her, Laura, when she told you her name?”\n\n“Yes, I remembered your asking me about Anne Catherick at Limmeridge, and your saying that she had once been considered like me.”\n\n“What reminded you of that, Laura?”\n\n“SHE reminded me. While I was looking at her, while she was very close to me, it came over my mind suddenly that we were like each other! Her face was pale and thin and weary—but the sight of it startled me, as if it had been the sight of my own face in the glass after a long illness. The discovery—I don’t know why—gave me such a shock, that I was perfectly incapable of speaking to her for the moment.”\n\n“Did she seem hurt by your silence?”\n\n“I am afraid she was hurt by it. ‘You have not got your mother’s face,’ she said, ‘or your mother’s heart. Your mother’s face was dark, and your mother’s heart, Miss Fairlie, was the heart of an angel.’ ‘I am sure I feel kindly towards you,’ I said, ‘though I may not be able to express it as I ought. Why do you call me Miss Fairlie?——’ ‘Because I love the name of Fairlie and hate the name of Glyde,’ she broke out violently. I had seen nothing like madness in her before this, but I fancied I saw it now in her eyes. ‘I only thought you might not know I was married,’ I said, remembering the wild letter she wrote to me at Limmeridge, and trying to quiet her. She sighed bitterly, and turned away from me. ‘Not know you were married?’ she repeated. ‘I am here BECAUSE you are married. I am here to make atonement to you, before I meet your mother in the world beyond the grave.’ She drew farther and farther away from me, till she was out of the boat-house, and then she watched and listened for a little while. When she turned round to speak again, instead of coming back, she stopped where she was, looking in at me, with a hand on each side of the entrance. ‘Did you see me at the lake last night?’ she said. ‘Did you hear me following you in the wood? I have been waiting for days together to speak to you alone—I have left the only friend I have in the world, anxious and frightened about me—I have risked being shut up again in the mad-house—and all for your sake, Miss Fairlie, all for your sake.’ Her words alarmed me, Marian, and yet there was something in the way she spoke that made me pity her with all my heart. I am sure my pity must have been sincere, for it made me bold enough to ask the poor creature to come in, and sit down in the boat-house, by my side.”\n\n“Did she do so?”\n\n“No. She shook her head, and told me she must stop where she was, to watch and listen, and see that no third person surprised us. And from first to last, there she waited at the entrance, with a hand on each side of it, sometimes bending in suddenly to speak to me, sometimes drawing back suddenly to look about her. ‘I was here yesterday,’ she said, ‘before it came dark, and I heard you, and the lady with you, talking together. I heard you tell her about your husband. I heard you say you had no influence to make him believe you, and no influence to keep him silent. Ah! I knew what those words meant—my conscience told me while I was listening. Why did I ever let you marry him! Oh, my fear—my mad, miserable, wicked fear!’ She covered up her face in her poor worn shawl, and moaned and murmured to herself behind it. I began to be afraid she might break out into some terrible despair which neither she nor I could master. ‘Try to quiet yourself,’ I said; ‘try to tell me how you might have prevented my marriage.’ She took the shawl from her face, and looked at me vacantly. ‘I ought to have had heart enough to stop at Limmeridge,’ she answered. ‘I ought never to have let the news of his coming there frighten me away. I ought to have warned you and saved you before it was too late. Why did I only have courage enough to write you that letter? Why did I only do harm, when I wanted and meant to do good? Oh, my fear—my mad, miserable, wicked fear!’ She repeated those words again, and hid her face again in the end of her poor worn shawl. It was dreadful to see her, and dreadful to hear her.”\n\n“Surely, Laura, you asked what the fear was which she dwelt on so earnestly?”\n\n“Yes, I asked that.”\n\n“And what did she say?”\n\n“She asked me in return, if I should not be afraid of a man who had shut me up in a mad-house, and who would shut me up again, if he could? I said, ‘Are you afraid still? Surely you would not be here if you were afraid now?’ ‘No,’ she said, ‘I am not afraid now.’ I asked why not. She suddenly bent forward into the boat-house, and said, ‘Can’t you guess why?’ I shook my head. ‘Look at me,’ she went on. I told her I was grieved to see that she looked very sorrowful and very ill. She smiled for the first time. ‘Ill?’ she repeated; ‘I’m dying. You know why I’m not afraid of him now. Do you think I shall meet your mother in heaven? Will she forgive me if I do?’ I was so shocked and so startled, that I could make no reply. ‘I have been thinking of it,’ she went on, ‘all the time I have been in hiding from your husband, all the time I lay ill. My thoughts have driven me here—I want to make atonement—I want to undo all I can of the harm I once did.’ I begged her as earnestly as I could to tell me what she meant. She still looked at me with fixed vacant eyes. ‘SHALL I undo the harm?’ she said to herself doubtfully. ‘You have friends to take your part. If YOU know his Secret, he will be afraid of you, he won’t dare use you as he used me. He must treat you mercifully for his own sake, if he is afraid of you and your friends. And if he treats you mercifully, and if I can say it was my doing——’ I listened eagerly for more, but she stopped at those words.”\n\n“You tried to make her go on?”\n\n“I tried, but she only drew herself away from me again, and leaned her face and arms against the side of the boat-house. ‘Oh!’ I heard her say, with a dreadful, distracted tenderness in her voice, ‘oh! if I could only be buried with your mother! If I could only wake at her side, when the angel’s trumpet sounds, and the graves give up their dead at the resurrection!‘—Marian! I trembled from head to foot—it was horrible to hear her. ‘But there is no hope of that,’ she said, moving a little, so as to look at me again, ‘no hope for a poor stranger like me. I shall not rest under the marble cross that I washed with my own hands, and made so white and pure for her sake. Oh no! oh no! God’s mercy, not man’s, will take me to her, where the wicked cease from troubling and the weary are at rest.’ She spoke those words quietly and sorrowfully, with a heavy, hopeless sigh, and then waited a little. Her face was confused and troubled, she seemed to be thinking, or trying to think. ‘What was it I said just now?’ she asked after a while. ‘When your mother is in my mind, everything else goes out of it. What was I saying? what was I saying?’ I reminded the poor creature, as kindly and delicately as I could. ‘Ah, yes, yes,’ she said, still in a vacant, perplexed manner. ‘You are helpless with your wicked husband. Yes. And I must do what I have come to do here—I must make it up to you for having been afraid to speak out at a better time.’ ‘What IS it you have to tell me?’ I asked. ‘The Secret that your cruel husband is afraid of,’ she answered. ‘I once threatened him with the Secret, and frightened him. You shall threaten him with the Secret, and frighten him too.’ Her face darkened, and a hard, angry stare fixed itself in her eyes. She began waving her hand at me in a vacant, unmeaning manner. ‘My mother knows the Secret,’ she said. ‘My mother has wasted under the Secret half her lifetime. One day, when I was grown up, she said something to ME. And the next day your husband——’”\n\n“Yes! yes! Go on. What did she tell you about your husband?”\n\n“She stopped again, Marian, at that point——”\n\n“And said no more?”\n\n“And listened eagerly. ‘Hush!’ she whispered, still waving her hand at me. ‘Hush!’ She moved aside out of the doorway, moved slowly and stealthily, step by step, till I lost her past the edge of the boat-house.”\n\n“Surely you followed her?”\n\n“Yes, my anxiety made me bold enough to rise and follow her. Just as I reached the entrance, she appeared again suddenly, round the side of the boat-house. ‘The Secret,’ I whispered to her—‘wait and tell me the Secret!’ She caught hold of my arm, and looked at me with wild frightened eyes. ‘Not now,’ she said, ‘we are not alone—we are watched. Come here to-morrow at this time—by yourself—mind—by yourself.’ She pushed me roughly into the boat-house again, and I saw her no more.”\n\n“Oh, Laura, Laura, another chance lost! If I had only been near you she should not have escaped us. On which side did you lose sight of her?”\n\n“On the left side, where the ground sinks and the wood is thickest.”\n\n“Did you run out again? did you call after her?”\n\n“How could I? I was too terrified to move or speak.”\n\n“But when you DID move—when you came out?”\n\n“I ran back here, to tell you what had happened.”\n\n“Did you see any one, or hear any one, in the plantation?”\n\n“No, it seemed to be all still and quiet when I passed through it.”\n\nI waited for a moment to consider. Was this third person, supposed to have been secretly present at the interview, a reality, or the creature of Anne Catherick’s excited fancy? It was impossible to determine. The one thing certain was, that we had failed again on the very brink of discovery—failed utterly and irretrievably, unless Anne Catherick kept her appointment at the boat-house for the next day.\n\n“Are you quite sure you have told me everything that passed? Every word that was said?” I inquired.\n\n“I think so,” she answered. “My powers of memory, Marian, are not like yours. But I was so strongly impressed, so deeply interested, that nothing of any importance can possibly have escaped me.”\n\n“My dear Laura, the merest trifles are of importance where Anne Catherick is concerned. Think again. Did no chance reference escape her as to the place in which she is living at the present time?”\n\n“None that I can remember.”\n\n“Did she not mention a companion and friend—a woman named Mrs. Clements?”\n\n“Oh yes! yes! I forgot that. She told me Mrs. Clements wanted sadly to go with her to the lake and take care of her, and begged and prayed that she would not venture into this neighbourhood alone.”\n\n“Was that all she said about Mrs. Clements?”\n\n“Yes, that was all.”\n\n“She told you nothing about the place in which she took refuge after leaving Todd’s Corner?”\n\n“Nothing—I am quite sure.”\n\n“Nor where she has lived since? Nor what her illness had been?”\n\n“No, Marian, not a word. Tell me, pray tell me, what you think about it. I don’t know what to think, or what to do next.”\n\n“You must do this, my love: You must carefully keep the appointment at the boat-house to-morrow. It is impossible to say what interests may not depend on your seeing that woman again. You shall not be left to yourself a second time. I will follow you at a safe distance. Nobody shall see me, but I will keep within hearing of your voice, if anything happens. Anne Catherick has escaped Walter Hartright, and has escaped you. Whatever happens, she shall not escape ME.”\n\nLaura’s eyes read mine attentively.\n\n“You believe,” she said, “in this secret that my husband is afraid of? Suppose, Marian, it should only exist after all in Anne Catherick’s fancy? Suppose she only wanted to see me and to speak to me, for the sake of old remembrances? Her manner was so strange—I almost doubted her. Would you trust her in other things?”\n\n“I trust nothing, Laura, but my own observation of your husband’s conduct. I judge Anne Catherick’s words by his actions, and I believe there is a secret.”\n\nI said no more, and got up to leave the room. Thoughts were troubling me which I might have told her if we had spoken together longer, and which it might have been dangerous for her to know. The influence of the terrible dream from which she had awakened me hung darkly and heavily over every fresh impression which the progress of her narrative produced on my mind. I felt the ominous future coming close, chilling me with an unutterable awe, forcing on me the conviction of an unseen design in the long series of complications which had now fastened round us. I thought of Hartright—as I saw him in the body when he said farewell; as I saw him in the spirit in my dream—and I too began to doubt now whether we were not advancing blindfold to an appointed and an inevitable end.\n\nLeaving Laura to go upstairs alone, I went out to look about me in the walks near the house. The circumstances under which Anne Catherick had parted from her had made me secretly anxious to know how Count Fosco was passing the afternoon, and had rendered me secretly distrustful of the results of that solitary journey from which Sir Percival had returned but a few hours since.\n\nAfter looking for them in every direction and discovering nothing, I returned to the house, and entered the different rooms on the ground floor one after another. They were all empty. I came out again into the hall, and went upstairs to return to Laura. Madame Fosco opened her door as I passed it in my way along the passage, and I stopped to see if she could inform me of the whereabouts of her husband and Sir Percival. Yes, she had seen them both from her window more than an hour since. The Count had looked up with his customary kindness, and had mentioned with his habitual attention to her in the smallest trifles, that he and his friend were going out together for a long walk.\n\nFor a long walk! They had never yet been in each other’s company with that object in my experience of them. Sir Percival cared for no exercise but riding, and the Count (except when he was polite enough to be my escort) cared for no exercise at all.\n\nWhen I joined Laura again, I found that she had called to mind in my absence the impending question of the signature to the deed, which, in the interest of discussing her interview with Anne Catherick, we had hitherto overlooked. Her first words when I saw her expressed her surprise at the absence of the expected summons to attend Sir Percival in the library.\n\n“You may make your mind easy on that subject,” I said. “For the present, at least, neither your resolution nor mine will be exposed to any further trial. Sir Percival has altered his plans—the business of the signature is put off.”\n\n“Put off?” Laura repeated amazedly. “Who told you so?”\n\n“My authority is Count Fosco. I believe it is to his interference that we are indebted for your husband’s sudden change of purpose.”\n\n“It seems impossible, Marian. If the object of my signing was, as we suppose, to obtain money for Sir Percival that he urgently wanted, how can the matter be put off?”\n\n“I think, Laura, we have the means at hand of setting that doubt at rest. Have you forgotten the conversation that I heard between Sir Percival and the lawyer as they were crossing the hall?”\n\n“No, but I don’t remember——”\n\n“I do. There were two alternatives proposed. One was to obtain your signature to the parchment. The other was to gain time by giving bills at three months. The last resource is evidently the resource now adopted, and we may fairly hope to be relieved from our share in Sir Percival’s embarrassments for some time to come.”\n\n“Oh, Marian, it sounds too good to be true!”\n\n“Does it, my love? You complimented me on my ready memory not long since, but you seem to doubt it now. I will get my journal, and you shall see if I am right or wrong.”\n\nI went away and got the book at once.\n\nOn looking back to the entry referring to the lawyer’s visit, we found that my recollection of the two alternatives presented was accurately correct. It was almost as great a relief to my mind as to Laura’s, to find that my memory had served me, on this occasion, as faithfully as usual. In the perilous uncertainty of our present situation, it is hard to say what future interests may not depend upon the regularity of the entries in my journal, and upon the reliability of my recollection at the time when I make them.\n\nLaura’s face and manner suggested to me that this last consideration had occurred to her as well as to myself. Anyway, it is only a trifling matter, and I am almost ashamed to put it down here in writing—it seems to set the forlornness of our situation in such a miserably vivid light. We must have little indeed to depend on, when the discovery that my memory can still be trusted to serve us is hailed as if it was the discovery of a new friend!\n\nThe first bell for dinner separated us. Just as it had done ringing, Sir Percival and the Count returned from their walk. We heard the master of the house storming at the servants for being five minutes late, and the master’s guest interposing, as usual, in the interests of propriety, patience, and peace.\n\nThe evening has come and gone. No extraordinary event has happened. But I have noticed certain peculiarities in the conduct of Sir Percival and the Count, which have sent me to my bed feeling very anxious and uneasy about Anne Catherick, and about the results which to-morrow may produce.\n\nI know enough by this time, to be sure, that the aspect of Sir Percival which is the most false, and which, therefore, means the worst, is his polite aspect. That long walk with his friend had ended in improving his manners, especially towards his wife. To Laura’s secret surprise and to my secret alarm, he called her by her Christian name, asked if she had heard lately from her uncle, inquired when Mrs. Vesey was to receive her invitation to Blackwater, and showed her so many other little attentions that he almost recalled the days of his hateful courtship at Limmeridge House. This was a bad sign to begin with, and I thought it more ominous still that he should pretend after dinner to fall asleep in the drawing-room, and that his eyes should cunningly follow Laura and me when he thought we neither of us suspected him. I have never had any doubt that his sudden journey by himself took him to Welmingham to question Mrs. Catherick—but the experience of to-night has made me fear that the expedition was not undertaken in vain, and that he has got the information which he unquestionably left us to collect. If I knew where Anne Catherick was to be found, I would be up to-morrow with sunrise and warn her.\n\nWhile the aspect under which Sir Percival presented himself to-night was unhappily but too familiar to me, the aspect under which the Count appeared was, on the other hand, entirely new in my experience of him. He permitted me, this evening, to make his acquaintance, for the first time, in the character of a Man of Sentiment—of sentiment, as I believe, really felt, not assumed for the occasion.\n\nFor instance, he was quiet and subdued—his eyes and his voice expressed a restrained sensibility. He wore (as if there was some hidden connection between his showiest finery and his deepest feeling) the most magnificent waistcoat he has yet appeared in—it was made of pale sea-green silk, and delicately trimmed with fine silver braid. His voice sank into the tenderest inflections, his smile expressed a thoughtful, fatherly admiration, whenever he spoke to Laura or to me. He pressed his wife’s hand under the table when she thanked him for trifling little attentions at dinner. He took wine with her. “Your health and happiness, my angel!” he said, with fond glistening eyes. He ate little or nothing, and sighed, and said “Good Percival!” when his friend laughed at him. After dinner, he took Laura by the hand, and asked her if she would be “so sweet as to play to him.” She complied, through sheer astonishment. He sat by the piano, with his watch-chain resting in folds, like a golden serpent, on the sea-green protuberance of his waistcoat. His immense head lay languidly on one side, and he gently beat time with two of his yellow-white fingers. He highly approved of the music, and tenderly admired Laura’s manner of playing—not as poor Hartright used to praise it, with an innocent enjoyment of the sweet sounds, but with a clear, cultivated, practical knowledge of the merits of the composition, in the first place, and of the merits of the player’s touch in the second. As the evening closed in, he begged that the lovely dying light might not be profaned, just yet, by the appearance of the lamps. He came, with his horribly silent tread, to the distant window at which I was standing, to be out of his way and to avoid the very sight of him—he came to ask me to support his protest against the lamps. If any one of them could only have burnt him up at that moment, I would have gone down to the kitchen and fetched it myself.\n\n“Surely you like this modest, trembling English twilight?” he said softly. “Ah! I love it. I feel my inborn admiration of all that is noble, and great, and good, purified by the breath of heaven on an evening like this. Nature has such imperishable charms, such inextinguishable tenderness for me!—I am an old, fat man—talk which would become your lips, Miss Halcombe, sounds like a derision and a mockery on mine. It is hard to be laughed at in my moments of sentiment, as if my soul was like myself, old and overgrown. Observe, dear lady, what a light is dying on the trees! Does it penetrate your heart, as it penetrates mine?”\n\nHe paused, looked at me, and repeated the famous lines of Dante on the Evening-time, with a melody and tenderness which added a charm of their own to the matchless beauty of the poetry itself.\n\n“Bah!” he cried suddenly, as the last cadence of those noble Italian words died away on his lips; “I make an old fool of myself, and only weary you all! Let us shut up the window in our bosoms and get back to the matter-of-fact world. Percival! I sanction the admission of the lamps. Lady Glyde—Miss Halcombe—Eleanor, my good wife—which of you will indulge me with a game at dominoes?”\n\nHe addressed us all, but he looked especially at Laura.\n\nShe had learnt to feel my dread of offending him, and she accepted his proposal. It was more than I could have done at that moment. I could not have sat down at the same table with him for any consideration. His eyes seemed to reach my inmost soul through the thickening obscurity of the twilight. His voice trembled along every nerve in my body, and turned me hot and cold alternately. The mystery and terror of my dream, which had haunted me at intervals all through the evening, now oppressed my mind with an unendurable foreboding and an unutterable awe. I saw the white tomb again, and the veiled woman rising out of it by Hartright’s side. The thought of Laura welled up like a spring in the depths of my heart, and filled it with waters of bitterness, never, never known to it before. I caught her by the hand as she passed me on her way to the table, and kissed her as if that night was to part us for ever. While they were all gazing at me in astonishment, I ran out through the low window which was open before me to the ground—ran out to hide from them in the darkness, to hide even from myself.\n\nWe separated that evening later than usual. Towards midnight the summer silence was broken by the shuddering of a low, melancholy wind among the trees. We all felt the sudden chill in the atmosphere, but the Count was the first to notice the stealthy rising of the wind. He stopped while he was lighting my candle for me, and held up his hand warningly—\n\n“Listen!” he said. “There will be a change to-morrow.”\n\n# VII\n\nJune 19th.—The events of yesterday warned me to be ready, sooner or later, to meet the worst. To-day is not yet at an end, and the worst has come.\n\nJudging by the closest calculation of time that Laura and I could make, we arrived at the conclusion that Anne Catherick must have appeared at the boat-house at half-past two o’clock on the afternoon of yesterday. I accordingly arranged that Laura should just show herself at the luncheon-table to-day, and should then slip out at the first opportunity, leaving me behind to preserve appearances, and to follow her as soon as I could safely do so. This mode of proceeding, if no obstacles occurred to thwart us, would enable her to be at the boat-house before half-past two, and (when I left the table, in my turn) would take me to a safe position in the plantation before three.\n\nThe change in the weather, which last night’s wind warned us to expect, came with the morning. It was raining heavily when I got up, and it continued to rain until twelve o’clock—when the clouds dispersed, the blue sky appeared, and the sun shone again with the bright promise of a fine afternoon.\n\nMy anxiety to know how Sir Percival and the Count would occupy the early part of the day was by no means set at rest, so far as Sir Percival was concerned, by his leaving us immediately after breakfast, and going out by himself, in spite of the rain. He neither told us where he was going nor when we might expect him back. We saw him pass the breakfast-room window hastily, with his high boots and his waterproof coat on—and that was all.\n\nThe Count passed the morning quietly indoors, some part of it in the library, some part in the drawing-room, playing odds and ends of music on the piano, and humming to himself. Judging by appearances, the sentimental side of his character was persistently inclined to betray itself still. He was silent and sensitive, and ready to sigh and languish ponderously (as only fat men CAN sigh and languish) on the smallest provocation.\n\nLuncheon-time came and Sir Percival did not return. The Count took his friend’s place at the table, plaintively devoured the greater part of a fruit tart, submerged under a whole jugful of cream, and explained the full merit of the achievement to us as soon as he had done. “A taste for sweets,” he said in his softest tones and his tenderest manner, “is the innocent taste of women and children. I love to share it with them—it is another bond, dear ladies, between you and me.”\n\nLaura left the table in ten minutes’ time. I was sorely tempted to accompany her. But if we had both gone out together we must have excited suspicion, and worse still, if we allowed Anne Catherick to see Laura, accompanied by a second person who was a stranger to her, we should in all probability forfeit her confidence from that moment, never to regain it again.\n\nI waited, therefore, as patiently as I could, until the servant came in to clear the table. When I quitted the room, there were no signs, in the house or out of it, of Sir Percival’s return. I left the Count with a piece of sugar between his lips, and the vicious cockatoo scrambling up his waistcoat to get at it, while Madame Fosco, sitting opposite to her husband, watched the proceedings of his bird and himself as attentively as if she had never seen anything of the sort before in her life. On my way to the plantation I kept carefully beyond the range of view from the luncheon-room window. Nobody saw me and nobody followed me. It was then a quarter to three o’clock by my watch.\n\nOnce among the trees I walked rapidly, until I had advanced more than half-way through the plantation. At that point I slackened my pace and proceeded cautiously, but I saw no one, and heard no voices. By little and little I came within view of the back of the boat-house—stopped and listened—then went on, till I was close behind it, and must have heard any persons who were talking inside. Still the silence was unbroken—still far and near no sign of a living creature appeared anywhere.\n\nAfter skirting round by the back of the building, first on one side and then on the other, and making no discoveries, I ventured in front of it, and fairly looked in. The place was empty.\n\nI called, “Laura!”—at first softly, then louder and louder. No one answered and no one appeared. For all that I could see and hear, the only human creature in the neighbourhood of the lake and the plantation was myself.\n\nMy heart began to beat violently, but I kept my resolution, and searched, first the boat-house and then the ground in front of it, for any signs which might show me whether Laura had really reached the place or not. No mark of her presence appeared inside the building, but I found traces of her outside it, in footsteps on the sand.\n\nI detected the footsteps of two persons—large footsteps like a man’s, and small footsteps, which, by putting my own feet into them and testing their size in that manner, I felt certain were Laura’s. The ground was confusedly marked in this way just before the boat-house. Close against one side of it, under shelter of the projecting roof, I discovered a little hole in the sand—a hole artificially made, beyond a doubt. I just noticed it, and then turned away immediately to trace the footsteps as far as I could, and to follow the direction in which they might lead me.\n\nThey led me, starting from the left-hand side of the boat-house, along the edge of the trees, a distance, I should think, of between two and three hundred yards, and then the sandy ground showed no further trace of them. Feeling that the persons whose course I was tracking must necessarily have entered the plantation at this point, I entered it too. At first I could find no path, but I discovered one afterwards, just faintly traced among the trees, and followed it. It took me, for some distance, in the direction of the village, until I stopped at a point where another foot-track crossed it. The brambles grew thickly on either side of this second path. I stood looking down it, uncertain which way to take next, and while I looked I saw on one thorny branch some fragments of fringe from a woman’s shawl. A closer examination of the fringe satisfied me that it had been torn from a shawl of Laura’s, and I instantly followed the second path. It brought me out at last, to my great relief, at the back of the house. I say to my great relief, because I inferred that Laura must, for some unknown reason, have returned before me by this roundabout way. I went in by the court-yard and the offices. The first person whom I met in crossing the servants’ hall was Mrs. Michelson, the housekeeper.\n\n“Do you know,” I asked, “whether Lady Glyde has come in from her walk or not?”\n\n“My lady came in a little while ago with Sir Percival,” answered the housekeeper. “I am afraid, Miss Halcombe, something very distressing has happened.”\n\nMy heart sank within me. “You don’t mean an accident?” I said faintly.\n\n“No, no—thank God, no accident. But my lady ran upstairs to her own room in tears, and Sir Percival has ordered me to give Fanny warning to leave in an hour’s time.”\n\nFanny was Laura’s maid—a good affectionate girl who had been with her for years—the only person in the house whose fidelity and devotion we could both depend upon.\n\n“Where is Fanny?” I inquired.\n\n“In my room, Miss Halcombe. The young woman is quite overcome, and I told her to sit down and try to recover herself.”\n\nI went to Mrs. Michelson’s room, and found Fanny in a corner, with her box by her side, crying bitterly.\n\nShe could give me no explanation whatever of her sudden dismissal. Sir Percival had ordered that she should have a month’s wages, in place of a month’s warning, and go. No reason had been assigned—no objection had been made to her conduct. She had been forbidden to appeal to her mistress, forbidden even to see her for a moment to say good-bye. She was to go without explanations or farewells, and to go at once.\n\nAfter soothing the poor girl by a few friendly words, I asked where she proposed to sleep that night. She replied that she thought of going to the little inn in the village, the landlady of which was a respectable woman, known to the servants at Blackwater Park. The next morning, by leaving early, she might get back to her friends in Cumberland without stopping in London, where she was a total stranger.\n\nI felt directly that Fanny’s departure offered us a safe means of communication with London and with Limmeridge House, of which it might be very important to avail ourselves. Accordingly, I told her that she might expect to hear from her mistress or from me in the course of the evening, and that she might depend on our both doing all that lay in our power to help her, under the trial of leaving us for the present. Those words said, I shook hands with her and went upstairs.\n\nThe door which led to Laura’s room was the door of an ante-chamber opening on to the passage. When I tried it, it was bolted on the inside.\n\nI knocked, and the door was opened by the same heavy, overgrown housemaid whose lumpish insensibility had tried my patience so severely on the day when I found the wounded dog.\n\nI had, since that time, discovered that her name was Margaret Porcher, and that she was the most awkward, slatternly, and obstinate servant in the house.\n\nOn opening the door she instantly stepped out to the threshold, and stood grinning at me in stolid silence.\n\n“Why do you stand there?” I said. “Don’t you see that I want to come in?”\n\n“Ah, but you mustn’t come in,” was the answer, with another and a broader grin still.\n\n“How dare you talk to me in that way? Stand back instantly!”\n\nShe stretched out a great red hand and arm on each side of her, so as to bar the doorway, and slowly nodded her addle head at me.\n\n“Master’s orders,” she said, and nodded again.\n\nI had need of all my self-control to warn me against contesting the matter with HER, and to remind me that the next words I had to say must be addressed to her master. I turned my back on her, and instantly went downstairs to find him. My resolution to keep my temper under all the irritations that Sir Percival could offer was, by this time, as completely forgotten—I say so to my shame—as if I had never made it. It did me good, after all I had suffered and suppressed in that house—it actually did me good to feel how angry I was.\n\nThe drawing-room and the breakfast-room were both empty. I went on to the library, and there I found Sir Percival, the Count, and Madame Fosco. They were all three standing up, close together, and Sir Percival had a little slip of paper in his hand. As I opened the door I heard the Count say to him, “No—a thousand times over, no.”\n\nI walked straight up to him, and looked him full in the face.\n\n“Am I to understand, Sir Percival, that your wife’s room is a prison, and that your housemaid is the gaoler who keeps it?” I asked.\n\n“Yes, that is what you are to understand,” he answered. “Take care my gaoler hasn’t got double duty to do—take care your room is not a prison too.”\n\n“Take YOU care how you treat your wife, and how you threaten ME,” I broke out in the heat of my anger. “There are laws in England to protect women from cruelty and outrage. If you hurt a hair of Laura’s head, if you dare to interfere with my freedom, come what may, to those laws I will appeal.”\n\nInstead of answering me he turned round to the Count.\n\n“What did I tell you?” he asked. “What do you say now?”\n\n“What I said before,” replied the Count—“No.”\n\nEven in the vehemence of my anger I felt his calm, cold, grey eyes on my face. They turned away from me as soon as he had spoken, and looked significantly at his wife. Madame Fosco immediately moved close to my side, and in that position addressed Sir Percival before either of us could speak again.\n\n“Favour me with your attention for one moment,” she said, in her clear icily-suppressed tones. “I have to thank you, Sir Percival, for your hospitality, and to decline taking advantage of it any longer. I remain in no house in which ladies are treated as your wife and Miss Halcombe have been treated here to-day!”\n\nSir Percival drew back a step, and stared at her in dead silence. The declaration he had just heard—a declaration which he well knew, as I well knew, Madame Fosco would not have ventured to make without her husband’s permission—seemed to petrify him with surprise. The Count stood by, and looked at his wife with the most enthusiastic admiration.\n\n“She is sublime!” he said to himself. He approached her while he spoke, and drew her hand through his arm. “I am at your service, Eleanor,” he went on, with a quiet dignity that I had never noticed in him before. “And at Miss Halcombe’s service, if she will honour me by accepting all the assistance I can offer her.”\n\n“Damn it! what do you mean?” cried Sir Percival, as the Count quietly moved away with his wife to the door.\n\n“At other times I mean what I say, but at this time I mean what my wife says,” replied the impenetrable Italian. “We have changed places, Percival, for once, and Madame Fosco’s opinion is—mine.”\n\nSir Percival crumpled up the paper in his hand, and pushing past the Count, with another oath, stood between him and the door.\n\n“Have your own way,” he said, with baffled rage in his low, half-whispering tones. “Have your own way—and see what comes of it.” With those words he left the room.\n\nMadame Fosco glanced inquiringly at her husband. “He has gone away very suddenly,” she said. “What does it mean?”\n\n“It means that you and I together have brought the worst-tempered man in all England to his senses,” answered the Count. “It means, Miss Halcombe, that Lady Glyde is relieved from a gross indignity, and you from the repetition of an unpardonable insult. Suffer me to express my admiration of your conduct and your courage at a very trying moment.”\n\n“Sincere admiration,” suggested Madame Fosco.\n\n“Sincere admiration,” echoed the Count.\n\nI had no longer the strength of my first angry resistance to outrage and injury to support me. My heart-sick anxiety to see Laura, my sense of my own helpless ignorance of what had happened at the boat-house, pressed on me with an intolerable weight. I tried to keep up appearances by speaking to the Count and his wife in the tone which they had chosen to adopt in speaking to me, but the words failed on my lips—my breath came short and thick—my eyes looked longingly, in silence, at the door. The Count, understanding my anxiety, opened it, went out, and pulled it to after him. At the same time Sir Percival’s heavy step descended the stairs. I heard them whispering together outside, while Madame Fosco was assuring me, in her calmest and most conventional manner, that she rejoiced, for all our sakes, that Sir Percival’s conduct had not obliged her husband and herself to leave Blackwater Park. Before she had done speaking the whispering ceased, the door opened, and the Count looked in.\n\n“Miss Halcombe,” he said, “I am happy to inform you that Lady Glyde is mistress again in her own house. I thought it might be more agreeable to you to hear of this change for the better from me than from Sir Percival, and I have therefore expressly returned to mention it.”\n\n“Admirable delicacy!” said Madame Fosco, paying back her husband’s tribute of admiration with the Count’s own coin, in the Count’s own manner. He smiled and bowed as if he had received a formal compliment from a polite stranger, and drew back to let me pass out first.\n\nSir Percival was standing in the hall. As I hurried to the stairs I heard him call impatiently to the Count to come out of the library.\n\n“What are you waiting there for?” he said. “I want to speak to you.”\n\n“And I want to think a little by myself,” replied the other. “Wait till later, Percival, wait till later.”\n\nNeither he nor his friend said any more. I gained the top of the stairs and ran along the passage. In my haste and my agitation I left the door of the ante-chamber open, but I closed the door of the bedroom the moment I was inside it.\n\nLaura was sitting alone at the far end of the room, her arms resting wearily on a table, and her face hidden in her hands. She started up with a cry of delight when she saw me.\n\n“How did you get here?” she asked. “Who gave you leave? Not Sir Percival?”\n\nIn my overpowering anxiety to hear what she had to tell me, I could not answer her—I could only put questions on my side. Laura’s eagerness to know what had passed downstairs proved, however, too strong to be resisted. She persistently repeated her inquiries.\n\n“The Count, of course,” I answered impatiently. “Whose influence in the house——”\n\nShe stopped me with a gesture of disgust.\n\n“Don’t speak of him,” she cried. “The Count is the vilest creature breathing! The Count is a miserable Spy——!”\n\nBefore we could either of us say another word we were alarmed by a soft knocking at the door of the bedroom.\n\nI had not yet sat down, and I went first to see who it was. When I opened the door Madame Fosco confronted me with my handkerchief in her hand.\n\n“You dropped this downstairs, Miss Halcombe,” she said, “and I thought I could bring it to you, as I was passing by to my own room.”\n\nHer face, naturally pale, had turned to such a ghastly whiteness that I started at the sight of it. Her hands, so sure and steady at all other times, trembled violently, and her eyes looked wolfishly past me through the open door, and fixed on Laura.\n\nShe had been listening before she knocked! I saw it in her white face, I saw it in her trembling hands, I saw it in her look at Laura.\n\nAfter waiting an instant she turned from me in silence, and slowly walked away.\n\nI closed the door again. “Oh, Laura! Laura! We shall both rue the day when you called the Count a Spy!”\n\n“You would have called him so yourself, Marian, if you had known what I know. Anne Catherick was right. There was a third person watching us in the plantation yesterday, and that third person—-”\n\n“Are you sure it was the Count?”\n\n“I am absolutely certain. He was Sir Percival’s spy—he was Sir Percival’s informer—he set Sir Percival watching and waiting, all the morning through, for Anne Catherick and for me.”\n\n“Is Anne found? Did you see her at the lake?”\n\n“No. She has saved herself by keeping away from the place. When I got to the boat-house no one was there.”\n\n“Yes? Yes?”\n\n“I went in and sat waiting for a few minutes. But my restlessness made me get up again, to walk about a little. As I passed out I saw some marks on the sand, close under the front of the boat-house. I stooped down to examine them, and discovered a word written in large letters on the sand. The word was—LOOK.”\n\n“And you scraped away the sand, and dug a hollow place in it?”\n\n“How do you know that, Marian?”\n\n“I saw the hollow place myself when I followed you to the boat-house. Go on—go on!”\n\n“Yes, I scraped away the sand on the surface, and in a little while I came to a strip of paper hidden beneath, which had writing on it. The writing was signed with Anne Catherick’s initials.”\n\n“Where is it?”\n\n“Sir Percival has taken it from me.”\n\n“Can you remember what the writing was? Do you think you can repeat it to me?”\n\n“In substance I can, Marian. It was very short. You would have remembered it, word for word.”\n\n“Try to tell me what the substance was before we go any further.”\n\nShe complied. I write the lines down here exactly as she repeated them to me. They ran thus—\n\n“I was seen with you, yesterday, by a tall, stout old man, and had to run to save myself. He was not quick enough on his feet to follow me, and he lost me among the trees. I dare not risk coming back here to-day at the same time. I write this, and hide it in the sand, at six in the morning, to tell you so. When we speak next of your wicked husband’s Secret we must speak safely, or not at all. Try to have patience. I promise you shall see me again and that soon.—A. C.”\n\nThe reference to the “tall, stout old man” (the terms of which Laura was certain that she had repeated to me correctly) left no doubt as to who the intruder had been. I called to mind that I had told Sir Percival, in the Count’s presence the day before, that Laura had gone to the boat-house to look for her brooch. In all probability he had followed her there, in his officious way, to relieve her mind about the matter of the signature, immediately after he had mentioned the change in Sir Percival’s plans to me in the drawing-room. In this case he could only have got to the neighbourhood of the boat-house at the very moment when Anne Catherick discovered him. The suspiciously hurried manner in which she parted from Laura had no doubt prompted his useless attempt to follow her. Of the conversation which had previously taken place between them he could have heard nothing. The distance between the house and the lake, and the time at which he left me in the drawing-room, as compared with the time at which Laura and Anne Catherick had been speaking together, proved that fact to us at any rate, beyond a doubt.\n\nHaving arrived at something like a conclusion so far, my next great interest was to know what discoveries Sir Percival had made after Count Fosco had given him his information.\n\n“How came you to lose possession of the letter?” I asked. “What did you do with it when you found it in the sand?”\n\n“After reading it once through,” she replied, “I took it into the boat-house with me to sit down and look over it a second time. While I was reading a shadow fell across the paper. I looked up, and saw Sir Percival standing in the doorway watching me.”\n\n“Did you try to hide the letter?”\n\n“I tried, but he stopped me. ‘You needn’t trouble to hide that,’ he said. ‘I happen to have read it.’ I could only look at him helplessly—I could say nothing. ‘You understand?’ he went on; ‘I have read it. I dug it up out of the sand two hours since, and buried it again, and wrote the word above it again, and left it ready to your hands. You can’t lie yourself out of the scrape now. You saw Anne Catherick in secret yesterday, and you have got her letter in your hand at this moment. I have not caught HER yet, but I have caught YOU. Give me the letter.’ He stepped close up to me—I was alone with him, Marian—what could I do?—I gave him the letter.”\n\n“What did he say when you gave it to him?”\n\n“At first he said nothing. He took me by the arm, and led me out of the boat-house, and looked about him on all sides, as if he was afraid of our being seen or heard. Then he clasped his hand fast round my arm, and whispered to me, ‘What did Anne Catherick say to you yesterday? I insist on hearing every word, from first to last.’”\n\n“Did you tell him?”\n\n“I was alone with him, Marian—his cruel hand was bruising my arm—what could I do?”\n\n“Is the mark on your arm still? Let me see it.”\n\n“Why do you want to see it?”\n\n“I want to see it, Laura, because our endurance must end, and our resistance must begin to-day. That mark is a weapon to strike him with. Let me see it now—I may have to swear to it at some future time.”\n\n“Oh, Marian, don’t look so—don’t talk so! It doesn’t hurt me now!”\n\n“Let me see it!”\n\nShe showed me the marks. I was past grieving over them, past crying over them, past shuddering over them. They say we are either better than men, or worse. If the temptation that has fallen in some women’s way, and made them worse, had fallen in mine at that moment—Thank God! my face betrayed nothing that his wife could read. The gentle, innocent, affectionate creature thought I was frightened for her and sorry for her, and thought no more.\n\n“Don’t think too seriously of it, Marian,” she said simply, as she pulled her sleeve down again. “It doesn’t hurt me now.”\n\n“I will try to think quietly of it, my love, for your sake.—Well! well! And you told him all that Anne Catherick had said to you—all that you told me?”\n\n“Yes, all. He insisted on it—I was alone with him—I could conceal nothing.”\n\n“Did he say anything when you had done?”\n\n“He looked at me, and laughed to himself in a mocking, bitter way. ‘I mean to have the rest out of you,’ he said, ‘do you hear?—the rest.’ I declared to him solemnly that I had told him everything I knew. ‘Not you,’ he answered, ‘you know more than you choose to tell. Won’t you tell it? You shall! I’ll wring it out of you at home if I can’t wring it out of you here.’ He led me away by a strange path through the plantation—a path where there was no hope of our meeting you—and he spoke no more till we came within sight of the house. Then he stopped again, and said, ‘Will you take a second chance, if I give it to you? Will you think better of it, and tell me the rest?’ I could only repeat the same words I had spoken before. He cursed my obstinacy, and went on, and took me with him to the house. ‘You can’t deceive me,’ he said, ‘you know more than you choose to tell. I’ll have your secret out of you, and I’ll have it out of that sister of yours as well. There shall be no more plotting and whispering between you. Neither you nor she shall see each other again till you have confessed the truth. I’ll have you watched morning, noon, and night, till you confess the truth.’ He was deaf to everything I could say. He took me straight upstairs into my own room. Fanny was sitting there, doing some work for me, and he instantly ordered her out. ‘I’ll take good care YOU’RE not mixed up in the conspiracy,’ he said. ‘You shall leave this house to-day. If your mistress wants a maid, she shall have one of my choosing.’ He pushed me into the room, and locked the door on me. He set that senseless woman to watch me outside, Marian! He looked and spoke like a madman. You may hardly understand it—he did indeed.”\n\n“I do understand it, Laura. He is mad—mad with the terrors of a guilty conscience. Every word you have said makes me positively certain that when Anne Catherick left you yesterday you were on the eve of discovering a secret which might have been your vile husband’s ruin, and he thinks you HAVE discovered it. Nothing you can say or do will quiet that guilty distrust, and convince his false nature of your truth. I don’t say this, my love, to alarm you. I say it to open your eyes to your position, and to convince you of the urgent necessity of letting me act, as I best can, for your protection while the chance is our own. Count Fosco’s interference has secured me access to you to-day, but he may withdraw that interference to-morrow. Sir Percival has already dismissed Fanny because she is a quick-witted girl, and devotedly attached to you, and has chosen a woman to take her place who cares nothing for your interests, and whose dull intelligence lowers her to the level of the watch-dog in the yard. It is impossible to say what violent measures he may take next, unless we make the most of our opportunities while we have them.”\n\n“What can we do, Marian? Oh, if we could only leave this house, never to see it again!”\n\n“Listen to me, my love, and try to think that you are not quite helpless so long as I am here with you.”\n\n“I will think so—I do think so. Don’t altogether forget poor Fanny in thinking of me. She wants help and comfort too.”\n\n“I will not forget her. I saw her before I came up here, and I have arranged to communicate with her to-night. Letters are not safe in the post-bag at Blackwater Park, and I shall have two to write to-day, in your interests, which must pass through no hands but Fanny’s.”\n\n“What letters?”\n\n“I mean to write first, Laura, to Mr. Gilmore’s partner, who has offered to help us in any fresh emergency. Little as I know of the law, I am certain that it can protect a woman from such treatment as that ruffian has inflicted on you to-day. I will go into no details about Anne Catherick, because I have no certain information to give. But the lawyer shall know of those bruises on your arm, and of the violence offered to you in this room—he shall, before I rest to-night!”\n\n“But think of the exposure, Marian!”\n\n“I am calculating on the exposure. Sir Percival has more to dread from it than you have. The prospect of an exposure may bring him to terms when nothing else will.”\n\nI rose as I spoke, but Laura entreated me not to leave her. “You will drive him to desperation,” she said, “and increase our dangers tenfold.”\n\nI felt the truth—the disheartening truth—of those words. But I could not bring myself plainly to acknowledge it to her. In our dreadful position there was no help and no hope for us but in risking the worst. I said so in guarded terms. She sighed bitterly, but did not contest the matter. She only asked about the second letter that I had proposed writing. To whom was it to be addressed?\n\n“To Mr. Fairlie,” I said. “Your uncle is your nearest male relative, and the head of the family. He must and shall interfere.”\n\nLaura shook her head sorrowfully.\n\n“Yes, yes,” I went on, “your uncle is a weak, selfish, worldly man, I know, but he is not Sir Percival Glyde, and he has no such friend about him as Count Fosco. I expect nothing from his kindness or his tenderness of feeling towards you or towards me, but he will do anything to pamper his own indolence, and to secure his own quiet. Let me only persuade him that his interference at this moment will save him inevitable trouble and wretchedness and responsibility hereafter, and he will bestir himself for his own sake. I know how to deal with him, Laura—I have had some practice.”\n\n“If you could only prevail on him to let me go back to Limmeridge for a little while and stay there quietly with you, Marian, I could be almost as happy again as I was before I was married!”\n\nThose words set me thinking in a new direction. Would it be possible to place Sir Percival between the two alternatives of either exposing himself to the scandal of legal interference on his wife’s behalf, or of allowing her to be quietly separated from him for a time under pretext of a visit to her uncle’s house? And could he, in that case, be reckoned on as likely to accept the last resource? It was doubtful—more than doubtful. And yet, hopeless as the experiment seemed, surely it was worth trying. I resolved to try it in sheer despair of knowing what better to do.\n\n“Your uncle shall know the wish you have just expressed,” I said, “and I will ask the lawyer’s advice on the subject as well. Good may come of it—and will come of it, I hope.”\n\nSaying that I rose again, and again Laura tried to make me resume my seat.\n\n“Don’t leave me,” she said uneasily. “My desk is on that table. You can write here.”\n\nIt tried me to the quick to refuse her, even in her own interests. But we had been too long shut up alone together already. Our chance of seeing each other again might entirely depend on our not exciting any fresh suspicions. It was full time to show myself, quietly and unconcernedly, among the wretches who were at that very moment, perhaps, thinking of us and talking of us downstairs. I explained the miserable necessity to Laura, and prevailed on her to recognise it as I did.\n\n“I will come back again, love, in an hour or less,” I said. “The worst is over for to-day. Keep yourself quiet and fear nothing.”\n\n“Is the key in the door, Marian? Can I lock it on the inside?”\n\n“Yes, here is the key. Lock the door, and open it to nobody until I come upstairs again.”\n\nI kissed her and left her. It was a relief to me as I walked away to hear the key turned in the lock, and to know that the door was at her own command.\n\n# VIII\n\nJune 19th.—I had only got as far as the top of the stairs when the locking of Laura’s door suggested to me the precaution of also locking my own door, and keeping the key safely about me while I was out of the room. My journal was already secured with other papers in the table drawer, but my writing materials were left out. These included a seal bearing the common device of two doves drinking out of the same cup, and some sheets of blotting-paper, which had the impression on them of the closing lines of my writing in these pages traced during the past night. Distorted by the suspicion which had now become a part of myself, even such trifles as these looked too dangerous to be trusted without a guard—even the locked table drawer seemed to be not sufficiently protected in my absence until the means of access to it had been carefully secured as well.\n\nI found no appearance of any one having entered the room while I had been talking with Laura. My writing materials (which I had given the servant instructions never to meddle with) were scattered over the table much as usual. The only circumstance in connection with them that at all struck me was that the seal lay tidily in the tray with the pencils and the wax. It was not in my careless habits (I am sorry to say) to put it there, neither did I remember putting it there. But as I could not call to mind, on the other hand, where else I had thrown it down, and as I was also doubtful whether I might not for once have laid it mechanically in the right place, I abstained from adding to the perplexity with which the day’s events had filled my mind by troubling it afresh about a trifle. I locked the door, put the key in my pocket, and went downstairs.\n\nMadame Fosco was alone in the hall looking at the weather-glass.\n\n“Still falling,” she said. “I am afraid we must expect more rain.”\n\nHer face was composed again to its customary expression and its customary colour. But the hand with which she pointed to the dial of the weather-glass still trembled.\n\nCould she have told her husband already that she had overheard Laura reviling him, in my company, as a “spy?” My strong suspicion that she must have told him, my irresistible dread (all the more overpowering from its very vagueness) of the consequences which might follow, my fixed conviction, derived from various little self-betrayals which women notice in each other, that Madame Fosco, in spite of her well-assumed external civility, had not forgiven her niece for innocently standing between her and the legacy of ten thousand pounds—all rushed upon my mind together, all impelled me to speak in the vain hope of using my own influence and my own powers of persuasion for the atonement of Laura’s offence.\n\n“May I trust to your kindness to excuse me, Madame Fosco, if I venture to speak to you on an exceedingly painful subject?”\n\nShe crossed her hands in front of her and bowed her head solemnly, without uttering a word, and without taking her eyes off mine for a moment.\n\n“When you were so good as to bring me back my handkerchief,” I went on, “I am very, very much afraid you must have accidentally heard Laura say something which I am unwilling to repeat, and which I will not attempt to defend. I will only venture to hope that you have not thought it of sufficient importance to be mentioned to the Count?”\n\n“I think it of no importance whatever,” said Madame Fosco sharply and suddenly. “But,” she added, resuming her icy manner in a moment, “I have no secrets from my husband even in trifles. When he noticed just now that I looked distressed, it was my painful duty to tell him why I was distressed, and I frankly acknowledge to you, Miss Halcombe, that I HAVE told him.”\n\nI was prepared to hear it, and yet she turned me cold all over when she said those words.\n\n“Let me earnestly entreat you, Madame Fosco—let me earnestly entreat the Count—to make some allowances for the sad position in which my sister is placed. She spoke while she was smarting under the insult and injustice inflicted on her by her husband, and she was not herself when she said those rash words. May I hope that they will be considerately and generously forgiven?”\n\n“Most assuredly,” said the Count’s quiet voice behind me. He had stolen on us with his noiseless tread and his book in his hand from the library.\n\n“When Lady Glyde said those hasty words,” he went on, “she did me an injustice which I lament—and forgive. Let us never return to the subject, Miss Halcombe; let us all comfortably combine to forget it from this moment.”\n\n“You are very kind,” I said, “you relieve me inexpressibly.”\n\nI tried to continue, but his eyes were on me; his deadly smile that hides everything was set, hard, and unwavering on his broad, smooth face. My distrust of his unfathomable falseness, my sense of my own degradation in stooping to conciliate his wife and himself, so disturbed and confused me, that the next words failed on my lips, and I stood there in silence.\n\n“I beg you on my knees to say no more, Miss Halcombe—I am truly shocked that you should have thought it necessary to say so much.” With that polite speech he took my hand—oh, how I despise myself! oh, how little comfort there is even in knowing that I submitted to it for Laura’s sake!—he took my hand and put it to his poisonous lips. Never did I know all my horror of him till then. That innocent familiarity turned my blood as if it had been the vilest insult that a man could offer me. Yet I hid my disgust from him—I tried to smile—I, who once mercilessly despised deceit in other women, was as false as the worst of them, as false as the Judas whose lips had touched my hand.\n\nI could not have maintained my degrading self-control—it is all that redeems me in my own estimation to know that I could not—if he had still continued to keep his eyes on my face. His wife’s tigerish jealousy came to my rescue and forced his attention away from me the moment he possessed himself of my hand. Her cold blue eyes caught light, her dull white cheeks flushed into bright colour, she looked years younger than her age in an instant.\n\n“Count!” she said. “Your foreign forms of politeness are not understood by Englishwomen.”\n\n“Pardon me, my angel! The best and dearest Englishwoman in the world understands them.” With those words he dropped my hand and quietly raised his wife’s hand to his lips in place of it.\n\nI ran back up the stairs to take refuge in my own room. If there had been time to think, my thoughts, when I was alone again, would have caused me bitter suffering. But there was no time to think. Happily for the preservation of my calmness and my courage there was time for nothing but action.\n\nThe letters to the lawyer and to Mr. Fairlie were still to be written, and I sat down at once without a moment’s hesitation to devote myself to them.\n\nThere was no multitude of resources to perplex me—there was absolutely no one to depend on, in the first instance, but myself. Sir Percival had neither friends nor relatives in the neighbourhood whose intercession I could attempt to employ. He was on the coldest terms—in some cases on the worst terms—with the families of his own rank and station who lived near him. We two women had neither father nor brother to come to the house and take our parts. There was no choice but to write those two doubtful letters, or to put Laura in the wrong and myself in the wrong, and to make all peaceable negotiation in the future impossible by secretly escaping from Blackwater Park. Nothing but the most imminent personal peril could justify our taking that second course. The letters must be tried first, and I wrote them.\n\nI said nothing to the lawyer about Anne Catherick, because (as I had already hinted to Laura) that topic was connected with a mystery which we could not yet explain, and which it would therefore be useless to write about to a professional man. I left my correspondent to attribute Sir Percival’s disgraceful conduct, if he pleased, to fresh disputes about money matters, and simply consulted him on the possibility of taking legal proceedings for Laura’s protection in the event of her husband’s refusal to allow her to leave Blackwater Park for a time and return with me to Limmeridge. I referred him to Mr. Fairlie for the details of this last arrangement—I assured him that I wrote with Laura’s authority—and I ended by entreating him to act in her name to the utmost extent of his power and with the least possible loss of time.\n\nThe letter to Mr. Fairlie occupied me next. I appealed to him on the terms which I had mentioned to Laura as the most likely to make him bestir himself; I enclosed a copy of my letter to the lawyer to show him how serious the case was, and I represented our removal to Limmeridge as the only compromise which would prevent the danger and distress of Laura’s present position from inevitably affecting her uncle as well as herself at no very distant time.\n\nWhen I had done, and had sealed and directed the two envelopes, I went back with the letters to Laura’s room, to show her that they were written.\n\n“Has anybody disturbed you?” I asked, when she opened the door to me.\n\n“Nobody has knocked,” she replied. “But I heard some one in the outer room.”\n\n“Was it a man or a woman?”\n\n“A woman. I heard the rustling of her gown.”\n\n“A rustling like silk?”\n\n“Yes, like silk.”\n\nMadame Fosco had evidently been watching outside. The mischief she might do by herself was little to be feared. But the mischief she might do, as a willing instrument in her husband’s hands, was too formidable to be overlooked.\n\n“What became of the rustling of the gown when you no longer heard it in the ante-room?” I inquired. “Did you hear it go past your wall, along the passage?”\n\n“Yes. I kept still and listened, and just heard it.”\n\n“Which way did it go?”\n\n“Towards your room.”\n\nI considered again. The sound had not caught my ears. But I was then deeply absorbed in my letters, and I write with a heavy hand and a quill pen, scraping and scratching noisily over the paper. It was more likely that Madame Fosco would hear the scraping of my pen than that I should hear the rustling of her dress. Another reason (if I had wanted one) for not trusting my letters to the post-bag in the hall.\n\nLaura saw me thinking. “More difficulties!” she said wearily; “more difficulties and more dangers!”\n\n“No dangers,” I replied. “Some little difficulty, perhaps. I am thinking of the safest way of putting my two letters into Fanny’s hands.”\n\n“You have really written them, then? Oh, Marian, run no risks—pray, pray run no risks!”\n\n“No, no—no fear. Let me see—what o’clock is it now?”\n\nIt was a quarter to six. There would be time for me to get to the village inn, and to come back again before dinner. If I waited till the evening I might find no second opportunity of safely leaving the house.\n\n“Keep the key turned in the lock, Laura,” I said, “and don’t be afraid about me. If you hear any inquiries made, call through the door, and say that I am gone out for a walk.”\n\n“When shall you be back?”\n\n“Before dinner, without fail. Courage, my love. By this time to-morrow you will have a clear-headed, trustworthy man acting for your good. Mr. Gilmore’s partner is our next best friend to Mr. Gilmore himself.”\n\nA moment’s reflection, as soon as I was alone, convinced me that I had better not appear in my walking-dress until I had first discovered what was going on in the lower part of the house. I had not ascertained yet whether Sir Percival was indoors or out.\n\nThe singing of the canaries in the library, and the smell of tobacco-smoke that came through the door, which was not closed, told me at once where the Count was. I looked over my shoulder as I passed the doorway, and saw to my surprise that he was exhibiting the docility of the birds in his most engagingly polite manner to the housekeeper. He must have specially invited her to see them—for she would never have thought of going into the library of her own accord. The man’s slightest actions had a purpose of some kind at the bottom of every one of them. What could be his purpose here?\n\nIt was no time then to inquire into his motives. I looked about for Madame Fosco next, and found her following her favourite circle round and round the fish-pond.\n\nI was a little doubtful how she would meet me, after the outbreak of jealousy of which I had been the cause so short a time since. But her husband had tamed her in the interval, and she now spoke to me with the same civility as usual. My only object in addressing myself to her was to ascertain if she knew what had become of Sir Percival. I contrived to refer to him indirectly, and after a little fencing on either side she at last mentioned that he had gone out.\n\n“Which of the horses has he taken?” I asked carelessly.\n\n“None of them,” she replied. “He went away two hours since on foot. As I understood it, his object was to make fresh inquiries about the woman named Anne Catherick. He appears to be unreasonably anxious about tracing her. Do you happen to know if she is dangerously mad, Miss Halcombe?”\n\n“I do not, Countess.”\n\n“Are you going in?”\n\n“Yes, I think so. I suppose it will soon be time to dress for dinner.”\n\nWe entered the house together. Madame Fosco strolled into the library, and closed the door. I went at once to fetch my hat and shawl. Every moment was of importance, if I was to get to Fanny at the inn and be back before dinner.\n\nWhen I crossed the hall again no one was there, and the singing of the birds in the library had ceased. I could not stop to make any fresh investigations. I could only assure myself that the way was clear, and then leave the house with the two letters safe in my pocket.\n\nOn my way to the village I prepared myself for the possibility of meeting Sir Percival. As long as I had him to deal with alone I felt certain of not losing my presence of mind. Any woman who is sure of her own wits is a match at any time for a man who is not sure of his own temper. I had no such fear of Sir Percival as I had of the Count. Instead of fluttering, it had composed me, to hear of the errand on which he had gone out. While the tracing of Anne Catherick was the great anxiety that occupied him, Laura and I might hope for some cessation of any active persecution at his hands. For our sakes now, as well as for Anne’s, I hoped and prayed fervently that she might still escape him.\n\nI walked on as briskly as the heat would let me till I reached the cross-road which led to the village, looking back from time to time to make sure that I was not followed by any one.\n\nNothing was behind me all the way but an empty country waggon. The noise made by the lumbering wheels annoyed me, and when I found that the waggon took the road to the village, as well as myself, I stopped to let it go by and pass out of hearing. As I looked toward it, more attentively than before, I thought I detected at intervals the feet of a man walking close behind it, the carter being in front, by the side of his horses. The part of the cross-road which I had just passed over was so narrow that the waggon coming after me brushed the trees and thickets on either side, and I had to wait until it went by before I could test the correctness of my impression. Apparently that impression was wrong, for when the waggon had passed me the road behind it was quite clear.\n\nI reached the inn without meeting Sir Percival, and without noticing anything more, and was glad to find that the landlady had received Fanny with all possible kindness. The girl had a little parlour to sit in, away from the noise of the taproom, and a clean bedchamber at the top of the house. She began crying again at the sight of me, and said, poor soul, truly enough, that it was dreadful to feel herself turned out into the world as if she had committed some unpardonable fault, when no blame could be laid at her door by anybody—not even by her master, who had sent her away.\n\n“Try to make the best of it, Fanny,” I said. “Your mistress and I will stand your friends, and will take care that your character shall not suffer. Now, listen to me. I have very little time to spare, and I am going to put a great trust in your hands. I wish you to take care of these two letters. The one with the stamp on it you are to put into the post when you reach London to-morrow. The other, directed to Mr. Fairlie, you are to deliver to him yourself as soon as you get home. Keep both the letters about you and give them up to no one. They are of the last importance to your mistress’s interests.”\n\nFanny put the letters into the bosom of her dress. “There they shall stop, miss,” she said, “till I have done what you tell me.”\n\n“Mind you are at the station in good time to-morrow morning,” I continued. “And when you see the housekeeper at Limmeridge give her my compliments, and say that you are in my service until Lady Glyde is able to take you back. We may meet again sooner than you think. So keep a good heart, and don’t miss the seven o’clock train.”\n\n“Thank you, miss—thank you kindly. It gives one courage to hear your voice again. Please to offer my duty to my lady, and say I left all the things as tidy as I could in the time. Oh, dear! dear! who will dress her for dinner to-day? It really breaks my heart, miss, to think of it.”\n\nWhen I got back to the house I had only a quarter of an hour to spare to put myself in order for dinner, and to say two words to Laura before I went downstairs.\n\n“The letters are in Fanny’s hands,” I whispered to her at the door. “Do you mean to join us at dinner?”\n\n“Oh, no, no—not for the world.”\n\n“Has anything happened? Has any one disturbed you?”\n\n“Yes—just now—Sir Percival——”\n\n“Did he come in?”\n\n“No, he frightened me by a thump on the door outside. I said, ‘Who’s there?’ ‘You know,’ he answered. ‘Will you alter your mind, and tell me the rest? You shall! Sooner or later I’ll wring it out of you. You know where Anne Catherick is at this moment.’ ‘Indeed, indeed,’ I said, ‘I don’t.’ ‘You do!’ he called back. ‘I’ll crush your obstinacy—mind that!—I’ll wring it out of you!’ He went away with those words—went away, Marian, hardly five minutes ago.”\n\nHe had not found Anne! We were safe for that night—he had not found her yet.\n\n“You are going downstairs, Marian? Come up again in the evening.”\n\n“Yes, yes. Don’t be uneasy if I am a little late—I must be careful not to give offence by leaving them too soon.”\n\nThe dinner-bell rang and I hastened away.\n\nSir Percival took Madame Fosco into the dining-room, and the Count gave me his arm. He was hot and flushed, and was not dressed with his customary care and completeness. Had he, too, been out before dinner, and been late in getting back? or was he only suffering from the heat a little more severely than usual?\n\nHowever this might be, he was unquestionably troubled by some secret annoyance or anxiety, which, with all his powers of deception, he was not able entirely to conceal. Through the whole of dinner he was almost as silent as Sir Percival himself, and he, every now and then, looked at his wife with an expression of furtive uneasiness which was quite new in my experience of him. The one social obligation which he seemed to be self-possessed enough to perform as carefully as ever was the obligation of being persistently civil and attentive to me. What vile object he has in view I cannot still discover, but be the design what it may, invariable politeness towards myself, invariable humility towards Laura, and invariable suppression (at any cost) of Sir Percival’s clumsy violence, have been the means he has resolutely and impenetrably used to get to his end ever since he set foot in this house. I suspected it when he first interfered in our favour, on the day when the deed was produced in the library, and I feel certain of it now.\n\nWhen Madame Fosco and I rose to leave the table, the Count rose also to accompany us back to the drawing-room.\n\n“What are you going away for?” asked Sir Percival—“I mean YOU, Fosco.”\n\n“I am going away because I have had dinner enough, and wine enough,” answered the Count. “Be so kind, Percival, as to make allowances for my foreign habit of going out with the ladies, as well as coming in with them.”\n\n“Nonsense! Another glass of claret won’t hurt you. Sit down again like an Englishman. I want half an hour’s quiet talk with you over our wine.”\n\n“A quiet talk, Percival, with all my heart, but not now, and not over the wine. Later in the evening, if you please—later in the evening.”\n\n“Civil!” said Sir Percival savagely. “Civil behaviour, upon my soul, to a man in his own house!”\n\nI had more than once seen him look at the Count uneasily during dinner-time, and had observed that the Count carefully abstained from looking at him in return. This circumstance, coupled with the host’s anxiety for a little quiet talk over the wine, and the guest’s obstinate resolution not to sit down again at the table, revived in my memory the request which Sir Percival had vainly addressed to his friend earlier in the day to come out of the library and speak to him. The Count had deferred granting that private interview, when it was first asked for in the afternoon, and had again deferred granting it, when it was a second time asked for at the dinner-table. Whatever the coming subject of discussion between them might be, it was clearly an important subject in Sir Percival’s estimation—and perhaps (judging from his evident reluctance to approach it) a dangerous subject as well, in the estimation of the Count.\n\nThese considerations occurred to me while we were passing from the dining-room to the drawing-room. Sir Percival’s angry commentary on his friend’s desertion of him had not produced the slightest effect. The Count obstinately accompanied us to the tea-table—waited a minute or two in the room—went out into the hall—and returned with the post-bag in his hands. It was then eight o’clock—the hour at which the letters were always despatched from Blackwater Park.\n\n“Have you any letter for the post, Miss Halcombe?” he asked, approaching me with the bag.\n\nI saw Madame Fosco, who was making the tea, pause, with the sugar-tongs in her hand, to listen for my answer.\n\n“No, Count, thank you. No letters to-day.”\n\nHe gave the bag to the servant, who was then in the room; sat down at the piano, and played the air of the lively Neapolitan street-song, “La mia Carolina,” twice over. His wife, who was usually the most deliberate of women in all her movements, made the tea as quickly as I could have made it myself—finished her own cup in two minutes, and quietly glided out of the room.\n\nI rose to follow her example—partly because I suspected her of attempting some treachery upstairs with Laura, partly because I was resolved not to remain alone in the same room with her husband.\n\nBefore I could get to the door the Count stopped me, by a request for a cup of tea. I gave him the cup of tea, and tried a second time to get away. He stopped me again—this time by going back to the piano, and suddenly appealing to me on a musical question in which he declared that the honour of his country was concerned.\n\nI vainly pleaded my own total ignorance of music, and total want of taste in that direction. He only appealed to me again with a vehemence which set all further protest on my part at defiance. “The English and the Germans (he indignantly declared) were always reviling the Italians for their inability to cultivate the higher kinds of music. We were perpetually talking of our Oratorios, and they were perpetually talking of their Symphonies. Did we forget and did they forget his immortal friend and countryman, Rossini? What was Moses in Egypt but a sublime oratorio, which was acted on the stage instead of being coldly sung in a concert-room? What was the overture to Guillaume Tell but a symphony under another name? Had I heard Moses in Egypt? Would I listen to this, and this, and this, and say if anything more sublimely sacred and grand had ever been composed by mortal man?”—And without waiting for a word of assent or dissent on my part, looking me hard in the face all the time, he began thundering on the piano, and singing to it with loud and lofty enthusiasm—only interrupting himself, at intervals, to announce to me fiercely the titles of the different pieces of music: “Chorus of Egyptians in the Plague of Darkness, Miss Halcombe!”—“Recitativo of Moses with the tables of the Law.”—“Prayer of Israelites, at the passage of the Red Sea. Aha! Aha! Is that sacred? is that sublime?” The piano trembled under his powerful hands, and the teacups on the table rattled, as his big bass voice thundered out the notes, and his heavy foot beat time on the floor.\n\nThere was something horrible—something fierce and devilish—in the outburst of his delight at his own singing and playing, and in the triumph with which he watched its effect upon me as I shrank nearer and nearer to the door. I was released at last, not by my own efforts, but by Sir Percival’s interposition. He opened the dining-room door, and called out angrily to know what “that infernal noise” meant. The Count instantly got up from the piano. “Ah! if Percival is coming,” he said, “harmony and melody are both at an end. The Muse of Music, Miss Halcombe, deserts us in dismay, and I, the fat old minstrel, exhale the rest of my enthusiasm in the open air!” He stalked out into the verandah, put his hands in his pockets, and resumed the “Recitativo of Moses”, sotto voce, in the garden.\n\nI heard Sir Percival call after him from the dining-room window. But he took no notice—he seemed determined not to hear. That long-deferred quiet talk between them was still to be put off, was still to wait for the Count’s absolute will and pleasure.\n\nHe had detained me in the drawing-room nearly half an hour from the time when his wife left us. Where had she been, and what had she been doing in that interval?\n\nI went upstairs to ascertain, but I made no discoveries, and when I questioned Laura, I found that she had not heard anything. Nobody had disturbed her, no faint rustling of the silk dress had been audible, either in the ante-room or in the passage.\n\nIt was then twenty minutes to nine. After going to my room to get my journal, I returned, and sat with Laura, sometimes writing, sometimes stopping to talk with her. Nobody came near us, and nothing happened. We remained together till ten o’clock. I then rose, said my last cheering words, and wished her good-night. She locked her door again after we had arranged that I should come in and see her the first thing in the morning.\n\nI had a few sentences more to add to my diary before going to bed myself, and as I went down again to the drawing-room after leaving Laura for the last time that weary day, I resolved merely to show myself there, to make my excuses, and then to retire an hour earlier than usual for the night.\n\nSir Percival, and the Count and his wife, were sitting together. Sir Percival was yawning in an easy-chair, the Count was reading, Madame Fosco was fanning herself. Strange to say, HER face was flushed now. She, who never suffered from the heat, was most undoubtedly suffering from it to-night.\n\n“I am afraid, Countess, you are not quite so well as usual?” I said.\n\n“The very remark I was about to make to you,” she replied. “You are looking pale, my dear.”\n\nMy dear! It was the first time she had ever addressed me with that familiarity! There was an insolent smile too on her face when she said the words.\n\n“I am suffering from one of my bad headaches,” I answered coldly.\n\n“Ah, indeed? Want of exercise, I suppose? A walk before dinner would have been just the thing for you.” She referred to the “walk” with a strange emphasis. Had she seen me go out? No matter if she had. The letters were safe now in Fanny’s hands.\n\n“Come and have a smoke, Fosco,” said Sir Percival, rising, with another uneasy look at his friend.\n\n“With pleasure, Percival, when the ladies have gone to bed,” replied the Count.\n\n“Excuse me, Countess, if I set you the example of retiring,” I said. “The only remedy for such a headache as mine is going to bed.”\n\nI took my leave. There was the same insolent smile on the woman’s face when I shook hands with her. Sir Percival paid no attention to me. He was looking impatiently at Madame Fosco, who showed no signs of leaving the room with me. The Count smiled to himself behind his book. There was yet another delay to that quiet talk with Sir Percival—and the Countess was the impediment this time.\n\n# IX\n\nJune 19th.—Once safely shut into my own room, I opened these pages, and prepared to go on with that part of the day’s record which was still left to write.\n\nFor ten minutes or more I sat idle, with the pen in my hand, thinking over the events of the last twelve hours. When I at last addressed myself to my task, I found a difficulty in proceeding with it which I had never experienced before. In spite of my efforts to fix my thoughts on the matter in hand, they wandered away with the strangest persistency in the one direction of Sir Percival and the Count, and all the interest which I tried to concentrate on my journal centred instead in that private interview between them which had been put off all through the day, and which was now to take place in the silence and solitude of the night.\n\nIn this perverse state of my mind, the recollection of what had passed since the morning would not come back to me, and there was no resource but to close my journal and to get away from it for a little while.\n\nI opened the door which led from my bedroom into my sitting-room, and having passed through, pulled it to again, to prevent any accident in case of draught with the candle left on the dressing-table. My sitting-room window was wide open, and I leaned out listlessly to look at the night.\n\nIt was dark and quiet. Neither moon nor stars were visible. There was a smell like rain in the still, heavy air, and I put my hand out of window. No. The rain was only threatening, it had not come yet.\n\nI remained leaning on the window-sill for nearly a quarter of an hour, looking out absently into the black darkness, and hearing nothing, except now and then the voices of the servants, or the distant sound of a closing door, in the lower part of the house.\n\nJust as I was turning away wearily from the window to go back to the bedroom and make a second attempt to complete the unfinished entry in my journal, I smelt the odour of tobacco-smoke stealing towards me on the heavy night air. The next moment I saw a tiny red spark advancing from the farther end of the house in the pitch darkness. I heard no footsteps, and I could see nothing but the spark. It travelled along in the night, passed the window at which I was standing, and stopped opposite my bedroom window, inside which I had left the light burning on the dressing-table.\n\nThe spark remained stationary for a moment, then moved back again in the direction from which it had advanced. As I followed its progress I saw a second red spark, larger than the first, approaching from the distance. The two met together in the darkness. Remembering who smoked cigarettes and who smoked cigars, I inferred immediately that the Count had come out first to look and listen under my window, and that Sir Percival had afterwards joined him. They must both have been walking on the lawn—or I should certainly have heard Sir Percival’s heavy footfall, though the Count’s soft step might have escaped me, even on the gravel walk.\n\nI waited quietly at the window, certain that they could neither of them see me in the darkness of the room.\n\n“What’s the matter?” I heard Sir Percival say in a low voice. “Why don’t you come in and sit down?”\n\n“I want to see the light out of that window,” replied the Count softly.\n\n“What harm does the light do?”\n\n“It shows she is not in bed yet. She is sharp enough to suspect something, and bold enough to come downstairs and listen, if she can get the chance. Patience, Percival—patience.”\n\n“Humbug! You’re always talking of patience.”\n\n“I shall talk of something else presently. My good friend, you are on the edge of your domestic precipice, and if I let you give the women one other chance, on my sacred word of honour they will push you over it!”\n\n“What the devil do you mean?”\n\n“We will come to our explanations, Percival, when the light is out of that window, and when I have had one little look at the rooms on each side of the library, and a peep at the staircase as well.”\n\nThey slowly moved away, and the rest of the conversation between them (which had been conducted throughout in the same low tones) ceased to be audible. It was no matter. I had heard enough to determine me on justifying the Count’s opinion of my sharpness and my courage. Before the red sparks were out of sight in the darkness I had made up my mind that there should be a listener when those two men sat down to their talk—and that the listener, in spite of all the Count’s precautions to the contrary, should be myself. I wanted but one motive to sanction the act to my own conscience, and to give me courage enough for performing it—and that motive I had. Laura’s honour, Laura’s happiness—Laura’s life itself—might depend on my quick ears and my faithful memory to-night.\n\nI had heard the Count say that he meant to examine the rooms on each side of the library, and the staircase as well, before he entered on any explanation with Sir Percival. This expression of his intentions was necessarily sufficient to inform me that the library was the room in which he proposed that the conversation should take place. The one moment of time which was long enough to bring me to that conclusion was also the moment which showed me a means of baffling his precautions—or, in other words, of hearing what he and Sir Percival said to each other, without the risk of descending at all into the lower regions of the house.\n\nIn speaking of the rooms on the ground floor I have mentioned incidentally the verandah outside them, on which they all opened by means of French windows, extending from the cornice to the floor. The top of this verandah was flat, the rain-water being carried off from it by pipes into tanks which helped to supply the house. On the narrow leaden roof, which ran along past the bedrooms, and which was rather less, I should think, than three feet below the sills of the window, a row of flower-pots was ranged, with wide intervals between each pot—the whole being protected from falling in high winds by an ornamental iron railing along the edge of the roof.\n\nThe plan which had now occurred to me was to get out at my sitting-room window on to this roof, to creep along noiselessly till I reached that part of it which was immediately over the library window, and to crouch down between the flower-pots, with my ear against the outer railing. If Sir Percival and the Count sat and smoked to-night, as I had seen them sitting and smoking many nights before, with their chairs close at the open window, and their feet stretched on the zinc garden seats which were placed under the verandah, every word they said to each other above a whisper (and no long conversation, as we all know by experience, can be carried on IN a whisper) must inevitably reach my ears. If, on the other hand, they chose to-night to sit far back inside the room, then the chances were that I should hear little or nothing—and in that case, I must run the far more serious risk of trying to outwit them downstairs.\n\nStrongly as I was fortified in my resolution by the desperate nature of our situation, I hoped most fervently that I might escape this last emergency. My courage was only a woman’s courage after all, and it was very near to failing me when I thought of trusting myself on the ground floor, at the dead of night, within reach of Sir Percival and the Count.\n\nI went softly back to my bedroom to try the safer experiment of the verandah roof first.\n\nA complete change in my dress was imperatively necessary for many reasons. I took off my silk gown to begin with, because the slightest noise from it on that still night might have betrayed me. I next removed the white and cumbersome parts of my underclothing, and replaced them by a petticoat of dark flannel. Over this I put my black travelling cloak, and pulled the hood on to my head. In my ordinary evening costume I took up the room of three men at least. In my present dress, when it was held close about me, no man could have passed through the narrowest spaces more easily than I. The little breadth left on the roof of the verandah, between the flower-pots on one side and the wall and the windows of the house on the other, made this a serious consideration. If I knocked anything down, if I made the least noise, who could say what the consequences might be?\n\nI only waited to put the matches near the candle before I extinguished it, and groped my way back into the sitting-room. I locked that door, as I had locked my bedroom door—then quietly got out of the window, and cautiously set my feet on the leaden roof of the verandah.\n\nMy two rooms were at the inner extremity of the new wing of the house in which we all lived, and I had five windows to pass before I could reach the position it was necessary to take up immediately over the library. The first window belonged to a spare room which was empty. The second and third windows belonged to Laura’s room. The fourth window belonged to Sir Percival’s room. The fifth belonged to the Countess’s room. The others, by which it was not necessary for me to pass, were the windows of the Count’s dressing-room, of the bathroom, and of the second empty spare room.\n\nNo sound reached my ears—the black blinding darkness of the night was all round me when I first stood on the verandah, except at that part of it which Madame Fosco’s window overlooked. There, at the very place above the library to which my course was directed—there I saw a gleam of light! The Countess was not yet in bed.\n\nIt was too late to draw back—it was no time to wait. I determined to go on at all hazards, and trust for security to my own caution and to the darkness of the night. “For Laura’s sake!” I thought to myself, as I took the first step forward on the roof, with one hand holding my cloak close round me, and the other groping against the wall of the house. It was better to brush close by the wall than to risk striking my feet against the flower-pots within a few inches of me, on the other side.\n\nI passed the dark window of the spare room, trying the leaden roof at each step with my foot before I risked resting my weight on it. I passed the dark windows of Laura’s room (“God bless her and keep her to-night!”). I passed the dark window of Sir Percival’s room. Then I waited a moment, knelt down with my hands to support me, and so crept to my position, under the protection of the low wall between the bottom of the lighted window and the verandah roof.\n\nWhen I ventured to look up at the window itself I found that the top of it only was open, and that the blind inside was drawn down. While I was looking I saw the shadow of Madame Fosco pass across the white field of the blind—then pass slowly back again. Thus far she could not have heard me, or the shadow would surely have stopped at the blind, even if she had wanted courage enough to open the window and look out?\n\nI placed myself sideways against the railing of the verandah—first ascertaining, by touching them, the position of the flower-pots on either side of me. There was room enough for me to sit between them and no more. The sweet-scented leaves of the flower on my left hand just brushed my cheek as I lightly rested my head against the railing.\n\nThe first sounds that reached me from below were caused by the opening or closing (most probably the latter) of three doors in succession—the doors, no doubt, leading into the hall and into the rooms on each side of the library, which the Count had pledged himself to examine. The first object that I saw was the red spark again travelling out into the night from under the verandah, moving away towards my window, waiting a moment, and then returning to the place from which it had set out.\n\n“The devil take your restlessness! When do you mean to sit down?” growled Sir Percival’s voice beneath me.\n\n“Ouf! how hot it is!” said the Count, sighing and puffing wearily.\n\nHis exclamation was followed by the scraping of the garden chairs on the tiled pavement under the verandah—the welcome sound which told me they were going to sit close at the window as usual. So far the chance was mine. The clock in the turret struck the quarter to twelve as they settled themselves in their chairs. I heard Madame Fosco through the open window yawning, and saw her shadow pass once more across the white field of the blind.\n\nMeanwhile, Sir Percival and the Count began talking together below, now and then dropping their voices a little lower than usual, but never sinking them to a whisper. The strangeness and peril of my situation, the dread, which I could not master, of Madame Fosco’s lighted window, made it difficult, almost impossible, for me, at first, to keep my presence of mind, and to fix my attention solely on the conversation beneath. For some minutes I could only succeed in gathering the general substance of it. I understood the Count to say that the one window alight was his wife’s, that the ground floor of the house was quite clear, and that they might now speak to each other without fear of accidents. Sir Percival merely answered by upbraiding his friend with having unjustifiably slighted his wishes and neglected his interests all through the day. The Count thereupon defended himself by declaring that he had been beset by certain troubles and anxieties which had absorbed all his attention, and that the only safe time to come to an explanation was a time when they could feel certain of being neither interrupted nor overheard. “We are at a serious crisis in our affairs, Percival,” he said, “and if we are to decide on the future at all, we must decide secretly to-night.”\n\nThat sentence of the Count’s was the first which my attention was ready enough to master exactly as it was spoken. From this point, with certain breaks and interruptions, my whole interest fixed breathlessly on the conversation, and I followed it word for word.\n\n“Crisis?” repeated Sir Percival. “It’s a worse crisis than you think for, I can tell you.”\n\n“So I should suppose, from your behaviour for the last day or two,” returned the other coolly. “But wait a little. Before we advance to what I do NOT know, let us be quite certain of what I DO know. Let us first see if I am right about the time that is past, before I make any proposal to you for the time that is to come.”\n\n“Stop till I get the brandy and water. Have some yourself.”\n\n“Thank you, Percival. The cold water with pleasure, a spoon, and the basin of sugar. Eau sucrée, my friend—nothing more.”\n\n“Sugar-and-water for a man of your age!—There! mix your sickly mess. You foreigners are all alike.”\n\n“Now listen, Percival. I will put our position plainly before you, as I understand it, and you shall say if I am right or wrong. You and I both came back to this house from the Continent with our affairs very seriously embarrassed—”\n\n“Cut it short! I wanted some thousands and you some hundreds, and without the money we were both in a fair way to go to the dogs together. There’s the situation. Make what you can of it. Go on.”\n\n“Well, Percival, in your own solid English words, you wanted some thousands and I wanted some hundreds, and the only way of getting them was for you to raise the money for your own necessity (with a small margin beyond for my poor little hundreds) by the help of your wife. What did I tell you about your wife on our way to England?—and what did I tell you again when we had come here, and when I had seen for myself the sort of woman Miss Halcombe was?”\n\n“How should I know? You talked nineteen to the dozen, I suppose, just as usual.”\n\n“I said this: Human ingenuity, my friend, has hitherto only discovered two ways in which a man can manage a woman. One way is to knock her down—a method largely adopted by the brutal lower orders of the people, but utterly abhorrent to the refined and educated classes above them. The other way (much longer, much more difficult, but in the end not less certain) is never to accept a provocation at a woman’s hands. It holds with animals, it holds with children, and it holds with women, who are nothing but children grown up. Quiet resolution is the one quality the animals, the children, and the women all fail in. If they can once shake this superior quality in their master, they get the better of HIM. If they can never succeed in disturbing it, he gets the better of THEM. I said to you, Remember that plain truth when you want your wife to help you to the money. I said, Remember it doubly and trebly in the presence of your wife’s sister, Miss Halcombe. Have you remembered it? Not once in all the implications that have twisted themselves about us in this house. Every provocation that your wife and her sister could offer to you, you instantly accepted from them. Your mad temper lost the signature to the deed, lost the ready money, set Miss Halcombe writing to the lawyer for the first time.”\n\n“First time! Has she written again?”\n\n“Yes, she has written again to-day.”\n\nA chair fell on the pavement of the verandah—fell with a crash, as if it had been kicked down.\n\nIt was well for me that the Count’s revelation roused Sir Percival’s anger as it did. On hearing that I had been once more discovered I started so that the railing against which I leaned cracked again. Had he followed me to the inn? Did he infer that I must have given my letters to Fanny when I told him I had none for the post-bag? Even if it was so, how could he have examined the letters when they had gone straight from my hand to the bosom of the girl’s dress?\n\n“Thank your lucky star,” I heard the Count say next, “that you have me in the house to undo the harm as fast as you do it. Thank your lucky star that I said No when you were mad enough to talk of turning the key to-day on Miss Halcombe, as you turned it in your mischievous folly on your wife. Where are your eyes? Can you look at Miss Halcombe and not see that she has the foresight and the resolution of a man? With that woman for my friend I would snap these fingers of mine at the world. With that woman for my enemy, I, with all my brains and experience—I, Fosco, cunning as the devil himself, as you have told me a hundred times—I walk, in your English phrase, upon egg-shells! And this grand creature—I drink her health in my sugar-and-water—this grand creature, who stands in the strength of her love and her courage, firm as a rock, between us two and that poor, flimsy, pretty blonde wife of yours—this magnificent woman, whom I admire with all my soul, though I oppose her in your interests and in mine, you drive to extremities as if she was no sharper and no bolder than the rest of her sex. Percival! Percival! you deserve to fail, and you HAVE failed.”\n\nThere was a pause. I write the villain’s words about myself because I mean to remember them—because I hope yet for the day when I may speak out once for all in his presence, and cast them back one by one in his teeth.\n\nSir Percival was the first to break the silence again.\n\n“Yes, yes, bully and bluster as much as you like,” he said sulkily; “the difficulty about the money is not the only difficulty. You would be for taking strong measures with the women yourself—if you knew as much as I do.”\n\n“We will come to that second difficulty all in good time,” rejoined the Count. “You may confuse yourself, Percival, as much as you please, but you shall not confuse me. Let the question of the money be settled first. Have I convinced your obstinacy? have I shown you that your temper will not let you help yourself?—Or must I go back, and (as you put it in your dear straightforward English) bully and bluster a little more?”\n\n“Pooh! It’s easy enough to grumble at ME. Say what is to be done—that’s a little harder.”\n\n“Is it? Bah! This is what is to be done: You give up all direction in the business from to-night—you leave it for the future in my hands only. I am talking to a Practical British man—ha? Well, Practical, will that do for you?”\n\n“What do you propose if I leave it all to you?”\n\n“Answer me first. Is it to be in my hands or not?”\n\n“Say it is in your hands—what then?”\n\n“A few questions, Percival, to begin with. I must wait a little yet, to let circumstances guide me, and I must know, in every possible way, what those circumstances are likely to be. There is no time to lose. I have told you already that Miss Halcombe has written to the lawyer to-day for the second time.”\n\n“How did you find it out? What did she say?”\n\n“If I told you, Percival, we should only come back at the end to where we are now. Enough that I have found it out—and the finding has caused that trouble and anxiety which made me so inaccessible to you all through to-day. Now, to refresh my memory about your affairs—it is some time since I talked them over with you. The money has been raised, in the absence of your wife’s signature, by means of bills at three months—raised at a cost that makes my poverty-stricken foreign hair stand on end to think of it! When the bills are due, is there really and truly no earthly way of paying them but by the help of your wife?”\n\n“None.”\n\n“What! You have no money at the bankers?”\n\n“A few hundreds, when I want as many thousands.”\n\n“Have you no other security to borrow upon?”\n\n“Not a shred.”\n\n“What have you actually got with your wife at the present moment?”\n\n“Nothing but the interest of her twenty thousand pounds—barely enough to pay our daily expenses.”\n\n“What do you expect from your wife?”\n\n“Three thousand a year when her uncle dies.”\n\n“A fine fortune, Percival. What sort of a man is this uncle? Old?”\n\n“No—neither old nor young.”\n\n“A good-tempered, freely-living man? Married? No—I think my wife told me, not married.”\n\n“Of course not. If he was married, and had a son, Lady Glyde would not be next heir to the property. I’ll tell you what he is. He’s a maudlin, twaddling, selfish fool, and bores everybody who comes near him about the state of his health.”\n\n“Men of that sort, Percival, live long, and marry malevolently when you least expect it. I don’t give you much, my friend, for your chance of the three thousand a year. Is there nothing more that comes to you from your wife?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Absolutely nothing?”\n\n“Absolutely nothing—except in case of her death.”\n\n“Aha! in the case of her death.”\n\nThere was another pause. The Count moved from the verandah to the gravel walk outside. I knew that he had moved by his voice. “The rain has come at last,” I heard him say. It had come. The state of my cloak showed that it had been falling thickly for some little time.\n\nThe Count went back under the verandah—I heard the chair creak beneath his weight as he sat down in it again.\n\n“Well, Percival,” he said, “and in the case of Lady Glyde’s death, what do you get then?”\n\n“If she leaves no children——”\n\n“Which she is likely to do?”\n\n“Which she is not in the least likely to do——”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“Why, then I get her twenty thousand pounds.”\n\n“Paid down?”\n\n“Paid down.”\n\nThey were silent once more. As their voices ceased Madame Fosco’s shadow darkened the blind again. Instead of passing this time, it remained, for a moment, quite still. I saw her fingers steal round the corner of the blind, and draw it on one side. The dim white outline of her face, looking out straight over me, appeared behind the window. I kept still, shrouded from head to foot in my black cloak. The rain, which was fast wetting me, dripped over the glass, blurred it, and prevented her from seeing anything. “More rain!” I heard her say to herself. She dropped the blind, and I breathed again freely.\n\nThe talk went on below me, the Count resuming it this time.\n\n“Percival! do you care about your wife?”\n\n“Fosco! that’s rather a downright question.”\n\n“I am a downright man, and I repeat it.”\n\n“Why the devil do you look at me in that way?”\n\n“You won’t answer me? Well, then, let us say your wife dies before the summer is out——”\n\n“Drop it, Fosco!”\n\n“Let us say your wife dies——”\n\n“Drop it, I tell you!”\n\n“In that case, you would gain twenty thousand pounds, and you would lose——”\n\n“I should lose the chance of three thousand a year.”\n\n“The REMOTE chance, Percival—the remote chance only. And you want money, at once. In your position the gain is certain—the loss doubtful.”\n\n“Speak for yourself as well as for me. Some of the money I want has been borrowed for you. And if you come to gain, my wife’s death would be ten thousand pounds in your wife’s pocket. Sharp as you are, you seem to have conveniently forgotten Madame Fosco’s legacy. Don’t look at me in that way! I won’t have it! What with your looks and your questions, upon my soul, you make my flesh creep!”\n\n“Your flesh? Does flesh mean conscience in English? I speak of your wife’s death as I speak of a possibility. Why not? The respectable lawyers who scribble-scrabble your deeds and your wills look the deaths of living people in the face. Do lawyers make your flesh creep? Why should I? It is my business to-night to clear up your position beyond the possibility of mistake, and I have now done it. Here is your position. If your wife lives, you pay those bills with her signature to the parchment. If your wife dies, you pay them with her death.”\n\nAs he spoke the light in Madame Fosco’s room was extinguished, and the whole second floor of the house was now sunk in darkness.\n\n“Talk! talk!” grumbled Sir Percival. “One would think, to hear you, that my wife’s signature to the deed was got already.”\n\n“You have left the matter in my hands,” retorted the Count, “and I have more than two months before me to turn round in. Say no more about it, if you please, for the present. When the bills are due, you will see for yourself if my ‘talk! talk!’ is worth something, or if it is not. And now, Percival, having done with the money matters for to-night, I can place my attention at your disposal, if you wish to consult me on that second difficulty which has mixed itself up with our little embarrassments, and which has so altered you for the worse, that I hardly know you again. Speak, my friend—and pardon me if I shock your fiery national tastes by mixing myself a second glass of sugar-and-water.”\n\n“It’s very well to say speak,” replied Sir Percival, in a far more quiet and more polite tone than he had yet adopted, “but it’s not so easy to know how to begin.”\n\n“Shall I help you?” suggested the Count. “Shall I give this private difficulty of yours a name? What if I call it—Anne Catherick?”\n\n“Look here, Fosco, you and I have known each other for a long time, and if you have helped me out of one or two scrapes before this, I have done the best I could to help you in return, as far as money would go. We have made as many friendly sacrifices, on both sides, as men could, but we have had our secrets from each other, of course—haven’t we?”\n\n“You have had a secret from me, Percival. There is a skeleton in your cupboard here at Blackwater Park that has peeped out in these last few days at other people besides yourself.”\n\n“Well, suppose it has. If it doesn’t concern you, you needn’t be curious about it, need you?”\n\n“Do I look curious about it?”\n\n“Yes, you do.”\n\n“So! so! my face speaks the truth, then? What an immense foundation of good there must be in the nature of a man who arrives at my age, and whose face has not yet lost the habit of speaking the truth!—Come, Glyde! let us be candid one with the other. This secret of yours has sought me: I have not sought it. Let us say I am curious—do you ask me, as your old friend, to respect your secret, and to leave it, once for all, in your own keeping?”\n\n“Yes—that’s just what I do ask.”\n\n“Then my curiosity is at an end. It dies in me from this moment.”\n\n“Do you really mean that?”\n\n“What makes you doubt me?”\n\n“I have had some experience, Fosco, of your roundabout ways, and I am not so sure that you won’t worm it out of me after all.”\n\nThe chair below suddenly creaked again—I felt the trellis-work pillar under me shake from top to bottom. The Count had started to his feet, and had struck it with his hand in indignation.\n\n“Percival! Percival!” he cried passionately, “do you know me no better than that? Has all your experience shown you nothing of my character yet? I am a man of the antique type! I am capable of the most exalted acts of virtue—when I have the chance of performing them. It has been the misfortune of my life that I have had few chances. My conception of friendship is sublime! Is it my fault that your skeleton has peeped out at me? Why do I confess my curiosity? You poor superficial Englishman, it is to magnify my own self-control. I could draw your secret out of you, if I liked, as I draw this finger out of the palm of my hand—you know I could! But you have appealed to my friendship, and the duties of friendship are sacred to me. See! I trample my base curiosity under my feet. My exalted sentiments lift me above it. Recognise them, Percival! imitate them, Percival! Shake hands—I forgive you.”\n\nHis voice faltered over the last words—faltered, as if he were actually shedding tears!\n\nSir Percival confusedly attempted to excuse himself, but the Count was too magnanimous to listen to him.\n\n“No!” he said. “When my friend has wounded me, I can pardon him without apologies. Tell me, in plain words, do you want my help?”\n\n“Yes, badly enough.”\n\n“And you can ask for it without compromising yourself?”\n\n“I can try, at any rate.”\n\n“Try, then.”\n\n“Well, this is how it stands:—I told you to-day that I had done my best to find Anne Catherick, and failed.”\n\n“Yes, you did.”\n\n“Fosco! I’m a lost man if I DON’T find her.”\n\n“Ha! Is it so serious as that?”\n\nA little stream of light travelled out under the verandah, and fell over the gravel-walk. The Count had taken the lamp from the inner part of the room to see his friend clearly by the light of it.\n\n“Yes!” he said. “Your face speaks the truth this time. Serious, indeed—as serious as the money matters themselves.”\n\n“More serious. As true as I sit here, more serious!”\n\nThe light disappeared again and the talk went on.\n\n“I showed you the letter to my wife that Anne Catherick hid in the sand,” Sir Percival continued. “There’s no boasting in that letter, Fosco—she DOES know the Secret.”\n\n“Say as little as possible, Percival, in my presence, of the Secret. Does she know it from you?”\n\n“No, from her mother.”\n\n“Two women in possession of your private mind—bad, bad, bad, my friend! One question here, before we go any farther. The motive of your shutting up the daughter in the asylum is now plain enough to me, but the manner of her escape is not quite so clear. Do you suspect the people in charge of her of closing their eyes purposely, at the instance of some enemy who could afford to make it worth their while?”\n\n“No, she was the best-behaved patient they had—and, like fools, they trusted her. She’s just mad enough to be shut up, and just sane enough to ruin me when she’s at large—if you understand that?”\n\n“I do understand it. Now, Percival, come at once to the point, and then I shall know what to do. Where is the danger of your position at the present moment?”\n\n“Anne Catherick is in this neighbourhood, and in communication with Lady Glyde—there’s the danger, plain enough. Who can read the letter she hid in the sand, and not see that my wife is in possession of the Secret, deny it as she may?”\n\n“One moment, Percival. If Lady Glyde does know the Secret, she must know also that it is a compromising secret for you. As your wife, surely it is her interest to keep it?”\n\n“Is it? I’m coming to that. It might be her interest if she cared two straws about me. But I happen to be an encumbrance in the way of another man. She was in love with him before she married me—she’s in love with him now—an infernal vagabond of a drawing-master, named Hartright.”\n\n“My dear friend! what is there extraordinary in that? They are all in love with some other man. Who gets the first of a woman’s heart? In all my experience I have never yet met with the man who was Number One. Number Two, sometimes. Number Three, Four, Five, often. Number One, never! He exists, of course—but I have not met with him.”\n\n“Wait! I haven’t done yet. Who do you think helped Anne Catherick to get the start, when the people from the mad-house were after her? Hartright. Who do you think saw her again in Cumberland? Hartright. Both times he spoke to her alone. Stop! don’t interrupt me. The scoundrel’s as sweet on my wife as she is on him. He knows the Secret, and she knows the Secret. Once let them both get together again, and it’s her interest and his interest to turn their information against me.”\n\n“Gently, Percival—gently! Are you insensible to the virtue of Lady Glyde?”\n\n“That for the virtue of Lady Glyde! I believe in nothing about her but her money. Don’t you see how the case stands? She might be harmless enough by herself; but if she and that vagabond Hartright——”\n\n“Yes, yes, I see. Where is Mr. Hartright?”\n\n“Out of the country. If he means to keep a whole skin on his bones, I recommend him not to come back in a hurry.”\n\n“Are you sure he is out of the country?”\n\n“Certain. I had him watched from the time he left Cumberland to the time he sailed. Oh, I’ve been careful, I can tell you! Anne Catherick lived with some people at a farmhouse near Limmeridge. I went there myself, after she had given me the slip, and made sure that they knew nothing. I gave her mother a form of letter to write to Miss Halcombe, exonerating me from any bad motive in putting her under restraint. I’ve spent, I’m afraid to say how much, in trying to trace her, and in spite of it all, she turns up here and escapes me on my own property! How do I know who else may see her, who else may speak to her? That prying scoundrel, Hartright, may come back without my knowing it, and may make use of her to-morrow——”\n\n“Not he, Percival! While I am on the spot, and while that woman is in the neighbourhood, I will answer for our laying hands on her before Mr. Hartright—even if he does come back. I see! yes, yes, I see! The finding of Anne Catherick is the first necessity—make your mind easy about the rest. Your wife is here, under your thumb—Miss Halcombe is inseparable from her, and is, therefore, under your thumb also—and Mr. Hartright is out of the country. This invisible Anne of yours is all we have to think of for the present. You have made your inquiries?”\n\n“Yes. I have been to her mother, I have ransacked the village—and all to no purpose.”\n\n“Is her mother to be depended on?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“She has told your secret once.”\n\n“She won’t tell it again.”\n\n“Why not? Are her own interests concerned in keeping it, as well as yours?”\n\n“Yes—deeply concerned.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it, Percival, for your sake. Don’t be discouraged, my friend. Our money matters, as I told you, leave me plenty of time to turn round in, and I may search for Anne Catherick to-morrow to better purpose than you. One last question before we go to bed.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“It is this. When I went to the boat-house to tell Lady Glyde that the little difficulty of her signature was put off, accident took me there in time to see a strange woman parting in a very suspicious manner from your wife. But accident did not bring me near enough to see this same woman’s face plainly. I must know how to recognise our invisible Anne. What is she like?”\n\n“Like? Come! I’ll tell you in two words. She’s a sickly likeness of my wife.”\n\nThe chair creaked, and the pillar shook once more. The Count was on his feet again—this time in astonishment.\n\n“What!!!” he exclaimed eagerly.\n\n“Fancy my wife, after a bad illness, with a touch of something wrong in her head—and there is Anne Catherick for you,” answered Sir Percival.\n\n“Are they related to each other?”\n\n“Not a bit of it.”\n\n“And yet so like?”\n\n“Yes, so like. What are you laughing about?”\n\nThere was no answer, and no sound of any kind. The Count was laughing in his smooth silent internal way.\n\n“What are you laughing about?” reiterated Sir Percival.\n\n“Perhaps at my own fancies, my good friend. Allow me my Italian humour—do I not come of the illustrious nation which invented the exhibition of Punch? Well, well, well, I shall know Anne Catherick when I see her—and so enough for to-night. Make your mind easy, Percival. Sleep, my son, the sleep of the just, and see what I will do for you when daylight comes to help us both. I have my projects and my plans here in my big head. You shall pay those bills and find Anne Catherick—my sacred word of honour on it, but you shall! Am I a friend to be treasured in the best corner of your heart, or am I not? Am I worth those loans of money which you so delicately reminded me of a little while since? Whatever you do, never wound me in my sentiments any more. Recognise them, Percival! imitate them, Percival! I forgive you again—I shake hands again. Good-night!”\n\nNot another word was spoken. I heard the Count close the library door. I heard Sir Percival barring up the window-shutters. It had been raining, raining all the time. I was cramped by my position and chilled to the bones. When I first tried to move, the effort was so painful to me that I was obliged to desist. I tried a second time, and succeeded in rising to my knees on the wet roof.\n\nAs I crept to the wall, and raised myself against it, I looked back, and saw the window of the Count’s dressing-room gleam into light. My sinking courage flickered up in me again, and kept my eyes fixed on his window, as I stole my way back, step by step, past the wall of the house.\n\nThe clock struck the quarter after one, when I laid my hands on the window-sill of my own room. I had seen nothing and heard nothing which could lead me to suppose that my retreat had been discovered.\n\n# X\n\nJune 20th.—Eight o’clock. The sun is shining in a clear sky. I have not been near my bed—I have not once closed my weary wakeful eyes. From the same window at which I looked out into the darkness of last night, I look out now at the bright stillness of the morning.\n\nI count the hours that have passed since I escaped to the shelter of this room by my own sensations—and those hours seem like weeks.\n\nHow short a time, and yet how long to ME—since I sank down in the darkness, here, on the floor—drenched to the skin, cramped in every limb, cold to the bones, a useless, helpless, panic-stricken creature.\n\nI hardly know when I roused myself. I hardly know when I groped my way back to the bedroom, and lighted the candle, and searched (with a strange ignorance, at first, of where to look for them) for dry clothes to warm me. The doing of these things is in my mind, but not the time when they were done.\n\nCan I even remember when the chilled, cramped feeling left me, and the throbbing heat came in its place?\n\nSurely it was before the sun rose? Yes, I heard the clock strike three. I remember the time by the sudden brightness and clearness, the feverish strain and excitement of all my faculties which came with it. I remember my resolution to control myself, to wait patiently hour after hour, till the chance offered of removing Laura from this horrible place, without the danger of immediate discovery and pursuit. I remember the persuasion settling itself in my mind that the words those two men had said to each other would furnish us, not only with our justification for leaving the house, but with our weapons of defence against them as well. I recall the impulse that awakened in me to preserve those words in writing, exactly as they were spoken, while the time was my own, and while my memory vividly retained them. All this I remember plainly: there is no confusion in my head yet. The coming in here from the bedroom, with my pen and ink and paper, before sunrise—the sitting down at the widely-opened window to get all the air I could to cool me—the ceaseless writing, faster and faster, hotter and hotter, driving on more and more wakefully, all through the dreadful interval before the house was astir again—how clearly I recall it, from the beginning by candle-light, to the end on the page before this, in the sunshine of the new day!\n\nWhy do I sit here still? Why do I weary my hot eyes and my burning head by writing more? Why not lie down and rest myself, and try to quench the fever that consumes me, in sleep?\n\nI dare not attempt it. A fear beyond all other fears has got possession of me. I am afraid of this heat that parches my skin. I am afraid of the creeping and throbbing that I feel in my head. If I lie down now, how do I know that I may have the sense and the strength to rise again?\n\nOh, the rain, the rain—the cruel rain that chilled me last night!\n\nNine o’clock. Was it nine struck, or eight? Nine, surely? I am shivering again—shivering, from head to foot, in the summer air. Have I been sitting here asleep? I don’t know what I have been doing.\n\nOh, my God! am I going to be ill?\n\nIll, at such a time as this!\n\nMy head—I am sadly afraid of my head. I can write, but the lines all run together. I see the words. Laura—I can write Laura, and see I write it. Eight or nine—which was it?\n\nSo cold, so cold—oh, that rain last night!—and the strokes of the clock, the strokes I can’t count, keep striking in my head——\n\nNote [At this place the entry in the Diary ceases to be legible. The two or three lines which follow contain fragments of words only, mingled with blots and scratches of the pen. The last marks on the paper bear some resemblance to the first two letters (L and A) of the name of Lady Glyde.\n\nOn the next page of the Diary, another entry appears. It is in a man’s handwriting, large, bold, and firmly regular, and the date is “June the 21st.” It contains these lines—]\n\n# POSTSCRIPT BY A SINCERE FRIEND\n\nThe illness of our excellent Miss Halcombe has afforded me the opportunity of enjoying an unexpected intellectual pleasure.\n\nI refer to the perusal (which I have just completed) of this interesting Diary.\n\nThere are many hundred pages here. I can lay my hand on my heart, and declare that every page has charmed, refreshed, delighted me.\n\nTo a man of my sentiments it is unspeakably gratifying to be able to say this.\n\nAdmirable woman!\n\nI allude to Miss Halcombe.\n\nStupendous effort!\n\nI refer to the Diary.\n\nYes! these pages are amazing. The tact which I find here, the discretion, the rare courage, the wonderful power of memory, the accurate observation of character, the easy grace of style, the charming outbursts of womanly feeling, have all inexpressibly increased my admiration of this sublime creature, of this magnificent Marian. The presentation of my own character is masterly in the extreme. I certify, with my whole heart, to the fidelity of the portrait. I feel how vivid an impression I must have produced to have been painted in such strong, such rich, such massive colours as these. I lament afresh the cruel necessity which sets our interests at variance, and opposes us to each other. Under happier circumstances how worthy I should have been of Miss Halcombe—how worthy Miss Halcombe would have been of ME.\n\nThe sentiments which animate my heart assure me that the lines I have just written express a Profound Truth.\n\nThose sentiments exalt me above all merely personal considerations. I bear witness, in the most disinterested manner, to the excellence of the stratagem by which this unparalleled woman surprised the private interview between Percival and myself—also to the marvellous accuracy of her report of the whole conversation from its beginning to its end.\n\nThose sentiments have induced me to offer to the unimpressionable doctor who attends on her my vast knowledge of chemistry, and my luminous experience of the more subtle resources which medical and magnetic science have placed at the disposal of mankind. He has hitherto declined to avail himself of my assistance. Miserable man!\n\nFinally, those sentiments dictate the lines—grateful, sympathetic, paternal lines—which appear in this place. I close the book. My strict sense of propriety restores it (by the hands of my wife) to its place on the writer’s table. Events are hurrying me away. Circumstances are guiding me to serious issues. Vast perspectives of success unroll themselves before my eyes. I accomplish my destiny with a calmness which is terrible to myself. Nothing but the homage of my admiration is my own. I deposit it with respectful tenderness at the feet of Miss Halcombe.\n\nI breathe my wishes for her recovery.\n\nI condole with her on the inevitable failure of every plan that she has formed for her sister’s benefit. At the same time, I entreat her to believe that the information which I have derived from her Diary will in no respect help me to contribute to that failure. It simply confirms the plan of conduct which I had previously arranged. I have to thank these pages for awakening the finest sensibilities in my nature—nothing more.\n\nTo a person of similar sensibility this simple assertion will explain and excuse everything.\n\nMiss Halcombe is a person of similar sensibility.\n\nIn that persuasion I sign myself, Fosco.\n\nTHE STORY CONTINUED BY FREDERICK FAIRLIE, ESQ., OF LIMMERIDGE HOUSE[2]\n\n[2] The manner in which Mr. Fairlie’s Narrative, and other Narratives that are shortly to follow it, were originally obtained, forms the subject of an explanation which will appear at a later period.\n\nIt is the grand misfortune of my life that nobody will let me alone.\n\nWhy—I ask everybody—why worry ME? Nobody answers that question, and nobody lets me alone. Relatives, friends, and strangers all combine to annoy me. What have I done? I ask myself, I ask my servant, Louis, fifty times a day—what have I done? Neither of us can tell. Most extraordinary!\n\nThe last annoyance that has assailed me is the annoyance of being called upon to write this Narrative. Is a man in my state of nervous wretchedness capable of writing narratives? When I put this extremely reasonable objection, I am told that certain very serious events relating to my niece have happened within my experience, and that I am the fit person to describe them on that account. I am threatened if I fail to exert myself in the manner required, with consequences which I cannot so much as think of without perfect prostration. There is really no need to threaten me. Shattered by my miserable health and my family troubles, I am incapable of resistance. If you insist, you take your unjust advantage of me, and I give way immediately. I will endeavour to remember what I can (under protest), and to write what I can (also under protest), and what I can’t remember and can’t write, Louis must remember and write for me. He is an ass, and I am an invalid, and we are likely to make all sorts of mistakes between us. How humiliating!\n\nI am told to remember dates. Good heavens! I never did such a thing in my life—how am I to begin now?\n\nI have asked Louis. He is not quite such an ass as I have hitherto supposed. He remembers the date of the event, within a week or two—and I remember the name of the person. The date was towards the end of June, or the beginning of July, and the name (in my opinion a remarkably vulgar one) was Fanny.\n\nAt the end of June, or the beginning of July, then, I was reclining in my customary state, surrounded by the various objects of Art which I have collected about me to improve the taste of the barbarous people in my neighbourhood. That is to say, I had the photographs of my pictures, and prints, and coins, and so forth, all about me, which I intend, one of these days, to present (the photographs, I mean, if the clumsy English language will let me mean anything) to present to the institution at Carlisle (horrid place!), with a view to improving the tastes of the members (Goths and Vandals to a man). It might be supposed that a gentleman who was in course of conferring a great national benefit on his countrymen was the last gentleman in the world to be unfeelingly worried about private difficulties and family affairs. Quite a mistake, I assure you, in my case.\n\nHowever, there I was, reclining, with my art-treasures about me, and wanting a quiet morning. Because I wanted a quiet morning, of course Louis came in. It was perfectly natural that I should inquire what the deuce he meant by making his appearance when I had not rung my bell. I seldom swear—it is such an ungentlemanlike habit—but when Louis answered by a grin, I think it was also perfectly natural that I should damn him for grinning. At any rate, I did.\n\nThis rigorous mode of treatment, I have observed, invariably brings persons in the lower class of life to their senses. It brought Louis to HIS senses. He was so obliging as to leave off grinning, and inform me that a Young Person was outside wanting to see me. He added (with the odious talkativeness of servants), that her name was Fanny.\n\n“Who is Fanny?”\n\n“Lady Glyde’s maid, sir.”\n\n“What does Lady Glyde’s maid want with me?”\n\n“A letter, sir——”\n\n“Take it.”\n\n“She refuses to give it to anybody but you, sir.”\n\n“Who sends the letter?”\n\n“Miss Halcombe, sir.”\n\nThe moment I heard Miss Halcombe’s name I gave up. It is a habit of mine always to give up to Miss Halcombe. I find, by experience, that it saves noise. I gave up on this occasion. Dear Marian!\n\n“Let Lady Glyde’s maid come in, Louis. Stop! Do her shoes creak?”\n\nI was obliged to ask the question. Creaking shoes invariably upset me for the day. I was resigned to see the Young Person, but I was NOT resigned to let the Young Person’s shoes upset me. There is a limit even to my endurance.\n\nLouis affirmed distinctly that her shoes were to be depended upon. I waved my hand. He introduced her. Is it necessary to say that she expressed her sense of embarrassment by shutting up her mouth and breathing through her nose? To the student of female human nature in the lower orders, surely not.\n\nLet me do the girl justice. Her shoes did NOT creak. But why do Young Persons in service all perspire at the hands? Why have they all got fat noses and hard cheeks? And why are their faces so sadly unfinished, especially about the corners of the eyelids? I am not strong enough to think deeply myself on any subject, but I appeal to professional men, who are. Why have we no variety in our breed of Young Persons?\n\n“You have a letter for me, from Miss Halcombe? Put it down on the table, please, and don’t upset anything. How is Miss Halcombe?”\n\n“Very well, thank you, sir.”\n\n“And Lady Glyde?”\n\nI received no answer. The Young Person’s face became more unfinished than ever, and I think she began to cry. I certainly saw something moist about her eyes. Tears or perspiration? Louis (whom I have just consulted) is inclined to think, tears. He is in her class of life, and he ought to know best. Let us say, tears.\n\nExcept when the refining process of Art judiciously removes from them all resemblance to Nature, I distinctly object to tears. Tears are scientifically described as a Secretion. I can understand that a secretion may be healthy or unhealthy, but I cannot see the interest of a secretion from a sentimental point of view. Perhaps my own secretions being all wrong together, I am a little prejudiced on the subject. No matter. I behaved, on this occasion, with all possible propriety and feeling. I closed my eyes and said to Louis—\n\n“Endeavour to ascertain what she means.”\n\nLouis endeavoured, and the Young Person endeavoured. They succeeded in confusing each other to such an extent that I am bound in common gratitude to say, they really amused me. I think I shall send for them again when I am in low spirits. I have just mentioned this idea to Louis. Strange to say, it seems to make him uncomfortable. Poor devil!\n\nSurely I am not expected to repeat my niece’s maid’s explanation of her tears, interpreted in the English of my Swiss valet? The thing is manifestly impossible. I can give my own impressions and feelings perhaps. Will that do as well? Please say, Yes.\n\nMy idea is that she began by telling me (through Louis) that her master had dismissed her from her mistress’s service. (Observe, throughout, the strange irrelevancy of the Young Person. Was it my fault that she had lost her place?) On her dismissal, she had gone to the inn to sleep. (I don’t keep the inn—why mention it to ME?) Between six o’clock and seven Miss Halcombe had come to say good-bye, and had given her two letters, one for me, and one for a gentleman in London. (I am not a gentleman in London—hang the gentleman in London!) She had carefully put the two letters into her bosom (what have I to do with her bosom?); she had been very unhappy, when Miss Halcombe had gone away again; she had not had the heart to put bit or drop between her lips till it was near bedtime, and then, when it was close on nine o’clock, she had thought she should like a cup of tea. (Am I responsible for any of these vulgar fluctuations, which begin with unhappiness and end with tea?) Just as she was WARMING THE POT (I give the words on the authority of Louis, who says he knows what they mean, and wishes to explain, but I snub him on principle)—just as she was warming the pot the door opened, and she was STRUCK OF A HEAP (her own words again, and perfectly unintelligible this time to Louis, as well as to myself) by the appearance in the inn parlour of her ladyship the Countess. I give my niece’s maid’s description of my sister’s title with a sense of the highest relish. My poor dear sister is a tiresome woman who married a foreigner. To resume: the door opened, her ladyship the Countess appeared in the parlour, and the Young Person was struck of a heap. Most remarkable!\n\nI must really rest a little before I can get on any farther. When I have reclined for a few minutes, with my eyes closed, and when Louis has refreshed my poor aching temples with a little eau-de-Cologne, I may be able to proceed.\n\nHer ladyship the Countess——\n\nNo. I am able to proceed, but not to sit up. I will recline and dictate. Louis has a horrid accent, but he knows the language, and can write. How very convenient!\n\nHer ladyship, the Countess, explained her unexpected appearance at the inn by telling Fanny that she had come to bring one or two little messages which Miss Halcombe in her hurry had forgotten. The Young Person thereupon waited anxiously to hear what the messages were, but the Countess seemed disinclined to mention them (so like my sister’s tiresome way!) until Fanny had had her tea. Her ladyship was surprisingly kind and thoughtful about it (extremely unlike my sister), and said, “I am sure, my poor girl, you must want your tea. We can let the messages wait till afterwards. Come, come, if nothing else will put you at your ease, I’ll make the tea and have a cup with you.” I think those were the words, as reported excitably, in my presence, by the Young Person. At any rate, the Countess insisted on making the tea, and carried her ridiculous ostentation of humility so far as to take one cup herself, and to insist on the girl’s taking the other. The girl drank the tea, and according to her own account, solemnised the extraordinary occasion five minutes afterwards by fainting dead away for the first time in her life. Here again I use her own words. Louis thinks they were accompanied by an increased secretion of tears. I can’t say myself. The effort of listening being quite as much as I could manage, my eyes were closed.\n\nWhere did I leave off? Ah, yes—she fainted after drinking a cup of tea with the Countess—a proceeding which might have interested me if I had been her medical man, but being nothing of the sort I felt bored by hearing of it, nothing more. When she came to herself in half an hour’s time she was on the sofa, and nobody was with her but the landlady. The Countess, finding it too late to remain any longer at the inn, had gone away as soon as the girl showed signs of recovering, and the landlady had been good enough to help her upstairs to bed.\n\nLeft by herself, she had felt in her bosom (I regret the necessity of referring to this part of the subject a second time), and had found the two letters there quite safe, but strangely crumpled. She had been giddy in the night, but had got up well enough to travel in the morning. She had put the letter addressed to that obtrusive stranger, the gentleman in London into the post, and had now delivered the other letter into my hands as she was told. This was the plain truth, and though she could not blame herself for any intentional neglect, she was sadly troubled in her mind, and sadly in want of a word of advice. At this point Louis thinks the secretions appeared again. Perhaps they did, but it is of infinitely greater importance to mention that at this point also I lost my patience, opened my eyes, and interfered.\n\n“What is the purport of all this?” I inquired.\n\nMy niece’s irrelevant maid stared, and stood speechless.\n\n“Endeavour to explain,” I said to my servant. “Translate me, Louis.”\n\nLouis endeavoured and translated. In other words, he descended immediately into a bottomless pit of confusion, and the Young Person followed him down. I really don’t know when I have been so amused. I left them at the bottom of the pit as long as they diverted me. When they ceased to divert me, I exerted my intelligence, and pulled them up again.\n\nIt is unnecessary to say that my interference enabled me, in due course of time, to ascertain the purport of the Young Person’s remarks.\n\nI discovered that she was uneasy in her mind, because the train of events that she had just described to me had prevented her from receiving those supplementary messages which Miss Halcombe had intrusted to the Countess to deliver. She was afraid the messages might have been of great importance to her mistress’s interests. Her dread of Sir Percival had deterred her from going to Blackwater Park late at night to inquire about them, and Miss Halcombe’s own directions to her, on no account to miss the train in the morning, had prevented her from waiting at the inn the next day. She was most anxious that the misfortune of her fainting-fit should not lead to the second misfortune of making her mistress think her neglectful, and she would humbly beg to ask me whether I would advise her to write her explanations and excuses to Miss Halcombe, requesting to receive the messages by letter, if it was not too late. I make no apologies for this extremely prosy paragraph. I have been ordered to write it. There are people, unaccountable as it may appear, who actually take more interest in what my niece’s maid said to me on this occasion than in what I said to my niece’s maid. Amusing perversity!\n\n“I should feel very much obliged to you, sir, if you would kindly tell me what I had better do,” remarked the Young Person.\n\n“Let things stop as they are,” I said, adapting my language to my listener. “I invariably let things stop as they are. Yes. Is that all?”\n\n“If you think it would be a liberty in me, sir, to write, of course I wouldn’t venture to do so. But I am so very anxious to do all I can to serve my mistress faithfully——”\n\nPeople in the lower class of life never know when or how to go out of a room. They invariably require to be helped out by their betters. I thought it high time to help the Young Person out. I did it with two judicious words—\n\n“Good-morning.”\n\nSomething outside or inside this singular girl suddenly creaked. Louis, who was looking at her (which I was not), says she creaked when she curtseyed. Curious. Was it her shoes, her stays, or her bones? Louis thinks it was her stays. Most extraordinary!\n\nAs soon as I was left by myself I had a little nap—I really wanted it. When I awoke again I noticed dear Marian’s letter. If I had had the least idea of what it contained I should certainly not have attempted to open it. Being, unfortunately for myself, quite innocent of all suspicion, I read the letter. It immediately upset me for the day.\n\nI am, by nature, one of the most easy-tempered creatures that ever lived—I make allowances for everybody, and I take offence at nothing. But as I have before remarked, there are limits to my endurance. I laid down Marian’s letter, and felt myself—justly felt myself—an injured man.\n\nI am about to make a remark. It is, of course, applicable to the very serious matter now under notice, or I should not allow it to appear in this place.\n\nNothing, in my opinion, sets the odious selfishness of mankind in such a repulsively vivid light as the treatment, in all classes of society, which the Single people receive at the hands of the Married people. When you have once shown yourself too considerate and self-denying to add a family of your own to an already overcrowded population, you are vindictively marked out by your married friends, who have no similar consideration and no similar self-denial, as the recipient of half their conjugal troubles, and the born friend of all their children. Husbands and wives TALK of the cares of matrimony, and bachelors and spinsters BEAR them. Take my own case. I considerately remain single, and my poor dear brother Philip inconsiderately marries. What does he do when he dies? He leaves his daughter to ME. She is a sweet girl—she is also a dreadful responsibility. Why lay her on my shoulders? Because I am bound, in the harmless character of a single man, to relieve my married connections of all their own troubles. I do my best with my brother’s responsibility—I marry my niece, with infinite fuss and difficulty, to the man her father wanted her to marry. She and her husband disagree, and unpleasant consequences follow. What does she do with those consequences? She transfers them to ME. Why transfer them to ME? Because I am bound, in the harmless character of a single man, to relieve my married connections of all their own troubles. Poor single people! Poor human nature!\n\nIt is quite unnecessary to say that Marian’s letter threatened me. Everybody threatens me. All sorts of horrors were to fall on my devoted head if I hesitated to turn Limmeridge House into an asylum for my niece and her misfortunes. I did hesitate, nevertheless.\n\nI have mentioned that my usual course, hitherto, had been to submit to dear Marian, and save noise. But on this occasion, the consequences involved in her extremely inconsiderate proposal were of a nature to make me pause. If I opened Limmeridge House as an asylum to Lady Glyde, what security had I against Sir Percival Glyde’s following her here in a state of violent resentment against ME for harbouring his wife? I saw such a perfect labyrinth of troubles involved in this proceeding that I determined to feel my ground, as it were. I wrote, therefore, to dear Marian to beg (as she had no husband to lay claim to her) that she would come here by herself, first, and talk the matter over with me. If she could answer my objections to my own perfect satisfaction, then I assured her that I would receive our sweet Laura with the greatest pleasure, but not otherwise.\n\nI felt, of course, at the time, that this temporising on my part would probably end in bringing Marian here in a state of virtuous indignation, banging doors. But then, the other course of proceeding might end in bringing Sir Percival here in a state of virtuous indignation, banging doors also, and of the two indignations and bangings I preferred Marian’s, because I was used to her. Accordingly I despatched the letter by return of post. It gained me time, at all events—and, oh dear me! what a point that was to begin with.\n\nWhen I am totally prostrated (did I mention that I was totally prostrated by Marian’s letter?) it always takes me three days to get up again. I was very unreasonable—I expected three days of quiet. Of course I didn’t get them.\n\nThe third day’s post brought me a most impertinent letter from a person with whom I was totally unacquainted. He described himself as the acting partner of our man of business—our dear, pig-headed old Gilmore—and he informed me that he had lately received, by the post, a letter addressed to him in Miss Halcombe’s handwriting. On opening the envelope, he had discovered, to his astonishment, that it contained nothing but a blank sheet of note-paper. This circumstance appeared to him so suspicious (as suggesting to his restless legal mind that the letter had been tampered with) that he had at once written to Miss Halcombe, and had received no answer by return of post. In this difficulty, instead of acting like a sensible man and letting things take their proper course, his next absurd proceeding, on his own showing, was to pester me by writing to inquire if I knew anything about it. What the deuce should I know about it? Why alarm me as well as himself? I wrote back to that effect. It was one of my keenest letters. I have produced nothing with a sharper epistolary edge to it since I tendered his dismissal in writing to that extremely troublesome person, Mr. Walter Hartright.\n\nMy letter produced its effect. I heard nothing more from the lawyer.\n\nThis perhaps was not altogether surprising. But it was certainly a remarkable circumstance that no second letter reached me from Marian, and that no warning signs appeared of her arrival. Her unexpected absence did me amazing good. It was so very soothing and pleasant to infer (as I did of course) that my married connections had made it up again. Five days of undisturbed tranquillity, of delicious single blessedness, quite restored me. On the sixth day I felt strong enough to send for my photographer, and to set him at work again on the presentation copies of my art-treasures, with a view, as I have already mentioned, to the improvement of taste in this barbarous neighbourhood. I had just dismissed him to his workshop, and had just begun coquetting with my coins, when Louis suddenly made his appearance with a card in his hand.\n\n“Another Young Person?” I said. “I won’t see her. In my state of health Young Persons disagree with me. Not at home.”\n\n“It is a gentleman this time, sir.”\n\nA gentleman of course made a difference. I looked at the card.\n\nGracious Heaven! my tiresome sister’s foreign husband, Count Fosco.\n\nIs it necessary to say what my first impression was when I looked at my visitor’s card? Surely not! My sister having married a foreigner, there was but one impression that any man in his senses could possibly feel. Of course the Count had come to borrow money of me.\n\n“Louis,” I said, “do you think he would go away if you gave him five shillings?”\n\nLouis looked quite shocked. He surprised me inexpressibly by declaring that my sister’s foreign husband was dressed superbly, and looked the picture of prosperity. Under these circumstances my first impression altered to a certain extent. I now took it for granted that the Count had matrimonial difficulties of his own to contend with, and that he had come, like the rest of the family, to cast them all on my shoulders.\n\n“Did he mention his business?” I asked.\n\n“Count Fosco said he had come here, sir, because Miss Halcombe was unable to leave Blackwater Park.”\n\nFresh troubles, apparently. Not exactly his own, as I had supposed, but dear Marian’s. Troubles, anyway. Oh dear!\n\n“Show him in,” I said resignedly.\n\nThe Count’s first appearance really startled me. He was such an alarmingly large person that I quite trembled. I felt certain that he would shake the floor and knock down my art-treasures. He did neither the one nor the other. He was refreshingly dressed in summer costume—his manner was delightfully self-possessed and quiet—he had a charming smile. My first impression of him was highly favourable. It is not creditable to my penetration—as the sequel will show—to acknowledge this, but I am a naturally candid man, and I DO acknowledge it notwithstanding.\n\n“Allow me to present myself, Mr. Fairlie,” he said. “I come from Blackwater Park, and I have the honour and the happiness of being Madame Fosco’s husband. Let me take my first and last advantage of that circumstance by entreating you not to make a stranger of me. I beg you will not disturb yourself—I beg you will not move.”\n\n“You are very good,” I replied. “I wish I was strong enough to get up. Charmed to see you at Limmeridge. Please take a chair.”\n\n“I am afraid you are suffering to-day,” said the Count.\n\n“As usual,” I said. “I am nothing but a bundle of nerves dressed up to look like a man.”\n\n“I have studied many subjects in my time,” remarked this sympathetic person. “Among others the inexhaustible subject of nerves. May I make a suggestion, at once the simplest and the most profound? Will you let me alter the light in your room?”\n\n“Certainly—if you will be so very kind as not to let any of it in on me.”\n\nHe walked to the window. Such a contrast to dear Marian! so extremely considerate in all his movements!\n\n“Light,” he said, in that delightfully confidential tone which is so soothing to an invalid, “is the first essential. Light stimulates, nourishes, preserves. You can no more do without it, Mr. Fairlie, than if you were a flower. Observe. Here, where you sit, I close the shutters to compose you. There, where you do NOT sit, I draw up the blind and let in the invigorating sun. Admit the light into your room if you cannot bear it on yourself. Light, sir, is the grand decree of Providence. You accept Providence with your own restrictions. Accept light on the same terms.”\n\nI thought this very convincing and attentive. He had taken me in up to that point about the light, he had certainly taken me in.\n\n“You see me confused,” he said, returning to his place—“on my word of honour, Mr. Fairlie, you see me confused in your presence.”\n\n“Shocked to hear it, I am sure. May I inquire why?”\n\n“Sir, can I enter this room (where you sit a sufferer), and see you surrounded by these admirable objects of Art, without discovering that you are a man whose feelings are acutely impressionable, whose sympathies are perpetually alive? Tell me, can I do this?”\n\nIf I had been strong enough to sit up in my chair I should, of course, have bowed. Not being strong enough, I smiled my acknowledgments instead. It did just as well, we both understood one another.\n\n“Pray follow my train of thought,” continued the Count. “I sit here, a man of refined sympathies myself, in the presence of another man of refined sympathies also. I am conscious of a terrible necessity for lacerating those sympathies by referring to domestic events of a very melancholy kind. What is the inevitable consequence? I have done myself the honour of pointing it out to you already. I sit confused.”\n\nWas it at this point that I began to suspect he was going to bore me? I rather think it was.\n\n“Is it absolutely necessary to refer to these unpleasant matters?” I inquired. “In our homely English phrase, Count Fosco, won’t they keep?”\n\nThe Count, with the most alarming solemnity, sighed and shook his head.\n\n“Must I really hear them?”\n\nHe shrugged his shoulders (it was the first foreign thing he had done since he had been in the room), and looked at me in an unpleasantly penetrating manner. My instincts told me that I had better close my eyes. I obeyed my instincts.\n\n“Please break it gently,” I pleaded. “Anybody dead?”\n\n“Dead!” cried the Count, with unnecessary foreign fierceness. “Mr. Fairlie, your national composure terrifies me. In the name of Heaven, what have I said or done to make you think me the messenger of death?”\n\n“Pray accept my apologies,” I answered. “You have said and done nothing. I make it a rule in these distressing cases always to anticipate the worst. It breaks the blow by meeting it half-way, and so on. Inexpressibly relieved, I am sure, to hear that nobody is dead. Anybody ill?”\n\nI opened my eyes and looked at him. Was he very yellow when he came in, or had he turned very yellow in the last minute or two? I really can’t say, and I can’t ask Louis, because he was not in the room at the time.\n\n“Anybody ill?” I repeated, observing that my national composure still appeared to affect him.\n\n“That is part of my bad news, Mr. Fairlie. Yes. Somebody is ill.”\n\n“Grieved, I am sure. Which of them is it?”\n\n“To my profound sorrow, Miss Halcombe. Perhaps you were in some degree prepared to hear this? Perhaps when you found that Miss Halcombe did not come here by herself, as you proposed, and did not write a second time, your affectionate anxiety may have made you fear that she was ill?”\n\nI have no doubt my affectionate anxiety had led to that melancholy apprehension at some time or other, but at the moment my wretched memory entirely failed to remind me of the circumstance. However, I said yes, in justice to myself. I was much shocked. It was so very uncharacteristic of such a robust person as dear Marian to be ill, that I could only suppose she had met with an accident. A horse, or a false step on the stairs, or something of that sort.\n\n“Is it serious?” I asked.\n\n“Serious—beyond a doubt,” he replied. “Dangerous—I hope and trust not. Miss Halcombe unhappily exposed herself to be wetted through by a heavy rain. The cold that followed was of an aggravated kind, and it has now brought with it the worst consequence—fever.”\n\nWhen I heard the word fever, and when I remembered at the same moment that the unscrupulous person who was now addressing me had just come from Blackwater Park, I thought I should have fainted on the spot.\n\n“Good God!” I said. “Is it infectious?”\n\n“Not at present,” he answered, with detestable composure. “It may turn to infection—but no such deplorable complication had taken place when I left Blackwater Park. I have felt the deepest interest in the case, Mr. Fairlie—I have endeavoured to assist the regular medical attendant in watching it—accept my personal assurances of the uninfectious nature of the fever when I last saw it.”\n\nAccept his assurances! I never was farther from accepting anything in my life. I would not have believed him on his oath. He was too yellow to be believed. He looked like a walking-West-Indian-epidemic. He was big enough to carry typhus by the ton, and to dye the very carpet he walked on with scarlet fever. In certain emergencies my mind is remarkably soon made up. I instantly determined to get rid of him.\n\n“You will kindly excuse an invalid,” I said—“but long conferences of any kind invariably upset me. May I beg to know exactly what the object is to which I am indebted for the honour of your visit?”\n\nI fervently hoped that this remarkably broad hint would throw him off his balance—confuse him—reduce him to polite apologies—in short, get him out of the room. On the contrary, it only settled him in his chair. He became additionally solemn, and dignified, and confidential. He held up two of his horrid fingers and gave me another of his unpleasantly penetrating looks. What was I to do? I was not strong enough to quarrel with him. Conceive my situation, if you please. Is language adequate to describe it? I think not.\n\n“The objects of my visit,” he went on, quite irrepressibly, “are numbered on my fingers. They are two. First, I come to bear my testimony, with profound sorrow, to the lamentable disagreements between Sir Percival and Lady Glyde. I am Sir Percival’s oldest friend—I am related to Lady Glyde by marriage—I am an eye-witness of all that has happened at Blackwater Park. In those three capacities I speak with authority, with confidence, with honourable regret. Sir, I inform you, as the head of Lady Glyde’s family, that Miss Halcombe has exaggerated nothing in the letter which she wrote to your address. I affirm that the remedy which that admirable lady has proposed is the only remedy that will spare you the horrors of public scandal. A temporary separation between husband and wife is the one peaceable solution of this difficulty. Part them for the present, and when all causes of irritation are removed, I, who have now the honour of addressing you—I will undertake to bring Sir Percival to reason. Lady Glyde is innocent, Lady Glyde is injured, but—follow my thought here!—she is, on that very account (I say it with shame), the cause of irritation while she remains under her husband’s roof. No other house can receive her with propriety but yours. I invite you to open it.”\n\nCool. Here was a matrimonial hailstorm pouring in the South of England, and I was invited, by a man with fever in every fold of his coat, to come out from the North of England and take my share of the pelting. I tried to put the point forcibly, just as I have put it here. The Count deliberately lowered one of his horrid fingers, kept the other up, and went on—rode over me, as it were, without even the common coachmanlike attention of crying “Hi!” before he knocked me down.\n\n“Follow my thought once more, if you please,” he resumed. “My first object you have heard. My second object in coming to this house is to do what Miss Halcombe’s illness has prevented her from doing for herself. My large experience is consulted on all difficult matters at Blackwater Park, and my friendly advice was requested on the interesting subject of your letter to Miss Halcombe. I understood at once—for my sympathies are your sympathies—why you wished to see her here before you pledged yourself to inviting Lady Glyde. You are most right, sir, in hesitating to receive the wife until you are quite certain that the husband will not exert his authority to reclaim her. I agree to that. I also agree that such delicate explanations as this difficulty involves are not explanations which can be properly disposed of by writing only. My presence here (to my own great inconvenience) is the proof that I speak sincerely. As for the explanations themselves, I—Fosco—I, who know Sir Percival much better than Miss Halcombe knows him, affirm to you, on my honour and my word, that he will not come near this house, or attempt to communicate with this house, while his wife is living in it. His affairs are embarrassed. Offer him his freedom by means of the absence of Lady Glyde. I promise you he will take his freedom, and go back to the Continent at the earliest moment when he can get away. Is this clear to you as crystal? Yes, it is. Have you questions to address to me? Be it so, I am here to answer. Ask, Mr. Fairlie—oblige me by asking to your heart’s content.”\n\nHe had said so much already in spite of me, and he looked so dreadfully capable of saying a great deal more also in spite of me, that I declined his amiable invitation in pure self-defence.\n\n“Many thanks,” I replied. “I am sinking fast. In my state of health I must take things for granted. Allow me to do so on this occasion. We quite understand each other. Yes. Much obliged, I am sure, for your kind interference. If I ever get better, and ever have a second opportunity of improving our acquaintance—”\n\nHe got up. I thought he was going. No. More talk, more time for the development of infectious influences—in my room, too—remember that, in my room!\n\n“One moment yet,” he said, “one moment before I take my leave. I ask permission at parting to impress on you an urgent necessity. It is this, sir. You must not think of waiting till Miss Halcombe recovers before you receive Lady Glyde. Miss Halcombe has the attendance of the doctor, of the housekeeper at Blackwater Park, and of an experienced nurse as well—three persons for whose capacity and devotion I answer with my life. I tell you that. I tell you, also, that the anxiety and alarm of her sister’s illness has already affected the health and spirits of Lady Glyde, and has made her totally unfit to be of use in the sick-room. Her position with her husband grows more and more deplorable and dangerous every day. If you leave her any longer at Blackwater Park, you do nothing whatever to hasten her sister’s recovery, and at the same time, you risk the public scandal, which you and I, and all of us, are bound in the sacred interests of the family to avoid. With all my soul, I advise you to remove the serious responsibility of delay from your own shoulders by writing to Lady Glyde to come here at once. Do your affectionate, your honourable, your inevitable duty, and whatever happens in the future, no one can lay the blame on you. I speak from my large experience—I offer my friendly advice. Is it accepted—Yes, or No?”\n\nI looked at him—merely looked at him—with my sense of his amazing assurance, and my dawning resolution to ring for Louis and have him shown out of the room expressed in every line of my face. It is perfectly incredible, but quite true, that my face did not appear to produce the slightest impression on him. Born without nerves—evidently born without nerves.\n\n“You hesitate?” he said. “Mr. Fairlie! I understand that hesitation. You object—see, sir, how my sympathies look straight down into your thoughts!—you object that Lady Glyde is not in health and not in spirits to take the long journey, from Hampshire to this place, by herself. Her own maid is removed from her, as you know, and of other servants fit to travel with her, from one end of England to another, there are none at Blackwater Park. You object, again, that she cannot comfortably stop and rest in London, on her way here, because she cannot comfortably go alone to a public hotel where she is a total stranger. In one breath, I grant both objections—in another breath, I remove them. Follow me, if you please, for the last time. It was my intention, when I returned to England with Sir Percival, to settle myself in the neighbourhood of London. That purpose has just been happily accomplished. I have taken, for six months, a little furnished house in the quarter called St. John’s Wood. Be so obliging as to keep this fact in your mind, and observe the programme I now propose. Lady Glyde travels to London (a short journey)—I myself meet her at the station—I take her to rest and sleep at my house, which is also the house of her aunt—when she is restored I escort her to the station again—she travels to this place, and her own maid (who is now under your roof) receives her at the carriage-door. Here is comfort consulted—here are the interests of propriety consulted—here is your own duty—duty of hospitality, sympathy, protection, to an unhappy lady in need of all three—smoothed and made easy, from the beginning to the end. I cordially invite you, sir, to second my efforts in the sacred interests of the family. I seriously advise you to write, by my hands, offering the hospitality of your house (and heart), and the hospitality of my house (and heart), to that injured and unfortunate lady whose cause I plead to-day.”\n\nHe waved his horrid hand at me—he struck his infectious breast—he addressed me oratorically, as if I was laid up in the House of Commons. It was high time to take a desperate course of some sort. It was also high time to send for Louis, and adopt the precaution of fumigating the room.\n\nIn this trying emergency an idea occurred to me—an inestimable idea which, so to speak, killed two intrusive birds with one stone. I determined to get rid of the Count’s tiresome eloquence, and of Lady Glyde’s tiresome troubles, by complying with this odious foreigner’s request, and writing the letter at once. There was not the least danger of the invitation being accepted, for there was not the least chance that Laura would consent to leave Blackwater Park while Marian was lying there ill. How this charmingly convenient obstacle could have escaped the officious penetration of the Count, it was impossible to conceive—but it HAD escaped him. My dread that he might yet discover it, if I allowed him any more time to think, stimulated me to such an amazing degree, that I struggled into a sitting position—seized, really seized, the writing materials by my side, and produced the letter as rapidly as if I had been a common clerk in an office. “Dearest Laura, Please come, whenever you like. Break the journey by sleeping in London at your aunt’s house. Grieved to hear of dear Marian’s illness. Ever affectionately yours.” I handed these lines, at arm’s length, to the Count—I sank back in my chair—I said, “Excuse me—I am entirely prostrated—I can do no more. Will you rest and lunch downstairs? Love to all, and sympathy, and so on. Good-morning.”\n\nHe made another speech—the man was absolutely inexhaustible. I closed my eyes—I endeavoured to hear as little as possible. In spite of my endeavours I was obliged to hear a great deal. My sister’s endless husband congratulated himself, and congratulated me, on the result of our interview—he mentioned a great deal more about his sympathies and mine—he deplored my miserable health—he offered to write me a prescription—he impressed on me the necessity of not forgetting what he had said about the importance of light—he accepted my obliging invitation to rest and lunch—he recommended me to expect Lady Glyde in two or three days’ time—he begged my permission to look forward to our next meeting, instead of paining himself and paining me, by saying farewell—he added a great deal more, which, I rejoice to think, I did not attend to at the time, and do not remember now. I heard his sympathetic voice travelling away from me by degrees—but, large as he was, I never heard him. He had the negative merit of being absolutely noiseless. I don’t know when he opened the door, or when he shut it. I ventured to make use of my eyes again, after an interval of silence—and he was gone.\n\nI rang for Louis, and retired to my bathroom. Tepid water, strengthened with aromatic vinegar, for myself, and copious fumigation for my study, were the obvious precautions to take, and of course I adopted them. I rejoice to say they proved successful. I enjoyed my customary siesta. I awoke moist and cool.\n\nMy first inquiries were for the Count. Had we really got rid of him? Yes—he had gone away by the afternoon train. Had he lunched, and if so, upon what? Entirely upon fruit-tart and cream. What a man! What a digestion!\n\nAm I expected to say anything more? I believe not. I believe I have reached the limits assigned to me. The shocking circumstances which happened at a later period did not, I am thankful to say, happen in my presence. I do beg and entreat that nobody will be so very unfeeling as to lay any part of the blame of those circumstances on me. I did everything for the best. I am not answerable for a deplorable calamity, which it was quite impossible to foresee. I am shattered by it—I have suffered under it, as nobody else has suffered. My servant, Louis (who is really attached to me in his unintelligent way), thinks I shall never get over it. He sees me dictating at this moment, with my handkerchief to my eyes. I wish to mention, in justice to myself, that it was not my fault, and that I am quite exhausted and heartbroken. Need I say more?\n\n(Housekeeper at Blackwater Park)\n\n# I\n\nI am asked to state plainly what I know of the progress of Miss Halcombe’s illness and of the circumstances under which Lady Glyde left Blackwater Park for London.\n\nThe reason given for making this demand on me is, that my testimony is wanted in the interests of truth. As the widow of a clergyman of the Church of England (reduced by misfortune to the necessity of accepting a situation), I have been taught to place the claims of truth above all other considerations. I therefore comply with a request which I might otherwise, through reluctance to connect myself with distressing family affairs, have hesitated to grant.\n\nI made no memorandum at the time, and I cannot therefore be sure to a day of the date, but I believe I am correct in stating that Miss Halcombe’s serious illness began during the last fortnight or ten days in June. The breakfast hour was late at Blackwater Park—sometimes as late as ten, never earlier than half-past nine. On the morning to which I am now referring, Miss Halcombe (who was usually the first to come down) did not make her appearance at the table. After the family had waited a quarter of an hour, the upper housemaid was sent to see after her, and came running out of the room dreadfully frightened. I met the servant on the stairs, and went at once to Miss Halcombe to see what was the matter. The poor lady was incapable of telling me. She was walking about her room with a pen in her hand, quite light-headed, in a state of burning fever.\n\nLady Glyde (being no longer in Sir Percival’s service, I may, without impropriety, mention my former mistress by her name, instead of calling her my lady) was the first to come in from her own bedroom. She was so dreadfully alarmed and distressed that she was quite useless. The Count Fosco, and his lady, who came upstairs immediately afterwards, were both most serviceable and kind. Her ladyship assisted me to get Miss Halcombe to her bed. His lordship the Count remained in the sitting-room, and having sent for my medicine-chest, made a mixture for Miss Halcombe, and a cooling lotion to be applied to her head, so as to lose no time before the doctor came. We applied the lotion, but we could not get her to take the mixture. Sir Percival undertook to send for the doctor. He despatched a groom, on horseback, for the nearest medical man, Mr. Dawson, of Oak Lodge.\n\nMr. Dawson arrived in less than an hour’s time. He was a respectable elderly man, well known all round the country, and we were much alarmed when we found that he considered the case to be a very serious one.\n\nHis lordship the Count affably entered into conversation with Mr. Dawson, and gave his opinions with a judicious freedom. Mr. Dawson, not over-courteously, inquired if his lordship’s advice was the advice of a doctor, and being informed that it was the advice of one who had studied medicine unprofessionally, replied that he was not accustomed to consult with amateur physicians. The Count, with truly Christian meekness of temper, smiled and left the room. Before he went out he told me that he might be found, in case he was wanted in the course of the day, at the boat-house on the banks of the lake. Why he should have gone there, I cannot say. But he did go, remaining away the whole day till seven o’clock, which was dinner-time. Perhaps he wished to set the example of keeping the house as quiet as possible. It was entirely in his character to do so. He was a most considerate nobleman.\n\nMiss Halcombe passed a very bad night, the fever coming and going, and getting worse towards the morning instead of better. No nurse fit to wait on her being at hand in the neighbourhood, her ladyship the Countess and myself undertook the duty, relieving each other. Lady Glyde, most unwisely, insisted on sitting up with us. She was much too nervous and too delicate in health to bear the anxiety of Miss Halcombe’s illness calmly. She only did herself harm, without being of the least real assistance. A more gentle and affectionate lady never lived—but she cried, and she was frightened, two weaknesses which made her entirely unfit to be present in a sick-room.\n\nSir Percival and the Count came in the morning to make their inquiries.\n\nSir Percival (from distress, I presume, at his lady’s affliction and at Miss Halcombe’s illness) appeared much confused and unsettled in his mind. His lordship testified, on the contrary, a becoming composure and interest. He had his straw hat in one hand, and his book in the other, and he mentioned to Sir Percival in my hearing that he would go out again and study at the lake. “Let us keep the house quiet,” he said. “Let us not smoke indoors, my friend, now Miss Halcombe is ill. You go your way, and I will go mine. When I study I like to be alone. Good-morning, Mrs. Michelson.”\n\nSir Percival was not civil enough—perhaps I ought in justice to say, not composed enough—to take leave of me with the same polite attention. The only person in the house, indeed, who treated me, at that time or at any other, on the footing of a lady in distressed circumstances, was the Count. He had the manners of a true nobleman—he was considerate towards every one. Even the young person (Fanny by name) who attended on Lady Glyde was not beneath his notice. When she was sent away by Sir Percival, his lordship (showing me his sweet little birds at the time) was most kindly anxious to know what had become of her, where she was to go the day she left Blackwater Park, and so on. It is in such little delicate attentions that the advantages of aristocratic birth always show themselves. I make no apology for introducing these particulars—they are brought forward in justice to his lordship, whose character, I have reason to know, is viewed rather harshly in certain quarters. A nobleman who can respect a lady in distressed circumstances, and can take a fatherly interest in the fortunes of an humble servant girl, shows principles and feelings of too high an order to be lightly called in question. I advance no opinions—I offer facts only. My endeavour through life is to judge not that I be not judged. One of my beloved husband’s finest sermons was on that text. I read it constantly—in my own copy of the edition printed by subscription, in the first days of my widowhood—and at every fresh perusal I derive an increase of spiritual benefit and edification.\n\nThere was no improvement in Miss Halcombe, and the second night was even worse than the first. Mr. Dawson was constant in his attendance. The practical duties of nursing were still divided between the Countess and myself, Lady Glyde persisting in sitting up with us, though we both entreated her to take some rest. “My place is by Marian’s bedside,” was her only answer. “Whether I am ill, or well, nothing will induce me to lose sight of her.”\n\nTowards midday I went downstairs to attend to some of my regular duties. An hour afterwards, on my way back to the sick-room, I saw the Count (who had gone out again early, for the third time) entering the hall, to all appearance in the highest good spirits. Sir Percival, at the same moment, put his head out of the library door, and addressed his noble friend, with extreme eagerness, in these words—\n\n“Have you found her?”\n\nHis lordship’s large face became dimpled all over with placid smiles, but he made no reply in words. At the same time Sir Percival turned his head, observed that I was approaching the stairs, and looked at me in the most rudely angry manner possible.\n\n“Come in here and tell me about it,” he said to the Count. “Whenever there are women in a house they’re always sure to be going up or down stairs.”\n\n“My dear Percival,” observed his lordship kindly, “Mrs. Michelson has duties. Pray recognise her admirable performance of them as sincerely as I do! How is the sufferer, Mrs. Michelson?”\n\n“No better, my lord, I regret to say.”\n\n“Sad—most sad!” remarked the Count. “You look fatigued, Mrs. Michelson. It is certainly time you and my wife had some help in nursing. I think I may be the means of offering you that help. Circumstances have happened which will oblige Madame Fosco to travel to London either to-morrow or the day after. She will go away in the morning and return at night, and she will bring back with her, to relieve you, a nurse of excellent conduct and capacity, who is now disengaged. The woman is known to my wife as a person to be trusted. Before she comes here say nothing about her, if you please, to the doctor, because he will look with an evil eye on any nurse of my providing. When she appears in this house she will speak for herself, and Mr. Dawson will be obliged to acknowledge that there is no excuse for not employing her. Lady Glyde will say the same. Pray present my best respects and sympathies to Lady Glyde.”\n\nI expressed my grateful acknowledgments for his lordship’s kind consideration. Sir Percival cut them short by calling to his noble friend (using, I regret to say, a profane expression) to come into the library, and not to keep him waiting there any longer.\n\nI proceeded upstairs. We are poor erring creatures, and however well established a woman’s principles may be she cannot always keep on her guard against the temptation to exercise an idle curiosity. I am ashamed to say that an idle curiosity, on this occasion, got the better of my principles, and made me unduly inquisitive about the question which Sir Percival had addressed to his noble friend at the library door. Who was the Count expected to find in the course of his studious morning rambles at Blackwater Park? A woman, it was to be presumed, from the terms of Sir Percival’s inquiry. I did not suspect the Count of any impropriety—I knew his moral character too well. The only question I asked myself was—Had he found her?\n\nTo resume. The night passed as usual without producing any change for the better in Miss Halcombe. The next day she seemed to improve a little. The day after that her ladyship the Countess, without mentioning the object of her journey to any one in my hearing, proceeded by the morning train to London—her noble husband, with his customary attention, accompanying her to the station.\n\nI was now left in sole charge of Miss Halcombe, with every apparent chance, in consequence of her sister’s resolution not to leave the bedside, of having Lady Glyde herself to nurse next.\n\nThe only circumstance of any importance that happened in the course of the day was the occurrence of another unpleasant meeting between the doctor and the Count.\n\nHis lordship, on returning from the station, stepped up into Miss Halcombe’s sitting-room to make his inquiries. I went out from the bedroom to speak to him, Mr. Dawson and Lady Glyde being both with the patient at the time. The Count asked me many questions about the treatment and the symptoms. I informed him that the treatment was of the kind described as “saline,” and that the symptoms, between the attacks of fever, were certainly those of increasing weakness and exhaustion. Just as I was mentioning these last particulars, Mr. Dawson came out from the bedroom.\n\n“Good-morning, sir,” said his lordship, stepping forward in the most urbane manner, and stopping the doctor, with a high-bred resolution impossible to resist, “I greatly fear you find no improvement in the symptoms to-day?”\n\n“I find decided improvement,” answered Mr. Dawson.\n\n“You still persist in your lowering treatment of this case of fever?” continued his lordship.\n\n“I persist in the treatment which is justified by my own professional experience,” said Mr. Dawson.\n\n“Permit me to put one question to you on the vast subject of professional experience,” observed the Count. “I presume to offer no more advice—I only presume to make an inquiry. You live at some distance, sir, from the gigantic centres of scientific activity—London and Paris. Have you ever heard of the wasting effects of fever being reasonably and intelligibly repaired by fortifying the exhausted patient with brandy, wine, ammonia, and quinine? Has that new heresy of the highest medical authorities ever reached your ears—Yes or No?”\n\n“When a professional man puts that question to me I shall be glad to answer him,” said the doctor, opening the door to go out. “You are not a professional man, and I beg to decline answering you.”\n\nBuffeted in this inexcusably uncivil way on one cheek, the Count, like a practical Christian, immediately turned the other, and said, in the sweetest manner, “Good-morning, Mr. Dawson.”\n\nIf my late beloved husband had been so fortunate as to know his lordship, how highly he and the Count would have esteemed each other!\n\nHer ladyship the Countess returned by the last train that night, and brought with her the nurse from London. I was instructed that this person’s name was Mrs. Rubelle. Her personal appearance, and her imperfect English when she spoke, informed me that she was a foreigner.\n\nI have always cultivated a feeling of humane indulgence for foreigners. They do not possess our blessings and advantages, and they are, for the most part, brought up in the blind errors of Popery. It has also always been my precept and practice, as it was my dear husband’s precept and practice before me (see Sermon XXIX. in the Collection by the late Rev. Samuel Michelson, M.A.), to do as I would be done by. On both these accounts I will not say that Mrs. Rubelle struck me as being a small, wiry, sly person, of fifty or thereabouts, with a dark brown or Creole complexion and watchful light grey eyes. Nor will I mention, for the reasons just alleged, that I thought her dress, though it was of the plainest black silk, inappropriately costly in texture and unnecessarily refined in trimming and finish, for a person in her position in life. I should not like these things to be said of me, and therefore it is my duty not to say them of Mrs. Rubelle. I will merely mention that her manners were, not perhaps unpleasantly reserved, but only remarkably quiet and retiring—that she looked about her a great deal, and said very little, which might have arisen quite as much from her own modesty as from distrust of her position at Blackwater Park; and that she declined to partake of supper (which was curious perhaps, but surely not suspicious?), although I myself politely invited her to that meal in my own room.\n\nAt the Count’s particular suggestion (so like his lordship’s forgiving kindness!), it was arranged that Mrs. Rubelle should not enter on her duties until she had been seen and approved by the doctor the next morning. I sat up that night. Lady Glyde appeared to be very unwilling that the new nurse should be employed to attend on Miss Halcombe. Such want of liberality towards a foreigner on the part of a lady of her education and refinement surprised me. I ventured to say, “My lady, we must all remember not to be hasty in our judgments on our inferiors—especially when they come from foreign parts.” Lady Glyde did not appear to attend to me. She only sighed, and kissed Miss Halcombe’s hand as it lay on the counterpane. Scarcely a judicious proceeding in a sick-room, with a patient whom it was highly desirable not to excite. But poor Lady Glyde knew nothing of nursing—nothing whatever, I am sorry to say.\n\nThe next morning Mrs. Rubelle was sent to the sitting-room, to be approved by the doctor on his way through to the bedroom.\n\nI left Lady Glyde with Miss Halcombe, who was slumbering at the time, and joined Mrs. Rubelle, with the object of kindly preventing her from feeling strange and nervous in consequence of the uncertainty of her situation. She did not appear to see it in that light. She seemed to be quite satisfied, beforehand, that Mr. Dawson would approve of her, and she sat calmly looking out of window, with every appearance of enjoying the country air. Some people might have thought such conduct suggestive of brazen assurance. I beg to say that I more liberally set it down to extraordinary strength of mind.\n\nInstead of the doctor coming up to us, I was sent for to see the doctor. I thought this change of affairs rather odd, but Mrs. Rubelle did not appear to be affected by it in any way. I left her still calmly looking out of the window, and still silently enjoying the country air.\n\nMr. Dawson was waiting for me by himself in the breakfast-room.\n\n“About this new nurse, Mrs. Michelson,” said the doctor.\n\n“Yes, sir?”\n\n“I find that she has been brought here from London by the wife of that fat old foreigner, who is always trying to interfere with me. Mrs. Michelson, the fat old foreigner is a quack.”\n\nThis was very rude. I was naturally shocked at it.\n\n“Are you aware, sir,” I said, “that you are talking of a nobleman?”\n\n“Pooh! He isn’t the first quack with a handle to his name. They’re all Counts—hang ’em!”\n\n“He would not be a friend of Sir Percival Glyde’s, sir, if he was not a member of the highest aristocracy—excepting the English aristocracy, of course.”\n\n“Very well, Mrs. Michelson, call him what you like, and let us get back to the nurse. I have been objecting to her already.”\n\n“Without having seen her, sir?”\n\n“Yes, without having seen her. She may be the best nurse in existence, but she is not a nurse of my providing. I have put that objection to Sir Percival, as the master of the house. He doesn’t support me. He says a nurse of my providing would have been a stranger from London also, and he thinks the woman ought to have a trial, after his wife’s aunt has taken the trouble to fetch her from London. There is some justice in that, and I can’t decently say No. But I have made it a condition that she is to go at once, if I find reason to complain of her. This proposal being one which I have some right to make, as medical attendant, Sir Percival has consented to it. Now, Mrs. Michelson, I know I can depend on you, and I want you to keep a sharp eye on the nurse for the first day or two, and to see that she gives Miss Halcombe no medicines but mine. This foreign nobleman of yours is dying to try his quack remedies (mesmerism included) on my patient, and a nurse who is brought here by his wife may be a little too willing to help him. You understand? Very well, then, we may go upstairs. Is the nurse there? I’ll say a word to her before she goes into the sick-room.”\n\nWe found Mrs. Rubelle still enjoying herself at the window. When I introduced her to Mr. Dawson, neither the doctor’s doubtful looks nor the doctor’s searching questions appeared to confuse her in the least. She answered him quietly in her broken English, and though he tried hard to puzzle her, she never betrayed the least ignorance, so far, about any part of her duties. This was doubtless the result of strength of mind, as I said before, and not of brazen assurance, by any means.\n\nWe all went into the bedroom.\n\nMrs. Rubelle looked very attentively at the patient, curtseyed to Lady Glyde, set one or two little things right in the room, and sat down quietly in a corner to wait until she was wanted. Her ladyship seemed startled and annoyed by the appearance of the strange nurse. No one said anything, for fear of rousing Miss Halcombe, who was still slumbering, except the doctor, who whispered a question about the night. I softly answered, “Much as usual,” and then Mr. Dawson went out. Lady Glyde followed him, I suppose to speak about Mrs. Rubelle. For my own part, I had made up my mind already that this quiet foreign person would keep her situation. She had all her wits about her, and she certainly understood her business. So far, I could hardly have done much better by the bedside myself.\n\nRemembering Mr. Dawson’s caution to me, I subjected Mrs. Rubelle to a severe scrutiny at certain intervals for the next three or four days. I over and over again entered the room softly and suddenly, but I never found her out in any suspicious action. Lady Glyde, who watched her as attentively as I did, discovered nothing either. I never detected a sign of the medicine bottles being tampered with, I never saw Mrs. Rubelle say a word to the Count, or the Count to her. She managed Miss Halcombe with unquestionable care and discretion. The poor lady wavered backwards and forwards between a sort of sleepy exhaustion, which was half faintness and half slumbering, and attacks of fever which brought with them more or less of wandering in her mind. Mrs. Rubelle never disturbed her in the first case, and never startled her in the second, by appearing too suddenly at the bedside in the character of a stranger. Honour to whom honour is due (whether foreign or English)—and I give her privilege impartially to Mrs. Rubelle. She was remarkably uncommunicative about herself, and she was too quietly independent of all advice from experienced persons who understood the duties of a sick-room—but with these drawbacks, she was a good nurse, and she never gave either Lady Glyde or Mr. Dawson the shadow of a reason for complaining of her.\n\nThe next circumstance of importance that occurred in the house was the temporary absence of the Count, occasioned by business which took him to London. He went away (I think) on the morning of the fourth day after the arrival of Mrs. Rubelle, and at parting he spoke to Lady Glyde very seriously, in my presence, on the subject of Miss Halcombe.\n\n“Trust Mr. Dawson,” he said, “for a few days more, if you please. But if there is not some change for the better in that time, send for advice from London, which this mule of a doctor must accept in spite of himself. Offend Mr. Dawson, and save Miss Halcombe. I say this seriously, on my word of honour and from the bottom of my heart.”\n\nHis lordship spoke with extreme feeling and kindness. But poor Lady Glyde’s nerves were so completely broken down that she seemed quite frightened at him. She trembled from head to foot, and allowed him to take his leave without uttering a word on her side. She turned to me when he had gone, and said, “Oh, Mrs. Michelson, I am heartbroken about my sister, and I have no friend to advise me! Do you think Mr. Dawson is wrong? He told me himself this morning that there was no fear, and no need to send for another doctor.”\n\n“With all respect to Mr. Dawson,” I answered, “in your ladyship’s place I should remember the Count’s advice.”\n\nLady Glyde turned away from me suddenly, with an appearance of despair, for which I was quite unable to account.\n\n“HIS advice!” she said to herself. “God help us—HIS advice!”\n\nThe Count was away from Blackwater Park, as nearly as I remember, a week.\n\nSir Percival seemed to feel the loss of his lordship in various ways, and appeared also, I thought, much depressed and altered by the sickness and sorrow in the house. Occasionally he was so very restless that I could not help noticing it, coming and going, and wandering here and there and everywhere in the grounds. His inquiries about Miss Halcombe, and about his lady (whose failing health seemed to cause him sincere anxiety), were most attentive. I think his heart was much softened. If some kind clerical friend—some such friend as he might have found in my late excellent husband—had been near him at this time, cheering moral progress might have been made with Sir Percival. I seldom find myself mistaken on a point of this sort, having had experience to guide me in my happy married days.\n\nHer ladyship the Countess, who was now the only company for Sir Percival downstairs, rather neglected him, as I considered—or, perhaps, it might have been that he neglected her. A stranger might almost have supposed that they were bent, now they were left together alone, on actually avoiding one another. This, of course, could not be. But it did so happen, nevertheless, that the Countess made her dinner at luncheon-time, and that she always came upstairs towards evening, although Mrs. Rubelle had taken the nursing duties entirely off her hands. Sir Percival dined by himself, and William (the man out of livery) made the remark, in my hearing, that his master had put himself on half rations of food and on a double allowance of drink. I attach no importance to such an insolent observation as this on the part of a servant. I reprobated it at the time, and I wish to be understood as reprobating it once more on this occasion.\n\nIn the course of the next few days Miss Halcombe did certainly seem to all of us to be mending a little. Our faith in Mr. Dawson revived. He appeared to be very confident about the case, and he assured Lady Glyde, when she spoke to him on the subject, that he would himself propose to send for a physician the moment he felt so much as the shadow of a doubt crossing his own mind.\n\nThe only person among us who did not appear to be relieved by these words was the Countess. She said to me privately, that she could not feel easy about Miss Halcombe on Mr. Dawson’s authority, and that she should wait anxiously for her husband’s opinion on his return. That return, his letters informed her, would take place in three days’ time. The Count and Countess corresponded regularly every morning during his lordship’s absence. They were in that respect, as in all others, a pattern to married people.\n\nOn the evening of the third day I noticed a change in Miss Halcombe, which caused me serious apprehension. Mrs. Rubelle noticed it too. We said nothing on the subject to Lady Glyde, who was then lying asleep, completely overpowered by exhaustion, on the sofa in the sitting-room.\n\nMr. Dawson did not pay his evening visit till later than usual. As soon as he set eyes on his patient I saw his face alter. He tried to hide it, but he looked both confused and alarmed. A messenger was sent to his residence for his medicine-chest, disinfecting preparations were used in the room, and a bed was made up for him in the house by his own directions. “Has the fever turned to infection?” I whispered to him. “I am afraid it has,” he answered; “we shall know better to-morrow morning.”\n\nBy Mr. Dawson’s own directions Lady Glyde was kept in ignorance of this change for the worse. He himself absolutely forbade her, on account of her health, to join us in the bedroom that night. She tried to resist—there was a sad scene—but he had his medical authority to support him, and he carried his point.\n\nThe next morning one of the men-servants was sent to London at eleven o’clock, with a letter to a physician in town, and with orders to bring the new doctor back with him by the earliest possible train. Half an hour after the messenger had gone the Count returned to Blackwater Park.\n\nThe Countess, on her own responsibility, immediately brought him in to see the patient. There was no impropriety that I could discover in her taking this course. His lordship was a married man, he was old enough to be Miss Halcombe’s father, and he saw her in the presence of a female relative, Lady Glyde’s aunt. Mr. Dawson nevertheless protested against his presence in the room, but I could plainly remark the doctor was too much alarmed to make any serious resistance on this occasion.\n\nThe poor suffering lady was past knowing any one about her. She seemed to take her friends for enemies. When the Count approached her bedside her eyes, which had been wandering incessantly round and round the room before, settled on his face with a dreadful stare of terror, which I shall remember to my dying day. The Count sat down by her, felt her pulse and her temples, looked at her very attentively, and then turned round upon the doctor with such an expression of indignation and contempt in his face, that the words failed on Mr. Dawson’s lips, and he stood for a moment, pale with anger and alarm—pale and perfectly speechless.\n\nHis lordship looked next at me.\n\n“When did the change happen?” he asked.\n\nI told him the time.\n\n“Has Lady Glyde been in the room since?”\n\nI replied that she had not. The doctor had absolutely forbidden her to come into the room on the evening before, and had repeated the order again in the morning.\n\n“Have you and Mrs. Rubelle been made aware of the full extent of the mischief?” was his next question.\n\nWe were aware, I answered, that the malady was considered infectious. He stopped me before I could add anything more.\n\n“It is typhus fever,” he said.\n\nIn the minute that passed, while these questions and answers were going on, Mr. Dawson recovered himself, and addressed the Count with his customary firmness.\n\n“It is NOT typhus fever,” he remarked sharply. “I protest against this intrusion, sir. No one has a right to put questions here but me. I have done my duty to the best of my ability—”\n\nThe Count interrupted him—not by words, but only by pointing to the bed. Mr. Dawson seemed to feel that silent contradiction to his assertion of his own ability, and to grow only the more angry under it.\n\n“I say I have done my duty,” he reiterated. “A physician has been sent for from London. I will consult on the nature of the fever with him, and with no one else. I insist on your leaving the room.”\n\n“I entered this room, sir, in the sacred interests of humanity,” said the Count. “And in the same interests, if the coming of the physician is delayed, I will enter it again. I warn you once more that the fever has turned to typhus, and that your treatment is responsible for this lamentable change. If that unhappy lady dies, I will give my testimony in a court of justice that your ignorance and obstinacy have been the cause of her death.”\n\nBefore Mr. Dawson could answer, before the Count could leave us, the door was opened from the sitting-room, and we saw Lady Glyde on the threshold.\n\n“I MUST and WILL come in,” she said, with extraordinary firmness.\n\nInstead of stopping her, the Count moved into the sitting-room, and made way for her to go in. On all other occasions he was the last man in the world to forget anything, but in the surprise of the moment he apparently forgot the danger of infection from typhus, and the urgent necessity of forcing Lady Glyde to take proper care of herself.\n\nTo my astonishment Mr. Dawson showed more presence of mind. He stopped her ladyship at the first step she took towards the bedside. “I am sincerely sorry, I am sincerely grieved,” he said. “The fever may, I fear, be infectious. Until I am certain that it is not, I entreat you to keep out of the room.”\n\nShe struggled for a moment, then suddenly dropped her arms and sank forward. She had fainted. The Countess and I took her from the doctor and carried her into her own room. The Count preceded us, and waited in the passage till I came out and told him that we had recovered her from the swoon.\n\nI went back to the doctor to tell him, by Lady Glyde’s desire, that she insisted on speaking to him immediately. He withdrew at once to quiet her ladyship’s agitation, and to assure her of the physician’s arrival in the course of a few hours. Those hours passed very slowly. Sir Percival and the Count were together downstairs, and sent up from time to time to make their inquiries. At last, between five and six o’clock, to our great relief, the physician came.\n\nHe was a younger man than Mr. Dawson, very serious and very decided. What he thought of the previous treatment I cannot say, but it struck me as curious that he put many more questions to myself and to Mrs. Rubelle than he put to the doctor, and that he did not appear to listen with much interest to what Mr. Dawson said, while he was examining Mr. Dawson’s patient. I began to suspect, from what I observed in this way, that the Count had been right about the illness all the way through, and I was naturally confirmed in that idea when Mr. Dawson, after some little delay, asked the one important question which the London doctor had been sent for to set at rest.\n\n“What is your opinion of the fever?” he inquired.\n\n“Typhus,” replied the physician. “Typhus fever beyond all doubt.”\n\nThat quiet foreign person, Mrs. Rubelle, crossed her thin brown hands in front of her, and looked at me with a very significant smile. The Count himself could hardly have appeared more gratified if he had been present in the room and had heard the confirmation of his own opinion.\n\nAfter giving us some useful directions about the management of the patient, and mentioning that he would come again in five days’ time, the physician withdrew to consult in private with Mr. Dawson. He would offer no opinion on Miss Halcombe’s chances of recovery—he said it was impossible at that stage of the illness to pronounce one way or the other.\n\nThe five days passed anxiously.\n\nCountess Fosco and myself took it by turns to relieve Mrs. Rubelle, Miss Halcombe’s condition growing worse and worse, and requiring our utmost care and attention. It was a terribly trying time. Lady Glyde (supported, as Mr. Dawson said, by the constant strain of her suspense on her sister’s account) rallied in the most extraordinary manner, and showed a firmness and determination for which I should myself never have given her credit. She insisted on coming into the sick-room two or three times every day, to look at Miss Halcombe with her own eyes, promising not to go too close to the bed, if the doctor would consent to her wishes so far. Mr. Dawson very unwillingly made the concession required of him—I think he saw that it was hopeless to dispute with her. She came in every day, and she self-denyingly kept her promise. I felt it personally so distressing (as reminding me of my own affliction during my husband’s last illness) to see how she suffered under these circumstances, that I must beg not to dwell on this part of the subject any longer. It is more agreeable to me to mention that no fresh disputes took place between Mr. Dawson and the Count. His lordship made all his inquiries by deputy, and remained continually in company with Sir Percival downstairs.\n\nOn the fifth day the physician came again and gave us a little hope. He said the tenth day from the first appearance of the typhus would probably decide the result of the illness, and he arranged for his third visit to take place on that date. The interval passed as before—except that the Count went to London again one morning and returned at night.\n\nOn the tenth day it pleased a merciful Providence to relieve our household from all further anxiety and alarm. The physician positively assured us that Miss Halcombe was out of danger. “She wants no doctor now—all she requires is careful watching and nursing for some time to come, and that I see she has.” Those were his own words. That evening I read my husband’s touching sermon on Recovery from Sickness, with more happiness and advantage (in a spiritual point of view) than I ever remember to have derived from it before.\n\nThe effect of the good news on poor Lady Glyde was, I grieve to say, quite overpowering. She was too weak to bear the violent reaction, and in another day or two she sank into a state of debility and depression which obliged her to keep her room. Rest and quiet, and change of air afterwards, were the best remedies which Mr. Dawson could suggest for her benefit. It was fortunate that matters were no worse, for, on the very day after she took to her room, the Count and the doctor had another disagreement—and this time the dispute between them was of so serious a nature that Mr. Dawson left the house.\n\nI was not present at the time, but I understood that the subject of dispute was the amount of nourishment which it was necessary to give to assist Miss Halcombe’s convalescence after the exhaustion of the fever. Mr. Dawson, now that his patient was safe, was less inclined than ever to submit to unprofessional interference, and the Count (I cannot imagine why) lost all the self-control which he had so judiciously preserved on former occasions, and taunted the doctor, over and over again, with his mistake about the fever when it changed to typhus. The unfortunate affair ended in Mr. Dawson’s appealing to Sir Percival, and threatening (now that he could leave without absolute danger to Miss Halcombe) to withdraw from his attendance at Blackwater Park if the Count’s interference was not peremptorily suppressed from that moment. Sir Percival’s reply (though not designedly uncivil) had only resulted in making matters worse, and Mr. Dawson had thereupon withdrawn from the house in a state of extreme indignation at Count Fosco’s usage of him, and had sent in his bill the next morning.\n\nWe were now, therefore, left without the attendance of a medical man. Although there was no actual necessity for another doctor—nursing and watching being, as the physician had observed, all that Miss Halcombe required—I should still, if my authority had been consulted, have obtained professional assistance from some other quarter, for form’s sake.\n\nThe matter did not seem to strike Sir Percival in that light. He said it would be time enough to send for another doctor if Miss Halcombe showed any signs of a relapse. In the meanwhile we had the Count to consult in any minor difficulty, and we need not unnecessarily disturb our patient in her present weak and nervous condition by the presence of a stranger at her bedside. There was much that was reasonable, no doubt, in these considerations, but they left me a little anxious nevertheless. Nor was I quite satisfied in my own mind of the propriety of our concealing the doctor’s absence as we did from Lady Glyde. It was a merciful deception, I admit—for she was in no state to bear any fresh anxieties. But still it was a deception, and, as such, to a person of my principles, at best a doubtful proceeding.\n\nA second perplexing circumstance which happened on the same day, and which took me completely by surprise, added greatly to the sense of uneasiness that was now weighing on my mind.\n\nI was sent for to see Sir Percival in the library. The Count, who was with him when I went in, immediately rose and left us alone together. Sir Percival civilly asked me to take a seat, and then, to my great astonishment, addressed me in these terms—\n\n“I want to speak to you, Mrs. Michelson, about a matter which I decided on some time ago, and which I should have mentioned before, but for the sickness and trouble in the house. In plain words, I have reasons for wishing to break up my establishment immediately at this place—leaving you in charge, of course, as usual. As soon as Lady Glyde and Miss Halcombe can travel they must both have change of air. My friends, Count Fosco and the Countess, will leave us before that time to live in the neighbourhood of London, and I have reasons for not opening the house to any more company, with a view to economising as carefully as I can. I don’t blame you, but my expenses here are a great deal too heavy. In short, I shall sell the horses, and get rid of all the servants at once. I never do things by halves, as you know, and I mean to have the house clear of a pack of useless people by this time to-morrow.”\n\nI listened to him, perfectly aghast with astonishment.\n\n“Do you mean, Sir Percival, that I am to dismiss the indoor servants under my charge without the usual month’s warning?” I asked.\n\n“Certainly I do. We may all be out of the house before another month, and I am not going to leave the servants here in idleness, with no master to wait on.”\n\n“Who is to do the cooking, Sir Percival, while you are still staying here?”\n\n“Margaret Porcher can roast and boil—keep her. What do I want with a cook if I don’t mean to give any dinner-parties?”\n\n“The servant you have mentioned is the most unintelligent servant in the house, Sir Percival.”\n\n“Keep her, I tell you, and have a woman in from the village to do the cleaning and go away again. My weekly expenses must and shall be lowered immediately. I don’t send for you to make objections, Mrs. Michelson—I send for you to carry out my plans of economy. Dismiss the whole lazy pack of indoor servants to-morrow, except Porcher. She is as strong as a horse—and we’ll make her work like a horse.”\n\n“You will excuse me for reminding you, Sir Percival, that if the servants go to-morrow they must have a month’s wages in lieu of a month’s warning.”\n\n“Let them! A month’s wages saves a month’s waste and gluttony in the servants’ hall.”\n\nThis last remark conveyed an aspersion of the most offensive kind on my management. I had too much self-respect to defend myself under so gross an imputation. Christian consideration for the helpless position of Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde, and for the serious inconvenience which my sudden absence might inflict on them, alone prevented me from resigning my situation on the spot. I rose immediately. It would have lowered me in my own estimation to have permitted the interview to continue a moment longer.\n\n“After that last remark, Sir Percival, I have nothing more to say. Your directions shall be attended to.” Pronouncing those words, I bowed my head with the most distant respect, and went out of the room.\n\nThe next day the servants left in a body. Sir Percival himself dismissed the grooms and stablemen, sending them, with all the horses but one, to London. Of the whole domestic establishment, indoors and out, there now remained only myself, Margaret Porcher, and the gardener—this last living in his own cottage, and being wanted to take care of the one horse that remained in the stables.\n\nWith the house left in this strange and lonely condition—with the mistress of it ill in her room—with Miss Halcombe still as helpless as a child—and with the doctor’s attendance withdrawn from us in enmity—it was surely not unnatural that my spirits should sink, and my customary composure be very hard to maintain. My mind was ill at ease. I wished the poor ladies both well again, and I wished myself away from Blackwater Park.\n\n# II\n\nThe next event that occurred was of so singular a nature that it might have caused me a feeling of superstitious surprise, if my mind had not been fortified by principle against any pagan weakness of that sort. The uneasy sense of something wrong in the family which had made me wish myself away from Blackwater Park, was actually followed, strange to say, by my departure from the house. It is true that my absence was for a temporary period only, but the coincidence was, in my opinion, not the less remarkable on that account.\n\nMy departure took place under the following circumstances—\n\nA day or two after the servants all left I was again sent for to see Sir Percival. The undeserved slur which he had cast on my management of the household did not, I am happy to say, prevent me from returning good for evil to the best of my ability, by complying with his request as readily and respectfully as ever. It cost me a struggle with that fallen nature, which we all share in common, before I could suppress my feelings. Being accustomed to self-discipline, I accomplished the sacrifice.\n\nI found Sir Percival and Count Fosco sitting together again. On this occasion his lordship remained present at the interview, and assisted in the development of Sir Percival’s views.\n\nThe subject to which they now requested my attention related to the healthy change of air by which we all hoped that Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde might soon be enabled to profit. Sir Percival mentioned that both the ladies would probably pass the autumn (by invitation of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire) at Limmeridge House, Cumberland. But before they went there, it was his opinion, confirmed by Count Fosco (who here took up the conversation and continued it to the end), that they would benefit by a short residence first in the genial climate of Torquay. The great object, therefore, was to engage lodgings at that place, affording all the comforts and advantages of which they stood in need, and the great difficulty was to find an experienced person capable of choosing the sort of residence which they wanted. In this emergency the Count begged to inquire, on Sir Percival’s behalf, whether I would object to give the ladies the benefit of my assistance, by proceeding myself to Torquay in their interests.\n\nIt was impossible for a person in my situation to meet any proposal, made in these terms, with a positive objection.\n\nI could only venture to represent the serious inconvenience of my leaving Blackwater Park in the extraordinary absence of all the indoor servants, with the one exception of Margaret Porcher. But Sir Percival and his lordship declared that they were both willing to put up with inconvenience for the sake of the invalids. I next respectfully suggested writing to an agent at Torquay, but I was met here by being reminded of the imprudence of taking lodgings without first seeing them. I was also informed that the Countess (who would otherwise have gone to Devonshire herself) could not, in Lady Glyde’s present condition, leave her niece, and that Sir Percival and the Count had business to transact together which would oblige them to remain at Blackwater Park. In short, it was clearly shown me that if I did not undertake the errand, no one else could be trusted with it. Under these circumstances, I could only inform Sir Percival that my services were at the disposal of Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde.\n\nIt was thereupon arranged that I should leave the next morning, that I should occupy one or two days in examining all the most convenient houses in Torquay, and that I should return with my report as soon as I conveniently could. A memorandum was written for me by his lordship, stating the requisites which the place I was sent to take must be found to possess, and a note of the pecuniary limit assigned to me was added by Sir Percival.\n\nMy own idea on reading over these instructions was, that no such residence as I saw described could be found at any watering-place in England, and that, even if it could by chance be discovered, it would certainly not be parted with for any period on such terms as I was permitted to offer. I hinted at these difficulties to both the gentlemen, but Sir Percival (who undertook to answer me) did not appear to feel them. It was not for me to dispute the question. I said no more, but I felt a very strong conviction that the business on which I was sent away was so beset by difficulties that my errand was almost hopeless at starting.\n\nBefore I left I took care to satisfy myself that Miss Halcombe was going on favourably.\n\nThere was a painful expression of anxiety in her face which made me fear that her mind, on first recovering itself, was not at ease. But she was certainly strengthening more rapidly than I could have ventured to anticipate, and she was able to send kind messages to Lady Glyde, saying that she was fast getting well, and entreating her ladyship not to exert herself again too soon. I left her in charge of Mrs. Rubelle, who was still as quietly independent of every one else in the house as ever. When I knocked at Lady Glyde’s door before going away, I was told that she was still sadly weak and depressed, my informant being the Countess, who was then keeping her company in her room. Sir Percival and the Count were walking on the road to the lodge as I was driven by in the chaise. I bowed to them and quitted the house, with not a living soul left in the servants’ offices but Margaret Porcher.\n\nEvery one must feel what I have felt myself since that time, that these circumstances were more than unusual—they were almost suspicious. Let me, however, say again that it was impossible for me, in my dependent position, to act otherwise than I did.\n\nThe result of my errand at Torquay was exactly what I had foreseen. No such lodgings as I was instructed to take could be found in the whole place, and the terms I was permitted to give were much too low for the purpose, even if I had been able to discover what I wanted. I accordingly returned to Blackwater Park, and informed Sir Percival, who met me at the door, that my journey had been taken in vain. He seemed too much occupied with some other subject to care about the failure of my errand, and his first words informed me that even in the short time of my absence another remarkable change had taken place in the house.\n\nThe Count and Countess Fosco had left Blackwater Park for their new residence in St. John’s Wood.\n\nI was not made aware of the motive for this sudden departure—I was only told that the Count had been very particular in leaving his kind compliments to me. When I ventured on asking Sir Percival whether Lady Glyde had any one to attend to her comforts in the absence of the Countess, he replied that she had Margaret Porcher to wait on her, and he added that a woman from the village had been sent for to do the work downstairs.\n\nThe answer really shocked me—there was such a glaring impropriety in permitting an under-housemaid to fill the place of confidential attendant on Lady Glyde. I went upstairs at once, and met Margaret on the bedroom landing. Her services had not been required (naturally enough), her mistress having sufficiently recovered that morning to be able to leave her bed. I asked next after Miss Halcombe, but I was answered in a slouching, sulky way, which left me no wiser than I was before.\n\nI did not choose to repeat the question, and perhaps provoke an impertinent reply. It was in every respect more becoming to a person in my position to present myself immediately in Lady Glyde’s room.\n\nI found that her ladyship had certainly gained in health during the last few days. Although still sadly weak and nervous, she was able to get up without assistance, and to walk slowly about her room, feeling no worse effect from the exertion than a slight sensation of fatigue. She had been made a little anxious that morning about Miss Halcombe, through having received no news of her from any one. I thought this seemed to imply a blamable want of attention on the part of Mrs. Rubelle, but I said nothing, and remained with Lady Glyde to assist her to dress. When she was ready we both left the room together to go to Miss Halcombe.\n\nWe were stopped in the passage by the appearance of Sir Percival. He looked as if he had been purposely waiting there to see us.\n\n“Where are you going?” he said to Lady Glyde.\n\n“To Marian’s room,” she answered.\n\n“It may spare you a disappointment,” remarked Sir Percival, “if I tell you at once that you will not find her there.”\n\n“Not find her there!”\n\n“No. She left the house yesterday morning with Fosco and his wife.”\n\nLady Glyde was not strong enough to bear the surprise of this extraordinary statement. She turned fearfully pale, and leaned back against the wall, looking at her husband in dead silence.\n\nI was so astonished myself that I hardly knew what to say. I asked Sir Percival if he really meant that Miss Halcombe had left Blackwater Park.\n\n“I certainly mean it,” he answered.\n\n“In her state, Sir Percival! Without mentioning her intentions to Lady Glyde!”\n\nBefore he could reply her ladyship recovered herself a little and spoke.\n\n“Impossible!” she cried out in a loud, frightened manner, taking a step or two forward from the wall. “Where was the doctor? where was Mr. Dawson when Marian went away?”\n\n“Mr. Dawson wasn’t wanted, and wasn’t here,” said Sir Percival. “He left of his own accord, which is enough of itself to show that she was strong enough to travel. How you stare! If you don’t believe she has gone, look for yourself. Open her room door, and all the other room doors if you like.”\n\nShe took him at his word, and I followed her. There was no one in Miss Halcombe’s room but Margaret Porcher, who was busy setting it to rights. There was no one in the spare rooms or the dressing-rooms when we looked into them afterwards. Sir Percival still waited for us in the passage. As we were leaving the last room that we had examined Lady Glyde whispered, “Don’t go, Mrs. Michelson! don’t leave me, for God’s sake!” Before I could say anything in return she was out again in the passage, speaking to her husband.\n\n“What does it mean, Sir Percival? I insist—I beg and pray you will tell me what it means.”\n\n“It means,” he answered, “that Miss Halcombe was strong enough yesterday morning to sit up and be dressed, and that she insisted on taking advantage of Fosco’s going to London to go there too.”\n\n“To London!”\n\n“Yes—on her way to Limmeridge.”\n\nLady Glyde turned and appealed to me.\n\n“You saw Miss Halcombe last,” she said. “Tell me plainly, Mrs. Michelson, did you think she looked fit to travel?”\n\n“Not in MY opinion, your ladyship.”\n\nSir Percival, on his side, instantly turned and appealed to me also.\n\n“Before you went away,” he said, “did you, or did you not, tell the nurse that Miss Halcombe looked much stronger and better?”\n\n“I certainly made the remark, Sir Percival.”\n\nHe addressed her ladyship again the moment I offered that reply.\n\n“Set one of Mrs. Michelson’s opinions fairly against the other,” he said, “and try to be reasonable about a perfectly plain matter. If she had not been well enough to be moved do you think we should any of us have risked letting her go? She has got three competent people to look after her—Fosco and your aunt, and Mrs. Rubelle, who went away with them expressly for that purpose. They took a whole carriage yesterday, and made a bed for her on the seat in case she felt tired. To-day, Fosco and Mrs. Rubelle go on with her themselves to Cumberland.”\n\n“Why does Marian go to Limmeridge and leave me here by myself?” said her ladyship, interrupting Sir Percival.\n\n“Because your uncle won’t receive you till he has seen your sister first,” he replied. “Have you forgotten the letter he wrote to her at the beginning of her illness? It was shown to you, you read it yourself, and you ought to remember it.”\n\n“I do remember it.”\n\n“If you do, why should you be surprised at her leaving you? You want to be back at Limmeridge, and she has gone there to get your uncle’s leave for you on his own terms.”\n\nPoor Lady Glyde’s eyes filled with tears.\n\n“Marian never left me before,” she said, “without bidding me good-bye.”\n\n“She would have bid you good-bye this time,” returned Sir Percival, “if she had not been afraid of herself and of you. She knew you would try to stop her, she knew you would distress her by crying. Do you want to make any more objections? If you do, you must come downstairs and ask questions in the dining-room. These worries upset me. I want a glass of wine.”\n\nHe left us suddenly.\n\nHis manner all through this strange conversation had been very unlike what it usually was. He seemed to be almost as nervous and fluttered, every now and then, as his lady herself. I should never have supposed that his health had been so delicate, or his composure so easy to upset.\n\nI tried to prevail on Lady Glyde to go back to her room, but it was useless. She stopped in the passage, with the look of a woman whose mind was panic-stricken.\n\n“Something has happened to my sister!” she said.\n\n“Remember, my lady, what surprising energy there is in Miss Halcombe,” I suggested. “She might well make an effort which other ladies in her situation would be unfit for. I hope and believe there is nothing wrong—I do indeed.”\n\n“I must follow Marian,” said her ladyship, with the same panic-stricken look. “I must go where she has gone, I must see that she is alive and well with my own eyes. Come! come down with me to Sir Percival.”\n\nI hesitated, fearing that my presence might be considered an intrusion. I attempted to represent this to her ladyship, but she was deaf to me. She held my arm fast enough to force me to go downstairs with her, and she still clung to me with all the little strength she had at the moment when I opened the dining-room door.\n\nSir Percival was sitting at the table with a decanter of wine before him. He raised the glass to his lips as we went in and drained it at a draught. Seeing that he looked at me angrily when he put it down again, I attempted to make some apology for my accidental presence in the room.\n\n“Do you suppose there are any secrets going on here?” he broke out suddenly; “there are none—there is nothing underhand, nothing kept from you or from any one.” After speaking those strange words loudly and sternly, he filled himself another glass of wine and asked Lady Glyde what she wanted of him.\n\n“If my sister is fit to travel I am fit to travel” said her ladyship, with more firmness than she had yet shown. “I come to beg you will make allowances for my anxiety about Marian, and let me follow her at once by the afternoon train.”\n\n“You must wait till to-morrow,” replied Sir Percival, “and then if you don’t hear to the contrary you can go. I don’t suppose you are at all likely to hear to the contrary, so I shall write to Fosco by to-night’s post.”\n\nHe said those last words holding his glass up to the light, and looking at the wine in it instead of at Lady Glyde. Indeed he never once looked at her throughout the conversation. Such a singular want of good breeding in a gentleman of his rank impressed me, I own, very painfully.\n\n“Why should you write to Count Fosco?” she asked, in extreme surprise.\n\n“To tell him to expect you by the midday train,” said Sir Percival. “He will meet you at the station when you get to London, and take you on to sleep at your aunt’s in St. John’s Wood.”\n\nLady Glyde’s hand began to tremble violently round my arm—why I could not imagine.\n\n“There is no necessity for Count Fosco to meet me,” she said. “I would rather not stay in London to sleep.”\n\n“You must. You can’t take the whole journey to Cumberland in one day. You must rest a night in London—and I don’t choose you to go by yourself to an hotel. Fosco made the offer to your uncle to give you house-room on the way down, and your uncle has accepted it. Here! here is a letter from him addressed to yourself. I ought to have sent it up this morning, but I forgot. Read it and see what Mr. Fairlie himself says to you.”\n\nLady Glyde looked at the letter for a moment and then placed it in my hands.\n\n“Read it,” she said faintly. “I don’t know what is the matter with me. I can’t read it myself.”\n\nIt was a note of only four lines—so short and so careless that it quite struck me. If I remember correctly it contained no more than these words—\n\n“Dearest Laura, Please come whenever you like. Break the journey by sleeping at your aunt’s house. Grieved to hear of dear Marian’s illness. Affectionately yours, Frederick Fairlie.”\n\n“I would rather not go there—I would rather not stay a night in London,” said her ladyship, breaking out eagerly with those words before I had quite done reading the note, short as it was. “Don’t write to Count Fosco! Pray, pray don’t write to him!”\n\nSir Percival filled another glass from the decanter so awkwardly that he upset it and spilt all the wine over the table. “My sight seems to be failing me,” he muttered to himself, in an odd, muffled voice. He slowly set the glass up again, refilled it, and drained it once more at a draught. I began to fear, from his look and manner, that the wine was getting into his head.\n\n“Pray don’t write to Count Fosco,” persisted Lady Glyde, more earnestly than ever.\n\n“Why not, I should like to know?” cried Sir Percival, with a sudden burst of anger that startled us both. “Where can you stay more properly in London than at the place your uncle himself chooses for you—at your aunt’s house? Ask Mrs. Michelson.”\n\nThe arrangement proposed was so unquestionably the right and the proper one, that I could make no possible objection to it. Much as I sympathised with Lady Glyde in other respects, I could not sympathise with her in her unjust prejudices against Count Fosco. I never before met with any lady of her rank and station who was so lamentably narrow-minded on the subject of foreigners. Neither her uncle’s note nor Sir Percival’s increasing impatience seemed to have the least effect on her. She still objected to staying a night in London, she still implored her husband not to write to the Count.\n\n“Drop it!” said Sir Percival, rudely turning his back on us. “If you haven’t sense enough to know what is best for yourself other people must know it for you. The arrangement is made and there is an end of it. You are only wanted to do what Miss Halcombe has done before you—-”\n\n“Marian?” repeated her Ladyship, in a bewildered manner; “Marian sleeping in Count Fosco’s house!”\n\n“Yes, in Count Fosco’s house. She slept there last night to break the journey, and you are to follow her example, and do what your uncle tells you. You are to sleep at Fosco’s to-morrow night, as your sister did, to break the journey. Don’t throw too many obstacles in my way! don’t make me repent of letting you go at all!”\n\nHe started to his feet, and suddenly walked out into the verandah through the open glass doors.\n\n“Will your ladyship excuse me,” I whispered, “if I suggest that we had better not wait here till Sir Percival comes back? I am very much afraid he is over-excited with wine.”\n\nShe consented to leave the room in a weary, absent manner.\n\nAs soon as we were safe upstairs again, I did all I could to compose her ladyship’s spirits. I reminded her that Mr. Fairlie’s letters to Miss Halcombe and to herself did certainly sanction, and even render necessary, sooner or later, the course that had been taken. She agreed to this, and even admitted, of her own accord, that both letters were strictly in character with her uncle’s peculiar disposition—but her fears about Miss Halcombe, and her unaccountable dread of sleeping at the Count’s house in London, still remained unshaken in spite of every consideration that I could urge. I thought it my duty to protest against Lady Glyde’s unfavourable opinion of his lordship, and I did so, with becoming forbearance and respect.\n\n“Your ladyship will pardon my freedom,” I remarked, in conclusion, “but it is said, ‘by their fruits ye shall know them.’ I am sure the Count’s constant kindness and constant attention, from the very beginning of Miss Halcombe’s illness, merit our best confidence and esteem. Even his lordship’s serious misunderstanding with Mr. Dawson was entirely attributable to his anxiety on Miss Halcombe’s account.”\n\n“What misunderstanding?” inquired her ladyship, with a look of sudden interest.\n\nI related the unhappy circumstances under which Mr. Dawson had withdrawn his attendance—mentioning them all the more readily because I disapproved of Sir Percival’s continuing to conceal what had happened (as he had done in my presence) from the knowledge of Lady Glyde.\n\nHer ladyship started up, with every appearance of being additionally agitated and alarmed by what I had told her.\n\n“Worse! worse than I thought!” she said, walking about the room, in a bewildered manner. “The Count knew Mr. Dawson would never consent to Marian’s taking a journey—he purposely insulted the doctor to get him out of the house.”\n\n“Oh, my lady! my lady!” I remonstrated.\n\n“Mrs. Michelson!” she went on vehemently, “no words that ever were spoken will persuade me that my sister is in that man’s power and in that man’s house with her own consent. My horror of him is such, that nothing Sir Percival could say and no letters my uncle could write, would induce me, if I had only my own feelings to consult, to eat, drink, or sleep under his roof. But my misery of suspense about Marian gives me the courage to follow her anywhere, to follow her even into Count Fosco’s house.”\n\nI thought it right, at this point, to mention that Miss Halcombe had already gone on to Cumberland, according to Sir Percival’s account of the matter.\n\n“I am afraid to believe it!” answered her ladyship. “I am afraid she is still in that man’s house. If I am wrong, if she has really gone on to Limmeridge, I am resolved I will not sleep to-morrow night under Count Fosco’s roof. My dearest friend in the world, next to my sister, lives near London. You have heard me, you have heard Miss Halcombe, speak of Mrs. Vesey? I mean to write, and propose to sleep at her house. I don’t know how I shall get there—I don’t know how I shall avoid the Count—but to that refuge I will escape in some way, if my sister has gone to Cumberland. All I ask of you to do, is to see yourself that my letter to Mrs. Vesey goes to London to-night, as certainly as Sir Percival’s letter goes to Count Fosco. I have reasons for not trusting the post-bag downstairs. Will you keep my secret, and help me in this? it is the last favour, perhaps, that I shall ever ask of you.”\n\nI hesitated, I thought it all very strange, I almost feared that her ladyship’s mind had been a little affected by recent anxiety and suffering. At my own risk, however, I ended by giving my consent. If the letter had been addressed to a stranger, or to any one but a lady so well known to me by report as Mrs. Vesey, I might have refused. I thank God—looking to what happened afterwards—I thank God I never thwarted that wish, or any other, which Lady Glyde expressed to me, on the last day of her residence at Blackwater Park.\n\nThe letter was written and given into my hands. I myself put it into the post-box in the village that evening.\n\nWe saw nothing more of Sir Percival for the rest of the day.\n\nI slept, by Lady Glyde’s own desire, in the next room to hers, with the door open between us. There was something so strange and dreadful in the loneliness and emptiness of the house, that I was glad, on my side, to have a companion near me. Her ladyship sat up late, reading letters and burning them, and emptying her drawers and cabinets of little things she prized, as if she never expected to return to Blackwater Park. Her sleep was sadly disturbed when she at last went to bed—she cried out in it several times, once so loud that she woke herself. Whatever her dreams were, she did not think fit to communicate them to me. Perhaps, in my situation, I had no right to expect that she should do so. It matters little now. I was sorry for her, I was indeed heartily sorry for her all the same.\n\nThe next day was fine and sunny. Sir Percival came up, after breakfast, to tell us that the chaise would be at the door at a quarter to twelve—the train to London stopping at our station at twenty minutes after. He informed Lady Glyde that he was obliged to go out, but added that he hoped to be back before she left. If any unforeseen accident delayed him, I was to accompany her to the station, and to take special care that she was in time for the train. Sir Percival communicated these directions very hastily—walking here and there about the room all the time. Her ladyship looked attentively after him wherever he went. He never once looked at her in return.\n\nShe only spoke when he had done, and then she stopped him as he approached the door, by holding out her hand.\n\n“I shall see you no more,” she said, in a very marked manner. “This is our parting—our parting, it may be for ever. Will you try to forgive me, Percival, as heartily as I forgive YOU?”\n\nHis face turned of an awful whiteness all over, and great beads of perspiration broke out on his bald forehead. “I shall come back,” he said, and made for the door, as hastily as if his wife’s farewell words had frightened him out of the room.\n\nI had never liked Sir Percival, but the manner in which he left Lady Glyde made me feel ashamed of having eaten his bread and lived in his service. I thought of saying a few comforting and Christian words to the poor lady, but there was something in her face, as she looked after her husband when the door closed on him, that made me alter my mind and keep silence.\n\nAt the time named the chaise drew up at the gates. Her ladyship was right—Sir Percival never came back. I waited for him till the last moment, and waited in vain.\n\nNo positive responsibility lay on my shoulders, and yet I did not feel easy in my mind. “It is of your own free will,” I said, as the chaise drove through the lodge-gates, “that your ladyship goes to London?”\n\n“I will go anywhere,” she answered, “to end the dreadful suspense that I am suffering at this moment.”\n\nShe had made me feel almost as anxious and as uncertain about Miss Halcombe as she felt herself. I presumed to ask her to write me a line, if all went well in London. She answered, “Most willingly, Mrs. Michelson.”\n\n“We all have our crosses to bear, my lady,” I said, seeing her silent and thoughtful, after she had promised to write.\n\nShe made no reply—she seemed to be too much wrapped up in her own thoughts to attend to me.\n\n“I fear your ladyship rested badly last night,” I remarked, after waiting a little.\n\n“Yes,” she said, “I was terribly disturbed by dreams.”\n\n“Indeed, my lady?” I thought she was going to tell me her dreams, but no, when she spoke next it was only to ask a question.\n\n“You posted the letter to Mrs. Vesey with your own hands?”\n\n“Yes, my lady.”\n\n“Did Sir Percival say, yesterday, that Count Fosco was to meet me at the terminus in London?”\n\n“He did, my lady.”\n\nShe sighed heavily when I answered that last question, and said no more.\n\nWe arrived at the station, with hardly two minutes to spare. The gardener (who had driven us) managed about the luggage, while I took the ticket. The whistle of the train was sounding when I joined her ladyship on the platform. She looked very strangely, and pressed her hand over her heart, as if some sudden pain or fright had overcome her at that moment.\n\n“I wish you were going with me!” she said, catching eagerly at my arm when I gave her the ticket.\n\nIf there had been time, if I had felt the day before as I felt then, I would have made my arrangements to accompany her, even though the doing so had obliged me to give Sir Percival warning on the spot. As it was, her wishes, expressed at the last moment only, were expressed too late for me to comply with them. She seemed to understand this herself before I could explain it, and did not repeat her desire to have me for a travelling companion. The train drew up at the platform. She gave the gardener a present for his children, and took my hand, in her simple hearty manner, before she got into the carriage.\n\n“You have been very kind to me and to my sister,” she said—“kind when we were both friendless. I shall remember you gratefully, as long as I live to remember any one. Good-bye—and God bless you!”\n\nShe spoke those words with a tone and a look which brought the tears into my eyes—she spoke them as if she was bidding me farewell for ever.\n\n“Good-bye, my lady,” I said, putting her into the carriage, and trying to cheer her; “good-bye, for the present only; good-bye, with my best and kindest wishes for happier times.”\n\nShe shook her head, and shuddered as she settled herself in the carriage. The guard closed the door. “Do you believe in dreams?” she whispered to me at the window. “My dreams, last night, were dreams I have never had before. The terror of them is hanging over me still.” The whistle sounded before I could answer, and the train moved. Her pale quiet face looked at me for the last time—looked sorrowfully and solemnly from the window. She waved her hand, and I saw her no more.\n\nTowards five o’clock on the afternoon of that same day, having a little time to myself in the midst of the household duties which now pressed upon me, I sat down alone in my own room, to try and compose my mind with the volume of my husband’s Sermons. For the first time in my life I found my attention wandering over those pious and cheering words. Concluding that Lady Glyde’s departure must have disturbed me far more seriously than I had myself supposed, I put the book aside, and went out to take a turn in the garden. Sir Percival had not yet returned, to my knowledge, so I could feel no hesitation about showing myself in the grounds.\n\nOn turning the corner of the house, and gaining a view of the garden, I was startled by seeing a stranger walking in it. The stranger was a woman—she was lounging along the path with her back to me, and was gathering the flowers.\n\nAs I approached she heard me, and turned round.\n\nMy blood curdled in my veins. The strange woman in the garden was Mrs. Rubelle!\n\nI could neither move nor speak. She came up to me, as composedly as ever, with her flowers in her hand.\n\n“What is the matter, ma’am?” she said quietly.\n\n“You here!” I gasped out. “Not gone to London! Not gone to Cumberland!”\n\nMrs. Rubelle smelt at her flowers with a smile of malicious pity.\n\n“Certainly not,” she said. “I have never left Blackwater Park.”\n\nI summoned breath enough and courage enough for another question.\n\n“Where is Miss Halcombe?”\n\nMrs. Rubelle fairly laughed at me this time, and replied in these words—\n\n“Miss Halcombe, ma’am, has not left Blackwater Park either.”\n\nWhen I heard that astounding answer, all my thoughts were startled back on the instant to my parting with Lady Glyde. I can hardly say I reproached myself, but at that moment I think I would have given many a year’s hard savings to have known four hours earlier what I knew now.\n\nMrs. Rubelle waited, quietly arranging her nosegay, as if she expected me to say something.\n\nI could say nothing. I thought of Lady Glyde’s worn-out energies and weakly health, and I trembled for the time when the shock of the discovery that I had made would fall on her. For a minute or more my fears for the poor ladies silenced me. At the end of that time Mrs. Rubelle looked up sideways from her flowers, and said, “Here is Sir Percival, ma’am, returned from his ride.”\n\nI saw him as soon as she did. He came towards us, slashing viciously at the flowers with his riding-whip. When he was near enough to see my face he stopped, struck at his boot with the whip, and burst out laughing, so harshly and so violently that the birds flew away, startled, from the tree by which he stood.\n\n“Well, Mrs. Michelson,” he said, “you have found it out at last, have you?”\n\nI made no reply. He turned to Mrs. Rubelle.\n\n“When did you show yourself in the garden?”\n\n“I showed myself about half an hour ago, sir. You said I might take my liberty again as soon as Lady Glyde had gone away to London.”\n\n“Quite right. I don’t blame you—I only asked the question.” He waited a moment, and then addressed himself once more to me. “You can’t believe it, can you?” he said mockingly. “Here! come along and see for yourself.”\n\nHe led the way round to the front of the house. I followed him, and Mrs. Rubelle followed me. After passing through the iron gates he stopped, and pointed with his whip to the disused middle wing of the building.\n\n“There!” he said. “Look up at the first floor. You know the old Elizabethan bedrooms? Miss Halcombe is snug and safe in one of the best of them at this moment. Take her in, Mrs. Rubelle (you have got your key?); take Mrs. Michelson in, and let her own eyes satisfy her that there is no deception this time.”\n\nThe tone in which he spoke to me, and the minute or two that had passed since we left the garden, helped me to recover my spirits a little. What I might have done at this critical moment, if all my life had been passed in service, I cannot say. As it was, possessing the feelings, the principles, and the bringing up of a lady, I could not hesitate about the right course to pursue. My duty to myself, and my duty to Lady Glyde, alike forbade me to remain in the employment of a man who had shamefully deceived us both by a series of atrocious falsehoods.\n\n“I must beg permission, Sir Percival, to speak a few words to you in private,” I said. “Having done so, I shall be ready to proceed with this person to Miss Halcombe’s room.”\n\nMrs. Rubelle, whom I had indicated by a slight turn of my head, insolently sniffed at her nosegay and walked away, with great deliberation, towards the house door.\n\n“Well,” said Sir Percival sharply, “what is it now?”\n\n“I wish to mention, sir, that I am desirous of resigning the situation I now hold at Blackwater Park.” That was literally how I put it. I was resolved that the first words spoken in his presence should be words which expressed my intention to leave his service.\n\nHe eyed me with one of his blackest looks, and thrust his hands savagely into the pockets of his riding-coat.\n\n“Why?” he said; “why, I should like to know?”\n\n“It is not for me, Sir Percival, to express an opinion on what has taken place in this house. I desire to give no offence. I merely wish to say that I do not feel it consistent with my duty to Lady Glyde and to myself to remain any longer in your service.”\n\n“Is it consistent with your duty to me to stand there, casting suspicion on me to my face?” he broke out in his most violent manner. “I see what you’re driving at. You have taken your own mean, underhand view of an innocent deception practised on Lady Glyde for her own good. It was essential to her health that she should have a change of air immediately, and you know as well as I do she would never have gone away if she had been told Miss Halcombe was still left here. She has been deceived in her own interests—and I don’t care who knows it. Go, if you like—there are plenty of housekeepers as good as you to be had for the asking. Go when you please—but take care how you spread scandals about me and my affairs when you’re out of my service. Tell the truth, and nothing but the truth, or it will be the worse for you! See Miss Halcombe for yourself—see if she hasn’t been as well taken care of in one part of the house as in the other. Remember the doctor’s own orders that Lady Glyde was to have a change of air at the earliest possible opportunity. Bear all that well in mind, and then say anything against me and my proceedings if you dare!”\n\nHe poured out these words fiercely, all in a breath, walking backwards and forwards, and striking about him in the air with his whip.\n\nNothing that he said or did shook my opinion of the disgraceful series of falsehoods that he had told in my presence the day before, or of the cruel deception by which he had separated Lady Glyde from her sister, and had sent her uselessly to London, when she was half distracted with anxiety on Miss Halcombe’s account. I naturally kept these thoughts to myself, and said nothing more to irritate him; but I was not the less resolved to persist in my purpose. A soft answer turneth away wrath, and I suppressed my own feelings accordingly when it was my turn to reply.\n\n“While I am in your service, Sir Percival,” I said, “I hope I know my duty well enough not to inquire into your motives. When I am out of your service, I hope I know my own place well enough not to speak of matters which don’t concern me—”\n\n“When do you want to go?” he asked, interrupting me without ceremony. “Don’t suppose I am anxious to keep you—don’t suppose I care about your leaving the house. I am perfectly fair and open in this matter, from first to last. When do you want to go?”\n\n“I should wish to leave at your earliest convenience, Sir Percival.”\n\n“My convenience has nothing to do with it. I shall be out of the house for good and all to-morrow morning, and I can settle your accounts to-night. If you want to study anybody’s convenience, it had better be Miss Halcombe’s. Mrs. Rubelle’s time is up to-day, and she has reasons for wishing to be in London to-night. If you go at once, Miss Halcombe won’t have a soul left here to look after her.”\n\nI hope it is unnecessary for me to say that I was quite incapable of deserting Miss Halcombe in such an emergency as had now befallen Lady Glyde and herself. After first distinctly ascertaining from Sir Percival that Mrs. Rubelle was certain to leave at once if I took her place, and after also obtaining permission to arrange for Mr. Dawson’s resuming his attendance on his patient, I willingly consented to remain at Blackwater Park until Miss Halcombe no longer required my services. It was settled that I should give Sir Percival’s solicitor a week’s notice before I left, and that he was to undertake the necessary arrangements for appointing my successor. The matter was discussed in very few words. At its conclusion Sir Percival abruptly turned on his heel, and left me free to join Mrs. Rubelle. That singular foreign person had been sitting composedly on the door-step all this time, waiting till I could follow her to Miss Halcombe’s room.\n\nI had hardly walked half-way towards the house when Sir Percival, who had withdrawn in the opposite direction, suddenly stopped and called me back.\n\n“Why are you leaving my service?” he asked.\n\nThe question was so extraordinary, after what had just passed between us, that I hardly knew what to say in answer to it.\n\n“Mind! I don’t know why you are going,” he went on. “You must give a reason for leaving me, I suppose, when you get another situation. What reason? The breaking up of the family? Is that it?”\n\n“There can be no positive objection, Sir Percival, to that reason——”\n\n“Very well! That’s all I want to know. If people apply for your character, that’s your reason, stated by yourself. You go in consequence of the breaking up of the family.”\n\nHe turned away again before I could say another word, and walked out rapidly into the grounds. His manner was as strange as his language. I acknowledge he alarmed me.\n\nEven the patience of Mrs. Rubelle was getting exhausted, when I joined her at the house door.\n\n“At last!” she said, with a shrug of her lean foreign shoulders. She led the way into the inhabited side of the house, ascended the stairs, and opened with her key the door at the end of the passage, which communicated with the old Elizabethan rooms—a door never previously used, in my time, at Blackwater Park. The rooms themselves I knew well, having entered them myself on various occasions from the other side of the house. Mrs. Rubelle stopped at the third door along the old gallery, handed me the key of it, with the key of the door of communication, and told me I should find Miss Halcombe in that room. Before I went in I thought it desirable to make her understand that her attendance had ceased. Accordingly, I told her in plain words that the charge of the sick lady henceforth devolved entirely on myself.\n\n“I am glad to hear it, ma’am,” said Mrs. Rubelle. “I want to go very much.”\n\n“Do you leave to-day?” I asked, to make sure of her.\n\n“Now that you have taken charge, ma’am, I leave in half an hour’s time. Sir Percival has kindly placed at my disposition the gardener, and the chaise, whenever I want them. I shall want them in half an hour’s time to go to the station. I am packed up in anticipation already. I wish you good-day, ma’am.”\n\nShe dropped a brisk curtsey, and walked back along the gallery, humming a little tune, and keeping time to it cheerfully with the nosegay in her hand. I am sincerely thankful to say that was the last I saw of Mrs. Rubelle.\n\nWhen I went into the room Miss Halcombe was asleep. I looked at her anxiously, as she lay in the dismal, high, old-fashioned bed. She was certainly not in any respect altered for the worse since I had seen her last. She had not been neglected, I am bound to admit, in any way that I could perceive. The room was dreary, and dusty, and dark, but the window (looking on a solitary court-yard at the back of the house) was opened to let in the fresh air, and all that could be done to make the place comfortable had been done. The whole cruelty of Sir Percival’s deception had fallen on poor Lady Glyde. The only ill-usage which either he or Mrs. Rubelle had inflicted on Miss Halcombe consisted, so far as I could see, in the first offence of hiding her away.\n\nI stole back, leaving the sick lady still peacefully asleep, to give the gardener instructions about bringing the doctor. I begged the man, after he had taken Mrs. Rubelle to the station, to drive round by Mr. Dawson’s, and leave a message in my name, asking him to call and see me. I knew he would come on my account, and I knew he would remain when he found Count Fosco had left the house.\n\nIn due course of time the gardener returned, and said that he had driven round by Mr. Dawson’s residence, after leaving Mrs. Rubelle at the station. The doctor sent me word that he was poorly in health himself, but that he would call, if possible, the next morning.\n\nHaving delivered his message the gardener was about to withdraw, but I stopped him to request that he would come back before dark, and sit up that night, in one of the empty bedrooms, so as to be within call in case I wanted him. He understood readily enough my unwillingness to be left alone all night in the most desolate part of that desolate house, and we arranged that he should come in between eight and nine.\n\nHe came punctually, and I found cause to be thankful that I had adopted the precaution of calling him in. Before midnight Sir Percival’s strange temper broke out in the most violent and most alarming manner, and if the gardener had not been on the spot to pacify him on the instant, I am afraid to think what might have happened.\n\nAlmost all the afternoon and evening he had been walking about the house and grounds in an unsettled, excitable manner, having, in all probability, as I thought, taken an excessive quantity of wine at his solitary dinner. However that may be, I heard his voice calling loudly and angrily in the new wing of the house, as I was taking a turn backwards and forwards along the gallery the last thing at night. The gardener immediately ran down to him, and I closed the door of communication, to keep the alarm, if possible, from reaching Miss Halcombe’s ears. It was full half an hour before the gardener came back. He declared that his master was quite out of his senses—not through the excitement of drink, as I had supposed, but through a kind of panic or frenzy of mind, for which it was impossible to account. He had found Sir Percival walking backwards and forwards by himself in the hall, swearing, with every appearance of the most violent passion, that he would not stop another minute alone in such a dungeon as his own house, and that he would take the first stage of his journey immediately in the middle of the night. The gardener, on approaching him, had been hunted out, with oaths and threats, to get the horse and chaise ready instantly. In a quarter of an hour Sir Percival had joined him in the yard, had jumped into the chaise, and, lashing the horse into a gallop, had driven himself away, with his face as pale as ashes in the moonlight. The gardener had heard him shouting and cursing at the lodge-keeper to get up and open the gate—had heard the wheels roll furiously on again in the still night, when the gate was unlocked—and knew no more.\n\nThe next day, or a day or two after, I forget which, the chaise was brought back from Knowlesbury, our nearest town, by the ostler at the old inn. Sir Percival had stopped there, and had afterwards left by the train—for what destination the man could not tell. I never received any further information, either from himself or from any one else, of Sir Percival’s proceedings, and I am not even aware, at this moment, whether he is in England or out of it. He and I have not met since he drove away like an escaped criminal from his own house, and it is my fervent hope and prayer that we may never meet again.\n\nMy own part of this sad family story is now drawing to an end.\n\nI have been informed that the particulars of Miss Halcombe’s waking, and of what passed between us when she found me sitting by her bedside, are not material to the purpose which is to be answered by the present narrative. It will be sufficient for me to say in this place, that she was not herself conscious of the means adopted to remove her from the inhabited to the uninhabited part of the house. She was in a deep sleep at the time, whether naturally or artificially produced she could not say. In my absence at Torquay, and in the absence of all the resident servants except Margaret Porcher (who was perpetually eating, drinking, or sleeping, when she was not at work), the secret transfer of Miss Halcombe from one part of the house to the other was no doubt easily performed. Mrs. Rubelle (as I discovered for myself, in looking about the room) had provisions, and all other necessaries, together with the means of heating water, broth, and so on, without kindling a fire, placed at her disposal during the few days of her imprisonment with the sick lady. She had declined to answer the questions which Miss Halcombe naturally put, but had not, in other respects, treated her with unkindness or neglect. The disgrace of lending herself to a vile deception is the only disgrace with which I can conscientiously charge Mrs. Rubelle.\n\nI need write no particulars (and I am relieved to know it) of the effect produced on Miss Halcombe by the news of Lady Glyde’s departure, or by the far more melancholy tidings which reached us only too soon afterwards at Blackwater Park. In both cases I prepared her mind beforehand as gently and as carefully as possible, having the doctor’s advice to guide me, in the last case only, through Mr. Dawson’s being too unwell to come to the house for some days after I had sent for him. It was a sad time, a time which it afflicts me to think of or to write of now. The precious blessings of religious consolation which I endeavoured to convey were long in reaching Miss Halcombe’s heart, but I hope and believe they came home to her at last. I never left her till her strength was restored. The train which took me away from that miserable house was the train which took her away also. We parted very mournfully in London. I remained with a relative at Islington, and she went on to Mr. Fairlie’s house in Cumberland.\n\nI have only a few lines more to write before I close this painful statement. They are dictated by a sense of duty.\n\nIn the first place, I wish to record my own personal conviction that no blame whatever, in connection with the events which I have now related, attaches to Count Fosco. I am informed that a dreadful suspicion has been raised, and that some very serious constructions are placed upon his lordship’s conduct. My persuasion of the Count’s innocence remains, however, quite unshaken. If he assisted Sir Percival in sending me to Torquay, he assisted under a delusion, for which, as a foreigner and a stranger, he was not to blame. If he was concerned in bringing Mrs. Rubelle to Blackwater Park, it was his misfortune and not his fault, when that foreign person was base enough to assist a deception planned and carried out by the master of the house. I protest, in the interests of morality, against blame being gratuitously and wantonly attached to the proceedings of the Count.\n\nIn the second place, I desire to express my regret at my own inability to remember the precise day on which Lady Glyde left Blackwater Park for London. I am told that it is of the last importance to ascertain the exact date of that lamentable journey, and I have anxiously taxed my memory to recall it. The effort has been in vain. I can only remember now that it was towards the latter part of July. We all know the difficulty, after a lapse of time, of fixing precisely on a past date unless it has been previously written down. That difficulty is greatly increased in my case by the alarming and confusing events which took place about the period of Lady Glyde’s departure. I heartily wish I had made a memorandum at the time. I heartily wish my memory of the date was as vivid as my memory of that poor lady’s face, when it looked at me sorrowfully for the last time from the carriage window.\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED IN SEVERAL NARRATIVES\n\n1. THE NARRATIVE OF HESTER PINHORN, COOK IN THE SERVICE OF COUNT FOSCO\n\n[Taken down from her own statement]\n\nI am sorry to say that I have never learnt to read or write. I have been a hard-working woman all my life, and have kept a good character. I know that it is a sin and wickedness to say the thing which is not, and I will truly beware of doing so on this occasion. All that I know I will tell, and I humbly beg the gentleman who takes this down to put my language right as he goes on, and to make allowances for my being no scholar.\n\nIn this last summer I happened to be out of place (through no fault of my own), and I heard of a situation as plain cook, at Number Five, Forest Road, St. John’s Wood. I took the place on trial. My master’s name was Fosco. My mistress was an English lady. He was Count and she was Countess. There was a girl to do housemaid’s work when I got there. She was not over-clean or tidy, but there was no harm in her. I and she were the only servants in the house.\n\nOur master and mistress came after we got in; and as soon as they did come we were told, downstairs, that company was expected from the country.\n\nThe company was my mistress’s niece, and the back bedroom on the first floor was got ready for her. My mistress mentioned to me that Lady Glyde (that was her name) was in poor health, and that I must be particular in my cooking accordingly. She was to come that day, as well as I can remember—but whatever you do, don’t trust my memory in the matter. I am sorry to say it’s no use asking me about days of the month, and such-like. Except Sundays, half my time I take no heed of them, being a hard-working woman and no scholar. All I know is Lady Glyde came, and when she did come, a fine fright she gave us all surely. I don’t know how master brought her to the house, being hard at work at the time. But he did bring her in the afternoon, I think, and the housemaid opened the door to them, and showed them into the parlour. Before she had been long down in the kitchen again with me, we heard a hurry-skurry upstairs, and the parlour bell ringing like mad, and my mistress’s voice calling out for help.\n\nWe both ran up, and there we saw the lady laid on the sofa, with her face ghastly white, and her hands fast clenched, and her head drawn down to one side. She had been taken with a sudden fright, my mistress said, and master he told us she was in a fit of convulsions. I ran out, knowing the neighbourhood a little better than the rest of them, to fetch the nearest doctor’s help. The nearest help was at Goodricke’s and Garth’s, who worked together as partners, and had a good name and connection, as I have heard, all round St. John’s Wood. Mr. Goodricke was in, and he came back with me directly.\n\nIt was some time before he could make himself of much use. The poor unfortunate lady fell out of one fit into another, and went on so till she was quite wearied out, and as helpless as a new-born babe. We then got her to bed. Mr. Goodricke went away to his house for medicine, and came back again in a quarter of an hour or less. Besides the medicine he brought a bit of hollow mahogany wood with him, shaped like a kind of trumpet, and after waiting a little while, he put one end over the lady’s heart and the other to his ear, and listened carefully.\n\nWhen he had done he says to my mistress, who was in the room, “This is a very serious case,” he says, “I recommend you to write to Lady Glyde’s friends directly.” My mistress says to him, “Is it heart-disease?” And he says, “Yes, heart-disease of a most dangerous kind.” He told her exactly what he thought was the matter, which I was not clever enough to understand. But I know this, he ended by saying that he was afraid neither his help nor any other doctor’s help was likely to be of much service.\n\nMy mistress took this ill news more quietly than my master. He was a big, fat, odd sort of elderly man, who kept birds and white mice, and spoke to them as if they were so many Christian children. He seemed terribly cut up by what had happened. “Ah! poor Lady Glyde! poor dear Lady Glyde!” he says, and went stalking about, wringing his fat hands more like a play-actor than a gentleman. For one question my mistress asked the doctor about the lady’s chances of getting round, he asked a good fifty at least. I declare he quite tormented us all, and when he was quiet at last, out he went into the bit of back garden, picking trumpery little nosegays, and asking me to take them upstairs and make the sick-room look pretty with them. As if THAT did any good. I think he must have been, at times, a little soft in his head. But he was not a bad master—he had a monstrous civil tongue of his own, and a jolly, easy, coaxing way with him. I liked him a deal better than my mistress. She was a hard one, if ever there was a hard one yet.\n\nTowards night-time the lady roused up a little. She had been so wearied out, before that, by the convulsions, that she never stirred hand or foot, or spoke a word to anybody. She moved in the bed now, and stared about her at the room and us in it. She must have been a nice-looking lady when well, with light hair, and blue eyes and all that. Her rest was troubled at night—at least so I heard from my mistress, who sat up alone with her. I only went in once before going to bed to see if I could be of any use, and then she was talking to herself in a confused, rambling manner. She seemed to want sadly to speak to somebody who was absent from her somewhere. I couldn’t catch the name the first time, and the second time master knocked at the door, with his regular mouthful of questions, and another of his trumpery nosegays.\n\nWhen I went in early the next morning, the lady was clean worn out again, and lay in a kind of faint sleep. Mr. Goodricke brought his partner, Mr. Garth, with him to advise. They said she must not be disturbed out of her rest on any account. They asked my mistress many questions, at the other end of the room, about what the lady’s health had been in past times, and who had attended her, and whether she had ever suffered much and long together under distress of mind. I remember my mistress said “Yes” to that last question. And Mr. Goodricke looked at Mr. Garth, and shook his head; and Mr. Garth looked at Mr. Goodricke, and shook his head. They seemed to think that the distress might have something to do with the mischief at the lady’s heart. She was but a frail thing to look at, poor creature! Very little strength at any time, I should say—very little strength.\n\nLater on the same morning, when she woke, the lady took a sudden turn, and got seemingly a great deal better. I was not let in again to see her, no more was the housemaid, for the reason that she was not to be disturbed by strangers. What I heard of her being better was through my master. He was in wonderful good spirits about the change, and looked in at the kitchen window from the garden, with his great big curly-brimmed white hat on, to go out.\n\n“Good Mrs. Cook,” says he, “Lady Glyde is better. My mind is more easy than it was, and I am going out to stretch my big legs with a sunny little summer walk. Shall I order for you, shall I market for you, Mrs. Cook? What are you making there? A nice tart for dinner? Much crust, if you please—much crisp crust, my dear, that melts and crumbles delicious in the mouth.” That was his way. He was past sixty, and fond of pastry. Just think of that!\n\nThe doctor came again in the forenoon, and saw for himself that Lady Glyde had woke up better. He forbid us to talk to her, or to let her talk to us, in case she was that way disposed, saying she must be kept quiet before all things, and encouraged to sleep as much as possible. She did not seem to want to talk whenever I saw her, except overnight, when I couldn’t make out what she was saying—she seemed too much worn down. Mr. Goodricke was not nearly in such good spirits about her as master. He said nothing when he came downstairs, except that he would call again at five o’clock.\n\nAbout that time (which was before master came home again) the bell rang hard from the bedroom, and my mistress ran out into the landing, and called to me to go for Mr. Goodricke, and tell him the lady had fainted. I got on my bonnet and shawl, when, as good luck would have it, the doctor himself came to the house for his promised visit.\n\nI let him in, and went upstairs along with him. “Lady Glyde was just as usual,” says my mistress to him at the door; “she was awake, and looking about her in a strange, forlorn manner, when I heard her give a sort of half cry, and she fainted in a moment.” The doctor went up to the bed, and stooped down over the sick lady. He looked very serious, all on a sudden, at the sight of her, and put his hand on her heart.\n\nMy mistress stared hard in Mr. Goodricke’s face. “Not dead!” says she, whispering, and turning all of a tremble from head to foot.\n\n“Yes,” says the doctor, very quiet and grave. “Dead. I was afraid it would happen suddenly when I examined her heart yesterday.” My mistress stepped back from the bedside while he was speaking, and trembled and trembled again. “Dead!” she whispers to herself; “dead so suddenly! dead so soon! What will the Count say?” Mr. Goodricke advised her to go downstairs, and quiet herself a little. “You have been sitting up all night,” says he, “and your nerves are shaken. This person,” says he, meaning me, “this person will stay in the room till I can send for the necessary assistance.” My mistress did as he told her. “I must prepare the Count,” she says. “I must carefully prepare the Count.” And so she left us, shaking from head to foot, and went out.\n\n“Your master is a foreigner,” says Mr. Goodricke, when my mistress had left us. “Does he understand about registering the death?” “I can’t rightly tell, sir,” says I, “but I should think not.” The doctor considered a minute, and then says he, “I don’t usually do such things,” says he, “but it may save the family trouble in this case if I register the death myself. I shall pass the district office in half an hour’s time, and I can easily look in. Mention, if you please, that I will do so.” “Yes, sir,” says I, “with thanks, I’m sure, for your kindness in thinking of it.” “You don’t mind staying here till I can send you the proper person?” says he. “No, sir,” says I; “I’ll stay with the poor lady till then. I suppose nothing more could be done, sir, than was done?” says I. “No,” says he, “nothing; she must have suffered sadly before ever I saw her—the case was hopeless when I was called in.” “Ah, dear me! we all come to it, sooner or later, don’t we, sir?” says I. He gave no answer to that—he didn’t seem to care about talking. He said, “Good-day,” and went out.\n\nI stopped by the bedside from that time till the time when Mr. Goodricke sent the person in, as he had promised. She was, by name, Jane Gould. I considered her to be a respectable-looking woman. She made no remark, except to say that she understood what was wanted of her, and that she had winded a many of them in her time.\n\nHow master bore the news, when he first heard it, is more than I can tell, not having been present. When I did see him he looked awfully overcome by it, to be sure. He sat quiet in a corner, with his fat hands hanging over his thick knees, and his head down, and his eyes looking at nothing. He seemed not so much sorry, as scared and dazed like, by what had happened. My mistress managed all that was to be done about the funeral. It must have cost a sight of money—the coffin, in particular, being most beautiful. The dead lady’s husband was away, as we heard, in foreign parts. But my mistress (being her aunt) settled it with her friends in the country (Cumberland, I think) that she should be buried there, in the same grave along with her mother. Everything was done handsomely, in respect of the funeral, I say again, and master went down to attend the burying in the country himself. He looked grand in his deep mourning, with his big solemn face, and his slow walk, and his broad hatband—that he did!\n\nIn conclusion. I have to say, in answer to questions put to me—\n\n(1) That neither I nor my fellow-servant ever saw my master give Lady Glyde any medicine himself.\n\n(2) That he was never, to my knowledge and belief, left alone in the room with Lady Glyde.\n\n(3) That I am not able to say what caused the sudden fright, which my mistress informed me had seized the lady on her first coming into the house. The cause was never explained, either to me or to my fellow-servant.\n\nThe above statement has been read over in my presence. I have nothing to add to it, or to take away from it. I say, on my oath as a Christian woman, this is the truth.\n\n(Signed) HESTER PINHORN, Her + Mark.\n\n# 2. THE NARRATIVE OF THE DOCTOR\n\nTo the Registrar of the Sub-District in which the undermentioned death took place.—I hereby certify that I attended Lady Glyde, aged Twenty-One last Birthday; that I last saw her on Thursday the 25th July 1850; that she died on the same day at No. 5 Forest Road, St. John’s Wood, and that the cause of her death was Aneurism. Duration of disease not known.\n\n(Signed) Alfred Goodricke.\n\nProf. Title. M.R.C.S. Eng., L.S.A. Address, 12 Croydon Gardens St. John's Wood.\n\n# 3. THE NARRATIVE OF JANE GOULD\n\nI was the person sent in by Mr. Goodricke to do what was right and needful by the remains of a lady who had died at the house named in the certificate which precedes this. I found the body in charge of the servant, Hester Pinhorn. I remained with it, and prepared it at the proper time for the grave. It was laid in the coffin in my presence, and I afterwards saw the coffin screwed down previous to its removal. When that had been done, and not before, I received what was due to me and left the house. I refer persons who may wish to investigate my character to Mr. Goodricke. He will bear witness that I can be trusted to tell the truth.\n\n(Signed) JANE GOULD\n\n# 4. THE NARRATIVE OF THE TOMBSTONE\n\nSacred to the Memory of Laura, Lady Glyde, wife of Sir Percival Glyde, Bart., of Blackwater Park, Hampshire, and daughter of the late Philip Fairlie, Esq., of Limmeridge House, in this parish. Born March 27th, 1829; married December 22nd, 1849; died July 25th, 1850.\n\n# 5. THE NARRATIVE OF WALTER HARTRIGHT\n\nEarly in the summer of 1850 I and my surviving companions left the wilds and forests of Central America for home. Arrived at the coast, we took ship there for England. The vessel was wrecked in the Gulf of Mexico—I was among the few saved from the sea. It was my third escape from peril of death. Death by disease, death by the Indians, death by drowning—all three had approached me; all three had passed me by.\n\nThe survivors of the wreck were rescued by an American vessel bound for Liverpool. The ship reached her port on the thirteenth day of October 1850. We landed late in the afternoon, and I arrived in London the same night.\n\nThese pages are not the record of my wanderings and my dangers away from home. The motives which led me from my country and my friends to a new world of adventure and peril are known. From that self-imposed exile I came back, as I had hoped, prayed, believed I should come back—a changed man. In the waters of a new life I had tempered my nature afresh. In the stern school of extremity and danger my will had learnt to be strong, my heart to be resolute, my mind to rely on itself. I had gone out to fly from my own future. I came back to face it, as a man should.\n\nTo face it with that inevitable suppression of myself which I knew it would demand from me. I had parted with the worst bitterness of the past, but not with my heart’s remembrance of the sorrow and the tenderness of that memorable time. I had not ceased to feel the one irreparable disappointment of my life—I had only learnt to bear it. Laura Fairlie was in all my thoughts when the ship bore me away, and I looked my last at England. Laura Fairlie was in all my thoughts when the ship brought me back, and the morning light showed the friendly shore in view.\n\nMy pen traces the old letters as my heart goes back to the old love. I write of her as Laura Fairlie still. It is hard to think of her, it is hard to speak of her, by her husband’s name.\n\nThere are no more words of explanation to add on my appearance for the second time in these pages. This narrative, if I have the strength and the courage to write it, may now go on.\n\nMy first anxieties and first hopes when the morning came centred in my mother and my sister. I felt the necessity of preparing them for the joy and surprise of my return, after an absence during which it had been impossible for them to receive any tidings of me for months past. Early in the morning I sent a letter to the Hampstead Cottage, and followed it myself in an hour’s time.\n\nWhen the first meeting was over, when our quiet and composure of other days began gradually to return to us, I saw something in my mother’s face which told me that a secret oppression lay heavy on her heart. There was more than love—there was sorrow in the anxious eyes that looked on me so tenderly—there was pity in the kind hand that slowly and fondly strengthened its hold on mine. We had no concealments from each other. She knew how the hope of my life had been wrecked—she knew why I had left her. It was on my lips to ask as composedly as I could if any letter had come for me from Miss Halcombe, if there was any news of her sister that I might hear. But when I looked in my mother’s face I lost courage to put the question even in that guarded form. I could only say, doubtingly and restrainedly—\n\n“You have something to tell me.”\n\nMy sister, who had been sitting opposite to us, rose suddenly without a word of explanation—rose and left the room.\n\nMy mother moved closer to me on the sofa and put her arms round my neck. Those fond arms trembled—the tears flowed fast over the faithful loving face.\n\n“Walter!” she whispered, “my own darling! my heart is heavy for you. Oh, my son! my son! try to remember that I am still left!”\n\nMy head sank on her bosom. She had said all in saying those words.\n\nIt was the morning of the third day since my return—the morning of the sixteenth of October.\n\nI had remained with them at the cottage—I had tried hard not to embitter the happiness of my return to THEM as it was embittered to ME. I had done all man could to rise after the shock, and accept my life resignedly—to let my great sorrow come in tenderness to my heart, and not in despair. It was useless and hopeless. No tears soothed my aching eyes, no relief came to me from my sister’s sympathy or my mother’s love.\n\nOn that third morning I opened my heart to them. At last the words passed my lips which I had longed to speak on the day when my mother told me of her death.\n\n“Let me go away alone for a little while,” I said. “I shall bear it better when I have looked once more at the place where I first saw her—when I have knelt and prayed by the grave where they have laid her to rest.”\n\nI departed on my journey—my journey to the grave of Laura Fairlie.\n\nIt was a quiet autumn afternoon when I stopped at the solitary station, and set forth alone on foot by the well-remembered road. The waning sun was shining faintly through thin white clouds—the air was warm and still—the peacefulness of the lonely country was overshadowed and saddened by the influence of the falling year.\n\nI reached the moor—I stood again on the brow of the hill—I looked on along the path—and there were the familiar garden trees in the distance, the clear sweeping semicircle of the drive, the high white walls of Limmeridge House. The chances and changes, the wanderings and dangers of months and months past, all shrank and shrivelled to nothing in my mind. It was like yesterday since my feet had last trodden the fragrant heathy ground. I thought I should see her coming to meet me, with her little straw hat shading her face, her simple dress fluttering in the air, and her well-filled sketch-book ready in her hand.\n\nOh death, thou hast thy sting! oh, grave, thou hast thy victory!\n\nI turned aside, and there below me in the glen was the lonesome grey church, the porch where I had waited for the coming of the woman in white, the hills encircling the quiet burial-ground, the brook bubbling cold over its stony bed. There was the marble cross, fair and white, at the head of the tomb—the tomb that now rose over mother and daughter alike.\n\nI approached the grave. I crossed once more the low stone stile, and bared my head as I touched the sacred ground. Sacred to gentleness and goodness, sacred to reverence and grief.\n\nI stopped before the pedestal from which the cross rose. On one side of it, on the side nearest to me, the newly-cut inscription met my eyes—the hard, clear, cruel black letters which told the story of her life and death. I tried to read them. I did read as far as the name. “Sacred to the Memory of Laura——” The kind blue eyes dim with tears—the fair head drooping wearily—the innocent parting words which implored me to leave her—oh, for a happier last memory of her than this; the memory I took away with me, the memory I bring back with me to her grave!\n\nA second time I tried to read the inscription. I saw at the end the date of her death, and above it——\n\nAbove it there were lines on the marble—there was a name among them which disturbed my thoughts of her. I went round to the other side of the grave, where there was nothing to read, nothing of earthly vileness to force its way between her spirit and mine.\n\nI knelt down by the tomb. I laid my hands, I laid my head on the broad white stone, and closed my weary eyes on the earth around, on the light above. I let her come back to me. Oh, my love! my love! my heart may speak to you NOW! It is yesterday again since we parted—yesterday, since your dear hand lay in mine—yesterday, since my eyes looked their last on you. My love! my love!\n\nTime had flowed on, and silence had fallen like thick night over its course.\n\nThe first sound that came after the heavenly peace rustled faintly like a passing breath of air over the grass of the burial-ground. I heard it nearing me slowly, until it came changed to my ear—came like footsteps moving onward—then stopped.\n\nI looked up.\n\nThe sunset was near at hand. The clouds had parted—the slanting light fell mellow over the hills. The last of the day was cold and clear and still in the quiet valley of the dead.\n\nBeyond me, in the burial-ground, standing together in the cold clearness of the lower light, I saw two women. They were looking towards the tomb, looking towards me.\n\nTwo.\n\nThey came a little on, and stopped again. Their veils were down, and hid their faces from me. When they stopped, one of them raised her veil. In the still evening light I saw the face of Marian Halcombe.\n\nChanged, changed as if years had passed over it! The eyes large and wild, and looking at me with a strange terror in them. The face worn and wasted piteously. Pain and fear and grief written on her as with a brand.\n\nI took one step towards her from the grave. She never moved—she never spoke. The veiled woman with her cried out faintly. I stopped. The springs of my life fell low, and the shuddering of an unutterable dread crept over me from head to foot.\n\nThe woman with the veiled face moved away from her companion, and came towards me slowly. Left by herself, standing by herself, Marian Halcombe spoke. It was the voice that I remembered—the voice not changed, like the frightened eyes and the wasted face.\n\n“My dream! my dream!” I heard her say those words softly in the awful silence. She sank on her knees, and raised her clasped hands to heaven. “Father! strengthen him. Father! help him in his hour of need.”\n\nThe woman came on, slowly and silently came on. I looked at her—at her, and at none other, from that moment.\n\nThe voice that was praying for me faltered and sank low—then rose on a sudden, and called affrightedly, called despairingly to me to come away.\n\nBut the veiled woman had possession of me, body and soul. She stopped on one side of the grave. We stood face to face with the tombstone between us. She was close to the inscription on the side of the pedestal. Her gown touched the black letters.\n\nThe voice came nearer, and rose and rose more passionately still. “Hide your face! don’t look at her! Oh, for God’s sake, spare him——”\n\nThe woman lifted her veil.\n\n“Sacred to the Memory of Laura, Lady Glyde——”\n\nLaura, Lady Glyde, was standing by the inscription, and was looking at me over the grave.\n\n[The Second Epoch of the Story closes here.]\n\nI open a new page. I advance my narrative by one week.\n\nThe history of the interval which I thus pass over must remain unrecorded. My heart turns faint, my mind sinks in darkness and confusion when I think of it. This must not be, if I who write am to guide, as I ought, you who read. This must not be, if the clue that leads through the windings of the story is to remain from end to end untangled in my hands.\n\nA life suddenly changed—its whole purpose created afresh, its hopes and fears, its struggles, its interests, and its sacrifices all turned at once and for ever into a new direction—this is the prospect which now opens before me, like the burst of view from a mountain’s top. I left my narrative in the quiet shadow of Limmeridge church—I resume it, one week later, in the stir and turmoil of a London street.\n\nThe street is in a populous and a poor neighbourhood. The ground floor of one of the houses in it is occupied by a small newsvendor’s shop, and the first floor and the second are let as furnished lodgings of the humblest kind.\n\nI have taken those two floors in an assumed name. On the upper floor I live, with a room to work in, a room to sleep in. On the lower floor, under the same assumed name, two women live, who are described as my sisters. I get my bread by drawing and engraving on wood for the cheap periodicals. My sisters are supposed to help me by taking in a little needle-work. Our poor place of abode, our humble calling, our assumed relationship, and our assumed name, are all used alike as a means of hiding us in the house-forest of London. We are numbered no longer with the people whose lives are open and known. I am an obscure, unnoticed man, without patron or friend to help me. Marian Halcombe is nothing now but my eldest sister, who provides for our household wants by the toil of her own hands. We two, in the estimation of others, are at once the dupes and the agents of a daring imposture. We are supposed to be the accomplices of mad Anne Catherick, who claims the name, the place, and the living personality of dead Lady Glyde.\n\nThat is our situation. That is the changed aspect in which we three must appear, henceforth, in this narrative, for many and many a page to come.\n\nIn the eye of reason and of law, in the estimation of relatives and friends, according to every received formality of civilised society, “Laura, Lady Glyde,” lay buried with her mother in Limmeridge churchyard. Torn in her own lifetime from the list of the living, the daughter of Philip Fairlie and the wife of Percival Glyde might still exist for her sister, might still exist for me, but to all the world besides she was dead. Dead to her uncle, who had renounced her; dead to the servants of the house, who had failed to recognise her; dead to the persons in authority, who had transmitted her fortune to her husband and her aunt; dead to my mother and my sister, who believed me to be the dupe of an adventuress and the victim of a fraud; socially, morally, legally—dead.\n\nAnd yet alive! Alive in poverty and in hiding. Alive, with the poor drawing-master to fight her battle, and to win the way back for her to her place in the world of living beings.\n\nDid no suspicion, excited by my own knowledge of Anne Catherick’s resemblance to her, cross my mind, when her face was first revealed to me? Not the shadow of a suspicion, from the moment when she lifted her veil by the side of the inscription which recorded her death.\n\nBefore the sun of that day had set, before the last glimpse of the home which was closed against her had passed from our view, the farewell words I spoke, when we parted at Limmeridge House, had been recalled by both of us—repeated by me, recognised by her. “If ever the time comes, when the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength will give you a moment’s happiness, or spare you a moment’s sorrow, will you try to remember the poor drawing-master who has taught you?” She, who now remembered so little of the trouble and terror of a later time, remembered those words, and laid her poor head innocently and trustingly on the bosom of the man who had spoken them. In that moment, when she called me by my name, when she said, “They have tried to make me forget everything, Walter; but I remember Marian, and I remember YOU”—in that moment, I, who had long since given her my love, gave her my life, and thanked God that it was mine to bestow on her. Yes! the time had come. From thousands on thousands of miles away—through forest and wilderness, where companions stronger than I had fallen by my side, through peril of death thrice renewed, and thrice escaped, the Hand that leads men on the dark road to the future had led me to meet that time. Forlorn and disowned, sorely tried and sadly changed—her beauty faded, her mind clouded—robbed of her station in the world, of her place among living creatures—the devotion I had promised, the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength, might be laid blamelessly now at those dear feet. In the right of her calamity, in the right of her friendlessness, she was mine at last! Mine to support, to protect, to cherish, to restore. Mine to love and honour as father and brother both. Mine to vindicate through all risks and all sacrifices—through the hopeless struggle against Rank and Power, through the long fight with armed deceit and fortified Success, through the waste of my reputation, through the loss of my friends, through the hazard of my life.\n\n# II\n\nMy position is defined—my motives are acknowledged. The story of Marian and the story of Laura must come next.\n\nI shall relate both narratives, not in the words (often interrupted, often inevitably confused) of the speakers themselves, but in the words of the brief, plain, studiously simple abstract which I committed to writing for my own guidance, and for the guidance of my legal adviser. So the tangled web will be most speedily and most intelligibly unrolled.\n\nThe story of Marian begins where the narrative of the housekeeper at Blackwater Park left off.\n\nOn Lady Glyde’s departure from her husband’s house, the fact of that departure, and the necessary statement of the circumstances under which it had taken place, were communicated to Miss Halcombe by the housekeeper. It was not till some days afterwards (how many days exactly, Mrs. Michelson, in the absence of any written memorandum on the subject, could not undertake to say) that a letter arrived from Madame Fosco announcing Lady Glyde’s sudden death in Count Fosco’s house. The letter avoided mentioning dates, and left it to Mrs. Michelson’s discretion to break the news at once to Miss Halcombe, or to defer doing so until that lady’s health should be more firmly established.\n\nHaving consulted Mr. Dawson (who had been himself delayed, by ill health, in resuming his attendance at Blackwater Park), Mrs. Michelson, by the doctor’s advice, and in the doctor’s presence, communicated the news, either on the day when the letter was received, or on the day after. It is not necessary to dwell here upon the effect which the intelligence of Lady Glyde’s sudden death produced on her sister. It is only useful to the present purpose to say that she was not able to travel for more than three weeks afterwards. At the end of that time she proceeded to London accompanied by the housekeeper. They parted there—Mrs. Michelson previously informing Miss Halcombe of her address, in case they might wish to communicate at a future period.\n\nOn parting with the housekeeper Miss Halcombe went at once to the office of Messrs. Gilmore & Kyrle to consult with the latter gentleman in Mr. Gilmore’s absence. She mentioned to Mr. Kyrle what she had thought it desirable to conceal from every one else (Mrs. Michelson included)—her suspicion of the circumstances under which Lady Glyde was said to have met her death. Mr. Kyrle, who had previously given friendly proof of his anxiety to serve Miss Halcombe, at once undertook to make such inquiries as the delicate and dangerous nature of the investigation proposed to him would permit.\n\nTo exhaust this part of the subject before going farther, it may be mentioned that Count Fosco offered every facility to Mr. Kyrle, on that gentleman’s stating that he was sent by Miss Halcombe to collect such particulars as had not yet reached her of Lady Glyde’s decease. Mr. Kyrle was placed in communication with the medical man, Mr. Goodricke, and with the two servants. In the absence of any means of ascertaining the exact date of Lady Glyde’s departure from Blackwater Park, the result of the doctor’s and the servants’ evidence, and of the volunteered statements of Count Fosco and his wife, was conclusive to the mind of Mr. Kyrle. He could only assume that the intensity of Miss Halcombe’s suffering, under the loss of her sister, had misled her judgment in a most deplorable manner, and he wrote her word that the shocking suspicion to which she had alluded in his presence was, in his opinion, destitute of the smallest fragment of foundation in truth. Thus the investigation by Mr. Gilmore’s partner began and ended.\n\nMeanwhile, Miss Halcombe had returned to Limmeridge House, and had there collected all the additional information which she was able to obtain.\n\nMr. Fairlie had received his first intimation of his niece’s death from his sister, Madame Fosco, this letter also not containing any exact reference to dates. He had sanctioned his sister’s proposal that the deceased lady should be laid in her mother’s grave in Limmeridge churchyard. Count Fosco had accompanied the remains to Cumberland, and had attended the funeral at Limmeridge, which took place on the 30th of July. It was followed, as a mark of respect, by all the inhabitants of the village and the neighbourhood. On the next day the inscription (originally drawn out, it was said, by the aunt of the deceased lady, and submitted for approval to her brother, Mr. Fairlie) was engraved on one side of the monument over the tomb.\n\nOn the day of the funeral, and for one day after it, Count Fosco had been received as a guest at Limmeridge House, but no interview had taken place between Mr. Fairlie and himself, by the former gentleman’s desire. They had communicated by writing, and through this medium Count Fosco had made Mr. Fairlie acquainted with the details of his niece’s last illness and death. The letter presenting this information added no new facts to the facts already known, but one very remarkable paragraph was contained in the postscript. It referred to Anne Catherick.\n\nThe substance of the paragraph in question was as follows—\n\nIt first informed Mr. Fairlie that Anne Catherick (of whom he might hear full particulars from Miss Halcombe when she reached Limmeridge) had been traced and recovered in the neighbourhood of Blackwater Park, and had been for the second time placed under the charge of the medical man from whose custody she had once escaped.\n\nThis was the first part of the postscript. The second part warned Mr. Fairlie that Anne Catherick’s mental malady had been aggravated by her long freedom from control, and that the insane hatred and distrust of Sir Percival Glyde, which had been one of her most marked delusions in former times, still existed under a newly-acquired form. The unfortunate woman’s last idea in connection with Sir Percival was the idea of annoying and distressing him, and of elevating herself, as she supposed, in the estimation of the patients and nurses, by assuming the character of his deceased wife, the scheme of this personation having evidently occurred to her after a stolen interview which she had succeeded in obtaining with Lady Glyde, and at which she had observed the extraordinary accidental likeness between the deceased lady and herself. It was to the last degree improbable that she would succeed a second time in escaping from the Asylum, but it was just possible she might find some means of annoying the late Lady Glyde’s relatives with letters, and in that case Mr. Fairlie was warned beforehand how to receive them.\n\nThe postscript, expressed in these terms, was shown to Miss Halcombe when she arrived at Limmeridge. There were also placed in her possession the clothes Lady Glyde had worn, and the other effects she had brought with her to her aunt’s house. They had been carefully collected and sent to Cumberland by Madame Fosco.\n\nSuch was the posture of affairs when Miss Halcombe reached Limmeridge in the early part of September.\n\nShortly afterwards she was confined to her room by a relapse, her weakened physical energies giving way under the severe mental affliction from which she was now suffering. On getting stronger again, in a month’s time, her suspicion of the circumstances described as attending her sister’s death still remained unshaken. She had heard nothing in the interim of Sir Percival Glyde, but letters had reached her from Madame Fosco, making the most affectionate inquiries on the part of her husband and herself. Instead of answering these letters, Miss Halcombe caused the house in St. John’s Wood, and the proceedings of its inmates, to be privately watched.\n\nNothing doubtful was discovered. The same result attended the next investigations, which were secretly instituted on the subject of Mrs. Rubelle. She had arrived in London about six months before with her husband. They had come from Lyons, and they had taken a house in the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, to be fitted up as a boarding-house for foreigners, who were expected to visit England in large numbers to see the Exhibition of 1851. Nothing was known against husband or wife in the neighbourhood. They were quiet people, and they had paid their way honestly up to the present time. The final inquiries related to Sir Percival Glyde. He was settled in Paris, and living there quietly in a small circle of English and French friends.\n\nFoiled at all points, but still not able to rest, Miss Halcombe next determined to visit the Asylum in which she then supposed Anne Catherick to be for the second time confined. She had felt a strong curiosity about the woman in former days, and she was now doubly interested—first, in ascertaining whether the report of Anne Catherick’s attempted personation of Lady Glyde was true, and secondly (if it proved to be true), in discovering for herself what the poor creature’s real motives were for attempting the deceit.\n\nAlthough Count Fosco’s letter to Mr. Fairlie did not mention the address of the Asylum, that important omission cast no difficulties in Miss Halcombe’s way. When Mr. Hartright had met Anne Catherick at Limmeridge, she had informed him of the locality in which the house was situated, and Miss Halcombe had noted down the direction in her diary, with all the other particulars of the interview exactly as she heard them from Mr. Hartright’s own lips. Accordingly she looked back at the entry and extracted the address—furnished herself with the Count’s letter to Mr. Fairlie as a species of credential which might be useful to her, and started by herself for the Asylum on the eleventh of October.\n\nShe passed the night of the eleventh in London. It had been her intention to sleep at the house inhabited by Lady Glyde’s old governess, but Mrs. Vesey’s agitation at the sight of her lost pupil’s nearest and dearest friend was so distressing that Miss Halcombe considerately refrained from remaining in her presence, and removed to a respectable boarding-house in the neighbourhood, recommended by Mrs. Vesey’s married sister. The next day she proceeded to the Asylum, which was situated not far from London on the northern side of the metropolis.\n\nShe was immediately admitted to see the proprietor.\n\nAt first he appeared to be decidedly unwilling to let her communicate with his patient. But on her showing him the postscript to Count Fosco’s letter—on her reminding him that she was the “Miss Halcombe” there referred to—that she was a near relative of the deceased Lady Glyde—and that she was therefore naturally interested, for family reasons, in observing for herself the extent of Anne Catherick’s delusion in relation to her late sister—the tone and manner of the owner of the Asylum altered, and he withdrew his objections. He probably felt that a continued refusal, under these circumstances, would not only be an act of discourtesy in itself, but would also imply that the proceedings in his establishment were not of a nature to bear investigation by respectable strangers.\n\nMiss Halcombe’s own impression was that the owner of the Asylum had not been received into the confidence of Sir Percival and the Count. His consenting at all to let her visit his patient seemed to afford one proof of this, and his readiness in making admissions which could scarcely have escaped the lips of an accomplice, certainly appeared to furnish another.\n\nFor example, in the course of the introductory conversation which took place, he informed Miss Halcombe that Anne Catherick had been brought back to him with the necessary order and certificates by Count Fosco on the twenty-seventh of July—the Count also producing a letter of explanations and instructions signed by Sir Percival Glyde. On receiving his inmate again, the proprietor of the Asylum acknowledged that he had observed some curious personal changes in her. Such changes no doubt were not without precedent in his experience of persons mentally afflicted. Insane people were often at one time, outwardly as well as inwardly, unlike what they were at another—the change from better to worse, or from worse to better, in the madness having a necessary tendency to produce alterations of appearance externally. He allowed for these, and he allowed also for the modification in the form of Anne Catherick’s delusion, which was reflected no doubt in her manner and expression. But he was still perplexed at times by certain differences between his patient before she had escaped and his patient since she had been brought back. Those differences were too minute to be described. He could not say of course that she was absolutely altered in height or shape or complexion, or in the colour of her hair and eyes, or in the general form of her face—the change was something that he felt more than something that he saw. In short, the case had been a puzzle from the first, and one more perplexity was added to it now.\n\nIt cannot be said that this conversation led to the result of even partially preparing Miss Halcombe’s mind for what was to come. But it produced, nevertheless, a very serious effect upon her. She was so completely unnerved by it, that some little time elapsed before she could summon composure enough to follow the proprietor of the Asylum to that part of the house in which the inmates were confined.\n\nOn inquiry, it turned out that the supposed Anne Catherick was then taking exercise in the grounds attached to the establishment. One of the nurses volunteered to conduct Miss Halcombe to the place, the proprietor of the Asylum remaining in the house for a few minutes to attend to a case which required his services, and then engaging to join his visitor in the grounds.\n\nThe nurse led Miss Halcombe to a distant part of the property, which was prettily laid out, and after looking about her a little, turned into a turf walk, shaded by a shrubbery on either side. About half-way down this walk two women were slowly approaching. The nurse pointed to them and said, “There is Anne Catherick, ma’am, with the attendant who waits on her. The attendant will answer any questions you wish to put.” With those words the nurse left her to return to the duties of the house.\n\nMiss Halcombe advanced on her side, and the women advanced on theirs. When they were within a dozen paces of each other, one of the women stopped for an instant, looked eagerly at the strange lady, shook off the nurse’s grasp on her, and the next moment rushed into Miss Halcombe’s arms. In that moment Miss Halcombe recognised her sister—recognised the dead-alive.\n\nFortunately for the success of the measures taken subsequently, no one was present at that moment but the nurse. She was a young woman, and she was so startled that she was at first quite incapable of interfering. When she was able to do so her whole services were required by Miss Halcombe, who had for the moment sunk altogether in the effort to keep her own senses under the shock of the discovery. After waiting a few minutes in the fresh air and the cool shade, her natural energy and courage helped her a little, and she became sufficiently mistress of herself to feel the necessity of recalling her presence of mind for her unfortunate sister’s sake.\n\nShe obtained permission to speak alone with the patient, on condition that they both remained well within the nurse’s view. There was no time for questions—there was only time for Miss Halcombe to impress on the unhappy lady the necessity of controlling herself, and to assure her of immediate help and rescue if she did so. The prospect of escaping from the Asylum by obedience to her sister’s directions was sufficient to quiet Lady Glyde, and to make her understand what was required of her. Miss Halcombe next returned to the nurse, placed all the gold she then had in her pocket (three sovereigns) in the nurse’s hands, and asked when and where she could speak to her alone.\n\nThe woman was at first surprised and distrustful. But on Miss Halcombe’s declaring that she only wanted to put some questions which she was too much agitated to ask at that moment, and that she had no intention of misleading the nurse into any dereliction of duty, the woman took the money, and proposed three o’clock on the next day as the time for the interview. She might then slip out for half an hour, after the patients had dined, and she would meet the lady in a retired place, outside the high north wall which screened the grounds of the house. Miss Halcombe had only time to assent, and to whisper to her sister that she should hear from her on the next day, when the proprietor of the Asylum joined them. He noticed his visitor’s agitation, which Miss Halcombe accounted for by saying that her interview with Anne Catherick had a little startled her at first. She took her leave as soon after as possible—that is to say, as soon as she could summon courage to force herself from the presence of her unfortunate sister.\n\nA very little reflection, when the capacity to reflect returned, convinced her that any attempt to identify Lady Glyde and to rescue her by legal means, would, even if successful, involve a delay that might be fatal to her sister’s intellects, which were shaken already by the horror of the situation to which she had been consigned. By the time Miss Halcombe had got back to London, she had determined to effect Lady Glyde’s escape privately, by means of the nurse.\n\nShe went at once to her stockbroker, and sold out of the funds all the little property she possessed, amounting to rather less than seven hundred pounds. Determined, if necessary, to pay the price of her sister’s liberty with every farthing she had in the world, she repaired the next day, having the whole sum about her in bank-notes, to her appointment outside the Asylum wall.\n\nThe nurse was there. Miss Halcombe approached the subject cautiously by many preliminary questions. She discovered, among other particulars, that the nurse who had in former times attended on the true Anne Catherick had been held responsible (although she was not to blame for it) for the patient’s escape, and had lost her place in consequence. The same penalty, it was added, would attach to the person then speaking to her, if the supposed Anne Catherick was missing a second time; and, moreover, the nurse in this case had an especial interest in keeping her place. She was engaged to be married, and she and her future husband were waiting till they could save, together, between two and three hundred pounds to start in business. The nurse’s wages were good, and she might succeed, by strict economy, in contributing her small share towards the sum required in two years’ time.\n\nOn this hint Miss Halcombe spoke. She declared that the supposed Anne Catherick was nearly related to her, that she had been placed in the Asylum under a fatal mistake, and that the nurse would be doing a good and a Christian action in being the means of restoring them to one another. Before there was time to start a single objection, Miss Halcombe took four bank-notes of a hundred pounds each from her pocket-book, and offered them to the woman, as a compensation for the risk she was to run, and for the loss of her place.\n\nThe nurse hesitated, through sheer incredulity and surprise. Miss Halcombe pressed the point on her firmly.\n\n“You will be doing a good action,” she repeated; “you will be helping the most injured and unhappy woman alive. There is your marriage portion for a reward. Bring her safely to me here, and I will put these four bank-notes into your hand before I claim her.”\n\n“Will you give me a letter saying those words, which I can show to my sweetheart when he asks how I got the money?” inquired the woman.\n\n“I will bring the letter with me, ready written and signed,” answered Miss Halcombe.\n\n“Then I’ll risk it,” said the nurse.\n\n“When?”\n\n“To-morrow.”\n\nIt was hastily agreed between them that Miss Halcombe should return early the next morning and wait out of sight among the trees—always, however, keeping near the quiet spot of ground under the north wall. The nurse could fix no time for her appearance, caution requiring that she should wait and be guided by circumstances. On that understanding they separated.\n\nMiss Halcombe was at her place, with the promised letter and the promised bank-notes, before ten the next morning. She waited more than an hour and a half. At the end of that time the nurse came quickly round the corner of the wall holding Lady Glyde by the arm. The moment they met Miss Halcombe put the bank-notes and the letter into her hand, and the sisters were united again.\n\nThe nurse had dressed Lady Glyde, with excellent forethought, in a bonnet, veil, and shawl of her own. Miss Halcombe only detained her to suggest a means of turning the pursuit in a false direction, when the escape was discovered at the Asylum. She was to go back to the house, to mention in the hearing of the other nurses that Anne Catherick had been inquiring latterly about the distance from London to Hampshire, to wait till the last moment, before discovery was inevitable, and then to give the alarm that Anne was missing. The supposed inquiries about Hampshire, when communicated to the owner of the Asylum, would lead him to imagine that his patient had returned to Blackwater Park, under the influence of the delusion which made her persist in asserting herself to be Lady Glyde, and the first pursuit would, in all probability, be turned in that direction.\n\nThe nurse consented to follow these suggestions, the more readily as they offered her the means of securing herself against any worse consequences than the loss of her place, by remaining in the Asylum, and so maintaining the appearance of innocence, at least. She at once returned to the house, and Miss Halcombe lost no time in taking her sister back with her to London. They caught the afternoon train to Carlisle the same afternoon, and arrived at Limmeridge, without accident or difficulty of any kind, that night.\n\nDuring the latter part of their journey they were alone in the carriage, and Miss Halcombe was able to collect such remembrances of the past as her sister’s confused and weakened memory was able to recall. The terrible story of the conspiracy so obtained was presented in fragments, sadly incoherent in themselves, and widely detached from each other. Imperfect as the revelation was, it must nevertheless be recorded here before this explanatory narrative closes with the events of the next day at Limmeridge House.\n\nLady Glyde’s recollection of the events which followed her departure from Blackwater Park began with her arrival at the London terminus of the South Western Railway. She had omitted to make a memorandum beforehand of the day on which she took the journey. All hope of fixing that important date by any evidence of hers, or of Mrs. Michelson’s, must be given up for lost.\n\nOn the arrival of the train at the platform Lady Glyde found Count Fosco waiting for her. He was at the carriage door as soon as the porter could open it. The train was unusually crowded, and there was great confusion in getting the luggage. Some person whom Count Fosco brought with him procured the luggage which belonged to Lady Glyde. It was marked with her name. She drove away alone with the Count in a vehicle which she did not particularly notice at the time.\n\nHer first question, on leaving the terminus, referred to Miss Halcombe. The Count informed her that Miss Halcombe had not yet gone to Cumberland, after-consideration having caused him to doubt the prudence of her taking so long a journey without some days’ previous rest.\n\nLady Glyde next inquired whether her sister was then staying in the Count’s house. Her recollection of the answer was confused, her only distinct impression in relation to it being that the Count declared he was then taking her to see Miss Halcombe. Lady Glyde’s experience of London was so limited that she could not tell, at the time, through what streets they were driving. But they never left the streets, and they never passed any gardens or trees. When the carriage stopped, it stopped in a small street behind a square—a square in which there were shops, and public buildings, and many people. From these recollections (of which Lady Glyde was certain) it seems quite clear that Count Fosco did not take her to his own residence in the suburb of St. John’s Wood.\n\nThey entered the house, and went upstairs to a back room, either on the first or second floor. The luggage was carefully brought in. A female servant opened the door, and a man with a dark beard, apparently a foreigner, met them in the hall, and with great politeness showed them the way upstairs. In answer to Lady Glyde’s inquiries, the Count assured her that Miss Halcombe was in the house, and that she should be immediately informed of her sister’s arrival. He and the foreigner then went away and left her by herself in the room. It was poorly furnished as a sitting-room, and it looked out on the backs of houses.\n\nThe place was remarkably quiet—no footsteps went up or down the stairs—she only heard in the room beneath her a dull, rumbling sound of men’s voices talking. Before she had been long left alone the Count returned, to explain that Miss Halcombe was then taking rest, and could not be disturbed for a little while. He was accompanied into the room by a gentleman (an Englishman), whom he begged to present as a friend of his.\n\nAfter this singular introduction—in the course of which no names, to the best of Lady Glyde’s recollection, had been mentioned—she was left alone with the stranger. He was perfectly civil, but he startled and confused her by some odd questions about herself, and by looking at her, while he asked them, in a strange manner. After remaining a short time he went out, and a minute or two afterwards a second stranger—also an Englishman—came in. This person introduced himself as another friend of Count Fosco’s, and he, in his turn, looked at her very oddly, and asked some curious questions—never, as well as she could remember, addressing her by name, and going out again, after a little while, like the first man. By this time she was so frightened about herself, and so uneasy about her sister, that she had thoughts of venturing downstairs again, and claiming the protection and assistance of the only woman she had seen in the house—the servant who answered the door.\n\nJust as she had risen from her chair, the Count came back into the room.\n\nThe moment he appeared she asked anxiously how long the meeting between her sister and herself was to be still delayed. At first he returned an evasive answer, but on being pressed, he acknowledged, with great apparent reluctance, that Miss Halcombe was by no means so well as he had hitherto represented her to be. His tone and manner, in making this reply, so alarmed Lady Glyde, or rather so painfully increased the uneasiness which she had felt in the company of the two strangers, that a sudden faintness overcame her, and she was obliged to ask for a glass of water. The Count called from the door for water, and for a bottle of smelling-salts. Both were brought in by the foreign-looking man with the beard. The water, when Lady Glyde attempted to drink it, had so strange a taste that it increased her faintness, and she hastily took the bottle of salts from Count Fosco, and smelt at it. Her head became giddy on the instant. The Count caught the bottle as it dropped out of her hand, and the last impression of which she was conscious was that he held it to her nostrils again.\n\nFrom this point her recollections were found to be confused, fragmentary, and difficult to reconcile with any reasonable probability.\n\nHer own impression was that she recovered her senses later in the evening, that she then left the house, that she went (as she had previously arranged to go, at Blackwater Park) to Mrs. Vesey’s—that she drank tea there, and that she passed the night under Mrs. Vesey’s roof. She was totally unable to say how, or when, or in what company she left the house to which Count Fosco had brought her. But she persisted in asserting that she had been to Mrs. Vesey’s, and still more extraordinary, that she had been helped to undress and get to bed by Mrs. Rubelle! She could not remember what the conversation was at Mrs. Vesey’s or whom she saw there besides that lady, or why Mrs. Rubelle should have been present in the house to help her.\n\nHer recollection of what happened to her the next morning was still more vague and unreliable.\n\nShe had some dim idea of driving out (at what hour she could not say) with Count Fosco, and with Mrs. Rubelle again for a female attendant. But when, and why, she left Mrs. Vesey she could not tell; neither did she know what direction the carriage drove in, or where it set her down, or whether the Count and Mrs. Rubelle did or did not remain with her all the time she was out. At this point in her sad story there was a total blank. She had no impressions of the faintest kind to communicate—no idea whether one day, or more than one day, had passed—until she came to herself suddenly in a strange place, surrounded by women who were all unknown to her.\n\nThis was the Asylum. Here she first heard herself called by Anne Catherick’s name, and here, as a last remarkable circumstance in the story of the conspiracy, her own eyes informed her that she had Anne Catherick’s clothes on. The nurse, on the first night in the Asylum, had shown her the marks on each article of her underclothing as it was taken off, and had said, not at all irritably or unkindly, “Look at your own name on your own clothes, and don’t worry us all any more about being Lady Glyde. She’s dead and buried, and you’re alive and hearty. Do look at your clothes now! There it is, in good marking ink, and there you will find it on all your old things, which we have kept in the house—Anne Catherick, as plain as print!” And there it was, when Miss Halcombe examined the linen her sister wore, on the night of their arrival at Limmeridge House.\n\nThese were the only recollections—all of them uncertain, and some of them contradictory—which could be extracted from Lady Glyde by careful questioning on the journey to Cumberland. Miss Halcombe abstained from pressing her with any inquiries relating to events in the Asylum—her mind being but too evidently unfit to bear the trial of reverting to them. It was known, by the voluntary admission of the owner of the mad-house, that she was received there on the twenty-seventh of July. From that date until the fifteenth of October (the day of her rescue) she had been under restraint, her identity with Anne Catherick systematically asserted, and her sanity, from first to last, practically denied. Faculties less delicately balanced, constitutions less tenderly organised, must have suffered under such an ordeal as this. No man could have gone through it and come out of it unchanged.\n\nArriving at Limmeridge late on the evening of the fifteenth, Miss Halcombe wisely resolved not to attempt the assertion of Lady Glyde’s identity until the next day.\n\nThe first thing in the morning she went to Mr. Fairlie’s room, and using all possible cautions and preparations beforehand, at last told him in so many words what had happened. As soon as his first astonishment and alarm had subsided, he angrily declared that Miss Halcombe had allowed herself to be duped by Anne Catherick. He referred her to Count Fosco’s letter, and to what she had herself told him of the personal resemblance between Anne and his deceased niece, and he positively declined to admit to his presence, even for one minute only, a madwoman, whom it was an insult and an outrage to have brought into his house at all.\n\nMiss Halcombe left the room—waited till the first heat of her indignation had passed away—decided on reflection that Mr. Fairlie should see his niece in the interests of common humanity before he closed his doors on her as a stranger—and thereupon, without a word of previous warning, took Lady Glyde with her to his room. The servant was posted at the door to prevent their entrance, but Miss Halcombe insisted on passing him, and made her way into Mr. Fairlie’s presence, leading her sister by the hand.\n\nThe scene that followed, though it only lasted for a few minutes, was too painful to be described—Miss Halcombe herself shrank from referring to it. Let it be enough to say that Mr. Fairlie declared, in the most positive terms, that he did not recognise the woman who had been brought into his room—that he saw nothing in her face and manner to make him doubt for a moment that his niece lay buried in Limmeridge churchyard, and that he would call on the law to protect him if before the day was over she was not removed from the house.\n\nTaking the very worst view of Mr. Fairlie’s selfishness, indolence, and habitual want of feeling, it was manifestly impossible to suppose that he was capable of such infamy as secretly recognising and openly disowning his brother’s child. Miss Halcombe humanely and sensibly allowed all due force to the influence of prejudice and alarm in preventing him from fairly exercising his perceptions, and accounted for what had happened in that way. But when she next put the servants to the test, and found that they too were, in every case, uncertain, to say the least of it, whether the lady presented to them was their young mistress or Anne Catherick, of whose resemblance to her they had all heard, the sad conclusion was inevitable that the change produced in Lady Glyde’s face and manner by her imprisonment in the Asylum was far more serious than Miss Halcombe had at first supposed. The vile deception which had asserted her death defied exposure even in the house where she was born, and among the people with whom she had lived.\n\nIn a less critical situation the effort need not have been given up as hopeless even yet.\n\nFor example, the maid, Fanny, who happened to be then absent from Limmeridge, was expected back in two days, and there would be a chance of gaining her recognition to start with, seeing that she had been in much more constant communication with her mistress, and had been much more heartily attached to her than the other servants. Again, Lady Glyde might have been privately kept in the house or in the village to wait until her health was a little recovered and her mind was a little steadied again. When her memory could be once more trusted to serve her, she would naturally refer to persons and events in the past with a certainty and a familiarity which no impostor could simulate, and so the fact of her identity, which her own appearance had failed to establish, might subsequently be proved, with time to help her, by the surer test of her own words.\n\nBut the circumstances under which she had regained her freedom rendered all recourse to such means as these simply impracticable. The pursuit from the Asylum, diverted to Hampshire for the time only, would infallibly next take the direction of Cumberland. The persons appointed to seek the fugitive might arrive at Limmeridge House at a few hours’ notice, and in Mr. Fairlie’s present temper of mind they might count on the immediate exertion of his local influence and authority to assist them. The commonest consideration for Lady Glyde’s safety forced on Miss Halcombe the necessity of resigning the struggle to do her justice, and of removing her at once from the place of all others that was now most dangerous to her—the neighbourhood of her own home.\n\nAn immediate return to London was the first and wisest measure of security which suggested itself. In the great city all traces of them might be most speedily and most surely effaced. There were no preparations to make—no farewell words of kindness to exchange with any one. On the afternoon of that memorable day of the sixteenth Miss Halcombe roused her sister to a last exertion of courage, and without a living soul to wish them well at parting, the two took their way into the world alone, and turned their backs for ever on Limmeridge House.\n\nThey had passed the hill above the churchyard, when Lady Glyde insisted on turning back to look her last at her mother’s grave. Miss Halcombe tried to shake her resolution, but, in this one instance, tried in vain. She was immovable. Her dim eyes lit with a sudden fire, and flashed through the veil that hung over them—her wasted fingers strengthened moment by moment round the friendly arm by which they had held so listlessly till this time. I believe in my soul that the hand of God was pointing their way back to them, and that the most innocent and the most afflicted of His creatures was chosen in that dread moment to see it.\n\nThey retraced their steps to the burial-ground, and by that act sealed the future of our three lives.\n\n# III\n\nThis was the story of the past—the story so far as we knew it then.\n\nTwo obvious conclusions presented themselves to my mind after hearing it. In the first place, I saw darkly what the nature of the conspiracy had been, how chances had been watched, and how circumstances had been handled to ensure impunity to a daring and an intricate crime. While all details were still a mystery to me, the vile manner in which the personal resemblance between the woman in white and Lady Glyde had been turned to account was clear beyond a doubt. It was plain that Anne Catherick had been introduced into Count Fosco’s house as Lady Glyde—it was plain that Lady Glyde had taken the dead woman’s place in the Asylum—the substitution having been so managed as to make innocent people (the doctor and the two servants certainly, and the owner of the mad-house in all probability) accomplices in the crime.\n\nThe second conclusion came as the necessary consequence of the first. We three had no mercy to expect from Count Fosco and Sir Percival Glyde. The success of the conspiracy had brought with it a clear gain to those two men of thirty thousand pounds—twenty thousand to one, ten thousand to the other through his wife. They had that interest, as well as other interests, in ensuring their impunity from exposure, and they would leave no stone unturned, no sacrifice unattempted, no treachery untried, to discover the place in which their victim was concealed, and to part her from the only friends she had in the world—Marian Halcombe and myself.\n\nThe sense of this serious peril—a peril which every day and every hour might bring nearer and nearer to us—was the one influence that guided me in fixing the place of our retreat. I chose it in the far east of London, where there were fewest idle people to lounge and look about them in the streets. I chose it in a poor and a populous neighbourhood—because the harder the struggle for existence among the men and women about us, the less the risk of their having the time or taking the pains to notice chance strangers who came among them. These were the great advantages I looked to, but our locality was a gain to us also in another and a hardly less important respect. We could live cheaply by the daily work of my hands, and could save every farthing we possessed to forward the purpose, the righteous purpose, of redressing an infamous wrong—which, from first to last, I now kept steadily in view.\n\nIn a week’s time Marian Halcombe and I had settled how the course of our new lives should be directed.\n\nThere were no other lodgers in the house, and we had the means of going in and out without passing through the shop. I arranged, for the present at least, that neither Marian nor Laura should stir outside the door without my being with them, and that in my absence from home they should let no one into their rooms on any pretence whatever. This rule established, I went to a friend whom I had known in former days—a wood engraver in large practice—to seek for employment, telling him, at the same time, that I had reasons for wishing to remain unknown.\n\nHe at once concluded that I was in debt, expressed his regret in the usual forms, and then promised to do what he could to assist me. I left his false impression undisturbed, and accepted the work he had to give. He knew that he could trust my experience and my industry. I had what he wanted, steadiness and facility, and though my earnings were but small, they sufficed for our necessities. As soon as we could feel certain of this, Marian Halcombe and I put together what we possessed. She had between two and three hundred pounds left of her own property, and I had nearly as much remaining from the purchase-money obtained by the sale of my drawing-master’s practice before I left England. Together we made up between us more than four hundred pounds. I deposited this little fortune in a bank, to be kept for the expense of those secret inquiries and investigations which I was determined to set on foot, and to carry on by myself if I could find no one to help me. We calculated our weekly expenditure to the last farthing, and we never touched our little fund except in Laura’s interests and for Laura’s sake.\n\nThe house-work, which, if we had dared trust a stranger near us, would have been done by a servant, was taken on the first day, taken as her own right, by Marian Halcombe. “What a woman’s hands ARE fit for,” she said, “early and late, these hands of mine shall do.” They trembled as she held them out. The wasted arms told their sad story of the past, as she turned up the sleeves of the poor plain dress that she wore for safety’s sake; but the unquenchable spirit of the woman burnt bright in her even yet. I saw the big tears rise thick in her eyes, and fall slowly over her cheeks as she looked at me. She dashed them away with a touch of her old energy, and smiled with a faint reflection of her old good spirits. “Don’t doubt my courage, Walter,” she pleaded, “it’s my weakness that cries, not ME. The house-work shall conquer it if I can’t.” And she kept her word—the victory was won when we met in the evening, and she sat down to rest. Her large steady black eyes looked at me with a flash of their bright firmness of bygone days. “I am not quite broken down yet,” she said. “I am worth trusting with my share of the work.” Before I could answer, she added in a whisper, “And worth trusting with my share in the risk and the danger too. Remember that, if the time comes!”\n\nI did remember it when the time came.\n\nAs early as the end of October the daily course of our lives had assumed its settled direction, and we three were as completely isolated in our place of concealment as if the house we lived in had been a desert island, and the great network of streets and the thousands of our fellow-creatures all round us the waters of an illimitable sea. I could now reckon on some leisure time for considering what my future plan of action should be, and how I might arm myself most securely at the outset for the coming struggle with Sir Percival and the Count.\n\nI gave up all hope of appealing to my recognition of Laura, or to Marian’s recognition of her, in proof of her identity. If we had loved her less dearly, if the instinct implanted in us by that love had not been far more certain than any exercise of reasoning, far keener than any process of observation, even we might have hesitated on first seeing her.\n\nThe outward changes wrought by the suffering and the terror of the past had fearfully, almost hopelessly, strengthened the fatal resemblance between Anne Catherick and herself. In my narrative of events at the time of my residence in Limmeridge House, I have recorded, from my own observation of the two, how the likeness, striking as it was when viewed generally, failed in many important points of similarity when tested in detail. In those former days, if they had both been seen together side by side, no person could for a moment have mistaken them one for the other—as has happened often in the instances of twins. I could not say this now. The sorrow and suffering which I had once blamed myself for associating even by a passing thought with the future of Laura Fairlie, HAD set their profaning marks on the youth and beauty of her face; and the fatal resemblance which I had once seen and shuddered at seeing, in idea only, was now a real and living resemblance which asserted itself before my own eyes. Strangers, acquaintances, friends even who could not look at her as we looked, if she had been shown to them in the first days of her rescue from the Asylum, might have doubted if she were the Laura Fairlie they had once seen, and doubted without blame.\n\nThe one remaining chance, which I had at first thought might be trusted to serve us—the chance of appealing to her recollection of persons and events with which no impostor could be familiar, was proved, by the sad test of our later experience, to be hopeless. Every little caution that Marian and I practised towards her—every little remedy we tried, to strengthen and steady slowly the weakened, shaken faculties, was a fresh protest in itself against the risk of turning her mind back on the troubled and the terrible past.\n\nThe only events of former days which we ventured on encouraging her to recall were the little trivial domestic events of that happy time at Limmeridge, when I first went there and taught her to draw. The day when I roused those remembrances by showing her the sketch of the summer-house which she had given me on the morning of our farewell, and which had never been separated from me since, was the birthday of our first hope. Tenderly and gradually, the memory of the old walks and drives dawned upon her, and the poor weary pining eyes looked at Marian and at me with a new interest, with a faltering thoughtfulness in them, which from that moment we cherished and kept alive. I bought her a little box of colours, and a sketch-book like the old sketch-book which I had seen in her hands on the morning that we first met. Once again—oh me, once again!—at spare hours saved from my work, in the dull London light, in the poor London room, I sat by her side to guide the faltering touch, to help the feeble hand. Day by day I raised and raised the new interest till its place in the blank of her existence was at last assured—till she could think of her drawing and talk of it, and patiently practise it by herself, with some faint reflection of the innocent pleasure in my encouragement, the growing enjoyment in her own progress, which belonged to the lost life and the lost happiness of past days.\n\nWe helped her mind slowly by this simple means, we took her out between us to walk on fine days, in a quiet old City square near at hand, where there was nothing to confuse or alarm her—we spared a few pounds from the fund at the banker’s to get her wine, and the delicate strengthening food that she required—we amused her in the evenings with children’s games at cards, with scrap-books full of prints which I borrowed from the engraver who employed me—by these, and other trifling attentions like them, we composed her and steadied her, and hoped all things, as cheerfully as we could from time and care, and love that never neglected and never despaired of her. But to take her mercilessly from seclusion and repose—to confront her with strangers, or with acquaintances who were little better than strangers—to rouse the painful impressions of her past life which we had so carefully hushed to rest—this, even in her own interests, we dared not do. Whatever sacrifices it cost, whatever long, weary, heartbreaking delays it involved, the wrong that had been inflicted on her, if mortal means could grapple it, must be redressed without her knowledge and without her help.\n\nThis resolution settled, it was next necessary to decide how the first risk should be ventured, and what the first proceedings should be.\n\nAfter consulting with Marian, I resolved to begin by gathering together as many facts as could be collected—then to ask the advice of Mr. Kyrle (whom we knew we could trust), and to ascertain from him, in the first instance, if the legal remedy lay fairly within our reach. I owed it to Laura’s interests not to stake her whole future on my own unaided exertions, so long as there was the faintest prospect of strengthening our position by obtaining reliable assistance of any kind.\n\nThe first source of information to which I applied was the journal kept at Blackwater Park by Marian Halcombe. There were passages in this diary relating to myself which she thought it best that I should not see. Accordingly, she read to me from the manuscript, and I took the notes I wanted as she went on. We could only find time to pursue this occupation by sitting up late at night. Three nights were devoted to the purpose, and were enough to put me in possession of all that Marian could tell.\n\nMy next proceeding was to gain as much additional evidence as I could procure from other people without exciting suspicion. I went myself to Mrs. Vesey to ascertain if Laura’s impression of having slept there was correct or not. In this case, from consideration for Mrs. Vesey’s age and infirmity, and in all subsequent cases of the same kind from considerations of caution, I kept our real position a secret, and was always careful to speak of Laura as “the late Lady Glyde.”\n\nMrs. Vesey’s answer to my inquiries only confirmed the apprehensions which I had previously felt. Laura had certainly written to say she would pass the night under the roof of her old friend—but she had never been near the house.\n\nHer mind in this instance, and, as I feared, in other instances besides, confusedly presented to her something which she had only intended to do in the false light of something which she had really done. The unconscious contradiction of herself was easy to account for in this way—but it was likely to lead to serious results. It was a stumble on the threshold at starting—it was a flaw in the evidence which told fatally against us.\n\nWhen I next asked for the letter which Laura had written to Mrs. Vesey from Blackwater Park, it was given to me without the envelope, which had been thrown into the wastepaper basket, and long since destroyed. In the letter itself no date was mentioned—not even the day of the week. It only contained these lines:—“Dearest Mrs. Vesey, I am in sad distress and anxiety, and I may come to your house to-morrow night, and ask for a bed. I can’t tell you what is the matter in this letter—I write it in such fear of being found out that I can fix my mind on nothing. Pray be at home to see me. I will give you a thousand kisses, and tell you everything. Your affectionate Laura.” What help was there in those lines? None.\n\nOn returning from Mrs. Vesey’s, I instructed Marian to write (observing the same caution which I practised myself) to Mrs. Michelson. She was to express, if she pleased, some general suspicion of Count Fosco’s conduct, and she was to ask the housekeeper to supply us with a plain statement of events, in the interests of truth. While we were waiting for the answer, which reached us in a week’s time, I went to the doctor in St. John’s Wood, introducing myself as sent by Miss Halcombe to collect, if possible, more particulars of her sister’s last illness than Mr. Kyrle had found the time to procure. By Mr. Goodricke’s assistance, I obtained a copy of the certificate of death, and an interview with the woman (Jane Gould) who had been employed to prepare the body for the grave. Through this person I also discovered a means of communicating with the servant, Hester Pinhorn. She had recently left her place in consequence of a disagreement with her mistress, and she was lodging with some people in the neighbourhood whom Mrs. Gould knew. In the manner here indicated I obtained the Narratives of the housekeeper, of the doctor, of Jane Gould, and of Hester Pinhorn, exactly as they are presented in these pages.\n\nFurnished with such additional evidence as these documents afforded, I considered myself to be sufficiently prepared for a consultation with Mr. Kyrle, and Marian wrote accordingly to mention my name to him, and to specify the day and hour at which I requested to see him on private business.\n\nThere was time enough in the morning for me to take Laura out for her walk as usual, and to see her quietly settled at her drawing afterwards. She looked up at me with a new anxiety in her face as I rose to leave the room, and her fingers began to toy doubtfully, in the old way, with the brushes and pencils on the table.\n\n“You are not tired of me yet?” she said. “You are not going away because you are tired of me? I will try to do better—I will try to get well. Are you as fond of me, Walter, as you used to be, now I am so pale and thin, and so slow in learning to draw?”\n\nShe spoke as a child might have spoken, she showed me her thoughts as a child might have shown them. I waited a few minutes longer—waited to tell her that she was dearer to me now than she had ever been in the past times. “Try to get well again,” I said, encouraging the new hope in the future which I saw dawning in her mind, “try to get well again, for Marian’s sake and for mine.”\n\n“Yes,” she said to herself, returning to her drawing. “I must try, because they are both so fond of me.” She suddenly looked up again. “Don’t be gone long! I can’t get on with my drawing, Walter, when you are not here to help me.”\n\n“I shall soon be back, my darling—soon be back to see how you are getting on.”\n\nMy voice faltered a little in spite of me. I forced myself from the room. It was no time, then, for parting with the self-control which might yet serve me in my need before the day was out.\n\nAs I opened the door, I beckoned to Marian to follow me to the stairs. It was necessary to prepare her for a result which I felt might sooner or later follow my showing myself openly in the streets.\n\n“I shall, in all probability, be back in a few hours,” I said, “and you will take care, as usual, to let no one inside the doors in my absence. But if anything happens——”\n\n“What can happen?” she interposed quickly. “Tell me plainly, Walter, if there is any danger, and I shall know how to meet it.”\n\n“The only danger,” I replied, “is that Sir Percival Glyde may have been recalled to London by the news of Laura’s escape. You are aware that he had me watched before I left England, and that he probably knows me by sight, although I don’t know him?”\n\nShe laid her hand on my shoulder and looked at me in anxious silence. I saw she understood the serious risk that threatened us.\n\n“It is not likely,” I said, “that I shall be seen in London again so soon, either by Sir Percival himself or by the persons in his employ. But it is barely possible that an accident may happen. In that case, you will not be alarmed if I fail to return to-night, and you will satisfy any inquiry of Laura’s with the best excuse that you can make for me? If I find the least reason to suspect that I am watched, I will take good care that no spy follows me back to this house. Don’t doubt my return, Marian, however it may be delayed—and fear nothing.”\n\n“Nothing!” she answered firmly. “You shall not regret, Walter, that you have only a woman to help you.” She paused, and detained me for a moment longer. “Take care!” she said, pressing my hand anxiously—“take care!”\n\nI left her, and set forth to pave the way for discovery—the dark and doubtful way, which began at the lawyer’s door.\n\n# IV\n\nNo circumstance of the slightest importance happened on my way to the offices of Messrs. Gilmore & Kyrle, in Chancery Lane.\n\nWhile my card was being taken in to Mr. Kyrle, a consideration occurred to me which I deeply regretted not having thought of before. The information derived from Marian’s diary made it a matter of certainty that Count Fosco had opened her first letter from Blackwater Park to Mr. Kyrle, and had, by means of his wife, intercepted the second. He was therefore well aware of the address of the office, and he would naturally infer that if Marian wanted advice and assistance, after Laura’s escape from the Asylum, she would apply once more to the experience of Mr. Kyrle. In this case the office in Chancery Lane was the very first place which he and Sir Percival would cause to be watched, and if the same persons were chosen for the purpose who had been employed to follow me, before my departure from England, the fact of my return would in all probability be ascertained on that very day. I had thought, generally, of the chances of my being recognised in the streets, but the special risk connected with the office had never occurred to me until the present moment. It was too late now to repair this unfortunate error in judgment—too late to wish that I had made arrangements for meeting the lawyer in some place privately appointed beforehand. I could only resolve to be cautious on leaving Chancery Lane, and not to go straight home again under any circumstances whatever.\n\nAfter waiting a few minutes I was shown into Mr. Kyrle’s private room. He was a pale, thin, quiet, self-possessed man, with a very attentive eye, a very low voice, and a very undemonstrative manner—not (as I judged) ready with his sympathy where strangers were concerned, and not at all easy to disturb in his professional composure. A better man for my purpose could hardly have been found. If he committed himself to a decision at all, and if the decision was favourable, the strength of our case was as good as proved from that moment.\n\n“Before I enter on the business which brings me here,” I said, “I ought to warn you, Mr. Kyrle, that the shortest statement I can make of it may occupy some little time.”\n\n“My time is at Miss Halcombe’s disposal,” he replied. “Where any interests of hers are concerned, I represent my partner personally, as well as professionally. It was his request that I should do so, when he ceased to take an active part in business.”\n\n“May I inquire whether Mr. Gilmore is in England?”\n\n“He is not, he is living with his relatives in Germany. His health has improved, but the period of his return is still uncertain.”\n\nWhile we were exchanging these few preliminary words, he had been searching among the papers before him, and he now produced from them a sealed letter. I thought he was about to hand the letter to me, but, apparently changing his mind, he placed it by itself on the table, settled himself in his chair, and silently waited to hear what I had to say.\n\nWithout wasting a moment in prefatory words of any sort, I entered on my narrative, and put him in full possession of the events which have already been related in these pages.\n\nLawyer as he was to the very marrow of his bones, I startled him out of his professional composure. Expressions of incredulity and surprise, which he could not repress, interrupted me several times before I had done. I persevered, however, to the end, and as soon as I reached it, boldly asked the one important question—\n\n“What is your opinion, Mr. Kyrle?”\n\nHe was too cautious to commit himself to an answer without taking time to recover his self-possession first.\n\n“Before I give my opinion,” he said, “I must beg permission to clear the ground by a few questions.”\n\nHe put the questions—sharp, suspicious, unbelieving questions, which clearly showed me, as they proceeded, that he thought I was the victim of a delusion, and that he might even have doubted, but for my introduction to him by Miss Halcombe, whether I was not attempting the perpetration of a cunningly-designed fraud.\n\n“Do you believe that I have spoken the truth, Mr. Kyrle?” I asked, when he had done examining me.\n\n“So far as your own convictions are concerned, I am certain you have spoken the truth,” he replied. “I have the highest esteem for Miss Halcombe, and I have therefore every reason to respect a gentleman whose mediation she trusts in a matter of this kind. I will even go farther, if you like, and admit, for courtesy’s sake and for argument’s sake, that the identity of Lady Glyde as a living person is a proved fact to Miss Halcombe and yourself. But you come to me for a legal opinion. As a lawyer, and as a lawyer only, it is my duty to tell you, Mr. Hartright, that you have not the shadow of a case.”\n\n“You put it strongly, Mr. Kyrle.”\n\n“I will try to put it plainly as well. The evidence of Lady Glyde’s death is, on the face of it, clear and satisfactory. There is her aunt’s testimony to prove that she came to Count Fosco’s house, that she fell ill, and that she died. There is the testimony of the medical certificate to prove the death, and to show that it took place under natural circumstances. There is the fact of the funeral at Limmeridge, and there is the assertion of the inscription on the tomb. That is the case you want to overthrow. What evidence have you to support the declaration on your side that the person who died and was buried was not Lady Glyde? Let us run through the main points of your statement and see what they are worth. Miss Halcombe goes to a certain private Asylum, and there sees a certain female patient. It is known that a woman named Anne Catherick, and bearing an extraordinary personal resemblance to Lady Glyde, escaped from the Asylum; it is known that the person received there last July was received as Anne Catherick brought back; it is known that the gentleman who brought her back warned Mr. Fairlie that it was part of her insanity to be bent on personating his dead niece; and it is known that she did repeatedly declare herself in the Asylum (where no one believed her) to be Lady Glyde. These are all facts. What have you to set against them? Miss Halcombe’s recognition of the woman, which recognition after-events invalidate or contradict. Does Miss Halcombe assert her supposed sister’s identity to the owner of the Asylum, and take legal means for rescuing her? No, she secretly bribes a nurse to let her escape. When the patient has been released in this doubtful manner, and is taken to Mr. Fairlie, does he recognise her? Is he staggered for one instant in his belief of his niece’s death? No. Do the servants recognise her? No. Is she kept in the neighbourhood to assert her own identity, and to stand the test of further proceedings? No, she is privately taken to London. In the meantime you have recognised her also, but you are not a relative—you are not even an old friend of the family. The servants contradict you, and Mr. Fairlie contradicts Miss Halcombe, and the supposed Lady Glyde contradicts herself. She declares she passed the night in London at a certain house. Your own evidence shows that she has never been near that house, and your own admission is that her condition of mind prevents you from producing her anywhere to submit to investigation, and to speak for herself. I pass over minor points of evidence on both sides to save time, and I ask you, if this case were to go now into a court of law—to go before a jury, bound to take facts as they reasonably appear—where are your proofs?”\n\nI was obliged to wait and collect myself before I could answer him. It was the first time the story of Laura and the story of Marian had been presented to me from a stranger’s point of view—the first time the terrible obstacles that lay across our path had been made to show themselves in their true character.\n\n“There can be no doubt,” I said, “that the facts, as you have stated them, appear to tell against us, but——”\n\n“But you think those facts can be explained away,” interposed Mr. Kyrle. “Let me tell you the result of my experience on that point. When an English jury has to choose between a plain fact ON the surface and a long explanation UNDER the surface, it always takes the fact in preference to the explanation. For example, Lady Glyde (I call the lady you represent by that name for argument’s sake) declares she has slept at a certain house, and it is proved that she has not slept at that house. You explain this circumstance by entering into the state of her mind, and deducing from it a metaphysical conclusion. I don’t say the conclusion is wrong—I only say that the jury will take the fact of her contradicting herself in preference to any reason for the contradiction that you can offer.”\n\n“But is it not possible,” I urged, “by dint of patience and exertion, to discover additional evidence? Miss Halcombe and I have a few hundred pounds——”\n\nHe looked at me with a half-suppressed pity, and shook his head.\n\n“Consider the subject, Mr. Hartright, from your own point of view,” he said. “If you are right about Sir Percival Glyde and Count Fosco (which I don’t admit, mind), every imaginable difficulty would be thrown in the way of your getting fresh evidence. Every obstacle of litigation would be raised—every point in the case would be systematically contested—and by the time we had spent our thousands instead of our hundreds, the final result would, in all probability, be against us. Questions of identity, where instances of personal resemblance are concerned, are, in themselves, the hardest of all questions to settle—the hardest, even when they are free from the complications which beset the case we are now discussing. I really see no prospect of throwing any light whatever on this extraordinary affair. Even if the person buried in Limmeridge churchyard be not Lady Glyde, she was, in life, on your own showing, so like her, that we should gain nothing, if we applied for the necessary authority to have the body exhumed. In short, there is no case, Mr. Hartright—there is really no case.”\n\nI was determined to believe that there WAS a case, and in that determination shifted my ground, and appealed to him once more.\n\n“Are there not other proofs that we might produce besides the proof of identity?” I asked.\n\n“Not as you are situated,” he replied. “The simplest and surest of all proofs, the proof by comparison of dates, is, as I understand, altogether out of your reach. If you could show a discrepancy between the date of the doctor’s certificate and the date of Lady Glyde’s journey to London, the matter would wear a totally different aspect, and I should be the first to say, Let us go on.”\n\n“That date may yet be recovered, Mr. Kyrle.”\n\n“On the day when it is recovered, Mr. Hartright, you will have a case. If you have any prospect, at this moment, of getting at it—tell me, and we shall see if I can advise you.”\n\nI considered. The housekeeper could not help us—Laura could not help us—Marian could not help us. In all probability, the only persons in existence who knew the date were Sir Percival and the Count.\n\n“I can think of no means of ascertaining the date at present,” I said, “because I can think of no persons who are sure to know it, but Count Fosco and Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nMr. Kyrle’s calmly attentive face relaxed, for the first time, into a smile.\n\n“With your opinion of the conduct of those two gentlemen,” he said, “you don’t expect help in that quarter, I presume? If they have combined to gain large sums of money by a conspiracy, they are not likely to confess it, at any rate.”\n\n“They may be forced to confess it, Mr. Kyrle.”\n\n“By whom?”\n\n“By me.”\n\nWe both rose. He looked me attentively in the face with more appearance of interest than he had shown yet. I could see that I had perplexed him a little.\n\n“You are very determined,” he said. “You have, no doubt, a personal motive for proceeding, into which it is not my business to inquire. If a case can be produced in the future, I can only say, my best assistance is at your service. At the same time I must warn you, as the money question always enters into the law question, that I see little hope, even if you ultimately established the fact of Lady Glyde’s being alive, of recovering her fortune. The foreigner would probably leave the country before proceedings were commenced, and Sir Percival’s embarrassments are numerous enough and pressing enough to transfer almost any sum of money he may possess from himself to his creditors. You are of course aware——”\n\nI stopped him at that point.\n\n“Let me beg that we may not discuss Lady Glyde’s affairs,” I said. “I have never known anything about them in former times, and I know nothing of them now—except that her fortune is lost. You are right in assuming that I have personal motives for stirring in this matter. I wish those motives to be always as disinterested as they are at the present moment——”\n\nHe tried to interpose and explain. I was a little heated, I suppose, by feeling that he had doubted me, and I went on bluntly, without waiting to hear him.\n\n“There shall be no money motive,” I said, “no idea of personal advantage in the service I mean to render to Lady Glyde. She has been cast out as a stranger from the house in which she was born—a lie which records her death has been written on her mother’s tomb—and there are two men, alive and unpunished, who are responsible for it. That house shall open again to receive her in the presence of every soul who followed the false funeral to the grave—that lie shall be publicly erased from the tombstone by the authority of the head of the family, and those two men shall answer for their crime to ME, though the justice that sits in tribunals is powerless to pursue them. I have given my life to that purpose, and, alone as I stand, if God spares me, I will accomplish it.”\n\nHe drew back towards his table, and said nothing. His face showed plainly that he thought my delusion had got the better of my reason, and that he considered it totally useless to give me any more advice.\n\n“We each keep our opinion, Mr. Kyrle,” I said, “and we must wait till the events of the future decide between us. In the meantime, I am much obliged to you for the attention you have given to my statement. You have shown me that the legal remedy lies, in every sense of the word, beyond our means. We cannot produce the law proof, and we are not rich enough to pay the law expenses. It is something gained to know that.”\n\nI bowed and walked to the door. He called me back and gave me the letter which I had seen him place on the table by itself at the beginning of our interview.\n\n“This came by post a few days ago,” he said. “Perhaps you will not mind delivering it? Pray tell Miss Halcombe, at the same time, that I sincerely regret being, thus far, unable to help her, except by advice, which will not be more welcome, I am afraid, to her than to you.”\n\nI looked at the letter while he was speaking. It was addressed to “Miss Halcombe. Care of Messrs. Gilmore & Kyrle, Chancery Lane.” The handwriting was quite unknown to me.\n\nOn leaving the room I asked one last question.\n\n“Do you happen to know,” I said, “if Sir Percival Glyde is still in Paris?”\n\n“He has returned to London,” replied Mr. Kyrle. “At least I heard so from his solicitor, whom I met yesterday.”\n\nAfter that answer I went out.\n\nOn leaving the office the first precaution to be observed was to abstain from attracting attention by stopping to look about me. I walked towards one of the quietest of the large squares on the north of Holborn, then suddenly stopped and turned round at a place where a long stretch of pavement was left behind me.\n\nThere were two men at the corner of the square who had stopped also, and who were standing talking together. After a moment’s reflection I turned back so as to pass them. One moved as I came near, and turned the corner leading from the square into the street. The other remained stationary. I looked at him as I passed and instantly recognised one of the men who had watched me before I left England.\n\nIf I had been free to follow my own instincts, I should probably have begun by speaking to the man, and have ended by knocking him down. But I was bound to consider consequences. If I once placed myself publicly in the wrong, I put the weapons at once into Sir Percival’s hands. There was no choice but to oppose cunning by cunning. I turned into the street down which the second man had disappeared, and passed him, waiting in a doorway. He was a stranger to me, and I was glad to make sure of his personal appearance in case of future annoyance. Having done this, I again walked northward till I reached the New Road. There I turned aside to the west (having the men behind me all the time), and waited at a point where I knew myself to be at some distance from a cab-stand, until a fast two-wheel cab, empty, should happen to pass me. One passed in a few minutes. I jumped in and told the man to drive rapidly towards Hyde Park. There was no second fast cab for the spies behind me. I saw them dart across to the other side of the road, to follow me by running, until a cab or a cab-stand came in their way. But I had the start of them, and when I stopped the driver and got out, they were nowhere in sight. I crossed Hyde Park and made sure, on the open ground, that I was free. When I at last turned my steps homewards, it was not till many hours later—not till after dark.\n\nI found Marian waiting for me alone in the little sitting-room. She had persuaded Laura to go to rest, after first promising to show me her drawing the moment I came in. The poor little dim faint sketch—so trifling in itself, so touching in its associations—was propped up carefully on the table with two books, and was placed where the faint light of the one candle we allowed ourselves might fall on it to the best advantage. I sat down to look at the drawing, and to tell Marian, in whispers, what had happened. The partition which divided us from the next room was so thin that we could almost hear Laura’s breathing, and we might have disturbed her if we had spoken aloud.\n\nMarian preserved her composure while I described my interview with Mr. Kyrle. But her face became troubled when I spoke next of the men who had followed me from the lawyer’s office, and when I told her of the discovery of Sir Percival’s return.\n\n“Bad news, Walter,” she said, “the worst news you could bring. Have you nothing more to tell me?”\n\n“I have something to give you,” I replied, handing her the note which Mr. Kyrle had confided to my care.\n\nShe looked at the address and recognised the handwriting instantly.\n\n“You know your correspondent?” I said.\n\n“Too well,” she answered. “My correspondent is Count Fosco.”\n\nWith that reply she opened the note. Her face flushed deeply while she read it—her eyes brightened with anger as she handed it to me to read in my turn.\n\nThe note contained these lines—\n\n“Impelled by honourable admiration—honourable to myself, honourable to you—I write, magnificent Marian, in the interests of your tranquillity, to say two consoling words—\n\n“Fear nothing!\n\n“Exercise your fine natural sense and remain in retirement. Dear and admirable woman, invite no dangerous publicity. Resignation is sublime—adopt it. The modest repose of home is eternally fresh—enjoy it. The storms of life pass harmless over the valley of Seclusion—dwell, dear lady, in the valley.\n\n“Do this and I authorise you to fear nothing. No new calamity shall lacerate your sensibilities—sensibilities precious to me as my own. You shall not be molested, the fair companion of your retreat shall not be pursued. She has found a new asylum in your heart. Priceless asylum!—I envy her and leave her there.\n\n“One last word of affectionate warning, of paternal caution, and I tear myself from the charm of addressing you—I close these fervent lines.\n\n“Advance no farther than you have gone already, compromise no serious interests, threaten nobody. Do not, I implore you, force me into action—ME, the Man of Action—when it is the cherished object of my ambition to be passive, to restrict the vast reach of my energies and my combinations for your sake. If you have rash friends, moderate their deplorable ardour. If Mr. Hartright returns to England, hold no communication with him. I walk on a path of my own, and Percival follows at my heels. On the day when Mr. Hartright crosses that path, he is a lost man.”\n\nThe only signature to these lines was the initial letter F, surrounded by a circle of intricate flourishes. I threw the letter on the table with all the contempt that I felt for it.\n\n“He is trying to frighten you—a sure sign that he is frightened himself,” I said.\n\nShe was too genuine a woman to treat the letter as I treated it. The insolent familiarity of the language was too much for her self-control. As she looked at me across the table, her hands clenched themselves in her lap, and the old quick fiery temper flamed out again brightly in her cheeks and her eyes.\n\n“Walter!” she said, “if ever those two men are at your mercy, and if you are obliged to spare one of them, don’t let it be the Count.”\n\n“I will keep this letter, Marian, to help my memory when the time comes.”\n\nShe looked at me attentively as I put the letter away in my pocket-book.\n\n“When the time comes?” she repeated. “Can you speak of the future as if you were certain of it?—certain after what you have heard in Mr. Kyrle’s office, after what has happened to you to-day?”\n\n“I don’t count the time from to-day, Marian. All I have done to-day is to ask another man to act for me. I count from to-morrow——”\n\n“Why from to-morrow?”\n\n“Because to-morrow I mean to act for myself.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“I shall go to Blackwater by the first train, and return, I hope, at night.”\n\n“To Blackwater!”\n\n“Yes. I have had time to think since I left Mr. Kyrle. His opinion on one point confirms my own. We must persist to the last in hunting down the date of Laura’s journey. The one weak point in the conspiracy, and probably the one chance of proving that she is a living woman, centre in the discovery of that date.”\n\n“You mean,” said Marian, “the discovery that Laura did not leave Blackwater Park till after the date of her death on the doctor’s certificate?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“What makes you think it might have been AFTER? Laura can tell us nothing of the time she was in London.”\n\n“But the owner of the Asylum told you that she was received there on the twenty-seventh of July. I doubt Count Fosco’s ability to keep her in London, and to keep her insensible to all that was passing around her, more than one night. In that case, she must have started on the twenty-sixth, and must have come to London one day after the date of her own death on the doctor’s certificate. If we can prove that date, we prove our case against Sir Percival and the Count.”\n\n“Yes, yes—I see! But how is the proof to be obtained?”\n\n“Mrs. Michelson’s narrative has suggested to me two ways of trying to obtain it. One of them is to question the doctor, Mr. Dawson, who must know when he resumed his attendance at Blackwater Park after Laura left the house. The other is to make inquiries at the inn to which Sir Percival drove away by himself at night. We know that his departure followed Laura’s after the lapse of a few hours, and we may get at the date in that way. The attempt is at least worth making, and to-morrow I am determined it shall be made.”\n\n“And suppose it fails—I look at the worst now, Walter; but I will look at the best if disappointments come to try us—suppose no one can help you at Blackwater?”\n\n“There are two men who can help me, and shall help me in London—Sir Percival and the Count. Innocent people may well forget the date—but THEY are guilty, and THEY know it. If I fail everywhere else, I mean to force a confession out of one or both of them on my own terms.”\n\nAll the woman flushed up in Marian’s face as I spoke.\n\n“Begin with the Count,” she whispered eagerly. “For my sake, begin with the Count.”\n\n“We must begin, for Laura’s sake, where there is the best chance of success,” I replied.\n\nThe colour faded from her face again, and she shook her head sadly.\n\n“Yes,” she said, “you are right—it was mean and miserable of me to say that. I try to be patient, Walter, and succeed better now than I did in happier times. But I have a little of my old temper still left, and it will get the better of me when I think of the Count!”\n\n“His turn will come,” I said. “But, remember, there is no weak place in his life that we know of yet.” I waited a little to let her recover her self-possession, and then spoke the decisive words—\n\n“Marian! There is a weak place we both know of in Sir Percival’s life——”\n\n“You mean the Secret!”\n\n“Yes: the Secret. It is our only sure hold on him. I can force him from his position of security, I can drag him and his villainy into the face of day, by no other means. Whatever the Count may have done, Sir Percival has consented to the conspiracy against Laura from another motive besides the motive of gain. You heard him tell the Count that he believed his wife knew enough to ruin him? You heard him say that he was a lost man if the secret of Anne Catherick was known?”\n\n“Yes! yes! I did.”\n\n“Well, Marian, when our other resources have failed us, I mean to know the Secret. My old superstition clings to me, even yet. I say again the woman in white is a living influence in our three lives. The End is appointed—the End is drawing us on—and Anne Catherick, dead in her grave, points the way to it still!”\n\nThe story of my first inquiries in Hampshire is soon told.\n\nMy early departure from London enabled me to reach Mr. Dawson’s house in the forenoon. Our interview, so far as the object of my visit was concerned, led to no satisfactory result.\n\nMr. Dawson’s books certainly showed when he had resumed his attendance on Miss Halcombe at Blackwater Park, but it was not possible to calculate back from this date with any exactness, without such help from Mrs. Michelson as I knew she was unable to afford. She could not say from memory (who, in similar cases, ever can?) how many days had elapsed between the renewal of the doctor’s attendance on his patient and the previous departure of Lady Glyde. She was almost certain of having mentioned the circumstance of the departure to Miss Halcombe, on the day after it happened—but then she was no more able to fix the date of the day on which this disclosure took place, than to fix the date of the day before, when Lady Glyde had left for London. Neither could she calculate, with any nearer approach to exactness, the time that had passed from the departure of her mistress, to the period when the undated letter from Madame Fosco arrived. Lastly, as if to complete the series of difficulties, the doctor himself, having been ill at the time, had omitted to make his usual entry of the day of the week and month when the gardener from Blackwater Park had called on him to deliver Mrs. Michelson’s message.\n\nHopeless of obtaining assistance from Mr. Dawson, I resolved to try next if I could establish the date of Sir Percival’s arrival at Knowlesbury.\n\nIt seemed like a fatality! When I reached Knowlesbury the inn was shut up, and bills were posted on the walls. The speculation had been a bad one, as I was informed, ever since the time of the railway. The new hotel at the station had gradually absorbed the business, and the old inn (which we knew to be the inn at which Sir Percival had put up), had been closed about two months since. The proprietor had left the town with all his goods and chattels, and where he had gone I could not positively ascertain from any one. The four people of whom I inquired gave me four different accounts of his plans and projects when he left Knowlesbury.\n\nThere were still some hours to spare before the last train left for London, and I drove back again in a fly from the Knowlesbury station to Blackwater Park, with the purpose of questioning the gardener and the person who kept the lodge. If they, too, proved unable to assist me, my resources for the present were at an end, and I might return to town.\n\nI dismissed the fly a mile distant from the park, and getting my directions from the driver, proceeded by myself to the house.\n\nAs I turned into the lane from the high-road, I saw a man, with a carpet-bag, walking before me rapidly on the way to the lodge. He was a little man, dressed in shabby black, and wearing a remarkably large hat. I set him down (as well as it was possible to judge) for a lawyer’s clerk, and stopped at once to widen the distance between us. He had not heard me, and he walked on out of sight, without looking back. When I passed through the gates myself, a little while afterwards, he was not visible—he had evidently gone on to the house.\n\nThere were two women in the lodge. One of them was old, the other I knew at once, by Marian’s description of her, to be Margaret Porcher.\n\nI asked first if Sir Percival was at the Park, and receiving a reply in the negative, inquired next when he had left it. Neither of the women could tell me more than that he had gone away in the summer. I could extract nothing from Margaret Porcher but vacant smiles and shakings of the head. The old woman was a little more intelligent, and I managed to lead her into speaking of the manner of Sir Percival’s departure, and of the alarm that it caused her. She remembered her master calling her out of bed, and remembered his frightening her by swearing—but the date at which the occurrence happened was, as she honestly acknowledged, “quite beyond her.”\n\nOn leaving the lodge I saw the gardener at work not far off. When I first addressed him, he looked at me rather distrustfully, but on my using Mrs. Michelson’s name, with a civil reference to himself, he entered into conversation readily enough. There is no need to describe what passed between us—it ended, as all my other attempts to discover the date had ended. The gardener knew that his master had driven away, at night, “some time in July, the last fortnight or the last ten days in the month”—and knew no more.\n\nWhile we were speaking together I saw the man in black, with the large hat, come out from the house, and stand at some little distance observing us.\n\nCertain suspicions of his errand at Blackwater Park had already crossed my mind. They were now increased by the gardener’s inability (or unwillingness) to tell me who the man was, and I determined to clear the way before me, if possible, by speaking to him. The plainest question I could put as a stranger would be to inquire if the house was allowed to be shown to visitors. I walked up to the man at once, and accosted him in those words.\n\nHis look and manner unmistakably betrayed that he knew who I was, and that he wanted to irritate me into quarrelling with him. His reply was insolent enough to have answered the purpose, if I had been less determined to control myself. As it was, I met him with the most resolute politeness, apologised for my involuntary intrusion (which he called a “trespass,”) and left the grounds. It was exactly as I suspected. The recognition of me when I left Mr. Kyrle’s office had been evidently communicated to Sir Percival Glyde, and the man in black had been sent to the Park in anticipation of my making inquiries at the house or in the neighbourhood. If I had given him the least chance of lodging any sort of legal complaint against me, the interference of the local magistrate would no doubt have been turned to account as a clog on my proceedings, and a means of separating me from Marian and Laura for some days at least.\n\nI was prepared to be watched on the way from Blackwater Park to the station, exactly as I had been watched in London the day before. But I could not discover at the time, whether I was really followed on this occasion or not. The man in black might have had means of tracking me at his disposal of which I was not aware, but I certainly saw nothing of him, in his own person, either on the way to the station, or afterwards on my arrival at the London terminus in the evening. I reached home on foot, taking the precaution, before I approached our own door, of walking round by the loneliest street in the neighbourhood, and there stopping and looking back more than once over the open space behind me. I had first learnt to use this stratagem against suspected treachery in the wilds of Central America—and now I was practising it again, with the same purpose and with even greater caution, in the heart of civilised London!\n\nNothing had happened to alarm Marian during my absence. She asked eagerly what success I had met with. When I told her she could not conceal her surprise at the indifference with which I spoke of the failure of my investigations thus far.\n\nThe truth was, that the ill-success of my inquiries had in no sense daunted me. I had pursued them as a matter of duty, and I had expected nothing from them. In the state of my mind at that time, it was almost a relief to me to know that the struggle was now narrowed to a trial of strength between myself and Sir Percival Glyde. The vindictive motive had mingled itself all along with my other and better motives, and I confess it was a satisfaction to me to feel that the surest way, the only way left, of serving Laura’s cause, was to fasten my hold firmly on the villain who had married her.\n\nWhile I acknowledge that I was not strong enough to keep my motives above the reach of this instinct of revenge, I can honestly say something in my own favour on the other side. No base speculation on the future relations of Laura and myself, and on the private and personal concessions which I might force from Sir Percival if I once had him at my mercy, ever entered my mind. I never said to myself, “If I do succeed, it shall be one result of my success that I put it out of her husband’s power to take her from me again.” I could not look at her and think of the future with such thoughts as those. The sad sight of the change in her from her former self, made the one interest of my love an interest of tenderness and compassion which her father or her brother might have felt, and which I felt, God knows, in my inmost heart. All my hopes looked no farther on now than to the day of her recovery. There, till she was strong again and happy again—there, till she could look at me as she had once looked, and speak to me as she had once spoken—the future of my happiest thoughts and my dearest wishes ended.\n\nThese words are written under no prompting of idle self-contemplation. Passages in this narrative are soon to come which will set the minds of others in judgment on my conduct. It is right that the best and the worst of me should be fairly balanced before that time.\n\nOn the morning after my return from Hampshire I took Marian upstairs into my working-room, and there laid before her the plan that I had matured thus far, for mastering the one assailable point in the life of Sir Percival Glyde.\n\nThe way to the Secret lay through the mystery, hitherto impenetrable to all of us, of the woman in white. The approach to that in its turn might be gained by obtaining the assistance of Anne Catherick’s mother, and the only ascertainable means of prevailing on Mrs. Catherick to act or to speak in the matter depended on the chance of my discovering local particulars and family particulars first of all from Mrs. Clements. After thinking the subject over carefully, I felt certain that I could only begin the new inquiries by placing myself in communication with the faithful friend and protectress of Anne Catherick.\n\nThe first difficulty then was to find Mrs. Clements.\n\nI was indebted to Marian’s quick perception for meeting this necessity at once by the best and simplest means. She proposed to write to the farm near Limmeridge (Todd’s Corner), to inquire whether Mrs. Clements had communicated with Mrs. Todd during the past few months. How Mrs. Clements had been separated from Anne it was impossible for us to say, but that separation once effected, it would certainly occur to Mrs. Clements to inquire after the missing woman in the neighbourhood of all others to which she was known to be most attached—the neighbourhood of Limmeridge. I saw directly that Marian’s proposal offered us a prospect of success, and she wrote to Mrs. Todd accordingly by that day’s post.\n\nWhile we were waiting for the reply, I made myself master of all the information Marian could afford on the subject of Sir Percival’s family, and of his early life. She could only speak on these topics from hearsay, but she was reasonably certain of the truth of what little she had to tell.\n\nSir Percival was an only child. His father, Sir Felix Glyde, had suffered from his birth under a painful and incurable deformity, and had shunned all society from his earliest years. His sole happiness was in the enjoyment of music, and he had married a lady with tastes similar to his own, who was said to be a most accomplished musician. He inherited the Blackwater property while still a young man. Neither he nor his wife after taking possession, made advances of any sort towards the society of the neighbourhood, and no one endeavoured to tempt them into abandoning their reserve, with the one disastrous exception of the rector of the parish.\n\nThe rector was the worst of all innocent mischief-makers—an over-zealous man. He had heard that Sir Felix had left College with the character of being little better than a revolutionist in politics and an infidel in religion, and he arrived conscientiously at the conclusion that it was his bounden duty to summon the lord of the manor to hear sound views enunciated in the parish church. Sir Felix fiercely resented the clergyman’s well-meant but ill-directed interference, insulting him so grossly and so publicly, that the families in the neighbourhood sent letters of indignant remonstrance to the Park, and even the tenants of the Blackwater property expressed their opinion as strongly as they dared. The baronet, who had no country tastes of any kind, and no attachment to the estate or to any one living on it, declared that society at Blackwater should never have a second chance of annoying him, and left the place from that moment.\n\nAfter a short residence in London he and his wife departed for the Continent, and never returned to England again. They lived part of the time in France and part in Germany—always keeping themselves in the strict retirement which the morbid sense of his own personal deformity had made a necessity to Sir Felix. Their son, Percival, had been born abroad, and had been educated there by private tutors. His mother was the first of his parents whom he lost. His father had died a few years after her, either in 1825 or 1826. Sir Percival had been in England, as a young man, once or twice before that period, but his acquaintance with the late Mr. Fairlie did not begin till after the time of his father’s death. They soon became very intimate, although Sir Percival was seldom, or never, at Limmeridge House in those days. Mr. Frederick Fairlie might have met him once or twice in Mr. Philip Fairlie’s company, but he could have known little of him at that or at any other time. Sir Percival’s only intimate friend in the Fairlie family had been Laura’s father.\n\nThese were all the particulars that I could gain from Marian. They suggested nothing which was useful to my present purpose, but I noted them down carefully, in the event of their proving to be of importance at any future period.\n\nMrs. Todd’s reply (addressed, by our own wish, to a post-office at some distance from us) had arrived at its destination when I went to apply for it. The chances, which had been all against us hitherto, turned from this moment in our favour. Mrs. Todd’s letter contained the first item of information of which we were in search.\n\nMrs. Clements, it appeared, had (as we had conjectured) written to Todd’s Corner, asking pardon in the first place for the abrupt manner in which she and Anne had left their friends at the farm-house (on the morning after I had met the woman in white in Limmeridge churchyard), and then informing Mrs. Todd of Anne’s disappearance, and entreating that she would cause inquiries to be made in the neighbourhood, on the chance that the lost woman might have strayed back to Limmeridge. In making this request, Mrs. Clements had been careful to add to it the address at which she might always be heard of, and that address Mrs. Todd now transmitted to Marian. It was in London, and within half an hour’s walk of our own lodging.\n\nIn the words of the proverb, I was resolved not to let the grass grow under my feet. The next morning I set forth to seek an interview with Mrs. Clements. This was my first step forward in the investigation. The story of the desperate attempt to which I now stood committed begins here.\n\n# VI\n\nThe address communicated by Mrs. Todd took me to a lodging-house situated in a respectable street near the Gray’s Inn Road.\n\nWhen I knocked the door was opened by Mrs. Clements herself. She did not appear to remember me, and asked what my business was. I recalled to her our meeting in Limmeridge churchyard at the close of my interview there with the woman in white, taking special care to remind her that I was the person who assisted Anne Catherick (as Anne had herself declared) to escape the pursuit from the Asylum. This was my only claim to the confidence of Mrs. Clements. She remembered the circumstance the moment I spoke of it, and asked me into the parlour, in the greatest anxiety to know if I had brought her any news of Anne.\n\nIt was impossible for me to tell her the whole truth without, at the same time, entering into particulars on the subject of the conspiracy, which it would have been dangerous to confide to a stranger. I could only abstain most carefully from raising any false hopes, and then explain that the object of my visit was to discover the persons who were really responsible for Anne’s disappearance. I even added, so as to exonerate myself from any after-reproach of my own conscience, that I entertained not the least hope of being able to trace her—that I believed we should never see her alive again—and that my main interest in the affair was to bring to punishment two men whom I suspected to be concerned in luring her away, and at whose hands I and some dear friends of mine had suffered a grievous wrong. With this explanation I left it to Mrs. Clements to say whether our interest in the matter (whatever difference there might be in the motives which actuated us) was not the same, and whether she felt any reluctance to forward my object by giving me such information on the subject of my inquiries as she happened to possess.\n\nThe poor woman was at first too much confused and agitated to understand thoroughly what I said to her. She could only reply that I was welcome to anything she could tell me in return for the kindness I had shown to Anne; but as she was not very quick and ready, at the best of times, in talking to strangers, she would beg me to put her in the right way, and to say where I wished her to begin.\n\nKnowing by experience that the plainest narrative attainable from persons who are not accustomed to arrange their ideas, is the narrative which goes far enough back at the beginning to avoid all impediments of retrospection in its course, I asked Mrs. Clements to tell me first what had happened after she had left Limmeridge, and so, by watchful questioning, carried her on from point to point, till we reached the period of Anne’s disappearance.\n\nThe substance of the information which I thus obtained was as follows:—\n\nOn leaving the farm at Todd’s Corner, Mrs. Clements and Anne had travelled that day as far as Derby, and had remained there a week on Anne’s account. They had then gone on to London, and had lived in the lodging occupied by Mrs. Clements at that time for a month or more, when circumstances connected with the house and the landlord had obliged them to change their quarters. Anne’s terror of being discovered in London or its neighbourhood, whenever they ventured to walk out, had gradually communicated itself to Mrs. Clements, and she had determined on removing to one of the most out-of-the-way places in England—to the town of Grimsby in Lincolnshire, where her deceased husband had passed all his early life. His relatives were respectable people settled in the town—they had always treated Mrs. Clements with great kindness, and she thought it impossible to do better than go there and take the advice of her husband’s friends. Anne would not hear of returning to her mother at Welmingham, because she had been removed to the Asylum from that place, and because Sir Percival would be certain to go back there and find her again. There was serious weight in this objection, and Mrs. Clements felt that it was not to be easily removed.\n\nAt Grimsby the first serious symptoms of illness had shown themselves in Anne. They appeared soon after the news of Lady Glyde’s marriage had been made public in the newspapers, and had reached her through that medium.\n\nThe medical man who was sent for to attend the sick woman discovered at once that she was suffering from a serious affection of the heart. The illness lasted long, left her very weak, and returned at intervals, though with mitigated severity, again and again. They remained at Grimsby, in consequence, during the first half of the new year, and there they might probably have stayed much longer, but for the sudden resolution which Anne took at this time to venture back to Hampshire, for the purpose of obtaining a private interview with Lady Glyde.\n\nMrs. Clements did all in her power to oppose the execution of this hazardous and unaccountable project. No explanation of her motives was offered by Anne, except that she believed the day of her death was not far off, and that she had something on her mind which must be communicated to Lady Glyde, at any risk, in secret. Her resolution to accomplish this purpose was so firmly settled that she declared her intention of going to Hampshire by herself if Mrs. Clements felt any unwillingness to go with her. The doctor, on being consulted, was of opinion that serious opposition to her wishes would, in all probability, produce another and perhaps a fatal fit of illness, and Mrs. Clements, under this advice, yielded to necessity, and once more, with sad forebodings of trouble and danger to come, allowed Anne Catherick to have her own way.\n\nOn the journey from London to Hampshire Mrs. Clements discovered that one of their fellow-passengers was well acquainted with the neighbourhood of Blackwater, and could give her all the information she needed on the subject of localities. In this way she found out that the only place they could go to, which was not dangerously near to Sir Percival’s residence, was a large village called Sandon. The distance here from Blackwater Park was between three and four miles—and that distance, and back again, Anne had walked on each occasion when she had appeared in the neighbourhood of the lake.\n\nFor the few days during which they were at Sandon without being discovered they had lived a little away from the village, in the cottage of a decent widow-woman who had a bedroom to let, and whose discreet silence Mrs. Clements had done her best to secure, for the first week at least. She had also tried hard to induce Anne to be content with writing to Lady Glyde, in the first instance; but the failure of the warning contained in the anonymous letter sent to Limmeridge had made Anne resolute to speak this time, and obstinate in the determination to go on her errand alone.\n\nMrs. Clements, nevertheless, followed her privately on each occasion when she went to the lake, without, however, venturing near enough to the boat-house to be witness of what took place there. When Anne returned for the last time from the dangerous neighbourhood, the fatigue of walking, day after day, distances which were far too great for her strength, added to the exhausting effect of the agitation from which she had suffered, produced the result which Mrs. Clements had dreaded all along. The old pain over the heart and the other symptoms of the illness at Grimsby returned, and Anne was confined to her bed in the cottage.\n\nIn this emergency the first necessity, as Mrs. Clements knew by experience, was to endeavour to quiet Anne’s anxiety of mind, and for this purpose the good woman went herself the next day to the lake, to try if she could find Lady Glyde (who would be sure, as Anne said, to take her daily walk to the boat-house), and prevail on her to come back privately to the cottage near Sandon. On reaching the outskirts of the plantation Mrs. Clements encountered, not Lady Glyde, but a tall, stout, elderly gentleman, with a book in his hand—in other words, Count Fosco.\n\nThe Count, after looking at her very attentively for a moment, asked if she expected to see any one in that place, and added, before she could reply, that he was waiting there with a message from Lady Glyde, but that he was not quite certain whether the person then before him answered the description of the person with whom he was desired to communicate.\n\nUpon this Mrs. Clements at once confided her errand to him, and entreated that he would help to allay Anne’s anxiety by trusting his message to her. The Count most readily and kindly complied with her request. The message, he said, was a very important one. Lady Glyde entreated Anne and her good friend to return immediately to London, as she felt certain that Sir Percival would discover them if they remained any longer in the neighbourhood of Blackwater. She was herself going to London in a short time, and if Mrs. Clements and Anne would go there first, and would let her know what their address was, they should hear from her and see her in a fortnight or less. The Count added that he had already attempted to give a friendly warning to Anne herself, but that she had been too much startled by seeing that he was a stranger to let him approach and speak to her.\n\nTo this Mrs. Clements replied, in the greatest alarm and distress, that she asked nothing better than to take Anne safely to London, but that there was no present hope of removing her from the dangerous neighbourhood, as she lay ill in her bed at that moment. The Count inquired if Mrs. Clements had sent for medical advice, and hearing that she had hitherto hesitated to do so, from the fear of making their position publicly known in the village, informed her that he was himself a medical man, and that he would go back with her if she pleased, and see what could be done for Anne. Mrs. Clements (feeling a natural confidence in the Count, as a person trusted with a secret message from Lady Glyde) gratefully accepted the offer, and they went back together to the cottage.\n\nAnne was asleep when they got there. The Count started at the sight of her (evidently from astonishment at her resemblance to Lady Glyde). Poor Mrs. Clements supposed that he was only shocked to see how ill she was. He would not allow her to be awakened—he was contented with putting questions to Mrs. Clements about her symptoms, with looking at her, and with lightly touching her pulse. Sandon was a large enough place to have a grocer’s and druggist’s shop in it, and thither the Count went to write his prescription and to get the medicine made up. He brought it back himself, and told Mrs. Clements that the medicine was a powerful stimulant, and that it would certainly give Anne strength to get up and bear the fatigue of a journey to London of only a few hours. The remedy was to be administered at stated times on that day and on the day after. On the third day she would be well enough to travel, and he arranged to meet Mrs. Clements at the Blackwater station, and to see them off by the midday train. If they did not appear he would assume that Anne was worse, and would proceed at once to the cottage.\n\nAs events turned out, no such emergency as this occurred.\n\nThis medicine had an extraordinary effect on Anne, and the good results of it were helped by the assurance Mrs. Clements could now give her that she would soon see Lady Glyde in London. At the appointed day and time (when they had not been quite so long as a week in Hampshire altogether), they arrived at the station. The Count was waiting there for them, and was talking to an elderly lady, who appeared to be going to travel by the train to London also. He most kindly assisted them, and put them into the carriage himself, begging Mrs. Clements not to forget to send her address to Lady Glyde. The elderly lady did not travel in the same compartment, and they did not notice what became of her on reaching the London terminus. Mrs. Clements secured respectable lodgings in a quiet neighbourhood, and then wrote, as she had engaged to do, to inform Lady Glyde of the address.\n\nA little more than a fortnight passed, and no answer came.\n\nAt the end of that time a lady (the same elderly lady whom they had seen at the station) called in a cab, and said that she came from Lady Glyde, who was then at an hotel in London, and who wished to see Mrs. Clements, for the purpose of arranging a future interview with Anne. Mrs. Clements expressed her willingness (Anne being present at the time, and entreating her to do so) to forward the object in view, especially as she was not required to be away from the house for more than half an hour at the most. She and the elderly lady (clearly Madame Fosco) then left in the cab. The lady stopped the cab, after it had driven some distance, at a shop before they got to the hotel, and begged Mrs. Clements to wait for her for a few minutes while she made a purchase that had been forgotten. She never appeared again.\n\nAfter waiting some time Mrs. Clements became alarmed, and ordered the cabman to drive back to her lodgings. When she got there, after an absence of rather more than half an hour, Anne was gone.\n\nThe only information to be obtained from the people of the house was derived from the servant who waited on the lodgers. She had opened the door to a boy from the street, who had left a letter for “the young woman who lived on the second floor” (the part of the house which Mrs. Clements occupied). The servant had delivered the letter, had then gone downstairs, and five minutes afterwards had observed Anne open the front door and go out, dressed in her bonnet and shawl. She had probably taken the letter with her, for it was not to be found, and it was therefore impossible to tell what inducement had been offered to make her leave the house. It must have been a strong one, for she would never stir out alone in London of her own accord. If Mrs. Clements had not known this by experience nothing would have induced her to go away in the cab, even for so short a time as half an hour only.\n\nAs soon as she could collect her thoughts, the first idea that naturally occurred to Mrs. Clements was to go and make inquiries at the Asylum, to which she dreaded that Anne had been taken back.\n\nShe went there the next day, having been informed of the locality in which the house was situated by Anne herself. The answer she received (her application having in all probability been made a day or two before the false Anne Catherick had really been consigned to safe keeping in the Asylum) was, that no such person had been brought back there. She had then written to Mrs. Catherick at Welmingham to know if she had seen or heard anything of her daughter, and had received an answer in the negative. After that reply had reached her, she was at the end of her resources, and perfectly ignorant where else to inquire or what else to do. From that time to this she had remained in total ignorance of the cause of Anne’s disappearance and of the end of Anne’s story.\n\n# VII\n\nThus far the information which I had received from Mrs. Clements—though it established facts of which I had not previously been aware—was of a preliminary character only.\n\nIt was clear that the series of deceptions which had removed Anne Catherick to London, and separated her from Mrs. Clements, had been accomplished solely by Count Fosco and the Countess, and the question whether any part of the conduct of husband or wife had been of a kind to place either of them within reach of the law might be well worthy of future consideration. But the purpose I had now in view led me in another direction than this. The immediate object of my visit to Mrs. Clements was to make some approach at least to the discovery of Sir Percival’s secret, and she had said nothing as yet which advanced me on my way to that important end. I felt the necessity of trying to awaken her recollections of other times, persons, and events than those on which her memory had hitherto been employed, and when I next spoke I spoke with that object indirectly in view.\n\n“I wish I could be of any help to you in this sad calamity,” I said. “All I can do is to feel heartily for your distress. If Anne had been your own child, Mrs. Clements, you could have shown her no truer kindness—you could have made no readier sacrifices for her sake.”\n\n“There’s no great merit in that, sir,” said Mrs. Clements simply. “The poor thing was as good as my own child to me. I nursed her from a baby, sir, bringing her up by hand—and a hard job it was to rear her. It wouldn’t go to my heart so to lose her if I hadn’t made her first short clothes and taught her to walk. I always said she was sent to console me for never having chick or child of my own. And now she’s lost the old times keep coming back to my mind, and even at my age I can’t help crying about her—I can’t indeed, sir!”\n\nI waited a little to give Mrs. Clements time to compose herself. Was the light that I had been looking for so long glimmering on me—far off, as yet—in the good woman’s recollections of Anne’s early life?\n\n“Did you know Mrs. Catherick before Anne was born?” I asked.\n\n“Not very long, sir—not above four months. We saw a great deal of each other in that time, but we were never very friendly together.”\n\nHer voice was steadier as she made that reply. Painful as many of her recollections might be, I observed that it was unconsciously a relief to her mind to revert to the dimly-seen troubles of the past, after dwelling so long on the vivid sorrows of the present.\n\n“Were you and Mrs. Catherick neighbours?” I inquired, leading her memory on as encouragingly as I could.\n\n“Yes, sir—neighbours at Old Welmingham.”\n\n“OLD Welmingham? There are two places of that name, then, in Hampshire?”\n\n“Well, sir, there used to be in those days—better than three-and-twenty years ago. They built a new town about two miles off, convenient to the river—and Old Welmingham, which was never much more than a village, got in time to be deserted. The new town is the place they call Welmingham now—but the old parish church is the parish church still. It stands by itself, with the houses pulled down or gone to ruin all round it. I’ve lived to see sad changes. It was a pleasant, pretty place in my time.”\n\n“Did you live there before your marriage, Mrs. Clements?”\n\n“No, sir—I’m a Norfolk woman. It wasn’t the place my husband belonged to either. He was from Grimsby, as I told you, and he served his apprenticeship there. But having friends down south, and hearing of an opening, he got into business at Southampton. It was in a small way, but he made enough for a plain man to retire on, and settled at Old Welmingham. I went there with him when he married me. We were neither of us young, but we lived very happy together—happier than our neighbour, Mr. Catherick, lived along with his wife when they came to Old Welmingham a year or two afterwards.”\n\n“Was your husband acquainted with them before that?”\n\n“With Catherick, sir—not with his wife. She was a stranger to both of us. Some gentlemen had made interest for Catherick, and he got the situation of clerk at Welmingham church, which was the reason of his coming to settle in our neighbourhood. He brought his newly-married wife along with him, and we heard in course of time she had been lady’s-maid in a family that lived at Varneck Hall, near Southampton. Catherick had found it a hard matter to get her to marry him, in consequence of her holding herself uncommonly high. He had asked and asked, and given the thing up at last, seeing she was so contrary about it. When he HAD given it up she turned contrary just the other way, and came to him of her own accord, without rhyme or reason seemingly. My poor husband always said that was the time to have given her a lesson. But Catherick was too fond of her to do anything of the sort—he never checked her either before they were married or after. He was a quick man in his feelings, letting them carry him a deal too far, now in one way and now in another, and he would have spoilt a better wife than Mrs. Catherick if a better had married him. I don’t like to speak ill of any one, sir, but she was a heartless woman, with a terrible will of her own—fond of foolish admiration and fine clothes, and not caring to show so much as decent outward respect to Catherick, kindly as he always treated her. My husband said he thought things would turn out badly when they first came to live near us, and his words proved true. Before they had been quite four months in our neighbourhood there was a dreadful scandal and a miserable break-up in their household. Both of them were in fault—I am afraid both of them were equally in fault.”\n\n“You mean both husband and wife?”\n\n“Oh, no, sir! I don’t mean Catherick—he was only to be pitied. I meant his wife and the person—”\n\n“And the person who caused the scandal?”\n\n“Yes, sir. A gentleman born and brought up, who ought to have set a better example. You know him, sir—and my poor dear Anne knew him only too well.”\n\n“Sir Percival Glyde?”\n\n“Yes, Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nMy heart beat fast—I thought I had my hand on the clue. How little I knew then of the windings of the labyrinths which were still to mislead me!\n\n“Did Sir Percival live in your neighbourhood at that time?” I asked.\n\n“No, sir. He came among us as a stranger. His father had died not long before in foreign parts. I remember he was in mourning. He put up at the little inn on the river (they have pulled it down since that time), where gentlemen used to go to fish. He wasn’t much noticed when he first came—it was a common thing enough for gentlemen to travel from all parts of England to fish in our river.”\n\n“Did he make his appearance in the village before Anne was born?”\n\n“Yes, sir. Anne was born in the June month of eighteen hundred and twenty-seven—and I think he came at the end of April or the beginning of May.”\n\n“Came as a stranger to all of you? A stranger to Mrs. Catherick as well as to the rest of the neighbours?”\n\n“So we thought at first, sir. But when the scandal broke out, nobody believed they were strangers. I remember how it happened as well as if it was yesterday. Catherick came into our garden one night, and woke us by throwing up a handful of gravel from the walk at our window. I heard him beg my husband, for the Lord’s sake, to come down and speak to him. They were a long time together talking in the porch. When my husband came back upstairs he was all of a tremble. He sat down on the side of the bed and he says to me, ‘Lizzie! I always told you that woman was a bad one—I always said she would end ill, and I’m afraid in my own mind that the end has come already. Catherick has found a lot of lace handkerchiefs, and two fine rings, and a new gold watch and chain, hid away in his wife’s drawer—things that nobody but a born lady ought ever to have—and his wife won’t say how she came by them.’ ‘Does he think she stole them?’ says I. ‘No,’ says he, ‘stealing would be bad enough. But it’s worse than that, she’s had no chance of stealing such things as those, and she’s not a woman to take them if she had. They’re gifts, Lizzie—there’s her own initials engraved inside the watch—and Catherick has seen her talking privately, and carrying on as no married woman should, with that gentleman in mourning, Sir Percival Glyde. Don’t you say anything about it—I’ve quieted Catherick for to-night. I’ve told him to keep his tongue to himself, and his eyes and his ears open, and to wait a day or two, till he can be quite certain.’ ‘I believe you are both of you wrong,’ says I. ‘It’s not in nature, comfortable and respectable as she is here, that Mrs. Catherick should take up with a chance stranger like Sir Percival Glyde.’ ‘Ay, but is he a stranger to her?’ says my husband. ‘You forget how Catherick’s wife came to marry him. She went to him of her own accord, after saying No over and over again when he asked her. There have been wicked women before her time, Lizzie, who have used honest men who loved them as a means of saving their characters, and I’m sorely afraid this Mrs. Catherick is as wicked as the worst of them. We shall see,’ says my husband, ‘we shall soon see.’ And only two days afterwards we did see.”\n\nMrs. Clements waited for a moment before she went on. Even in that moment, I began to doubt whether the clue that I thought I had found was really leading me to the central mystery of the labyrinth after all. Was this common, too common, story of a man’s treachery and a woman’s frailty the key to a secret which had been the lifelong terror of Sir Percival Glyde?\n\n“Well, sir, Catherick took my husband’s advice and waited,” Mrs. Clements continued. “And as I told you, he hadn’t long to wait. On the second day he found his wife and Sir Percival whispering together quite familiar, close under the vestry of the church. I suppose they thought the neighbourhood of the vestry was the last place in the world where anybody would think of looking after them, but, however that may be, there they were. Sir Percival, being seemingly surprised and confounded, defended himself in such a guilty way that poor Catherick (whose quick temper I have told you of already) fell into a kind of frenzy at his own disgrace, and struck Sir Percival. He was no match (and I am sorry to say it) for the man who had wronged him, and he was beaten in the cruelest manner, before the neighbours, who had come to the place on hearing the disturbance, could run in to part them. All this happened towards evening, and before nightfall, when my husband went to Catherick’s house, he was gone, nobody knew where. No living soul in the village ever saw him again. He knew too well, by that time, what his wife’s vile reason had been for marrying him, and he felt his misery and disgrace, especially after what had happened to him with Sir Percival, too keenly. The clergyman of the parish put an advertisement in the paper begging him to come back, and saying that he should not lose his situation or his friends. But Catherick had too much pride and spirit, as some people said—too much feeling, as I think, sir—to face his neighbours again, and try to live down the memory of his disgrace. My husband heard from him when he had left England, and heard a second time, when he was settled and doing well in America. He is alive there now, as far as I know, but none of us in the old country—his wicked wife least of all—are ever likely to set eyes on him again.”\n\n“What became of Sir Percival?” I inquired. “Did he stay in the neighbourhood?”\n\n“Not he, sir. The place was too hot to hold him. He was heard at high words with Mrs. Catherick the same night when the scandal broke out, and the next morning he took himself off.”\n\n“And Mrs. Catherick? Surely she never remained in the village among the people who knew of her disgrace?”\n\n“She did, sir. She was hard enough and heartless enough to set the opinions of all her neighbours at flat defiance. She declared to everybody, from the clergyman downwards, that she was the victim of a dreadful mistake, and that all the scandal-mongers in the place should not drive her out of it, as if she was a guilty woman. All through my time she lived at Old Welmingham, and after my time, when the new town was building, and the respectable neighbours began moving to it, she moved too, as if she was determined to live among them and scandalise them to the very last. There she is now, and there she will stop, in defiance of the best of them, to her dying day.”\n\n“But how has she lived through all these years?” I asked. “Was her husband able and willing to help her?”\n\n“Both able and willing, sir,” said Mrs. Clements. “In the second letter he wrote to my good man, he said she had borne his name, and lived in his home, and, wicked as she was, she must not starve like a beggar in the street. He could afford to make her some small allowance, and she might draw for it quarterly at a place in London.”\n\n“Did she accept the allowance?”\n\n“Not a farthing of it, sir. She said she would never be beholden to Catherick for bit or drop, if she lived to be a hundred. And she has kept her word ever since. When my poor dear husband died, and left all to me, Catherick’s letter was put in my possession with the other things, and I told her to let me know if she was ever in want. ‘I’ll let all England know I’m in want,’ she said, ‘before I tell Catherick, or any friend of Catherick’s. Take that for your answer, and give it to HIM for an answer, if he ever writes again.’”\n\n“Do you suppose that she had money of her own?”\n\n“Very little, if any, sir. It was said, and said truly, I am afraid, that her means of living came privately from Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\nAfter that last reply I waited a little, to reconsider what I had heard. If I unreservedly accepted the story so far, it was now plain that no approach, direct or indirect, to the Secret had yet been revealed to me, and that the pursuit of my object had ended again in leaving me face to face with the most palpable and the most disheartening failure.\n\nBut there was one point in the narrative which made me doubt the propriety of accepting it unreservedly, and which suggested the idea of something hidden below the surface.\n\nI could not account to myself for the circumstance of the clerk’s guilty wife voluntarily living out all her after-existence on the scene of her disgrace. The woman’s own reported statement that she had taken this strange course as a practical assertion of her innocence did not satisfy me. It seemed, to my mind, more natural and more probable to assume that she was not so completely a free agent in this matter as she had herself asserted. In that case, who was the likeliest person to possess the power of compelling her to remain at Welmingham? The person unquestionably from whom she derived the means of living. She had refused assistance from her husband, she had no adequate resources of her own, she was a friendless, degraded woman—from what source should she derive help but from the source at which report pointed—Sir Percival Glyde?\n\nReasoning on these assumptions, and always bearing in mind the one certain fact to guide me, that Mrs. Catherick was in possession of the Secret, I easily understood that it was Sir Percival’s interest to keep her at Welmingham, because her character in that place was certain to isolate her from all communication with female neighbours, and to allow her no opportunities of talking incautiously in moments of free intercourse with inquisitive bosom friends. But what was the mystery to be concealed? Not Sir Percival’s infamous connection with Mrs. Catherick’s disgrace, for the neighbours were the very people who knew of it—not the suspicion that he was Anne’s father, for Welmingham was the place in which that suspicion must inevitably exist. If I accepted the guilty appearances described to me as unreservedly as others had accepted them, if I drew from them the same superficial conclusion which Mr. Catherick and all his neighbours had drawn, where was the suggestion, in all that I had heard, of a dangerous secret between Sir Percival and Mrs. Catherick, which had been kept hidden from that time to this?\n\nAnd yet, in those stolen meetings, in those familiar whisperings between the clerk’s wife and “the gentleman in mourning,” the clue to discovery existed beyond a doubt.\n\nWas it possible that appearances in this case had pointed one way while the truth lay all the while unsuspected in another direction? Could Mrs. Catherick’s assertion, that she was the victim of a dreadful mistake, by any possibility be true? Or, assuming it to be false, could the conclusion which associated Sir Percival with her guilt have been founded in some inconceivable error? Had Sir Percival, by any chance, courted the suspicion that was wrong for the sake of diverting from himself some other suspicion that was right? Here—if I could find it—here was the approach to the Secret, hidden deep under the surface of the apparently unpromising story which I had just heard.\n\nMy next questions were now directed to the one object of ascertaining whether Mr. Catherick had or had not arrived truly at the conviction of his wife’s misconduct. The answers I received from Mrs. Clements left me in no doubt whatever on that point. Mrs. Catherick had, on the clearest evidence, compromised her reputation, while a single woman, with some person unknown, and had married to save her character. It had been positively ascertained, by calculations of time and place into which I need not enter particularly, that the daughter who bore her husband’s name was not her husband’s child.\n\nThe next object of inquiry, whether it was equally certain that Sir Percival must have been the father of Anne, was beset by far greater difficulties. I was in no position to try the probabilities on one side or on the other in this instance by any better test than the test of personal resemblance.\n\n“I suppose you often saw Sir Percival when he was in your village?” I said.\n\n“Yes, sir, very often,” replied Mrs. Clements.\n\n“Did you ever observe that Anne was like him?”\n\n“She was not at all like him, sir.”\n\n“Was she like her mother, then?”\n\n“Not like her mother either, sir. Mrs. Catherick was dark, and full in the face.”\n\nNot like her mother and not like her (supposed) father. I knew that the test by personal resemblance was not to be implicitly trusted, but, on the other hand, it was not to be altogether rejected on that account. Was it possible to strengthen the evidence by discovering any conclusive facts in relation to the lives of Mrs. Catherick and Sir Percival before they either of them appeared at Old Welmingham? When I asked my next questions I put them with this view.\n\n“When Sir Percival first arrived in your neighbourhood,” I said, “did you hear where he had come from last?”\n\n“No, sir. Some said from Blackwater Park, and some said from Scotland—but nobody knew.”\n\n“Was Mrs. Catherick living in service at Varneck Hall immediately before her marriage?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“And had she been long in her place?”\n\n“Three or four years, sir; I am not quite certain which.”\n\n“Did you ever hear the name of the gentleman to whom Varneck Hall belonged at that time?”\n\n“Yes, sir. His name was Major Donthorne.”\n\n“Did Mr. Catherick, or did any one else you knew, ever hear that Sir Percival was a friend of Major Donthorne’s, or ever see Sir Percival in the neighbourhood of Varneck Hall?”\n\n“Catherick never did, sir, that I can remember—nor any one else either, that I know of.”\n\nI noted down Major Donthorne’s name and address, on the chance that he might still be alive, and that it might be useful at some future time to apply to him. Meanwhile, the impression on my mind was now decidedly adverse to the opinion that Sir Percival was Anne’s father, and decidedly favourable to the conclusion that the secret of his stolen interviews with Mrs. Catherick was entirely unconnected with the disgrace which the woman had inflicted on her husband’s good name. I could think of no further inquiries which I might make to strengthen this impression—I could only encourage Mrs. Clements to speak next of Anne’s early days, and watch for any chance-suggestion which might in this way offer itself to me.\n\n“I have not heard yet,” I said, “how the poor child, born in all this sin and misery, came to be trusted, Mrs. Clements, to your care.”\n\n“There was nobody else, sir, to take the little helpless creature in hand,” replied Mrs. Clements. “The wicked mother seemed to hate it—as if the poor baby was in fault!—from the day it was born. My heart was heavy for the child, and I made the offer to bring it up as tenderly as if it was my own.”\n\n“Did Anne remain entirely under your care from that time?”\n\n“Not quite entirely, sir. Mrs. Catherick had her whims and fancies about it at times, and used now and then to lay claim to the child, as if she wanted to spite me for bringing it up. But these fits of hers never lasted for long. Poor little Anne was always returned to me, and was always glad to get back—though she led but a gloomy life in my house, having no playmates, like other children, to brighten her up. Our longest separation was when her mother took her to Limmeridge. Just at that time I lost my husband, and I felt it was as well, in that miserable affliction, that Anne should not be in the house. She was between ten and eleven years old then, slow at her lessons, poor soul, and not so cheerful as other children—but as pretty a little girl to look at as you would wish to see. I waited at home till her mother brought her back, and then I made the offer to take her with me to London—the truth being, sir, that I could not find it in my heart to stop at Old Welmingham after my husband’s death, the place was so changed and so dismal to me.”\n\n“And did Mrs. Catherick consent to your proposal?”\n\n“No, sir. She came back from the north harder and bitterer than ever. Folks did say that she had been obliged to ask Sir Percival’s leave to go, to begin with; and that she only went to nurse her dying sister at Limmeridge because the poor woman was reported to have saved money—the truth being that she hardly left enough to bury her. These things may have soured Mrs. Catherick likely enough, but however that may be, she wouldn’t hear of my taking the child away. She seemed to like distressing us both by parting us. All I could do was to give Anne my direction, and to tell her privately, if she was ever in trouble, to come to me. But years passed before she was free to come. I never saw her again, poor soul, till the night she escaped from the mad-house.”\n\n“You know, Mrs. Clements, why Sir Percival Glyde shut her up?”\n\n“I only know what Anne herself told me, sir. The poor thing used to ramble and wander about it sadly. She said her mother had got some secret of Sir Percival’s to keep, and had let it out to her long after I left Hampshire—and when Sir Percival found she knew it, he shut her up. But she never could say what it was when I asked her. All she could tell me was, that her mother might be the ruin and destruction of Sir Percival if she chose. Mrs. Catherick may have let out just as much as that, and no more. I’m next to certain I should have heard the whole truth from Anne, if she had really known it as she pretended to do, and as she very likely fancied she did, poor soul.”\n\nThis idea had more than once occurred to my own mind. I had already told Marian that I doubted whether Laura was really on the point of making any important discovery when she and Anne Catherick were disturbed by Count Fosco at the boat-house. It was perfectly in character with Anne’s mental affliction that she should assume an absolute knowledge of the secret on no better grounds than vague suspicion, derived from hints which her mother had incautiously let drop in her presence. Sir Percival’s guilty distrust would, in that case, infallibly inspire him with the false idea that Anne knew all from her mother, just as it had afterwards fixed in his mind the equally false suspicion that his wife knew all from Anne.\n\nThe time was passing, the morning was wearing away. It was doubtful, if I stayed longer, whether I should hear anything more from Mrs. Clements that would be at all useful to my purpose. I had already discovered those local and family particulars, in relation to Mrs. Catherick, of which I had been in search, and I had arrived at certain conclusions, entirely new to me, which might immensely assist in directing the course of my future proceedings. I rose to take my leave, and to thank Mrs. Clements for the friendly readiness she had shown in affording me information.\n\n“I am afraid you must have thought me very inquisitive,” I said. “I have troubled you with more questions than many people would have cared to answer.”\n\n“You are heartily welcome, sir, to anything I can tell you,” answered Mrs. Clements. She stopped and looked at me wistfully. “But I do wish,” said the poor woman, “you could have told me a little more about Anne, sir. I thought I saw something in your face when you came in which looked as if you could. You can’t think how hard it is not even to know whether she is living or dead. I could bear it better if I was only certain. You said you never expected we should see her alive again. Do you know, sir—do you know for truth—that it has pleased God to take her?”\n\nI was not proof against this appeal; it would have been unspeakably mean and cruel of me if I had resisted it.\n\n“I am afraid there is no doubt of the truth,” I answered gently; “I have the certainty in my own mind that her troubles in this world are over.”\n\nThe poor woman dropped into her chair and hid her face from me. “Oh, sir,” she said, “how do you know it? Who can have told you?”\n\n“No one has told me, Mrs. Clements. But I have reasons for feeling sure of it—reasons which I promise you shall know as soon as I can safely explain them. I am certain she was not neglected in her last moments—I am certain the heart complaint from which she suffered so sadly was the true cause of her death. You shall feel as sure of this as I do, soon—you shall know, before long, that she is buried in a quiet country churchyard—in a pretty, peaceful place, which you might have chosen for her yourself.”\n\n“Dead!” said Mrs. Clements, “dead so young, and I am left to hear it! I made her first short frocks. I taught her to walk. The first time she ever said Mother she said it to me—and now I am left and Anne is taken! Did you say, sir,” said the poor woman, removing the handkerchief from her face, and looking up at me for the first time, “did you say that she had been nicely buried? Was it the sort of funeral she might have had if she had really been my own child?”\n\nI assured her that it was. She seemed to take an inexplicable pride in my answer—to find a comfort in it which no other and higher considerations could afford. “It would have broken my heart,” she said simply, “if Anne had not been nicely buried—but how do you know it, sir? who told you?” I once more entreated her to wait until I could speak to her unreservedly. “You are sure to see me again,” I said, “for I have a favour to ask when you are a little more composed—perhaps in a day or two.”\n\n“Don’t keep it waiting, sir, on my account,” said Mrs. Clements. “Never mind my crying if I can be of use. If you have anything on your mind to say to me, sir, please to say it now.”\n\n“I only wish to ask you one last question,” I said. “I only want to know Mrs. Catherick’s address at Welmingham.”\n\nMy request so startled Mrs. Clements, that, for the moment, even the tidings of Anne’s death seemed to be driven from her mind. Her tears suddenly ceased to flow, and she sat looking at me in blank amazement.\n\n“For the Lord’s sake, sir!” she said, “what do you want with Mrs. Catherick!”\n\n“I want this, Mrs. Clements,” I replied, “I want to know the secret of those private meetings of hers with Sir Percival Glyde. There is something more in what you have told me of that woman’s past conduct, and of that man’s past relations with her, than you or any of your neighbours ever suspected. There is a secret we none of us know between those two, and I am going to Mrs. Catherick with the resolution to find it out.”\n\n“Think twice about it, sir!” said Mrs. Clements, rising in her earnestness and laying her hand on my arm. “She’s an awful woman—you don’t know her as I do. Think twice about it.”\n\n“I am sure your warning is kindly meant, Mrs. Clements. But I am determined to see the woman, whatever comes of it.”\n\nMrs. Clements looked me anxiously in the face.\n\n“I see your mind is made up, sir,” she said. “I will give you the address.”\n\nI wrote it down in my pocket-book and then took her hand to say farewell.\n\n“You shall hear from me soon,” I said; “you shall know all that I have promised to tell you.”\n\nMrs. Clements sighed and shook her head doubtfully.\n\n“An old woman’s advice is sometimes worth taking, sir,” she said. “Think twice before you go to Welmingham.”\n\n# VIII\n\nWhen I reached home again after my interview with Mrs. Clements, I was struck by the appearance of a change in Laura.\n\nThe unvarying gentleness and patience which long misfortune had tried so cruelly and had never conquered yet, seemed now to have suddenly failed her. Insensible to all Marian’s attempts to soothe and amuse her, she sat, with her neglected drawing pushed away on the table, her eyes resolutely cast down, her fingers twining and untwining themselves restlessly in her lap. Marian rose when I came in, with a silent distress in her face, waited for a moment to see if Laura would look up at my approach, whispered to me, “Try if you can rouse her,” and left the room.\n\nI sat down in the vacant chair—gently unclasped the poor, worn, restless fingers, and took both her hands in mine.\n\n“What are you thinking of, Laura? Tell me, my darling—try and tell me what it is.”\n\nShe struggled with herself, and raised her eyes to mine. “I can’t feel happy,” she said, “I can’t help thinking——” She stopped, bent forward a little, and laid her head on my shoulder, with a terrible mute helplessness that struck me to the heart.\n\n“Try to tell me,” I repeated gently; “try to tell me why you are not happy.”\n\n“I am so useless—I am such a burden on both of you,” she answered, with a weary, hopeless sigh. “You work and get money, Walter, and Marian helps you. Why is there nothing I can do? You will end in liking Marian better than you like me—you will, because I am so helpless! Oh, don’t, don’t, don’t treat me like a child!”\n\nI raised her head, and smoothed away the tangled hair that fell over her face, and kissed her—my poor, faded flower! my lost, afflicted sister! “You shall help us, Laura,” I said, “you shall begin, my darling, to-day.”\n\nShe looked at me with a feverish eagerness, with a breathless interest, that made me tremble for the new life of hope which I had called into being by those few words.\n\nI rose, and set her drawing materials in order, and placed them near her again.\n\n“You know that I work and get money by drawing,” I said. “Now you have taken such pains, now you are so much improved, you shall begin to work and get money too. Try to finish this little sketch as nicely and prettily as you can. When it is done I will take it away with me, and the same person will buy it who buys all that I do. You shall keep your own earnings in your own purse, and Marian shall come to you to help us, as often as she comes to me. Think how useful you are going to make yourself to both of us, and you will soon be as happy, Laura, as the day is long.”\n\nHer face grew eager, and brightened into a smile. In the moment while it lasted, in the moment when she again took up the pencils that had been laid aside, she almost looked like the Laura of past days.\n\nI had rightly interpreted the first signs of a new growth and strength in her mind, unconsciously expressing themselves in the notice she had taken of the occupations which filled her sister’s life and mine. Marian (when I told her what had passed) saw, as I saw, that she was longing to assume her own little position of importance, to raise herself in her own estimation and in ours—and, from that day, we tenderly helped the new ambition which gave promise of the hopeful, happier future, that might now not be far off. Her drawings, as she finished them, or tried to finish them, were placed in my hands. Marian took them from me and hid them carefully, and I set aside a little weekly tribute from my earnings, to be offered to her as the price paid by strangers for the poor, faint, valueless sketches, of which I was the only purchaser. It was hard sometimes to maintain our innocent deception, when she proudly brought out her purse to contribute her share towards the expenses, and wondered with serious interest, whether I or she had earned the most that week. I have all those hidden drawings in my possession still—they are my treasures beyond price—the dear remembrances that I love to keep alive—the friends in past adversity that my heart will never part from, my tenderness never forget.\n\nAm I trifling, here, with the necessities of my task? am I looking forward to the happier time which my narrative has not yet reached? Yes. Back again—back to the days of doubt and dread, when the spirit within me struggled hard for its life, in the icy stillness of perpetual suspense. I have paused and rested for a while on my forward course. It is not, perhaps, time wasted, if the friends who read these pages have paused and rested too.\n\nI took the first opportunity I could find of speaking to Marian in private, and of communicating to her the result of the inquiries which I had made that morning. She seemed to share the opinion on the subject of my proposed journey to Welmingham, which Mrs. Clements had already expressed to me.\n\n“Surely, Walter,” she said, “you hardly know enough yet to give you any hope of claiming Mrs. Catherick’s confidence? Is it wise to proceed to these extremities, before you have really exhausted all safer and simpler means of attaining your object? When you told me that Sir Percival and the Count were the only two people in existence who knew the exact date of Laura’s journey, you forgot, and I forgot, that there was a third person who must surely know it—I mean Mrs. Rubelle. Would it not be far easier, and far less dangerous, to insist on a confession from her, than to force it from Sir Percival?”\n\n“It might be easier,” I replied, “but we are not aware of the full extent of Mrs. Rubelle’s connivance and interest in the conspiracy, and we are therefore not certain that the date has been impressed on her mind, as it has been assuredly impressed on the minds of Sir Percival and the Count. It is too late, now, to waste the time on Mrs. Rubelle, which may be all-important to the discovery of the one assailable point in Sir Percival’s life. Are you thinking a little too seriously, Marian, of the risk I may run in returning to Hampshire? Are you beginning to doubt whether Sir Percival Glyde may not in the end be more than a match for me?”\n\n“He will not be more than your match,” she replied decidedly, “because he will not be helped in resisting you by the impenetrable wickedness of the Count.”\n\n“What has led you to that conclusion?” I replied, in some surprise.\n\n“My own knowledge of Sir Percival’s obstinacy and impatience of the Count’s control,” she answered. “I believe he will insist on meeting you single-handed—just as he insisted at first on acting for himself at Blackwater Park. The time for suspecting the Count’s interference will be the time when you have Sir Percival at your mercy. His own interests will then be directly threatened, and he will act, Walter, to terrible purpose in his own defence.”\n\n“We may deprive him of his weapons beforehand,” I said. “Some of the particulars I have heard from Mrs. Clements may yet be turned to account against him, and other means of strengthening the case may be at our disposal. There are passages in Mrs. Michelson’s narrative which show that the Count found it necessary to place himself in communication with Mr. Fairlie, and there may be circumstances which compromise him in that proceeding. While I am away, Marian, write to Mr. Fairlie and say that you want an answer describing exactly what passed between the Count and himself, and informing you also of any particulars that may have come to his knowledge at the same time in connection with his niece. Tell him that the statement you request will, sooner or later, be insisted on, if he shows any reluctance to furnish you with it of his own accord.”\n\n“The letter shall be written, Walter. But are you really determined to go to Welmingham?”\n\n“Absolutely determined. I will devote the next two days to earning what we want for the week to come, and on the third day I go to Hampshire.”\n\nWhen the third day came I was ready for my journey.\n\nAs it was possible that I might be absent for some little time, I arranged with Marian that we were to correspond every day—of course addressing each other by assumed names, for caution’s sake. As long as I heard from her regularly, I should assume that nothing was wrong. But if the morning came and brought me no letter, my return to London would take place, as a matter of course, by the first train. I contrived to reconcile Laura to my departure by telling her that I was going to the country to find new purchasers for her drawings and for mine, and I left her occupied and happy. Marian followed me downstairs to the street door.\n\n“Remember what anxious hearts you leave here,” she whispered, as we stood together in the passage. “Remember all the hopes that hang on your safe return. If strange things happen to you on this journey—if you and Sir Percival meet——”\n\n“What makes you think we shall meet?” I asked.\n\n“I don’t know—I have fears and fancies that I cannot account for. Laugh at them, Walter, if you like—but, for God’s sake, keep your temper if you come in contact with that man!”\n\n“Never fear, Marian! I answer for my self-control.”\n\nWith those words we parted.\n\nI walked briskly to the station. There was a glow of hope in me. There was a growing conviction in my mind that my journey this time would not be taken in vain. It was a fine, clear, cold morning. My nerves were firmly strung, and I felt all the strength of my resolution stirring in me vigorously from head to foot.\n\nAs I crossed the railway platform, and looked right and left among the people congregated on it, to search for any faces among them that I knew, the doubt occurred to me whether it might not have been to my advantage if I had adopted a disguise before setting out for Hampshire. But there was something so repellent to me in the idea—something so meanly like the common herd of spies and informers in the mere act of adopting a disguise—that I dismissed the question from consideration almost as soon as it had risen in my mind. Even as a mere matter of expediency the proceeding was doubtful in the extreme. If I tried the experiment at home the landlord of the house would sooner or later discover me, and would have his suspicions aroused immediately. If I tried it away from home the same persons might see me, by the commonest accident, with the disguise and without it, and I should in that way be inviting the notice and distrust which it was my most pressing interest to avoid. In my own character I had acted thus far—and in my own character I was resolved to continue to the end.\n\nThe train left me at Welmingham early in the afternoon.\n\nIs there any wilderness of sand in the deserts of Arabia, is there any prospect of desolation among the ruins of Palestine, which can rival the repelling effect on the eye, and the depressing influence on the mind, of an English country town in the first stage of its existence, and in the transition state of its prosperity? I asked myself that question as I passed through the clean desolation, the neat ugliness, the prim torpor of the streets of Welmingham. And the tradesmen who stared after me from their lonely shops—the trees that drooped helpless in their arid exile of unfinished crescents and squares—the dead house-carcasses that waited in vain for the vivifying human element to animate them with the breath of life—every creature that I saw, every object that I passed, seemed to answer with one accord: The deserts of Arabia are innocent of our civilised desolation—the ruins of Palestine are incapable of our modern gloom!\n\nI inquired my way to the quarter of the town in which Mrs. Catherick lived, and on reaching it found myself in a square of small houses, one story high. There was a bare little plot of grass in the middle, protected by a cheap wire fence. An elderly nursemaid and two children were standing in a corner of the enclosure, looking at a lean goat tethered to the grass. Two foot-passengers were talking together on one side of the pavement before the houses, and an idle little boy was leading an idle little dog along by a string on the other. I heard the dull tinkling of a piano at a distance, accompanied by the intermittent knocking of a hammer nearer at hand. These were all the sights and sounds of life that encountered me when I entered the square.\n\nI walked at once to the door of Number Thirteen—the number of Mrs. Catherick’s house—and knocked, without waiting to consider beforehand how I might best present myself when I got in. The first necessity was to see Mrs. Catherick. I could then judge, from my own observation, of the safest and easiest manner of approaching the object of my visit.\n\nThe door was opened by a melancholy middle-aged woman servant. I gave her my card, and asked if I could see Mrs. Catherick. The card was taken into the front parlour, and the servant returned with a message requesting me to mention what my business was.\n\n“Say, if you please, that my business relates to Mrs. Catherick’s daughter,” I replied. This was the best pretext I could think of, on the spur of the moment, to account for my visit.\n\nThe servant again retired to the parlour, again returned, and this time begged me, with a look of gloomy amazement, to walk in.\n\nI entered a little room, with a flaring paper of the largest pattern on the walls. Chairs, tables, cheffonier, and sofa, all gleamed with the glutinous brightness of cheap upholstery. On the largest table, in the middle of the room, stood a smart Bible, placed exactly in the centre on a red and yellow woollen mat and at the side of the table nearest to the window, with a little knitting-basket on her lap, and a wheezing, blear-eyed old spaniel crouched at her feet, there sat an elderly woman, wearing a black net cap and a black silk gown, and having slate-coloured mittens on her hands. Her iron-grey hair hung in heavy bands on either side of her face—her dark eyes looked straight forward, with a hard, defiant, implacable stare. She had full square cheeks, a long, firm chin, and thick, sensual, colourless lips. Her figure was stout and sturdy, and her manner aggressively self-possessed. This was Mrs. Catherick.\n\n“You have come to speak to me about my daughter,” she said, before I could utter a word on my side. “Be so good as to mention what you have to say.”\n\nThe tone of her voice was as hard, as defiant, as implacable as the expression of her eyes. She pointed to a chair, and looked me all over attentively, from head to foot, as I sat down in it. I saw that my only chance with this woman was to speak to her in her own tone, and to meet her, at the outset of our interview, on her own ground.\n\n“You are aware,” I said, “that your daughter has been lost?”\n\n“I am perfectly aware of it.”\n\n“Have you felt any apprehension that the misfortune of her loss might be followed by the misfortune of her death?”\n\n“Yes. Have you come here to tell me she is dead?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nShe put that extraordinary question without the slightest change in her voice, her face, or her manner. She could not have appeared more perfectly unconcerned if I had told her of the death of the goat in the enclosure outside.\n\n“Why?” I repeated. “Do you ask why I come here to tell you of your daughter’s death?”\n\n“Yes. What interest have you in me, or in her? How do you come to know anything about my daughter?”\n\n“In this way. I met her on the night when she escaped from the Asylum, and I assisted her in reaching a place of safety.”\n\n“You did very wrong.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear her mother say so.”\n\n“Her mother does say so. How do you know she is dead?”\n\n“I am not at liberty to say how I know it—but I DO know it.”\n\n“Are you at liberty to say how you found out my address?”\n\n“Certainly. I got your address from Mrs. Clements.”\n\n“Mrs. Clements is a foolish woman. Did she tell you to come here?”\n\n“She did not.”\n\n“Then, I ask you again, why did you come?”\n\nAs she was determined to have her answer, I gave it to her in the plainest possible form.\n\n“I came,” I said, “because I thought Anne Catherick’s mother might have some natural interest in knowing whether she was alive or dead.”\n\n“Just so,” said Mrs. Catherick, with additional self-possession. “Had you no other motive?”\n\nI hesitated. The right answer to that question was not easy to find at a moment’s notice.\n\n“If you have no other motive,” she went on, deliberately taking off her slate-coloured mittens, and rolling them up, “I have only to thank you for your visit, and to say that I will not detain you here any longer. Your information would be more satisfactory if you were willing to explain how you became possessed of it. However, it justifies me, I suppose, in going into mourning. There is not much alteration necessary in my dress, as you see. When I have changed my mittens, I shall be all in black.”\n\nShe searched in the pocket of her gown, drew out a pair of black lace mittens, put them on with the stoniest and steadiest composure, and then quietly crossed her hands in her lap.\n\n“I wish you good morning,” she said.\n\nThe cool contempt of her manner irritated me into directly avowing that the purpose of my visit had not been answered yet.\n\n“I HAVE another motive in coming here,” I said.\n\n“Ah! I thought so,” remarked Mrs. Catherick.\n\n“Your daughter’s death——”\n\n“What did she die of?”\n\n“Of disease of the heart.”\n\n“Yes. Go on.”\n\n“Your daughter’s death has been made the pretext for inflicting serious injury on a person who is very dear to me. Two men have been concerned, to my certain knowledge, in doing that wrong. One of them is Sir Percival Glyde.”\n\n“Indeed!”\n\nI looked attentively to see if she flinched at the sudden mention of that name. Not a muscle of her stirred—the hard, defiant, implacable stare in her eyes never wavered for an instant.\n\n“You may wonder,” I went on, “how the event of your daughter’s death can have been made the means of inflicting injury on another person.”\n\n“No,” said Mrs. Catherick; “I don’t wonder at all. This appears to be your affair. You are interested in my affairs. I am not interested in yours.”\n\n“You may ask, then,” I persisted, “why I mention the matter in your presence.”\n\n“Yes, I DO ask that.”\n\n“I mention it because I am determined to bring Sir Percival Glyde to account for the wickedness he has committed.”\n\n“What have I to do with your determination?”\n\n“You shall hear. There are certain events in Sir Percival’s past life which it is necessary for my purpose to be fully acquainted with. YOU know them—and for that reason I come to YOU.”\n\n“What events do you mean?”\n\n“Events that occurred at Old Welmingham when your husband was parish-clerk at that place, and before the time when your daughter was born.”\n\nI had reached the woman at last through the barrier of impenetrable reserve that she had tried to set up between us. I saw her temper smouldering in her eyes—as plainly as I saw her hands grow restless, then unclasp themselves, and begin mechanically smoothing her dress over her knees.\n\n“What do you know of those events?” she asked.\n\n“All that Mrs. Clements could tell me,” I answered.\n\nThere was a momentary flush on her firm square face, a momentary stillness in her restless hands, which seemed to betoken a coming outburst of anger that might throw her off her guard. But no—she mastered the rising irritation, leaned back in her chair, crossed her arms on her broad bosom, and with a smile of grim sarcasm on her thick lips, looked at me as steadily as ever.\n\n“Ah! I begin to understand it all now,” she said, her tamed and disciplined anger only expressing itself in the elaborate mockery of her tone and manner. “You have got a grudge of your own against Sir Percival Glyde, and I must help you to wreak it. I must tell you this, that, and the other about Sir Percival and myself, must I? Yes, indeed? You have been prying into my private affairs. You think you have found a lost woman to deal with, who lives here on sufferance, and who will do anything you ask for fear you may injure her in the opinions of the town’s-people. I see through you and your precious speculation—I do! and it amuses me. Ha! ha!”\n\nShe stopped for a moment, her arms tightened over her bosom, and she laughed to herself—a hard, harsh, angry laugh.\n\n“You don’t know how I have lived in this place, and what I have done in this place, Mr. What’s-your-name,” she went on. “I’ll tell you, before I ring the bell and have you shown out. I came here a wronged woman—I came here robbed of my character and determined to claim it back. I’ve been years and years about it—and I HAVE claimed it back. I have matched the respectable people fairly and openly on their own ground. If they say anything against me now they must say it in secret—they can’t say it, they daren’t say it, openly. I stand high enough in this town to be out of your reach. THE CLERGYMAN BOWS TO ME. Aha! you didn’t bargain for that when you came here. Go to the church and inquire about me—you will find Mrs. Catherick has her sitting like the rest of them, and pays the rent on the day it’s due. Go to the town-hall. There’s a petition lying there—a petition of the respectable inhabitants against allowing a circus to come and perform here and corrupt our morals—yes! OUR morals. I signed that petition this morning. Go to the bookseller’s shop. The clergyman’s Wednesday evening Lectures on Justification by Faith are publishing there by subscription—I’m down on the list. The doctor’s wife only put a shilling in the plate at our last charity sermon—I put half-a-crown. Mr. Churchwarden Soward held the plate, and bowed to me. Ten years ago he told Pigrum the chemist I ought to be whipped out of the town at the cart’s tail. Is your mother alive? Has she got a better Bible on her table than I have got on mine? Does she stand better with her trades-people than I do with mine? Has she always lived within her income? I have always lived within mine. Ah! there IS the clergyman coming along the square. Look, Mr. What’s-your-name—look, if you please!”\n\nShe started up with the activity of a young woman, went to the window, waited till the clergyman passed, and bowed to him solemnly. The clergyman ceremoniously raised his hat, and walked on. Mrs. Catherick returned to her chair, and looked at me with a grimmer sarcasm than ever.\n\n“There!” she said. “What do you think of that for a woman with a lost character? How does your speculation look now?”\n\nThe singular manner in which she had chosen to assert herself, the extraordinary practical vindication of her position in the town which she had just offered, had so perplexed me that I listened to her in silent surprise. I was not the less resolved, however, to make another effort to throw her off her guard. If the woman’s fierce temper once got beyond her control, and once flamed out on me, she might yet say the words which would put the clue in my hands.\n\n“How does your speculation look now?” she repeated.\n\n“Exactly as it looked when I first came in,” I answered. “I don’t doubt the position you have gained in the town, and I don’t wish to assail it even if I could. I came here because Sir Percival Glyde is, to my certain knowledge, your enemy, as well as mine. If I have a grudge against him, you have a grudge against him too. You may deny it if you like, you may distrust me as much as you please, you may be as angry as you will—but, of all the women in England, you, if you have any sense of injury, are the woman who ought to help me to crush that man.”\n\n“Crush him for yourself,” she said; “then come back here, and see what I say to you.”\n\nShe spoke those words as she had not spoken yet, quickly, fiercely, vindictively. I had stirred in its lair the serpent-hatred of years, but only for a moment. Like a lurking reptile it leaped up at me as she eagerly bent forward towards the place in which I was sitting. Like a lurking reptile it dropped out of sight again as she instantly resumed her former position in the chair.\n\n“You won’t trust me?” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You are afraid?”\n\n“Do I look as if I was?”\n\n“You are afraid of Sir Percival Glyde?”\n\n“Am I?”\n\nHer colour was rising, and her hands were at work again smoothing her gown. I pressed the point farther and farther home, I went on without allowing her a moment of delay.\n\n“Sir Percival has a high position in the world,” I said; “it would be no wonder if you were afraid of him. Sir Percival is a powerful man, a baronet, the possessor of a fine estate, the descendant of a great family——”\n\nShe amazed me beyond expression by suddenly bursting out laughing.\n\n“Yes,” she repeated, in tones of the bitterest, steadiest contempt. “A baronet, the possessor of a fine estate, the descendant of a great family. Yes, indeed! A great family—especially by the mother’s side.”\n\nThere was no time to reflect on the words that had just escaped her, there was only time to feel that they were well worth thinking over the moment I left the house.\n\n“I am not here to dispute with you about family questions,” I said. “I know nothing of Sir Percival’s mother——”\n\n“And you know as little of Sir Percival himself,” she interposed sharply.\n\n“I advise you not to be too sure of that,” I rejoined. “I know some things about him, and I suspect many more.”\n\n“What do you suspect?”\n\n“I’ll tell you what I DON’T suspect. I DON’T suspect him of being Anne’s father.”\n\nShe started to her feet, and came close up to me with a look of fury.\n\n“How dare you talk to me about Anne’s father! How dare you say who was her father, or who wasn’t!” she broke out, her face quivering, her voice trembling with passion.\n\n“The secret between you and Sir Percival is not THAT secret,” I persisted. “The mystery which darkens Sir Percival’s life was not born with your daughter’s birth, and has not died with your daughter’s death.”\n\nShe drew back a step. “Go!” she said, and pointed sternly to the door.\n\n“There was no thought of the child in your heart or in his,” I went on, determined to press her back to her last defences. “There was no bond of guilty love between you and him when you held those stolen meetings, when your husband found you whispering together under the vestry of the church.”\n\nHer pointing hand instantly dropped to her side, and the deep flush of anger faded from her face while I spoke. I saw the change pass over her—I saw that hard, firm, fearless, self-possessed woman quail under a terror which her utmost resolution was not strong enough to resist when I said those five last words, “the vestry of the church.”\n\nFor a minute or more we stood looking at each other in silence. I spoke first.\n\n“Do you still refuse to trust me?” I asked.\n\nShe could not call the colour that had left it back to her face, but she had steadied her voice, she had recovered the defiant self-possession of her manner when she answered me.\n\n“I do refuse,” she said.\n\n“Do you still tell me to go?”\n\n“Yes. Go—and never come back.”\n\nI walked to the door, waited a moment before I opened it, and turned round to look at her again.\n\n“I may have news to bring you of Sir Percival which you don’t expect,” I said, “and in that case I shall come back.”\n\n“There is no news of Sir Percival that I don’t expect, except——”\n\nShe stopped, her pale face darkened, and she stole back with a quiet, stealthy, cat-like step to her chair.\n\n“Except the news of his death,” she said, sitting down again, with the mockery of a smile just hovering on her cruel lips, and the furtive light of hatred lurking deep in her steady eyes.\n\nAs I opened the door of the room to go out, she looked round at me quickly. The cruel smile slowly widened her lips—she eyed me, with a strange stealthy interest, from head to foot—an unutterable expectation showed itself wickedly all over her face. Was she speculating, in the secrecy of her own heart, on my youth and strength, on the force of my sense of injury and the limits of my self-control, and was she considering the lengths to which they might carry me, if Sir Percival and I ever chanced to meet? The bare doubt that it might be so drove me from her presence, and silenced even the common forms of farewell on my lips. Without a word more, on my side or on hers, I left the room.\n\nAs I opened the outer door, I saw the same clergyman who had already passed the house once, about to pass it again, on his way back through the square. I waited on the door-step to let him go by, and looked round, as I did so, at the parlour window.\n\nMrs. Catherick had heard his footsteps approaching, in the silence of that lonely place, and she was on her feet at the window again, waiting for him. Not all the strength of all the terrible passions I had roused in that woman’s heart, could loosen her desperate hold on the one fragment of social consideration which years of resolute effort had just dragged within her grasp. There she was again, not a minute after I had left her, placed purposely in a position which made it a matter of common courtesy on the part of the clergyman to bow to her for a second time. He raised his hat once more. I saw the hard ghastly face behind the window soften, and light up with gratified pride—I saw the head with the grim black cap bend ceremoniously in return. The clergyman had bowed to her, and in my presence, twice in one day!\n\n# IX\n\nI left the house, feeling that Mrs. Catherick had helped me a step forward, in spite of herself. Before I had reached the turning which led out of the square, my attention was suddenly aroused by the sound of a closing door behind me.\n\nI looked round, and saw an undersized man in black on the door-step of a house, which, as well as I could judge, stood next to Mrs. Catherick’s place of abode—next to it, on the side nearest to me. The man did not hesitate a moment about the direction he should take. He advanced rapidly towards the turning at which I had stopped. I recognised him as the lawyer’s clerk, who had preceded me in my visit to Blackwater Park, and who had tried to pick a quarrel with me, when I asked him if I could see the house.\n\nI waited where I was, to ascertain whether his object was to come to close quarters and speak on this occasion. To my surprise he passed on rapidly, without saying a word, without even looking up in my face as he went by. This was such a complete inversion of the course of proceeding which I had every reason to expect on his part, that my curiosity, or rather my suspicion, was aroused, and I determined on my side to keep him cautiously in view, and to discover what the business might be in which he was now employed. Without caring whether he saw me or not, I walked after him. He never looked back, and he led me straight through the streets to the railway station.\n\nThe train was on the point of starting, and two or three passengers who were late were clustering round the small opening through which the tickets were issued. I joined them, and distinctly heard the lawyer’s clerk demand a ticket for the Blackwater station. I satisfied myself that he had actually left by the train before I came away.\n\nThere was only one interpretation that I could place on what I had just seen and heard. I had unquestionably observed the man leaving a house which closely adjoined Mrs. Catherick’s residence. He had been probably placed there, by Sir Percival’s directions, as a lodger, in anticipation of my inquiries leading me, sooner or later, to communicate with Mrs. Catherick. He had doubtless seen me go in and come out, and he had hurried away by the first train to make his report at Blackwater Park, to which place Sir Percival would naturally betake himself (knowing what he evidently knew of my movements), in order to be ready on the spot, if I returned to Hampshire. Before many days were over, there seemed every likelihood now that he and I might meet.\n\nWhatever result events might be destined to produce, I resolved to pursue my own course, straight to the end in view, without stopping or turning aside for Sir Percival or for any one. The great responsibility which weighed on me heavily in London—the responsibility of so guiding my slightest actions as to prevent them from leading accidentally to the discovery of Laura’s place of refuge—was removed, now that I was in Hampshire. I could go and come as I pleased at Welmingham, and if I chanced to fail in observing any necessary precautions, the immediate results, at least, would affect no one but myself.\n\nWhen I left the station the winter evening was beginning to close in. There was little hope of continuing my inquiries after dark to any useful purpose in a neighbourhood that was strange to me. Accordingly, I made my way to the nearest hotel, and ordered my dinner and my bed. This done, I wrote to Marian, to tell her that I was safe and well, and that I had fair prospects of success. I had directed her, on leaving home, to address the first letter she wrote to me (the letter I expected to receive the next morning) to “The Post-Office, Welmingham,” and I now begged her to send her second day’s letter to the same address.\n\nI could easily receive it by writing to the postmaster if I happened to be away from the town when it arrived.\n\nThe coffee-room of the hotel, as it grew late in the evening, became a perfect solitude. I was left to reflect on what I had accomplished that afternoon as uninterruptedly as if the house had been my own. Before I retired to rest I had attentively thought over my extraordinary interview with Mrs. Catherick from beginning to end, and had verified at my leisure the conclusions which I had hastily drawn in the earlier part of the day.\n\nThe vestry of Old Welmingham church was the starting-point from which my mind slowly worked its way back through all that I had heard Mrs. Catherick say, and through all I had seen Mrs. Catherick do.\n\nAt the time when the neighbourhood of the vestry was first referred to in my presence by Mrs. Clements, I had thought it the strangest and most unaccountable of all places for Sir Percival to select for a clandestine meeting with the clerk’s wife. Influenced by this impression, and by no other, I had mentioned “the vestry of the church” before Mrs. Catherick on pure speculation—it represented one of the minor peculiarities of the story which occurred to me while I was speaking. I was prepared for her answering me confusedly or angrily, but the blank terror that seized her when I said the words took me completely by surprise. I had long before associated Sir Percival’s Secret with the concealment of a serious crime which Mrs. Catherick knew of, but I had gone no further than this. Now the woman’s paroxysm of terror associated the crime, either directly or indirectly, with the vestry, and convinced me that she had been more than the mere witness of it—she was also the accomplice, beyond a doubt.\n\nWhat had been the nature of the crime? Surely there was a contemptible side to it, as well as a dangerous side, or Mrs. Catherick would not have repeated my own words, referring to Sir Percival’s rank and power, with such marked disdain as she had certainly displayed. It was a contemptible crime then and a dangerous crime, and she had shared in it, and it was associated with the vestry of the church.\n\nThe next consideration to be disposed of led me a step farther from this point.\n\nMrs. Catherick’s undisguised contempt for Sir Percival plainly extended to his mother as well. She had referred with the bitterest sarcasm to the great family he had descended from—“especially by the mother’s side.” What did this mean?\n\nThere appeared to be only two explanations of it. Either his mother’s birth had been low, or his mother’s reputation was damaged by some hidden flaw with which Mrs. Catherick and Sir Percival were both privately acquainted? I could only put the first explanation to the test by looking at the register of her marriage, and so ascertaining her maiden name and her parentage as a preliminary to further inquiries.\n\nOn the other hand, if the second case supposed were the true one, what had been the flaw in her reputation? Remembering the account which Marian had given me of Sir Percival’s father and mother, and of the suspiciously unsocial secluded life they had both led, I now asked myself whether it might not be possible that his mother had never been married at all. Here again the register might, by offering written evidence of the marriage, prove to me, at any rate, that this doubt had no foundation in truth. But where was the register to be found? At this point I took up the conclusions which I had previously formed, and the same mental process which had discovered the locality of the concealed crime, now lodged the register also in the vestry of Old Welmingham church.\n\nThese were the results of my interview with Mrs. Catherick—these were the various considerations, all steadily converging to one point, which decided the course of my proceedings on the next day.\n\nThe morning was cloudy and lowering, but no rain fell. I left my bag at the hotel to wait there till I called for it, and, after inquiring the way, set forth on foot for Old Welmingham church.\n\nIt was a walk of rather more than two miles, the ground rising slowly all the way.\n\nOn the highest point stood the church—an ancient, weather-beaten building, with heavy buttresses at its sides, and a clumsy square tower in front. The vestry at the back was built out from the church, and seemed to be of the same age. Round the building at intervals appeared the remains of the village which Mrs. Clements had described to me as her husband’s place of abode in former years, and which the principal inhabitants had long since deserted for the new town. Some of the empty houses had been dismantled to their outer walls, some had been left to decay with time, and some were still inhabited by persons evidently of the poorest class. It was a dreary scene, and yet, in the worst aspect of its ruin, not so dreary as the modern town that I had just left. Here there was the brown, breezy sweep of surrounding fields for the eye to repose on—here the trees, leafless as they were, still varied the monotony of the prospect, and helped the mind to look forward to summer-time and shade.\n\nAs I moved away from the back of the church, and passed some of the dismantled cottages in search of a person who might direct me to the clerk, I saw two men saunter out after me from behind a wall. The tallest of the two—a stout muscular man in the dress of a gamekeeper—was a stranger to me. The other was one of the men who had followed me in London on the day when I left Mr. Kyrle’s office. I had taken particular notice of him at the time; and I felt sure that I was not mistaken in identifying the fellow on this occasion.\n\nNeither he nor his companion attempted to speak to me, and both kept themselves at a respectful distance, but the motive of their presence in the neighbourhood of the church was plainly apparent. It was exactly as I had supposed—Sir Percival was already prepared for me. My visit to Mrs. Catherick had been reported to him the evening before, and those two men had been placed on the look-out near the church in anticipation of my appearance at Old Welmingham. If I had wanted any further proof that my investigations had taken the right direction at last, the plan now adopted for watching me would have supplied it.\n\nI walked on away from the church till I reached one of the inhabited houses, with a patch of kitchen garden attached to it on which a labourer was at work. He directed me to the clerk’s abode, a cottage at some little distance off, standing by itself on the outskirts of the forsaken village. The clerk was indoors, and was just putting on his greatcoat. He was a cheerful, familiar, loudly-talkative old man, with a very poor opinion (as I soon discovered) of the place in which he lived, and a happy sense of superiority to his neighbours in virtue of the great personal distinction of having once been in London.\n\n“It’s well you came so early, sir,” said the old man, when I had mentioned the object of my visit. “I should have been away in ten minutes more. Parish business, sir, and a goodish long trot before it’s all done for a man at my age. But, bless you, I’m strong on my legs still! As long as a man don’t give at his legs, there’s a deal of work left in him. Don’t you think so yourself, sir?”\n\nHe took his keys down while he was talking from a hook behind the fireplace, and locked his cottage door behind us.\n\n“Nobody at home to keep house for me,” said the clerk, with a cheerful sense of perfect freedom from all family encumbrances. “My wife’s in the churchyard there, and my children are all married. A wretched place this, isn’t it, sir? But the parish is a large one—every man couldn’t get through the business as I do. It’s learning does it, and I’ve had my share, and a little more. I can talk the Queen’s English (God bless the Queen!), and that’s more than most of the people about here can do. You’re from London, I suppose, sir? I’ve been in London a matter of five-and-twenty year ago. What’s the news there now, if you please?”\n\nChattering on in this way, he led me back to the vestry. I looked about to see if the two spies were still in sight. They were not visible anywhere. After having discovered my application to the clerk, they had probably concealed themselves where they could watch my next proceedings in perfect freedom.\n\nThe vestry door was of stout old oak, studded with strong nails, and the clerk put his large heavy key into the lock with the air of a man who knew that he had a difficulty to encounter, and who was not quite certain of creditably conquering it.\n\n“I’m obliged to bring you this way, sir,” he said, “because the door from the vestry to the church is bolted on the vestry side. We might have got in through the church otherwise. This is a perverse lock, if ever there was one yet. It’s big enough for a prison-door—it’s been hampered over and over again, and it ought to be changed for a new one. I’ve mentioned that to the churchwarden fifty times over at least—he’s always saying, ‘I’ll see about it’—and he never does see. Ah, it’s a sort of lost corner, this place. Not like London—is it, sir? Bless you, we are all asleep here! We don’t march with the times.”\n\nAfter some twisting and turning of the key, the heavy lock yielded, and he opened the door.\n\nThe vestry was larger than I should have supposed it to be, judging from the outside only. It was a dim, mouldy, melancholy old room, with a low, raftered ceiling. Round two sides of it, the sides nearest to the interior of the church, ran heavy wooden presses, worm-eaten and gaping with age. Hooked to the inner corner of one of these presses hung several surplices, all bulging out at their lower ends in an irreverent-looking bundle of limp drapery. Below the surplices, on the floor, stood three packing-cases, with the lids half off, half on, and the straw profusely bursting out of their cracks and crevices in every direction. Behind them, in a corner, was a litter of dusty papers, some large and rolled up like architects’ plans, some loosely strung together on files like bills or letters. The room had once been lighted by a small side window, but this had been bricked up, and a lantern skylight was now substituted for it. The atmosphere of the place was heavy and mouldy, being rendered additionally oppressive by the closing of the door which led into the church. This door also was composed of solid oak, and was bolted at the top and bottom on the vestry side.\n\n“We might be tidier, mightn’t we, sir?” said the cheerful clerk; “but when you’re in a lost corner of a place like this, what are you to do? Why, look here now, just look at these packing-cases. There they’ve been, for a year or more, ready to go down to London—there they are, littering the place, and there they’ll stop as long as the nails hold them together. I’ll tell you what, sir, as I said before, this is not London. We are all asleep here. Bless you, WE don’t march with the times!”\n\n“What is there in the packing-cases?” I asked.\n\n“Bits of old wood carvings from the pulpit, and panels from the chancel, and images from the organ-loft,” said the clerk. “Portraits of the twelve apostles in wood, and not a whole nose among ’em. All broken, and worm-eaten, and crumbling to dust at the edges. As brittle as crockery, sir, and as old as the church, if not older.”\n\n“And why were they going to London? To be repaired?”\n\n“That’s it, sir, to be repaired, and where they were past repair, to be copied in sound wood. But, bless you, the money fell short, and there they are, waiting for new subscriptions, and nobody to subscribe. It was all done a year ago, sir. Six gentlemen dined together about it, at the hotel in the new town. They made speeches, and passed resolutions, and put their names down, and printed off thousands of prospectuses. Beautiful prospectuses, sir, all flourished over with Gothic devices in red ink, saying it was a disgrace not to restore the church and repair the famous carvings, and so on. There are the prospectuses that couldn’t be distributed, and the architect’s plans and estimates, and the whole correspondence which set everybody at loggerheads and ended in a dispute, all down together in that corner, behind the packing-cases. The money dribbled in a little at first—but what CAN you expect out of London? There was just enough, you know, to pack the broken carvings, and get the estimates, and pay the printer’s bill, and after that there wasn’t a halfpenny left. There the things are, as I said before. We have nowhere else to put them—nobody in the new town cares about accommodating us—we’re in a lost corner—and this is an untidy vestry—and who’s to help it?—that’s what I want to know.”\n\nMy anxiety to examine the register did not dispose me to offer much encouragement to the old man’s talkativeness. I agreed with him that nobody could help the untidiness of the vestry, and then suggested that we should proceed to our business without more delay.\n\n“Ay, ay, the marriage-register, to be sure,” said the clerk, taking a little bunch of keys from his pocket. “How far do you want to look back, sir?”\n\nMarian had informed me of Sir Percival’s age at the time when we had spoken together of his marriage engagement with Laura. She had then described him as being forty-five years old. Calculating back from this, and making due allowance for the year that had passed since I had gained my information, I found that he must have been born in eighteen hundred and four, and that I might safely start on my search through the register from that date.\n\n“I want to begin with the year eighteen hundred and four,” I said.\n\n“Which way after that, sir?” asked the clerk. “Forwards to our time or backwards away from us?”\n\n“Backwards from eighteen hundred and four.”\n\nHe opened the door of one of the presses—the press from the side of which the surplices were hanging—and produced a large volume bound in greasy brown leather. I was struck by the insecurity of the place in which the register was kept. The door of the press was warped and cracked with age, and the lock was of the smallest and commonest kind. I could have forced it easily with the walking-stick I carried in my hand.\n\n“Is that considered a sufficiently secure place for the register?” I inquired. “Surely a book of such importance as this ought to be protected by a better lock, and kept carefully in an iron safe?”\n\n“Well, now, that’s curious!” said the clerk, shutting up the book again, just after he had opened it, and smacking his hand cheerfully on the cover. “Those were the very words my old master was always saying years and years ago, when I was a lad. ‘Why isn’t the register’ (meaning this register here, under my hand)—‘why isn’t it kept in an iron safe?’ If I’ve heard him say that once, I’ve heard him say it a hundred times. He was the solicitor in those days, sir, who had the appointment of vestry-clerk to this church. A fine hearty old gentleman, and the most particular man breathing. As long as he lived he kept a copy of this book in his office at Knowlesbury, and had it posted up regular, from time to time, to correspond with the fresh entries here. You would hardly think it, but he had his own appointed days, once or twice in every quarter, for riding over to this church on his old white pony, to check the copy, by the register, with his own eyes and hands. ‘How do I know?’ (he used to say) ‘how do I know that the register in this vestry may not be stolen or destroyed? Why isn’t it kept in an iron safe? Why can’t I make other people as careful as I am myself? Some of these days there will be an accident happen, and when the register’s lost, then the parish will find out the value of my copy.’ He used to take his pinch of snuff after that, and look about him as bold as a lord. Ah! the like of him for doing business isn’t easy to find now. You may go to London and not match him, even THERE. Which year did you say, sir? Eighteen hundred and what?”\n\n“Eighteen hundred and four,” I replied, mentally resolving to give the old man no more opportunities of talking, until my examination of the register was over.\n\nThe clerk put on his spectacles, and turned over the leaves of the register, carefully wetting his finger and thumb at every third page. “There it is, sir,” said he, with another cheerful smack on the open volume. “There’s the year you want.”\n\nAs I was ignorant of the month in which Sir Percival was born, I began my backward search with the early part of the year. The register-book was of the old-fashioned kind, the entries being all made on blank pages in manuscript, and the divisions which separated them being indicated by ink lines drawn across the page at the close of each entry.\n\nI reached the beginning of the year eighteen hundred and four without encountering the marriage, and then travelled back through December eighteen hundred and three—through November and October—through——\n\nNo! not through September also. Under the heading of that month in the year I found the marriage.\n\nI looked carefully at the entry. It was at the bottom of a page, and was for want of room compressed into a smaller space than that occupied by the marriages above. The marriage immediately before it was impressed on my attention by the circumstance of the bridegroom’s Christian name being the same as my own. The entry immediately following it (on the top of the next page) was noticeable in another way from the large space it occupied, the record in this case registering the marriages of two brothers at the same time. The register of the marriage of Sir Felix Glyde was in no respect remarkable except for the narrowness of the space into which it was compressed at the bottom of the page. The information about his wife was the usual information given in such cases. She was described as “Cecilia Jane Elster, of Park-View Cottages, Knowlesbury, only daughter of the late Patrick Elster, Esq., formerly of Bath.”\n\nI noted down these particulars in my pocket-book, feeling as I did so both doubtful and disheartened about my next proceedings. The Secret which I had believed until this moment to be within my grasp seemed now farther from my reach than ever.\n\nWhat suggestions of any mystery unexplained had arisen out of my visit to the vestry? I saw no suggestions anywhere. What progress had I made towards discovering the suspected stain on the reputation of Sir Percival’s mother? The one fact I had ascertained vindicated her reputation. Fresh doubts, fresh difficulties, fresh delays began to open before me in interminable prospect. What was I to do next? The one immediate resource left to me appeared to be this. I might institute inquiries about “Miss Elster of Knowlesbury,” on the chance of advancing towards the main object of my investigation, by first discovering the secret of Mrs. Catherick’s contempt for Sir Percival’s mother.\n\n“Have you found what you wanted, sir?” said the clerk, as I closed the register-book.\n\n“Yes,” I replied, “but I have some inquiries still to make. I suppose the clergyman who officiated here in the year eighteen hundred and three is no longer alive?”\n\n“No, no, sir, he was dead three or four years before I came here, and that was as long ago as the year twenty-seven. I got this place, sir,” persisted my talkative old friend, “through the clerk before me leaving it. They say he was driven out of house and home by his wife—and she’s living still down in the new town there. I don’t know the rights of the story myself—all I know is I got the place. Mr. Wansborough got it for me—the son of my old master that I was telling you of. He’s a free, pleasant gentleman as ever lived—rides to the hounds, keeps his pointers and all that. He’s vestry-clerk here now as his father was before him.”\n\n“Did you not tell me your former master lived at Knowlesbury?” I asked, calling to mind the long story about the precise gentleman of the old school with which my talkative friend had wearied me before he opened the register-book.\n\n“Yes, to be sure, sir,” replied the clerk. “Old Mr. Wansborough lived at Knowlesbury, and young Mr. Wansborough lives there too.”\n\n“You said just now he was vestry-clerk, like his father before him. I am not quite sure that I know what a vestry-clerk is.”\n\n“Don’t you indeed, sir?—and you come from London too! Every parish church, you know, has a vestry-clerk and a parish-clerk. The parish-clerk is a man like me (except that I’ve got a deal more learning than most of them—though I don’t boast of it). The vestry-clerk is a sort of an appointment that the lawyers get, and if there’s any business to be done for the vestry, why there they are to do it. It’s just the same in London. Every parish church there has got its vestry-clerk—and you may take my word for it he’s sure to be a lawyer.”\n\n“Then young Mr. Wansborough is a lawyer, I suppose?”\n\n“Of course he is, sir! A lawyer in High Street, Knowlesbury—the old offices that his father had before him. The number of times I’ve swept those offices out, and seen the old gentleman come trotting in to business on his white pony, looking right and left all down the street and nodding to everybody! Bless you, he was a popular character!—he’d have done in London!”\n\n“How far is it to Knowlesbury from this place?”\n\n“A long stretch, sir,” said the clerk, with that exaggerated idea of distances, and that vivid perception of difficulties in getting from place to place, which is peculiar to all country people. “Nigh on five mile, I can tell you!”\n\nIt was still early in the forenoon. There was plenty of time for a walk to Knowlesbury and back again to Welmingham; and there was no person probably in the town who was fitter to assist my inquiries about the character and position of Sir Percival’s mother before her marriage than the local solicitor. Resolving to go at once to Knowlesbury on foot, I led the way out of the vestry.\n\n“Thank you kindly, sir,” said the clerk, as I slipped my little present into his hand. “Are you really going to walk all the way to Knowlesbury and back? Well! you’re strong on your legs, too—and what a blessing that is, isn’t it? There’s the road, you can’t miss it. I wish I was going your way—it’s pleasant to meet with gentlemen from London in a lost corner like this. One hears the news. Wish you good-morning, sir, and thank you kindly once more.”\n\nWe parted. As I left the church behind me I looked back, and there were the two men again on the road below, with a third in their company, that third person being the short man in black whom I had traced to the railway the evening before.\n\nThe three stood talking together for a little while, then separated. The man in black went away by himself towards Welmingham—the other two remained together, evidently waiting to follow me as soon as I walked on.\n\nI proceeded on my way without letting the fellows see that I took any special notice of them. They caused me no conscious irritation of feeling at that moment—on the contrary, they rather revived my sinking hopes. In the surprise of discovering the evidence of the marriage, I had forgotten the inference I had drawn on first perceiving the men in the neighbourhood of the vestry. Their reappearance reminded me that Sir Percival had anticipated my visit to Old Welmingham church as the next result of my interview with Mrs. Catherick—otherwise he would never have placed his spies there to wait for me. Smoothly and fairly as appearances looked in the vestry, there was something wrong beneath them—there was something in the register-book, for aught I knew, that I had not discovered yet.\n\n# X\n\nOnce out of sight of the church, I pressed forward briskly on my way to Knowlesbury.\n\nThe road was, for the most part, straight and level. Whenever I looked back over it I saw the two spies steadily following me. For the greater part of the way they kept at a safe distance behind. But once or twice they quickened their pace, as if with the purpose of overtaking me, then stopped, consulted together, and fell back again to their former position. They had some special object evidently in view, and they seemed to be hesitating or differing about the best means of accomplishing it. I could not guess exactly what their design might be, but I felt serious doubts of reaching Knowlesbury without some mischance happening to me on the way. These doubts were realised.\n\nI had just entered on a lonely part of the road, with a sharp turn at some distance ahead, and had just concluded (calculating by time) that I must be getting near to the town, when I suddenly heard the steps of the men close behind me.\n\nBefore I could look round, one of them (the man by whom I had been followed in London) passed rapidly on my left side and hustled me with his shoulder. I had been more irritated by the manner in which he and his companion had dogged my steps all the way from Old Welmingham than I was myself aware of, and I unfortunately pushed the fellow away smartly with my open hand. He instantly shouted for help. His companion, the tall man in the gamekeeper’s clothes, sprang to my right side, and the next moment the two scoundrels held me pinioned between them in the middle of the road.\n\nThe conviction that a trap had been laid for me, and the vexation of knowing that I had fallen into it, fortunately restrained me from making my position still worse by an unavailing struggle with two men, one of whom would, in all probability, have been more than a match for me single-handed. I repressed the first natural movement by which I had attempted to shake them off, and looked about to see if there was any person near to whom I could appeal.\n\nA labourer was at work in an adjoining field who must have witnessed all that had passed. I called to him to follow us to the town. He shook his head with stolid obstinacy, and walked away in the direction of a cottage which stood back from the high-road. At the same time the men who held me between them declared their intention of charging me with an assault. I was cool enough and wise enough now to make no opposition. “Drop your hold of my arms,” I said, “and I will go with you to the town.” The man in the gamekeeper’s dress roughly refused. But the shorter man was sharp enough to look to consequences, and not to let his companion commit himself by unnecessary violence. He made a sign to the other, and I walked on between them with my arms free.\n\nWe reached the turning in the road, and there, close before us, were the suburbs of Knowlesbury. One of the local policemen was walking along the path by the roadside. The men at once appealed to him. He replied that the magistrate was then sitting at the town-hall, and recommended that we should appear before him immediately.\n\nWe went on to the town-hall. The clerk made out a formal summons, and the charge was preferred against me, with the customary exaggeration and the customary perversion of the truth on such occasions. The magistrate (an ill-tempered man, with a sour enjoyment in the exercise of his own power) inquired if any one on or near the road had witnessed the assault, and, greatly to my surprise, the complainant admitted the presence of the labourer in the field. I was enlightened, however, as to the object of the admission by the magistrate’s next words. He remanded me at once for the production of the witness, expressing, at the same time, his willingness to take bail for my reappearance if I could produce one responsible surety to offer it. If I had been known in the town he would have liberated me on my own recognisances, but as I was a total stranger it was necessary that I should find responsible bail.\n\nThe whole object of the stratagem was now disclosed to me. It had been so managed as to make a remand necessary in a town where I was a perfect stranger, and where I could not hope to get my liberty on bail. The remand merely extended over three days, until the next sitting of the magistrate. But in that time, while I was in confinement, Sir Percival might use any means he pleased to embarrass my future proceedings—perhaps to screen himself from detection altogether—without the slightest fear of any hindrance on my part. At the end of the three days the charge would, no doubt, be withdrawn, and the attendance of the witness would be perfectly useless.\n\nMy indignation, I may almost say, my despair, at this mischievous check to all further progress—so base and trifling in itself, and yet so disheartening and so serious in its probable results—quite unfitted me at first to reflect on the best means of extricating myself from the dilemma in which I now stood. I had the folly to call for writing materials, and to think of privately communicating my real position to the magistrate. The hopelessness and the imprudence of this proceeding failed to strike me before I had actually written the opening lines of the letter. It was not till I had pushed the paper away—not till, I am ashamed to say, I had almost allowed the vexation of my helpless position to conquer me—that a course of action suddenly occurred to my mind, which Sir Percival had probably not anticipated, and which might set me free again in a few hours. I determined to communicate the situation in which I was placed to Mr. Dawson, of Oak Lodge.\n\nI had visited this gentleman’s house, it may be remembered, at the time of my first inquiries in the Blackwater Park neighbourhood, and I had presented to him a letter of introduction from Miss Halcombe, in which she recommended me to his friendly attention in the strongest terms. I now wrote, referring to this letter, and to what I had previously told Mr. Dawson of the delicate and dangerous nature of my inquiries. I had not revealed to him the truth about Laura, having merely described my errand as being of the utmost importance to private family interests with which Miss Halcombe was concerned. Using the same caution still, I now accounted for my presence at Knowlesbury in the same manner, and I put it to the doctor to say whether the trust reposed in me by a lady whom he well knew, and the hospitality I had myself received in his house, justified me or not in asking him to come to my assistance in a place where I was quite friendless.\n\nI obtained permission to hire a messenger to drive away at once with my letter in a conveyance which might be used to bring the doctor back immediately. Oak Lodge was on the Knowlesbury side of Blackwater. The man declared he could drive there in forty minutes, and could bring Mr. Dawson back in forty more. I directed him to follow the doctor wherever he might happen to be, if he was not at home, and then sat down to wait for the result with all the patience and all the hope that I could summon to help me.\n\nIt was not quite half-past one when the messenger departed. Before half-past three he returned, and brought the doctor with him. Mr. Dawson’s kindness, and the delicacy with which he treated his prompt assistance quite as a matter of course, almost overpowered me. The bail required was offered, and accepted immediately. Before four o’clock, on that afternoon, I was shaking hands warmly with the good old doctor—a free man again—in the streets of Knowlesbury.\n\nMr. Dawson hospitably invited me to go back with him to Oak Lodge, and take up my quarters there for the night. I could only reply that my time was not my own, and I could only ask him to let me pay my visit in a few days, when I might repeat my thanks, and offer to him all the explanations which I felt to be only his due, but which I was not then in a position to make. We parted with friendly assurances on both sides, and I turned my steps at once to Mr. Wansborough’s office in the High Street.\n\nTime was now of the last importance.\n\nThe news of my being free on bail would reach Sir Percival, to an absolute certainty, before night. If the next few hours did not put me in a position to justify his worst fears, and to hold him helpless at my mercy, I might lose every inch of the ground I had gained, never to recover it again. The unscrupulous nature of the man, the local influence he possessed, the desperate peril of exposure with which my blindfold inquiries threatened him—all warned me to press on to positive discovery, without the useless waste of a single minute. I had found time to think while I was waiting for Mr. Dawson’s arrival, and I had well employed it. Certain portions of the conversation of the talkative old clerk, which had wearied me at the time, now recurred to my memory with a new significance, and a suspicion crossed my mind darkly which had not occurred to me while I was in the vestry. On my way to Knowlesbury, I had only proposed to apply to Mr. Wansborough for information on the subject of Sir Percival’s mother. My object now was to examine the duplicate register of Old Welmingham Church.\n\nMr. Wansborough was in his office when I inquired for him.\n\nHe was a jovial, red-faced, easy-looking man—more like a country squire than a lawyer—and he seemed to be both surprised and amused by my application. He had heard of his father’s copy of the register, but had not even seen it himself. It had never been inquired after, and it was no doubt in the strong room among other papers that had not been disturbed since his father’s death. It was a pity (Mr. Wansborough said) that the old gentleman was not alive to hear his precious copy asked for at last. He would have ridden his favourite hobby harder than ever now. How had I come to hear of the copy? was it through anybody in the town?\n\nI parried the question as well as I could. It was impossible at this stage of the investigation to be too cautious, and it was just as well not to let Mr. Wansborough know prematurely that I had already examined the original register. I described myself, therefore, as pursuing a family inquiry, to the object of which every possible saving of time was of great importance. I was anxious to send certain particulars to London by that day’s post, and one look at the duplicate register (paying, of course, the necessary fees) might supply what I required, and save me a further journey to Old Welmingham. I added that, in the event of my subsequently requiring a copy of the original register, I should make application to Mr. Wansborough’s office to furnish me with the document.\n\nAfter this explanation no objection was made to producing the copy. A clerk was sent to the strong room, and after some delay returned with the volume. It was of exactly the same size as the volume in the vestry, the only difference being that the copy was more smartly bound. I took it with me to an unoccupied desk. My hands were trembling—my head was burning hot—I felt the necessity of concealing my agitation as well as I could from the persons about me in the room, before I ventured on opening the book.\n\nOn the blank page at the beginning, to which I first turned, were traced some lines in faded ink. They contained these words—\n\n“Copy of the Marriage Register of Welmingham Parish Church. Executed under my orders, and afterwards compared, entry by entry, with the original, by myself. (Signed) Robert Wansborough, vestry-clerk.” Below this note there was a line added, in another handwriting, as follows: “Extending from the first of January, 1800, to the thirtieth of June, 1815.”\n\nI turned to the month of September, eighteen hundred and three. I found the marriage of the man whose Christian name was the same as my own. I found the double register of the marriages of the two brothers. And between these entries, at the bottom of the page?\n\nNothing! Not a vestige of the entry which recorded the marriage of Sir Felix Glyde and Cecilia Jane Elster in the register of the church!\n\nMy heart gave a great bound, and throbbed as if it would stifle me. I looked again—I was afraid to believe the evidence of my own eyes. No! not a doubt. The marriage was not there. The entries on the copy occupied exactly the same places on the page as the entries in the original. The last entry on one page recorded the marriage of the man with my Christian name. Below it there was a blank space—a space evidently left because it was too narrow to contain the entry of the marriages of the two brothers, which in the copy, as in the original, occupied the top of the next page. That space told the whole story! There it must have remained in the church register from eighteen hundred and three (when the marriages had been solemnised and the copy had been made) to eighteen hundred and twenty-seven, when Sir Percival appeared at Old Welmingham. Here, at Knowlesbury, was the chance of committing the forgery shown to me in the copy, and there, at Old Welmingham, was the forgery committed in the register of the church.\n\nMy head turned giddy—I held by the desk to keep myself from falling. Of all the suspicions which had struck me in relation to that desperate man, not one had been near the truth.\n\nThe idea that he was not Sir Percival Glyde at all, that he had no more claim to the baronetcy and to Blackwater Park than the poorest labourer who worked on the estate, had never once occurred to my mind. At one time I had thought he might be Anne Catherick’s father—at another time I had thought he might have been Anne Catherick’s husband—the offence of which he was really guilty had been, from first to last, beyond the widest reach of my imagination.\n\nThe paltry means by which the fraud had been effected, the magnitude and daring of the crime that it represented, the horror of the consequences involved in its discovery, overwhelmed me. Who could wonder now at the brute-restlessness of the wretch’s life—at his desperate alternations between abject duplicity and reckless violence—at the madness of guilty distrust which had made him imprison Anne Catherick in the Asylum, and had given him over to the vile conspiracy against his wife, on the bare suspicion that the one and the other knew his terrible secret? The disclosure of that secret might, in past years, have hanged him—might now transport him for life. The disclosure of that secret, even if the sufferers by his deception spared him the penalties of the law, would deprive him at one blow of the name, the rank, the estate, the whole social existence that he had usurped. This was the Secret, and it was mine! A word from me, and house, lands, baronetcy, were gone from him for ever—a word from me, and he was driven out into the world, a nameless, penniless, friendless outcast! The man’s whole future hung on my lips—and he knew it by this time as certainly as I did!\n\nThat last thought steadied me. Interests far more precious than my own depended on the caution which must now guide my slightest actions. There was no possible treachery which Sir Percival might not attempt against me. In the danger and desperation of his position he would be staggered by no risks, he would recoil at no crime—he would literally hesitate at nothing to save himself.\n\nI considered for a minute. My first necessity was to secure positive evidence in writing of the discovery that I had just made, and in the event of any personal misadventure happening to me, to place that evidence beyond Sir Percival’s reach. The copy of the register was sure to be safe in Mr. Wansborough’s strong room. But the position of the original in the vestry was, as I had seen with my own eyes, anything but secure.\n\nIn this emergency I resolved to return to the church, to apply again to the clerk, and to take the necessary extract from the register before I slept that night. I was not then aware that a legally-certified copy was necessary, and that no document merely drawn out by myself could claim the proper importance as a proof. I was not aware of this, and my determination to keep my present proceedings a secret prevented me from asking any questions which might have procured the necessary information. My one anxiety was the anxiety to get back to Old Welmingham. I made the best excuses I could for the discomposure in my face and manner which Mr. Wansborough had already noticed, laid the necessary fee on his table, arranged that I should write to him in a day or two, and left the office, with my head in a whirl and my blood throbbing through my veins at fever heat.\n\nIt was just getting dark. The idea occurred to me that I might be followed again and attacked on the high-road.\n\nMy walking-stick was a light one, of little or no use for purposes of defence. I stopped before leaving Knowlesbury and bought a stout country cudgel, short, and heavy at the head. With this homely weapon, if any one man tried to stop me I was a match for him. If more than one attacked me I could trust to my heels. In my school-days I had been a noted runner, and I had not wanted for practice since in the later time of my experience in Central America.\n\nI started from the town at a brisk pace, and kept the middle of the road.\n\nA small misty rain was falling, and it was impossible for the first half of the way to make sure whether I was followed or not. But at the last half of my journey, when I supposed myself to be about two miles from the church, I saw a man run by me in the rain, and then heard the gate of a field by the roadside shut to sharply. I kept straight on, with my cudgel ready in my hand, my ears on the alert, and my eyes straining to see through the mist and the darkness. Before I had advanced a hundred yards there was a rustling in the hedge on my right, and three men sprang out into the road.\n\nI drew aside on the instant to the footpath. The two foremost men were carried beyond me before they could check themselves. The third was as quick as lightning. He stopped, half turned, and struck at me with his stick. The blow was aimed at hazard, and was not a severe one. It fell on my left shoulder. I returned it heavily on his head. He staggered back and jostled his two companions just as they were both rushing at me. This circumstance gave me a moment’s start. I slipped by them, and took to the middle of the road again at the top of my speed.\n\nThe two unhurt men pursued me. They were both good runners—the road was smooth and level, and for the first five minutes or more I was conscious that I did not gain on them. It was perilous work to run for long in the darkness. I could barely see the dim black line of the hedges on either side, and any chance obstacle in the road would have thrown me down to a certainty. Ere long I felt the ground changing—it descended from the level at a turn, and then rose again beyond. Downhill the men rather gained on me, but uphill I began to distance them. The rapid, regular thump of their feet grew fainter on my ear, and I calculated by the sound that I was far enough in advance to take to the fields with a good chance of their passing me in the darkness. Diverging to the footpath, I made for the first break that I could guess at, rather than see, in the hedge. It proved to be a closed gate. I vaulted over, and finding myself in a field, kept across it steadily with my back to the road. I heard the men pass the gate, still running, then in a minute more heard one of them call to the other to come back. It was no matter what they did now, I was out of their sight and out of their hearing. I kept straight across the field, and when I had reached the farther extremity of it, waited there for a minute to recover my breath.\n\nIt was impossible to venture back to the road, but I was determined nevertheless to get to Old Welmingham that evening.\n\nNeither moon nor stars appeared to guide me. I only knew that I had kept the wind and rain at my back on leaving Knowlesbury, and if I now kept them at my back still, I might at least be certain of not advancing altogether in the wrong direction.\n\nProceeding on this plan, I crossed the country—meeting with no worse obstacles than hedges, ditches, and thickets, which every now and then obliged me to alter my course for a little while—until I found myself on a hill-side, with the ground sloping away steeply before me. I descended to the bottom of the hollow, squeezed my way through a hedge, and got out into a lane. Having turned to the right on leaving the road, I now turned to the left, on the chance of regaining the line from which I had wandered. After following the muddy windings of the lane for ten minutes or more, I saw a cottage with a light in one of the windows. The garden gate was open to the lane, and I went in at once to inquire my way.\n\nBefore I could knock at the door it was suddenly opened, and a man came running out with a lighted lantern in his hand. He stopped and held it up at the sight of me. We both started as we saw each other. My wanderings had led me round the outskirts of the village, and had brought me out at the lower end of it. I was back at Old Welmingham, and the man with the lantern was no other than my acquaintance of the morning, the parish clerk.\n\nHis manner appeared to have altered strangely in the interval since I had last seen him. He looked suspicious and confused—his ruddy cheeks were deeply flushed—and his first words, when he spoke, were quite unintelligible to me.\n\n“Where are the keys?” he asked. “Have you taken them?”\n\n“What keys?” I repeated. “I have this moment come from Knowlesbury. What keys do you mean?”\n\n“The keys of the vestry. Lord save us and help us! what shall I do? The keys are gone! Do you hear?” cried the old man, shaking the lantern at me in his agitation, “the keys are gone!”\n\n“How? When? Who can have taken them?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said the clerk, staring about him wildly in the darkness. “I’ve only just got back. I told you I had a long day’s work this morning—I locked the door and shut the window down—it’s open now, the window’s open. Look! somebody has got in there and taken the keys.”\n\nHe turned to the casement window to show me that it was wide open. The door of the lantern came loose from its fastening as he swayed it round, and the wind blew the candle out instantly.\n\n“Get another light,” I said, “and let us both go to the vestry together. Quick! quick!”\n\nI hurried him into the house. The treachery that I had every reason to expect, the treachery that might deprive me of every advantage I had gained, was at that moment, perhaps, in process of accomplishment. My impatience to reach the church was so great that I could not remain inactive in the cottage while the clerk lit the lantern again. I walked out, down the garden path, into the lane.\n\nBefore I had advanced ten paces a man approached me from the direction leading to the church. He spoke respectfully as we met. I could not see his face, but judging by his voice only, he was a perfect stranger to me.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Sir Percival——” he began.\n\nI stopped him before he could say more.\n\n“The darkness misleads you,” I said. “I am not Sir Percival.”\n\nThe man drew back directly.\n\n“I thought it was my master,” he muttered, in a confused, doubtful way.\n\n“You expected to meet your master here?”\n\n“I was told to wait in the lane.”\n\nWith that answer he retraced his steps. I looked back at the cottage and saw the clerk coming out, with the lantern lighted once more. I took the old man’s arm to help him on the more quickly. We hastened along the lane, and passed the person who had accosted me. As well as I could see by the light of the lantern, he was a servant out of livery.\n\n“Who’s that?” whispered the clerk. “Does he know anything about the keys?”\n\n“We won’t wait to ask him,” I replied. “We will go on to the vestry first.”\n\nThe church was not visible, even by daytime, until the end of the lane was reached. As we mounted the rising ground which led to the building from that point, one of the village children—a boy—came close up to us, attracted by the light we carried, and recognised the clerk.\n\n“I say, measter,” said the boy, pulling officiously at the clerk’s coat, “there be summun up yander in the church. I heerd un lock the door on hisself—I heerd un strike a loight wi’ a match.”\n\nThe clerk trembled and leaned against me heavily.\n\n“Come! come!” I said encouragingly. “We are not too late. We will catch the man, whoever he is. Keep the lantern, and follow me as fast as you can.”\n\nI mounted the hill rapidly. The dark mass of the church-tower was the first object I discerned dimly against the night sky. As I turned aside to get round to the vestry, I heard heavy footsteps close to me. The servant had ascended to the church after us. “I don’t mean any harm,” he said, when I turned round on him, “I’m only looking for my master.” The tones in which he spoke betrayed unmistakable fear. I took no notice of him and went on.\n\nThe instant I turned the corner and came in view of the vestry, I saw the lantern-skylight on the roof brilliantly lit up from within. It shone out with dazzling brightness against the murky, starless sky.\n\nI hurried through the churchyard to the door.\n\nAs I got near there was a strange smell stealing out on the damp night air. I heard a snapping noise inside—I saw the light above grow brighter and brighter—a pane of the glass cracked—I ran to the door and put my hand on it. The vestry was on fire!\n\nBefore I could move, before I could draw my breath after that discovery, I was horror-struck by a heavy thump against the door from the inside. I heard the key worked violently in the lock—I heard a man’s voice behind the door, raised to a dreadful shrillness, screaming for help.\n\nThe servant who had followed me staggered back shuddering, and dropped to his knees. “Oh, my God!” he said, “it’s Sir Percival!”\n\nAs the words passed his lips the clerk joined us, and at the same moment there was another and a last grating turn of the key in the lock.\n\n“The Lord have mercy on his soul!” said the old man. “He is doomed and dead. He has hampered the lock.”\n\nI rushed to the door. The one absorbing purpose that had filled all my thoughts, that had controlled all my actions, for weeks and weeks past, vanished in an instant from my mind. All remembrance of the heartless injury the man’s crimes had inflicted—of the love, the innocence, the happiness he had pitilessly laid waste—of the oath I had sworn in my own heart to summon him to the terrible reckoning that he deserved—passed from my memory like a dream. I remembered nothing but the horror of his situation. I felt nothing but the natural human impulse to save him from a frightful death.\n\n“Try the other door!” I shouted. “Try the door into the church! The lock’s hampered. You’re a dead man if you waste another moment on it.”\n\nThere had been no renewed cry for help when the key was turned for the last time. There was no sound now of any kind, to give token that he was still alive. I heard nothing but the quickening crackle of the flames, and the sharp snap of the glass in the skylight above.\n\nI looked round at my two companions. The servant had risen to his feet—he had taken the lantern, and was holding it up vacantly at the door. Terror seemed to have struck him with downright idiocy—he waited at my heels, he followed me about when I moved like a dog. The clerk sat crouched up on one of the tombstones, shivering, and moaning to himself. The one moment in which I looked at them was enough to show me that they were both helpless.\n\nHardly knowing what I did, acting desperately on the first impulse that occurred to me, I seized the servant and pushed him against the vestry wall. “Stoop!” I said, “and hold by the stones. I am going to climb over you to the roof—I am going to break the skylight, and give him some air!”\n\nThe man trembled from head to foot, but he held firm. I got on his back, with my cudgel in my mouth, seized the parapet with both hands, and was instantly on the roof. In the frantic hurry and agitation of the moment, it never struck me that I might let out the flame instead of letting in the air. I struck at the skylight, and battered in the cracked, loosened glass at a blow. The fire leaped out like a wild beast from its lair. If the wind had not chanced, in the position I occupied, to set it away from me, my exertions might have ended then and there. I crouched on the roof as the smoke poured out above me with the flame. The gleams and flashes of the light showed me the servant’s face staring up vacantly under the wall—the clerk risen to his feet on the tombstone, wringing his hands in despair—and the scanty population of the village, haggard men and terrified women, clustered beyond in the churchyard—all appearing and disappearing, in the red of the dreadful glare, in the black of the choking smoke. And the man beneath my feet!—the man, suffocating, burning, dying so near us all, so utterly beyond our reach!\n\nThe thought half maddened me. I lowered myself from the roof, by my hands, and dropped to the ground.\n\n“The key of the church!” I shouted to the clerk. “We must try it that way—we may save him yet if we can burst open the inner door.”\n\n“No, no, no!” cried the old man. “No hope! the church key and the vestry key are on the same ring—both inside there! Oh, sir, he’s past saving—he’s dust and ashes by this time!”\n\n“They’ll see the fire from the town,” said a voice from among the men behind me. “There’s a ingine in the town. They’ll save the church.”\n\nI called to that man—HE had his wits about him—I called to him to come and speak to me. It would be a quarter of an hour at least before the town engine could reach us. The horror of remaining inactive all that time was more than I could face. In defiance of my own reason I persuaded myself that the doomed and lost wretch in the vestry might still be lying senseless on the floor, might not be dead yet. If we broke open the door, might we save him? I knew the strength of the heavy lock—I knew the thickness of the nailed oak—I knew the hopelessness of assailing the one and the other by ordinary means. But surely there were beams still left in the dismantled cottages near the church? What if we got one, and used it as a battering-ram against the door?\n\nThe thought leaped through me like the fire leaping out of the shattered skylight. I appealed to the man who had spoken first of the fire-engine in the town. “Have you got your pickaxes handy?” Yes, they had. “And a hatchet, and a saw, and a bit of rope?” Yes! yes! yes! I ran down among the villagers, with the lantern in my hand. “Five shillings apiece to every man who helps me!” They started into life at the words. That ravenous second hunger of poverty—the hunger for money—roused them into tumult and activity in a moment. “Two of you for more lanterns, if you have them! Two of you for the pickaxes and the tools! The rest after me to find the beam!” They cheered—with shrill starveling voices they cheered. The women and the children fled back on either side. We rushed in a body down the churchyard path to the first empty cottage. Not a man was left behind but the clerk—the poor old clerk standing on the flat tombstone sobbing and wailing over the church. The servant was still at my heels—his white, helpless, panic-stricken face was close over my shoulder as we pushed into the cottage. There were rafters from the torn-down floor above, lying loose on the ground—but they were too light. A beam ran across over our heads, but not out of reach of our arms and our pickaxes—a beam fast at each end in the ruined wall, with ceiling and flooring all ripped away, and a great gap in the roof above, open to the sky. We attacked the beam at both ends at once. God! how it held—how the brick and mortar of the wall resisted us! We struck, and tugged, and tore. The beam gave at one end—it came down with a lump of brickwork after it. There was a scream from the women all huddled in the doorway to look at us—a shout from the men—two of them down but not hurt. Another tug all together—and the beam was loose at both ends. We raised it, and gave the word to clear the doorway. Now for the work! now for the rush at the door! There is the fire streaming into the sky, streaming brighter than ever to light us! Steady along the churchyard path—steady with the beam for a rush at the door. One, two, three—and off. Out rings the cheering again, irrepressibly. We have shaken it already, the hinges must give if the lock won’t. Another run with the beam! One, two, three—and off. It’s loose! the stealthy fire darts at us through the crevice all round it. Another, and a last rush! The door falls in with a crash. A great hush of awe, a stillness of breathless expectation, possesses every living soul of us. We look for the body. The scorching heat on our faces drives us back: we see nothing—above, below, all through the room, we see nothing but a sheet of living fire.\n\n“Where is he?” whispered the servant, staring vacantly at the flames.\n\n“He’s dust and ashes,” said the clerk. “And the books are dust and ashes—and oh, sirs! the church will be dust and ashes soon.”\n\nThose were the only two who spoke. When they were silent again, nothing stirred in the stillness but the bubble and the crackle of the flames.\n\nHark!\n\nA harsh rattling sound in the distance—then the hollow beat of horses’ hoofs at full gallop—then the low roar, the all-predominant tumult of hundreds of human voices clamouring and shouting together. The engine at last.\n\nThe people about me all turned from the fire, and ran eagerly to the brow of the hill. The old clerk tried to go with the rest, but his strength was exhausted. I saw him holding by one of the tombstones. “Save the church!” he cried out faintly, as if the firemen could hear him already.\n\n“Save the church!”\n\nThe only man who never moved was the servant. There he stood, his eyes still fastened on the flames in a changeless, vacant stare. I spoke to him, I shook him by the arm. He was past rousing. He only whispered once more, “Where is he?”\n\nIn ten minutes the engine was in position, the well at the back of the church was feeding it, and the hose was carried to the doorway of the vestry. If help had been wanted from me I could not have afforded it now. My energy of will was gone—my strength was exhausted—the turmoil of my thoughts was fearfully and suddenly stilled, now I knew that he was dead.\n\nI stood useless and helpless—looking, looking, looking into the burning room.\n\nI saw the fire slowly conquered. The brightness of the glare faded—the steam rose in white clouds, and the smouldering heaps of embers showed red and black through it on the floor. There was a pause—then an advance all together of the firemen and the police which blocked up the doorway—then a consultation in low voices—and then two men were detached from the rest, and sent out of the churchyard through the crowd. The crowd drew back on either side in dead silence to let them pass.\n\nAfter a while a great shudder ran through the people, and the living lane widened slowly. The men came back along it with a door from one of the empty houses. They carried it to the vestry and went in. The police closed again round the doorway, and men stole out from among the crowd by twos and threes and stood behind them to be the first to see. Others waited near to be the first to hear. Women and children were among these last.\n\nThe tidings from the vestry began to flow out among the crowd—they dropped slowly from mouth to mouth till they reached the place where I was standing. I heard the questions and answers repeated again and again in low, eager tones all round me.\n\n“Have they found him?” “Yes.”—“Where?” “Against the door, on his face.”—“Which door?” “The door that goes into the church. His head was against it—he was down on his face.”—“Is his face burnt?” “No.” “Yes, it is.” “No, scorched, not burnt—he lay on his face, I tell you.”—“Who was he? A lord, they say.” “No, not a lord. SIR Something; Sir means Knight.” “And Baronight, too.” “No.” “Yes, it does.”—“What did he want in there?” “No good, you may depend on it.”—“Did he do it on purpose?”—“Burn himself on purpose!”—“I don’t mean himself, I mean the vestry.”—“Is he dreadful to look at?” “Dreadful!”—“Not about the face, though?” “No, no, not so much about the face. Don’t anybody know him?” “There’s a man says he does.”—“Who?” “A servant, they say. But he’s struck stupid-like, and the police don’t believe him.”—“Don’t anybody else know who it is?” “Hush——!”\n\nThe loud, clear voice of a man in authority silenced the low hum of talking all round me in an instant.\n\n“Where is the gentleman who tried to save him?” said the voice.\n\n“Here, sir—here he is!” Dozens of eager faces pressed about me—dozens of eager arms parted the crowd. The man in authority came up to me with a lantern in his hand.\n\n“This way, sir, if you please,” he said quietly.\n\nI was unable to speak to him, I was unable to resist him when he took my arm. I tried to say that I had never seen the dead man in his lifetime—that there was no hope of identifying him by means of a stranger like me. But the words failed on my lips. I was faint, and silent, and helpless.\n\n“Do you know him, sir?”\n\nI was standing inside a circle of men. Three of them opposite to me were holding lanterns low down to the ground. Their eyes, and the eyes of all the rest, were fixed silently and expectantly on my face. I knew what was at my feet—I knew why they were holding the lanterns so low to the ground.\n\n“Can you identify him, sir?”\n\nMy eyes dropped slowly. At first I saw nothing under them but a coarse canvas cloth. The dripping of the rain on it was audible in the dreadful silence. I looked up, along the cloth, and there at the end, stark and grim and black, in the yellow light—there was his dead face.\n\nSo, for the first and last time, I saw him. So the Visitation of God ruled it that he and I should meet.\n\n# XI\n\nThe inquest was hurried for certain local reasons which weighed with the coroner and the town authorities. It was held on the afternoon of the next day. I was necessarily one among the witnesses summoned to assist the objects of the investigation.\n\nMy first proceeding in the morning was to go to the post-office, and inquire for the letter which I expected from Marian. No change of circumstances, however extraordinary, could affect the one great anxiety which weighed on my mind while I was away from London. The morning’s letter, which was the only assurance I could receive that no misfortune had happened in my absence, was still the absorbing interest with which my day began.\n\nTo my relief, the letter from Marian was at the office waiting for me.\n\nNothing had happened—they were both as safe and as well as when I had left them. Laura sent her love, and begged that I would let her know of my return a day beforehand. Her sister added, in explanation of this message, that she had saved “nearly a sovereign” out of her own private purse, and that she had claimed the privilege of ordering the dinner and giving the dinner which was to celebrate the day of my return. I read these little domestic confidences in the bright morning with the terrible recollection of what had happened the evening before vivid in my memory. The necessity of sparing Laura any sudden knowledge of the truth was the first consideration which the letter suggested to me. I wrote at once to Marian to tell her what I have told in these pages—presenting the tidings as gradually and gently as I could, and warning her not to let any such thing as a newspaper fall in Laura’s way while I was absent. In the case of any other woman, less courageous and less reliable, I might have hesitated before I ventured on unreservedly disclosing the whole truth. But I owed it to Marian to be faithful to my past experience of her, and to trust her as I trusted myself.\n\nMy letter was necessarily a long one. It occupied me until the time came for proceeding to the inquest.\n\nThe objects of the legal inquiry were necessarily beset by peculiar complications and difficulties. Besides the investigation into the manner in which the deceased had met his death, there were serious questions to be settled relating to the cause of the fire, to the abstraction of the keys, and to the presence of a stranger in the vestry at the time when the flames broke out. Even the identification of the dead man had not yet been accomplished. The helpless condition of the servant had made the police distrustful of his asserted recognition of his master. They had sent to Knowlesbury overnight to secure the attendance of witnesses who were well acquainted with the personal appearance of Sir Percival Glyde, and they had communicated, the first thing in the morning, with Blackwater Park. These precautions enabled the coroner and jury to settle the question of identity, and to confirm the correctness of the servant’s assertion; the evidence offered by competent witnesses, and by the discovery of certain facts, being subsequently strengthened by an examination of the dead man’s watch. The crest and the name of Sir Percival Glyde were engraved inside it.\n\nThe next inquiries related to the fire.\n\nThe servant and I, and the boy who had heard the light struck in the vestry, were the first witnesses called. The boy gave his evidence clearly enough, but the servant’s mind had not yet recovered the shock inflicted on it—he was plainly incapable of assisting the objects of the inquiry, and he was desired to stand down.\n\nTo my own relief, my examination was not a long one. I had not known the deceased—I had never seen him—I was not aware of his presence at Old Welmingham—and I had not been in the vestry at the finding of the body. All I could prove was that I had stopped at the clerk’s cottage to ask my way—that I had heard from him of the loss of the keys—that I had accompanied him to the church to render what help I could—that I had seen the fire—that I had heard some person unknown, inside the vestry, trying vainly to unlock the door—and that I had done what I could, from motives of humanity, to save the man. Other witnesses, who had been acquainted with the deceased, were asked if they could explain the mystery of his presumed abstraction of the keys, and his presence in the burning room. But the coroner seemed to take it for granted, naturally enough, that I, as a total stranger in the neighbourhood, and a total stranger to Sir Percival Glyde, could not be in a position to offer any evidence on these two points.\n\nThe course that I was myself bound to take, when my formal examination had closed, seemed clear to me. I did not feel called on to volunteer any statement of my own private convictions; in the first place, because my doing so could serve no practical purpose, now that all proof in support of any surmises of mine was burnt with the burnt register; in the second place, because I could not have intelligibly stated my opinion—my unsupported opinion—without disclosing the whole story of the conspiracy, and producing beyond a doubt the same unsatisfactory effect an the minds of the coroner and the jury, which I had already produced on the mind of Mr. Kyrle.\n\nIn these pages, however, and after the time that has now elapsed, no such cautions and restraints as are here described need fetter the free expression of my opinion. I will state briefly, before my pen occupies itself with other events, how my own convictions lead me to account for the abstraction of the keys, for the outbreak of the fire, and for the death of the man.\n\nThe news of my being free on bail drove Sir Percival, as I believe, to his last resources. The attempted attack on the road was one of those resources, and the suppression of all practical proof of his crime, by destroying the page of the register on which the forgery had been committed, was the other, and the surest of the two. If I could produce no extract from the original book to compare with the certified copy at Knowlesbury, I could produce no positive evidence, and could threaten him with no fatal exposure. All that was necessary to the attainment of his end was, that he should get into the vestry unperceived, that he should tear out the page in the register, and that he should leave the vestry again as privately as he had entered it.\n\nOn this supposition, it is easy to understand why he waited until nightfall before he made the attempt, and why he took advantage of the clerk’s absence to possess himself of the keys. Necessity would oblige him to strike a light to find his way to the right register, and common caution would suggest his locking the door on the inside in case of intrusion on the part of any inquisitive stranger, or on my part, if I happened to be in the neighbourhood at the time.\n\nI cannot believe that it was any part of his intention to make the destruction of the register appear to be the result of accident, by purposely setting the vestry on fire. The bare chance that prompt assistance might arrive, and that the books might, by the remotest possibility, be saved, would have been enough, on a moment’s consideration, to dismiss any idea of this sort from his mind. Remembering the quantity of combustible objects in the vestry—the straw, the papers, the packing-cases, the dry wood, the old worm-eaten presses—all the probabilities, in my estimation, point to the fire as the result of an accident with his matches or his light.\n\nHis first impulse, under these circumstances, was doubtless to try to extinguish the flames, and failing in that, his second impulse (ignorant as he was of the state of the lock) had been to attempt to escape by the door which had given him entrance. When I had called to him, the flames must have reached across the door leading into the church, on either side of which the presses extended, and close to which the other combustible objects were placed. In all probability, the smoke and flame (confined as they were to the room) had been too much for him when he tried to escape by the inner door. He must have dropped in his death-swoon—he must have sunk in the place where he was found—just as I got on the roof to break the skylight window. Even if we had been able, afterwards, to get into the church, and to burst open the door from that side, the delay must have been fatal. He would have been past saving, long past saving, by that time. We should only have given the flames free ingress into the church—the church, which was now preserved, but which, in that event, would have shared the fate of the vestry. There is no doubt in my mind, there can be no doubt in the mind of any one, that he was a dead man before ever we got to the empty cottage, and worked with might and main to tear down the beam.\n\nThis is the nearest approach that any theory of mine can make towards accounting for a result which was visible matter of fact. As I have described them, so events passed to us outside. As I have related it, so his body was found.\n\nThe inquest was adjourned over one day—no explanation that the eye of the law could recognise having been discovered thus far to account for the mysterious circumstances of the case.\n\nIt was arranged that more witnesses should be summoned, and that the London solicitor of the deceased should be invited to attend. A medical man was also charged with the duty of reporting on the mental condition of the servant, which appeared at present to debar him from giving any evidence of the least importance. He could only declare, in a dazed way, that he had been ordered, on the night of the fire, to wait in the lane, and that he knew nothing else, except that the deceased was certainly his master.\n\nMy own impression was, that he had been first used (without any guilty knowledge on his own part) to ascertain the fact of the clerk’s absence from home on the previous day, and that he had been afterwards ordered to wait near the church (but out of sight of the vestry) to assist his master, in the event of my escaping the attack on the road, and of a collision occurring between Sir Percival and myself. It is necessary to add, that the man’s own testimony was never obtained to confirm this view. The medical report of him declared that what little mental faculty he possessed was seriously shaken; nothing satisfactory was extracted from him at the adjourned inquest, and for aught I know to the contrary, he may never have recovered to this day.\n\nI returned to the hotel at Welmingham so jaded in body and mind, so weakened and depressed by all that I had gone through, as to be quite unfit to endure the local gossip about the inquest, and to answer the trivial questions that the talkers addressed to me in the coffee-room. I withdrew from my scanty dinner to my cheap garret-chamber to secure myself a little quiet, and to think undisturbed of Laura and Marian.\n\nIf I had been a richer man I would have gone back to London, and would have comforted myself with a sight of the two dear faces again that night. But I was bound to appear, if called on, at the adjourned inquest, and doubly bound to answer my bail before the magistrate at Knowlesbury. Our slender resources had suffered already, and the doubtful future—more doubtful than ever now—made me dread decreasing our means unnecessarily by allowing myself an indulgence even at the small cost of a double railway journey in the carriages of the second class.\n\nThe next day—the day immediately following the inquest—was left at my own disposal. I began the morning by again applying at the post-office for my regular report from Marian. It was waiting for me as before, and it was written throughout in good spirits. I read the letter thankfully, and then set forth with my mind at ease for the day to go to Old Welmingham, and to view the scene of the fire by the morning light.\n\nWhat changes met me when I got there!\n\nThrough all the ways of our unintelligible world the trivial and the terrible walk hand in hand together. The irony of circumstances holds no mortal catastrophe in respect. When I reached the church, the trampled condition of the burial-ground was the only serious trace left to tell of the fire and the death. A rough hoarding of boards had been knocked up before the vestry doorway. Rude caricatures were scrawled on it already, and the village children were fighting and shouting for the possession of the best peep-hole to see through. On the spot where I had heard the cry for help from the burning room, on the spot where the panic-stricken servant had dropped on his knees, a fussy flock of poultry was now scrambling for the first choice of worms after the rain; and on the ground at my feet, where the door and its dreadful burden had been laid, a workman’s dinner was waiting for him, tied up in a yellow basin, and his faithful cur in charge was yelping at me for coming near the food. The old clerk, looking idly at the slow commencement of the repairs, had only one interest that he could talk about now—the interest of escaping all blame for his own part on account of the accident that had happened. One of the village women, whose white wild face I remembered the picture of terror when we pulled down the beam, was giggling with another woman, the picture of inanity, over an old washing-tub. There is nothing serious in mortality! Solomon in all his glory was Solomon with the elements of the contemptible lurking in every fold of his robes and in every corner of his palace.\n\nAs I left the place, my thoughts turned, not for the first time, to the complete overthrow that all present hope of establishing Laura’s identity had now suffered through Sir Percival’s death. He was gone—and with him the chance was gone which had been the one object of all my labours and all my hopes.\n\nCould I look at my failure from no truer point of view than this?\n\nSuppose he had lived, would that change of circumstance have altered the result? Could I have made my discovery a marketable commodity, even for Laura’s sake, after I had found out that robbery of the rights of others was the essence of Sir Percival’s crime? Could I have offered the price of MY silence for HIS confession of the conspiracy, when the effect of that silence must have been to keep the right heir from the estates, and the right owner from the name? Impossible! If Sir Percival had lived, the discovery, from which (in my ignorance of the true nature of the Secret) I had hoped so much, could not have been mine to suppress or to make public, as I thought best, for the vindication of Laura’s rights. In common honesty and common honour I must have gone at once to the stranger whose birthright had been usurped—I must have renounced the victory at the moment when it was mine by placing my discovery unreservedly in that stranger’s hands—and I must have faced afresh all the difficulties which stood between me and the one object of my life, exactly as I was resolved in my heart of hearts to face them now!\n\nI returned to Welmingham with my mind composed, feeling more sure of myself and my resolution than I had felt yet.\n\nOn my way to the hotel I passed the end of the square in which Mrs. Catherick lived. Should I go back to the house, and make another attempt to see her? No. That news of Sir Percival’s death, which was the last news she ever expected to hear, must have reached her hours since. All the proceedings at the inquest had been reported in the local paper that morning—there was nothing I could tell her which she did not know already. My interest in making her speak had slackened. I remembered the furtive hatred in her face when she said, “There is no news of Sir Percival that I don’t expect—except the news of his death.” I remembered the stealthy interest in her eyes when they settled on me at parting, after she had spoken those words. Some instinct, deep in my heart, which I felt to be a true one, made the prospect of again entering her presence repulsive to me—I turned away from the square, and went straight back to the hotel.\n\nSome hours later, while I was resting in the coffee-room, a letter was placed in my hands by the waiter. It was addressed to me by name, and I found on inquiry that it had been left at the bar by a woman just as it was near dusk, and just before the gas was lighted. She had said nothing, and she had gone away again before there was time to speak to her, or even to notice who she was.\n\nI opened the letter. It was neither dated nor signed, and the handwriting was palpably disguised. Before I had read the first sentence, however, I knew who my correspondent was—Mrs. Catherick.\n\nThe letter ran as follows—I copy it exactly, word for word:—\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED BY MRS. CATHERICK\n\nSIR,—You have not come back, as you said you would. No matter—I know the news, and I write to tell you so. Did you see anything particular in my face when you left me? I was wondering, in my own mind, whether the day of his downfall had come at last, and whether you were the chosen instrument for working it. You were, and you HAVE worked it.\n\nYou were weak enough, as I have heard, to try and save his life. If you had succeeded, I should have looked upon you as my enemy. Now you have failed, I hold you as my friend. Your inquiries frightened him into the vestry by night—your inquiries, without your privity and against your will, have served the hatred and wreaked the vengeance of three-and-twenty years. Thank you, sir, in spite of yourself.\n\nI owe something to the man who has done this. How can I pay my debt? If I was a young woman still I might say, “Come, put your arm round my waist, and kiss me, if you like.” I should have been fond enough of you even to go that length, and you would have accepted my invitation—you would, sir, twenty years ago! But I am an old woman now. Well! I can satisfy your curiosity, and pay my debt in that way. You HAD a great curiosity to know certain private affairs of mine when you came to see me—private affairs which all your sharpness could not look into without my help—private affairs which you have not discovered, even now. You SHALL discover them—your curiosity shall be satisfied. I will take any trouble to please you, my estimable young friend!\n\nYou were a little boy, I suppose, in the year twenty-seven? I was a handsome young woman at that time, living at Old Welmingham. I had a contemptible fool for a husband. I had also the honour of being acquainted (never mind how) with a certain gentleman (never mind whom). I shall not call him by his name. Why should I? It was not his own. He never had a name: you know that, by this time, as well as I do.\n\nIt will be more to the purpose to tell you how he worked himself into my good graces. I was born with the tastes of a lady, and he gratified them—in other words, he admired me, and he made me presents. No woman can resist admiration and presents—especially presents, provided they happen to be just the thing she wants. He was sharp enough to know that—most men are. Naturally he wanted something in return—all men do. And what do you think was the something? The merest trifle. Nothing but the key of the vestry, and the key of the press inside it, when my husband’s back was turned. Of course he lied when I asked him why he wished me to get him the keys in that private way. He might have saved himself the trouble—I didn’t believe him. But I liked my presents, and I wanted more. So I got him the keys, without my husband’s knowledge, and I watched him, without his own knowledge. Once, twice, four times I watched him, and the fourth time I found him out.\n\nI was never over-scrupulous where other people’s affairs were concerned, and I was not over-scrupulous about his adding one to the marriages in the register on his own account.\n\nOf course I knew it was wrong, but it did no harm to me, which was one good reason for not making a fuss about it. And I had not got a gold watch and chain, which was another, still better—and he had promised me one from London only the day before, which was a third, best of all. If I had known what the law considered the crime to be, and how the law punished it, I should have taken proper care of myself, and have exposed him then and there. But I knew nothing, and I longed for the gold watch. All the conditions I insisted on were that he should take me into his confidence and tell me everything. I was as curious about his affairs then as you are about mine now. He granted my conditions—why, you will see presently.\n\nThis, put in short, is what I heard from him. He did not willingly tell me all that I tell you here. I drew some of it from him by persuasion and some of it by questions. I was determined to have all the truth, and I believe I got it.\n\nHe knew no more than any one else of what the state of things really was between his father and mother till after his mother’s death. Then his father confessed it, and promised to do what he could for his son. He died having done nothing—not having even made a will. The son (who can blame him?) wisely provided for himself. He came to England at once, and took possession of the property. There was no one to suspect him, and no one to say him nay. His father and mother had always lived as man and wife—none of the few people who were acquainted with them ever supposed them to be anything else. The right person to claim the property (if the truth had been known) was a distant relation, who had no idea of ever getting it, and who was away at sea when his father died. He had no difficulty so far—he took possession, as a matter of course. But he could not borrow money on the property as a matter of course. There were two things wanted of him before he could do this. One was a certificate of his birth, and the other was a certificate of his parents’ marriage. The certificate of his birth was easily got—he was born abroad, and the certificate was there in due form. The other matter was a difficulty, and that difficulty brought him to Old Welmingham.\n\nBut for one consideration he might have gone to Knowlesbury instead.\n\nHis mother had been living there just before she met with his father—living under her maiden name, the truth being that she was really a married woman, married in Ireland, where her husband had ill-used her, and had afterwards gone off with some other person. I give you this fact on good authority—Sir Felix mentioned it to his son as the reason why he had not married. You may wonder why the son, knowing that his parents had met each other at Knowlesbury, did not play his first tricks with the register of that church, where it might have been fairly presumed his father and mother were married. The reason was that the clergyman who did duty at Knowlesbury church, in the year eighteen hundred and three (when, according to his birth certificate, his father and mother OUGHT to have been married), was alive still when he took possession of the property in the New Year of eighteen hundred and twenty-seven. This awkward circumstance forced him to extend his inquiries to our neighbourhood. There no such danger existed, the former clergyman at our church having been dead for some years.\n\nOld Welmingham suited his purpose as well as Knowlesbury. His father had removed his mother from Knowlesbury, and had lived with her at a cottage on the river, a little distance from our village. People who had known his solitary ways when he was single did not wonder at his solitary ways when he was supposed to be married. If he had not been a hideous creature to look at, his retired life with the lady might have raised suspicions; but, as things were, his hiding his ugliness and his deformity in the strictest privacy surprised nobody. He lived in our neighbourhood till he came in possession of the Park. After three or four and twenty years had passed, who was to say (the clergyman being dead) that his marriage had not been as private as the rest of his life, and that it had not taken place at Old Welmingham church?\n\nSo, as I told you, the son found our neighbourhood the surest place he could choose to set things right secretly in his own interests. It may surprise you to hear that what he really did to the marriage register was done on the spur of the moment—done on second thoughts.\n\nHis first notion was only to tear the leaf out (in the right year and month), to destroy it privately, to go back to London, and to tell the lawyers to get him the necessary certificate of his father’s marriage, innocently referring them of course to the date on the leaf that was gone. Nobody could say his father and mother had NOT been married after that, and whether, under the circumstances, they would stretch a point or not about lending him the money (he thought they would), he had his answer ready at all events, if a question was ever raised about his right to the name and the estate.\n\nBut when he came to look privately at the register for himself, he found at the bottom of one of the pages for the year eighteen hundred and three a blank space left, seemingly through there being no room to make a long entry there, which was made instead at the top of the next page. The sight of this chance altered all his plans. It was an opportunity he had never hoped for, or thought of—and he took it—you know how. The blank space, to have exactly tallied with his birth certificate, ought to have occurred in the July part of the register. It occurred in the September part instead. However, in this case, if suspicious questions were asked, the answer was not hard to find. He had only to describe himself as a seven months’ child.\n\nI was fool enough, when he told me his story, to feel some interest and some pity for him—which was just what he calculated on, as you will see. I thought him hardly used. It was not his fault that his father and mother were not married, and it was not his father’s and mother’s fault either. A more scrupulous woman than I was—a woman who had not set her heart on a gold watch and chain—would have found some excuses for him. At all events, I held my tongue, and helped to screen what he was about.\n\nHe was some time getting the ink the right colour (mixing it over and over again in pots and bottles of mine), and some time afterwards in practising the handwriting. But he succeeded in the end, and made an honest woman of his mother after she was dead in her grave! So far, I don’t deny that he behaved honourably enough to myself. He gave me my watch and chain, and spared no expense in buying them; both were of superior workmanship, and very expensive. I have got them still—the watch goes beautifully.\n\nYou said the other day that Mrs. Clements had told you everything she knew. In that case there is no need for me to write about the trumpery scandal by which I was the sufferer—the innocent sufferer, I positively assert. You must know as well as I do what the notion was which my husband took into his head when he found me and my fine-gentleman acquaintance meeting each other privately and talking secrets together. But what you don’t know is how it ended between that same gentleman and myself. You shall read and see how he behaved to me.\n\nThe first words I said to him, when I saw the turn things had taken, were, “Do me justice—clear my character of a stain on it which you know I don’t deserve. I don’t want you to make a clean breast of it to my husband—only tell him, on your word of honour as a gentleman, that he is wrong, and that I am not to blame in the way he thinks I am. Do me that justice, at least, after all I have done for you.” He flatly refused, in so many words. He told me plainly that it was his interest to let my husband and all my neighbours believe the falsehood—because, as long as they did so they were quite certain never to suspect the truth. I had a spirit of my own, and I told him they should know the truth from my lips. His reply was short, and to the point. If I spoke, I was a lost woman, as certainly as he was a lost man.\n\nYes! it had come to that. He had deceived me about the risk I ran in helping him. He had practised on my ignorance, he had tempted me with his gifts, he had interested me with his story—and the result of it was that he made me his accomplice. He owned this coolly, and he ended by telling me, for the first time, what the frightful punishment really was for his offence, and for any one who helped him to commit it. In those days the law was not so tender-hearted as I hear it is now. Murderers were not the only people liable to be hanged, and women convicts were not treated like ladies in undeserved distress. I confess he frightened me—the mean impostor! the cowardly blackguard! Do you understand now how I hated him? Do you understand why I am taking all this trouble—thankfully taking it—to gratify the curiosity of the meritorious young gentleman who hunted him down?\n\nWell, to go on. He was hardly fool enough to drive me to downright desperation. I was not the sort of woman whom it was quite safe to hunt into a corner—he knew that, and wisely quieted me with proposals for the future.\n\nI deserved some reward (he was kind enough to say) for the service I had done him, and some compensation (he was so obliging as to add) for what I had suffered. He was quite willing—generous scoundrel!—to make me a handsome yearly allowance, payable quarterly, on two conditions. First, I was to hold my tongue—in my own interests as well as in his. Secondly, I was not to stir away from Welmingham without first letting him know, and waiting till I had obtained his permission. In my own neighbourhood, no virtuous female friends would tempt me into dangerous gossiping at the tea-table. In my own neighbourhood, he would always know where to find me. A hard condition, that second one—but I accepted it.\n\nWhat else was I to do? I was left helpless, with the prospect of a coming incumbrance in the shape of a child. What else was I to do? Cast myself on the mercy of my runaway idiot of a husband who had raised the scandal against me? I would have died first. Besides, the allowance WAS a handsome one. I had a better income, a better house over my head, better carpets on my floors, than half the women who turned up the whites of their eyes at the sight of me. The dress of Virtue, in our parts, was cotton print. I had silk.\n\nSo I accepted the conditions he offered me, and made the best of them, and fought my battle with my respectable neighbours on their own ground, and won it in course of time—as you saw yourself. How I kept his Secret (and mine) through all the years that have passed from that time to this, and whether my late daughter, Anne, ever really crept into my confidence, and got the keeping of the Secret too—are questions, I dare say, to which you are curious to find an answer. Well! my gratitude refuses you nothing. I will turn to a fresh page and give you the answer immediately. But you must excuse one thing—you must excuse my beginning, Mr. Hartright, with an expression of surprise at the interest which you appear to have felt in my late daughter. It is quite unaccountable to me. If that interest makes you anxious for any particulars of her early life, I must refer you to Mrs. Clements, who knows more of the subject than I do. Pray understand that I do not profess to have been at all overfond of my late daughter. She was a worry to me from first to last, with the additional disadvantage of being always weak in the head. You like candour, and I hope this satisfies you.\n\nThere is no need to trouble you with many personal particulars relating to those past times. It will be enough to say that I observed the terms of the bargain on my side, and that I enjoyed my comfortable income in return, paid quarterly.\n\nNow and then I got away and changed the scene for a short time, always asking leave of my lord and master first, and generally getting it. He was not, as I have already told you, fool enough to drive me too hard, and he could reasonably rely on my holding my tongue for my own sake, if not for his. One of my longest trips away from home was the trip I took to Limmeridge to nurse a half-sister there, who was dying. She was reported to have saved money, and I thought it as well (in case any accident happened to stop my allowance) to look after my own interests in that direction. As things turned out, however, my pains were all thrown away, and I got nothing, because nothing was to be had.\n\nI had taken Anne to the north with me, having my whims and fancies, occasionally, about my child, and getting, at such times, jealous of Mrs. Clements’ influence over her. I never liked Mrs. Clements. She was a poor, empty-headed, spiritless woman—what you call a born drudge—and I was now and then not averse to plaguing her by taking Anne away. Not knowing what else to do with my girl while I was nursing in Cumberland, I put her to school at Limmeridge. The lady of the manor, Mrs. Fairlie (a remarkably plain-looking woman, who had entrapped one of the handsomest men in England into marrying her), amused me wonderfully by taking a violent fancy to my girl. The consequence was, she learnt nothing at school, and was petted and spoilt at Limmeridge House. Among other whims and fancies which they taught her there, they put some nonsense into her head about always wearing white. Hating white and liking colours myself, I determined to take the nonsense out of her head as soon as we got home again.\n\nStrange to say, my daughter resolutely resisted me. When she HAD got a notion once fixed in her mind she was, like other half-witted people, as obstinate as a mule in keeping it. We quarrelled finely, and Mrs. Clements, not liking to see it, I suppose, offered to take Anne away to live in London with her. I should have said Yes, if Mrs. Clements had not sided with my daughter about her dressing herself in white. But being determined she should NOT dress herself in white, and disliking Mrs. Clements more than ever for taking part against me, I said No, and meant No, and stuck to No. The consequence was, my daughter remained with me, and the consequence of that, in its turn, was the first serious quarrel that happened about the Secret.\n\nThe circumstance took place long after the time I have just been writing of. I had been settled for years in the new town, and was steadily living down my bad character and slowly gaining ground among the respectable inhabitants. It helped me forward greatly towards this object to have my daughter with me. Her harmlessness and her fancy for dressing in white excited a certain amount of sympathy. I left off opposing her favourite whim on that account, because some of the sympathy was sure, in course of time, to fall to my share. Some of it did fall. I date my getting a choice of the two best sittings to let in the church from that time, and I date the clergyman’s first bow from my getting the sittings.\n\nWell, being settled in this way, I received a letter one morning from that highly born gentleman (now deceased) in answer to one of mine, warning him, according to agreement, of my wishing to leave the town for a little change of air and scene.\n\nThe ruffianly side of him must have been uppermost, I suppose, when he got my letter, for he wrote back, refusing me in such abominably insolent language, that I lost all command over myself, and abused him, in my daughter’s presence, as “a low impostor whom I could ruin for life if I chose to open my lips and let out his Secret.” I said no more about him than that, being brought to my senses as soon as those words had escaped me by the sight of my daughter’s face looking eagerly and curiously at mine. I instantly ordered her out of the room until I had composed myself again.\n\nMy sensations were not pleasant, I can tell you, when I came to reflect on my own folly. Anne had been more than usually crazy and queer that year, and when I thought of the chance there might be of her repeating my words in the town, and mentioning HIS name in connection with them, if inquisitive people got hold of her, I was finely terrified at the possible consequences. My worst fears for myself, my worst dread of what he might do, led me no farther than this. I was quite unprepared for what really did happen only the next day.\n\nOn that next day, without any warning to me to expect him, he came to the house.\n\nHis first words, and the tone in which he spoke them, surly as it was, showed me plainly enough that he had repented already of his insolent answer to my application, and that he had come in a mighty bad temper to try and set matters right again before it was too late. Seeing my daughter in the room with me (I had been afraid to let her out of my sight after what had happened the day before) he ordered her away. They neither of them liked each other, and he vented the ill-temper on HER which he was afraid to show to ME.\n\n“Leave us,” he said, looking at her over his shoulder. She looked back over HER shoulder and waited as if she didn’t care to go. “Do you hear?” he roared out, “leave the room.” “Speak to me civilly,” says she, getting red in the face. “Turn the idiot out,” says he, looking my way. She had always had crazy notions of her own about her dignity, and that word “idiot” upset her in a moment. Before I could interfere she stepped up to him in a fine passion. “Beg my pardon, directly,” says she, “or I’ll make it the worse for you. I’ll let out your Secret. I can ruin you for life if I choose to open my lips.” My own words!—repeated exactly from what I had said the day before—repeated, in his presence, as if they had come from herself. He sat speechless, as white as the paper I am writing on, while I pushed her out of the room. When he recovered himself——\n\nNo! I am too respectable a woman to mention what he said when he recovered himself. My pen is the pen of a member of the rector’s congregation, and a subscriber to the “Wednesday Lectures on Justification by Faith”—how can you expect me to employ it in writing bad language? Suppose, for yourself, the raging, swearing frenzy of the lowest ruffian in England, and let us get on together, as fast as may be, to the way in which it all ended.\n\nIt ended, as you probably guess by this time, in his insisting on securing his own safety by shutting her up.\n\nI tried to set things right. I told him that she had merely repeated, like a parrot, the words she had heard me say and that she knew no particulars whatever, because I had mentioned none. I explained that she had affected, out of crazy spite against him, to know what she really did NOT know—that she only wanted to threaten him and aggravate him for speaking to her as he had just spoken—and that my unlucky words gave her just the chance of doing mischief of which she was in search. I referred him to other queer ways of hers, and to his own experience of the vagaries of half-witted people—it was all to no purpose—he would not believe me on my oath—he was absolutely certain I had betrayed the whole Secret. In short, he would hear of nothing but shutting her up.\n\nUnder these circumstances, I did my duty as a mother. “No pauper Asylum,” I said, “I won’t have her put in a pauper Asylum. A Private Establishment, if you please. I have my feelings as a mother, and my character to preserve in the town, and I will submit to nothing but a Private Establishment, of the sort which my genteel neighbours would choose for afflicted relatives of their own.” Those were my words. It is gratifying to me to reflect that I did my duty. Though never overfond of my late daughter, I had a proper pride about her. No pauper stain—thanks to my firmness and resolution—ever rested on MY child.\n\nHaving carried my point (which I did the more easily, in consequence of the facilities offered by private Asylums), I could not refuse to admit that there were certain advantages gained by shutting her up. In the first place, she was taken excellent care of—being treated (as I took care to mention in the town) on the footing of a lady. In the second place, she was kept away from Welmingham, where she might have set people suspecting and inquiring, by repeating my own incautious words.\n\nThe only drawback of putting her under restraint was a very slight one. We merely turned her empty boast about knowing the Secret into a fixed delusion. Having first spoken in sheer crazy spitefulness against the man who had offended her, she was cunning enough to see that she had seriously frightened him, and sharp enough afterwards to discover that HE was concerned in shutting her up. The consequence was she flamed out into a perfect frenzy of passion against him, going to the Asylum, and the first words she said to the nurses, after they had quieted her, were, that she was put in confinement for knowing his Secret, and that she meant to open her lips and ruin him, when the right time came.\n\nShe may have said the same thing to you, when you thoughtlessly assisted her escape. She certainly said it (as I heard last summer) to the unfortunate woman who married our sweet-tempered, nameless gentleman lately deceased. If either you, or that unlucky lady, had questioned my daughter closely, and had insisted on her explaining what she really meant, you would have found her lose all her self-importance suddenly, and get vacant, and restless, and confused—you would have discovered that I am writing nothing here but the plain truth. She knew that there was a Secret—she knew who was connected with it—she knew who would suffer by its being known—and beyond that, whatever airs of importance she may have given herself, whatever crazy boasting she may have indulged in with strangers, she never to her dying day knew more.\n\nHave I satisfied your curiosity? I have taken pains enough to satisfy it at any rate. There is really nothing else I have to tell you about myself or my daughter. My worst responsibilities, so far as she was concerned, were all over when she was secured in the Asylum. I had a form of letter relating to the circumstances under which she was shut up, given me to write, in answer to one Miss Halcombe, who was curious in the matter, and who must have heard plenty of lies about me from a certain tongue well accustomed to the telling of the same. And I did what I could afterwards to trace my runaway daughter, and prevent her from doing mischief by making inquiries myself in the neighbourhood where she was falsely reported to have been seen. But these, and other trifles like them, are of little or no interest to you after what you have heard already.\n\nSo far, I have written in the friendliest possible spirit. But I cannot close this letter without adding a word here of serious remonstrance and reproof, addressed to yourself.\n\nIn the course of your personal interview with me, you audaciously referred to my late daughter’s parentage on the father’s side, as if that parentage was a matter of doubt. This was highly improper and very ungentlemanlike on your part! If we see each other again, remember, if you please, that I will allow no liberties to be taken with my reputation, and that the moral atmosphere of Welmingham (to use a favourite expression of my friend the rector’s) must not be tainted by loose conversation of any kind. If you allow yourself to doubt that my husband was Anne’s father, you personally insult me in the grossest manner. If you have felt, and if you still continue to feel, an unhallowed curiosity on this subject, I recommend you, in your own interests, to check it at once, and for ever. On this side of the grave, Mr. Hartright, whatever may happen on the other, THAT curiosity will never be gratified.\n\nPerhaps, after what I have just said, you will see the necessity of writing me an apology. Do so, and I will willingly receive it. I will, afterwards, if your wishes point to a second interview with me, go a step farther, and receive you. My circumstances only enable me to invite you to tea—not that they are at all altered for the worse by what has happened. I have always lived, as I think I told you, well within my income, and I have saved enough, in the last twenty years, to make me quite comfortable for the rest of my life. It is not my intention to leave Welmingham. There are one or two little advantages which I have still to gain in the town. The clergyman bows to me—as you saw. He is married, and his wife is not quite so civil. I propose to join the Dorcas Society, and I mean to make the clergyman’s wife bow to me next.\n\nIf you favour me with your company, pray understand that the conversation must be entirely on general subjects. Any attempted reference to this letter will be quite useless—I am determined not to acknowledge having written it. The evidence has been destroyed in the fire, I know, but I think it desirable to err on the side of caution, nevertheless.\n\nOn this account no names are mentioned here, nor is any signature attached to these lines: the handwriting is disguised throughout, and I mean to deliver the letter myself, under circumstances which will prevent all fear of its being traced to my house. You can have no possible cause to complain of these precautions, seeing that they do not affect the information I here communicate, in consideration of the special indulgence which you have deserved at my hands. My hour for tea is half-past five, and my buttered toast waits for nobody.\n\n# I\n\nMy first impulse, after reading Mrs. Catherick’s extraordinary narrative, was to destroy it. The hardened shameless depravity of the whole composition, from beginning to end—the atrocious perversity of mind which persistently associated me with a calamity for which I was in no sense answerable, and with a death which I had risked my life in trying to avert—so disgusted me, that I was on the point of tearing the letter, when a consideration suggested itself which warned me to wait a little before I destroyed it.\n\nThis consideration was entirely unconnected with Sir Percival. The information communicated to me, so far as it concerned him, did little more than confirm the conclusions at which I had already arrived.\n\nHe had committed his offence, as I had supposed him to have committed it, and the absence of all reference, on Mrs. Catherick’s part, to the duplicate register at Knowlesbury, strengthened my previous conviction that the existence of the book, and the risk of detection which it implied, must have been necessarily unknown to Sir Percival. My interest in the question of the forgery was now at an end, and my only object in keeping the letter was to make it of some future service in clearing up the last mystery that still remained to baffle me—the parentage of Anne Catherick on the father’s side. There were one or two sentences dropped in her mother’s narrative, which it might be useful to refer to again, when matters of more immediate importance allowed me leisure to search for the missing evidence. I did not despair of still finding that evidence, and I had lost none of my anxiety to discover it, for I had lost none of my interest in tracing the father of the poor creature who now lay at rest in Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.\n\nAccordingly, I sealed up the letter and put it away carefully in my pocket-book, to be referred to again when the time came.\n\nThe next day was my last in Hampshire. When I had appeared again before the magistrate at Knowlesbury, and when I had attended at the adjourned inquest, I should be free to return to London by the afternoon or the evening train.\n\nMy first errand in the morning was, as usual, to the post-office. The letter from Marian was there, but I thought when it was handed to me that it felt unusually light. I anxiously opened the envelope. There was nothing inside but a small strip of paper folded in two. The few blotted hurriedly-written lines which were traced on it contained these words:\n\n“Come back as soon as you can. I have been obliged to move. Come to Gower’s Walk, Fulham (number five). I will be on the look-out for you. Don’t be alarmed about us, we are both safe and well. But come back.—Marian.”\n\nThe news which those lines contained—news which I instantly associated with some attempted treachery on the part of Count Fosco—fairly overwhelmed me. I stood breathless with the paper crumpled up in my hand. What had happened? What subtle wickedness had the Count planned and executed in my absence? A night had passed since Marian’s note was written—hours must elapse still before I could get back to them—some new disaster might have happened already of which I was ignorant. And here, miles and miles away from them, here I must remain—held, doubly held, at the disposal of the law!\n\nI hardly know to what forgetfulness of my obligations anxiety and alarm might not have tempted me, but for the quieting influence of my faith in Marian. My absolute reliance on her was the one earthly consideration which helped me to restrain myself, and gave me courage to wait. The inquest was the first of the impediments in the way of my freedom of action. I attended it at the appointed time, the legal formalities requiring my presence in the room, but as it turned out, not calling on me to repeat my evidence. This useless delay was a hard trial, although I did my best to quiet my impatience by following the course of the proceedings as closely as I could.\n\nThe London solicitor of the deceased (Mr. Merriman) was among the persons present. But he was quite unable to assist the objects of the inquiry. He could only say that he was inexpressibly shocked and astonished, and that he could throw no light whatever on the mysterious circumstances of the case. At intervals during the adjourned investigation, he suggested questions which the Coroner put, but which led to no results. After a patient inquiry, which lasted nearly three hours, and which exhausted every available source of information, the jury pronounced the customary verdict in cases of sudden death by accident. They added to the formal decision a statement, that there had been no evidence to show how the keys had been abstracted, how the fire had been caused, or what the purpose was for which the deceased had entered the vestry. This act closed the proceedings. The legal representative of the dead man was left to provide for the necessities of the interment, and the witnesses were free to retire.\n\nResolved not to lose a minute in getting to Knowlesbury, I paid my bill at the hotel, and hired a fly to take me to the town. A gentleman who heard me give the order, and who saw that I was going alone, informed me that he lived in the neighbourhood of Knowlesbury, and asked if I would have any objection to his getting home by sharing the fly with me. I accepted his proposal as a matter of course.\n\nOur conversation during the drive was naturally occupied by the one absorbing subject of local interest.\n\nMy new acquaintance had some knowledge of the late Sir Percival’s solicitor, and he and Mr. Merriman had been discussing the state of the deceased gentleman’s affairs and the succession to the property. Sir Percival’s embarrassments were so well known all over the county that his solicitor could only make a virtue of necessity and plainly acknowledge them. He had died without leaving a will, and he had no personal property to bequeath, even if he had made one, the whole fortune which he had derived from his wife having been swallowed up by his creditors. The heir to the estate (Sir Percival having left no issue) was a son of Sir Felix Glyde’s first cousin, an officer in command of an East Indiaman. He would find his unexpected inheritance sadly encumbered, but the property would recover with time, and, if “the captain” was careful, he might be a rich man yet before he died.\n\nAbsorbed as I was in the one idea of getting to London, this information (which events proved to be perfectly correct) had an interest of its own to attract my attention. I thought it justified me in keeping secret my discovery of Sir Percival’s fraud. The heir, whose rights he had usurped, was the heir who would now have the estate. The income from it, for the last three-and-twenty years, which should properly have been his, and which the dead man had squandered to the last farthing, was gone beyond recall. If I spoke, my speaking would confer advantage on no one. If I kept the secret, my silence concealed the character of the man who had cheated Laura into marrying him. For her sake, I wished to conceal it—for her sake, still, I tell this story under feigned names.\n\nI parted with my chance companion at Knowlesbury, and went at once to the town-hall. As I had anticipated, no one was present to prosecute the case against me—the necessary formalities were observed, and I was discharged. On leaving the court a letter from Mr. Dawson was put into my hand. It informed me that he was absent on professional duty, and it reiterated the offer I had already received from him of any assistance which I might require at his hands. I wrote back, warmly acknowledging my obligations to his kindness, and apologising for not expressing my thanks personally, in consequence of my immediate recall on pressing business to town.\n\nHalf an hour later I was speeding back to London by the express train.\n\n# II\n\nIt was between nine and ten o’clock before I reached Fulham, and found my way to Gower’s Walk.\n\nBoth Laura and Marian came to the door to let me in. I think we had hardly known how close the tie was which bound us three together, until the evening came which united us again. We met as if we had been parted for months instead of for a few days only. Marian’s face was sadly worn and anxious. I saw who had known all the danger and borne all the trouble in my absence the moment I looked at her. Laura’s brighter looks and better spirits told me how carefully she had been spared all knowledge of the dreadful death at Welmingham, and of the true reason of our change of abode.\n\nThe stir of the removal seemed to have cheered and interested her. She only spoke of it as a happy thought of Marian’s to surprise me on my return with a change from the close, noisy street to the pleasant neighbourhood of trees and fields and the river. She was full of projects for the future—of the drawings she was to finish—of the purchasers I had found in the country who were to buy them—of the shillings and sixpences she had saved, till her purse was so heavy that she proudly asked me to weigh it in my own hand. The change for the better which had been wrought in her during the few days of my absence was a surprise to me for which I was quite unprepared—and for all the unspeakable happiness of seeing it, I was indebted to Marian’s courage and to Marian’s love.\n\nWhen Laura had left us, and when we could speak to one another without restraint, I tried to give some expression to the gratitude and the admiration which filled my heart. But the generous creature would not wait to hear me. That sublime self-forgetfulness of women, which yields so much and asks so little, turned all her thoughts from herself to me.\n\n“I had only a moment left before post-time,” she said, “or I should have written less abruptly. You look worn and weary, Walter. I am afraid my letter must have seriously alarmed you?”\n\n“Only at first,” I replied. “My mind was quieted, Marian, by my trust in you. Was I right in attributing this sudden change of place to some threatened annoyance on the part of Count Fosco?”\n\n“Perfectly right,” she said. “I saw him yesterday, and worse than that, Walter—I spoke to him.”\n\n“Spoke to him? Did he know where we lived? Did he come to the house?”\n\n“He did. To the house—but not upstairs. Laura never saw him—Laura suspects nothing. I will tell you how it happened: the danger, I believe and hope, is over now. Yesterday, I was in the sitting-room, at our old lodgings. Laura was drawing at the table, and I was walking about and setting things to rights. I passed the window, and as I passed it, looked out into the street. There, on the opposite side of the way, I saw the Count, with a man talking to him——”\n\n“Did he notice you at the window?”\n\n“No—at least, I thought not. I was too violently startled to be quite sure.”\n\n“Who was the other man? A stranger?”\n\n“Not a stranger, Walter. As soon as I could draw my breath again, I recognised him. He was the owner of the Lunatic Asylum.”\n\n“Was the Count pointing out the house to him?”\n\n“No, they were talking together as if they had accidentally met in the street. I remained at the window looking at them from behind the curtain. If I had turned round, and if Laura had seen my face at that moment——Thank God, she was absorbed over her drawing! They soon parted. The man from the Asylum went one way, and the Count the other. I began to hope they were in the street by chance, till I saw the Count come back, stop opposite to us again, take out his card-case and pencil, write something, and then cross the road to the shop below us. I ran past Laura before she could see me, and said I had forgotten something upstairs. As soon as I was out of the room I went down to the first landing and waited—I was determined to stop him if he tried to come upstairs. He made no such attempt. The girl from the shop came through the door into the passage, with his card in her hand—a large gilt card with his name, and a coronet above it, and these lines underneath in pencil: ‘Dear lady’ (yes! the villain could address me in that way still)—‘dear lady, one word, I implore you, on a matter serious to us both.’ If one can think at all, in serious difficulties, one thinks quick. I felt directly that it might be a fatal mistake to leave myself and to leave you in the dark, where such a man as the Count was concerned. I felt that the doubt of what he might do, in your absence, would be ten times more trying to me if I declined to see him than if I consented. ‘Ask the gentleman to wait in the shop,’ I said. ‘I will be with him in a moment.’ I ran upstairs for my bonnet, being determined not to let him speak to me indoors. I knew his deep ringing voice, and I was afraid Laura might hear it, even in the shop. In less than a minute I was down again in the passage, and had opened the door into the street. He came round to meet me from the shop. There he was in deep mourning, with his smooth bow and his deadly smile, and some idle boys and women near him, staring at his great size, his fine black clothes, and his large cane with the gold knob to it. All the horrible time at Blackwater came back to me the moment I set eyes on him. All the old loathing crept and crawled through me, when he took off his hat with a flourish and spoke to me, as if we had parted on the friendliest terms hardly a day since.”\n\n“You remember what he said?”\n\n“I can’t repeat it, Walter. You shall know directly what he said about you—-but I can’t repeat what he said to me. It was worse than the polite insolence of his letter. My hands tingled to strike him, as if I had been a man! I only kept them quiet by tearing his card to pieces under my shawl. Without saying a word on my side, I walked away from the house (for fear of Laura seeing us), and he followed, protesting softly all the way. In the first by-street I turned, and asked him what he wanted with me. He wanted two things. First, if I had no objection, to express his sentiments. I declined to hear them. Secondly, to repeat the warning in his letter. I asked, what occasion there was for repeating it. He bowed and smiled, and said he would explain. The explanation exactly confirmed the fears I expressed before you left us. I told you, if you remember, that Sir Percival would be too headstrong to take his friend’s advice where you were concerned, and that there was no danger to be dreaded from the Count till his own interests were threatened, and he was roused into acting for himself?”\n\n“I recollect, Marian.”\n\n“Well, so it has really turned out. The Count offered his advice, but it was refused. Sir Percival would only take counsel of his own violence, his own obstinacy, and his own hatred of you. The Count let him have his way, first privately ascertaining, in case of his own interests being threatened next, where we lived. You were followed, Walter, on returning here, after your first journey to Hampshire, by the lawyer’s men for some distance from the railway, and by the Count himself to the door of the house. How he contrived to escape being seen by you he did not tell me, but he found us out on that occasion, and in that way. Having made the discovery, he took no advantage of it till the news reached him of Sir Percival’s death, and then, as I told you, he acted for himself, because he believed you would next proceed against the dead man’s partner in the conspiracy. He at once made his arrangements to meet the owner of the Asylum in London, and to take him to the place where his runaway patient was hidden, believing that the results, whichever way they ended, would be to involve you in interminable legal disputes and difficulties, and to tie your hands for all purposes of offence, so far as he was concerned. That was his purpose, on his own confession to me. The only consideration which made him hesitate, at the last moment——”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“It is hard to acknowledge it, Walter, and yet I must. I was the only consideration. No words can say how degraded I feel in my own estimation when I think of it, but the one weak point in that man’s iron character is the horrible admiration he feels for me. I have tried, for the sake of my own self-respect, to disbelieve it as long as I could; but his looks, his actions, force on me the shameful conviction of the truth. The eyes of that monster of wickedness moistened while he was speaking to me—they did, Walter! He declared that at the moment of pointing out the house to the doctor, he thought of my misery if I was separated from Laura, of my responsibility if I was called on to answer for effecting her escape, and he risked the worst that you could do to him, the second time, for my sake. All he asked was that I would remember the sacrifice, and restrain your rashness, in my own interests—interests which he might never be able to consult again. I made no such bargain with him—I would have died first. But believe him or not, whether it is true or false that he sent the doctor away with an excuse, one thing is certain, I saw the man leave him without so much as a glance at our window, or even at our side of the way.”\n\n“I believe it, Marian. The best men are not consistent in good—why should the worst men be consistent in evil? At the same time, I suspect him of merely attempting to frighten you, by threatening what he cannot really do. I doubt his power of annoying us, by means of the owner of the Asylum, now that Sir Percival is dead, and Mrs. Catherick is free from all control. But let me hear more. What did the Count say of me?”\n\n“He spoke last of you. His eyes brightened and hardened, and his manner changed to what I remember it in past times—to that mixture of pitiless resolution and mountebank mockery which makes it so impossible to fathom him. ‘Warn Mr. Hartright!’ he said in his loftiest manner. ‘He has a man of brains to deal with, a man who snaps his big fingers at the laws and conventions of society, when he measures himself with ME. If my lamented friend had taken my advice, the business of the inquest would have been with the body of Mr. Hartright. But my lamented friend was obstinate. See! I mourn his loss—inwardly in my soul, outwardly on my hat. This trivial crape expresses sensibilities which I summon Mr. Hartright to respect. They may be transformed to immeasurable enmities if he ventures to disturb them. Let him be content with what he has got—with what I leave unmolested, for your sake, to him and to you. Say to him (with my compliments), if he stirs me, he has Fosco to deal with. In the English of the Popular Tongue, I inform him—Fosco sticks at nothing. Dear lady, good morning.’ His cold grey eyes settled on my face—he took off his hat solemnly—bowed, bare-headed—and left me.”\n\n“Without returning? without saying more last words?”\n\n“He turned at the corner of the street, and waved his hand, and then struck it theatrically on his breast. I lost sight of him after that. He disappeared in the opposite direction to our house, and I ran back to Laura. Before I was indoors again, I had made up my mind that we must go. The house (especially in your absence) was a place of danger instead of a place of safety, now that the Count had discovered it. If I could have felt certain of your return, I should have risked waiting till you came back. But I was certain of nothing, and I acted at once on my own impulse. You had spoken, before leaving us, of moving into a quieter neighbourhood and purer air, for the sake of Laura’s health. I had only to remind her of that, and to suggest surprising you and saving you trouble by managing the move in your absence, to make her quite as anxious for the change as I was. She helped me to pack up your things, and she has arranged them all for you in your new working-room here.”\n\n“What made you think of coming to this place?”\n\n“My ignorance of other localities in the neighbourhood of London. I felt the necessity of getting as far away as possible from our old lodgings, and I knew something of Fulham, because I had once been at school there. I despatched a messenger with a note, on the chance that the school might still be in existence. It was in existence—the daughters of my old mistress were carrying it on for her, and they engaged this place from the instructions I had sent. It was just post-time when the messenger returned to me with the address of the house. We moved after dark—we came here quite unobserved. Have I done right, Walter? Have I justified your trust in me?”\n\nI answered her warmly and gratefully, as I really felt. But the anxious look still remained on her face while I was speaking, and the first question she asked, when I had done, related to Count Fosco.\n\nI saw that she was thinking of him now with a changed mind. No fresh outbreak of anger against him, no new appeal to me to hasten the day of reckoning escaped her. Her conviction that the man’s hateful admiration of herself was really sincere, seemed to have increased a hundredfold her distrust of his unfathomable cunning, her inborn dread of the wicked energy and vigilance of all his faculties. Her voice fell low, her manner was hesitating, her eyes searched into mine with an eager fear when she asked me what I thought of his message, and what I meant to do next after hearing it.\n\n“Not many weeks have passed, Marian,” I answered, “since my interview with Mr. Kyrle. When he and I parted, the last words I said to him about Laura were these: ‘Her uncle’s house shall open to receive her, in the presence of every soul who followed the false funeral to the grave; the lie that records her death shall be publicly erased from the tombstone by the authority of the head of the family, and the two men who have wronged her shall answer for their crime to ME, though the justice that sits in tribunals is powerless to pursue them.’ One of those men is beyond mortal reach. The other remains, and my resolution remains.”\n\nHer eyes lit up—her colour rose. She said nothing, but I saw all her sympathies gathering to mine in her face.\n\n“I don’t disguise from myself, or from you,” I went on, “that the prospect before us is more than doubtful. The risks we have run already are, it may be, trifles compared with the risks that threaten us in the future, but the venture shall be tried, Marian, for all that. I am not rash enough to measure myself against such a man as the Count before I am well prepared for him. I have learnt patience—I can wait my time. Let him believe that his message has produced its effect—let him know nothing of us, and hear nothing of us—let us give him full time to feel secure—his own boastful nature, unless I seriously mistake him, will hasten that result. This is one reason for waiting, but there is another more important still. My position, Marian, towards you and towards Laura ought to be a stronger one than it is now before I try our last chance.”\n\nShe leaned near to me, with a look of surprise.\n\n“How can it be stronger?” she asked.\n\n“I will tell you,” I replied, “when the time comes. It has not come yet—it may never come at all. I may be silent about it to Laura for ever—I must be silent now, even to YOU, till I see for myself that I can harmlessly and honourably speak. Let us leave that subject. There is another which has more pressing claims on our attention. You have kept Laura, mercifully kept her, in ignorance of her husband’s death——”\n\n“Oh, Walter, surely it must be long yet before we tell her of it?”\n\n“No, Marian. Better that you should reveal it to her now, than that accident, which no one can guard against, should reveal it to her at some future time. Spare her all the details—break it to her very tenderly, but tell her that he is dead.”\n\n“You have a reason, Walter, for wishing her to know of her husband’s death besides the reason you have just mentioned?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n“A reason connected with that subject which must not be mentioned between us yet?—which may never be mentioned to Laura at all?”\n\nShe dwelt on the last words meaningly. When I answered her in the affirmative, I dwelt on them too.\n\nHer face grew pale. For a while she looked at me with a sad, hesitating interest. An unaccustomed tenderness trembled in her dark eyes and softened her firm lips, as she glanced aside at the empty chair in which the dear companion of all our joys and sorrows had been sitting.\n\n“I think I understand,” she said. “I think I owe it to her and to you, Walter, to tell her of her husband’s death.”\n\nShe sighed, and held my hand fast for a moment—then dropped it abruptly, and left the room. On the next day Laura knew that his death had released her, and that the error and the calamity of her life lay buried in his tomb.\n\nHis name was mentioned among us no more. Thenceforward, we shrank from the slightest approach to the subject of his death, and in the same scrupulous manner, Marian and I avoided all further reference to that other subject, which, by her consent and mine, was not to be mentioned between us yet. It was not the less present in our minds—it was rather kept alive in them by the restraint which we had imposed on ourselves. We both watched Laura more anxiously than ever, sometimes waiting and hoping, sometimes waiting and fearing, till the time came.\n\nBy degrees we returned to our accustomed way of life. I resumed the daily work, which had been suspended during my absence in Hampshire. Our new lodgings cost us more than the smaller and less convenient rooms which we had left, and the claim thus implied on my increased exertions was strengthened by the doubtfulness of our future prospects. Emergencies might yet happen which would exhaust our little fund at the banker’s, and the work of my hands might be, ultimately, all we had to look to for support. More permanent and more lucrative employment than had yet been offered to me was a necessity of our position—a necessity for which I now diligently set myself to provide.\n\nIt must not be supposed that the interval of rest and seclusion of which I am now writing, entirely suspended, on my part, all pursuit of the one absorbing purpose with which my thoughts and actions are associated in these pages. That purpose was, for months and months yet, never to relax its claims on me. The slow ripening of it still left me a measure of precaution to take, an obligation of gratitude to perform, and a doubtful question to solve.\n\nThe measure of precaution related, necessarily, to the Count. It was of the last importance to ascertain, if possible, whether his plans committed him to remaining in England—or, in other words, to remaining within my reach. I contrived to set this doubt at rest by very simple means. His address in St. John’s Wood being known to me, I inquired in the neighbourhood, and having found out the agent who had the disposal of the furnished house in which he lived, I asked if number five, Forest Road, was likely to be let within a reasonable time. The reply was in the negative. I was informed that the foreign gentleman then residing in the house had renewed his term of occupation for another six months, and would remain in possession until the end of June in the following year. We were then at the beginning of December only. I left the agent with my mind relieved from all present fear of the Count’s escaping me.\n\nThe obligation I had to perform took me once more into the presence of Mrs. Clements. I had promised to return, and to confide to her those particulars relating to the death and burial of Anne Catherick which I had been obliged to withhold at our first interview. Changed as circumstances now were, there was no hindrance to my trusting the good woman with as much of the story of the conspiracy as it was necessary to tell. I had every reason that sympathy and friendly feeling could suggest to urge on me the speedy performance of my promise, and I did conscientiously and carefully perform it. There is no need to burden these pages with any statement of what passed at the interview. It will be more to the purpose to say, that the interview itself necessarily brought to my mind the one doubtful question still remaining to be solved—the question of Anne Catherick’s parentage on the father’s side.\n\nA multitude of small considerations in connection with this subject—trifling enough in themselves, but strikingly important when massed together—had latterly led my mind to a conclusion which I resolved to verify. I obtained Marian’s permission to write to Major Donthorne, of Varneck Hall (where Mrs. Catherick had lived in service for some years previous to her marriage), to ask him certain questions. I made the inquiries in Marian’s name, and described them as relating to matters of personal history in her family, which might explain and excuse my application. When I wrote the letter I had no certain knowledge that Major Donthorne was still alive—I despatched it on the chance that he might be living, and able and willing to reply.\n\nAfter a lapse of two days proof came, in the shape of a letter, that the Major was living, and that he was ready to help us.\n\nThe idea in my mind when I wrote to him, and the nature of my inquiries will be easily inferred from his reply. His letter answered my questions by communicating these important facts—\n\nIn the first place, “the late Sir Percival Glyde, of Blackwater Park,” had never set foot in Varneck Hall. The deceased gentleman was a total stranger to Major Donthorne, and to all his family.\n\nIn the second place, “the late Mr. Philip Fairlie, of Limmeridge House,” had been, in his younger days, the intimate friend and constant guest of Major Donthorne. Having refreshed his memory by looking back to old letters and other papers, the Major was in a position to say positively that Mr. Philip Fairlie was staying at Varneck Hall in the month of August, eighteen hundred and twenty-six, and that he remained there for the shooting during the month of September and part of October following. He then left, to the best of the Major’s belief, for Scotland, and did not return to Varneck Hall till after a lapse of time, when he reappeared in the character of a newly-married man.\n\nTaken by itself, this statement was, perhaps, of little positive value, but taken in connection with certain facts, every one of which either Marian or I knew to be true, it suggested one plain conclusion that was, to our minds, irresistible.\n\nKnowing, now, that Mr. Philip Fairlie had been at Varneck Hall in the autumn of eighteen hundred and twenty-six, and that Mrs. Catherick had been living there in service at the same time, we knew also—first, that Anne had been born in June, eighteen hundred and twenty-seven; secondly, that she had always presented an extraordinary personal resemblance to Laura; and, thirdly, that Laura herself was strikingly like her father. Mr. Philip Fairlie had been one of the notoriously handsome men of his time. In disposition entirely unlike his brother Frederick, he was the spoilt darling of society, especially of the women—an easy, light-hearted, impulsive, affectionate man—generous to a fault—constitutionally lax in his principles, and notoriously thoughtless of moral obligations where women were concerned. Such were the facts we knew—such was the character of the man. Surely the plain inference that follows needs no pointing out?\n\nRead by the new light which had now broken upon me, even Mrs. Catherick’s letter, in despite of herself, rendered its mite of assistance towards strengthening the conclusion at which I had arrived. She had described Mrs. Fairlie (in writing to me) as “plain-looking,” and as having “entrapped the handsomest man in England into marrying her.” Both assertions were gratuitously made, and both were false. Jealous dislike (which, in such a woman as Mrs. Catherick, would express itself in petty malice rather than not express itself at all) appeared to me to be the only assignable cause for the peculiar insolence of her reference to Mrs. Fairlie, under circumstances which did not necessitate any reference at all.\n\nThe mention here of Mrs. Fairlie’s name naturally suggests one other question. Did she ever suspect whose child the little girl brought to her at Limmeridge might be?\n\nMarian’s testimony was positive on this point. Mrs. Fairlie’s letter to her husband, which had been read to me in former days—the letter describing Anne’s resemblance to Laura, and acknowledging her affectionate interest in the little stranger—had been written, beyond all question, in perfect innocence of heart. It even seemed doubtful, on consideration, whether Mr. Philip Fairlie himself had been nearer than his wife to any suspicion of the truth. The disgracefully deceitful circumstances under which Mrs. Catherick had married, the purpose of concealment which the marriage was intended to answer, might well keep her silent for caution’s sake, perhaps for her own pride’s sake also, even assuming that she had the means, in his absence, of communicating with the father of her unborn child.\n\nAs this surmise floated through my mind, there rose on my memory the remembrance of the Scripture denunciation which we have all thought of in our time with wonder and with awe: “The sins of the fathers shall be visited on the children.” But for the fatal resemblance between the two daughters of one father, the conspiracy of which Anne had been the innocent instrument and Laura the innocent victim could never have been planned. With what unerring and terrible directness the long chain of circumstances led down from the thoughtless wrong committed by the father to the heartless injury inflicted on the child!\n\nThese thoughts came to me, and others with them, which drew my mind away to the little Cumberland churchyard where Anne Catherick now lay buried. I thought of the bygone days when I had met her by Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, and met her for the last time. I thought of her poor helpless hands beating on the tombstone, and her weary, yearning words, murmured to the dead remains of her protectress and her friend: “Oh, if I could die, and be hidden and at rest with YOU!” Little more than a year had passed since she breathed that wish; and how inscrutably, how awfully, it had been fulfilled! The words she had spoken to Laura by the shores of the lake, the very words had now come true. “Oh, if I could only be buried with your mother! If I could only wake at her side when the angel’s trumpet sounds and the graves give up their dead at the resurrection!” Through what mortal crime and horror, through what darkest windings of the way down to death—the lost creature had wandered in God’s leading to the last home that, living, she never hoped to reach! In that sacred rest I leave her—in that dread companionship let her remain undisturbed.\n\nSo the ghostly figure which has haunted these pages, as it haunted my life, goes down into the impenetrable gloom. Like a shadow she first came to me in the loneliness of the night. Like a shadow she passes away in the loneliness of the dead.\n\n# III\n\nFour months elapsed. April came—the month of spring—the month of change.\n\nThe course of time had flowed through the interval since the winter peacefully and happily in our new home. I had turned my long leisure to good account, had largely increased my sources of employment, and had placed our means of subsistence on surer grounds. Freed from the suspense and the anxiety which had tried her so sorely and hung over her so long, Marian’s spirits rallied, and her natural energy of character began to assert itself again, with something, if not all, of the freedom and the vigour of former times.\n\nMore pliable under change than her sister, Laura showed more plainly the progress made by the healing influences of her new life. The worn and wasted look which had prematurely aged her face was fast leaving it, and the expression which had been the first of its charms in past days was the first of its beauties that now returned. My closest observations of her detected but one serious result of the conspiracy which had once threatened her reason and her life. Her memory of events, from the period of her leaving Blackwater Park to the period of our meeting in the burial-ground of Limmeridge Church, was lost beyond all hope of recovery. At the slightest reference to that time she changed and trembled still, her words became confused, her memory wandered and lost itself as helplessly as ever. Here, and here only, the traces of the past lay deep—too deep to be effaced.\n\nIn all else she was now so far on the way to recovery that, on her best and brightest days, she sometimes looked and spoke like the Laura of old times. The happy change wrought its natural result in us both. From their long slumber, on her side and on mine, those imperishable memories of our past life in Cumberland now awoke, which were one and all alike, the memories of our love.\n\nGradually and insensibly our daily relations towards each other became constrained. The fond words which I had spoken to her so naturally, in the days of her sorrow and her suffering, faltered strangely on my lips. In the time when my dread of losing her was most present to my mind, I had always kissed her when she left me at night and when she met me in the morning. The kiss seemed now to have dropped between us—to be lost out of our lives. Our hands began to tremble again when they met. We hardly ever looked long at one another out of Marian’s presence. The talk often flagged between us when we were alone. When I touched her by accident I felt my heart beating fast, as it used to beat at Limmeridge House—I saw the lovely answering flush glowing again in her cheeks, as if we were back among the Cumberland Hills in our past characters of master and pupil once more. She had long intervals of silence and thoughtfulness, and denied she had been thinking when Marian asked her the question. I surprised myself one day neglecting my work to dream over the little water-colour portrait of her which I had taken in the summer-house where we first met—just as I used to neglect Mr. Fairlie’s drawings to dream over the same likeness when it was newly finished in the bygone time. Changed as all the circumstances now were, our position towards each other in the golden days of our first companionship seemed to be revived with the revival of our love. It was as if Time had drifted us back on the wreck of our early hopes to the old familiar shore!\n\nTo any other woman I could have spoken the decisive words which I still hesitated to speak to HER. The utter helplessness of her position—her friendless dependence on all the forbearing gentleness that I could show her—my fear of touching too soon some secret sensitiveness in her which my instinct as a man might not have been fine enough to discover—these considerations, and others like them, kept me self-distrustfully silent. And yet I knew that the restraint on both sides must be ended, that the relations in which we stood towards one another must be altered in some settled manner for the future, and that it rested with me, in the first instance, to recognise the necessity for a change.\n\nThe more I thought of our position, the harder the attempt to alter it appeared, while the domestic conditions on which we three had been living together since the winter remained undisturbed. I cannot account for the capricious state of mind in which this feeling originated, but the idea nevertheless possessed me that some previous change of place and circumstances, some sudden break in the quiet monotony of our lives, so managed as to vary the home aspect under which we had been accustomed to see each other, might prepare the way for me to speak, and might make it easier and less embarrassing for Laura and Marian to hear.\n\nWith this purpose in view, I said, one morning, that I thought we had all earned a little holiday and a change of scene. After some consideration, it was decided that we should go for a fortnight to the sea-side.\n\nOn the next day we left Fulham for a quiet town on the south coast. At that early season of the year we were the only visitors in the place. The cliffs, the beach, and the walks inland were all in the solitary condition which was most welcome to us. The air was mild—the prospects over hill and wood and down were beautifully varied by the shifting April light and shade, and the restless sea leapt under our windows, as if it felt, like the land, the glow and freshness of spring.\n\nI owed it to Marian to consult her before I spoke to Laura, and to be guided afterwards by her advice.\n\nOn the third day from our arrival I found a fit opportunity of speaking to her alone. The moment we looked at one another, her quick instinct detected the thought in my mind before I could give it expression. With her customary energy and directness she spoke at once, and spoke first.\n\n“You are thinking of that subject which was mentioned between us on the evening of your return from Hampshire,” she said. “I have been expecting you to allude to it for some time past. There must be a change in our little household, Walter, we cannot go on much longer as we are now. I see it as plainly as you do—as plainly as Laura sees it, though she says nothing. How strangely the old times in Cumberland seem to have come back! You and I are together again, and the one subject of interest between us is Laura once more. I could almost fancy that this room is the summer-house at Limmeridge, and that those waves beyond us are beating on our sea-shore.”\n\n“I was guided by your advice in those past days,” I said, “and now, Marian, with reliance tenfold greater I will be guided by it again.”\n\nShe answered by pressing my hand. I saw that she was deeply touched by my reference to the past. We sat together near the window, and while I spoke and she listened, we looked at the glory of the sunlight shining on the majesty of the sea.\n\n“Whatever comes of this confidence between us,” I said, “whether it ends happily or sorrowfully for ME, Laura’s interests will still be the interests of my life. When we leave this place, on whatever terms we leave it, my determination to wrest from Count Fosco the confession which I failed to obtain from his accomplice, goes back with me to London, as certainly as I go back myself. Neither you nor I can tell how that man may turn on me, if I bring him to bay; we only know, by his own words and actions, that he is capable of striking at me through Laura, without a moment’s hesitation, or a moment’s remorse. In our present position I have no claim on her which society sanctions, which the law allows, to strengthen me in resisting him, and in protecting HER. This places me at a serious disadvantage. If I am to fight our cause with the Count, strong in the consciousness of Laura’s safety, I must fight it for my Wife. Do you agree to that, Marian, so far?”\n\n“To every word of it,” she answered.\n\n“I will not plead out of my own heart,” I went on; “I will not appeal to the love which has survived all changes and all shocks—I will rest my only vindication of myself for thinking of her, and speaking of her as my wife, on what I have just said. If the chance of forcing a confession from the Count is, as I believe it to be, the last chance left of publicly establishing the fact of Laura’s existence, the least selfish reason that I can advance for our marriage is recognised by us both. But I may be wrong in my conviction—other means of achieving our purpose may be in our power, which are less uncertain and less dangerous. I have searched anxiously, in my own mind, for those means, and I have not found them. Have you?”\n\n“No. I have thought about it too, and thought in vain.”\n\n“In all likelihood,” I continued, “the same questions have occurred to you, in considering this difficult subject, which have occurred to me. Ought we to return with her to Limmeridge, now that she is like herself again, and trust to the recognition of her by the people of the village, or by the children at the school? Ought we to appeal to the practical test of her handwriting? Suppose we did so. Suppose the recognition of her obtained, and the identity of the handwriting established. Would success in both those cases do more than supply an excellent foundation for a trial in a court of law? Would the recognition and the handwriting prove her identity to Mr. Fairlie and take her back to Limmeridge House, against the evidence of her aunt, against the evidence of the medical certificate, against the fact of the funeral and the fact of the inscription on the tomb? No! We could only hope to succeed in throwing a serious doubt on the assertion of her death, a doubt which nothing short of a legal inquiry can settle. I will assume that we possess (what we have certainly not got) money enough to carry this inquiry on through all its stages. I will assume that Mr. Fairlie’s prejudices might be reasoned away—that the false testimony of the Count and his wife, and all the rest of the false testimony, might be confuted—that the recognition could not possibly be ascribed to a mistake between Laura and Anne Catherick, or the handwriting be declared by our enemies to be a clever fraud—all these are assumptions which, more or less, set plain probabilities at defiance; but let them pass—and let us ask ourselves what would be the first consequence or the first questions put to Laura herself on the subject of the conspiracy. We know only too well what the consequence would be, for we know that she has never recovered her memory of what happened to her in London. Examine her privately, or examine her publicly, she is utterly incapable of assisting the assertion of her own case. If you don’t see this, Marian, as plainly as I see it, we will go to Limmeridge and try the experiment to-morrow.”\n\n“I DO see it, Walter. Even if we had the means of paying all the law expenses, even if we succeeded in the end, the delays would be unendurable, the perpetual suspense, after what we have suffered already, would be heartbreaking. You are right about the hopelessness of going to Limmeridge. I wish I could feel sure that you are right also in determining to try that last chance with the Count. IS it a chance at all?”\n\n“Beyond a doubt, Yes. It is the chance of recovering the lost date of Laura’s journey to London. Without returning to the reasons I gave you some time since, I am still as firmly persuaded as ever that there is a discrepancy between the date of that journey and the date on the certificate of death. There lies the weak point of the whole conspiracy—it crumbles to pieces if we attack it in that way, and the means of attacking it are in possession of the Count. If I succeed in wresting them from him, the object of your life and mine is fulfilled. If I fail, the wrong that Laura has suffered will, in this world, never be redressed.”\n\n“Do you fear failure yourself, Walter?”\n\n“I dare not anticipate success, and for that very reason, Marian, I speak openly and plainly as I have spoken now. In my heart and my conscience I can say it, Laura’s hopes for the future are at their lowest ebb. I know that her fortune is gone—I know that the last chance of restoring her to her place in the world lies at the mercy of her worst enemy, of a man who is now absolutely unassailable, and who may remain unassailable to the end. With every worldly advantage gone from her, with all prospect of recovering her rank and station more than doubtful, with no clearer future before her than the future which her husband can provide, the poor drawing-master may harmlessly open his heart at last. In the days of her prosperity, Marian, I was only the teacher who guided her hand—I ask for it, in her adversity, as the hand of my wife!”\n\nMarian’s eyes met mine affectionately—I could say no more. My heart was full, my lips were trembling. In spite of myself I was in danger of appealing to her pity. I got up to leave the room. She rose at the same moment, laid her hand gently on my shoulder, and stopped me.\n\n“Walter!” she said, “I once parted you both, for your good and for hers. Wait here, my brother!—wait, my dearest, best friend, till Laura comes, and tells you what I have done now!”\n\nFor the first time since the farewell morning at Limmeridge she touched my forehead with her lips. A tear dropped on my face as she kissed me. She turned quickly, pointed to the chair from which I had risen, and left the room.\n\nI sat down alone at the window to wait through the crisis of my life. My mind in that breathless interval felt like a total blank. I was conscious of nothing but a painful intensity of all familiar perceptions. The sun grew blinding bright, the white sea birds chasing each other far beyond me seemed to be flitting before my face, the mellow murmur of the waves on the beach was like thunder in my ears.\n\nThe door opened, and Laura came in alone. So she had entered the breakfast-room at Limmeridge House on the morning when we parted. Slowly and falteringly, in sorrow and in hesitation, she had once approached me. Now she came with the haste of happiness in her feet, with the light of happiness radiant in her face. Of their own accord those dear arms clasped themselves round me, of their own accord the sweet lips came to meet mine. “My darling!” she whispered, “we may own we love each other now?” Her head nestled with a tender contentedness on my bosom. “Oh,” she said innocently, “I am so happy at last!”\n\nTen days later we were happier still. We were married.\n\n# IV\n\nThe course of this narrative, steadily flowing on, bears me away from the morning-time of our married life, and carries me forward to the end.\n\nIn a fortnight more we three were back in London, and the shadow was stealing over us of the struggle to come.\n\nMarian and I were careful to keep Laura in ignorance of the cause that had hurried us back—the necessity of making sure of the Count. It was now the beginning of May, and his term of occupation at the house in Forest Road expired in June. If he renewed it (and I had reasons, shortly to be mentioned, for anticipating that he would), I might be certain of his not escaping me. But if by any chance he disappointed my expectations and left the country, then I had no time to lose in arming myself to meet him as I best might.\n\nIn the first fulness of my new happiness, there had been moments when my resolution faltered—moments when I was tempted to be safely content, now that the dearest aspiration of my life was fulfilled in the possession of Laura’s love. For the first time I thought faint-heartedly of the greatness of the risk, of the adverse chances arrayed against me, of the fair promise of our new life, and of the peril in which I might place the happiness which we had so hardly earned. Yes! let me own it honestly. For a brief time I wandered, in the sweet guiding of love, far from the purpose to which I had been true under sterner discipline and in darker days. Innocently Laura had tempted me aside from the hard path—innocently she was destined to lead me back again.\n\nAt times, dreams of the terrible past still disconnectedly recalled to her, in the mystery of sleep, the events of which her waking memory had lost all trace. One night (barely two weeks after our marriage), when I was watching her at rest, I saw the tears come slowly through her closed eyelids, I heard the faint murmuring words escape her which told me that her spirit was back again on the fatal journey from Blackwater Park. That unconscious appeal, so touching and so awful in the sacredness of her sleep, ran through me like fire. The next day was the day we came back to London—the day when my resolution returned to me with tenfold strength.\n\nThe first necessity was to know something of the man. Thus far, the true story of his life was an impenetrable mystery to me.\n\nI began with such scanty sources of information as were at my own disposal. The important narrative written by Mr. Frederick Fairlie (which Marian had obtained by following the directions I had given to her in the winter) proved to be of no service to the special object with which I now looked at it. While reading it I reconsidered the disclosure revealed to me by Mrs. Clements of the series of deceptions which had brought Anne Catherick to London, and which had there devoted her to the interests of the conspiracy. Here, again, the Count had not openly committed himself—here, again, he was, to all practical purpose, out of my reach.\n\nI next returned to Marian’s journal at Blackwater Park. At my request she read to me again a passage which referred to her past curiosity about the Count, and to the few particulars which she had discovered relating to him.\n\nThe passage to which I allude occurs in that part of her journal which delineates his character and his personal appearance. She describes him as “not having crossed the frontiers of his native country for years past”—as “anxious to know if any Italian gentlemen were settled in the nearest town to Blackwater Park”—as “receiving letters with all sorts of odd stamps on them, and one with a large official-looking seal on it.” She is inclined to consider that his long absence from his native country may be accounted for by assuming that he is a political exile. But she is, on the other hand, unable to reconcile this idea with the reception of the letter from abroad bearing “the large official-looking seal”—letters from the Continent addressed to political exiles being usually the last to court attention from foreign post-offices in that way.\n\nThe considerations thus presented to me in the diary, joined to certain surmises of my own that grew out of them, suggested a conclusion which I wondered I had not arrived at before. I now said to myself—what Laura had once said to Marian at Blackwater Park, what Madame Fosco had overheard by listening at the door—the Count is a spy!\n\nLaura had applied the word to him at hazard, in natural anger at his proceedings towards herself. I applied it to him with the deliberate conviction that his vocation in life was the vocation of a spy. On this assumption, the reason for his extraordinary stay in England so long after the objects of the conspiracy had been gained, became, to my mind, quite intelligible.\n\nThe year of which I am now writing was the year of the famous Crystal Palace Exhibition in Hyde Park. Foreigners in unusually large numbers had arrived already, and were still arriving in England. Men were among us by hundreds whom the ceaseless distrustfulness of their governments had followed privately, by means of appointed agents, to our shores. My surmises did not for a moment class a man of the Count’s abilities and social position with the ordinary rank and file of foreign spies. I suspected him of holding a position of authority, of being entrusted by the government which he secretly served with the organisation and management of agents specially employed in this country, both men and women, and I believed Mrs. Rubelle, who had been so opportunely found to act as nurse at Blackwater Park, to be, in all probability, one of the number.\n\nAssuming that this idea of mine had a foundation in truth, the position of the Count might prove to be more assailable than I had hitherto ventured to hope. To whom could I apply to know something more of the man’s history and of the man himself than I knew now?\n\nIn this emergency it naturally occurred to my mind that a countryman of his own, on whom I could rely, might be the fittest person to help me. The first man whom I thought of under these circumstances was also the only Italian with whom I was intimately acquainted—my quaint little friend, Professor Pesca.\n\nThe professor has been so long absent from these pages that he has run some risk of being forgotten altogether.\n\nIt is the necessary law of such a story as mine that the persons concerned in it only appear when the course of events takes them up—they come and go, not by favour of my personal partiality, but by right of their direct connection with the circumstances to be detailed. For this reason, not Pesca alone, but my mother and sister as well, have been left far in the background of the narrative. My visits to the Hampstead cottage, my mother’s belief in the denial of Laura’s identity which the conspiracy had accomplished, my vain efforts to overcome the prejudice on her part and on my sister’s to which, in their jealous affection for me, they both continued to adhere, the painful necessity which that prejudice imposed on me of concealing my marriage from them till they had learnt to do justice to my wife—all these little domestic occurrences have been left unrecorded because they were not essential to the main interest of the story. It is nothing that they added to my anxieties and embittered my disappointments—the steady march of events has inexorably passed them by.\n\nFor the same reason I have said nothing here of the consolation that I found in Pesca’s brotherly affection for me, when I saw him again after the sudden cessation of my residence at Limmeridge House. I have not recorded the fidelity with which my warm-hearted little friend followed me to the place of embarkation when I sailed for Central America, or the noisy transport of joy with which he received me when we next met in London. If I had felt justified in accepting the offers of service which he made to me on my return, he would have appeared again long ere this. But, though I knew that his honour and his courage were to be implicitly relied on, I was not so sure that his discretion was to be trusted, and, for that reason only, I followed the course of all my inquiries alone. It will now be sufficiently understood that Pesca was not separated from all connection with me and my interests, although he has hitherto been separated from all connection with the progress of this narrative. He was as true and as ready a friend of mine still as ever he had been in his life.\n\nBefore I summoned Pesca to my assistance it was necessary to see for myself what sort of man I had to deal with. Up to this time I had never once set eyes on Count Fosco.\n\nThree days after my return with Laura and Marian to London, I set forth alone for Forest Road, St. John’s Wood, between ten and eleven o’clock in the morning. It was a fine day—I had some hours to spare—and I thought it likely, if I waited a little for him, that the Count might be tempted out. I had no great reason to fear the chance of his recognising me in the daytime, for the only occasion when I had been seen by him was the occasion on which he had followed me home at night.\n\nNo one appeared at the windows in the front of the house. I walked down a turning which ran past the side of it, and looked over the low garden wall. One of the back windows on the lower floor was thrown up and a net was stretched across the opening. I saw nobody, but I heard, in the room, first a shrill whistling and singing of birds, then the deep ringing voice which Marian’s description had made familiar to me. “Come out on my little finger, my pret-pret-pretties!” cried the voice. “Come out and hop upstairs! One, two, three—and up! Three, two, one—and down! One, two, three—twit-twit-twit-tweet!” The Count was exercising his canaries as he used to exercise them in Marian’s time at Blackwater Park.\n\nI waited a little while, and the singing and the whistling ceased. “Come, kiss me, my pretties!” said the deep voice. There was a responsive twittering and chirping—a low, oily laugh—a silence of a minute or so, and then I heard the opening of the house door. I turned and retraced my steps. The magnificent melody of the Prayer in Rossini’s Moses, sung in a sonorous bass voice, rose grandly through the suburban silence of the place. The front garden gate opened and closed. The Count had come out.\n\nHe crossed the road and walked towards the western boundary of the Regent’s Park. I kept on my own side of the way, a little behind him, and walked in that direction also.\n\nMarian had prepared me for his high stature, his monstrous corpulence, and his ostentatious mourning garments, but not for the horrible freshness and cheerfulness and vitality of the man. He carried his sixty years as if they had been fewer than forty. He sauntered along, wearing his hat a little on one side, with a light jaunty step, swinging his big stick, humming to himself, looking up from time to time at the houses and gardens on either side of him with superb, smiling patronage. If a stranger had been told that the whole neighbourhood belonged to him, that stranger would not have been surprised to hear it. He never looked back, he paid no apparent attention to me, no apparent attention to any one who passed him on his own side of the road, except now and then, when he smiled and smirked, with an easy paternal good humour, at the nursery-maids and the children whom he met. In this way he led me on, till we reached a colony of shops outside the western terraces of the Park.\n\nHere he stopped at a pastrycook’s, went in (probably to give an order), and came out again immediately with a tart in his hand. An Italian was grinding an organ before the shop, and a miserable little shrivelled monkey was sitting on the instrument. The Count stopped, bit a piece for himself out of the tart, and gravely handed the rest to the monkey. “My poor little man!” he said, with grotesque tenderness, “you look hungry. In the sacred name of humanity, I offer you some lunch!” The organ-grinder piteously put in his claim to a penny from the benevolent stranger. The Count shrugged his shoulders contemptuously, and passed on.\n\nWe reached the streets and the better class of shops between the New Road and Oxford Street. The Count stopped again and entered a small optician’s shop, with an inscription in the window announcing that repairs were neatly executed inside. He came out again with an opera-glass in his hand, walked a few paces on, and stopped to look at a bill of the opera placed outside a music-seller’s shop. He read the bill attentively, considered a moment, and then hailed an empty cab as it passed him. “Opera Box-office,” he said to the man, and was driven away.\n\nI crossed the road, and looked at the bill in my turn. The performance announced was Lucrezia Borgia, and it was to take place that evening. The opera-glass in the Count’s hand, his careful reading of the bill, and his direction to the cabman, all suggested that he proposed making one of the audience. I had the means of getting an admission for myself and a friend to the pit by applying to one of the scene-painters attached to the theatre, with whom I had been well acquainted in past times. There was a chance at least that the Count might be easily visible among the audience to me and to any one with me, and in this case I had the means of ascertaining whether Pesca knew his countryman or not that very night.\n\nThis consideration at once decided the disposal of my evening. I procured the tickets, leaving a note at the Professor’s lodgings on the way. At a quarter to eight I called to take him with me to the theatre. My little friend was in a state of the highest excitement, with a festive flower in his button-hole, and the largest opera-glass I ever saw hugged up under his arm.\n\n“Are you ready?” I asked.\n\n“Right-all-right,” said Pesca.\n\nWe started for the theatre.\n\n# V\n\nThe last notes of the introduction to the opera were being played, and the seats in the pit were all filled, when Pesca and I reached the theatre.\n\nThere was plenty of room, however, in the passage that ran round the pit—precisely the position best calculated to answer the purpose for which I was attending the performance. I went first to the barrier separating us from the stalls, and looked for the Count in that part of the theatre. He was not there. Returning along the passage, on the left-hand side from the stage, and looking about me attentively, I discovered him in the pit. He occupied an excellent place, some twelve or fourteen seats from the end of a bench, within three rows of the stalls. I placed myself exactly on a line with him; Pesca standing by my side. The Professor was not yet aware of the purpose for which I had brought him to the theatre, and he was rather surprised that we did not move nearer to the stage.\n\nThe curtain rose, and the opera began.\n\nThroughout the whole of the first act we remained in our position—the Count, absorbed by the orchestra and the stage, never casting so much as a chance glance at us. Not a note of Donizetti’s delicious music was lost on him. There he sat, high above his neighbours, smiling, and nodding his great head enjoyingly from time to time. When the people near him applauded the close of an air (as an English audience in such circumstances always WILL applaud), without the least consideration for the orchestral movement which immediately followed it, he looked round at them with an expression of compassionate remonstrance, and held up one hand with a gesture of polite entreaty. At the more refined passages of the singing, at the more delicate phases of the music, which passed unapplauded by others, his fat hands, adorned with perfectly-fitting black kid gloves, softly patted each other, in token of the cultivated appreciation of a musical man. At such times, his oily murmur of approval, “Bravo! Bra-a-a-a!” hummed through the silence, like the purring of a great cat. His immediate neighbours on either side—hearty, ruddy-faced people from the country, basking amazedly in the sunshine of fashionable London—seeing and hearing him, began to follow his lead. Many a burst of applause from the pit that night started from the soft, comfortable patting of the black-gloved hands. The man’s voracious vanity devoured this implied tribute to his local and critical supremacy with an appearance of the highest relish. Smiles rippled continuously over his fat face. He looked about him, at the pauses in the music, serenely satisfied with himself and his fellow-creatures. “Yes! yes! these barbarous English people are learning something from ME. Here, there, and everywhere, I—Fosco—am an influence that is felt, a man who sits supreme!” If ever face spoke, his face spoke then, and that was its language.\n\nThe curtain fell on the first act, and the audience rose to look about them. This was the time I had waited for—the time to try if Pesca knew him.\n\nHe rose with the rest, and surveyed the occupants of the boxes grandly with his opera-glass. At first his back was towards us, but he turned round in time, to our side of the theatre, and looked at the boxes above us, using his glass for a few minutes—then removing it, but still continuing to look up. This was the moment I chose, when his full face was in view, for directing Pesca’s attention to him.\n\n“Do you know that man?” I asked.\n\n“Which man, my friend?”\n\n“The tall, fat man, standing there, with his face towards us.”\n\nPesca raised himself on tiptoe, and looked at the Count.\n\n“No,” said the Professor. “The big fat man is a stranger to me. Is he famous? Why do you point him out?”\n\n“Because I have particular reasons for wishing to know something of him. He is a countryman of yours—his name is Count Fosco. Do you know that name?”\n\n“Not I, Walter. Neither the name nor the man is known to me.”\n\n“Are you quite sure you don’t recognise him? Look again—look carefully. I will tell you why I am so anxious about it when we leave the theatre. Stop! let me help you up here, where you can see him better.”\n\nI helped the little man to perch himself on the edge of the raised dais upon which the pit-seats were all placed. His small stature was no hindrance to him—here he could see over the heads of the ladies who were seated near the outermost part of the bench.\n\nA slim, light-haired man standing by us, whom I had not noticed before—a man with a scar on his left cheek—looked attentively at Pesca as I helped him up, and then looked still more attentively, following the direction of Pesca’s eyes, at the Count. Our conversation might have reached his ears, and might, as it struck me, have roused his curiosity.\n\nMeanwhile, Pesca fixed his eyes earnestly on the broad, full, smiling face turned a little upward, exactly opposite to him.\n\n“No,” he said, “I have never set my two eyes on that big fat man before in all my life.”\n\nAs he spoke the Count looked downwards towards the boxes behind us on the pit tier.\n\nThe eyes of the two Italians met.\n\nThe instant before I had been perfectly satisfied, from his own reiterated assertion, that Pesca did not know the Count. The instant afterwards I was equally certain that the Count knew Pesca!\n\nKnew him, and—more surprising still—FEARED him as well! There was no mistaking the change that passed over the villain’s face. The leaden hue that altered his yellow complexion in a moment, the sudden rigidity of all his features, the furtive scrutiny of his cold grey eyes, the motionless stillness of him from head to foot told their own tale. A mortal dread had mastered him body and soul—and his own recognition of Pesca was the cause of it!\n\nThe slim man with the scar on his cheek was still close by us. He had apparently drawn his inference from the effect produced on the Count by the sight of Pesca as I had drawn mine. He was a mild, gentlemanlike man, looking like a foreigner, and his interest in our proceedings was not expressed in anything approaching to an offensive manner.\n\nFor my own part I was so startled by the change in the Count’s face, so astounded at the entirely unexpected turn which events had taken, that I knew neither what to say or do next. Pesca roused me by stepping back to his former place at my side and speaking first.\n\n“How the fat man stares!” he exclaimed. “Is it at ME? Am I famous? How can he know me when I don’t know him?”\n\nI kept my eye still on the Count. I saw him move for the first time when Pesca moved, so as not to lose sight of the little man in the lower position in which he now stood. I was curious to see what would happen if Pesca’s attention under these circumstances was withdrawn from him, and I accordingly asked the Professor if he recognised any of his pupils that evening among the ladies in the boxes. Pesca immediately raised the large opera-glass to his eyes, and moved it slowly all round the upper part of the theatre, searching for his pupils with the most conscientious scrutiny.\n\nThe moment he showed himself to be thus engaged the Count turned round, slipped past the persons who occupied seats on the farther side of him from where we stood, and disappeared in the middle passage down the centre of the pit. I caught Pesca by the arm, and to his inexpressible astonishment, hurried him round with me to the back of the pit to intercept the Count before he could get to the door. Somewhat to my surprise, the slim man hastened out before us, avoiding a stoppage caused by some people on our side of the pit leaving their places, by which Pesca and myself were delayed. When we reached the lobby the Count had disappeared, and the foreigner with the scar was gone too.\n\n“Come home,” I said; “come home, Pesca, to your lodgings. I must speak to you in private—I must speak directly.”\n\n“My-soul-bless-my-soul!” cried the Professor, in a state of the extremest bewilderment. “What on earth is the matter?”\n\nI walked on rapidly without answering. The circumstances under which the Count had left the theatre suggested to me that his extraordinary anxiety to escape Pesca might carry him to further extremities still. He might escape me, too, by leaving London. I doubted the future if I allowed him so much as a day’s freedom to act as he pleased. And I doubted that foreign stranger, who had got the start of us, and whom I suspected of intentionally following him out.\n\nWith this double distrust in my mind, I was not long in making Pesca understand what I wanted. As soon as we two were alone in his room, I increased his confusion and amazement a hundredfold by telling him what my purpose was as plainly and unreservedly as I have acknowledged it here.\n\n“My friend, what can I do?” cried the Professor, piteously appealing to me with both hands. “Deuce-what-the-deuce! how can I help you, Walter, when I don’t know the man?”\n\n“HE knows YOU—he is afraid of you—he has left the theatre to escape you. Pesca! there must be a reason for this. Look back into your own life before you came to England. You left Italy, as you have told me yourself, for political reasons. You have never mentioned those reasons to me, and I don’t inquire into them now. I only ask you to consult your own recollections, and to say if they suggest no past cause for the terror which the first sight of you produced in that man.”\n\nTo my unutterable surprise, these words, harmless as they appeared to ME, produced the same astounding effect on Pesca which the sight of Pesca had produced on the Count. The rosy face of my little friend whitened in an instant, and he drew back from me slowly, trembling from head to foot.\n\n“Walter!” he said. “You don’t know what you ask.”\n\nHe spoke in a whisper—he looked at me as if I had suddenly revealed to him some hidden danger to both of us. In less than one minute of time he was so altered from the easy, lively, quaint little man of all my past experience, that if I had met him in the street, changed as I saw him now, I should most certainly not have known him again.\n\n“Forgive me, if I have unintentionally pained and shocked you,” I replied. “Remember the cruel wrong my wife has suffered at Count Fosco’s hands. Remember that the wrong can never be redressed, unless the means are in my power of forcing him to do her justice. I spoke in HER interests, Pesca—I ask you again to forgive me—I can say no more.”\n\nI rose to go. He stopped me before I reached the door.\n\n“Wait,” he said. “You have shaken me from head to foot. You don’t know how I left my country, and why I left my country. Let me compose myself, let me think, if I can.”\n\nI returned to my chair. He walked up and down the room, talking to himself incoherently in his own language. After several turns backwards and forwards, he suddenly came up to me, and laid his little hands with a strange tenderness and solemnity on my breast.\n\n“On your heart and soul, Walter,” he said, “is there no other way to get to that man but the chance-way through ME?”\n\n“There is no other way,” I answered.\n\nHe left me again, opened the door of the room and looked out cautiously into the passage, closed it once more, and came back.\n\n“You won your right over me, Walter,” he said, “on the day when you saved my life. It was yours from that moment, when you pleased to take it. Take it now. Yes! I mean what I say. My next words, as true as the good God is above us, will put my life into your hands.”\n\nThe trembling earnestness with which he uttered this extraordinary warning, carried with it, to my mind, the conviction that he spoke the truth.\n\n“Mind this!” he went on, shaking his hands at me in the vehemence of his agitation. “I hold no thread, in my own mind, between that man Fosco, and the past time which I call back to me for your sake. If you find the thread, keep it to yourself—tell me nothing—on my knees I beg and pray, let me be ignorant, let me be innocent, let me be blind to all the future as I am now!”\n\nHe said a few words more, hesitatingly and disconnectedly, then stopped again.\n\nI saw that the effort of expressing himself in English, on an occasion too serious to permit him the use of the quaint turns and phrases of his ordinary vocabulary, was painfully increasing the difficulty he had felt from the first in speaking to me at all. Having learnt to read and understand his native language (though not to speak it), in the earlier days of our intimate companionship, I now suggested to him that he should express himself in Italian, while I used English in putting any questions which might be necessary to my enlightenment. He accepted the proposal. In his smooth-flowing language, spoken with a vehement agitation which betrayed itself in the perpetual working of his features, in the wildness and the suddenness of his foreign gesticulations, but never in the raising of his voice, I now heard the words which armed me to meet the last struggle, that is left for this story to record.[3]\n\n[3] It is only right to mention here, that I repeat Pesco’s statement to me with the careful suppressions and alterations which the serious nature of the subject and my own sense of duty to my friend demand. My first and last concealments from the reader are those which caution renders absolutely necessary in this portion of the narrative.\n\n“You know nothing of my motive for leaving Italy,” he began, “except that it was for political reasons. If I had been driven to this country by the persecution of my government, I should not have kept those reasons a secret from you or from any one. I have concealed them because no government authority has pronounced the sentence of my exile. You have heard, Walter, of the political societies that are hidden in every great city on the continent of Europe? To one of those societies I belonged in Italy—and belong still in England. When I came to this country, I came by the direction of my chief. I was over-zealous in my younger time—I ran the risk of compromising myself and others. For those reasons I was ordered to emigrate to England and to wait. I emigrated—I have waited—I wait still. To-morrow I may be called away—ten years hence I may be called away. It is all one to me—I am here, I support myself by teaching, and I wait. I violate no oath (you shall hear why presently) in making my confidence complete by telling you the name of the society to which I belong. All I do is to put my life in your hands. If what I say to you now is ever known by others to have passed my lips, as certainly as we two sit here, I am a dead man.”\n\nHe whispered the next words in my ear. I keep the secret which he thus communicated. The society to which he belonged will be sufficiently individualised for the purpose of these pages, if I call it “The Brotherhood,” on the few occasions when any reference to the subject will be needed in this place.\n\n“The object of the Brotherhood,” Pesca went on, “is, briefly, the object of other political societies of the same sort—the destruction of tyranny and the assertion of the rights of the people. The principles of the Brotherhood are two. So long as a man’s life is useful, or even harmless only, he has the right to enjoy it. But, if his life inflicts injury on the well-being of his fellow-men, from that moment he forfeits the right, and it is not only no crime, but a positive merit, to deprive him of it. It is not for me to say in what frightful circumstances of oppression and suffering this society took its rise. It is not for you to say—you Englishmen, who have conquered your freedom so long ago, that you have conveniently forgotten what blood you shed, and what extremities you proceeded to in the conquering—it is not for you to say how far the worst of all exasperations may, or may not, carry the maddened men of an enslaved nation. The iron that has entered into our souls has gone too deep for you to find it. Leave the refugee alone! Laugh at him, distrust him, open your eyes in wonder at that secret self which smoulders in him, sometimes under the everyday respectability and tranquillity of a man like me—sometimes under the grinding poverty, the fierce squalor, of men less lucky, less pliable, less patient than I am—but judge us not! In the time of your first Charles you might have done us justice—the long luxury of your own freedom has made you incapable of doing us justice now.”\n\nAll the deepest feelings of his nature seemed to force themselves to the surface in those words—all his heart was poured out to me for the first time in our lives—but still his voice never rose, still his dread of the terrible revelation he was making to me never left him.\n\n“So far,” he resumed, “you think the society like other societies. Its object (in your English opinion) is anarchy and revolution. It takes the life of a bad king or a bad minister, as if the one and the other were dangerous wild beasts to be shot at the first opportunity. I grant you this. But the laws of the Brotherhood are the laws of no other political society on the face of the earth. The members are not known to one another. There is a president in Italy; there are presidents abroad. Each of these has his secretary. The presidents and the secretaries know the members, but the members, among themselves, are all strangers, until their chiefs see fit, in the political necessity of the time, or in the private necessity of the society, to make them known to each other. With such a safeguard as this there is no oath among us on admittance. We are identified with the Brotherhood by a secret mark, which we all bear, which lasts while our lives last. We are told to go about our ordinary business, and to report ourselves to the president, or the secretary, four times a year, in the event of our services being required. We are warned, if we betray the Brotherhood, or if we injure it by serving other interests, that we die by the principles of the Brotherhood—die by the hand of a stranger who may be sent from the other end of the world to strike the blow—or by the hand of our own bosom-friend, who may have been a member unknown to us through all the years of our intimacy. Sometimes the death is delayed—sometimes it follows close on the treachery. It is our first business to know how to wait—our second business to know how to obey when the word is spoken. Some of us may wait our lives through, and may not be wanted. Some of us may be called to the work, or to the preparation for the work, the very day of our admission. I myself—the little, easy, cheerful man you know, who, of his own accord, would hardly lift up his handkerchief to strike down the fly that buzzes about his face—I, in my younger time, under provocation so dreadful that I will not tell you of it, entered the Brotherhood by an impulse, as I might have killed myself by an impulse. I must remain in it now—it has got me, whatever I may think of it in my better circumstances and my cooler manhood, to my dying day. While I was still in Italy I was chosen secretary, and all the members of that time, who were brought face to face with my president, were brought face to face also with me.”\n\nI began to understand him—I saw the end towards which his extraordinary disclosure was now tending. He waited a moment, watching me earnestly—watching till he had evidently guessed what was passing in my mind before he resumed.\n\n“You have drawn your own conclusion already,” he said. “I see it in your face. Tell me nothing—keep me out of the secret of your thoughts. Let me make my one last sacrifice of myself, for your sake, and then have done with this subject, never to return to it again.”\n\nHe signed to me not to answer him—rose—removed his coat—and rolled up the shirt-sleeve on his left arm.\n\n“I promised you that this confidence should be complete,” he whispered, speaking close at my ear, with his eyes looking watchfully at the door. “Whatever comes of it you shall not reproach me with having hidden anything from you which it was necessary to your interests to know. I have said that the Brotherhood identifies its members by a mark that lasts for life. See the place, and the mark on it for yourself.”\n\nHe raised his bare arm, and showed me, high on the upper part of it and in the inner side, a brand deeply burnt in the flesh and stained of a bright blood-red colour. I abstain from describing the device which the brand represented. It will be sufficient to say that it was circular in form, and so small that it would have been completely covered by a shilling coin.\n\n“A man who has this mark, branded in this place,” he said, covering his arm again, “is a member of the Brotherhood. A man who has been false to the Brotherhood is discovered sooner or later by the chiefs who know him—presidents or secretaries, as the case may be. And a man discovered by the chiefs is dead. NO HUMAN LAWS CAN PROTECT HIM. Remember what you have seen and heard—draw what conclusions YOU like—act as you please. But, in the name of God, whatever you discover, whatever you do, tell me nothing! Let me remain free from a responsibility which it horrifies me to think of—which I know, in my conscience, is not my responsibility now. For the last time I say it—on my honour as a gentleman, on my oath as a Christian, if the man you pointed out at the Opera knows ME, he is so altered, or so disguised, that I do not know him. I am ignorant of his proceedings or his purposes in England. I never saw him, I never heard the name he goes by, to my knowledge, before to-night. I say no more. Leave me a little, Walter. I am overpowered by what has happened—I am shaken by what I have said. Let me try to be like myself again when we meet next.”\n\nHe dropped into a chair, and turning away from me, hid his face in his hands. I gently opened the door so as not to disturb him, and spoke my few parting words in low tones, which he might hear or not, as he pleased.\n\n“I will keep the memory of to-night in my heart of hearts,” I said. “You shall never repent the trust you have reposed in me. May I come to you to-morrow? May I come as early as nine o’clock?”\n\n“Yes, Walter,” he replied, looking up at me kindly, and speaking in English once more, as if his one anxiety now was to get back to our former relations towards each other. “Come to my little bit of breakfast before I go my ways among the pupils that I teach.”\n\n“Good-night, Pesca.”\n\n“Good-night, my friend.”\n\n# VI\n\nMy first conviction as soon as I found myself outside the house, was that no alternative was left me but to act at once on the information I had received—to make sure of the Count that night, or to risk the loss, if I only delayed till the morning, of Laura’s last chance. I looked at my watch—it was ten o’clock.\n\nNot the shadow of a doubt crossed my mind of the purpose for which the Count had left the theatre. His escape from us, that evening, was beyond all question the preliminary only to his escape from London. The mark of the Brotherhood was on his arm—I felt as certain of it as if he had shown me the brand; and the betrayal of the Brotherhood was on his conscience—I had seen it in his recognition of Pesca.\n\nIt was easy to understand why that recognition had not been mutual. A man of the Count’s character would never risk the terrible consequences of turning spy without looking to his personal security quite as carefully as he looked to his golden reward. The shaven face, which I had pointed out at the Opera, might have been covered by a beard in Pesca’s time—his dark brown hair might be a wig—his name was evidently a false one. The accident of time might have helped him as well—his immense corpulence might have come with his later years. There was every reason why Pesca should not have known him again—every reason also why he should have known Pesca, whose singular personal appearance made a marked man of him, go where he might.\n\nI have said that I felt certain of the purpose in the Count’s mind when he escaped us at the theatre. How could I doubt it, when I saw, with my own eyes, that he believed himself, in spite of the change in his appearance, to have been recognised by Pesca, and to be therefore in danger of his life? If I could get speech of him that night, if I could show him that I, too knew of the mortal peril in which he stood, what result would follow? Plainly this. One of us must be master of the situation—one of us must inevitably be at the mercy of the other.\n\nI owed it to myself to consider the chances against me before I confronted them. I owed it to my wife to do all that lay in my power to lessen the risk.\n\nThe chances against me wanted no reckoning up—they were all merged in one. If the Count discovered, by my own avowal, that the direct way to his safety lay through my life, he was probably the last man in existence who would shrink from throwing me off my guard and taking that way, when he had me alone within his reach. The only means of defence against him on which I could at all rely to lessen the risk, presented themselves, after a little careful thinking, clearly enough. Before I made any personal acknowledgment of my discovery in his presence, I must place the discovery itself where it would be ready for instant use against him, and safe from any attempt at suppression on his part. If I laid the mine under his feet before I approached him, and if I left instructions with a third person to fire it on the expiration of a certain time, unless directions to the contrary were previously received under my own hand, or from my own lips—in that event the Count’s security was absolutely dependent upon mine, and I might hold the vantage ground over him securely, even in his own house.\n\nThis idea occurred to me when I was close to the new lodgings which we had taken on returning from the sea-side. I went in without disturbing any one, by the help of my key. A light was in the hall, and I stole up with it to my workroom to make my preparations, and absolutely to commit myself to an interview with the Count, before either Laura or Marian could have the slightest suspicion of what I intended to do.\n\nA letter addressed to Pesca represented the surest measure of precaution which it was now possible for me to take. I wrote as follows—\n\n“The man whom I pointed out to you at the Opera is a member of the Brotherhood, and has been false to his trust. Put both these assertions to the test instantly. You know the name he goes by in England. His address is No. 5 Forest Road, St. John’s Wood. On the love you once bore me, use the power entrusted to you without mercy and without delay against that man. I have risked all and lost all—and the forfeit of my failure has been paid with my life.”\n\nI signed and dated these lines, enclosed them in an envelope, and sealed it up. On the outside I wrote this direction: “Keep the enclosure unopened until nine o’clock to-morrow morning. If you do not hear from me, or see me, before that time, break the seal when the clock strikes, and read the contents.” I added my initials, and protected the whole by enclosing it in a second sealed envelope, addressed to Pesca at his lodgings.\n\nNothing remained to be done after this but to find the means of sending my letter to its destination immediately. I should then have accomplished all that lay in my power. If anything happened to me in the Count’s house, I had now provided for his answering it with his life.\n\nThat the means of preventing his escape, under any circumstances whatever, were at Pesca’s disposal, if he chose to exert them, I did not for an instant doubt. The extraordinary anxiety which he had expressed to remain unenlightened as to the Count’s identity—or, in other words, to be left uncertain enough about facts to justify him to his own conscience in remaining passive—betrayed plainly that the means of exercising the terrible justice of the Brotherhood were ready to his hand, although, as a naturally humane man, he had shrunk from plainly saying as much in my presence. The deadly certainty with which the vengeance of foreign political societies can hunt down a traitor to the cause, hide himself where he may, had been too often exemplified, even in my superficial experience, to allow of any doubt. Considering the subject only as a reader of newspapers, cases recurred to my memory, both in London and in Paris, of foreigners found stabbed in the streets, whose assassins could never be traced—of bodies and parts of bodies thrown into the Thames and the Seine, by hands that could never be discovered—of deaths by secret violence which could only be accounted for in one way. I have disguised nothing relating to myself in these pages, and I do not disguise here that I believed I had written Count Fosco’s death-warrant, if the fatal emergency happened which authorised Pesca to open my enclosure.\n\nI left my room to go down to the ground floor of the house, and speak to the landlord about finding me a messenger. He happened to be ascending the stairs at the time, and we met on the landing. His son, a quick lad, was the messenger he proposed to me on hearing what I wanted. We had the boy upstairs, and I gave him his directions. He was to take the letter in a cab, to put it into Professor Pesca’s own hands, and to bring me back a line of acknowledgment from that gentleman—returning in the cab, and keeping it at the door for my use. It was then nearly half-past ten. I calculated that the boy might be back in twenty minutes, and that I might drive to St. John’s Wood, on his return, in twenty minutes more.\n\nWhen the lad had departed on his errand I returned to my own room for a little while, to put certain papers in order, so that they might be easily found in case of the worst. The key of the old-fashioned bureau in which the papers were kept I sealed up, and left it on my table, with Marian’s name written on the outside of the little packet. This done, I went downstairs to the sitting-room, in which I expected to find Laura and Marian awaiting my return from the Opera. I felt my hand trembling for the first time when I laid it on the lock of the door.\n\nNo one was in the room but Marian. She was reading, and she looked at her watch, in surprise, when I came in.\n\n“How early you are back!” she said. “You must have come away before the Opera was over.”\n\n“Yes,” I replied, “neither Pesca nor I waited for the end. Where is Laura?”\n\n“She had one of her bad headaches this evening, and I advised her to go to bed when we had done tea.”\n\nI left the room again on the pretext of wishing to see whether Laura was asleep. Marian’s quick eyes were beginning to look inquiringly at my face—Marian’s quick instinct was beginning to discover that I had something weighing on my mind.\n\nWhen I entered the bedchamber, and softly approached the bedside by the dim flicker of the night-lamp, my wife was asleep.\n\nWe had not been married quite a month yet. If my heart was heavy, if my resolution for a moment faltered again, when I looked at her face turned faithfully to my pillow in her sleep—when I saw her hand resting open on the coverlid, as if it was waiting unconsciously for mine—surely there was some excuse for me? I only allowed myself a few minutes to kneel down at the bedside, and to look close at her—so close that her breath, as it came and went, fluttered on my face. I only touched her hand and her cheek with my lips at parting. She stirred in her sleep and murmured my name, but without waking. I lingered for an instant at the door to look at her again. “God bless and keep you, my darling!” I whispered, and left her.\n\nMarian was at the stairhead waiting for me. She had a folded slip of paper in her hand.\n\n“The landlord’s son has brought this for you,” she said. “He has got a cab at the door—he says you ordered him to keep it at your disposal.”\n\n“Quite right, Marian. I want the cab—I am going out again.”\n\nI descended the stairs as I spoke, and looked into the sitting-room to read the slip of paper by the light on the table. It contained these two sentences in Pesca’s handwriting—\n\n“Your letter is received. If I don’t see you before the time you mention, I will break the seal when the clock strikes.”\n\nI placed the paper in my pocket-book, and made for the door. Marian met me on the threshold, and pushed me back into the room, where the candle-light fell full on my face. She held me by both hands, and her eyes fastened searchingly on mine.\n\n“I see!” she said, in a low eager whisper. “You are trying the last chance to-night.”\n\n“Yes, the last chance and the best,” I whispered back.\n\n“Not alone! Oh, Walter, for God’s sake, not alone! Let me go with you. Don’t refuse me because I’m only a woman. I must go! I will go! I’ll wait outside in the cab!”\n\nIt was my turn now to hold HER. She tried to break away from me and get down first to the door.\n\n“If you want to help me,” I said, “stop here and sleep in my wife’s room to-night. Only let me go away with my mind easy about Laura, and I answer for everything else. Come, Marian, give me a kiss, and show that you have the courage to wait till I come back.”\n\nI dared not allow her time to say a word more. She tried to hold me again. I unclasped her hands, and was out of the room in a moment. The boy below heard me on the stairs, and opened the hall-door. I jumped into the cab before the driver could get off the box. “Forest Road, St. John’s Wood,” I called to him through the front window. “Double fare if you get there in a quarter of an hour.” “I’ll do it, sir.” I looked at my watch. Eleven o’clock. Not a minute to lose.\n\nThe rapid motion of the cab, the sense that every instant now was bringing me nearer to the Count, the conviction that I was embarked at last, without let or hindrance, on my hazardous enterprise, heated me into such a fever of excitement that I shouted to the man to go faster and faster. As we left the streets, and crossed St. John’s Wood Road, my impatience so completely overpowered me that I stood up in the cab and stretched my head out of the window, to see the end of the journey before we reached it. Just as a church clock in the distance struck the quarter past, we turned into the Forest Road. I stopped the driver a little away from the Count’s house, paid and dismissed him, and walked on to the door.\n\nAs I approached the garden gate, I saw another person advancing towards it also from the direction opposite to mine. We met under the gas lamp in the road, and looked at each other. I instantly recognised the light-haired foreigner with the scar on his cheek, and I thought he recognised me. He said nothing, and instead of stopping at the house, as I did, he slowly walked on. Was he in the Forest Road by accident? Or had he followed the Count home from the Opera?\n\nI did not pursue those questions. After waiting a little till the foreigner had slowly passed out of sight, I rang the gate bell. It was then twenty minutes past eleven—late enough to make it quite easy for the Count to get rid of me by the excuse that he was in bed.\n\nThe only way of providing against this contingency was to send in my name without asking any preliminary questions, and to let him know, at the same time, that I had a serious motive for wishing to see him at that late hour. Accordingly, while I was waiting, I took out my card and wrote under my name “On important business.” The maid-servant answered the door while I was writing the last word in pencil, and asked me distrustfully what I “pleased to want.”\n\n“Be so good as to take that to your master,” I replied, giving her the card.\n\nI saw, by the girl’s hesitation of manner, that if I had asked for the Count in the first instance she would only have followed her instructions by telling me he was not at home. She was staggered by the confidence with which I gave her the card. After staring at me, in great perturbation, she went back into the house with my message, closing the door, and leaving me to wait in the garden.\n\nIn a minute or so she reappeared. “Her master’s compliments, and would I be so obliging as to say what my business was?” “Take my compliments back,” I replied, “and say that the business cannot be mentioned to any one but your master.” She left me again, again returned, and this time asked me to walk in.\n\nI followed her at once. In another moment I was inside the Count’s house.\n\n# VII\n\nThere was no lamp in the hall, but by the dim light of the kitchen candle, which the girl had brought upstairs with her, I saw an elderly lady steal noiselessly out of a back room on the ground floor. She cast one viperish look at me as I entered the hall, but said nothing, and went slowly upstairs without returning my bow. My familiarity with Marian’s journal sufficiently assured me that the elderly lady was Madame Fosco.\n\nThe servant led me to the room which the Countess had just left. I entered it, and found myself face to face with the Count.\n\nHe was still in his evening dress, except his coat, which he had thrown across a chair. His shirt-sleeves were turned up at the wrists, but no higher. A carpet-bag was on one side of him, and a box on the other. Books, papers, and articles of wearing apparel were scattered about the room. On a table, at one side of the door, stood the cage, so well known to me by description, which contained his white mice. The canaries and the cockatoo were probably in some other room. He was seated before the box, packing it, when I went in, and rose with some papers in his hand to receive me. His face still betrayed plain traces of the shock that had overwhelmed him at the Opera. His fat cheeks hung loose, his cold grey eyes were furtively vigilant, his voice, look, and manner were all sharply suspicious alike, as he advanced a step to meet me, and requested, with distant civility, that I would take a chair.\n\n“You come here on business, sir?” he said. “I am at a loss to know what that business can possibly be.”\n\nThe unconcealed curiosity, with which he looked hard in my face while he spoke, convinced me that I had passed unnoticed by him at the Opera. He had seen Pesca first, and from that moment till he left the theatre he had evidently seen nothing else. My name would necessarily suggest to him that I had not come into his house with other than a hostile purpose towards himself, but he appeared to be utterly ignorant thus far of the real nature of my errand.\n\n“I am fortunate in finding you here to-night,” I said. “You seem to be on the point of taking a journey?”\n\n“Is your business connected with my journey?”\n\n“In some degree.”\n\n“In what degree? Do you know where I am going to?”\n\n“No. I only know why you are leaving London.”\n\nHe slipped by me with the quickness of thought, locked the door, and put the key in his pocket.\n\n“You and I, Mr. Hartright, are excellently well acquainted with one another by reputation,” he said. “Did it, by any chance, occur to you when you came to this house that I was not the sort of man you could trifle with?”\n\n“It did occur to me,” I replied. “And I have not come to trifle with you. I am here on a matter of life and death, and if that door which you have locked was open at this moment, nothing you could say or do would induce me to pass through it.”\n\nI walked farther into the room, and stood opposite to him on the rug before the fireplace. He drew a chair in front of the door, and sat down on it, with his left arm resting on the table. The cage with the white mice was close to him, and the little creatures scampered out of their sleeping-place as his heavy arm shook the table, and peered at him through the gaps in the smartly painted wires.\n\n“On a matter of life and death,” he repeated to himself. “Those words are more serious, perhaps, than you think. What do you mean?”\n\n“What I say.”\n\nThe perspiration broke out thickly on his broad forehead. His left hand stole over the edge of the table. There was a drawer in it, with a lock, and the key was in the lock. His finger and thumb closed over the key, but did not turn it.\n\n“So you know why I am leaving London?” he went on. “Tell me the reason, if you please.” He turned the key, and unlocked the drawer as he spoke.\n\n“I can do better than that,” I replied. “I can SHOW you the reason, if you like.”\n\n“How can you show it?”\n\n“You have got your coat off,” I said. “Roll up the shirt-sleeve on your left arm, and you will see it there.”\n\nThe same livid leaden change passed over his face which I had seen pass over it at the theatre. The deadly glitter in his eyes shone steady and straight into mine. He said nothing. But his left hand slowly opened the table-drawer, and softly slipped into it. The harsh grating noise of something heavy that he was moving unseen to me sounded for a moment, then ceased. The silence that followed was so intense that the faint ticking nibble of the white mice at their wires was distinctly audible where I stood.\n\nMy life hung by a thread, and I knew it. At that final moment I thought with HIS mind, I felt with HIS fingers—I was as certain as if I had seen it of what he kept hidden from me in the drawer.\n\n“Wait a little,” I said. “You have got the door locked—you see I don’t move—you see my hands are empty. Wait a little. I have something more to say.”\n\n“You have said enough,” he replied, with a sudden composure so unnatural and so ghastly that it tried my nerves as no outbreak of violence could have tried them. “I want one moment for my own thoughts, if you please. Do you guess what I am thinking about?”\n\n“Perhaps I do.”\n\n“I am thinking,” he remarked quietly, “whether I shall add to the disorder in this room by scattering your brains about the fireplace.”\n\nIf I had moved at that moment, I saw in his face that he would have done it.\n\n“I advise you to read two lines of writing which I have about me,” I rejoined, “before you finally decide that question.”\n\nThe proposal appeared to excite his curiosity. He nodded his head. I took Pesca’s acknowledgment of the receipt of my letter out of my pocket-book, handed it to him at arm’s length, and returned to my former position in front of the fireplace.\n\nHe read the lines aloud: “Your letter is received. If I don’t hear from you before the time you mention, I will break the seal when the clock strikes.”\n\nAnother man in his position would have needed some explanation of those words—the Count felt no such necessity. One reading of the note showed him the precaution that I had taken as plainly as if he had been present at the time when I adopted it. The expression of his face changed on the instant, and his hand came out of the drawer empty.\n\n“I don’t lock up my drawer, Mr. Hartright,” he said, “and I don’t say that I may not scatter your brains about the fireplace yet. But I am a just man even to my enemy, and I will acknowledge beforehand that they are cleverer brains than I thought them. Come to the point, sir! You want something of me?”\n\n“I do, and I mean to have it.”\n\n“On conditions?”\n\n“On no conditions.”\n\nHis hand dropped into the drawer again.\n\n“Bah! we are travelling in a circle,” he said, “and those clever brains of yours are in danger again. Your tone is deplorably imprudent, sir—moderate it on the spot! The risk of shooting you on the place where you stand is less to me than the risk of letting you out of this house, except on conditions that I dictate and approve. You have not got my lamented friend to deal with now—you are face to face with Fosco! If the lives of twenty Mr. Hartrights were the stepping-stones to my safety, over all those stones I would go, sustained by my sublime indifference, self-balanced by my impenetrable calm. Respect me, if you love your own life! I summon you to answer three questions before you open your lips again. Hear them—they are necessary to this interview. Answer them—they are necessary to ME.” He held up one finger of his right hand. “First question!” he said. “You come here possessed of information which may be true or may be false—where did you get it?”\n\n“I decline to tell you.”\n\n“No matter—I shall find out. If that information is true—mind I say, with the whole force of my resolution, if—you are making your market of it here by treachery of your own or by treachery of some other man. I note that circumstance for future use in my memory, which forgets nothing, and proceed.” He held up another finger. “Second question! Those lines you invited me to read are without signature. Who wrote them?”\n\n“A man whom I have every reason to depend on, and whom you have every reason to fear.”\n\nMy answer reached him to some purpose. His left hand trembled audibly in the drawer.\n\n“How long do you give me,” he asked, putting his third question in a quieter tone, “before the clock strikes and the seal is broken?”\n\n“Time enough for you to come to my terms,” I replied.\n\n“Give me a plainer answer, Mr. Hartright. What hour is the clock to strike?”\n\n“Nine, to-morrow morning.”\n\n“Nine, to-morrow morning? Yes, yes—your trap is laid for me before I can get my passport regulated and leave London. It is not earlier, I suppose? We will see about that presently—I can keep you hostage here, and bargain with you to send for your letter before I let you go. In the meantime, be so good next as to mention your terms.”\n\n“You shall hear them. They are simple, and soon stated. You know whose interests I represent in coming here?”\n\nHe smiled with the most supreme composure, and carelessly waved his right hand.\n\n“I consent to hazard a guess,” he said jeeringly. “A lady’s interests, of course!”\n\n“My Wife’s interests.”\n\nHe looked at me with the first honest expression that had crossed his face in my presence—an expression of blank amazement. I could see that I sank in his estimation as a dangerous man from that moment. He shut up the drawer at once, folded his arms over his breast, and listened to me with a smile of satirical attention.\n\n“You are well enough aware,” I went on, “of the course which my inquiries have taken for many months past, to know that any attempted denial of plain facts will be quite useless in my presence. You are guilty of an infamous conspiracy! And the gain of a fortune of ten thousand pounds was your motive for it.”\n\nHe said nothing. But his face became overclouded suddenly by a lowering anxiety.\n\n“Keep your gain,” I said. (His face lightened again immediately, and his eyes opened on me in wider and wider astonishment.) “I am not here to disgrace myself by bargaining for money which has passed through your hands, and which has been the price of a vile crime——”\n\n“Gently, Mr. Hartright. Your moral clap-traps have an excellent effect in England—keep them for yourself and your own countrymen, if you please. The ten thousand pounds was a legacy left to my excellent wife by the late Mr. Fairlie. Place the affair on those grounds, and I will discuss it if you like. To a man of my sentiments, however, the subject is deplorably sordid. I prefer to pass it over. I invite you to resume the discussion of your terms. What do you demand?”\n\n“In the first place, I demand a full confession of the conspiracy, written and signed in my presence by yourself.”\n\nHe raised his finger again. “One!” he said, checking me off with the steady attention of a practical man.\n\n“In the second place, I demand a plain proof, which does not depend on your personal asseveration, of the date at which my wife left Blackwater Park and travelled to London.”\n\n“So! so! you can lay your finger, I see, on the weak place,” he remarked composedly. “Any more?”\n\n“At present, no more.”\n\n“Good! you have mentioned your terms, now listen to mine. The responsibility to myself of admitting what you are pleased to call the ‘conspiracy’ is less, perhaps, upon the whole, than the responsibility of laying you dead on that hearthrug. Let us say that I meet your proposal—on my own conditions. The statement you demand of me shall be written, and the plain proof shall be produced. You call a letter from my late lamented friend informing me of the day and hour of his wife’s arrival in London, written, signed, and dated by himself, a proof, I suppose? I can give you this. I can also send you to the man of whom I hired the carriage to fetch my visitor from the railway, on the day when she arrived—his order-book may help you to your date, even if his coachman who drove me proves to be of no use. These things I can do, and will do, on conditions. I recite them. First condition! Madame Fosco and I leave this house when and how we please, without interference of any kind on your part. Second condition! You wait here, in company with me, to see my agent, who is coming at seven o’clock in the morning to regulate my affairs. You give my agent a written order to the man who has got your sealed letter to resign his possession of it. You wait here till my agent places that letter unopened in my hands, and you then allow me one clear half-hour to leave the house—after which you resume your own freedom of action and go where you please. Third condition! You give me the satisfaction of a gentleman for your intrusion into my private affairs, and for the language you have allowed yourself to use to me at this conference. The time and place, abroad, to be fixed in a letter from my hand when I am safe on the Continent, and that letter to contain a strip of paper measuring accurately the length of my sword. Those are my terms. Inform me if you accept them—Yes or No.”\n\nThe extraordinary mixture of prompt decision, far-sighted cunning, and mountebank bravado in this speech, staggered me for a moment—and only for a moment. The one question to consider was, whether I was justified or not in possessing myself of the means of establishing Laura’s identity at the cost of allowing the scoundrel who had robbed her of it to escape me with impunity. I knew that the motive of securing the just recognition of my wife in the birthplace from which she had been driven out as an impostor, and of publicly erasing the lie that still profaned her mother’s tombstone, was far purer, in its freedom from all taint of evil passion, than the vindictive motive which had mingled itself with my purpose from the first. And yet I cannot honestly say that my own moral convictions were strong enough to decide the struggle in me by themselves. They were helped by my remembrance of Sir Percival’s death. How awfully, at the last moment, had the working of the retribution THERE been snatched from my feeble hands! What right had I to decide, in my poor mortal ignorance of the future, that this man, too, must escape with impunity because he escaped ME? I thought of these things—perhaps with the superstition inherent in my nature, perhaps with a sense worthier of me than superstition. It was hard, when I had fastened my hold on him at last, to loosen it again of my own accord—but I forced myself to make the sacrifice. In plainer words, I determined to be guided by the one higher motive of which I was certain, the motive of serving the cause of Laura and the cause of Truth.\n\n“I accept your conditions,” I said. “With one reservation on my part.”\n\n“What reservation may that be?” he asked.\n\n“It refers to the sealed letter,” I answered. “I require you to destroy it unopened in my presence as soon as it is placed in your hands.”\n\nMy object in making this stipulation was simply to prevent him from carrying away written evidence of the nature of my communication with Pesca. The fact of my communication he would necessarily discover, when I gave the address to his agent in the morning. But he could make no use of it on his own unsupported testimony—even if he really ventured to try the experiment—which need excite in me the slightest apprehension on Pesca’s account.\n\n“I grant your reservation,” he replied, after considering the question gravely for a minute or two. “It is not worth dispute—the letter shall be destroyed when it comes into my hands.”\n\nHe rose, as he spoke, from the chair in which he had been sitting opposite to me up to this time. With one effort he appeared to free his mind from the whole pressure on it of the interview between us thus far. “Ouf!” he cried, stretching his arms luxuriously, “the skirmish was hot while it lasted. Take a seat, Mr. Hartright. We meet as mortal enemies hereafter—let us, like gallant gentlemen, exchange polite attentions in the meantime. Permit me to take the liberty of calling for my wife.”\n\nHe unlocked and opened the door. “Eleanor!” he called out in his deep voice. The lady of the viperish face came in. “Madame Fosco—Mr. Hartright,” said the Count, introducing us with easy dignity. “My angel,” he went on, addressing his wife, “will your labours of packing up allow you time to make me some nice strong coffee? I have writing business to transact with Mr. Hartright—and I require the full possession of my intelligence to do justice to myself.”\n\nMadame Fosco bowed her head twice—once sternly to me, once submissively to her husband, and glided out of the room.\n\nThe Count walked to a writing-table near the window, opened his desk, and took from it several quires of paper and a bundle of quill pens. He scattered the pens about the table, so that they might lie ready in all directions to be taken up when wanted, and then cut the paper into a heap of narrow slips, of the form used by professional writers for the press. “I shall make this a remarkable document,” he said, looking at me over his shoulder. “Habits of literary composition are perfectly familiar to me. One of the rarest of all the intellectual accomplishments that a man can possess is the grand faculty of arranging his ideas. Immense privilege! I possess it. Do you?”\n\nHe marched backwards and forwards in the room, until the coffee appeared, humming to himself, and marking the places at which obstacles occurred in the arrangement of his ideas, by striking his forehead from time to time with the palm of his hand. The enormous audacity with which he seized on the situation in which I placed him, and made it the pedestal on which his vanity mounted for the one cherished purpose of self-display, mastered my astonishment by main force. Sincerely as I loathed the man, the prodigious strength of his character, even in its most trivial aspects, impressed me in spite of myself.\n\nThe coffee was brought in by Madame Fosco. He kissed her hand in grateful acknowledgment, and escorted her to the door; returned, poured out a cup of coffee for himself, and took it to the writing-table.\n\n“May I offer you some coffee, Mr. Hartright?” he said, before he sat down.\n\nI declined.\n\n“What! you think I shall poison you?” he said gaily. “The English intellect is sound, so far as it goes,” he continued, seating himself at the table; “but it has one grave defect—it is always cautious in the wrong place.”\n\nHe dipped his pen in the ink, placed the first slip of paper before him with a thump of his hand on the desk, cleared his throat, and began. He wrote with great noise and rapidity, in so large and bold a hand, and with such wide spaces between the lines, that he reached the bottom of the slip in not more than two minutes certainly from the time when he started at the top. Each slip as he finished it was paged, and tossed over his shoulder out of his way on the floor. When his first pen was worn out, THAT went over his shoulder too, and he pounced on a second from the supply scattered about the table. Slip after slip, by dozens, by fifties, by hundreds, flew over his shoulders on either side of him till he had snowed himself up in paper all round his chair. Hour after hour passed—and there I sat watching, there he sat writing. He never stopped, except to sip his coffee, and when that was exhausted, to smack his forehead from time to time. One o’clock struck, two, three, four—and still the slips flew about all round him; still the untiring pen scraped its way ceaselessly from top to bottom of the page, still the white chaos of paper rose higher and higher all round his chair. At four o’clock I heard a sudden splutter of the pen, indicative of the flourish with which he signed his name. “Bravo!” he cried, springing to his feet with the activity of a young man, and looking me straight in the face with a smile of superb triumph.\n\n“Done, Mr. Hartright!” he announced with a self-renovating thump of his fist on his broad breast. “Done, to my own profound satisfaction—to YOUR profound astonishment, when you read what I have written. The subject is exhausted: the man—Fosco—is not. I proceed to the arrangement of my slips—to the revision of my slips—to the reading of my slips—addressed emphatically to your private ear. Four o’clock has just struck. Good! Arrangement, revision, reading, from four to five. Short snooze of restoration for myself from five to six. Final preparations from six to seven. Affair of agent and sealed letter from seven to eight. At eight, en route. Behold the programme!”\n\nHe sat down cross-legged on the floor among his papers, strung them together with a bodkin and a piece of string—revised them, wrote all the titles and honours by which he was personally distinguished at the head of the first page, and then read the manuscript to me with loud theatrical emphasis and profuse theatrical gesticulation. The reader will have an opportunity, ere long, of forming his own opinion of the document. It will be sufficient to mention here that it answered my purpose.\n\nHe next wrote me the address of the person from whom he had hired the fly, and handed me Sir Percival’s letter. It was dated from Hampshire on the 25th of July, and it announced the journey of “Lady Glyde” to London on the 26th. Thus, on the very day (the 25th) when the doctor’s certificate declared that she had died in St. John’s Wood, she was alive, by Sir Percival’s own showing, at Blackwater—and, on the day after, she was to take a journey! When the proof of that journey was obtained from the flyman, the evidence would be complete.\n\n“A quarter-past five,” said the Count, looking at his watch. “Time for my restorative snooze. I personally resemble Napoleon the Great, as you may have remarked, Mr. Hartright—I also resemble that immortal man in my power of commanding sleep at will. Excuse me one moment. I will summon Madame Fosco, to keep you from feeling dull.”\n\nKnowing as well as he did, that he was summoning Madame Fosco to ensure my not leaving the house while he was asleep, I made no reply, and occupied myself in tying up the papers which he had placed in my possession.\n\nThe lady came in, cool, pale, and venomous as ever. “Amuse Mr. Hartright, my angel,” said the Count. He placed a chair for her, kissed her hand for the second time, withdrew to a sofa, and, in three minutes, was as peacefully and happily asleep as the most virtuous man in existence.\n\nMadame Fosco took a book from the table, sat down, and looked at me, with the steady vindictive malice of a woman who never forgot and never forgave.\n\n“I have been listening to your conversation with my husband,” she said. “If I had been in HIS place—I would have laid you dead on the hearthrug.”\n\nWith those words she opened her book, and never looked at me or spoke to me from that time till the time when her husband woke.\n\nHe opened his eyes and rose from the sofa, accurately to an hour from the time when he had gone to sleep.\n\n“I feel infinitely refreshed,” he remarked. “Eleanor, my good wife, are you all ready upstairs? That is well. My little packing here can be completed in ten minutes—my travelling-dress assumed in ten minutes more. What remains before the agent comes?” He looked about the room, and noticed the cage with his white mice in it. “Ah!” he cried piteously, “a last laceration of my sympathies still remains. My innocent pets! my little cherished children! what am I to do with them? For the present we are settled nowhere; for the present we travel incessantly—the less baggage we carry the better for ourselves. My cockatoo, my canaries, and my little mice—who will cherish them when their good Papa is gone?”\n\nHe walked about the room deep in thought. He had not been at all troubled about writing his confession, but he was visibly perplexed and distressed about the far more important question of the disposal of his pets. After long consideration he suddenly sat down again at the writing-table.\n\n“An idea!” he exclaimed. “I will offer my canaries and my cockatoo to this vast Metropolis—my agent shall present them in my name to the Zoological Gardens of London. The Document that describes them shall be drawn out on the spot.”\n\nHe began to write, repeating the words as they flowed from his pen.\n\n“Number one. Cockatoo of transcendent plumage: attraction, of himself, to all visitors of taste. Number two. Canaries of unrivalled vivacity and intelligence: worthy of the garden of Eden, worthy also of the garden in the Regent’s Park. Homage to British Zoology. Offered by Fosco.”\n\nThe pen spluttered again, and the flourish was attached to his signature.\n\n“Count! you have not included the mice,” said Madame Fosco.\n\nHe left the table, took her hand, and placed it on his heart.\n\n“All human resolution, Eleanor,” he said solemnly, “has its limits. MY limits are inscribed on that Document. I cannot part with my white mice. Bear with me, my angel, and remove them to their travelling cage upstairs.”\n\n“Admirable tenderness!” said Madame Fosco, admiring her husband, with a last viperish look in my direction. She took up the cage carefully, and left the room.\n\nThe Count looked at his watch. In spite of his resolute assumption of composure, he was getting anxious for the agent’s arrival. The candles had long since been extinguished, and the sunlight of the new morning poured into the room. It was not till five minutes past seven that the gate bell rang, and the agent made his appearance. He was a foreigner with a dark beard.\n\n“Mr. Hartright—Monsieur Rubelle,” said the Count, introducing us. He took the agent (a foreign spy, in every line of his face, if ever there was one yet) into a corner of the room, whispered some directions to him, and then left us together. “Monsieur Rubelle,” as soon as we were alone, suggested with great politeness that I should favour him with his instructions. I wrote two lines to Pesca, authorising him to deliver my sealed letter “to the bearer,” directed the note, and handed it to Monsieur Rubelle.\n\nThe agent waited with me till his employer returned, equipped in travelling costume. The Count examined the address of my letter before he dismissed the agent. “I thought so!” he said, turning on me with a dark look, and altering again in his manner from that moment.\n\nHe completed his packing, and then sat consulting a travelling map, making entries in his pocket-book, and looking every now and then impatiently at his watch. Not another word, addressed to myself, passed his lips. The near approach of the hour for his departure, and the proof he had seen of the communication established between Pesca and myself, had plainly recalled his whole attention to the measures that were necessary for securing his escape.\n\nA little before eight o’clock, Monsieur Rubelle came back with my unopened letter in his hand. The Count looked carefully at the superscription and the seal, lit a candle, and burnt the letter. “I perform my promise,” he said, “but this matter, Mr. Hartright, shall not end here.”\n\nThe agent had kept at the door the cab in which he had returned. He and the maid-servant now busied themselves in removing the luggage. Madame Fosco came downstairs, thickly veiled, with the travelling cage of the white mice in her hand. She neither spoke to me nor looked towards me. Her husband escorted her to the cab. “Follow me as far as the passage,” he whispered in my ear; “I may want to speak to you at the last moment.”\n\nI went out to the door, the agent standing below me in the front garden. The Count came back alone, and drew me a few steps inside the passage.\n\n“Remember the Third condition!” he whispered. “You shall hear from me, Mr. Hartright—I may claim from you the satisfaction of a gentleman sooner than you think for.” He caught my hand before I was aware of him, and wrung it hard—then turned to the door, stopped, and came back to me again.\n\n“One word more,” he said confidentially. “When I last saw Miss Halcombe, she looked thin and ill. I am anxious about that admirable woman. Take care of her, sir! With my hand on my heart, I solemnly implore you, take care of Miss Halcombe!”\n\nThose were the last words he said to me before he squeezed his huge body into the cab and drove off.\n\nThe agent and I waited at the door a few moments looking after him. While we were standing together, a second cab appeared from a turning a little way down the road. It followed the direction previously taken by the Count’s cab, and as it passed the house and the open garden gate, a person inside looked at us out of the window. The stranger at the Opera again!—the foreigner with a scar on his left cheek.\n\n“You wait here with me, sir, for half an hour more!” said Monsieur Rubelle.\n\n“I do.”\n\nWe returned to the sitting-room. I was in no humour to speak to the agent, or to allow him to speak to me. I took out the papers which the Count had placed in my hands, and read the terrible story of the conspiracy told by the man who had planned and perpetrated it.\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED BY ISIDOR, OTTAVIO, BALDASSARE FOSCO\n\n(Count of the Holy Roman Empire, Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Brazen Crown, Perpetual Arch-Master of the Rosicrucian Masons of Mesopotamia; Attached (in Honorary Capacities) to Societies Musical, Societies Medical, Societies Philosophical, and Societies General Benevolent, throughout Europe; etc. etc. etc.)\n\n# THE COUNT’S NARRATIVE\n\nIn the summer of eighteen hundred and fifty I arrived in England, charged with a delicate political mission from abroad. Confidential persons were semi-officially connected with me, whose exertions I was authorised to direct, Monsieur and Madame Rubelle being among the number. Some weeks of spare time were at my disposal, before I entered on my functions by establishing myself in the suburbs of London. Curiosity may stop here to ask for some explanation of those functions on my part. I entirely sympathise with the request. I also regret that diplomatic reserve forbids me to comply with it.\n\nI arranged to pass the preliminary period of repose, to which I have just referred, in the superb mansion of my late lamented friend, Sir Percival Glyde. HE arrived from the Continent with his wife. I arrived from the Continent with MINE. England is the land of domestic happiness—how appropriately we entered it under these domestic circumstances!\n\nThe bond of friendship which united Percival and myself was strengthened, on this occasion, by a touching similarity in the pecuniary position on his side and on mine. We both wanted money. Immense necessity! Universal want! Is there a civilised human being who does not feel for us? How insensible must that man be! Or how rich!\n\nI enter into no sordid particulars, in discussing this part of the subject. My mind recoils from them. With a Roman austerity, I show my empty purse and Percival’s to the shrinking public gaze. Let us allow the deplorable fact to assert itself, once for all, in that manner, and pass on.\n\nWe were received at the mansion by the magnificent creature who is inscribed on my heart as “Marian,” who is known in the colder atmosphere of society as “Miss Halcombe.”\n\nJust Heaven! with what inconceivable rapidity I learnt to adore that woman. At sixty, I worshipped her with the volcanic ardour of eighteen. All the gold of my rich nature was poured hopelessly at her feet. My wife—poor angel!—my wife, who adores me, got nothing but the shillings and the pennies. Such is the World, such Man, such Love. What are we (I ask) but puppets in a show-box? Oh, omnipotent Destiny, pull our strings gently! Dance us mercifully off our miserable little stage!\n\nThe preceding lines, rightly understood, express an entire system of philosophy. It is mine.\n\nI resume.\n\nThe domestic position at the commencement of our residence at Blackwater Park has been drawn with amazing accuracy, with profound mental insight, by the hand of Marian herself. (Pass me the intoxicating familiarity of mentioning this sublime creature by her Christian name.) Accurate knowledge of the contents of her journal—to which I obtained access by clandestine means, unspeakably precious to me in the remembrance—warns my eager pen from topics which this essentially exhaustive woman has already made her own.\n\nThe interests—interests, breathless and immense!—with which I am here concerned, begin with the deplorable calamity of Marian’s illness.\n\nThe situation at this period was emphatically a serious one. Large sums of money, due at a certain time, were wanted by Percival (I say nothing of the modicum equally necessary to myself), and the one source to look to for supplying them was the fortune of his wife, of which not one farthing was at his disposal until her death. Bad so far, and worse still farther on. My lamented friend had private troubles of his own, into which the delicacy of my disinterested attachment to him forbade me from inquiring too curiously. I knew nothing but that a woman, named Anne Catherick, was hidden in the neighbourhood, that she was in communication with Lady Glyde, and that the disclosure of a secret, which would be the certain ruin of Percival, might be the result. He had told me himself that he was a lost man, unless his wife was silenced, and unless Anne Catherick was found. If he was a lost man, what would become of our pecuniary interests? Courageous as I am by nature, I absolutely trembled at the idea!\n\nThe whole force of my intelligence was now directed to the finding of Anne Catherick. Our money affairs, important as they were, admitted of delay—but the necessity of discovering the woman admitted of none. I only knew her by description, as presenting an extraordinary personal resemblance to Lady Glyde. The statement of this curious fact—intended merely to assist me in identifying the person of whom we were in search—when coupled with the additional information that Anne Catherick had escaped from a mad-house, started the first immense conception in my mind, which subsequently led to such amazing results. That conception involved nothing less than the complete transformation of two separate identities. Lady Glyde and Anne Catherick were to change names, places, and destinies, the one with the other—the prodigious consequences contemplated by the change being the gain of thirty thousand pounds, and the eternal preservation of Sir Percival’s secret.\n\nMy instincts (which seldom err) suggested to me, on reviewing the circumstances, that our invisible Anne would, sooner or later, return to the boat-house at the Blackwater lake. There I posted myself, previously mentioning to Mrs. Michelson, the housekeeper, that I might be found when wanted, immersed in study, in that solitary place. It is my rule never to make unnecessary mysteries, and never to set people suspecting me for want of a little seasonable candour on my part. Mrs. Michelson believed in me from first to last. This ladylike person (widow of a Protestant priest) overflowed with faith. Touched by such superfluity of simple confidence in a woman of her mature years, I opened the ample reservoirs of my nature and absorbed it all.\n\nI was rewarded for posting myself sentinel at the lake by the appearance—not of Anne Catherick herself, but of the person in charge of her. This individual also overflowed with simple faith, which I absorbed in myself, as in the case already mentioned. I leave her to describe the circumstances (if she has not done so already) under which she introduced me to the object of her maternal care. When I first saw Anne Catherick she was asleep. I was electrified by the likeness between this unhappy woman and Lady Glyde. The details of the grand scheme which had suggested themselves in outline only, up to that period, occurred to me, in all their masterly combination, at the sight of the sleeping face. At the same time, my heart, always accessible to tender influences, dissolved in tears at the spectacle of suffering before me. I instantly set myself to impart relief. In other words, I provided the necessary stimulant for strengthening Anne Catherick to perform the journey to London.\n\nThe best years of my life have been passed in the ardent study of medical and chemical science. Chemistry especially has always had irresistible attractions for me from the enormous, the illimitable power which the knowledge of it confers. Chemists—I assert it emphatically—might sway, if they pleased, the destinies of humanity. Let me explain this before I go further.\n\nMind, they say, rules the world. But what rules the mind? The body (follow me closely here) lies at the mercy of the most omnipotent of all potentates—the Chemist. Give me—Fosco—chemistry; and when Shakespeare has conceived Hamlet, and sits down to execute the conception—with a few grains of powder dropped into his daily food, I will reduce his mind, by the action of his body, till his pen pours out the most abject drivel that has ever degraded paper. Under similar circumstances, revive me the illustrious Newton. I guarantee that when he sees the apple fall he shall EAT IT, instead of discovering the principle of gravitation. Nero’s dinner shall transform Nero into the mildest of men before he has done digesting it, and the morning draught of Alexander the Great shall make Alexander run for his life at the first sight of the enemy the same afternoon. On my sacred word of honour it is lucky for Society that modern chemists are, by incomprehensible good fortune, the most harmless of mankind. The mass are worthy fathers of families, who keep shops. The few are philosophers besotted with admiration for the sound of their own lecturing voices, visionaries who waste their lives on fantastic impossibilities, or quacks whose ambition soars no higher than our corns. Thus Society escapes, and the illimitable power of Chemistry remains the slave of the most superficial and the most insignificant ends.\n\nWhy this outburst? Why this withering eloquence?\n\nBecause my conduct has been misrepresented, because my motives have been misunderstood. It has been assumed that I used my vast chemical resources against Anne Catherick, and that I would have used them if I could against the magnificent Marian herself. Odious insinuations both! All my interests were concerned (as will be seen presently) in the preservation of Anne Catherick’s life. All my anxieties were concentrated on Marian’s rescue from the hands of the licensed imbecile who attended her, and who found my advice confirmed from first to last by the physician from London. On two occasions only—both equally harmless to the individual on whom I practised—did I summon to myself the assistance of chemical knowledge. On the first of the two, after following Marian to the inn at Blackwater (studying, behind a convenient waggon which hid me from her, the poetry of motion, as embodied in her walk), I availed myself of the services of my invaluable wife, to copy one and to intercept the other of two letters which my adored enemy had entrusted to a discarded maid. In this case, the letters being in the bosom of the girl’s dress, Madame Fosco could only open them, read them, perform her instructions, seal them, and put them back again by scientific assistance—which assistance I rendered in a half-ounce bottle. The second occasion, when the same means were employed, was the occasion (to which I shall soon refer) of Lady Glyde’s arrival in London. Never at any other time was I indebted to my Art as distinguished from myself. To all other emergencies and complications my natural capacity for grappling, single-handed, with circumstances, was invariably equal. I affirm the all-pervading intelligence of that capacity. At the expense of the Chemist I vindicate the Man.\n\nRespect this outburst of generous indignation. It has inexpressibly relieved me. En route! Let us proceed.\n\nHaving suggested to Mrs. Clement (or Clements, I am not sure which) that the best method of keeping Anne out of Percival’s reach was to remove her to London—having found that my proposal was eagerly received, and having appointed a day to meet the travellers at the station and to see them leave it, I was at liberty to return to the house and to confront the difficulties which still remained to be met.\n\nMy first proceeding was to avail myself of the sublime devotion of my wife. I had arranged with Mrs. Clements that she should communicate her London address, in Anne’s interests, to Lady Glyde. But this was not enough. Designing persons in my absence might shake the simple confidence of Mrs. Clements, and she might not write after all. Who could I find capable of travelling to London by the train she travelled by, and of privately seeing her home? I asked myself this question. The conjugal part of me immediately answered—Madame Fosco.\n\nAfter deciding on my wife’s mission to London, I arranged that the journey should serve a double purpose. A nurse for the suffering Marian, equally devoted to the patient and to myself, was a necessity of my position. One of the most eminently confidential and capable women in existence was by good fortune at my disposal. I refer to that respectable matron, Madame Rubelle, to whom I addressed a letter, at her residence in London, by the hands of my wife.\n\nOn the appointed day Mrs. Clements and Anne Catherick met me at the station. I politely saw them off, I politely saw Madame Fosco off by the same train. The last thing at night my wife returned to Blackwater, having followed her instructions with the most unimpeachable accuracy. She was accompanied by Madame Rubelle, and she brought me the London address of Mrs. Clements. After-events proved this last precaution to have been unnecessary. Mrs. Clements punctually informed Lady Glyde of her place of abode. With a wary eye on future emergencies, I kept the letter.\n\nThe same day I had a brief interview with the doctor, at which I protested, in the sacred interests of humanity, against his treatment of Marian’s case. He was insolent, as all ignorant people are. I showed no resentment, I deferred quarrelling with him till it was necessary to quarrel to some purpose. My next proceeding was to leave Blackwater myself. I had my London residence to take in anticipation of coming events. I had also a little business of the domestic sort to transact with Mr. Frederick Fairlie. I found the house I wanted in St. John’s Wood. I found Mr. Fairlie at Limmeridge, Cumberland.\n\nMy own private familiarity with the nature of Marian’s correspondence had previously informed me that she had written to Mr. Fairlie, proposing, as a relief to Lady Glyde’s matrimonial embarrassments, to take her on a visit to her uncle in Cumberland. This letter I had wisely allowed to reach its destination, feeling at the time that it could do no harm, and might do good. I now presented myself before Mr. Fairlie to support Marian’s own proposal—with certain modifications which, happily for the success of my plans, were rendered really inevitable by her illness. It was necessary that Lady Glyde should leave Blackwater alone, by her uncle’s invitation, and that she should rest a night on the journey at her aunt’s house (the house I had in St. John’s Wood) by her uncle’s express advice. To achieve these results, and to secure a note of invitation which could be shown to Lady Glyde, were the objects of my visit to Mr. Fairlie. When I have mentioned that this gentleman was equally feeble in mind and body, and that I let loose the whole force of my character on him, I have said enough. I came, saw, and conquered Fairlie.\n\nOn my return to Blackwater Park (with the letter of invitation) I found that the doctor’s imbecile treatment of Marian’s case had led to the most alarming results. The fever had turned to typhus. Lady Glyde, on the day of my return, tried to force herself into the room to nurse her sister. She and I had no affinities of sympathy—she had committed the unpardonable outrage on my sensibilities of calling me a spy—she was a stumbling-block in my way and in Percival’s—but, for all that, my magnanimity forbade me to put her in danger of infection with my own hand. At the same time I offered no hindrance to her putting herself in danger. If she had succeeded in doing so, the intricate knot which I was slowly and patiently operating on might perhaps have been cut by circumstances. As it was, the doctor interfered and she was kept out of the room.\n\nI had myself previously recommended sending for advice to London. This course had been now taken. The physician, on his arrival, confirmed my view of the case. The crisis was serious. But we had hope of our charming patient on the fifth day from the appearance of the typhus. I was only once absent from Blackwater at this time—when I went to London by the morning train to make the final arrangements at my house in St. John’s Wood, to assure myself by private inquiry that Mrs. Clements had not moved, and to settle one or two little preliminary matters with the husband of Madame Rubelle. I returned at night. Five days afterwards the physician pronounced our interesting Marian to be out of all danger, and to be in need of nothing but careful nursing. This was the time I had waited for. Now that medical attendance was no longer indispensable, I played the first move in the game by asserting myself against the doctor. He was one among many witnesses in my way whom it was necessary to remove. A lively altercation between us (in which Percival, previously instructed by me, refused to interfere) served the purpose in view. I descended on the miserable man in an irresistible avalanche of indignation, and swept him from the house.\n\nThe servants were the next encumbrances to get rid of. Again I instructed Percival (whose moral courage required perpetual stimulants), and Mrs. Michelson was amazed, one day, by hearing from her master that the establishment was to be broken up. We cleared the house of all the servants but one, who was kept for domestic purposes, and whose lumpish stupidity we could trust to make no embarrassing discoveries. When they were gone, nothing remained but to relieve ourselves of Mrs. Michelson—a result which was easily achieved by sending this amiable lady to find lodgings for her mistress at the sea-side.\n\nThe circumstances were now exactly what they were required to be. Lady Glyde was confined to her room by nervous illness, and the lumpish housemaid (I forget her name) was shut up there at night in attendance on her mistress. Marian, though fast recovering, still kept her bed, with Mrs. Rubelle for nurse. No other living creatures but my wife, myself, and Percival were in the house. With all the chances thus in our favour I confronted the next emergency, and played the second move in the game.\n\nThe object of the second move was to induce Lady Glyde to leave Blackwater unaccompanied by her sister. Unless we could persuade her that Marian had gone on to Cumberland first, there was no chance of removing her, of her own free will, from the house. To produce this necessary operation in her mind, we concealed our interesting invalid in one of the uninhabited bedrooms at Blackwater. At the dead of night Madame Fosco, Madame Rubelle, and myself (Percival not being cool enough to be trusted) accomplished the concealment. The scene was picturesque, mysterious, dramatic in the highest degree. By my directions the bed had been made, in the morning, on a strong movable framework of wood. We had only to lift the framework gently at the head and foot, and to transport our patient where we pleased, without disturbing herself or her bed. No chemical assistance was needed or used in this case. Our interesting Marian lay in the deep repose of convalescence. We placed the candles and opened the doors beforehand. I, in right of my great personal strength, took the head of the framework—my wife and Madame Rubelle took the foot. I bore my share of that inestimably precious burden with a manly tenderness, with a fatherly care. Where is the modern Rembrandt who could depict our midnight procession? Alas for the Arts! alas for this most pictorial of subjects! The modern Rembrandt is nowhere to be found.\n\nThe next morning my wife and I started for London, leaving Marian secluded, in the uninhabited middle of the house, under care of Madame Rubelle, who kindly consented to imprison herself with her patient for two or three days. Before taking our departure I gave Percival Mr. Fairlie’s letter of invitation to his niece (instructing her to sleep on the journey to Cumberland at her aunt’s house), with directions to show it to Lady Glyde on hearing from me. I also obtained from him the address of the Asylum in which Anne Catherick had been confined, and a letter to the proprietor, announcing to that gentleman the return of his runaway patient to medical care.\n\nI had arranged, at my last visit to the metropolis, to have our modest domestic establishment ready to receive us when we arrived in London by the early train. In consequence of this wise precaution, we were enabled that same day to play the third move in the game—the getting possession of Anne Catherick.\n\nDates are of importance here. I combine in myself the opposite characteristics of a Man of Sentiment and a Man of Business. I have all the dates at my fingers’ ends.\n\nOn Wednesday, the 24th of July 1850, I sent my wife in a cab to clear Mrs. Clements out of the way, in the first place. A supposed message from Lady Glyde in London was sufficient to obtain this result. Mrs. Clements was taken away in the cab, and was left in the cab, while my wife (on pretence of purchasing something at a shop) gave her the slip, and returned to receive her expected visitor at our house in St. John’s Wood. It is hardly necessary to add that the visitor had been described to the servants as “Lady Glyde.”\n\nIn the meanwhile I had followed in another cab, with a note for Anne Catherick, merely mentioning that Lady Glyde intended to keep Mrs. Clements to spend the day with her, and that she was to join them under care of the good gentleman waiting outside, who had already saved her from discovery in Hampshire by Sir Percival. The “good gentleman” sent in this note by a street boy, and paused for results a door or two farther on. At the moment when Anne appeared at the house door and closed it this excellent man had the cab door open ready for her, absorbed her into the vehicle, and drove off.\n\n(Pass me, here, one exclamation in parenthesis. How interesting this is!)\n\nOn the way to Forest Road my companion showed no fear. I can be paternal—no man more so—when I please, and I was intensely paternal on this occasion. What titles I had to her confidence! I had compounded the medicine which had done her good—I had warned her of her danger from Sir Percival. Perhaps I trusted too implicitly to these titles—perhaps I underrated the keenness of the lower instincts in persons of weak intellect—it is certain that I neglected to prepare her sufficiently for a disappointment on entering my house. When I took her into the drawing-room—when she saw no one present but Madame Fosco, who was a stranger to her—she exhibited the most violent agitation; if she had scented danger in the air, as a dog scents the presence of some creature unseen, her alarm could not have displayed itself more suddenly and more causelessly. I interposed in vain. The fear from which she was suffering I might have soothed, but the serious heart-disease, under which she laboured, was beyond the reach of all moral palliatives. To my unspeakable horror she was seized with convulsions—a shock to the system, in her condition, which might have laid her dead at any moment at our feet.\n\nThe nearest doctor was sent for, and was told that “Lady Glyde” required his immediate services. To my infinite relief, he was a capable man. I represented my visitor to him as a person of weak intellect, and subject to delusions, and I arranged that no nurse but my wife should watch in the sick-room. The unhappy woman was too ill, however, to cause any anxiety about what she might say. The one dread which now oppressed me was the dread that the false Lady Glyde might die before the true Lady Glyde arrived in London.\n\nI had written a note in the morning to Madame Rubelle, telling her to join me at her husband’s house on the evening of Friday the 26th, with another note to Percival, warning him to show his wife her uncle’s letter of invitation, to assert that Marian had gone on before her, and to despatch her to town by the midday train, on the 26th, also. On reflection I had felt the necessity, in Anne Catherick’s state of health, of precipitating events, and of having Lady Glyde at my disposal earlier than I had originally contemplated. What fresh directions, in the terrible uncertainty of my position, could I now issue? I could do nothing but trust to chance and the doctor. My emotions expressed themselves in pathetic apostrophes, which I was just self-possessed enough to couple, in the hearing of other people, with the name of “Lady Glyde.” In all other respects Fosco, on that memorable day, was Fosco shrouded in total eclipse.\n\nShe passed a bad night, she awoke worn out, but later in the day she revived amazingly. My elastic spirits revived with her. I could receive no answers from Percival and Madame Rubelle till the morning of the next day, the 26th. In anticipation of their following my directions, which, accident apart, I knew they would do, I went to secure a fly to fetch Lady Glyde from the railway, directing it to be at my house on the 26th, at two o’clock. After seeing the order entered in the book, I went on to arrange matters with Monsieur Rubelle. I also procured the services of two gentlemen who could furnish me with the necessary certificates of lunacy. One of them I knew personally—the other was known to Monsieur Rubelle. Both were men whose vigorous minds soared superior to narrow scruples—both were labouring under temporary embarrassments—both believed in ME.\n\nIt was past five o’clock in the afternoon before I returned from the performance of these duties. When I got back Anne Catherick was dead. Dead on the 25th, and Lady Glyde was not to arrive in London till the 26th!\n\nI was stunned. Meditate on that. Fosco stunned!\n\nIt was too late to retrace our steps. Before my return the doctor had officiously undertaken to save me all trouble by registering the death, on the date when it happened, with his own hand. My grand scheme, unassailable hitherto, had its weak place now—no efforts on my part could alter the fatal event of the 25th. I turned manfully to the future. Percival’s interests and mine being still at stake, nothing was left but to play the game through to the end. I recalled my impenetrable calm—and played it.\n\nOn the morning of the 26th Percival’s letter reached me, announcing his wife’s arrival by the midday train. Madame Rubelle also wrote to say she would follow in the evening. I started in the fly, leaving the false Lady Glyde dead in the house, to receive the true Lady Glyde on her arrival by the railway at three o’clock. Hidden under the seat of the carriage, I carried with me all the clothes Anne Catherick had worn on coming into my house—they were destined to assist the resurrection of the woman who was dead in the person of the woman who was living. What a situation! I suggest it to the rising romance writers of England. I offer it, as totally new, to the worn-out dramatists of France.\n\nLady Glyde was at the station. There was great crowding and confusion, and more delay than I liked (in case any of her friends had happened to be on the spot), in reclaiming her luggage. Her first questions, as we drove off, implored me to tell her news of her sister. I invented news of the most pacifying kind, assuring her that she was about to see her sister at my house. My house, on this occasion only, was in the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, and was in the occupation of Monsieur Rubelle, who received us in the hall.\n\nI took my visitor upstairs into a back room, the two medical gentlemen being there in waiting on the floor beneath to see the patient, and to give me their certificates. After quieting Lady Glyde by the necessary assurances about her sister, I introduced my friends separately to her presence. They performed the formalities of the occasion briefly, intelligently, conscientiously. I entered the room again as soon as they had left it, and at once precipitated events by a reference of the alarming kind to “Miss Halcombe’s” state of health.\n\nResults followed as I had anticipated. Lady Glyde became frightened, and turned faint. For the second time, and the last, I called Science to my assistance. A medicated glass of water and a medicated bottle of smelling-salts relieved her of all further embarrassment and alarm. Additional applications later in the evening procured her the inestimable blessing of a good night’s rest. Madame Rubelle arrived in time to preside at Lady Glyde’s toilet. Her own clothes were taken away from her at night, and Anne Catherick’s were put on her in the— morning, with the strictest regard to propriety, by the matronly hands of the good Rubelle. Throughout the day I kept our patient in a state of partially-suspended consciousness, until the dexterous assistance of my medical friends enabled me to procure the necessary order rather earlier than I had ventured to hope. That evening (the evening of the 27th) Madame Rubelle and I took our revived “Anne Catherick” to the Asylum. She was received with great surprise, but without suspicion, thanks to the order and certificates, to Percival’s letter, to the likeness, to the clothes, and to the patient’s own confused mental condition at the time. I returned at once to assist Madame Fosco in the preparations for the burial of the False “Lady Glyde,” having the clothes and luggage of the true “Lady Glyde” in my possession. They were afterwards sent to Cumberland by the conveyance which was used for the funeral. I attended the funeral, with becoming dignity, attired in the deepest mourning.\n\nMy narrative of these remarkable events, written under equally remarkable circumstances, closes here. The minor precautions which I observed in communicating with Limmeridge House are already known—so is the magnificent success of my enterprise—so are the solid pecuniary results which followed it. I have to assert, with the whole force of my conviction, that the one weak place in my scheme would never have been found out if the one weak place in my heart had not been discovered first. Nothing but my fatal admiration for Marian restrained me from stepping in to my own rescue when she effected her sister’s escape. I ran the risk, and trusted in the complete destruction of Lady Glyde’s identity. If either Marian or Mr. Hartright attempted to assert that identity, they would publicly expose themselves to the imputation of sustaining a rank deception, they would be distrusted and discredited accordingly, and they would therefore be powerless to place my interests or Percival’s secret in jeopardy. I committed one error in trusting myself to such a blindfold calculation of chances as this. I committed another when Percival had paid the penalty of his own obstinacy and violence, by granting Lady Glyde a second reprieve from the mad-house, and allowing Mr. Hartright a second chance of escaping me. In brief, Fosco, at this serious crisis, was untrue to himself. Deplorable and uncharacteristic fault! Behold the cause, in my heart—behold, in the image of Marian Halcombe, the first and last weakness of Fosco’s life!\n\nAt the ripe age of sixty, I make this unparalleled confession. Youths! I invoke your sympathy. Maidens! I claim your tears.\n\nA word more, and the attention of the reader (concentrated breathlessly on myself) shall be released.\n\nMy own mental insight informs me that three inevitable questions will be asked here by persons of inquiring minds. They shall be stated—they shall be answered.\n\nFirst question. What is the secret of Madame Fosco’s unhesitating devotion of herself to the fulfilment of my boldest wishes, to the furtherance of my deepest plans? I might answer this by simply referring to my own character, and by asking, in my turn, Where, in the history of the world, has a man of my order ever been found without a woman in the background self-immolated on the altar of his life? But I remember that I am writing in England, I remember that I was married in England, and I ask if a woman’s marriage obligations in this country provide for her private opinion of her husband’s principles? No! They charge her unreservedly to love, honour, and obey him. That is exactly what my wife has done. I stand here on a supreme moral elevation, and I loftily assert her accurate performance of her conjugal duties. Silence, Calumny! Your sympathy, Wives of England, for Madame Fosco!\n\nSecond question. If Anne Catherick had not died when she did, what should I have done? I should, in that case, have assisted worn-out Nature in finding permanent repose. I should have opened the doors of the Prison of Life, and have extended to the captive (incurably afflicted in mind and body both) a happy release.\n\nThird question. On a calm revision of all the circumstances—Is my conduct worthy of any serious blame? Most emphatically, No! Have I not carefully avoided exposing myself to the odium of committing unnecessary crime? With my vast resources in chemistry, I might have taken Lady Glyde’s life. At immense personal sacrifice I followed the dictates of my own ingenuity, my own humanity, my own caution, and took her identity instead. Judge me by what I might have done. How comparatively innocent! how indirectly virtuous I appear in what I really did!\n\nI announced on beginning it that this narrative would be a remarkable document. It has entirely answered my expectations. Receive these fervid lines—my last legacy to the country I leave for ever. They are worthy of the occasion, and worthy of FOSCO.\n\n# I\n\nWhen I closed the last leaf of the Count’s manuscript the half-hour during which I had engaged to remain at Forest Road had expired. Monsieur Rubelle looked at his watch and bowed. I rose immediately, and left the agent in possession of the empty house. I never saw him again—I never heard more of him or of his wife. Out of the dark byways of villainy and deceit they had crawled across our path—into the same byways they crawled back secretly and were lost.\n\nIn a quarter of an hour after leaving Forest Road I was at home again.\n\nBut few words sufficed to tell Laura and Marian how my desperate venture had ended, and what the next event in our lives was likely to be. I left all details to be described later in the day, and hastened back to St. John’s Wood, to see the person of whom Count Fosco had ordered the fly, when he went to meet Laura at the station.\n\nThe address in my possession led me to some “livery stables,” about a quarter of a mile distant from Forest Road. The proprietor proved to be a civil and respectable man. When I explained that an important family matter obliged me to ask him to refer to his books for the purpose of ascertaining a date with which the record of his business transactions might supply me, he offered no objection to granting my request. The book was produced, and there, under the date of “July 26th, 1850,” the order was entered in these words—\n\n“Brougham to Count Fosco, 5 Forest Road. Two o’clock. (John Owen).”\n\nI found on inquiry that the name of “John Owen,” attached to the entry, referred to the man who had been employed to drive the fly. He was then at work in the stable-yard, and was sent for to see me at my request.\n\n“Do you remember driving a gentleman, in the month of July last, from Number Five Forest Road to the Waterloo Bridge station?” I asked.\n\n“Well, sir,” said the man, “I can’t exactly say I do.”\n\n“Perhaps you remember the gentleman himself? Can you call to mind driving a foreigner last summer—a tall gentleman and remarkably fat?”\n\nThe man’s face brightened directly. “I remember him, sir! The fattest gentleman as ever I see, and the heaviest customer as ever I drove. Yes, yes—I call him to mind, sir! We DID go to the station, and it WAS from Forest Road. There was a parrot, or summat like it, screeching in the window. The gentleman was in a mortal hurry about the lady’s luggage, and he gave me a handsome present for looking sharp and getting the boxes.”\n\nGetting the boxes! I recollected immediately that Laura’s own account of herself on her arrival in London described her luggage as being collected for her by some person whom Count Fosco brought with him to the station. This was the man.\n\n“Did you see the lady?” I asked. “What did she look like? Was she young or old?”\n\n“Well, sir, what with the hurry and the crowd of people pushing about, I can’t rightly say what the lady looked like. I can’t call nothing to mind about her that I know of excepting her name.”\n\n“You remember her name?”\n\n“Yes, sir. Her name was Lady Glyde.”\n\n“How do you come to remember that, when you have forgotten what she looked like?”\n\nThe man smiled, and shifted his feet in some little embarrassment.\n\n“Why, to tell you the truth, sir,” he said, “I hadn’t been long married at that time, and my wife’s name, before she changed it for mine, was the same as the lady’s—meaning the name of Glyde, sir. The lady mentioned it herself. ‘Is your name on your boxes, ma’am?’ says I. ‘Yes,’ says she, ‘my name is on my luggage—it is Lady Glyde.’ ‘Come!’ I says to myself, ‘I’ve a bad head for gentlefolks’ names in general—but THIS one comes like an old friend, at any rate.’ I can’t say nothing about the time, sir, it might be nigh on a year ago, or it mightn’t. But I can swear to the stout gentleman, and swear to the lady’s name.”\n\nThere was no need that he should remember the time—the date was positively established by his master’s order-book. I felt at once that the means were now in my power of striking down the whole conspiracy at a blow with the irresistible weapon of plain fact. Without a moment’s hesitation, I took the proprietor of the livery stables aside and told him what the real importance was of the evidence of his order-book and the evidence of his driver. An arrangement to compensate him for the temporary loss of the man’s services was easily made, and a copy of the entry in the book was taken by myself, and certified as true by the master’s own signature. I left the livery stables, having settled that John Owen was to hold himself at my disposal for the next three days, or for a longer period if necessity required it.\n\nI now had in my possession all the papers that I wanted—the district registrar’s own copy of the certificate of death, and Sir Percival’s dated letter to the Count, being safe in my pocket-book.\n\nWith this written evidence about me, and with the coachman’s answers fresh in my memory, I next turned my steps, for the first time since the beginning of all my inquiries, in the direction of Mr. Kyrle’s office. One of my objects in paying him this second visit was, necessarily, to tell him what I had done. The other was to warn him of my resolution to take my wife to Limmeridge the next morning, and to have her publicly received and recognised in her uncle’s house. I left it to Mr. Kyrle to decide under these circumstances, and in Mr. Gilmore’s absence, whether he was or was not bound, as the family solicitor, to be present on that occasion in the family interests.\n\nI will say nothing of Mr. Kyrle’s amazement, or of the terms in which he expressed his opinion of my conduct from the first stage of the investigation to the last. It is only necessary to mention that he at once decided on accompanying us to Cumberland.\n\nWe started the next morning by the early train. Laura, Marian, Mr. Kyrle, and myself in one carriage, and John Owen, with a clerk from Mr. Kyrle’s office, occupying places in another. On reaching the Limmeridge station we went first to the farmhouse at Todd’s Corner. It was my firm determination that Laura should not enter her uncle’s house till she appeared there publicly recognised as his niece. I left Marian to settle the question of accommodation with Mrs. Todd, as soon as the good woman had recovered from the bewilderment of hearing what our errand was in Cumberland, and I arranged with her husband that John Owen was to be committed to the ready hospitality of the farm-servants. These preliminaries completed, Mr. Kyrle and I set forth together for Limmeridge House.\n\nI cannot write at any length of our interview with Mr. Fairlie, for I cannot recall it to mind without feelings of impatience and contempt, which make the scene, even in remembrance only, utterly repulsive to me. I prefer to record simply that I carried my point. Mr. Fairlie attempted to treat us on his customary plan. We passed without notice his polite insolence at the outset of the interview. We heard without sympathy the protestations with which he tried next to persuade us that the disclosure of the conspiracy had overwhelmed him. He absolutely whined and whimpered at last like a fretful child. “How was he to know that his niece was alive when he was told that she was dead? He would welcome dear Laura with pleasure, if we would only allow him time to recover. Did we think he looked as if he wanted hurrying into his grave? No. Then, why hurry him?” He reiterated these remonstrances at every available opportunity, until I checked them once for all, by placing him firmly between two inevitable alternatives. I gave him his choice between doing his niece justice on my terms, or facing the consequence of a public assertion of her existence in a court of law. Mr. Kyrle, to whom he turned for help, told him plainly that he must decide the question then and there. Characteristically choosing the alternative which promised soonest to release him from all personal anxiety, he announced with a sudden outburst of energy, that he was not strong enough to bear any more bullying, and that we might do as we pleased.\n\nMr. Kyrle and I at once went downstairs, and agreed upon a form of letter which was to be sent round to the tenants who had attended the false funeral, summoning them, in Mr. Fairlie’s name, to assemble in Limmeridge House on the next day but one. An order referring to the same date was also written, directing a statuary in Carlisle to send a man to Limmeridge churchyard for the purpose of erasing an inscription—Mr. Kyrle, who had arranged to sleep in the house, undertaking that Mr. Fairlie should hear these letters read to him, and should sign them with his own hand.\n\nI occupied the interval day at the farm in writing a plain narrative of the conspiracy, and in adding to it a statement of the practical contradiction which facts offered to the assertion of Laura’s death. This I submitted to Mr. Kyrle before I read it the next day to the assembled tenants. We also arranged the form in which the evidence should be presented at the close of the reading. After these matters were settled, Mr. Kyrle endeavoured to turn the conversation next to Laura’s affairs. Knowing, and desiring to know nothing of those affairs, and doubting whether he would approve, as a man of business, of my conduct in relation to my wife’s life-interest in the legacy left to Madame Fosco, I begged Mr. Kyrle to excuse me if I abstained from discussing the subject. It was connected, as I could truly tell him, with those sorrows and troubles of the past which we never referred to among ourselves, and which we instinctively shrank from discussing with others.\n\nMy last labour, as the evening approached, was to obtain “The Narrative of the Tombstone,” by taking a copy of the false inscription on the grave before it was erased.\n\nThe day came—the day when Laura once more entered the familiar breakfast-room at Limmeridge House. All the persons assembled rose from their seats as Marian and I led her in. A perceptible shock of surprise, an audible murmur of interest ran through them, at the sight of her face. Mr. Fairlie was present (by my express stipulation), with Mr. Kyrle by his side. His valet stood behind him with a smelling-bottle ready in one hand, and a white handkerchief, saturated with eau-de-Cologne, in the other.\n\nI opened the proceedings by publicly appealing to Mr. Fairlie to say whether I appeared there with his authority and under his express sanction. He extended an arm, on either side, to Mr. Kyrle and to his valet—was by them assisted to stand on his legs, and then expressed himself in these terms: “Allow me to present Mr. Hartright. I am as great an invalid as ever, and he is so very obliging as to speak for me. The subject is dreadfully embarrassing. Please hear him, and don’t make a noise!” With those words he slowly sank back again into the chair, and took refuge in his scented pocket-handkerchief.\n\nThe disclosure of the conspiracy followed, after I had offered my preliminary explanation, first of all, in the fewest and the plainest words. I was there present (I informed my hearers) to declare, first, that my wife, then sitting by me, was the daughter of the late Mr. Philip Fairlie; secondly, to prove by positive facts, that the funeral which they had attended in Limmeridge churchyard was the funeral of another woman; thirdly, to give them a plain account of how it had all happened. Without further preface, I at once read the narrative of the conspiracy, describing it in clear outline, and dwelling only upon the pecuniary motive for it, in order to avoid complicating my statement by unnecessary reference to Sir Percival’s secret. This done, I reminded my audience of the date on the inscription in the churchyard (the 25th), and confirmed its correctness by producing the certificate of death. I then read them Sir Percival’s letter of the 25th, announcing his wife’s intended journey from Hampshire to London on the 26th. I next showed that she had taken that journey, by the personal testimony of the driver of the fly, and I proved that she had performed it on the appointed day, by the order-book at the livery stables. Marian then added her own statement of the meeting between Laura and herself at the mad-house, and of her sister’s escape. After which I closed the proceedings by informing the persons present of Sir Percival’s death and of my marriage.\n\nMr. Kyrle rose when I resumed my seat, and declared, as the legal adviser of the family, that my case was proved by the plainest evidence he had ever heard in his life. As he spoke those words, I put my arm round Laura, and raised her so that she was plainly visible to every one in the room. “Are you all of the same opinion?” I asked, advancing towards them a few steps, and pointing to my wife.\n\nThe effect of the question was electrical. Far down at the lower end of the room one of the oldest tenants on the estate started to his feet, and led the rest with him in an instant. I see the man now, with his honest brown face and his iron-grey hair, mounted on the window-seat, waving his heavy riding-whip over his head, and leading the cheers. “There she is, alive and hearty—God bless her! Gi’ it tongue, lads! Gi’ it tongue!” The shout that answered him, reiterated again and again, was the sweetest music I ever heard. The labourers in the village and the boys from the school, assembled on the lawn, caught up the cheering and echoed it back on us. The farmers’ wives clustered round Laura, and struggled which should be first to shake hands with her, and to implore her, with the tears pouring over their own cheeks, to bear up bravely and not to cry. She was so completely overwhelmed, that I was obliged to take her from them, and carry her to the door. There I gave her into Marian’s care—Marian, who had never failed us yet, whose courageous self-control did not fail us now. Left by myself at the door, I invited all the persons present (after thanking them in Laura’s name and mine) to follow me to the churchyard, and see the false inscription struck off the tombstone with their own eyes.\n\nThey all left the house, and all joined the throng of villagers collected round the grave, where the statuary’s man was waiting for us. In a breathless silence, the first sharp stroke of the steel sounded on the marble. Not a voice was heard—not a soul moved, till those three words, “Laura, Lady Glyde,” had vanished from sight. Then there was a great heave of relief among the crowd, as if they felt that the last fetters of the conspiracy had been struck off Laura herself, and the assembly slowly withdrew. It was late in the day before the whole inscription was erased. One line only was afterwards engraved in its place: “Anne Catherick, July 25th, 1850.”\n\nI returned to Limmeridge House early enough in the evening to take leave of Mr. Kyrle. He and his clerk, and the driver of the fly, went back to London by the night train. On their departure an insolent message was delivered to me from Mr. Fairlie—who had been carried from the room in a shattered condition, when the first outbreak of cheering answered my appeal to the tenantry. The message conveyed to us “Mr. Fairlie’s best congratulations,” and requested to know whether “we contemplated stopping in the house.” I sent back word that the only object for which we had entered his doors was accomplished—that I contemplated stopping in no man’s house but my own—and that Mr. Fairlie need not entertain the slightest apprehension of ever seeing us or hearing from us again. We went back to our friends at the farm to rest that night, and the next morning—escorted to the station, with the heartiest enthusiasm and good will, by the whole village and by all the farmers in the neighbourhood—we returned to London.\n\nAs our view of the Cumberland hills faded in the distance, I thought of the first disheartening circumstances under which the long struggle that was now past and over had been pursued. It was strange to look back and to see, now, that the poverty which had denied us all hope of assistance had been the indirect means of our success, by forcing me to act for myself. If we had been rich enough to find legal help, what would have been the result? The gain (on Mr. Kyrle’s own showing) would have been more than doubtful—the loss, judging by the plain test of events as they had really happened, certain. The law would never have obtained me my interview with Mrs. Catherick. The law would never have made Pesca the means of forcing a confession from the Count.\n\n# II\n\nTwo more events remain to be added to the chain before it reaches fairly from the outset of the story to the close.\n\nWhile our new sense of freedom from the long oppression of the past was still strange to us, I was sent for by the friend who had given me my first employment in wood engraving, to receive from him a fresh testimony of his regard for my welfare. He had been commissioned by his employers to go to Paris, and to examine for them a fresh discovery in the practical application of his Art, the merits of which they were anxious to ascertain. His own engagements had not allowed him leisure time to undertake the errand, and he had most kindly suggested that it should be transferred to me. I could have no hesitation in thankfully accepting the offer, for if I acquitted myself of my commission as I hoped I should, the result would be a permanent engagement on the illustrated newspaper, to which I was now only occasionally attached.\n\nI received my instructions and packed up for the journey the next day. On leaving Laura once more (under what changed circumstances!) in her sister’s care, a serious consideration recurred to me, which had more than once crossed my wife’s mind, as well as my own, already—I mean the consideration of Marian’s future. Had we any right to let our selfish affection accept the devotion of all that generous life? Was it not our duty, our best expression of gratitude, to forget ourselves, and to think only of HER? I tried to say this when we were alone for a moment, before I went away. She took my hand, and silenced me at the first words.\n\n“After all that we three have suffered together,” she said, “there can be no parting between us till the last parting of all. My heart and my happiness, Walter, are with Laura and you. Wait a little till there are children’s voices at your fireside. I will teach them to speak for me in THEIR language, and the first lesson they say to their father and mother shall be—We can’t spare our aunt!”\n\nMy journey to Paris was not undertaken alone. At the eleventh hour Pesca decided that he would accompany me. He had not recovered his customary cheerfulness since the night at the Opera, and he determined to try what a week’s holiday would do to raise his spirits.\n\nI performed the errand entrusted to me, and drew out the necessary report, on the fourth day from our arrival in Paris. The fifth day I arranged to devote to sight-seeing and amusements in Pesca’s company.\n\nOur hotel had been too full to accommodate us both on the same floor. My room was on the second story, and Pesca’s was above me, on the third. On the morning of the fifth day I went upstairs to see if the Professor was ready to go out. Just before I reached the landing I saw his door opened from the inside—a long, delicate, nervous hand (not my friend’s hand certainly) held it ajar. At the same time I heard Pesca’s voice saying eagerly, in low tones, and in his own language—“I remember the name, but I don’t know the man. You saw at the Opera he was so changed that I could not recognise him. I will forward the report—I can do no more.” “No more need be done,” answered the second voice. The door opened wide, and the light-haired man with the scar on his cheek—the man I had seen following Count Fosco’s cab a week before—came out. He bowed as I drew aside to let him pass—his face was fearfully pale—and he held fast by the banisters as he descended the stairs.\n\nI pushed open the door and entered Pesca’s room. He was crouched up, in the strangest manner, in a corner of the sofa. He seemed to shrink from me when I approached him.\n\n“Am I disturbing you?” I asked. “I did not know you had a friend with you till I saw him come out.”\n\n“No friend,” said Pesca eagerly. “I see him to-day for the first time and the last.”\n\n“I am afraid he has brought you bad news?”\n\n“Horrible news, Walter! Let us go back to London—I don’t want to stop here—I am sorry I ever came. The misfortunes of my youth are very hard upon me,” he said, turning his face to the wall, “very hard upon me in my later time. I try to forget them—and they will not forget ME!”\n\n“We can’t return, I am afraid, before the afternoon,” I replied. “Would you like to come out with me in the meantime?”\n\n“No, my friend, I will wait here. But let us go back to-day—pray let us go back.”\n\nI left him with the assurance that he should leave Paris that afternoon. We had arranged the evening before to ascend the Cathedral of Notre Dame, with Victor Hugo’s noble romance for our guide. There was nothing in the French capital that I was more anxious to see, and I departed by myself for the church.\n\nApproaching Notre Dame by the river-side, I passed on my way the terrible dead-house of Paris—the Morgue. A great crowd clamoured and heaved round the door. There was evidently something inside which excited the popular curiosity, and fed the popular appetite for horror.\n\nI should have walked on to the church if the conversation of two men and a woman on the outskirts of the crowd had not caught my ear. They had just come out from seeing the sight in the Morgue, and the account they were giving of the dead body to their neighbours described it as the corpse of a man—a man of immense size, with a strange mark on his left arm.\n\nThe moment those words reached me I stopped and took my place with the crowd going in. Some dim foreshadowing of the truth had crossed my mind when I heard Pesca’s voice through the open door, and when I saw the stranger’s face as he passed me on the stairs of the hotel. Now the truth itself was revealed to me—revealed in the chance words that had just reached my ears. Other vengeance than mine had followed that fated man from the theatre to his own door—from his own door to his refuge in Paris. Other vengeance than mine had called him to the day of reckoning, and had exacted from him the penalty of his life. The moment when I had pointed him out to Pesca at the theatre in the hearing of that stranger by our side, who was looking for him too—was the moment that sealed his doom. I remembered the struggle in my own heart, when he and I stood face to face—the struggle before I could let him escape me—and shuddered as I recalled it.\n\nSlowly, inch by inch, I pressed in with the crowd, moving nearer and nearer to the great glass screen that parts the dead from the living at the Morgue—nearer and nearer, till I was close behind the front row of spectators, and could look in.\n\nThere he lay, unowned, unknown, exposed to the flippant curiosity of a French mob! There was the dreadful end of that long life of degraded ability and heartless crime! Hushed in the sublime repose of death, the broad, firm, massive face and head fronted us so grandly that the chattering Frenchwomen about me lifted their hands in admiration, and cried in shrill chorus, “Ah, what a handsome man!” The wound that had killed him had been struck with a knife or dagger exactly over his heart. No other traces of violence appeared about the body except on the left arm, and there, exactly in the place where I had seen the brand on Pesca’s arm, were two deep cuts in the shape of the letter T, which entirely obliterated the mark of the Brotherhood. His clothes, hung above him, showed that he had been himself conscious of his danger—they were clothes that had disguised him as a French artisan. For a few moments, but not for longer, I forced myself to see these things through the glass screen. I can write of them at no greater length, for I saw no more.\n\nThe few facts in connection with his death which I subsequently ascertained (partly from Pesca and partly from other sources), may be stated here before the subject is dismissed from these pages.\n\nHis body was taken out of the Seine in the disguise which I have described, nothing being found on him which revealed his name, his rank, or his place of abode. The hand that struck him was never traced, and the circumstances under which he was killed were never discovered. I leave others to draw their own conclusions in reference to the secret of the assassination as I have drawn mine. When I have intimated that the foreigner with the scar was a member of the Brotherhood (admitted in Italy after Pesca’s departure from his native country), and when I have further added that the two cuts, in the form of a T, on the left arm of the dead man, signified the Italian word “Traditore,” and showed that justice had been done by the Brotherhood on a traitor, I have contributed all that I know towards elucidating the mystery of Count Fosco’s death.\n\nThe body was identified the day after I had seen it by means of an anonymous letter addressed to his wife. He was buried by Madame Fosco in the cemetery of Père la Chaise. Fresh funeral wreaths continue to this day to be hung on the ornamental bronze railings round the tomb by the Countess’s own hand. She lives in the strictest retirement at Versailles. Not long since she published a biography of her deceased husband. The work throws no light whatever on the name that was really his own or on the secret history of his life—it is almost entirely devoted to the praise of his domestic virtues, the assertion of his rare abilities, and the enumeration of the honours conferred on him. The circumstances attending his death are very briefly noticed, and are summed up on the last page in this sentence—“His life was one long assertion of the rights of the aristocracy and the sacred principles of Order, and he died a martyr to his cause.”\n\n# III\n\nThe summer and autumn passed after my return from Paris, and brought no changes with them which need be noticed here. We lived so simply and quietly that the income which I was now steadily earning sufficed for all our wants.\n\nIn the February of the new year our first child was born—a son. My mother and sister and Mrs. Vesey were our guests at the little christening party, and Mrs. Clements was present to assist my wife on the same occasion. Marian was our boy’s godmother, and Pesca and Mr. Gilmore (the latter acting by proxy) were his godfathers. I may add here that when Mr. Gilmore returned to us a year later he assisted the design of these pages, at my request, by writing the Narrative which appears early in the story under his name, and which, though first in order of precedence, was thus, in order of time, the last that I received.\n\nThe only event in our lives which now remains to be recorded, occurred when our little Walter was six months old.\n\nAt that time I was sent to Ireland to make sketches for certain forthcoming illustrations in the newspaper to which I was attached. I was away for nearly a fortnight, corresponding regularly with my wife and Marian, except during the last three days of my absence, when my movements were too uncertain to enable me to receive letters. I performed the latter part of my journey back at night, and when I reached home in the morning, to my utter astonishment there was no one to receive me. Laura and Marian and the child had left the house on the day before my return.\n\nA note from my wife, which was given to me by the servant, only increased my surprise, by informing me that they had gone to Limmeridge House. Marian had prohibited any attempt at written explanations—I was entreated to follow them the moment I came back—complete enlightenment awaited me on my arrival in Cumberland—and I was forbidden to feel the slightest anxiety in the meantime. There the note ended. It was still early enough to catch the morning train. I reached Limmeridge House the same afternoon.\n\nMy wife and Marian were both upstairs. They had established themselves (by way of completing my amazement) in the little room which had been once assigned to me for a studio, when I was employed on Mr. Fairlie’s drawings. On the very chair which I used to occupy when I was at work Marian was sitting now, with the child industriously sucking his coral upon her lap—while Laura was standing by the well-remembered drawing-table which I had so often used, with the little album that I had filled for her in past times open under her hand.\n\n“What in the name of heaven has brought you here?” I asked. “Does Mr. Fairlie know——?”\n\nMarian suspended the question on my lips by telling me that Mr. Fairlie was dead. He had been struck by paralysis, and had never rallied after the shock. Mr. Kyrle had informed them of his death, and had advised them to proceed immediately to Limmeridge House.\n\nSome dim perception of a great change dawned on my mind. Laura spoke before I had quite realised it. She stole close to me to enjoy the surprise which was still expressed in my face.\n\n“My darling Walter,” she said, “must we really account for our boldness in coming here? I am afraid, love, I can only explain it by breaking through our rule, and referring to the past.”\n\n“There is not the least necessity for doing anything of the kind,” said Marian. “We can be just as explicit, and much more interesting, by referring to the future.” She rose and held up the child kicking and crowing in her arms. “Do you know who this is, Walter?” she asked, with bright tears of happiness gathering in her eyes.\n\n“Even MY bewilderment has its limits,” I replied. “I think I can still answer for knowing my own child.”\n\n“Child!” she exclaimed, with all her easy gaiety of old times. “Do you talk in that familiar manner of one of the landed gentry of England? Are you aware, when I present this illustrious baby to your notice, in whose presence you stand? Evidently not! Let me make two eminent personages known to one another: Mr. Walter Hartright—THE HEIR OF LIMMERIDGE.”\n\nSo she spoke. In writing those last words, I have written all. The pen falters in my hand. The long, happy labour of many months is over. Marian was the good angel of our lives—let Marian end our Story.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-583"]="(클레멘츠 여관 소속, 그림 교사)\n\n이것은 한 여인의 인내가 무엇을 견뎌 낼 수 있는지, 그리고 한 남자의 결의가 무엇을 이루어 낼 수 있는지에 관한 이야기다.\n\n만일 법이라는 기계가 모든 의혹의 사건을 낱낱이 헤아리고 모든 심리의 과정을 이끌어 가되 황금이라는 기름의 매끄러운 힘을 그저 적당히만 빌리면 되는 것이었다면, 이 지면을 채우는 사건들도 법정에서 세인의 관심을 얼마간 차지했을는지 모른다.\n\n그러나 법은 어떤 피할 수 없는 경우에는 여전히 두둑한 지갑에 미리 고용된 하인이다. 그리하여 이 이야기는 여기 이 자리에서 처음으로 이야기되기에 이르렀다. 판사가 한때 그것을 들었을 법한 그대로, 이제 독자가 그것을 들으리라. 이 폭로의 처음부터 끝까지, 중요한 정황은 어느 하나도 전해 들은 증언에 기대어 서술되지 않을 것이다. 이 서두를 적는 사람(이름은 월터 하트라이트)이 기록될 사건들과 남들보다 더 가까이 얽혀 있는 대목에서는, 그가 몸소 그것을 서술할 것이다. 그의 겪음이 미치지 못하는 데 이르면 그는 서술자의 자리에서 물러날 것이며, 그가 멈춘 지점에서부터 그 소임은 문제된 정황을 제 지식으로 증언할 수 있는 다른 이들이—그가 그들에 앞서 그러했듯이 그만큼 명료하고 분명하게—이어 갈 것이다.\n\n그리하여 여기 제시되는 이야기는 한 자루 이상의 펜으로 이야기될 것이니, 이는 법을 어긴 죄의 이야기가 법정에서 한 사람 이상의 증인에 의해 이야기되는 것과 같다. 두 경우 모두 그 목적은 하나다—진실을 언제나 가장 직접적이고 가장 알기 쉬운 모습으로 드러내는 것, 그리고 사건들과 가장 가까이 얽혀 있던 이들로 하여금 저마다의 단계에서 제가 겪은 바를 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 이야기하게 함으로써 하나의 완결된 일련의 사건이 흘러간 자취를 따라가는 것이다.\n\n그림 교사이며 스물여덟 살 된 월터 하트라이트의 말을 먼저 듣기로 하자.\n\n# II\n\n7월의 마지막 날이었다. 길고 무더웠던 여름이 저물어 가고 있었다. 런던 포도(鋪道)의 지친 순례자인 우리는 옥수수밭에 드리운 구름 그림자와 바닷가의 가을 바람을 떠올리기 시작하던 참이었다.\n\n내 딱한 처지로 말하자면, 스러져 가는 여름은 나를 건강도 잃고 기운도 잃고, 사실대로 말하자면 돈까지 잃은 꼴로 남겨 놓았다. 지난 한 해 나는 여느 때만큼 내 직업상의 밑천을 알뜰히 건사하지 못했다. 그리하여 내 헤픈 씀씀이는 이제 나를, 햄스테드에 있는 어머니의 오두막과 시내에 있는 내 하숙방을 오가며 가을을 검소하게 보내야 하는 처지로 몰아넣고 있었다.\n\n그날 저녁은, 내 기억에, 고요하고 흐렸다. 런던의 공기는 더할 나위 없이 무거웠고, 멀리서 들려오는 거리 마차 소리는 더없이 희미했다. 내 안의 작은 생명의 맥박과 나를 둘러싼 도시의 큰 심장은, 저무는 해와 발맞추어 나른하게, 더욱더 나른하게 가라앉는 듯했다. 나는 읽는다기보다는 오히려 그 위에서 꿈을 꾸고 있던 책에서 정신을 차리고, 교외의 서늘한 밤공기를 쐬러 하숙방을 나섰다. 그날은 내가 어머니와 누이와 함께 지내곤 하던 일주일에 두 번의 저녁 가운데 하루였다. 그래서 나는 햄스테드 쪽으로, 북쪽을 향해 발걸음을 돌렸다.\n\n앞으로 이야기해야 할 사건들 때문에 여기서 밝혀 둘 필요가 있는데, 내가 지금 적고 있는 그 무렵에 내 아버지는 이미 몇 해 전에 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 누이 세라와 나는 다섯 남매 가운데 유일하게 살아남은 둘이었다. 내 아버지는 나에 앞서 그림 선생이었다. 그의 노고는 그를 그 직업에서 크게 성공시켰다. 그리고 제 노동에 기대어 살아가는 이들의 앞날을 마련해 주려는 그 애틋한 근심은, 결혼한 때부터 그로 하여금 대개의 사람들이 그런 목적에 떼어 두어야 한다고 여기는 것보다 훨씬 많은 몫의 수입을 생명 보험에 붓게 만들었다. 그의 훌륭한 신중함과 극기 덕분에 어머니와 누이는 아버지가 세상을 떠난 뒤에도 그가 살아 있을 때와 다름없이 세상에 아쉬울 것 없이 남겨졌다. 나는 아버지의 고객을 물려받았고, 인생의 출발점에서 나를 기다리는 앞날에 감사할 온갖 이유가 있었다.\n\n고요한 황혼이 아직 히스 들판의 가장 높은 등성이 위에서 떨리고 있었고, 발아래 런던의 풍경은 흐린 밤의 그늘 속 검은 심연으로 가라앉아 있었다. 그때 나는 어머니의 오두막 문 앞에 서 있었다. 초인종을 채 울리기도 전에 집 문이 거칠게 열렸다. 하인이 있어야 할 자리에 내 훌륭한 이탈리아인 친구 페스카 교수가 나타나더니, 영국식 환호를 날카로운 이국풍으로 흉내 내면서 반가이 나를 맞으러 뛰쳐나왔다.\n\n교수는 자기 자신을 위해서도, 그리고 덧붙이는 것을 허락받자면 나를 위해서도, 정식으로 소개될 영예를 누릴 만하다. 우연이 그를, 이 지면이 펼쳐 보이고자 하는 그 기이한 한 집안 이야기의 출발점으로 만들어 놓았기 때문이다.\n\n내가 이 이탈리아인 친구와 처음 알게 된 것은, 그가 제 나라 말을 가르치고 내가 그림을 가르치던 어떤 대저택들에서 그를 만나면서였다. 그때 내가 그의 생애에 대해 아는 것이라고는, 그가 한때 파도바 대학교에서 어떤 자리를 맡았었다는 것, 정치적인 까닭으로(그 까닭의 성격을 그는 누구에게도 한결같이 말하기를 꺼렸다) 이탈리아를 떠났다는 것, 그리고 여러 해 동안 런던에서 어학 선생으로 버젓이 자리 잡고 있었다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n실제로 난쟁이는 아니면서도—머리끝부터 발끝까지 몸의 균형은 더할 나위 없이 잘 잡혀 있었으니까—페스카는, 내 생각에, 구경거리 방을 벗어난 곳에서 내가 본 사람 가운데 가장 작은 인간이었다. 어디서나 그 생김새로 눈길을 끄는 그는, 그 성격의 해롭잖은 별남으로 인해 뭇사람들 사이에서 한층 더 두드러졌다. 그의 삶을 지배하는 생각은, 자기에게 피난처와 살아갈 방편을 마련해 준 이 나라에 대한 고마움을, 있는 힘을 다해 스스로 영국인이 됨으로써 나타내야 한다는 것인 듯했다. 언제나 우산을 지니고 다니고 언제나 각반과 흰 모자를 걸침으로써 이 국민 전체에게 경의를 표하는 것만으로는 만족하지 못하고, 교수는 생김새뿐 아니라 습관과 놀이에서까지 영국인이 되기를 열망했다. 우리 국민이 운동 경기를 좋아하는 것으로 두드러진다는 것을 알아채자, 그 작은 사내는 순진한 마음에서, 함께 낄 기회만 생기면 우리 영국의 온갖 운동과 놀이에 즉흥적으로 몸을 던졌다. 우리의 국민적 각반과 국민적 흰 모자를 받아들였던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 우리의 국민적 야외 놀이도 의지의 힘만으로 받아들일 수 있으리라 굳게 믿으면서.\n\n나는 그가 여우 사냥에서, 또 크리켓 경기장에서 앞뒤 없이 제 팔다리를 내걸고 덤비는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 나는 그가 브라이턴의 바다에서 꼭 그만큼 앞뒤 없이 제 목숨을 내거는 것을 보았다.\n\n우리는 그곳에서 우연히 만나 함께 해수욕을 하고 있었다. 만일 우리가 내 나라에만 있는 어떤 운동에 열중하고 있었다면 나는 물론 페스카를 조심스레 지켜보았을 것이다. 그러나 외국인들도 대개 물속에서는 영국인 못지않게 제 몸을 건사할 줄 아는 법이라, 헤엄치는 재주가 교수가 즉흥적으로 배울 수 있다고 믿는 사내다운 운동 목록에 하나를 더 보탤 뿐일지도 모른다는 생각은 나에게 떠오르지 않았다. 둘이 함께 물가에서 헤엄쳐 나간 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 친구가 나를 따라오지 못하는 것을 알아채고 멈춰 서서 그를 찾으려 돌아보았다. 소스라치고 놀랍게도, 나와 바닷가 사이에는 잠깐 수면 위로 버둥거리다 이내 시야에서 사라지는 두 개의 작고 하얀 팔밖에 보이는 것이 없었다. 내가 그를 향해 잠수해 들어가 보니, 딱한 작은 사내는 자갈이 우묵하게 팬 바닥에 얌전히 웅크린 채, 내가 전에 본 어느 때보다도 몇 곱절이나 작아 보이는 모습으로 누워 있었다. 그를 끌어올리는 몇 분 사이에 공기가 그를 되살아나게 했고, 그는 내 부축을 받아 탈의 기계의 계단을 올랐다. 그의 생기가 얼마간 돌아오자 헤엄에 대한 그 놀라운 착각도 되돌아왔다. 달그락거리는 이가 말을 허락하자마자 그는 멍하니 웃으며, 그건 틀림없이 쥐가 났던 탓이었을 거라고 말했다.\n\n말끔히 정신을 차리고 바닷가에서 나에게 합류했을 때, 그의 따뜻한 남국의 천성은 삽시간에 온갖 인위적인 영국식 절제를 뚫고 터져 나왔다. 그는 더없이 격렬한 애정의 표현으로 나를 뒤덮었고—과장된 이탈리아식으로 열렬히 외치기를, 이제부터 제 목숨을 내 뜻대로 맡기겠노라 했으며—내가 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 만한 어떤 봉사로 제 고마움을 증명할 기회를 찾기 전에는 다시는 결코 행복해질 수 없으리라 단언했다.\n\n나는 이 모험 전체를 한낱 농담거리로만 다루기를 고집함으로써 그의 눈물과 다짐의 격류를 막으려 애썼다. 그리고 마침내, 내가 여기기로는, 나에 대한 페스카의 벅찬 은혜감을 얼마간 덜어 주는 데 성공했다. 그때 나는 거의 생각지 못했다—그 뒤 우리의 즐거운 휴가가 끝나 갈 무렵에도 거의 생각지 못했다—내 고마운 벗이 그토록 애타게 바라던, 나를 도울 그 기회가 머지않아 찾아오리라는 것을, 그가 그 순간 그것을 냉큼 붙잡으리라는 것을, 그리고 그렇게 함으로써 그가 내 삶의 물줄기 전체를 새로운 물길로 돌려놓고 나를 나 자신조차 거의 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 바꿔 놓으리라는 것을.\n\n그러나 사실이 그러했다. 만일 내가 자갈 바닥 물속에 누운 페스카 교수를 향해 잠수해 들어가지 않았더라면, 나는 아마 십중팔구 이 지면이 이야기할 사연에 결코 얽히지 않았을 것이다—어쩌면 나는, 내 온 생각 속에 살아 있고 내 온 기운을 사로잡았으며 이제 내 삶의 목적을 이끄는 유일한 길잡이가 된 그 여인의 이름조차 결코 듣지 못했을 것이다.\n\n# III\n\n어머니의 대문 앞에서 우리가 마주쳤던 그날 저녁, 페스카의 얼굴과 태도는 무언가 심상찮은 일이 일어났음을 나에게 알려 주기에 충분하고도 남았다. 그러나 당장 설명을 청하는 것은 아무 소용이 없었다. 그가 두 손으로 나를 안으로 끌어들이는 동안, 나는 다만 이렇게 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이었다—(내 버릇을 아는지라) 그가 그날 밤 나를 꼭 만나려고 오두막에 찾아왔다는 것, 그리고 여느 때와 달리 유난히 반가운 종류의 어떤 소식을 전하려 한다는 것을.\n\n우리 둘은 몹시 급작스럽고 위엄이라고는 없는 꼴로 응접실에 뛰어들었다. 어머니는 열린 창가에 앉아 웃으며 부채질을 하고 계셨다. 페스카는 어머니가 각별히 아끼는 이들 가운데 하나였고, 그의 가장 별난 기행조차도 어머니의 눈에는 언제나 용서할 만한 것이었다. 가엾고 다정한 분! 그 작은 교수가 당신 아들에게 깊이, 고마운 마음으로 정을 붙이고 있음을 알아챈 첫 순간부터, 어머니는 그에게 아낌없이 마음을 열었고, 그의 알쏭달쏭한 이국의 별스러움을 하나하나 이해해 보려는 시도조차 없이 있는 그대로 받아들였다.\n\n내 누이 세라는 젊음이 주는 온갖 이점을 지녔으면서도, 이상하게도 덜 너그러웠다. 세라는 페스카의 훌륭한 심성을 마땅히 인정했다. 그러나 어머니가 나를 위해 그를 받아들이듯 무조건 받아들이지는 못했다. 겉모양 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 페스카의 타고난 태도에 세라의 섬나라식 예법 관념이 끊임없이 반기를 들었고, 그 별난 작은 외국인을 어머니가 스스럼없이 대하는 것에 세라는 늘 얼마쯤 대놓고 놀라워했다. 나는 내 누이의 경우뿐 아니라 다른 이들의 경우에서도 이런 것을 눈여겨보았다—우리 젊은 세대는 우리 윗세대 어떤 이들만큼 정겹지도, 충동적이지도 않다는 것을. 나는 노인들이 어떤 기대되는 즐거움을 앞두고 얼굴이 상기되어 들뜬 모습을 늘 보는데, 정작 그 즐거움은 그들의 차분한 손자 손녀의 태연함을 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 못한다. 궁금하다, 우리는 우리 어른들이 그들 시절에 그러했던 만큼 지금도 그토록 참된 소년 소녀들일까? 교육의 그 대단한 진보가 걸음을 너무 크게 내디딘 것은 아닐까, 그리고 이 근대의 나날에 우리는 이 세상 어느 것보다도 아주 조금은 지나치게 곱게 자란 것은 아닐까?\n\n그 물음들에 딱 잘라 답해 보려는 것은 아니지만, 적어도 이것만은 적어 둘 수 있다—나는 어머니와 누이가 페스카와 어울려 함께 있는 모습을 볼 때마다, 언제나 어머니 쪽이 둘 가운데 훨씬 젊은 여인임을 발견하곤 했다. 이를테면 이번에도, 우리가 소년처럼 응접실로 굴러 들어온 꼴을 두고 늙은 어머니가 마음껏 웃고 계시는 동안, 세라는 교수가 나를 문간에서 맞으려 서둘러 다가오다 탁자에서 떨어뜨린 찻잔의 깨진 조각들을 심란하게 주워 모으고 있었다.\n\n“무슨 일이 났을지 모르겠구나, 월터.” 어머니가 말했다. “네가 조금만 더 늦었더라면 말이다. 페스카는 조바심에 반쯤 실성했고, 나는 궁금증에 반쯤 실성했단다. 교수가 무언가 놀라운 소식을 가지고 왔는데, 그게 너와 관계있다지 뭐냐. 그런데 이 야박한 양반이 친구 월터가 나타나기 전에는 눈곱만 한 귀띔조차 해 주지 않겠다는 거야.”\n\n“약도 올라라, 짝이 안 맞게 됐잖아.” 세라가 깨진 잔의 잔해 위로 서글피 마음을 쏟은 채 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다.\n\n이런 말들이 오가는 동안, 페스카는 도자기가 제 손에 당한 돌이킬 수 없는 참사를 다행히도 안달스레 모른 채, 우리 셋 모두를 마주 볼 수 있도록—청중을 향해 연설하는 웅변가의 자세로—커다란 안락의자 하나를 방 반대편 끝으로 끌어가고 있었다. 등받이를 우리 쪽으로 돌려놓은 그는 무릎으로 의자에 뛰어 올라, 즉석 설교단에서 세 사람의 작은 회중을 향해 들뜬 목소리로 말을 건넸다.\n\n“자, 나의 좋은 분들이여.” 페스카가 입을 열었다(그는 ‘귀한 벗들’을 뜻할 때면 언제나 ‘좋은 분들’이라 했다). “내 말을 들으시오. 때가 왔소이다—내 좋은 소식을 읊겠소—드디어 내가 말하겠소.”\n\n“옳소, 옳소!” 어머니가 그 익살에 장단을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n“다음에 저이가 깰 건요, 엄마.” 세라가 속삭였다. “저 제일 좋은 안락의자 등받이일걸요.”\n\n“나는 내 지난 삶으로 돌아가, 창조된 존재 가운데 가장 고귀한 이에게 말을 거오.” 페스카는 의자 맨 윗살 너머로 이 변변찮은 나를 향해 격하게 부르짖으며 이어 갔다. “누가 (쥐가 나서) 바다 밑에 죽어 있는 나를 발견했소이까? 그리고 누가 나를 물 위로 끌어올렸소이까? 그리고 내가 내 목숨과 내 옷을 되찾았을 때 나는 무어라 말했소이까?”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리를 너무 많이 했지.” 나는 되도록 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 이 화제에 조금이라도 부추김을 주면 어김없이 교수의 감정이 눈물의 홍수로 터져 나오기 때문이었다.\n\n“나는 말했소.” 페스카가 굽히지 않고 이어 갔다. “내 목숨은 남은 평생 내 소중한 벗 월터의 것이라고—과연 그러하오. 나는 말했소, 월터를 위해 무언가 좋은 일을 할 기회를 찾기 전에는 결코 다시 행복해질 수 없으리라고—그리고 나는 이 더없이 복된 날이 오기까지 나 자신에게 흡족한 적이 한 번도 없었소. 그런데 이제.” 열정에 찬 작은 사내가 목청을 한껏 높여 외쳤다. “차고 넘치는 행복이 땀처럼 내 살갗의 땀구멍마다에서 솟구쳐 나오오. 내 믿음과 영혼과 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 드디어 그 무언가가 이루어졌으니, 이제 할 말은 오직 하나—좋아-다-좋아!”\n\n여기서 밝혀 둘 필요가 있겠다—페스카는 옷차림과 몸가짐과 놀이에서뿐 아니라 말투에서도 완벽한 영국인임을 자랑스러워했다. 우리의 가장 흔한 회화 표현 몇 가지를 주워 담은 그는 생각날 때마다 그것들을 대화에 흩뿌렸는데, 그 소리를 몹시 즐기면서도 뜻은 대체로 알지 못한 채, 제멋대로 합성어와 되풀이 표현으로 바꾸어 놓고, 마치 하나의 긴 음절이라도 되는 양 늘 서로 이어 붙여 말하곤 했다.\n\n“내가 내 조국의 말을 가르치는 런던의 고급 저택들 가운데.” 교수는 더는 서두 없이 오래 미뤄 둔 설명으로 돌진했다. “포틀랜드라 불리는 큰 동네에 아주 근사한 집이 하나 있소. 그게 어딘지들 아시지요? 그럼, 그럼—물론-물론이고말고. 그 근사한 집에는, 나의 좋은 분들이여, 안에 근사한 식구가 들어 있소. 곱고 통통한 마마 한 분, 곱고 통통한 젊은 아가씨 셋, 곱고 통통한 젊은 도련님 둘, 그리고 그 가운데 가장 곱고 가장 통통한 파파 한 분—눈까지 황금에 파묻힌 대단한 상인인데, 한때는 근사한 사내였으나 벗어진 머리와 두 겹 턱을 보건대 지금은 더 이상 근사하지 않소. 자, 잘 들으시오! 나는 젊은 아가씨들에게 숭고한 단테를 가르치는데, 아!—오-내-영혼에-축복이!—숭고한 단테가 그 셋의 어여쁜 머리를 얼마나 헤매게 하는지는 사람의 말로 다 할 수가 없소이다! 상관없소—다 때가 되면 되는 법이고—수업이 많을수록 나에게는 좋은 일이오. 자, 잘 들으시오! 내가 오늘도 여느 때처럼 젊은 아가씨들을 가르치고 있다 상상해 보시오. 우리 넷은 다 함께 단테의 지옥으로 내려가 있소. 제7옥(獄)에서—아니 그건 아무래도 좋소. 곱고 통통한 세 아가씨에게는 어느 옥이나 매한가지니까—어쨌든 제7옥에서 내 제자들이 딱 막혀 버렸소. 그래서 나는 그들을 다시 나아가게 하려고 읊고, 풀이하고, 부질없는 열정으로 벌겋게 달아올라 나 자신을 부풀리고 있는데—바깥 복도에서 삐걱 하는 구두 소리가 나더니, 벗어진 머리에 두 겹 턱을 한 그 대단한 상인, 황금빛 파파가 들어오는 게 아니겠소.—하! 나의 좋은 분들이여, 이제 나는 당신들이 생각하는 것보다 본론에 더 가까이 와 있소. 여태 참을성 있게 들으셨소? 아니면 속으로 이랬소? ‘제기랄-이런-제기랄! 페스카가 오늘 밤 말이 참 길구먼’ 하고?”\n\n우리는 깊이 흥미로워하고 있노라 밝혔다. 교수는 이어 갔다.\n\n“황금빛 파파의 손에는 편지 한 통이 들려 있소. 그리고 집안의 그 예사롭고 속된 볼일로 우리의 지옥에서 우리를 방해하는 데 대해 양해를 구한 뒤, 그는 세 젊은 아가씨에게 몸을 돌려, 당신네 영국인이 이 복된 세상에서 무슨 할 말이든 시작할 때 늘 그러하듯, 거창한 오오 소리로 말을 뗐소. ‘오, 얘들아.’ 대단한 상인이 말하오. ‘여기 내 친구 미스터——의 편지가 한 통 왔구나.’ (이름은 내 머릿속에서 미끄러져 나갔소만, 상관없소. 다시 돌아올 테니. 그럼, 그럼—좋아-다-좋아.) 그러니까 파파가 말하오. ‘내 친구 미스터의 편지가 왔는데, 이이가 시골에 있는 제 집으로 내려갈 그림 선생을 하나 나더러 추천해 달라는구나.’ 오-내-영혼에-축복이! 황금빛 파파가 그 말을 하는 걸 들었을 때, 내가 그이에게 닿을 만큼 컸더라면 나는 그이의 목에 두 팔을 두르고 오래도록 고마운 포옹으로 그이를 내 가슴에 끌어안았을 것이오! 실은 그저 의자에서 튀어 올랐을 뿐이지만. 내 자리는 가시방석이었고 내 영혼은 말하고 싶어 불타올랐으나, 나는 혀를 붙들고 파파가 계속하게 두었소. ‘너희 혹시.’ 이 돈 많은 선량한 양반이 친구의 편지를 황금 같은 손가락과 엄지로 이리저리 만지작거리며 말하오. ‘너희 혹시, 얘들아, 내가 추천할 만한 그림 선생을 아느냐?’ 세 젊은 아가씨가 서로를 바라보더니, (없어서는 안 될 거창한 오오로 시작해) 말하오. ‘오, 저런, 아버지! 하지만 여기 페스카 씨가——’ 내 이름이 나오자 나는 더는 참을 수 없었소—당신들 생각이, 나의 좋은 분들이여, 피처럼 내 머리로 솟구쳐 올랐소—나는 마치 땅에서 못이 솟아 의자 밑바닥을 뚫고 올라오기라도 한 듯 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 그 대단한 상인에게 몸을 돌려 (영어 표현으로) 말하오. ‘친애하는 선생, 나에게 그 사람이 있소이다! 세상에서 첫째가고 으뜸가는 그림 선생 말이오! 오늘 밤 우편으로 그를 추천하시고, 짐 보따리째(또 영어 표현이오—하!), 짐 보따리째 내일 기차로 그를 떠나보내시오!’ ‘잠깐, 잠깐.’ 파파가 말하오. ‘그이가 외국인이오, 영국인이오?’ ‘등뼈까지 영국인이오.’ 내가 대답하오. ‘점잖은 사람이고?’ 파파가 말하오. ‘선생.’ 내가 말하오(그의 이 마지막 질문이 나를 모욕한지라, 나는 그이에게 스스럼없이 굴기를 그만두었소이다). ‘선생! 천재의 불멸의 불꽃이 이 영국인의 가슴에 타오르오. 게다가 그의 아버지도 그에 앞서 그것을 지녔었소!’ ‘아무려면 어떻소.’ 이 황금빛 야만인 파파가 말하오. ‘그 천재성일랑 아무래도 좋소, 페스카 씨. 이 나라에서는 점잖음이 함께 따라오지 않는 한 천재는 필요 없소이다—따라온다면야 매우 기꺼이 맞아들이지요, 참으로 기껍고말고. 당신 친구가 추천장을 내놓을 수 있소—그의 인품을 말해 주는 편지들 말이오?’ 나는 손을 무심히 내저었소. ‘편지요?’ 내가 말하오. ‘하! 오-내-영혼에-축복이! 그야 여부가 있겠소이까! 원한다면 편지도 몇 권 분량이고 추천장도 서류철로 한 아름이오!’ ‘한두 통이면 되오.’ 이 점액질에 돈 많은 사내가 말하오. ‘그의 이름과 주소와 함께 나에게 보내라 하시오. 그리고—잠깐, 잠깐, 페스카 씨—당신 친구에게 가기 전에, 각서를 하나 받아 가는 게 좋겠소.’ ‘은행권!’ 나는 발끈해 말하오. ‘은행권일랑 사양이오, 내 용감한 영국인이 먼저 그것을 벌기 전에는.’ ‘은행권이라니!’ 파파가 몹시 놀라 말하오. ‘누가 은행권을 말했소? 내 말은 조건을 적은 각서—그가 해야 할 일을 적은 비망록이오. 수업을 계속하시오, 페스카 씨, 내가 친구 편지에서 필요한 대목을 뽑아 주리다.’ 이 장사와 돈의 사내가 펜과 잉크와 종이 앞에 앉고, 나는 세 젊은 아가씨를 뒤딸린 채 다시 한번 단테의 지옥으로 내려가오. 십 분쯤 지나 각서가 다 쓰이고, 파파의 구두가 바깥 복도에서 삐걱삐걱 멀어져 가오. 그 순간부터, 내 믿음과 영혼과 명예를 걸고, 나는 더는 아무것도 모르오! 드디어 내가 기회를 붙잡았다는, 그리고 세상에서 가장 소중한 벗을 위한 나의 고마운 봉사가 이미 다 이루어진 것이나 진배없다는 그 영광스러운 생각이 내 머리로 날아올라 나를 취하게 만드오. 내가 어떻게 젊은 아가씨들과 나 자신을 그 지옥에서 다시 끌어냈는지, 그 뒤 내 다른 볼일이 어떻게 처리됐는지, 내 저녁 한 술이 어떻게 목구멍으로 미끄러져 내려갔는지, 나는 달나라 사내만큼도 모르오. 나에게는 이것으로 족하오—내가 지금 여기, 그 대단한 상인의 각서를 손에 들고, 실물처럼 또렷하게, 불처럼 뜨겁게, 임금님처럼 행복하게 서 있다는 것이! 하! 하! 하! 좋아-좋아-좋아-다-좋아!” 여기서 교수는 조건을 적은 비망록을 머리 위로 휘두르며, 영국식 환호를 날카로운 이탈리아식으로 흉내 낸 소리로 길고 유창한 이야기를 마무리했다.\n\n교수가 이야기를 마치는 순간 어머니가 상기된 뺨과 빛나는 눈으로 일어섰다. 그러고는 그 작은 사내의 두 손을 다정하게 붙잡았다.\n\n“나의 착하고 좋은 페스카.” 어머니가 말했다. “나는 월터에 대한 당신의 참된 애정을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없어요—하지만 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 더 그것을 믿게 됐답니다!”\n\n“월터를 위해 우리가 페스카 교수님께 참으로 큰 신세를 진 게 분명해요.” 세라가 덧붙였다. 세라는 말하면서 이번에는 제가 안락의자로 다가가려는 듯 반쯤 일어섰으나, 페스카가 어머니의 손에 황홀하게 입 맞추고 있는 것을 보고는 정색을 하고 도로 자리에 앉았다. ‘저 스스럼없는 작은 사내가 우리 어머니를 저렇게 대한다면, 나는 어떻게 대하려나?’ 얼굴은 때로 진실을 말한다. 그리고 그것은 세라가 도로 앉을 때 그 마음에 든 생각임이 틀림없었다.\n\n나 자신은 페스카의 그 친절한 마음 씀을 고맙게 느끼면서도, 이제 내 앞에 놓인 앞날의 일자리에 대한 기대만큼 마음이 들뜨지는 않았다. 교수가 어머니의 손을 완전히 놓아주고, 내가 나를 대신한 그의 주선에 따뜻이 고마움을 표한 뒤, 나는 그의 점잖은 후원자가 내 검토를 위해 작성했다는 조건 각서를 좀 보게 해 달라 청했다.\n\n페스카가 손을 의기양양하게 휘두르며 나에게 그 종이를 건넸다.\n\n“읽어 보시오!” 작은 사내가 위엄 있게 말했다. “장담하건대 나의 벗이여, 황금빛 파파의 글씨는 나팔의 혀로 제 뜻을 절로 말해 준다오.”\n\n어쨌거나 조건 각서는 명료하고, 솔직하고, 빠짐없었다. 그것은 나에게 이렇게 알려 주었다.\n\n첫째, 컴벌랜드 리머리지 하우스의 프레더릭 페얼리 님이 넉 달을 확정 기간으로 하여 충분히 유능한 그림 선생을 고용하고자 한다는 것.\n\n둘째, 그 선생이 수행해야 할 임무는 두 가지 종류라는 것. 그는 두 젊은 숙녀에게 수채화 그리는 법을 가르치는 일을 감독해야 하며, 그 뒤 틈나는 시간은 완전한 방치 상태에 놓이도록 내버려 둔 값진 소묘 수집품을 손질하고 표구하는 일에 바쳐야 한다는 것.\n\n셋째, 이 임무를 맡아 제대로 수행할 사람에게 제시되는 조건은 주급 사 기니이며, 리머리지 하우스에 거주해야 하고, 그곳에서 신사의 대우를 받게 되리라는 것.\n\n넷째, 그리고 마지막으로, 인품과 능력에 대해 나무랄 데 없는 추천을 갖출 수 없는 사람은 이 자리에 지원할 생각을 아예 하지 말라는 것. 추천장은 런던에 있는 페얼리 씨의 친구에게 보내야 하며, 그 친구가 필요한 모든 절차를 마무리할 권한을 위임받았다는 것. 이 지시에 이어 포틀랜드 플레이스에 있는 페스카의 고용주 이름과 주소가 적혀 있었고—각서, 곧 비망록은 거기서 끝났다.\n\n이 고용 제안이 내밀어 보이는 앞날은 분명 매력적인 것이었다. 그 일자리는 수월하면서도 유쾌할 것 같았다. 그것은 내가 가장 한가한 가을철에 나에게 제안되었고, 조건은 내 직업에서의 개인적 경험으로 미루어 놀랍도록 후했다. 나는 그것을 알고 있었다. 그 제안된 일자리를 손에 넣는 데 성공한다면 나는 스스로를 매우 운 좋은 사람으로 여겨야 마땅함을 알고 있었다—그런데도, 그 비망록을 읽자마자 나는 이 일에 나설 마음이 나지 않는, 설명할 길 없는 거리낌을 내 안에서 느꼈다. 내 지난 경험을 통틀어, 내 의무와 내 마음이 지금처럼 이토록 괴롭게, 이토록 까닭 없이 어긋난 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n“오, 월터, 네 아버지도 이런 기회는 없었단다!” 어머니가 조건 각서를 읽고 나에게 도로 건네주며 말했다.\n\n“그처럼 훌륭한 분들과 알고 지내다니.” 세라가 의자에서 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “게다가 그처럼 흐뭇할 만큼 대등한 조건으로 말이에요!”\n\n“그래, 그래. 조건은 어느 모로 보나 충분히 솔깃하지.” 나는 조바심을 내며 대꾸했다. “하지만 추천장을 보내기 전에, 좀 생각할 시간을 가졌으면 해서——”\n\n“생각이라니!” 어머니가 소리쳤다. “아니, 월터, 대체 너 왜 그러니?”\n\n“생각이라니!” 누이가 되받았다. “이런 형편에 하기엔 참 별스러운 소리네!”\n\n“생각이라니!” 교수도 끼어들었다. “생각할 게 무어가 있소? 이 물음에 답해 보시오! 그대는 건강이 좋지 않다 투덜대지 않았소, 그대가 이른바 시골 바람 한 모금이라 부르는 것을 그리워하지 않았소? 자! 지금 그대 손안에 있는 저 종이가 넉 달 동안 시골 바람을 목이 메도록 실컷 들이켤 것을 그대에게 내밀고 있소이다. 그렇지 아니하오? 하! 또—그대는 돈을 원하오. 자! 주급 사 황금 기니가 아무것도 아니오? 오-내-영혼에-축복이! 그걸 나에게만 준다면—내 구두도 황금빛 파파의 것처럼, 그 안에서 걷는 사내의 넘쳐 나는 부유함을 뽐내며 삐걱거릴 것이오! 주급 사 기니, 게다가 그 위에 두 젊은 아가씨의 매혹적인 벗까지! 게다가 그 위에 그대의 잠자리, 그대의 아침, 그대의 저녁, 그대가 배 터지게 먹을 영국식 차와 점심과 거품 이는 맥주까지 죄다 거저—아니, 월터, 나의 착하고 좋은 벗이여—제기랄-이런-제기랄!—내 평생 처음으로, 그대를 보며 놀라워하기에 내 머릿속 눈이 모자라오!”\n\n내 태도에 대한 어머니의 뚜렷한 놀라움도, 새 일자리가 나에게 내미는 이점에 대한 페스카의 열띤 열거도, 리머리지 하우스로 가기 싫은 나의 까닭 없는 마음을 흔드는 데는 아무런 힘이 없었다. 컴벌랜드로 가는 것에 대해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 자잘한 반대를 다 꺼내 보고, 그것들이 하나하나 반박당해 내가 완전히 낭패한 뒤, 나는 마지막 걸림돌을 세워 보려고, 내가 페얼리 씨의 젊은 숙녀들에게 자연을 사생하는 법을 가르치는 동안 런던의 내 제자들은 어떻게 되느냐고 물었다. 이에 대한 뻔한 대답은, 그들 대부분은 가을 여행을 떠나 있을 테고, 집에 남아 있는 몇 안 되는 이들은 내 동료 그림 선생 하나에게 맡기면 된다는 것이었다—전에 비슷한 형편에서 내가 그의 제자들을 대신 맡아 준 적이 있는 사람이었다. 누이는 그 신사분이 내가 시내를 떠나기를 원할 경우 이번 철에 기꺼이 제 도움을 내주겠노라 분명히 밝혔음을 나에게 일깨워 주었다. 어머니는 부질없는 변덕이 나 자신의 이익과 나 자신의 건강을 가로막게 두지 말라고 진지하게 호소했다. 그리고 페스카는, 제 목숨을 구해 준 벗에게 처음으로 내밀 수 있게 된 그 고마운 봉사의 제안을 내가 물리침으로써 제 가슴에 상처 내지 말아 달라고 애처롭게 간청했다.\n\n이 만류에 서린 뚜렷한 진심과 애정은 조금이라도 좋은 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구든 움직였을 것이다. 나 자신의 그 까닭 모를 삐딱함을 이기지는 못했으나, 나에게는 적어도 그것을 진심으로 부끄러워하고, 뜻을 굽혀 요구되는 대로 다 하겠노라 약속함으로써 이 논의를 기분 좋게 끝맺을 만큼의 미덕은 있었다.\n\n그날 저녁의 나머지는 컴벌랜드에서 두 젊은 숙녀와 함께할 내 앞날을 익살스레 그려 보며 제법 흥겹게 흘러갔다. 페스카는 우리의 국민적 술 그로그에 힘입어—그 술은 목구멍으로 넘어간 지 오 분 만에 더없이 신기한 방식으로 그의 머리로 올라가는 듯했다—빠르게 잇달아 여러 차례 연설을 함으로써 완전한 영국인으로 대접받을 제 자격을 내세웠다. 어머니의 건강, 누이의 건강, 나의 건강, 그리고 페얼리 씨와 두 젊은 아가씨의 건강을 한꺼번에 위하는 축배를 제안하고는, 곧바로 저 스스로 일동을 대신해 애처롭게 답례의 인사를 올렸다. “비밀 하나 말하리다, 월터.” 우리가 함께 집으로 걸어가는 동안 나의 작은 벗이 은밀히 말했다. “내 자신의 웅변을 떠올리니 얼굴이 화끈거리오. 내 영혼이 야망으로 터질 것 같소. 언젠가 나는 그대들의 고귀한 의회로 들어가겠소. 하원 의원 페스카 각하가 되는 것이 내 온 삶의 꿈이라오!”\n\n이튿날 아침 나는 포틀랜드 플레이스에 있는 교수의 고용주에게 내 추천장을 보냈다. 사흘이 지났고, 나는 은근한 만족감과 함께, 내 서류가 충분히 명확하다고 판정받지 못한 모양이라 짐작했다. 그러나 나흗날에 답이 왔다. 페얼리 씨가 나를 고용하기로 했으며 즉시 컴벌랜드로 출발해 달라는 내용이었다. 여정에 필요한 모든 지시가 추신에 꼼꼼하고도 또렷하게 덧붙여져 있었다.\n\n나는 몹시 내키지 않는 마음으로, 이튿날 이른 아침에 런던을 떠날 채비를 갖추었다. 저녁 무렵 페스카가 만찬 모임에 가는 길에 작별 인사를 하러 들렀다.\n\n“그대가 없는 동안 나는 이 영광스러운 생각으로 눈물을 닦으리다.” 교수가 유쾌하게 말했다. “그대의 세상 출셋길에 첫 밀침을 준 것이 바로 이 상서로운 내 손이라는 생각으로 말이오. 가시오, 나의 벗이여! 그대의 해가 컴벌랜드에서 빛나거든(영국 속담이오), 하늘에 맹세코 그대의 건초를 거두시오. 두 젊은 아가씨 가운데 하나와 혼인하시오. 하원 의원 하트라이트 각하가 되시오. 그리고 그대가 사다리 꼭대기에 올랐을 때, 밑바닥에 있는 페스카가 그 모든 것을 해냈음을 기억하시오!”\n\n나는 작별 농담을 던지는 작은 벗과 함께 웃어 보려 했으나, 내 마음은 마음대로 되지 않았다. 그가 가벼운 작별의 말을 하는 동안 내 안에서 무언가가 거의 아플 만큼 삐걱거렸다.\n\n다시 혼자 남겨졌을 때, 이제 할 일이라고는 햄스테드 오두막까지 걸어가 어머니와 세라에게 작별을 고하는 것밖에 남지 않았다.\n\n# IV\n\n더위는 온종일 견디기 괴로울 만큼 숨 막혔고, 이제는 후텁지근하고 무더운 밤이었다.\n\n어머니와 누이가 마지막 말을 어찌나 많이 했던지, 그리고 오 분만 더 기다리라고 어찌나 여러 번 붙들었던지, 하인이 내 뒤로 정원 문을 잠갔을 때는 거의 자정이 다 된 시각이었다. 나는 런던으로 돌아가는 가장 가까운 길을 따라 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가다가, 멈춰 서서 머뭇거렸다.\n\n달은 별 하나 없는 짙푸른 하늘에 둥글고 널따랗게 떠 있었고, 히스 들판의 울퉁불퉁한 땅은 그 신비로운 빛 속에서, 아래에 펼쳐진 대도시로부터 수백 마일은 떨어져 있는 듯 자못 황량해 보였다. 어쩔 수 없이 내려가야 할 때보다 조금이라도 더 일찍 런던의 열기와 침침함 속으로 내려간다는 생각이 나를 물리쳤다. 바람 한 점 통하지 않는 하숙방에서 잠자리에 든다는 것과 서서히 질식해 간다는 것은, 지금 이 안절부절못하는 내 몸과 마음의 상태에서는 하나이자 같은 것으로 여겨졌다. 나는 갈 수 있는 가장 에두르는 길로 더 맑은 공기를 마시며 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 외로운 히스 들판을 가로지르는 하얗게 굽이진 오솔길을 따라가다가, 핀칠리 로드로 접어들어 가장 탁 트인 교외를 거쳐 런던으로 다가가고, 그렇게 동틀 무렵의 서늘함 속에서 리전트 파크의 서쪽으로 돌아오기로 한 것이다.\n\n나는 그 광경의 거룩한 고요를 만끽하며, 그리고 내 사방의 울퉁불퉁한 땅 위로 빛과 그늘이 서로 뒤따르며 부드럽게 갈마드는 모습에 감탄하며, 히스 들판을 천천히 굽이굽이 내려갔다. 이 밤 산책의 첫머리이자 가장 어여쁜 대목을 지나는 동안에는, 내 마음은 그 풍경이 자아내는 인상에 그저 가만히 열려 있었다. 나는 어떤 주제에 대해서도 거의 아무 생각을 하지 않았다—실로, 내 자신의 감각에 관한 한, 나는 아예 생각이란 걸 했다고 하기 어려울 정도였다.\n\n그러나 히스 들판을 벗어나 볼 것이 별로 없는 샛길로 접어들자, 곧 닥칠 내 생활과 일의 변화가 자연히 불러일으키는 생각들이 차츰차츰 내 주의를 오로지 그것들 쪽으로만 끌어당겼다. 그 길의 끝에 다다랐을 무렵 나는 리머리지 하우스에 대한, 페얼리 씨에 대한, 그리고 머잖아 내가 수채화 연습을 감독하게 될 두 숙녀에 대한 나 자신의 부질없는 공상에 완전히 빠져 있었다.\n\n나는 이제 네 갈래 길이 만나는, 내 산책의 바로 그 지점에 이르러 있었다—내가 되돌아온 햄스테드로 가는 길, 핀칠리로 가는 길, 웨스트엔드로 가는 길, 그리고 런던으로 돌아가는 길. 나는 기계적으로 이 마지막 방향으로 접어들어, 컴벌랜드의 젊은 숙녀들이 어떻게 생겼을까 하염없이 궁금해하며—그렇게 기억한다—외로운 큰길을 따라 어슬렁거리고 있었다—바로 그때, 순식간에, 등 뒤에서 누군가의 손이 내 어깨 위에 가볍게 그리고 느닷없이 얹히는 감촉에 내 몸의 피가 방울방울 죄다 멎어 버렸다.\n\n나는 지팡이 손잡이를 감싼 손가락에 힘을 주며 그 즉시 돌아섰다.\n\n거기, 넓고 환한 큰길 한복판에—거기, 바로 그 순간 땅에서 솟아났거나 하늘에서 떨어지기라도 한 듯—머리끝부터 발끝까지 흰옷을 두른 한 외로운 여인의 모습이 서 있었다. 내가 그녀를 마주하자, 그녀의 얼굴은 나를 향해 진지한 물음을 담은 채 숙여져 있었고, 그녀의 손은 런던 위에 드리운 검은 구름을 가리키고 있었다.\n\n이 심상찮은 유령 같은 존재가 깊은 밤, 그 외진 곳에서 내 앞에 불쑥 나타난 그 느닷없음에 나는 너무나 심하게 놀란 나머지, 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 물을 겨를도 없었다. 낯선 여인이 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n“저게 런던으로 가는 길인가요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그 유별난 물음을 던지는 그녀를 나는 유심히 바라보았다. 그때는 거의 한 시가 다 된 시각이었다. 달빛으로 내가 또렷이 분간할 수 있었던 것이라고는, 핏기 없이 앳된 얼굴—뺨과 턱께가 야위고 날카로워 보이는—커다랗고 진지하며 애틋하게 골똘한 눈, 초조하고 미덥지 못한 입술, 그리고 옅고 누르스름한 갈색빛의 밝은 머리칼뿐이었다. 그녀의 태도에는 거친 데도, 정숙하지 못한 데도 없었다. 조용하고 자제된, 조금은 우수에 젖고 조금은 의심에 물든 태도였다—딱히 숙녀의 태도라고 할 수도 없으면서, 동시에 세상에서 가장 낮은 신분의 여인의 태도라고 할 수도 없었다. 그 목소리는, 아직 내가 들은 것이 얼마 되지 않았음에도, 그 어조에 이상하리만치 잔잔하고 기계적인 무언가가 있었으며, 말투는 유난히 빨랐다. 그녀는 손에 작은 가방 하나를 들고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 옷차림—보닛도, 숄도, 가운도 모두 흰색인—은, 내가 짐작하는 한, 분명 그리 곱거나 값비싼 감으로 지은 것은 아니었다. 그녀의 몸매는 가냘프고 키는 보통보다 조금 큰 편이었으며—그 걸음걸이와 몸짓에는 지나침이라고는 티끌만큼도 없었다. 이것이 그 어스름한 빛 속에서, 그리고 우리 만남의 어리둥절할 만큼 기이한 형편 속에서 내가 그녀에 대해 살펴볼 수 있었던 전부였다. 그녀가 어떤 부류의 여인인지, 자정이 지난 지 한 시간이나 된 시각에 어쩌다 홀로 큰길에 나와 있게 되었는지, 나는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다. 다만 한 가지 확신이 든 것은, 아무리 미심쩍게 늦은 시각이고 아무리 미심쩍게 외진 곳이라 해도, 인간 가운데 가장 상스러운 자라 할지라도 그녀가 말을 건 그 뜻만은 그릇 해석할 수 없으리라는 것이었다.\n\n“제 말 들으셨어요?” 그녀는 조금의 짜증이나 조바심도 없이 여전히 조용하고 빠르게 말했다. “저게 런던으로 가는 길이냐고 물었어요.”\n\n“네.” 내가 대답했다. “저 길입니다. 세인트존스우드와 리전트 파크로 이어지지요. 제가 곧바로 대답 못 한 걸 용서하세요. 길에 갑자기 나타나신 통에 좀 놀라서요. 지금도 그게 도무지 납득이 안 됩니다.”\n\n“제가 무슨 나쁜 짓이라도 한다고 의심하시는 건 아니지요? 저는 나쁜 짓 안 했어요. 사고를 당했을 뿐이에요—이렇게 늦은 밤에 혼자 여기 있게 되다니 정말 불운해요. 왜 저를 나쁜 짓 한다고 의심하세요?”\n\n그녀는 쓸데없이 간절하고 동요된 어조로 말하며, 나에게서 몇 걸음 뒷걸음질쳤다. 나는 그녀를 안심시키려 최선을 다했다.\n\n“제가 당신을 의심할 생각이 조금이라도 있다고 여기지 마세요.” 내가 말했다. “할 수만 있다면 당신을 돕고 싶다는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없습니다. 그저 길에 당신이 나타난 것이 놀라웠을 뿐이에요. 당신을 보기 바로 직전까지만 해도 길이 텅 비어 있는 것만 같았거든요.”\n\n그녀는 몸을 돌려, 런던으로 가는 길과 햄스테드로 가는 길이 만나는 지점, 산울타리에 틈이 벌어진 곳을 되짚어 가리켰다.\n\n“당신이 오는 소리를 들었어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그래서 말을 걸어 볼 엄두를 내기 전에, 당신이 어떤 사람인지 보려고 저기 숨어 있었지요. 당신이 지나갈 때까지도 그럴까 말까 두려워 망설였어요. 그러고는 하는 수 없이 몰래 뒤따라와 당신을 건드린 거고요.”\n\n몰래 뒤따라와 나를 건드렸다고? 어째서 나를 부르지 않고? 아무리 좋게 말해도 기이한 일이었다.\n\n“당신을 믿어도 될까요?” 그녀가 물었다. “제가 사고를 당했다고 해서 저를 나쁘게 보시진 않는 거죠?” 그녀는 당황해 말을 멈추더니, 가방을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 옮겨 쥐고, 쓰라리게 한숨지었다.\n\n이 여인의 외로움과 무력함이 내 마음을 움직였다. 그녀를 돕고 아껴 주고 싶은 자연스러운 충동이, 좀 더 나이 들고 지혜롭고 냉정한 사람이라면 이 기이한 위급함에서 제 편으로 불러냈을 법한 판단과 조심성과 세상 물정에 밝은 요령을 눌러 버렸다.\n\n“해롭지 않은 일이라면 무엇이든 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다.” 내가 말했다. “당신의 그 기이한 처지를 저에게 설명하는 것이 괴로우시다면, 다시는 그 이야기로 돌아갈 생각 마세요. 저는 당신에게 아무 설명도 청할 권리가 없습니다. 제가 어떻게 도와드리면 될지 말씀만 하세요. 할 수 있다면 하겠습니다.”\n\n“정말 친절하시네요, 그리고 당신을 만나서 정말, 정말 고마워요.” 그 말을 할 때 그녀의 목소리에는, 내가 그녀에게서 처음 들은 여인다운 다정함의 기미가 떨렸다. 그러나 여전히 나에게 고정된 그 크고 애틋하게 골똘한 눈에는 눈물이 반짝이지 않았다. “저는 전에 딱 한 번 런던에 와 봤을 뿐이에요.” 그녀는 점점 더 빠르게 이어 갔다. “그래서 저기 저쪽은 아무것도 몰라요. 삯마차나 아무 마차라도 잡을 수 있을까요? 너무 늦었나요? 모르겠어요. 삯마차를 어디서 잡는지 알려 주시면—그리고 저를 방해하지 않겠다고, 제가 원하는 때에 원하는 대로 당신 곁을 떠나게 해 주겠다고 약속만 해 주시면—런던에 저를 반겨 맞아 줄 친구가 하나 있어요—그 밖에는 아무것도 바라지 않아요—약속해 주시겠어요?”\n\n그녀는 근심스레 길 위아래를 살피고, 다시 가방을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 옮겨 쥐고, “약속해 주시겠어요?”라는 말을 되풀이하고는, 보고 있기 괴로울 만큼 애원하는 두려움과 혼란을 담아 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 여기, 아주 속수무책으로 내 처분에 내맡겨진 한 낯선 이가 있었다—그리고 그 낯선 이는 의지가지없는 한 여인이었다. 가까이에는 집도 없었고, 의논해 볼 지나가는 이도 없었으며, 설령 그 방법을 안다 한들 그녀를 다스릴 권한을 나에게 줄 어떠한 세상의 권리도 내 쪽에는 없었다. 나는 뒷날 벌어진 일들의 그늘이 내가 지금 글을 쓰는 바로 이 종이를 어둡게 물들이는 가운데, 나 자신을 미덥지 못해하며 이 줄들을 적는다. 그럼에도 나는 다시 말한다, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n내가 정작 한 일은, 그녀에게 이것저것 물어 시간을 벌어 보려는 것이었다. “런던의 그 친구분이 이렇게 늦은 시각에 당신을 맞아 주리라 확신하십니까?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“틀림없어요. 제가 원하는 때에 원하는 대로 당신 곁을 떠나게 해 주겠다는 말만 해 주세요—저를 방해하지 않겠다는 말만요. 약속해 주시겠어요?”\n\n세 번째로 그 말을 되풀이하면서, 그녀는 나에게 바짝 다가와 느닷없이 부드럽고 은밀하게 제 손을 내 가슴에 얹었다—가느다란 손이었다. 그 후텁지근한 밤인데도, (내 손으로 그 손을 떼어 냈을 때) 차가운 손이었다. 내가 젊었음을 기억하라. 나를 건드린 그 손이 한 여인의 손이었음을 기억하라.\n\n“약속해 주시겠어요?”\n\n“네.”\n\n한 마디! 하루에도 시간마다 누구나 입에 올리는 그 흔하고 친숙한 한 마디. 아, 나여! 그리고 이제 그것을 적으면서 나는 떨고 있다.\n\n우리는 런던 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 새로운 하루의 첫 고요한 시각 속을 함께 걸어갔다—나와, 그 이름도, 그 성품도, 그 사연도, 그 삶의 목적도, 그 순간 내 곁에 있다는 사실 그 자체마저도 나에게는 헤아릴 길 없는 수수께끼였던 이 여인이. 마치 꿈만 같았다. 내가 정말 월터 하트라이트였던가? 이것이 정말, 휴일이면 나들이객들이 일요일마다 거니는 그 잘 알려진, 별일 없는 길이었던가? 내가 정말 한 시간 남짓 전에 어머니 오두막의 그 조용하고 점잖고 판에 박힌 가정의 분위기를 떠나온 것이었던가? 나는 너무나 얼떨떨했고—또한 무언가 자책에 가까운 어렴풋한 느낌을 너무나 뚜렷이 의식하고 있었다—그래서 한동안 그 기이한 동행에게 말을 걸지 못했다. 우리 사이의 침묵을 처음 깬 것은 다시금 그녀의 목소리였다.\n\n“한 가지 여쭙고 싶어요.” 그녀가 불쑥 말했다. “런던에 아는 사람이 많으세요?”\n\n“네, 아주 많지요.”\n\n“지체 높고 작위 있는 분들도 많이요?” 그 기이한 물음에는 틀림없는 의심의 어조가 배어 있었다. 나는 대답을 망설였다.\n\n“몇 분요.” 나는 잠깐 침묵한 뒤에 말했다.\n\n“많이”—그녀는 딱 멈춰 서서 내 얼굴을 살피듯 바라보았다—“준남작 지위의 분들도 많이요?”\n\n너무 놀라 대답하지 못한 채, 나는 이번엔 내가 그녀에게 되물었다.\n\n“어째서 그런 걸 물으십니까?”\n\n“당신이 모르는 준남작이 하나 있기를, 저 자신을 위해 바라기 때문이에요.”\n\n“그 사람 이름을 말해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“못 해요—감히 못 해요—그 이름을 입에 올리면 저는 제정신을 잃어요.” 그녀는 크게, 거의 사납다 싶게 말하며, 움켜쥔 손을 허공에 치켜들고 격하게 흔들었다. 그러더니 별안간 다시 자신을 다스리고는, 속삭임으로 낮춘 어조로 덧붙였다. “당신이 아는 분들이 누구누구인지 말해 주세요.”\n\n이런 사소한 일에 그녀의 비위를 맞춰 주기를 마다하기는 어려운지라, 나는 세 사람의 이름을 댔다. 둘은 내가 딸들을 가르치는 집안 가장들의 이름이었고, 하나는 언젠가 자기를 위해 스케치를 해 달라며 제 요트로 나를 뱃놀이에 데려간 적이 있는 어느 독신 신사의 이름이었다.\n\n“아! 당신은 그 사람을 모르는군요.” 그녀는 안도의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “당신 자신은 지체 높고 작위 있는 분인가요?”\n\n“천만에요. 저는 한낱 그림 선생일 뿐입니다.”\n\n그 대답이—어쩌면 조금은 씁쓸하게—내 입에서 새어 나오는 순간, 그녀는 제 모든 행동을 특징짓는 그 느닷없음으로 내 팔을 잡았다.\n\n“지체 높고 작위 있는 분이 아니라니.” 그녀는 혼잣말로 되뇌었다. “고마우신 하느님! 이분은 믿어도 되겠어요.”\n\n나는 여태 동행을 배려하여 내 호기심을 눌러 왔다. 그러나 이제는 그 호기심이 나를 이기고 말았다.\n\n“지체 높고 작위 있는 어떤 사람에 대해 원망할 심각한 까닭이 있으신 게 아닌가 싶군요?” 내가 말했다. “당신이 저에게 이름 대기를 꺼리는 그 준남작이 당신에게 무언가 참혹한 몹쓸 짓을 저지른 게 아닌지요? 이 기이한 야밤에 당신이 이렇게 밖에 나와 있는 것도 그 사람 탓입니까?”\n\n“묻지 마세요. 그 이야기를 하게 만들지 마세요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “지금은 그럴 형편이 못 돼요. 저는 참혹하게 다뤄졌고 참혹하게 짓밟혔어요. 걸음을 빨리해 주시고 저에게 말을 걸지 않아 주신다면, 그 어느 때보다 더 친절하신 거예요. 할 수만 있다면 저는 못 견디게 마음을 가라앉히고 싶어요.”\n\n우리는 다시 빠른 걸음으로 나아갔다. 그리고 적어도 반 시간 동안은 어느 쪽에서도 한 마디도 오가지 않았다. 이따금, 더는 캐물어선 안 되는지라, 나는 그녀의 얼굴을 몰래 훔쳐보았다. 언제나 한결같았다. 꼭 다문 입술, 찌푸린 이마, 골똘하면서도 넋 나간 듯 곧장 앞을 응시하는 눈. 우리가 첫 집들에 다다라 새로 지은 웨슬리교 대학 가까이에 이르러서야 비로소 그 굳어 있던 이목구비가 풀리며 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n“런던에 사세요?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“네.” 내가 대답하는데, 문득 이런 생각이 들었다—그녀가 나에게 도움이나 조언을 청할 어떤 생각을 품었을지도 모르며, 그렇다면 곧 내가 집을 비운다는 것을 미리 알려 그녀가 겪을지 모를 실망을 덜어 주어야 한다는 것이. 그래서 나는 덧붙였다. “하지만 내일이면 한동안 런던을 떠나 있을 겁니다. 시골로 내려가거든요.”\n\n“어디로요?” 그녀가 물었다. “북쪽인가요, 남쪽인가요?”\n\n“북쪽—컴벌랜드로요.”\n\n“컴벌랜드!” 그녀는 그 말을 애틋하게 되뇌었다. “아! 저도 거기 갈 수 있으면 좋으련만. 저는 한때 컴벌랜드에서 행복했어요.”\n\n나는 이 여인과 나 사이에 드리운 장막을 걷어 보려고 다시 한번 애썼다.\n\n“어쩌면 그 아름다운 호수 지방에서 태어나셨나 보군요.” 내가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “저는 햄프셔에서 태어났어요. 하지만 한때 잠깐 컴벌랜드로 학교를 다닌 적이 있지요. 호수요? 저는 호수 같은 건 기억나지 않아요. 제가 다시 보고 싶은 건 리머리지 마을이랑 리머리지 하우스예요.”\n\n이번엔 내가 별안간 멈춰 설 차례였다. 그 순간 잔뜩 들뜬 내 호기심의 상태에서, 기이한 동행의 입에서 페얼리 씨의 거처가 우연히 튀어나온 것은 나를 놀라움으로 휘청거리게 만들었다.\n\n“누가 우리 뒤에서 부르는 소리 들리지 않았어요?” 내가 멈추는 순간 그녀가 겁에 질려 길 위아래를 살피며 물었다.\n\n“아니요, 아니요. 그저 리머리지 하우스라는 이름에 놀랐을 뿐입니다. 며칠 전에 어떤 컴벌랜드 사람들이 그 이름을 말하는 걸 들었거든요.”\n\n“아! 우리 식구는 아니에요. 페얼리 부인은 돌아가셨고, 그 남편도 돌아가셨어요. 그리고 그분들의 어린 딸은 이맘때면 혼인해 떠났을지도 모르지요. 지금 리머리지에 누가 사는지 저는 말할 수 없어요. 그 이름을 가진 이가 거기 더 남아 있다면, 페얼리 부인을 위해서라도 제가 그들을 사랑한다는 것밖에는 저는 몰라요.”\n\n그녀는 더 말하려는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이, 우리는 애비뉴 로드 꼭대기에 있는 통행료 관문이 보이는 데까지 이르렀다. 그녀의 손이 내 팔을 꽉 죄었고, 그녀는 우리 앞의 관문을 근심스레 바라보았다.\n\n“관문지기가 내다보고 있나요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n그는 내다보고 있지 않았다. 우리가 관문을 지날 때 그 근처에는 다른 누구도 없었다. 가스등과 집들이 눈에 들어오자 그녀는 동요하며 조바심을 내는 듯했다.\n\n“여기가 런던이에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “제가 탈 만한 마차가 보이나요? 저는 지치고 겁이 나요. 저는 안에 틀어박혀 실려 가고 싶어요.”\n\n나는 빈 마차를 만나는 행운이 따르지 않는 한, 삯마차 정류장에 가려면 좀 더 걸어야 한다고 그녀에게 설명했다. 그러고는 컴벌랜드 이야기를 다시 꺼내 보려 했다. 소용없었다. 안에 틀어박혀 실려 간다는 그 생각이 이제 그녀의 마음을 온통 사로잡고 있었다. 그녀는 그것 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수도, 말할 수도 없었다.\n\n애비뉴 로드를 삼분의 일도 채 내려가지 못했을 때, 나는 몇 집 아래, 길 건너편의 한 집 앞에 삯마차가 멎는 것을 보았다. 한 신사가 내려서 정원 문으로 들어갔다. 마부가 다시 마부석에 오르는 것을 보고 나는 그 마차를 불러 세웠다. 우리가 길을 건널 때 동행의 조바심은 어찌나 커졌던지, 그녀는 나를 거의 뛰다시피 하게 만들었다.\n\n“너무 늦었어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “너무 늦어서 서두르는 것뿐이에요.”\n\n“토트넘 코트 로드 쪽으로 가시는 게 아니라면 못 태워 드립니다, 나리.” 내가 마차 문을 열자 마부가 공손히 말했다. “제 말이 완전히 지쳐서, 마구간까지밖에는 더 못 갑니다.”\n\n“네, 네. 저한테는 그거면 돼요. 저도 그쪽으로 가요—저도 그쪽으로 가요.” 그녀는 숨 가쁘게 다급한 어조로 말하며, 내 곁을 밀치고 마차 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n나는 그녀를 마차에 태우기 전에, 그 사내가 공손할 뿐 아니라 술도 취하지 않았음을 확인해 두었다. 그리고 이제, 그녀가 안에 앉자, 나는 그녀가 목적지에 무사히 내리는 것을 보게 해 달라고 간청했다.\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.” 그녀가 격하게 말했다. “저는 이제 아주 안전하고 아주 행복해요. 당신이 신사라면 약속을 기억하세요. 제가 세울 때까지 마부가 몰아가게 두세요. 고마워요—오! 고마워요, 고마워요!”\n\n내 손이 마차 문에 얹혀 있었다. 그녀는 그 손을 제 손으로 붙잡아 입 맞추고는 밀쳐 냈다. 바로 그 순간 마차가 달리기 시작했다—나는 다시 그것을 세우려는 어렴풋한 생각에, 왜인지도 잘 모른 채 길로 뛰어들었다—그녀를 겁주고 괴롭힐까 두려워 머뭇거렸다—마침내 소리쳐 불렀으나, 마부의 주의를 끌 만큼 크지는 못했다. 바퀴 소리가 멀리서 차츰 희미해졌다—마차는 길 위의 검은 그늘 속으로 녹아들었다—흰옷을 입은 여인은 사라졌다.\n\n십 분 남짓 지났다. 나는 여전히 길 같은 쪽에 있었다. 이제는 기계적으로 몇 걸음 앞으로 걷다가, 이제는 다시 넋 놓고 멈춰 섰다. 어느 순간에는 내 자신의 그 겪음이 정말이었는지 의심하는 나를 발견했고, 또 어느 순간에는 무언가 잘못을 저질렀다는 꺼림칙한 느낌에 어지럽고 괴로웠다—그러면서도 정작 어떻게 했어야 옳았을지는 어리둥절하게도 알지 못한 채였다. 내가 어디로 가는지도, 다음에 무엇을 하려는지도 나는 거의 알지 못했다. 나 자신의 뒤엉킨 생각의 혼란 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못하고 있는데, 그때 바로 등 뒤에서 빠르게 다가오는 바퀴 소리에 나는 불현듯 제정신으로 되돌려졌다—거의 잠에서 깨어났다고 해도 좋을 만큼.\n\n돌아보려고 멈춰 섰을 때 나는 길의 어두운 쪽, 몇몇 정원 나무들의 짙은 그늘 속에 있었다. 길 건너 좀 더 밝은 쪽, 나에게서 조금 아래에서는 순경 하나가 리전트 파크 방향으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걷고 있었다.\n\n마차가 나를 지나쳐 갔다—두 사내가 모는 지붕 없는 이륜마차였다.\n\n“서!” 한 사람이 소리쳤다. “저기 순경이 있다. 저이한테 물어보자.”\n\n내가 서 있던 어두운 자리에서 몇 야드 지난 곳에서 말이 그 즉시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n“순경!” 처음 말한 자가 외쳤다. “이 길로 여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 봤소?”\n\n“어떤 여자 말입니까, 나리?”\n\n“라벤더빛 가운을 입은 여자——”\n\n“아니, 아니.” 두 번째 사내가 끼어들었다. “우리가 준 옷은 침대 위에서 발견됐잖나. 우리한테 올 때 입고 온 옷차림 그대로 달아났을 게 틀림없어. 흰옷일세, 순경. 흰옷을 입은 여자란 말이오.”\n\n“그런 여자는 못 봤습니다, 나리.”\n\n“자네든 자네 부하 누구든 그 여자를 만나거든 붙잡아서, 이 주소로 잘 지켜 데려다주게. 비용은 다 내지, 게다가 후한 사례금까지 얹어서.”\n\n순경은 아래로 건네받은 명함을 들여다보았다.\n\n“어째서 그 여자를 붙잡아야 합니까, 나리? 그 여자가 무슨 짓을 했습니까?”\n\n“짓이라니! 그 여자는 내 정신병원에서 달아났소. 잊지 말게. 흰옷을 입은 여자야. 몰아라.”\n\n“그 여자는 바로 내가 운영하는 정신병원에서 달아난 것이오!”\n\n그 말이 자아낸 그 끔찍한 추론이 새로운 계시처럼 나에게 번뜩였다고는 사실대로 말할 수 없다. 그녀가 원하는 대로 자유로이 행동하게 두겠다는 나의 경솔한 약속 뒤에 흰옷을 입은 여인이 나에게 던진 그 기이한 물음들 가운데 몇몇은, 그녀가 천성적으로 들뜨고 안정되지 못했거나, 아니면 무언가 최근의 두려운 충격이 그녀의 심신의 균형을 흐트러뜨렸으리라는 결론을 넌지시 비추기는 했었다. 그러나 정신병원이라는 그 이름 자체에 우리 모두가 결부시키는 완전한 광기라는 생각은, 정직하게 단언하건대, 그녀와 관련해서는 나에게 결코 떠오른 적이 없었다. 나는 그녀의 말에서도 행동에서도 그 당시 그것을 뒷받침할 만한 것을 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그리고 낯선 사내가 순경에게 건넨 그 말이 그녀에게 새로이 드리운 빛으로도, 지금 나는 그것을 뒷받침할 만한 것을 아무것도 볼 수 없었다.\n\n내가 무슨 짓을 한 것인가? 모든 부당한 감금 가운데 가장 소름 끼치는 것의 희생자를 도와 달아나게 한 것인가, 아니면 그 행동을 자비로이 다스리는 것이 내 의무이자 뭇사람의 의무인 어느 가엾은 존재를 런던이라는 드넓은 세상에 풀어놓아 버린 것인가? 이 물음이 떠올랐을 때, 그리고 그것이 너무 뒤늦게 던져진 물음임을 자책하며 느꼈을 때, 나는 가슴이 메슥거렸다.\n\n마침내 클레멘츠 여관의 하숙방으로 돌아왔을 때, 이렇게 어지러운 마음 상태로는 잠자리에 들 생각을 해 봐야 소용없었다. 몇 시간 지나지 않아 컴벌랜드로 가는 여정에 나서야 할 터였다. 나는 앉아서, 처음엔 스케치를, 그다음엔 독서를 해 보려 했으나—흰옷을 입은 여인이 나와 내 연필 사이에, 나와 내 책 사이에 끼어들었다. 그 의지가지없는 존재는 무슨 해라도 입지 않았을까? 그것이 내 첫 생각이었다, 비록 나는 그것과 마주하기를 이기적으로 꺼렸지만. 그보다는 곱씹기가 덜 괴로운 다른 생각들이 뒤따랐다. 그녀는 어디서 삯마차를 세웠을까? 이제 그녀는 어떻게 됐을까? 그녀는 이륜마차의 사내들에게 뒤를 밟혀 붙잡혔을까? 아니면 아직도 제 행동을 다스릴 수 있어서, 우리 둘은 저마다 멀찍이 갈라진 길을 따라, 다시 한번 마주치게 될 신비로운 미래의 한 지점을 향해 가고 있는 것일까?\n\n문을 잠그고, 런던의 일들과 런던의 제자들과 런던의 벗들에게 작별을 고하고, 새로운 관심사와 새로운 삶을 향해 다시 움직일 시각이 왔을 때는 오히려 마음이 놓였다. 다른 때 같으면 그토록 진저리 나고 어리둥절하던 철도 종착역의 부산함과 혼잡마저 나를 흔들어 깨워 나에게 이로웠다.\n\n여정 지시서는 나에게 칼라일까지 가서, 거기서 해안 방향으로 달리는 지선(支線) 철도로 갈아타라고 일러 주었다. 시작부터 불운이 닥쳐, 우리 기관차가 랭커스터와 칼라일 사이에서 고장 났다. 이 사고로 생긴 지체 탓에 나는 곧바로 갈아타려던 지선 열차를 놓치고 말았다. 몇 시간을 기다려야 했다. 그리고 뒤늦은 열차가 마침내 나를 리머리지 하우스에서 가장 가까운 역에 내려놓았을 때는 열 시가 지난 뒤였고, 밤이 어찌나 어두웠던지 페얼리 씨가 나를 위해 대기시켜 두라 일러 놓은 조랑말 마차로 가는 길조차 겨우 분간할 지경이었다.\n\n마부는 내 도착이 늦은 것에 눈에 띄게 언짢아하고 있었다. 그는 영국 하인 특유의, 지극히 공손한 뿌루퉁함의 상태에 있었다. 우리는 완전한 침묵 속에서 어둠을 뚫고 느릿느릿 마차를 몰아갔다. 길은 험했고, 짙은 밤의 어둠은 땅을 빨리 지나가기 어렵게 만들었다. 내 시계로, 역을 떠난 때로부터 거의 한 시간 반이 지나서야 나는 멀리서 바다 소리와, 매끄러운 자갈길 위를 구르는 우리 바퀴의 사각거림을 들었다. 우리는 진입로에 들어서기 전에 문 하나를 지났고, 저택 앞에 마차를 세우기 전에 또 하나의 문을 지났다. 나는 제복을 입지 않은 근엄한 남자 하인의 맞이를 받아, 가족은 이미 밤 인사를 하고 잠자리에 들었다는 말을 듣고는, 크고 높다란 방으로 안내되었다. 그 방에서는 쓸쓸한 마호가니 식탁의 황량한 벌판 한쪽 끝에, 내 밤참이 처량한 모양새로 나를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n나는 너무 지치고 기운이 없어 많이 먹지도 마시지도 못했다. 더욱이 홀로 온 사내가 아니라 무슨 작은 만찬 모임이라도 저택에 당도한 양 근엄한 하인이 나를 정성스레 시중드는 통에 더 그랬다. 십오 분 만에 나는 침실로 올라갈 채비가 되었다. 근엄한 하인은 나를 아담하게 꾸며진 방으로 안내하고는—“아침 식사는 아홉 시입니다, 나리”라고 말하고—모든 것이 제자리에 있는지 사방을 둘러보고, 소리 없이 물러갔다.\n\n“오늘 밤 나는 꿈에서 무엇을 보게 될까?” 나는 촛불을 끄며 혼자 생각했다. “흰옷을 입은 여인일까? 아니면 이 컴벌랜드 저택의 알지 못하는 거주자들일까?” 이 집에서, 마치 그 집안의 벗처럼 잠을 자면서도, 정작 그 식구를 얼굴조차 하나도 알지 못한다는 것은 기이한 느낌이었다!\n\n# VI\n\n이튿날 아침 일어나 블라인드를 걷어 올리자, 널따란 팔월 햇살 아래 바다가 내 앞에 환하게 펼쳐졌고, 멀리 스코틀랜드 해안이 녹아드는 듯한 푸른 선들로 수평선을 가장자리 두르고 있었다.\n\n벽돌과 회반죽의 풍경에 진저리 나던 런던 생활 끝에, 그 광경은 나에게 너무나 뜻밖의 놀라움이자 너무나 큰 변화여서, 그것을 바라보는 순간 나는 새로운 삶과 새로운 생각 속으로 불쑥 터져 나오는 듯했다. 현재나 미래에 대해 어떤 더 또렷한 생각도 얻지 못한 채, 별안간 과거와의 친숙함을 잃어버렸다는 어수선한 느낌이 내 마음을 사로잡았다. 겨우 며칠밖에 되지 않은 일들이, 마치 여러 달 전에 일어나기라도 한 양 내 기억 속에서 아스라이 물러났다. 지금의 이 일자리를 나에게 마련해 준 방법에 대한 페스카의 별스러운 발표며, 어머니와 누이와 함께 보낸 작별의 저녁이며, 햄스테드에서 집으로 돌아오는 길의 그 신비로운 겪음마저도—죄다, 내 삶의 어느 지난 시대에나 일어났을 법한 일들처럼 되어 버렸다. 흰옷을 입은 여인이 여전히 내 마음속에 있었음에도, 그녀의 모습은 벌써 흐릿하고 희미해진 듯했다.\n\n아홉 시가 조금 못 되어 나는 저택의 일 층으로 내려갔다. 전날 밤의 그 근엄한 남자 하인이 복도를 헤매는 나와 마주치더니, 딱하다는 듯 아침 식사실로 가는 길을 일러 주었다.\n\n하인이 문을 열자, 방을 둘러본 내 첫 눈길에, 창이 많이 난 긴 방 한복판에 잘 차려진 아침 식탁이 놓여 있는 것이 들어왔다. 식탁에서 나에게 가장 먼 창으로 눈을 옮기니, 한 숙녀가 나를 등진 채 그 앞에 서 있는 것이 보였다. 내 눈이 그녀에게 머무는 순간, 나는 그 자태의 보기 드문 아름다움과, 꾸밈없는 그 몸가짐의 우아함에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 그녀의 몸매는 컸으나 너무 크지는 않았고, 어여쁘게 잘 여물었으나 뚱뚱하지는 않았다. 머리는 어깨 위에 편안하면서도 유연한 굳건함으로 얹혀 있었다. 그 허리는—사내의 눈에는 완벽함 그 자체였다. 제자리에 자연스레 놓여, 제 둘레를 자연스레 채우고 있었으며, 코르셋에 눌려 일그러지지 않았음이 눈에 뜨이게, 흐뭇하게 드러났으니까. 그녀는 내가 방에 들어온 것을 듣지 못했다. 그래서 나는 그녀의 주의를 끄는 가장 덜 무안한 방법으로 가까이 있는 의자 하나를 옮기기 전에, 잠시나마 그녀를 흠모하는 호사를 나 자신에게 허락했다. 그녀는 곧바로 나를 향해 돌아섰다. 방의 저 먼 끝에서 다가오기 시작하는 순간, 그녀의 팔다리와 몸의 온갖 움직임이 지닌 그 편안한 우아함은, 그녀의 얼굴을 또렷이 보고 싶은 기대로 나를 설레게 했다. 그녀가 창가를 떠났다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다, 이 숙녀는 가무잡잡하구나. 그녀가 몇 걸음 앞으로 나왔다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다, 이 숙녀는 젊구나. 그녀가 더 가까이 다가왔다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다(말로는 다 표현할 수 없는 놀라움과 함께), 이 숙녀는 못생겼구나!\n\n자연은 그르치는 법이 없다는 저 낡고 판에 박힌 격언이 이토록 대놓고 뒤집힌 적은 없었다—사랑스러운 자태의 아름다운 약속이, 그 위에 얹힌 얼굴과 머리로 인해 이토록 기이하고 소스라치게 배신당한 적은 없었다. 그 숙녀의 낯빛은 거의 거무스름했고, 윗입술의 검은 솜털은 거의 콧수염이나 다름없었다. 그녀는 크고 단단한, 사내 같은 입과 턱을 지녔고, 튀어나온 듯 꿰뚫는, 결연한 갈색 눈을 지녔으며, 이마 위 유난히 아래까지 내려온 숱 많은 새까만 머리칼을 지녔다. 그녀의 표정은—밝고 솔직하고 총명한—입을 다물고 있는 동안에는, 부드러움과 유순함이라는 저 여성스러운 매력을 통째로 결여한 듯 보였다. 살아 있는 가장 어여쁜 여인의 아름다움조차 그것 없이는 미완의 아름다움이건만. 조각가라면 본떠 만들고 싶어 애가 탔을 어깨 위에 이런 얼굴이 얹힌 것을 보는 것은—그 균형 잡힌 팔다리가 움직일 때 제 아름다움을 은근히 드러내는 그 정숙한 몸짓의 우아함에 마음을 빼앗겼다가, 이내 그 완벽하게 빚어진 자태가 끝나는 지점의 이목구비의 사내 같은 생김새와 사내 같은 표정에 거의 밀쳐지듯 하는 것은—꿈속에서 우리 모두에게 익숙한 그 어쩔 수 없는 불편함, 곧 우리가 꿈의 그 모순과 어긋남을 알아채면서도 도무지 아귀를 맞출 수 없을 때 느끼는 그것과 묘하게 닮은 감각을 느끼는 일이었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨인가요?” 숙녀가 묻듯이 말했다. 그녀의 가무잡잡한 얼굴은 미소로 환해지며, 말을 시작하는 순간 부드러워지고 여인다워졌다. “저희는 어젯밤 당신을 뵐 희망을 완전히 접고 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었답니다. 저희가 소홀했던 듯 보인 것을 사과드려요. 그리고 저를 당신의 제자 가운데 하나로 소개하게 해 주세요. 우리 악수할까요? 어차피 이르든 늦든 하게 될 텐데—늦게보다야 이르게가 낫지 않겠어요?”\n\n이 별스러운 환영의 말은 맑고 낭랑하며 유쾌한 목소리로 건네졌다. 내밀어진 손은—다소 컸으나 아름답게 빚어진—좋은 가문에서 자란 여인의 그 편안하고 꾸밈없는 자신감으로 나에게 건네졌다. 우리는 마치 몇 년째 서로 알고 지낸 사이인 양, 그리고 미리 약속해 옛이야기를 나누러 리머리지 하우스에서 만나기라도 한 양, 더없이 정답고 스스럼없는 태도로 아침 식탁에 나란히 앉았다.\n\n“당신이 자신의 처지를 좋게 받아들이겠노라 기분 좋게 마음먹고 이곳에 오셨기를 바라요.” 숙녀가 이어 갔다. “오늘 아침은 아침 식탁에서 저 말고는 다른 벗 없이 견디는 것부터 시작하셔야 할 거예요. 제 동생은 지극히 여성스러운 병, 곧 가벼운 두통을 앓느라 제 방에 있어요. 그 애의 늙은 가정교사 베지 부인이 원기를 돋우는 차로 갸륵하게 시중들고 있고요. 제 삼촌 페얼리 씨는 저희 어느 끼니에도 결코 함께하지 않으세요. 병약한 분이라, 당신 처소에서 독신 생활을 하시지요. 이 집에는 저 말고 다른 사람이 없어요. 젊은 숙녀 둘이 여기 묵고 있었는데 어제 절망해서 떠나 버렸답니다. 그럴 만도 했지요. 그들이 머무는 내내 (페얼리 씨가 병약한 탓에) 저희 집에는 시시덕거리고 춤추고 잡담을 나눌 만한 남성이라는 편의물이 하나도 없었거든요. 그러니 그 결과 저희는 다투는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았어요, 특히 저녁 식사 때면요. 여자 넷이 날마다 단둘이 저녁을 먹으면서 다투지 않기를 어찌 바라겠어요? 저희는 그런 바보들이라, 식탁에서 서로를 즐겁게 해 주질 못한답니다. 보세요, 저는 제 성(性)을 대단하게 여기지 않아요, 하트라이트 씨—차를 드릴까요, 커피를 드릴까요?—어떤 여자도 제 성을 대단하게 여기진 않지요, 저처럼 스스럼없이 털어놓는 여자는 몇 없지만요. 아이참, 어리둥절해 보이시네요. 왜죠? 아침으로 무얼 드실지 궁금하세요? 아니면 제 무람없는 말투에 놀라셨나요? 앞쪽이라면, 벗으로서 충고하건대, 팔꿈치 옆의 저 찬 햄에는 손대지 마시고 오믈렛이 나올 때까지 기다리세요. 뒤쪽이라면, 마음을 가라앉히시라고 차를 좀 드리고, 여자가 할 수 있는 (그런데 이건 여담이지만 참 보잘것없는) 온갖 애를 써서 제 입을 다물어 보겠어요.”\n\n그녀는 즐거이 웃으며 찻잔을 나에게 건넸다. 그 가벼운 말의 흐름과, 생판 낯선 이를 대하는 그 발랄한 스스럼없음에는, 꾸밈없는 자연스러움과, 자기 자신 및 자기 처지에 대한 편안하고도 타고난 자신감이 함께 배어 있어, 세상에 둘도 없이 대담한 사내조차 그녀를 우러르게 만들 만했다. 그녀와 함께 있으면서 격식을 차리고 뻣뻣하게 굴기란 불가능했으나, 생각으로나마 그녀에게 티끌만 한 무례를 범하기란 불가능함을 넘어서는 일이었다. 나는 그녀의 그 밝고 명랑한 기운에 절로 물들면서도—그녀의 솔직하고 발랄한 태도에 맞추어 답하려 애쓰면서도—이것을 본능적으로 느꼈다.\n\n“네, 네.” 어리둥절한 내 표정을 해명하려고 내가 내놓을 수 있는 유일한 설명을 넌지시 비추자 그녀가 말했다. “알겠어요. 당신은 이 집에서 아주 생판 낯선 처지라, 이 갸륵한 거주자들에 대한 저의 스스럼없는 언급에 어리둥절하신 거예요. 그럴 만하지요. 진작 그 생각을 했어야 하는데. 어쨌든 이제 바로잡을 수 있어요. 이 화제의 그 대목을 되도록 빨리 끝내 버리게, 저 자신부터 시작해 볼까요? 제 이름은 매리언 홀콤이에요. 그리고 저는 페얼리 씨를 삼촌이라, 페얼리 양을 동생이라 부른다는 점에서, 여느 여자들만큼이나 부정확하답니다. 제 어머니는 두 번 혼인하셨어요. 첫 번째는 제 아버지 홀콤 씨와, 두 번째는 제 이복동생의 아버지 페얼리 씨와요. 저희 둘 다 고아라는 것 말고는, 저희는 모든 면에서 더없이 서로 다르답니다. 제 아버지는 가난한 분이었고, 페얼리 양의 아버지는 부유한 분이었어요. 저는 가진 게 하나도 없고, 그 애는 재산이 있지요. 저는 가무잡잡하고 못생겼고, 그 애는 살결 곱고 어여뻐요. 다들 저를 심술궂고 별나다 여기고요(더없이 옳은 말이지요), 다들 그 애를 상냥하고 매력적이라 여겨요(더더욱 옳은 말이고요). 한마디로, 그 애는 천사예요. 그리고 저는—— 저 마멀레이드나 좀 드셔 보세요, 하트라이트 씨, 그리고 그 문장의 끝은 여자의 체면을 봐서라도 당신이 알아서 채우시고요. 페얼리 씨에 대해 무얼 말씀드려야 할까요? 정말이지, 저도 잘 모르겠어요. 그분은 아침 식사 뒤에 틀림없이 당신을 부르러 사람을 보내실 테니, 당신 스스로 그분을 살펴보시면 돼요. 그동안 저는 이것만 알려 드리지요. 첫째, 그분은 돌아가신 페얼리 씨의 남동생이라는 것. 둘째, 그분은 독신이라는 것. 셋째, 그분은 페얼리 양의 후견인이라는 것. 저는 그 애 없이는 못 살고, 그 애는 저 없이는 못 살아요. 그래서 제가 리머리지 하우스에 있게 된 거고요. 제 동생과 저는 진심으로 서로를 아껴요. 이런 형편에 그건 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 일이라고 당신은 말씀하시겠지요, 저도 전적으로 동감이에요—하지만 사실이 그렇답니다. 하트라이트 씨, 당신은 저희 둘 다를 흡족하게 하시거나, 아니면 저희 둘 다를 흡족하게 못 하시거나예요. 그리고 더 골치 아픈 건, 당신이 오로지 저희 벗에만 내던져지리라는 거예요. 베지 부인은 온갖 으뜸가는 미덕을 두루 갖춘 훌륭한 분이지만, 있으나 마나 한 존재고요. 페얼리 씨는 누구의 벗이 되기엔 너무 병약한 분이에요. 저는 그분이 무슨 병인지 모르고, 의사들도 그분이 무슨 병인지 모르고, 그분 자신도 자기가 무슨 병인지 몰라요. 저희는 다들 신경 탓이라고 하는데, 그렇게 말하면서 저희 가운데 누구도 자기가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 몰라요. 어쨌거나, 오늘 그분을 뵈면 그분의 자잘한 별스러움을 맞춰 드리기를 권해요. 그분의 주화며 판화며 수채화 수집품을 칭찬하시면, 그분의 마음을 얻으실 거예요. 정말이지, 당신이 조용한 시골 생활에 만족하실 수 있다면, 여기서 아주 잘 지내지 못할 까닭이 없어 보여요. 아침 식사부터 점심까지는 페얼리 씨의 그림들이 당신을 붙들어 둘 거예요. 점심 뒤에는 페얼리 양과 제가 사생첩을 둘러메고 나가, 당신의 지도 아래 자연을 엉터리로 옮겨 그리지요. 그림은 그 애의 좋아하는 취미예요, 알아 두세요, 제 취미가 아니라. 여자는 그림을 못 그려요—마음이 너무 들뜨고, 눈이 너무 산만하거든요. 상관없어요—제 동생이 좋아하니, 저는 그 애를 위해 잉글랜드의 어느 여자 못지않게 태연히 물감을 낭비하고 종이를 버린답니다. 저녁 시간으로 말하자면, 저희가 당신이 그 시간을 넘기게 도울 수 있을 것 같아요. 페얼리 양은 연주를 아주 훌륭하게 해요. 제 딱한 처지로 말하면, 저는 음표 하나 다른 음표와 구별하지 못하지만, 체스며 주사위 놀이며 에카르테로 당신과 맞설 수 있고, (여자로서 어쩔 수 없는 흠은 있으나) 심지어 당구로도 당신과 겨룰 수 있어요. 이 계획이 어떠세요? 저희의 조용하고 규칙적인 생활에 스스로를 맞추실 수 있겠어요? 아니면 안절부절못하며, 리머리지 하우스의 이 따분한 분위기 속에서 남몰래 변화와 모험을 갈망하실 참인가요?”\n\n그녀는 여기까지 그 우아하게 놀리는 듯한 말투로 쉼 없이 이야기했고, 나로서는 예의상 요구되는 대수롭잖은 대꾸 말고는 아무런 끼어듦도 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 마지막 물음 속 표현의 굽이, 아니 그보다는 우연히 나온 한 마디 “모험”이라는 말이, 그 입술에서 가벼이 떨어졌음에도, 흰옷을 입은 여인과의 만남으로 내 생각을 되돌려 놓았다. 그리고 그 낯선 이가 스스로 페얼리 부인을 언급한 것이 나에게 일러 준 그 연관—정신병원에서 달아난 이름 없는 도망자와 리머리지 하우스의 옛 안주인 사이에 한때 틀림없이 존재했을 그 연관—을 밝혀내라고 나를 재촉했다.\n\n“설령 제가 인간 가운데 가장 안절부절못하는 사람이라 해도.” 내가 말했다. “한동안은 모험을 갈망할 위험이 없을 겁니다. 이 집에 당도하기 바로 전날 밤, 저는 한 가지 모험을 만났거든요. 그리고 그 놀라움과 흥분은, 장담하건대, 홀콤 양, 훨씬 더 긴 기간까지는 아니더라도 제가 컴벌랜드에 머무는 내내 저에게 남아 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“어머, 정말요, 하트라이트 씨! 들어 봐도 될까요?”\n\n“들으실 자격이 있으시지요. 그 모험의 주된 인물은 저에게는 생판 낯선 이였고, 어쩌면 당신에게도 생판 낯선 이일지 모릅니다. 그러나 그 여인은 분명, 돌아가신 페얼리 부인의 이름을 더없이 진심 어린 고마움과 흠모의 말로 입에 올렸습니다.”\n\n“제 어머니의 이름을요! 형언할 수 없이 흥미롭군요. 어서 계속하세요.”\n\n나는 즉시, 내가 흰옷을 입은 여인을 만나게 된 정황을 있었던 그대로 낱낱이 이야기했다. 그리고 그녀가 페얼리 부인과 리머리지 하우스에 대해 나에게 한 말을 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 되풀이했다.\n\n홀콤 양의 밝고 결연한 눈은 이야기의 처음부터 끝까지 내 눈을 골똘히 들여다보았다. 그녀의 얼굴은 생생한 흥미와 놀라움을 드러냈으나, 그 이상은 아무것도 드러내지 않았다. 그녀도 나 자신과 마찬가지로 이 수수께끼의 어떤 실마리도 알지 못하는 것이 분명했다.\n\n“제 어머니를 가리키는 그 말들, 정말 확실한가요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“틀림없습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “그녀가 누구든 간에, 그 여인은 한때 리머리지 마을에서 학교를 다녔고, 페얼리 부인에게 각별한 친절을 받았으며, 그 친절을 고맙게 기억하여 이 집안의 살아 있는 모든 이들에게 애정 어린 관심을 느끼고 있었습니다. 그녀는 페얼리 부인과 그 남편이 둘 다 세상을 떠난 것을 알고 있었고, 페얼리 양에 대해서는 마치 어린 시절 서로 알고 지내기라도 한 양 이야기했습니다.”\n\n“그녀가 이 고장 사람은 아니라고 잡아뗐다고, 그렇게 말씀하셨던 것 같은데요?”\n\n“네, 그녀는 자기가 햄프셔에서 왔다고 하더군요.”\n\n“그리고 그 이름은 끝내 알아내지 못하셨고요?”\n\n“전혀요.”\n\n“참 기이하네요. 하트라이트 씨, 당신이 그 가엾은 이에게 자유를 준 것은 아주 옳은 일이었다고 생각해요. 당신 앞에서 그 자유를 누리기에 마땅찮음을 드러낼 만한 짓은 그녀가 아무것도 하지 않은 듯하니까요. 하지만 그 이름을 알아내는 데 조금만 더 단호하셨더라면 하고 아쉬워요. 저희는 어떻게든 이 수수께끼를 정말 풀어내야 해요. 페얼리 씨나 제 동생에게는 아직 이 이야기를 하지 않으시는 게 좋겠어요. 그 두 사람 다, 분명히, 그 여인이 누구인지도, 저희와 관련된 그녀의 지난 내력이 무엇일 수 있는지도 저 자신만큼이나 전혀 모르거든요. 게다가 그 둘은, 저마다 사뭇 다른 방식으로 다소 신경이 예민하고 여려서, 당신은 공연히 한 사람은 안절부절못하게 하고 다른 한 사람은 겁먹게 할 뿐일 거예요. 저 자신으로 말하면, 저는 온통 호기심으로 불타오르고 있어서, 이 순간부터 제 온 기운을 이 알아내는 일에 바치겠어요. 제 어머니가 두 번째 혼인 뒤 이곳에 오셨을 때, 그분은 지금 있는 그대로의 마을 학교를 분명 세우셨어요. 하지만 옛 교사들은 다 죽었거나 다른 데로 갔으니, 그쪽에서는 밝혀질 것을 바랄 수 없어요. 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 유일한 다른 방도는——”\n\n이 대목에서 하인이 들어오는 바람에 저희 이야기가 끊겼다. 하인은 페얼리 씨의 전갈을 가져왔는데, 내가 아침 식사를 마치는 대로 나를 기꺼이 만나 뵙고 싶어 하신다는 것이었다.\n\n“복도에서 기다리게.” 홀콤 양이 그 특유의 빠르고 시원시원한 태도로 나를 대신해 하인에게 답했다. “하트라이트 씨가 곧 나가실 테니.” 그러고는 다시 나를 향해 말을 이었다. “제가 말씀드리려던 건, 제 동생과 제가 제 어머니의 편지를 잔뜩 모아 두었다는 거예요. 제 아버지와 그 애 아버지 앞으로 부친 편지들이지요. 달리 정보를 얻을 방도가 없으니, 저는 오전 시간을 어머니가 페얼리 씨와 주고받은 서신을 훑어보는 데 쓰겠어요. 그분은 런던을 좋아하셔서 시골집을 늘 비우셨고, 어머니는 그럴 때면 리머리지에서 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그분께 적어 알리곤 하셨거든요. 어머니의 편지에는 그분이 그토록 깊은 관심을 쏟으셨던 그 학교에 대한 언급이 가득해요. 그러니 저희가 다시 만날 무렵이면 제가 무언가를 알아냈을 공산이 크다고 봐요. 점심시간은 두 시예요, 하트라이트 씨. 그때쯤 제 동생을 당신께 소개하는 기쁨을 누리겠고, 오후에는 이웃을 마차로 두루 돌며 저희가 아끼는 온갖 경치를 당신께 보여 드리는 데 쓰겠어요. 그럼 두 시까지, 안녕히.”\n\n그녀는 자기가 하는 모든 것과 자기가 하는 모든 말을 특징짓는 그 발랄한 우아함, 그 흐뭇한 세련된 스스럼없음으로 나에게 고갯짓을 하고는, 방의 아래쪽 끝에 있는 문으로 사라졌다. 그녀가 나를 떠나자마자, 나는 복도 쪽으로 발걸음을 돌려, 처음으로 페얼리 씨를 뵈러 가는 길에 하인을 뒤따랐다.\n\n# VII\n\n나를 안내하는 이는 나를 위층으로 이끌어, 지난밤 내가 잔 침실로 도로 이어지는 복도로 데려갔다. 그러고는 그 옆방 문을 열며 안을 들여다보아 달라 청했다.\n\n“주인 나리의 분부로, 나리께서 쓰실 거실을 보여 드리고, 위치와 채광이 마음에 드시는지 여쭈러 왔습니다, 나리.” 하인이 말했다.\n\n그 방과, 그 방에 딸린 온갖 것이 마음에 들지 않았다면 나는 실로 여간 까다로운 사람이 아니었을 것이다. 활 모양의 퇴창은 아침에 침실에서 감탄하며 바라본 바로 그 사랑스러운 풍경을 내다보고 있었다. 가구는 호사와 아름다움의 극치였다. 한복판의 탁자는 화사하게 장정된 책들과 우아한 필기도구, 아름다운 꽃들로 환했다. 창가의 두 번째 탁자는 수채화를 표구하는 데 필요한 온갖 재료로 덮여 있었고, 거기에는 마음대로 펴거나 접을 수 있는 작은 이젤이 하나 붙어 있었다. 벽에는 화사하게 물들인 사라사가 드리워져 있었고, 바닥에는 옥수숫빛과 붉은빛의 인도산 돗자리가 깔려 있었다. 그것은 내가 여태 본 가장 어여쁘고 가장 호사스러운 작은 거실이었고, 나는 더없이 뜨거운 열의로 감탄했다.\n\n근엄한 하인은 너무나 잘 길들여진지라 티끌만 한 만족감도 내비치지 않았다. 내 찬사의 말이 죄다 바닥나자 그는 얼음장같이 정중하게 절하고는, 다시 복도로 나가라며 소리 없이 문을 열어 주었다.\n\n우리는 모퉁이를 돌아 긴 두 번째 복도로 접어들었고, 그 끝에서 짧은 층계를 올라, 작은 원형의 위층 홀을 가로질러, 짙은 초록 나사 천으로 덮인 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 하인은 이 문을 열고 나를 몇 야드 더 이끌어 두 번째 문 앞으로 데려가더니, 그것마저 열어, 우리 앞에 드리운 옅은 바다빛 초록 비단 커튼 두 폭을 드러냈다. 그중 한 폭을 소리 없이 걷어 올리고는, 나직이 “하트라이트 씨입니다”라는 말을 읊고, 나를 남겨 두고 떠났다.\n\n나는 웅장하게 조각된 천장이 있고, 바닥에는 발밑에 벨벳 더미라도 밟는 듯 두툼하고 부드러운 양탄자가 깔린, 크고 높다란 방 안에 있었다. 방의 한쪽 벽은 나로서는 아주 처음 보는 어떤 진귀한 상감 목재로 만든 긴 책장이 차지하고 있었다. 그것은 높이가 여섯 자를 넘지 않았고, 꼭대기는 서로 일정한 간격으로 늘어놓은 대리석 조각상들로 꾸며져 있었다. 맞은편에는 고풍스러운 장식장 둘이 서 있었고, 그 사이, 그 위에는 유리로 보호된 성모자화(聖母子畵)가 걸려 있었는데, 액자 아래쪽 도금한 명패에 라파엘로의 이름이 적혀 있었다. 문 안쪽에 서 있는 내 오른편과 왼편에는 뷸 세공과 쪽매붙임으로 꾸민 장식장과 작은 받침대들이 있었고, 그 위에는 드레스덴 자기 인형들과, 진귀한 꽃병들, 상아 장식물들, 그리고 금과 은과 보석으로 모서리마다 반짝이는 노리개와 골동품들이 잔뜩 놓여 있었다. 나를 마주한 방의 아래쪽 끝에서는, 창들이 가려지고 햇빛은 문에 드리운 커튼과 똑같은 옅은 바다빛 초록의 커다란 블라인드로 누그러져 있었다. 그렇게 만들어진 빛은 감미롭도록 부드럽고 신비로우며 은은했다. 그 빛은 방 안의 온갖 물건에 고루 내려앉았다. 그 빛은 이곳을 사로잡은 깊은 정적과 그윽한 은둔의 분위기를 한층 짙게 하는 데 이바지했다. 그리고 그 빛은, 한쪽 팔걸이에 독서용 서견대가, 다른 쪽에 작은 탁자가 달린 커다란 안락의자에 나른하게 몸을 기댄 채 무기력하게 늘어져 있는 저택 주인의 외로운 형체를, 어울리는 안식의 후광으로 에워싸고 있었다.\n\n한 사내가 화장실 밖으로 나와 있을 때, 그리고 마흔을 넘겼을 때의 그 생김새를 그의 나잇대를 짐작하는 믿을 만한 길잡이로 받아들일 수 있다면—그건 자못 미덥지 못한 일이지만—내가 그를 보았을 때 페얼리 씨의 나이는 쉰이 넘고 예순은 안 되는 것으로 어림잡는 것이 무리 없어 보였다. 수염 없는 그의 얼굴은 야위고 지치고 훤히 비칠 만큼 창백했으나 주름은 없었다. 코는 높고 매부리 같았다. 눈은 흐릿한 잿빛 도는 파랑으로 크고 튀어나왔으며, 눈꺼풀 가장자리께가 다소 붉었다. 머리칼은 숱이 적고 보기에 부드러웠으며, 회색으로 바뀌는 제 변화를 가장 늦게 드러내는 그 옅은 모래빛이었다. 그는 천보다 훨씬 얇은 어떤 감으로 지은 짙은 프록코트에, 티끌 하나 없이 흰 조끼와 바지를 입고 있었다. 발은 여자처럼 앙증맞았고, 담황색 비단 양말에 자그마한 여자 같은 청동빛 가죽 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 두 개의 반지가 그의 희고 섬세한 손을 꾸미고 있었는데, 그 값어치는 경험 없는 내 눈으로도 거의 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼임을 알아챌 수 있었다. 전체로 보아 그는 연약하고 나른하게 짜증스러우며 지나치게 세련된 인상을 풍겼다—사내에게 결부되어 유난히, 그리고 불쾌하게 섬세한 무언가이면서, 동시에 그것을 여자의 생김새에 옮겨 놓았다 한들 결코 자연스럽거나 어울려 보이지는 못했을 무언가였다. 아침에 홀콤 양을 겪은 터라 나는 이 집의 누구에게든 호감을 가질 마음가짐이 되어 있었으나, 페얼리 씨를 처음 본 순간 내 호의는 단호히 문을 닫아걸었다.\n\n그에게 더 가까이 다가가면서, 나는 그가 처음 짐작한 만큼 그렇게 아주 일 없이 있는 것은 아님을 알아챘다. 그의 곁 큰 원탁 위, 다른 진귀하고 아름다운 물건들 틈에는, 흑단과 은으로 만든 자그마한 장식장이 놓여 있었는데, 짙은 자줏빛 벨벳을 안에 댄 작은 서랍들에 온갖 모양과 크기의 주화가 늘어놓여 있었다. 그 서랍 가운데 하나가 그의 의자에 딸린 작은 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 그 곁에는 자그마한 보석 세공용 붓 몇 자루와 섀미 가죽 “뭉치” 하나, 그리고 작은 액체 병 하나가, 주화에서 발견될 법한 우연한 얼룩을 갖가지로 지우는 데 쓰이기를 기다리고 있었다. 내가 그의 의자에서 알맞은 거리 안으로 다가가 절하려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 그의 연약하고 흰 손가락들은 내 서투른 눈에는 가장자리가 너덜너덜한 지저분한 백랍 메달처럼 보이는 무언가를 무기력하게 만지작거리고 있었다.\n\n“리머리지에 당신을 모시게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다, 하트라이트 씨.” 그가 짜증스레 갈라지는 목소리로 말했는데, 그 목소리는 도무지 유쾌하달 수 없는 방식으로, 거슬리게 높은 음조와 졸린 듯 나른한 발음을 뒤섞어 놓았다. “앉으시지요. 그리고 의자는 굳이 옮기지 마시고요. 제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 움직임이든 저에게는 지독히도 괴롭답니다. 당신 화실은 보셨습니까? 쓸 만하던가요?”\n\n“방금 그 방을 보고 오는 길입니다, 페얼리 씨. 그리고 장담하건대——”\n\n그는 눈을 감고 흰 손 하나를 애원하듯 치켜들어 내 말을 문장 한복판에서 막았다. 나는 놀라 말을 멈추었다. 그러자 그 갈라지는 목소리가 나에게 이런 설명을 베풀어 주었다—\n\n“부디 용서하세요. 그런데 조금 더 낮은 음으로 말씀해 주실 수 있을까요? 제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 큰 소리든 저에게는 형언할 수 없는 고문이랍니다. 병자를 너그러이 봐주시겠지요? 저는 제 딱한 건강 상태가 누구에게든 하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 말을 당신께 할 뿐이에요. 그래요. 그런데 방이 정말 마음에 드십니까?”\n\n“그보다 더 어여쁘고 더 편안한 것은 바랄 수 없을 정도입니다.” 나는 목소리를 낮추며 대답했고, 페얼리 씨의 이기적인 허세와 페얼리 씨의 딱한 신경이 하나이자 같은 것임을 벌써 알아채기 시작했다.\n\n“참으로 기쁘군요. 하트라이트 씨, 여기서 당신은 자신의 처지가 마땅히 인정받는 것을 보게 될 겁니다. 이 집에는 예술가의 사회적 지위에 대한 저 끔찍한 영국식 야만의 감정 따위는 없어요. 제 젊은 시절의 많은 부분을 외국에서 보낸지라, 그 점에서 저는 섬나라의 껍질을 아주 벗어 던졌지요. 이웃의 지주 양반들—가증스러운 말이지만 쓸 수밖에 없나 봅니다—에 대해서도 같은 말을 할 수 있으면 좋으련만. 그들은 예술에서는 딱한 고트족이랍니다, 하트라이트 씨. 정말이지, 카를 오세가 티치아노를 위해 그 붓을 주워 주는 걸 봤다면 놀라 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 사람들이지요. 이 주화 쟁반을 장식장에 도로 넣고, 그 옆의 것을 저에게 건네주시겠어요? 제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 수고든 저에게는 말할 수 없이 언짢답니다. 그래요. 고맙습니다.”\n\n방금 몸소 예를 들어 베풀어 준 그 진보적인 사회 이론에 대한 실천적 논평인 양, 페얼리 씨의 태연한 청은 나를 다소 우습게 만들었다. 나는 되도록 공손하게 서랍 하나를 도로 넣고 다른 것을 그에게 건넸다. 그는 곧바로 새 주화 무리와 작은 붓들을 만지작거리기 시작했고, 나에게 말하는 내내 그것들을 나른히 바라보며 감탄했다.\n\n“천 번 감사하고 천 번 사과합니다. 주화를 좋아하십니까? 그렇군요. 예술 취향 말고도 또 하나의 공통 취향을 우리가 지녔다니 참으로 기쁩니다. 자, 우리 사이의 금전 문제인데요—말씀해 주세요—만족스러우십니까?”\n\n“더없이 만족스럽습니다, 페얼리 씨.”\n\n“참으로 기쁘군요. 그리고—다음은 뭐였더라? 아! 생각났어요. 그래요. 당신이 고맙게도 받아들여 예술적 재능의 혜택을 저에게 베풀어 주시는 그 보수에 관해서는, 첫 주가 끝날 무렵 제 집사가 당신을 찾아뵙고 당신의 뜻을 여쭐 겁니다. 그리고—다음은 뭐였더라? 묘하지요? 훨씬 더 할 말이 많았는데, 아주 까맣게 잊어버린 모양입니다. 종을 좀 울려 주시겠어요? 저 구석에 있어요. 그래요. 고맙습니다.”\n\n내가 종을 울리자, 새 하인 하나가 소리 없이 나타났다—판에 박힌 미소에 완벽하게 빗질된 머리를 한 외국인—온몸이 하나부터 열까지 시종인 자였다.\n\n“루이.” 페얼리 씨가 주화용 자그마한 붓 하나로 손가락 끝을 나른하게 털며 말했다. “오늘 아침 내 수첩에 몇 가지 적어 두었다. 내 수첩을 찾아라. 천 번 용서하세요, 하트라이트 씨, 제가 당신을 지루하게 하는 게 아닌지요.”\n\n내가 대답하기도 전에 그가 지친 듯 다시 눈을 감았고, 또 그가 나를 지루하게 한 것이 정말 틀림없었으므로, 나는 잠자코 앉아 라파엘로의 성모자화를 올려다보았다. 그동안 시종은 방을 나가더니, 이내 작은 상아 수첩을 들고 돌아왔다. 페얼리 씨는 먼저 가볍게 한숨지어 마음을 놓은 뒤, 한 손으로 수첩이 툭 펼쳐지게 두고, 다른 손으로는 더 이상의 분부를 기다리라는 신호로 자그마한 붓을 치켜들었다.\n\n“그래. 바로 그거야!” 페얼리 씨가 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다. “루이, 저 화첩을 내려라.” 그는 말하며 창가의 마호가니 받침대 위에 놓인 여러 화첩을 가리켰다. “아니. 초록 등의 것 말고—그건 내 렘브란트 에칭이 들어 있으니까, 하트라이트 씨. 에칭을 좋아하십니까? 그래요? 또 하나의 공통 취향을 우리가 지녔다니 참으로 기쁘군요. 붉은 등의 화첩이다, 루이. 떨어뜨리지 마라! 루이가 저 화첩을 떨어뜨리면 제가 어떤 고문을 겪을지 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다, 하트라이트 씨. 의자 위에 안전하게 놓였나? 당신이 보시기엔 안전한가요, 하트라이트 씨? 그래요? 참으로 기쁩니다. 정말 아주 안전하다고 여기신다면, 저 소묘들을 좀 봐 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요. 루이, 물러가라. 이 멍청한 것. 내가 수첩을 들고 있는 게 안 보이느냐? 내가 이걸 들고 있고 싶어 하는 줄 아느냐? 그럼 시키지 않아도 이 수첩을 나에게서 받아 가면 될 것을. 천 번 용서하세요, 하트라이트 씨. 하인이란 것들은 참 멍청하지요, 안 그래요? 어서 말씀해 주세요—저 소묘들을 어떻게 보십니까? 어느 경매에서 아주 참혹한 꼴로 왔지요—지난번에 들여다봤을 때 끔찍한 상인과 중개인 손 냄새가 나는 것만 같았어요. 당신이 저것들을 맡아 주실 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n내 신경은 페얼리 씨의 콧구멍을 거스른 그 천한 손가락 냄새를 알아챌 만큼 섬세하지는 못했으나, 내 안목은 그 소묘들을 넘겨 보는 동안 그 값어치를 알아볼 만큼은 충분히 다져져 있었다. 그것들은 대부분 정말 훌륭한 영국 수채화의 표본들이었고, 예전 소유자의 손에서 받은 듯한 대접보다는 훨씬 나은 대접을 받을 만한 것들이었다.\n\n“이 소묘들은.” 내가 대답했다. “조심스레 펴서 표구할 필요가 있습니다. 그리고 제 소견으로는, 그럴 만한 값어치가 충분히——”\n\n“실례합니다.” 페얼리 씨가 끼어들었다. “당신이 말씀하시는 동안 제가 눈을 감아도 괜찮겠습니까? 이 정도 빛조차 제 눈에는 너무 과하답니다. 그래요?”\n\n“이 소묘들은 온갖 시간과 수고를 들일 만한 값어치가 충분히——라고 말하려던 참이었습니다.”\n\n페얼리 씨가 별안간 다시 눈을 뜨더니, 어쩔 줄 모르는 놀라움의 표정으로 창 쪽으로 눈을 굴렸다.\n\n“부디 저를 용서해 주세요, 하트라이트 씨.” 그가 힘없이 파르르 떨며 말했다. “그런데 정원에서—제 개인 정원, 저 아래에서—끔찍한 아이들 소리가 들리는 게 분명해요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 페얼리 씨. 저는 아무 소리도 못 들었습니다만.”\n\n“제 부탁 하나 들어주세요—제 딱한 신경을 그토록 잘 맞춰 주셨으니—블라인드 한 귀퉁이를 들어 올려 주는 호의를 베풀어 주세요. 저에게 햇빛이 들게는 마시고요, 하트라이트 씨! 블라인드를 올리셨나요? 그래요? 그럼 부디 정원을 들여다보고 아주 확실히 확인해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠어요?”\n\n나는 이 새로운 청에 따랐다. 정원은 사방이 빈틈없이 담으로 둘러싸여 있었다. 그 성스러운 은둔의 어느 구석에도 크든 작든 사람이라고는 하나도 나타나지 않았다. 나는 그 흐뭇한 사실을 페얼리 씨에게 알렸다.\n\n“천 번 감사합니다. 제 착각이었나 봅니다. 다행히도 이 집에는 아이가 없지요. 하지만 하인들(신경이라고는 없이 태어난 자들)이 마을 아이들을 부추기거든요. 그런 개구쟁이들이라니—오, 저런, 그런 개구쟁이들이라니! 고백해도 될까요, 하트라이트 씨?—저는 아이들의 구조가 개혁되기를 못 견디게 바랍니다. 자연의 유일한 발상이란 그것들을 끊임없는 소음을 만들어 내는 기계로 만드는 것뿐인가 봐요. 정녕 우리의 흐뭇한 라파엘로의 구상이 한없이 더 낫지 않겠어요?”\n\n그는 성모화를 가리켰는데, 그 윗부분에는 담황색 구름의 풍선 위에 턱을 얹을 앉을자리를 하늘의 은총으로 마련받은, 이탈리아 예술의 판에 박힌 아기 천사들이 그려져 있었다.\n\n“정말 모범적인 가족이지요!” 페얼리 씨가 아기 천사들을 곁눈질하며 말했다. “저렇게 어여쁜 둥근 얼굴에, 저렇게 어여쁜 보드라운 날개에—그리고 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요. 돌아다닐 지저분한 작은 다리도 없고, 비명 지를 시끄러운 작은 허파도 없지요. 지금의 구조보다 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어난가요! 허락해 주신다면 다시 눈을 감겠습니다. 그런데 저 소묘들을 정말 맡아 주실 수 있겠지요? 참으로 기쁩니다. 그 밖에 정할 게 또 있나요? 있다면, 제가 잊어버린 모양입니다. 루이를 다시 부를까요?”\n\n이 무렵 나도 내 쪽에서, 페얼리 씨가 그의 쪽에서 그러한 것 못지않게 이 면담을 얼른 끝맺고 싶어진지라, 필요한 제안을 내 책임으로 내놓아 하인을 부를 필요를 없애 보아야겠다고 생각했다.\n\n“페얼리 씨, 논의할 남은 유일한 문제는.” 내가 말했다. “제가 두 젊은 숙녀에게 전하기로 되어 있는 사생 지도에 관한 것인 듯합니다.”\n\n“아! 바로 그거예요.” 페얼리 씨가 말했다. “그 부분의 조정에까지 파고들 만큼 기운이 있으면 좋으련만—그렇질 못해요. 당신의 친절한 도움으로 덕을 보는 숙녀들이, 하트라이트 씨, 스스로 정하고 결정하고 그래야지요. 제 조카딸은 당신의 그 매혹적인 예술을 좋아합니다. 제 딱한 결점을 스스로 알아챌 만큼은 그것을 알지요. 부디 그 애에게 공을 들여 주세요. 그래요. 그 밖에 또 있나요? 없군요. 우리는 서로를 아주 잘 이해했지요—그렇지 않나요? 저에게는 당신을 그 흐뭇한 일에서 더 붙들어 둘 권리가 없어요—그렇지요? 모든 걸 정해 놓으니 참 유쾌하군요—볼일을 마치니 참 시원스레 마음이 놓입니다. 화첩을 당신 방으로 나르게 루이를 좀 불러 주시겠어요?”\n\n“괜찮으시다면, 페얼리 씨, 제가 직접 그것을 나르겠습니다.”\n\n“정말요? 그럴 만한 기운이 있으신가요? 그토록 기운이 세다니 참 좋군요! 정말 떨어뜨리지 않으실 수 있겠어요? 리머리지에 당신을 모시게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다, 하트라이트 씨. 저는 워낙 앓는 사람이라 당신 벗을 많이 누리기를 감히 바라기 어렵네요. 부디 문을 쾅 닫지 않도록, 화첩을 떨어뜨리지 않도록 각별히 애써 주시겠어요? 고맙습니다. 커튼도 부디 살살—거기서 나는 아주 작은 소리조차 칼처럼 제 몸을 관통한답니다. 그래요. 안녕히 가세요!”\n\n바다빛 초록 커튼이 닫히고 두 겹의 나사 문이 내 뒤로 닫히자, 나는 그 너머 작은 원형 홀에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 길고 호사스러운 안도의 숨을 내쉬었다. 페얼리 씨의 방 바깥으로 다시 나와 있는 나를 발견하는 것은, 깊이 잠수했다가 물 위로 떠오르는 것과도 같았다.\n\n오전 시간을 보내려고 어여쁜 작은 화실에 편안히 자리 잡자마자, 내가 다다른 첫 번째 결심은, 그가 다시 방문해 달라며 특별히 초대하는 극히 있을 법하지 않은 경우가 아니고서는, 저택 주인이 거처하는 처소 쪽으로는 두 번 다시 발걸음을 돌리지 않겠다는 것이었다. 페얼리 씨에 관한 이 흡족한 앞으로의 처신 방침을 정해 놓자, 나는 곧 마음의 평온을 되찾았다—고용주의 거만한 스스럼없음과 뻔뻔한 정중함이 잠시나마 나에게서 앗아 갔던 그 평온을. 오전의 남은 시간은 소묘들을 훑어보고, 묶음으로 정리하고, 너덜너덜한 가장자리를 다듬고, 그것들을 표구하는 일에 앞서 필요한 다른 채비를 갖추는 데 제법 유쾌하게 흘러갔다. 어쩌면 나는 이보다 더 많이 나아갔어야 했는지도 모른다. 그러나 점심시간이 다가올수록 나는 안절부절못하고 마음이 뒤숭숭해져, 그 일이 한낱 보잘것없는 손일에 지나지 않았음에도 도무지 거기에 주의를 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다.\n\n두 시에 나는 다소 조마조마한 마음으로 다시 아침 식사실로 내려갔다. 저택의 그 자리에 곧 다시 나타나는 일에는 얼마간 흥미로운 기대가 걸려 있었다. 페얼리 양에게 소개되는 일이 이제 코앞에 다가와 있었고, 홀콤 양이 어머니의 편지를 뒤져 그녀가 내다본 결과를 얻어 냈다면, 흰옷을 입은 여인의 수수께끼를 밝힐 때가 온 것이었다.\n\n# VIII\n\n방에 들어서니, 홀콤 양과 한 노부인이 점심 식탁에 앉아 있었다.\n\n그 노부인은, 나에게 소개되고 보니, 페얼리 양의 옛 가정교사 베지 부인이었다. 아침 식탁에서 발랄한 동행이 나에게 “온갖 으뜸가는 미덕을 두루 갖추었으나 있으나 마나 한 존재”라고 짤막하게 일러 준 바로 그 사람이었다. 이 노부인의 성품에 대한 홀콤 양의 소묘가 얼마나 참된지에 대해, 나는 그저 변변찮은 증언을 보태는 것 말고는 할 수 있는 일이 거의 없다. 베지 부인은 인간의 침착함과 여성의 상냥함을 그대로 몸으로 빚어 놓은 듯했다. 잔잔한 삶을 잔잔히 누리는 즐거움이 통통하고 온화한 얼굴에 졸린 미소로 어려 있었다. 우리 가운데 어떤 이는 삶을 내달려 지나고, 어떤 이는 삶을 어슬렁어슬렁 지난다. 베지 부인은 삶을 앉은 채로 지났다. 이르나 늦으나 집 안에 앉아 있었고, 정원에 앉아 있었고, 복도의 뜻밖의 창가 자리에 앉아 있었고, 벗들이 산책에 데리고 나가려 하면 (야전용 접이 의자에) 앉았고, 무엇을 바라보기 전에도, 무엇을 말하기 전에도, 가장 흔한 물음에 예, 아니요를 답하기 전에도 앉았다—입가에는 언제나 같은 잔잔한 미소를, 머리는 언제나 같은 멍하니 골똘한 각도로, 손과 팔은 언제나 같은 아늑히 편안한 자세로 둔 채, 집안 형편이 어찌 바뀌든 한결같았다. 온화하고 고분고분하며, 이루 말할 수 없이 평온하고 무해한 노부인이었으니, 태어난 그 시각부터 실제로 살아 있어 왔으리라는 생각조차 어쩌다도 불러일으키는 법이 없었다. 자연은 이 세상에서 할 일이 너무나 많고, 나란히 존재하는 그토록 어마어마하게 다양한 산물을 만들어 내는 데 골몰해 있으니, 틀림없이 이따금은 한꺼번에 진행하는 그 갖가지 과정을 분간하지 못할 만큼 허둥대고 어수선해질 것이다. 이 관점에서 출발하건대, 베지 부인이 태어날 때 자연은 양배추를 만드는 데 골몰해 있었으며, 그 선량한 부인은 우리 모두의 어머니이신 그분의 마음속에 자리한 채소에 대한 몰두의 결과를 겪은 것이라는 게 언제까지나 나의 개인적인 확신으로 남을 것이다.\n\n“자, 베지 부인.” 홀콤 양이, 곁의 무덤덤한 노부인과 대비되어 그 어느 때보다 밝고 예리하고 시원시원해 보이는 얼굴로 말했다. “무얼 드시겠어요? 커틀릿?”\n\n베지 부인은 오목한 손을 식탁 가장자리에 포개고 온화하게 웃으며 말했다. “그래요, 그러지요.”\n\n“하트라이트 씨 맞은편에 있는 게 뭐죠? 삶은 닭인가요? 베지 부인, 부인은 커틀릿보다 삶은 닭을 더 좋아하시는 줄 알았는데요?”\n\n베지 부인은 오목한 손을 식탁 가장자리에서 떼어 대신 무릎에 포개고, 삶은 닭을 향해 곰곰이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “그래요, 그러지요.”\n\n“그럼 오늘은 어느 걸 드시겠어요? 하트라이트 씨가 닭을 좀 덜어 드릴까요? 아니면 제가 커틀릿을 좀 덜어 드릴까요?”\n\n베지 부인은 오목한 손 하나를 다시 식탁 가장자리에 올리고, 졸린 듯 머뭇거리며 말했다. “아무거나 좋을 대로요.”\n\n“어머나! 그건 제 취향이 아니라 부인 취향의 문제잖아요. 둘 다 조금씩 드시는 게 어때요? 그리고 하트라이트 씨가 부인께 고기를 발라 드리고 싶어 안달이 난 얼굴이니, 닭부터 시작하시고요.”\n\n베지 부인은 다른 오목한 손을 다시 식탁 가장자리에 올렸다. 한순간 흐릿하게 밝아졌다가 다음 순간 다시 사그라들더니, 고분고분 고개를 숙이며 말했다. “그럼 그러지요, 나리.”\n\n정녕 온화하고 고분고분하며 이루 말할 수 없이 평온하고 무해한 노부인이렷다! 그러나 베지 부인에 대해서는 지금은 이쯤이면 어쩌면 충분하리라.\n\n이 내내 페얼리 양은 나타날 기미가 없었다. 우리는 점심을 다 먹었으나, 여전히 그녀는 나타나지 않았다. 무엇 하나 놓치는 법 없는 예리한 눈의 홀콤 양은, 내가 이따금 문 쪽으로 던지는 눈길을 알아챘다.\n\n“무슨 생각이신지 알겠어요, 하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “다른 제자가 어떻게 됐는지 궁금하신 거지요. 그 애는 아래층에 내려와 두통은 나았지만, 점심에 함께할 만큼 입맛은 아직 돌아오지 않았답니다. 저에게 맡겨 주신다면, 정원 어딘가에서 그 애를 찾아낼 수 있을 것 같아요.”\n\n그녀는 곁의 의자에 놓인 양산을 집어 들고, 방 아래쪽의 잔디밭으로 이어지는 긴 창문을 통해 앞장서 나갔다. 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 우리는 베지 부인을 여전히 식탁에 앉힌 채 남겨 두고 나왔다. 오목한 손은 여전히 식탁 가장자리에 포갠 채로, 오후 내내 그 자세로 눌러앉은 듯 보였다.\n\n잔디밭을 가로지를 때 홀콤 양이 의미심장하게 나를 바라보며 고개를 저었다.\n\n“당신의 그 신비로운 겪음은.” 그녀가 말했다. “아직도 어울리는 그 한밤의 어둠에 싸인 채로군요. 저는 오전 내내 어머니 편지를 훑어보았지만 아직 아무것도 알아내지 못했어요. 하지만 낙심 마세요, 하트라이트 씨. 이건 호기심의 문제고, 당신에게는 여자를 동맹으로 두었으니까요. 그런 조건이라면 이르든 늦든 성공은 틀림없어요. 편지는 아직 다 보지 못했어요. 아직 세 묶음이 남아 있으니, 제가 저녁 내내 그것에 매달리리라는 것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요.”\n\n그러니, 이렇게 하여 아침에 품은 내 기대 하나가 여전히 이루어지지 않은 셈이었다. 나는 그다음으로, 페얼리 양에게 소개되는 일이 아침 식사 때부터 그녀에 대해 품어 온 기대를 저버리게 될지 궁금해지기 시작했다.\n\n“그런데 페얼리 씨와는 어떻게 지내셨어요?” 우리가 잔디밭을 벗어나 관목숲으로 접어들 때 홀콤 양이 물었다. “오늘 아침 유난히 신경이 곤두서 계셨나요? 대답을 궁리하실 것 없어요, 하트라이트 씨. 궁리할 수밖에 없다는 그 사실 하나로 저에게는 충분하니까요. 그분이 유난히 신경이 곤두서 계셨다는 걸 당신 얼굴에서 알겠어요. 그리고 저는 당신을 같은 상태로 몰아넣고 싶지 않을 만큼 상냥한 사람이니, 더는 묻지 않겠어요.”\n\n그녀가 말하는 동안 우리는 굽이진 오솔길로 접어들어, 미니어처 스위스 산장 모양으로 나무를 엮어 지은 어여쁜 정자에 다가갔다. 문의 계단을 오르는데, 정자의 하나뿐인 방에는 한 젊은 숙녀가 있었다. 그녀는 소박한 탁자 곁에 서서, 나무 틈으로 펼쳐진 황야와 언덕의 내륙 풍경을 내다보며, 곁에 놓인 작은 사생첩의 책장을 넋 놓고 넘기고 있었다. 이 사람이 페얼리 양이었다.\n\n그녀를 어떻게 묘사할 수 있을까? 그녀를 나 자신의 감정에서, 그리고 뒷날 벌어진 그 모든 일에서 어떻게 떼어 놓을 수 있을까? 내 눈이 그녀에게 처음 머물렀을 때의 모습 그대로—지금 이 지면에서 그녀를 보려는 눈에 마땅히 비쳐야 할 모습 그대로—그녀를 어떻게 다시 볼 수 있을까?\n\n내가 처음 그녀를 본 그 자리, 그 자세로 뒷날 그려 둔 로라 페얼리의 수채화가, 이 글을 쓰는 동안 내 책상 위에 놓여 있다. 그것을 바라보노라면, 정자의 어두운 초록빛 도는 갈색 배경에서 밝고 앳된 형체 하나가 환하게 떠오른다. 소박한 모슬린 드레스를 입은 그 형체는, 여린 파랑과 흰색이 서로 갈마드는 널따란 줄무늬로 지어진 것이다. 같은 감으로 만든 스카프가 어깨를 산뜻하고 촘촘히 감싸고 있고, 드레스와 어울리게 리본으로 수수하고 검소하게 꾸민 자연색 밀짚모자가 머리를 덮으며, 얼굴 윗부분에 부드러운 진줏빛 그림자를 드리운다. 그녀의 머리칼은 어찌나 아련하고 옅은 갈색인지—아마빛은 아니면서도 거의 그만큼 밝고, 금빛은 아니면서도 거의 그만큼 윤이 나서—여기저기서 모자의 그림자 속으로 녹아들 듯하다. 그것은 수수하게 가르마를 타 귀 뒤로 넘겨져 있고, 그 선은 이마를 가로지르며 자연스레 물결친다. 눈썹은 머리칼보다 다소 짙고, 눈은 시인들이 그토록 자주 노래하나 실제 삶에서는 좀처럼 볼 수 없는 그 부드럽고 맑은 터키옥빛 파랑이다. 빛깔로도 사랑스럽고 모양으로도 사랑스러운 눈—크고 다정하며 고요히 사려 깊은—그러나 무엇보다도 아름다운 것은, 그 가장 깊은 밑바닥에 깃들어, 표정의 온갖 변화를 뚫고 더 순수하고 더 나은 세계의 빛으로 비쳐 나오는 그 맑은 진실함의 눈빛이었다. 그 눈이 온 얼굴에 뿌리는 매력—더없이 은은하면서도 더없이 또렷이 드러나는—은, 얼굴 다른 곳의 사소한 자연스러운 인간적 흠들을 어찌나 덮고 뒤바꿔 놓는지, 다른 이목구비의 잘잘못을 견주어 가늠하기가 어려울 지경이다. 얼굴 아랫부분이 턱을 향해 너무 여리게 다듬어져 윗부분과 온전히 어여쁜 균형을 이루지 못한다는 것, 코가 매부리 곡선(아무리 그 자체로 완벽하다 한들 여자에게는 언제나 딱딱하고 매정한)을 피하려다 반대쪽 극단으로 조금 치우쳐 이상적인 곧은 선을 놓쳤다는 것, 그리고 그 달콤하고 예민한 입술이 미소 지을 때 살짝 신경질적으로 오므라들어 한쪽 입가가 뺨 쪽으로 조금 당겨 올라간다는 것—이런 것을 알아차리기가 어렵다. 이런 흠들을 다른 여자의 얼굴에서라면 짚어 낼 수 있을지 모르나, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 그것에 눈길을 붙들기가 쉽지 않다. 그것들이 어찌나 은밀하게 그녀의 표정 속 개성적이고 특징적인 온갖 것과 이어져 있는지, 그리고 그 표정이 다른 온갖 이목구비에서 온전히 살아 움직이려면 어찌나 눈의 그 움직이는 충동에 기대는지 모르기 때문이다.\n\n길고 행복한 나날의 정답고 참을성 있는 노고인 나의 이 딱한 초상화가, 나에게 이런 것들을 보여 주는가? 아, 그 어렴풋한 기계적인 그림 속에는 그것들이 얼마나 적고, 내가 그것을 바라보는 마음속에는 얼마나 많은지! 살결 곱고 여린 소녀가 어여쁜 밝은 드레스를 입고 사생첩의 책장을 만지작거리다가, 그것에서 눈을 들어 진실하고 순진한 파란 눈으로 올려다본다—그것이 그림이 말할 수 있는 전부다. 어쩌면 생각과 펜의 더 깊은 손길조차 저희 언어로 말할 수 있는 전부인지도 모른다. 아름다움에 대한 우리의 어렴풋한 관념에 처음으로 생명과 빛과 형체를 주는 여인은, 그녀가 나타나기 전까지 우리에게 알려지지 않은 채 남아 있던 우리 영혼의 빈자리를 채운다. 말로 하기엔 너무 깊고, 생각으로 하기에도 거의 너무 깊은 공감이, 그런 순간에는 감각이 느끼고 표현의 수단이 담아낼 수 있는 매력과는 다른 매력에 의해 건드려진다. 여인의 아름다움 밑에 깔린 그 신비는, 우리 자신의 영혼 속 더 깊은 신비와 혈연을 맺기 전에는 결코 온갖 표현이 미치는 곳 위로 들려 올라가지 않는다. 그때, 오직 그때에야, 그것은 이 세상에서 연필과 펜으로부터 빛이 내리비치는 그 좁은 영역 너머로 건너간 것이다.\n\n그녀를, 당신 안의 그 맥박을 처음으로 뛰게 한 첫 여인을 떠올렸던 것처럼 떠올려 보라—다른 여자들은 그것을 흔들 재주가 없던 그 맥박을. 그 다정하고 솔직한 파란 눈이, 우리 둘 다 그토록 잘 기억하는 그 둘도 없는 눈빛으로, 나의 눈과 마주쳤듯이 당신의 눈과 마주치게 하라. 그녀의 목소리가, 당신이 한때 가장 사랑한 그 음악을, 나의 귀에 그러했듯 당신의 귀에도 그토록 감미롭게 가락 맞추어 들려주게 하라. 이 지면에서 오가는 그녀의 발소리가, 당신의 심장이 한때 그 사뿐한 낙하에 박자를 맞추던 저 다른 발소리와 같게 하라. 그녀를 당신 자신의 공상이 마음속에 기른 아이로 삼으라. 그러면 그녀는, 내 마음속에 깃든 살아 있는 여인으로서, 더욱더 또렷이 당신 안에서 자라날 것이다.\n\n내 눈이 처음 그녀를 바라보았을 때 나에게 몰려든 감정들—우리 모두가 아는, 대부분의 가슴속에서 생겨나 그토록 많은 가슴에서 다시 스러지고 그토록 적은 가슴에서 그 밝은 존재를 되살리는 낯익은 감정들—가운데, 나를 어지럽히고 어리둥절하게 하는 하나가 있었다. 페얼리 양 앞에서 이상하게 어긋나고 까닭 없이 어울리지 않아 보이는 하나가.\n\n살결 고운 얼굴과 머리의 매력, 그 달콤한 표정, 그리고 마음을 끄는 소박한 몸가짐이 자아내는 생생한 인상에 뒤섞여, 어렴풋한 방식으로 무언가 모자란다는 생각을 나에게 넌지시 비추는 또 하나의 인상이 있었다. 어느 때는 그녀에게 무언가 모자란 것처럼, 또 어느 때는 나 자신에게 무언가 모자라, 마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 그녀를 이해하지 못하게 가로막는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그 인상은 언제나 가장 모순된 방식으로 가장 강하게 다가왔다—그녀가 나를 바라볼 때, 다시 말해 내가 그녀 얼굴의 조화와 매력을 가장 뚜렷이 의식하면서도 동시에 도무지 밝혀낼 수 없는 미완의 느낌에 가장 어지러워질 때. 무언가 모자란다, 무언가 모자란다—그것이 어디에 있는지, 그것이 무엇인지, 나는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n이 기묘한 공상의 변덕(그때는 그렇게 여겼다)이 미친 영향은, 페얼리 양과의 첫 만남 동안 나를 편안하게 해 주는 종류의 것이 아니었다. 그녀가 건넨 몇 마디 다정한 환영의 말에도, 나는 답례의 판에 박힌 문구로 그녀에게 고마움을 표할 만큼조차 마음을 가누지 못했다. 나의 머뭇거림을 알아채고는, 당연하게도 그것을 내 쪽의 순간적인 수줍음 탓으로 돌렸을 홀콤 양이, 여느 때처럼 편안하고 시원스럽게 말하는 일을 제 손에 떠맡았다.\n\n“저기 좀 보세요, 하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 탁자 위의 사생첩과, 여전히 그것을 만지작거리는 여리고 자그마한 손을 가리키며 말했다. “드디어 모범 제자를 찾았다는 걸 이젠 인정하시겠지요? 당신이 이 집에 계시다는 말을 듣는 순간, 이 애는 더없이 소중한 사생첩을 움켜쥐고 온 자연을 똑바로 마주 보며 시작하고 싶어 안달이랍니다!”\n\n페얼리 양은 시원스러운 명랑함으로 웃었는데, 그 웃음은 마치 우리 머리 위 햇살의 한 조각이기라도 한 양 그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 환하게 번져 나왔다.\n\n“칭찬받을 게 없는 데서 스스로 칭찬을 받으면 안 되지요.” 그녀가 말했다. 맑고 진실한 파란 눈이 홀콤 양과 나를 번갈아 바라보았다. “그림을 좋아하기는 하지만, 저는 제 무지를 너무나 잘 알아서 시작하고 싶은 마음보다 두려운 마음이 더 커요. 이제 당신이 여기 계신 걸 알고 나니, 어릴 적 수업을 훑어보던 것처럼 제 사생을 훑어보게 돼요—제가 들려드릴 만한 사람이 못 될까 봐 못 견디게 두려워하던 그 시절처럼요.”\n\n그녀는 아주 어여쁘고 소박하게, 그리고 별스럽게 어린애 같은 진지함으로 그 고백을 하고는, 사생첩을 제 쪽 탁자 가까이로 끌어당겼다. 홀콤 양은 그 특유의 시원시원하고 딱 부러지는 태도로 이 자그마한 무안함의 매듭을 곧바로 끊어 냈다.\n\n“잘 그렸든 못 그렸든 그저 그렇든.” 그녀가 말했다. “제자의 사생은 선생의 심판이라는 불의 시련을 거쳐야만 해—그걸로 끝인 거야. 그것들을 마차에 가지고 가서, 하트라이트 씨가 끊임없이 덜컹거리고 방해받는 형편에서 처음으로 그것들을 보게 하는 건 어떨까, 로라? 마차를 타고 가는 내내, 그이가 풍경을 올려다볼 때의 있는 그대로의 자연과, 다시 우리 사생첩을 내려다볼 때의 있는 그대로가 아닌 자연 사이에서 그이를 헷갈리게만 할 수 있다면, 우리는 그이를 칭찬을 늘어놓는다는 마지막 절박한 도피처로 몰아넣고, 우리의 소중한 허영의 깃털을 하나도 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채 그이의 전문가다운 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 수 있을 거야.”\n\n“하트라이트 씨가 저에게는 아무 칭찬도 하지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.” 우리 모두 정자를 나설 때 페얼리 양이 말했다.\n\n“어째서 그런 바람을 말씀하시는지 감히 여쭤도 될까요?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“당신이 저에게 하시는 말은 다 믿을 테니까요.” 그녀가 소박하게 대답했다.\n\n그 몇 마디 말로 그녀는 제 성품 전체의 열쇠를 나에게 저도 모르게 건네주었다—제 천성 속에서 제 진실함의 감각으로부터 순진하게 자라난, 남에 대한 그 너그러운 믿음의 열쇠를. 그때 나는 그저 직관으로 그것을 알았을 뿐이다. 이제 나는 경험으로 그것을 안다.\n\n우리는 약속한 마차 나들이를 위해 지붕 없는 마차에 오르기 전에, 그저 인기척 없는 점심 식탁의 그 자리를 여전히 차지하고 있는 선량한 베지 부인을 깨우려 잠시 기다렸다. 노부인과 홀콤 양은 뒷자리에 앉았고, 페얼리 양과 나는 앞자리에 나란히 앉아, 그 사이에 사생첩을 펼쳐 놓았다—드디어 내 전문가의 눈앞에 버젓이 펼쳐진 것이다. 소묘에 대한 진지한 비평은, 설령 내가 자청해 할 마음이 있었다 한들, 자기 자신과 동생과 여자들 일반이 하는 미술의 우스꽝스러운 면 말고는 아무것도 보지 않겠다는 홀콤 양의 발랄한 결의 탓에 불가능해졌다. 나는 기계적으로 넘겨 본 사생보다, 오간 대화를 훨씬 더 수월하게 기억한다. 그 대화 가운데, 특히 페얼리 양이 얼마간 끼어든 부분은, 마치 겨우 몇 시간 전에 들은 것인 양 여태 내 기억에 생생히 새겨져 있다.\n\n그렇다! 이 첫날 나는 그녀가 곁에 있는 매력에 홀려 나 자신과 내 처지를 잊게 두었음을 인정하겠다. 연필을 쓰고 물감을 섞는 일에 대해 그녀가 나에게 던진 더없이 사소한 물음들, 내가 가르칠 수 있는 온갖 것을 배우고 내가 보여 줄 수 있는 온갖 것을 알아내려는 그토록 간절한 열망으로 내 눈을 들여다보는 그 사랑스러운 눈의 더없이 미미한 표정 변화가, 우리가 지나친 가장 빼어난 풍경보다도, 물결치는 황야와 평평한 백사장 위로 서로 흘러드는 가장 웅장한 빛과 그늘의 변화보다도 더 많은 내 주의를 끌었다. 어느 때든, 어떤 인간적 관심의 형편에서든, 우리가 그 한가운데 살아가는 자연계의 사물들이 우리의 가슴과 마음을 실로 얼마나 조금밖에 붙들지 못하는지를 보는 것은 기이하지 아니한가? 우리가 괴로울 때 위안을, 기쁠 때 공감을 자연에서 구하는 것은 오직 책 속에서뿐이다. 근대 시가 그토록 많이, 그토록 유창하게 묘사하는 저 무생물 세계의 아름다움에 대한 감탄은, 우리 가운데 가장 나은 이에게조차 우리 본성의 타고난 본능 가운데 하나가 아니다. 어린 시절 우리 가운데 누구도 그것을 지니지 못한다. 배우지 못한 남자든 여자든 그것을 지니지 못한다. 바다와 뭍의 끊임없이 변하는 경이 한가운데서 가장 남김없이 삶을 보내는 이들이야말로, 제 생업의 인간적 관심과 직접 결부되지 않은 자연의 온갖 모습에 가장 두루 무감한 이들이기도 하다. 우리가 사는 이 땅의 아름다움을 알아보는 우리의 능력은, 실은 우리 모두가 하나의 기예로서 익히는 문명의 소양 가운데 하나다. 게다가 그 능력이란 우리 마음이 가장 나른하고 가장 하는 일 없을 때 말고는 우리 가운데 누구도 좀처럼 부려 쓰지 않는 것이다. 자연의 매력이, 우리 자신이나 우리 벗들의 즐겁거나 괴로운 관심과 감정에서 대체 얼마만 한 몫을 차지한 적이 있는가? 그것들이, 날마다 우리 가운데 하나에게서 다른 하나에게로 입말로 오가는 그 숱한 자잘한 개인적 경험담에서 대체 얼마만 한 자리를 차지하는가? 우리 마음이 헤아릴 수 있는 온갖 것, 우리 가슴이 배울 수 있는 온갖 것은, 지구의 낯이 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 초라한 경치에서도 가장 풍요로운 경치에서와 똑같은 확실함, 똑같은 이로움, 똑같은 흡족함으로 이루어질 수 있다. 피조물과 그를 둘러싼 창조물 사이의 이 타고난 공감의 부재에는 분명 까닭이 있으니, 그것은 어쩌면 인간과 그의 지상 영역의 사뭇 다른 운명에서 찾을 수 있을지 모른다. 눈이 두루 훑을 수 있는 가장 웅장한 산의 경치는 소멸로 정해져 있다. 순수한 가슴이 느낄 수 있는 가장 작은 인간적 관심은 불멸로 정해져 있다.\n\n우리가 나온 지 거의 세 시간이 되어서야 마차가 다시 리머리지 하우스의 문을 지났다.\n\n돌아오는 길에 나는 이튿날 오후 내 지도 아래 그릴 첫 번째 풍경 지점을 숙녀들이 스스로 정하게 두었다. 그들이 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입으러 물러가고, 내가 다시 작은 거실에 혼자 남았을 때, 문득 내 기운이 나를 떠나는 듯했다. 나는 마음이 불편하고 나 자신에게 못마땅했다—왜인지 나도 잘 몰랐다. 어쩌면 나는 이제 처음으로, 우리의 마차 나들이를 그림 선생의 자격으로는 너무 적게, 손님의 자격으로는 너무 많이 즐겼음을 의식한 것인지도 몰랐다. 어쩌면 처음 그녀에게 소개될 때 나를 어리둥절하게 한, 페얼리 양에게든 나 자신에게든 무언가 모자란다는 그 기이한 느낌이 여전히 나를 따라다닌 것인지도 몰랐다. 어쨌거나, 저녁 식사 시간이 나를 고독에서 불러내어 저택 숙녀들의 벗으로 도로 데려갔을 때 내 기운은 오히려 놓였다.\n\n응접실에 들어서면서, 나는 그들이 이제 입은 드레스의, 빛깔이라기보다 감의 기묘한 대비에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 베지 부인과 홀콤 양은 저마다 (제 나이에 가장 어울리는 방식으로) 값진 옷을 입었으니, 앞의 이는 은회색으로, 뒤의 이는 가무잡잡한 낯빛과 검은 머리에 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 여린 앵초빛 노랑으로 입었건만, 페얼리 양은 소박한 흰 모슬린으로 꾸밈없이, 거의 초라하다 싶게 차려입고 있었다. 그것은 티끌 하나 없이 깨끗했고 아름답게 걸쳐져 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것은 가난한 이의 아내나 딸이 입었을 법한 종류의 드레스였고, 겉모습으로 보자면 그녀를 제 가정교사보다도 형편이 덜 넉넉해 보이게 했다. 뒷날 페얼리 양의 성품을 더 알게 되었을 때, 나는 이 잘못된 쪽으로의 기묘한 대비가, 그녀의 타고난 여린 감수성과 제 부(富)를 조금이라도 몸에 드러내는 것에 대한 타고난 격렬한 혐오에서 비롯된 것임을 알게 되었다. 베지 부인도 홀콤 양도, 옷차림의 이점이 가난한 두 숙녀를 저버리고 부유한 한 숙녀 쪽으로 기울게끔 그녀를 결코 구슬리지 못했다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자 우리는 함께 응접실로 돌아왔다. 페얼리 씨는 (자기를 위해 티치아노의 붓을 주워 준 그 군주의 장엄한 겸양을 흉내 내어) 만찬 뒤 내가 즐길 만한 포도주에 관해 내 뜻을 물으라고 집사에게 일러 두었으나, 나는 내가 고른 술병들 사이에 홀로 거창하게 앉아 있으려는 유혹을 물리칠 만큼은 단호했고, 리머리지 하우스에 머무는 동안 문명화된 외국식으로 늘 숙녀들과 함께 식탁을 떠나게 해 달라고 청할 만큼은 분별이 있었다.\n\n저녁의 남은 시간을 보내려고 이제 물러간 그 응접실은 일 층에 있었고, 아침 식사실과 같은 모양과 크기였다. 아래쪽 끝의 커다란 유리문은, 온통 흐드러진 꽃으로 그 전체를 아름답게 꾸민 테라스로 열려 있었다. 우리가 방에 들어설 때, 부드럽고 뿌연 황혼이 마침 나뭇잎과 꽃송이를 하나같이 제 차분한 빛깔과 어우러지게 물들이고 있었고, 열린 유리문 너머로 꽃들의 달콤한 저녁 내음이 향긋한 환영으로 우리를 맞았다. 선량한 베지 부인은 (언제나 일행 가운데 가장 먼저 앉는 사람인지라) 구석의 안락의자를 차지하고는 편안히 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다 잠들었다. 내 청에 따라 페얼리 양이 피아노 앞에 앉았다. 그 악기 곁의 자리로 그녀를 뒤따르는데, 홀콤 양이 옆 창문 하나의 우묵한 자리로 물러나, 사그라드는 저녁 빛의 마지막 고요한 빛살로 어머니 편지를 마저 뒤져 나가는 것이 보였다.\n\n이 글을 쓰는 동안 그 평온한 응접실의 가정 풍경이 얼마나 생생히 되살아나는지! 내가 앉은 자리에서 나는 홀콤 양의 우아한 형체를 볼 수 있었으니, 반은 부드러운 빛 속에, 반은 신비로운 그늘 속에 잠긴 채 무릎 위 편지에 골똘히 몸을 숙이고 있었다. 그런가 하면 나에게 더 가까이에서는, 피아노 연주자의 고운 옆얼굴이 방 안쪽 벽의 아스라이 짙어 가는 배경을 등지고 여리게 윤곽 지어져 있었다. 바깥 테라스에서는 다닥다닥 모인 꽃과 긴 풀과 덩굴이 가벼운 저녁 공기에 어찌나 살랑살랑 흔들리는지, 그 사각이는 소리가 우리에게까지 결코 닿지 않았다. 하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었고, 동쪽 하늘 어름에서는 달빛의 새벽 같은 신비가 벌써 떨리기 시작했다. 평화와 은둔의 느낌이 온갖 생각과 감정을 넋 잃은, 세속을 벗어난 안식으로 달래 놓았다. 그리고 짙어 가는 빛과 더불어 더욱 짙어 가던 그 향긋한 고요는, 피아노에서 모차르트 음악의 천상의 다정함이 그 위로 살며시 스며들 때, 한층 더 부드러운 힘으로 우리 위를 맴도는 듯했다. 그것은 결코 잊지 못할 광경과 소리의 저녁이었다.\n\n우리는 모두 저마다 고른 자리에 잠자코 앉아 있었다—베지 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있고, 페얼리 양은 여전히 연주하고, 홀콤 양은 여전히 읽으며—빛이 우리를 저버릴 때까지. 이 무렵 달은 어느새 테라스 쪽으로 돌아와 있었고, 부드럽고 신비로운 빛살이 벌써 방 아래쪽 끝을 비스듬히 가로지르고 있었다. 황혼의 어둠에서 바뀐 그 광경이 어찌나 아름다웠던지, 하인이 등불을 들여왔을 때 우리는 뜻을 모아 그것을 물리고, 피아노 위 두 자루 촛불의 희미한 빛 말고는 그 큰 방을 불 밝히지 않은 채로 두었다.\n\n반 시간 남짓 음악이 여전히 이어졌다. 그 뒤 테라스에 비친 달빛 풍경의 아름다움이 페얼리 양을 그것을 보러 밖으로 이끌었고, 나는 그녀를 뒤따랐다. 피아노 위 촛불이 밝혀졌을 때 홀콤 양은 그 빛의 도움으로 편지를 계속 살피려고 자리를 옮긴 참이었다. 우리는 그녀를, 악기 한쪽 곁의 낮은 의자에 앉힌 채로 두고 나왔는데, 그녀는 읽기에 어찌나 빠져 있는지 우리가 자리를 옮기는 것도 알아채지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n내 짐작으로 우리가 유리문 바로 앞 테라스에 함께 나와 있은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았을 때였다. 그리고 페얼리 양은 내 권유에 따라 밤공기에 대비해 흰 손수건을 머리에 막 두르고 있었는데—그때 나는 홀콤 양의 목소리를 들었다—낮고 다급하며 그 타고난 발랄한 어조와는 달라진 목소리로 내 이름을 부르는 것을.\n\n“하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “잠깐 이리 와 주시겠어요? 당신께 할 말이 있어요.”\n\n나는 곧바로 다시 방에 들어섰다. 피아노는 안쪽 벽을 따라 절반쯤 되는 자리에 놓여 있었다. 테라스에서 가장 먼 쪽 악기 곁에 홀콤 양이 앉아 있었는데, 편지를 무릎에 흩뜨려 놓고, 그 가운데 하나를 골라 손에 든 채 촛불 가까이 대고 있었다. 테라스에 가장 가까운 쪽에는 낮은 긴 의자가 놓여 있었고, 나는 거기에 자리 잡았다. 이 자리에서 나는 유리문에서 멀지 않아, 페얼리 양이 달빛 가득한 속에서 테라스를 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 천천히 거닐며 그 트인 문 앞을 오가는 것을 또렷이 볼 수 있었다.\n\n“이 편지의 마지막 대목을 제가 읽는 동안 들어 주셨으면 해요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “그것이 런던으로 가는 길에서의 당신의 기이한 겪음에 무슨 빛이라도 던져 주는지 말씀해 주세요. 이 편지는 제 어머니가 두 번째 남편 페얼리 씨 앞으로 부친 것이고, 날짜로 보아 열한 해에서 열두 해쯤 전을 가리켜요. 그 무렵 페얼리 씨 부부와 제 이복동생 로라는 여러 해째 이 집에 살고 있었고, 저는 파리의 학교에서 학업을 마치느라 그들에게서 떨어져 있었지요.”\n\n그녀는 진지하게, 그리고 내가 보기엔 조금 불안한 기색으로도 바라보고 말했다. 편지를 읽기 시작하기에 앞서 그것을 촛불 앞으로 들어 올리는 순간, 페얼리 양이 테라스에서 우리 곁을 지나며 잠시 안을 들여다보다가, 우리가 무언가에 몰두한 것을 보고는 천천히 걸어갔다.\n\n홀콤 양은 다음과 같이 읽기 시작했다—\n\n“‘사랑하는 필립, 당신은 제 학교와 제 학생들 이야기를 노상 듣느라 지겨우시겠지요. 제발 그 탓을 저에게 말고 리머리지 생활의 그 따분한 한결같음에 돌려 주세요. 게다가 이번에는 새 학생에 대해 정말 흥미로운 이야깃거리가 있답니다.\n\n“‘마을 가게의 늙은 켐프 부인을 아시지요. 그런데 그이가 여러 해 앓은 끝에 의사가 마침내 손을 놓았고, 날마다 조금씩 죽어 가고 있어요. 하나뿐인 살아 있는 혈육인 여동생이 지난주에 그이를 돌보러 왔지요. 이 여동생은 멀리 햄프셔에서 온 사람인데—이름은 캐서릭 부인이에요. 나흘 전 캐서릭 부인이 저를 보러 이곳에 왔는데, 하나뿐인 아이를 데리고 왔어요. 우리 사랑스러운 로라보다 한 살쯤 위인 어여쁜 어린 소녀랍니다——’”\n\n읽는 이의 입술에서 그 마지막 문장이 떨어질 때, 페얼리 양이 다시 한번 테라스에서 우리 곁을 지났다. 그녀는 저녁 일찍 연주하던 가락 가운데 하나를 나직이 혼자 흥얼거리고 있었다. 홀콤 양은 그녀가 다시 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 편지를 이어 갔다—\n\n“‘캐서릭 부인은 점잖고 몸가짐 바르며 버젓한 여인이에요. 중년이고, 그럭저럭, 그저 그럭저럭 반반했던 흔적이 남아 있지요. 하지만 그 몸가짐과 생김새에는 제가 도무지 알 수 없는 무언가가 있어요. 그이는 제 이야기에 대해서는 아주 대놓고 비밀스럽다 할 만큼 말을 아끼고, 그 얼굴에는—뭐라 묘사할 수 없는—무언가 마음에 걸리는 것이 있음을 저에게 넌지시 비치는 표정이 있어요. 그이는 온통, 당신이 걸어 다니는 수수께끼라 부를 만한 사람이에요. 하지만 그이가 리머리지 하우스에 온 볼일은 아주 간단했어요. 언니 켐프 부인의 마지막 병간호를 하러 햄프셔를 떠날 때, 집에 그 어린 소녀를 돌봐 줄 사람이 없어 딸을 데려올 수밖에 없었대요. 켐프 부인은 일주일 안에 죽을 수도 있고 몇 달을 끌 수도 있는데, 캐서릭 부인의 볼일은, 켐프 부인이 죽은 뒤 그 애를 학교에서 데리고 나가 어머니와 함께 다시 집으로 간다는 조건 아래, 딸 앤이 제 학교에 다니는 혜택을 누리게 해 달라는 것이었어요. 저는 즉시 승낙했고, 로라와 제가 산책을 나갈 때 바로 그날 그 어린 소녀(이제 막 열한 살이에요)를 학교에 데려다주었지요.’”\n\n또 한 번 페얼리 양의 형체가—눈 같은 모슬린 드레스 속에서 밝고 부드럽게, 턱 아래로 묶은 손수건의 흰 주름이 얼굴을 어여쁘게 감싼 채로—달빛 속에서 우리 곁을 지났다. 또 한 번 홀콤 양은 그녀가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 이어 갔다—\n\n“‘필립, 저는 제 새 학생에게 격한 애정을 품게 됐어요. 그 까닭은 당신을 놀래 드리려고 맨 마지막까지 아껴 두려 해요. 그 애 어머니가 저에게 제 이야기를 거의 안 한 것만큼이나 그 애에 대해서도 거의 말해 주지 않아서, 저는 (수업으로 그 애를 시험해 본 첫날 알아냈지요) 그 가엾은 어린것의 지력이 제 나이에 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 자라 있지 않다는 것을 스스로 알아낼 수밖에 없었어요. 이걸 알고 이튿날 그 애를 집으로 불러, 의사에게 몰래 부탁해 와서 그 애를 지켜보고 물어보게 하고는 그 소견을 저에게 말해 달라 했지요. 의사 소견은 그 애가 자라면서 그것을 벗어나리라는 거예요. 하지만 학교에서 그 애를 잘 길러 주는 일이 지금으로선 대단히 중요하다고 해요. 관념을 받아들이는 데 유난히 더딘 것은, 그 관념이 일단 마음에 들어오면 유난히 끈덕지게 붙들고 있음을 뜻하기 때문이래요. 자, 여보, 당신의 그 대충대충 하는 버릇으로 제가 백치에게 정을 붙였다고 상상하시면 안 돼요. 이 가엾은 어린 앤 캐서릭은 다정하고 정 많고 고마움을 아는 소녀예요. 그리고 (한 가지 예로 당신이 판단해 보시도록) 더없이 별스럽게 불쑥, 놀란 듯, 반쯤 겁먹은 투로 더없이 별스럽고 어여쁜 말을 한답니다. 옷차림은 아주 말끔한데도, 그 옷은 빛깔과 무늬에서 안목이 딱하게도 모자라요. 그래서 어제 저는 우리 사랑스러운 로라의 낡은 흰 원피스와 흰 모자 몇 벌을 앤 캐서릭에게 맞게 고쳐 주기로 했어요. 그 애 낯빛의 어린 소녀는 다른 무엇을 입는 것보다 온통 흰옷을 입는 편이 더 말끔하고 더 보기 좋다고 그 애에게 일러 주면서요. 그 애는 잠깐 머뭇거리며 어리둥절해 보이더니, 이내 얼굴을 붉히며 알아들은 듯했어요. 그 작은 손이 별안간 제 손을 꼭 잡았지요. 그 애는 제 손에 입 맞추며 (오, 어찌나 간절하게!) 말했어요. “저는 살아 있는 한 언제까지나 흰옷을 입겠어요. 그러면 제가 떠나가 마님을 더는 뵙지 못하게 되어도, 마님을 기억하고 제가 아직도 마님을 기쁘게 해 드린다고 생각하는 데 도움이 될 거예요.” 이건 그 애가 그토록 어여쁘게 하는 별스러운 말들의 한 가지 본보기일 뿐이에요. 가엾은 어린것! 그 애가 자라면서 늘려 입을 수 있도록 넉넉히 깊게 단을 접은 흰 원피스를 잔뜩 마련해 주려고 해요——’”\n\n홀콤 양은 잠시 멈추고 피아노 너머로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“큰길에서 만난 그 의지가지없는 여인이 젊어 보였나요?” 그녀가 물었다. “스물둘이나 스물셋쯤 될 만큼 젊었어요?”\n\n“네, 홀콤 양, 그만큼 젊었습니다.”\n\n“그리고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 온통 흰옷으로, 기이하게 차려입고 있었고요?”\n\n“온통 흰옷이었습니다.”\n\n그 대답이 내 입에서 새어 나오는 동안, 페얼리 양이 세 번째로 테라스에 미끄러지듯 나타났다. 산책을 계속하는 대신, 그녀는 우리를 등진 채 멈춰 서서 테라스 난간에 기대어 그 너머 정원을 내려다보았다. 내 눈은 달빛 속 그녀의 모슬린 드레스와 머리쓰개의 흰 반짝임에 고정되었고, 이름을 붙일 수 없는 어떤 감각—내 맥박을 빠르게 하고 내 가슴에 설렘을 일으키는 감각—이 나를 슬며시 사로잡기 시작했다.\n\n“온통 흰옷이었다고요?” 홀콤 양이 되뇌었다. “하트라이트 씨, 이 편지에서 가장 중요한 문장들은 끝부분에 있어요. 그건 곧바로 읽어 드릴게요. 하지만 당신이 만난 여인의 흰옷 차림과, 제 어머니의 어린 학생에게서 그 기이한 대답을 이끌어 낸 흰 원피스의 이 우연의 일치에 잠시 마음이 머무는 걸 어쩔 수가 없네요. 그 아이의 지력의 결함을 알아내고 그 애가 그것을 ‘자라면서 벗어나리라’ 예언했을 때, 의사가 틀렸을 수도 있어요. 그 애는 결코 그것을 벗어나지 못했을 수도 있고, 그 소녀에게 진지한 감정이었던 흰옷을 입는다는 그 옛 고마운 소망이, 그 여인에게도 여전히 진지한 감정일지 몰라요.”\n\n나는 몇 마디 대꾸했다—무슨 말이었는지 나도 잘 모르겠다. 내 온 주의는 페얼리 양의 모슬린 드레스의 흰 반짝임에 쏠려 있었다.\n\n“이 편지의 마지막 문장들을 들어 보세요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “당신을 놀라게 하리라 봐요.”\n\n그녀가 편지를 촛불 빛으로 들어 올리자, 페얼리 양이 난간에서 돌아서 미심쩍은 듯 테라스 위아래를 살피더니, 유리문 쪽으로 한 걸음 다가서다 멈춰 서서 우리를 마주 보았다.\n\n그러는 동안 홀콤 양은 앞서 말한 마지막 문장들을 나에게 읽어 주었다—\n\n“‘그리고 이제, 여보, 종이가 다 되었으니, 이제 어린 앤 캐서릭에 대한 제 애정의 진짜 까닭, 그 놀라운 까닭을 말할게요. 사랑하는 필립, 그 애는 절반만큼도 어여쁘지 않지만, 그럼에도 이따금 볼 수 있는 그 우연한 닮음의 유별난 변덕 가운데 하나로, 머리칼과 낯빛과 눈 빛깔과 얼굴 모양에서, 살아 있는 판박이랍니다——’”\n\n홀콤 양이 다음 말을 채 발음하기도 전에 나는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다. 외로운 큰길에서 내 어깨에 손이 얹혔을 때 내 온몸을 훑고 지나간 그 똑같은 느낌의 전율이 다시 나를 오싹하게 했다.\n\n거기 페얼리 양이 서 있었다, 달빛 속에 홀로 흰 형체로. 그 자세로, 그 고갯짓으로, 그 낯빛으로, 그 얼굴 모양으로, 그 거리에서 그런 형편 아래, 흰옷을 입은 여인의 살아 있는 판박이로! 여러 시간 동안 내 마음을 어지럽히던 그 의문이 순식간에 확신으로 번뜩였다. 그 “무언가 모자란다”던 것은, 정신병원에서 달아난 도망자와 리머리지 하우스의 내 제자 사이의 그 불길한 닮음을 나 스스로 알아본 것이었다.\n\n“당신도 보이는군요!” 홀콤 양이 말했다. 그녀는 소용없어진 편지를 떨어뜨렸고, 나와 마주친 그 눈이 번뜩였다. “이제 당신도 보이는군요, 열한 해 전 제 어머니가 본 그대로!”\n\n“보입니다—말로 다 할 수 없이 마지못해 보입니다. 그 의지가지없고 벗도 없는 길 잃은 여인을, 한낱 우연한 닮음만으로라도 페얼리 양과 결부시키는 것은, 지금 우리를 바라보며 서 있는 저 밝은 존재의 앞날에 그늘을 드리우는 것만 같습니다. 되도록 빨리 그 인상을 다시 지워 버리게 해 주세요. 저 스산한 달빛에서 그녀를 안으로 불러 주세요—제발 불러 주세요!”\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 당신은 저를 놀라게 하시는군요. 여자야 어떻든, 저는 십구 세기의 남자라면 미신 따위는 뛰어넘은 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“제발 그녀를 불러 주세요!”\n\n“쉿, 쉿! 그녀가 제 발로 오고 있어요. 그녀 앞에서는 아무 말도 마세요. 이 닮음의 발견은 당신과 저 사이의 비밀로 해 두어요. 들어와, 로라, 들어와서 피아노로 베지 부인을 깨우렴. 하트라이트 씨가 음악을 좀 더 청하시는데, 이번에는 가장 가볍고 가장 흥겨운 걸로 듣고 싶으시대.”\n\n이렇게 하여 리머리지 하우스에서의 사건 많던 나의 첫날이 끝났다.\n\n홀콤 양과 나는 비밀을 지켰다. 그 닮음을 발견한 뒤로도 흰옷을 입은 여인의 수수께끼 위로 새로운 빛이 비칠 조짐은 없어 보였다. 처음으로 안전한 기회가 오자 홀콤 양은 조심스레 이복동생을 이끌어 그들의 어머니와 옛 시절, 그리고 앤 캐서릭에 대해 이야기하게 했다. 그러나 리머리지의 그 어린 학생에 대한 페얼리 양의 기억은 더없이 어렴풋하고 두루뭉술한 것뿐이었다. 그녀는 자신과 어머니가 아끼던 제자 사이의 닮음을, 지난 시절에 있었으리라 여겨지던 무언가로 기억했다. 그러나 흰 드레스를 선물받은 일이나, 그 아이가 그에 대한 고마움을 순진하게 나타낸 그 별스러운 말투에 대해서는 언급하지 않았다. 그녀는 앤이 리머리지에 겨우 몇 달 머물렀다가 햄프셔의 제 집으로 돌아갔다는 것은 기억했으나, 그 어머니와 딸이 그 뒤로 다시 돌아왔는지, 소식이라도 들려왔는지는 말하지 못했다. 아직 읽지 않고 남겨 둔 페얼리 부인의 몇 안 되는 편지를 홀콤 양이 더 뒤져 보아도, 여전히 우리를 어리둥절하게 하는 미심쩍음을 밝히는 데는 아무 도움이 되지 않았다. 우리는 밤중에 내가 만난 그 불행한 여인을 앤 캐서릭으로 밝혀냈고—적어도, 그 가엾은 존재의 아마도 결함 있는 지력의 상태를, 온통 흰옷을 입고 있다는 그 유별남과, 다 자란 나이에도 페얼리 부인을 향한 어린애 같은 고마움이 이어지고 있다는 것과 결부시키는 데 얼마간 나아갔다—그리고 그때 우리가 아는 한, 우리의 발견은 거기서 끝났다.\n\n날이 지나고 주가 지나며, 황금빛 가을의 자취가 나무들의 푸른 여름을 뚫고 그 밝은 길을 눈에 뜨이게 굽이쳐 갔다. 평화롭고 빠르게 흐르는 행복한 시간이여! 한때 그대가 나를 스쳐 지나갔듯, 이제 내 이야기가 그토록 빠르게 그대를 스쳐 지나간다. 그대가 그토록 아낌없이 내 가슴에 쏟아부은 온갖 즐거움의 보물 가운데, 이 지면에 적을 만큼 뜻과 값어치가 있는 것이 나에게 얼마나 남아 있는가? 사람이 할 수 있는 모든 고백 가운데 가장 서글픈 것—제 어리석음에 대한 고백 말고는 아무것도 없다.\n\n그 고백이 드러내는 비밀은 별 힘 들이지 않고 말할 수 있을 것이다, 이미 은연중에 새어 나와 버렸으니까. 페얼리 양을 묘사하는 데 실패한 그 딱하고 힘없는 말들이, 그녀가 내 안에 일깨운 감정을 드러내는 데는 성공했다. 우리 모두가 그렇다. 우리의 말은 우리를 해칠 때는 거인이요, 우리를 도울 때는 난쟁이다.\n\n나는 그녀를 사랑했다.\n\n아! 나는 그 세 마디에 담긴 온갖 서글픔과 온갖 조롱을 얼마나 잘 아는가. 나는 이 애처로운 고백을 읽으며 나를 가엾이 여기는 더없이 다정한 여인과 함께 한숨지을 수 있다. 나는 그것을, 그것을 업신여기며 저에게서 내던지는 더없이 냉정한 사내만큼이나 쓰라리게 비웃을 수 있다. 나는 그녀를 사랑했다! 나를 가엾이 여기든 나를 업신여기든, 나는 진실을 인정하려는 그 흔들림 없는 결의로 그것을 고백한다.\n\n나에게 변명거리라고는 없었던가? 분명, 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 고용살이를 하던 그 형편 속에서 얼마간의 변명거리는 찾을 수 있었다.\n\n내 아침 시간은 내 방의 고요와 은둔 속에서 잔잔하게 잇달아 지나갔다. 고용주의 소묘를 표구하는 데 손과 눈을 흐뭇하게 놀릴 만큼의 일이 꼭 있었고, 그러는 동안 내 마음은 제멋대로 뻗는 생각의 그 위험한 호사를 누리도록 풀려 있었다. 위험한 고독이었으니, 나를 굳세게 하기에는 모자라고 나른하게 하기에는 넉넉할 만큼 오래 이어졌기 때문이다. 위험한 고독이었으니, 그 뒤로 날마다 주마다 두 여인의 벗 속에서 홀로 보내는 오후와 저녁이 이어졌기 때문이다. 그 하나는 우아함과 재치와 고귀한 교양의 온갖 소양을 지녔고, 다른 하나는 사내의 마음을 정화하고 사로잡을 수 있는 아름다움과 부드러움과 소박한 진실의 온갖 매력을 지닌 이였다. 선생과 제자의 그 위험한 친밀함 속에서, 내 손이 페얼리 양의 손 가까이 있지 않은 날, 우리가 함께 사생첩 위로 몸을 숙일 때 내 뺨이 그녀의 뺨에 거의 닿지 않은 날은 하루도 없었다. 그녀가 내 붓의 움직임 하나하나를 유심히 지켜볼수록, 나는 그녀 머리칼의 향과 그 숨결의 따뜻한 향기를 더 가까이 들이마셨다. 그녀 눈의 바로 그 빛 속에서 사는 것이 내 소임의 일부였다—어느 때는 그녀에게 몸을 숙여, 그 가슴에 닿는다는 생각만으로도 떨릴 만큼 가까이 다가가고, 또 어느 때는 그녀가 내가 무얼 하는지 보려고 나에게 몸을 숙이는 것을 느꼈는데, 어찌나 가까이 숙였던지 나에게 말할 때 그 목소리가 나직이 잦아들고, 미처 거두어들이기도 전에 그 리본이 바람에 내 뺨을 스쳤다.\n\n오후의 사생 나들이에 뒤따르는 저녁들은, 이 순진하고 이 어쩔 수 없는 친밀함을 막기보다 오히려 다채롭게 했다. 그녀가 그토록 다정한 감정으로, 그토록 여린 여성스러운 취향으로 연주하던 음악에 대한 나의 타고난 애호와, 내가 내 예술을 부려 그녀에게 준 즐거움을 제 예술을 부려 나에게 되돌려 주는 것에 대한 그녀의 타고난 기쁨은, 우리를 서로 점점 더 가까이 끌어당기는 또 하나의 끈을 자아낼 따름이었다. 우연히 오간 대화들, 식탁에서 우리 자리의 위치라는 그런 사소한 것마저 정해 놓은 소박한 습관들, 제자로서의 그녀의 열의 위로 반짝이면서도 언제나 선생으로서의 내 노심초사를 겨누던 홀콤 양의 늘 준비된 놀림의 장난, 페얼리 양과 나를 자기를 한 번도 방해하지 않는 두 모범 청년으로 짝지어 놓던 가엾은 베지 부인의 졸린 승낙의 그 해롭잖은 표현—이 자잘한 것들 하나하나가, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 것들이 어우러져, 우리를 같은 가정의 분위기 속에 함께 감싸고, 우리 둘을 저도 모르게 같은 가망 없는 결말로 이끌었다.\n\n나는 내 처지를 기억하고 남몰래 경계 태세를 갖췄어야 했다. 그렇게 하기는 했으나, 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다. 다른 여자들에게는 통했고 다른 유혹들로부터 나를 지켜 준 온갖 조심성, 온갖 경험이, 그녀에게는 통하지 않았다. 온갖 나이대, 온갖 부류의 아름다움을 지닌 젊은 아가씨들과 이토록 가까이 붙어 있는 것이 여러 해 동안 내 직업이었다. 나는 그 처지를 내 인생의 소명의 일부로 받아들였다. 위층으로 올라가기 전에 우산을 그 바깥 현관에 두고 가듯, 내 나이에 어울리는 온갖 감정을 고용주의 바깥 현관에 두고 가도록 태연히 나 자신을 길들여 왔다. 내 인생의 처지가 여자 제자 가운데 누구도 나에게 지극히 예사로운 관심 이상을 느끼지 않으리라는 보증으로 여겨진다는 것을, 그리고 내가 아름답고 매혹적인 여인들 사이에, 해롭잖은 집짐승이 그들 사이에 받아들여지듯 받아들여진다는 것을, 나는 오래전에 태연히, 또 당연한 일로 이해하게 되었다. 이 지켜 주는 경험을 나는 일찍이 얻었고, 이 지켜 주는 경험은 나를 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 한 번도 벗어나게 두지 않고, 내 딱하고 좁은 길을 따라 곧장 엄격하고 단단히 이끌어 왔다. 그런데 이제 나와 내 믿음직한 부적이 처음으로 갈라섰다. 그렇다, 힘겹게 얻은 내 자제심은 애초에 지닌 적조차 없었던 양 나에게서 완전히 사라져 버렸다. 여자가 얽힌 다른 위태로운 형편에서 날마다 다른 사내들에게서 사라지듯, 나에게서도 사라졌다. 이제 나는 안다, 처음부터 나 자신에게 물었어야 했음을. 그녀가 어느 방에 들어서면 그 방이 나에게 집보다 나아지고, 그녀가 다시 나가면 사막처럼 황량해지는 것은 어째서인지—다른 어떤 여자에게서도 눈여겨보거나 기억한 적 없던 그녀 옷차림의 자잘한 변화를 나는 언제나 눈여겨보고 기억하는 것은 어째서인지—(밤과 아침에 악수할 때) 내 평생 다른 어떤 여자도 그렇게 보고 듣고 만진 적 없듯 그녀를 보고 듣고 만지는 것은 어째서인지 물었어야 했다. 나는 내 마음속을 들여다보고, 거기 이 새로운 싹이 돋아나고 있음을 알아채어, 그것이 어릴 때 뽑아냈어야 했다. 이 더없이 쉽고 더없이 간단한 자기 수양의 일이 어째서 언제나 나에게는 너무 벅찼던가? 그 해명은 이미, 내 고백에 넉넉하고도 분명한 그 세 마디에 적혀 있다. 나는 그녀를 사랑했다.\n\n날이 지나고 주가 지났다. 컴벌랜드에 머문 지 어느새 석 달째로 접어들고 있었다. 우리의 잔잔한 은둔 속 그 감미로운 단조로움이, 물살을 따라 미끄러져 내려가는 헤엄치는 이를 태운 매끄러운 냇물처럼 나와 더불어 흘러갔다. 과거에 대한 온갖 기억, 미래에 대한 온갖 생각, 내 처지의 거짓됨과 가망 없음에 대한 온갖 자각이, 내 안에서 잠잠해져 속임수 같은 안식에 잠겨 있었다. 내 심장이 스스로 나에게 불러 주는 세이렌의 노래에 홀려, 온갖 광경에 눈을 감고 온갖 위험의 소리에 귀를 닫은 채, 나는 그 치명적인 바위 쪽으로 점점 더 가까이 떠내려갔다. 마침내 나를 흔들어 깨워, 내 자신의 나약함을 자책하는 불현듯한 자각으로 소스라치게 한 그 경고는, 온갖 경고 가운데 가장 꾸밈없고 가장 참되고 가장 다정한 것이었으니, 그것이 그녀에게서 소리 없이 왔기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 어느 날 밤 여느 때처럼 헤어졌다. 그때도, 그 전 어느 때도, 나를 드러내거나 그녀를 진실의 불현듯한 앎으로 소스라치게 할 만한 말은 내 입에서 한 마디도 떨어지지 않았다. 그러나 아침에 다시 만났을 때, 그녀에게 변화가 와 있었다—나에게 모든 것을 말해 주는 변화가.\n\n나는 그때도 움츠러들었고—지금도 움츠러든다—그녀 마음의 가장 깊은 성소를 침범해, 내 것을 열어 보였듯 그것을 남들에게 열어 보이는 일에서. 그녀가 처음으로 내 비밀을 알아챈 그때가, 내가 굳게 믿건대, 그녀가 처음으로 제 비밀을 알아챈 그때였으며, 또한 그녀가 하룻밤 사이에 나를 향해 달라진 그때였다는 것만 말해 두면 족하리라. 남을 속이기에는 너무 진실한 그녀의 천성은, 제 자신을 속이기에는 너무 고귀했다. 내가 잠재워 둔 그 의문이 처음으로 그 지친 무게를 그녀 가슴에 얹었을 때, 그 진실한 얼굴은 모든 것을 인정하며, 제 솔직하고 소박한 언어로 말했다—그이가 가엾다, 나 자신이 가엾다.\n\n그것은 이렇게 말했고, 그때 내가 헤아릴 수 없었던 더 많은 것을 말했다. 남들 앞에서 내 온갖 바람을 더 큰 친절과 더 빠른 시원스러움으로 헤아려 주는 쪽으로—우리가 어쩌다 단둘이 남겨질 때면 언제나, 붙잡을 수 있는 첫 일거리에 자신을 파묻으려는 어색함과 서글픔과 조바심 어린 근심 쪽으로 바뀐 그녀의 태도를, 나는 너무나 잘 이해했다. 그 달콤하고 예민한 입술이 이제 그토록 드물게, 그토록 삼가며 미소 짓는 것은 어째서인지, 그 맑은 파란 눈이 어느 때는 천사의 연민으로, 어느 때는 아이의 순진한 어리둥절함으로 나를 바라보는 것은 어째서인지, 나는 이해했다. 그러나 그 변화는 이것 이상을 뜻했다. 그녀의 손에는 냉랭함이 있었고, 그 얼굴에는 부자연스러운 굳음이 있었으며, 그 온갖 몸짓에는 끊임없는 두려움과 매달리는 자책의 말 없는 표현이 있었다. 그녀에게서, 또 나에게서 비롯되었다고 내가 짚어 낼 수 있는 감정, 우리가 함께 느끼면서도 인정하지 않던 그 감정은 이런 것이 아니었다. 그녀 안의 그 변화에는, 아직도 남몰래 우리를 서로 끌어당기는 어떤 요소들과, 그만큼 남몰래 우리를 갈라놓기 시작하는 다른 요소들이 있었다.\n\n내 의문과 어리둥절함 속에서, 오로지 나 혼자의 힘으로 찾아내도록 남겨진 무언가 감춰진 것에 대한 어렴풋한 의심 속에서, 나는 홀콤 양의 표정과 태도에서 깨달음을 얻으려 살폈다. 우리 같은 친밀함 속에서 살자면, 우리 가운데 누구 하나에게 심각한 변화가 일어나면 그것이 다른 이들에게도 공명하듯 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었다. 페얼리 양의 변화는 그 이복언니에게 비쳐 나타났다. 나를 향한 감정이 달라졌음을 넌지시라도 비추는 말은 홀콤 양의 입에서 한 마디도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그 꿰뚫는 눈은 언제나 나를 지켜보는 새로운 버릇이 들어 있었다. 어느 때는 그 눈빛이 억눌린 분노 같았고, 어느 때는 억눌린 두려움 같았으며, 어느 때는 그 어느 쪽도 아닌—한마디로 내가 이해할 수 있는 그 무엇도 아닌 것 같았다. 한 주가 지나도록 우리 셋은 여전히 서로를 향한 이 은밀한 어색함의 처지에 놓여 있었다. 이제 너무 늦게야 내 안에 깨어난, 내 자신의 비참한 나약함과 자기 망각에 대한 자각으로 더 심해진 내 처지는 견딜 수 없어지고 있었다. 나는 내가 놓인 이 억눌림을 당장, 그리고 영영 떨쳐 내야 함을 느꼈다—그러나 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 나은지, 무슨 말을 먼저 해야 하는지는 내가 알 수 있는 것 이상이었다.\n\n이 속수무책과 굴욕의 처지에서 나를 구해 준 것은 홀콤 양이었다. 그 입술이 나에게 쓰라리고, 어쩔 수 없고, 뜻밖인 진실을 말해 주었다. 그 진심 어린 친절이 그것을 듣는 충격 아래 나를 버텨 주었다. 그 분별과 용기가, 리머리지 하우스에서 나에게도 남들에게도 일어날 수 있는 최악을 위협하던 사태를 올바른 쓰임으로 돌려놓았다.\n\n# X\n\n그 주의 어느 목요일이었고, 내가 컴벌랜드에 머문 지 석 달째가 거의 끝나 갈 무렵이었다.\n\n아침에 여느 때와 같은 시각에 아침 식사실로 내려가니, 내가 그녀를 알게 된 이래 처음으로, 홀콤 양이 식탁의 늘 앉던 자리에 없었다.\n\n페얼리 양은 잔디밭에 나가 있었다. 그녀는 나에게 고개를 숙였으나 안으로 들어오지는 않았다. 우리 둘 가운데 누구를 흔들어 놓을 만한 말은 내 입에서도, 그녀의 입에서도 한 마디 떨어지지 않았다—그런데도 인정하지 않은 그 어색함이 우리로 하여금 단둘이 마주하기를 똑같이 움츠러들게 했다. 베지 부인이나 홀콤 양이 들어올 때까지, 그녀는 잔디밭에서 기다렸고 나는 아침 식사실에서 기다렸다. 겨우 두 주 전만 해도 나는 얼마나 얼른 그녀에게 다가갔을 것이며, 우리는 얼마나 선뜻 악수를 나누고 늘 하던 이야기 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔을 것인가.\n\n몇 분 뒤 홀콤 양이 들어왔다. 무언가에 골몰한 기색이었고, 늦은 데 대한 사과도 다소 넋 놓은 채로 했다.\n\n“붙들려 있었어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “페얼리 씨가 저에게 이야기하고 싶어 하시는 집안일에 대해 상의하느라고요.”\n\n페얼리 양이 정원에서 들어왔고, 우리 사이에 여느 때와 같은 아침 인사가 오갔다. 그녀의 손은 그 어느 때보다 차갑게 내 손에 와닿았다. 그녀는 나를 바라보지 않았고, 몹시 창백했다. 잠시 뒤 방에 들어온 베지 부인조차 그것을 알아챘다.\n\n“바람이 바뀐 탓인가 봐요.” 노부인이 말했다. “겨울이 오고 있어요—아, 얘야, 겨울이 곧 오는구나!”\n\n그녀의 마음속에도 내 마음속에도 겨울은 이미 와 있었다!\n\n한때 그날의 계획을 유쾌하고 정겹게 의논하는 것으로 그토록 가득 차 있던 우리의 아침 식사는, 짧고 조용했다. 페얼리 양은 대화의 긴 침묵의 억눌림을 느끼는 듯, 그것을 메워 달라고 언니를 애원하듯 바라보았다. 홀콤 양은 한두 번 머뭇거리다 스스로를 다잡기를, 더없이 그녀답지 않은 태도로 되풀이한 끝에, 마침내 입을 열었다.\n\n“오늘 아침 네 삼촌을 뵈었단다, 로라.” 그녀가 말했다. “그분은 자줏빛 방을 채비해 두는 게 좋겠다고 하시더라. 그리고 내가 너에게 말한 걸 다시 확인해 주셨어. 그날은 월요일이야—화요일이 아니라.”\n\n이 말이 오가는 동안 페얼리 양은 제 앞의 식탁을 내려다보았다. 그녀의 손가락은 식탁보에 흩어진 부스러기 사이를 초조하게 움직였다. 뺨의 창백함이 입술로 번졌고, 입술 자체가 눈에 뜨이게 떨렸다. 이것을 알아챈 것은 그 자리의 나뿐만이 아니었다. 홀콤 양도 그것을 보고는, 즉시 우리에게 식탁에서 일어나는 본을 보였다.\n\n베지 부인과 페얼리 양은 함께 방을 나섰다. 그 다정하고 서글픈 파란 눈이 잠시, 다가오는 긴 작별을 미리 아는 슬픔으로 나를 바라보았다. 나는 내 가슴속에서 그에 응하는 아픔을 느꼈다—머잖아 그녀를 잃을 것이며, 그 잃음으로 인해 그녀를 더욱 변함없이 사랑하리라 나에게 일러 주는 그 아픔을.\n\n문이 그녀 뒤로 닫히자 나는 정원 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 홀콤 양이 잔디밭으로 이어지는 큰 창가에, 모자를 손에 들고 숄을 팔에 걸친 채 서서 나를 유심히 바라보고 있었다.\n\n“당신 방에서 일을 시작하기 전에.” 그녀가 물었다. “좀 내어 줄 짬이 있으세요?”\n\n“물론입니다, 홀콤 양. 저에게는 언제나 당신을 위한 시간이 있지요.”\n\n“당신께 은밀히 한마디 하고 싶어요, 하트라이트 씨. 모자를 쓰고 정원으로 나오세요. 이 아침 시각에는 거기서 방해받을 일이 없을 거예요.”\n\n우리가 잔디밭으로 나서는데, 아래 정원사 하나가—한낱 소년이었다—손에 편지를 들고 저택 쪽으로 가다가 우리 곁을 지났다. 홀콤 양이 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n“그 편지, 나에게 온 거니?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“아니라예, 아씨. 그저 페얼리 아씨한테 온 거라 카던데예.” 소년이 말하며 편지를 내밀었다.\n\n홀콤 양은 그에게서 그것을 받아 주소를 들여다보았다.\n\n“낯선 필체네.” 그녀가 혼잣말로 말했다. “로라에게 편지 보낼 사람이 누굴까? 이걸 어디서 받았니?” 그녀가 정원사에게 말을 이었다.\n\n“글쎄예, 아씨.” 소년이 말했다. “그냥 어떤 여자한테서 받았는데예.”\n\n“어떤 여자?”\n\n“나이가 아주 지긋한 여자라예.”\n\n“오, 노파로구나. 아는 사람이니?”\n\n“그 여자가 저한테는 생판 낯선 사람 말고 딴것이었다고는 지가 감히 말 못 하겠는데예.”\n\n“어느 쪽으로 갔니?”\n\n“저 문이라예.” 아래 정원사가 아주 느긋하게 남쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 팔을 한 번 죽 휘둘러 잉글랜드의 그쪽 전부를 아우르듯 가리키며 말했다.\n\n“이상하네.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “구걸 편지인가 봐. 자.” 그녀가 편지를 소년에게 도로 건네며 덧붙였다. “저택으로 가져가서 하인 아무에게나 주렴. 자 그럼, 하트라이트 씨, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 걸으실까요.”\n\n그녀는 나를 이끌고 잔디밭을 가로질러, 내가 리머리지에 도착한 이튿날 그녀를 뒤따라 걷던 바로 그 오솔길을 따라갔다.\n\n로라 페얼리와 내가 처음 서로를 본 그 작은 정자에서, 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 함께 걷는 내내 한결같이 지켜 온 침묵을 깼다.\n\n“당신께 할 말은 여기서 할 수 있어요.”\n\n그 말과 함께 그녀는 정자로 들어가, 안의 작은 원탁 곁 의자 하나에 앉고는 나에게 다른 하나에 앉으라 손짓했다. 아침 식사실에서 그녀가 나에게 말을 걸었을 때 나는 무슨 이야기가 나올지 어렴풋이 짐작했다. 이제 나는 그것을 확신했다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신께 솔직한 고백을 하는 것으로 시작하겠어요. 제가 몹시 싫어하는 미사여구도, 진심으로 업신여기는 겉치레 인사도 없이 말하겠어요—당신이 저희와 함께 지내시는 동안 저는 당신에게 강한 우정 어린 마음을 느끼게 되었어요. 당신이 그토록 심상찮은 형편에서 만난 그 불행한 여인을 대한 처신을 처음 저에게 말씀하셨을 때, 저는 이미 당신에게 호감을 품었지요. 그 일을 다룬 방식이 신중하지는 못했을지 몰라도, 그것은 천성적으로 신사인 사람의 자제심과 섬세함과 연민을 보여 주었어요. 그것은 제가 당신에게 좋은 것을 기대하게 만들었고, 당신은 제 기대를 저버리지 않으셨어요.”\n\n그녀는 잠시 멈추었다—그러나 동시에, 이야기를 이어 가기 전에 나에게서 대답을 기다리지 않겠다는 표시로 손을 들어 보였다. 정자에 들어설 때 내 안에는 흰옷을 입은 여인에 대한 생각이 없었다. 그러나 이제 홀콤 양 자신의 말이 그 겪음의 기억을 도로 내 마음에 되살려 놓았다. 그것은 이 면담 내내 거기 머물렀다—머물렀고, 결과가 없지도 않았다.\n\n“당신의 벗으로서.” 그녀가 이어 갔다. “저는 지금 당장, 제 나름의 꾸밈없고 무뚝뚝하고 딱 부러지는 말로 당신께 말하겠어요—저는 당신의 비밀을 알아냈어요. 명심하세요, 다른 누구의 도움이나 귀띔도 없이요. 하트라이트 씨, 당신은 경솔하게도 제 동생 로라에게 정을—진지하고 헌신적인 정이 아닐까 두렵지만—붙이도록 스스로를 내버려 두셨어요. 저는 당신께 그것을 굳이 여러 말로 고백하는 아픔을 지우지 않겠어요. 당신이 그것을 부인하기엔 너무 정직한 사람임을 제가 보고 알기 때문이에요. 저는 당신을 나무라지도 않아요—가망 없는 애정에 마음을 연 당신이 가여울 뿐이지요. 당신은 어떤 뒷수도 쓰려 하지 않았고—은밀히 제 동생에게 말을 걸지도 않았어요. 당신은 나약함과 자기 이익에 대한 소홀함의 죄는 있으나, 그보다 더한 죄는 없어요. 만일 당신이 단 한 가지 점에서라도 덜 섬세하고 덜 겸손하게 굴었다면, 저는 당신께 한순간의 통보도, 누구와의 한순간의 상의도 없이 이 집을 떠나라고 했을 거예요. 하지만 사정이 이러하니, 저는 당신의 나이와 처지의 불운을 나무랄 뿐—당신을 나무라진 않아요. 악수해요—저는 당신께 아픔을 주었어요. 더 많은 아픔을 드리려 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요—우선 당신의 벗 매리언 홀콤과 악수부터 해요.”\n\n그 불현듯한 친절—그토록 자비롭게 대등한 처지에서 나를 맞아 주고, 그토록 섬세하고 너그러운 느닷없음으로 곧장 내 가슴과 내 명예와 내 용기에 호소하는 그 따뜻하고 고결하고 두려움 없는 공감이, 순식간에 나를 압도했다. 그녀가 내 손을 잡을 때 나는 그녀를 바라보려 했으나, 내 눈은 흐렸다. 나는 그녀에게 고마움을 표하려 했으나, 내 목소리가 나오지 않았다.\n\n“제 말을 들으세요.” 그녀가 내 자제심의 무너짐을 짐짓 못 본 체하며 사려 깊게 말했다. “제 말을 들으시고, 이 일을 당장 끝내 버려요. 지금 제가 할 말에서, 사회적 불평등이라는—제 생각엔 딱딱하고 매정한—그 문제로 파고들지 않아도 된다는 것이 저에게는 정말 참된 안도예요. 당신을 뼛속까지 시험할 그 형편이, 저와 한 지붕 아래서 정답게 지내 온 사람에게 신분과 지위 문제를 굴욕적으로 들먹여 아픔을 주어야 하는 그 야박한 짓을 저에게서 덜어 주네요. 당신은 리머리지 하우스를 떠나셔야 해요, 하트라이트 씨, 더 큰 해가 생기기 전에요. 당신께 그것을 말하는 것이 제 의무예요. 그리고 설령 당신이 잉글랜드에서 가장 오래되고 가장 부유한 가문의 대표라 해도, 꼭 같은 절박한 처지 아래 저는 그것을 말하는 것이 똑같이 제 의무일 거예요. 당신이 그림 선생이라서 저희를 떠나셔야 하는 게 아니에요——”\n\n그녀는 잠시 멈추고, 얼굴을 온전히 나에게 돌려, 탁자 너머로 손을 뻗어 내 팔에 단단히 손을 얹었다.\n\n“당신이 그림 선생이라서가 아니라.” 그녀가 되풀이했다. “로라 페얼리가 혼인을 약속했기 때문이에요.”\n\n그 마지막 말이 총알처럼 내 가슴을 파고들었다. 내 팔은 그것을 붙든 손의 감각을 죄다 잃었다. 나는 움직이지도, 말하지도 않았다. 우리 발치의 마른 잎을 흩날리던 매서운 가을바람이, 마치 내 미친 희망도 마른 잎이라 나머지처럼 바람에 휩쓸려 가기라도 한 양, 별안간 그토록 차갑게 나에게 와닿았다. 희망이라니! 약혼했든 안 했든, 그녀는 나에게서 똑같이 멀었다. 다른 사내들이라면 내 처지에서 그것을 기억했을까? 나만큼 그녀를 사랑했다면 그러지 못했으리라.\n\n아픔은 지나갔고, 그것이 남긴 무디게 저리는 통증밖에 남지 않았다. 나는 다시 홀콤 양의 손이, 내 팔을 쥔 힘을 더하는 것을 느꼈다—나는 고개를 들어 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀의 크고 검은 눈이 나에게 못 박힌 채, 내가 느끼고 그녀가 본, 내 얼굴의 하얗게 질리는 변화를 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n“짓눌러 버려요!” 그녀가 말했다. “여기, 당신이 그녀를 처음 본 이곳에서, 짓눌러 버려요! 여자처럼 그 아래 움츠러들지 마세요. 그것을 뽑아내요, 사내처럼 발밑에 짓밟아 버려요!”\n\n그녀가 말하는 그 억눌린 격렬함, 그녀의 의지의 힘이—나에게 고정한 눈빛에, 그리고 아직 놓지 않은 내 팔을 쥔 그 손에 응축되어—내 의지에 전해져 나를 다잡아 주었다. 우리는 둘 다 잠시 잠자코 기다렸다. 그 시간이 끝날 무렵 나는 내 사내다움에 대한 그녀의 너그러운 믿음을 저버리지 않았다—적어도 겉으로는 자제심을 되찾았다.\n\n“이제 제정신이 드셨어요?”\n\n“당신과 그녀에게 용서를 청할 만큼은 제정신이 들었습니다, 홀콤 양. 당신의 충고에 따를 만큼, 그리고 달리 고마움을 증명할 수 없다면 그렇게라도 제 고마움을 증명할 만큼은 제정신이 들었습니다.”\n\n“당신은 그 말로 이미 그것을 증명하셨어요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “하트라이트 씨, 우리 사이에 숨김은 끝났어요. 제 동생이 저도 모르게 저에게 드러내 보인 것을, 저는 당신께 숨기는 척할 수가 없어요. 당신 자신을 위해서뿐 아니라 그 애를 위해서도 당신은 저희를 떠나셔야 해요. 이곳에서의 당신의 존재, 저희와의 어쩔 수 없는 친밀함이, 다른 온갖 면에서 하느님도 아시다시피 아무리 해롭지 않았다 해도, 그 애를 흔들어 놓고 비참하게 만들었어요. 제 목숨보다 그 애를 더 사랑하는 저, 제 신앙을 믿듯 그 순수하고 고귀하고 순진한 천성을 믿게 된 저는—혼인 약속에 어긋나는 감정의 첫 그림자가 그 애의 뜻과 달리 그 마음에 들어선 이래 그 애가 겪어 온 자책의 은밀한 괴로움을 너무나 잘 알아요. 그 애의 약혼이 그 애의 애정을 강하게 붙든 적이 있었다고는—이제 벌어진 일을 두고 그렇게 말하려 해 봐야 소용없겠지만—저는 말하지 않겠어요. 그것은 사랑이 아니라 명예의 약혼이에요. 그 애 아버지가 두 해 전 임종 자리에서 그것을 승낙하셨어요. 그 애 자신은 그것을 반기지도 물리치지도 않았어요—그저 기꺼이 받아들였을 뿐이에요. 당신이 이곳에 오기 전까지 그 애는, 크게 끌리지도 크게 물리지도 않는 남자와 혼인해, 혼인 전이 아니라 혼인 뒤에 (미워하기를 배우지 않는다면!) 그를 사랑하기를 배우는 수백의 다른 여자들과 같은 처지였어요. 저는 말로 다 할 수 없이 간절히 바라요—당신도 자기를 희생하는 용기로 함께 바라야 해요—그 옛 평온과 옛 만족을 어지럽힌 새 생각과 감정이, 두 번 다시 뽑아낼 수 없을 만큼 너무 깊이 뿌리내리지는 않았기를요. 당신의 부재가 (당신의 명예와 용기와 분별을 덜 믿었다면 제가 지금처럼 그것에 기대지 않았겠지요) 제 노력을 도울 것이고, 시간이 우리 셋 모두를 도울 거예요. 당신에 대한 제 첫 믿음이 아주 헛짚은 것은 아니었음을 아는 것만으로도 무언가 위안이 돼요. 당신에게 호소해 온 그 낯설고 버림받은 이에게—그 호소가 헛되지 않았던 이에게—그러했던 만큼, 당신이 스스로와의 관계를 불운하게도 잊어버린 그 제자에게도 덜 정직하거나 덜 사내답거나 덜 사려 깊지 않으리라는 것을 아는 것만으로도 무언가 위안이 돼요.”\n\n또다시 흰옷을 입은 여인에 대한 우연한 언급! 앤 캐서릭의 기억을 불러일으켜, 그녀를 피할 길 없는 숙명처럼 우리 사이에 세워 놓지 않고서는, 페얼리 양과 나에 대해 이야기할 가망이 없단 말인가?\n\n“제가 약속을 깨는 데 대해 페얼리 씨에게 무슨 변명을 하면 될지 말씀해 주세요.” 내가 말했다. “그 변명이 받아들여진 뒤 언제 떠나면 될지 말씀해 주세요. 당신과 당신의 충고에 온전히 따르기로 약속합니다.”\n\n“무엇보다 시간이 중요해요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “오늘 아침 제가 다음 월요일과 자줏빛 방을 정돈해 두어야 할 필요를 언급한 걸 들으셨지요. 월요일에 저희가 맞을 손님은——”\n\n나는 그녀가 더 자세히 말하기를 기다릴 수 없었다. 이제 내가 아는 것을 알고 나니, 아침 식탁에서의 페얼리 양의 표정과 태도의 기억이, 리머리지 하우스에서 맞을 그 손님이 그녀의 미래의 남편임을 나에게 일러 주었다. 나는 그것을 도로 억누르려 했으나, 그 순간 내 안에서 내 의지보다 강한 무언가가 솟구쳐, 나는 홀콤 양의 말을 끊었다.\n\n“오늘 떠나게 해 주세요.” 내가 쓰라리게 말했다. “빠를수록 좋습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 오늘은 안 돼요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “고용 기간이 끝나기 전에 떠나는 것에 대해 당신이 페얼리 씨에게 댈 수 있는 유일한 까닭은, 뜻밖의 사정으로 당장 런던으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 청할 수밖에 없다는 것뿐이에요. 그걸 말하는 건 내일, 우편이 오는 시각까지 기다리셔야 해요. 그래야 그분이 런던에서 온 편지의 도착과 결부시켜 당신 계획의 갑작스러운 변화를 이해하실 테니까요. 아무리 해롭지 않은 종류라도 속임수로 내려가는 것은 비참하고 역겨운 일이에요—하지만 저는 페얼리 씨를 알아요. 당신이 자기를 가지고 논다는 의심을 한번 그분 안에 불러일으키면, 그분은 당신을 놓아주기를 거부하실 거예요. 그분께는 금요일 아침에 말씀드리세요. 그다음에는 (고용주와의 당신 자신의 이익을 위해) 끝내지 못한 일을 되도록 어수선하지 않게 남겨 두는 데 시간을 쓰시고, 토요일에 이곳을 떠나세요. 그때면 당신에게도, 우리 모두에게도 충분히 이른 때일 거예요, 하트라이트 씨.”\n\n당신 뜻에 더없이 엄격히 따라 행동할 것을 믿으셔도 된다고 그녀에게 다짐하기도 전에, 우리는 둘 다 관목숲에서 다가오는 발소리에 소스라쳤다. 누군가 우리를 찾으러 저택에서 오고 있었다! 나는 피가 내 뺨으로 몰렸다가 다시 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈다. 이런 때, 이런 형편에 우리에게 빠르게 다가오는 그 세 번째 사람이 페얼리 양일 수 있을까?\n\n그것은 안도였다—그녀를 향한 내 처지가 이미 그토록 서글프게, 그토록 가망 없이 바뀌어 버렸기에—우리를 방해한 그 사람이 정자 입구에 나타나 다름 아닌 페얼리 양의 하녀임이 드러났을 때, 그것은 나에게 정말이지 안도였다.\n\n“잠깐 말씀 좀 드려도 될까요, 아씨?” 소녀가 다소 허둥대고 어수선한 태도로 말했다.\n\n홀콤 양은 계단을 내려가 관목숲으로 가더니, 하녀와 함께 몇 걸음 옆으로 물러났다.\n\n혼자 남겨진 나의 마음은, 어떤 말로도 묘사할 수 없는 의지가지없는 비참함의 느낌과 함께, 곧 돌아가야 할 외로운 런던 집의 고독과 절망으로 되돌아갔다. 나의 다정한 늙은 어머니와, 컴벌랜드에서의 내 앞날을 그토록 순진하게 함께 기뻐하던 누이에 대한 생각—그것들이 내 가슴에서 그토록 오래 쫓겨나 있었음을 이제 처음으로 깨닫는 것이 나의 부끄러움이자 자책인 그런 생각들이, 오래도록 소홀히 대한 옛 벗들의 정겨운 서글픔과 함께 나에게 되돌아왔다. 나의 어머니와 누이, 내가 깨진 약속에서 돌아와 내 비참한 비밀을 고백하며 그들에게 돌아갈 때 그들은 무엇을 느낄까—햄스테드 오두막에서의 그 마지막 행복한 밤에 그토록 희망에 차 나와 헤어졌던 그들이!\n\n또 앤 캐서릭이라니! 이제는 어머니와 누이와의 작별 저녁의 기억조차, 런던으로 돌아오던 그 달빛 산책의 다른 기억과 결부되지 않고서는 나에게 되돌아올 수 없었다. 그것은 무슨 뜻인가? 그 여인과 내가 다시 한번 만나게 될 것인가? 적어도 그럴 가능성은 있었다. 그녀는 내가 런던에 산다는 것을 알았을까? 그렇다. 나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다—준남작 지위의 남자를 많이 아느냐고 그토록 미심쩍게 물은 그 기이한 물음의 전이든 후든. 전이든 후든—그때 내 마음은 어느 쪽인지 기억할 만큼 차분하지 못했다.\n\n몇 분이 지난 뒤 홀콤 양이 하녀를 보내고 나에게 돌아왔다. 이제 그녀도 허둥대고 어수선해 보였다.\n\n“필요한 건 다 정했어요, 하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “우리는 벗이 마땅히 그러해야 하듯 서로를 이해했으니, 이제 곧바로 저택으로 돌아가도 되겠어요. 사실을 말하자면, 저는 로라가 걱정돼요. 그 애가 당장 저를 보고 싶다고 사람을 보냈는데, 하녀 말로는 오늘 아침 받은 편지 때문에 그 애가 몹시 동요한 듯하다는군요—틀림없이, 우리가 이곳에 오기 전에 제가 저택으로 보낸 바로 그 편지겠지요.”\n\n우리는 함께 서둘러 관목숲 오솔길을 되짚어 갔다. 홀콤 양은 제 쪽에서 할 필요가 있다고 여긴 말을 다 마쳤으나, 나는 내 쪽에서 하고 싶은 말을 다 마치지 못했다. 리머리지에서 맞을 그 손님이 페얼리 양의 미래의 남편임을 알아챈 순간부터, 나는 그가 누구인지 알고 싶은 쓰라린 호기심, 불타는 시샘 어린 조바심을 느끼고 있었다. 그 물음을 던질 앞으로의 기회가 쉽게 오지 않을 수도 있는지라, 나는 저택으로 돌아가는 길에 위험을 무릅쓰고 그것을 물었다.\n\n“우리가 서로를 이해했다고 이제 친절히 말씀해 주셨으니, 홀콤 양.” 내가 말했다. “당신의 관용에 대한 제 고마움과 당신 뜻에 대한 제 순종을 이제 확신하시니, 감히 여쭤도 될까요—누가”—(나는 머뭇거렸다—억지로 그를 떠올리게 만들었으나, 그를 그녀의 정혼자로서 입에 올리는 것은 더더욱 힘들었다)—“페얼리 양과 약혼한 그 신사가 누구인지요?”\n\n그녀의 마음은 동생에게서 받은 전갈에 온통 쏠려 있는 것이 분명했다. 그녀는 서둘러, 넋 놓은 채로 대답했다—\n\n“햄프셔에 큰 재산을 가진 신사예요.”\n\n햄프셔라니! 앤 캐서릭의 고향. 또다시, 그리고 거듭 또다시, 흰옷을 입은 여인. 거기에는 숙명이 있었다.\n\n“그리고 그 이름은요?” 나는 되도록 조용하고 무심하게 말했다.\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이에요.”\n\n경—퍼시벌 경이라니! 내가 어쩌다 알지도 모를 준남작 지위의 남자에 대한 앤 캐서릭의 물음—그 미심쩍은 물음이, 정자에서 홀콤 양이 나에게 돌아옴으로써 내 마음에서 채 물러가기도 전에, 그녀 자신의 대답으로 다시 불려 나왔다. 나는 별안간 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이에요.” 그녀는 내가 앞서 한 대답을 못 들은 줄 알고 되풀이했다.\n\n“기사(騎士)입니까, 준남작입니까?” 나는 더는 감출 수 없는 동요와 함께 물었다.\n\n그녀는 잠시 멈추었다가, 다소 냉랭하게 대답했다—\n\n“준남작이지요, 물론.”\n\n# XI\n\n저택으로 돌아오는 동안 양쪽 어느 편에서도 더는 한 마디도 오가지 않았다. 홀콤 양은 곧장 동생의 방으로 서둘러 갔고, 나는 내 화실로 물러나 아직 배접하고 손보지 못한 페얼리 씨의 그림들을 다른 사람의 손에 넘기기 전에 모두 정리해 두려 했다. 여태 억눌러 왔던 생각들, 내 처지를 그 어느 때보다 견디기 어렵게 만드는 생각들이, 혼자가 된 지금 나에게 몰려들었다.\n\n그녀는 약혼한 몸이었고, 그녀의 미래의 남편은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이었다. 준남작의 지위를 지닌 사람이며, 햄프셔에 재산을 소유한 사람.\n\n영국에는 준남작이 수백 명 있었고, 햄프셔에는 지주가 수십 명 있었다. 통상적인 증거의 규칙으로 판단한다면, 나에게는 여기까지로는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 흰옷을 입은 여인이 나에게 던졌던 그 수상쩍은 물음의 말들과 연결시킬 근거가 그림자만큼도 없었다. 그런데도 나는 그를 그 말들과 연결시키고 있었다. 그것은 이제 그가 내 마음속에서 페얼리 양과 결부되어 버렸고, 페얼리 양은 또 그녀대로, 두 사람 사이의 그 불길한 닮음을 내가 발견한 그 밤 이래로 앤 캐서릭과 결부되어 있었기 때문일까? 그날 아침의 일들이 나를 이미 그토록 무너뜨려 놓아서, 흔한 우연과 흔한 일치가 내 상상에 던져 주는 어떤 망상에도 내가 휘둘리게 된 것일까? 뭐라 말할 수 없었다. 다만 여름 별장에서 돌아오는 길에 홀콤 양과 나 사이에 오간 일이 나를 몹시 이상하게 뒤흔들어 놓았다는 것만은 느낄 수 있었다. 미래의 어둠 속에 숨어 우리 모두에게 보이지 않는 어떤 위험이 있다는 예감이 나를 강하게 사로잡았다. 내가 이미, 컴벌랜드를 곧 떠난다 해도 끊어 낼 힘이 없는 어떤 사건의 사슬에 매여 버린 것은 아닐까 하는 의심—우리 가운데 누구도 그 끝을 실제 그대로의 끝으로 보고 있지 못한 것은 아닐까 하는 의심—이 점점 더 어둡게 내 마음 위로 몰려들었다. 짧고도 주제넘었던 내 사랑의 비참한 종말이 안겨 준 고통의 감각은, 그토록 사무치는 것이었음에도, 무언가가 어렴풋이 다가오고 있다는, 무언가가 보이지 않게 위협하고 있다는, 시간이 우리 머리 위에 그것을 들고 있다는 더 강한 감각에 눌려 무디어지고 잦아드는 듯했다.\n\n그림들을 붙들고 앉은 지 반 시간 남짓 되었을 때, 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 내가 대답하자 문이 열렸고, 놀랍게도 홀콤 양이 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n그녀의 태도는 노기에 차 있었고 동요해 있었다. 내가 의자를 권하기도 전에 그녀는 스스로 의자를 끌어당겨, 내 곁에 바짝 붙여 놓고 앉았다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 적어도 오늘 하루만큼은 우리 사이에 오갈 괴로운 이야깃거리가 다 떨어졌기를 바랐어요. 그런데 그렇게 되지가 않는군요. 다가오는 혼사를 두고 제 동생을 겁주려는 어떤 음험한 흉계가 벌어지고 있어요. 제가 정원사를 저택으로 올려 보내는 걸 보셨지요, 낯선 필체로 페얼리 양 앞으로 주소가 적힌 편지를 들려서요?”\n\n“보았지요.”\n\n“그 편지는 익명의 편지예요—퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 제 동생의 눈에 깎아내리려는 비열한 수작이지요. 그것이 그 애를 어찌나 동요시키고 놀라게 했던지, 제가 그 애의 방을 나와 여기로 올 수 있을 만큼 마음을 가라앉혀 주는 데 이루 말할 수 없는 애를 먹었답니다. 이것이 제가 당신과 의논해서는 안 될 집안일이고, 당신으로서는 아무런 관심도 이해관계도 느낄 수 없는 일이라는 건 압니다만——”\n\n“무슨 말씀을요, 홀콤 양. 페얼리 양의 행복이나 당신의 행복에 관계되는 일이라면 무엇에든 저는 더할 나위 없이 깊은 관심과 이해를 느낍니다.”\n\n“그렇게 말씀해 주시니 기뻐요. 이 집 안에서든 밖에서든 저에게 조언해 줄 수 있는 분은 당신뿐이에요. 페얼리 씨는 그 건강 상태에다, 온갖 종류의 성가신 일과 알 수 없는 일이라면 질색을 하시니 아예 생각할 수도 없고요. 목사님은 선량하지만 무른 분이라 제 직분의 판에 박힌 일 말고는 아무것도 모르시지요. 그리고 이웃들은 딱, 곤경과 위험의 때에 번거롭게 할 수 없는 그런 편안하고 느긋한 지인들이고요. 제가 알고 싶은 건 이거예요. 제가 지금 당장 이 편지를 쓴 사람을 알아낼 수 있는 조치를 취해야 할까요? 아니면 기다렸다가 내일 페얼리 씨의 법률 고문에게 부탁해야 할까요? 하루를 벌 것이냐 잃을 것이냐 하는—어쩌면 아주 중대할지도 모르는—문제예요. 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주세요, 하트라이트 씨. 이미 필요에 몰려 아주 미묘한 사정 아래 당신을 제 속내로 끌어들이지 않았더라면, 이렇게 어찌할 바 모르는 제 처지조차도 아마 변명이 되지 못했을 거예요. 하지만 사정이 이러하니, 우리 사이에 그 모든 일이 지나간 마당에, 당신이 겨우 석 달 사귄 벗이라는 사실을 잊는다 해도 제가 그르지는 않겠지요.”\n\n그녀가 나에게 편지를 건넸다. 편지는 아무런 서두의 인사말도 없이 불쑥 이렇게 시작되었다—\n\n“당신은 꿈을 믿나요? 당신 자신을 위해서, 나는 당신이 믿기를 바랍니다. 성경이 꿈과 그 이루어짐에 대해 무어라 하는지 보세요(창세기 40장 8절, 41장 25절, 다니엘 4장 18절에서 25절). 그리고 너무 늦기 전에 내가 보내는 경고를 받아들이세요.\n\n“어젯밤 나는 당신 꿈을 꾸었어요, 페얼리 양. 나는 어느 교회의 성찬 난간 안쪽에 서 있는 꿈을 꾸었지요—나는 제단 탁자의 한쪽에, 그리고 성직자는 흰 예복을 걸치고 기도서를 든 채 다른 쪽에 서 있었어요.\n\n“얼마 뒤 교회의 통로를 따라 한 남자와 한 여자가 혼인하러 우리 쪽으로 걸어왔습니다. 그 여자는 당신이었어요. 당신은 그 아름다운 흰 비단 드레스와 길고 하얀 레이스 면사포 속에서 어찌나 곱고 순결해 보였던지, 내 가슴이 당신 때문에 아팠고 눈에 눈물이 고였답니다.\n\n“그것은 연민의 눈물이었어요, 아가씨, 하늘이 축복하는 눈물이지요. 그리고 그 눈물은 우리 모두가 흘리는 여느 날의 눈물처럼 내 눈에서 떨어지는 대신, 두 줄기 빛으로 변해 당신과 함께 제단에 서 있는 그 남자에게로 점점 더 가까이 비스듬히 뻗어 가더니, 마침내 그의 가슴에 닿았습니다. 두 줄기 빛은 나와 그 사람 사이에 두 개의 무지개처럼 아치를 그리며 솟아올랐어요. 나는 그 빛을 따라 바라보았고, 그의 가장 깊은 마음속을 들여다보았습니다.\n\n“당신이 혼인하려던 그 남자의 겉모습은 보기에 그런대로 훤했어요. 키가 크지도 작지도 않았지요—중간 키에서 조금 못 미쳤어요. 몸이 가볍고 활달하고 기운이 넘치는 남자였고—보기에 마흔댓 살쯤 되었습니다. 얼굴은 창백하고 이마 위쪽으로는 머리가 벗어졌으나, 나머지 머리에는 검은 머리카락이 있었어요. 턱의 수염은 밀었지만 뺨과 윗입술에는 곱고 짙은 갈색으로 기르도록 두었더군요. 눈도 갈색이었고 아주 반짝였습니다. 코는 곧고 잘생기고 여자의 것이라 해도 좋을 만큼 섬세했어요. 손도 그러했지요. 그는 이따금 마른기침에 시달렸고, 흰 오른손을 입에 갖다 댈 때 손등을 가로지른 오랜 상처의 붉은 흉터가 드러났습니다. 내가 꿈에서 본 사람이 바로 그 사람인가요? 당신이 가장 잘 알겠지요, 페얼리 양. 내가 속은 것인지 아닌지는 당신이 말할 수 있을 거예요. 이제 내가 그 겉모습 아래에서 본 것을 읽어 보세요—간청하니, 읽고 깨우침을 얻으세요.\n\n“나는 그 두 줄기 빛을 따라 바라보았고, 그의 가장 깊은 마음속을 들여다보았습니다. 그것은 밤처럼 새까맸고, 그 위에는 타락한 천사의 필적인 붉은 불꽃 글자로 이렇게 적혀 있었어요. ‘연민도 없고 뉘우침도 없다. 그는 다른 이들의 길에 비참함을 뿌려 왔고, 앞으로 살아가며 제 곁에 선 이 여자의 길에도 비참함을 뿌리리라.’ 나는 그것을 읽었고, 그러자 빛줄기가 옮겨 가 그의 어깨 너머를 가리켰습니다. 그리고 거기, 그의 뒤에는 악귀 하나가 웃으며 서 있었어요. 다시 빛줄기가 옮겨 가 당신의 어깨 너머를 가리켰습니다. 그리고 거기, 당신 뒤에는 천사 하나가 울며 서 있었어요. 그리고 빛줄기가 세 번째로 옮겨 가, 당신과 그 남자 사이를 곧장 가리켰습니다. 빛은 넓어지고 또 넓어지며 두 사람을 서로에게서 떼어 밀어냈어요. 그리고 성직자는 혼인 예식문을 찾았으나 헛일이었지요. 그것은 책에서 사라져 버렸고, 그는 책장을 덮어 절망 속에 내려놓았습니다. 그리고 나는 눈에 눈물이 가득하고 가슴이 두근거리는 채로 깨어났어요—나는 꿈을 믿으니까요.\n\n“당신도 믿으세요, 페얼리 양—당신 자신을 위해 간청하니, 나처럼 믿으세요. 요셉과 다니엘, 그리고 성경 속의 다른 이들도 꿈을 믿었습니다. 그 손에 흉터가 있는 남자의 지난 삶을 캐어 보세요, 당신을 그의 가련한 아내로 만들 그 말을 입에 담기 전에 말이에요. 나는 이 경고를 나를 위해 하는 것이 아니라 당신을 위해 하는 것입니다. 나는 당신의 안녕에, 내가 숨을 쉬는 한 사그라지지 않을 관심을 두고 있어요. 당신 어머니의 딸은 내 마음속에 애틋한 자리를 차지하고 있답니다—당신 어머니는 나의 첫 번째, 나의 가장 좋은, 나의 단 하나뿐인 벗이었으니까요.”\n\n그 기이한 편지는 어떤 종류의 서명도 없이 거기서 끝나 있었다.\n\n필체는 실마리를 잡을 가망을 조금도 주지 않았다. 그것은 줄이 그어진 종이 위에, 전문 용어로 “작은 글씨체”라 부르는, 옹색하고 판에 박힌 습자책 글씨로 적혀 있었다. 힘이 없고 흐릿했으며 잉크 얼룩으로 지저분해져 있었으나, 그 밖에는 이렇다 할 특징이 없었다.\n\n“이건 무식한 사람의 편지가 아니에요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “그러면서도 상류층의 교육받은 사람이 쓴 편지라기에는 분명 너무 두서가 없고요. 혼례복과 면사포를 들먹인 대목이며 자잘한 표현들을 보면 어떤 여자의 손에서 나온 것 같아요. 어떻게 생각하세요, 하트라이트 씨?”\n\n“저도 그렇게 생각합니다. 제가 보기에 이건 단지 여자의 편지일 뿐 아니라, 그 정신이 필시——한 여자의 편지입니다.”\n\n“정신이 온전치 못한?” 홀콤 양이 말을 받았다. “저에게도 그렇게 보였어요.”\n\n나는 대답하지 않았다. 말을 하는 동안 내 눈은 편지의 마지막 문장에 머물러 있었다. “당신 어머니의 딸은 내 마음속에 애틋한 자리를 차지하고 있답니다—당신 어머니는 나의 첫 번째, 나의 가장 좋은, 나의 단 하나뿐인 벗이었으니까요.” 그 말과, 편지를 쓴 사람의 제정신 여부에 대해 방금 내 입에서 새어 나온 의심이 함께 내 마음에 작용하여 하나의 생각을 떠올리게 했는데, 나는 그것을 드러내 놓고 말하기는커녕 남몰래 품는 것조차 문자 그대로 두려웠다. 나는 내 자신의 판단력이 균형을 잃을 위험에 처한 것은 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다. 일어나는 온갖 이상한 일과 튀어나오는 온갖 뜻밖의 말을 언제나 똑같은 숨은 근원과 똑같은 불길한 영향으로 거슬러 올라가 잇는 것은 거의 편집광이나 다름없어 보였다. 나는 이번만큼은 나 자신의 용기와 나 자신의 분별을 지키기 위해, 명백한 사실이 뒷받침하지 않는 어떤 결론에도 이르지 않기로, 그리고 추측의 모습으로 나를 꾀는 모든 것에 단호히 등을 돌리기로 결심했다.\n\n“이것을 쓴 사람을 추적할 가망이 조금이라도 있다면.” 나는 편지를 홀콤 양에게 돌려주며 말했다. “기회가 오는 그 순간에 붙잡는 편이 나쁠 것은 없겠지요. 제 생각에는 정원사에게 그 편지를 준 나이 든 여자에 대해 다시 한번 물어보고, 그런 뒤에 마을에서 계속 알아보아야 할 것 같습니다. 그런데 먼저 한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 방금 내일 페얼리 씨의 법률 고문에게 부탁하는 쪽도 있다고 말씀하셨지요. 그분과 더 일찍 연락할 방법은 없습니까? 어째서 오늘은 안 됩니까?”\n\n“설명을 하자면.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다. “동생의 혼약과 얽힌 몇 가지 사정을 이야기해야만 하는데, 오늘 아침에는 그것을 당신에게 말할 필요도 없고 그러는 게 좋지도 않다고 생각했지요. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 월요일에 이곳으로 오는 목적 가운데 하나는, 여태 전혀 정해지지 않은 채로 남아 있는 혼례 시기를 정하려는 거예요. 그분은 그 일이 올해가 가기 전에 치러지기를 몹시 바라고 있어요.”\n\n“페얼리 양이 그 바람을 알고 있습니까?” 나는 다급히 물었다.\n\n“그 애는 전혀 눈치채지 못하고 있고, 이런 일이 있은 마당에 제가 그 애에게 알려 주는 책임을 떠맡지는 않겠어요. 퍼시벌 경은 자기 뜻을 페얼리 씨에게만 말했고, 페얼리 씨는 로라의 후견인으로서 그 뜻을 밀어 줄 준비도 되어 있고 그러기를 바란다고 저에게 직접 말씀하셨어요. 그분은 런던에 있는 집안 변호사 길모어 씨에게 편지를 보내셨고요. 마침 길모어 씨는 일 때문에 글래스고에 가 있어서, 런던으로 돌아오는 길에 리머리지 하우스에 들르겠다는 답장을 보내왔어요. 내일 도착해서 며칠 우리와 함께 머물 텐데, 퍼시벌 경이 자기 뜻을 펼 시간을 주기 위해서지요. 그분이 뜻을 이루면 길모어 씨는 그때 런던으로 돌아가면서 제 동생의 혼인 재산 계약에 대한 지시를 가지고 갈 거예요. 이제 제가 어째서 법률 자문을 내일까지 기다리자고 말하는지 아시겠지요, 하트라이트 씨? 길모어 씨는 페얼리 가문 두 대에 걸친 오래되고 검증된 벗이고, 우리는 그분을 다른 누구에게도 그러지 못할 만큼 믿을 수 있어요.”\n\n혼인 재산 계약! 그 두 마디를 듣기만 했는데도 나는 질투 어린 절망에 찔렸고, 그것은 내 안의 더 높고 더 나은 본능에 독이 되었다. 나는 생각하기 시작했다—이것을 고백하기란 괴로운 일이나, 내가 이제 밝히기로 다짐한 이 끔찍한 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 하나도 숨겨서는 안 된다—나는 익명의 편지에 담긴 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 막연한 혐의들을, 가증스러울 만큼 간절한 희망을 품고 생각하기 시작했다. 만약 그 터무니없는 고발들이 진실이라는 토대 위에 서 있는 것이라면? 만약 그 진실이 승낙의 치명적인 말이 오가고 혼인 재산 계약이 작성되기 전에 증명될 수 있다면? 그 뒤로 나는 그때 나를 움직인 감정이 페얼리 양의 이익에 대한 순수한 헌신에서 시작해 거기서 끝났다고 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 나 자신을 속여 그리 믿게 하는 데 끝내 성공하지 못했고, 이제 와 남들을 속이려 들어서도 안 된다. 그 감정은 그녀와 혼인할 그 남자에 대한 무모하고 앙심에 찬 가망 없는 증오에서 시작해 거기서 끝났다.\n\n“무엇이든 알아내야 한다면.” 나는 이제 나를 이끌고 있는 그 새로운 영향 아래에서 말했다. “단 일 분도 헛되이 흘려보내지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 저로서는 다시 한번, 정원사에게 두 번째로 물어보고 그 직후에 곧바로 마을에서 알아보는 것이 옳겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 수 없습니다.”\n\n“두 가지 다 제가 도움이 될 수 있을 것 같아요.” 홀콤 양이 일어서며 말했다. “당장 나가요, 하트라이트 씨. 그리고 함께 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 봐요.”\n\n나는 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주려고 손잡이를 잡고 있었으나—길을 나서기 전에 중요한 질문 하나를 하려고 문득 멈춰 섰다.\n\n“그 익명의 편지의 한 문단에는.” 내가 말했다. “세세한 인물 묘사가 몇 문장 들어 있더군요. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 이름은 나오지 않는다는 걸 압니다만—그 묘사가 조금이라도 그분과 닮았습니까?”\n\n“정확해요—나이를 마흔다섯이라 한 것까지도——”\n\n마흔다섯. 그런데 그녀는 아직 스물한 살도 되지 않았다! 그 나이의 남자가 그 나이의 아내를 맞는 일은 날마다 있었고—경험은 그런 혼인이 흔히 가장 행복한 축에 든다는 것을 보여 주었다. 나도 그것을 알고 있었다—그런데도 그의 나이를 그녀의 나이와 견주어 보자, 그 나이가 언급되었다는 사실만으로도 그에 대한 나의 맹목적인 증오와 불신이 더해졌다.\n\n“정확해요.” 홀콤 양이 말을 이었다. “오른손의 흉터까지도요. 그건 몇 해 전 이탈리아를 여행하던 때에 입은 상처의 흉터예요. 그분 외모의 온갖 특징을 편지를 쓴 사람이 속속들이 훤히 알고 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어요.”\n\n“기억이 맞다면 그분이 앓는 기침까지도 나와 있지요?”\n\n“네, 그것도 정확하게요. 본인은 대수롭지 않게 여기지만, 때로는 벗들이 그분을 걱정하게 만들지요.”\n\n“그분의 인품에 대해 수군거리는 말이 들린 적은 한 번도 없겠지요?”\n\n“하트라이트 씨! 그 파렴치한 편지에 흔들릴 만큼 부당하신 분은 아니시겠지요?”\n\n나는 피가 뺨으로 몰리는 것을 느꼈다. 그것이 정말로 나를 흔들어 놓았다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“그러지 않기를 바랍니다.” 나는 당황하여 대답했다. “그런 질문을 할 자격이 저에게 없었는지도 모르겠군요.”\n\n“물어봐 주셔서 섭섭하지 않아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “덕분에 퍼시벌 경의 평판에 정당한 대접을 해 드릴 수 있으니까요. 하트라이트 씨, 그분에 대한 수군거림은 단 한 마디도 저나 저희 집안에 닿은 적이 없어요. 그분은 경합 선거를 두 번이나 성공적으로 치르셨고, 그 시련에서 아무런 상처 없이 나오셨지요. 영국에서 그렇게 할 수 있는 사람이라면, 그 인품이 확립된 사람이에요.”\n\n나는 말없이 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주고 뒤따라 나갔다. 그녀는 나를 납득시키지 못했다. 설령 기록의 천사가 하늘에서 내려와 그녀의 말을 뒷받침해 주고 죽을 목숨인 내 눈앞에 제 책을 펼쳐 보였다 한들, 그 기록의 천사조차 나를 납득시키지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n우리는 정원사가 여느 때처럼 일하고 있는 것을 보았다. 아무리 캐물어도 그 소년의 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 우둔함에서는 조금이라도 중요한 대답 하나 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그에게 편지를 준 여자는 나이 든 여자였다. 그 여자는 그에게 한 마디도 하지 않았고, 몹시 서두르며 남쪽을 향해 가 버렸다. 정원사가 우리에게 해 줄 수 있는 말은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n마을은 저택의 남쪽에 있었다. 그래서 우리는 다음으로 마을을 향해 갔다.\n\n# XII\n\n리머리지에서 우리의 탐문은 사방으로, 온갖 부류와 처지의 사람들을 상대로 끈기 있게 이어졌다. 그러나 아무 성과도 없었다. 마을 사람 셋은 분명히 그 여자를 보았다고 우리에게 장담했지만, 그녀의 생김새를 도무지 설명하지 못했고 마지막으로 보았을 때 그녀가 어느 쪽으로 가고 있었는지에 대해서도 도무지 의견이 맞지 않았으므로, 전면적 무지라는 일반 법칙에 대한 이 세 사람의 빛나는 예외는 도움도 관찰력도 없는 그 이웃들의 무리보다 우리에게 조금도 더 실질적인 보탬이 되지 못했다.\n\n쓸모없는 조사의 행로는 이윽고 우리를 페얼리 부인이 세운 학교가 자리한 마을 끝자락으로 데려갔다. 남학생들이 쓰는 쪽 건물 옆을 지나면서, 나는 그 직책상 이 고장에서 가장 지각 있는 사람이라고 짐작해도 좋을 교사에게 마지막으로 한 번 물어보는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 제안했다.\n\n“선생님은 아마 학생들을 가르치느라 바빴을 거예요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “바로 그 여자가 마을을 지나갔다가 되돌아온 그 시각에 말이죠. 그래도 해 볼 수는 있겠네요.”\n\n우리는 운동장 울타리 안으로 들어가, 건물 뒤편에 있는 문으로 돌아가려고 교실 창가를 지나 걸었다. 나는 잠시 창 앞에 멈춰 서서 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n교사는 높은 책상 앞에 나를 등지고 앉아, 자기 앞에 죄다 모여 있는 학생들을 향해 일장 훈시를 늘어놓는 듯했는데, 한 명만은 예외였다. 그 한 명의 예외는 몸이 다부지고 머리가 새하얀 소년으로, 나머지 전부와 떨어져 구석의 걸상 위에 서 있었다—외따로 떨어진 벌과 수치의 무인도에 갇힌, 가엾은 작은 크루소였다.\n\n돌아가서 보니 문은 반쯤 열려 있었고, 우리 둘이 현관 지붕 아래 잠시 멈춰 선 사이 교사의 목소리가 또렷이 들려왔다.\n\n“자, 얘들아.” 그 목소리가 말했다. “내가 하는 말을 명심해라. 이 학교에서 귀신 이야기가 한 마디라도 더 들리면, 너희 모두에게 좋지 않을 것이다. 귀신 따위는 없다. 그러므로 귀신을 믿는 아이는 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 믿는 것이며, 리머리지 학교에 다니면서 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 믿는 아이는 이성과 규율에 등을 돌리는 것이니 그에 마땅한 벌을 받아야 한다. 너희 모두 제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 저기 걸상 위에 망신스럽게 서 있는 것을 보고 있다. 저 아이가 벌을 받는 것은 어젯밤 귀신을 보았다고 말했기 때문이 아니라, 너무 뻔뻔하고 너무 고집이 세서 이치에 귀를 기울이지 않기 때문이며, 그런 것은 결코 있을 수 없다고 내가 일러 주었는데도 귀신을 보았다고 우겨 대기 때문이다. 달리 방법이 없다면, 나는 제이콥 포슬스웨이트에게서 회초리로 귀신을 몰아낼 작정이다. 그리고 그 일이 너희 중 누구에게라도 번진다면, 나는 한 걸음 더 나아가 학교 전체에서 회초리로 귀신을 몰아낼 작정이다.”\n\n“방문하기에 딱한 순간을 고른 모양이네요.” 홀콤 양이 말하며, 교사의 훈시가 끝나는 참에 문을 밀어 열고 앞장서 들어갔다.\n\n우리의 등장은 소년들 사이에 강한 파문을 일으켰다. 그들은 우리가 제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 회초리 맞는 것을 보려고 일부러 찾아왔다고 여기는 눈치였다.\n\n“다들 집에 가서 점심을 먹어라.” 교사가 말했다. “제이콥만 빼고. 제이콥은 있는 자리에 그대로 있어야 한다. 그리고 귀신이 원한다면 귀신이 저 아이의 점심을 가져다주겠지.”\n\n학교 친구들과 점심의 전망이 한꺼번에 사라지자 제이콥의 꿋꿋함도 무너졌다. 그는 주머니에서 두 손을 꺼내 제 주먹을 물끄러미 들여다보더니, 아주 신중하게 그것을 눈까지 들어 올렸고, 눈에 닿자 천천히 빙글빙글 비벼 댔는데, 그 동작에는 일정한 간격을 두고 잇따르는 짧은 훌쩍임의 발작이 곁들여졌다—어린 슬픔이 쏘아 올리는 콧소리의 조포(弔砲)였다.\n\n“여쭤볼 것이 있어서 왔습니다, 뎀스터 씨.” 홀콤 양이 교사에게 말을 건넸다. “선생님이 귀신을 쫓아내는 일에 몰두해 계실 줄은 미처 몰랐네요. 대체 무슨 일이죠? 정말로 무슨 일이 있었던 건가요?”\n\n“저 못된 아이가 어제 저녁에 귀신을 보았다고 떠들어 대며 학교 전체를 겁먹게 했습니다, 홀콤 양.” 교사가 대답했다. “제가 아무리 타일러도 여전히 그 터무니없는 이야기를 우겨 대고 있고요.”\n\n“참으로 놀랍군요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “저 아이들 중에 귀신을 볼 만한 상상력을 가진 아이가 있으리라고는 생각도 못 했어요. 리머리지에서 어린 정신을 길러 내는 고된 노역에 참으로 새로운 짐이 하나 더해진 셈이네요. 부디 잘 헤쳐 나가시기를 진심으로 빕니다, 뎀스터 씨. 그건 그렇고, 제가 왜 여기 왔는지, 무엇을 알고 싶은지 말씀드리죠.”\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 우리가 이미 마을의 거의 모든 사람에게 물었던 것과 똑같은 질문을 교사에게 던졌다. 돌아온 것은 똑같이 맥 빠지는 대답이었다. 뎀스터 씨는 우리가 찾는 그 낯선 여자를 본 적이 없었다.\n\n“저택으로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠어요, 하트라이트 씨.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “우리가 원하는 정보는 아무래도 찾을 수 없는 모양이니까요.”\n\n그녀는 뎀스터 씨에게 인사를 하고 교실을 나서려던 참이었는데, 참회의 걸상 위에서 가엾게 훌쩍이고 있는 제이콥 포슬스웨이트의 처량한 처지가 그 곁을 지나던 그녀의 눈길을 끌었고, 문을 열기 전에 그 작은 죄수에게 한마디 건네려고 너그러이 걸음을 멈추게 했다.\n\n“이 어리석은 아이야.” 그녀가 말했다. “왜 뎀스터 선생님께 용서를 빌고 귀신 이야기는 입을 다물지 않는 거니?”\n\n“에!—그래도 지는 기신을 봤다 카이예.” 제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 겁에 질린 눈으로 빤히 바라보며 울음을 터뜨린 채 우겨 댔다.\n\n“허튼소리! 너는 그런 걸 본 적이 없어. 귀신이라니! 무슨 귀신이——”\n\n“죄송합니다만, 홀콤 양.” 교사가 다소 거북한 듯 끼어들었다. “저 아이에게 캐묻지 않으시는 편이 좋을 듯합니다. 저 아이 이야기의 완강한 어리석음은 도저히 믿기 어려울 지경이라, 자칫 아이가 아무것도 모르고——”\n\n“아무것도 모르고 뭘 한다는 거죠?” 홀콤 양이 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n“아무것도 모르고 아가씨의 심기를 상하게 할까 봐서요.” 뎀스터 씨가 몹시 당황한 낯으로 말했다.\n\n“참, 뎀스터 씨, 제 심기가 저런 개구쟁이 하나에 상할 만큼 약하다고 여기시다니 저에게 대단한 찬사를 보내시는군요!” 그녀는 빈정대는 듯 도전적인 태도로 어린 제이콥에게 돌아서서 직접 캐묻기 시작했다. “자!” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 이 일을 전부 알아야겠다. 이 못된 아이야, 귀신을 언제 봤니?”\n\n“어제 저녁예, 해거름에예.” 제이콥이 대답했다.\n\n“아! 어제 저녁 땅거미 질 무렵에 봤다는 거지? 그래, 어떻게 생겼더냐?”\n\n“온통 하얗지예—기신이라믄 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로예.” 귀신을 보았다는 아이가 제 나이답지 않은 확신을 가지고 대답했다.\n\n“그래서 그건 어디 있었니?”\n\n“저 짝 저기, 교회 무덤가에예—기신이라믄 마땅히 있어야 할 데라예.”\n\n“‘기신’이라면 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로—‘기신’이라면 마땅히 있어야 할 데—아니 이 어린 바보야, 너는 마치 갓난아기 적부터 귀신의 예절과 풍습에 익숙하기라도 했던 것처럼 말하는구나! 어쨌든 네 이야기는 손끝까지 훤히 꿰고 있는 모양이다. 다음에는 그게 누구의 귀신이었는지까지 나에게 일러 줄 수 있다고 하겠구나?”\n\n“에! 그거야 지가 말할 수 있지예.” 제이콥이 음울한 승리감을 띤 태도로 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다.\n\n홀콤 양이 제자를 심문하는 동안 뎀스터 씨는 이미 여러 번 말을 꺼내려 했고, 이제는 제 말이 들리도록 제법 단호하게 끼어들었다.\n\n“실례합니다, 홀콤 양.” 그가 말했다. “감히 말씀드리자면, 그런 질문을 하시는 것은 저 아이를 부추기는 일일 뿐입니다.”\n\n“하나만 더 묻고 나면 아주 만족하겠어요, 뎀스터 씨. 자.” 그녀가 소년에게 돌아서며 말을 이었다. “그래, 그게 누구의 귀신이었니?”\n\n“페얼리 마님의 기신이라예.” 제이콥이 속삭이듯 대답했다.\n\n이 놀라운 대답이 홀콤 양에게 미친 효과는, 그녀가 그것을 듣지 못하게 하려고 교사가 보였던 조바심을 충분히 정당화해 주었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 분노로 새빨개졌다—그녀는 어린 제이콥을 향해 성난 기색으로 홱 돌아섰고, 그 서슬에 겁을 먹은 아이는 새로 울음을 터뜨렸다—그녀는 아이에게 말하려고 입술을 열었다가—이내 스스로를 다잡고, 아이 대신 교사에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n“저런 아이에게 제 말에 대한 책임을 묻는 것은.” 그녀가 말했다. “부질없는 일이죠. 그 생각을 저 아이 머릿속에 넣어 준 것은 다른 사람들이라고 저는 거의 확신해요. 이 마을에, 뎀스터 씨, 여기 사는 모든 이가 제 어머니의 기억에 마땅히 바쳐야 할 존경과 감사를 잊어버린 사람들이 있다면, 제가 그들을 찾아낼 것이고, 저에게 페얼리 씨에 대한 영향력이 조금이라도 있다면 그들은 그 대가를 치르게 될 거예요.”\n\n“아니기를 바랍니다—아니, 정말이지 확신합니다, 홀콤 양—아가씨께서 잘못 짐작하신 거라고요.” 교사가 말했다. “이 일은 저 아이 자신의 삐뚤어진 고집과 어리석음에서 시작해 거기서 끝납니다. 저 아이는 어제 저녁 교회 묘지를 지나다가 흰옷을 입은 여자를 보았거나, 보았다고 생각했습니다. 그리고 그 형체는, 실제였든 상상이었든, 대리석 십자가 옆에 서 있었지요. 저 아이도, 리머리지의 다른 모든 사람도 그것이 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위에 세워진 기념비라는 것을 압니다. 이 두 가지 정황이면 아가씨를 그토록 당연하게 놀라게 한 그 대답을 저 아이 스스로 떠올리기에 충분하지 않겠습니까?”\n\n홀콤 양은 납득한 것 같지 않았지만, 교사의 사건 설명이 너무나 사리에 맞아 대놓고 반박할 수 없다는 것을 분명히 느꼈다. 그녀는 그저 신경 써 주어 고맙다는 인사와, 의혹이 풀리면 다시 찾아오겠다는 약속으로 대답을 대신했다. 그렇게 말하고 그녀는 인사를 하고는 앞장서서 교실을 나섰다.\n\n이 기이한 장면 내내 나는 한쪽에 떨어져 서서 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 나 나름의 결론을 끌어내고 있었다. 다시 우리 둘만 남게 되자마자 홀콤 양은 내가 들은 것에 대해 어떤 의견이라도 세웠는지 물었다.\n\n“아주 확고한 의견이 있습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “제가 믿기로는, 그 아이의 이야기에는 사실의 근거가 있습니다. 고백하건대 저는 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위 기념비를 보고, 그 주변의 땅을 살펴보고 싶어 견딜 수가 없군요.”\n\n“무덤은 보시게 해 드리죠.”\n\n그녀는 그렇게 대답한 뒤 말을 멈추고, 우리가 걸어가는 동안 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. “교실에서 있었던 일이.” 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. “그 편지 문제에서 제 정신을 어찌나 완전히 앗아 갔는지, 다시 그리로 돌아가려 하면 조금 어리둥절해져요. 우리는 더 알아볼 생각을 전부 접고, 내일 이 일을 길모어 씨의 손에 맡길 때까지 기다려야 할까요?”\n\n“결코 그렇지 않습니다, 홀콤 양. 교실에서 있었던 일은 오히려 제가 이 조사를 끝까지 밀고 나가도록 부추깁니다.”\n\n“어째서 그것이 당신을 부추기죠?”\n\n“당신이 저에게 그 편지를 읽어 보라고 주셨을 때 제가 느꼈던 어떤 의심을 그것이 굳혀 주기 때문입니다.”\n\n“지금 이 순간까지 그 의심을 저에게 숨기신 데에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있으셨겠죠, 하트라이트 씨?”\n\n“저 스스로 그것을 키우기가 두려웠습니다. 저는 그것이 전혀 터무니없다고 생각했고—제 상상 속의 어떤 비뚤어짐이 낳은 결과로 여겨 믿지 않았습니다. 하지만 이제는 더 그럴 수가 없군요. 당신의 질문에 대한 그 아이 자신의 대답뿐만 아니라, 그 이야기를 설명하면서 교사의 입에서 우연히 떨어진 한 마디 표현까지, 그 생각을 다시 제 머릿속으로 밀어 넣었습니다. 앞으로의 사건들이 그 생각을 망상으로 판명할지도 모르지요, 홀콤 양. 하지만 지금 이 순간 제 안에서는, 교회 묘지에서 보였다는 그 귀신과 익명 편지를 쓴 사람이 같은 사람이라는 믿음이 강합니다.”\n\n그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 창백해진 얼굴로 내 얼굴을 간절히 들여다보았다.\n\n“어떤 사람인데요?”\n\n“교사가 자기도 모르게 당신에게 알려 주었습니다. 그가 그 아이가 교회 묘지에서 본 형체를 이야기하면서 그것을 ‘흰옷을 입은 여자’라고 불렀지요.”\n\n“앤 캐서릭은 아니겠죠?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 앤 캐서릭입니다.”\n\n그녀는 내 팔에 손을 끼우고 무겁게 기대었다.\n\n“왜인지는 모르겠어요.” 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “하지만 당신의 그 의심에는 저를 흠칫 놀라게 하고 기운을 앗아 가는 무언가가 있어요. 저는 느껴져요——” 그녀는 말을 멈추고 웃어넘기려 했다. “하트라이트 씨.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “제가 무덤을 보여 드리고, 그러고는 곧장 저택으로 돌아가겠어요. 로라를 너무 오래 혼자 두지 않는 게 좋겠어요. 돌아가서 그 애 곁에 앉아 있는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n그녀가 말할 때 우리는 교회 묘지 가까이에 있었다. 잿빛 돌로 지어진 음산한 건물인 교회는, 사방을 둘러싼 황야 위로 불어오는 매서운 바람을 피할 수 있도록 작은 골짜기 안에 자리 잡고 있었다. 매장지는 교회 옆에서 언덕의 비탈을 따라 조금 위로 뻗어 올라가 있었다. 그것은 거칠고 나지막한 돌담으로 둘러싸여 있었고, 한쪽 끄트머리를 빼면 하늘을 향해 휑하니 열려 있었는데, 그 한쪽 끝에서는 시내가 돌투성이 언덕 비탈을 따라 졸졸 흘러내렸고 키 작은 나무 한 무리가 짧고 메마른 풀 위로 좁은 그림자를 드리우고 있었다. 그 시내와 나무들 바로 너머, 교회 묘지로 여러 지점에서 들어갈 수 있게 놓인 세 개의 돌계단 가운데 하나에서 멀지 않은 곳에, 페얼리 부인의 무덤을 그 주위에 흩어진 더 초라한 기념비들과 구별해 주는 흰 대리석 십자가가 솟아 있었다.\n\n“여기서부터는 함께 갈 필요가 없겠네요.” 홀콤 양이 무덤을 가리키며 말했다. “방금 저에게 말씀하신 그 생각을 뒷받침할 무언가를 찾으시거든 알려 주세요. 저택에서 다시 뵙기로 해요.”\n\n그녀는 나를 두고 떠났다. 나는 곧장 교회 묘지로 내려가, 페얼리 부인의 무덤으로 곧바로 이어지는 돌계단을 넘었다.\n\n무덤 주위의 풀은 너무 짧았고 땅은 너무 단단해서 발자국의 흔적이 남아 있지 않았다. 거기까지에서 실망한 나는, 다음으로 십자가와 그 아래 비문이 새겨진 네모난 대리석 덩이를 주의 깊게 들여다보았다.\n\n십자가의 본래 흰빛은 여기저기 비바람의 얼룩으로 조금 흐려져 있었고, 그 아래 네모난 덩이도 비문이 새겨진 면의 절반 남짓이 같은 상태였다. 그러나 나머지 절반은 얼룩이나 티끌이 어떤 종류도 유난히 없다는 점으로 곧바로 내 눈길을 끌었다. 나는 더 가까이 들여다보았고, 그것이 닦여 있다는 것을 알았다—최근에, 위에서 아래로 쓸어내리는 방향으로 닦여 있었다. 닦인 부분과 닦이지 않은 부분 사이의 경계선은 비문이 대리석의 빈자리를 남겨 놓은 곳마다 뚜렷이 더듬을 수 있었다—인위적인 방법으로 생긴 선답게 또렷하게. 누가 이 대리석을 닦기 시작했고, 누가 그것을 끝내지 않은 채 내버려 두었을까?\n\n나는 그 물음을 어떻게 풀어야 할지 궁금해하며 주위를 둘러보았다. 내가 서 있는 자리에서는 사람이 사는 집의 기미라고는 하나도 보이지 않았다—매장지는 죽은 이들의 쓸쓸한 차지로 남겨져 있었다. 나는 교회로 되돌아가 건물 뒤편에 이를 때까지 그 둘레를 걸었다. 그런 다음 그 너머의 경계 담을 또 다른 돌계단으로 넘어, 버려진 채석장으로 내려가는 오솔길의 초입에 서 있는 나를 발견했다. 채석장 한쪽 벽에 기대어 방 두 칸짜리 작은 오두막이 지어져 있었고, 문밖에서는 한 노파가 빨래를 하고 있었다.\n\n나는 그녀에게 다가가 교회와 매장지에 대해 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 그녀는 기꺼이 말을 받았고, 거의 첫마디부터 자기 남편이 교회 서기와 묘지기 두 가지 일을 맡고 있다고 알려 주었다. 나는 다음으로 페얼리 부인의 기념비를 칭찬하는 말을 몇 마디 했다. 노파는 고개를 저으며, 내가 그것을 가장 좋은 상태로 본 것이 아니라고 말했다. 그것을 돌보는 일은 제 남편의 소임인데, 남편이 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 앓고 기운이 없어 일요일에 제 소임을 다하러 교회까지 기어들어 가기도 힘들 지경이었고, 그래서 기념비가 방치되어 왔다는 것이었다. 남편은 이제 조금 나아지고 있고, 한 주나 열흘쯤 뒤에는 일을 시작해 그것을 닦을 만큼 기운을 차리기를 바라고 있다고 했다.\n\n아주 짙은 컴벌랜드 사투리로 길게 두서없이 늘어놓은 대답에서 뽑아낸 이 이야기는, 내가 무엇보다 알고 싶었던 것을 전부 말해 주었다. 나는 그 가난한 여인에게 얼마간 돈을 쥐여 주고 곧장 리머리지 하우스로 돌아왔다.\n\n기념비를 반쯤 닦아 놓은 것은 분명 낯선 손의 일이었다. 여기까지 알아낸 것을, 땅거미 질 무렵 보였다는 귀신 이야기를 듣고 내가 품었던 의심과 이어 보니, 그날 저녁 몰래 페얼리 부인의 무덤을 지켜보리라는—해 질 녘에 그리로 돌아가 밤이 내릴 때까지 그것이 보이는 자리에서 기다리리라는—내 결심을 굳히는 데 더 필요한 것은 없었다. 기념비를 닦는 일은 끝맺지 못한 채 남겨져 있었고, 그것을 시작한 사람이 마저 끝내러 돌아올지도 몰랐다.\n\n저택으로 돌아온 나는 내가 하려는 일을 홀콤 양에게 알렸다. 내가 뜻을 설명하는 동안 그녀는 놀라고 불안한 기색이었으나, 그 실행에 대해 딱 잘라 반대하지는 않았다. 그녀는 다만 “잘 끝나기를 바라요.”라고만 말했다.\n\n그녀가 다시 나에게서 떠나려는 참에, 나는 그녀를 멈춰 세우고 되도록 담담하게 페얼리 양의 안부를 물었다. 그녀는 기분이 좀 나아졌고, 홀콤 양은 오후 햇살이 남아 있는 동안 그녀가 조금 걷도록 권해 볼 수 있기를 바라고 있었다.\n\n나는 내 방으로 돌아가 그림들을 정리하던 일을 다시 시작했다. 그 일은 해야만 하는 일이었고, 나 자신에게서, 그리고 내 앞에 놓인 가망 없는 미래에서 내 주의를 돌리는 데 도움이 될 무엇에든 마음을 붙들어 두는 일은 갑절로 필요했다. 나는 이따금 일손을 멈추고 창밖을 내다보며, 해가 점점 더 지평선 가까이 가라앉는 하늘을 바라보았다. 그런 때 한번은 내 창 아래 넓은 자갈길 위에 있는 한 사람의 모습을 보았다. 페얼리 양이었다.\n\n아침 이후로 나는 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그때도 그녀와 거의 말을 나누지 못했다. 리머리지에서 나에게 남은 것은 단 하루뿐이었고, 그날이 지나면 내 눈은 다시는 그녀를 보지 못할지도 몰랐다. 이 생각만으로도 나를 창가에 붙들어 두기에 충분했다. 나는 그녀가 올려다보아도 나를 보지 못하도록 차양을 손보아 둘 만큼의 배려는 있었으나, 적어도 내 눈만은 그녀가 걷는 길을 눈길이 닿는 데까지 좇게 하고 싶은 유혹을 뿌리칠 힘은 없었다.\n\n그녀는 갈색 망토를 걸치고, 그 안에는 수수한 검정 비단 드레스를 입고 있었다. 머리에는 우리가 처음 만나던 날 아침에 썼던 것과 똑같은 소박한 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다. 지금은 거기에 베일이 달려 있어 그녀의 얼굴을 나에게서 가렸다. 그녀 곁에는 산책 때마다 따라다니는 사랑스러운 동무인 작은 이탈리아 그레이하운드가 종종걸음을 쳤는데, 여린 살갗에 매서운 공기가 닿지 않도록 진홍빛 나사 덮개를 맵시 있게 두르고 있었다. 그녀는 개를 알아보지 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 고개를 조금 떨구고 두 팔을 망토 안에 포갠 채 곧장 앞으로 걸어갔다. 그날 아침 그녀의 약혼 소식을 들었을 때 내 앞에서 바람에 소용돌이치던 마른 잎들이, 그녀 앞에서도 바람에 소용돌이쳤고, 그녀가 스러져 가는 창백한 햇빛 속을 걸어가는 동안 떠올랐다 내려앉았다 하며 그녀의 발치에 흩어졌다. 개는 오들오들 떨며 눈길과 격려를 바라고 조바심을 내며 그녀의 옷자락에 몸을 비볐다. 그러나 그녀는 끝내 그것을 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 마른 잎들이 오솔길 위에서 제 주위로 소용돌이치는 가운데 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 걸어갔다—아파 오는 내 눈이 더는 그녀를 볼 수 없을 때까지 걸어갔고, 나는 다시 나 자신의 무거운 마음과 함께 혼자 남겨졌다.\n\n다시 한 시간이 지나자 나는 일을 끝냈고, 해 질 녘이 가까웠다. 나는 현관에서 모자와 외투를 챙겨 아무도 마주치지 않고 집을 빠져나왔다.\n\n서쪽 하늘에는 구름이 사납게 일었고, 바다에서 불어오는 바람은 싸늘했다. 바닷가가 멀었는데도 파도 소리는 사이에 놓인 황야를 넘어 밀려와, 내가 교회 묘지에 들어설 때 음산하게 내 귓속을 때렸다. 살아 있는 것이라고는 하나도 보이지 않았다. 자리를 고르고 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위로 솟은 흰 십자가에 눈을 둔 채 기다리며 지켜보는 동안, 그곳은 어느 때보다도 쓸쓸해 보였다.\n\n# XIII\n\n교회 묘지는 사방으로 훤히 트인 곳이었으므로, 나는 내가 자리 잡을 위치를 고르는 데 조심하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n교회의 정문은 매장지에 면한 쪽에 있었고, 그 문은 양옆이 벽으로 막힌 현관 지붕에 가려져 있었다. 몸을 숨긴다는 데 대한 자연스러운 거부감 때문에 잠시 망설이기는 했으나—목적을 이루기 위해서는 그 은신이 없어서는 안 될 일이었으므로—나는 결국 그 현관 안으로 들어가기로 마음을 정했다. 양쪽 옆벽에는 총안(銃眼)처럼 좁은 창이 하나씩 뚫려 있었다. 그중 한 창으로는 페얼리 부인의 무덤이 보였다. 다른 창은 무덤지기의 오두막이 지어져 있는 채석장 쪽을 향해 있었다. 내 앞으로, 현관 입구 정면에는 아무것도 없는 맨 매장지 한 뙈기와 나지막한 돌담 한 줄, 그리고 쓸쓸한 갈색 언덕 한 자락이 있었고, 그 위로는 저녁놀 구름이 억세고 한결같은 바람에 밀려 무겁게 흘러가고 있었다. 살아 있는 것이라고는 보이지도 들리지도 않았다—새 한 마리 곁을 날지 않았고, 무덤지기의 오두막에서 개 짖는 소리 하나 나지 않았다. 둔중한 파도 소리가 멎는 사이사이를 무덤 가까이 선 왜소한 나무들의 을씨년스러운 바스락거림과, 돌바닥 위를 흐르는 개울의 차갑고 희미한 물소리가 메웠다. 을씨년스러운 풍경이요, 을씨년스러운 시각이었다. 교회 현관 아래 은신처에서 저녁의 분(分)들을 하나하나 헤아리는 동안 내 기운은 빠르게 가라앉았다.\n\n아직 땅거미가 지지는 않았고—지는 해의 빛이 여전히 하늘에 머물러 있었으며, 홀로 지키는 시간의 첫 반 시간이 조금 지났을 뿐이었는데—그때 발소리와 목소리가 들려왔다. 발소리는 교회 반대편에서 다가오고 있었고, 목소리는 여자의 것이었다.\n\n“편지 일은 걱정 말아요, 아가씨.” 그 목소리가 말했다. “내가 그 아이한테 아주 안전하게 건네줬고, 그 아이도 아무 말 없이 나한테서 받아 갔어요. 그 애는 제 갈 길로 가고 나는 내 갈 길로 왔지요. 그 뒤로 산 사람 하나 나를 따라오지 않았어요—그건 내가 장담해요.”\n\n이 말은 내 주의를 거의 고통스러울 지경의 기대로 팽팽하게 끌어올렸다. 잠시 침묵이 흘렀으나 발소리는 여전히 다가왔다. 다음 순간 두 사람이, 둘 다 여자였는데, 현관 창으로 내다보이는 내 시야 안을 지나갔다. 그들은 곧장 무덤을 향해 걷고 있었고, 그래서 나에게는 등을 돌리고 있었다.\n\n여자들 중 하나는 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르고 있었다. 다른 하나는 짙은 청색의 긴 여행용 망토를 걸치고 두건을 머리 위로 끌어 덮고 있었다. 망토 아래로 가운이 몇 인치쯤 드러나 보였다. 그 빛깔을 알아보는 순간 내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다—흰색이었다.\n\n교회와 무덤 사이 절반쯤 나아갔을 때 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 망토를 걸친 여자가 동행 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. 그러나 보닛을 썼더라면 이제는 내가 볼 수 있었을 그 옆얼굴이, 두툼하게 앞으로 튀어나온 두건 가장자리에 가려져 있었다.\n\n“그 편안하고 따뜻한 망토, 꼭 벗지 말고 있어요.” 내가 이미 들었던 바로 그 목소리가—숄을 두른 여자의 목소리가 말했다. “어제 온통 흰옷을 입은 게 너무 눈에 띈다고 한 토드 부인 말이 맞아요. 나는 아가씨가 여기 있는 동안 좀 걷고 있을게요. 교회 묘지란 데는 아가씨한테는 어떨지 몰라도 나한테는 도무지 맞지 않아서요. 내가 돌아오기 전에 하고 싶은 일을 끝내요. 그리고 밤이 되기 전에 꼭 집으로 돌아가도록 합시다.”\n\n그 말과 함께 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 왔던 길을 되짚어 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 향한 채 다가왔다. 나이 지긋한 여자의 얼굴이었다. 볕에 그을고 거칠지만 건강해 보였으며, 그 표정에는 부정하거나 수상쩍은 구석이 조금도 없었다. 교회 가까이에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 숄을 몸에 더 바싹 여몄다.\n\n“별나지.” 그녀가 혼잣말을 했다. “내가 저 애를 알게 된 이후로 늘 별났어, 그 변덕이며 그 하는 짓이며. 그래도 해롭지는 않아—가엾은 것, 어린애만큼이나 해롭지가 않지.”\n\n그녀는 한숨을 쉬더니—불안한 눈으로 매장지를 둘러보고—이 을씨년스러운 광경이 조금도 마음에 들지 않는다는 듯 고개를 내젓고는, 교회 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다.\n\n나는 잠시, 그녀를 따라가 말을 걸어야 할지 말지 망설였다. 그녀의 동행과 얼굴을 맞대고 싶은 간절한 조바심이 아니라는 쪽으로 마음을 정하게 해 주었다. 숄을 두른 여자는 그녀가 돌아올 때까지 교회 묘지 근처에서 기다리기만 하면 반드시 만날 수 있었다—하기야 그녀가 내가 찾고 있는 정보를 줄 수 있을지는 몹시 의심스러웠지만. 편지를 전달한 사람은 별로 중요하지 않았다. 그것을 쓴 사람이야말로 유일한 관심의 중심이자 유일한 정보의 원천이었고, 그 사람이 지금 이 교회 묘지 안에서 내 앞에 있다고 나는 확신하게 되었다.\n\n이런 생각들이 마음속을 스쳐 가는 동안, 나는 망토를 걸친 여자가 무덤 가까이 다가가 잠시 그것을 바라보며 서 있는 것을 보았다. 이윽고 그녀는 사방을 빙 둘러보더니, 망토 밑에서 흰 아마포 천인지 손수건인지를 꺼내 개울 쪽으로 비켜 갔다. 그 작은 개울은 담장 밑에 뚫린 자그마한 아치를 지나 교회 묘지로 흘러들었다가, 수십 야드를 굽이돌아 흐른 뒤 비슷한 구멍을 통해 다시 흘러 나갔다. 그녀는 천을 물에 적셔 무덤으로 돌아왔다. 나는 그녀가 흰 십자가에 입을 맞추고, 이어 비문 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 젖은 천으로 그것을 닦기 시작하는 것을 보았다.\n\n어떻게 하면 그녀를 놀라게 할 위험을 가장 줄이면서 모습을 드러낼 수 있을까 궁리한 끝에, 나는 앞의 담을 넘어 바깥으로 돌아 나갔다가 무덤 가까이 있는 층계 디딤대를 통해 다시 교회 묘지로 들어가기로 했다. 그래야 내가 다가가는 모습을 그녀가 볼 수 있을 터였다. 그녀는 자기 일에 어찌나 몰두해 있었던지 내가 디딤대를 넘어설 때까지도 다가오는 소리를 듣지 못했다. 그러다 고개를 들더니, 나직한 비명과 함께 벌떡 일어나, 말도 못 하고 꼼짝도 못 하는 공포에 사로잡힌 채 나를 마주하고 섰다.\n\n“놀라지 마십시오.” 내가 말했다. “설마 나를 기억하지 못하는 건 아니겠지요?”\n\n나는 말하면서 멈춰 섰다가—그러고는 몇 걸음 조용히 나아갔다가—다시 멈춰 섰고—그렇게 조금씩 조금씩 다가가 마침내 그녀 가까이에 이르렀다. 내 마음에 아직 어떤 의심이 남아 있었다 해도 이제는 말끔히 가셨을 것이다. 저기, 겁에 질린 채 스스로를 증언하며—페얼리 부인의 무덤 너머로 나를 마주한 그 얼굴은, 밤중의 큰길에서 처음으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았던 바로 그 얼굴이었다.\n\n“나를 기억하지요?” 내가 말했다. “우리는 아주 늦은 시각에 만났고, 나는 당신이 런던으로 가는 길을 찾도록 도와주었습니다. 설마 그것을 잊지는 않았겠지요?”\n\n그녀의 이목구비가 풀리며, 그녀는 안도의 깊은 숨을 내쉬었다. 공포가 그녀의 얼굴에 새겨 놓았던 죽음 같은 고요 아래에서, 알아봄이라는 새로운 생기가 천천히 움직이는 것이 보였다.\n\n“지금 당장은 나에게 말을 하려 애쓰지 마십시오.” 내가 말을 이었다. “정신을 추스를 시간을 가지세요—내가 친구라는 것을 온전히 확신할 시간을 가지세요.”\n\n“저에게 참 친절하시군요.” 그녀가 중얼거렸다. “그때 그러셨던 것처럼 지금도 친절하세요.”\n\n그녀가 말을 멈추었고, 나도 나대로 침묵을 지켰다. 나는 그녀에게만 마음을 가라앉힐 시간을 준 것이 아니라, 나 자신을 위한 시간도 벌고 있었다. 창백하고 스산한 저녁빛 아래, 그 여자와 나는 다시 마주하고 있었다. 우리 사이에는 무덤이 있었고, 우리 둘레에는 죽은 이들이 있었으며, 쓸쓸한 언덕들이 사방에서 우리를 에워싸고 있었다. 그 을씨년스러운 골짜기의 저녁 정적 속에서 우리가 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있는 시간과 장소와 정황—우리 사이에 오갈 다음 몇 마디 우연한 말에 매달려 있을지도 모르는 한평생의 이해관계—내가 달리 알지 못하는 한, 로라 페얼리의 온 미래가 제 어머니의 무덤 곁에서 떨고 서 있는 이 버림받은 존재의 신뢰를 내가 얻느냐 잃느냐에 따라 좋게든 나쁘게든 결정될지도 모른다는 생각—이 모든 것이, 내가 앞으로 나아갈 한 치 한 치가 온전히 달려 있는 그 침착함과 자제력을 뒤흔들려고 위협했다. 그것을 느끼면서 나는 내 모든 역량을 그러모으려 애썼다. 생각할 수 있는 그 몇 순간을 최대한 살리려고 온 힘을 다했다.\n\n“이제 좀 진정되었습니까?” 다시 말을 걸어도 되겠다 싶었을 때 내가 말했다. “겁내지 않고, 또 내가 친구라는 것도 잊지 않고, 나와 이야기할 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“여기 어떻게 오셨어요?” 그녀는 내가 방금 한 말에는 아랑곳하지 않고 물었다.\n\n“지난번 만났을 때 내가 컴벌랜드로 갈 참이라고 말했던 것을 기억하지 못합니까? 그 뒤로 줄곧 컴벌랜드에 있었습니다—그동안 내내 리머리지 하우스에 머물러 왔지요.”\n\n“리머리지 하우스에요!” 그 말을 되뇌는 그녀의 창백한 얼굴이 환해졌고, 헤매던 눈이 갑작스러운 관심을 담고 나에게 못 박혔다. “아, 얼마나 행복하셨을까요!” 그녀는 이전의 불신이라고는 그림자도 남지 않은 표정으로 나를 열심히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n나는 새로이 일어난 그녀의 신뢰를 이용해, 여태껏 조심하느라 삼가 왔던 주의와 호기심을 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈다. 달빛 아래 테라스에서 그토록 불길하게 그녀를 내 기억에 되살려 놓았던 그 또 다른 사랑스러운 얼굴로 마음을 가득 채운 채 나는 그녀를 바라보았다. 나는 페얼리 양에게서 앤 캐서릭의 닮은꼴을 보았었다. 이제는 앤 캐서릭에게서 페얼리 양의 닮은꼴을 보았다—두 사람 사이의 닮은 점만이 아니라 닮지 않은 점까지 함께 눈앞에 놓였기에 그만큼 더 또렷하게 보였다. 얼굴의 전체적인 윤곽과 이목구비의 전체적인 균형에서—머리카락의 빛깔과 입술 언저리의 작고 예민한 흔들림에서—키와 몸집에서, 그리고 머리와 몸을 가누는 자세에서, 그 닮음은 내가 지금껏 느껴 본 어느 때보다도 더 놀랍게 보였다. 그러나 닮음은 거기서 끝나고, 세부에서의 다름이 시작되었다. 페얼리 양의 살빛이 지닌 섬세한 아름다움, 눈의 투명한 맑음, 피부의 매끄러운 순결함, 입술에 어린 부드러운 혈색—그 모든 것이 지금 내 쪽으로 돌려진 이 지치고 여윈 얼굴에는 없었다. 그런 것을 생각한다는 것만으로도 나 자신이 미웠지만, 그럼에도 눈앞의 여자를 바라보는 동안 한 가지 생각이 기어이 내 마음속으로 밀고 들어왔다. 세부에서는 그토록 불완전하다고 내가 지금 본 그 닮음을 완성하는 데 모자란 것은, 앞날에 닥칠 단 하나의 슬픈 변화뿐이라는 생각이었다. 언젠가 슬픔과 고통이 페얼리 양의 젊음과 아름다움 위에 그 더럽히는 자국을 새긴다면, 그때에야, 오직 그때에야 앤 캐서릭과 그녀는 우연한 닮음이 낳은 쌍둥이 자매가, 서로를 비추는 살아 있는 거울이 될 터였다.\n\n그 생각에 나는 몸서리쳤다. 그런 생각이 마음속을 스쳐 갔다는 사실 자체가 품고 있는 듯한, 미래에 대한 눈멀고 이치 없는 불신에는 어딘가 끔찍한 데가 있었다. 앤 캐서릭의 손이 내 어깨에 얹히는 것을 느끼고 정신이 들었을 때, 그것은 반가운 방해였다. 그 손길은 우리가 처음 만난 밤에 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 굳어 버리게 했던 그 손길만큼이나 은밀하고 갑작스러웠다.\n\n“저를 보고 계시는군요. 그리고 무언가를 생각하고 계세요.” 그녀가 그 이상하리만치 숨 가쁘고 빠른 말투로 말했다. “무슨 생각이에요?”\n\n“별것 아닙니다.” 내가 대답했다. “그저 당신이 어떻게 여기까지 오게 되었는지 궁금했을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“저에게 아주 잘해 주는 친구와 함께 왔어요. 여기 온 지는 이틀밖에 안 됐고요.”\n\n“그리고 어제 이곳을 찾아냈던 거군요?”\n\n“그걸 어떻게 아세요?”\n\n“그저 짐작해 보았을 뿐입니다.”\n\n그녀는 나에게서 몸을 돌려 다시 한 번 비문 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n“여기가 아니면 제가 어디로 가겠어요?” 그녀가 말했다. “저에게 어머니보다 더 나은 분이었던 그 친구가, 리머리지에서 제가 찾아뵐 수 있는 유일한 친구인걸요. 아, 그분 무덤에 얼룩이 진 것을 보면 가슴이 아파요! 그분을 생각하면 눈처럼 희게 지켜져야 마땅한데. 어제 이걸 닦기 시작하고 싶은 마음이 들었고, 오늘도 이어서 하러 오지 않을 수가 없었어요. 그게 잘못된 일인가요? 아니었으면 좋겠어요. 페얼리 부인을 위해 하는 일이라면 무엇도 잘못일 리 없잖아요?”\n\n은인의 친절에 대한 오래된 감사의 마음이 여전히 이 가련한 존재의 마음을 지배하는 생각임이 분명했다—더 젊고 행복하던 시절의 그 첫 인상 이후로 다른 어떤 지속적인 인상에도 너무나 분명히 열린 적이 없었던 그 좁은 마음을 말이다. 나는 그녀의 신뢰를 얻을 최선의 길이, 그녀가 이 매장지에 하러 온 그 천진한 일을 계속하도록 북돋아 주는 데 있음을 알아차렸다. 그래도 된다고 내가 말하자 그녀는 곧바로 일을 다시 시작했고, 마치 감각이 있는 것이라도 되는 양 단단한 대리석을 부드럽게 어루만졌으며, 마치 소녀 시절의 잃어버린 날들이 되돌아와 페얼리 부인의 무릎 앞에서 다시 한 번 끈기 있게 배운 것을 외우기라도 하듯, 비문의 글귀를 몇 번이고 되풀이해 혼잣말로 속삭였다.\n\n“아주 이상하게 여기실까요.” 나는 앞으로 던질 질문들을 위해 되도록 조심스럽게 길을 닦으며 말했다. “여기서 당신을 만난 것이 나에게는 놀라움일 뿐 아니라 안심이기도 하다고 고백한다면 말입니다. 그날 밤 당신이 나를 두고 마차에서 내린 뒤로 나는 당신 일이 몹시 마음에 걸렸습니다.”\n\n그녀는 재빨리, 그리고 의심스러운 눈길로 고개를 들었다.\n\n“마음에 걸렸다고요.” 그녀가 되뇌었다. “왜요?”\n\n“그날 밤 우리가 헤어진 뒤에 이상한 일이 있었습니다. 두 남자가 이륜마차를 타고 나를 앞질러 왔지요. 그들은 내가 서 있는 곳을 보지는 못했지만 내 근처에 멈춰 서서, 길 건너편에 있던 순경에게 말을 걸었습니다.”\n\n그녀는 즉시 하던 일을 멈추었다. 비문을 닦던 젖은 천을 쥔 손이 옆으로 툭 떨어졌다. 다른 손은 무덤 머리맡의 대리석 십자가를 붙들었다. 그녀의 얼굴이 천천히 나를 향해 돌려졌고, 거기에는 다시 한 번 텅 빈 공포의 표정이 굳어 있었다. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 계속하기로 했다—이제 와서 물러서기에는 너무 늦었다.\n\n“그 두 남자는 순경에게,” 내가 말했다. “당신을 보았느냐고 물었습니다. 순경은 보지 못했다고 했지요. 그러자 그중 한 남자가 다시 입을 열어, 당신이 자기 정신병원에서 도망쳤다고 말했습니다.”\n\n그녀는 내 마지막 말이 추격자들을 자기 뒤로 풀어놓기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n“멈춰요! 그리고 끝까지 들으세요.” 내가 외쳤다. “멈춰요! 그러면 내가 어떻게 당신 편이 되어 주었는지 알게 될 겁니다. 나에게서 한마디만 나왔어도 그 남자들에게 당신이 어느 쪽으로 갔는지 알려 주었을 텐데—나는 그 한마디를 끝내 하지 않았습니다. 나는 당신의 도피를 도왔습니다—그것을 안전하고 확실하게 만들어 주었어요. 생각해 보세요, 생각하려고 애써 보세요. 내가 하는 말을 이해하려고 애써 보세요.”\n\n내 태도가 내 말보다 그녀에게 더 영향을 미치는 것 같았다. 그녀는 이 새로운 생각을 붙잡으려고 애를 썼다. 그녀의 두 손은 젖은 천을 이 손에서 저 손으로 머뭇머뭇 옮겼는데, 내가 그녀를 처음 본 밤에 작은 여행 가방을 옮기던 것과 똑같았다. 천천히 내 말의 뜻이 그녀 마음의 혼란과 동요를 뚫고 길을 내는 듯했다. 천천히 그녀의 이목구비가 풀렸고, 그녀의 눈은 빠르게 사라져 가는 두려움만큼 호기심을 얻어 가는 표정으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“당신은 제가 그 정신병원으로 돌아가야 한다고 생각하지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“물론 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다. 당신이 거기서 빠져나온 것이 기쁘고—내가 당신을 도왔다는 것이 기쁩니다.”\n\n“네, 네, 당신은 정말로 저를 도와주셨어요. 가장 어려운 대목에서 도와주셨죠.” 그녀가 조금 멍하니 말을 이었다. “도망쳐 나오는 건 쉬웠어요. 안 그랬으면 빠져나오지 못했을 거예요. 그 사람들은 다른 사람들을 의심하듯 저를 의심하지는 않았거든요. 저는 아주 조용하고, 아주 순종적이고, 아주 잘 겁을 먹었으니까요. 런던을 찾아가는 게 어려운 대목이었는데, 거기서 당신이 도와주셨어요. 제가 그때 고맙다고 인사드렸던가요? 지금 아주 고맙게 인사드려요.”\n\n“당신이 나를 만난 곳에서 그 정신병원은 멀었습니까? 자, 나를 친구로 믿는다는 것을 보여 주고, 그곳이 어디였는지 말해 주세요.”\n\n그녀는 그 장소를 말했다—그 위치로 미루어 알 수 있듯 사설 정신병원이었고, 내가 그녀를 보았던 지점에서 그리 멀지 않은 사설 정신병원이었다—그러고는 내가 그 대답을 어디에 쓸지 뻔히 의심하는 낯빛으로, 조금 전의 물음을 근심스레 되풀이했다. “저를 다시 데려가야 한다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠, 그렇죠?”\n\n“다시 말하지만, 나는 당신이 빠져나온 것이 기쁘고—나와 헤어진 뒤에 잘 지냈다는 것이 기쁩니다.” 내가 대답했다. “런던에 찾아갈 친구가 있다고 했었지요. 그 친구를 찾았습니까?”\n\n“네. 아주 늦은 시각이었지만 그 집에 바느질을 하느라 깨어 있던 아가씨가 있어서, 그 사람이 클레멘츠 부인을 깨우는 걸 도와주었어요. 클레멘츠 부인이 제 친구예요. 착하고 다정한 분이지만 페얼리 부인 같지는 않아요. 아, 아니에요, 페얼리 부인 같은 분은 아무도 없어요!”\n\n“클레멘츠 부인은 오래된 친구입니까? 오래전부터 알고 지냈나요?”\n\n“네, 예전에 고향인 햄프셔에서 우리 이웃이었어요. 저를 예뻐하셨고, 제가 어린 계집아이였을 때 저를 돌봐 주셨죠. 여러 해 전, 우리 곁을 떠나실 때 제 기도서에 런던 어디에서 살게 될지를 적어 주시면서 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. ‘앤, 언제든 어려운 일이 생기거든 나에게 오너라. 나에게는 안 된다고 말릴 남편도 살아 있지 않고 돌봐야 할 자식도 없으니, 내가 너를 돌봐 주마.’ 다정한 말이지요, 그렇지 않아요? 다정한 말이라서 제가 기억하는 것 같아요. 그것 말고는 기억하는 게 얼마 없어요—얼마 없어요, 정말 얼마 없어요!”\n\n“당신을 돌봐 줄 아버지나 어머니는 없었습니까?”\n\n“아버지요?—저는 아버지를 본 적이 없어요—어머니가 아버지 얘기를 하는 것도 들어 본 적이 없고요. 아버지요? 아, 저런! 돌아가셨겠지요.”\n\n“그럼 어머니는요?”\n\n“어머니하고는 사이가 좋지 않아요. 우리는 서로에게 골칫거리이고 두려움이에요.”\n\n서로에게 골칫거리이고 두려움이라니! 그 말에 처음으로, 그녀를 가두어 놓은 사람이 그녀의 어머니일지도 모른다는 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다.\n\n“어머니 얘기는 묻지 마세요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “저는 클레멘츠 부인 얘기가 더 좋아요. 클레멘츠 부인은 당신과 같아요. 제가 그 정신병원으로 돌아가야 한다고 생각하지 않으시고, 제가 거기서 빠져나온 걸 당신처럼 기뻐하세요. 그분은 제 불행을 두고 우셨어요. 그리고 그건 모두에게 비밀로 해야 한다고 하셨고요.”\n\n그녀의 “불행”. 그녀는 그 말을 어떤 뜻으로 쓰고 있는 것일까? 익명의 편지를 쓴 동기를 설명해 줄 만한 뜻으로? 자기를 파멸시킨 남자의 결혼에 익명의 훼방을 놓게 만든, 너무나 흔하고 너무나 상투적인 그 동기임을 드러내는 뜻으로? 나는 어느 쪽에서든 더 많은 말이 오가기 전에 이 의문을 밝혀 보기로 결심했다.\n\n“무슨 불행 말입니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“제가 갇혔던 불행이지요.” 그녀는 내 질문에 놀란 기색이 역력한 채로 대답했다. “그 말고 무슨 불행이 있을 수 있겠어요?”\n\n나는 되도록 섬세하고 참을성 있게 밀고 나가기로 마음먹었다. 이제부터 앞질러 얻어 내는 조사의 걸음 하나하나를 절대적으로 확신하는 것이 대단히 중요했다.\n\n“또 다른 불행이 있습니다.” 내가 말했다. “여자가 겪을 수 있는, 그리고 그로 인해 한평생의 슬픔과 수치를 당할 수도 있는 불행 말입니다.”\n\n“그게 뭔데요?” 그녀가 다급하게 물었다.\n\n“자기 자신의 정절과, 자기가 사랑하는 남자의 신의와 명예를 너무 순진하게 믿은 불행이지요.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n그녀는 아이 같은 천진한 당혹감으로 나를 올려다보았다. 어떤 혼란도, 낯빛의 변화도—표면으로 떠오르려 애쓰는 은밀한 수치의 자각 같은 것은 아주 희미한 자취조차—그녀의 얼굴에는 나타나지 않았다. 다른 온갖 감정은 그토록 투명하게 드러내던 그 얼굴에 말이다. 지금 그녀의 표정과 태도가 나를 확신시켜 준 것만큼 나를 확신시켜 줄 수 있는 말은 세상에 없었을 것이다. 그녀가 그 편지를 써서 페얼리 양에게 보낸 동기라고 내가 지목했던 것은 명백하고 분명하게 틀렸다는 확신 말이다. 적어도 그 의문은 이제 풀렸다. 그러나 그것이 걷히자마자 새로운 불확실의 전망이 열렸다. 확실한 증언으로 내가 알고 있듯, 그 편지는 이름을 대지는 않았어도 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 가리키고 있었다. 그녀가 자신이 쓴 그런 표현으로 그를 페얼리 양에게 은밀히 고발하기까지에는, 깊은 상처의 감정에서 비롯한 어떤 강한 동기가 있었을 것이다. 그리고 그 동기가 정절과 명예의 상실에서 온 것이 아님은 의문의 여지가 없었다. 그가 그녀에게 어떤 몹쓸 짓을 저질렀든 그런 종류의 것은 아니었다. 그렇다면 어떤 종류의 것일 수 있을까?\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.” 그녀는 내가 마지막으로 한 말의 뜻을 알아내려고 무던히 애를 쓰다가, 그것도 헛되이 애쓴 끝에 말했다.\n\n“괜찮습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “아까 하던 이야기를 계속하지요. 런던에서 클레멘츠 부인과 얼마나 오래 지냈는지, 그리고 어떻게 여기까지 오게 되었는지 말해 주세요.”\n\n“얼마나 오래요?” 그녀가 되뇌었다. “이틀 전에 우리 둘이 이곳으로 올 때까지 클레멘츠 부인과 함께 지냈어요.”\n\n“그럼 마을에서 지내고 있군요?” 내가 말했다. “여기 온 지 이틀밖에 안 됐다고는 해도, 내가 당신 소식을 못 들었다니 이상하군요.”\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요, 마을은 아니에요. 3마일 떨어진 농장이에요. 그 농장을 아세요? 토드 코너라고 하던데요.”\n\n나는 그곳을 아주 잘 기억하고 있었다—마차로 나갈 때 자주 그 앞을 지나쳤다. 그 근방에서 가장 오래된 농장 가운데 하나로, 내륙 쪽 두 언덕이 만나는 자리의 외지고 아늑한 곳에 있었다.\n\n“토드 코너에 있는 분들이 클레멘츠 부인의 친척이에요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “그분들이 자주 와서 보라고 청했다지 뭐예요. 부인은 가겠다고, 조용하고 공기 좋은 데서 지내라고 저도 데려가겠다고 하셨고요. 참 다정하지요, 그렇지 않아요? 저는 조용하고, 안전하고, 남의 눈에 띄지 않을 수만 있다면 어디든 갔을 거예요. 그런데 토드 코너가 리머리지 근처라는 말을 들었을 때는—아! 어찌나 기뻤는지 학교와 마을과 리머리지 하우스를 다시 보러 맨발로라도 그 먼 길을 걸어갔을 거예요. 토드 코너 사람들은 아주 좋은 분들이에요. 거기서 오래 지낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 그분들에 대해서, 그리고 클레멘츠 부인에 대해서 딱 한 가지 마음에 들지 않는 게 있는데——”\n\n“그게 뭡니까?”\n\n“온통 흰옷만 입는다고 저를 놀리시는 거예요—너무 유별나 보인다고들 하시죠. 그분들이 뭘 아신다고요? 페얼리 부인이 제일 잘 아셨어요. 페얼리 부인이라면 절대로 저에게 이 보기 싫은 파란 망토를 입히지 않으셨을 거예요! 아! 그분은 살아 계실 때 흰색을 좋아하셨고, 여기 그분 무덤에도 흰 돌이 있어요—그리고 저는 그분을 위해 그것을 더 희게 만들고 있고요. 그분도 자주 흰옷을 입으셨고, 어린 따님에게도 늘 흰옷을 입히셨어요. 페얼리 양은 잘 지내시나요, 행복하신가요? 그분도 여자아이였을 때처럼 지금도 흰옷을 입으시나요?”\n\n페얼리 양에 대해 물을 때 그녀의 목소리는 잦아들었고, 그녀는 고개를 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 돌렸다. 나는 달라진 그녀의 태도에서, 익명의 편지를 보내며 저지른 위험을 불안하게 의식하고 있다는 낌새를 읽었다고 생각했고, 곧바로 그녀가 놀란 나머지 그것을 실토하게끔 대답을 짜기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“페얼리 양은 오늘 아침 그리 건강하지도, 그리 행복하지도 않았습니다.” 내가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 몇 마디를 중얼거렸으나, 어찌나 뒤죽박죽으로, 또 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 말했던지 나는 그 뜻을 짐작조차 할 수 없었다.\n\n“페얼리 양이 오늘 아침 왜 건강하지도 행복하지도 않았는지 나에게 물었습니까?” 내가 말을 이었다.\n\n“아니요.” 그녀가 재빨리, 그리고 다급하게 말했다—“아, 아니에요, 그런 건 묻지 않았어요.”\n\n“묻지 않아도 내가 말해 주지요.” 내가 계속했다. “페얼리 양은 당신의 편지를 받았습니다.”\n\n그녀는 조금 전부터 무릎을 꿇은 채, 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 비문 둘레에 마지막으로 남은 비바람 얼룩을 조심스레 지우고 있었다. 내가 방금 그녀에게 건넨 말의 첫 문장에 그녀는 하던 일을 멈추고, 무릎을 꿇은 채로 천천히 몸을 돌려 나를 마주 보았다. 두 번째 문장은 말 그대로 그녀를 돌처럼 굳혀 놓았다. 쥐고 있던 천이 손에서 떨어졌고—입술이 벌어졌으며—본디 그 얼굴에 있던 얼마 안 되는 핏기마저 한순간에 가셔 버렸다.\n\n“그걸 어떻게 아세요?” 그녀가 힘없이 말했다. “누가 그걸 당신에게 보여 줬나요?” 피가 그녀의 얼굴로 되몰렸다—자기 입에서 나온 말이 스스로를 드러내 버렸다는 자각이 마음에 밀어닥치면서, 걷잡을 수 없이 되몰렸다. 그녀는 절망하여 두 손을 마주 쳤다. “저는 그런 걸 쓴 적 없어요.” 그녀가 겁에 질려 헐떡였다. “저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요!”\n\n“아니요.” 내가 말했다. “당신이 그것을 썼고, 당신은 그것에 대해 알고 있습니다. 그런 편지를 보낸 것은 잘못이었고, 페얼리 양을 겁먹게 한 것도 잘못이었습니다. 그분이 들어야 마땅하고 또 반드시 들어야 할 말이 있었다면, 당신이 직접 리머리지 하우스로 갔어야 했습니다—당신 입으로 그 아가씨에게 말했어야 했습니다.”\n\n그녀는 무덤의 평평한 돌 위로 몸을 웅크려 얼굴을 거기에 파묻은 채 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n“당신이 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다면, 페얼리 양은 그분의 어머니가 그러셨던 것처럼 당신에게 착하고 다정하게 대해 줄 겁니다.” 내가 말을 이었다. “페얼리 양은 당신의 비밀을 지켜 줄 것이고, 당신이 어떤 해도 입지 않게 해 줄 겁니다. 내일 그 농장에서 그분을 만나겠습니까? 리머리지 하우스의 정원에서 그분을 만나겠습니까?”\n\n“아, 제가 죽어서 당신과 함께 감추어져 쉴 수만 있다면!” 그녀의 입술이 묘비에 바싹 붙어 그 말을 중얼거렸다. 아래에 누운 죽은 이를 향한 애틋한 사랑의 어조로 중얼거렸다. “제가 당신을 위해 당신의 아이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 아시잖아요! 아, 페얼리 부인! 페얼리 부인! 그분을 어떻게 구해야 할지 저에게 말해 주세요. 다시 한 번 저의 사랑하는 분, 저의 어머니가 되어 주셔서, 무엇이 가장 좋은 길인지 저에게 말해 주세요.”\n\n나는 그녀의 입술이 돌에 입 맞추는 소리를 들었다—그녀의 두 손이 격정에 차서 돌을 두드리는 것을 보았다. 그 소리와 그 광경이 나를 깊이 흔들었다. 나는 몸을 굽혀 그 가엾고 힘없는 두 손을 내 손에 부드럽게 감싸 쥐고 그녀를 달래려 애썼다.\n\n소용이 없었다. 그녀는 내 손에서 자기 손을 낚아채고는 얼굴을 돌에서 조금도 떼지 않았다. 무슨 수를 써서라도, 어떤 방법으로든 그녀를 진정시켜야 한다는 절박함을 깨닫고, 나는 나와 관련해서, 또 나의 평가와 관련해서 그녀가 유일하게 마음을 쓰는 듯이 보이는 그 불안에 호소했다—자기 행동의 주인이 될 자격이 있음을 나에게 납득시키고 싶어 하는 불안 말이다.\n\n“자, 자.” 내가 부드럽게 말했다. “마음을 가라앉히려 애써 보세요. 그러지 않으면 당신에 대한 내 생각이 바뀌게 될 겁니다. 당신을 그 정신병원에 넣은 사람에게 어떤 그럴 만한 사정이 있었을지도 모른다고 내가 생각하게 만들지 마세요——”\n\n다음 말은 내 입술에서 스러졌다. 그녀를 정신병원에 넣은 사람을 무심코 입에 올리는 위험을 무릅쓴 그 순간, 그녀는 무릎을 세우며 벌떡 일어났다. 참으로 놀랍고도 섬뜩한 변화가 그녀에게 일어났다. 평소에는 그 신경질적인 예민함과 연약함과 불안정함으로 그토록 애처롭게 보이던 그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 미친 듯이 강렬한 증오와 공포의 표정으로 어두워졌고, 그 표정이 이목구비 하나하나에 사납고 부자연스러운 힘을 불어넣었다. 그녀의 두 눈은 어스름한 저녁빛 속에서 야수의 눈처럼 크게 벌어졌다. 그녀는 옆에 떨어져 있던 천을 마치 죽여 버릴 수 있는 산 짐승이기라도 한 듯 움켜쥐고는, 두 손으로 어찌나 경련하듯 힘껏 비틀어 짰던지 그 안에 남아 있던 몇 방울 물기가 그녀 아래의 돌 위로 뚝뚝 흘러내렸다.\n\n“다른 이야기를 하세요.” 그녀가 이 사이로 속삭이듯 말했다. “그 이야기를 하시면 저는 제정신을 잃고 말 거예요.”\n\n채 일 분도 되지 않은 조금 전까지 그녀의 마음을 채우고 있던 온화한 생각의 자취는 이제 죄다 쓸려 나간 듯했다. 페얼리 부인의 친절이 남긴 인상이, 내가 짐작했던 것처럼 그녀의 기억에 남은 유일하게 강한 인상은 아님이 분명했다. 리머리지에서 보낸 학창 시절의 고마운 기억과 나란히, 정신병원 감금으로 그녀가 당한 부당함에 대한 원한 어린 기억이 자리 잡고 있었다. 그 부당한 짓을 저지른 사람은 누구인가? 정말로 그녀의 어머니일 수 있을까?\n\n그 마지막 지점까지 캐묻기를 포기하기란 힘든 일이었으나, 나는 그것을 계속하겠다는 생각을 모두 버리도록 나 자신을 다잡았다. 지금 내 눈앞에 있는 그녀의 모습을 보면서, 그녀의 평정을 되돌려 놓아야 한다는 필요와 인간다움 말고 다른 것을 생각한다면 그것은 잔인한 일이었을 것이다.\n\n“당신을 괴롭히는 이야기는 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다.” 내가 달래듯 말했다.\n\n“뭔가 원하시는 게 있잖아요.” 그녀가 날카롭게, 의심스럽게 대꾸했다. “저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요. 저에게 말하세요—무엇을 원하시는지 말해 주세요.”\n\n“나는 그저 당신이 마음을 가라앉히기를, 그리고 좀 더 진정되거든 내가 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 보기를 바랄 뿐입니다.”\n\n“말이라고요?” 그녀는 잠시 멈추고—손에 쥔 천을 앞뒤로 비틀며 혼잣말로 속삭였다. “저이가 무슨 말을 했더라?” 그녀는 다시 나를 향해 몸을 돌리며 조바심 어린 몸짓으로 고개를 저었다. “왜 저를 도와주지 않으세요?” 그녀가 대뜸 성난 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“네, 네.” 내가 말했다. “도와드리지요. 그러면 곧 기억이 날 겁니다. 내가 부탁하는 것은, 내일 페얼리 양을 만나 그 편지에 대한 진실을 그분께 말씀드리라는 것입니다.”\n\n“아! 페얼리 양—페얼리—페얼리——”\n\n사랑스럽고 익숙한 그 이름을 입에 올리는 것만으로도 그녀는 진정되는 듯했다. 얼굴이 부드러워지며 다시 본래의 그 얼굴로 돌아갔다.\n\n“페얼리 양을 두려워할 필요는 없습니다.” 내가 말을 이었다. “그 편지 때문에 곤경에 빠질까 봐 두려워할 필요도 없고요. 그분은 이미 그 일에 대해 아주 많이 알고 계셔서, 당신이 전부 털어놓는 데 아무 어려움도 없을 겁니다. 감출 것이 거의 남지 않은 마당에 감출 필요도 거의 없지요. 당신은 편지에 아무 이름도 적지 않았지만, 페얼리 양은 당신이 쓴 그 사람이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이라는 것을 알고 있습니다——”\n\n내가 그 이름을 입에 올리는 순간 그녀는 벌떡 일어섰고, 교회 묘지에 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 비명이 그녀에게서 터져 나와, 그 무서움에 내 심장이 안에서 펄쩍 뛰었다. 방금 그녀의 얼굴에서 물러갔던 그 어둡고 일그러진 표정이 두 배, 세 배 짙어진 채로 다시 얼굴에 내려앉았다. 그 이름에 터진 비명과, 곧바로 뒤따른 그 되풀이된 증오와 공포의 표정이 모든 것을 말해 주었다. 이제는 마지막 의심조차 남지 않았다. 그녀의 어머니는 그녀를 정신병원에 가둔 죄가 없었다. 한 남자가 그녀를 가둔 것이었다—그리고 그 남자는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이었다.\n\n그 비명은 내 귀에만 닿은 것이 아니었다. 한쪽에서는 무덤지기 오두막의 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸고, 다른 쪽에서는 그녀의 동행인 숄을 두른 여자, 그녀가 클레멘츠 부인이라고 말했던 그 여자의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n“가요! 지금 가요!” 왜소한 나무 덤불 뒤에서 그 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n다음 순간 클레멘츠 부인이 허둥지둥 시야에 들어왔다.\n\n“당신은 누구요?” 그녀가 층계 디딤대에 발을 올려놓으며 단호하게 나를 마주하고 소리쳤다. “어떻게 감히 저렇게 가엾고 힘없는 여자를 겁먹게 한단 말이오?”\n\n내가 대답도 하기 전에 그녀는 앤 캐서릭 곁에 다가가 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안았다. “왜 그래요, 아가씨?” 그녀가 말했다. “저 사람이 아가씨한테 무슨 짓을 했어요?”\n\n“아무것도요.” 그 가엾은 사람이 대답했다. “아무것도 안 했어요. 그냥 제가 놀랐을 뿐이에요.”\n\n클레멘츠 부인은 두려움 없는 분노로 나를 향해 돌아섰고, 나는 그 때문에 오히려 그녀를 존경하게 되었다.\n\n“저 성난 눈길을 받아 마땅한 사람이라면 나는 진심으로 나 자신이 부끄러웠을 겁니다.” 내가 말했다. “그러나 나는 그럴 만하지 않습니다. 불행히도 그럴 뜻 없이 이분을 놀라게 하고 말았습니다. 이분이 나를 본 것은 이번이 처음이 아닙니다. 직접 물어보십시오. 내가 이분이든 다른 어느 여인이든 일부러 해칠 수 있는 사람이 아니라고 말해 줄 겁니다.”\n\n나는 앤 캐서릭이 듣고 이해할 수 있도록 또렷하게 말했고, 그 말과 그 뜻이 그녀에게 가닿았음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n“네, 네.” 그녀가 말했다—“이분은 전에 저에게 잘해 주셨어요—저를 도와주셨어요——” 그녀는 나머지 말을 친구의 귀에 대고 속삭였다.\n\n“정말 신기한 일이네!” 클레멘츠 부인이 어리둥절한 표정으로 말했다. “하지만 그러면 사정이 완전히 달라지지요. 선생님께 그렇게 거칠게 말씀드린 건 죄송합니다. 하지만 낯선 사람이 보기에 그 모양새가 수상쩍었다는 것은 인정하셔야 해요. 저 애의 변덕을 받아 주고 이런 데에 혼자 있게 한 제 잘못이 선생님 잘못보다 크지요. 자, 아가씨—이제 집으로 갑시다.”\n\n나는 이 착한 여인이 돌아가는 길을 조금 불안해하는 눈치라고 생각하고, 두 사람이 집이 보이는 곳에 이를 때까지 함께 가겠다고 청했다. 클레멘츠 부인은 정중히 고마움을 표하고는 사양했다. 황야에 이르기만 하면 농장 일꾼들을 틀림없이 만나게 될 거라고 그녀는 말했다.\n\n“나를 용서해 주십시오.” 앤 캐서릭이 떠나려고 친구의 팔을 잡을 때 내가 말했다. 그녀를 겁주고 동요시킬 뜻이라고는 조금도 없었건만, 그 가엾고 창백하고 겁먹은 얼굴을 바라보노라니 가슴이 아팠다.\n\n“해 볼게요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “하지만 당신은 너무 많은 걸 알고 있어요—이제부터 당신은 늘 저를 겁나게 할 것만 같아요.”\n\n클레멘츠 부인이 나를 흘긋 보더니 딱하다는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n“안녕히 계세요, 선생님.” 그녀가 말했다. “어쩔 수 없는 일이었다는 건 저도 압니다. 하지만 겁을 준 상대가 이 아이가 아니라 저였더라면 좋았을 텐데요.”\n\n두 사람은 몇 걸음 멀어져 갔다. 나는 그들이 나를 두고 떠난 줄 알았으나, 앤이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니 동무에게서 떨어져 나왔다.\n\n“잠깐만 기다려요.” 그녀가 말했다. “작별 인사를 해야 해요.”\n\n그녀는 무덤으로 되돌아가 두 손을 대리석 십자가 위에 다정하게 얹고는 거기에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“이제 좀 나아졌어요.” 그녀가 조용히 나를 올려다보며 한숨지었다. “당신을 용서할게요.”\n\n그녀는 다시 동행에게로 갔고, 두 사람은 묘지를 떠났다. 나는 그들이 교회 근처에 멈추어 서서, 오두막에서 나와 멀찍이서 우리를 지켜보며 기다리고 있던 묘지기의 아내와 이야기를 나누는 것을 보았다. 그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 황야로 이어지는 오솔길을 따라 올라갔다. 나는 앤 캐서릭이 사라져 가는 모습을, 그녀의 자취가 황혼 속에 온전히 스러질 때까지 바라보았다—마치 그것이 이 고단한 세상에서 흰옷을 입은 여인을 보는 마지막이기라도 한 듯, 그토록 애타고 서글프게 바라보았다.\n\n# XIV\n\n반 시간 뒤 나는 저택으로 돌아와, 그동안 있었던 일을 하나도 빠짐없이 홀콤 양에게 알리고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 처음부터 끝까지 흔들림 없는 침묵의 주의로 내 말을 들었는데, 그녀와 같은 기질과 성정을 지닌 여인에게 그것은 내 이야기가 그녀를 얼마나 심각하게 흔들어 놓았는지를 보여 줄 수 있는 무엇보다 강력한 증거였다.\n\n“마음이 놓이지 않는군요.” 내가 이야기를 마쳤을 때 그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. “앞일을 생각하면 마음이 몹시도 놓이지 않아요.”\n\n“앞일은.” 내가 조심스레 말했다. “우리가 지금을 어떻게 쓰느냐에 달려 있을지도 모릅니다. 앤 캐서릭이 저에게 말했던 것보다 여자에게는 더 선뜻, 더 거리낌 없이 말할 가능성도 없지 않습니다. 만약 페얼리 양이——”\n\n“그건 한순간도 생각할 수 없어요.” 홀콤 양이 더없이 단호한 태도로 말을 잘랐다.\n\n“그렇다면 이렇게 제안드리겠습니다.” 나는 말을 이었다. “당신이 직접 앤 캐서릭을 만나서, 그 사람의 신뢰를 얻기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 보시는 겁니다. 저로서는 이미 그 딱한 사람을 그토록 불행하게 놀라게 해 놓은 마당에, 두 번째로 또 놀라게 한다는 생각은 견디기 어렵습니다. 내일 저와 함께 그 농가까지 가시는 데 무슨 걸리는 점이라도 있습니까?”\n\n“전혀 없어요. 로라를 위하는 일이라면 어디든 가고 무엇이든 하겠어요. 그곳 이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?”\n\n“잘 아시는 곳일 겁니다. 토드 코너라고 하더군요.”\n\n“물론 알지요. 토드 코너는 페얼리 씨의 농장 가운데 하나예요. 우리 집 우유 담당 하녀가 그 농부의 둘째 딸이고요. 그 애는 이 집과 아버지의 농장 사이를 끊임없이 오가니, 우리가 알아 두면 쓸모 있을 무언가를 듣거나 보았을지도 몰라요. 지금 바로 그 애가 아래층에 있는지 알아볼까요?”\n\n그녀가 종을 울려 하인에게 심부름을 보냈다. 하인은 돌아와서, 그 하녀가 지금 농장에 가 있다고 알렸다. 지난 사흘 동안 집에 가지 못했던 터라, 가정부가 그날 저녁 한두 시간 다녀오도록 허락해 주었다는 것이었다.\n\n“그 애하고는 내일 이야기하면 되겠군요.” 하인이 다시 방을 나가자 홀콤 양이 말했다. “그동안, 제가 앤 캐서릭을 만나서 얻어 내야 할 것이 무엇인지 분명히 알아 두고 싶어요. 그 사람을 정신병원에 가둔 이가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이라는 데 대해서는 당신 마음에 아무 의심도 없나요?”\n\n“그림자만큼의 의심도 없습니다. 남은 수수께끼는 오직 그의 동기라는 수수께끼뿐입니다. 두 사람의 신분 차이가 워낙 커서 아무리 먼 인척 관계조차 생각할 수 없어 보이는데, 설령 그 여자가 정말로 감호를 받아야 할 처지였다고 인정한다 하더라도, 어째서 하필 그가 그 여자를 가둔다는 중대한 책임을 떠맡은 사람이어야 했는지를 아는 일이야말로 무엇보다 중요합니다——”\n\n“사설 정신병원이라고 말씀하셨던 것 같은데요?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 사설 정신병원입니다. 가난한 사람이라면 도저히 감당할 수 없는 액수의 돈이 그 여자를 환자로 수용하는 비용으로 치러졌을 것이 분명한 곳이지요.”\n\n“의심이 어디에 놓여 있는지 알겠어요, 하트라이트 씨. 그리고 내일 앤 캐서릭이 우리를 돕든 돕지 않든, 그 의심은 반드시 풀리도록 하겠다고 약속드리지요. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경은 길모어 씨를 납득시키고 저를 납득시키지 않고서는 이 집에 오래 머물지 못할 거예요. 제 동생의 앞날은 제 삶에서 가장 소중한 걱정거리이고, 저는 그 애에게 웬만큼 영향력이 있어서 혼사에 관한 한 그 앞날을 정하는 데 얼마간 힘을 쓸 수 있답니다.”\n\n우리는 그날 밤 인사를 나누고 헤어졌다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 전날 저녁의 일들에 밀려 내 기억에서 사라져 있던 걸림돌 하나가 끼어들어 우리가 곧바로 농장으로 나서지 못하게 했다. 그날은 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 보내는 마지막 날이었고, 우편이 들어오는 대로 홀콤 양의 조언을 따라, 뜻하지 않게 런던으로 돌아가야 할 사정이 생겼음을 들어 계약 기간을 한 달 앞당겨 끝내게 해 달라고 페얼리 씨에게 청해야 했던 것이다.\n\n겉으로 보이는 모양새만큼은 이 핑계가 그럴듯해 보이도록, 다행히도 그날 아침 우편이 런던의 벗들에게서 온 편지 두 통을 나에게 가져다주었다. 나는 그것을 곧장 내 방으로 가지고 올라간 뒤, 하인을 시켜 페얼리 씨께 사무적인 용건으로 언제 뵐 수 있겠는지 여쭙는 전갈을 보냈다.\n\n나는 하인이 돌아오기를 기다리면서, 그 주인이 내 청을 어떻게 받아들일지에 대해 털끝만 한 불안도 느끼지 않았다. 페얼리 씨가 허락하든 하지 않든 나는 떠나야 했다. 이제부터 내 삶을 페얼리 양의 삶에서 갈라놓을 그 쓸쓸한 여정의 첫걸음을 이미 내디뎠다는 자각이, 나 자신과 관련된 모든 문제에 대해 내 감각을 무디게 만들어 놓은 듯했다. 나는 가난한 사내의 발끈하기 잘하는 자존심 따위와는 이미 결별해 있었고, 예술가로서의 자잘한 허영이란 허영과도 모두 결별해 있었다. 페얼리 씨가 무례하게 굴기로 마음먹는다 한들, 그 어떤 무례로도 이제 나에게 상처를 낼 수는 없었다.\n\n하인은 내가 어느 정도 짐작하고 있던 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다. 페얼리 씨께서는 하필 그날 아침 건강 상태가 그러하여 저를 맞이하는 기쁨을 누릴 가망이 전혀 없음을 유감으로 여기신다고 했다. 그러니 부디 사과를 받아 주시고, 하실 말씀은 편지의 형식으로 알려 주시면 고맙겠다는 것이었다. 이 집에 머문 석 달 동안 이와 비슷한 전갈이 이따금씩 나에게 전해져 왔다. 그 기간 내내 페얼리 씨는 나를 “소유”하게 되어 기뻐하셨으나, 두 번째로 나를 만날 만큼 몸이 좋았던 적은 한 번도 없었다. 내가 배접하고 손본 그림 묶음이 새로 나올 때마다 하인이 내 “문안”과 함께 그것을 주인에게 가져갔고, 페얼리 씨의 “다정한 인사”와 “깊은 감사”, 그리고 건강 상태가 여전히 자기 방에 홀로 갇힌 죄수로 남아 있기를 강요한다는 “진심 어린 유감”만을 들고 빈손으로 돌아왔다. 양쪽 모두에게 이보다 더 만족스러운 조정은 도무지 있을 수 없었을 것이다. 그런 사정 아래에서 우리 둘 가운데 누가 페얼리 씨의 그 편의를 봐주는 신경에 더 고마운 은혜를 느꼈는지는 말하기 어려울 것이다.\n\n나는 곧바로 앉아 편지를 썼는데, 되도록 정중하고 되도록 분명하고 되도록 짧게 내 뜻을 밝혔다. 페얼리 씨는 답장을 서두르지 않았다. 그 답이 내 손에 놓이기까지 거의 한 시간이 흘렀다. 그것은 상아처럼 매끄럽고 판지만큼이나 두꺼운 편지지 위에 보랏빛 잉크로, 아름다우리만치 고르고 단정한 글씨로 쓰여 있었으며, 나에게 이런 말로 건네졌다—\n\n“페얼리 씨가 하트라이트 씨께 인사를 전합니다. 페얼리 씨는 하트라이트 씨의 청원에 (현재의 건강 상태로는) 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라고 실망하였습니다. 페얼리 씨는 사무에 밝은 사람이 아니나, 사무에 밝은 자기 집사와 상의하였고, 그 사람은 계약을 파기하게 해 달라는 하트라이트 씨의 요청이 아마도 생사가 걸린 경우가 아니고서는 그 어떤 필요로도 정당화될 수 없다는 페얼리 씨의 견해를 확인해 주었습니다. 예술과 그 종사자들을 향한 저 지극한 흠모의 정, 곧 그것을 가꾸는 일이 고통 속에 사는 페얼리 씨의 삶에 위안이자 행복이 되어 주는 그 정이 만약 쉽사리 흔들릴 수 있는 것이었다면, 하트라이트 씨의 이번 처사는 그것을 흔들어 놓았을 것입니다. 그러나 흔들어 놓지 못하였습니다—오직 하트라이트 씨 개인의 경우를 제외하고는 말입니다.\n\n“자기 견해를 밝혔으니—다시 말해 격심한 신경의 고통이 그에게 무엇이든 밝히도록 허락하는 한도까지는 밝혔으니—페얼리 씨로서는 자신에게 제기된 저 지극히 이례적인 청원에 관하여 자기 결정을 표명하는 것 말고는 덧붙일 말이 없습니다. 몸과 마음의 완전한 안정이 그의 경우 더없이 중요한 만큼, 페얼리 씨는 양쪽 모두에게 본질적으로 짜증스러울 수밖에 없는 사정 아래에서 하트라이트 씨가 이 집에 머물러 그 안정을 어지럽히도록 두지 않겠습니다. 따라서 페얼리 씨는 오로지 자기 자신의 평온을 지킨다는 뜻에서 거절할 권리를 포기하며—하트라이트 씨에게 떠나도 좋다고 알립니다.”\n\n나는 편지를 접어 다른 서류들과 함께 치워 두었다. 한때는 그것을 모욕으로 여겨 분개했을 시절도 있었으나, 나는 이제 그것을 계약에서 풀려났다는 서면 증서로 받아들였다. 아래층 아침 식사실로 내려가 홀콤 양에게 함께 농장으로 걸어갈 준비가 되었다고 알렸을 때, 그것은 이미 내 마음에서 떠나 있었고 기억에서도 거의 사라져 있었다.\n\n“페얼리 씨가 만족스러운 답을 주셨나요?” 저택을 나서며 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“떠나도 좋다고 허락하셨습니다, 홀콤 양.”\n\n그녀는 재빨리 나를 올려다보더니, 내가 그녀를 알게 된 이래 처음으로 스스로 내 팔에 손을 얹었다. 고용에서 벗어나도 좋다는 그 허락이 어떤 식으로 주어졌는지 그녀가 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 윗사람으로서가 아니라 벗으로서 나에게 위로를 건네고 있다는 것을, 그 어떤 말로도 그토록 섬세하게 표현할 수는 없었을 것이다. 나는 그 사내의 무례한 편지에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했으나, 그 여인의 속죄하는 다정함은 깊이 느꼈다.\n\n농장으로 가는 길에 우리는 홀콤 양이 혼자 집 안으로 들어가고 나는 부르면 들릴 만한 거리 밖에서 기다리기로 정했다. 이런 방식을 택한 것은, 전날 저녁 교회 묘지에서 있었던 일이 있고 난 뒤라 내가 곁에 있으면 앤 캐서릭의 신경질적인 두려움이 되살아나, 낯선 부인이 다가오는 것에 대해 그녀가 한층 더 의심을 품게 될까 염려해서였다. 홀콤 양은 우선 농부의 아내에게—어떤 식으로든 기꺼이 자기를 도와주리라 굳게 믿고 있는 사람이었다—말을 걸어 볼 생각으로 나를 남겨 두고 갔고, 나는 그 집 가까운 언저리에서 그녀를 기다렸다.\n\n나는 한동안 혼자 남겨지리라고 단단히 각오하고 있었다. 그런데 놀랍게도 오 분이 조금 넘었을 뿐인데 홀콤 양이 돌아왔다.\n\n“앤 캐서릭이 만나기를 거절합니까?” 나는 깜짝 놀라 물었다.\n\n“앤 캐서릭은 가 버렸어요.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다.\n\n“가 버렸다고요?”\n\n“클레멘츠 부인과 함께 갔어요. 두 사람 다 오늘 아침 여덟 시에 농장을 떠났대요.”\n\n나는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다—그저 진상을 밝힐 우리의 마지막 기회가 그들과 함께 사라져 버렸다는 것만을 느낄 수 있었다.\n\n“토드 부인이 그 손님들에 대해 아는 것은 저도 다 알아요.” 홀콤 양이 말을 이었다. “그런데도 그분이 그렇듯 저 역시 캄캄한 채로 남아 있지요. 두 사람은 어젯밤 당신과 헤어진 뒤 무사히 돌아왔고, 저녁의 앞부분은 여느 때처럼 토드 씨네 식구들과 함께 보냈어요. 그런데 저녁 식사 시간 바로 전에 앤 캐서릭이 갑자기 아득해지는 발작에 사로잡혀 모두를 놀라게 했답니다. 농장에 도착하던 날에도 그보다는 덜 놀라운 비슷한 발작이 한 번 있었는데, 그때 토드 부인은 그것을 그 사람이 마침 읽고 있던 무언가와 연결 지었어요. 농장 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 우리 고장 신문이었고, 그 사람이 그것을 집어 든 지 겨우 일이 분밖에 되지 않았을 때였지요.”\n\n“토드 부인은 신문의 어느 대목이 그런 식으로 그녀를 흔들어 놓았는지 알고 있습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아니요.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다. “그분은 신문을 훑어보았지만 누구를 동요시킬 만한 것은 아무것도 찾지 못했대요. 하지만 저도 제 차례로 훑어보게 해 달라고 청했지요. 그런데 펼친 바로 첫 면에서, 편집자가 얼마 되지도 않는 자기 기삿거리를 우리 집안일에서 끌어다 불려 놓았고, 런던 신문들에서 옮겨 온 상류층 혼사 소식들 사이에 제 동생의 혼약을 실어 놓은 것을 발견했어요. 저는 곧바로 이것이 앤 캐서릭을 그토록 이상하게 흔들어 놓은 그 대목이라고 결론지었고, 그 안에서 그 사람이 이튿날 우리 집으로 보낸 편지의 기원까지도 본 듯했답니다.”\n\n“두 경우 다 의심의 여지가 없겠군요. 그런데 어제저녁의 두 번째 실신 발작에 대해서는 무엇을 들으셨습니까?”\n\n“아무것도요. 그 원인은 완전한 수수께끼예요. 방 안에 낯선 사람은 없었어요. 손님이라고는 우리 집 우유 하녀뿐이었는데, 말씀드렸듯이 토드 씨의 딸 가운데 하나지요. 그리고 오간 이야기라고는 고장 일에 대한 여느 때의 잡담뿐이었고요. 사람들은 그 사람이 소리치는 것을 들었고, 눈에 띄는 아무런 까닭도 없이 낯빛이 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았대요. 토드 부인과 클레멘츠 부인이 그 사람을 위층으로 데려갔고, 클레멘츠 부인이 곁에 남았지요. 두 사람이 여느 때 잠자리에 드는 시각이 한참 지나도록 이야기를 나누는 소리가 들렸고, 오늘 아침 일찍 클레멘츠 부인이 토드 부인을 한쪽으로 불러내, 이제 떠나야 한다는 말로 이루 표현할 길 없을 만큼 그분을 놀라게 했어요. 토드 부인이 그 손님에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 유일한 설명은, 무슨 일이 있었는데 그것은 농가의 누구의 잘못도 아니지만 앤 캐서릭이 당장 리머리지를 떠나기로 마음먹을 만큼 심각한 일이라는 것뿐이었지요. 클레멘츠 부인에게 더 분명히 말하라고 다그쳐 봐야 아무 소용이 없었어요. 그분은 그저 고개를 저으며, 앤을 생각해서라도 아무도 자기에게 캐묻지 말아 주기를 빌고 또 빈다고 했대요. 자기 자신도 몹시 동요한 기색이 역력한 채로 되풀이할 수 있는 말이라고는, 앤이 떠나야 하고, 자기가 함께 떠나야 하며, 두 사람이 어디로 향하든 그 행선지는 누구에게도 비밀로 해야 한다는 것뿐이었고요. 토드 부인이 손님을 붙들며 만류하고 거절하던 이야기는 굳이 옮기지 않겠어요. 결국 그분이 두 사람을 가장 가까운 역까지 태워다 주는 것으로 끝났는데, 벌써 세 시간도 더 전의 일이에요. 그분은 가는 길에 좀 더 분명히 말하게 하려고 무진 애를 썼지만 헛일이었고, 두 사람을 역사 문밖에 내려놓을 때는 그 예의 없이 갑작스러운 출발과 자기에게 티끌만 한 속내도 털어놓지 않으려는 매정한 태도에 어찌나 상하고 언짢았던지, 작별 인사를 하려고 멈추지도 않은 채 화가 나서 마차를 몰아 가 버렸답니다. 벌어진 일은 정확히 이러해요. 하트라이트 씨, 당신의 기억을 더듬어서, 오늘 아침 그 두 여자의 그 심상찮은 출발을 조금이라도 설명해 줄 만한 일이 어제저녁 묘지에서 있었는지 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n“홀콤 양, 저로서는 먼저 농가 사람들을 놀라게 한 앤 캐서릭의 그 갑작스러운 변화부터 설명해 보고 싶습니다. 그것은 그녀와 제가 헤어지고도 몇 시간이나 지난 뒤, 혹시라도 제가 불행히 일으켰을지 모를 어떤 격한 동요라도 가라앉기에 충분한 시간이 흐른 뒤의 일이니까요. 그녀가 정신이 아득해졌을 때 방 안에서 오가던 잡담에 대해 특별히 물어보셨습니까?”\n\n“네. 그런데 그날 저녁 토드 부인은 집안일에 마음을 빼앗겨 농가 거실의 이야기에 온전히 귀를 기울이지 못했던 모양이에요. 그저 ‘그냥 소식들’이었다고밖에 말해 주지 못했는데—짐작건대 다들 여느 때처럼 서로에 대해 이야기했다는 뜻이겠지요.”\n\n“그 우유 하녀의 기억이 어머니의 기억보다 나을지도 모르겠습니다.” 내가 말했다. “돌아가는 대로 그 처녀와 이야기해 보시는 편이 좋겠습니다, 홀콤 양.”\n\n내 제안은 우리가 저택으로 돌아온 그 즉시 실행에 옮겨졌다. 홀콤 양이 나를 하인들이 쓰는 구역으로 돌아 데려갔고, 우리는 그 처녀가 우유실에서 소매를 어깨까지 걷어붙인 채 커다란 우유통을 닦으며 일하는 내내 흥겹게 노래를 부르고 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n“이 신사분을 모시고 네 우유실을 보러 왔단다, 해나.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “이 집의 볼거리 가운데 하나이고, 언제나 네 솜씨를 자랑해 주는 곳이지.”\n\n처녀는 얼굴을 붉히고 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 늘 물건들을 단정하고 깨끗하게 간수하려고 최선을 다하고 있기를 바란다고 수줍게 말했다.\n\n“방금 너희 아버지 댁에 다녀오는 길이란다.” 홀콤 양이 말을 이었다. “어제저녁에 거기 가 있었다고 들었는데, 집에 손님들이 와 있었다지?”\n\n“예, 아가씨.”\n\n“그중 한 분이 정신이 아득해져 몸져누우셨다고 들었다. 그분을 놀라게 할 만한 말이나 행동은 없었겠지? 아주 무서운 이야기 같은 걸 하고 있지는 않았고?”\n\n“아유, 그럼요, 아가씨!” 처녀가 웃으며 말했다. “저희는 그냥 소식들 이야기만 했는데예.”\n\n“토드 코너에서는 언니들이 너에게 소식을 전해 주었겠구나?”\n\n“예, 아가씨.”\n\n“그리고 너는 리머리지 하우스의 소식을 그 사람들에게 전해 주었고?”\n\n“예, 아가씨. 그리고 그 딱한 분을 놀라게 할 만한 말은 정말이지 아무것도 없었어예. 그분이 몸져누우실 때 마침 이야기하고 있던 게 저였거든예. 저는 여태 한 번도 정신이 아득해져 본 적이 없어서, 그걸 보고 어찌나 놀랐는지 몰라예, 아가씨.”\n\n그 애에게 더 물어볼 새도 없이, 우유실 문간에서 달걀 바구니를 받으라는 부름이 와서 그 애는 자리를 떴다. 그 애가 우리 곁을 떠나자 나는 홀콤 양에게 속삭였다—\n\n“어젯밤에 리머리지 하우스에 손님이 올 예정이라는 이야기를 혹시 꺼냈는지 물어봐 주십시오.”\n\n홀콤 양은 눈짓으로 알아들었다는 것을 나에게 보이고는, 우유 하녀가 우리에게 돌아오자마자 그 물음을 던졌다.\n\n“아, 예, 아가씨, 그 이야기는 했어예.” 처녀가 스스럼없이 말했다. “손님이 오신다는 것하고 얼룩소한테 생긴 사고 이야기가 제가 농장에 가져갈 소식의 전부였거든예.”\n\n“이름도 말했니? 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 월요일에 오실 예정이라고 말했어?”\n\n“예, 아가씨—퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 오신다고 말했어예. 그게 나쁜 일이 아니었으면 좋겠어예—제가 잘못한 게 아니었으면 좋겠어예.”\n\n“아, 아니란다, 나쁠 것 없어. 가시지요, 하트라이트 씨. 일하는 걸 더 붙들고 있으면 해나가 우리를 성가시게 여기기 시작할 거예요.”\n\n다시 단둘이 되자마자 우리는 걸음을 멈추고 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n“이제도 당신 마음에 의심이 남아 있습니까, 홀콤 양?”\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 의심을 걷어 내야 할 거예요, 하트라이트 씨—그러지 못한다면 로라 페얼리는 결코 그의 아내가 되지 않아요.”\n\n# XV\n\n우리가 집 앞쪽으로 돌아 걸어가는데 역에서 온 삯마차 한 대가 진입로를 따라 다가왔다. 홀콤 양은 마차가 멎을 때까지 현관 계단에서 기다렸다가, 발판이 내려지자마자 날렵하게 내려선 노신사에게 악수를 청하러 나아갔다. 길모어 씨가 도착한 것이다.\n\n서로 소개를 받았을 때 나는 좀처럼 감추기 어려운 관심과 호기심을 품고 그를 바라보았다. 이 노인은 내가 리머리지 하우스를 떠난 뒤에도 이곳에 남을 사람이었다. 그는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명을 듣게 될 것이고, 홀콤 양이 판단을 내리는 데 자신의 경험으로 도움을 줄 것이었다. 그는 혼사 문제가 매듭지어질 때까지 머물 것이고, 만일 그 문제가 긍정으로 결정된다면 페얼리 양을 그 약혼에 돌이킬 수 없이 붙들어 매는 재산 계약서를 작성할 손도 바로 그의 손이었다. 지금 내가 아는 것에 비하면 그때는 아무것도 모르던 시절이었건만, 그럼에도 나는 그 집안의 변호사를, 생면부지의 사람을 마주하고서는 여태 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 관심으로 바라보았다.\n\n겉모습으로 보면 길모어 씨는 늙은 변호사라는 통념과는 정반대였다. 안색은 불그레하고 혈색이 좋았다. 흰 머리는 다소 길게 기른 채 정성껏 빗어 넘겼고, 검은 상의와 조끼와 바지는 흠잡을 데 없이 단정하게 몸에 맞았다. 흰 넥타이는 공들여 매어져 있었으며, 연보라색 염소 가죽 장갑은 유행에 밝은 성직자의 손을 꾸민다 해도 두려울 것도 나무랄 것도 없을 만한 물건이었다. 그의 태도에는 옛 예법 학파의 격식 있는 우아함과 세련됨이 기분 좋게 배어 있었고, 거기에 늘 자기 능력을 최상으로 유지해야만 하는 직업을 가진 사람다운 활기찬 예리함과 민첩함이 더해져 있었다. 낙천적인 기질과 순조로운 출발, 그 뒤로 이어진 명예롭고 안락한 번영의 긴 이력, 그리고 쾌활하고 부지런하며 두루 존경받는 노년. 길모어 씨를 처음 소개받고 내가 받은 전반적인 인상이 그러했으며, 훗날 더 오래고 더 깊은 교유로 알게 된 바가 그 인상을 확인해 주었을 뿐이라는 사실도 그를 위해 덧붙여 두는 것이 공평하겠다.\n\n나는 노신사와 홀콤 양이 함께 집 안으로 들어가, 낯선 사람이 있다는 거북함 없이 집안일을 이야기하도록 자리를 비켜 주었다. 두 사람이 응접실로 가느라 홀을 가로지르는 동안, 나는 다시 계단을 내려와 혼자 정원을 거닐었다.\n\n리머리지 하우스에서 내게 남은 시간은 이제 셀 수 있을 만큼이었다. 이튿날 아침 떠나는 일은 돌이킬 수 없이 정해졌고, 익명의 편지가 불러온 그 조사에서 내가 맡은 몫도 끝이 났다. 이제 남은 짧은 시간 동안, 어쩔 수 없이 내 마음에 가해야 했던 그 차갑고 잔인한 억제에서 마음을 다시 풀어놓는다 한들, 그리하여 내 행복과 내 사랑의 짧은 꿈결 같던 시절과 얽힌 풍경들에 작별을 고한다 한들, 나 자신 말고는 누구도 해를 입을 리 없었다.\n\n나는 본능적으로 서재 창 아래 산책길로 발을 돌렸다. 전날 저녁 그녀가 작은 개를 데리고 서 있던 그곳으로. 그리고 그 사랑스러운 발이 그토록 자주 밟았던 오솔길을 따라가다가 그녀의 장미원으로 통하는 쪽문에 이르렀다. 이제 그곳에는 겨울의 헐벗음이 스산하게 퍼져 있었다. 그녀가 내게 이름으로 구별하는 법을 가르쳐 준 꽃들, 내가 그녀에게 그것을 보고 그리는 법을 가르쳐 준 그 꽃들은 사라지고, 화단 사이로 난 자그마한 흰 길들은 벌써 축축하게 푸른 이끼가 앉아 있었다. 나는 나무들이 늘어선 길로 나아갔다. 팔월 저녁의 따스한 향기를 함께 들이마시던 곳, 발밑 땅에 아롱지던 그늘과 햇빛의 무수한 어우러짐을 함께 감탄하며 바라보던 곳이었다. 신음하는 가지에서 잎사귀들이 내 주위로 떨어졌고, 대기에 밴 흙냄새 나는 부패가 뼛속까지 나를 서늘하게 했다. 조금 더 가자 나는 저택 부지를 벗어나, 가장 가까운 언덕으로 완만하게 굽어 오르는 좁은 길을 따라가고 있었다. 우리가 앉아 쉬던 길가의 베어 넘어진 늙은 나무는 비에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 우리 앞 거친 돌담 아래 포근히 자리 잡아 내가 그녀를 위해 그려 주었던 고사리와 풀 무더기는 물웅덩이로 변해, 젖어 늘어진 잡초 섬 둘레에 괴어 있었다. 나는 언덕 꼭대기에 올라, 더 행복하던 시절 우리가 그토록 자주 감탄하던 경치를 바라보았다. 그것은 차갑고 황량했다. 더 이상 내가 기억하던 그 경치가 아니었다. 그녀가 곁에 있어 비추던 햇살은 내게서 멀어졌고, 그녀 목소리의 매력은 더 이상 내 귓가에 속삭이지 않았다. 지금 내가 내려다보고 선 바로 이 자리에서 그녀는 마지막 남은 부모였던 아버지 이야기를 내게 들려주었다. 두 사람이 얼마나 서로를 아꼈는지, 집 안 어떤 방에 들어설 때, 또 아버지와 얽힌 잊혔던 일거리며 놀이를 다시 손에 잡을 때 아직도 얼마나 애달프게 그를 그리워하는지를. 그 말을 들으며 바라보던 그 경치가, 홀로 언덕 꼭대기에 선 지금 내가 보는 이 경치와 과연 같은 것이었을까? 나는 돌아서서 그곳을 떠났다. 황야를 지나 모래언덕을 돌아 해변으로 내려가며 왔던 길을 되짚었다. 그곳에는 하얗게 성난 파도가 있었고, 뛰노는 물결의 무수한 영광이 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 언젠가 양산으로 모래 위에 무심한 그림을 그리던 그 자리는 어디에 있는가. 우리가 나란히 앉아 있던 그 자리, 그녀가 나 자신과 내 집에 대해 이야기를 청하고, 내 어머니와 누이에 대해 여자다운 세심한 질문을 던지며, 내가 언젠가 그 쓸쓸한 하숙방을 떠나 아내와 내 집을 갖게 될지 순진하게 궁금해하던 그 자리는. 바람과 물결은 이미 오래전에 그녀가 모래 위 자국으로 남긴 흔적을 지워 버렸다. 나는 바닷가 풍경의 드넓은 단조로움을 둘러보았다. 우리 둘이 볕 좋은 시간을 한가로이 흘려보내던 그 자리는, 마치 내가 한 번도 알지 못했던 곳처럼 내게서 사라졌고, 마치 내가 이미 낯선 나라의 해변에 서 있기라도 한 듯 생소했다.\n\n텅 빈 해변의 정적이 내 가슴을 차갑게 때렸다. 나는 집으로, 그리고 걸음마다 그녀를 말해 주는 흔적이 남아 있는 정원으로 돌아왔다.\n\n서쪽 테라스 산책길에서 나는 길모어 씨와 마주쳤다. 그는 분명 나를 찾고 있었다. 서로를 알아보자마자 걸음을 빨리했기 때문이다. 내 마음 상태는 낯선 사람과 어울리기에 도무지 맞지 않았으나, 만남은 피할 수 없었으므로 나는 되도록 잘 넘겨 보자고 마음먹었다.\n\n“마침 뵙고 싶던 바로 그분이시군요.” 노신사가 말했다. “두어 마디 드릴 말씀이 있었는데, 괜찮으시다면 지금 기회를 빌리겠습니다. 터놓고 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양과 저는 집안일을 의논하던 참이었습니다. 제가 여기 온 까닭이 되는 그 일 말입니다. 그러다 이야기 도중에 자연스럽게 홀콤 양이 익명의 편지와 얽힌 그 불쾌한 사건을 제게 들려주었고, 지금까지의 처리 과정에서 선생께서 참으로 훌륭하고도 온당하게 맡아 주신 몫도 이야기해 주었지요. 그 몫이 있는 만큼, 선생께서 시작하신 조사의 앞으로의 처리가 믿을 만한 손에 맡겨지는지 알고 싶으신 마음이 여느 경우와는 달리 각별하시리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다. 그 점이라면 마음을 아주 놓으십시오. 그 일은 제 손에 맡겨질 것이니까요.”\n\n“길모어 씨, 그 일을 조언하고 처리하시기에는 어느 모로 보나 저보다 훨씬 적임이십니다. 혹 실례가 되지 않는다면, 앞으로 어떻게 하실지 이미 정하셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“정할 수 있는 데까지는 정했습니다, 하트라이트 씨. 저는 그 편지 사본을 사정을 적은 진술서와 함께 런던에 있는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 사무변호사에게 보낼 참입니다. 저와도 얼마간 안면이 있는 사람이지요. 편지 원본은 여기 두었다가 퍼시벌 경이 도착하는 대로 보여 드리겠습니다. 두 여자의 행방을 좇는 일은 이미 손을 써 두었습니다. 페얼리 씨의 하인 하나를, 입이 무거운 사람으로 골라 역에 보내 알아보게 했지요. 그자에게는 여비와 지시를 주었으니, 실마리라도 잡히면 여자들을 뒤쫓을 것입니다. 월요일에 퍼시벌 경이 올 때까지 할 수 있는 일은 이것이 전부입니다. 저로서는 신사이자 명예를 아는 사람에게 기대할 수 있는 해명이라면 무엇이든 그분이 선선히 내놓으리라는 데 조금도 의심이 없습니다. 퍼시벌 경으로 말하자면 대단히 높은 분이시지요. 지위도 훌륭하고, 평판에는 의심할 구석이 없습니다. 결과에 대해서는 아주 마음이 놓입니다. 아주 놓인다고 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다. 제 경험으로 보면 이런 종류의 일은 끊임없이 일어납니다. 익명의 편지, 불행한 여자, 개탄스러운 세태 말입니다. 이 사건에 유별난 얽힘이 있다는 것은 부정하지 않겠습니다. 그러나 사건 자체는 참으로 딱하게도 흔합니다. 흔하고말고요.”\n\n“길모어 씨, 유감스럽게도 저는 이 사건을 보는 견해가 선생과 다른 듯합니다.”\n\n“그러시겠지요. 그러시겠지요. 저는 늙은이라 실제적인 견해를 취하고, 선생은 젊은이라 낭만적인 견해를 취하십니다. 우리 견해를 두고 다투지는 맙시다. 하트라이트 씨, 저는 직업상 언제나 논쟁의 공기 속에서 살아가는 사람이라, 지금 여기서 그러하듯 거기서 벗어나는 것이 더없이 반갑습니다. 우리는 일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 기다려 보지요. 그럼요, 그럼요, 기다려 봅시다. 참 멋진 곳이군요. 사냥은 잘됩니까? 아마 아니겠지요. 페얼리 씨 땅에는 사냥터로 관리하는 데가 없지 싶습니다. 그래도 멋진 곳이고, 사람들도 유쾌합니다. 듣자니 하트라이트 씨는 그림을 그리신다지요? 부러운 재주입니다. 화풍은 어떠신지요?”\n\n우리는 이런저런 이야기로 흘러갔다. 아니, 정확히는 길모어 씨가 말하고 나는 듣기만 했다. 내 마음은 그에게서도, 그가 그토록 유창하게 늘어놓는 화제에서도 멀리 떠나 있었다. 지난 두 시간의 홀로 걷기가 내게 작용을 일으켜, 리머리지 하우스를 떠나는 것을 앞당기자는 생각을 마음에 심어 놓았던 것이다. 작별을 고하는 그 힘겨운 시련을 무엇 하러 단 일 분이라도 쓸데없이 늘여야 한단 말인가? 이제 누가 내게 더 무슨 소용을 바란단 말인가? 내가 컴벌랜드에 머물러서 이룰 수 있는 쓸모 있는 목적은 없었고, 고용주가 허락한 퇴거에는 시일의 제약도 없었다. 그렇다면 지금 당장 끝내지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?\n\n나는 끝내기로 마음먹었다. 아직 해가 남은 시간이 몇 시간 있었고, 런던으로 돌아가는 여정을 그날 오후에 시작하지 못할 이유가 없었다. 나는 그 자리에서 떠오른 대로 예의 바른 핑계를 하나 대어 길모어 씨에게서 물러나, 곧장 집으로 돌아왔다.\n\n내 방으로 올라가는 길에 계단에서 홀콤 양과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 내 서두르는 몸짓과 달라진 태도를 보고 내게 새로운 작정이 생겼음을 알아채고는 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었다.\n\n나는 떠남을 앞당기려 생각하게 된 이유를 여기 적은 그대로 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“안 돼요, 안 돼요.” 그녀가 간곡하고도 다정하게 말했다. “친구처럼 우리 곁을 떠나세요. 한 번만 더 우리와 빵을 나누세요. 여기 머물러 저녁을 드시고, 여기 머물러 우리가 당신과 보내는 마지막 저녁을 처음 저녁들처럼 즐겁게 보내도록 도와주세요. 제 청이에요. 베지 부인의 청이기도 하고요.” 그녀는 잠시 망설이더니 덧붙였다. “로라의 청이기도 해요.”\n\n나는 남겠다고 약속했다. 하느님은 아시리라, 나는 그들 중 누구에게도 슬픈 인상의 그림자조차 남기고 싶지 않았다.\n\n저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울릴 때까지는 내 방이 내게 가장 알맞은 자리였다. 나는 아래층으로 내려갈 시간이 될 때까지 그곳에서 기다렸다.\n\n나는 그날 하루 종일 페얼리 양과 말을 나누지 않았다. 아니, 얼굴조차 보지 못했다. 응접실에 들어섰을 때의 그 첫 대면은 그녀의 자제심에도 내 자제심에도 힘겨운 시련이었다. 그녀 역시 우리의 마지막 저녁이 지나간 황금 같은 시절을, 다시는 오지 않을 그 시절을 되살리도록 최선을 다한 터였다. 그녀는 자신이 가진 옷 가운데 내가 무엇보다 예쁘다고 하던 옷을 입고 있었다. 옛스러운 레이스로 정갈하고 어여쁘게 장식한 짙푸른 비단옷이었다. 그녀는 예전처럼 선뜻 나를 맞으러 나왔고, 더 행복하던 날들의 그 솔직하고 순진한 호의로 내게 손을 내밀었다. 내 손안에서 떨리던 차가운 손가락, 한복판에 새빨간 점 하나가 타오르던 창백한 뺨, 입술 위에 살아남으려 애쓰다가 내가 바라보는 사이 스러져 버린 희미한 미소가 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 침착함이 어떤 자기희생 위에 지탱되고 있는지를 내게 말해 주었다. 내 마음은 그녀를 더 가까이 끌어당길 수 없을 만큼 이미 가득 차 있었으니, 그러지 않았다면 나는 그때 여태껏 사랑한 적 없는 방식으로 그녀를 사랑했으리라.\n\n길모어 씨는 우리에게 큰 도움이 되었다. 그는 기분이 몹시 좋았고 지칠 줄 모르는 기운으로 이야기를 이끌었다. 홀콤 양이 꿋꿋이 그를 거들었고, 나도 그녀를 본받으려 있는 힘을 다했다. 표정의 가장 미미한 변화까지 내가 읽을 줄 알게 된 그 다정한 푸른 눈이, 우리가 처음 식탁에 앉았을 때 애원하듯 나를 바라보았다. 언니를 도와 주세요, 그 상냥하고 근심 어린 얼굴은 그렇게 말하는 듯했다. 언니를 도와 주세요, 그러면 저를 돕는 거예요.\n\n우리는 적어도 겉보기로는 그런대로 즐겁게 저녁 식사를 마쳤다. 부인들이 식탁에서 일어나고 길모어 씨와 나만 식당에 남았을 때, 새로운 관심거리가 나타나 우리의 주의를 끌었고, 그 덕분에 나는 꼭 필요하고 반가운 몇 분간의 침묵으로 마음을 가라앉힐 기회를 얻었다. 앤 캐서릭과 클레멘츠 부인의 자취를 좇으라고 보냈던 하인이 보고를 가지고 돌아와, 곧바로 식당으로 안내되어 들어온 것이다.\n\n“그래, 무엇을 알아냈나?” 길모어 씨가 물었다.\n\n“알아낸 바로는요, 어르신,” 하인이 대답했다. “두 여자가 여기 우리 역에서 칼라일까지 표를 끊었습니다.”\n\n“그 말을 들었으면 당연히 칼라일로 갔겠지?”\n\n“갔습니다, 어르신. 그런데 송구스럽게도 그 뒤로는 아무 자취도 찾지 못했습니다.”\n\n“철도에서 물어보았나?”\n\n“예, 어르신.”\n\n“여러 여관에서도?”\n\n“예, 어르신.”\n\n“내가 써 준 진술서는 경찰서에 맡기고?”\n\n“그리했습니다, 어르신.”\n\n“그래, 이 사람아, 자네도 할 수 있는 일은 다 했고 나도 할 수 있는 일은 다 했으니, 이 일은 다음 소식이 있을 때까지 그대로 두어야겠네.” 하인이 물러가자 노신사는 말을 이었다. “우리는 으뜸패를 다 냈습니다, 하트라이트 씨. 적어도 당분간은 그 여자들이 우리보다 한 수 앞섰고, 이제 우리에게 남은 방도라고는 다음 월요일에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 이곳에 올 때까지 기다리는 것뿐입니다. 잔을 다시 채우지 않으시렵니까? 좋은 포트와인이군요. 잡스럽지 않고 묵직한 옛 술입니다. 하기야 제 지하 저장고에는 이보다 나은 것이 있습니다만.”\n\n우리는 응접실로 돌아왔다. 내 생애 가장 행복한 저녁들을 보낸 방, 이 마지막 밤이 지나면 다시는 보지 못할 그 방으로. 해가 짧아지고 날이 추워진 뒤로 그곳의 모습도 달라져 있었다. 테라스 쪽 유리문은 닫힌 채 두꺼운 커튼에 가려져 있었다. 우리가 즐겨 앉아 있던 그 부드러운 황혼의 어스름 대신, 이제는 등불의 환하고 눈부신 빛이 내 눈을 부시게 했다. 모든 것이 달라져 있었다. 안에서도 밖에서도 모든 것이 달라져 있었다.\n\n홀콤 양과 길모어 씨는 카드 탁자에 나란히 앉았고, 베지 부인은 늘 앉던 의자를 차지했다. 저들의 저녁을 어떻게 쓸지에는 아무런 구속이 없었고, 그것을 보고 있자니 내 저녁을 어떻게 쓸지에 걸린 구속이 한층 더 아프게 느껴졌다. 페얼리 양이 악보대 근처에서 머뭇거리는 것이 보였다. 예전 같으면 나도 그 곁으로 갔을 것이다. 나는 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다. 어디로 가야 할지도, 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그녀가 나를 한 번 재빨리 흘긋 보더니 악보대에서 악보 한 장을 불쑥 집어 들고는, 스스로 내 쪽으로 다가왔다.\n\n“당신이 그토록 좋아하시던 모차르트의 그 짧은 곡들을 좀 쳐 드릴까요?” 그녀는 초조하게 악보를 펴며, 말하는 내내 악보를 내려다본 채 물었다.\n\n내가 고맙다는 말을 하기도 전에 그녀는 서둘러 피아노로 갔다. 그 곁에 있는, 내가 늘 차지하던 의자가 비어 있었다. 그녀는 화음을 몇 개 짚더니 나를 돌아보았고, 이내 다시 악보로 눈을 돌렸다.\n\n“예전 자리에 앉지 않으시겠어요?” 그녀는 몹시 불쑥, 그리고 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“마지막 밤이니 앉아도 되겠지요.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그저 악보에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 외워서 알고 있는 곡, 예전에는 책 없이도 몇 번이고 되풀이해 치던 곡이었건만. 내 말을 들었다는 것을, 내가 자기 곁에 있음을 알고 있다는 것을 나는 오직 한 가지로 알 수 있었다. 내게 가까운 쪽 뺨의 붉은 점이 스러지고 얼굴 전체가 하얗게 질려 가는 것을 보고서였다.\n\n“떠나신다니 정말 슬퍼요.” 그녀가 말했다. 목소리는 거의 속삭임으로 잦아들었고, 눈은 점점 더 뚫어지게 악보를 향했으며, 손가락은 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 기묘하고도 열에 들뜬 기운으로 건반 위를 날아다녔다.\n\n“그 다정한 말씀을, 페얼리 양, 저는 내일이 오고 또 가 버린 뒤로도 오래도록 기억하겠습니다.”\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 더욱 하얗게 질렸고, 그녀는 얼굴을 나에게서 더 멀리 돌렸다.\n\n“내일 이야기는 하지 마세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “오늘 밤 이야기는 음악이 대신 하게 해 주세요. 우리 말보다 더 행복한 말로요.”\n\n그녀의 입술이 떨리더니 억누르려 애썼으나 헛되이 새어 나온 희미한 한숨이 흘렀다. 손가락이 건반 위에서 흔들렸고, 그녀는 틀린 음을 짚었으며, 그것을 바로잡으려다 스스로 헝클어져 두 손을 성난 듯 무릎에 떨어뜨렸다. 카드놀이를 하던 홀콤 양과 길모어 씨가 탁자에서 놀란 얼굴로 고개를 들었다. 의자에서 졸던 베지 부인조차 음악이 갑자기 그치자 잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이냐고 물었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 휘스트는 하실 줄 아시지요?” 홀콤 양이 내가 앉아 있는 자리에 의미심장하게 눈길을 던지며 물었다.\n\n나는 그 뜻을 알았다. 그녀가 옳다는 것도 알았기에, 나는 곧바로 일어나 카드 탁자로 갔다. 내가 피아노를 떠날 때 페얼리 양은 악보를 한 장 넘기고 아까보다 확실해진 손길로 다시 건반을 짚었다.\n\n“꼭 칠 거예요.” 그녀는 거의 격정적으로 건반을 때리며 말했다. “마지막 밤이니 꼭 칠 거예요.”\n\n“베지 부인, 이리 오세요.” 홀콤 양이 말했다. “길모어 씨와 저는 에카르테에 싫증이 났어요. 오셔서 하트라이트 씨의 휘스트 짝이 되어 주세요.”\n\n늙은 변호사가 빈정거리듯 웃었다. 이기고 있던 쪽은 그였고, 방금 킹까지 뒤집어 낸 참이었다. 그는 홀콤 양이 카드 탁자의 자리 배치를 불쑥 바꾼 것을 지고 있는 판을 견디지 못하는 여자의 성미 탓으로 돌린 것이 분명했다.\n\n그날 저녁 남은 시간은 그녀에게서 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 받지 못한 채 흘러갔다. 그녀는 피아노 앞자리를 지켰고 나는 카드 탁자 앞자리를 지켰다. 그녀는 쉬지 않고 연주했다. 마치 음악만이 자기 자신에게서 벗어날 유일한 피난처인 듯이. 어떤 때 그녀의 손가락은 머뭇거리는 애틋함으로, 부드럽고 애처롭고 스러져 가는 다정함으로 음을 어루만졌는데, 그 소리는 말로 다할 수 없이 아름답고도 구슬펐다. 또 어떤 때는 손가락이 흔들리며 그녀를 저버리거나, 그 일이 짐이라도 되는 듯 기계적으로 악기 위를 서둘러 지나갔다. 그러나 음악에 실어 보내는 표정이 아무리 변하고 흔들려도, 연주를 계속하겠다는 결심만은 결코 흔들리지 않았다. 그녀가 피아노에서 일어난 것은 우리 모두가 작별 인사를 하려고 일어섰을 때뿐이었다.\n\n문에 가장 가까이 있던 베지 부인이 제일 먼저 내게 악수를 청했다.\n\n“이제 다시 뵙지 못하겠군요, 하트라이트 씨.” 노부인이 말했다. “떠나신다니 참으로 섭섭합니다. 그동안 무척 친절하고 자상하셨어요. 나 같은 늙은이는 친절과 자상함을 알아본답니다. 부디 행복하시기를 빕니다. 부디 다정한 작별이 되기를 빕니다.”\n\n그다음은 길모어 씨였다.\n\n“앞으로 더 가까이 지낼 기회가 있기를 바랍니다, 하트라이트 씨. 그 자잘한 일거리가 제 손에 안전히 맡겨졌다는 것은 잘 아시지요? 그럼요, 그럼요, 물론이지요. 이런, 어찌 이리 춥습니까! 문간에 오래 붙들어 두어서는 안 되겠군요. 봉 보야주입니다, 선생. 프랑스 사람들 말마따나 봉 보야주.”\n\n이어 홀콤 양이었다.\n\n“내일 아침 일곱 시 반이에요.” 그녀는 그렇게 말하더니 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. “저는 당신이 생각하시는 것보다 많이 듣고 많이 보았어요. 오늘 밤 당신의 처신이 저를 평생 당신의 친구로 만들었습니다.”\n\n페얼리 양이 마지막이었다. 그녀의 손을 잡을 때, 그리고 이튿날 아침을 떠올릴 때, 나는 그녀를 차마 바라볼 자신이 없었다.\n\n“제가 아주 이른 시각에 떠나야 합니다.” 내가 말했다. “페얼리 양, 저는 당신이 아직……”\n\n“아니에요, 아니에요.” 그녀가 서둘러 말을 가로막았다. “제가 방에서 나오기 전은 아니에요. 저도 매리언과 함께 아침 식사에 내려올 거예요. 저는 그렇게 은혜를 모르지도, 지난 세 달을 그렇게 쉬이 잊지도……”\n\n목소리가 끊겼고, 그녀의 손이 내 손을 부드럽게 감쌌다가 이내 툭 놓았다. 내가 “안녕히 주무세요”라고 말할 새도 없이 그녀는 사라졌다.\n\n끝은 빠르게 나를 향해 다가온다. 리머리지 하우스에서 맞은 마지막 아침의 빛이 그러했듯, 피할 길 없이 다가온다.\n\n내가 아래층으로 내려간 것은 겨우 일곱 시 반이었으나, 두 사람은 이미 아침 식탁에서 나를 기다리고 있었다. 싸늘한 공기 속에서, 어스름한 빛 속에서, 집 안의 침울한 아침 정적 속에서 우리 셋은 함께 앉아 먹으려 애쓰고 이야기하려 애썼다. 겉모습을 지켜 내려는 싸움은 가망도 소용도 없었으므로, 나는 그것을 끝내려고 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n내가 손을 내밀고 가장 가까이 있던 홀콤 양이 그 손을 잡는 순간, 페얼리 양이 홱 돌아서더니 서둘러 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n“그편이 낫습니다.” 문이 닫히자 홀콤 양이 말했다. “당신에게나 저 아이에게나 그편이 나아요.”\n\n나는 말을 꺼내기까지 잠시 기다려야 했다. 작별의 말 한마디, 작별의 눈길 한 번 없이 그녀를 잃는 것은 견디기 힘든 일이었다. 나는 스스로를 다잡았다. 홀콤 양에게 격에 맞는 말로 작별을 고하려 애썼으나, 기꺼이 하고 싶었던 그 모든 이별의 말이 한 문장으로 오그라들고 말았다.\n\n“제가 편지를 받을 자격이 있겠습니까?” 내가 할 수 있는 말이라고는 그것뿐이었다.\n\n“당신은 우리 두 사람이 살아 있는 한 제가 해 드릴 수 있는 모든 것을 받을 자격을 훌륭하게 얻으셨어요. 끝이 어떻게 되든 반드시 알려 드리겠습니다.”\n\n“그리고 언젠가 다시 제 도움이 필요할 때가 온다면, 제 주제넘음과 어리석음에 대한 기억이 잊힌 지 한참 뒤라도……”\n\n나는 더 말을 이을 수 없었다. 목소리가 떨렸고, 나도 모르게 눈시울이 젖었다.\n\n그녀가 두 손으로 내 손을 붙잡더니 남자처럼 힘 있고 굳건한 악력으로 그것을 꽉 쥐었다. 검은 눈이 반짝였고, 볕에 그은 얼굴빛이 짙게 달아올랐다. 그녀의 관대함과 연민에서 나온 맑은 내면의 빛으로 그 얼굴의 힘과 기운이 빛나며 아름다워졌다.\n\n“저는 당신을 믿겠어요. 언젠가 그런 때가 온다면 저는 당신을 제 친구이자 저 아이의 친구로, 제 오라비이자 저 아이의 오라비로 믿겠어요.” 그녀는 말을 멈추더니 나를 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그 두려움 없고 고결한 사람은 누이가 그러하듯 입술로 내 이마에 입을 맞추고 나를 세례명으로 불렀다. “신의 가호가 있기를, 월터!” 그녀가 말했다. “여기 혼자 남아 마음을 가라앉히세요. 우리 둘 다를 위해 제가 여기 있지 않는 편이 낫겠어요. 위층 발코니에서 당신이 가는 것을 보는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n그녀는 방을 나갔다. 나는 창 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 그곳에는 쓸쓸한 가을 풍경 말고는 나를 마주할 것이 아무것도 없었다. 나 역시 내 차례가 되어 그 방을 나서기 전에, 그리하여 영영 그 방을 떠나기 전에 스스로를 다스리려고 돌아선 것이었다.\n\n일 분이 지났을까, 그보다 길지는 않았을 것이다. 문이 다시 살며시 열리는 소리가 들렸고, 카펫 위로 여자 옷자락이 스치는 소리가 내 쪽으로 다가왔다. 돌아서는 나의 심장이 사납게 뛰었다. 페얼리 양이 방 저쪽 끝에서 내게로 다가오고 있었다.\n\n우리 눈이 마주쳤을 때, 그리고 우리 둘뿐임을 알았을 때 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 머뭇거렸다. 그러더니 여자들이 사소한 위급에는 그토록 자주 잃으면서도 큰 위급에는 좀처럼 잃지 않는 그 용기로, 이상하리만치 창백하고 이상하리만치 조용한 얼굴로 내게 더 가까이 다가왔다. 한 손은 걸어가는 길에 놓인 탁자를 따라 끌듯 짚었고, 다른 손에는 무언가를 옆구리께에 들고 있었는데 그것은 옷자락 주름에 가려져 있었다.\n\n“이걸 찾으러 응접실에 잠깐 다녀왔어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “여기 머무셨던 일과, 뒤에 남겨 두고 가시는 벗들을 떠올리게 해 줄지도 모르니까요. 제가 이걸 그렸을 때 아주 많이 늘었다고 말씀해 주셨잖아요. 그래서 마음에 드실지도 모른다고 생각해서……”\n\n그녀는 고개를 돌리며 작은 스케치 한 장을 내밀었다. 우리가 처음 만났던 그 정자를 그녀가 처음부터 끝까지 제 손으로 그린 것이었다. 그녀가 그것을 내밀 때 그 손에서 종이가 떨렸고, 내가 그것을 건네받을 때 내 손에서도 떨렸다.\n\n내가 느끼는 바를 말하기가 두려워, 나는 이렇게만 대답했다. “이것은 결코 제 곁을 떠나지 않을 겁니다. 평생토록 제가 가장 소중히 여기는 보물이 될 겁니다. 참으로 감사합니다. 작별 인사도 없이 저를 보내지 않으신 것도 참으로 감사합니다.”\n\n“어머!” 그녀가 순진하게 말했다. “그토록 행복한 날들을 함께 보내고서, 제가 어떻게 그냥 보내 드릴 수 있겠어요!”\n\n“그런 날들은 다시 오지 않을지도 모릅니다, 페얼리 양. 제가 사는 길과 당신이 사는 길은 아주 멀리 떨어져 있으니까요. 그러나 만일 제 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다 바친 헌신이 당신에게 한순간의 행복을 드리거나 한순간의 슬픔을 덜어 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 당신을 가르쳤던 이 가난한 그림 선생을 기억해 주시겠습니까? 홀콤 양은 저를 믿겠노라 약속해 주셨습니다. 당신도 약속해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그 다정한 푸른 눈에 어린 작별의 슬픔이 차오르는 눈물 너머로 흐릿하게 빛났다.\n\n“약속할게요.” 그녀는 끊어지는 목소리로 말했다. “아, 저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요! 온 마음을 다해 약속해요.”\n\n나는 조금 더 그녀에게 다가서서 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“페얼리 양, 당신을 사랑하는 벗이 많습니다. 당신의 행복한 앞날은 숱한 소망들이 품은 소중한 바람입니다. 헤어지면서 이 말을 드려도 될까요. 그것은 제 소망이 품은 소중한 바람이기도 하다고요.”\n\n눈물이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 빠르게 흘러내렸다. 그녀는 떨리는 한 손을 탁자에 짚어 몸을 지탱하며 다른 손을 내게 내밀었다. 나는 그 손을 내 손에 받아 꼭 붙들었다. 내 머리가 그 위로 수그러졌고, 내 눈물이 그 위에 떨어졌으며, 내 입술이 그 손을 눌렀다. 사랑에서가 아니었다. 아, 그 마지막 순간에 사랑에서가 아니라, 절망의 고통과 자포자기에서였다.\n\n“제발 부탁이니, 저를 두고 가세요!” 그녀가 힘없이 말했다.\n\n애원하는 그 말과 함께 그녀의 가슴에 숨은 비밀이 터져 나왔다. 나에게는 그 말을 들을 권리도, 그 말에 답할 권리도 없었다. 그것은 그녀의 신성한 연약함의 이름으로 나를 그 방에서 내쫓는 말이었다. 모든 것이 끝났다. 나는 그녀의 손을 놓았고, 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 앞을 가리는 눈물이 그녀를 내 시야에서 지워 버렸으므로, 나는 마지막으로 그녀를 보려고 눈물을 훔쳐 냈다. 그녀가 의자에 주저앉으며 두 팔을 탁자 위로 떨어뜨리고 그 고운 머리를 그 위에 지쳐 떨구는 모습을 한 번 보았다. 작별의 눈길 한 번, 그리고 문은 그녀를 가리며 닫혔다. 우리 사이에는 이별의 커다란 심연이 열렸고, 로라 페얼리의 모습은 이미 지나간 날의 기억이 되어 있었다.\n\n하트라이트의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다.\n\n(챈서리 레인의 사무변호사)\n\n# I\n\n나는 벗인 월터 하트라이트 씨의 요청으로 이 글을 쓴다. 이 글은 페얼리 양의 이해관계에 중대한 영향을 미친 어떤 사건들, 곧 하트라이트 씨가 리머리지 하우스를 떠난 뒤에 일어난 사건들을 기술하기 위한 것이다.\n\n내 서술이 중요한 한 부분을 이루는 저 기이한 집안 이야기를 세상에 내놓는 일에 대해, 내 개인적 견해가 찬성인지 반대인지는 여기서 말할 필요가 없다. 하트라이트 씨가 그 책임을 스스로 떠맡았고, 앞으로 이야기될 사정들이 밝혀 주겠지만 그는 그럴 권리를 충분히 얻었으므로, 그 권리를 행사하겠다면 그것으로 족하다. 이 이야기를 가장 진실하고 가장 생생한 방식으로 남에게 전하기 위해 그가 택한 방침은, 사건이 진행되는 각 단계마다 그 당시 그 사건에 직접 관계했던 사람들이 저마다 그 대목을 이야기하도록 하는 것이다. 내가 여기 서술자로 등장하는 것은 그 방침에서 나온 필연적인 결과다. 나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 컴벌랜드에 머무는 동안 그 자리에 있었고, 그가 페얼리 씨의 지붕 아래 짧게 체류한 결과 생긴 중요한 일 하나에 직접 관여했다. 따라서 이 사건의 사슬에 새 고리를 몇 개 잇고, 하트라이트 씨가 일단은 놓아 둔 그 지점에서 사슬 자체를 이어받는 것이 내 소임이다.\n\n나는 십일월 이일 금요일에 리머리지 하우스에 도착했다.\n\n내 목적은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 올 때까지 페얼리 씨 댁에 머무는 것이었다. 그의 방문으로 퍼시벌 경과 페얼리 양의 혼인 날짜가 정해진다면, 나는 필요한 지시를 받아 런던으로 돌아가 그 아가씨의 혼인 계약서를 작성하는 일에 착수할 참이었다.\n\n금요일에는 페얼리 씨가 나를 만나 주는 호의를 베풀지 않았다. 그는 여러 해 전부터 병약한 몸이었거나 스스로 그렇다고 여겨 왔는데, 그날은 나를 맞을 만큼 건강하지 못했던 것이다. 내가 처음 만난 집안사람은 홀콤 양이었다. 그녀는 현관에서 나를 맞았고, 얼마 전부터 리머리지에 머물고 있던 하트라이트 씨를 소개해 주었다.\n\n페얼리 양은 그날 늦게, 만찬 자리에서야 보았다. 안색이 좋지 않아 보여 나는 마음이 아팠다. 그녀는 상냥하고 사랑스러운 아가씨로, 훌륭했던 어머니가 그러했듯 주위 사람 모두에게 다정하고 세심하다. 다만 생김새로 말하자면 아버지를 닮았다. 페얼리 부인은 눈과 머리칼이 검었는데, 그 맏딸인 홀콤 양이 어머니를 몹시 닮았다. 페얼리 양은 저녁에 우리에게 연주를 들려주었으나, 내 생각에는 평소만 못했다. 우리는 휘스트를 한 판 쳤는데, 승부로 보자면 그 고귀한 놀이에 대한 모독이나 다름없었다. 나는 하트라이트 씨를 처음 소개받았을 때 좋은 인상을 받았으나, 그가 제 나이에 따르는 사교상의 결점에서 자유롭지 못하다는 것을 곧 알아차렸다. 요즘 세대의 젊은이들이 하지 못하는 것이 세 가지 있다. 그들은 술잔을 앞에 두고 진득하게 앉아 있지 못하고, 휘스트를 칠 줄 모르며, 여자에게 인사치레 한마디 건네지 못한다. 하트라이트 씨도 이 일반 법칙의 예외는 아니었다. 그 점만 빼면, 그 초기의 짧은 사귐 속에서도 그는 겸손하고 신사다운 청년으로 보였다.\n\n그렇게 금요일이 지나갔다. 그날 내 주의를 끈 더 심각한 일들, 곧 페얼리 양에게 온 익명의 편지와, 그 일을 전해 듣고 내가 취하는 것이 옳다고 판단한 조치와, 그 정황에 대해 있을 수 있는 모든 해명을 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 기꺼이 내놓으리라던 내 확신에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 내가 알기로 이 앞의 서술에서 이미 남김없이 다루어졌기 때문이다.\n\n토요일에는 내가 아침을 먹으러 내려가기 전에 하트라이트 씨가 이미 떠나고 없었다. 페얼리 양은 온종일 방에 틀어박혀 있었고, 홀콤 양은 내가 보기에 기운이 없어 보였다. 이 집은 필립 페얼리 부부가 계시던 시절의 그 집이 아니었다. 나는 오전에 혼자 산책을 나가, 삼십 년도 더 전에 집안일을 처리하러 리머리지에 머물 적에 처음 보았던 곳들을 둘러보았다. 그곳들 또한 예전의 그곳들이 아니었다.\n\n두 시에 페얼리 씨가 나를 만날 만큼 몸이 낫다고 사람을 보내왔다. 적어도 그만은 내가 처음 알았을 때와 조금도 달라지지 않았다. 그의 이야기는 늘 그렇듯 한결같은 것이었다. 온통 자기 자신과 자기 병, 그 훌륭하다는 주화들, 견줄 데 없다는 렘브란트 동판화 이야기뿐이었다. 내가 이 집에 온 용건을 꺼내려 하자마자 그는 눈을 감고 내가 자기를 “심란하게 한다”고 말했다. 나는 그 주제로 자꾸만 되돌아가며 기어이 그를 심란하게 했다. 내가 확인할 수 있었던 것은 이뿐이었다. 그는 조카딸의 혼인을 이미 정해진 일로 보고 있고, 그 아이 아버지가 승낙했으며, 자기도 승낙했고, 바람직한 혼사이며, 이 성가신 일이 끝나면 개인적으로 기쁘겠다는 것이었다. 재산 설정에 관해서라면, 내가 조카딸과 상의하고, 그 다음에는 집안 사정에 대한 내 지식을 마음껏 깊이 파고들어, 모든 것을 준비해 놓은 뒤, 후견인으로서 그가 할 몫은 적당한 때에 “예”라고 말하는 것으로 한정해 준다면, 아무렴, 그는 무한한 기쁨으로 내 뜻에도 다른 누구의 뜻에도 따르겠다는 것이었다. 그러는 동안 보다시피 그는 방에 갇힌 무력한 환자였다. 내가 보기에 그가 시달림을 당해도 될 사람 같은가? 아니지. 그렇다면 어찌하여 그를 시달리게 하는가?\n\n후견인이라는 자리에 있으면서 자기 주장이라고는 이토록 놀랍도록 없는 페얼리 씨의 태도에, 나는 어쩌면 조금 놀랐을지도 모른다. 집안 사정에 대한 내 지식이, 그가 독신이며 리머리지 재산에 대해 종신 수익권 이상은 가지고 있지 않다는 사실을 일깨워 주지 않았다면 말이다. 사정이 그러했으므로 나는 그 면담의 결과에 놀라지도 실망하지도 않았다. 페얼리 씨는 그저 내 예상을 그대로 확인해 주었을 뿐이며, 그것으로 끝이었다.\n\n일요일은 바깥도 안도 따분한 날이었다. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 사무 변호사에게서 편지가 한 통 왔는데, 내가 보낸 익명 편지 사본과 사건 진술서를 잘 받았다는 회신이었다. 페얼리 양은 오후에 우리와 자리를 함께했는데, 창백하고 풀이 죽어 도무지 평소의 그녀 같지 않았다. 나는 그녀와 얼마간 이야기를 나누다가 조심스럽게 퍼시벌 경 이야기를 넌지시 꺼내 보았다. 그녀는 듣기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다른 화제는 무엇이든 기꺼이 이어 갔으나, 이 화제만은 그대로 흘려 버리게 두었다. 나는 그녀가 혹시 약혼을 후회하고 있는 것은 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다. 젊은 아가씨들이 흔히 그러듯, 후회가 너무 늦게 찾아온 경우처럼 말이다.\n\n월요일에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 도착했다.\n\n나는 그가 태도와 용모로 보자면 대단히 호감 가는 사람임을 알았다. 이마 위쪽이 벗어지고 얼굴에 다소 주름과 세월의 자국이 있어 내가 예상했던 것보다는 나이 들어 보였으나, 몸놀림은 젊은이처럼 활기찼고 기운도 젊은이 못지않았다. 홀콤 양과의 인사는 유쾌할 만큼 정답고 꾸밈이 없었으며, 내가 그에게 소개되었을 때 나를 맞는 태도도 어찌나 편안하고 기분 좋던지 우리는 오랜 친구처럼 어울렸다. 그가 도착했을 때 페얼리 양은 자리에 없었으나 십 분쯤 뒤에 방으로 들어왔다. 퍼시벌 경은 일어서서 더없이 우아하게 인사를 건넸다. 그 아가씨의 안색이 나빠진 것을 보고 그가 드러낸 걱정은 다정함과 존중이 뒤섞인 것이었고, 어조와 목소리와 몸가짐 어디에도 젠체하는 구석이 없이 섬세하여, 그의 좋은 가정교육과 좋은 분별력을 똑같이 잘 보여 주었다. 이런 마당에 페얼리 양이 그의 앞에서 여전히 서먹하고 불편해하며, 기회가 오자마자 다시 방을 나가 버리는 것을 보고 나는 다소 놀랐다. 퍼시벌 경은 그녀가 자기를 맞는 태도의 서먹함도, 우리 자리에서 갑자기 물러가는 것도 알은체하지 않았다. 그녀가 있는 동안 그는 자기 관심을 억지로 들이밀지 않았고, 그녀가 나간 뒤에도 그 일을 입에 올려 홀콤 양을 난처하게 하지 않았다. 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 그와 함께 지내는 동안, 이 일에서든 다른 어떤 일에서든 그의 요령과 안목은 한 번도 어긋나는 법이 없었다.\n\n페얼리 양이 방을 나가자마자 그는 익명의 편지 이야기를 스스로 꺼내어, 그 문제로 우리가 겪을 곤란을 모두 덜어 주었다. 그는 햄프셔에서 오는 길에 런던에 들러 사무 변호사를 만나고, 내가 보낸 서류를 읽었으며, 말로 전할 수 있는 가장 신속하고 가장 완전한 해명으로 우리 마음을 풀어 주고자 하는 마음에 컴벌랜드까지 곧장 왔다고 했다. 그가 그런 뜻을 밝히는 것을 듣고 나는 그가 살펴볼 수 있도록 간직해 두었던 편지 원본을 내밀었다. 그는 고맙다면서도 보기를 사양했다. 사본을 이미 보았으니 원본은 우리 손에 그대로 두어도 조금도 상관없다는 것이었다.\n\n그가 곧바로 들어간 그 진술 자체는, 내가 처음부터 그러리라고 예상했던 대로 단순하고 흡족했다.\n\n그의 말에 따르면, 캐서릭 부인은 지난 세월 동안 그의 집안과 그 자신에게 충실히 봉사하여 그로 하여금 얼마간 신세를 지게 한 사람이었다. 그녀는 두 겹으로 불행했다. 남편이라는 자가 그녀를 버리고 떠났고, 하나뿐인 아이는 아주 어릴 적부터 정신 기능에 이상이 있었다. 혼인으로 인해 그녀는 퍼시벌 경의 소유지가 있는 곳에서 멀리 떨어진 햄프셔의 한 지역으로 옮겨 갔으나, 그는 그녀를 놓치지 않으려고 마음을 써 왔다. 지난날의 봉사를 생각하여 그 딱한 여인에게 품은 우호적인 마음이, 재난을 견디는 그녀의 인내와 용기에 대한 감탄으로 크게 굳어졌기 때문이다. 세월이 흐르면서 그 불행한 딸의 정신 질환 증세는 심각해져, 마침내 제대로 된 의료의 보호 아래 두지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 캐서릭 부인 자신도 그 필요를 인정했으나, 동시에 그녀와 같은 점잖은 신분의 사람들이 흔히 갖는 편견, 곧 제 아이를 극빈자로 공립 정신병원에 들여보내는 데 대한 거부감도 느꼈다. 퍼시벌 경은 어느 신분에서든 정직한 자존심을 존중하듯 이 편견을 존중했고, 자신과 자기 집안의 이익에 일찍이 마음을 다해 준 캐서릭 부인에게 고마움을 표하는 뜻으로, 믿을 만한 사설 요양원에 딸을 두는 비용을 자기가 부담하기로 마음먹었다. 그런데 어머니로서도 유감이고 그 자신으로서도 유감스럽게도, 그 불행한 아이는 자기를 감호 아래 두는 데 정황상 그가 맡게 된 몫을 알아내고, 그 결과 그에 대해 더없이 강렬한 증오와 불신을 품게 되었다. 요양원 안에서 여러 모양으로 드러났던 그 증오와 불신에, 그녀가 탈출한 뒤에 쓴 그 익명의 편지도 분명히 돌릴 수 있다는 것이었다. 만일 홀콤 양이나 길모어 씨가 기억하는 그 문서의 내용이 이 견해와 맞지 않는다면, 혹은 그 요양원에 대해 더 자세한 사항을 알고 싶다면, 어떤 물음에도 답하고 어떤 의문도 풀어 줄 준비가 되어 있다고 했다. 그러면서 그는 요양원 주소는 물론, 환자를 입원시킬 때 소견서를 쓴 두 의사의 이름과 주소까지 일러 주었다. 그는 사무 변호사에게 비용을 아끼지 말고 그녀를 찾아내어 다시 의료의 보호 아래 돌려보내라고 지시함으로써 그 불행한 젊은 여인에 대한 자기 도리를 다했으며, 이제는 페얼리 양과 그 집안에 대한 도리도 똑같이 분명하고 솔직한 방식으로 다하기만을 바랄 뿐이라고 했다.\n\n이 호소에 가장 먼저 대답한 사람은 나였다. 내가 갈 길은 분명했다. 어떤 정황에서 어떤 형식으로 이루어진 인간의 진술이든 다툴 수 있다는 것이 법의 크나큰 아름다움이다. 만일 내가 직업상 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명 자체를 근거로 그에게 불리한 논거를 세워야 할 처지였다면, 나는 의심할 여지 없이 그렇게 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 내 소임은 그쪽에 있지 않았다. 내 구실은 순전히 재판관의 것이었다. 나는 방금 들은 해명을 저울에 달고, 그것을 내놓은 신사의 높은 평판에 마땅한 무게를 다 실어 준 뒤, 퍼시벌 경 자신의 진술로 볼 때 개연성이 명백히 그의 편인지 명백히 그의 반대편인지를 정직하게 판단해야 했다. 내 확신은 개연성이 명백히 그의 편이라는 것이었고, 따라서 나는 그의 해명이 내 생각에는 의문의 여지 없이 흡족하다고 선언했다.\n\n홀콤 양은 나를 아주 진지하게 바라본 뒤 자기 쪽에서도 같은 취지로 몇 마디 했다. 다만 어딘가 망설이는 기색이 있었는데, 내가 보기에 정황상 그럴 만한 까닭은 없었다. 퍼시벌 경이 그것을 알아챘는지 아닌지는 단언할 수 없다. 내 생각으로는 알아챈 듯한데, 이제는 예의에 조금도 어긋나지 않게 그 화제를 흘려보낼 수 있었을 텐데도 그가 일부러 그 이야기를 다시 꺼냈기 때문이다.\n\n“제가 사실을 있는 그대로 말씀드린 상대가 길모어 씨뿐이었다면,” 그가 말했다. “이 불행한 일에 대해 더 언급할 필요는 없다고 여기겠지요. 저는 길모어 씨가 신사로서 제 말을 믿어 주시리라 마땅히 기대할 수 있고, 그렇게 저를 공정히 대해 주신 이상 우리 사이에서 이 문제에 관한 논의는 끝난 것입니다. 그러나 숙녀를 대하는 제 처지는 그와 같지 않습니다. 저는 숙녀께는, 살아 있는 어떤 남자에게도 양보하지 않을 것을 빚지고 있습니다. 곧 제 주장이 진실이라는 증거 말입니다. 홀콤 양, 당신은 그 증거를 요구하실 수 없으니, 당신께, 그리고 더욱이 페얼리 양께 그것을 내놓는 것이 제 도리입니다. 부디 이 불행한 여인의 어머니에게, 곧 캐서릭 부인에게 지금 당장 편지를 써서, 제가 방금 드린 해명을 뒷받침할 증언을 청해 주시기를 부탁드립니다.”\n\n나는 홀콤 양의 낯빛이 달라지고 다소 불편해하는 것을 보았다. 정중하게 표현되기는 했으나 퍼시벌 경의 제안은, 내게 그렇게 보였듯 그녀에게도, 조금 전 그녀의 태도에 드러난 그 망설임을 아주 은근히 겨냥한 것으로 보였던 것이다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 제가 경을 믿지 못한다고 여기시어 저를 그렇게 부당하게 대하시지는 않으시겠지요.” 그녀가 얼른 말했다.\n\n“물론 그렇지 않습니다, 홀콤 양. 제 제안은 순전히 당신께 마음을 쓰는 뜻에서 드리는 것입니다. 그래도 굳이 이 청을 밀어붙이는 제 고집을 용서해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그는 말하면서 필기용 탁자로 걸어가 의자를 끌어다 놓고 편지지 상자를 열었다.\n\n“부디 그 쪽지를 써 주십시오.” 그가 말했다. “저를 위한 호의로 말입니다. 몇 분이면 될 일입니다. 캐서릭 부인에게 두 가지만 물으시면 됩니다. 첫째, 따님이 요양원에 들어간 것이 부인이 알고 승낙한 일이었는지. 둘째, 그 일에서 제가 맡은 몫이 부인의 감사를 받을 만한 것이었는지. 이 불쾌한 문제에 대해 길모어 씨의 마음은 편안하고, 당신의 마음도 편안합니다. 부디 그 쪽지를 써서 제 마음도 편안하게 해 주십시오.”\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 저로서는 거절하고 싶은 마음이 훨씬 큰데도 경께서 이렇게 청을 들어드리지 않을 수 없게 만드시는군요.”\n\n그 말과 함께 홀콤 양은 자리에서 일어나 필기용 탁자로 갔다. 퍼시벌 경은 고맙다고 하며 그녀에게 펜을 건네고는 벽난로 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 페얼리 양의 작은 이탈리아 그레이하운드가 깔개 위에 누워 있었다. 그는 손을 내밀며 기분 좋게 개를 불렀다.\n\n“이리 오너라, 니나.” 그가 말했다. “우리 서로 기억하고 있지, 그렇지 않으냐?”\n\n애완견들이 대개 그러하듯 겁 많고 심술궂은 그 작은 짐승은 그를 매섭게 올려다보더니, 내민 손에서 움츠러들며 낑낑거리고 부들부들 떨다가 소파 밑으로 숨어 버렸다. 개가 자기를 맞는 태도 따위의 사소한 일로 그가 언짢아졌을 리는 거의 없겠으나, 그럼에도 나는 그가 매우 갑작스레 창가 쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 보았다. 어쩌면 그는 이따금 성미가 급해지는 사람인지도 모른다. 그렇다면 나는 그 심정을 이해할 수 있다. 나 역시 이따금 성미가 급해지니까.\n\n홀콤 양이 쪽지를 쓰는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 다 쓰고 나자 그녀는 필기용 탁자에서 일어나 펼친 종이를 그대로 퍼시벌 경에게 건넸다. 그는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 그것을 받아 들더니, 내용을 들여다보지도 않고 곧바로 접어 봉하고 겉봉에 주소를 쓴 뒤 말없이 그녀에게 돌려주었다. 나는 평생 그보다 더 우아하고 더 품위 있게 처신하는 것을 본 적이 없다.\n\n“이 편지를 꼭 제가 부쳐야 한다는 말씀이지요, 퍼시벌 경?” 홀콤 양이 말했다.\n\n“부쳐 주시기를 청합니다.” 그가 대답했다. “그리고 이제 다 써서 봉했으니, 그 편지가 언급하는 그 불행한 여인에 대해 마지막으로 한두 가지만 여쭙게 해 주십시오. 익명 편지를 쓴 사람이 어떤 정황에서 밝혀졌는지를 적어 길모어 씨께서 친절히 제 사무 변호사에게 보내 주신 서한은 읽었습니다. 그런데 그 진술이 다루지 않은 점들이 몇 가지 있습니다. 앤 캐서릭이 페얼리 양을 보았습니까?”\n\n“결코 아닙니다.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다.\n\n“당신은 보았습니까?”\n\n“아니요.”\n\n“그러면 그녀는 이곳 교회 묘지에서 우연히 마주쳤다는 하트라이트 씨라는 분 말고는, 이 집안 사람 누구도 보지 못했다는 말씀이군요?”\n\n“아무도 보지 못했습니다.”\n\n“하트라이트 씨는 리머리지에서 그림 교사로 일하셨다지요? 수채화 협회 가운데 어느 한 곳의 회원입니까?”\n\n“그런 줄로 압니다.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다.\n\n그는 마지막 대답을 곱씹기라도 하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에 있는 동안 어디서 지냈는지는 알아내셨습니까?”\n\n“네. 황야에 있는 토드 코너라는 농가입니다.”\n\n“그 딱한 사람을 찾아내는 것은 우리 모두가 그 사람 자신에게 지고 있는 도리입니다.” 퍼시벌 경이 말을 이었다. “토드 코너에서 무슨 말이든 했다면 그것이 그녀를 찾는 데 도움이 될지도 모르지요. 혹시나 해서 제가 그리로 가서 알아보겠습니다. 그러는 동안, 저로서는 도저히 이 괴로운 이야기를 페얼리 양과 나눌 마음이 나지 않으니, 홀콤 양께서 부디 그분께 필요한 설명을 해 주시는 일을 맡아 주시기를 청합니다. 물론 그 쪽지의 답장을 받은 뒤로 미루시고요.”\n\n홀콤 양은 그의 청을 따르겠다고 약속했다. 그는 고맙다고 하고는 기분 좋게 고갯짓을 하고, 자기 방에 자리를 잡으러 우리 곁을 떠났다. 그가 문을 여는데 그 심술궂은 그레이하운드가 소파 밑에서 뾰족한 주둥이를 내밀고 그를 향해 짖어 대며 물려고 덤볐다.\n\n“오전 일을 훌륭히 해내셨습니다, 홀콤 양.” 우리 둘만 남자마자 내가 말했다. “걱정스러운 하루가 벌써 잘 마무리되었군요.”\n\n“네.” 그녀가 대답했다. “그렇겠지요. 선생님 마음이 흡족하시다니 저도 무척 기쁩니다.”\n\n“제 마음이라니요! 그 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 계신데 홀콤 양 마음도 당연히 편안하시겠지요?”\n\n“아, 그럼요. 어찌 안 그렇겠어요? 그럴 리 없다는 것쯤은 저도 압니다.” 그녀는 내게라기보다 자기 자신에게 말하듯 말을 이었다. “그래도 월터 하트라이트가 그 해명 자리에 있을 만큼, 그리고 제게 그 쪽지를 쓰라고 청하는 것을 들을 만큼 오래 여기 머물렀더라면 싶은 마음이 드는군요.”\n\n나는 이 마지막 말에 조금 놀랐고, 어쩌면 조금 언짢기도 했다.\n\n“사건들이 하트라이트 씨를 그 편지 일과 아주 특별하게 얽어 놓은 것은 사실입니다.” 내가 말했다. “여러 사정을 감안할 때 그가 대단히 조심스럽고 분별 있게 처신했다는 것도 기꺼이 인정합니다. 그러나 퍼시벌 경의 진술이 홀콤 양이나 제 마음에 미친 영향과 관련해, 그가 그 자리에 있었다 한들 무슨 쓸모 있는 영향을 끼칠 수 있었을지는 도무지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“그저 문득 든 생각이었어요.” 그녀가 멍하니 말했다. “논할 것도 없는 일입니다, 길모어 씨. 선생님의 경험이야말로 제가 바랄 수 있는 가장 좋은 길잡이여야 하고, 또 그러합니다.”\n\n그녀가 이렇게 두드러진 태도로 책임을 통째로 내 어깨에 떠넘기는 것이 나는 그리 마음에 들지 않았다. 페얼리 씨가 그랬다면 나는 놀라지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 단호하고 명석한 홀콤 양이야말로, 제 의견을 밝히기를 꺼리는 모습을 보이리라고는 세상에서 가장 상상하기 어려운 사람이었다.\n\n“아직도 어떤 의심이 마음에 걸리신다면,” 내가 말했다. “지금 곧 제게 말씀하시는 것이 어떻겠습니까? 솔직히 말씀해 보십시오. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 믿지 못할 이유라도 있으신 겁니까?”\n\n“전혀 없습니다.”\n\n“그의 해명에서 있을 법하지 않거나 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 데라도 보이십니까?”\n\n“그분이 그 해명이 진실이라는 증거까지 내놓으셨는데 제가 어떻게 그렇다고 하겠습니까? 길모어 씨, 그 여인의 어머니가 하는 증언보다 그분께 더 나은 증언이 있을 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“더 나은 것은 없지요. 홀콤 양이 보내신 문의 쪽지에 대한 답이 흡족하게 나온다면, 적어도 저로서는 퍼시벌 경의 벗 된 사람이 그에게 그 이상 무엇을 바랄 수 있을지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“그럼 쪽지를 부치고,” 그녀가 방을 나가려 일어서며 말했다. “답장이 올 때까지 이 이야기는 더 꺼내지 말기로 하지요. 제 망설임에는 아무 무게도 두지 마세요. 요즘 로라 일로 지나치게 마음을 졸였다는 것 말고는 달리 까닭을 댈 수가 없군요. 길모어 씨, 근심이란 우리 가운데 가장 굳센 사람도 흔들어 놓는 법이니까요.”\n\n그녀는 그 마지막 말을 하면서 본디 단단하던 목소리가 떨리는 채로 불쑥 나를 두고 가 버렸다. 예민하고 격렬하고 열정적인 천성, 이 하찮고 껍데기뿐인 시대에 만에 하나 있을까 말까 한 여인이다. 나는 그녀를 어릴 적부터 알아 왔고, 그녀가 자라는 동안 한두 번이 아닌 집안의 시련 속에서 시험받는 것을 보아 왔다. 그 오랜 경험 때문에 나는 여기 적은 정황에서 보인 그녀의 망설임에, 다른 여자였다면 결코 느끼지 않았을 무게를 두게 되었다. 불안해하거나 의심할 까닭이라고는 하나도 보이지 않았건만, 그럼에도 그녀는 나를 조금 불안하게, 조금 의심스럽게 만들어 놓았다. 젊었을 적이라면 나는 스스로도 이치에 닿지 않는 이 마음 상태에 짜증을 내며 안달했으리라. 나이가 든 지금은 더 잘 알기에, 달관한 마음으로 밖에 나가 걸으며 그것을 털어 버렸다.\n\n우리 일행은 저녁 식사 시간에 다시 모두 한자리에 모였다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 어찌나 떠들썩하게 들떠 있었던지, 그날 아침 면담에서 조용한 재치와 세련됨과 분별력으로 내게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던 바로 그 사람이라고는 좀처럼 알아보기 어려울 지경이었다. 내가 알아챌 수 있었던 예전 모습의 유일한 흔적은 페얼리 양을 대하는 태도에서 이따금 되살아났다. 그녀의 눈짓 한 번, 말 한마디에 그의 가장 큰 웃음소리가 멎었고 가장 흥겨운 이야기의 흐름이 끊겼으며, 그는 순식간에 식탁의 다른 누구도 아닌 오직 그녀에게만 온 정신을 쏟았다. 그는 결코 대놓고 그녀를 대화에 끌어들이려 하지 않았으나, 그녀가 무심코 대화에 흘러들 만한 아주 작은 기회조차 놓치는 법이 없었고, 그렇게 마련된 유리한 상황에서 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. 재치와 섬세함이 덜한 남자였다면 생각나는 즉시 대놓고 그녀에게 늘어놓았을 그런 말들이었다. 다소 뜻밖이었던 것은, 페얼리 양이 그의 배려를 알아차리면서도 그것에 마음이 움직이지는 않는 듯 보였다는 점이다. 그가 자신을 바라보거나 말을 걸 때면 그녀는 이따금 조금 당황했으나, 결코 그를 향해 마음이 따뜻해지지는 않았다. 지위와 재산, 훌륭한 가문 교육과 준수한 용모, 신사의 존중과 연인의 헌신이 모두 겸손하게 그녀의 발치에 놓였건만, 겉으로 드러난 바로는 그 모두가 헛되이 바쳐지고 있었다.\n\n이튿날, 곧 화요일 아침에 퍼시벌 경은 (하인 하나를 길잡이로 데리고) 토드 코너로 갔다. 뒤에 들은 바로는 그의 탐문은 아무런 성과도 얻지 못했다. 돌아와서는 페얼리 씨와 면담했고, 오후에는 홀콤 양과 함께 말을 타고 나갔다. 그 밖에 기록할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다. 저녁은 여느 때처럼 지나갔다. 퍼시벌 경에게도 변화가 없었고, 페얼리 양에게도 변화가 없었다.\n\n수요일 우편이 하나의 사건을 실어 왔다 — 캐서릭 부인의 답장이었다. 나는 그 문서를 베껴 두어 지금까지 보관해 왔으니, 이 자리에 그대로 옮겨 두는 편이 좋겠다. 내용은 다음과 같았다 —\n\n“부인께. 제 딸 앤이 저의 인지와 승낙 아래 의사의 관리에 맡겨졌는지, 또 그 일에서 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 맡으신 몫이 그분께 감사의 뜻을 표할 만한 것이었는지를 문의하신 귀하의 서한을 받았음을 알려 드립니다. 그 두 가지 물음에 모두 그렇다는 저의 대답을 받아 주시기 바라오며, 귀하의 충실한 종으로 남겠습니다.\n\n“제인 앤 캐서릭.”\n\n짧고 날카롭고 요점만 담긴 글이었다. 형식으로 보자면 여자가 쓴 것치고는 제법 사무적인 편지였고, 내용으로 보자면 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 진술에 대해 바랄 수 있는 한 가장 명백한 확인이었다. 이것이 내 견해였고, 몇 가지 사소한 유보를 달기는 했으나 홀콤 양의 견해이기도 했다. 퍼시벌 경은 편지를 보여 주었을 때 그 날카롭고 짤막한 어조에 별로 놀라는 기색이 없었다. 그는 캐서릭 부인이 말수가 적은 여자이며, 머리가 맑고 솔직하며 상상력이라고는 없는 사람이어서, 말하는 그대로 짧고 평범하게 쓴다고 우리에게 일러 주었다.\n\n답장을 받은 이상 다음으로 해치워야 할 일은 페얼리 양에게 퍼시벌 경의 해명을 알리는 것이었다. 홀콤 양이 그 일을 맡기로 하고 동생에게 가려고 방을 나섰는데, 갑자기 다시 돌아오더니 내가 신문을 읽고 있던 안락의자 옆에 앉았다. 퍼시벌 경은 마구간을 보러 조금 전에 나간 뒤였고, 방에는 우리 둘밖에 없었다.\n\n“우리가 정말이지 할 수 있는 일은 다 한 것이겠지요?” 그녀가 캐서릭 부인의 편지를 손안에서 이리저리 돌리고 접으며 말했다.\n\n“우리가 퍼시벌 경을 알고 믿는 그의 친구라면, 필요한 일은 다 했고 그 이상까지 했습니다.” 나는 되살아난 그녀의 망설임에 조금 짜증이 나서 대답했다. “그러나 우리가 그를 의심하는 적이라면——”\n\n“그런 쪽은 생각조차 할 수 없습니다.” 그녀가 말을 가로챘다. “우리는 퍼시벌 경의 친구예요. 그리고 너그러움과 참을성이 그분에 대한 우리의 존중을 더해 준다면, 우리는 퍼시벌 경을 흠모하는 사람이기까지 해야 마땅하겠지요. 그분이 어제 페얼리 씨를 만났고, 그 뒤에 저와 함께 나갔다는 건 아시지요.”\n\n“그렇습니다. 두 분이 함께 말을 타고 떠나는 것을 보았지요.”\n\n“우리는 앤 캐서릭 이야기로, 그리고 하트라이트 씨가 그 여자와 마주친 기이한 정황 이야기로 승마를 시작했어요. 하지만 곧 그 화제를 접었고, 다음으로 퍼시벌 경이 로라와의 약혼에 대해 더없이 사심 없는 말투로 이야기했지요. 그분은 로라가 기운이 없어 보인다는 것을 알아차렸고, 달리 듣는 바가 없다면 이번 방문 동안 자기를 대하는 태도가 달라진 것도 그 탓으로 돌릴 생각이라고 했어요. 그러나 그 변화에 더 심각한 이유가 있다면, 페얼리 씨든 저든 로라의 마음에 어떤 강요도 하지 말아 달라고 간청하겠다는 겁니다. 그런 경우에 그분이 바라는 것이라고는, 두 사람 사이의 약혼이 어떤 정황에서 맺어졌는지, 또 구애가 시작된 때부터 지금까지 자신의 처신이 어떠했는지를 로라가 마지막으로 한 번 되새겨 달라는 것뿐이었어요. 그 두 가지를 충분히 헤아려 본 뒤에도 로라가 자기 남편이 되는 영예에 대한 권리 주장을 거두어 달라고 진심으로 바란다면 — 그리고 그 말을 로라 자신의 입으로 분명히 해 준다면 — 자기를 희생해서 로라가 약혼에서 완전히 자유로이 물러날 수 있게 하겠다고 했어요.”\n\n“그보다 더 말할 수 있는 남자는 없습니다, 홀콤 양. 제 경험에 비추어 보건대 그런 처지에 놓인 남자 가운데 그만큼 말할 사람도 몇 되지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n내가 그 말을 마치자 그녀는 잠시 말이 없었고, 곤혹과 괴로움이 뒤섞인 묘한 표정으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“저는 아무도 고발하지 않고, 아무것도 의심하지 않습니다.” 그녀가 불쑥 입을 열었다. “하지만 로라를 이 혼인으로 설득하는 책임만은 질 수 없고, 지지도 않겠어요.”\n\n“그것이야말로 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 본인이 당신께 취해 달라고 청한 태도가 아닙니까.” 나는 놀라서 대답했다. “그분은 동생분의 마음을 억지로 몰아가지 말아 달라고 부탁했지요.”\n\n“그런데 제가 그 전언을 로라에게 전하면, 그분은 저더러 에둘러 그 마음을 억지로 몰아가게 만드는 셈이에요.”\n\n“어떻게 그럴 수가 있습니까?”\n\n“길모어 씨, 로라에 대해 아시는 바를 되짚어 보세요. 제가 로라에게 약혼의 정황을 되새겨 보라고 말한다면, 저는 그 아이 천성 가운데 가장 강한 두 가지 감정에 대뜸 호소하는 셈이 됩니다 — 아버지에 대한 추억을 향한 사랑과, 진실을 지키려는 엄격한 마음 말이에요. 그 아이가 평생 약속을 어긴 적이 한 번도 없다는 걸 아시잖아요. 아버지의 병이 치명적으로 접어들던 무렵에 이 약혼을 맺었다는 것도, 그 아버지가 임종의 자리에서 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과의 혼인을 희망에 차서 기쁘게 이야기했다는 것도 아시잖아요.”\n\n고백하건대 나는 사태를 그렇게 보는 시각에 조금 충격을 받았다.\n\n“설마,” 내가 말했다. “퍼시벌 경이 어제 당신에게 그런 말을 할 때 방금 말씀하신 그런 결과를 노리고 계산했다는 뜻은 아니겠지요?”\n\n그녀의 솔직하고 두려움 없는 얼굴이 입을 열기도 전에 대답을 대신했다.\n\n“제가 그만한 비열함을 의심하는 남자와 한순간이라도 같이 있을 사람으로 보이세요?” 그녀가 성난 목소리로 물었다.\n\n그런 식으로 그녀의 진심 어린 분노가 나를 향해 번뜩이는 것이 나는 좋았다. 내 직업에서는 악의는 그토록 많이 보면서 분노는 그토록 적게 보는 법이다.\n\n“그렇다면,” 내가 말했다. “실례를 무릅쓰고 우리 법률가의 말투로 말씀드리자면, 당신은 지금 사건 기록 밖으로 나가 계십니다. 결과가 어떻게 되든, 퍼시벌 경에게는 동생분이 약혼의 파기를 요구하기 전에 그 약혼을 온갖 합리적인 관점에서 신중히 헤아려 주기를 기대할 권리가 있습니다. 그 불운한 편지가 동생분에게 그분에 대한 편견을 심어 놓았다면, 당장 가서 그분이 당신 눈에도 내 눈에도 결백을 밝혔다고 일러 주십시오. 그러고 나서 동생분이 그분에게 무슨 이의를 제기할 수 있겠습니까? 이 년도 더 전에 사실상 남편으로 받아들인 남자에 대해 마음을 바꾼다면, 대체 무슨 변명이 있을 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“법과 이치의 눈으로 보면, 길모어 씨, 아무 변명도 없겠지요. 그래도 로라가 여전히 망설이고 저 또한 여전히 망설인다면, 우리의 이 이상한 처신을 좋으실 대로 양쪽 다 변덕 탓으로 돌리셔도 좋습니다. 그런 비난이야 우리가 견딜 수 있는 만큼 견뎌야겠지요.”\n\n그 말을 남기고 그녀는 갑자기 일어나 나를 두고 나갔다. 분별 있는 여자가 진지한 질문을 받고 가벼운 대답으로 그것을 피해 갈 때는, 백에 아흔아홉은 무언가 감추고 있다는 확실한 표시다. 나는 다시 신문을 읽기 시작하면서, 홀콤 양과 페얼리 양 사이에 퍼시벌 경에게도 나에게도 숨기고 있는 비밀이 있으리라고 강하게 의심했다. 그것은 우리 두 사람 모두에게, 특히 퍼시벌 경에게 가혹한 일이라고 나는 생각했다.\n\n나의 의심은 — 더 정확히 말하자면 나의 확신은 — 그날 늦게 홀콤 양을 다시 만났을 때 그녀의 말투와 태도로 확인되었다. 그녀는 동생과의 면담 결과를 내게 전하면서 수상쩍을 만큼 짧고 조심스러웠다. 들어 보니 페얼리 양은 편지에 얽힌 일을 올바른 관점에서 설명해 주는 동안 조용히 듣고 있었으나, 홀콤 양이 이어서 퍼시벌 경이 리머리지를 찾아온 목적은 혼례 날짜를 정하도록 설득하려는 것이라고 말하자, 시간을 달라고 애원하며 그 화제에 관한 이야기를 더는 못 하게 막아 버렸다는 것이다. 퍼시벌 경이 당분간 자기를 놓아주는 데 동의해 준다면 올해가 가기 전에 최종 답을 드리겠노라고 했다. 그녀가 어찌나 불안해하고 동요하며 이 유예를 간청했던지, 홀콤 양은 필요하다면 자기 영향력을 써서 그것을 얻어 주겠다고 약속했고, 페얼리 양의 간곡한 애원에 따라 혼인 문제에 관한 논의는 거기서 모두 끝나고 말았다.\n\n이렇게 제안된 순전히 일시적인 방편은 그 젊은 아가씨에게는 꽤 편했을지 몰라도, 이 글을 쓰는 사람에게는 다소 난감한 것으로 드러났다. 그날 아침 우편이 동업자의 편지를 가져왔는데, 그 때문에 나는 이튿날 오후 기차로 시내에 돌아가지 않으면 안 되었다. 올해가 다 가도록 리머리지 하우스를 다시 찾아올 기회는 없을 공산이 아주 컸다. 그렇게 되면, 설령 페얼리 양이 끝내 약혼을 지키기로 마음먹는다 해도, 재산 설정 증서를 작성하기 전에 반드시 거쳐야 할 본인과의 대면 협의가 사실상 불가능에 가까워지고, 마땅히 양쪽이 말로 주고받아야 할 문제들을 글로 적어 오갈 수밖에 없게 될 터였다. 나는 유예 요청에 관해 퍼시벌 경의 의향을 물을 때까지는 이 난처함에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 그런 청을 즉시 들어주지 않을 만큼 무례한 신사가 아니었다. 홀콤 양이 그 사실을 알려 주자 나는 리머리지를 떠나기 전에 반드시 동생분과 이야기를 나누어야겠다고 말했고, 그리하여 이튿날 아침 페얼리 양의 거실에서 그녀를 만나기로 정해졌다. 그녀는 만찬에 내려오지도, 저녁에 우리와 어울리지도 않았다. 몸이 편치 않다는 것이 구실이었고, 그 말을 들었을 때 퍼시벌 경은 그럴 만도 하게 조금 언짢은 얼굴이었다고 나는 생각했다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 조반이 끝나자마자 나는 페얼리 양의 거실로 올라갔다. 딱한 아가씨는 어찌나 창백하고 슬퍼 보이던지, 또 어찌나 선뜻 곱게 나를 맞으러 나오던지, 계단을 올라오는 내내 다듬어 온 결심 — 그 변덕과 우유부단함을 훈계하겠다는 결심 — 이 그 자리에서 허물어지고 말았다. 나는 그녀를 일어섰던 의자로 도로 데려가 앉히고 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다. 성미 까다로운 애완 그레이하운드가 방 안에 있었으므로 나는 짖고 물어뜯는 환영을 단단히 각오했다. 그런데 이상하게도 그 변덕스러운 작은 짐승은 내가 앉자마자 무릎으로 뛰어올라 뾰족한 주둥이를 내 손에 스스럼없이 들이밀며 내 예상을 배반했다.\n\n“얘야, 너는 어릴 적에 곧잘 내 무릎에 앉곤 했지.” 내가 말했다. “그런데 이제는 네 작은 개가 그 빈 옥좌를 물려받기로 작정한 모양이구나. 저 예쁜 그림은 네가 그린 것이냐?”\n\n나는 그녀 옆 탁자에 놓인 작은 화첩을 가리켰다. 내가 들어왔을 때 그녀가 들여다보고 있던 것이 분명했다. 펼쳐진 쪽에는 작은 수채 풍경화가 아주 정갈하게 붙어 있었다. 내 질문을 이끌어 낸 것은 바로 그 그림이었다 — 참으로 부질없는 질문이었으나, 입을 떼자마자 어찌 그 아이에게 사무 이야기부터 꺼낼 수 있었겠는가?\n\n“아니에요.” 그녀는 그림에서 눈을 돌리며 다소 당황한 듯 말했다. “제가 그린 게 아니에요.”\n\n그녀의 손가락에는 어릴 적부터 내가 기억하는 안절부절못하는 버릇이 있었다. 누군가 자기에게 말을 걸면 언제나 손에 잡히는 첫 물건을 만지작거리는 버릇이었다. 이번에는 그 손가락이 화첩으로 옮겨 가더니 작은 수채화의 가장자리를 무심히 어루만졌다. 얼굴에는 우울한 빛이 짙어졌다. 그녀는 그림을 보지도, 나를 보지도 않았다. 눈길이 방 안의 이 물건 저 물건으로 불안하게 옮겨 다니며, 내가 이야기하러 온 목적이 무엇인지 짐작하고 있음을 훤히 드러냈다. 그것을 보고 나는 되도록 지체 없이 본론으로 들어가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다.\n\n“얘야, 내가 여기 온 볼일 가운데 하나는 너에게 작별을 고하는 것이란다.” 내가 말을 꺼냈다. “오늘 런던으로 돌아가야 해. 그리고 떠나기 전에 네 신상 문제에 관해 한마디 나누고 싶구나.”\n\n“가신다니 정말 서운해요, 길모어 씨.” 그녀가 다정하게 나를 바라보며 말했다. “여기 계시니 행복하던 옛날 같았는데요.”\n\n“다시 와서 그 즐거운 기억들을 한 번 더 떠올릴 수 있으면 좋으련만.” 나는 말을 이었다. “하지만 앞일이 어찌 될지 알 수 없으니, 기회가 있을 때 붙잡아 지금 이야기해야겠다. 나는 네 오랜 변호사이자 오랜 벗이니, 네가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 혼인할 가능성에 대해 일깨워 준다 해도 언짢게 여기지 않으리라 믿는다.”\n\n그녀는 작은 화첩이 별안간 뜨거워져 손을 데기라도 한 듯 급히 손을 떼었다. 손가락들은 무릎 위에서 초조하게 얽혔고, 눈은 다시 바닥으로 떨어졌으며, 얼굴에는 거의 고통에 가까워 보이는 굳은 표정이 내려앉았다.\n\n“제 혼약 이야기를 꼭 해야만 하나요?” 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n“언급은 해야 하지만,” 내가 대답했다. “거기 오래 머물 필요는 없다. 네가 혼인할 수도 있고 하지 않을 수도 있다고만 해 두자. 앞의 경우라면 나는 미리 네 재산 설정 증서를 작성할 채비를 해 두어야 하고, 예의상 먼저 네 뜻을 묻지 않고 그리해서는 안 되지. 네 바람이 무엇인지 들을 기회는 지금뿐일지도 모른다. 그러니 네가 혼인한다고 가정해 보고, 지금 네 처지가 어떠하며 네가 원한다면 앞으로 그것을 어떻게 만들 수 있는지를 되도록 짧게 일러 주마.”\n\n나는 그녀에게 혼인 재산 설정의 목적을 설명한 뒤, 그녀의 재산 전망이 정확히 어떠한지를 일러 주었다 — 첫째로는 성년이 되었을 때, 둘째로는 삼촌이 세상을 떠났을 때를 나누어, 종신 수익권만 갖는 재산과 그녀 자신의 처분에 맡겨진 재산의 구분을 짚어 주었다. 그녀는 여전히 굳은 표정으로, 여전히 무릎 위에서 초조하게 두 손을 맞잡은 채 주의 깊게 들었다.\n\n“자, 이제,” 나는 마무리 삼아 말했다. “우리가 가정한 경우에 네가 나더러 너를 위해 넣어 달라고 바라는 조건이 있는지 말해 보아라 — 물론 너는 아직 성년이 아니니 후견인의 승낙을 받아야 하겠지만.”\n\n그녀는 의자에서 불안하게 몸을 움직이더니, 갑자기 아주 간절한 눈으로 내 얼굴을 바라보았다.\n\n“정말로 그렇게 된다면,” 그녀가 가냘프게 입을 열었다. “제가 만약——”\n\n“혼인하게 된다면 말이지.” 내가 말을 거들어 주었다.\n\n“그이가 저를 매리언에게서 떼어 놓지 못하게 해 주세요.” 그녀가 갑자기 기운을 내어 외쳤다. “아, 길모어 씨, 매리언이 저와 함께 살도록 법으로 정해 주세요!”\n\n다른 상황이었다면 내 질문과 그에 앞선 긴 설명을 이토록 지극히 여성다운 방식으로 알아들은 데 대해 어쩌면 웃음이 났을지도 모른다. 그러나 그 말을 할 때의 표정과 목소리는 나를 그저 진지하게 만드는 정도를 넘어 — 나를 괴롭게 했다. 몇 마디 되지 않는 그 말에는 지나간 것에 대한 필사적인 매달림이 배어 있었고, 그것은 앞날에 불길한 조짐이었다.\n\n“매리언 홀콤 양이 너와 함께 사는 문제라면 사사로운 약정으로 얼마든지 정할 수 있다.” 내가 말했다. “내 질문을 잘못 알아들은 모양이구나. 그것은 네 재산에 관한 이야기였다 — 네 돈을 어떻게 처분하겠느냐는 것이지. 네가 성년이 되어 유언장을 쓴다고 하면, 그 돈이 누구에게 가기를 바라겠느냐?”\n\n“매리언은 제게 어머니이자 언니였어요.” 착하고 정 많은 아가씨가 말했다. 말하는 동안 예쁜 푸른 눈이 촉촉이 반짝였다. “매리언에게 남겨도 될까요, 길모어 씨?”\n\n“물론이지, 얘야.” 내가 대답했다. “하지만 그것이 얼마나 큰 돈인지 잊지 말아라. 전부 홀콤 양에게 가기를 바라느냐?”\n\n그녀는 망설였다. 낯빛이 붉어졌다 옅어졌다 하더니, 손이 다시 슬그머니 작은 화첩으로 돌아갔다.\n\n“전부는 아니에요.” 그녀가 말했다. “매리언 말고도 또 한 사람이——”\n\n그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 낯빛이 더 붉어졌고, 화첩 위에 놓인 손의 손가락들이 그림 가장자리를 가만가만 두드렸다. 마치 기억이 좋아하던 곡조를 떠올려 그 손가락들을 저절로 움직이게 하기라도 한 것처럼.\n\n“홀콤 양 말고 집안의 다른 사람을 말하는 것이냐?” 그녀가 말을 잇지 못하는 것을 보고 내가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n짙어지던 낯빛이 이마와 목까지 번졌고, 초조한 손가락들이 갑자기 책 가장자리를 꽉 움켜쥐었다.\n\n“또 한 사람이 있어요.” 그녀는 내 마지막 말을 분명히 들었으면서도 못 들은 척하며 말했다. “작은 기념품이라도 받으면 기뻐할 사람이 또 있어요 — 제가, 제가 남길 수만 있다면요. 제가 먼저 죽는다면 해로울 것도 없잖아요——”\n\n그녀는 다시 말을 멈추었다. 두 뺨에 번졌던 붉은빛이 갑자기 밀려왔던 것만큼이나 갑자기 빠져나갔다. 화첩 위의 손이 힘을 풀고 조금 떨리더니 책을 저만치 밀어 놓았다. 그녀는 한순간 나를 바라보다가 의자에 앉은 채 고개를 옆으로 돌렸다. 자세를 바꾸는 사이 손수건이 바닥에 떨어졌고, 그녀는 황급히 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻어 내게서 감추었다.\n\n슬픈 일이었다! 내 기억 속의 그 아이는 하루 종일 웃으며 지내던 더없이 발랄하고 행복한 아이였는데, 이제 나이와 아름다움이 한창 꽃필 때에 이토록 무너지고 이토록 짓눌린 모습을 보게 되다니!\n\n그 아이가 내게 안긴 괴로움 속에서 나는 흘러간 세월도, 그 세월이 서로의 처지에 만들어 놓은 변화도 잊어버렸다. 나는 의자를 그 아이 가까이 당기고 양탄자에서 손수건을 주워 들었으며, 얼굴을 가린 두 손을 부드럽게 떼어 주었다. “울지 말아라, 얘야.” 나는 그렇게 말하고, 마치 그 아이가 열 해 전의 그 어린 로라 페얼리이기라도 한 듯 눈에 고이는 눈물을 내 손으로 닦아 주었다.\n\n그것이 그 아이를 진정시키는 데 내가 택할 수 있었던 가장 좋은 방법이었다. 그 아이는 내 어깨에 머리를 기대고 눈물 사이로 희미하게 미소 지었다.\n\n“제가 주책없이 굴어서 정말 죄송해요.” 그녀가 꾸밈없이 말했다. “요즘 몸이 좋지 않았어요 — 요사이 몹시 기운이 없고 신경이 예민해져서, 혼자 있으면 이유도 없이 자주 울곤 해요. 이제 괜찮아요 — 길모어 씨, 이제 제대로 대답할 수 있어요, 정말이에요.”\n\n“아니다, 아니야, 얘야.” 내가 대답했다. “그 문제는 당분간 끝난 것으로 하자꾸나. 네 이익을 최선을 다해 지켜도 좋다는 뜻은 충분히 말해 주었으니, 세세한 것은 다음 기회에 정하면 된다. 이제 사무 이야기는 그만하고 다른 이야기를 하자.”\n\n나는 곧바로 그 아이를 다른 화제로 이끌었다. 십 분쯤 지나자 기분이 나아졌고, 나는 작별하려고 일어섰다.\n\n“또 오세요.” 그녀가 간절하게 말했다. “다시 와 주시기만 하면, 저를 위하고 제 이익을 위해 베풀어 주시는 그 마음에 더 어울리는 사람이 되도록 애써 볼게요.”\n\n여전히 지나간 것에 매달리고 있었다 — 홀콤 양이 그 나름의 방식으로 그러하듯, 나 또한 내 나름의 방식으로 그 아이에게 표상하는 그 지나간 시절에! 인생의 초입에 선 그 아이가 뒤를 돌아보는 모습, 바로 인생의 끝자락에 선 내가 뒤를 돌아보듯 하는 그 모습이 나를 몹시 괴롭게 했다.\n\n“내가 다시 온다면, 그때는 네가 더 나아져 있기를 바란다.” 내가 말했다. “더 건강하고 더 행복해져 있기를. 하느님의 축복이 있기를, 얘야!”\n\n그 아이는 입 맞춰 달라고 뺨을 내미는 것으로만 대답했다. 변호사에게도 마음이란 것이 있어서, 그 아이와 작별하는 동안 내 마음도 조금은 아렸다.\n\n우리 둘 사이의 면담은 통틀어 반 시간을 겨우 넘겼을 뿐이었다. 그 아이는 내 앞에서 혼인을 앞두고 드러낸 그 뚜렷한 괴로움과 두려움의 수수께끼를 설명할 말은 한마디도 흘리지 않았건만, 그러면서도 어떻게 어째서인지 나로서는 알 수 없는 채로 나를 제 편으로 끌어들이고 말았다. 나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 아이의 처사에 대해 불평할 만한 정당한 이유가 있다고 여기며 그 방에 들어갔다. 그리고 일이 그 아이가 그의 말을 그대로 받아들여 약혼의 파기를 요구하는 쪽으로 끝나기를 남몰래 바라며 그 방을 나왔다. 내 나이와 경험을 지닌 사람이라면 이토록 사리에 닿지 않게 흔들려서는 안 되는 법이었다. 나 자신을 위해 아무 변명도 할 수 없다. 다만 사실대로 말할 수 있을 뿐이다 — 그러하였노라고.\n\n떠날 시각이 다가오고 있었다. 나는 페얼리 씨에게 사람을 보내, 원하신다면 작별 인사를 하러 들르겠으나 다소 서두르는 것은 양해해 달라고 전했다. 그는 종잇조각에 연필로 적은 답신을 보내왔다. “다정한 마음과 온갖 행운을 비오, 길모어. 어떤 종류든 서두름이란 내게는 이루 말할 수 없이 해롭다오. 부디 몸조심하시오. 안녕히.”\n\n떠나기 직전에 나는 홀콤 양을 잠시 단둘이 만났다.\n\n“로라에게 하시려던 말씀은 다 하셨나요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “동생분은 몹시 쇠약하고 신경이 곤두서 있더군요 — 돌봐 줄 당신이 곁에 있어 다행입니다.”\n\n홀콤 양의 날카로운 눈이 내 얼굴을 유심히 살폈다.\n\n“로라에 대한 생각이 달라지고 계시네요.” 그녀가 말했다. “어제보다 그 아이를 너그럽게 헤아리려 하시는군요.”\n\n분별 있는 남자라면 아무 채비 없이 여자와 말의 검술 시합에 나서는 법이 없다. 나는 이렇게만 대답했다 —\n\n“무슨 일이 생기는지 알려 주십시오. 당신에게서 소식을 들을 때까지 나는 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n그녀는 여전히 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. “이 일이 다 끝났으면, 그것도 잘 끝났으면 좋겠어요, 길모어 씨 — 당신도 그러시겠지요.” 그 말을 남기고 그녀는 나를 두고 갔다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 더없이 정중하게 마차 문까지 나를 배웅하겠다고 우겼다.\n\n“혹 제 근방에 오시는 일이 있거든,” 그가 말했다. “제가 우리 사이를 더 두터이 하고 싶어 진심으로 애태우고 있다는 것을 부디 잊지 마십시오. 이 집안의 오래 검증된 믿음직한 벗이라면 제 어느 집에서든 언제나 반가운 손님일 것입니다.”\n\n참으로 거부하기 어려운 사람이었다 — 예의 바르고 사려 깊으며 오만이라고는 반갑게도 찾아볼 수 없는, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 신사인 사람. 역으로 마차를 달리는 동안 나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 이익을 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 줄 수 있을 것 같았다 — 세상의 그 무엇이라도, 다만 그의 아내가 될 사람의 혼인 재산 설정 증서를 작성하는 일만은 빼고.\n\n# III\n\n런던으로 돌아온 뒤 일주일이 지나도록 홀콤 양에게서는 아무런 소식도 오지 않았다.\n\n여드레째 되는 날, 그녀의 필체로 쓰인 편지 한 통이 내 책상 위 다른 편지들 사이에 놓여 있었다.\n\n편지는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 확실히 받아들여졌다는 것, 그리고 혼례가 그가 애초에 바란 대로 연내에 치러지리라는 것을 알리고 있었다. 십중팔구 예식은 십이월 마지막 보름 안에 거행될 터였다. 페얼리 양의 스물한 번째 생일은 삼월 하순이었다. 그러므로 이 일정대로라면 그녀는 성년이 되기 석 달쯤 전에 퍼시벌 경의 아내가 되는 셈이었다.\n\n놀랄 일도 아니었고 서글퍼할 일도 아니었건만, 그럼에도 나는 놀랐고 서글펐다. 홀콤 양의 편지가 만족스럽지 못할 만큼 짧았던 데서 오는 자잘한 실망감까지 이 감정에 섞여 들어, 그날 하루 내 평온을 흐트러뜨리는 데 한몫을 했다. 내 서신 상대는 여섯 줄로 예정된 혼사를 알렸고, 다시 세 줄로 퍼시벌 경이 컴벌랜드를 떠나 햄프셔의 자기 저택으로 돌아갔음을 전했으며, 마지막 두 문장으로 첫째, 로라에게 변화와 유쾌한 교제가 몹시 필요하다는 것, 둘째, 그래서 자신이 그런 변화를 곧바로 시험해 보기로 마음먹고 동생을 데리고 요크셔의 오랜 친구들을 방문하러 떠나겠다는 것을 알려 왔다. 편지는 거기서 끝났다. 내가 페얼리 양을 마지막으로 본 때로부터 단 일주일 만에 그녀가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 받아들이기로 결심하게 된 정황이 무엇이었는지, 그것을 설명하는 말은 한 마디도 없었다.\n\n뒷날 이 갑작스러운 결심의 까닭은 내게 낱낱이 설명되었다. 그러나 전해 들은 이야기에 기대어 그것을 어설프게 늘어놓는 것은 내 소임이 아니다. 그 정황은 홀콤 양이 몸소 겪은 일이며, 내 기록에 이어 그녀의 기록이 나올 때 그녀가 일어난 그대로 하나하나 서술할 것이다. 그동안 내가 해야 할 분명한 의무는 — 내 차례를 마치고 펜을 내려놓아 이 이야기에서 물러나기 전에 — 페얼리 양의 혼사와 얽힌 일 가운데 내가 관여한 마지막 사건 하나, 곧 혼인 재산 계약서를 작성한 경위를 적어 두는 일이다.\n\n이 문서를 알아들을 수 있게 이야기하려면 먼저 신부의 재산 관계에 관한 몇 가지 사항을 짚지 않을 수 없다. 나는 되도록 짧고 평이하게, 법조계의 모호한 표현과 전문 용어를 걷어내고 설명해 보겠다. 이 문제는 지극히 중요하다. 이 글을 읽는 모든 분께 미리 일러 둔다. 페얼리 양의 상속 재산은 페얼리 양 이야기에서 매우 중대한 부분이며, 앞으로 이어질 기록들을 이해하고자 한다면 이 대목에 관한 한 길모어의 경험이 곧 독자의 경험이 되어야 한다.\n\n페얼리 양이 기대할 수 있는 재산은 두 가지였다. 하나는 삼촌이 세상을 떠날 때 물려받게 될지도 모르는 부동산, 곧 토지였고, 다른 하나는 성년이 되는 순간 온전히 자기 것이 되는 동산, 곧 돈이었다.\n\n먼저 토지부터 살펴보자.\n\n페얼리 양의 친조부(편의상 노(老) 페얼리 씨라 부르기로 하자) 시절에 리머리지 영지의 한사(限嗣) 상속 순위는 이러했다 —\n\n노 페얼리 씨는 세상을 떠나면서 필립, 프레더릭, 아서 세 아들을 남겼다. 맏이인 필립이 영지를 이어받되, 그가 아들 없이 죽으면 재산은 둘째 아우 프레더릭에게 넘어가고, 프레더릭 또한 아들 없이 죽으면 재산은 셋째 아우 아서에게 넘어가도록 되어 있었다.\n\n실제로 일이 흘러간 바를 보면, 필립 페얼리 씨는 외동딸 하나만 남기고 죽었으니 그 딸이 이 이야기의 로라이며, 그리하여 영지는 법의 절차에 따라 둘째 아우인 독신자 프레더릭에게 넘어갔다. 셋째 아우 아서는 필립이 죽기 여러 해 전에 이미 세상을 떠나면서 아들 하나와 딸 하나를 남겼다. 그 아들은 열여덟 살에 옥스퍼드에서 물에 빠져 죽었다. 그의 죽음으로 필립 페얼리 씨의 딸 로라는 영지의 추정 상속인이 되었고, 삼촌 프레더릭이 남자 후사를 남기지 않고 죽는다면 자연의 순리에 따라 그 뒤를 이을 가망이 충분했다.\n\n그러므로 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 혼인하여 상속인을 남기는 일이 벌어지지 않는 한 — 이 세상에서 그가 할 법하지 않은 일을 꼽자면 바로 그 두 가지였다 — 조카 로라는 그가 죽을 때 재산을 갖게 될 터였다. 다만 그녀가 갖는 것은 종신 수익권뿐임을 잊어서는 안 된다. 그녀가 혼인하지 않은 채 죽거나 자식 없이 죽으면 영지는 사촌 매그덜런, 곧 아서 페얼리 씨의 딸에게 되돌아간다. 반대로 그녀가 온당한 혼인 재산 계약을 맺고 — 다시 말해 내가 그녀를 위해 맺어 주려는 그 계약을 맺고 — 혼인한다면, 영지에서 나오는 수입(넉넉히 연 삼천 파운드)은 그녀가 살아 있는 동안 그녀 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 된다. 그녀가 남편보다 먼저 죽으면 남편은 당연히 자기 생전에 그 수입을 계속 누리게 되기를 기대할 것이다. 아들을 두면 그 아들이 상속인이 되어 사촌 매그덜런은 배제된다. 그러니 퍼시벌 경이 페얼리 양과 혼인함으로써 얻을 전망은 (아내가 부동산에서 기대할 몫에 관한 한) 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 죽는 날 그에게 두 가지 이익을 약속하고 있었다. 첫째는 연 삼천 파운드를 쓰는 것(아내가 살아 있는 동안은 아내의 허락으로, 아내가 죽은 뒤에는 그가 아내보다 오래 산다면 자기 권리로), 둘째는 아들이 있다면 그 아들에게 돌아갈 리머리지의 상속이었다.\n\n부동산에 관해서는, 또 페얼리 양의 혼인에 즈음한 그 수입의 처분에 관해서는 이쯤 해 두자. 여기까지는 신부 쪽 혼인 재산 계약을 놓고 퍼시벌 경의 변호사와 나 사이에 어떤 난관이나 견해차가 생길 여지가 거의 없었다.\n\n다음으로 살펴볼 것은 동산, 다시 말해 페얼리 양이 스물한 살이 되는 순간 권리를 갖게 되는 돈이다.\n\n상속 재산의 이 부분은 그 자체로 넉넉한 자산이었다. 아버지의 유언에 따라 나온 것으로 액수는 이만 파운드였다. 이 밖에 그녀는 만 파운드에 대한 종신 수익권도 가지고 있었는데, 그 만 파운드는 그녀가 죽으면 아버지의 하나뿐인 누이인 고모 엘리너에게 돌아가게 되어 있었다. 어째서 고모가 조카딸이 죽을 때까지 유산을 기다려야 하는 처지가 되었는지 여기서 잠시 멈추어 설명해 둔다면, 이 집안 사정을 독자 앞에 되도록 또렷하게 펼쳐 보이는 데 크게 도움이 될 것이다.\n\n필립 페얼리 씨는 누이 엘리너가 처녀로 있는 동안에는 더없이 사이좋게 지냈다. 그러나 그 누이가 꽤 늦은 나이에 혼인을 하게 되었고, 그 혼인이 그녀를 포스코라는 이름의 이탈리아 신사와, 아니 정확히는 이탈리아 귀족과 — 그는 백작이라는 작위를 자랑스레 지니고 있었으므로 — 맺어 주자, 페얼리 씨는 누이의 처사를 어찌나 못마땅히 여겼던지 그녀와 왕래를 끊어 버렸고, 심지어 유언장에서 그 이름을 지워 버리기까지 했다. 집안의 다른 이들은 누이의 혼인에 대한 이 심각한 노여움의 표시를 다들 어느 정도 지나치다고 여겼다. 포스코 백작은 부유한 사람은 아니었으나 그렇다고 무일푼의 협잡꾼도 아니었다. 많지는 않아도 그럭저럭 살 만한 수입이 그에게는 있었다. 영국에서 여러 해를 살았고 사교계에서 훌륭한 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 그러나 이런 장점들도 페얼리 씨에게는 아무 소용이 없었다. 여러 견해에서 그는 구식 영국인이었고, 외국인이라는 단 하나의 이유만으로 외국인을 미워했다. 뒷날 그가 겨우 응한 것은 — 주로 페얼리 양이 간청한 덕분이었는데 — 누이의 이름을 유언장의 본디 자리에 되돌려 놓되, 그 돈의 수입은 딸에게 종신으로 주고 원금 자체는 고모가 딸보다 먼저 죽을 경우 사촌 매그덜런에게 가도록 함으로써 누이를 계속 기다리게 만드는 것이었다. 두 여인의 나이 차를 생각하면 자연의 순리대로 고모가 만 파운드를 손에 넣을 가망은 이로써 극히 희박해졌다. 그리고 포스코 백작부인은 이런 경우에 흔히 그러하듯 오라비의 처사를 부당하게 원망하여, 조카딸 만나기를 거부하고 페얼리 씨의 유언장에 자기 이름이 되돌아온 것이 페얼리 양의 간청 덕분이었다는 사실도 믿으려 하지 않았다.\n\n만 파운드의 내력은 이러했다. 여기서도 퍼시벌 경의 법률 고문과 어려움이 생길 일은 없었다. 수입은 아내가 뜻대로 쓰고, 원금은 그녀가 죽으면 고모나 사촌에게 돌아갈 터였다.\n\n예비 설명이 이제 모두 걷혔으니, 마침내 이 사건의 진짜 매듭에 이른다 — 곧 이만 파운드다.\n\n이 금액은 그녀가 스물한 살을 채우는 순간 온전히 페얼리 양 자신의 것이 되며, 그것이 앞으로 어떻게 처분되느냐는 우선 내가 혼인 재산 계약서에서 그녀를 위해 얻어 낼 수 있는 조건에 달려 있었다. 그 문서에 담긴 다른 조항들은 형식적인 것이어서 여기 옮길 것도 없다. 그러나 그 돈에 관한 조항은 그냥 지나치기에는 너무나 중요하다. 몇 줄이면 필요한 요지를 밝히기에 충분할 것이다.\n\n이만 파운드에 관한 내 요구 조건은 간단히 이러했다. 전액을 신탁으로 묶어 그 수입을 신부에게 종신으로 주고, 그 뒤에는 퍼시벌 경에게 종신으로 주며, 원금은 이 혼인에서 태어난 자녀에게 돌아가게 한다. 자녀가 없을 경우 원금은 신부가 유언으로 지정하는 대로 처분하되, 그러기 위해 나는 그녀에게 유언을 작성할 권리를 남겨 두었다. 이 조건들의 효과는 이렇게 요약할 수 있다. 글라이드 부인이 자녀를 남기지 않고 죽으면, 이복 언니인 홀콤 양과 그 밖에 그녀가 은혜를 베풀고 싶어 할 친척이나 친구들이 남편이 죽은 뒤에 그녀가 정해 준 몫대로 그 돈을 나누어 갖게 된다. 반대로 자녀를 남기고 죽으면, 자연히 또 필연적으로 그 자녀의 권리가 다른 모든 권리에 앞선다. 이것이 그 조항이었다. 이 글을 읽는 이라면 누구든 이 조항이 모든 당사자에게 공평한 정의를 나누어 주었다는 데 동의하지 않을 수 없으리라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n내 제안이 남편 쪽에서 어떻게 받아들여졌는지 이제 보기로 하자.\n\n홀콤 양의 편지가 내게 닿았을 무렵 나는 평소보다도 더 바빴다. 그러나 나는 혼인 재산 계약을 위해 짬을 냈다. 홀콤 양에게서 혼사 소식을 들은 때로부터 일주일도 되기 전에 나는 계약서를 작성하여 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사에게 검토용으로 보냈다.\n\n이틀이 지나 문서는 준남작 측 변호사의 주석과 의견이 붙은 채 내게 돌아왔다. 그의 이의는 대체로 지극히 사소하고 형식적인 것들이었는데, 이만 파운드에 관한 조항에 이르자 사정이 달라졌다. 그 대목에는 붉은 잉크로 두 줄이 그어져 있었고 다음과 같은 주석이 달려 있었다 —\n\n“받아들일 수 없음. 원금은, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 글라이드 부인보다 오래 살고 자녀가 없을 경우, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 귀속할 것.”\n\n다시 말해 이만 파운드는 한 푼도 홀콤 양에게든 글라이드 부인의 다른 어떤 친척이나 친구에게든 가지 않는다는 것이었다. 그녀가 자녀를 남기지 않으면 그 전액이 남편의 주머니로 슬며시 굴러 들어가게 된다는 것이었다.\n\n이 뻔뻔한 제안에 내가 쓴 답장은 내가 할 수 있는 한 짧고 매서웠다. “친애하는 선생. 페얼리 양의 혼인 재산 계약 건. 귀하가 이의를 제기하신 조항을 나는 지금 그대로 유지합니다. 이만 줄입니다.” 응수는 십오 분 만에 돌아왔다. “친애하는 선생. 페얼리 양의 혼인 재산 계약 건. 귀하가 이의를 제기하신 붉은 잉크를 나는 지금 그대로 유지합니다. 이만 줄입니다.” 요즘 통용되는 그 고약한 말투로 하자면 우리 둘은 이제 “교착 상태”에 빠진 것이었고, 남은 길이라고는 양쪽이 각자의 의뢰인에게 문의하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n사정이 그러한 만큼 — 페얼리 양이 아직 스물한 살을 채우지 못했으므로 — 내 의뢰인은 그녀의 후견인인 프레더릭 페얼리 씨였다. 나는 그날 우편으로 편지를 써서 사태를 있는 그대로 그의 앞에 내놓았고, 내가 작성한 대로 그 조항을 지키도록 그를 설득하려고 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 논거를 들이댔을 뿐 아니라, 이만 파운드에 관한 내 계약 조항에 반대하는 밑바닥에 돈 욕심이 깔려 있다는 것도 분명히 밝혔다. 퍼시벌 경 쪽 증서 조항들이 절차에 따라 내 검토에 넘어왔을 때 나는 그의 재정 형편을 알 수밖에 없었는데, 그로써 너무나도 분명해진 것은 그의 영지에 걸린 빚이 어마어마하며, 그의 수입은 명목상으로는 크다 해도 그만한 지위의 사람에게는 사실상 없는 것이나 다름없다는 사실이었다. 당장 쓸 현금이 없다는 것이야말로 퍼시벌 경의 삶을 짓누르는 현실적 곤경이었고, 계약서의 그 조항에 붙은 그 변호사의 주석은 그것을 노골적이고 이기적으로 드러낸 것에 지나지 않았다.\n\n페얼리 씨의 답장은 곧바로 다음 우편으로 도착했는데, 지극히 종잡을 수 없고 동떨어진 내용이었다. 알아듣기 쉬운 영어로 옮기자면 사실상 이런 뜻이었다. “친애하는 길모어가, 아득히 먼 우발 사태 따위의 사소한 일로 자기 벗이자 의뢰인을 괴롭히지 않는 친절을 베풀어 줄 수는 없겠소? 스물한 살 젊은 여인이 마흔다섯 사내보다 먼저, 그것도 자식 없이 죽을 성싶기나 하오? 한편으로 이토록 비참한 세상에서 평온과 고요의 값어치를 아무리 높이 친들 지나칠 리가 있겠소? 그 두 가지 천상의 축복을 이만 파운드의 아득한 가망 따위 하찮은 속세의 것과 맞바꿔 준다면, 그거야말로 공정한 거래가 아니겠소? 아무렴, 그렇고말고. 그렇다면 어찌 아니 하겠소?”\n\n나는 역겨워하며 편지를 내던졌다. 그것이 막 바닥으로 팔랑팔랑 떨어졌을 때 문 두드리는 소리가 나더니 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사 메리먼 씨가 안내를 받아 들어왔다. 이 세상에는 온갖 종류의 약삭빠른 자들이 있지만, 그중에서도 가장 상대하기 어려운 것은 몸에 밴 쾌활함을 가면 삼아 사람을 속여 넘기는 부류라고 나는 생각한다. 살집 좋고 배부르고 웃음 띠고 다정한 사업가야말로 흥정의 상대로는 가장 가망 없는 축이다. 메리먼 씨가 바로 그런 부류였다.\n\n“길모어 선생, 그동안 안녕하셨는지요?” 그는 제 상냥함의 온기로 온통 달아오른 채 말을 꺼냈다. “이렇게 정정하신 모습을 뵈니 반갑습니다, 선생. 문 앞을 지나다가 혹시 제게 하실 말씀이 있으실까 하여 들러 보았지요. 자, 부디, 부디 우리 사이의 이 자그마한 이견을 될 수만 있다면 말로 매듭지읍시다! 의뢰인에게서 소식은 받으셨습니까?”\n\n“받았네. 자네는 자네 의뢰인에게서 소식을 받았나?”\n\n“아이고, 친애하는 선생! 그분에게서 쓸모 있는 소식이라도 왔더라면 좋았을 것을요. 이 책임이 제 어깨에서 벗겨지기를 진심으로 바랍니다만, 그분이 워낙 고집이 세셔서요 — 아니, 결단이 굳으시다고 해 두지요 — 도무지 벗겨 주시질 않습니다. ‘메리먼, 세부는 자네에게 맡기네. 내 이익을 위해 옳다고 생각하는 대로 하고, 이 일이 다 끝날 때까지 나는 개인적으로 손을 뗀 사람으로 여기게.’ 보름 전에 퍼시벌 경께서 하신 말씀이 그것이고, 지금 제가 얻어 낼 수 있는 것이라고는 그 말씀의 되풀이뿐입니다. 길모어 선생, 아시다시피 저는 모진 사람이 아닙니다. 개인적으로, 또 사사로이 말씀드리자면 저는 지금 이 순간에도 제가 쓴 그 주석을 지워 버리고 싶습니다. 그러나 퍼시벌 경께서 이 문제를 들여다보려 하지 않으신다면, 퍼시벌 경께서 당신의 이익 일체를 눈감고 제 손에만 맡기신다면, 제가 그 이익을 주장하는 길 말고 무슨 길을 택할 수 있겠습니까? 제 손은 묶여 있습니다 — 아시겠지요, 친애하는 선생? — 제 손은 묶여 있어요.”\n\n“그러면 자네는 그 조항에 붙인 주석을 한 글자도 물리지 않겠다는 말인가?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다 — 빌어먹을 노릇이지요! 달리 도리가 없습니다.” 그는 벽난로로 걸어가 몸을 녹이며 걸쭉하고 흥겨운 저음으로 노랫가락 끝자락을 흥얼거렸다. “그쪽에서는 뭐라고 합니까?” 그가 말을 이었다. “자, 어서 말씀해 주십시오 — 그쪽에서는 뭐라고 합니까?”\n\n나는 그에게 말하기가 부끄러웠다. 시간을 벌어 보려 했고 — 아니, 그보다 더 못한 짓을 했다. 법률가의 본능이 나를 이겨서, 나는 흥정까지 시도했던 것이다.\n\n“이만 파운드는 신부 쪽 사람들이 이틀 말미에 포기하기에는 꽤 큰 액수일세.” 내가 말했다.\n\n“참으로 옳은 말씀입니다.” 메리먼 씨가 생각에 잠긴 듯 제 구두를 내려다보며 대꾸했다. “잘 짚으셨습니다, 선생 — 참으로 잘 짚으셨어요!”\n\n“남편 쪽 이익만이 아니라 신부 집안의 이익도 인정하는 절충안이었다면 아마 내 의뢰인이 그렇게까지 놀라지는 않았을 걸세.” 내가 말을 이었다. “자, 자! 이 우발 사태라는 것도 결국은 흥정의 문제로 귀착되는군. 자네 쪽에서 받아들일 최소는 얼마인가?”\n\n“저희가 받아들일 최소는,” 메리먼 씨가 말했다, “일만-구천-구백-구십구-파운드-십구-실링-십일-펜스-삼-파딩입니다. 하하하! 실례했습니다, 길모어 선생. 저도 농담 한마디쯤은 해야지요.”\n\n“참으로 자잘한 농담이군.” 내가 한마디 했다. “그 농담은 딱 그것을 위해 남겨 둔 그 자투리 파딩만큼의 값어치가 있네.”\n\n메리먼 씨는 아주 기뻐했다. 그는 내 응수에 방 안이 울리도록 웃어 젖혔다. 내 쪽은 그 절반만큼도 유쾌하지 않았다. 나는 용건으로 돌아가 면담을 끝맺었다.\n\n“오늘이 금요일이군.” 내가 말했다. “최종 답변은 다음 주 화요일까지 말미를 주게.”\n\n“여부가 있겠습니까.” 메리먼 씨가 대답했다. “원하신다면 더 오래 드리지요, 친애하는 선생.” 그는 가려고 모자를 집어 들었다가 다시 내게 말을 건넸다. “그런데 말입니다,” 그가 말했다, “컴벌랜드의 그쪽 의뢰인들은 익명의 편지를 쓴 그 여자에 대해 그 뒤로 아무 소식도 듣지 못했겠지요?”\n\n“더는 없네.” 내가 대답했다. “자네들은 그 여자의 자취를 찾지 못했나?”\n\n“아직입니다.” 나의 법조계 벗이 말했다. “그러나 단념하지는 않습니다. 퍼시벌 경께서는 누군가가 그 여자를 숨겨 주고 있다고 의심하시고, 저희는 그 누군가를 감시하고 있습니다.”\n\n“컴벌랜드에서 그 여자와 함께 있었다는 노파를 말하는 건가?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“전혀 다른 사람입니다, 선생.” 메리먼 씨가 대답했다. “그 노파는 아직 손에 넣지 못했지요. 저희가 말하는 누군가는 남자입니다. 여기 런던에서 바로 코앞에 두고 지켜보고 있는데, 애초에 그 여자가 정신병원에서 달아나도록 도운 데 관여했으리라고 강하게 의심하고 있습니다. 퍼시벌 경께서는 당장 그를 추궁하려 하셨지만 제가 말씀드렸지요. ‘안 됩니다. 추궁해 봐야 경계심만 돋울 뿐입니다 — 지켜보면서 기다리십시오.’ 어찌 되는지 보아야지요. 저런 여자가 밖을 돌아다니는 것은 위험한 일입니다, 길모어 선생. 다음에 무슨 짓을 할지 아무도 모르지요. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생. 다음 주 화요일에는 좋은 소식을 들려주시기를 기대하겠습니다.” 그는 상냥하게 웃고는 나갔다.\n\n이 법조계 벗과 나눈 대화의 뒷부분에서 내 정신은 다소 딴 데 가 있었다. 나는 혼인 재산 계약 문제가 어찌나 걱정스러웠던지 다른 어떤 일에도 마음을 쏟을 여유가 없었고, 다시 혼자 남겨진 순간 다음에 어떻게 해야 할지를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n다른 의뢰인의 일이었다면 나는 개인적으로 아무리 마뜩잖더라도 지시대로 움직여, 그 자리에서 이만 파운드에 관한 요구를 접었을 것이다. 그러나 페얼리 양을 상대로 그런 사무적인 무심함으로 처신할 수는 없었다. 나는 그녀에게 정직한 애정과 감탄을 품고 있었고 — 그녀의 아버지가 사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 친절한 후원자이자 벗이었음을 고맙게 기억하고 있었으며 — 혼인 재산 계약서를 작성하는 내내 내가 늙은 독신자만 아니었더라면 제 딸에게 느꼈을 법한 마음으로 그녀를 대하고 있었다. 그러니 그녀를 위하는 일, 그녀의 이익이 걸린 일이라면 나는 어떤 개인적 희생도 아끼지 않으리라 마음먹고 있었다. 페얼리 씨에게 두 번째로 편지를 쓰는 것은 생각할 것도 없었다. 그것은 그에게 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 기회를 한 번 더 주는 일일 뿐이었다. 직접 만나 몸소 따져 묻는다면 그나마 쓸모가 있을지도 몰랐다. 이튿날은 토요일이었다. 나는 왕복표를 끊어 이 늙은 뼈다귀를 덜컹거리며 컴벌랜드까지 내려가, 정당하고 떳떳하고 명예로운 길을 택하도록 그를 설득해 보기로 마음먹었다. 물론 가망이야 형편없이 적었지만, 그렇게라도 해 보고 나면 내 양심은 편해질 터였다. 그러면 나는 옛 벗의 하나뿐인 아이를 위해 내 처지에서 할 수 있는 일을 다 한 셈이 될 것이었다.\n\n토요일 날씨는 아름다웠다. 서풍이 불고 햇살이 밝았다. 요즘 들어 머리가 다시 그득하고 짓눌리는 느낌이 도져 있었는데, 두 해도 더 전에 의사가 그토록 엄중히 경고했던 바로 그 증세였으므로, 나는 이 기회에 운동을 좀 더 해 두기로 하고 가방을 먼저 부친 다음 유스턴 광장의 역까지 걸어가기로 했다. 홀본으로 나서는데 빠른 걸음으로 지나가던 신사 하나가 걸음을 멈추고 내게 말을 걸었다. 월터 하트라이트 씨였다.\n\n그가 먼저 인사를 건네지 않았더라면 나는 틀림없이 그를 지나쳤을 것이다. 어찌나 달라졌던지 알아보기조차 어려웠다. 얼굴은 창백하고 초췌했으며, 몸가짐은 조급하고 안정을 잃었고, 리머리지에서 보았을 때 단정하고 신사다웠던 것으로 기억하는 옷차림은 이제 어찌나 추레한지 내 사무소 서기가 그런 꼴을 하고 있었다면 정말이지 부끄러웠을 정도였다.\n\n“컴벌랜드에서 돌아오신 지 오래되셨습니까?” 그가 물었다. “최근에 홀콤 양에게서 소식을 들었습니다. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명이 만족스러운 것으로 받아들여졌다는 것도 알고 있습니다. 혼례가 곧 치러집니까? 혹시 아십니까, 길모어 선생?”\n\n그는 어찌나 빨리 말하고 질문들을 어찌나 이상하고 어지럽게 몰아붙이던지 나는 따라가기조차 힘들었다. 그가 어쩌다 리머리지 집안과 가깝게 지냈다 한들, 그 집안의 사사로운 일을 알 권리가 그에게 있다고는 보이지 않았으므로, 나는 페얼리 양의 혼사 이야기에서 되도록 무리 없이 그를 떼어 놓기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“때가 되면 알게 될 것이오, 하트라이트 씨.” 내가 말했다. “때가 되면 알게 되겠지요. 신문에서 혼인 소식을 눈여겨보고 있으면 크게 어긋나지는 않을 것이오. 이런 말을 하는 것을 언짢게 여기지 마시오만, 지난번에 만났을 때보다 안색이 좋지 않아 보여 안타깝소.”\n\n순간적인 신경질적 경련이 그의 입가와 눈가에 파르르 스쳤고, 그것을 보자 나는 그토록 의미심장하게 말을 아껴 대답한 나 자신을 절반쯤 나무라게 되었다.\n\n“그분의 혼사를 물을 권리가 제게는 없었지요.” 그가 쓰라리게 말했다. “저도 다른 사람들처럼 신문에 나기를 기다려야겠습니다. 네,” — 그는 내가 무슨 사과의 말을 꺼내기도 전에 말을 이었다 — “요즘 몸이 좋지 않았습니다. 다른 나라로 가서 풍경과 일을 바꿔 볼 참입니다. 홀콤 양께서 친절히 힘을 써 주셨고, 제 추천서도 만족스럽다는 평을 받았습니다. 아주 먼 곳입니다만, 저는 어디로 가든, 기후가 어떻든, 얼마나 오래 떠나 있든 상관없습니다.” 그는 이렇게 말하는 동안 양옆으로 우리를 스쳐 지나가는 낯선 사람들의 무리를 이상하고 의심 가득한 눈길로 둘러보았다. 마치 그중 누군가가 우리를 지켜보고 있을지도 모른다고 여기는 사람처럼.\n\n“그 일이 잘 풀리고 무사히 돌아오시기를 바라오.” 나는 그렇게 말한 뒤, 페얼리 집안 이야기에서 그를 아주 밀어내 버리지는 않으려고 이렇게 덧붙였다. “나는 오늘 볼일이 있어 리머리지로 내려가는 길이오. 홀콤 양과 페얼리 양은 지금 요크셔의 친구들을 방문하러 떠나고 없소.”\n\n그의 눈이 환해졌고 무언가 대답하려는 듯했으나, 아까와 같은 순간적인 신경의 경련이 다시 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그는 내 손을 잡아 힘껏 쥐고는 한 마디도 더 하지 않은 채 인파 속으로 사라졌다. 그는 내게 낯선 사람이나 다름없었지만, 나는 잠시 그 자리에 서서 거의 아쉬운 마음으로 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 젊은이들이 그릇된 길로 접어들기 시작할 때 겉으로 어떤 조짐과 표시가 나타나는지 알 만큼은 나도 직업상 겪어 본 터였다. 그리하여 다시 역을 향해 걸음을 옮기면서, 안타깝지만 나는 하트라이트 씨의 앞날에 대해 의심을 넘어선 무엇을 느꼈다고 말해 두어야겠다.\n\n# IV\n\n이른 기차로 떠난 덕에 나는 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 리머리지에 닿았다. 집 안은 숨이 막힐 만큼 텅 비고 적막했다. 두 아가씨가 없는 동안 그 선량한 베지 부인이 말벗이 되어 주려니 했으나, 그녀는 감기로 방에 틀어박혀 있었다. 하인들은 나를 보고 어찌나 놀랐던지 우스꽝스러울 만큼 허둥지둥 부산을 떨며 온갖 성가신 실수를 저질렀다. 그만한 나이면 분별이 있을 법한 집사조차 차게 식어 버린 포트와인 한 병을 내왔다. 페얼리 씨의 건강에 관한 보고는 여느 때와 똑같았고, 내가 도착을 알리는 전갈을 올려 보내자 돌아온 대답인즉, 내일 아침이면 기꺼이 나를 만나겠으나 내가 나타났다는 갑작스러운 소식에 심계항진이 도져 그날 저녁 내내 몸져누웠다는 것이었다. 밤새 바람이 음산하게 울부짖었고, 텅 빈 집 안 이곳저곳 사방에서 이상하게 갈라지고 삐걱대는 소리가 들려왔다. 나는 더할 나위 없이 비참하게 잤고, 이튿날 아침 몹시 언짢은 기분으로 일어나 혼자 아침을 먹었다.\n\n열 시에 나는 페얼리 씨의 거처로 안내되었다. 그는 늘 있던 방, 늘 앉던 의자에, 늘 그렇듯 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 심신의 상태로 있었다. 내가 들어갔을 때, 그의 시종은 주인 앞에 서서 내 사무실 책상만큼이나 길고 넓은 묵직한 동판화첩을 감상하시라고 받쳐 들고 있었다. 그 가엾은 외국인은 더없이 비굴한 낯으로 히죽거리며 피곤에 겨워 곧 쓰러질 듯했고, 그동안 주인은 태연히 동판화를 한 장씩 넘기며 돋보기의 힘을 빌려 그 감춰진 아름다움을 밝혀내고 있었다.\n\n“더없이 좋은 옛 벗이시여,” 페얼리 씨가 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 나른하게 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 말했다. “아주 안녕하신가요? 이 외로운 처지의 나를 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니 얼마나 고마운 일인지. 사랑하는 길모어!”\n\n내가 나타나면 시종을 물릴 줄 알았는데, 그런 일은 조금도 일어나지 않았다. 시종은 주인의 의자 앞에 선 채로 동판화첩의 무게에 부들부들 떨고 있었고, 페얼리 씨는 희고 가는 손가락 사이에서 돋보기를 유유히 돌리며 앉아 있었다.\n\n“대단히 중요한 일로 말씀을 드리러 왔습니다.” 내가 말했다. “그러니 우리 둘만 있는 편이 낫겠다고 청하더라도 양해해 주시지요.”\n\n가엾은 시종이 고마운 눈길로 나를 보았다. 페얼리 씨는 상상할 수 있는 최대의 경악을 얼굴에 담고서 내 마지막 말을 힘없이 되뇌었다. “둘만 있는 편이 낫다.”\n\n나는 시시껄렁한 수작을 받아 줄 기분이 아니었으므로, 내 말뜻을 반드시 알아듣게 하리라 마음먹었다.\n\n“저 사람에게 물러가도 좋다고 허락해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.” 나는 시종을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n페얼리 씨는 눈썹을 치켜올리고 입술을 오므리며 비꼬는 듯한 놀라움을 지어 보였다.\n\n“사람이라니요?” 그가 되물었다. “속 뒤집는 길모어 노인장, 저것을 사람이라 부르시다니 대체 무슨 뜻입니까? 저건 사람 따위가 아니에요. 반 시간 전, 내가 동판화를 찾기 전이라면 사람이었겠지요. 또 반 시간 뒤에, 내가 더는 동판화가 필요 없어지면 다시 사람이 될 테고요. 지금 저것은 그저 화첩 받침대일 뿐입니다. 길모어, 화첩 받침대를 두고 무슨 이의가 있습니까?”\n\n“이의가 있습니다. 페얼리 씨, 세 번째로 청합니다만, 우리 둘만 있게 해 주십시오.”\n\n내 어조와 태도에는 내 청을 들어주는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다. 그는 하인을 보더니 짜증스럽게 제 옆의 의자를 가리켰다.\n\n“동판화는 내려놓고 나가라.” 그가 말했다. “내가 보던 자리를 잃어버려서 나를 뒤집어 놓지는 말고. 내 자리를 잃어버렸느냐, 아니냐? 정말 잃어버리지 않았단 말이지? 그리고 내 손종은 손 닿는 데 잘 놓아두었느냐? 그래? 그럼 대체 왜 안 나가는 게냐?”\n\n시종이 나갔다. 페얼리 씨는 의자에 앉은 채 몸을 비틀어 돌리고는 고운 아마포 손수건으로 돋보기를 닦더니, 펼쳐진 동판화첩을 곁눈질로 살피는 즐거움에 빠져들었다. 이런 상황에서 성미를 다스리기란 쉽지 않았으나, 나는 끝내 다스렸다.\n\n“저는 개인적으로 대단한 불편을 무릅쓰고 여기까지 왔습니다.” 내가 말했다. “조카분과 가문의 이익을 위해서지요. 그러니 그 대가로 어른의 주의를 좀 얻을 만한 자격은 조금이나마 갖추었다고 생각합니다.”\n\n“나를 몰아세우지 마세요!” 페얼리 씨가 의자에 힘없이 파묻히며 눈을 감고 외쳤다. “제발 나를 몰아세우지 마세요. 나는 그럴 만큼 튼튼하지 못합니다.”\n\n나는 로라 페얼리를 생각해서, 그의 도발에 넘어가지 않기로 작정했다.\n\n“제 목적은,” 내가 말을 이었다. “보내신 편지를 다시 생각해 주십사 간청하는 것, 그리고 조카분과 그분에게 속한 모든 이의 정당한 권리를 제가 포기하도록 강요하지 말아 주십사 하는 것입니다. 이 사안을 한 번만 더, 이것을 마지막으로 말씀드리게 해 주십시오.”\n\n페얼리 씨는 고개를 내젓고는 애처롭게 한숨을 쉬었다.\n\n“길모어, 그건 참으로 매정한 처사요—몹시 매정해.” 그가 말했다. “뭐, 괜찮습니다. 계속하시지요.”\n\n나는 요점을 하나하나 신중히 짚어 주었다—생각할 수 있는 온갖 각도에서 이 문제를 그 앞에 펼쳐 보였다. 내가 말하는 내내 그는 눈을 감은 채 의자에 기대어 있었다. 내 말이 끝나자 그는 나른하게 눈을 뜨더니 탁자에서 은제 각성제 병을 집어 들고 부드러운 만족감을 담아 킁킁 냄새를 맡았다.\n\n“착한 길모어!” 그가 냄새를 맡는 틈틈이 말했다. “이 얼마나 고마운 일인지! 당신 덕에 내가 인간이라는 존재와 화해하게 되는군요!”\n\n“페얼리 씨, 분명한 물음에는 분명한 답을 주십시오. 다시 말씀드립니다만, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게는 그 돈의 수입 이상을 바랄 근거가 털끝만큼도 없습니다. 조카분에게 자식이 없다면 원금 자체는 그분의 관리 아래 있어야 하고, 그분의 가문으로 되돌아와야 마땅합니다. 어른께서 뜻을 굽히지 않으시면 퍼시벌 경은 양보할 수밖에 없습니다—양보할 수밖에 없어요, 정말입니다. 아니면 순전히 돈을 노리고 페얼리 양과 혼인한다는 비열한 혐의를 스스로 뒤집어쓰게 되겠지요.”\n\n페얼리 씨는 은제 각성제 병을 장난스럽게 내 쪽으로 흔들어 보였다.\n\n“친애하는 길모어 노인장, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게도 지체와 가문을 싫어하십니까? 글라이드가 하필 준남작이라는 이유로 어쩌면 그렇게도 그를 미워하십니까? 당신은 어쩌면 그리도 급진파인지—오, 이런, 어쩌면 그리도 급진파인지!”\n\n급진파라니!!! 나는 웬만한 도발은 다 참아 넘길 수 있었으나, 평생 가장 건전한 보수주의 원칙을 지켜 온 사람으로서 급진파라 불리는 것만은 도저히 참을 수 없었다. 피가 끓어올랐다—나는 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다—분노로 말문이 막혔다.\n\n“방을 흔들지 마세요!” 페얼리 씨가 소리쳤다. “제발 좀, 방을 흔들지 마세요! 세상 모든 길모어 가운데 가장 훌륭한 분이여, 나는 기분 상하게 할 뜻이 없었습니다. 내 견해야말로 워낙 자유주의적이어서 나 자신도 급진파가 아닌가 싶은걸요. 그래요. 우리는 한 쌍의 급진파입니다. 부디 화내지 마세요. 나는 다툴 수가 없습니다—그럴 기력이 없어요. 이 이야기는 그만두는 게 어떻습니까? 그래요. 이리 와서 이 어여쁜 동판화들을 좀 보시지요. 이 선들의 천상 같은 진줏빛을 이해하는 법을 내가 가르쳐 드리게 해 주세요. 어서요, 착한 길모어!”\n\n그가 이렇게 넋두리를 늘어놓는 동안, 다행히도 내 자존심을 위해서는, 나는 제정신을 되찾고 있었다. 다시 입을 열었을 때 나는 그의 무례를 마땅히 받아야 할 침묵의 경멸로 대할 만큼 침착해져 있었다.\n\n“어르신, 제가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 어떤 편견에서 이런 말씀을 드린다고 여기신다면 그건 전적으로 잘못 아신 겁니다.” 내가 말했다. “그가 이 문제를 제 변호사의 지시에 그토록 남김없이 내맡겨 본인에게는 어떤 호소도 할 수 없게 만든 점은 유감스러울지 모르나, 저는 그에게 편견을 품고 있지 않습니다. 제가 드린 말씀은 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 누구에게도, 신분이 높든 낮든 똑같이 적용될 것입니다. 제가 지키는 원칙은 널리 인정된 원칙입니다. 여기서 가장 가까운 읍내에 가셔서 처음 만나는 점잖은 사무변호사에게 물어보십시오. 그는 남남의 처지에서, 제가 벗의 처지에서 드리는 말씀과 똑같은 말을 해 드릴 것입니다. 그는 신부의 재산을 그가 혼인할 남자에게 통째로 넘기는 것은 모든 원칙에 어긋난다고 일러 줄 것입니다. 그리고 법률가로서 마땅히 지켜야 할 조심성을 근거로, 어떤 경우에도 남편에게 아내의 죽음으로 2만 파운드를 얻는 이해관계를 안겨 주는 일은 거절할 것입니다.”\n\n“정말 그럴까요, 길모어?” 페얼리 씨가 말했다. “그 절반이라도 그런 끔찍한 말을 지껄인다면, 나는 당장 종을 딸랑거려 루이를 불러서 그자를 집 밖으로 내쫓아 버릴 겁니다. 정말입니다.”\n\n“페얼리 씨, 저를 화나게 하지 못하실 겁니다—조카분을 위해서, 또 그분 아버님을 위해서, 저를 화나게 하지 못하실 겁니다. 제가 이 방을 나서기 전에, 이 떳떳지 못한 재산 설정의 책임을 어른께서 온전히 어깨에 지셔야 합니다.”\n\n“그러지 마세요!—제발 그러지 마세요!” 페얼리 씨가 말했다. “길모어, 당신 시간이 얼마나 귀한지 생각하셔서 그것을 헛되이 버리지 마세요. 할 수만 있다면 나도 당신과 논쟁하겠지만, 그럴 수가 없습니다—그럴 기력이 없어요. 당신은 나를 뒤집어 놓고, 당신 자신을 뒤집어 놓고, 글라이드를 뒤집어 놓고, 로라까지 뒤집어 놓으려 하는군요. 게다가—오, 이런!—그 모든 게 세상에서 일어날 법하기로 가장 마지막인 일 때문이라니. 아니요, 친애하는 벗이여—평화와 안온을 위하여, 단연코 아니요!”\n\n“그렇다면 편지에 밝히신 결정을 그대로 고수하시겠다는 뜻으로 알아도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“그래요, 부디. 마침내 서로 이해하게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지. 자, 다시 앉으시지요—어서요!”\n\n나는 곧장 문으로 걸어갔고, 페얼리 씨는 체념한 듯 손종을 “딸랑거렸다”. 방을 나서기 전에 나는 몸을 돌려 그에게 마지막으로 말을 건넸다.\n\n“어르신, 앞으로 무슨 일이 벌어지든,” 내가 말했다. “경고를 드려야 할 제 마땅한 의무는 다했다는 것만은 기억해 주십시오. 이 가문의 충실한 벗이자 종복으로서, 헤어지며 말씀드립니다. 어른께서 페얼리 양을 위해 저더러 억지로 작성하게 하시는 그런 재산 설정 아래라면, 제게 딸이 있다 한들 세상 어떤 남자와도 혼인시키지 않을 것입니다.”\n\n등 뒤에서 문이 열리고, 시종이 문턱에서 기다리며 서 있었다.\n\n“루이,” 페얼리 씨가 말했다. “길모어 씨를 배웅해 드리고 나서 다시 와서 내 동판화를 받쳐 들어라. 아래층에서 점심을 잘 차려 주라고 해라. 길모어, 제발 저 게으른 짐승 같은 하인들에게 점심을 잘 대접하라고 시키세요!”\n\n나는 너무나 역겨워 대꾸할 수도 없었다—발꿈치를 돌려 말없이 그를 남겨 두고 나왔다. 오후 두 시에 상행 열차가 있었고, 나는 그 기차로 런던에 돌아왔다.\n\n화요일에 나는 수정된 재산 설정서를 제출했다. 그것은 페얼리 양이 제 입으로 가장 혜택을 주고 싶다고 내게 일러 준 바로 그 사람들의 상속권을 사실상 빼앗는 문서였다. 나로서는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었다. 내가 맡기를 거절했다면 다른 변호사가 그 증서를 작성했을 테니까.\n\n내 소임은 끝났다. 이 가족 이야기의 사건들에서 내가 개인적으로 맡은 몫은 방금 이른 지점을 넘어가지 않는다. 이제 곧 뒤따를 기이한 정황들은 내 것 아닌 다른 이들의 붓이 그려 낼 것이다. 나는 무겁고 슬픈 마음으로 이 짧은 기록을 닫는다. 무겁고 슬픈 마음으로, 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 남기고 온 작별의 말을 여기 다시 적는다.—내가 로라 페얼리를 위해 어쩔 수 없이 작성해야 했던 그런 재산 설정 아래라면, 내게 딸이 있다 한들 세상 어떤 남자와도 혼인시키지 않았을 것이다.\n\n길모어 씨의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다.\n\n(그녀의 일기에서 발췌한 것)\n\n[1] 여기와 그 밖의 곳에서 홀콤 양의 일기 가운데 생략한 대목들은, 페얼리 양이나 이 지면에서 그녀와 관계를 맺는 인물들과 아무런 관련이 없는 부분들뿐이다.\n\n오늘 아침 길모어 씨가 우리를 떠났다.\n\n로라와 나눈 면담이 그에게 스스로 인정하고 싶은 것보다 훨씬 큰 슬픔과 놀라움을 안긴 것이 분명했다. 헤어질 때의 그 표정과 태도로 보아, 로라가 무심결에 자기 우울의 진짜 비밀과 나의 근심거리를 그에게 흘려 버린 것은 아닐까 두려웠다. 그가 떠난 뒤 이 의심이 어찌나 커지던지, 나는 퍼시벌 경과 말을 타러 나가자는 청을 거절하고 대신 로라의 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n로라의 그 불행한 연정이 얼마나 깊은지 내가 전혀 모르고 있었다는 사실을 깨달은 뒤로, 나는 이 곤란하고 개탄스러운 문제에서 나 자신을 몹시도 믿지 못하게 되었다. 나를 가엾은 하트라이트에게 이끌었고 그를 진심으로 존경하고 흠모하게 만들었던 그 섬세함과 인내와 명예심이야말로, 로라의 타고난 감수성과 타고난 너그러움에 가장 거스를 수 없이 호소하는 자질이라는 것을 나는 진작 알았어야 했다. 그런데도 로라가 제 발로 내게 마음을 열기 전까지, 나는 이 새로운 감정이 그토록 깊이 뿌리를 내렸으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했다. 한때 나는 시간과 보살핌이 그것을 걷어 가리라 여겼다. 이제는 그것이 로라에게 남아 그 아이의 평생을 바꾸어 놓지나 않을까 두렵다. 내가 이만한 판단 착오를 저질렀다는 발견은 다른 모든 일에 대해서도 나를 망설이게 만든다. 나는 더없이 명백한 증거들 앞에서도 퍼시벌 경에 대해 망설인다. 로라에게 말을 거는 일조차 망설인다. 바로 오늘 아침에도 문고리에 손을 얹은 채, 물으러 온 것을 물어야 할지 말아야 할지 망설였다.\n\n방에 들어서니 로라는 몹시 초조한 얼굴로 서성이고 있었다. 뺨은 상기되고 흥분한 기색이었으며, 곧바로 앞으로 나와 내가 입을 열기도 전에 먼저 말을 건넸다.\n\n“언니를 기다렸어요.” 로라가 말했다. “이리 와서 나랑 소파에 앉아요. 매리언! 나 더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없어요—반드시 끝내야 하고, 끝낼 거예요.”\n\n뺨에는 핏기가 지나쳤고, 태도에는 기운이 지나쳤고, 목소리에는 단호함이 지나쳤다. 하트라이트의 그림이 담긴 작은 화첩—혼자 있을 때마다 로라가 꿈꾸듯 들여다보는 그 운명의 화첩—이 한쪽 손에 들려 있었다. 나는 우선 부드럽되 단호하게 그것을 빼앗아 곁상 위, 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 놓았다.\n\n“얘야, 어떻게 하고 싶은지 차분히 말해 보렴.” 내가 말했다. “길모어 씨가 무슨 조언이라도 하셨니?”\n\n로라는 고개를 저었다. “아니요, 지금 제가 생각하는 일에 대해서는요. 그분은 제게 아주 친절하고 좋으셨어요, 매리언. 부끄럽지만 제가 울어서 그분을 괴롭게 해 드렸죠. 저는 비참할 만큼 무력해요—제 자신을 다스리질 못하겠어요. 저를 위해서도, 우리 모두를 위해서도, 이걸 끝낼 만한 용기를 내야 해요.”\n\n“파혼을 요구할 용기 말이니?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아니요.” 로라가 담담히 말했다. “언니, 진실을 말할 용기요.”\n\n로라는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고 내 가슴에 조용히 머리를 기댔다. 맞은편 벽에는 아버지의 세밀 초상화가 걸려 있었다. 나는 그 아이 위로 몸을 숙이다가, 내 가슴에 머리를 얹은 채 그 초상을 바라보고 있음을 보았다.\n\n“저는 결코 약혼에서 놓아 달라 요구할 수 없어요.” 로라가 말을 이었다. “어느 쪽으로 끝나든 제게는 비참하게 끝날 수밖에 없어요. 매리언, 제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 그 비참함을 더 무겁게 만들 것—제가 약속을 깨뜨렸고 아버지의 임종 유언을 잊었다는 기억—만은 보태지 않는 것뿐이에요.”\n\n“그럼 어떻게 하겠다는 거니?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경께 제 입으로 진실을 말씀드리는 거예요.” 로라가 대답했다. “그리고 그분이 원하신다면 저를 놓아주시도록 하는 거죠. 제가 청해서가 아니라, 모든 걸 아셨기 때문에요.”\n\n“로라, ‘모든 걸’이라니 무슨 뜻이니? 퍼시벌 경은 이 약혼이 네 뜻에 어긋난다는 것만 알면 충분히 안 셈이야(본인이 내게 직접 그렇게 말했단다).”\n\n“아버지가 제 동의를 얻어 저를 위해 맺어 주신 약혼인데, 제가 어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있겠어요? 저는 약속을 지켰을 거예요, 행복하게는 아니었겠지만 그래도 만족하며—” 로라는 말을 멈추고 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리더니 제 뺨을 내 뺨에 바싹 붙였다. “매리언, 처음 퍼시벌 경의 아내가 되겠다고 약속했을 때는 없었던 또 다른 사랑이 제 마음속에 자라나지만 않았다면, 저는 그 약혼을 지켰을 거예요.”\n\n“로라! 그에게 고백을 해서 너 자신을 낮추는 짓은 절대로 하지 않을 거지?”\n\n“그분이 알 권리가 있는 것을 감춘 채 놓여난다면, 그거야말로 정말 제 자신을 낮추는 일이에요.”\n\n“그에게는 그걸 알 권리가 털끝만큼도 없어!”\n\n“아니에요, 매리언, 그건 틀렸어요! 저는 누구도 속여서는 안 돼요—무엇보다도 아버지가 저를 맡기셨고 제가 제 자신을 드린 그분을 속여서는 안 되죠.” 로라는 제 입술을 내 입술에 대고 입을 맞추었다. “내 사랑하는 언니,” 로라가 나직이 말했다. “언니는 나를 너무 아끼고 나를 너무 자랑스러워해서, 언니 자신의 일이라면 잊지 않았을 것을 내 일에서는 잊어버리는 거예요. 퍼시벌 경이 제 동기를 의심하고 원한다면 제 행실을 그르게 판단하시더라도, 제가 먼저 마음속으로 그분을 배신하고 그다음엔 그 배신을 감춰 제 잇속을 챙길 만큼 비열해지는 것보다는 낫잖아요.”\n\n나는 놀라서 그 아이를 내게서 떼어 놓고 바라보았다. 우리가 살아오면서 처음으로 자리가 바뀌었다—결심은 온통 저쪽에, 망설임은 온통 이쪽에 있었다. 나는 창백하고 고요하고 체념한 젊은 얼굴을 들여다보았다—나를 마주 보는 사랑 어린 눈 속에서 순결하고 티 없는 마음을 보았다—그러자 입술까지 올라왔던 초라한 세속의 조심성과 반론들이 제 스스로의 공허함 속에서 오그라들어 사그라졌다. 나는 말없이 고개를 떨구었다. 그 아이의 처지였다면, 수많은 여자를 거짓되게 만드는 그 경멸스러울 만큼 하찮은 자존심이 곧 나의 자존심이었을 테고, 나 역시 거짓된 사람이 되었으리라.\n\n“매리언, 화내지 말아요.” 내 침묵을 오해한 로라가 말했다.\n\n나는 그저 그 아이를 다시 끌어당겨 안는 것으로 대답했다. 입을 열면 울음이 터질까 두려웠다. 내 눈물은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 쉽게 흐르지 않는다—거의 남자의 눈물처럼, 몸을 갈가리 찢는 듯한 흐느낌과 함께 터져 나와 주위 사람들을 모두 놀라게 한다.\n\n“나는 여러 날 이 일을 생각했어요, 언니.” 로라가 손가락으로 내 머리카락을 감았다 풀었다 하며 말을 이었다. 그 어린애 같은 손버릇은 가엾은 베지 부인이 지금도 그토록 참을성 있게, 그러나 그토록 헛되이 고쳐 주려 애쓰는 것이었다. “아주 진지하게 생각했어요. 그리고 제 양심이 옳다고 말해 줄 때는 제 용기를 믿을 수 있어요. 내일 그분께 말하게 해 줘요—매리언, 언니가 있는 자리에서요. 그르다 할 말은 하나도 하지 않을게요. 언니나 제가 부끄러워할 말도 하나도요—하지만, 아, 이 비참한 숨김을 끝내면 제 마음이 얼마나 가벼워질까요! 제 쪽에서는 책임져야 할 기만이 하나도 없다는 것만 알고 느끼게 해 주세요. 그러고 나서, 제가 드릴 말씀을 그분이 다 들으시고 나면, 저를 어떻게 대하시든 그분 뜻대로 하시게 하고요.”\n\n로라는 한숨을 쉬고는 다시 내 가슴의 예전 자리에 머리를 뉘었다. 끝이 어찌 될지 모른다는 슬픈 불안이 내 마음을 짓눌렀으나, 여전히 나 자신을 믿지 못하는 터라 그 아이가 바라는 대로 하겠다고 말했다. 로라는 고맙다고 했고, 우리는 차츰 다른 이야기로 넘어갔다.\n\n저녁 식사에 로라는 다시 우리와 자리를 함께했고, 퍼시벌 경을 대하는 태도가 지금까지 내가 본 어느 때보다 편안하고 제 본모습에 가까웠다. 저녁에는 피아노 앞에 앉아 손재주만 요란하고 가락은 없이 화려하기만 한 새 악곡들을 골랐다. 가엾은 하트라이트가 그토록 좋아하던 모차르트의 그 어여쁜 옛 선율들은 그가 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 치지 않았다. 그 악보집은 이제 보면대에 없다. 누가 발견하고 그것으로 연주해 달라 청할까 봐 로라가 손수 치워 버린 것이다.\n\n로라가 퍼시벌 경에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넬 때까지, 나는 아침의 그 결심이 바뀌었는지 어떤지 알아볼 기회가 없었다—그리고 그때 그 아이의 말이 결심이 그대로임을 내게 일러 주었다. 로라는 아주 조용히, 아침 식사 뒤에 드릴 말씀이 있으며 자기 거실에서 나와 함께 기다리고 있겠노라고 말했다. 그 말에 퍼시벌 경은 낯빛이 변했고, 내 차례가 되어 그의 손을 잡았을 때 나는 그 손이 조금 떨리는 것을 느꼈다. 이튿날 아침의 일이 그의 앞날을 결정지을 터였고, 그도 분명 그것을 알고 있었다.\n\n나는 여느 때처럼 우리 두 침실 사이의 문으로 들어가, 잠들기 전의 로라에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하려 했다. 입을 맞추려 몸을 숙이다가 나는 하트라이트의 그림이 담긴 작은 화첩이 베개 밑에 반쯤 숨겨져 있는 것을 보았다. 어릴 적 가장 아끼던 장난감을 감추어 두던 바로 그 자리였다. 차마 무슨 말을 할 마음이 나지 않아, 나는 그저 화첩을 가리키며 고개를 저었다. 로라는 두 손을 뻗어 내 두 뺨을 감싸더니, 우리 입술이 닿을 때까지 내 얼굴을 제 얼굴 쪽으로 끌어내렸다.\n\n“오늘 밤만은 거기 두게 해 줘요.” 로라가 속삭였다. “내일은 잔인할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 제가 이것과 영영 작별하게 만들지도 모르니까요.”\n\n9일.—오늘 아침의 첫 소식은 내 기운을 북돋아 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다—가엾은 월터 하트라이트에게서 편지가 온 것이다. 앤 캐서릭의 편지가 불러일으킨 의심을 퍼시벌 경이 어떻게 벗어 버렸는지 적어 보낸 내 편지에 대한 답장이었다. 그는 퍼시벌 경의 해명에 대해 짧고 씁쓸하게 쓰면서, 자기보다 윗자리에 있는 이들의 처신에 의견을 낼 권리가 자기에게는 없다는 말만 할 뿐이었다. 그것도 서글픈 일이지만, 이따금 자기 자신에 대해 언급한 대목들이 나를 더욱 아프게 한다. 그는 옛 습관과 일로 돌아가려는 노력이 날마다 쉬워지기는커녕 더 힘들어진다고 하면서, 내게 조금이라도 영향력이 있다면 그것을 써서 영국을 떠나야만 하는 일자리, 새로운 풍경과 새로운 사람들 속으로 자신을 데려가 줄 일자리를 구해 달라고 간청한다. 편지 끝의 한 대목이 나를 거의 경악하게 만든 탓에, 나는 이 청을 한결 더 기꺼이 들어주고 싶어졌다.\n\n앤 캐서릭에 대해서는 보지도 듣지도 못했다고 밝힌 뒤, 그는 문득 말을 끊더니 런던에 돌아온 뒤로 낯선 사내들에게 줄곧 감시당하고 미행당하고 있다고 더없이 불쑥, 더없이 알 수 없는 투로 넌지시 내비친다. 이 기이한 의심을 특정한 인물을 짚어 증명할 수는 없다고 그도 인정하지만, 그 의심 자체는 밤낮으로 자기를 떠나지 않는다고 단언한다. 이것이 나를 겁먹게 했다. 로라를 향한 그 하나의 고정된 생각이 그의 정신에 감당하기 힘든 짐이 되어 가는 듯 보이기 때문이다. 나는 곧바로 런던에 있는 어머니의 유력한 옛 벗들 몇 사람에게 편지를 써서 그의 처지를 눈여겨봐 달라고 힘써 부탁하려 한다. 풍경이 바뀌고 일이 바뀌는 것이 그의 인생의 이 고비에서 정말로 그를 구원할지도 모른다.\n\n무척 다행스럽게도, 퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사에 함께하지 못하겠다는 사과를 보내왔다. 자기 방에서 일찍 커피를 한 잔 들었고 아직 그곳에서 편지를 쓰는 중이라는 것이었다. 열한 시가 괜찮은 시각이라면 그때 페얼리 양과 홀콤 양을 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠노라고 했다.\n\n전갈이 전해지는 동안 내 눈은 로라의 얼굴에 머물러 있었다. 아침에 그 방에 들어갔을 때 나는 그 아이가 영문을 알 수 없을 만큼 고요하고 침착한 것을 보았는데, 아침 식사 내내 그대로였다. 심지어 퍼시벌 경을 기다리며 그 방 소파에 나란히 앉아 있을 때에도 로라는 여전히 자제력을 잃지 않았다.\n\n“매리언, 나를 걱정하지 말아요.” 그 아이가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. “길모어 씨 같은 오랜 벗 앞이나 언니 같은 사랑하는 자매 앞에서는 제가 흐트러질지 몰라도, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 앞에서는 흐트러지지 않을 거예요.”\n\n나는 말없이 놀라며 그 아이를 바라보고 그 말에 귀를 기울였다. 그토록 가까이 지낸 그 오랜 세월 내내 그 아이의 성품 속 이 조용한 힘은 내게 감추어져 있었다—사랑이 그것을 찾아내고 고통이 그것을 불러내기 전까지는 그 아이 자신에게도 감추어져 있었다.\n\n벽난로 선반 위 시계가 열한 시를 칠 때 퍼시벌 경이 문을 두드리고 들어왔다. 얼굴의 모든 선에 억눌린 불안과 동요가 서려 있었다. 평소에도 그를 괴롭히는 그 마르고 날카로운 기침이 어느 때보다 쉴 새 없이 그를 성가시게 하는 듯했다. 그는 탁자를 사이에 두고 우리 맞은편에 앉았고, 로라는 내 곁에 그대로 있었다. 나는 두 사람을 유심히 살펴보았는데, 둘 중 더 창백한 쪽은 그였다.\n\n그는 여느 때의 편안한 태도를 지키려 애쓰는 기색을 뚜렷이 드러내며 대수롭지 않은 말 몇 마디를 했다. 그러나 목소리는 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았고, 눈에 어린 안절부절못하는 불안도 감추어지지 않았다. 그 자신도 그것을 느꼈던 모양인지, 문장 한가운데서 말을 멈추더니 당황을 더 감추려는 시도마저 그만두었다.\n\n로라가 그에게 말을 건네기 전, 딱 한순간 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 드릴 말씀이 있습니다.” 로라가 말했다. “우리 두 사람 모두에게 매우 중요한 일에 관해서요. 제 언니가 여기 있는 것은 언니가 있어야 제게 힘이 되고 용기가 나기 때문이에요. 제가 이제부터 드릴 말씀은 언니가 한마디도 일러 준 것이 아닙니다—제 생각에서 나온 말이지 언니 생각에서 나온 말이 아니에요. 더 나아가기 전에 그 점을 너그러이 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다.”\n\n퍼시벌 경이 고개를 숙였다. 여기까지 로라는 겉으로 완벽하게 평온하고 완벽하게 예의 바른 태도로 말을 이어 왔다. 로라가 그를 보았고, 그도 로라를 보았다. 적어도 처음에는, 두 사람 다 서로를 분명히 이해하기로 작정한 듯 보였다.\n\n“매리언에게서 들었습니다.” 로라가 말을 이었다. “제가 약혼에서 놓아 달라 청하기만 하면 경께서 놓아주시리라고요. 퍼시벌 경, 제게 그런 전갈을 보내신 것은 너그럽고 관대한 처사였습니다. 그 제안이 고맙다고 말씀드리는 것은 경께 마땅한 도리를 다하는 일일 뿐이고, 그 제안을 받아들이지 않겠다고 말씀드리는 것 또한 제게 마땅한 도리를 다하는 일일 뿐이라고 저는 믿고 또 바랍니다.”\n\n주의 깊던 그의 얼굴이 조금 풀렸다. 그러나 나는 그의 한쪽 발이 탁자 밑 양탄자를 부드럽게, 조용히, 쉴 새 없이 두드리고 있는 것을 보았고, 그가 속으로는 여전히 초조하다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“저는 잊지 않았습니다.” 로라가 말했다. “경께서 제게 청혼의 영광을 베푸시기 전에 아버지의 허락을 구하셨다는 것을요. 제가 약혼에 동의하면서 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지도 아마 잊지 않으셨겠지요? 저는 감히, 경께 약속을 드리기로 마음을 정한 데는 아버지의 영향과 조언이 주된 몫을 했다고 말씀드렸습니다. 저는 아버지의 인도를 따랐습니다. 아버지야말로 언제나 모든 조언자 가운데 가장 진실하고, 모든 보호자와 벗 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 자애로운 분임을 알았기 때문이에요. 이제 아버지를 잃었고—사랑할 것이라곤 그 기억뿐이지만, 세상을 떠난 그 사랑하는 벗에 대한 제 믿음은 한 번도 흔들린 적이 없습니다. 저는 지금 이 순간에도, 예전에 믿었던 것과 똑같이 진실하게 믿습니다. 아버지는 무엇이 최선인지 아셨고, 아버지의 소망과 바람이 곧 제 소망과 바람이어야 한다고요.”\n\n로라의 목소리가 처음으로 떨렸다. 가만있지 못하는 손가락들이 슬며시 내 무릎으로 들어와 내 한 손을 꼭 붙들었다. 또 한 번 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 퍼시벌 경이 입을 열었다.\n\n“여쭤도 되겠습니까,” 그가 말했다. “제가 지금까지 지닌 것 가운데 가장 큰 영광이자 가장 큰 행복이었던 그 신뢰에, 제가 언제고 값하지 못한 일이 있었습니까?”\n\n“경의 처신에서 나무랄 것이라고는 하나도 찾지 못했습니다.” 로라가 대답했다. “경께서는 언제나 한결같은 섬세함과 한결같은 인내로 저를 대해 주셨습니다. 경께서는 제 신뢰를 받으실 만했고, 제 눈에 그보다 훨씬 더 중요한 일로는, 제 신뢰가 그로부터 자라난 아버지의 신뢰까지 받으실 만했습니다. 설령 제가 구실을 찾고 싶었다 한들, 서약에서 놓아 달라 청할 구실을 경께서는 조금도 주지 않으셨습니다. 여기까지 드린 말씀은 경께 진 빚을 남김없이 인정하고 싶은 마음에서 나온 것입니다. 그 빚에 대한 마음, 아버지의 기억에 대한 마음, 제 자신의 약속에 대한 마음, 그 모두가 지금 이 처지에서 물러서는 선례를 제 쪽에서 만드는 일을 금합니다. 우리 약혼을 깨뜨리는 일은 온전히 경의 뜻이고 경의 행위여야 합니다, 퍼시벌 경—제 것이 아니라요.”\n\n불안하게 두드리던 그의 발이 문득 멎었고, 그는 탁자 너머로 몸을 다급하게 앞으로 기울였다.\n\n“제 행위라고요?” 그가 말했다. “제 쪽에서 물러설 무슨 이유가 있을 수 있단 말입니까?”\n\n로라의 숨결이 빨라지는 소리가 들렸다—그 손이 차가워지는 것이 느껴졌다. 우리 둘만 있을 때 내게 한 말에도 불구하고, 나는 그 아이가 걱정되기 시작했다. 그것은 내 잘못된 걱정이었다.\n\n“말씀드리기 몹시 어려운 이유입니다.” 로라가 대답했다. “퍼시벌 경, 제게 변화가 있었습니다—경께서 경 자신에 대해서도 저에 대해서도 우리 약혼을 파기할 근거로 삼기에 충분할 만큼 중대한 변화가요.”\n\n그의 얼굴은 다시 어찌나 창백해졌던지 입술마저 핏기를 잃었다. 그는 탁자에 놓여 있던 팔을 들어 의자에서 몸을 조금 옆으로 돌리고는 그 손에 머리를 기대어, 우리에게는 옆얼굴만 보이게 되었다.\n\n“무슨 변화입니까?” 그가 물었다. 그 물음을 던지는 어조가 내 귀에 거슬렸다—그 안에는 고통스럽도록 억눌린 무언가가 있었다.\n\n로라는 무겁게 한숨을 쉬고 내 쪽으로 조금 기대어 제 어깨를 내 어깨에 기댔다. 그 떨림이 느껴져 내가 대신 말해 주어 그 짐을 덜어 주려 했다. 로라는 내 손을 지그시 눌러 나를 만류하고는, 다시 한 번 퍼시벌 경에게 말을 건넸다. 다만 이번에는 그를 바라보지 않은 채였다.\n\n“저는 이렇게 들었고, 또 그렇게 믿습니다.” 로라가 말했다. “모든 애정 가운데 가장 깊고 가장 참된 것은 아내가 남편에게 품어야 할 애정이라고요. 우리 약혼이 시작되었을 때, 그 애정은 제가 할 수만 있다면 드려야 할 것이었고 경께서 하실 수만 있다면 얻으셔야 할 것이었습니다. 퍼시벌 경, 이제는 그렇지 않다고 제가 고백한다면, 저를 용서하시고 저를 너그러이 봐주시겠습니까?”\n\n로라의 눈에 눈물이 조금 고이더니, 말을 멈추고 그의 대답을 기다리는 동안 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내렸다. 그는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 로라가 대답을 시작할 무렵 그는 머리를 받치고 있던 손을 옮겨 얼굴을 가려 버렸다. 내게는 탁자 위로 드러난 그 몸의 윗부분밖에 보이지 않았다. 근육 하나 움직이지 않았다. 머리를 받친 손의 손가락들은 머리카락 속에 깊이 파묻혀 있었다. 그것은 감춘 분노를 나타낼 수도 있었고 감춘 슬픔을 나타낼 수도 있었다—어느 쪽인지 가늠하기 어려웠다—그 손가락에는 무언가를 알려 줄 만한 떨림이 없었다. 그 순간, 그의 인생의 고비이자 로라의 인생의 고비였던 그 순간에, 그의 속마음의 비밀을 일러 줄 것은 아무것도, 정말이지 아무것도 없었다.\n\n나는 로라를 위해 그가 스스로 태도를 밝히게 만들기로 작정했다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경!” 내가 날카롭게 끼어들었다. “제 동생이 이토록 많은 말을 했는데 아무 하실 말씀이 없으신가요? 제가 보기에는,” 나는 불운한 성미가 나를 이기게 두고 이렇게 덧붙였다. “경의 처지에 있는 세상 어떤 남자가 이 아이에게서 들을 권리가 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했습니다만.”\n\n그 경솔한 마지막 문장은 그가 마음만 먹으면 나를 피해 빠져나갈 길을 열어 주었고, 그는 즉시 그것을 이용했다.\n\n“용서하십시오, 홀콤 양.” 그가 여전히 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 말했다. “제가 그런 권리를 주장한 적이 없다는 점을 상기시켜 드리더라도 용서해 주십시오.”\n\n그를 그가 벗어난 지점으로 도로 데려다 놓을 몇 마디 분명한 말이 막 내 입술에 걸려 있었는데, 로라가 다시 입을 열어 나를 제지했다.\n\n“제 괴로운 고백이 헛되지 않았기를 바랍니다.” 로라가 말을 이었다. “그 고백으로 제가 앞으로 드릴 말씀에 대한 경의 온전한 신뢰를 얻었기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“부디 그 점은 믿어 주십시오.” 그는 그 짧은 대답을 따뜻하게 건네며 말하는 동안 손을 탁자 위로 내려놓고 다시 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그를 스쳐 갔던 겉모습의 변화가 무엇이었든 이제는 사라지고 없었다. 그의 얼굴은 간절하고 기대에 차 있었다—로라의 다음 말을 듣고 싶은 더없이 절박한 조바심 말고는 아무것도 담겨 있지 않았다.\n\n“제가 어떤 이기적인 동기에서 말씀드린 것이 아님을 알아주셨으면 합니다.” 로라가 말했다. “퍼시벌 경, 방금 들으신 말을 듣고 저를 떠나신다 해도, 제가 다른 남자와 혼인하도록 떠나시는 것이 아닙니다. 남은 평생 제가 홀로 사는 여자로 남도록 허락하시는 것일 뿐이에요. 경께 지은 제 잘못은 제 생각 속에서 시작되어 제 생각 속에서 끝났습니다. 결코 그 이상으로 나아갈 수 없습니다. 한마디 말도 오간 적이—” 로라는 다음에 쓸 말을 두고 망설이며 말을 멈췄다. 잠시 스친 그 혼란은 지켜보기에 몹시 슬프고 몹시 아팠다. “한마디 말도 오간 적이 없습니다.” 로라가 참을성 있게, 그러나 단호하게 말을 이었다. “저와, 지금 경 앞에서 그를 향한 제 마음이나 저를 향한 그의 마음을 두고 처음이자 마지막으로 언급하는 그 사람 사이에는요—앞으로도 결코 한마디 말도 오갈 수 없습니다—그도 저도 이 세상에서 다시 만날 일은 없을 테니까요. 더 말하지 않아도 되게 해 주시기를 간절히 청하고, 지금 드린 말씀을 제 말 그대로 믿어 주시기를 청합니다. 그것은 진실입니다, 퍼시벌 경—제 감정을 아무리 희생해서라도 제 약혼자께서 들으실 권리가 있다고 제가 여기는 진실입니다. 저를 용서해 주시리라 그 너그러움을 믿고, 제 비밀을 지켜 주시리라 그 명예를 믿습니다.”\n\n“그 두 가지 믿음 모두 제게는 신성합니다.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 두 가지 다 신성하게 지켜질 것입니다.”\n\n그렇게 대답한 뒤 그는 말을 멈추고, 더 들을 말이 있기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 로라를 바라보았다.\n\n“저는 하고 싶은 말을 다 했습니다.” 그녀가 조용히 덧붙였다. “당신이 약혼에서 물러나시더라도 그것이 정당하다고 할 만큼은 충분히, 아니 넘치도록 말씀드렸어요.”\n\n“충분히, 아니 넘치도록 말씀하셨습니다.” 그가 대답했다. “이 약혼을 지키는 일이 제 인생에서 가장 소중한 목표가 되게 할 만큼 말입니다.” 그 말과 함께 그는 의자에서 일어나, 그녀가 앉아 있는 자리를 향해 몇 걸음 다가섰다.\n\n그녀는 몹시 흠칫 놀랐고, 희미한 놀라움의 외마디 소리가 새어 나왔다. 그녀가 입에 담은 말 하나하나가 자신도 모르는 사이에 그녀의 순결함과 진실함을, 순결하고 진실한 여인의 값을 매길 수 없는 가치를 속속들이 아는 남자 앞에 낱낱이 드러내고 말았던 것이다. 그녀 자신의 고결한 처신이야말로 처음부터 끝까지, 그녀가 거기에 걸었던 모든 희망의 숨은 적이었다. 나는 처음부터 이것을 두려워하고 있었다. 그녀가 내게 조금이라도 기회를 허락했더라면 나는 이 일을 막았을 것이다. 나는 이미 일이 다 벌어진 지금조차도, 그를 그르다고 몰아붙일 빌미가 될 한마디가 퍼시벌 경의 입에서 나오기를 기다리며 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n“당신을 단념하는 일을 저에게 맡기셨군요, 페얼리 양.” 그가 말을 이었다. “저는 방금 자기 성(性) 가운데 가장 고결한 사람임을 스스로 보여 준 여인을 단념할 만큼 무정한 사람이 못 됩니다.”\n\n그가 어찌나 따뜻하고 진심 어린 어조로, 어찌나 격정에 찬 열의로, 그러면서도 어찌나 완벽하게 섬세하게 말했던지, 그녀는 고개를 들고 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 갑작스러운 생기와 기백을 띤 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n“아닙니다!” 그녀가 단호하게 말했다. “사랑을 줄 수 없으면서 결혼으로 자신을 내주어야 한다면, 그 여자는 자기 성 가운데 가장 비참한 사람입니다.”\n\n“앞으로는 그 사랑을 주실 수 없겠습니까?” 그가 물었다. “남편 된 사람의 삶의 유일한 목표가 그 사랑을 받을 자격을 갖추는 일이라면 말입니다.”\n\n“결코 없습니다!” 그녀가 대답했다. “그래도 굳이 우리 약혼을 지키겠다고 고집하신다면, 저는 당신의 참되고 충실한 아내는 될 수 있겠지요, 퍼시벌 경. 하지만 당신을 사랑하는 아내는, 제가 제 마음을 안다면, 결코 되지 못합니다!”\n\n그 용감한 말을 하는 그녀는 너무나 저항할 수 없이 아름다워서, 살아 있는 어떤 남자도 그녀 앞에서 마음을 굳게 다잡을 수는 없었을 것이다. 나는 퍼시벌 경에게 잘못이 있다고 느끼려고, 또 그렇게 말하려고 무진 애를 썼지만, 내 안의 여자는 나도 모르게 그를 가엾게 여기고 있었다.\n\n“당신의 신의와 진실을 감사히 받겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “당신이 내줄 수 있는 가장 적은 것이, 세상의 다른 어떤 여인에게서 바랄 수 있는 가장 큰 것보다 제게는 더 소중합니다.”\n\n그녀의 왼손은 여전히 내 손을 잡고 있었지만 오른손은 옆구리에 힘없이 늘어져 있었다. 그는 그 손을 가만히 들어 입술로 가져갔다. 입을 맞추었다기보다는 그저 입술을 대었을 뿐이었다. 그러고는 내게 고개를 숙여 인사하고, 더없이 섬세하고 사려 깊게, 소리 없이 방을 나갔다.\n\n그가 나가고 나서도 그녀는 움직이지도 한마디 하지도 않았다. 차갑고 고요하게, 눈을 바닥에 붙박은 채 내 곁에 앉아 있을 뿐이었다. 말을 거는 것이 부질없고 소용없는 짓임을 알았기에 나는 그저 팔을 둘러 그녀를 말없이 내게 끌어안았다. 우리는 길고 지루하게 느껴지는 시간 동안 그렇게 함께 있었다. 어찌나 길고 지루했던지 나는 불안해져서, 무언가 달라지기를 바라며 그녀에게 나직이 말을 걸었다.\n\n내 목소리에 그녀는 문득 정신이 든 듯 흠칫 놀랐다. 그녀는 갑자기 내게서 몸을 빼내고 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n“나도 견뎌 내야지, 매리언. 할 수 있는 데까지.” 그녀가 말했다. “내 새 삶에는 힘든 의무들이 있고, 그중 하나가 오늘부터 시작되는 거야.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 창가의 작은 탁자로 갔다. 거기에는 그녀의 그림 도구들이 놓여 있었다. 그녀는 그것들을 조심스럽게 그러모아 장식장 서랍에 넣었다. 그러고는 서랍을 잠그고 열쇠를 내게 가져왔다.\n\n“그이를 떠올리게 하는 것과는 모두 헤어져야 해.” 그녀가 말했다. “열쇠는 매리언 마음대로 아무 데나 두어. 나는 다시는 그게 필요하지 않을 테니까.”\n\n내가 한마디 하기도 전에 그녀는 책장 쪽으로 돌아서서 월터 하트라이트의 그림들이 든 화첩을 꺼냈다. 그녀는 그 작은 책을 손에 애틋하게 든 채 잠시 망설이다가, 그것을 입술로 들어 올려 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“오, 로라! 로라!” 나는 말했다. 화가 나서도 아니고 나무라려는 것도 아니었다. 내 목소리에도 오직 슬픔뿐이었고, 내 마음에도 오직 슬픔뿐이었다.\n\n“이번이 마지막이야, 매리언.” 그녀가 애원하듯 말했다. “영영 작별을 고하는 거야.”\n\n그녀는 책을 탁자에 내려놓고 머리를 여며 두었던 빗을 뽑았다. 머리카락이 비할 데 없이 아름답게 등과 어깨 위로 흘러내려 허리 한참 아래까지 그녀를 감싸고 떨어졌다. 그녀는 그중에서 가늘고 긴 한 타래를 갈라내어 잘라 낸 다음, 화첩의 첫 백지에 동그라미 모양으로 조심스럽게 꽂아 두었다. 그것이 고정되자마자 그녀는 서둘러 책을 덮고 내 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n“매리언은 그이에게 편지를 쓰고, 그이도 매리언에게 편지를 쓰잖아.” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 살아 있는 동안 그이가 내 안부를 묻거든 언제나 잘 지낸다고 말해 줘. 불행하다는 말은 절대 하지 말고. 그이를 괴롭히지 마, 매리언. 나를 위해서, 그이를 괴롭히지 마. 만약 내가 먼저 죽거든, 그이의 그림이 든 이 작은 책을 내 머리카락과 함께 그이에게 주겠다고 약속해 줘. 내가 세상에 없을 때라면, 내가 이 손으로 직접 거기에 넣어 두었다고 말해도 해 될 게 없을 거야. 그리고 말해 줘. 오, 매리언, 그때 나를 대신해서 말해 줘. 내가 나 자신을 위해서는 결코 할 수 없는 그 말을. 내가 그이를 사랑했다고 말해 줘!”\n\n그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고 마지막 말을 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 그 말을 입 밖에 낸다는 격정적인 기쁨이 어찌나 배어 있던지, 듣고 있자니 내 가슴이 찢어질 것만 같았다. 그토록 오랫동안 스스로에게 지워 온 억제가 그 처음이자 마지막인 애정의 폭발 속에서 모두 무너져 내렸다. 그녀는 발작적인 격렬함으로 내게서 몸을 떼어 내더니, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 온몸을 뒤흔드는 흐느낌과 눈물의 발작 속에서 소파에 몸을 던졌다.\n\n나는 그녀를 달래고 타이르려 애썼지만 헛일이었다. 그녀는 달랠 수 있는 상태도, 타이를 수 있는 상태도 이미 지나 있었다. 그것이 우리 두 사람에게는 그 잊지 못할 하루의 슬프고 갑작스러운 끝이었다. 발작이 제풀에 잦아들었을 때 그녀는 너무 지쳐서 말도 하지 못했다. 오후 무렵 그녀는 잠이 들었고, 나는 그녀가 깨어났을 때 눈에 띄지 않도록 그림책을 치워 두었다. 그녀가 다시 눈을 뜨고 나를 바라보았을 때, 내 마음이야 어떻든 내 얼굴은 평온했다. 우리는 그날 아침의 그 괴로운 면담에 대해 서로 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 퍼시벌 경의 이름도 입에 오르지 않았다. 그날 남은 시간 동안 우리 둘 중 누구도 월터 하트라이트를 다시 입에 담지 않았다.\n\n10일.—오늘 아침 그녀가 차분하고 평소의 그녀다워진 것을 보고, 나는 어제의 그 괴로운 화제로 돌아갔다. 오로지 이 통탄스러운 결혼에 대해 그녀 자신이 두 사람 어느 쪽에게도 할 수 없는 말을 내가 더 분명하고 강하게 퍼시벌 경과 페얼리 씨에게 하도록 허락해 달라고 애원하기 위해서였다. 그녀는 내 항변이 한창일 때 부드럽지만 단호하게 말을 잘랐다.\n\n“나는 결정을 어제에 맡겨 두었어.” 그녀가 말했다. “그리고 어제가 정말로 결정을 내렸고. 이제 되돌리기에는 너무 늦었어.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 오늘 오후 로라의 방에서 있었던 일에 대해 내게 이야기했다. 그는 그녀가 자기에게 보여 준 유례없는 신뢰가 그녀의 결백함과 성실함에 대한 확신을 그만큼 자기 마음속에 불러일으켰기에, 그녀 앞에 있던 그 순간에도 물러난 뒤에도 한순간의 치졸한 질투조차 느낀 적이 없다고 내게 확언했다. 자기가 그녀의 존경과 애정 속에서 이루었을 진전을 가로막은 그 불행한 연정을 몹시 애석해하기는 하지만, 그는 그것이 과거에도 고백된 적 없이 남아 있었고 앞으로 상상할 수 있는 어떤 상황의 변화가 오더라도 미래에도 고백되지 않은 채로 남으리라 굳게 믿는다고 했다. 이것이 그의 절대적인 확신이었다. 그리고 그가 그 확신에 대해 내놓을 수 있는 가장 강력한 증거는, 그 연정이 최근의 것인지 아닌지, 또 그 상대가 누구였는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 조금도 없다고 지금 내놓는 바로 그 확언이라고 했다. 페얼리 양에 대한 무조건적인 신뢰가 있기에 그는 그녀가 자기에게 말하는 것이 옳다고 여긴 만큼으로 만족했으며, 더 듣고 싶은 조바심이라고는 티끌만큼도 없다는 점에서 정직하게 결백하다는 것이었다.\n\n그는 그 말을 마치고 기다리며 나를 바라보았다. 나는 그에 대한 내 부당한 편견을 너무나 의식하고 있었고, 방금 스스로 묻지 않겠다고 밝힌 바로 그 질문들에 내가 충동적으로 답해 주기를 그가 노리고 계산하는 것일지도 모른다는 치졸한 의심을 너무나 의식하고 있었기에, 스스로 얼마간 당혹감 비슷한 것을 느끼면서 이 대목에 대한 언급을 모조리 피해 버렸다. 그와 동시에 나는 로라를 위해 변론할 기회라면 아무리 작은 것이라도 놓치지 않겠다고 결심하고 있었으므로, 그의 아량이 한 걸음 더 나아가 그를 약혼에서 완전히 물러나게 하지는 못한 것이 유감이라고 대담하게 말했다.\n\n여기서도 그는 자신을 변호하려 들지 않음으로써 나를 무장 해제시켰다. 그는 다만 이 차이만은 기억해 달라고 청할 뿐이라고 했다. 페얼리 양이 자기를 버리도록 허락하는 것은 그저 순순히 따르는 문제일 뿐이지만, 자기가 억지로 페얼리 양을 버리는 것은 달리 말하면 자기 희망의 자살자가 되라고 요구하는 일이라는 차이 말이다. 전날 그녀의 처신은 두 해에 걸친 변함없는 사랑과 흠모를 오히려 더 굳게 만들어 놓아서, 이제 그로서는 그 감정에 맞서 능동적으로 다투는 일이 전혀 불가능해졌다는 것이었다. 내가 그를 나약하고 이기적이며 그가 그토록 떠받드는 바로 그 여인에게 무정하다고 여긴다면, 그는 되도록 체념하는 마음으로 내 의견에 머리를 숙이겠다고 했다. 다만 이것만은 함께 물어보고 싶다고 했다. 결코 인정할 수 없는 불행히 자리 잡은 연정에 시달리며 여위어 가는 독신 여자로서의 그녀의 앞날이, 그녀가 밟고 다니는 땅까지 숭배하는 남자의 아내로서의 앞날보다 훨씬 더 밝은 전망을 약속한다고 할 수 있겠느냐고. 뒤의 경우에는 아무리 희미하더라도 세월이 가져다줄 희망이 있지만, 앞의 경우에는 그녀 자신의 말대로 희망이라고는 전혀 없지 않으냐고.\n\n나는 그에게 대답했다. 무슨 설득력 있는 할 말이 있어서라기보다는, 내 혀가 여자의 혀여서 대답하지 않고는 못 배기기 때문이었다. 전날 로라가 취한 행로가 그에게 유리한 패를 내주었다는 것, 그가 원하기만 하면 집어 들 수 있는 패를 내주었다는 것, 그리고 그가 실제로 그것을 집어 들기로 했다는 것은 너무나 명백했다. 그때도 나는 그렇게 느꼈고, 지금 내 방에서 이 줄들을 쓰면서도 똑같이 강하게 느낀다. 남은 유일한 희망은 그의 동기가 정말로 그가 말하는 대로 로라를 향한 애정의 거스를 수 없는 힘에서 솟아난 것이기를 바라는 것뿐이다.\n\n오늘 밤 일기를 닫기 전에 기록해 두어야 할 것이 있다. 나는 오늘 가엾은 하트라이트를 위해 런던에 있는 어머니의 옛 친구 두 분에게 편지를 썼다. 두 분 다 영향력과 지위가 있는 분들이다. 그를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 있다면 그분들은 반드시 해 주시리라 나는 확신한다. 로라를 빼면, 나는 지금 월터에 대해 느끼는 만큼 누군가를 걱정해 본 적이 없다. 그가 우리를 떠난 뒤 일어난 모든 일은 그에 대한 나의 깊은 존경과 연민을 키워 놓기만 했다. 그가 외국에서 일자리를 얻도록 돕는 것이 옳은 일이기를 바란다. 그 일이 좋게 끝나기를 더없이 간절하고 애타게 바란다.\n\n11일.—퍼시벌 경이 페얼리 씨와 면담을 가졌고, 나에게도 그 자리에 오라는 전갈이 왔다.\n\n가 보니 페얼리 씨는 “집안의 골칫거리”(조카딸의 결혼을 그는 그렇게 부르기를 즐겼다)가 마침내 정리될 전망에 크게 안도하고 있었다. 거기까지는 내 의견을 그에게 말해야겠다는 생각이 들지 않았다. 그러나 그가 특유의 사람 속을 뒤집는 나른한 태도로, 이제는 퍼시벌 경의 뜻에 따라 결혼 시기를 정하는 편이 좋겠다고 제안하기에 이르자, 나는 로라의 결정을 재촉하는 데 대한 항의를 말로 담을 수 있는 한 가장 강하게 퍼부어 페얼리 씨의 신경을 공격하는 만족을 누렸다. 퍼시벌 경은 즉시 내 반대의 무게를 느낀다고 확언하며, 그 제안이 자기 쪽의 어떤 간섭 때문에 나온 것이 아님을 믿어 달라고 청했다. 페얼리 씨는 의자에 등을 기대고 눈을 감고는, 우리 둘 다 인간 본성의 명예가 된다고 말하더니, 퍼시벌 경도 나도 반대의 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다는 듯 태연하게 그 제안을 되풀이했다. 결국 나는 로라가 먼저 스스로 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않는 한 그 문제를 그녀에게 입에 올리지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 거절하는 것으로 끝을 냈다. 그 선언을 하고 나는 곧바로 방을 나왔다. 퍼시벌 경은 몹시 난처하고 괴로운 표정이었고, 페얼리 씨는 벨벳 발받침 위로 게으른 다리를 죽 뻗으며 말했다. “아, 매리언! 그 튼튼한 신경 조직이 얼마나 부러운지 모릅니다! 문은 쾅 닫지 마세요!”\n\n로라의 방에 가 보니 그녀가 나를 찾았고, 베지 부인이 내가 페얼리 씨와 함께 있다고 알려 주었다고 했다. 그녀는 곧바로 내가 무슨 일로 불려 갔었는지 물었고, 나는 있었던 일을 전부 말해 주었다. 내가 실제로 느낀 짜증과 분함을 감추려 하지도 않았다. 그녀의 대답은 이루 말할 수 없이 나를 놀라게 하고 괴롭혔다. 그것은 내가 그녀에게서 기대했을 법한 대답 중 가장 마지막의 것이었다.\n\n“삼촌 말씀이 옳아.” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 매리언에게도, 내 주위 모든 사람에게도 이미 충분히 말썽과 걱정을 끼쳤어. 더는 끼치지 않게 해 줘, 매리언. 퍼시벌 경이 정하시게 해.”\n\n나는 열을 내며 항의했지만, 내가 무슨 말을 해도 그녀를 움직이지 못했다.\n\n“나는 약혼에 매인 몸이야.” 그녀가 대답했다. “나는 예전의 삶과 결별했어. 내가 미룬다고 해서 그 불길한 날이 덜 확실하게 오는 것도 아니잖아. 아니야, 매리언! 다시 한번 말하지만 삼촌 말씀이 옳아. 나는 이미 충분히 말썽을 끼쳤고 충분히 걱정을 끼쳤어. 더는 끼치지 않겠어.”\n\n그녀는 예전에는 유순함 그 자체였지만, 지금은 체념 속에서, 거의 절망 속에서라고 해도 좋을 만큼 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 수동성을 보이고 있었다. 내가 그녀를 그토록 사랑하지만, 차라리 그녀가 격하게 동요했더라면 내 마음이 덜 아팠을 것이다. 지금 내가 보는 것처럼 차갑고 무감각한 그녀의 모습은 타고난 성품과 너무나 딴판이어서 충격적이었다.\n\n12일.—퍼시벌 경이 아침 식사 자리에서 로라에 대해 몇 가지를 물어 와서, 나는 그녀가 한 말을 그에게 전할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n우리가 이야기하는 동안 그녀가 직접 내려와 우리와 자리를 함께했다. 그녀는 퍼시벌 경 앞에서도 내 앞에서만큼이나 부자연스러울 만큼 침착했다. 아침 식사가 끝나자 그는 창가 후미진 자리에서 그녀에게 몇 마디 사사로이 건넬 기회를 얻었다. 두 사람이 함께 있었던 것은 이삼 분에 지나지 않았고, 헤어지면서 그녀는 베지 부인과 함께 방을 나갔으며 퍼시벌 경은 내게로 왔다. 그는 결혼 시기를 그녀 자신의 뜻과 마음대로 정하는 특권을 부디 그대로 지녀 달라고 그녀에게 간청했노라고 말했다. 그에 대해 그녀는 그저 고맙다는 뜻만 표하고, 그의 바람이 무엇인지 홀콤 양에게 말해 달라고 청했다는 것이었다.\n\n더 쓸 인내심이 없다. 이 경우에도 다른 모든 경우와 마찬가지로, 퍼시벌 경은 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 해도 아랑곳없이 자기에게 가능한 최대한의 명예를 안은 채 자기 뜻을 관철했다. 그의 바람은 지금도 물론 그가 처음 여기에 왔을 때와 같다. 그리고 로라는 결혼이라는 피할 수 없는 하나의 희생에 스스로를 내맡긴 채 여전히 차갑도록 희망 없이 견디고만 있다. 하트라이트를 떠올리게 하던 소소한 소일거리와 유품들과 헤어지면서, 그녀는 자신의 다정함과 감수성까지 모두 떠나보낸 듯하다. 이 줄들을 쓰는 지금은 오후 세 시밖에 되지 않았는데, 퍼시벌 경은 신랑다운 행복한 조바심으로 벌써 우리 곁을 떠나 햄프셔의 자기 집에서 신부를 맞을 준비를 하러 갔다. 이를 막을 무슨 특별한 일이 일어나지 않는 한, 두 사람은 그가 결혼하고 싶어 했던 바로 그 시기에, 올해가 가기 전에 결혼하게 될 것이다. 이 글을 쓰는 내 손가락이 다 타는 것 같다!\n\n13일.—로라 걱정에 잠 못 이룬 밤이었다. 아침 무렵 나는 환경을 바꾸어 보면 그녀를 일으켜 세울 수 있을지 시도해 보기로 결심했다. 리머리지에서 그녀를 데리고 나가 옛 친구들의 정다운 얼굴로 둘러싸 준다면, 설마 지금 같은 무감각한 마비 상태에 그대로 머물러 있지는 않겠지? 얼마간 궁리한 끝에 나는 요크셔의 아널드 댁에 편지를 쓰기로 정했다. 그들은 소박하고 마음씨 곱고 손님을 반기는 사람들이고, 그녀는 어릴 적부터 그들을 알아 왔다. 편지를 우편 자루에 넣고 나서 나는 그녀에게 내가 한 일을 말했다. 그녀가 반대하고 거부할 기개를 보였더라면 나는 오히려 마음이 놓였을 것이다. 그러나 그러지 않았다. 그녀는 그저 이렇게 말했을 뿐이다. “매리언과 함께라면 어디든 갈게. 매리언 말이 맞을 거야. 환경이 바뀌면 내게 좋겠지.”\n\n14일.—길모어 씨에게 편지를 써서 이 비참한 결혼이 정말로 성사될 전망이라는 것을 알리고, 로라를 위해 환경을 바꿔 보려는 내 생각도 언급했다. 자세한 사정까지 쓸 마음은 나지 않았다. 그런 이야기는 올해가 끝나 갈 무렵에 해도 늦지 않다.\n\n15일.—내게 온 편지가 세 통. 첫 번째는 아널드 댁에서 온 것으로, 로라와 나를 만날 기대에 기쁨이 넘쳤다. 두 번째는 월터 하트라이트를 위해 내가 편지를 보냈던 신사분 가운데 한 분에게서 온 것으로, 다행히 내 부탁을 들어줄 기회를 찾아냈다고 알려 왔다. 세 번째는 월터 본인에게서 온 것으로, 가엾게도 그는 집과 조국과 벗들을 떠날 기회를 준 데 대해 더없이 따뜻한 말로 내게 고마워하고 있었다. 중앙아메리카의 폐허가 된 도시들을 발굴하려는 사설 탐험대가 곧 리버풀에서 출항할 예정이라고 한다. 이미 동행하도록 임명되었던 제도사가 겁을 먹고 막판에 물러났는데, 월터가 그 자리를 채우게 되었다는 것이다. 그는 온두라스에 상륙한 때부터 최소 여섯 달간 고용되며, 발굴이 성공적이고 자금이 버텨 준다면 그 뒤로 일 년 더 일하게 된다. 그의 편지는 모두 배에 오르고 수로 안내인이 배를 떠날 때 내게 작별의 몇 줄을 쓰겠다는 약속으로 끝난다. 나는 그와 내가 이 일에서 둘 다 최선을 다해 행동하고 있기를 간절히 바라고 기도할 따름이다. 그가 내딛는 걸음이 어찌나 중대해 보이는지, 그것을 떠올리기만 해도 나는 흠칫 놀란다. 그렇지만 그 불행한 처지에서, 어떻게 그가 집에 남아 있기를 기대하거나 바랄 수 있겠는가?\n\n16일.—마차가 문 앞에 와 있다. 로라와 나는 오늘 아널드 댁을 방문하러 길을 나선다.\n\n# POLESDEAN LODGE, YORKSHIRE.\n\n23일.—이 새로운 풍경 속에서, 이 마음씨 고운 사람들 틈에서 보낸 한 주가 그녀에게 얼마쯤 도움이 되었다. 내가 바랐던 만큼은 아니지만. 나는 적어도 한 주는 더 머물기로 마음을 정했다. 꼭 돌아가야 할 절대적인 필요가 생기기 전에는 리머리지로 돌아가 봐야 소용없다.\n\n24일.—오늘 아침 우편으로 슬픈 소식이 왔다. 중앙아메리카 탐험대는 21일에 출항했다. 우리는 참된 사람과 헤어졌다. 우리는 충실한 벗을 잃었다. 월터 하트라이트는 영국을 떠났다.\n\n25일.—어제는 슬픈 소식이더니 오늘은 불길한 소식이다. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 썼고, 페얼리 씨는 로라와 내게 편지를 써서 우리를 당장 리머리지로 불러들였다.\n\n이게 무슨 뜻일까? 우리가 없는 사이에 결혼 날짜가 정해진 것일까?\n\n# LIMMERIDGE HOUSE.\n\n11월 27일.—나의 불길한 예감이 그대로 들어맞았다. 결혼식은 12월 22일로 정해졌다.\n\n우리가 폴스딘 로지로 떠난 다음 날, 퍼시벌 경은 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 보냈던 모양이다. 햄프셔에 있는 자기 저택의 수리와 개조가 애초 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 오래 걸리겠다는 내용이었다. 견적서가 되도록 빨리 자기에게 제출될 예정인데, 결혼식이 언제쯤 치러질지 정확한 시기를 알 수 있다면 인부들과 확정적인 약속을 맺는 데 큰 도움이 되리라는 것이었다. 그러면 시간에 관한 모든 계산을 세울 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 그해 겨울 자기 집을 찾아오기로 되어 있던 친구들에게—집이 인부들 손에 넘어가 있는 동안에는 물론 손님을 맞을 수 없으니—필요한 사과 편지도 쓸 수 있으리라고 했다.\n\n이 편지에 페얼리 씨는 답장을 보내, 퍼시벌 경 본인이 결혼 날짜를 정해 제안해 달라고, 다만 페얼리 양의 승낙을 조건으로 하되 그 승낙을 얻어 내는 일에는 후견인인 자기가 기꺼이 최선을 다하겠노라고 했다. 퍼시벌 경은 다음 우편으로 회신하여, (처음부터 품고 있던 자기 생각과 소망에 딱 들어맞게, 라고 해야 할까?) 12월 하순을—아마도 22일이나 24일, 혹은 신부와 후견인이 더 좋아할 만한 다른 어느 날을—제안했다. 정작 신부는 자기 의사를 말할 자리에 없었으므로, 후견인은 그 아이가 없는 사이에 언급된 날짜 가운데 가장 이른 날, 곧 12월 22일로 결정해 버렸고, 그에 따라 우리를 리머리지로 불러들이는 편지를 보냈던 것이다.\n\n어제 단둘이 만난 자리에서 이런 자초지종을 내게 설명한 뒤, 페얼리 씨는 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 태도로 오늘 내가 필요한 교섭을 시작해 주면 좋겠다고 했다. 로라의 뜻을 먼저 얻지 못하는 한 반대해 보아야 소용없다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 그 아이와 이야기해 보겠다고 응낙했다. 다만 어떤 경우에도 퍼시벌 경의 뜻에 그 아이가 동의하도록 만드는 일은 맡지 않겠다고 그 자리에서 분명히 못 박았다. 페얼리 씨는 나의 “훌륭한 양심”을 칭찬했는데, 그 말투란 마치 함께 산책을 나갔다가 나의 “훌륭한 체질”을 칭찬할 때와 조금도 다를 바가 없었다. 그리고 그는 집안의 책임 하나를 자기 어깨에서 내 어깨로 옮겨 놓았다는 사실만으로 지금까지는 아주 흡족한 듯 보였다.\n\n오늘 아침 나는 약속대로 로라에게 이야기했다. 퍼시벌 경이 떠난 이후로 그 아이가 그토록 이상하리만치, 그토록 굳세게 지켜 온 침착함—거의 무감각이라 해도 좋을 그 태도—도 내가 전해야 했던 소식의 충격 앞에서는 견디지 못했다. 그 아이는 얼굴이 창백해지더니 심하게 몸을 떨었다.\n\n“그렇게 빨리는 안 돼요!” 로라가 애원했다. “아, 매리언, 그렇게 빨리는 안 돼요!”\n\n그 아이가 내비칠 수 있는 가장 희미한 기색만으로도 내게는 충분했다. 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 페얼리 씨를 상대로 그 아이 대신 싸움을 벌이려고 방을 나서려 일어섰다.\n\n내 손이 막 문에 닿았을 때, 그 아이가 내 옷자락을 꽉 붙들어 나를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n“놓아 다오!” 내가 말했다. “네 삼촌과 퍼시벌 경이 모든 것을 제 뜻대로만 할 수는 없다고 말해 주고 싶어 혀가 근질거린다.”\n\n그 아이는 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬면서도 여전히 내 옷을 놓지 않았다.\n\n“안 돼요!” 로라가 힘없이 말했다. “너무 늦었어요, 매리언, 너무 늦었어요!”\n\n“단 일 분도 늦지 않았다.” 내가 되받았다. “시간 문제야말로 우리의 문제야. 그러니 내가 그것을 여자답게 한껏 이용하도록 나를 믿고 맡겨 다오, 로라.”\n\n말하면서 나는 내 가운에서 그 아이의 손을 떼어 냈다. 그러나 그 아이는 같은 순간 두 팔을 내 허리에 둘러 감아, 전보다 더 단단히 나를 붙들었다.\n\n“그래 봐야 우리를 더 큰 곤란과 더 큰 혼란에 빠뜨릴 뿐이에요.” 로라가 말했다. “매리언과 삼촌을 서로 등지게 만들고, 새로운 불평거리를 안은 퍼시벌 경을 다시 이곳으로 불러들이게 될 거예요—”\n\n“그러면 더욱 좋지!” 나는 격정에 차 외쳤다. “그 사람 불평거리 따위가 무슨 대수냐? 그 사람 마음 편하자고 네 가슴이 찢어져야 한단 말이냐? 하늘 아래 어떤 남자도 우리 여자들에게서 그런 희생을 받을 자격은 없다. 남자들이란! 그들은 우리의 순결과 평화를 해치는 적이야. 부모의 사랑과 자매의 우정에서 우리를 끌어내고, 우리의 몸과 영혼을 통째로 제 것으로 가져가서는, 개를 개집에 사슬로 매어 두듯 우리의 무력한 삶을 제 삶에 붙들어 매지. 그러고서 그중 가장 나은 자가 우리에게 무엇을 돌려주더냐? 놓아 다오, 로라—생각만 해도 나는 미칠 것 같다!”\n\n눈물이—비참하고 나약한, 분함과 노여움에서 나오는 여자의 눈물이—내 눈에 솟구쳤다. 그 아이는 슬프게 미소 지으며 내 얼굴 위에 손수건을 덮어, 내가 무엇보다 경멸한다는 것을 저도 알고 있는 그 나약함이 드러난 것을 나 대신 가려 주었다.\n\n“아, 매리언!” 로라가 말했다. “매리언이 울다니! 처지가 뒤바뀌어 이 눈물이 제 것이었다면 매리언이 제게 무슨 말을 했을지 생각해 보세요. 매리언의 사랑도 용기도 헌신도, 이르든 늦든 반드시 닥칠 일을 바꾸지는 못해요. 삼촌 뜻대로 하시게 두어요. 제가 희생하기만 하면 막을 수 있는 괴로움과 속앓이라면 더는 만들지 말아요. 제가 결혼하면 저와 함께 살아 주겠다고 말해 줘요, 매리언—그리고 더는 아무 말도 말아요.”\n\n하지만 나는 더 말했다. 나에게는 아무 위안도 되지 않고 그 아이를 괴롭히기만 하는 그 하찮은 눈물을 억지로 삼키고, 되도록 침착하게 이치를 따지고 간청했다. 소용없었다. 그 아이는 제가 결혼한 뒤 함께 살겠다는 약속을 내게 두 번이나 되풀이하게 하더니, 느닷없이 한 가지 질문을 던져 그 아이를 향한 내 슬픔과 연민을 전혀 다른 방향으로 돌려놓았다.\n\n“우리가 폴스딘에 있는 동안에요.” 로라가 말했다. “매리언에게 편지가 한 통 왔었지요——”\n\n달라진 목소리, 갑자기 내게서 눈길을 돌려 내 어깨에 얼굴을 묻던 그 태도, 질문을 채 마치기도 전에 말을 멈추게 한 그 머뭇거림—이 모든 것이, 절반쯤밖에 내뱉지 못한 그 물음이 누구를 가리키는지 너무나 분명하게 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n“로라, 우리 둘은 다시는 그 사람 이야기를 하지 않기로 하지 않았니.” 내가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n“그 사람에게서 편지가 왔지요?” 그 아이는 물러서지 않았다.\n\n“그래.” 내가 대답했다. “정 알아야겠다면.”\n\n“또 편지를 쓸 생각인가요?”\n\n나는 망설였다. 그가 영국을 떠났다는 사실도, 그의 새로운 희망과 계획을 도우려던 내 노력이 어떤 식으로 그의 출발과 얽히게 되었는지도, 나는 그 아이에게 말하기가 두려웠다. 무슨 대답을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그는 앞으로 몇 달, 어쩌면 몇 해 동안 어떤 편지도 닿을 수 없는 곳으로 떠나 버렸는데.\n\n“내가 다시 편지를 쓸 생각이라고 치자.” 마침내 내가 말했다. “그러면 어쩌겠다는 거냐, 로라?”\n\n내 목에 닿은 그 아이의 뺨이 불덩이처럼 뜨거워졌고, 나를 감은 두 팔이 떨리며 더욱 조여들었다.\n\n“그이에게 22일 이야기는 하지 말아요.” 로라가 속삭였다. “약속해 줘요, 매리언—다음에 편지를 쓸 때 제 이름조차 꺼내지 않겠다고 부디 약속해 줘요.”\n\n나는 약속했다. 얼마나 비통한 심정으로 그 약속을 했는지는 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없다. 그 아이는 곧바로 내 허리에서 팔을 풀고 창가로 걸어가, 내게 등을 돌린 채 밖을 내다보고 서 있었다. 잠시 후 그 아이는 다시 입을 열었으나, 몸을 돌리지도 않았고 제 얼굴을 조금이라도 내가 볼 수 있게 하지도 않았다.\n\n“삼촌 방으로 가시려는 거지요?” 로라가 물었다. “삼촌이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 무엇이든 따르겠다고 전해 주시겠어요? 저를 두고 가셔도 괜찮아요, 매리언. 잠시 혼자 있는 편이 나을 거예요.”\n\n나는 밖으로 나왔다. 복도로 나서자마자 손가락 하나만 들어 올려 페얼리 씨와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 땅끝으로 보내 버릴 수 있었다면, 나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그 손가락을 들었으리라. 이번만큼은 나의 그 고약한 성미가 내 편이 되어 주었다. 노여움의 열기에 눈물이 죄다 타 버리지 않았더라면, 나는 완전히 무너져 격렬하게 울음을 터뜨렸을 것이다. 하지만 실제로는 페얼리 씨의 방으로 뛰어들어 되도록 거칠게 “로라가 22일에 동의했습니다” 하고 외치고는, 대답 한마디 기다리지 않고 다시 뛰쳐나왔다. 나는 문을 쾅 닫았고, 그 소리로 그날 남은 시간 내내 페얼리 씨의 신경이 산산조각 났기를 바란다.\n\n28일.—오늘 아침 나는 가엾은 하트라이트의 작별 편지를 다시 읽었다. 그가 떠났다는 사실을 로라에게 숨기는 것이 과연 현명한 처사인지, 어제부터 마음에 의심이 들었기 때문이다.\n\n곰곰이 생각해 보아도 내 판단이 옳은 듯하다. 중앙아메리카 탐험을 위한 준비에 관해 편지에 언급된 대목들은 하나같이 그 일을 이끄는 이들조차 그것이 위험하다는 것을 알고 있음을 보여 준다. 이 사실을 알게 된 것이 나조차 불안하게 만든다면, 그 아이에게는 어떠하겠는가? 도움이 절실한 순간이—언젠가 그런 순간이 닥쳐 우리를 무력하게 만든다면—그때 헌신을 믿고 기댈 수 있는 단 하나의 친구를 그의 출발이 앗아 갔다는 사실만으로도 이미 충분히 괴롭다. 하물며 그가 우리 곁을 떠나 나쁜 기후와 거친 땅과 소요에 휩싸인 주민들의 위험을 마주하러 갔다는 것을 안다면 그보다 훨씬 더 견디기 어려울 것이다. 절박하고 분명한 필요도 없이 이런 이야기를 로라에게 하는 것은 잔인한 솔직함이 아니겠는가?\n\n한 걸음 더 나아가, 언젠가 이 편지가 엉뚱한 손에 들어갈까 두려우니 당장 태워 버려야 하지 않을까 하는 생각마저 든다. 그 편지는 쓴 사람과 나 사이에 영영 비밀로 남아야 할 표현으로 로라를 언급하고 있을 뿐 아니라, 리머리지를 떠난 뒤로 자신이 은밀히 감시당하고 있다는 그 고집스럽고 설명할 길 없고 불안하기 짝이 없는 의심을 거듭 되풀이하고 있다. 그는 런던 거리에서 자기를 따라다니던 낯선 두 남자의 얼굴을, 탐험대의 출항을 구경하러 리버풀에 모인 군중 속에서 자기를 지켜보고 있는 모습으로 다시 보았다고 단언한다. 그리고 배에 오를 때 등 뒤에서 앤 캐서릭이라는 이름이 불리는 것을 분명히 들었다고 잘라 말한다. 그의 표현을 그대로 옮기면 이렇다. “이 사건들에는 의미가 있습니다. 이 사건들은 반드시 어떤 결말로 이어질 것입니다. 앤 캐서릭의 수수께끼는 아직 풀리지 않았습니다. 그 여자가 다시는 내 앞을 지나가지 않을지도 모르지만, 혹시라도 홀콤 양 앞을 지나가거든 내가 그 기회를 썼던 것보다 부디 더 잘 써 주십시오. 나는 굳은 확신에서 말하는 것입니다. 내가 하는 말을 기억해 주시기를 간청합니다.” 이것이 그가 쓴 그대로의 말이다. 내가 그 말을 잊을 염려는 없다. 앤 캐서릭과 관련된 하트라이트의 말이라면 내 기억은 지나칠 만큼 쉽게 곱씹으니까. 그러나 그 편지를 간직하는 데에는 위험이 있다. 아주 사소한 우연만으로도 그것이 낯선 이들의 손에 넘어갈 수 있다. 내가 병이 들 수도 있고—죽을 수도 있다. 당장 태워 버리고 근심 하나를 더는 편이 낫다.\n\n태웠다. 그의 작별 편지—어쩌면 그가 내게 쓰는 마지막 편지가 될지도 모를 그것—의 재가 벽난로 바닥에 검은 부스러기 몇 조각으로 남아 있다. 이것이 그 모든 슬픈 이야기의 슬픈 끝인가? 아, 끝일 리 없다—분명히, 분명히 아직 끝은 아니다!\n\n29일.—결혼 준비가 시작되었다. 재봉사가 주문을 받으러 왔다. 로라는 여자의 개인적인 관심이 무엇보다도 가장 깊이 얽히는 이 문제에 대해 조금도 동요하지 않고, 조금도 마음을 쓰지 않는다. 모든 것을 재봉사와 나에게 맡겨 버렸다. 가엾은 하트라이트가 준남작이었고 그 아이 아버지가 택한 남편이었다면, 그 아이는 얼마나 다르게 굴었을까! 얼마나 조바심 내고 변덕을 부렸을 것이며, 아무리 솜씨 좋은 재봉사라도 그 아이를 만족시키기가 얼마나 힘겨웠을까!\n\n30일.—퍼시벌 경에게서 날마다 소식이 온다. 가장 최근 소식은 그의 저택 개조가 제대로 마무리되려면 넉 달에서 여섯 달은 걸리리라는 것이다. 페인트공과 도배공과 실내장식업자가 화려함뿐 아니라 행복까지 만들어 낼 수 있다면, 나는 로라가 앞으로 살 집에서 벌어지는 그들의 작업에 관심을 가졌을 것이다. 그러나 실상 퍼시벌 경의 지난번 편지에서 그의 온갖 계획과 구상에 대한 나의 완전한 무관심을 그나마 흔들어 놓은 대목은 신혼여행에 관한 부분뿐이다. 그는 로라가 몸이 약하고 이번 겨울이 유난히 혹독하리라 하니, 그 아이를 로마로 데려가 이듬해 초여름까지 이탈리아에 머물자고 제안한다. 만약 이 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다면, 자기는 런던 시내에 따로 거처가 없기는 하지만 그 목적에 가장 알맞은 가구 딸린 집을 구해 런던에서 사교철을 보내는 것도 마찬가지로 좋다고 한다.\n\n나 자신과 내 감정은 전적으로 문제 밖에 두고—그렇게 하는 것이 내 의무이며 나는 그렇게 해 왔다—적어도 나로서는 이 두 제안 가운데 첫 번째를 받아들이는 것이 옳다는 데 조금도 의심이 없다. 어느 쪽이든 로라와 나의 이별은 피할 수 없다. 두 사람이 외국으로 나가면 런던에 머무는 경우보다 이별이 더 길어지겠지만, 그 불리함에 맞세워야 할 것은 그 아이에게 돌아올 이로움이다. 온화한 기후에서 겨울을 나는 것, 그리고 그보다 더 중요하게는, 세상에서 가장 흥미로운 나라를 난생처음 여행한다는 그 놀라움과 설렘만으로도 그 아이의 기운을 북돋우고 새로운 삶과 화해하게 하는 데 크나큰 도움이 되리라는 것이다. 그 아이는 런던의 판에 박힌 유흥과 자극에서 위안을 찾을 성품이 아니다. 그런 것들은 이 개탄스러운 결혼이 처음으로 주는 압박을 그 아이에게 더욱 무겁게 얹어 놓을 뿐이리라. 나는 그 아이의 새 삶이 시작되는 것이 말로 다 못 할 만큼 두렵다. 그러나 여행을 한다면 얼마간 희망이 보이고, 집에 머문다면 아무 희망도 보이지 않는다.\n\n일기의 이 마지막 대목을 돌이켜 보고, 내가 결혼과 로라와의 이별을 마치 이미 정해진 일을 적듯 쓰고 있음을 발견하니 이상한 노릇이다. 벌써부터 이토록 잔인하리만치 차분한 태도로 앞일을 내다본다는 것이 참으로 차갑고 무정해 보인다. 그러나 때가 이렇게 가까워진 마당에 달리 어떤 태도가 가능하겠는가? 한 달이 채 지나기 전에 그 아이는 내 로라가 아니라 그의 로라가 될 것이다! 그의 로라라니! 그 두 마디가 담고 있는 뜻을 나는 도무지 실감하지 못하겠다. 그 아이의 결혼에 대해 쓰는 것이 마치 그 아이의 죽음에 대해 쓰는 것과 같기라도 한 듯, 내 정신은 그 말에 둔해지고 얼얼해질 따름이다.\n\n12월 1일.—슬프고 슬픈 날, 길게 적을 마음이 도무지 나지 않는 하루였다. 어젯밤 나약하게 미루었던 일을 오늘 아침에는 하는 수 없이 해야 했다. 신혼여행에 관한 퍼시벌 경의 제안을 그 아이에게 이야기한 것이다.\n\n어디를 가든 내가 함께 있으리라 굳게 믿고 있던 터라, 그 가엾은 아이는—여러모로 아직 어린아이나 다름없다—피렌체와 로마와 나폴리의 경이를 보게 되리라는 기대에 거의 행복해했다. 그 환상을 깨뜨리고 그 아이를 모진 진실 앞에 마주 세우는 일에 내 가슴은 찢어질 듯했다. 나는 어떤 남자든 결혼 초에는 아내의 애정을 두고 경쟁자를—그것이 여자 경쟁자라 할지라도—참지 못한다는 것을, 나중에는 어떻게 하든 간에 처음에는 그렇다는 것을 그 아이에게 말해야만 했다. 또 내가 그 아이의 지붕 아래 오래도록 함께 살 수 있을지는, 결혼 초에 두 사람 사이에 끼어들어 아내의 가장 은밀한 비밀을 맡아 두는 사람 노릇을 함으로써 퍼시벌 경의 질투와 불신을 자극하지 않느냐에 전적으로 달려 있다고 그 아이에게 일러 주어야만 했다. 이 세상 지혜의 그 더럽히는 쓴 즙을 나는 한 방울 한 방울 그 순결한 마음과 티 없는 정신에 부어 넣었고, 그러는 동안 내 안의 더 높고 더 나은 감정은 모두 이 비참한 소임 앞에서 움츠러들었다. 이제 다 끝났다. 그 아이는 모질고도 피할 수 없는 교훈을 배웠다. 소녀 시절의 단순한 환상은 사라졌고, 그것을 벗겨 낸 것은 내 손이었다. 그의 손보다는 내 손이 낫다—그것만이 내 유일한 위안이다—그의 손보다는 내 손이 낫다.\n\n그리하여 첫 번째 제안이 받아들여진 제안이 되었다. 두 사람은 이탈리아로 가고, 나는 퍼시벌 경의 허락을 얻어 두 사람이 영국으로 돌아올 때 만나 함께 지내도록 준비하기로 했다. 달리 말하면, 나는 태어나 처음으로 개인적인 부탁을 해야 하며, 그것도 하필 어떤 종류의 무거운 신세도 지고 싶지 않은 바로 그 사람에게 해야 한다. 그래! 로라를 위해서라면 그보다 더한 일도 할 수 있으리라 생각한다.\n\n2일.—돌이켜 보니 나는 언제나 퍼시벌 경을 깎아내리는 말로 적고 있었다. 일이 이렇게 된 마당에, 나는 그에 대한 편견을 반드시 뿌리 뽑아야 하고 또 뽑을 것이다. 그것이 처음에 어떻게 내 마음에 들어왔는지 도무지 알 수 없다. 예전에는 분명 그런 것이 없었다.\n\n그의 아내가 되기를 꺼리는 로라의 마음이 나를 그에게 등 돌리게 한 것일까? 하트라이트의, 충분히 이해할 만한 그 편견이 나도 모르는 사이에 내게 옮은 것일까? 퍼시벌 경의 해명이 있었고 그 해명이 사실임을 입증하는 증거까지 내 손에 있는데도, 앤 캐서릭의 그 편지가 여전히 내 마음속에 잠복한 불신을 남겨 놓은 것일까? 내 감정이 왜 이런 상태인지 나는 설명할 수 없다. 다만 한 가지 확실한 것은, 부당하게 그를 의심함으로써 퍼시벌 경에게 잘못을 저지르지 않는 것이 내 의무—이제는 곱절로 내 의무—라는 사실이다. 만약 그에 대해 언제나 같은 식으로 나쁘게 쓰는 것이 내 버릇이 되어 버렸다면, 나는 이 못난 습성을 반드시 고쳐야 하고 또 고칠 것이다. 설령 그러기 위해 결혼이 끝날 때까지 이 일기장을 덮어야 한다 해도 말이다! 나는 나 자신이 몹시 못마땅하다—오늘은 더 쓰지 않겠다.\n\n12월 16일.—꼬박 보름이 지나도록 나는 이 일기장을 한 번도 펴지 않았다. 일기에서 충분히 오래 떨어져 있었으니, 적어도 퍼시벌 경에 관한 한 좀 더 건강하고 나은 마음으로 돌아왔기를 바란다.\n\n지난 두 주 동안 기록할 일은 그리 많지 않다. 드레스는 거의 다 완성되었고, 새 여행 가방들이 런던에서 이곳으로 도착했다. 가엾고 사랑스러운 로라는 온종일 잠시도 내 곁을 떠나지 않고, 어젯밤에는 둘 다 잠을 이루지 못하자 내 침대로 기어 들어와 거기서 이야기를 나누었다. “이렇게 곧 매리언을 잃게 되잖아요.” 그 아이가 말했다. “그러니 있을 수 있을 때 실컷 곁에 있어야지요.”\n\n두 사람은 리머리지 교회에서 결혼식을 올리기로 했고, 고맙게도 이웃 가운데 식에 초대된 사람은 하나도 없다. 유일한 손님은 우리의 오랜 친구 아널드 씨로, 로라의 손을 잡고 신랑에게 넘겨 주기 위해 폴스딘에서 오기로 했다. 그 아이의 삼촌은 몸이 너무 약해서 지금 같은 궂은 날씨에 문밖을 나설 엄두를 내지 못하기 때문이다. 오늘부터는 우리 앞날의 밝은 면만 보기로 굳게 마음먹지 않았더라면, 인생에서 가장 중요한 순간에 로라 쪽 남자 친척이 한 사람도 없다는 그 서글픈 사실이 나를 몹시 우울하게, 또 몹시 앞날을 믿지 못하게 만들었으리라. 그러나 나는 우울과 불신과는 이제 작별했다—다시 말해, 이 일기에 그 둘 중 어느 것에 대해서도 쓰지 않기로 했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 내일 도착한다. 그는 우리가 그를 엄격한 예법대로 대하기를 바란다면, 결혼 전 리머리지에 머무는 짧은 기간 동안 목사관에 신세를 지게 해 달라고 우리 교구 목사에게 편지를 써서 청하겠다고 했다. 사정이 이러하니 페얼리 씨도 나도 사소한 형식과 예절에 신경 쓸 필요가 조금도 없다고 여겼다. 이 거친 황야의 고장, 이 크고 외로운 저택에서라면, 다른 곳 사람들을 옭아매는 하찮은 관습 따위는 우리에게 미치지 못한다고 얼마든지 주장할 만하다. 나는 퍼시벌 경에게 편지를 써서 그 정중한 제안에 감사를 표하고, 예전처럼 리머리지 하우스의 늘 쓰던 방을 쓰시라고 청했다.\n\n17일.—그가 오늘 도착했다. 내 눈에는 얼마간 지치고 근심스러워 보였으나, 그래도 더할 나위 없이 기분 좋은 사람처럼 이야기하고 웃었다. 그는 참으로 아름다운 보석 선물을 몇 가지 가져왔고, 로라는 더없이 우아하게, 적어도 겉으로는 완벽하게 침착한 태도로 그것을 받았다. 이 힘겨운 시기에 겉모습을 지키느라 그 아이가 치르고 있을 싸움을 짐작하게 하는 유일한 조짐은, 혼자 남겨지는 것을 갑자기 꺼리게 되었다는 점이다. 여느 때처럼 제 방으로 물러가기는커녕, 그 아이는 그리로 가기를 두려워하는 듯하다. 오늘 점심 뒤에 산책하려고 모자를 쓰러 내가 위층에 올라갔더니 그 아이는 자청해서 따라나섰고, 저녁 식사 전에도 옷을 갈아입는 동안 서로 이야기할 수 있도록 우리 두 방 사이의 문을 활짝 열어 두었다. “늘 무언가 할 일을 주세요.” 그 아이가 말했다. “늘 누군가와 함께 있게 해 주세요. 생각하게 두지 말아요—지금 제가 바라는 건 그것뿐이에요, 매리언—생각하게 두지 말아요.”\n\n그 아이의 이 서글픈 변화는 퍼시벌 경에게는 그 아이의 매력을 더해 줄 뿐이다. 내가 보기에 그는 그것을 자기에게 유리한 쪽으로 해석한다. 그 아이의 뺨에는 열에 들뜬 홍조가, 눈에는 열에 들뜬 광채가 어려 있는데, 그는 그것을 미모의 회복이자 기운의 회복으로 반긴다. 오늘 저녁 식사 자리에서 그 아이는 어찌나 거짓되고 어찌나 제 성품에 어울리지 않게 명랑하고 무심한 태도로 이야기하던지, 나는 속으로 그 아이를 입 다물게 하고 데리고 나가고 싶은 마음이 간절했다. 퍼시벌 경의 기쁨과 놀라움은 이루 말할 수 없어 보였다. 그가 도착했을 때 내가 그 얼굴에서 보았던 근심은 말끔히 사라졌고, 내 눈에조차 그는 실제 나이보다 족히 열 살은 젊어 보였다.\n\n의심할 여지가 없다—어떤 이상한 심술이 나 자신으로 하여금 그것을 보지 못하게 하지만—로라의 미래의 남편이 매우 잘생긴 남자라는 데에는 의심할 여지가 없다. 우선 반듯한 이목구비는 그 자체로 이점인데, 그는 그것을 갖추었다. 남자든 여자든 밝은 갈색 눈은 큰 매력인데, 그는 그것도 갖추었다. 벗어진 머리조차, 그의 경우처럼 이마 위쪽만 벗어진 것이라면 남자에게 어울리지 않기는커녕 오히려 어울린다. 머리가 높아 보이고 얼굴에 지성이 더해지기 때문이다. 우아하고 편안한 몸가짐, 지칠 줄 모르는 활기, 막힘없고 유연한 화술—이 모두가 두말할 나위 없는 장점이고, 이 모두를 그는 분명히 지니고 있다. 로라의 비밀을 모르는 길모어 씨가 그 아이가 약혼을 후회한다는 데 놀랐다 한들, 어찌 그를 탓할 수 있겠는가? 그의 자리에 있었다면 누구라도 우리의 그 선량한 옛 친구와 같은 생각을 했으리라. 지금 이 순간 퍼시벌 경에게서 어떤 결점을 발견했는지 솔직히 말해 보라 한다면, 나는 두 가지밖에 짚어 낼 수 없다. 하나는 그의 끊임없는 안절부절과 흥분하기 쉬운 성미인데, 이는 남달리 왕성한 기질 탓이라고 얼마든지 볼 수 있다. 다른 하나는 하인들에게 말할 때의 짧고 날카롭고 성마른 말투인데, 이 역시 결국은 나쁜 버릇에 지나지 않을 수 있다. 아니, 나는 그것을 부인할 수 없고 부인하지도 않겠다—퍼시벌 경은 매우 잘생기고 매우 유쾌한 남자다. 자! 마침내 이렇게 적어 놓았으니, 끝나서 기쁘다.\n\n18일.—오늘 아침 몸이 나른하고 기분이 가라앉아, 나는 로라를 베지 부인에게 맡겨 두고 요즘 너무 소홀히 했던 한낮의 활기찬 산책을 혼자 나갔다. 나는 토드 코너로 이어지는, 황야를 가로지르는 마르고 바람 좋은 길을 택했다. 나선 지 반 시간쯤 되었을 때, 농장 쪽에서 퍼시벌 경이 나를 향해 걸어오는 것을 보고 나는 몹시 놀랐다. 그는 지팡이를 휘두르며 여느 때처럼 고개를 꼿꼿이 들고 사냥 재킷 앞자락을 바람에 펄럭이면서 빠르게 걷고 있었다. 우리가 마주쳤을 때 그는 내가 무언가 묻기를 기다리지도 않고, 지난번 리머리지에 왔던 이후로 토드 씨나 토드 부인이 앤 캐서릭에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 들은 것이 있는지 알아보러 농장에 다녀오는 길이라고 대뜸 말했다.\n\n“물론 아무 소식도 못 들었다고 하던가요?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“전혀 없다더군요.” 그가 대답했다. “저는 그 여자를 아주 놓쳐 버린 것이 아닌가 하고 심각하게 걱정되기 시작합니다. 혹시 아십니까,” 그는 내 얼굴을 아주 유심히 들여다보며 말을 이었다. “그 화가—하트라이트 씨—가 우리에게 무언가 더 알려 줄 만한 처지에 있는지요?”\n\n“그분은 컴벌랜드를 떠난 뒤로 그 여자의 소식을 듣지도, 그 여자를 보지도 못했습니다.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n“참으로 안타깝군요.” 퍼시벌 경이 말했다. 실망한 사람 같은 말투였으나, 이상하게도 동시에 안도한 사람처럼 보였다. “그 딱한 여자에게 어떤 불행이 닥쳤을지 알 도리가 없습니다. 그토록 절실히 필요한 보살핌과 보호를 되찾아 주려던 제 노력이 모두 허사가 되었으니 이루 말할 수 없이 분합니다.”\n\n이번에는 그가 정말로 분해 보였다. 나는 몇 마디 위로의 말을 건넸고, 우리는 저택으로 돌아오는 길에 다른 이야기를 나누었다. 황야에서 그와 우연히 마주친 일이 그의 성품의 또 다른 좋은 면을 드러내 준 것이 아니겠는가? 결혼을 코앞에 두고 앤 캐서릭을 떠올리고, 로라와 함께 있었다면 훨씬 즐겁게 보낼 수 있었을 시간에 그 먼 토드 코너까지 걸어가 그 여자의 소식을 물어보다니, 참으로 남달리 사려 깊고 이기심 없는 일이 아닌가? 순전히 자비로운 동기에서 한 행동일 수밖에 없음을 생각하면, 이런 상황에서 그의 처신은 보기 드문 선한 마음을 보여 주며 각별한 칭찬을 받아 마땅하다. 그래! 나는 그에게 각별한 칭찬을 보낸다—그것으로 끝이다.\n\n19일.—퍼시벌 경의 미덕이라는 마르지 않는 광맥에서 또 새로운 것들이 발견되었다.\n\n오늘 나는 그가 아내를 데리고 영국으로 돌아왔을 때 그 지붕 아래 함께 머물고자 하는 내 뜻을 꺼내 보았다. 그 방향으로 첫마디를 던지기가 무섭게 그는 내 손을 따뜻하게 붙잡으며, 실은 자기 쪽에서 내게 간청하고 싶어 못 견디던 바로 그 제안을 내가 해 주었다고 말했다. 자기가 아내를 위해 진심으로 얻고 싶었던 벗은 다른 누구도 아닌 나였다면서, 결혼 전에 늘 그랬던 것과 똑같이 결혼 뒤에도 로라와 함께 살겠다고 제안해 준 것은 자기에게 두고두고 베푼 은혜이니 부디 그리 믿어 달라고 했다.\n\n우리 두 사람 모두에게 베푼 그 사려 깊은 친절에 대해 로라와 내 이름으로 감사를 표한 뒤, 우리는 그의 신혼여행 이야기로 넘어가 로마에서 로라가 소개받게 될 영국인 사교계에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다. 그는 이번 겨울 외국에서 만나리라 기대하는 몇몇 친구의 이름을 죽 늘어놓았다. 내 기억이 맞다면 한 사람만 빼고 모두 영국인이었다. 그 한 사람의 예외가 포스코 백작이었다.\n\n백작의 이름이 나온 것, 그리고 그와 그의 아내가 대륙에서 신혼부부를 만날 듯하다는 사실을 알게 된 것은, 처음으로 로라의 결혼을 뚜렷하게 유리한 빛 아래 놓아 준다. 그것은 집안의 반목을 치유하는 계기가 될 법하다. 지금까지 포스코 백작부인은 유산 문제에서 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 보인 처사에 대한 순전한 앙심 때문에, 로라의 고모로서 지켜야 할 도리를 잊기로 작정하고 있었다. 그러나 이제는 그런 태도를 더 고집할 수 없게 되었다. 퍼시벌 경과 포스코 백작은 오래되고 굳건한 친구 사이이니, 두 사람의 아내도 예의를 갖추어 만나는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없을 것이다. 처녀 시절의 포스코 백작부인은 내가 만나 본 여자 가운데 가장 무례한 축에 들었다—변덕스럽고, 요구가 많고, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 허영에 차 있었다. 만약 그 남편이 그녀를 제정신으로 돌려놓는 데 성공했다면, 그는 이 집안 모든 사람의 감사를 받아 마땅하며, 우선 내 감사부터 받아도 좋을 것이다.\n\n나는 백작을 알고 싶어 마음이 조급해지기 시작한다. 그는 로라의 남편의 가장 절친한 벗이고, 그 점에서 내 관심을 가장 강하게 자극한다. 로라도 나도 그를 본 적이 없다. 내가 그에 관해 아는 것이라고는, 몇 해 전 로마의 트리니타 델 몬테 계단에서 그가 우연히 그 자리에 있었던 덕분에, 퍼시벌 경이 손에 상처를 입고 다음 순간에는 가슴에 상처를 입을 뻔했던 그 위태로운 찰나에 강도와 암살에서 벗어날 수 있었다는 것뿐이다. 또 기억나는 것은, 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 제 누이의 결혼에 터무니없는 반대를 하던 때에 백작이 그 문제에 관해 매우 절제되고 사리에 밝은 편지를 보냈으나, 부끄럽게도 그 편지가 답장을 받지 못한 채 남았다는 사실이다. 퍼시벌 경의 친구에 대해 내가 아는 것은 이것이 전부다. 그가 언젠가 영국에 오게 될까? 내가 그를 좋아하게 될까?\n\n내 펜이 한낱 억측으로 달아나고 있다. 냉정한 사실로 돌아가자. 그의 아내와 함께 살겠다는 나의 당돌한 제안을 퍼시벌 경이 받아들인 태도가 친절 이상이었다는 것, 거의 애정 어린 것이었다는 사실만은 분명하다. 내가 시작한 대로만 계속해 나갈 수 있다면 로라의 남편이 나를 두고 불평할 일은 없으리라 믿는다. 나는 이미 그가 잘생겼고, 유쾌하고, 불행한 이들에 대한 따뜻한 마음이 가득하고, 나에 대한 다정한 친절이 가득하다고 적어 놓았다. 정말이지 퍼시벌 경의 가장 열렬한 친구라는 이 새로운 배역을 맡은 나 자신을 나조차 알아보기 어려울 지경이다.\n\n20일.—나는 퍼시벌 경이 밉다! 그가 잘생겼다는 것을 딱 잘라 부인한다. 나는 그를 지독히 성마르고 불쾌하며, 친절과 따뜻한 마음이라고는 도무지 없는 사람이라고 여긴다. 어젯밤 신혼부부의 명함이 집으로 배달되었다. 로라가 꾸러미를 열고 제 앞날의 이름이 인쇄된 것을 처음으로 보았다. 퍼시벌 경은 페얼리 양을 이미 글라이드 부인으로 바꾸어 놓은 그 새 명함을 그 아이의 어깨 너머로 스스럼없이 들여다보더니, 더없이 역겨운 자기만족의 미소를 띠고 그 아이의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다. 그것이 무슨 말이었는지 나는 모른다—로라가 말해 주기를 거부했으니까—그러나 나는 그 아이의 얼굴이 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았고, 기절하는 줄로만 알았다. 그는 그 변화를 알아채지도 못했다—자기가 그 아이를 아프게 할 말을 했다는 것을 야만스러우리만큼 의식하지 못하는 듯했다. 그를 향한 나의 옛 적개심이 그 순간 되살아났고, 그 뒤로 지나간 시간들도 그것을 조금도 흩어 놓지 못했다. 나는 그 어느 때보다 더 사리에 어긋나고 더 부당하다. 세 마디로—내 펜이 어쩌면 이토록 술술 적어 내는지!—세 마디로, 나는 그가 밉다.\n\n21일.—이 불안한 시기의 근심들이 마침내 나를 조금 흔들어 놓은 것일까? 나는 지난 며칠 동안 하늘도 아시다시피 내 진심과는 한참 동떨어진 가벼운 어조로 글을 써 왔고, 일기의 지난 대목들을 돌이켜 보다 그것을 발견하고는 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n지난 한 주 동안 열에 들뜬 로라의 기분이 내게 옮았는지도 모른다. 그렇다면 그 발작은 이미 내게서 지나갔고, 나를 아주 이상한 마음 상태에 남겨 두었다. 어젯밤부터 줄곧 한 가지 생각이 끈질기게 내 마음을 파고들고 있다. 결혼을 가로막을 무슨 일인가가 아직 일어나리라는 생각이다. 무엇이 이런 기이한 공상을 낳았을까? 로라의 앞날에 대한 나의 염려가 간접적으로 빚어낸 것일까? 아니면 결혼식 날이 가까워질수록 퍼시벌 경의 태도에서 분명히 눈에 띄게 심해지는 안절부절과 짜증이 나도 모르는 사이에 그런 생각을 심어 준 것일까? 알 수 없다. 내가 그런 생각을 품고 있다는 것만은 안다—이런 상황에서 여자의 머리에 떠오른 것 중 가장 터무니없는 생각이 아니겠는가?—그러나 아무리 애를 써도 그 근원을 되짚을 수가 없다.\n\n이 마지막 하루는 온통 혼란과 비참함이었다. 이 일을 어떻게 적을 수 있을까?—그러면서도 나는 적어야 한다. 무엇이든 내 어두운 생각을 곱씹는 것보다는 낫다.\n\n요즘 우리 모두가 너무 소홀히 하고 잊고 지냈던 친절한 베지 부인이, 아무 악의 없이 이 하루의 시작을 슬프게 만들었다. 부인은 지난 몇 달 동안 사랑하는 제자를 위해 따뜻한 셰틀랜드 숄을 몰래 만들고 있었다—그 연세와 그 생활 습관을 지닌 여인이 해냈다고는 믿기 어려울 만큼 아름답고 놀라운 솜씨였다. 그 선물이 오늘 아침에 전해졌고, 어머니 없이 자란 어린 시절의 다정한 옛 벗이자 보호자였던 부인이 자랑스레 그 숄을 어깨에 걸쳐 주자, 마음 여린 가엾은 로라는 그만 무너져 울음을 터뜨렸다. 두 사람을 달래거나 내 눈물을 닦을 겨를도 거의 없이, 나는 페얼리 씨에게 불려 가 결혼식 날 자신의 안정을 지키기 위한 그의 준비 사항에 대해 장황한 설명을 듣는 영광을 누려야 했다.\n\n“사랑하는 로라”는 그분의 선물을 받게 되어 있었다—보석 대신 다정한 삼촌의 머리카락을 장식으로 박은 초라한 반지, 안쪽에는 마음이 통하는 정과 영원한 우정 운운하는 무정한 프랑스어 명문이 새겨진 반지를. “사랑하는 로라”는 이 애틋한 정표를 지체 없이 내 손에서 받아야 했다. 페얼리 씨 앞에 나서기 전에, 선물이 불러일으킨 감동에서 넉넉히 회복할 시간을 갖도록. “사랑하는 로라”는 그날 저녁에 그분께 잠깐 문안을 드려야 했고, 부디 소란을 피우지 말아 주어야 했다. “사랑하는 로라”는 이튿날 아침 웨딩드레스 차림으로 다시 한 번 잠깐 문안을 드려야 했고, 이번에도 부디 소란을 피우지 말아 주어야 했다. “사랑하는 로라”는 떠나기 전에 세 번째로 한 번 더 들러야 했다. 다만 언제 떠나는지를 말해 그분의 감정을 후벼 파는 일은 없어야 했고, 눈물도 없어야 했다—“제발 부탁이니, 다정하고 가정적이고 더없이 사랑스럽고 매력적으로 침착한 모든 것의 이름으로 부탁하네, 매리언, 눈물은 없이!” 이런 때에 이 가엾도록 이기적인 시시덕거림을 듣고 있자니 어찌나 부아가 치밀던지, 폴스딘에서 아널드 씨가 도착한 덕에 아래층의 새 일거리로 불려 가지 않았더라면 나는 틀림없이 페얼리 씨가 평생 들어 본 적 없는 가장 모질고 무례한 진실을 몇 마디 퍼부어 그분을 기함하게 만들었을 것이다.\n\n그날의 나머지는 이루 말할 수가 없다. 그 하루가 어떻게 지나갔는지 이 집에서 제대로 아는 사람은 아무도 없으리라 믿는다. 자잘한 일들이 뒤죽박죽 겹쳐 쌓이는 혼란에 모두가 정신이 없었다. 잊고 있던 옷들이 배달되어 왔고—짐 가방은 쌌다가 풀었다가 다시 싸야 했으며—멀고 가까운 벗들, 지체 높은 벗들과 낮은 벗들에게서 선물이 들어왔다. 우리는 하나같이 공연히 서둘렀고, 하나같이 내일을 초조하게 기다렸다. 특히 퍼시벌 경은 어찌나 안절부절못하는지 한자리에 오 분도 머물지 못했다. 그 짧고 날카로운 기침은 어느 때보다 그를 괴롭혔다. 그는 온종일 들락날락했고, 갑자기 어찌나 캐묻기를 좋아하게 되었는지 사소한 심부름으로 집에 온 낯선 사람들에게까지 이것저것 물어 댔다. 여기에 더해, 로라와 내 마음속에 한시도 떠나지 않던 한 가지 생각—우리가 내일이면 헤어진다는 것—과, 둘 다 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 둘 다 늘 품고 있던 그 끈질긴 두려움, 곧 이 개탄스러운 결혼이 그 애 인생의 단 하나뿐인 치명적 과오이자 내 인생의 단 하나뿐인 가망 없는 슬픔이 되고 말리라는 두려움을 더해 보라. 우리가 가깝고 행복하게 지내 온 그 오랜 세월 동안 처음으로 우리는 서로의 얼굴을 마주 보기를 거의 피했고, 누가 먼저랄 것도 없이 저녁 내내 단둘이 이야기하는 일을 삼갔다. 더는 이 대목에 머물 수가 없다. 앞으로 어떤 슬픔이 나를 기다리고 있든, 나는 언제까지나 이 십이월 이십일일을 내 생애에서 가장 위안 없고 가장 비참한 날로 돌아보게 될 것이다.\n\n나는 자정이 한참 지난 시각, 내 방의 고요 속에서 이 글을 쓰고 있다. 로라가 소녀 시절부터 써 온 그 예쁘고 자그마한 흰 침대에 누운 모습을 몰래 들여다보고 막 돌아온 참이다.\n\n그 애는 거기 누워 있었다. 내가 바라보고 있는 줄도 모른 채—조용히, 내가 감히 바라던 것보다 더 조용히, 그러나 잠들지는 않은 채로. 야등의 희미한 빛에 그 애의 눈이 반쯤밖에 감기지 않은 것이 보였다—눈꺼풀 사이로 눈물 자국이 반짝였다. 내가 준 자그마한 기념품—그저 브로치 하나였지만—이 침대 곁 탁자 위에, 기도서와 그 애가 어디를 가든 지니고 다니는 아버지의 작은 초상화와 함께 놓여 있었다. 나는 잠시 베개 뒤에 서서 그 애를 내려다보았다. 그 애는 내 아래에 누워, 한쪽 팔과 손을 흰 이불 위에 얹은 채, 어찌나 미동도 없이 고요히 숨을 쉬는지 잠옷의 주름 장식조차 흔들리지 않았다—나는 서서 그 애를 바라보았다. 수천 번을 그렇게 보아 온 것처럼, 그리고 다시는 그렇게 볼 수 없을 것처럼—그러고는 살그머니 내 방으로 돌아왔다. 나의 사랑! 그 많은 재산과 그 많은 아름다움을 지니고도 너는 어쩌면 이토록 벗이 없느냐! 너를 위해서라면 제 심장의 목숨이라도 내놓을 단 한 사람은 멀리 떠나, 이 사나운 밤에 무서운 바다 위에서 흔들리고 있다. 네게 또 누가 남아 있느냐? 아버지도 없고, 오라비도 없고—이 서글픈 글을 쓰며 달랠 수 없는 슬픔과 이겨 낼 수 없는 의혹 속에서 네 곁을 지키며 아침을 기다리는, 무력하고 쓸모없는 여자 하나뿐이 아니냐. 아, 내일이면 그 사내의 손에 얼마나 큰 것이 맡겨지는가! 그가 언젠가 그것을 잊는다면—그가 언젠가 그 애의 머리카락 한 올이라도 상하게 한다면!——\n\n십이월 이십이일. 일곱 시. 사납고 어수선한 아침이다. 그 애는 방금 일어났다—막상 때가 닥치고 보니 어제보다 나아 보이고 차분하다.\n\n열 시. 그 애는 옷을 다 갖춰 입었다. 우리는 서로 입을 맞추었고—서로 용기를 잃지 말자고 약속했다. 나는 잠시 내 방에 물러나 있다. 소용돌이치며 뒤엉킨 생각들 가운데서, 무언가 방해가 생겨 이 결혼을 막아 주지 않을까 하는 그 이상한 공상이 아직도 내 머릿속을 맴돌고 있음을 알아차린다. 그것이 그의 머릿속에도 맴돌고 있을까? 창문으로 내다보니 그가 문 앞의 마차들 사이를 불안하게 이리저리 오가고 있다.—내가 어쩌자고 이런 어리석은 소리를 적고 있는가! 결혼은 기정사실이다. 삼십 분도 못 되어 우리는 교회로 출발한다.\n\n열한 시. 다 끝났다. 두 사람은 부부가 되었다.\n\n세 시. 그들이 떠났다! 나는 울다 못해 앞이 보이지 않는다—더는 쓸 수가 없다——\n\n[이야기의 제1기는 여기서 끝난다.]\n\n# BLACKWATER PARK, HAMPSHIRE.\n\n1850년 6월 11일.—돌이켜볼 여섯 달—로라와 내가 마지막으로 서로를 본 뒤로 길고 외로운 여섯 달이 흘렀다!\n\n앞으로 며칠을 더 기다려야 하나? 단 하루! 내일, 십이일이면 여행자들이 영국으로 돌아온다. 나는 내 행복을 좀처럼 실감하지 못하겠다—앞으로 스물네 시간이면 로라와 나를 갈라놓았던 이별의 마지막 날이 끝난다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없다.\n\n그녀와 그녀의 남편은 겨울 내내 이탈리아에 있었고, 그 뒤에는 티롤에 있었다. 두 사람은 포스코 백작 부부와 함께 돌아온다. 백작 부부는 런던 근교 어딘가에 자리를 잡을 생각이며, 거처를 정하기 전에 여름 몇 달 동안 블랙워터 파크에 머물기로 약속했다. 로라만 돌아온다면 누가 함께 오든 상관없다. 퍼시벌 경이 원한다면 이 집을 바닥부터 천장까지 사람으로 채워도 좋다—그의 아내와 내가 이 집에서 함께 살 수만 있다면.\n\n그러는 사이 나는 이렇게 블랙워터 파크에 자리를 잡았다. (주(州) 향토지가 친절하게 알려주는 바로는) “준남작 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 유서 깊고 흥미로운 저택”이자, (이제 내 몫으로 감히 덧붙이자면) 평범한 노처녀 매리언 홀콤의 앞날의 거처가 될 이곳에. 나는 지금 아늑한 작은 거실에 자리 잡아 곁에 찻잔을 놓아두었고, 이 세상에서 내가 가진 전부는 상자 세 개와 가방 하나에 담겨 내 주위에 늘어서 있다.\n\n나는 어제 리머리지를 떠났다. 그 전날 파리에서 온 로라의 반가운 편지를 받은 뒤였다. 그때까지 나는 두 사람을 런던에서 만나야 할지 햄프셔에서 만나야 할지 알 수 없었는데, 이 마지막 편지가 알려주기를 퍼시벌 경은 사우샘프턴에 상륙해 곧장 시골집으로 향할 작정이라고 했다. 그는 외국에서 돈을 너무 많이 써서 남은 사교철 동안 런던에서 지낼 비용을 댈 돈이 하나도 없다. 그래서 여름과 가을을 블랙워터에서 조용히 보내기로 알뜰하게 결심한 것이다. 로라는 흥분과 낯선 풍경이라면 이미 넘치도록 겪었으므로, 남편의 신중함이 마련해준 시골의 고요와 은거를 기쁘게 여긴다. 나로 말하자면, 그녀와 함께라면 어디서든 행복할 준비가 되어 있다. 그러니 우선은 우리 모두 각자의 방식대로 흡족한 셈이다.\n\n어젯밤에는 런던에서 잤고, 오늘은 이런저런 방문과 심부름으로 그곳에서 하도 오래 붙들려 있어서 오늘 저녁 땅거미가 진 뒤에야 블랙워터에 닿았다.\n\n여태까지 받은 이곳의 어렴풋한 인상으로 판단하건대, 이곳은 리머리지와 정반대다.\n\n집은 아주 평평한 땅 위에 서 있고, 북쪽 지방 사람인 내 감각으로는 나무에 둘러싸여—거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 갇혀 있는 듯하다. 문을 열어준 남자 하인과, 내 방까지 안내해주고 차를 내어준 아주 예의 바른 가정부 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다. 나는 이층 긴 복도 끝에 아담한 거실과 침실을 하나씩 얻었다. 하인들과 남는 방 몇 개는 삼층에 있고, 생활하는 방들은 모두 일층에 있다. 아직 그 방들은 하나도 보지 못했고, 이 집에 대해서는 한쪽 별채가 오백 년 되었다는 것, 한때는 둘레에 해자가 있었다는 것, 그리고 블랙워터라는 이름이 정원 안의 호수에서 왔다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모른다.\n\n방금 열한 시를 알리는 종이 집 한가운데 솟은 작은 탑에서 유령처럼 엄숙하게 울렸다. 들어올 때 본 그 탑이다. 큰 개 한 마리가 종소리에 잠이 깬 모양인지 어느 모퉁이 너머에서 구슬프게 짖어대며 하품을 하고 있다. 아래층 복도에서 울리는 발소리와 현관문의 빗장과 자물쇠를 채우는 쇳소리가 들린다. 하인들이 자러 가는 모양이다. 나도 그들을 따라야 할까?\n\n아니, 나는 조금도 졸리지 않다. 졸리다고? 내가 그런 말을 했던가? 다시는 눈을 감지 못할 것만 같다. 내일이면 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 보고 그 익숙한 목소리를 듣는다는 생각만으로도 나는 끊임없는 흥분의 열병에 들떠 있다. 내게 남자의 특권이 있다면 나는 당장 퍼시벌 경의 가장 좋은 말을 끌어내어 동쪽으로, 떠오르는 해를 맞으러 밤새 달렸을 것이다—몇 시간이고 쉼 없이 이어지는 길고 험하고 고된 질주를, 저 유명한 노상강도의 요크행 말달리기처럼. 그러나 나는 한낱 여자일 뿐이고 평생 인내와 예절과 치마에 매인 몸이니, 가정부의 의견을 존중하며 가냘프고 여자다운 방식으로 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓸 수밖에 없다.\n\n책 읽기는 엄두도 못 낸다—책에 도무지 정신을 붙들어 맬 수가 없다. 글이라도 써서 졸음과 피로를 불러올 수 있을지 해보자. 요즘 내 일기는 지독히 소홀했다. 지금처럼 새 삶의 문턱에 선 채로, 지난 여섯 달 동안의 사람들과 사건들, 우연과 변화 가운데 나는 무엇을 떠올릴 수 있을까—로라의 결혼식 날 이후로 이어진 그 길고 지치고 텅 빈 시간을?\n\n월터 하트라이트가 내 기억의 맨 앞에 있고, 그는 부재중인 내 벗들의 어슴푸레한 행렬에서 가장 먼저 지나간다. 탐험대가 온두라스에 상륙한 뒤 그에게서 몇 줄을 받았는데, 지금까지 그가 쓴 어떤 글보다도 밝고 희망에 찬 어조였다. 한 달인가 여섯 주쯤 뒤에 나는 어느 미국 신문의 발췌 기사를 보았다. 그 탐험가들이 내륙 여정에 올랐다는 소식이었다. 그들이 마지막으로 목격된 것은 저마다 어깨에 총을 메고 등에 짐을 진 채 야생의 태고림으로 들어가는 모습이었다. 그 뒤로 문명은 그들의 자취를 모두 잃었다. 월터에게서 더는 한 줄도 오지 않았고, 탐험대에 관한 소식 한 조각도 어느 신문에 실린 적이 없다.\n\n앤 캐서릭과 그 동행인 클레멘츠 부인의 운명과 행방 위에도 똑같이 짙고 낙담스러운 어둠이 드리워 있다. 두 사람 중 누구에 대해서도 아무 소식이 없다. 그들이 이 나라 안에 있는지 밖에 있는지, 살았는지 죽었는지 아무도 모른다. 퍼시벌 경의 변호사조차 모든 희망을 버리고, 달아난 이들을 찾는 헛된 수색을 마침내 그만두라고 지시했다.\n\n우리의 좋은 옛 벗 길모어 씨는 활발하던 직업 인생에 슬픈 제동이 걸렸다. 이른 봄에 우리는 그가 책상 앞에서 의식을 잃은 채 발견되었고 그 발작이 뇌졸중으로 진단되었다는 소식에 놀랐다. 그는 오래전부터 머리가 무겁고 짓눌리는 듯하다고 호소해왔고, 의사는 아직 젊은이인 양 새벽부터 밤까지 일을 계속하다가는 어떤 결과가 따를지 그에게 경고했었다. 그리하여 이제 그는 적어도 앞으로 일 년 동안은 사무실에 나가지 말고, 평소의 생활방식을 통째로 바꾸어 몸의 휴식과 마음의 안정을 구하라는 엄명을 받았다. 따라서 업무는 동업자가 맡아 이어가게 되었고, 그 자신은 지금 이 순간 독일에 가 있다. 그곳에 자리 잡고 상업에 종사하는 친척들을 찾아간 것이다. 이렇게 또 한 사람의 진실한 벗이자 믿음직한 조언자를 우리는 잃었다—부디 한동안만 잃은 것이기를 나는 간절히 바라고 믿는다.\n\n가엾은 베지 부인은 런던까지 나와 함께 여행했다. 로라와 내가 둘 다 집을 떠난 뒤 그녀를 리머리지에 홀로 버려둘 수는 없었기에, 우리는 클래펌에서 학교를 운영하는 그녀의 결혼하지 않은 여동생과 함께 살도록 주선했다. 그녀는 올가을에 이곳으로 자기 제자를—거의 자기가 기른 아이라 해도 좋을 이를—만나러 올 것이다. 나는 그 착한 노부인을 목적지까지 무사히 데려다주고 친척에게 맡기고 왔다. 그녀는 몇 달 뒤면 로라를 다시 볼 수 있다는 생각에 조용히 행복해했다.\n\n페얼리 씨로 말하자면, 우리 여자들이 집에서 사라진 것에 그가 이루 말할 수 없이 안도했다고 적어도 부당한 말은 아니리라. 그가 조카딸을 그리워한다는 생각은 그야말로 터무니없다—예전에도 그는 그녀를 보려 하지도 않은 채 몇 달씩 흘려보내곤 했다—그리고 나와 베지 부인의 경우, 우리 둘의 떠남에 가슴이 반쯤 무너진다고 그가 한 말은 우리를 치워버리게 되어 남몰래 기뻐한다는 고백과 다름없다고 나는 여기련다. 그의 최근 변덕은 사진사 둘을 쉴 새 없이 부려 자기가 가진 온갖 보물과 진기한 물건들을 태양 그림으로 찍어내게 하는 것이다. 그 사진 모음의 완전한 한 벌은 칼라일 기술학교에 기증될 예정인데, 최고급 판지에 붙이고 그 아래에는 요란한 붉은 글씨로 이렇게 새긴다. “라파엘로 작 성모자상. 프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.” “티글라트 필레세르 시대의 동전. 프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.” “유일무이한 렘브란트 에칭. 다른 어떤 인쇄본에도 없는 모퉁이의 인쇄 얼룩 때문에 유럽 전역에 ‘얼룩’이라 알려짐. 삼백 기니로 평가됨. 프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.” 이런 식의 사진 수십 장이, 모두 이런 식의 문구를 달고서, 내가 컴벌랜드를 떠나기 전에 완성되었고, 앞으로 수백 장이 더 남아 있다. 이 새로운 관심거리가 그를 붙들어둘 터이니 페얼리 씨는 앞으로 몇 달이고 행복한 사람일 것이며, 가엾은 두 사진사는 그가 지금까지 시종 한 사람에게만 안겨온 사회적 순교를 나누어 지게 될 것이다.\n\n내 기억에서 가장 앞자리를 차지하는 사람들과 사건들에 대해서는 이쯤 해두자. 그렇다면 내 마음에서 가장 앞자리를 차지하는 단 한 사람은 어떤가? 이 줄들을 쓰는 내내 로라는 내 생각 속에 있었다. 오늘 밤 일기를 덮기 전에, 지난 여섯 달 동안의 그녀에 대해 나는 무엇을 떠올릴 수 있을까?\n\n내게는 그녀의 편지들밖에 길잡이가 없는데, 우리 서신이 다룰 수 있는 모든 물음 가운데 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 그 편지들이 하나같이 나를 어둠 속에 남겨둔다.\n\n그는 그녀를 다정하게 대하는가? 결혼식 날 내가 그녀와 헤어졌을 때보다 지금 그녀는 더 행복한가? 내 편지에는 모두 이 두 가지 물음이, 어느 때는 이런 식으로 어느 때는 저런 식으로, 다소 직접적으로 담겨 있었다. 그런데 오직 그 대목만은 모두 답이 없거나, 마치 내가 그녀의 건강 상태만 물은 양 대답이 돌아왔다. 그녀는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 알려온다—자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고, 여행이 몸에 맞는다고, 태어나 처음으로 감기 한 번 걸리지 않고 겨울을 나고 있다고. 그러나 자기 결혼을 받아들이게 되었다고, 이제는 십이월 이십이일을 돌이켜보아도 쓰라린 후회와 아쉬움이 없다고 분명히 말해주는 구절은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없다. 편지에서 남편의 이름은 그저 함께 여행 중이며 여정의 모든 준비를 맡은 친구의 이름을 대듯 언급될 뿐이다. “퍼시벌 경”이 우리가 며칟날 떠나기로 정했다, “퍼시벌 경”이 어느 길로 가기로 결정했다. 이따금 “퍼시벌”이라고만 쓸 때도 있지만 아주 드물다—열에 아홉은 작위를 붙여 부른다.\n\n그의 습관과 견해가 그녀의 것을 조금이라도 바꾸고 물들였다는 흔적을 나는 찾을 수 없다. 젊고 싱그럽고 예민한 여인에게 결혼이 은연중에 일으키는 그 흔한 정신의 변화가 로라에게는 도무지 일어나지 않은 듯하다. 그녀는 눈앞의 온갖 경이 속에서도 자기 생각과 인상을 적어 보내는데, 그 문장은 남편이 아니라 내가 그녀와 함께 여행하고 있었다면 다른 누군가에게 썼을 법한 그대로다. 두 사람 사이에 어떤 종류의 교감이 존재한다는 기색은 어디에서도 보이지 않는다. 여행 이야기에서 벗어나 영국에서 자기를 기다리는 앞날을 이야기할 때조차, 그녀의 상상은 내 동생으로서의 미래에만 골몰하고 퍼시벌 경의 아내로서의 미래는 한사코 언급하지 않는다. 이 모든 것에도 그녀가 결혼 생활에서 정말로 불행하다고 내게 경고하는 불평의 저음은 없다. 우리 서신에서 내가 받은 인상은, 다행히도, 그런 괴로운 결론으로 나를 이끌지는 않는다. 다만 내가 그녀를 누이라는 옛 자리에서 떼어내어 편지라는 매개를 통해 아내라는 새로운 자리에서 바라볼 때, 나는 슬픈 무기력과 변치 않는 무관심만을 볼 뿐이다. 다시 말해, 지난 여섯 달 동안 내게 편지를 써온 사람은 언제나 로라 페얼리였지 결코 글라이드 부인이 아니었다.\n\n남편의 성품과 행실에 대해 그녀가 지키는 그 이상한 침묵을, 그녀는 남편의 절친한 벗 포스코 백작의 이름이 나오는 최근 편지의 몇 안 되는 대목에서도 거의 똑같은 결의로 지킨다.\n\n무슨 까닭인지 백작 부부는 지난가을 끝 무렵에 갑자기 계획을 바꾸어, 로마 대신 빈으로 간 모양이다. 퍼시벌 경은 영국을 떠날 때 로마에서 그들을 만나리라 예상했었다. 두 사람은 봄이 되어서야 빈을 떠나 티롤까지 와서, 귀국길에 오른 신혼부부를 맞았다. 로라는 포스코 백작부인과의 만남에 대해서는 곧잘 쓴다. 고모가 훨씬 좋은 쪽으로 변해서—처녀 시절보다 아내가 된 지금이 훨씬 차분하고 훨씬 분별 있어서—여기서 다시 만나면 내가 알아보지도 못할 거라고 장담한다. 그러나 포스코 백작에 대해서는 (그의 아내보다 그가 나는 한없이 더 궁금한데) 로라는 얄미울 만큼 조심스럽고 말이 없다. 그저 그가 알 수 없는 사람이라고만 하고, 내가 그를 직접 보고 내 나름의 판단을 먼저 세울 때까지는 자기 인상을 말해주지 않겠다고 한다.\n\n내가 보기에 이것은 백작에게 좋지 않은 징조다. 로라는 대개의 사람들이 나이 들며 잃어버리는, 벗을 본능으로 알아보는 아이의 섬세한 능력을 훨씬 온전하게 간직하고 있다. 그러니 포스코 백작에 대한 그녀의 첫인상이 좋지 않았다는 내 짐작이 옳다면, 나부터도 그 저명한 외국인을 눈으로 보기도 전에 의심하고 불신하게 될 위험이 적잖다. 그러나 인내, 인내다—이 불확실함도, 그 밖의 숱한 불확실함도 그리 오래가지는 못한다. 내일이면 내 모든 의혹이 머지않아 풀릴 길에 들어서는 것을 보게 되리라.\n\n열두 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 열린 창밖을 내다보다가 이제야 이 지면을 덮으러 돌아왔다.\n\n고요하고 후텁지근하고 달 없는 밤이다. 별은 흐릿하고 몇 개 되지 않는다. 사방의 시야를 가로막은 나무들은 멀리서 검고 단단한 덩어리로 어렴풋이 보여, 커다란 바위벽 같다. 개구리 우는 소리가 희미하게 멀리서 들리고, 큰 시계의 여운은 종소리가 그친 뒤에도 바람 한 점 없는 고요 속에 오래도록 웅웅거린다. 낮의 블랙워터 파크는 어떤 모습일까? 밤의 이곳은 그다지 마음에 들지 않는다.\n\n12일.—조사와 발견의 하루—여러 이유로, 감히 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 흥미로운 하루였다.\n\n나는 물론 집부터 구경하기 시작했다.\n\n건물의 본채는 그 지나치게 과대평가된 여인, 엘리자베스 여왕 시대의 것이다. 일층에는 낮은 천장의 어마어마하게 긴 회랑 두 개가 나란히 뻗어 있는데, 흉측한 가족 초상화들 때문에 한층 더 어둡고 음침하다—그 그림들은 하나같이 태워버리고 싶다. 두 회랑 위층의 방들은 그런대로 손질되어 있으나 좀처럼 쓰이지 않는다. 안내를 맡은 예의 바른 가정부가 그 방들을 보여주겠다고 했지만, 사려 깊게도 방들이 꽤 어질러져 있을 것 같다고 덧붙였다. 내 치마와 양말의 온전함에 대한 존경심은 이 왕국의 모든 엘리자베스 시대 침실에 대한 존경심을 한없이 웃돌므로, 나는 깨끗한 새 옷을 더럽힐 위험을 무릅쓰고 먼지와 때의 상층부를 탐사하기를 딱 잘라 거절했다. 가정부는 “저도 아가씨와 똑같은 생각입니다” 하고 말했고, 나를 오랜만에 만난 가장 분별 있는 여자로 여기는 눈치였다.\n\n본채 이야기는 이쯤 해두자. 그 양쪽 끝에는 별채가 하나씩 덧붙어 있다. (집으로 다가갈 때) 왼편의 반쯤 무너진 별채는 한때 홀로 서 있던 주거였고 십사세기에 지어졌다. 퍼시벌 경의 외가 조상 가운데 누군가가—누구인지 나는 기억나지도 않고 알 바도 아니다—앞서 말한 엘리자베스 여왕 시대에 본채를 거기에 직각으로 붙여 지었다. 가정부는 “옛 별채”의 건축이 안팎으로 안목 있는 이들에게 대단히 훌륭하다는 평을 듣는다고 일러주었다. 좀 더 살펴본 끝에 나는 안목 있는 이들이 퍼시벌 경의 그 고색창연한 물건 앞에서 자기 안목을 발휘하려면 먼저 습기와 어둠과 쥐에 대한 두려움을 죄다 마음에서 몰아내야 한다는 것을 알아냈다. 사정이 그러하니 나는 주저 없이 내가 안목이라고는 전혀 없는 사람임을 인정하고, “옛 별채”도 앞서 엘리자베스 시대 침실들에게 한 것과 똑같이 대하자고 제안했다. 가정부는 다시 한 번 “저도 아가씨와 똑같은 생각입니다” 하고 말했고, 다시 한 번 내 비범한 상식에 대한 감탄을 숨기지 않으며 나를 바라보았다.\n\n다음으로 우리는 오른편 별채로 갔다. 블랙워터 파크의 그 놀라운 건축적 뒤죽박죽을 완성하기라도 하듯, 이 별채는 조지 이세 시대에 지어졌다.\n\n이곳이 사람이 살 만한 부분으로, 로라를 위해 안쪽을 수리하고 새로 꾸민 곳이다. 내 두 방과 그 밖의 좋은 침실들은 모두 이층에 있고, 아래층에는 응접실과 식당과 아침 거실과 서재, 그리고 로라를 위한 예쁘고 작은 규방이 있다. 모두 밝고 현대적인 방식으로 아주 곱게 꾸며졌고, 즐거운 현대의 사치품들로 아주 우아하게 갖추어져 있다. 어느 방도 리머리지의 우리 방들만큼 넓고 시원하지는 않지만 하나같이 살기에 기분 좋아 보인다. 나는 블랙워터 파크에 대해 들은 이야기 때문에, 앉기 괴로운 골동 의자들과 침침한 색유리와 곰팡내 나는 퀴퀴한 휘장들, 그리고 안락함이라는 감각 없이 태어난 사람들이 벗들의 편의는 아랑곳하지 않고 주위에 쌓아두는 온갖 야만스러운 잡동사니를 몹시 두려워하고 있었다. 십구세기가 이 낯선 나의 새 보금자리에도 밀고 들어와 우리 일상에서 그 지저분한 “좋았던 옛 시절”을 쓸어내버렸음을 알게 되니 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓인다.\n\n나는 오전을 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다—얼마간은 아래층 방들에서, 얼마간은 집의 세 면과 앞쪽을 지키는 높은 쇠울타리와 대문이 이루는 커다란 사각 마당에서. 마당 한가운데에는 돌로 두른 커다란 원형 연못이 있고 그 복판에는 우의적인 납 괴물상이 서 있다. 연못에는 금빛 은빛 물고기가 가득하고, 내가 밟아본 중 가장 부드러운 잔디의 널따란 띠가 그것을 둘러싸고 있다. 나는 점심때까지 그늘진 쪽에서 제법 기분 좋게 서성이다가, 그 뒤 챙 넓은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 따뜻하고 아름다운 햇빛 속으로 혼자 나가 정원을 둘러보았다.\n\n낮의 빛은 블랙워터에 나무가 너무 많다는 어젯밤의 인상을 확인해주었다. 집은 나무에 숨이 막힌다. 나무는 대개 어리고 지나치게 빽빽하게 심겨 있다. 짐작건대 퍼시벌 경 이전에 영지 전체에서 재목을 결딴내다시피 베어낸 일이 있었고, 그다음 소유자가 화가 난 조바심으로 그 빈자리를 되도록 빽빽하고 빠르게 메우려 한 것이리라. 집 앞을 둘러본 뒤 나는 왼편에 화단이 있는 것을 보고, 그쪽에 무엇이 있는지 알아보려 그리로 걸어갔다.\n\n가까이서 보니 정원은 작고 볼품없고 손질이 되어 있지 않았다. 나는 그곳을 뒤로하고 둘레 울타리의 작은 문을 열었다가 전나무 숲에 들어서 있었다.\n\n사람이 손을 대어 만든 예쁘장한 굽잇길이 나무들 사이로 나를 이끌었고, 북쪽 지방에서 얻은 경험은 곧 내가 모래땅과 히스 벌판에 가까워지고 있음을 일러주었다. 전나무들 사이로 반 마일 남짓 걸었을까, 길이 급하게 꺾이더니 나무들이 양옆에서 홀연히 사라졌고, 나는 문득 드넓게 트인 공간의 가장자리에 서서 이 집에 이름을 준 블랙워터 호수를 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n발아래로 비스듬히 흘러내린 땅은 온통 모래였고, 군데군데 그 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 히스 덤불의 작은 둔덕이 몇 개 있었다. 호수는 분명 한때 내가 선 자리까지 차올랐다가 차츰 마르고 줄어 예전 크기의 삼분의 일에도 못 미치게 되었다. 나는 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 우묵한 곳에서, 뒤엉킨 갈대와 골풀과 자잘한 흙무더기에 여러 웅덩이와 못으로 갈린 그 고요하고 썩은 물을 보았다. 건너편 기슭에는 나무들이 다시 빽빽이 솟아 시야를 가로막고 느리고 얕은 물 위에 검은 그림자를 드리웠다. 호수로 내려가면서 보니 건너편 땅은 축축한 늪지로, 억센 풀과 음침한 버드나무가 무성했다. 해가 비치는 트인 모래 쪽에서는 제법 맑던 물이, 내 맞은편 물컹한 둑과 무성하게 드리운 덤불과 뒤엉킨 나무의 그늘 아래 더 깊이 고인 곳에서는 검고 독기 어려 보였다. 늪 쪽으로 다가갈수록 개구리는 울어대고, 쥐들은 저희가 살아 있는 그림자라도 되는 양 그늘진 물속을 들락날락하며 미끄러졌다. 나는 그곳에서 물에 반쯤 잠긴 채 뒤집힌 낡은 배의 썩은 잔해를 보았다. 나무 사이 틈으로 스민 병약한 햇빛 한 조각이 그 마른 표면에 어른거렸고, 그 빛 한복판에 뱀 한 마리가 기괴하게 몸을 사린 채 음험하리만치 미동도 없이 볕을 쬐고 있었다. 멀리서도 가까이서도 그 풍경은 한결같이 적막과 쇠락의 을씨년스러운 인상을 자아냈고, 머리 위 여름 하늘의 눈부신 밝음은 그 빛이 내리쬐는 황무지의 음울과 메마름을 더 깊고 모질게 만들 뿐인 듯했다. 나는 몸을 돌려 히스가 깔린 높은 땅으로 되짚어 올라가면서, 아까의 길에서 조금 벗어나 전나무 숲 바깥자락에 서 있는 허름한 낡은 나무 헛간 쪽으로 발길을 잡았다. 그 헛간은 그때까지 호수의 넓고 거친 경치에 견주면 눈길을 나눌 만큼 대단한 것이 못 되었던 것이다.\n\n헛간에 다가가서 보니 그것은 한때 보트 창고였고, 그 뒤에 안으로 전나무 의자 하나와 걸상 몇 개와 탁자를 들여 일종의 투박한 정자로 바꾸려 한 흔적이 있었다. 나는 안으로 들어가 잠시 앉아 쉬며 숨을 골랐다.\n\n보트 창고에 들어간 지 일 분도 되지 않았을 때, 내 가쁜 숨소리가 발밑의 무언가에 아주 이상하게 되울린다는 생각이 들었다. 나는 한동안 귀를 곤두세워 들었고, 내가 앉은 의자 아래 땅바닥에서 나오는 듯한 낮고 탁하게 흐느끼는 숨소리를 들었다. 내 신경은 사소한 일에 쉽게 흔들리지 않지만 이때만은 놀라서 벌떡 일어섰다—소리쳐 불렀고—아무 대답도 없자—달아난 용기를 다시 불러모아 의자 밑을 들여다보았다.\n\n그곳 가장 안쪽 구석에 웅크린 채, 내 공포의 가련한 원인이 누워 있었다—작고 불쌍한 개, 검고 흰 스패니얼이었다. 내가 들여다보며 부르자 그 짐승은 힘없이 신음했으나 몸은 조금도 움직이지 않았다. 나는 의자를 치우고 더 가까이 들여다보았다. 가엾은 강아지의 눈은 빠르게 흐려지고 있었고, 윤기 나는 흰 옆구리에는 핏방울이 맺혀 있었다. 약하고 무력하고 말 못 하는 짐승의 고통이야말로 이 세상이 보여줄 수 있는 온갖 슬픈 광경 가운데 가장 슬픈 것 중 하나이리라. 나는 그 가엾은 개를 할 수 있는 한 부드럽게 안아 올리고, 옷자락 앞섶을 그 둘레로 그러모아 임시 그물침대 같은 것을 만들어 눕혔다. 그렇게 나는 그 짐승을 되도록 아프지 않게, 되도록 빠르게 집으로 데려왔다.\n\n현관에 아무도 없기에 나는 곧장 내 거실로 올라가 낡은 숄 하나로 개의 잠자리를 만들어주고 종을 울렸다. 세상에 있을 법한 하녀 중 가장 크고 가장 뚱뚱한 하녀가 대답하러 왔는데, 성인의 인내심마저 뒤흔들었을 법한 명랑한 아둔함에 젖어 있었다. 그 아이의 살진 흐물흐물한 얼굴은 바닥에 놓인 다친 짐승을 보자 정말로 활짝 웃음으로 벌어졌다.\n\n“거기 뭐가 그렇게 우습지?” 나는 그 애가 내 하인이라도 되는 양 화를 내며 물었다. “이게 누구 개인지 아니?”\n\n“아뇨, 아가씨, 그건 정말 모르겠는데요.” 그 애는 몸을 숙여 스패니얼의 다친 옆구리를 내려다보더니—새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯 갑자기 얼굴이 환해져서는—만족스럽게 킥킥거리며 상처를 가리키고 말했다. “저건 백스터가 한 짓이구먼요, 그렇고말고요.”\n\n나는 어찌나 부아가 치미는지 그 애의 뺨이라도 갈길 뻔했다. “백스터?” 내가 말했다. “네가 백스터라고 부르는 그 짐승 같은 자는 누구지?”\n\n그 애는 어느 때보다 더 신나게 다시 씩 웃었다. “아이고, 아가씨! 백스터는 사냥터지기예요. 낯선 개가 돌아다니면서 사냥질하는 걸 보면 붙잡아다 쏴버리지요. 그게 사냥터지기 직분이구먼요, 아가씨. 저 개는 죽을 것 같은데요. 여기가 총 맞은 자리 아녜요? 저건 백스터가 한 짓이라니까요. 백스터가 한 짓이고 백스터의 직분이구먼요, 아가씨.”\n\n나는 백스터가 개 대신 저 하녀를 쏘았더라면 좋았을 걸 하고 바랄 만큼 마음이 모질어질 뻔했다. 이 지독히도 말이 통하지 않는 인물에게 우리 발치의 고통받는 짐승을 돌보는 데 무슨 도움을 기대하기란 전혀 소용없는 일임을 알고, 나는 그 애에게 내 인사와 함께 가정부를 좀 와달라고 청하라고 일렀다. 그 애는 들어올 때와 똑같이 입이 귀에 걸리도록 웃으며 나갔다. 문이 등 뒤로 닫힐 때 그 애가 혼자 나직이 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸다. “백스터가 한 짓이고 백스터의 직분이지, 바로 그런 거라고.”\n\n가정부는 제법 교양과 지각이 있는 사람이라, 생각이 깊게도 우유와 따뜻한 물을 가지고 위층으로 올라왔다. 바닥의 개를 보는 순간 그녀는 흠칫 놀라며 낯빛이 변했다.\n\n“어머, 세상에,” 가정부가 소리쳤다. “저건 틀림없이 캐서릭 부인의 개예요!”\n\n“누구의 개라고요?” 나는 몹시 놀라 물었다.\n\n“캐서릭 부인이요. 홀콤 양께서는 캐서릭 부인을 아시는 모양이군요?”\n\n“직접 아는 사이는 아니지만 이야기는 들었어요. 그분이 여기 사나요? 딸에 대한 소식이 있었나요?”\n\n“아니요, 홀콤 양. 그분은 소식을 물으러 여기 오셨었지요.”\n\n“언제요?”\n\n“바로 어제요. 그분 말씀이, 따님의 인상착의와 맞는 낯선 여자가 이 근처에서 목격되었다고 누가 알려주었다더군요. 여기서는 그런 소문이 들려온 적이 없고, 캐서릭 부인을 위해 마을에 사람을 보내 알아보게 했을 때도 마을에는 그런 소문이 알려져 있지 않았습니다. 그분이 오실 때 이 가엾은 작은 개를 데리고 오신 건 분명하고요, 돌아가실 때 개가 종종걸음으로 뒤따라 나가는 것도 제가 봤습니다. 아마 그 짐승이 숲으로 헤매 들어갔다가 총을 맞은 게지요. 어디서 찾으셨어요, 홀콤 양?”\n\n“호수가 내다보이는 낡은 헛간에서요.”\n\n“아, 그렇군요, 그쪽이 숲 쪽이지요. 가엾은 것이 개들이 흔히 그러듯 죽으려고 가장 가까운 은신처까지 제 몸을 끌고 갔나 봅니다. 홀콤 양께서 우유로 입술을 축여주시면 저는 상처에 엉긴 털을 씻어내겠습니다. 이제 와서 손을 써봐야 너무 늦은 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽습니다만, 그래도 해보는 수밖에요.”\n\n캐서릭 부인! 그 이름은 마치 가정부가 방금 그것을 입에 올려 나를 놀라게 한 것처럼 여전히 내 귓가에 울렸다. 우리가 개를 돌보는 동안 월터 하트라이트가 내게 남긴 당부의 말이 기억에 되살아났다. “언젠가 앤 캐서릭이 당신 앞을 지나가거든, 홀콤 양, 내가 놓친 그 기회를 나보다 더 잘 쓰십시오.” 다친 스패니얼을 찾아낸 일이 벌써 캐서릭 부인이 블랙워터 파크에 다녀갔다는 발견으로 나를 이끌었으니, 그 일이 다시 무언가 더 큰 것으로 이어질지도 몰랐다. 나는 지금 내게 주어진 이 기회를 최대한 활용해 되도록 많은 정보를 얻어내기로 마음먹었다.\n\n“캐서릭 부인이 이 근처 어딘가에 산다고 하셨던가요?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아이고, 아니에요,” 가정부가 말했다. “그분은 웰밍엄에 사십니다. 이 주의 정반대 끝이지요—적어도 이십오 마일은 떨어져 있습니다.”\n\n“캐서릭 부인을 몇 해 전부터 알고 지내셨겠군요?”\n\n“천만에요, 홀콤 양. 저는 어제 그분이 여기 오시기 전까지는 한 번도 뵌 적이 없습니다. 물론 이야기는 들었지요. 퍼시벌 경께서 친절을 베푸셔서 그분 따님을 의사에게 맡겨 돌보게 하셨다는 이야기를 들었으니까요. 캐서릭 부인은 태도가 좀 이상한 분이지만 겉보기에는 대단히 점잖으십니다. 따님이 이 근처에서 목격되었다는 소문에 아무 근거가 없다는 것을—적어도 저희 중 누구도 근거를 찾아낼 수 없다는 것을—아시고는 몹시 낙심하신 듯했습니다.”\n\n“저는 캐서릭 부인에게 제법 관심이 있어요.” 나는 대화를 되도록 오래 끌며 말을 이었다. “어제 그분을 뵐 수 있도록 조금만 일찍 도착했더라면 좋았을걸요. 오래 계셨나요?”\n\n“네,” 가정부가 말했다. “한참 계셨습니다. 제가 낯선 신사분과 이야기하러 불려가지만 않았더라면 더 오래 계셨을 겁니다—퍼시벌 경께서 언제 돌아오시는지 물으러 온 신사분이었지요. 캐서릭 부인은 하녀가 그 손님의 용건을 제게 전하는 것을 듣자마자 자리에서 일어나 곧바로 떠나셨습니다. 헤어지면서 저에게, 여기 다녀간 일을 퍼시벌 경께 알릴 필요는 없다고 하시더군요. 저는 그게 꽤 이상한 말이라고 생각했습니다. 더구나 저처럼 책임 있는 자리에 있는 사람한테 하시는 말씀으로는요.”\n\n나도 그것이 이상한 말이라고 생각했다. 퍼시벌 경은 리머리지에서 분명 자기와 캐서릭 부인 사이에 더없이 완전한 신뢰가 존재한다고 내가 믿게끔 말했었다. 정말 그렇다면 그녀가 무엇 때문에 블랙워터 파크에 다녀간 일을 그에게 비밀로 하려고 애쓰겠는가?\n\n“아마도,” 나는 가정부가 캐서릭 부인의 작별 인사에 대해 내 의견을 듣고 싶어 하는 것을 알아채고 말했다. “아마도 그분은 자기가 다녀갔다는 말이 잃어버린 딸을 아직 찾지 못했다는 사실을 상기시켜 퍼시벌 경을 공연히 언짢게 할까 봐 그러셨겠지요. 그 이야기를 많이 하시던가요?”\n\n“거의 안 하셨습니다.” 가정부가 대답했다. “주로 퍼시벌 경 이야기를 하셨고, 그분이 어디를 여행하고 계셨는지, 새 부인은 어떤 분인지 아주 많이 물으셨어요. 이 근방에서 따님의 자취를 찾지 못한 것에 대해서도 슬퍼한다기보다 마음이 상하고 언짢으신 듯했습니다. ‘그 애는 포기하겠어요.’ 그게 제가 기억하는 그분의 마지막 말씀이었습니다. ‘그 애는 잃은 셈치고 포기하겠어요, 부인.’ 그러고는 곧장 글라이드 부인에 대한 질문으로 넘어가셔서, 아름답고 상냥한 분인지, 어여쁘고 건강하고 젊은 분인지 알고 싶어 하시고——아이고, 저런! 이렇게 될 줄 알았습니다. 보세요, 홀콤 양, 가엾은 것이 드디어 고통에서 벗어났네요!”\n\n개는 죽어 있었다. 희미하게 흐느끼는 듯한 소리를 한 번 내더니 한순간 사지에 경련이 일었다. 바로 “곱고 건강하고 젊은”이라는 마지막 말이 가정부의 입에서 떨어지던 그 순간이었다. 변화는 놀랄 만큼 갑작스럽게 찾아왔다. 눈 깜짝할 사이에 그 가엾은 짐승은 우리 손 밑에서 숨이 끊어져 있었다.\n\n여덟 시. 나는 방금 아래층에서 혼자 쓸쓸히 저녁을 먹고 돌아왔다. 창문 너머로 보이는 황량한 나무숲 위로 저녁놀이 붉게 타오르고, 나는 여행자들이 돌아오기를 기다리는 초조함을 달래려고 다시 일기장을 들여다보고 있다. 내 계산대로라면 벌써 도착했어야 하는데. 졸음이 밀려오는 저녁의 고요 속에서 이 집은 어찌 이리도 적막하고 외로운지! 아아! 마차 바퀴 소리를 듣고 아래층으로 달려 내려가 로라의 품에 안기기까지 앞으로 몇 분이나 더 기다려야 하는 걸까?\n\n가엾은 작은 개! 블랙워터 파크에서 보낸 나의 첫날이 죽음과 얽히지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을. 비록 떠돌이 짐승 한 마리의 죽음일 뿐이라 해도.\n\n웰밍엄. 나만의 이 사사로운 기록들을 되짚어보다가 알았는데, 웰밍엄이란 캐서릭 부인이 사는 고장의 이름이다. 그 여자의 짤막한 답장은 아직 내가 간직하고 있다. 퍼시벌 경이 억지로 쓰게 한, 그 불행한 딸에 관한 편지에 대한 답장 말이다. 언젠가 안전한 기회가 생기면 그 쪽지를 소개장 삼아 들고 가서, 캐서릭 부인을 직접 만나 무엇을 알아낼 수 있을지 시험해볼 작정이다. 그 여자가 이곳에 다녀간 일을 퍼시벌 경이 알지 못하도록 감추고 싶어 하는 까닭을 나는 이해할 수 없다. 그리고 딸 앤이 결국 이 근방에 있지 않다는 것도, 가정부가 확신하는 것의 절반만큼도 나는 확신할 수 없다. 이런 다급한 처지에서라면 월터 하트라이트는 뭐라고 말했을까? 가엾고 다정한 하트라이트! 나는 벌써부터 그의 정직한 조언과 기꺼운 도움이 아쉬워지기 시작한다.\n\n분명 무슨 소리가 들렸다. 아래층에서 부산하게 오가는 발소리였을까? 그래! 말발굽 소리가 들린다. 굴러오는 바퀴 소리가 들린다——\n\n# II\n\n6월 15일.—그들이 도착하며 일었던 소동도 이제 가라앉을 만큼 시간이 흘렀다. 여행자들이 돌아온 지 이틀이 지났고, 그 사이에 블랙워터 파크에서 우리 생활을 움직이는 새로운 기계 장치도 그런대로 잘 굴러가게 되었다. 이제 나는 평소만큼 차분하게 기록을 이어 갈 수 있으리라는 작은 희망을 품고 일기로 돌아온다.\n\n로라가 돌아온 뒤로 문득 떠오른 묘한 생각을 하나 적어 두는 것으로 시작해야겠다.\n\n한 가족의 두 사람, 혹은 절친한 두 벗이 헤어져 하나는 외국으로 가고 하나는 집에 남아 있을 때, 여행하고 돌아온 쪽과 집을 지킨 쪽이 처음 다시 마주하는 순간이면 집에 남아 있던 쪽은 언제나 괴로운 열세에 놓이는 듯하다. 한쪽이 열심히 얻어 온 새로운 생각과 새로운 습관이, 다른 쪽이 가만히 지켜 온 옛 생각과 옛 습관과 느닷없이 부딪치면, 처음에는 세상에서 가장 다정한 혈육 사이도, 가장 애틋한 벗 사이도 마음이 갈라지고, 양쪽 다 예상하지 못했고 양쪽 다 어쩌지 못하는 어색함이 둘 사이에 불쑥 들어서는 것 같다. 로라와 다시 만난 첫 기쁨이 지나가고, 이야기할 만큼 숨과 침착을 되찾으려 손을 맞잡고 나란히 앉았을 때, 나는 곧바로 그 낯섦을 느꼈고 로라도 그것을 느끼고 있음을 알 수 있었다. 이제 우리가 옛 습관을 대부분 되찾은 터라 그 느낌은 얼마간 옅어졌고, 머지않아 아주 사라질 것이다. 그러나 다시 함께 살게 된 지금 내가 로라에 대해 품게 된 첫인상에 그것이 영향을 끼친 것만은 분명하다—오직 그 때문에 여기 적어 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했다.\n\n로라는 내가 조금도 변하지 않았다고 했지만, 나는 로라가 변했다고 느꼈다.\n\n모습이 변했고, 한 가지 점에서는 성격도 변했다. 예전보다 덜 아름다워졌다고 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없다—다만 내게는 덜 아름다워졌다고 말할 수 있을 뿐이다.\n\n나의 눈과 나의 기억으로 로라를 보지 않는 사람이라면 아마 더 나아졌다고 여길 것이다. 얼굴에는 예전보다 혈색이 돌고 윤곽이 또렷하고 둥글어졌으며, 몸매도 처녀 시절보다 한결 자리가 잡히고 모든 동작이 안정되고 편안해 보인다. 그러나 로라를 바라보노라면 무언가가 빠져 있다—한때 로라 페얼리의 행복하고 순결한 삶에 속해 있었으나 이제 글라이드 부인에게서는 찾을 수 없는 무언가가. 옛날 그 얼굴에는 싱그러움과 부드러움이, 끊임없이 변하면서도 언제나 남아 있던 아름다움의 애틋함이 있었고, 그 매력은 말로도, 가엾은 하트라이트가 늘 하던 말대로 그림으로도 옮길 수 없는 것이었다. 그것이 사라졌다. 로라가 돌아온 날 저녁, 갑작스러운 만남에 격해져 얼굴이 창백해졌을 때 나는 그 희미한 잔영을 한순간 본 듯했으나, 그 뒤로 다시는 나타나지 않았다. 로라의 편지 어디에도 그런 외모의 변화를 짐작하게 하는 대목은 없었다. 오히려 편지들은 결혼이 적어도 겉모습만은 로라를 조금도 바꾸어 놓지 않았으리라 기대하게 만들었다. 어쩌면 나는 지난날 로라의 편지를 잘못 읽었고, 지금은 로라의 얼굴을 잘못 읽고 있는 것일까? 아무려면 어떠랴! 지난 여섯 달 사이에 로라의 아름다움이 늘었든 줄었든, 그 이별은 어느 쪽으로든 그 사랑스러운 사람 자체를 내게 그 어느 때보다 소중하게 만들어 주었으니, 어쨌든 그것만은 로라의 결혼이 낳은 하나의 좋은 결과다!\n\n두 번째 변화, 곧 로라의 성격에서 내가 알아차린 변화는 놀랍지 않았다. 이 경우에는 편지의 어조로 이미 마음의 준비가 되어 있었기 때문이다. 다시 집에 돌아온 지금도 로라는 결혼 생활에 관해서라면 어떤 자세한 이야기도 꺼리는데, 이는 편지로만 소식을 주고받던 헤어져 있던 내내 내가 보아 온 그대로다. 내가 그 금지된 화제에 처음 다가서려 하자 로라는 내 입술에 손을 얹었는데, 그 눈빛과 몸짓은 소녀 시절, 우리 사이에 아무 비밀도 없던 행복한 옛 시절을 뭉클하게, 거의 아프도록 떠올리게 했다.\n\n“매리언, 우리 둘이 함께 있을 때는 말이야,” 로라가 말했다. “내 결혼 생활을 있는 그대로 받아들이고, 그 이야기는 되도록 적게 말하고 적게 생각하는 편이 서로에게 더 행복하고 편할 거야. 나에 관한 거라면 무엇이든 다 이야기해 주고 싶어, 언니,” 로라는 내 허리에 두른 리본을 초조하게 풀었다 조였다 하며 말을 이었다. “털어놓는 게 거기서 그칠 수만 있다면 말이야. 하지만 그럴 수가 없어—그이 이야기까지 하게 될 테니까. 이제 나는 결혼한 몸이니, 그이를 위해서도, 언니를 위해서도, 나를 위해서도 그런 이야기는 피하는 게 낫다고 생각해. 그런 이야기가 언니를 괴롭히거나 나를 괴롭힐 거라는 말은 아니야—절대로 그렇게는 생각하지 말아 줘. 다만—나는 이렇게 행복하고 싶어, 언니를 되찾았으니까. 그리고 언니도 그렇게 행복했으면 좋겠어——” 로라는 문득 말을 끊더니 우리가 이야기하고 있던 방, 곧 나의 거실을 둘러보았다. “아!” 로라는 알아본 기쁨에 환하게 웃으며 손뼉을 쳤다. “벌써 옛 친구를 또 하나 찾았네! 언니 책장 말이야, 매리언—그 정답고 자그마하고 낡은 새틴우드 책장—리머리지에서 가져왔다니 얼마나 반가운지! 그리고 비만 오면 언니가 꼭 들고 나가던 그 흉하고 묵직한 남자용 우산도! 그리고 무엇보다도 먼저, 언제나처럼 나를 바라보고 있는 언니의 그 사랑스럽고 까무잡잡하고 총명한 집시 같은 얼굴! 여기 있으니 다시 집에 돌아온 것만 같아. 어떻게 하면 더 집처럼 만들 수 있을까? 아버지 초상화를 내 방 말고 언니 방에 걸어야지—그리고 리머리지에서 가져온 내 자잘한 보물들도 다 여기 둘 거야—그리고 우리는 이 정다운 네 벽에 둘러싸여 날마다 몇 시간이고 함께 보내는 거야. 오, 매리언!” 로라는 갑자기 내 무릎 앞 발받침에 앉아 내 얼굴을 간절히 올려다보며 말했다. “약속해 줘, 절대로 결혼해서 나를 떠나지 않겠다고. 이런 말은 이기적이지만, 언니는 혼자인 편이 훨씬 나아—남편을—남편을 아주 사랑한다면 모를까—하지만 언니는 나 말고는 누구도 그렇게 사랑하지 않을 거지, 그렇지?” 로라는 다시 말을 멈추고, 내 두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고는 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다. “요즘 편지 많이 썼어? 편지 많이 받았어?” 로라가 낮게, 갑자기 달라진 목소리로 물었다. 나는 그 물음이 무슨 뜻인지 알았으나, 먼저 나서서 부추기지 않는 것이 도리라고 생각했다. “그이한테서 소식 들었어?” 로라는 얼굴을 여전히 내 손에 묻은 채 그 손에 입 맞추며, 이제야 감히 꺼낸 더 직접적인 물음을 용서해 달라는 듯 나를 어르며 말을 이었다. “잘 지내고 행복하대? 자기 일도 잘되어 간대? 마음을 추스르고—나를 잊었대?”\n\n로라는 그런 질문을 하지 말았어야 했다. 퍼시벌 경이 약혼의 이행을 요구하던 그 아침에, 하트라이트의 그림첩을 영영 내 손에 넘겨주며 스스로 다졌던 결심을 기억했어야 했다. 그러나, 아아! 좋은 결심을 한 번도 무너뜨리거나 뒷걸음질하는 일 없이 끝까지 밀고 나가는 흠 없는 사람이 어디 있단 말인가? 참된 사랑으로 한번 마음에 박힌 모습을 정말로 가슴에서 뜯어낸 여자가 어디 있단 말인가? 책은 그런 이 세상 사람 같지 않은 존재가 있었다고 말한다—그러나 우리 자신의 경험은 그 책에 무어라 답하는가?\n\n나는 로라를 나무라려 하지 않았다. 어쩌면 다른 여자라면 같은 처지에서 가장 가까운 벗에게조차 숨길 이유가 있었을 것을 내게 그대로 내보이는 그 두려움 없는 솔직함이 진심으로 고마웠기 때문일 테고, 어쩌면 내가 그 자리에 있었더라도 같은 질문을 하고 같은 생각을 했으리라는 것을 내 마음과 양심으로 느꼈기 때문일 것이다. 내가 정직하게 할 수 있는 일이라고는, 요즘 그에게 편지를 쓴 적도 그에게서 소식을 받은 적도 없다고 대답하고, 이야기를 덜 위험한 화제로 돌리는 것뿐이었다.\n\n로라가 돌아온 뒤 처음으로 나눈 그 은밀한 이야기에는 나를 슬프게 하는 것이 많았다. 우리 생애 처음으로 금지된 화제가 둘 사이에 놓임으로써 로라의 결혼이 우리 사이에 빚어낸 변화, 로라 자신의 마지못한 말이 이제 내 마음에 들이미는 것—남편과 로라 사이에 따뜻한 정도, 깊은 공감도 없다는 우울한 확신, 그리고 그 불행한 애정의 힘이 아무리 순결하게, 아무리 해롭지 않게라 해도 여전히 로라의 가슴 깊이 예전 그대로 뿌리내려 있다는 괴로운 발견—이 모든 것은 나만큼 로라를 아끼고 나만큼 로라를 절실히 헤아리는 여자라면 누구든 슬프게 만들 사실들이다.\n\n그에 맞설 위안은 하나뿐이다—나를 위로해야 마땅하고, 실제로 나를 위로해 주는 위안 하나. 로라의 성품에 깃든 모든 우아함과 온화함, 그 천성의 꾸밈없는 다정함, 다가서는 이마다 로라를 사랑하고 기뻐하게 만들던 그 달콤하고 소박하고 여성스러운 매력이 로라와 함께 내게 돌아왔다는 것. 다른 인상들은 나도 이따금 조금 의심스러워진다. 그러나 이 마지막, 가장 좋고 가장 행복한 인상만은 하루하루 시간이 갈수록 더욱더 확신하게 된다.\n\n이제 로라에게서 그 동행들에게로 눈을 돌려 보자. 먼저 그 남편이 내 관심을 차지해야 마땅하다. 돌아온 뒤로 퍼시벌 경에게서 그에 대한 내 평가를 나아지게 할 만한 무엇을 보았던가?\n\n딱히 말하기 어렵다. 돌아온 뒤로 자잘한 짜증거리와 성가신 일들이 그를 따라다닌 듯한데, 그런 처지에서 가장 좋은 모습을 보일 수 있는 사람이 어디 있겠는가. 내 눈에는 잉글랜드를 떠날 때보다 야위어 보인다. 지겨운 기침과 불편한 안절부절못함은 확실히 심해졌다. 태도는—적어도 나를 대하는 태도는—예전보다 훨씬 퉁명스럽다. 돌아온 날 저녁, 그는 옛날의 격식과 정중함이라고는 거의 없이 나를 맞았다—반가운 인사말도 없고—나를 보아 각별히 기뻐하는 기색도 없고—짧게 손을 한 번 잡아 흔들고는 무뚝뚝하게 “안녕하십니까, 홀콤 양—다시 뵈어 반갑습니다” 한마디뿐이었다. 그는 나를 블랙워터 파크에 딸린 필수 비품 가운데 하나쯤으로 받아들이고, 내가 제자리에 자리 잡은 것을 확인하고 만족한 뒤, 그대로 나를 완전히 지나쳐 버리는 듯했다.\n\n대개의 남자는 밖에서는 감추던 성정의 일단을 제 집에서 드러내는 법인데, 퍼시벌 경은 벌써 정돈과 규칙에 대한 병적인 집착을 드러냈다. 내가 여태 알던 그의 성격에 비추어 보면 아주 새로운 발견이다. 내가 서재에서 책을 꺼내 탁자 위에 두면 그는 따라와서 도로 꽂아 놓는다. 내가 의자에서 일어나 앉았던 자리에 그대로 두면 그는 조심스레 벽 쪽 제자리로 되돌려 놓는다. 양탄자에 떨어진 꽃잎을 주우면서, 마치 그것이 양탄자에 구멍을 내는 시뻘건 잉걸불이라도 되는 양 못마땅하게 중얼거리고, 식탁보에 주름이 잡혔거나 만찬 식탁의 나이프 하나가 제자리에 없으면 마치 하인들이 자기를 몸소 모욕하기라도 한 듯 사납게 호통을 친다.\n\n그가 돌아온 뒤로 그를 괴롭힌 듯한 자잘한 성가신 일들에 대해서는 앞에서도 언급했다. 내가 알아차린 그의 나쁜 쪽 변화도 상당 부분 거기서 비롯된 것일 수 있다. 나는 그렇다고 나 자신을 설득해 보려 한다. 벌써부터 앞날에 낙담하고 싶지 않기 때문이다. 오랜 부재 끝에 제 집에 발을 들여놓는 바로 그 순간에 성가신 일과 마주치는 것은 누구의 성미에도 견디기 힘든 노릇이며, 실제로 그 짜증스러운 일이 내가 보는 앞에서 퍼시벌 경에게 일어났다.\n\n그들이 도착한 날 저녁, 가정부는 주인 내외와 손님들을 맞으려고 나를 따라 현관으로 나왔다. 그녀를 보자마자 퍼시벌 경은 최근에 찾아온 사람이 있었느냐고 물었다. 가정부는 앞서 내게 말했던 그대로, 주인의 귀가 시기를 묻고 간 낯선 신사의 방문을 이야기했다. 그는 곧바로 그 신사의 이름을 물었다. 이름은 남기지 않았다고 했다. 신사의 용건은? 용건은 말하지 않았다고 했다. 그 신사는 어떻게 생겼던가? 가정부는 그를 묘사해 보려 했으나, 주인이 알아볼 만한 어떤 개인적 특징으로도 그 이름 없는 방문객을 짚어 내지 못했다. 퍼시벌 경은 눈살을 찌푸리고 성난 듯 바닥을 구르더니, 아무에게도 아랑곳하지 않고 집 안으로 걸어 들어갔다. 그런 하찮은 일에 왜 그토록 마음이 흐트러졌는지 나는 알 수 없다—그러나 그가 심각하게 마음이 흐트러진 것만은 의심할 여지가 없다.\n\n대체로 보아, 지금 분명 그의 마음을 남몰래 괴롭히고 있는 근심이 무엇이든 시간이 그것을 떨쳐 내게 해 줄 때까지는, 제 집에서의 그의 태도와 말과 행실에 대해 단정적인 판단을 내리는 일은 삼가는 편이 나을 것이다. 나는 새 장으로 넘어가겠고, 내 펜은 당분간 로라의 남편을 내버려 두겠다.\n\n두 손님—포스코 백작과 백작부인—이 내 목록의 다음 차례다. 백작부인부터 처리하기로 하자. 그 여자와는 되도록 빨리 볼일을 끝내는 편이 나으니.\n\n다시 만나면 숙모를 알아보지도 못할 거라고 로라가 편지에 써 보낸 것은 결코 과장이 아니었다. 결혼이 여자에게 일으킨 변화 가운데 포스코 백작부인에게 일어난 것만 한 변화를 나는 여태 본 적이 없다.\n\n엘리너 페얼리(서른일곱 살)였을 때 그녀는 늘 거드름 피우는 헛소리를 늘어놓았고, 허영심 많고 어리석은 여자가 오래 참는 남자들에게 지울 수 있는 온갖 자잘한 요구로 그 불쌍한 남자들을 볶아 댔다. 포스코 백작부인(마흔세 살)이 된 지금은 몇 시간이고 한마디도 없이 앉아, 더없이 기이한 방식으로 제 안에 얼어붙어 있다. 예전에 얼굴 양옆으로 늘어뜨리고 다니던 그 흉측하고 우스꽝스러운 애교머리는 이제 옛날 가발에서나 볼 법한 아주 짧은 곱슬머리의 뻣뻣한 줄로 바뀌었다. 수수한 부인용 모자가 머리를 덮고 있어, 내가 기억하는 한 그녀의 평생에 처음으로 점잖은 여자처럼 보이게 한다. 이제는 아무도(물론 남편은 논외로 하고) 그녀에게서 예전에 누구나 보던 것을 보지 못한다—쇄골과 어깨뼈 언저리에 드러나던 여자 골격의 구조 말이다. 목까지 높이 올라오는 수수한 검정이나 회색 드레스—처녀 시절이라면 그때그때 기분에 따라 비웃거나 비명을 질렀을 옷을 입고, 그녀는 구석에 말없이 앉아 있다. 살결의 땀구멍까지 백묵처럼 보일 만큼 메마른 흰 두 손은 단조로운 자수를 놓거나 백작이 손수 피울 담배를 끝없이 마느라 쉴 새가 없다. 그 차가운 푸른 눈이 이따금 일감에서 떨어질 때면 대개 남편에게로 향하는데, 그 눈빛은 충직한 개의 눈에서 우리 모두가 익히 보아 온 말 없는 순종의 물음 그것이다. 얼음 같은 절제의 겉껍질 아래에서 내가 여태 알아챈 유일한 내면의 해빙 비슷한 것은, 백작이 말을 걸거나 특별한 관심이나 주의에 가까운 눈길을 주는 이 집안의 어떤 여자(하녀들까지 포함해서)에게든 억눌린 범 같은 질투가 한두 번 새어 나온 일뿐이다. 이 한 가지를 빼면 그녀는 아침이나 낮이나 밤이나, 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 날이 좋으나 궂으나, 언제나 조각상처럼 차갑고 그 조각상을 깎아 낸 돌처럼 속을 알 수 없다. 세상살이의 통상적인 쓸모로 보자면 그녀에게 일어난 이 놀라운 변화는 의심할 바 없이 좋은 쪽으로의 변화다. 그 덕에 그녀는 예의 바르고 조용하고 나서지 않는, 거치적거리는 법이 없는 여자가 되었으니 말이다. 그녀의 은밀한 속마음이 정말로 나아졌는지 아니면 나빠졌는지는 또 다른 문제다. 나는 한두 번 그 오므린 입술 위로 표정이 홱 바뀌는 것을 보았고, 그 차분한 목소리에서 문득 억양이 달라지는 것을 들었는데, 그 때문에 지금의 억눌린 상태가 그녀의 천성 속 위험한 무언가를, 예전 자유로운 생활에서라면 해롭지 않게 증발해 버렸을 무언가를 봉인해 버린 것이 아닌가 의심하게 되었다. 이 생각이 아주 틀렸을 가능성도 충분히 있다. 그러나 내 느낌으로는 내가 옳다. 시간이 밝혀 주리라.\n\n그러면 이 놀라운 변신을 이루어 낸 마법사—한때 제멋대로이던 이 잉글랜드 여자를 친정 식구들조차 알아보지 못할 만큼 길들여 놓은 외국인 남편—백작 그 사람은 어떤가? 백작에 대해서는 무어라 해야 하나?\n\n한마디로 말하면 이렇다. 그는 무엇이든 길들일 수 있을 것처럼 보이는 사람이다. 여자가 아니라 암범과 결혼했더라도 그는 그 암범을 길들였을 것이다. 나와 결혼했더라면 나도 그의 아내가 그러듯 그의 담배를 말고 있을 것이다—그가 나를 바라보면 그녀가 그러듯 나도 입을 다물고 있을 것이다.\n\n이 은밀한 종이 앞에서조차 고백하기가 거의 두렵다. 그 남자는 나의 흥미를 끌었고, 나를 끌어당겼고, 기어이 그를 좋아하게 만들었다. 짧은 이틀 만에 그는 내 호의 어린 평가 속으로 곧장 걸어 들어왔는데, 어떻게 그 기적을 부렸는지는 나로서도 알 길이 없다.\n\n이렇게 그를 마음에 떠올려 보니, 내가 그를 얼마나 또렷하게 보고 있는지가 참으로 놀랍다!—퍼시벌 경보다도, 페얼리 씨보다도, 월터 하트라이트보다도, 로라 자신을 유일한 예외로 하면 내가 떠올리는 그 어떤 자리에 없는 사람보다도 훨씬 또렷하게! 나는 그의 목소리를 지금 그가 말하고 있기라도 한 듯 들을 수 있다. 어제 그가 나눈 이야기를 지금 듣고 있기라도 한 듯 알고 있다. 그를 어떻게 묘사해야 하나? 그의 외모와 습관과 소일거리에는, 다른 남자에게서 보았더라면 가장 매서운 말로 나무라거나 가장 가차 없이 비웃었을 별난 구석들이 있다. 그런데 그에게서만은 그것을 나무라지도 비웃지도 못하게 만드는 것은 대체 무엇인가?\n\n이를테면 그는 엄청나게 뚱뚱하다. 나는 여태껏 살진 인간이라면 유난히 싫어해 왔다. 지나친 비대함과 지나친 쾌활함을 떼어 놓을 수 없는 짝으로 엮는 세상의 통념은, 상냥한 사람만이 살이 찐다고 선언하거나, 아니면 우연히 몸에 붙은 그 수많은 파운드의 살이 그 살을 이고 다니는 사람의 성정에 곧바로 좋은 영향을 준다고 선언하는 것과 다름없다고 나는 늘 주장해 왔다. 그리고 이웃의 그 어떤 마른 사람, 그 어떤 못된 사람 못지않게 인색하고 악랄하고 잔인한 뚱뚱한 사람들의 예를 들어 이 두 가지 터무니없는 주장을 한결같이 반박해 왔다. 헨리 8세가 상냥한 인물이었던가? 교황 알렉산데르 6세가 선한 사람이었던가? 살인자 매닝 씨와 살인자 매닝 부인은 둘 다 유난히 뚱뚱한 사람들이 아니었던가? 잉글랜드를 통틀어 가장 잔인한 여자들로 소문난 삯간호부들은 대체로 잉글랜드를 통틀어 가장 뚱뚱한 여자들이기도 하지 않던가?—이런 식으로 현대와 고대, 국내와 국외, 상류와 하류를 오가며 수십 가지 예를 들어 왔다. 지금 이 순간에도 이 문제에 대해 이토록 강경한 견해를 온 힘을 다해 붙들고 있건만, 그런데도 여기 헨리 8세만큼이나 뚱뚱한 포스코 백작이 있어, 자신의 그 밉살스러운 비만이 아무 걸림돌도 되지 않은 채 단 하루 만에 내 호의 안에 자리를 잡았다. 참으로 놀라운 일이다!\n\n그의 얼굴이 그를 그렇게 추천해 준 것일까?\n\n얼굴 때문일지도 모른다. 그는 위대한 나폴레옹을 크게 확대해 놓은 듯 놀랍도록 닮았다. 이목구비에는 나폴레옹의 장대한 균형이 있고, 표정은 그 위대한 군인의 얼굴에 서린 웅장하도록 고요하고 흔들림 없는 힘을 떠올리게 한다. 이 놀라운 닮음이 처음에 나를 사로잡은 것은 분명하다. 그러나 그에게는 그 닮음 말고도 나를 더 강하게 사로잡은 무언가가 있다. 지금 내가 찾으려 하는 그 영향력은 그의 눈에 있는 것 같다. 내가 여태 본 것 가운데 가장 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 잿빛 눈이며, 때때로 거기에는 차갑고 맑고 아름답고 거역할 수 없는 광채가 어리는데, 그 빛은 나로 하여금 그를 바라보지 않을 수 없게 만들면서도, 막상 바라볼 때면 차라리 느끼지 않았으면 싶은 감각을 불러일으킨다. 얼굴과 머리의 다른 부분에도 이상한 특징들이 있다. 이를테면 안색은 유난히 누르스름하면서도 희멀건데, 짙은 갈색 머리카락과 너무나 어울리지 않아서 나는 그 머리가 가발이 아닌가 의심한다. 그리고 온통 말끔히 면도한 그 얼굴은, (퍼시벌 경의 말로는) 예순에 가까운 나이인데도 내 얼굴보다 더 매끄럽고 어떤 자국도 주름도 없다. 그러나 내가 보기에 여태 본 모든 남자들로부터 그를 구별해 주는 두드러진 신체적 특징은 이런 것들이 아니다. 그를 뭇사람의 대열에서 도드라지게 하는 뚜렷한 특이함은, 적어도 지금으로서 내가 말할 수 있는 한, 오로지 그 눈의 범상치 않은 표정과 범상치 않은 힘에 있다.\n\n그의 태도와 우리말을 구사하는 솜씨도 어느 정도는 그가 내 호감을 얻는 데 한몫했을 것이다. 그에게는 여자의 말을 들을 때의 그 조용한 공손함, 기꺼워하며 귀 기울이는 관심 어린 표정, 여자에게 말할 때 목소리에 어리는 은근한 부드러움이 있는데, 우리가 무어라 하든 우리 가운데 누구도 그것에는 저항하지 못한다. 여기에 더해 영어를 남달리 능란하게 구사하는 것도 당연히 그에게 도움이 된다. 많은 이탈리아 사람이 우리네 억세고 딱딱한 북방 언어를 익히는 데 놀라운 재능을 보인다는 말은 자주 들었지만, 포스코 백작을 보기 전까지 나는 어떤 외국인이 그가 하듯이 영어를 할 수 있으리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없다. 억양만으로는 그가 우리와 같은 나라 사람이 아님을 알아채기가 거의 불가능한 때가 있고, 유창함으로 말하자면 백작만큼 말이 끊기거나 되풀이되는 일 없이 이야기할 수 있는 잉글랜드 태생은 아주 드물다. 문장 구성이야 다소간 외국식일지 몰라도, 나는 그가 잘못된 표현을 쓰거나 낱말을 고르느라 한순간이라도 머뭇거리는 것을 아직 들어 본 적이 없다.\n\n이 기이한 남자의 아주 사소한 특징들에도 하나같이 놀랍도록 독창적이고 어리둥절할 만큼 모순된 데가 있다. 그렇게 뚱뚱하고 그렇게 나이가 들었는데도 그의 몸놀림은 놀랍도록 가볍고 부드럽다. 방 안에서는 우리 여자들 누구 못지않게 소리가 나지 않고, 게다가 누가 보아도 분명한 정신의 굳건함과 힘을 지녔으면서도 우리 가운데 가장 약한 사람만큼이나 신경이 예민하다. 뜻하지 않은 소리에 로라 못지않게 지독하리만치 잘 놀란다. 어제 퍼시벌 경이 스패니얼 한 마리를 때렸을 때 그는 움찔하며 몸서리를 쳤고, 나는 백작에 견주어 내가 다정함도 감수성도 없다는 것이 부끄러울 지경이었다.\n\n이 마지막 사건 이야기를 하다 보니 아직 언급하지 않은 그의 가장 기이한 특징 하나가 떠오른다—애완동물에 대한 유별난 애정이다.\n\n그중 일부는 대륙에 두고 왔지만, 그는 이 집에 코카투 한 마리와 카나리아 두 마리, 그리고 흰쥐 한 가족 전부를 데려왔다. 이 별난 총아들에게 필요한 것은 모두 손수 챙기고, 그 짐승들이 놀랄 만큼 자기를 따르고 자기와 스스럼없이 굴도록 길들여 놓았다. 다른 사람에게는 더없이 사납고 음험한 새인 코카투가 그에게만은 정말로 애정을 품은 듯하다. 그가 새장에서 꺼내 주면 새는 그의 무릎으로 폴짝 뛰어올라 그 거대한 몸을 발톱으로 기어오르고는, 상상할 수 있는 가장 어리광 어린 몸짓으로 볏을 그의 누르스름한 이중 턱에 비벼 댄다. 카나리아 새장 문을 열어 놓고 부르기만 하면, 예쁘고 영리하게 길들여진 그 작은 것들은 겁 없이 그의 손에 내려앉고, 그가 “위층으로 올라가라”고 하면 통통하게 펼친 손가락을 하나씩 하나씩 밟고 올라가, 맨 꼭대기 손가락에 이르면 목이 터져라 기뻐하며 함께 노래한다. 그의 흰쥐들은 그가 직접 도안하고 만든, 알록달록 칠한 철사 세공 탑 속에서 산다. 쥐들도 카나리아만큼이나 순해서 카나리아처럼 늘 밖으로 풀려 나온다. 쥐들은 그의 온몸을 기어 다니며 조끼 속을 들락날락하고, 눈처럼 흰 몸으로 짝을 지어 그 널찍한 어깨 위에 앉는다. 그는 다른 애완동물보다 쥐를 더 아끼는 듯 보이는데, 쥐들을 보며 미소 짓고 입을 맞추고 온갖 애칭으로 부른다. 잉글랜드 사람 가운데 이런 어린애 같은 취미와 놀이를 즐기는 이가 있다고 가정할 수 있다면, 그 사람은 분명 그것을 다소 부끄러워하며 어른들 앞에서는 서둘러 변명하려 들 것이다. 그러나 백작은 자신의 거대한 몸집과 가녀린 작은 애완동물들 사이의 놀라운 대비에서 아무런 우스움도 못 느끼는 모양이다. 그는 잉글랜드 여우 사냥꾼들이 모인 자리에서도 태연히 흰쥐에게 입 맞추고 카나리아에게 재잘거릴 것이며, 그들이 아무리 크게 웃어 대도 그저 야만인이라고 딱하게 여길 뿐일 것이다.\n\n이렇게 적어 놓고 보면 도무지 믿기지 않지만, 코카투에게 노처녀 같은 애정을 쏟고 흰쥐를 다루는 데 손풍금 소년 같은 자잘한 재주를 부리는 바로 그 사람이, 무언가 그를 자극하는 일이 생기면 대담하고 독립적인 사고와 온갖 언어로 된 책에 대한 지식과 유럽 절반의 수도에서 겪은 사교계의 경험을 담아 이야기할 수 있다는 것 또한 분명한 사실이다. 그것이면 문명 세계의 어떤 모임에서도 그를 좌중의 주인공으로 만들기에 충분하다. 이 카나리아 조련사, 흰쥐를 위한 탑의 건축가는 (퍼시벌 경이 직접 내게 말해 준 바로는) 당대 첫손에 꼽히는 실험 화학자 가운데 하나이며, 여러 놀라운 발명 가운데 죽은 뒤의 몸을 대리석처럼 단단하게 굳혀 영원히 보존하는 방법도 발견했다고 한다. 뜻하지 않은 소리에 놀라고 집 스패니얼이 매 맞는 것을 보면 움찔할 만큼 신경이 곱게 조율된 이 뚱뚱하고 굼뜬 노신사는, 도착한 이튿날 아침 마구간 뜰로 들어가 사슬에 묶인 블러드하운드—먹이를 주는 마부조차 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있으려 할 만큼 사나운 짐승—의 머리에 손을 얹었다. 그의 아내와 나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 짧기는 했지만 나는 그 뒤에 벌어진 광경을 잊지 못할 것이다.\n\n“그 개 조심하십쇼, 나리,” 마부가 말했다. “아무한테나 달려듭니다요!” “그건 말일세, 친구,” 백작이 조용히 대답했다. “모두가 저를 무서워하기 때문이지. 나한테도 달려드는지 어디 보세.” 그러고는 십 분 전까지 카나리아들이 앉아 있던 통통하고 누르스름하게 흰 손가락을 그 무시무시한 짐승의 머리에 얹고, 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다. “너희 덩치 큰 개들은 죄다 겁쟁이야,” 그는 자기 얼굴과 개의 얼굴을 한 치 거리에 두고 짐승에게 경멸하듯 말했다. “너는 가엾은 고양이나 죽이겠지, 이 지독한 겁쟁이야. 굶주린 거지에게나 달려들겠지, 이 지독한 겁쟁이야. 방심한 틈에 덮칠 수 있는 것—네 큰 몸뚱이와 못된 흰 이빨과 침 흘리는 피에 굶주린 아가리를 무서워하는 것, 그런 것한테나 달려들기를 좋아하지. 너는 지금 당장 내 목을 조를 수도 있어, 이 비열하고 가련한 골목대장아. 그런데도 감히 내 얼굴을 마주 보지도 못하는군. 내가 너를 무서워하지 않으니까. 생각을 고쳐먹고 내 살진 목덜미에 이빨을 시험해 보겠느냐? 흥! 그럴 리가!” 그는 뜰에 있던 사람들의 놀란 얼굴을 보고 웃으며 돌아섰고, 개는 얌전히 제 집으로 기어 들어갔다. “아! 내 고운 조끼!” 그가 애처롭게 말했다. “여기 온 것이 후회스럽군. 저 짐승의 침이 내 깨끗하고 예쁜 조끼에 묻었어.” 이 말은 그의 이해할 수 없는 별스러움 가운데 또 하나를 잘 보여 준다. 그는 세상 그 어떤 얼빠진 멋쟁이 못지않게 좋은 옷을 좋아해서, 블랙워터 파크에 머문 이틀 동안 벌써 네 벌의 화려한 조끼를 차려입고 나타났다—하나같이 밝고 요란한 색이고, 하나같이 그의 몸집에 비추어 보아도 엄청나게 큰 것들이었다.\n\n사소한 일에서 보이는 그의 재치와 영리함은 그 성격의 기이한 모순이나 평소 취향과 소일거리의 어린애 같은 하찮음 못지않게 눈에 띈다.\n\n그가 이곳에 머무는 동안 우리 모두와 더없이 좋은 사이로 지낼 작정이라는 것은 이미 알 수 있다. 로라가 속으로 자기를 싫어한다는 것을 그는 분명 알아차렸다(내가 캐묻자 로라도 그렇다고 털어놓았다)—그러나 로라가 꽃을 유난히 좋아한다는 것도 알아냈다. 로라가 꽃다발을 갖고 싶어 할 때면 언제나 그가 손수 꺾어 다듬은 꽃다발이 준비되어 있고, 게다가 내게는 아주 재미있게도, 그는 늘 똑같은 꽃을 똑같은 방식으로 묶은 것을 하나 더 교묘하게 마련해 두어, 얼음처럼 질투 많은 아내가 자기가 홀대받았다고 생각할 겨를조차 없게 만든다. 사람들 앞에서 백작부인을 다루는 솜씨는 볼 만한 구경거리다. 그는 아내에게 절을 하고, 늘 “나의 천사”라고 부르고, 카나리아를 손가락에 앉혀 아내에게 문안을 드리고 노래를 불러 주게 하며, 아내가 담배를 건네면 그 손에 입을 맞춘다. 그 답례로 사탕을 주는데, 주머니에서 상자를 꺼내 장난스럽게 아내의 입에 넣어 준다. 그가 아내를 다스리는 쇠막대는 사람들 앞에 결코 나타나지 않는다—그것은 은밀한 막대이고, 언제나 위층에 간직되어 있다.\n\n나를 자기편으로 만드는 방법은 전혀 다르다. 그는 마치 내가 남자이기라도 한 듯 진지하고 사리에 맞게 이야기함으로써 내 허영심을 부추긴다. 그렇다! 그에게서 떨어져 있으면 나는 그 속을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있다—여기 내 방에 올라와 그를 떠올릴 때면 그가 내 허영심을 부추긴다는 것을 안다—그런데도 아래층으로 내려가 다시 그와 자리를 함께하면, 그는 또다시 내 눈을 가릴 것이고 나는 또다시 우쭐해질 것이다. 마치 그 속을 꿰뚫어 본 적이 한 번도 없었던 것처럼! 그는 자기 아내를 다루듯, 로라를 다루듯, 마구간 뜰의 블러드하운드를 다루듯 나를 다룰 수 있고, 퍼시벌 경 그 사람 또한 하루 종일 시시각각 그렇게 다룬다. “나의 좋은 퍼시벌! 자네의 그 투박한 잉글랜드식 유머가 어찌나 마음에 드는지!”—“나의 좋은 퍼시벌! 자네의 그 단단한 잉글랜드식 상식이 어찌나 즐거운지!” 퍼시벌 경이 그의 여자 같은 취향과 놀이를 두고 아무리 무례한 말을 퍼부어도 그는 그런 식으로 조용히 흘려버린다—늘 준남작을 세례명으로 부르고, 더없이 침착한 우월감으로 미소 지으며, 어깨를 두드려 주고, 사람 좋은 아버지가 제멋대로인 아들을 참아 주듯 너그럽게 참아 준다.\n\n이 기이하도록 독창적인 남자에게 도무지 흥미를 느끼지 않을 수 없어서, 나는 퍼시벌 경에게 그의 지난 삶에 대해 묻게 되었다.\n\n퍼시벌 경도 그 일에 대해서는 아는 것이 적거나, 아니면 내게 말해줄 마음이 적은 모양이다. 그와 백작이 처음 만난 것은 여러 해 전 로마에서였고, 그때의 위험한 사정에 관해서는 내가 다른 데서 이미 언급한 바 있다. 그 뒤로 두 사람은 런던에서, 파리에서, 빈에서 늘 붙어 다녔다. 그러나 이탈리아에서 다시 만난 적은 한 번도 없다. 백작은 어찌 된 영문인지 지난 몇 해 동안 제 고국의 국경을 넘은 일이 없기 때문이다. 혹시 무슨 정치적 박해의 희생양이 된 것은 아닐까? 아무튼 그는 영국에 머물고 있을지 모를 제 나라 사람이라면 누구든 놓치지 않으려고 애국심에 불타 신경을 곤두세우는 듯하다. 도착한 날 저녁에도 그는 여기서 가장 가까운 읍내가 얼마나 떨어져 있는지, 그리고 혹시 그곳에 자리 잡고 사는 이탈리아 신사를 우리가 아는지 물었다. 그가 대륙의 누군가와 편지를 주고받고 있는 것만은 분명하다. 그에게 오는 편지에는 온갖 낯선 우표가 붙어 있으니 말이다. 오늘 아침에도 아침 식탁의 그의 자리에 한 통이 놓여 기다리고 있는 것을 보았는데, 커다란 관인처럼 보이는 봉인이 찍혀 있었다. 혹시 제 나라 정부와 편지를 주고받는 것일까? 하지만 그렇다면 그가 정치적 망명자일지 모른다는 나의 또 다른 짐작과는 좀처럼 아귀가 맞지 않는다.\n\n포스코 백작에 대해 어쩌면 이렇게나 많이 써놓았는지! 그런데 그게 다 무엇이 되었다는 말인가?——가엾고 다정한 길모어 씨라면 그 속을 알 수 없는 사무적인 말투로 그렇게 물었으리라. 나로서는 다만 이렇게 되풀이할 수밖에 없다. 이토록 짧은 사귐인데도 나는 분명히 백작에게 야릇한, 반은 내키고 반은 내키지 않는 호감을 느낀다고. 그는 퍼시벌 경에게 확실히 얻어낸 것과 똑같은 종류의 우위를 내게도 세워놓은 듯하다. 그 뚱뚱한 친구를 대하는 태도가 이따금 거리낌 없고 심지어 무례하기까지 한데도, 퍼시벌 경은 그럼에도 백작의 심기를 크게 거스르지 않으려고 조심하는 것이 내 눈에는 훤히 보인다. 나 또한 그를 두려워하고 있는 것일까? 지금껏 살아오면서 적으로 두기가 이토록 꺼려지는 사내를 본 적이 없는 것만은 분명하다. 그것이 내가 그를 좋아하기 때문일까, 아니면 그를 무서워하기 때문일까? 키 사?——포스코 백작이라면 제 나라 말로 그렇게 말했을 것이다. 누가 알겠는가?\n\n6월 16일.——오늘은 내 생각과 인상 말고도 적어둘 일이 생겼다. 손님이 한 사람 찾아왔다. 로라와 나로서는 전혀 모르는 사람이고, 퍼시벌 경도 전혀 예상하지 못했던 모양이다.\n\n우리는 모두 점심 식사 중이었다. 베란다로 통하는 새 프랑스식 창문이 달린 그 방이었다. 백작은(기숙학교 여학생들 말고는 어떤 인간이 그렇게 먹어치우는 것을 내 눈으로 본 적이 없을 만큼 과자를 게걸스레 먹는다) 네 번째 타르트를 자못 진지한 얼굴로 청해서 우리를 웃겨놓은 참이었다. 바로 그때 하인이 들어와 손님이 왔음을 알렸다.\n\n“메리먼 씨가 방금 도착하셨습니다, 퍼시벌 경. 지금 바로 뵙기를 청하십니다.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 흠칫 놀라더니 성난 듯 당황한 표정으로 하인을 바라보았다.\n\n“메리먼 씨라고!” 그는 제 귀를 의심하는 사람처럼 되뇌었다.\n\n“예, 퍼시벌 경. 런던에서 오신 메리먼 씨입니다.”\n\n“어디 있나?”\n\n“서재에 계십니다, 퍼시벌 경.”\n\n마지막 대답이 떨어지자마자 그는 식탁에서 일어나, 우리 중 누구에게도 한마디 없이 서둘러 방을 나갔다.\n\n“메리먼 씨가 누구예요?” 로라가 나를 쳐다보며 물었다.\n\n“나도 도무지 모르겠구나.” 내가 대답으로 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n백작은 네 번째 타르트를 다 먹어치우고 곁상으로 가서 제 심술궂은 앵무새를 돌보고 있었다. 그는 새를 어깨에 앉힌 채 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n“메리먼 씨는 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사입니다.” 그가 조용히 말했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사. 로라의 물음에 대한 더없이 명료한 대답이었지만, 사정이 사정인 만큼 만족스럽지는 않았다. 만일 메리먼 씨가 의뢰인의 부름을 특별히 받고 온 것이라면, 그 부름에 따르려고 도성을 떠나온 데에 놀라울 것은 하나도 없다. 그러나 변호사가 부름도 받지 않고 런던에서 햄프셔까지 내려왔다면, 그리고 그가 어느 신사의 집에 나타난 일이 그 신사 자신을 심각하게 놀라게 했다면, 그 법률가 손님이 매우 중대하고 매우 뜻밖의 소식을 지니고 왔다고 보아도 무방할 것이다. 그것이 아주 좋은 소식일지 아주 나쁜 소식일지는 알 수 없으나, 어느 쪽이든 흔해빠진 일상의 소식일 리는 없는 것이다.\n\n로라와 나는 십오 분 남짓 식탁에 말없이 앉아, 무슨 일이 벌어졌을까 불안하게 궁금해하며 퍼시벌 경이 곧 돌아오기를 기다렸다. 그가 돌아올 낌새는 없었고, 우리는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나서려 했다.\n\n백작은 여느 때처럼 자상하게, 앵무새에게 먹이를 주던 구석에서 새를 여전히 어깨에 앉힌 채 걸어 나와 우리를 위해 문을 열어주었다. 로라와 포스코 백작부인이 먼저 나갔다. 내가 막 그들을 따라 나서려는데 그가 손짓을 하더니, 내가 그의 앞을 지나기 전에 참으로 야릇한 태도로 말을 건넸다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 그는 마치 내가 그 자리에서 낱낱이 말로 털어놓기라도 한 것처럼, 그 순간 내 마음속에 떠올랐으나 입 밖에 내지 않은 생각에 조용히 대답했다. “그렇습니다, 홀콤 양. 무슨 일이 벌어진 것이 맞습니다.”\n\n나는 “저는 그런 말 한 적 없는데요” 하고 대꾸하려던 참이었다. 그러나 심술궂은 앵무새가 잘린 날개를 곤두세우며 째지는 소리로 울어대는 바람에 순식간에 온 신경이 곤두섰고, 나는 그 방을 벗어나게 된 것이 그저 고맙기만 했다.\n\n나는 계단 아래에서 로라와 합류했다. 그 애의 마음속에 있던 생각은 내 마음속에 있던 생각, 포스코 백작이 불시에 알아맞힌 바로 그 생각과 똑같았다. 그리고 그 애가 입을 열었을 때 그 말은 거의 백작의 말을 그대로 되울린 것이었다. 그 애 역시 무슨 일이 벌어진 것 같아 두렵다고 내게 가만히 속삭였다.\n\n# III\n\n6월 16일.—오늘 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 이 날의 기록에 몇 줄 더 덧붙여 두어야겠다.\n\n퍼시벌 경이 사무 변호사 메리먼 씨를 서재에서 맞으려고 점심 식탁에서 일어난 지 두 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 나는 농원을 거닐려고 혼자 방을 나섰다. 마침 층계참 끝에 이르렀을 때 서재 문이 열리고 두 신사가 나왔다. 계단에 모습을 드러내어 그들을 방해하지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 두 사람이 현관을 지나갈 때까지 내려가는 것을 미루기로 했다. 그들은 서로 조심스러운 목소리로 이야기했지만, 그 말은 내 귀에 닿을 만큼 충분히 또렷하게 발음되었다.\n\n“마음 놓으십시오, 퍼시벌 경.” 변호사가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. “모든 것은 글라이드 부인께 달려 있습니다.”\n\n나는 잠깐 내 방으로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌린 참이었으나, 낯선 사람의 입에서 로라의 이름이 나오는 소리에 그 자리에 딱 멈춰 섰다. 엿듣는 것은 매우 나쁘고 매우 떳떳지 못한 짓이었으리라. 그러나 우리 여자들 전체를 통틀어, 명예의 추상적 원칙이 한쪽을 가리키고 애정과 거기서 자라난 이해관계가 다른 쪽을 가리킬 때, 그 원칙에 따라 자기 행동을 다스릴 수 있는 여자가 대체 어디 있단 말인가?\n\n나는 엿들었다—그리고 같은 상황이라면 다시 엿들을 것이다—그렇다! 달리 도리가 없다면 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고서라도.\n\n“잘 알아들으셨겠지요, 퍼시벌 경.” 변호사가 말을 이었다. “글라이드 부인께서 증인 한 사람—특별히 신중을 기하고 싶으시다면 증인 두 사람—앞에서 서명을 하시고, 그런 다음 봉인에 손가락을 얹고 ‘나는 이것을 나의 행위이자 증서로서 교부합니다’라고 말씀하시면 됩니다. 그것이 일주일 안에 이루어진다면 이 조치는 완벽하게 성사될 것이고 걱정거리도 다 끝날 겁니다. 만일 그렇지 않다면——”\n\n“‘그렇지 않다면’이라니 무슨 소리요?” 퍼시벌 경이 성난 목소리로 물었다. “해야 할 일이라면 반드시 되고야 말 것이오. 내가 약속하리다, 메리먼.”\n\n“옳으신 말씀입니다, 퍼시벌 경—지당하십니다. 다만 모든 거래에는 두 갈래 길이 있고, 저희 법률가들은 그 둘 다를 대담하게 정면으로 바라보기를 좋아하지요. 만에 하나 무슨 뜻밖의 사정으로 이 조치가 이루어지지 않는다면, 저로서는 상대편에게 삼 개월짜리 어음을 받아들이게 할 수 있을 듯합니다. 그러나 어음 만기가 돌아올 때 그 돈을 어떻게 마련하실지——”\n\n“어음 따위 집어치우시오! 돈은 오직 한 가지 방법으로만 구할 수 있고, 다시 말하지만 그 방법으로 반드시 구하고야 말 것이오. 가시기 전에 포도주나 한 잔 하시오, 메리먼.”\n\n“감사합니다만, 퍼시벌 경. 상행 열차를 타려면 한시도 지체할 수 없습니다. 조치가 끝나는 대로 알려 주시겠지요? 그리고 제가 권해 드린 조심할 점도 잊지 마시고——”\n\n“물론 잊지 않겠소. 문 앞에 이륜마차를 대 놓았소. 내 마부가 눈 깜짝할 새에 역까지 모셔다 줄 거요. 벤저민, 미친 듯이 몰아라! 어서 타시오. 메리먼 씨가 기차를 놓치면 네 자리는 없는 줄 알아라. 꽉 붙잡으시오, 메리먼. 혹시 뒤집히거든 악마가 제 편은 구해 주려니 믿으시고.” 이런 작별 인사를 던지고 준남작은 몸을 돌려 서재로 돌아갔다.\n\n많이 듣지는 못했지만, 내 귀에 닿은 그 얼마 안 되는 말만으로도 나는 불안해지기에 충분했다. “무슨 일이 있었다”는 그 “무슨 일”은 너무나도 분명하게 심각한 금전 곤경이었고, 퍼시벌 경이 거기서 벗어나는 일은 로라에게 달려 있었다. 로라가 남편의 은밀한 곤경에 휘말리는 광경을 떠올리자 나는 낙담으로 가득 찼다. 사업에 무지하고 퍼시벌 경을 뿌리 깊게 불신하는 탓에 그 낙담이 부풀려졌던 것도 틀림없으리라. 나는 나가려던 것을 그만두고 곧장 로라의 방으로 돌아가 들은 것을 이야기했다.\n\n로라는 내 나쁜 소식을 어찌나 침착하게 받아들이는지 나를 놀라게 했다. 로라는 지금껏 내가 짐작해 온 것보다 남편의 성격과 남편의 곤경에 대해 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있는 것이 분명하다.\n\n“그럴까 봐 걱정했어.” 로라가 말했다. “이름 밝히기를 마다한 그 낯선 신사가 찾아왔다는 말을 들었을 때부터.”\n\n“그럼 그 신사가 누구였다고 생각하니?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경에게 무거운 채권을 가진 사람이겠지.” 로라가 대답했다. “그리고 오늘 메리먼 씨가 여기 온 것도 그 사람 때문일 거야.”\n\n“그 채권에 대해 뭔가 아는 게 있어?”\n\n“아니, 자세한 건 몰라.”\n\n“로라, 먼저 읽어 보지 않고는 아무것도 서명하지 않을 거지?”\n\n“물론이지, 매리언. 해가 없고 정직하게 그이를 도울 수 있는 일이라면 뭐든 할 거야—언니와 내 삶을 되도록 편하고 행복하게 만들기 위해서. 하지만 영문도 모르는 채로는 아무것도 하지 않을 거야. 훗날 언젠가 부끄러워할 이유가 생길지도 모르니까. 이제 그 얘기는 그만하자. 언니는 벌써 모자를 쓰고 있네—우리 정원에 나가서 오후를 꿈결처럼 흘려보내면 어떨까?”\n\n집을 나선 우리는 가장 가까운 그늘 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n집 앞 나무들 사이의 트인 공터를 지날 때, 거기 포스코 백작이 있었다. 6월 오후의 뜨거운 볕을 온몸으로 쬐며 잔디 위를 천천히 오락가락하고 있었다. 그는 보랏빛 리본을 두른 챙 넓은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다. 앞가슴에 흰 장식 자수가 잔뜩 놓인 파란 블라우스가 그 엄청난 몸을 덮고 있었고, 한때 허리였을 자리쯤에는 폭 넓은 주홍색 가죽 띠가 둘려 있었다. 발목께에 또 흰 장식 자수를 드러낸 난징목면 바지와 자줏빛 모로코가죽 슬리퍼가 그의 아랫도리를 꾸미고 있었다. 그는 「세비야의 이발사」에 나오는 피가로의 그 유명한 노래를 부르고 있었는데, 이탈리아 사람의 목이 아니고서는 결코 들을 수 없는 그 또랑또랑하고 유창한 발성이었다. 그는 콘체르티나로 반주를 하면서 황홀한 듯 두 팔을 치켜올리고 우아하게 고개를 이리저리 돌리고 비틀었으니, 남장을 하고 가장무도회에 나온 뚱뚱한 성 체칠리아 같았다. “피가로 콰! 피가로 라! 피가로 수! 피가로 주!” 백작은 이렇게 노래하며 팔을 쭉 뻗어 콘체르티나를 경쾌하게 튕겨 올리고는, 악기 한쪽 옆으로 몸을 굽혀 우리에게 인사했다. 스무 살 적 피가로 본인 같은 경쾌한 기품과 우아함이었다.\n\n“내 말을 믿어, 로라. 저 사람은 퍼시벌 경의 곤경에 대해 뭔가 알고 있어.” 우리가 안전한 거리에서 백작의 인사에 답례하며 내가 말했다.\n\n“왜 그렇게 생각해?” 로라가 물었다.\n\n“그렇지 않고서야 메리먼 씨가 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사라는 걸 어떻게 알았겠니?” 내가 대꾸했다. “게다가 내가 점심 식당에서 너를 따라 나왔을 때, 내가 한마디 묻지도 않았는데 무슨 일이 생겼다고 나한테 말해 주더라. 틀림없어. 저 사람은 우리보다 많이 알고 있어.”\n\n“알고 있더라도 아무것도 묻지 마. 우리 속내를 저 사람한테 털어놓지 마!”\n\n“로라, 너 저 사람을 아주 단단히 싫어하는 것 같구나. 그이가 무슨 말이나 행동을 했기에 그러니?”\n\n“아무것도 없어, 매리언. 오히려 돌아오는 여행 내내 더없이 친절하고 세심했고, 나를 배려해서 퍼시벌의 성마른 폭발을 여러 번 막아 주기까지 했어. 어쩌면 그 사람이 내 남편에게 나보다 훨씬 큰 힘을 지녔기 때문에 싫은 건지도 몰라. 어쩌면 그 사람이 끼어들어 준 덕을 봐야 한다는 게 내 자존심을 상하게 하는지도 모르고. 내가 아는 건 오직 하나, 나는 그 사람이 정말 싫다는 거야.”\n\n그날의 남은 시간과 저녁은 제법 조용히 지나갔다. 백작과 나는 체스를 두었다. 처음 두 판은 그가 정중하게 나에게 이기도록 내주었고, 내가 그 속을 알아차린 것을 눈치채자 용서를 구하더니, 세 번째 판에서는 십 분 만에 나를 외통수로 몰았다. 퍼시벌 경은 저녁 내내 변호사의 방문에 대해 단 한 번도 언급하지 않았다. 그러나 그 일 때문인지 다른 무엇 때문인지, 그에게 유별나게 좋은 쪽으로의 변화가 일어나 있었다. 그는 리머리지에서 시험 기간을 보내던 시절처럼 우리 모두에게 정중하고 상냥했으며, 아내에게 어찌나 놀랍도록 다정하고 친절하던지, 얼음장 같은 포스코 부인마저 짐짓 놀란 얼굴로 그를 바라볼 정도였다. 이게 무슨 뜻일까? 짐작이 갈 것도 같다—로라도 짐작하고 있을까 두렵다—그리고 포스코 백작이 알고 있다는 것만은 확실하다. 나는 저녁나절에 퍼시벌 경이 백작에게서 승낙을 구하듯 그를 쳐다보는 것을 한 번 이상 목격했다.\n\n6월 17일.—사건이 많았던 하루. 여기에 재난의 하루이기도 했다는 말까지 덧붙이지 않아도 되기를 간절히 바란다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사 자리에서도 어제저녁처럼, 우리 머리 위에 드리워진 그 알 수 없는 “조치”(변호사가 그렇게 불렀다)에 대해 아무 말이 없었다. 그러나 한 시간 뒤에 그는 불쑥 아침 거실로 들어왔다. 그의 아내와 나는 모자를 쓴 채 포스코 부인이 합류하기를 기다리고 있던 참이었는데, 그는 백작을 찾았다.\n\n“곧 여기로 오실 거예요.” 내가 말했다.\n\n“실은 말이오.” 퍼시벌 경이 초조하게 방 안을 오가며 말을 이었다. “그저 사무적인 형식을 갖출 일이 있어서 포스코와 그 부인이 서재에 있어 주었으면 하오. 그리고 로라, 당신도 잠깐 거기 있어 주었으면 좋겠소.” 그는 말을 멈추더니 그제야 처음으로 우리가 외출 차림인 것을 알아차린 듯했다. “방금 들어온 거요.” 그가 물었다. “아니면 이제 막 나가려던 참이오?”\n\n“오늘 아침엔 다 함께 호수에 갈까 생각하던 참이었어요.” 로라가 말했다. “하지만 다른 볼일을 말씀하시려는 거라면——”\n\n“아니, 아니오.” 그가 서둘러 대답했다. “내 볼일은 기다려도 되오. 아침 식사 뒤가 아니라 점심 뒤라도 마찬가지지. 다들 호수에 간다고? 좋은 생각이오. 한가한 아침을 보냅시다—나도 한몫 끼겠소.”\n\n자기 계획과 뜻을 남들의 편의에 맞추겠다는, 그답지 않은 선선함이 그 말에서 드러났으니, 설령 그 말은 잘못 알아들을 수 있었다 해도 그 태도만은 오해할 여지가 없었다. 그는 자기 입으로 말한 서재의 그 사무적인 절차를 미룰 핑곗거리가 생긴 것을 분명 안도하고 있었다. 피할 수 없는 결론이 떠오르자 내 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다.\n\n바로 그때 백작과 그 부인이 우리와 합류했다. 부인은 남편의 수놓은 담배쌈지와, 저 끝없는 궐련을 만들 종이 뭉치를 손에 들고 있었다. 신사는 여느 때처럼 블라우스와 밀짚모자 차림으로 사랑스러운 흰 생쥐들이 든 화려한 작은 탑 모양 새장을 들고 있었고, 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 부드러운 상냥함으로 생쥐들에게, 그리고 우리에게 미소를 지었다.\n\n“허락해 주신다면.” 백작이 말했다. “저의 작은 가족—가엾고-순하고-예쁜-생쥐들도 우리와 함께 바람을 쐬러 데려가겠습니다. 집 주변에는 개들이 있는데, 제 가련한 흰 자식들을 개들의 자비에 맡겨 두라고요? 아, 결코 안 될 말이지요!”\n\n그는 탑 모양 새장의 창살 너머로 자기 작은 흰 자식들을 향해 아비처럼 짹짹거렸고, 우리는 모두 호수를 향해 집을 나섰다.\n\n농원에 들어서자 퍼시벌 경은 우리에게서 떨어져 나가 어디론가 사라졌다. 이런 자리에서 늘 일행에게서 저 혼자 떨어져 나가고, 혼자 있을 때면 늘 제가 쓸 새 지팡이를 깎는 데 몰두하는 것이 그 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 기질의 일부인 모양이다. 아무거나 되는대로 자르고 쳐 내는 그 행위 자체가 그를 즐겁게 하는 듯하다. 그는 제 손으로 만든 지팡이로 집 안을 가득 채워 놓았지만, 그중 어느 하나도 두 번 집어 드는 법이 없다. 한 번 쓰고 나면 그것에 대한 흥미는 죄다 소진되고, 계속 나아가 더 많이 만드는 것밖에 생각하지 않는다.\n\n낡은 보트 창고에서 그는 다시 우리와 합류했다. 우리가 모두 자리를 잡고 앉은 뒤에 오간 대화를 오간 그대로 여기 적어 두겠다. 그것은 나에게는 중요한 대화다. 포스코 백작이 내 생각과 감정에 행사해 온 영향력을 심각하게 불신하고, 앞으로는 있는 힘껏 단호히 물리치도록 나를 몰아붙였기 때문이다.\n\n보트 창고는 우리 모두가 들어갈 만큼 넉넉했지만 퍼시벌 경은 밖에 남아 주머니도끼로 가장 새로 만든 지팡이를 다듬고 있었다. 우리 여자 셋은 큰 걸상에 넉넉히 자리를 잡았다. 로라는 바느질감을 들었고 포스코 부인은 궐련을 말기 시작했다. 나는 여느 때처럼 할 일이 없었다. 내 손은 늘 남자 손처럼 서툴렀고 앞으로도 그럴 것이다. 백작은 자기 몸에 비해 몇 치수는 작은 걸상을 선선히 차지하고, 창고 벽에 등을 기댄 채 그 위에서 몸의 균형을 잡았다. 벽은 그 무게에 삐걱거리며 신음했다. 그는 탑 모양 새장을 무릎에 올려놓고 여느 때처럼 생쥐들을 풀어 제 몸 위를 기어다니게 했다. 예쁘고 순해 보이는 작은 짐승들이지만, 그것들이 사람 몸을 기어다니는 광경은 웬일인지 나에게 유쾌하지 않다. 그것을 보면 내 신경에도 이상하게 스멀거리는 감각이 일고, 감옥에서 죽어 가는 사람들 위로 지하 감방의 기어다니는 것들이 아무 방해도 받지 않고 달려드는 끔찍한 광경이 떠오른다.\n\n아침 날씨는 바람이 불고 흐렸으며, 황량한 호수 위로 그늘과 햇빛이 빠르게 번갈아 스치는 통에 그 풍경은 갑절로 거칠고 기괴하고 음울해 보였다.\n\n“어떤 사람들은 저걸 그림 같다고들 하지.” 퍼시벌 경이 다 깎지 못한 지팡이로 넓은 경치를 가리키며 말했다. “나는 신사의 영지에 난 얼룩이라고 부르오. 우리 증조부 시절에는 호수가 여기까지 차 있었소. 지금 저 꼴을 보시오! 어디를 재도 깊이가 넉 자가 안 되고 온통 웅덩이와 물구덩이뿐이오. 여유만 있다면 저 물을 죄다 빼고 나무를 심어 버리고 싶소. 우리 집 마름이란 자는(미신에 찌든 멍청이지) 저 호수에 사해처럼 저주가 걸려 있는 게 틀림없다고 하더군. 어떻게 생각하시오, 포스코? 딱 살인하기 좋은 장소 같지 않소?”\n\n“여보시게 퍼시벌.” 백작이 나무라듯 말했다. “자네의 그 견실한 영국식 분별력이 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 건가? 저 물은 너무 얕아서 시체를 감출 수 없고, 사방이 모래라 살인자의 발자국이 그대로 찍힌다네. 통틀어 말해서 내가 평생 눈으로 본 중에 살인하기에 가장 나쁜 장소일세.”\n\n“허튼소리!” 퍼시벌 경이 지팡이를 사납게 깎아 대며 말했다. “내 말뜻을 알면서 그러오. 저 을씨년스러운 경치, 저 외진 자리 말이오. 알아들을 마음이 있으면 알아들으면 되고—그럴 마음이 없다면 내 뜻을 굳이 설명하느라 애쓰지는 않겠소.”\n\n“왜 그러겠나?” 백작이 물었다. “자네 뜻은 아무나 두 마디로 설명할 수 있는데 말일세. 바보가 살인을 저지르려 한다면 자네 호수가 그가 첫 번째로 고를 장소일세. 현명한 자가 살인을 저지르려 한다면 자네 호수가 그가 맨 마지막으로 고를 장소고. 그게 자네 뜻인가? 그렇다면 자, 설명은 다 만들어져 여기 있네. 받아 두게, 퍼시벌. 자네의 착한 포스코의 축복과 함께.”\n\n로라는 백작을 향한 혐오가 얼굴에 조금 지나치게 드러난 채로 그를 바라보았다. 그는 생쥐들에 정신이 팔려 그것을 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n“저 호수 경치가 살인이라는 생각처럼 끔찍한 것과 엮이는 걸 듣자니 유감이군요.” 로라가 말했다. “그리고 포스코 백작께서 굳이 살인자를 부류로 나누셔야 한다면, 말을 고르시는 데 아주 잘못하신 것 같아요. 그들을 그저 바보라고 부르는 건 그들이 누릴 자격이 조금도 없는 관대함을 베푸는 것처럼 들리는걸요. 그리고 그들을 현명한 사람이라고 부르는 건 제게는 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 소리로 들려요. 저는 참으로 현명한 사람은 참으로 선한 사람이며 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어한다고 늘 들어 왔거든요.”\n\n“친애하는 부인.” 백작이 말했다. “참으로 훌륭한 생각이고, 저는 그런 말을 습자책 맨 윗줄에 적혀 있는 걸 본 적이 있지요.” 그는 흰 생쥐 한 마리를 손바닥에 올려놓고 그 특유의 익살맞은 투로 그것에게 말했다. “나의 예쁘고 매끈한 흰 악당아, 여기 너를 위한 도덕 교훈이 있다. 참으로 현명한 생쥐는 참으로 착한 생쥐다. 부디 그 말을 네 동무들에게 전하고, 살아 있는 동안 다시는 새장 창살을 갉지 말아라.”\n\n“뭐든지 우스개로 돌리기는 쉽지요.” 로라가 단호하게 말했다. “하지만 포스코 백작님, 큰 범죄자였던 현명한 사람의 실례를 저에게 하나 대 보시는 건 그리 쉽지 않으실 거예요.”\n\n백작은 그 거대한 어깨를 으쓱하고는 더없이 다정한 태도로 로라에게 미소를 지었다.\n\n“지당한 말씀!” 그가 말했다. “바보의 범죄란 들통난 범죄고, 현명한 자의 범죄란 들통나지 않은 범죄니까요. 제가 실례를 하나 댈 수 있다면, 그건 현명한 자의 실례가 아니겠지요. 친애하는 글라이드 부인, 부인의 건전한 영국식 상식이 저에게는 너무 벅찼습니다. 이번엔 제가 외통수로군요, 홀콤 양—안 그렇습니까?”\n\n“잘 버텨라, 로라.” 문가 제자리에서 듣고 있던 퍼시벌 경이 비웃듯 말했다. “다음엔 범죄란 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고 말해 주려무나. 자, 습자책 도덕 한 자락 더 나가오, 포스코. 범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다. 이런 지긋지긋한 허튼소리가 있나!”\n\n“저는 그게 사실이라고 믿어요.” 로라가 조용히 말했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경이 어찌나 격렬하게, 어찌나 터무니없이 웃음을 터뜨렸는지 우리 모두가 화들짝 놀랐고—누구보다 백작이 놀랐다.\n\n“저도 그렇게 믿어요.” 로라를 구하러 나서며 내가 말했다.\n\n아내의 말에는 영문 모르게 재미있어하던 퍼시벌 경이, 내 말에는 그만큼이나 영문 모르게 짜증을 냈다. 그는 새 지팡이로 모래를 사납게 내리치고는 우리에게서 걸어 나갔다.\n\n“딱하기도 하지, 우리 퍼시벌!” 포스코 백작이 그의 뒷모습을 유쾌하게 바라보며 외쳤다. “영국식 우울증의 희생자예요. 그런데 친애하는 홀콤 양, 친애하는 글라이드 부인, 정말로 범죄가 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고 믿으십니까? 그리고 당신은, 나의 천사여.” 그는 아직 한마디도 하지 않은 아내를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. “당신도 그렇게 생각하오?”\n\n“저는 가르침을 기다립니다.” 백작부인이 로라와 나를 겨냥한 얼음장 같은 질책의 어조로 대답했다. “식견 있는 남자분들 앞에서 감히 제 의견을 내놓기 전에 말이지요.”\n\n“아, 그러세요?” 내가 말했다. “백작부인, 저는 부인께서 여성의 권리를 옹호하시던 시절을 기억합니다. 그리고 여성이 자유로이 의견을 말하는 것도 그 권리 가운데 하나였지요.”\n\n“이 문제에 대한 당신의 견해는 어떠신가요, 백작?” 포스코 부인이 태연히 궐련을 말면서, 나에게는 눈길 하나 주지 않고 물었다.\n\n백작은 대답하기 전에 통통한 새끼손가락으로 흰 생쥐 한 마리를 생각에 잠긴 듯 쓰다듬었다.\n\n“참으로 놀라운 일이지요.” 그가 말했다. “사회가 제 최악의 결점들을 하찮은 상투적 문구 한 조각으로 얼마나 손쉽게 위로하는지 말입니다. 사회가 범죄를 적발하려고 세워 놓은 기구는 한심할 만큼 무력합니다—그런데도 그것이 잘 돌아간다고 말하는 도덕적 경구 하나만 지어내 보십시오, 그 순간부터 모든 사람의 눈이 그 실책들에 대해 멀어 버립니다. 범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고요? 그리고 살인은 반드시 드러난다(이것도 또 하나의 도덕적 경구지요)고요? 그것이 사실인지 큰 도시에서 검시 심리를 주재하는 검시관들에게 물어보십시오, 글라이드 부인. 그것이 사실인지 생명 보험 회사의 서기들에게 물어보십시오, 홀콤 양. 두 분 나라의 신문을 읽어 보십시오. 신문에까지 실리는 몇 안 되는 사건 중에도, 살해된 시신은 발견되었으나 살인자는 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 사례가 있지 않습니까? 보도된 사건에 보도되지 않은 사건을 곱하고, 발견된 시신에 발견되지 않은 시신을 곱해 보십시오. 그러면 어떤 결론에 이르십니까? 이것입니다. 발각되는 어리석은 범죄자들이 있고, 빠져나가는 현명한 범죄자들이 있다는 것. 범죄를 감추는 일, 혹은 범죄를 적발하는 일, 그게 무엇입니까? 한쪽에는 경찰, 다른 한쪽에는 개인이 벌이는 재주 겨루기지요. 범죄자가 우악스럽고 무지한 바보일 때는 열에 아홉은 경찰이 이깁니다. 범죄자가 단호하고 교육받은, 지극히 총명한 사람일 때는 열에 아홉은 경찰이 집니다. 경찰이 이기면 대개 그 이야기를 죄다 듣게 되지요. 경찰이 지면 대개 아무 이야기도 듣지 못하고요. 그런데도 두 분은 이 흔들리는 토대 위에다 범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다는 편안한 도덕률을 세워 올립니다! 그렇지요—두 분이 아시는 범죄는 모두 그렇습니다. 그러면 나머지는요?”\n\n“지독히도 옳은 말씀이고, 아주 잘 표현했소.” 보트 창고 입구에서 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. 퍼시벌 경이 평정을 되찾고, 우리가 백작의 말을 듣고 있는 사이에 돌아와 있었던 것이다.\n\n“더러는 사실이겠지요.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 전부 아주 잘 표현했을 수도 있고요. 하지만 저는 포스코 백작께서 왜 사회에 대한 범죄자의 승리를 그토록 의기양양하게 기리셔야 하는지, 그리고 퍼시벌 경께서는 왜 그러시는 백작께 그토록 소리 높여 박수를 보내셔야 하는지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“들었소, 포스코?” 퍼시벌 경이 물었다. “내 충고를 듣고 청중과 화해하시오. 미덕은 훌륭한 것이라고 말해 주시오—그럼 다들 좋아할 거요, 내가 장담하리다.”\n\n백작은 소리 없이 속으로 웃었고, 그의 조끼 안에 있던 흰 생쥐 두 마리가 제 밑에서 벌어지는 내부의 진동에 놀라 허둥지둥 뛰쳐나와 다시 제 새장으로 기어들어 갔다.\n\n“미덕에 대해서는, 여보시게 퍼시벌, 숙녀분들께서 나에게 말씀해 주실 걸세.” 그가 말했다. “숙녀분들이 나보다 나은 권위자시지. 미덕이 무엇인지 아시니까 말이야. 나는 모르고.”\n\n“저 소리 들었소?” 퍼시벌 경이 말했다. “끔찍하지 않소?”\n\n“사실입니다.” 백작이 조용히 말했다. “저는 세계의 시민이고, 살아오는 동안 하도 여러 종류의 미덕을 만난 터라, 이 늘그막에 어느 것이 옳은 종류이고 어느 것이 그른 종류인지 말하기가 난감합니다. 여기 영국에는 이런 미덕이 있습니다. 그리고 저기 중국에는 저런 미덕이 있지요. 그리고 영국인 존은 내 미덕이야말로 진짜 미덕이라고 말합니다. 그리고 중국인 존도 내 미덕이야말로 진짜 미덕이라고 말하고요. 그래서 제가 한쪽에는 예라 하고 다른 쪽에는 아니오라 합니다만, 목이 긴 장화를 신은 존의 경우나 변발을 늘어뜨린 존의 경우나 저로서는 어리둥절하기가 매한가지입니다. 아, 귀여운 생쥐야! 이리 와서 내게 입 맞추렴. 덕 있는 사람에 대한 너만의 은밀한 생각은 무엇이냐, 나의 예-예-예쁜 것아? 너를 따뜻하게 해 주고 먹을 것을 넉넉히 주는 사람이겠지. 그것도 좋은 생각이야. 적어도 알아들을 수는 있으니까.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 백작님.” 내가 끼어들었다. “백작님의 비유를 받아들인다 해도, 영국에는 중국에 없는 의심할 여지 없는 미덕이 하나 분명 있습니다. 중국 관헌은 지극히 하찮은 구실로 죄 없는 사람들을 수천 명씩 죽입니다. 우리 영국인은 그런 죄에서 온전히 자유롭습니다—우리는 그런 무서운 범죄를 저지르지 않아요—우리는 무분별한 유혈을 진심으로 몸서리치게 싫어합니다.”\n\n“옳은 말이야, 매리언.” 로라가 말했다. “잘 짚었고, 잘 표현했어.”\n\n“부디 백작께서 말씀을 이어 가시게 두시지요.” 포스코 부인이 엄격한 예의를 갖추어 말했다. “젊은 숙녀분들, 이분은 하시는 모든 말씀에 훌륭한 이유가 있지 않고서는 결코 입을 여시지 않는다는 걸 알게 되실 거예요.”\n\n“고맙소, 나의 천사여.” 백작이 대답했다. “봉봉 하나 드시겠소?” 그는 주머니에서 예쁘장한 상감 세공 상자를 꺼내 뚜껑을 연 채 탁자에 올려놓았다. “쇼콜라 아 라 바니유!” 그 속을 알 수 없는 사내가 유쾌하게 외치며 상자 안의 사탕을 달그락거리고는 사방으로 인사를 했다. “이 매혹적인 모임에 대한 경의의 표시로 포스코가 바칩니다.”\n\n“계속 말씀하시지요, 백작.” 그의 아내가 나를 겨냥한 심술궂은 어조로 말했다. “홀콤 양에게 대답해 주시면 고맙겠어요.”\n\n“홀콤 양에게는 대답할 말이 없습니다.” 그 예의 바른 이탈리아인이 대답했다. “다시 말해, 그분이 말씀하신 데까지는 그렇다는 거지요. 그렇습니다! 저는 그분 말씀에 동의합니다. 존 불은 정말로 중국인 존의 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어합니다. 그는 이 세상에 존재하는 어떤 노신사보다도 이웃의 허물을 찾아내는 데는 가장 빠르고, 제 허물을 찾아내는 데는 가장 느린 노신사지요. 그렇다면 그가 이런 점에서, 자기가 단죄하는 저 사람들이 저희 방식으로 그러한 것보다 그렇게나 많이 나은 사람일까요? 홀콤 양, 영국 사회는 범죄의 적인 만큼이나 자주 범죄의 공범입니다. 그렇습니다! 그렇고말고요! 이 나라에서도 범죄란 다른 나라에서와 마찬가지입니다—적인 만큼이나 자주 한 사람과 그 주변 사람들에게 좋은 벗이 되어 주지요. 큰 악당은 제 처자식을 먹여 살립니다. 그가 나쁘면 나쁠수록 그 처자식은 그만큼 더 여러분의 동정의 대상이 되고요. 그는 흔히 제 자신도 먹여 살립니다. 늘 돈을 꾸러 다니는 방탕한 낭비꾼은, 더할 수 없이 절박한 궁핍에 몰려 단 한 번 돈을 꾸는 지독히 정직한 사람보다 친구들에게서 더 많이 얻어 냅니다. 앞의 경우에 친구들은 조금도 놀라지 않고, 그래서 줍니다. 뒤의 경우에 친구들은 몹시 놀라고, 그래서 망설이지요. 악당 씨가 그 인생의 끝에 가서 사는 감옥이, 정직 씨가 그 인생의 끝에 가서 사는 구빈원보다 더 불편한 곳일까요? 자선가 존 하워드 선생은 비참을 덜어 주고 싶을 때 감옥으로 그것을 찾아갑니다. 죄악이 비참한 곳이지요—미덕 또한 비참한 오두막과 움막으로 가는 것이 아니라 말입니다. 가장 널리 사람들의 동정을 얻은 영국 시인은 누구입니까—애절한 글과 애절한 그림의 소재로 가장 손쉬운 이는 누구지요? 문서 위조로 인생을 시작해 자살로 그것을 끝낸 그 참한 젊은이, 여러분의 사랑스럽고 낭만적이고 흥미로운 채터턴이지요. 굶주리는 가난한 삯바느질 여공 둘 중에 누가 더 잘 풀린다고 생각하십니까—유혹을 물리치고 정직하게 사는 여자입니까, 유혹에 넘어가 도둑질을 하는 여자입니까? 그 도둑질이야말로 두 번째 여자의 팔자를 고쳐 준다는 걸 여러분 모두 아십니다—그것이 사람 좋고 자비로운 영국 방방곡곡에 그 여자를 광고해 주지요—그래서 그 여자는 계명을 지킨 자로서는 굶어 죽도록 버려졌을 터인데, 계명을 깨뜨린 자로서 구제를 받습니다. 이리 오너라, 나의 쾌활한 작은 생쥐야! 자! 얍! 짠! 내가 너를 잠시 동안 어엿한 숙녀로 변신시켜 주마. 거기 내 커다란 손바닥 위에 서 있거라, 얘야, 그리고 들어 보렴. 생쥐야, 네가 사랑하는 가난한 남자와 결혼한다면 네 친구의 절반은 너를 가엾어하고 나머지 절반은 너를 나무란다. 그런데 이번엔 거꾸로 네가 마음에도 없는 남자에게 금을 받고 네 몸을 판다면, 네 친구들은 모두 너를 두고 기뻐하고, 공중 예배의 목사는 모든 인간 거래 중에 가장 비열한 그 천한 참극을 승인해 주며, 나중에 네가 예의 바르게 조반에 초대만 해 주면 네 식탁에서 싱글벙글 웃고 있을 게다. 자! 얍! 짠! 다시 생쥐가 되어 찍찍 울어라. 네가 조금만 더 오래 숙녀 노릇을 하고 있다가는 사회가 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어한다고 나에게 말하기 시작할 테고—그러면, 생쥐야, 나는 네 눈과 귀가 정말 조금이라도 쓸모가 있는지 의심하게 될 테니까. 아! 저는 나쁜 사람이지요, 글라이드 부인, 그렇지 않습니까? 저는 남들이 생각만 하는 것을 말합니다. 그리고 온 세상이 가면을 진짜 얼굴로 받아들이자고 한통속으로 짜고 있을 때, 그 통통한 판지를 잡아 뜯어 그 아래 앙상한 뼈를 드러내 보이는 것이 제 무모한 손이지요. 두 분의 다정한 평가 속에서 제 자신을 더 해치기 전에 이 커다란 코끼리 다리를 일으켜 세우겠습니다—일어나서 저 혼자 가벼운 산책이나 하고 오겠습니다. 친애하는 숙녀 여러분, 두 분의 훌륭한 셰리든이 말했듯이, 저는 갑니다—제 인격은 뒤에 남겨 놓고서.”\n\n그는 일어나 새장을 탁자에 올려놓고, 그 안의 생쥐를 세느라 잠시 멈추었다. “하나, 둘, 셋, 넷——하!” 그가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 외쳤다. “하느님 맙소사, 다섯 번째는 어디 있나—가장 어리고, 가장 희고, 가장 사랑스러운—내 생쥐들 중의 베냐민이!”\n\n로라도 나도 재미있어할 만한 마음 상태가 아니었다. 백작의 그 매끄러운 냉소는 우리 둘 다 움츠러들 만한, 그 본성의 새로운 면모를 드러내 보였다. 그러나 그토록 커다란 사내가 그토록 조그만 생쥐를 잃고 괴로워하는 그 우스꽝스러운 꼴만은 견뎌 낼 재간이 없었다. 우리는 저도 모르게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 남편이 보트 창고를 구석구석 뒤질 수 있도록 포스코 부인이 몸소 앞장서서 창고를 비워 주려고 일어서자, 우리도 그를 따라 나가려고 일어섰다.\n\n우리가 세 걸음도 떼기 전에 백작의 재빠른 눈이 우리가 앉아 있던 걸상 밑에서 잃어버린 생쥐를 찾아냈다. 그는 긴 의자를 옆으로 밀치고 그 작은 짐승을 손에 집어 들었으나, 그러다 문득 무릎을 꿇은 자세 그대로 멈춰 서서 바로 제 밑 땅바닥의 한 지점을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다.\n\n다시 일어섰을 때 그의 손은 어찌나 떨리는지 생쥐를 새장에 도로 넣기도 힘들 지경이었고, 그 얼굴은 온통 희부연 납빛 누런 기색이었다.\n\n“퍼시벌!” 그가 속삭이듯 말했다. “퍼시벌! 이리 와 보게.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 지난 십 분 동안 우리 중 누구에게도 신경을 쓰지 않았다. 모래에 숫자를 적었다가 지팡이 끝으로 그것을 다시 지우는 일에 온통 빠져 있었다.\n\n“이번엔 또 무슨 일이오?” 그가 태평하게 보트 창고 안으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오며 물었다.\n\n“저기 아무것도 안 보이나?” 백작이 한 손으로 신경질적으로 그의 옷깃을 붙잡고 다른 손으로 생쥐를 찾아낸 자리 근처를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n“마른 모래가 잔뜩 보이는군.” 퍼시벌 경이 대답했다. “그리고 그 한가운데에 흙 자국이 하나 있고.”\n\n“흙이 아닐세.” 백작이 속삭이며 남은 손마저 갑자기 퍼시벌 경의 옷깃에 걸고 흥분해서 그것을 흔들었다. “피야.”\n\n로라는 그가 아무리 나직하게 속삭였다 해도 그 마지막 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 있었다. 로라는 겁에 질린 얼굴로 나를 돌아보았다.\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리야, 얘.” 내가 말했다. “놀랄 것 없어. 그저 가엾은 길 잃은 개의 피일 뿐이니까.”\n\n모두가 깜짝 놀랐고, 모두의 눈이 묻는 듯이 나에게 못 박혔다.\n\n“그걸 어떻게 아시오?” 퍼시벌 경이 먼저 입을 열어 물었다.\n\n“여러분이 다들 외국에서 돌아오시던 날, 제가 여기서 죽어 가는 그 개를 발견했어요.” 내가 대답했다. “그 가엾은 것이 농원으로 잘못 들어왔다가 사냥터지기의 총에 맞았지요.”\n\n“누구 개였소?” 퍼시벌 경이 물었다. “내 개는 아니었소?”\n\n“그 가엾은 것을 살리려고 해 봤어?” 로라가 간절하게 물었다. “분명 살리려고 해 봤겠지, 매리언?”\n\n“그럼.” 내가 말했다. “가정부와 내가 둘 다 최선을 다했어—하지만 그 개는 치명상을 입어서 우리 손 안에서 죽었어.”\n\n“누구 개였소?” 퍼시벌 경이 조금 짜증스레 제 물음을 한 번 더 되풀이하며 물고 늘어졌다. “내 개였소?”\n\n“아니요, 경의 개는 아니었습니다.”\n\n“그럼 누구 개요? 가정부는 알고 있었소?”\n\n캐서릭 부인이 블랙워터 파크에 다녀간 것을 퍼시벌 경이 알지 못하도록 숨기고 싶어 했다던 가정부의 이야기가 그 마지막 질문을 받는 순간 내 기억에 되살아났고, 나는 그 질문에 답하는 것이 과연 분별 있는 일인지 반쯤 의심했다. 그러나 모두의 불안을 가라앉히려는 마음에 나는 생각 없이 이미 너무 멀리 나아가 있었으니, 이제 와 물러선다면 의심을 불러일으켜 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들 위험만 있었다. 결과는 따지지 말고 당장 대답하는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“네.” 내가 말했다. “가정부는 알고 있었습니다. 캐서릭 부인의 개라고 저에게 말해 주었어요.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 그때까지 포스코 백작과 함께 보트 창고 안쪽 끝에 있었고, 나는 문간에서 그에게 말하고 있었다. 그러나 캐서릭 부인의 이름이 내 입술을 스치는 순간 그는 백작을 거칠게 밀치고 지나와, 훤한 대낮 아래 나와 얼굴을 맞대고 섰다.\n\n“가정부가 어떻게 그게 캐서릭 부인의 개인 줄 알았소?” 그가 물었다. 그는 찌푸린 관심과 주의로 내 눈에 제 눈을 못 박았는데, 그것이 나를 반쯤은 화나게 하고 반쯤은 섬뜩하게 했다.\n\n“가정부가 그것을 안 것은.” 내가 조용히 말했다. “캐서릭 부인이 그 개를 데리고 왔기 때문입니다.”\n\n“개를 데리고 왔다고? 어디로 데리고 왔단 말이오?”\n\n“이 집으로요.”\n\n“대체 캐서릭 부인이 이 집에 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이오?”\n\n그가 그 질문을 던진 태도는 그것을 표현한 말씨보다도 더 무례했다. 나는 그의 예의 없음에 대한 내 심중을 말없이 그에게서 돌아서는 것으로 표시했다.\n\n내가 몸을 움직이는 바로 그때 백작의 달래는 손이 그의 어깨에 얹혔고, 백작의 감미로운 목소리가 그를 진정시키려고 끼어들었다.\n\n“여보시게 퍼시벌!—차분히—차분히!”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 더없이 성난 얼굴로 돌아보았다. 백작은 그저 미소를 지으며 그 진정제를 다시 한번 발라 주었다.\n\n“차분히, 나의 좋은 벗이여—차분히!”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 망설이다가 나를 몇 걸음 따라오더니, 놀랍게도 나에게 사과를 건넸다.\n\n“실례했습니다, 홀콤 양.” 그가 말했다. “요즘 몸이 좋지 않아서 아무래도 신경이 좀 날카로워진 모양입니다. 하지만 캐서릭 부인이 대체 여기서 무엇을 바랐는지는 알고 싶군요. 언제 왔습니까? 그 여자를 본 사람이 가정부뿐이었나요?”\n\n“가정부 한 사람뿐이었어요.” 내가 대답했다. “제가 아는 한은 그래요.”\n\n백작이 다시 끼어들었다.\n\n“그렇다면 가정부에게 물어보면 되지 않겠나?” 그가 말했다. “퍼시벌, 어찌하여 곧장 소식의 원천으로 가지 않는 건가?”\n\n“옳은 말이야!” 퍼시벌 경이 말했다. “물론 제일 먼저 물어볼 사람은 가정부지. 그걸 스스로 깨닫지 못하다니 나도 어지간히 어리석군.” 그 말과 함께 그는 곧바로 우리를 떠나 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n처음에는 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 백작의 참견은, 퍼시벌 경이 등을 돌리자마자 그 속셈을 스스로 드러냈다. 그는 캐서릭 부인에 대해, 그리고 그 여자가 블랙워터 파크를 찾아온 까닭에 대해 나에게 물어볼 것이 산더미였는데, 그런 질문들은 친구가 있는 자리에서는 도저히 꺼낼 수 없는 것들이었다. 나는 예의를 잃지 않는 선에서 되도록 짧게 대답했다. 포스코 백작과 나 사이에 속내를 주고받는 일이 조금이라도 생기지 않도록 이미 마음을 굳혀 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 로라가 자기도 모르게 그를 도와 내가 아는 것을 죄다 끌어내게 만들었다. 로라가 직접 이것저것 묻는 바람에, 나로서는 그 물음에 대답하거나 아니면 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 맡아 지키는 사람이라는 아주 달갑지 않고 아주 그릇된 배역을 떠맡거나, 둘 중 하나밖에 길이 없었던 것이다. 결국 십 분쯤 지났을 때 백작은 캐서릭 부인에 대해, 그리고 하트라이트가 그 딸 앤을 만난 때부터 오늘에 이르기까지 우리를 그토록 기이하게 그 여자와 얽히게 만든 사건들에 대해, 내가 아는 만큼을 알게 되었다.\n\n내가 알려 준 이야기가 그에게 미친 영향은 한 가지 점에서 제법 기묘했다.\n\n그는 퍼시벌 경과 그토록 가까운 사이이고, 대체로 퍼시벌 경의 사적인 일들과도 그토록 밀접하게 얽혀 있는 듯 보이건만, 앤 캐서릭의 진짜 내력에 대해서는 분명 나만큼이나 아무것도 모르고 있다. 이 가엾은 여자를 둘러싼 풀리지 않은 수수께끼는, 그 실마리를 퍼시벌 경이 이 세상에서 가장 절친한 벗에게마저 감추어 왔다는 확신이 내 안에서 굳어짐에 따라, 내 눈에는 이제 갑절로 수상쩍게 비친다. 내 입에서 떨어지는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않고 게걸스레 들이켜는 동안 백작의 눈빛과 태도에 어린 간절한 호기심은 도무지 잘못 볼 수 없는 것이었다. 호기심에도 여러 종류가 있음을 나는 안다. 그러나 아무것도 몰라 그저 놀랐을 때의 호기심만은 달리 해석할 여지가 없다. 내가 평생 그런 호기심을 본 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 백작의 얼굴에서였다.\n\n묻고 답하는 사이에 우리는 모두 조용히 수목원을 가로질러 돌아오고 있었다. 집에 이르자마자 그 앞에서 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 퍼시벌 경의 이륜마차였는데, 말이 이미 매여 있고 마부가 마구간 웃옷 차림으로 그 곁에 서서 기다리고 있었다. 이 뜻밖의 광경을 믿어도 좋다면, 가정부를 캐물은 일이 이미 중요한 결과를 낳은 셈이었다.\n\n“훌륭한 말이로군, 친구.” 백작이 더할 나위 없이 살갑고 허물없는 태도로 마부에게 말을 건넸다. “마차를 몰고 나갈 참인가?”\n\n“저는 안 갑니다, 나리.” 사내가 자기 마구간 웃옷을 내려다보며 대답했다. 이 외국 신사가 그 옷을 제복으로 여긴 것은 아닌지 미심쩍어하는 기색이 역력했다. “주인 나리께서 손수 모십니다.”\n\n“아하!” 백작이 말했다. “정말 그런가? 대신 몰아 줄 자네가 있는데도 굳이 수고를 자청하다니 놀랍군. 오늘 저 어여쁘고 윤기 흐르는 좋은 말을 아주 멀리까지 끌고 가서 지치게 할 셈인가?”\n\n“모르겠습니다, 나리.” 사내가 대답했다. “그리고 저 말은 암말입죠, 나리. 우리 마구간을 통틀어 기운이 제일 센 놈입니다. 이름은 브라운 몰리인데요, 나리, 쓰러질 때까지 달리는 놈이지요. 퍼시벌 경께서는 가까운 길에는 보통 요크의 아이작을 쓰십니다.”\n\n“그러면 먼 길에는 그 윤기 흐르고 기운찬 브라운 몰리를 쓴다는 말이지?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 나리.”\n\n“논리적 추론입니다, 홀콤 양.” 백작이 몸을 홱 돌려 나를 향하며 말을 이었다. “퍼시벌 경은 오늘 먼 길을 가는 것입니다.”\n\n나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 가정부를 통해 알게 된 것과 눈앞에 보이는 것으로부터 나에게는 나대로 끌어낼 추론이 있었고, 그것을 포스코 백작과 나눌 마음은 없었다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 컴벌랜드에 있을 때(나는 속으로 생각했다) 앤의 일로 토드 코너의 식구들을 캐물으려고 먼 길을 걸어갔었다. 이제 햄프셔에 와 있는 지금, 또다시 앤의 일로 웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인을 캐물으려고 먼 길을 마차로 달려가려는 것일까?\n\n우리는 모두 집 안으로 들어갔다. 현관 홀을 가로지르는데 퍼시벌 경이 서재에서 나와 우리를 맞았다. 그는 서두르는 기색에 얼굴이 창백하고 불안해 보였다. 그럼에도 우리에게 말을 걸 때만큼은 더없이 정중한 태도였다.\n\n“안됐지만 여러분 곁을 떠나야겠습니다.” 그가 입을 열었다. “먼 길이라—도저히 미룰 수 없는 일이 생겼어요. 내일 이른 시간에는 돌아오겠습니다. 다만 떠나기 전에, 오늘 아침에 말씀드린 그 사소한 사무 절차를 매듭짓고 싶군요. 로라, 서재로 들어와 주겠소? 잠깐이면 됩니다, 그저 형식일 뿐이니. 백작부인, 부인께도 폐를 좀 끼쳐도 될까요? 포스코, 자네와 백작부인이 서명의 증인이 되어 주었으면 하네. 그뿐일세. 어서들 들어와서 끝내 버립시다.”\n\n그는 그들이 다 들어갈 때까지 서재 문을 잡고 있다가, 뒤따라 들어가서는 조용히 문을 닫았다.\n\n나는 그 뒤로도 잠시 동안 홀에 혼자 선 채로 있었다. 가슴은 빠르게 뛰었고 마음에는 서글픈 불안이 밀려들었다. 이윽고 나는 계단 쪽으로 걸어가, 천천히 내 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n# IV\n\n6월 17일.—내 손이 막 방문 손잡이에 닿았을 때, 아래층에서 나를 부르는 퍼시벌 경의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n“죄송하지만 다시 아래층으로 내려와 주셔야겠습니다.” 그가 말했다. “제 탓이 아니라 포스코 탓입니다, 홀콤 양. 이 사람이 제 아내가 증인 가운데 하나가 되는 것을 두고 말도 안 되는 이의를 내놓는 바람에, 서재로 함께 와 주십사 청하지 않을 수 없게 되었어요.”\n\n나는 퍼시벌 경과 함께 곧바로 방으로 들어갔다. 로라는 필기용 탁자 옆에서 정원용 모자를 불안하게 손에 쥐고 이리저리 비틀며 기다리고 있었다. 포스코 백작부인은 그 곁 안락의자에 앉아 태연한 얼굴로 제 남편을 흠모하듯 바라보고 있었고, 백작은 서재 저쪽 끝에 혼자 서서 창가 화분의 시든 잎을 따내는 중이었다.\n\n내가 모습을 보이자마자 백작은 나를 맞으러 다가와 해명을 늘어놓았다.\n\n“천 번이고 용서를 구합니다, 홀콤 양.” 그가 말했다. “영국인들이 제 동포에게 어떤 평판을 붙여 주는지 아시지요? 선량한 존 불의 눈에 우리 이탈리아인은 하나같이 교활하고 의심 많은 족속입니다. 부디 저 또한 제 동족보다 나을 것 없는 인간으로 여겨 주십시오. 저는 교활한 이탈리아인이요, 의심 많은 이탈리아인입니다. 당신께서도 그렇게 생각하셨겠지요, 친애하는 홀콤 양, 아니십니까? 그런데 말입니다! 제가 증인으로 나서는 마당에 포스코 부인까지 글라이드 부인의 서명에 증인이 되는 것을 반대하는 것, 바로 그것이 제 교활함의 일부이자 제 의심의 일부랍니다.”\n\n“그가 반대할 근거라고는 그림자만큼도 없습니다.” 퍼시벌 경이 끼어들었다. “영국 법에서는 포스코 부인도 그 남편과 마찬가지로 서명의 증인이 될 수 있다고 이 사람에게 설명해 주었는데도 말입니다.”\n\n“인정하네.” 백작이 말을 이었다. “영국 법은 그렇다고 말하지만, 포스코의 양심은 아니라고 말하지.” 그는 통통한 손가락을 블라우스 가슴팍에 활짝 펴 얹고는, 마치 제 양심을 이 자리에 새로 더해진 빛나는 일원으로 우리 모두에게 소개하려는 듯 엄숙하게 절을 했다. “글라이드 부인이 서명하려는 이 문서가 대체 무엇인지,” 그가 말을 이었다, “저는 알지도 못하고 알고 싶지도 않습니다. 제가 드리는 말씀은 이것뿐입니다. 훗날 퍼시벌이나 그의 대리인들이 두 증인에게 확인을 구해야 할 사정이 생길 수도 있지요. 그런 경우라면 두 증인이 서로 완전히 독립된 두 개의 견해를 대표하는 편이 분명 바람직합니다. 그런데 제 아내가 저와 나란히 서명한다면 그럴 수가 없습니다. 우리 둘 사이에는 견해가 하나밖에 없고, 그 견해란 곧 제 것이니까요. 훗날 어느 날엔가 포스코 부인이 제 강압 아래 움직였을 뿐이며 사실상 증인이랄 것도 못 되었다는 말을 얼굴에 대고 듣고 싶지는 않습니다. 제 이름이 (남편의 가장 가까운 벗으로서) 오르고 홀콤 양 당신의 이름이 (아내의 가장 가까운 벗으로서) 오르도록 하자고 제안하는 것은 어디까지나 퍼시벌을 위해서입니다. 저를 예수회 수사라 여기셔도 좋습니다—지푸라기나 쪼개는 사람이라고—사소한 것과 변덕과 양심의 가책에 매달리는 사내라고—그래도 의심 많은 이탈리아인 기질과 편치 못한 이탈리아인 양심을 너그러이 헤아리시어 부디 제 뜻을 받아 주시기 바랍니다.” 그는 다시 절하고 몇 걸음 물러서더니, 소개할 때만큼이나 정중하게 제 양심을 우리 자리에서 물렸다.\n\n백작이 말한 그 양심의 가책이란 것은 어쩌면 명예롭고 지당한 것이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것을 표현하는 그의 태도에는 이 서명이라는 일에 관여하고 싶지 않은 내 마음을 한층 키우는 무언가가 있었다. 로라를 생각하는 마음보다 덜 중요한 어떤 이유로도 나는 증인이 되겠다고 승낙하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 근심에 찬 그녀의 얼굴을 한 번 보는 것만으로, 나는 그녀를 저버리느니 무엇이든 무릅쓰기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n“기꺼이 이 방에 남겠습니다.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 제 쪽에서 사소한 가책이나마 제기할 이유를 찾지 못한다면, 증인으로서 저를 믿으셔도 좋습니다.”\n\n퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 나를 날카롭게 쳐다보았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 포스코 부인이 의자에서 일어서며 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그녀는 남편과 눈이 마주쳤고, 방을 나가라는 지시를 받은 것이 분명했다.\n\n“나가실 것 없습니다.” 퍼시벌 경이 말했다.\n\n포스코 부인은 다시 한번 남편의 지시를 구했고, 다시 한번 그것을 받았으며, 우리끼리 일을 보도록 자리를 비켜 주는 편이 좋겠다고 말하고는 단호히 걸어 나갔다. 백작은 담배에 불을 붙이고 창가의 꽃들에게로 돌아가, 벌레를 죽이지나 않을까 심히 걱정하는 얼굴로 잎사귀에 대고 자잘한 연기를 뿜어 댔다.\n\n그러는 동안 퍼시벌 경은 책장 하나 아래의 벽장을 열고, 세로로 여러 번 접힌 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈다. 그는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 맨 마지막으로 접힌 자락만 펼친 뒤 나머지에는 손을 얹은 채로 두었다. 펼쳐진 마지막 자락에는 아무것도 쓰이지 않은 양피지 띠가 드러났고, 군데군데 작은 봉인 딱지가 붙어 있었다. 글씨가 쓰인 줄은 하나도 남김없이, 그가 여전히 접은 채 손으로 누르고 있는 부분에 감추어져 있었다. 로라와 나는 서로를 마주 보았다. 그녀의 얼굴은 창백했으나 망설임도 두려움도 비치지 않았다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 펜을 잉크에 적셔 아내에게 건넸다. “여기에 이름을 쓰시오.” 그가 자리를 가리키며 말했다. “홀콤 양, 당신과 포스코는 그다음에 저 두 봉인 딱지 맞은편에 서명하시면 됩니다. 이리 오게, 포스코! 서명의 증인이 되는 일은 창밖이나 멍하니 내다보며 꽃에다 연기를 뿜어서 되는 게 아닐세.”\n\n백작은 담배를 던져 버리고 두 손을 블라우스의 진홍색 허리띠에 아무렇게나 찔러 넣은 채, 시선은 퍼시벌 경의 얼굴에 붙박은 채로 탁자로 다가왔다. 남편 반대편에서 펜을 손에 든 로라 역시 그를 바라보았다. 그는 두 사람 사이에 서서 접힌 양피지를 탁자에 단단히 눌러 놓고, 맞은편에 앉은 나를 힐끗 건너다보았다. 그 얼굴에는 의심과 당혹이 어찌나 음험하게 뒤섞여 있던지, 제 집에 있는 신사라기보다 피고석에 선 죄수처럼 보였다.\n\n“거기에 서명하시오.” 그가 불쑥 로라 쪽으로 돌아서서 양피지의 그 자리를 다시 한번 가리키며 되풀이했다.\n\n“제가 서명해야 하는 것이 무엇인가요?” 그녀가 조용히 물었다.\n\n“설명할 시간이 없소.” 그가 대답했다. “마차가 문 앞에 와 있고 나는 곧장 떠나야 하오. 게다가 시간이 있다 한들 당신은 이해하지 못할 거요. 순전히 형식적인 문서요, 법률 용어니 뭐니 그런 것투성이란 말이오. 자! 어서! 이름을 쓰고 되도록 빨리 끝냅시다.”\n\n“그래도 이름을 쓰기 전에 제가 무엇에 서명하는지는 알아야 마땅하지 않을까요, 퍼시벌 경?”\n\n“쓸데없는 소리! 여자가 사업에 무슨 상관이오? 다시 말하지만 당신은 이해 못 하오.”\n\n“그래도 이해해 보려는 것쯤은 하게 해 주세요. 길모어 씨는 제가 해야 할 일이 있을 때면 언제나 먼저 설명해 주셨고, 저는 언제나 알아들었어요.”\n\n“그랬겠지. 그자는 당신의 고용인이었으니 설명할 의무가 있었소. 나는 당신 남편이니 그럴 의무가 없소. 대체 언제까지 나를 여기 붙들어 둘 셈이오? 다시 말하지만 무얼 읽을 시간 따위는 없소—마차가 문 앞에서 기다린단 말이오. 마지막으로 묻겠소, 서명하겠소, 하지 않겠소?”\n\n그녀는 여전히 펜을 손에 쥐고 있었지만, 그것으로 이름을 쓰려는 기색은 조금도 보이지 않았다.\n\n“제 서명이 저를 무언가에 묶어 놓는 것이라면,” 그녀가 말했다, “그 묶임이 무엇인지 알 권리쯤은 제게 있지 않겠어요?”\n\n그는 양피지를 들어 올려 성난 손길로 탁자를 내리쳤다.\n\n“터놓고 말하시오!” 그가 말했다. “당신은 늘 진실을 말하기로 이름난 사람이었지. 홀콤 양도 아랑곳 말고 포스코도 아랑곳 말고—단도직입으로 말해 보시오, 나를 믿지 못하겠다고.”\n\n백작이 허리띠에서 한 손을 빼내어 퍼시벌 경의 어깨에 얹었다. 퍼시벌 경은 짜증스럽게 그 손을 떨쳐 냈다. 백작은 조금도 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 다시 손을 얹었다.\n\n“그 못된 성미를 좀 다스리게, 퍼시벌.” 그가 말했다. “글라이드 부인의 말씀이 옳으이.”\n\n“옳다고!” 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다. “남편을 믿지 못하는 아내가 옳다는 건가!”\n\n“당신을 믿지 못한다고 저를 몰아세우시는 건 부당하고 잔인해요.” 로라가 말했다. “이 문서가 제게 무엇을 요구하는지 서명하기 전에 알고 싶어 하는 것이 부당한 일인지, 매리언에게 물어보세요.”\n\n“홀콤 양에게 호소하는 건 용납할 수 없소.” 퍼시벌 경이 쏘아붙였다. “홀콤 양은 이 일과 아무 상관이 없소.”\n\n나는 그때까지 한마디도 하지 않았고, 지금도 되도록 말하고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 나를 향해 돌린 로라의 얼굴에 서린 괴로움과 그 남편의 무례하고 부당한 처사 앞에서, 의견을 요청받은 이상 그녀를 위해 내 생각을 말하는 것 말고는 달리 길이 없었다.\n\n“실례합니다만, 퍼시벌 경.” 내가 말했다. “서명의 증인 가운데 한 사람으로서, 저는 이 일과 상관이 있다고 감히 생각합니다. 로라의 이의는 제가 보기에 지극히 온당하고, 저 자신만을 두고 말씀드리자면, 서명하기를 바라시는 그 문서가 무엇인지 로라가 먼저 이해하지 못하는 한 저는 그 서명의 증인이 되는 책임을 떠맡을 수 없습니다.”\n\n“참으로 태연한 선언이로군!” 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다. “다음번에 남의 집에 스스로 찾아오실 때에는, 홀콤 양, 상관도 없는 일에 그 집 아내 편을 들어 남편에게 맞서는 것으로 대접을 갚지는 마시기를 권합니다.”\n\n나는 그가 나를 때리기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어섰다. 내가 남자였다면 나는 제 집 문지방에서 그를 때려눕히고 그 집을 떠나, 세상 어떤 사정이 있어도 두 번 다시 그 문턱을 넘지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 나는 한낱 여자였고—그리고 나는 그의 아내를 그토록 깊이 사랑했다!\n\n고맙게도 그 변함없는 사랑이 나를 붙들어 주어, 나는 한마디도 하지 않고 다시 자리에 앉았다. 그녀는 내가 무엇을 견디었고 무엇을 억눌렀는지 알고 있었다. 그녀는 눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 탁자를 돌아 내게로 달려왔다. “오, 매리언!” 그녀가 나직이 속삭였다. “어머니가 살아 계셨더라도 이보다 더해 주시지는 못했을 거야!”\n\n“돌아와서 서명하시오!” 탁자 저편에서 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다.\n\n“서명할까?” 그녀가 내 귀에 대고 물었다. “언니가 하라고 하면 할게.”\n\n“안 돼.” 나는 대답했다. “옳음도 진실도 네 편이야—먼저 읽어 보지 않은 것에는 아무것도 서명하지 마.”\n\n“돌아와서 서명하란 말이오!” 그가 가장 크고 가장 성난 목소리로 되풀이했다.\n\n로라와 나를 가까이서 말없이 지켜보고 있던 백작이 두 번째로 끼어들었다.\n\n“퍼시벌!” 그가 말했다. “나는 지금 숙녀분들 앞에 있다는 것을 잊지 않고 있네. 부디 자네도 그것을 잊지 말아 주게.”\n\n퍼시벌 경이 분노로 말문이 막힌 채 그를 향해 홱 돌아섰다. 백작의 굳센 손이 천천히 그의 어깨를 조여 왔고, 백작의 흔들림 없는 목소리가 조용히 되풀이했다. “부디 자네도 그것을 잊지 말아 주게.”\n\n두 사람은 서로를 마주 보았다. 퍼시벌 경은 천천히 백작의 손 아래에서 어깨를 빼내고, 천천히 백작의 눈길에서 얼굴을 돌리고, 잠시 완강하게 탁자 위의 양피지를 내려다보다가 입을 열었다. 납득한 사람의 온당한 승복이라기보다는 길들여진 짐승의 뚱한 복종에 가까운 말투였다.\n\n“나는 누구를 모욕할 생각은 없소.” 그가 말했다. “하지만 내 아내의 고집은 성인의 인내심이라도 시험할 만하오. 이건 그저 형식적인 문서라고 이미 말했는데 더 무엇을 바란단 말이오? 뭐라고들 하시든 좋소만, 남편에게 대드는 것이 아내의 도리일 수는 없소. 글라이드 부인, 다시 한번 그리고 이번이 마지막이오—서명하겠소, 하지 않겠소?”\n\n로라는 그가 있는 쪽 탁자로 돌아가 다시 펜을 들었다.\n\n“기꺼이 서명하겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “저를 책임질 줄 아는 사람으로 대해 주시기만 한다면요. 다른 누구에게도 해가 가지 않고 나쁜 결과로 이어지지만 않는다면, 제게 어떤 희생이 요구되든 저는 개의치 않아요—”\n\n“누가 당신에게 희생을 요구한다고 했소?” 그가 아까의 격정을 반쯤 억누른 채 말을 잘랐다.\n\n“제 말은 다만,” 그녀가 이어 말했다, “떳떳하게 양보할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇도 거절하지 않겠다는 뜻이었어요. 제가 무엇인지도 모르는 약정에 이름을 쓰는 일을 두고 꺼림칙해한다면, 어째서 그것을 그토록 심하게 나무라시나요? 포스코 백작의 가책은 그토록 너그럽게 받아 주시면서 제 가책은 그렇게 대하시는 건, 제가 보기에 좀 가혹한 것 같아요.”\n\n에두른 것이기는 했으나 남편에 대한 백작의 남다른 영향력을 건드린 이 불행하면서도 더없이 자연스러운 언급은, 사그라들던 퍼시벌 경의 성미에 순식간에 다시 불을 붙였다.\n\n“가책이라!” 그가 되풀이했다. “당신의 가책이라니! 이제 와서 꺼림칙하다는 소리를 하기에는 좀 늦었소. 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 삼아 나와 결혼했을 때, 그런 종류의 나약함 따위는 이미 다 넘어선 줄 알았는데.”\n\n그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지는 순간, 로라는 펜을 내던지고—내가 그녀를 알아 온 모든 세월 동안 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정으로 그를 바라보더니—죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 그에게 등을 돌렸다.\n\n이토록 노골적이고 이토록 신랄한 경멸의 강렬한 표현은 도무지 그녀답지 않았고 그녀의 성품과 너무나 동떨어진 것이어서, 우리 모두를 침묵하게 만들었다. 남편이 방금 그녀에게 던진 말의 그 표면적인 야비함 아래에는 분명 무언가 감추어진 것이 있었다. 그 밑에는 내가 전혀 알지 못하는 어떤 모욕이 도사리고 있었으되, 그것이 남긴 능욕의 자국은 낯선 사람이라도 알아볼 만큼 그녀의 얼굴에 뚜렷했다.\n\n낯선 사람이 아니었던 백작은 나만큼이나 똑똑히 그것을 보았다. 로라에게 가려고 내가 의자에서 일어섰을 때, 나는 그가 퍼시벌 경에게 숨죽여 속삭이는 소리를 들었다. “이 바보 같으니!”\n\n내가 다가가자 로라는 나보다 앞서 문 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 바로 그때 그녀의 남편이 다시 한번 그녀에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n“그러니까 내게 서명해 줄 수 없다, 이 말이오?” 그가 물었다. 제 입에서 나온 방자한 말이 스스로에게 심각한 손해를 입혔음을 자각한 사람의 달라진 어조였다.\n\n“방금 제게 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니,” 그녀가 단호히 대답했다, “저 양피지의 첫 글자부터 마지막 글자까지 한 줄도 빠짐없이 읽기 전에는 서명하지 않겠어요. 가요, 매리언, 우리는 여기 충분히 오래 있었어요.”\n\n“잠깐만요!” 퍼시벌 경이 다시 입을 열기 전에 백작이 끼어들었다—“잠깐만요, 글라이드 부인, 간청드립니다!”\n\n로라는 그를 본체만체하고 방을 나가려 했으나, 내가 그녀를 붙들었다.\n\n“백작을 적으로 만들면 안 돼!” 나는 속삭였다. “무슨 일이 있어도 백작을 적으로 만들지는 마!”\n\n그녀는 내 말에 따랐다. 나는 다시 문을 닫았고, 우리는 그 곁에 서서 기다렸다. 퍼시벌 경은 탁자에 앉아 접힌 양피지 위에 팔꿈치를 얹고 움켜쥔 주먹에 머리를 기댔다. 백작은 우리 사이에 서 있었다—다른 모든 것의 주인이듯, 우리가 놓인 이 끔찍한 처지의 주인으로서.\n\n“글라이드 부인.” 그가 말했다. 우리 자신이 아니라 우리의 가련한 처지를 향한 듯한 부드러움이 담긴 목소리였다. “제가 감히 한 가지 제안을 드리더라도 부디 용서해 주시고, 이 집 안주인께 품은 깊은 존경과 우정 어린 마음에서 드리는 말씀임을 믿어 주십시오.” 그는 홱 퍼시벌 경 쪽으로 돌아섰다. “묻겠네.” 그가 말했다. “자네 팔꿈치 밑에 있는 저것에 반드시 오늘 서명을 받아야만 하나?”\n\n“내 계획과 바람에는 그래야 하네.” 상대가 뚱하게 대꾸했다. “하지만 자네도 보았듯이 그런 사정은 글라이드 부인에게 아무 힘도 없더군.”\n\n“내 단순한 물음에 단순하게 답하게. 서명하는 일을 내일로 미룰 수 있나, 없나?”\n\n“그러라면야 그럴 수 있지.”\n\n“그렇다면 여기서 무엇하러 시간을 허비하고 있나? 서명은 내일까지 기다리게 하게—자네가 돌아올 때까지 기다리게 해.”\n\n퍼시벌 경이 이맛살을 찌푸리고 욕설을 뱉으며 고개를 들었다.\n\n“자네가 내게 쓰는 그 말투는 마음에 들지 않아.” 그가 말했다. “어떤 사내에게서도 참아 줄 수 없는 말투야.”\n\n“나는 자네를 위해 충고하는 걸세.” 백작이 조용한 경멸의 미소를 띠고 대꾸했다. “시간을 좀 두게—글라이드 부인께도 시간을 드리고. 마차가 문 앞에서 기다리는 걸 잊었나? 내 말투가 놀랍다고—허? 그렇겠지—이건 제 성미를 다스릴 줄 아는 사내의 말투일세. 내가 그동안 자네에게 좋은 충고를 몇 첩이나 처방해 주었던가? 셀 수 없을 만큼일세. 내가 한 번이라도 틀린 적이 있던가? 한 번이라도 있거든 대 보게. 가게! 다녀오게. 서명 문제는 내일까지 기다려도 되네. 기다리게 두고—돌아와서 다시 꺼내게.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 망설이며 시계를 보았다. 그날 떠나야 할 그 은밀한 여정에 대한 조바심이 백작의 말에 되살아나, 로라의 서명을 받아 내려는 조바심과 그의 마음을 두고 다투고 있는 것이 분명했다. 그는 잠시 궁리하다가 의자에서 일어섰다.\n\n“내게 답할 시간이 없을 때에는 말로 나를 눌러 이기기가 쉽겠지.” 그가 말했다. “자네 충고를 따르겠네, 포스코—그게 필요해서도 아니고 그걸 믿어서도 아니라, 여기 더 붙들려 있을 수 없어서야.” 그는 말을 멈추고 침울한 눈길로 아내를 돌아보았다. “내일 돌아왔을 때 서명해 주지 않는다면—!” 그 뒤의 말은 그가 다시 책장 벽장을 열고 양피지를 도로 넣어 잠그는 소리에 묻혀 버렸다. 그는 탁자에서 모자와 장갑을 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 향했다. 로라와 나는 그가 지나가도록 물러섰다. “내일을 잊지 마시오!” 그가 아내에게 말하고는 나가 버렸다.\n\n우리는 그가 홀을 가로질러 마차를 몰고 떠날 시간을 주려고 기다렸다. 우리가 문 가까이 서 있는 동안 백작이 다가왔다.\n\n“홀콤 양, 방금 퍼시벌의 가장 못난 모습을 보셨습니다.” 그가 말했다. “오랜 벗으로서 저는 그가 딱하고 또 부끄럽습니다. 오랜 벗으로서 약속드리지요, 내일은 오늘처럼 그런 수치스러운 모습으로 폭발하는 일은 없을 것입니다.”\n\n그가 말하는 동안 로라는 내 팔을 잡았고, 말이 끝나자 의미심장하게 그 팔을 꼭 눌렀다. 제 집에서, 남편의 잘못을 대신 변명하는 역할을 남편의 남자 친구가 태연히 떠맡는 것을 곁에 서서 지켜보는 일은 어떤 여자에게든 견디기 힘든 시련이었을 것이다—그녀에게도 그것은 시련이었다. 나는 백작에게 정중히 감사를 표하고 그녀를 데리고 나왔다. 그렇다! 나는 그에게 감사했다. 내가 블랙워터 파크에 계속 머무르도록 하는 것이 그의 이해관계이거나 그의 변덕이라는 것을, 그리고 나에 대한 퍼시벌 경의 처사를 겪은 뒤로는 백작의 영향력이 받쳐 주지 않고서는 이곳에 남기를 바랄 수도 없다는 것을, 말로 다 할 수 없는 무력감과 굴욕감과 함께 이미 느끼고 있었기 때문이다. 세상 무엇보다도 내가 가장 두려워하는 그 영향력이, 실은 로라가 가장 큰 곤경에 처한 이 시각에 나를 그녀 곁에 붙들어 두는 유일한 끈이었던 것이다!\n\n우리가 홀로 나왔을 때 마찻바퀴가 진입로 자갈을 으스러뜨리는 소리가 들렸다. 퍼시벌 경이 길을 떠난 것이다.\n\n“저이가 어디로 가는 걸까, 매리언?” 로라가 속삭였다. “저이가 새로 하는 일마다 앞날이 무서워져. 짐작 가는 게 있어?”\n\n그날 아침 그녀가 겪은 일을 생각하니, 나는 내 짐작을 말해 주고 싶지 않았다.\n\n“내가 저이의 비밀을 어떻게 알겠니?” 나는 얼버무렸다.\n\n“가정부는 알고 있지 않을까?” 그녀가 물고 늘어졌다.\n\n“그럴 리 없어.” 나는 대답했다. “그 사람도 우리만큼이나 아무것도 모를 거야.”\n\n로라는 미심쩍은 얼굴로 고개를 저었다.\n\n“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에서 목격되었다는 소문이 있다고 가정부에게서 듣지 않았어? 저이가 그 사람을 찾으러 간 건 아닐까?”\n\n“나는 그 일을 아예 생각하지 않는 편으로 마음을 가라앉히고 싶구나, 로라. 그런 일이 있었으니 너도 나를 따라 하는 게 좋겠다. 내 방으로 가서 좀 쉬면서 마음을 진정시키자꾸나.”\n\n우리는 창가에 나란히 앉아, 향기로운 여름 공기가 얼굴 위로 스치도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n“매리언, 나 언니 얼굴을 못 보겠어.” 그녀가 말했다. “나 때문에 아래층에서 그런 일을 참아 냈으니 말이야. 오, 내 사랑, 그 생각만 하면 가슴이 미어질 것 같아! 하지만 갚을게—꼭 갚을게, 정말이야!”\n\n“쉿! 쉿!” 나는 대답했다. “그런 말 하지 마. 내 자존심이 조금 상한 것쯤이 네 행복을 그토록 끔찍하게 희생시키는 일에 비하면 무엇이겠니?”\n\n“저이가 내게 한 말 들었지?” 그녀가 빠르고 격하게 말을 이었다. “언니는 그 말을 들었지만—그게 무슨 뜻인지는 몰라—내가 왜 펜을 내던지고 등을 돌렸는지 몰라.” 그녀는 갑자기 동요하여 일어서더니 방 안을 서성였다. “언니가 괴로워할까 봐, 우리 새 삶의 첫머리에 언니를 불행하게 만들까 봐, 나는 많은 것을 언니에게 숨겨 왔어, 매리언. 저이가 나를 어떻게 대해 왔는지 언니는 몰라. 그렇지만 알아야 마땅하지, 오늘 저이가 나를 어떻게 대하는지 보았으니까. 내가 감히 꺼림칙해한다고 저이가 비웃는 걸 들었잖아—어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 삼아 자기와 결혼했다고 하는 말을 들었잖아.” 그녀는 다시 앉았다. 얼굴은 붉게 달아올랐고 두 손은 무릎 위에서 뒤틀리며 얽히고 있었다. “지금은 그 이야기를 못 하겠어.” 그녀가 말했다. “지금 말하면 울음이 터져 버릴 거야—나중에, 매리언, 내가 좀 더 마음을 다잡았을 때 할게. 머리가 아파, 언니—아파, 아파, 아파. 언니 각성제 병 어디 있어? 언니 이야기나 하게 해 줘. 언니를 위해서라도 서명해 줄걸 그랬어. 내일은 해 줄까? 언니를 곤란하게 하느니 차라리 내가 나를 곤란하게 하는 편이 나아. 언니가 저이에게 맞서 내 편을 들었으니, 내가 또 거절하면 저이는 모든 탓을 언니에게 돌릴 거야. 우리 어떡하지? 오, 우리를 도와주고 조언해 줄 친구가 있었으면!—정말로 믿을 수 있는 친구가!”\n\n그녀는 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬었다. 나는 그녀의 얼굴에서 그녀가 하트라이트를 생각하고 있음을 보았다—그녀의 마지막 말이 나까지 그를 생각하게 만들었기에 더욱 또렷이 보였다. 결혼한 지 겨우 여섯 달 만에 우리는 그가 작별의 말로 내밀었던 그 성실한 도움을 필요로 하고 있었다. 언젠가 그것을 필요로 하게 되리라고, 그때의 나는 얼마나 생각지 못했던가!\n\n“우리 힘으로 할 수 있는 것을 해야지.” 내가 말했다. “차분하게 의논해 보자, 로라—최선을 정하기 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 해 보자.”\n\n그녀가 알고 있는 남편의 재정적 곤경과 내가 엿들은 그와 변호사의 대화를 맞추어 보니, 우리는 서재의 그 양피지가 돈을 빌리기 위해 작성된 것이며 퍼시벌 경의 목적을 이루는 데에 로라의 서명이 반드시 필요하다는 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었다.\n\n두 번째 물음, 곧 그 돈을 얻어 내는 법적 계약이 어떤 성질의 것이며 로라가 내용을 모른 채 서명할 경우 스스로 어느 정도의 개인적 책임을 지게 되는가 하는 문제는, 우리 둘 중 누구의 지식과 경험으로도 도저히 미칠 수 없는 고려를 필요로 했다. 나 자신의 확신으로 말하자면, 양피지의 감추어진 내용은 더없이 비열하고 더없이 기만적인 종류의 거래를 숨기고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n내가 이런 결론에 이른 것은 퍼시벌 경이 그 문서를 보여 주지도 설명하지도 않으려 했기 때문이 아니다. 그런 거절이야 그의 고집스러운 기질과 군림하려 드는 성미만으로도 얼마든지 나올 수 있는 것이었으니까. 그의 정직함을 의심하게 된 유일한 까닭은, 블랙워터 파크에서 그의 말투와 태도에 나타난 변화를 내가 목격했다는 데 있었다. 그 변화는 리머리지 하우스에서 시험받던 기간 내내 그가 한 편의 연기를 하고 있었음을 내게 확신시켰다. 그 공들인 조심성, 길모어 씨의 구식 관념과 그토록 기분 좋게 어울리던 그 예법 바른 정중함, 로라에게 보이던 겸손함, 나에게 보이던 솔직함, 페얼리 씨에게 보이던 절제—이 모두가 비열하고 교활하고 야만적인 사내의 술책이었다. 그는 몸에 밴 이중성이 목적을 이루자마자 위장을 벗어 던졌고, 바로 그날 서재에서 제 본모습을 드러내 보인 것이다. 이 깨달음이 로라 때문에 내게 안긴 슬픔에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 내 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없기 때문이다. 그것을 굳이 언급하는 것은 다만, 그 슬픔이 결과야 어찌 되든 내용을 먼저 알기 전에는 그녀가 양피지에 서명하는 것을 반대하기로 나를 결심하게 했기 때문이다.\n\n이런 사정에서, 내일이 왔을 때 우리에게 남은 단 하나의 방책은 서명을 거부할 근거를 미리 마련해 두는 것이었다. 퍼시벌 경의 결심을 흔들 만큼, 그리고 우리 두 여자도 사업의 법과 의무를 그 못지않게 알고 있다고 그가 의심하게 만들 만큼, 상업적으로든 법적으로든 충분히 단단한 근거 위에 선 거부 말이다.\n\n얼마간 궁리한 끝에, 나는 이 가련한 처지에서 우리를 신중하게 도와주리라 믿을 만한, 손닿는 곳의 유일한 정직한 사람에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었다. 그 사람은 길모어 씨의 동업자인 카일 씨였다. 우리의 옛 벗이 건강 때문에 사무를 접고 런던을 떠나야 했던 지금은 그가 업무를 맡고 있었다. 나는 그 동업자의 성실함과 신중함, 그리고 로라의 모든 사정에 대한 정확한 지식을 전적으로 신뢰해도 좋다는 길모어 씨 본인의 보증이 내게 있음을 로라에게 설명했고, 그녀의 온전한 동의를 얻어 곧바로 앉아 편지를 썼다. 나는 우리의 처지를 있는 그대로 카일 씨에게 알리는 것으로 시작해서, 오해나 착오의 위험 없이 그가 알아들을 수 있도록 평이하고 단도직입적인 말로 그의 조언을 청했다. 편지는 내가 할 수 있는 한 짧게 썼고, 부디 쓸데없는 사과와 쓸데없는 세부로 무겁지 않기를 바랐다.\n\n봉투에 주소를 막 쓰려는 참에, 글을 쓰느라 애쓰고 골몰하는 사이 내 머릿속에서 완전히 빠져나가 있던 장애물 하나를 로라가 발견했다.\n\n“답장을 어떻게 제때 받지?” 그녀가 물었다. “언니 편지는 내일 아침에야 런던에 닿을 텐데, 우편으로는 그 답장이 그다음 날 아침에나 여기 도착할 거야.”\n\n이 어려움을 넘길 유일한 방법은 변호사 사무실에서 특별 심부름꾼 편으로 답장을 가져오게 하는 것이었다. 나는 그런 취지의 추신을 덧붙여, 심부름꾼을 답장과 함께 오전 열한 시 기차로 보내 달라고 청했다. 그러면 한 시 이십 분에 우리 역에 닿을 것이고, 늦어도 두 시까지는 블랙워터 파크에 이를 수 있을 터였다. 그는 나를 찾도록, 다른 누구의 물음에도 답하지 않도록, 그리고 편지를 내 손 외에는 어떤 손에도 건네지 않도록 지시받아야 했다.\n\n“혹시 퍼시벌 경이 내일 두 시 전에 돌아온다면,” 나는 로라에게 말했다, “네가 취할 가장 현명한 방책은 아침 내내 책이나 바느질거리를 들고 뜰에 나가 있다가, 심부름꾼이 편지를 가지고 도착할 만한 시간이 지나기 전에는 집에 나타나지 않는 거야. 나는 무슨 사고나 착오가 없도록 아침 내내 여기서 그를 기다릴게. 이렇게 해 두면 불시에 당하는 일은 피할 수 있으리라 믿는다. 이제 응접실로 내려가자. 우리 둘이 너무 오래 틀어박혀 있으면 의심을 살지도 모르니까.”\n\n“의심?” 그녀가 되뇌었다. “퍼시벌 경이 집을 떠났는데 누구의 의심을 산다는 거야? 포스코 백작 말이야?”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지, 로라.”\n\n“언니도 나만큼이나 그 사람이 싫어지기 시작한 거구나, 매리언.”\n\n“아니, 싫은 건 아니야. 싫음에는 늘 얼마간 경멸이 섞여 있는 법인데—나는 백작에게서 경멸할 만한 것을 하나도 찾지 못하겠어.”\n\n“그 사람이 무섭지는 않지, 그렇지?”\n\n“어쩌면 무서운지도 모르지—조금은.”\n\n“오늘 우리 편을 들어 나서 준 사람인데 무섭다니!”\n\n“그래. 나는 퍼시벌 경의 난폭함보다 그 사람이 끼어드는 것이 더 무서워. 서재에서 내가 한 말을 기억해 두렴. 무슨 일이 있어도, 로라, 백작을 적으로 만들지는 마!”\n\n우리는 아래층으로 내려갔다. 로라는 응접실로 들어갔고, 나는 편지를 손에 든 채 홀을 가로질러, 맞은편 벽에 걸린 우편 자루에 그것을 넣으러 갔다.\n\n현관문이 열려 있었고, 그 앞을 지나가면서 나는 포스코 백작과 그의 아내가 바깥 계단에 서서 이야기를 나누며 얼굴을 이쪽으로 돌리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n백작부인이 다소 급하게 홀로 들어와, 오 분쯤 따로 이야기를 나눌 여유가 있느냐고 물었다. 그런 사람에게서 그런 청을 받으니 조금 놀랐지만, 나는 편지를 자루에 넣고 얼마든지 좋다고 대답했다. 그녀는 평소와 달리 다정하고 스스럼없이 내 팔짱을 끼더니, 빈방으로 데려가는 대신 커다란 연못을 둘러싼 잔디밭 띠로 나를 이끌고 나갔다.\n\n계단에서 백작 곁을 지날 때 그는 절을 하고 미소를 지었다. 그러고는 곧장 집 안으로 들어가면서 현관문을 밀어 닫았으나, 완전히 닫지는 않았다.\n\n백작부인은 나를 데리고 연못 주위를 천천히 돌았다. 나는 무슨 대단한 속내 이야기라도 맡게 되려니 했는데, 포스코 부인이 내 귀에만 들려주려던 이야기란 것이 서재에서 있었던 일에 대한 정중한 위로의 말에 지나지 않는다는 것을 알고 놀랐다. 남편이 그 자리에서 오간 모든 일과 퍼시벌 경이 내게 얼마나 무례하게 말했는지를 그녀에게 이야기했다는 것이다. 그 소식이 나와 로라를 생각할 때 어찌나 충격적이고 괴로웠던지, 만일 그런 일이 또 벌어진다면 이 집을 떠남으로써 퍼시벌 경의 터무니없는 처사에 대한 제 뜻을 분명히 밝히기로 마음먹었다고 했다. 백작도 그 생각에 찬성했으니, 이제 나도 찬성해 주기를 바란다는 것이었다.\n\n포스코 부인처럼 유난히 말수 없는 여자에게서 나온 행동치고는 참으로 이상한 일이라고 나는 생각했다. 더구나 바로 그날 아침 보트 창고에서 나눈 대화에서 우리 사이에 날 선 말들이 오간 뒤였으니 더욱 그랬다. 그러나 나보다 손위인 사람이 정중하고 다정하게 다가올 때 정중하고 다정하게 응대하는 것은 분명한 내 도리였다. 그래서 나는 백작부인에게 그녀와 같은 어조로 대답했고, 이제 양쪽 다 할 말은 다 했다고 여겨 집으로 돌아가려 했다.\n\n그러나 포스코 부인은 나를 놓아줄 생각이 없어 보였고, 나로서는 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 이야기를 계속할 작정인 듯했다. 여태껏 여자들 가운데 가장 과묵했던 그녀가 이제는 결혼 생활에 대해, 퍼시벌 경과 로라에 대해, 제 자신의 행복에 대해, 유산 문제로 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 자기에게 했던 처사에 대해, 그 밖에 대여섯 가지 화제에 대해 막힘없는 상투어를 늘어놓으며 나를 들볶았다. 그렇게 삼십 분이 넘도록 나를 연못 주위로 빙빙 돌게 붙들어 두어 나를 완전히 지치게 만들었다. 그녀가 그것을 알아차렸는지 어떤지는 알 수 없으나, 그녀는 시작할 때만큼이나 불쑥 말을 멈추더니—현관문 쪽을 바라보고는 순식간에 그 얼음 같은 태도로 돌아가, 내가 그녀에게서 풀려날 구실을 생각해 내기도 전에 제 쪽에서 먼저 내 팔을 놓아 버렸다.\n\n문을 밀어 열고 홀에 들어서는 순간, 나는 또다시 백작과 정면으로 마주쳤다. 그는 마침 우편 자루에 편지 한 통을 넣고 있는 참이었다.\n\n편지를 떨어뜨려 넣고 자루를 닫은 뒤, 그는 내게 포스코 부인을 어디에 두고 왔느냐고 물었다. 내가 말해 주자 그는 곧장 현관문으로 나가 아내에게 갔다. 내게 말할 때의 그의 태도가 어찌나 유별나게 조용하고 가라앉아 있던지, 나는 몸이라도 편찮은 걸까 아니면 기분이 상한 걸까 궁금해하며 돌아서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다.\n\n어째서 내가 그다음으로 한 일이 곧장 우편 가방으로 올라가 내 편지를 도로 꺼내어, 막연한 불신에 사로잡힌 채 그것을 다시 들여다보는 것이었는지, 그리고 어째서 두 번째로 들여다보자마자 더 안전하게 해 두려고 봉투를 봉인해야겠다는 생각이 즉시 떠올랐는지 — 그것은 내가 헤아리기에는 너무 깊거나 너무 얕은 수수께끼다. 누구나 알다시피 여자란 스스로에게조차 설명하지 못하는 충동에 따라 곧잘 행동하는 법이고, 나로서는 그런 충동 가운데 하나가 이번 일에서 보인 내 알 수 없는 처신의 숨은 원인이었으리라 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이다.\n\n나를 움직인 것이 무엇이었든, 내 방에서 편지를 봉인하려고 준비하는 순간 나는 그 충동에 따르기를 잘했다고 자축할 만한 이유를 발견했다. 나는 처음에 여느 때처럼 접착면에 침을 묻혀 아래 종이에 눌러 붙이는 식으로 봉투를 닫아 두었는데, 꼬박 사십오 분이 지난 지금 손가락으로 건드려 보니 봉투는 붙어 있지도, 찢어지지도 않은 채 그 자리에서 대뜸 열려 버렸다. 내가 제대로 붙여 두지 않았던 걸까? 아니면 접착풀에 무슨 결함이라도 있었던 걸까?\n\n아니면, 혹시—— 아니! 그 세 번째 추측이 내 마음속에서 꿈틀거리는 것을 느끼는 것만으로도 충분히 넌더리가 난다. 그것이 흰 종이 위에 검은 글씨로 또렷이 적혀 나를 마주 보고 있는 꼴은 차라리 보고 싶지 않다.\n\n내일이 거의 두렵다 — 내 분별과 자제에 너무나 많은 것이 달려 있으니. 어쨌든 결코 잊지 않으리라 자신하는 두 가지 조심이 있다. 나는 백작과 다정한 겉모습을 유지하도록 조심해야 하고, 사무소에서 온 심부름꾼이 내 편지에 대한 답을 가지고 이곳에 올 때 단단히 경계해야 한다.\n\n# V\n\n6월 17일.—만찬 시간이 되어 우리가 다시 모였을 때, 포스코 백작은 여느 때처럼 더없이 좋은 기분이었다. 그는 그날 오후 서재에서 있었던 일에 대한 기억을 우리 머릿속에서 말끔히 지워 버리기로 작정한 사람처럼, 우리의 흥미를 끌고 우리를 즐겁게 하려고 애를 썼다. 여행하며 겪은 모험담의 생생한 묘사, 외국에서 만난 저명한 인물들에 관한 재미있는 일화, 여러 나라의 사회 풍습에 대한 기묘한 비교—유럽 전역의 남녀를 가리지 않고 끌어온 실례로 뒷받침된—그리고 이류 이탈리아 소도시의 유행을 주름잡으며 이류 이탈리아 신문에 프랑스풍의 터무니없는 연애소설을 써 대던 젊은 시절의 순진한 어리석음에 대한 익살스러운 고백까지, 그 모든 것이 그의 입술에서 어찌나 쉽고도 흥겹게 줄줄이 흘러나오던지, 또 그 모든 것이 저마다 다른 우리의 호기심과 관심사를 어찌나 곧바로 그리고 어찌나 섬세하게 겨냥하던지, 로라와 나는 포스코 백작부인 못지않은 주의를 기울여—모순처럼 들릴지 모르나 못지않은 감탄까지 품고서—그의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 여자는 남자의 사랑도, 남자의 명성도, 남자의 용모도, 남자의 돈도 뿌리칠 수 있지만, 자기에게 말을 거는 법을 아는 남자의 혀만은 뿌리치지 못한다.\n\n만찬이 끝난 뒤, 그가 우리에게 남긴 호의적인 인상이 아직 마음속에 생생할 때, 백작은 겸손하게 물러나 서재로 독서를 하러 갔다.\n\n로라는 긴 저녁의 끝자락을 즐기자며 정원을 거닐자고 했다. 예의상 포스코 백작부인에게도 같이 가자고 청하지 않을 수 없었으나, 이번에는 그녀가 미리 분부를 받아 둔 모양인지 부디 양해해 달라고 했다. “백작께서 아마 담배를 새로 채워 달라고 하실 거예요.” 그녀가 변명 삼아 말했다. “그리고 그이 마음에 들게 마는 건 저 말고는 아무도 못 하지요.” 그 말을 하는 동안 그녀의 차가운 푸른 눈이 거의 온기를 띠었다—자기 주인이자 남편이 담배 연기로 마음을 가라앉히는 데에 쓰이는 시중꾼 노릇이 실로 자랑스럽다는 얼굴이었다!\n\n로라와 나는 단둘이 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n안개가 끼고 후텁지근한 저녁이었다. 공기 속에는 무언가 시들어 가는 기운이 감돌았다. 정원의 꽃들은 고개를 떨구었고, 땅은 이슬 한 방울 없이 바싹 말라 있었다. 고요한 나무들 너머로 보이는 서쪽 하늘은 옅은 노란빛이었고, 해는 아지랑이 속에서 희미하게 저물고 있었다. 비가 머지않아 올 듯했다—아마도 밤이 내리면서 함께 쏟아질 터였다.\n\n“어느 쪽으로 갈까?” 내가 물었다\n\n“호수 쪽으로 가요, 매리언, 언니만 괜찮다면요.” 로라가 대답했다.\n\n“로라, 넌 어쩐지 그 음침한 호수를 유난히 좋아하는구나.”\n\n“아니에요, 호수가 아니라 그 언저리 풍경이요. 모래밭과 히스와 전나무들만이 이 넓은 곳에서 리머리지를 떠올리게 하는 유일한 것들이거든요. 하지만 언니가 다른 쪽이 좋으시면 그리로 걸어요.”\n\n“나는 블랙워터 파크에 딱히 좋아하는 산책길이 없단다, 얘야. 어디나 매한가지야. 호수로 가자꾸나—여기보다는 트인 곳이 좀 시원할지도 모르지.”\n\n우리는 말없이 그늘진 수림을 지나 걸었다. 저녁 공기의 후텁지근함이 우리 둘을 짓눌렀고, 보트 창고에 이르자 우리는 반갑게 안으로 들어가 앉아 쉬었다.\n\n흰 안개가 호수 위에 낮게 깔려 있었다. 건너편 기슭의 짙은 갈색 나무 띠가 그 위로 솟아, 마치 하늘에 떠 있는 난쟁이 숲처럼 보였다. 우리가 앉은 자리에서 비스듬히 내려가는 모래땅은 안개의 바깥 겹 속으로 알 수 없이 사라져 버렸다. 정적은 소름 끼쳤다. 잎사귀 스치는 소리 하나 없고—숲에서 새 우는 소리도 없고—가려진 호수의 웅덩이에서 물새가 우는 소리도 없었다. 오늘 밤은 개구리 울음소리마저 그쳐 있었다.\n\n“정말 황량하고 음울하네요.” 로라가 말했다. “하지만 여기서라면 다른 어디보다 단둘이 있을 수 있어요.”\n\n로라는 조용히 말하면서, 흔들림 없는 사려 깊은 눈으로 모래와 안개의 황무지를 바라보았다. 그 애의 마음이 너무나 꽉 차 있어서, 이미 내 마음에 들러붙어 버린 바깥 풍경의 스산한 인상 따위는 느끼지 못한다는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다.\n\n“매리언, 언니가 혼자 짐작하도록 더 내버려 두지 않고 제 결혼 생활의 진실을 말씀드리겠다고 약속했지요.” 로라가 말문을 열었다. “그 비밀은 제가 언니한테 숨긴 첫 번째 비밀이었어요. 그리고 마지막이 되게 하겠다고 마음먹었고요. 아시다시피 제가 입을 다물었던 건 언니를 위해서였어요—어쩌면 저 자신을 위해서이기도 했겠지요. 자기 온 생을 바친 남자가 하필이면 그 선물을 세상에서 가장 하찮게 여기는 남자라고 고백하는 건 여자에게 몹시 힘든 일이에요. 언니가 결혼한 몸이었다면—더구나 행복하게 결혼한 몸이었다면—아무리 다정하고 진실한 사람이라도 결혼하지 않은 여자로서는 결코 느낄 수 없는 마음으로 저를 헤아려 주셨을 거예요.”\n\n내가 무슨 대답을 할 수 있었을까? 나는 그저 그 애의 손을 잡고, 눈으로 담아낼 수 있는 한 온 마음을 다해 그 애를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“얼마나 여러 번,” 로라가 말을 이었다. “언니가 스스로 ‘가난’이라 부르며 웃어넘기는 걸 들었는지 몰라요. 얼마나 여러 번 제 재산을 두고 축하한다며 짐짓 우스개 연설을 늘어놓으셨는지요! 아, 매리언, 다시는 웃지 마세요. 언니의 가난을 하느님께 감사하세요—그 덕분에 언니는 자기 자신의 주인이 되었고, 제게 떨어진 운명을 면하셨으니까요.”\n\n젊은 아내의 입에서 나온 서글픈 서두였다!—조용하고 꾸밈없는 진실이 담겨 있어 더욱 서글펐다. 우리 모두가 블랙워터 파크에서 함께 보낸 며칠은, 그 애의 남편이 무엇 때문에 그 애와 결혼했는지를 내게—아니 누구에게든—보여 주기에 충분하고도 남았다.\n\n“제 실망과 시련이 얼마나 일찍 시작되었는지, 아니 그것이 어떤 것이었는지조차 들으시게 해서 언니를 괴롭히지는 않을게요.” 그 애가 말했다. “제 기억 속에 담아 두는 것만으로도 이미 충분히 괴로우니까요. 다만 제가 난생처음이자 마지막으로 항의라는 걸 해 보았을 때 그이가 그것을 어떻게 받아들였는지 말씀드리면, 그이가 늘 저를 어떻게 대해 왔는지 낱낱이 말로 설명한 것이나 다름없이 아시게 될 거예요. 로마에서 어느 날 우리가 함께 말을 타고 케킬리아 메텔라의 무덤까지 나갔을 때였어요. 하늘은 잔잔하고 아름다웠고, 웅장한 옛 폐허는 근사해 보였어요. 그리고 옛날에 한 남편의 사랑이 아내를 기리려고 저것을 세웠다는 생각이 들자, 저는 그때껏 느껴 본 적 없이 애틋하고 간절한 마음으로 제 남편을 바라보게 되었지요. ‘당신도 저를 위해 저런 무덤을 지어 주시겠어요, 퍼시벌?’ 하고 물었어요. ‘결혼 전에는 저를 끔찍이 사랑한다고 하셨잖아요. 그런데 그 뒤로는——’ 더는 말을 이을 수가 없었어요. 매리언! 그이는 저를 쳐다보지조차 않고 있었어요! 저는 눈에 눈물이 고인 걸 그이에게 보이지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶어 베일을 내렸어요. 그이가 제 말에 아무 관심도 두지 않았다고 여겼는데, 실은 아니었어요. 그이는 ‘가지’ 하고는 저를 말에 태워 주면서 혼자 웃었어요. 자기 말에 올라타서는 우리가 말을 몰아 떠나는 동안 또 웃었고요. ‘내가 당신 무덤을 지어 준다면,’ 그이가 말했어요. ‘그건 당신 돈으로 짓는 게 되겠지. 케킬리아 메텔라도 재산이 있어서 제 무덤값을 치렀는지 궁금하군.’ 저는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어요—베일 뒤에서 울고 있는데 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어요? ‘아, 당신처럼 살결 흰 여자들은 하나같이 뾰로통하지.’ 그이가 말했어요. ‘뭘 원하는 거요? 찬사와 달콤한 말인가? 좋소! 오늘 아침은 내 기분이 괜찮으니. 찬사는 바쳐진 걸로, 그 말들은 다 해 준 걸로 치시오.’ 남자들은 우리에게 모진 말을 할 때, 우리가 그것을 얼마나 잘 기억하는지, 그것이 우리를 얼마나 해치는지 도무지 모르지요. 차라리 제가 계속 울었더라면 나았을 텐데, 그이의 경멸이 제 눈물을 말려 버리고 제 마음을 굳게 만들었어요. 그때부터예요, 매리언, 제가 월터 하트라이트를 생각하는 저 자신을 다시는 억누르지 않게 된 것은요. 우리가 남몰래 서로를 그토록 아끼던 그 행복한 나날의 기억이 되돌아와 저를 위로하도록 내버려 두었어요. 그것 말고 제가 어디서 위안을 구할 수 있었겠어요? 우리가 함께 있었더라면 언니가 더 나은 길로 저를 이끌어 주셨겠지요. 잘못인 줄 알아요, 언니. 하지만 제게 변명의 여지조차 없는 잘못이었는지 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n나는 그 애에게서 얼굴을 돌리지 않을 수 없었다. “묻지 마라!” 내가 말했다. “내가 너처럼 고통을 겪어 보기라도 했니? 무슨 자격으로 내가 판가름을 한단 말이냐?”\n\n“저는 그이를 생각하곤 했어요.” 그 애가 목소리를 낮추고 내게로 더 가까이 다가앉으며 말을 이었다. “퍼시벌이 오페라 사람들 틈에 끼려고 밤이면 저를 혼자 두고 나갈 때면 그이를 생각하곤 했어요. 하느님께서 저를 가난으로 축복해 주시고 제가 그이의 아내가 되었더라면 저는 어떤 사람이 되었을까 상상하곤 했지요. 값싸지만 단정한 옷을 입고 집에 앉아, 그이가 우리 두 사람의 밥벌이를 하는 동안 그이를 기다리는 저를 그려 보았어요—집에 앉아 그이를 위해 일하고, 그이를 위해 일해야 하기에 더욱더 그이를 사랑하는 저를요—그이가 지쳐 들어오면 맞이하여 모자와 외투를 받아 주고, 매리언, 그이를 위해 배워 둔 자잘한 요리로 저녁상에서 그이를 기쁘게 해 주는 저를요. 아! 부디 그이가 저를 떠올리고 제 모습을 그려 볼 만큼 외롭고 서글픈 처지가 되는 일은 없기를 바라요, 제가 그이를 생각하고 그이의 모습을 그려 보듯이는요!”\n\n그 애가 그 서글픈 말을 하는 동안, 잃어버렸던 다정함이 모두 그 목소리로 돌아왔고, 잃어버렸던 아름다움이 모두 그 얼굴로 떨리듯 되살아났다. 그 애의 눈은 우리 앞에 펼쳐진 시들고 적막하고 불길한 풍경을, 마치 어둡고 위협적인 하늘 속에서 정겨운 컴벌랜드의 언덕을 보기라도 하는 것처럼 사랑스럽게 바라보았다.\n\n“월터 얘기는 이제 그만하자.” 내가 겨우 마음을 다잡고 말했다. “아, 로라, 지금 그 사람 얘기를 해서 우리 둘 다 비참해지는 일만은 하지 말자꾸나!”\n\n그 애는 정신을 차리고 다정한 눈길로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“언니를 한순간이라도 아프게 하느니,” 그 애가 대답했다. “차라리 그이에 대해 영영 입을 다물겠어요.”\n\n“너를 위해서 하는 말이야.” 내가 간청하듯 말했다. “다 널 생각해서 하는 말이란다. 네 남편이 그 말을 듣기라도 하면——”\n\n“듣는다 해도 놀라지 않을 거예요.”\n\n그 애는 지치고 차가운 평정으로 그 이상한 대답을 했다. 대답을 하는 순간 달라진 그 애의 태도가, 대답 그 자체 못지않게 나를 놀라게 했다.\n\n“놀라지 않는다니!” 나는 되뇌었다. “로라! 네가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 생각해 보렴—무섭구나!”\n\n“사실인걸요.” 그 애가 말했다. “오늘 언니 방에서 얘기할 때 말씀드리려던 게 바로 그거예요. 리머리지에서 그이에게 마음을 열었을 때 제가 지녔던 유일한 비밀은 해로울 것 없는 비밀이었어요, 매리언—언니도 그렇게 말씀하셨잖아요. 제가 감춘 건 이름뿐이었는데, 그이가 그걸 알아냈어요.”\n\n나는 그 말을 들었으나 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 그 애의 마지막 말이 내 안에 아직 살아 있던 작은 희망마저 죽여 버렸다.\n\n“로마에서 일어난 일이에요.” 그 애가 여전히 지치고 차가운 평정으로 말을 이었다. “퍼시벌 경의 친구분들인 마클랜드 씨 부부가 영국인들을 위해 연 작은 모임에 갔었지요. 마클랜드 부인은 그림을 아주 잘 그린다는 평판이 있었고, 손님들 몇몇이 청해서 그림을 보여 주게 되었어요. 우리 모두 감탄했는데, 제가 한 어떤 말이 그분의 관심을 특히 저에게로 끌었나 봐요. ‘부인도 분명 그림을 그리시지요?’ 하고 물으시더군요. ‘예전에 조금 그렸습니다만,’ 하고 제가 대답했어요. ‘지금은 그만두었습니다.’ ‘한번 그려 보신 분이라면,’ 그분이 말했어요. ‘언젠가 다시 붙잡게 되실 거예요. 그렇게 되시거든 제가 선생님을 한 분 추천해 드리고 싶군요.’ 저는 아무 말도 하지 않고—왜인지는 언니도 아시잖아요, 매리언—화제를 돌리려 했어요. 그런데 마클랜드 부인이 계속하시더군요. ‘저는 온갖 선생님을 다 겪어 보았지만,’ 그분이 말을 이었어요. ‘그중에서도 가장 훌륭하고 가장 총명하고 가장 자상했던 분은 하트라이트 씨였어요. 언제든 다시 그림을 붙잡으시게 되면 꼭 그분을 선생으로 삼아 보세요. 젊은 분인데—겸손하고 신사다운 분이라—틀림없이 마음에 드실 거예요.’ 그런 말이 낯선 사람들 앞에서, 그것도 신랑 신부를 만나러 초대된 낯선 사람들 앞에서 공공연히 저에게 건네졌다고 생각해 보세요! 저는 제 자신을 다잡으려고 할 수 있는 걸 다 했어요—아무 말도 하지 않고 그림들에 바짝 고개를 숙였지요. 겨우 다시 고개를 들었을 때 제 눈과 남편의 눈이 마주쳤고, 저는 그 눈빛에서 제 얼굴이 저를 팔아 버렸다는 걸 알았어요. ‘하트라이트 씨 일은 생각해 보기로 하지.’ 그이가 내내 저를 쳐다보며 말했어요. ‘영국에 돌아가면 말이오. 마클랜드 부인, 저도 같은 생각입니다—글라이드 부인이 틀림없이 그를 마음에 들어 할 것 같군요.’ 그이가 마지막 말에 힘을 주는 바람에 제 뺨이 화끈거렸고, 숨이 막힐 듯 심장이 뛰었어요. 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았어요. 우리는 일찍 자리를 떴어요. 그이는 호텔로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 말이 없었어요. 저를 부축해 내려 주고 여느 때처럼 뒤따라 위층으로 올라왔지요. 그런데 응접실에 들어서자마자 그이는 문을 잠그고 저를 의자에 밀어 앉히더니, 제 어깨에 두 손을 얹은 채 저를 내려다보고 섰어요. ‘리머리지에서 당신이 그 뻔뻔스러운 고백을 하던 그날 아침부터,’ 그이가 말했어요. ‘나는 그 사내를 찾아내고 싶었소. 그런데 오늘 밤 당신 얼굴에서 찾아냈지. 당신 그림 선생이 그 사내였고, 이름은 하트라이트요. 당신도 후회하게 될 거고, 그자도 후회하게 될 거요. 목숨이 붙어 있는 마지막 순간까지. 이제 가서 자고, 원한다면 그자의 꿈이나 꾸시오. 그 어깨에 내 채찍 자국이 난 꼴로 말이오.’ 요즘도 저에게 화가 날 때면 그이는 언니가 계신 자리에서 제가 인정했던 그 일을 비웃음이나 협박과 함께 들먹여요. 제가 그이를 믿고 털어놓은 것을 그이가 제멋대로 끔찍하게 해석하는 걸 막을 힘이 저에게는 없어요. 저를 믿게 만들 힘도, 입을 다물게 할 힘도 없고요. 오늘 그이가 제게 자기와 결혼한 건 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 꾸민 것이라고 말했을 때 언니가 놀란 얼굴을 하셨지요. 다음번에 그이가 심사가 뒤틀릴 때 그 말을 또 하는 걸 들으시면, 그때는 놀라지 않으실 거예요——아, 매리언! 그만! 그만요! 아파요!”\n\n나는 그 애를 두 팔로 끌어안고 있었는데, 회한의 쓰라림과 고통이 그 팔을 죔쇠처럼 그 애를 둘러 조이고 있었던 것이다. 그렇다! 나의 회한이었다. 리머리지의 정자에서 내 모진 말이 월터의 심장을 찔렀을 때 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 창백한 절망이, 말 없고 견딜 수 없는 질책이 되어 내 앞에 되살아났다. 내 손이 길을 가리켜, 내 동생이 사랑한 남자를 한 걸음 한 걸음 제 나라와 제 벗들에게서 멀리 떠나가게 만들었다. 나는 그 두 젊은 마음 사이에 서서 둘을 영영 갈라놓았고, 그의 삶과 그 애의 삶이 그 소행의 증거로 나란히 망가진 채 내 앞에 놓여 있었다. 내가 이 짓을 했다. 그것도 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 위해서.\n\n퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 위해서.\n\n그 애가 말하는 소리가 들렸고, 나는 그 목소리의 어조로 그 애가 나를 위로하고 있다는 걸 알았다—침묵의 질책 말고는 받을 자격이 없는 나를! 내 생각에 사로잡힌 그 비참함을 내가 다스리기까지 얼마나 오랜 시간이 걸렸는지는 알 수 없다. 나는 그 애가 내게 입을 맞추고 있다는 것을 먼저 깨달았고, 그러고 나서야 내 눈이 문득 깨어나 바깥 사물을 감각하는 듯하더니, 내가 기계적으로 눈앞의 호수 풍경을 곧장 바라보고 있다는 걸 알았다.\n\n“늦었어요.” 그 애가 속삭이는 소리가 들렸다. “수림 속은 어두워질 거예요.” 그 애는 내 팔을 흔들며 되풀이했다. “매리언! 수림 속이 어두워질 거예요.”\n\n“조금만 더 있자.” 내가 말했다. “잠깐만, 마음을 추스르게.”\n\n나는 아직 그 애를 마주 볼 자신이 없어서, 눈을 풍경에 못 박아 둔 채로 있었다.\n\n정말로 늦은 시각이었다. 하늘에 걸린 짙은 갈색 나무 띠는 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서 흐려져, 길게 늘어진 연기 자락처럼 어렴풋해져 있었다. 아래쪽 호수 위의 안개는 슬그머니 몸을 불리며 우리 쪽으로 다가와 있었다. 정적은 여전히 숨 막힐 듯했으나 그 소름 끼치던 기운은 가시고, 그 고요함이 지닌 엄숙한 신비만이 남아 있었다.\n\n“집에서 너무 멀리 왔어요.” 그 애가 속삭였다. “돌아가요.”\n\n그 애가 갑자기 멈추더니, 내게서 얼굴을 돌려 보트 창고 입구 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n“매리언!” 그 애가 심하게 떨면서 말했다. “아무것도 안 보이세요? 보세요!”\n\n“어디?”\n\n“저 아래요, 우리 아래쪽에.”\n\n그 애가 손으로 가리켰다. 내 눈이 그 손을 좇았고, 나에게도 그것이 보였다.\n\n살아 있는 형체 하나가 저 멀리 히스 황무지 위를 지나가고 있었다. 그것은 보트 창고에서 바라보이는 우리의 시야를 가로질러, 안개의 바깥 가장자리를 따라 어둑하게 지나갔다. 저만치 우리 앞에서 멈추어—기다리다가—다시 나아갔다. 흰 안개 구름을 뒤에 그리고 위에 두른 채 천천히 움직이더니—천천히, 천천히, 마침내 보트 창고 가장자리를 스쳐 미끄러져 가고는 더 이상 보이지 않았다.\n\n우리 둘 다 그날 저녁 우리 사이에 오간 일로 이미 넋이 나가 있었다. 로라가 감히 수림 속으로 들어서기까지, 또 내가 그 애를 집으로 데려가겠다고 마음을 정하기까지 몇 분이 지나갔다.\n\n“남자였을까요, 여자였을까요?” 마침내 우리가 바깥의 어둡고 축축한 공기 속으로 걸음을 옮길 때 그 애가 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n“나도 모르겠구나.”\n\n“어느 쪽 같으세요?”\n\n“여자처럼 보이더구나.”\n\n“전 긴 망토를 걸친 남자일까 봐 무서웠어요.”\n\n“남자일 수도 있지. 이렇게 어스름한 빛 속에서는 확실히 알 수가 없단다.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 매리언! 무서워요—길이 안 보여요. 그 형체가 우리를 따라오면 어쩌죠?”\n\n“그럴 리 없다, 로라. 정말이지 놀랄 만한 일은 아무것도 없어. 호숫가는 마을에서 멀지 않고, 낮이든 밤이든 누구나 자유로이 거닐 수 있는 곳이야. 오히려 여태 그곳에서 사람 하나 못 본 게 신기할 지경이지.”\n\n우리는 이제 수림 안에 들어와 있었다. 몹시 어두웠다—어찌나 어두운지 길을 놓치지 않기가 쉽지 않을 정도였다. 나는 로라에게 팔을 내주었고, 우리는 돌아가는 길을 할 수 있는 한 빨리 걸었다.\n\n절반도 채 지나지 않았을 때 그 애가 멈춰 서더니 나까지 멈추게 했다. 그 애는 귀를 기울이고 있었다.\n\n“쉿.” 그 애가 속삭였다. “뒤에서 무슨 소리가 나요.”\n\n“마른 잎이겠지.” 나는 그 애를 안심시키려고 말했다. “아니면 나무에서 떨어진 잔가지거나.”\n\n“지금은 여름이고 바람 한 점 없잖아요, 매리언. 들어 보세요!”\n\n나에게도 그 소리가 들렸다—우리를 뒤따르는 가벼운 발소리 같은 소리가.\n\n“그게 누구든 무엇이든 상관없다.” 내가 말했다. “계속 걷자. 조금만 더 가면, 놀랄 일이 생기더라도 소리쳐서 집에 들릴 만큼 가까워질 테니.”\n\n우리는 빠르게 걸었다—어찌나 빨랐던지 수림을 거의 다 빠져나와 불 켜진 창문들이 보일 무렵에는 로라가 숨이 차 있었다.\n\n나는 그 애가 숨을 고르도록 잠시 기다렸다. 다시 나아가려는 참에 그 애가 또 나를 붙들더니, 한 번 더 귀를 기울이라고 손짓했다. 우리 둘 다 뒤편 나무들의 검은 깊숙한 곳에서 길고 무거운 한숨 소리를 또렷이 들었다.\n\n“거기 누구요?” 내가 소리쳐 물었다.\n\n아무 대답도 없었다.\n\n“거기 누구요?” 내가 다시 물었다.\n\n한순간 정적이 흐르더니, 이내 가벼운 발소리가 다시 들려왔다. 점점 희미해지며—어둠 속으로 잦아들며—잦아들고, 잦아들고, 잦아들어—마침내 정적 속으로 사라져 버렸다.\n\n우리는 나무들 사이를 서둘러 빠져나와 그 너머 트인 잔디밭으로 나섰고, 그곳을 빠르게 가로질러, 서로 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 집에 이르렀다.\n\n현관 등불 빛 속에서 로라는 새하얗게 질린 뺨과 놀란 눈으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“무서워서 반쯤 죽는 줄 알았어요.” 그 애가 말했다. “대체 누구였을까요?”\n\n“내일 짐작이나 해 보자꾸나.” 내가 대답했다. “그때까지는 우리가 듣고 본 것을 아무에게도 말하지 마라.”\n\n“왜요?”\n\n“침묵은 안전하니까. 그리고 이 집에서 우리에게는 안전이 필요하니까.”\n\n나는 곧바로 로라를 위층으로 올려 보내고는, 잠시 기다려 모자를 벗고 머리를 매만진 다음, 책을 찾는 척하며 서재로 첫 조사에 나섰다.\n\n거기 백작이 이 집에서 가장 큰 안락의자를 가득 채우고 앉아, 오토만에 발을 올리고 넥타이를 무릎에 걸쳐 놓고 셔츠 깃을 활짝 풀어 헤친 채, 담배를 피우며 태연히 책을 읽고 있었다. 그리고 그 옆 걸상에는 포스코 백작부인이 얌전한 아이처럼 앉아 담배를 말고 있었다. 남편도 아내도 그날 저녁 늦게까지 밖에 있다가 방금 허둥지둥 집에 돌아왔을 리는 도무지 없었다. 나는 그들에게 눈길을 두는 순간, 서재를 찾은 내 목적이 이루어졌음을 느꼈다.\n\n내가 방에 들어서자 포스코 백작은 예의 바르게 당황하며 일어나 넥타이를 맸다.\n\n“부디 방해가 되지 않았으면 좋겠군요.” 내가 말했다. “저는 그저 책을 한 권 가지러 왔을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“나처럼 덩치 큰 불행한 사내들은 하나같이 더위에 시달리지요.” 백작이 커다란 초록색 부채로 자못 근엄하게 부채질을 하며 말했다. “나의 훌륭한 아내와 자리를 바꿀 수만 있다면 좋으련만. 저 사람은 지금 이 순간 바깥 연못 속의 물고기만큼이나 서늘하답니다.”\n\n백작부인은 남편의 기묘한 비유에 힘입어 마음을 좀 누그러뜨렸다. “저는 결코 덥지가 않답니다, 홀콤 양.” 그녀는 자기 미덕 가운데 하나를 고백하는 여자처럼 겸손한 태도로 말했다.\n\n“글라이드 부인과 함께 저녁에 밖에 나가셨던가요?” 내가 겉모양을 갖추려고 선반에서 책 한 권을 꺼내는 동안 백작이 물었다.\n\n“네, 바람을 좀 쐬러 나갔었지요.”\n\n“어느 쪽으로 가셨는지 여쭈어도 될까요?”\n\n“호수 쪽으로요—보트 창고까지 갔습니다.”\n\n“아하? 보트 창고까지 말입니까?”\n\n다른 때 같았으면 나는 그의 호기심을 불쾌하게 여겼을지도 모른다. 그러나 오늘 밤에는 그와 그의 아내 모두 호수의 그 수수께끼 같은 출현과 무관하다는 또 하나의 증거로 여기며 반겼다.\n\n“오늘 저녁에는 더 이상 모험은 없으셨겠지요?” 그가 말을 이었다. “다친 개를 발견하셨던 것 같은 발견도 더는 없었고요?”\n\n그는 그 헤아릴 길 없는 잿빛 눈을 나에게 못 박았다. 그 눈 속에는 언제나 나로 하여금 그를 쳐다보지 않을 수 없게 만들고, 쳐다보는 동안 내내 나를 불안하게 만드는 차갑고 맑고 뿌리칠 수 없는 광채가 있다. 그럴 때면 그의 마음이 내 마음속을 캐고 있다는 형언할 수 없는 의심이 나를 짓누르는데, 지금도 그것이 나를 짓눌렀다.\n\n“없었습니다.” 나는 짧게 대답했다. “모험도—발견도 없었어요.”\n\n나는 그에게서 눈을 돌려 방을 나가려 했다. 이상하게 들리겠지만, 포스코 백작부인이 남편을 먼저 움직이고 눈길을 돌리게 만들어 나를 도와주지 않았더라면 나는 그 시도에 성공하지 못했으리라는 생각이 든다.\n\n“백작, 홀콤 양을 계속 세워 두고 계시잖아요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그가 내게 의자를 가져다주려고 돌아서는 순간 나는 기회를 붙잡아—고맙다는 인사를 하고—양해를 구한 뒤—빠져나왔다.\n\n한 시간 뒤 로라의 하녀가 마침 제 마님의 방에 와 있기에, 나는 하인들이 시간을 어떻게 보냈는지 알아볼 셈으로 밤공기가 답답하다는 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n“아래층에서는 더위에 많이 시달렸니?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아뇨, 아가씨.” 하녀가 말했다. “이렇다 하게 느끼지는 않았습니다.”\n\n“그럼 숲에 나가 있었던 게로구나?”\n\n“몇몇은 나갈까 생각했지요, 아가씨. 그런데 요리사가 부엌문 밖 시원한 안뜰로 의자를 내가겠다고 하기에, 다시 생각해 보고는 나머지도 다들 의자를 거기로 내갔습니다.”\n\n이제 남은 것은 가정부의 행방뿐이었다.\n\n“마이컬슨 부인은 벌써 잠자리에 들었니?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아닐 겁니다, 아가씨.” 하녀가 웃으며 말했다. “마이컬슨 부인은 지금쯤 자리에 드는 게 아니라 일어나실 참일걸요.”\n\n“어째서? 무슨 말이냐? 마이컬슨 부인이 대낮에 잠자리에 들기라도 했다는 게냐?”\n\n“그런 건 아닙니다, 아가씨. 하지만 그와 다를 바 없지요. 저녁 내내 자기 방 소파에서 주무셨거든요.”\n\n서재에서 내 눈으로 본 것과 방금 로라의 하녀에게서 들은 것을 맞추어 보면 한 가지 결론이 불가피해 보인다. 우리가 호수에서 본 형체는 포스코 백작부인의 것도, 그 남편의 것도, 하인들 가운데 누구의 것도 아니었다. 우리가 등 뒤에서 들은 발소리는 이 집에 속한 그 누구의 발소리도 아니었다.\n\n대체 누구였을까?\n\n물어봐야 소용없어 보인다. 나는 그 형체가 남자의 것이었는지 여자의 것이었는지조차 판단할 수가 없다. 다만 여자의 것이었다고 생각한다는 말밖에는 할 수가 없다.\n\n# VI\n\n6월 18일.— 어제저녁 보트 창고에서 로라가 들려준 이야기를 듣고 겪었던 자책의 괴로움이 밤의 적막 속에서 되살아나, 나는 몇 시간이나 잠들지 못한 채 비참하게 뒤척였다.\n\n결국 나는 촛불을 켜고 옛 일기장들을 뒤지며, 그 애의 불행한 결혼이라는 치명적인 잘못에 내 몫이 정말로 얼마나 있었는지, 또 그 애를 거기서 구해 내기 위해 내가 한때 무엇을 할 수 있었는지를 살펴보았다. 그 결과는 나를 조금이나마 달래 주었다. 아무리 눈멀고 무지하게 행동했다 해도 나는 최선이라고 믿는 바를 행했다는 것이 드러났기 때문이다. 우는 일은 대개 내게 해롭지만 어젯밤만은 그렇지 않았다—오히려 마음이 가벼워진 것 같다. 오늘 아침 나는 굳은 결심과 고요한 마음으로 자리에서 일어났다. 이제 퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 다시는 나를 화나게 하지 못할 것이며, 내가 온갖 굴욕과 모욕과 위협을 무릅쓰고 이 집에 머무는 것은 오직 로라를 돕기 위해서, 로라를 위해서라는 사실을 한순간도 잊지 않을 것이다.\n\n오늘 아침이라면 호숫가의 그 그림자와 나무숲에서 들린 발소리를 두고 우리끼리 마음껏 이런저런 추측을 해 볼 수도 있었겠지만, 그 모든 것은 로라를 몹시 속상하게 만든 사소한 사고 때문에 중단되고 말았다. 그 애가 결혼 전날 내가 기념으로 준 작은 브로치를 잃어버린 것이다. 어제저녁 나갈 때는 분명히 달고 있었으니, 보트 창고에서든 돌아오는 길에서든 옷에서 떨어졌으리라고 짐작할 수밖에 없다. 하인들을 보내 찾게 했지만 헛수고로 돌아왔다. 그래서 이제는 로라가 직접 찾으러 나갔다. 찾든 못 찾든, 길모어 씨의 동업자에게서 오는 편지가 내 손에 들어오기 전에 퍼시벌 경이 돌아온다면, 그 애가 집에 없는 이유를 대는 데 이 분실 사건이 도움이 될 것이다.\n\n방금 한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다. 나는 런던에서 오는 심부름꾼을 여기서 기다리는 편이 나을지, 아니면 조용히 빠져나가 문지기 집 대문 밖에서 그를 지켜보는 편이 나을지 궁리하는 중이다.\n\n이 집의 모든 사람과 모든 것을 향한 내 의심은 두 번째 방법이 낫겠다는 쪽으로 기운다. 백작은 아침 식사실에 안전하게 붙들려 있다. 십 분 전 이층으로 뛰어 올라가면서 문 너머로 그가 카나리아들에게 재주를 시키는 소리를 들었다. “내 새끼손가락 위로 나오렴, 예쁘-예쁘-예쁜이들아! 나와서 계단을 깡충깡충 올라가 보자! 하나, 둘, 셋—올라가! 셋, 둘, 하나—내려가! 하나, 둘, 셋—짹-짹-짹-쪽!” 새들은 여느 때처럼 황홀하게 노래를 터뜨렸고, 백작은 마치 자기도 새라도 된 양 짹짹거리고 휘파람을 불며 화답했다. 내 방문이 열려 있어 바로 이 순간에도 그 날카로운 노랫소리와 휘파람 소리가 들려온다. 눈에 띄지 않고 정말로 빠져나가려면 지금이 그때다.\n\n네 시.— 마지막 기록을 한 뒤로 흘러간 세 시간이 블랙워터 파크에서 벌어지는 일들의 행로를 통째로 새로운 방향으로 돌려놓았다. 그것이 좋은 쪽인지 나쁜 쪽인지는 나로서는 판단할 수도, 감히 판단하려 들 수도 없다.\n\n우선 내가 중단했던 자리로 되돌아가자. 그러지 않으면 나 자신의 뒤엉킨 생각 속에서 길을 잃고 말 테니.\n\n나는 마음먹은 대로, 런던에서 오는 내 편지를 지닌 심부름꾼을 문지기 집 대문에서 맞으려고 밖으로 나갔다. 계단에서는 아무도 마주치지 않았다. 현관에서는 백작이 여전히 새들에게 재주를 시키는 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 바깥 안뜰을 가로지르다가, 커다란 연못 둘레를 빙빙 도는 그 특유의 산책을 홀로 하고 있는 포스코 백작부인과 마주쳤다. 나는 서두르는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않으려고 곧바로 걸음을 늦추었고, 조심하는 뜻에서 점심 전에 외출할 생각이 있느냐고 묻기까지 했다. 그 여자는 더없이 다정하게 미소 지으며 집 근처에 있는 편이 좋다고 말하고는, 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕이고 현관 안으로 들어갔다. 뒤를 돌아보니, 내가 마차 대문 옆의 쪽문을 열기도 전에 그 여자는 이미 문을 닫은 뒤였다.\n\n채 십오 분도 되지 않아 나는 문지기 집에 이르렀다.\n\n바깥 오솔길은 왼쪽으로 급히 꺾였다가 백 야드쯤 곧게 뻗어 나간 뒤, 다시 오른쪽으로 급히 꺾여 큰길과 이어졌다. 한쪽으로는 문지기 집에서, 다른 쪽으로는 역으로 가는 길에서 보이지 않는 그 두 굽이 사이에서, 나는 앞뒤로 오가며 기다렸다. 양옆으로는 높은 산울타리가 서 있었고, 내 시계로 이십 분 동안 나는 아무것도 보지 못했고 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다. 그 시간이 끝날 무렵 마차 소리가 귀에 들어왔고, 두 번째 굽이 쪽으로 나아가던 나는 역에서 오는 삯마차와 마주쳤다. 나는 마부에게 세우라는 신호를 보냈다. 마부가 시키는 대로 하자 점잖아 보이는 남자가 무슨 일인가 하고 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었다.\n\n“실례합니다.” 내가 말했다. “블랙워터 파크로 가시는 길이 맞는지요?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 부인.”\n\n“누구에게 가는 편지를 가지고 계신가요?”\n\n“홀콤 양에게 가는 편지입니다, 부인.”\n\n“그 편지는 제게 주시면 됩니다. 제가 홀콤입니다.”\n\n남자는 모자에 손을 대어 인사하고는 곧바로 삯마차에서 내려 편지를 건네주었다.\n\n나는 그 자리에서 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같은 글을 읽었다. 조심하는 뜻에서 원본은 없애 버리는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 여기에 옮겨 적어 둔다.\n\n“친애하는 부인께.— 오늘 아침에 받은 부인의 편지는 저를 몹시 근심스럽게 했습니다. 되도록 간략하고 분명하게 답을 드리겠습니다.\n\n“부인께서 알려 주신 사정을 신중히 검토하고, 혼인 재산 계약서에 규정된 글라이드 부인의 지위에 관해 제가 아는 바를 아울러 살펴본 결과, 유감스럽게도 저는 신탁 재산을 퍼시벌 경에게 대여하는 일—달리 말하면 글라이드 부인의 재산 이만 파운드 가운데 얼마를 대여하는 일—이 계획되고 있으며, 명백한 신탁 위반에 부인의 승낙을 확보해 두고 훗날 이의를 제기할 때 그 서명을 근거로 부인에게 맞서기 위하여 글라이드 부인을 그 증서의 당사자로 삼으려 한다는 결론에 이르렀습니다. 지금과 같은 처지에 놓인 분에게 어떤 종류의 증서든 서명이 필요할 까닭은, 이 밖의 어떤 가정으로도 설명되지 않습니다.\n\n“문제의 증서가 제가 짐작할 수밖에 없는 그런 문서라고 할 때, 글라이드 부인께서 거기에 서명하신다면 부인의 수탁자들은 그 이만 파운드에서 퍼시벌 경에게 돈을 융통해 줄 수 있게 됩니다. 그렇게 빌려준 금액이 상환되지 않고, 또 글라이드 부인께 자녀가 생긴다면, 그 아이들의 재산은 그렇게 융통된 액수만큼—많든 적든—줄어들게 됩니다. 더 분명하게 말씀드리면, 글라이드 부인께서 달리 알고 계신 바가 없는 한, 이 거래는 아직 태어나지도 않은 자녀들에 대한 사기가 될 수 있습니다.\n\n“이처럼 중대한 사정을 고려할 때, 저는 글라이드 부인께서 서명을 미루는 이유로, 그 증서를 먼저 집안의 사무 변호사인 저에게—제 동업자 길모어 씨가 자리를 비운 터이니—보여 주기를 바란다고 밝히시기를 권합니다. 이 방침에 대해서는 어떤 합당한 반대도 있을 수 없습니다. 그 거래가 떳떳한 것이라면 제가 승인하는 데 아무 어려움이 없을 터이기 때문입니다.\n\n“더 필요한 도움이나 조언이 있으면 언제든 기꺼이 드리겠다는 뜻을 진심으로 전하며, 이만 줄이겠습니다. 부인의 충실한 종,\n\n“윌리엄 카일 올림.”\n\n나는 이 친절하고 사리에 밝은 편지를 몹시 고마운 마음으로 읽었다. 이 편지는 로라에게 서명을 거부할 반박할 수 없는 이유를, 그것도 우리 둘 다 이해할 수 있는 이유를 마련해 주었다. 심부름꾼은 내가 다 읽고 나면 지시를 받으려고 곁에서 기다리고 있었다.\n\n“편지 잘 알겠으며 대단히 감사드린다고 전해 주시겠어요?” 내가 말했다. “지금으로서는 달리 회답할 것이 없습니다.”\n\n내가 편지를 손에 펼쳐 든 채 그 말을 하고 있던 바로 그 순간, 포스코 백작이 큰길 쪽에서 오솔길 모퉁이를 돌아 나와, 마치 땅에서 솟아나기라도 한 듯 내 앞에 섰다.\n\n이 세상에서 그를 만나리라고는 가장 마지막으로나 생각했을 바로 그 자리에 그가 불쑥 나타났으니, 나는 완전히 허를 찔렸다. 심부름꾼은 내게 인사를 하고 다시 삯마차에 올랐다. 나는 그에게 한마디도 하지 못했고, 그의 인사에 답례조차 하지 못했다. 들켰다는—그것도 하필 그 남자에게 들켰다는—확신이 나를 그야말로 돌처럼 굳어 버리게 했다.\n\n“댁으로 돌아가시는 길입니까, 홀콤 양?” 그는 자기 쪽에서는 조금도 놀란 기색을 보이지 않고, 자기가 말하는 동안 떠나가는 삯마차를 돌아보지도 않은 채 물었다.\n\n나는 겨우 정신을 추슬러 그렇다는 몸짓을 해 보였다.\n\n“저도 돌아가는 길입니다.” 그가 말했다. “모시고 갈 기쁨을 부디 허락해 주십시오. 제 팔을 잡으시겠습니까? 저를 보고 놀라신 모양이군요!”\n\n나는 그의 팔을 잡았다. 흩어졌던 감각들 가운데 가장 먼저 돌아온 것은, 무엇을 희생하더라도 이 사람을 적으로 만들지는 말라고 경고하는 감각이었다.\n\n“저를 보고 놀라신 모양이군요!” 그는 특유의 조용하면서도 집요한 태도로 되풀이했다.\n\n“백작님이 아침 식사실에서 새들과 계신 소리를 들었다고 생각했거든요.” 나는 되도록 침착하고 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n“그렇습니다. 하지만 내 작은 깃털 아이들도, 친애하는 부인, 여느 아이들과 어찌나 똑같은지요. 그놈들에게도 심술궂은 날이 있는데 오늘 아침이 바로 그런 날이었습니다. 내가 그놈들을 새장에 도로 넣고 있는데 아내가 들어와서는, 홀콤 양이 혼자 산책을 나가시기에 두고 왔다고 하더군요. 부인이 아내에게 그렇게 말씀하셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“그렇습니다.”\n\n“그런데 홀콤 양, 함께 걸을 수 있다는 기쁨은 제가 뿌리치기에는 너무나 큰 유혹이었습니다. 제 나이쯤 되면 그만한 고백은 해로울 것이 없지요, 그렇지 않습니까? 저는 모자를 집어 들고 호위를 자청하러 나섰습니다. 포스코처럼 뚱뚱한 늙은이라도 호위가 아예 없는 것보다야 낫지 않겠습니까? 길을 잘못 들어—절망한 채 되돌아왔는데—이렇게, 감히 말씀드려도 될까요, 소원의 절정에 이르러 여기 서 있습니다.”\n\n그는 이런 찬사조로 유창하게 말을 이어 갔고, 덕분에 나는 침착함을 유지하는 것 말고는 달리 애쓸 일이 없었다. 그는 오솔길에서 자기가 본 것에 대해서도, 내가 여태 손에 쥐고 있는 편지에 대해서도 아주 먼발치로조차 언급하지 않았다. 이 불길한 신중함은, 그가 더없이 떳떳하지 못한 방법으로 내가 로라를 위해 변호사에게 도움을 청했다는 비밀을 알아냈다는 것, 그리고 이제 내가 그 답장을 은밀히 받았다는 사실까지 확인한 이상 자기 목적에 필요한 만큼은 충분히 알아냈으므로 자기가 내 마음속에 불러일으켰을 것이 분명한 의심을 가라앉히는 데만 마음을 쏟고 있다는 것을 내게 확신시켜 주었다. 이런 상황에서 나는 그럴듯한 변명으로 그를 속이려 들지 않을 만큼은 지혜로웠고, 또 그를 두려워하면서도 그의 팔에 얹은 내 손이 더럽혀지는 것 같은 기분을 느낄 만큼은 여자였다.\n\n집 앞 마찻길에서 우리는 마구간으로 끌려가는 이륜마차와 마주쳤다. 퍼시벌 경이 막 돌아온 것이다. 그는 우리를 맞으러 현관으로 나왔다. 그의 여행이 어떤 결과를 낳았든, 적어도 그 사나운 성미를 누그러뜨리지는 못한 모양이었다.\n\n“오! 둘이나 돌아왔군.” 그가 험악한 얼굴로 말했다. “집을 이렇게 비워 두다니 대체 어찌 된 일이오? 글라이드 부인은 어디 있소?”\n\n나는 브로치를 잃어버린 일을 이야기하고, 로라가 그것을 찾으러 나무숲에 갔다고 말했다.\n\n“브로치든 뭐든 간에.” 그는 뾰로통하게 으르렁거렸다. “오늘 오후 서재에서 만나기로 한 약속이나 잊지 말라고 하시오. 삼십 분 안에 얼굴을 보겠소.”\n\n나는 백작의 팔에서 손을 떼고 천천히 계단을 올라갔다. 그는 특유의 장중한 절로 나를 예우한 다음, 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 있는 이 집 주인에게 유쾌하게 말을 걸었다.\n\n“말해 보게, 퍼시벌.” 그가 말했다. “드라이브는 즐거웠는가? 그 어여쁘고 윤기 흐르는 브라운 몰리는 조금이라도 지쳐서 돌아왔는가?”\n\n“브라운 몰리고 뭐고 다 집어치우게—드라이브도! 나는 점심이나 먹어야겠네.”\n\n“그리고 나는 먼저 자네와 오 분만 이야기하고 싶네, 퍼시벌.” 백작이 응수했다. “오 분이면 되네, 친구. 여기 잔디밭에서.”\n\n“무슨 이야기인가?”\n\n“자네와 아주 깊이 관련된 용건일세.”\n\n나는 현관문을 지나가면서 이 물음과 대답을 들을 만큼, 그리고 퍼시벌 경이 뚱하게 망설이며 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 것을 볼 만큼은 오래 머뭇거렸다.\n\n“그 지긋지긋한 잔소리로 또 나를 들볶을 작정이라면.” 그가 말했다. “나만은 듣지 않겠네. 점심이나 먹어야겠어.”\n\n“이리 나와서 내 이야기를 듣게.” 백작은 친구가 퍼부을 수 있는 가장 무례한 말에도 조금도 흔들리지 않은 채 되풀이했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경이 계단을 내려갔다. 백작은 그의 팔을 잡고 부드럽게 이끌어 데려갔다. 그 “용건”이란 서명 문제를 두고 하는 말임이 틀림없었다. 두 사람이 로라와 나에 대해 이야기하고 있다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 나는 불안으로 가슴이 미어지고 정신이 아뜩했다. 바로 그 순간 두 사람이 서로 무슨 말을 주고받는지 아는 것이 우리 둘에게는 더없이 중대한 일일 수도 있었건만, 그 말은 단 한마디도 내 귀에 닿을 길이 없었다.\n\n나는 변호사의 편지를 가슴에 품은 채—이쯤 되니 자물쇠를 채워 두는 것조차 미덥지 않았다—이 방 저 방으로 집 안을 돌아다녔고, 마침내 조바심의 압박에 반쯤 미칠 지경이 되었다. 로라가 돌아온 기척은 없었고, 나는 그 애를 찾으러 나가 볼까 생각했다. 그러나 아침나절의 시련과 근심으로 기운이 다 빠져 있던 터라 한낮의 더위가 나를 완전히 짓눌렀고, 문까지 가 보려다가 결국 응접실로 되돌아와 가장 가까운 소파에 누워 몸을 추스를 수밖에 없었다.\n\n겨우 마음을 가라앉히고 있는데 문이 살며시 열리며 백작이 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n“천 번 용서를 구합니다, 홀콤 양.” 그가 말했다. “좋은 소식을 전하는 사람이기에 감히 방해할 뿐입니다. 아시다시피 무슨 일에서나 변덕스러운 퍼시벌이 막판에 마음을 바꾸기로 했고, 그리하여 서명 건은 당분간 미루어졌습니다. 우리 모두에게 큰 안도이지요, 홀콤 양. 홀콤 양의 얼굴에서도 그것을 기쁘게 읽습니다. 이 반가운 변화를 글라이드 부인께 전하실 때, 부디 제 각별한 경의와 축하의 말씀도 함께 전해 주십시오.”\n\n그는 내가 놀라움에서 채 벗어나기도 전에 자리를 떴다. 서명 문제에서 이처럼 뜻밖의 방침 변경이 일어난 것이 그의 영향력 덕분이며, 어제 내가 런던에 도움을 청한 사실과 오늘 그 답장을 받은 사실을 그가 알아냈기에 확실한 성공이 보장된 개입의 수단을 손에 넣었다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n나는 이런 인상들을 느꼈지만, 정신도 몸의 탈진을 함께 나누어 가진 듯하여, 불확실한 현재나 위협적인 미래에 유익하게 견주어 그것들을 곱씹을 형편이 못 되었다. 나는 두 번째로 뛰쳐나가 로라를 찾으려 했으나 머리가 어질어질하고 무릎이 후들거렸다. 마음과는 몹시 달리, 다시 포기하고 소파로 돌아가는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n집 안의 고요와 열린 창 밖에서 나지막이 웅웅거리는 여름 벌레들의 소리가 나를 달래 주었다. 눈이 저절로 감겼고, 나는 차츰 이상한 상태로 빠져들었다. 그것은 깨어 있는 것이 아니었으니 내 주위에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못했고, 잠든 것도 아니었으니 나 자신이 쉬고 있다는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그 상태에서, 지친 몸이 쉬는 동안 열에 들뜬 내 정신은 나에게서 풀려났고, 무아지경이랄까 몽상이랄까—무어라 불러야 할지 모르겠으나—그 속에서 나는 월터 하트라이트를 보았다. 그날 아침 일어난 뒤로 나는 그를 떠올린 적이 없었고, 로라도 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그를 가리키는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그런데도 나는 지금, 지나간 시간이 되돌아와 우리 둘이 다시 리머리지 하우스에 함께 있기라도 한 듯 또렷하게 그를 보았다.\n\n그는 여러 사람들 가운데 하나로 내게 나타났는데, 다른 이들의 얼굴은 하나도 또렷이 알아볼 수 없었다. 그들은 모두 거대한 폐허가 된 신전의 계단에 누워 있었다. 줄기를 끝없이 휘감은 무성한 덩굴과, 잎사귀와 줄기와 가지 뒤에서 이따금 희끄무레 번득이며 이를 드러내고 웃는 흉측한 돌 우상들을 거느린 거대한 열대의 나무들이 신전을 에워싸고 하늘을 가려, 계단 위의 그 가련한 무리 위로 음산한 그림자를 드리웠다. 하얀 김이 땅에서 슬며시 뒤틀리며 피어올라 연기처럼 화환을 이루며 사람들에게 다가가더니, 그들을 건드려 저마다 누운 자리에서 하나씩 하나씩 시체로 뻗게 만들었다. 월터를 향한 연민과 두려움의 고통이 내 혀를 풀어놓아, 나는 그에게 달아나라고 애원했다. “돌아와요, 돌아와요!” 나는 말했다. “그 애에게, 그리고 나에게 한 약속을 기억해요. 역병이 당신에게 닿아 다른 이들처럼 당신을 죽여 눕히기 전에 우리에게 돌아와요!”\n\n그는 이 세상 것 같지 않은 고요를 얼굴에 띠고 나를 바라보았다. “기다리십시오.” 그가 말했다. “저는 돌아갑니다. 큰길에서 그 길 잃은 여인을 만난 밤은, 아직 보이지 않는 어떤 섭리의 도구가 되도록 제 삶을 따로 떼어 놓은 밤이었습니다. 이곳 황야에서 길을 잃고 있든, 저곳 태어난 땅에서 환영을 받든, 저는 여전히 저와 당신과 당신과 저의 사랑을 받는 그분을 알 수 없는 응보와 피할 수 없는 종말로 이끄는 어두운 길을 걷고 있습니다. 기다리며 지켜보십시오. 다른 이들을 건드리는 역병은 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”\n\n나는 그를 다시 보았다. 그는 여전히 숲속에 있었고, 그와 함께 헤매던 동료들의 수는 아주 적게 줄어 있었다. 신전도 사라지고 우상들도 사라졌으며, 그 자리에는 손에 활을 들고 시위에 화살을 메긴 검고 왜소한 사람들의 형상이 나무들 사이에 살기를 띠고 숨어 있었다. 나는 다시 한번 월터가 걱정되어 그에게 경고하려고 소리쳤다. 다시 한번 그는 흔들림 없는 고요를 얼굴에 띤 채 나를 돌아보았다.\n\n“어두운 길 위에서.” 그가 말했다. “또 한 걸음입니다. 기다리며 지켜보십시오. 다른 이들을 맞히는 화살은 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”\n\n나는 세 번째로 그를 보았다. 거친 모래 해안에 좌초한 난파선 위였다. 짐이 넘치도록 실린 보트들은 그를 남겨 둔 채 뭍을 향해 멀어져 갔고, 그 혼자 배와 함께 가라앉도록 남겨져 있었다. 나는 맨 뒤의 보트를 불러 세워 목숨을 위한 마지막 안간힘을 써 보라고 그에게 외쳤다. 그 고요한 얼굴이 나를 돌아보았고, 흔들림 없는 목소리가 변함없는 대답을 돌려주었다. “여정의 또 한 걸음입니다. 기다리며 지켜보십시오. 다른 이들을 빠뜨리는 바다는 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”\n\n나는 마지막으로 그를 보았다. 그는 흰 대리석 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고 있었고, 그 아래 무덤에서 베일을 쓴 여인의 그림자가 솟아올라 그의 곁에서 기다렸다. 이 세상 것 같지 않던 그의 얼굴의 고요는 이 세상 것 같지 않은 슬픔으로 바뀌어 있었다. 그러나 그 말의 무서운 확신만은 그대로였다. “점점 더 어두워지고.” 그가 말했다. “점점 더 멀어집니다. 죽음은 선한 이와 아름다운 이와 젊은 이를 데려가고—저는 남겨 둡니다. 스러뜨리는 역병, 꿰뚫는 화살, 빠뜨리는 바다, 사랑과 희망 위로 닫히는 무덤, 그 모두가 제 여정의 걸음들이며, 저를 종말에 점점 더 가까이 데려갑니다.”\n\n말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움에, 눈물로도 다 할 수 없는 슬픔에 내 가슴은 내려앉았다. 어둠이 대리석 무덤 곁의 순례자를 에워쌌고—무덤에서 나온 베일 쓴 여인을 에워쌌고—그들을 바라보던 꿈꾸는 자를 에워쌌다. 나는 더 보지도 듣지도 못했다.\n\n어깨에 놓인 손이 나를 깨웠다. 로라의 손이었다.\n\n그 애는 소파 옆에 무릎을 꿇은 채였다. 얼굴은 상기되어 있고 흥분한 기색이었으며, 눈은 사납고 당황한 빛으로 내 눈과 마주쳤다. 나는 그 애를 보는 순간 소스라쳤다.\n\n“무슨 일이야?” 내가 물었다. “뭐가 그렇게 무서웠어?”\n\n그 애는 반쯤 열린 문 쪽을 돌아보고는, 내 귀에 입술을 바싹 대고 속삭이듯 대답했다—\n\n“매리언!—호숫가의 그 그림자—어젯밤의 그 발소리—방금 그 여자를 봤어! 방금 그 여자와 이야기까지 했어!”\n\n“맙소사, 누구를?”\n\n“앤 캐서릭.”\n\n나는 로라의 얼굴과 태도에 깃든 동요에 몹시 놀랐고, 꿈에서 막 깨어난 순간의 인상들에 몹시 심란해 있던 터라, 그 이름이 그 애의 입술을 스치는 순간 내게 터져 나온 그 계시를 감당할 형편이 못 되었다. 나는 그저 바닥에 못 박힌 듯 서서 숨죽인 채 그 애를 바라볼 뿐이었다.\n\n그 애는 방금 겪은 일에 너무 사로잡혀 있어서 자기 대답이 내게 미친 영향을 알아차리지 못했다. “앤 캐서릭을 봤어! 앤 캐서릭과 이야기했다니까!” 그 애는 내가 못 들은 줄 알고 되풀이했다. “아, 매리언, 언니한테 할 이야기가 얼마나 많은지! 이리 와—여기서는 누가 끼어들지도 몰라—당장 내 방으로 가자.”\n\n그렇게 다급히 말하며 그 애는 내 손을 잡고 서재를 지나, 일층 끝 방으로—오로지 그 애만 쓰도록 꾸며진 방으로—나를 데려갔다. 그곳이라면 하녀 말고는 어느 누구도 우리를 불쑥 찾아올 구실이 없었다. 그 애는 나를 앞세워 밀어 넣고 문을 잠근 뒤, 안쪽에 드리워진 사라사 커튼을 쳤다.\n\n나를 사로잡았던 그 이상한 마비의 느낌은 여전히 남아 있었다. 그러나 오래도록 그 애 주위에, 그리고 내 주위에 모여들려고 위협해 오던 얽히고설킨 일들이 이제 갑자기 우리 둘을 단단히 옭아매었다는 확신이 점점 커지며 내 마음을 파고들기 시작했다. 나는 그것을 말로 표현할 수 없었고, 내 생각 속에서조차 어렴풋이나마 실감하기 어려웠다. “앤 캐서릭!” 나는 아무 소용도 없이, 어찌할 바도 모른 채 되풀이하며 혼잣말로 속삭였다. “앤 캐서릭!”\n\n로라는 나를 가장 가까운 자리로, 방 한가운데의 등받이 없는 긴 의자로 이끌었다. “봐!” 그 애가 말했다. “여기 좀 봐!” 그러고는 자기 옷의 가슴께를 가리켰다.\n\n나는 잃어버렸던 브로치가 제자리에 다시 꽂혀 있는 것을 그제야 보았다. 그것을 보는 데에도, 그러고 나서 손으로 만져 보는 데에도 무언가 실재하는 것이 있었고, 그것이 내 생각 속의 소용돌이와 혼란을 가라앉히고 마음을 다잡도록 도와주는 듯했다.\n\n“브로치는 어디서 찾았어?” 그토록 중대한 순간에 내가 그 애에게 처음으로 건넬 수 있었던 말은 그런 하찮은 물음이었다.\n\n“그 여자가 찾아 줬어, 매리언.”\n\n“어디서?”\n\n“보트 창고 바닥에서. 아, 어디서부터 시작해야 하지—어떻게 이야기해야 하지! 그 여자는 나한테 참 이상하게 말했어—얼굴빛이 무섭도록 아파 보였어—그러다 갑자기 가 버렸어!”\n\n밀려드는 기억의 소용돌이가 그 애의 마음을 짓누르자 목소리가 높아졌다. 이 집에 있는 동안 밤낮으로 내 마음을 짓누르는 그 뿌리 깊은 불신이 그 순간 나를 일깨워 그 애에게 주의를 주게 했다. 바로 조금 전에 브로치를 본 것이 나를 일깨워 묻게 했던 것처럼.\n\n“목소리를 낮춰.” 내가 말했다. “창문이 열려 있고, 그 아래로 정원 길이 지나가. 처음부터 시작하렴, 로라. 그 여자와 네 사이에 오간 말을 한마디도 빼놓지 말고 다 이야기해 줘.”\n\n“창문을 닫을까?”\n\n“아니, 목소리만 낮춰—앤 캐서릭이 네 남편의 지붕 아래에서는 위험한 이야깃거리라는 것만 잊지 마. 그 여자를 어디서 처음 봤어?”\n\n“보트 창고에서, 매리언. 언니도 알다시피 나는 브로치를 찾으러 나가서 나무숲 사이 오솔길을 따라 걸으며 한 걸음 한 걸음 조심스럽게 땅을 살폈어. 그렇게 한참 만에 보트 창고까지 갔고, 안으로 들어서자마자 무릎을 꿇고 바닥을 뒤졌지. 문간을 등진 채 여전히 찾고 있는데, 등 뒤에서 부드럽고 낯선 목소리가 ‘페얼리 양’ 하고 부르는 소리가 들렸어.”\n\n“페얼리 양이라고!”\n\n“응, 내 옛 이름—영영 떠나보낸 줄로만 알았던 그 정답고 익숙한 이름. 나는 벌떡 일어났어—겁이 나서가 아니라, 그 목소리는 누구를 겁줄 만한 것이 아니라 너무나 다정하고 상냥했으니까—그저 몹시 놀라서였지. 거기 문간에서 나를 바라보며, 전에 본 기억이라고는 도무지 없는 얼굴의 여자가 서 있었어—”\n\n“어떤 옷차림이었어?”\n\n“깔끔하고 예쁜 흰 드레스를 입고, 그 위에 낡고 얇은 초라한 검은 숄을 걸치고 있었어. 보닛은 갈색 밀짚이었는데 숄만큼이나 초라하고 해져 있었지. 나는 그 드레스와 나머지 옷차림 사이의 차이에 눈길이 갔고, 내가 그걸 알아챈 걸 그 여자도 눈치챘어. ‘내 보닛이랑 숄은 보지 마세요.’ 그 여자가 빠르고 숨 가쁘고 불쑥 내뱉는 투로 말했어. ‘흰옷을 입을 수 없다면 무얼 입든 상관없어요. 드레스는 얼마든지 보셔도 돼요—그건 부끄럽지 않으니까요.’ 참 이상하지 않아? 내가 그 여자를 달랠 말을 꺼내기도 전에 한 손을 내밀었는데, 그 손에 내 브로치가 있었어. 나는 어찌나 기쁘고 고맙던지, 정말 느낀 그대로를 말하려고 아주 가까이 다가갔지. ‘그렇게 고마우시면 작은 친절 하나만 베풀어 주시겠어요?’ 그 여자가 물었어. ‘그럼요.’ 내가 대답했어. ‘제 힘이 닿는 친절이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 드릴게요.’ ‘그럼 이왕 제가 찾았으니, 브로치를 제가 달아 드리게 해 주세요.’ 그 부탁이 어찌나 뜻밖이었는지, 매리언, 게다가 어찌나 유별나게 간절히 청하던지 나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 한두 걸음 물러섰어. ‘아!’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘당신 어머니라면 제가 브로치를 달게 해 주셨을 텐데.’ 그 목소리와 눈빛에도, 또 그렇게 나무라듯 우리 어머니를 들먹이는 데에도 무언가가 있어서, 나는 내가 의심한 것이 부끄러워졌어. 나는 브로치를 쥔 그 손을 잡아 내 옷 가슴께로 살며시 가져갔지. ‘우리 어머니를 아셨어요?’ 내가 말했어. ‘아주 오래전 일인가요? 제가 전에 당신을 본 적이 있나요?’ 그 여자의 손은 브로치를 채우느라 바빴는데, 문득 멈추더니 그 손을 내 가슴에 지그시 눌렀어. ‘리머리지의 어느 좋은 봄날, 당신 어머니가 양옆에 어린 여자아이를 하나씩 데리고 학교로 가는 길을 걸어 내려가던 걸 기억 못 하시죠.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘저는 그 뒤로 다른 건 아무것도 생각해 본 적이 없어서 그 일을 기억해요. 그 어린 여자아이 하나가 당신이었고, 다른 하나가 저였어요. 예쁘고 영리한 페얼리 양과 가엾고 얼빠진 앤 캐서릭은 그때는 지금보다 서로 훨씬 가까웠지요!’”\n\n“그 여자가 이름을 말했을 때 기억이 났니, 로라?”\n\n“응, 리머리지에서 언니가 앤 캐서릭에 대해 내게 물었던 것도, 한때 그 여자가 나를 닮았다는 말을 들었다고 언니가 했던 것도 기억났어.”\n\n“무엇 때문에 그 일이 떠올랐어, 로라?”\n\n“그 여자가 떠오르게 했어. 내가 그 여자를 바라보는 동안, 그 여자가 나한테 아주 가까이 있는 동안, 우리가 서로 닮았다는 생각이 불쑥 떠올랐어! 그 얼굴은 창백하고 여위고 지쳐 있었지만—그걸 보는 순간 나는 오랜 병을 앓고 난 뒤 거울에 비친 내 얼굴을 보기라도 한 듯 소스라쳤어. 그 발견이—왜인지는 모르겠지만—어찌나 충격이었는지, 나는 그 순간 그 여자에게 도무지 한마디도 할 수가 없었어.”\n\n“네가 잠자코 있어서 그 여자가 상처를 받은 것 같았니?”\n\n“상처를 받은 것 같아 마음이 아파. ‘당신은 어머니의 얼굴을 닮지 않았군요.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘어머니의 마음도요. 당신 어머니의 얼굴은 검은빛이었고, 당신 어머니의 마음은, 페얼리 양, 천사의 마음이었어요.’ ‘저도 당신에게 다정한 마음을 품고 있는 건 분명해요.’ 내가 말했어. ‘마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 잘 표현하지는 못할지라도요. 그런데 왜 저를 페얼리 양이라고 부르시나요——’ ‘페얼리라는 이름은 사랑하고 글라이드라는 이름은 미워하니까요.’ 그 여자가 사납게 터뜨렸어. 그전까지는 그 여자에게서 미친 기색이라고는 조금도 보지 못했는데, 그때는 그 눈에서 그것을 본 것 같았어. ‘제가 결혼한 걸 모르실 수도 있겠다 싶었을 뿐이에요.’ 내가 말했어. 리머리지에서 그 여자가 내게 보낸 그 정신 나간 편지를 떠올리며, 진정시키려고 애쓰면서. 그 여자는 씁쓸하게 한숨을 쉬고 나에게서 몸을 돌렸어. ‘결혼한 걸 모른다고요?’ 그 여자가 되뇌었어. ‘저는 당신이 결혼했기 때문에 여기 온 거예요. 저세상에서 당신 어머니를 만나기 전에, 당신에게 속죄하려고 온 거예요.’ 그 여자는 점점 더 나에게서 멀어져 마침내 보트 창고 밖으로 나갔고, 그러고는 잠시 동안 살피며 귀를 기울였어. 다시 말하려고 돌아섰을 때는 안으로 들어오는 대신 그 자리에 선 채로, 입구 양쪽에 한 손씩 짚고 나를 들여다보며 이렇게 말했어. ‘어젯밤 호숫가에서 저를 보셨죠? 숲속에서 제가 뒤따라오는 소리를 들으셨죠? 저는 당신과 단둘이 이야기하려고 며칠씩이나 기다렸어요—세상에 하나뿐인 친구를 저 때문에 애태우고 겁먹은 채로 두고 왔고요—다시 정신병원에 갇힐 위험까지 무릅썼어요—그게 다 당신을 위해서예요, 페얼리 양, 다 당신을 위해서요.’ 그 말이 나를 놀라게 했어, 매리언. 그런데도 그 말투에는 무언가가 있어서 나는 온 마음으로 그 여자가 가엾어졌어. 내 연민이 진심이었던 게 분명해. 그 덕분에 나는 그 가엾은 사람에게 안으로 들어와 보트 창고에 내 옆에 앉으라고 청할 만큼 대담해졌으니까.”\n\n“그 여자가 그렇게 했니?”\n\n“아니. 고개를 젓더니, 아무도 우리를 불쑥 덮치지 못하도록 살피고 귀 기울여야 하니 그 자리에 있어야 한다고 했어. 그러고는 처음부터 끝까지 입구에 서서 양쪽에 한 손씩 짚은 채, 어떤 때는 불쑥 몸을 안으로 기울여 내게 말하고, 어떤 때는 불쑥 물러나 사방을 둘러보았지. ‘어제도 여기 왔었어요.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘어두워지기 전에요. 그리고 당신하고 같이 있던 부인이 이야기하는 걸 들었어요. 당신이 남편에 대해 그분에게 이야기하는 걸 들었어요. 남편이 당신 말을 믿게 할 힘도 없고 입을 다물게 할 힘도 없다고 하는 걸 들었어요. 아! 저는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았어요—듣고 있는 동안 제 양심이 알려 주었어요. 왜 제가 당신을 그 사람과 결혼하게 내버려 뒀을까! 아, 내 두려움—내 미치고 비참하고 사악한 두려움!’ 그 여자는 낡고 해진 숄로 얼굴을 가리고, 그 뒤에서 신음하며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸어. 나는 그 여자도 나도 감당할 수 없는 무서운 절망에 빠져 버릴까 봐 겁이 나기 시작했어. ‘진정하려고 해 봐요.’ 내가 말했어. ‘제 결혼을 어떻게 막을 수 있었다는 건지 말해 봐요.’ 그 여자는 얼굴에서 숄을 걷고 멍하니 나를 바라보았어. ‘리머리지에 머무를 만한 담력이 있었어야 했어요.’ 그 여자가 대답했어. ‘그 사람이 거기 온다는 소식에 겁먹고 달아나지 말았어야 했어요. 너무 늦기 전에 당신에게 경고하고 당신을 구했어야 했어요. 왜 나는 그 편지 한 통 쓸 용기밖에 없었을까? 왜 나는 좋은 일을 하고 싶었고 하려고 했으면서 해만 끼쳤을까? 아, 내 두려움—내 미치고 비참하고 사악한 두려움!’ 그 여자는 그 말을 다시 되풀이하고, 다시 낡고 해진 숄 자락에 얼굴을 묻었어. 그 모습을 보는 것도 그 소리를 듣는 것도 끔찍했어.”\n\n“분명히 물어봤겠지, 로라. 그 여자가 그토록 간절하게 되뇌던 두려움이 무엇이었는지?”\n\n“응, 물어봤어.”\n\n“그래서 뭐라고 했어?”\n\n“그 여자는 되레 나에게 물었어. 자기를 정신병원에 가둔 남자, 할 수만 있다면 다시 가둘 남자를 두려워하지 않겠느냐고. 내가 말했지. ‘지금도 두려우세요? 정말 두렵다면 여기 오지도 않으셨을 텐데요.’ ‘아니요.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘지금은 두렵지 않아요.’ 나는 왜 그런지 물었어. 그 여자는 갑자기 보트 창고 안으로 몸을 기울이며 말했어. ‘왜인지 짐작 못 하시겠어요?’ 나는 고개를 저었어. ‘저를 보세요.’ 그 여자가 말을 이었어. 나는 당신이 몹시 슬퍼 보이고 몹시 아파 보여서 마음이 아프다고 했어. 그 여자가 처음으로 미소를 지었어. ‘아프다고요?’ 그 여자가 되뇌었어. ‘저는 죽어 가고 있어요. 이제 제가 왜 그 사람을 두려워하지 않는지 아시겠죠. 제가 천국에서 당신 어머니를 만날 수 있을까요? 만난다면 저를 용서해 주실까요?’ 나는 어찌나 충격을 받고 놀랐던지 아무 대답도 할 수 없었어. ‘줄곧 그 생각을 했어요.’ 그 여자가 말을 이었어. ‘당신 남편을 피해 숨어 지내는 동안 내내, 병들어 누워 있는 동안 내내요. 그 생각이 저를 여기까지 몰고 왔어요—저는 속죄하고 싶어요—제가 예전에 끼친 해를 할 수 있는 데까지 되돌리고 싶어요.’ 나는 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 되도록 간절히 물었어. 그 여자는 여전히 초점 없는 눈으로 나를 바라보고만 있었어. ‘내가 그 해를 되돌릴 수 있을까?’ 그 여자는 미심쩍은 듯 혼잣말을 했어. ‘당신에게는 편들어 줄 친구들이 있지요. 당신이 그 사람의 비밀을 안다면 그 사람은 당신을 두려워할 테고, 저를 다루던 대로 당신을 다루지는 못할 거예요. 당신과 당신 친구들이 두렵다면, 그 사람은 자기 자신을 위해서라도 당신을 너그럽게 대해야 할 테지요. 그리고 그 사람이 당신을 너그럽게 대하고, 그게 내가 한 일이었다고 말할 수 있다면——’ 나는 더 들으려고 귀를 곤두세웠지만, 그 여자는 거기서 말을 멈추었어.”\n\n“말을 더 하게 하려고 애써 봤겠지?”\n\n“해 보았어. 하지만 그이는 또다시 내게서 몸을 빼더니 얼굴과 두 팔을 보트 창고 벽에 기댔어. ‘아!’ 하고 그이가 말하는 소리가 들렸는데, 목소리에 무섭도록 넋 나간 애틋함이 서려 있었어. ‘아! 내가 당신 어머니 곁에 묻힐 수만 있다면! 천사의 나팔이 울리고 무덤들이 부활의 죽은 자들을 내어 놓을 때 그분 곁에서 깨어날 수만 있다면!’—매리언, 나는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었어. 그 말을 듣는 게 끔찍했어. ‘하지만 그럴 가망은 없어요.’ 그이가 조금 몸을 움직여 다시 나를 볼 수 있게 하면서 말했어. ‘나 같은 가엾은 타관 사람에게는 아무 가망도 없지요. 나는 내 손으로 씻어서 그분을 위해 그토록 희고 정갈하게 만들어 놓은 그 대리석 십자가 아래에 눕지 못할 거예요. 아, 안 돼요! 아, 안 돼요! 사람의 자비가 아니라 하느님의 자비가 나를 그분께 데려다줄 거예요. 악한 자들이 괴롭히기를 그치고 지친 자들이 안식을 얻는 그곳으로요.’ 그이는 그 말을 조용히, 슬프게, 무겁고 절망적인 한숨과 함께 내뱉고는 잠시 잠자코 있었어. 그이의 얼굴은 혼란스럽고 어지러워 보였고, 무언가를 생각하는지 생각해 내려 애쓰는지 알 수 없었어. ‘내가 방금 무슨 말을 했지요?’ 한참 만에 그이가 물었어. ‘당신 어머니가 마음에 떠오르면 다른 건 전부 사라져 버려요. 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었지요? 무슨 말을 하고 있었지요?’ 나는 그 가엾은 사람에게 될 수 있는 대로 다정하고 조심스럽게 일러 주었어. ‘아, 그래요, 그래요.’ 그이는 여전히 멍하고 어리둥절한 태도로 말했어. ‘당신은 그 못된 남편 앞에서 아무 힘이 없지요. 그래요. 그러니 나는 여기 온 목적을 이뤄야 해요—더 좋은 때에 털어놓기를 두려워했던 것을 당신에게 갚아야 해요.’ ‘내게 하려는 말이 대체 뭐지요?’ 하고 내가 물었어. ‘당신의 잔인한 남편이 두려워하는 그 비밀이지요.’ 그이가 대답했어. ‘나는 한 번 그 비밀로 그 사람을 위협해서 겁을 준 적이 있어요. 당신도 그 비밀로 그를 위협해서 겁을 주게 될 거예요.’ 그이의 얼굴이 어두워지더니 딱딱하고 성난 응시가 두 눈에 박혔어. 그이는 멍하니 뜻 모를 몸짓으로 내게 손을 흔들기 시작했어. ‘우리 어머니가 그 비밀을 알아요.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘우리 어머니는 그 비밀 밑에서 한평생의 절반을 시들어 버렸어요. 내가 다 자란 어느 날, 어머니가 나에게 무슨 말을 했어요. 그리고 그 이튿날 당신 남편이——’”\n\n“그래! 그래! 계속해 봐. 그 여자가 네 남편에 대해 무슨 말을 했다는 거야?”\n\n“매리언, 바로 그 대목에서 그이가 또 말을 멈췄어——”\n\n“그러고는 더 아무 말도 안 했고?”\n\n“그러고는 열심히 귀를 기울였어. ‘쉿!’ 그이는 여전히 내게 손을 흔들며 속삭였어. ‘쉿!’ 그러고는 문간에서 옆으로 비켜서더니 천천히, 살금살금, 한 걸음씩 물러나서 마침내 보트 창고 모서리 너머로 내 눈에서 사라져 버렸어.”\n\n“설마 뒤를 따라가지 않은 건 아니겠지?”\n\n“따라갔어. 조바심이 나서 일어나 뒤를 밟을 만큼은 대담해졌으니까. 입구에 막 다다랐을 때 그이가 보트 창고 옆쪽에서 갑자기 다시 나타났어. ‘그 비밀요.’ 나는 그이에게 속삭였어. ‘기다렸다가 그 비밀을 말해 줘요!’ 그이는 내 팔을 붙잡고 겁에 질린 사나운 눈으로 나를 보았어. ‘지금은 안 돼요.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘우리는 둘만 있는 게 아니에요—누가 지켜보고 있어요. 내일 이 시각에 여기로 와요—혼자서—명심해요—혼자서.’ 그이는 나를 보트 창고 안으로 거칠게 밀어 넣었고, 그 뒤로는 그이를 보지 못했어.”\n\n“아, 로라, 로라, 또 한 번 기회를 놓쳤구나! 내가 네 곁에만 있었더라면 그 여자가 우리를 빠져나가지는 못했을 텐데. 어느 쪽으로 사라지는 걸 봤어?”\n\n“왼쪽이었어. 땅이 꺼지고 숲이 제일 우거진 쪽.”\n\n“다시 뛰어나가 봤어? 뒤에다 대고 불러 봤고?”\n\n“어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠어? 너무 무서워서 움직이지도 말하지도 못했는걸.”\n\n“그래도 움직이기는 움직였을 때—밖으로 나왔을 때는?”\n\n“여기로 달려왔지, 무슨 일이 있었는지 네게 말하려고.”\n\n“나무숲에서 누구를 보거나 누구 소리를 듣지는 않았어?”\n\n“아니, 지나올 때는 온통 고요하고 조용하기만 한 것 같았어.”\n\n나는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그 만남 자리에 몰래 있었다는 제삼자는 실재하는 사람이었을까, 아니면 앤 캐서릭의 흥분한 상상이 지어낸 존재였을까? 판단할 길이 없었다. 확실한 것은 단 하나, 우리가 발견의 문턱에서 또 한 번 실패했다는 것—앤 캐서릭이 이튿날 보트 창고의 약속을 지키지 않는다면 철저히, 돌이킬 수 없이 실패했다는 것이었다.\n\n“네가 겪은 일을 하나도 빠짐없이 말해 준 게 확실해? 오간 말 한마디 한마디까지?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“그런 것 같아.” 로라가 대답했다. “매리언, 내 기억력은 네 것 같지 않잖아. 하지만 워낙 강한 인상을 받았고 워낙 깊이 마음이 쏠려 있었으니, 조금이라도 중요한 건 놓쳤을 리 없어.”\n\n“얘 로라, 앤 캐서릭에 관한 일이라면 아무리 하찮은 것도 다 중요해. 다시 생각해 봐. 그 여자가 지금 어디에 묵고 있는지 지나가는 말로라도 흘리지 않았어?”\n\n“기억나는 건 없어.”\n\n“동행이자 친구인 여자, 클레멘츠 부인이라는 사람 얘기는 안 했어?”\n\n“아, 맞아! 맞아! 그걸 잊고 있었네. 클레멘츠 부인이 호수까지 같이 가서 자기를 돌봐 주고 싶어 몹시 안달했다고, 이 근방에 혼자 들어가지 말라고 사정하고 빌었다고 했어.”\n\n“클레멘츠 부인에 대해 한 말이 그게 전부야?”\n\n“응, 그게 전부야.”\n\n“토드 코너를 떠난 뒤에 어디로 몸을 피했는지는 아무 말도 안 했고?”\n\n“아무 말도 안 했어. 확실해.”\n\n“그 뒤로 어디서 살았는지도? 무슨 병을 앓았는지도?”\n\n“아니, 매리언, 한마디도. 말해 줘, 제발 말해 줘, 넌 이 일을 어떻게 생각하는지. 나는 어떻게 생각해야 할지, 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 모르겠어.”\n\n“이렇게 해야 해, 얘. 내일 보트 창고의 약속을 꼭 지켜야 해. 그 여자를 다시 만나는 데 얼마나 큰 이해가 걸려 있을지는 아무도 모르는 일이야. 두 번은 너를 혼자 두지 않을 거야. 안전한 거리를 두고 뒤를 따라갈게. 아무도 나를 보지 못하겠지만, 무슨 일이 생기면 네 목소리가 들리는 데까지는 붙어 있을 거야. 앤 캐서릭은 월터 하트라이트에게서 달아났고 너에게서도 달아났어. 무슨 일이 있어도 나에게서는 달아나지 못해.”\n\n로라의 눈이 내 눈을 유심히 읽었다.\n\n“너는 믿는 거지?” 로라가 말했다. “내 남편이 두려워한다는 그 비밀을 말이야. 매리언, 만약 그게 결국 앤 캐서릭의 공상 속에만 있는 거라면? 만약 그 여자가 옛 기억 때문에 그저 나를 보고 나와 이야기하고 싶었을 뿐이라면? 태도가 어찌나 이상하던지—나는 그 여자를 거의 믿지 못할 뻔했어. 넌 다른 일에서도 그 여자를 믿겠어?”\n\n“로라, 나는 아무것도 믿지 않아. 네 남편의 행실을 내 눈으로 지켜본 것만 믿을 뿐이야. 나는 앤 캐서릭의 말을 그 사람의 행동으로 판단하고, 그래서 비밀이 있다고 믿는 거야.”\n\n나는 더 말하지 않고 일어나 방을 나섰다. 우리가 더 오래 이야기했더라면 로라에게 털어놓았을지도 모를, 그러나 그 애가 알기에는 위험했을 생각들이 나를 괴롭히고 있었다. 나를 깨워 놓은 그 무서운 꿈의 여운이, 그 애의 이야기가 진행되면서 내 마음에 새로 찍히는 인상마다 어둡고 무겁게 드리웠다. 나는 불길한 미래가 바짝 다가오는 것을 느꼈다. 그것은 말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움으로 나를 서늘하게 했고, 이제 우리를 옭아맨 그 길고 긴 뒤얽힘의 연쇄 속에 보이지 않는 어떤 설계가 있다는 확신을 내게 억지로 심어 주었다. 나는 하트라이트를 떠올렸다—작별을 고하던 날 몸으로 마주 보았던 그를, 그리고 꿈속에서 혼으로 보았던 그를—그리고 나 역시 우리가 눈을 가린 채 정해진, 피할 수 없는 결말을 향해 나아가고 있는 것은 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다.\n\n로라를 혼자 위층으로 올려 보내고, 나는 집 근처 산책로를 둘러보러 나갔다. 앤 캐서릭이 그 애와 헤어진 정황 때문에 나는 포스코 백작이 오후를 어떻게 보내고 있는지 남몰래 알고 싶어졌고, 퍼시벌 경이 몇 시간 전에 돌아온 그 홀로 다녀온 여행의 결과가 남몰래 미덥지 않았다.\n\n사방을 다 찾아보았으나 아무것도 알아내지 못하고, 나는 집으로 돌아와 아래층 방들을 하나씩 차례로 들여다보았다. 모두 비어 있었다. 나는 다시 현관홀로 나와 로라에게 돌아가려고 위층으로 올라갔다. 복도를 지나는데 포스코 부인이 문을 열었기에, 나는 걸음을 멈추고 남편과 퍼시벌 경의 행방을 아는지 물어보았다. 그렇다, 부인은 한 시간도 더 전에 창문으로 두 사람을 다 보았다고 했다. 백작은 여느 때처럼 다정하게 위를 올려다보았고, 아무리 사소한 일에도 아내를 배려하는 그 습관대로, 자기와 친구가 함께 긴 산책을 나간다고 말해 두었다는 것이었다.\n\n긴 산책이라니! 내가 두 사람을 겪어 본 바로는 그런 목적으로 함께 있어 본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 퍼시벌 경은 승마 말고는 어떤 운동에도 관심이 없었고, 백작은 (친절을 베풀어 내 길동무가 되어 줄 때를 빼면) 어떤 운동에도 도무지 관심이 없었다.\n\n다시 로라에게 갔을 때, 나는 그 애가 내가 없는 동안 눈앞에 닥친 증서 서명 문제를 떠올렸다는 것을 알았다. 앤 캐서릭과의 만남을 이야기하는 데 정신이 팔려 우리가 여태 잊고 있던 문제였다. 나를 보자마자 그 애가 꺼낸 첫마디는, 서재로 퍼시벌 경을 만나러 오라는 예상된 호출이 없다는 데 대한 놀라움이었다.\n\n“그 문제라면 마음을 놓아도 돼.” 내가 말했다. “적어도 당분간은 네 결심도 내 결심도 더 시험받을 일이 없을 거야. 퍼시벌 경이 계획을 바꿨어—서명 건은 미뤄졌어.”\n\n“미뤄졌다고?” 로라가 놀라며 되뇌었다. “누가 그래?”\n\n“포스코 백작이 알려 줬어. 네 남편이 갑자기 뜻을 바꾼 건 그 사람이 끼어든 덕분이라고 나는 생각해.”\n\n“그럴 리가 없어, 매리언. 내가 서명하는 목적이 우리 짐작대로 퍼시벌 경이 급히 필요로 하는 돈을 마련하는 거라면, 어떻게 그 일을 미룰 수 있겠어?”\n\n“로라, 그 의문을 가라앉힐 방법이 바로 가까이에 있는 것 같은데. 퍼시벌 경과 그 변호사가 현관홀을 가로질러 갈 때 내가 들은 대화를 잊었어?”\n\n“아니, 하지만 기억이 안 나——”\n\n“나는 기억해. 두 가지 방안이 나왔어. 하나는 네 서명을 그 양피지 문서에 받아 내는 것. 다른 하나는 석 달 만기 어음을 끊어서 시간을 버는 것. 마지막 수단이 지금 채택된 것이 분명해. 그러니 당분간은 퍼시벌 경의 곤경에서 우리 몫을 덜게 되리라고 기대해도 좋을 거야.”\n\n“아, 매리언, 너무 좋아서 참말 같지가 않아!”\n\n“그래? 얘, 얼마 전에는 내 기억력이 빠르다고 칭찬해 놓고 지금은 그걸 의심하는 것 같구나. 내 일기를 가져올 테니, 내 말이 맞는지 틀리는지 네가 직접 봐.”\n\n나는 곧바로 가서 일기장을 가져왔다.\n\n변호사의 방문에 관한 기록을 되짚어 보니, 제시된 두 방안에 대한 내 기억이 정확히 옳았음이 드러났다. 이번에도 내 기억이 여느 때처럼 충실하게 제 몫을 해 주었다는 사실은 로라에게 못지않게 내 마음에도 큰 위안이었다. 지금 우리가 처한 위태로운 불확실 속에서, 앞으로 어떤 이해가 내 일기 기록의 규칙성에, 그리고 그것을 적을 당시 내 기억의 신뢰성에 달려 있게 될지 누가 알겠는가.\n\n로라의 얼굴과 태도를 보니 이 마지막 생각이 나뿐 아니라 그 애에게도 떠오른 모양이었다. 어차피 사소한 일이고, 여기 이렇게 적어 두기가 부끄러울 지경이다—우리 처지의 막막함을 너무나도 비참하도록 또렷하게 비춰 보이는 것 같아서. 내 기억이 아직 우리를 위해 제 몫을 하리라는 발견이 마치 새 친구를 얻은 발견인 양 반가운 판이니, 우리에게 기댈 것이 얼마나 없는 셈인가!\n\n저녁 식사를 알리는 첫 종이 우리를 갈라놓았다. 종소리가 막 그쳤을 때 퍼시벌 경과 백작이 산책에서 돌아왔다. 이 집 주인이 오 분 늦었다고 하인들에게 호통치는 소리와, 주인의 손님이 여느 때처럼 예의와 인내와 평화를 위하여 끼어드는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n저녁이 왔다가 갔다. 별다른 일은 일어나지 않았다. 그러나 나는 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 행동에서 어떤 기이한 점들을 알아챘고, 그 때문에 앤 캐서릭에 대해서도 내일이 빚어낼 결과에 대해서도 몹시 불안하고 마음이 놓이지 않는 채로 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n이제 나는 퍼시벌 경의 여러 얼굴 가운데 가장 거짓된 얼굴, 따라서 가장 나쁜 것을 뜻하는 얼굴이 바로 정중한 얼굴이라는 것을 충분히 알 만큼은 안다. 친구와 함께한 그 긴 산책은 그의 태도를 좋게 만들어 놓는 것으로 끝났고, 특히 아내를 대하는 태도가 그러했다. 로라를 남몰래 놀라게 하고 나를 남몰래 불안하게 하도록, 그는 아내를 세례명으로 불렀고, 요즘 삼촌에게서 소식을 들었는지 물었으며, 베지 부인이 블랙워터로 오라는 초대를 언제 받게 되느냐고 묻고, 그 밖에도 자잘한 배려를 어찌나 많이 보이던지 리머리지 하우스에서의 그 밉살스러운 구애 시절이 거의 되살아날 지경이었다. 시작부터 나쁜 징조였다. 게다가 저녁 식사 뒤에 응접실에서 잠든 척하면서, 우리 둘 다 자기를 의심하지 않는다고 여길 때면 로라와 나를 교활하게 눈으로 좇는 것을 보니 더욱 불길하게 느껴졌다. 그가 혼자 훌쩍 떠난 그 여행이 웰밍엄으로 캐서릭 부인을 캐물으러 간 것임을 나는 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없다—그러나 오늘 밤의 일로 나는 그 원정이 헛되지 않았을까 봐, 그가 캐내러 간 것이 분명한 그 정보를 손에 넣었을까 봐 두려워졌다. 앤 캐서릭이 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면 나는 내일 해가 뜰 때 일어나 그 여자에게 경고하러 갈 것이다.\n\n오늘 밤 퍼시벌 경이 내보인 얼굴은 유감스럽게도 내게 너무나 익숙한 것이었던 반면, 백작이 내보인 얼굴은 그를 겪어 온 내 경험에 비추어 전혀 새로운 것이었다. 그는 오늘 저녁 처음으로 나에게 감상(感傷)의 인간이라는 모습으로 자기와 알고 지낼 기회를 주었다—그것도 내가 보기에는 이 자리를 위해 꾸며 낸 것이 아니라 정말로 느끼는 감상이었다.\n\n이를테면 그는 조용하고 차분했고, 그의 눈과 목소리는 절제된 감수성을 드러냈다. 그는 (가장 화려한 옷치레와 가장 깊은 감정 사이에 무슨 숨은 연관이라도 있는 것처럼) 여태 입고 나온 것 중 가장 근사한 조끼를 입고 있었다—연한 바다빛 초록 비단으로 지어 고운 은색 끈으로 섬세하게 장식한 것이었다. 로라나 나에게 말할 때면 그의 목소리는 더없이 부드러운 억양으로 내려앉았고, 그의 미소는 사려 깊고 자애로운 감탄을 드러냈다. 그는 저녁 식탁에서 사소한 배려에 아내가 고마워하자 식탁 밑으로 그 손을 지그시 눌러 주었다. 그는 아내와 잔을 나누었다. “당신의 건강과 행복을 위하여, 나의 천사!” 그가 애정으로 반짝이는 눈을 하고 말했다. 그는 거의 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 한숨을 내쉬었으며, 친구가 자기를 비웃자 “착한 퍼시벌!” 하고 말했다. 식사가 끝난 뒤 그는 로라의 손을 잡고 “친절을 베풀어 자기를 위해 한 곡 쳐 줄” 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그 애는 순전히 놀란 나머지 그러겠다고 했다. 그는 피아노 옆에 앉았는데, 시곗줄이 바다빛 초록 조끼의 불룩한 배 위에 황금 뱀처럼 겹겹이 늘어져 있었다. 그의 거대한 머리는 나른하게 한쪽으로 기울었고, 그는 누르스름하고 흰 손가락 두 개로 가만가만 박자를 맞추었다. 그는 그 곡을 크게 칭찬했고 로라의 연주 솜씨를 애틋하게 상찬했다—가엾은 하트라이트가 고운 소리를 천진하게 즐기며 칭찬하던 식이 아니라, 우선은 그 곡의 됨됨이에 대해, 다음으로는 연주자의 터치가 지닌 됨됨이에 대해 또렷하고 교양 있고 실질적인 지식을 갖춘 사람의 칭찬이었다. 저녁이 저물어 갈 무렵 그는 이 사랑스럽게 스러지는 빛이 아직은 등불 따위로 더럽혀지지 않게 해 달라고 청했다. 그러고는 그 소름 끼치도록 소리 없는 걸음으로, 그를 피해 그의 모습조차 보지 않으려고 내가 서 있던 저쪽 끝 창가로 다가왔다—등불에 반대하는 자기 항의에 힘을 보태 달라고 청하려고 온 것이다. 그 등불 가운데 하나가 그 순간 그를 태워 없앨 수만 있었다면, 나는 부엌으로 내려가 그것을 손수 가져왔을 것이다.\n\n“이 수줍고 떨리는 영국의 황혼이 마음에 드시지요?” 그가 부드럽게 말했다. “아! 나는 이것을 사랑합니다. 이런 저녁이면 고귀하고 위대하고 선한 모든 것에 대한 내 타고난 감탄이 하늘의 숨결로 정화되는 것을 느낍니다. 자연은 나에게 이토록 스러지지 않는 매력을, 이토록 꺼지지 않는 애정을 지니고 있지요!—나는 늙고 뚱뚱한 사내입니다—홀콤 양의 입술에서 나오면 어울렸을 이런 말이 내 입에서 나오면 조롱이나 익살처럼 들리지요. 내 감상의 순간에 웃음거리가 되는 것은 괴로운 일입니다. 마치 내 영혼도 나처럼 늙고 살이 쪄 버렸다는 듯이 말이지요. 보십시오, 친애하는 아가씨, 저 나무들 위로 어떤 빛이 스러져 가는지! 저 빛이 내 가슴을 파고들듯 당신의 가슴도 파고듭니까?”\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 나를 바라보더니, 저녁 무렵을 노래한 단테의 그 유명한 시구를 읊었다. 그 가락과 애틋함은 견줄 데 없이 아름다운 그 시 자체에 그만의 매력을 한 겹 더해 주었다.\n\n“쳇!” 그 고귀한 이탈리아어 낱말들의 마지막 여운이 입술에서 사라지자마자 그가 불쑥 외쳤다. “내가 늙은 바보 짓을 하고 있군요. 여러분을 지루하게만 하고 있어요! 우리 가슴속의 창문은 닫아 두고 사실의 세계로 돌아갑시다. 퍼시벌! 등불을 들이는 것을 내가 허락하겠네. 글라이드 부인—홀콤 양—엘리너, 나의 좋은 아내—누가 나와 도미노 한 판 해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그는 우리 모두에게 말을 걸었지만, 특히 로라를 바라보았다.\n\n그 애는 내가 그의 비위를 거스를까 봐 두려워한다는 것을 느끼는 법을 익혀 온 터라 그의 제안을 받아들였다. 그 순간의 나로서는 도저히 할 수 없는 일이었다. 무슨 일이 있어도 나는 그와 같은 탁자에 앉을 수 없었다. 그의 눈은 짙어 가는 황혼의 어둠을 뚫고 내 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳까지 닿는 것 같았다. 그의 목소리는 내 몸의 모든 신경을 따라 떨렸고 나를 번갈아 뜨겁게 또 차갑게 만들었다. 저녁 내내 틈틈이 나를 따라다니던 그 꿈의 신비와 공포가 이제는 견딜 수 없는 예감과 말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움으로 내 마음을 짓눌렀다. 나는 그 흰 무덤을, 그리고 하트라이트 곁에서 그 속에서 일어서던 베일 쓴 여인을 다시 보았다. 로라 생각이 내 마음 깊은 곳에서 샘물처럼 솟아올라, 이제껏 한 번도 알지 못했던 쓰디쓴 물로 내 마음을 가득 채웠다. 그 애가 탁자로 가느라 내 곁을 지날 때 나는 그 손을 붙잡고, 그날 밤이 우리를 영영 갈라놓기라도 할 것처럼 입을 맞추었다. 모두가 놀라 나를 바라보는 사이, 나는 눈앞에 땅까지 트여 있던 낮은 창문을 통해 뛰쳐나갔다—그들에게서, 나 자신에게서까지 숨으려고 어둠 속으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n그날 저녁 우리는 여느 때보다 늦게 헤어졌다. 자정 무렵 여름의 정적이 나무들 사이로 낮고 구슬픈 바람이 몸서리치듯 지나가는 소리에 깨졌다. 우리는 모두 대기가 갑자기 서늘해진 것을 느꼈으나, 바람이 슬그머니 일어나는 것을 맨 먼저 알아챈 사람은 백작이었다. 그는 나를 위해 초에 불을 붙여 주다 말고 멈추더니 경고하듯 손을 들어 올렸다—\n\n“들어 보십시오!” 그가 말했다. “내일은 달라질 겁니다.”\n\n# VII\n\n6월 19일.—어제의 사건들은 내게 머지않아 최악의 사태를 맞을 각오를 하라고 경고했다. 오늘은 아직 끝나지도 않았는데, 최악은 이미 닥쳐 버렸다.\n\n로라와 내가 최대한 정밀하게 시각을 따져 본 끝에, 우리는 앤 캐서릭이 어제 오후 두 시 반에 보트 창고에 나타났음이 틀림없다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그래서 나는 오늘 로라가 점심 식탁에 잠깐 얼굴만 비쳤다가 첫 기회에 슬쩍 빠져나가고, 나는 남아서 겉모양을 지키다가 안전해지는 대로 뒤따라가기로 계획을 세웠다. 이렇게 하면 우리를 가로막을 장애가 생기지 않는 한 로라는 두 시 반 전에 보트 창고에 닿을 수 있고, (내 차례로 식탁을 뜨면) 나는 세 시 전에 잡목림 안의 안전한 자리에 이를 수 있을 터였다.\n\n어젯밤 바람이 예고했던 날씨의 변화는 아침과 함께 찾아왔다. 내가 일어났을 때 비가 억수같이 쏟아지고 있었고, 열두 시까지 계속 내리다가—구름이 흩어지고 푸른 하늘이 드러나며 해가 다시 비쳐 맑은 오후를 환하게 약속했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경과 백작이 낮의 앞부분을 어떻게 보낼지 알고 싶던 내 조바심은, 적어도 퍼시벌 경에 관한 한, 그가 아침 식사 직후 비를 무릅쓰고 혼자 나가 버리는 바람에 조금도 가라앉지 않았다. 그는 어디로 가는지도, 언제 돌아올지도 우리에게 말하지 않았다. 우리는 그가 목이 높은 장화에 방수 외투를 걸치고 아침 식사실 창 앞을 총총히 지나가는 것을 보았을 뿐, 그것이 전부였다.\n\n백작은 오전을 실내에서 조용히 보냈는데, 얼마간은 서재에서, 얼마간은 응접실에서 피아노로 이런저런 곡조각을 치며 혼자 흥얼거렸다. 겉으로 보아서는 그의 성격 중 감상적인 면이 여전히 집요하게 드러나려 하고 있었다. 그는 말이 없고 예민했으며, 아주 사소한 자극에도 (오직 뚱뚱한 남자만이 할 수 있는 방식으로) 묵직하게 한숨짓고 나른하게 시들어 보일 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n점심시간이 되었으나 퍼시벌 경은 돌아오지 않았다. 백작은 식탁에서 친구의 자리를 차지하고는, 크림 한 주전자를 통째로 부어 잠기게 한 과일 타르트를 구슬픈 표정으로 태반이나 먹어 치우고, 다 먹자마자 그 위업의 온전한 가치를 우리에게 설명했다. “단것을 좋아하는 취향은,” 그가 더할 나위 없이 부드러운 어조와 다정한 태도로 말했다. “여자와 아이의 순진무구한 취향이지요. 나는 그분들과 그 취향을 함께 나누는 것이 좋습니다—그것 또한 하나의 끈이니까요, 친애하는 부인들, 여러분과 나 사이를 잇는.”\n\n로라는 십 분 만에 식탁을 떴다. 나도 따라가고 싶은 마음이 간절했다. 그러나 우리 둘이 함께 나갔다면 틀림없이 의심을 샀을 테고, 더 나쁘게는, 앤 캐서릭에게 낯선 제삼자를 데리고 온 로라의 모습을 보이게 했다면 우리는 십중팔구 그 순간부터 그 여자의 신뢰를 잃고 다시는 되찾지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그래서 나는 하인이 식탁을 치우러 들어올 때까지 되도록 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 방을 나설 때 집 안에도 밖에도 퍼시벌 경이 돌아온 기색은 없었다. 나는 백작을 남겨 두고 나왔다. 그는 입술 사이에 설탕 조각을 물고 있었고, 그 심술궂은 앵무새는 그것을 차지하려고 그의 조끼를 기어오르고 있었으며, 남편 맞은편에 앉은 포스코 백작부인은 평생 그런 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없는 사람처럼 새와 남편의 거동을 골똘히 지켜보고 있었다. 잡목림으로 가는 길에 나는 점심 식사실 창에서 보이는 범위를 조심스럽게 피해 다녔다. 아무도 나를 보지 않았고 아무도 나를 따라오지 않았다. 내 시계로 그때가 세 시 십오 분 전이었다.\n\n나무들 사이에 들어서자 나는 잡목림의 절반을 넘게 나아갈 때까지 빠르게 걸었다. 그 지점에서 걸음을 늦추고 조심스럽게 나아갔으나, 아무도 보이지 않았고 사람 목소리도 들리지 않았다. 조금씩 보트 창고 뒤편이 눈에 들어왔고—나는 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다—그러고는 계속 나아가 바로 그 뒤까지 다가갔으니, 안에서 이야기하는 사람이 있었다면 반드시 들렸을 것이다. 그래도 침묵은 깨지지 않았고, 멀리서도 가까이서도 살아 있는 것의 기척은 어디에도 나타나지 않았다.\n\n건물 뒤편을 이쪽저쪽으로 돌아가며 살폈지만 아무것도 발견하지 못하자, 나는 큰맘 먹고 앞으로 나가 안을 정면으로 들여다보았다. 안은 비어 있었다.\n\n나는 “로라!” 하고 불렀다—처음에는 나직하게, 그다음에는 점점 더 크게. 아무도 대답하지 않았고 아무도 나타나지 않았다. 내가 보고 들을 수 있는 한, 호수와 잡목림 근처에 있는 유일한 사람은 나뿐이었다.\n\n심장이 세차게 뛰기 시작했으나 나는 결심을 지켰고, 먼저 보트 창고 안을, 그다음 그 앞의 땅을 뒤지며 로라가 정말로 이곳에 닿았는지 알려 줄 만한 자취를 찾았다. 건물 안에는 그녀가 다녀간 표시가 없었지만, 밖에서는 모래에 찍힌 발자국으로 그 흔적을 찾아냈다.\n\n나는 두 사람의 발자국을 찾아냈다—남자 것처럼 큰 발자국과, 내 발을 직접 대어 크기를 재어 본 결과 로라의 것이 틀림없다고 확신하게 된 작은 발자국이었다. 보트 창고 바로 앞의 땅이 그런 식으로 어지럽게 밟혀 있었다. 그 한쪽 옆, 튀어나온 지붕 아래 가려진 자리에서 나는 모래에 파인 작은 구덩이를 발견했다—의심할 여지 없이 사람이 판 구덩이였다. 나는 그것을 눈여겨보기만 하고 곧바로 돌아서서, 발자국을 갈 수 있는 데까지 좇으며 그것이 나를 어디로 이끄는지 따라가 보기로 했다.\n\n발자국은 보트 창고 왼편에서 시작해 나무들 가장자리를 따라, 내 짐작으로 이삼백 야드쯤 나를 데려갔고, 그 뒤로는 모래땅에 더 이상 자취가 남아 있지 않았다. 내가 뒤쫓던 사람들이 이 지점에서 잡목림으로 들어갔음이 틀림없다고 느낀 나도 그 안으로 들어섰다. 처음에는 길을 찾을 수 없었지만 곧 나무들 사이로 희미하게 나 있는 길 하나를 찾아내어 그것을 따라갔다. 그 길은 얼마 동안 마을 쪽으로 나를 데려갔고, 나는 또 다른 발길 자국이 그 길과 엇갈리는 지점에서 멈춰 섰다. 이 두 번째 길의 양옆으로는 가시덤불이 무성하게 자라 있었다. 다음에 어느 쪽으로 가야 할지 몰라 그 길을 내려다보며 서 있는데, 가시 돋친 가지 하나에 여자 숄에서 떨어져 나온 술 조각이 걸려 있는 것이 보였다. 그 술을 더 가까이 살펴보니 로라의 숄에서 찢겨 나온 것이 분명했고, 나는 즉시 두 번째 길을 따라갔다. 그 길은 마침내, 참으로 다행스럽게도, 저택 뒤편으로 나를 데려다주었다. 다행스럽다고 말하는 까닭은, 로라가 무슨 까닭인지는 몰라도 이 에움길로 나보다 먼저 돌아갔음이 틀림없다고 짐작했기 때문이다. 나는 안뜰과 부속 건물을 지나 안으로 들어갔다. 하인들이 쓰는 복도를 가로지르다 처음 마주친 사람은 가정부 마이컬슨 부인이었다.\n\n“혹시 아세요,” 내가 물었다. “글라이드 부인께서 산책에서 돌아오셨는지요?”\n\n“마님께서는 조금 전에 퍼시벌 경과 함께 들어오셨습니다.” 가정부가 대답했다. “홀콤 양, 아무래도 몹시 언짢은 일이 있었던 모양입니다.”\n\n가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. “사고가 났다는 말씀은 아니지요?” 나는 힘없이 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 아닙니다—고맙게도 사고는 아닙니다. 하지만 마님께서는 눈물을 흘리며 당신 방으로 뛰어 올라가셨고, 퍼시벌 경께서는 저더러 패니에게 한 시간 안에 나가라고 통고하라 하셨습니다.”\n\n패니는 로라의 몸종이었다—여러 해 동안 그녀를 모셔 온 착하고 정 많은 아이로, 이 집에서 우리 둘이 그 성실함과 헌신을 믿을 수 있는 유일한 사람이었다.\n\n“패니는 어디 있나요?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“제 방에 있습니다, 홀콤 양. 그 아이가 하도 넋을 놓기에 앉아서 마음을 좀 추스르라고 일러 두었습니다.”\n\n나는 마이컬슨 부인의 방으로 갔고, 패니가 구석에 자기 상자를 옆에 둔 채 서럽게 울고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n그 아이는 자기가 왜 갑자기 쫓겨나는지 아무 설명도 해 주지 못했다. 퍼시벌 경은 한 달 전 예고 대신 한 달 치 급료를 주어 내보내라고 명령했다. 이유는 밝혀지지 않았고—그 아이의 처신에 무슨 트집이 잡힌 것도 아니었다. 그 아이는 여주인에게 하소연하는 것도, 작별 인사를 하려고 잠깐 얼굴을 보는 것조차 금지당했다. 설명도 작별도 없이, 그것도 당장 떠나야 했다.\n\n다정한 몇 마디로 가엾은 아이를 달랜 뒤, 나는 그날 밤 어디서 잘 생각이냐고 물었다. 그 아이는 마을의 작은 여관으로 갈까 한다고, 그 집 안주인이 점잖은 여자이고 블랙워터 파크의 하인들도 아는 사람이라고 대답했다. 이튿날 아침 일찍 떠나면, 생판 낯선 런던에 머무르지 않고도 컴벌랜드의 친지들에게 돌아갈 수 있을 터였다.\n\n나는 패니가 떠나는 것이 우리에게 런던 및 리머리지 하우스와 통할 안전한 수단이 되어 주며, 그것을 이용하는 일이 매우 중요할지도 모른다는 것을 곧바로 느꼈다. 그래서 나는 저녁 사이에 여주인이나 나에게서 소식이 있을 것이라고, 그리고 당분간 우리를 떠나는 시련 속에서도 우리 둘이 힘닿는 데까지 그 아이를 도우리라 믿어도 좋다고 말해 주었다. 그 말을 하고 나서 나는 그 아이와 악수하고 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n로라의 방으로 통하는 문은 복도로 열리는 앞방의 문이었다. 열어 보니 안에서 빗장이 걸려 있었다.\n\n내가 두드리자, 다친 개를 발견하던 날 그 굼뜬 무신경으로 내 인내심을 호되게 시험했던 바로 그 크고 미련한 하녀가 문을 열었다.\n\n그 뒤로 나는 그 여자의 이름이 마거릿 포처이며, 이 집에서 가장 굼뜨고 지저분하고 고집 센 하인이라는 것을 알아냈다.\n\n문을 열자 그 여자는 곧바로 문지방으로 나서더니, 멍하니 말없이 히죽거리며 나를 마주 보고 섰다.\n\n“왜 거기 서 있는 거냐?” 내가 말했다. “내가 들어가려는 것이 보이지 않느냐?”\n\n“아, 하지만 들어가시면 안 됩니다.” 그것이 대답이었고, 히죽거림은 한층 더 커졌다.\n\n“네가 감히 나에게 그런 말버릇을 쓰느냐? 당장 비켜서라!”\n\n그 여자는 크고 붉은 손과 팔을 양옆으로 뻗어 문간을 가로막고는, 멍청한 머리를 천천히 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n“주인 나리 분부입니다.” 그 여자는 그렇게 말하고 다시 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n저 여자를 상대로 이 문제를 다투어서는 안 된다고 스스로를 타이르고, 다음에 할 말은 반드시 그 여자의 주인에게 해야 한다는 것을 잊지 않으려면 내 자제력을 남김없이 끌어모아야 했다. 나는 그 여자에게 등을 돌리고 곧장 아래층으로 그를 찾으러 내려갔다. 퍼시벌 경이 어떤 부아를 돋우더라도 성을 내지 않겠다던 내 결심은, 이때쯤에는—부끄럽지만 고백하건대—애초에 그런 결심을 한 적조차 없는 것처럼 깨끗이 잊혀 있었다. 그 집에서 그토록 참고 눌러 온 끝에, 내가 얼마나 화가 났는지를 느끼는 것은—정말이지 속이 후련했다.\n\n응접실과 아침 식사실은 둘 다 비어 있었다. 서재로 가 보니 퍼시벌 경과 백작과 포스코 백작부인이 있었다. 세 사람은 모두 바싹 붙어 서 있었고, 퍼시벌 경은 손에 작은 종잇조각을 들고 있었다. 문을 여는데 백작이 그에게 “안 되오—천 번을 물어도 안 되오.” 하고 말하는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n나는 곧장 그에게 걸어가 정면으로 얼굴을 마주 보았다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 부인의 방이 감옥이고 댁의 하녀가 그 방을 지키는 간수라고 알아들으면 되겠습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“그렇소, 그렇게 알아들으면 되겠소.” 그가 대답했다. “내 간수가 두 몫을 하게 되지 않도록 조심하시오—당신 방도 감옥이 되지 않도록 조심하란 말이오.”\n\n“당신 아내를 어떻게 대하는지, 나를 어떻게 협박하는지 당신이나 조심하시지요.” 나는 치미는 분노에 못 이겨 쏘아붙였다. “영국에는 여자를 학대와 폭력에서 지켜 주는 법이 있습니다. 로라의 머리카락 한 올이라도 다치게 하거나, 감히 내 자유를 침해한다면, 무슨 일이 있어도 나는 그 법에 호소하겠습니다.”\n\n그는 내게 대답하는 대신 백작 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n“내가 뭐라고 했소?” 그가 물었다. “이제 뭐라고 하겠소?”\n\n“전에 한 말 그대로요.” 백작이 대답했다—“안 되오.”\n\n분노가 격렬한 와중에도 나는 그의 차분하고 차가운 잿빛 눈이 내 얼굴에 머무는 것을 느꼈다. 그 눈은 말이 끝나자마자 나를 떠나 의미심장하게 자기 아내를 바라보았다. 포스코 백작부인은 즉시 내 곁으로 바싹 다가섰고, 그 자리에서 우리 둘 중 누구도 다시 입을 열기 전에 퍼시벌 경에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n“잠깐만 제 말에 귀를 기울여 주십시오.” 그녀는 맑고 얼음처럼 억눌린 어조로 말했다. “퍼시벌 경, 베풀어 주신 후대에 감사드리며, 더는 그 호의를 누리지 않겠습니다. 오늘 이 집에서 부인과 홀콤 양이 받은 것과 같은 대우를 숙녀에게 하는 집에 저는 머무르지 않겠습니다!”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 한 걸음 물러서서 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에 그녀를 노려보았다. 방금 들은 선언—나도 잘 알고 그도 잘 알다시피 포스코 백작부인이 남편의 허락 없이는 감히 입에 올리지 못했을 그 선언은, 그를 놀라움으로 굳어 버리게 한 듯했다. 백작은 옆에 서서 더할 나위 없이 열렬한 감탄의 눈으로 아내를 바라보았다.\n\n“숭고하구려!” 그는 혼잣말처럼 말했다. 그렇게 말하며 아내에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼웠다. “나는 당신 뜻대로 하겠소, 엘리너.” 그는 내가 여태 그에게서 본 적 없는 조용한 위엄을 갖추고 말을 이었다. “그리고 홀콤 양의 뜻대로도 하지요, 내가 드릴 수 있는 모든 도움을 받아 주시는 영광을 베푸신다면.”\n\n“빌어먹을! 그게 무슨 소리요?” 백작이 아내와 함께 조용히 문 쪽으로 걸어가자 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다.\n\n“여느 때는 내 말이 곧 내 뜻이지만, 이번만은 내 아내의 말이 곧 내 뜻이오.” 그 속을 알 수 없는 이탈리아인이 대답했다. “이번 한 번은 우리가 자리를 바꾸었소, 퍼시벌. 그리고 포스코 백작부인의 의견이—곧 내 의견이오.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 손에 든 종이를 구겨 쥐고, 또 한 번 욕설을 내뱉으며 백작을 밀치고 지나가 그와 문 사이에 버티고 섰다.\n\n“마음대로 하시오.” 그는 낮게 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리에 좌절한 분노를 담아 말했다. “마음대로 하시오—그리고 그 끝이 어찌 되는지 보시오.” 그 말을 남기고 그는 방을 나갔다.\n\n포스코 백작부인이 남편을 캐묻듯 바라보았다. “아주 갑자기 나가 버리는군요.” 그녀가 말했다. “무슨 뜻일까요?”\n\n“당신과 내가 힘을 합쳐 영국에서 가장 성마른 사내를 제정신으로 돌려놓았다는 뜻이오.” 백작이 대답했다. “홀콤 양, 글라이드 부인은 심한 모욕에서 벗어났고, 당신은 용서할 수 없는 무례를 되풀이해 겪는 일에서 벗어났다는 뜻입니다. 참으로 견디기 힘든 순간에 보여 주신 처신과 용기에 대한 제 감탄을 표하도록 허락해 주십시오.”\n\n“진심 어린 감탄이지요.” 포스코 백작부인이 거들었다.\n\n“진심 어린 감탄.” 백작이 되뇌었다.\n\n나에게는 더 이상 처음의 그 성난 저항으로 모욕과 상처를 버텨 낼 힘이 남아 있지 않았다. 로라를 보고 싶은 병적인 조바심과, 보트 창고에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 모르는 내 무력함이 견딜 수 없는 무게로 나를 짓눌렀다. 나는 백작과 그의 아내가 나에게 쓰기로 한 그 어조에 맞추어 말함으로써 겉모양을 유지하려 애썼지만, 말이 입술에서 나오지 않았다—숨이 짧고 가빠졌고—두 눈은 말없이 문 쪽을 애타게 바라보고 있었다. 백작은 내 조바심을 알아채고 문을 열더니 밖으로 나가 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 바로 그때 퍼시벌 경의 묵직한 발소리가 계단을 내려왔다. 포스코 백작부인이 더없이 침착하고 판에 박힌 태도로, 퍼시벌 경의 처사 때문에 남편과 자기가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나지 않아도 되게 되어 우리 모두를 위해 기쁘다고 나에게 이르는 동안, 나는 두 사람이 밖에서 소곤거리는 소리를 들었다. 그녀의 말이 끝나기도 전에 소곤거림이 그치고 문이 열리며 백작이 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n“홀콤 양,” 그가 말했다. “글라이드 부인께서 다시 이 댁의 안주인이 되셨음을 알려 드리게 되어 기쁩니다. 이 반가운 변화를 퍼시벌 경보다는 나에게서 들으시는 편이 더 마음 편하시리라 생각하여, 일부러 다시 와서 말씀드리는 것입니다.”\n\n“감탄스러운 배려로군요!” 포스코 백작부인이 남편의 감탄이라는 헌사를 백작 자신의 화폐로, 백작 자신의 방식대로 되갚으며 말했다. 그는 마치 예의 바른 낯선 사람에게서 격식 차린 찬사를 받은 것처럼 미소 지으며 고개를 숙였고, 내가 먼저 지나가도록 물러섰다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 현관 홀에 서 있었다. 내가 서둘러 계단으로 가는데, 그가 조바심 내며 백작에게 서재에서 나오라고 부르는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n“거기서 뭘 기다리는 거요?” 그가 말했다. “당신과 할 말이 있소.”\n\n“나는 혼자서 좀 생각하고 싶소.” 상대가 대답했다. “나중에 합시다, 퍼시벌, 나중에.”\n\n그도 그의 친구도 더는 말이 없었다. 나는 계단 꼭대기에 이르러 복도를 달렸다. 서두르고 마음이 들뜬 나머지 앞방 문은 열어 둔 채였지만, 침실 문은 들어서자마자 닫았다.\n\n로라는 방 저쪽 끝에 홀로 앉아 두 팔을 지친 듯 탁자에 얹고 얼굴을 손에 묻고 있었다. 그녀는 나를 보자 기쁨의 소리를 지르며 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n“어떻게 여기 왔어?” 그녀가 물었다. “누가 허락해 줬어? 퍼시벌 경은 아닐 테고?”\n\n그녀가 들려줄 이야기를 듣고 싶은 조바심에 짓눌린 나는 대답할 수가 없었다—나로서는 이쪽에서 물을 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 아래층에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 알고 싶어 하는 로라의 열의는 뿌리치기에 너무 강했다. 그녀는 끈질기게 물음을 되풀이했다.\n\n“백작이지, 누구겠니.” 나는 조바심 내며 대답했다. “이 집에서 누구의 힘이——”\n\n그녀는 혐오스럽다는 몸짓으로 내 말을 막았다.\n\n“그 사람 이야기는 하지 마.” 그녀가 외쳤다. “백작은 세상에 숨 쉬는 것 중 가장 비열한 인간이야! 백작은 비참한 첩자야——!”\n\n우리 둘 중 누구도 다시 한마디 하기 전에, 침실 문을 나직이 두드리는 소리에 우리는 화들짝 놀랐다.\n\n나는 아직 앉지 않았던 터라 누구인지 보러 먼저 갔다. 문을 여니 포스코 백작부인이 내 손수건을 손에 들고 나를 마주 보고 서 있었다.\n\n“홀콤 양, 아래층에서 이걸 떨어뜨리셨더군요.” 그녀가 말했다. “제 방으로 가는 길이라 가져다 드리면 좋겠다 싶었습니다.”\n\n본디 창백한 그녀의 얼굴이 어찌나 소름 끼치게 하얗게 질렸던지 나는 그것을 보고 흠칫했다. 여느 때는 그토록 확실하고 흔들림 없던 두 손이 심하게 떨렸고, 두 눈은 나를 지나쳐 열린 문 너머 로라에게 늑대처럼 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n그녀는 문을 두드리기 전에 엿듣고 있었던 것이다! 나는 그것을 그 하얀 얼굴에서 보았고, 떨리는 두 손에서 보았고, 로라를 바라보는 그 눈길에서 보았다.\n\n잠시 기다리더니 그녀는 말없이 나에게서 돌아서서 천천히 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n나는 다시 문을 닫았다. “아, 로라! 로라! 네가 백작을 첩자라고 부른 오늘을 우리 둘 다 후회하게 될 거야!”\n\n“언니도 내가 아는 걸 알았다면 그렇게 불렀을 거야, 매리언. 앤 캐서릭 말이 옳았어. 어제 잡목림에서 우리를 지켜보던 제삼자가 있었고, 그 제삼자는——”\n\n“그게 백작이었던 게 확실하니?”\n\n“절대적으로 확실해. 그가 퍼시벌 경의 첩자였어—그가 퍼시벌 경의 밀고자였어—그가 퍼시벌 경더러 오전 내내 앤 캐서릭과 나를 지켜보며 기다리게 만든 거야.”\n\n“앤은 찾았니? 호수에서 그 여자를 보았어?”\n\n“아니. 그 사람은 그곳에 오지 않아서 스스로를 지켰어. 내가 보트 창고에 갔을 때 거긴 아무도 없었어.”\n\n“그래서? 그래서?”\n\n“안에 들어가 몇 분 앉아서 기다렸어. 그런데 안절부절못하겠어서 다시 일어나 조금 거닐었지. 나가면서 보트 창고 앞쪽 바로 아래 모래에 무슨 자국이 있는 걸 봤어. 몸을 굽혀 살펴보니 모래 위에 큰 글씨로 한 낱말이 쓰여 있었어. 그 낱말은—보라, 였어.”\n\n“그래서 모래를 긁어내고 그 자리를 우묵하게 팠구나?”\n\n“그걸 언니가 어떻게 알아, 매리언?”\n\n“내가 너를 따라 보트 창고에 갔을 때 그 우묵한 자리를 직접 보았단다. 계속해—계속해!”\n\n“응, 나는 표면의 모래를 긁어냈고, 조금 지나 그 밑에 숨겨져 있던 종잇조각에 닿았는데 거기 글씨가 있었어. 그 글에는 앤 캐서릭의 머리글자가 서명되어 있었고.”\n\n“그건 어디 있니?”\n\n“퍼시벌 경이 나한테서 빼앗아 갔어.”\n\n“무슨 내용이었는지 기억할 수 있겠니? 나에게 그대로 되풀이해 줄 수 있을 것 같아?”\n\n“요지는 할 수 있어, 매리언. 아주 짧았거든. 언니라면 한 마디 한 마디 다 기억했을 거야.”\n\n“더 나아가기 전에 그 요지가 무엇이었는지 말해 보렴.”\n\n그녀는 그대로 했다. 나는 그녀가 나에게 되풀이해 준 그대로 그 글줄을 여기에 적어 둔다. 그것은 이러했다—\n\n“어제 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 노인이 나와 당신이 함께 있는 것을 보았고, 나는 몸을 지키려고 달아나야 했습니다. 그는 발이 느려 나를 따라오지 못했고 나무들 사이에서 나를 놓쳤습니다. 나는 감히 오늘 같은 시각에 이곳으로 다시 올 위험을 무릅쓰지 못하겠습니다. 그 말씀을 드리려고 아침 여섯 시에 이 글을 써서 모래에 묻어 둡니다. 당신의 사악한 남편의 비밀을 다음번에 이야기할 때는 안전하게 이야기해야 하며, 그러지 못한다면 아예 이야기하지 말아야 합니다. 부디 참고 기다려 주십시오. 다시, 그것도 곧 나를 만나게 되리라고 약속드립니다.—A. C.”\n\n“키가 크고 뚱뚱한 노인”이라는 대목은(로라는 그 표현을 나에게 틀림없이 그대로 옮겼다고 확신했다) 침입자가 누구였는지 의심할 여지를 남기지 않았다. 나는 전날 백작이 있는 자리에서 퍼시벌 경에게 로라가 브로치를 찾으러 보트 창고에 갔다고 말했던 일을 떠올렸다. 십중팔구 그는 응접실에서 나에게 퍼시벌 경의 계획이 바뀌었다는 말을 전한 직후, 서명 문제로 그녀의 마음을 풀어 주겠다며 특유의 참견하는 방식으로 그녀를 뒤따라갔을 것이다. 그렇다면 그는 앤 캐서릭이 그를 발견한 바로 그 순간에야 보트 창고 근처에 이를 수 있었을 것이다. 그 여자가 수상하리만치 서둘러 로라와 헤어진 것이 그로 하여금 헛되이 뒤쫓게 만들었을 터였다. 그 전에 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화는 그가 아무것도 듣지 못했을 것이다. 저택과 호수 사이의 거리, 그리고 그가 응접실에서 나를 떠난 시각을 로라와 앤 캐서릭이 이야기를 나눈 시각과 견주어 보면, 적어도 우리에게는 그 사실이 의심할 여지 없이 증명되었다.\n\n여기까지 어느 정도 결론 비슷한 것에 이르자, 다음으로 내 큰 관심사는 포스코 백작이 알려 준 뒤에 퍼시벌 경이 무엇을 알아냈는가 하는 것이었다.\n\n“어쩌다 그 편지를 빼앗기게 되었니?” 내가 물었다. “모래에서 그걸 찾아낸 뒤에 어떻게 했어?”\n\n“한 번 죽 읽고 나서,” 그녀가 대답했다. “앉아서 다시 한 번 훑어보려고 보트 창고 안으로 가지고 들어갔어. 읽고 있는데 종이 위로 그림자가 드리웠어. 올려다보니 퍼시벌 경이 문간에 서서 나를 지켜보고 있었어.”\n\n“편지를 감추려고 해 봤니?”\n\n“그러려고 했지만 그이가 막았어. ‘그걸 감추려고 애쓸 것 없소.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘내가 이미 읽었으니까.’ 나는 어쩔 줄 몰라 그이를 바라볼 수밖에 없었어—아무 말도 못 했어. ‘알겠소?’ 그이가 말을 이었어. ‘내가 읽었소. 두 시간 전에 모래에서 파내어 읽고, 다시 묻고, 그 위에 그 낱말을 다시 써서, 당신 손에 들어가도록 그대로 두었소. 이제 거짓말로 이 곤경을 빠져나갈 수는 없소. 당신은 어제 몰래 앤 캐서릭을 만났고, 지금 이 순간 그 여자의 편지를 손에 쥐고 있소. 아직 그 여자는 잡지 못했지만 당신은 잡았소. 편지를 이리 주시오.’ 그이가 나에게 바싹 다가섰어—나는 그이와 단둘이었어, 매리언—내가 어쩌겠어?—편지를 주고 말았어.”\n\n“네가 그걸 건네주자 그가 뭐라고 하던?”\n\n“처음에는 아무 말도 안 했어. 내 팔을 잡고 보트 창고 밖으로 데리고 나가더니, 우리가 남의 눈에 띄거나 소리가 들릴까 두려운 사람처럼 사방을 둘러보았어. 그러고는 내 팔을 손으로 꽉 움켜쥐고 나에게 속삭였어. ‘어제 앤 캐서릭이 당신한테 뭐라고 했소? 처음부터 끝까지 한마디도 빼놓지 말고 들어야겠소.’”\n\n“말했니?”\n\n“그이와 단둘이었어, 매리언—그이의 잔인한 손이 내 팔에 멍이 들도록 죄고 있었어—내가 어쩌겠어?”\n\n“팔에 그 자국이 아직 있니? 좀 보자.”\n\n“그건 왜 보려고 해?”\n\n“보고 싶은 건, 로라, 우리의 인내가 끝나야 하고 우리의 저항이 오늘부터 시작되어야 하기 때문이야. 그 자국은 그를 칠 무기란다. 지금 보여 다오—언젠가 그것을 두고 선서해야 할지도 모르니까.”\n\n“아, 매리언, 그런 얼굴 하지 마—그런 말 하지 마! 이제 아프지도 않아!”\n\n“보여 다오!”\n\n그녀는 나에게 자국을 보여 주었다. 나는 그것을 슬퍼할 단계도, 눈물 흘릴 단계도, 몸서리칠 단계도 이미 지나 있었다. 사람들은 우리 여자가 남자보다 낫거나 아니면 더 나쁘다고들 한다. 어떤 여자들에게 닥쳐 그들을 더 나쁘게 만든 그 유혹이 그 순간 나에게 닥쳤더라면—하느님, 감사합니다! 내 얼굴은 그의 아내가 읽어 낼 만한 것을 아무것도 드러내지 않았다. 그 온순하고 순진하고 정 많은 것은 내가 자기를 걱정하고 가엾어하는 줄로만 알았고, 그 이상은 생각하지 않았다.\n\n“너무 심각하게 여기지 마, 매리언.” 그녀는 소매를 도로 내리며 담담하게 말했다. “이제 아프지도 않은걸.”\n\n“너를 위해서 침착하게 생각해 보도록 애쓰마, 얘야.—그래! 그래! 그러고 나서 앤 캐서릭이 너에게 한 말을 전부 그에게 이야기했다고—나에게 이야기한 것을 전부?”\n\n“응, 전부. 그이가 우겨 댔고—나는 그이와 단둘이었고—아무것도 숨길 수가 없었어.”\n\n“네 말이 끝나자 그가 무슨 말을 하던?”\n\n“나를 바라보며 비웃듯 씁쓸하게 혼자 웃었어. ‘나머지도 당신한테서 캐낼 작정이오.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘알겠소?—나머지 말이오.’ 나는 아는 것은 다 말했다고 엄숙하게 밝혔어. ‘천만에.’ 그이가 대답했어. ‘당신은 말하려는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있소. 말하지 않겠다고? 말하게 될 거요! 여기서 짜낼 수 없다면 집에 가서라도 짜내고야 말겠소.’ 그이는 잡목림을 가로지르는 이상한 길로—언니를 만날 가망이 전혀 없는 길로—나를 끌고 갔고, 저택이 보이는 데 이를 때까지 더는 말이 없었어. 그러다 다시 멈춰 서서 말했어. ‘두 번째 기회를 주면 받겠소? 마음을 고쳐먹고 나머지를 말하겠소?’ 나는 앞서 한 말을 되풀이하는 수밖에 없었어. 그이는 내 고집에 욕설을 퍼붓고는, 계속 걸어서 나를 데리고 집으로 들어갔어. ‘나를 속일 수는 없소.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘당신은 말하려는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있소. 당신한테서 비밀을 캐내고, 당신의 그 언니한테서도 캐내고야 말겠소. 당신들 사이에 더는 모의도 속닥거림도 없을 거요. 진실을 털어놓기 전에는 당신도 그 여자도 서로 얼굴을 못 볼 거요. 진실을 털어놓을 때까지 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 당신을 감시하게 하겠소.’ 내가 무슨 말을 해도 그이는 귀머거리였어. 그이는 나를 곧장 위층 내 방으로 데려갔어. 패니가 거기 앉아 내 일감을 손질하고 있었는데, 그이는 대뜸 그 애더러 나가라고 명령했어. ‘네가 이 음모에 끼어들지 못하게 단단히 손을 써 두겠다.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘너는 오늘 이 집을 떠나라. 네 여주인에게 몸종이 필요하다면 내가 고른 사람을 붙여 주지.’ 그이는 나를 방 안으로 밀어 넣고 문을 잠갔어. 그 멍청한 여자를 밖에 세워 나를 감시하게 했어, 매리언! 그이의 낯빛도 말투도 미친 사람 같았어. 언니는 잘 믿기지 않겠지만—정말 그랬어.”\n\n“나는 믿는단다, 로라. 그 사람은 미쳤어—죄지은 양심의 공포로 미친 거야. 네가 한 말을 하나하나 들으니, 어제 앤 캐서릭이 너를 떠났을 때 너는 그 비열한 남편을 파멸시킬 수도 있는 비밀을 알아내기 직전이었고, 그 사람은 네가 그것을 이미 알아냈다고 여기고 있다는 확신이 굳어질 뿐이야. 네가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 해도 그 죄 많은 의심을 잠재우거나, 그 거짓된 천성에게 네가 정직하다는 것을 납득시키지는 못해. 얘야, 이런 말을 하는 건 너를 겁주려는 게 아니야. 네가 처한 처지에 눈뜨게 하고, 기회가 우리 것일 동안 내가 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 너를 지키도록 나서야 한다는 절박한 필요를 납득시키려는 거야. 포스코 백작이 끼어든 덕분에 오늘은 내가 너에게 올 수 있었지만, 그 사람은 내일이면 그 참견을 거두어 버릴지도 몰라. 퍼시벌 경은 패니가 눈치 빠르고 너에게 헌신적이라는 이유로 벌써 내보냈고, 네 이익에는 아무 관심도 없으며 그 아둔한 머리가 마당의 개나 다름없는 수준인 여자를 그 자리에 앉혔어. 기회가 있을 때 최대한 살리지 않는다면 그 사람이 다음에 어떤 난폭한 수를 쓸지 알 수 없어.”\n\n“우리가 뭘 할 수 있어, 매리언? 아, 이 집을 떠나 다시는 보지 않을 수만 있다면!”\n\n“내 말을 들으렴, 얘야. 그리고 내가 너와 함께 여기 있는 한 네가 아주 무력한 것은 아니라고 생각하도록 애써 보렴.”\n\n“그렇게 생각할게—정말 그렇게 생각해. 나를 생각하느라 가엾은 패니를 아주 잊어버리지는 마. 그 애도 도움과 위로가 필요해.”\n\n“잊지 않으마. 여기 올라오기 전에 그 아이를 보았고, 오늘 밤에 연락하기로 해 두었단다. 블랙워터 파크의 우편 자루에 넣는 편지는 안전하지 않으니, 오늘 너를 위해 두 통을 써야 하는데 그건 패니 말고 누구의 손도 거쳐서는 안 돼.”\n\n“무슨 편지들인데?”\n\n“먼저, 로라, 새로 급한 일이 생기면 돕겠다고 한 길모어 씨의 동업자에게 쓰려고 해. 나는 법을 아는 게 별로 없지만, 오늘 그 무뢰한이 너에게 가한 것 같은 대우에서 법이 여자를 지켜 줄 수 있다는 것만은 확신해. 앤 캐서릭에 대해서는 확실한 정보를 줄 게 없으니 자세히 쓰지 않겠어. 하지만 네 팔의 그 멍과 이 방에서 너에게 가해진 폭력만은 변호사가 알아야 해—오늘 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 반드시 알게 될 거야!”\n\n“하지만 소문이 날 걸 생각해 봐, 매리언!”\n\n“나는 그 소문을 계산에 넣고 있어. 소문이 나면 너보다 퍼시벌 경이 더 두려워할 게 많아. 다른 무엇으로도 안 될 때 소문이 날지 모른다는 전망이 그를 굽히게 할 수도 있어.”\n\n말하면서 나는 일어섰지만, 로라가 자기를 두고 가지 말라고 애원했다. “언니는 그이를 자포자기하게 만들 거야.” 그녀가 말했다. “그러면 우리의 위험은 열 배로 커질 거고.”\n\n나는 그 말이 진실임을—낙담스러운 진실임을 느꼈다. 그러나 그것을 그녀에게 대놓고 인정할 수는 없었다. 우리의 끔찍한 처지에서는 최악을 무릅쓰는 것 말고 우리에게 도움도 희망도 없었다. 나는 조심스러운 말로 그렇게 말했다. 그녀는 쓰디쓰게 한숨을 쉬었지만 그 문제로 다투지는 않았다. 다만 내가 쓰겠다고 한 두 번째 편지에 대해 물었다. 그것은 누구에게 보내는 것이냐고.\n\n“페얼리 씨에게.” 내가 말했다. “네 삼촌은 너의 가장 가까운 남자 친척이고 집안의 어른이야. 그분이 나서야 하고, 나서게 될 거야.”\n\n로라는 서글프게 고개를 저었다.\n\n“그래, 그래.” 내가 말을 이었다. “네 삼촌이 나약하고 이기적이고 세속적인 사람인 건 나도 알아. 하지만 그분은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아니고, 곁에 포스코 백작 같은 친구도 없어. 나는 그분이 너나 나에게 품은 친절이나 다정한 마음 같은 건 조금도 기대하지 않아. 하지만 그분은 자기 게으름을 떠받들고 자기 평온을 지키기 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 사람이야. 지금 나서 주는 것이 앞으로 반드시 닥칠 성가심과 곤경과 책임을 면하게 해 준다고 설득하기만 하면, 그분은 자기를 위해서라도 몸을 움직일 거야. 나는 그분을 다루는 법을 알아, 로라—몇 번 해 봤으니까.”\n\n“삼촌을 설득해서 내가 잠시 리머리지로 돌아가 언니와 조용히 지낼 수 있게만 해 주신다면, 매리언, 나는 결혼하기 전만큼이나 다시 행복해질 수 있을 텐데!”\n\n그 말은 내 생각을 새로운 방향으로 돌려놓았다. 퍼시벌 경을 두 갈래 선택 앞에 세워 둘 수는 없을까? 하나는 아내를 대신한 법적 개입이 불러올 추문에 스스로를 내맡기는 길이고, 다른 하나는 삼촌 댁을 방문한다는 구실로 아내가 한동안 조용히 그의 곁을 떠나 있도록 허락하는 길이다. 그리고 그런 처지에 몰린다면, 그가 뒤엣것을 마지막 방책으로 받아들이리라고 기대해도 좋을까? 그것은 의심스러웠다—의심스럽다 못한 일이었다. 그럼에도 이 시도가 아무리 가망 없어 보인들, 분명 해 볼 값어치는 있었다. 달리 무엇을 해야 좋을지 도무지 알 수 없다는 순전한 절망 속에서, 나는 그것을 해 보기로 결심했다.\n\n“네가 방금 말한 바람을 삼촌께 알려 드리마.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 그 문제에 대해 변호사의 조언도 구해 보겠어. 좋은 결과가 나올 수도 있어—아니, 나오리라고 나는 믿어.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 나는 다시 일어섰고, 로라는 또다시 나를 자리에 앉히려 했다.\n\n“가지 마.” 로라가 불안하게 말했다. “내 문갑이 저 탁자 위에 있어. 여기서 쓰면 되잖아.”\n\n그 애의 청을, 그것도 그 애 자신을 위한 일로 물리치는 것은 뼈아픈 노릇이었다. 그러나 우리는 이미 너무 오래 단둘이 틀어박혀 있었다. 다시 서로를 만날 수 있느냐는 새로운 의심을 사지 않는 데에 온전히 달려 있을지도 몰랐다. 어쩌면 바로 그 순간 아래층에서 우리를 생각하고 우리 이야기를 하고 있을 그 몹쓸 자들 틈에, 조용하고 무심한 낯으로 모습을 드러낼 때가 무르익은 것이다. 나는 그 비참한 필요를 로라에게 설명했고, 나와 같이 그것을 받아들이도록 그 애를 설득했다.\n\n“한 시간이나 그보다 짧은 사이에 다시 올게, 얘야.” 내가 말했다. “오늘 치 최악은 지나갔어. 가만히 있으면서 아무것도 무서워하지 마.”\n\n“열쇠가 문에 꽂혀 있니, 매리언? 안에서 잠글 수 있을까?”\n\n“그래, 여기 열쇠가 있어. 문을 잠그고, 내가 다시 올라올 때까지 누구에게도 열어 주지 마.”\n\n나는 그 애에게 입을 맞추고 방을 나왔다. 걸어 나오면서 자물쇠 안에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리를 듣고, 그 문이 그 애 뜻대로 되어 있음을 아는 것은 내게 하나의 위안이었다.\n\n# VIII\n\n6월 19일.—계단 꼭대기에 이르렀을 뿐인데 로라의 방문이 잠기는 소리가 들려, 나도 내 방문을 잠그고 방을 비우는 동안 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있어야겠다는 조심성이 떠올랐다. 일기는 이미 다른 서류들과 함께 탁자 서랍에 넣어 잠가 두었지만, 필기도구는 그대로 밖에 나와 있었다. 그중에는 같은 잔에서 물을 마시는 두 마리 비둘기라는 흔한 도안이 새겨진 봉인 도장과, 지난밤 이 지면에 적은 마지막 줄들이 그대로 찍혀 있는 압지 몇 장이 있었다. 이제 내 일부가 되어 버린 의심에 뒤틀려 보인 탓인지, 이런 사소한 물건들조차 지키는 사람 없이 두기에는 너무 위험해 보였다. 잠긴 탁자 서랍마저도, 그리로 들어가는 길까지 조심스레 막아 두기 전에는 내가 없는 동안 충분히 지켜지지 못하는 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n로라와 이야기하는 동안 누군가 방에 들어왔던 흔적은 보이지 않았다. (하인들에게 절대 손대지 말라고 일러 둔) 필기도구들은 여느 때와 다름없이 탁자 위에 흩어져 있었다. 그와 관련해 조금이라도 마음에 걸린 단 한 가지는, 봉인 도장이 연필과 봉랍이 놓인 쟁반 안에 얌전히 놓여 있다는 점이었다. 부끄럽지만 그것을 거기에 두는 것은 덜렁대는 내 습관이 아니었고, 거기 둔 기억도 없었다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 달리 어디에 던져 두었는지도 떠오르지 않았고, 어쩌다 한 번쯤 무심코 제자리에 놓았을 가능성도 없지 않았기에, 하루의 사건들로 이미 어지러운 머리를 사소한 일로 새삼 괴롭히지는 않기로 했다. 나는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n포스코 백작부인이 현관 홀에 혼자 서서 청우계를 들여다보고 있었다.\n\n“여전히 내려가는군요.” 부인이 말했다. “비가 더 오리라고 각오해야 할까 봐요.”\n\n그 얼굴은 다시 여느 때의 표정과 여느 때의 혈색을 되찾고 있었다. 그러나 청우계의 눈금을 가리키는 손은 여전히 떨리고 있었다.\n\n로라가 그를 “첩자”라고 헐뜯는 말을, 그것도 내 앞에서 한 것을 엿들었다고 부인은 벌써 남편에게 말해 버렸을까? 틀림없이 말했으리라는 강한 의심, 뒤따를 결과에 대한, 막연하기에 더욱 짓누르는 억누를 수 없는 두려움, 그리고 여자들이 서로에게서 알아채는 온갖 사소한 자기 폭로에서 얻어 낸 확신, 곧 포스코 백작부인이 겉으로는 예의를 잘 꾸며 보이지만 알지도 못한 채 자기와 만 파운드의 유산 사이를 가로막은 조카딸을 아직 용서하지 않았다는 확신, 이 모든 것이 한꺼번에 밀려들어, 내 영향력과 설득의 힘으로 로라의 잘못을 대신 속죄해 보겠다는 헛된 희망에서 입을 열게 만들었다.\n\n“포스코 백작부인, 대단히 괴로운 이야기를 감히 꺼내더라도 너그러이 용서해 주시겠습니까?”\n\n부인은 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 한마디 말도 없이, 한순간도 내 눈에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 엄숙하게 고개를 숙였다.\n\n“친절하게도 제 손수건을 가져다주셨을 때 말입니다.” 나는 말을 이었다. “로라가 하는 말을 우연히 들으셨을까 봐 몹시, 몹시 걱정스럽습니다. 저는 그 말을 되풀이하고 싶지도 않고 변호할 생각도 없습니다. 다만 부인께서 그 말을 백작께 전할 만큼 중요한 일로 여기지는 않으셨기를 감히 바랄 뿐입니다.”\n\n“전혀 중요하지 않다고 생각합니다.” 포스코 백작부인이 날카롭고 갑작스럽게 말했다. “하지만,” 그러고는 곧 얼음 같은 태도를 되찾으며 덧붙였다. “저는 아무리 사소한 일이라도 남편에게 숨기는 것이 없습니다. 아까 제가 괴로워 보인다고 남편이 눈치챘을 때, 왜 괴로운지 말하는 것이 제 괴로운 의무였지요. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양, 저는 남편에게 말했습니다.”\n\n나는 그런 대답을 들을 각오가 되어 있었는데도, 그 말에 온몸이 서늘해졌다.\n\n“간절히 청합니다, 포스코 백작부인. 백작께도 간절히 청합니다. 제 동생이 놓인 딱한 처지를 얼마간 헤아려 주십시오. 동생은 남편에게서 받은 모욕과 부당한 처사에 쓰라려하던 중에 그런 말을 한 것이고, 그 경솔한 말을 할 때는 제정신이 아니었습니다. 너그럽고 관대하게 용서해 주시기를 바라도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“물론이고말고요.” 등 뒤에서 백작의 조용한 목소리가 들렸다. 그는 서재에서 책을 손에 든 채 소리 없는 걸음으로 우리에게 다가와 있었다.\n\n“글라이드 부인이 그 성급한 말을 하셨을 때,” 그가 말을 이었다. “저를 부당하게 대하신 것이지요. 유감스럽지만, 저는 용서합니다. 이 이야기는 다시 꺼내지 맙시다, 홀콤 양. 우리 모두 사이좋게 힘을 모아 이 순간부터 잊어버리기로 하지요.”\n\n“참으로 친절하십니다.” 내가 말했다. “말로 다 못 할 만큼 마음이 놓입니다.”\n\n더 말을 이으려 했지만 그의 눈이 나를 향해 있었다. 모든 것을 감추는 그 치명적인 미소가 넓고 매끈한 얼굴 위에 굳은 채 흔들림 없이 걸려 있었다. 헤아릴 수 없는 그의 거짓됨에 대한 불신과, 그와 그 아내에게 몸을 굽혀 비위를 맞추고 있는 나 자신에 대한 굴욕감이 나를 몹시 흔들고 혼란스럽게 해서, 다음 말이 입술에서 사라져 버렸고 나는 말없이 그 자리에 서 있었다.\n\n“무릎 꿇고 청하오니 더는 말씀 마십시오, 홀콤 양. 이렇게까지 말씀하셔야 한다고 여기셨다니 참으로 마음이 아픕니다.” 그 정중한 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 잡았다. 아, 나는 내 자신이 얼마나 경멸스러운가! 아, 로라를 위해 참았다는 것을 안들 위안이 얼마나 하찮은가! 그는 내 손을 잡아 그 독기 어린 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그때까지 나는 그에 대한 내 공포의 크기를 온전히 알지 못했다. 그 악의 없는 친밀함이, 남자가 여자에게 던질 수 있는 가장 저열한 모욕이기라도 한 듯 내 피를 뒤집어 놓았다. 그런데도 나는 역겨움을 감추었다. 웃어 보이려 애썼다. 한때 다른 여자들의 위선을 가차 없이 경멸하던 내가, 그중 가장 나쁜 여자만큼이나, 내 손에 입술을 댄 저 유다만큼이나 거짓되었다.\n\n그가 계속 내 얼굴에 눈을 두고 있었다면 나는 그 굴욕적인 자제를 끝까지 지키지 못했을 것이다. 지키지 못했으리라는 것을 아는 일만이 내 눈에 비친 나 자신을 그나마 구해 준다. 그가 내 손을 붙잡은 순간, 아내의 호랑이 같은 질투가 나를 구하러 와서 그의 주의를 내게서 억지로 떼어 놓았다. 차가운 푸른 눈에 불이 붙고, 핏기 없이 희던 뺨이 발갛게 달아올라, 그 여자는 순식간에 제 나이보다 몇 살은 젊어 보였다.\n\n“백작!” 부인이 말했다. “당신의 외국식 예법은 영국 여자들에게는 통하지 않아요.”\n\n“미안하오, 나의 천사여! 세상에서 제일 훌륭하고 제일 사랑스러운 영국 여인은 그 예법을 알아준다오.” 그 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 놓고, 그 대신 아내의 손을 조용히 들어 입술로 가져갔다.\n\n나는 계단을 뛰어올라 내 방으로 피신했다. 생각할 겨를이 있었다면, 혼자가 된 순간 그 생각들은 나를 몹시 괴롭혔을 것이다. 그러나 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 내 침착함과 용기를 지키기에는 다행스럽게도, 행동할 겨를밖에 없었다.\n\n변호사와 페얼리 씨에게 보낼 편지가 아직 남아 있었고, 나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 곧바로 앉아 그 일에 매달렸다.\n\n나를 망설이게 할 만큼 방도가 많은 것도 아니었다. 우선은 나 자신 말고 기댈 사람이 전혀 없었다. 퍼시벌 경에게는 중재를 부탁해 볼 만한 친구도 친척도 이 근방에 없었다. 그는 가까이 사는 같은 신분과 지위의 집안들과 더없이 서먹한 사이였고, 몇몇과는 아주 나쁜 사이였다. 우리 두 여자에게는 이 집에 와서 우리 편을 들어 줄 아버지도 오라비도 없었다. 그 미덥지 못한 편지 두 통을 쓰든지, 아니면 블랙워터 파크에서 몰래 달아나 로라와 나를 잘못한 쪽으로 만들고 앞으로의 평화로운 교섭을 모조리 불가능하게 만들든지, 둘 중 하나였다. 두 번째 길을 택하는 것은 신변에 가장 절박한 위험이 닥쳤을 때에만 정당화될 수 있었다. 먼저 편지를 시험해 보아야 했고, 그래서 나는 편지를 썼다.\n\n변호사에게는 앤 캐서릭에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 이미 로라에게 넌지시 말했듯이 그 문제는 우리가 아직 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼와 얽혀 있어서, 직업인에게 편지로 쓴들 소용이 없을 것이기 때문이었다. 퍼시벌 경의 수치스러운 행동에 대해서는, 원한다면 돈 문제로 다시 다툰 탓으로 여기도록 상대에게 맡겨 두고, 다만 남편이 로라가 한동안 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 나와 함께 리머리지로 돌아가는 것을 허락하지 않을 경우 로라를 보호하기 위한 법적 절차를 밟을 수 있는지만 상의했다. 이 마지막 조처의 자세한 내용은 페얼리 씨에게 물어보라고 일러두었고, 로라의 위임을 받아 쓰는 것임을 분명히 밝혔으며, 힘닿는 데까지 되도록 시간을 허비하지 말고 로라의 이름으로 움직여 달라고 간청하며 끝맺었다.\n\n다음은 페얼리 씨에게 보낼 편지였다. 나는 그를 움직이게 하기에 가장 그럴듯하다고 로라에게 말했던 그 논리로 호소했다. 사태가 얼마나 심각한지 보여 주려고 변호사에게 보낸 편지의 사본을 동봉했고, 리머리지로 옮겨 가는 것이야말로 로라의 현재 처지가 낳는 위험과 고통이 머지않아 조카딸뿐 아니라 삼촌에게까지 미치는 것을 막아 줄 유일한 절충안이라고 설명했다.\n\n다 쓰고 봉투 두 장을 봉하고 수신인을 적은 뒤, 편지를 들고 로라의 방으로 돌아가 편지를 다 썼음을 보여 주었다.\n\n“누가 방해하지는 않았니?” 로라가 문을 열어 주었을 때 내가 물었다.\n\n“문을 두드린 사람은 없었어.” 로라가 대답했다. “그런데 바깥방에 누가 있는 소리가 들렸어.”\n\n“남자였니, 여자였니?”\n\n“여자였어. 옷자락 스치는 소리가 들렸거든.”\n\n“비단 스치는 소리 같던?”\n\n“응, 비단 같았어.”\n\n포스코 백작부인이 밖에서 지키고 있었던 게 분명했다. 그 여자 혼자서 부릴 수 있는 해코지야 별로 두려울 게 없었다. 그러나 남편의 손에 기꺼이 쥐인 도구로서 부릴 수 있는 해코지는 그냥 지나치기에는 너무 무서웠다.\n\n“바깥방에서 그 옷자락 소리가 더 들리지 않게 된 다음에는 어떻게 됐니?” 내가 물었다. “네 방 벽을 지나 복도 쪽으로 가는 소리가 들리던?”\n\n“응. 가만히 숨죽이고 귀 기울이니까 겨우 들렸어.”\n\n“어느 쪽으로 갔어?”\n\n“언니 방 쪽으로.”\n\n나는 다시 생각에 잠겼다. 그 소리는 내 귀에 들리지 않았다. 그러나 그때 나는 편지에 깊이 빠져 있었고, 나는 손에 힘을 주어 깃펜으로 종이 위를 사각사각 긁으며 요란하게 쓴다. 내가 그 여자의 옷자락 소리를 듣기보다 그 여자가 내 펜 소리를 들었을 가능성이 더 컸다. 더 필요했다면, 편지를 현관의 우편 가방에 맡기지 말아야 할 또 하나의 이유였다.\n\n로라는 내가 생각에 잠긴 것을 보았다. “또 어려운 일이구나!” 로라가 지친 목소리로 말했다. “어려운 일도 더 생기고 위험도 더 생기는구나!”\n\n“위험은 없어.” 내가 대답했다. “조금 까다로울 뿐이지, 아마. 편지 두 통을 패니 손에 넘길 가장 안전한 방법을 생각하는 중이야.”\n\n“그럼 정말로 다 썼구나? 아, 매리언, 위험한 짓은 하지 마. 제발, 제발 위험한 짓은 하지 마!”\n\n“아니야, 아니야. 걱정 마. 어디 보자, 지금 몇 시지?”\n\n여섯 시 십오 분 전이었다. 마을 여관까지 갔다가 저녁 식사 전에 돌아올 시간은 있었다. 저녁까지 기다리면 무사히 집을 빠져나갈 두 번째 기회는 없을지도 몰랐다.\n\n“문은 계속 잠가 두렴, 로라.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 내 걱정은 하지 마. 누가 뭘 묻는 소리가 들리거든 문 너머로 내가 산책 나갔다고 대답해.”\n\n“언제 돌아올 거야?”\n\n“저녁 식사 전에는 꼭. 힘을 내, 얘야. 내일 이맘때면 냉철하고 믿음직한 사람이 너를 위해 나서 줄 거야. 길모어 씨의 동업자는 길모어 씨 다음으로 우리에게 좋은 친구지.”\n\n혼자가 되자마자 잠깐 생각해 보니, 집 아래층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 먼저 알아보기 전에는 외출복 차림으로 나서지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 확신이 들었다. 퍼시벌 경이 집 안에 있는지 밖에 있는지 아직 확인하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n서재에서 들려오는 카나리아 노랫소리와, 닫히지 않은 문틈으로 새어 나오는 담배 연기 냄새가 백작이 어디 있는지 대번에 알려 주었다. 문간을 지나며 어깨 너머로 들여다보다가, 그가 더없이 상냥하고 정중한 태도로 가정부에게 새들의 재롱을 보여 주고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그가 일부러 청해서 부른 것이 틀림없었다. 가정부가 제 발로 서재에 들어갈 생각을 할 리 없으니까. 저 사람은 아무리 사소한 행동이라도 그 밑바닥에는 어떤 목적이 깔려 있다. 여기서 그의 목적은 무엇일까?\n\n그때는 그의 속셈을 캘 때가 아니었다. 다음으로 포스코 백작부인을 찾아보았더니, 늘 그러듯 연못 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌고 있었다.\n\n얼마 전 내가 원인이 되어 터진 질투의 소동이 있었으니, 부인이 나를 어떻게 대할지 조금 불안했다. 그러나 그사이 남편이 길들여 놓은 뒤라, 이제 부인은 여느 때와 다름없이 정중하게 말을 건넸다. 내가 말을 붙인 유일한 목적은 퍼시벌 경이 어찌 되었는지 부인이 아는지 알아보는 것이었다. 나는 에둘러 그를 화제에 올렸고, 서로 조금 떠본 끝에 부인은 마침내 그가 밖에 나갔다고 말했다.\n\n“말은 어느 걸 타고 가셨나요?” 내가 무심한 척 물었다.\n\n“한 마리도 안 탔어요.” 부인이 대답했다. “두 시간 전에 걸어서 나가셨지요. 제가 알기로는 앤 캐서릭이라는 여자에 대해 새로 알아보러 가신 거예요. 그 여자를 찾는 데 지나칠 만큼 안달하시는 것 같더군요. 혹시 그 여자가 위험할 만큼 미쳤는지 아세요, 홀콤 양?”\n\n“모릅니다, 백작부인.”\n\n“들어가시려고요?”\n\n“네, 그럴까 합니다. 곧 저녁 식사 옷차림을 할 시간일 테니까요.”\n\n우리는 함께 집으로 들어갔다. 포스코 백작부인은 어슬렁어슬렁 서재로 들어가 문을 닫았다. 나는 곧장 모자와 숄을 가지러 갔다. 여관에 있는 패니에게 갔다가 저녁 식사 전에 돌아오려면 한순간도 허투루 쓸 수 없었다.\n\n다시 현관을 가로지를 때는 아무도 없었고, 서재의 새소리도 그쳐 있었다. 새로 살펴볼 겨를은 없었다. 길이 열려 있음을 확인하고, 편지 두 통을 주머니에 안전히 넣은 채 집을 나서는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n마을로 가는 길에 나는 퍼시벌 경과 마주칠 가능성에 대비해 마음을 다잡았다. 그를 혼자서 상대하는 한, 내 정신을 놓치지 않으리라는 자신이 있었다. 제 지혜를 확신하는 여자는 제 성미를 다스리지 못하는 남자쯤은 언제라도 당해 낼 수 있다. 나는 퍼시벌 경을 백작만큼 두려워하지는 않았다. 그가 무슨 볼일로 나갔는지 듣고 나니 마음이 흔들리기는커녕 오히려 가라앉았다. 앤 캐서릭을 찾는 일이 그를 사로잡은 가장 큰 근심인 동안에는, 로라와 나는 그가 우리를 적극적으로 괴롭히는 일이 얼마간 멈추기를 바랄 수 있었다. 이제는 앤을 위해서만이 아니라 우리를 위해서도, 그 여자가 여전히 그를 피해 있기를 나는 간절히 바라고 기도했다.\n\n나는 더위가 허락하는 한 빠른 걸음으로 걸어 마을로 이어지는 갈림길에 이르렀고, 이따금 뒤를 돌아보며 누가 따라오지 않는지 확인했다.\n\n오는 내내 뒤에는 빈 시골 짐마차 한 대밖에 없었다. 덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리가 거슬렸고, 그 마차도 나처럼 마을로 가는 길로 접어드는 것을 보고 나는 걸음을 멈춰 마차를 먼저 보내고 소리가 멀어지기를 기다렸다. 아까보다 더 유심히 마차 쪽을 바라보다가, 나는 마차 뒤에 바짝 붙어 걷는 남자의 발이 이따금 보이는 것 같다고 생각했다. 마부는 앞쪽에서 말 옆에 붙어 걷고 있었다. 내가 방금 지나온 갈림길은 워낙 좁아서 뒤따라오는 마차가 양옆의 나무와 덤불을 스칠 정도였으니, 마차가 지나갈 때까지 기다려야만 내 짐작이 맞는지 확인할 수 있었다. 아무래도 그 짐작은 틀렸던 모양이다. 마차가 나를 지나쳐 간 뒤 그 뒤의 길은 텅 비어 있었으니까.\n\n나는 퍼시벌 경과 마주치지도 않고 더 눈에 띄는 것도 없이 여관에 이르렀고, 여관 안주인이 패니를 더없이 친절하게 맞아 주었다는 것을 알고 기뻤다. 그 아이는 술청의 소음에서 떨어진 작은 거실과, 집 꼭대기의 깨끗한 침실을 얻어 있었다. 나를 보자 다시 울음을 터뜨리더니, 딱하게도 참으로 옳은 말을 했다. 누구도, 심지어 자기를 내보낸 주인조차 자기 탓이라 할 것이 없는데, 무슨 용서 못 할 잘못이라도 저지른 양 세상으로 내쫓긴 기분이 드는 것은 끔찍하다고.\n\n“되도록 좋게 생각하렴, 패니.” 내가 말했다. “아씨와 내가 네 편이 되어 줄 테고, 네 평판에 흠이 가지 않도록 해 주마. 자, 잘 들어라. 시간이 얼마 없는데, 네게 아주 큰일을 맡기려 한다. 이 편지 두 통을 잘 간수해 다오. 우표가 붙은 것은 내일 런던에 닿거든 우체통에 넣어라. 페얼리 씨 앞으로 된 다른 하나는 집에 도착하는 대로 네가 직접 그분께 전해 드려라. 두 통 다 몸에 지니고, 누구에게도 내주지 마라. 아씨의 이해가 걸린 더없이 중요한 편지들이다.”\n\n패니는 편지를 옷 앞섶에 넣었다. “아가씨가 시키신 일을 다 할 때까지 여기 넣어 두겠어요.” 그 아이가 말했다.\n\n“내일 아침 역에 늦지 않게 나가야 한다.” 내가 말을 이었다. “그리고 리머리지에서 가정부를 만나거든 내 안부를 전하고, 글라이드 부인이 너를 다시 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 네가 나를 모시고 있는 것이라고 말해라. 생각보다 빨리 다시 만나게 될지도 모르지. 그러니 마음 단단히 먹고, 일곱 시 기차를 놓치지 마라.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 아가씨. 정말 고맙습니다. 아가씨 목소리를 다시 들으니 힘이 납니다. 마님께 문안 여쭈어 주시고, 시간이 없는 대로 물건들은 최대한 정돈해 두었다고 말씀드려 주세요. 아이고, 저런! 오늘은 누가 마님 저녁 식사 옷을 입혀 드릴까요? 그 생각을 하면 정말 가슴이 미어집니다, 아가씨.”\n\n집에 돌아오니 저녁 식사 채비를 하고 아래층에 내려가기 전에 로라에게 두어 마디 할 시간이 십오 분밖에 남지 않았다.\n\n“편지는 패니 손에 넘어갔어.” 문간에서 내가 속삭였다. “저녁 식사에 함께 나올 거니?”\n\n“아, 아니, 아니. 무슨 일이 있어도 싫어.”\n\n“무슨 일 있었니? 누가 방해했어?”\n\n“응, 방금, 퍼시벌 경이——”\n\n“들어왔었니?”\n\n“아니, 밖에서 문을 쾅 쳐서 나를 놀라게 했어. 내가 ‘누구세요?’ 했더니 ‘알면서.’ 하고 대답하더구나. ‘마음을 고쳐먹고 나머지를 말할 텐가? 말하게 될 거다! 언젠가는 내가 기어이 짜내고 말 테니까. 당신은 앤 캐서릭이 지금 어디 있는지 알고 있어.’ ‘정말이에요, 정말이지,’ 하고 내가 말했지. ‘저는 몰라요.’ ‘알고 있어!’ 하고 그이가 되받았어. ‘그 고집을 꺾어 놓고 말겠다. 명심해! 기어이 짜내고 말겠어!’ 그 말을 남기고 갔어. 매리언, 채 오 분도 안 됐어.”\n\n그는 앤을 찾지 못한 것이다! 그날 밤은 우리가 무사했다. 그는 아직 앤을 찾지 못했다.\n\n“내려가려는 거지, 매리언? 저녁에 다시 올라와 줘.”\n\n“그래, 그래. 내가 좀 늦더라도 불안해하지 마. 너무 일찍 자리를 뜨는 바람에 저 사람들 심기를 건드리지 않도록 조심해야 하니까.”\n\n저녁 식사 종이 울려 나는 서둘러 갔다.\n\n퍼시벌 경이 포스코 백작부인을 식당으로 안내했고, 백작이 내게 팔을 내밀었다. 그는 덥고 얼굴이 붉었으며, 여느 때처럼 빈틈없이 차려입지 않았다. 그 역시 저녁 식사 전에 밖에 나갔다가 늦게 돌아온 것일까? 아니면 그저 여느 때보다 더위를 좀 더 심하게 타는 것뿐일까?\n\n어느 쪽이든, 그는 무언가 남모를 성가심이나 근심에 시달리고 있는 것이 분명했고, 그 온갖 기만의 재주로도 그것을 온전히 감추지는 못했다. 식사 내내 그는 퍼시벌 경 못지않게 말이 없었고, 이따금 아내를 은밀히 불안해하는 눈빛으로 바라보았는데, 내가 아는 한 그런 모습은 처음이었다. 그가 여전히 여느 때처럼 빈틈없이 해내는 유일한 사교적 의무는, 내게 한결같이 정중하고 세심하게 대하는 일이었다. 그가 무슨 고약한 목적을 품고 있는지는 아직도 알아낼 수 없지만, 그 속셈이 무엇이든, 나에 대한 변함없는 예의, 로라에 대한 변함없는 겸손, 그리고 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 퍼시벌 경의 서투른 난폭함을 변함없이 억누르는 것, 이것이 그가 이 집에 발을 들인 뒤로 목적을 이루기 위해 단호하고 빈틈없이 써 온 수단이다. 서재에서 그 증서가 나온 날 그가 처음으로 우리 편을 들어 끼어들었을 때 나는 그것을 의심했고, 이제는 확신한다.\n\n포스코 백작부인과 내가 자리에서 일어나 식탁을 뜨자, 백작도 우리를 응접실까지 배웅하려고 일어섰다.\n\n“왜 가는 거야?” 퍼시벌 경이 물었다. “자네 말이야, 포스코.”\n\n“저녁도 충분히 먹었고 술도 충분히 마셨으니 가는 걸세.” 백작이 대답했다. “여보게 퍼시벌, 숙녀들과 함께 들어오듯 함께 나가기도 하는 내 외국식 습관을 좀 봐주게나.”\n\n“허튼소리! 클라레 한 잔 더 한다고 해될 것 없네. 영국 사람답게 다시 앉게. 술잔을 놓고 자네와 삼십 분쯤 조용히 이야기하고 싶으이.”\n\n“조용한 이야기라면 기꺼이 하지, 퍼시벌. 하지만 지금은 아니고, 술잔을 놓고도 아닐세. 괜찮다면 저녁 늦게, 저녁 늦게 하세.”\n\n“예의도 바르시군!” 퍼시벌 경이 사납게 말했다. “남의 집에 와서 주인에게 하는 짓치고 참으로 예의 바르구먼!”\n\n나는 식사 중에 그가 백작을 불안한 눈으로 바라보는 것을 한 번 이상 보았고, 백작은 그 눈길을 마주 보기를 조심스레 피한다는 것도 알아챘다. 이 일과, 술잔을 놓고 잠깐 조용히 이야기하고 싶어 하는 주인의 조바심, 그리고 식탁에 다시 앉기를 완강히 마다하는 손님의 태도를 함께 놓고 보니, 그날 낮에 퍼시벌 경이 서재에서 나와 이야기 좀 하자고 친구에게 청했다가 헛물켠 일이 다시 떠올랐다. 백작은 오후에 그 단둘의 면담을 처음 청받았을 때 미루었고, 저녁 식탁에서 두 번째로 청받았을 때도 또 미루었다. 두 사람 사이에 오갈 이야기의 주제가 무엇이든, 그것은 퍼시벌 경이 보기에는 분명 중요한 주제였고, 그 이야기를 꺼리는 기색이 뚜렷한 것으로 미루어 보면 백작이 보기에는 아마 위험한 주제이기도 했다.\n\n이런 생각들이 식당에서 응접실로 옮겨 가는 동안 떠올랐다. 친구가 저를 버리고 간다며 퍼시벌 경이 성을 내며 한 말은 조금도 효과가 없었다. 백작은 고집스레 우리를 따라 찻상까지 왔다가, 방에서 일이 분쯤 기다리더니, 현관으로 나갔다가, 우편 가방을 손에 들고 돌아왔다. 그때가 여덟 시, 블랙워터 파크에서 늘 편지를 부치러 보내는 시각이었다.\n\n“부치실 편지가 있으신가요, 홀콤 양?” 그가 가방을 들고 다가오며 물었다.\n\n차를 따르던 포스코 백작부인이 설탕 집게를 손에 든 채 내 대답을 들으려고 멈추는 것이 보였다.\n\n“없습니다, 백작님, 고맙습니다. 오늘은 편지가 없어요.”\n\n그는 그때 방에 있던 하인에게 가방을 넘기고, 피아노 앞에 앉아 흥겨운 나폴리 거리 노래 「라 미아 카롤리나」의 가락을 두 번 연달아 쳤다. 무슨 일에나 여자들 중에서도 가장 굼뜬 그의 아내는, 나라도 그보다 빨리는 못 했을 만큼 재빨리 차를 따라, 이 분 만에 제 잔을 비우고, 소리 없이 방을 빠져나갔다.\n\n나도 그 여자를 따라 일어섰다. 한편으로는 그 여자가 위층에서 로라에게 무슨 흉계를 꾸미려는 게 아닌가 의심스러웠기 때문이고, 한편으로는 그 남편과 한방에 단둘이 남지 않기로 마음먹었기 때문이었다.\n\n문에 이르기도 전에 백작이 차 한 잔을 청하며 나를 붙잡았다. 나는 차를 따라 주고 두 번째로 빠져나가려 했다. 그는 또 나를 붙잡았다. 이번에는 피아노로 돌아가더니, 조국의 명예가 걸린 문제라고 선언하며 느닷없이 음악 이야기로 내게 묻는 것이었다.\n\n나는 음악에 대해 아무것도 모르고 그쪽으로는 취미도 전혀 없다고 헛되이 변명했다. 그는 내가 더 항변할 여지를 남기지 않는 열의로 다시 물어 올 뿐이었다. 영국인과 독일인은, 그가 분개하여 말하기를, 이탈리아인이 높은 차원의 음악을 가꿀 줄 모른다고 늘 헐뜯는다. 우리는 늘 오라토리오 이야기를 하고 저들은 늘 교향곡 이야기를 한다. 우리는, 또 저들은 그의 불멸의 벗이자 동포인 로시니를 잊었단 말인가? 「이집트의 모세」야말로 연주회장에서 차갑게 불리는 대신 무대 위에서 연기된 숭고한 오라토리오가 아니고 무엇인가? 「기욤 텔」 서곡이야말로 이름만 다른 교향곡이 아니고 무엇인가? 「이집트의 모세」를 들어 본 적이 있는가? 이것과 이것과 이것을 들어 보고, 인간의 손으로 이보다 더 숭고하고 성스럽고 웅장한 것이 지어진 적이 있는지 말해 보겠는가? 그러고는 내 동의도 반대도 기다리지 않고, 내내 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 피아노를 우레같이 두들기고 크고 드높은 열정으로 노래하기 시작했다. 다만 이따금 스스로 멈추고 사나운 기세로 곡목을 알려 줄 뿐이었다. “어둠의 재앙 속 이집트인들의 합창, 홀콤 양!” “율법판을 든 모세의 레치타티보.” “홍해를 건널 때 이스라엘 백성의 기도. 아하! 아하! 이것이 성스럽지 않소? 이것이 숭고하지 않소?” 그 억센 손 아래 피아노가 떨었고, 굵은 저음이 음들을 우레처럼 쏟아 내고 무거운 발이 바닥을 구르며 박자를 맞추는 동안 탁자 위의 찻잔들이 달그락거렸다.\n\n자신의 노래와 연주에 도취한 그 환희의 폭발에는, 그리고 내가 문 쪽으로 점점 더 물러나는 동안 그것이 내게 미치는 효과를 바라보는 그 득의양양함에는 무언가 끔찍한 것이, 무언가 사납고 악마 같은 것이 있었다. 마침내 나는 풀려났는데, 내 힘이 아니라 퍼시벌 경이 끼어든 덕분이었다. 그가 식당 문을 열고 저 “빌어먹을 소음”이 무슨 짓이냐고 성난 목소리로 외쳤던 것이다. 백작은 즉시 피아노에서 일어섰다. “아! 퍼시벌이 온다면,” 그가 말했다. “화성도 선율도 끝장이지요. 음악의 여신은 기겁하여 우리를 버리고 떠나니, 늙고 뚱뚱한 이 악사는 남은 열정을 바깥바람에 흩뿌리는 수밖에요, 홀콤 양!” 그는 성큼성큼 베란다로 나가 주머니에 손을 찌르고, 정원에서 나직한 소리로 「모세의 레치타티보」를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n퍼시벌 경이 식당 창문에서 그를 부르는 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 그는 아랑곳하지 않았다. 아예 들으려 하지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 그토록 미뤄 온 두 사람의 조용한 이야기는 여전히 미뤄지고, 여전히 백작의 뜻과 기분이 내킬 때를 기다려야 했다.\n\n그는 아내가 우리를 떠난 때부터 거의 반 시간 동안 나를 응접실에 붙들어 두었다. 그동안 그 여자는 어디에 있었고, 무엇을 하고 있었을까?\n\n알아보려고 위층으로 올라갔지만 아무것도 알아내지 못했고, 로라에게 물어보니 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다고 했다. 아무도 로라를 방해하지 않았고, 바깥방에서든 복도에서든 비단 옷자락이 스치는 희미한 소리조차 들리지 않았다는 것이다.\n\n그때가 아홉 시 이십 분 전이었다. 내 방에 가서 일기를 가져온 다음 돌아와 로라 곁에 앉아, 때로는 쓰고 때로는 멈추어 이야기를 나누었다. 아무도 우리 가까이 오지 않았고 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 우리는 열 시까지 함께 있었다. 그러고 나서 나는 일어나 마지막으로 기운을 북돋우는 말을 건네고 잘 자라는 인사를 했다. 아침에 제일 먼저 내가 들여다보러 오기로 정한 뒤, 로라는 다시 문을 잠갔다.\n\n잠자리에 들기 전에 일기에 몇 줄 더 보태야 했고, 그 고단한 하루의 마지막으로 로라를 두고 나와 다시 응접실로 내려가면서, 나는 그저 얼굴만 비치고 양해를 구한 뒤 여느 때보다 한 시간 일찍 물러나기로 마음먹었다.\n\n퍼시벌 경과 백작 부부가 함께 앉아 있었다. 퍼시벌 경은 안락의자에서 하품을 하고, 백작은 책을 읽고, 포스코 백작부인은 부채질을 하고 있었다. 이상하게도 이번에는 그 여자의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올라 있었다. 더위를 타는 법이 없던 사람이 오늘 밤만은 틀림없이 더위에 시달리고 있었다.\n\n“백작부인, 오늘은 여느 때만큼 편치 않으신 것 같군요?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“저도 마침 그 말씀을 드리려던 참이었어요.” 부인이 대답했다. “안색이 창백하시군요, 그대.”\n\n그대라니! 그 여자가 나를 그토록 허물없이 부른 것은 처음이었다! 그 말을 할 때 얼굴에는 무례한 미소까지 떠올라 있었다.\n\n“고약한 두통이 도져서요.” 내가 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n“아, 그러세요? 운동 부족이겠지요? 저녁 식사 전에 산책이라도 하셨으면 딱 좋았을 텐데요.” 부인은 “산책”이라는 말을 묘하게 힘주어 말했다. 내가 나가는 것을 보았을까? 보았다 한들 상관없다. 편지는 이제 패니의 손에 안전히 들어가 있으니까.\n\n“담배나 한 대 피우러 가세, 포스코.” 퍼시벌 경이 또 한 번 불안한 눈길로 친구를 보며 일어서서 말했다.\n\n“기꺼이 하지, 퍼시벌. 숙녀들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에 말일세.” 백작이 대답했다.\n\n“백작부인, 제가 먼저 물러나는 본을 보이더라도 용서하십시오.” 내가 말했다. “저 같은 두통에는 잠자리에 드는 것 말고는 약이 없으니까요.”\n\n나는 작별 인사를 했다. 악수를 할 때 그 여자의 얼굴에는 여전히 무례한 미소가 걸려 있었다. 퍼시벌 경은 내게 눈길도 주지 않았다. 그는 나와 함께 방을 나갈 기색이 조금도 없는 포스코 백작부인을 초조하게 바라보고 있었다. 백작은 책 뒤에서 혼자 미소 지었다. 퍼시벌 경이 바라는 그 조용한 이야기는 또 한 번 미뤄지게 되었고, 이번에는 백작부인이 걸림돌이었다.\n\n# IX\n\n6월 19일.—내 방에 무사히 들어가 문을 닫아걸자마자 나는 이 일기장을 펴고, 아직 쓰지 못한 채 남아 있던 그날의 기록을 마저 이어 쓸 채비를 했다.\n\n십 분 남짓 나는 펜을 손에 쥔 채 아무것도 쓰지 못하고 앉아, 지난 열두 시간 동안 일어난 일들을 곱씹었다. 마침내 붓을 들려 했을 때 나는 여태껏 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 곤란을 느꼈다. 눈앞의 일에 생각을 붙들어 매려고 아무리 애를 써도, 생각은 기이하리만치 집요하게 오직 한 방향으로—퍼시벌 경과 백작 쪽으로—흘러가 버렸다. 일기에 쏟으려 했던 관심은 그 대신 온통 두 사람 사이의 밀담으로 쏠렸다. 하루 종일 미뤄지다가 이제 밤의 정적과 고요 속에서 열리게 될 그 밀담으로.\n\n이런 삐딱한 심사로는 아침 이후에 있었던 일들이 도무지 떠오르지 않았고, 일기장을 덮고 잠시 그것에서 벗어나는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다.\n\n나는 침실에서 거실로 통하는 문을 열고 나간 다음, 화장대에 놓아둔 촛불이 바람에 흔들려 사고라도 날까 봐 문을 다시 닫았다. 거실 창은 활짝 열려 있었고, 나는 맥없이 몸을 내밀어 밤을 내다보았다.\n\n어둡고 고요했다. 달도 별도 보이지 않았다. 미동도 없는 무거운 공기 속에 비 냄새 같은 것이 났고, 나는 창밖으로 손을 내밀어 보았다. 아니었다. 비는 아직 을러대기만 할 뿐 내리지는 않았다.\n\n나는 십오 분 가까이 창턱에 기대어 선 채 캄캄한 어둠 속을 멍하니 내다보았다. 이따금 아래층에서 들려오는 하인들의 목소리나 멀리서 문 닫히는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다.\n\n지친 몸을 창에서 돌려 침실로 돌아가 일기의 못다 쓴 대목을 다시 붙들어 볼 참이었는데, 바로 그때 무거운 밤공기를 타고 담배 연기 냄새가 살금살금 다가오는 것이 느껴졌다. 다음 순간 나는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 조그맣고 붉은 불똥 하나가 집 저편 끝에서 다가오는 것을 보았다. 발소리는 들리지 않았고 보이는 것은 그 불똥뿐이었다. 그것은 밤을 가로질러 나아와 내가 서 있던 창을 지나쳐, 화장대 위에 불을 켜 둔 내 침실 창 맞은편에서 멈추었다.\n\n불똥은 잠시 그 자리에 머물러 있다가 다시 왔던 방향으로 되돌아갔다. 그 움직임을 눈으로 좇는데 첫 번째 것보다 큰 두 번째 붉은 불똥이 멀리서 다가오는 것이 보였다. 두 불똥은 어둠 속에서 만났다. 누가 궐련을 피우고 누가 여송연을 피우는지 떠올린 나는, 백작이 먼저 나와 내 창 아래에서 살피고 엿듣다가 뒤늦게 퍼시벌 경이 합류했음을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 둘 다 잔디밭 위를 걷고 있었던 게 틀림없었다. 그렇지 않았다면 퍼시벌 경의 육중한 발소리만은 분명 들렸을 테니까. 백작의 사뿐한 걸음이야 자갈길 위에서도 놓쳤을 수 있겠지만.\n\n나는 창가에서 조용히 기다렸다. 어두운 방 안에 있는 나를 둘 중 누구도 볼 수 없으리라 확신하면서.\n\n“무슨 일인가?” 퍼시벌 경이 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것이 들렸다. “왜 들어와 앉지 않고?”\n\n“저 창의 불이 꺼지는 걸 보고 싶소.” 백작이 부드럽게 대답했다.\n\n“불이 켜져 있는 게 무슨 해가 된다고?”\n\n“아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다는 뜻이지. 그 여자는 무언가 눈치챌 만큼 영민하고, 틈만 나면 아래층으로 내려와 엿들을 만큼 대담하오. 인내요, 퍼시벌—인내.”\n\n“허튼소리! 자넨 늘 인내 타령이군.”\n\n“곧 다른 얘기도 하게 될 거요. 이보시오 벗이여, 그대는 지금 가정이라는 낭떠러지 끝에 서 있고, 만일 내가 그 여인들에게 기회를 한 번만 더 준다면, 내 신성한 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대 그들은 그대를 그 아래로 밀어 버릴 거요!”\n\n“대체 무슨 소리를 하는 건가?”\n\n“설명은 차차 하기로 합시다, 퍼시벌. 저 창의 불이 꺼지고, 내가 서재 양옆 방들을 한번 살펴보고 계단도 슬쩍 엿본 뒤에.”\n\n두 사람은 천천히 멀어져 갔고, 그 뒤로 이어진 이야기는 (처음부터 끝까지 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 나눈 터라) 더는 들리지 않았다. 상관없었다. 나의 영민함과 용기에 대한 백작의 평가가 옳았음을 입증해 보이기로 마음먹기에는 이미 충분히 들었으니까. 붉은 불똥들이 어둠 속으로 사라지기도 전에 나는 결심을 굳혔다. 저 두 사람이 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눌 때 엿듣는 자가 반드시 있어야 하며, 백작이 아무리 조심스레 대비하더라도 그 엿듣는 자는 바로 나여야 한다고. 그 행위를 내 양심 앞에서 정당화하고 실행에 옮길 용기를 낼 동기 하나가 필요했을 뿐인데, 그 동기라면 내게 있었다. 로라의 명예, 로라의 행복—아니 로라의 목숨 그 자체가 오늘 밤 내 밝은 귀와 충실한 기억력에 달려 있을지도 몰랐다.\n\n백작이 퍼시벌 경과 어떤 설명을 시작하기 전에 서재 양옆 방들과 계단까지 살펴보겠다고 말하는 것을 나는 들었다. 그 말만으로도 그가 이야기를 나눌 장소로 서재를 염두에 두고 있음을 알기에 충분했다. 그 결론에 이르기까지 걸린 그 짧은 한순간은, 동시에 그의 경계를 무력화할 방법—달리 말해 집 아래층으로 내려가는 위험을 전혀 무릅쓰지 않고도 그와 퍼시벌 경이 주고받는 말을 들을 방법—을 내게 일러 준 순간이기도 했다.\n\n일층 방들을 이야기하면서 나는 그 바깥의 베란다를 지나가는 말로 언급한 적이 있다. 방들은 모두 처마 장식에서 바닥까지 내려오는 프랑스식 창을 통해 그 베란다로 열려 있었다. 이 베란다의 지붕은 평평했고, 빗물은 관을 타고 집에 물을 대는 물탱크로 흘러 들어갔다. 침실들 앞을 따라 뻗은 그 좁다란 납 지붕은 창틀에서 채 삼 피트가 안 되게 내려온 자리에 있었는데, 그 위에는 화분들이 서로 넉넉한 간격을 두고 한 줄로 놓여 있었고, 강풍에 떨어지지 않도록 지붕 가장자리를 따라 장식용 쇠 난간이 둘러쳐져 있었다.\n\n이제 내게 떠오른 계획은 거실 창으로 빠져나가 그 지붕에 올라선 뒤, 소리 없이 기어가 서재 창 바로 위에 해당하는 자리에 이르러 화분들 사이에 몸을 웅크리고 바깥 난간에 귀를 대는 것이었다. 만일 오늘 밤에도 퍼시벌 경과 백작이 여러 날 밤 그래 왔듯 열린 창가에 바싹 의자를 붙여 앉아 베란다 아래 놓인 아연 정원 의자에 발을 뻗은 채 담배를 피운다면, 속삭임보다 조금이라도 큰 소리로 주고받는 말은 (그리고 우리 모두 겪어서 알듯이 긴 대화를 속삭임만으로 나눌 수는 없다) 어김없이 내 귀에 닿을 터였다. 반면 오늘 밤 그들이 방 안쪽 깊숙이 앉기로 한다면 나는 거의 아무것도 듣지 못할 공산이 컸다. 그럴 경우에는 아래층에서 그들의 눈을 속이는 훨씬 위험한 모험을 감행해야 했다.\n\n우리가 처한 절박한 사정이 내 결심을 아무리 단단히 떠받쳐 주었어도, 나는 그 마지막 사태만은 피할 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다. 내 용기란 결국 여자의 용기일 뿐이었고, 한밤중에 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 손이 닿는 곳에서 일층에 내 몸을 맡긴다는 생각을 하자 그 용기는 거의 무너질 지경이었다.\n\n나는 우선 더 안전한 베란다 지붕 쪽을 시도해 보려고 살며시 침실로 돌아갔다.\n\n여러 이유에서 옷을 완전히 갈아입는 일이 무엇보다 시급했다. 우선 비단 드레스를 벗었다. 이 고요한 밤에 그 옷에서 나는 아주 작은 소리도 나를 들키게 할 수 있었기 때문이다. 다음으로 희고 부피 큰 속옷들을 벗고 짙은 색 플란넬 페티코트로 갈아입었다. 그 위에 검은 여행용 망토를 걸치고 두건을 머리에 뒤집어썼다. 평소의 저녁 차림으로는 적어도 남자 셋 몫의 자리를 차지했다. 지금 차림으로 옷을 몸에 바싹 여미면 아무리 좁은 틈이라도 나보다 수월하게 빠져나갈 남자는 없을 터였다. 한쪽에는 화분들이, 다른 쪽에는 집의 벽과 창이 있는 베란다 지붕 위의 얼마 안 되는 폭을 생각하면 이는 심각하게 따져 볼 문제였다. 무엇 하나라도 넘어뜨린다면, 아주 작은 소리라도 낸다면, 그 결과가 어떠할지 누가 알겠는가?\n\n나는 성냥을 초 가까이에 놓아둔 뒤에야 불을 끄고 더듬더듬 거실로 돌아갔다. 침실 문을 잠갔던 것처럼 그 문도 잠그고—그런 다음 조용히 창을 넘어 조심스레 베란다의 납 지붕에 발을 디뎠다.\n\n내 두 방은 우리가 모두 지내는 새 별채의 가장 안쪽 끝에 있었고, 서재 바로 위의 자리에 이르려면 창 다섯 개를 지나야 했다. 첫 번째 창은 비어 있는 손님방의 것이었다. 두 번째와 세 번째 창은 로라의 방이었다. 네 번째 창은 퍼시벌 경의 방이었다. 다섯 번째는 백작부인의 방이었다. 내가 지날 필요가 없는 나머지 창들은 백작의 화장방과 욕실, 그리고 두 번째 빈 손님방의 창이었다.\n\n아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 처음 베란다에 올라섰을 때 사방은 눈이 멀 듯 새까만 밤의 어둠뿐이었다—포스코 부인의 창이 내려다보는 그 한 곳만 빼고. 하필 내가 가려던 그 자리, 서재 바로 위 그곳에서 나는 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다! 백작부인은 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 것이다.\n\n물러서기에는 너무 늦었고 기다릴 겨를도 없었다. 나는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰고 나아가되 오직 나 자신의 조심성과 밤의 어둠에 안전을 맡기기로 했다. “로라를 위해서!” 한 손으로는 망토를 몸에 바싹 여미고 다른 손으로는 집의 벽을 더듬으며 지붕 위로 첫걸음을 내디딜 때 나는 속으로 그렇게 되뇌었다. 몇 인치 옆에 있는 화분에 발이 부딪히는 위험을 무릅쓰느니 벽에 바싹 붙어 스치듯 가는 편이 나았다.\n\n어두운 손님방 창을 지났다. 한 발 한 발 내디딜 때마다 체중을 싣기 전에 먼저 발끝으로 납 지붕을 짚어 보았다. 로라의 방의 어두운 창들을 지났다(“하느님, 오늘 밤 그 아이를 지켜 주소서!”). 퍼시벌 경의 방의 어두운 창을 지났다. 그런 다음 잠시 멈추었다가 두 손으로 몸을 지탱하며 무릎을 꿇고, 불 켜진 창 아래쪽과 베란다 지붕 사이의 나지막한 벽에 몸을 숨긴 채 목표한 자리까지 기어갔다.\n\n큰맘 먹고 창을 올려다보니 윗부분만 열려 있고 안쪽 블라인드는 내려져 있었다. 보고 있는 사이 포스코 부인의 그림자가 블라인드의 흰 화폭을 가로질러 지나갔다가—다시 천천히 되돌아가는 것이 보였다. 여기까지는 그녀가 내 소리를 듣지 못한 것이다. 들었다면 창을 열고 내다볼 용기까지는 없었더라도 그림자는 분명 블라인드 앞에 멈춰 섰을 테니까.\n\n나는 베란다 난간에 옆으로 몸을 붙였다. 먼저 양옆 화분의 위치를 손으로 더듬어 확인한 뒤였다. 그 사이에 내가 앉을 만한 자리가 딱 그만큼 있었고 그 이상은 없었다. 난간에 머리를 살며시 기대자 왼쪽 화분에 핀 꽃의 향긋한 잎사귀가 내 뺨을 스쳤다.\n\n아래에서 처음 들려온 소리는 문 세 개가 잇달아 열리거나 닫히는(십중팔구 닫히는) 소리였다. 백작이 살펴보겠다고 다짐한, 현관과 서재 양옆 방으로 통하는 문들이 분명했다. 처음 눈에 들어온 것은 다시 그 붉은 불똥이었다. 베란다 아래에서 밤 속으로 나와 내 창 쪽으로 옮겨 가더니 잠시 멎었다가 출발했던 자리로 되돌아갔다.\n\n“가만 좀 못 있겠나! 대체 언제 앉을 셈인가?” 발밑에서 퍼시벌 경의 목소리가 으르렁거렸다.\n\n“후! 덥기도 하지!” 백작이 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬고 씩씩거리며 말했다.\n\n그 탄식에 뒤이어 베란다 아래 타일 바닥에 정원 의자를 끄는 소리가 났다. 여느 때처럼 창가에 바싹 붙어 앉으려 한다는 것을 알려 주는 반가운 소리였다. 여기까지는 운이 내 편이었다. 두 사람이 의자에 자리를 잡을 때 탑의 시계가 열두 시 십오 분 전을 알렸다. 열린 창 너머로 포스코 부인이 하품하는 소리가 들렸고, 그녀의 그림자가 블라인드의 흰 화폭을 또 한 번 가로지르는 것이 보였다.\n\n그러는 동안 퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아래에서 이야기를 시작했다. 이따금 평소보다 목소리를 조금 낮추기는 했지만 속삭임까지 떨어뜨리지는 않았다. 내가 처한 상황의 기이함과 위험, 그리고 도무지 떨쳐지지 않는 포스코 부인의 불 켜진 창에 대한 두려움 탓에, 처음에는 정신을 붙들고 아래의 대화에만 주의를 모으기가 어려웠다. 아니, 거의 불가능했다. 몇 분 동안은 대화의 대강만 겨우 짚어 낼 수 있었다. 불이 켜진 그 한 창은 자기 아내의 방이고, 집 일층은 아무도 없이 말끔하니 이제 사고를 걱정하지 않고 이야기해도 된다고 백작이 말하는 것을 나는 알아들었다. 퍼시벌 경은 그저 하루 종일 자기 뜻을 부당하게 무시하고 자기 이익을 소홀히 했다고 벗을 나무랄 뿐이었다. 그러자 백작은 온 신경을 앗아 간 어떤 골칫거리와 근심에 시달렸으며, 방해받거나 엿들릴 염려가 없다고 확신할 수 있는 때라야 안심하고 설명할 수 있다고 항변했다. “우리 일은 심각한 고비에 와 있소, 퍼시벌.” 그가 말했다. “앞날을 결정하려면 오늘 밤 은밀히 결정해야 하오.”\n\n백작의 그 문장이야말로 내 주의력이 말 그대로 온전히 붙잡아 낸 첫 문장이었다. 이 대목부터는 몇 번의 끊김과 방해가 있기는 했지만 내 온 관심이 숨죽인 채 그 대화에 붙박였고, 나는 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 따라갔다.\n\n“고비?” 퍼시벌 경이 되뇌었다. “자네가 생각하는 것보다 더 고약한 고비일세, 내 말해 두지.”\n\n“지난 하루 이틀 그대의 처신을 보니 그러리라 짐작은 했소.” 상대가 냉랭하게 대꾸했다. “허나 잠깐. 내가 모르는 데로 나아가기 전에, 내가 아는 것부터 확실히 해 둡시다. 앞으로 올 시간에 대해 무언가 제안하기 전에, 지나간 시간에 대해 내 짐작이 맞는지부터 봅시다.”\n\n“브랜디에 물 좀 가져올 동안 잠깐. 자네도 좀 들게.”\n\n“고맙소, 퍼시벌. 찬물을 주면 기꺼이 들겠소. 숟가락 하나와 설탕 그릇도. 오 쉬크레, 그러니까 설탕물 말이오, 벗이여—그 이상은 아무것도.”\n\n“자네 나이에 설탕물이라니!—자! 그 느끼한 물건이나 타 드시게. 외국인들이란 다 똑같군.”\n\n“자, 들으시오, 퍼시벌. 내가 이해하는 대로 우리 처지를 있는 그대로 펼쳐 놓을 테니 맞는지 틀리는지 그대가 말해 주오. 그대와 나는 둘 다 형편이 몹시 궁색해진 채로 대륙에서 이 집으로 돌아왔소—”\n\n“짧게 하게! 나는 수천 파운드가, 자네는 수백 파운드가 필요했고, 그 돈이 없으면 우리 둘 다 사이좋게 파멸할 판이었지. 사정은 그렇네. 알아서 요리해 보게. 계속하게.”\n\n“좋소, 퍼시벌. 그대의 든든한 영국식 말투를 빌리자면, 그대는 수천 파운드가 나는 수백 파운드가 필요했고, 그것을 마련할 유일한 길은 그대가 아내의 힘을 빌려 자기 급한 사정을 메울 돈을(내 가엾은 수백 파운드 몫의 여유를 조금 얹어서) 조달하는 것이었소. 영국으로 오는 길에 내가 그대의 아내에 대해 뭐라고 말했소?—그리고 이곳에 와서 홀콤 양이 어떤 여자인지 내 눈으로 직접 본 뒤에 다시 뭐라고 말했소?”\n\n“내가 그걸 어떻게 아나? 늘 그렇듯 쉴 새 없이 지껄여 댔겠지.”\n\n“나는 이렇게 말했소. 벗이여, 인간의 재간은 지금껏 남자가 여자를 다루는 방법을 단 두 가지밖에 찾아내지 못했소. 하나는 여자를 때려눕히는 것이오. 짐승 같은 하층민들이 널리 쓰는 방법이지만, 그 위에 있는 세련되고 교양 있는 계급에게는 도무지 혐오스러운 짓이지. 다른 하나는(훨씬 오래 걸리고 훨씬 어렵지만 결국은 그에 못지않게 확실한데) 여자가 걸어오는 도발을 결코 받아 주지 않는 것이오. 이 이치는 짐승에게도 통하고 아이에게도 통하며, 다 자란 아이나 다름없는 여자에게도 통하오. 짐승도 아이도 여자도 하나같이 갖추지 못한 자질이 바로 조용한 결단력이오. 저들이 주인의 그 우월한 자질을 한 번이라도 흔들 수 있다면 저들이 그를 이기는 것이고, 결코 흔들지 못한다면 그가 저들을 이기는 것이오. 나는 그대에게 말했소. 아내에게 돈 문제를 도와 달라 할 때 그 명백한 진리를 기억하라고. 그리고 아내의 언니인 홀콤 양 앞에서는 그것을 두 배 세 배로 기억하라고 말했소. 그대는 기억했소? 이 집에서 우리를 얽어 든 온갖 사태 가운데 단 한 번도 기억하지 않았소. 그대의 아내와 그 언니가 걸어올 수 있는 도발이란 도발은 그대가 죄다 즉시 받아 주었소. 그 미친 성미 탓에 증서에 서명을 받지 못했고, 당장 쓸 현금을 놓쳤고, 홀콤 양이 처음으로 변호사에게 편지를 쓰게 만들었소.”\n\n“처음이라니! 또 썼단 말인가?”\n\n“그렇소, 오늘 또 썼소.”\n\n베란다 바닥에 의자가 쓰러졌다—발로 걷어차기라도 한 듯 요란한 소리를 내며 쓰러졌다.\n\n백작의 폭로가 그렇게 퍼시벌 경의 분노를 돋운 것은 내게 다행이었다. 내 일이 또다시 발각되었음을 듣는 순간 나는 어찌나 놀랐는지 기대고 있던 난간이 다시 삐걱 소리를 냈다. 그가 나를 따라 여관까지 왔던 걸까? 우편 자루에 넣을 편지가 없다고 내가 말했을 때, 편지를 패니에게 건넸으리라 짐작한 걸까? 설령 그렇다 해도, 편지가 내 손에서 곧장 그 아이의 옷섶으로 들어갔는데 어떻게 그것들을 살펴볼 수 있었단 말인가?\n\n“그대의 행운의 별에 감사하시오.” 이어서 백작이 말하는 것이 들렸다. “그대가 저지르는 해악을 저지르는 족족 되돌려 놓을 내가 이 집에 있다는 것을 말이오. 그대가 미친 나머지, 짓궂은 어리석음으로 아내에게 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔듯 오늘 홀콤 양에게도 그리하자고 했을 때 내가 안 된다고 말한 것을, 그 행운의 별에 감사하시오. 그대의 눈은 어디에 달렸소? 홀콤 양을 보고도 그 여인이 남자의 선견지명과 결단력을 지녔다는 걸 모르겠소? 그 여인을 내 벗으로 둘 수 있다면 나는 이 손가락을 튕겨 온 세상을 비웃겠소. 그 여인이 내 적이라면, 이 두뇌와 경험을 다 갖춘 나, 그대가 백 번도 더 말했듯 악마만큼이나 교활한 이 포스코조차도—그대들 영국 말로—달걀 껍데기 위를 걷는 심정이오! 그런데 이 대단한 여인을—나는 설탕물로 그 건강을 위해 축배를 드오—사랑과 용기의 힘으로 바위처럼 굳건히 서서 우리 두 사람과 그대의 저 가엾고 여리고 어여쁜 금발 아내 사이를 가로막고 선 이 대단한 여인을, 내가 그대의 이익과 나의 이익을 위해 맞서고는 있으나 온 영혼으로 감탄해 마지않는 이 훌륭한 여인을, 그대는 마치 여느 여자들보다 더 영민할 것도 더 대담할 것도 없다는 듯이 극단으로 몰아붙이고 있소. 퍼시벌! 퍼시벌! 그대는 실패해 마땅하고, 또 실제로 실패했소.”\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 나에 대한 저 악당의 말을 여기 적어 두는 것은 그것을 기억하려 함이다. 언젠가 그의 면전에서 단 한 번 속 시원히 말하고, 그 말들을 하나하나 그의 얼굴에 되던져 줄 날이 오기를 아직 바라기 때문이다.\n\n침묵을 다시 깬 쪽은 퍼시벌 경이었다.\n\n“그래, 그래, 실컷 윽박지르고 큰소리치게.” 그가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “돈 문제만 문제가 아닐세. 내가 아는 만큼 자네도 안다면—자네야말로 그 여인들에게 강경한 수를 쓰자고 할 걸세.”\n\n“그 두 번째 골칫거리는 때가 되면 다루기로 합시다.” 백작이 응수했다. “퍼시벌, 그대는 마음껏 스스로 갈피를 잃어도 좋으나 나까지 갈피를 잃게 하지는 못하오. 돈 문제부터 매듭짓읍시다. 내가 그대의 고집을 꺾었소? 그 성미가 그대 자신을 돕지 못하게 한다는 걸 내가 보여 주었소?—아니면 다시 돌아가서 (그대의 사랑스러운 직설적 영국 말로 하자면) 좀 더 윽박지르고 큰소리쳐야겠소?”\n\n“쳇! 나를 타박하기야 쉽지. 어떻게 해야 하는지 말해 보게—그게 좀 더 어려운 일이니.”\n\n“그렇소? 흥! 해야 할 일은 이것이오. 그대는 오늘 밤부터 이 일에 대한 지휘를 전부 내려놓고, 앞으로는 오직 내 손에만 맡기시오. 나는 지금 실무적인 영국 신사와 이야기하고 있소—그렇지 않소? 자, 실무가 양반, 그만하면 되겠소?”\n\n“내가 자네에게 전부 맡긴다면 자네는 무엇을 하겠다는 건가?”\n\n“먼저 대답부터 하시오. 내 손에 맡길 거요, 말 거요?”\n\n“맡긴다고 치세—그다음은?”\n\n“우선 몇 가지 물어봅시다, 퍼시벌. 나는 아직 조금 더 기다리며 사정이 이끄는 대로 따라야 하고, 그 사정이 어떻게 흘러갈지를 가능한 모든 방면에서 알아야 하오. 지체할 시간이 없소. 홀콤 양이 오늘 두 번째로 변호사에게 편지를 썼다는 건 이미 말했소.”\n\n“그걸 어떻게 알아냈나? 뭐라고 썼던가?”\n\n“그걸 말해 주면, 퍼시벌, 우리는 결국 지금 이 자리로 되돌아올 뿐이오. 내가 알아냈다는 것으로 족하오—그리고 그것을 알아내느라 생긴 골칫거리와 근심 때문에 오늘 하루 종일 내가 그대에게 그토록 붙잡히지 않았던 거요. 자, 그대의 사정을 되짚어 봅시다—함께 그 일을 논한 지도 꽤 되었으니. 아내의 서명이 없는 채로 돈은 석 달짜리 어음으로 마련되었소—생각만 해도 이 가난뱅이 외국인의 머리카락이 곤두설 만큼 비싼 값에! 어음 만기가 돌아오면, 정말이지 참으로 그대 아내의 도움 말고는 그것을 갚을 길이 이 세상에 없소?”\n\n“없네.”\n\n“무어라! 은행에 돈이 한 푼도 없단 말이오?”\n\n“수천이 필요한 판에 수백 파운드쯤 있지.”\n\n“빌릴 만한 다른 담보도 없소?”\n\n“실오라기 하나 없네.”\n\n“지금 이 순간 아내에게서 실제로 받고 있는 것은 무엇이오?”\n\n“이만 파운드의 이자뿐이지—하루하루 쓰기에도 빠듯하네.”\n\n“아내에게서 기대하는 것은 무엇이오?”\n\n“삼촌이 죽으면 연 삼천 파운드.”\n\n“훌륭한 재산이오, 퍼시벌. 그 삼촌이란 사람은 어떤 이오? 늙었소?”\n\n“아니—늙지도 젊지도 않네.”\n\n“성정이 무던하고 마음 편히 사는 사람이오? 결혼은 했소? 아니지—내 아내가 결혼하지 않았다고 말해 준 것 같은데.”\n\n“물론 안 했네. 결혼해서 아들이라도 있었다면 글라이드 부인이 그 재산의 다음 상속인이 될 리 없지. 그가 어떤 위인인지 말해 주지. 눈물이나 짜고 시시껄렁한 소리나 늘어놓는 이기적인 바보인 데다, 곁에 오는 사람마다 붙잡고 제 건강 타령으로 진저리를 내게 하는 자일세.”\n\n“그런 부류는 오래 사오, 퍼시벌. 게다가 전혀 뜻하지 않을 때 심술궂게 장가를 들지. 벗이여, 나는 그 연 삼천 파운드에 대한 그대의 몫을 그리 높이 쳐 주지 않겠소. 아내에게서 그대에게 오는 것이 그 밖에는 없소?”\n\n“없네.”\n\n“정말 아무것도?”\n\n“정말 아무것도—아내가 죽을 경우를 빼면.”\n\n“아하! 아내가 죽을 경우라.”\n\n또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다. 백작이 베란다에서 바깥 자갈길로 자리를 옮겼다. 목소리로 그가 움직였음을 알았다. “드디어 비가 오는군.” 그가 말하는 것이 들렸다. 정말 비가 오고 있었다. 내 망토의 상태를 보니 이미 꽤 오랫동안 굵게 내리고 있었던 모양이었다.\n\n백작이 베란다 아래로 돌아왔다—그가 다시 앉을 때 그 무게에 의자가 삐걱거리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n“자, 퍼시벌.” 그가 말했다. “글라이드 부인이 죽을 경우에는 그대가 무엇을 얻소?”\n\n“아내가 자식을 남기지 않는다면——”\n\n“그럴 법하오?”\n\n“그럴 법이라곤 조금도 없네——”\n\n“그러면?”\n\n“그야, 아내의 이만 파운드가 내게 오지.”\n\n“즉시 지급으로?”\n\n“즉시 지급으로.”\n\n두 사람은 또 한 번 잠잠해졌다. 목소리가 그치자 포스코 부인의 그림자가 다시 블라인드를 어둡게 물들였다. 이번에는 지나가는 대신 잠시 그대로 멈춰 섰다. 그녀의 손가락이 블라인드 모서리로 슬며시 다가와 한쪽으로 젖히는 것이 보였다. 곧장 나를 향해 내다보는 그녀의 희끄무레한 얼굴 윤곽이 창 뒤에 나타났다. 나는 검은 망토로 머리부터 발끝까지 뒤집어쓴 채 꼼짝하지 않았다. 나를 빠르게 적셔 오던 비가 유리창에 흘러내려 뿌옇게 흐려 놓은 덕에 그녀는 아무것도 보지 못했다. “비가 또 오네!” 그녀가 혼잣말하는 소리가 들렸다. 그녀는 블라인드를 내렸고, 나는 다시 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n아래에서 이야기가 이어졌다. 이번에는 백작이 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n“퍼시벌! 그대는 아내를 아끼오?”\n\n“포스코! 그건 좀 노골적인 질문이군.”\n\n“나는 노골적인 사람이오. 그러니 다시 묻겠소.”\n\n“대체 왜 나를 그런 눈으로 보는 건가?”\n\n“대답하지 않겠다? 좋소, 그렇다면 그대의 아내가 이 여름이 가기 전에 죽는다고 칩시다——”\n\n“그만두게, 포스코!”\n\n“그대의 아내가 죽는다고 칩시다——”\n\n“그만두라니까!”\n\n“그럴 경우 그대는 이만 파운드를 얻고, 잃는 것은——”\n\n“연 삼천 파운드의 가망을 잃겠지.”\n\n“그 아득한 가망이오, 퍼시벌—그저 아득한 가망일 뿐. 게다가 그대에게는 지금 당장 돈이 필요하오. 그대의 처지에서 얻는 것은 확실하고—잃는 것은 불확실하오.”\n\n“내 얘기만 하지 말고 자네 얘기도 하게. 내게 필요한 돈 가운데 일부는 자네를 위해 빌린 걸세. 그리고 얻는 것을 따지자면, 내 아내가 죽으면 자네 아내 주머니에 만 파운드가 들어가지. 그렇게 영민한 사람이 포스코 부인의 유산은 편리하게 잊어버린 모양이군. 그런 눈으로 나를 보지 말게! 참을 수가 없네! 자네의 그 눈초리에 그런 질문까지, 맹세코 내 살갗에 소름이 다 돋는군!”\n\n“그대의 살갗? 영국 말에서는 살갗이 곧 양심을 뜻하오? 나는 그대 아내의 죽음을 하나의 가능성으로서 말할 뿐이오. 어찌 못 하겠소? 그대의 증서와 유언장을 끄적거리는 점잖은 변호사들도 살아 있는 사람들의 죽음을 정면으로 마주보오. 변호사들이 그대의 살갗에 소름을 돋게 하오? 그런데 어찌 내가 그러겠소? 오늘 밤 내 할 일은 그대의 처지를 한 치의 오해도 없도록 밝히는 것이고, 이제 그 일을 마쳤소. 그대의 처지는 이러하오. 아내가 살아 있으면 그대는 그 양피지에 아내의 서명을 받아 어음을 갚소. 아내가 죽으면 그대는 아내의 죽음으로 그것을 갚소.”\n\n그가 말하는 사이 포스코 부인의 방 불이 꺼졌고, 이제 집의 이층 전체가 어둠에 잠겼다.\n\n“말! 말뿐이군!” 퍼시벌 경이 투덜거렸다. “자네 말을 듣고 있자면 내 아내의 서명을 이미 증서에 받아 낸 것 같겠어.”\n\n“이 일을 내 손에 맡겨 두지 않았소.” 백작이 되받았다. “그리고 내게는 몸을 돌릴 시간이 두 달 넘게 있소. 부디 지금은 그 얘기를 더 하지 맙시다. 어음 만기가 되면 나의 ‘말! 말뿐!’이 값어치가 있는지 없는지 그대 눈으로 직접 보게 될 거요. 자, 퍼시벌, 오늘 밤 돈 문제는 마무리했으니, 우리의 자잘한 곤경에 뒤엉킨 데다 그대를 알아보기 힘들 만큼 나쁘게 바꿔 놓은 그 두 번째 골칫거리를 내게 상의하고 싶다면 이제 내 주의를 그대에게 내어 주겠소. 말해 보시오, 벗이여—그리고 내가 설탕물을 두 잔째 타 마셔 그대의 격렬한 애국적 취향에 충격을 준다면 용서하시오.”\n\n“말해 보라기야 쉽지.” 퍼시벌 경이 여태 쓴 적 없는 훨씬 차분하고 정중한 어조로 대답했다. “허나 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 알기가 그리 쉽지 않네.”\n\n“내가 도와줄까요?” 백작이 넌지시 말했다. “그대의 그 은밀한 골칫거리에 이름을 붙여 볼까요? 내가 그것을—앤 캐서릭이라 부른다면?”\n\n“이보게 포스코, 자네와 나는 서로 알고 지낸 지 오래고, 자네가 전에 한두 번 내 곤경에서 나를 건져 주었다면 나도 돈이 닿는 한 힘껏 자네를 도와 갚았네. 사람으로서 할 수 있는 만큼 서로 우정 어린 희생을 치러 왔지만, 물론 서로에게 비밀은 있어 왔지—그렇지 않은가?”\n\n“비밀을 지녀 온 쪽은 그대요, 퍼시벌. 이 블랙워터 파크의 그대 벽장 속에는 해골이 하나 있고, 지난 며칠 사이 그것이 그대 말고 다른 사람들에게도 얼굴을 내밀었소.”\n\n“그렇다고 치세. 자네와 상관없는 일이라면 궁금해할 것 없지 않은가?”\n\n“내가 궁금해하는 얼굴로 보이오?”\n\n“그래, 그렇게 보이네.”\n\n“그렇군! 그렇군! 그렇다면 내 얼굴은 진실을 말하고 있구려? 내 나이에 이르러서도 얼굴이 진실을 말하는 버릇을 아직 잃지 않은 사람이라면, 그 천성에 얼마나 어마어마한 선의 토대가 깔려 있는 것이겠소!—자, 글라이드! 서로 솔직해집시다. 그대의 이 비밀은 제가 나를 찾아온 것이지 내가 그것을 찾아간 것이 아니오. 내가 궁금해한다고 칩시다—그대는 옛 벗인 내게, 그대의 비밀을 존중하여 그것을 영영 그대 자신의 몫으로 남겨 두라고 청하는 거요?”\n\n“그래—바로 그걸 청하는 걸세.”\n\n“그렇다면 내 호기심은 여기서 끝이오. 이 순간부터 내 안에서 죽었소.”\n\n“정말 그렇게 하겠다는 건가?”\n\n“무엇 때문에 나를 의심하오?”\n\n“포스코, 나는 자네의 그 에두르는 수법을 겪어 봐서 아네. 결국 자네가 나에게서 그걸 캐내지 않으리라고는 그리 자신할 수가 없어.”\n\n아래에서 의자가 갑자기 다시 삐걱거렸다—내 밑의 격자 기둥이 위에서 아래까지 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌다. 백작이 벌떡 일어서면서 분에 못 이겨 그 기둥을 손으로 내리친 것이었다.\n\n“퍼시벌! 퍼시벌!” 그가 격정에 차서 외쳤다. “그대는 나를 그것밖에 모르오? 그 오랜 세월을 겪고도 내 성품에 대해 아직 아무것도 알지 못했단 말이오? 나는 옛 시대의 인간이오! 나는 가장 드높은 덕행을 능히 행할 수 있는 사람이오—그럴 기회가 주어지기만 한다면. 그런 기회가 별로 없었다는 것이 내 생애의 불운이었소. 우정에 대한 내 관념은 숭고하오! 그대의 해골이 내게 얼굴을 내민 것이 내 탓이오? 내가 왜 내 호기심을 고백하겠소? 이 가엾고 얄팍한 영국인이여, 그것은 나 자신의 자제력을 더욱 돋보이게 하려 함이오. 마음만 먹으면 나는 이 손가락을 손바닥에서 빼내듯 그대에게서 비밀을 뽑아낼 수 있소—그대도 알지 않소! 허나 그대가 내 우정에 호소했고, 우정의 의무란 내게 신성한 것이오. 보시오! 나는 내 천한 호기심을 발밑에 짓밟아 버리오. 나의 드높은 감정이 나를 그 위로 들어 올리는 것이오. 그것을 알아보시오, 퍼시벌! 그것을 본받으시오, 퍼시벌! 악수합시다—내 그대를 용서하오.”\n\n마지막 말에서 그의 목소리가 떨렸다. 정말로 눈물이라도 흘리는 것처럼 떨렸다!\n\n퍼시벌 경이 어리둥절해하며 변명을 해 보려 했으나, 백작은 너무나 아량이 넓어 그의 말을 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n“아니오!” 그가 말했다. “벗이 나를 상하게 했을 때, 나는 사과 없이도 그를 용서할 수 있소. 꾸밈없는 말로 말해 보시오. 내 도움이 필요하오?”\n\n“그래, 절실하네.”\n\n“그러면서도 자신을 위태롭게 하지 않고 도움을 청할 수 있겠소?”\n\n“적어도 해 볼 수는 있네.”\n\n“그럼 해 보시오.”\n\n“좋아, 사정은 이렇네.—오늘 내가 앤 캐서릭을 찾으려고 온갖 애를 다 썼지만 실패했다고 자네에게 말했지.”\n\n“그랬소.”\n\n“포스코! 그 여자를 찾지 못하면 나는 파멸한 사람일세.”\n\n“허! 그 정도로 심각하오?”\n\n가느다란 빛줄기가 베란다 아래로 흘러나와 자갈길 위에 떨어졌다. 백작이 방 안쪽에서 등을 가져와 그 불빛으로 벗의 얼굴을 똑똑히 보려는 것이었다.\n\n“그렇군!” 그가 말했다. “이번에는 그대의 얼굴이 진실을 말하는구려. 정말이지 심각하오—돈 문제만큼이나 심각하오.”\n\n“그보다 더 심각하네. 내가 여기 앉아 있는 게 사실인 것만큼이나 확실하게, 그보다 더 심각해!”\n\n불빛이 다시 사라졌고 이야기는 계속되었다.\n\n“앤 캐서릭이 모래 속에 숨겨 둔, 내 아내 앞으로 쓴 그 편지를 자네에게 보여 주지 않았나.” 퍼시벌 경이 말을 이었다. “그 편지에는 허풍 같은 건 없어, 포스코. 그 애는 정말로 그 비밀을 알고 있단 말일세.”\n\n“내 앞에서는 그 비밀 이야기를 되도록 적게 하게, 퍼시벌. 그 여자가 자네에게서 그것을 알았나?”\n\n“아니, 제 어미한테서.”\n\n“여자 둘이 자네의 은밀한 속내를 쥐고 있다니—나쁘군, 나쁘군, 나쁘군, 이보게! 더 나아가기 전에 여기서 한 가지만 묻겠네. 자네가 그 딸을 정신병원에 가둔 동기는 이제 내게 충분히 분명해졌네만, 그 여자가 빠져나온 경위는 그다지 분명하지가 않아. 그 여자를 맡고 있던 자들이, 값을 넉넉히 치를 만한 어떤 적의 사주를 받고 일부러 눈을 감아 주었다고 의심하나?”\n\n“아니, 그 애는 거기서 제일 얌전한 환자였어—그래서 바보같이 그 애를 믿어 버린 거지. 가둬 둘 만큼은 미쳤고, 풀려나 있으면 나를 망칠 만큼은 멀쩡해—무슨 말인지 알겠나?”\n\n“알겠네. 자, 퍼시벌, 이제 곧장 요점으로 들어가게. 그래야 내가 무엇을 해야 할지 알지. 지금 이 순간 자네 처지에서 위험은 어디에 있나?”\n\n“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에 있고, 글라이드 부인과 연락을 주고받고 있어—위험은 거기 있지, 뻔하지 않나. 그 애가 모래 속에 숨긴 그 편지를 읽고서, 내 아내가 아무리 아니라고 잡아떼도 그 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 걸 알아채지 못할 사람이 어디 있겠나?”\n\n“잠깐, 퍼시벌. 글라이드 부인이 정말로 그 비밀을 안다면, 그것이 자네를 옭아맬 비밀이라는 것도 알 테지. 아내 된 사람으로서, 그것을 덮어 두는 편이 당연히 자기에게도 이롭지 않겠나?”\n\n“그럴까? 마침 그 이야기를 하려던 참이야. 그 여자가 나를 손톱만큼이라도 아낀다면 이로울지도 모르지. 그런데 하필 내가 다른 남자에게 걸리적거리는 방해물이란 말이야. 나와 혼인하기 전부터 그놈을 사랑했고—지금도 그놈을 사랑하고 있어—하트라이트라는 이름의, 그림 교사 나부랭이 떠돌이 놈이지.”\n\n“이보게! 그게 뭐가 그리 별스러운 일인가? 여자들은 하나같이 다른 남자를 사랑하지. 여자의 마음에서 첫째 자리를 차지하는 사람이 누군가? 내 평생의 경험을 통틀어, 나는 아직 1번이었던 남자를 만나 본 적이 없네. 2번은 더러 있지. 3번, 4번, 5번은 흔하고. 1번은 결코 없어! 물론 그런 자가 존재하기야 하겠지—다만 나는 만나 보지 못했네.”\n\n“기다려! 아직 안 끝났어. 정신병원 사람들이 앤 캐서릭을 뒤쫓을 때 그 애가 한발 앞서 달아나도록 도와준 게 누구라고 생각하나? 하트라이트야. 컴벌랜드에서 그 애를 다시 만난 게 누구라고 생각하나? 하트라이트지. 두 번 다 단둘이 이야기를 나눴어. 가만! 말 끊지 마. 그 악당은 내 아내한테 홀딱 빠져 있고, 내 아내도 그놈한테 그래. 그놈도 그 비밀을 알고, 그 여자도 그 비밀을 알아. 그 둘이 다시 만나기만 해 봐, 자기들이 쥔 것을 나한테 들이대는 것이 여자에게도 이롭고 그놈에게도 이로운 일이 된단 말이야.”\n\n“진정하게, 퍼시벌—진정해! 자네는 글라이드 부인의 정숙함을 조금도 믿지 않는 건가?”\n\n“글라이드 부인의 정숙함 따위! 그 여자에 대해 내가 믿는 것은 오직 그 재산뿐이야. 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 모르겠나? 혼자라면 별 해가 없을지도 모르지. 하지만 그 여자와 저 떠돌이 하트라이트가 손을 잡는다면——”\n\n“그래, 그래, 알겠네. 하트라이트 씨는 지금 어디 있나?”\n\n“나라 밖에. 제 몸뚱이 가죽을 성하게 붙이고 살 생각이라면, 서둘러 돌아오지 않는 편이 좋을 걸세.”\n\n“그자가 정말 나라 밖에 있는 것이 확실한가?”\n\n“확실해. 그놈이 컴벌랜드를 떠난 때부터 배에 오를 때까지 사람을 붙여 감시했어. 아, 내가 얼마나 조심했는지 자네는 모를 걸세! 앤 캐서릭은 리머리지 근처 어느 농가에서 사람들과 함께 지냈어. 그 애가 나를 따돌리고 달아난 뒤에 내가 직접 거기까지 가서, 그 사람들이 아무것도 모른다는 것을 확인했지. 그 어미한테는 홀콤 양 앞으로 보낼 편지 문안을 주어서, 내가 그 애를 가둔 데에 나쁜 뜻이 없었다는 것을 밝히게 했어. 그 애의 자취를 좇느라 얼마를 썼는지는 입에 담기도 두려울 지경인데, 그러고도 그 애는 여기에 나타나서 내 땅 안에서 나를 따돌린단 말이야! 또 누가 그 애를 볼지, 또 누가 그 애에게 말을 걸지 내가 어찌 알겠나? 그 캐고 다니는 악당 하트라이트가 내가 모르는 사이에 돌아와서, 당장 내일이라도 그 애를 써먹을지도——”\n\n“그럴 리 없네, 퍼시벌! 내가 이곳에 있고 그 여자가 이 근방에 있는 한, 하트라이트 씨보다 먼저 우리가 그 여자를 손에 넣도록 내가 책임지겠네—설령 그자가 정말 돌아온다 해도 말이야. 알겠어! 그래, 그래, 알겠네! 앤 캐서릭을 찾아내는 것이 첫째로 급한 일이야—나머지는 마음을 놓게. 자네 아내는 여기, 자네 손아귀 안에 있네—홀콤 양은 그 부인과 떨어질 줄 모르니 역시 자네 손아귀 안에 있고—하트라이트 씨는 나라 밖에 있지. 당장 우리가 생각할 것은 자네의 그 보이지 않는 앤뿐이야. 알아볼 만큼은 알아보았나?”\n\n“했지. 그 어미한테도 가 보았고, 마을을 샅샅이 뒤졌어—그런데 죄다 헛일이야.”\n\n“그 어미는 믿을 만한가?”\n\n“믿을 만해.”\n\n“그 여자는 자네 비밀을 이미 한 번 발설했네.”\n\n“두 번은 발설하지 않을 거야.”\n\n“어째서? 그것을 감추는 데에 자네뿐 아니라 그 여자 자신의 이해도 걸려 있는가?”\n\n“그래—아주 깊이 걸려 있지.”\n\n“그 말을 들으니 자네를 위해서 기쁘군, 퍼시벌. 낙심하지 말게, 이보게. 우리 돈 문제는 아까도 말했듯 내가 몸을 돌릴 여유를 넉넉히 남겨 주고 있으니, 내일은 내가 자네보다 더 나은 성과를 거두며 앤 캐서릭을 찾아 나설 수도 있겠지. 잠자리에 들기 전에 마지막으로 한 가지만 묻겠네.”\n\n“그게 뭔가?”\n\n“이런 것일세. 서명이라는 자잘한 문제가 미뤄졌다는 것을 글라이드 부인에게 알리러 내가 배 창고로 갔을 때, 우연이 나를 그리로 이끌어, 낯선 여자가 자네 아내와 몹시 수상쩍은 태도로 헤어지는 장면을 마침 보게 되었네. 그러나 그 우연도 나를 그 여자의 얼굴을 또렷이 볼 만큼 가까이 데려다주지는 않았어. 나는 우리의 보이지 않는 앤을 어떻게 알아볼지 알아야겠네. 그 여자는 어떻게 생겼나?”\n\n“어떻게 생겼냐고? 어디! 두 마디로 말해 주지. 병색이 도는 내 아내를 빼닮았어.”\n\n의자가 삐걱거렸고, 기둥이 다시 한 번 흔들렸다. 백작이 또 일어선 것이다—이번에는 놀라서.\n\n“뭐라고!!!” 그가 다급하게 소리쳤다.\n\n“내 아내가 크게 앓고 난 모습을 떠올려 보게, 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 기색까지 얹어서—그러면 그게 바로 자네가 찾는 앤 캐서릭이야.” 퍼시벌 경이 대답했다.\n\n“둘이 친척인가?”\n\n“천만의 말씀이지.”\n\n“그런데도 그렇게 닮았다는 건가?”\n\n“그래, 그렇게 닮았어. 뭐가 그리 우스운가?”\n\n아무 대답도, 어떤 소리도 없었다. 백작은 그 특유의 매끄럽고 소리 없는, 속으로 삼키는 방식으로 웃고 있었다.\n\n“뭐가 그리 우습냐니까?” 퍼시벌 경이 되물었다.\n\n“아마도 나 자신의 공상이 우스운 게지, 나의 좋은 친구여. 내 이탈리아식 익살을 눈감아 주게—나는 저 펀치 인형극을 만들어 낸 영예로운 나라의 태생이 아닌가? 자, 자, 자, 앤 캐서릭을 보면 내가 알아볼 걸세—오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐네. 마음을 편히 하게, 퍼시벌. 자게, 나의 아들이여, 의인의 잠을 자게. 그리고 날이 밝아 우리 두 사람을 도와줄 때 내가 자네를 위해 무엇을 하는지 보게. 내 이 커다란 머릿속에 나의 구상과 나의 계획이 들어 있네. 자네는 그 어음들을 갚을 것이고 앤 캐서릭도 찾아낼 걸세—내 신성한 명예를 걸고 말하네만, 자네는 반드시 그렇게 될 거야! 나는 자네 마음속 가장 좋은 자리에 간직해 둘 만한 벗인가, 아닌가? 조금 전 자네가 그토록 섬세하게 상기시켜 준 그 돈을 빌려줄 만한 값어치가 있는 사람인가, 아닌가? 무슨 짓을 하든, 다시는 내 감정에 상처를 주지 말게. 그것을 알아보게, 퍼시벌! 그것을 본받게, 퍼시벌! 나는 다시 자네를 용서하네—다시 손을 내미네. 잘 자게!”\n\n그 뒤로는 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 백작이 서재 문을 닫는 소리가 들렸다. 퍼시벌 경이 창 덧문에 빗장을 지르는 소리가 들렸다. 비가 오고 있었다, 그동안 내내 비가 오고 있었다. 나는 웅크린 자세 탓에 몸이 굳었고 뼛속까지 얼어 있었다. 처음 움직여 보려 했을 때는 그 동작이 어찌나 아프던지 그만둘 수밖에 없었다. 두 번째로 시도해서, 젖은 지붕 위에서 무릎을 세우고 일어나는 데까지는 성공했다.\n\n벽 쪽으로 기어가 그 벽에 몸을 기대어 일어서면서 나는 뒤를 돌아보았고, 백작의 옷방 창문이 환하게 밝아지는 것을 보았다. 가라앉던 내 용기가 다시 안에서 파닥이며 살아났고, 나는 집의 벽을 따라 한 걸음 한 걸음 몰래 돌아오는 동안 그 창문에서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n내가 내 방 창턱에 손을 얹었을 때 시계가 한 시 십오 분을 알렸다. 내 퇴각이 발각되었으리라고 짐작할 만한 것은 아무것도 보지 못했고 아무것도 듣지 못했다.\n\n# X\n\n6월 20일.—여덟 시. 해가 맑은 하늘에서 빛나고 있다. 나는 침대 근처에도 가지 않았다—지친 채 말똥말똥한 눈을 한 번도 감지 못했다. 어젯밤 어둠 속을 내다보던 바로 그 창가에서, 나는 지금 아침의 환한 고요를 내다본다.\n\n이 방의 은신처로 달아난 뒤로 몇 시간이 흘렀는지를 나는 내 몸의 느낌만으로 헤아린다—그리고 그 몇 시간이 몇 주처럼 여겨진다.\n\n얼마나 짧은 시간인가, 그런데도 나에게는 얼마나 긴가—여기 이 바닥에, 어둠 속에 주저앉았던 그때부터. 살갗까지 흠뻑 젖고, 사지가 하나같이 뒤틀리고, 뼛속까지 차갑고, 쓸모없고 무력하고 공포에 질린 한 생물이 되어.\n\n언제 정신을 차렸는지 나는 잘 모르겠다. 언제 더듬더듬 침실로 돌아가 촛불을 켜고, (처음에는 어디를 뒤져야 할지도 이상하리만치 알지 못한 채) 몸을 덥힐 마른 옷을 찾았는지도 잘 모르겠다. 그 일들을 했다는 것은 머릿속에 있으나, 그것을 한 시각은 없다.\n\n얼어붙어 뒤틀리던 느낌이 사라지고 그 자리에 두근거리는 열이 들어선 때를, 나는 기억이나 할 수 있을까?\n\n분명 해가 뜨기 전이었을 것이다. 그래, 시계가 세 번 치는 소리를 들었다. 그 시각을 기억하는 것은, 그와 함께 찾아온 갑작스러운 밝음과 또렷함, 온 감각의 열에 들뜬 팽팽함과 흥분 때문이다. 나 자신을 다스리자고, 즉시 발각되어 추격당할 위험 없이 로라를 이 끔찍한 곳에서 데리고 나갈 기회가 올 때까지 한 시간 또 한 시간 참을성 있게 기다리자고 결심했던 것을 기억한다. 그 두 사람이 서로 주고받은 말들이 우리에게 이 집을 떠날 명분뿐 아니라 그들에 맞설 방어의 무기까지 되어 주리라는 확신이 마음속에 자리 잡던 것을 기억한다. 시간이 아직 내 것이고 기억이 그 말들을 생생히 붙들고 있는 동안에, 들은 그대로 한 마디도 틀리지 않게 글로 남겨 두어야겠다는 충동이 내 안에서 깨어나던 것을 나는 되새긴다. 이 모두를 나는 또렷이 기억한다. 아직 내 머릿속에는 아무 혼란도 없다. 해가 뜨기 전에 펜과 잉크와 종이를 들고 침실에서 이리로 건너온 일—몸을 식힐 바람을 조금이라도 더 받으려고 활짝 열어젖힌 창가에 앉은 일—집안이 다시 깨어나기 전의 그 무서운 동안 내내, 더 빠르게 더 빠르게, 더 뜨겁게 더 뜨겁게, 점점 더 말똥말똥해지며 쉬지 않고 써 내려간 일—촛불로 시작해 새날의 햇빛 속에서 앞 장의 끝에 이르기까지, 나는 그것을 얼마나 또렷이 되새기는가!\n\n왜 나는 아직도 여기 앉아 있는가? 왜 더 씀으로써 뜨거운 눈과 타는 머리를 지치게 하는가? 왜 누워서 쉬며, 나를 태우는 열을 잠으로 꺼 보려 하지 않는가?\n\n감히 그럴 엄두가 나지 않는다. 다른 어떤 두려움보다 큰 두려움이 나를 사로잡았다. 내 살갗을 바싹 말리는 이 열이 두렵다. 머릿속에서 기어 다니며 두근거리는 것이 두렵다. 지금 누우면, 다시 일어날 정신과 힘이 내게 남아 있으리라고 무엇으로 알겠는가?\n\n아, 그 비, 그 비—어젯밤 나를 얼어붙게 한 그 모진 비!\n\n아홉 시. 아홉 번이 울린 것인가, 여덟 번인가? 분명 아홉 번이겠지? 나는 또 떨고 있다—여름 공기 속에서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있다. 내가 여기 앉은 채로 잠들었던가? 내가 무엇을 하고 있었는지 모르겠다.\n\n오, 하느님! 나는 병이 나려는 것인가?\n\n하필 이런 때에 병이라니!\n\n머리가—내 머리가 몹시 두렵다. 쓸 수는 있는데 줄들이 죄다 뒤엉킨다. 글자가 보인다. 로라—나는 로라라고 쓸 수 있고, 내가 그렇게 쓰는 것이 보인다. 여덟 번인가 아홉 번인가—어느 쪽이었지?\n\n너무 춥다, 너무 추워—아, 어젯밤 그 비!—그리고 시계 소리가, 셀 수 없는 그 소리가, 자꾸 머릿속에서 울려——\n\n주(註) [이 대목에서 일기의 기록은 알아볼 수 없게 된다. 뒤따르는 두세 줄에는 낱말의 조각들만이 잉크 얼룩과 펜으로 긁힌 자국에 뒤섞여 남아 있다. 종이에 남은 마지막 자국은 글라이드 부인의 이름 첫 두 글자(L과 A)와 얼마간 닮아 있다.\n\n일기의 다음 장에는 또 하나의 기록이 나타난다. 그것은 크고 굵으며 흔들림 없이 고른 남자의 필적이고, 날짜는 “6월 21일”이다. 거기에는 이런 글이 적혀 있다—]\n\n# POSTSCRIPT BY A SINCERE FRIEND\n\n우리의 훌륭하신 홀콤 양의 병환은 나에게 뜻밖의 지적 쾌락을 누릴 기회를 베풀어 주었다.\n\n내가 말하는 것은 이 흥미로운 일기의 통독이다(방금 막 끝마쳤다).\n\n여기에는 수백 쪽이 있다. 나는 가슴에 손을 얹고 단언할 수 있으니, 그 한 쪽 한 쪽이 나를 매혹하였고, 상쾌하게 하였고, 기쁘게 하였다.\n\n나와 같은 감성을 지닌 사내에게 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것은 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇한 일이다.\n\n감탄스러운 여인이여!\n\n내가 가리키는 것은 홀콤 양이다.\n\n경이로운 역작이여!\n\n내가 가리키는 것은 이 일기다.\n\n그렇다! 이 지면들은 놀랍다. 여기서 내가 발견하는 기지, 그 신중함, 그 보기 드문 담대함, 그 경탄스러운 기억력, 인물에 대한 정확한 관찰, 유려한 문체의 기품, 여인다운 감정이 매혹적으로 터져 나오는 대목들—이 모든 것이 이 숭고한 존재, 이 장엄한 매리언을 향한 나의 감복을 형언할 수 없이 드높였다. 내 자신의 인물됨을 그려 낸 솜씨는 극도로 대가답다. 나는 온 마음을 다하여 그 초상의 충실함을 증언한다. 이토록 강렬하고, 이토록 풍부하고, 이토록 육중한 빛깔로 그려질 만큼 내가 얼마나 생생한 인상을 남겼을 것인지 나는 절감한다. 우리의 이해를 서로 어긋나게 하고 우리를 맞세우는 저 잔인한 필연을 나는 새삼 한탄한다. 더 행복한 사정 아래였다면 나는 홀콤 양에게 얼마나 어울리는 사내였을 것이며—홀콤 양은 나에게 얼마나 어울리는 여인이었을 것인가.\n\n내 가슴을 뛰게 하는 감정들이 나에게 확신시켜 주노니, 내가 방금 적은 줄들은 하나의 심오한 진실을 말한 것이다.\n\n그 감정들은 나를 한낱 사사로운 이해타산 위로 드높여 준다. 나는 지극히 사심 없는 태도로 증언하노라—이 견줄 데 없는 여인이 퍼시벌과 나 사이의 은밀한 밀담을 불시에 알아낸 그 계략의 탁월함을, 또한 그 대화를 처음부터 끝까지 옮겨 적은 그 놀라운 정확함을.\n\n그 감정들에 이끌려 나는 그녀를 돌보는 저 감동할 줄 모르는 의사에게 나의 방대한 화학 지식과, 의학과 자기(磁氣) 과학이 인류의 손에 쥐여 준 더 미묘한 방편들에 대한 나의 빛나는 경험을 내어놓았다. 그는 지금껏 내 도움을 받기를 마다해 왔다. 가련한 사내여!\n\n끝으로, 그 감정들이 이 자리에 나타나는 이 줄들을—감사에 차고, 연민에 차고, 아버지와 같은 이 줄들을—받아쓰게 한다. 나는 책을 덮는다. 나의 엄정한 예법 감각이 이것을 (내 아내의 손을 빌려) 글쓴이의 탁자 위 제자리에 돌려놓는다. 사건들이 나를 재촉하여 몰아간다. 정황이 나를 중대한 국면으로 이끌어 간다. 성공의 광대한 전망이 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진다. 나는 나 자신에게조차 두려울 만큼의 침착함으로 내 운명을 이룬다. 오직 나의 감복이 바치는 경의만이 내 것이다. 나는 그것을 공손한 애틋함으로 홀콤 양의 발치에 놓아 둔다.\n\n나는 그녀의 쾌유를 비는 마음을 숨결에 실어 보낸다.\n\n나는 그녀가 동생을 위해 세운 모든 계획이 필연코 실패하고 말리라는 데 대하여 그녀에게 애도를 표한다. 동시에 나는 그녀에게 간청하노니, 내가 그녀의 일기에서 얻은 정보가 그 실패를 앞당기는 데에 어떤 식으로도 보탬이 되지 않으리라는 것을 믿어 주기 바란다. 그것은 다만 내가 앞서 마련해 둔 행동 계획을 확인해 주었을 뿐이다. 이 지면들에 대하여 나는 내 천성 속의 가장 고운 감수성을 일깨워 준 것을 고마워해야 하니—그 이상은 아무것도 없다.\n\n비슷한 감수성을 지닌 사람에게라면 이 단순한 단언이 모든 것을 설명하고 또 용서해 줄 것이다.\n\n홀콤 양은 비슷한 감수성을 지닌 사람이다.\n\n그렇게 믿으며 나는 이렇게 서명한다, 포스코.\n\n리머리지 하우스의 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 이어 가는 이야기[2]\n\n[2] 페얼리 씨의 서술과, 곧 뒤따를 다른 서술들이 애초에 어떤 경위로 입수되었는지는 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 설명의 주제가 된다.\n\n아무도 나를 가만히 내버려 두지 않는다는 것, 그것이 내 인생의 크나큰 불행입니다.\n\n어째서—나는 모두에게 묻습니다—어째서 하필 나를 못살게 구는 겁니까? 아무도 그 물음에 답하지 않고, 아무도 나를 가만두지 않습니다. 친척이며 친구며 낯선 사람이며 하나같이 합심해서 나를 성가시게 합니다. 내가 무슨 짓을 했다고? 나는 나 자신에게 묻고, 내 하인 루이에게 하루에 쉰 번은 묻습니다—내가 대체 무슨 짓을 했느냐고. 우리 둘 중 누구도 답하지 못합니다. 참으로 기이한 일이지요!\n\n가장 최근에 나를 덮친 성가심은 이 서술을 쓰라는 요구를 받은 성가심입니다. 나처럼 신경이 이 딱한 상태에 있는 사람이 서술 따위를 쓸 수 있겠습니까? 내가 이 지극히 온당한 이의를 제기하면, 내 조카딸에 관한 매우 중대한 사건들이 내가 겪은 범위 안에서 일어났으니 그런 까닭에 그것을 적어 내기에 내가 적임자라는 대답이 돌아옵니다. 요구받은 대로 애써 힘쓰지 않으면 생각만 해도 완전히 기진해 버릴 결과가 따르리라는 위협까지 받습니다. 나를 위협할 필요는 정말이지 없습니다. 비참한 건강과 집안의 우환에 무너져 버린 나에게는 저항할 힘이 없으니까요. 굳이 우기신다면 그것은 나에게 부당한 이점을 취하는 것이고, 나는 즉시 굴복합니다. 나는 (항의하는 마음으로) 기억나는 것을 애써 기억해 보고, (역시 항의하는 마음으로) 쓸 수 있는 것을 애써 써 보겠습니다. 그리고 기억나지 않고 쓸 수 없는 것은 루이가 나를 대신해 기억하고 써야 합니다. 그는 얼간이고 나는 병자이니, 우리 둘 사이에서 온갖 오류가 나올 법합니다. 이 얼마나 굴욕적인지요!\n\n날짜를 기억하라는 말을 듣습니다. 저런 세상에! 나는 평생 그런 짓을 해 본 적이 없는데—이제 와서 어떻게 시작하라는 겁니까?\n\n루이에게 물어보았습니다. 그는 내가 여태 짐작했던 만큼 얼간이는 아니더군요. 그는 그 일이 있었던 날짜를 한두 주 오차 안에서 기억하고—나는 그 사람의 이름을 기억합니다. 날짜는 6월 말이나 7월 초였고, 이름은 (내 생각에는 유난히 상스러운 이름인데) 패니였습니다.\n\n그러니까 6월 말이나 7월 초에, 나는 여느 때와 같은 자세로 비스듬히 누워, 이웃의 야만스러운 사람들의 안목을 개선하고자 내 주위에 모아 둔 온갖 미술품에 둘러싸여 있었습니다. 그러니까 내 그림들의 사진이며 판화며 주화 따위가 죄다 내 주위에 있었다는 말이지요. 나는 언젠가 그것들을 (사진들 말입니다, 이 서투른 영어라는 언어가 나로 하여금 무언가를 뜻할 수 있게 허락해 준다면) 칼라일(끔찍한 곳!)의 협회에 기증하여 그 회원들(하나같이 고트족이요 반달족인 자들)의 안목을 개선할 작정입니다. 온 국민에게 크나큰 국가적 은덕을 베푸는 중인 신사라면 사사로운 곤란과 집안일로 매정하게 시달릴 일이 세상에서 가장 없는 신사이리라고 여길 만도 하지요. 내 경우엔 완전한 착각입니다, 정말입니다.\n\n어쨌든 나는 거기 그렇게, 미술의 보물들을 곁에 두고 비스듬히 누운 채 조용한 오전을 바라고 있었습니다. 내가 조용한 오전을 바랐으니 당연히 루이가 들어왔지요. 내가 종을 울리지도 않았는데 대체 무슨 심산으로 나타났느냐고 묻는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었습니다. 나는 좀처럼 욕을 하지 않습니다—그렇게 신사답지 못한 버릇이 또 없으니까요—그러나 루이가 히죽 웃는 것으로 대답했을 때, 히죽거린다고 그에게 욕을 퍼부은 것 또한 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다고 생각합니다. 아무튼 나는 그렇게 했습니다.\n\n이 엄한 처방은, 내가 관찰한 바로는, 하층 계급 사람들을 어김없이 제정신으로 돌아오게 합니다. 그것은 루이를 그의 제정신으로 돌아오게 했습니다. 그는 친절하게도 히죽거리기를 그만두고, 밖에 어떤 젊은 여자가 나를 만나고 싶어 한다고 알렸습니다. 그는 (하인들 특유의 그 밉살스러운 수다스러움으로) 그 여자의 이름이 패니라고 덧붙였습니다.\n\n“패니가 누구냐?”\n\n“글라이드 부인의 하녀입니다, 나리.”\n\n“글라이드 부인의 하녀가 나한테 무슨 볼일이냐?”\n\n“편지가 한 통 있다고——”\n\n“받아 두어라.”\n\n“나리 말고는 아무에게도 드릴 수 없다고 합니다.”\n\n“누가 보낸 편지냐?”\n\n“홀콤 양입니다, 나리.”\n\n홀콤 양의 이름을 듣는 순간 나는 포기했습니다. 홀콤 양에게는 언제나 지고 마는 것이 내 버릇입니다. 경험으로 보건대 그편이 소란을 덜어 주더군요. 이번에도 나는 포기했습니다. 사랑스러운 매리언!\n\n“글라이드 부인의 하녀를 들여보내라, 루이. 잠깐! 그 여자 신발에서 삐걱 소리가 나느냐?”\n\n그 질문은 하지 않을 수 없었습니다. 삐걱거리는 신발은 어김없이 하루 종일 나를 망쳐 놓으니까요. 나는 그 젊은 여자를 만나는 데까지는 체념했지만, 그 젊은 여자의 신발이 나를 망쳐 놓게 두는 데까지 체념한 것은 아니었습니다. 내 인내에도 한계라는 것이 있습니다.\n\n루이는 그 여자의 신발은 믿어도 좋다고 분명히 단언했습니다. 나는 손을 저었습니다. 그가 여자를 들여보냈습니다. 그 여자가 입을 꾹 다물고 코로 숨을 쉬는 것으로 자기가 거북하다는 뜻을 나타냈다는 말을 굳이 해야 할까요? 하층 계급 여성 인간 본성의 연구자에게라면 분명 그럴 필요가 없겠지요.\n\n그 아이에게 공정하게 굴기로 합시다. 그 아이의 신발은 삐걱거리지 않았습니다. 그런데 어째서 남의집살이하는 젊은 여자들은 하나같이 손에 땀이 나는 걸까요? 어째서 하나같이 코는 뭉툭하고 뺨은 딱딱한 걸까요? 그리고 어째서 그 얼굴들은, 특히 눈꺼풀 언저리가, 그토록 딱하게도 마무리가 덜 된 것일까요? 나는 어떤 주제든 스스로 깊이 생각할 만큼 튼튼하지 못하니, 그럴 만한 전문가들에게 여쭙습니다. 어째서 우리네 젊은 여자라는 품종에는 변화가 없는 것입니까?\n\n“홀콤 양에게서 온 편지를 나에게 가져왔다지? 탁자 위에 내려놓아라, 무엇 하나 넘어뜨리지 말고. 홀콤 양은 안녕하시냐?”\n\n“네, 안녕하십니다, 나리. 감사합니다.”\n\n“그러면 글라이드 부인은?”\n\n나는 아무 대답도 받지 못했습니다. 그 젊은 여자의 얼굴은 어느 때보다 더 마무리가 덜 된 꼴이 되었고, 내 생각에는 울기 시작한 것 같았습니다. 눈가에 무언가 축축한 것이 있는 것만은 분명히 보았습니다. 눈물일까요, 땀일까요? (방금 물어본) 루이는 눈물 쪽으로 기웁니다. 그는 그 아이와 같은 계급이니 가장 잘 알겠지요. 눈물이라고 해 둡시다.\n\n예술의 정련 과정이 슬기롭게도 눈물에서 자연을 닮은 구석을 모조리 걷어 낸 경우가 아니라면, 나는 눈물이라는 것에 딱 잘라 반대합니다. 눈물은 과학적으로 분비물이라고 서술됩니다. 분비물이 건강할 수도 있고 건강하지 않을 수도 있다는 것은 이해합니다만, 감상적인 관점에서 분비물이 무슨 흥미가 있는지는 알 수가 없습니다. 아마도 내 자신의 분비물이 죄다 잘못되어 있는 탓에 이 문제에 대해 내가 조금 편견을 갖고 있는지도 모르지요. 아무래도 좋습니다. 나는 이번 경우에 있을 수 있는 온갖 예의와 정으로 처신했습니다. 나는 눈을 감고 루이에게 말했습니다—\n\n“저 아이가 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지 알아내도록 애써 보아라.”\n\n루이가 애를 썼고, 그 젊은 여자도 애를 썼습니다. 두 사람은 서로를 어찌나 헷갈리게 만드는 데 성공했는지, 나는 그저 고마운 마음에서 말하지 않을 수 없습니다—그들은 정말로 나를 즐겁게 해 주었습니다. 기분이 가라앉을 때면 그 둘을 다시 불러야겠다 싶습니다. 방금 이 생각을 루이에게 말했습니다. 이상하게도 그는 그 말에 불편해하는 것 같더군요. 딱한 녀석!\n\n설마 내 조카딸의 하녀가 자기 눈물을 설명한 것을, 그것도 내 스위스인 시종의 영어로 옮겨진 것을, 내가 그대로 되풀이하리라고 기대하시는 건 아니겠지요? 그건 명백히 불가능한 일입니다. 내 자신의 인상과 감정이라면 아마 전해 드릴 수 있겠습니다. 그것으로도 괜찮을까요? 그렇다고 말씀해 주십시오.\n\n내 짐작으로 그 아이는 (루이를 통해) 자기 주인 나리가 자기를 마님 시중에서 내보냈다는 말로 시작했습니다. (내내 이 젊은 여자의 이야기가 얼마나 엉뚱하게 겉도는지 눈여겨보십시오. 그 아이가 일자리를 잃은 것이 내 탓입니까?) 쫓겨난 뒤 그 아이는 여관에 가서 자기로 했다더군요. (여관은 내가 하는 것이 아닌데—어째서 나에게 그 이야기를 합니까?) 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 홀콤 양이 작별 인사를 하러 와서 편지 두 통을 주었는데, 하나는 나에게, 하나는 런던의 어떤 신사에게 가는 것이었습니다. (나는 런던의 신사가 아닙니다—런던의 그 신사 따위 될 대로 되라지요!) 그 아이는 두 통의 편지를 조심스레 품속에 넣어 두었고 (그 아이의 품이 나와 무슨 상관입니까?), 홀콤 양이 다시 가 버리자 몹시 침울해졌고, 잠자리에 들 무렵이 다 되도록 입에 한 술 한 모금 넣을 마음이 나지 않다가, 아홉 시가 다 되어서야 차를 한 잔 마시고 싶어졌다고 합니다. (침울함으로 시작해 차로 끝나는 이런 상스러운 감정의 기복에 내가 무슨 책임이 있습니까?) 마침 그 아이가 주전자를 데우고 있을 때 (이 말은 루이의 권위를 빌려 옮깁니다. 그는 그 뜻을 안다면서 설명하고 싶어 했지만 나는 원칙적으로 그를 면박했습니다)—마침 주전자를 데우고 있을 때 문이 열렸고, 그 아이는 여관 응접실에 백작부인 마님이 나타나신 것을 보고 억이 막혔다고 합니다 (역시 그 아이 자신의 말인데, 이번에는 루이에게도 나에게도 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말이었습니다). 내 조카딸의 하녀가 내 누이의 작위를 일컫는 것을 나는 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 기분으로 옮겨 적습니다. 내 가엾은 누이는 외국인과 결혼한 성가신 여자입니다. 다시 이어 가자면, 문이 열렸고, 백작부인 마님이 응접실에 나타나셨고, 그 젊은 여자는 억이 막혔습니다. 참으로 놀랍기도 하지요!\n\n더 나아가기 전에 정말이지 조금 쉬어야겠습니다. 몇 분쯤 눈을 감고 비스듬히 누워 있고, 루이가 내 딱하게 지끈거리는 관자놀이를 오드콜로뉴로 좀 식혀 주고 나면, 계속할 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다.\n\n백작부인 마님께서는——\n\n아니오. 계속할 수는 있겠으나 일어나 앉아 있을 수는 없습니다. 비스듬히 누운 채 받아쓰게 하겠습니다. 루이는 억양이 끔찍하지만 말은 알아듣고 글도 쓸 줄 압니다. 참으로 편리하지요!\n\n백작부인 마님께서는 여관에 뜻밖에 나타나신 까닭을, 홀콤 양이 서두르는 통에 잊어버린 자잘한 전갈 한두 가지를 전하러 왔노라고 패니에게 말씀하시는 것으로 설명하셨습니다. 그러자 그 젊은 여자는 그 전갈이 무엇인지 애가 타서 들으려 했으나, 백작부인은 패니가 차를 다 마실 때까지는 그것을 말할 마음이 없어 보이셨다더군요 (내 누이의 그 성가신 방식 그대로지요!). 마님은 그 일에 놀라우리만치 다정하고 사려 깊으셨고 (내 누이와는 극히 딴판입니다), 이렇게 말씀하셨답니다. “가엾은 아이야, 네가 차를 마시고 싶은 게 틀림없구나. 전갈은 그다음으로 미뤄도 된단다. 자, 자, 달리 네 마음을 편하게 할 것이 없다면, 내가 차를 끓여 너와 한 잔 같이 마시마.” 내 앞에서 그 젊은 여자가 흥분해서 전한 바로는 그 말이 그러했던 것 같습니다. 아무튼 백작부인은 기어이 차를 끓이겠다고 우기셨고, 겸손을 내세우는 그 우스꽝스러운 과시를 어찌나 밀고 나가셨는지 손수 한 잔을 드시고는 다른 한 잔을 그 아이에게 기어이 마시게 하셨습니다. 그 아이는 차를 마셨고, 자기 말로는 오 분 뒤에 난생처음 까무러쳐 기절함으로써 그 예사롭지 않은 자리를 엄숙히 기렸다고 합니다. 여기서도 역시 그 아이 자신의 말을 씁니다. 루이는 그 말에 분비물의 증가가 곁들여졌다고 생각합니다. 나로서는 뭐라 말할 수 없습니다. 듣는 노력만으로도 내가 감당할 수 있는 전부였던지라 내 눈은 감겨 있었으니까요.\n\n어디까지 이야기했더라? 아, 그렇지—백작부인과 차를 한 잔 마신 뒤에 기절했다고 했지요. 내가 그 아이의 주치의였다면 흥미가 갔을 법한 사건이지만, 나는 그런 것이 전혀 아니므로 그 이야기를 듣는 것이 지루했을 뿐 그 이상은 아무것도 아니었습니다. 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 정신을 차렸을 때 그 아이는 소파에 누워 있었고, 곁에는 여관 안주인 말고는 아무도 없었습니다. 백작부인은 여관에 더 머물기에는 너무 늦었다고 보시고 그 아이가 깨어날 낌새를 보이자마자 떠나셨고, 여관 안주인이 친절하게도 그 아이를 부축해 위층 잠자리로 데려다주었습니다.\n\n혼자 남겨진 뒤 그 아이는 품속을 더듬어 보고 (이 부분을 두 번째로 언급해야 하는 것이 유감입니다), 편지 두 통이 무사히 거기 있으나 이상하게 구겨져 있는 것을 발견했습니다. 그 아이는 밤새 어지러웠지만 아침에는 길을 떠날 만큼은 추슬러 일어났습니다. 그 아이는 저 주제넘은 낯선 사람, 런던의 그 신사 앞으로 된 편지를 우체통에 넣었고, 이제 다른 편지를 시킨 대로 내 손에 건넸습니다. 이것이 있는 그대로의 사실이며, 일부러 소홀히 한 것으로 자신을 탓할 수는 없다 해도 그 아이는 마음이 몹시 괴로웠고 한마디 조언이 몹시 아쉬웠다는 것이었습니다. 이 대목에서 루이는 분비물이 다시 나타났다고 생각합니다. 아마 그랬을 테지만, 그보다 무한히 더 중요한 것은 바로 이 대목에서 나 또한 인내심을 잃고 눈을 뜨고 끼어들었다는 사실입니다.\n\n“이 모든 이야기의 요지가 무엇이냐?” 내가 물었습니다.\n\n엉뚱하게 겉도는 내 조카딸의 하녀는 나를 빤히 쳐다보며 말없이 서 있었습니다.\n\n“설명하도록 애써 보아라.” 나는 하인에게 말했습니다. “나를 통역해라, 루이.”\n\n루이가 애를 썼고 통역을 했습니다. 다시 말해 그는 즉시 바닥없는 혼란의 구렁으로 굴러떨어졌고, 그 젊은 여자도 뒤따라 굴러떨어졌습니다. 내가 언제 이토록 즐거웠는지 정말 모르겠습니다. 나는 그들이 나를 즐겁게 해 주는 동안에는 구렁 바닥에 그대로 내버려 두었습니다. 그들이 더는 즐겁게 해 주지 못하게 되자 나는 지력을 발휘하여 그들을 다시 끌어올려 주었습니다.\n\n내가 끼어든 덕분에 때가 이르러 그 젊은 여자가 하는 말의 요지를 알아낼 수 있었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요.\n\n내가 알아낸 바는 이러합니다. 그 아이는 방금 나에게 늘어놓은 그 일련의 사건들 때문에, 홀콤 양이 백작부인에게 전해 달라고 맡긴 그 나머지 전갈을 받지 못하게 되어 마음이 편치 않다는 것이었습니다. 그 전갈이 자기 마님의 이해에 대단히 중요한 것이었을까 봐 두려웠던 겁니다. 퍼시벌 경이 무서워서 밤늦게 블랙워터 파크로 그것을 물으러 가지 못했고, 아침 기차를 절대 놓치지 말라는 홀콤 양 자신의 당부 때문에 이튿날 여관에서 기다릴 수도 없었다고 합니다. 그 아이는 자기가 기절한 불행이 마님으로 하여금 자기를 소홀한 아이로 여기게 만드는 두 번째 불행으로 이어지지 않기를 몹시 애태우며 바랐고, 감히 여쭙건대 자기가 홀콤 양께 사정과 변명을 적어 보내고 아직 너무 늦지 않았다면 그 전갈을 편지로 받게 해 달라고 청하는 편이 좋겠는지 조언해 주십사 하는 것이었습니다. 나는 이 지극히 따분한 문단에 대해 아무 변명도 하지 않겠습니다. 이것을 쓰라는 명을 받았으니까요. 도무지 이해할 수 없는 노릇이지만, 이 자리에서 내가 조카딸의 하녀에게 한 말보다 조카딸의 하녀가 나에게 한 말에 실제로 더 관심을 갖는 사람들이 있습니다. 재미있는 심사의 뒤틀림이지요!\n\n“나리, 제가 어떻게 하는 게 좋을지 일러 주시면 참으로 감사하겠습니다.” 그 젊은 여자가 말했습니다.\n\n“일이란 있는 그대로 두는 법이다.” 나는 듣는 이에게 말을 맞추어 대답했습니다. “나는 언제나 일을 있는 그대로 둔다. 그래. 그게 전부냐?”\n\n“나리, 제가 편지를 쓰는 것이 주제넘은 짓이라 여기신다면 물론 감히 그러지 않겠습니다. 다만 마님을 충실히 모시려고 제가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절한지——”\n\n하층 계급 사람들은 언제 어떻게 방에서 물러나야 하는지 도무지 알지 못합니다. 그들은 어김없이 윗사람이 물러나도록 도와주기를 바라지요. 나는 그 젊은 여자를 물러나게 도와줄 때가 무르익었다고 보았습니다. 나는 슬기로운 두 마디로 그 일을 해냈습니다—\n\n“그만 가 보아라.”\n\n이 별난 아이의 몸 밖에서인지 안에서인지 갑자기 삐걱 소리가 났습니다. 그 아이를 보고 있던 (나는 보고 있지 않았습니다) 루이는, 그 아이가 무릎을 굽혀 인사할 때 삐걱거렸다고 합니다. 희한한 일이지요. 신발이었을까요, 코르셋이었을까요, 아니면 뼈였을까요? 루이는 코르셋이었으리라고 생각합니다. 참으로 기이한 일입니다!\n\n혼자 남게 되자마자 나는 잠깐 눈을 붙였습니다—정말이지 그럴 필요가 있었지요. 다시 깨어났을 때 나는 사랑스러운 매리언의 편지를 알아보았습니다. 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 조금이라도 짐작했더라면 나는 분명 그것을 열어 볼 생각조차 하지 않았을 겁니다. 불행히도 아무런 의심 없이 순진하기만 했던 나는 그 편지를 읽었습니다. 그것은 즉시 그날 하루를 망쳐 놓았습니다.\n\n나는 천성이 이제껏 살았던 누구보다도 성품이 무던한 사람입니다—누구에게나 너그럽고, 무엇에도 화를 내지 않지요. 그러나 앞서도 말했듯이 내 인내에도 한계가 있습니다. 나는 매리언의 편지를 내려놓았고, 스스로를—당연히 스스로를—피해 입은 사람으로 느꼈습니다.\n\n한마디 하려 합니다. 물론 지금 다루고 있는 매우 중대한 사안에 해당되는 말이니, 그렇지 않다면 이 자리에 적히도록 허락하지 않았을 겁니다.\n\n내 생각으로는, 모든 계층의 사회에서 결혼한 사람들의 손에 독신인 사람들이 받는 대접만큼 인류의 밉살스러운 이기심을 역겨우리만치 뚜렷하게 드러내 보이는 것도 없습니다. 이미 넘칠 만큼 붐비는 인구에 제 가족까지 보태지 않을 만큼 사려 깊고 자기를 억제하는 사람임을 한 번 보여 놓으면, 그런 사려도 그런 자기 억제도 없는 결혼한 친구들이 앙심이라도 품은 듯 당신을 찍어 두고는, 자기네 부부 우환의 절반을 받아 주는 사람이자 자기네 자식들 모두의 타고난 벗으로 삼습니다. 남편과 아내들은 결혼 생활의 근심을 말로 하고, 총각과 노처녀들은 그것을 짊어집니다. 내 경우를 보십시오. 나는 사려 깊게도 독신으로 남고, 내 가엾은 형 필립은 사려 없이 결혼합니다. 그가 죽을 때 무슨 짓을 합니까? 딸을 나에게 남깁니다. 그 아이는 사랑스러운 아이입니다—또한 무시무시한 책임이기도 합니다. 어째서 그것을 내 어깨에 지웁니까? 내가 독신 남자라는 무해한 신분에 매여, 결혼한 인척들의 우환을 죄다 덜어 줄 의무가 있기 때문이지요. 나는 형이 남긴 책임을 최선을 다해 감당합니다—무한한 소란과 어려움을 무릅쓰고, 그 아비가 원했던 사내에게 조카딸을 시집보냅니다. 그 아이와 남편이 사이가 틀어지고, 달갑지 않은 결과가 따릅니다. 그 아이는 그 결과를 어떻게 합니까? 그것을 나에게 떠넘깁니다. 어째서 나에게 떠넘깁니까? 내가 독신 남자라는 무해한 신분에 매여, 결혼한 인척들의 우환을 죄다 덜어 줄 의무가 있기 때문입니다. 가엾은 독신자들이여! 가엾은 인간 본성이여!\n\n매리언의 편지가 나를 위협했다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없습니다. 누구나 나를 위협하니까요. 리머리지 하우스를 조카딸과 그 아이의 불운을 위한 피난처로 내주기를 내가 망설이기라도 하면 온갖 끔찍한 일이 이 헌신적인 내 머리 위로 떨어질 참이었습니다. 그럼에도 나는 망설였습니다.\n\n지금껏 내 통상적인 방침은 사랑스러운 매리언에게 굴복하여 소란을 더는 것이었다고 앞서 말한 바 있습니다. 그러나 이번만은 그 아이의 지극히 사려 없는 제안에 딸린 결과들이 나를 멈칫하게 할 성질의 것이었습니다. 내가 리머리지 하우스를 글라이드 부인의 피난처로 열어 준다면, 아내를 숨겨 주었다고 나에게 격렬한 원한을 품은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 뒤를 따라 여기까지 오는 일에 대해 내게 무슨 안전 보장이 있겠습니까? 나는 이 일에 얽힌 우환의 미로가 어찌나 완벽한지 알아보고, 말하자면 땅을 짚어 보기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리하여 나는 사랑스러운 매리언에게 편지를 써서 (그 아이에게는 권리를 주장할 남편이 없으니) 먼저 그 아이 혼자 이리로 와서 그 문제를 나와 의논해 달라고 청했습니다. 그 아이가 내 이의에 나 자신이 아주 흡족할 만큼 답해 준다면 그때는 우리의 사랑스러운 로라를 더없는 기쁨으로 맞이하겠으나 그렇지 않으면 안 된다고 다짐해 두었습니다.\n\n물론 그때 나는 이렇게 시간을 버는 내 처사가 아마도 매리언을 의로운 분노에 차서 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 여기로 오게 만드는 것으로 끝나리라고 느꼈습니다. 그러나 다른 쪽 방침은 퍼시벌 경을 의로운 분노에 차서 역시 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 여기로 오게 만드는 것으로 끝날 수도 있었고, 두 가지 분노와 두 가지 쾅쾅거림 가운데 나는 매리언 쪽을 골랐습니다. 그 아이 쪽에는 익숙했으니까요. 그리하여 나는 그 편지를 회신 우편으로 부쳤습니다. 어쨌든 그것은 나에게 시간을 벌어 주었습니다—그리고, 아이고 저런! 그것만 해도 시작으로는 얼마나 대단한 것이었는지요.\n\n완전히 기진해 버리면 (매리언의 편지로 내가 완전히 기진해 버렸다는 말을 했던가요?) 나는 언제나 다시 일어나는 데 사흘이 걸립니다. 나는 무척 억지스러운 사람이었지요—사흘의 평온을 기대했으니까요. 물론 그것을 얻지 못했습니다.\n\n사흘째 되는 날 우편으로 전혀 알지 못하는 사람에게서 지극히 무례한 편지가 왔습니다. 그는 자기를 우리 집안 사무를 보아 주는 사람—우리의 사랑스럽고 고집불통인 늙은 길모어—의 업무 집행 동업자라고 밝히고는, 최근 우편으로 홀콤 양의 필적으로 자기 앞으로 온 편지를 한 통 받았다고 알려 왔습니다. 봉투를 열어 보니 놀랍게도 그 안에는 백지 편지지 한 장밖에 들어 있지 않더라는 것이었습니다. 이 정황이 그에게는 어찌나 수상쩍어 보였던지 (가만있지 못하는 그 법률가의 머리에 편지가 손을 탔다는 생각이 떠올랐던 거지요) 그는 즉시 홀콤 양에게 편지를 썼으나 회신 우편으로 답을 받지 못했다고 합니다. 이 곤경에서 분별 있는 사람답게 처신하여 일이 제 갈 길을 가도록 두는 대신, 그의 다음번 어처구니없는 처사는 자기 입으로 밝힌 바에 따르면 나에게 편지를 써서 내가 그 일에 대해 무언가 아는 것이 있는지 묻는 것으로 나를 성가시게 구는 일이었습니다. 대체 내가 그 일에 대해 무엇을 안다는 말입니까? 어째서 저 혼자 놀라는 데 그치지 않고 나까지 놀라게 합니까? 나는 그런 뜻으로 답장을 썼습니다. 내가 쓴 편지 중에서도 손꼽히게 날 선 편지였지요. 저 지극히 성가신 사람, 월터 하트라이트 씨에게 서면으로 해고를 통고한 이래로 그보다 날카로운 서한의 날을 세워 본 적이 없습니다.\n\n내 편지는 효과가 있었습니다. 그 변호사에게서는 더 이상 아무 소식도 없었습니다.\n\n이것은 어쩌면 그리 놀랄 일이 아니었을 겁니다. 그러나 매리언에게서 두 번째 편지가 오지 않았고 그 아이가 도착할 조짐도 보이지 않았다는 것은 분명 주목할 만한 정황이었습니다. 그 아이가 뜻밖에도 나타나지 않은 것은 나에게 놀랍도록 좋은 일을 해 주었습니다. 내 결혼한 인척들이 다시 화해했다고 (물론 나는 그렇게 추론했지요) 미루어 짐작하는 것은 어찌나 마음을 달래 주고 흐뭇한 일이던지요. 방해받지 않은 닷새의 고요, 감미로운 독신의 축복이 나를 아주 회복시켜 주었습니다. 엿새째 되는 날 나는 사진사를 부르러 보내, 앞서 말씀드린 대로 이 야만스러운 이웃의 안목을 개선할 목적으로 내 미술 보물들의 기증용 복제본 작업을 그에게 다시 맡길 만큼 기운을 차렸습니다. 막 그를 작업실로 물려 보내고 막 내 주화들과 시시덕거리기 시작했을 때, 루이가 명함 한 장을 손에 들고 불쑥 나타났습니다.\n\n“또 젊은 여자냐?” 내가 말했습니다. “만나지 않겠다. 내 건강 상태로는 젊은 여자들이 몸에 맞지 않아. 집에 없다고 해라.”\n\n“이번에는 신사분입니다, 나리.”\n\n신사라면 물론 이야기가 달라집니다. 나는 명함을 들여다보았습니다.\n\n이런 세상에! 내 성가신 누이의 외국인 남편, 포스코 백작이었습니다.\n\n손님의 명함을 보았을 때 내 첫인상이 어떠했는지 굳이 말해야 할까요? 분명 그럴 필요 없겠지요! 누이가 외국인과 결혼한 마당이니, 제정신인 사람이라면 도무지 하나의 인상밖에 가질 수 없었습니다. 물론 백작은 나에게 돈을 꾸러 온 것이었지요.\n\n“루이,” 내가 말했습니다. “네가 오 실링을 주면 저 사람이 돌아갈 것 같으냐?”\n\n루이는 몹시 충격을 받은 표정이었습니다. 그는 내 누이의 외국인 남편이 훌륭하게 차려입었고 부유함의 표본으로 보인다고 말해서 나를 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했습니다. 이런 사정이고 보니 내 첫인상은 어느 정도 바뀌었습니다. 나는 이제 백작이 감당해야 할 자기 나름의 부부 문제가 있어서, 집안의 나머지 사람들처럼 그것을 죄다 내 어깨에 지우러 왔으리라고 당연시하게 되었습니다.\n\n“무슨 용건인지 말하더냐?” 내가 물었습니다.\n\n“포스코 백작께서는 홀콤 양이 블랙워터 파크를 떠날 수 없어서 이리로 오셨다고 하십니다, 나리.”\n\n또 새로운 우환이로군요. 짐작했던 것처럼 딱히 그 사람 자신의 것은 아니고 사랑스러운 매리언의 것이었습니다. 어쨌거나 우환입니다. 아이고 저런!\n\n“들여보내라.” 나는 체념하여 말했습니다.\n\n백작이 처음 나타났을 때 저는 정말이지 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 어찌나 겁이 날 만큼 덩치가 큰 사람이던지 저는 그만 몸이 떨렸습니다. 저 사람이 마루를 흔들어 제 미술품들을 넘어뜨리고 말리라 확신했지요. 그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 하지 않았습니다. 여름옷 차림은 보기만 해도 시원했고, 태도는 더없이 침착하고 조용했으며, 미소는 매력적이었습니다. 그에 대한 저의 첫인상은 대단히 호의적이었습니다. 뒤에 이어질 일이 보여주듯 이렇게 인정하는 것은 제 안목에 그리 명예로운 일이 아닙니다만, 저는 천성이 솔직한 사람이라 그럼에도 불구하고 인정합니다.\n\n“제 소개를 하도록 허락해 주십시오, 페얼리 씨.” 그가 말했습니다. “저는 블랙워터 파크에서 왔고, 포스코 백작부인의 남편이 되는 영광과 행복을 누리고 있는 사람입니다. 그 인연을 처음이자 마지막으로 이용해, 저를 낯선 사람으로 대하지 말아 주십사 간청드리겠습니다. 부디 몸을 수고롭게 하지 마십시오. 부디 움직이지 마십시오.”\n\n“참으로 친절하십니다.” 제가 대답했습니다. “일어설 만큼 기운이 있으면 좋으련만. 리머리지에서 뵙게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다. 부디 의자에 앉으시지요.”\n\n“오늘은 편찮으신 모양이군요.” 백작이 말했습니다.\n\n“늘 그렇습니다.” 제가 말했습니다. “저는 사람처럼 보이게 차려입은 신경 뭉치에 지나지 않습니다.”\n\n“저는 지금까지 여러 분야를 공부해 왔습니다.” 이 인정 많은 인물이 말했습니다. “그중에는 끝을 알 수 없는 신경이라는 주제도 있었지요. 지극히 간단하면서도 지극히 깊은 제안을 하나 드려도 되겠습니까? 이 방의 빛을 제가 좀 손보아도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“그러시지요—그 빛이 조금이라도 제게 들이치지 않게만 해 주신다면 말입니다.”\n\n그는 창가로 걸어갔습니다. 사랑스러운 매리언과는 어찌나 대조적인지! 몸짓 하나하나가 그토록 사려 깊다니!\n\n“빛이란,” 그가 병자에게 그토록 위안이 되는 그 유쾌하고도 은밀한 어조로 말했습니다. “무엇보다 먼저 필요한 것입니다. 빛은 자극하고, 기르고, 보존합니다. 페얼리 씨, 당신이 한 송이 꽃이라 해도 빛 없이는 살 수 없을 것입니다. 보십시오. 여기, 당신이 앉아 계신 쪽은 마음을 가라앉히시도록 덧문을 닫습니다. 저기, 당신이 앉아 계시지 않은 쪽은 발을 걷어 올려 기운을 돋우는 햇빛을 들입니다. 몸에 직접 받으실 수 없다면 방 안에라도 빛을 들이십시오. 선생, 빛은 섭리가 내린 위대한 명령입니다. 당신은 스스로 정한 제한을 두고 섭리를 받아들이시지요. 빛도 같은 조건으로 받아들이십시오.”\n\n저는 이것이 무척 설득력 있고 세심하다고 생각했습니다. 빛에 관한 그 대목까지 그는 저를 감쪽같이 속였습니다. 분명히 속였지요.\n\n“제가 당황한 것이 보이시지요.” 그가 자기 자리로 돌아오며 말했습니다. “명예를 걸고 말씀드립니다만, 페얼리 씨, 저는 당신 앞에서 당황하고 있습니다.”\n\n“그런 말씀을 들으니 참으로 놀랍습니다. 이유를 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“선생, 제가 이 방에—고통받는 분이 앉아 계신 이 방에—들어와, 당신이 이 감탄스러운 예술품들에 둘러싸여 계신 것을 보고서, 당신이 감정이 몹시 예민하고 공감이 끊임없이 살아 있는 분이라는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 있겠습니까? 말씀해 보십시오, 제가 그럴 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n의자에 똑바로 앉을 만한 기운이 있었다면 저는 물론 고개를 숙여 보였을 것입니다. 그럴 기운이 없었으므로 대신 미소로 사의를 표했습니다. 그것으로도 충분했고, 우리는 서로를 이해했습니다.\n\n“부디 제 생각의 흐름을 따라와 주십시오.” 백작이 말을 이었습니다. “저 역시 섬세한 공감을 지닌 사람으로서, 마찬가지로 섬세한 공감을 지닌 또 한 사람 앞에 앉아 있습니다. 그런데 저는 몹시 우울한 종류의 집안일을 꺼냄으로써 그 공감을 갈가리 찢어 놓아야 할 끔찍한 필요를 느끼고 있습니다. 그 필연적인 결과가 무엇이겠습니까? 이미 영광스럽게도 당신께 말씀드린 그대로입니다. 저는 당황한 채 앉아 있습니다.”\n\n그가 나를 지루하게 만들려 한다고 제가 의심하기 시작한 것이 바로 이 대목이었을까요? 아마 그랬던 것 같습니다.\n\n“그 불쾌한 일들을 꼭 꺼내야만 합니까?” 제가 물었습니다. “우리 영국의 소박한 표현을 빌리자면, 포스코 백작, 그건 좀 묵혀 두면 안 되겠습니까?”\n\n백작은 더없이 겁나는 엄숙함으로 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 저었습니다.\n\n“정말로 제가 그것을 들어야만 합니까?”\n\n그는 어깨를 으쓱했고(그가 이 방에 들어온 이래 처음으로 보인 외국식 몸짓이었습니다), 불쾌하리만치 꿰뚫어 보는 태도로 저를 바라보았습니다. 눈을 감는 편이 낫겠다고 제 본능이 일러 주었습니다. 저는 본능에 따랐습니다.\n\n“제발 부드럽게 말씀해 주십시오.” 제가 애원했습니다. “누가 죽었습니까?”\n\n“죽다니요!” 백작이 쓸데없이 외국식으로 격렬하게 외쳤습니다. “페얼리 씨, 당신네 국민 특유의 그 침착함이 저를 겁나게 합니다. 맙소사, 제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 했기에 저를 죽음의 전령으로 여기시는 겁니까?”\n\n“부디 제 사과를 받아 주십시오.” 제가 대답했습니다. “당신은 아무 말도, 아무 짓도 하지 않았습니다. 저는 이런 괴로운 경우에 언제나 최악을 먼저 예상하는 것을 원칙으로 삼고 있습니다. 반쯤 마중 나가 맞으면 충격이 덜하니까요. 뭐 그런 것입니다. 아무도 죽지 않았다니 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓입니다. 누가 아픕니까?”\n\n저는 눈을 뜨고 그를 바라보았습니다. 그는 들어올 때부터 그렇게 누렇던가요, 아니면 지난 한두 분 사이에 그렇게 누레진 것인가요? 저는 정말 알 수 없고, 루이에게 물어볼 수도 없습니다. 그때 루이는 방에 없었으니까요.\n\n“누가 아픕니까?” 제가 되풀이했습니다. 제 국민 특유의 그 침착함이 여전히 그에게 영향을 미치는 듯 보였기 때문입니다.\n\n“그것이 제가 가져온 나쁜 소식의 일부입니다, 페얼리 씨. 그렇습니다. 누군가 아픕니다.”\n\n“참으로 안타깝습니다. 그중 누구입니까?”\n\n“더없이 깊은 슬픔과 함께 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양입니다. 어쩌면 이 소식을 들으실 마음의 준비가 어느 정도는 되어 계셨는지도 모르겠군요? 제안하신 대로 홀콤 양이 혼자 이곳으로 오지도 않고 두 번째 편지도 쓰지 않은 것을 아셨을 때, 애정 어린 염려 때문에 혹 그분이 아픈 것은 아닐까 걱정하시지 않았습니까?”\n\n제 애정 어린 염려가 언젠가 그런 우울한 걱정으로 이어졌으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다만, 그 순간 제 형편없는 기억력은 그런 사정을 도무지 떠올려 주지 못했습니다. 그래도 저는 제 자신의 명예를 위해 그렇다고 말했습니다. 저는 몹시 충격을 받았습니다. 사랑스러운 매리언처럼 튼튼한 사람이 앓는다는 것은 너무나 그답지 않은 일이어서, 저는 그가 사고를 당했으리라고밖에 생각할 수 없었습니다. 말이라든가, 계단에서 발을 헛디뎠다든가, 그런 종류의 일 말입니다.\n\n“심각합니까?” 제가 물었습니다.\n\n“심각합니다—의심할 여지 없이요.” 그가 대답했습니다. “위험하냐 하면—그렇지 않기를 바라고 또 믿습니다. 홀콤 양은 불행히도 억수 같은 비를 그대로 맞아 흠뻑 젖었습니다. 그 뒤에 온 감기가 고약한 종류였고, 이제 그것이 최악의 결과를 불러왔습니다—열병입니다.”\n\n열병이라는 말을 듣고, 동시에 지금 제게 말을 걸고 있는 이 파렴치한 인물이 방금 블랙워터 파크에서 왔다는 사실을 떠올렸을 때, 저는 그 자리에서 기절하는 줄 알았습니다.\n\n“맙소사!” 제가 말했습니다. “전염됩니까?”\n\n“지금은 아닙니다.” 그가 밉살스러울 만큼 침착하게 대답했습니다. “전염성으로 바뀔 수도 있습니다만, 제가 블랙워터 파크를 떠날 때까지는 그런 개탄스러운 사태는 일어나지 않았습니다. 저는 이 병에 깊은 관심을 기울여 왔습니다, 페얼리 씨—담당 의사를 도와 병세를 지켜보려고 애써 왔습니다—제가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그 열병에 전염성이 없었다는 저의 개인적인 보증을 받아 주십시오.”\n\n그의 보증을 받아 달라니! 제 평생 무언가를 받아들이는 것과 그토록 거리가 멀었던 적은 없었습니다. 그가 맹세를 했더라도 저는 믿지 않았을 것입니다. 그는 믿기에는 너무 누랬습니다. 걸어 다니는 서인도제도 전염병 같았습니다. 발진티푸스를 톤 단위로 나르고, 밟고 다니는 양탄자마다 성홍열로 물들일 만큼 덩치가 컸습니다. 어떤 위급한 상황에서는 제 마음이 놀랄 만큼 빨리 정해집니다. 저는 즉시 그를 쫓아내기로 결심했습니다.\n\n“병자의 사정을 너그러이 헤아려 주십시오.” 제가 말했습니다. “어떤 종류든 긴 이야기는 반드시 저를 무너뜨립니다. 이렇게 찾아와 주신 영광이 정확히 어떤 용건에서 비롯된 것인지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”\n\n저는 이 유난히 노골적인 암시가 그의 균형을 무너뜨리고—당황하게 만들고—정중한 사과로 몰아넣어—요컨대 그를 방에서 내보내 주기를 간절히 바랐습니다. 그런데 정반대로, 그것은 그를 의자에 더 눌러앉혔을 뿐이었습니다. 그는 한층 더 엄숙해지고, 위엄 있어지고, 은밀해졌습니다. 그는 그 끔찍한 손가락 두 개를 들어 보이더니 또 한 번 불쾌하리만치 꿰뚫는 눈길을 던졌습니다. 제가 어쩌겠습니까? 저는 그와 다툴 만한 기운이 없었습니다. 부디 제 처지를 헤아려 보십시오. 말로 그것을 다 표현할 수 있겠습니까? 저는 없다고 봅니다.\n\n“제 방문의 용건은,” 그가 도무지 걷잡을 수 없이 말을 이었습니다. “이 손가락에 헤아려 두었습니다. 두 가지입니다. 첫째, 저는 퍼시벌 경과 글라이드 부인 사이의 통탄스러운 불화에 대해 깊은 슬픔을 안고 증언하러 왔습니다. 저는 퍼시벌 경의 가장 오랜 친구이고, 혼인으로 글라이드 부인과 인척이 된 사람이며, 블랙워터 파크에서 벌어진 모든 일을 직접 본 목격자입니다. 그 세 가지 자격으로 저는 권위를 가지고, 확신을 가지고, 명예로운 유감을 담아 말씀드립니다. 선생, 글라이드 부인 집안의 가장이신 당신께 알려 드립니다. 홀콤 양이 당신께 보낸 편지에서 과장한 것은 하나도 없습니다. 저는 그 훌륭한 부인이 제안한 방책이야말로 당신을 세상의 추문이라는 끔찍한 일에서 구해 줄 유일한 방책이라고 단언합니다. 남편과 아내를 한동안 떼어 놓는 것이 이 난국의 유일하고도 평화로운 해결책입니다. 지금은 두 사람을 갈라놓으십시오. 그리고 갈등의 원인이 모두 사라졌을 때, 지금 당신께 말씀드리는 영광을 누리고 있는 제가—바로 제가 퍼시벌 경을 이치에 따르게 만들겠습니다. 글라이드 부인은 죄가 없고, 글라이드 부인은 상처받은 쪽입니다. 그러나—여기서 제 생각을 따라와 주십시오!—바로 그 때문에, 부인이 남편의 지붕 아래 머무는 한 부인은 (부끄러운 마음으로 말씀드립니다만) 갈등의 원인이 됩니다. 부인을 온당하게 맞아들일 수 있는 집은 당신의 집밖에 없습니다. 그 문을 열어 주시기를 청합니다.”\n\n참으로 태연하더군요. 잉글랜드 남부에서 부부 싸움의 우박이 쏟아지고 있는데, 저더러 옷 주름마다 열병을 묻히고 온 사내의 초대를 받아 잉글랜드 북부에서 나가 그 우박을 나눠 맞으라는 것이었습니다. 저는 여기 적은 그대로 그 점을 힘주어 말해 보려 했습니다. 백작은 그 끔찍한 손가락 하나를 천천히 내리고 다른 하나는 세워 둔 채 말을 이어 갔습니다. 말하자면 저를 밟고 지나간 셈인데, 사람을 치기 전에 “이랴!” 하고 외쳐 주는 마부의 흔한 배려조차 없었습니다.\n\n“한 번만 더 제 생각을 따라와 주십시오.” 그가 다시 말했습니다. “첫 번째 용건은 들으셨습니다. 제가 이 댁에 온 두 번째 용건은, 홀콤 양이 병 때문에 스스로 할 수 없게 된 일을 대신하는 것입니다. 블랙워터 파크에서는 어려운 일이 있을 때마다 제 풍부한 경험에 자문을 구하는데, 홀콤 양께 보내신 편지라는 흥미로운 문제에 대해서도 제 우정 어린 조언을 청해 왔습니다. 저는 즉시 알아들었습니다—제 공감은 곧 당신의 공감이니까요—글라이드 부인을 초대하겠다고 약속하기 전에 어째서 홀콤 양을 먼저 이곳에서 만나 보고 싶어 하셨는지를 말입니다. 선생, 남편이 아내를 되찾겠다고 권위를 휘두르지는 않으리라는 확신이 설 때까지 아내를 받아들이기를 망설이신 것은 지극히 옳습니다. 저는 그 점에 동의합니다. 또한 이런 난국에 따르는 미묘한 설명은 편지만으로 온당하게 처리될 수 있는 성질의 것이 아니라는 데도 동의합니다. 제가 (제게는 대단히 불편한 일임에도) 여기 와 있다는 것이 제 진심의 증거입니다. 설명 그 자체로 말하자면, 저는—포스코는—홀콤 양보다 퍼시벌 경을 훨씬 잘 아는 이 사람은, 명예와 말을 걸고 당신께 단언합니다. 아내가 이 집에 사는 동안 그는 이 집 가까이 오지도, 이 집에 연락을 취하려 하지도 않을 것입니다. 그의 재정 형편은 곤란한 상태입니다. 글라이드 부인이 자리를 비움으로써 그에게 자유를 주십시오. 장담하건대 그는 그 자유를 받아들이고, 빠져나갈 수 있는 가장 이른 순간에 대륙으로 돌아갈 것입니다. 이것이 수정처럼 분명하십니까? 그렇습니다, 분명합니다. 제게 하실 질문이 있으십니까? 그러시다면 저는 답하려고 여기 있습니다. 물으십시오, 페얼리 씨—마음껏 물어 주시면 고맙겠습니다.”\n\n제가 말리는데도 그는 이미 그토록 많은 말을 했고, 역시 제가 말리는데도 앞으로 훨씬 더 많은 말을 할 수 있어 보이는 것이 어찌나 무시무시하던지, 저는 순전히 자기 방어를 위해 그의 상냥한 제안을 사양했습니다.\n\n“천 번 감사합니다.” 제가 대답했습니다. “저는 빠르게 가라앉고 있습니다. 이런 건강 상태에서는 무엇이든 그러려니 하고 받아들일 수밖에 없습니다. 이번에도 그렇게 하도록 허락해 주십시오. 우리는 서로를 아주 잘 이해하고 있습니다. 그래요. 친절히 나서 주신 데 대해 참으로 감사드립니다. 혹시라도 제가 나아져서 다시 한번 친분을 두터이 할 기회가 온다면—”\n\n그가 일어섰습니다. 저는 그가 가려는 줄 알았습니다. 아니었습니다. 이야기가 더 이어졌고, 전염의 기운이 퍼질 시간도 더 이어졌습니다—그것도 제 방에서 말입니다. 그 점을 기억해 주십시오, 제 방에서요!\n\n“한 순간만 더.” 그가 말했습니다. “물러가기 전에 한 순간만 더 주십시오. 헤어지면서 한 가지 절박한 필요를 당신께 새겨 드릴 것을 허락해 주십시오. 이런 것입니다, 선생. 홀콤 양이 회복될 때까지 기다렸다가 글라이드 부인을 맞으실 생각은 하지 마십시오. 홀콤 양에게는 의사와 블랙워터 파크의 가정부와 경험 많은 간호사가 붙어 있습니다—그 능력과 헌신에 대해서는 제 목숨을 걸고 보증할 수 있는 세 사람입니다. 그 점을 말씀드립니다. 또한 이 점도 말씀드립니다. 언니의 병으로 인한 불안과 놀람이 이미 글라이드 부인의 건강과 기력을 해쳐, 부인은 병실에서 아무 도움도 될 수 없는 상태입니다. 남편과의 관계는 날마다 더 개탄스럽고 더 위태로워지고 있습니다. 부인을 블랙워터 파크에 더 오래 두신다 해도 언니의 회복을 앞당기는 데는 아무 보탬이 되지 않으며, 동시에 당신과 저, 그리고 우리 모두가 집안의 신성한 이익을 위해 반드시 피해야 할 세상의 추문을 무릅쓰게 됩니다. 온 마음을 다해 권합니다. 글라이드 부인께 당장 이리로 오라는 편지를 쓰셔서, 미루는 데 따르는 무거운 책임을 당신의 어깨에서 덜어 내십시오. 애정 어린 의무, 명예로운 의무, 피할 수 없는 의무를 다하십시오. 그러면 앞으로 무슨 일이 일어나든 아무도 당신을 탓할 수 없습니다. 저는 저의 풍부한 경험에서 말씀드리는 것이며, 우정 어린 조언을 드리는 것입니다. 받아들이시겠습니까—예입니까, 아니요입니까?”\n\n저는 그를 바라보았습니다—그저 바라보기만 했습니다—그의 놀라운 뻔뻔함에 대한 제 심정과, 루이를 불러 그를 방 밖으로 내보내게 하겠다는 막 싹튼 결심을 얼굴의 선 하나하나에 담아서 말입니다. 도무지 믿기지 않는 일입니다만 엄연한 사실입니다. 제 얼굴은 그에게 티끌만 한 인상도 주지 못한 듯했습니다. 신경 없이 태어난 사람—분명히 신경 없이 태어난 사람이었습니다.\n\n“망설이십니까?” 그가 말했습니다. “페얼리 씨! 저는 그 망설임을 이해합니다. 당신은 이렇게 반대하시는 것이지요—보십시오, 선생, 제 공감이 당신의 생각 속을 얼마나 곧장 들여다보는지!—글라이드 부인은 햄프셔에서 이곳까지 그 먼 길을 혼자 갈 만한 건강도 기력도 없다고 말입니다. 아시다시피 부인의 하녀는 부인에게서 떼어졌고, 잉글랜드의 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 부인과 동행할 만한 다른 하인은 블랙워터 파크에 하나도 없습니다. 또 이렇게도 반대하시겠지요. 부인은 오는 길에 런던에서 편안히 머물며 쉴 수도 없다고요. 전혀 낯선 여관에 혼자 들어가는 것이 부인으로서는 편안할 리 없으니까요. 한마디로 저는 두 가지 반대를 모두 인정합니다—그리고 또 한마디로 두 가지를 모두 없애 드리겠습니다. 마지막으로 한 번만 저를 따라와 주십시오. 저는 퍼시벌 경과 함께 잉글랜드로 돌아올 때부터 런던 근처에 자리를 잡을 생각이었습니다. 그 계획이 마침 다행히도 이루어졌습니다. 저는 세인트존스우드라는 동네에 가구가 딸린 작은 집을 여섯 달 기한으로 얻었습니다. 부디 이 사실을 마음에 새겨 두시고, 제가 지금 제안하는 순서를 살펴 주십시오. 글라이드 부인이 런던까지 여행합니다(짧은 여정입니다)—제가 직접 역으로 마중을 나갑니다—부인을 제 집으로 모셔 쉬고 주무시게 합니다. 그 집은 부인의 고모의 집이기도 하지요—기력을 되찾으시면 제가 다시 역까지 모셔다 드립니다—부인은 이곳으로 여행하고, 지금 당신의 지붕 아래 있는 부인의 하녀가 마차 문 앞에서 부인을 맞습니다. 여기에 편안함이 고려되어 있고, 여기에 예법의 이익이 고려되어 있으며, 여기에 당신의 의무가—그 셋을 모두 필요로 하는 불행한 부인에 대한 환대와 공감과 보호의 의무가—처음부터 끝까지 매끄럽고 수월하게 마련되어 있습니다. 선생, 집안의 신성한 이익을 위한 제 노력에 힘을 보태 주시기를 진심으로 청합니다. 오늘 제가 그 사정을 대신 아뢰고 있는 그 상처받고 불행한 부인에게, 당신의 집(과 마음)의 환대와 제 집(과 마음)의 환대를 내어 주겠다는 편지를 제 손을 통해 보내시기를 진지하게 권합니다.”\n\n그는 그 끔찍한 손을 제게 흔들어 보였고, 병균이 우글거릴 자기 가슴을 쳤으며, 마치 제가 하원 의사당에 드러누워 있기라도 한 듯 웅변조로 제게 연설했습니다. 무슨 수를 쓰든 필사적인 방책을 택해야 할 때였습니다. 또한 루이를 불러 방을 훈증 소독하는 예방책을 취해야 할 때이기도 했습니다.\n\n이 괴로운 위기 속에서 제게 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐습니다—말하자면 돌 하나로 성가신 새 두 마리를 잡는, 값을 매길 수 없는 생각이었습니다. 저는 이 혐오스러운 외국인의 청을 들어주어 당장 편지를 씀으로써, 백작의 지겨운 웅변과 글라이드 부인의 지겨운 곤경을 한꺼번에 떨쳐 버리기로 결심했습니다. 그 초대가 받아들여질 위험은 조금도 없었습니다. 매리언이 거기 앓아누워 있는 동안 로라가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나는 데 동의할 가능성은 조금도 없었으니까요. 이토록 기막히게 편리한 걸림돌이 어떻게 백작의 그 참견 잘하는 통찰을 피해 갈 수 있었는지는 도무지 짐작할 수 없습니다만, 어쨌든 그것은 그를 피해 갔습니다. 생각할 시간을 더 주면 그가 그것을 알아차릴지도 모른다는 두려움에 저는 놀라울 정도로 기운이 솟아, 안간힘을 써서 몸을 일으켜 앉고는 옆에 있던 필기도구를 집어 들었습니다. 정말로 집어 들었지요. 그러고는 사무실의 평범한 서기라도 된 듯 빠르게 편지를 써냈습니다. “사랑하는 로라, 네가 좋을 때 언제든 오너라. 런던 고모 댁에서 하룻밤 묵어 여정을 나누도록 하고. 사랑하는 매리언이 아프다니 마음이 아프구나. 언제나 너를 아끼는 사람으로부터.” 저는 팔을 뻗어 그 몇 줄을 백작에게 건네고 의자에 도로 파묻히며 말했습니다. “실례합니다—저는 완전히 기진맥진했습니다—더는 아무것도 못 하겠습니다. 아래층에서 쉬시며 점심이라도 드시겠습니까? 모두에게 안부와 위로를 전해 주시고요. 그럼 안녕히 가십시오.”\n\n그는 또 한바탕 연설을 했습니다—이 사람은 정말이지 마르지 않는 샘이었습니다. 저는 눈을 감고 되도록 적게 들으려고 애썼습니다. 그런 노력에도 불구하고 저는 아주 많은 것을 들어야만 했습니다. 제 누이의 그 끝도 없는 남편은 우리 면담의 결과를 두고 스스로를 축하하고 저를 축하했습니다—자기의 공감과 저의 공감에 대해 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 늘어놓았고—제 비참한 건강을 개탄했고—처방전을 써 주겠다고 했고—빛의 중요성에 대해 자기가 한 말을 잊지 말아야 한다고 못 박았고—쉬면서 점심을 들라는 제 너그러운 초대를 받아들였고—이삼일 안에 글라이드 부인이 도착할 것으로 예상하라고 권했고—자신과 저를 함께 아프게 하는 작별 인사를 하는 대신 다음 만남을 기대할 수 있도록 허락해 달라고 청했습니다—그 밖에도 훨씬 더 많은 말을 덧붙였는데, 그때도 귀담아듣지 않았고 지금도 기억나지 않는다는 것이 기쁠 따름입니다. 저는 그 인정 많은 목소리가 조금씩 멀어져 가는 것을 들었습니다만, 그렇게 덩치가 큰데도 그가 걷는 소리는 한 번도 듣지 못했습니다. 그에게는 완벽하게 소리를 내지 않는다는 소극적인 미덕이 있었습니다. 그가 언제 문을 열었는지, 언제 닫았는지 저는 모릅니다. 한동안 침묵이 흐른 뒤 저는 다시 눈을 써 볼 용기를 냈고, 그는 가고 없었습니다.\n\n저는 루이를 부르고 욕실로 물러났습니다. 향이 나는 식초를 탄 미지근한 물로 제 몸을 씻고 서재를 넉넉히 훈증하는 것이 당연히 취해야 할 예방책이었고, 물론 저는 그렇게 했습니다. 그것이 효과가 있었다고 말할 수 있어 기쁩니다. 저는 늘 하던 대로 낮잠을 즐겼습니다. 촉촉하고 서늘한 기분으로 깨어났지요.\n\n제가 맨 먼저 물은 것은 백작에 관한 것이었습니다. 우리가 정말로 그를 떨쳐 냈는가? 그렇습니다—그는 오후 기차로 떠났습니다. 점심은 들었는가, 들었다면 무엇을 들었는가? 오로지 과일 타르트와 크림뿐이었습니다. 어떤 인간인지! 어떤 소화력인지!\n\n제가 더 무슨 말을 하기를 기대하십니까? 저는 아니라고 봅니다. 제게 주어진 몫은 다 채웠다고 봅니다. 뒷날 벌어진 그 충격적인 일들은, 다행히도 제가 있는 자리에서 일어나지 않았습니다. 부디 간청드리건대, 그 일들의 책임 가운데 한 조각이라도 제게 지우는 그런 몰인정한 사람은 아무도 없기를 바랍니다. 저는 최선을 다해 모든 것을 했습니다. 도저히 내다볼 수 없었던 개탄스러운 재앙에 대해 저는 책임이 없습니다. 저는 그 일로 산산이 부서졌고, 그 누구도 겪지 않은 만큼 그 아래에서 고통받았습니다. 제 하인 루이는(제 나름의 아둔한 방식으로나마 정말로 저를 따르는 사람입니다) 제가 결코 이 일을 이겨 내지 못하리라고 생각합니다. 그는 제가 지금 이 순간 손수건을 눈에 대고 구술하는 모습을 보고 있습니다. 제 자신의 명예를 위해 밝혀 두고 싶은 것은, 그것이 제 잘못이 아니었다는 것과 제가 완전히 기진맥진하고 가슴이 찢어졌다는 것입니다. 더 말할 필요가 있겠습니까?\n\n(블랙워터 파크의 가정부)\n\n# I\n\n저는 홀콤 양의 병세가 어떻게 진행되었는지, 그리고 글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 가게 된 정황이 어떠했는지, 제가 아는 바를 있는 그대로 진술해 달라는 요청을 받았습니다.\n\n이런 요구를 제게 하는 이유는, 진실을 밝히는 데 제 증언이 필요하다는 것입니다. 영국 국교회 목사의 미망인으로서(불운으로 형편이 기울어 남의 집에 들어가 일하는 처지가 되었습니다만), 저는 진실의 요구를 다른 무엇보다 앞세우도록 배웠습니다. 그러므로 저는, 괴로운 집안일에 제 이름을 얽어 넣기가 내키지 않아 그렇지 않았더라면 망설였을 이 청을 받아들입니다.\n\n당시에 따로 적어 둔 것이 없어 날짜를 하루까지 정확히 말씀드릴 수는 없습니다만, 홀콤 양의 중병이 시작된 것은 6월의 마지막 두 주, 혹은 열흘 사이였다고 말씀드려도 틀리지 않으리라 믿습니다. 블랙워터 파크의 아침 식사 시간은 늦어서, 때로는 열 시가 되기도 했고 아무리 일러도 아홉 시 반은 넘겼습니다. 지금 말씀드리는 그날 아침, 늘 제일 먼저 내려오시던 홀콤 양이 식탁에 나타나지 않으셨습니다. 집안 식구들이 십오 분쯤 기다린 뒤에 수석 하녀를 올려보내 살펴보게 했더니, 그 아이가 몹시 겁에 질려 방에서 뛰어나왔습니다. 저는 계단에서 그 하인을 만나 곧바로 홀콤 양에게 무슨 일인지 보러 갔습니다. 가엾은 그분은 제게 아무 말도 하실 수 없는 상태였습니다. 손에 펜을 쥔 채 방 안을 이리저리 걸어 다니고 계셨는데, 정신은 온전치 못했고 열이 불덩이처럼 오른 상태였습니다.\n\n글라이드 부인은(이제 저는 퍼시벌 경 댁에서 일하는 몸이 아니니, 예전 안주인을 마님이라 부르는 대신 이름으로 말씀드려도 결례는 아니겠지요) 자기 침실에서 제일 먼저 건너오셨습니다. 그런데 어찌나 놀라고 상심하셨던지 아무 도움이 되지 못하셨습니다. 곧이어 올라오신 포스코 백작과 그 부인은 두 분 다 더없이 요긴하고 친절하셨습니다. 백작부인은 홀콤 양을 침대에 눕히는 일을 저와 함께해 주셨습니다. 백작 각하는 거실에 남으셔서, 제 약상자를 가져오게 하시더니 홀콤 양에게 드릴 물약과 머리에 얹을 냉찜질 물을 만드셨습니다. 의사가 오기 전에 조금이라도 시간을 허비하지 않으시려는 것이었지요. 저희는 냉찜질은 해 드렸지만 물약은 도무지 넘기게 할 수가 없었습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내겠다고 하셨습니다. 마부 하나를 말에 태워 가장 가까운 의사인 오크 로지의 도슨 씨에게 보내셨습니다.\n\n도슨 씨는 한 시간도 안 되어 도착했습니다. 그는 이 근방에서 널리 알려진 점잖은 노인이었는데, 그가 이 병을 매우 위중한 것으로 본다는 사실을 알고 저희는 크게 놀랐습니다.\n\n백작 각하는 도슨 씨와 상냥하게 대화를 나누시며 사려 깊고 거침없이 소견을 말씀하셨습니다. 도슨 씨는 그리 정중하지 못한 태도로, 각하의 조언이 의사로서의 조언이냐고 물었고, 정식으로 개업하지는 않았으나 의학을 공부한 사람의 조언이라는 대답을 듣자, 자기는 아마추어 의사와 상의하는 습관이 없다고 응수했습니다. 백작은 참으로 그리스도인다운 온유함으로 미소만 지으시고 방을 나가셨습니다. 나가시기 전에 그분은, 그날 중에 혹시 찾을 일이 있거든 호숫가의 보트 창고에 있을 것이라고 제게 일러 주셨습니다. 어째서 거기로 가셨는지는 저도 알 수 없습니다. 그러나 실제로 가셔서, 저녁 식사 시간인 일곱 시까지 온종일 돌아오지 않으셨습니다. 아마도 집안을 되도록 조용히 두는 본을 보이려 하셨던 것이겠지요. 그것이야말로 그분의 성품에 꼭 맞는 일이었습니다. 참으로 사려 깊은 귀족이셨습니다.\n\n홀콤 양은 몹시 나쁜 밤을 보내셨습니다. 열이 올랐다 내렸다 하다가 아침이 가까워질수록 나아지기는커녕 더 심해졌습니다. 근처에는 그분을 돌볼 만한 간호인이 없었으므로, 백작부인과 제가 번갈아 가며 그 일을 맡았습니다. 글라이드 부인은 참으로 지각없게도 저희와 함께 밤을 새우겠다고 고집하셨습니다. 그분은 신경이 너무 예민하고 몸이 너무 약해서 홀콤 양의 병으로 인한 불안을 차분히 견뎌 내지 못하셨습니다. 실질적인 도움은 조금도 못 되면서 당신 몸만 상하게 하셨을 뿐입니다. 그보다 더 온화하고 다정한 부인은 세상에 없었습니다만, 우시는 데다 겁을 내셨으니 이 두 가지 약점 때문에 병실에 계시기에는 전혀 적합하지 않으셨습니다.\n\n퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아침에 병세를 물으러 오셨습니다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은(부인의 상심과 홀콤 양의 병 때문이었으리라 짐작합니다만) 몹시 혼란스럽고 안정을 잃은 듯 보이셨습니다. 반대로 백작 각하는 어울리는 침착함과 관심을 보이셨습니다. 한 손에는 밀짚모자를, 다른 손에는 책을 드시고, 제가 듣는 자리에서 퍼시벌 경에게 다시 나가 호수에서 공부하겠노라고 말씀하셨습니다. “집안을 조용히 둡시다.” 하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “여보게, 홀콤 양이 앓고 있으니 실내에서는 담배를 피우지 마세. 자네는 자네 길로 가고 나는 내 길로 가겠네. 나는 공부할 때 혼자 있는 것을 좋아하니까. 안녕히 계십시오, 마이컬슨 부인.”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 저에게 같은 정중한 예의로 작별을 표할 만큼 예의 바르지, 공평하게 말하자면 마음이 가라앉아 있지 못하셨습니다. 사실 그 무렵이든 다른 어느 때든, 이 집에서 저를 형편이 기운 숙녀로 대접해 준 유일한 분은 백작이었습니다. 그분은 참된 귀족의 몸가짐을 지니셨고, 누구에게나 사려 깊으셨습니다. 글라이드 부인의 시중을 들던 어린 아이(패니라는 이름이었습니다)까지도 그분의 눈길 밖에 있지 않았습니다. 그 아이가 퍼시벌 경에게 쫓겨났을 때, 각하는(마침 제게 그 귀여운 새들을 보여 주고 계셨는데) 그 아이가 어찌 되었는지, 블랙워터 파크를 떠나는 날 어디로 갈 것인지 등을 참으로 친절하게 걱정하며 물으셨습니다. 귀족으로 태어난 이의 장점은 언제나 이런 사소하고 섬세한 배려에서 드러나는 법입니다. 이런 자잘한 이야기를 늘어놓는 데 대해 저는 변명하지 않겠습니다. 이것은 각하를 위한 공정함에서 내놓는 것이며, 제가 알기로 그분의 인품은 어떤 자리에서는 꽤 가혹하게 평가되고 있으니까요. 형편이 기운 숙녀를 존중할 줄 알고, 미천한 하녀 아이의 앞날에 아버지 같은 관심을 가질 줄 아는 귀족이라면, 함부로 의심받기에는 너무나 높은 차원의 원칙과 심성을 보여 주는 것입니다. 저는 의견을 내세우지 않습니다. 사실만 내놓을 뿐입니다. 평생 제가 힘써 온 바는, 심판받지 않으려거든 심판하지 말라는 것입니다. 사랑하는 제 남편의 가장 훌륭한 설교 가운데 하나가 그 구절을 본문으로 삼은 것이었습니다. 저는 그것을 늘 읽습니다. 과부가 된 첫 무렵에 예약 구독으로 인쇄한 판본 가운데 제 몫으로 가진 책으로 말입니다. 그리고 읽을 때마다 영적인 유익과 교훈을 더욱 얻습니다.\n\n홀콤 양은 조금도 차도가 없었고, 둘째 날 밤은 첫날 밤보다도 더 나빴습니다. 도슨 씨는 한결같이 곁을 지켰습니다. 실제 간병은 여전히 백작부인과 제가 나누어 맡았고, 글라이드 부인은 저희 둘이 제발 좀 쉬시라고 간청했는데도 함께 밤을 새우겠다고 버티셨습니다. “내 자리는 매리언의 머리맡이에요.”가 그분의 유일한 대답이었습니다. “내가 아프든 성하든, 무슨 일이 있어도 언니에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠어요.”\n\n정오 무렵 저는 제가 늘 하던 일을 보러 아래층으로 내려갔습니다. 한 시간 뒤 병실로 돌아가는 길에, 그날 아침 벌써 세 번째로 나가셨던 백작이 보기에도 몹시 기분이 좋으신 모습으로 현관에 들어서시는 것을 보았습니다. 바로 그때 퍼시벌 경이 서재 문으로 고개를 내밀고, 몹시 다급한 목소리로 그 고귀한 벗에게 이렇게 말을 건네셨습니다.\n\n“그 여자를 찾았나?”\n\n각하의 커다란 얼굴에 잔잔한 미소가 온통 보조개처럼 번졌지만, 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다. 그와 동시에 퍼시벌 경은 고개를 돌려 제가 계단으로 다가오는 것을 보시고는, 더할 나위 없이 무례하고 성난 눈길로 저를 노려보셨습니다.\n\n“여기 들어와서 얘기하게.” 그분이 백작에게 말씀하셨습니다. “집 안에 여자들이 있으면 꼭 계단을 오르내리고 있단 말이야.”\n\n“여보게 퍼시벌.” 각하가 부드럽게 말씀하셨습니다. “마이컬슨 부인께는 맡은 일이 있네. 부인이 그 일을 훌륭히 해내고 계신 것을 나만큼 진심으로 인정해 드리게! 환자분은 어떠십니까, 마이컬슨 부인?”\n\n“나아지지 않으셨습니다, 각하. 말씀드리기 송구합니다.”\n\n“안타깝군요, 참으로 안타깝습니다!” 백작이 말씀하셨습니다. “부인, 몹시 지쳐 보이십니다. 부인과 제 아내가 간병에 도움을 받을 때가 확실히 되었지요. 제가 그 도움을 마련해 드릴 수 있을 듯합니다. 사정이 생겨서 포스코 부인이 내일이나 모레쯤 런던에 다녀와야 하게 되었습니다. 아침에 떠나 밤에 돌아올 텐데, 부인의 짐을 덜어 드릴 간호인을 하나 데려올 겁니다. 품행과 능력이 훌륭하고 마침 일이 비어 있는 사람이지요. 제 아내가 믿을 만한 사람으로 알고 있습니다. 그 여자가 오기 전에는 부디 의사에게 아무 말도 마십시오. 제가 주선한 간호인이라면 무엇이든 곱지 않은 눈으로 볼 테니까요. 이 집에 나타나면 그 여자가 스스로 자기를 증명할 것이고, 도슨 씨도 그 사람을 쓰지 않을 구실이 없다고 인정할 수밖에 없을 겁니다. 글라이드 부인도 같은 말씀을 하시겠지요. 부디 글라이드 부인께 제 깊은 경의와 위로를 전해 주십시오.”\n\n저는 각하의 친절한 배려에 감사의 뜻을 표했습니다. 퍼시벌 경이 그 말을 도중에 끊고, 그 고귀한 벗을 불러(말씀드리기 민망합니다만 불경한 말을 섞어) 서재로 들어오라고, 더 이상 기다리게 하지 말라고 소리치셨습니다.\n\n저는 위층으로 올라갔습니다. 사람은 가엾게도 허물 많은 존재라, 여자의 신조가 아무리 굳게 서 있다 해도 부질없는 호기심의 유혹에 언제나 자신을 지키지는 못합니다. 부끄럽게도 그때 저는 부질없는 호기심에 제 신조가 지고 말아, 퍼시벌 경이 서재 문 앞에서 그 고귀한 벗에게 던진 물음을 지나치게 궁금해하고 말았습니다. 백작이 블랙워터 파크에서 학구적인 아침 산책을 하시는 동안 만나기로 되어 있던 사람은 누구였을까요? 퍼시벌 경의 물음투로 보아 여자였으리라 짐작되었습니다. 저는 백작에게 부적절한 구석이 있으리라고는 의심하지 않았습니다. 그분의 도덕적 인품을 너무 잘 알았으니까요. 제가 스스로에게 던진 물음은 오직 하나였습니다. 그분은 그 여자를 찾으셨을까?\n\n다시 이야기를 이어 가겠습니다. 그날 밤도 여느 때와 다름없이 지나갔고 홀콤 양에게 나아진 기미는 없었습니다. 이튿날은 조금 나아 보이셨습니다. 그다음 날 백작부인은, 제가 듣는 자리에서는 누구에게도 그 여행의 목적을 말하지 않으신 채 아침 기차로 런던을 향해 떠나셨습니다. 그 고귀한 남편은 늘 하시던 대로 정성껏 역까지 배웅하셨습니다.\n\n이제 홀콤 양을 돌보는 일은 오롯이 제 몫이 되었고, 동생분이 머리맡을 떠나지 않겠다고 고집하시는 바람에 다음번에는 글라이드 부인마저 제가 간호하게 되리라는 것이 눈에 훤히 보였습니다.\n\n그날 하루 동안 일어난 일 가운데 그나마 중요한 것이라고는, 의사와 백작 사이에 또 한 번 불쾌한 마주침이 있었던 것뿐입니다.\n\n각하는 역에서 돌아오시자 병세를 물으러 홀콤 양의 거실로 올라오셨습니다. 마침 도슨 씨와 글라이드 부인이 함께 환자 곁에 계셨으므로, 제가 침실에서 나가 그분과 이야기를 나누었습니다. 백작은 치료와 증상에 대해 여러 가지를 물으셨습니다. 저는 치료가 이른바 “염류” 요법이라는 것과, 열이 물러난 사이의 증상은 분명 쇠약과 탈진이 심해지는 것이라고 말씀드렸습니다. 이 마지막 대목을 말씀드리는 참에 도슨 씨가 침실에서 나왔습니다.\n\n“안녕하십니까, 선생.” 각하는 더없이 정중한 태도로 앞으로 나서며, 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 귀티 나는 결연함으로 의사를 붙들고 말씀하셨습니다. “오늘도 증상에 차도가 없는 것은 아닌지 몹시 염려됩니다만?”\n\n“분명한 차도가 있습니다.” 도슨 씨가 대답했습니다.\n\n“이 열병에 여전히 그 쇠약시키는 요법을 고집하시는 겁니까?” 각하가 이어 물으셨습니다.\n\n“저는 제 직업적 경험이 뒷받침하는 치료를 고수합니다.” 도슨 씨가 말했습니다.\n\n“직업적 경험이라는 저 광대한 주제에 대해 한 가지만 여쭙게 해 주십시오.” 백작이 말씀하셨습니다. “저는 더는 감히 조언하지 않겠습니다. 그저 감히 여쭙기만 하겠습니다. 선생은 과학 활동의 거대한 중심지인 런던과 파리에서 다소 떨어진 곳에 사시지요. 열병이 몸을 축내는 작용을, 지친 환자에게 브랜디와 포도주, 암모니아, 키니네로 기운을 북돋아 합리적이고 납득할 만하게 회복시킨다는 이야기를 들어 보신 적이 있습니까? 최고 의학 권위자들의 그 새로운 이단설이 선생의 귀에 닿은 적이 있습니까, 예입니까 아니오입니까?”\n\n“그 질문을 의사가 제게 한다면 기꺼이 대답하겠습니다.” 의사는 나가려고 문을 열며 말했습니다. “선생은 의사가 아니시니, 대답을 사양하겠습니다.”\n\n이렇게 변명의 여지 없이 무례한 방식으로 한쪽 뺨을 얻어맞고도, 백작은 실천하는 그리스도인답게 즉시 다른 뺨을 내밀며 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말씀하셨습니다. “안녕히 가십시오, 도슨 씨.”\n\n세상을 떠난 제 사랑하는 남편이 각하를 알 만한 복을 누렸더라면, 그이와 백작은 서로를 얼마나 높이 존경했을까요!\n\n백작부인은 그날 밤 마지막 기차로 돌아오시면서 런던에서 간호인을 데려오셨습니다. 저는 그 사람의 이름이 루벨 부인이라고 전해 들었습니다. 생김새와, 입을 열었을 때의 서투른 영어로 보아 외국인임을 알 수 있었습니다.\n\n저는 외국인에 대해 늘 너그러운 마음을 기르려고 애써 왔습니다. 그들은 우리가 누리는 축복과 이점을 갖지 못했고, 대개는 교황교의 눈먼 오류 속에서 자랐으니까요. 또한 남에게 대접받고 싶은 대로 남을 대접하라는 것이, 제 앞서 사랑하는 남편의 가르침이자 실천이었듯(고故 새뮤얼 마이컬슨 목사 문학사의 설교집 제29편을 보십시오) 제 가르침이자 실천이기도 했습니다. 이 두 가지 이유에서, 루벨 부인이 쉰 살 안팎의 몸집 작고 깡마르고 음흉한 사람으로, 낯빛은 짙은 갈색이거나 크리올 사람 같고 눈은 옅은 회색으로 사방을 살피더라는 말은 하지 않겠습니다. 방금 든 이유에서, 그 여자의 옷이 아주 수수한 검은 비단이기는 했으나 그런 처지의 사람에게는 어울리지 않게 감이 값지고 장식과 마무리가 쓸데없이 고급스러워 보이더라는 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 저 자신이 그런 말을 듣기는 싫으니, 루벨 부인에 대해서도 그런 말을 하지 않는 것이 제 도리입니다. 다만 이 정도만 말씀드리겠습니다. 그 여자의 태도는 불쾌할 만큼 쌀쌀맞다기보다 다만 유난히 조용하고 물러서 있는 편이었다는 것, 주위를 아주 많이 둘러보면서 말은 거의 하지 않았다는 것, 이는 블랙워터 파크에서 자기 처지를 미덥지 않게 여긴 탓일 수도 있으나 그만큼 겸손한 성품 탓일 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 제가 제 방으로 정중히 청했는데도 저녁 식사를 함께 들기를 사양했다는 것(이는 어쩌면 이상한 일이었을지 몰라도 결코 수상한 일은 아니지 않겠습니까?) 정도입니다.\n\n백작의 각별한 제안으로(참으로 각하다운 너그러운 친절이지요!), 루벨 부인은 이튿날 아침 의사가 보고 승낙하기 전에는 일을 시작하지 않기로 정해졌습니다. 그날 밤은 제가 새웠습니다. 글라이드 부인은 새 간호인을 홀콤 양에게 붙이는 것을 몹시 꺼리시는 듯했습니다. 교양과 품위를 갖추신 부인께서 외국인에게 그토록 너그럽지 못하시다니 저는 놀랐습니다. 저는 조심스레 말씀드렸습니다. “마님, 우리보다 못한 사람들을 성급히 판단하지 않도록 다들 유념해야지요. 더구나 외국에서 온 사람이라면요.” 글라이드 부인은 제 말을 듣고 계신 것 같지 않았습니다. 그저 한숨을 쉬시고는, 이불 위에 놓인 홀콤 양의 손에 입을 맞추셨습니다. 흥분시키지 않는 것이 무엇보다 중요한 환자가 있는 병실에서 결코 분별 있는 행동이라 할 수는 없었지요. 그러나 가엾은 글라이드 부인은 간병에 대해 아무것도 모르셨습니다. 애석하게도 정말 아무것도 모르셨습니다.\n\n이튿날 아침 루벨 부인은 의사가 침실로 가는 길에 보고 승낙하도록 거실로 보내졌습니다.\n\n저는 마침 잠들어 계신 홀콤 양 곁에 글라이드 부인을 남겨 두고, 자기 처지가 불확실한 탓에 그 여자가 낯설고 불안하게 느끼지 않도록 다정히 살펴 줄 생각으로 루벨 부인에게 갔습니다. 그런데 그 여자는 그렇게 여기는 것 같지 않았습니다. 도슨 씨가 자기를 승낙하리라고 미리부터 아주 흡족하게 확신하는 듯, 창밖을 내다보며 시골 공기를 즐기는 기색이 역력한 채로 태연히 앉아 있었습니다. 어떤 사람들은 그런 태도를 뻔뻔한 배짱이라 여겼을지도 모릅니다. 저는 좀 더 너그럽게 그것을 남다른 담력으로 여겼다고 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\n\n의사가 저희에게 올라오는 대신, 제가 의사를 보러 오라는 부름을 받았습니다. 저는 이렇게 일이 뒤바뀐 것이 좀 이상하다고 생각했지만, 루벨 부인은 그 일에 조금도 동요하는 기색이 없었습니다. 저는 그 여자가 여전히 태연히 창밖을 내다보며 여전히 말없이 시골 공기를 즐기도록 두고 나왔습니다.\n\n도슨 씨는 아침 식사 방에서 혼자 저를 기다리고 있었습니다.\n\n“이번에 온 간호인 말입니다, 마이컬슨 부인.” 의사가 말했습니다.\n\n“네, 선생님?”\n\n“그 여자는 늘 내 일에 참견하려 드는 저 뚱뚱한 늙은 외국인의 아내가 런던에서 데려온 사람이더군요. 마이컬슨 부인, 그 뚱뚱한 늙은 외국인은 돌팔이입니다.”\n\n이것은 매우 무례한 말이었습니다. 저는 당연히 충격을 받았습니다.\n\n“선생님.” 제가 말했습니다. “지금 귀족을 두고 하시는 말씀인 줄은 아십니까?”\n\n“흥! 이름 앞에 작위 붙인 돌팔이가 그자가 처음도 아니지요. 하나같이 백작입디다, 빌어먹을!”\n\n“최고의 귀족 가문 사람이 아니었다면 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 벗이 되지 못하셨겠지요, 선생님. 물론 영국 귀족은 별개입니다만.”\n\n“좋습니다, 마이컬슨 부인. 그자를 뭐라 부르든 마음대로 하시고, 간호인 이야기로 돌아갑시다. 나는 이미 그 여자를 반대했습니다.”\n\n“보지도 않으시고요, 선생님?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 보지도 않고요. 세상에 둘도 없는 간호인일지도 모르지만, 내가 구한 간호인은 아니니까요. 나는 이 집 주인인 퍼시벌 경께 그 반대 의견을 말씀드렸습니다. 경은 내 편을 들지 않으시더군요. 내가 구했더라도 런던에서 온 낯선 사람이기는 마찬가지였을 테고, 부인의 고모께서 수고스럽게 런던까지 가서 데려온 사람이니 한번 써 보아야 한다고 하십니다. 일리가 없지 않아서 나도 대놓고 안 된다고는 못 하겠더군요. 그러나 나는 그 여자에게 흠잡을 일이 생기면 즉시 내보낸다는 조건을 달았습니다. 이 정도 제안은 담당 의사로서 할 권리가 있는 것이라, 퍼시벌 경도 승낙하셨습니다. 자, 마이컬슨 부인, 나는 부인을 믿을 수 있다는 걸 압니다. 처음 하루 이틀은 그 간호인을 눈여겨봐 주시고, 홀콤 양께 내 약 말고 다른 약은 절대 드리지 못하게 해 주십시오. 부인이 그리 떠받드는 저 외국 귀족은 내 환자에게 자기 돌팔이 처방을(최면술까지 포함해서) 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났는데, 그 사람 아내가 데려온 간호인이라면 그를 돕는 데 지나치게 기꺼울지도 모릅니다. 아시겠지요? 좋습니다, 그럼 위층으로 올라가지요. 간호인이 거기 있습니까? 병실에 들어가기 전에 한마디 해 두어야겠습니다.”\n\n저희가 가 보니 루벨 부인은 여전히 창가에서 흡족해하고 있었습니다. 제가 그 여자를 도슨 씨에게 소개했을 때, 의사의 미심쩍은 눈길도 캐묻는 질문도 그 여자를 조금도 당황시키지 못하는 듯했습니다. 그 여자는 서투른 영어로 조용히 대답했고, 의사가 애써 말문을 막으려 했는데도 자기 일에 관해서는 어느 대목에서도 무지를 드러내는 법이 없었습니다. 이는 앞서 말씀드린 대로 결코 뻔뻔한 배짱이 아니라 분명 담력에서 나온 것이었습니다.\n\n저희는 다 함께 침실로 들어갔습니다.\n\n루벨 부인은 환자를 아주 유심히 살펴보고, 글라이드 부인에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고, 방 안의 자잘한 것 한두 가지를 바로잡아 놓고는, 부름을 받을 때까지 기다리려고 구석에 조용히 앉았습니다. 글라이드 부인은 낯선 간호인이 나타난 것에 놀라고 언짢아하시는 듯했습니다. 아직 잠들어 계신 홀콤 양을 깨울까 봐 아무도 입을 열지 않았는데, 다만 의사가 밤새 어떠했는지 속삭여 물었습니다. 저는 “여느 때와 비슷했습니다.”라고 나직이 대답했고, 그러고 나서 도슨 씨는 나갔습니다. 글라이드 부인이 그를 따라 나가셨는데, 루벨 부인 이야기를 하시려는 것이었겠지요. 저로서는 이 조용한 외국 여자가 자리를 지키게 되리라고 이미 마음속으로 결론을 내리고 있었습니다. 그 여자는 정신을 온전히 차리고 있었고, 자기 일을 확실히 알고 있었습니다. 적어도 거기까지는 저라도 머리맡에서 그보다 훨씬 잘하지는 못했을 것입니다.\n\n도슨 씨의 당부를 잊지 않고, 저는 그 뒤 사나흘 동안 이따금 루벨 부인을 매섭게 살폈습니다. 몇 번이고 소리 없이 불쑥 방에 들어가 보았지만, 수상한 짓을 하는 것을 한 번도 잡아내지 못했습니다. 저만큼이나 유심히 그 여자를 지켜보시던 글라이드 부인도 아무것도 발견하지 못하셨습니다. 저는 약병에 손을 댄 흔적을 조금도 찾지 못했고, 루벨 부인이 백작에게 한마디 하는 것도, 백작이 그 여자에게 한마디 하는 것도 본 적이 없습니다. 그 여자는 나무랄 데 없는 정성과 분별로 홀콤 양을 돌보았습니다. 가엾은 그분은 반쯤은 실신이고 반쯤은 선잠인 나른한 탈진 상태와, 정신이 어느 정도 오락가락하는 열의 발작 사이를 오갔습니다. 루벨 부인은 앞의 상태일 때는 결코 그분을 흔들어 깨우지 않았고, 뒤의 상태일 때는 낯선 얼굴로 머리맡에 불쑥 나타나 그분을 놀라게 하는 일이 결코 없었습니다. 공을 세운 이에게는 공을 돌려야 하는 법이니(외국인이든 영국인이든 말입니다) 저는 그 권리를 루벨 부인에게도 공평하게 드립니다. 그 여자는 자기 이야기를 유난히 하지 않았고, 병실 일을 아는 경험 많은 사람의 조언이라면 무엇이든 너무 조용히 아랑곳하지 않았습니다. 그러나 그런 흠을 빼면 좋은 간호인이었고, 글라이드 부인이나 도슨 씨에게 불평할 만한 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없습니다.\n\n그다음으로 이 집에 일어난 중요한 일은, 볼일이 생겨 런던에 가시게 된 백작이 잠시 집을 비우신 것이었습니다. 제 기억으로 그분은 루벨 부인이 온 지 나흘째 되는 날 아침에 떠나셨는데, 떠나시면서 제가 있는 자리에서 글라이드 부인께 홀콤 양의 일을 두고 아주 진지하게 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n“며칠만 더 도슨 씨를 믿어 보십시오.” 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “그러나 그동안 차도가 없거든 런던에서 의견을 구하십시오. 저 노새 같은 의사도 싫든 좋든 받아들일 수밖에 없을 겁니다. 도슨 씨의 비위를 거스르고, 홀콤 양을 구하십시오. 저는 명예를 걸고, 진심을 다해 진지하게 이 말씀을 드립니다.”\n\n각하는 지극한 정과 친절을 담아 말씀하셨습니다. 그러나 가엾은 글라이드 부인은 신경이 완전히 무너져 내려서 그분을 몹시 두려워하시는 것 같았습니다. 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떠시면서, 그분이 작별을 고하시는데도 한마디도 하지 못하셨습니다. 그분이 가신 뒤에 부인은 제게 돌아서서 말씀하셨습니다. “아, 마이컬슨 부인, 언니 때문에 가슴이 미어지는데 의논할 벗이 하나도 없어요! 도슨 씨가 틀렸다고 생각하세요? 오늘 아침에 그이가 직접 저에게 걱정할 것도 없고 다른 의사를 부를 필요도 없다고 말했는데요.”\n\n“도슨 씨께는 죄송한 말씀입니다만,” 제가 대답했습니다. “제가 마님이라면 백작님의 조언을 잊지 않겠습니다.”\n\n글라이드 부인은 갑자기 제게서 돌아서셨는데, 그 절망하는 듯한 모습을 저로서는 도무지 설명할 수 없었습니다.\n\n“그 사람의 조언이라니!” 부인이 혼잣말을 하셨습니다. “하느님, 저희를 도우소서. 그 사람의 조언이라니!”\n\n백작이 블랙워터 파크를 비우신 것은 제 기억으로 거의 일주일이었습니다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 각하가 안 계신 것을 여러모로 아쉬워하시는 듯했고, 제가 보기에는 집안의 병과 근심 때문에 몹시 침울해지고 달라지신 듯도 했습니다. 이따금 어찌나 안절부절못하시는지 제 눈에 띄지 않을 수 없었습니다. 오락가락하시고, 저택 뜰 여기저기 아무 데나 헤매고 다니셨지요. 홀콤 양에 대해서, 또 건강이 나빠지시는 것이 그분께 진심으로 근심을 안기는 듯한 부인에 대해서 물으시는 것은 더없이 자상하셨습니다. 저는 그분의 마음이 많이 누그러졌다고 생각합니다. 만일 어느 친절한 목사 벗, 세상을 떠난 제 훌륭한 남편 같은 그런 벗이 그때 곁에 있었더라면, 퍼시벌 경에게서 기쁜 도덕적 진전을 이끌어 낼 수도 있었을 것입니다. 저는 이런 문제에 대해서는 좀처럼 잘못 보는 일이 없습니다. 행복했던 결혼 시절의 경험이 저를 이끌어 주니까요.\n\n이제 아래층에서 퍼시벌 경의 유일한 말벗이 된 백작부인은 제가 보기에 그분을 오히려 소홀히 대하셨습니다. 어쩌면 그분이 부인을 소홀히 대하신 것인지도 모르지요. 낯선 이라면 두 분이 이제 단둘이 남게 되자 서로를 아예 피하기로 작정하신 것이라고 생각했을지도 모릅니다. 물론 그럴 리야 없겠지요. 그러나 어쨌든 백작부인은 점심시간에 저녁 식사를 대신 드셨고, 루벨 부인이 간병을 전부 떠맡아 손을 놓게 되셨는데도 저녁 무렵이면 늘 위층으로 올라오셨습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 혼자서 저녁을 드셨는데, 제복을 입지 않는 하인 윌리엄이 제가 듣는 데서 주인 어른이 음식은 절반으로 줄이고 술은 곱절로 늘리셨다고 말했습니다. 하인이 이런 건방진 소리를 하는 데에 저는 아무런 무게도 두지 않습니다. 그때도 그를 나무랐고, 지금 이 자리에서도 다시 한 번 나무라는 것으로 알아주시기 바랍니다.\n\n그 뒤 며칠 동안 홀콤 양은 저희 모두가 보기에 분명 조금씩 나아지시는 듯했습니다. 도슨 씨에 대한 저희의 믿음도 되살아났습니다. 그는 이 병에 대해 매우 자신 있어 보였고, 글라이드 부인이 그 문제로 말을 꺼내시자 조금이라도 의심의 그림자가 마음을 스치는 순간 자기 쪽에서 먼저 전문의를 부르자고 하겠노라고 장담했습니다.\n\n저희 중에 이 말을 듣고도 마음이 놓이지 않는 듯한 유일한 분은 백작부인이었습니다. 그분은 제게만 따로, 도슨 씨의 말만 믿고는 홀콤 양의 일을 마음 놓을 수 없으며 남편이 돌아와 의견을 말해 주기를 애타게 기다리겠다고 하셨습니다. 편지로 알려 온 바에 따르면 그 귀환은 사흘 뒤가 될 것이라 했습니다. 백작 내외분은 각하가 집을 비우신 동안 아침마다 어김없이 편지를 주고받으셨습니다. 그 점에서도, 다른 모든 점에서와 마찬가지로 두 분은 부부의 본보기셨습니다.\n\n사흘째 되는 날 저녁, 저는 홀콤 양에게서 심각한 걱정을 자아내는 변화를 알아차렸습니다. 루벨 부인도 그것을 알아차렸습니다. 저희는 그 일에 대해 글라이드 부인께 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그분은 그때 탈진에 완전히 짓눌려 거실 소파에 누워 잠들어 계셨습니다.\n\n도슨 씨는 여느 때보다 늦게야 저녁 왕진을 왔습니다. 환자를 보자마자 그의 얼굴빛이 달라지는 것이 제 눈에 보였습니다. 그는 감추려 했지만, 당황하고 놀란 기색이 역력했습니다. 사람을 그의 집으로 보내 약상자를 가져오게 했고, 방 안에 소독약을 썼으며, 그 자신의 지시로 이 집 안에 그가 잘 침상이 마련되었습니다. “열이 전염병으로 돌아섰습니까?” 하고 제가 그에게 속삭였습니다. “그런 것 같습니다.” 그가 대답했습니다. “내일 아침이면 더 확실히 알겠지요.”\n\n도슨 씨 본인의 지시에 따라 글라이드 부인께는 이 악화 사실을 알리지 않았습니다. 그는 부인의 건강을 이유로 그날 밤 침실에서 저희와 함께 계시는 것을 딱 잘라 금했습니다. 부인은 버티려 하셨고, 가슴 아픈 장면이 있었습니다. 그러나 그에게는 의사로서의 권위가 있었으므로 그의 뜻이 관철되었습니다.\n\n이튿날 아침 열한 시에 남자 하인 하나가 런던의 전문의에게 보내는 편지를 지니고, 되도록 가장 이른 기차로 그 새 의사를 모셔 오라는 분부를 받고 런던으로 떠났습니다. 심부름꾼이 떠난 지 반 시간 뒤에 백작이 블랙워터 파크로 돌아오셨습니다.\n\n백작부인은 자기 판단으로 곧장 그분을 환자에게 데려가셨습니다. 그렇게 하신 데에 제가 보기에 부적절한 구석은 없었습니다. 각하는 아내가 있는 분이었고, 홀콤 양의 아버지뻘 되는 나이였으며, 여자 친척인 글라이드 부인의 고모가 함께 있는 자리에서 그분을 보셨으니까요. 그런데도 도슨 씨는 그분이 방에 있는 것에 항의했지만, 그때 의사는 너무 놀란 나머지 이 일로 진지하게 버티지는 못하는 것이 제 눈에 분명히 보였습니다.\n\n병으로 고통받는 가엾은 그분은 이미 곁에 있는 사람을 알아보지 못하는 지경이었습니다. 벗을 적으로 여기시는 듯했습니다. 백작이 머리맡으로 다가가시자, 그때까지 쉴 새 없이 방 안을 이리저리 헤매던 그분의 눈이 그분의 얼굴에 멎으며 무서운 공포의 응시를 보냈는데, 저는 그것을 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 것입니다. 백작은 그분 곁에 앉아 맥과 관자놀이를 짚어 보시고, 아주 유심히 살펴보신 다음, 의사를 향해 홱 돌아서시는데 그 얼굴에 어린 분노와 경멸의 빛이 어찌나 대단하던지 도슨 씨의 입술에서 말이 얼어붙었고, 그는 잠시 노여움과 놀람으로 창백해진 채, 창백하게 그리고 아주 벙어리가 되어 서 있었습니다.\n\n각하는 다음으로 저를 보셨습니다.\n\n“변화가 언제 일어났습니까?” 그분이 물으셨습니다.\n\n저는 그 시각을 말씀드렸습니다.\n\n“그 뒤로 글라이드 부인이 이 방에 들어온 적이 있습니까?”\n\n저는 없다고 대답했습니다. 의사가 어제저녁에 부인이 방에 들어오시는 것을 딱 잘라 금했고, 아침에 그 분부를 다시 되풀이했다고 말씀드렸습니다.\n\n“부인과 루벨 부인은 이 사태가 얼마나 심각한지 온전히 알고 계십니까?” 하는 것이 그분의 다음 물음이었습니다.\n\n저는 이 병이 전염된다고 여겨진다는 것은 알고 있다고 대답했습니다. 제가 더 덧붙이기 전에 그분이 말을 막으셨습니다.\n\n“발진티푸스입니다.” 그분이 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n이런 문답이 오가는 동안 흐른 그 일 분 사이에 도슨 씨는 정신을 추슬러, 여느 때의 단호함으로 백작에게 말했습니다.\n\n“발진티푸스가 아닙니다.” 그가 날카롭게 말했습니다. “선생, 나는 이 무단 개입에 항의합니다. 여기서 질문할 권리는 나 말고 누구에게도 없습니다. 나는 내 능력이 미치는 한 최선을 다해 내 소임을—”\n\n백작이 그의 말을 끊으셨는데, 말로가 아니라 다만 침대를 가리켜 보이는 것으로였습니다. 도슨 씨는 자기 능력에 대한 주장에 그 말 없는 반박이 가해지는 것을 느끼는 듯했고, 그럴수록 더욱 화가 치미는 모양이었습니다.\n\n“나는 내 소임을 다했다고 말했습니다.” 그가 되풀이했습니다. “런던으로 전문의를 부르러 사람을 보냈습니다. 이 열병의 성질에 대해서는 그와 상의할 것이고, 다른 누구와도 상의하지 않겠습니다. 이 방에서 나가 주시기를 요구합니다.”\n\n“선생, 나는 인류애라는 신성한 뜻에서 이 방에 들어왔습니다.” 백작이 말씀하셨습니다. “그리고 같은 뜻에서, 전문의가 오는 것이 늦어진다면 나는 다시 이 방에 들어올 것입니다. 다시 한 번 경고하건대 이 열병은 발진티푸스로 돌아섰고, 이 통탄할 변화의 책임은 당신의 치료에 있습니다. 저 불행한 부인이 세상을 떠난다면, 나는 법정에서 당신의 무지와 고집이 그분의 죽음의 원인이었다고 증언하겠습니다.”\n\n도슨 씨가 대답하기도 전에, 백작이 저희 곁을 떠나기도 전에, 거실 쪽에서 문이 열리며 문지방에 서 계신 글라이드 부인이 저희 눈에 들어왔습니다.\n\n“나는 들어가야겠어요, 반드시 들어가겠어요.” 부인이 놀라운 단호함으로 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n백작은 부인을 막는 대신 거실로 물러서서 부인이 들어오시도록 길을 내주셨습니다. 다른 어떤 경우에도 그분은 세상에서 무엇 하나 잊는 일이 없는 분이었으나, 그 순간의 놀라움 속에서 발진티푸스가 옮을 위험도, 글라이드 부인이 자신을 제대로 돌보시도록 강권해야 할 절박한 필요도 잊어버리신 모양이었습니다.\n\n놀랍게도 도슨 씨가 더 침착한 판단력을 보였습니다. 그는 부인이 침대 쪽으로 첫걸음을 내딛는 순간 그 앞을 막아섰습니다. “진심으로 송구합니다, 진심으로 안타깝습니다.” 그가 말했습니다. “이 열병은 아무래도 전염성이 있을지 모릅니다. 그렇지 않다는 확신이 설 때까지는, 부디 이 방에 들어오지 마시기를 간청드립니다.”\n\n부인은 잠시 몸부림치다가 문득 두 팔을 늘어뜨리고 앞으로 쓰러졌습니다. 정신을 잃은 것이었습니다. 백작부인과 저는 부인을 의사에게서 넘겨받아 부인의 방으로 옮겼습니다. 백작이 앞서 걸어갔고, 제가 밖으로 나와 부인이 혼절에서 깨어났다고 알려드릴 때까지 복도에서 기다리고 있었습니다.\n\n저는 다시 의사에게로 가서, 글라이드 부인의 분부대로 부인이 지금 당장 의사와 이야기하기를 고집하신다고 전했습니다. 그는 곧바로 물러가 부인의 격동을 가라앉히고, 몇 시간 안에 전문의가 도착하리라는 것을 부인께 확언해 드렸습니다. 그 몇 시간이 무척이나 더디게 흘렀습니다. 퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아래층에 함께 있으면서 이따금 사람을 올려 보내 병세를 물었습니다. 마침내 다섯 시와 여섯 시 사이에, 우리 모두가 크게 안도하게도 전문의가 왔습니다.\n\n그는 도슨 씨보다 젊은 사람이었고, 매우 진지하며 매우 단호했습니다. 그가 그때까지의 치료를 어떻게 생각했는지는 제가 알 수 없으나, 그가 의사보다 저와 루벨 부인에게 훨씬 더 많은 질문을 던지는 것, 그리고 도슨 씨의 환자를 진찰하는 동안 도슨 씨의 말을 그다지 관심 있게 듣지 않는 듯 보이는 것이 저에게는 이상하게 여겨졌습니다. 이런 식으로 제가 관찰한 바로 미루어, 저는 백작이 이 병에 대해 처음부터 끝까지 옳았던 것이 아닌가 의심하기 시작했습니다. 그리고 도슨 씨가 얼마쯤 뜸을 들인 뒤, 런던의 전문의를 부른 목적이었던 바로 그 중대한 질문을 던졌을 때 저는 자연히 그 생각을 굳히게 되었습니다.\n\n“열병에 대한 선생의 소견은 어떠십니까?” 그가 물었습니다.\n\n“발진티푸스입니다.” 전문의가 대답했습니다. “의심할 여지 없는 발진티푸스 열병입니다.”\n\n그 조용한 외국인 루벨 부인은 야위고 거무스름한 두 손을 앞으로 모아 포개고서, 자못 의미심장한 미소를 띤 채 저를 바라보았습니다. 백작 자신이 그 방에 있으면서 제 소견이 확인되는 것을 직접 들었다 한들, 그보다 더 흡족한 표정을 지을 수는 없었을 것입니다.\n\n전문의는 환자를 돌보는 방법에 관해 유익한 지시를 몇 가지 내리고, 닷새 뒤에 다시 오겠다고 말한 다음, 도슨 씨와 따로 상의하려고 물러갔습니다. 홀콤 양의 회복 가능성에 대해서는 아무런 소견도 밝히려 하지 않았습니다. 병이 이 단계에 있는 이상 이렇다 저렇다 단언하기는 불가능하다는 것이었습니다.\n\n그 닷새는 애태우며 지나갔습니다.\n\n포스코 백작부인과 저는 번갈아 가며 루벨 부인과 교대했는데, 홀콤 양의 병세가 점점 더 나빠져 우리의 온 정성과 주의를 요구했기 때문입니다. 참으로 견디기 힘든 시간이었습니다. 글라이드 부인은 (도슨 씨의 말로는 언니 걱정에 조마조마한 마음이 끊임없이 팽팽하게 지탱해 준 덕분에) 참으로 놀라운 방식으로 기운을 되찾았고, 제가 여태 부인에게 있으리라고는 결코 생각지 못했던 굳셈과 결단을 보여 주었습니다. 부인은 하루에도 두세 번씩 병실에 들어와 자기 눈으로 직접 홀콤 양을 보아야겠다고 고집했고, 의사가 그만큼이라도 뜻을 들어준다면 침대에 너무 가까이 가지는 않겠다고 약속했습니다. 도슨 씨는 몹시 마지못해 요구받은 양보를 했습니다. 제 생각에 그는 부인과 다투어 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알아차렸던 것 같습니다. 부인은 날마다 들어왔고, 스스로를 억누르며 약속을 지켰습니다. 그런 처지에서 부인이 얼마나 괴로워하는지를 지켜보는 일이 (제 남편이 마지막 병을 앓던 때의 제 슬픔을 떠올리게 하여) 저 개인적으로도 어찌나 마음 아팠는지, 이 대목에 대해서는 더 이상 늘어놓지 않기를 부디 양해해 주시기 바랍니다. 그보다는 도슨 씨와 백작 사이에 새로운 다툼이 일어나지는 않았다는 말씀을 드리는 편이 저로서는 더 마음 편합니다. 백작 각하는 모든 문의를 사람을 시켜서 했고, 아래층에서 줄곧 퍼시벌 경과 함께 지냈습니다.\n\n닷샛날 전문의가 다시 와서 우리에게 약간의 희망을 주었습니다. 그는 발진티푸스 증상이 처음 나타난 날로부터 열흘째가 되는 날이 아마도 병의 결말을 판가름하리라고 말했고, 세 번째 왕진을 그날로 잡았습니다. 그 사이의 기간은 전과 다름없이 흘렀습니다. 다만 백작이 어느 날 아침 다시 런던에 갔다가 밤에 돌아온 일만은 달랐습니다.\n\n열흘째 되는 날, 자비로우신 섭리께서 우리 집안을 더 이상의 근심과 놀람에서 놓아 주시기를 기뻐하셨습니다. 전문의는 홀콤 양이 고비를 넘겼다고 단호히 확언했습니다. “이제 이분께는 의사가 필요 없습니다. 앞으로 얼마 동안 세심하게 지켜보며 간호하는 것, 그것만이 필요할 뿐인데 그것은 이미 갖추어져 있는 것으로 보입니다.” 그것이 그의 말 그대로였습니다. 그날 저녁 저는 남편이 쓴 「병에서의 회복」이라는 감동적인 설교를 읽었는데, 예전에 그것에서 얻었던 그 어떤 때보다도 (영적인 관점에서) 더 큰 행복과 유익을 느꼈습니다.\n\n그 반가운 소식이 가엾은 글라이드 부인에게 미친 영향은, 참으로 애석하게도 감당하기 어려운 것이었습니다. 부인은 너무 쇠약해서 그 격렬한 반동을 견디지 못했고, 하루 이틀 뒤에는 쇠약과 우울에 빠져 방에만 머물러야 했습니다. 안정과 평온, 그리고 그 뒤의 요양지 이동이 도슨 씨가 부인을 위해 내놓을 수 있는 최선의 처방이었습니다. 사정이 그 이상 나빠지지 않은 것은 다행이었습니다. 왜냐하면 부인이 방에 틀어박힌 바로 그 이튿날, 백작과 의사 사이에 또 한 번 의견 충돌이 있었기 때문입니다. 그런데 이번 다툼은 성질이 어찌나 심각했던지 도슨 씨가 집을 떠나 버리고 말았습니다.\n\n저는 그 자리에 있지 않았으나, 다툼의 주제는 열병으로 기진한 뒤 홀콤 양의 회복을 돕기 위해 얼마만큼의 영양을 공급해야 하는가 하는 문제였다고 들었습니다. 도슨 씨는 이제 환자가 안전해진 만큼 문외한의 간섭에 굽히고 들어갈 마음이 그 어느 때보다도 적었고, 백작은 (저로서는 까닭을 짐작할 수 없으나) 이전에 그토록 사려 깊게 지켜 오던 자제심을 모조리 잃고서, 열병이 발진티푸스로 바뀌었을 때 의사가 저지른 오진을 두고 몇 번이고 되풀이해 조롱했습니다. 이 딱한 일은 도슨 씨가 퍼시벌 경에게 호소하며, (이제는 홀콤 양에게 절대적인 위험을 끼치지 않고도 떠날 수 있게 된 만큼) 백작의 간섭을 그 순간부터 단호히 막아 주지 않는다면 블랙워터 파크에서의 왕진을 그만두겠다고 으름장을 놓는 것으로 끝났습니다. 퍼시벌 경의 대답은 (일부러 무례하게 굴려던 것은 아니었겠으나) 사태를 더 악화시키는 결과만 낳았고, 그리하여 도슨 씨는 포스코 백작이 자기를 대한 방식에 극도로 분개하며 이 집을 떠났고, 이튿날 아침 청구서를 보내왔습니다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 이제 의사의 왕진 없이 남겨졌습니다. 실제로 다른 의사가 꼭 필요한 것은 아니었지만, 즉 전문의가 말한 대로 홀콤 양에게 필요한 것은 간호와 보살핌뿐이었지만, 그래도 만약 제 권한을 물어 주었더라면 저는 형식을 갖추기 위해서라도 다른 곳에서 전문가의 도움을 구했을 것입니다.\n\n퍼시벌 경에게는 이 일이 그런 식으로 여겨지지 않는 모양이었습니다. 그는 홀콤 양이 재발 기미를 보이면 그때 다른 의사를 부르러 보내도 늦지 않다고 말했습니다. 그동안에는 사소한 어려움이 생길 때마다 백작에게 상의할 수 있으니, 지금처럼 쇠약하고 예민한 상태에 있는 환자를 낯선 사람이 머리맡에 있게 하여 공연히 어지럽힐 필요가 없다는 것이었습니다. 이런 고려에는 물론 지당한 점이 많았습니다만, 그럼에도 저는 다소 마음이 놓이지 않았습니다. 또한 우리가 그렇게 의사의 부재를 글라이드 부인에게 숨기는 것이 과연 온당한 일인지도 제 마음속으로는 썩 개운치가 않았습니다. 그것이 자비로운 속임이었다는 점은 인정합니다. 부인은 새로운 근심을 감당할 만한 상태가 전혀 아니었으니까요. 그러나 그래도 그것은 속임이었고, 그러한 이상 저와 같은 신조를 지닌 사람에게는 아무리 좋게 보아도 미심쩍은 처사였습니다.\n\n같은 날 일어난 두 번째 곤혹스러운 일은 저를 완전히 허를 찔린 상태로 몰아넣었고, 그때 제 마음을 짓누르고 있던 불안감을 크게 더해 주었습니다.\n\n저는 서재로 퍼시벌 경을 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았습니다. 제가 들어갔을 때 함께 있던 백작은 곧바로 일어나 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 정중하게 저에게 앉으라고 청하더니, 참으로 놀랍게도 저에게 이런 말을 꺼냈습니다.\n\n“마이컬슨 부인, 얼마 전에 내가 마음을 정한 일에 대해 이야기하고 싶소. 진작 말했어야 했는데 집안에 병이 나고 소란이 있어서 미뤄 두었소. 단도직입적으로 말하자면, 나는 지금 당장 이곳의 살림을 정리해야 할 이유가 있소. 물론 관리는 여느 때처럼 당신에게 맡기고 말이오. 글라이드 부인과 홀콤 양이 여행할 수 있게 되는 대로 두 사람 다 요양지를 옮겨야 하오. 내 벗인 포스코 백작 부부는 그 전에 우리를 떠나 런던 근교에서 살 것이오. 그리고 나는 될 수 있는 대로 아껴 쓸 생각이라, 이 집에 손님을 더 들이지 않을 이유가 있소. 당신을 탓하는 건 아니지만, 여기서 나가는 내 비용이 너무나 과하오. 요컨대 나는 말들을 팔아 치우고, 하인들도 당장 전부 내보낼 작정이오. 알다시피 나는 무슨 일이든 어중간하게 하는 법이 없소. 내일 이맘때까지는 쓸모없는 것들을 이 집에서 싹 치워 버릴 생각이오.”\n\n저는 놀라움에 완전히 아연실색한 채로 그 말을 들었습니다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 제가 관리하는 집안 하인들을 통상적인 한 달 전 예고도 없이 내보내라는 말씀이십니까?” 제가 물었습니다.\n\n“그렇소, 물론이오. 우리 모두가 한 달도 되기 전에 이 집을 떠날지도 모르는데, 시중들 주인도 없는 곳에 하인들을 빈둥거리게 놔둘 생각은 없소.”\n\n“퍼시벌 경께서 아직 여기 머무시는 동안 요리는 누가 합니까?”\n\n“마거릿 포처가 굽고 삶을 줄은 알지 않소. 그 애는 남겨 두시오. 만찬회를 열 생각도 없는데 요리사가 무슨 소용이오?”\n\n“말씀하신 그 하인은 이 집에서 가장 아둔한 하인입니다, 퍼시벌 경.”\n\n“남겨 두라고 하지 않았소. 그리고 마을에서 여자를 하나 불러다 청소만 시키고 돌려보내시오. 내 주간 비용은 당장 줄여야 하고, 또 줄일 것이오. 나는 반대 의견을 들으려고 당신을 부른 게 아니오, 마이컬슨 부인. 내 절약 계획을 실행하라고 부른 것이오. 포처만 빼고 게으른 집안 하인들을 내일 몽땅 내보내시오. 그 애는 말처럼 튼튼하니, 말처럼 부려 먹으면 될 것이오.”\n\n“외람되오나 한 말씀 여쭙겠습니다, 퍼시벌 경. 하인들이 내일 나간다면 한 달 전 예고를 대신하여 한 달 치 급료를 주어야 합니다.”\n\n“주라지! 한 달 치 급료면 하인방에서 한 달 동안 축내고 먹어 치울 것을 아끼는 셈이오.”\n\n이 마지막 말은 제 살림 관리에 대한 더없이 모욕적인 종류의 비방을 담고 있었습니다. 저는 그토록 무례한 누명 아래에서 자신을 변호하기에는 자존심이 너무 강했습니다. 홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인의 무력한 처지에 대한, 그리고 제가 갑작스레 자리를 비움으로써 두 분께 끼칠지도 모를 심각한 불편에 대한 그리스도인다운 배려, 오직 그것만이 제가 그 자리에서 일을 그만두는 것을 막아 주었습니다. 저는 곧바로 일어섰습니다. 그 면담을 한순간이라도 더 이어 가도록 내버려 두었다면 제 스스로에 대한 평가가 깎였을 것입니다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 그 마지막 말씀을 들은 이상 저는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 분부하신 대로 처리하겠습니다.” 그 말을 하고서 저는 더없이 서먹한 예의를 갖추어 고개를 숙이고는 방을 나왔습니다.\n\n이튿날 하인들은 한꺼번에 떠났습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 마부들과 마구간 일꾼들을 직접 해고하여, 말 한 필만 남기고 모두 런던으로 보냈습니다. 안팎의 모든 살림꾼 가운데 이제 남은 사람은 저와 마거릿 포처, 그리고 정원사뿐이었습니다. 이 마지막 사람은 자기 오두막에서 지냈고, 마구간에 남은 한 필의 말을 돌보기 위해 필요했습니다.\n\n집이 이렇게 낯설고 쓸쓸한 상태로 남겨지고, 그 안주인은 방에서 앓아누워 있고, 홀콤 양은 여전히 어린아이처럼 무력하고, 의사는 적의를 품고 왕진을 끊어 버린 마당이니, 제 기운이 가라앉고 늘 지녀 오던 평정을 지키기가 몹시 어려워진 것도 분명 이상한 일은 아니었습니다. 제 마음은 편치 않았습니다. 저는 가엾은 두 부인이 모두 다시 건강해지기를 바랐고, 그리고 저 자신은 블랙워터 파크에서 멀리 떠나 있기를 바랐습니다.\n\n# II\n\n다음으로 일어난 일은 어찌나 기이한 성질의 것이었던지, 제 마음이 원칙으로 단단히 무장되어 그런 종류의 이교도적 나약함을 물리치지 못했더라면 저는 미신적인 놀라움을 느꼈을지도 모릅니다. 이 집안에 무언가 잘못되어 가고 있다는 불안한 예감 때문에 저는 블랙워터 파크를 떠났으면 싶다고 바랐었는데, 이상하게도 그 뒤를 이어 실제로 제가 이 집을 떠나는 일이 벌어졌던 것입니다. 물론 제가 자리를 비운 것은 한동안뿐이었습니다만, 그렇다고 해서 그 우연의 일치가 덜 놀라운 것은 아니었다고 저는 생각합니다.\n\n제가 떠나게 된 경위는 다음과 같습니다—\n\n하인들이 모두 나가고 하루나 이틀 뒤에 저는 다시 퍼시벌 경께 불려 갔습니다. 그분이 제 살림 관리에 씌우신 부당한 오명도, 다행히 말씀드리자면, 제가 그분의 청에 여느 때와 다름없이 선뜻 공손히 응함으로써 악을 선으로 갚는 것을 막지는 못했습니다. 제 감정을 억누르기까지는, 우리 모두가 함께 지니고 있는 저 타락한 본성과 한바탕 싸움을 치러야 했습니다. 자기 절제에 익숙한 터라 저는 그 희생을 치러 냈습니다.\n\n가 보니 퍼시벌 경과 포스코 백작이 또다시 함께 앉아 계셨습니다. 이번에는 백작께서 면담 자리에 그대로 남으시어 퍼시벌 경의 뜻을 펼쳐 보이는 일을 거드셨습니다.\n\n두 분이 이번에 제 주의를 청하신 문제는, 홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인이 곧 그 덕을 볼 수 있으리라고 우리 모두가 바라던 요양차의 전지(轉地)에 관한 것이었습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 두 분이 아마도 (프레더릭 페얼리 씨의 초대로) 컴벌랜드의 리머리지 하우스에서 가을을 보내게 되리라고 말씀하셨습니다. 그러나 그리로 가기 전에 먼저 토키의 온화한 기후에서 잠시 지내는 편이 두 분께 이로우리라는 것이 퍼시벌 경의 의견이었고, 포스코 백작(여기서부터 대화를 이어받아 끝까지 이끌어 가셨습니다)께서도 이를 확인해 주셨습니다. 그러니 중요한 것은 그곳에 두 분께 필요한 온갖 편의와 이점을 갖춘 숙소를 얻는 일이었고, 큰 어려움은 그런 거처를 고를 만한 경험 있는 사람을 찾는 일이었습니다. 이 딱한 사정에서 백작께서는 퍼시벌 경을 대신하여, 제가 몸소 토키로 가서 두 분을 위해 힘을 보태 드리는 데 이의가 없겠는지 여쭙고자 한다고 하셨습니다.\n\n저 같은 처지의 사람이 그런 식으로 내놓은 제안을 딱 잘라 거절할 수는 없는 노릇이었습니다.\n\n저는 다만 마거릿 포처 하나를 빼고는 집 안 하인이 모조리 없는 이 유별난 형편에 제가 블랙워터 파크를 비우는 것이 여간 곤란한 일이 아니라는 점을 감히 말씀드릴 수 있었을 뿐입니다. 그러나 퍼시벌 경과 백작께서는 병자들을 위해서라면 두 분 다 기꺼이 불편을 감수하겠다고 하셨습니다. 저는 다음으로 토키의 중개인에게 편지를 보내는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 조심스레 여쭈었으나, 숙소를 직접 보지도 않고 얻는 것이 얼마나 경솔한 일인지 일깨움을 받았을 뿐입니다. 또 백작부인께서는 (그렇지 않았다면 몸소 데번셔로 가셨을 테지만) 글라이드 부인이 지금 같은 상태에서는 조카딸 곁을 떠날 수 없으시고, 퍼시벌 경과 백작께서는 함께 처리해야 할 일이 있어 블랙워터 파크에 남아 계셔야 한다는 말씀도 들었습니다. 요컨대, 제가 그 심부름을 맡지 않으면 달리 그 일을 맡길 만한 사람이 아무도 없다는 것이 분명히 드러났습니다. 이런 사정이니 저로서는 홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인을 위해 제 힘을 쓰겠노라고 퍼시벌 경께 아뢸 수밖에 없었습니다.\n\n그리하여 제가 이튿날 아침에 떠나 토키에서 가장 편리한 집들을 하루 이틀 동안 두루 살펴보고, 되도록 빨리 결과를 보고하러 돌아오는 것으로 정해졌습니다. 제가 얻어야 할 곳이 반드시 갖추어야 할 조건들을 적은 각서를 백작께서 써 주셨고, 퍼시벌 경은 거기에 제게 허락된 금액의 한도를 덧붙여 적으셨습니다.\n\n그 지시서를 읽어 보며 제가 든 생각은, 거기 적힌 것과 같은 거처는 영국의 어느 해변 요양지에서도 찾을 수 없으리라는 것, 설령 요행히 찾아낸다 한들 제게 허락된 조건으로는 결코 얼마 동안이라도 빌려주지 않으리라는 것이었습니다. 저는 두 분께 이런 어려움을 넌지시 말씀드렸습니다만, 퍼시벌 경은(대답을 맡으셨습니다) 그것을 어려움으로 여기시는 것 같지 않았습니다. 제가 따질 일은 아니었습니다. 저는 더 말하지 않았습니다만, 제가 떠맡아 가는 그 일이 어찌나 난관투성이인지 출발부터 거의 가망이 없는 심부름이라는 확신이 아주 강하게 들었습니다.\n\n떠나기 전에 저는 홀콤 양의 차도가 좋은지 반드시 제 눈으로 확인해 두었습니다.\n\n그분의 얼굴에는 괴로운 근심의 빛이 어려 있어서, 정신을 처음 되찾고 나서 마음이 편치 않으신 게 아닌가 하는 걱정이 들었습니다. 그러나 몸은 분명 제가 감히 바랐던 것보다 빠르게 회복되고 계셨고, 글라이드 부인께 다정한 전갈—자신은 빠르게 낫고 있으니 부디 다시 무리하지 마시라는 전갈을 보내실 만큼 기운도 있으셨습니다. 저는 여전히 집안의 누구에게도 조용히 아랑곳하지 않는 루벨 부인에게 그분을 맡기고 떠났습니다. 떠나기 전 글라이드 부인의 방문을 두드렸을 때는 그분이 아직 몹시 쇠약하고 침울하시다는 대답을 들었는데, 그렇게 알려 주신 이는 그때 방에서 마님과 함께 계시던 백작부인이었습니다. 제가 마차를 타고 지나갈 때 퍼시벌 경과 백작은 문간채로 난 길을 걷고 계셨습니다. 저는 두 분께 인사를 드리고 집을 떠났으니, 하인들 처소에는 마거릿 포처 말고는 살아 있는 사람이 하나도 남지 않았습니다.\n\n누구라도 그때 이후로 제가 느껴 온 바를 똑같이 느끼실 것입니다. 이런 정황들은 예사롭지 않은 정도를 넘어—거의 수상쩍기까지 했다는 것을 말입니다. 그러나 다시 한번 말씀드리건대, 남에게 매인 처지의 제가 달리 처신할 도리는 없었습니다.\n\n토키에서 제 심부름의 결과는 제가 내다본 그대로였습니다. 지시받은 것과 같은 숙소는 그 고장 어디에도 없었고, 제게 허락된 조건은 설령 원하는 곳을 찾아냈다 한들 턱없이 낮았습니다. 그리하여 저는 블랙워터 파크로 돌아와, 문간에서 저를 맞으신 퍼시벌 경께 헛걸음을 했다고 아뢰었습니다. 그분은 무언가 다른 일에 골몰해 계신 듯 제 심부름이 실패한 것 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않으셨고, 첫마디부터 제가 잠시 자리를 비운 그 짧은 사이에 집안에 또 한 번 놀라운 변화가 있었음을 알려 주셨습니다.\n\n포스코 백작 내외분이 세인트존스우드의 새 거처로 떠나 블랙워터 파크를 비웠다는 것이었습니다.\n\n그 갑작스러운 출발의 까닭은 제게 알려 주지 않았습니다—다만 백작께서 제게 각별히 안부를 남기고 가셨다는 말만 들었습니다. 백작부인이 안 계신 동안 글라이드 부인의 시중을 들어 드릴 사람이 있느냐고 감히 퍼시벌 경께 여쭈었더니, 마거릿 포처가 시중을 든다고 대답하시고는 아래층 일을 할 여자를 마을에서 부르러 보냈다고 덧붙이셨습니다.\n\n그 대답에 저는 정말이지 충격을 받았습니다—아래 하녀에게 글라이드 부인의 몸종 노릇을 맡기다니 예의에 어긋나도 너무나 어긋난 일이었습니다. 저는 곧바로 위층으로 올라가 침실 층계참에서 마거릿과 마주쳤습니다. (당연한 일이지만) 그 애의 시중은 필요치 않았으니, 마님께서 그날 아침 자리에서 일어나실 만큼 회복되셨기 때문입니다. 다음으로 홀콤 양의 안부를 물었으나, 늘어진 데다 뚱한 대답이 돌아와 저는 전보다 나을 것이 하나도 없었습니다.\n\n저는 그 물음을 되풀이하여 버릇없는 대꾸를 자아내고 싶지는 않았습니다. 제 처지의 사람으로서는 곧장 글라이드 부인의 방으로 가 뵙는 편이 모든 면에서 온당했습니다.\n\n마님께서는 지난 며칠 사이 확실히 건강이 나아지신 것을 알 수 있었습니다. 아직 몹시 쇠약하고 신경이 예민하시긴 했으나 남의 부축 없이 일어나실 수 있었고, 방 안을 천천히 거니셔도 약간 피로한 느낌 말고는 나빠지는 기색이 없으셨습니다. 그날 아침 마님께서는 아무에게서도 홀콤 양의 소식을 듣지 못하신 탓에 조금 불안해하고 계셨습니다. 저는 이것이 루벨 부인 쪽의 나무랄 만한 무성의를 뜻하는 듯싶었으나 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마님 곁에 남아 옷 입으시는 것을 도와 드렸습니다. 채비가 끝나자 우리는 홀콤 양에게 가려고 함께 방을 나섰습니다.\n\n복도에서 우리는 퍼시벌 경이 나타나시는 바람에 걸음을 멈추었습니다. 그분은 마치 우리를 보려고 일부러 거기서 기다리고 계셨던 것 같은 모습이었습니다.\n\n“어디 가는 길이오?” 그분이 글라이드 부인께 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n“매리언의 방에요.” 마님께서 대답하셨습니다.\n\n“실망을 덜어 드리자면,” 퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다. “거기 가도 그 사람은 없다고 지금 일러 두는 편이 낫겠군.”\n\n“거기 없다니요!”\n\n“그렇소. 어제 아침에 포스코 내외와 함께 이 집을 떠났소.”\n\n글라이드 부인은 이 엄청난 말이 안겨 준 충격을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 못하셨습니다. 얼굴이 무섭도록 창백해지며 벽에 등을 기대신 채, 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 남편을 바라보셨습니다.\n\n저 자신도 어찌나 놀랐는지 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐습니다. 저는 퍼시벌 경께 홀콤 양이 정말로 블랙워터 파크를 떠났다는 말씀이시냐고 여쭈었습니다.\n\n“물론 그렇다는 말이오.” 그분이 대답하셨습니다.\n\n“그런 몸으로요, 퍼시벌 경! 글라이드 부인께 뜻을 말씀도 드리지 않고요!”\n\n그분이 대답하시기도 전에 마님께서 조금 정신을 차리시고 입을 여셨습니다.\n\n“그럴 리가 없어요!” 마님께서 벽에서 한두 걸음 앞으로 나서시며 크고 겁에 질린 목소리로 외치셨습니다. “의사 선생님은 어디 계셨나요? 매리언이 떠날 때 도슨 선생님은 어디 계셨어요?”\n\n“도슨 씨는 필요하지도 않았고, 여기 있지도 않았소.” 퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다. “제 발로 그만두고 갔지. 그것만 봐도 그 사람이 여행할 만큼 튼튼했다는 게 드러나는 셈이오. 뭘 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보시오! 갔다는 걸 못 믿겠거든 직접 가서 보구려. 그 방 문을 열어 보고, 원한다면 다른 방문도 죄다 열어 보시오.”\n\n마님은 그 말대로 하셨고 저도 뒤따랐습니다. 홀콤 양의 방에는 방을 치우느라 바쁜 마거릿 포처 말고는 아무도 없었습니다. 이어 들여다본 빈방들과 화장방에도 아무도 없었습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 여전히 복도에서 우리를 기다리고 계셨습니다. 마지막으로 살펴본 방을 나서면서 글라이드 부인께서 속삭이셨습니다. “가지 마세요, 마이컬슨 부인! 제발이지 저를 혼자 두지 마세요!” 제가 무어라 대답하기도 전에 마님은 다시 복도로 나가 남편에게 말을 걸고 계셨습니다.\n\n“이게 무슨 뜻이죠, 퍼시벌 경? 말씀해 주세요—간청드려요, 제발 무슨 뜻인지 말씀해 주세요.”\n\n“뜻인즉,” 그분이 대답하셨습니다. “홀콤 양이 어제 아침에 일어나 앉아 옷을 입을 만큼 튼튼했고, 포스코가 런던에 가는 길을 이용해 자기도 같이 가겠다고 고집을 부렸다는 것이오.”\n\n“런던으로요!”\n\n“그렇소—리머리지로 가는 길에 말이오.”\n\n글라이드 부인께서 돌아서서 제게 호소하셨습니다.\n\n“홀콤 양을 마지막으로 본 건 부인이었죠.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다. “솔직히 말해 주세요, 마이컬슨 부인. 그이가 여행할 만해 보이던가요?”\n\n“제 눈에는 그렇지 않았습니다, 마님.”\n\n이번에는 퍼시벌 경께서 곧바로 저를 향해 돌아서서 역시 제게 물으셨습니다.\n\n“떠나기 전에,” 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “홀콤 양이 훨씬 튼튼하고 좋아 보인다고 간병인에게 말했소, 안 했소?”\n\n“그런 말씀을 드린 것은 사실입니다, 퍼시벌 경.”\n\n제가 그렇게 대답하자마자 그분은 다시 마님을 향해 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n“마이컬슨 부인의 두 가지 의견을 공평하게 견주어 보고,” 그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “이 뻔한 일에 대해 이치에 맞게 생각해 보시오. 옮겨도 될 만큼 몸이 낫지 않았다면 우리 중 누구라도 그 사람을 보내는 모험을 했겠소? 돌봐 줄 사람이 셋이나 유능하게 붙어 있소—포스코와 당신 고모, 그리고 바로 그 일을 위해 일부러 함께 떠난 루벨 부인 말이오. 어제 객차 한 칸을 통째로 잡아서, 피곤해할 때를 대비해 좌석에 잠자리까지 만들어 주었소. 오늘은 포스코와 루벨 부인이 그 사람을 데리고 컴벌랜드까지 갈 거요.”\n\n“매리언은 왜 나를 여기 혼자 두고 리머리지로 가는 거죠?” 마님께서 퍼시벌 경의 말을 끊고 물으셨습니다.\n\n“당신 삼촌이 당신 언니부터 만나 보기 전에는 당신을 받아들이지 않겠다니까 그렇지.” 그분이 대답하셨습니다. “그 사람이 병나던 무렵에 삼촌이 보낸 편지를 잊었소? 당신한테도 보여 줘서 직접 읽었으니 기억해야 마땅할 텐데.”\n\n“기억해요.”\n\n“기억한다면서 왜 그 사람이 당신을 두고 간 걸 놀라워하시오? 당신은 리머리지로 돌아가고 싶어 하고, 그 사람은 삼촌이 내건 조건대로 당신 허락을 받아 내려고 거기 간 거요.”\n\n가엾은 글라이드 부인의 눈에 눈물이 고였습니다.\n\n“매리언은 한 번도 나한테 작별 인사 없이 떠난 적이 없어요.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n“이번에도 작별 인사를 했을 거요.” 퍼시벌 경이 대꾸하셨습니다. “자기 자신과 당신이 두렵지만 않았다면 말이오. 당신이 붙잡으려 들 것을 알았고, 당신이 울어서 자기를 괴롭게 하리라는 것도 알았던 거지. 더 따질 게 있소? 있거든 아래층으로 내려와서 식당에서 묻구려. 이런 성가신 일은 사람 진을 빼는군. 포도주나 한 잔 해야겠소.”\n\n그분은 갑자기 우리를 두고 가 버리셨습니다.\n\n이 이상한 대화가 오가는 내내 그분의 태도는 평소와 아주 딴판이었습니다. 이따금 그분은 마님 못지않게 신경이 곤두서고 안절부절못하시는 듯이 보였습니다. 저는 그분의 건강이 그토록 여리다거나 침착함이 그토록 쉽게 흐트러지리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했습니다.\n\n저는 글라이드 부인을 방으로 돌아가시게 하려 애썼습니다만 소용없었습니다. 마님은 공포에 사로잡힌 여인의 얼굴로 복도에 멈춰 서 계셨습니다.\n\n“언니에게 무슨 일이 생긴 거예요!” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n“마님, 홀콤 양께 얼마나 놀라운 기력이 있는지 헤아려 보십시오.” 제가 여쭈었습니다. “같은 처지의 다른 부인들이라면 엄두도 못 낼 일을 그분은 능히 해내실 수 있습니다. 잘못된 일은 아무것도 없기를 바라고, 또 그렇게 믿습니다—정말입니다.”\n\n“매리언을 따라가야겠어요.” 마님께서 여전히 공포에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 말씀하셨습니다. “언니가 간 곳으로 가야 해요. 언니가 살아서 무사한지 이 눈으로 봐야겠어요. 자! 저와 함께 퍼시벌 경께 내려가 주세요.”\n\n제 존재가 주제넘은 참견으로 여겨질까 저어되어 망설였습니다. 마님께 그 점을 말씀드리려 하였으나 마님은 제 말을 들으려 하지 않으셨습니다. 마님은 제 팔을 어찌나 단단히 붙드셨는지 저는 함께 아래층으로 내려갈 수밖에 없었고, 제가 식당 문을 여는 순간에도 마님은 그때 남아 있던 얼마 안 되는 힘을 다해 제게 매달려 계셨습니다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 포도주 병을 앞에 놓고 탁자에 앉아 계셨습니다. 우리가 들어서자 그분은 잔을 입으로 가져가 단숨에 비우셨습니다. 잔을 내려놓으시며 저를 성난 눈으로 보시기에, 저는 얼떨결에 이 방에 들어온 것에 대해 무어라 변명을 하려 했습니다.\n\n“여기 무슨 비밀이라도 있는 줄 아나?” 그분이 별안간 터뜨리듯 말씀하셨습니다. “그런 건 없어—뒤가 구린 것도 없고, 당신이나 그 누구에게도 숨기는 것 따위 없단 말이야.” 그 이상한 말을 큰 소리로 매섭게 내뱉고 나서, 그분은 포도주를 한 잔 더 채우고는 글라이드 부인께 무슨 볼일이냐고 물으셨습니다.\n\n“언니가 여행할 만하다면 저도 여행할 만합니다.” 마님께서 여태껏 보이신 적 없는 단호함으로 말씀하셨습니다. “매리언에 대한 제 걱정을 헤아려 주시고, 오늘 오후 기차로 곧장 뒤따라가게 해 주십사 부탁드리러 왔어요.”\n\n“내일까지 기다려야 하오.” 퍼시벌 경이 대답하셨습니다. “그때 가서 반대되는 소식이 없거든 가시오. 반대되는 소식이 올 것 같지는 않으니, 오늘 밤 우편으로 포스코에게 편지를 써 두겠소.”\n\n그 마지막 말씀을 하실 때 그분은 잔을 불빛에 비추어 들고, 글라이드 부인이 아니라 그 안의 포도주를 들여다보고 계셨습니다. 실은 그 대화 내내 마님을 단 한 번도 쳐다보지 않으셨습니다. 그만한 지체의 신사에게서 그토록 유별난 예법의 결여를 보는 것은, 고백하건대, 제게 몹시 마음 아픈 일이었습니다.\n\n“어째서 포스코 백작께 편지를 쓰시나요?” 마님께서 몹시 놀라 물으셨습니다.\n\n“당신이 낮 기차로 갈 테니 기다리라고 일러 두려는 거요.” 퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다. “당신이 런던에 닿으면 역으로 마중 나와서, 세인트존스우드에 있는 당신 고모 집으로 데려가 재워 줄 거요.”\n\n글라이드 부인의 손이 제 팔을 감은 채 심하게 떨리기 시작했습니다—어째서인지 저로서는 짐작할 수 없었습니다.\n\n“포스코 백작이 저를 마중 나올 필요는 없어요.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다. “런던에서 자고 가지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.”\n\n“그래야 하오. 컴벌랜드까지 하루 만에 갈 수는 없소. 런던에서 하룻밤 쉬어야 하는데—당신 혼자 여관에 가는 건 내가 원치 않소. 포스코가 당신 삼촌에게, 내려가는 길에 재워 주겠다고 제안했고 삼촌이 그것을 받아들였소. 자! 여기 삼촌이 당신 앞으로 쓴 편지가 있소. 오늘 아침에 올려 보냈어야 했는데 잊었군. 읽어 보고 페얼리 씨가 직접 뭐라고 하는지 보시오.”\n\n글라이드 부인은 잠시 그 편지를 바라보시더니 제 손에 쥐여 주셨습니다.\n\n“읽어 주세요.” 마님께서 힘없이 말씀하셨습니다. “제가 왜 이러는지 모르겠어요. 도무지 혼자서는 읽을 수가 없네요.”\n\n그것은 겨우 네 줄짜리 쪽지였는데, 어찌나 짧고 무심한지 저로서는 적잖이 놀랐습니다. 제 기억이 옳다면 그 안에는 이런 말밖에 없었습니다—\n\n“사랑하는 로라, 아무 때나 오고 싶을 때 오려무나. 오는 길에 네 고모 댁에서 하룻밤 묵어 가고. 매리언이 앓는다니 마음이 아프구나. 사랑을 담아, 프레더릭 페얼리.”\n\n“거기는 가고 싶지 않아요—런던에서 하룻밤 묵고 싶지 않아요.” 마님께서 제가 그 짧은 쪽지를 미처 다 읽기도 전에 다급히 그 말을 터뜨리셨습니다. “포스코 백작께 편지하지 마세요! 제발, 제발 그이에게 편지하지 마세요!”\n\n퍼시벌 경은 술병에서 또 한 잔을 따르시다가 어찌나 서투르게 하셨는지 잔을 넘어뜨려 포도주를 죄다 탁자에 쏟으셨습니다. “눈이 어두워지는 모양이군.” 그분이 이상하게 먹먹한 목소리로 혼잣말을 중얼거리셨습니다. 그러고는 천천히 잔을 다시 세우고 채워서 또다시 단숨에 들이켜셨습니다. 저는 그 표정과 태도로 보아 술기운이 오르는 게 아닌가 걱정되기 시작했습니다.\n\n“제발 포스코 백작께 편지하지 마세요.” 글라이드 부인께서 어느 때보다 간절하게 매달리셨습니다.\n\n“왜 안 된다는 거요, 어디 들어나 봅시다!” 퍼시벌 경이 갑자기 노기를 터뜨리며 소리치시는 바람에 우리 둘 다 깜짝 놀랐습니다. “런던에서 당신 삼촌이 손수 정해 준 곳보다—당신 고모 집보다 더 마땅히 묵을 데가 어디 있소? 마이컬슨 부인에게 물어보시오.”\n\n제안된 그 조처는 의심할 여지 없이 옳고 온당한 것이어서 저로서는 도무지 반대할 도리가 없었습니다. 다른 일에서는 글라이드 부인께 그토록 마음이 쓰였습니다만, 포스코 백작에 대한 마님의 부당한 편견에는 공감할 수 없었습니다. 저는 그런 지체와 신분의 부인이 외국인에 관해서 그토록 딱하리만치 편협한 것을 이제껏 본 적이 없었습니다. 삼촌의 쪽지도, 점점 더해 가는 퍼시벌 경의 조바심도 마님께는 조금도 먹혀들지 않는 듯했습니다. 마님은 여전히 런던에서 하룻밤 묵는 것을 마다하셨고, 여전히 백작께 편지하지 말아 달라고 남편께 애원하셨습니다.\n\n“그만두시오!” 퍼시벌 경이 무례하게 우리에게 등을 돌리며 말씀하셨습니다. “자기한테 뭐가 제일 좋은지 분간할 머리가 없으면 남이 대신 알아줘야 하는 법이오. 이미 정해진 일이고, 그것으로 끝이오. 당신은 그저 홀콤 양이 먼저 한 대로 하기만 하면 되는데——”\n\n“매리언이요?” 마님께서 어리둥절한 태도로 되뇌셨습니다. “매리언이 포스코 백작 집에서 잤다고요!”\n\n“그렇소, 포스코 백작 집에서. 어젯밤 여정을 나누느라 거기서 잤소. 당신도 그 본을 따라 삼촌이 시키는 대로 하시오. 당신 언니가 그랬듯이 내일 밤 포스코 집에서 자고 여정을 나누란 말이오. 내 앞길에 걸림돌을 너무 많이 놓지 마시오! 아예 보내 준 걸 후회하게 만들지 말란 말이오!”\n\n그분은 벌떡 일어나시더니, 열려 있던 유리문을 지나 불쑥 베란다로 나가 버리셨습니다.\n\n“마님, 외람됩니다만,” 제가 속삭였습니다. “퍼시벌 경께서 돌아오실 때까지 여기서 기다리지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 여쭈어도 될까요? 아무래도 술기운이 지나치신 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽습니다.”\n\n마님은 지친 듯 넋 나간 태도로 방을 나서는 데 응하셨습니다.\n\n무사히 위층으로 올라오자마자 저는 마님의 마음을 가라앉혀 드리려고 할 수 있는 것을 다 했습니다. 저는 페얼리 씨가 홀콤 양과 마님께 보낸 편지들이 이번에 취해진 방침을 분명히 승인하고 있으며, 조만간 그리하도록 만들기까지 한다는 점을 일깨워 드렸습니다. 마님도 이에 동의하셨고, 두 편지 모두 삼촌의 별난 성정과 꼭 들어맞는다는 것을 스스로 인정까지 하셨습니다—그러나 홀콤 양에 대한 두려움과 런던의 백작 댁에서 자는 것에 대한 까닭 모를 공포는, 제가 아무리 이런저런 이유를 들어도 여전히 흔들리지 않았습니다. 저는 글라이드 부인이 백작을 나쁘게 보시는 데 대해 이의를 여쭙는 것이 제 도리라고 여겨, 분수에 맞는 조심스러움과 공경을 갖추어 그리 하였습니다.\n\n“마님, 제 주제넘음을 용서해 주십시오.” 저는 마무리 삼아 말씀드렸습니다. “허나 ‘그 열매로 그들을 알리라’ 하지 않았습니까. 홀콤 양께서 앓기 시작하신 바로 그때부터 백작께서 한결같이 베푸신 친절과 한결같은 보살핌은 마땅히 우리의 두터운 믿음과 존경을 받을 만하다고 저는 믿습니다. 백작께서 도슨 씨와 크게 틀어지신 것조차 오로지 홀콤 양을 염려하신 탓이었습니다.”\n\n“틀어지다니, 무슨 일인가요?” 마님께서 갑자기 관심을 보이는 얼굴로 물으셨습니다.\n\n저는 도슨 씨가 왕진을 그만두게 된 딱한 사정을 말씀드렸습니다—퍼시벌 경께서 (제가 보는 앞에서 그러셨듯이) 그 일을 글라이드 부인께 계속 숨기시는 것을 저는 못마땅하게 여겼던 터라 더더욱 선뜻 말씀드렸습니다.\n\n마님께서는 제가 말씀드린 것에 한층 더 동요하고 겁먹은 기색이 역력한 채 벌떡 일어나셨습니다.\n\n“더 나빠요! 생각했던 것보다 더 나빠요!” 마님께서 어리둥절한 걸음으로 방 안을 오가며 말씀하셨습니다. “백작은 도슨 선생님이 매리언의 여행을 결코 허락하지 않으리라는 걸 알았던 거예요—그이를 집에서 내보내려고 일부러 의사 선생님을 모욕한 거라고요.”\n\n“오, 마님! 마님!” 제가 말렸습니다.\n\n“마이컬슨 부인!” 마님께서 격하게 말을 이으셨습니다. “세상의 그 어떤 말로도, 제 언니가 스스로 원해서 그 사람의 손아귀에, 그 사람의 집에 있다고 저를 설득하지는 못할 거예요. 저는 그이가 어찌나 무서운지, 퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하든 삼촌이 무슨 편지를 쓰든, 제 마음만 따르면 되는 일이라면 그 지붕 아래서 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 않을 거예요. 하지만 매리언 걱정에 애가 타는 이 괴로움이, 언니를 어디로든—포스코 백작의 집까지도—따라갈 용기를 줍니다.”\n\n저는 이 대목에서, 퍼시벌 경의 말씀에 따르면 홀콤 양은 이미 컴벌랜드로 떠나셨다는 점을 말씀드리는 것이 옳다고 여겼습니다.\n\n“그 말을 믿기가 두려워요!” 마님께서 대답하셨습니다. “언니가 아직 그 사람 집에 있을까 봐 두려워요. 제 생각이 틀렸다면, 언니가 정말로 리머리지로 갔다면, 저는 내일 밤 포스코 백작의 지붕 아래서 자지 않기로 마음먹었어요. 언니 다음으로 이 세상에서 제일 소중한 벗이 런던 근처에 살아요. 저한테서도, 홀콤 양한테서도 베지 부인 이야기를 들으셨죠? 그분께 편지를 써서 그 댁에서 자게 해 달라고 청할 참이에요. 어떻게 거기까지 갈지도, 어떻게 백작을 피할지도 모르겠지만, 언니가 컴벌랜드로 갔다면 저는 어떻게든 그 피난처로 달아날 거예요. 부인께 부탁드리는 건 오직 하나, 퍼시벌 경의 편지가 포스코 백작께 가는 것만큼이나 확실하게 베지 부인께 보내는 제 편지도 오늘 밤 런던으로 가도록 손수 확인해 주시는 일이에요. 아래층 우편 가방을 믿지 못할 까닭이 제게는 있어요. 제 비밀을 지켜 주시고 이 일을 도와주시겠어요? 아마 이것이 제가 부인께 청하는 마지막 부탁이 될 거예요.”\n\n저는 망설였고, 이 모든 것이 매우 이상하다고 여겼으며, 마님의 정신이 최근의 근심과 고통으로 조금 상하신 게 아닌가 하는 걱정마저 들었습니다. 그러나 저는 결국 제 책임을 무릅쓰고 승낙하고 말았습니다. 그 편지가 낯선 사람 앞으로 된 것이었거나, 소문으로나마 제가 익히 아는 베지 부인 같은 분이 아닌 다른 누구 앞으로 된 것이었다면 저는 거절했을지도 모릅니다. 하느님께 감사드립니다—나중에 벌어진 일을 생각하면—글라이드 부인께서 블랙워터 파크에 머무신 마지막 날에 제게 말씀하신 그 소원을, 아니 그 어떤 소원도 제가 가로막지 않았음을 하느님께 감사드립니다.\n\n편지는 다 쓰여 제 손에 건네졌습니다. 저는 그날 저녁 그 편지를 손수 마을 우체통에 넣었습니다.\n\n그날 남은 시간 동안 우리는 퍼시벌 경을 더는 뵙지 못했습니다.\n\n저는 글라이드 부인께서 손수 청하신 대로, 사이의 문을 열어 둔 채 마님 방 옆방에서 잤습니다. 텅 비어 적막한 이 집에는 어찌나 이상하고 무서운 데가 있는지, 저로서도 곁에 사람이 있는 것이 다행이었습니다. 마님께서는 밤늦도록 앉아 편지들을 읽고는 태우셨고, 서랍과 장에서 아끼시던 자잘한 물건들을 꺼내어 비우셨는데, 마치 다시는 블랙워터 파크로 돌아오지 않으실 작정인 듯했습니다. 마침내 잠자리에 드신 뒤에도 마님의 잠은 몹시 뒤숭숭했습니다—몇 번이나 잠결에 소리를 지르셨고, 한 번은 어찌나 크게 지르셨는지 스스로 놀라 깨어나셨습니다. 어떤 꿈을 꾸셨든 마님은 그것을 제게 알려 주는 것이 마땅하다고 여기지 않으셨습니다. 어쩌면 제 처지에 그것을 기대할 권리는 없었겠지요. 이제 와 대수로운 일은 아닙니다. 저는 마님이 가여웠습니다. 그래도 정말이지 진심으로 가여웠습니다.\n\n이튿날은 맑고 화창했습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사 뒤에 올라오시어, 마차가 열두 시 십오 분 전에 문 앞에 대기할 것이며—런던행 기차가 우리 역에 열두 시 이십 분에 선다고 일러 주셨습니다. 그분은 글라이드 부인께 자신이 외출해야 한다고 알리시면서도, 마님이 떠나시기 전에 돌아오기를 바란다고 덧붙이셨습니다. 뜻밖의 일로 늦어지거든 제가 마님을 모시고 역까지 가서 기차 시간에 늦지 않도록 각별히 살피라고 하셨습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 이 지시를 아주 서둘러 전하셨는데—그러는 내내 방 안 이리저리를 서성이셨습니다. 마님께서는 그분이 어디로 가시든 눈여겨 그 뒤를 좇으셨습니다. 그분은 한 번도 마님을 마주 보지 않으셨습니다.\n\n마님은 그분의 말씀이 다 끝난 뒤에야 입을 여셨고, 그러고는 문 쪽으로 다가가시는 그분을 손을 내밀어 붙드셨습니다.\n\n“이제 다시는 뵙지 못하겠지요.” 마님께서 아주 힘주어 말씀하셨습니다. “이것이 우리의 이별이에요—영영 이별일지도 모르는 이별. 제가 당신을 진심으로 용서하듯이, 당신도 저를 용서해 주시겠어요?”\n\n그분의 얼굴은 온통 소름 끼치도록 하얗게 질렸고, 벗어진 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 돋았습니다. “돌아오겠소.” 그분은 그렇게 말씀하시고, 마치 아내의 작별 인사에 놀라 방에서 쫓겨 나가기라도 하는 듯 황급히 문으로 향하셨습니다.\n\n저는 퍼시벌 경을 좋아한 적이 한 번도 없었습니다만, 그분이 글라이드 부인을 두고 떠나신 그 태도는 그분의 밥을 얻어먹고 그분을 섬기며 살아온 것이 부끄럽게 느껴질 정도였습니다. 저는 가엾은 마님께 위로가 될 그리스도인다운 말씀을 몇 마디 드릴까 하였으나, 문이 남편의 뒤로 닫힌 뒤 그 뒤를 바라보시는 마님의 얼굴에는 무언가가 있어 저는 마음을 바꾸어 잠자코 있었습니다.\n\n정해진 시각에 마차가 대문 앞에 와서 섰습니다. 마님의 말씀이 옳았습니다—퍼시벌 경은 끝내 돌아오지 않으셨습니다. 저는 마지막 순간까지 그분을 기다렸으나 헛일이었습니다.\n\n제 어깨에 뚜렷한 책임이 지워진 것은 아니었습니다만, 그래도 마음이 편치 않았습니다. “마님께서 런던으로 가시는 것은,” 마차가 문간채 대문을 지날 때 제가 여쭈었습니다. “마님의 자유로운 뜻에서 하시는 일이지요?”\n\n“저는 어디든 갈 거예요.” 마님께서 대답하셨습니다. “지금 이 순간 제가 겪고 있는 이 끔찍한 애타는 마음을 끝낼 수만 있다면요.”\n\n마님 때문에 저까지 홀콤 양에 대해 거의 마님만큼이나 걱정스럽고 불안해졌습니다. 저는 런던에서 모든 일이 잘되거든 한 줄 적어 보내 주십사고 감히 청을 드렸습니다. 마님은 “기꺼이 그러지요, 마이컬슨 부인” 하고 대답하셨습니다.\n\n“우리 모두에게는 저마다 져야 할 십자가가 있는 법입니다, 마님.” 편지를 쓰겠노라 약속하신 뒤 마님이 잠자코 생각에 잠기신 것을 보고 제가 말씀드렸습니다.\n\n마님은 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다—당신 생각에 너무 깊이 잠기시어 제 말에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없으신 듯했습니다.\n\n“마님께서 간밤에 편히 쉬지 못하신 듯하여 염려됩니다.” 잠시 기다렸다가 제가 여쭈었습니다.\n\n“네.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다. “꿈 때문에 몹시 시달렸어요.”\n\n“저런, 마님?” 저는 마님이 꿈 이야기를 하시려나 보다 생각했습니다만 그렇지 않았고, 다음에 입을 여셨을 때는 그저 질문을 하나 하셨을 뿐입니다.\n\n“베지 부인께 보내는 편지를 손수 부치셨죠?”\n\n“네, 마님.”\n\n“퍼시벌 경이 어제, 포스코 백작이 런던 종착역으로 저를 마중 나온다고 하셨던가요?”\n\n“그리 말씀하셨습니다, 마님.”\n\n그 마지막 물음에 제가 대답하자 마님은 무겁게 한숨을 쉬시고는 더 말씀이 없으셨습니다.\n\n우리는 겨우 이 분 남짓 남기고 역에 닿았습니다. (마차를 몰아 준) 정원사가 짐을 맡았고 저는 표를 샀습니다. 승강장에서 마님과 합류했을 때는 기차의 기적 소리가 울리고 있었습니다. 마님은 아주 이상한 얼굴빛으로, 그 순간 갑작스러운 통증이나 두려움에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯 손으로 가슴을 누르고 계셨습니다.\n\n“부인이 저와 함께 가 주시면 좋겠어요!” 제가 표를 건네 드리자 마님께서 제 팔을 다급히 붙드시며 말씀하셨습니다.\n\n시간이 있었더라면, 그 전날 제가 그때와 같은 심정이었더라면, 저는 그 자리에서 퍼시벌 경께 일을 그만두겠다고 통고하는 한이 있더라도 마님을 모시고 갈 채비를 했을 것입니다. 그러나 사정이 그러하여, 마지막 순간에야 밝히신 마님의 바람은 제가 따르기에는 너무 늦게 밝혀진 것이었습니다. 마님은 제가 설명해 드리기도 전에 스스로 그것을 헤아리신 듯, 저를 길동무로 삼고 싶다는 말씀을 되풀이하지 않으셨습니다. 기차가 승강장에 와서 섰습니다. 마님은 정원사에게 그 아이들에게 줄 선물을 건네시고, 객차에 오르시기 전에 늘 그러시듯 소탈하고 다정한 태도로 제 손을 잡으셨습니다.\n\n“부인은 저와 제 언니에게 참으로 친절하셨어요.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다—“우리 둘 다 의지할 데 없을 때 베풀어 주신 친절이었지요. 제가 살아서 누군가를 기억하는 한, 부인을 고마운 마음으로 기억할 거예요. 안녕히 계세요—하느님의 축복이 있기를!”\n\n마님께서 그 말씀을 하신 어조와 눈빛에 제 눈에는 눈물이 고였습니다—마치 제게 영영 작별을 고하시는 듯한 어조요 눈빛이었습니다.\n\n“안녕히 가십시오, 마님.” 저는 마님을 객차에 모셔 태우고 기운을 북돋아 드리려 애쓰며 말씀드렸습니다. “안녕히 가십시오, 다만 잠시 동안의 작별입니다. 더 행복한 날들이 오기를 바라는 저의 가장 크고 따뜻한 마음과 함께, 안녕히 가십시오.”\n\n마님은 고개를 저으시고, 객차 안에 자리를 잡으시며 몸을 떠셨습니다. 차장이 문을 닫았습니다. “꿈을 믿으세요?” 마님이 창가에서 제게 속삭이셨습니다. “간밤에 꾼 꿈은 여태 한 번도 꾼 적 없는 꿈이었어요. 그 무서움이 아직도 제 위에 드리워 있어요.” 제가 대답하기도 전에 기적이 울리고 기차가 움직였습니다. 창백하고 고요한 마님의 얼굴이 마지막으로 저를 바라보았습니다—창문 너머로 슬프고도 엄숙하게 바라보았습니다. 마님이 손을 흔드셨고, 저는 그것으로 다시는 마님을 뵙지 못했습니다.\n\n바로 그날 오후 다섯 시 무렵, 이제 제게 몰려든 집안일 가운데서 잠시 짬이 나기에 저는 제 방에 홀로 앉아 남편의 설교집으로 마음을 가라앉혀 보려 하였습니다. 난생처음으로 저는 그 경건하고 힘이 되는 말씀들 위에서 정신이 딴 데로 흩어지는 것을 느꼈습니다. 글라이드 부인의 출발이 제가 스스로 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심하게 저를 어지럽혔던 모양이라고 여기며, 저는 책을 밀어 두고 정원을 한 바퀴 거닐러 나갔습니다. 제가 아는 한 퍼시벌 경은 아직 돌아오지 않으셨으므로 뜰에 모습을 드러내는 데 거리낄 것이 없었습니다.\n\n집 모퉁이를 돌아 정원이 눈에 들어왔을 때, 나는 낯선 사람이 그 안을 거닐고 있는 것을 보고 흠칫 놀랐습니다. 낯선 사람은 여자였습니다. 내게 등을 돌린 채 오솔길을 따라 어슬렁거리며 꽃을 꺾고 있었습니다.\n\n내가 다가가자 그 여자는 인기척을 듣고 몸을 돌렸습니다.\n\n피가 얼어붙는 것 같았습니다. 정원에 있던 그 낯선 여자는 루벨 부인이었습니다!\n\n나는 움직일 수도 말할 수도 없었습니다. 그 여자는 손에 꽃을 든 채 여느 때와 다름없이 태연한 걸음으로 내게 다가왔습니다.\n\n“무슨 일이신가요, 마님?” 그 여자가 조용히 말했습니다.\n\n“당신이 여기에!” 나는 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했습니다. “런던에 가지 않았군요! 컴벌랜드에도 가지 않았고요!”\n\n루벨 부인은 짓궂은 연민의 미소를 띤 채 손에 든 꽃 냄새를 맡았습니다.\n\n“물론 가지 않았지요.” 그 여자가 말했습니다. “저는 블랙워터 파크를 한 번도 떠난 적이 없습니다.”\n\n나는 또 하나의 질문을 던질 만한 숨과 용기를 겨우 끌어모았습니다.\n\n“홀콤 양은 어디 계십니까?”\n\n이번에는 루벨 부인이 대놓고 나를 비웃으며 이런 말로 대답했습니다—\n\n“홀콤 양도 블랙워터 파크를 떠나지 않으셨습니다, 마님.”\n\n그 어처구니없는 대답을 들은 순간, 내 모든 생각은 글라이드 부인과 헤어지던 장면으로 화들짝 되돌아갔습니다. 스스로를 나무랐다고까지는 말하기 어렵지만, 그 순간 나는 지금 알게 된 것을 네 시간만 일찍 알 수 있었더라면 여러 해 애써 모은 돈이라도 기꺼이 내놓았으리라고 생각합니다.\n\n루벨 부인은 내가 무슨 말이라도 하기를 기다리는 듯, 조용히 꽃다발을 매만지며 서 있었습니다.\n\n나는 아무 말도 할 수 없었습니다. 글라이드 부인의 소진된 기력과 쇠약한 건강을 떠올리며, 내가 알아낸 이 사실의 충격이 그분에게 닥칠 때를 생각하니 몸이 떨렸습니다. 한참 동안 그 딱한 두 분에 대한 걱정이 내 입을 막았습니다. 그러다 루벨 부인이 꽃에서 눈을 들어 곁눈질하며 말했습니다. “퍼시벌 경께서 승마를 마치고 돌아오시는군요, 마님.”\n\n나도 그 여자와 동시에 그분을 보았습니다. 그분은 승마용 채찍으로 꽃들을 사납게 후려치면서 우리 쪽으로 다가왔습니다. 내 얼굴이 보일 만큼 가까워지자 그분은 걸음을 멈추고 채찍으로 자기 장화를 친 다음, 어찌나 거칠고 요란하게 웃음을 터뜨렸던지 곁에 선 나무에서 새들이 놀라 날아가 버렸습니다.\n\n“허, 마이컬슨 부인,” 그분이 말했습니다. “드디어 알아냈군그래, 그렇지?”\n\n나는 대답하지 않았습니다. 그분은 루벨 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다.\n\n“언제부터 정원에 모습을 드러냈나?”\n\n“반 시간쯤 전부터입니다, 나리. 글라이드 부인께서 런던으로 떠나시는 대로 다시 자유롭게 다녀도 좋다고 하셨지요.”\n\n“옳은 말이야. 탓하는 게 아니라 그저 물어본 것뿐이니.” 그분은 잠시 뜸을 들이더니 다시 내게 말을 걸었습니다. “못 믿겠지, 안 그런가?” 그분이 비웃듯 말했습니다. “자! 따라와서 직접 눈으로 보게.”\n\n그분은 앞장서서 집 정면 쪽으로 돌아갔습니다. 나는 그분을 따라갔고, 루벨 부인이 내 뒤를 따랐습니다. 쇠문을 지나자 그분은 걸음을 멈추고 채찍으로 건물 가운데의 폐쇄된 별채를 가리켰습니다.\n\n“저기!” 그분이 말했습니다. “이층을 올려다봐. 그 옛 엘리자베스 시대 침실들을 알지? 홀콤 양은 지금 그중에서도 제일 좋은 방에 편안하고 안전하게 있어. 데리고 들어가게, 루벨 부인. 열쇠는 가지고 있지? 마이컬슨 부인을 안으로 데려가서, 이번만은 속임수가 없다는 걸 제 눈으로 확인하게 해 주라고.”\n\n그분이 내게 말한 말투와, 정원을 떠난 뒤 흐른 일이 분의 시간이 내 정신을 조금이나마 되찾게 해 주었습니다. 내 평생이 남의집살이로만 채워졌더라면 이 중대한 순간에 내가 어떻게 처신했을지 알 수 없습니다. 그러나 나는 숙녀로서의 감정과 신조와 교양을 지닌 사람이었기에, 마땅히 취해야 할 길을 두고 망설일 수 없었습니다. 나 자신에 대한 의무도, 글라이드 부인에 대한 의무도, 흉악한 거짓말을 거듭하여 우리 두 사람을 부끄럽게도 속인 사람 밑에 더 머무는 것을 한결같이 금하고 있었습니다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 잠시 은밀히 몇 말씀 여쭐 것을 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.” 내가 말했습니다. “말씀을 드리고 나면 이 사람과 함께 홀콤 양의 방으로 갈 준비가 되겠습니다.”\n\n내가 고개를 살짝 돌려 가리킨 루벨 부인은 무례하게 꽃다발 냄새를 킁 맡더니, 아주 느긋한 걸음으로 현관 쪽으로 걸어갔습니다.\n\n“그래,” 퍼시벌 경이 퉁명스럽게 말했습니다. “이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?”\n\n“나리, 제가 지금 블랙워터 파크에서 맡고 있는 자리를 그만두고자 한다는 말씀을 드리려 합니다.” 나는 글자 그대로 이렇게 말했습니다. 그분 앞에서 꺼내는 첫마디는 그분의 일자리를 떠나겠다는 뜻을 담은 말이어야 한다고 나는 마음을 굳히고 있었습니다.\n\n그분은 더없이 험악한 눈빛으로 나를 노려보며 승마복 주머니에 두 손을 거칠게 찔러 넣었습니다.\n\n“어째서?” 그분이 말했습니다. “어째서인지 좀 알아야겠는데?”\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 이 집에서 일어난 일에 대해 제가 의견을 말씀드릴 처지는 아닙니다. 저는 심기를 거스를 뜻이 없습니다. 다만 글라이드 부인과 저 자신에 대한 의무에 비추어, 더 이상 나리를 모시는 것이 옳지 않다고 여겨진다는 말씀을 드리고 싶을 뿐입니다.”\n\n“거기 서서 내 면전에 의심을 던지는 것은 나에 대한 자네 의무에 맞는 일인가?” 그분은 더없이 사나운 태도로 쏘아붙였습니다. “자네 속셈이 뭔지 알겠어. 글라이드 부인 자신을 위해 베푼 죄 없는 속임수를 두고 자네 혼자 비열하고 음흉한 해석을 내린 게지. 그분 건강에는 당장 공기를 바꾸는 것이 무엇보다 필요했고, 홀콤 양이 아직 여기 남아 있다는 말을 들었다면 그분이 결코 떠나지 않았으리라는 건 자네도 나만큼 잘 알잖나. 그분은 그분 자신의 이익을 위해 속은 거야. 누가 알든 나는 상관없어. 가고 싶으면 가게. 자네만 한 가정부야 부르기만 하면 얼마든지 구할 수 있으니. 언제든 마음대로 가라고. 다만 내 밑을 떠난 뒤에 나와 내 일에 대해 소문을 퍼뜨릴 때는 조심하는 게 좋을 거야. 진실을, 오직 진실만을 말하게. 안 그러면 자네에게 더 나쁠 테니! 홀콤 양을 직접 보게. 집의 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 똑같이 잘 보살핌을 받았는지 아닌지 말이야. 글라이드 부인은 되도록 빨리 공기를 바꾸어야 한다던 의사 자신의 지시도 잊지 말고. 그것들을 잘 새겨 두고, 그러고도 배짱이 있거든 나와 내 처사에 대해 무슨 말이든 해 보라고!”\n\n그분은 이 말들을 사납게, 단숨에 쏟아 내면서 앞뒤로 오가며 허공에 채찍을 휘둘러 댔습니다.\n\n그분이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든, 그 전날 내 앞에서 늘어놓은 부끄러운 거짓말들에 대한, 또 글라이드 부인을 언니와 떼어 놓고 홀콤 양 걱정으로 반쯤 넋이 나간 그분을 부질없이 런던으로 보낸 그 잔인한 속임수에 대한 내 판단은 조금도 흔들리지 않았습니다. 물론 나는 이런 생각을 마음에 담아 두고, 그분을 자극할 말은 더 하지 않았습니다. 그러나 내 뜻을 끝까지 밀고 나가겠다는 결심은 그만큼 더 굳었습니다. 부드러운 대답은 노여움을 물리치는 법이니, 내가 대답할 차례가 되었을 때 나는 그에 따라 내 감정을 눌렀습니다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 제가 나리를 모시는 동안에는,” 내가 말했습니다. “나리의 뜻을 캐묻지 않을 만큼의 도리는 안다고 생각합니다. 나리를 모시지 않게 된 뒤에는, 저와 상관없는 일을 입에 올리지 않을 만큼 제 분수는 안다고 생각합니다—”\n\n“언제 나가고 싶은가?” 그분이 예의도 없이 내 말을 끊으며 물었습니다. “내가 자네를 붙들고 싶어 한다고는 생각지 말게. 자네가 이 집을 떠나든 말든 내가 마음 쓴다고도 생각지 말고. 나는 이 일에 처음부터 끝까지 아주 공정하고 떳떳하니까. 언제 나가고 싶으냐고?”\n\n“나리께서 편하신 대로 되도록 이른 때에 떠났으면 합니다, 퍼시벌 경.”\n\n“내가 편하고 말고는 이 일과 상관없어. 나는 내일 아침이면 이 집을 영영 떠날 테고, 자네 셈은 오늘 밤에 치러 줄 수 있어. 누구 사정을 헤아리겠다면 차라리 홀콤 양 사정을 헤아리는 게 낫겠지. 루벨 부인은 오늘로 기한이 다 됐고, 오늘 밤 안에 런던에 가 있어야 할 사정이 있다더군. 자네가 당장 나가 버리면 홀콤 양을 돌볼 사람이 이 집에 하나도 남지 않을 거야.”\n\n글라이드 부인과 홀콤 양에게 닥친 이런 위급한 상황에서 내가 홀콤 양을 저버릴 수는 도저히 없었다는 말은 굳이 하지 않아도 되리라 생각합니다. 내가 그 자리를 대신 맡으면 루벨 부인이 틀림없이 곧바로 떠난다는 것을 퍼시벌 경에게서 분명히 확인하고, 또 도슨 의사가 환자를 다시 진료하도록 주선해도 좋다는 허락까지 받은 뒤에, 나는 홀콤 양이 더는 내 도움을 필요로 하지 않을 때까지 블랙워터 파크에 남기로 기꺼이 승낙했습니다. 떠나기 일주일 전에 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사에게 통고하고, 후임을 정하는 데 필요한 절차는 그 변호사가 맡기로 정해졌습니다. 이야기는 아주 짧은 말로 끝났습니다. 이야기가 끝나자 퍼시벌 경은 불쑥 발길을 돌렸고, 나는 루벨 부인에게로 갈 수 있게 되었습니다. 그 유별난 외국 여자는 그동안 내내 현관 층계에 태연히 앉아, 내가 자기를 따라 홀콤 양의 방으로 갈 수 있게 되기를 기다리고 있었습니다.\n\n내가 집 쪽으로 절반도 채 걸어가지 못했을 때, 반대 방향으로 물러가던 퍼시벌 경이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 나를 다시 불렀습니다.\n\n“자네는 왜 내 밑을 떠나는 건가?” 그분이 물었습니다.\n\n방금 우리 사이에 오간 일을 생각하면 그 질문은 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지, 나는 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없었습니다.\n\n“알아 두게! 나는 자네가 왜 나가는지 모르네.” 그분이 말을 이었습니다. “다른 자리를 구하려면 나를 떠나는 이유를 대야 할 것 아닌가. 무슨 이유로? 이 집안이 흩어지게 되어서? 그거면 되겠나?”\n\n“퍼시벌 경, 그 이유라면 딱히 반대할 까닭이 없겠습니다——”\n\n“좋아! 내가 알고 싶은 건 그것뿐이야. 사람들이 자네 신원을 물어 오면 그게 자네가 스스로 밝힌 이유가 되는 거야. 자네는 집안이 흩어지게 되어 나가는 거지.”\n\n그분은 내가 한마디 더 하기도 전에 다시 몸을 돌려 빠른 걸음으로 뜰 쪽으로 나가 버렸습니다. 그분의 태도는 그 말만큼이나 이상했습니다. 솔직히 말해 나는 겁이 났습니다.\n\n내가 현관에서 루벨 부인과 만났을 때, 그 여자는 참을성마저 바닥이 난 모양이었습니다.\n\n“이제야 오셨군요!” 그 여자가 마르고 외국인다운 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했습니다. 그러고는 앞장서서 사람이 사는 쪽 집으로 들어가 층계를 올라간 다음, 복도 끝에서 옛 엘리자베스 시대 방들과 통하는 문을 자기 열쇠로 열었습니다. 내가 이 집에 있는 동안 한 번도 쓰인 적이 없는 문이었습니다. 방들 자체는 나도 잘 알고 있었습니다. 집의 반대쪽에서 여러 차례 들어가 본 적이 있었으니까요. 루벨 부인은 그 낡은 회랑을 따라 세 번째 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추더니, 그 방 열쇠와 통용문 열쇠를 내게 건네주며 홀콤 양이 그 방에 계실 것이라고 말했습니다. 나는 안으로 들어가기 전에, 그 여자의 간병이 끝났다는 것을 분명히 알려 두는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했습니다. 그래서 이제부터는 병드신 부인을 돌보는 일이 온전히 내 몫이 되었다고 딱 잘라 말했습니다.\n\n“그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다, 마님.” 루벨 부인이 말했습니다. “저는 몹시 가고 싶었거든요.”\n\n“오늘 떠나는 겁니까?” 나는 확실히 해 두려고 물었습니다.\n\n“마님께서 맡으셨으니 저는 반 시간 뒤에 떠납니다. 퍼시벌 경께서 친절하게도 필요할 때면 언제든 정원사와 이륜마차를 쓰도록 해 주셨지요. 반 시간 뒤에 역으로 가는 데 쓸 참입니다. 짐은 미리 다 꾸려 두었습니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 마님.”\n\n그 여자는 날렵하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 손에 든 꽃다발로 흥겹게 박자를 맞추면서 회랑을 되짚어 걸어갔습니다. 그것이 내가 루벨 부인을 본 마지막이었다는 것을 진심으로 감사히 여깁니다.\n\n내가 방에 들어갔을 때 홀콤 양은 잠들어 있었습니다. 나는 음침하고 높다란 구식 침대에 누워 있는 그분을 걱정스레 바라보았습니다. 지난번에 뵈었을 때보다 어느 모로도 나빠지신 것 같지는 않았습니다. 내가 알아볼 수 있는 한 어떤 식으로도 방치되지는 않았다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없습니다. 방은 스산하고 먼지가 앉았으며 어두웠지만, 집 뒤편의 인적 없는 안뜰을 향한 창문은 신선한 공기가 들어오도록 열려 있었고, 그곳을 편하게 만들기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 되어 있었습니다. 퍼시벌 경의 속임수가 지닌 잔인함은 온전히 가엾은 글라이드 부인에게만 떨어졌던 것입니다. 그분이나 루벨 부인이 홀콤 양에게 저지른 유일한 학대는, 내가 보기에는 그분을 숨겨 놓았다는 처음의 잘못뿐이었습니다.\n\n나는 병드신 분이 여전히 평온하게 잠들어 있는 것을 두고 살며시 물러 나와, 정원사에게 의사를 모셔 오는 일에 대해 일렀습니다. 나는 그 사람에게, 루벨 부인을 역에 데려다준 뒤 도슨 의사 댁에 들러 내 이름으로 말을 전하여 와서 나를 만나 달라고 청해 달라고 부탁했습니다. 나를 봐서라도 그분이 와 주시리라는 것을, 또 포스코 백작이 이 집을 떠났다는 것을 알면 계속 머물러 주시리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었습니다.\n\n이윽고 정원사가 돌아와서, 루벨 부인을 역에 내려 준 뒤 도슨 의사 댁에 들렀다고 말했습니다. 의사는 자기도 몸이 좋지 않지만 될 수 있으면 이튿날 아침에 들르겠다는 전갈을 보내 왔습니다.\n\n전갈을 전한 정원사가 물러가려 하자 나는 그를 붙들고, 어두워지기 전에 돌아와 그날 밤은 빈 침실 하나에서 지새우며 내가 부르면 들릴 곳에 있어 달라고 청했습니다. 그 황량한 집에서도 가장 황량한 곳에 밤새 홀로 남기가 꺼려진다는 내 마음을 그는 어렵지 않게 알아들었고, 우리는 그가 여덟 시에서 아홉 시 사이에 오기로 정했습니다.\n\n그는 시간을 어기지 않고 왔고, 나는 그를 부르는 조심을 해 둔 것을 다행으로 여길 까닭이 있었습니다. 자정이 되기 전에 퍼시벌 경의 이상한 성미가 더없이 사납고 놀라운 모습으로 터져 나왔으니, 만일 정원사가 그 자리에서 곧바로 그분을 달래지 못했더라면 무슨 일이 일어났을지 생각하기도 두렵습니다.\n\n그날 오후와 저녁 내내 그분은 안절부절못하고 흥분한 모습으로 집 안과 뜰을 돌아다녔는데, 아마도 혼자 드신 저녁 식사에서 술을 지나치게 많이 드신 탓이었으리라고 나는 생각했습니다. 어쨌든 나는 밤늦게 회랑을 앞뒤로 오가고 있다가, 새 별채 쪽에서 그분이 크고 성난 목소리로 고함치는 것을 들었습니다. 정원사가 곧장 아래로 달려 내려갔고, 나는 그 소란이 홀콤 양의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 통용문을 닫았습니다. 정원사가 돌아오기까지는 꼬박 반 시간이 걸렸습니다. 그는 주인 나리가 완전히 제정신이 아니라고 했습니다. 내가 짐작한 대로 술기운 때문이 아니라, 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 일종의 공황이랄까 광란 때문이라는 것이었습니다. 그가 갔을 때 퍼시벌 경은 홀 안을 혼자 앞뒤로 오가며, 제 집 같은 감옥에 단 일 분도 더 혼자 머물지 않겠다고, 한밤중이라도 당장 여정의 첫 구간을 떠나겠다고 더없이 격렬한 기색으로 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었습니다. 정원사가 다가가자 그분은 욕설과 위협으로 그를 몰아세워 당장 말과 이륜마차를 준비하게 했습니다. 십오 분 만에 퍼시벌 경은 마당으로 나와 마차에 뛰어올랐고, 말에 채찍질을 하여 질주시키며 달빛 아래 잿빛처럼 창백한 얼굴로 손수 마차를 몰아 떠나갔습니다. 정원사는 그분이 문지기에게 일어나 문을 열라고 고함치며 저주를 퍼붓는 소리를 들었고, 문이 열리자 고요한 밤 속으로 바퀴 소리가 다시 미친 듯이 굴러가는 것을 들었을 뿐, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 알지 못했습니다.\n\n이튿날인지 하루 이틀 뒤인지 기억이 나지 않지만, 가장 가까운 읍인 놀즈베리에서 그곳 오래된 여관의 마부가 이륜마차를 돌려보내 왔습니다. 퍼시벌 경은 거기에 들렀다가 기차로 떠났다는데, 어디로 갔는지는 그 사람도 알지 못했습니다. 그 뒤로 나는 퍼시벌 경의 행적에 대해 그분에게서든 다른 누구에게서든 아무런 소식도 듣지 못했고, 지금 이 순간에도 그분이 영국에 계신지 아닌지조차 알지 못합니다. 그분이 마치 달아나는 죄인처럼 제 집에서 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤로 우리는 만난 적이 없으며, 다시는 만나는 일이 없기를 나는 간절히 바라고 기도합니다.\n\n이 슬픈 집안 이야기에서 내가 맡은 몫도 이제 끝에 다다르고 있습니다.\n\n홀콤 양이 깨어난 정황이며, 내가 침대 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 그분이 보았을 때 우리 사이에 오간 이야기는 이 기록이 이루려는 목적에 그리 중요하지 않다는 말을 나는 들었습니다. 여기서는 다만 이 말만 해 두면 충분하겠습니다. 그분 자신은 사람이 사는 쪽에서 사람이 살지 않는 쪽으로 옮겨진 방법을 의식하지 못했습니다. 그때 그분은 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 그것이 저절로 든 잠이었는지 약으로 만들어진 잠이었는지는 말할 수 없다고 했습니다. 내가 토키에 가 있고, 마거릿 포처를 뺀 하인들이 모두 자리를 비운 사이에 홀콤 양을 집의 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 몰래 옮기는 일은 틀림없이 손쉬웠을 것입니다. 그 마거릿 포처라는 여자는 일하지 않을 때면 늘 먹거나 마시거나 자고 있는 사람이었지요. 루벨 부인에게는, 내가 그 방을 둘러보다가 스스로 알아낸 바로는, 병드신 분과 함께 갇혀 지낸 며칠 동안 먹을 것과 그 밖의 온갖 필요한 물건이며, 불을 피우지 않고도 물이나 고깃국 따위를 데울 수 있는 도구까지 마련되어 있었습니다. 그 여자는 홀콤 양이 당연히 던졌을 질문에는 대답하기를 거부했지만, 다른 점에서는 그분을 매정하게 대하거나 소홀히 하지 않았습니다. 비열한 속임수에 몸을 빌려주었다는 것, 그것만이 내가 양심에 따라 루벨 부인에게 물을 수 있는 유일한 허물입니다.\n\n글라이드 부인이 떠났다는 소식이, 또 그 뒤 너무나 빨리 블랙워터 파크에 닿은 훨씬 더 슬픈 기별이 홀콤 양에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 자세히 적지 않아도 된다고 하니, 그 사실을 알게 되어 마음이 놓입니다. 두 경우 모두 나는 될 수 있는 대로 부드럽고 조심스럽게 그분의 마음을 미리 준비시켰는데, 의사의 조언을 받아 그리한 것은 나중 경우뿐이었습니다. 내가 사람을 보낸 뒤로도 도슨 의사가 며칠 동안 몸이 좋지 않아 이 집에 오지 못했기 때문입니다. 슬픈 시절이었고, 지금 떠올리거나 적기만 해도 마음이 아픈 시절이었습니다. 내가 전하려 애쓴 신앙의 귀한 위로가 홀콤 양의 마음에 닿기까지는 오래 걸렸지만, 마침내는 그분의 마음에 가닿았으리라고 나는 바라고 또 믿습니다. 나는 그분의 기력이 회복될 때까지 곁을 떠나지 않았습니다. 나를 그 비참한 집에서 데려다준 기차는 그분을 데려다준 기차이기도 했습니다. 우리는 런던에서 몹시 서글프게 헤어졌습니다. 나는 이즐링턴에 있는 친척 집에 머물렀고, 그분은 컴벌랜드의 페얼리 씨 댁으로 갔습니다.\n\n이 괴로운 진술을 마치기까지 몇 줄만 더 쓰면 됩니다. 그것은 의무감에서 나온 말입니다.\n\n첫째로, 지금까지 이야기한 일들과 관련하여 포스코 백작에게는 어떤 허물도 없다는 것이 내 개인적인 확신임을 기록해 두고 싶습니다. 무서운 의심이 일고 있으며 백작의 처신을 두고 매우 심각한 해석들이 나오고 있다는 말을 나는 들었습니다. 그러나 백작의 결백에 대한 내 믿음은 조금도 흔들리지 않습니다. 그분이 나를 토키로 보내는 데 퍼시벌 경을 도왔다 하더라도, 그것은 그릇된 믿음 아래에서 도운 것이며, 외국인이자 손님인 그분에게 그 책임을 물을 수는 없습니다. 그분이 루벨 부인을 블랙워터 파크에 데려오는 일에 관계했다 하더라도, 그 외국 여자가 이 집 주인이 꾸미고 저지른 속임수를 도울 만큼 비열했던 것은 그분의 잘못이 아니라 불운이었습니다. 나는 도덕을 위하여, 백작의 처신에 까닭 없이 함부로 허물이 씌워지는 데 항의합니다.\n\n둘째로, 글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 향한 정확한 날짜를 기억해 내지 못하는 내 무능을 유감스럽게 여긴다는 뜻을 밝히고 싶습니다. 그 애달픈 여행의 정확한 날짜를 확인하는 일이 무엇보다 중요하다는 말을 듣고, 나는 그것을 떠올리려고 애타게 기억을 쥐어짰습니다. 그러나 헛수고였습니다. 지금 기억나는 것이라고는 그것이 칠월 하순 무렵이었다는 것뿐입니다. 미리 적어 두지 않은 지난 날짜를 세월이 흐른 뒤에 정확히 짚어 내기가 얼마나 어려운지는 우리 모두 알고 있습니다. 게다가 내 경우에는 글라이드 부인이 떠날 무렵에 일어난 놀랍고 어지러운 일들 때문에 그 어려움이 한층 더 커졌습니다. 그때 적어 두지 못한 것이 진심으로 아쉽습니다. 마차 창문에서 마지막으로 나를 서글피 바라보던 그 가엾은 분의 얼굴이 내 기억에 남은 것만큼, 그 날짜에 대한 기억도 또렷했더라면 하고 진심으로 바랍니다.\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED IN SEVERAL NARRATIVES\n\n1. 포스코 백작 댁에서 요리사로 일한 헤스터 핀혼의 진술\n\n[본인의 진술을 그대로 받아 적음]\n\n죄송한 말씀입니다만 저는 글을 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 배우지 못했습니다. 평생 몸으로 일해 온 여자이고, 품행만은 나무랄 데 없이 지켜 왔습니다. 없는 일을 있다고 말하는 것은 죄요 사악한 짓이라는 걸 알고 있으니, 이번 일에서도 참말이지 그런 짓은 삼가겠습니다. 제가 아는 것은 다 말씀드리겠습니다. 그리고 이걸 받아 적으시는 나리께서 적어 나가시면서 제 말투를 바로잡아 주시고, 제가 배운 것 없는 사람인 걸 너그러이 봐주시기를 삼가 부탁드립니다.\n\n지난여름에 저는 제 잘못이라고는 없이 마침 일자리가 없었는데, 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지에 보통 요리사 자리가 있다는 말을 들었습니다. 저는 시험 삼아 그 자리에 들어갔습니다. 주인 나리 성함은 포스코였습니다. 마님은 영국 부인이셨고요. 나리는 백작이고 마님은 백작부인이었습니다. 제가 갔을 때 하녀 일을 하는 아이가 하나 있었습니다. 그리 깔끔하거나 야무지진 않았지만 나쁜 아이는 아니었습니다. 그 집 하인이라고는 저와 그 아이뿐이었습니다.\n\n나리와 마님은 저희가 들어간 뒤에 오셨습니다. 그리고 오시자마자 시골에서 손님이 오실 거라는 말이 아래층에 전해졌습니다.\n\n손님은 마님의 조카딸이었고, 이층 뒤쪽 침실이 그분을 위해 준비되었습니다. 마님은 저에게 글라이드 부인, 그게 그분 성함이었습니다만, 그분 몸이 편찮으시니 그에 맞게 음식에 각별히 신경을 쓰라고 이르셨습니다. 제 기억으로는 그분이 그날 오셨습니다만, 무슨 일이 있어도 날짜에 관해서는 제 기억을 믿지 마십시오. 죄송하지만 저한테 며칟날이니 하는 걸 물어 보셔야 소용없습니다. 주일날 말고는 저는 태반 날짜에 마음을 두지 않고 삽니다. 몸으로 일하는 여자인 데다 배운 게 없어서요. 제가 아는 것은 글라이드 부인이 오셨다는 것, 그리고 오셨을 때 저희 모두를 참말이지 몹시 놀라게 하셨다는 것뿐입니다. 그때 저는 한창 일하는 중이어서 나리가 그분을 어떻게 집에 모셔 왔는지는 모릅니다. 하지만 오후에 모셔 온 건 맞을 겁니다. 하녀 아이가 문을 열어 드리고 응접실로 안내했지요. 그 아이가 부엌으로 내려와 저와 함께 있은 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 위층에서 우당탕거리는 소리와 응접실 종이 미친 듯이 울리는 소리, 그리고 마님이 사람을 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다.\n\n저희 둘은 위로 뛰어올라갔고, 거기서 그 부인이 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다. 얼굴은 소름 끼치게 하얗고, 두 손은 꽉 움켜쥐었으며, 고개는 한쪽으로 꺾여 있었습니다. 갑자기 놀라셔서 그리 되었다고 마님은 말씀하셨고, 나리는 저희에게 그분이 경련을 일으켰다고 하셨습니다. 저는 다른 사람들보다 그 동네를 조금 더 잘 알았기에, 가장 가까운 의사를 부르러 뛰어나갔습니다. 가장 가까운 데가 구드리크 앤드 가스였는데, 동업으로 함께 일하는 이들이고 제가 듣기로 세인트존스우드 일대에서 평판도 좋고 손님도 많았습니다. 구드리크 선생이 계셔서 곧바로 저와 함께 와 주셨습니다.\n\n선생이 큰 도움이 되기까지는 한참이 걸렸습니다. 그 가엾은 부인은 발작이 끝나기 무섭게 또 발작을 일으켰고, 그렇게 계속하다가 아주 기진맥진해져서 갓난아기처럼 힘없이 늘어지셨습니다. 그제야 저희는 그분을 침대에 뉘었습니다. 구드리크 선생은 약을 가지러 댁에 갔다가 십오 분도 안 되어 돌아오셨습니다. 약 말고도 나팔 비슷하게 생긴 속 빈 마호가니 나무토막을 하나 가져오셨는데, 잠시 기다렸다가 한쪽 끝을 부인의 심장에 대고 다른 쪽 끝을 자기 귀에 대고는 주의 깊게 들으셨습니다.\n\n다 듣고 나서 선생은 방에 있던 마님께 이렇게 말했습니다. “이건 아주 심각한 경우입니다. 글라이드 부인의 가족에게 곧바로 편지를 쓰시기를 권합니다.” 마님이 선생께 “심장병인가요?” 하고 물으니 선생이 “그렇습니다. 아주 위험한 종류의 심장병입니다.” 하고 대답했습니다. 선생은 자기가 보기에 무엇이 문제인지 소상히 말씀하셨지만, 저는 알아들을 만큼 똑똑하지 못했습니다. 다만 이것만은 압니다. 선생은 자기 도움이든 다른 어떤 의사의 도움이든 그리 큰 소용이 없을까 두렵다는 말로 이야기를 맺었습니다.\n\n마님은 이 나쁜 소식을 나리보다 차분하게 받아들이셨습니다. 나리는 덩치가 크고 뚱뚱한 별난 노인으로, 새와 흰 쥐를 기르면서 마치 사람의 자식이라도 되는 양 그것들에게 말을 걸곤 하셨습니다. 나리는 그 일로 몹시 상심하신 듯했습니다. “아! 가엾은 글라이드 부인! 가엾고 가여운 글라이드 부인!” 하고 말하면서, 뚱뚱한 두 손을 비틀며 신사라기보다 연극배우처럼 성큼성큼 돌아다니셨습니다. 마님이 그 부인이 회복될 가망을 두고 의사에게 한 가지를 물으면 나리는 적어도 쉰 가지는 물으셨습니다. 참말이지 저희 모두를 어지간히 볶아 대셨는데, 겨우 잠잠해지시더니 뒤뜰 자락으로 나가서 볼품없는 작은 꽃다발들을 만들어서는, 그걸 위층으로 가져가 병실을 예쁘게 꾸며 달라고 저에게 부탁하셨습니다. 그런다고 무슨 소용이 있다고요. 저는 나리 머리가 이따금 좀 모자라신 게 아닌가 싶습니다. 그래도 나쁜 주인은 아니셨습니다. 입은 놀랍도록 공손하셨고, 유쾌하고 느긋하고 사람을 살살 구슬리는 데가 있으셨지요. 저는 마님보다 나리가 훨씬 좋았습니다. 마님은 모진 분이었습니다. 세상에 모진 사람이 있다면 바로 그런 분이었지요.\n\n밤이 가까워지자 부인이 조금 정신을 차렸습니다. 그 전에는 경련으로 어찌나 지치셨던지 손발 하나 까딱하지 않고 누구에게 말 한마디 하지 않으셨습니다. 이제 침대에서 몸을 움직이며 방과 방 안에 있는 저희를 둘러보셨습니다. 성하실 때는 고운 분이셨을 겁니다. 머리는 밝은 빛이고 눈은 파랗고 그랬으니까요. 밤에는 잠자리가 편치 않으셨습니다. 적어도 홀로 곁을 지키신 마님께 그렇게 들었습니다. 저는 잠자리에 들기 전에 도울 일이 있을까 하고 한 번 들어가 봤을 뿐인데, 그때 그분은 두서없이 횡설수설하며 혼잣말을 하고 계셨습니다. 어딘가 곁에 없는 누군가에게 몹시도 말을 걸고 싶어 하시는 것 같았습니다. 처음에는 그 이름을 알아듣지 못했고, 두 번째에는 나리가 늘 하시는 질문 보따리와 볼품없는 꽃다발을 또 하나 들고 문을 두드리셨습니다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침에 들어가 보니 부인은 또 완전히 기진하여 까무룩 잠든 것 같은 상태로 누워 계셨습니다. 구드리크 선생이 상의하려고 동업자인 가스 선생을 데리고 오셨습니다. 두 분은 무슨 일이 있어도 부인의 잠을 깨워서는 안 된다고 했습니다. 두 분은 방 저쪽 끝에서 마님께 여러 가지를 물었습니다. 부인의 건강이 예전에는 어떠했는지, 누가 진료했는지, 마음의 괴로움을 오래도록 심하게 겪은 일이 있는지 하는 것들이었습니다. 마지막 질문에 마님이 “네” 하고 대답하신 것이 기억납니다. 그러자 구드리크 선생이 가스 선생을 보며 고개를 저었고, 가스 선생이 구드리크 선생을 보며 고개를 저었습니다. 두 분은 그 괴로움이 부인의 심장 탈과 무슨 관계가 있으리라고 여기시는 듯했습니다. 가엾게도 부인은 보기에도 여린 분이었습니다! 언제나 기운이 아주 없으셨을 겁니다. 참으로 기운이 없으셨지요.\n\n같은 날 조금 늦게 잠에서 깨어난 부인은 갑자기 상태가 달라져서 겉보기에는 훨씬 좋아지셨습니다. 저는 다시 들어가 뵙지 못했고 하녀 아이도 마찬가지였습니다. 낯선 사람 때문에 방해를 받아서는 안 된다는 까닭에서였지요. 그분이 좋아지셨다는 이야기는 나리를 통해 들었습니다. 나리는 그 변화를 두고 기분이 아주 좋으셔서, 챙이 크게 말려 올라간 커다란 흰 모자를 쓰고 나가시는 길에 뜰에서 부엌 창문 안을 들여다보셨습니다.\n\n“착한 요리사 아주머니,” 나리가 말씀하셨습니다. “글라이드 부인이 좋아지셨소. 내 마음도 한결 놓여서, 볕 좋은 여름 산책으로 이 큰 다리를 좀 뻗으러 나가는 길이오. 뭘 좀 시켜다 줄까, 장을 봐다 줄까, 요리사 아주머니? 거기서 무얼 만들고 있소? 저녁에 낼 맛있는 타르트인가? 껍질은 넉넉하게, 부디 넉넉하게 해 주시오. 바삭바삭한 껍질 말이오, 아주머니. 입안에서 사르르 녹아 부서지는 그런 것으로.” 나리는 늘 그런 식이셨습니다. 예순이 넘은 분이 과자를 좋아하셨지요. 생각 좀 해 보십시오!\n\n의사는 오전에 다시 와서 글라이드 부인이 깨어나 좋아지신 것을 직접 보셨습니다. 선생은 저희에게 부인에게 말을 걸지도 말고, 부인이 말하고 싶어 하시더라도 말을 하시게 두지도 말라고 이르며, 무엇보다 조용히 계시게 하고 되도록 많이 주무시도록 해야 한다고 하셨습니다. 제가 뵐 때마다 부인은 말하고 싶어 하시는 것 같지 않았습니다. 무슨 말씀인지 알아들을 수 없었던 전날 밤만 빼고요. 너무 지치신 듯했습니다. 구드리크 선생은 나리만큼 부인의 상태를 낙관하지 않으셨습니다. 아래층에 내려와서는 다섯 시에 다시 들르겠다는 말 외에는 아무 말씀도 없으셨습니다.\n\n그 무렵, 나리가 아직 돌아오시기 전이었습니다만, 침실에서 종이 세차게 울렸고, 마님이 층계참으로 뛰어나와 저를 부르시며 구드리크 선생께 가서 부인이 기절하셨다고 전하라고 하셨습니다. 제가 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르는데, 다행히도 마침 선생이 약속한 왕진으로 집에 오셨습니다.\n\n저는 선생을 들여보내고 함께 위층으로 올라갔습니다. “글라이드 부인은 여느 때와 같았어요.” 마님이 문간에서 선생께 말했습니다. “깨어나서 이상하게도 쓸쓸한 모습으로 주위를 둘러보고 있었는데, 반쯤 비명 같은 소리를 내는가 싶더니 순식간에 기절해 버렸어요.” 선생은 침대로 다가가 병든 부인 위로 몸을 굽혔습니다. 그분을 보는 순간 갑자기 몹시 심각한 얼굴이 되더니, 부인의 심장에 손을 얹었습니다.\n\n마님이 구드리크 선생의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 쳐다보았습니다. “돌아가신 건 아니겠죠!” 마님이 속삭이듯 말하며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨었습니다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 선생이 아주 조용하고 무겁게 말했습니다. “돌아가셨습니다. 어제 심장을 진찰했을 때 갑자기 이렇게 되지 않을까 두려웠습니다.” 선생이 말하는 동안 마님은 침대 곁에서 물러서며 떨고 또 떨었습니다. “돌아가셨어!” 마님이 혼잣말로 속삭였습니다. “이렇게 갑자기 돌아가시다니! 이렇게 빨리 돌아가시다니! 백작이 뭐라고 할까?” 구드리크 선생은 마님께 아래층에 내려가 좀 진정하시라고 권했습니다. “밤새 앉아 지새우셨으니,” 선생이 말했습니다. “신경이 흔들리셨을 겁니다. 이 사람이,” 하고 선생은 저를 가리키며 말했습니다. “이 사람이 필요한 도움을 부르러 보낼 수 있을 때까지 방에 남아 있을 겁니다.” 마님은 선생이 이르는 대로 하셨습니다. “백작에게 미리 알려야 해요.” 마님이 말했습니다. “조심스럽게 미리 알려 드려야 해요.” 그러고는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 저희를 두고 나가셨습니다.\n\n“댁의 주인 양반은 외국 사람이지요.” 마님이 우리 곁을 떠난 뒤에 구드리크 선생님이 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다. “사망 신고하는 일은 알고 계신가요?” “저야 잘 모르겠습니다요, 선생님.” 하고 제가 말했지요. “하지만 모르실 것 같습니다요.” 선생님은 잠시 생각하시더니 이렇게 말씀하시더군요. “내가 보통 이런 일까지 하지는 않네만,” 하고 말씀하시길, “이번 경우에는 내가 직접 사망 신고를 해 두면 그 댁의 수고를 덜어 줄 수 있겠군. 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 관할 등록 사무소 앞을 지나갈 테니 들르는 거야 어려울 것 없지. 미안하지만 내가 그렇게 하겠다고 전해 주게.” “예, 선생님.” 하고 제가 말했지요. “그렇게까지 생각해 주시니 참말 고맙습니다요.” “적당한 사람을 보내 줄 때까지 자네가 여기 남아 있어도 괜찮겠나?” 하고 선생님이 말씀하셨습니다. “예, 선생님.” 하고 제가 말했지요. “그때까지 이 딱한 마님 곁을 지키고 있겠습니다요. 여쭙습니다만, 해 드린 것 말고 더 해 드릴 수 있는 일은 없었을까요, 선생님?” 하고 제가 말했지요. “없네.” 하고 선생님이 말씀하셨습니다. “아무것도 없었어. 내가 보기 훨씬 전부터 이 부인은 몹시 괴로워했을 걸세. 나를 불렀을 때는 이미 가망이 없었지.” “아이고, 저런! 우리 모두가 이르든 늦든 언젠가는 그리 되고 마는 거지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?” 하고 제가 말했지요. 선생님은 거기에는 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다. 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 없어 보이셨지요. 선생님은 “그럼 이만.” 하고는 나가셨습니다.\n\n그때부터 구드리크 선생님이 약속하신 대로 사람을 들여보내 주실 때까지 저는 침대 곁을 지켰습니다. 그 여자는 이름이 제인 굴드였습니다. 제가 보기에는 점잖아 보이는 여자였지요. 그 여자는 자기가 무슨 일을 하러 왔는지 알고 있다는 말과, 그동안 그런 시신을 여럿 거두어 보았다는 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다.\n\n주인 나리께서 그 소식을 처음 들으셨을 때 어떻게 견디셨는지는, 제가 그 자리에 없었으니 제 입으로 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 제가 나리를 뵈었을 때는 정말이지 몹시 상심하신 얼굴이셨습니다. 나리는 구석에 조용히 앉아 계셨는데, 살진 두 손을 굵은 무릎 위로 늘어뜨리고 고개를 떨군 채 아무것도 아닌 데를 바라보고 계셨지요. 슬퍼하신다기보다는 벌어진 일에 겁을 먹고 얼이 빠지신 것처럼 보이셨습니다. 장례에 관한 일은 모두 마님이 처리하셨습니다. 돈이 어마어마하게 들었을 겁니다. 특히 관이 더할 나위 없이 훌륭했으니까요. 돌아가신 마님의 남편 되시는 분은 우리가 듣기로 외국에 나가 계시다고 했습니다. 하지만 마님께서는 (돌아가신 분의 고모님이 되시니) 시골에 있는 그분 친척들과 (컴벌랜드였던 것 같습니다) 의논하셔서, 그곳에 어머니와 같은 무덤에 묻히도록 정하셨습니다. 다시 말씀드리지만 장례에 관해서는 모든 것이 훌륭하게 치러졌고, 나리께서도 몸소 시골로 내려가 매장에 참석하셨습니다. 짙은 상복을 입으신 데다 큼직하고 엄숙한 얼굴에 느릿한 걸음걸이, 넓은 모자 띠까지 두르신 모습이 참으로 위풍당당하셨습니다. 정말 그러셨지요!\n\n끝으로. 제게 주어진 물음에 답하여 다음과 같이 말씀드립니다—\n\n(1) 저나 저와 함께 일하던 하인이나, 주인 나리께서 글라이드 부인께 손수 약을 드리는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없습니다.\n\n(2) 제가 알고 믿는 한, 나리께서 글라이드 부인과 방 안에 단둘이 남아 계신 일은 한 번도 없었습니다.\n\n(3) 그 부인이 이 집에 처음 들어오셨을 때 갑작스러운 공포에 사로잡히셨다고 마님께서 제게 말씀하셨습니다만, 그 공포의 원인이 무엇이었는지는 저로서는 말씀드릴 수 없습니다. 그 까닭은 저에게도, 저와 함께 일하던 하인에게도 끝내 설명되지 않았습니다.\n\n위의 진술은 제가 있는 자리에서 낭독되었습니다. 저는 여기에 더할 것도 뺄 것도 없습니다. 기독교인 여자로서 맹세코 말씀드리건대, 이것은 사실입니다.\n\n(서명) 헤스터 핀혼, 본인의 + 표시.\n\n# 2. THE NARRATIVE OF THE DOCTOR\n\n아래에 기재된 사망이 발생한 관할 구역의 등록관 앞. — 본인은 이에 다음과 같이 증명합니다. 본인은 지난 생일로 스물한 살이 된 글라이드 부인을 진료하였으며, 1850년 7월 25일 목요일에 마지막으로 그를 진찰하였고, 그는 같은 날 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지에서 사망하였으며, 사인은 동맥류였습니다. 발병 기간은 알 수 없습니다.\n\n(서명) 앨프리드 구드리크.\n\n직함. 영국 왕립외과의사회 회원, 약제사협회 면허. 주소, 세인트존스우드 크로이던 가든스 12번지.\n\n# 3. THE NARRATIVE OF JANE GOULD\n\n저는 이 서류 앞에 놓인 증명서에 적힌 집에서 사망한 어느 부인의 유해에 대하여 마땅하고 필요한 일을 하도록 구드리크 선생께서 보내신 사람입니다. 저는 그 시신이 하녀 헤스터 핀혼의 손에 맡겨져 있는 것을 보았습니다. 저는 시신 곁에 남아 때가 되었을 때 매장을 위한 준비를 했습니다. 시신은 제가 보는 앞에서 관에 뉘어졌고, 그 뒤에 저는 관이 옮겨지기에 앞서 나사로 잠기는 것을 보았습니다. 그 일이 끝난 뒤에야, 그전이 아니라, 저는 제 몫을 받고 그 집을 떠났습니다. 제 사람됨을 알아보고자 하시는 분은 구드리크 선생께 물어보시기 바랍니다. 제가 진실을 말할 만한 사람이라는 것을 그분이 증언해 주실 것입니다.\n\n(서명) 제인 굴드\n\n# 4. THE NARRATIVE OF THE TOMBSTONE\n\n햄프셔 블랙워터 파크의 준남작 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 아내이며, 이 교구 리머리지 하우스의 고(故) 필립 페얼리 님의 딸인 로라 글라이드 부인을 기리며. 1829년 3월 27일 출생, 1849년 12월 22일 혼인, 1850년 7월 25일 사망.\n\n# 5. THE NARRATIVE OF WALTER HARTRIGHT\n\n1850년 초여름, 나와 살아남은 동료들은 중앙아메리카의 황야와 밀림을 떠나 고향으로 향했다. 해안에 이르러 우리는 그곳에서 영국행 배를 탔다. 배는 멕시코 만에서 난파했고, 나는 바다에서 구조된 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였다. 죽음의 위험에서 벗어난 것이 그것으로 세 번째였다. 병으로 인한 죽음, 인디언의 손에 죽는 죽음, 물에 빠져 죽는 죽음 — 그 셋 모두가 내게 다가왔고, 셋 모두가 나를 비켜 갔다.\n\n난파선의 생존자들은 리버풀로 향하던 미국 배에 구조되었다. 그 배가 항구에 닿은 것은 1850년 10월 13일이었다. 우리는 늦은 오후에 상륙했고, 나는 그날 밤 런던에 도착했다.\n\n이 글은 고향을 떠나 있는 동안 내가 겪은 방랑과 위험의 기록이 아니다. 나를 조국과 벗들에게서 떼어 내어 모험과 위험의 새로운 세계로 이끈 동기는 이미 알려져 있다. 스스로에게 지운 그 유배에서 나는, 내가 바라고 기도하고 또 그러하리라 믿었던 대로 — 달라진 사람이 되어 돌아왔다. 새로운 삶의 물속에서 나는 내 천성을 다시 담금질했다. 극한과 위험이라는 혹독한 학교에서 내 의지는 강해지는 법을, 내 마음은 굳건해지는 법을, 내 정신은 스스로에게 기대는 법을 배웠다. 나는 나 자신의 미래로부터 달아나려고 떠났다. 나는 사내라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 그 미래와 맞서기 위해 돌아왔다.\n\n그 미래가 내게 요구하리라는 것을 내가 알고 있던, 저 피할 수 없는 자기 억제와 더불어 맞서기 위해서. 나는 지난날의 가장 쓰라린 아픔과는 작별했으나, 그 잊지 못할 시절의 슬픔과 애틋함에 대한 마음속 기억과는 작별하지 못했다. 내 삶의 그 돌이킬 수 없는 단 하나의 좌절을 나는 여전히 느끼고 있었다. 다만 그것을 견디는 법을 배웠을 뿐이었다. 배가 나를 실어 가고 내가 영국을 마지막으로 바라보았을 때, 내 모든 생각 속에는 로라 페얼리가 있었다. 배가 나를 데리고 돌아오고 아침 햇살이 반가운 해안을 눈앞에 드러내 보였을 때에도, 내 모든 생각 속에는 로라 페얼리가 있었다.\n\n내 마음이 옛사랑으로 돌아가니 내 펜도 옛 이름의 글자를 그린다. 나는 그녀를 여전히 로라 페얼리라고 쓴다. 그녀를 남편의 성으로 떠올리는 것도, 남편의 성으로 부르는 것도 내게는 힘든 일이다.\n\n이 기록에 내가 두 번째로 등장하는 데 대해 덧붙일 설명은 더 없다. 이 이야기는, 내게 그것을 써 나갈 힘과 용기가 있다면, 이제 계속될 수 있으리라.\n\n아침이 왔을 때 나의 첫 근심과 첫 희망은 어머니와 누이에게 쏠려 있었다. 여러 달 동안 두 사람이 내 소식이라고는 한 자도 받아 볼 수 없었던 그 부재의 시간 뒤였으니, 돌아온 기쁨과 놀라움을 감당할 수 있도록 두 사람을 미리 준비시켜야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 이른 아침에 나는 햄스테드의 오두막으로 편지를 보냈고, 한 시간쯤 뒤에 그 편지를 뒤따라 내가 갔다.\n\n첫 만남이 지나가고 지난날의 고요와 침착이 우리에게 조금씩 되돌아오기 시작했을 때, 나는 어머니의 얼굴에서 어떤 무거운 비밀이 그 마음을 짓누르고 있음을 알려 주는 무언가를 보았다. 나를 그토록 다정하게 바라보는 그 근심 어린 눈에는 사랑만이 아니라 슬픔이 있었고, 천천히 애틋하게 내 손을 더 힘주어 쥐는 그 인자한 손에는 연민이 있었다. 우리 사이에는 감추는 것이 없었다. 어머니는 내 인생의 희망이 어떻게 부서졌는지 알고 계셨고, 내가 왜 어머니 곁을 떠났는지도 알고 계셨다. 홀콤 양에게서 내게 온 편지가 있었는지, 그 동생에 관해 내가 들을 만한 소식이 있는지 되도록 담담하게 물어보려고 나는 입을 열려 했다. 그러나 어머니의 얼굴을 들여다보는 순간, 그렇게 조심스러운 형태로조차 그 물음을 꺼낼 용기를 잃고 말았다. 나는 다만 머뭇거리며, 애써 자제하며 이렇게 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n“제게 하실 말씀이 있으시군요.”\n\n우리 맞은편에 앉아 있던 누이가 아무런 설명도 없이 갑자기 일어섰다. 일어서서 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n어머니는 소파 위에서 내 곁으로 더 가까이 다가와 두 팔로 내 목을 감싸 안으셨다. 그 다정한 두 팔이 떨렸고, 신실하고 사랑 어린 얼굴 위로 눈물이 빠르게 흘러내렸다.\n\n“월터야!” 어머니가 속삭이셨다. “내 귀한 아들아! 네 일을 생각하면 어미 가슴이 미어진다. 오, 내 아들아! 내 아들아! 그래도 어미가 아직 네 곁에 남아 있다는 걸 잊지 말아 다오!”\n\n내 머리가 어머니의 가슴에 묻혔다. 그 말씀으로 어머니는 이미 모든 것을 말한 셈이었다.\n\n돌아온 지 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 10월 16일 아침이었다.\n\n나는 오두막에서 두 사람과 함께 지냈다. 나에게 그러했듯 두 사람에게까지 내 귀향의 기쁨이 쓰디쓴 것이 되지 않도록 무던히도 애썼다. 충격을 딛고 일어서서 내 삶을 체념으로 받아들이려고, 내 크나큰 슬픔이 절망이 아니라 애틋함으로 내 마음에 찾아들게 하려고, 사람으로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보았다. 소용없고 가망 없는 일이었다. 어떤 눈물도 쑤시는 내 눈을 달래 주지 못했고, 누이의 위로에서도 어머니의 사랑에서도 내게 위안은 오지 않았다.\n\n그 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 나는 두 사람에게 마음을 열었다. 어머니에게서 그녀의 죽음을 전해 들은 날부터 입 밖에 내고 싶어 애태우던 말이 마침내 내 입술을 넘어 나왔다.\n\n“잠시만 저 혼자 다녀오게 해 주세요.” 내가 말했다. “제가 그분을 처음 본 그곳을 한 번만 더 바라보고 나면 — 그분을 뉘어 놓은 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고 기도하고 나면, 이 일을 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 것 같습니다.”\n\n나는 길을 떠났다. 로라 페얼리의 무덤으로 가는 길이었다.\n\n내가 그 외딴 역에 내려 눈에 익은 길을 홀로 걸어 나선 것은 고요한 가을 오후였다. 기우는 해가 엷은 흰 구름 사이로 희미하게 비치고 있었고, 공기는 따스하고 고요했다. 인적 없는 시골의 평온함 위로는 저물어 가는 한 해의 기운이 그늘을 드리워 서글픔을 더하고 있었다.\n\n황야에 이르러 나는 다시 언덕 마루에 섰다. 길을 따라 눈길을 던지니, 저 멀리 낯익은 정원의 나무들과 시원스레 휘어 도는 진입로의 반원, 리머리지 하우스의 높다란 흰 벽이 있었다. 지나간 여러 달 동안의 온갖 우여곡절과 방랑과 위험이 내 마음속에서 모조리 오그라들어 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어 버렸다. 내 발이 향기로운 히스 땅을 마지막으로 밟은 것이 어제 일만 같았다. 작은 밀짚모자로 얼굴을 가리고 수수한 옷자락을 바람에 나부끼며, 잔뜩 채워진 스케치북을 손에 들고 그녀가 나를 맞으러 오는 모습이 보일 것만 같았다.\n\n오 죽음이여, 그대에게는 쏘는 것이 있구나! 오 무덤이여, 그대에게는 이기는 것이 있구나!\n\n나는 옆으로 돌아섰다. 그러자 발아래 골짜기에는 쓸쓸한 잿빛 교회가, 흰옷을 입은 여인이 오기를 내가 기다렸던 그 현관이, 고요한 묘지를 둘러싼 언덕들이, 돌 바닥 위로 차갑게 재잘거리며 흐르는 시내가 있었다. 무덤 머리맡에는 곱고 흰 대리석 십자가가 서 있었다. 이제는 어머니와 딸을 함께 덮고 솟아오른 무덤이었다.\n\n나는 무덤으로 다가갔다. 낮은 돌 층계를 다시 한번 넘어, 그 성스러운 땅을 밟으며 모자를 벗었다. 온유함과 선함에 바쳐진 땅, 경외와 슬픔에 바쳐진 땅이었다.\n\n나는 십자가가 솟아 있는 대좌 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그 한쪽 면, 내게 가장 가까운 쪽 면에 새로 새긴 비문이 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀의 삶과 죽음을 이야기하는 딱딱하고 또렷하고 잔인한 검은 글자들이었다. 나는 그것을 읽으려 했다. 이름까지는 정말로 읽었다. “로라를 기리며——” 눈물로 흐려진 그 다정한 푸른 눈, 지쳐 떨구어진 그 고운 머리, 자기를 두고 떠나 달라고 애원하던 그 순결한 이별의 말 — 아, 이보다 더 행복한 마지막 기억을 그녀에 대해 지닐 수 있었더라면. 내가 지니고 떠났던 기억, 이제 그녀의 무덤으로 다시 가지고 온 기억이 하필 이것이라니!\n\n두 번째로 나는 비문을 읽으려 했다. 끝머리에 있는 그녀의 사망 날짜가 눈에 들어왔고, 그 위에는——\n\n그 위에는 대리석에 새겨진 몇 줄의 글이 있었고, 그 가운데에는 그녀를 향한 내 생각을 어지럽히는 이름이 하나 있었다. 나는 무덤 반대편으로 돌아갔다. 그쪽에는 읽을 것이 아무것도 없었고, 그녀의 영혼과 내 영혼 사이를 비집고 들어올 세속의 더러움도 아무것도 없었다.\n\n나는 무덤 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다. 넓고 흰 돌 위에 두 손을 얹고 머리를 얹은 채, 둘레의 땅에 대해서도 위의 빛에 대해서도 지친 두 눈을 감았다. 나는 그녀가 내게 돌아오도록 내버려 두었다. 오, 내 사랑! 내 사랑! 이제야 내 마음이 그대에게 말할 수 있구려! 우리가 헤어진 것이 다시 어제 일이 되는구려 — 그대의 사랑스러운 손이 내 손 안에 놓여 있던 것도 어제, 내 두 눈이 그대를 마지막으로 바라본 것도 어제가 되는구려. 내 사랑! 내 사랑!\n\n시간은 흘러갔고, 그 흐름 위로 짙은 밤처럼 침묵이 내려앉아 있었다.\n\n그 천상의 평화 뒤에 찾아온 첫 소리는 묘지의 풀 위를 스치는 한 줄기 숨결처럼 희미하게 바스락거렸다. 나는 그것이 천천히 내게 가까워지는 것을 들었고, 이윽고 그 소리는 내 귀에 달리 들려왔다. 앞으로 옮겨 오는 발소리처럼 들려오다가, 멈추었다.\n\n나는 고개를 들었다.\n\n해 질 녘이 가까웠다. 구름이 갈라져 비스듬한 빛이 언덕 위로 부드럽게 내려앉고 있었다. 죽은 이들의 고요한 골짜기에 남은 하루의 마지막은 차갑고 맑고 고요했다.\n\n저만치 묘지 안, 낮게 깔린 빛의 차가운 맑음 속에 나란히 서 있는 두 여자가 보였다. 그들은 무덤 쪽을, 나를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n둘이었다.\n\n그들은 조금 앞으로 나오다가 다시 멈추었다. 베일을 내리고 있어 얼굴은 내게 가려져 있었다. 멈춰 섰을 때 그중 한 사람이 베일을 걷어 올렸다. 고요한 저녁 빛 속에서 나는 매리언 홀콤의 얼굴을 보았다.\n\n달라져 있었다. 그 위로 몇 해가 지나가 버리기라도 한 듯 달라져 있었다! 두 눈은 커다랗고 사나웠으며, 그 안에 낯선 공포를 담은 채 나를 바라보고 있었다. 얼굴은 애처로울 만큼 상하고 여위어 있었다. 고통과 두려움과 슬픔이 낙인처럼 그녀에게 찍혀 있었다.\n\n나는 무덤에서 그녀에게로 한 걸음 내디뎠다. 그녀는 움직이지도, 말하지도 않았다. 그녀와 함께 있던 베일 쓴 여자가 희미하게 소리를 질렀다. 나는 멈춰 섰다. 내 생명의 샘이 낮게 잦아들었고, 말로 다 할 수 없는 공포의 전율이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 나를 타고 기어올랐다.\n\n베일로 얼굴을 가린 여자가 일행에게서 떨어져 나와 천천히 내게로 다가왔다. 홀로 남겨진 채, 홀로 선 채 매리언 홀콤이 입을 열었다. 내가 기억하는 바로 그 목소리였다. 겁에 질린 눈이나 여윈 얼굴과는 달리 변하지 않은 목소리였다.\n\n“내 꿈! 내 꿈이!” 그 무서운 침묵 속에서 그녀가 나직이 그렇게 말하는 것을 나는 들었다. 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 맞잡은 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올렸다. “아버지! 저 사람을 강하게 하소서. 아버지! 저 사람이 절실히 도움을 필요로 하는 이 순간에 도우소서.”\n\n여자는 다가왔다. 천천히, 소리 없이 다가왔다. 나는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그 순간부터 오직 그녀만을, 다른 누구도 아닌 그녀만을 바라보았다.\n\n나를 위해 기도하던 목소리가 흔들리며 낮게 잦아들더니, 별안간 높아져 겁에 질린 듯, 절망한 듯 내게 물러나라고 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 베일 쓴 여자가 내 몸과 영혼을 온통 사로잡고 있었다. 그녀는 무덤 한쪽에 멈춰 섰다. 우리는 묘비를 사이에 두고 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다. 그녀는 대좌 면에 새겨진 비문 바로 곁에 있었다. 그녀의 옷자락이 그 검은 글자들에 닿았다.\n\n목소리가 더 가까이 다가오며 점점 더, 점점 더 격정적으로 높아졌다. “얼굴을 가려요! 저 여자를 보지 말아요! 오, 하느님, 부디 저 사람을 살려 주——”\n\n여자가 베일을 걷어 올렸다.\n\n“로라 글라이드 부인을 기리며——”\n\n로라 글라이드 부인이 그 비문 곁에 서서, 무덤 너머로 나를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n[이야기의 제2기는 여기에서 끝난다.]\n\n나는 여기서 새 페이지를 연다. 내 이야기를 일주일 앞으로 옮겨 놓는다.\n\n이렇게 건너뛰는 그 사이의 내력은 기록되지 않은 채로 남아야 한다. 그 일을 떠올리면 가슴이 아득해지고 정신은 어둠과 혼란 속으로 가라앉는다. 글을 쓰는 내가 읽는 당신을 마땅히 이끌어야 한다면, 그래서는 안 된다. 이 이야기의 굽이진 길을 헤쳐 나가는 실마리가 처음부터 끝까지 내 손안에서 엉키지 않은 채 남아 있어야 한다면, 그래서는 안 된다.\n\n갑작스레 달라진 삶 — 그 목적 전부가 새로이 세워지고, 희망과 두려움, 투쟁과 관심사와 희생이 모두 한꺼번에 그리고 영원히 새로운 방향으로 돌아선 삶 — 이것이 지금 내 앞에 열리는 전망이다. 산꼭대기에 올라섰을 때 별안간 터져 나오는 시야처럼. 나는 리머리지 교회의 고요한 그늘 속에 내 이야기를 놓아두었다. 그리고 일주일 뒤, 런던 어느 거리의 소란과 혼잡 속에서 그것을 다시 잇는다.\n\n그 거리는 사람이 붐비는 가난한 동네에 있다. 거리에 늘어선 집들 가운데 하나는 1층을 작은 신문 가게가 차지하고 있고, 2층과 3층은 더없이 초라한 가구 딸린 셋방으로 내놓았다.\n\n나는 가명으로 그 두 층을 얻었다. 위층에서 내가 사는데, 일하는 방 하나와 잠자는 방 하나를 쓴다. 아래층에는 같은 가명으로 두 여자가 사는데, 내 누이들이라고 되어 있다. 나는 싸구려 정기간행물에 실을 그림을 그리고 목판에 새겨 밥벌이를 한다. 내 누이들은 삯바느질을 조금 맡아 나를 거드는 것으로 되어 있다. 우리의 초라한 거처, 보잘것없는 생업, 꾸며 낸 관계, 꾸며 낸 이름 — 이 모두가 한결같이 런던이라는 집들의 숲 속에 우리를 감추는 수단으로 쓰인다. 우리는 더 이상 삶이 훤히 드러나고 알려진 사람들 축에 들지 않는다. 나는 후원자도 도와줄 벗도 없는, 이름 없고 눈에 띄지 않는 사내다. 매리언 홀콤은 이제 제 손으로 일해 살림을 꾸려 가는 내 맏누이일 뿐이다. 남들이 보기에 우리 둘은 대담한 사기극에 속아 넘어간 자인 동시에 그 앞잡이다. 우리는 죽은 글라이드 부인의 이름과 지위와 살아 있는 인격을 제 것이라 주장하는 미친 앤 캐서릭의 공범으로 여겨지는 것이다.\n\n그것이 우리의 처지다. 그것이 앞으로 이 이야기에서 여러 페이지에 걸쳐 우리 세 사람이 지니고 나타나야 할, 달라진 모습이다.\n\n이성과 법의 눈으로 보나, 친척과 벗들의 판단으로 보나, 문명사회의 온갖 공인된 격식에 따르나, “로라, 글라이드 부인”은 리머리지 교회 묘지에 제 어머니와 나란히 묻혀 있었다. 살아 있는 동안에 산 자의 명부에서 찢겨 나간 필립 페얼리의 딸이자 퍼시벌 글라이드의 아내는 제 언니에게는 아직 살아 있을 수 있었고 나에게도 아직 살아 있을 수 있었으나, 그 밖의 온 세상에게 그녀는 죽은 사람이었다. 그녀를 내친 삼촌에게 죽었고, 그녀를 알아보지 못한 그 집 하인들에게 죽었고, 그녀의 재산을 남편과 고모에게 넘겨준 관계 당국의 사람들에게 죽었고, 내가 여자 사기꾼에게 홀린 얼간이요 협잡의 희생자라고 믿는 내 어머니와 누이에게 죽었다. 사회적으로도, 도덕적으로도, 법적으로도 — 죽은 사람이었다.\n\n그런데도 살아 있었다! 가난 속에서, 숨어 지내며 살아 있었다. 그녀를 대신해 싸우고 살아 있는 사람들의 세계에서 그녀의 자리를 되찾아 줄 가난한 그림 교사를 곁에 두고 살아 있었다.\n\n앤 캐서릭이 그녀를 닮았다는 것을 내가 익히 알고 있었으니, 그녀의 얼굴이 처음 내 앞에 드러났을 때 어떤 의심이 내 마음을 스치지는 않았던가? 그녀가 제 죽음을 새긴 비문 곁에서 베일을 걷어 올린 그 순간부터, 의심의 그림자조차 없었다.\n\n그날의 해가 지기 전에, 그녀를 향해 닫혀 버린 그 집의 마지막 모습이 우리 시야에서 사라지기 전에, 우리가 리머리지 하우스에서 헤어질 때 내가 했던 작별의 말이 우리 둘 모두에게 되살아났다 — 내가 그 말을 되풀이했고, 그녀가 그 말을 알아보았다. “언젠가 제 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다한 헌신이 아가씨께 한순간의 행복을 드리거나 한순간의 슬픔을 덜어 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 아가씨를 가르쳤던 가난한 그림 교사를 기억해 주시겠습니까?” 뒷날의 괴로움과 공포는 이제 거의 기억하지 못하는 그녀가 그 말만은 기억했고, 그 말을 했던 사내의 가슴에 천진하고도 믿음 가득하게 제 가엾은 머리를 기댔다. 그녀가 내 이름을 부르며 “저 사람들이 저더러 모든 걸 잊게 하려고 했어요, 월터. 하지만 저는 매리언을 기억하고, 당신을 기억해요” 하고 말하던 그 순간에, 오래전에 이미 그녀에게 내 사랑을 바쳤던 나는 그녀에게 내 목숨을 바쳤고, 그것이 그녀에게 내어 줄 수 있는 내 것임을 하느님께 감사드렸다. 그렇다! 그때가 온 것이다. 수천수만 마일 떨어진 곳에서부터 — 나보다 강한 동료들이 내 곁에서 쓰러져 간 숲과 황야를 지나, 세 번 거듭 닥쳤다가 세 번 벗어난 죽음의 위험을 지나, 사람들을 미래로 향한 어두운 길 위로 이끄는 그 손이 나를 그때와 만나도록 이끌어 온 것이다. 버림받고 부인당하고, 모질게 시달리고 슬프도록 달라진 그녀 — 아름다움은 시들고 정신은 흐려졌으며, 세상에서의 지위도 살아 있는 사람들 사이의 자리도 빼앗긴 그녀에게, 내가 약속했던 헌신을, 내 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다한 그 헌신을 이제는 떳떳이 그 사랑스러운 발치에 바칠 수 있었다. 그녀의 재난이 준 권리로, 그녀의 외로움이 준 권리로, 그녀는 마침내 내 사람이 되었다! 부양하고 지키고 아끼고 되살려 낼 내 사람. 아버지이자 오라비로서 사랑하고 존중할 내 사람. 온갖 위험과 온갖 희생을 무릅쓰고 — 신분과 권력에 맞선 가망 없는 싸움을 뚫고, 무장한 기만과 요새를 두른 성공과의 긴 싸움을 뚫고, 내 명성이 소진되는 일을 뚫고, 벗들을 잃는 일을 뚫고, 내 목숨이 위태로워지는 일을 뚫고 — 그 결백을 밝혀 주어야 할 내 사람이었다.\n\n# II\n\n내 처지는 분명해졌고, 내 동기도 밝혀졌다. 다음은 매리언의 이야기와 로라의 이야기가 올 차례다.\n\n나는 두 사람의 이야기를 그들 자신이 한 말 — 자주 끊기고 또 어쩔 수 없이 자주 뒤엉킨 그 말 — 그대로 옮기지 않고, 나 자신의 지침으로 삼고 또 내 법률 고문의 지침으로 삼기 위해 적어 두었던, 짧고 담백하며 애써 단순하게 다듬은 요약문의 말로 옮기려 한다. 그래야 뒤얽힌 실타래가 가장 빠르고 가장 알아보기 쉽게 풀릴 것이다.\n\n매리언의 이야기는 블랙워터 파크 가정부의 서술이 끝난 지점에서 시작된다.\n\n글라이드 부인이 남편의 집을 떠났을 때, 그 떠남이라는 사실과 그것이 이루어진 정황에 대한 필요한 설명은 가정부를 통해 홀콤 양에게 전해졌다. 포스코 백작의 집에서 글라이드 부인이 갑작스레 세상을 떠났다는 것을 알리는 편지가 포스코 백작부인에게서 온 것은 그로부터 며칠이 지난 뒤였다(정확히 며칠 뒤였는지는, 그 문제에 관해 적어 둔 기록이 없는 터라 마이컬슨 부인도 단언하지 못했다). 편지는 날짜를 밝히기를 피했고, 그 소식을 곧바로 홀콤 양에게 알릴지 아니면 그분의 건강이 좀 더 확실히 회복될 때까지 미룰지는 마이컬슨 부인의 판단에 맡겼다.\n\n마이컬슨 부인은 도슨 의사와 상의한 뒤 — 그 역시 병 때문에 블랙워터 파크로 다시 왕진 나오는 일이 늦어지고 있었다 — 의사의 조언에 따라, 그리고 의사가 있는 자리에서 그 소식을 전했다. 편지가 온 그날이었거나 그 이튿날이었다. 글라이드 부인의 급작스러운 죽음이라는 소식이 그 언니에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 여기서 길게 이야기할 필요가 없다. 지금의 목적에 소용되는 것은 그 뒤로 삼 주가 넘도록 그녀가 여행을 할 수 없었다는 사실뿐이다. 그 기간이 끝나자 그녀는 가정부와 함께 런던으로 향했다. 두 사람은 거기서 헤어졌는데, 그에 앞서 마이컬슨 부인은 훗날 서로 연락할 일이 있을지 모른다며 자기 주소를 홀콤 양에게 알려 주었다.\n\n가정부와 헤어진 뒤 홀콤 양은 곧바로 길모어 앤드 카일 사무소로 가서, 길모어 씨가 자리에 없는 터라 카일 씨와 상의했다. 그녀는 다른 누구에게도 — 마이컬슨 부인에게조차 — 감추는 편이 낫다고 여겼던 것을 카일 씨에게는 털어놓았다. 곧 글라이드 부인이 죽음을 맞았다고 하는 그 정황에 대한 의심이었다. 전에도 홀콤 양을 돕고자 하는 마음을 우정 어린 태도로 보여 준 바 있는 카일 씨는, 자기에게 맡겨진 그 조사의 미묘하고 위험한 성격이 허락하는 한도 안에서 알아보겠노라고 곧바로 나섰다.\n\n더 나아가기 전에 이 대목을 마저 정리해 두자면, 카일 씨가 글라이드 부인의 별세에 관해 아직 홀콤 양에게 전해지지 않은 자세한 사정을 알아 오라는 부탁을 받고 왔다고 밝히자 포스코 백작이 온갖 편의를 다 제공했다는 사실은 언급해 둘 만하다. 카일 씨는 의사인 구드리크 선생, 그리고 두 하인과 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 정확한 날짜를 확인할 방도가 전혀 없는 상황에서, 의사와 하인들의 증언, 그리고 포스코 백작 부부가 자진해서 내놓은 진술이 낳은 결과는 카일 씨의 마음속에서 결정적이었다. 그는 동생을 잃은 슬픔이 너무도 극심한 나머지 홀콤 양의 판단이 참으로 딱한 방향으로 흐려진 것이라고밖에 볼 수 없었고, 자기 앞에서 그녀가 내비쳤던 그 끔찍한 의심에는 자기 소견으로는 진실에 근거한 구석이 티끌만큼도 없다고 편지에 적어 보냈다. 이렇게 해서 길모어 씨 동업자의 조사는 시작되자마자 끝나 버렸다.\n\n그동안 홀콤 양은 리머리지 하우스로 돌아가, 거기서 얻어 낼 수 있는 모든 추가 정보를 모아 두었다.\n\n페얼리 씨는 조카딸의 죽음에 대한 첫 기별을 누이인 포스코 백작부인에게서 받았는데, 이 편지에도 날짜에 대한 정확한 언급은 없었다. 그는 세상을 떠난 부인을 리머리지 교회 묘지의 어머니 무덤에 함께 모시자는 누이의 제안을 승낙했다. 포스코 백작은 유해를 모시고 컴벌랜드까지 따라와, 7월 30일에 리머리지에서 치러진 장례에 참석했다. 그 뒤로는 마을과 인근의 주민 전부가 예를 표하며 뒤를 따랐다. 이튿날에는 비문이 — 처음에는 고인의 고모가 지어서 그 오라비인 페얼리 씨에게 승낙을 받으려고 보냈다고 한다 — 무덤 위 기념비의 한 면에 새겨졌다.\n\n장례가 있던 날과 그 다음 하루 동안 포스코 백작은 리머리지 하우스에 손님으로 머물렀으나, 페얼리 씨의 뜻에 따라 두 사람이 얼굴을 맞대는 일은 없었다. 두 사람은 글로 연락을 주고받았고, 그 방법으로 포스코 백작은 페얼리 씨에게 조카딸의 마지막 병세와 죽음에 대한 자세한 사정을 알렸다. 이 사연을 전한 편지는 이미 알려진 사실에 새로운 사실을 보태지 않았지만, 추신에는 매우 주목할 만한 한 대목이 들어 있었다. 앤 캐서릭에 관한 것이었다.\n\n문제의 그 대목이 담고 있는 내용은 다음과 같았다—\n\n그것은 먼저 페얼리 씨에게, 앤 캐서릭이 — 그에 관한 자세한 사정은 홀콤 양이 리머리지에 닿으면 그녀에게서 들을 수 있을 것이라며 — 블랙워터 파크 인근에서 행방이 밝혀져 붙잡혔고, 한때 그 손에서 달아났던 그 의사의 관리 아래 두 번째로 맡겨졌다는 것을 알렸다.\n\n이것이 추신의 첫 부분이었다. 두 번째 부분은 페얼리 씨에게, 앤 캐서릭의 정신 질환이 오랫동안 통제 없이 지낸 탓에 악화되었으며, 예전부터 그 여자의 가장 두드러진 망상 가운데 하나였던 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 광적인 증오와 불신이 새로 얻은 형태로 여전히 남아 있음을 일러 주었다. 그 불행한 여자가 퍼시벌 경과 관련해 마지막으로 품은 생각은, 그를 성가시게 하고 괴롭히며 아울러 그의 죽은 아내 행세를 함으로써 환자들과 간호인들 사이에서 제 격이 올라간다고 여기는 것이었다. 이렇게 남의 신분을 사칭하려는 계획은 그 여자가 용케 성사시킨 글라이드 부인과의 은밀한 만남 뒤에 떠오른 것이 분명한데, 그때 그 여자는 세상을 떠난 부인과 자신이 우연히도 놀랍게 닮았다는 것을 알아차렸던 것이다. 그 여자가 두 번째로 정신병원에서 달아나는 데 성공할 가능성은 지극히 희박하지만, 어떻게든 방도를 찾아 고 글라이드 부인의 친척들을 편지로 성가시게 굴지도 모르니, 그런 경우 어떻게 받아들여야 할지를 페얼리 씨에게 미리 일러 둔다는 것이었다.\n\n이런 말로 적힌 추신은 홀콤 양이 리머리지에 도착했을 때 그녀에게 보여졌다. 아울러 글라이드 부인이 입고 있던 옷과, 고모의 집에 가지고 갔던 그 밖의 소지품도 그녀 손에 넘겨졌다. 그것들은 포스코 백작부인이 정성껏 챙겨서 컴벌랜드로 보낸 것이었다.\n\n홀콤 양이 9월 초에 리머리지에 닿았을 때 사정은 이러했다.\n\n그 직후 그녀는 병이 도져 방에 갇혀 지냈다. 지금 겪고 있는 심한 마음의 고통 아래 약해진 몸의 기력이 무너진 것이었다. 한 달 만에 다시 기운을 차렸을 때에도, 동생의 죽음에 따랐다고 하는 그 정황에 대한 의심은 여전히 흔들림이 없었다. 그사이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대해서는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했으나, 포스코 백작부인에게서는 남편과 자신을 대신해 더없이 다정하게 안부를 묻는 편지가 여러 통 왔다. 홀콤 양은 그 편지들에 답하는 대신, 세인트존스우드의 그 집과 거기 사는 사람들의 동태를 남몰래 살피게 했다.\n\n의심스러운 것은 아무것도 나오지 않았다. 뒤이어 루벨 부인에 관해 몰래 진행한 조사도 같은 결과였다. 그 여자는 여섯 달쯤 전에 남편과 함께 런던에 왔다. 두 사람은 리옹에서 왔고, 1851년 박람회를 보러 영국에 많이 몰려올 것으로 예상되는 외국인들을 위한 하숙집으로 꾸미려고 레스터 광장 근처에 집을 한 채 얻었다. 동네에서 남편이나 아내에 대해 나쁘게 알려진 것은 없었다. 조용한 사람들이었고, 지금까지 셈은 정직하게 치러 왔다. 마지막 조사는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 관한 것이었다. 그는 파리에 자리를 잡고 영국인과 프랑스인 친구 몇몇의 좁은 무리 안에서 조용히 지내고 있었다.\n\n가는 곳마다 막혔으나 그래도 가만있을 수는 없었기에, 홀콤 양은 다음으로 앤 캐서릭이 두 번째로 갇혀 있으리라고 그때 생각하고 있던 정신병원을 찾아가 보기로 마음먹었다. 그녀는 예전부터 그 여자에 대해 강한 호기심을 느껴 왔고, 이제는 그 관심이 갑절이 되었다. 첫째로는 앤 캐서릭이 글라이드 부인 행세를 하려 했다는 보고가 사실인지 확인하기 위해서였고, 둘째로는 (그것이 사실로 드러난다면) 그 가엾은 것이 그런 속임수를 꾀한 진짜 동기가 무엇인지 제 눈으로 알아내기 위해서였다.\n\n포스코 백작이 페얼리 씨에게 보낸 편지에 정신병원 주소가 적혀 있지는 않았지만, 그 중요한 누락도 홀콤 양에게는 아무 걸림돌이 되지 못했다. 하트라이트 씨가 리머리지에서 앤 캐서릭을 만났을 때 그 여자가 그 집이 있는 고장을 그에게 일러 주었고, 홀콤 양은 그 만남의 다른 모든 세부 사항과 함께 하트라이트 씨의 입에서 나온 그대로 그 방향을 일기에 적어 두었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 기록을 다시 들춰 주소를 찾아내고, 쓸모가 있을지 모를 일종의 증빙으로 백작이 페얼리 씨에게 보낸 편지를 챙긴 다음, 10월 11일에 혼자서 정신병원을 향해 출발했다.\n\n그녀는 11일 밤을 런던에서 보냈다. 애초에는 글라이드 부인의 옛 가정교사가 사는 집에서 묵을 작정이었으나, 잃어버린 제자의 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 벗을 본 베지 부인이 어찌나 마음을 못 가누던지 보기가 딱해서, 홀콤 양은 그 앞에 더 머물기를 삼가고 베지 부인의 결혼한 여동생이 일러 준 근처의 점잖은 하숙집으로 옮겼다. 이튿날 그녀는 정신병원으로 향했는데, 그곳은 런던에서 그리 멀지 않은 이 대도시의 북쪽에 있었다.\n\n그녀는 곧바로 원장을 만날 수 있게 안내되었다.\n\n처음에 그는 자기 환자와 그녀가 이야기를 나누게 하는 것을 딱 잘라 꺼리는 눈치였다. 그러나 그녀가 포스코 백작 편지의 추신을 보이고 — 거기 언급된 “홀콤 양”이 바로 자기라는 것, 자기가 세상을 떠난 글라이드 부인의 가까운 친척이라는 것, 그러니 집안 사정상 죽은 동생과 관련한 앤 캐서릭의 망상이 어느 정도인지 직접 살펴보고 싶은 것이 당연하다는 것을 일깨우자 — 정신병원 주인의 어조와 태도가 달라지면서 그는 반대를 거두었다. 아마 그는 이런 상황에서 계속 거절하는 것이 그 자체로 무례한 짓일 뿐 아니라, 제 시설에서 벌어지는 일이 점잖은 외부인의 조사를 견딜 만한 성질이 못 된다는 뜻으로 비칠 수 있음을 느꼈을 것이다.\n\n홀콤 양 자신의 인상으로는, 정신병원 주인은 퍼시벌 경과 백작에게서 속내를 나눠 받은 사람이 아니었다. 자기 환자를 만나게 해 주기로 승낙한 것 자체가 그 한 가지 증거인 듯했고, 공범이라면 좀처럼 입 밖에 낼 수 없었을 이야기를 선선히 털어놓은 것도 분명 또 하나의 증거가 되는 듯했다.\n\n이를테면 처음에 나눈 대화 도중에 그는 홀콤 양에게, 앤 캐서릭이 7월 27일 포스코 백작의 손에 필요한 지시서와 증명서와 함께 자기에게 도로 맡겨졌다고 알려 주었다. 백작은 아울러 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 서명한 설명과 지시의 편지도 내놓았다는 것이었다. 그 수용자를 다시 받았을 때 정신병원 주인은 그 여자에게서 몇 가지 묘한 신체의 변화를 알아차렸다고 인정했다. 물론 그런 변화가 정신을 앓는 사람들을 겪어 온 그의 경험에서 전례 없는 일은 아니었다. 미친 사람들은 흔히 겉으로 보나 속으로 보나 때마다 딴사람 같기 마련이니, 광기가 나아지든 더해지든 그 변화는 겉모습에도 달라짐을 낳게 되어 있었다. 그는 그런 점을 감안했고, 앤 캐서릭의 망상이 형태를 바꾼 것도 감안했는데, 그 역시 그 여자의 태도와 표정에 나타났을 터였다. 그럼에도 그는 달아나기 전의 환자와 되돌아온 뒤의 환자 사이에 있는 어떤 차이 때문에 이따금 여전히 어리둥절해했다. 그 차이란 말로 설명하기에는 너무 미세한 것이었다. 물론 그 여자의 키나 몸매나 안색이, 또는 머리카락과 눈의 빛깔이, 또는 얼굴의 전체적인 생김이 아주 달라졌다고는 말할 수 없었다. 그 변화는 눈에 보이는 무엇이라기보다 느껴지는 무엇이었다. 요컨대 이 환자는 처음부터 수수께끼였는데, 이제 거기 수수께끼가 하나 더 보태진 셈이었다.\n\n이 대화가 앞으로 닥칠 일에 홀콤 양의 마음을 조금이나마 대비시켜 주는 결과를 낳았다고는 할 수 없다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것은 그녀에게 아주 심각한 영향을 미쳤다. 그 때문에 어찌나 마음이 흐트러졌던지, 수용자들이 갇혀 있는 구역으로 정신병원 주인을 따라나설 만큼 침착을 되찾기까지 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다.\n\n물어보니 앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자는 그때 시설에 딸린 뜰에서 산책을 하고 있다고 했다. 간호인 하나가 홀콤 양을 그리로 데려다주겠다고 나섰고, 정신병원 주인은 손이 필요한 환자를 보러 잠깐 집 안에 남았다가 뜰에서 손님과 합류하겠다고 했다.\n\n간호인은 홀콤 양을 부지의 멀찍한 곳으로 데려갔다. 예쁘게 꾸며 놓은 자리였는데, 잠시 둘레를 살피더니 양옆으로 관목이 그늘을 드리운 잔디 길로 접어들었다. 그 길 중간쯤에서 여자 둘이 천천히 다가오고 있었다. 간호인이 그들을 가리키며 말했다. “저기 앤 캐서릭이 있습니다, 부인. 옆에 있는 사람은 그 여자를 돌보는 사람이고요. 물어보실 것이 있으면 그 사람이 답해 드릴 겁니다.” 그 말을 남기고 간호인은 집안일로 돌아가려고 그녀를 두고 갔다.\n\n홀콤 양은 그쪽으로 나아갔고, 두 여자는 이쪽으로 다가왔다. 서로 열두어 걸음 안까지 가까워졌을 때, 여자 하나가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 낯선 부인을 뚫어져라 바라보더니, 저를 붙든 간호인의 손을 뿌리치고 다음 순간 홀콤 양의 품으로 달려들었다. 그 순간 홀콤 양은 제 동생을 알아보았다. 죽었다던 산 사람을 알아본 것이다.\n\n그 뒤에 취한 조치들이 성공한 데는 다행스럽게도, 그 순간 그 자리에는 간호인 말고 아무도 없었다. 젊은 여자였던 그 간호인은 어찌나 놀랐던지 처음에는 도무지 손을 쓰지 못했다. 겨우 손을 쓸 수 있게 되었을 때는 그 시중이 온통 홀콤 양에게 필요했으니, 이 발견의 충격 아래 제정신을 붙들려고 애쓰다 그만 잠시 폭삭 주저앉아 버렸던 것이다. 맑은 공기와 서늘한 그늘에서 몇 분을 보내자 타고난 기력과 용기가 그녀를 조금 일으켜 세웠고, 그녀는 불행한 동생을 위해서라도 침착함을 되찾아야 한다는 것을 느낄 만큼 자신을 추스를 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 두 사람 다 간호인의 시야 안에 확실히 머문다는 조건으로 환자와 단둘이 이야기할 허락을 얻었다. 이것저것 물어볼 겨를은 없었다. 다만 그 가엾은 부인에게 스스로를 다잡아야 한다는 것을 새겨 주고, 그렇게만 하면 곧 도움과 구출이 있으리라고 다짐해 줄 겨를만 있었다. 언니가 시키는 대로 하면 이 정신병원에서 벗어날 수 있다는 전망은 글라이드 부인을 진정시키고 제게 무엇이 요구되는지 알아듣게 하기에 충분했다. 이어 홀콤 양은 간호인에게로 돌아가, 그때 주머니에 있던 금화를 몽땅(소브린 금화 세 닢이었다) 간호인의 손에 쥐여 주고는 언제 어디서 단둘이 이야기할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n그 여자는 처음에 놀라며 미심쩍어했다. 그러나 홀콤 양이, 그저 지금은 너무 마음이 흔들려 물어보지 못한 몇 가지를 묻고 싶을 뿐이며 간호인을 꾀어 직분을 저버리게 할 뜻은 조금도 없다고 밝히자, 여자는 돈을 받고 이튿날 세 시를 만날 시각으로 내놓았다. 그때라면 환자들 식사가 끝난 뒤라 반 시간쯤 빠져나올 수 있으니, 이 집의 뜰을 가리고 있는 북쪽 높은 담 바깥의 한갓진 자리에서 만나 뵙겠다는 것이었다. 홀콤 양은 그러겠노라고 답하고 동생에게 내일 소식을 듣게 될 것이라고 속삭일 겨를밖에 없었는데, 그때 정신병원 주인이 그들에게 다가왔다. 그는 손님이 흔들려 있는 것을 알아챘고, 홀콤 양은 앤 캐서릭과 만난 것이 처음에는 좀 놀라웠던 탓이라고 둘러댔다. 그녀는 되도록 곧 자리를 떴다. 다시 말해 불행한 동생 앞에서 억지로 몸을 떼어 낼 용기를 낼 수 있게 되자마자 그렇게 했다.\n\n생각할 힘이 돌아오자, 조금만 헤아려 보아도 분명해졌다. 글라이드 부인의 신원을 밝히고 법의 힘으로 그녀를 구해 내려는 어떤 시도든, 설령 성공한다 해도 시간을 끌 수밖에 없을 터인데, 그 지체는 이미 제가 내던져진 처지의 참담함에 흔들려 버린 동생의 정신에 치명적일 수 있었다. 홀콤 양은 런던으로 돌아왔을 무렵 그 간호인을 통해 남몰래 글라이드 부인을 빼내기로 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그녀는 곧장 증권 중개인에게 가서 자기가 가진 얼마 안 되는 재산을 공채에서 몽땅 팔아 치웠는데, 칠백 파운드가 채 안 되는 돈이었다. 필요하다면 이 세상에 가진 마지막 한 푼까지 동생의 자유를 사는 값으로 치르겠다고 작정한 그녀는, 이튿날 그 돈 전부를 지폐로 몸에 지니고 정신병원 담 바깥의 약속 자리로 갔다.\n\n간호인은 와 있었다. 홀콤 양은 여러 가지 사전 질문으로 조심스럽게 본론에 다가갔다. 그녀는 다른 여러 사정과 함께, 예전에 진짜 앤 캐서릭을 맡았던 간호인이 그 환자의 탈출에 대해 (잘못이 없었는데도) 책임을 지고 그 자리를 잃었다는 것을 알아냈다. 앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자가 두 번째로 없어진다면 지금 자기와 이야기하고 있는 사람에게도 같은 벌이 떨어지리라는 말이 뒤따랐다. 게다가 이 간호인에게는 자리를 지켜야 할 각별한 사정이 있었다. 그녀는 약혼한 몸이었고, 장래의 남편과 둘이서 이삼백 파운드를 모아 장사를 시작하려고 기다리는 중이었다. 간호인의 급료는 넉넉했고, 알뜰히만 하면 이태 안에 필요한 돈에서 제 몫을 보탤 수 있을 성싶었다.\n\n이 실마리를 붙들고 홀콤 양은 입을 열었다. 그녀는 앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자가 자기와 가까운 친척이며, 돌이킬 수 없는 착오로 정신병원에 들어와 있는 것이니, 두 사람을 서로에게 돌려주는 매개가 되어 준다면 간호인은 착하고 그리스도인다운 일을 하는 셈이라고 밝혔다. 반대할 말이 한마디라도 나올 겨를도 없이, 홀콤 양은 지갑에서 백 파운드짜리 지폐 넉 장을 꺼내, 감수할 위험과 잃게 될 자리에 대한 보상으로 그 여자에게 내밀었다.\n\n간호인은 도무지 믿기지 않고 놀라워서 망설였다. 홀콤 양은 그 점을 굳게 밀어붙였다.\n\n“착한 일을 하시는 겁니다.” 그녀가 거듭 말했다. “세상에서 가장 심하게 짓밟히고 가장 불행한 여자를 돕는 일이에요. 그 삯으로 결혼 밑천이 여기 있고요. 그 사람을 무사히 이리로 데려와 주시면, 제가 그 사람을 넘겨받기 전에 이 지폐 넉 장을 손에 쥐여 드리겠습니다.”\n\n“그 돈을 어떻게 얻었느냐고 정혼한 사람이 물으면 보여 줄 수 있게, 그 말씀을 적은 편지를 주시겠어요?” 여자가 물었다.\n\n“편지는 미리 써서 서명해 가지고 오겠습니다.” 홀콤 양이 대답했다.\n\n“그럼 한번 해 보겠어요.” 간호인이 말했다.\n\n“언제요?”\n\n“내일요.”\n\n두 사람은 홀콤 양이 이튿날 아침 일찍 다시 와서 나무들 사이에 몸을 숨기고 기다리되, 언제나 북쪽 담 아래의 그 한적한 자리 가까이에 머물러 있기로 서둘러 합의했다. 간호인은 자기가 나타날 시각은 정하지 못했는데, 조심하자면 형편을 보아 가며 기다려야 했기 때문이다. 그렇게 이야기를 맞추고 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n홀콤 양은 이튿날 아침 열 시가 되기 전에 약속한 편지와 약속한 지폐를 가지고 그 자리에 나가 있었다. 그녀는 한 시간 반이 넘도록 기다렸다. 그 시간이 다 갈 무렵 간호인이 글라이드 부인의 팔을 붙든 채 담 모퉁이를 돌아 빠르게 나왔다. 만나는 순간 홀콤 양은 지폐와 편지를 그 손에 넘겨주었고, 자매는 다시 하나가 되었다.\n\n간호인은 매우 사려 깊게도 제 보닛과 베일과 숄을 글라이드 부인에게 입혀 놓았다. 홀콤 양은 정신병원에서 탈출이 발각되었을 때 추적을 엉뚱한 방향으로 돌릴 방책을 일러 주려고 잠깐만 그녀를 붙들었다. 간호인은 집으로 돌아가 다른 간호인들이 듣는 데서 앤 캐서릭이 요즘 런던에서 햄프셔까지 얼마나 되느냐고 묻곤 했다는 말을 흘리고, 발각이 불가피해지는 마지막 순간까지 기다렸다가 앤이 없어졌다고 알려야 했다. 햄프셔에 관해 물었다고 하는 그 말이 정신병원 주인에게 전해지면, 그는 자기 환자가 스스로 글라이드 부인이라고 우기게 만드는 그 망상에 이끌려 블랙워터 파크로 돌아갔으리라고 짐작할 터이고, 첫 추적은 십중팔구 그쪽으로 향할 것이었다.\n\n간호인은 이 방책을 따르기로 선선히 응했는데, 정신병원에 그대로 남아 적어도 겉으로는 아무것도 모르는 척함으로써 자리를 잃는 것보다 더 나쁜 결과는 면할 방도가 되어 주었기 때문이다. 그녀는 곧바로 집으로 돌아갔고, 홀콤 양은 지체 없이 동생을 데리고 런던으로 향했다. 두 사람은 그날 오후 칼라일행 오후 기차를 탔고, 그날 밤 아무런 사고나 어려움도 없이 리머리지에 닿았다.\n\n여정의 뒷부분에서 두 사람은 객실에 단둘이 있었고, 홀콤 양은 동생의 어지럽고 약해진 기억이 되살릴 수 있는 만큼의 지난 일들을 그러모을 수 있었다. 그렇게 얻어 낸 그 무서운 음모의 이야기는 조각조각 흩어진 채로, 그 자체로도 딱하리만치 앞뒤가 닿지 않고 서로 멀찍이 떨어진 모양으로 나타났다. 드러난 것이 아무리 성기다 해도, 이 해명의 서술이 이튿날 리머리지 하우스에서 벌어진 일로 끝맺기 전에 여기 적어 두어야만 한다.\n\n글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 뒤에 일어난 일들에 대한 기억은 사우스웨스턴 철도의 런던 종착역에 닿은 데서부터 시작되었다. 그녀는 그 여행을 떠난 날짜를 미리 적어 두지 못했다. 그 중요한 날짜를 그녀의 증언으로든 마이컬슨 부인의 증언으로든 확정하겠다는 희망은 모두 접어야 했다.\n\n기차가 승강장에 닿자 글라이드 부인은 포스코 백작이 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다. 짐꾼이 문을 열자마자 그는 객실 문 앞에 서 있었다. 기차는 유난히 붐볐고 짐을 찾느라 몹시 혼잡했다. 포스코 백작이 데려온 어떤 사람이 글라이드 부인의 짐을 찾아왔다. 짐에는 그녀의 이름이 붙어 있었다. 그녀는 그때 딱히 눈여겨보지 않은 마차를 타고 백작과 단둘이 떠났다.\n\n종착역을 떠나며 그녀가 맨 먼저 물은 것은 홀콤 양에 관한 것이었다. 백작은 홀콤 양이 아직 컴벌랜드로 가지 않았다면서, 다시 생각해 보니 며칠 미리 쉬지도 않고 그토록 긴 여행을 하는 것이 현명한지 의심스러웠기 때문이라고 알려 주었다.\n\n글라이드 부인은 이어서 언니가 그때 백작의 집에 머물고 있느냐고 물었다. 그 대답에 대한 기억은 어지러웠고, 그와 관련해 또렷하게 남은 인상이라고는 지금 홀콤 양을 만나러 데려가는 길이라고 백작이 말했다는 것뿐이었다. 글라이드 부인은 런던을 아는 것이 워낙 적어서 그때 어느 거리를 지나고 있는지 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그들은 내내 거리를 벗어나지 않았고, 정원이나 나무 곁을 지나는 일도 없었다. 마차가 멈춰 선 곳은 어느 광장 뒤편의 작은 거리였는데, 그 광장에는 가게와 공공건물이 있고 사람이 많았다. 이런 기억들로 미루어 (그리고 이 점에 대해서는 글라이드 부인도 확신하고 있었으므로) 포스코 백작이 그녀를 교외 세인트 존스 우드의 제 집으로 데려간 것이 아님은 아주 분명해 보인다.\n\n두 사람은 그 집에 들어가 2층인지 3층인지의 뒤쪽 방으로 올라갔다. 짐도 조심스럽게 안으로 들여왔다. 하녀 하나가 문을 열어 주었고, 검은 수염을 기른, 보아하니 외국인인 남자가 현관에서 그들을 맞아 아주 공손하게 위층으로 길을 안내했다. 글라이드 부인이 묻자 백작은 홀콤 양이 이 집 안에 있으며 동생이 왔다는 것을 곧 알리겠다고 다짐했다. 그러고 나서 그와 그 외국인은 그녀만 방에 남겨 두고 나가 버렸다. 방은 응접실치고는 세간이 초라했고, 창은 다른 집들의 뒤편을 향하고 있었다.\n\n그곳은 유난히 조용했다. 계단을 오르내리는 발소리도 없었고, 아래층 방에서 남자들의 목소리가 웅얼웅얼 낮게 울리는 것만 들릴 뿐이었다. 혼자 남겨진 지 얼마 되지 않아 백작이 돌아와서는, 홀콤 양이 지금 쉬고 있어서 당분간 방해할 수 없다고 설명했다. 그는 어떤 신사(영국인이었다)와 함께 방에 들어왔는데, 자기 친구라며 소개하겠다고 청했다.\n\n이 묘한 소개가 있고 나서 — 글라이드 부인의 기억으로는 그동안 이름은 하나도 나오지 않았다 — 그녀는 그 낯선 사람과 단둘이 남겨졌다. 그는 더없이 정중했지만, 그녀 자신에 관한 이상한 질문을 몇 가지 던지고 그것을 물으면서 야릇한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보아 그녀를 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 했다. 잠시 있다가 그가 나가더니, 일이 분 뒤에 또 다른 낯선 사람이 — 이 사람도 영국인이었다 — 들어왔다. 이 사람 역시 포스코 백작의 또 다른 친구라고 자기를 소개하고는, 그도 제 나름대로 아주 야릇하게 그녀를 바라보며 이상한 질문을 몇 가지 던졌는데, 그녀가 기억하는 한 한 번도 이름을 불러 그녀를 부르지는 않았고, 앞사람과 마찬가지로 조금 있다가 다시 나갔다. 이쯤 되자 그녀는 제 신변이 몹시 무서워지고 언니의 일이 몹시 불안해져서, 다시 아래층으로 내려가 이 집에서 본 유일한 여자 — 문을 열어 준 그 하녀 — 에게 보호와 도움을 청해 볼까 하는 생각까지 했다.\n\n그녀가 막 의자에서 일어섰을 때 백작이 다시 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n그가 나타나자마자 그녀는 언니와 자기가 만나는 일이 얼마나 더 미뤄져야 하느냐고 초조하게 물었다. 처음에 그는 얼버무리는 대답을 했으나, 다그치자 몹시 내키지 않아 하는 기색으로 홀콤 양의 상태가 지금껏 자기가 전한 것만큼 결코 좋지 않다고 털어놓았다. 이 대답을 하는 그의 어조와 태도가 어찌나 글라이드 부인을 놀라게 했는지, 아니 그보다도 두 낯선 사람과 함께 있으면서 느꼈던 불안을 어찌나 괴롭게 부풀려 놓았는지, 별안간 아뜩함이 그녀를 덮쳐 물 한 잔을 청할 수밖에 없었다. 백작은 문간에서 물과 각성제 병을 가져오라고 소리쳤다. 둘 다 수염을 기른 그 외국인 같은 남자가 들여왔다. 글라이드 부인이 마시려 하니 물에서 어찌나 이상한 맛이 나던지 아뜩함이 더해졌고, 그녀는 서둘러 포스코 백작에게서 각성제 병을 받아 냄새를 맡았다. 그 순간 머리가 어질했다. 손에서 떨어지는 병을 백작이 받아 들었고, 그녀가 의식한 마지막 인상은 그가 그것을 다시 그녀의 콧구멍에 갖다 댔다는 것이었다.\n\n이 지점부터 그녀의 기억은 어지럽고 조각나 있어서 어떤 그럴듯한 앞뒤와도 맞춰 보기가 어려웠다.\n\n그녀 자신의 인상으로는, 그날 저녁 늦게 정신이 들어 그 집을 나섰고, (블랙워터 파크에서 미리 정해 두었던 대로) 베지 부인 댁으로 갔으며, 거기서 차를 마시고 베지 부인의 지붕 아래에서 밤을 보냈다는 것이었다. 포스코 백작이 데려간 그 집을 어떻게, 언제, 누구와 함께 나섰는지는 도무지 말하지 못했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 베지 부인 댁에 갔었다고 우겼고, 더욱 놀랍게도 루벨 부인이 제 옷을 벗기고 잠자리에 들도록 거들어 주었다고 우겼다! 베지 부인 댁에서 무슨 이야기를 나누었는지, 그 부인 말고 누구를 보았는지, 루벨 부인이 왜 그 집에 있으면서 자기를 거들었는지는 기억하지 못했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 제게 무슨 일이 있었는지에 대한 기억은 더욱 흐릿하고 미덥지 못했다.\n\n그녀에게는 포스코 백작과 함께, 그리고 또다시 시중드는 여자로 루벨 부인을 데리고 (몇 시였는지는 말하지 못했다) 마차를 타고 나갔다는 어렴풋한 생각이 있었다. 그러나 언제, 왜 베지 부인 댁을 떠났는지는 말하지 못했고, 마차가 어느 쪽으로 갔는지, 어디서 자기를 내려 주었는지, 나가 있는 내내 백작과 루벨 부인이 곁에 있었는지 아닌지도 알지 못했다. 그 슬픈 이야기의 이 대목에는 온통 공백이 있었다. 그녀는 조금이나마 전할 수 있는 인상이 하나도 없었다. 하루가 지났는지 하루보다 더 지났는지조차 알지 못했다 — 낯선 곳에서, 온통 알지 못하는 여자들에게 둘러싸인 채 문득 정신이 든 그때까지는.\n\n그곳이 바로 그 정신병원이었다. 그녀는 그곳에서 처음으로 자신이 앤 캐서릭이라는 이름으로 불리는 것을 들었고, 또 그곳에서, 이 음모의 이야기에서 마지막으로 주목할 만한 정황으로서, 자신이 앤 캐서릭의 옷을 입고 있다는 사실을 제 눈으로 확인했다. 정신병원에서 맞은 첫날 밤, 간호사는 벗겨 내는 속옷 한 점 한 점에 새겨진 표시를 그녀에게 보여 주면서, 조금도 짜증스럽거나 매정하지 않은 어조로 이렇게 말했다. “당신 옷에 당신 이름이 있잖아요, 그것 좀 보시고, 글라이드 부인이니 뭐니 하면서 더는 우리 모두를 성가시게 하지 마세요. 그분은 죽어서 묻혔고, 당신은 이렇게 살아서 멀쩡하잖아요. 자, 옷을 좀 보시라니까요! 여기 이렇게 좋은 표시용 잉크로 적혀 있고, 우리가 집에 보관해 둔 당신의 옛 물건에도 죄다 이 이름이 있을 거예요—앤 캐서릭, 인쇄한 것처럼 또렷하게요!” 그리고 실제로 그 이름은 거기 있었다. 두 사람이 리머리지 하우스에 도착한 날 밤, 홀콤 양이 동생이 입고 있던 속옷을 살펴보았을 때에도 그대로.\n\n컴벌랜드로 가는 여정에서 조심스러운 질문으로 글라이드 부인에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 기억은 이것이 전부였다—모두가 불확실했고, 그중 몇몇은 서로 어긋나기까지 했다. 홀콤 양은 정신병원에서 일어난 일에 관한 물음으로는 그녀를 몰아붙이지 않았다. 그 일을 다시 떠올리는 시련을 견디기에는 그녀의 정신이 너무나 뚜렷하게 부실했기 때문이다. 그 미치광이 수용소 주인이 스스로 인정한 바에 따라, 그녀가 그곳에 받아들여진 것이 7월 27일이라는 사실은 알려져 있었다. 그날부터 10월 15일(그녀가 구출된 날)까지 그녀는 감금되어 있었고, 앤 캐서릭과 동일인이라는 주장이 조직적으로 되풀이되었으며, 그녀의 온전한 정신은 처음부터 끝까지 사실상 부정당했다. 그보다 덜 섬세하게 균형 잡힌 정신, 그보다 덜 여리게 짜인 체질이었더라도 이런 시련 아래에서는 상하고야 말았을 것이다. 그 누구도 그 일을 겪고서 조금도 달라지지 않은 채 빠져나올 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n15일 저녁 늦게 리머리지에 닿은 홀콤 양은 글라이드 부인의 신원을 주장하는 일을 이튿날까지 미루기로 현명하게 결심했다.\n\n아침이 밝자마자 그녀는 페얼리 씨의 방으로 찾아가, 미리 할 수 있는 모든 조심과 준비를 다한 끝에, 마침내 무슨 일이 있었는지를 낱낱이 말했다. 처음의 경악과 놀람이 가라앉자마자 그는 홀콤 양이 앤 캐서릭에게 감쪽같이 속아 넘어간 것이라고 성난 어조로 단언했다. 그는 포스코 백작의 편지를 들먹였고, 앤과 세상을 떠난 자기 조카딸이 얼마나 닮았는지에 대해 그녀 자신이 그에게 들려주었던 이야기까지 들먹이면서, 애초에 자기 집으로 데려온 것 자체가 모욕이요 폭거인 미친 여자를 단 1분이라도 제 앞에 들이는 일은 결단코 못 하겠다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n홀콤 양은 방을 나왔다—분노의 첫 열기가 가라앉기를 기다렸다—곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 페얼리 씨가 조카딸을 낯선 사람 취급하며 문을 닫아걸기 전에 최소한의 인간된 도리로라도 한 번은 그 얼굴을 보아야 한다고 결론지었다—그러고는 미리 한마디 예고도 없이 글라이드 부인을 데리고 그의 방으로 갔다. 하인이 문 앞을 지키고 서서 두 사람이 들어가지 못하게 막았으나, 홀콤 양은 기어이 그를 밀치고 지나가, 동생의 손을 잡고 이끈 채 페얼리 씨 앞으로 들어섰다.\n\n뒤이어 벌어진 장면은 몇 분밖에 이어지지 않았으나 차마 옮겨 적을 수 없을 만큼 고통스러웠다—홀콤 양 자신도 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 꺼렸다. 페얼리 씨가 더할 나위 없이 단호한 말로, 자기 방에 들어온 여자를 알아보지 못하겠다고, 조카딸이 리머리지 교회 묘지에 묻혀 있다는 사실을 단 한순간이라도 의심하게 만들 만한 것은 그 얼굴에도 몸가짐에도 없다고, 그리고 해가 저물기 전에 저 여자를 집에서 내보내지 않으면 법에 호소해 자신을 보호받겠다고 선언했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하리라.\n\n페얼리 씨의 이기심과 나태와 습관처럼 굳어진 무정함을 아무리 나쁘게 본다 해도, 형의 딸을 속으로는 알아보면서 겉으로는 모른 체하는 그런 파렴치한 짓을 그가 저지를 수 있으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없었다. 홀콤 양은 인정 있고 분별 있게, 편견과 놀람이 그로 하여금 제 지각을 공정하게 발휘하지 못하도록 막았으리라는 점을 충분히 인정하고, 벌어진 일을 그렇게 헤아렸다. 그러나 그다음으로 그녀가 하인들을 시험해 보았을 때, 그들 역시 하나같이—아무리 좋게 말해도—눈앞에 세워진 부인이 자기네 젊은 여주인인지, 아니면 그녀와 닮았다는 이야기를 다들 익히 들어 온 앤 캐서릭인지 확신하지 못한다는 사실을 알게 되자, 슬픈 결론을 피할 길이 없었다. 정신병원에 갇혀 지낸 일이 글라이드 부인의 얼굴과 몸가짐에 일으킨 변화는 홀콤 양이 처음에 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심각했던 것이다. 그녀가 죽었다고 주장한 그 사악한 기만은, 그녀가 태어난 집 안에서도, 그녀와 함께 살아온 사람들 사이에서도 끝내 들통나지 않았다.\n\n사정이 이토록 급박하지만 않았다면, 그 노력을 아직 가망 없다고 포기할 필요까지는 없었을 것이다.\n\n이를테면 마침 그때 리머리지를 비우고 있던 하녀 패니가 이틀 뒤면 돌아올 예정이었으니, 우선 그녀가 알아보아 주기를 기대해 볼 여지가 있었다. 패니는 다른 하인들보다 훨씬 더 자주 여주인과 마음을 나누어 왔고, 훨씬 더 진심으로 그녀를 따랐기 때문이다. 또한 글라이드 부인을 저택이나 마을에 남몰래 머물게 하면서 건강이 조금 회복되고 정신이 조금 가라앉기를 기다릴 수도 있었을 것이다. 그녀의 기억을 다시 믿을 수 있게 되면, 그녀는 자연히 지난날의 사람과 사건을 어떤 사칭꾼도 흉내 낼 수 없는 확신과 친숙함으로 이야기할 터였고, 그러면 겉모습으로는 세우지 못한 신원의 사실이 시간의 도움을 받아 그녀 자신의 말이라는 더 확실한 시험으로 나중에라도 증명될 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 자유를 되찾은 정황은 이런 수단에 기대는 일을 아예 실행 불가능하게 만들었다. 정신병원 쪽의 추적은 잠시 햄프셔로 방향이 틀어졌을 뿐, 다음에는 어김없이 컴벌랜드 쪽으로 향할 것이었다. 도망친 여자를 찾으라고 지명된 자들이 몇 시간 전 통보만으로 리머리지 하우스에 들이닥칠 수도 있었고, 지금의 페얼리 씨 심기로 보아 그들은 그가 이 고장에서 지닌 영향력과 권위를 즉각 발휘해 자기들을 도우리라고 기대할 만했다. 글라이드 부인의 안전을 조금이라도 헤아린다면, 홀콤 양으로서는 그녀의 정당한 권리를 되찾으려는 싸움을 접고, 이제는 세상 어느 곳보다 그녀에게 가장 위험해진 장소—곧 그녀 자신의 집 근처—에서 그녀를 당장 데리고 나오는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n곧바로 런던으로 돌아가는 것이 가장 먼저, 그리고 가장 현명하게 떠오른 안전책이었다. 그 거대한 도시에서라면 두 사람의 자취를 가장 빠르고 가장 확실하게 지울 수 있을 터였다. 챙길 준비랄 것도 없었고—다정한 작별 인사를 나눌 사람도 하나 없었다. 그 잊지 못할 16일 오후, 홀콤 양은 동생을 다잡아 마지막 용기를 짜내게 했고, 떠나는 길에 잘 가라고 빌어 줄 사람 하나 없이, 두 여자는 단둘이 세상 속으로 길을 나서며 리머리지 하우스에 영영 등을 돌렸다.\n\n교회 묘지 위쪽 언덕을 지났을 때, 글라이드 부인이 어머니의 무덤을 마지막으로 한 번 보아야겠다며 되돌아가자고 고집했다. 홀콤 양은 그 결심을 꺾어 보려 했으나, 오직 이 한 가지 일에서만은 애를 써도 소용이 없었다. 그녀는 요지부동이었다. 흐릿하던 두 눈에 갑작스러운 불이 붙어, 그 위에 드리운 장막을 뚫고 번쩍였다—그때까지 그토록 맥없이 매달려 있던 다정한 팔을 붙든 야윈 손가락에 순간순간 힘이 실렸다. 나는 영혼 깊이 믿는다. 그 순간 하느님의 손이 두 사람에게 돌아갈 길을 가리켜 보이셨고, 그분이 지으신 것들 가운데 가장 죄 없고 가장 시달린 사람이 그 두려운 순간에 그것을 볼 자로 택함받았음을.\n\n두 사람은 왔던 길을 되짚어 묘지로 돌아갔고, 그 한 가지 행동으로 우리 세 사람의 앞날이 결정되었다.\n\n# III\n\n이것이 지난 일의 전말이었다—적어도 그때까지 우리가 알고 있던 한에서의 이야기였다.\n\n그 이야기를 듣고 난 뒤 두 가지 결론이 저절로 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 첫째로, 나는 그 음모의 본질이 어떤 것이었는지, 어떻게 기회를 노렸고 어떻게 정황을 주물러서 대담하고도 복잡한 범죄에 아무런 처벌이 미치지 않도록 만들었는지를 어렴풋하게나마 알아보았다. 세부 사정은 여전히 내게 수수께끼였지만, 흰옷을 입은 여인과 글라이드 부인 사이의 용모의 닮음이 얼마나 비열하게 이용되었는지는 의심할 여지 없이 분명했다. 앤 캐서릭이 글라이드 부인 행세로 포스코 백작의 집에 들여보내졌다는 것도 분명했고, 글라이드 부인이 죽은 여자의 자리를 대신해 정신병원에 갇혔다는 것도 분명했다. 그리고 그 뒤바꿈은 아무 죄 없는 사람들—의사와 두 하인은 확실히, 그리고 십중팔구 정신병원의 소유주까지—을 범죄의 공범으로 만들도록 꾸며져 있었다.\n\n두 번째 결론은 첫 번째 결론에서 필연적으로 따라 나왔다. 우리 세 사람은 포스코 백작과 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게서 어떠한 자비도 기대할 수 없었다. 음모의 성공은 그 두 사람에게 삼만 파운드라는 뚜렷한 이득을 안겨 주었다—한 사람에게 이만 파운드, 다른 한 사람에게는 아내를 통해 만 파운드였다. 그들에게는 그 이득이 있었고, 그 밖의 이해관계도 있었으므로, 자기들의 죄가 폭로되지 않게 하려고 무슨 짓이든 할 것이었다. 그들은 온갖 수단을 다 뒤져 보고, 어떤 희생도 마다하지 않고, 어떤 배신도 서슴지 않으면서, 자기들의 희생자가 숨어 있는 곳을 찾아내어 그녀를 이 세상에 남은 단 두 사람의 벗—매리언 홀콤과 나에게서 떼어 놓으려 할 것이었다.\n\n이 엄중한 위험—날마다 시시각각 우리에게 점점 더 가까이 다가올지 모르는 위험—에 대한 인식이야말로 우리가 숨어 지낼 곳을 정할 때 나를 이끈 유일한 기준이었다. 나는 런던의 저 동쪽 끝, 거리에서 빈둥거리며 이리저리 둘러보는 한가한 사람이 가장 적은 곳을 골랐다. 나는 가난하고 사람이 빽빽한 동네를 골랐다—우리 주위의 남녀들이 먹고살기 위해 힘겹게 싸울수록, 그들 틈에 끼어든 낯선 사람을 눈여겨볼 시간이나 수고를 들일 위험이 적어지기 때문이었다. 내가 노린 것은 그런 커다란 이점들이었지만, 우리가 자리 잡은 곳은 그에 못지않게 중요한 또 다른 점에서도 우리에게 이로웠다. 우리는 내 손으로 하는 나날의 일감으로 값싸게 살아갈 수 있었고, 가진 돈은 한 푼도 남김없이 아껴 두었다가 그 목적, 저 정당한 목적—파렴치한 악행을 바로잡는 일—에 쓸 수 있었다. 나는 그때부터 처음부터 끝까지 그 목적만을 한결같이 눈앞에 두고 있었다.\n\n일주일 만에 매리언 홀콤과 나는 우리의 새로운 삶을 어떤 방향으로 이끌어 갈지 정해 놓았다.\n\n그 집에는 다른 세입자가 없었고, 우리는 가게를 지나지 않고도 드나들 수 있는 길이 있었다. 나는 적어도 당분간은 매리언도 로라도 내가 함께 있지 않으면 문밖으로 한 걸음도 나서지 않도록, 그리고 내가 집을 비운 사이에는 어떤 구실로도 방 안에 아무도 들이지 않도록 정해 두었다. 이 규칙을 세워 놓고 나서 나는 예전에 알고 지내던 친구—큰 일감을 맡아 하는 목판 조각가—를 찾아가 일자리를 부탁하면서, 동시에 내 이름을 드러내지 않고 지내야 할 사정이 있다고 말했다.\n\n그는 대뜸 내가 빚을 졌다고 단정하고는 으레 하는 말투로 안됐다는 뜻을 표하더니, 힘닿는 데까지 나를 돕겠다고 약속했다. 나는 그의 잘못된 짐작을 굳이 바로잡지 않고, 그가 주는 일을 받았다. 그는 내 솜씨와 내 성실함을 믿을 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 나에게는 그가 원하는 것, 곧 꾸준함과 손놀림의 능숙함이 있었고, 벌이는 얼마 되지 않았지만 우리가 살아가는 데는 넉넉했다. 그 점을 확실히 믿을 수 있게 되자마자 매리언 홀콤과 나는 각자 가진 것을 한데 모았다. 그녀에게는 자기 재산에서 남은 이삼백 파운드가 있었고, 나에게는 영국을 떠나기 전에 그림 교습 자리를 넘기고 받은 돈에서 남은 것이 거의 그만큼 있었다. 둘이 합치니 사백 파운드가 넘었다. 나는 이 얼마 안 되는 재산을 은행에 맡겨 두었다. 내가 기어이 시작하기로 마음먹은, 그리고 도와줄 사람을 아무도 찾지 못하면 나 혼자서라도 밀고 나갈 저 은밀한 조사와 탐문에 드는 비용으로 쓰기 위해서였다. 우리는 주간 지출을 마지막 한 푼까지 따져 가며 계산했고, 로라를 위한 일이 아니면, 로라를 위해서가 아니면 그 작은 자금에는 결코 손을 대지 않았다.\n\n집안일은, 우리가 낯선 사람을 곁에 두어도 좋다고 여겼더라면 하녀가 맡았을 그 일은, 첫날부터 매리언 홀콤이 제 몫이라는 듯 떠맡았다. “여자의 손으로 할 수 있는 일이라면,” 그녀가 말했다. “아침부터 밤까지 이 손으로 다 하겠어요.” 그녀가 내밀어 보인 두 손은 떨리고 있었다. 몸을 지키려고 걸친 초라하고 수수한 옷의 소매를 걷어 올리자, 여윈 팔이 지나간 나날의 서글픈 사연을 그대로 말해 주었다. 그러나 이 여인의 꺼지지 않는 기상은 아직도 그 안에서 환하게 타오르고 있었다. 그녀가 나를 바라볼 때 커다란 눈물방울이 눈에 그렁그렁 차올라 천천히 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것이 보였다. 그녀는 예전의 그 기운을 살짝 되살려 눈물을 훔쳐 내고는, 옛날의 밝은 기분을 어렴풋이 되비추는 미소를 지었다. “내 용기를 의심하지 말아요, 월터.” 그녀가 호소하듯 말했다. “우는 건 내 약한 구석이지 내가 아니에요. 내가 못 이기면 집안일이 그 약한 구석을 이겨 줄 거예요.” 그리고 그녀는 그 말을 지켰다. 저녁에 우리가 마주 앉고 그녀가 자리에 앉아 쉴 무렵에는 이미 승리를 거둔 뒤였다. 그녀의 크고 흔들림 없는 검은 눈이 지난날의 그 빛나는 결연함을 번뜩이며 나를 바라보았다. “아직 완전히 무너지지는 않았어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “내 몫의 일을 맡길 만은 하다고요.” 내가 대답하기도 전에 그녀는 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. “그리고 내 몫의 위험과 위기를 맡길 만도 하고요. 때가 오거든 그걸 잊지 말아요!”\n\n때가 왔을 때 나는 그 말을 잊지 않았다.\n\n시월이 끝나 갈 무렵에는 벌써 우리의 나날이 정해진 방향을 잡았고, 우리 세 사람은 숨어 지내는 곳에서 완전히 고립되어 있었다. 마치 우리가 사는 집이 무인도이고, 사방으로 뻗은 거대한 거리의 그물망과 우리를 둘러싼 수천의 동포들이 끝없는 바다의 물결이라도 되는 것 같았다. 이제 나는 앞으로 어떤 계획으로 움직여야 할지, 그리고 퍼시벌 경과 백작을 상대로 다가올 싸움에 대비해 처음부터 어떻게 하면 가장 든든하게 무장할 수 있을지를 궁리할 여유를 조금은 낼 수 있었다.\n\n나는 로라의 신원을 증명하는 데 내가 그녀를 알아본다는 사실이나 매리언이 그녀를 알아본다는 사실에 기대려던 희망을 모두 버렸다. 우리가 그녀를 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하지 않았더라면, 그 사랑이 우리 안에 심어 놓은 직감이 어떤 추론보다도 훨씬 확실하고 어떤 관찰보다도 훨씬 예리하지 않았더라면, 우리조차 그녀를 처음 보았을 때 망설였을지도 모른다.\n\n지나간 고통과 공포가 겉모습에 남긴 변화는, 앤 캐서릭과 그녀 사이의 그 치명적인 닮음을 무섭도록, 거의 절망적일 만큼 짙게 만들어 놓았다. 리머리지 하우스에 머물던 무렵의 일을 적은 내 기록에서, 나는 두 사람을 직접 살펴본 바에 따라, 그 닮음이 전체적으로 볼 때는 그토록 놀라웠으면서도 하나하나 뜯어보면 여러 중요한 대목에서 어긋난다는 것을 적어 두었다. 그 시절이었다면 두 사람을 나란히 세워 놓고 보았을 때 어느 누구도 쌍둥이를 두고 흔히 그러듯 한 사람을 다른 사람으로 잠시라도 잘못 볼 수는 없었을 것이다. 이제는 그렇게 말할 수 없었다. 한때 나는 지나가는 생각으로나마 로라 페얼리의 앞날에 슬픔과 고통을 결부시켰다는 것만으로도 스스로를 나무랐었는데, 그 슬픔과 고통이 이제 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움 위에 더러운 자국을 남겨 놓았다. 그리고 한때 관념으로만 보고도 몸서리쳤던 그 치명적인 닮음이, 이제는 내 두 눈앞에서 제 존재를 주장하는 실제의 살아 있는 닮음이 되어 있었다. 우리처럼 그녀를 바라볼 수 없는 낯선 사람들이나 지인들, 심지어 벗들까지도, 정신병원에서 구해 낸 첫 무렵에 그녀를 보았더라면 그녀가 예전에 보았던 그 로라 페얼리인지 의심했을 것이고, 그 의심을 나무랄 수도 없었을 것이다.\n\n처음에는 우리에게 도움이 되리라 믿었던 마지막 남은 가능성—어떤 사기꾼도 알 리 없는 사람들과 사건들에 대한 그녀의 기억에 호소하는 방법—은, 이후의 서글픈 경험이 시험해 본 끝에 가망 없는 것으로 드러났다. 매리언과 내가 그녀를 대하며 기울인 자잘한 조심 하나하나, 약해지고 흔들린 정신의 힘을 천천히 북돋우고 다잡으려고 시도한 자잘한 처방 하나하나가, 그 자체로 그녀의 마음을 저 어지럽고 끔찍한 과거로 되돌리는 위험에 대한 새로운 항변이었다.\n\n우리가 감히 그녀에게 떠올려 보라고 권할 수 있었던 지난날의 일이라고는, 내가 처음 리머리지에 가서 그녀에게 그림을 가르치던 그 행복한 시절의 사소하고 소소한 집안일들뿐이었다. 이별하던 날 아침에 그녀가 나에게 주었고 그 뒤로 한 번도 내 몸에서 떨어져 본 적 없던 정자 그림을 보여 주며 그 기억들을 일깨운 날은, 우리의 첫 희망이 태어난 날이었다. 다정하게, 그리고 조금씩, 옛날의 산책과 마차 나들이에 대한 기억이 그녀 안에서 밝아 왔고, 지치고 시들어 가던 가엾은 두 눈이 새로운 관심을 담고, 머뭇거리는 사려를 담고 매리언과 나를 바라보았다. 그 순간부터 우리는 그것을 소중히 여기며 꺼지지 않게 지켰다. 나는 그녀에게 작은 물감 상자 하나와, 우리가 처음 만난 날 아침에 그녀의 손에 들려 있던 옛 사생첩과 같은 사생첩 하나를 사다 주었다. 다시 한 번—아, 나에게 다시 한 번!—일에서 아껴 낸 짬을 내어, 흐릿한 런던의 빛 아래, 초라한 런던의 방에서, 나는 그녀 곁에 앉아 머뭇거리는 붓끝을 이끌고 힘없는 손을 거들었다. 나날이 나는 그 새로운 관심을 북돋우고 또 북돋워, 마침내 그것이 그녀의 텅 빈 삶 속에서 제자리를 잡게 했다. 마침내 그녀는 자기 그림을 생각하고 그 이야기를 하고, 혼자서 참을성 있게 연습할 수 있게 되었다. 내가 격려할 때의 천진한 기쁨, 자기 실력이 느는 데서 오는 커져 가는 즐거움—잃어버린 삶과 잃어버린 행복에 속해 있던 그것들이 어렴풋이 되비치기 시작했다.\n\n우리는 이런 소박한 방법으로 그녀의 마음을 천천히 도왔다. 날씨 좋은 날이면 우리 둘이 그녀를 사이에 두고 가까운 곳의 조용하고 오래된 시내 광장으로 산책을 나갔다. 그녀를 어리둥절하게 하거나 놀라게 할 것이 아무것도 없는 곳이었다. 우리는 은행에 맡긴 자금에서 몇 파운드를 덜어 내어 그녀에게 필요한 포도주와 몸을 돋우는 좋은 음식을 마련해 주었다. 저녁이면 아이들이 하는 카드놀이로, 나를 고용한 조각가에게서 빌려 온 판화가 가득한 그림책으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 이런 자잘한 배려며 그와 비슷한 자잘한 일들로 우리는 그녀를 가라앉히고 다잡아 주었고, 세월과 보살핌, 그리고 그녀를 결코 소홀히 하지도 단념하지도 않는 사랑에 힘입어 될 수 있는 한 밝게 온갖 것을 바랐다. 그러나 그녀를 은둔과 안식에서 매정하게 끌어내는 일—낯선 사람들이나, 낯선 사람이나 다름없는 지인들 앞에 그녀를 세우는 일—우리가 그토록 조심스레 잠재워 놓은 지난 삶의 괴로운 기억들을 다시 흔들어 깨우는 일, 그것만은 그녀 자신을 위한 일이라 해도 감히 할 수 없었다. 어떤 희생을 치러야 하든, 얼마나 길고 지치고 가슴 미어지는 지연을 겪어야 하든, 그녀에게 가해진 그 악행은, 사람의 힘으로 붙들어 볼 수 있는 것이라면, 그녀가 모르는 채로 그녀의 도움 없이 바로잡혀야 했다.\n\n이렇게 마음을 정하고 나니, 다음으로는 첫 모험을 어떻게 감행할 것인지, 첫 조치를 무엇으로 삼을 것인지 결정해야 했다.\n\n매리언과 의논한 끝에, 나는 우선 모을 수 있는 한 많은 사실을 그러모으고, 그런 다음 카일 씨(우리가 믿을 수 있다고 알고 있는 사람이었다)에게 조언을 구하여, 무엇보다 먼저 법적인 구제가 우리 손이 닿는 범위 안에 정말로 있는지를 그에게서 확인하기로 했다. 어떤 종류든 믿을 만한 도움을 얻어 우리 처지를 튼튼히 할 가망이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한, 나 혼자만의 힘에 그녀의 앞날 전부를 거는 것은 로라의 이익을 위해서 할 일이 아니었다.\n\n내가 맨 처음 손을 댄 정보의 원천은 매리언 홀콤이 블랙워터 파크에서 쓴 일기였다. 이 일기에는 나에 관한 대목들이 있었는데, 그녀는 내가 그것을 보지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그래서 그녀가 원고를 나에게 읽어 주었고, 나는 그녀가 읽어 나가는 동안 필요한 것을 받아 적었다. 이 일에 쓸 수 있는 시간이라고는 밤늦게까지 자지 않고 앉아 있는 때뿐이었다. 사흘 밤을 이 일에 바쳤고, 그것으로 매리언이 나에게 들려줄 수 있는 모든 것을 손에 넣기에 충분했다.\n\n다음으로 내가 한 일은, 의심을 사지 않으면서 다른 사람들에게서 얻어 낼 수 있는 만큼 증거를 더 모으는 것이었다. 나는 로라가 그곳에서 잤다고 하는 인상이 맞는지 아닌지 확인하러 직접 베지 부인을 찾아갔다. 이 경우에는 베지 부인의 나이와 병약함을 헤아려서, 그리고 그 뒤 같은 종류의 모든 경우에는 조심스러움을 위해서, 나는 우리의 실제 처지를 비밀에 부쳤고 로라를 언제나 “고인이 된 글라이드 부인”이라고 부르도록 신경 썼다.\n\n내 물음에 대한 베지 부인의 대답은 내가 전부터 품고 있던 우려를 확인해 줄 뿐이었다. 로라가 옛 벗의 지붕 아래에서 하룻밤을 묵겠다고 편지를 쓴 것은 분명했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 집 근처에도 온 적이 없었다.\n\n이 경우에, 그리고 내가 우려하기로는 이 밖의 다른 경우들에서도, 그녀의 마음은 하려고 마음먹었을 뿐인 일을 실제로 한 일인 양 잘못된 빛깔로 어지럽게 떠올리고 있었다. 그녀가 저도 모르게 자기 말을 뒤집는 것은 이렇게 설명하면 쉽게 납득할 수 있었지만, 그것은 심각한 결과를 낳을 만한 일이었다. 그것은 출발선의 문턱에서 발을 헛디딘 것이었고, 우리에게 치명적으로 불리하게 작용하는 증거의 흠이었다.\n\n이어서 내가 로라가 블랙워터 파크에서 베지 부인에게 쓴 편지를 달라고 하자, 편지는 봉투 없이 건네졌다. 봉투는 휴지통에 던져졌다가 오래전에 없어져 버렸던 것이다. 편지 자체에는 날짜가 적혀 있지 않았다. 요일조차 없었다. 거기에는 이런 몇 줄만이 담겨 있었다.—“가장 그리운 베지 부인, 저는 지금 몹시 괴롭고 불안합니다. 내일 밤 부인 댁으로 찾아가 하룻밤 재워 달라고 부탁드리게 될지도 모르겠어요. 무슨 일인지는 이 편지에 적을 수가 없습니다—들킬까 봐 어찌나 두려운지 아무 데도 마음을 붙일 수가 없어요. 부디 집에 계셔서 저를 맞아 주세요. 부인께 천 번의 입맞춤을 드리고, 모든 것을 다 말씀드리겠습니다. 부인을 사랑하는 로라 올림.” 이 몇 줄에 무슨 도움이 있었겠는가? 아무것도 없었다.\n\n베지 부인 댁에서 돌아온 뒤, 나는 매리언에게 마이컬슨 부인 앞으로 편지를 쓰라고 일렀다(내가 지키던 것과 같은 조심을 지키면서 말이다). 원한다면 포스코 백작의 처신에 대한 대략의 의혹을 내비쳐도 좋고, 진실을 위해서 그동안의 일을 있는 그대로 적어 보내 달라고 그 가정부에게 부탁하라고 했다. 일주일 만에 도착한 답장을 기다리는 동안, 나는 세인트존스우드의 의사를 찾아가, 카일 씨가 미처 알아볼 시간이 없었던 홀콤 양 동생의 마지막 병세에 대해 되도록 자세한 사정을 알아 오라고 홀콤 양이 보내서 왔노라고 나를 소개했다. 구드리크 선생의 도움으로 나는 사망 진단서 사본 한 통과, 시신을 염하도록 고용되었던 여자(제인 굴드)와의 면담을 얻어 냈다. 이 사람을 통해 나는 하녀 헤스터 핀혼과 연락할 방도도 알아냈다. 그녀는 얼마 전 주인마님과 다투는 바람에 일자리를 그만두고, 굴드 부인이 아는 근처 사람들 집에 머물고 있었다. 이런 식으로 나는 가정부의 이야기, 의사의 이야기, 제인 굴드의 이야기, 헤스터 핀혼의 이야기를 여기 이 지면에 실린 그대로 손에 넣었다.\n\n이 문서들이 제공한 추가 증거로 무장하고 나니 카일 씨와 상의할 준비가 충분히 되었다고 생각되어, 매리언은 그에 따라 편지를 써서 그에게 내 이름을 알리고 내가 사사로운 용무로 그를 만나 뵙고 싶은 날짜와 시각을 밝혔다.\n\n그날 아침에는 여느 때처럼 로라를 데리고 산책을 나갔다가 돌아와 그녀가 조용히 그림 앞에 자리 잡는 것을 지켜볼 만한 시간이 넉넉했다. 내가 방을 나서려고 일어서자 그녀는 얼굴에 새로운 불안을 띤 채 나를 올려다보았고, 그 손가락은 예전 버릇대로 탁자 위의 붓과 연필을 미덥지 못한 듯 만지작거리기 시작했다.\n\n“아직 제가 싫증 나신 건 아니죠?” 그녀가 말했다. “제가 싫증 나서 나가시는 건 아니죠? 더 잘하도록 애써 볼게요—얼른 나으려고 애써 볼게요. 월터, 이렇게 창백하고 여위고 그림 배우는 것도 이렇게 굼뜬 저를, 예전만큼 좋아하시나요?”\n\n그녀는 아이가 말하듯이 말했고, 아이가 제 속을 내보이듯이 나에게 제 생각을 내보였다. 나는 몇 분을 더 머물렀다—그녀가 지금 나에게는 지난 어느 때보다도 더 소중하다는 말을 해 주려고 머물렀다. “다시 건강해지도록 애써 봐요.” 나는 그녀의 마음속에서 밝아 오는 것이 보이는 그 새로운 앞날의 희망을 북돋우며 말했다. “다시 건강해지도록 애써 봐요, 매리언을 위해서 그리고 나를 위해서.”\n\n“네.” 그녀가 그림으로 돌아가면서 혼잣말처럼 말했다. “애써야지, 두 사람 다 나를 이렇게 좋아해 주니까.” 그러다 그녀는 문득 다시 고개를 들었다. “오래 나가 계시면 안 돼요! 월터, 당신이 곁에서 도와주지 않으면 저는 그림을 못 그리는걸요.”\n\n“곧 돌아올게요, 그대여—곧 돌아와서 얼마나 잘 그렸나 볼게요.”\n\n내 목소리는 나도 모르게 조금 떨렸다. 나는 억지로 몸을 떼어 방을 나섰다. 그때는 자제심을 놓아 버릴 때가 아니었다. 그 자제심이 그날이 저물기 전에 내가 절실히 필요로 할 때 아직 나를 도와줄지도 몰랐으니까.\n\n문을 열면서 나는 매리언에게 계단까지 따라 나오라고 손짓했다. 내가 거리에 드러내 놓고 모습을 보인 결과로 언젠가는 벌어질 수 있겠다 싶은 일에 대비하도록 그녀를 미리 일러 둘 필요가 있었다.\n\n“아마도 몇 시간이면 돌아올 거예요.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 늘 그랬듯이 내가 없는 동안 문 안으로 아무도 들이지 않도록 조심해 주고요. 하지만 만에 하나 무슨 일이 생기면——”\n\n“무슨 일이 생길 수 있는데요?” 그녀가 얼른 말을 가로챘다. “월터, 위험이 있다면 솔직히 말해 줘요. 그러면 내가 어떻게 맞설지 알 테니까요.”\n\n“유일한 위험은,” 내가 대답했다. “퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 로라가 빠져나왔다는 소식을 듣고 런던으로 불려 왔을지도 모른다는 것이죠. 내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 그가 나를 감시하게 했다는 것, 그래서 나는 그를 몰라도 그는 아마 내 얼굴을 알고 있으리라는 것은 당신도 아시잖아요?”\n\n그녀는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 걱정스러운 얼굴로 말없이 나를 바라보았다. 나는 그녀가 우리를 위협하는 그 심각한 위험을 알아차렸음을 알았다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경 본인이든 그가 부리는 사람들이든, 이렇게 빨리 런던에서 다시 나를 볼 것 같지는 않아요.” 내가 말했다. “하지만 뜻밖의 일이 생길 가능성이 아주 없지는 않죠. 그런 경우라도 내가 오늘 밤 돌아오지 못하더라도 놀라지 말고, 로라가 뭐라고 묻거든 당신이 지어낼 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 핑계로 답해 주겠어요? 감시받고 있다고 의심할 만한 낌새가 조금이라도 있으면, 어떤 첩자도 나를 따라 이 집까지 오지 못하도록 단단히 조심하겠어요. 아무리 늦어지더라도 내가 돌아오리라는 걸 의심하지 말아요, 매리언—그리고 아무것도 두려워하지 말고요.”\n\n“아무것도요!” 그녀가 단단하게 대답했다. “월터, 도와줄 사람이 여자 하나뿐이라는 걸 후회하게 만들지는 않겠어요.” 그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 나를 조금 더 붙들었다. “조심해요!” 그녀는 걱정스럽게 내 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. “조심해요!”\n\n나는 그녀를 남겨 두고, 진상을 밝힐 길을 닦으러 나섰다—변호사의 문간에서 시작되는, 어둡고도 앞을 알 수 없는 그 길을.\n\n# IV\n\n챈서리 레인에 있는 길모어 앤드 카일 법률 사무소로 가는 길에는 조금이라도 중요하달 만한 일이 하나도 일어나지 않았다.\n\n내 명함이 카일 씨에게 들어가는 동안, 진작 떠올리지 못한 것이 몹시 후회스러운 한 가지 생각이 문득 머리를 스쳤다. 매리언의 일기에서 얻은 정보로 미루어 볼 때, 포스코 백작이 블랙워터 파크에서 카일 씨에게 보낸 그녀의 첫 번째 편지를 뜯어보았고 아내를 시켜 두 번째 편지를 가로챘다는 것은 확실한 사실이었다. 그러니 그는 이 사무소의 주소를 훤히 알고 있었을 터이고, 로라가 정신병원에서 빠져나온 뒤 매리언이 조언과 도움을 구하려 든다면 다시 한 번 카일 씨의 경험에 기대리라고 자연스레 짐작했을 것이다. 그렇다면 챈서리 레인의 이 사무소야말로 그와 퍼시벌 경이 가장 먼저 감시를 붙일 곳이었고, 내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 나를 미행하던 바로 그자들이 그 일에 뽑혔다면 내가 돌아왔다는 사실은 십중팔구 바로 그날로 드러날 것이었다. 거리에서 얼굴이 알려질 가능성은 대략 헤아려 보았으나, 이 사무소에 얽힌 특별한 위험은 이 순간까지 한 번도 생각나지 않았다. 이 잘못된 판단을 돌이키기에는 이미 늦었다. 미리 은밀히 정해 둔 어딘가에서 변호사를 만나도록 손을 써 두었더라면 하고 바라기에도 늦었다. 나는 다만 챈서리 레인을 나설 때 조심하기로, 그리고 어떤 일이 있어도 곧장 집으로 돌아가지는 않기로 마음먹는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n몇 분을 기다린 뒤 나는 카일 씨의 집무실로 안내되었다. 그는 창백하고 여윈, 조용하고 침착한 사람으로, 눈매가 몹시 주의 깊고 목소리가 아주 낮았으며 태도에는 감정을 드러내는 구석이 조금도 없었다. 내가 보기에 낯선 사람에게 선뜻 동정을 내주는 인물도 아니었고, 직업적인 평정을 흐트러뜨리기가 결코 쉬운 사람도 아니었다. 내 목적에 이보다 더 알맞은 사람은 찾기 어려웠을 것이다. 그가 어떤 판단을 내리기로 하고 그 판단이 우리에게 유리하다면, 우리 주장의 힘은 그 순간부터 증명된 것이나 다름없었다.\n\n“저를 이리로 오게 한 용건에 들어가기 전에,” 내가 말했다. “미리 말씀드려 둘 것이 있습니다, 카일 씨. 아무리 짧게 말씀드려도 시간이 조금 걸릴지 모릅니다.”\n\n“제 시간은 홀콤 양이 쓰시라고 있는 것입니다.” 그가 대답했다. “그분의 이해가 걸린 일이라면 저는 제 동업자를 직무상으로만이 아니라 개인적으로도 대리합니다. 그분이 실무에서 손을 뗄 때 그렇게 해 달라고 부탁하셨으니까요.”\n\n“길모어 씨는 지금 영국에 계신지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“아닙니다, 독일에서 친척들과 지내고 계십니다. 건강은 좋아지셨지만 돌아오실 시기는 아직 정해지지 않았습니다.”\n\n이런 몇 마디 인사말을 주고받는 동안 그는 앞에 놓인 서류들을 뒤적이고 있었고, 이제 그 속에서 봉인된 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다. 나는 그가 그 편지를 내게 건네려는 줄 알았으나, 마음을 바꾼 듯 그것을 따로 탁자 위에 놓고는 의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉아, 내가 할 말을 잠자코 기다렸다.\n\n나는 서두를 꾸미느라 한순간도 허비하지 않고 곧바로 이야기에 들어가, 이 지면에 이미 적어 온 사건들을 그에게 남김없이 알렸다.\n\n뼛속까지 변호사인 사람이었건만 나는 그의 직업적인 평정을 흔들어 놓았다. 미처 억누르지 못한 불신과 놀람의 말이 내 이야기가 끝나기도 전에 몇 번이나 나를 가로막았다. 그래도 나는 끝까지 밀고 나갔고, 이야기를 마치자마자 대담하게 그 중대한 물음 하나를 던졌다.\n\n“어떻게 보십니까, 카일 씨?”\n\n그는 우선 평정을 되찾을 시간을 갖지도 않고 어떤 대답에 자신을 묶어 두기에는 지나치게 신중한 사람이었다.\n\n“제 견해를 말씀드리기 전에,” 그가 말했다. “몇 가지 질문으로 바닥을 정리하도록 허락해 주셔야겠습니다.”\n\n그는 질문을 던졌다. 날카롭고 미심쩍어하며 믿지 않는 질문들이었고, 그것이 이어지는 동안 나는 그가 나를 망상에 빠진 사람으로 여기고 있다는 것을, 그리고 홀콤 양의 소개가 없었더라면 내가 교묘하게 꾸민 사기를 저지르려는 것은 아닌지 의심했을지도 모른다는 것을 똑똑히 알 수 있었다.\n\n“제가 진실을 말했다고 믿으십니까, 카일 씨?” 그가 나를 다 캐묻고 났을 때 내가 물었다.\n\n“선생 자신의 확신에 관한 한, 진실을 말씀하셨다고 확신합니다.” 그가 대답했다. “저는 홀콤 양을 대단히 존경하며, 따라서 그분이 이런 일에 중재를 맡길 만큼 믿으시는 신사분을 존중할 온갖 이유가 있습니다. 원하신다면 한 걸음 더 나아가, 예의를 위해서 그리고 논의를 위해서, 글라이드 부인이 살아 있는 사람으로 그 자리에 있다는 것이 홀콤 양과 선생께는 증명된 사실이라고 인정하겠습니다. 그러나 선생께서는 법률적인 견해를 들으러 저를 찾아오셨습니다. 변호사로서, 오직 변호사로서 말씀드리는 것이 제 의무이니 말씀드립니다. 하트라이트 씨, 선생께는 소송이 될 만한 것이 그림자만큼도 없습니다.”\n\n“심하게 말씀하시는군요, 카일 씨.”\n\n“분명하게 말씀드리려고도 애써 보겠습니다. 글라이드 부인이 사망했다는 증거는 겉으로 보기에 명확하고 흠잡을 데가 없습니다. 그분이 포스코 백작의 집에 왔고, 병이 들었고, 죽었다는 것을 증명하는 고모의 증언이 있습니다. 사망을 증명하고 그것이 자연스러운 정황에서 일어났음을 보여 주는 의사 진단서의 증언이 있습니다. 리머리지에서 장례가 치러졌다는 사실이 있고, 무덤의 비문이 그렇게 단언하고 있습니다. 이것이 선생께서 뒤엎으려는 사건입니다. 죽어서 묻힌 사람이 글라이드 부인이 아니라는 선생 쪽의 주장을 뒷받침할 증거로는 무엇이 있습니까? 선생 진술의 요점들을 하나하나 훑어보며 그것들이 얼마나 값이 나가는지 봅시다. 홀콤 양이 어느 사립 정신병원에 가서 그곳의 어떤 여자 환자를 봅니다. 앤 캐서릭이라는 이름의, 글라이드 부인과 놀랍도록 닮은 여자가 그 병원에서 달아났다는 것은 알려진 사실입니다. 지난 7월에 그곳에 들어온 사람은 되잡혀 온 앤 캐서릭으로서 받아들여졌다는 것도 알려진 사실입니다. 그 여자를 데려온 신사가 죽은 조카를 사칭하려 드는 것이 그 여자 광증의 일부라고 페얼리 씨에게 일러 주었다는 것도 알려진 사실이고, 그 여자가 병원 안에서 자기가 글라이드 부인이라고 거듭 주장했다는 것도, 그리고 그곳에서 아무도 그 말을 믿지 않았다는 것도 알려진 사실입니다. 이것이 모두 사실입니다. 여기에 맞세울 것으로 선생께는 무엇이 있습니까? 홀콤 양이 그 여자를 알아보았다는 것뿐인데, 그 뒤에 일어난 일들이 그 확인을 무효로 만들거나 뒤집고 있습니다. 홀콤 양은 병원 주인에게 자기 동생이 맞다고 주장하고 법적인 절차를 밟아 그 여자를 구해 냈습니까? 아닙니다, 몰래 간호사를 매수해 달아나게 했습니다. 환자가 그런 미심쩍은 방식으로 풀려나 페얼리 씨에게 데려가졌을 때 그가 그 여자를 알아보았습니까? 조카가 죽었다는 그의 믿음이 한순간이라도 흔들렸습니까? 아닙니다. 하인들은 알아보았습니까? 아닙니다. 그 여자를 그 근처에 머무르게 하여 스스로 신원을 주장하게 하고 뒤이은 절차의 시험을 견디게 했습니까? 아닙니다, 남몰래 런던으로 데려갔습니다. 그동안 선생 또한 그 여자를 알아보셨지만, 선생은 친척이 아닙니다. 집안의 오랜 친구조차 아닙니다. 하인들은 선생의 말을 부인하고, 페얼리 씨는 홀콤 양의 말을 부인하며, 글라이드 부인이라는 그 사람은 스스로 제 말을 뒤집습니다. 그 사람은 런던의 어느 집에서 밤을 지냈다고 말합니다. 선생 자신의 증언은 그 사람이 그 집 근처에도 간 적이 없음을 보여 주고, 선생 자신이 인정하시기를 그 사람의 정신 상태 때문에 어디에도 내세워 조사를 받게 하거나 스스로 말하게 할 수 없다고 하십니다. 시간을 아끼려 양쪽의 자잘한 증거들은 넘어가고 여쭙겠습니다. 이 사건이 지금 법정으로, 사실을 상식이 보이는 대로 받아들일 수밖에 없는 배심 앞으로 간다면, 선생의 증거는 어디에 있습니까?”\n\n나는 그에게 대답하기 전에 잠시 기다리며 마음을 가다듬어야 했다. 로라의 이야기와 매리언의 이야기가 남의 눈을 통해 내 앞에 펼쳐진 것은 그때가 처음이었고, 우리 앞길을 가로막은 무서운 장애물들이 제 본모습을 드러낸 것도 그때가 처음이었다.\n\n“의심할 여지가 없습니다.” 내가 말했다. “말씀하신 대로의 사실들이 우리에게 불리하게 보인다는 것은요. 하지만——”\n\n“하지만 그 사실들은 해명해서 없앨 수 있다고 생각하시는 거지요.” 카일 씨가 말을 받았다. “그 점에 관해 제 경험이 알려 준 바를 말씀드리겠습니다. 영국의 배심은 겉으로 드러난 단순한 사실과 그 밑에 깔린 긴 설명 가운데 하나를 골라야 할 때면 언제나 설명보다 사실을 택합니다. 이를테면 글라이드 부인이, 논의를 위해 선생께서 대리하시는 그분을 그렇게 부르겠습니다만, 어느 집에서 잤다고 말하는데 그 집에서 자지 않았다는 것이 증명됩니다. 선생께서는 그분의 정신 상태를 파고들어 거기서 형이상학적인 결론을 끌어내어 이 사정을 설명하십니다. 저는 그 결론이 틀렸다고 말하는 것이 아닙니다. 다만 배심은 선생께서 내놓으실 수 있는 그 어떤 모순의 이유보다도 그분이 스스로 제 말을 뒤집었다는 사실 쪽을 택하리라는 말씀입니다.”\n\n“하지만 인내와 노력을 다한다면,” 내가 힘주어 말했다. “증거를 더 찾아내는 것이 가능하지 않겠습니까? 홀콤 양과 저에게는 수백 파운드가——”\n\n그는 반쯤 억누른 연민의 눈으로 나를 보며 고개를 저었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 이 일을 선생 자신의 관점에서 한번 생각해 보십시오.” 그가 말했다. “퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 포스코 백작에 대한 선생의 판단이 옳다면, 저는 인정하지 않습니다만 유념해 두십시오, 새 증거를 얻으려는 선생 앞에는 상상할 수 있는 온갖 어려움이 놓일 것입니다. 소송에서 나올 수 있는 방해란 방해는 다 나올 테고, 사건의 모든 쟁점이 조직적으로 다투어질 것이며, 우리가 수백 파운드가 아니라 수천 파운드를 쓰고 났을 즈음이면 최종 결과는 십중팔구 우리에게 불리할 것입니다. 사람이 닮았다는 사정이 얽힌 신원 문제는 그 자체로 가장 판가름하기 어려운 문제입니다. 지금 우리가 이야기하고 있는 이 사건을 둘러싼 복잡한 사정이 없을 때조차 그렇습니다. 저로서는 이 기이한 일에 조금이라도 빛을 비출 가망이 도무지 보이지 않습니다. 설령 리머리지 교회 묘지에 묻힌 사람이 글라이드 부인이 아니라 해도, 선생 말씀대로 그 사람은 살아 있을 때 그분과 너무나 닮았으니, 시신을 파낼 권한을 얻어 낸다 한들 우리가 얻을 것은 없습니다. 요컨대 소송이 될 만한 것이 없습니다, 하트라이트 씨. 정말로 없습니다.”\n\n나는 소송이 될 만한 것이 있다고 기어이 믿기로 작정했고, 그 작정에 따라 발판을 옮겨 다시 한 번 그에게 호소했다.\n\n“신원을 증명하는 것 말고 우리가 내놓을 수 있는 다른 증거는 없겠습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“선생께서 처하신 형편에서는 없습니다.” 그가 대답했다. “모든 증거 가운데 가장 단순하고 가장 확실한 것, 곧 날짜를 맞대어 보는 증명은 제가 알기로 선생의 손이 전혀 닿지 않는 곳에 있습니다. 의사 진단서의 날짜와 글라이드 부인이 런던으로 간 날짜 사이의 어긋남을 보여 주실 수 있다면 이 일은 아주 다른 모습을 띠게 될 테고, 저부터 나서서 시작합시다 하고 말씀드릴 것입니다.”\n\n“그 날짜는 아직 찾아낼 수 있습니다, 카일 씨.”\n\n“그 날짜를 찾아내는 날, 하트라이트 씨, 선생께는 소송이 생깁니다. 지금 이 순간 그것에 다가갈 가망이 조금이라도 있다면 말씀해 주십시오. 제가 조언을 드릴 수 있을지 보겠습니다.”\n\n나는 생각해 보았다. 가정부는 우리를 도울 수 없었다. 로라도 도울 수 없었다. 매리언도 도울 수 없었다. 그 날짜를 아는 사람은 이 세상에 십중팔구 퍼시벌 경과 백작뿐이었다.\n\n“지금으로서는 그 날짜를 알아낼 방법이 떠오르지 않습니다.” 내가 말했다. “그것을 틀림없이 알고 있을 사람으로는 포스코 백작과 퍼시벌 글라이드 경밖에 떠오르지 않기 때문입니다.”\n\n카일 씨의 차분하고 주의 깊은 얼굴이 처음으로 풀어지며 미소가 되었다.\n\n“그 두 신사의 행실에 대해 그런 견해를 갖고 계시니,” 그가 말했다. “그쪽에서 도움을 기대하지는 않으시겠지요? 두 사람이 공모하여 큰돈을 얻은 것이라면 어쨌든 그것을 자백할 리는 없을 테니까요.”\n\n“자백하도록 몰릴 수는 있습니다, 카일 씨.”\n\n“누구에게 말입니까?”\n\n“저에게 말입니다.”\n\n우리는 함께 일어섰다. 그는 지금까지 보인 것보다 더 관심이 어린 눈으로 내 얼굴을 찬찬히 들여다보았다. 내가 그를 조금 어리둥절하게 만들었다는 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n“대단히 굳게 마음먹으셨군요.” 그가 말했다. “일을 밀고 나가시는 데에 개인적인 동기가 있으시겠지요. 그것은 제가 물을 바가 아닙니다. 앞으로 소송이 될 만한 것이 갖추어진다면 제 힘껏 도와 드리겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 것이 없습니다. 다만 돈 문제는 언제나 법 문제에 따라붙는 법이니 미리 일러 두겠습니다. 설령 끝내 글라이드 부인이 살아 계시다는 사실을 확정하신다 해도 그분의 재산을 되찾을 가망은 거의 없어 보입니다. 그 외국인은 절차가 시작되기 전에 이 나라를 떠날 테고, 퍼시벌 경의 빚은 그가 가진 돈이 얼마든 거의 다 채권자들에게 넘어갈 만큼 많고도 급합니다. 물론 알고 계시겠지만——”\n\n나는 거기서 그를 막았다.\n\n“글라이드 부인의 재산 이야기는 하지 않았으면 합니다.” 내가 말했다. “저는 예전에도 그 일에 대해 아무것도 알지 못했고 지금도 아무것도 모릅니다. 다만 그분의 재산이 없어졌다는 것만 알 뿐입니다. 제가 이 일에 나서는 데에 개인적인 동기가 있다고 보신 것은 옳습니다. 저는 그 동기가 지금 이 순간처럼 언제까지나 사심 없는 것이기를 바랍니다——”\n\n그는 말을 끼워 넣어 해명하려 했다. 그가 나를 의심했다는 느낌에 조금 달아올랐던 모양으로, 나는 그의 말을 기다리지 않고 무뚝뚝하게 이어 나갔다.\n\n“돈이 동기가 되는 일은 없을 것입니다.” 내가 말했다. “제가 글라이드 부인께 해 드리려는 일에 제 이익을 셈하는 마음은 없을 것입니다. 그분은 자기가 태어난 집에서 남처럼 쫓겨났습니다. 그분의 죽음을 기록한 거짓말이 어머니의 무덤에 새겨졌습니다. 그리고 그 일에 책임이 있는 두 사내가 살아서 벌도 받지 않고 있습니다. 그 거짓 장례를 무덤까지 따라간 사람들이 하나도 빠짐없이 보는 앞에서 그 집은 다시 열려 그분을 맞아들일 것이고, 그 거짓말은 집안 어른의 권한으로 묘비에서 공공연히 지워질 것이며, 법정에 앉은 정의가 그자들을 뒤쫓을 힘이 없다 해도 그 두 사내는 제게 그 죄를 답해야 할 것입니다. 저는 그 일에 제 목숨을 바쳤고, 이렇게 홀로 서 있으나 하느님이 저를 살려 두시는 한 기어이 그것을 이루겠습니다.”\n\n그는 탁자 쪽으로 물러서며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 내 망상이 이성을 이겨 버렸다고 그가 생각한다는 것, 그리고 내게 더 조언해 보아야 아무 소용없다고 여긴다는 것이 그의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다.\n\n“우리는 저마다 제 생각을 지키기로 하지요, 카일 씨.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 앞으로 일어날 일들이 우리 사이를 판가름해 줄 때까지 기다려야겠지요. 그동안 제 진술을 귀 기울여 들어 주신 데 깊이 감사드립니다. 선생께서는 법이라는 구제책이 온갖 뜻에서 우리 힘 밖에 있음을 보여 주셨습니다. 우리는 법이 요구하는 증명을 내놓을 수 없고, 소송 비용을 댈 만큼 넉넉하지도 못합니다. 그것을 알게 된 것만도 얻은 바가 있습니다.”\n\n나는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그는 나를 불러 세워, 면담이 시작될 때 탁자 위에 따로 놓아두는 것을 내가 보았던 그 편지를 건네주었다.\n\n“며칠 전에 우편으로 온 것입니다.” 그가 말했다. “번거로우시겠지만 전해 주시겠습니까? 아울러 홀콤 양께 이렇게 전해 주십시오. 지금까지는 조언 말고 달리 도와 드릴 길이 없어 진심으로 유감이라고, 그 조언이라는 것도 선생께 그랬듯 그분께도 반갑지는 않을 듯하여 송구하다고요.”\n\n그가 말하는 동안 나는 그 편지를 들여다보았다. 수신인은 “챈서리 레인, 길모어 앤드 카일 법률 사무소 전교, 홀콤 양”이라고 적혀 있었다. 필적은 내가 전혀 모르는 것이었다.\n\n방을 나서면서 나는 마지막으로 한 가지를 물었다.\n\n“혹시 아십니까,” 내가 말했다. “퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아직 파리에 있는지요?”\n\n“런던으로 돌아왔습니다.” 카일 씨가 대답했다. “적어도 어제 만난 그의 사무 변호사에게서 그렇게 들었습니다.”\n\n그 대답을 듣고 나는 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n사무소를 나서면서 지켜야 할 첫 번째 조심은 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보아 남의 눈길을 끄는 짓을 삼가는 것이었다. 나는 홀본 북쪽의 큰 광장들 가운데 가장 한적한 곳을 향해 걸었고, 뒤로 긴 보도가 훤히 남는 자리에 이르렀을 때 문득 멈추어 서서 뒤를 돌아보았다.\n\n광장 모퉁이에 사내 둘이 있었는데 그들도 걸음을 멈추고 서서 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 잠시 생각한 끝에 나는 그들 곁을 지나가도록 되돌아섰다. 내가 가까이 가자 한 사람이 몸을 움직여 광장에서 거리로 이어지는 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다. 다른 하나는 그 자리에 그대로 있었다. 나는 지나가면서 그를 보았고, 내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 나를 감시하던 자들 가운데 하나임을 대번에 알아보았다.\n\n내 본능이 시키는 대로 할 수 있었다면 나는 아마 그 사내에게 말을 거는 것으로 시작해 그를 때려눕히는 것으로 끝냈을 것이다. 그러나 나는 뒷일을 헤아리지 않을 수 없었다. 한 번이라도 남들이 보는 앞에서 내가 잘못한 처지가 된다면, 그것은 곧바로 퍼시벌 경의 손에 무기를 쥐여 주는 일이었다. 꾀에는 꾀로 맞서는 수밖에 없었다. 나는 두 번째 사내가 사라진 거리로 접어들어, 어느 문간에서 기다리고 있던 그를 지나쳤다. 그는 내가 모르는 얼굴이었고, 앞으로 성가신 일이 생길 때를 대비해 그 생김새를 똑똑히 익혀 둔 것이 다행이었다. 그러고 나서 나는 다시 북쪽으로 걸어 뉴 로드에 이르렀다. 거기서 서쪽으로 방향을 틀어, 그동안 내내 사내들을 뒤에 달고서, 마차 승강장에서 어지간히 떨어졌다 싶은 지점에 이르러 빈 이륜 쾌속 마차가 지나가기를 기다렸다. 몇 분 만에 한 대가 지나갔다. 나는 뛰어올라 마부에게 하이드 파크 쪽으로 빨리 몰라고 일렀다. 뒤에 있던 첩자들에게는 뒤따를 빠른 마차가 없었다. 그들이 길 건너편으로 내달아, 마차든 승강장이든 눈에 띌 때까지 달려서 나를 쫓으려는 것이 보였다. 그러나 나는 그들보다 앞서 있었고, 마부를 세우고 내렸을 때 그들은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 나는 하이드 파크를 가로지르며 탁 트인 땅에서 내가 자유로워졌음을 확인했다. 마침내 집으로 발길을 돌린 것은 그로부터 여러 시간이 지난 뒤, 날이 어두워지고 나서였다.\n\n매리언은 작은 거실에서 혼자 나를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀는 내가 들어오는 대로 그림을 보여 주겠다고 약속한 끝에 로라를 겨우 재우러 보낸 참이었다. 그 가엾고 흐릿하고 희미한 소묘는, 그 자체로는 하잘것없으나 거기 얽힌 사연으로는 그토록 가슴을 치는 것이었는데, 책 두 권에 조심스레 받쳐 탁자 위에 세워져 있었고, 우리가 스스로에게 허락한 단 하나의 촛불이 뿜는 희미한 빛이 가장 잘 비치는 자리에 놓여 있었다. 나는 그 그림을 들여다보며 매리언에게 그동안 있었던 일을 속삭여 들려주려고 자리에 앉았다. 옆방과 우리를 가르는 칸막이가 어찌나 얇은지 로라의 숨소리가 들릴 지경이어서, 소리 내어 말했다가는 그녀를 깨울 수도 있었다.\n\n매리언은 내가 카일 씨와의 면담을 이야기하는 동안 평정을 지켰다. 그러나 그다음으로 변호사 사무소에서부터 나를 미행한 사내들 이야기를 하고 퍼시벌 경이 돌아왔다는 사실을 알렸을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 근심으로 흐려졌다.\n\n“나쁜 소식이군요, 월터.” 그녀가 말했다. “가져올 수 있는 소식 가운데 가장 나쁜 소식이에요. 더 해 줄 이야기는 없나요?”\n\n“드릴 것이 하나 있어요.” 내가 대답하며, 카일 씨가 내 손에 맡긴 쪽지를 그녀에게 건넸다.\n\n그녀는 수신인을 보고 대번에 그 필적을 알아보았다.\n\n“보낸 사람을 아시겠어요?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“너무나 잘 알지요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “내게 편지를 보낸 사람은 포스코 백작이에요.”\n\n그렇게 대답하고 그녀는 쪽지를 펼쳤다. 읽어 내려가는 동안 그녀의 얼굴이 짙게 달아올랐고, 이번에는 나더러 읽으라며 그것을 건넬 때 그녀의 두 눈은 분노로 번쩍였다.\n\n쪽지에는 이런 글이 적혀 있었다.\n\n“떳떳한 흠모에 이끌려, 나 자신에게도 떳떳하고 그대에게도 떳떳한 그 흠모에 이끌려 나는 씁니다, 장엄한 매리언이여. 그대의 평온을 위하여, 위로가 될 두 마디를 전하고자.\n\n“아무것도 두려워하지 마시오!\n\n“타고난 그 훌륭한 분별을 발휘하여 물러나 계시오. 사랑스럽고 감탄스러운 여인이여, 위험한 세상의 이목을 부르지 마시오. 체념은 숭고하니 그것을 취하시오. 가정의 조촐한 안식은 영원히 새로우니 그것을 누리시오. 인생의 폭풍은 은둔의 골짜기 위를 해를 끼치지 않고 지나가는 법이니, 부인이여, 그 골짜기에 머무시오.\n\n“그리하면 나는 그대에게 아무것도 두려워하지 말라고 이를 권한을 얻습니다. 어떤 새로운 재앙도 그대의 감수성을, 내 것만큼이나 내게 소중한 그 감수성을 찢어 놓지 않을 것이오. 그대는 시달리지 않을 것이며, 그대가 은거하는 곳의 아리따운 동반자도 쫓기지 않을 것이오. 그 여인은 그대의 마음속에서 새 안식처를 찾았소. 값을 매길 수 없는 안식처여! 나는 그 여인을 부러워하며 거기 그대로 두겠소.\n\n“애정 어린 경고, 아비 같은 당부의 말 한마디를 마지막으로 남기고 나는 그대에게 말을 건네는 이 매혹에서 나를 떼어 놓겠소. 이 뜨거운 몇 줄을 이만 맺겠소.\n\n“이미 나아간 데서 한 걸음도 더 나아가지 말고, 중대한 이해를 건드리지 말며, 누구도 위협하지 마시오. 부디 간청하건대 나를 행동으로 몰지 마시오. 행동의 사람인 나를 말이오. 그대를 위하여 가만히 있는 것, 내 정력과 계략이 미치는 그 광대한 범위를 스스로 묶어 두는 것이야말로 내 야심이 소중히 여기는 목적일진대. 그대에게 무모한 벗이 있거든 그 딱한 열의를 누그러뜨리시오. 하트라이트 씨가 영국으로 돌아오거든 그와 아무 왕래도 하지 마시오. 나는 나만의 길을 걷고, 퍼시벌은 내 뒤꿈치를 따라옵니다. 하트라이트 씨가 그 길을 가로지르는 날, 그는 끝장난 사람이오.”\n\n이 글에 붙은 서명이라고는 복잡한 장식 곡선의 동그라미에 둘러싸인 첫 글자 F 하나뿐이었다. 나는 그 편지에 대해 느낀 경멸을 그대로 담아 그것을 탁자 위에 내던졌다.\n\n“당신을 겁주려는 겁니다. 자기가 겁먹었다는 확실한 표시지요.” 내가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 나처럼 그 편지를 대하기에는 너무나 진솔한 여자였다. 그 무례하도록 허물없는 말투는 그녀의 자제심으로 감당하기에 벅찼다. 탁자 너머로 나를 바라보는 그녀의 두 손은 무릎 위에서 꼭 움켜쥐어졌고, 예전의 그 빠르고 불같은 성미가 다시 그녀의 뺨과 두 눈에서 환하게 타올랐다.\n\n“월터!” 그녀가 말했다. “언젠가 그 두 사람이 당신 손아귀에 들어오고, 그중 하나를 살려 주어야만 한다면, 그게 백작이 되게는 하지 말아요.”\n\n“이 편지는 간직해 두겠어요, 매리언. 때가 왔을 때 잊지 않도록요.”\n\n내가 편지를 수첩에 넣는 동안 그녀는 나를 유심히 바라보았다.\n\n“때가 왔을 때라니요?” 그녀가 되뇌었다. “앞일을 확신하는 사람처럼 말할 수 있나요? 카일 씨 사무소에서 들은 이야기를 겪고도, 오늘 당신에게 일어난 일을 겪고도요?”\n\n“나는 오늘부터 날을 세지 않아요, 매리언. 오늘 내가 한 일이라고는 남에게 나를 대신해 나서 달라고 부탁한 것뿐이에요. 나는 내일부터 셉니다——”\n\n“어째서 내일부터죠?”\n\n“내일부터는 내가 나 자신을 위해 나설 작정이니까요.”\n\n“어떻게요?”\n\n“첫차로 블랙워터에 갔다가, 바라건대 밤에는 돌아올 생각이에요.”\n\n“블랙워터라니요!”\n\n“그래요. 카일 씨와 헤어진 뒤로 생각할 시간이 있었어요. 한 가지 점에서 그의 견해는 내 생각을 확실히 뒷받침해 줍니다. 우리는 로라가 런던으로 간 날짜를 끝까지 캐내야 해요. 이 음모의 단 하나뿐인 약점, 그리고 아마도 그이가 살아 있는 사람임을 증명할 단 하나뿐인 기회가 그 날짜를 밝혀내는 데에 모여 있어요.”\n\n“그러니까,” 매리언이 말했다. “로라가 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 것이 의사 진단서에 적힌 사망 날짜보다 뒤였다는 걸 밝혀내자는 말이군요?”\n\n“그래요.”\n\n“왜 그게 뒤였을 거라고 생각하죠? 로라는 런던에 있던 동안의 일은 아무것도 말해 주지 못하는데요.”\n\n“하지만 병원 주인이 당신에게 그이를 7월 27일에 받았다고 말했잖아요. 나는 포스코 백작이 그이를 런던에 붙들어 두고 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 조금도 알아채지 못하게 만드는 일을 하룻밤 넘게 해낼 수 있었을지 의심스러워요. 그렇다면 그이는 26일에 떠났을 것이고, 의사 진단서에 적힌 자기 사망 날짜보다 하루 뒤에 런던에 닿았을 겁니다. 그 날짜를 증명할 수 있다면 우리는 퍼시벌 경과 백작을 상대로 우리 주장을 증명하는 셈이에요.”\n\n“그래요, 그래요, 알겠어요! 그런데 그 증명을 어떻게 얻지요?”\n\n“마이컬슨 부인의 기록을 보고 그것을 얻어 볼 두 가지 길이 떠올랐어요. 하나는 의사인 도슨 씨에게 물어보는 것입니다. 로라가 집을 떠난 뒤 언제부터 블랙워터 파크 왕진을 다시 시작했는지 그는 알고 있을 테니까요. 다른 하나는 퍼시벌 경이 그날 밤 혼자 마차를 몰고 간 여관에 가서 알아보는 것이에요. 그가 떠난 것이 로라가 떠난 지 몇 시간 뒤였다는 것은 우리가 알고 있으니, 그 길로 날짜에 이를 수 있을지도 몰라요. 적어도 해 볼 만한 일이고, 내일은 기어이 해 보고야 말겠습니다.”\n\n“그런데 그게 실패하면요? 월터, 지금은 최악을 보고 있지만 실망스러운 일이 우리를 시험하러 오면 그때는 최선을 보겠어요. 블랙워터에서 아무도 당신을 도와줄 수 없다면 어쩌죠?”\n\n“런던에는 나를 도울 수 있고 또 반드시 돕게 될 두 사람이 있어요. 퍼시벌 경과 백작이지요. 죄 없는 사람이야 날짜를 잊을 수도 있겠지만 그자들은 죄가 있고 스스로 그것을 압니다. 다른 데서 다 실패한다면 나는 내 방식대로 둘 가운데 하나, 혹은 둘 다에게서 자백을 짜낼 작정이에요.”\n\n내가 그렇게 말하자 매리언의 얼굴에 여자다운 것이 모조리 확 달아올랐다.\n\n“백작부터 시작해요.” 그녀가 다급하게 속삭였다. “나를 위해서, 백작부터 시작해요.”\n\n“로라를 위해서, 성공할 가망이 가장 큰 쪽부터 시작해야 해요.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴에서 다시 핏기가 가셨고, 그녀는 슬프게 고개를 저었다.\n\n“그래요.” 그녀가 말했다. “당신 말이 옳아요. 그런 말을 한 건 내가 옹졸하고 못난 탓이에요. 나도 참으려고 애쓰고 있어요, 월터. 행복하던 시절보다 지금이 더 잘되기도 하고요. 하지만 예전의 그 성미가 아직 조금 남아 있어서, 백작 생각만 하면 그것이 나를 이겨 버려요!”\n\n“그자의 차례도 올 겁니다.” 내가 말했다. “하지만 잊지 말아요. 우리가 아는 한 그의 삶에는 아직 약한 데가 없어요.” 나는 그녀가 평정을 되찾도록 잠시 기다렸다가 결정적인 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“매리언! 퍼시벌 경의 삶에는 우리 둘 다 알고 있는 약한 데가 있어요——”\n\n“비밀 말이군요!”\n\n“그래요, 그 비밀입니다. 그것이 우리가 그를 붙들 유일하고도 확실한 손잡이예요. 다른 어떤 수단으로도 나는 그를 안전한 자리에서 끌어내릴 수 없고, 그와 그의 악행을 환한 대낮으로 끌어낼 수 없어요. 백작이 무슨 짓을 했든, 퍼시벌 경이 로라를 해치는 음모에 동의한 데에는 돈이라는 동기 말고 다른 동기가 있었어요. 그가 백작에게, 아내가 자기를 파멸시키기에 넉넉할 만큼 알고 있다고 믿는다 말하는 걸 들으셨지요? 앤 캐서릭의 비밀이 알려지면 자기는 끝장이라고 말하는 걸 들으셨지요?”\n\n“네! 그래요! 들었어요.”\n\n“그렇다면 매리언, 다른 방도가 모두 우리를 저버렸을 때 나는 그 비밀을 알아낼 작정이에요. 옛날의 그 미신이 아직도 내게서 떠나지 않습니다. 다시 말하지만 흰옷을 입은 여인은 우리 세 사람의 삶에 살아 있는 힘이에요. 끝은 정해져 있고, 그 끝이 우리를 끌어당기고 있으며, 무덤 속에서 죽어 있는 앤 캐서릭이 아직도 그리로 가는 길을 가리키고 있어요!”\n\n햄프셔에서 벌인 나의 첫 조사에 관한 이야기는 짧게 끝맺을 수 있다.\n\n런던을 이른 시각에 떠난 덕분에 나는 오전 중에 도슨 의사의 집에 닿을 수 있었다. 그러나 내 방문의 목적에 관한 한, 우리의 면담은 아무런 만족스러운 결과도 낳지 못했다.\n\n도슨 의사의 진료 기록부에는 그가 블랙워터 파크에서 홀콤 양의 진료를 다시 시작한 날짜가 분명히 적혀 있었다. 그러나 그 날짜에서 거슬러 올라가 조금이라도 정확하게 셈을 하려면 마이컬슨 부인의 도움이 있어야 했고, 나는 그녀가 그런 도움을 줄 수 없다는 것을 이미 알고 있었다. 그녀는 의사가 환자의 진료를 다시 시작하기까지, 그에 앞서 글라이드 부인이 떠난 날로부터 며칠이 흘렀는지를 기억만으로는 말하지 못했다(이런 경우에 그것을 말할 수 있는 사람이 대체 어디 있겠는가?). 그녀는 부인이 떠난 사정을 그 이튿날 홀콤 양에게 이야기했다는 것만은 거의 확신했으나, 그 이야기를 한 날의 날짜를 짚어내지 못하기는 글라이드 부인이 런던으로 떠난 그 전날의 날짜를 짚어내지 못하는 것과 다를 바가 없었다. 여주인이 떠난 때로부터 날짜 없는 포스코 백작부인의 편지가 도착한 때까지 얼마나 지났는지도 그녀는 조금이라도 더 정확하게 헤아리지 못했다. 마지막으로, 난관의 목록을 완성하기라도 하려는 듯, 의사 자신도 그 무렵 앓고 있던 터라 블랙워터 파크의 정원사가 마이컬슨 부인의 전갈을 들고 찾아왔던 요일과 날짜를 늘 하던 대로 기입해 두지 않았다.\n\n도슨 의사에게서 도움을 얻기는 글렀으므로, 나는 다음으로 퍼시벌 경이 놀즈베리에 도착한 날짜를 확정할 수 있는지 알아보기로 했다.\n\n마치 운명의 장난 같았다! 놀즈베리에 닿았을 때 그 여관은 문을 닫았고 벽에는 공고문이 붙어 있었다. 내가 들은 바로는 철도가 놓인 뒤로 그 장사는 줄곧 신통치 않았다고 한다. 역에 새로 생긴 호텔이 손님을 차츰 다 빨아들였고, 우리가 퍼시벌 경이 묵었던 곳으로 알고 있는 그 낡은 여관은 두 달쯤 전에 문을 닫았다는 것이다. 주인은 가재도구를 죄다 챙겨 마을을 떠났는데, 어디로 갔는지는 누구에게 물어도 확실히 알아낼 수 없었다. 내가 물어본 네 사람은 그가 놀즈베리를 떠날 때 품었던 계획과 속셈에 대해 저마다 다른 네 가지 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n런던행 막차가 떠나기까지는 아직 몇 시간이 남아 있었다. 그래서 나는 놀즈베리 역에서 삯마차를 얻어 타고 다시 블랙워터 파크로 향했다. 정원사와 문간채를 지키는 사람을 만나 캐물을 작정이었다. 그들마저 나를 도울 수 없다면 당장 쓸 수 있는 수단은 다 떨어진 셈이니, 그때는 런던으로 돌아가면 될 일이었다.\n\n나는 파크에서 1마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 삯마차를 돌려보내고, 마부에게 길을 물어 둔 다음 혼자서 저택을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n큰길에서 좁은 길로 접어들 때, 나는 융단 천으로 만든 여행 가방을 든 사내가 문간채 쪽으로 빠르게 앞서 걸어가는 것을 보았다. 몸집이 작은 사내였고, 낡아빠진 검은 옷을 입었으며, 유난히 큰 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 겉으로 짐작할 수 있는 한도에서 나는 그를 변호사 사무실의 서기쯤으로 보았고, 곧바로 걸음을 멈추어 사이를 벌렸다. 그는 내 기척을 듣지 못했는지 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 걸어가 시야에서 사라졌다. 조금 뒤에 내가 직접 대문을 지날 때 그는 보이지 않았다. 저택 쪽으로 계속 올라간 것이 분명했다.\n\n문간채에는 여자가 둘 있었다. 한 사람은 노파였고, 다른 한 사람은 매리언에게서 들은 인상착의로 보아 곧바로 마거릿 포처임을 알 수 있었다.\n\n나는 먼저 퍼시벌 경이 파크에 계시냐고 물었고, 아니라는 대답을 듣고는 언제 떠났느냐고 다시 물었다. 두 여자 모두 그가 여름에 떠났다는 것 말고는 아무것도 말해 주지 못했다. 마거릿 포처에게서는 멍한 웃음과 고개를 젓는 시늉 말고는 아무것도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 노파는 그보다 조금 나아서, 나는 그녀가 퍼시벌 경이 떠나던 정황과 그때 자기가 받은 놀라움에 대해 이야기하도록 이끌 수 있었다. 그녀는 주인이 자기를 잠자리에서 불러냈던 일과, 욕설을 퍼부어 자기를 겁먹게 했던 일을 기억하고 있었다. 그러나 그 일이 있었던 날짜는, 그녀 자신이 솔직히 인정했듯이 “도무지 알 수 없는” 것이었다.\n\n문간채를 나서다가 나는 멀지 않은 곳에서 일하고 있는 정원사를 보았다. 처음 말을 걸었을 때 그는 자못 미심쩍은 눈으로 나를 보았으나, 내가 마이컬슨 부인의 이름을 대며 그에 대해서도 정중하게 언급하자 선선히 대화에 응했다. 우리 사이에 오간 말을 옮길 필요는 없다. 날짜를 알아내려던 다른 모든 시도가 그랬듯 이번에도 같은 결말로 끝났으니까. 정원사는 주인이 밤에 마차를 타고 떠났다는 것, 그것이 “7월 어느 무렵, 그달 마지막 두 주나 마지막 열흘 사이”라는 것만 알 뿐 그 이상은 알지 못했다.\n\n우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안, 나는 큰 모자를 쓴 그 검은 옷의 사내가 저택에서 나와 조금 떨어진 곳에 서서 우리를 지켜보는 것을 보았다.\n\n그가 블랙워터 파크에 온 용건에 대해서는 이미 어떤 의심이 내 머리를 스치고 지나간 터였다. 그 사내가 누구인지 말해 주지 못하는(혹은 말해 주지 않으려는) 정원사의 태도가 그 의심을 한층 키웠고, 나는 가능하다면 그에게 직접 말을 붙여 앞길을 트기로 마음먹었다. 낯선 사람으로서 던질 수 있는 가장 무난한 질문은 이 저택을 구경할 수 있게 해 주느냐고 묻는 것이었다. 나는 곧장 그 사내에게 걸어가 그 말로 말을 붙였다.\n\n그의 눈빛과 태도는 그가 내가 누구인지 알고 있으며 나를 자극해 시비를 걸게 만들고 싶어 한다는 것을 뚜렷이 드러냈다. 그의 대답은, 내가 스스로를 억누르려는 결심을 덜 굳게 했더라면 충분히 그 목적을 이루었을 만큼 무례했다. 그러나 나는 더없이 단호한 예의로 그를 맞았고, 본의 아닌 침입을(그는 그것을 “무단 침입”이라 불렀다) 사과한 뒤 그 부지를 떠났다. 내가 의심한 그대로였다. 카일 씨의 사무실을 나설 때 누군가 나를 알아본 사실이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 전해진 것이 분명했고, 내가 저택이나 그 근방에서 캐고 다닐 것을 예상해 그 검은 옷의 사내를 파크로 보내 둔 것이었다. 만일 내가 그에게 나를 상대로 어떤 법적 고발을 제기할 빌미라도 조금 주었더라면, 지방 치안판사의 개입이 틀림없이 내 일을 가로막는 족쇄로, 그리고 적어도 며칠 동안은 나를 매리언과 로라에게서 떼어놓는 수단으로 이용되었을 것이다.\n\n나는 전날 런던에서 감시당했던 것과 똑같이 블랙워터 파크에서 역까지 가는 길에도 감시당하리라 각오하고 있었다. 그러나 그때는 내가 정말로 미행을 당하고 있는지 아닌지 알아낼 수 없었다. 그 검은 옷의 사내에게는 내가 알지 못하는 추적 수단이 마련되어 있었을지도 모르지만, 역으로 가는 길에서도, 그 뒤 저녁에 런던 종착역에 내렸을 때도 그의 모습만은 전혀 보이지 않았다. 나는 걸어서 집으로 향했고, 우리 집 문에 다다르기 전에 그 일대에서 가장 인적 없는 거리를 빙 둘러 걷고 거기서 걸음을 멈춰 등 뒤의 트인 공간을 몇 번씩 돌아보는 조심을 잊지 않았다. 배신이 의심될 때 쓰는 이 술책을 나는 중앙아메리카의 황야에서 처음 익혔다. 그런데 이제 나는 문명의 심장인 런던 한복판에서, 같은 목적으로, 그때보다 한층 더 조심스럽게 그것을 다시 쓰고 있었다!\n\n내가 없는 동안 매리언을 놀라게 할 만한 일은 아무것도 없었다. 그녀는 성과가 어떠했느냐고 다급히 물었다. 내가 이야기해 주자, 그녀는 지금까지의 조사가 실패했다는 것을 내가 무심하게 말하는 데 놀라움을 감추지 못했다.\n\n사실인즉, 조사가 신통치 않았다고 해서 내가 조금이라도 기가 꺾인 것은 아니었다. 나는 그 일을 의무로 여겨 밀고 나갔을 뿐, 거기서 무엇을 기대한 적은 없었다. 그 무렵 내 마음 상태로는, 이제 싸움이 나와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 사이의 힘겨루기로 좁혀졌다는 것을 아는 편이 오히려 마음이 놓이기까지 했다. 복수의 동기는 처음부터 줄곧 내 다른, 더 나은 동기들에 섞여 들어 있었다. 그리고 로라의 일을 위하는 가장 확실한 길, 이제 남은 유일한 길이 그녀와 결혼한 그 악당의 목덜미를 단단히 움켜쥐는 것이라는 사실이 나에게 만족스러웠음을 나는 고백한다.\n\n내 동기를 이 복수의 본능이 미치지 못하는 곳에 붙들어 둘 만큼 내가 강하지 못했음을 인정하면서도, 다른 한편으로 나 자신을 위해 정직하게 해 둘 말이 있다. 로라와 나의 앞날의 관계에 대해서나, 퍼시벌 경을 한번 내 손아귀에 넣기만 하면 그에게서 억지로 받아낼 수 있을 사사롭고 개인적인 양보에 대해서나, 나는 단 한 번도 비열한 계산을 품은 적이 없다. “내가 정말로 성공한다면, 그 성공의 한 결과로 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 다시 내게서 빼앗아 갈 힘을 잃게 되리라” 하고 스스로에게 말한 적이 없다. 그녀를 바라보면서 그런 생각으로 앞날을 그릴 수는 없었다. 예전의 그녀와 달라져 버린 그 서글픈 모습은, 내 사랑의 유일한 관심을 그녀의 아버지나 오라비가 품었을 법한 다정함과 연민의 관심으로 바꾸어 놓았고, 하느님은 아시겠지만 나는 그것을 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에서 느끼고 있었다. 이제 내 모든 희망은 그녀가 회복될 날 너머를 내다보지 않았다. 거기까지, 그녀가 다시 건강해지고 다시 행복해질 때까지, 거기까지, 그녀가 예전처럼 나를 바라보고 예전처럼 나에게 말을 걸 수 있게 될 때까지 — 내 가장 행복한 생각과 가장 간절한 소망의 앞날은 거기서 끝났다.\n\n이 말들은 부질없는 자기 성찰에 이끌려 쓰는 것이 아니다. 이 이야기에는 머지않아 남들이 내 행동을 두고 판단을 내리게 될 대목이 나온다. 그때가 오기 전에 내 가장 나은 면과 가장 못난 면이 공평하게 저울에 놓이는 것이 옳다.\n\n햄프셔에서 돌아온 이튿날 아침, 나는 매리언을 위층 내 작업실로 데리고 올라가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 생애에서 유일하게 파고들 만한 지점을 손에 넣기 위해 지금까지 다듬어 온 계획을 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다.\n\n그 비밀에 이르는 길은 지금껏 우리 누구도 뚫지 못한 수수께끼, 곧 흰옷을 입은 여인이라는 수수께끼를 지나고 있었다. 그리고 그 수수께끼에 다가가는 길은 다시 앤 캐서릭의 어머니에게서 도움을 얻는 데 있었으며, 캐서릭 부인이 이 일에 나서거나 입을 열도록 설득할 유일하게 확실한 방법은 클레멘츠 부인에게서 그 고장의 사정과 집안의 내력을 먼저 알아낼 수 있느냐에 달려 있었다. 이 문제를 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 나는 앤 캐서릭의 충실한 벗이자 보호자였던 그 여인과 연락이 닿는 것으로만 새 조사를 시작할 수 있으리라고 확신하게 되었다.\n\n그러니 첫 번째 난관은 클레멘츠 부인을 찾아내는 일이었다.\n\n이 필요를 가장 좋고 가장 간단한 방법으로 그 자리에서 해결한 것은 매리언의 빠른 눈치 덕분이었다. 그녀는 리머리지 근처의 농장, 곧 토드 코너에 편지를 보내 지난 몇 달 사이에 클레멘츠 부인이 토드 부인에게 연락한 일이 있었는지 물어보자고 했다. 클레멘츠 부인이 어쩌다 앤과 헤어지게 되었는지는 우리로서는 알 도리가 없었지만, 일단 헤어지고 나면 클레멘츠 부인이 사라진 그 여인의 행방을 그녀가 가장 애착을 가졌다고 알려진 바로 그 고장, 리머리지 근방에 수소문해 보리라는 것은 틀림없었다. 나는 매리언의 제안이 우리에게 성공의 가망을 열어 준다는 것을 곧바로 알아보았고, 그녀는 그날 우편으로 토드 부인에게 편지를 부쳤다.\n\n답장을 기다리는 동안 나는 퍼시벌 경의 집안과 그의 젊은 시절에 대해 매리언이 알려 줄 수 있는 것을 모조리 내 것으로 만들었다. 그 일에 관해 그녀가 말할 수 있는 것은 전해 들은 이야기뿐이었으나, 그 얼마 안 되는 이야기가 사실이라는 것만은 그런대로 확신하고 있었다.\n\n퍼시벌 경은 외아들이었다. 그의 아버지 펠릭스 글라이드 경은 태어날 때부터 고통스럽고 고칠 수 없는 기형을 안고 있었으며, 아주 어릴 적부터 사람들과의 교제를 모두 피했다. 그의 유일한 낙은 음악을 즐기는 것이었고, 그래서 자기와 취향이 비슷한 여인, 아주 뛰어난 연주가라는 평을 듣던 여인과 결혼했다. 그는 아직 젊은 나이에 블랙워터의 재산을 물려받았다. 그도 그의 아내도 그것을 손에 넣은 뒤 인근 사교계에 다가서려는 시늉조차 하지 않았고, 이들을 그 은둔에서 끌어내 보려고 애쓴 사람도 아무도 없었다. 다만 그 교구의 목사 한 사람이 불행한 예외였다.\n\n그 목사는 악의 없이 말썽을 일으키는 부류 가운데서도 가장 고약한 축, 곧 지나치게 열심인 사람이었다. 그는 펠릭스 경이 정치에서는 혁명가나 다름없고 종교에서는 불신자라는 평판을 안고 대학을 나왔다는 말을 들은 터라, 그 영지의 주인을 교구 교회로 불러내 올바른 가르침을 듣게 하는 것이 자기의 마땅한 의무라는 결론에 양심적으로 이르렀다. 펠릭스 경은 선의에서 나왔으나 방향을 잘못 잡은 그 성직자의 참견에 사납게 발끈했고, 어찌나 심하게, 또 어찌나 공공연하게 그를 모욕했던지 인근의 여러 집안이 파크로 분노에 찬 항의 편지를 보냈고 블랙워터 영지의 소작인들까지 감히 할 수 있는 한도까지 세게 제 뜻을 밝혔다. 시골 취향이라고는 조금도 없고 영지에도, 거기 사는 어느 누구에게도 애착이 없던 그 준남작은 블랙워터의 사교계가 자기를 성가시게 할 두 번째 기회는 결코 없을 것이라고 선언하고는 그 길로 그곳을 떠났다.\n\n런던에서 잠시 지낸 뒤 그와 그의 아내는 대륙으로 떠났고, 다시는 영국으로 돌아오지 않았다. 그들은 얼마간은 프랑스에서, 얼마간은 독일에서 살았는데, 자기 몸의 기형에 대한 병적인 자의식 때문에 펠릭스 경에게 필수가 되어 버린 그 엄격한 은둔만은 언제나 지켰다. 아들 퍼시벌은 외국에서 태어났고 거기서 가정교사들에게 교육을 받았다. 부모 가운데 먼저 여읜 쪽은 어머니였다. 아버지는 그로부터 몇 해 뒤인 1825년이나 1826년에 세상을 떠났다. 퍼시벌 경은 그전에도 젊은 나이로 한두 번 영국에 온 일이 있었으나, 고인이 된 페얼리 씨와의 교분은 아버지가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 시작되었다. 두 사람은 곧 아주 가까운 사이가 되었지만, 그 시절 퍼시벌 경이 리머리지 하우스에 온 일은 드물었거나 아예 없었다. 프레더릭 페얼리 씨는 필립 페얼리 씨와 함께 있는 자리에서 그를 한두 번 만났을지 모르나, 그때든 다른 어느 때든 그에 대해 아는 것은 거의 없었을 것이다. 페얼리 집안에서 퍼시벌 경과 가깝게 지낸 유일한 사람은 로라의 아버지였다.\n\n매리언에게서 얻어낼 수 있는 자세한 사정은 이것이 전부였다. 지금의 내 목적에 쓸모 있는 것은 아무것도 없었지만, 나는 훗날 어느 때고 그것들이 중요한 것으로 드러날 경우에 대비해 꼼꼼히 적어 두었다.\n\n토드 부인의 답장은, 우리 쪽 부탁대로 우리 집에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 우체국 앞으로 부쳐졌는데, 내가 찾으러 갔을 때 이미 그곳에 도착해 있었다. 그때까지 줄곧 우리에게 불리하기만 하던 운이 이 순간부터 우리 편으로 돌아섰다. 토드 부인의 편지에는 우리가 찾고 있던 첫 번째 소식이 담겨 있었다.\n\n알고 보니 클레멘츠 부인은 우리가 짐작한 대로 토드 코너에 편지를 보냈다. 먼저 자기와 앤이 그 농가의 친구들 곁을 갑작스레 떠난 일을(내가 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 흰옷을 입은 여인을 만난 그 이튿날 아침이었다) 용서해 달라고 청한 다음, 앤이 사라졌다는 사실을 토드 부인에게 알리고, 잃어버린 그 여자가 리머리지로 되돌아왔을지도 모르니 그 근방을 수소문해 달라고 간곡히 부탁하는 내용이었다. 그렇게 부탁하면서 클레멘츠 부인은 언제든 자기 소식을 들을 수 있는 주소를 잊지 않고 덧붙여 두었고, 이제 토드 부인이 그 주소를 매리언에게 전해 준 것이었다. 그것은 런던 안, 우리 하숙에서 걸어서 삼십 분도 안 되는 곳이었다.\n\n속담에 있는 말대로, 나는 발밑에 풀이 자라도록 내버려 두지 않기로 마음먹었다. 이튿날 아침 나는 클레멘츠 부인을 만나 보러 길을 나섰다. 이것이 이 조사에서 내가 내디딘 첫걸음이었다. 이제 내가 몸을 던지기로 한 그 필사의 시도에 관한 이야기는 여기서 시작된다.\n\n# VI\n\n토드 부인이 알려 준 주소를 따라가자, 그레이스 인 로드 가까운 곳의 점잖은 거리에 자리 잡은 하숙집이 나왔다.\n\n내가 문을 두드리자 클레멘츠 부인이 손수 문을 열었다. 그녀는 나를 기억하지 못하는 눈치로, 무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다. 나는 리머리지 묘지에서 흰옷을 입은 여인과 이야기를 마친 뒤 그녀를 만났던 일을 상기시키면서, 앤이 스스로 밝힌 대로 내가 앤 캐서릭을 도와 정신병원의 추적에서 벗어나게 해 준 사람이라는 점을 각별히 힘주어 일깨웠다. 그것이 클레멘츠 부인의 신뢰를 얻을 나의 유일한 근거였다. 내가 그 이야기를 꺼내자마자 그녀는 그때 일을 떠올렸고, 앤의 소식을 가져왔는지 몹시 애타게 알고 싶어 하며 나를 응접실로 청해 들였다.\n\n음모의 자세한 내막까지 들어가지 않고서 그녀에게 진실을 온전히 말해 주기란 불가능한 일이었고, 그 내막은 낯선 사람에게 털어놓기에는 위험한 것이었다. 나는 다만 헛된 희망을 부추기는 일만은 극도로 삼가면서, 내가 찾아온 목적은 앤의 실종에 실제로 책임이 있는 자들을 밝혀내는 데 있다고 설명할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 게다가 나중에 내 양심의 가책을 받는 일이 없도록 이런 말까지 덧붙였다. 그녀를 찾아낼 수 있으리라는 희망은 조금도 품고 있지 않다는 것, 우리가 그녀를 다시 살아서 보는 일은 없으리라 믿는다는 것, 그리고 이 일에서 내 주된 관심사는 그녀를 꾀어내는 데 관련되었으리라 의심되는 두 사내—나와 나의 소중한 벗들이 그 손에 참혹한 해를 입은 두 사내—를 벌받게 하는 것이라는 이야기였다. 이렇게 설명한 뒤, 이 일에 대한 우리의 관심이 (우리를 움직이는 동기에는 차이가 있을지언정) 결국 같은 것이 아닌지, 그리고 내가 묻는 문제에 관해 그녀가 알고 있는 바를 알려 주어 내 목적을 도와주는 데 꺼리는 마음이 있는지를 클레멘츠 부인 스스로 말하도록 맡겼다.\n\n가엾은 부인은 처음에는 너무 혼란스럽고 동요한 나머지 내 말을 온전히 알아듣지 못했다. 그녀는 다만, 내가 앤에게 베푼 친절에 보답하는 뜻에서 자기가 아는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 말해 주겠다고 대답할 뿐이었다. 다만 자기는 평소에도 낯선 이와 이야기할 때 머리가 빠르게 돌아가는 편이 못 되니, 부디 갈피를 잡아 주고 어디서부터 시작하면 좋을지 일러 달라고 청했다.\n\n제 생각을 정리하는 데 익숙하지 않은 사람에게서 얻어낼 수 있는 가장 명료한 이야기란, 도중에 되짚어 올라가야 하는 번거로움이 없도록 처음부터 충분히 멀리 거슬러 시작하는 이야기라는 것을 나는 경험으로 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 클레멘츠 부인에게 먼저 리머리지를 떠난 뒤에 있었던 일부터 말해 달라고 청했고, 그런 다음 조심스레 질문을 던져 가며 한 대목에서 다음 대목으로 그녀를 이끌어, 마침내 앤이 사라진 시기에까지 이르렀다.\n\n내가 그렇게 하여 얻어낸 이야기의 요지는 다음과 같았다.—\n\n토드 코너의 농가를 떠난 그날 클레멘츠 부인과 앤은 더비까지 갔고, 앤의 몸 때문에 그곳에서 일주일을 머물렀다. 그다음 두 사람은 런던으로 올라가, 당시 클레멘츠 부인이 얻어 살던 하숙에서 한 달 남짓 지냈는데, 그 집과 집주인에 얽힌 사정 때문에 거처를 옮기지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 밖으로 나설 때마다 런던이나 그 근방에서 발각될까 두려워하는 앤의 공포가 차츰 클레멘츠 부인에게도 옮아, 그녀는 잉글랜드에서 가장 외진 곳 가운데 하나로—링컨셔의 그림즈비라는 고을로 옮겨 가기로 마음먹었다. 세상을 떠난 그녀의 남편이 젊은 시절을 온통 그곳에서 보냈던 것이다. 남편의 친척들은 그 고을에 자리 잡은 점잖은 사람들이었고 늘 클레멘츠 부인을 크게 다정히 대해 주었으니, 그리로 가서 남편 친구들의 조언을 듣는 것보다 나은 길은 없다고 그녀는 생각했다. 앤은 웰밍엄의 어머니에게로 돌아가는 일은 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 자기가 바로 그곳에서 정신병원으로 끌려갔으니, 퍼시벌 경이 반드시 그리로 되돌아와 자기를 다시 찾아내리라는 것이었다. 그 반대에는 무겁게 새겨들을 만한 이유가 있었고, 클레멘츠 부인도 쉽사리 물리칠 수 있는 이유가 아니라고 느꼈다.\n\n그림즈비에서 앤에게 병의 첫 심각한 징후가 나타났다. 그 징후는 글라이드 부인의 결혼 소식이 신문에 공표되고 그 지면을 통해 그녀에게까지 전해진 직후에 나타난 것이었다.\n\n앓는 여인을 보러 불려 온 의사는 그녀가 심장에 심각한 병을 앓고 있음을 대번에 알아보았다. 병은 오래 이어져 그녀를 몹시 쇠약하게 만들었고, 정도는 다소 누그러졌으나 이따금씩 거듭 되살아났다. 그런 까닭에 두 사람은 새해의 전반기 내내 그림즈비에 머물렀고, 아마 훨씬 더 오래 머물렀을 터였다. 다만 이 무렵 앤이 글라이드 부인과 은밀히 만나 보겠다는 목적으로 햄프셔로 되돌아가겠다는 갑작스러운 결심을 하지만 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n클레멘츠 부인은 이 위험하고도 영문 모를 계획이 실행에 옮겨지지 못하도록 온 힘을 다해 막았다. 앤은 자기 동기에 대해 아무런 설명도 내놓지 않았다. 다만 자기가 죽을 날이 머지않았다고 믿는다는 것, 그리고 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 은밀히 글라이드 부인에게 전해야 할 마음속의 일이 있다는 것뿐이었다. 그 뜻을 이루겠다는 결심이 어찌나 굳었던지, 클레멘츠 부인이 함께 가기를 꺼린다면 자기 혼자서라도 햄프셔로 가겠노라고 그녀는 잘라 말했다. 의견을 물으니 의사는, 그녀의 뜻을 심하게 거스르면 십중팔구 또 한 차례, 어쩌면 목숨을 앗아 갈 발작을 일으키게 되리라는 견해였다. 이 조언을 듣고 클레멘츠 부인은 어쩔 수 없이 뜻을 굽혔고, 앞으로 닥칠 곤경과 위험에 대한 서글픈 예감을 안은 채 또 한 번 앤 캐서릭이 제 뜻대로 하도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n런던에서 햄프셔로 가는 길에 클레멘츠 부인은 같은 칸에 탄 승객 하나가 블랙워터 근방을 훤히 알고 있어 지리에 관해 필요한 것을 모두 일러 줄 수 있다는 사실을 알게 되었다. 그리하여 그녀는 퍼시벌 경의 저택에 위험할 만큼 가깝지 않으면서 두 사람이 갈 수 있는 유일한 곳이 샌던이라는 큰 마을임을 알아냈다. 그곳에서 블랙워터 파크까지의 거리는 삼사 마일쯤 되었는데, 앤은 호숫가 근처에 모습을 드러낼 때마다 그 거리를 걸어서 오갔던 것이다.\n\n샌던에서 발각되지 않고 지낸 며칠 동안 두 사람은 마을에서 조금 떨어진 곳, 방 하나를 세놓던 점잖은 과부의 오두막에서 지냈고, 클레멘츠 부인은 적어도 첫 주만이라도 그 여인이 입을 다물어 주도록 힘껏 애를 썼다. 또한 우선은 글라이드 부인에게 편지를 쓰는 것으로 만족하라고 앤을 설득하려 무던히도 애를 썼다. 그러나 리머리지로 보낸 익명의 편지에 담긴 경고가 아무 소용이 없었던 터라, 앤은 이번에는 직접 말을 하겠다고 마음을 굳혔고 혼자서 그 일을 하러 가겠다고 고집을 부렸다.\n\n그럼에도 클레멘츠 부인은 앤이 호수로 갈 때마다 몰래 그 뒤를 따랐다. 다만 보트 창고에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 지켜볼 만큼 가까이 다가갈 엄두는 내지 못했다. 앤이 그 위험한 근방에서 마지막으로 돌아왔을 때, 제 기력에 견주어 턱없이 먼 거리를 날마다 걸어 다닌 피로에다 그녀가 겪은 격한 동요의 소모까지 겹쳐, 클레멘츠 부인이 내내 두려워하던 결과가 나타났다. 심장 위쪽의 그 해묵은 통증과 그림즈비에서 앓던 병의 다른 증세들이 되살아났고, 앤은 오두막의 침상에 몸져눕고 말았다.\n\n이 위급한 상황에서 무엇보다 먼저 해야 할 일은, 클레멘츠 부인이 경험으로 알고 있듯이, 앤의 불안한 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰는 것이었다. 그래서 이 착한 부인은 이튿날 손수 호수로 갔다. 앤의 말대로라면 글라이드 부인이 틀림없이 날마다 보트 창고까지 산책을 나올 터이니, 그분을 만나 샌던 근처의 오두막으로 남몰래 와 달라고 청해 볼 수 있을까 싶어서였다. 수풀 가장자리에 이르렀을 때 클레멘츠 부인이 마주친 것은 글라이드 부인이 아니라, 손에 책을 든 키 크고 살진 노신사—다시 말해 포스코 백작이었다.\n\n백작은 잠시 그녀를 아주 유심히 살펴본 뒤, 그곳에서 누군가를 만나기로 되어 있느냐고 물었다. 그러고는 그녀가 대답하기도 전에, 자기는 글라이드 부인의 전갈을 가지고 여기서 기다리는 중인데 지금 눈앞에 있는 사람이 자기가 말을 전하라고 부탁받은 그 사람의 인상착의와 맞는지 썩 확신이 서지 않는다고 덧붙였다.\n\n이 말에 클레멘츠 부인은 곧바로 자기가 온 용건을 그에게 털어놓고, 그 전갈을 자기에게 맡겨 앤의 불안을 가라앉히는 데 도움을 달라고 간청했다. 백작은 아주 선선히, 그리고 친절하게 그 청을 들어주었다. 그가 말하기를, 전갈은 매우 중요한 것이었다. 글라이드 부인은 앤과 그녀의 착한 벗에게 곧바로 런던으로 돌아가 달라고 간곡히 청한다는 것이었다. 두 사람이 블랙워터 근방에 더 오래 머문다면 퍼시벌 경이 반드시 찾아내리라 확신한다는 것이었다. 부인 자신도 머지않아 런던으로 갈 터이니, 클레멘츠 부인과 앤이 먼저 그리로 가서 주소를 알려 준다면 보름이나 그 안에 소식을 듣고 만나게 되리라 했다. 백작은 자기도 앤에게 직접 다정하게 경고를 해 주려 해 보았으나, 그녀가 낯선 사람인 자기를 보고 너무 놀란 나머지 가까이 가서 말을 붙이도록 놓아두지 않았다고 덧붙였다.\n\n이에 클레멘츠 부인은 몹시 놀라고 애가 타서, 앤을 무사히 런던으로 데려가는 것이야말로 자기가 무엇보다 바라는 바이지만 지금은 그 아이가 병상에 누워 있으니 그 위험한 근방에서 데리고 나갈 가망이 당장은 없다고 대답했다. 백작은 클레멘츠 부인이 의사를 부른 적이 있는지 물었고, 마을에 자기들 처지가 알려질까 두려워 여태 부르기를 망설였다는 말을 듣자, 자기가 바로 의사이니 원한다면 함께 돌아가 앤을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠다고 알려 주었다. 클레멘츠 부인은 (글라이드 부인의 은밀한 전갈을 맡은 사람인 만큼 백작을 자연스레 믿었으므로) 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 함께 오두막으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그들이 도착했을 때 앤은 잠들어 있었다. 백작은 그녀를 보는 순간 흠칫 놀랐다(글라이드 부인과 닮은 모습에 놀란 것이 분명했다). 가엾은 클레멘츠 부인은 그가 그저 앤의 병세가 얼마나 심한지를 보고 충격을 받았으려니 여겼다. 그는 앤을 깨우지 못하게 했다. 클레멘츠 부인에게 증세를 물어보고, 그녀를 들여다보고, 맥을 가볍게 짚어 보는 것으로 만족했다. 샌던은 식료품점 겸 약재상이 있을 만큼은 큰 곳이었으므로, 백작은 그리로 가서 처방을 적고 약을 지어 오게 했다. 그는 손수 약을 가져와서는, 그것이 강력한 흥분제이니 앤이 자리에서 일어나 몇 시간밖에 걸리지 않는 런던행 여정의 고단함을 견딜 만한 기운을 틀림없이 얻게 되리라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 일러 주었다. 약은 그날과 이튿날에 정해진 시각마다 먹여야 했다. 사흘째가 되면 앤이 길을 떠날 만큼 나아질 터였고, 그는 블랙워터 역에서 클레멘츠 부인과 만나 정오 기차로 두 사람을 떠나보내기로 약속을 잡았다. 만일 두 사람이 나타나지 않으면 앤의 병이 더 나빠진 것으로 여기고 곧장 오두막으로 가겠다고 했다.\n\n그러나 일이 되어 간 형편으로는 그런 위급한 사태는 벌어지지 않았다.\n\n이 약은 앤에게 놀라운 효험을 냈고, 이제 런던에서 곧 글라이드 부인을 만나게 되리라고 클레멘츠 부인이 장담해 줄 수 있었던 것이 그 좋은 결과를 거들었다. 약속한 날 약속한 시각에 (햄프셔에 머문 기간을 다 합쳐도 채 일주일이 되지 않았을 무렵) 두 사람은 역에 닿았다. 백작이 거기서 그들을 기다리고 있었는데, 역시 기차로 런던까지 가는 듯한 어느 나이 지긋한 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그는 아주 친절하게 두 사람을 거들어 손수 객차에 태워 주면서, 글라이드 부인에게 주소 보내는 것을 잊지 말라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 당부했다. 그 나이 지긋한 부인은 같은 칸에 타지 않았고, 두 사람은 런던 종착역에 닿았을 때 그녀가 어디로 갔는지 알아차리지 못했다. 클레멘츠 부인은 조용한 동네에 번듯한 하숙을 얻은 다음, 약속한 대로 글라이드 부인에게 편지를 써서 주소를 알렸다.\n\n보름이 조금 넘게 지났으나 아무런 답장도 오지 않았다.\n\n그 무렵이 지났을 때 한 부인이(역에서 보았던 바로 그 나이 지긋한 부인이었다) 삯마차를 타고 찾아와, 자기는 글라이드 부인의 심부름으로 왔노라고 말했다. 글라이드 부인은 지금 런던의 어느 호텔에 머물고 있는데, 앞으로 앤과 만날 일을 의논하려고 클레멘츠 부인을 만나고 싶어 한다는 것이었다. 클레멘츠 부인은 (그때 자리에 있던 앤이 꼭 그렇게 해 달라고 간청하기도 했으므로) 그 일을 돕는 데 기꺼이 응하겠노라 밝혔다. 더구나 집을 비우는 시간이 길어야 반 시간을 넘지 않아도 된다니 더욱 그러했다. 그리하여 그녀와 그 나이 지긋한 부인은(포스코 백작부인이 분명했다) 삯마차를 타고 떠났다. 마차가 얼마쯤 달린 뒤, 그 부인은 호텔에 닿기 전 어느 가게 앞에서 마차를 세우고는, 깜빡 잊고 사지 못한 물건을 사 올 동안 몇 분만 기다려 달라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 청했다. 그러고는 다시 나타나지 않았다.\n\n한참을 기다리던 클레멘츠 부인은 덜컥 겁이 나서 마부에게 하숙집으로 되돌아가 달라고 일렀다. 반 시간 남짓 집을 비운 끝에 그곳에 닿았을 때, 앤은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n그 집 사람들에게서 얻을 수 있는 정보라고는 하숙인들의 시중을 드는 하녀에게서 나온 것이 전부였다. 하녀는 거리에서 온 사내아이에게 문을 열어 주었는데, 그 아이가 “이층에 사는 젊은 여자분”(클레멘츠 부인이 쓰던 층이었다) 앞으로 온 편지를 두고 갔다는 것이었다. 하녀는 편지를 전해 준 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔고, 오 분쯤 지나 앤이 보닛에 숄을 두른 차림으로 현관문을 열고 나가는 것을 보았다. 앤은 그 편지를 지니고 갔던 모양이다. 편지를 찾을 수 없었으니, 그녀를 집에서 나가게 만든 유인이 무엇이었는지는 알 길이 없었다. 그것은 필시 대단히 강한 것이었을 터다. 그녀는 결코 제 발로 런던 거리에 혼자 나서는 법이 없었으니까. 클레멘츠 부인이 그것을 경험으로 알고 있지 않았더라면, 아무리 반 시간뿐인 짧은 동안이라 해도 삯마차를 타고 집을 비우는 일은 결코 하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n정신을 겨우 추스르자마자 클레멘츠 부인에게 자연히 떠오른 첫 생각은, 앤이 도로 끌려갔을까 봐 두려워하던 그 정신병원에 가서 알아보는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 이튿날 그리로 갔다. 그 병원이 어디에 있는지는 앤에게서 직접 들어 알고 있었다. 그녀가 받은 대답은 (그녀가 문의한 것은 십중팔구 가짜 앤 캐서릭이 실제로 그 병원에 갇히기 하루 이틀 전이었을 것이다) 그런 사람이 되돌아온 적은 없다는 것이었다. 그래서 그녀는 웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인에게 편지를 써서 딸을 보거나 딸의 소식을 들은 적이 있는지 물었으나, 없다는 답장을 받았다. 그 답장이 온 뒤로 그녀는 방도가 다했고, 달리 어디에 물어야 할지 무엇을 해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그때부터 지금까지 그녀는 앤이 사라진 까닭도, 앤의 이야기가 어떻게 끝났는지도 전혀 알지 못한 채 지내 왔다.\n\n# VII\n\n여기까지 클레멘츠 부인에게서 들은 이야기는—내가 그때까지 알지 못했던 사실들을 밝혀 주기는 했으나—어디까지나 예비적인 성격의 것에 지나지 않았다.\n\n앤 캐서릭을 런던으로 옮겨 놓고 클레멘츠 부인과 떼어 놓은 그 일련의 기만이 오로지 포스코 백작과 백작부인의 손으로 이루어졌다는 것은 분명했다. 그 부부의 행위 가운데 어느 부분이 법의 손이 닿을 만한 것이었는지는 훗날 충분히 따져 볼 만한 문제였다. 그러나 내가 지금 눈앞에 두고 있는 목적은 나를 그와는 다른 방향으로 이끌었다. 클레멘츠 부인을 찾아온 당면한 목적은 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 밝혀내는 일에 적어도 한 걸음이라도 다가서는 것이었는데, 부인은 그 중대한 목표로 나를 나아가게 할 만한 이야기는 아직 아무것도 하지 않았다. 나는 그때까지 부인의 기억이 머물러 있던 시절과 사람들과 사건들이 아닌, 그 이전의 것들에 대한 회상을 일깨워 볼 필요를 느꼈고, 다음에 입을 열었을 때는 그 목적을 간접적으로 염두에 두고 말했다.\n\n“이 슬픈 재난에 제가 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있으면 좋으련만.” 내가 말했다. “제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 부인의 비통함에 진심으로 함께하는 것뿐입니다. 앤이 부인의 친자식이었다 해도, 클레멘츠 부인, 그보다 더 참된 사랑을 보여 주실 수는 없었을 겁니다—그 아이를 위해 그보다 더 선뜻 희생하실 수도 없었을 거예요.”\n\n“그건 대단할 것도 없는 일입니다, 선생님.” 클레멘츠 부인이 담담하게 말했다. “그 가엾은 것은 저에게 친자식이나 다름없었어요. 젖먹이 때부터 제가 젖도 없이 손으로 먹여 키웠지요, 선생님—키우기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었답니다. 제가 그 아이 첫 배내옷을 지어 주지 않았더라면, 걸음마를 가르치지 않았더라면 잃은 게 이렇게까지 가슴에 사무치지는 않았을 거예요. 저는 늘 그 아이가 제게 자식 하나 없는 걸 위로해 주라고 보내진 아이라고 말했지요. 그런데 이제 그 아이를 잃고 나니 옛날 일들이 자꾸만 떠올라서, 이 나이에도 그 아이 생각에 우는 걸 어쩔 수가 없습니다—정말 어쩔 수가 없어요, 선생님!”\n\n나는 클레멘츠 부인이 마음을 가라앉힐 시간을 주려고 잠시 기다렸다. 내가 그토록 오래 찾아 헤매던 그 빛이—아직은 아득히 멀리서나마—이 선량한 부인이 간직한 앤의 어린 시절 기억 속에서 희미하게 비쳐 오고 있는 것일까?\n\n“앤이 태어나기 전부터 캐서릭 부인을 아셨습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“그리 오래는 아니고요, 선생님—넉 달이 채 안 되었지요. 그동안 서로 자주 만나기는 했지만, 사이가 그리 살가웠던 적은 없었습니다.”\n\n그 대답을 할 때 부인의 목소리는 한결 안정되어 있었다. 그 회상들 가운데 많은 것이 아무리 괴로운 것이라 해도, 눈앞의 생생한 슬픔에 그토록 오래 잠겨 있다가 희미하게 보이는 옛 시절의 고통으로 되돌아가는 것이 부인의 마음에는 자기도 모르게 위안이 되고 있음을 나는 알아차렸다.\n\n“부인과 캐서릭 부인은 이웃이었습니까?” 나는 될 수 있는 대로 부인의 기억을 북돋아 이끌며 물었다.\n\n“네, 선생님—올드 웰밍엄에서 이웃이었지요.”\n\n“올드 웰밍엄이라고요? 그러면 햄프셔에 그 이름을 가진 곳이 두 군데 있는 겁니까?”\n\n“그게요, 선생님, 그 시절에는 그랬습니다—스물세 해도 더 전 일이지요. 강에 가깝게 두 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 새 읍을 세웠고, 원래도 마을 정도밖에 안 되던 올드 웰밍엄은 차츰 사람이 다 빠져나가 버렸어요. 지금 웰밍엄이라고들 부르는 곳은 새 읍입니다—하지만 옛 교구 교회는 여전히 그대로 교구 교회지요. 주위의 집들은 헐리거나 무너져 내리고 그 교회만 홀로 서 있답니다. 저도 참 서글픈 변화들을 다 보고 살았네요. 제가 살던 시절에는 정답고 어여쁜 곳이었는데요.”\n\n“결혼하시기 전부터 그곳에 사셨습니까, 클레멘츠 부인?”\n\n“아니요, 선생님—저는 노퍽 사람입니다. 제 남편이 원래 살던 고장도 아니었고요. 말씀드렸다시피 남편은 그림즈비 출신이라 거기서 도제 생활을 했습니다. 그런데 남쪽에 아는 이들이 있었고 자리가 났다는 말을 듣고서 사우샘프턴에서 장사를 시작했지요. 규모는 작았지만 평범한 사람 하나 물러앉기에 넉넉할 만큼은 벌어서 올드 웰밍엄에 자리를 잡았습니다. 저는 남편이 저와 혼인하면서 그이를 따라 그리로 갔지요. 우리 둘 다 젊은 나이는 아니었지만 아주 화목하게 살았습니다—한두 해 뒤에 올드 웰밍엄으로 온 이웃 캐서릭 씨가 그 부인과 살던 것보다야 훨씬 화목했지요.”\n\n“그전부터 바깥어른께서 그 부부를 아셨던 겁니까?”\n\n“캐서릭은 알았지요, 선생님—부인은 몰랐고요. 그 여자는 우리 둘 다에게 낯선 사람이었습니다. 어떤 신사 분들이 캐서릭을 위해 힘을 써 주어서 웰밍엄 교회의 서기 자리를 얻었는데, 그래서 우리 동네에 와 살게 된 것이었어요. 그이는 갓 혼인한 아내를 데리고 왔는데, 얼마 뒤에 그 여자가 사우샘프턴 근처 바넥 홀에 사는 집안에서 시녀로 있었다는 말을 들었습니다. 캐서릭은 그 여자가 어찌나 도도하게 굴었던지 혼인 승낙을 받아 내느라 애를 먹었다더군요. 청하고 또 청하다가, 그 여자가 하도 어깃장을 놓으니 결국 단념했답니다. 그런데 막상 단념하고 나니까 이번엔 정반대로 어깃장을 놓아, 아무 까닭도 이유도 없어 보이는데 제 발로 그이에게 왔다는 거예요. 제 가엾은 남편은 늘 그때가 그 여자 버릇을 고쳐 놓았어야 할 때였다고 말했습니다. 그렇지만 캐서릭은 그 여자를 너무 사랑해서 그런 짓은 할 사람이 못 되었지요—혼인 전에도 후에도 한 번 나무란 적이 없었어요. 감정이 앞서는 사람이라 이쪽으로든 저쪽으로든 그 감정에 너무 멀리 끌려다녔으니, 캐서릭 부인보다 나은 여자를 얻었더라도 그 사람은 아내를 버려 놓았을 겁니다. 저는 남의 험담을 하고 싶지 않습니다만, 선생님, 그 여자는 무정한 여자였어요. 무서운 고집을 지닌 데다—어리석은 칭찬과 고운 옷가지를 좋아하고, 캐서릭이 늘 다정하게 대해 주는데도 겉으로나마 마땅한 예의조차 차릴 줄 몰랐지요. 제 남편은 그 부부가 우리 옆에 살러 왔을 때부터 일이 좋지 않게 될 것 같다고 했는데, 그 말이 그대로 들어맞았습니다. 우리 동네에 온 지 넉 달이 채 되기도 전에 끔찍한 추문이 나고 그 집은 비참하게 깨어지고 말았어요. 둘 다 잘못이었지요—안타깝지만 둘 다 똑같이 잘못이었습니다.”\n\n“부부 둘 다를 말씀하시는 겁니까?”\n\n“아이고, 아니에요, 선생님! 캐서릭을 말하는 게 아닙니다—그이는 그저 딱할 뿐이었지요. 제가 말한 건 그 아내와 그 사람이—”\n\n“그러면 그 추문을 일으킨 사람 말씀이군요?”\n\n“그렇습니다, 선생님. 태생도 가정 교육도 훌륭한 신사였으니 마땅히 본을 보였어야 할 사람이지요. 선생님도 아시는 분입니다—그리고 제 가엾은 앤도 그이를 너무나 잘 알고 있었고요.”\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니까?”\n\n“네, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니다.”\n\n내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다—나는 마침내 실마리를 손에 쥐었다고 생각했다. 앞으로도 나를 헤매게 할 미로의 굽이굽이를 그때의 나는 얼마나 몰랐던가!\n\n“그 무렵 퍼시벌 경이 부인 동네에 살고 있었습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“아닙니다, 선생님. 그이는 낯선 사람으로 우리 사이에 나타났어요. 그이 부친이 얼마 전에 외국에서 세상을 떠난 참이었지요. 상복 차림이었던 게 기억납니다. 강가의 작은 여관에 묵었는데—그 여관은 그 뒤에 헐렸습니다—신사분들이 낚시하러 오면 묵던 곳이었지요. 처음 왔을 때는 별로 눈에 띄지도 않았습니다—잉글랜드 각지에서 신사들이 우리 강으로 낚시하러 오는 일은 흔했으니까요.”\n\n“앤이 태어나기 전에 그가 마을에 나타났습니까?”\n\n“네, 선생님. 앤은 1827년 6월에 태어났고요—그이는 4월 말이나 5월 초에 왔던 것 같습니다.”\n\n“여러분 모두에게 낯선 사람으로 왔다는 말씀이지요? 다른 이웃들에게도 그랬듯이 캐서릭 부인에게도 낯선 사람이었나요?”\n\n“처음에는 우리도 그렇게 생각했지요, 선생님. 그런데 추문이 터지고 나니 아무도 그 둘이 초면이었다고 믿지 않았습니다. 그 일이 어떻게 벌어졌는지 저는 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억해요. 어느 날 밤 캐서릭이 우리 집 마당에 들어와서는 오솔길의 자갈을 한 줌 집어 창문에 던져 우리를 깨웠습니다. 그이가 제 남편더러 제발 좀 내려와서 이야기를 들어 달라고 사정하는 소리가 들렸어요. 둘이 현관에서 한참을 이야기했지요. 남편이 이층으로 돌아왔을 때는 온몸을 떨고 있었습니다. 침대 가장자리에 앉더니 저에게 이러더군요. ‘리지! 내가 늘 그 여자는 못된 여자라고 하지 않았소—끝이 좋지 않을 거라고 늘 말했는데, 아무래도 그 끝이 벌써 온 것 같아 겁이 나오. 캐서릭이 제 아내 서랍 속에 감춰 둔 레이스 손수건 여러 장하고 좋은 반지 두 개, 새 금시계와 줄까지 찾아냈다는군—태생이 귀한 부인네나 가질 법한 물건들인데, 아내는 그것들이 어디서 났는지 말하지를 않는다지.’ ‘그이는 아내가 훔쳤다고 생각하는 건가요?’ 제가 물었습니다. ‘아니오.’ 남편이 말했어요. ‘훔쳤다면 그것도 나쁜 일이지만, 이건 그보다 더 나쁘오. 그 여자가 그런 물건을 훔칠 기회도 없었거니와, 기회가 있었대도 훔칠 여자가 아니오. 그건 선물이오, 리지—시계 안쪽에 그 여자 이름 첫 글자가 새겨져 있다는군—게다가 캐서릭은 아내가 그 상복 입은 신사, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 남몰래 이야기하고 유부녀답지 않게 굴어 대는 걸 보았다오. 당신은 이 일을 입 밖에 내지 마오—오늘 밤은 내가 캐서릭을 달래 놓았소. 입은 다물고 눈과 귀는 열어 두고서, 확실해질 때까지 하루 이틀만 기다리라고 일러두었소.’ ‘저는 두 분 다 잘못 보신 것 같아요.’ 제가 말했습니다. ‘여기서 저만큼 편안하고 남부럽지 않게 사는 캐서릭 부인이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 같은 뜨내기 낯선 사람과 놀아난다는 건 사람 이치에 맞지 않아요.’ ‘그렇지, 하지만 그 사람이 그 여자한테도 낯선 사람일까?’ 남편이 말했지요. ‘캐서릭의 아내가 어쩌다 그이와 혼인하게 되었는지 잊었소. 청할 때는 몇 번이고 싫다더니 제 발로 그이를 찾아갔지 않소. 리지, 세상에는 예로부터 자기 몸을 사랑해 준 정직한 사내를 제 이름을 지키는 방편으로 써먹은 못된 여자들이 있었소. 아무래도 이 캐서릭 부인도 그중에 가장 못된 여자만큼이나 못된 것 같아 몹시 걱정이오. 두고 보면 알겠지.’ 남편이 말했습니다. ‘머잖아 알게 될 거요.’ 그리고 꼭 이틀 뒤에 우리는 알게 되었지요.”\n\n클레멘츠 부인은 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이야기를 이어 갔다. 그 잠깐 사이에도 나는 내가 찾았다고 여긴 실마리가 정말로 이 미로의 한복판에 있는 수수께끼로 나를 이끌어 가고 있는 것인지 의심이 들기 시작했다. 남자의 배신과 여자의 부정이라는, 흔하디흔한 이 이야기가 과연 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 평생토록 떨게 한 그 비밀의 열쇠일 수 있단 말인가?\n\n“그래서요, 선생님, 캐서릭은 제 남편 말대로 기다렸습니다.” 클레멘츠 부인이 말을 이었다. “그리고 말씀드렸다시피 오래 기다릴 것도 없었지요. 이틀째 되는 날 그이는 제 아내와 퍼시벌 경이 교회 성구실 바로 아래에서 아주 스스럼없이 소곤거리고 있는 걸 보았습니다. 아마 두 사람은 성구실 근처야말로 세상에서 아무도 자기들을 찾을 생각을 않을 곳이라고 여겼겠지요. 하여간 어찌 되었든 두 사람은 거기 있었어요. 퍼시벌 경은 놀라고 당황한 듯 보였는데, 어찌나 켕기는 태도로 변명을 하던지 가엾은 캐서릭은—성미가 급한 사람이라고 이미 말씀드렸지요—제 치욕에 미친 사람처럼 되어 퍼시벌 경을 때렸습니다. 자기를 욕보인 그 사내의 적수가 되지 못했지요, 안타깝게도요. 소란을 듣고 달려온 이웃들이 두 사람을 뜯어말리기도 전에 그이는 참혹하게 얻어맞았습니다. 이 모든 일이 저녁 무렵에 벌어졌는데, 날이 저물기도 전에 제 남편이 캐서릭의 집에 가 보니 그이는 이미 떠나고 없었어요. 아무도 어디로 갔는지 몰랐지요. 마을에서 그이를 다시 본 사람은 하나도 없습니다. 그때쯤엔 아내가 자기와 혼인한 더러운 속셈이 무엇이었는지 너무나 잘 알게 되었고, 더구나 퍼시벌 경에게 그런 일까지 당하고 나니 제 비참함과 치욕을 견디기 어려웠던 거예요. 교구 목사님이 신문에 광고를 내어 돌아오라고, 자리도 벗들도 잃지 않을 거라고 사정했습니다. 그렇지만 캐서릭은 자존심과 기개가 너무 강해서—제가 보기엔 마음이 너무 여려서였지만요, 선생님—다시 이웃들 앞에 서서 제 치욕의 기억을 이겨 내며 살아갈 수가 없었던 겁니다. 제 남편은 그이가 잉글랜드를 떠난 뒤에 소식을 들었고, 그이가 아메리카에 자리 잡고 잘 지낸다는 소식을 한 번 더 들었어요. 제가 아는 한 그이는 지금도 거기서 살아 있습니다만, 이 옛 고향의 누구도—무엇보다 그 못된 아내는—다시는 그이를 볼 일이 없을 겁니다.”\n\n“퍼시벌 경은 어떻게 되었습니까?” 내가 물었다. “그 동네에 그대로 머물렀나요?”\n\n“천만에요, 선생님. 그 자리에 붙어 있기엔 너무 뜨거웠지요. 추문이 터진 바로 그날 밤 캐서릭 부인과 언성을 높여 다투는 소리가 들렸고, 이튿날 아침에 훌쩍 떠나 버렸습니다.”\n\n“그러면 캐서릭 부인은요? 설마 제 치욕을 다 아는 사람들 틈에 그대로 마을에 남지는 않았겠지요?”\n\n“남았습니다, 선생님. 이웃들의 눈총 따위는 대놓고 무시할 만큼 모질고 무정한 여자였으니까요. 그 여자는 목사님을 비롯해 만나는 사람마다 자기는 끔찍한 오해의 피해자라고, 이 동네의 그 어떤 입방아꾼도 자기를 죄지은 여자처럼 몰아내지는 못한다고 큰소리쳤습니다. 제가 거기 사는 동안 내내 그 여자는 올드 웰밍엄에 살았고, 제가 떠난 뒤에도 새 읍이 세워지면서 점잖은 이웃들이 그리로 옮겨 가자 자기도 옮겨 갔습니다. 마치 끝까지 그 사람들 틈에 살면서 그들에게 눈꼴사나운 꼴을 보이겠다고 작정한 사람처럼요. 지금도 거기 있고, 죽는 날까지 아무리 훌륭한 이들이 뭐라 해도 거기 눌러 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“그런데 그 오랜 세월을 어떻게 살아왔을까요?” 내가 물었다. “남편이 도울 형편도 되고 도울 마음도 있었습니까?”\n\n“형편도 되고 마음도 있었지요, 선생님.” 클레멘츠 부인이 말했다. “그이가 제 남편에게 보낸 두 번째 편지에 이렇게 썼습니다. 그래도 그 여자가 제 이름을 지녔고 제 집에서 살았으니, 아무리 못됐다 해도 길거리 거지처럼 굶겨서는 안 된다고요. 얼마간 생활비를 대 줄 형편은 되니 런던의 어느 곳에서 석 달에 한 번씩 찾아가면 된다고 했지요.”\n\n“그 여자가 그 생활비를 받았습니까?”\n\n“한 푼도 안 받았어요, 선생님. 백 살까지 산다 해도 캐서릭에게 한 입 한 모금도 신세 지지 않겠다고 했답니다. 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 말을 지켰지요. 제 가엾은 남편이 죽으면서 모든 걸 저에게 남겼을 때 캐서릭의 편지도 다른 물건들과 함께 제 손에 들어왔고, 저는 그 여자에게 궁하거든 언제든 알리라고 했습니다. ‘내가 캐서릭이나 캐서릭의 친구 나부랭이한테 궁하다고 말하느니,’ 그 여자가 말하더군요. ‘온 잉글랜드에 내가 궁하다고 알리겠소. 그걸 답으로 삼으시오. 그 사람이 또 편지하거든 그대로 답으로 전하시오.’”\n\n“그 여자에게 제 재산이 있었으리라고 보십니까?”\n\n“있어야 아주 조금이었을 겁니다, 선생님. 그 여자의 생활비가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게서 몰래 나온다는 말이 돌았는데, 아무래도 그건 사실이었던 것 같아요.”\n\n그 마지막 대답을 듣고 나서 나는 들은 이야기를 다시 헤아려 보려고 잠시 말이 없었다. 여기까지의 이야기를 남김없이 그대로 받아들인다면, 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그 비밀에 다가서는 길은 아직 내게 하나도 드러나지 않았다는 것, 그리고 내 목적의 추구는 또다시 가장 명백하고 가장 낙담스러운 실패와 마주하는 것으로 끝났다는 것이 이제 분명했다.\n\n그러나 그 이야기에는 그것을 남김없이 그대로 받아들여도 좋을지 의심하게 만드는 지점이 하나 있었고, 그 지점은 표면 아래 무언가 감추어져 있다는 생각을 불러일으켰다.\n\n교회 서기의 죄지은 아내가 남은 평생을 제 치욕의 무대에서 자진해 살아간다는 사정을 나는 스스로 납득할 수가 없었다. 자신의 결백을 몸으로 주장하기 위해 그 기이한 길을 택했다는 그 여자 자신의 말은 나를 만족시키지 못했다. 그보다는 그 여자가 이 일에서 스스로 주장하는 것만큼 자유로운 처지가 아니었다고 보는 편이 더 자연스럽고 더 그럴듯해 보였다. 그렇다면 그 여자를 웰밍엄에 붙들어 둘 힘을 지녔을 사람은 누구겠는가? 그 여자에게 살아갈 방편을 대 주는 바로 그 사람일 수밖에 없었다. 그 여자는 남편의 도움을 거절했고, 제 힘으로 살 만한 재산도 없었으며, 벗도 없이 몰락한 여자였다—소문이 가리키는 그 출처, 곧 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 말고 어디서 도움을 받을 수 있었겠는가?\n\n이런 가정 위에서 따져 보되, 나를 이끌어 줄 단 하나의 확실한 사실—캐서릭 부인이 그 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 사실—을 늘 염두에 두자, 나는 그 여자를 웰밍엄에 붙들어 두는 것이 퍼시벌 경에게 이롭다는 것을 어렵지 않게 이해할 수 있었다. 그곳에서의 그 여자의 평판은 이웃 여자들과의 왕래를 반드시 끊어 놓을 것이고, 캐묻기 좋아하는 벗과 허물없이 어울리다 무심코 입을 놀릴 기회를 아예 주지 않을 터였기 때문이다. 그런데 감추어야 할 그 수수께끼란 대체 무엇인가? 캐서릭 부인의 몰락에 퍼시벌 경이 부끄럽게 얽혀 있다는 것은 아니었다. 이웃 사람들이야말로 그것을 다 아는 이들이었으니까. 그가 앤의 아버지라는 의심도 아니었다. 웰밍엄이야말로 그 의심이 필연적으로 존재할 수밖에 없는 곳이었으니까. 내가 전해 들은 그 죄스러운 정황들을 남들이 받아들인 것처럼 나도 남김없이 받아들이고, 거기서 캐서릭 씨와 그 이웃들이 모두 끌어낸 것과 똑같은 피상적인 결론을 끌어낸다면, 내가 들은 그 모든 이야기 가운데 어디에 퍼시벌 경과 캐서릭 부인 사이에 그때부터 지금까지 감춰져 온 위험한 비밀이 있다는 암시가 있단 말인가?\n\n그런데도 그 몰래 만나던 자리, 서기의 아내와 “상복 입은 신사” 사이의 그 허물없는 속삭임 속에 발견의 실마리가 있다는 것만은 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n이 경우에 겉으로 드러난 정황은 한쪽을 가리키는데 진실은 내내 아무도 의심하지 않는 다른 쪽에 놓여 있었을 가능성은 없을까? 자신이 끔찍한 오해의 피해자라는 캐서릭 부인의 주장이 어쩌면 사실일 수는 없을까? 혹은 그것이 거짓이라 치더라도, 퍼시벌 경을 그 여자의 죄와 결부시킨 그 결론이 무언가 상상도 못 할 착오 위에 세워진 것일 수는 없을까? 퍼시벌 경이 혹시라도 옳은 다른 의심을 자기에게서 돌려놓기 위해 일부러 그 그릇된 의심을 자초한 것은 아닐까? 여기에—내가 찾아낼 수만 있다면—여기에, 방금 들은 별 볼 일 없어 보이는 이야기의 표면 밑 깊숙한 곳에 그 비밀로 다가서는 길이 있었다.\n\n이제 내 다음 질문들은 캐서릭 씨가 제 아내의 부정을 진실로 확신하기에 이르렀는가 아닌가를 확인하는 한 가지 목적을 향했다. 클레멘츠 부인에게서 받은 대답들은 그 점에 관해 내게 한 점의 의심도 남기지 않았다. 캐서릭 부인은 더없이 분명한 증거로 미루어, 처녀 시절에 이름 모를 누군가와의 일로 제 평판을 더럽혔고, 제 체면을 지키려고 혼인한 것이었다. 굳이 자세히 들어갈 필요 없는 시간과 장소의 계산을 통해, 그 남편의 성을 지닌 딸이 그 남편의 자식이 아니라는 것은 확실하게 밝혀져 있었다.\n\n다음 탐구의 대상, 곧 퍼시벌 경이 앤의 아버지임이 그와 똑같이 확실한가 하는 문제에는 훨씬 큰 어려움이 따랐다. 이 경우 나는 어느 쪽 개연성을 따져 보든 용모의 닮음이라는 시금석보다 나은 시금석을 쓸 처지가 못 되었다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경이 마을에 있는 동안 자주 보셨겠지요?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“네, 선생님, 아주 자주 보았지요.” 클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다.\n\n“앤이 그를 닮았다고 느끼신 적이 있습니까?”\n\n“그 아이는 그이를 조금도 닮지 않았습니다, 선생님.”\n\n“그럼 어머니는 닮았습니까?”\n\n“어머니도 닮지 않았어요, 선생님. 캐서릭 부인은 살빛이 검고 얼굴이 통통했지요.”\n\n어머니도 닮지 않고, 아버지라고들 하는 사람도 닮지 않았다. 용모의 닮음이라는 시금석을 곧이곧대로 믿을 수 없다는 것은 나도 알고 있었지만, 그렇다고 그 때문에 그것을 통째로 내버릴 것도 아니었다. 캐서릭 부인과 퍼시벌 경이 올드 웰밍엄에 나타나기 전의 삶에 관하여 결정적인 사실을 찾아냄으로써 이 증거를 보강할 수는 없을까? 다음 질문들은 그런 생각을 가지고 던진 것이었다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경이 처음 그 동네에 왔을 때,” 내가 말했다. “그가 직전에 어디에서 왔는지 들으셨습니까?”\n\n“아니요, 선생님. 어떤 이들은 블랙워터 파크에서 왔다 하고 어떤 이들은 스코틀랜드에서 왔다고 했지만—아무도 몰랐습니다.”\n\n“캐서릭 부인은 혼인하기 바로 전까지 바넥 홀에서 일하고 있었습니까?”\n\n“네, 선생님.”\n\n“그 자리에 오래 있었습니까?”\n\n“서너 해쯤요, 선생님. 몇 해인지는 확실치 않습니다.”\n\n“그 무렵 바넥 홀의 주인이 누구였는지 이름을 들으신 적 있습니까?”\n\n“네, 선생님. 돈손 소령이라는 분이었습니다.”\n\n“캐서릭 씨나 부인이 아시는 다른 누구라도, 퍼시벌 경이 돈손 소령의 친구라는 말을 들었거나 퍼시벌 경이 바넥 홀 근처에 있는 것을 본 적이 있습니까?”\n\n“캐서릭은 그런 적 없었습니다, 선생님, 제가 기억하는 한은요—제가 아는 한 다른 누구도 없었고요.”\n\n나는 돈손 소령이 아직 살아 있을지도 모르고 훗날 그에게 문의하는 것이 쓸모 있을지도 모른다는 생각에서 그의 이름과 주소를 적어 두었다. 한편 내 마음에 남은 인상은 이제 퍼시벌 경이 앤의 아버지라는 견해에는 확실히 부정적인 쪽으로, 그리고 그가 캐서릭 부인과 몰래 만난 그 비밀은 그 여자가 제 남편의 명예에 안긴 치욕과는 전혀 무관하다는 결론에는 확실히 우호적인 쪽으로 기울어 있었다. 이 인상을 굳혀 줄 만한 추가 질문은 더 떠오르지 않았다—나로서는 클레멘츠 부인이 이제 앤의 어린 시절을 이야기하도록 이끌면서, 그런 식으로 우연히 떠오를지 모를 어떤 실마리를 지켜보는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n“아직 못 들은 이야기가 있습니다.” 내가 말했다. “그 죄와 비참함 속에 태어난 가엾은 아이가 어떻게 부인의 손에 맡겨지게 되었는지 말입니다, 클레멘츠 부인.”\n\n“그 가엾고 힘없는 것을 거두어 줄 사람이 달리 아무도 없었거든요, 선생님.” 클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다. “그 못된 어미는 태어난 날부터 그 아이를 미워하는 것 같았어요—가엾은 갓난것이 무슨 잘못이라도 한 것처럼요! 저는 그 아이가 안쓰러워 견딜 수가 없어서 제 자식처럼 애지중지 키우겠다고 나섰지요.”\n\n“앤은 그때부터 줄곧 부인의 손에서 자랐습니까?”\n\n“아주 줄곧은 아니었어요, 선생님. 캐서릭 부인은 이따금 변덕이 나서, 제가 키우는 게 심술이 났는지 가끔씩 아이를 제 것이라며 데려가곤 했습니다. 그렇지만 그런 발작은 오래가는 법이 없었지요. 가엾은 어린 앤은 늘 저에게 돌아왔고, 늘 돌아오는 걸 기뻐했습니다—다른 아이들처럼 같이 놀 동무도 없이 우리 집에서 쓸쓸하게 지냈는데도 말이에요. 가장 오래 떨어져 있었던 건 그 어미가 아이를 리머리지로 데려갔을 때였습니다. 마침 그때 제가 남편을 잃었는데, 그 참담한 슬픔 가운데 앤이 집에 없었던 게 차라리 다행이다 싶었지요. 그때 그 아이는 열 살에서 열한 살 사이였는데, 딱하게도 배우는 게 더디고 다른 아이들처럼 밝지도 않았지만—보기에는 더할 나위 없이 어여쁜 계집아이였습니다. 저는 그 어미가 아이를 데려올 때까지 집에서 기다렸다가, 그 아이를 런던으로 데려가겠다고 청했어요—사실은요, 선생님, 남편이 죽은 뒤로는 도무지 올드 웰밍엄에 머무를 마음이 나지 않았습니다. 그곳이 너무 달라지고 너무 쓸쓸해져서요.”\n\n“캐서릭 부인이 그 제안을 받아들였습니까?”\n\n“아니요, 선생님. 그 여자는 북쪽에서 그 어느 때보다 모질고 독해져서 돌아왔습니다. 사람들 말로는 애초에 떠나려면 퍼시벌 경의 허락부터 받아야 했다고 하더군요. 그리고 리머리지로 죽어 가는 제 언니를 간호하러 간 것도 그 가엾은 이가 돈을 모아 두었다는 소문 때문이었는데—실은 장례 치를 만큼도 남기지 못했다지요. 그런 일들이 캐서릭 부인의 심사를 뒤틀어 놓았을 법도 합니다만, 어찌 되었든 그 여자는 제가 아이를 데려가겠다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았습니다. 우리 둘을 갈라놓아 둘 다 괴롭히는 걸 즐기는 것 같았어요. 제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 앤에게 제 주소를 일러 주고, 혹시라도 어려운 일이 생기면 저를 찾아오라고 몰래 말해 두는 것뿐이었지요. 그렇지만 그 아이가 자유로이 올 수 있게 되기까지는 몇 해가 걸렸습니다. 그 아이가 정신병원에서 도망쳐 나온 그날 밤까지, 가엾은 것을 저는 다시 보지 못했어요.”\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 왜 그 아이를 가두었는지 아십니까, 클레멘츠 부인?”\n\n“저는 앤이 저에게 해 준 말밖에는 모릅니다, 선생님. 그 가엾은 것은 그 이야기가 나오면 딱하게도 횡설수설 종잡을 수 없게 되곤 했어요. 제 어미가 퍼시벌 경의 무슨 비밀을 지키고 있었는데, 제가 햄프셔를 떠난 지 한참 뒤에 그걸 그 아이에게 흘렸다고 하더군요—그리고 퍼시벌 경이 그 아이가 그걸 안다는 걸 알고서 가두었다고요. 하지만 제가 물어도 그게 무엇인지는 끝내 말하지 못했습니다. 그 아이가 해 줄 수 있는 말이라고는, 제 어미가 마음만 먹으면 퍼시벌 경을 파멸시켜 망하게 할 수 있다는 것뿐이었어요. 캐서릭 부인이 흘린 것도 딱 그만큼이지 그 이상은 아니었을 겁니다. 앤이 정말로 제가 안다고 내세운 만큼 알고 있었다면—또 가엾게도 스스로 안다고 여겼던 만큼 알고 있었다면—저에게 사실을 다 털어놓았을 게 거의 틀림없거든요.”\n\n이런 생각은 내 마음에도 한 번 이상 떠올랐던 것이었다. 로라와 앤 캐서릭이 배 창고에서 포스코 백작에게 방해받았을 때 로라가 정말로 무슨 중대한 발견을 앞두고 있었는지 의심스럽다고 나는 이미 매리언에게 말한 바 있었다. 어머니가 제 앞에서 무심코 흘린 암시에서 나온 막연한 의심 말고는 아무 근거도 없이 그 비밀을 온전히 안다고 여기는 것은 앤의 정신 질환에 꼭 들어맞는 일이었다. 그렇다면 퍼시벌 경의 켕기는 의심은 앤이 제 어미에게서 다 들어 알고 있다는 그릇된 생각을 그에게 어김없이 불어넣었을 것이고, 그것은 나중에 제 아내가 앤에게서 다 들어 알고 있다는 똑같이 그릇된 의심을 그의 마음에 박아 넣은 것과 다르지 않았다.\n\n시간은 흘러 아침나절이 저물어 가고 있었다. 더 머문다 한들 클레멘츠 부인에게서 내 목적에 조금이라도 쓸모 있는 이야기를 더 들을 수 있을지는 의심스러웠다. 나는 캐서릭 부인에 관하여 찾고 있던 그 고장과 집안의 자세한 사정들을 이미 알아냈고, 앞으로의 행보를 정하는 데 대단히 큰 도움이 될 만한, 나로서는 전혀 새로운 몇 가지 결론에 이르러 있었다. 나는 작별을 고하고, 기꺼이 정보를 나누어 준 클레멘츠 부인의 다정한 호의에 감사를 표하려고 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n“제가 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는다고 여기셨을 것 같아 송구합니다.” 내가 말했다. “웬만한 사람이라면 대답하고 싶지 않았을 만큼 많은 질문으로 폐를 끼쳤습니다.”\n\n“제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 드리겠습니다, 선생님.” 클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다. 부인은 말을 멈추고 애타는 눈으로 나를 바라보았다. “하지만 저는요.” 그 가엾은 부인이 말했다. “선생님께서 앤에 대해 조금만 더 말씀해 주셨으면 싶습니다. 들어오실 때 선생님 얼굴에서 무언가 말씀해 주실 수 있을 것 같은 기색을 본 듯했거든요. 그 아이가 살았는지 죽었는지조차 모른다는 게 얼마나 견디기 힘든지 선생님은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 확실하기만 하다면 차라리 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 텐데요. 선생님은 그 아이를 다시 살아서 보리라고는 기대하지 않는다고 하셨지요. 아십니까, 선생님—정말로 아십니까—하느님께서 그 아이를 데려가셨는지요?”\n\n나는 이 호소를 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 이것을 뿌리쳤다면 그것은 말할 수 없이 비열하고 잔인한 짓이었을 것이다.\n\n“안타깝지만 사실인 것을 의심할 여지가 없습니다.” 나는 부드럽게 대답했다. “그 아이가 이 세상에서 겪던 고통이 끝났다는 것을 저는 마음속으로 확신합니다.”\n\n가엾은 부인은 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 내게서 얼굴을 감추었다. “아이고, 선생님.” 부인이 말했다. “그걸 어떻게 아십니까? 누가 그런 말을 했단 말입니까?”\n\n“누가 말해 준 것이 아닙니다, 클레멘츠 부인. 그러나 제게는 그것을 확신할 만한 근거가 있습니다—안전하게 설명할 수 있게 되는 대로 반드시 알려 드리겠다고 약속하는 그런 근거 말입니다. 저는 그 아이가 마지막 순간에 돌봄을 받지 못한 것이 아니었음을 확신합니다—그토록 딱하게 앓던 심장병이 그 아이의 참된 사인이었다는 것도 확신합니다. 머잖아 부인께서도 저만큼 이것을 확신하시게 될 겁니다—그 아이가 조용한 시골 교회 묘지에, 부인께서 손수 골라 주셨을 법한 어여쁘고 평화로운 자리에 묻혀 있다는 것도 오래지 않아 아시게 될 거예요.”\n\n“죽었다니!” 클레멘츠 부인이 말했다. “그 어린 나이에 죽고, 나는 남아서 그 소식을 듣는구나! 내가 그 아이 첫 배내옷을 지었는데. 내가 걸음마를 가르쳤는데. 그 아이가 난생처음 엄마라고 부른 것도 나한테였는데—그런데 이제 나는 남고 앤은 가 버렸다니!” 가엾은 부인은 얼굴에서 손수건을 떼고 처음으로 나를 올려다보며 말했다. “선생님, 방금 그 아이가 곱게 묻혔다고 하셨습니까? 정말로 제 친자식이었다면 치러 주었을 그런 장례였나요?”\n\n나는 그렇다고 부인을 안심시켰다. 부인은 내 대답에 알 수 없는 자부심을 느끼는 듯했고—더 높고 다른 어떤 위로도 줄 수 없는 위안을 거기서 찾는 듯했다. “앤이 곱게 묻히지 못했다면 제 가슴이 미어졌을 겁니다.” 부인이 담담하게 말했다. “그런데 선생님은 그걸 어떻게 아십니까? 누가 말해 주었나요?” 나는 다시 한번 모든 것을 숨김없이 말할 수 있을 때까지 기다려 달라고 간청했다. “저를 다시 보시게 될 겁니다.” 내가 말했다. “마음이 조금 가라앉으시거든—아마 하루 이틀 안에—청할 부탁이 있으니까요.”\n\n“저 때문에 미루지 마십시오, 선생님.” 클레멘츠 부인이 말했다. “제가 쓸모가 될 수 있다면 제가 우는 건 아랑곳 마세요. 저에게 하실 말씀이 마음에 있으시거든, 선생님, 지금 해 주십시오.”\n\n“마지막으로 한 가지만 여쭙고자 합니다.” 내가 말했다. “웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인 주소만 알고 싶습니다.”\n\n내 청에 클레멘츠 부인은 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 순간만은 앤의 죽음이라는 소식조차 머릿속에서 밀려난 듯했다. 흐르던 눈물이 갑자기 멎었고, 부인은 어리둥절한 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n“맙소사, 선생님!” 부인이 말했다. “캐서릭 부인에게 무슨 볼일이 있으시다고요!”\n\n“이런 볼일입니다, 클레멘츠 부인.” 내가 대답했다. “저는 그 여자와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 몰래 만나던 그 일의 비밀을 알고 싶습니다. 부인께서 들려주신 그 여자의 지난 행실과 그 남자가 그 여자와 맺었던 지난 관계에는, 부인도 이웃 누구도 짐작조차 못 한 무언가가 더 있습니다. 그 둘 사이에는 우리 중 누구도 모르는 비밀이 있고, 저는 그것을 기어이 밝혀내겠다는 결심으로 캐서릭 부인을 찾아가려는 겁니다.”\n\n“다시 한번 생각해 보십시오, 선생님!” 클레멘츠 부인이 간절한 마음에 자리에서 일어나 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. “그 여자는 무서운 여자예요—선생님은 제가 아는 만큼 그 여자를 모르십니다. 다시 한번 생각해 보세요.”\n\n“부인의 경고가 호의에서 나온 것임을 잘 압니다, 클레멘츠 부인. 그러나 무슨 일이 있더라도 저는 그 여자를 만나기로 마음먹었습니다.”\n\n클레멘츠 부인은 근심스러운 눈으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n“마음을 굳히셨군요, 선생님.” 부인이 말했다. “주소를 알려 드리겠습니다.”\n\n나는 그것을 수첩에 적고 나서 작별 인사를 하려고 부인의 손을 잡았다.\n\n“곧 소식 전해 드리겠습니다.” 내가 말했다. “말씀드리기로 약속한 것을 모두 아시게 될 겁니다.”\n\n클레멘츠 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 미덥지 않다는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n“늙은이의 충고가 때로는 새겨들을 만한 법입니다, 선생님.” 부인이 말했다. “웰밍엄에 가시기 전에 다시 한번 생각해 보세요.”\n\n# VIII\n\n클레멘츠 부인과의 면담을 마치고 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때, 나는 로라에게 어떤 변화가 나타난 것을 보고 가슴이 철렁했다.\n\n오랜 불행이 그토록 모질게 시험하고도 끝내 꺾지 못했던 그 한결같은 온화함과 인내가, 이제 갑자기 그녀를 저버린 듯했다. 달래고 기분을 풀어 주려는 매리언의 온갖 노력에도 아랑곳없이, 그녀는 손도 대지 않은 그림을 탁자 저편으로 밀어 둔 채, 눈을 완강히 내리깔고, 무릎 위에서 손가락을 쉼 없이 얽었다 풀었다 하며 앉아 있었다. 내가 들어서자 매리언은 얼굴에 말 없는 근심을 띠고 일어섰고, 내가 다가가면 로라가 고개를 들까 싶어 잠시 지켜보더니, 나에게 “당신이 기운을 북돋아 줄 수 있는지 해 봐요” 하고 속삭이고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n나는 비어 있는 의자에 앉아—그 가엾고 여위고 쉴 줄 모르는 손가락을 부드럽게 풀어 주고, 그녀의 두 손을 내 손으로 감쌌다.\n\n“무슨 생각을 하고 있어요, 로라? 말해 봐요, 내 사랑—그게 무엇인지 말해 보려고 애써 봐요.”\n\n그녀는 스스로와 싸우다가 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보았다. “행복하다고 느낄 수가 없어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “자꾸만 생각이 나서——” 그녀는 말을 멈추고 몸을 조금 앞으로 숙이더니 내 어깨에 머리를 기댔다. 그 무섭도록 말 없는 무력함이 내 가슴을 찔렀다.\n\n“말해 보려고 해 봐요.” 나는 부드럽게 되풀이했다. “왜 행복하지 않은지 말해 보려고 해 봐요.”\n\n“나는 너무 쓸모가 없어요—두 사람한테 짐만 되고요.” 그녀가 지치고 희망 없는 한숨과 함께 대답했다. “당신은 일해서 돈을 벌고, 매리언은 당신을 돕잖아요. 왜 나는 할 수 있는 게 하나도 없죠? 결국 당신은 나보다 매리언을 더 좋아하게 될 거예요—그렇게 될 거예요, 내가 이렇게 아무것도 못 하니까! 아, 제발, 제발, 제발 나를 어린애처럼 대하지 말아요!”\n\n나는 그녀의 머리를 들어 올리고, 얼굴 위로 흘러내린 헝클어진 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 입을 맞추었다—나의 가엾고 빛바랜 꽃이여! 잃어버린, 상처 입은 나의 누이여! “당신은 우리를 도울 거예요, 로라.” 내가 말했다. “오늘부터 시작하는 거예요, 내 사랑.”\n\n그녀는 열에 들뜬 간절함으로, 숨 막힐 듯한 관심으로 나를 바라보았다. 그 몇 마디로 내가 불러낸 희망의 새 생명이 도리어 나를 떨리게 할 만큼.\n\n나는 일어나 그녀의 화구를 정돈해서 다시 그녀 곁에 놓아 주었다.\n\n“내가 그림을 그려 일하고 돈을 버는 건 알고 있죠.” 내가 말했다. “이제 당신도 그토록 공을 들였고 그토록 솜씨가 늘었으니, 당신도 일해서 돈을 벌기 시작하는 거예요. 이 작은 스케치를 할 수 있는 한 곱고 예쁘게 마무리해 봐요. 다 되면 내가 가지고 나가서, 내 그림을 전부 사 주는 바로 그 사람에게 팔아 줄게요. 당신이 번 돈은 당신 지갑에 넣어 두는 거예요. 그리고 매리언이 나를 도우러 오는 만큼 자주 당신한테도 우리를 도우러 올 거고요. 당신이 우리 두 사람에게 얼마나 쓸모 있는 사람이 될지 생각해 봐요. 그러면 곧 하루가 긴 만큼 내내 행복해질 거예요, 로라.”\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 간절해지더니 미소로 환해졌다. 그 미소가 머무는 동안, 밀쳐 두었던 연필을 다시 집어 드는 그 순간, 그녀는 거의 지난날의 로라처럼 보였다.\n\n나는 그녀의 마음속에서 새로운 성장과 힘이 처음 내비친 신호를 옳게 읽어 냈던 것이다. 그 신호는 언니와 나의 삶을 채우고 있는 일에 그녀가 관심을 보인 데서 저도 모르게 드러나 있었다. 매리언은 (내가 그동안의 일을 이야기하자) 나와 마찬가지로, 로라가 자기 나름의 작은 소임을 맡아 자기 눈에도 우리 눈에도 스스로를 높이고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아차렸다—그리고 그날부터 우리는 이제 멀지 않을지도 모를 희망차고 더 행복한 미래를 약속해 주는 그 새로운 야심을 정성껏 거들었다. 그녀가 완성한, 혹은 완성하려 애쓴 그림들은 내 손에 맡겨졌다. 매리언은 그것을 나에게서 받아 조심스레 감추어 두었고, 나는 내 벌이에서 매주 얼마씩을 따로 떼어, 그 가엾고 희미하고 값어치 없는—내가 유일한 구매자였던—스케치의 값으로 낯선 사람들이 치른 돈인 양 그녀에게 건넸다. 그녀가 자랑스럽게 지갑을 꺼내 살림에 제 몫을 보태면서, 그 주에 나와 자기 가운데 누가 더 많이 벌었는지 자못 진지하게 궁금해할 때면, 우리의 그 죄 없는 속임수를 지켜 내기가 때때로 힘들었다. 감춰 둔 그 그림들을 나는 지금도 모두 간직하고 있다—값을 매길 수 없는 나의 보물이요—내가 살려 두기를 좋아하는 소중한 기억이요—내 마음이 결코 놓지 않을, 내 애틋함이 결코 잊지 않을, 지난 역경 속의 벗들이다.\n\n나는 지금 내 임무의 절박함을 두고 한가한 소리를 하고 있는 것인가? 내 이야기가 아직 이르지 못한 더 행복한 시절을 앞질러 바라보고 있는 것인가? 그렇다. 다시 돌아가자—의혹과 두려움의 나날로, 끝없는 불안의 얼음 같은 정적 속에서 내 안의 정신이 살아남으려 안간힘을 쓰던 그 시절로. 나는 앞으로 나아가던 길에서 잠시 멈추어 쉬었다. 이 글을 읽는 벗들도 함께 멈추어 쉬었다면, 어쩌면 헛되이 보낸 시간은 아니리라.\n\n나는 매리언과 단둘이 이야기할 기회를 되도록 빨리 잡아, 그날 아침 내가 벌인 조사의 결과를 그녀에게 전했다. 웰밍엄으로 가겠다는 내 계획에 대해 그녀는 이미 클레멘츠 부인이 나에게 밝혔던 것과 같은 의견을 품고 있는 듯했다.\n\n“월터, 정말이지,” 그녀가 말했다, “캐서릭 부인의 신뢰를 얻으리라 기대할 만큼 아직 충분히 알고 있는 건 아니잖아요? 목적을 이룰 더 안전하고 간단한 수단을 정말로 다 써 보기도 전에 그런 극단으로 나아가는 게 현명할까요? 로라가 여행한 정확한 날짜를 아는 사람이 세상에 퍼시벌 경과 백작 둘뿐이라고 당신이 말했을 때, 당신도 나도 그 날짜를 반드시 알고 있을 세 번째 사람을 잊고 있었어요—루벨 부인 말이에요. 퍼시벌 경에게서 억지로 자백을 받아 내는 것보다 그 여자에게서 받아 내는 편이 훨씬 쉽고 훨씬 덜 위험하지 않겠어요?”\n\n“쉬울지도 모르지요.” 내가 대답했다. “하지만 루벨 부인이 그 음모에 어느 정도까지 가담했고 어떤 이해관계를 가졌는지 우리는 전부 알지 못하고, 따라서 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 머릿속에 그 날짜가 분명히 새겨져 있는 것처럼 그 여자의 머릿속에도 새겨져 있으리라 확신할 수 없습니다. 퍼시벌 경의 생애에서 공략할 수 있는 단 하나의 지점을 찾아내는 데 무엇보다 소중할 시간을, 이제 와서 루벨 부인에게 허비하기에는 너무 늦었어요. 매리언, 내가 햄프셔로 돌아갔을 때 무릅쓸 위험을 너무 심각하게 생각하는 것 아닙니까? 결국 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 나보다 한 수 위가 아닐까 의심하기 시작한 건가요?”\n\n“그 사람은 당신보다 한 수 위가 되지 못해요.” 그녀가 단호하게 대답했다. “당신에게 맞서는 데 백작의 그 헤아릴 수 없는 악의 도움을 받지 못할 테니까요.”\n\n“무엇을 보고 그런 결론을 내렸습니까?” 나는 다소 놀라며 되물었다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경의 고집과, 백작의 통제를 견디지 못하는 그 성미를 내가 잘 아니까요.” 그녀가 대답했다. “그 사람은 혼자서 당신을 상대하겠다고 우길 거예요—블랙워터 파크에서 처음에 제 힘으로 일을 처리하겠다고 우겼던 것처럼요. 백작이 끼어들지 의심해야 할 때는, 당신이 퍼시벌 경을 손아귀에 넣었을 때예요. 그때는 백작 자신의 이익이 곧바로 위협받을 테고, 그러면 그는 자기를 지키기 위해 무서운 목적으로 움직일 거예요, 월터.”\n\n“미리 그의 무기를 빼앗아 둘 수도 있지요.” 내가 말했다. “클레멘츠 부인에게서 들은 이야기 가운데 몇 가지는 아직 그에게 불리하게 쓸 수 있을지 모르고, 사건을 더 굳건히 다질 다른 수단도 우리에게 있을 겁니다. 마이컬슨 부인의 진술에는 백작이 페얼리 씨와 연락을 취할 필요가 있었음을 보여 주는 대목이 있고, 그 과정에서 그를 옭아맬 정황이 있을지도 모릅니다. 매리언, 내가 떠나 있는 동안 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 써서, 백작과 그 사이에 오간 일을 정확히 적어 보내 달라고, 아울러 같은 시기에 조카에 관해 그가 알게 된 사정이 있으면 그것도 알려 달라고 답장을 요구하세요. 그가 자진해서 그 진술을 내놓기를 꺼린다면 언젠가는 반드시 요구받게 될 것이라고 일러 두고요.”\n\n“편지는 쓸게요, 월터. 그런데 정말로 웰밍엄에 갈 작정인가요?”\n\n“확고히 작정했습니다. 다음 이틀은 다가올 한 주에 필요한 돈을 버는 데 쓰고, 사흘째 되는 날 햄프셔로 가겠습니다.”\n\n사흘째 날이 오자 나는 떠날 채비를 마쳤다.\n\n얼마간 집을 비우게 될지도 몰랐으므로, 나는 매리언과 날마다 서로 편지를 주고받기로 정해 두었다—물론 조심하느라 서로 가명으로 부르기로 했다. 그녀에게서 규칙적으로 소식이 오는 한 나는 아무 일도 없다고 여길 참이었다. 그러나 아침이 되어도 편지가 오지 않는다면, 나는 두말없이 첫 기차로 런던에 돌아올 것이었다. 나는 로라에게 그녀의 그림과 내 그림을 사 줄 새 구매자를 찾으러 시골에 간다고 말해 내 출발을 납득시켰고, 그녀를 일거리에 몰두한 행복한 모습으로 남겨 두고 나왔다. 매리언이 아래층 현관문까지 나를 따라 내려왔다.\n\n“여기 얼마나 애타는 마음들을 두고 가는지 잊지 말아요.” 복도에 함께 서 있을 때 그녀가 속삭였다. “당신이 무사히 돌아오기를 바라는 온갖 희망도요. 이번 여행에서 이상한 일이 생기거든—당신과 퍼시벌 경이 마주치거든——”\n\n“우리가 마주치리라고 생각하는 까닭이 뭡니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“모르겠어요—까닭을 알 수 없는 두려움과 망상이 자꾸 들어요. 웃어도 좋아요, 월터—하지만 제발, 그 사람과 맞닥뜨리거든 부디 화를 참아요!”\n\n“걱정 말아요, 매리언! 내 자제력은 내가 책임집니다.”\n\n그 말을 끝으로 우리는 헤어졌다.\n\n나는 힘차게 역으로 걸어갔다. 내 안에 희망의 불빛이 있었다. 이번 여행은 헛되지 않으리라는 확신이 마음속에서 자라고 있었다. 맑고 서늘한 좋은 아침이었다. 신경은 팽팽히 조여져 있었고, 결의의 힘이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 씩씩하게 요동치는 것이 느껴졌다.\n\n승강장을 가로지르며 아는 얼굴이 있는지 모여 선 사람들 사이를 좌우로 둘러보는 동안, 햄프셔로 떠나기 전에 변장을 했더라면 유리하지 않았을까 하는 의문이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 생각에는 나를 밀어내는 무엇이—변장을 한다는 그 행위 자체에 흔해빠진 밀정과 밀고자 무리처럼 비열한 무엇이—있었기에, 나는 그 의문이 떠오르자마자 거의 곧바로 고려 대상에서 지워 버렸다. 순전히 편의의 문제로 따져도 그 방책은 극히 미심쩍었다. 집에서 그런 시도를 한다면 집주인이 언젠가는 나를 알아보고 즉시 의심을 품을 터였다. 집 밖에서 시도한다면 같은 사람들이 지극히 사소한 우연으로 변장한 나와 변장하지 않은 나를 다 보게 될 수 있고, 그러면 나는 무엇보다 피해야 할 주목과 불신을 스스로 불러들이는 셈이었다. 나는 여기까지 내 본모습으로 행동해 왔다—그리고 끝까지 내 본모습으로 밀고 나가기로 결심했다.\n\n기차는 이른 오후에 나를 웰밍엄에 내려놓았다.\n\n아라비아 사막의 어느 모래 황무지가, 팔레스타인 폐허의 어느 황량한 풍경이, 존재의 첫 단계에 있으면서 번영의 과도기에 놓인 영국 시골 소읍이 눈에 주는 거부감과 마음에 끼치는 침울함에 견줄 수 있으랴? 웰밍엄 거리의 말끔한 황량함, 단정한 추함, 얌전한 무기력 속을 지나며 나는 스스로에게 그렇게 물었다. 그리고 쓸쓸한 가게에서 나를 빤히 내다보던 상인들도—미완성인 초승달꼴 거리와 광장의 메마른 유배지에서 힘없이 늘어진 나무들도—생명의 숨결로 저희를 살려 낼 사람이라는 요소를 헛되이 기다리는 죽은 집의 시체들도—내가 본 모든 생물, 내가 지나친 모든 사물이 한목소리로 이렇게 대답하는 것 같았다. 아라비아의 사막은 우리네 문명화된 황량함을 알지 못하고—팔레스타인의 폐허는 우리네 현대의 음울함을 감당하지 못한다고!\n\n나는 캐서릭 부인이 사는 동네를 물어 찾아갔고, 그곳에 이르러 보니 단층 소형 주택들이 늘어선 광장 안이었다. 한가운데에는 값싼 철망 울타리로 둘러친 휑한 잔디밭이 조금 있었다. 나이 지긋한 유모 하나와 아이 둘이 울타리 한구석에 서서, 잔디에 매어 놓은 여윈 염소를 바라보고 있었다. 집들 앞 보도 한쪽에서는 행인 둘이 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 다른 쪽에서는 한가한 사내아이가 한가한 강아지를 끈에 매어 끌고 가고 있었다. 멀리서 피아노의 둔탁한 소리가 들리고, 그보다 가까운 곳에서 간간이 망치 두드리는 소리가 섞여 들었다. 내가 그 광장에 들어섰을 때 나를 맞이한 삶의 광경과 소리는 그것이 전부였다.\n\n나는 곧장 13번지—캐서릭 부인 집의 번지—문 앞으로 걸어가, 안에 들어가서 어떻게 나를 소개하는 것이 가장 좋을지 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고 문을 두드렸다. 우선 급한 것은 캐서릭 부인을 만나는 일이었다. 그런 다음에야 내가 직접 관찰한 바에 따라, 내 방문의 목적에 다가가는 가장 안전하고 손쉬운 방법을 판단할 수 있을 터였다.\n\n문을 연 사람은 우울한 얼굴의 중년 하녀였다. 나는 명함을 건네며 캐서릭 부인을 뵐 수 있는지 물었다. 명함이 앞쪽 응접실로 들어갔고, 하녀는 무슨 용건인지 밝혀 달라는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다.\n\n“미안하지만, 캐서릭 부인의 따님에 관한 용건이라고 전해 주십시오.” 내가 대답했다. 그 자리에서 얼른 떠올릴 수 있는, 내 방문을 설명할 가장 그럴듯한 구실이었다.\n\n하녀는 다시 응접실로 물러갔다가 다시 돌아왔고, 이번에는 침울한 놀라움이 서린 얼굴로 들어오시라고 청했다.\n\n나는 벽에 가장 큰 무늬의 요란한 벽지를 바른 작은 방으로 들어섰다. 의자며 탁자며 찬장이며 소파가 모두 값싼 가구의 끈적한 광택으로 번들거렸다. 방 한가운데 가장 큰 탁자 위에는 빨갛고 노란 모직 깔개 정중앙에 정확히 놓인 말쑥한 성경책이 있었고, 창에 가장 가까운 탁자 옆에는—무릎에 작은 뜨개질 바구니를 얹고, 발치에 숨을 쌕쌕대는 눈곱 낀 늙은 스패니얼을 웅크린 채로—검은 망사 모자에 검은 비단 가운을 입고 두 손에 슬레이트빛 반장갑을 낀 나이 든 여인이 앉아 있었다. 희끗희끗한 잿빛 머리는 얼굴 양옆으로 묵직하게 갈라져 내려와 있었고—검은 눈은 굳세고 도전적이고 무자비한 응시로 똑바로 앞을 향하고 있었다. 볼은 도톰하고 각졌으며, 턱은 길고 단단했고, 입술은 두툼하고 관능적이면서 핏기가 없었다. 몸집은 다부지고 튼튼했으며, 태도는 공격적일 만큼 침착했다. 이 사람이 캐서릭 부인이었다.\n\n“제 딸 이야기를 하러 오셨군요.” 내가 미처 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그녀가 말했다. “하실 말씀이 무엇인지 말씀해 보시지요.”\n\n목소리의 어조는 눈빛만큼이나 굳세고 도전적이고 무자비했다. 그녀는 의자를 가리켰고, 내가 거기 앉는 동안 머리끝에서 발끝까지 나를 유심히 훑어보았다. 이 여자를 상대할 유일한 기회는 그녀 자신의 어조로 말하는 것, 면담이 시작되는 순간부터 그녀 자신의 땅에서 그녀를 맞상대하는 것뿐임을 나는 알아차렸다.\n\n“따님이 행방불명된 것은 알고 계시겠지요?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“물론 잘 알고 있습니다.”\n\n“행방불명이라는 불행에 뒤이어 사망이라는 불행이 닥쳤을지도 모른다는 염려를 해 보신 적은 없습니까?”\n\n“있지요. 그 애가 죽었다는 말을 하러 오셨나요?”\n\n“그렇습니다.”\n\n“왜죠?”\n\n그녀는 목소리도 얼굴도 태도도 조금도 바꾸지 않은 채 그 기이한 질문을 던졌다. 바깥 울타리 안의 염소가 죽었다는 이야기를 했더라도 그보다 더 완벽하게 무심해 보일 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n“왜냐고요?” 나는 되물었다. “따님의 죽음을 알려 드리러 제가 여기 온 까닭을 물으시는 겁니까?”\n\n“그래요. 나에게든 그 애에게든 무슨 관심이 있으시죠? 내 딸에 대해 대체 어떻게 아시게 됐나요?”\n\n“이렇게 되었습니다. 저는 따님이 정신병원에서 도망친 날 밤에 그분을 만났고, 안전한 곳에 이르도록 도왔습니다.”\n\n“아주 잘못하신 겁니다.”\n\n“그 애 어머니께 그런 말을 들으니 유감입니다.”\n\n“그 애 어머니가 하는 말이 그래요. 그 애가 죽은 걸 어떻게 아시죠?”\n\n“어떻게 아는지는 말씀드릴 수 없습니다—그러나 저는 분명히 알고 있습니다.”\n\n“제 주소를 어떻게 알아내셨는지는 말씀하실 수 있나요?”\n\n“물론입니다. 클레멘츠 부인에게서 주소를 얻었습니다.”\n\n“클레멘츠 부인은 어리석은 여자예요. 그 여자가 여기 오라고 하던가요?”\n\n“아닙니다.”\n\n“그러면 다시 묻겠습니다. 왜 오셨죠?”\n\n그녀가 기어이 대답을 들으려 했으므로, 나는 더없이 단순한 형태로 대답을 주었다.\n\n“제가 온 것은,” 내가 말했다, “앤 캐서릭의 어머니라면 딸이 살았는지 죽었는지 아는 데 자연스러운 관심을 가지실 거라고 생각했기 때문입니다.”\n\n“그렇겠지요.” 캐서릭 부인이 한층 더 침착해져서 말했다. “다른 동기는 없으셨나요?”\n\n나는 망설였다. 그 질문에 대한 옳은 대답은 순간에 찾아내기 쉽지 않았다.\n\n“다른 동기가 없으시다면,” 그녀는 슬레이트빛 반장갑을 천천히 벗어 둘둘 말면서 말을 이었다, “방문해 주신 데 감사드리고 더는 붙들지 않겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 게 없군요. 그 소식을 어떻게 알게 되셨는지 기꺼이 설명해 주신다면 더 만족스러울 텐데요. 어쨌든 이만하면 제가 상복을 입어도 되겠지요. 보시다시피 제 옷은 크게 고칠 것도 없습니다. 반장갑만 바꿔 끼면 온통 검은색이 되니까요.”\n\n그녀는 가운 주머니를 뒤져 검은 레이스 반장갑 한 켤레를 꺼내더니, 돌처럼 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 그것을 끼고는 조용히 두 손을 무릎 위에 포갰다.\n\n“그럼 안녕히 가십시오.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그 태도의 서늘한 경멸에 부아가 치민 나는, 아직 내 방문의 목적이 이루어지지 않았다고 곧바로 털어놓고 말았다.\n\n“저는 여기 온 다른 동기가 있습니다.” 내가 말했다.\n\n“아! 그럴 줄 알았지요.” 캐서릭 부인이 말했다.\n\n“따님의 죽음이——”\n\n“무슨 병으로 죽었나요?”\n\n“심장병입니다.”\n\n“그렇군요. 계속하시죠.”\n\n“따님의 죽음은 저에게 몹시 소중한 사람에게 심각한 해를 입히는 구실로 이용되었습니다. 제가 확실히 아는 바로는 두 사람이 그 못된 짓에 관여했습니다. 그중 하나가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니다.”\n\n“저런!”\n\n나는 그 이름이 갑자기 나왔을 때 그녀가 움찔하는지 눈여겨보았다. 근육 하나 까딱하지 않았다—굳세고 도전적이고 무자비한 눈의 응시는 한순간도 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n“따님의 죽음이라는 사건이 어떻게 다른 사람에게 해를 입히는 수단이 될 수 있었는지 의아하실 겁니다.” 내가 말을 이었다.\n\n“아니요.” 캐서릭 부인이 말했다. “조금도 의아하지 않습니다. 그건 선생 일인 듯하군요. 선생은 내 일에 관심이 있고, 나는 선생 일에 관심이 없습니다.”\n\n“그러면 왜 제가 부인 앞에서 이 문제를 꺼내는지 물으시겠지요.” 나는 물러서지 않았다.\n\n“그래요, 그건 정말로 묻고 싶군요.”\n\n“제가 이 말씀을 드리는 것은, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 저지른 악행에 대해 반드시 책임을 묻겠다고 결심했기 때문입니다.”\n\n“선생 결심이 나와 무슨 상관이죠?”\n\n“들어 보시면 압니다. 퍼시벌 경의 지난 생애에는 제 목적을 위해 반드시 소상히 알아야 할 어떤 사건들이 있습니다. 부인께서 그것을 알고 계십니다—그래서 저는 부인을 찾아온 것입니다.”\n\n“무슨 사건 말인가요?”\n\n“부인의 남편이 올드 웰밍엄의 교회 서기로 있던 시절, 따님이 태어나기 전에 그곳에서 일어난 사건들입니다.”\n\n마침내 나는 그녀가 우리 사이에 세우려 했던 뚫을 수 없는 과묵의 장벽을 뚫고 그 여자에게 가닿았다. 그녀의 눈에서 노기가 이글거리는 것이 보였다—두 손이 다시 안절부절못하다가 깍지를 풀고 무릎 위 옷자락을 기계적으로 쓸어내리기 시작하는 것이 보인 만큼이나 또렷하게.\n\n“그 사건들에 대해 무엇을 아시죠?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“클레멘츠 부인이 저에게 말해 줄 수 있는 것은 전부.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n그 단단하고 각진 얼굴에 순간 홍조가 스쳤고, 쉼 없던 두 손이 순간 멎었다. 경계를 무너뜨릴 만한 노여움의 폭발이 다가온다는 조짐 같았다. 그러나 아니었다—그녀는 치미는 분을 억누르고 의자에 등을 기대더니, 넓은 가슴 위에 팔짱을 끼고, 두툼한 입술에 험상궂은 빈정거림의 미소를 띤 채 여전히 흔들림 없이 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“아! 이제야 다 알겠군요.” 그녀가 말했다. 길들여지고 다스려진 그 노여움은 오직 어조와 태도의 공들인 조롱으로만 드러났다. “선생은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 사사로운 원한이 있고, 나는 선생이 그 원한을 푸는 걸 도와야 한다는 거군요. 퍼시벌 경과 나에 대해 이런저런 이야기를 다 해 드려야 한다, 그거죠? 정말 그런가요? 선생은 내 사생활을 캐고 다녔군요. 남의 눈치를 보며 여기 얹혀사는 타락한 여자를 상대하게 됐다고, 읍내 사람들 앞에서 평판을 깎아 버릴까 두려워 무엇이든 시키는 대로 할 여자를 찾아냈다고 생각하는 거죠. 나는 선생과 선생의 그 갸륵한 속셈을 훤히 꿰뚫어 봅니다—그렇고말고요! 그리고 그게 우습군요. 하! 하!”\n\n그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 가슴 위로 팔을 더 죄더니 혼자 웃었다—굳고 거칠고 성난 웃음이었다.\n\n“선생, 이름이 뭐라고 하셨죠, 선생은 내가 이곳에서 어떻게 살아왔는지, 이곳에서 무엇을 해냈는지 모릅니다.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “종을 울려 선생을 내보내기 전에 말해 드리지요. 나는 억울한 일을 당한 여자로 이곳에 왔습니다—평판을 빼앗긴 채로 와서, 그것을 되찾겠다고 마음먹었지요. 몇 해고 몇 해고 그 일에 매달렸고—나는 그것을 되찾았습니다. 나는 점잖은 사람들과 그들의 땅에서 정정당당하게 겨루어 대등해졌어요. 이제 그들이 나를 두고 무슨 말을 하려면 몰래 해야 합니다—대놓고는 못 해요, 감히 못 합니다. 나는 이 읍내에서 선생 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 높이 서 있습니다. 목사님이 나에게 인사를 합니다. 아하! 여기 올 때 그건 미처 셈에 넣지 못하셨겠지요. 교회에 가서 나에 대해 물어보세요—캐서릭 부인도 남들처럼 제 좌석을 가지고 있고 자릿세를 기한에 꼬박꼬박 낸다는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다. 읍사무소에도 가 보세요. 거기 청원서가 하나 놓여 있어요—서커스단이 들어와 공연을 하며 우리 풍속을 어지럽히지 못하게 해 달라는 점잖은 주민들의 청원서 말입니다—그래요, 우리 풍속이오. 나는 오늘 아침에 그 청원서에 서명했습니다. 서점에도 가 보세요. 목사님의 수요일 저녁 강론집 ‘믿음으로 말미암은 칭의’가 예약 구독으로 출간되는 중인데—내 이름이 명단에 올라 있습니다. 지난번 자선 설교 때 의사 부인은 헌금 접시에 겨우 1실링을 넣었지만—나는 반 크라운을 넣었어요. 접시를 받쳐 든 교회 위원 소워드 씨가 나에게 인사를 했지요. 십 년 전에 그 사람은 약제사 피그럼에게 나 같은 여자는 수레 꽁무니에 매달아 채찍질하며 읍에서 쫓아내야 한다고 말했습니다. 선생 어머니는 살아 계신가요? 그분 탁자 위에 내 것보다 더 좋은 성경책이 놓여 있나요? 그분은 나만큼 단골 상인들에게 신용을 얻고 계신가요? 그분은 늘 수입에 맞게 살아오셨나요? 나는 늘 내 수입에 맞게 살았습니다. 아! 저기 목사님이 광장을 지나오시는군요. 보세요, 선생, 이름이 뭐라고 하셨죠—어디 좀 보시라니까요!”\n\n그녀는 젊은 여자처럼 날래게 일어나 창가로 가서, 목사가 지나가기를 기다렸다가 정중하게 인사를 했다. 목사는 격식을 차려 모자를 들어 올리고는 걸어갔다. 캐서릭 부인은 의자로 돌아와, 그 어느 때보다 험상궂은 빈정거림을 띠고 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“보셨죠!” 그녀가 말했다. “평판 잃은 여자치고 저만하면 어떻습니까? 이제 선생의 그 속셈은 어때 보이나요?”\n\n그녀가 스스로를 내세우려고 택한 그 별난 방식, 읍내에서의 제 위치를 방금 눈앞에 실증해 보인 그 유별난 방법에 나는 어리둥절해져서, 말없이 놀란 채 그녀의 말을 듣고만 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 경계를 무너뜨리려는 노력을 한 번 더 해 보겠다는 결심만은 조금도 흔들리지 않았다. 이 여자의 사나운 성미가 한 번이라도 통제를 벗어나 나에게 불길처럼 터져 나온다면, 실마리를 내 손에 쥐여 줄 말이 아직 튀어나올지도 몰랐다.\n\n“선생의 그 속셈은 이제 어때 보이나요?” 그녀가 되풀이했다.\n\n“처음 들어왔을 때와 똑같아 보입니다.” 내가 대답했다. “부인이 이 읍내에서 얻으신 지위를 저는 의심하지 않고, 설령 할 수 있다 해도 그것을 헐뜯을 생각이 없습니다. 제가 여기 온 것은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이, 제가 확실히 아는 바로는, 저에게만이 아니라 부인께도 원수이기 때문입니다. 제가 그에게 원한이 있다면 부인께도 그에게 원한이 있습니다. 부인하셔도 좋고, 저를 마음껏 불신하셔도 좋고, 얼마든지 노여워하셔도 좋습니다—그러나 잉글랜드의 모든 여자 가운데, 부인이야말로 억울함을 느낄 줄 아신다면, 그자를 짓밟는 일을 저와 함께해야 할 분입니다.”\n\n“혼자서 짓밟으세요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그런 다음에 여기 다시 오시면, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 들으실 겁니다.”\n\n그녀는 지금껏 하지 않던 투로 그 말을 했다. 빠르고, 사납고, 앙심에 차서. 나는 여러 해 묵은 뱀 같은 증오를 그 굴에서 건드려 놓았던 것이다. 그러나 그것도 한순간이었다. 그녀가 내가 앉은 자리 쪽으로 열렬히 몸을 숙일 때, 숨어 있던 파충류처럼 그것이 나에게 달려들었다. 그리고 그녀가 곧바로 의자에서 아까의 자세로 돌아가자, 숨어 있던 파충류처럼 그것은 다시 자취를 감추었다.\n\n“저를 믿지 못하시겠습니까?” 내가 말했다.\n\n“못 믿습니다.”\n\n“두려우신 겁니까?”\n\n“내가 두려워하는 사람으로 보이나요?”\n\n“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 두려우신 겁니까?”\n\n“내가요?”\n\n그녀의 낯빛이 붉어지고 있었고, 두 손은 다시 가운을 쓸어내리며 움직이고 있었다. 나는 그 지점을 점점 더 깊이 파고들었고, 그녀에게 한순간의 여유도 주지 않고 밀어붙였다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경은 세상에서 높은 지위에 있습니다.” 내가 말했다. “부인이 그를 두려워한다 해도 이상할 것이 없지요. 퍼시벌 경은 힘 있는 사람이고, 준남작이고, 훌륭한 영지의 소유자이며, 대단한 가문의 후손이니까요——”\n\n그녀는 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨려 나를 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했다.\n\n“그래요.” 그녀가 더없이 신랄하고 흔들림 없는 경멸의 어조로 되뇌었다. “준남작이고, 훌륭한 영지의 소유자이고, 대단한 가문의 후손이지요. 정말 그렇고말고요! 대단한 가문—특히 어머니 쪽으로요.”\n\n방금 그녀의 입에서 새어 나온 말을 곱씹을 겨를은 없었다. 다만 그 집을 나서는 순간 곰곰이 생각해 볼 값어치가 충분한 말이라는 느낌만이 스쳤을 뿐이다.\n\n“저는 부인과 가문 문제로 다투러 온 것이 아닙니다.” 내가 말했다. “저는 퍼시벌 경의 어머니에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다——”\n\n“퍼시벌 경 본인에 대해서도 아는 게 그만큼 없지요.” 그녀가 날카롭게 끼어들었다.\n\n“그렇게 자신하지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다.” 내가 받아쳤다. “저는 그에 대해 몇 가지를 알고 있고, 그보다 훨씬 많은 것을 의심하고 있습니다.”\n\n“무엇을 의심하시죠?”\n\n“제가 의심하지 않는 것을 말씀드리지요. 저는 그가 앤의 아버지라고 의심하지 않습니다.”\n\n그녀는 벌떡 일어나 격노한 얼굴로 내 앞에 바싹 다가섰다.\n\n“감히 나에게 앤의 아버지 이야기를 하다니! 감히 누가 그 애 아비였고 누가 아니었는지를 말하다니!” 그녀가 얼굴을 떨고 격정에 목소리를 떨며 소리쳤다.\n\n“부인과 퍼시벌 경 사이의 비밀은 그런 비밀이 아닙니다.” 나는 물러서지 않았다. “퍼시벌 경의 생애를 어둡게 하는 그 수수께끼는 따님의 출생과 함께 태어나지 않았고, 따님의 죽음과 함께 죽지도 않았습니다.”\n\n그녀는 한 걸음 물러섰다. “나가세요!” 그녀가 말하며 준엄하게 문을 가리켰다.\n\n“부인의 마음에도 그의 마음에도 그 아이에 대한 생각은 없었습니다.” 나는 그녀를 마지막 방어선까지 몰아붙이기로 작정하고 말을 이었다. “남편분이 두 분이 소곤거리는 것을 발견했던 그때, 교회 성구실 아래에서 남몰래 만나던 그 자리에는, 부인과 그 사람 사이의 죄스러운 사랑 같은 것은 없었습니다.”\n\n가리키던 손이 즉시 옆으로 떨어졌고, 내가 말하는 동안 노여움의 짙은 홍조가 얼굴에서 걷혔다. 나는 그 변화가 그녀를 스쳐 지나가는 것을 보았다—“교회 성구실”이라는 그 마지막 말을 내가 했을 때, 굳세고 단단하고 겁 없고 침착하던 그 여자가 자기의 온 결의로도 버텨 낼 수 없는 공포에 움츠러드는 것을 보았다.\n\n우리는 일 분 남짓 말없이 서로를 마주 보고 서 있었다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 나였다.\n\n“아직도 저를 믿지 못하시겠습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n얼굴에서 가신 핏기를 그녀는 되불러오지 못했지만, 나에게 대답할 때는 목소리를 가다듬고 그 도전적이고 침착한 태도를 되찾은 뒤였다.\n\n“못 믿습니다.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아직도 나가라고 하시겠습니까?”\n\n“그래요. 나가세요—그리고 두 번 다시 오지 마세요.”\n\n나는 문 쪽으로 걸어가, 문을 열기 전에 잠시 멈추어 서서, 몸을 돌려 그녀를 다시 바라보았다.\n\n“퍼시벌 경에 관해 부인이 예상하지 못한 소식을 가져오게 될지도 모릅니다.” 내가 말했다. “그렇게 되면 저는 다시 오겠습니다.”\n\n“퍼시벌 경에 관해 내가 예상하지 못할 소식은 없습니다. 다만——”\n\n그녀는 말을 멈추었고, 창백한 얼굴이 어두워졌으며, 조용하고 은밀한 고양이 같은 걸음으로 슬며시 의자로 돌아갔다.\n\n“그가 죽었다는 소식만 빼고요.” 그녀는 다시 앉으며 말했다. 잔인한 입술에는 조롱의 미소가 어렴풋이 감돌았고, 흔들림 없는 눈 속 깊은 곳에는 증오의 은밀한 빛이 도사리고 있었다.\n\n방을 나가려고 문을 열었을 때, 그녀가 홱 나를 돌아보았다. 잔인한 미소가 천천히 입술을 벌려 갔다—그녀는 야릇하고 은밀한 관심으로 나를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 훑어보았다—이루 말할 수 없는 기대가 얼굴 전체에 사악하게 떠올랐다. 그녀는 제 마음속 은밀한 곳에서 내 젊음과 힘을, 내가 느끼는 억울함의 크기와 내 자제력의 한계를 가늠하고 있었던 것일까? 그리고 퍼시벌 경과 내가 언젠가 마주치게 된다면 그것들이 나를 어디까지 몰고 갈지 헤아리고 있었던 것일까? 그럴지도 모른다는 의심만으로도 나는 그 앞을 떠나야 했고, 흔한 작별 인사조차 입에서 나오지 않았다. 내 쪽에서도 그녀 쪽에서도 더는 한마디 없이, 나는 방을 나섰다.\n\n바깥문을 열었을 때, 이미 한 번 이 집 앞을 지나갔던 그 목사가 광장을 돌아 돌아가는 길에 다시 이 집 앞을 지나려는 참이었다. 나는 그가 지나가도록 문간에 서서 기다렸고, 그러면서 응접실 창을 돌아보았다.\n\n캐서릭 부인은 그 적막한 곳의 정적 속에서 다가오는 발소리를 듣고, 다시 창가에 일어서서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 내가 그 여자의 마음속에 불러일으킨 온갖 무서운 격정의 힘을 다 합쳐도, 여러 해의 굳센 노력이 이제 막 손안에 끌어다 놓은 그 한 조각 사회적 인정에 대한 그녀의 필사적인 집착을 떼어 놓을 수는 없었다. 내가 그 곁을 떠난 지 채 일 분도 되지 않아 그녀는 다시 그 자리에, 목사가 두 번째로 인사를 하는 것이 예사로운 예의가 되도록 일부러 잡은 위치에 서 있었다. 그는 다시 한번 모자를 들어 올렸다. 나는 창 너머 그 굳고 소름 끼치는 얼굴이 누그러지며 흡족한 자부심으로 환해지는 것을 보았다—험상궂은 검은 모자를 쓴 머리가 답례로 격식 차려 숙여지는 것을 보았다. 목사가 그녀에게 인사를 한 것이다, 그것도 내가 보는 앞에서, 하루에 두 번씩이나!\n\n# IX\n\n나는 그 집을 나서면서, 캐서릭 부인이 본인의 뜻과는 상관없이 나를 한 걸음 앞으로 나아가게 해 주었다고 느꼈다. 광장을 벗어나는 모퉁이에 이르기도 전에, 등 뒤에서 문 닫히는 소리가 들려 나는 문득 주의를 빼앗겼다.\n\n돌아보니, 검은 옷을 입은 자그마한 사내가 어느 집 현관 계단에 서 있었다. 내가 짐작하기로 그 집은 캐서릭 부인이 사는 집 바로 옆, 그중에서도 내 쪽에 가까운 편의 이웃집이었다. 사내는 어느 쪽으로 갈지 조금도 망설이지 않았다. 그는 내가 걸음을 멈춘 그 모퉁이를 향해 빠르게 다가왔다. 나는 그가 블랙워터 파크로 나보다 먼저 다녀갔던 그 변호사의 서기임을, 그리고 내가 저택을 볼 수 있겠느냐고 물었을 때 나에게 시비를 걸려 했던 자임을 알아보았다.\n\n나는 그 자리에 선 채로, 이번에는 그가 바짝 다가와 말을 걸 작정인지 아닌지를 확인해 보려 했다. 그런데 놀랍게도 그는 한마디도 하지 않고, 지나치면서 내 얼굴을 쳐다보지조차 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 지나가 버렸다. 그것은 내가 마땅히 예상했던 그의 행동과는 완전히 정반대였으므로, 내 호기심이, 아니 오히려 내 의심이 일깨워졌다. 나 또한 그를 조심스럽게 시야에 두고, 그가 지금 무슨 일에 부려지고 있는지 알아내기로 마음먹었다. 그가 나를 보든 말든 개의치 않고 나는 그의 뒤를 따라 걸었다. 그는 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았고, 거리를 지나 곧장 철도역까지 나를 이끌었다.\n\n기차는 막 출발하려는 참이었고, 늦은 승객 두어 사람이 표를 파는 작은 창구 앞에 몰려 있었다. 나는 그들 틈에 끼어들어, 변호사의 서기가 블랙워터 역까지 가는 표를 달라고 하는 소리를 똑똑히 들었다. 나는 그가 실제로 그 기차를 타고 떠나는 것을 내 눈으로 확인한 뒤에야 그 자리를 떠났다.\n\n방금 보고 들은 것에 대해 내가 내릴 수 있는 해석은 하나뿐이었다. 나는 그 사내가 캐서릭 부인의 집과 바짝 붙어 있는 집에서 나오는 것을 분명히 보았다. 그는 아마도 퍼시벌 경의 지시로, 내 조사가 조만간 캐서릭 부인과의 접촉으로 이어지리라 예상하고, 하숙인으로 그 집에 자리를 잡고 있었을 것이다. 그는 틀림없이 내가 드나드는 것을 보았고, 첫 기차로 서둘러 블랙워터 파크로 가서 보고하려는 것이었다. 퍼시벌 경은 (내 행적에 관해 그가 분명히 알고 있는 바로 미루어) 내가 햄프셔로 돌아올 경우 그 자리에서 곧바로 대응하기 위해 자연히 그곳에 가 있을 터였다. 이제 며칠 안에 그와 내가 마주치게 될 가능성이 매우 커 보였다.\n\n앞으로 어떤 결과가 벌어지도록 정해져 있든, 나는 퍼시벌 경을 위해서든 그 누구를 위해서든 멈추거나 옆길로 새지 않고, 내가 정한 길을 목표한 끝까지 곧장 밀고 나가기로 결심했다. 런던에서 나를 무겁게 짓누르던 그 큰 책임—내 사소한 행동 하나까지도 뜻하지 않게 로라의 은신처를 드러내는 일로 이어지지 않도록 다스려야 한다는 책임—은 내가 햄프셔에 와 있는 지금은 사라졌다. 웰밍엄에서라면 나는 마음대로 오갈 수 있었고, 혹시 필요한 조심을 지키지 못하는 일이 생기더라도 적어도 그 즉각적인 결과는 나 자신 말고는 아무에게도 미치지 않을 터였다.\n\n역을 나섰을 때 겨울 저녁이 어둑어둑 내려앉기 시작하고 있었다. 낯선 고장에서 어두워진 뒤에 조사를 이어 간들 쓸 만한 성과를 얻으리라는 희망은 거의 없었다. 그래서 나는 가장 가까운 여관으로 찾아가 저녁 식사와 잠자리를 청했다. 그러고 나서 매리언에게 편지를 써서, 나는 무사하고 건강하며 성공의 전망도 괜찮다고 알렸다. 집을 떠나올 때 나는 그녀에게 첫 편지(다음 날 아침에 받아 보리라 기대하던 편지)를 “웰밍엄 우체국”으로 부치라고 일러 두었는데, 이제 이틀째 편지도 같은 주소로 보내 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n편지가 도착할 무렵 내가 마침 이 도시를 떠나 있게 되더라도, 우체국장에게 편지를 써 두면 어렵지 않게 받아 볼 수 있을 터였다.\n\n여관의 다실은 밤이 깊어 가면서 완전히 텅 비었다. 나는 마치 그 집이 내 집이기라도 한 듯 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 그날 오후에 이룬 일을 곱씹을 수 있었다. 잠자리에 들기 전에 나는 캐서릭 부인과의 그 기이한 면담을 처음부터 끝까지 찬찬히 되짚어 보았고, 낮에 성급하게 내렸던 결론들을 느긋하게 검토해 확인했다.\n\n구(舊)웰밍엄 교회의 성구실은 내 생각이 캐서릭 부인이 하는 말로 들은 모든 것, 캐서릭 부인이 하는 것으로 본 모든 것을 천천히 되짚어 나가는 출발점이었다.\n\n클레멘츠 부인이 내 앞에서 성구실 근처를 처음 입에 올렸을 때, 나는 퍼시벌 경이 교회 서기의 아내와 은밀히 만나기로 고른 장소치고 그보다 더 이상하고 더 이해할 수 없는 곳은 없다고 생각했다. 다른 이유가 아니라 바로 그 인상에 이끌려, 나는 캐서릭 부인 앞에서 순전히 넘겨짚어 “교회의 성구실”이라는 말을 꺼냈다. 그것은 말하는 도중에 문득 떠오른, 그 이야기의 사소한 특이점 가운데 하나였을 뿐이다. 나는 그녀가 당황해서, 혹은 화를 내며 대답하리라 각오하고 있었지만, 그 말을 했을 때 그녀를 사로잡은 그 새하얀 공포는 나를 완전히 허를 찌르듯 놀라게 했다. 나는 오래전부터 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을, 캐서릭 부인이 알고 있는 어떤 중대한 범죄를 감추는 일과 연결 지어 생각해 왔지만, 그 이상은 나아가지 못하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그 여자의 발작적인 공포가 그 범죄를 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 성구실과 결부시켰고, 그녀가 단순한 목격자 이상이었음을—의심할 여지 없이 공범이기도 했음을 나에게 확신시켰다.\n\n그 범죄의 성격은 어떤 것이었을까? 거기에는 위험한 면뿐 아니라 경멸스러운 면도 분명히 있었을 것이다. 그렇지 않고서야 캐서릭 부인이 퍼시벌 경의 지위와 권세를 두고 내가 한 말을, 그토록 뚜렷한 멸시를 담아 되풀이했을 리가 없다. 그러니까 그것은 경멸스러운 범죄인 동시에 위험한 범죄였고, 그녀도 거기에 한몫 끼었으며, 교회의 성구실과 얽혀 있었다.\n\n다음으로 정리해야 할 문제는 나를 이 지점에서 한 걸음 더 나아가게 했다.\n\n퍼시벌 경에 대한 캐서릭 부인의 노골적인 경멸은 명백히 그의 어머니에게까지 미치고 있었다. 그녀는 그가 물려받은 그 대단한 가문을 두고 “특히 어머니 쪽으로 말이지요”라며 더없이 신랄한 빈정거림을 던졌다. 그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까?\n\n거기에는 두 가지 설명밖에 없어 보였다. 그의 어머니의 출신이 미천했거나, 아니면 캐서릭 부인과 퍼시벌 경이 둘 다 남몰래 알고 있는 어떤 숨은 흠 때문에 그의 어머니의 평판이 훼손되어 있거나 둘 중 하나였다. 첫 번째 설명은 그녀의 혼인 등록부를 살펴보아 처녀 적 이름과 친정 내력을 확인하는 것으로만 검증할 수 있었고, 그것이 더 깊은 조사로 나아가는 첫걸음이 될 터였다.\n\n한편, 만일 두 번째로 가정한 경우가 참이라면, 그녀의 평판에 있던 흠이란 무엇이었을까? 매리언이 나에게 들려주었던 퍼시벌 경의 아버지와 어머니 이야기, 그리고 두 사람이 함께 지냈다는 그 수상쩍게 비사교적이고 폐쇄적인 생활을 떠올리며, 나는 이제 그의 어머니가 애초에 혼인을 한 적이 아예 없었을 가능성은 없는지 스스로에게 물었다. 여기서도 등록부는 혼인의 문서 증거를 보여 줌으로써, 적어도 이 의혹이 사실에 아무 근거가 없음을 나에게 증명해 줄 수 있을 터였다. 그런데 그 등록부는 어디에 있을까? 이 대목에서 나는 앞서 세워 둔 결론들을 다시 집어 들었고, 감추어진 범죄의 장소를 찾아냈던 것과 똑같은 사고의 과정이 이제 등록부 역시 구웰밍엄 교회의 성구실에 놓아두었다.\n\n이것이 캐서릭 부인과의 면담에서 얻은 결과였다. 그리고 이것이, 모두가 한 지점으로 꾸준히 모여들며 이튿날 내가 나아갈 길을 결정지은 여러 고찰이었다.\n\n아침은 구름이 끼고 잔뜩 찌푸렸으나 비는 내리지 않았다. 나는 가방을 여관에 맡겨 두고 찾으러 올 때까지 보관해 달라 이른 뒤, 길을 물어 걸어서 구웰밍엄 교회로 나섰다.\n\n두 마일이 조금 넘는 길이었고, 지면은 내내 완만하게 높아졌다.\n\n가장 높은 지점에 교회가 서 있었다. 옆구리에 육중한 버팀벽을 두르고 앞쪽에는 투박한 사각 종탑을 세운, 오래되어 비바람에 시달린 건물이었다. 뒤편의 성구실은 교회에서 내밀어 지은 것으로, 같은 시대의 것으로 보였다. 건물 둘레 여기저기에는 클레멘츠 부인이 예전에 자기 남편이 살던 곳이라고 나에게 말해 준 그 마을의 잔해가 남아 있었다. 주요 주민들은 오래전에 그곳을 버리고 새 도시로 옮겨 간 뒤였다. 빈집들 가운데 더러는 바깥벽만 남기고 헐렸고, 더러는 세월에 그대로 삭아 가도록 버려졌으며, 더러는 아직도 누가 보아도 가장 가난한 계층의 사람들이 살고 있었다. 을씨년스러운 풍경이었으나, 그 폐허의 가장 흉한 모습조차도 내가 방금 떠나온 그 현대적인 도시만큼 을씨년스럽지는 않았다. 여기에는 눈을 쉬게 해 줄, 갈색으로 바람이 쓸고 가는 들판이 사방으로 펼쳐져 있었다. 여기에는 잎을 다 떨군 채로나마 단조로운 경치에 변화를 주고, 여름날과 그늘을 내다보게 마음을 도와주는 나무들이 있었다.\n\n교회 뒤편에서 물러나 나와서, 서기가 있는 곳을 일러 줄 만한 사람을 찾아 헐린 오두막들 사이를 지나가는데, 두 사내가 담장 뒤에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나를 뒤따라 나오는 것이 보였다. 둘 가운데 키가 큰 쪽—사냥터지기 차림의 다부지고 근육질인 사내—은 나에게 낯선 자였다. 다른 하나는 내가 카일 씨의 사무실을 나서던 날 런던에서 나를 미행했던 사내들 가운데 하나였다. 나는 그때 그를 특히 눈여겨 두었기에, 이번에도 그 작자를 알아본 것이 틀림없다고 확신했다.\n\n그도 그의 동행도 나에게 말을 걸려 하지 않았고, 둘 다 조심스레 거리를 두었지만, 그들이 교회 근처에 나타난 까닭은 뻔히 드러나 있었다. 내가 짐작한 그대로였다. 퍼시벌 경은 이미 나를 맞을 준비를 갖추고 있었던 것이다. 내가 캐서릭 부인을 찾아간 일은 전날 저녁에 이미 그에게 보고되었고, 그 두 사내는 내가 구웰밍엄에 나타날 것을 예상하고 교회 부근에 파수꾼으로 배치되어 있었다. 내 조사가 마침내 옳은 방향을 잡았다는 증거가 더 필요했다면, 나를 감시하려고 지금 동원된 이 계략이 바로 그것을 대 주었을 것이다.\n\n나는 교회를 벗어나 계속 걸어, 사람이 사는 집 하나에 이르렀다. 딸린 텃밭 한 뙈기에서 일꾼 하나가 일을 하고 있었다. 그는 서기가 사는 곳을 일러 주었는데, 조금 떨어진 곳, 버려진 마을 변두리에 홀로 서 있는 오두막이었다. 서기는 집 안에 있었고, 마침 외투를 걸치던 참이었다. 그는 쾌활하고 스스럼없으며 목청 좋게 말이 많은 노인으로, (내가 곧 알게 되었듯이) 자기가 사는 고장을 아주 하찮게 여기면서, 한때 런던에 가 보았다는 대단한 개인적 영예 덕분에 이웃들보다 우월하다는 흐뭇한 자부심을 품고 있었다.\n\n“일찍 오셔서 다행이올시다, 선생님.” 내가 찾아온 용건을 말하자 노인이 말했다. “십 분만 늦었어도 저는 나가고 없었을 겝니다. 교구 일이지요, 선생님. 제 나이에는 다 마치자면 꽤나 오래 종종걸음을 쳐야 하는 일입니다. 하지만요, 저는 아직 다리 하나는 튼튼하답니다! 사람이란 다리만 안 무너지면 아직 할 일이 잔뜩 남아 있는 법이지요. 선생님도 그리 생각지 않으십니까?”\n\n그는 이야기하는 동안 벽난로 뒤 고리에서 열쇠를 내려 들더니, 우리가 나온 뒤 오두막 문을 잠갔다.\n\n“집을 봐 줄 사람이 하나도 없어놔서요.” 서기가 온갖 식솔의 짐에서 완전히 벗어난 자의 쾌활한 홀가분함으로 말했다. “제 안사람은 저기 교회 묘지에 누웠고, 자식들은 죄다 짝을 지어 나갔지요. 참 형편없는 데지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님? 하지만 교구는 넓습니다. 아무나 저처럼 일을 해낼 수 있는 게 아니에요. 그게 다 배움 덕이지요. 저는 배울 만큼 배웠고, 조금 더 배웠습죠. 저는 여왕님의 영어를 할 줄 압니다(여왕님께 축복이 있기를!). 이 근방 사람들 태반은 못 하는 일이지요. 선생님은 런던서 오셨지요, 아마? 저도 런던에 가 봤습니다, 한 이십오 년쯤 전에요. 요새 거기 소식은 어떻습니까, 여쭈어도 될는지요?”\n\n이런 식으로 쉴 새 없이 지껄이며 그는 나를 성구실로 데려갔다. 나는 그 두 밀정이 아직 보이는지 둘러보았다. 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 내가 서기를 찾아간 것을 알아낸 뒤로, 그들은 아마 내 다음 행동을 마음 놓고 지켜볼 수 있는 곳에 몸을 숨겼을 것이다.\n\n성구실 문은 튼튼한 못을 촘촘히 박은 두꺼운 참나무 문이었다. 서기는 크고 묵직한 열쇠를 자물쇠에 꽂았는데, 만만찮은 상대를 만나리라는 것을 알면서도 이번에도 무사히 이겨 낼지는 썩 자신이 없는 사람의 태도였다.\n\n“이쪽으로 모실 수밖에 없습니다, 선생님.” 그가 말했다. “성구실에서 예배당으로 통하는 문은 성구실 쪽에서 빗장이 걸려 있어서요. 아니면 예배당을 통해 들어갔을 겝니다. 이건 세상에 둘도 없이 심술궂은 자물쇠지요. 감옥 문에나 어울릴 만큼 커다랗고, 하도 여러 번 성가시게 굴어 왔으니 새것으로 바꿔야 마땅합니다. 제가 교구 위원한테 그 말을 적어도 쉰 번은 했지요. 그이는 늘 ‘내가 알아보지’ 하고는, 도무지 알아보는 법이 없어요. 아이고, 여기는 세상에서 잊힌 구석이올시다. 런던하고는 다르지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님? 참말이지, 여기서는 다들 잠들어 있습니다! 우리는 시대를 따라 걷지를 않아요.”\n\n열쇠를 이리저리 비틀고 돌린 끝에 그 육중한 자물쇠가 풀렸고, 그는 문을 열었다.\n\n성구실은 바깥에서만 보고 짐작했던 것보다 넓었다. 서까래가 드러난 낮은 천장을 인, 어둑하고 곰팡내 나는 음울한 옛 방이었다. 예배당 안쪽에 가까운 두 면에는 세월에 좀먹고 틈이 벌어진 묵직한 나무 궤장이 죽 늘어서 있었다. 그중 한 궤장의 안쪽 모서리에 걸린 고리에는 성직자 예복 여러 벌이 걸려 있었는데, 하나같이 아랫자락이 불룩하게 부풀어 올라 축 늘어진 천 뭉치처럼 불경스러워 보였다. 예복 아래 바닥에는 포장 상자 세 개가 뚜껑을 반은 덮고 반은 벗긴 채 놓여 있었고, 갈라진 틈이란 틈마다 짚이 사방으로 잔뜩 삐져나와 있었다. 그 뒤 구석에는 먼지 앉은 서류 뭉치가 어지러이 쌓여 있었는데, 더러는 커다랗게 건축가의 도면처럼 말려 있었고, 더러는 청구서나 편지처럼 서류철에 헐겁게 꿰여 있었다. 이 방은 한때 작은 옆창으로 빛을 들였으나 그 창은 벽돌로 막혀 버렸고, 지금은 그 대신 채광창이 나 있었다. 방 안의 공기는 무겁고 곰팡내가 났으며, 예배당으로 통하는 문이 닫혀 있어 답답함이 한층 더했다. 그 문 역시 단단한 참나무로 짜였고, 성구실 쪽에서 위아래로 빗장이 걸려 있었다.\n\n“좀 더 말끔했으면 좋았을 텐데요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?” 쾌활한 서기가 말했다. “하지만 이런 잊힌 구석에 처박혀 있으면 무슨 수가 있겠습니까? 아, 여기 좀 보십시오, 이 포장 상자들 좀 보시라고요. 저것들이 저기 저러고 있은 지가 일 년이 넘습니다, 런던으로 내려보낼 채비를 다 갖춘 채로요. 저기 저렇게 자리만 어지럽히고 있지요. 못이 저것들을 붙들고 있는 한 저기 그대로 있을 겝니다. 제가 말씀드리지 않았습니까, 선생님, 여기는 런던이 아니라고요. 여기서는 다들 잠들어 있습니다. 참말이지, 우리는 시대를 따라 걷지를 않아요!”\n\n“그 상자들 안에는 무엇이 들었습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“설교단에서 떼어 낸 낡은 목조 조각들이랑, 성단소에서 뜯어낸 판벽이랑, 오르간 다락에서 내린 조각상들입지요.” 서기가 말했다. “나무로 새긴 열두 사도의 상인데, 그중에 코가 성한 놈이 하나도 없습니다. 죄다 부서지고 좀먹어서 가장자리는 바스러져 가루가 되고 있지요. 사기그릇처럼 잘 깨지는 데다, 교회만큼이나 오래된 것들입니다. 어쩌면 교회보다 더 오래됐을지도요.”\n\n“그것들이 런던으로 갈 일은 왜 생긴 겁니까? 수리하려고요?”\n\n“그렇습지요, 선생님, 수리하려고요. 그리고 손댈 수 없을 만큼 상한 것은 성한 나무로 본떠 새로 만들려고요. 그런데 어쩌겠습니까, 돈이 모자라서 저것들이 저기 저렇게 새 기부금을 기다리고 있는데, 기부하는 사람이 없습니다. 다 일 년 전 일이지요, 선생님. 신사분 여섯이 그 일로 새 도시의 여관에서 함께 만찬을 드셨습니다. 연설을 하고, 결의를 통과시키고, 이름을 적어 넣고, 취지서를 수천 장 찍어 냈지요. 아주 훌륭한 취지서였습니다, 선생님. 붉은 잉크로 고딕 문양을 잔뜩 둘러 놓고, 교회를 복원하지 않고 그 이름난 조각들을 손보지 않는 것은 수치라는 둥 하는 말들을 적어 놓았지요. 그때 다 나눠 주지 못한 취지서들이랑, 건축가의 도면과 견적서랑, 모두를 서로 으르렁대게 만들어 결국 다툼으로 끝나 버린 편지 뭉치가 몽땅 저기 저 구석, 포장 상자들 뒤에 처박혀 있습니다. 처음에는 돈이 조금씩 들어왔지요. 하지만 런던 바깥에서 무얼 기대할 수 있겠습니까? 부서진 조각들을 포장하고, 견적을 뽑고, 인쇄소 값을 치를 만큼만 겨우 됐고, 그러고 나니 반 페니도 남지 않았습니다. 그래서 그것들이 저기 저러고 있는 겝니다, 아까 말씀드린 대로요. 달리 둘 데가 없어요. 새 도시에서는 아무도 우리를 거들어 줄 마음이 없고, 우리는 잊힌 구석에 있고, 그러니 성구실이 어수선한 겝니다. 누구더러 어쩌란 말입니까? 저야말로 그게 알고 싶습니다.”\n\n등록부를 살펴보고 싶은 조바심 탓에 나는 이 노인의 수다에 별로 장단을 맞춰 줄 기분이 아니었다. 나는 성구실이 어수선한 것을 누구도 어쩔 수 없다는 데 동의한 뒤, 더 지체하지 말고 우리 용건으로 넘어가자고 제안했다.\n\n“암요, 암요, 혼인 등록부지요, 물론입니다.” 서기가 주머니에서 작은 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내며 말했다. “얼마나 옛날까지 거슬러 보시려는 겝니까, 선생님?”\n\n매리언은 로라와 퍼시벌 경의 약혼에 대해 나와 이야기를 나누던 때에 그의 나이를 알려 준 적이 있었다. 그때 그녀는 그를 마흔다섯 살이라고 했다. 거기서 거꾸로 셈해 보고, 내가 그 이야기를 들은 뒤로 흘러간 한 해를 마땅히 감안하니, 그는 1804년에 태어났음이 틀림없었고 나는 그 해부터 등록부를 뒤지기 시작하면 안전하겠다는 결론이 나왔다.\n\n“1804년부터 시작하고 싶습니다.” 내가 말했다.\n\n“그다음은 어느 쪽으로 갑니까, 선생님?” 서기가 물었다. “우리 시대 쪽으로 내려옵니까, 아니면 거꾸로 거슬러 올라갑니까?”\n\n“1804년에서 거꾸로 거슬러 갑니다.”\n\n그는 궤장 하나의 문을 열었다. 옆면에 성직자 예복이 걸려 있던 바로 그 궤장이었다. 그러고는 기름때 낀 갈색 가죽으로 장정한 커다란 책 한 권을 꺼냈다. 나는 등록부가 보관된 자리가 허술한 데 놀랐다. 궤장 문은 세월에 뒤틀리고 갈라져 있었고, 자물쇠는 가장 작고 가장 흔한 종류였다. 내가 손에 들고 있던 지팡이로도 쉽게 부술 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n“이곳이 등록부를 두기에 충분히 안전한 자리로 여겨집니까?” 나는 물었다. “이만큼 중요한 책이라면 더 좋은 자물쇠로 지키고, 쇠금고에 조심스레 넣어 두어야 하지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“아니, 이런, 그거 참 신통한 말씀이십니다!” 서기가 책을 펼치자마자 도로 덮고는 표지를 쾌활하게 탁 치며 말했다. “여러 해 전에, 제가 아직 애송이였을 적에 제 옛 주인 어르신이 늘 하시던 말씀이 바로 그것이었지요. ‘어째서 이 등록부를’(제 손 밑에 있는 바로 이 등록부 말입니다) ‘어째서 이걸 쇠금고에 넣어 두지 않는 게냐?’ 제가 그 말씀을 한 번 들었으면 백 번은 들었습니다. 그 어른은 당시 이 교회의 성구실 서기 자리를 맡고 계시던 사무 변호사셨습니다. 훌륭하고 정정한 노신사이자, 세상에 둘도 없이 꼼꼼한 분이셨지요. 살아 계신 동안 내내 그 어른은 놀즈베리의 사무실에 이 책의 사본을 두고, 여기 새로 오르는 기재 사항과 맞도록 때때로 어김없이 적어 넣게 하셨습니다. 믿기 어려우시겠지만, 그 어른은 분기마다 한두 번씩 날을 따로 정해 두고, 늙은 백마 조랑말을 타고 이 교회까지 오셔서 당신 눈과 당신 손으로 등록부와 사본을 대조하셨지요. ‘내가 어찌 알겠느냐?’ 하고 늘 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이 성구실의 등록부가 도둑맞거나 없어지지 않으리라고 내가 어찌 알겠느냐? 어째서 이걸 쇠금고에 넣지 않는 게냐? 어째서 나는 남들을 나만큼 조심스럽게 만들 수가 없단 말이냐? 언젠가는 사고가 나고 말 게다. 등록부를 잃고 나서야 이 교구는 내 사본의 값어치를 알게 될 게야.’ 그러고 나면 어른은 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고, 영주님처럼 당당하게 사방을 둘러보시곤 했지요. 아이고! 일 처리에서 그 어른 같은 분은 요새 쉬 찾아볼 수 없습니다. 런던에 가신들 그 어른만 한 분은 못 만나실 겁니다, 거기서도 말이지요. 몇 년이라고 하셨습니까, 선생님? 1800 몇 년입니까?”\n\n“1804년입니다.” 나는 대답하면서, 등록부 조사를 마칠 때까지는 이 노인에게 더 이상 이야기할 기회를 주지 않으리라 속으로 다짐했다.\n\n서기는 안경을 쓰고, 세 장마다 손가락과 엄지에 조심스레 침을 발라 가며 등록부를 넘겼다. “여기 있습니다, 선생님.” 그가 펼쳐진 책을 또 한 번 쾌활하게 탁 치며 말했다. “찾으시는 해가 여기 있습니다.”\n\n퍼시벌 경이 몇 월에 태어났는지 몰랐으므로, 나는 그해 초부터 거슬러 올라가는 조사를 시작했다. 등록부 책은 옛날식이어서, 기재 사항이 모두 빈 종이에 손으로 적혀 있었고, 각 항목이 끝나는 자리에 잉크로 가로줄을 그어 서로를 구분해 놓았다.\n\n나는 그 혼인 기록을 만나지 못한 채 1804년의 첫머리에 이르렀고, 그다음에는 1803년 12월을 지나 거슬러 올라갔다. 11월과 10월을 지나고, 다시—\n\n아니! 9월까지 지나가지는 않았다. 그해 그달의 표제 아래에서 나는 그 혼인 기록을 찾아냈다.\n\n나는 그 항목을 찬찬히 들여다보았다. 그것은 한 면의 맨 아래에 있었고, 자리가 모자란 탓에 위쪽 혼인들이 차지한 것보다 좁은 공간에 눌러 적혀 있었다. 바로 앞의 혼인 항목은 신랑의 이름이 내 이름과 같다는 사정 때문에 내 주의에 남았다. 바로 뒤에 이어지는 항목은(다음 면의 맨 위에 있었다) 차지한 자리가 유난히 넓다는 점에서 또 다르게 눈에 띄었는데, 그 기록은 형제 두 사람의 혼인을 한꺼번에 등록한 것이었다. 펠릭스 글라이드 경의 혼인 기록 자체는 그 면 아래쪽 좁은 자리에 눌러 적혔다는 점 말고는 어느 모로도 특별할 것이 없었다. 그의 아내에 관한 사항도 그런 경우에 흔히 적히는 통상적인 내용이었다. 그녀는 “놀즈베리 파크뷰 코티지 거주, 전에 바스에 살던 고(故) 패트릭 엘스터 님의 외동딸 세실리아 제인 엘스터”라고 적혀 있었다.\n\n나는 이 사항들을 수첩에 적어 넣었는데, 그러면서 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 막막하고 맥이 빠지는 심정이었다. 이 순간까지 손에 넣었다고 믿었던 그 비밀이 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 멀리 달아나 버린 듯했다.\n\n성구실 방문에서 풀리지 않은 수수께끼의 실마리라 할 만한 것이 무엇이 나왔는가? 나는 아무런 실마리도 보지 못했다. 퍼시벌 경의 어머니의 평판에 있으리라 짐작했던 흠을 밝히는 일에서 나는 얼마나 나아갔는가? 내가 확인한 단 하나의 사실은 오히려 그녀의 평판을 옹호해 주었다. 새로운 의혹, 새로운 난관, 새로운 지체가 끝없이 이어질 전망으로 내 앞에 열리기 시작했다. 다음으로 나는 무엇을 해야 하는가? 당장 내게 남은 방도는 하나뿐인 듯했다. 나는 “놀즈베리의 엘스터 양”에 대해 수소문해 볼 수 있었다. 퍼시벌 경의 어머니를 향한 캐서릭 부인의 경멸이 어디서 비롯되었는지를 먼저 알아냄으로써, 내 조사의 본래 목표에 다가서게 될지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n“원하시던 것을 찾으셨습니까, 선생님?” 내가 등록부 책을 덮자 서기가 물었다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 나는 대답했다. “하지만 아직 알아보아야 할 것이 몇 가지 있습니다. 1803년에 이곳에서 예식을 주관한 목사님은 이제 생존해 계시지 않겠지요?”\n\n“그럼요, 그럼요, 선생님. 그분은 제가 여기 오기 서너 해 전에 돌아가셨습니다. 제가 온 게 벌써 27년의 일이니까요. 저는 이 자리를 말입지요, 선생님,” 수다스러운 내 늙은 벗이 기어이 말을 이었다. “제 앞의 서기가 그만두는 바람에 얻었습니다. 사람들 말로는 그이가 마누라한테 시달려 집이며 살림이며 다 내던지고 쫓겨났다더군요. 그 여편네는 아직도 저 아래 새 도시에 살아 있습니다. 저야 그 얘기의 옳고 그름은 모르지요. 제가 아는 건 제가 이 자리를 얻었다는 것뿐입니다. 완즈버러 씨가 저를 위해 얻어 주셨지요. 아까 말씀드린 제 옛 주인 어른의 아드님 말입니다. 세상에 다시 없이 소탈하고 유쾌한 신사분이시지요. 사냥개를 따라 말을 달리시고, 사냥개도 여러 마리 두시고 뭐 그러십니다. 지금은 그분이 아버님이 그러셨듯이 여기 성구실 서기이십니다.”\n\n“전 주인이 놀즈베리에 사셨다고 하지 않으셨습니까?” 나는, 등록부 책을 펼치기 전에 이 수다스러운 벗이 나를 지치게 하며 늘어놓았던, 그 옛 시대의 꼼꼼한 신사에 관한 긴 이야기를 떠올리며 물었다.\n\n“그럼요, 물론입지요, 선생님.” 서기가 대답했다. “완즈버러 노인 어르신은 놀즈베리에 사셨고, 젊은 완즈버러 씨도 거기 사십니다.”\n\n“방금 그분이 아버지가 그랬듯이 성구실 서기라고 하셨지요. 성구실 서기가 무엇인지를 제가 잘 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“정말 모르십니까, 선생님? 게다가 런던서 오신 분이!—아시다시피 교구 교회에는 어디나 성구실 서기와 교구 서기가 있습니다. 교구 서기는 저 같은 사람이지요(다만 저는 그 사람들 태반보다 배움이 훨씬 많습니다만, 뭐 자랑삼는 건 아닙니다). 성구실 서기라는 건 변호사들이 얻는 일종의 임명직이라서, 성구실을 위해 처리할 일이 생기면 그이들이 있어 그 일을 하는 겝니다. 런던도 꼭 마찬가지입지요. 거기도 교구 교회마다 성구실 서기가 있고, 제 말을 믿으셔도 좋습니다만, 그이는 틀림없이 변호사입니다.”\n\n“그러면 젊은 완즈버러 씨도 변호사겠군요?”\n\n“당연하지요, 선생님! 놀즈베리 하이 스트리트의 변호사입니다. 아버님이 쓰시던 그 옛 사무실을 그대로 쓰지요. 제가 그 사무실을 쓸어 낸 게 몇 번인지 모릅니다. 노신사께서 백마 조랑말을 타고 일하러 총총 들어오시면서 거리 좌우를 죽 둘러보고 사람마다 고개를 끄덕여 주시던 걸 얼마나 보았는지요! 참말이지, 그 어른은 인기 있는 분이셨습니다! 런던에서도 통하셨을 겝니다!”\n\n“여기서 놀즈베리까지는 얼마나 됩니까?”\n\n“한참 가셔야지요, 선생님.” 서기가, 거리를 부풀려 재고 이곳에서 저곳으로 가는 어려움을 유난히 실감 나게 여기는, 시골 사람 특유의 그 버릇대로 말했다. “오 마일은 족히 될걸요!”\n\n아직 오전 이른 시각이었다. 놀즈베리까지 걸어갔다가 웰밍엄으로 되돌아올 시간은 넉넉했다. 그리고 퍼시벌 경의 어머니가 혼인하기 전의 성품과 처지에 관해 내 조사를 도와줄 사람으로, 그 도시에서 그곳 사무 변호사보다 더 적합한 이는 아마 없을 터였다. 당장 걸어서 놀즈베리로 가기로 결심하고, 나는 앞장서서 성구실을 나섰다.\n\n“고맙습니다, 선생님.” 내가 작은 사례를 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주자 서기가 말했다. “정말로 놀즈베리까지 내내 걸어갔다 오실 참입니까? 저런! 선생님도 다리가 튼튼하시구먼요. 그게 얼마나 큰 복입니까, 안 그렇습니까? 길은 저기입니다, 놓치실 리 없지요. 저도 같이 갔으면 좋겠습니다만. 이런 잊힌 구석에서 런던 신사분을 뵙는 건 즐거운 일이지요. 소식도 좀 듣고요. 좋은 아침 되십시오, 선생님. 다시 한번 고맙습니다.”\n\n우리는 헤어졌다. 교회를 뒤로하고 걸어 나오다가 돌아보니, 아래쪽 길에 그 두 사내가 다시 나와 있었고, 세 번째 사람이 그들과 함께 있었다. 그 세 번째 인물은 전날 저녁에 내가 철도역까지 뒤쫓았던 검은 옷의 자그마한 사내였다.\n\n셋은 잠시 함께 서서 이야기를 나누더니 이내 갈라섰다. 검은 옷의 사내는 혼자서 웰밍엄 쪽으로 떠났고, 나머지 둘은 함께 남아 내가 걸음을 옮기는 대로 곧 뒤를 밟으려고 기다리는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n나는 그자들에게 내가 그들을 특별히 눈여겨보고 있다는 티를 내지 않은 채 가던 길을 계속했다. 그 순간 그들은 나에게 아무런 짜증도 불러일으키지 않았다. 오히려 가라앉던 내 희망을 되살려 주는 편이었다. 혼인의 증거를 발견하고 놀란 나머지, 나는 성구실 근처에서 그 사내들을 처음 보았을 때 이끌어 냈던 추론을 잊고 있었다. 그들이 다시 나타난 것은, 캐서릭 부인과의 면담 다음에 내가 구웰밍엄 교회를 찾아갈 것을 퍼시벌 경이 미리 내다보고 있었음을 나에게 일깨워 주었다. 그렇지 않고서야 나를 기다리라고 그곳에 밀정을 배치했을 리가 없다. 성구실에서 겉으로는 매끄럽고 그럴듯해 보였지만, 그 아래에는 뭔가 잘못된 것이 있었다. 내가 아직 알아내지 못한 무언가가, 어쩌면 그 등록부 책 안에 있었다.\n\n# X\n\n교회가 시야에서 사라지자마자, 나는 놀즈베리를 향한 길을 부지런히 재촉했다.\n\n길은 대부분 곧고 평탄했다. 뒤를 돌아볼 때마다 두 첩자가 꾸준히 나를 뒤따라오는 것이 보였다. 길의 대부분에서 그들은 안전한 거리를 두고 뒤에 처져 있었다. 그러나 한두 번은 나를 따라잡으려는 듯 걸음을 재촉했다가, 이내 멈춰 서서 서로 의논한 뒤 다시 본래 자리로 물러났다. 그들에게 무언가 특별한 목적이 있는 것은 분명했고, 그것을 이루는 최선의 방법을 두고 망설이거나 의견이 갈리는 듯했다. 그들의 계략이 정확히 무엇인지는 짐작할 수 없었으나, 도중에 무슨 사달이 나지 않고 놀즈베리에 닿을 수 있을지 나는 심각하게 의심스러웠다. 그 의심은 현실이 되었다.\n\n인적 없는 구간에 막 들어선 참이었다. 얼마 앞쪽에 급하게 꺾이는 모퉁이가 있었고, 시간을 헤아려 보건대 이제 읍내에 거의 다다랐겠거니 하고 막 생각을 끝낸 참에, 나는 문득 등 뒤 바로 가까이에서 두 사내의 발소리를 들었다.\n\n미처 돌아보기도 전에 그중 하나가 — 런던에서 나를 미행했던 그 사내가 — 잰걸음으로 내 왼쪽을 스쳐 지나며 어깨로 나를 떠밀었다. 올드 웰밍엄에서부터 줄곧 그와 그의 동료가 내 뒤를 밟아 온 그 태도에 나는 스스로 자각한 것보다 훨씬 더 화가 나 있었고, 불행히도 손바닥으로 그 사내를 세게 밀쳐 냈다. 그는 즉시 도와달라고 소리쳤다. 그의 동료, 사냥터지기 옷차림의 키 큰 사내가 내 오른쪽으로 달려들었고, 다음 순간 두 악당은 길 한복판에서 나를 사이에 끼워 두 팔을 붙들어 꼼짝 못 하게 만들었다.\n\n나를 노린 덫이 놓여 있었다는 확신과, 거기에 걸려들었음을 아는 데서 오는 분함이 다행히도 나를 붙들어, 두 사내와 부질없는 몸싸움을 벌여 처지를 더 나쁘게 만드는 일만은 면하게 했다. 둘 중 하나는 아마 일대일로도 나보다 한 수 위였을 것이다. 나는 그들을 뿌리치려던 첫 본능적인 몸짓을 억누르고, 도움을 청할 만한 사람이 근처에 있는지 둘러보았다.\n\n옆 밭에서 일꾼 하나가 일하고 있었는데, 지금까지 벌어진 일을 다 보았을 것이 틀림없었다. 나는 그에게 읍내까지 우리를 따라와 달라고 외쳤다. 그는 무뚝뚝하고 고집스럽게 고개를 저으며 큰길에서 물러앉은 오두막 쪽으로 걸어가 버렸다. 그와 동시에 나를 사이에 끼고 붙든 사내들은 나를 폭행죄로 고발하겠다고 선언했다. 나는 이제 반항하지 않을 만큼은 냉정했고 또 현명했다. “내 팔을 놓으시오.” 내가 말했다. “그러면 함께 읍내로 가겠소.” 사냥터지기 옷차림의 사내는 거칠게 거절했다. 그러나 키가 작은 쪽은 뒷일을 헤아릴 만큼 약았고, 동료가 쓸데없는 폭력으로 스스로 발목을 잡히게 두지 않았다. 그가 다른 쪽에게 신호를 보냈고, 나는 두 팔이 자유로운 채로 그들 사이에 끼어 걸어갔다.\n\n우리는 길의 모퉁이에 이르렀고, 바로 눈앞에 놀즈베리의 변두리가 펼쳐져 있었다. 지역 순경 하나가 길가 인도를 따라 걷고 있었다. 사내들은 곧바로 그에게 호소했다. 순경은 지금 시각이면 치안판사가 읍청사에 나와 있을 테니 즉시 그 앞에 출두하는 게 좋겠다고 대답했다.\n\n우리는 읍청사로 갔다. 서기가 정식 소환장을 작성했고, 이런 자리에 으레 따르는 과장과 으레 따르는 사실 왜곡을 곁들여 내게 죄가 제기되었다. 치안판사는 — 성마른 사람으로, 제 권한을 휘두르는 데서 시큰둥한 재미를 느끼는 부류였다 — 길 위나 그 근처에서 그 폭행을 목격한 사람이 있는지 물었고, 놀랍게도 고발인은 밭에 일꾼이 있었음을 인정했다. 그러나 그 인정의 속셈은 치안판사의 다음 말로 곧 밝혀졌다. 그는 증인을 불러 세우기 위해 나를 즉시 구류하겠다고 하면서, 다만 책임질 만한 보증인 한 사람을 세울 수 있다면 재출두를 조건으로 보석을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 내가 이 읍내에 알려진 사람이었다면 그는 내 서약만으로 풀어주었을 테지만, 나는 완전한 타관 사람이었으므로 책임 있는 보증인을 구해야만 했다.\n\n이제 그 술책의 전모가 내게 드러났다. 내가 아무 연고도 없어 보석으로 풀려날 가망이 없는 읍내에서 구류가 불가피하도록 꾸며진 일이었다. 구류는 다음 치안판사 개정일까지 사흘에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 내가 갇혀 있는 그 사흘 동안 퍼시벌 경은 내 쪽에서 방해할 걱정이라고는 조금도 없이, 앞으로의 내 행보를 가로막을 — 어쩌면 발각을 아예 모면할 — 온갖 수단을 마음대로 쓸 수 있었다. 사흘이 지나면 고발은 틀림없이 취하될 것이고, 증인의 출석 따위는 아무 쓸모도 없어질 터였다.\n\n이 심술궂은 발목잡기에 대한 나의 분노는 — 아니, 거의 절망이라 해도 좋으리라 — 그 자체로는 그토록 비열하고 하찮으면서도 초래될 결과는 그토록 낙담스럽고 심각한 것이었기에, 처음에는 내가 처한 곤경에서 벗어날 최선의 방책을 궁리할 여력조차 앗아 갔다. 나는 어리석게도 필기도구를 청해, 내 진짜 처지를 치안판사에게 은밀히 알릴 생각을 했다. 그 짓이 얼마나 가망 없고 경솔한지는 실제로 편지의 첫머리를 쓰고 난 뒤에야 비로소 떠올랐다. 종이를 밀쳐 내고서야 — 부끄러운 말이지만, 무력한 처지의 분함에 거의 굴복하려던 순간에야 — 문득 한 가지 방책이 머리에 떠올랐다. 퍼시벌 경도 미처 예상하지 못했을 방책이었고, 몇 시간 안에 나를 다시 자유롭게 해 줄지도 모르는 방책이었다. 나는 내가 놓인 사정을 오크 로지의 도슨 의사에게 알리기로 마음먹었다.\n\n기억하시겠지만, 나는 블랙워터 파크 근방에서 처음 탐문을 벌이던 무렵 이 신사의 집을 찾아간 적이 있었고, 그때 홀콤 양이 나를 최대한 호의로 대해 달라고 강력히 부탁하는 소개 편지를 그에게 건넸었다. 나는 이제 그 편지와, 앞서 도슨 의사에게 내 조사가 얼마나 미묘하고 위험한 성격의 것인지 말해 두었던 바를 상기시키며 글을 썼다. 로라에 관한 진실은 그에게 밝히지 않았고, 다만 내 볼일이 홀콤 양이 관련된 사사로운 집안의 이해에 지극히 중대한 것이라고만 설명해 두었었다. 지금도 같은 신중함을 지켜, 놀즈베리에 와 있는 까닭도 같은 식으로 둘러댔다. 그리고 그가 잘 아는 어느 부인이 내게 맡긴 신뢰와, 그의 집에서 내가 받은 환대에 비추어, 아는 이 하나 없는 이곳에서 도움을 청하는 것이 온당한지 아닌지 의사 자신이 판단해 달라고 적었다.\n\n나는 심부름꾼을 사서 내 편지를 지니고 당장 마차로 달려가되, 그 마차로 곧바로 의사를 모셔 올 수 있도록 하는 허락을 얻었다. 오크 로지는 블랙워터에서 놀즈베리 쪽에 있었다. 심부름꾼은 사십 분이면 그곳에 닿고 다시 사십 분이면 도슨 의사를 모셔 올 수 있다고 장담했다. 나는 의사가 집에 없거든 어디에 있든 찾아가라고 일러 두고는, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 인내와 온갖 희망을 짜내어 결과를 기다리며 앉아 있었다.\n\n심부름꾼이 떠난 것은 한 시 반이 채 되기 전이었다. 세 시 반이 되기 전에 그는 의사를 모시고 돌아왔다. 도슨 의사의 친절함과, 그 즉각적인 도움을 지극히 당연한 일처럼 대하는 그 조심스러운 태도에 나는 거의 압도되었다. 요구된 보석이 제시되었고 곧바로 받아들여졌다. 그날 오후 네 시가 되기 전에 나는 놀즈베리 거리에서 — 다시 자유로운 몸이 되어 — 그 선량한 노의사와 뜨겁게 악수하고 있었다.\n\n도슨 의사는 함께 오크 로지로 돌아가 그날 밤 묵어 가라고 후하게 청했다. 나는 다만 내 시간이 내 것이 아니라고 대답할 수밖에 없었고, 며칠 뒤에 찾아뵙게 해 달라고, 그때 다시 감사를 드리고 마땅히 그분이 들으실 자격이 있으나 지금은 도저히 드릴 수 없는 모든 설명을 올리겠다고 청할 수밖에 없었다. 우리는 서로 다정한 인사를 나누고 헤어졌고, 나는 곧장 하이 스트리트에 있는 완즈버러 씨의 사무실로 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n이제 시간이 무엇보다 중요했다.\n\n내가 보석으로 풀려났다는 소식은 밤이 되기 전에 반드시 퍼시벌 경의 귀에 들어갈 것이었다. 앞으로 몇 시간 안에 그의 최악의 두려움이 옳았음을 입증하고 그를 내 손아귀에 무력하게 붙들어 둘 처지에 서지 못한다면, 나는 애써 얻은 발판을 한 뼘도 남김없이 잃고 영영 되찾지 못할지도 몰랐다. 그 사내의 파렴치한 본성, 그가 이 고장에서 가진 영향력, 앞을 더듬는 내 조사가 그를 위협하는 폭로의 절박한 위험 — 이 모두가 단 일 분도 헛되이 버리지 말고 확실한 발견을 향해 밀고 나가라고 나를 채근했다. 도슨 의사가 오기를 기다리는 동안 나는 생각할 시간을 얻었고, 그 시간을 잘 썼다. 그 수다스러운 늙은 서기와 나눈 대화의 어떤 대목들은 당시에는 나를 지루하게 했으나 이제 새로운 의미를 띠고 기억에 되살아났고, 성구실에 있을 때는 떠오르지 않았던 어두운 의심 하나가 마음을 스쳤다. 놀즈베리로 오는 길에 나는 퍼시벌 경의 어머니에 관한 정보를 얻으려고 완즈버러 씨를 찾을 작정이었을 뿐이다. 그러나 이제 내 목적은 올드 웰밍엄 교회 대장의 사본을 살펴보는 것이었다.\n\n완즈버러 씨는 내가 찾았을 때 사무실에 있었다.\n\n그는 쾌활하고 얼굴이 불그레하며 느긋해 보이는 사람으로 — 변호사라기보다 시골 지주에 가까웠다 — 내 청을 듣고 놀라면서도 재미있어하는 눈치였다. 그는 아버지가 남긴 대장 사본에 대해 들어는 보았으나 자기 눈으로 본 적조차 없었다. 그것을 찾는 사람이 여태 한 명도 없었으니, 아버지가 세상을 떠난 뒤로 손대지 않은 다른 서류들과 함께 틀림없이 금고방에 있을 것이라고 했다. 노인장이 살아 계셔서 그 귀한 사본을 마침내 찾는 소리를 들으셨다면 좋았을 것을, 하고 완즈버러 씨는 아쉬워했다. 그랬다면 아버지는 그 애지중지하던 취미에 어느 때보다 더 열을 올리셨으리라는 것이다. 그런데 나는 어떻게 그 사본 이야기를 들었는가? 읍내의 누군가에게서 들었는가?\n\n나는 되도록 그 질문을 슬쩍 피했다. 조사가 이 단계에 이른 이상 아무리 조심해도 지나치지 않았고, 이미 원본 대장을 살펴보았다는 사실을 완즈버러 씨에게 섣불리 알리지 않는 편이 나았다. 그래서 나는 집안일을 알아보는 중인데 그 목적상 시간을 조금이라도 아끼는 것이 매우 중요하다고 나 자신을 설명했다. 그날 우편으로 어떤 세부 사항을 런던에 부치고 싶은데, 사본 대장을 한 번 들여다보면 — 물론 필요한 수수료는 내고서 — 내가 원하는 것을 얻어 올드 웰밍엄까지 다시 다녀오는 수고를 덜 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 나는 나중에 원본 대장의 등본이 필요해질 경우에는 완즈버러 씨의 사무소에 그 서류의 발급을 신청하겠다는 말도 덧붙였다.\n\n이 설명이 있고 나자 사본을 내주는 데 아무 이의도 없었다. 서기 하나가 금고방으로 보내졌고, 얼마쯤 지체된 뒤 그 책을 들고 돌아왔다. 그것은 성구실에 있던 책과 크기가 꼭 같았고, 다만 사본 쪽이 더 말끔하게 제본되어 있다는 점만 달랐다. 나는 그것을 들고 비어 있는 책상으로 갔다. 손이 떨리고 머리가 불덩이처럼 달아올랐다. 책을 펴기 전에 나는 방 안 사람들에게 내 동요를 되도록 감출 필요를 느꼈다.\n\n맨 앞의 백지에 — 내가 제일 먼저 펼친 곳이었다 — 빛바랜 잉크로 몇 줄이 적혀 있었다. 거기에는 이런 말이 담겨 있었다 —\n\n“웰밍엄 교구 교회 혼인 대장의 사본. 나의 지시로 작성되었고, 그 뒤 내가 직접 원본과 한 항목씩 대조하였음. (서명) 로버트 완즈버러, 성구실 서기.” 이 기록 아래에는 다른 필체로 한 줄이 덧붙어 있었는데, 다음과 같았다. “1800년 1월 1일부터 1815년 6월 30일까지에 해당함.”\n\n나는 1803년 9월 항목을 펼쳤다. 내 세례명과 같은 이름을 가진 사내의 혼인 기록을 찾아냈다. 두 형제의 혼인이 나란히 적힌 이중 기록도 찾아냈다. 그런데 그 두 기록 사이, 페이지 아래쪽에는?\n\n아무것도 없었다! 교회 대장에 적혀 있던 펠릭스 글라이드 경과 세실리아 제인 엘스터의 혼인 기록은 흔적조차 없었다!\n\n심장이 크게 뛰어올랐고, 숨이 막힐 듯 두근거렸다. 나는 다시 들여다보았다 — 내 눈이 본 증거를 차마 믿기가 두려웠다. 아니! 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그 혼인은 거기 없었다. 사본의 기록들은 원본의 기록들과 페이지 위에서 정확히 같은 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 한쪽 페이지의 마지막 기록은 나와 같은 세례명을 가진 사내의 혼인이었다. 그 아래에는 빈자리가 있었다 — 두 형제의 혼인 기록을 담기에는 너무 좁아서 비워 둔 것이 분명한 자리였고, 그 기록은 사본에서도 원본에서와 마찬가지로 다음 페이지 맨 위를 차지하고 있었다. 그 빈자리가 모든 사연을 말해 주었다! 교회 대장의 그 자리는 혼인이 거행되고 사본이 만들어진 1803년부터 퍼시벌 경이 올드 웰밍엄에 나타난 1827년까지 그대로 비어 있었음이 틀림없다. 여기 놀즈베리에서는 위조를 저지를 기회가 사본을 통해 내 눈앞에 드러났고, 저기 올드 웰밍엄에서는 그 위조가 교회 대장에 실제로 저질러져 있었던 것이다.\n\n머리가 어지러워져 나는 쓰러지지 않으려고 책상을 붙들었다. 그 필사적인 사내를 두고 내게 떠올랐던 온갖 의심 가운데 진실 근처에라도 간 것은 하나도 없었다.\n\n그가 애초에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아니라는 것, 준남작 작위와 블랙워터 파크에 대해 그 영지에서 일하는 가장 가난한 일꾼만큼의 권리도 없다는 것 — 그런 생각은 단 한 번도 내 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 한때 나는 그가 앤 캐서릭의 아버지가 아닐까 여겼고, 또 한때는 앤 캐서릭의 남편이었던 것이 아닐까 여겼다. 그가 실제로 저지른 죄는 처음부터 끝까지 내 상상이 미칠 수 있는 가장 먼 데보다도 훨씬 멀리 있었다.\n\n그 사기가 이루어진 하찮은 수단, 그것이 뜻하는 범죄의 크기와 대담함, 발각될 경우 뒤따를 결과의 끔찍함이 나를 압도했다. 그 가련한 자의 짐승 같은 안절부절, 비굴한 이중성과 무모한 폭력 사이를 오가던 그 필사적인 진폭, 앤 캐서릭을 정신병원에 가둔 짓과 제 아내에게 저지른 비열한 음모에 몸을 던지게 만든 죄지은 자의 미치광이 같은 불신 — 그 두 여자가 자기의 무서운 비밀을 안다는 막연한 의심 하나만으로 그리한 것을 이제 누가 이상하게 여기겠는가? 그 비밀이 드러났다면 지난 세월에는 그를 교수대에 올렸을 것이고, 지금이라면 종신 유형에 처할 것이었다. 설령 그의 속임수에 당한 이들이 법의 처벌만은 면하게 해 준다 해도, 그 비밀이 드러나면 그는 자기가 가로챈 이름과 지위와 영지와 사회적 존재 전부를 단번에 잃을 터였다. 이것이 바로 그 비밀이었고, 그것은 내 손에 있었다! 내가 한마디만 하면 저택도 땅도 준남작 작위도 그에게서 영영 사라진다 — 내가 한마디만 하면 그는 이름도 돈도 벗도 없는 부랑자가 되어 세상 밖으로 내쫓긴다! 그 사내의 앞날 전체가 내 입술에 매달려 있었고 — 이제 그도 나만큼이나 그것을 확실히 알고 있었다!\n\n그 마지막 생각이 나를 다잡았다. 이제 내 사소한 행동 하나하나까지 이끌어야 할 신중함에, 내 것보다 훨씬 더 귀한 이해가 걸려 있었다. 퍼시벌 경이 내게 저지르지 못할 배신이란 없었다. 그 위태롭고 절박한 처지에서 그는 어떤 위험 앞에서도 물러서지 않을 것이고 어떤 범죄 앞에서도 움츠러들지 않을 것이었다. 제 몸을 구하기 위해서라면 그는 말 그대로 무슨 짓이든 서슴지 않을 사람이었다.\n\n나는 잠시 궁리했다. 무엇보다 먼저 할 일은 방금 내가 해낸 발견을 문서로 확실히 남기는 것, 그리고 내 신상에 무슨 변고가 생길 경우에 대비해 그 증거를 퍼시벌 경의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 두는 것이었다. 대장 사본은 완즈버러 씨의 금고방에 있으니 안전할 것이 분명했다. 그러나 성구실에 있는 원본의 처지는, 내 눈으로 직접 보았듯이 안전과는 거리가 멀었다.\n\n이 다급한 사정에 나는 교회로 되돌아가 다시 서기에게 부탁해서, 그날 밤 잠들기 전에 대장에서 필요한 부분을 베껴 두기로 결심했다. 그때 나는 법적으로 인증된 등본이라야 하며 나 혼자 작성한 서류는 증거로서 마땅한 무게를 지닐 수 없다는 것을 알지 못했다. 나는 그것을 몰랐고, 지금 하는 일을 비밀로 지키겠다는 결심 때문에 그 정보를 얻을 수도 있었을 질문을 아무것도 하지 못했다. 내 유일한 조바심은 올드 웰밍엄으로 돌아가려는 조바심이었다. 나는 완즈버러 씨가 이미 눈치챈 내 얼굴과 태도의 흐트러짐에 대해 되도록 그럴듯한 변명을 둘러대고, 필요한 수수료를 그의 탁자에 올려놓고, 하루이틀 안에 편지를 드리겠다고 약속한 뒤, 머릿속이 어지럽고 열병처럼 피가 혈관을 두드리는 채로 사무실을 나섰다.\n\n막 어두워지려는 참이었다. 큰길에서 다시 미행당하고 습격당할지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다.\n\n내 지팡이는 가벼운 것이라 방어에는 거의, 아니 전혀 쓸모가 없었다. 나는 놀즈베리를 떠나기 전에 걸음을 멈추고 튼튼한 시골 몽둥이를 하나 샀다. 짧고 머리가 묵직한 것이었다. 이 투박한 무기만 있으면 한 사람이 나를 막아서는 정도야 맞설 만했다. 여럿이 덤빈다면 두 다리를 믿으면 되었다. 학창 시절 나는 이름난 달리기 선수였고, 그 뒤 중앙아메리카에서 겪은 시절에도 연습이 모자란 적은 없었다.\n\n나는 빠른 걸음으로 읍내를 나서서 길 한복판으로만 걸었다.\n\n안개 같은 가랑비가 내리고 있어서, 길의 전반부 동안에는 미행이 붙었는지 아닌지 확인할 수 없었다. 그러나 후반부에 들어 교회에서 이 마일쯤 떨어졌겠거니 짐작하던 참에, 한 사내가 빗속에서 나를 앞질러 달려가는 것이 보였고, 이어 길가 밭의 문이 탁 닫히는 소리가 들렸다. 나는 몽둥이를 손에 쥐고 귀를 곤두세우고 안개와 어둠 너머를 뚫어 보려 눈을 부릅뜬 채 곧장 걸어 나갔다. 백 야드도 채 나아가기 전에 오른쪽 산울타리에서 부스럭 소리가 나더니 사내 셋이 길로 뛰어나왔다.\n\n나는 순간적으로 옆의 보도로 비켜섰다. 앞선 두 사내는 미처 몸을 멈추지 못하고 나를 지나쳐 갔다. 세 번째는 번개처럼 빨랐다. 그는 멈춰 서서 반쯤 돌아서더니 막대기로 나를 후려쳤다. 아무렇게나 겨눈 일격이라 심하지는 않았다. 그것은 내 왼쪽 어깨에 떨어졌다. 나는 그의 머리에 묵직하게 되갚아 주었다. 그는 비틀거리며 물러나다가, 마침 나에게 달려들던 두 동료와 부딪쳤다. 그 덕에 나는 한순간의 여유를 얻었다. 나는 그들 곁을 빠져나가 다시 길 한복판으로 나서서 전속력으로 달렸다.\n\n다치지 않은 두 사내가 나를 뒤쫓았다. 둘 다 잘 달렸다. 길은 매끄럽고 평탄했으며, 처음 오 분 남짓 동안 나는 그들과의 거리를 벌리지 못하고 있음을 느꼈다. 어둠 속에서 오래 달리는 것은 위험한 일이었다. 양옆 산울타리의 희미한 검은 선이 겨우 보일 뿐이어서 길에 무엇이라도 걸리면 영락없이 나동그라질 판이었다. 이윽고 땅이 달라지는 것이 느껴졌다. 굽이에서 평지가 내리막으로 떨어졌다가 그 너머에서 다시 솟아올랐다. 내리막에서는 사내들이 오히려 나를 따라붙었으나, 오르막에서는 내가 거리를 벌리기 시작했다. 규칙적으로 빠르게 울리던 그들의 발소리가 귀에서 점점 희미해졌고, 나는 그 소리로 미루어 어둠 속에서 그들이 나를 지나쳐 갈 만큼 충분히 앞섰다고 셈하고 들판으로 들어가기로 했다. 보도 쪽으로 벗어나, 보인다기보다 짐작으로 산울타리의 첫 번째 틈을 향해 나아갔다. 그것은 닫힌 문이었다. 나는 뛰어넘었고, 밭 안에 들어선 것을 알고는 길을 등진 채 꾸준히 밭을 가로질렀다. 사내들이 여전히 달리며 그 문을 지나치는 소리가 들렸고, 일 분쯤 뒤에는 하나가 다른 하나에게 돌아오라고 외치는 소리가 들렸다. 이제 그들이 무엇을 하든 상관없었다. 나는 그들의 눈에서도 귀에서도 벗어나 있었다. 나는 밭을 곧장 가로질러 끝까지 간 뒤, 거기서 잠시 숨을 돌리며 기다렸다.\n\n큰길로 되돌아가는 모험은 할 수 없었으나, 그럼에도 나는 그날 저녁 안에 올드 웰밍엄에 닿고야 말겠다고 마음먹었다.\n\n길잡이가 되어 줄 달도 별도 없었다. 다만 놀즈베리를 떠날 때 바람과 비를 등지고 걸었다는 것만은 알았으므로, 지금도 그것들을 등지고 간다면 적어도 아주 엉뚱한 방향으로 나아가지는 않으리라 확신할 수 있었다.\n\n이 방침대로 나는 들판을 가로질렀다. 이따금 잠시 진로를 바꾸게 만드는 산울타리와 도랑과 덤불 말고는 더 나쁜 장애를 만나지 않은 채 나아가다가, 어느 비탈에 이르러 앞으로 땅이 가파르게 내려가는 것을 보았다. 나는 골짜기 바닥까지 내려가 산울타리를 비집고 빠져나와 좁은 길로 나섰다. 큰길을 벗어날 때 오른쪽으로 돌았으므로, 이번에는 내가 벗어난 선으로 되돌아가 볼 요량으로 왼쪽으로 돌았다. 십 분 남짓 진창인 좁은 길의 굽이를 따라간 끝에, 창 하나에 불이 켜진 오두막이 보였다. 정원 문이 길 쪽으로 열려 있어서 나는 길을 물으려고 곧장 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n문을 두드리기도 전에 문이 갑자기 열리더니 한 사내가 불 켜진 등을 손에 들고 뛰어나왔다. 그는 나를 보고 멈춰 서서 등을 치켜들었다. 우리는 서로를 알아보고 둘 다 흠칫했다. 헤매던 걸음이 나를 마을 변두리로 빙 둘러 데려와 아래쪽 끝으로 내놓았던 것이다. 나는 올드 웰밍엄에 돌아와 있었고, 등을 든 사내는 다름 아닌 그날 아침에 만난 그 교구 서기였다.\n\n그의 태도는 내가 마지막으로 본 뒤로 그사이에 이상하게 달라져 있었다. 그는 의심스럽고 얼떨떨한 낯빛이었고, 불그레하던 뺨은 시뻘겋게 달아올라 있었다. 그리고 그가 입을 열어 처음 한 말은 내게 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n“열쇠는 어디 있습니까?” 그가 물었다. “선생이 가져가셨소?”\n\n“무슨 열쇠 말이오?” 내가 되물었다. “나는 방금 놀즈베리에서 오는 길이오. 대체 무슨 열쇠를 말하는 겁니까?”\n\n“성구실 열쇠 말이오. 주여, 우리를 구하시고 도우소서! 어떡하면 좋단 말인가? 열쇠가 없어졌소! 들립니까?” 노인은 흥분해서 등을 내 앞에 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다. “열쇠가 없어졌단 말이오!”\n\n“어떻게 말이오? 언제? 대체 누가 가져갔단 말입니까?”\n\n“모르겠소.” 서기는 어둠 속에서 이리저리 미친 듯이 두리번거리며 말했다. “나도 방금 막 돌아왔소. 아침에 하루 종일 일이 많다고 말씀드렸지요 — 문을 잠그고 창문도 내려 닫았단 말입니다 — 그런데 지금은 열려 있어요, 창문이 열려 있단 말이오. 보시오! 누가 저기 들어가서 열쇠를 가져갔어요.”\n\n그는 여닫이창 쪽으로 돌아서서 그것이 활짝 열려 있음을 내게 보여 주었다. 그가 등을 휘두르는 바람에 등피 문이 걸쇠에서 풀렸고, 바람이 순식간에 촛불을 꺼 버렸다.\n\n“불을 다시 켜 오시오.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 둘이 함께 성구실로 갑시다. 어서! 어서요!”\n\n나는 그를 재촉해 집 안으로 들여보냈다. 내가 예상할 만한 온갖 이유가 있었던 그 배신, 내가 얻어 낸 모든 이점을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 그 배신이 어쩌면 바로 그 순간에 진행되고 있을 터였다. 교회에 닿고 싶은 조바심이 어찌나 컸던지 서기가 등에 다시 불을 붙이는 동안 오두막 안에 가만히 있을 수가 없었다. 나는 밖으로 나가 정원 길을 따라 내려가 좁은 길로 들어섰다.\n\n열 걸음도 채 나아가기 전에 교회 쪽에서 오는 사내 하나가 내게 다가왔다. 우리가 마주쳤을 때 그는 공손하게 말을 건넸다. 얼굴은 보이지 않았으나 목소리로만 판단하건대 나로서는 전혀 모르는 사람이었다.\n\n“실례합니다, 퍼시벌 경——” 그가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n나는 그가 더 말하기 전에 그를 막았다.\n\n“어두워서 잘못 보신 거요.” 내가 말했다. “나는 퍼시벌 경이 아니오.”\n\n사내는 즉시 물러섰다.\n\n“저희 주인님이신 줄 알았습니다.” 그는 얼떨떨하고 미심쩍은 투로 중얼거렸다.\n\n“여기서 주인을 만나기로 되어 있었소?”\n\n“이 길에서 기다리라는 분부를 받았습니다.”\n\n그 대답을 남기고 그는 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔다. 오두막 쪽을 돌아보니 서기가 다시 불을 붙인 등을 들고 나오는 것이 보였다. 나는 노인의 팔을 붙들어 걸음을 더 재촉하게 했다. 우리는 좁은 길을 서둘러 지나가며 아까 내게 말을 건 사람 옆을 스쳤다. 등불 빛에 보이는 대로 판단하건대 그는 제복을 입지 않은 하인이었다.\n\n“저 사람은 누굽니까?” 서기가 속삭였다. “열쇠에 대해 뭘 알고 있는 걸까요?”\n\n“물어보느라 지체할 것 없소.” 내가 대답했다. “먼저 성구실로 갑시다.”\n\n교회는 낮에도 좁은 길 끝에 이르기 전에는 보이지 않았다. 그 지점에서 건물로 이어지는 오르막을 오르는데, 우리가 든 불빛에 이끌린 마을 아이 하나가 — 사내아이였다 — 바짝 다가와 서기를 알아보았다.\n\n“저기예, 아저씨.” 아이가 서기의 웃옷을 참견하듯 잡아당기며 말했다. “교회 저 우에 누가 있어예. 안에서 지가 문을 잠그는 소리를 내가 들었다카이 — 성냥으로 불 켜는 소리도 들었다카이.”\n\n서기는 부들부들 떨며 내게 무겁게 기대었다.\n\n“자! 자!” 나는 기운을 북돋우며 말했다. “아직 늦지 않았소. 누구든 그자를 붙잡읍시다. 등을 들고 되도록 빨리 나를 따라오시오.”\n\n나는 서둘러 언덕을 올랐다. 밤하늘을 배경으로 어렴풋이 제일 먼저 알아본 것은 교회 탑의 검은 덩어리였다. 성구실 쪽으로 돌아가려고 옆으로 비켜서는데 바로 곁에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸다. 그 하인이 우리 뒤를 따라 교회까지 올라온 것이었다. “해치려는 게 아닙니다.” 내가 돌아서서 노려보자 그가 말했다. “저는 그저 주인님을 찾고 있을 뿐입니다.” 그 말투에는 틀림없는 두려움이 배어 있었다. 나는 그를 아랑곳하지 않고 계속 나아갔다.\n\n모퉁이를 돌아 성구실이 눈에 들어온 순간, 나는 지붕의 채광창이 안에서부터 환하게 밝혀져 있는 것을 보았다. 그것은 별 하나 없는 컴컴한 하늘을 배경으로 눈부시게 빛나고 있었다.\n\n나는 교회 묘지를 가로질러 문으로 달려갔다.\n\n가까이 다가가자 축축한 밤공기 속으로 이상한 냄새가 새어 나오고 있었다. 안에서 무언가 탁탁거리는 소리가 들렸다 — 위쪽의 불빛이 점점 더 밝아지는 것이 보였다 — 유리 한 장이 쩍 갈라졌다 — 나는 문으로 달려가 손을 얹었다. 성구실에 불이 난 것이었다!\n\n미처 움직이기도 전에, 그것을 알고 숨을 돌리기도 전에, 나는 안쪽에서 문에 부딪히는 묵직한 소리에 소름이 끼쳤다. 열쇠가 자물쇠 안에서 거칠게 돌아가는 소리가 들렸다 — 문 뒤에서 무섭도록 새된 소리로 높아진 한 사내의 목소리가 살려 달라고 비명을 지르는 것이 들렸다.\n\n나를 뒤따라온 하인이 몸서리치며 비틀비틀 물러나더니 무릎을 꿇었다. “오, 하느님!” 그가 말했다. “퍼시벌 경이십니다!”\n\n그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지는 순간 서기가 우리에게 합류했고, 동시에 자물쇠 속에서 열쇠가 갈리는 소리를 내며 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아갔다.\n\n“주여, 저 영혼에 자비를 베푸소서!” 노인이 말했다. “저분은 이제 끝장이오, 죽은 목숨이오. 자물쇠를 망가뜨려 버렸소.”\n\n나는 문으로 달려들었다. 몇 주 동안이나 내 모든 생각을 채우고 내 모든 행동을 지배해 온 그 한 가지 골똘한 목적이 순식간에 마음에서 사라졌다. 그 사내의 죄가 안긴 무정한 상처에 대한 기억도, 그가 무자비하게 짓밟은 사랑과 순결과 행복에 대한 기억도, 그에게 마땅한 무서운 심판을 반드시 치르게 하겠노라고 마음속에 세운 맹세에 대한 기억도 모두 꿈처럼 내 기억에서 지워졌다. 나는 그가 처한 상황의 끔찍함 외에는 아무것도 기억하지 못했다. 그를 무서운 죽음에서 구해 내려는 자연스러운 인간의 충동 외에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했다.\n\n“다른 문을 열어 보시오!” 나는 소리쳤다. “교회로 통하는 문 말이오! 자물쇠가 망가졌소. 거기에 한순간이라도 더 지체하면 당신은 죽은 목숨이오.”\n\n열쇠가 마지막으로 돌아간 뒤로는 살려 달라는 외침이 다시 들리지 않았다. 그가 아직 살아 있음을 알려 줄 어떤 소리도 이제는 나지 않았다. 들리는 것이라고는 점점 빨라지는 불길의 탁탁거림과 위쪽 채광창 유리가 날카롭게 튀는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n나는 두 동행을 둘러보았다. 하인은 일어서서 등을 들고는 멍하니 문 쪽으로 치켜들고 있었다. 공포가 그를 완전한 백치로 만들어 버린 듯했다. 그는 내 발치에서 기다리다가 내가 움직이면 개처럼 나를 따라다녔다. 서기는 묘비 하나에 웅크리고 앉아 벌벌 떨며 혼자 신음하고 있었다. 그들을 바라본 그 한순간만으로도 둘 다 아무 쓸모가 없다는 것을 알기에 충분했다.\n\n내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 모른 채, 문득 떠오른 첫 충동에 따라 필사적으로 움직여, 나는 하인을 붙들어 성구실 벽에 밀어붙였다. “엎드리시오!” 내가 말했다. “그리고 돌을 붙잡고 버티시오. 당신을 밟고 지붕으로 올라가겠소 — 채광창을 깨뜨려서 저 사람에게 공기를 넣어 주겠소!”\n\n사내는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었으나 굳게 버텨 주었다. 나는 몽둥이를 입에 물고 그의 등에 올라서서 두 손으로 난간을 붙잡았고, 순식간에 지붕 위에 올라섰다. 그 미친 듯한 다급함과 동요 속에서, 공기를 들여보내는 대신 불길을 내보내게 될지도 모른다는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았다. 나는 채광창을 내리쳐 금이 가고 헐거워진 유리를 단번에 두들겨 부수었다. 불길이 굴에서 뛰쳐나오는 들짐승처럼 솟구쳤다. 내가 선 자리에서 마침 바람이 불길을 내게서 밀어내 주지 않았더라면, 내 안간힘은 그 자리에서 끝나 버렸을 것이다. 나는 연기가 불길과 함께 머리 위로 쏟아져 나오는 동안 지붕에 웅크렸다. 어른거리며 번쩍이는 불빛에 벽 아래에서 멍하니 올려다보는 하인의 얼굴이 보였고, 묘비 위에 일어서서 절망에 겨워 두 손을 비틀어 대는 서기가 보였고, 그 너머 교회 묘지에 몰려든 마을의 몇 안 되는 사람들, 초췌한 사내들과 겁에 질린 여인들이 보였다. 모두가 그 무시무시한 붉은 불빛 속에, 숨 막히는 검은 연기 속에 나타났다 사라졌다 했다. 그리고 내 발밑의 그 사내! — 우리 모두와 그토록 가까이에서 질식하고 불타고 죽어 가면서도, 우리 손이 도무지 닿지 않는 그 사내!\n\n그 생각에 나는 반쯤 미칠 지경이었다. 나는 두 손으로 지붕에 매달려 몸을 내린 뒤 땅으로 뛰어내렸다.\n\n“교회 열쇠 말이오!” 나는 서기에게 소리쳤다. “그쪽으로 해 봐야 하오 — 안쪽 문을 부수고 열 수만 있다면 아직 저 사람을 구할 수 있을지 모르오.”\n\n“안 되오, 안 돼요, 안 됩니다!” 노인이 외쳤다. “가망 없소! 교회 열쇠와 성구실 열쇠는 같은 고리에 꿰여 있소 — 둘 다 저 안에 있단 말이오! 아, 선생, 저분은 이미 구할 수 없소 — 지금쯤은 재와 티끌이 되었을 거요!”\n\n“읍내에서 불을 볼 거라예.” 내 뒤의 사람들 틈에서 한 목소리가 말했다. “읍내에 소방 펌푸가 있으니께. 교회는 살릴 거라예.”\n\n나는 그 남자를 불렀다—그는 정신을 놓지 않고 있었다—나는 그에게 이리 와서 나와 이야기하자고 소리쳤다. 마을의 소방 펌프가 여기까지 닿으려면 적어도 십오 분은 걸릴 터였다. 그 시간 내내 아무것도 하지 못한 채 있어야 한다는 공포는 내가 견딜 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 나는 내 이성을 거스르면서까지, 성구실 안에서 파멸을 맞은 그 가엾은 자가 아직 바닥에 정신을 잃고 누워 있을 뿐 죽지는 않았을지도 모른다고 스스로를 설득했다. 문을 부수고 들어간다면 그를 구할 수 있지 않을까? 나는 그 육중한 자물쇠의 힘을 알고 있었다—못을 박은 참나무 문의 두께를 알고 있었다—평범한 방법으로 그 둘에 맞서 보아야 가망이 없다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 교회 근처에 헐어 놓은 오두막들에는 아직 들보가 남아 있지 않겠는가? 그중 하나를 가져다 문을 쳐부술 공성추로 쓴다면 어떨까?\n\n그 생각은 부서진 채광창으로 치솟는 불길처럼 내 몸을 꿰뚫고 지나갔다. 나는 마을의 소방 펌프를 처음 입에 올린 남자에게 말을 걸었다. “곡괭이는 가까이 있소?” 있다고 했다. “손도끼와 톱과 밧줄 한 가닥도?” 있다, 있다, 있다! 나는 등불을 손에 든 채 마을 사람들 사이로 뛰어 내려갔다. “나를 돕는 사람에게는 한 사람당 오 실링씩 주겠소!” 그 말에 사람들이 벌떡 살아났다. 가난이 품은 그 두 번째 게걸스러운 굶주림—돈에 대한 굶주림—이 순식간에 그들을 소란과 활기 속으로 몰아넣었다. “둘은 등불을 더 가져오시오, 있거든! 둘은 곡괭이와 연장을 가져오고! 나머지는 나를 따라와 들보를 찾읍시다!” 그들이 환호했다—굶주려 새된 목소리로 환호했다. 여자들과 아이들이 양옆으로 물러났다. 우리는 한 덩어리가 되어 교회 묘지 길을 따라 첫 번째 빈 오두막으로 달려 내려갔다. 뒤에 남은 사람은 서기 하나뿐이었다—그 딱한 늙은 서기는 평평한 묘석 위에 서서 교회를 두고 흐느끼며 통곡하고 있었다. 하인은 여전히 내 뒤꿈치를 바짝 따라왔다—우리가 오두막 안으로 밀고 들어갈 때 그의 하얗고 무력하고 공포에 질린 얼굴이 내 어깨 너머에 바싹 붙어 있었다. 위층에서 뜯겨 나온 서까래들이 땅바닥에 흩어져 있었으나 너무 가벼웠다. 우리 머리 위로 들보 하나가 가로질러 있었는데, 팔과 곡괭이가 닿지 못할 높이는 아니었다—무너진 벽에 양끝이 단단히 박힌 들보였고, 천장과 마루는 죄다 뜯겨 나갔으며, 위쪽 지붕에는 하늘이 훤히 보이는 커다란 구멍이 뚫려 있었다. 우리는 들보의 양끝을 한꺼번에 공격했다. 아, 어찌나 단단히 붙어 있던지—벽의 벽돌과 회반죽이 어찌나 우리에게 버티던지! 우리는 치고, 당기고, 뜯어냈다. 들보가 한쪽 끝에서 빠졌다—벽돌 덩이 하나를 달고 내려왔다. 문간에 옹기종기 모여 우리를 보고 있던 여자들에게서 비명이 터졌고—남자들에게서는 고함이 터졌다—둘이 넘어졌으나 다치지는 않았다. 다 함께 한 번 더 당기자—들보가 양끝에서 모두 풀려났다. 우리는 그것을 들어 올리고 문간을 비우라고 외쳤다. 자, 이제 일이다! 이제 문으로 돌진이다! 저기 불길이 하늘로 뻗어 오른다, 그 어느 때보다 밝게 뻗어 올라 우리를 비춘다! 묘지 길을 따라 침착하게—들보를 들고 침착하게, 문을 향해 돌진할 준비. 하나, 둘, 셋—간다. 다시 환호가 터져 나온다, 걷잡을 수 없이. 벌써 문이 흔들렸다, 자물쇠가 버틴다면 돌쩌귀라도 나갈 것이다. 들보를 들고 한 번 더! 하나, 둘, 셋—간다. 헐거워졌다! 그 틈으로 사방에서 불길이 슬금슬금 우리를 향해 혀를 내민다. 한 번 더, 마지막 돌진! 문이 요란한 소리를 내며 안으로 무너진다. 커다란 경외의 침묵이, 숨죽인 기다림의 정적이 우리 모두의 살아 있는 넋을 사로잡는다. 우리는 시신을 찾는다. 얼굴을 태우는 열기가 우리를 뒤로 밀어낸다. 아무것도 보이지 않는다—위에도, 아래에도, 방 안 어디에도, 살아 움직이는 불의 장막 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다.\n\n“그분은 어디 있습니까?” 하인이 불길을 멍하니 응시하며 속삭였다.\n\n“재가 되고 먼지가 되었지요.” 서기가 말했다. “장부들도 재가 되고 먼지가 되었고—아아, 여러분! 교회도 곧 재가 되고 먼지가 될 겁니다.”\n\n입을 연 사람은 그 둘뿐이었다. 그들이 다시 잠잠해지자, 그 정적 속에서 움직이는 것이라고는 불길이 부글거리고 탁탁 튀는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n들어 보라!\n\n멀리서 거칠게 덜컹거리는 소리—이어 전속력으로 달리는 말발굽의 텅 빈 울림—이어 낮은 포효, 수백 명이 한꺼번에 외치고 고함치는, 모든 것을 압도하는 소란. 마침내 소방 펌프가 온 것이었다.\n\n내 주위의 사람들이 모두 불에서 등을 돌려 언덕마루로 앞다투어 달려갔다. 늙은 서기도 나머지 사람들을 따라가려 했으나 기운이 다해 있었다. 나는 그가 묘석 하나를 붙잡고 서 있는 것을 보았다. “교회를 구해 주시오!” 그는 마치 소방대원들이 벌써 자기 말을 들을 수 있기라도 한 듯 가냘프게 외쳤다.\n\n“교회를 구해 주시오!”\n\n끝내 움직이지 않은 단 한 사람은 그 하인이었다. 그는 그 자리에 선 채, 변함없이 텅 빈 눈길로 불길에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 나는 그에게 말을 걸었고, 그의 팔을 흔들었다. 그는 깨울 수 있는 상태가 아니었다. 그는 다만 한 번 더 속삭였다. “그분은 어디 있습니까?”\n\n십 분 만에 소방 펌프가 자리를 잡았고, 교회 뒤편 우물이 물을 대었으며, 호스가 성구실 문간까지 끌려갔다. 이제 와서 나에게 도움을 청했더라도 나는 아무것도 해 줄 수 없었을 것이다. 내 의지의 기력은 사라졌고—힘은 다했으며—그가 죽었다는 것을 알게 된 지금, 소용돌이치던 내 생각은 무섭도록 갑작스럽게 잠잠해져 있었다.\n\n나는 쓸모없이 무력하게 서서, 불타는 방 안을 보고, 보고, 또 보았다.\n\n나는 불이 서서히 제압되는 것을 보았다. 이글거리던 빛이 사그라들고—수증기가 흰 구름처럼 피어올랐으며, 그 사이로 바닥에 쌓인 잉걸불 더미가 붉고 검게 드러났다. 잠시 멈춤이 있었고—이어 소방대원들과 경찰이 다 함께 앞으로 나아가 문간을 막았고—이어 낮은 목소리로 의논이 오갔고—그런 다음 두 사람이 무리에서 떨어져 나와 군중을 헤치고 교회 묘지 밖으로 보내졌다. 군중은 죽은 듯 조용히 양옆으로 물러나 그들을 지나가게 했다.\n\n얼마 뒤 사람들 사이로 커다란 전율이 훑고 지나갔고, 살아 있는 사람들로 이루어진 그 길이 천천히 넓어졌다. 두 사람은 빈집에서 떼어 온 문짝 하나를 들고 그 길을 따라 돌아왔다. 그들은 그것을 성구실로 옮겨 안으로 들어갔다. 경찰이 다시 문간을 에워쌌고, 군중 속에서 남자들이 둘씩 셋씩 슬그머니 빠져나와 먼저 보려고 그들 뒤에 섰다. 다른 이들은 먼저 들으려고 가까이서 기다렸다. 여자들과 아이들이 그 나중 무리에 섞여 있었다.\n\n성구실에서 나온 소식이 군중 사이로 퍼져 나가기 시작했다—그것은 입에서 입으로 천천히 흘러 내가 서 있는 자리까지 이르렀다. 나는 낮고 다급한 어조로 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 질문과 대답을 사방에서 들었다.\n\n“찾았대?” “찾았대.”—“어디서?” “문 앞에서, 엎드린 채로.”—“어느 문?” “교회로 통하는 문. 머리가 그 문에 닿아 있었고—엎어져 있었대.”—“얼굴도 탔대?” “아니.” “탔다니까.” “아니야, 그을렸을 뿐 타지는 않았어—엎드려 있었다고 하잖아.”—“누구래? 귀족이라던데.” “아니야, 귀족은 아니고. ‘서’ 뭐라던데, ‘서’는 기사라는 뜻이야.” “‘준남작’인가 하는 것도 되고.” “아니야.” “된다니까.”—“거기서 뭘 하려던 거래?” “좋은 일은 아니었겠지, 그건 틀림없어.”—“일부러 그런 걸까?”—“일부러 제 몸을 태워?”—“제 몸 말고, 성구실 말이야.”—“보기에 끔찍해?” “끔찍하지!”—“그래도 얼굴은 아니지?” “응, 응, 얼굴은 그렇게까지는 아니래. 아는 사람 없나?” “안다는 사람이 하나 있다던데.”—“누구?” “하인이라나 봐. 그런데 넋이 나가 버려서 경찰이 그 말을 안 믿는대.”—“그 사람 말고 누군지 아는 이는 없어?” “쉿——!”\n\n권위 있는 어떤 사람의 크고 또렷한 목소리가 내 주위에서 낮게 웅웅거리던 말소리를 순식간에 잠재웠다.\n\n“그를 구하려 했던 신사분은 어디 계십니까?” 그 목소리가 말했다.\n\n“여기요, 나리—여기 계십니다!” 열띤 얼굴 수십이 내 주위로 몰려들었고—열띤 팔 수십이 군중을 갈랐다. 권위 있는 그 남자가 등불을 손에 들고 내게 다가왔다.\n\n“이쪽으로 오시지요, 선생.” 그가 조용히 말했다.\n\n나는 그에게 말을 할 수도 없었고, 그가 내 팔을 잡았을 때 거스를 수도 없었다. 나는 죽은 그 사람을 살아생전에 본 적이 없다고—나 같은 낯선 사람을 통해 그의 신원을 밝힐 가망은 없다고 말하려 했다. 그러나 말이 입술에서 힘을 잃었다. 나는 아득하고, 말이 없었고, 무력했다.\n\n“이 사람을 아십니까, 선생?”\n\n나는 사람들이 둘러선 원 안에 서 있었다. 내 맞은편에 선 세 사람이 등불을 땅바닥 가까이 낮게 들고 있었다. 그들의 눈은, 그리고 나머지 모두의 눈은, 말없이 무언가를 기다리며 내 얼굴에 고정되어 있었다. 나는 내 발치에 무엇이 있는지 알았다—그들이 왜 등불을 그토록 땅바닥 가까이 들고 있는지 알았다.\n\n“신원을 확인해 주시겠습니까, 선생?”\n\n내 눈이 천천히 내려갔다. 처음에는 그 아래로 거친 범포 천 한 장밖에 보이지 않았다. 그 위로 빗방울이 떨어지는 소리가 그 무서운 정적 속에서 또렷이 들렸다. 나는 천을 따라 위쪽으로 눈길을 옮겼고, 그 끝에서, 누런 불빛 속에 뻣뻣하고 험상궂고 시커멓게—거기 그의 죽은 얼굴이 있었다.\n\n그리하여 처음이자 마지막으로 나는 그를 보았다. 그리하여 하느님의 징벌은 그와 내가 그렇게 만나도록 정하셨다.\n\n# XI\n\n검시는 검시관과 읍 당국자들에게 무겁게 작용한 몇 가지 지역 사정 때문에 서둘러 진행되었다. 그것은 이튿날 오후에 열렸다. 나는 당연하게도 그 조사의 목적을 돕기 위해 소환된 증인들 가운데 한 사람이었다.\n\n그날 아침 내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 우체국에 가서 매리언에게서 오기로 되어 있는 편지를 묻는 것이었다. 아무리 이례적인 상황 변화가 있다 한들, 런던을 떠나 있는 동안 내 마음을 짓누르던 그 하나의 큰 불안만은 어쩌지 못했다. 내가 자리를 비운 사이에 아무 불행도 일어나지 않았음을 확인시켜 줄 유일한 증거인 아침 편지는, 여전히 내 하루를 열어 주는 온 마음을 사로잡는 관심사였다.\n\n다행히도 매리언의 편지는 우체국에서 나를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n아무 일도 없었다—두 사람 다 내가 떠나올 때와 똑같이 무사하고 잘 지내고 있었다. 로라는 사랑을 전하며, 내가 돌아오는 날을 하루 앞서 미리 알려 달라고 청했다. 그 언니는 이 부탁에 설명을 덧붙여, 자기 개인 지갑에서 “1소버린 가까이” 모아 두었으며, 내가 돌아오는 날을 축하할 만찬을 주문하고 차려 낼 특권을 자기가 갖겠노라 주장했다고 적었다. 나는 그 자잘한 집안 이야기들을, 바로 전날 저녁에 벌어진 일의 끔찍한 기억이 아직 생생한 채로, 밝은 아침 햇빛 속에서 읽었다. 로라가 그 진상을 갑작스레 알게 되는 일만은 막아야 한다는 것이 그 편지가 내게 일깨운 첫 번째 고려 사항이었다. 나는 곧바로 매리언에게 편지를 써서 이 지면에 적어 온 것을 그대로 알렸다—되도록 서서히, 되도록 부드럽게 소식을 전하고, 내가 없는 동안 신문 따위가 로라의 눈에 띄지 않게 하라고 당부했다. 용기와 미더움이 덜한 다른 여자였다면, 나는 진실을 남김없이 털어놓기 전에 망설였을지도 모른다. 그러나 나는 매리언에 대해 지금껏 겪어 온 바에 충실해야 했고, 나 자신을 믿듯 그녀를 믿어야 했다.\n\n내 편지는 어쩔 수 없이 긴 것이 되었다. 검시가 열리는 곳으로 갈 시간이 될 때까지 나는 줄곧 그 편지에 매달려 있었다.\n\n법적 심리의 목적들은 필연적으로 남다른 복잡함과 어려움에 둘러싸여 있었다. 고인이 어떻게 죽음에 이르렀는가를 조사하는 일 말고도, 화재의 원인과 열쇠의 절취, 그리고 불길이 치솟을 무렵 성구실에 낯선 자가 있었다는 사실에 관하여 밝혀야 할 중대한 문제들이 있었다. 죽은 자의 신원 확인조차 아직 끝나지 않은 터였다. 하인의 정신이 온전치 못한 상태였기에 경찰은 그가 주인을 알아보았다고 주장한 것을 미덥지 않게 여겼다. 그들은 간밤에 놀즈베리로 사람을 보내어 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 용모를 잘 아는 증인들의 출석을 확보해 두었고, 아침이 되자마자 블랙워터 파크에 연락을 취했다. 이런 조처 덕분에 검시관과 배심은 신원 문제를 매듭짓고 하인의 진술이 옳았음을 확인할 수 있었다. 유능한 증인들이 내놓은 증언과 몇 가지 사실의 발견은, 뒤이어 죽은 자의 시계를 살펴봄으로써 한층 더 뒷받침되었다. 시계 안쪽에는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 문장과 이름이 새겨져 있었다.\n\n다음 심문은 화재에 관한 것이었다.\n\n하인과 나, 그리고 성구실에서 불 켜는 소리를 들었다는 소년이 맨 먼저 불려 나온 증인이었다. 소년은 제법 또렷하게 증언했으나, 하인의 정신은 그가 받은 충격에서 아직 회복되지 못한 상태였다—그가 조사의 목적에 도움이 될 수 없다는 것은 명백했고, 그래서 그는 내려가라는 말을 들었다.\n\n다행히도 나에 대한 신문은 길지 않았다. 나는 고인을 알지 못했고—그를 본 적이 한 번도 없었으며—그가 올드 웰밍엄에 와 있다는 것도 알지 못했고—시신이 발견되었을 때 성구실 안에 있지도 않았다. 내가 입증할 수 있는 것이라고는, 길을 묻느라 서기의 오두막에 들렀다는 것—그에게서 열쇠를 잃어버렸다는 말을 들었다는 것—힘닿는 데까지 돕고자 그와 함께 교회로 갔다는 것—불을 보았다는 것—성구실 안에서 누군지 모를 사람이 헛되이 문을 열려고 애쓰는 소리를 들었다는 것—그리고 인간된 도리로 그 사람을 구하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다는 것뿐이었다. 고인을 알고 지내던 다른 증인들은, 그가 열쇠를 몰래 가져갔으리라는 점과 불타는 방 안에 있었던 까닭의 수수께끼를 설명할 수 있겠느냐는 물음을 받았다. 그러나 검시관은, 그럴 만도 하게, 이 고장에 전혀 낯선 사람이자 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게도 전혀 낯선 사람인 내가 그 두 가지 점에 관하여 무슨 증언을 내놓을 처지가 못 된다고 당연하게 여기는 듯했다.\n\n정식 신문이 끝났을 때 나 자신이 취해야 할 길은 분명해 보였다. 나는 내 사사로운 확신을 자진하여 진술할 까닭이 없다고 느꼈다. 첫째로는, 내 추측을 뒷받침할 모든 증거가 불타 버린 등기부와 함께 재가 된 마당에 그렇게 해 봐야 아무 실질적 소용이 없기 때문이었고, 둘째로는, 음모의 전말을 다 드러내지 않고서는 내 의견을—아무 뒷받침도 없는 내 의견을—알아듣게 진술할 수 없었을 뿐 아니라, 그렇게 하면 이미 카일 씨의 마음에 남긴 것과 똑같이 미덥지 못한 인상을 검시관과 배심의 마음에도 틀림없이 남기게 될 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n그러나 이 지면에서는, 그리고 그때로부터 이만한 세월이 흐른 지금에는, 여기 적어 온 그런 조심이나 삼감이 내 의견을 자유로이 밝히는 것을 얽맬 까닭이 없다. 내 펜이 다른 사건들을 다루기 전에, 열쇠의 절취와 화재의 발생과 그 사람의 죽음을 내 확신이 어떻게 설명하는지를 간략히 적어 두겠다.\n\n내가 보석으로 풀려났다는 소식은, 내가 믿기로, 퍼시벌 경을 마지막 수단으로 몰아갔다. 길에서의 습격 시도가 그 수단 가운데 하나였고, 위조가 저질러진 등기부의 그 쪽을 없애 버림으로써 자기 범죄의 실질적 증거를 모조리 지워 버리는 것이 다른 하나이자 둘 중 더 확실한 쪽이었다. 놀즈베리에 있는 인증 사본과 대조할 원본의 발췌를 내가 내놓지 못한다면, 나는 확실한 증거를 내놓을 수 없고 치명적인 폭로로 그를 위협할 수도 없었다. 그가 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 것이라고는, 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 성구실에 들어가서, 등기부의 그 쪽을 뜯어내고, 들어갈 때처럼 남몰래 성구실을 빠져나오는 것뿐이었다.\n\n이렇게 가정하면, 그가 왜 밤이 되기를 기다렸다가 일을 벌였는지, 왜 서기가 자리를 비운 틈을 타서 열쇠를 손에 넣었는지 쉽게 이해가 된다. 필요한 등기부를 찾으려면 불을 켤 수밖에 없었을 것이고, 웬만한 조심성이라면 캐묻기 좋아하는 낯선 자가 끼어들 경우에 대비하여, 혹은 마침 내가 그 근처에 있을 경우에 대비하여 안에서 문을 잠그라고 일러 주었을 것이다.\n\n나는 그가 일부러 성구실에 불을 질러 등기부의 소실을 사고인 것처럼 꾸미려 했다고는 믿을 수 없다. 도움의 손길이 재빨리 닿을지도 모른다는 실낱같은 가능성, 그래서 아무리 희박하나마 장부들이 구조될지도 모른다는 가능성만으로도, 잠깐만 생각해 보면 그런 종류의 발상은 그의 머리에서 지워 버리기에 충분했을 것이다. 성구실 안에 있던 불붙기 쉬운 물건들의 양—짚이며 서류며 포장 상자며 마른 나무며 벌레 먹은 낡은 장들—을 떠올려 보면, 내 판단으로는 모든 개연성이 그 불을 그의 성냥이나 등불로 인한 사고의 결과로 가리키고 있다.\n\n이런 정황에서 그가 처음 보인 충동은 틀림없이 불길을 끄려는 것이었을 테고, 그것이 안 되자 두 번째 충동은 (자물쇠가 어떤 상태인지 몰랐으므로) 자기가 들어온 그 문으로 빠져나가려는 것이었으리라. 내가 그를 불렀을 때, 불길은 이미 교회로 통하는 문 앞을 가로질러 번져 있었을 것이다. 그 문 양옆으로 장들이 늘어서 있었고, 바로 그 곁에 불붙기 쉬운 다른 물건들이 놓여 있었으니 말이다. 십중팔구 그가 안쪽 문으로 빠져나가려 했을 때, (그 방 안에 갇혀 있던) 연기와 불길이 그에게 너무 벅찼을 것이다. 그는 죽음의 혼절 속에 쓰러졌을 것이다—시신이 발견된 그 자리에 주저앉았을 것이다—내가 채광창을 깨려고 지붕에 막 올라섰을 바로 그때에. 설령 우리가 그 뒤에 교회 안으로 들어가서 그쪽에서 문을 부수어 열 수 있었다 하더라도, 그 지체는 치명적이었을 것이다. 그때쯤이면 그는 이미 구할 수 없는, 진작에 구할 수 없는 사람이 되어 있었으리라. 우리는 다만 불길이 교회 안으로 거침없이 들어오게 했을 뿐일 테고—지금은 무사히 남은 그 교회도 그랬더라면 성구실과 같은 운명을 겪었을 것이다. 우리가 그 빈 오두막에 이르러 젖 먹던 힘까지 다해 들보를 뜯어내기 전에 그는 이미 죽은 사람이었다는 것을, 나는 조금도 의심하지 않으며 누구라도 의심할 수 없을 것이다.\n\n이것이 눈에 보이는 사실로 드러난 결과를 두고 내가 세울 수 있는 어떤 추론이든 가장 가깝게 다가갈 수 있는 설명이다. 내가 적어 온 그대로, 밖에 있던 우리에게 일들은 그렇게 지나갔다. 내가 이야기한 그대로, 그의 시신은 그렇게 발견되었다.\n\n검시는 하루 연기되었다—이 사건의 알 수 없는 정황들을 설명해 줄, 법의 눈이 인정할 만한 해명이 아직 아무것도 발견되지 않았기 때문이었다.\n\n증인을 더 소환하고, 고인의 런던 사무 변호사에게 출석을 요청하기로 정해졌다. 또한 의사 한 사람에게는 하인의 정신 상태를 보고할 임무가 맡겨졌는데, 그 상태로는 당장 조금이라도 중요한 증언을 하기 어려워 보였기 때문이다. 그는 다만 얼떨떨한 투로, 불이 난 밤에 좁은 길에서 기다리라는 명을 받았다는 것과, 고인이 자기 주인인 것만은 틀림없다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모른다고 되뇔 뿐이었다.\n\n내 나름의 짐작은 이러했다. 그는 먼저 (본인은 아무 죄의식 없이) 전날 서기가 집을 비운다는 사실을 알아내는 데 쓰였고, 그다음에는 내가 길에서의 습격을 벗어나 퍼시벌 경과 나 사이에 충돌이 벌어질 경우 주인을 돕도록 교회 가까이에 (다만 성구실에서는 보이지 않는 곳에) 기다리라는 명을 받았으리라는 것이다. 덧붙여 두어야 할 것은, 이 견해를 확인해 줄 증언을 그 사내에게서는 끝내 얻지 못했다는 사실이다. 그에 대한 의사의 소견서는 그가 지녔던 얼마 안 되는 정신 능력마저 심하게 흔들렸다고 밝혔다. 속개된 검시에서도 그에게서 이렇다 할 것은 아무것도 끌어내지 못했으며, 내가 아는 한 그는 오늘까지도 회복하지 못했을지 모른다.\n\n나는 웰밍엄의 여관으로 돌아왔는데, 몸도 마음도 어찌나 지쳤는지, 겪어 낸 일들로 어찌나 쇠약해지고 가라앉았는지, 검시를 둘러싼 이 고장 사람들의 수군거림을 견디거나 식당에서 떠들어 대는 이들이 내게 던지는 하찮은 물음에 대답할 형편이 도무지 못 되었다. 나는 변변찮은 저녁 식사를 물리고 값싼 다락방으로 물러나, 잠깐이나마 고요를 얻고 방해받지 않고 로라와 매리언을 생각하기로 했다.\n\n내가 더 넉넉한 처지였다면 런던으로 돌아가서 그날 밤 다시 그 그리운 두 얼굴을 보는 것으로 스스로를 위로했을 것이다. 그러나 나는 부름을 받으면 속개된 검시에 출석해야 했고, 놀즈베리의 치안 판사 앞에 보석에 답해야 할 의무는 더더욱 무거웠다. 우리의 얄팍한 재원은 이미 축이 나 있었고, 어느 때보다도 더 불투명해진 앞날은, 이등칸 기차를 두 번 타는 적은 비용일망정 나 자신에게 그런 호사를 허락하여 살림을 공연히 줄이는 일을 두렵게 만들었다.\n\n이튿날은—검시 바로 다음 날은—내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 남겨졌다. 나는 아침에 다시 우체국에 들러 매리언에게서 오는 정기 보고를 찾는 것으로 하루를 시작했다. 편지는 여느 때처럼 나를 기다리고 있었고, 처음부터 끝까지 밝은 기분으로 쓰여 있었다. 나는 고마운 마음으로 그것을 읽고, 하루 동안 마음을 놓은 채 올드 웰밍엄으로 가서 아침 햇빛 속에 화재의 현장을 보려고 길을 나섰다.\n\n그곳에 이르렀을 때 나를 맞은 것은 얼마나 달라진 광경이었던가!\n\n우리가 알 수 없는 이 세상의 온갖 길목에서 사소한 것과 끔찍한 것은 손을 맞잡고 나란히 걷는다. 정황의 아이러니는 죽음의 어떤 참사도 존중하지 않는다. 내가 교회에 이르렀을 때, 불과 죽음을 말해 주는 진지한 흔적이라고는 짓밟힌 묘지의 꼴뿐이었다. 성구실 문간 앞에는 널빤지로 얼기설기 가림막이 세워져 있었다. 그 위에는 벌써 서투른 낙서 그림들이 그려져 있었고, 마을 아이들은 안을 들여다보기 좋은 구멍을 차지하겠다고 다투며 소리를 질러 댔다. 불타는 방에서 살려 달라는 외침이 들려오던 그 자리에서, 겁에 질린 하인이 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉던 그 자리에서, 이제는 닭들이 부산스레 몰려다니며 비 온 뒤에 나온 지렁이를 먼저 차지하려고 아우성이었다. 그리고 문짝과 그 무시무시한 짐이 놓여 있던 내 발치의 땅바닥에는, 노란 사발에 싸맨 인부의 점심이 주인을 기다리고 있었고, 그것을 지키는 충직한 잡종개가 음식에 가까이 왔다고 나를 향해 짖어 댔다. 늙은 서기는 더디게 시작된 수리 공사를 하릴없이 바라보며, 이제 입에 올릴 수 있는 관심사라고는 오직 하나, 이번 사고에 대해 제 몫의 책임을 면하는 일뿐이었다. 우리가 들보를 뜯어낼 때 공포의 초상 같던 새하얗게 질린 얼굴이 아직 내 기억에 남아 있는 마을 여자 하나는, 얼빠짐의 초상 같은 다른 여자와 낡은 빨래통을 사이에 두고 킬킬거리고 있었다. 죽어야 할 목숨에 진지한 것이란 아무것도 없구나! 온갖 영화를 누린 솔로몬도, 옷자락 주름마다 궁전 구석마다 하찮음의 씨앗을 숨긴 솔로몬이었다.\n\n그곳을 떠나면서 내 생각은, 이번이 처음은 아니었지만, 퍼시벌 경의 죽음으로 로라의 신원을 밝히려던 지금의 모든 희망이 온통 무너져 버렸다는 데로 향했다. 그는 갔다—그리고 그와 함께, 내 모든 수고와 모든 희망의 유일한 목표였던 그 기회도 사라졌다.\n\n나는 내 실패를 이보다 더 참된 눈으로 볼 수는 없었던가?\n\n그가 살아 있었다고 치자, 그 정황의 변화가 결과를 바꾸어 놓았겠는가? 남의 권리를 빼앗는 것이 퍼시벌 경이 저지른 범죄의 본질임을 알아낸 뒤에, 아무리 로라를 위해서라 한들 내가 그 발견을 흥정거리로 삼을 수 있었겠는가? 내 침묵의 대가로 그의 음모 자백을 얻어 낼 수 있었겠는가—그 침묵의 결과가 정당한 상속인에게서 영지를, 정당한 주인에게서 이름을 계속 빼앗아 두는 것일 수밖에 없는데? 될 수 없는 일이다! 퍼시벌 경이 살아 있었다면, (그 비밀의 참된 성질을 몰랐기에) 내가 그토록 큰 기대를 걸었던 그 발견은, 로라의 권리를 되찾기 위해 내 좋을 대로 덮어 두거나 세상에 알릴 수 있는 내 것이 될 수 없었을 것이다. 여느 정직함과 여느 명예심으로 보아도 나는 당장 그 타고난 권리를 빼앗긴 낯선 이에게 갔어야 했고—내 발견을 남김없이 그 낯선 이의 손에 넘겨줌으로써 승리가 내 것이 된 바로 그 순간에 승리를 포기했어야 했으며—나와 내 삶의 유일한 목표 사이를 가로막은 온갖 어려움을, 지금 내가 마음속 깊이 맞서기로 다짐한 그대로, 다시금 마주해야 했을 것이다!\n\n나는 마음이 가라앉은 채로, 지금껏 느껴 본 어느 때보다도 나 자신과 내 결의에 확신을 품고 웰밍엄으로 돌아왔다.\n\n여관으로 가는 길에 나는 캐서릭 부인이 사는 광장의 끄트머리를 지났다. 그 집으로 돌아가 그녀를 만나려고 다시 한번 애써 볼까? 아니다. 그녀가 들으리라고는 끝끝내 생각지 못했을 퍼시벌 경의 죽음 소식은 벌써 몇 시간 전에 그녀에게 닿았을 것이다. 검시에서 오간 모든 절차가 그날 아침 지역 신문에 실렸으니—그녀가 아직 모르는 것으로서 내가 일러 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그녀의 입을 열게 하는 데 대한 내 관심도 시들해져 있었다. 나는 그녀가 “퍼시벌 경에 관해 내가 예상하지 못할 소식은 없습니다. 다만 그가 죽었다는 소식만 빼고요.” 하고 말할 때 얼굴에 어리던 숨은 증오를 떠올렸다. 그 말을 하고 나서 헤어질 때 나를 향해 멎던 그 눈길의 은밀한 관심을 떠올렸다. 내 마음 깊은 곳의 어떤 본능이—나는 그것이 참되다고 느꼈다—그녀 앞에 다시 나선다는 생각을 역겹게 만들었다. 나는 광장에서 발길을 돌려 곧장 여관으로 돌아왔다.\n\n몇 시간 뒤, 식당에서 쉬고 있는데 급사가 편지 한 통을 내 손에 건네주었다. 내 이름 앞으로 온 것이었고, 물어보니 땅거미가 질 무렵, 가스등을 켜기 바로 전에 어떤 여자가 계산대에 두고 갔다는 것이었다. 그 여자는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 말을 붙이거나 누구인지 눈여겨볼 겨를도 없이 이미 가 버린 뒤였다.\n\n나는 편지를 열었다. 날짜도 서명도 없었고, 필체는 누가 보아도 일부러 꾸민 것이었다. 그러나 첫 문장을 다 읽기도 전에 나는 이 편지를 보낸 이가 누구인지 알았다—캐서릭 부인이었다.\n\n편지의 내용은 다음과 같다—나는 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 옮긴다:—\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED BY MRS. CATHERICK\n\n선생께,—말씀하신 대로 돌아오지 않으셨더군요. 상관없습니다—저는 그 소식을 알고 있고, 그것을 안다고 알려 드리려 이렇게 씁니다. 저를 떠나실 때 제 얼굴에서 무언가 남다른 것을 보셨는지요? 저는 속으로 궁금해하고 있었답니다. 그자가 무너지는 날이 마침내 온 것인지, 그리고 그 일을 이루도록 뽑힌 도구가 바로 당신인지를요. 과연 그러했고, 당신은 기어이 그 일을 이루어 내셨습니다.\n\n듣자 하니 당신은 그자의 목숨을 구해 보려 할 만큼 무르셨더군요. 만약 성공하셨더라면 저는 당신을 제 원수로 여겼을 것입니다. 이제 실패하셨으니 저는 당신을 제 벗으로 삼겠습니다. 당신의 캐물음이 그자를 겁주어 밤중에 성구실로 몰아넣었고—당신의 캐물음이, 당신도 모르는 사이에 당신 뜻과는 어긋나게, 스물세 해에 걸친 증오를 섬기고 그 복수를 이루어 주었습니다. 고맙습니다, 선생. 당신 뜻과는 상관없이 말이지요.\n\n저는 이 일을 해 주신 분께 빚을 졌습니다. 그 빚을 어떻게 갚을까요? 제가 아직 젊은 여자였다면 이렇게 말했을지도 모르지요. “자, 제 허리에 팔을 두르고, 좋으시다면 입을 맞추세요.” 저는 그만큼은 당신이 마음에 들었을 테고, 당신도 그 청을 받아들이셨을 것입니다—받아들이고말고요, 선생, 스무 해 전이었다면요! 그러나 이제 저는 늙은 여자입니다. 그러니 이렇게 하지요! 저는 당신의 호기심을 채워 드리는 것으로 빚을 갚을 수 있습니다. 저를 만나러 오셨을 때 당신은 제 사사로운 일들을 알고 싶어 몹시도 궁금해하셨지요—당신의 그 온갖 명민함으로도 제 도움 없이는 들여다볼 수 없었던 사사로운 일들, 지금 이 순간까지도 당신이 알아내지 못한 사사로운 일들 말입니다. 이제 그것들을 알게 되실 겁니다—당신의 호기심은 채워질 것입니다. 저는 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않겠습니다, 나의 훌륭하신 젊은 벗이여!\n\n1827년이면 당신은 아직 어린 사내아이였겠지요? 저는 그때 올드 웰밍엄에 살던, 얼굴이 반반한 젊은 여자였습니다. 남편이라고 있는 것은 하찮은 바보였고요. 또한 저는 어떤 신사분과 (어떻게 그리되었는지는 아무래도 좋습니다) 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸습니다 (누구였는지도 아무래도 좋습니다). 그자의 이름을 부르지는 않겠습니다. 그럴 까닭이 어디 있나요? 그것은 그자의 이름이 아니었으니까요. 그자에게는 애초에 이름이란 것이 없었지요. 이제쯤은 당신도 저만큼이나 그 사실을 알고 계실 테고요.\n\n그자가 어떻게 제 환심을 샀는지 말씀드리는 편이 더 요긴하겠지요. 저는 귀부인의 취향을 타고났고, 그자는 그것을 채워 주었습니다—달리 말하면 저를 찬탄했고, 제게 선물을 했지요. 찬탄과 선물을 물리칠 수 있는 여자는 없습니다—더구나 그 선물이 마침 갖고 싶던 바로 그것일 때는요. 그자는 그쯤은 알 만큼 약았습니다—사내들이란 대개 그렇지요. 당연히 그자는 답례로 무언가를 바랐습니다—사내들이란 다 그러니까요. 그런데 그 무언가가 무엇이었을 것 같습니까? 그야말로 하찮은 것이었습니다. 남편이 등을 돌린 사이에 성구실 열쇠와 그 안에 있는 장의 열쇠를 얻어다 주는 것, 그뿐이었지요. 왜 그런 은밀한 방식으로 열쇠를 구해 달라고 하느냐고 물었을 때 그자는 물론 거짓말을 했습니다. 그런 수고는 접어 두어도 좋았을 텐데요—저는 그 말을 믿지 않았으니까요. 그러나 저는 선물이 마음에 들었고, 더 갖고 싶었습니다. 그래서 남편 몰래 열쇠를 얻어다 주었고, 그자 몰래 그자를 지켜보았습니다. 한 번, 두 번, 네 번을 지켜보았고, 네 번째에 그자의 속셈을 알아냈습니다.\n\n저는 남의 일에 관해서라면 그리 까다롭게 군 적이 없었고, 그자가 제 잇속을 위해 등기부의 혼인 기록에 하나를 보태는 일에 대해서도 까다롭게 굴지 않았습니다.\n\n물론 그것이 나쁜 짓인 줄은 알았습니다만, 제게 해가 될 것은 없었고, 그것이 소란을 피우지 않을 한 가지 그럴듯한 이유였습니다. 게다가 저는 금시계와 금줄이 없었는데, 그것이 두 번째 이유로 앞의 것보다 나았고—그자가 바로 전날 런던에서 하나 구해다 주겠노라 약속했으니, 그것이 세 번째 이유로 무엇보다도 나았지요. 법이 그 범죄를 어떻게 보고 어떻게 벌하는지 제가 알았더라면, 저는 제 몸을 제대로 사려서 그 자리에서 그자를 들추어냈을 것입니다. 그러나 저는 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 금시계가 몹시도 갖고 싶었습니다. 제가 고집한 조건이라고는 그자가 저를 믿고 모든 것을 털어놓아야 한다는 것뿐이었습니다. 그때 저는 그자의 일을 궁금해했는데, 지금 당신이 제 일을 궁금해하는 것과 꼭 같았지요. 그자는 제 조건을 들어주었습니다—왜 그랬는지는 곧 아시게 될 겁니다.\n\n그자에게서 들은 이야기를 간추리면 이렇습니다. 여기 적는 것을 그자가 다 순순히 말해 준 것은 아닙니다. 얼마쯤은 구슬려서, 얼마쯤은 캐물어서 끌어냈지요. 저는 진실을 남김없이 알아내겠다고 작정했고, 그리했다고 믿습니다.\n\n그자는 자기 아버지와 어머니 사이가 실제로 어떤 형편이었는지를 어머니가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 알았고, 그전까지는 남들이나 다를 바 없이 몰랐습니다. 그때 아버지가 그것을 털어놓고 아들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠노라 약속했습니다. 그러나 그는 아무것도 하지 못한 채—유언장조차 남기지 않은 채 죽었습니다. 아들은 (누가 그를 나무라겠습니까?) 슬기롭게 제 앞가림을 했습니다. 곧바로 잉글랜드로 건너와 재산을 차지했지요. 그를 의심할 사람도 없었고, 안 된다고 할 사람도 없었습니다. 그의 아버지와 어머니는 늘 부부로 살아왔으니까요—그들을 알던 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데 누구도 그 밖의 무엇이라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었습니다. (진실이 알려졌더라면) 그 재산을 요구할 정당한 사람은 먼 친척이었는데, 그는 그것을 얻으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고 아버지가 죽었을 때 바다에 나가 있었습니다. 여기까지는 그자에게 아무 어려움이 없었습니다—그는 당연한 일인 양 재산을 차지했지요. 그러나 그 재산을 담보로 돈을 빌리는 일만은 당연한 일인 양 되지 않았습니다. 그러려면 두 가지가 필요했습니다. 하나는 자기 출생 증명서였고, 다른 하나는 부모의 혼인 증명서였지요. 출생 증명서는 쉽게 구했습니다—그는 외국에서 태어났고, 증명서가 격식대로 갖추어져 있었으니까요. 다른 하나가 문제였고, 그 문제가 그자를 올드 웰밍엄으로 데려왔습니다.\n\n한 가지 사정만 아니었다면 그자는 그 대신 놀즈베리로 갔을지도 모릅니다.\n\n그자의 어머니는 아버지를 만나기 바로 전까지 거기서 살았습니다—처녀 적 이름을 쓰고 살았지요. 실은 이미 혼인한 몸이었는데, 아일랜드에서 결혼했고 남편이 그 여자를 학대하다가 나중에 다른 사람과 달아나 버렸다는 것이 진상이었습니다. 이 사실은 확실한 데서 나온 것이니 믿으셔도 좋습니다—펠릭스 경이 자기가 왜 혼인하지 않았는지 그 까닭으로 아들에게 이야기했으니까요. 부모가 놀즈베리에서 만난 것을 알면서도 아들이 왜 그 교회의 등기부부터 손대지 않았는지 의아하실 겁니다. 아버지와 어머니가 거기서 결혼했다고 보는 것이 그럴듯했을 텐데 말이지요. 그 까닭은, (출생 증명서에 따르면 아버지와 어머니가 마땅히 혼인했어야 할 해인) 1803년에 놀즈베리 교회에서 직무를 맡았던 목사가, 그자가 1827년 새해에 재산을 차지할 때까지도 아직 살아 있었기 때문입니다. 이 껄끄러운 사정 때문에 그자는 조사를 우리 동네로까지 넓힐 수밖에 없었습니다. 여기에는 그런 위험이 없었지요. 우리 교회의 전임 목사는 몇 해 전에 이미 죽고 없었으니까요.\n\n올드 웰밍엄은 놀즈베리 못지않게 그자의 목적에 들어맞았습니다. 그 아버지는 어머니를 놀즈베리에서 데려와, 우리 마을에서 조금 떨어진 강가의 오두막에서 함께 살았습니다. 그가 홀몸일 적의 은둔벽을 알던 사람들은 혼인한 것으로 여겨진 뒤의 은둔벽도 이상하게 여기지 않았지요. 그가 보기에 흉측한 몰골이 아니었다면 그 부인과의 은거 생활이 의심을 샀을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 사정이 그러했으니, 그가 자기 추한 얼굴과 뒤틀린 몸을 더없이 철저한 은둔 속에 감추는 것을 아무도 이상히 여기지 않았습니다. 그는 파크를 차지하게 될 때까지 우리 동네에서 살았습니다. 스물서너 해가 지난 뒤에, (목사도 죽고 없는 마당에) 그의 혼인 역시 그의 다른 삶처럼 남몰래 치러졌으며 그것이 올드 웰밍엄 교회에서 있었던 일이 아니라고 누가 말할 수 있었겠습니까?\n\n그러니 말씀드린 대로, 아들은 제 잇속을 위해 몰래 일을 바로잡기에 우리 동네가 고를 수 있는 가장 확실한 곳임을 알았습니다. 그자가 혼인 등기부에 실제로 저지른 짓이 그 자리에서 즉흥으로—고쳐 생각한 끝에 나온 것이었다는 말을 들으면 놀라실지도 모르겠습니다.\n\n그자의 애초 생각은 그저 (해당하는 해와 달의) 그 장을 뜯어내어 남몰래 없애 버리고, 런던으로 돌아가 변호사들에게 아버지의 혼인 증명서를 떼어 오라고 시키면서, 물론 시치미를 뚝 떼고 사라진 그 장의 날짜를 일러 주는 것이었습니다. 그다음에는 그의 아버지와 어머니가 혼인하지 않았다고 아무도 말할 수 없을 테고, 그런 사정에서 변호사들이 돈을 빌려주는 일에 규정을 조금 눅여 줄지 어떨지는 몰라도 (그는 그러리라 여겼습니다) 어쨌든 자기 이름과 영지에 대한 권리에 의문이 제기되는 날에는 대답을 마련해 둔 셈이었지요.\n\n그런데 등기부를 몰래 제 눈으로 들여다보다가, 그자는 1803년 부분의 어느 쪽 맨 아래에 빈자리가 남아 있는 것을 발견했습니다. 긴 기록을 거기 적을 자리가 없어서 대신 다음 쪽 맨 위에 적은 모양이었지요. 이 우연을 보자 그자의 계획은 온통 바뀌었습니다. 그것은 그가 바라지도, 생각해 보지도 못한 기회였고—그자는 그것을 잡았습니다—어떻게 잡았는지는 당신도 아시지요. 그 빈자리가 출생 증명서와 정확히 들어맞으려면 등기부의 7월 부분에 있어야 했습니다. 그런데 그것은 9월 부분에 있었지요. 그러나 이 경우에도 의심스러운 물음이 나온다면 대답을 찾기는 어렵지 않았습니다. 자기가 일곱 달 만에 난 아이라고 말하기만 하면 되었으니까요.\n\n그자가 제게 그 이야기를 들려주었을 때, 저는 어리석게도 그자에게 얼마쯤 흥미와 얼마쯤 연민을 느꼈습니다—그자가 노린 것이 바로 그것이었음을 곧 아시게 될 겁니다. 저는 그자가 몹쓸 대접을 받았다고 생각했습니다. 아버지와 어머니가 혼인하지 않은 것이 그의 잘못은 아니었고, 아버지와 어머니의 잘못도 아니었으니까요. 저보다 더 까다로운 여자였다면—금시계와 금줄에 마음을 빼앗기지 않은 여자였다면—그자에게 무슨 변명거리를 찾아 주었을지도 모르지요. 어쨌든 저는 입을 다물었고, 그자가 하는 일을 감춰 주는 데 손을 보탰습니다.\n\n그자는 잉크 빛깔을 제대로 내느라 한참을 (제 단지며 병에다 몇 번이고 되풀이해 섞어 가며) 애썼고, 그 뒤로도 한참을 필체를 익히느라 애썼습니다. 그러나 끝내 해냈고, 무덤 속에 든 뒤의 제 어머니를 떳떳한 여자로 만들어 주었지요! 거기까지는 그자가 제게도 제법 명예롭게 처신했다는 것을 부인하지 않겠습니다. 제게 시계와 줄을 주었고, 그것들을 사는 데 돈을 아끼지 않았습니다. 둘 다 솜씨가 뛰어난 물건이었고, 아주 값진 것이었지요. 저는 지금도 그것들을 갖고 있습니다—그 시계는 참 잘도 갑니다.\n\n지난번에 당신은 클레멘츠 부인이 아는 것을 다 이야기해 주었다고 하셨지요. 그렇다면 제가 그 하찮은 추문에 대해 쓸 필요는 없겠습니다—제가 그 피해자였고, 죄 없는 피해자였다고 저는 분명히 말해 둡니다. 저와 그 훌륭하신 신사 나리가 남몰래 만나서 비밀을 주고받는 것을 보았을 때 제 남편의 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박혔는지는 당신도 저만큼이나 아실 테지요. 그러나 당신이 모르는 것은 그 신사와 저 사이가 어떻게 끝났느냐 하는 것입니다. 그자가 저를 어떻게 대했는지 읽어 보고 아시기 바랍니다.\n\n일이 그렇게 돌아가는 것을 보고 제가 그자에게 맨 처음 한 말은 이러했습니다. “제게 정당한 몫을 해 주세요—제가 받을 까닭이 없다는 걸 당신도 아시는 그 오명을 씻어 주세요. 제 남편에게 다 털어놓으라는 게 아닙니다—다만 신사로서 명예를 걸고, 그가 잘못 알았으며 제가 그이가 생각하는 그런 식으로 나무랄 짓을 하지 않았다고만 말해 주세요. 제가 당신을 위해 해 드린 그 모든 것을 생각하면, 그 정도 정당함은 베풀어 주셔야지요.” 그자는 딱 잘라 거절했습니다. 그러고는 제 남편과 이웃들이 그 거짓을 계속 믿게 두는 편이 자기에게 이롭다고 대놓고 말했습니다—그들이 그렇게 믿는 한 진실만은 결코 의심하지 않을 것이 확실하니까요. 저에게도 제 기개가 있어서, 제 입으로 그들에게 진실을 알리겠다고 했습니다. 그자의 대답은 짧고도 핵심을 찔렀습니다. 제가 입을 연다면, 그가 파멸한 사내가 되는 것만큼이나 저 또한 확실히 파멸한 여자가 되리라는 것이었지요.\n\n그렇습니다! 일이 거기까지 온 것입니다. 그자는 제가 자기를 돕느라 무릅쓴 위험에 대해 저를 속였습니다. 제 무지를 이용했고, 선물로 저를 꾀었고, 제 관심을 자기 이야기에 붙들어 두었습니다—그리고 그 결과 저를 자기 공범으로 만들어 놓았습니다. 그자는 이것을 태연히 인정했고, 끝으로 그 죄에 대한, 그리고 그 죄를 돕는 자에 대한 형벌이 실제로 얼마나 무서운 것인지를 그제야 처음으로 일러 주었습니다. 그 시절의 법은 요즘 듣기로 그렇다는 것만큼 인정이 많지 않았습니다. 교수형에 처해질 수 있는 것이 살인자만은 아니었고, 여자 죄수라고 해서 억울한 곤경에 빠진 귀부인처럼 대접받지도 않았지요. 고백하건대 그자는 저를 겁먹게 만들었습니다—그 비열한 사기꾼! 그 겁쟁이 악당! 이제 제가 그자를 얼마나 미워했는지 아시겠습니까? 제가 왜 이 모든 수고를—기꺼운 마음으로 이 수고를—무릅쓰고, 그자를 사냥해 잡은 갸륵하신 젊은 신사분의 호기심을 채워 드리려 하는지 아시겠습니까?\n\n자, 이야기를 이어 가지요. 그자는 저를 아주 절망으로까지 몰아넣을 만큼 어리석지는 않았습니다. 저는 궁지로 몰아넣어도 마냥 안전한 그런 여자가 아니었지요—그자도 그것을 알았기에, 앞날에 대한 제안으로 슬기롭게 저를 달랬습니다.\n\n제가 그자에게 해 준 일에 대해서는 얼마간 보답을 받을 만하고 (친절하게도 그렇게 말해 주더군요), 제가 겪은 일에 대해서도 얼마간 보상을 받을 만하다는 것이었습니다 (황송하게도 그렇게 덧붙이더군요). 그자는—이 너그러우신 악당께서는—두 가지 조건 아래 제게 넉넉한 연금을 분기마다 나누어 지급할 뜻이 아주 흔쾌히 있었습니다. 첫째, 저는 그의 이익을 위해서만이 아니라 제 이익을 위해서도 입을 다물어야 한다는 것. 둘째, 저는 먼저 그에게 알리고 허락을 얻을 때까지 기다리지 않고서는 웰밍엄을 떠나 움직여서는 안 된다는 것이었습니다. 제 동네에 있으면 정숙한 여자 친구들이 저를 꾀어 다과상 앞에서 위험한 수다를 떨게 할 일이 없을 테고, 제 동네에 있으면 그는 언제든 저를 어디서 찾을지 알 테니까요. 그 두 번째 조건은 가혹한 것이었습니다—그러나 저는 받아들였습니다.\n\n달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까? 저는 아무 의지할 데 없이 버려진 처지였고, 게다가 아이라는 짐이 다가오고 있었습니다. 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까? 저에게 그 추문을 뒤집어씌워 놓고 달아난 그 얼간이 남편의 자비에 몸을 맡길까요? 차라리 죽는 편이 나았습니다. 게다가 그 수당은 실로 후한 것이었습니다. 저를 보기만 하면 흰자위를 뒤집던 그 여자들의 절반보다도 저는 수입이 나았고, 머리 위의 집이 나았고, 바닥에 깔린 양탄자가 나았습니다. 우리 고장에서 미덕의 옷은 무명 날염이었지요. 저는 비단을 입었습니다.\n\n그리하여 저는 그가 내놓은 조건을 받아들였고, 그 조건에서 뽑아낼 수 있는 것은 모두 뽑아냈으며, 점잖은 이웃들과 그들의 땅에서 싸움을 벌였고, 시간이 흐르는 동안 그 싸움에서 이겼습니다—당신 눈으로 직접 보신 그대로입니다. 그때부터 지금까지 흘러간 그 모든 세월 동안 제가 어떻게 그의 비밀(그리고 저의 비밀)을 지켜 왔는지, 그리고 죽은 제 딸 앤이 정말로 제 속내에 파고들어 그 비밀까지 함께 간직하게 되었는지—이런 것들이 아마 당신이 답을 알고 싶어 하는 물음이겠지요. 좋습니다! 저의 감사는 당신에게 아무것도 거절하지 않습니다. 새 장을 넘겨 곧바로 답을 드리지요. 다만 한 가지는 양해해 주셔야겠습니다—하트라이트 씨, 죽은 제 딸에게 당신이 품은 듯한 그 관심에 대해 놀라움을 표하는 것으로 말문을 여는 것을 양해해 주십시오. 저로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 일입니다. 그 관심 때문에 그 애의 어린 시절에 관한 자세한 사정이 궁금하시다면, 저보다 그 문제를 더 잘 아는 클레멘츠 부인에게 물어보시라고 하는 수밖에 없습니다. 그리고 제가 죽은 딸을 조금이라도 끔찍이 아꼈던 척할 생각은 없다는 점을 부디 이해해 주십시오. 그 애는 처음부터 끝까지 제게 근심거리였고, 게다가 늘 머리가 모자란다는 결점까지 있었습니다. 당신은 솔직함을 좋아하시니, 이만하면 만족하시리라 믿습니다.\n\n지난 시절과 관련된 개인적인 사정을 시시콜콜 늘어놓아 당신을 성가시게 할 필요는 없겠지요. 제 쪽에서는 그 거래의 조건을 지켰고, 그 대가로 분기마다 지급되는 넉넉한 수입을 누렸다고만 말해 두면 충분할 것입니다.\n\n이따금 저는 잠시 집을 떠나 다른 곳에서 바람을 쐬고 왔는데, 언제나 제 주인이자 상전에게 먼저 허락을 청했고 대개는 허락을 받아냈습니다. 이미 말씀드렸다시피 그는 저를 지나치게 몰아붙일 만큼 어리석지는 않았고, 그를 위해서는 아니더라도 제 자신을 위해서라도 제가 입을 다물리라고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었습니다. 집을 떠나 있었던 가장 긴 여행 가운데 하나가 리머리지로 가서 죽어 가는 이복 자매를 간호한 일이었습니다. 그 여자가 돈을 모아 두었다는 소문이 있었고, 저는 (혹시라도 무슨 일이 생겨 수당이 끊길 경우를 대비해) 그쪽으로도 제 몫을 챙겨 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했지요. 그러나 일이 돌아간 결과 제 수고는 모두 헛되었고, 저는 아무것도 얻지 못했습니다. 애초에 얻을 것이 없었으니까요.\n\n저는 앤을 데리고 북쪽으로 갔습니다. 제 아이에 대해 이따금 변덕과 애착이 일었고, 그럴 때면 클레멘츠 부인이 그 애에게 미치는 영향력에 샘이 났기 때문입니다. 저는 클레멘츠 부인을 좋아한 적이 없습니다. 가엾고 머리가 텅 비고 기개라고는 없는 여자였지요—당신들 말로 하자면 천생 부림당할 팔자인 사람 말입니다—그래서 저는 이따금 앤을 데려가 그 여자를 애태우는 것도 싫지 않았습니다. 컴벌랜드에서 간병을 하는 동안 제 딸을 달리 어찌할 방도가 없어서, 저는 그 애를 리머리지의 학교에 넣었습니다. 그 고장 지주의 안주인인 페얼리 부인(놀랄 만큼 못생긴 여자였는데도 영국에서 가장 잘생긴 남자 중 하나를 홀려 결혼에 끌어들인 사람이지요)이 제 딸에게 갑작스레 푹 빠져 버려서 저를 몹시 재미나게 했습니다. 그 결과 그 애는 학교에서 아무것도 배우지 못했고, 리머리지 하우스에서 응석받이가 되어 버릇만 나빠졌습니다. 거기서 그 애에게 가르쳐 준 온갖 변덕과 공상 가운데는, 늘 흰옷만 입어야 한다는 터무니없는 생각을 머릿속에 심어 준 것도 있었습니다. 저는 흰색을 싫어하고 색깔 있는 옷을 좋아하는 사람이라, 집에 돌아가는 대로 그 어리석은 생각을 그 애 머리에서 뽑아내야겠다고 마음먹었습니다.\n\n이상하게도 제 딸은 완강하게 저에게 저항했습니다. 한번 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박히면 그 애는 다른 얼빠진 사람들이 다 그렇듯 노새처럼 고집스럽게 그것을 붙들었습니다. 우리는 실컷 다투었고, 클레멘츠 부인은 그 꼴을 보기가 딱했던지 앤을 데려가 런던에서 함께 살겠다고 나섰습니다. 클레멘츠 부인이 흰옷 입는 문제에서 제 딸 편을 들지만 않았다면 저는 그러라고 했을 것입니다. 그러나 그 애에게 흰옷을 입히지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 데다, 저를 거스르고 편을 든 그 여자가 그 어느 때보다 미워졌기에, 저는 안 된다고 말했고, 안 된다는 뜻이었으며, 안 된다는 데서 물러서지 않았습니다. 그 결과 제 딸은 저와 함께 남았고, 그 결과가 다시 낳은 것이 바로 그 비밀을 둘러싼 첫 번째 심각한 다툼이었습니다.\n\n그 일은 방금 제가 적은 시기보다 한참 뒤에 일어났습니다. 저는 새 읍내에 자리를 잡고 여러 해를 지내며 나쁜 평판을 꾸준히 씻어 내고 점잖은 주민들 사이에서 천천히 발판을 넓혀 가고 있었습니다. 딸을 데리고 있는 것이 이 목적에 크게 도움이 되었습니다. 그 애의 무해함과 흰옷을 입으려는 별난 취향이 얼마간의 동정을 불러일으켰거든요. 그래서 저는 그 애가 좋아하는 그 변덕에 반대하기를 그만두었습니다. 그 동정의 얼마쯤은 시간이 지나면 반드시 제 몫으로도 떨어질 것이 분명했기 때문입니다. 실제로 얼마쯤은 떨어졌습니다. 교회에서 세를 놓는 가장 좋은 자리 두 개를 골라 가질 수 있게 된 것도 그때부터였고, 목사님이 처음으로 저에게 인사를 건넨 것도 그 자리를 얻은 뒤부터였습니다.\n\n자, 이렇게 자리를 잡고 지내던 어느 날 아침, 저는 그 지체 높으신 신사(지금은 고인이 되었지요)에게서 편지 한 통을 받았습니다. 약정에 따라, 잠시 바람도 쐬고 경치도 바꿀 겸 읍내를 떠나고 싶다고 미리 알린 제 편지에 대한 답장이었습니다.\n\n제 편지를 받았을 때 그의 불한당 같은 면이 고개를 쳐들었던 모양입니다. 그는 지독히도 무례한 말투로 거절하는 답장을 보내왔고, 저는 그만 자제력을 완전히 잃고 제 딸이 보는 앞에서 그를 두고 “내가 입을 열어 그 비밀을 터뜨리기로 마음만 먹으면 평생을 망가뜨릴 수 있는 천한 사기꾼”이라고 욕했습니다. 그에 대해서는 그 이상 말하지 않았습니다. 그 말이 입에서 튀어나오자마자 제 얼굴을 열심히, 그리고 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보는 딸아이의 얼굴이 눈에 들어와 정신이 번쩍 들었기 때문입니다. 저는 마음을 다시 가라앉힐 때까지 그 애를 즉시 방에서 내보냈습니다.\n\n제 어리석음을 곱씹게 되었을 때의 기분이 유쾌하지 않았다는 것만은 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 그해에 앤은 여느 때보다도 더 미친 듯하고 이상했는데, 만약 캐묻기 좋아하는 사람들이 그 애를 붙들기라도 하면 그 애가 읍내에서 제 말을 되풀이하고 그 말에 그의 이름까지 갖다 붙일 가능성이 있다는 데 생각이 미치자, 저는 그로 인해 벌어질지 모를 결과에 몹시 겁이 났습니다. 제 자신에 대한 최악의 두려움도, 그가 저지를지 모를 일에 대한 최악의 공포도 거기까지밖에 미치지 못했습니다. 바로 그다음 날 실제로 벌어진 일에 대해서는 저는 전혀 대비가 되어 있지 않았습니다.\n\n바로 그다음 날, 온다는 예고 한마디 없이 그가 집으로 찾아왔습니다.\n\n그의 첫마디와 그것을 내뱉는 어조는, 무뚝뚝하기는 했어도, 그가 제 청원에 대한 자신의 무례한 답장을 이미 후회하고 있으며 너무 늦기 전에 일을 다시 바로잡아 보려고 잔뜩 뒤틀린 기분으로 찾아왔다는 것을 저에게 충분히 분명하게 보여 주었습니다. 방 안에 딸이 저와 함께 있는 것을 보자(전날 일이 있은 뒤로 저는 그 애를 제 눈에서 떼어 놓기가 겁이 났습니다) 그는 그 애더러 나가라고 명령했습니다. 그 둘은 서로를 좋아하지 않았고, 그는 저에게는 감히 드러내지 못하는 심술을 그 애에게 쏟아부었습니다.\n\n“자리를 비켜라.” 어깨 너머로 그 애를 보며 그가 말했습니다. 그 애도 제 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 갈 마음이 없다는 듯 버티고 서 있었습니다. “안 들리느냐?” 그가 고함을 질렀습니다. “방에서 나가라니까.” “말씀을 곱게 하세요.” 얼굴이 붉어지며 그 애가 말했습니다. “저 얼간이를 내보내시오.” 제 쪽을 보며 그가 말했습니다. 그 애는 자기 위신에 관해서는 늘 얼빠진 사람다운 고집을 품고 있던 터라, 그 “얼간이”라는 말에 대번에 발끈했습니다. 제가 끼어들 새도 없이 그 애는 대단한 기세로 그에게 다가섰습니다. “당장 사과하세요.” 그 애가 말했습니다. “안 그러면 더 험한 꼴을 보게 해 드리죠. 당신 비밀을 터뜨려 버릴 테니까요. 내가 입을 열기로 마음만 먹으면 당신 평생을 망가뜨릴 수 있어요.” 제가 했던 바로 그 말이었습니다!—전날 제가 한 말을 고스란히 되풀이한 것이었지요—그것도 그가 있는 앞에서, 마치 그 애 자신에게서 나온 말인 양 되풀이한 것입니다. 제가 그 애를 방 밖으로 밀어내는 동안 그는 지금 제가 글을 쓰고 있는 이 종이만큼이나 하얘진 얼굴로 말 한마디 못 하고 앉아 있었습니다. 그러다 정신을 차리고 나서는——\n\n아니요! 저는 그가 정신을 차린 뒤에 무슨 말을 했는지 입에 올리기에는 너무 점잖은 여자입니다. 제 펜은 목사님 회중의 일원이자 “믿음으로 말미암은 의(義)에 관한 수요 강좌”의 후원자인 사람의 펜입니다—그런 펜으로 상스러운 말을 받아쓰기를 어찌 기대하실 수 있겠습니까? 영국에서 가장 천한 불한당이 미쳐 날뛰며 욕설을 퍼붓는 광경을 알아서 상상하시고, 우리는 되도록 빨리 그 모든 일이 어떻게 끝났는지로 함께 나아가기로 하지요.\n\n그 일은, 이쯤이면 당신도 짐작하시겠지만, 그가 자기 안전을 지키겠다며 그 애를 가두어야 한다고 우기는 것으로 끝났습니다.\n\n저는 일을 바로잡아 보려 했습니다. 그 애는 그저 앵무새처럼 제가 하는 말을 듣고 되풀이했을 뿐이며, 제가 아무것도 말해 주지 않았으니 자세한 내막은 조금도 알지 못한다고 그에게 말했습니다. 그 애는 그를 향한 미친 앙심에서 실제로는 알지 못하는 것을 아는 척했을 뿐이고—방금 그가 자기에게 그런 식으로 말한 데 대해 그를 위협하고 약을 올리고 싶었을 뿐이며—운 나쁘게 튀어나온 제 말이 마침 그 애가 찾고 있던 심술 부릴 기회를 주었을 뿐이라고 설명했습니다. 그 애의 다른 별난 행동들과, 얼빠진 사람들의 변덕에 대한 그 자신의 경험까지 들먹였지만—아무 소용이 없었습니다—그는 제가 맹세를 해도 믿지 않았습니다—그는 제가 비밀을 통째로 발설했다고 완전히 확신하고 있었습니다. 요컨대 그는 그 애를 가두는 것 말고는 어떤 말도 들으려 하지 않았습니다.\n\n이런 상황에서 저는 어머니로서의 도리를 다했습니다. “빈민 정신병원은 안 됩니다.” 저는 말했습니다. “그 애를 빈민 정신병원에 넣게 두지는 않겠어요. 굳이 하시겠다면 사립 시설로 하시지요. 저에게도 어머니로서의 심정이 있고 읍내에서 지켜야 할 체면이 있으니, 저희 점잖은 이웃들이 자기네 병든 친척을 위해 고를 법한 그런 사립 시설이 아니면 어떤 것에도 응하지 않겠습니다.” 그것이 제가 한 말이었습니다. 제가 도리를 다했다는 것을 돌이켜 보면 흡족합니다. 죽은 딸을 끔찍이 아낀 적은 없었어도 저는 그 애에 대해 마땅한 자존심을 지니고 있었습니다. 제 단호함과 결단 덕분에 빈민이라는 오점은 제 아이에게 결코 묻지 않았습니다.\n\n제 주장을 관철하고 나자(사립 정신병원이 제공하는 편의 덕분에 그것은 한결 수월했습니다) 저는 그 애를 가둠으로써 얻어지는 이점이 분명히 있다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었습니다. 우선 그 애는 극진한 보살핌을 받았습니다—제가 읍내에 빠짐없이 알린 대로, 숙녀 대접을 받으면서 말입니다. 다음으로 그 애는 웰밍엄에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 되었는데, 그곳에 그대로 있었더라면 제가 경솔하게 내뱉은 말을 되풀이해 사람들이 의심하고 캐묻게 만들었을지도 모를 일이었습니다.\n\n그 애를 가두어 두는 데 따르는 단점은 하나뿐이었고 그나마도 아주 사소했습니다. 우리는 비밀을 안다는 그 애의 헛된 허풍을 고정된 망상으로 바꿔 놓았을 뿐이니까요. 처음에는 자기를 모욕한 남자에게 순전히 미친 앙심으로 내뱉은 말이었지만, 그 애는 자기가 그를 단단히 겁먹게 했다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 교활했고, 나중에는 자기를 가둔 일에 그가 관련되어 있다는 것을 알아낼 만큼은 영리했습니다. 그 결과 그 애는 그를 향한 완전한 광란의 분노로 타올랐고, 그 상태로 정신병원에 들어갔습니다. 간호인들이 그 애를 진정시킨 뒤 그 애가 처음으로 한 말은, 자기는 그의 비밀을 알기 때문에 갇힌 것이며 때가 되면 입을 열어 그를 파멸시킬 작정이라는 것이었습니다.\n\n당신이 생각 없이 그 애의 탈출을 도왔을 때에도 그 애가 당신에게 같은 말을 했을지 모르겠군요. 지난여름에 들은 바로는, 최근에 세상을 떠난 그 상냥한 성품의 이름 없는 신사와 결혼한 불행한 여인에게도 그 애가 분명 그 말을 했다더군요. 당신이든 그 딱한 부인이든 제 딸을 바짝 캐물어 정말로 무슨 뜻인지 설명해 보라고 다그쳤더라면, 그 애가 대번에 그 잘난 척을 다 잃고 멍해지고 안절부절못하고 혼란스러워지는 것을 보셨을 테고—제가 여기에 적는 것이 순전한 사실뿐임을 알아차리셨을 것입니다. 그 애는 비밀이 있다는 것을 알았고—누가 거기에 얽혀 있는지 알았고—그것이 알려지면 누가 곤경에 빠지는지 알았습니다. 그리고 그 너머로는, 스스로 아무리 대단한 척을 했든 낯선 사람들 앞에서 아무리 미친 허풍을 늘어놓았든, 죽는 날까지 그 이상은 알지 못했습니다.\n\n당신의 호기심을 만족시켜 드렸는지요? 어쨌든 그것을 만족시켜 드리려고 어지간히 공을 들이기는 했습니다. 저 자신이나 제 딸에 관해 당신에게 더 들려드릴 이야기는 정말이지 없습니다. 그 애와 관련해 제가 짊어졌던 최악의 책임은 그 애가 정신병원에 안전히 들어가면서 모두 끝났습니다. 그 애가 갇히게 된 정황에 관해 홀콤 양이라는 분이 궁금해하며 물어 왔을 때에는, 답장에 쓸 편지의 문안을 받아서 그대로 옮겨 적었습니다. 그분은 그런 이야기를 옮기는 데 아주 익숙한 어떤 혀에게서 저에 관한 거짓말을 잔뜩 들었을 것이 틀림없습니다. 그리고 그 뒤에는 달아난 딸의 행방을 좇고 그 애가 말썽을 부리지 못하게 하려고 제가 할 수 있는 일을 했는데, 그 애가 목격되었다는 헛소문이 도는 동네에서 직접 사람들에게 묻고 다니기도 했습니다. 그러나 이런 일이나 그와 비슷한 자잘한 일들은, 당신이 이미 들으신 이야기에 비하면 흥미랄 것이 거의 또는 전혀 없겠지요.\n\n여기까지 저는 더할 나위 없이 다정한 마음으로 썼습니다. 그러나 이 편지를 닫기 전에 당신을 향한 진지한 항의와 책망의 말을 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없습니다.\n\n저를 직접 만나 이야기하는 자리에서 당신은 죽은 제 딸의 아비 쪽 혈통을, 마치 그것이 의심스러운 문제라도 되는 양 뻔뻔스럽게 입에 올렸습니다. 그것은 당신으로서 대단히 온당치 못하고 신사답지 못한 짓이었습니다! 우리가 다시 만나게 되거든 부디 기억해 주십시오. 저는 제 평판을 함부로 다루는 일을 결코 용납하지 않을 것이며, 웰밍엄의 도덕적 공기는(제 친구인 목사님이 즐겨 쓰는 표현을 빌리자면) 그 어떤 종류의 방정치 못한 대화로도 더럽혀져서는 안 됩니다. 당신이 스스로 제 남편이 앤의 아버지라는 사실을 의심한다면, 그것은 당신이 저를 가장 저열한 방식으로 모욕하는 것입니다. 이 문제에 대해 부정한 호기심을 느꼈다면, 그리고 지금도 여전히 느끼고 있다면, 저는 당신 자신을 위해서 그것을 당장, 그리고 영원히 접으시라고 권합니다. 하트라이트 씨, 무덤 저편에서는 어찌 될지 몰라도 무덤 이편에서는 그 호기심이 결코 채워지지 않을 것입니다.\n\n방금 제가 한 말을 듣고 나면 아마 당신도 저에게 사과 편지를 써야 할 필요를 느끼시겠지요. 그렇게 하십시오. 그러면 기꺼이 받아들이겠습니다. 그런 다음, 당신이 저와 두 번째로 만나 뵙기를 원하신다면 한 걸음 더 나아가 당신을 맞아들이겠습니다. 제 형편으로는 차 한잔 대접하는 것밖에 청할 수가 없습니다—그렇다고 이번 일로 형편이 조금이라도 나빠진 것은 아닙니다. 말씀드린 것 같습니다만 저는 늘 수입 안에서 넉넉히 살아왔고, 지난 이십 년 동안 남은 평생을 아주 편안히 지낼 만큼은 모아 두었습니다. 웰밍엄을 떠날 생각은 없습니다. 이 읍내에서 아직 얻어야 할 자잘한 이점이 한두 가지 남아 있으니까요. 목사님은 저에게 인사를 합니다—당신도 보셨듯이 말이지요. 그분에게는 부인이 있는데 그 부인은 그만큼 예의 바르지가 않습니다. 저는 도르카스 회에 들어갈 작정이고, 다음에는 그 목사님 부인이 저에게 인사하도록 만들 생각입니다.\n\n당신이 저를 찾아 주는 영광을 베푸신다면, 대화는 전적으로 일반적인 화제로만 이루어져야 한다는 점을 부디 이해해 주십시오. 이 편지를 조금이라도 언급하려는 시도는 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다—저는 이것을 썼다는 사실을 인정하지 않기로 마음먹었으니까요. 증거는 불에 타 없어졌다는 것을 알고 있습니다만, 그래도 조심하는 쪽으로 치우쳐 두는 편이 바람직하다고 생각합니다.\n\n그런 까닭에 여기에는 아무 이름도 적지 않았고, 이 글에는 서명도 붙이지 않았습니다. 글씨체는 처음부터 끝까지 위장했으며, 편지는 제가 직접, 저희 집으로 추적당할 염려가 전혀 없을 만한 정황에서 전달할 작정입니다. 이런 조심 때문에 당신이 불평할 이유는 조금도 없을 것입니다. 그것이 제가 여기에 전하는 정보에는 아무 영향도 주지 않으니까요. 이 정보는 당신이 제게서 받아 마땅한 특별한 호의를 헤아려 전해 드리는 것입니다. 제가 차를 마시는 시간은 다섯 시 반이고, 제 버터 바른 토스트는 누구도 기다려 주지 않습니다.\n\n# I\n\n캐서릭 부인의 그 기이한 수기를 다 읽고 난 뒤 내게 처음 일어난 충동은 그것을 없애 버리자는 것이었다. 처음부터 끝까지 그 글 전체에 굳어 붙어 있는 뻔뻔한 타락, 내게는 아무 책임도 없는 재난에, 그리고 막아 보려고 내가 목숨까지 걸었던 죽음에 나를 끝끝내 결부시키는 그 악랄하게 뒤틀린 심사가 어찌나 역겨웠던지, 나는 그 편지를 찢어 버리기 직전까지 갔다. 그때 한 가지 생각이 떠올라, 없애기 전에 조금만 기다리라고 나를 일깨웠다.\n\n이 생각은 퍼시벌 경과는 전혀 상관이 없었다. 그에 관한 한, 내게 전해진 정보는 이미 내가 도달해 있던 결론을 확인해 준 것 이상은 되지 못했다.\n\n그는 내가 짐작한 그대로 죄를 저질렀다. 그리고 캐서릭 부인이 놀즈베리에 있는 사본 등록부에 대해 한마디도 비치지 않았다는 사실은, 그 책의 존재와 그것이 안고 있는 발각의 위험을 퍼시벌 경이 끝내 몰랐으리라는 내 이전의 확신을 한층 굳혀 주었다. 위조 문제에 대한 내 관심은 이제 끝났고, 그 편지를 간직해 두는 유일한 목적은 아직도 나를 가로막고 선 마지막 수수께끼, 곧 아버지 쪽으로 본 앤 캐서릭의 혈통을 밝히는 데 훗날 얼마간 쓸모가 있게 하려는 것이었다. 그 어머니의 수기에는 한두 문장 흘리듯 떨어뜨려 놓은 대목이 있었는데, 더 급한 일들이 정리되어 사라진 증거를 찾아 나설 여유가 생겼을 때 다시 들춰 보면 요긴할지도 몰랐다. 나는 그 증거를 언젠가 찾아내리라는 희망을 버리지 않았고, 그것을 밝혀내려는 조바심 또한 조금도 잃지 않았다. 이제 페얼리 부인의 무덤에 잠들어 있는 그 가엾은 사람의 아버지를 좇는 일에 대한 내 관심이 조금도 식지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 나는 그 편지를 봉하여 수첩 속에 조심스레 간수해 두고, 때가 오면 다시 꺼내 보기로 했다.\n\n다음 날은 내가 햄프셔에서 보내는 마지막 날이었다. 놀즈베리에서 치안 판사 앞에 다시 출두하고 연기되었던 검시 심문에도 참석하고 나면, 그다음에는 오후나 저녁 기차로 자유로이 런던에 돌아갈 수 있을 터였다.\n\n그날 아침 내가 맨 먼저 볼일을 본 곳은 여느 때처럼 우체국이었다. 매리언의 편지가 와 있었으나, 손에 건네받는 순간 나는 그것이 유난히 가볍다고 느꼈다. 나는 조바심을 내며 봉투를 뜯었다. 안에는 두 번 접은 작은 종잇조각 하나밖에 없었다. 거기에 급히 갈겨써서 얼룩진 몇 줄에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다.\n\n“할 수 있는 대로 빨리 돌아와요. 어쩔 수 없이 거처를 옮겨야 했어요. 풀럼의 가워스 워크 오 번지로 와요. 내가 나와서 당신을 지켜보고 있을게요. 우리 걱정은 하지 말아요, 우리 둘 다 무사하고 잘 있어요. 그래도 어서 돌아와요.—매리언.”\n\n그 몇 줄이 담고 있는 소식은, 나는 그것을 대번에 포스코 백작 쪽의 무슨 배신 기도와 결부시켰거니와, 나를 완전히 압도해 버렸다. 나는 그 종이를 손안에 구겨 쥔 채 숨도 못 쉬고 서 있었다. 무슨 일이 벌어진 것인가? 내가 없는 사이에 백작은 어떤 교묘한 악행을 꾸미고 실행에 옮겼단 말인가? 매리언이 그 쪽지를 쓴 뒤로 하룻밤이 지나 있었다. 내가 그들에게 돌아가기까지는 아직도 몇 시간이 더 흘러야 했다. 내가 모르는 사이에 벌써 또 다른 재앙이 닥쳤을지도 모를 일이었다. 그런데 나는 여기, 그들에게서 몇 마일이고 떨어진 이곳에 머물러 있어야 했다. 법의 처분에 묶인 채로, 그것도 이중으로 묶인 채로!\n\n불안과 경악이 나를 어떤 의무 망각으로까지 꾀어냈을지, 매리언에 대한 내 믿음이 마음을 가라앉혀 주지 않았더라면 나로서도 알 수 없는 일이다. 그녀에 대한 절대적인 신뢰야말로 나를 자제하게 하고 기다릴 용기를 준 이 세상의 유일한 버팀목이었다. 검시 심문은 내 행동의 자유를 가로막는 첫 번째 걸림돌이었다. 나는 정해진 시각에 출석했다. 법의 절차가 내가 그 방에 있을 것을 요구했기 때문인데, 막상 열어 보니 내 증언을 되풀이하라고 나를 부르지는 않았다. 이 쓸데없는 지체는 견디기 힘든 시련이었으나, 나는 심리의 진행을 될 수 있는 대로 꼼꼼히 좇으며 초조함을 달래려고 애썼다.\n\n고인의 런던 사무 변호사(메리먼 씨)도 참석한 사람들 가운데 있었다. 그러나 그는 이 조사의 목적에 도움이 될 만한 것을 조금도 내놓지 못했다. 그는 다만 이루 말할 수 없이 충격을 받고 놀랐다는 것, 그리고 이 사건의 불가사의한 정황에 대해서는 아무런 빛도 던져 줄 수 없다는 것만을 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 연기되었던 심리가 진행되는 동안 그는 이따금 검시관이 대신 물어 줄 질문을 내놓았으나, 어느 것도 아무런 결과로 이어지지 않았다. 거의 세 시간 가까이 이어지며 얻을 수 있는 정보의 원천을 남김없이 다 써 버린 끈기 있는 조사 끝에, 배심은 사고로 인한 급사 사건에 으레 내려지는 평결을 선고했다. 그들은 그 형식적인 결정에 한 가지 진술을 덧붙였다. 열쇠가 어떻게 빼돌려졌는지, 불이 어떻게 났는지, 고인이 무슨 목적으로 성구실에 들어갔는지를 밝혀 줄 증거는 하나도 없었다는 것이었다. 이로써 절차는 끝났다. 죽은 사람의 법정 대리인에게는 매장에 필요한 일들을 처리할 몫이 남겨졌고, 증인들은 물러가도 좋았다.\n\n놀즈베리로 가는 데 단 일 분도 허비하지 않기로 마음먹은 나는 여관에서 셈을 치르고 삯마차를 한 대 세내어 그 읍으로 향했다. 내가 마차를 부르는 소리를 듣고 또 내가 혼자 가는 것을 본 어떤 신사가, 자기는 놀즈베리 근처에 사는 사람인데 마차를 같이 타고 집으로 돌아가도 괜찮겠느냐고 물었다. 나는 두말없이 그 청을 받아들였다.\n\n마차를 타고 가는 동안 우리의 이야기는 자연히 그 고장의 관심을 온통 사로잡고 있는 그 한 가지 화제로 채워졌다.\n\n새로 알게 된 그 사람은 고인이 된 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사와 어느 정도 아는 사이여서, 그와 메리먼 씨는 고인의 재정 형편과 재산 상속 문제를 이야기하던 참이었다. 퍼시벌 경이 진 빚은 주 안에 워낙 널리 알려져 있어서, 그의 사무 변호사로서도 어쩔 수 없는 노릇을 차라리 미덕으로 삼아 그것을 솔직히 인정하는 수밖에 없었다. 그는 유언장을 남기지 않고 죽었고, 설령 유언장을 만들어 두었다 한들 물려줄 개인 재산조차 없었다. 아내에게서 얻은 재산은 통째로 채권자들에게 삼켜져 버렸기 때문이다. 퍼시벌 경에게 자식이 없었으므로 그 영지의 상속인은 펠릭스 글라이드 경의 사촌의 아들, 곧 동인도 무역선의 지휘를 맡고 있는 사관이었다. 그는 뜻하지 않게 굴러든 그 유산이 딱하게도 빚에 잔뜩 짓눌려 있음을 알게 되겠지만, 세월이 지나면 재산은 되살아날 터이고, “그 선장”이 조심스럽게만 처신한다면 죽기 전에 부자가 될 수도 있으리라는 것이었다.\n\n런던으로 가야 한다는 그 한 가지 생각에 온통 사로잡혀 있었으면서도, 이 이야기는 그 나름의 흥미로 내 주의를 끌었다. 뒷날의 일들은 이것이 조금도 틀림없는 사실임을 증명해 주었다. 나는 이 이야기 덕분에 퍼시벌 경의 사기를 밝혀낸 사실을 비밀에 부쳐도 좋다는 명분이 선다고 생각했다. 그가 권리를 가로챘던 그 상속인이 바로 이제 영지를 차지하게 될 상속인이었다. 지난 스물세 해 동안 마땅히 그의 것이었어야 했고 죽은 사람이 마지막 한 푼까지 탕진해 버린 그 수입은 이제 되찾을 길이 없었다. 내가 입을 연다 한들 그 말은 누구에게도 이득을 주지 못할 것이었다. 내가 비밀을 지키면, 내 침묵은 로라를 속여 자기와 결혼하게 만든 사내의 정체를 덮어 주게 된다. 로라를 위하여 나는 그것을 덮어 두고 싶었다. 지금도 로라를 위하여 나는 이 이야기를 지어낸 이름들로 적고 있다.\n\n나는 놀즈베리에서 우연히 만난 동행과 헤어져 곧바로 읍사무소로 갔다. 예상했던 대로 나를 걸어 소송을 이어 갈 사람은 아무도 나와 있지 않았다. 필요한 절차가 지켜졌고, 나는 방면되었다. 법정을 나서는데 도슨 의사가 보낸 편지 한 통이 내 손에 쥐어졌다. 그가 왕진 때문에 자리를 비웠다는 것과, 내게 필요한 도움이 있다면 무엇이든 주겠다고 이미 해 두었던 제안을 다시 한번 되풀이한다는 내용이었다. 나는 그의 호의에 큰 신세를 졌음을 따뜻이 인정하고, 급한 볼일로 곧장 런던으로 불려 가게 되어 직접 찾아뵙고 감사를 표하지 못하는 것을 사과하는 답장을 써 보냈다.\n\n삼십 분 뒤 나는 급행열차를 타고 런던으로 되돌아가고 있었다.\n\n# II\n\n풀럼에 이르러 가워스 워크로 가는 길을 찾아냈을 때는 아홉 시와 열 시 사이였다.\n\n로라와 매리언이 함께 문간까지 나와 나를 맞아들였다. 우리 셋을 묶고 있는 유대가 얼마나 깊은지, 우리는 다시 하나로 모인 그 저녁이 오기 전까지는 미처 알지 못했던 것 같다. 우리는 며칠이 아니라 몇 달을 떨어져 있었던 사람들처럼 만났다. 매리언의 얼굴은 애처로울 만큼 수척하고 근심에 잠겨 있었다. 내가 없는 동안 모든 위험을 혼자 알고 모든 고생을 혼자 짊어진 사람이 누구였는지, 나는 그녀를 보는 순간 알아차렸다. 로라의 밝아진 낯빛과 나아진 기분은, 웰밍엄에서 벌어진 그 끔찍한 죽음과 우리가 거처를 옮긴 진짜 이유를 그녀가 얼마나 세심하게 모른 채 지낼 수 있었는지를 말해 주었다.\n\n이사의 부산함이 오히려 그녀의 기운을 북돋우고 흥미를 돋운 모양이었다. 로라는 그 일을 두고, 답답하고 시끄러운 거리에서 나무와 들판과 강이 있는 쾌적한 동네로 옮겨 와 돌아오는 나를 놀래 주려 한 매리언의 멋진 착상이라고만 말했다. 그녀는 앞날의 계획으로 가득 차 있었다—마저 끝내야 할 그림 이야기, 그 그림을 사 주기로 한 시골의 구매자들 이야기, 자기가 모아 둔 실링과 육펜스 동전 이야기까지. 지갑이 어찌나 묵직해졌던지 그녀는 자랑스럽게 내 손으로 무게를 가늠해 보라고 내밀었다. 내가 없던 며칠 사이에 그녀에게 일어난 이 좋은 변화는 전혀 예상하지 못한 놀라움이었다. 그리고 그것을 보는 형언할 수 없는 행복은 모두 매리언의 용기와 매리언의 사랑 덕분이었다.\n\n로라가 자리를 뜨고 우리 둘이 거리낌 없이 이야기할 수 있게 되었을 때, 나는 가슴에 가득 찬 감사와 경탄을 어떻게든 말로 옮겨 보려 했다. 그러나 그 너그러운 사람은 내 말을 끝까지 들으려 하지 않았다. 그토록 많이 내어 주고 그토록 적게 바라는 여자의 그 숭고한 자기망각이, 그녀의 모든 생각을 자기 자신에게서 나에게로 돌려놓았다.\n\n“우편 시간까지 잠깐밖에 여유가 없었어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그러지 않았다면 그렇게 불쑥 쓰지는 않았을 거예요. 지치고 고단해 보이는군요, 월터. 내 편지가 당신을 몹시 놀라게 한 건 아닌지 걱정이에요.”\n\n“처음에만 그랬어요.” 내가 대답했다. “당신을 믿는 마음으로 이내 진정이 되었지요, 매리언. 이 갑작스러운 거처 이동을 포스코 백작 쪽에서 무슨 위협이 있었던 탓으로 짐작했는데, 맞나요?”\n\n“정확해요.” 그녀가 말했다. “어제 그 사람을 봤어요. 그보다 더한 일도 있지요, 월터—내가 그 사람과 말을 했어요.”\n\n“말을 했다고요? 그자가 우리 사는 곳을 알던가요? 집까지 왔던가요?”\n\n“왔어요. 집까지—하지만 위층으로는 오지 않았어요. 로라는 그 사람을 보지 못했어요—로라는 아무것도 눈치채지 못했고요. 어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄게요. 위험은, 나는 그렇게 믿고 또 그러기를 바라는데, 이제 지나갔어요. 어제 나는 예전 하숙집 거실에 있었어요. 로라는 탁자에서 그림을 그리고 있었고, 나는 이리저리 걸어 다니며 물건을 정리하고 있었지요. 창가를 지나다가 무심코 거리를 내다보았어요. 그런데 길 건너편에 백작이 서 있고, 웬 남자가 그와 이야기를 나누고 있지 뭐예요—”\n\n“그자가 창가의 당신을 알아채던가요?”\n\n“아니요—적어도 못 봤다고 생각해요. 너무 심하게 놀라서 확실하다고는 못 하겠지만요.”\n\n“다른 남자는 누구였어요? 낯선 사람이던가요?”\n\n“낯선 사람이 아니었어요, 월터. 겨우 숨을 돌리고 나서 나는 그 사람을 알아보았어요. 그 정신병원의 원장이었어요.”\n\n“백작이 그 사람에게 집을 가리켜 보이던가요?”\n\n“아니요, 두 사람은 거리에서 우연히 마주친 것처럼 이야기를 나누고 있었어요. 나는 커튼 뒤에서 그들을 지켜보며 창가에 그대로 서 있었어요. 만약 내가 돌아섰더라면, 그리고 로라가 그 순간 내 얼굴을 보았더라면—하느님 감사하게도 로라는 그림에 푹 빠져 있었어요! 두 사람은 곧 헤어졌어요. 병원에서 온 남자는 이쪽으로, 백작은 저쪽으로 갔지요. 나는 두 사람이 우연히 그 거리에 있었을 뿐이라고 생각하기 시작했는데, 그때 백작이 되돌아와 다시 우리 집 맞은편에 멈춰 서더니 명함집과 연필을 꺼내 무언가를 적고는 길을 건너 우리 아래층 가게로 들어가는 것이 보였어요. 나는 로라가 보기 전에 그 앞을 지나 달려 나가며 위층에 뭘 두고 왔다고 말했어요. 방을 나서자마자 나는 첫 번째 층계참까지 내려가 기다렸어요—그 사람이 올라오려 하면 기어이 막을 작정이었지요. 그는 그러려 하지 않았어요. 가게 아가씨가 그의 명함을 들고 문을 통해 복도로 나왔어요—이름 위에 관(冠) 문장이 박힌 커다란 금박 명함이었고, 그 아래에 연필로 이런 글이 적혀 있었어요. ‘친애하는 부인’(그래요! 그 악당은 아직도 나를 그렇게 부를 수 있더군요)—‘친애하는 부인, 우리 두 사람 모두에게 중대한 일로 한마디만 청합니다.’ 위급한 처지에서도 생각이란 걸 할 수 있다면, 그때는 생각이 빨라지는 법이에요. 나는 백작 같은 사람이 얽힌 일에서 나 자신과 당신을 어둠 속에 내버려 두는 것이 치명적인 실수가 될 수 있다는 걸 대번에 느꼈어요. 그를 만나기를 거절한다면, 당신이 없는 사이 그가 무슨 짓을 할지 모른다는 의심이 만나는 쪽보다 열 배는 더 나를 괴롭히리라는 것도 느꼈고요. ‘그분께 가게에서 기다려 달라고 전해 주세요.’ 내가 말했어요. ‘곧 내려가겠다고요.’ 나는 보닛을 가지러 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔어요. 집 안에서는 절대 그와 말을 섞지 않을 작정이었으니까요. 나는 그의 깊고 울리는 목소리를 알고 있었고, 가게에서라도 로라가 그 소리를 들을까 겁이 났어요. 일 분도 안 되어 나는 다시 복도로 내려와 거리로 나가는 문을 열었어요. 그가 가게에서 돌아 나와 나를 맞았어요. 짙은 상복 차림에, 그 매끄러운 목례와 그 소름 끼치는 미소를 띠고 서 있었지요. 그 옆에는 한가한 아이들과 여자들이 몇 모여 그의 거대한 몸집과 훌륭한 검은 옷과 금 손잡이가 달린 큼직한 지팡이를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었어요. 그를 보는 순간 블랙워터에서의 그 끔찍한 시간이 모두 되살아났어요. 그가 과장된 몸짓으로 모자를 벗고, 마치 우리가 바로 어제까지 더없이 다정한 사이로 헤어지기라도 한 듯 내게 말을 걸었을 때, 예전의 그 혐오감이 온몸을 기어 다녔어요.”\n\n“그가 뭐라고 했는지 기억나요?”\n\n“그 말을 옮길 수는 없어요, 월터. 당신에 대해 뭐라고 했는지는 곧 알려 드리겠지만—내게 한 말은 옮길 수가 없어요. 그 편지에 담겼던 정중한 무례보다 더 지독했어요. 내가 남자였다면 그를 후려치고 싶어 손이 근질거릴 지경이었어요! 숄 밑에서 그의 명함을 갈기갈기 찢는 것으로 겨우 손을 가만두었지요. 나는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 (로라가 우리를 볼까 봐) 집에서 멀어지는 쪽으로 걸었고, 그는 내내 부드럽게 항변하며 따라왔어요. 첫 골목에 들어서서 나는 돌아서서 내게 무슨 볼일이냐고 물었어요. 그가 원하는 것은 두 가지였어요. 첫째는, 내가 싫다고 하지 않는다면 자기 심정을 털어놓는 것이었어요. 나는 듣기를 거절했지요. 둘째는 편지에 쓴 경고를 되풀이하는 것이었어요. 나는 그걸 되풀이할 무슨 까닭이 있느냐고 물었어요. 그는 절하고 미소 지으며 설명하겠다고 했어요. 그 설명은 당신이 우리 곁을 떠나기 전에 내가 말했던 우려를 그대로 확인해 주었어요. 기억하겠지만, 나는 당신 문제에 관해서라면 퍼시벌 경이 너무 고집이 세서 친구의 충고를 듣지 않을 것이고, 백작 쪽에서는 자기 이해가 위협받아 스스로 나서게 되기 전까지는 두려워할 위험이 없다고 말했잖아요?”\n\n“기억해요, 매리언.”\n\n“그런데 정말로 그렇게 되었어요. 백작은 충고를 했지만 거절당했지요. 퍼시벌 경은 자기 자신의 난폭함, 자기 자신의 고집, 그리고 당신에 대한 자기 자신의 증오만을 따랐어요. 백작은 그가 하는 대로 내버려 두었어요. 다만 다음 차례로 자기 이해가 위협받을 경우에 대비해 우리가 어디 사는지를 남몰래 알아 두었지요. 월터, 당신이 햄프셔로 처음 다녀온 뒤 이곳으로 돌아올 때 당신은 미행당했어요. 역에서부터 한동안은 변호사가 붙인 사내들이, 그다음에는 집 문간까지 백작이 직접 따라붙었지요. 어떻게 당신 눈에 띄지 않았는지는 말해 주지 않았지만, 아무튼 그는 그때 그런 식으로 우리를 찾아냈어요. 알아내고도 그는 퍼시벌 경의 사망 소식이 들려오기 전까지는 그것을 이용하지 않았어요. 그러다가, 아까 말했듯이, 당신이 다음 차례로 그 죽은 자의 음모 동료를 치리라고 믿었기에 스스로 나선 거예요. 그는 즉시 런던에서 그 정신병원 원장을 만나 달아난 환자가 숨어 있는 곳으로 데려갈 준비를 했어요. 그 결과가 어느 쪽으로 끝나든 당신을 끝없는 법적 분쟁과 곤경에 얽어매어, 적어도 자기에 관한 한 당신이 공격에 나설 손발을 묶어 놓게 되리라 믿었으니까요. 그것이 그의 목적이었어요. 내게 스스로 실토한 바로는요. 마지막 순간에 그를 망설이게 한 단 하나의 고려는—”\n\n“뭐였어요?”\n\n“인정하기 괴롭지만, 월터, 그래도 말해야겠지요. 그 단 하나의 고려가 나였어요. 그 생각을 하면 내가 스스로 얼마나 천하게 여겨지는지 말로 다 할 수 없어요. 하지만 그 쇠 같은 성격에 난 단 하나의 약점이 나에 대한 그 끔찍한 흠모예요. 나는 내 자존심을 위해서라도 될 수 있는 한 오래 그것을 믿지 않으려 애썼어요. 그러나 그의 눈빛과 행동이 그 부끄러운 사실을 기어이 내게 확신시켜요. 나와 말하는 동안 그 사악한 괴물의 두 눈이 젖어들었어요—정말이에요, 월터! 그는 의사에게 그 집을 가리켜 보이려던 순간, 내가 로라와 떨어지면 얼마나 비참할지, 그녀를 도망시킨 일에 대해 책임을 추궁당하면 어찌 될지를 생각했노라고, 그래서 나를 위해 두 번째로 당신이 자기에게 할 수 있는 최악의 일을 무릅쓴 것이라고 했어요. 그가 청한 것이라고는 내가 그 희생을 기억해 주고, 나 자신의 이익을 위해—그가 다시는 돌보아 줄 수 없을지도 모를 그 이익을 위해—당신의 성급함을 눌러 달라는 것뿐이었어요. 나는 그런 거래를 하지 않았어요—차라리 죽었을 거예요. 하지만 그를 믿든 안 믿든, 그가 핑계를 대어 의사를 돌려보냈다는 게 사실이든 거짓이든, 한 가지는 분명해요. 나는 그 남자가 우리 창문 쪽은커녕 우리 쪽 길조차 한 번 쳐다보지 않고 그와 헤어지는 것을 보았어요.”\n\n“나도 믿어요, 매리언. 가장 선한 사람도 선함에 한결같지 못한데, 가장 악한 사람이라고 악함에 한결같으란 법이 있겠어요? 그와 동시에 나는 그가 실제로는 할 수 없는 일을 위협하며 당신을 겁주려 했을 뿐이라고 의심해요. 퍼시벌 경이 죽고 캐서릭 부인도 모든 속박에서 풀려난 지금, 그가 그 정신병원 원장을 이용해 우리를 괴롭힐 힘이 있을지 의심스러워요. 그건 그렇고, 더 들려줘요. 백작이 나에 대해서는 뭐라고 했나요?”\n\n“당신 이야기는 맨 나중에 했어요. 그의 눈이 번쩍이며 굳어졌고, 태도는 내가 예전에 기억하던 그것으로—그를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없게 만드는 그 무자비한 결의와 광대 같은 조롱이 뒤섞인 태도로 바뀌었어요. ‘하트라이트 씨에게 경고하시오!’ 그가 더없이 거만한 투로 말했어요. ‘그는 두뇌를 가진 사람을 상대하고 있소. 나와 겨루려 들 때 사회의 법과 관습 따위는 굵은 손가락 하나로 튕겨 버리는 사람 말이오. 애도하는 내 벗이 내 충고를 들었더라면, 검시 심문이 다루어야 할 것은 하트라이트 씨의 시신이었을 것이오. 그러나 애도하는 내 벗은 고집이 셌소. 보시오! 나는 그의 죽음을 애도하고 있소—속으로는 내 영혼으로, 겉으로는 내 모자로. 이 하찮은 검은 띠가 나타내는 감정을 존중하라고 나는 하트라이트 씨에게 요구하는 바이오. 그가 감히 그것을 건드린다면 그 감정은 가늠할 수 없는 적의로 변할 것이오. 그가 얻은 것으로 만족하게 하시오—그대를 위하여 내가 손대지 않고 그와 그대에게 남겨 두는 것으로 말이오. 그에게 (내 인사와 함께) 이렇게 전하시오. 나를 건드리면 그는 포스코를 상대하게 되리라고. 세간의 말투로 옮기자면, 이렇게 알려 두겠소—포스코는 못 할 짓이 없다고. 친애하는 부인, 안녕히 계시오.’ 그 차가운 잿빛 두 눈이 내 얼굴에 머물렀어요—그는 엄숙하게 모자를 벗고—맨머리로 절하고—떠났어요.”\n\n“다시 돌아오지도 않고요? 마지막 말을 더 하지도 않고요?”\n\n“거리 모퉁이에서 돌아서더니 손을 흔들고는, 그 손을 연극배우처럼 자기 가슴에 탁 얹었어요. 그 뒤로는 그를 보지 못했어요. 그는 우리 집과 반대 방향으로 사라졌고, 나는 로라에게 달려갔지요. 집 안으로 들어서기도 전에 나는 우리가 떠나야 한다고 마음을 굳혔어요. 백작이 그곳을 알아낸 이상, (특히 당신이 없는 동안에는) 그 집은 안전한 곳이 아니라 위험한 곳이었으니까요. 당신이 돌아올 날을 확실히 알 수 있었다면 돌아올 때까지 기다리는 위험을 감수했을 거예요. 하지만 나는 아무것도 확신할 수 없었고, 그래서 곧바로 내 판단대로 움직였어요. 당신은 떠나기 전에 로라의 건강을 위해 더 조용한 동네, 더 맑은 공기를 찾아 옮기자는 이야기를 한 적이 있지요. 나는 로라에게 그 말을 상기시키고, 당신이 없는 동안 이사를 해치워서 당신을 놀래 주고 수고를 덜어 주자고 제안하기만 하면 되었어요. 그러자 로라도 나만큼이나 그 변화를 바라게 되었지요. 로라가 당신 물건 꾸리는 걸 도왔고, 여기 당신의 새 작업실에 그것을 다 정리해 두었답니다.”\n\n“여기로 올 생각은 어떻게 했어요?”\n\n“런던 근교의 다른 동네를 몰라서요. 예전 하숙집에서 되도록 멀리 떨어져야 한다고 느꼈는데, 풀럼은 조금 알고 있었어요. 한때 이곳 학교에 다닌 적이 있거든요. 그 학교가 아직 남아 있을까 하는 요행을 걸고 쪽지를 들려 심부름꾼을 보냈어요. 학교는 남아 있었어요—옛 여선생님의 딸들이 어머니를 대신해 학교를 꾸려 가고 있었고, 내가 보낸 부탁대로 이 집을 얻어 주었지요. 심부름꾼이 집 주소를 가지고 돌아왔을 때가 마침 우편 시간이었어요. 우리는 어두워진 뒤에 옮겼어요—아무 눈에도 띄지 않고 여기로 왔지요. 내가 잘한 건가요, 월터? 나를 믿어 준 당신 마음에 부끄럽지 않게 한 건가요?”\n\n나는 정말로 느낀 그대로, 따뜻하고 고마운 마음을 담아 대답했다. 그러나 내가 말하는 동안에도 근심 어린 표정은 여전히 그녀의 얼굴에 남아 있었고, 내 말이 끝나자 그녀가 맨 처음 던진 물음은 포스코 백작에 관한 것이었다.\n\n나는 그녀가 이제 달라진 마음으로 그를 생각하고 있음을 알았다. 그를 향한 새로운 분노의 폭발도, 셈을 치를 날을 앞당기라는 새로운 호소도 그녀의 입에서 나오지 않았다. 자신을 향한 그 사내의 가증스러운 흠모가 실은 진심이라는 확신은, 헤아릴 수 없는 그의 교활함에 대한 불신과 그의 모든 능력에 깃든 사악한 정력과 경계심에 대한 타고난 두려움을 백 배로 키워 놓은 듯했다. 그의 전언을 어떻게 생각하는지, 그것을 들은 다음 내가 무엇을 할 작정인지 물을 때 그녀의 목소리는 낮게 잠겼고, 태도는 머뭇거렸으며, 두 눈은 애타는 두려움을 담고 내 눈을 파고들었다.\n\n“카일 씨를 만난 지 몇 주 되지 않았어요, 매리언.” 내가 대답했다. “그와 헤어질 때 로라에 대해 내가 마지막으로 한 말은 이랬어요. ‘그 가짜 장례 행렬을 따라 무덤까지 갔던 모든 사람이 보는 앞에서 그녀의 삼촌 집 문이 열려 그녀를 맞아들일 것이오. 그녀가 죽었다고 기록한 그 거짓말은 가문 어른의 권위로 묘비에서 공개적으로 지워질 것이오. 그리고 그녀를 짓밟은 두 사내는, 법정에 앉은 정의가 그들을 쫓을 힘이 없다 해도, 나에게 그 죄를 치를 것이오.’ 그 두 사람 가운데 하나는 이제 인간의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 갔어요. 다른 하나는 남아 있고, 내 결심도 남아 있어요.”\n\n그녀의 두 눈이 빛났다—뺨에 혈색이 올랐다. 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 나는 그녀의 온 마음이 내 편으로 모여드는 것을 그 얼굴에서 다 보았다.\n\n“나 자신에게도, 당신에게도 감추지 않겠어요.” 나는 말을 이었다. “우리 앞에 놓인 전망은 의심스럽다는 말로도 부족해요. 우리가 이미 무릅쓴 위험은 앞으로 우리를 위협할 위험에 비하면 어쩌면 사소한 것일지도 몰라요. 그래도 매리언, 이 모험은 기어이 해 볼 겁니다. 나는 백작 같은 사람을 상대로 충분히 준비되기도 전에 덤벼들 만큼 무모하지는 않아요. 나는 참는 법을 배웠어요—내 때를 기다릴 수 있어요. 그의 전언이 효과를 냈다고 믿게 둡시다—우리에 대해 아무것도 알지 못하고 아무 소식도 듣지 못하게 둡시다—마음 놓을 시간을 넉넉히 줍시다. 내가 그를 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 스스로 뽐내기 좋아하는 그 천성이 그 결과를 앞당겨 줄 거예요. 이것이 기다리는 한 가지 이유인데, 그보다 더 중요한 이유가 또 하나 있어요. 매리언, 마지막 승부를 걸기 전에 당신과 로라에 대한 내 위치가 지금보다 더 든든해져야 해요.”\n\n그녀는 놀란 얼굴로 내 쪽에 몸을 기울였다.\n\n“어떻게 더 든든해질 수 있다는 거죠?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“때가 오면 말해 줄게요.” 내가 대답했다. “아직 그때가 오지 않았어요—어쩌면 영영 오지 않을지도 몰라요. 나는 로라에게 그 일을 영원히 말하지 못할 수도 있어요—지금은 당신에게조차 침묵해야 해요. 해를 끼치지 않고 떳떳하게 말할 수 있다고 스스로 확인하기 전까지는요. 그 이야기는 그만둡시다. 더 급히 우리 마음을 써야 할 일이 또 있어요. 당신은 로라에게, 자비롭게도 로라에게 남편의 죽음을 알리지 않고 지켜 왔지요—”\n\n“아, 월터, 그 이야기를 그 애에게 하기까지는 아직 한참 있어야 하지 않겠어요?”\n\n“아니에요, 매리언. 누구도 막을 수 없는 우연이 언젠가 그 사실을 알려 주게 되느니, 지금 당신이 알려 주는 편이 나아요. 자세한 이야기는 다 덜어 내고—아주 부드럽게 말을 꺼내되, 그가 죽었다는 것만은 알려 줘요.”\n\n“월터, 방금 말한 이유 말고도 그 애가 남편의 죽음을 알기 바라는 다른 이유가 있는 거죠?”\n\n“있어요.”\n\n“아직 우리 사이에서 말해서는 안 되는 그 이야기와—어쩌면 로라에게는 영영 말하지 못할지도 모르는 그 이야기와 관련된 이유인가요?”\n\n그녀는 마지막 말에 뜻을 담아 힘주어 머물렀다. 내가 그렇다고 대답할 때, 나 역시 그 말에 힘주어 머물렀다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 창백해졌다. 한동안 그녀는 슬프고 머뭇거리는 관심을 담아 나를 바라보았다. 우리의 모든 기쁨과 슬픔을 함께한 그 사랑스러운 동무가 앉아 있던 빈 의자를 곁눈으로 흘긋 보았을 때, 낯선 다정함이 그녀의 검은 눈에 떨렸고 굳게 다문 입술을 부드럽게 풀어 놓았다.\n\n“알 것 같아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그 애를 위해서도, 당신을 위해서도, 내가 그 애에게 남편의 죽음을 알리는 것이 도리라는 생각이 들어요, 월터.”\n\n그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 잠시 내 손을 꼭 잡았다—그러더니 불쑥 그 손을 놓고 방을 나갔다. 이튿날 로라는 그의 죽음이 자신을 풀어 주었다는 것을, 그리고 자기 삶의 그 잘못과 재난이 그의 무덤에 함께 묻혔다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그 뒤로 우리 사이에서 그의 이름이 오르내리는 일은 없었다. 그날 이후 우리는 그의 죽음이라는 화제에 조금이라도 다가가는 것을 피했고, 꼭 그만큼 조심스럽게 매리언과 나는 그녀와 나의 합의로 아직은 우리 사이에서 입에 올리지 않기로 한 그 다른 이야기에 대해서도 더는 언급하지 않았다. 그렇다고 그것이 우리 마음에서 사라진 것은 아니었다—오히려 우리가 스스로에게 지운 그 절제 때문에 마음속에서 더욱 생생하게 살아 있었다. 우리 둘은 그 어느 때보다 애타는 마음으로 로라를 지켜보았다. 어떤 때는 기다리며 바라고, 어떤 때는 기다리며 두려워하면서, 그 때가 올 때까지.\n\n우리는 차츰 예전의 생활로 돌아갔다. 나는 햄프셔에 가 있는 동안 중단되었던 매일의 일을 다시 시작했다. 새 하숙은 우리가 떠나온 더 작고 불편한 방들보다 비용이 더 들었고, 그만큼 내가 더 힘써 벌어야 한다는 요구는 앞날이 불투명하다는 사실 때문에 한층 무거워졌다. 은행에 넣어 둔 얼마 안 되는 돈을 바닥낼 위급한 일이 아직 닥칠 수도 있었고, 그렇게 되면 결국 우리를 먹여 살릴 것은 내 두 손의 일뿐일 터였다. 지금까지 내게 주어진 것보다 더 안정적이고 더 벌이가 나은 일자리는 우리 처지에 반드시 필요한 것이었다—나는 이제 그것을 마련하려고 부지런히 나섰다.\n\n내가 지금 쓰고 있는 이 휴식과 은둔의 기간 동안, 이 기록에 담긴 내 모든 생각과 행동이 매여 있는 그 하나의 절실한 목적을 내가 완전히 접어 두었으리라고 여겨서는 안 된다. 그 목적은 앞으로도 여러 달 동안 나를 놓아주지 않을 것이었다. 그것이 더디게 익어 가는 동안에도 내게는 취해야 할 예방 조치 하나와, 이행해야 할 감사의 의무 하나와, 풀어야 할 의심스러운 물음 하나가 남아 있었다.\n\n예방 조치는 당연히 백작과 관련된 것이었다. 그의 계획이 그를 영국에 머무르게 하는지—달리 말해 내 손이 닿는 범위 안에 머무르게 하는지를 될 수 있으면 확인해 두는 일이 무엇보다 중요했다. 나는 아주 간단한 방법으로 이 의심을 가라앉혔다. 세인트존스우드에 있는 그의 주소를 알고 있었으므로 나는 그 동네에서 수소문하여 그가 사는 가구 딸린 집을 관리하는 중개인을 찾아냈고, 포리스트 로드 5번지가 머지않아 세를 놓게 되겠느냐고 물었다. 대답은 아니라는 것이었다. 지금 그 집에 사는 외국인 신사가 앞으로 여섯 달 더 임차 기간을 갱신했으며 이듬해 유월 말까지 그 집을 쓸 것이라는 이야기였다. 그때는 아직 십이월 초순에 지나지 않았다. 나는 백작이 내게서 달아날지 모른다는 당장의 걱정을 덜고 중개인과 헤어졌다.\n\n내가 이행해야 할 의무는 나를 다시 한번 클레멘츠 부인 앞으로 데려갔다. 나는 다시 찾아가겠다고, 그리고 처음 만났을 때는 어쩔 수 없이 감추어야 했던 앤 캐서릭의 죽음과 매장에 관한 자세한 사정을 털어놓겠다고 약속했었다. 사정이 달라진 지금은 그 음모의 내막 가운데 이야기해야 할 만큼을 그 선량한 부인에게 믿고 알리는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었다. 연민과 우정이 일깨울 수 있는 온갖 이유가 그 약속을 어서 지키라고 나를 재촉했고, 나는 양심적으로 또 정성껏 그것을 지켰다. 그 자리에서 오간 이야기를 이 기록에 굳이 담을 필요는 없다. 그보다는 그 만남 자체가 아직 풀리지 않고 남아 있던 그 의심스러운 물음—앤 캐서릭의 아버지 쪽 혈통에 관한 물음—을 자연히 내 머리에 떠올렸다는 사실을 말해 두는 편이 나을 것이다.\n\n이 문제와 얽힌 자잘한 정황들이—하나하나는 사소하지만 한데 모아 놓으면 놀랄 만큼 중요한 것들이—근래 들어 나를 어떤 결론으로 이끌었고, 나는 그것을 확인해 보기로 마음먹었다. 나는 매리언의 허락을 얻어 바넥 홀의 돈손 소령에게—캐서릭 부인이 결혼하기 전 몇 해 동안 하인으로 일했던 곳이다—몇 가지를 묻는 편지를 썼다. 나는 매리언의 이름으로 물었고, 그 문의가 그녀 집안의 내력에 관한 일이라고 밝혀 내가 편지를 보내는 까닭을 설명하고 양해를 구했다. 편지를 쓸 때 나는 돈손 소령이 아직 살아 있는지조차 확실히 알지 못했다—그가 살아 있고, 답할 수 있고 또 답해 줄 마음이 있기를 요행에 걸고 부친 것이었다.\n\n이틀이 지나자 소령이 살아 있으며 우리를 기꺼이 도우려 한다는 증거가 편지의 형태로 도착했다.\n\n내가 그에게 편지를 쓸 때 품고 있던 생각과 내가 물은 것들의 성격은 그의 답장에서 쉽게 미루어 알 수 있을 것이다. 그의 편지는 다음과 같은 중요한 사실들을 알려 줌으로써 내 물음에 답해 주었다—\n\n첫째로, “블랙워터 파크의 고(故) 퍼시벌 글라이드 경”은 바넥 홀에 한 번도 발을 들인 적이 없었다. 세상을 떠난 그 신사는 돈손 소령에게도, 그의 온 집안에게도 전혀 낯선 사람이었다.\n\n둘째로, “리머리지 하우스의 고(故) 필립 페얼리 씨”는 젊은 시절 돈손 소령의 절친한 벗이자 늘 찾아오는 손님이었다. 소령은 옛 편지와 여러 서류를 들춰 기억을 되살린 끝에, 필립 페얼리 씨가 1826년 팔월에 바넥 홀에 머물렀고, 이어 구월과 시월 얼마간을 사냥철 동안 그곳에서 지냈다고 단언할 수 있었다. 그 뒤 그는 소령이 알기로는 스코틀랜드로 떠났고, 한동안 세월이 흐른 뒤에야 바넥 홀에 다시 나타났는데, 그때는 갓 결혼한 사람의 신분이었다.\n\n이 진술만 따로 놓고 보면 아마 확실한 값어치는 별로 없었을 것이다. 그러나 매리언이나 내가 사실로 알고 있는 몇 가지 정황과 이어 놓고 보면, 그것은 우리 마음에 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 하나의 명백한 결론을 가리켰다.\n\n이제 필립 페얼리 씨가 1826년 가을에 바넥 홀에 있었다는 것과 캐서릭 부인이 같은 시기에 그곳에서 하인으로 살고 있었다는 것을 알게 된 우리는, 그 밖에도 이런 것들을 알고 있었다. 첫째, 앤이 1827년 유월에 태어났다는 것. 둘째, 앤이 언제나 로라와 놀랄 만큼 닮은 모습이었다는 것. 셋째, 로라 자신이 아버지를 몹시 닮았다는 것. 필립 페얼리 씨는 당대에 미남으로 이름난 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 성격은 동생 프레더릭과 전혀 달라서, 사교계의, 특히 여자들의 응석받이였다—태평하고 쾌활하고 충동적이고 다정한 사람, 지나칠 만큼 너그러운 사람, 천성적으로 품행이 헤펐고 여자 문제에서는 도덕적 의무를 대수롭지 않게 여기기로 소문난 사람이었다. 우리가 아는 사실이 이러했고, 그 사람의 됨됨이가 이러했다. 여기서 따라 나오는 뻔한 추론을 굳이 짚어 줄 필요가 있겠는가?\n\n이제 내게 밝아 온 새로운 빛으로 읽으니, 캐서릭 부인의 편지조차 본인의 뜻과는 달리 내가 다다른 결론을 뒷받침하는 데 제 몫의 도움을 보태 주었다. 그녀는 (내게 보낸 글에서) 페얼리 부인을 “인물이 변변찮은” 여자로, 그리고 “영국에서 가장 잘생긴 남자를 옭아매어 결혼한” 여자로 그렸다. 두 가지 주장 모두 굳이 할 필요가 없는 말이었고, 두 가지 모두 거짓이었다. 아무 언급도 할 까닭이 없는 자리에서 페얼리 부인을 그토록 유별나게 무례하게 언급한 데에는, 질투 섞인 미움—캐서릭 부인 같은 여자에게서라면 아예 드러나지 않느니 좀스러운 악의로 드러날 그런 미움—말고는 달리 짚을 만한 원인이 없어 보였다.\n\n여기서 페얼리 부인의 이름이 나온 김에 또 하나의 물음이 자연스레 떠오른다. 그녀는 리머리지로 데려와진 그 어린 소녀가 누구의 아이일지 한 번이라도 의심한 적이 있었을까?\n\n이 점에 대해서는 매리언의 증언이 분명했다. 예전에 내게 읽어 주었던, 페얼리 부인이 남편에게 보낸 편지—앤이 로라와 닮았다고 쓰고 그 낯선 어린아이에게 애정 어린 관심을 품었노라 털어놓은 그 편지—는 의심할 나위 없이 조금도 티 없는 마음으로 쓰인 것이었다. 곰곰 생각해 보면 필립 페얼리 씨 자신이 그 아내보다 진실에 조금이라도 더 가까이 다가가 의심한 적이 있었는지조차 미덥지 않았다. 캐서릭 부인이 결혼하게 된 그 낯 뜨겁도록 기만적인 정황과 그 결혼으로 감추려 한 목적을 생각하면, 설령 그가 없는 동안 뱃속 아이의 아비에게 연락할 방도가 그녀에게 있었다 해도, 조심하느라, 어쩌면 제 자존심 때문에도 그녀는 얼마든지 입을 다물고 있었을 만했다.\n\n이런 추측이 머릿속을 스칠 때, 우리 모두가 살아오며 한 번쯤 놀라움과 두려움으로 떠올려 보았을 성경의 그 준엄한 말씀이 내 기억에 떠올랐다. “아비의 죄가 자식에게 미치리라.” 한 아버지의 두 딸이 그토록 치명적으로 닮지만 않았더라면, 앤이 죄 없는 도구가 되고 로라가 죄 없는 희생자가 된 그 음모는 애초에 꾸며질 수도 없었을 것이다. 아버지가 저지른 경솔한 잘못에서 그 자식에게 가해진 무정한 상해에 이르기까지, 그 기나긴 정황의 사슬은 얼마나 어김없고 얼마나 무섭도록 곧바르게 이어져 내려왔던가!\n\n이런 생각들이, 그리고 그와 함께 다른 생각들이 내게 찾아와, 내 마음을 앤 캐서릭이 이제 묻혀 있는 컴벌랜드의 그 작은 교회 묘지로 데려갔다. 나는 페얼리 부인의 무덤가에서 그녀를 만났던 지난날을, 그리고 마지막으로 그녀를 만났던 때를 떠올렸다. 나는 묘비를 두드리던 그 가엾고 무력한 두 손과, 자신을 지켜 준 보호자이자 벗의 유해를 향해 중얼거리던 그 지치고 애타는 말을 떠올렸다. “아, 내가 죽어서 당신과 함께 감추어져 쉴 수 있다면!” 그녀가 그 소원을 내뱉은 지 겨우 일 년 남짓 지났을 뿐인데, 그것이 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이, 얼마나 무섭게 이루어졌는가! 호숫가에서 로라에게 했던 그 말이, 바로 그 말이 이제 그대로 이루어진 것이다. “아, 당신 어머니 곁에 묻힐 수만 있다면! 천사의 나팔이 울리고 무덤들이 죽은 자를 내어놓는 부활의 날에, 그분 곁에서 깨어날 수만 있다면!” 인간의 어떤 죄와 참혹을 지나, 죽음으로 내려가는 길의 어떤 캄캄한 굽이를 지나—그 길 잃은 사람은 하느님의 인도를 받아, 살아서는 결코 다다르기를 바랄 수 없었던 마지막 집에 이르도록 헤매었던가! 그 거룩한 안식 속에 나는 그녀를 남겨 둔다—그 두려운 동행 속에서 그녀가 방해받지 않고 머물기를.\n\n그리하여 내 삶에 붙어 다녔듯 이 기록에도 붙어 다니던 그 유령 같은 모습은 뚫을 수 없는 어둠 속으로 내려간다. 그림자처럼 그녀는 밤의 외로움 속에서 처음 내게 왔다. 그림자처럼 그녀는 죽은 자의 외로움 속으로 사라진다.\n\n# III\n\n넉 달이 흘렀다. 4월이 왔다—봄의 달이자, 변화의 달이었다.\n\n겨울이 지난 뒤의 그 기간 동안, 시간은 우리의 새 보금자리에서 평온하고 행복하게 흘러갔다. 나는 오래 남아돌던 여가를 잘 활용하여 일거리의 원천을 크게 늘렸고, 우리의 생계 기반을 한결 든든한 자리에 올려놓았다. 그토록 모질게 그녀를 시험하며 그토록 오래 그녀 위에 드리워 있던 불안과 초조에서 벗어나자, 매리언의 기운은 되살아났고, 타고난 기개도 다시 제 모습을 드러내기 시작했다. 예전만큼은 아닐지언정, 그 자유로움과 활력의 일부가 돌아온 것이다.\n\n언니보다 변화에 더 유연했던 로라는 새로운 삶이 베푸는 치유의 힘이 이루어낸 진전을 한층 뚜렷하게 보여주었다. 그 얼굴을 나이보다 늙어 보이게 만들었던 초췌하고 야윈 기색은 빠르게 걷혀 갔고, 지난날 그 얼굴의 첫째가는 매력이었던 그 표정이 이제 되돌아오는 아름다움의 첫째가 되었다. 아무리 가까이서 지켜보아도, 한때 그녀의 이성과 목숨을 위협했던 그 음모가 남긴 심각한 자취는 단 하나뿐이었다. 블랙워터 파크를 떠나던 때부터 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 우리가 다시 만나던 때까지의 기억은 되찾을 가망 없이 사라져 버렸다. 그 시기를 조금이라도 언급하면 그녀는 여전히 낯빛이 변하고 몸을 떨었으며, 말이 뒤엉키고, 기억은 예전처럼 속절없이 헤매다 길을 잃었다. 여기, 오직 여기에만 과거의 흔적이 깊이 박혀 있었다—지워내기에는 너무도 깊이.\n\n그 밖의 모든 면에서 그녀는 회복의 길을 아주 멀리까지 걸어와서, 가장 좋고 밝은 날이면 이따금 옛날의 로라처럼 보이고 옛날의 로라처럼 말했다. 이 행복한 변화는 우리 두 사람 안에서 자연스러운 결과를 낳았다. 그녀 쪽에서도 내 쪽에서도, 컴벌랜드에서 보낸 지난날의 그 스러지지 않는 기억들이 긴 잠에서 깨어났다. 그것들은 하나같이 모두 우리 사랑의 기억이었다.\n\n서서히, 그리고 알지 못하는 사이에, 서로를 대하는 우리의 일상은 조심스러워졌다. 그녀가 슬픔과 고통에 잠겨 있던 시절 그토록 자연스럽게 건네던 다정한 말들이 이상하게도 내 입술에서 머뭇거렸다. 그녀를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움이 내 마음을 가장 크게 짓누르던 때에는, 밤에 그녀가 나를 두고 물러갈 때도 아침에 나를 맞이할 때도 나는 늘 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 그런데 이제 그 입맞춤은 우리 사이에서 툭 떨어져 버린 듯했다—우리 삶에서 사라져 버린 듯했다. 손이 닿으면 우리의 손은 다시 떨리기 시작했다. 매리언이 없는 자리에서는 서로를 오래 바라보는 일이 좀처럼 없었다. 단둘이 있으면 대화가 자주 끊겼다. 뜻하지 않게 그녀에게 손이 닿으면 심장이 빠르게 뛰는 것이 느껴졌다. 리머리지 하우스에서 그러했듯이. 그러면 그 사랑스러운 홍조가 화답하듯 그녀의 뺨에 다시 피어오르는 것이 보였다. 마치 우리가 컴벌랜드의 언덕으로 돌아가 다시 선생과 제자라는 옛 자리에 서 있기라도 한 것처럼. 그녀는 오래도록 말없이 생각에 잠기곤 했고, 매리언이 무슨 생각을 하느냐고 물으면 아무 생각도 하지 않았다고 부인했다. 어느 날 나는 일을 내팽개친 채, 우리가 처음 만난 여름 별채에서 그려두었던 그녀의 작은 수채 초상화를 바라보며 몽상에 잠긴 나 자신에 스스로 놀랐다. 지나간 시절 그 초상이 갓 완성되었을 때 페얼리 씨의 그림들을 내팽개치고 같은 초상을 바라보며 몽상에 잠기던 것과 꼭 같았다. 모든 정황이 이토록 달라졌는데도, 우리가 처음으로 함께하던 그 황금 같은 날들의 서로에 대한 자리가 우리 사랑의 되살아남과 더불어 되살아나는 듯했다. 마치 시간이 우리를 젊은 날 희망의 난파선에 실어 옛적 낯익은 물가로 도로 떠밀어다 놓은 것만 같았다!\n\n다른 여자에게라면 나는 그 결정적인 말을 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그런데 그녀에게만은 여전히 그 말을 하기가 망설여졌다. 그녀가 처한 처지의 그 지독한 무력함—내가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 너그러운 다정함에 오로지 기대어 있는, 달리 의지할 데 없는 그 신세—사내인 내 직감으로는 미처 알아차릴 만큼 섬세하지 못했을지도 모를 어떤 은밀한 예민함을 너무 이르게 건드리지나 않을까 하는 두려움—이런 생각들, 그리고 그와 비슷한 다른 생각들이 나를 스스로 미덥지 못하게 만들어 입을 다물게 했다. 그러면서도 나는 알고 있었다. 양쪽 모두의 이 조심스러움은 끝나야 한다는 것을, 우리가 서로에 대해 서 있는 자리는 앞날을 위해 어떤 확실한 방식으로 바뀌어야 한다는 것을, 그리고 그 변화의 필요를 먼저 인정해야 할 사람은 다름 아닌 나라는 것을.\n\n우리의 처지를 생각하면 할수록, 겨울 이래로 우리 세 사람이 함께 지내온 살림의 조건이 그대로 남아 있는 한 그것을 바꿔보려는 시도는 더 어렵게만 보였다. 이런 마음이 어디서 비롯된 것인지, 그 변덕스러운 심사를 나로서는 설명할 길이 없다. 그러나 어쨌든 한 가지 생각이 나를 사로잡았다. 먼저 장소와 정황을 좀 바꾸어보는 것, 우리 삶의 조용한 단조로움에 갑작스러운 틈을 하나 내는 것, 그것도 우리가 서로를 늘 보아온 그 집안의 얼굴이 달라지도록 잘 꾸며서 그리한다면, 내가 말을 꺼낼 길이 닦일 수도 있고 로라와 매리언이 그 말을 듣기도 한결 쉽고 덜 거북해질 수 있으리라는 생각이었다.\n\n그런 뜻을 품고서 나는 어느 날 아침, 우리 모두 짧은 휴가와 자리를 바꿔볼 자격은 벌어놓은 것 같다고 말했다. 얼마쯤 의논한 끝에 우리는 보름 동안 바닷가로 가기로 정했다.\n\n이튿날 우리는 풀럼을 떠나 남쪽 해안의 조용한 소읍으로 갔다. 한 해의 그 이른 철에 그곳을 찾은 사람은 우리뿐이었다. 벼랑도, 모래밭도, 내륙으로 난 산책길도 모두 우리에게 더없이 반가운 그 호젓한 상태였다. 공기는 부드러웠다—언덕과 숲과 구릉 너머로 펼쳐진 풍경은 4월의 빛과 그늘이 옮겨 다니는 데 따라 아름답게 변화했고, 쉼 없는 바다는 우리 창 아래에서 뛰놀았다. 마치 뭍과 마찬가지로 봄의 열기와 싱그러움을 느끼기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n로라에게 말하기 전에 매리언과 먼저 의논하고, 그런 다음 그녀의 조언을 따르는 것이 마땅히 내가 매리언에게 해야 할 도리였다.\n\n도착한 지 사흘째 되던 날, 나는 그녀와 단둘이 이야기할 알맞은 기회를 얻었다. 우리가 서로를 바라본 그 순간, 그녀의 빠른 직감은 내가 입 밖에 내기도 전에 내 마음속 생각을 알아차렸다. 늘 그렇듯 기운차고 단도직입적으로 그녀는 곧바로, 그리고 먼저 말했다.\n\n“햄프셔에서 돌아온 날 저녁에 우리 사이에 나왔던 그 이야기를 생각하고 있는 거죠.” 그녀가 말했다. “한동안 당신이 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 기다리고 있었어요. 우리 이 작은 살림에는 변화가 있어야 해요, 월터. 지금 이대로는 그리 오래 갈 수 없어요. 당신 눈에 보이는 만큼 내 눈에도 훤히 보여요—로라는 아무 말도 하지 않지만, 그 애 눈에 보이는 만큼이나요. 컴벌랜드의 그 옛날이 어쩌면 이렇게 고스란히 돌아온 것 같을까요! 당신과 나는 다시 함께 있고, 우리 사이에 걸린 단 하나의 관심사는 또다시 로라예요. 이 방이 리머리지의 여름 별채이고, 저 너머 저 물결이 우리 바닷가에 부딪는 파도라고 해도 곧이들을 것만 같아요.”\n\n“지나간 그 시절에도 나는 당신의 조언을 따랐지요.” 내가 말했다. “그리고 이제, 매리언, 그때보다 열 배는 더 큰 믿음으로 다시 그 조언을 따르겠습니다.”\n\n그녀는 내 손을 꼭 쥐는 것으로 대답했다. 지난날에 대한 내 언급에 그녀가 깊이 마음이 움직였음을 나는 알아보았다. 우리는 창가에 나란히 앉았고, 내가 말하고 그녀가 듣는 동안, 우리는 장엄한 바다 위에 쏟아지는 눈부신 햇빛을 바라보았다.\n\n“우리 사이의 이 흉금 없는 이야기에서 무엇이 나오든,” 내가 말했다. “그것이 나에게 행복하게 끝나든 슬프게 끝나든, 로라의 이익은 앞으로도 내 삶의 이익일 겁니다. 우리가 이곳을 떠날 때, 어떤 사정으로 떠나게 되든, 그자의 공범에게서 얻어내지 못한 그 자백을 포스코 백작에게서 기어이 빼앗아내겠다는 내 결심은 나와 함께 런던으로 돌아갈 겁니다. 내가 돌아가는 것만큼이나 확실하게요. 그자를 궁지에 몰아넣었을 때 그자가 나에게 어떻게 덤벼들지는 당신도 나도 알 수 없습니다. 다만 그자가 제 입으로 한 말과 제 손으로 한 짓으로 미루어, 그자가 잠시도 망설이지 않고 한 조각 뉘우침도 없이 로라를 통해 나를 칠 수 있는 인간이라는 것만은 압니다. 지금 우리 처지로는, 그자에게 맞서고 그녀를 지키는 데 나를 굳게 받쳐줄, 세상이 인정하고 법이 허락하는 그런 권리가 나에게는 그녀에 대해 하나도 없습니다. 이것이 나를 심각하게 불리한 자리에 세웁니다. 로라가 안전하다는 확신 위에 굳건히 서서 백작과 우리의 싸움을 치러야 한다면, 나는 그 싸움을 내 아내를 위해 치러야 합니다. 여기까지, 매리언, 동의하십니까?”\n\n“한 마디 한 마디 다 동의해요.” 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“내 마음속을 근거로 호소하지는 않겠습니다.” 나는 말을 이었다. “온갖 변화와 온갖 충격을 다 견디고 살아남은 그 사랑에 기대지도 않겠습니다. 그녀를 마음에 두고, 그녀를 내 아내라 일컫는 나 자신에 대한 유일한 변명을 나는 방금 말한 그것 위에만 두겠습니다. 백작에게서 자백을 강제로 얻어낼 그 기회가, 내가 믿는 대로, 로라가 살아 있다는 사실을 세상 앞에 세울 마지막 기회라면, 우리의 혼인을 위해 내가 내세울 수 있는 가장 이기적이지 않은 이유는 우리 두 사람이 함께 인정하는 것이 됩니다. 그러나 내 확신이 틀렸을 수도 있습니다—우리 목적을 이룰 다른 수단, 덜 불확실하고 덜 위험한 수단이 우리 손안에 있을지도 모릅니다. 나는 내 머릿속에서 애타게 그런 수단을 찾아보았지만, 찾지 못했습니다. 당신은 찾았습니까?”\n\n“아니요. 나도 그 생각을 해봤지만, 헛일이었어요.”\n\n“십중팔구,” 내가 이어 말했다. “이 어려운 문제를 생각하면서 나에게 떠올랐던 것과 똑같은 물음들이 당신에게도 떠올랐을 겁니다. 그녀가 다시 제 모습을 되찾은 지금, 그녀와 함께 리머리지로 돌아가서 마을 사람들이나 학교 아이들이 그녀를 알아보아 주기를 믿어보아야 할까요? 그녀의 필적이라는 실질적인 증거에 호소해야 할까요? 그렇게 했다고 칩시다. 사람들이 그녀를 알아보았고 필적이 같다는 것도 입증되었다고 칩시다. 그 두 가지 모두 성공한들, 법정에서 재판을 벌일 훌륭한 토대를 마련하는 것 이상의 무엇이 되겠습니까? 그 알아봄과 그 필적이, 그녀 고모의 증언에 맞서서, 의사 진단서의 증언에 맞서서, 장례를 치렀다는 사실과 무덤에 새겨진 비문의 사실에 맞서서, 페얼리 씨에게 그녀가 그녀임을 증명하고 그녀를 리머리지 하우스로 되돌려놓을 수 있겠습니까? 없습니다! 우리는 기껏해야 그녀가 죽었다는 주장에 심각한 의심을 던지는 데 성공하기를 바랄 수 있을 뿐이고, 그 의심은 법적인 심리가 아니고서는 결코 결말이 나지 않습니다. 이 심리를 모든 단계에 걸쳐 끌고 갈 만한 돈이 우리에게 있다고 쳐봅시다(사실 우리에게는 분명 없지만 말입니다). 페얼리 씨의 편견을 이치로 풀어낼 수 있다고 쳐봅시다—백작 부부의 거짓 증언과 그 밖의 온갖 거짓 증언을 모조리 뒤엎을 수 있다고, 사람들이 그녀를 알아본 것을 로라와 앤 캐서릭을 혼동한 착오로 돌릴 수 없다고, 우리 적들이 그 필적을 교묘한 위조라고 우길 수 없다고 쳐봅시다—이 모든 가정은 크든 작든 뻔한 개연성을 거스르는 것입니다. 그러나 그것도 다 그렇다 치고, 이렇게 자문해봅시다. 그 음모라는 주제를 두고 로라 자신에게 던져질 첫 번째 결과, 혹은 첫 번째 물음은 무엇이겠는가 하고 말입니다. 그 결과가 어떠할지 우리는 너무나 잘 알고 있습니다. 그녀가 런던에서 자기에게 일어난 일에 대한 기억을 끝내 되찾지 못했다는 것을 알기 때문입니다. 사사로이 캐묻든 공개적으로 캐묻든, 그녀는 자기 사건의 주장을 거들 능력이 전혀 없습니다. 매리언, 이것이 내가 보는 만큼 훤히 당신 눈에 보이지 않는다면, 우리는 내일 당장 리머리지로 가서 그 시험을 해봅시다.”\n\n“나도 훤히 보여요, 월터. 설령 그 모든 소송 비용을 댈 방도가 있다 해도, 설령 끝내 이긴다 해도, 그 지체는 견딜 수 없을 거예요. 이미 겪을 만큼 겪은 뒤에 끝없이 이어질 그 애태움은 가슴이 찢어지는 일일 거고요. 리머리지로 가는 것이 가망 없다는 당신 말이 옳아요. 백작을 상대로 그 마지막 기회를 걸어보겠다는 결심에서도 당신이 옳다는 확신이 들면 좋겠는데. 그게 대체 기회이기는 한가요?”\n\n“의심할 여지 없이, 그렇습니다. 그것은 로라가 런던으로 간 날짜, 그 잃어버린 날짜를 되찾을 기회입니다. 얼마 전에 당신에게 말했던 근거들을 다시 늘어놓지 않더라도, 나는 그 여정의 날짜와 사망 진단서에 적힌 날짜 사이에 어긋남이 있다고 여전히 처음처럼 굳게 믿습니다. 음모 전체의 약한 고리가 바로 거기에 있습니다—그쪽으로 치고 들어가면 그것은 산산이 무너집니다. 그리고 그것을 칠 수단은 백작이 쥐고 있습니다. 내가 그자에게서 그것을 빼앗아내는 데 성공한다면, 당신과 내 삶의 목적은 이루어집니다. 내가 실패한다면, 로라가 당한 그 부당함은 이 세상에서 결코 바로잡히지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“당신 자신은 실패가 두렵나요, 월터?”\n\n“나는 감히 성공을 미리 점치지 못합니다. 그리고 바로 그 때문에, 매리언, 지금처럼 이렇게 터놓고 꾸밈없이 말하는 겁니다. 내 마음으로도 내 양심으로도 이렇게 말할 수 있습니다. 앞날에 대한 로라의 희망은 지금 가장 밑바닥까지 내려가 있습니다. 그녀의 재산이 사라졌다는 것을 나는 압니다—그녀를 세상에서 제자리로 되돌려놓을 마지막 기회가 그녀의 가장 악랄한 원수의 손에, 지금으로서는 도무지 건드릴 수 없고 어쩌면 끝까지 건드릴 수 없을지도 모를 한 사내의 손에 놓여 있다는 것을 압니다. 세속의 온갖 이점이 그녀에게서 다 사라지고, 지위와 신분을 되찾을 가망이라고는 몹시도 미덥지 못하고, 남편이 마련해줄 수 있는 앞날 말고는 더 밝은 앞날이랄 것이 없는 지금이라면, 가난한 그림 선생도 마침내 아무 해될 것 없이 제 마음을 열어 보여도 되겠지요. 그녀가 부유하던 시절, 매리언, 나는 그저 그녀의 손을 이끌어주던 선생일 뿐이었습니다—이제 그녀가 곤궁해진 지금, 나는 그 손을 내 아내의 손으로 달라고 청하는 겁니다!”\n\n매리언의 눈이 다정하게 내 눈과 마주쳤다—나는 더 말할 수 없었다. 가슴이 벅찼고 입술이 떨렸다. 나도 모르게 그녀의 연민에 호소할 지경에 이르러 있었다. 나는 방을 나가려고 일어섰다. 그녀도 그 순간 함께 일어나 내 어깨에 가만히 손을 얹고 나를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n“월터!” 그녀가 말했다. “나는 한때 당신을 위해서, 또 그 애를 위해서 두 사람을 갈라놓았어요. 여기서 기다려요, 내 동생!—기다려요, 나의 더없이 소중하고 가장 좋은 벗이여, 로라가 와서 내가 이번에 무슨 일을 했는지 당신에게 말해줄 때까지.”\n\n리머리지에서 작별하던 그 아침 이후 처음으로 그녀는 내 이마에 입술을 대었다. 그녀가 입 맞출 때 눈물 한 방울이 내 얼굴에 떨어졌다. 그녀는 얼른 몸을 돌려 내가 일어섰던 의자를 가리키고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n나는 내 인생의 고비를 홀로 견디려고 창가에 앉았다. 숨죽인 그 시간 동안 내 머릿속은 온통 텅 빈 것 같았다. 익숙한 감각이란 감각이 모두 고통스러울 만큼 예민해졌다는 것 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다. 햇빛은 눈이 멀 만큼 밝아졌고, 저 멀리 서로를 쫓아다니는 흰 바닷새들은 내 얼굴 앞에서 파닥이는 듯했으며, 모래밭에 부딪는 파도의 부드러운 속삭임은 귓속에서 천둥처럼 울렸다.\n\n문이 열리고 로라가 혼자 들어왔다. 우리가 헤어지던 그 아침에도 그녀는 리머리지 하우스의 아침 식사실로 그렇게 들어왔었다. 그때는 슬픔과 망설임 속에서 느릿느릿 더듬거리며 나에게 다가왔다. 이제 그녀는 행복으로 서두르는 걸음으로, 얼굴에 행복의 빛을 환히 담고서 왔다. 그 사랑스러운 두 팔이 저절로 나를 감싸 안았고, 그 다정한 입술이 저절로 내 입술을 찾아왔다. “내 사랑,” 그녀가 속삭였다. “우리 이제 서로 사랑한다고 말해도 되는 거죠?” 그녀의 머리가 부드러운 만족감에 잠겨 내 가슴에 파묻혔다. “아,” 그녀가 천진하게 말했다. “나 이제야 정말 행복해요!”\n\n열흘 뒤 우리는 한층 더 행복했다. 우리는 부부가 되었다.\n\n# IV\n\n이 이야기의 흐름은 꾸준히 앞으로 나아가며, 나를 우리 결혼 생활의 아침으로부터 데리고 나와 그 끝을 향해 실어 간다.\n\n다시 두 주가 지나 우리 세 사람은 런던으로 돌아와 있었고, 다가올 싸움의 그림자가 우리 위로 조용히 덮여 오고 있었다.\n\n매리언과 나는 우리를 서둘러 돌아오게 만든 이유를 — 백작의 소재를 확실히 해 두어야 한다는 필요를 — 로라가 모르도록 조심했다. 때는 5월 초였고, 포리스트 로드의 그 집에 대한 그의 거주 기한은 6월에 만료될 예정이었다. 그가 기한을 갱신한다면(그리고 나는 곧 이야기할 몇 가지 이유로 그러리라 예상하고 있었다), 그가 내 손에서 빠져나가지 못하리라 확신할 수 있었다. 그러나 혹시라도 그가 내 예상을 저버리고 이 나라를 떠난다면, 나는 그를 맞아 싸울 무장을 갖추는 데 한시도 허비할 수 없었다.\n\n새로운 행복이 처음으로 벅차게 차오르던 무렵, 내 결심이 흔들리던 순간들이 있었다 — 내 생애의 가장 간절한 소망이 로라의 사랑을 얻음으로써 이루어졌으니 이제 안전하게 만족해 버리자는 유혹에 빠지던 순간들이. 나는 난생처음으로 그 위험의 크기를, 나를 상대로 늘어선 불리한 승산을, 우리 새 삶의 아름다운 약속을, 그리고 그토록 어렵게 얻어 낸 행복을 내가 몰아넣을지도 모를 위태로움을 겁먹은 마음으로 헤아렸다. 그렇다! 정직하게 인정하자. 짧은 동안 나는 사랑의 달콤한 인도에 이끌려, 더 혹독한 단련 아래 더 어두운 나날 속에서 지켜 왔던 그 목적으로부터 멀리 헤매었다. 로라는 순진하게도 나를 그 험한 길에서 비켜서게 했고 — 순진하게도 그녀는 나를 다시 그 길로 데려다 놓을 운명이었다.\n\n때때로 끔찍한 과거의 꿈들이 잠의 신비 속에서, 깨어 있는 기억으로는 흔적조차 잃어버린 사건들을 그녀에게 두서없이 되살려 놓곤 했다. 어느 밤(결혼한 지 겨우 두 주밖에 지나지 않았을 때), 잠든 그녀를 지켜보고 있던 나는 감긴 눈꺼풀 사이로 눈물이 천천히 배어 나오는 것을 보았고, 그녀의 넋이 다시 블랙워터 파크에서 시작된 그 운명의 여정으로 돌아가 있음을 알려 주는 희미한 중얼거림을 들었다. 잠의 신성함 속에서 그토록 애처롭고 그토록 두렵던 그 무의식의 호소가 불길처럼 나를 관통했다. 그 이튿날이 우리가 런던으로 돌아온 날이었다 — 내 결심이 열 배의 힘으로 내게 돌아온 날이었다.\n\n가장 먼저 필요한 일은 그 사내에 관해 무엇이든 알아내는 것이었다. 그때까지 그의 생애의 참된 내력은 내게 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 수수께끼였다.\n\n나는 내 손안에 있는 빈약한 정보원들부터 시작했다. 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 쓴 그 중요한 기록은(매리언이 지난겨울 내가 일러 준 지시를 따라 얻어 낸 것이었다) 지금 내가 그것을 들여다보는 특별한 목적에는 아무 도움도 되지 않는 것으로 드러났다. 그것을 읽으면서 나는 클레멘츠 부인이 내게 밝혀 준 사실을 — 앤 캐서릭을 런던으로 데려왔고 거기서 그녀를 음모의 이해에 바쳐 버린 일련의 기만들을 — 다시 헤아려 보았다. 여기서도 역시 백작은 드러내 놓고 자신을 얽어매지 않았다 — 여기서도 역시 그는 실질적으로 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었다.\n\n다음으로 나는 블랙워터 파크에서 쓰인 매리언의 일기로 돌아갔다. 내 청에 따라 그녀는 백작에 대한 지난날의 호기심과 그에 관해 알아낸 몇 가지 세부를 적어 둔 대목을 내게 다시 읽어 주었다.\n\n내가 말하는 그 대목은 그의 성격과 외모를 그려 낸 부분에 나온다. 그녀는 그를 이렇게 적고 있다 — “여러 해 동안 고국의 국경을 넘어 본 일이 없다”고, “블랙워터 파크에서 가장 가까운 읍내에 정착해 사는 이탈리아 신사가 있는지 몹시 알고 싶어 한다”고, “온갖 기묘한 소인이 찍힌 편지들을 받는데, 그중 하나에는 크고 관청 것처럼 보이는 봉인이 찍혀 있었다”고. 그녀는 그가 고국을 그토록 오래 떠나 있는 까닭을 그가 정치적 망명자라고 가정하면 설명할 수 있으리라는 쪽으로 기운다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 그녀는 그 생각을, “크고 관청 것처럼 보이는 봉인”이 찍힌 그 외국 편지를 받았다는 사실과는 도무지 조화시키지 못한다 — 대륙에서 정치적 망명자 앞으로 보내는 편지야말로 그런 식으로 외국 우체국의 주목을 사려 들지 않는 법이기 때문이다.\n\n일기가 이렇게 내 앞에 내놓은 정황들에, 거기서 자라난 내 나름의 몇 가지 추측이 더해지자, 어째서 진작 이르지 못했을까 싶은 결론 하나가 떠올랐다. 이제 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다 — 언젠가 로라가 블랙워터 파크에서 매리언에게 했던 그 말, 포스코 백작부인이 문에 귀를 대고 엿들었던 그 말을. 백작은 첩자다!\n\n로라는 자신에게 저지른 그의 처사에 대한 당연한 분노에서 되는대로 그 말을 그에게 붙였을 뿐이다. 나는 그의 생업이 첩자의 생업이라는 확고한 신념을 가지고 그 말을 그에게 붙였다. 이 가정 위에 서고 보니, 음모의 목적이 이미 달성된 뒤에도 그가 그토록 오래 영국에 머무는 그 기이한 일의 이유가 내게는 아주 이해할 만한 것이 되었다.\n\n내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그해는 하이드 파크에서 저 유명한 수정궁 박람회가 열린 해였다. 외국인들이 이미 유례없이 많이 들어와 있었고, 지금도 계속 영국으로 들어오는 중이었다. 제 나라 정부의 그칠 줄 모르는 의심이 지정된 요원들을 통해 은밀히 뒤를 밟아 우리 해안까지 따라온 사람들이 수백 명 단위로 우리 가운데 있었다. 내 추측은 백작만 한 능력과 사회적 지위를 지닌 인물을 여느 외국인 첩자들의 하급 무리와 한 부류로 묶은 것이 결코 아니었다. 나는 그가 권한 있는 자리를 차지하고 있으리라고, 그가 은밀히 섬기는 정부로부터 이 나라에서 특별히 부리는 남녀 요원들을 조직하고 관리하는 일을 맡고 있으리라고 의심했으며, 블랙워터 파크에서 간호인 노릇을 하도록 그토록 때맞춰 구해졌던 루벨 부인도 십중팔구 그 무리 가운데 하나이리라 믿었다.\n\n이 생각이 사실에 근거를 두고 있다고 가정한다면, 백작의 처지는 내가 지금껏 감히 바라던 것보다 더 공략하기 쉬운 것으로 드러날지도 몰랐다. 그 사내의 내력과 그 사내 자신에 대해 지금 내가 아는 것보다 더 많이 알려면 나는 누구를 찾아가야 하는가?\n\n이 다급한 처지에서 자연히 내 머리에 떠오른 것은, 내가 믿을 수 있는 그의 동포야말로 나를 도울 가장 알맞은 사람이리라는 생각이었다. 이런 사정에서 내가 맨 먼저 떠올린 사람은 또한 내가 친밀하게 알고 지내는 단 한 사람의 이탈리아인이었다 — 나의 별난 작은 친구, 페스카 교수였다.\n\n교수는 이 지면에서 너무도 오래 자리를 비운 탓에, 아예 잊혀 버릴 위험까지 무릅쓴 셈이 되었다.\n\n내 이야기 같은 것에는 어쩔 수 없는 법칙이 있으니, 거기 관련된 사람들은 사건의 흐름이 그들을 집어 올릴 때에만 나타난다는 것이다 — 그들은 내 개인적 편애의 은혜로가 아니라, 낱낱이 적어야 할 정황과 곧바로 이어져 있다는 권리에 따라 오고 간다. 그런 까닭에 페스카뿐 아니라 내 어머니와 누이도 이 이야기의 훨씬 뒤편에 남겨져 왔다. 햄스테드 오두막을 찾아가던 일, 음모가 이루어 낸 로라의 신원 부인을 어머니가 그대로 믿고 있던 일, 나를 아끼는 질투 어린 애정에서 어머니와 누이가 한결같이 붙들고 놓지 않던 그 편견을 깨뜨리려던 내 헛된 노력, 그리고 그 편견 탓에 두 사람이 내 아내를 정당하게 대할 줄 알게 되기까지 내 결혼을 숨겨야만 했던 괴로운 처지 — 이 모든 자잘한 집안일들은 이야기의 주된 관심에 꼭 필요하지 않았기에 적히지 않은 채로 남았다. 그것들이 내 근심을 더하고 내 실망을 쓰라리게 했다 한들 아무 상관이 없다 — 사건의 꾸준한 행진은 가차 없이 그것들을 지나쳐 갔다.\n\n같은 이유로 나는 리머리지 하우스에서의 체류가 갑작스레 끝난 뒤 페스카를 다시 만났을 때 그의 형제 같은 애정에서 얻은 위안에 대해서도 여기 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 내가 중앙아메리카로 떠날 때 그 다정한 작은 친구가 얼마나 한결같이 승선지까지 나를 따라왔는지도, 다음번 런던에서 다시 만났을 때 그가 얼마나 요란한 기쁨으로 나를 맞았는지도 적지 않았다. 내가 돌아왔을 때 그가 내놓은 도움의 제안을 받아들여도 좋다고 여겼더라면, 그는 진작에 다시 이 지면에 등장했을 것이다. 그러나 그의 명예심과 용기는 온전히 기댈 만하다는 것을 알면서도, 그의 신중함까지 믿어도 좋을지는 그만큼 확신이 서지 않았고, 오직 그 이유 하나 때문에 나는 모든 조사를 혼자서 밀고 나갔다. 페스카가 지금까지 이 이야기의 진행과는 아무 연결도 없이 떨어져 있었을 뿐, 나와 내 일과의 연결에서까지 떨어져 있었던 것은 아님을 이제 충분히 이해할 것이다. 그는 여전히, 평생 그러했던 그대로 진실하고 선뜻 나서는 내 친구였다.\n\n페스카를 불러 도움을 청하기 전에, 내가 상대해야 할 자가 어떤 인간인지 내 눈으로 직접 확인할 필요가 있었다. 그때까지 나는 포스코 백작을 단 한 번도 본 적이 없었다.\n\n로라와 매리언과 함께 런던으로 돌아온 지 사흘째 되던 날, 나는 아침 열 시와 열한 시 사이에 혼자 세인트존스우드의 포리스트 로드로 나섰다. 날은 화창했고 — 내게는 몇 시간의 여유가 있었으며 — 조금 기다리다 보면 백작이 밖으로 나오도록 이끌릴 법도 하다고 생각했다. 낮에 그가 나를 알아볼 가능성은 그다지 두려워할 이유가 없었으니, 그가 나를 본 유일한 경우는 그가 밤중에 내 집까지 나를 뒤따라왔던 그때뿐이었기 때문이다.\n\n집 앞쪽 창문에는 아무도 나타나지 않았다. 나는 그 집 옆을 따라 지나는 골목으로 걸어 내려가 낮은 정원 담 너머를 넘겨다보았다. 아래층 뒤쪽 창문 하나가 밀어 올려져 있었고, 그 열린 곳에는 그물이 쳐져 있었다. 아무도 보이지 않았으나, 방 안에서 처음에는 새들의 날카로운 휘파람 소리와 노랫소리가, 이어서 매리언의 묘사로 내게 익숙해진 그 깊고 울리는 목소리가 들려왔다. “내 새끼손가락 위로 나오너라, 나의 예-예-예쁜이들아!” 그 목소리가 외쳤다. “나와서 위로 깡충 뛰어라! 하나, 둘, 셋 — 올라가! 셋, 둘, 하나 — 내려와! 하나, 둘, 셋 — 짹-짹-짹-찌잇!” 백작은 매리언이 블랙워터 파크에 있던 시절에 그러했듯 카나리아들을 훈련시키고 있었다.\n\n나는 잠시 기다렸고, 노랫소리와 휘파람 소리가 그쳤다. “자, 내게 입 맞추어라, 나의 예쁜이들아!” 그 깊은 목소리가 말했다. 이에 답하는 지저귐과 짹짹거림이 있었고 — 낮고 기름진 웃음소리가 나더니 — 한 일 분쯤의 침묵이 이어졌으며, 그러고 나서 나는 현관문이 열리는 소리를 들었다. 나는 몸을 돌려 왔던 길을 되짚었다. 로시니의 「모세」에 나오는 저 장엄한 기도의 선율이 우렁찬 저음으로 불려, 그 교외의 고요를 뚫고 웅장하게 솟아올랐다. 앞뜰 문이 열렸다가 닫혔다. 백작이 나온 것이다.\n\n그는 길을 건너 리젠트 파크의 서쪽 경계를 향해 걸어갔다. 나는 내 쪽 길가에 붙어 그보다 조금 뒤처진 채, 나 역시 같은 방향으로 걸었다.\n\n매리언은 그의 큰 키와 괴물 같은 비대함과 요란한 상복 차림에 대해서는 나를 준비시켜 주었으나, 그 사내의 소름 끼치도록 싱싱하고 쾌활하고 활기 넘치는 모습에 대해서는 그러지 못했다. 그는 예순 해의 나이를 마흔 해도 채 안 되는 것처럼 지고 다녔다. 그는 모자를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울여 쓰고 가볍고 경쾌한 걸음으로 큰 지팡이를 흔들며 혼자 흥얼거리면서, 이따금 양옆의 집들과 정원들을 당당하고 미소 띤 시혜의 눈길로 올려다보며 어슬렁어슬렁 걸었다. 어느 낯선 이에게 이 동네 전체가 저 사람 것이라고 일러 준다 해도, 그 낯선 이는 그 말을 듣고 놀라지 않았을 것이다. 그는 결코 뒤를 돌아보지 않았고, 내게도 겉으로는 아무 주의를 기울이지 않았으며, 제 쪽 길에서 그를 스쳐 지나가는 어느 누구에게도 겉으로는 아무 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 다만 이따금 마주치는 보모들과 아이들을 향해 넉넉한 아버지 같은 좋은 기분으로 웃음 짓고 히죽거릴 때만은 예외였다. 이런 식으로 그는 나를 이끌고 나아갔고, 마침내 우리는 공원 서쪽 연립 주택가 바깥에 늘어선 상점 무리에 이르렀다.\n\n여기서 그는 어느 제과점 앞에 멈추어 안으로 들어갔다가(아마 주문을 하려는 것이었으리라), 곧바로 타르트 하나를 손에 들고 다시 나왔다. 이탈리아인 하나가 가게 앞에서 손풍금을 돌리고 있었고, 비참하게 쪼그라든 작은 원숭이 한 마리가 그 악기 위에 앉아 있었다. 백작은 걸음을 멈추고 타르트를 제 몫으로 한 입 베어 문 뒤, 남은 것을 엄숙하게 원숭이에게 건넸다. “가엾은 나의 작은 친구여!” 그가 기괴한 다정함으로 말했다. “배가 고파 보이는구나. 인류애의 거룩한 이름으로, 내 그대에게 점심을 좀 대접하노라!” 손풍금장이는 애처로운 몸짓으로 이 자비로운 낯선 이에게 한 푼만 달라고 청했다. 백작은 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그대로 지나쳐 갔다.\n\n우리는 뉴 로드와 옥스퍼드 스트리트 사이의 거리들과 한결 나은 부류의 상점들에 이르렀다. 백작은 다시 걸음을 멈추고 자그마한 안경점으로 들어갔는데, 그 진열창에는 수리를 정갈하게 해 드린다는 문구가 붙어 있었다. 그는 오페라글라스를 손에 들고 다시 나와 몇 걸음 걷다가, 어느 악보상 앞에 내걸린 오페라 광고를 보려고 멈추어 섰다. 그는 그 광고를 꼼꼼히 읽고 잠시 생각하더니, 마침 곁을 지나가는 빈 마차를 불러 세웠다. “오페라 매표소로.” 그가 마부에게 말하고는 그대로 실려 갔다.\n\n나는 길을 건너 이번에는 내가 그 광고를 들여다보았다. 예고된 공연은 「루크레치아 보르자」였고, 바로 그날 저녁에 열릴 예정이었다. 백작의 손에 들린 오페라글라스와, 그가 광고를 꼼꼼히 읽던 모습과, 마부에게 이른 행선지가 모두 그가 그 관객 가운데 한 사람이 될 작정임을 일러 주고 있었다. 나는 예전에 잘 알고 지내던, 그 극장에 딸린 무대 화가 한 사람에게 부탁하면 나와 친구 한 사람 몫의 일층 뒷자리 입장권을 구할 방도가 있었다. 적어도 관객 가운데 있는 백작이 나에게도, 나와 함께 있는 사람에게도 쉽게 눈에 띌 기회는 있는 셈이었고, 그렇다면 바로 그날 밤에 페스카가 그 동포를 아는지 모르는지 확인할 방도가 내게 있었다.\n\n이 생각으로 내 저녁 일정은 곧바로 정해졌다. 나는 표를 구했고, 가는 길에 교수의 하숙집에 쪽지를 남겼다. 여덟 시 십오 분 전에 나는 그를 극장으로 데려가려고 그를 찾아갔다. 나의 작은 친구는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 상태였으며, 단춧구멍에 축제용 꽃을 꽂고 내가 여태 본 것 중 가장 큰 오페라글라스를 겨드랑이에 꼭 끼고 있었다.\n\n“준비되셨습니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“좋아-다-좋아!” 페스카가 말했다.\n\n우리는 극장을 향해 출발했다.\n\n# V\n\n우리가 극장에 닿았을 때는 오페라 서곡의 마지막 음들이 연주되는 중이었고, 일층 평석의 자리는 이미 빈틈없이 들어차 있었다.\n\n그러나 평석을 빙 둘러 나 있는 통로에는 자리가 넉넉했다. 내가 그날 공연에 온 목적을 이루기에는 바로 그 자리가 더할 나위 없이 알맞았다. 나는 먼저 특등석과 우리를 갈라놓는 난간으로 가서 극장의 그쪽 구역에 백작이 있는지 살폈다. 그는 거기 없었다. 무대를 기준으로 왼편 통로를 따라 되돌아오며 주위를 주의 깊게 둘러본 끝에, 나는 평석에서 그를 발견했다. 그는 아주 좋은 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 긴 의자의 끝에서 열두어 자리 혹은 열네 자리쯤 안쪽, 특등석에서 세 줄도 떨어지지 않은 곳이었다. 나는 그와 정확히 일직선이 되는 위치에 섰고, 페스카는 내 곁에 섰다. 교수는 내가 무슨 목적으로 자기를 극장에 데려왔는지 아직 알지 못했으므로, 우리가 무대 쪽으로 더 다가가지 않는 것을 오히려 의아해했다.\n\n막이 오르고 오페라가 시작되었다.\n\n1막 내내 우리는 그 자리를 지켰다. 백작은 관현악과 무대에 온통 마음을 빼앗겨 우리 쪽으로는 눈길 한 번 흘리지 않았다. 도니제티의 감미로운 음악은 한 음도 그를 그냥 지나치지 않았다. 그는 이웃한 사람들보다 머리 하나쯤 높이 솟은 채 앉아 미소를 짓고, 이따금 그 커다란 머리를 흡족한 듯 끄덕였다. 곁의 사람들이 아리아가 끝나자마자—영국 관객이 그런 자리에서 늘 그러하듯—뒤이어 곧바로 흘러나오는 관현악 악절은 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 박수를 쳐대면, 그는 가엾다는 듯 나무라는 표정으로 그들을 돌아보며 정중히 청하는 몸짓으로 한 손을 들어 보였다. 노래의 한결 세련된 대목, 남들은 박수도 보내지 않고 지나치는 음악의 한결 섬세한 굽이에 이르면, 꼭 맞는 검은 염소가죽 장갑을 낀 그의 통통한 두 손이 음악을 아는 사람다운 교양 있는 감상의 표시로 서로를 부드럽게 두드렸다. 그럴 때면 “브라보! 브라아아아!” 하는 그의 기름진 찬탄이 정적을 뚫고, 커다란 고양이가 그르렁대는 소리처럼 낮게 울렸다. 양옆에 앉은 사람들—혈색 좋고 서글서글한 시골 사람들로, 유행의 도시 런던의 햇살을 얼떨떨하게 쬐고 있던—은 그를 보고 듣더니 그가 하는 대로 따라 하기 시작했다. 그날 밤 평석에서 터져 나온 갈채는 여러 차례, 그 검은 장갑 낀 손의 부드럽고 편안한 두드림에서 시작되었다. 그 사내의 게걸스러운 허영은 자기가 그 자리의 우두머리이자 비평의 권위라는 이 은근한 찬사를, 더할 나위 없이 흡족해하는 낯빛으로 삼켜버렸다. 미소가 그 살진 얼굴 위로 끊임없이 물결쳤다. 음악이 멎는 사이사이 그는 자기 자신과 동료 인간들에게 두루 만족한 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보았다. “그래! 그래! 이 야만스러운 영국 사람들이 나에게서 무언가를 배우고 있구나. 여기서나 저기서나 어디서나, 나 포스코는 느껴지는 영향력이요, 지존의 자리에 앉은 사람이다!” 얼굴이 말을 할 수 있는 것이라면 그때 그의 얼굴이 말을 하고 있었으니, 그 말이 바로 그것이었다.\n\n1막이 끝나며 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 주위를 둘러보려 자리에서 일어섰다. 내가 기다려온 순간이었다. 페스카가 그를 아는지 시험해볼 때가 온 것이다.\n\n그도 다른 사람들과 함께 일어나, 오페라글라스로 특별석의 사람들을 거만하게 훑어보았다. 처음에는 등을 우리 쪽으로 두고 있었으나, 이윽고 우리가 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 우리 머리 위의 특별석을 몇 분 동안 안경으로 바라보았다. 그러고는 안경을 내렸으나 여전히 위쪽을 계속 올려다보고 있었다. 그의 얼굴이 온전히 드러난 이때야말로 페스카의 주의를 그에게 돌리기 위해 내가 고른 순간이었다.\n\n“저 사람 아십니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“어느 사람 말인가, 나의 친구?”\n\n“저기 서서 우리 쪽으로 얼굴을 두고 있는, 키 크고 살진 사람 말입니다.”\n\n페스카가 발끝으로 몸을 돋우어 백작을 바라보았다.\n\n“아니.” 교수가 말했다. “저 덩치 큰 뚱뚱한 사람은 내게 낯선 사람일세. 유명한 사람인가? 왜 저 사람을 가리키는 겐가?”\n\n“저 사람에 대해 뭐라도 알고 싶은 각별한 사정이 있어서요. 교수님과 같은 나라 사람입니다. 이름은 포스코 백작이고요. 그 이름을 아십니까?”\n\n“모르네, 월터. 이름도 사람도 내가 아는 바 없네.”\n\n“정말 못 알아보시겠습니까? 다시 한번 보세요, 찬찬히 보세요. 제가 왜 이렇게 애가 타는지는 극장을 나가서 말씀드리겠습니다. 잠깐만요! 여기 올라서시게 제가 부축해드리죠. 여기서라면 더 잘 보이실 겁니다.”\n\n나는 그 작은 사람이 평석 의자들이 놓인 높은 단의 가장자리에 올라서도록 부축했다. 작은 키는 그에게 아무런 방해도 되지 않았다. 여기서라면 긴 의자의 바깥쪽 끝에 앉은 부인들의 머리 너머로 내다볼 수 있었다.\n\n우리 곁에 서 있던, 그때까지 내가 눈여겨보지 못했던 호리호리한 금발 남자—왼쪽 뺨에 흉터가 있는 남자—가 내가 페스카를 올려주는 동안 페스카를 유심히 바라보았고, 이어 페스카의 시선이 향하는 쪽을 따라 백작을 한층 더 유심히 바라보았다. 우리 대화가 그의 귀에 닿았을 수도 있었고, 내 짐작으로는 그 대화가 그의 호기심을 자극했을 수도 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안 페스카는 자기 바로 맞은편에서 조금 위로 향한, 넓고 살진 웃는 얼굴에 눈길을 진지하게 붙박고 있었다.\n\n“아닐세.” 그가 말했다. “평생 저 덩치 큰 뚱뚱한 사람을 이 두 눈으로 본 적이 없네.”\n\n그가 그렇게 말하는 동안 백작은 우리 뒤쪽, 평석 층의 특별석 쪽으로 눈길을 내렸다.\n\n두 이탈리아 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다.\n\n바로 조금 전까지만 해도 나는 그가 거듭 밝힌 말에 따라 페스카가 백작을 모른다고 완전히 믿고 있었다. 그런데 바로 그다음 순간, 나는 백작이 페스카를 안다는 것을 그만큼이나 확실하게 알아차렸다!\n\n알아보았을 뿐 아니라—더욱 놀랍게도—두려워하기까지 했다! 그 악당의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나간 변화는 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다. 누렇던 안색을 한순간에 납빛으로 바꿔놓은 그 빛깔, 이목구비 전체가 문득 굳어버린 것, 차가운 잿빛 눈의 은밀한 살핌, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 미동도 없는 그 정지 상태가 저마다 사연을 말해주고 있었다. 죽음 같은 공포가 그의 몸과 마음을 사로잡았으며, 그 까닭은 그가 페스카를 알아보았다는 사실 바로 그것이었다!\n\n뺨에 흉터가 있는 호리호리한 남자는 여전히 우리 가까이에 있었다. 그도 페스카를 본 백작에게 일어난 변화에서 내가 끌어낸 것과 같은 추론을 끌어낸 모양이었다. 그는 온순하고 신사다운 사람으로 외국인처럼 보였으며, 우리 일에 대한 그의 관심은 조금도 무례하다 할 만한 방식으로 드러나지는 않았다.\n\n나로 말하자면, 백작의 얼굴에 일어난 변화에 너무나 놀랐고 사태가 전혀 뜻밖의 방향으로 돌아간 데 너무나 어리둥절해서, 다음에 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 페스카가 원래 있던 내 곁자리로 물러서서 먼저 말을 걸어옴으로써 나를 정신 들게 했다.\n\n“저 뚱뚱한 사람이 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보는지!” 그가 소리쳤다. “나를 보는 건가? 내가 유명 인사인가? 내가 저 사람을 모르는데 저 사람이 어떻게 나를 알겠나?”\n\n나는 여전히 백작에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 페스카가 움직이자 그도 처음으로 몸을 움직이는 것이 보였다. 이제 낮은 자리로 내려선 그 작은 사람을 시야에서 놓치지 않으려는 것이었다. 나는 이런 상황에서 페스카의 주의가 그에게서 거두어지면 어떤 일이 벌어질지 궁금했고, 그래서 교수에게 오늘 저녁 특별석의 부인들 가운데 가르치는 제자가 보이느냐고 물었다. 페스카는 곧바로 커다란 오페라글라스를 눈에 갖다 대고, 더없이 성실한 눈길로 제자들을 찾으며 극장 위층을 천천히 두루 살폈다.\n\n그가 그 일에 몰두한 것을 보이자마자 백작은 몸을 돌려, 우리가 선 쪽에서 먼 편에 앉은 사람들 곁을 슬쩍 빠져나가 평석 한가운데로 난 중앙 통로로 사라졌다. 나는 페스카의 팔을 붙들고, 그가 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라거나 말거나, 백작이 문에 이르기 전에 가로막으려고 그를 데리고 평석 뒤편으로 서둘러 돌아갔다. 다소 놀랍게도 그 호리호리한 남자가 우리보다 먼저 서둘러 빠져나갔다. 우리 쪽 평석의 몇몇 사람이 자리를 뜨는 바람에 길이 막혀 페스카와 내가 지체하는 사이에 그는 그 정체를 피해 간 것이다. 우리가 로비에 이르렀을 때 백작은 사라지고 없었고, 흉터 있는 그 외국인도 가고 없었다.\n\n“집으로 갑시다.” 내가 말했다. “교수님 숙소로 가시죠, 페스카. 단둘이 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 지금 곧 말씀드려야 합니다.”\n\n“오-내-영혼에-축복이!” 교수가 극도의 어리둥절함에 빠져 외쳤다. “대체 무슨 일인가?”\n\n나는 대답 없이 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다. 백작이 극장을 떠난 정황으로 미루어, 페스카를 피하려는 그의 유별난 조바심이 그를 더한 극단으로 몰고 갈지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그는 런던을 떠남으로써 나에게서도 달아날 수 있었다. 그에게 단 하루라도 제 마음대로 움직일 자유를 허락한다면 앞일이 어찌 될지 알 수 없었다. 그리고 우리보다 앞서 나간 그 낯선 외국인도 미덥지 않았다. 나는 그가 일부러 백작을 뒤따라 나갔으리라 의심했다.\n\n이 두 겹의 불신을 품은 채, 나는 오래지 않아 페스카에게 내가 바라는 바를 알아듣게 했다. 그의 방에 단둘이 남자마자, 나는 여기 적어놓은 것만큼이나 분명하고 숨김없이 내 목적을 이야기함으로써 그의 혼란과 경악을 백 배로 키워놓았다.\n\n“나의 친구, 내가 무얼 할 수 있단 말인가?” 교수가 두 손을 내밀어 애처롭게 호소하며 외쳤다. “제기랄-이런-제기랄! 내가 그 사람을 모르는데 어떻게 자네를 돕겠나, 월터?”\n\n“그가 교수님을 압니다. 그는 교수님을 두려워합니다. 교수님을 피하려고 극장을 떠났고요. 페스카! 여기에는 반드시 까닭이 있습니다. 영국에 오시기 전 교수님의 삶을 돌이켜보세요. 교수님은 정치적인 이유로 이탈리아를 떠났다고 제게 직접 말씀하셨습니다. 그 이유를 제게 말씀하신 적은 한 번도 없고, 지금도 저는 그것을 캐묻지 않겠습니다. 다만 교수님의 기억을 더듬어보시고, 교수님을 처음 본 순간 그 사람이 보인 공포를 설명해줄 만한 지난날의 원인이 정말 짚이지 않는지만 말씀해주십시오.”\n\n이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도, 나에게는 아무 해도 없어 보이던 이 말이 페스카에게, 페스카를 본 것이 백작에게 일으켰던 것과 똑같이 아연한 효과를 일으켰다. 내 작은 친구의 발그레하던 얼굴이 한순간에 하얗게 질렸고, 그는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨면서 천천히 내게서 물러섰다.\n\n“월터!” 그가 말했다. “자네는 자네가 무엇을 청하는지 모르네.”\n\n그는 속삭이듯 말했다. 마치 내가 우리 둘 모두에게 닥친 어떤 숨은 위험을 불쑥 알려주기라도 한 듯한 눈으로 나를 바라보았다. 채 일 분도 지나지 않아 그는 내가 여태 알아온 그 소탈하고 활기차고 별난 작은 사람과 너무나 달라져서, 지금 보는 그 모습으로 길에서 마주쳤다면 나는 틀림없이 그를 알아보지 못했을 것이다.\n\n“뜻하지 않게 마음을 아프게 하고 놀라게 해드렸다면 용서하십시오.” 내가 대답했다. “제 아내가 포스코 백작의 손에 겪은 잔인한 부당함을 생각해주십시오. 그가 아내에게 정당한 대접을 하도록 제가 강제할 수단을 손에 넣지 못하는 한, 그 부당함은 결코 바로잡힐 수 없다는 것을 생각해주십시오. 저는 아내를 위해서 말씀드린 겁니다, 페스카. 다시 한번 용서를 구합니다. 더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.”\n\n나는 가려고 일어섰다. 그가 내가 문에 이르기 전에 나를 붙들었다.\n\n“기다리게.” 그가 말했다. “자네가 나를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 뒤흔들어놓았네. 내가 어떻게 조국을 떠났는지, 왜 떠났는지 자네는 모르지. 마음을 좀 가라앉히게 해주게. 생각을 할 수 있다면, 생각을 좀 하게 해주게.”\n\n나는 다시 의자로 돌아갔다. 그는 방 안을 오락가락하며 제 나라 말로 두서없이 혼잣말을 했다. 몇 번을 왔다 갔다 하더니 그는 문득 내게 다가와, 그 작은 두 손을 이상하리만치 다정하고 엄숙하게 내 가슴에 얹었다.\n\n“자네 마음과 영혼을 걸고 말해주게, 월터.” 그가 말했다. “그 사람에게 이르는 길이, 어쩌다 나를 거치게 된 이 길밖에는 정말 없는 겐가?”\n\n“다른 길은 없습니다.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n그는 다시 나를 두고 물러나, 방문을 열고 복도를 조심스레 내다보더니 문을 도로 닫고 돌아왔다.\n\n“자네는 나에 대한 권리를 얻었네, 월터.” 그가 말했다. “내 목숨을 구해준 그날에 말일세. 그 순간부터 내 목숨은 자네 것이었으니, 자네가 원할 때 가져가면 되네. 지금 가져가게. 그래! 나는 진심으로 하는 말일세. 우리 위에 계신 선하신 하느님이 참되신 것만큼이나 참되게, 이제부터 내가 할 말은 내 목숨을 자네 손에 쥐여주는 것이네.”\n\n그가 이 예사롭지 않은 경고를 내뱉을 때의 떨리는 진지함은, 그가 진실을 말하고 있다는 확신을 내 마음에 심어주었다.\n\n“이것만은 명심하게!” 그는 격한 동요에 못 이겨 두 손을 내게 흔들어 보이며 말을 이었다. “내 마음속에는 저 포스코라는 사람과, 자네를 위해 되불러오는 그 지난 시절 사이를 잇는 실이 한 오라기도 없네. 만일 자네가 그 실을 찾거든 자네 혼자만 알고 있게. 내게는 아무 말도 하지 말게. 무릎 꿇고 빌고 애원하네, 나를 모르는 채로 있게 해주게, 죄 없는 채로 있게 해주게, 지금처럼 앞일에 대해서는 눈먼 채로 있게 해주게!”\n\n그는 몇 마디를 더, 머뭇거리며 앞뒤 없이 말하다가 다시 멈추었다.\n\n나는 그가 평소 쓰는 어휘의 그 별스러운 말투와 표현을 허락하지 않을 만큼 심각한 자리에서 영어로 자기를 표현하려 애쓰는 일이, 애초부터 내게 말을 꺼내는 것 자체에 느껴온 어려움을 고통스럽게 키우고 있음을 알아차렸다. 우리가 가깝게 지내던 초기에 나는 그의 모국어를 (말하지는 못해도) 읽고 이해할 만큼은 익혀두었으므로, 이제 그에게 이탈리아어로 이야기하라고 권하고, 내가 알아야 할 것을 묻는 데는 내가 영어를 쓰기로 했다. 그가 이 제안을 받아들였다. 유창하게 흐르는 그의 말로, 끊임없이 씰룩이는 표정과 느닷없고 격렬한 외국식 몸짓에는 드러나되 결코 목소리를 높이는 데는 드러나지 않는 격한 동요 속에서, 나는 이제 이 이야기가 기록해야 할 마지막 싸움에 맞설 무기를 내게 쥐여준 그 말들을 들었다.[3]\n\n[3] 여기서 밝혀두는 것이 옳겠다. 나는 페스카가 내게 한 이야기를, 이 문제의 심각한 성격과 친구에 대한 나 자신의 의무감이 요구하는 대로 조심스럽게 삭제하고 고쳐서 옮긴다. 내가 독자에게 감추는 것은 이것이 처음이자 마지막이며, 이 대목에서는 신중을 기하자면 그렇게 하지 않을 수 없다.\n\n“자네는 내가 이탈리아를 떠난 동기에 대해서, 그것이 정치적인 이유였다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르네.” 그가 말을 시작했다. “만일 내가 정부의 박해에 떠밀려 이 나라에 왔다면, 나는 그 이유를 자네에게도 다른 누구에게도 숨기지 않았을 걸세. 내가 그것을 숨긴 것은 어떤 정부 당국도 내게 추방을 선고한 적이 없기 때문이네. 월터, 유럽 대륙의 큰 도시마다 숨어 있는 정치 결사에 대해 들어본 적이 있는가? 나는 이탈리아에서 그런 결사 가운데 하나에 속해 있었고, 영국에서도 여전히 속해 있네. 내가 이 나라에 온 것은 우리 우두머리의 지시에 따른 것이었네. 젊은 시절 나는 지나치게 열성이었네. 나 자신과 다른 이들을 위태롭게 할 위험을 무릅썼지. 그래서 나는 영국으로 이주해 기다리라는 명을 받았네. 나는 이주했고, 기다려왔고, 지금도 기다리고 있네. 내일 부름을 받을 수도 있고, 십 년 뒤에 부름을 받을 수도 있지. 내게는 매한가지네. 나는 여기 있고, 가르치는 일로 먹고살며, 기다리네. 내가 속한 결사의 이름을 자네에게 말해줌으로써 내 고백을 온전한 것으로 만든다 해도 나는 아무 맹세도 어기는 것이 아닐세(그 까닭은 곧 듣게 될 걸세). 내가 하는 일이란 내 목숨을 자네 손에 맡기는 것뿐이지. 지금 자네에게 하는 이 말이 내 입에서 나왔다는 사실이 언젠가 다른 이들에게 알려진다면, 우리 둘이 여기 앉아 있는 것이 확실한 만큼이나 확실하게 나는 죽은 목숨이네.”\n\n그는 다음 말을 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 그렇게 전해 들은 그 비밀을 나는 지킨다. 그가 속한 결사는, 여기서 그 문제를 언급할 필요가 있는 몇 안 되는 자리에서 내가 그것을 “형제단”이라고만 불러도 이 지면의 목적에는 충분히 특정될 것이다.\n\n“형제단의 목적은,” 페스카가 말을 이었다. “간단히 말해 같은 종류의 다른 정치 결사들의 목적과 같네. 폭정을 무너뜨리고 인민의 권리를 세우는 것이지. 형제단의 원칙은 두 가지네. 한 사람의 삶이 이롭거나, 하다못해 해롭지만 않은 동안에는 그에게 그 삶을 누릴 권리가 있네. 그러나 그의 삶이 동료 인간들의 안녕을 해치는 순간부터 그는 그 권리를 잃으며, 그에게서 그 삶을 빼앗는 것은 죄가 아닐 뿐 아니라 분명한 공덕이 되네. 이 결사가 얼마나 끔찍한 억압과 고통의 정황 속에서 생겨났는지는 내가 말할 바가 아닐세. 자네들이 말할 바도 아니지. 자유를 쟁취한 지 하도 오래되어 그 쟁취 과정에서 자기들이 어떤 피를 흘렸고 어떤 극단으로 치달았는지 편리하게 잊어버린 자네들 영국 사람이, 노예가 된 나라의 미쳐버린 사람들을 세상에서 가장 참혹한 분노가 어디까지 몰아갈 수 있고 없는지를 말할 자격은 없네. 우리 영혼에 박힌 쇠는 자네들이 찾아낼 수 없을 만큼 깊이 들어가 있네. 망명자를 내버려두게! 그를 비웃고, 그를 의심하고, 그의 안에서 연기를 피우는 그 은밀한 자아—때로는 나 같은 사람의 일상적인 점잖음과 평온함 아래, 때로는 나만큼 운 좋지도 유순하지도 참을성 있지도 못한 사람들의 짓누르는 가난과 사나운 누추함 아래 도사린 그 자아—에 놀라 눈을 크게 뜨게. 그러나 우리를 심판하지는 말게! 자네들의 첫 번째 찰스 왕 시절이었다면 자네들도 우리를 공정하게 대할 수 있었을 걸세. 자네들 자신의 자유를 오래도록 누려온 안락함이 이제 자네들에게서 우리를 공정하게 대할 능력을 앗아가 버렸네.”\n\n그 말에는 그의 천성 깊은 곳의 온갖 감정이 죄다 표면으로 밀려 올라오는 듯했다. 평생 처음으로 그의 마음이 통째로 내게 쏟아져 나왔다. 그럼에도 그의 목소리는 여전히 높아지지 않았고, 자기가 내게 하고 있는 그 무서운 폭로에 대한 두려움도 여전히 그를 떠나지 않았다.\n\n“여기까지는,” 그가 다시 말을 이었다. “자네는 이 결사가 다른 결사들과 비슷하다고 생각하겠지. 그 목적이란 (자네들 영국식 견해로는) 무정부와 혁명이라고 말이야. 악한 왕이나 악한 대신의 목숨을 빼앗기를, 마치 그 둘이 기회가 닿는 대로 쏘아 죽여야 할 위험한 맹수라도 되는 양 한다고. 그건 인정하겠네. 그러나 형제단의 규율은 이 지상의 어떤 다른 정치 결사의 규율과도 같지 않네. 단원들은 서로를 알지 못하네. 이탈리아에 회장이 한 사람 있고, 국외에도 회장들이 있네. 그들 각자에게는 서기가 있지. 회장과 서기는 단원들을 알지만, 단원들끼리는 모두가 서로에게 낯선 사람일세. 시대의 정치적 필요나 결사의 내부적 필요에 따라 우두머리들이 서로를 알게 하는 것이 마땅하다고 볼 때까지는 말이네. 이런 안전장치가 있으므로 우리에게는 가입할 때 하는 맹세가 없네. 우리는 목숨이 다할 때까지 지워지지 않는, 우리 모두가 지니는 비밀 표식으로 형제단의 일원임이 확인되네. 우리는 각자 평범한 생업에 종사하되, 우리의 봉사가 필요할 경우에 대비해 일 년에 네 번 회장이나 서기에게 자신을 알리라는 지시를 받네. 우리가 형제단을 배신하거나 다른 이익에 봉사하여 형제단을 해칠 경우에는 형제단의 원칙에 따라 죽는다는 경고를 받네. 세상 반대편 끝에서 그 일격을 가하러 보내진 낯선 사람의 손에 죽거나, 아니면 서로 가깝게 지낸 그 모든 세월 내내 우리가 모르는 단원이었을 수도 있는 절친한 벗의 손에 죽는 것이지. 때로는 죽음이 늦춰지고, 때로는 배신 직후에 곧바로 닥치네. 우리의 첫째 임무는 기다릴 줄 아는 것이고, 둘째 임무는 명령이 떨어졌을 때 복종할 줄 아는 것이네. 우리 가운데 어떤 이는 평생을 기다리고도 필요해지지 않을 수 있네. 어떤 이는 가입한 바로 그날에 일에, 혹은 일의 준비에 불려 나갈 수도 있지. 나 자신은—자네가 아는 그 자그맣고 소탈하고 쾌활한 사람, 제 얼굴 둘레를 윙윙대는 파리를 잡겠다고 손수건 한 장 들어 올리는 일조차 좀처럼 하지 않을 이 사람은—젊은 시절에, 자네에게 이야기하지 않으려는 그토록 끔찍한 도발을 겪고서, 충동적으로 자살했을지도 모를 그런 충동으로 형제단에 들어갔네. 이제 나는 그 안에 남아 있어야 하네. 형편이 나아지고 장년의 냉정을 얻은 지금 내가 그것을 어떻게 생각하든, 그것은 죽는 날까지 나를 붙들고 있네. 아직 이탈리아에 있을 때 나는 서기로 뽑혔고, 그 시절의 단원들 가운데 우리 회장과 얼굴을 맞댄 사람은 모두 나와도 얼굴을 맞대었네.”\n\n나는 그의 말을 알아듣기 시작했다. 그의 예사롭지 않은 고백이 이제 어디를 향해 가고 있는지 보였던 것이다. 그는 잠시 말을 멈추고 나를 간절하게 지켜보았다. 내 마음속에 무엇이 지나가고 있는지 분명히 알아챘다 싶을 때까지 지켜보고 나서야 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n“자네는 이미 자네 나름의 결론을 내렸군.” 그가 말했다. “자네 얼굴에 그렇게 쓰여 있네. 내게는 아무 말도 말게. 자네 생각의 비밀에서 나를 빼놓아주게. 자네를 위해 나 자신을 바치는 마지막 희생을 한 번만 더 하게 해주고, 그런 다음 이 이야기는 끝내고 다시는 여기로 돌아오지 마세.”\n\n그는 대답하지 말라는 손짓을 하고, 일어서서 외투를 벗고 왼팔의 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올렸다.\n\n“이 고백을 온전히 하겠다고 자네에게 약속했지.” 그는 문 쪽을 경계하듯 바라보며 내 귀 가까이에서 속삭였다. “이 일이 어떻게 되든, 자네의 이해에 알아야 할 필요가 있는 것을 내가 감추었다고 나를 나무라지는 못할 걸세. 형제단은 평생 지워지지 않는 표식으로 단원을 확인한다고 내가 말했지. 그 자리와 거기 새겨진 표식을 자네 눈으로 직접 보게.”\n\n그는 맨팔을 들어 올려, 팔의 윗부분 높직한 곳 안쪽에 살 속 깊이 지져 새기고 선명한 핏빛으로 물들인 낙인을 보여주었다. 그 낙인이 나타내는 문양을 묘사하는 일은 삼가겠다. 그것이 둥근 모양이었고 실링 은화 하나로 완전히 덮일 만큼 작았다는 말이면 충분할 것이다.\n\n“이 자리에 이 표식이 지져진 사람은,” 그가 팔을 다시 덮으며 말했다. “형제단의 일원이네. 형제단을 배신한 사람은 그를 아는 우두머리들—회장이든 서기든—에게 조만간 발각되네. 그리고 우두머리들에게 발각된 사람은 죽은 목숨이네. 어떤 인간의 법도 그를 지켜주지 못하네. 자네가 보고 들은 것을 기억하게. 자네 좋을 대로 결론을 내리고, 자네 좋을 대로 행동하게. 그러나 하느님의 이름으로 부탁하건대, 자네가 무엇을 알아내든 무엇을 하든 내게는 아무 말도 말게! 생각만 해도 소름 끼치는 그 책임에서 나를 자유롭게 놔두게. 양심에 비추어 그것이 지금 내 책임이 아님을 나는 아네. 마지막으로 한 번 더 말하네. 신사로서의 명예를 걸고, 그리스도인으로서의 맹세를 걸고 말하건대, 만일 자네가 오페라 극장에서 가리킨 그 사람이 나를 안다면, 그는 내가 알아보지 못할 만큼 달라졌거나 그만큼 감쪽같이 변장한 것이네. 나는 그가 영국에서 무슨 일을 하는지 무슨 속셈인지 아무것도 모르네. 내가 아는 한 나는 오늘 밤 이전에 그를 본 적도, 그가 쓰는 이름을 들은 적도 없네. 더는 말하지 않겠네. 나를 잠시 혼자 있게 해주게, 월터. 나는 일어난 일에 압도되어 있고, 내가 한 말에 뒤흔들려 있네. 다음에 만날 때는 다시 예전의 나로 돌아가 있도록 애써보겠네.”\n\n그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉더니 내게서 몸을 돌리고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다. 나는 그를 방해하지 않으려고 문을 조용히 열고, 그가 듣든 말든 그의 뜻에 맡긴 채 나직한 목소리로 몇 마디 작별 인사를 했다.\n\n“오늘 밤의 일을 제 마음 깊은 곳에 간직하겠습니다.” 내가 말했다. “저에게 베풀어주신 그 믿음을 결코 후회하시게 하지 않겠습니다. 내일 찾아뵈어도 되겠습니까? 아홉 시쯤 일찍 와도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“그러게, 월터.” 그는 다정하게 나를 올려다보며, 이제는 오로지 우리 사이의 예전 관계로 되돌아가는 것만이 그의 걱정이라는 듯 다시 영어로 말했다. “내가 가르치는 제자들에게 가기 전에, 나의 소박한 아침식사 자리로 오게나.”\n\n“안녕히 주무세요, 페스카.”\n\n“잘 자게, 나의 친구.”\n\n# VI\n\n그 집 밖으로 나서자마자 내게 처음으로 확고해진 생각은, 방금 들은 정보에 따라 지체 없이 움직이는 것 말고는 다른 길이 없다는 것이었다. 그날 밤 안에 백작을 붙들어야 한다, 아침까지 미루기라도 하면 로라의 마지막 기회를 잃을 위험을 감수해야 한다. 나는 시계를 보았다. 열 시였다.\n\n백작이 극장을 떠난 목적에 대해서는 한 점의 의심도 내 마음을 스치지 않았다. 그날 저녁 그가 우리에게서 달아난 것은, 두말할 나위 없이 런던에서 달아나기 위한 준비 단계일 뿐이었다. 형제단의 표식이 그의 팔에 있었다. 그가 직접 낙인을 보여 준 것이나 다름없이 나는 확신했다. 그리고 형제단에 대한 배신이 그의 양심 위에 얹혀 있었다. 나는 그것을 그가 페스카를 알아보는 눈빛에서 보았다.\n\n어째서 그 알아봄이 서로 오가지 않았는지는 쉽게 이해할 수 있었다. 백작 같은 성격의 사내라면 밀고자가 되는 무서운 결과를 무릅쓰면서, 황금 같은 보수를 챙기는 만큼이나 제 신변의 안전도 세심하게 챙기지 않았을 리 없다. 내가 오페라 극장에서 가리켜 보인 그 말끔히 면도한 얼굴은 페스카가 알던 시절에는 수염에 덮여 있었을지도 모른다. 짙은 갈색 머리카락은 가발일 수도 있다. 이름은 두말할 것 없이 가명이었다. 세월이라는 우연도 그를 도왔을 것이다. 그 어마어마한 비대함은 나중에 나이가 들면서 생긴 것일 수도 있었다. 페스카가 그를 다시 알아보지 못한 데에는 온갖 이유가 있었고, 반대로 그가 페스카를 알아보았을 데에도 온갖 이유가 있었다. 페스카의 유별난 생김새는 어디를 가든 그를 눈에 띄는 사람으로 만들어 놓았으니까.\n\n극장에서 우리를 따돌렸을 때 백작의 마음속에 있던 목적을 나는 확신했다고 앞서 말했다. 어찌 의심할 수 있었겠는가. 그가 겉모습을 바꾸었음에도 페스카에게 정체를 들켰다고 스스로 믿고 있으며, 그래서 제 목숨이 위태롭다고 여기고 있음을 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았는데. 만일 내가 그날 밤 그와 말을 나눌 수 있다면, 그가 처한 치명적인 위험을 나 또한 알고 있음을 그에게 내보일 수 있다면, 어떤 결과가 따라올 것인가. 뻔한 일이다. 우리 둘 가운데 하나가 상황의 주인이 되고, 우리 둘 가운데 하나는 어쩔 수 없이 상대의 처분에 맡겨진다.\n\n맞서기 전에 내게 불리한 승산부터 헤아려 보는 것이 나 자신에 대한 도리였다. 위험을 줄이기 위해 내 힘이 미치는 모든 일을 하는 것이 아내에 대한 도리였다.\n\n내게 불리한 승산은 굳이 셈해 볼 것도 없었다. 모두가 한 가지로 모아졌다. 만일 백작이 내 입으로 하는 고백을 통해 제가 안전해지는 가장 곧은 길이 내 목숨을 지나간다는 사실을 알아차린다면, 나를 혼자 제 손이 닿는 곳에 두고서 내 방심을 틈타 그 길로 나아가기를 주저할 사람은 이 세상에 그가 마지막일 것이다. 위험을 줄이는 데 조금이라도 기댈 만한 방어 수단은, 잠시 신중히 궁리해 보니 제법 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 그의 면전에서 내가 알아낸 것을 스스로 인정하기 전에, 그 알아낸 내용 자체를 그에게 즉시 쓸 수 있도록 준비해 두되 그가 어떻게든 뭉개려 해도 안전한 곳에 놓아두어야 한다. 그에게 다가가기 전에 그의 발밑에 지뢰를 묻어 두고, 정해진 시각이 지나기 전까지 내 손으로 쓴 글이나 내 입으로 하는 말로 취소 지시가 오지 않으면 그것을 터뜨리라고 제삼자에게 일러 둔다면, 그때 백작의 안전은 전적으로 내 안전에 달리게 되고, 나는 그의 집 안에서도 든든하게 우위를 쥘 수 있을 것이다.\n\n이 생각이 떠오른 것은 바닷가에서 돌아온 뒤 우리가 얻은 새 하숙집 가까이에 이르렀을 때였다. 나는 열쇠를 써서 아무도 깨우지 않고 안으로 들어갔다. 현관에 등불이 하나 켜져 있기에 그것을 들고 살그머니 내 작업실로 올라가, 준비를 갖추고 백작과의 면담에 돌이킬 수 없이 나 자신을 묶어 두기로 했다. 로라도 매리언도 내가 무엇을 하려는지 조금도 눈치채지 못하게 하려면 그래야 했다.\n\n페스카 앞으로 쓰는 편지가 그때 내가 취할 수 있는 가장 확실한 예방책이었다. 나는 이렇게 썼다.\n\n“내가 오페라 극장에서 자네에게 가리켜 보인 사내는 형제단의 일원이며, 자신이 맡은 바를 배신했다. 이 두 가지 주장을 지금 당장 시험해 보게. 그가 영국에서 쓰는 이름은 자네도 안다. 주소는 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지. 자네가 한때 내게 품었던 우정을 걸고, 자네에게 맡겨진 권한을 그 사내에게 자비 없이, 지체 없이 행사해 주게. 나는 모든 것을 걸었고 모든 것을 잃었다. 그리고 내 실패의 대가는 내 목숨으로 치렀다.”\n\n나는 이 글에 서명하고 날짜를 적어 봉투에 넣고 봉인했다. 겉면에는 이렇게 지시를 써 두었다. “내일 아침 아홉 시까지 안의 편지를 뜯지 말 것. 그 시각 전에 내 소식을 듣거나 나를 보지 못하거든, 시계가 아홉 시를 칠 때 봉인을 뜯고 내용을 읽을 것.” 거기에 내 머리글자를 덧붙이고, 그 전부를 다시 봉인한 두 번째 봉투에 넣어 페스카의 하숙집 주소로 앞면을 적어 안전하게 만들었다.\n\n이제 남은 일은 편지를 즉시 목적지로 보낼 방법을 찾는 것뿐이었다. 그러고 나면 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 일은 다 한 셈이었다. 백작의 집에서 내게 무슨 일이 생긴다면, 그 값을 그가 제 목숨으로 치르도록 나는 이미 마련해 둔 것이다.\n\n어떤 사정에서든 그의 도주를 막을 수단이 페스카의 손안에 있다는 것, 그가 마음만 먹으면 그 수단을 쓸 수 있다는 것을 나는 한순간도 의심하지 않았다. 백작의 정체에 대해서는 끝까지 모르는 채로 있고 싶다고, 다시 말해 가만히 있는 자신을 제 양심 앞에서 정당화할 만큼은 사실을 모르는 채로 남겨 달라고 그가 그토록 애타게 청했던 것은, 형제단의 무서운 심판을 집행할 수단이 그의 손안에 갖추어져 있음을 뚜렷이 드러내는 일이었다. 다만 천성이 인정 많은 사람인지라 내 앞에서 그것을 대놓고 말하기를 꺼렸을 뿐이다. 외국의 정치 결사들이 대의를 배신한 자를 어디에 숨든 기어이 찾아내어 응징하는 그 죽음처럼 확실한 솜씨는, 내 얕은 경험 안에서도 너무 여러 번 실례가 있었기에 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그저 신문을 읽는 사람으로서 이 문제를 떠올려 보아도, 런던에서든 파리에서든 거리에서 칼에 찔린 채 발견되었으나 그 자객은 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 외국인들의 사건, 누구의 손인지 결코 알아낼 수 없는 손에 의해 템스강과 센강에 던져진 시신과 시신 조각들, 오직 한 가지로밖에는 설명할 길이 없는 은밀한 폭력에 의한 죽음들이 기억에 되살아났다. 나는 이 기록에서 나 자신에 관한 어떤 것도 감추지 않았으며, 여기서도 감추지 않겠다. 페스카가 내 봉투를 뜯을 권한을 갖게 되는 그 치명적인 사태가 벌어진다면, 나는 방금 포스코 백작의 사형 집행 영장을 써 놓은 것이라고 나는 믿고 있었다.\n\n나는 방을 나와 아래층으로 내려갔다. 집주인에게 심부름꾼을 하나 구해 달라고 부탁하기 위해서였다. 마침 그가 계단을 올라오던 참이어서 우리는 층계참에서 마주쳤다. 내 용건을 듣고 그가 내놓은 심부름꾼은 제 아들이었는데, 눈치 빠른 아이였다. 우리는 소년을 위층으로 불러올렸고 나는 그에게 지시를 일러 주었다. 삯마차를 타고 편지를 가져가 페스카 교수의 손에 직접 건네고, 그분에게서 받았다는 한 줄을 받아 오라고, 그리고 그 마차를 타고 돌아와 내가 쓸 수 있도록 문 앞에 세워 두라고 했다. 그때가 열 시 반이 다 되어 가는 시각이었다. 나는 소년이 이십 분이면 돌아올 수 있고, 그가 돌아오면 다시 이십 분이면 세인트존스우드까지 갈 수 있으리라고 셈했다.\n\n소년이 심부름을 떠난 뒤, 나는 잠시 내 방으로 돌아가 서류 몇 가지를 정리해 두었다. 최악의 경우에 쉽게 찾을 수 있게 하기 위해서였다. 서류를 넣어 둔 구식 문갑의 열쇠는 봉해서 탁자 위에 두었고, 그 작은 꾸러미 겉면에는 매리언의 이름을 적어 두었다. 그러고 나서 나는 아래층 거실로 내려갔다. 로라와 매리언이 오페라에서 돌아올 나를 기다리고 있으리라 여긴 방이었다. 문의 손잡이에 손을 얹는 순간, 나는 처음으로 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n방에는 매리언밖에 없었다. 그녀는 책을 읽고 있다가, 내가 들어서자 놀라서 시계를 보았다.\n\n“어쩜 이렇게 일찍 오셨어요!” 그녀가 말했다. “오페라가 끝나기도 전에 나오신 게 분명하군요.”\n\n“그래.” 내가 대답했다. “페스카도 나도 끝까지 기다리지 않았어. 로라는 어디 있지?”\n\n“오늘 저녁에 그 지독한 두통이 또 도져서, 차를 마시고 나서 자리에 눕는 게 좋겠다고 제가 권했어요.”\n\n나는 로라가 잠들었는지 보고 싶다는 핑계를 대고 다시 방을 나섰다. 매리언의 재빠른 눈이 내 얼굴을 캐묻듯 바라보기 시작했고, 매리언의 재빠른 직감이 내 마음에 무언가 얹혀 있음을 알아채기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n침실에 들어가 희미하게 일렁이는 취침등 불빛을 의지해 살며시 침대 곁으로 다가갔을 때, 아내는 잠들어 있었다.\n\n우리가 결혼한 지 아직 한 달도 채 되지 않았다. 잠결에도 내 베개 쪽으로 미더이 돌린 그 얼굴을 바라보았을 때, 마치 무의식중에 내 손을 기다리기라도 하는 듯 이불 위에 펼쳐 놓인 그 손을 보았을 때, 내 가슴이 무겁고 내 결심이 한순간 다시 흔들렸다 한들 그만한 변명은 있지 않겠는가. 나는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 가까이 들여다보는 데 몇 분만을 허락했다. 오가는 숨결이 내 얼굴에 나부낄 만큼 가까이서. 헤어지며 그녀의 손과 뺨에 입술을 댔을 뿐이다. 그녀는 잠결에 뒤척이며 내 이름을 중얼거렸으나 깨지는 않았다. 나는 문가에서 잠시 머뭇거리며 그녀를 다시 한번 바라보았다. “하느님이 그대를 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를, 나의 사랑.” 나는 속삭이고 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n매리언이 계단 위에서 나를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀의 손에는 접힌 종잇조각이 들려 있었다.\n\n“집주인 아들이 이걸 가져왔어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “문 앞에 삯마차를 세워 두었대요. 오라버니가 대기시켜 두라고 하셨다더군요.”\n\n“잘했군, 매리언. 그 마차가 필요해. 나는 다시 나가야 해.”\n\n말하면서 나는 계단을 내려갔고, 탁자 위의 불빛으로 그 종잇조각을 읽으려고 거실을 들여다보았다. 페스카의 필체로 두 문장이 적혀 있었다.\n\n“자네 편지 받았네. 자네가 말한 시각까지 자네를 보지 못하면, 시계가 그 시각을 칠 때 봉인을 뜯겠네.”\n\n나는 그 종이를 수첩에 끼워 넣고 문 쪽으로 향했다. 매리언이 문지방에서 나를 맞아 방 안으로 밀어 넣었고, 촛불이 내 얼굴을 환히 비추었다. 그녀는 두 손으로 나를 붙잡고, 그 눈으로 내 눈을 파고들듯 응시했다.\n\n“알겠어요!” 그녀가 낮고 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다. “오늘 밤 마지막 기회를 걸어 보시려는 거군요.”\n\n“그래, 마지막 기회이자 가장 좋은 기회지.” 나도 속삭여 대답했다.\n\n“혼자는 안 돼요! 오, 월터, 제발 혼자는 안 돼요! 저도 함께 가게 해 주세요. 제가 한낱 여자라는 이유로 거절하지 마세요. 저는 가야 해요! 저는 가겠어요! 밖에서 마차 안에 앉아 기다릴게요!”\n\n이번에는 내가 그녀를 붙들 차례였다. 그녀는 내게서 벗어나 먼저 문으로 내려가려 했다.\n\n“나를 돕고 싶거든,” 내가 말했다. “여기 남아서 오늘 밤 아내 방에서 함께 자 줘. 로라 걱정만 놓고 떠날 수 있게 해 주면, 나머지는 다 내가 책임지마. 자, 매리언, 입 맞춰 주고, 내가 돌아올 때까지 기다릴 용기가 있다는 걸 보여 다오.”\n\n나는 그녀에게 한마디라도 더 할 틈을 감히 주지 않았다. 그녀가 다시 나를 붙들려 했다. 나는 그 손을 풀고 순식간에 방을 빠져나왔다. 아래층의 소년이 계단에서 나는 소리를 듣고 현관문을 열어 주었다. 마부가 마부석에서 내리기도 전에 나는 마차에 뛰어올랐다. “세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드로.” 나는 앞창 너머로 그에게 외쳤다. “십오 분 안에 닿으면 삯을 두 배로 주겠소.” “해 보입지요, 나리.” 나는 시계를 보았다. 열한 시. 일 분도 허비할 수 없었다.\n\n마차의 빠른 움직임, 이제 매 순간이 나를 백작에게 가까이 데려가고 있다는 느낌, 마침내 아무 방해도 걸림도 없이 이 위태로운 일에 몸을 실었다는 확신이 나를 열병 같은 흥분으로 달구어, 나는 마부에게 더 빨리, 더 빨리 달리라고 소리쳤다. 시가지를 벗어나 세인트존스우드 로드를 가로지를 무렵에는 조바심이 나를 완전히 짓눌러, 나는 마차 안에 일어서서 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 아직 닿지도 않은 여정의 끝을 미리 보려 했다. 멀리서 교회 시계가 십오 분을 알리는 그때, 우리는 포리스트 로드로 접어들었다. 나는 백작의 집에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 마차를 세우게 하고, 삯을 치러 돌려보낸 뒤 그 집 문까지 걸어갔다.\n\n정원 문에 다가가는데, 나와 반대쪽 방향에서 또 한 사람이 그리로 다가오는 것이 보였다. 우리는 길가의 가스등 아래에서 마주쳐 서로를 쳐다보았다. 나는 뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 금발의 외국인을 대번에 알아보았고, 그도 나를 알아보았다고 생각했다. 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나처럼 그 집 앞에 멈추는 대신 천천히 걸어 지나갔다. 그가 포리스트 로드에 있는 것은 우연이었을까? 아니면 오페라 극장에서부터 백작을 뒤따라 집까지 온 것일까?\n\n나는 그 의문들을 좇지 않았다. 그 외국인이 천천히 걸어 시야에서 사라질 때까지 잠시 기다린 뒤, 나는 문의 초인종을 울렸다. 그때가 열한 시 이십 분이었다. 백작이 잠자리에 들었다는 핑계로 나를 손쉽게 물리칠 수 있을 만큼 늦은 시각이었다.\n\n그런 사태에 대비하는 유일한 길은, 미리 아무것도 묻지 않고 내 이름부터 들여보내면서, 동시에 이렇게 늦은 시각에 그를 만나려는 데에는 심상찮은 이유가 있음을 알리는 것이었다. 그래서 기다리는 동안 나는 명함을 꺼내 이름 아래에 “긴한 용무로”라고 적었다. 마지막 글자를 연필로 쓰고 있을 때 하녀가 문을 열더니, 미심쩍은 얼굴로 “무슨 볼일이시냐”고 물었다.\n\n“수고스럽겠지만 이것을 주인어른께 갖다 드리게.” 나는 명함을 건네며 대답했다.\n\n그 아가씨가 머뭇거리는 태도로 보아, 내가 처음부터 백작을 뵙자고 청했더라면 그녀는 지시받은 대로 주인이 댁에 안 계신다고 말했으리라는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 내가 명함을 건네는 그 자신만만한 태도에 흔들렸다. 몹시 당황한 얼굴로 나를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그녀는 내 전갈을 들고 집 안으로 들어가면서 문을 닫아 버렸고, 나는 정원에 남아 기다리게 되었다.\n\n한 일 분쯤 지나 그녀가 다시 나타났다. “주인어른께서 인사 전하시며, 무슨 용무이신지 말씀해 주시면 고맙겠다고 하십니다.” “내 인사도 전해 드리고,” 내가 대답했다. “주인어른 말고 다른 어느 누구에게도 말할 수 없는 용무라고 여쭈게.” 그녀는 다시 나를 두고 갔다가 다시 돌아왔고, 이번에는 안으로 드시라고 청했다.\n\n나는 곧바로 그녀를 따라갔다. 다음 순간 나는 백작의 집 안에 들어와 있었다.\n\n# VII\n\n현관에는 등불이 없었으나, 하녀가 들고 올라온 부엌 촛불의 희미한 빛으로 나는 나이 지긋한 부인 하나가 아래층 안쪽 방에서 소리 없이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다. 내가 현관에 들어서자 그 부인은 독사 같은 눈길을 한 번 던졌을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 내 인사에 답하지도 않은 채 천천히 위층으로 올라갔다. 매리언의 일기를 속속들이 알고 있던 나로서는 그 나이 든 부인이 포스코 백작부인이라는 것을 알아보기에 충분했다.\n\n하녀는 백작부인이 방금 나온 방으로 나를 안내했다. 나는 그 방에 들어섰고, 백작과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다.\n\n그는 아직 야회복 차림이었는데, 상의만은 벗어서 의자에 걸쳐 두고 있었다. 셔츠 소매는 손목께까지 걷어 올렸을 뿐 그 위로는 올리지 않았다. 한쪽 옆에는 융단 여행 가방이, 다른 쪽에는 궤짝이 놓여 있었다. 책과 서류와 옷가지가 방 안에 어질러져 있었다. 문 한쪽 탁자 위에는 이야기로만 익히 들어 알고 있던 그 새장이 놓여 있었고, 그 안에는 그의 흰 쥐들이 들어 있었다. 카나리아와 코카투는 아마 다른 방에 있는 모양이었다. 내가 들어갔을 때 그는 궤짝 앞에 앉아 짐을 꾸리는 중이었는데, 서류 몇 장을 손에 든 채 일어서서 나를 맞았다. 그의 얼굴에는 오페라 극장에서 그를 덮쳤던 충격의 흔적이 아직도 뚜렷이 남아 있었다. 살진 두 볼은 축 늘어졌고, 차가운 잿빛 눈은 남몰래 경계하고 있었으며, 목소리도 표정도 태도도 하나같이 날카롭게 의심에 차 있었다. 그는 나를 맞으러 한 걸음 나서더니, 서먹한 예의를 갖추어 앉으라고 권했다.\n\n“업무차 오셨습니까, 선생?” 그가 말했다. “대체 무슨 업무인지 나로서는 도무지 짐작이 가지 않는군요.”\n\n그가 말하면서 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보는 그 감추지 않은 호기심으로 미루어, 나는 오페라 극장에서 그가 나를 알아보지 못하고 지나쳤음을 확신했다. 그는 페스카를 먼저 보았고, 그 순간부터 극장을 떠날 때까지 다른 것은 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았던 것이 분명했다. 내 이름을 듣고 나면 내가 그에게 적의를 품고 이 집에 들어왔다는 것쯤은 당연히 짐작하겠지만, 그때까지 그는 내가 찾아온 진짜 용건이 무엇인지는 전혀 모르는 눈치였다.\n\n“오늘 밤 여기서 백작을 만나게 되어 다행입니다.” 내가 말했다. “막 여행을 떠나려던 참인 것 같군요?”\n\n“당신 용건이 내 여행과 관계있는 일이오?”\n\n“어느 정도는 그렇습니다.”\n\n“어느 정도라니? 내가 어디로 가는지 아시오?”\n\n“모릅니다. 다만 당신이 왜 런던을 떠나는지는 압니다.”\n\n그는 생각처럼 빠르게 내 옆을 스쳐 지나가 문을 잠그고, 열쇠를 자기 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 당신과 나는 서로에 대해 소문으로 아주 잘 알고 있지요.” 그가 말했다. “혹시 이 집에 들어올 때, 내가 함부로 놀려 볼 만한 사람이 아니라는 생각은 해 보셨소?”\n\n“해 봤습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “그리고 나는 당신을 놀리러 온 것이 아닙니다. 나는 생사가 걸린 일로 여기 왔고, 당신이 잠근 저 문이 지금 이 순간 열려 있다 해도, 당신이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 나를 저 문 밖으로 내보내지는 못할 겁니다.”\n\n나는 방 안쪽으로 더 걸어 들어가, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 그와 마주 보고 섰다. 그는 문 앞으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 거기에 앉아 왼팔을 탁자에 얹었다. 흰 쥐가 든 새장이 그의 바로 곁에 있었는데, 그의 묵직한 팔이 탁자를 흔들자 작은 것들이 잠자리에서 뛰쳐나와 산뜻하게 칠한 철사 틈으로 그를 내다보았다.\n\n“생사가 걸린 일이라.” 그가 혼잣말처럼 되뇌었다. “그 말은 아마 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 무거운 말일 거요. 무슨 뜻이오?”\n\n“말 그대로입니다.”\n\n그의 넓은 이마에 굵은 땀방울이 배어 나왔다. 왼손이 탁자 가장자리로 슬며시 옮겨 갔다. 거기에는 자물쇠 달린 서랍이 있었고, 열쇠가 그 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있었다. 그의 엄지와 검지가 열쇠를 감쌌지만, 돌리지는 않았다.\n\n“그러니까 내가 왜 런던을 떠나는지 안다는 말이지요?” 그가 말을 이었다. “그 이유를 말해 보시오.” 그렇게 말하면서 그는 열쇠를 돌려 서랍을 열었다.\n\n“말보다 더 나은 것을 할 수 있습니다.” 내가 대답했다. “원하신다면 그 이유를 보여 드리지요.”\n\n“어떻게 보여 준다는 거요?”\n\n“상의를 벗고 계시는군요.” 내가 말했다. “왼팔의 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올려 보십시오. 거기 있을 겁니다.”\n\n극장에서 그의 얼굴에 스치는 것을 보았던 그 납빛의 창백한 변화가 다시 한번 지나갔다. 그의 두 눈에 어린 살기 어린 광채가 흔들림 없이 곧장 내 눈을 찔러 왔다. 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 왼손이 천천히 탁자 서랍을 열고 소리 없이 그 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 내 눈에 보이지 않는 무거운 무언가를 움직이는 거친 마찰음이 잠깐 들리다가 그쳤다. 뒤이은 정적이 어찌나 깊던지, 흰 쥐들이 철사를 갉는 희미한 소리가 내가 선 자리에서도 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n내 목숨은 실오라기 하나에 걸려 있었고, 나도 그것을 알고 있었다. 그 마지막 순간에 나는 그의 머리로 생각했고 그의 손가락으로 느꼈다. 그가 서랍 속에 감추어 두고 내게 보이지 않는 것이 무엇인지, 나는 직접 눈으로 본 것이나 다름없이 확신했다.\n\n“잠깐 기다리십시오.” 내가 말했다. “문은 당신이 잠갔습니다—보시다시피 나는 움직이지 않습니다—보시다시피 내 손은 비어 있습니다. 잠깐만 기다리십시오. 할 말이 더 있습니다.”\n\n“할 말은 충분히 하셨소.” 그가 대답했다. 그 갑작스러운 침착함은 어찌나 부자연스럽고 소름 끼치던지, 어떤 격렬한 폭발도 그만큼 내 신경을 시험하지는 못했을 것이다. “잠깐 내 생각을 정리할 시간을 주시오. 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 짐작이 가시오?”\n\n“아마도 갑니다.”\n\n“나는 지금,” 그가 조용히 말했다. “당신 골을 벽난로 앞에 흩뿌려서 이 방의 어지러움을 조금 더 보태 줄까 생각하는 중이오.”\n\n그 순간 내가 조금이라도 움직였다면 그는 정말로 그렇게 했으리라는 것을, 나는 그의 얼굴에서 읽었다.\n\n“그 문제를 최종적으로 결정하시기 전에,” 내가 받아 말했다. “내가 지니고 있는 두 줄짜리 글을 읽어 보시기를 권합니다.”\n\n그 제안은 그의 호기심을 자극한 듯했다. 그가 고개를 끄덕였다. 나는 내 편지를 받았다는 페스카의 회신을 수첩에서 꺼내 팔을 뻗어 그에게 건네고, 벽난로 앞의 원래 자리로 돌아왔다.\n\n그는 그 두 줄을 소리 내어 읽었다. “편지 받았소. 당신이 말한 시각까지 당신에게서 소식이 없으면, 시계가 칠 때 봉인을 뜯겠소.”\n\n그의 처지에 있는 다른 사람이라면 그 말에 대해 얼마간 설명을 요구했을 것이다—백작은 그럴 필요를 느끼지 않았다. 그 쪽지를 한 번 읽는 것만으로, 그는 내가 취해 둔 대비책을 마치 내가 그 대비를 하던 자리에 함께 있었기라도 한 듯 환히 알아차렸다. 그의 얼굴빛이 그 즉시 달라졌고, 서랍에서 나온 손은 비어 있었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 나는 내 서랍을 잠그지 않겠소.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 당신 골을 벽난로 앞에 흩뿌리는 일이 아직 없으리라고 말하지도 않겠소. 하지만 나는 적에게도 공정한 사람이오. 그러니 미리 인정해 두지요—당신 머리는 내가 생각했던 것보다 영리한 머리요. 본론으로 들어갑시다, 선생! 내게서 무언가 원하는 게 있소?”\n\n“있습니다. 그리고 반드시 얻어 낼 작정입니다.”\n\n“조건부로?”\n\n“아무 조건 없이.”\n\n그의 손이 다시 서랍 속으로 떨어졌다.\n\n“쳇! 우리는 제자리를 맴돌고 있군.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 당신의 그 영리한 머리는 또다시 위험에 처했소. 당신 말투는 한심할 만큼 경솔하오, 선생—당장 누그러뜨리시오! 내가 지시하고 승낙한 조건 없이 당신을 이 집에서 내보내는 위험에 비하면, 당신이 서 있는 그 자리에서 당신을 쏘아 버리는 위험쯤은 내게 아무것도 아니오. 지금 당신이 상대하는 것은 애도해 마지않는 내 친구가 아니오—당신은 포스코와 얼굴을 마주하고 있소! 하트라이트 씨 스무 명의 목숨이 내 안전으로 가는 징검돌이라면, 나는 그 돌을 모조리 밟고 건너갈 것이오. 나의 숭고한 무심함에 떠받쳐지고, 나의 뚫을 수 없는 평정으로 균형을 잡은 채 말이오. 당신 목숨이 아깝거든 나를 존중하시오! 다시 입을 열기 전에 세 가지 질문에 답할 것을 요구하오. 들으시오—이 면담에 필요한 질문들이오. 답하시오—내게 필요한 것들이오.” 그는 오른손 손가락 하나를 세웠다. “첫 번째 질문!” 그가 말했다. “당신은 참일 수도 있고 거짓일 수도 있는 정보를 가지고 여기 왔소—그것을 어디서 얻었소?”\n\n“말하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“상관없소—내가 알아낼 테니. 만일 그 정보가 참이라면—내 결심의 온 힘을 다해 말하건대, 만일이오—당신은 당신 자신의 배신이든 다른 누군가의 배신이든 그것을 밑천 삼아 여기서 값을 흥정하고 있는 것이오. 나는 그 정황을 뒷날 쓰기 위해 내 기억에 새겨 두겠소. 내 기억은 무엇 하나 잊는 법이 없소. 그러면 다음으로 넘어가지요.” 그는 손가락을 하나 더 세웠다. “두 번째 질문! 당신이 읽어 보라고 준 그 두 줄에는 서명이 없소. 누가 썼소?”\n\n“내가 믿을 만한 온갖 이유가 있는 사람, 그리고 당신이 두려워할 만한 온갖 이유가 있는 사람입니다.”\n\n내 대답은 얼마간 효과가 있었다. 서랍 속에서 그의 왼손이 소리가 들릴 만큼 떨렸다.\n\n“시계가 치고 봉인이 뜯길 때까지,” 그가 세 번째 질문을 한결 낮은 어조로 던지며 물었다. “내게 얼마나 시간을 주겠소?”\n\n“당신이 내 조건을 받아들이기에 충분한 시간입니다.” 내가 대답했다.\n\n“더 분명히 대답하시오, 하트라이트 씨. 그 시계가 몇 시를 치오?”\n\n“내일 아침 9시입니다.”\n\n“내일 아침 9시라? 그렇지, 그렇지—내가 여권 수속을 마치고 런던을 떠나기도 전에 덫을 놓아 두었군. 그보다 이르지는 않겠지요? 그건 곧 알아보기로 합시다—나는 당신을 여기 인질로 잡아 둘 수도 있고, 당신을 놓아주기 전에 사람을 보내 그 편지를 가져오게 하라고 흥정할 수도 있으니까. 그건 그렇고, 이제 당신 조건을 말해 보시오.”\n\n“말씀드리지요. 간단하고, 곧 끝납니다. 내가 여기 오면서 누구의 이익을 대변하고 있는지 아십니까?”\n\n그는 더할 나위 없이 침착한 미소를 지으며 오른손을 무심히 저었다.\n\n“한번 맞혀 보기로 하지요.” 그가 비웃듯 말했다. “물론 어느 부인의 이익이겠지!”\n\n“내 아내의 이익입니다.”\n\n그는 내 앞에서 처음으로 꾸밈없는 표정을 지으며 나를 바라보았다—그것은 완전한 경악의 표정이었다. 그 순간부터 위험한 인물로서의 내 값이 그의 눈에서 뚝 떨어지는 것이 보였다. 그는 즉시 서랍을 닫고, 두 팔을 가슴 위로 포개고, 빈정거리는 주의를 담은 미소를 띤 채 내 말에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n“지난 여러 달 동안 내 조사가 어떤 길을 밟아 왔는지 당신도 충분히 알고 있을 테니,” 내가 말을 이었다. “내 앞에서 명백한 사실을 부인해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다는 것도 알 겁니다. 당신은 파렴치한 음모를 저지른 죄인입니다! 그리고 만 파운드의 재산을 손에 넣는 것이 그 동기였습니다.”\n\n그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 침울한 불안으로 어두워졌다.\n\n“그 돈은 당신이 가지십시오.” 내가 말했다. (그의 얼굴이 즉시 다시 밝아졌고, 두 눈이 점점 더 크게 놀라며 나를 향해 열렸다.) “나는 당신 손을 거쳐 간 돈, 비열한 범죄의 값이었던 돈을 흥정해서 스스로를 욕되게 하려고 여기 온 것이 아닙니다——”\n\n“진정하시오, 하트라이트 씨. 당신네 도덕적 상투어는 영국에서라면 아주 훌륭한 효과를 내지—부디 당신 자신과 당신 동포들에게나 쓰시오. 그 만 파운드는 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 나의 훌륭한 아내에게 남긴 유산이오. 그 문제를 그런 토대 위에 올려놓는다면 원하는 대로 논해 보겠소. 그러나 나 같은 감정을 지닌 사람에게 그 화제는 한심할 만큼 천박하오. 그냥 넘어가는 편이 좋겠소. 당신 조건 이야기로 돌아갑시다. 무엇을 요구하시오?”\n\n“첫째, 나는 그 음모에 관한 완전한 자백을 요구합니다. 내가 보는 앞에서 당신 손으로 쓰고 서명한 것이어야 합니다.”\n\n그는 다시 손가락을 세웠다. “하나!” 그가 말하며 실무가다운 차분한 주의력으로 내 요구를 하나씩 헤아렸다.\n\n“둘째, 나는 당신의 개인적인 단언에 기대지 않는 명백한 증거를 요구합니다. 내 아내가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 간 날짜에 관한 증거 말입니다.”\n\n“호오! 호오! 당신은 약한 지점에 손가락을 짚을 줄 아는군.” 그가 태연하게 말했다. “더 있소?”\n\n“지금으로서는 더 없습니다.”\n\n“좋소! 당신 조건은 들었으니, 이제 내 조건을 들으시오. 당신이 굳이 ‘음모’라 부르는 것을 인정함으로써 내가 지게 될 책임은, 아마도 전체적으로 보아 당신을 저 난롯가 깔개 위에 시체로 눕히는 책임보다는 가벼울 거요. 그러니 내가 당신 제안을 받아들이겠다고 해 둡시다—내 조건 아래서 말이오. 당신이 요구하는 진술서는 작성될 것이고, 명백한 증거도 제시될 거요. 애석하게도 고인이 된 내 친구가 자기 아내가 런던에 도착하는 날짜와 시각을 알려 온 편지, 그가 손수 쓰고 서명하고 날짜를 적은 그 편지라면 증거가 되겠지요? 그건 내가 줄 수 있소. 또 그 여인이 도착하던 날 철도역에서 내 손님을 데려오도록 내가 마차를 빌렸던 사람에게 당신을 보낼 수도 있소—그 사람의 예약 장부가 날짜를 찾는 데 도움이 될 거요. 설령 나를 태우고 몰았던 마부가 아무 쓸모 없는 것으로 드러나더라도 말이오. 이런 일들을 나는 할 수 있고, 또 하겠소—조건부로. 그 조건을 읊어 보겠소. 첫째 조건! 포스코 부인과 나는 우리가 원하는 때에 원하는 방식으로 이 집을 떠나며, 당신은 어떤 식으로도 방해하지 않는다. 둘째 조건! 당신은 나와 함께 여기서 기다려, 아침 7시에 내 일을 정리하러 오는 내 대리인을 만난다. 당신은 그 대리인에게, 당신의 봉인된 편지를 가지고 있는 사람 앞으로 그 편지를 내놓으라는 서면 지시를 써 준다. 당신은 내 대리인이 그 편지를 뜯지 않은 채로 내 손에 놓을 때까지 여기서 기다리고, 그런 뒤 내가 이 집을 떠날 수 있도록 꼬박 30분을 준다—그다음에는 당신의 행동의 자유를 되찾아 어디로든 가면 되오. 셋째 조건! 당신은 내 사사로운 일에 끼어든 것과, 이 자리에서 감히 내게 쓴 언사에 대하여 신사로서의 결투를 내게 허락한다. 시간과 장소는 국외로 하되, 내가 대륙에서 안전해졌을 때 내 손으로 쓴 편지로 정해 알리겠소. 그리고 그 편지에는 내 검의 길이를 정확히 잰 종잇조각이 들어 있을 거요. 이것이 내 조건이오. 받아들이는지 아닌지 알려 주시오—예스요, 노요.”\n\n이 말 속에 뒤섞인 즉각적인 결단과 멀리 내다보는 교활함과 협잡꾼 같은 허세의 그 기묘한 배합은 나를 한순간 휘청이게 했다—그러나 한순간뿐이었다. 고려할 문제는 오직 하나였다. 로라의 신원을 밝힐 수단을 손에 넣기 위하여, 그 신원을 그녀에게서 빼앗은 악당이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 내 손을 벗어나도록 놓아주는 것이 과연 정당한가 하는 것이었다. 사기꾼으로 몰려 쫓겨났던 고향 땅에서 내 아내가 정당한 인정을 되찾게 하고, 아직도 그 어머니의 묘비를 더럽히고 있는 거짓말을 세상 앞에서 지우려는 동기가, 처음부터 내 목적에 섞여 들어 있던 복수의 동기보다 훨씬 순수하며 악한 정념의 얼룩이 없다는 것을 나는 알고 있었다. 그러면서도 솔직히 말해, 내 안의 싸움을 그 도덕적 확신만으로 판가름 낼 만큼 그것이 강했다고는 말할 수 없다. 거기에는 퍼시벌 경의 죽음에 대한 기억이 힘을 보탰다. 그 마지막 순간에, 그곳에서 응보의 손길은 얼마나 무섭게도 내 힘없는 손에서 낚아채였던가! 미래에 대해 아무것도 모르는 가련한 인간의 무지 속에서, 이 사람 역시 내게서 벗어났으니 아무 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나가리라고 단정할 권리가 내게 무엇이 있단 말인가? 나는 그런 것들을 생각했다—어쩌면 내 천성에 배어 있는 미신으로, 어쩌면 미신보다는 나에게 더 어울리는 감각으로. 마침내 그를 붙잡았는데 내 발로 그 손을 다시 푸는 것은 괴로운 일이었다—그러나 나는 그 희생을 치르도록 나 자신을 몰아세웠다. 더 쉽게 말하자면, 나는 내가 확신하는 단 하나의 더 높은 동기, 곧 로라의 대의와 진실의 대의에 봉사한다는 동기를 따르기로 결심했다.\n\n“당신 조건을 받아들이겠습니다.” 내가 말했다. “다만 내 쪽에서 한 가지 유보를 두겠습니다.”\n\n“그 유보란 무엇이오?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“봉인된 편지에 관한 것입니다.” 내가 대답했다. “그 편지가 당신 손에 들어오는 즉시, 내가 보는 앞에서 뜯지 않은 채로 없애기를 요구합니다.”\n\n내가 이 조항을 내건 목적은 단지, 내가 페스카와 주고받은 연락의 내용을 적은 증거를 그가 들고 가지 못하게 하려는 데 있었다. 내가 연락을 취했다는 사실 자체는 아침에 내가 그의 대리인에게 주소를 적어 주는 순간 그가 알아차릴 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 뒷받침할 것 없는 그 자신의 증언만으로는—설령 그가 정말로 그 모험을 해 볼 마음을 먹는다 해도—페스카를 두고 내가 조금이라도 불안해할 만한 쓸모를 얻어 낼 수는 없을 터였다.\n\n“당신 유보를 받아들이겠소.” 그는 그 문제를 일이 분 남짓 진지하게 헤아려 본 뒤에 대답했다. “다툴 값어치가 없는 일이오—그 편지는 내 손에 들어오는 대로 없애겠소.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그는 그때까지 나와 마주 보고 앉아 있던 의자에서 일어났다. 그는 단 한 번의 몸짓으로, 여기까지의 면담이 그의 마음에 지운 짐 전체를 훌훌 털어 버린 듯했다. “우프!” 그가 두 팔을 흐드러지게 뻗으며 외쳤다. “접전이 벌어지는 동안은 제법 뜨거웠소. 앉으시오, 하트라이트 씨. 앞으로 우리는 불구대천의 원수로 만나게 되겠지만—그때까지는 의젓한 신사답게 정중한 예를 주고받읍시다. 실례를 무릅쓰고 내 아내를 부르겠소.”\n\n그는 잠긴 문을 열고 활짝 열어젖혔다. “엘리너!” 그가 굵은 목소리로 불렀다. 독사 같은 얼굴의 부인이 들어왔다. “포스코 부인—하트라이트 씨요.” 백작이 여유로운 위엄을 갖추어 우리를 서로에게 소개했다. “나의 천사,” 그가 아내를 향해 말을 이었다. “짐 꾸리는 일 중에도 나를 위해 진하고 좋은 커피를 좀 끓여 줄 짬이 나겠소? 하트라이트 씨와 처리할 글 쓰는 일이 있는데—나 자신에게 부끄럽지 않으려면 내 지력을 온전히 지녀야 하거든.”\n\n포스코 부인은 두 번 고개를 숙였다—한 번은 나를 향해 엄하게, 한 번은 남편을 향해 순종적으로—그러고는 미끄러지듯 방을 나갔다.\n\n백작은 창가의 필기 탁자로 걸어가 책상을 열고, 종이 여러 첩과 깃펜 한 다발을 꺼냈다. 그는 펜을 탁자 위에 흩어 놓아 어느 방향에서든 필요할 때 곧바로 집을 수 있게 해 두고, 그런 다음 종이를 잘라 신문사에 글을 대는 문필가들이 쓰는 형태의 좁고 긴 쪽지 더미를 만들었다. “이것을 주목할 만한 문서로 만들어 보이겠소.” 그가 어깨 너머로 나를 돌아보며 말했다. “글을 짓는 습관이라면 나는 아주 익숙하오. 사람이 지닐 수 있는 온갖 지적 재능 가운데 가장 드문 것 하나가 자기 생각을 정연하게 배열하는 위대한 능력이오. 엄청난 특권이지! 나는 그것을 가지고 있소. 당신은 어떻소?”\n\n그는 커피가 나올 때까지 방 안을 왔다 갔다 행진하며 콧노래를 흥얼거렸고, 생각을 배열하는 데 걸림돌이 나타나는 대목마다 이따금 손바닥으로 자기 이마를 쳐서 표를 해 두었다. 내가 그를 몰아넣은 처지를 오히려 움켜쥐고서, 그것을 자기 과시라는 그 소중한 목적을 위해 허영심이 올라설 받침대로 삼는 그 엄청난 대담함은 힘으로 밀어붙이듯 내 놀라움을 압도했다. 나는 그를 진심으로 혐오했지만, 그 성격의 가장 사소한 면모에서까지 드러나는 어마어마한 힘은 나도 모르게 나를 감탄시켰다.\n\n커피는 포스코 부인이 들여왔다. 그는 고마움을 담아 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 문까지 배웅한 뒤, 돌아와서 자기 몫의 커피를 한 잔 따라 필기 탁자로 가져갔다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨, 커피 좀 드시겠소?” 그가 앉기 전에 물었다.\n\n나는 사양했다.\n\n“아니! 내가 당신을 독살할 거라고 생각하시오?” 그가 유쾌하게 말했다. “영국인의 지성은 미치는 데까지는 건전하오.” 그가 탁자 앞에 앉으며 말을 이었다. “하지만 한 가지 중대한 결함이 있소—언제나 엉뚱한 데서 조심을 한단 말이오.”\n\n그는 펜을 잉크에 적시고, 첫 번째 쪽지를 앞에 놓고, 책상을 손바닥으로 탁 치고, 목을 가다듬고, 시작했다. 그는 요란하고 빠르게, 아주 크고 굵은 글씨로 행간을 넓게 벌려 가며 써 내려갔으므로, 쪽지 맨 위에서 시작해 맨 아래에 이르기까지 확실히 2분이 넘게 걸리지 않았다. 한 장을 끝낼 때마다 쪽수를 매기고 어깨 너머 바닥으로 던져 치웠다. 첫 번째 펜이 다 닳자 그것 역시 어깨 너머로 날아갔고, 그는 탁자에 흩어 놓은 것들 가운데서 두 번째 펜을 낚아챘다. 쪽지가 한 장 한 장, 열 장씩, 쉰 장씩, 백 장씩 그의 양쪽 어깨 너머로 날아가, 마침내 의자 둘레에 종이 눈이 쌓여 그를 파묻어 버렸다. 몇 시간이 흐르고 또 흘렀다—나는 그 자리에 앉아 보고 있었고, 그는 그 자리에 앉아 쓰고 있었다. 그는 커피를 홀짝일 때 말고는, 그리고 커피가 다 떨어진 뒤로 이따금 이마를 탁 칠 때 말고는 멈추는 법이 없었다. 1시를 알리는 종이 울리고, 2시, 3시, 4시—그래도 여전히 쪽지는 그의 사방으로 날아다녔고, 여전히 지칠 줄 모르는 펜은 쉬지 않고 종이 위에서 아래로 사각거리며 나아갔으며, 여전히 하얀 종이의 혼돈은 그의 의자 둘레에서 점점 더 높이 쌓여 갔다. 4시가 되었을 때 나는 펜이 갑자기 튀는 소리를 들었다. 그가 자기 이름에 장식 획을 그으며 서명하는 소리였다. “브라보!” 그가 젊은이 같은 활기로 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 더없이 의기양양한 미소를 띠고 내 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n“다 됐소, 하트라이트 씨!” 그가 자신을 되살리듯 넓은 가슴을 주먹으로 쿵 치며 알렸다. “다 됐소—나 자신은 깊이 만족스럽게, 당신은 내가 쓴 것을 읽으면 깊이 경악하게 될 만큼! 주제는 소진되었으나 사람은—포스코라는 사람은 소진되지 않았소. 이제 나는 내 쪽지들을 배열하는 일로, 내 쪽지들을 손보는 일로, 내 쪽지들을 낭독하는 일로 나아가겠소—오직 당신 한 사람의 귀를 향하여 힘주어 읽어 드리지. 방금 4시를 쳤소. 좋소! 배열과 수정과 낭독, 4시부터 5시까지. 원기를 회복할 짧은 선잠, 5시부터 6시까지. 마지막 준비, 6시부터 7시까지. 대리인과 봉인된 편지 건, 7시부터 8시까지. 8시에 출발. 보시오, 이것이 일정표요!”\n\n그는 종이 더미 속에 책상다리를 하고 바닥에 앉아, 송곳과 노끈으로 쪽지들을 꿰어 묶고—손을 보고—자신에게 딸린 온갖 칭호와 훈위를 첫 장 머리에 죄다 써넣은 다음, 우렁찬 연극조의 억양과 넘쳐 나는 연극조의 몸짓을 곁들여 그 원고를 내게 읽어 주었다. 독자도 머지않아 그 문서에 대해 스스로 판단할 기회를 얻게 될 것이다. 여기서는 그것이 내 목적에 부합했다는 사실만 말해 두면 충분하겠다.\n\n그는 이어서 자기가 삯마차를 빌렸던 사람의 주소를 적어 주고, 퍼시벌 경의 편지를 내게 건넸다. 그 편지는 7월 25일에 햄프셔에서 쓴 것이었고, “글라이드 부인”이 26일에 런던으로 간다는 소식을 알리고 있었다. 그러니까 의사의 진단서가 그녀가 세인트존스우드에서 죽었다고 적은 바로 그날(25일)에, 퍼시벌 경 자신의 진술에 따르면 그녀는 블랙워터에 살아 있었고—그 이튿날에야 여행길에 오를 예정이었던 것이다! 그 여행의 증거를 삯마차꾼에게서 얻어 내기만 하면 증거는 완결될 것이었다.\n\n“5시 15분.” 백작이 시계를 보며 말했다. “원기를 되찾을 선잠 시간이오. 하트라이트 씨, 당신도 눈치챘을지 모르나 나는 개인적으로 위대한 나폴레옹을 닮았소—뜻대로 잠을 불러올 수 있다는 점에서도 나는 그 불멸의 인물을 닮았지. 잠깐만 실례하겠소. 당신이 심심하지 않도록 포스코 부인을 부르겠소.”\n\n그가 포스코 부인을 부르는 것은 자기가 자는 동안 내가 집을 떠나지 못하게 하려는 것임을 나 역시 그 못지않게 잘 알고 있었으므로, 나는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 그가 내 손에 넘긴 서류들을 묶는 일에 몰두했다.\n\n부인이 들어왔다. 여전히 서늘하고 창백하고 독기 어린 얼굴이었다. “나의 천사, 하트라이트 씨를 심심찮게 해 드리시오.” 백작이 말했다. 그는 아내에게 의자를 놓아 주고, 두 번째로 그 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 소파로 물러가더니 3분 만에 세상에서 가장 덕망 높은 사람처럼 평온하고 행복하게 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n포스코 부인은 탁자에서 책을 한 권 집어 들고 자리에 앉아, 결코 잊지 않고 결코 용서하지 않는 여자의 흔들림 없는 앙심을 담아 나를 바라보았다.\n\n“남편과 나누시는 이야기를 듣고 있었습니다.” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 그이 자리에 있었다면—당신을 저 난롯가 깔개 위에 시체로 눕혔을 겁니다.”\n\n그 말과 함께 그녀는 책을 펼쳤고, 그때부터 남편이 깨어날 때까지 나를 쳐다보지도 말을 걸지도 않았다.\n\n그는 잠든 시각으로부터 정확히 한 시간 만에 눈을 뜨고 소파에서 일어났다.\n\n“한없이 개운하군.” 그가 말했다. “엘리너, 착한 내 아내, 위층은 다 준비되었소? 잘했소. 여기 내 자잘한 짐은 10분이면 마무리할 수 있고—여행복으로 갈아입는 데 다시 10분이면 되겠지. 대리인이 오기 전에 남은 일이 무엇이 있소?” 그는 방 안을 둘러보다가 흰 쥐가 든 새장을 발견했다. “아!” 그가 애처롭게 외쳤다. “내 정을 찢는 마지막 일이 아직 남아 있구나. 죄 없는 내 귀염둥이들! 사랑스러운 내 어린것들! 너희를 어찌한단 말이냐? 당분간 우리는 어디에도 자리 잡지 못하고, 당분간 쉬지 않고 떠돌아야 하는데—짐이 적을수록 우리에게 좋으니. 내 코카투, 내 카나리아들, 내 작은 쥐들—너희들의 착한 아빠가 떠나고 나면 누가 너희를 아껴 준단 말이냐?”\n\n그는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 방 안을 서성였다. 자백서를 쓰는 일로는 조금도 괴로워하지 않던 그가, 애완동물을 어디에 맡기느냐 하는 훨씬 더 중대한 문제 앞에서는 눈에 띄게 당혹스러워하고 괴로워했다. 오랜 궁리 끝에 그는 갑자기 다시 필기 탁자 앞에 앉았다.\n\n“좋은 생각이 났다!” 그가 외쳤다. “내 카나리아들과 코카투를 이 거대한 수도에 바치겠다—내 대리인이 내 이름으로 그것들을 런던 동물원에 헌정하도록 하지. 그것들을 소개하는 문서는 이 자리에서 작성하겠다.”\n\n그는 펜에서 흘러나오는 말을 소리 내어 되뇌며 쓰기 시작했다.\n\n“일번. 더할 나위 없이 빼어난 깃털을 지닌 코카투. 그 자체로 안목 있는 모든 관람객을 끌어당기는 매력물임. 이번. 견줄 데 없는 활기와 지성을 지닌 카나리아들. 에덴동산에 어울리며, 리젠트 파크의 동산에도 어울림. 영국 동물학에 바치는 경의. 포스코 증정.”\n\n펜이 다시 튀는 소리를 냈고, 그의 서명에는 장식 획이 덧붙었다.\n\n“백작! 쥐들은 넣지 않으셨군요.” 포스코 부인이 말했다.\n\n그는 탁자를 떠나 아내의 손을 잡고 자기 가슴에 얹었다.\n\n“엘리너, 인간의 모든 결심에는,” 그가 엄숙하게 말했다. “한계가 있는 법이오. 나의 한계는 저 문서에 새겨져 있소. 나는 내 흰 쥐들과 헤어질 수 없소. 나를 이해해 주시오, 나의 천사. 그리고 저 아이들을 위층의 여행용 새장으로 옮겨 주시오.”\n\n“참으로 감탄스러운 다정함이에요!” 포스코 부인이 남편을 우러러보며 말하고는, 마지막으로 독사 같은 눈길을 내 쪽에 던졌다. 그녀는 새장을 조심스럽게 들고 방을 나갔다.\n\n백작은 시계를 보았다. 태연한 척 굳게 마음먹고 있었음에도, 그는 대리인이 오기를 초조하게 기다리고 있었다. 촛불은 이미 오래전에 꺼졌고, 새 아침의 햇살이 방 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔다. 대문 초인종이 울리고 대리인이 모습을 드러낸 것은 7시 5분이 되어서였다. 그는 검은 수염을 기른 외국인이었다.\n\n“하트라이트 씨—루벨 씨요.” 백작이 우리를 소개하며 말했다. 그는 그 대리인을(세상에 외국인 첩자라는 것이 있다면 얼굴 어느 한 줄까지도 영락없는 첩자였다) 방 한구석으로 데려가 몇 가지 지시를 속삭이고는, 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔다. 단둘이 되자마자 “루벨 씨”는 더없이 정중하게, 자기에게 지시서를 써 주십사 청했다. 나는 페스카에게 두 줄을 써서 봉인된 편지를 “지참인”에게 넘겨 달라고 위임하고, 겉봉에 수신인을 적어 루벨 씨에게 건넸다.\n\n대리인은 고용주가 여행복 차림으로 돌아올 때까지 나와 함께 기다렸다. 백작은 대리인을 내보내기 전에 내 편지의 수신인을 살펴보았다. “그럴 줄 알았소!” 그가 어두운 눈빛으로 나를 돌아보며 말했고, 그 순간부터 태도가 다시 달라졌다.\n\n그는 짐 꾸리기를 마치고, 그런 다음 앉아서 여행 지도를 들여다보며 수첩에 무언가를 적고, 이따금 초조하게 시계를 보았다. 내게 건네는 말은 그의 입에서 단 한마디도 더 나오지 않았다. 출발 시각이 가까워진 것과, 페스카와 나 사이에 연락이 닿아 있다는 증거를 눈으로 확인한 것이, 분명 그의 온 신경을 도피를 확실히 하는 데 필요한 조치들로 되돌려 놓은 것이었다.\n\n8시가 조금 못 되어 루벨 씨가 뜯지 않은 내 편지를 손에 들고 돌아왔다. 백작은 겉봉의 글씨와 봉인을 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤, 촛불을 켜고 그 편지를 태웠다. “나는 내 약속을 지키오.” 그가 말했다. “그러나 이 일은, 하트라이트 씨, 여기서 끝나지 않을 거요.”\n\n대리인은 자기가 타고 온 마차를 문 앞에 세워 두고 있었다. 그와 하녀가 이제 짐을 내가는 일에 분주해졌다. 포스코 부인이 두꺼운 베일을 쓰고, 흰 쥐들의 여행용 새장을 손에 든 채 아래층으로 내려왔다. 그녀는 내게 말을 걸지도, 내 쪽을 쳐다보지도 않았다. 남편이 아내를 마차까지 배웅했다. “복도까지 나를 따라오시오.” 그가 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “마지막 순간에 당신에게 할 말이 있을지도 모르니.”\n\n나는 문간으로 나갔고, 대리인은 내 아래쪽 앞뜰에 서 있었다. 백작이 혼자 돌아와 나를 복도 안쪽으로 몇 걸음 끌어당겼다.\n\n“셋째 조건을 잊지 마시오!” 그가 속삭였다. “내게서 소식이 갈 거요, 하트라이트 씨—당신이 생각하는 것보다 이르게 신사로서의 결투를 청할지도 모르오.” 그는 내가 알아차리기도 전에 내 손을 붙잡아 힘껏 움켜쥐었다—그러고는 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다가 멈추어 서더니, 다시 내게로 돌아왔다.\n\n“한마디만 더 하겠소.” 그가 은밀하게 말했다. “내가 마지막으로 홀콤 양을 뵈었을 때, 그분은 야위고 병약해 보였소. 나는 그 훌륭한 여인이 걱정스럽소. 그분을 잘 보살펴 주시오, 선생! 가슴에 손을 얹고 엄숙히 간청하오, 홀콤 양을 잘 보살펴 주시오!”\n\n그것이 그가 거대한 몸을 마차 안으로 밀어 넣고 떠나기 전에 내게 남긴 마지막 말이었다.\n\n대리인과 나는 문간에서 잠시 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었다. 우리가 그렇게 함께 서 있는 동안, 길 조금 아래쪽 모퉁이에서 두 번째 마차가 나타났다. 그 마차는 앞서 백작의 마차가 간 방향을 그대로 따라갔고, 집 앞과 열린 정원 문을 지나칠 때 안에 탄 사람이 창밖으로 우리를 내다보았다. 오페라 극장에서 보았던 그 낯선 사내였다!—왼뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 외국인이었다.\n\n“선생, 여기서 나와 함께 30분만 더 기다려 주시지요!” 루벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그러겠습니다.”\n\n우리는 거실로 돌아갔다. 나는 그 대리인에게 말을 걸 기분도, 그가 내게 말을 걸도록 내버려 둘 기분도 아니었다. 나는 백작이 내 손에 넘긴 서류를 꺼내, 그 음모를 계획하고 실행한 자가 직접 들려주는 그 끔찍한 이야기를 읽었다.\n\n# THE STORY CONTINUED BY ISIDOR, OTTAVIO, BALDASSARE FOSCO\n\n(신성 로마 제국의 백작, 청동 왕관 훈장 대십자 기사, 메소포타미아 장미십자 프리메이슨단 종신 대사부(大師父); 유럽 전역의 음악 협회, 의학 협회, 철학 협회, 일반 자선 협회의 (명예직) 회원 등등 등등)\n\n# THE COUNT’S NARRATIVE\n\n1850년 여름, 나는 국외로부터 미묘한 정치적 임무를 띠고 영국에 도착했다. 반쯤은 공적으로 나와 연결된 믿을 만한 사람들이 있었고 나는 그들의 활동을 지휘할 권한을 위임받았는데, 루벨 씨 부부도 그 가운데 들어 있었다. 런던 교외에 거처를 정하고 내 직무에 들어가기까지, 나에게는 몇 주간의 여유가 주어져 있었다. 호기심이 여기서 걸음을 멈추고 그 직무가 대체 무엇이었는지 나에게 설명을 요구할지도 모르겠다. 나는 그 요구에 전적으로 공감한다. 그리고 외교적 신중함이 그에 응하는 것을 금하고 있음을 또한 유감으로 여긴다.\n\n나는 앞서 말한 그 예비적 휴식기를, 애석하게 세상을 떠난 나의 벗 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 장려한 저택에서 보내기로 정해 두었다. 그는 아내를 데리고 대륙에서 건너왔다. 나 또한 내 아내를 데리고 대륙에서 건너왔다. 영국은 가정적 행복의 나라다—그토록 가정적인 정황 속에서 우리가 그 땅에 발을 들였으니, 이 얼마나 어울리는 입국인가!\n\n퍼시벌과 나를 묶어 준 우정의 끈은 이 시기에, 그의 형편과 내 형편이 금전 문제에서 눈물겹도록 닮아 있었던 덕분에 한층 단단해졌다. 우리 둘 다 돈이 필요했다. 막대한 궁핍! 만인의 결핍! 문명인이라면 우리를 딱하게 여기지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가? 그런 자가 있다면 얼마나 무감각한 인간이겠는가! 아니면 얼마나 부자이겠는가!\n\n이 대목을 이야기하면서 나는 구차한 세목에는 들어가지 않겠다. 내 정신은 그런 것들 앞에서 움츠러든다. 나는 로마인의 준엄함으로, 텅 빈 내 지갑과 퍼시벌의 지갑을 움찔하는 대중의 눈앞에 내보인다. 이 개탄스러운 사실은 그런 방식으로 단 한 번에 스스로를 밝히도록 두고, 우리는 그냥 지나가기로 하자.\n\n저택에서 우리를 맞아 준 이는 내 가슴에 “매리언”이라 새겨져 있는, 그리고 사교계의 차가운 공기 속에서는 “홀콤 양”이라 불리는 저 장엄한 존재였다.\n\n정의로운 하늘이여! 나는 얼마나 상상도 못 할 속도로 그 여인을 흠모하게 되었던가. 예순의 나이에 나는 열여덟의 화산 같은 열정으로 그녀를 숭배했다. 내 풍요로운 천성의 황금은 모조리 가망 없이 그녀의 발치에 쏟아부어졌다. 내 아내는—가엾은 천사여!—나를 숭배하는 내 아내는 실링과 페니 몇 닢밖에 얻지 못했다. 세상이란 그런 것이고, 인간이란 그런 것이며, 사랑이란 그런 것이다. 우리가 (묻거니와) 인형 상자 속의 꼭두각시가 아니면 무엇이란 말인가? 오, 전능한 운명이여, 우리의 실을 부드럽게 당겨 다오! 이 비참하고 조그만 무대에서 우리를 자비롭게 춤추듯 퇴장시켜 다오!\n\n앞의 몇 줄은 제대로 이해하기만 하면 하나의 완결된 철학 체계를 담고 있다. 그것이 곧 나의 철학이다.\n\n다시 이어 가겠다.\n\n블랙워터 파크에서 우리가 머물기 시작하던 무렵의 그 집안 형편은, 매리언 본인의 손으로 놀라우리만치 정확하게, 심오한 정신적 통찰과 더불어 그려져 있다. (이 숭고한 존재를 세례명으로 부르는 도취적인 허물없음을 부디 눈감아 주기 바란다.) 그녀의 일기 내용을 정확히 알고 있다는 사실은—나는 은밀한 수단으로 그것을 손에 넣었고, 그 기억은 내게 이루 말할 수 없이 소중하다—본질적으로 남김이 없는 이 여인이 이미 제 것으로 만들어 놓은 화제들로부터 내 성급한 펜을 물러서게 한다.\n\n내가 여기서 다루려는 이해관계는—숨 막히고 막대한 이해관계다!—매리언이 병으로 쓰러진 저 개탄스러운 재앙에서 시작된다.\n\n이 시기의 상황은 단연코 심각한 것이었다. 정해진 기일에 갚아야 할 거액의 돈이 퍼시벌에게 필요했고(나 자신에게도 그에 못지않게 필요했던 소액에 대해서는 말하지 않겠다), 그것을 마련할 유일한 원천은 그의 아내의 재산이었는데, 그 재산은 그녀가 죽기 전에는 단 한 푼도 그의 뜻대로 쓸 수 없었다. 여기까지도 나쁘지만 그 앞은 더 나빴다. 애석한 내 벗에게는 그만의 은밀한 고민거리가 있었고, 사심 없는 나의 우정이 지닌 섬세함은 그 일을 지나치게 캐묻는 것을 금했다. 내가 아는 것이라고는 앤 캐서릭이라는 여자가 인근에 숨어 있다는 것, 그녀가 글라이드 부인과 연락을 주고받고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 결과로 퍼시벌을 확실히 파멸시킬 비밀이 드러날지도 모른다는 것뿐이었다. 그는 아내의 입을 막지 못하고 앤 캐서릭을 찾아내지 못하면 자신은 끝장난 사람이라고 내게 직접 말했다. 그가 끝장난다면 우리의 금전적 이해관계는 어찌 되겠는가? 천성이 담대한 나조차도 그 생각에 그야말로 몸이 떨렸다!\n\n이제 내 지성의 온 힘은 앤 캐서릭을 찾아내는 데로 향했다. 돈 문제는 아무리 중대하다 해도 미룰 여지가 있었지만, 그 여자를 찾아내야 할 필요는 조금도 미룰 수 없었다. 나는 그녀를 오직 전해 들은 인상착의로만 알고 있었는데, 글라이드 부인과 놀라울 만큼 닮았다는 것이었다. 이 기이한 사실은—본래 우리가 찾는 인물을 알아보는 데 도움을 주려는 목적으로만 전해진 것이었지만—앤 캐서릭이 정신병원에서 달아났다는 추가 정보와 맞물리면서, 훗날 그토록 놀라운 결과로 이어진 최초의 거대한 구상을 내 머릿속에 촉발했다. 그 구상은 다름 아닌 두 개의 서로 다른 신원을 통째로 뒤바꾸는 일이었다. 글라이드 부인과 앤 캐서릭이 서로 이름과 자리와 운명을 맞바꾸는 것—그 교환으로 노린 어마어마한 결과는 삼만 파운드의 획득과 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 영구히 지켜 내는 일이었다.\n\n정황을 되짚어 보던 내게, 좀처럼 빗나가지 않는 내 직감은 우리의 보이지 않는 앤이 조만간 블랙워터 호수의 보트 창고로 돌아오리라고 일러 주었다. 나는 그곳에 자리를 잡았고, 미리 가정부인 마이컬슨 부인에게 볼일이 있거든 그 외딴 곳에서 연구에 몰두해 있는 나를 찾으면 된다고 일러 두었다. 불필요한 비밀을 만들지 않는 것, 그리고 때맞춘 약간의 솔직함이 모자라 사람들이 나를 의심하게 만들지 않는 것이 내 원칙이다. 마이컬슨 부인은 처음부터 끝까지 나를 믿었다. 이 기품 있는 부인은(신교 목사의 미망인이다) 믿음이 넘쳐흘렀다. 그만한 연배의 여인에게 그처럼 소박한 신뢰가 넘쳐나는 데 감동하여, 나는 내 천성의 너른 저수지를 열어 그것을 남김없이 빨아들였다.\n\n호숫가에 보초로 자리를 잡은 보람은 앤 캐서릭 본인이 아니라 그녀를 돌보는 사람의 등장으로 돌아왔다. 이 인물 역시 소박한 믿음이 넘쳐흘렀고, 앞서 말한 경우와 마찬가지로 나는 그것을 내 안으로 빨아들였다. 그녀가 어떤 정황에서 자신이 어머니처럼 돌보던 그 사람에게 나를 소개했는지는 (아직 하지 않았다면) 그녀 자신이 이야기하도록 남겨 두겠다. 내가 앤 캐서릭을 처음 보았을 때 그녀는 잠들어 있었다. 나는 이 불행한 여자와 글라이드 부인의 닮은꼴에 전기를 맞은 듯 충격을 받았다. 그때까지 윤곽으로만 떠올라 있던 저 원대한 계획의 세부가, 잠든 얼굴을 바라보는 순간 완벽하게 짜맞춰진 모습으로 내게 떠올랐다. 동시에 언제나 부드러운 감화에 열려 있는 내 심장은 눈앞의 고통스러운 광경 앞에서 눈물로 녹아내렸다. 나는 즉시 그 고통을 덜어 주는 일에 착수했다. 다시 말해, 앤 캐서릭이 런던까지 갈 수 있도록 기운을 북돋울 강장제를 마련해 주었다.\n\n내 인생의 가장 좋은 시절은 의학과 화학을 열렬히 연구하는 데 바쳐졌다. 특히 화학은 그 지식이 부여하는 어마어마하고 무한한 힘 때문에 언제나 내게 거부할 수 없는 매력이었다. 화학자는—나는 힘주어 단언한다—마음만 먹으면 인류의 운명을 좌지우지할 수 있다. 더 나아가기 전에 이 말을 설명해 두겠다.\n\n사람들은 정신이 세계를 지배한다고 말한다. 그러면 정신은 무엇이 지배하는가? 육체다(여기서는 나를 바짝 따라오라). 그리고 육체는 모든 군주 가운데 가장 전능한 자, 곧 화학자의 처분 아래 놓여 있다. 나에게—이 포스코에게—화학을 다오. 그러면 셰익스피어가 햄릿을 구상하고 그 구상을 실행하려 자리에 앉을 때, 그의 일용할 음식에 가루 몇 알갱이만 떨어뜨려도 나는 그의 육체를 통해 그의 정신을 무너뜨려, 그 펜이 종이를 더럽힌 것 중 가장 비천한 헛소리를 쏟아 내게 만들 것이다. 같은 조건에서 저 저명한 뉴턴을 되살려 보라. 나는 장담하거니와 그는 사과가 떨어지는 것을 보고 만유인력의 원리를 발견하는 대신 그것을 먹어 치울 것이다. 네로의 저녁 식사는 그 소화가 끝나기도 전에 네로를 세상에서 가장 온순한 인간으로 바꿔 놓을 것이고, 알렉산드로스 대왕이 아침에 마신 물약은 그날 오후 적을 보자마자 알렉산드로스가 목숨을 걸고 달아나게 만들 것이다. 내 신성한 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 근대의 화학자들이 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 행운 덕분에 인류 가운데 가장 무해한 자들이라는 사실은 사회를 위해 다행한 일이다. 그 다수는 가게를 꾸리는 훌륭한 가장들이다. 소수는 제 강연 목소리에 취해 감탄에 빠진 철학자들이거나, 허황된 불가능에 인생을 허비하는 몽상가들이거나, 야심이 우리 발의 티눈보다 높이 날지 못하는 돌팔이들이다. 그리하여 사회는 무사하고, 화학의 무한한 힘은 가장 피상적이고 가장 하찮은 목적의 노예로 남아 있다.\n\n어찌하여 이런 격발인가? 어찌하여 이런 통렬한 웅변인가?\n\n내 행동이 곡해되었기 때문이고, 내 동기가 오해되었기 때문이다. 사람들은 내가 앤 캐서릭에게 나의 방대한 화학적 수단을 썼으리라고, 그리고 할 수만 있었다면 저 장엄한 매리언에게조차 그것을 썼으리라고 넘겨짚었다. 둘 다 가증스러운 억측이다! 내 모든 이해관계는 (곧 드러나겠지만) 앤 캐서릭을 살려 두는 데 걸려 있었다. 내 모든 근심은 그녀를 돌보던 저 면허 가진 얼간이의 손아귀에서 매리언을 구해 내는 데 집중되어 있었고, 내 조언은 런던에서 온 의사에 의해 처음부터 끝까지 옳았음이 확인되었다. 단 두 번만—그것도 내가 시술한 당사자에게는 똑같이 무해했던 두 번만—나는 화학 지식의 도움을 불러들였다. 그 첫 번째는 블랙워터의 여관까지 매리언을 뒤따라간 뒤였다(나를 가려 준 마침맞은 짐수레 뒤에서, 그녀의 걸음걸이에 구현된 운동의 시를 연구하면서 말이다). 그때 나는 더없이 소중한 내 아내의 수고를 빌려, 내가 흠모하는 저 적수가 쫓겨난 하녀에게 맡긴 두 통의 편지 가운데 하나는 베끼고 하나는 가로챘다. 이 경우 편지들이 그 처녀의 옷가슴 속에 있었으므로, 포스코 부인은 과학의 도움을 빌려야만 그것을 열고 읽고 지시받은 일을 행하고 다시 봉해 제자리에 돌려놓을 수 있었다—그 도움은 반 온스들이 작은 병에 담아 내가 제공했다. 같은 수단을 쓴 두 번째는 글라이드 부인이 런던에 도착하던 때였다(이 일은 곧 이야기하겠다). 그 밖의 어느 때에도 나는 나 자신과 구별되는 내 기술에 신세를 진 적이 없다. 다른 모든 위급과 얽힘에 대해서는, 홀몸으로 정황과 맞붙는 내 타고난 역량이 언제나 넉넉했다. 나는 그 역량이 모든 것에 두루 미치는 지성임을 단언한다. 화학자를 희생시켜 나는 인간의 명예를 옹호한다.\n\n이 너그러운 분노의 격발을 존중해 주기 바란다. 덕분에 나는 이루 말할 수 없이 후련해졌다. 자, 출발! 계속 나아가자.\n\n클레멘트 부인에게(아니면 클레멘츠 부인인가, 어느 쪽인지 나는 확실히 모르겠다) 앤을 퍼시벌의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 두는 최선의 방법은 그녀를 런던으로 옮기는 것이라고 일러 주고—내 제안이 반색하며 받아들여지는 것을 확인하고—역에서 그 여행자들을 만나 떠나보낼 날까지 정해 둔 뒤, 나는 마음 놓고 저택으로 돌아가 아직 남아 있는 난제들과 마주할 수 있었다.\n\n내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 아내의 숭고한 헌신을 활용하는 것이었다. 나는 클레멘츠 부인과, 앤을 위해서 런던의 주소를 글라이드 부인에게 알려 주기로 약속을 해 두었다. 그러나 그것만으로는 충분치 않았다. 내가 없는 사이에 흉계를 꾸미는 자들이 클레멘츠 부인의 순박한 신뢰를 흔들어 놓아, 결국 그녀가 편지를 쓰지 않게 될 수도 있었다. 그녀가 타는 바로 그 기차로 런던까지 가서 은밀히 그녀의 집까지 따라가 확인해 줄 사람을 어디서 찾는단 말인가? 나는 스스로에게 이 물음을 던졌다. 내 안의 남편 된 부분이 즉각 대답했다—포스코 부인이라고.\n\n아내를 런던으로 보내기로 정한 뒤, 나는 그 여행이 두 가지 목적에 쓰이도록 조처했다. 앓고 있는 매리언을 돌볼 간호인, 그것도 환자에게만큼이나 나에게도 충실한 간호인은 내 처지에 반드시 필요한 존재였다. 다행히도 세상에서 가장 입이 무겁고 유능한 여인 가운데 하나를 나는 마음대로 쓸 수 있었다. 저 존경할 만한 부인, 루벨 부인 말이다. 나는 아내의 손에 편지를 들려 런던의 그녀 거처로 보냈다.\n\n정한 날, 클레멘츠 부인과 앤 캐서릭이 역에서 나를 만났다. 나는 정중히 그들을 배웅했고, 같은 기차로 떠나는 포스코 부인도 정중히 배웅했다. 그날 밤늦게 아내는 블랙워터로 돌아왔는데, 내 지시를 나무랄 데 없이 정확하게 따른 뒤였다. 그녀는 루벨 부인과 함께였고, 클레멘츠 부인의 런던 주소를 가지고 왔다. 나중에 벌어진 일들로 보아 이 마지막 대비책은 불필요했음이 드러났다. 클레멘츠 부인은 어김없이 글라이드 부인에게 자기 거처를 알렸던 것이다. 나는 앞날의 위급에 대비하는 조심스러운 눈으로 그 편지를 간직해 두었다.\n\n같은 날 나는 의사와 짧게 면담을 했고, 그 자리에서 인류의 신성한 이익을 위해 매리언의 병증에 대한 그의 처치에 항의했다. 그는 무례했다. 무지한 자들이 다 그렇듯이. 나는 노여움을 드러내지 않았다. 실효를 거둘 만한 때에 다투기로 하고 그와의 싸움은 미뤄 두었다. 다음으로 내가 한 일은 나 자신이 블랙워터를 떠나는 것이었다. 앞으로 벌어질 일들을 내다보고 런던의 거처를 얻어 두어야 했다. 또한 프레더릭 페얼리 씨와 처리할 소소한 집안일도 있었다. 나는 세인트존스우드에서 원하던 집을 찾아냈다. 그리고 컴벌랜드의 리머리지에서 페얼리 씨를 찾아냈다.\n\n매리언의 서신 왕래가 어떤 성격인지 내밀히 알고 있던 터라, 나는 그녀가 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 써서 글라이드 부인의 결혼 생활의 곤경을 덜어 주고자 그녀를 컴벌랜드의 삼촌 댁으로 데려가겠다고 제안했음을 이미 알고 있었다. 이 편지는 당장은 해가 될 것이 없고 오히려 득이 될 수도 있겠다 싶어, 내가 현명하게도 목적지에 그대로 닿게 두었던 것이다. 이제 나는 매리언 자신의 제안을 뒷받침하고자 페얼리 씨 앞에 나섰다—다만 몇 군데를 손보아서였는데, 내 계획의 성공을 위해서는 다행스럽게도 그녀의 병 덕분에 그 수정이 정말로 불가피해져 있었다. 글라이드 부인은 삼촌의 초대를 받아 홀로 블랙워터를 떠나야 했고, 삼촌의 명시적인 권유에 따라 여정 중 하룻밤을 고모 집에서(내가 세인트존스우드에 얻어 둔 그 집에서) 묵어야 했다. 이 결과를 얻어 내는 것, 그리고 글라이드 부인에게 보여 줄 수 있는 초대 편지를 확보하는 것이 페얼리 씨를 찾아간 목적이었다. 이 신사가 정신도 육체도 똑같이 허약했으며 내가 내 성격의 온 힘을 그에게 풀어놓았다고만 말해 두면, 그것으로 충분할 것이다. 나는 왔고, 보았고, 페얼리를 정복했다.\n\n초대 편지를 손에 넣고 블랙워터 파크로 돌아와 보니, 매리언의 병에 대한 의사의 얼빠진 처치가 더없이 우려스러운 결과로 이어져 있었다. 열병이 발진티푸스로 번진 것이다. 내가 돌아온 날 글라이드 부인은 언니를 간호하겠다며 억지로 방에 들어가려 했다. 그녀와 나 사이에는 마음이 통하는 구석이 없었고—그녀는 나를 첩자라 부르는, 내 감수성에 대한 용서받지 못할 폭거를 저질렀으며—내 앞길과 퍼시벌의 앞길을 가로막는 걸림돌이었다. 그럼에도 불구하고 내 아량은 내 손으로 그녀를 전염의 위험에 빠뜨리는 일을 금했다. 동시에 그녀가 스스로 위험에 뛰어드는 것을 나는 조금도 막지 않았다. 만약 그녀가 그렇게 하는 데 성공했더라면, 내가 더디고 참을성 있게 풀어 나가던 그 얽히고설킨 매듭이 어쩌면 정황의 힘으로 단칼에 끊겼을지도 모른다. 실제로는 의사가 끼어들었고, 그녀는 그 방에 들어가지 못했다.\n\n런던에 자문을 청하자고 진작 권한 사람은 나 자신이었다. 이제 그 절차가 밟혔다. 도착한 의사는 내 진단을 확인해 주었다. 고비는 심각했다. 그러나 발진티푸스가 나타난 날로부터 닷새째에 우리는 이 매력적인 환자에게 희망을 걸 수 있게 되었다. 이 무렵 내가 블랙워터를 비운 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다—아침 기차로 런던에 가서 세인트존스우드 집의 마지막 준비를 마치고, 클레멘츠 부인이 거처를 옮기지 않았음을 은밀한 탐문으로 확인하고, 루벨 부인의 남편과 사소한 예비 문제 한두 가지를 매듭짓던 날이었다. 나는 밤에 돌아왔다. 닷새 뒤 의사는 우리의 흥미로운 매리언이 모든 위험에서 벗어났으며 이제 세심한 간병 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않다고 선언했다. 이때가 바로 내가 기다려 온 순간이었다. 의사의 시중이 더는 없어서는 안 될 것이 아니게 되자, 나는 그 의사에게 나를 내세움으로써 이 승부의 첫 수를 두었다. 그는 내 앞길에 놓인 여러 목격자 가운데 하나였고, 치워야 할 인물이었다. 우리 둘 사이의 격렬한 언쟁이(퍼시벌은 내가 미리 일러 둔 대로 끼어들기를 거부했다) 그 목적에 부합했다. 나는 걷잡을 수 없는 분노의 눈사태가 되어 그 가련한 인간을 덮쳤고, 그를 집 밖으로 쓸어냈다.\n\n다음으로 치워야 할 방해물은 하인들이었다. 나는 다시 퍼시벌을 움직였고(그의 도덕적 용기는 끊임없는 자극제를 필요로 했다), 마이컬슨 부인은 어느 날 주인에게서 이 집 살림을 해산한다는 말을 듣고 경악했다. 우리는 집안일을 위해 한 사람만 남기고 하인을 모두 내보냈는데, 남긴 아이는 그 굼뜬 우둔함 덕분에 곤란한 것을 알아챌 리 없다고 믿을 만했다. 그들이 떠나고 나자 이제 마이컬슨 부인을 떼어 놓는 일만 남았는데, 이 상냥한 부인을 바닷가로 보내 여주인이 묵을 숙소를 알아보게 하는 것으로 손쉽게 이루어졌다.\n\n이제 정황은 우리가 바라던 그대로였다. 글라이드 부인은 신경성 질환으로 방에 갇혀 있었고, 그 굼뜬 하녀는(이름은 잊었다) 밤이면 여주인의 시중을 들러 그 방에 함께 들어갔다. 매리언은 빠르게 회복되고 있었으나 아직 자리보전을 하고 있었고, 루벨 부인이 간호를 맡았다. 그 집에는 내 아내와 나, 그리고 퍼시벌 말고는 살아 있는 사람이 없었다. 이렇게 모든 기회가 우리에게 유리해진 가운데 나는 다음 고비와 맞섰고, 이 승부의 두 번째 수를 두었다.\n\n두 번째 수의 목적은 글라이드 부인이 언니 없이 홀로 블랙워터를 떠나도록 유도하는 것이었다. 매리언이 먼저 컴벌랜드로 갔다고 그녀를 납득시키지 못한다면, 그녀 스스로의 뜻으로 그 집을 떠나게 할 가망은 없었다. 그녀의 마음에 그 필요한 작용을 일으키기 위해, 우리는 우리의 흥미로운 환자를 블랙워터의 빈 침실 중 하나에 숨겼다. 깊은 밤에 포스코 부인과 루벨 부인과 내가(퍼시벌은 침착하지 못해 믿고 맡길 수 없었다) 그 은닉을 해냈다. 그 광경은 그림 같고 신비롭고 더없이 극적이었다. 내 지시에 따라 침상은 아침에 튼튼하고 옮길 수 있는 나무틀 위에 마련되어 있었다. 우리는 그 틀의 머리와 발을 가만히 들어 올리기만 하면, 환자도 침상도 흐트러뜨리지 않고 원하는 곳으로 옮길 수 있었다. 이 경우에는 화학의 도움이 필요하지도 않았고 쓰이지도 않았다. 우리의 흥미로운 매리언은 회복기의 깊은 잠에 빠져 있었다. 우리는 미리 촛불을 놓아두고 문을 열어 두었다. 나는 대단한 완력을 지닌 자격으로 틀의 머리 쪽을 들었고, 아내와 루벨 부인이 발 쪽을 들었다. 나는 그 값을 매길 수 없이 소중한 짐의 내 몫을 사내다운 다정함으로, 아버지 같은 조심스러움으로 감당했다. 우리의 한밤중 행렬을 그려 낼 근대의 렘브란트는 어디에 있는가? 아, 예술이여! 아, 이토록 그림 같은 소재여! 근대의 렘브란트는 어디에도 없다.\n\n이튿날 아침 아내와 나는 런던으로 떠났고, 매리언은 사람이 살지 않는 집 한가운데에 격리되어 루벨 부인의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다. 루벨 부인은 이삼 일 동안 환자와 함께 스스로를 가두는 데 흔쾌히 동의해 주었다. 떠나기 전에 나는 퍼시벌에게 페얼리 씨가 조카딸에게 보낸 초대 편지를(컴벌랜드로 가는 도중 고모 집에서 하룻밤 묵으라는 당부가 담긴 편지를) 건네주며, 내 연락을 받는 대로 글라이드 부인에게 보이라고 일러 두었다. 또한 그에게서 앤 캐서릭이 갇혀 있던 정신병원의 주소와, 달아난 환자가 다시 의료의 보호 아래로 돌아온다는 사실을 원장에게 알리는 편지를 받아 두었다.\n\n지난번 수도를 다녀올 때 나는 우리가 이른 기차로 런던에 도착하는 대로 곧바로 맞을 수 있도록 조촐한 살림을 준비해 두라고 조처해 두었다. 이 현명한 대비 덕분에 우리는 바로 그날 이 승부의 세 번째 수를 둘 수 있었다—앤 캐서릭을 손에 넣는 수였다.\n\n여기서는 날짜가 중요하다. 나는 감성의 인간과 사무의 인간이라는 상반된 특성을 한 몸에 겸비하고 있다. 날짜란 날짜는 모두 내 손끝에 있다.\n\n1850년 7월 24일 수요일, 나는 우선 클레멘츠 부인을 치워 놓기 위해 아내를 삯마차에 태워 보냈다. 런던에 있는 글라이드 부인이 보냈다는 전갈 하나면 그 목적에는 충분했다. 클레멘츠 부인은 마차에 실려 나갔고 마차에 남겨졌다. 그사이 내 아내는(가게에서 무엇을 산다는 핑계로) 그녀를 따돌리고 세인트존스우드의 우리 집으로 돌아가, 오기로 되어 있는 손님을 맞았다. 그 손님이 하인들에게 “글라이드 부인”이라고 소개되어 있었음은 굳이 덧붙일 것도 없다.\n\n그동안 나는 다른 마차를 타고 뒤따라가 있었는데, 앤 캐서릭에게 보낼 쪽지를 지니고 있었다. 쪽지에는 그저 글라이드 부인이 클레멘츠 부인을 붙들어 하루를 함께 보낼 작정이니, 밖에서 기다리는 친절한 신사분—햄프셔에서 퍼시벌 경에게 발각되는 것을 이미 막아 준 그분—의 보살핌을 받아 두 사람에게 합류하라는 말만 적혀 있었다. 그 “친절한 신사”는 거리의 아이를 시켜 이 쪽지를 들여보내고는 한두 집 건너에서 결과를 기다렸다. 앤이 문간에 나타나 문을 닫는 순간, 이 훌륭한 사내는 그녀를 위해 마차 문을 열어 놓고 있다가 그녀를 차 안으로 빨아들이고 그대로 달려갔다.\n\n(여기서 괄호 속 감탄 한마디를 눈감아 주기 바란다. 이 얼마나 흥미로운가!)\n\n포리스트 로드로 가는 길에 내 동승자는 아무런 두려움도 보이지 않았다. 나는 마음만 먹으면 아버지처럼 굴 수 있다—그 점에서 나를 능가할 자는 없다—그리고 그때 나는 지극히 아버지 같았다. 그녀의 신뢰를 받을 자격이 내게 얼마나 많았던가! 그녀에게 이로웠던 약을 조제한 것도 나였고, 퍼시벌 경에게서 오는 위험을 그녀에게 일러 준 것도 나였다. 어쩌면 나는 그 자격을 너무 굳게 믿었는지도 모른다—어쩌면 지력이 약한 사람들에게 있는 낮은 본능의 예민함을 얕보았는지도 모른다—어쨌든 내 집에 들어설 때의 실망에 대해 그녀를 충분히 대비시키지 못한 것만은 분명하다. 내가 그녀를 응접실로 데리고 들어갔을 때—그녀에게 낯선 사람인 포스코 부인 말고는 아무도 없는 것을 그녀가 보았을 때—그녀는 더없이 격렬한 동요를 드러냈다. 개가 보이지 않는 무언가의 기척을 맡듯 그녀가 공기 중에서 위험의 냄새를 맡았다 한들, 그보다 더 갑작스럽고 더 까닭 없이 경보를 드러낼 수는 없었을 것이다. 내가 끼어들어 보았으나 소용없었다. 그녀가 겪고 있던 그 두려움이라면 내가 달래 줄 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀가 앓고 있던 중한 심장병은 어떤 정신적 완화책도 미치지 못하는 것이었다. 이루 말할 수 없이 소름 끼치게도 그녀는 경련에 사로잡혔다—그 몸 상태에서 그런 충격은 언제라도 그녀를 우리 발치에 시체로 눕혀 놓을 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n가장 가까운 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈고, “글라이드 부인”이 당장 진료를 받아야 한다고 전했다. 더없이 다행스럽게도 그는 유능한 사람이었다. 나는 내 손님을 지력이 약하고 망상에 시달리는 사람이라고 그에게 소개했으며, 병실에는 내 아내 말고는 어떤 간호인도 지키지 못하도록 조처했다. 그러나 그 불행한 여인은 무슨 말을 할지 걱정할 것도 없을 만큼 위중했다. 이제 나를 짓누르는 유일한 두려움은, 진짜 글라이드 부인이 런던에 도착하기 전에 가짜 글라이드 부인이 죽어 버리지나 않을까 하는 것이었다.\n\n나는 그날 아침 루벨 부인에게 26일 금요일 저녁에 남편의 집에서 나와 합류하라는 쪽지를 써 보냈고, 퍼시벌에게도 아내에게 삼촌의 초대 편지를 보여 주고 매리언이 먼저 떠났다고 못 박은 뒤 역시 26일 정오 기차로 그녀를 런던으로 보내라고 당부하는 쪽지를 써 보냈다. 앤 캐서릭의 건강 상태를 헤아려 보고 나는 일을 앞당겨야 할 필요를, 그리고 애초 예상보다 이르게 글라이드 부인을 내 뜻대로 둘 필요를 절감했던 것이다. 내 처지의 그 끔찍한 불확실 속에서 이제 무슨 새 지시를 내릴 수 있었겠는가? 나는 운과 의사에게 맡기는 것 외에 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 내 감정은 애처로운 탄식으로 터져 나왔고, 다른 사람들이 듣는 데서는 그 탄식에 “글라이드 부인”이라는 이름을 붙일 만큼의 자제력만은 겨우 남아 있었다. 그 밖의 모든 면에서, 그 잊지 못할 날의 포스코는 온통 개기일식에 뒤덮인 포스코였다.\n\n그녀는 밤을 험하게 넘겼고 기진한 채 깨어났으나, 그날 늦게 놀랄 만큼 기운을 되찾았다. 탄력 좋은 내 기분도 그녀와 함께 되살아났다. 퍼시벌과 루벨 부인의 답은 이튿날인 26일 아침에나 받을 수 있었다. 사고만 없다면 그들이 내 지시를 따르리라는 것을 알고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 미리 내다보고 글라이드 부인을 역에서 데려올 삯마차를 잡으러 나가, 26일 두 시에 우리 집으로 오도록 일러 두었다. 주문이 장부에 적히는 것을 확인한 뒤, 나는 루벨 씨와 일을 매듭지으러 갔다. 또한 필요한 정신이상 진단서를 떼어 줄 수 있는 신사 두 사람의 도움도 확보했다. 그중 한 사람은 내가 개인적으로 아는 사이였고, 다른 한 사람은 루벨 씨가 아는 사이였다. 둘 다 그 활달한 정신이 옹졸한 양심의 가책 따위는 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 사람들이었고, 둘 다 일시적인 궁핍에 시달리고 있었으며, 둘 다 이 몸 포스코를 믿었다.\n\n이 일들을 마치고 돌아온 것은 오후 다섯 시가 넘어서였다. 돌아와 보니 앤 캐서릭은 죽어 있었다. 25일에 죽은 것이다. 그런데 글라이드 부인은 26일에야 런던에 도착할 참이었다!\n\n나는 망연자실했다. 이것을 곱씹어 보라. 포스코가 망연자실했다니!\n\n되돌리기에는 너무 늦었다. 내가 돌아오기 전에 그 의사가 주제넘게도 내 수고를 덜어 준답시고 사망한 날짜 그대로 제 손으로 사망 신고를 해 버린 것이다. 그때까지 빈틈없던 내 원대한 계획에 이제 약한 곳이 생겼다—25일이라는 그 치명적인 사실은 내가 어떤 노력을 해도 바꿀 수 없었다. 나는 사내답게 앞날 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 퍼시벌의 이해와 나의 이해가 여전히 걸려 있는 이상, 남은 것은 이 승부를 끝까지 두는 일뿐이었다. 나는 뚫을 수 없는 평정을 되찾았다—그리고 승부를 두었다.\n\n26일 아침 퍼시벌의 편지가 도착해 아내가 정오 기차로 떠난다고 알려 왔다. 루벨 부인도 저녁에 뒤따르겠다고 편지를 보내왔다. 나는 가짜 글라이드 부인을 죽은 채로 집에 남겨 둔 채 삯마차를 타고 나서, 세 시에 기차로 도착하는 진짜 글라이드 부인을 맞으러 갔다. 마차 좌석 밑에 감추어 나는 앤 캐서릭이 내 집에 들어올 때 입고 있던 옷을 모두 싣고 갔다—그 옷들은 죽은 여자가 산 여자의 몸으로 부활하는 일을 거들 운명이었다. 이 얼마나 대단한 상황인가! 나는 이것을 영국의 신진 소설가들에게 권한다. 이것을 완전히 새로운 것으로서, 진이 빠진 프랑스의 극작가들에게 바친다.\n\n글라이드 부인은 역에 나와 있었다. 사람이 크게 붐비고 어수선했으며, 짐을 찾는 데 내 마음에 들지 않을 만큼 시간이 지체되었다(혹시라도 그녀의 친지 가운데 누가 그 자리에 있었을까 봐 하는 말이다). 마차가 떠나자 그녀는 첫마디로 언니의 소식을 알려 달라고 애원했다. 나는 더없이 마음을 놓게 할 소식을 지어내어, 곧 내 집에서 언니를 보게 될 것이라고 그녀를 안심시켰다. 이번만은 내 집이 레스터 스퀘어 근처에 있었고 루벨 씨가 거처하고 있었는데, 그가 현관에서 우리를 맞았다.\n\n나는 손님을 데리고 위층 뒷방으로 올라갔고, 두 의료계 신사는 환자를 진찰하고 진단서를 써 주려고 아래층에서 기다리고 있었다. 언니에 대해 필요한 만큼 안심시켜 글라이드 부인을 진정시킨 뒤, 나는 두 벗을 따로따로 그녀 앞에 들여보냈다. 그들은 이 자리에 필요한 절차를 짧고 명민하고 성실하게 수행했다. 그들이 방을 나서자마자 나는 다시 방에 들어가, “홀콤 양”의 건강 상태에 대해 놀랄 만한 이야기를 꺼냄으로써 곧바로 사태를 재촉했다.\n\n결과는 내가 예상한 대로 따라왔다. 글라이드 부인은 겁에 질렸고 정신이 아득해졌다. 두 번째이자 마지막으로 나는 과학을 도움으로 불러들였다. 약을 탄 물 한 잔과 약을 처리한 각성제 병 하나가 그녀에게서 그 이상의 곤혹과 경계를 모두 걷어 갔다. 저녁 늦게 몇 차례 더 쓴 처치는 그녀에게 값을 매길 수 없는 축복인 하룻밤의 단잠을 마련해 주었다. 루벨 부인은 글라이드 부인의 몸단장을 맡을 시간에 맞춰 도착했다. 밤에 그녀 자신의 옷을 벗겨 두었고, 아침에 앤 캐서릭의 옷을 입혔는데, 예법을 가장 엄격히 지켜 가며 저 훌륭한 루벨 부인의 원숙한 손이 그 일을 했다. 나는 그날 내내 우리 환자를 의식이 반쯤 정지된 상태로 두었고, 마침내 내 의료계 벗들의 능란한 도움 덕분에 감히 바랐던 것보다 이르게 필요한 명령서를 손에 넣을 수 있었다. 그날 저녁(27일 저녁) 루벨 부인과 나는 되살아난 우리의 “앤 캐서릭”을 정신병원으로 데려갔다. 그녀는 큰 놀라움 속에 인계되었으나 아무런 의심도 사지 않았다. 명령서와 진단서 덕분에, 퍼시벌의 편지 덕분에, 그 닮은꼴 덕분에, 그 옷 덕분에, 그리고 그때 환자 자신의 혼미한 정신 상태 덕분에. 나는 곧바로 돌아와 가짜 “글라이드 부인”의 장례 준비를 하는 포스코 부인을 도왔고, 진짜 “글라이드 부인”의 옷과 짐은 내가 가지고 있었다. 그것들은 나중에 장례에 쓰인 운송편으로 컴벌랜드에 보내졌다. 나는 가장 깊은 상복을 갖춰 입고 합당한 위엄을 지키며 장례에 참석했다.\n\n이 놀라운 사건들에 대한, 그에 못지않게 놀라운 정황 속에서 쓰인 나의 기록은 여기서 끝난다. 리머리지 하우스와 연락을 주고받으며 내가 지켰던 사소한 조심들은 이미 알려져 있다—내 사업의 장대한 성공도, 그 뒤를 따른 든든한 금전적 결실도 마찬가지다. 나는 내 확신의 온 힘을 실어 단언해야겠다. 내 계획의 단 하나 약한 곳은, 내 심장의 단 하나 약한 곳이 먼저 발견되지 않았더라면 결코 드러나지 않았으리라고. 매리언이 동생을 탈출시켰을 때 내가 나서서 나 자신을 구하지 못하도록 막은 것은 오직 그녀를 향한 치명적인 흠모뿐이었다. 나는 그 위험을 무릅썼고, 글라이드 부인의 신원이 완전히 파괴되었음을 믿었다. 매리언이나 하트라이트 씨가 그 신원을 주장하려 든다면, 그들은 세상 앞에 지독한 사기를 떠받든다는 혐의를 스스로 뒤집어쓸 것이고, 그에 따라 불신받고 신용을 잃을 것이며, 따라서 내 이익이나 퍼시벌의 비밀을 위태롭게 할 힘이 없으리라 여겼다. 나는 이런 눈 가린 확률 계산에 나를 맡긴 데서 한 가지 잘못을 저질렀다. 또 한 가지 잘못은, 퍼시벌이 제 고집과 난폭함의 대가를 치른 뒤에 글라이드 부인에게 정신병원으로부터 두 번째 사면을 베풀고 하트라이트 씨에게 나를 벗어날 두 번째 기회를 허락한 것이다. 요컨대 이 중대한 고비에서 포스코는 자기 자신에게 충실하지 못했다. 개탄스럽고 그답지 않은 과실이여! 그 원인을 내 심장에서 보라—매리언 홀콤이라는 모습에서, 포스코의 생애에서 처음이자 마지막인 약점을 보라!\n\n예순의 원숙한 나이에 나는 이 유례없는 고백을 한다. 젊은이들이여! 그대들의 공감을 청한다. 처녀들이여! 그대들의 눈물을 요구한다.\n\n한마디만 더 하면, 숨죽인 채 나에게 집중되어 있는 독자의 주의를 놓아주겠다.\n\n나 자신의 정신적 통찰이 일러 주는 바, 탐구심 있는 이들은 여기서 필연적인 세 가지 질문을 던질 것이다. 그 질문들을 내가 제시하겠다—그리고 내가 답하겠다.\n\n첫 번째 질문. 포스코 백작부인이 내 가장 대담한 소망의 성취와 내 가장 깊은 계획의 추진에 스스로를 주저 없이 바친 비밀은 무엇인가? 나는 그저 내 성품을 가리키는 것만으로 이에 답할 수도 있으리라. 그리고 이번에는 내 쪽에서 되물을 수도 있으리라. 세계의 역사를 통틀어, 나 같은 부류의 사내에게 그 배후에서 그의 생애의 제단 위에 스스로를 제물로 바친 여인이 없었던 예가 대체 어디 있더냐고. 그러나 나는 내가 영국에서 이 글을 쓰고 있음을 기억하고, 내가 영국에서 혼인하였음을 기억한다. 그리하여 나는 묻는다. 이 나라에서 아내의 혼인 의무가 남편의 신조에 대한 그녀 개인의 견해라는 것까지 마련해 주던가? 아니다! 그 의무는 아내에게 아무 유보 없이 남편을 사랑하고 존경하고 순종하라 명한다. 내 아내가 행한 것이 바로 그것이다. 나는 지고한 도덕적 높이에 서서, 그녀가 아내의 본분을 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 수행하였음을 드높이 단언하는 바이다. 침묵하라, 중상이여! 잉글랜드의 아내들이여, 포스코 백작부인에게 그대들의 공감을!\n\n두 번째 질문. 만일 앤 캐서릭이 그때 죽지 않았더라면 나는 어찌하였겠는가? 그 경우 나는 기진한 자연이 영구한 안식을 찾도록 거들었을 것이다. 나는 생명이라는 감옥의 문을 열어, 그 수인에게—정신도 육체도 아울러 불치의 병에 시달리던 그 수인에게—행복한 해방을 베풀었을 것이다.\n\n세 번째 질문. 모든 정황을 차분히 되짚어 볼 때—내 행위는 진지한 비난을 받을 만한가? 참으로 단호하게, 아니다! 나는 불필요한 범죄를 저질러 스스로를 증오의 표적으로 내놓는 일을 조심스레 피하지 않았던가? 화학에 관한 나의 방대한 자산을 쓰자면 나는 글라이드 부인의 목숨을 앗을 수도 있었다. 나는 막대한 개인적 희생을 치르면서 내 자신의 독창성이, 내 자신의 인간애가, 내 자신의 신중함이 명하는 바를 따랐고, 목숨 대신 그녀의 신분을 취하였다. 내가 할 수도 있었던 일에 견주어 나를 판단하라. 내가 실제로 행한 바는 얼마나 비교적 결백한가! 얼마나 간접적으로 유덕해 보이는가!\n\n이 글을 시작하며 나는 이 수기가 주목할 만한 문서가 되리라고 공언하였다. 그것은 내 기대에 온전히 부응하였다. 이 뜨거운 글줄들을 받으라—내가 영원히 떠나는 나라에 남기는 나의 마지막 유산이다. 이 글줄들은 이 자리에 값하며, 또한 다음 이름에 값한다. 포스코.\n\n# I\n\n백작의 원고 마지막 장을 덮었을 때, 내가 포리스트 로드에 머물기로 약속했던 삼십 분은 이미 다 지나 있었다. 루벨 씨는 시계를 들여다보고 고개를 숙였다. 나는 곧바로 일어나, 그 빈집을 대리인의 손에 맡긴 채 그곳을 떠났다. 나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했다—그에 대해서도, 그의 아내에 대해서도 더는 아무 소식을 듣지 못했다. 악행과 기만의 어두운 뒷골목에서 기어 나와 우리 앞길을 가로질렀던 그들은, 바로 그 뒷골목 속으로 남몰래 도로 기어들어가 자취를 감추었다.\n\n포리스트 로드를 떠난 지 십오 분 만에 나는 다시 집에 돌아와 있었다.\n\n나의 필사적인 모험이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그리고 우리 삶에 다음으로 닥칠 일이 무엇일지를 로라와 매리언에게 알리는 데에는 몇 마디면 충분했다. 자세한 이야기는 그날 늦게 들려주기로 미뤄 두고, 나는 서둘러 세인트존스우드로 되돌아갔다. 포스코 백작이 로라를 맞으러 역으로 나갈 때 삯마차를 주문했던 그 사람을 만나기 위해서였다.\n\n내가 지니고 있던 주소는 포리스트 로드에서 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 “대여 마구간”으로 나를 이끌었다. 주인은 예의 바르고 점잖은 사람이었다. 중요한 집안일 때문에 부득이 장부를 들춰 보아야겠다고, 그 영업 기록에서 확인하고 싶은 날짜가 하나 있다고 설명하자, 그는 내 청을 들어주는 데 아무런 이의도 달지 않았다. 장부를 가져오게 했더니, “1850년 7월 26일”이라는 날짜 아래 그 주문이 이런 말로 적혀 있었다—\n\n“포스코 백작, 포리스트 로드 5번지로 브로엄 마차. 두 시. (존 오언).”\n\n물어보니 그 항목에 붙은 “존 오언”이라는 이름은 그 삯마차를 몰았던 사람을 가리키는 것이었다. 그는 마침 마구간 뜰에서 일하고 있었고, 내 청에 따라 나를 만나러 불려 왔다.\n\n“작년 7월에 어떤 신사분을 포리스트 로드 5번지에서 워털루 브리지 역까지 태워다 드린 일을 기억하십니까?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“글쎄올시다, 나리,” 사내가 말했다. “딱히 기억이 난다고는 못 하겠는뎁쇼.”\n\n“그러면 그 신사분 자체는 기억나실지도 모르겠군요. 지난여름에 외국인 한 분을 태우신 일을 떠올려 보실 수 있겠습니까—키가 크고 유난히 뚱뚱한 신사분 말입니다.”\n\n사내의 얼굴이 대번에 환해졌다. “그분이라면 기억납니다요, 나리! 제가 여태껏 본 중에 제일 뚱뚱한 신사분이었고, 여태껏 태워 본 중에 제일 무거운 손님이었습죠. 그럼요, 그럼요—그분이라면 떠오릅니다, 나리! 우리가 역까지 간 게 맞고, 떠난 데가 포리스트 로드가 맞습니다. 창가에서 앵무새인지 뭔지가 꽥꽥거리고 있었습죠. 그 신사분은 부인의 짐 때문에 죽을 듯이 서두르셨고, 제가 눈치 빠르게 굴어 상자들을 챙겨 드렸더니 후하게 사례를 주셨습니다요.”\n\n상자들을 챙겨 주었다니! 나는 곧바로 떠올렸다. 로라가 런던에 도착했을 때의 일을 스스로 이야기하면서, 자기 짐은 포스코 백작이 역에 데리고 온 어떤 사람이 대신 챙겨 주었다고 했던 것을. 바로 이 사내였다.\n\n“그 부인은 보셨습니까?” 내가 물었다. “어떻게 생긴 분이었지요? 젊었습니까, 나이가 들었습니까?”\n\n“글쎄올시다, 나리, 서두르는 통에다 사람들이 밀치락달치락하는 통에, 그 부인이 어떻게 생겼는지는 제대로 말씀드리기가 어렵습죠. 이름 말고는 그분에 대해 떠오르는 게 아무것도 없습니다요.”\n\n“이름은 기억하신다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“예, 나리. 그 부인 이름은 글라이드 부인이었습니다.”\n\n“어떻게 생겼는지는 잊어버리고서, 어떻게 이름은 기억하십니까?”\n\n사내는 웃으면서, 조금 겸연쩍은 듯 발을 옮겨 디뎠다.\n\n“그게, 사실대로 말씀드리자면 말입니다, 나리,” 그가 말했다. “그때 제가 결혼한 지 얼마 안 됐었는데, 제 아내가 제 성으로 바꾸기 전 이름이 그 부인 이름하고 똑같았습죠—그러니까 글라이드라는 이름 말입니다요, 나리. 그 부인이 직접 그 이름을 말씀하셨습니다. ‘상자에 성함이 적혀 있습니까, 마님?’ 제가 그랬죠. ‘그래요,’ 하시더군요, ‘내 이름이 짐에 적혀 있어요—글라이드 부인이에요.’ ‘옳거니!’ 제가 속으로 그랬습죠, ‘내가 대체로 지체 높은 분들 성함에는 머리가 나쁜 편이다만—이 이름만은 어쨌든 옛 친구처럼 반갑게 다가오는구나.’ 시기는 뭐라 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다, 나리. 한 해쯤 됐을 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있고요. 하지만 그 뚱뚱한 신사분에 대해서는 맹세할 수 있고, 그 부인 이름에 대해서도 맹세할 수 있습니다요.”\n\n그가 시기까지 기억할 필요는 없었다—날짜는 그의 주인이 지닌 주문 장부로 확실하게 입증되어 있었으니까. 나는 명백한 사실이라는 저항할 수 없는 무기로 음모 전체를 단칼에 쳐서 무너뜨릴 수단이 이제 내 손안에 들어왔음을 단번에 느꼈다. 나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 대여 마구간 주인을 한쪽으로 데려가, 그의 주문 장부가 지닌 증거와 그의 마부가 지닌 증언이 실제로 얼마나 중대한 것인지를 이야기했다. 그 사람의 일손을 한동안 잃게 되는 데 대한 보상 문제는 쉽게 합의되었고, 장부의 해당 항목은 내가 직접 베껴 적은 뒤 주인의 친필 서명으로 사실임을 증명받았다. 나는 존 오언이 앞으로 사흘 동안, 필요하다면 그보다 더 오래 내 뜻에 따라 대기하기로 정해 놓고 대여 마구간을 나섰다.\n\n이제 내가 원하던 서류는 모두 내 손안에 있었다—관할 구역 등록관이 직접 떼어 준 사망 증명서 사본과, 날짜가 적힌 퍼시벌 경의 편지가 내 수첩 속에 안전하게 들어 있었다.\n\n이 문서 증거를 몸에 지니고, 마부의 대답을 기억 속에 생생히 담은 채, 나는 온갖 조사를 시작한 이래 처음으로 카일 씨의 사무실 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다. 그를 두 번째로 찾아가는 목적 하나는 당연히 내가 해낸 일을 알리는 것이었다. 다른 하나는, 내일 아침 아내를 리머리지로 데려가 그녀의 삼촌 집에서 공개적으로 맞아들여지고 그 신분을 인정받게 하겠다는 나의 결심을 미리 알려 두는 것이었다. 이런 상황에서, 그리고 길모어 씨가 자리를 비운 터에, 집안의 사무 변호사로서 집안의 이익을 위해 그 자리에 참석할 의무가 있는지 없는지는 카일 씨의 판단에 맡겼다.\n\n카일 씨가 얼마나 놀랐는지, 또 조사의 첫 단계부터 마지막까지 내가 한 처신에 대해 그가 어떤 말로 자기 의견을 표현했는지는 아무것도 적지 않겠다. 다만 그가 그 자리에서 곧바로 우리와 함께 컴벌랜드로 가겠다고 결정했다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하겠다.\n\n우리는 이튿날 아침 이른 기차로 출발했다. 로라와 매리언과 카일 씨와 나는 한 객실에 탔고, 존 오언은 카일 씨 사무실의 서기 한 사람과 함께 다른 객실에 자리를 잡았다. 리머리지 역에 닿자 우리는 먼저 토드 코너의 농가로 갔다. 로라가 삼촌의 조카딸로 공개적으로 인정받은 모습으로 그곳에 나타나기 전에는 그 집에 발을 들이지 않게 하겠다는 것이 나의 확고한 결심이었다. 나는 착한 토드 부인이 우리가 컴벌랜드에 온 용건을 듣고 받은 어리둥절함에서 정신을 차리자마자 매리언에게 그녀와 숙소 문제를 정하도록 맡기고, 그녀의 남편과는 존 오언을 농장 일꾼들의 선선한 대접에 맡기기로 이야기해 두었다. 이런 준비를 마치고 나서 카일 씨와 나는 함께 리머리지 하우스로 향했다.\n\n페얼리 씨와의 면담에 대해서는 길게 쓸 수가 없다. 그 일은 떠올리기만 해도 조바심과 경멸의 감정이 함께 치밀어 올라, 그저 기억 속에서일 뿐인데도 그 장면이 도무지 견딜 수 없이 역겨워지기 때문이다. 나는 그저 내 뜻을 관철했다고만 적어 두고 싶다. 페얼리 씨는 늘 하던 방식대로 우리를 상대하려 들었다. 면담 첫머리의 정중한 무례는 우리가 못 들은 척 넘겼다. 이어서 그가 음모의 폭로에 자기가 압도당했노라고 우리를 설득하려 늘어놓은 항변들도 우리는 아무 동정 없이 들어 넘겼다. 마침내 그는 영락없이 보채는 아이처럼 칭얼거리고 훌쩍거렸다. “조카가 죽었다는 말을 들었는데 살아 있는 줄을 그가 어떻게 알았겠는가? 회복할 시간만 좀 준다면 사랑스러운 로라를 기꺼이 맞아들이겠다. 자기가 무덤 속으로 서둘러 떠밀려 들어가고 싶어 하는 사람처럼 보이는가? 아니잖은가. 그런데 어째서 사람을 재촉하는가?” 그는 틈만 나면 이런 하소연을 되풀이했고, 나는 결국 그를 피할 수 없는 두 가지 선택지 사이에 단단히 세워 놓음으로써 그것을 단번에 끝내 버렸다. 나는 그에게 내가 제시한 조건대로 조카에게 정당한 대우를 해 주든지, 아니면 법정에서 그녀의 생존을 공개적으로 주장했을 때 따라올 결과를 감당하든지 택하라고 했다. 그가 도움을 청하며 돌아본 카일 씨는, 지금 이 자리에서 그 문제를 결정하셔야 한다고 딱 잘라 말했다. 그는 자기 개인의 근심에서 가장 빨리 벗어나게 해 줄 쪽을 고르는, 그다운 선택을 하고는, 느닷없이 기운을 짜내어 자기는 더 이상 이런 윽박지름을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 않으니 우리 마음대로 하라고 선언했다.\n\n카일 씨와 나는 곧바로 아래층으로 내려가, 가짜 장례식에 참석했던 소작인들에게 돌릴 편지의 서식을 함께 정했다. 페얼리 씨의 이름으로, 모레 리머리지 하우스에 모여 달라고 부르는 내용이었다. 같은 날짜를 가리키는 지시서도 한 통 작성해, 칼라일의 석공에게 사람을 하나 리머리지 교회 묘지로 보내 비문 하나를 지워 달라고 요청했다—그 집에서 자기로 되어 있던 카일 씨는, 페얼리 씨가 이 편지들을 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 자기 손으로 직접 서명하게끔 하는 일을 맡았다.\n\n그 사이의 하루를 나는 농가에서 음모의 경위를 담백하게 적은 이야기를 쓰고, 거기에 로라가 죽었다는 주장에 사실들이 실제로 어떻게 어긋나는지를 정리한 진술을 덧붙이는 데 썼다. 나는 이것을 이튿날 모인 소작인들 앞에서 읽기 전에 먼저 카일 씨에게 보였다. 우리는 낭독이 끝날 때 증거를 어떤 형식으로 제시할지도 함께 정했다. 이런 문제들이 정리되자 카일 씨는 이번에는 화제를 로라의 재산 문제로 돌리려 했다. 나는 그 문제에 대해 아는 바가 없었고 알고 싶지도 않았으며, 포스코 백작부인에게 남겨진 유산에 대한 아내의 종신 수익권과 관련해 내가 취한 처신을 사업하는 사람의 눈으로 그가 좋게 볼지 어떨지도 미심쩍었으므로, 그 이야기를 삼가더라도 양해해 달라고 카일 씨에게 청했다. 그 문제는, 내가 그에게 사실대로 말할 수 있었듯이, 우리끼리도 결코 입에 올리지 않고 남에게 이야기하기는 본능적으로 꺼리게 되는 지난날의 슬픔과 고통에 얽혀 있었다.\n\n저녁이 다가올 무렵 내가 한 마지막 일은, 무덤 위의 그 거짓 비문이 지워지기 전에 그것을 베껴 적어 “묘비의 이야기”를 얻어 두는 것이었다.\n\n그날이 왔다—로라가 리머리지 하우스의 낯익은 아침 식사실에 다시 한번 들어서는 날이. 매리언과 내가 그녀를 이끌고 들어서자 모여 있던 사람들이 모두 자리에서 일어났다. 그녀의 얼굴을 본 순간, 눈에 보이는 놀라움의 전율과 귀에 들리는 관심의 웅성거림이 그들 사이로 번져 갔다. 페얼리 씨도 (내가 분명히 못 박아 둔 대로) 그 자리에 있었고, 그 곁에는 카일 씨가 앉아 있었다. 그의 시종은 한 손에는 각성제 병을, 다른 손에는 오드콜로뉴를 흠뻑 적신 하얀 손수건을 들고 그의 뒤에 서 있었다.\n\n나는 페얼리 씨에게, 내가 그의 권한과 명시적인 승낙 아래 이 자리에 나선 것인지를 공개적으로 밝혀 달라고 청하는 것으로 절차를 시작했다. 그는 양옆으로 한 팔씩을 뻗어 한쪽은 카일 씨에게, 한쪽은 시종에게 내밀었고—두 사람의 부축을 받아 제 다리로 일어서더니, 이렇게 말했다. “하트라이트 씨를 소개하게 해 주십시오. 저는 여전히 더할 나위 없는 병자여서, 이분이 참으로 친절하게도 저를 대신해 말씀해 주십니다. 이 문제는 끔찍하게 난처한 것입니다. 부디 이분의 말씀을 들어 주시고, 소란은 피우지 말아 주십시오!” 그 말을 하고 나서 그는 천천히 다시 의자 속으로 파묻히며, 향내 나는 손수건 속으로 피신했다.\n\n내가 먼저 가장 짧고 가장 평이한 말로 예비 설명을 마친 뒤, 음모의 폭로가 이어졌다. 나는 (청중에게 이렇게 알렸다) 첫째로 지금 내 곁에 앉아 있는 내 아내가 고 필립 페얼리 씨의 딸임을 선언하기 위해, 둘째로 그들이 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 참석했던 그 장례식이 다른 여자의 장례식이었음을 확실한 사실들로 입증하기 위해, 셋째로 이 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지를 그들에게 담백하게 설명하기 위해 이 자리에 나왔노라고 했다. 더 이상의 서두 없이 나는 곧바로 음모의 경위를 적은 글을 읽어, 그 윤곽을 또렷하게 그려 보이되 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 불필요하게 끌어들여 내 진술을 복잡하게 만들지 않으려고 금전적 동기에만 초점을 맞추었다. 그것이 끝나자 나는 청중에게 교회 묘지 비문에 새겨진 날짜(25일)를 상기시키고, 사망 증명서를 내보여 그 날짜가 옳음을 확인해 주었다. 이어서 나는 25일자로 된 퍼시벌 경의 편지를 읽어 주었다. 그의 아내가 26일에 햄프셔에서 런던으로 떠날 예정이라고 알린 편지였다. 다음으로 나는 삯마차 마부의 직접 증언으로 그녀가 실제로 그 여행을 했음을 보였고, 대여 마구간의 주문 장부로 그녀가 그 정해진 날에 그 여행을 했음을 입증했다. 그러자 매리언이 정신병원에서 로라와 만난 일과 동생이 그곳을 빠져나온 일에 대한 자기 진술을 덧붙였다. 그런 다음 나는 퍼시벌 경의 죽음과 나의 결혼을 그 자리에 모인 사람들에게 알리는 것으로 절차를 마쳤다.\n\n내가 다시 자리에 앉자 카일 씨가 일어나, 집안의 법률 고문으로서, 내 주장은 자기가 평생 들어 본 가장 명백한 증거로 입증되었다고 선언했다. 그가 그 말을 하는 동안 나는 로라를 팔로 감싸 일으켜 세워, 방 안의 모든 사람에게 또렷이 보이도록 했다. “여러분도 모두 같은 생각이십니까?” 나는 그들 쪽으로 몇 걸음 나아가 아내를 가리키며 물었다.\n\n그 물음이 일으킨 효과는 전기와도 같았다. 방의 저 아래쪽 끝에서 이 영지의 가장 나이 많은 소작인 가운데 한 사람이 벌떡 일어나더니, 순식간에 나머지 사람들을 이끌었다. 나는 지금도 그 사람이 눈에 선하다. 정직해 보이는 볕에 그은 얼굴과 희끗한 잿빛 머리를 하고서 창가 자리에 올라서서, 묵직한 승마 채찍을 머리 위로 휘두르며 환호를 이끌던 그 모습이. “저기 아씨가 살아서 정정하게 계신다—하느님의 축복이 있기를! 목청껏 질러라, 얘들아! 목청껏 질러!” 그에게 화답해 몇 번이고 거듭 터져 나온 그 함성은 내가 평생 들어 본 가장 아름다운 음악이었다. 잔디밭에 모여 있던 마을의 일꾼들과 학교 아이들이 그 환호를 받아 우리에게 되울려 주었다. 농부의 아내들은 로라를 에워싸고 서로 먼저 그녀와 악수하려고, 제 볼 위로 눈물을 쏟으면서 부디 씩씩하게 견디고 울지 말라고 애원하려고 다투었다. 그녀가 너무나 벅차 어쩔 줄을 몰라 해서, 나는 그녀를 그들에게서 떼어 내 문간까지 안고 가야 했다. 거기서 나는 그녀를 매리언에게 맡겼다—여태 한 번도 우리를 저버린 적 없는 매리언, 그 용감한 자제력이 이번에도 우리를 저버리지 않은 매리언에게. 문간에 혼자 남은 나는 (로라와 나의 이름으로 감사를 전한 뒤) 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람에게, 나를 따라 교회 묘지로 가서 저 거짓 비문이 묘비에서 깎여 나가는 것을 제 눈으로 보아 달라고 청했다.\n\n그들은 모두 집을 나섰고, 무덤 둘레에 모여 있던 마을 사람들의 무리에 모두 합류했다. 그곳에서는 석공의 일꾼이 우리를 기다리고 있었다. 숨을 죽인 정적 속에서 강철 정의 첫 날카로운 타격이 대리석 위에 울렸다. “로라, 글라이드 부인”이라는 그 세 마디가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 아무 소리도 들리지 않았고—아무도 움직이지 않았다. 그러고 나자 마치 음모의 마지막 족쇄가 로라 자신에게서 떨어져 나간 것을 느끼기라도 한 듯 군중 사이에서 커다란 안도의 물결이 일었고, 모였던 사람들은 천천히 흩어졌다. 비문 전체가 다 지워진 것은 그날 늦은 시각이었다. 그 자리에는 나중에 단 한 줄만이 새겨졌다. “앤 캐서릭, 1850년 7월 25일.”\n\n나는 카일 씨에게 작별 인사를 하려고 저녁 이른 시각에 리머리지 하우스로 돌아갔다. 그와 그의 서기, 그리고 삯마차 마부는 밤 기차로 런던에 돌아갔다. 그들이 떠나자 페얼리 씨에게서 무례한 전갈이 하나 내게 전해졌다—소작인들에게 던진 나의 물음에 환호가 처음 터져 나왔을 때 넋이 나간 몰골로 방에서 실려 나갔던 그 사람에게서 말이다. 전갈은 우리에게 “페얼리 씨의 진심 어린 축하”를 전하면서, “우리가 이 집에 머물 생각인지”를 알고 싶다고 했다. 나는 우리가 그 집 문을 들어선 유일한 목적은 이미 이루어졌다고, 나는 내 집이 아닌 어느 누구의 집에도 머물 생각이 없다고, 그리고 페얼리 씨는 우리를 다시 보거나 우리에게서 소식을 듣게 될 걱정은 손톱만큼도 하실 필요가 없다고 답을 보냈다. 우리는 그날 밤 농장의 벗들에게 돌아가 쉬었고, 이튿날 아침—온 마을 사람들과 이웃의 농부들 모두가 더없이 뜨거운 열의와 호의로 역까지 배웅해 주는 가운데—런던으로 돌아왔다.\n\n컴벌랜드의 언덕들이 멀리 시야에서 흐려져 갈 때, 나는 이제 다 지나가 버린 그 긴 싸움을 시작할 무렵의, 낙담스럽기만 하던 처지를 떠올렸다. 이제 와 돌이켜보면 참으로 이상한 일이었다. 우리에게서 남의 도움을 바랄 여지를 모두 앗아 갔던 그 가난이, 나로 하여금 스스로 나서서 움직이게 만듦으로써 도리어 우리 성공의 간접적인 수단이 되었다는 것이. 우리가 법률적인 도움을 살 만큼 부유했더라면 결과가 어떠했을까? (카일 씨 자신의 설명대로라면) 얻는 것은 극히 미심쩍었을 테고—실제로 일어난 사건들이라는 명백한 시험대에 비추어 판단하건대, 잃는 것은 확실했을 것이다. 법은 결코 내게 캐서릭 부인과의 면담을 얻어다 주지 못했을 것이다. 법은 결코 페스카를 백작에게서 자백을 받아 내는 수단으로 만들어 주지 못했을 것이다.\n\n# II\n\n이 사슬이 이야기의 첫머리에서 끝까지 온전히 이어지려면, 아직 두 가지 사건을 더 이어 붙여야 한다.\n\n오랜 세월 우리를 짓눌러 온 과거에서 벗어났다는 그 새로운 자유의 감각이 아직 우리에게 낯설던 무렵, 나는 목판 조각 일의 첫 일자리를 내게 주었던 그 친구에게서 부름을 받았고, 나의 앞날을 염려하는 그의 마음을 다시 한 번 새롭게 확인하게 되었다. 그는 고용주들로부터 파리에 가서 자기 기술의 실제 응용에 관한 새로운 발명 하나를 그들을 대신하여 살펴보고 오라는 위임을 받은 참이었다. 고용주들은 그 발명이 과연 값어치가 있는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 했다. 그러나 그 자신은 다른 일에 매여 그 심부름을 떠맡을 겨를이 없었고, 그리하여 참으로 친절하게도 그 일을 내게 넘기자고 제안한 것이었다. 나로서는 그 제안을 감사히 받아들이는 데 조금도 망설일 이유가 없었다. 내가 바라는 대로 맡은 소임을 잘 해내기만 한다면, 그 결과로 지금은 이따금씩만 관계를 맺고 있는 그 화보 신문에 정식으로 자리를 얻게 될 터였기 때문이다.\n\n나는 필요한 지시를 받고 이튿날 여행 짐을 꾸렸다. 로라를 다시 한 번 그 언니의 보살핌에 맡기고 떠나면서—아, 지난번과는 얼마나 달라진 사정 속에서였던가!—전에도 여러 차례 아내의 마음에도 내 마음에도 떠올랐던 진지한 생각 하나가 되살아났다. 매리언의 앞날에 관한 생각이었다. 우리의 이기적인 애정이 그 너그러운 생애의 헌신을 통째로 받아 챙길 권리가 우리에게 대체 있는가? 우리 자신을 잊고 오직 그녀만을 생각하는 것이야말로 우리의 도리이자, 우리가 감사를 표하는 가장 좋은 길이 아니겠는가? 나는 떠나기 전 잠시 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 이 말을 꺼내 보려 했다. 그녀는 내 손을 잡았고, 첫마디에서 나를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n“우리 셋이 함께 그 모든 일을 겪어 낸 마당에,” 그녀가 말했다, “마지막의 마지막 이별이 오기 전까지 우리 사이에 헤어짐이란 있을 수 없어요. 내 마음도 내 행복도, 월터, 로라와 당신 곁에 있어요. 조금만 기다려 봐요, 당신들 난롯가에서 아이들 목소리가 들려올 때까지. 나는 그 아이들에게 아이들의 말로 나를 대신해 말하는 법을 가르치겠어요. 그리고 그 아이들이 제 아버지와 어머니 앞에서 맨 처음 외우게 될 말은 이것이 될 거예요—우리 이모는 절대로 내드릴 수 없어요!”\n\n파리로 가는 길에 나 혼자만 오른 것은 아니었다. 막판에 이르러 페스카가 나와 동행하겠다고 마음을 정했다. 그는 오페라 극장에서의 그 밤 이후로 평소의 쾌활함을 되찾지 못하고 있었고, 일주일의 휴가가 기분을 돋우는 데 얼마나 도움이 될지 시험해 보기로 결심한 것이었다.\n\n파리에 도착한 지 나흘째 되는 날, 나는 내게 맡겨진 소임을 마치고 필요한 보고서를 작성했다. 닷새째 되는 날은 페스카와 함께 구경을 다니고 즐기는 데 쓰기로 정해 두었다.\n\n우리가 묵은 호텔은 손님이 너무 많아 우리 둘을 같은 층에 재울 수 없었다. 내 방은 삼층에 있었고 페스카의 방은 그 위 사층에 있었다. 닷새째 되는 날 아침, 나는 교수가 나갈 채비를 마쳤는지 보려고 위층으로 올라갔다. 층계참에 채 이르기 직전, 나는 그의 방문이 안쪽에서 열리는 것을 보았다—길고 가녀리고 신경질적인 손 하나가(분명 내 친구의 손은 아니었다) 문을 조금 열어 붙들고 있었다. 그와 동시에 페스카의 목소리가 들렸다. 낮은 소리로, 다급하게, 제 나라 말로 이렇게 말하고 있었다—“이름은 기억하지만 그 사람은 모릅니다. 오페라 극장에서 보셨듯이 그자는 너무나 달라져서 나는 알아보지 못했습니다. 보고는 올리겠습니다—그 이상은 할 수 없습니다.” “그 이상은 할 필요도 없소.” 두 번째 목소리가 대답했다. 문이 활짝 열리더니, 뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 금발 사내—일주일 전에 포스코 백작의 마차를 뒤쫓아 가던 것을 내가 보았던 바로 그 사내가 걸어 나왔다. 내가 길을 비켜 주자 그는 고개를 숙여 인사했다—그의 얼굴은 무섭도록 창백했다—그리고 계단을 내려가는 동안 난간을 꽉 붙들고 있었다.\n\n나는 문을 밀고 페스카의 방으로 들어섰다. 그는 소파 한구석에 참으로 기이한 모양새로 몸을 웅크린 채 있었다. 내가 다가가자 그는 나를 피해 움츠러드는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n“내가 방해가 되나?” 내가 물었다. “저 사람이 나오는 걸 보고서야 자네에게 손님이 있는 줄 알았네.”\n\n“손님이 아닐세.” 페스카가 다급히 말했다. “저 사람을 오늘 처음 보았고, 오늘이 마지막일세.”\n\n“안 좋은 소식을 가져온 모양이지?”\n\n“끔찍한 소식이야, 월터! 우리 런던으로 돌아가세—나는 여기 더 머물고 싶지 않네—여기 온 게 후회스러워. 젊은 시절의 그 불행들이 나를 몹시도 무겁게 짓누르는군.” 그는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다. “나이 든 뒤에까지 이토록 무겁게 짓누른단 말일세. 나는 그것들을 잊으려 애쓰는데—그것들은 나를 잊어 주지 않아!”\n\n“오후가 되기 전에는 떠날 수 없을 것 같은데.” 내가 대답했다. “그동안 나와 함께 나가 보지 않겠나?”\n\n“아닐세, 친구. 나는 여기서 기다리겠네. 하지만 오늘 돌아가세—제발 돌아가세.”\n\n나는 그가 그날 오후에 파리를 떠나게 될 것이라고 다짐해 주고 그를 남겨 둔 채 나왔다. 우리는 전날 저녁에 빅토르 위고의 고매한 소설을 길잡이 삼아 노트르담 대성당에 올라가 보기로 약속해 두었던 터였다. 프랑스의 수도에서 내가 그보다 더 보고 싶었던 것은 없었으므로, 나는 혼자서 그 성당을 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n강가를 따라 노트르담으로 다가가던 길에 나는 파리의 그 무시무시한 시체 안치소—모르그를 지나게 되었다. 커다란 군중이 문 앞에 몰려 왁자하게 떠들며 밀치락달치락하고 있었다. 안에 무언가 뭇사람의 호기심을 자극하고 뭇사람의 공포에 대한 갈증을 채워 주는 것이 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n군중의 언저리에 있던 두 남자와 한 여자의 이야기가 내 귀에 들어오지 않았더라면 나는 그대로 성당을 향해 걸어갔을 것이다. 그들은 모르그 안의 구경거리를 보고 방금 나온 참이었는데, 곁에 선 사람들에게 그 시신에 대해 들려주는 이야기인즉, 그것은 한 사내의 주검이며—엄청나게 몸집이 큰 사내로, 왼팔에 이상한 표시가 있더라는 것이었다.\n\n그 말이 내 귀에 닿는 순간 나는 걸음을 멈추고 안으로 들어가는 군중 틈에 자리를 잡았다. 열린 문틈으로 페스카의 목소리를 들었을 때, 그리고 호텔 계단에서 나를 스쳐 지나가는 그 낯선 사내의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 진실의 어렴풋한 그림자가 이미 내 마음을 스쳐 갔었다. 이제 방금 내 귀에 닿은 우연한 몇 마디 말로 그 진실 자체가 내게 드러난 것이었다. 나 아닌 다른 이의 복수가 그 운명 지어진 사내를 극장에서 그의 집 문간까지, 그의 집 문간에서 파리의 은신처까지 뒤쫓았던 것이다. 나 아닌 다른 이의 복수가 그를 셈을 치르는 날로 불러내어 그에게서 목숨이라는 대가를 받아 낸 것이었다. 극장에서, 그자를 함께 찾고 있던 그 낯선 사내가 우리 곁에서 듣고 있는 가운데, 내가 페스카에게 그자를 가리켜 보인 바로 그 순간이—그의 운명을 확정 지은 순간이었다. 나는 그와 내가 얼굴을 맞대고 섰을 때 내 마음속에서 벌어졌던 그 싸움을—그를 놓아 보내 주기까지 내가 치러야 했던 그 싸움을 떠올렸고, 그것을 되새기며 몸서리쳤다.\n\n나는 천천히, 한 치 한 치 군중과 더불어 안으로 밀려 들어갔고, 모르그에서 죽은 자와 산 자를 갈라놓는 커다란 유리 칸막이에 점점 더 가까이, 점점 더 가까이 다가갔다. 마침내 나는 맨 앞줄 구경꾼들의 바로 등 뒤에 이르러 안을 들여다볼 수 있게 되었다.\n\n거기에 그가 누워 있었다. 거두는 이도 없이, 아는 이도 없이, 프랑스 군중의 경박한 호기심 앞에 내걸린 채로! 능력을 타락시켜 쓰고 무정한 죄악으로 채웠던 그 기나긴 생애의 끔찍한 결말이 바로 이것이었다! 죽음의 숭고한 안식 속에 잠잠해진 그 넓고 굳세고 우람한 얼굴과 머리는 어찌나 당당하게 우리를 마주 보고 있었던지, 내 주위의 재잘거리는 프랑스 여인들이 감탄하여 두 손을 치켜들고 새된 소리로 입을 모아 외쳤다. “아, 이 얼마나 잘생긴 사내인가!” 그를 죽인 상처는 칼이나 단검으로 심장 바로 위를 정확히 찔러 낸 것이었다. 왼팔을 제외하면 시신 어디에도 다른 폭력의 흔적은 없었다. 그리고 그 왼팔에는, 페스카의 팔에서 내가 낙인을 보았던 바로 그 자리에, T 자 모양으로 깊이 벤 두 줄기 칼자국이 있어 형제단의 표식을 온전히 지워 없애고 있었다. 그의 머리 위에 걸린 옷가지들은 그 자신이 제 위험을 알고 있었음을 말해 주었다—그것은 그를 프랑스 직공으로 꾸며 주던 옷이었다. 잠시 동안, 그러나 그 이상은 아니게, 나는 유리 칸막이 너머로 이 광경들을 억지로 바라보았다. 그 이상 길게 적을 수는 없다. 나는 더는 보지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그의 죽음과 관련하여 내가 뒤에 알아낸 몇 안 되는 사실들은(일부는 페스카에게서, 일부는 다른 경로로 얻은 것이다) 이 주제를 이 지면에서 물리기 전에 여기에 적어 두어도 좋으리라.\n\n그의 시신은 내가 앞서 묘사한 그 변장 차림 그대로 센 강에서 건져 올려졌고, 그의 이름도 신분도 거처도 알려 줄 만한 것은 몸에서 아무것도 발견되지 않았다. 그를 찌른 손은 끝내 밝혀지지 않았고, 그가 살해된 정황도 끝내 드러나지 않았다. 나는 이 암살의 비밀에 관하여, 내가 나대로 결론을 내렸듯 다른 이들도 저마다 결론을 내리도록 맡겨 두겠다. 흉터가 있는 그 외국인이 형제단의 일원이었다는 것(페스카가 조국을 떠난 뒤 이탈리아에서 가입한 것이다)을 내가 넌지시 밝혔고, 나아가 죽은 사내의 왼팔에 새겨진 T 자 모양의 두 칼자국이 이탈리아 말 “트라디토레”, 곧 배신자를 뜻하며 형제단이 배신자에게 정의를 집행하였음을 보여 주는 것이라고 덧붙였으니, 포스코 백작의 죽음에 얽힌 수수께끼를 밝히는 데 내가 아는 바는 모두 내놓은 셈이다.\n\n시신은 내가 그것을 본 이튿날, 그의 아내에게 부쳐진 익명의 편지로 신원이 확인되었다. 그는 포스코 백작부인의 손으로 페르 라셰즈 묘지에 묻혔다. 오늘까지도 새 조화가 백작부인 자신의 손에 의해 무덤을 두른 장식 청동 난간에 계속 걸리고 있다. 그녀는 베르사유에서 극히 은둔한 생활을 하고 있다. 얼마 전 그녀는 세상을 떠난 남편의 전기를 펴냈다. 그 책은 그의 진짜 이름이 무엇이었는지에 대해서도, 그의 생애의 은밀한 내력에 대해서도 아무런 빛을 던지지 못한다—그 책은 거의 전부가 그의 가정적 미덕에 대한 찬사와 그의 보기 드문 재능에 대한 단언, 그리고 그에게 수여된 영예들의 열거에 바쳐져 있다. 그의 죽음에 얽힌 정황은 아주 간략하게만 언급되며, 마지막 쪽에서 이런 문장으로 정리된다—“그의 생애는 귀족의 권리와 신성한 질서의 원리를 향한 하나의 기나긴 선언이었으며, 그는 자신의 대의를 위해 순교하였다.”\n\n# III\n\n파리에서 돌아온 뒤로 여름과 가을이 지나갔으나, 그 계절들은 여기에 적어 둘 만한 어떤 변화도 데려오지 않았다. 우리는 더없이 소박하고 조용하게 살았으므로, 내가 이제 꾸준히 벌어들이게 된 수입만으로도 우리에게 필요한 모든 것이 넉넉히 채워졌다.\n\n이듬해 2월에 우리의 첫아이가 태어났다—아들이었다. 조촐한 세례 잔치에는 어머니와 누이와 베지 부인이 손님으로 오셨고, 클레멘츠 부인도 같은 자리에서 아내를 거들려고 참석했다. 매리언이 우리 아이의 대모가 되었고, 페스카와 길모어 씨(뒤의 사람은 대리인을 세워서)가 대부가 되었다. 여기에 덧붙여 두자면, 길모어 씨는 한 해 뒤 우리에게 돌아왔을 때 내 청을 받아들여 이 지면의 구상을 도와주었으니, 이 이야기의 앞부분에 그의 이름으로 실린 서술이 바로 그가 써 준 글이다. 그 글은 배열의 순서로는 앞자리를 차지하고 있으나, 시간의 순서로는 이렇듯 내가 가장 나중에 받은 글이었다.\n\n이제 우리 삶에서 기록해 두어야 할 사건은 단 하나 남았는데, 그것은 우리의 어린 월터가 태어난 지 여섯 달이 되었을 무렵에 일어났다.\n\n그 무렵 나는 내가 몸담고 있던 신문에 곧 실릴 삽화의 밑그림을 그리기 위해 아일랜드로 파견되었다. 나는 거의 두 주일 동안 집을 비웠고, 그동안 아내와 매리언과 꼬박꼬박 편지를 주고받았다. 다만 마지막 사흘만은 내 이동이 너무 불확실해 편지를 받을 수가 없었다. 돌아오는 여정의 뒷부분은 밤에 지나왔고, 아침에 집에 닿아 보니 참으로 놀랍게도 나를 맞아 줄 사람이 아무도 없었다. 로라와 매리언과 아이는 내가 돌아오기 전날 집을 떠났던 것이다.\n\n하인이 건네준 아내의 쪽지는 그들이 리머리지 하우스로 갔다는 것을 알려 주어 내 놀라움을 키우기만 했다. 매리언이 글로 설명하려는 시도를 일절 금했다는 것, 돌아오는 즉시 뒤따라와 달라는 간청, 컴벌랜드에 도착하면 모든 것이 환히 밝혀지리라는 것, 그리고 그동안 조금이라도 불안해하는 일은 금한다는 것. 쪽지는 거기서 끝나 있었다. 아직은 아침 기차를 잡을 만큼 이른 시각이었다. 나는 그날 오후에 리머리지 하우스에 닿았다.\n\n아내와 매리언은 둘 다 위층에 있었다. 그들은 (내 경악을 완성이라도 하려는 듯) 예전에 페얼리 씨의 그림을 손보는 일을 하던 시절 내게 화실로 내주었던 그 작은 방에 자리를 잡고 있었다. 내가 일할 때면 늘 차지하고 앉던 바로 그 의자에 이제 매리언이 앉아 있었고, 아이는 그 무릎 위에서 산호를 열심히 빨고 있었다. 그러는 동안 로라는 내가 그토록 자주 쓰던, 잊을 수 없는 그 제도용 탁자 곁에 서서, 지난날 내가 그녀를 위해 채워 주었던 작은 화첩을 손 밑에 펼쳐 놓고 있었다.\n\n“도대체 무슨 일로 여기까지 온 거요?” 내가 물었다. “페얼리 씨는 알고 있——?”\n\n매리언은 페얼리 씨가 세상을 떠났다고 알려 주어 내 입술 위의 물음을 멎게 했다. 그는 중풍으로 쓰러졌고, 그 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못했다는 것이었다. 카일 씨가 그들에게 그의 죽음을 알리고, 지체 없이 리머리지 하우스로 가라고 권했다고 했다.\n\n거대한 변화가 일어났다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 내 마음에 어슴푸레 밝아 왔다. 내가 그것을 채 다 깨닫기도 전에 로라가 입을 열었다. 그녀는 아직 내 얼굴에 남아 있는 놀라움을 즐기려고 슬며시 내게 다가왔다.\n\n“사랑하는 월터,” 그녀가 말했다. “우리가 이렇게 대담하게 여기 온 것을 정말 해명해야 하나요? 여보, 아무래도 저는 우리의 규칙을 깨고 지난날을 들추지 않고서는 설명할 길이 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n“그런 짓을 할 필요는 조금도 없어요.” 매리언이 말했다. “미래를 이야기하면 똑같이 분명하면서도 훨씬 더 흥미롭게 설명할 수 있으니까요.” 그녀는 일어서서, 팔에 안긴 채 발을 차며 옹알거리는 아이를 들어 올렸다. “이 아이가 누군지 아세요, 월터?” 그녀가 물었다. 행복에 겨운 눈물이 그 눈에 환하게 차오르고 있었다.\n\n“나의 얼떨떨함에도 한계는 있어요.” 내가 대답했다. “내 아이쯤은 아직 알아볼 수 있다고 자신하는데요.”\n\n“아이라뇨!” 그녀가 옛 시절의 그 거침없는 쾌활함을 고스란히 되찾아 외쳤다. “잉글랜드 지주 계급의 어느 분을 두고 그렇게 허물없이 말해도 되나요? 이 고귀하신 아기를 당신 앞에 소개하는 이 순간, 당신이 누구 앞에 서 있는지 알기나 하세요? 분명 모르시는군요! 두 분 저명하신 분을 서로 소개해 드리겠습니다. 월터 하트라이트 씨—리머리지의 상속인이십니다.”\n\n그녀는 그렇게 말했다. 그 마지막 말을 적음으로써 나는 적어야 할 모든 것을 다 적었다. 펜이 내 손안에서 머뭇거린다. 여러 달에 걸친 길고도 행복한 노고가 끝났다. 매리언은 우리 삶의 선한 천사였다—우리의 이야기를 맺는 일은 매리언에게 맡기자.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-583"]=[[["(of Clement’s Inn, Teacher of Drawing)", "(클레멘츠 여관 소속, 그림 교사)"]], [["This is the story of what a Woman’s patience can endure, and what a Man’s resolution can achieve.", "이것은 한 여인의 인내가 무엇을 견뎌 낼 수 있는지, 그리고 한 남자의 결의가 무엇을 이루어 낼 수 있는지에 관한 이야기다."]], [["If the machinery of the Law could be depended on to fathom every case of suspicion, and to conduct every process of inquiry, with moderate assistance only from the lubricating influences of oil of gold, the events which fill these pages might have claimed their share of the public attention in a Court of Justice.", "만일 법이라는 기계가 모든 의혹의 사건을 낱낱이 헤아리고 모든 심리의 과정을 이끌어 가되 황금이라는 기름의 매끄러운 힘을 그저 적당히만 빌리면 되는 것이었다면, 이 지면을 채우는 사건들도 법정에서 세인의 관심을 얼마간 차지했을는지 모른다."]], [["But the Law is still, in certain inevitable cases, the pre-engaged servant of the long purse; and the story is left to be told, for the first time, in this place.", "그러나 법은 어떤 피할 수 없는 경우에는 여전히 두둑한 지갑에 미리 고용된 하인이다. 그리하여 이 이야기는 여기 이 자리에서 처음으로 이야기되기에 이르렀다."], ["As the Judge might once have heard it, so the Reader shall hear it now.", "판사가 한때 그것을 들었을 법한 그대로, 이제 독자가 그것을 들으리라."], ["No circumstance of importance, from the beginning to the end of the disclosure, shall be related on hearsay evidence.", "이 폭로의 처음부터 끝까지, 중요한 정황은 어느 하나도 전해 들은 증언에 기대어 서술되지 않을 것이다."], ["When the writer of these introductory lines (Walter Hartright by name) happens to be more closely connected than others with the incidents to be recorded, he will describe them in his own person.", "이 서두를 적는 사람(이름은 월터 하트라이트)이 기록될 사건들과 남들보다 더 가까이 얽혀 있는 대목에서는, 그가 몸소 그것을 서술할 것이다."], ["When his experience fails, he will retire from the position of narrator; and his task will be continued, from the point at which he has left it off, by other persons who can speak to the circumstances under notice from their own knowledge, just as clearly and positively as he has spoken before them.", "그의 겪음이 미치지 못하는 데 이르면 그는 서술자의 자리에서 물러날 것이며, 그가 멈춘 지점에서부터 그 소임은 문제된 정황을 제 지식으로 증언할 수 있는 다른 이들이—그가 그들에 앞서 그러했듯이 그만큼 명료하고 분명하게—이어 갈 것이다."]], [["Thus, the story here presented will be told by more than one pen, as the story of an offence against the laws is told in Court by more than one witness—with the same object, in both cases, to present the truth always in its most direct and most intelligible aspect; and to trace the course of one complete series of events, by making the persons who have been most closely connected with them, at each successive stage, relate their own experience, word for word.", "그리하여 여기 제시되는 이야기는 한 자루 이상의 펜으로 이야기될 것이니, 이는 법을 어긴 죄의 이야기가 법정에서 한 사람 이상의 증인에 의해 이야기되는 것과 같다. 두 경우 모두 그 목적은 하나다—진실을 언제나 가장 직접적이고 가장 알기 쉬운 모습으로 드러내는 것, 그리고 사건들과 가장 가까이 얽혀 있던 이들로 하여금 저마다의 단계에서 제가 겪은 바를 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 이야기하게 함으로써 하나의 완결된 일련의 사건이 흘러간 자취를 따라가는 것이다."]], [["Let Walter Hartright, teacher of drawing, aged twenty-eight years, be heard first.", "그림 교사이며 스물여덟 살 된 월터 하트라이트의 말을 먼저 듣기로 하자."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["It was the last day of July.", "7월의 마지막 날이었다."], ["The long hot summer was drawing to a close; and we, the weary pilgrims of the London pavement, were beginning to think of the cloud-shadows on the corn-fields, and the autumn breezes on the sea-shore.", "길고 무더웠던 여름이 저물어 가고 있었다. 런던 포도(鋪道)의 지친 순례자인 우리는 옥수수밭에 드리운 구름 그림자와 바닷가의 가을 바람을 떠올리기 시작하던 참이었다."]], [["For my own poor part, the fading summer left me out of health, out of spirits, and, if the truth must be told, out of money as well.", "내 딱한 처지로 말하자면, 스러져 가는 여름은 나를 건강도 잃고 기운도 잃고, 사실대로 말하자면 돈까지 잃은 꼴로 남겨 놓았다."], ["During the past year I had not managed my professional resources as carefully as usual; and my extravagance now limited me to the prospect of spending the autumn economically between my mother’s cottage at Hampstead and my own chambers in town.", "지난 한 해 나는 여느 때만큼 내 직업상의 밑천을 알뜰히 건사하지 못했다. 그리하여 내 헤픈 씀씀이는 이제 나를, 햄스테드에 있는 어머니의 오두막과 시내에 있는 내 하숙방을 오가며 가을을 검소하게 보내야 하는 처지로 몰아넣고 있었다."]], [["The evening, I remember, was still and cloudy; the London air was at its heaviest; the distant hum of the street-traffic was at its faintest; the small pulse of the life within me, and the great heart of the city around me, seemed to be sinking in unison, languidly and more languidly, with the sinking sun.", "그날 저녁은, 내 기억에, 고요하고 흐렸다. 런던의 공기는 더할 나위 없이 무거웠고, 멀리서 들려오는 거리 마차 소리는 더없이 희미했다."], ["I roused myself from the book which I was dreaming over rather than reading, and left my chambers to meet the cool night air in the suburbs.", "내 안의 작은 생명의 맥박과 나를 둘러싼 도시의 큰 심장은, 저무는 해와 발맞추어 나른하게, 더욱더 나른하게 가라앉는 듯했다. 나는 읽는다기보다는 오히려 그 위에서 꿈을 꾸고 있던 책에서 정신을 차리고, 교외의 서늘한 밤공기를 쐬러 하숙방을 나섰다."], ["It was one of the two evenings in every week which I was accustomed to spend with my mother and my sister.", "그날은 내가 어머니와 누이와 함께 지내곤 하던 일주일에 두 번의 저녁 가운데 하루였다."], ["So I turned my steps northward in the direction of Hampstead.", "그래서 나는 햄스테드 쪽으로, 북쪽을 향해 발걸음을 돌렸다."]], [["Events which I have yet to relate make it necessary to mention in this place that my father had been dead some years at the period of which I am now writing; and that my sister Sarah and I were the sole survivors of a family of five children.", "앞으로 이야기해야 할 사건들 때문에 여기서 밝혀 둘 필요가 있는데, 내가 지금 적고 있는 그 무렵에 내 아버지는 이미 몇 해 전에 세상을 떠난 뒤였고, 누이 세라와 나는 다섯 남매 가운데 유일하게 살아남은 둘이었다."], ["My father was a drawing-master before me.", "내 아버지는 나에 앞서 그림 선생이었다."], ["His exertions had made him highly successful in his profession; and his affectionate anxiety to provide for the future of those who were dependent on his labours had impelled him, from the time of his marriage, to devote to the insuring of his life a much larger portion of his income than most men consider it necessary to set aside for that purpose.", "그의 노고는 그를 그 직업에서 크게 성공시켰다. 그리고 제 노동에 기대어 살아가는 이들의 앞날을 마련해 주려는 그 애틋한 근심은, 결혼한 때부터 그로 하여금 대개의 사람들이 그런 목적에 떼어 두어야 한다고 여기는 것보다 훨씬 많은 몫의 수입을 생명 보험에 붓게 만들었다."], ["Thanks to his admirable prudence and self-denial my mother and sister were left, after his death, as independent of the world as they had been during his lifetime.", "그의 훌륭한 신중함과 극기 덕분에 어머니와 누이는 아버지가 세상을 떠난 뒤에도 그가 살아 있을 때와 다름없이 세상에 아쉬울 것 없이 남겨졌다."], ["I succeeded to his connection, and had every reason to feel grateful for the prospect that awaited me at my starting in life.", "나는 아버지의 고객을 물려받았고, 인생의 출발점에서 나를 기다리는 앞날에 감사할 온갖 이유가 있었다."]], [["The quiet twilight was still trembling on the topmost ridges of the heath; and the view of London below me had sunk into a black gulf in the shadow of the cloudy night, when I stood before the gate of my mother’s cottage.", "고요한 황혼이 아직 히스 들판의 가장 높은 등성이 위에서 떨리고 있었고, 발아래 런던의 풍경은 흐린 밤의 그늘 속 검은 심연으로 가라앉아 있었다. 그때 나는 어머니의 오두막 문 앞에 서 있었다."], ["I had hardly rung the bell before the house door was opened violently; my worthy Italian friend, Professor Pesca, appeared in the servant’s place; and darted out joyously to receive me, with a shrill foreign parody on an English cheer.", "초인종을 채 울리기도 전에 집 문이 거칠게 열렸다. 하인이 있어야 할 자리에 내 훌륭한 이탈리아인 친구 페스카 교수가 나타나더니, 영국식 환호를 날카로운 이국풍으로 흉내 내면서 반가이 나를 맞으러 뛰쳐나왔다."]], [["On his own account, and, I must be allowed to add, on mine also, the Professor merits the honour of a formal introduction.", "교수는 자기 자신을 위해서도, 그리고 덧붙이는 것을 허락받자면 나를 위해서도, 정식으로 소개될 영예를 누릴 만하다."], ["Accident has made him the starting-point of the strange family story which it is the purpose of these pages to unfold.", "우연이 그를, 이 지면이 펼쳐 보이고자 하는 그 기이한 한 집안 이야기의 출발점으로 만들어 놓았기 때문이다."]], [["I had first become acquainted with my Italian friend by meeting him at certain great houses where he taught his own language and I taught drawing.", "내가 이 이탈리아인 친구와 처음 알게 된 것은, 그가 제 나라 말을 가르치고 내가 그림을 가르치던 어떤 대저택들에서 그를 만나면서였다."], ["All I then knew of the history of his life was, that he had once held a situation in the University of Padua; that he had left Italy for political reasons (the nature of which he uniformly declined to mention to any one); and that he had been for many years respectably established in London as a teacher of languages.", "그때 내가 그의 생애에 대해 아는 것이라고는, 그가 한때 파도바 대학교에서 어떤 자리를 맡았었다는 것, 정치적인 까닭으로(그 까닭의 성격을 그는 누구에게도 한결같이 말하기를 꺼렸다) 이탈리아를 떠났다는 것, 그리고 여러 해 동안 런던에서 어학 선생으로 버젓이 자리 잡고 있었다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Without being actually a dwarf—for he was perfectly well proportioned from head to foot—Pesca was, I think, the smallest human being I ever saw out of a show-room.", "실제로 난쟁이는 아니면서도—머리끝부터 발끝까지 몸의 균형은 더할 나위 없이 잘 잡혀 있었으니까—페스카는, 내 생각에, 구경거리 방을 벗어난 곳에서 내가 본 사람 가운데 가장 작은 인간이었다."], ["Remarkable anywhere, by his personal appearance, he was still further distinguished among the rank and file of mankind by the harmless eccentricity of his character.", "어디서나 그 생김새로 눈길을 끄는 그는, 그 성격의 해롭잖은 별남으로 인해 뭇사람들 사이에서 한층 더 두드러졌다."], ["The ruling idea of his life appeared to be, that he was bound to show his gratitude to the country which had afforded him an asylum and a means of subsistence by doing his utmost to turn himself into an Englishman.", "그의 삶을 지배하는 생각은, 자기에게 피난처와 살아갈 방편을 마련해 준 이 나라에 대한 고마움을, 있는 힘을 다해 스스로 영국인이 됨으로써 나타내야 한다는 것인 듯했다."], ["Not content with paying the nation in general the compliment of invariably carrying an umbrella, and invariably wearing gaiters and a white hat, the Professor further aspired to become an Englishman in his habits and amusements, as well as in his personal appearance.", "언제나 우산을 지니고 다니고 언제나 각반과 흰 모자를 걸침으로써 이 국민 전체에게 경의를 표하는 것만으로는 만족하지 못하고, 교수는 생김새뿐 아니라 습관과 놀이에서까지 영국인이 되기를 열망했다."], ["Finding us distinguished, as a nation, by our love of athletic exercises, the little man, in the innocence of his heart, devoted himself impromptu to all our English sports and pastimes whenever he had the opportunity of joining them; firmly persuaded that he could adopt our national amusements of the field by an effort of will precisely as he had adopted our national gaiters and our national white hat.", "우리 국민이 운동 경기를 좋아하는 것으로 두드러진다는 것을 알아채자, 그 작은 사내는 순진한 마음에서, 함께 낄 기회만 생기면 우리 영국의 온갖 운동과 놀이에 즉흥적으로 몸을 던졌다. 우리의 국민적 각반과 국민적 흰 모자를 받아들였던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 우리의 국민적 야외 놀이도 의지의 힘만으로 받아들일 수 있으리라 굳게 믿으면서."]], [["I had seen him risk his limbs blindly at a fox-hunt and in a cricket-field; and soon afterwards I saw him risk his life, just as blindly, in the sea at Brighton.", "나는 그가 여우 사냥에서, 또 크리켓 경기장에서 앞뒤 없이 제 팔다리를 내걸고 덤비는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 나는 그가 브라이턴의 바다에서 꼭 그만큼 앞뒤 없이 제 목숨을 내거는 것을 보았다."]], [["We had met there accidentally, and were bathing together.", "우리는 그곳에서 우연히 만나 함께 해수욕을 하고 있었다."], ["If we had been engaged in any exercise peculiar to my own nation I should, of course, have looked after Pesca carefully; but as foreigners are generally quite as well able to take care of themselves in the water as Englishmen, it never occurred to me that the art of swimming might merely add one more to the list of manly exercises which the Professor believed that he could learn impromptu.", "만일 우리가 내 나라에만 있는 어떤 운동에 열중하고 있었다면 나는 물론 페스카를 조심스레 지켜보았을 것이다. 그러나 외국인들도 대개 물속에서는 영국인 못지않게 제 몸을 건사할 줄 아는 법이라, 헤엄치는 재주가 교수가 즉흥적으로 배울 수 있다고 믿는 사내다운 운동 목록에 하나를 더 보탤 뿐일지도 모른다는 생각은 나에게 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Soon after we had both struck out from shore, I stopped, finding my friend did not gain on me, and turned round to look for him.", "둘이 함께 물가에서 헤엄쳐 나간 지 얼마 되지 않아, 나는 친구가 나를 따라오지 못하는 것을 알아채고 멈춰 서서 그를 찾으려 돌아보았다."], ["To my horror and amazement, I saw nothing between me and the beach but two little white arms which struggled for an instant above the surface of the water, and then disappeared from view.", "소스라치고 놀랍게도, 나와 바닷가 사이에는 잠깐 수면 위로 버둥거리다 이내 시야에서 사라지는 두 개의 작고 하얀 팔밖에 보이는 것이 없었다."], ["When I dived for him, the poor little man was lying quietly coiled up at the bottom, in a hollow of shingle, looking by many degrees smaller than I had ever seen him look before.", "내가 그를 향해 잠수해 들어가 보니, 딱한 작은 사내는 자갈이 우묵하게 팬 바닥에 얌전히 웅크린 채, 내가 전에 본 어느 때보다도 몇 곱절이나 작아 보이는 모습으로 누워 있었다."], ["During the few minutes that elapsed while I was taking him in, the air revived him, and he ascended the steps of the machine with my assistance.", "그를 끌어올리는 몇 분 사이에 공기가 그를 되살아나게 했고, 그는 내 부축을 받아 탈의 기계의 계단을 올랐다."], ["With the partial recovery of his animation came the return of his wonderful delusion on the subject of swimming.", "그의 생기가 얼마간 돌아오자 헤엄에 대한 그 놀라운 착각도 되돌아왔다."], ["As soon as his chattering teeth would let him speak, he smiled vacantly, and said he thought it must have been the Cramp.", "달그락거리는 이가 말을 허락하자마자 그는 멍하니 웃으며, 그건 틀림없이 쥐가 났던 탓이었을 거라고 말했다."]], [["When he had thoroughly recovered himself, and had joined me on the beach, his warm Southern nature broke through all artificial English restraints in a moment.", "말끔히 정신을 차리고 바닷가에서 나에게 합류했을 때, 그의 따뜻한 남국의 천성은 삽시간에 온갖 인위적인 영국식 절제를 뚫고 터져 나왔다."], ["He overwhelmed me with the wildest expressions of affection—exclaimed passionately, in his exaggerated Italian way, that he would hold his life henceforth at my disposal—and declared that he should never be happy again until he had found an opportunity of proving his gratitude by rendering me some service which I might remember, on my side, to the end of my days.", "그는 더없이 격렬한 애정의 표현으로 나를 뒤덮었고—과장된 이탈리아식으로 열렬히 외치기를, 이제부터 제 목숨을 내 뜻대로 맡기겠노라 했으며—내가 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 만한 어떤 봉사로 제 고마움을 증명할 기회를 찾기 전에는 다시는 결코 행복해질 수 없으리라 단언했다."]], [["I did my best to stop the torrent of his tears and protestations by persisting in treating the whole adventure as a good subject for a joke; and succeeded at last, as I imagined, in lessening Pesca’s overwhelming sense of obligation to me.", "나는 이 모험 전체를 한낱 농담거리로만 다루기를 고집함으로써 그의 눈물과 다짐의 격류를 막으려 애썼다. 그리고 마침내, 내가 여기기로는, 나에 대한 페스카의 벅찬 은혜감을 얼마간 덜어 주는 데 성공했다."], ["Little did I think then—little did I think afterwards when our pleasant holiday had drawn to an end—that the opportunity of serving me for which my grateful companion so ardently longed was soon to come; that he was eagerly to seize it on the instant; and that by so doing he was to turn the whole current of my existence into a new channel, and to alter me to myself almost past recognition.", "그때 나는 거의 생각지 못했다—그 뒤 우리의 즐거운 휴가가 끝나 갈 무렵에도 거의 생각지 못했다—내 고마운 벗이 그토록 애타게 바라던, 나를 도울 그 기회가 머지않아 찾아오리라는 것을, 그가 그 순간 그것을 냉큼 붙잡으리라는 것을, 그리고 그렇게 함으로써 그가 내 삶의 물줄기 전체를 새로운 물길로 돌려놓고 나를 나 자신조차 거의 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 바꿔 놓으리라는 것을."]], [["Yet so it was.", "그러나 사실이 그러했다."], ["If I had not dived for Professor Pesca when he lay under water on his shingle bed, I should in all human probability never have been connected with the story which these pages will relate—I should never, perhaps, have heard even the name of the woman who has lived in all my thoughts, who has possessed herself of all my energies, who has become the one guiding influence that now directs the purpose of my life.", "만일 내가 자갈 바닥 물속에 누운 페스카 교수를 향해 잠수해 들어가지 않았더라면, 나는 아마 십중팔구 이 지면이 이야기할 사연에 결코 얽히지 않았을 것이다—어쩌면 나는, 내 온 생각 속에 살아 있고 내 온 기운을 사로잡았으며 이제 내 삶의 목적을 이끄는 유일한 길잡이가 된 그 여인의 이름조차 결코 듣지 못했을 것이다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["Pesca’s face and manner, on the evening when we confronted each other at my mother’s gate, were more than sufficient to inform me that something extraordinary had happened.", "어머니의 대문 앞에서 우리가 마주쳤던 그날 저녁, 페스카의 얼굴과 태도는 무언가 심상찮은 일이 일어났음을 나에게 알려 주기에 충분하고도 남았다."], ["It was quite useless, however, to ask him for an immediate explanation.", "그러나 당장 설명을 청하는 것은 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["I could only conjecture, while he was dragging me in by both hands, that (knowing my habits) he had come to the cottage to make sure of meeting me that night, and that he had some news to tell of an unusually agreeable kind.", "그가 두 손으로 나를 안으로 끌어들이는 동안, 나는 다만 이렇게 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이었다—(내 버릇을 아는지라) 그가 그날 밤 나를 꼭 만나려고 오두막에 찾아왔다는 것, 그리고 여느 때와 달리 유난히 반가운 종류의 어떤 소식을 전하려 한다는 것을."]], [["We both bounced into the parlour in a highly abrupt and undignified manner.", "우리 둘은 몹시 급작스럽고 위엄이라고는 없는 꼴로 응접실에 뛰어들었다."], ["My mother sat by the open window laughing and fanning herself.", "어머니는 열린 창가에 앉아 웃으며 부채질을 하고 계셨다."], ["Pesca was one of her especial favourites and his wildest eccentricities were always pardonable in her eyes.", "페스카는 어머니가 각별히 아끼는 이들 가운데 하나였고, 그의 가장 별난 기행조차도 어머니의 눈에는 언제나 용서할 만한 것이었다."], ["Poor dear soul!", "가엾고 다정한 분!"], ["from the first moment when she found out that the little Professor was deeply and gratefully attached to her son, she opened her heart to him unreservedly, and took all his puzzling foreign peculiarities for granted, without so much as attempting to understand any one of them.", "그 작은 교수가 당신 아들에게 깊이, 고마운 마음으로 정을 붙이고 있음을 알아챈 첫 순간부터, 어머니는 그에게 아낌없이 마음을 열었고, 그의 알쏭달쏭한 이국의 별스러움을 하나하나 이해해 보려는 시도조차 없이 있는 그대로 받아들였다."]], [["My sister Sarah, with all the advantages of youth, was, strangely enough, less pliable.", "내 누이 세라는 젊음이 주는 온갖 이점을 지녔으면서도, 이상하게도 덜 너그러웠다."], ["She did full justice to Pesca’s excellent qualities of heart; but she could not accept him implicitly, as my mother accepted him, for my sake.", "세라는 페스카의 훌륭한 심성을 마땅히 인정했다. 그러나 어머니가 나를 위해 그를 받아들이듯 무조건 받아들이지는 못했다."], ["Her insular notions of propriety rose in perpetual revolt against Pesca’s constitutional contempt for appearances; and she was always more or less undisguisedly astonished at her mother’s familiarity with the eccentric little foreigner.", "겉모양 따위는 아랑곳하지 않는 페스카의 타고난 태도에 세라의 섬나라식 예법 관념이 끊임없이 반기를 들었고, 그 별난 작은 외국인을 어머니가 스스럼없이 대하는 것에 세라는 늘 얼마쯤 대놓고 놀라워했다."], ["I have observed, not only in my sister’s case, but in the instances of others, that we of the young generation are nothing like so hearty and so impulsive as some of our elders.", "나는 내 누이의 경우뿐 아니라 다른 이들의 경우에서도 이런 것을 눈여겨보았다—우리 젊은 세대는 우리 윗세대 어떤 이들만큼 정겹지도, 충동적이지도 않다는 것을."], ["I constantly see old people flushed and excited by the prospect of some anticipated pleasure which altogether fails to ruffle the tranquillity of their serene grandchildren.", "나는 노인들이 어떤 기대되는 즐거움을 앞두고 얼굴이 상기되어 들뜬 모습을 늘 보는데, 정작 그 즐거움은 그들의 차분한 손자 손녀의 태연함을 조금도 흐트러뜨리지 못한다."], ["Are we, I wonder, quite such genuine boys and girls now as our seniors were in their time?", "궁금하다, 우리는 우리 어른들이 그들 시절에 그러했던 만큼 지금도 그토록 참된 소년 소녀들일까?"], ["Has the great advance in education taken rather too long a stride; and are we in these modern days, just the least trifle in the world too well brought up?", "교육의 그 대단한 진보가 걸음을 너무 크게 내디딘 것은 아닐까, 그리고 이 근대의 나날에 우리는 이 세상 어느 것보다도 아주 조금은 지나치게 곱게 자란 것은 아닐까?"]], [["Without attempting to answer those questions decisively, I may at least record that I never saw my mother and my sister together in Pesca’s society, without finding my mother much the younger woman of the two.", "그 물음들에 딱 잘라 답해 보려는 것은 아니지만, 적어도 이것만은 적어 둘 수 있다—나는 어머니와 누이가 페스카와 어울려 함께 있는 모습을 볼 때마다, 언제나 어머니 쪽이 둘 가운데 훨씬 젊은 여인임을 발견하곤 했다."], ["On this occasion, for example, while the old lady was laughing heartily over the boyish manner in which we tumbled into the parlour, Sarah was perturbedly picking up the broken pieces of a teacup, which the Professor had knocked off the table in his precipitate advance to meet me at the door.", "이를테면 이번에도, 우리가 소년처럼 응접실로 굴러 들어온 꼴을 두고 늙은 어머니가 마음껏 웃고 계시는 동안, 세라는 교수가 나를 문간에서 맞으려 서둘러 다가오다 탁자에서 떨어뜨린 찻잔의 깨진 조각들을 심란하게 주워 모으고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t know what would have happened, Walter,”", "“무슨 일이 났을지 모르겠구나, 월터.”"], ["said my mother, “if you had delayed much longer.", "어머니가 말했다. “네가 조금만 더 늦었더라면 말이다."], ["Pesca has been half mad with impatience, and I have been half mad with curiosity.", "페스카는 조바심에 반쯤 실성했고, 나는 궁금증에 반쯤 실성했단다."], ["The Professor has brought some wonderful news with him, in which he says you are concerned; and he has cruelly refused to give us the smallest hint of it till his friend Walter appeared.”", "교수가 무언가 놀라운 소식을 가지고 왔는데, 그게 너와 관계있다지 뭐냐. 그런데 이 야박한 양반이 친구 월터가 나타나기 전에는 눈곱만 한 귀띔조차 해 주지 않겠다는 거야.”"]], [["“Very provoking: it spoils the Set,”", "“약도 올라라, 짝이 안 맞게 됐잖아.”"], ["murmured Sarah to herself, mournfully absorbed over the ruins of the broken cup.", "세라가 깨진 잔의 잔해 위로 서글피 마음을 쏟은 채 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."]], [["While these words were being spoken, Pesca, happily and fussily unconscious of the irreparable wrong which the crockery had suffered at his hands, was dragging a large arm-chair to the opposite end of the room, so as to command us all three, in the character of a public speaker addressing an audience.", "이런 말들이 오가는 동안, 페스카는 도자기가 제 손에 당한 돌이킬 수 없는 참사를 다행히도 안달스레 모른 채, 우리 셋 모두를 마주 볼 수 있도록—청중을 향해 연설하는 웅변가의 자세로—커다란 안락의자 하나를 방 반대편 끝으로 끌어가고 있었다."], ["Having turned the chair with its back towards us, he jumped into it on his knees, and excitedly addressed his small congregation of three from an impromptu pulpit.", "등받이를 우리 쪽으로 돌려놓은 그는 무릎으로 의자에 뛰어 올라, 즉석 설교단에서 세 사람의 작은 회중을 향해 들뜬 목소리로 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Now, my good dears,”", "“자, 나의 좋은 분들이여.”"], ["began Pesca (who always said “good dears” when he meant “worthy friends”), “listen to me.", "페스카가 입을 열었다(그는 ‘귀한 벗들’을 뜻할 때면 언제나 ‘좋은 분들’이라 했다)."], ["The time has come—I recite my good news—I speak at last.”", "“내 말을 들으시오. 때가 왔소이다—내 좋은 소식을 읊겠소—드디어 내가 말하겠소.”"]], [["“Hear, hear!”", "“옳소, 옳소!”"], ["said my mother, humouring the joke.", "어머니가 그 익살에 장단을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“The next thing he will break, mamma,”", "“다음에 저이가 깰 건요, 엄마.”"], ["whispered Sarah, “will be the back of the best arm-chair.”", "세라가 속삭였다. “저 제일 좋은 안락의자 등받이일걸요.”"]], [["“I go back into my life, and I address myself to the noblest of created beings,”", "“나는 내 지난 삶으로 돌아가, 창조된 존재 가운데 가장 고귀한 이에게 말을 거오.”"], ["continued Pesca, vehemently apostrophising my unworthy self over the top rail of the chair.", "페스카는 의자 맨 윗살 너머로 이 변변찮은 나를 향해 격하게 부르짖으며 이어 갔다. “누가 (쥐가 나서) 바다 밑에 죽어 있는 나를 발견했소이까?"], ["“Who found me dead at the bottom of the sea (through Cramp); and who pulled me up to the top; and what did I say when I got into my own life and my own clothes again?”", "그리고 누가 나를 물 위로 끌어올렸소이까? 그리고 내가 내 목숨과 내 옷을 되찾았을 때 나는 무어라 말했소이까?”"]], [["“Much more than was at all necessary,”", "“쓸데없는 소리를 너무 많이 했지.”"], ["I answered as doggedly as possible; for the least encouragement in connection with this subject invariably let loose the Professor’s emotions in a flood of tears.", "나는 되도록 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다. 이 화제에 조금이라도 부추김을 주면 어김없이 교수의 감정이 눈물의 홍수로 터져 나오기 때문이었다."]], [["“I said,”", "“나는 말했소.”"], ["persisted Pesca, “that my life belonged to my dear friend, Walter, for the rest of my days—and so it does.", "페스카가 굽히지 않고 이어 갔다. “내 목숨은 남은 평생 내 소중한 벗 월터의 것이라고—과연 그러하오."], ["I said that I should never be happy again till I had found the opportunity of doing a good Something for Walter—and I have never been contented with myself till this most blessed day.", "나는 말했소, 월터를 위해 무언가 좋은 일을 할 기회를 찾기 전에는 결코 다시 행복해질 수 없으리라고—그리고 나는 이 더없이 복된 날이 오기까지 나 자신에게 흡족한 적이 한 번도 없었소. 그런데 이제.”"], ["Now,”", "열정에 찬 작은 사내가 목청을 한껏 높여 외쳤다."], ["cried the enthusiastic little man at the top of his voice, “the overflowing happiness bursts out of me at every pore of my skin, like a perspiration; for on my faith, and soul, and honour, the something is done at last, and the only word to say now is—Right-all-right!”", "“차고 넘치는 행복이 땀처럼 내 살갗의 땀구멍마다에서 솟구쳐 나오오. 내 믿음과 영혼과 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 드디어 그 무언가가 이루어졌으니, 이제 할 말은 오직 하나—좋아-다-좋아!”"]], [["It may be necessary to explain here that Pesca prided himself on being a perfect Englishman in his language, as well as in his dress, manners, and amusements.", "여기서 밝혀 둘 필요가 있겠다—페스카는 옷차림과 몸가짐과 놀이에서뿐 아니라 말투에서도 완벽한 영국인임을 자랑스러워했다."], ["Having picked up a few of our most familiar colloquial expressions, he scattered them about over his conversation whenever they happened to occur to him, turning them, in his high relish for their sound and his general ignorance of their sense, into compound words and repetitions of his own, and always running them into each other, as if they consisted of one long syllable.", "우리의 가장 흔한 회화 표현 몇 가지를 주워 담은 그는 생각날 때마다 그것들을 대화에 흩뿌렸는데, 그 소리를 몹시 즐기면서도 뜻은 대체로 알지 못한 채, 제멋대로 합성어와 되풀이 표현으로 바꾸어 놓고, 마치 하나의 긴 음절이라도 되는 양 늘 서로 이어 붙여 말하곤 했다."]], [["“Among the fine London Houses where I teach the language of my native country,”", "“내가 내 조국의 말을 가르치는 런던의 고급 저택들 가운데.”"], ["said the Professor, rushing into his long-deferred explanation without another word of preface, “there is one, mighty fine, in the big place called Portland.", "교수는 더는 서두 없이 오래 미뤄 둔 설명으로 돌진했다. “포틀랜드라 불리는 큰 동네에 아주 근사한 집이 하나 있소."], ["You all know where that is?", "그게 어딘지들 아시지요?"], ["Yes, yes—course-of-course.", "그럼, 그럼—물론-물론이고말고."], ["The fine house, my good dears, has got inside it a fine family.", "그 근사한 집에는, 나의 좋은 분들이여, 안에 근사한 식구가 들어 있소."], ["A Mamma, fair and fat; three young Misses, fair and fat; two young Misters, fair and fat; and a Papa, the fairest and the fattest of all, who is a mighty merchant, up to his eyes in gold—a fine man once, but seeing that he has got a naked head and two chins, fine no longer at the present time.", "곱고 통통한 마마 한 분, 곱고 통통한 젊은 아가씨 셋, 곱고 통통한 젊은 도련님 둘, 그리고 그 가운데 가장 곱고 가장 통통한 파파 한 분—눈까지 황금에 파묻힌 대단한 상인인데, 한때는 근사한 사내였으나 벗어진 머리와 두 겹 턱을 보건대 지금은 더 이상 근사하지 않소. 자, 잘 들으시오!"], ["Now mind! I teach the sublime Dante to the young Misses, and ah!—my-soul-bless-my-soul!—it is not in human language to say how the sublime Dante puzzles the pretty heads of all three!", "나는 젊은 아가씨들에게 숭고한 단테를 가르치는데, 아!—오-내-영혼에-축복이!—숭고한 단테가 그 셋의 어여쁜 머리를 얼마나 헤매게 하는지는 사람의 말로 다 할 수가 없소이다!"], ["No matter—all in good time—and the more lessons the better for me. Now mind!", "상관없소—다 때가 되면 되는 법이고—수업이 많을수록 나에게는 좋은 일이오."], ["Imagine to yourselves that I am teaching the young Misses to-day, as usual.", "자, 잘 들으시오! 내가 오늘도 여느 때처럼 젊은 아가씨들을 가르치고 있다 상상해 보시오."], ["We are all four of us down together in the Hell of Dante.", "우리 넷은 다 함께 단테의 지옥으로 내려가 있소. 제7옥(獄)에서—아니 그건 아무래도 좋소."], ["At the Seventh Circle—but no matter for that: all the Circles are alike to the three young Misses, fair and fat,—at the Seventh Circle, nevertheless, my pupils are sticking fast; and I, to set them going again, recite, explain, and blow myself up red-hot with useless enthusiasm, when—a creak of boots in the passage outside, and in comes the golden Papa, the mighty merchant with the naked head and the two chins.—Ha!", "곱고 통통한 세 아가씨에게는 어느 옥이나 매한가지니까—어쨌든 제7옥에서 내 제자들이 딱 막혀 버렸소. 그래서 나는 그들을 다시 나아가게 하려고 읊고, 풀이하고, 부질없는 열정으로 벌겋게 달아올라 나 자신을 부풀리고 있는데—바깥 복도에서 삐걱 하는 구두 소리가 나더니, 벗어진 머리에 두 겹 턱을 한 그 대단한 상인, 황금빛 파파가 들어오는 게 아니겠소.—하!"], ["my good dears, I am closer than you think for to the business, now.", "나의 좋은 분들이여, 이제 나는 당신들이 생각하는 것보다 본론에 더 가까이 와 있소."], ["Have you been patient so far?", "여태 참을성 있게 들으셨소?"], ["or have you said to yourselves, ‘Deuce-what-the-deuce!", "아니면 속으로 이랬소? ‘제기랄-이런-제기랄!"], ["Pesca is long-winded to-night?’”", "페스카가 오늘 밤 말이 참 길구먼’ 하고?”"]], [["We declared that we were deeply interested.", "우리는 깊이 흥미로워하고 있노라 밝혔다."], ["The Professor went on:", "교수는 이어 갔다."]], [["“In his hand, the golden Papa has a letter; and after he has made his excuse for disturbing us in our Infernal Region with the common mortal Business of the house, he addresses himself to the three young Misses, and begins, as you English begin everything in this blessed world that you have to say, with a great O.", "“황금빛 파파의 손에는 편지 한 통이 들려 있소. 그리고 집안의 그 예사롭고 속된 볼일로 우리의 지옥에서 우리를 방해하는 데 대해 양해를 구한 뒤, 그는 세 젊은 아가씨에게 몸을 돌려, 당신네 영국인이 이 복된 세상에서 무슨 할 말이든 시작할 때 늘 그러하듯, 거창한 오오 소리로 말을 뗐소."], ["‘O, my dears,’ says the mighty merchant, ‘I have got here a letter from my friend, Mr.——‘(the name has slipped out of my mind; but no matter; we shall come back to that; yes, yes—right-all-right).", "‘오, 얘들아.’ 대단한 상인이 말하오. ‘여기 내 친구 미스터——의 편지가 한 통 왔구나.’ (이름은 내 머릿속에서 미끄러져 나갔소만, 상관없소."], ["So the Papa says, ‘I have got a letter from my friend, the Mister; and he wants a recommend from me, of a drawing-master, to go down to his house in the country.’ My-soul-bless-my-soul!", "다시 돌아올 테니. 그럼, 그럼—좋아-다-좋아.) 그러니까 파파가 말하오."], ["when I heard the golden Papa say those words, if I had been big enough to reach up to him, I should have put my arms round his neck, and pressed him to my bosom in a long and grateful hug!", "‘내 친구 미스터의 편지가 왔는데, 이이가 시골에 있는 제 집으로 내려갈 그림 선생을 하나 나더러 추천해 달라는구나.’ 오-내-영혼에-축복이! 황금빛 파파가 그 말을 하는 걸 들었을 때, 내가 그이에게 닿을 만큼 컸더라면 나는 그이의 목에 두 팔을 두르고 오래도록 고마운 포옹으로 그이를 내 가슴에 끌어안았을 것이오!"], ["As it was, I only bounced upon my chair.", "실은 그저 의자에서 튀어 올랐을 뿐이지만."], ["My seat was on thorns, and my soul was on fire to speak but I held my tongue, and let Papa go on.", "내 자리는 가시방석이었고 내 영혼은 말하고 싶어 불타올랐으나, 나는 혀를 붙들고 파파가 계속하게 두었소."], ["‘Perhaps you know,’ says this good man of money, twiddling his friend’s letter this way and that, in his golden fingers and thumbs, ‘perhaps you know, my dears, of a drawing-master that I can recommend?’ The three young Misses all look at each other, and then say (with the indispensable great O to begin) “O, dear no, Papa!", "‘너희 혹시.’ 이 돈 많은 선량한 양반이 친구의 편지를 황금 같은 손가락과 엄지로 이리저리 만지작거리며 말하오. ‘너희 혹시, 얘들아, 내가 추천할 만한 그림 선생을 아느냐?’ 세 젊은 아가씨가 서로를 바라보더니, (없어서는 안 될 거창한 오오로 시작해) 말하오."], ["But here is Mr.", "‘오, 저런, 아버지!"], ["Pesca——’ At the mention of myself I can hold no longer—the thought of you, my good dears, mounts like blood to my head—I start from my seat, as if a spike had grown up from the ground through the bottom of my chair—I address myself to the mighty merchant, and I say (English phrase) ‘Dear sir, I have the man!", "하지만 여기 페스카 씨가——’ 내 이름이 나오자 나는 더는 참을 수 없었소—당신들 생각이, 나의 좋은 분들이여, 피처럼 내 머리로 솟구쳐 올랐소—나는 마치 땅에서 못이 솟아 의자 밑바닥을 뚫고 올라오기라도 한 듯 자리에서 벌떡 일어나, 그 대단한 상인에게 몸을 돌려 (영어 표현으로) 말하오. ‘친애하는 선생, 나에게 그 사람이 있소이다!"], ["The first and foremost drawing-master of the world!", "세상에서 첫째가고 으뜸가는 그림 선생 말이오!"], ["Recommend him by the post to-night, and send him off, bag and baggage (English phrase again—ha!), send him off, bag and baggage, by the train to-morrow!’ ‘Stop, stop,’ says Papa; ‘is he a foreigner, or an Englishman?’ ‘English to the bone of his back,’ I answer.", "오늘 밤 우편으로 그를 추천하시고, 짐 보따리째(또 영어 표현이오—하!), 짐 보따리째 내일 기차로 그를 떠나보내시오!’ ‘잠깐, 잠깐.’ 파파가 말하오. ‘그이가 외국인이오, 영국인이오?’ ‘등뼈까지 영국인이오.’ 내가 대답하오."], ["‘Respectable?’ says Papa.", "‘점잖은 사람이고?’ 파파가 말하오."], ["‘Sir,’ I say (for this last question of his outrages me, and I have done being familiar with him—) ‘Sir!", "‘선생.’ 내가 말하오(그의 이 마지막 질문이 나를 모욕한지라, 나는 그이에게 스스럼없이 굴기를 그만두었소이다). ‘선생!"], ["the immortal fire of genius burns in this Englishman’s bosom, and, what is more, his father had it before him!’ ‘Never mind,’ says the golden barbarian of a Papa, ‘never mind about his genius, Mr.", "천재의 불멸의 불꽃이 이 영국인의 가슴에 타오르오. 게다가 그의 아버지도 그에 앞서 그것을 지녔었소!’ ‘아무려면 어떻소.’ 이 황금빛 야만인 파파가 말하오."], ["Pesca. We don’t want genius in this country, unless it is accompanied by respectability—and then we are very glad to have it, very glad indeed.", "‘그 천재성일랑 아무래도 좋소, 페스카 씨."], ["Can your friend produce testimonials—letters that speak to his character?’ I wave my hand negligently.", "이 나라에서는 점잖음이 함께 따라오지 않는 한 천재는 필요 없소이다—따라온다면야 매우 기꺼이 맞아들이지요, 참으로 기껍고말고. 당신 친구가 추천장을 내놓을 수 있소—그의 인품을 말해 주는 편지들 말이오?’ 나는 손을 무심히 내저었소."], ["‘Letters?’ I say.", "‘편지요?’ 내가 말하오."], ["‘Ha!", "‘하!"], ["my-soul-bless-my-soul!", "오-내-영혼에-축복이!"], ["I should think so, indeed!", "그야 여부가 있겠소이까!"], ["Volumes of letters and portfolios of testimonials, if you like!’ ‘One or two will do,’ says this man of phlegm and money.", "원한다면 편지도 몇 권 분량이고 추천장도 서류철로 한 아름이오!’ ‘한두 통이면 되오.’ 이 점액질에 돈 많은 사내가 말하오."], ["‘Let him send them to me, with his name and address.", "‘그의 이름과 주소와 함께 나에게 보내라 하시오."], ["And—stop, stop, Mr. Pesca—before you go to your friend, you had better take a note.’ ‘Bank-note!’ I say, indignantly.", "그리고—잠깐, 잠깐, 페스카 씨—당신 친구에게 가기 전에, 각서를 하나 받아 가는 게 좋겠소.’ ‘은행권!’ 나는 발끈해 말하오."], ["‘No bank-note, if you please, till my brave Englishman has earned it first.’ ‘Bank-note!’ says Papa, in a great surprise, ‘who talked of bank-note?", "‘은행권일랑 사양이오, 내 용감한 영국인이 먼저 그것을 벌기 전에는.’ ‘은행권이라니!’ 파파가 몹시 놀라 말하오. ‘누가 은행권을 말했소?"], ["I mean a note of the terms—a memorandum of what he is expected to do.", "내 말은 조건을 적은 각서—그가 해야 할 일을 적은 비망록이오."], ["Go on with your lesson, Mr. Pesca, and I will give you the necessary extract from my friend’s letter.’ Down sits the man of merchandise and money to his pen, ink, and paper; and down I go once again into the Hell of Dante, with my three young Misses after me.", "수업을 계속하시오, 페스카 씨, 내가 친구 편지에서 필요한 대목을 뽑아 주리다.’ 이 장사와 돈의 사내가 펜과 잉크와 종이 앞에 앉고, 나는 세 젊은 아가씨를 뒤딸린 채 다시 한번 단테의 지옥으로 내려가오."], ["In ten minutes’ time the note is written, and the boots of Papa are creaking themselves away in the passage outside.", "십 분쯤 지나 각서가 다 쓰이고, 파파의 구두가 바깥 복도에서 삐걱삐걱 멀어져 가오."], ["From that moment, on my faith, and soul, and honour, I know nothing more!", "그 순간부터, 내 믿음과 영혼과 명예를 걸고, 나는 더는 아무것도 모르오!"], ["The glorious thought that I have caught my opportunity at last, and that my grateful service for my dearest friend in the world is as good as done already, flies up into my head and makes me drunk.", "드디어 내가 기회를 붙잡았다는, 그리고 세상에서 가장 소중한 벗을 위한 나의 고마운 봉사가 이미 다 이루어진 것이나 진배없다는 그 영광스러운 생각이 내 머리로 날아올라 나를 취하게 만드오."], ["How I pull my young Misses and myself out of our Infernal Region again, how my other business is done afterwards, how my little bit of dinner slides itself down my throat, I know no more than a man in the moon.", "내가 어떻게 젊은 아가씨들과 나 자신을 그 지옥에서 다시 끌어냈는지, 그 뒤 내 다른 볼일이 어떻게 처리됐는지, 내 저녁 한 술이 어떻게 목구멍으로 미끄러져 내려갔는지, 나는 달나라 사내만큼도 모르오."], ["Enough for me, that here I am, with the mighty merchant’s note in my hand, as large as life, as hot as fire, and as happy as a king!", "나에게는 이것으로 족하오—내가 지금 여기, 그 대단한 상인의 각서를 손에 들고, 실물처럼 또렷하게, 불처럼 뜨겁게, 임금님처럼 행복하게 서 있다는 것이!"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["right-right-right-all-right!”", "좋아-좋아-좋아-다-좋아!”"], ["Here the Professor waved the memorandum of terms over his head, and ended his long and voluble narrative with his shrill Italian parody on an English cheer.", "여기서 교수는 조건을 적은 비망록을 머리 위로 휘두르며, 영국식 환호를 날카로운 이탈리아식으로 흉내 낸 소리로 길고 유창한 이야기를 마무리했다."]], [["My mother rose the moment he had done, with flushed cheeks and brightened eyes.", "교수가 이야기를 마치는 순간 어머니가 상기된 뺨과 빛나는 눈으로 일어섰다."], ["She caught the little man warmly by both hands.", "그러고는 그 작은 사내의 두 손을 다정하게 붙잡았다."]], [["“My dear, good Pesca,”", "“나의 착하고 좋은 페스카.”"], ["she said, “I never doubted your true affection for Walter—but I am more than ever persuaded of it now!”", "어머니가 말했다. “나는 월터에 대한 당신의 참된 애정을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없어요—하지만 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 더 그것을 믿게 됐답니다!”"]], [["“I am sure we are very much obliged to Professor Pesca, for Walter’s sake,”", "“월터를 위해 우리가 페스카 교수님께 참으로 큰 신세를 진 게 분명해요.”"], ["added Sarah.", "세라가 덧붙였다."], ["She half rose, while she spoke, as if to approach the arm-chair, in her turn; but, observing that Pesca was rapturously kissing my mother’s hands, looked serious, and resumed her seat.", "세라는 말하면서 이번에는 제가 안락의자로 다가가려는 듯 반쯤 일어섰으나, 페스카가 어머니의 손에 황홀하게 입 맞추고 있는 것을 보고는 정색을 하고 도로 자리에 앉았다."], ["“If the familiar little man treats my mother in that way, how will he treat ME?”", "‘저 스스럼없는 작은 사내가 우리 어머니를 저렇게 대한다면, 나는 어떻게 대하려나?’ 얼굴은 때로 진실을 말한다."], ["Faces sometimes tell truth; and that was unquestionably the thought in Sarah’s mind, as she sat down again.", "그리고 그것은 세라가 도로 앉을 때 그 마음에 든 생각임이 틀림없었다."]], [["Although I myself was gratefully sensible of the kindness of Pesca’s motives, my spirits were hardly so much elevated as they ought to have been by the prospect of future employment now placed before me.", "나 자신은 페스카의 그 친절한 마음 씀을 고맙게 느끼면서도, 이제 내 앞에 놓인 앞날의 일자리에 대한 기대만큼 마음이 들뜨지는 않았다."], ["When the Professor had quite done with my mother’s hand, and when I had warmly thanked him for his interference on my behalf, I asked to be allowed to look at the note of terms which his respectable patron had drawn up for my inspection.", "교수가 어머니의 손을 완전히 놓아주고, 내가 나를 대신한 그의 주선에 따뜻이 고마움을 표한 뒤, 나는 그의 점잖은 후원자가 내 검토를 위해 작성했다는 조건 각서를 좀 보게 해 달라 청했다."]], [["Pesca handed me the paper, with a triumphant flourish of the hand.", "페스카가 손을 의기양양하게 휘두르며 나에게 그 종이를 건넸다."]], [["“Read!” said the little man majestically.", "“읽어 보시오!”"], ["“I promise you my friend, the writing of the golden Papa speaks with a tongue of trumpets for itself.”", "작은 사내가 위엄 있게 말했다. “장담하건대 나의 벗이여, 황금빛 파파의 글씨는 나팔의 혀로 제 뜻을 절로 말해 준다오.”"]], [["The note of terms was plain, straightforward, and comprehensive, at any rate.", "어쨌거나 조건 각서는 명료하고, 솔직하고, 빠짐없었다."], ["It informed me,", "그것은 나에게 이렇게 알려 주었다."]], [["First, That Frederick Fairlie, Esquire, of Limmeridge House, Cumberland, wanted to engage the services of a thoroughly competent drawing-master, for a period of four months certain.", "첫째, 컴벌랜드 리머리지 하우스의 프레더릭 페얼리 님이 넉 달을 확정 기간으로 하여 충분히 유능한 그림 선생을 고용하고자 한다는 것."]], [["Secondly, That the duties which the master was expected to perform would be of a twofold kind.", "둘째, 그 선생이 수행해야 할 임무는 두 가지 종류라는 것."], ["He was to superintend the instruction of two young ladies in the art of painting in water-colours; and he was to devote his leisure time, afterwards, to the business of repairing and mounting a valuable collection of drawings, which had been suffered to fall into a condition of total neglect.", "그는 두 젊은 숙녀에게 수채화 그리는 법을 가르치는 일을 감독해야 하며, 그 뒤 틈나는 시간은 완전한 방치 상태에 놓이도록 내버려 둔 값진 소묘 수집품을 손질하고 표구하는 일에 바쳐야 한다는 것."]], [["Thirdly, That the terms offered to the person who should undertake and properly perform these duties were four guineas a week; that he was to reside at Limmeridge House; and that he was to be treated there on the footing of a gentleman.", "셋째, 이 임무를 맡아 제대로 수행할 사람에게 제시되는 조건은 주급 사 기니이며, 리머리지 하우스에 거주해야 하고, 그곳에서 신사의 대우를 받게 되리라는 것."]], [["Fourthly, and lastly, That no person need think of applying for this situation unless he could furnish the most unexceptionable references to character and abilities.", "넷째, 그리고 마지막으로, 인품과 능력에 대해 나무랄 데 없는 추천을 갖출 수 없는 사람은 이 자리에 지원할 생각을 아예 하지 말라는 것."], ["The references were to be sent to Mr. Fairlie’s friend in London, who was empowered to conclude all necessary arrangements.", "추천장은 런던에 있는 페얼리 씨의 친구에게 보내야 하며, 그 친구가 필요한 모든 절차를 마무리할 권한을 위임받았다는 것."], ["These instructions were followed by the name and address of Pesca’s employer in Portland Place—and there the note, or memorandum, ended.", "이 지시에 이어 포틀랜드 플레이스에 있는 페스카의 고용주 이름과 주소가 적혀 있었고—각서, 곧 비망록은 거기서 끝났다."]], [["The prospect which this offer of an engagement held out was certainly an attractive one.", "이 고용 제안이 내밀어 보이는 앞날은 분명 매력적인 것이었다."], ["The employment was likely to be both easy and agreeable; it was proposed to me at the autumn time of the year when I was least occupied; and the terms, judging by my personal experience in my profession, were surprisingly liberal.", "그 일자리는 수월하면서도 유쾌할 것 같았다. 그것은 내가 가장 한가한 가을철에 나에게 제안되었고, 조건은 내 직업에서의 개인적 경험으로 미루어 놀랍도록 후했다."], ["I knew this; I knew that I ought to consider myself very fortunate if I succeeded in securing the offered employment—and yet, no sooner had I read the memorandum than I felt an inexplicable unwillingness within me to stir in the matter.", "나는 그것을 알고 있었다. 그 제안된 일자리를 손에 넣는 데 성공한다면 나는 스스로를 매우 운 좋은 사람으로 여겨야 마땅함을 알고 있었다—그런데도, 그 비망록을 읽자마자 나는 이 일에 나설 마음이 나지 않는, 설명할 길 없는 거리낌을 내 안에서 느꼈다."], ["I had never in the whole of my previous experience found my duty and my inclination so painfully and so unaccountably at variance as I found them now.", "내 지난 경험을 통틀어, 내 의무와 내 마음이 지금처럼 이토록 괴롭게, 이토록 까닭 없이 어긋난 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["“Oh, Walter, your father never had such a chance as this!”", "“오, 월터, 네 아버지도 이런 기회는 없었단다!”"], ["said my mother, when she had read the note of terms and had handed it back to me.", "어머니가 조건 각서를 읽고 나에게 도로 건네주며 말했다."]], [["“Such distinguished people to know,”", "“그처럼 훌륭한 분들과 알고 지내다니.”"], ["remarked Sarah, straightening herself in the chair; “and on such gratifying terms of equality too!”", "세라가 의자에서 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “게다가 그처럼 흐뭇할 만큼 대등한 조건으로 말이에요!”"]], [["“Yes, yes; the terms, in every sense, are tempting enough,”", "“그래, 그래. 조건은 어느 모로 보나 충분히 솔깃하지.”"], ["I replied impatiently.", "나는 조바심을 내며 대꾸했다."], ["“But before I send in my testimonials, I should like a little time to consider——”", "“하지만 추천장을 보내기 전에, 좀 생각할 시간을 가졌으면 해서——”"]], [["“Consider!”", "“생각이라니!”"], ["exclaimed my mother.", "어머니가 소리쳤다."], ["“Why, Walter, what is the matter with you?”", "“아니, 월터, 대체 너 왜 그러니?”"]], [["“Consider!”", "“생각이라니!”"], ["echoed my sister.", "누이가 되받았다."], ["“What a very extraordinary thing to say, under the circumstances!”", "“이런 형편에 하기엔 참 별스러운 소리네!”"]], [["“Consider!”", "“생각이라니!”"], ["chimed in the Professor.", "교수도 끼어들었다."], ["“What is there to consider about?", "“생각할 게 무어가 있소?"], ["Answer me this!", "이 물음에 답해 보시오!"], ["Have you not been complaining of your health, and have you not been longing for what you call a smack of the country breeze?", "그대는 건강이 좋지 않다 투덜대지 않았소, 그대가 이른바 시골 바람 한 모금이라 부르는 것을 그리워하지 않았소?"], ["Well!", "자!"], ["there in your hand is the paper that offers you perpetual choking mouthfuls of country breeze for four months’ time.", "지금 그대 손안에 있는 저 종이가 넉 달 동안 시골 바람을 목이 메도록 실컷 들이켤 것을 그대에게 내밀고 있소이다."], ["Is it not so?", "그렇지 아니하오?"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Again—you want money.", "또—그대는 돈을 원하오."], ["Well!", "자!"], ["Is four golden guineas a week nothing?", "주급 사 황금 기니가 아무것도 아니오?"], ["My-soul-bless-my-soul!", "오-내-영혼에-축복이!"], ["only give it to me—and my boots shall creak like the golden Papa’s, with a sense of the overpowering richness of the man who walks in them!", "그걸 나에게만 준다면—내 구두도 황금빛 파파의 것처럼, 그 안에서 걷는 사내의 넘쳐 나는 부유함을 뽐내며 삐걱거릴 것이오!"], ["Four guineas a week, and, more than that, the charming society of two young misses!", "주급 사 기니, 게다가 그 위에 두 젊은 아가씨의 매혹적인 벗까지!"], ["and, more than that, your bed, your breakfast, your dinner, your gorging English teas and lunches and drinks of foaming beer, all for nothing—why, Walter, my dear good friend—deuce-what-the-deuce!—for the first time in my life I have not eyes enough in my head to look, and wonder at you!”", "게다가 그 위에 그대의 잠자리, 그대의 아침, 그대의 저녁, 그대가 배 터지게 먹을 영국식 차와 점심과 거품 이는 맥주까지 죄다 거저—아니, 월터, 나의 착하고 좋은 벗이여—제기랄-이런-제기랄!—내 평생 처음으로, 그대를 보며 놀라워하기에 내 머릿속 눈이 모자라오!”"]], [["Neither my mother’s evident astonishment at my behaviour, nor Pesca’s fervid enumeration of the advantages offered to me by the new employment, had any effect in shaking my unreasonable disinclination to go to Limmeridge House.", "내 태도에 대한 어머니의 뚜렷한 놀라움도, 새 일자리가 나에게 내미는 이점에 대한 페스카의 열띤 열거도, 리머리지 하우스로 가기 싫은 나의 까닭 없는 마음을 흔드는 데는 아무런 힘이 없었다."], ["After starting all the petty objections that I could think of to going to Cumberland, and after hearing them answered, one after another, to my own complete discomfiture, I tried to set up a last obstacle by asking what was to become of my pupils in London while I was teaching Mr. Fairlie’s young ladies to sketch from nature.", "컴벌랜드로 가는 것에 대해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 자잘한 반대를 다 꺼내 보고, 그것들이 하나하나 반박당해 내가 완전히 낭패한 뒤, 나는 마지막 걸림돌을 세워 보려고, 내가 페얼리 씨의 젊은 숙녀들에게 자연을 사생하는 법을 가르치는 동안 런던의 내 제자들은 어떻게 되느냐고 물었다."], ["The obvious answer to this was, that the greater part of them would be away on their autumn travels, and that the few who remained at home might be confided to the care of one of my brother drawing-masters, whose pupils I had once taken off his hands under similar circumstances.", "이에 대한 뻔한 대답은, 그들 대부분은 가을 여행을 떠나 있을 테고, 집에 남아 있는 몇 안 되는 이들은 내 동료 그림 선생 하나에게 맡기면 된다는 것이었다—전에 비슷한 형편에서 내가 그의 제자들을 대신 맡아 준 적이 있는 사람이었다. 누이는 그 신사분이 내가 시내를 떠나기를 원할 경우 이번 철에 기꺼이 제 도움을 내주겠노라 분명히 밝혔음을 나에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["My sister reminded me that this gentleman had expressly placed his services at my disposal, during the present season, in case I wished to leave town; my mother seriously appealed to me not to let an idle caprice stand in the way of my own interests and my own health; and Pesca piteously entreated that I would not wound him to the heart by rejecting the first grateful offer of service that he had been able to make to the friend who had saved his life.", "어머니는 부질없는 변덕이 나 자신의 이익과 나 자신의 건강을 가로막게 두지 말라고 진지하게 호소했다. 그리고 페스카는, 제 목숨을 구해 준 벗에게 처음으로 내밀 수 있게 된 그 고마운 봉사의 제안을 내가 물리침으로써 제 가슴에 상처 내지 말아 달라고 애처롭게 간청했다."]], [["The evident sincerity and affection which inspired these remonstrances would have influenced any man with an atom of good feeling in his composition.", "이 만류에 서린 뚜렷한 진심과 애정은 조금이라도 좋은 마음을 지닌 사람이라면 누구든 움직였을 것이다."], ["Though I could not conquer my own unaccountable perversity, I had at least virtue enough to be heartily ashamed of it, and to end the discussion pleasantly by giving way, and promising to do all that was wanted of me.", "나 자신의 그 까닭 모를 삐딱함을 이기지는 못했으나, 나에게는 적어도 그것을 진심으로 부끄러워하고, 뜻을 굽혀 요구되는 대로 다 하겠노라 약속함으로써 이 논의를 기분 좋게 끝맺을 만큼의 미덕은 있었다."]], [["The rest of the evening passed merrily enough in humorous anticipations of my coming life with the two young ladies in Cumberland.", "그날 저녁의 나머지는 컴벌랜드에서 두 젊은 숙녀와 함께할 내 앞날을 익살스레 그려 보며 제법 흥겹게 흘러갔다."], ["Pesca, inspired by our national grog, which appeared to get into his head, in the most marvellous manner, five minutes after it had gone down his throat, asserted his claims to be considered a complete Englishman by making a series of speeches in rapid succession, proposing my mother’s health, my sister’s health, my health, and the healths, in mass, of Mr.", "페스카는 우리의 국민적 술 그로그에 힘입어—그 술은 목구멍으로 넘어간 지 오 분 만에 더없이 신기한 방식으로 그의 머리로 올라가는 듯했다—빠르게 잇달아 여러 차례 연설을 함으로써 완전한 영국인으로 대접받을 제 자격을 내세웠다. 어머니의 건강, 누이의 건강, 나의 건강, 그리고 페얼리 씨와 두 젊은 아가씨의 건강을 한꺼번에 위하는 축배를 제안하고는, 곧바로 저 스스로 일동을 대신해 애처롭게 답례의 인사를 올렸다."], ["Fairlie and the two young Misses, pathetically returning thanks himself, immediately afterwards, for the whole party. “A secret, Walter,”", "“비밀 하나 말하리다, 월터.”"], ["said my little friend confidentially, as we walked home together.", "우리가 함께 집으로 걸어가는 동안 나의 작은 벗이 은밀히 말했다."], ["“I am flushed by the recollection of my own eloquence.", "“내 자신의 웅변을 떠올리니 얼굴이 화끈거리오."], ["My soul bursts itself with ambition.", "내 영혼이 야망으로 터질 것 같소."], ["One of these days I go into your noble Parliament.", "언젠가 나는 그대들의 고귀한 의회로 들어가겠소."], ["It is the dream of my whole life to be Honourable Pesca, M.P.!”", "하원 의원 페스카 각하가 되는 것이 내 온 삶의 꿈이라오!”"]], [["The next morning I sent my testimonials to the Professor’s employer in Portland Place.", "이튿날 아침 나는 포틀랜드 플레이스에 있는 교수의 고용주에게 내 추천장을 보냈다."], ["Three days passed, and I concluded, with secret satisfaction, that my papers had not been found sufficiently explicit.", "사흘이 지났고, 나는 은근한 만족감과 함께, 내 서류가 충분히 명확하다고 판정받지 못한 모양이라 짐작했다."], ["On the fourth day, however, an answer came.", "그러나 나흗날에 답이 왔다."], ["It announced that Mr. Fairlie accepted my services, and requested me to start for Cumberland immediately.", "페얼리 씨가 나를 고용하기로 했으며 즉시 컴벌랜드로 출발해 달라는 내용이었다."], ["All the necessary instructions for my journey were carefully and clearly added in a postscript.", "여정에 필요한 모든 지시가 추신에 꼼꼼하고도 또렷하게 덧붙여져 있었다."]], [["I made my arrangements, unwillingly enough, for leaving London early the next day.", "나는 몹시 내키지 않는 마음으로, 이튿날 이른 아침에 런던을 떠날 채비를 갖추었다."], ["Towards evening Pesca looked in, on his way to a dinner-party, to bid me good-bye.", "저녁 무렵 페스카가 만찬 모임에 가는 길에 작별 인사를 하러 들렀다."]], [["“I shall dry my tears in your absence,” said the Professor gaily, “with this glorious thought.", "“그대가 없는 동안 나는 이 영광스러운 생각으로 눈물을 닦으리다.”"], ["It is my auspicious hand that has given the first push to your fortune in the world.", "교수가 유쾌하게 말했다. “그대의 세상 출셋길에 첫 밀침을 준 것이 바로 이 상서로운 내 손이라는 생각으로 말이오."], ["Go, my friend!", "가시오, 나의 벗이여!"], ["When your sun shines in Cumberland (English proverb), in the name of heaven make your hay.", "그대의 해가 컴벌랜드에서 빛나거든(영국 속담이오), 하늘에 맹세코 그대의 건초를 거두시오. 두 젊은 아가씨 가운데 하나와 혼인하시오."], ["Marry one of the two young Misses; become Honourable Hartright, M.P.; and when you are on the top of the ladder remember that Pesca, at the bottom, has done it all!”", "하원 의원 하트라이트 각하가 되시오. 그리고 그대가 사다리 꼭대기에 올랐을 때, 밑바닥에 있는 페스카가 그 모든 것을 해냈음을 기억하시오!”"]], [["I tried to laugh with my little friend over his parting jest, but my spirits were not to be commanded.", "나는 작별 농담을 던지는 작은 벗과 함께 웃어 보려 했으나, 내 마음은 마음대로 되지 않았다."], ["Something jarred in me almost painfully while he was speaking his light farewell words.", "그가 가벼운 작별의 말을 하는 동안 내 안에서 무언가가 거의 아플 만큼 삐걱거렸다."]], [["When I was left alone again nothing remained to be done but to walk to the Hampstead cottage and bid my mother and Sarah good-bye.", "다시 혼자 남겨졌을 때, 이제 할 일이라고는 햄스테드 오두막까지 걸어가 어머니와 세라에게 작별을 고하는 것밖에 남지 않았다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["The heat had been painfully oppressive all day, and it was now a close and sultry night.", "더위는 온종일 견디기 괴로울 만큼 숨 막혔고, 이제는 후텁지근하고 무더운 밤이었다."]], [["My mother and sister had spoken so many last words, and had begged me to wait another five minutes so many times, that it was nearly midnight when the servant locked the garden-gate behind me.", "어머니와 누이가 마지막 말을 어찌나 많이 했던지, 그리고 오 분만 더 기다리라고 어찌나 여러 번 붙들었던지, 하인이 내 뒤로 정원 문을 잠갔을 때는 거의 자정이 다 된 시각이었다."], ["I walked forward a few paces on the shortest way back to London, then stopped and hesitated.", "나는 런던으로 돌아가는 가장 가까운 길을 따라 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가다가, 멈춰 서서 머뭇거렸다."]], [["The moon was full and broad in the dark blue starless sky, and the broken ground of the heath looked wild enough in the mysterious light to be hundreds of miles away from the great city that lay beneath it.", "달은 별 하나 없는 짙푸른 하늘에 둥글고 널따랗게 떠 있었고, 히스 들판의 울퉁불퉁한 땅은 그 신비로운 빛 속에서, 아래에 펼쳐진 대도시로부터 수백 마일은 떨어져 있는 듯 자못 황량해 보였다."], ["The idea of descending any sooner than I could help into the heat and gloom of London repelled me.", "어쩔 수 없이 내려가야 할 때보다 조금이라도 더 일찍 런던의 열기와 침침함 속으로 내려간다는 생각이 나를 물리쳤다."], ["The prospect of going to bed in my airless chambers, and the prospect of gradual suffocation, seemed, in my present restless frame of mind and body, to be one and the same thing.", "바람 한 점 통하지 않는 하숙방에서 잠자리에 든다는 것과 서서히 질식해 간다는 것은, 지금 이 안절부절못하는 내 몸과 마음의 상태에서는 하나이자 같은 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["I determined to stroll home in the purer air by the most roundabout way I could take; to follow the white winding paths across the lonely heath; and to approach London through its most open suburb by striking into the Finchley Road, and so getting back, in the cool of the new morning, by the western side of the Regent’s Park.", "나는 갈 수 있는 가장 에두르는 길로 더 맑은 공기를 마시며 어슬렁어슬렁 집으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 외로운 히스 들판을 가로지르는 하얗게 굽이진 오솔길을 따라가다가, 핀칠리 로드로 접어들어 가장 탁 트인 교외를 거쳐 런던으로 다가가고, 그렇게 동틀 무렵의 서늘함 속에서 리전트 파크의 서쪽으로 돌아오기로 한 것이다."]], [["I wound my way down slowly over the heath, enjoying the divine stillness of the scene, and admiring the soft alternations of light and shade as they followed each other over the broken ground on every side of me.", "나는 그 광경의 거룩한 고요를 만끽하며, 그리고 내 사방의 울퉁불퉁한 땅 위로 빛과 그늘이 서로 뒤따르며 부드럽게 갈마드는 모습에 감탄하며, 히스 들판을 천천히 굽이굽이 내려갔다."], ["So long as I was proceeding through this first and prettiest part of my night walk my mind remained passively open to the impressions produced by the view; and I thought but little on any subject—indeed, so far as my own sensations were concerned, I can hardly say that I thought at all.", "이 밤 산책의 첫머리이자 가장 어여쁜 대목을 지나는 동안에는, 내 마음은 그 풍경이 자아내는 인상에 그저 가만히 열려 있었다. 나는 어떤 주제에 대해서도 거의 아무 생각을 하지 않았다—실로, 내 자신의 감각에 관한 한, 나는 아예 생각이란 걸 했다고 하기 어려울 정도였다."]], [["But when I had left the heath and had turned into the by-road, where there was less to see, the ideas naturally engendered by the approaching change in my habits and occupations gradually drew more and more of my attention exclusively to themselves.", "그러나 히스 들판을 벗어나 볼 것이 별로 없는 샛길로 접어들자, 곧 닥칠 내 생활과 일의 변화가 자연히 불러일으키는 생각들이 차츰차츰 내 주의를 오로지 그것들 쪽으로만 끌어당겼다."], ["By the time I had arrived at the end of the road I had become completely absorbed in my own fanciful visions of Limmeridge House, of Mr. Fairlie, and of the two ladies whose practice in the art of water-colour painting I was so soon to superintend.", "그 길의 끝에 다다랐을 무렵 나는 리머리지 하우스에 대한, 페얼리 씨에 대한, 그리고 머잖아 내가 수채화 연습을 감독하게 될 두 숙녀에 대한 나 자신의 부질없는 공상에 완전히 빠져 있었다."]], [["I had now arrived at that particular point of my walk where four roads met—the road to Hampstead, along which I had returned, the road to Finchley, the road to West End, and the road back to London.", "나는 이제 네 갈래 길이 만나는, 내 산책의 바로 그 지점에 이르러 있었다—내가 되돌아온 햄스테드로 가는 길, 핀칠리로 가는 길, 웨스트엔드로 가는 길, 그리고 런던으로 돌아가는 길."], ["I had mechanically turned in this latter direction, and was strolling along the lonely high-road—idly wondering, I remember, what the Cumberland young ladies would look like—when, in one moment, every drop of blood in my body was brought to a stop by the touch of a hand laid lightly and suddenly on my shoulder from behind me.", "나는 기계적으로 이 마지막 방향으로 접어들어, 컴벌랜드의 젊은 숙녀들이 어떻게 생겼을까 하염없이 궁금해하며—그렇게 기억한다—외로운 큰길을 따라 어슬렁거리고 있었다—바로 그때, 순식간에, 등 뒤에서 누군가의 손이 내 어깨 위에 가볍게 그리고 느닷없이 얹히는 감촉에 내 몸의 피가 방울방울 죄다 멎어 버렸다."]], [["I turned on the instant, with my fingers tightening round the handle of my stick.", "나는 지팡이 손잡이를 감싼 손가락에 힘을 주며 그 즉시 돌아섰다."]], [["There, in the middle of the broad bright high-road—there, as if it had that moment sprung out of the earth or dropped from the heaven—stood the figure of a solitary Woman, dressed from head to foot in white garments, her face bent in grave inquiry on mine, her hand pointing to the dark cloud over London, as I faced her.", "거기, 넓고 환한 큰길 한복판에—거기, 바로 그 순간 땅에서 솟아났거나 하늘에서 떨어지기라도 한 듯—머리끝부터 발끝까지 흰옷을 두른 한 외로운 여인의 모습이 서 있었다. 내가 그녀를 마주하자, 그녀의 얼굴은 나를 향해 진지한 물음을 담은 채 숙여져 있었고, 그녀의 손은 런던 위에 드리운 검은 구름을 가리키고 있었다."]], [["I was far too seriously startled by the suddenness with which this extraordinary apparition stood before me, in the dead of night and in that lonely place, to ask what she wanted.", "이 심상찮은 유령 같은 존재가 깊은 밤, 그 외진 곳에서 내 앞에 불쑥 나타난 그 느닷없음에 나는 너무나 심하게 놀란 나머지, 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 물을 겨를도 없었다."], ["The strange woman spoke first.", "낯선 여인이 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“Is that the road to London?”", "“저게 런던으로 가는 길인가요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["I looked attentively at her, as she put that singular question to me.", "그 유별난 물음을 던지는 그녀를 나는 유심히 바라보았다."], ["It was then nearly one o’clock.", "그때는 거의 한 시가 다 된 시각이었다."], ["All I could discern distinctly by the moonlight was a colourless, youthful face, meagre and sharp to look at about the cheeks and chin; large, grave, wistfully attentive eyes; nervous, uncertain lips; and light hair of a pale, brownish-yellow hue.", "달빛으로 내가 또렷이 분간할 수 있었던 것이라고는, 핏기 없이 앳된 얼굴—뺨과 턱께가 야위고 날카로워 보이는—커다랗고 진지하며 애틋하게 골똘한 눈, 초조하고 미덥지 못한 입술, 그리고 옅고 누르스름한 갈색빛의 밝은 머리칼뿐이었다."], ["There was nothing wild, nothing immodest in her manner: it was quiet and self-controlled, a little melancholy and a little touched by suspicion; not exactly the manner of a lady, and, at the same time, not the manner of a woman in the humblest rank of life.", "그녀의 태도에는 거친 데도, 정숙하지 못한 데도 없었다. 조용하고 자제된, 조금은 우수에 젖고 조금은 의심에 물든 태도였다—딱히 숙녀의 태도라고 할 수도 없으면서, 동시에 세상에서 가장 낮은 신분의 여인의 태도라고 할 수도 없었다."], ["The voice, little as I had yet heard of it, had something curiously still and mechanical in its tones, and the utterance was remarkably rapid.", "그 목소리는, 아직 내가 들은 것이 얼마 되지 않았음에도, 그 어조에 이상하리만치 잔잔하고 기계적인 무언가가 있었으며, 말투는 유난히 빨랐다."], ["She held a small bag in her hand: and her dress—bonnet, shawl, and gown all of white—was, so far as I could guess, certainly not composed of very delicate or very expensive materials.", "그녀는 손에 작은 가방 하나를 들고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 옷차림—보닛도, 숄도, 가운도 모두 흰색인—은, 내가 짐작하는 한, 분명 그리 곱거나 값비싼 감으로 지은 것은 아니었다."], ["Her figure was slight, and rather above the average height—her gait and actions free from the slightest approach to extravagance.", "그녀의 몸매는 가냘프고 키는 보통보다 조금 큰 편이었으며—그 걸음걸이와 몸짓에는 지나침이라고는 티끌만큼도 없었다."], ["This was all that I could observe of her in the dim light and under the perplexingly strange circumstances of our meeting.", "이것이 그 어스름한 빛 속에서, 그리고 우리 만남의 어리둥절할 만큼 기이한 형편 속에서 내가 그녀에 대해 살펴볼 수 있었던 전부였다."], ["What sort of a woman she was, and how she came to be out alone in the high-road, an hour after midnight, I altogether failed to guess.", "그녀가 어떤 부류의 여인인지, 자정이 지난 지 한 시간이나 된 시각에 어쩌다 홀로 큰길에 나와 있게 되었는지, 나는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다."], ["The one thing of which I felt certain was, that the grossest of mankind could not have misconstrued her motive in speaking, even at that suspiciously late hour and in that suspiciously lonely place.", "다만 한 가지 확신이 든 것은, 아무리 미심쩍게 늦은 시각이고 아무리 미심쩍게 외진 곳이라 해도, 인간 가운데 가장 상스러운 자라 할지라도 그녀가 말을 건 그 뜻만은 그릇 해석할 수 없으리라는 것이었다."]], [["“Did you hear me?”", "“제 말 들으셨어요?”"], ["she said, still quietly and rapidly, and without the least fretfulness or impatience.", "그녀는 조금의 짜증이나 조바심도 없이 여전히 조용하고 빠르게 말했다."], ["“I asked if that was the way to London.”", "“저게 런던으로 가는 길이냐고 물었어요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["I replied, “that is the way: it leads to St.", "내가 대답했다. “저 길입니다."], ["John’s Wood and the Regent’s Park.", "세인트존스우드와 리전트 파크로 이어지지요."], ["You must excuse my not answering you before.", "제가 곧바로 대답 못 한 걸 용서하세요."], ["I was rather startled by your sudden appearance in the road; and I am, even now, quite unable to account for it.”", "길에 갑자기 나타나신 통에 좀 놀라서요. 지금도 그게 도무지 납득이 안 됩니다.”"]], [["“You don’t suspect me of doing anything wrong, do you?", "“제가 무슨 나쁜 짓이라도 한다고 의심하시는 건 아니지요?"], ["I have done nothing wrong.", "저는 나쁜 짓 안 했어요."], ["I have met with an accident—I am very unfortunate in being here alone so late.", "사고를 당했을 뿐이에요—이렇게 늦은 밤에 혼자 여기 있게 되다니 정말 불운해요."], ["Why do you suspect me of doing wrong?”", "왜 저를 나쁜 짓 한다고 의심하세요?”"]], [["She spoke with unnecessary earnestness and agitation, and shrank back from me several paces.", "그녀는 쓸데없이 간절하고 동요된 어조로 말하며, 나에게서 몇 걸음 뒷걸음질쳤다."], ["I did my best to reassure her.", "나는 그녀를 안심시키려 최선을 다했다."]], [["“Pray don’t suppose that I have any idea of suspecting you,”", "“제가 당신을 의심할 생각이 조금이라도 있다고 여기지 마세요.”"], ["I said, “or any other wish than to be of assistance to you, if I can.", "내가 말했다. “할 수만 있다면 당신을 돕고 싶다는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없습니다."], ["I only wondered at your appearance in the road, because it seemed to me to be empty the instant before I saw you.”", "그저 길에 당신이 나타난 것이 놀라웠을 뿐이에요. 당신을 보기 바로 직전까지만 해도 길이 텅 비어 있는 것만 같았거든요.”"]], [["She turned, and pointed back to a place at the junction of the road to London and the road to Hampstead, where there was a gap in the hedge.", "그녀는 몸을 돌려, 런던으로 가는 길과 햄스테드로 가는 길이 만나는 지점, 산울타리에 틈이 벌어진 곳을 되짚어 가리켰다."]], [["“I heard you coming,”", "“당신이 오는 소리를 들었어요.”"], ["she said, “and hid there to see what sort of man you were, before I risked speaking.", "그녀가 말했다. “그래서 말을 걸어 볼 엄두를 내기 전에, 당신이 어떤 사람인지 보려고 저기 숨어 있었지요."], ["I doubted and feared about it till you passed; and then I was obliged to steal after you, and touch you.”", "당신이 지나갈 때까지도 그럴까 말까 두려워 망설였어요. 그러고는 하는 수 없이 몰래 뒤따라와 당신을 건드린 거고요.”"]], [["Steal after me and touch me?", "몰래 뒤따라와 나를 건드렸다고?"], ["Why not call to me?", "어째서 나를 부르지 않고?"], ["Strange, to say the least of it.", "아무리 좋게 말해도 기이한 일이었다."]], [["“May I trust you?” she asked.", "“당신을 믿어도 될까요?”"], ["“You don’t think the worse of me because I have met with an accident?”", "그녀가 물었다. “제가 사고를 당했다고 해서 저를 나쁘게 보시진 않는 거죠?”"], ["She stopped in confusion; shifted her bag from one hand to the other; and sighed bitterly.", "그녀는 당황해 말을 멈추더니, 가방을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 옮겨 쥐고, 쓰라리게 한숨지었다."]], [["The loneliness and helplessness of the woman touched me.", "이 여인의 외로움과 무력함이 내 마음을 움직였다."], ["The natural impulse to assist her and to spare her got the better of the judgment, the caution, the worldly tact, which an older, wiser, and colder man might have summoned to help him in this strange emergency.", "그녀를 돕고 아껴 주고 싶은 자연스러운 충동이, 좀 더 나이 들고 지혜롭고 냉정한 사람이라면 이 기이한 위급함에서 제 편으로 불러냈을 법한 판단과 조심성과 세상 물정에 밝은 요령을 눌러 버렸다."]], [["“You may trust me for any harmless purpose,” I said.", "“해롭지 않은 일이라면 무엇이든 저를 믿으셔도 됩니다.”"], ["“If it troubles you to explain your strange situation to me, don’t think of returning to the subject again.", "내가 말했다. “당신의 그 기이한 처지를 저에게 설명하는 것이 괴로우시다면, 다시는 그 이야기로 돌아갈 생각 마세요."], ["I have no right to ask you for any explanations.", "저는 당신에게 아무 설명도 청할 권리가 없습니다."], ["Tell me how I can help you; and if I can, I will.”", "제가 어떻게 도와드리면 될지 말씀만 하세요. 할 수 있다면 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“You are very kind, and I am very, very thankful to have met you.”", "“정말 친절하시네요, 그리고 당신을 만나서 정말, 정말 고마워요.”"], ["The first touch of womanly tenderness that I had heard from her trembled in her voice as she said the words; but no tears glistened in those large, wistfully attentive eyes of hers, which were still fixed on me.", "그 말을 할 때 그녀의 목소리에는, 내가 그녀에게서 처음 들은 여인다운 다정함의 기미가 떨렸다. 그러나 여전히 나에게 고정된 그 크고 애틋하게 골똘한 눈에는 눈물이 반짝이지 않았다."], ["“I have only been in London once before,”", "“저는 전에 딱 한 번 런던에 와 봤을 뿐이에요.”"], ["she went on, more and more rapidly, “and I know nothing about that side of it, yonder.", "그녀는 점점 더 빠르게 이어 갔다. “그래서 저기 저쪽은 아무것도 몰라요."], ["Can I get a fly, or a carriage of any kind?", "삯마차나 아무 마차라도 잡을 수 있을까요?"], ["Is it too late?", "너무 늦었나요?"], ["I don’t know.", "모르겠어요."], ["If you could show me where to get a fly—and if you will only promise not to interfere with me, and to let me leave you, when and how I please—I have a friend in London who will be glad to receive me—I want nothing else—will you promise?”", "삯마차를 어디서 잡는지 알려 주시면—그리고 저를 방해하지 않겠다고, 제가 원하는 때에 원하는 대로 당신 곁을 떠나게 해 주겠다고 약속만 해 주시면—런던에 저를 반겨 맞아 줄 친구가 하나 있어요—그 밖에는 아무것도 바라지 않아요—약속해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["She looked anxiously up and down the road; shifted her bag again from one hand to the other; repeated the words, “Will you promise?” and looked hard in my face, with a pleading fear and confusion that it troubled me to see.", "그녀는 근심스레 길 위아래를 살피고, 다시 가방을 한 손에서 다른 손으로 옮겨 쥐고, “약속해 주시겠어요?”라는 말을 되풀이하고는, 보고 있기 괴로울 만큼 애원하는 두려움과 혼란을 담아 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["What could I do?", "내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Here was a stranger utterly and helplessly at my mercy—and that stranger a forlorn woman.", "여기, 아주 속수무책으로 내 처분에 내맡겨진 한 낯선 이가 있었다—그리고 그 낯선 이는 의지가지없는 한 여인이었다."], ["No house was near; no one was passing whom I could consult; and no earthly right existed on my part to give me a power of control over her, even if I had known how to exercise it.", "가까이에는 집도 없었고, 의논해 볼 지나가는 이도 없었으며, 설령 그 방법을 안다 한들 그녀를 다스릴 권한을 나에게 줄 어떠한 세상의 권리도 내 쪽에는 없었다."], ["I trace these lines, self-distrustfully, with the shadows of after-events darkening the very paper I write on; and still I say, what could I do?", "나는 뒷날 벌어진 일들의 그늘이 내가 지금 글을 쓰는 바로 이 종이를 어둡게 물들이는 가운데, 나 자신을 미덥지 못해하며 이 줄들을 적는다. 그럼에도 나는 다시 말한다, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["What I did do, was to try and gain time by questioning her.", "내가 정작 한 일은, 그녀에게 이것저것 물어 시간을 벌어 보려는 것이었다."], ["“Are you sure that your friend in London will receive you at such a late hour as this?”", "“런던의 그 친구분이 이렇게 늦은 시각에 당신을 맞아 주리라 확신하십니까?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Quite sure.", "“틀림없어요."], ["Only say you will let me leave you when and how I please—only say you won’t interfere with me.", "제가 원하는 때에 원하는 대로 당신 곁을 떠나게 해 주겠다는 말만 해 주세요—저를 방해하지 않겠다는 말만요."], ["Will you promise?”", "약속해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["As she repeated the words for the third time, she came close to me and laid her hand, with a sudden gentle stealthiness, on my bosom—a thin hand; a cold hand (when I removed it with mine) even on that sultry night.", "세 번째로 그 말을 되풀이하면서, 그녀는 나에게 바짝 다가와 느닷없이 부드럽고 은밀하게 제 손을 내 가슴에 얹었다—가느다란 손이었다. 그 후텁지근한 밤인데도, (내 손으로 그 손을 떼어 냈을 때) 차가운 손이었다."], ["Remember that I was young; remember that the hand which touched me was a woman’s.", "내가 젊었음을 기억하라. 나를 건드린 그 손이 한 여인의 손이었음을 기억하라."]], [["“Will you promise?”", "“약속해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“네.”"]], [["One word!", "한 마디!"], ["The little familiar word that is on everybody’s lips, every hour in the day.", "하루에도 시간마다 누구나 입에 올리는 그 흔하고 친숙한 한 마디. 아, 나여!"], ["Oh me! and I tremble, now, when I write it.", "그리고 이제 그것을 적으면서 나는 떨고 있다."]], [["We set our faces towards London, and walked on together in the first still hour of the new day—I, and this woman, whose name, whose character, whose story, whose objects in life, whose very presence by my side, at that moment, were fathomless mysteries to me.", "우리는 런던 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌려, 새로운 하루의 첫 고요한 시각 속을 함께 걸어갔다—나와, 그 이름도, 그 성품도, 그 사연도, 그 삶의 목적도, 그 순간 내 곁에 있다는 사실 그 자체마저도 나에게는 헤아릴 길 없는 수수께끼였던 이 여인이."], ["It was like a dream.", "마치 꿈만 같았다."], ["Was I Walter Hartright?", "내가 정말 월터 하트라이트였던가?"], ["Was this the well-known, uneventful road, where holiday people strolled on Sundays?", "이것이 정말, 휴일이면 나들이객들이 일요일마다 거니는 그 잘 알려진, 별일 없는 길이었던가?"], ["Had I really left, little more than an hour since, the quiet, decent, conventionally domestic atmosphere of my mother’s cottage?", "내가 정말 한 시간 남짓 전에 어머니 오두막의 그 조용하고 점잖고 판에 박힌 가정의 분위기를 떠나온 것이었던가?"], ["I was too bewildered—too conscious also of a vague sense of something like self-reproach—to speak to my strange companion for some minutes.", "나는 너무나 얼떨떨했고—또한 무언가 자책에 가까운 어렴풋한 느낌을 너무나 뚜렷이 의식하고 있었다—그래서 한동안 그 기이한 동행에게 말을 걸지 못했다."], ["It was her voice again that first broke the silence between us.", "우리 사이의 침묵을 처음 깬 것은 다시금 그녀의 목소리였다."]], [["“I want to ask you something,”", "“한 가지 여쭙고 싶어요.”"], ["she said suddenly.", "그녀가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“Do you know many people in London?”", "“런던에 아는 사람이 많으세요?”"]], [["“Yes, a great many.”", "“네, 아주 많지요.”"]], [["“Many men of rank and title?”", "“지체 높고 작위 있는 분들도 많이요?”"], ["There was an unmistakable tone of suspicion in the strange question.", "그 기이한 물음에는 틀림없는 의심의 어조가 배어 있었다."], ["I hesitated about answering it.", "나는 대답을 망설였다."]], [["“Some,”", "“몇 분요.”"], ["I said, after a moment’s silence.", "나는 잠깐 침묵한 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“Many”—she came to a full stop, and looked me searchingly in the face—“many men of the rank of Baronet?”", "“많이”—그녀는 딱 멈춰 서서 내 얼굴을 살피듯 바라보았다—“준남작 지위의 분들도 많이요?”"]], [["Too much astonished to reply, I questioned her in my turn.", "너무 놀라 대답하지 못한 채, 나는 이번엔 내가 그녀에게 되물었다."]], [["“Why do you ask?”", "“어째서 그런 걸 물으십니까?”"]], [["“Because I hope, for my own sake, there is one Baronet that you don’t know.”", "“당신이 모르는 준남작이 하나 있기를, 저 자신을 위해 바라기 때문이에요.”"]], [["“Will you tell me his name?”", "“그 사람 이름을 말해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["“I can’t—I daren’t—I forget myself when I mention it.”", "“못 해요—감히 못 해요—그 이름을 입에 올리면 저는 제정신을 잃어요.” 그녀는 크게, 거의 사납다 싶게 말하며, 움켜쥔 손을 허공에 치켜들고 격하게 흔들었다."], ["She spoke loudly and almost fiercely, raised her clenched hand in the air, and shook it passionately; then, on a sudden, controlled herself again, and added, in tones lowered to a whisper “Tell me which of them YOU know.”", "그러더니 별안간 다시 자신을 다스리고는, 속삭임으로 낮춘 어조로 덧붙였다. “당신이 아는 분들이 누구누구인지 말해 주세요.”"]], [["I could hardly refuse to humour her in such a trifle, and I mentioned three names.", "이런 사소한 일에 그녀의 비위를 맞춰 주기를 마다하기는 어려운지라, 나는 세 사람의 이름을 댔다."], ["Two, the names of fathers of families whose daughters I taught; one, the name of a bachelor who had once taken me a cruise in his yacht, to make sketches for him.", "둘은 내가 딸들을 가르치는 집안 가장들의 이름이었고, 하나는 언젠가 자기를 위해 스케치를 해 달라며 제 요트로 나를 뱃놀이에 데려간 적이 있는 어느 독신 신사의 이름이었다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["you DON’T know him,”", "당신은 그 사람을 모르는군요.”"], ["she said, with a sigh of relief.", "그녀는 안도의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“Are you a man of rank and title yourself?”", "“당신 자신은 지체 높고 작위 있는 분인가요?”"]], [["“Far from it.", "“천만에요."], ["I am only a drawing-master.”", "저는 한낱 그림 선생일 뿐입니다.”"]], [["As the reply passed my lips—a little bitterly, perhaps—she took my arm with the abruptness which characterised all her actions.", "그 대답이—어쩌면 조금은 씁쓸하게—내 입에서 새어 나오는 순간, 그녀는 제 모든 행동을 특징짓는 그 느닷없음으로 내 팔을 잡았다."]], [["“Not a man of rank and title,”", "“지체 높고 작위 있는 분이 아니라니.”"], ["she repeated to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말로 되뇌었다."], ["“Thank God!", "“고마우신 하느님!"], ["I may trust HIM.”", "이분은 믿어도 되겠어요.”"]], [["I had hitherto contrived to master my curiosity out of consideration for my companion; but it got the better of me now.", "나는 여태 동행을 배려하여 내 호기심을 눌러 왔다. 그러나 이제는 그 호기심이 나를 이기고 말았다."]], [["“I am afraid you have serious reason to complain of some man of rank and title?” I said.", "“지체 높고 작위 있는 어떤 사람에 대해 원망할 심각한 까닭이 있으신 게 아닌가 싶군요?”"], ["“I am afraid the baronet, whose name you are unwilling to mention to me, has done you some grievous wrong?", "내가 말했다. “당신이 저에게 이름 대기를 꺼리는 그 준남작이 당신에게 무언가 참혹한 몹쓸 짓을 저지른 게 아닌지요?"], ["Is he the cause of your being out here at this strange time of night?”", "이 기이한 야밤에 당신이 이렇게 밖에 나와 있는 것도 그 사람 탓입니까?”"]], [["“Don’t ask me: don’t make me talk of it,”", "“묻지 마세요. 그 이야기를 하게 만들지 마세요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I’m not fit now.", "“지금은 그럴 형편이 못 돼요."], ["I have been cruelly used and cruelly wronged.", "저는 참혹하게 다뤄졌고 참혹하게 짓밟혔어요."], ["You will be kinder than ever, if you will walk on fast, and not speak to me.", "걸음을 빨리해 주시고 저에게 말을 걸지 않아 주신다면, 그 어느 때보다 더 친절하신 거예요."], ["I sadly want to quiet myself, if I can.”", "할 수만 있다면 저는 못 견디게 마음을 가라앉히고 싶어요.”"]], [["We moved forward again at a quick pace; and for half an hour, at least, not a word passed on either side.", "우리는 다시 빠른 걸음으로 나아갔다. 그리고 적어도 반 시간 동안은 어느 쪽에서도 한 마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["From time to time, being forbidden to make any more inquiries, I stole a look at her face.", "이따금, 더는 캐물어선 안 되는지라, 나는 그녀의 얼굴을 몰래 훔쳐보았다."], ["It was always the same; the lips close shut, the brow frowning, the eyes looking straight forward, eagerly and yet absently.", "언제나 한결같았다. 꼭 다문 입술, 찌푸린 이마, 골똘하면서도 넋 나간 듯 곧장 앞을 응시하는 눈."], ["We had reached the first houses, and were close on the new Wesleyan college, before her set features relaxed and she spoke once more.", "우리가 첫 집들에 다다라 새로 지은 웨슬리교 대학 가까이에 이르러서야 비로소 그 굳어 있던 이목구비가 풀리며 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Do you live in London?”", "“런던에 사세요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.”", "“네.”"], ["As I answered, it struck me that she might have formed some intention of appealing to me for assistance or advice, and that I ought to spare her a possible disappointment by warning her of my approaching absence from home.", "내가 대답하는데, 문득 이런 생각이 들었다—그녀가 나에게 도움이나 조언을 청할 어떤 생각을 품었을지도 모르며, 그렇다면 곧 내가 집을 비운다는 것을 미리 알려 그녀가 겪을지 모를 실망을 덜어 주어야 한다는 것이."], ["So I added, “But to-morrow I shall be away from London for some time.", "그래서 나는 덧붙였다. “하지만 내일이면 한동안 런던을 떠나 있을 겁니다."], ["I am going into the country.”", "시골로 내려가거든요.”"]], [["“Where?”", "“어디로요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["“North or south?”", "“북쪽인가요, 남쪽인가요?”"]], [["“North—to Cumberland.”", "“북쪽—컴벌랜드로요.”"]], [["“Cumberland!”", "“컴벌랜드!”"], ["she repeated the word tenderly.", "그녀는 그 말을 애틋하게 되뇌었다."], ["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I wish I was going there too.", "저도 거기 갈 수 있으면 좋으련만."], ["I was once happy in Cumberland.”", "저는 한때 컴벌랜드에서 행복했어요.”"]], [["I tried again to lift the veil that hung between this woman and me.", "나는 이 여인과 나 사이에 드리운 장막을 걷어 보려고 다시 한번 애썼다."]], [["“Perhaps you were born,”", "“어쩌면 그 아름다운 호수 지방에서 태어나셨나 보군요.”"], ["I said, “in the beautiful Lake country.”", "내가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니에요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I was born in Hampshire; but I once went to school for a little while in Cumberland.", "“저는 햄프셔에서 태어났어요. 하지만 한때 잠깐 컴벌랜드로 학교를 다닌 적이 있지요."], ["Lakes?", "호수요?"], ["I don’t remember any lakes.", "저는 호수 같은 건 기억나지 않아요."], ["It’s Limmeridge village, and Limmeridge House, I should like to see again.”", "제가 다시 보고 싶은 건 리머리지 마을이랑 리머리지 하우스예요.”"]], [["It was my turn now to stop suddenly.", "이번엔 내가 별안간 멈춰 설 차례였다."], ["In the excited state of my curiosity, at that moment, the chance reference to Mr. Fairlie’s place of residence, on the lips of my strange companion, staggered me with astonishment.", "그 순간 잔뜩 들뜬 내 호기심의 상태에서, 기이한 동행의 입에서 페얼리 씨의 거처가 우연히 튀어나온 것은 나를 놀라움으로 휘청거리게 만들었다."]], [["“Did you hear anybody calling after us?”", "“누가 우리 뒤에서 부르는 소리 들리지 않았어요?”"], ["she asked, looking up and down the road affrightedly, the instant I stopped.", "내가 멈추는 순간 그녀가 겁에 질려 길 위아래를 살피며 물었다."]], [["“No, no.", "“아니요, 아니요."], ["I was only struck by the name of Limmeridge House.", "그저 리머리지 하우스라는 이름에 놀랐을 뿐입니다."], ["I heard it mentioned by some Cumberland people a few days since.”", "며칠 전에 어떤 컴벌랜드 사람들이 그 이름을 말하는 걸 들었거든요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["not my people. Mrs.", "우리 식구는 아니에요."], ["Fairlie is dead; and her husband is dead; and their little girl may be married and gone away by this time.", "페얼리 부인은 돌아가셨고, 그 남편도 돌아가셨어요. 그리고 그분들의 어린 딸은 이맘때면 혼인해 떠났을지도 모르지요."], ["I can’t say who lives at Limmeridge now.", "지금 리머리지에 누가 사는지 저는 말할 수 없어요."], ["If any more are left there of that name, I only know I love them for Mrs. Fairlie’s sake.”", "그 이름을 가진 이가 거기 더 남아 있다면, 페얼리 부인을 위해서라도 제가 그들을 사랑한다는 것밖에는 저는 몰라요.”"]], [["She seemed about to say more; but while she was speaking, we came within view of the turnpike, at the top of the Avenue Road.", "그녀는 더 말하려는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이, 우리는 애비뉴 로드 꼭대기에 있는 통행료 관문이 보이는 데까지 이르렀다."], ["Her hand tightened round my arm, and she looked anxiously at the gate before us.", "그녀의 손이 내 팔을 꽉 죄었고, 그녀는 우리 앞의 관문을 근심스레 바라보았다."]], [["“Is the turnpike man looking out?”", "“관문지기가 내다보고 있나요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["He was not looking out; no one else was near the place when we passed through the gate.", "그는 내다보고 있지 않았다. 우리가 관문을 지날 때 그 근처에는 다른 누구도 없었다."], ["The sight of the gas-lamps and houses seemed to agitate her, and to make her impatient.", "가스등과 집들이 눈에 들어오자 그녀는 동요하며 조바심을 내는 듯했다."]], [["“This is London,”", "“여기가 런던이에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Do you see any carriage I can get?", "“제가 탈 만한 마차가 보이나요?"], ["I am tired and frightened.", "저는 지치고 겁이 나요."], ["I want to shut myself in and be driven away.”", "저는 안에 틀어박혀 실려 가고 싶어요.”"]], [["I explained to her that we must walk a little further to get to a cab-stand, unless we were fortunate enough to meet with an empty vehicle; and then tried to resume the subject of Cumberland.", "나는 빈 마차를 만나는 행운이 따르지 않는 한, 삯마차 정류장에 가려면 좀 더 걸어야 한다고 그녀에게 설명했다. 그러고는 컴벌랜드 이야기를 다시 꺼내 보려 했다."], ["It was useless.", "소용없었다."], ["That idea of shutting herself in, and being driven away, had now got full possession of her mind.", "안에 틀어박혀 실려 간다는 그 생각이 이제 그녀의 마음을 온통 사로잡고 있었다."], ["She could think and talk of nothing else.", "그녀는 그것 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수도, 말할 수도 없었다."]], [["We had hardly proceeded a third of the way down the Avenue Road when I saw a cab draw up at a house a few doors below us, on the opposite side of the way.", "애비뉴 로드를 삼분의 일도 채 내려가지 못했을 때, 나는 몇 집 아래, 길 건너편의 한 집 앞에 삯마차가 멎는 것을 보았다."], ["A gentleman got out and let himself in at the garden door.", "한 신사가 내려서 정원 문으로 들어갔다."], ["I hailed the cab, as the driver mounted the box again.", "마부가 다시 마부석에 오르는 것을 보고 나는 그 마차를 불러 세웠다."], ["When we crossed the road, my companion’s impatience increased to such an extent that she almost forced me to run.", "우리가 길을 건널 때 동행의 조바심은 어찌나 커졌던지, 그녀는 나를 거의 뛰다시피 하게 만들었다."]], [["“It’s so late,”", "“너무 늦었어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I am only in a hurry because it’s so late.”", "“너무 늦어서 서두르는 것뿐이에요.”"]], [["“I can’t take you, sir, if you’re not going towards Tottenham Court Road,”", "“토트넘 코트 로드 쪽으로 가시는 게 아니라면 못 태워 드립니다, 나리.”"], ["said the driver civilly, when I opened the cab door.", "내가 마차 문을 열자 마부가 공손히 말했다."], ["“My horse is dead beat, and I can’t get him no further than the stable.”", "“제 말이 완전히 지쳐서, 마구간까지밖에는 더 못 갑니다.”"]], [["“Yes, yes.", "“네, 네."], ["That will do for me.", "저한테는 그거면 돼요."], ["I’m going that way—I’m going that way.”", "저도 그쪽으로 가요—저도 그쪽으로 가요.”"], ["She spoke with breathless eagerness, and pressed by me into the cab.", "그녀는 숨 가쁘게 다급한 어조로 말하며, 내 곁을 밀치고 마차 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["I had assured myself that the man was sober as well as civil before I let her enter the vehicle.", "나는 그녀를 마차에 태우기 전에, 그 사내가 공손할 뿐 아니라 술도 취하지 않았음을 확인해 두었다."], ["And now, when she was seated inside, I entreated her to let me see her set down safely at her destination.", "그리고 이제, 그녀가 안에 앉자, 나는 그녀가 목적지에 무사히 내리는 것을 보게 해 달라고 간청했다."]], [["“No, no, no,” she said vehemently.", "“아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요.”"], ["“I’m quite safe, and quite happy now.", "그녀가 격하게 말했다. “저는 이제 아주 안전하고 아주 행복해요."], ["If you are a gentleman, remember your promise.", "당신이 신사라면 약속을 기억하세요."], ["Let him drive on till I stop him.", "제가 세울 때까지 마부가 몰아가게 두세요."], ["Thank you—oh!", "고마워요—오!"], ["thank you, thank you!”", "고마워요, 고마워요!”"]], [["My hand was on the cab door.", "내 손이 마차 문에 얹혀 있었다."], ["She caught it in hers, kissed it, and pushed it away.", "그녀는 그 손을 제 손으로 붙잡아 입 맞추고는 밀쳐 냈다."], ["The cab drove off at the same moment—I started into the road, with some vague idea of stopping it again, I hardly knew why—hesitated from dread of frightening and distressing her—called, at last, but not loudly enough to attract the driver’s attention.", "바로 그 순간 마차가 달리기 시작했다—나는 다시 그것을 세우려는 어렴풋한 생각에, 왜인지도 잘 모른 채 길로 뛰어들었다—그녀를 겁주고 괴롭힐까 두려워 머뭇거렸다—마침내 소리쳐 불렀으나, 마부의 주의를 끌 만큼 크지는 못했다."], ["The sound of the wheels grew fainter in the distance—the cab melted into the black shadows on the road—the woman in white was gone.", "바퀴 소리가 멀리서 차츰 희미해졌다—마차는 길 위의 검은 그늘 속으로 녹아들었다—흰옷을 입은 여인은 사라졌다."]], [["Ten minutes or more had passed.", "십 분 남짓 지났다."], ["I was still on the same side of the way; now mechanically walking forward a few paces; now stopping again absently.", "나는 여전히 길 같은 쪽에 있었다. 이제는 기계적으로 몇 걸음 앞으로 걷다가, 이제는 다시 넋 놓고 멈춰 섰다."], ["At one moment I found myself doubting the reality of my own adventure; at another I was perplexed and distressed by an uneasy sense of having done wrong, which yet left me confusedly ignorant of how I could have done right.", "어느 순간에는 내 자신의 그 겪음이 정말이었는지 의심하는 나를 발견했고, 또 어느 순간에는 무언가 잘못을 저질렀다는 꺼림칙한 느낌에 어지럽고 괴로웠다—그러면서도 정작 어떻게 했어야 옳았을지는 어리둥절하게도 알지 못한 채였다."], ["I hardly knew where I was going, or what I meant to do next; I was conscious of nothing but the confusion of my own thoughts, when I was abruptly recalled to myself—awakened, I might almost say—by the sound of rapidly approaching wheels close behind me.", "내가 어디로 가는지도, 다음에 무엇을 하려는지도 나는 거의 알지 못했다. 나 자신의 뒤엉킨 생각의 혼란 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못하고 있는데, 그때 바로 등 뒤에서 빠르게 다가오는 바퀴 소리에 나는 불현듯 제정신으로 되돌려졌다—거의 잠에서 깨어났다고 해도 좋을 만큼."]], [["I was on the dark side of the road, in the thick shadow of some garden trees, when I stopped to look round.", "돌아보려고 멈춰 섰을 때 나는 길의 어두운 쪽, 몇몇 정원 나무들의 짙은 그늘 속에 있었다."], ["On the opposite and lighter side of the way, a short distance below me, a policeman was strolling along in the direction of the Regent’s Park.", "길 건너 좀 더 밝은 쪽, 나에게서 조금 아래에서는 순경 하나가 리전트 파크 방향으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걷고 있었다."]], [["The carriage passed me—an open chaise driven by two men.", "마차가 나를 지나쳐 갔다—두 사내가 모는 지붕 없는 이륜마차였다."]], [["“Stop!”", "“서!”"], ["cried one.", "한 사람이 소리쳤다."], ["“There’s a policeman.", "“저기 순경이 있다."], ["Let’s ask him.”", "저이한테 물어보자.”"]], [["The horse was instantly pulled up, a few yards beyond the dark place where I stood.", "내가 서 있던 어두운 자리에서 몇 야드 지난 곳에서 말이 그 즉시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Policeman!”", "“순경!”"], ["cried the first speaker.", "처음 말한 자가 외쳤다."], ["“Have you seen a woman pass this way?”", "“이 길로 여자 하나가 지나가는 걸 봤소?”"]], [["“What sort of woman, sir?”", "“어떤 여자 말입니까, 나리?”"]], [["“A woman in a lavender-coloured gown——”", "“라벤더빛 가운을 입은 여자——”"]], [["“No, no,”", "“아니, 아니.”"], ["interposed the second man.", "두 번째 사내가 끼어들었다."], ["“The clothes we gave her were found on her bed.", "“우리가 준 옷은 침대 위에서 발견됐잖나."], ["She must have gone away in the clothes she wore when she came to us.", "우리한테 올 때 입고 온 옷차림 그대로 달아났을 게 틀림없어."], ["In white, policeman.", "흰옷일세, 순경."], ["A woman in white.”", "흰옷을 입은 여자란 말이오.”"]], [["“I haven’t seen her, sir.”", "“그런 여자는 못 봤습니다, 나리.”"]], [["“If you or any of your men meet with the woman, stop her, and send her in careful keeping to that address.", "“자네든 자네 부하 누구든 그 여자를 만나거든 붙잡아서, 이 주소로 잘 지켜 데려다주게."], ["I’ll pay all expenses, and a fair reward into the bargain.”", "비용은 다 내지, 게다가 후한 사례금까지 얹어서.”"]], [["The policeman looked at the card that was handed down to him.", "순경은 아래로 건네받은 명함을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Why are we to stop her, sir?", "“어째서 그 여자를 붙잡아야 합니까, 나리?"], ["What has she done?”", "그 여자가 무슨 짓을 했습니까?”"]], [["“Done!", "“짓이라니!"], ["She has escaped from my Asylum.", "그 여자는 내 정신병원에서 달아났소."], ["Don’t forget; a woman in white.", "잊지 말게. 흰옷을 입은 여자야."], ["Drive on.”", "몰아라.”"]], [["“She has escaped from my Asylum!”", "“그 여자는 바로 내가 운영하는 정신병원에서 달아난 것이오!”"]], [["I cannot say with truth that the terrible inference which those words suggested flashed upon me like a new revelation.", "그 말이 자아낸 그 끔찍한 추론이 새로운 계시처럼 나에게 번뜩였다고는 사실대로 말할 수 없다."], ["Some of the strange questions put to me by the woman in white, after my ill-considered promise to leave her free to act as she pleased, had suggested the conclusion either that she was naturally flighty and unsettled, or that some recent shock of terror had disturbed the balance of her faculties.", "그녀가 원하는 대로 자유로이 행동하게 두겠다는 나의 경솔한 약속 뒤에 흰옷을 입은 여인이 나에게 던진 그 기이한 물음들 가운데 몇몇은, 그녀가 천성적으로 들뜨고 안정되지 못했거나, 아니면 무언가 최근의 두려운 충격이 그녀의 심신의 균형을 흐트러뜨렸으리라는 결론을 넌지시 비추기는 했었다."], ["But the idea of absolute insanity which we all associate with the very name of an Asylum, had, I can honestly declare, never occurred to me, in connection with her.", "그러나 정신병원이라는 그 이름 자체에 우리 모두가 결부시키는 완전한 광기라는 생각은, 정직하게 단언하건대, 그녀와 관련해서는 나에게 결코 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["I had seen nothing, in her language or her actions, to justify it at the time; and even with the new light thrown on her by the words which the stranger had addressed to the policeman, I could see nothing to justify it now.", "나는 그녀의 말에서도 행동에서도 그 당시 그것을 뒷받침할 만한 것을 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그리고 낯선 사내가 순경에게 건넨 그 말이 그녀에게 새로이 드리운 빛으로도, 지금 나는 그것을 뒷받침할 만한 것을 아무것도 볼 수 없었다."]], [["What had I done?", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 것인가?"], ["Assisted the victim of the most horrible of all false imprisonments to escape; or cast loose on the wide world of London an unfortunate creature, whose actions it was my duty, and every man’s duty, mercifully to control?", "모든 부당한 감금 가운데 가장 소름 끼치는 것의 희생자를 도와 달아나게 한 것인가, 아니면 그 행동을 자비로이 다스리는 것이 내 의무이자 뭇사람의 의무인 어느 가엾은 존재를 런던이라는 드넓은 세상에 풀어놓아 버린 것인가?"], ["I turned sick at heart when the question occurred to me, and when I felt self-reproachfully that it was asked too late.", "이 물음이 떠올랐을 때, 그리고 그것이 너무 뒤늦게 던져진 물음임을 자책하며 느꼈을 때, 나는 가슴이 메슥거렸다."]], [["In the disturbed state of my mind, it was useless to think of going to bed, when I at last got back to my chambers in Clement’s Inn.", "마침내 클레멘츠 여관의 하숙방으로 돌아왔을 때, 이렇게 어지러운 마음 상태로는 잠자리에 들 생각을 해 봐야 소용없었다."], ["Before many hours elapsed it would be necessary to start on my journey to Cumberland.", "몇 시간 지나지 않아 컴벌랜드로 가는 여정에 나서야 할 터였다."], ["I sat down and tried, first to sketch, then to read—but the woman in white got between me and my pencil, between me and my book.", "나는 앉아서, 처음엔 스케치를, 그다음엔 독서를 해 보려 했으나—흰옷을 입은 여인이 나와 내 연필 사이에, 나와 내 책 사이에 끼어들었다."], ["Had the forlorn creature come to any harm?", "그 의지가지없는 존재는 무슨 해라도 입지 않았을까?"], ["That was my first thought, though I shrank selfishly from confronting it.", "그것이 내 첫 생각이었다, 비록 나는 그것과 마주하기를 이기적으로 꺼렸지만."], ["Other thoughts followed, on which it was less harrowing to dwell.", "그보다는 곱씹기가 덜 괴로운 다른 생각들이 뒤따랐다."], ["Where had she stopped the cab?", "그녀는 어디서 삯마차를 세웠을까?"], ["What had become of her now?", "이제 그녀는 어떻게 됐을까?"], ["Had she been traced and captured by the men in the chaise?", "그녀는 이륜마차의 사내들에게 뒤를 밟혀 붙잡혔을까?"], ["Or was she still capable of controlling her own actions; and were we two following our widely parted roads towards one point in the mysterious future, at which we were to meet once more?", "아니면 아직도 제 행동을 다스릴 수 있어서, 우리 둘은 저마다 멀찍이 갈라진 길을 따라, 다시 한번 마주치게 될 신비로운 미래의 한 지점을 향해 가고 있는 것일까?"]], [["It was a relief when the hour came to lock my door, to bid farewell to London pursuits, London pupils, and London friends, and to be in movement again towards new interests and a new life.", "문을 잠그고, 런던의 일들과 런던의 제자들과 런던의 벗들에게 작별을 고하고, 새로운 관심사와 새로운 삶을 향해 다시 움직일 시각이 왔을 때는 오히려 마음이 놓였다."], ["Even the bustle and confusion at the railway terminus, so wearisome and bewildering at other times, roused me and did me good.", "다른 때 같으면 그토록 진저리 나고 어리둥절하던 철도 종착역의 부산함과 혼잡마저 나를 흔들어 깨워 나에게 이로웠다."]], [["My travelling instructions directed me to go to Carlisle, and then to diverge by a branch railway which ran in the direction of the coast.", "여정 지시서는 나에게 칼라일까지 가서, 거기서 해안 방향으로 달리는 지선(支線) 철도로 갈아타라고 일러 주었다."], ["As a misfortune to begin with, our engine broke down between Lancaster and Carlisle.", "시작부터 불운이 닥쳐, 우리 기관차가 랭커스터와 칼라일 사이에서 고장 났다."], ["The delay occasioned by this accident caused me to be too late for the branch train, by which I was to have gone on immediately.", "이 사고로 생긴 지체 탓에 나는 곧바로 갈아타려던 지선 열차를 놓치고 말았다. 몇 시간을 기다려야 했다."], ["I had to wait some hours; and when a later train finally deposited me at the nearest station to Limmeridge House, it was past ten, and the night was so dark that I could hardly see my way to the pony-chaise which Mr. Fairlie had ordered to be in waiting for me.", "그리고 뒤늦은 열차가 마침내 나를 리머리지 하우스에서 가장 가까운 역에 내려놓았을 때는 열 시가 지난 뒤였고, 밤이 어찌나 어두웠던지 페얼리 씨가 나를 위해 대기시켜 두라 일러 놓은 조랑말 마차로 가는 길조차 겨우 분간할 지경이었다."]], [["The driver was evidently discomposed by the lateness of my arrival.", "마부는 내 도착이 늦은 것에 눈에 띄게 언짢아하고 있었다."], ["He was in that state of highly respectful sulkiness which is peculiar to English servants.", "그는 영국 하인 특유의, 지극히 공손한 뿌루퉁함의 상태에 있었다."], ["We drove away slowly through the darkness in perfect silence.", "우리는 완전한 침묵 속에서 어둠을 뚫고 느릿느릿 마차를 몰아갔다."], ["The roads were bad, and the dense obscurity of the night increased the difficulty of getting over the ground quickly.", "길은 험했고, 짙은 밤의 어둠은 땅을 빨리 지나가기 어렵게 만들었다."], ["It was, by my watch, nearly an hour and a half from the time of our leaving the station before I heard the sound of the sea in the distance, and the crunch of our wheels on a smooth gravel drive.", "내 시계로, 역을 떠난 때로부터 거의 한 시간 반이 지나서야 나는 멀리서 바다 소리와, 매끄러운 자갈길 위를 구르는 우리 바퀴의 사각거림을 들었다."], ["We had passed one gate before entering the drive, and we passed another before we drew up at the house.", "우리는 진입로에 들어서기 전에 문 하나를 지났고, 저택 앞에 마차를 세우기 전에 또 하나의 문을 지났다."], ["I was received by a solemn man-servant out of livery, was informed that the family had retired for the night, and was then led into a large and lofty room where my supper was awaiting me, in a forlorn manner, at one extremity of a lonesome mahogany wilderness of dining-table.", "나는 제복을 입지 않은 근엄한 남자 하인의 맞이를 받아, 가족은 이미 밤 인사를 하고 잠자리에 들었다는 말을 듣고는, 크고 높다란 방으로 안내되었다. 그 방에서는 쓸쓸한 마호가니 식탁의 황량한 벌판 한쪽 끝에, 내 밤참이 처량한 모양새로 나를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["I was too tired and out of spirits to eat or drink much, especially with the solemn servant waiting on me as elaborately as if a small dinner party had arrived at the house instead of a solitary man.", "나는 너무 지치고 기운이 없어 많이 먹지도 마시지도 못했다. 더욱이 홀로 온 사내가 아니라 무슨 작은 만찬 모임이라도 저택에 당도한 양 근엄한 하인이 나를 정성스레 시중드는 통에 더 그랬다."], ["In a quarter of an hour I was ready to be taken up to my bedchamber.", "십오 분 만에 나는 침실로 올라갈 채비가 되었다."], ["The solemn servant conducted me into a prettily furnished room—said, “Breakfast at nine o’clock, sir”—looked all round him to see that everything was in its proper place, and noiselessly withdrew.", "근엄한 하인은 나를 아담하게 꾸며진 방으로 안내하고는—“아침 식사는 아홉 시입니다, 나리”라고 말하고—모든 것이 제자리에 있는지 사방을 둘러보고, 소리 없이 물러갔다."]], [["“What shall I see in my dreams to-night?”", "“오늘 밤 나는 꿈에서 무엇을 보게 될까?”"], ["I thought to myself, as I put out the candle; “the woman in white?", "나는 촛불을 끄며 혼자 생각했다. “흰옷을 입은 여인일까?"], ["or the unknown inhabitants of this Cumberland mansion?”", "아니면 이 컴벌랜드 저택의 알지 못하는 거주자들일까?”"], ["It was a strange sensation to be sleeping in the house, like a friend of the family, and yet not to know one of the inmates, even by sight!", "이 집에서, 마치 그 집안의 벗처럼 잠을 자면서도, 정작 그 식구를 얼굴조차 하나도 알지 못한다는 것은 기이한 느낌이었다!"]], [["# VI", "# VI"]], [["When I rose the next morning and drew up my blind, the sea opened before me joyously under the broad August sunlight, and the distant coast of Scotland fringed the horizon with its lines of melting blue.", "이튿날 아침 일어나 블라인드를 걷어 올리자, 널따란 팔월 햇살 아래 바다가 내 앞에 환하게 펼쳐졌고, 멀리 스코틀랜드 해안이 녹아드는 듯한 푸른 선들로 수평선을 가장자리 두르고 있었다."]], [["The view was such a surprise, and such a change to me, after my weary London experience of brick and mortar landscape, that I seemed to burst into a new life and a new set of thoughts the moment I looked at it.", "벽돌과 회반죽의 풍경에 진저리 나던 런던 생활 끝에, 그 광경은 나에게 너무나 뜻밖의 놀라움이자 너무나 큰 변화여서, 그것을 바라보는 순간 나는 새로운 삶과 새로운 생각 속으로 불쑥 터져 나오는 듯했다."], ["A confused sensation of having suddenly lost my familiarity with the past, without acquiring any additional clearness of idea in reference to the present or the future, took possession of my mind.", "현재나 미래에 대해 어떤 더 또렷한 생각도 얻지 못한 채, 별안간 과거와의 친숙함을 잃어버렸다는 어수선한 느낌이 내 마음을 사로잡았다."], ["Circumstances that were but a few days old faded back in my memory, as if they had happened months and months since.", "겨우 며칠밖에 되지 않은 일들이, 마치 여러 달 전에 일어나기라도 한 양 내 기억 속에서 아스라이 물러났다."], ["Pesca’s quaint announcement of the means by which he had procured me my present employment; the farewell evening I had passed with my mother and sister; even my mysterious adventure on the way home from Hampstead—had all become like events which might have occurred at some former epoch of my existence.", "지금의 이 일자리를 나에게 마련해 준 방법에 대한 페스카의 별스러운 발표며, 어머니와 누이와 함께 보낸 작별의 저녁이며, 햄스테드에서 집으로 돌아오는 길의 그 신비로운 겪음마저도—죄다, 내 삶의 어느 지난 시대에나 일어났을 법한 일들처럼 되어 버렸다."], ["Although the woman in white was still in my mind, the image of her seemed to have grown dull and faint already.", "흰옷을 입은 여인이 여전히 내 마음속에 있었음에도, 그녀의 모습은 벌써 흐릿하고 희미해진 듯했다."]], [["A little before nine o’clock, I descended to the ground-floor of the house.", "아홉 시가 조금 못 되어 나는 저택의 일 층으로 내려갔다."], ["The solemn man-servant of the night before met me wandering among the passages, and compassionately showed me the way to the breakfast-room.", "전날 밤의 그 근엄한 남자 하인이 복도를 헤매는 나와 마주치더니, 딱하다는 듯 아침 식사실로 가는 길을 일러 주었다."]], [["My first glance round me, as the man opened the door, disclosed a well-furnished breakfast-table, standing in the middle of a long room, with many windows in it.", "하인이 문을 열자, 방을 둘러본 내 첫 눈길에, 창이 많이 난 긴 방 한복판에 잘 차려진 아침 식탁이 놓여 있는 것이 들어왔다. 식탁에서 나에게 가장 먼 창으로 눈을 옮기니, 한 숙녀가 나를 등진 채 그 앞에 서 있는 것이 보였다."], ["I looked from the table to the window farthest from me, and saw a lady standing at it, with her back turned towards me.", "내 눈이 그녀에게 머무는 순간, 나는 그 자태의 보기 드문 아름다움과, 꾸밈없는 그 몸가짐의 우아함에 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["The instant my eyes rested on her, I was struck by the rare beauty of her form, and by the unaffected grace of her attitude.", "그녀의 몸매는 컸으나 너무 크지는 않았고, 어여쁘게 잘 여물었으나 뚱뚱하지는 않았다. 머리는 어깨 위에 편안하면서도 유연한 굳건함으로 얹혀 있었다."], ["Her figure was tall, yet not too tall; comely and well-developed, yet not fat; her head set on her shoulders with an easy, pliant firmness; her waist, perfection in the eyes of a man, for it occupied its natural place, it filled out its natural circle, it was visibly and delightfully undeformed by stays.", "그 허리는—사내의 눈에는 완벽함 그 자체였다. 제자리에 자연스레 놓여, 제 둘레를 자연스레 채우고 있었으며, 코르셋에 눌려 일그러지지 않았음이 눈에 뜨이게, 흐뭇하게 드러났으니까."], ["She had not heard my entrance into the room; and I allowed myself the luxury of admiring her for a few moments, before I moved one of the chairs near me, as the least embarrassing means of attracting her attention.", "그녀는 내가 방에 들어온 것을 듣지 못했다. 그래서 나는 그녀의 주의를 끄는 가장 덜 무안한 방법으로 가까이 있는 의자 하나를 옮기기 전에, 잠시나마 그녀를 흠모하는 호사를 나 자신에게 허락했다."], ["She turned towards me immediately.", "그녀는 곧바로 나를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["The easy elegance of every movement of her limbs and body as soon as she began to advance from the far end of the room, set me in a flutter of expectation to see her face clearly.", "방의 저 먼 끝에서 다가오기 시작하는 순간, 그녀의 팔다리와 몸의 온갖 움직임이 지닌 그 편안한 우아함은, 그녀의 얼굴을 또렷이 보고 싶은 기대로 나를 설레게 했다."], ["She left the window—and I said to myself, The lady is dark.", "그녀가 창가를 떠났다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다, 이 숙녀는 가무잡잡하구나."], ["She moved forward a few steps—and I said to myself, The lady is young.", "그녀가 몇 걸음 앞으로 나왔다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다, 이 숙녀는 젊구나."], ["She approached nearer—and I said to myself (with a sense of surprise which words fail me to express), The lady is ugly!", "그녀가 더 가까이 다가왔다—그리고 나는 속으로 말했다(말로는 다 표현할 수 없는 놀라움과 함께), 이 숙녀는 못생겼구나!"]], [["Never was the old conventional maxim, that Nature cannot err, more flatly contradicted—never was the fair promise of a lovely figure more strangely and startlingly belied by the face and head that crowned it.", "자연은 그르치는 법이 없다는 저 낡고 판에 박힌 격언이 이토록 대놓고 뒤집힌 적은 없었다—사랑스러운 자태의 아름다운 약속이, 그 위에 얹힌 얼굴과 머리로 인해 이토록 기이하고 소스라치게 배신당한 적은 없었다."], ["The lady’s complexion was almost swarthy, and the dark down on her upper lip was almost a moustache.", "그 숙녀의 낯빛은 거의 거무스름했고, 윗입술의 검은 솜털은 거의 콧수염이나 다름없었다."], ["She had a large, firm, masculine mouth and jaw; prominent, piercing, resolute brown eyes; and thick, coal-black hair, growing unusually low down on her forehead.", "그녀는 크고 단단한, 사내 같은 입과 턱을 지녔고, 튀어나온 듯 꿰뚫는, 결연한 갈색 눈을 지녔으며, 이마 위 유난히 아래까지 내려온 숱 많은 새까만 머리칼을 지녔다."], ["Her expression—bright, frank, and intelligent—appeared, while she was silent, to be altogether wanting in those feminine attractions of gentleness and pliability, without which the beauty of the handsomest woman alive is beauty incomplete.", "그녀의 표정은—밝고 솔직하고 총명한—입을 다물고 있는 동안에는, 부드러움과 유순함이라는 저 여성스러운 매력을 통째로 결여한 듯 보였다. 살아 있는 가장 어여쁜 여인의 아름다움조차 그것 없이는 미완의 아름다움이건만."], ["To see such a face as this set on shoulders that a sculptor would have longed to model—to be charmed by the modest graces of action through which the symmetrical limbs betrayed their beauty when they moved, and then to be almost repelled by the masculine form and masculine look of the features in which the perfectly shaped figure ended—was to feel a sensation oddly akin to the helpless discomfort familiar to us all in sleep, when we recognise yet cannot reconcile the anomalies and contradictions of a dream.", "조각가라면 본떠 만들고 싶어 애가 탔을 어깨 위에 이런 얼굴이 얹힌 것을 보는 것은—그 균형 잡힌 팔다리가 움직일 때 제 아름다움을 은근히 드러내는 그 정숙한 몸짓의 우아함에 마음을 빼앗겼다가, 이내 그 완벽하게 빚어진 자태가 끝나는 지점의 이목구비의 사내 같은 생김새와 사내 같은 표정에 거의 밀쳐지듯 하는 것은—꿈속에서 우리 모두에게 익숙한 그 어쩔 수 없는 불편함, 곧 우리가 꿈의 그 모순과 어긋남을 알아채면서도 도무지 아귀를 맞출 수 없을 때 느끼는 그것과 묘하게 닮은 감각을 느끼는 일이었다."]], [["“Mr. Hartright?”", "“하트라이트 씨인가요?”"], ["said the lady interrogatively, her dark face lighting up with a smile, and softening and growing womanly the moment she began to speak.", "숙녀가 묻듯이 말했다. 그녀의 가무잡잡한 얼굴은 미소로 환해지며, 말을 시작하는 순간 부드러워지고 여인다워졌다."], ["“We resigned all hope of you last night, and went to bed as usual.", "“저희는 어젯밤 당신을 뵐 희망을 완전히 접고 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었답니다."], ["Accept my apologies for our apparent want of attention; and allow me to introduce myself as one of your pupils.", "저희가 소홀했던 듯 보인 것을 사과드려요. 그리고 저를 당신의 제자 가운데 하나로 소개하게 해 주세요."], ["Shall we shake hands?", "우리 악수할까요?"], ["I suppose we must come to it sooner or later—and why not sooner?”", "어차피 이르든 늦든 하게 될 텐데—늦게보다야 이르게가 낫지 않겠어요?”"]], [["These odd words of welcome were spoken in a clear, ringing, pleasant voice.", "이 별스러운 환영의 말은 맑고 낭랑하며 유쾌한 목소리로 건네졌다."], ["The offered hand—rather large, but beautifully formed—was given to me with the easy, unaffected self-reliance of a highly-bred woman.", "내밀어진 손은—다소 컸으나 아름답게 빚어진—좋은 가문에서 자란 여인의 그 편안하고 꾸밈없는 자신감으로 나에게 건네졌다."], ["We sat down together at the breakfast-table in as cordial and customary a manner as if we had known each other for years, and had met at Limmeridge House to talk over old times by previous appointment.", "우리는 마치 몇 년째 서로 알고 지낸 사이인 양, 그리고 미리 약속해 옛이야기를 나누러 리머리지 하우스에서 만나기라도 한 양, 더없이 정답고 스스럼없는 태도로 아침 식탁에 나란히 앉았다."]], [["“I hope you come here good-humouredly determined to make the best of your position,”", "“당신이 자신의 처지를 좋게 받아들이겠노라 기분 좋게 마음먹고 이곳에 오셨기를 바라요.”"], ["continued the lady.", "숙녀가 이어 갔다."], ["“You will have to begin this morning by putting up with no other company at breakfast than mine.", "“오늘 아침은 아침 식탁에서 저 말고는 다른 벗 없이 견디는 것부터 시작하셔야 할 거예요."], ["My sister is in her own room, nursing that essentially feminine malady, a slight headache; and her old governess, Mrs.", "제 동생은 지극히 여성스러운 병, 곧 가벼운 두통을 앓느라 제 방에 있어요."], ["Vesey, is charitably attending on her with restorative tea. My uncle, Mr.", "그 애의 늙은 가정교사 베지 부인이 원기를 돋우는 차로 갸륵하게 시중들고 있고요."], ["Fairlie, never joins us at any of our meals: he is an invalid, and keeps bachelor state in his own apartments.", "제 삼촌 페얼리 씨는 저희 어느 끼니에도 결코 함께하지 않으세요. 병약한 분이라, 당신 처소에서 독신 생활을 하시지요."], ["There is nobody else in the house but me.", "이 집에는 저 말고 다른 사람이 없어요."], ["Two young ladies have been staying here, but they went away yesterday, in despair; and no wonder.", "젊은 숙녀 둘이 여기 묵고 있었는데 어제 절망해서 떠나 버렸답니다."], ["All through their visit (in consequence of Mr.", "그럴 만도 했지요."], ["Fairlie’s invalid condition) we produced no such convenience in the house as a flirtable, danceable, small-talkable creature of the male sex; and the consequence was, we did nothing but quarrel, especially at dinner-time.", "그들이 머무는 내내 (페얼리 씨가 병약한 탓에) 저희 집에는 시시덕거리고 춤추고 잡담을 나눌 만한 남성이라는 편의물이 하나도 없었거든요. 그러니 그 결과 저희는 다투는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았어요, 특히 저녁 식사 때면요."], ["How can you expect four women to dine together alone every day, and not quarrel?", "여자 넷이 날마다 단둘이 저녁을 먹으면서 다투지 않기를 어찌 바라겠어요?"], ["We are such fools, we can’t entertain each other at table.", "저희는 그런 바보들이라, 식탁에서 서로를 즐겁게 해 주질 못한답니다."], ["You see I don’t think much of my own sex, Mr. Hartright—which will you have, tea or coffee?—no woman does think much of her own sex, although few of them confess it as freely as I do.", "보세요, 저는 제 성(性)을 대단하게 여기지 않아요, 하트라이트 씨—차를 드릴까요, 커피를 드릴까요?—어떤 여자도 제 성을 대단하게 여기진 않지요, 저처럼 스스럼없이 털어놓는 여자는 몇 없지만요."], ["Dear me, you look puzzled. Why?", "아이참, 어리둥절해 보이시네요."], ["Are you wondering what you will have for breakfast?", "왜죠? 아침으로 무얼 드실지 궁금하세요?"], ["or are you surprised at my careless way of talking?", "아니면 제 무람없는 말투에 놀라셨나요?"], ["In the first case, I advise you, as a friend, to have nothing to do with that cold ham at your elbow, and to wait till the omelette comes in.", "앞쪽이라면, 벗으로서 충고하건대, 팔꿈치 옆의 저 찬 햄에는 손대지 마시고 오믈렛이 나올 때까지 기다리세요."], ["In the second case, I will give you some tea to compose your spirits, and do all a woman can (which is very little, by-the-bye) to hold my tongue.”", "뒤쪽이라면, 마음을 가라앉히시라고 차를 좀 드리고, 여자가 할 수 있는 (그런데 이건 여담이지만 참 보잘것없는) 온갖 애를 써서 제 입을 다물어 보겠어요.”"]], [["She handed me my cup of tea, laughing gaily.", "그녀는 즐거이 웃으며 찻잔을 나에게 건넸다."], ["Her light flow of talk, and her lively familiarity of manner with a total stranger, were accompanied by an unaffected naturalness and an easy inborn confidence in herself and her position, which would have secured her the respect of the most audacious man breathing.", "그 가벼운 말의 흐름과, 생판 낯선 이를 대하는 그 발랄한 스스럼없음에는, 꾸밈없는 자연스러움과, 자기 자신 및 자기 처지에 대한 편안하고도 타고난 자신감이 함께 배어 있어, 세상에 둘도 없이 대담한 사내조차 그녀를 우러르게 만들 만했다."], ["While it was impossible to be formal and reserved in her company, it was more than impossible to take the faintest vestige of a liberty with her, even in thought.", "그녀와 함께 있으면서 격식을 차리고 뻣뻣하게 굴기란 불가능했으나, 생각으로나마 그녀에게 티끌만 한 무례를 범하기란 불가능함을 넘어서는 일이었다."], ["I felt this instinctively, even while I caught the infection of her own bright gaiety of spirits—even while I did my best to answer her in her own frank, lively way.", "나는 그녀의 그 밝고 명랑한 기운에 절로 물들면서도—그녀의 솔직하고 발랄한 태도에 맞추어 답하려 애쓰면서도—이것을 본능적으로 느꼈다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "“네, 네.”"], ["she said, when I had suggested the only explanation I could offer, to account for my perplexed looks, “I understand.", "어리둥절한 내 표정을 해명하려고 내가 내놓을 수 있는 유일한 설명을 넌지시 비추자 그녀가 말했다. “알겠어요."], ["You are such a perfect stranger in the house, that you are puzzled by my familiar references to the worthy inhabitants.", "당신은 이 집에서 아주 생판 낯선 처지라, 이 갸륵한 거주자들에 대한 저의 스스럼없는 언급에 어리둥절하신 거예요."], ["Natural enough: I ought to have thought of it before.", "그럴 만하지요. 진작 그 생각을 했어야 하는데."], ["At any rate, I can set it right now.", "어쨌든 이제 바로잡을 수 있어요."], ["Suppose I begin with myself, so as to get done with that part of the subject as soon as possible?", "이 화제의 그 대목을 되도록 빨리 끝내 버리게, 저 자신부터 시작해 볼까요? 제 이름은 매리언 홀콤이에요."], ["My name is Marian Halcombe; and I am as inaccurate as women usually are, in calling Mr. Fairlie my uncle, and Miss Fairlie my sister.", "그리고 저는 페얼리 씨를 삼촌이라, 페얼리 양을 동생이라 부른다는 점에서, 여느 여자들만큼이나 부정확하답니다."], ["My mother was twice married: the first time to Mr.", "제 어머니는 두 번 혼인하셨어요."], ["Halcombe, my father; the second time to Mr. Fairlie, my half-sister’s father.", "첫 번째는 제 아버지 홀콤 씨와, 두 번째는 제 이복동생의 아버지 페얼리 씨와요."], ["Except that we are both orphans, we are in every respect as unlike each other as possible.", "저희 둘 다 고아라는 것 말고는, 저희는 모든 면에서 더없이 서로 다르답니다."], ["My father was a poor man, and Miss Fairlie’s father was a rich man.", "제 아버지는 가난한 분이었고, 페얼리 양의 아버지는 부유한 분이었어요."], ["I have got nothing, and she has a fortune.", "저는 가진 게 하나도 없고, 그 애는 재산이 있지요."], ["I am dark and ugly, and she is fair and pretty.", "저는 가무잡잡하고 못생겼고, 그 애는 살결 곱고 어여뻐요."], ["Everybody thinks me crabbed and odd (with perfect justice); and everybody thinks her sweet-tempered and charming (with more justice still).", "다들 저를 심술궂고 별나다 여기고요(더없이 옳은 말이지요), 다들 그 애를 상냥하고 매력적이라 여겨요(더더욱 옳은 말이고요). 한마디로, 그 애는 천사예요."], ["In short, she is an angel; and I am—— Try some of that marmalade, Mr. Hartright, and finish the sentence, in the name of female propriety, for yourself.", "그리고 저는—— 저 마멀레이드나 좀 드셔 보세요, 하트라이트 씨, 그리고 그 문장의 끝은 여자의 체면을 봐서라도 당신이 알아서 채우시고요."], ["What am I to tell you about Mr. Fairlie?", "페얼리 씨에 대해 무얼 말씀드려야 할까요?"], ["Upon my honour, I hardly know.", "정말이지, 저도 잘 모르겠어요."], ["He is sure to send for you after breakfast, and you can study him for yourself.", "그분은 아침 식사 뒤에 틀림없이 당신을 부르러 사람을 보내실 테니, 당신 스스로 그분을 살펴보시면 돼요."], ["In the meantime, I may inform you, first, that he is the late Mr.", "그동안 저는 이것만 알려 드리지요. 첫째, 그분은 돌아가신 페얼리 씨의 남동생이라는 것."], ["Fairlie’s younger brother; secondly, that he is a single man; and thirdly, that he is Miss Fairlie’s guardian.", "둘째, 그분은 독신이라는 것. 셋째, 그분은 페얼리 양의 후견인이라는 것."], ["I won’t live without her, and she can’t live without me; and that is how I come to be at Limmeridge House.", "저는 그 애 없이는 못 살고, 그 애는 저 없이는 못 살아요. 그래서 제가 리머리지 하우스에 있게 된 거고요."], ["My sister and I are honestly fond of each other; which, you will say, is perfectly unaccountable, under the circumstances, and I quite agree with you—but so it is.", "제 동생과 저는 진심으로 서로를 아껴요. 이런 형편에 그건 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 일이라고 당신은 말씀하시겠지요, 저도 전적으로 동감이에요—하지만 사실이 그렇답니다."], ["You must please both of us, Mr. Hartright, or please neither of us: and, what is still more trying, you will be thrown entirely upon our society.", "하트라이트 씨, 당신은 저희 둘 다를 흡족하게 하시거나, 아니면 저희 둘 다를 흡족하게 못 하시거나예요."], ["Mrs. Vesey is an excellent person, who possesses all the cardinal virtues, and counts for nothing; and Mr.", "그리고 더 골치 아픈 건, 당신이 오로지 저희 벗에만 내던져지리라는 거예요."], ["Fairlie is too great an invalid to be a companion for anybody.", "베지 부인은 온갖 으뜸가는 미덕을 두루 갖춘 훌륭한 분이지만, 있으나 마나 한 존재고요."], ["I don’t know what is the matter with him, and the doctors don’t know what is the matter with him, and he doesn’t know himself what is the matter with him.", "페얼리 씨는 누구의 벗이 되기엔 너무 병약한 분이에요. 저는 그분이 무슨 병인지 모르고, 의사들도 그분이 무슨 병인지 모르고, 그분 자신도 자기가 무슨 병인지 몰라요."], ["We all say it’s on the nerves, and we none of us know what we mean when we say it.", "저희는 다들 신경 탓이라고 하는데, 그렇게 말하면서 저희 가운데 누구도 자기가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 몰라요."], ["However, I advise you to humour his little peculiarities, when you see him to-day.", "어쨌거나, 오늘 그분을 뵈면 그분의 자잘한 별스러움을 맞춰 드리기를 권해요."], ["Admire his collection of coins, prints, and water-colour drawings, and you will win his heart.", "그분의 주화며 판화며 수채화 수집품을 칭찬하시면, 그분의 마음을 얻으실 거예요."], ["Upon my word, if you can be contented with a quiet country life, I don’t see why you should not get on very well here.", "정말이지, 당신이 조용한 시골 생활에 만족하실 수 있다면, 여기서 아주 잘 지내지 못할 까닭이 없어 보여요."], ["From breakfast to lunch, Mr. Fairlie’s drawings will occupy you.", "아침 식사부터 점심까지는 페얼리 씨의 그림들이 당신을 붙들어 둘 거예요."], ["After lunch, Miss Fairlie and I shoulder our sketch-books, and go out to misrepresent Nature, under your directions.", "점심 뒤에는 페얼리 양과 제가 사생첩을 둘러메고 나가, 당신의 지도 아래 자연을 엉터리로 옮겨 그리지요."], ["Drawing is her favourite whim, mind, not mine.", "그림은 그 애의 좋아하는 취미예요, 알아 두세요, 제 취미가 아니라."], ["Women can’t draw—their minds are too flighty, and their eyes are too inattentive.", "여자는 그림을 못 그려요—마음이 너무 들뜨고, 눈이 너무 산만하거든요."], ["No matter—my sister likes it; so I waste paint and spoil paper, for her sake, as composedly as any woman in England.", "상관없어요—제 동생이 좋아하니, 저는 그 애를 위해 잉글랜드의 어느 여자 못지않게 태연히 물감을 낭비하고 종이를 버린답니다."], ["As for the evenings, I think we can help you through them.", "저녁 시간으로 말하자면, 저희가 당신이 그 시간을 넘기게 도울 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["Miss Fairlie plays delightfully.", "페얼리 양은 연주를 아주 훌륭하게 해요."], ["For my own poor part, I don’t know one note of music from the other; but I can match you at chess, backgammon, ecarte, and (with the inevitable female drawbacks) even at billiards as well.", "제 딱한 처지로 말하면, 저는 음표 하나 다른 음표와 구별하지 못하지만, 체스며 주사위 놀이며 에카르테로 당신과 맞설 수 있고, (여자로서 어쩔 수 없는 흠은 있으나) 심지어 당구로도 당신과 겨룰 수 있어요."], ["What do you think of the programme?", "이 계획이 어떠세요?"], ["Can you reconcile yourself to our quiet, regular life?", "저희의 조용하고 규칙적인 생활에 스스로를 맞추실 수 있겠어요?"], ["or do you mean to be restless, and secretly thirst for change and adventure, in the humdrum atmosphere of Limmeridge House?”", "아니면 안절부절못하며, 리머리지 하우스의 이 따분한 분위기 속에서 남몰래 변화와 모험을 갈망하실 참인가요?”"]], [["She had run on thus far, in her gracefully bantering way, with no other interruptions on my part than the unimportant replies which politeness required of me.", "그녀는 여기까지 그 우아하게 놀리는 듯한 말투로 쉼 없이 이야기했고, 나로서는 예의상 요구되는 대수롭잖은 대꾸 말고는 아무런 끼어듦도 없었다."], ["The turn of the expression, however, in her last question, or rather the one chance word, “adventure,” lightly as it fell from her lips, recalled my thoughts to my meeting with the woman in white, and urged me to discover the connection which the stranger’s own reference to Mrs.", "그러나 그녀의 마지막 물음 속 표현의 굽이, 아니 그보다는 우연히 나온 한 마디 “모험”이라는 말이, 그 입술에서 가벼이 떨어졌음에도, 흰옷을 입은 여인과의 만남으로 내 생각을 되돌려 놓았다."], ["Fairlie informed me must once have existed between the nameless fugitive from the Asylum, and the former mistress of Limmeridge House.", "그리고 그 낯선 이가 스스로 페얼리 부인을 언급한 것이 나에게 일러 준 그 연관—정신병원에서 달아난 이름 없는 도망자와 리머리지 하우스의 옛 안주인 사이에 한때 틀림없이 존재했을 그 연관—을 밝혀내라고 나를 재촉했다."]], [["“Even if I were the most restless of mankind,”", "“설령 제가 인간 가운데 가장 안절부절못하는 사람이라 해도.”"], ["I said, “I should be in no danger of thirsting after adventures for some time to come.", "내가 말했다. “한동안은 모험을 갈망할 위험이 없을 겁니다."], ["The very night before I arrived at this house, I met with an adventure; and the wonder and excitement of it, I can assure you, Miss Halcombe, will last me for the whole term of my stay in Cumberland, if not for a much longer period.”", "이 집에 당도하기 바로 전날 밤, 저는 한 가지 모험을 만났거든요. 그리고 그 놀라움과 흥분은, 장담하건대, 홀콤 양, 훨씬 더 긴 기간까지는 아니더라도 제가 컴벌랜드에 머무는 내내 저에게 남아 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“You don’t say so, Mr. Hartright!", "“어머, 정말요, 하트라이트 씨!"], ["May I hear it?”", "들어 봐도 될까요?”"]], [["“You have a claim to hear it.", "“들으실 자격이 있으시지요."], ["The chief person in the adventure was a total stranger to me, and may perhaps be a total stranger to you; but she certainly mentioned the name of the late Mrs.", "그 모험의 주된 인물은 저에게는 생판 낯선 이였고, 어쩌면 당신에게도 생판 낯선 이일지 모릅니다."], ["Fairlie in terms of the sincerest gratitude and regard.”", "그러나 그 여인은 분명, 돌아가신 페얼리 부인의 이름을 더없이 진심 어린 고마움과 흠모의 말로 입에 올렸습니다.”"]], [["“Mentioned my mother’s name!", "“제 어머니의 이름을요!"], ["You interest me indescribably.", "형언할 수 없이 흥미롭군요."], ["Pray go on.”", "어서 계속하세요.”"]], [["I at once related the circumstances under which I had met the woman in white, exactly as they had occurred; and I repeated what she had said to me about Mrs.", "나는 즉시, 내가 흰옷을 입은 여인을 만나게 된 정황을 있었던 그대로 낱낱이 이야기했다."], ["Fairlie and Limmeridge House, word for word.", "그리고 그녀가 페얼리 부인과 리머리지 하우스에 대해 나에게 한 말을 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 되풀이했다."]], [["Miss Halcombe’s bright resolute eyes looked eagerly into mine, from the beginning of the narrative to the end.", "홀콤 양의 밝고 결연한 눈은 이야기의 처음부터 끝까지 내 눈을 골똘히 들여다보았다."], ["Her face expressed vivid interest and astonishment, but nothing more.", "그녀의 얼굴은 생생한 흥미와 놀라움을 드러냈으나, 그 이상은 아무것도 드러내지 않았다."], ["She was evidently as far from knowing of any clue to the mystery as I was myself.", "그녀도 나 자신과 마찬가지로 이 수수께끼의 어떤 실마리도 알지 못하는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“Are you quite sure of those words referring to my mother?”", "“제 어머니를 가리키는 그 말들, 정말 확실한가요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Quite sure,”", "“틀림없습니다.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Whoever she may be, the woman was once at school in the village of Limmeridge, was treated with especial kindness by Mrs. Fairlie, and, in grateful remembrance of that kindness, feels an affectionate interest in all surviving members of the family.", "“그녀가 누구든 간에, 그 여인은 한때 리머리지 마을에서 학교를 다녔고, 페얼리 부인에게 각별한 친절을 받았으며, 그 친절을 고맙게 기억하여 이 집안의 살아 있는 모든 이들에게 애정 어린 관심을 느끼고 있었습니다."], ["She knew that Mrs. Fairlie and her husband were both dead; and she spoke of Miss Fairlie as if they had known each other when they were children.”", "그녀는 페얼리 부인과 그 남편이 둘 다 세상을 떠난 것을 알고 있었고, 페얼리 양에 대해서는 마치 어린 시절 서로 알고 지내기라도 한 양 이야기했습니다.”"]], [["“You said, I think, that she denied belonging to this place?”", "“그녀가 이 고장 사람은 아니라고 잡아뗐다고, 그렇게 말씀하셨던 것 같은데요?”"]], [["“Yes, she told me she came from Hampshire.”", "“네, 그녀는 자기가 햄프셔에서 왔다고 하더군요.”"]], [["“And you entirely failed to find out her name?”", "“그리고 그 이름은 끝내 알아내지 못하셨고요?”"]], [["“Entirely.”", "“전혀요.”"]], [["“Very strange. I think you were quite justified, Mr.", "“참 기이하네요."], ["Hartright, in giving the poor creature her liberty, for she seems to have done nothing in your presence to show herself unfit to enjoy it.", "하트라이트 씨, 당신이 그 가엾은 이에게 자유를 준 것은 아주 옳은 일이었다고 생각해요. 당신 앞에서 그 자유를 누리기에 마땅찮음을 드러낼 만한 짓은 그녀가 아무것도 하지 않은 듯하니까요."], ["But I wish you had been a little more resolute about finding out her name.", "하지만 그 이름을 알아내는 데 조금만 더 단호하셨더라면 하고 아쉬워요."], ["We must really clear up this mystery, in some way.", "저희는 어떻게든 이 수수께끼를 정말 풀어내야 해요."], ["You had better not speak of it yet to Mr. Fairlie, or to my sister.", "페얼리 씨나 제 동생에게는 아직 이 이야기를 하지 않으시는 게 좋겠어요."], ["They are both of them, I am certain, quite as ignorant of who the woman is, and of what her past history in connection with us can be, as I am myself.", "그 두 사람 다, 분명히, 그 여인이 누구인지도, 저희와 관련된 그녀의 지난 내력이 무엇일 수 있는지도 저 자신만큼이나 전혀 모르거든요."], ["But they are also, in widely different ways, rather nervous and sensitive; and you would only fidget one and alarm the other to no purpose.", "게다가 그 둘은, 저마다 사뭇 다른 방식으로 다소 신경이 예민하고 여려서, 당신은 공연히 한 사람은 안절부절못하게 하고 다른 한 사람은 겁먹게 할 뿐일 거예요."], ["As for myself, I am all aflame with curiosity, and I devote my whole energies to the business of discovery from this moment.", "저 자신으로 말하면, 저는 온통 호기심으로 불타오르고 있어서, 이 순간부터 제 온 기운을 이 알아내는 일에 바치겠어요."], ["When my mother came here, after her second marriage, she certainly established the village school just as it exists at the present time.", "제 어머니가 두 번째 혼인 뒤 이곳에 오셨을 때, 그분은 지금 있는 그대로의 마을 학교를 분명 세우셨어요."], ["But the old teachers are all dead, or gone elsewhere; and no enlightenment is to be hoped for from that quarter.", "하지만 옛 교사들은 다 죽었거나 다른 데로 갔으니, 그쪽에서는 밝혀질 것을 바랄 수 없어요."], ["The only other alternative I can think of——”", "제가 떠올릴 수 있는 유일한 다른 방도는——”"]], [["At this point we were interrupted by the entrance of the servant, with a message from Mr.", "이 대목에서 하인이 들어오는 바람에 저희 이야기가 끊겼다."], ["Fairlie, intimating that he would be glad to see me, as soon as I had done breakfast.", "하인은 페얼리 씨의 전갈을 가져왔는데, 내가 아침 식사를 마치는 대로 나를 기꺼이 만나 뵙고 싶어 하신다는 것이었다."]], [["“Wait in the hall,”", "“복도에서 기다리게.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, answering the servant for me, in her quick, ready way. “Mr.", "홀콤 양이 그 특유의 빠르고 시원시원한 태도로 나를 대신해 하인에게 답했다."], ["Hartright will come out directly. I was about to say,”", "“하트라이트 씨가 곧 나가실 테니.”"], ["she went on, addressing me again, “that my sister and I have a large collection of my mother’s letters, addressed to my father and to hers.", "그러고는 다시 나를 향해 말을 이었다. “제가 말씀드리려던 건, 제 동생과 제가 제 어머니의 편지를 잔뜩 모아 두었다는 거예요."], ["In the absence of any other means of getting information, I will pass the morning in looking over my mother’s correspondence with Mr.", "제 아버지와 그 애 아버지 앞으로 부친 편지들이지요. 달리 정보를 얻을 방도가 없으니, 저는 오전 시간을 어머니가 페얼리 씨와 주고받은 서신을 훑어보는 데 쓰겠어요."], ["Fairlie. He was fond of London, and was constantly away from his country home; and she was accustomed, at such times, to write and report to him how things went on at Limmeridge.", "그분은 런던을 좋아하셔서 시골집을 늘 비우셨고, 어머니는 그럴 때면 리머리지에서 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그분께 적어 알리곤 하셨거든요."], ["Her letters are full of references to the school in which she took so strong an interest; and I think it more than likely that I may have discovered something when we meet again.", "어머니의 편지에는 그분이 그토록 깊은 관심을 쏟으셨던 그 학교에 대한 언급이 가득해요. 그러니 저희가 다시 만날 무렵이면 제가 무언가를 알아냈을 공산이 크다고 봐요."], ["The luncheon hour is two, Mr. Hartright.", "점심시간은 두 시예요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["I shall have the pleasure of introducing you to my sister by that time, and we will occupy the afternoon in driving round the neighbourhood and showing you all our pet points of view.", "그때쯤 제 동생을 당신께 소개하는 기쁨을 누리겠고, 오후에는 이웃을 마차로 두루 돌며 저희가 아끼는 온갖 경치를 당신께 보여 드리는 데 쓰겠어요."], ["Till two o’clock, then, farewell.”", "그럼 두 시까지, 안녕히.”"]], [["She nodded to me with the lively grace, the delightful refinement of familiarity, which characterised all that she did and all that she said; and disappeared by a door at the lower end of the room.", "그녀는 자기가 하는 모든 것과 자기가 하는 모든 말을 특징짓는 그 발랄한 우아함, 그 흐뭇한 세련된 스스럼없음으로 나에게 고갯짓을 하고는, 방의 아래쪽 끝에 있는 문으로 사라졌다."], ["As soon as she had left me, I turned my steps towards the hall, and followed the servant, on my way, for the first time, to the presence of Mr. Fairlie.", "그녀가 나를 떠나자마자, 나는 복도 쪽으로 발걸음을 돌려, 처음으로 페얼리 씨를 뵈러 가는 길에 하인을 뒤따랐다."]], [["# VII", "# VII"]], [["My conductor led me upstairs into a passage which took us back to the bedchamber in which I had slept during the past night; and opening the door next to it, begged me to look in.", "나를 안내하는 이는 나를 위층으로 이끌어, 지난밤 내가 잔 침실로 도로 이어지는 복도로 데려갔다. 그러고는 그 옆방 문을 열며 안을 들여다보아 달라 청했다."]], [["“I have my master’s orders to show you your own sitting-room, sir,”", "“주인 나리의 분부로, 나리께서 쓰실 거실을 보여 드리고, 위치와 채광이 마음에 드시는지 여쭈러 왔습니다, 나리.”"], ["said the man, “and to inquire if you approve of the situation and the light.”", "하인이 말했다."]], [["I must have been hard to please, indeed, if I had not approved of the room, and of everything about it.", "그 방과, 그 방에 딸린 온갖 것이 마음에 들지 않았다면 나는 실로 여간 까다로운 사람이 아니었을 것이다. 활 모양의 퇴창은 아침에 침실에서 감탄하며 바라본 바로 그 사랑스러운 풍경을 내다보고 있었다."], ["The bow-window looked out on the same lovely view which I had admired, in the morning, from my bedroom.", "가구는 호사와 아름다움의 극치였다. 한복판의 탁자는 화사하게 장정된 책들과 우아한 필기도구, 아름다운 꽃들로 환했다."], ["The furniture was the perfection of luxury and beauty; the table in the centre was bright with gaily bound books, elegant conveniences for writing, and beautiful flowers; the second table, near the window, was covered with all the necessary materials for mounting water-colour drawings, and had a little easel attached to it, which I could expand or fold up at will; the walls were hung with gaily tinted chintz; and the floor was spread with Indian matting in maize-colour and red.", "창가의 두 번째 탁자는 수채화를 표구하는 데 필요한 온갖 재료로 덮여 있었고, 거기에는 마음대로 펴거나 접을 수 있는 작은 이젤이 하나 붙어 있었다. 벽에는 화사하게 물들인 사라사가 드리워져 있었고, 바닥에는 옥수숫빛과 붉은빛의 인도산 돗자리가 깔려 있었다."], ["It was the prettiest and most luxurious little sitting-room I had ever seen; and I admired it with the warmest enthusiasm.", "그것은 내가 여태 본 가장 어여쁘고 가장 호사스러운 작은 거실이었고, 나는 더없이 뜨거운 열의로 감탄했다."]], [["The solemn servant was far too highly trained to betray the slightest satisfaction.", "근엄한 하인은 너무나 잘 길들여진지라 티끌만 한 만족감도 내비치지 않았다."], ["He bowed with icy deference when my terms of eulogy were all exhausted, and silently opened the door for me to go out into the passage again.", "내 찬사의 말이 죄다 바닥나자 그는 얼음장같이 정중하게 절하고는, 다시 복도로 나가라며 소리 없이 문을 열어 주었다."]], [["We turned a corner, and entered a long second passage, ascended a short flight of stairs at the end, crossed a small circular upper hall, and stopped in front of a door covered with dark baize.", "우리는 모퉁이를 돌아 긴 두 번째 복도로 접어들었고, 그 끝에서 짧은 층계를 올라, 작은 원형의 위층 홀을 가로질러, 짙은 초록 나사 천으로 덮인 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The servant opened this door, and led me on a few yards to a second; opened that also, and disclosed two curtains of pale sea-green silk hanging before us; raised one of them noiselessly; softly uttered the words, “Mr.", "하인은 이 문을 열고 나를 몇 야드 더 이끌어 두 번째 문 앞으로 데려가더니, 그것마저 열어, 우리 앞에 드리운 옅은 바다빛 초록 비단 커튼 두 폭을 드러냈다."], ["Hartright,” and left me.", "그중 한 폭을 소리 없이 걷어 올리고는, 나직이 “하트라이트 씨입니다”라는 말을 읊고, 나를 남겨 두고 떠났다."]], [["I found myself in a large, lofty room, with a magnificent carved ceiling, and with a carpet over the floor, so thick and soft that it felt like piles of velvet under my feet.", "나는 웅장하게 조각된 천장이 있고, 바닥에는 발밑에 벨벳 더미라도 밟는 듯 두툼하고 부드러운 양탄자가 깔린, 크고 높다란 방 안에 있었다."], ["One side of the room was occupied by a long book-case of some rare inlaid wood that was quite new to me.", "방의 한쪽 벽은 나로서는 아주 처음 보는 어떤 진귀한 상감 목재로 만든 긴 책장이 차지하고 있었다."], ["It was not more than six feet high, and the top was adorned with statuettes in marble, ranged at regular distances one from the other.", "그것은 높이가 여섯 자를 넘지 않았고, 꼭대기는 서로 일정한 간격으로 늘어놓은 대리석 조각상들로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["On the opposite side stood two antique cabinets; and between them, and above them, hung a picture of the Virgin and Child, protected by glass, and bearing Raphael’s name on the gilt tablet at the bottom of the frame.", "맞은편에는 고풍스러운 장식장 둘이 서 있었고, 그 사이, 그 위에는 유리로 보호된 성모자화(聖母子畵)가 걸려 있었는데, 액자 아래쪽 도금한 명패에 라파엘로의 이름이 적혀 있었다."], ["On my right hand and on my left, as I stood inside the door, were chiffoniers and little stands in buhl and marquetterie, loaded with figures in Dresden china, with rare vases, ivory ornaments, and toys and curiosities that sparkled at all points with gold, silver, and precious stones.", "문 안쪽에 서 있는 내 오른편과 왼편에는 뷸 세공과 쪽매붙임으로 꾸민 장식장과 작은 받침대들이 있었고, 그 위에는 드레스덴 자기 인형들과, 진귀한 꽃병들, 상아 장식물들, 그리고 금과 은과 보석으로 모서리마다 반짝이는 노리개와 골동품들이 잔뜩 놓여 있었다. 나를 마주한 방의 아래쪽 끝에서는, 창들이 가려지고 햇빛은 문에 드리운 커튼과 똑같은 옅은 바다빛 초록의 커다란 블라인드로 누그러져 있었다."], ["At the lower end of the room, opposite to me, the windows were concealed and the sunlight was tempered by large blinds of the same pale sea-green colour as the curtains over the door.", "그렇게 만들어진 빛은 감미롭도록 부드럽고 신비로우며 은은했다. 그 빛은 방 안의 온갖 물건에 고루 내려앉았다."], ["The light thus produced was deliciously soft, mysterious, and subdued; it fell equally upon all the objects in the room; it helped to intensify the deep silence, and the air of profound seclusion that possessed the place; and it surrounded, with an appropriate halo of repose, the solitary figure of the master of the house, leaning back, listlessly composed, in a large easy-chair, with a reading-easel fastened on one of its arms, and a little table on the other.", "그 빛은 이곳을 사로잡은 깊은 정적과 그윽한 은둔의 분위기를 한층 짙게 하는 데 이바지했다. 그리고 그 빛은, 한쪽 팔걸이에 독서용 서견대가, 다른 쪽에 작은 탁자가 달린 커다란 안락의자에 나른하게 몸을 기댄 채 무기력하게 늘어져 있는 저택 주인의 외로운 형체를, 어울리는 안식의 후광으로 에워싸고 있었다."]], [["If a man’s personal appearance, when he is out of his dressing-room, and when he has passed forty, can be accepted as a safe guide to his time of life—which is more than doubtful—Mr.", "한 사내가 화장실 밖으로 나와 있을 때, 그리고 마흔을 넘겼을 때의 그 생김새를 그의 나잇대를 짐작하는 믿을 만한 길잡이로 받아들일 수 있다면—그건 자못 미덥지 못한 일이지만—내가 그를 보았을 때 페얼리 씨의 나이는 쉰이 넘고 예순은 안 되는 것으로 어림잡는 것이 무리 없어 보였다."], ["Fairlie’s age, when I saw him, might have been reasonably computed at over fifty and under sixty years.", "수염 없는 그의 얼굴은 야위고 지치고 훤히 비칠 만큼 창백했으나 주름은 없었다. 코는 높고 매부리 같았다."], ["His beardless face was thin, worn, and transparently pale, but not wrinkled; his nose was high and hooked; his eyes were of a dim greyish blue, large, prominent, and rather red round the rims of the eyelids; his hair was scanty, soft to look at, and of that light sandy colour which is the last to disclose its own changes towards grey.", "눈은 흐릿한 잿빛 도는 파랑으로 크고 튀어나왔으며, 눈꺼풀 가장자리께가 다소 붉었다. 머리칼은 숱이 적고 보기에 부드러웠으며, 회색으로 바뀌는 제 변화를 가장 늦게 드러내는 그 옅은 모래빛이었다."], ["He was dressed in a dark frock-coat, of some substance much thinner than cloth, and in waistcoat and trousers of spotless white.", "그는 천보다 훨씬 얇은 어떤 감으로 지은 짙은 프록코트에, 티끌 하나 없이 흰 조끼와 바지를 입고 있었다."], ["His feet were effeminately small, and were clad in buff-coloured silk stockings, and little womanish bronze-leather slippers.", "발은 여자처럼 앙증맞았고, 담황색 비단 양말에 자그마한 여자 같은 청동빛 가죽 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다."], ["Two rings adorned his white delicate hands, the value of which even my inexperienced observation detected to be all but priceless.", "두 개의 반지가 그의 희고 섬세한 손을 꾸미고 있었는데, 그 값어치는 경험 없는 내 눈으로도 거의 값을 매길 수 없을 만큼임을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Upon the whole, he had a frail, languidly-fretful, over-refined look—something singularly and unpleasantly delicate in its association with a man, and, at the same time, something which could by no possibility have looked natural and appropriate if it had been transferred to the personal appearance of a woman.", "전체로 보아 그는 연약하고 나른하게 짜증스러우며 지나치게 세련된 인상을 풍겼다—사내에게 결부되어 유난히, 그리고 불쾌하게 섬세한 무언가이면서, 동시에 그것을 여자의 생김새에 옮겨 놓았다 한들 결코 자연스럽거나 어울려 보이지는 못했을 무언가였다."], ["My morning’s experience of Miss Halcombe had predisposed me to be pleased with everybody in the house; but my sympathies shut themselves up resolutely at the first sight of Mr. Fairlie.", "아침에 홀콤 양을 겪은 터라 나는 이 집의 누구에게든 호감을 가질 마음가짐이 되어 있었으나, 페얼리 씨를 처음 본 순간 내 호의는 단호히 문을 닫아걸었다."]], [["On approaching nearer to him, I discovered that he was not so entirely without occupation as I had at first supposed.", "그에게 더 가까이 다가가면서, 나는 그가 처음 짐작한 만큼 그렇게 아주 일 없이 있는 것은 아님을 알아챘다."], ["Placed amid the other rare and beautiful objects on a large round table near him, was a dwarf cabinet in ebony and silver, containing coins of all shapes and sizes, set out in little drawers lined with dark purple velvet.", "그의 곁 큰 원탁 위, 다른 진귀하고 아름다운 물건들 틈에는, 흑단과 은으로 만든 자그마한 장식장이 놓여 있었는데, 짙은 자줏빛 벨벳을 안에 댄 작은 서랍들에 온갖 모양과 크기의 주화가 늘어놓여 있었다."], ["One of these drawers lay on the small table attached to his chair; and near it were some tiny jeweller’s brushes, a wash-leather “stump,”", "그 서랍 가운데 하나가 그의 의자에 딸린 작은 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 그 곁에는 자그마한 보석 세공용 붓 몇 자루와 섀미 가죽 “뭉치”"], ["and a little bottle of liquid, all waiting to be used in various ways for the removal of any accidental impurities which might be discovered on the coins.", "하나, 그리고 작은 액체 병 하나가, 주화에서 발견될 법한 우연한 얼룩을 갖가지로 지우는 데 쓰이기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["His frail white fingers were listlessly toying with something which looked, to my uninstructed eyes, like a dirty pewter medal with ragged edges, when I advanced within a respectful distance of his chair, and stopped to make my bow.", "내가 그의 의자에서 알맞은 거리 안으로 다가가 절하려고 멈춰 섰을 때, 그의 연약하고 흰 손가락들은 내 서투른 눈에는 가장자리가 너덜너덜한 지저분한 백랍 메달처럼 보이는 무언가를 무기력하게 만지작거리고 있었다."]], [["“So glad to possess you at Limmeridge, Mr. Hartright,”", "“리머리지에 당신을 모시게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["he said in a querulous, croaking voice, which combined, in anything but an agreeable manner, a discordantly high tone with a drowsily languid utterance.", "그가 짜증스레 갈라지는 목소리로 말했는데, 그 목소리는 도무지 유쾌하달 수 없는 방식으로, 거슬리게 높은 음조와 졸린 듯 나른한 발음을 뒤섞어 놓았다."], ["“Pray sit down.", "“앉으시지요."], ["And don’t trouble yourself to move the chair, please.", "그리고 의자는 굳이 옮기지 마시고요."], ["In the wretched state of my nerves, movement of any kind is exquisitely painful to me.", "제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 움직임이든 저에게는 지독히도 괴롭답니다."], ["Have you seen your studio?", "당신 화실은 보셨습니까?"], ["Will it do?”", "쓸 만하던가요?”"]], [["“I have just come from seeing the room, Mr.", "“방금 그 방을 보고 오는 길입니다, 페얼리 씨."], ["Fairlie; and I assure you——”", "그리고 장담하건대——”"]], [["He stopped me in the middle of the sentence, by closing his eyes, and holding up one of his white hands imploringly.", "그는 눈을 감고 흰 손 하나를 애원하듯 치켜들어 내 말을 문장 한복판에서 막았다. 나는 놀라 말을 멈추었다."], ["I paused in astonishment; and the croaking voice honoured me with this explanation—", "그러자 그 갈라지는 목소리가 나에게 이런 설명을 베풀어 주었다—"]], [["“Pray excuse me.", "“부디 용서하세요."], ["But could you contrive to speak in a lower key?", "그런데 조금 더 낮은 음으로 말씀해 주실 수 있을까요?"], ["In the wretched state of my nerves, loud sound of any kind is indescribable torture to me.", "제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 큰 소리든 저에게는 형언할 수 없는 고문이랍니다."], ["You will pardon an invalid?", "병자를 너그러이 봐주시겠지요?"], ["I only say to you what the lamentable state of my health obliges me to say to everybody.", "저는 제 딱한 건강 상태가 누구에게든 하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 말을 당신께 할 뿐이에요. 그래요."], ["Yes. And you really like the room?”", "그런데 방이 정말 마음에 드십니까?”"]], [["“I could wish for nothing prettier and nothing more comfortable,” I answered, dropping my voice, and beginning to discover already that Mr.", "“그보다 더 어여쁘고 더 편안한 것은 바랄 수 없을 정도입니다.”"], ["Fairlie’s selfish affectation and Mr. Fairlie’s wretched nerves meant one and the same thing.", "나는 목소리를 낮추며 대답했고, 페얼리 씨의 이기적인 허세와 페얼리 씨의 딱한 신경이 하나이자 같은 것임을 벌써 알아채기 시작했다."]], [["“So glad.", "“참으로 기쁘군요."], ["You will find your position here, Mr. Hartright, properly recognised.", "하트라이트 씨, 여기서 당신은 자신의 처지가 마땅히 인정받는 것을 보게 될 겁니다."], ["There is none of the horrid English barbarity of feeling about the social position of an artist in this house.", "이 집에는 예술가의 사회적 지위에 대한 저 끔찍한 영국식 야만의 감정 따위는 없어요."], ["So much of my early life has been passed abroad, that I have quite cast my insular skin in that respect.", "제 젊은 시절의 많은 부분을 외국에서 보낸지라, 그 점에서 저는 섬나라의 껍질을 아주 벗어 던졌지요."], ["I wish I could say the same of the gentry—detestable word, but I suppose I must use it—of the gentry in the neighbourhood.", "이웃의 지주 양반들—가증스러운 말이지만 쓸 수밖에 없나 봅니다—에 대해서도 같은 말을 할 수 있으면 좋으련만."], ["They are sad Goths in Art, Mr. Hartright.", "그들은 예술에서는 딱한 고트족이랍니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["People, I do assure you, who would have opened their eyes in astonishment, if they had seen Charles the Fifth pick up Titian’s brush for him.", "정말이지, 카를 오세가 티치아노를 위해 그 붓을 주워 주는 걸 봤다면 놀라 눈이 휘둥그레졌을 사람들이지요."], ["Do you mind putting this tray of coins back in the cabinet, and giving me the next one to it?", "이 주화 쟁반을 장식장에 도로 넣고, 그 옆의 것을 저에게 건네주시겠어요?"], ["In the wretched state of my nerves, exertion of any kind is unspeakably disagreeable to me.", "제 신경이 이 딱한 상태라, 어떤 종류의 수고든 저에게는 말할 수 없이 언짢답니다. 그래요."], ["Yes. Thank you.”", "고맙습니다.”"]], [["As a practical commentary on the liberal social theory which he had just favoured me by illustrating, Mr. Fairlie’s cool request rather amused me.", "방금 몸소 예를 들어 베풀어 준 그 진보적인 사회 이론에 대한 실천적 논평인 양, 페얼리 씨의 태연한 청은 나를 다소 우습게 만들었다."], ["I put back one drawer and gave him the other, with all possible politeness.", "나는 되도록 공손하게 서랍 하나를 도로 넣고 다른 것을 그에게 건넸다."], ["He began trifling with the new set of coins and the little brushes immediately; languidly looking at them and admiring them all the time he was speaking to me.", "그는 곧바로 새 주화 무리와 작은 붓들을 만지작거리기 시작했고, 나에게 말하는 내내 그것들을 나른히 바라보며 감탄했다."]], [["“A thousand thanks and a thousand excuses.", "“천 번 감사하고 천 번 사과합니다."], ["Do you like coins? Yes.", "주화를 좋아하십니까?"], ["So glad we have another taste in common besides our taste for Art.", "그렇군요. 예술 취향 말고도 또 하나의 공통 취향을 우리가 지녔다니 참으로 기쁩니다."], ["Now, about the pecuniary arrangements between us—do tell me—are they satisfactory?”", "자, 우리 사이의 금전 문제인데요—말씀해 주세요—만족스러우십니까?”"]], [["“Most satisfactory, Mr. Fairlie.”", "“더없이 만족스럽습니다, 페얼리 씨.”"]], [["“So glad.", "“참으로 기쁘군요."], ["And—what next?", "그리고—다음은 뭐였더라?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I remember. Yes.", "생각났어요."], ["In reference to the consideration which you are good enough to accept for giving me the benefit of your accomplishments in art, my steward will wait on you at the end of the first week, to ascertain your wishes.", "그래요. 당신이 고맙게도 받아들여 예술적 재능의 혜택을 저에게 베풀어 주시는 그 보수에 관해서는, 첫 주가 끝날 무렵 제 집사가 당신을 찾아뵙고 당신의 뜻을 여쭐 겁니다."], ["And—what next? Curious, is it not?", "그리고—다음은 뭐였더라?"], ["I had a great deal more to say: and I appear to have quite forgotten it.", "묘하지요? 훨씬 더 할 말이 많았는데, 아주 까맣게 잊어버린 모양입니다."], ["Do you mind touching the bell?", "종을 좀 울려 주시겠어요?"], ["In that corner.", "저 구석에 있어요. 그래요."], ["Yes. Thank you.”", "고맙습니다.”"]], [["I rang; and a new servant noiselessly made his appearance—a foreigner, with a set smile and perfectly brushed hair—a valet every inch of him.", "내가 종을 울리자, 새 하인 하나가 소리 없이 나타났다—판에 박힌 미소에 완벽하게 빗질된 머리를 한 외국인—온몸이 하나부터 열까지 시종인 자였다."]], [["“Louis,” said Mr.", "“루이.”"], ["Fairlie, dreamily dusting the tips of his fingers with one of the tiny brushes for the coins, “I made some entries in my tablettes this morning.", "페얼리 씨가 주화용 자그마한 붓 하나로 손가락 끝을 나른하게 털며 말했다. “오늘 아침 내 수첩에 몇 가지 적어 두었다."], ["Find my tablettes.", "내 수첩을 찾아라."], ["A thousand pardons, Mr. Hartright, I’m afraid I bore you.”", "천 번 용서하세요, 하트라이트 씨, 제가 당신을 지루하게 하는 게 아닌지요.”"]], [["As he wearily closed his eyes again, before I could answer, and as he did most assuredly bore me, I sat silent, and looked up at the Madonna and Child by Raphael.", "내가 대답하기도 전에 그가 지친 듯 다시 눈을 감았고, 또 그가 나를 지루하게 한 것이 정말 틀림없었으므로, 나는 잠자코 앉아 라파엘로의 성모자화를 올려다보았다."], ["In the meantime, the valet left the room, and returned shortly with a little ivory book.", "그동안 시종은 방을 나가더니, 이내 작은 상아 수첩을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Mr. Fairlie, after first relieving himself by a gentle sigh, let the book drop open with one hand, and held up the tiny brush with the other, as a sign to the servant to wait for further orders.", "페얼리 씨는 먼저 가볍게 한숨지어 마음을 놓은 뒤, 한 손으로 수첩이 툭 펼쳐지게 두고, 다른 손으로는 더 이상의 분부를 기다리라는 신호로 자그마한 붓을 치켜들었다."]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["Just so!” said Mr.", "바로 그거야!”"], ["Fairlie, consulting the tablettes.", "페얼리 씨가 수첩을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“Louis, take down that portfolio.”", "“루이, 저 화첩을 내려라.”"], ["He pointed, as he spoke, to several portfolios placed near the window, on mahogany stands.", "그는 말하며 창가의 마호가니 받침대 위에 놓인 여러 화첩을 가리켰다."], ["“No.", "“아니."], ["Not the one with the green back—that contains my Rembrandt etchings, Mr. Hartright.", "초록 등의 것 말고—그건 내 렘브란트 에칭이 들어 있으니까, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Do you like etchings?", "에칭을 좋아하십니까? 그래요?"], ["Yes? So glad we have another taste in common.", "또 하나의 공통 취향을 우리가 지녔다니 참으로 기쁘군요."], ["The portfolio with the red back, Louis.", "붉은 등의 화첩이다, 루이."], ["Don’t drop it!", "떨어뜨리지 마라!"], ["You have no idea of the tortures I should suffer, Mr. Hartright, if Louis dropped that portfolio.", "루이가 저 화첩을 떨어뜨리면 제가 어떤 고문을 겪을지 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Is it safe on the chair?", "의자 위에 안전하게 놓였나?"], ["Do YOU think it safe, Mr.", "당신이 보시기엔 안전한가요, 하트라이트 씨?"], ["Hartright?", "그래요?"], ["Yes? So glad.", "참으로 기쁩니다."], ["Will you oblige me by looking at the drawings, if you really think they are quite safe.", "정말 아주 안전하다고 여기신다면, 저 소묘들을 좀 봐 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요."], ["Louis, go away.", "루이, 물러가라."], ["What an ass you are.", "이 멍청한 것."], ["Don’t you see me holding the tablettes?", "내가 수첩을 들고 있는 게 안 보이느냐?"], ["Do you suppose I want to hold them?", "내가 이걸 들고 있고 싶어 하는 줄 아느냐?"], ["Then why not relieve me of the tablettes without being told?", "그럼 시키지 않아도 이 수첩을 나에게서 받아 가면 될 것을."], ["A thousand pardons, Mr.", "천 번 용서하세요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright; servants are such asses, are they not?", "하인이란 것들은 참 멍청하지요, 안 그래요?"], ["Do tell me—what do you think of the drawings?", "어서 말씀해 주세요—저 소묘들을 어떻게 보십니까?"], ["They have come from a sale in a shocking state—I thought they smelt of horrid dealers’ and brokers’ fingers when I looked at them last.", "어느 경매에서 아주 참혹한 꼴로 왔지요—지난번에 들여다봤을 때 끔찍한 상인과 중개인 손 냄새가 나는 것만 같았어요."], ["CAN you undertake them?”", "당신이 저것들을 맡아 주실 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["Although my nerves were not delicate enough to detect the odour of plebeian fingers which had offended Mr. Fairlie’s nostrils, my taste was sufficiently educated to enable me to appreciate the value of the drawings, while I turned them over.", "내 신경은 페얼리 씨의 콧구멍을 거스른 그 천한 손가락 냄새를 알아챌 만큼 섬세하지는 못했으나, 내 안목은 그 소묘들을 넘겨 보는 동안 그 값어치를 알아볼 만큼은 충분히 다져져 있었다."], ["They were, for the most part, really fine specimens of English water-colour art; and they had deserved much better treatment at the hands of their former possessor than they appeared to have received.", "그것들은 대부분 정말 훌륭한 영국 수채화의 표본들이었고, 예전 소유자의 손에서 받은 듯한 대접보다는 훨씬 나은 대접을 받을 만한 것들이었다."]], [["“The drawings,”", "“이 소묘들은.” 내가 대답했다."], ["I answered, “require careful straining and mounting; and, in my opinion, they are well worth——”", "“조심스레 펴서 표구할 필요가 있습니다. 그리고 제 소견으로는, 그럴 만한 값어치가 충분히——”"]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "“실례합니다.”"], ["interposed Mr. Fairlie.", "페얼리 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["“Do you mind my closing my eyes while you speak?", "“당신이 말씀하시는 동안 제가 눈을 감아도 괜찮겠습니까?"], ["Even this light is too much for them.", "이 정도 빛조차 제 눈에는 너무 과하답니다."], ["Yes?”", "그래요?”"]], [["“I was about to say that the drawings are well worth all the time and trouble——”", "“이 소묘들은 온갖 시간과 수고를 들일 만한 값어치가 충분히——라고 말하려던 참이었습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Fairlie suddenly opened his eyes again, and rolled them with an expression of helpless alarm in the direction of the window.", "페얼리 씨가 별안간 다시 눈을 뜨더니, 어쩔 줄 모르는 놀라움의 표정으로 창 쪽으로 눈을 굴렸다."]], [["“I entreat you to excuse me, Mr. Hartright,”", "“부디 저를 용서해 주세요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["he said in a feeble flutter.", "그가 힘없이 파르르 떨며 말했다."], ["“But surely I hear some horrid children in the garden—my private garden—below?”", "“그런데 정원에서—제 개인 정원, 저 아래에서—끔찍한 아이들 소리가 들리는 게 분명해요.”"]], [["“I can’t say, Mr.", "“글쎄요, 페얼리 씨."], ["Fairlie. I heard nothing myself.”", "저는 아무 소리도 못 들었습니다만.”"]], [["“Oblige me—you have been so very good in humouring my poor nerves—oblige me by lifting up a corner of the blind.", "“제 부탁 하나 들어주세요—제 딱한 신경을 그토록 잘 맞춰 주셨으니—블라인드 한 귀퉁이를 들어 올려 주는 호의를 베풀어 주세요."], ["Don’t let the sun in on me, Mr. Hartright!", "저에게 햇빛이 들게는 마시고요, 하트라이트 씨!"], ["Have you got the blind up?", "블라인드를 올리셨나요? 그래요?"], ["Yes? Then will you be so very kind as to look into the garden and make quite sure?”", "그럼 부디 정원을 들여다보고 아주 확실히 확인해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠어요?”"]], [["I complied with this new request.", "나는 이 새로운 청에 따랐다."], ["The garden was carefully walled in, all round.", "정원은 사방이 빈틈없이 담으로 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["Not a human creature, large or small, appeared in any part of the sacred seclusion.", "그 성스러운 은둔의 어느 구석에도 크든 작든 사람이라고는 하나도 나타나지 않았다."], ["I reported that gratifying fact to Mr. Fairlie.", "나는 그 흐뭇한 사실을 페얼리 씨에게 알렸다."]], [["“A thousand thanks.", "“천 번 감사합니다."], ["My fancy, I suppose.", "제 착각이었나 봅니다."], ["There are no children, thank Heaven, in the house; but the servants (persons born without nerves) will encourage the children from the village.", "다행히도 이 집에는 아이가 없지요. 하지만 하인들(신경이라고는 없이 태어난 자들)이 마을 아이들을 부추기거든요."], ["Such brats—oh, dear me, such brats! Shall I confess it, Mr.", "그런 개구쟁이들이라니—오, 저런, 그런 개구쟁이들이라니!"], ["Hartright?—I sadly want a reform in the construction of children.", "고백해도 될까요, 하트라이트 씨?—저는 아이들의 구조가 개혁되기를 못 견디게 바랍니다."], ["Nature’s only idea seems to be to make them machines for the production of incessant noise.", "자연의 유일한 발상이란 그것들을 끊임없는 소음을 만들어 내는 기계로 만드는 것뿐인가 봐요."], ["Surely our delightful Raffaello’s conception is infinitely preferable?”", "정녕 우리의 흐뭇한 라파엘로의 구상이 한없이 더 낫지 않겠어요?”"]], [["He pointed to the picture of the Madonna, the upper part of which represented the conventional cherubs of Italian Art, celestially provided with sitting accommodation for their chins, on balloons of buff-coloured cloud.", "그는 성모화를 가리켰는데, 그 윗부분에는 담황색 구름의 풍선 위에 턱을 얹을 앉을자리를 하늘의 은총으로 마련받은, 이탈리아 예술의 판에 박힌 아기 천사들이 그려져 있었다."]], [["“Quite a model family!”", "“정말 모범적인 가족이지요!”"], ["said Mr. Fairlie, leering at the cherubs.", "페얼리 씨가 아기 천사들을 곁눈질하며 말했다."], ["“Such nice round faces, and such nice soft wings, and—nothing else.", "“저렇게 어여쁜 둥근 얼굴에, 저렇게 어여쁜 보드라운 날개에—그리고 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요."], ["No dirty little legs to run about on, and no noisy little lungs to scream with.", "돌아다닐 지저분한 작은 다리도 없고, 비명 지를 시끄러운 작은 허파도 없지요."], ["How immeasurably superior to the existing construction!", "지금의 구조보다 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어난가요!"], ["I will close my eyes again, if you will allow me.", "허락해 주신다면 다시 눈을 감겠습니다."], ["And you really can manage the drawings? So glad.", "그런데 저 소묘들을 정말 맡아 주실 수 있겠지요?"], ["Is there anything else to settle?", "참으로 기쁩니다. 그 밖에 정할 게 또 있나요?"], ["if there is, I think I have forgotten it.", "있다면, 제가 잊어버린 모양입니다."], ["Shall we ring for Louis again?”", "루이를 다시 부를까요?”"]], [["Being, by this time, quite as anxious, on my side, as Mr. Fairlie evidently was on his, to bring the interview to a speedy conclusion, I thought I would try to render the summoning of the servant unnecessary, by offering the requisite suggestion on my own responsibility.", "이 무렵 나도 내 쪽에서, 페얼리 씨가 그의 쪽에서 그러한 것 못지않게 이 면담을 얼른 끝맺고 싶어진지라, 필요한 제안을 내 책임으로 내놓아 하인을 부를 필요를 없애 보아야겠다고 생각했다."]], [["“The only point, Mr. Fairlie, that remains to be discussed,”", "“페얼리 씨, 논의할 남은 유일한 문제는.”"], ["I said, “refers, I think, to the instruction in sketching which I am engaged to communicate to the two young ladies.”", "내가 말했다. “제가 두 젊은 숙녀에게 전하기로 되어 있는 사생 지도에 관한 것인 듯합니다.”"]], [["“Ah! just so,”", "“아!"], ["said Mr. Fairlie.", "바로 그거예요.”"], ["“I wish I felt strong enough to go into that part of the arrangement—but I don’t.", "페얼리 씨가 말했다. “그 부분의 조정에까지 파고들 만큼 기운이 있으면 좋으련만—그렇질 못해요."], ["The ladies who profit by your kind services, Mr. Hartright, must settle, and decide, and so on, for themselves.", "당신의 친절한 도움으로 덕을 보는 숙녀들이, 하트라이트 씨, 스스로 정하고 결정하고 그래야지요."], ["My niece is fond of your charming art.", "제 조카딸은 당신의 그 매혹적인 예술을 좋아합니다."], ["She knows just enough about it to be conscious of her own sad defects.", "제 딱한 결점을 스스로 알아챌 만큼은 그것을 알지요."], ["Please take pains with her.", "부디 그 애에게 공을 들여 주세요."], ["Yes.", "그래요."], ["Is there anything else?", "그 밖에 또 있나요? 없군요."], ["No. We quite understand each other—don’t we?", "우리는 서로를 아주 잘 이해했지요—그렇지 않나요?"], ["I have no right to detain you any longer from your delightful pursuit—have I?", "저에게는 당신을 그 흐뭇한 일에서 더 붙들어 둘 권리가 없어요—그렇지요?"], ["So pleasant to have settled everything—such a sensible relief to have done business.", "모든 걸 정해 놓으니 참 유쾌하군요—볼일을 마치니 참 시원스레 마음이 놓입니다."], ["Do you mind ringing for Louis to carry the portfolio to your own room?”", "화첩을 당신 방으로 나르게 루이를 좀 불러 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“I will carry it there myself, Mr. Fairlie, if you will allow me.”", "“괜찮으시다면, 페얼리 씨, 제가 직접 그것을 나르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Will you really?", "“정말요?"], ["Are you strong enough?", "그럴 만한 기운이 있으신가요?"], ["How nice to be so strong!", "그토록 기운이 세다니 참 좋군요!"], ["Are you sure you won’t drop it?", "정말 떨어뜨리지 않으실 수 있겠어요?"], ["So glad to possess you at Limmeridge, Mr. Hartright.", "리머리지에 당신을 모시게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["I am such a sufferer that I hardly dare hope to enjoy much of your society.", "저는 워낙 앓는 사람이라 당신 벗을 많이 누리기를 감히 바라기 어렵네요."], ["Would you mind taking great pains not to let the doors bang, and not to drop the portfolio?", "부디 문을 쾅 닫지 않도록, 화첩을 떨어뜨리지 않도록 각별히 애써 주시겠어요?"], ["Thank you.", "고맙습니다."], ["Gently with the curtains, please—the slightest noise from them goes through me like a knife.", "커튼도 부디 살살—거기서 나는 아주 작은 소리조차 칼처럼 제 몸을 관통한답니다. 그래요."], ["Yes. GOOD morning!”", "안녕히 가세요!”"]], [["When the sea-green curtains were closed, and when the two baize doors were shut behind me, I stopped for a moment in the little circular hall beyond, and drew a long, luxurious breath of relief.", "바다빛 초록 커튼이 닫히고 두 겹의 나사 문이 내 뒤로 닫히자, 나는 그 너머 작은 원형 홀에서 잠시 멈춰 서서 길고 호사스러운 안도의 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["It was like coming to the surface of the water after deep diving, to find myself once more on the outside of Mr. Fairlie’s room.", "페얼리 씨의 방 바깥으로 다시 나와 있는 나를 발견하는 것은, 깊이 잠수했다가 물 위로 떠오르는 것과도 같았다."]], [["As soon as I was comfortably established for the morning in my pretty little studio, the first resolution at which I arrived was to turn my steps no more in the direction of the apartments occupied by the master of the house, except in the very improbable event of his honouring me with a special invitation to pay him another visit.", "오전 시간을 보내려고 어여쁜 작은 화실에 편안히 자리 잡자마자, 내가 다다른 첫 번째 결심은, 그가 다시 방문해 달라며 특별히 초대하는 극히 있을 법하지 않은 경우가 아니고서는, 저택 주인이 거처하는 처소 쪽으로는 두 번 다시 발걸음을 돌리지 않겠다는 것이었다."], ["Having settled this satisfactory plan of future conduct in reference to Mr. Fairlie, I soon recovered the serenity of temper of which my employer’s haughty familiarity and impudent politeness had, for the moment, deprived me.", "페얼리 씨에 관한 이 흡족한 앞으로의 처신 방침을 정해 놓자, 나는 곧 마음의 평온을 되찾았다—고용주의 거만한 스스럼없음과 뻔뻔한 정중함이 잠시나마 나에게서 앗아 갔던 그 평온을."], ["The remaining hours of the morning passed away pleasantly enough, in looking over the drawings, arranging them in sets, trimming their ragged edges, and accomplishing the other necessary preparations in anticipation of the business of mounting them.", "오전의 남은 시간은 소묘들을 훑어보고, 묶음으로 정리하고, 너덜너덜한 가장자리를 다듬고, 그것들을 표구하는 일에 앞서 필요한 다른 채비를 갖추는 데 제법 유쾌하게 흘러갔다. 어쩌면 나는 이보다 더 많이 나아갔어야 했는지도 모른다."], ["I ought, perhaps, to have made more progress than this; but, as the luncheon-time drew near, I grew restless and unsettled, and felt unable to fix my attention on work, even though that work was only of the humble manual kind.", "그러나 점심시간이 다가올수록 나는 안절부절못하고 마음이 뒤숭숭해져, 그 일이 한낱 보잘것없는 손일에 지나지 않았음에도 도무지 거기에 주의를 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다."]], [["At two o’clock I descended again to the breakfast-room, a little anxiously.", "두 시에 나는 다소 조마조마한 마음으로 다시 아침 식사실로 내려갔다."], ["Expectations of some interest were connected with my approaching reappearance in that part of the house.", "저택의 그 자리에 곧 다시 나타나는 일에는 얼마간 흥미로운 기대가 걸려 있었다."], ["My introduction to Miss Fairlie was now close at hand; and, if Miss Halcombe’s search through her mother’s letters had produced the result which she anticipated, the time had come for clearing up the mystery of the woman in white.", "페얼리 양에게 소개되는 일이 이제 코앞에 다가와 있었고, 홀콤 양이 어머니의 편지를 뒤져 그녀가 내다본 결과를 얻어 냈다면, 흰옷을 입은 여인의 수수께끼를 밝힐 때가 온 것이었다."]], [["# VIII", "# VIII"]], [["When I entered the room, I found Miss Halcombe and an elderly lady seated at the luncheon-table.", "방에 들어서니, 홀콤 양과 한 노부인이 점심 식탁에 앉아 있었다."]], [["The elderly lady, when I was presented to her, proved to be Miss Fairlie’s former governess, Mrs.", "그 노부인은, 나에게 소개되고 보니, 페얼리 양의 옛 가정교사 베지 부인이었다."], ["Vesey, who had been briefly described to me by my lively companion at the breakfast-table, as possessed of “all the cardinal virtues, and counting for nothing.”", "아침 식탁에서 발랄한 동행이 나에게 “온갖 으뜸가는 미덕을 두루 갖추었으나 있으나 마나 한 존재”라고 짤막하게 일러 준 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["I can do little more than offer my humble testimony to the truthfulness of Miss Halcombe’s sketch of the old lady’s character.", "이 노부인의 성품에 대한 홀콤 양의 소묘가 얼마나 참된지에 대해, 나는 그저 변변찮은 증언을 보태는 것 말고는 할 수 있는 일이 거의 없다."], ["Mrs. Vesey looked the personification of human composure and female amiability.", "베지 부인은 인간의 침착함과 여성의 상냥함을 그대로 몸으로 빚어 놓은 듯했다."], ["A calm enjoyment of a calm existence beamed in drowsy smiles on her plump, placid face.", "잔잔한 삶을 잔잔히 누리는 즐거움이 통통하고 온화한 얼굴에 졸린 미소로 어려 있었다."], ["Some of us rush through life, and some of us saunter through life.", "우리 가운데 어떤 이는 삶을 내달려 지나고, 어떤 이는 삶을 어슬렁어슬렁 지난다."], ["Mrs. Vesey SAT through life.", "베지 부인은 삶을 앉은 채로 지났다."], ["Sat in the house, early and late; sat in the garden; sat in unexpected window-seats in passages; sat (on a camp-stool) when her friends tried to take her out walking; sat before she looked at anything, before she talked of anything, before she answered Yes, or No, to the commonest question—always with the same serene smile on her lips, the same vacantly-attentive turn of the head, the same snugly-comfortable position of her hands and arms, under every possible change of domestic circumstances.", "이르나 늦으나 집 안에 앉아 있었고, 정원에 앉아 있었고, 복도의 뜻밖의 창가 자리에 앉아 있었고, 벗들이 산책에 데리고 나가려 하면 (야전용 접이 의자에) 앉았고, 무엇을 바라보기 전에도, 무엇을 말하기 전에도, 가장 흔한 물음에 예, 아니요를 답하기 전에도 앉았다—입가에는 언제나 같은 잔잔한 미소를, 머리는 언제나 같은 멍하니 골똘한 각도로, 손과 팔은 언제나 같은 아늑히 편안한 자세로 둔 채, 집안 형편이 어찌 바뀌든 한결같았다."], ["A mild, a compliant, an unutterably tranquil and harmless old lady, who never by any chance suggested the idea that she had been actually alive since the hour of her birth.", "온화하고 고분고분하며, 이루 말할 수 없이 평온하고 무해한 노부인이었으니, 태어난 그 시각부터 실제로 살아 있어 왔으리라는 생각조차 어쩌다도 불러일으키는 법이 없었다."], ["Nature has so much to do in this world, and is engaged in generating such a vast variety of co-existent productions, that she must surely be now and then too flurried and confused to distinguish between the different processes that she is carrying on at the same time.", "자연은 이 세상에서 할 일이 너무나 많고, 나란히 존재하는 그토록 어마어마하게 다양한 산물을 만들어 내는 데 골몰해 있으니, 틀림없이 이따금은 한꺼번에 진행하는 그 갖가지 과정을 분간하지 못할 만큼 허둥대고 어수선해질 것이다."], ["Starting from this point of view, it will always remain my private persuasion that Nature was absorbed in making cabbages when Mrs. Vesey was born, and that the good lady suffered the consequences of a vegetable preoccupation in the mind of the Mother of us all.", "이 관점에서 출발하건대, 베지 부인이 태어날 때 자연은 양배추를 만드는 데 골몰해 있었으며, 그 선량한 부인은 우리 모두의 어머니이신 그분의 마음속에 자리한 채소에 대한 몰두의 결과를 겪은 것이라는 게 언제까지나 나의 개인적인 확신으로 남을 것이다."]], [["“Now, Mrs. Vesey,”", "“자, 베지 부인.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, looking brighter, sharper, and readier than ever, by contrast with the undemonstrative old lady at her side, “what will you have?", "홀콤 양이, 곁의 무덤덤한 노부인과 대비되어 그 어느 때보다 밝고 예리하고 시원시원해 보이는 얼굴로 말했다. “무얼 드시겠어요?"], ["A cutlet?”", "커틀릿?”"]], [["Mrs.", "베지 부인은 오목한 손을 식탁 가장자리에 포개고 온화하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["Vesey crossed her dimpled hands on the edge of the table, smiled placidly, and said, “Yes, dear.”", "“그래요, 그러지요.”"]], [["“What is that opposite Mr. Hartright?", "“하트라이트 씨 맞은편에 있는 게 뭐죠?"], ["Boiled chicken, is it not?", "삶은 닭인가요?"], ["I thought you liked boiled chicken better than cutlet, Mrs. Vesey?”", "베지 부인, 부인은 커틀릿보다 삶은 닭을 더 좋아하시는 줄 알았는데요?”"]], [["Mrs.", "베지 부인은 오목한 손을 식탁 가장자리에서 떼어 대신 무릎에 포개고, 삶은 닭을 향해 곰곰이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["Vesey took her dimpled hands off the edge of the table and crossed them on her lap instead; nodded contemplatively at the boiled chicken, and said, “Yes, dear.”", "“그래요, 그러지요.”"]], [["“Well, but which will you have, to-day?", "“그럼 오늘은 어느 걸 드시겠어요?"], ["Shall Mr. Hartright give you some chicken?", "하트라이트 씨가 닭을 좀 덜어 드릴까요?"], ["or shall I give you some cutlet?”", "아니면 제가 커틀릿을 좀 덜어 드릴까요?”"]], [["Mrs.", "베지 부인은 오목한 손 하나를 다시 식탁 가장자리에 올리고, 졸린 듯 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["Vesey put one of her dimpled hands back again on the edge of the table; hesitated drowsily, and said, “Which you please, dear.”", "“아무거나 좋을 대로요.”"]], [["“Mercy on me!", "“어머나!"], ["it’s a question for your taste, my good lady, not for mine.", "그건 제 취향이 아니라 부인 취향의 문제잖아요."], ["Suppose you have a little of both?", "둘 다 조금씩 드시는 게 어때요?"], ["and suppose you begin with the chicken, because Mr. Hartright looks devoured by anxiety to carve for you.”", "그리고 하트라이트 씨가 부인께 고기를 발라 드리고 싶어 안달이 난 얼굴이니, 닭부터 시작하시고요.”"]], [["Mrs.", "베지 부인은 다른 오목한 손을 다시 식탁 가장자리에 올렸다."], ["Vesey put the other dimpled hand back on the edge of the table; brightened dimly one moment; went out again the next; bowed obediently, and said, “If you please, sir.”", "한순간 흐릿하게 밝아졌다가 다음 순간 다시 사그라들더니, 고분고분 고개를 숙이며 말했다. “그럼 그러지요, 나리.”"]], [["Surely a mild, a compliant, an unutterably tranquil and harmless old lady!", "정녕 온화하고 고분고분하며 이루 말할 수 없이 평온하고 무해한 노부인이렷다!"], ["But enough, perhaps, for the present, of Mrs. Vesey.", "그러나 베지 부인에 대해서는 지금은 이쯤이면 어쩌면 충분하리라."]], [["All this time, there were no signs of Miss Fairlie.", "이 내내 페얼리 양은 나타날 기미가 없었다."], ["We finished our luncheon; and still she never appeared.", "우리는 점심을 다 먹었으나, 여전히 그녀는 나타나지 않았다."], ["Miss Halcombe, whose quick eye nothing escaped, noticed the looks that I cast, from time to time, in the direction of the door.", "무엇 하나 놓치는 법 없는 예리한 눈의 홀콤 양은, 내가 이따금 문 쪽으로 던지는 눈길을 알아챘다."]], [["“I understand you, Mr. Hartright,”", "“무슨 생각이신지 알겠어요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she said; “you are wondering what has become of your other pupil.", "그녀가 말했다. “다른 제자가 어떻게 됐는지 궁금하신 거지요."], ["She has been downstairs, and has got over her headache; but has not sufficiently recovered her appetite to join us at lunch.", "그 애는 아래층에 내려와 두통은 나았지만, 점심에 함께할 만큼 입맛은 아직 돌아오지 않았답니다."], ["If you will put yourself under my charge, I think I can undertake to find her somewhere in the garden.”", "저에게 맡겨 주신다면, 정원 어딘가에서 그 애를 찾아낼 수 있을 것 같아요.”"]], [["She took up a parasol lying on a chair near her, and led the way out, by a long window at the bottom of the room, which opened on to the lawn. It is almost unnecessary to say that we left Mrs.", "그녀는 곁의 의자에 놓인 양산을 집어 들고, 방 아래쪽의 잔디밭으로 이어지는 긴 창문을 통해 앞장서 나갔다."], ["Vesey still seated at the table, with her dimpled hands still crossed on the edge of it; apparently settled in that position for the rest of the afternoon.", "굳이 말할 것도 없이, 우리는 베지 부인을 여전히 식탁에 앉힌 채 남겨 두고 나왔다. 오목한 손은 여전히 식탁 가장자리에 포갠 채로, 오후 내내 그 자세로 눌러앉은 듯 보였다."]], [["As we crossed the lawn, Miss Halcombe looked at me significantly, and shook her head.", "잔디밭을 가로지를 때 홀콤 양이 의미심장하게 나를 바라보며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“That mysterious adventure of yours,”", "“당신의 그 신비로운 겪음은.”"], ["she said, “still remains involved in its own appropriate midnight darkness.", "그녀가 말했다. “아직도 어울리는 그 한밤의 어둠에 싸인 채로군요."], ["I have been all the morning looking over my mother’s letters, and I have made no discoveries yet.", "저는 오전 내내 어머니 편지를 훑어보았지만 아직 아무것도 알아내지 못했어요."], ["However, don’t despair, Mr. Hartright.", "하지만 낙심 마세요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["This is a matter of curiosity; and you have got a woman for your ally.", "이건 호기심의 문제고, 당신에게는 여자를 동맹으로 두었으니까요."], ["Under such conditions success is certain, sooner or later.", "그런 조건이라면 이르든 늦든 성공은 틀림없어요."], ["The letters are not exhausted.", "편지는 아직 다 보지 못했어요."], ["I have three packets still left, and you may confidently rely on my spending the whole evening over them.”", "아직 세 묶음이 남아 있으니, 제가 저녁 내내 그것에 매달리리라는 것만은 믿으셔도 좋아요.”"]], [["Here, then, was one of my anticipations of the morning still unfulfilled.", "그러니, 이렇게 하여 아침에 품은 내 기대 하나가 여전히 이루어지지 않은 셈이었다."], ["I began to wonder, next, whether my introduction to Miss Fairlie would disappoint the expectations that I had been forming of her since breakfast-time.", "나는 그다음으로, 페얼리 양에게 소개되는 일이 아침 식사 때부터 그녀에 대해 품어 온 기대를 저버리게 될지 궁금해지기 시작했다."]], [["“And how did you get on with Mr. Fairlie?”", "“그런데 페얼리 씨와는 어떻게 지내셨어요?”"], ["inquired Miss Halcombe, as we left the lawn and turned into a shrubbery.", "우리가 잔디밭을 벗어나 관목숲으로 접어들 때 홀콤 양이 물었다."], ["“Was he particularly nervous this morning?", "“오늘 아침 유난히 신경이 곤두서 계셨나요?"], ["Never mind considering about your answer, Mr.", "대답을 궁리하실 것 없어요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright. The mere fact of your being obliged to consider is enough for me.", "궁리할 수밖에 없다는 그 사실 하나로 저에게는 충분하니까요."], ["I see in your face that he WAS particularly nervous; and, as I am amiably unwilling to throw you into the same condition, I ask no more.”", "그분이 유난히 신경이 곤두서 계셨다는 걸 당신 얼굴에서 알겠어요. 그리고 저는 당신을 같은 상태로 몰아넣고 싶지 않을 만큼 상냥한 사람이니, 더는 묻지 않겠어요.”"]], [["We turned off into a winding path while she was speaking, and approached a pretty summer-house, built of wood, in the form of a miniature Swiss chalet.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 우리는 굽이진 오솔길로 접어들어, 미니어처 스위스 산장 모양으로 나무를 엮어 지은 어여쁜 정자에 다가갔다."], ["The one room of the summer-house, as we ascended the steps of the door, was occupied by a young lady.", "문의 계단을 오르는데, 정자의 하나뿐인 방에는 한 젊은 숙녀가 있었다."], ["She was standing near a rustic table, looking out at the inland view of moor and hill presented by a gap in the trees, and absently turning over the leaves of a little sketch-book that lay at her side.", "그녀는 소박한 탁자 곁에 서서, 나무 틈으로 펼쳐진 황야와 언덕의 내륙 풍경을 내다보며, 곁에 놓인 작은 사생첩의 책장을 넋 놓고 넘기고 있었다."], ["This was Miss Fairlie.", "이 사람이 페얼리 양이었다."]], [["How can I describe her?", "그녀를 어떻게 묘사할 수 있을까?"], ["How can I separate her from my own sensations, and from all that has happened in the later time?", "그녀를 나 자신의 감정에서, 그리고 뒷날 벌어진 그 모든 일에서 어떻게 떼어 놓을 수 있을까?"], ["How can I see her again as she looked when my eyes first rested on her—as she should look, now, to the eyes that are about to see her in these pages?", "내 눈이 그녀에게 처음 머물렀을 때의 모습 그대로—지금 이 지면에서 그녀를 보려는 눈에 마땅히 비쳐야 할 모습 그대로—그녀를 어떻게 다시 볼 수 있을까?"]], [["The water-colour drawing that I made of Laura Fairlie, at an after period, in the place and attitude in which I first saw her, lies on my desk while I write.", "내가 처음 그녀를 본 그 자리, 그 자세로 뒷날 그려 둔 로라 페얼리의 수채화가, 이 글을 쓰는 동안 내 책상 위에 놓여 있다."], ["I look at it, and there dawns upon me brightly, from the dark greenish-brown background of the summer-house, a light, youthful figure, clothed in a simple muslin dress, the pattern of it formed by broad alternate stripes of delicate blue and white.", "그것을 바라보노라면, 정자의 어두운 초록빛 도는 갈색 배경에서 밝고 앳된 형체 하나가 환하게 떠오른다. 소박한 모슬린 드레스를 입은 그 형체는, 여린 파랑과 흰색이 서로 갈마드는 널따란 줄무늬로 지어진 것이다."], ["A scarf of the same material sits crisply and closely round her shoulders, and a little straw hat of the natural colour, plainly and sparingly trimmed with ribbon to match the gown, covers her head, and throws its soft pearly shadow over the upper part of her face.", "같은 감으로 만든 스카프가 어깨를 산뜻하고 촘촘히 감싸고 있고, 드레스와 어울리게 리본으로 수수하고 검소하게 꾸민 자연색 밀짚모자가 머리를 덮으며, 얼굴 윗부분에 부드러운 진줏빛 그림자를 드리운다."], ["Her hair is of so faint and pale a brown—not flaxen, and yet almost as light; not golden, and yet almost as glossy—that it nearly melts, here and there, into the shadow of the hat.", "그녀의 머리칼은 어찌나 아련하고 옅은 갈색인지—아마빛은 아니면서도 거의 그만큼 밝고, 금빛은 아니면서도 거의 그만큼 윤이 나서—여기저기서 모자의 그림자 속으로 녹아들 듯하다."], ["It is plainly parted and drawn back over her ears, and the line of it ripples naturally as it crosses her forehead.", "그것은 수수하게 가르마를 타 귀 뒤로 넘겨져 있고, 그 선은 이마를 가로지르며 자연스레 물결친다."], ["The eyebrows are rather darker than the hair; and the eyes are of that soft, limpid, turquoise blue, so often sung by the poets, so seldom seen in real life.", "눈썹은 머리칼보다 다소 짙고, 눈은 시인들이 그토록 자주 노래하나 실제 삶에서는 좀처럼 볼 수 없는 그 부드럽고 맑은 터키옥빛 파랑이다."], ["Lovely eyes in colour, lovely eyes in form—large and tender and quietly thoughtful—but beautiful above all things in the clear truthfulness of look that dwells in their inmost depths, and shines through all their changes of expression with the light of a purer and a better world.", "빛깔로도 사랑스럽고 모양으로도 사랑스러운 눈—크고 다정하며 고요히 사려 깊은—그러나 무엇보다도 아름다운 것은, 그 가장 깊은 밑바닥에 깃들어, 표정의 온갖 변화를 뚫고 더 순수하고 더 나은 세계의 빛으로 비쳐 나오는 그 맑은 진실함의 눈빛이었다."], ["The charm—most gently and yet most distinctly expressed—which they shed over the whole face, so covers and transforms its little natural human blemishes elsewhere, that it is difficult to estimate the relative merits and defects of the other features.", "그 눈이 온 얼굴에 뿌리는 매력—더없이 은은하면서도 더없이 또렷이 드러나는—은, 얼굴 다른 곳의 사소한 자연스러운 인간적 흠들을 어찌나 덮고 뒤바꿔 놓는지, 다른 이목구비의 잘잘못을 견주어 가늠하기가 어려울 지경이다."], ["It is hard to see that the lower part of the face is too delicately refined away towards the chin to be in full and fair proportion with the upper part; that the nose, in escaping the aquiline bend (always hard and cruel in a woman, no matter how abstractedly perfect it may be), has erred a little in the other extreme, and has missed the ideal straightness of line; and that the sweet, sensitive lips are subject to a slight nervous contraction, when she smiles, which draws them upward a little at one corner, towards the cheek.", "얼굴 아랫부분이 턱을 향해 너무 여리게 다듬어져 윗부분과 온전히 어여쁜 균형을 이루지 못한다는 것, 코가 매부리 곡선(아무리 그 자체로 완벽하다 한들 여자에게는 언제나 딱딱하고 매정한)을 피하려다 반대쪽 극단으로 조금 치우쳐 이상적인 곧은 선을 놓쳤다는 것, 그리고 그 달콤하고 예민한 입술이 미소 지을 때 살짝 신경질적으로 오므라들어 한쪽 입가가 뺨 쪽으로 조금 당겨 올라간다는 것—이런 것을 알아차리기가 어렵다."], ["It might be possible to note these blemishes in another woman’s face but it is not easy to dwell on them in hers, so subtly are they connected with all that is individual and characteristic in her expression, and so closely does the expression depend for its full play and life, in every other feature, on the moving impulse of the eyes.", "이런 흠들을 다른 여자의 얼굴에서라면 짚어 낼 수 있을지 모르나, 그녀의 얼굴에서는 그것에 눈길을 붙들기가 쉽지 않다. 그것들이 어찌나 은밀하게 그녀의 표정 속 개성적이고 특징적인 온갖 것과 이어져 있는지, 그리고 그 표정이 다른 온갖 이목구비에서 온전히 살아 움직이려면 어찌나 눈의 그 움직이는 충동에 기대는지 모르기 때문이다."]], [["Does my poor portrait of her, my fond, patient labour of long and happy days, show me these things?", "길고 행복한 나날의 정답고 참을성 있는 노고인 나의 이 딱한 초상화가, 나에게 이런 것들을 보여 주는가?"], ["Ah, how few of them are in the dim mechanical drawing, and how many in the mind with which I regard it!", "아, 그 어렴풋한 기계적인 그림 속에는 그것들이 얼마나 적고, 내가 그것을 바라보는 마음속에는 얼마나 많은지!"], ["A fair, delicate girl, in a pretty light dress, trifling with the leaves of a sketch-book, while she looks up from it with truthful, innocent blue eyes—that is all the drawing can say; all, perhaps, that even the deeper reach of thought and pen can say in their language, either.", "살결 곱고 여린 소녀가 어여쁜 밝은 드레스를 입고 사생첩의 책장을 만지작거리다가, 그것에서 눈을 들어 진실하고 순진한 파란 눈으로 올려다본다—그것이 그림이 말할 수 있는 전부다. 어쩌면 생각과 펜의 더 깊은 손길조차 저희 언어로 말할 수 있는 전부인지도 모른다."], ["The woman who first gives life, light, and form to our shadowy conceptions of beauty, fills a void in our spiritual nature that has remained unknown to us till she appeared.", "아름다움에 대한 우리의 어렴풋한 관념에 처음으로 생명과 빛과 형체를 주는 여인은, 그녀가 나타나기 전까지 우리에게 알려지지 않은 채 남아 있던 우리 영혼의 빈자리를 채운다."], ["Sympathies that lie too deep for words, too deep almost for thoughts, are touched, at such times, by other charms than those which the senses feel and which the resources of expression can realise.", "말로 하기엔 너무 깊고, 생각으로 하기에도 거의 너무 깊은 공감이, 그런 순간에는 감각이 느끼고 표현의 수단이 담아낼 수 있는 매력과는 다른 매력에 의해 건드려진다."], ["The mystery which underlies the beauty of women is never raised above the reach of all expression until it has claimed kindred with the deeper mystery in our own souls.", "여인의 아름다움 밑에 깔린 그 신비는, 우리 자신의 영혼 속 더 깊은 신비와 혈연을 맺기 전에는 결코 온갖 표현이 미치는 곳 위로 들려 올라가지 않는다."], ["Then, and then only, has it passed beyond the narrow region on which light falls, in this world, from the pencil and the pen.", "그때, 오직 그때에야, 그것은 이 세상에서 연필과 펜으로부터 빛이 내리비치는 그 좁은 영역 너머로 건너간 것이다."]], [["Think of her as you thought of the first woman who quickened the pulses within you that the rest of her sex had no art to stir.", "그녀를, 당신 안의 그 맥박을 처음으로 뛰게 한 첫 여인을 떠올렸던 것처럼 떠올려 보라—다른 여자들은 그것을 흔들 재주가 없던 그 맥박을."], ["Let the kind, candid blue eyes meet yours, as they met mine, with the one matchless look which we both remember so well.", "그 다정하고 솔직한 파란 눈이, 우리 둘 다 그토록 잘 기억하는 그 둘도 없는 눈빛으로, 나의 눈과 마주쳤듯이 당신의 눈과 마주치게 하라."], ["Let her voice speak the music that you once loved best, attuned as sweetly to your ear as to mine.", "그녀의 목소리가, 당신이 한때 가장 사랑한 그 음악을, 나의 귀에 그러했듯 당신의 귀에도 그토록 감미롭게 가락 맞추어 들려주게 하라."], ["Let her footstep, as she comes and goes, in these pages, be like that other footstep to whose airy fall your own heart once beat time.", "이 지면에서 오가는 그녀의 발소리가, 당신의 심장이 한때 그 사뿐한 낙하에 박자를 맞추던 저 다른 발소리와 같게 하라."], ["Take her as the visionary nursling of your own fancy; and she will grow upon you, all the more clearly, as the living woman who dwells in mine.", "그녀를 당신 자신의 공상이 마음속에 기른 아이로 삼으라. 그러면 그녀는, 내 마음속에 깃든 살아 있는 여인으로서, 더욱더 또렷이 당신 안에서 자라날 것이다."]], [["Among the sensations that crowded on me, when my eyes first looked upon her—familiar sensations which we all know, which spring to life in most of our hearts, die again in so many, and renew their bright existence in so few—there was one that troubled and perplexed me: one that seemed strangely inconsistent and unaccountably out of place in Miss Fairlie’s presence.", "내 눈이 처음 그녀를 바라보았을 때 나에게 몰려든 감정들—우리 모두가 아는, 대부분의 가슴속에서 생겨나 그토록 많은 가슴에서 다시 스러지고 그토록 적은 가슴에서 그 밝은 존재를 되살리는 낯익은 감정들—가운데, 나를 어지럽히고 어리둥절하게 하는 하나가 있었다. 페얼리 양 앞에서 이상하게 어긋나고 까닭 없이 어울리지 않아 보이는 하나가."]], [["Mingling with the vivid impression produced by the charm of her fair face and head, her sweet expression, and her winning simplicity of manner, was another impression, which, in a shadowy way, suggested to me the idea of something wanting.", "살결 고운 얼굴과 머리의 매력, 그 달콤한 표정, 그리고 마음을 끄는 소박한 몸가짐이 자아내는 생생한 인상에 뒤섞여, 어렴풋한 방식으로 무언가 모자란다는 생각을 나에게 넌지시 비추는 또 하나의 인상이 있었다."], ["At one time it seemed like something wanting in HER: at another, like something wanting in myself, which hindered me from understanding her as I ought.", "어느 때는 그녀에게 무언가 모자란 것처럼, 또 어느 때는 나 자신에게 무언가 모자라, 마땅히 그래야 하는 만큼 그녀를 이해하지 못하게 가로막는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["The impression was always strongest in the most contradictory manner, when she looked at me; or, in other words, when I was most conscious of the harmony and charm of her face, and yet, at the same time, most troubled by the sense of an incompleteness which it was impossible to discover.", "그 인상은 언제나 가장 모순된 방식으로 가장 강하게 다가왔다—그녀가 나를 바라볼 때, 다시 말해 내가 그녀 얼굴의 조화와 매력을 가장 뚜렷이 의식하면서도 동시에 도무지 밝혀낼 수 없는 미완의 느낌에 가장 어지러워질 때."], ["Something wanting, something wanting—and where it was, and what it was, I could not say.", "무언가 모자란다, 무언가 모자란다—그것이 어디에 있는지, 그것이 무엇인지, 나는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["The effect of this curious caprice of fancy (as I thought it then) was not of a nature to set me at my ease, during a first interview with Miss Fairlie.", "이 기묘한 공상의 변덕(그때는 그렇게 여겼다)이 미친 영향은, 페얼리 양과의 첫 만남 동안 나를 편안하게 해 주는 종류의 것이 아니었다."], ["The few kind words of welcome which she spoke found me hardly self-possessed enough to thank her in the customary phrases of reply.", "그녀가 건넨 몇 마디 다정한 환영의 말에도, 나는 답례의 판에 박힌 문구로 그녀에게 고마움을 표할 만큼조차 마음을 가누지 못했다."], ["Observing my hesitation, and no doubt attributing it, naturally enough, to some momentary shyness on my part, Miss Halcombe took the business of talking, as easily and readily as usual, into her own hands.", "나의 머뭇거림을 알아채고는, 당연하게도 그것을 내 쪽의 순간적인 수줍음 탓으로 돌렸을 홀콤 양이, 여느 때처럼 편안하고 시원스럽게 말하는 일을 제 손에 떠맡았다."]], [["“Look there, Mr. Hartright,”", "“저기 좀 보세요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she said, pointing to the sketch-book on the table, and to the little delicate wandering hand that was still trifling with it.", "그녀가 탁자 위의 사생첩과, 여전히 그것을 만지작거리는 여리고 자그마한 손을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“Surely you will acknowledge that your model pupil is found at last?", "“드디어 모범 제자를 찾았다는 걸 이젠 인정하시겠지요?"], ["The moment she hears that you are in the house, she seizes her inestimable sketch-book, looks universal Nature straight in the face, and longs to begin!”", "당신이 이 집에 계시다는 말을 듣는 순간, 이 애는 더없이 소중한 사생첩을 움켜쥐고 온 자연을 똑바로 마주 보며 시작하고 싶어 안달이랍니다!”"]], [["Miss Fairlie laughed with a ready good-humour, which broke out as brightly as if it had been part of the sunshine above us, over her lovely face.", "페얼리 양은 시원스러운 명랑함으로 웃었는데, 그 웃음은 마치 우리 머리 위 햇살의 한 조각이기라도 한 양 그 사랑스러운 얼굴에 환하게 번져 나왔다."]], [["“I must not take credit to myself where no credit is due,”", "“칭찬받을 게 없는 데서 스스로 칭찬을 받으면 안 되지요.”"], ["she said, her clear, truthful blue eyes looking alternately at Miss Halcombe and at me.", "그녀가 말했다. 맑고 진실한 파란 눈이 홀콤 양과 나를 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["“Fond as I am of drawing, I am so conscious of my own ignorance that I am more afraid than anxious to begin. Now I know you are here, Mr.", "“그림을 좋아하기는 하지만, 저는 제 무지를 너무나 잘 알아서 시작하고 싶은 마음보다 두려운 마음이 더 커요."], ["Hartright, I find myself looking over my sketches, as I used to look over my lessons when I was a little girl, and when I was sadly afraid that I should turn out not fit to be heard.”", "이제 당신이 여기 계신 걸 알고 나니, 어릴 적 수업을 훑어보던 것처럼 제 사생을 훑어보게 돼요—제가 들려드릴 만한 사람이 못 될까 봐 못 견디게 두려워하던 그 시절처럼요.”"]], [["She made the confession very prettily and simply, and, with quaint, childish earnestness, drew the sketch-book away close to her own side of the table.", "그녀는 아주 어여쁘고 소박하게, 그리고 별스럽게 어린애 같은 진지함으로 그 고백을 하고는, 사생첩을 제 쪽 탁자 가까이로 끌어당겼다."], ["Miss Halcombe cut the knot of the little embarrassment forthwith, in her resolute, downright way.", "홀콤 양은 그 특유의 시원시원하고 딱 부러지는 태도로 이 자그마한 무안함의 매듭을 곧바로 끊어 냈다."]], [["“Good, bad, or indifferent,”", "“잘 그렸든 못 그렸든 그저 그렇든.”"], ["she said, “the pupil’s sketches must pass through the fiery ordeal of the master’s judgment—and there’s an end of it.", "그녀가 말했다. “제자의 사생은 선생의 심판이라는 불의 시련을 거쳐야만 해—그걸로 끝인 거야."], ["Suppose we take them with us in the carriage, Laura, and let Mr. Hartright see them, for the first time, under circumstances of perpetual jolting and interruption?", "그것들을 마차에 가지고 가서, 하트라이트 씨가 끊임없이 덜컹거리고 방해받는 형편에서 처음으로 그것들을 보게 하는 건 어떨까, 로라?"], ["If we can only confuse him all through the drive, between Nature as it is, when he looks up at the view, and Nature as it is not when he looks down again at our sketch-books, we shall drive him into the last desperate refuge of paying us compliments, and shall slip through his professional fingers with our pet feathers of vanity all unruffled.”", "마차를 타고 가는 내내, 그이가 풍경을 올려다볼 때의 있는 그대로의 자연과, 다시 우리 사생첩을 내려다볼 때의 있는 그대로가 아닌 자연 사이에서 그이를 헷갈리게만 할 수 있다면, 우리는 그이를 칭찬을 늘어놓는다는 마지막 절박한 도피처로 몰아넣고, 우리의 소중한 허영의 깃털을 하나도 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채 그이의 전문가다운 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 수 있을 거야.”"]], [["“I hope Mr. Hartright will pay ME no compliments,”", "“하트라이트 씨가 저에게는 아무 칭찬도 하지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Miss Fairlie, as we all left the summer-house.", "우리 모두 정자를 나설 때 페얼리 양이 말했다."]], [["“May I venture to inquire why you express that hope?”", "“어째서 그런 바람을 말씀하시는지 감히 여쭤도 될까요?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Because I shall believe all that you say to me,”", "“당신이 저에게 하시는 말은 다 믿을 테니까요.”"], ["she answered simply.", "그녀가 소박하게 대답했다."]], [["In those few words she unconsciously gave me the key to her whole character: to that generous trust in others which, in her nature, grew innocently out of the sense of her own truth.", "그 몇 마디 말로 그녀는 제 성품 전체의 열쇠를 나에게 저도 모르게 건네주었다—제 천성 속에서 제 진실함의 감각으로부터 순진하게 자라난, 남에 대한 그 너그러운 믿음의 열쇠를."], ["I only knew it intuitively then.", "그때 나는 그저 직관으로 그것을 알았을 뿐이다."], ["I know it by experience now.", "이제 나는 경험으로 그것을 안다."]], [["We merely waited to rouse good Mrs. Vesey from the place which she still occupied at the deserted luncheon-table, before we entered the open carriage for our promised drive.", "우리는 약속한 마차 나들이를 위해 지붕 없는 마차에 오르기 전에, 그저 인기척 없는 점심 식탁의 그 자리를 여전히 차지하고 있는 선량한 베지 부인을 깨우려 잠시 기다렸다."], ["The old lady and Miss Halcombe occupied the back seat, and Miss Fairlie and I sat together in front, with the sketch-book open between us, fairly exhibited at last to my professional eyes.", "노부인과 홀콤 양은 뒷자리에 앉았고, 페얼리 양과 나는 앞자리에 나란히 앉아, 그 사이에 사생첩을 펼쳐 놓았다—드디어 내 전문가의 눈앞에 버젓이 펼쳐진 것이다."], ["All serious criticism on the drawings, even if I had been disposed to volunteer it, was rendered impossible by Miss Halcombe’s lively resolution to see nothing but the ridiculous side of the Fine Arts, as practised by herself, her sister, and ladies in general.", "소묘에 대한 진지한 비평은, 설령 내가 자청해 할 마음이 있었다 한들, 자기 자신과 동생과 여자들 일반이 하는 미술의 우스꽝스러운 면 말고는 아무것도 보지 않겠다는 홀콤 양의 발랄한 결의 탓에 불가능해졌다."], ["I can remember the conversation that passed far more easily than the sketches that I mechanically looked over.", "나는 기계적으로 넘겨 본 사생보다, 오간 대화를 훨씬 더 수월하게 기억한다."], ["That part of the talk, especially, in which Miss Fairlie took any share, is still as vividly impressed on my memory as if I had heard it only a few hours ago.", "그 대화 가운데, 특히 페얼리 양이 얼마간 끼어든 부분은, 마치 겨우 몇 시간 전에 들은 것인 양 여태 내 기억에 생생히 새겨져 있다."]], [["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["let me acknowledge that on this first day I let the charm of her presence lure me from the recollection of myself and my position.", "이 첫날 나는 그녀가 곁에 있는 매력에 홀려 나 자신과 내 처지를 잊게 두었음을 인정하겠다."], ["The most trifling of the questions that she put to me, on the subject of using her pencil and mixing her colours; the slightest alterations of expression in the lovely eyes that looked into mine with such an earnest desire to learn all that I could teach, and to discover all that I could show, attracted more of my attention than the finest view we passed through, or the grandest changes of light and shade, as they flowed into each other over the waving moorland and the level beach.", "연필을 쓰고 물감을 섞는 일에 대해 그녀가 나에게 던진 더없이 사소한 물음들, 내가 가르칠 수 있는 온갖 것을 배우고 내가 보여 줄 수 있는 온갖 것을 알아내려는 그토록 간절한 열망으로 내 눈을 들여다보는 그 사랑스러운 눈의 더없이 미미한 표정 변화가, 우리가 지나친 가장 빼어난 풍경보다도, 물결치는 황야와 평평한 백사장 위로 서로 흘러드는 가장 웅장한 빛과 그늘의 변화보다도 더 많은 내 주의를 끌었다."], ["At any time, and under any circumstances of human interest, is it not strange to see how little real hold the objects of the natural world amid which we live can gain on our hearts and minds?", "어느 때든, 어떤 인간적 관심의 형편에서든, 우리가 그 한가운데 살아가는 자연계의 사물들이 우리의 가슴과 마음을 실로 얼마나 조금밖에 붙들지 못하는지를 보는 것은 기이하지 아니한가?"], ["We go to Nature for comfort in trouble, and sympathy in joy, only in books.", "우리가 괴로울 때 위안을, 기쁠 때 공감을 자연에서 구하는 것은 오직 책 속에서뿐이다."], ["Admiration of those beauties of the inanimate world, which modern poetry so largely and so eloquently describes, is not, even in the best of us, one of the original instincts of our nature.", "근대 시가 그토록 많이, 그토록 유창하게 묘사하는 저 무생물 세계의 아름다움에 대한 감탄은, 우리 가운데 가장 나은 이에게조차 우리 본성의 타고난 본능 가운데 하나가 아니다."], ["As children, we none of us possess it.", "어린 시절 우리 가운데 누구도 그것을 지니지 못한다."], ["No uninstructed man or woman possesses it.", "배우지 못한 남자든 여자든 그것을 지니지 못한다."], ["Those whose lives are most exclusively passed amid the ever-changing wonders of sea and land are also those who are most universally insensible to every aspect of Nature not directly associated with the human interest of their calling.", "바다와 뭍의 끊임없이 변하는 경이 한가운데서 가장 남김없이 삶을 보내는 이들이야말로, 제 생업의 인간적 관심과 직접 결부되지 않은 자연의 온갖 모습에 가장 두루 무감한 이들이기도 하다."], ["Our capacity of appreciating the beauties of the earth we live on is, in truth, one of the civilised accomplishments which we all learn as an Art; and, more, that very capacity is rarely practised by any of us except when our minds are most indolent and most unoccupied.", "우리가 사는 이 땅의 아름다움을 알아보는 우리의 능력은, 실은 우리 모두가 하나의 기예로서 익히는 문명의 소양 가운데 하나다. 게다가 그 능력이란 우리 마음이 가장 나른하고 가장 하는 일 없을 때 말고는 우리 가운데 누구도 좀처럼 부려 쓰지 않는 것이다."], ["How much share have the attractions of Nature ever had in the pleasurable or painful interests and emotions of ourselves or our friends?", "자연의 매력이, 우리 자신이나 우리 벗들의 즐겁거나 괴로운 관심과 감정에서 대체 얼마만 한 몫을 차지한 적이 있는가?"], ["What space do they ever occupy in the thousand little narratives of personal experience which pass every day by word of mouth from one of us to the other?", "그것들이, 날마다 우리 가운데 하나에게서 다른 하나에게로 입말로 오가는 그 숱한 자잘한 개인적 경험담에서 대체 얼마만 한 자리를 차지하는가?"], ["All that our minds can compass, all that our hearts can learn, can be accomplished with equal certainty, equal profit, and equal satisfaction to ourselves, in the poorest as in the richest prospect that the face of the earth can show.", "우리 마음이 헤아릴 수 있는 온갖 것, 우리 가슴이 배울 수 있는 온갖 것은, 지구의 낯이 보여 줄 수 있는 가장 초라한 경치에서도 가장 풍요로운 경치에서와 똑같은 확실함, 똑같은 이로움, 똑같은 흡족함으로 이루어질 수 있다."], ["There is surely a reason for this want of inborn sympathy between the creature and the creation around it, a reason which may perhaps be found in the widely-differing destinies of man and his earthly sphere.", "피조물과 그를 둘러싼 창조물 사이의 이 타고난 공감의 부재에는 분명 까닭이 있으니, 그것은 어쩌면 인간과 그의 지상 영역의 사뭇 다른 운명에서 찾을 수 있을지 모른다."], ["The grandest mountain prospect that the eye can range over is appointed to annihilation.", "눈이 두루 훑을 수 있는 가장 웅장한 산의 경치는 소멸로 정해져 있다."], ["The smallest human interest that the pure heart can feel is appointed to immortality.", "순수한 가슴이 느낄 수 있는 가장 작은 인간적 관심은 불멸로 정해져 있다."]], [["We had been out nearly three hours, when the carriage again passed through the gates of Limmeridge House.", "우리가 나온 지 거의 세 시간이 되어서야 마차가 다시 리머리지 하우스의 문을 지났다."]], [["On our way back I had let the ladies settle for themselves the first point of view which they were to sketch, under my instructions, on the afternoon of the next day.", "돌아오는 길에 나는 이튿날 오후 내 지도 아래 그릴 첫 번째 풍경 지점을 숙녀들이 스스로 정하게 두었다."], ["When they withdrew to dress for dinner, and when I was alone again in my little sitting-room, my spirits seemed to leave me on a sudden.", "그들이 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입으러 물러가고, 내가 다시 작은 거실에 혼자 남았을 때, 문득 내 기운이 나를 떠나는 듯했다."], ["I felt ill at ease and dissatisfied with myself, I hardly knew why.", "나는 마음이 불편하고 나 자신에게 못마땅했다—왜인지 나도 잘 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps I was now conscious for the first time of having enjoyed our drive too much in the character of a guest, and too little in the character of a drawing-master.", "어쩌면 나는 이제 처음으로, 우리의 마차 나들이를 그림 선생의 자격으로는 너무 적게, 손님의 자격으로는 너무 많이 즐겼음을 의식한 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps that strange sense of something wanting, either in Miss Fairlie or in myself, which had perplexed me when I was first introduced to her, haunted me still.", "어쩌면 처음 그녀에게 소개될 때 나를 어리둥절하게 한, 페얼리 양에게든 나 자신에게든 무언가 모자란다는 그 기이한 느낌이 여전히 나를 따라다닌 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["Anyhow, it was a relief to my spirits when the dinner-hour called me out of my solitude, and took me back to the society of the ladies of the house.", "어쨌거나, 저녁 식사 시간이 나를 고독에서 불러내어 저택 숙녀들의 벗으로 도로 데려갔을 때 내 기운은 오히려 놓였다."]], [["I was struck, on entering the drawing-room, by the curious contrast, rather in material than in colour, of the dresses which they now wore.", "응접실에 들어서면서, 나는 그들이 이제 입은 드레스의, 빛깔이라기보다 감의 기묘한 대비에 마음을 빼앗겼다."], ["While Mrs. Vesey and Miss Halcombe were richly clad (each in the manner most becoming to her age), the first in silver-grey, and the second in that delicate primrose-yellow colour which matches so well with a dark complexion and black hair, Miss Fairlie was unpretendingly and almost poorly dressed in plain white muslin.", "베지 부인과 홀콤 양은 저마다 (제 나이에 가장 어울리는 방식으로) 값진 옷을 입었으니, 앞의 이는 은회색으로, 뒤의 이는 가무잡잡한 낯빛과 검은 머리에 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 여린 앵초빛 노랑으로 입었건만, 페얼리 양은 소박한 흰 모슬린으로 꾸밈없이, 거의 초라하다 싶게 차려입고 있었다."], ["It was spotlessly pure: it was beautifully put on; but still it was the sort of dress which the wife or daughter of a poor man might have worn, and it made her, so far as externals went, look less affluent in circumstances than her own governess.", "그것은 티끌 하나 없이 깨끗했고 아름답게 걸쳐져 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것은 가난한 이의 아내나 딸이 입었을 법한 종류의 드레스였고, 겉모습으로 보자면 그녀를 제 가정교사보다도 형편이 덜 넉넉해 보이게 했다."], ["At a later period, when I learnt to know more of Miss Fairlie’s character, I discovered that this curious contrast, on the wrong side, was due to her natural delicacy of feeling and natural intensity of aversion to the slightest personal display of her own wealth. Neither Mrs.", "뒷날 페얼리 양의 성품을 더 알게 되었을 때, 나는 이 잘못된 쪽으로의 기묘한 대비가, 그녀의 타고난 여린 감수성과 제 부(富)를 조금이라도 몸에 드러내는 것에 대한 타고난 격렬한 혐오에서 비롯된 것임을 알게 되었다."], ["Vesey nor Miss Halcombe could ever induce her to let the advantage in dress desert the two ladies who were poor, to lean to the side of the one lady who was rich.", "베지 부인도 홀콤 양도, 옷차림의 이점이 가난한 두 숙녀를 저버리고 부유한 한 숙녀 쪽으로 기울게끔 그녀를 결코 구슬리지 못했다."]], [["When the dinner was over we returned together to the drawing-room.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자 우리는 함께 응접실로 돌아왔다."], ["Although Mr. Fairlie (emulating the magnificent condescension of the monarch who had picked up Titian’s brush for him) had instructed his butler to consult my wishes in relation to the wine that I might prefer after dinner, I was resolute enough to resist the temptation of sitting in solitary grandeur among bottles of my own choosing, and sensible enough to ask the ladies’ permission to leave the table with them habitually, on the civilised foreign plan, during the period of my residence at Limmeridge House.", "페얼리 씨는 (자기를 위해 티치아노의 붓을 주워 준 그 군주의 장엄한 겸양을 흉내 내어) 만찬 뒤 내가 즐길 만한 포도주에 관해 내 뜻을 물으라고 집사에게 일러 두었으나, 나는 내가 고른 술병들 사이에 홀로 거창하게 앉아 있으려는 유혹을 물리칠 만큼은 단호했고, 리머리지 하우스에 머무는 동안 문명화된 외국식으로 늘 숙녀들과 함께 식탁을 떠나게 해 달라고 청할 만큼은 분별이 있었다."]], [["The drawing-room, to which we had now withdrawn for the rest of the evening, was on the ground-floor, and was of the same shape and size as the breakfast-room.", "저녁의 남은 시간을 보내려고 이제 물러간 그 응접실은 일 층에 있었고, 아침 식사실과 같은 모양과 크기였다."], ["Large glass doors at the lower end opened on to a terrace, beautifully ornamented along its whole length with a profusion of flowers.", "아래쪽 끝의 커다란 유리문은, 온통 흐드러진 꽃으로 그 전체를 아름답게 꾸민 테라스로 열려 있었다."], ["The soft, hazy twilight was just shading leaf and blossom alike into harmony with its own sober hues as we entered the room, and the sweet evening scent of the flowers met us with its fragrant welcome through the open glass doors.", "우리가 방에 들어설 때, 부드럽고 뿌연 황혼이 마침 나뭇잎과 꽃송이를 하나같이 제 차분한 빛깔과 어우러지게 물들이고 있었고, 열린 유리문 너머로 꽃들의 달콤한 저녁 내음이 향긋한 환영으로 우리를 맞았다."], ["Good Mrs. Vesey (always the first of the party to sit down) took possession of an arm-chair in a corner, and dozed off comfortably to sleep.", "선량한 베지 부인은 (언제나 일행 가운데 가장 먼저 앉는 사람인지라) 구석의 안락의자를 차지하고는 편안히 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다 잠들었다."], ["At my request Miss Fairlie placed herself at the piano.", "내 청에 따라 페얼리 양이 피아노 앞에 앉았다."], ["As I followed her to a seat near the instrument, I saw Miss Halcombe retire into a recess of one of the side windows, to proceed with the search through her mother’s letters by the last quiet rays of the evening light.", "그 악기 곁의 자리로 그녀를 뒤따르는데, 홀콤 양이 옆 창문 하나의 우묵한 자리로 물러나, 사그라드는 저녁 빛의 마지막 고요한 빛살로 어머니 편지를 마저 뒤져 나가는 것이 보였다."]], [["How vividly that peaceful home-picture of the drawing-room comes back to me while I write!", "이 글을 쓰는 동안 그 평온한 응접실의 가정 풍경이 얼마나 생생히 되살아나는지!"], ["From the place where I sat I could see Miss Halcombe’s graceful figure, half of it in soft light, half in mysterious shadow, bending intently over the letters in her lap; while, nearer to me, the fair profile of the player at the piano was just delicately defined against the faintly-deepening background of the inner wall of the room.", "내가 앉은 자리에서 나는 홀콤 양의 우아한 형체를 볼 수 있었으니, 반은 부드러운 빛 속에, 반은 신비로운 그늘 속에 잠긴 채 무릎 위 편지에 골똘히 몸을 숙이고 있었다. 그런가 하면 나에게 더 가까이에서는, 피아노 연주자의 고운 옆얼굴이 방 안쪽 벽의 아스라이 짙어 가는 배경을 등지고 여리게 윤곽 지어져 있었다."], ["Outside, on the terrace, the clustering flowers and long grasses and creepers waved so gently in the light evening air, that the sound of their rustling never reached us.", "바깥 테라스에서는 다닥다닥 모인 꽃과 긴 풀과 덩굴이 가벼운 저녁 공기에 어찌나 살랑살랑 흔들리는지, 그 사각이는 소리가 우리에게까지 결코 닿지 않았다."], ["The sky was without a cloud, and the dawning mystery of moonlight began to tremble already in the region of the eastern heaven.", "하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었고, 동쪽 하늘 어름에서는 달빛의 새벽 같은 신비가 벌써 떨리기 시작했다."], ["The sense of peace and seclusion soothed all thought and feeling into a rapt, unearthly repose; and the balmy quiet, that deepened ever with the deepening light, seemed to hover over us with a gentler influence still, when there stole upon it from the piano the heavenly tenderness of the music of Mozart.", "평화와 은둔의 느낌이 온갖 생각과 감정을 넋 잃은, 세속을 벗어난 안식으로 달래 놓았다. 그리고 짙어 가는 빛과 더불어 더욱 짙어 가던 그 향긋한 고요는, 피아노에서 모차르트 음악의 천상의 다정함이 그 위로 살며시 스며들 때, 한층 더 부드러운 힘으로 우리 위를 맴도는 듯했다."], ["It was an evening of sights and sounds never to forget.", "그것은 결코 잊지 못할 광경과 소리의 저녁이었다."]], [["We all sat silent in the places we had chosen—Mrs. Vesey still sleeping, Miss Fairlie still playing, Miss Halcombe still reading—till the light failed us.", "우리는 모두 저마다 고른 자리에 잠자코 앉아 있었다—베지 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있고, 페얼리 양은 여전히 연주하고, 홀콤 양은 여전히 읽으며—빛이 우리를 저버릴 때까지."], ["By this time the moon had stolen round to the terrace, and soft, mysterious rays of light were slanting already across the lower end of the room.", "이 무렵 달은 어느새 테라스 쪽으로 돌아와 있었고, 부드럽고 신비로운 빛살이 벌써 방 아래쪽 끝을 비스듬히 가로지르고 있었다."], ["The change from the twilight obscurity was so beautiful that we banished the lamps, by common consent, when the servant brought them in, and kept the large room unlighted, except by the glimmer of the two candles at the piano.", "황혼의 어둠에서 바뀐 그 광경이 어찌나 아름다웠던지, 하인이 등불을 들여왔을 때 우리는 뜻을 모아 그것을 물리고, 피아노 위 두 자루 촛불의 희미한 빛 말고는 그 큰 방을 불 밝히지 않은 채로 두었다."]], [["For half an hour more the music still went on.", "반 시간 남짓 음악이 여전히 이어졌다."], ["After that the beauty of the moonlight view on the terrace tempted Miss Fairlie out to look at it, and I followed her.", "그 뒤 테라스에 비친 달빛 풍경의 아름다움이 페얼리 양을 그것을 보러 밖으로 이끌었고, 나는 그녀를 뒤따랐다."], ["When the candles at the piano had been lighted Miss Halcombe had changed her place, so as to continue her examination of the letters by their assistance.", "피아노 위 촛불이 밝혀졌을 때 홀콤 양은 그 빛의 도움으로 편지를 계속 살피려고 자리를 옮긴 참이었다."], ["We left her, on a low chair, at one side of the instrument, so absorbed over her reading that she did not seem to notice when we moved.", "우리는 그녀를, 악기 한쪽 곁의 낮은 의자에 앉힌 채로 두고 나왔는데, 그녀는 읽기에 어찌나 빠져 있는지 우리가 자리를 옮기는 것도 알아채지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["We had been out on the terrace together, just in front of the glass doors, hardly so long as five minutes, I should think; and Miss Fairlie was, by my advice, just tying her white handkerchief over her head as a precaution against the night air—when I heard Miss Halcombe’s voice—low, eager, and altered from its natural lively tone—pronounce my name.", "내 짐작으로 우리가 유리문 바로 앞 테라스에 함께 나와 있은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았을 때였다. 그리고 페얼리 양은 내 권유에 따라 밤공기에 대비해 흰 손수건을 머리에 막 두르고 있었는데—그때 나는 홀콤 양의 목소리를 들었다—낮고 다급하며 그 타고난 발랄한 어조와는 달라진 목소리로 내 이름을 부르는 것을."]], [["“Mr. Hartright,”", "“하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she said, “will you come here for a minute?", "그녀가 말했다. “잠깐 이리 와 주시겠어요?"], ["I want to speak to you.”", "당신께 할 말이 있어요.”"]], [["I entered the room again immediately.", "나는 곧바로 다시 방에 들어섰다."], ["The piano stood about half-way down along the inner wall.", "피아노는 안쪽 벽을 따라 절반쯤 되는 자리에 놓여 있었다."], ["On the side of the instrument farthest from the terrace Miss Halcombe was sitting with the letters scattered on her lap, and with one in her hand selected from them, and held close to the candle.", "테라스에서 가장 먼 쪽 악기 곁에 홀콤 양이 앉아 있었는데, 편지를 무릎에 흩뜨려 놓고, 그 가운데 하나를 골라 손에 든 채 촛불 가까이 대고 있었다."], ["On the side nearest to the terrace there stood a low ottoman, on which I took my place.", "테라스에 가장 가까운 쪽에는 낮은 긴 의자가 놓여 있었고, 나는 거기에 자리 잡았다."], ["In this position I was not far from the glass doors, and I could see Miss Fairlie plainly, as she passed and repassed the opening on to the terrace, walking slowly from end to end of it in the full radiance of the moon.", "이 자리에서 나는 유리문에서 멀지 않아, 페얼리 양이 달빛 가득한 속에서 테라스를 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 천천히 거닐며 그 트인 문 앞을 오가는 것을 또렷이 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“I want you to listen while I read the concluding passages in this letter,”", "“이 편지의 마지막 대목을 제가 읽는 동안 들어 주셨으면 해요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["“Tell me if you think they throw any light upon your strange adventure on the road to London. The letter is addressed by my mother to her second husband, Mr.", "“그것이 런던으로 가는 길에서의 당신의 기이한 겪음에 무슨 빛이라도 던져 주는지 말씀해 주세요."], ["Fairlie, and the date refers to a period of between eleven and twelve years since. At that time Mr.", "이 편지는 제 어머니가 두 번째 남편 페얼리 씨 앞으로 부친 것이고, 날짜로 보아 열한 해에서 열두 해쯤 전을 가리켜요."], ["and Mrs. Fairlie, and my half-sister Laura, had been living for years in this house; and I was away from them completing my education at a school in Paris.”", "그 무렵 페얼리 씨 부부와 제 이복동생 로라는 여러 해째 이 집에 살고 있었고, 저는 파리의 학교에서 학업을 마치느라 그들에게서 떨어져 있었지요.”"]], [["She looked and spoke earnestly, and, as I thought, a little uneasily as well.", "그녀는 진지하게, 그리고 내가 보기엔 조금 불안한 기색으로도 바라보고 말했다."], ["At the moment when she raised the letter to the candle before beginning to read it, Miss Fairlie passed us on the terrace, looked in for a moment, and seeing that we were engaged, slowly walked on.", "편지를 읽기 시작하기에 앞서 그것을 촛불 앞으로 들어 올리는 순간, 페얼리 양이 테라스에서 우리 곁을 지나며 잠시 안을 들여다보다가, 우리가 무언가에 몰두한 것을 보고는 천천히 걸어갔다."]], [["Miss Halcombe began to read as follows:—", "홀콤 양은 다음과 같이 읽기 시작했다—"]], [["“‘You will be tired, my dear Philip, of hearing perpetually about my schools and my scholars.", "“‘사랑하는 필립, 당신은 제 학교와 제 학생들 이야기를 노상 듣느라 지겨우시겠지요."], ["Lay the blame, pray, on the dull uniformity of life at Limmeridge, and not on me.", "제발 그 탓을 저에게 말고 리머리지 생활의 그 따분한 한결같음에 돌려 주세요."], ["Besides, this time I have something really interesting to tell you about a new scholar.", "게다가 이번에는 새 학생에 대해 정말 흥미로운 이야깃거리가 있답니다."]], [["“‘You know old Mrs. Kempe at the village shop.", "“‘마을 가게의 늙은 켐프 부인을 아시지요."], ["Well, after years of ailing, the doctor has at last given her up, and she is dying slowly day by day.", "그런데 그이가 여러 해 앓은 끝에 의사가 마침내 손을 놓았고, 날마다 조금씩 죽어 가고 있어요. 하나뿐인 살아 있는 혈육인 여동생이 지난주에 그이를 돌보러 왔지요."], ["Her only living relation, a sister, arrived last week to take care of her.", "이 여동생은 멀리 햄프셔에서 온 사람인데—이름은 캐서릭 부인이에요."], ["This sister comes all the way from Hampshire—her name is Mrs. Catherick.", "나흘 전 캐서릭 부인이 저를 보러 이곳에 왔는데, 하나뿐인 아이를 데리고 왔어요."], ["Four days ago Mrs. Catherick came here to see me, and brought her only child with her, a sweet little girl about a year older than our darling Laura——’”", "우리 사랑스러운 로라보다 한 살쯤 위인 어여쁜 어린 소녀랍니다——’”"]], [["As the last sentence fell from the reader’s lips, Miss Fairlie passed us on the terrace once more.", "읽는 이의 입술에서 그 마지막 문장이 떨어질 때, 페얼리 양이 다시 한번 테라스에서 우리 곁을 지났다."], ["She was softly singing to herself one of the melodies which she had been playing earlier in the evening.", "그녀는 저녁 일찍 연주하던 가락 가운데 하나를 나직이 혼자 흥얼거리고 있었다."], ["Miss Halcombe waited till she had passed out of sight again, and then went on with the letter—", "홀콤 양은 그녀가 다시 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 편지를 이어 갔다—"]], [["“‘Mrs. Catherick is a decent, well-behaved, respectable woman; middle-aged, and with the remains of having been moderately, only moderately, nice-looking.", "“‘캐서릭 부인은 점잖고 몸가짐 바르며 버젓한 여인이에요."], ["There is something in her manner and in her appearance, however, which I can’t make out.", "중년이고, 그럭저럭, 그저 그럭저럭 반반했던 흔적이 남아 있지요."], ["She is reserved about herself to the point of downright secrecy, and there is a look in her face—I can’t describe it—which suggests to me that she has something on her mind.", "하지만 그 몸가짐과 생김새에는 제가 도무지 알 수 없는 무언가가 있어요. 그이는 제 이야기에 대해서는 아주 대놓고 비밀스럽다 할 만큼 말을 아끼고, 그 얼굴에는—뭐라 묘사할 수 없는—무언가 마음에 걸리는 것이 있음을 저에게 넌지시 비치는 표정이 있어요."], ["She is altogether what you would call a walking mystery. Her errand at Limmeridge House, however, was simple enough.", "그이는 온통, 당신이 걸어 다니는 수수께끼라 부를 만한 사람이에요."], ["When she left Hampshire to nurse her sister, Mrs.", "하지만 그이가 리머리지 하우스에 온 볼일은 아주 간단했어요."], ["Kempe, through her last illness, she had been obliged to bring her daughter with her, through having no one at home to take care of the little girl. Mrs.", "언니 켐프 부인의 마지막 병간호를 하러 햄프셔를 떠날 때, 집에 그 어린 소녀를 돌봐 줄 사람이 없어 딸을 데려올 수밖에 없었대요."], ["Kempe may die in a week’s time, or may linger on for months; and Mrs. Catherick’s object was to ask me to let her daughter, Anne, have the benefit of attending my school, subject to the condition of her being removed from it to go home again with her mother, after Mrs.", "켐프 부인은 일주일 안에 죽을 수도 있고 몇 달을 끌 수도 있는데, 캐서릭 부인의 볼일은, 켐프 부인이 죽은 뒤 그 애를 학교에서 데리고 나가 어머니와 함께 다시 집으로 간다는 조건 아래, 딸 앤이 제 학교에 다니는 혜택을 누리게 해 달라는 것이었어요."], ["Kempe’s death. I consented at once, and when Laura and I went out for our walk, we took the little girl (who is just eleven years old) to the school that very day.’”", "저는 즉시 승낙했고, 로라와 제가 산책을 나갈 때 바로 그날 그 어린 소녀(이제 막 열한 살이에요)를 학교에 데려다주었지요.’”"]], [["Once more Miss Fairlie’s figure, bright and soft in its snowy muslin dress—her face prettily framed by the white folds of the handkerchief which she had tied under her chin—passed by us in the moonlight.", "또 한 번 페얼리 양의 형체가—눈 같은 모슬린 드레스 속에서 밝고 부드럽게, 턱 아래로 묶은 손수건의 흰 주름이 얼굴을 어여쁘게 감싼 채로—달빛 속에서 우리 곁을 지났다."], ["Once more Miss Halcombe waited till she was out of sight, and then went on—", "또 한 번 홀콤 양은 그녀가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 기다렸다가 이어 갔다—"]], [["“‘I have taken a violent fancy, Philip, to my new scholar, for a reason which I mean to keep till the last for the sake of surprising you.", "“‘필립, 저는 제 새 학생에게 격한 애정을 품게 됐어요. 그 까닭은 당신을 놀래 드리려고 맨 마지막까지 아껴 두려 해요."], ["Her mother having told me as little about the child as she told me of herself, I was left to discover (which I did on the first day when we tried her at lessons) that the poor little thing’s intellect is not developed as it ought to be at her age.", "그 애 어머니가 저에게 제 이야기를 거의 안 한 것만큼이나 그 애에 대해서도 거의 말해 주지 않아서, 저는 (수업으로 그 애를 시험해 본 첫날 알아냈지요) 그 가엾은 어린것의 지력이 제 나이에 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 자라 있지 않다는 것을 스스로 알아낼 수밖에 없었어요."], ["Seeing this I had her up to the house the next day, and privately arranged with the doctor to come and watch her and question her, and tell me what he thought.", "이걸 알고 이튿날 그 애를 집으로 불러, 의사에게 몰래 부탁해 와서 그 애를 지켜보고 물어보게 하고는 그 소견을 저에게 말해 달라 했지요."], ["His opinion is that she will grow out of it.", "의사 소견은 그 애가 자라면서 그것을 벗어나리라는 거예요."], ["But he says her careful bringing-up at school is a matter of great importance just now, because her unusual slowness in acquiring ideas implies an unusual tenacity in keeping them, when they are once received into her mind.", "하지만 학교에서 그 애를 잘 길러 주는 일이 지금으로선 대단히 중요하다고 해요. 관념을 받아들이는 데 유난히 더딘 것은, 그 관념이 일단 마음에 들어오면 유난히 끈덕지게 붙들고 있음을 뜻하기 때문이래요."], ["Now, my love, you must not imagine, in your off-hand way, that I have been attaching myself to an idiot.", "자, 여보, 당신의 그 대충대충 하는 버릇으로 제가 백치에게 정을 붙였다고 상상하시면 안 돼요."], ["This poor little Anne Catherick is a sweet, affectionate, grateful girl, and says the quaintest, prettiest things (as you shall judge by an instance), in the most oddly sudden, surprised, half-frightened way.", "이 가엾은 어린 앤 캐서릭은 다정하고 정 많고 고마움을 아는 소녀예요. 그리고 (한 가지 예로 당신이 판단해 보시도록) 더없이 별스럽게 불쑥, 놀란 듯, 반쯤 겁먹은 투로 더없이 별스럽고 어여쁜 말을 한답니다."], ["Although she is dressed very neatly, her clothes show a sad want of taste in colour and pattern.", "옷차림은 아주 말끔한데도, 그 옷은 빛깔과 무늬에서 안목이 딱하게도 모자라요."], ["So I arranged, yesterday, that some of our darling Laura’s old white frocks and white hats should be altered for Anne Catherick, explaining to her that little girls of her complexion looked neater and better all in white than in anything else.", "그래서 어제 저는 우리 사랑스러운 로라의 낡은 흰 원피스와 흰 모자 몇 벌을 앤 캐서릭에게 맞게 고쳐 주기로 했어요. 그 애 낯빛의 어린 소녀는 다른 무엇을 입는 것보다 온통 흰옷을 입는 편이 더 말끔하고 더 보기 좋다고 그 애에게 일러 주면서요."], ["She hesitated and seemed puzzled for a minute, then flushed up, and appeared to understand.", "그 애는 잠깐 머뭇거리며 어리둥절해 보이더니, 이내 얼굴을 붉히며 알아들은 듯했어요."], ["Her little hand clasped mine suddenly.", "그 작은 손이 별안간 제 손을 꼭 잡았지요."], ["She kissed it, Philip, and said (oh, so earnestly!), “I will always wear white as long as I live.", "그 애는 제 손에 입 맞추며 (오, 어찌나 간절하게!) 말했어요. “저는 살아 있는 한 언제까지나 흰옷을 입겠어요."], ["It will help me to remember you, ma’am, and to think that I am pleasing you still, when I go away and see you no more.”", "그러면 제가 떠나가 마님을 더는 뵙지 못하게 되어도, 마님을 기억하고 제가 아직도 마님을 기쁘게 해 드린다고 생각하는 데 도움이 될 거예요.”"], ["This is only one specimen of the quaint things she says so prettily.", "이건 그 애가 그토록 어여쁘게 하는 별스러운 말들의 한 가지 본보기일 뿐이에요."], ["Poor little soul!", "가엾은 어린것!"], ["She shall have a stock of white frocks, made with good deep tucks, to let out for her as she grows——’”", "그 애가 자라면서 늘려 입을 수 있도록 넉넉히 깊게 단을 접은 흰 원피스를 잔뜩 마련해 주려고 해요——’”"]], [["Miss Halcombe paused, and looked at me across the piano.", "홀콤 양은 잠시 멈추고 피아노 너머로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Did the forlorn woman whom you met in the high-road seem young?”", "“큰길에서 만난 그 의지가지없는 여인이 젊어 보였나요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["“Young enough to be two- or three-and-twenty?”", "“스물둘이나 스물셋쯤 될 만큼 젊었어요?”"]], [["“Yes, Miss Halcombe, as young as that.”", "“네, 홀콤 양, 그만큼 젊었습니다.”"]], [["“And she was strangely dressed, from head to foot, all in white?”", "“그리고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 온통 흰옷으로, 기이하게 차려입고 있었고요?”"]], [["“All in white.”", "“온통 흰옷이었습니다.”"]], [["While the answer was passing my lips Miss Fairlie glided into view on the terrace for the third time.", "그 대답이 내 입에서 새어 나오는 동안, 페얼리 양이 세 번째로 테라스에 미끄러지듯 나타났다."], ["Instead of proceeding on her walk, she stopped, with her back turned towards us, and, leaning on the balustrade of the terrace, looked down into the garden beyond.", "산책을 계속하는 대신, 그녀는 우리를 등진 채 멈춰 서서 테라스 난간에 기대어 그 너머 정원을 내려다보았다."], ["My eyes fixed upon the white gleam of her muslin gown and head-dress in the moonlight, and a sensation, for which I can find no name—a sensation that quickened my pulse, and raised a fluttering at my heart—began to steal over me.", "내 눈은 달빛 속 그녀의 모슬린 드레스와 머리쓰개의 흰 반짝임에 고정되었고, 이름을 붙일 수 없는 어떤 감각—내 맥박을 빠르게 하고 내 가슴에 설렘을 일으키는 감각—이 나를 슬며시 사로잡기 시작했다."]], [["“All in white?” Miss Halcombe repeated.", "“온통 흰옷이었다고요?”"], ["“The most important sentences in the letter, Mr.", "홀콤 양이 되뇌었다."], ["Hartright, are those at the end, which I will read to you immediately.", "“하트라이트 씨, 이 편지에서 가장 중요한 문장들은 끝부분에 있어요."], ["But I can’t help dwelling a little upon the coincidence of the white costume of the woman you met, and the white frocks which produced that strange answer from my mother’s little scholar.", "그건 곧바로 읽어 드릴게요. 하지만 당신이 만난 여인의 흰옷 차림과, 제 어머니의 어린 학생에게서 그 기이한 대답을 이끌어 낸 흰 원피스의 이 우연의 일치에 잠시 마음이 머무는 걸 어쩔 수가 없네요."], ["The doctor may have been wrong when he discovered the child’s defects of intellect, and predicted that she would ‘grow out of them.’ She may never have grown out of them, and the old grateful fancy about dressing in white, which was a serious feeling to the girl, may be a serious feeling to the woman still.”", "그 아이의 지력의 결함을 알아내고 그 애가 그것을 ‘자라면서 벗어나리라’ 예언했을 때, 의사가 틀렸을 수도 있어요. 그 애는 결코 그것을 벗어나지 못했을 수도 있고, 그 소녀에게 진지한 감정이었던 흰옷을 입는다는 그 옛 고마운 소망이, 그 여인에게도 여전히 진지한 감정일지 몰라요.”"]], [["I said a few words in answer—I hardly know what.", "나는 몇 마디 대꾸했다—무슨 말이었는지 나도 잘 모르겠다."], ["All my attention was concentrated on the white gleam of Miss Fairlie’s muslin dress.", "내 온 주의는 페얼리 양의 모슬린 드레스의 흰 반짝임에 쏠려 있었다."]], [["“Listen to the last sentences of the letter,”", "“이 편지의 마지막 문장들을 들어 보세요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["“I think they will surprise you.”", "“당신을 놀라게 하리라 봐요.”"]], [["As she raised the letter to the light of the candle, Miss Fairlie turned from the balustrade, looked doubtfully up and down the terrace, advanced a step towards the glass doors, and then stopped, facing us.", "그녀가 편지를 촛불 빛으로 들어 올리자, 페얼리 양이 난간에서 돌아서 미심쩍은 듯 테라스 위아래를 살피더니, 유리문 쪽으로 한 걸음 다가서다 멈춰 서서 우리를 마주 보았다."]], [["Meanwhile Miss Halcombe read me the last sentences to which she had referred—", "그러는 동안 홀콤 양은 앞서 말한 마지막 문장들을 나에게 읽어 주었다—"]], [["“‘And now, my love, seeing that I am at the end of my paper, now for the real reason, the surprising reason, for my fondness for little Anne Catherick.", "“‘그리고 이제, 여보, 종이가 다 되었으니, 이제 어린 앤 캐서릭에 대한 제 애정의 진짜 까닭, 그 놀라운 까닭을 말할게요."], ["My dear Philip, although she is not half so pretty, she is, nevertheless, by one of those extraordinary caprices of accidental resemblance which one sometimes sees, the living likeness, in her hair, her complexion, the colour of her eyes, and the shape of her face——’”", "사랑하는 필립, 그 애는 절반만큼도 어여쁘지 않지만, 그럼에도 이따금 볼 수 있는 그 우연한 닮음의 유별난 변덕 가운데 하나로, 머리칼과 낯빛과 눈 빛깔과 얼굴 모양에서, 살아 있는 판박이랍니다——’”"]], [["I started up from the ottoman before Miss Halcombe could pronounce the next words.", "홀콤 양이 다음 말을 채 발음하기도 전에 나는 긴 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["A thrill of the same feeling which ran through me when the touch was laid upon my shoulder on the lonely high-road chilled me again.", "외로운 큰길에서 내 어깨에 손이 얹혔을 때 내 온몸을 훑고 지나간 그 똑같은 느낌의 전율이 다시 나를 오싹하게 했다."]], [["There stood Miss Fairlie, a white figure, alone in the moonlight; in her attitude, in the turn of her head, in her complexion, in the shape of her face, the living image, at that distance and under those circumstances, of the woman in white!", "거기 페얼리 양이 서 있었다, 달빛 속에 홀로 흰 형체로. 그 자세로, 그 고갯짓으로, 그 낯빛으로, 그 얼굴 모양으로, 그 거리에서 그런 형편 아래, 흰옷을 입은 여인의 살아 있는 판박이로!"], ["The doubt which had troubled my mind for hours and hours past flashed into conviction in an instant.", "여러 시간 동안 내 마음을 어지럽히던 그 의문이 순식간에 확신으로 번뜩였다."], ["That “something wanting” was my own recognition of the ominous likeness between the fugitive from the asylum and my pupil at Limmeridge House.", "그 “무언가 모자란다”던 것은, 정신병원에서 달아난 도망자와 리머리지 하우스의 내 제자 사이의 그 불길한 닮음을 나 스스로 알아본 것이었다."]], [["“You see it!”", "“당신도 보이는군요!”"], ["said Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["She dropped the useless letter, and her eyes flashed as they met mine.", "그녀는 소용없어진 편지를 떨어뜨렸고, 나와 마주친 그 눈이 번뜩였다."], ["“You see it now, as my mother saw it eleven years since!”", "“이제 당신도 보이는군요, 열한 해 전 제 어머니가 본 그대로!”"]], [["“I see it—more unwillingly than I can say.", "“보입니다—말로 다 할 수 없이 마지못해 보입니다."], ["To associate that forlorn, friendless, lost woman, even by an accidental likeness only, with Miss Fairlie, seems like casting a shadow on the future of the bright creature who stands looking at us now.", "그 의지가지없고 벗도 없는 길 잃은 여인을, 한낱 우연한 닮음만으로라도 페얼리 양과 결부시키는 것은, 지금 우리를 바라보며 서 있는 저 밝은 존재의 앞날에 그늘을 드리우는 것만 같습니다."], ["Let me lose the impression again as soon as possible.", "되도록 빨리 그 인상을 다시 지워 버리게 해 주세요."], ["Call her in, out of the dreary moonlight—pray call her in!”", "저 스산한 달빛에서 그녀를 안으로 불러 주세요—제발 불러 주세요!”"]], [["“Mr. Hartright, you surprise me.", "“하트라이트 씨, 당신은 저를 놀라게 하시는군요."], ["Whatever women may be, I thought that men, in the nineteenth century, were above superstition.”", "여자야 어떻든, 저는 십구 세기의 남자라면 미신 따위는 뛰어넘은 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“Pray call her in!”", "“제발 그녀를 불러 주세요!”"]], [["“Hush, hush!", "“쉿, 쉿!"], ["She is coming of her own accord.", "그녀가 제 발로 오고 있어요."], ["Say nothing in her presence.", "그녀 앞에서는 아무 말도 마세요."], ["Let this discovery of the likeness be kept a secret between you and me.", "이 닮음의 발견은 당신과 저 사이의 비밀로 해 두어요."], ["Come in, Laura, come in, and wake Mrs. Vesey with the piano.", "들어와, 로라, 들어와서 피아노로 베지 부인을 깨우렴."], ["Mr. Hartright is petitioning for some more music, and he wants it, this time, of the lightest and liveliest kind.”", "하트라이트 씨가 음악을 좀 더 청하시는데, 이번에는 가장 가볍고 가장 흥겨운 걸로 듣고 싶으시대.”"]], [["So ended my eventful first day at Limmeridge House.", "이렇게 하여 리머리지 하우스에서의 사건 많던 나의 첫날이 끝났다."]], [["Miss Halcombe and I kept our secret.", "홀콤 양과 나는 비밀을 지켰다."], ["After the discovery of the likeness no fresh light seemed destined to break over the mystery of the woman in white.", "그 닮음을 발견한 뒤로도 흰옷을 입은 여인의 수수께끼 위로 새로운 빛이 비칠 조짐은 없어 보였다."], ["At the first safe opportunity Miss Halcombe cautiously led her half-sister to speak of their mother, of old times, and of Anne Catherick.", "처음으로 안전한 기회가 오자 홀콤 양은 조심스레 이복동생을 이끌어 그들의 어머니와 옛 시절, 그리고 앤 캐서릭에 대해 이야기하게 했다."], ["Miss Fairlie’s recollections of the little scholar at Limmeridge were, however, only of the most vague and general kind.", "그러나 리머리지의 그 어린 학생에 대한 페얼리 양의 기억은 더없이 어렴풋하고 두루뭉술한 것뿐이었다."], ["She remembered the likeness between herself and her mother’s favourite pupil, as something which had been supposed to exist in past times; but she did not refer to the gift of the white dresses, or to the singular form of words in which the child had artlessly expressed her gratitude for them.", "그녀는 자신과 어머니가 아끼던 제자 사이의 닮음을, 지난 시절에 있었으리라 여겨지던 무언가로 기억했다. 그러나 흰 드레스를 선물받은 일이나, 그 아이가 그에 대한 고마움을 순진하게 나타낸 그 별스러운 말투에 대해서는 언급하지 않았다."], ["She remembered that Anne had remained at Limmeridge for a few months only, and had then left it to go back to her home in Hampshire; but she could not say whether the mother and daughter had ever returned, or had ever been heard of afterwards.", "그녀는 앤이 리머리지에 겨우 몇 달 머물렀다가 햄프셔의 제 집으로 돌아갔다는 것은 기억했으나, 그 어머니와 딸이 그 뒤로 다시 돌아왔는지, 소식이라도 들려왔는지는 말하지 못했다."], ["No further search, on Miss Halcombe’s part, through the few letters of Mrs. Fairlie’s writing which she had left unread, assisted in clearing up the uncertainties still left to perplex us.", "아직 읽지 않고 남겨 둔 페얼리 부인의 몇 안 되는 편지를 홀콤 양이 더 뒤져 보아도, 여전히 우리를 어리둥절하게 하는 미심쩍음을 밝히는 데는 아무 도움이 되지 않았다."], ["We had identified the unhappy woman whom I had met in the night-time with Anne Catherick—we had made some advance, at least, towards connecting the probably defective condition of the poor creature’s intellect with the peculiarity of her being dressed all in white, and with the continuance, in her maturer years, of her childish gratitude towards Mrs. Fairlie—and there, so far as we knew at that time, our discoveries had ended.", "우리는 밤중에 내가 만난 그 불행한 여인을 앤 캐서릭으로 밝혀냈고—적어도, 그 가엾은 존재의 아마도 결함 있는 지력의 상태를, 온통 흰옷을 입고 있다는 그 유별남과, 다 자란 나이에도 페얼리 부인을 향한 어린애 같은 고마움이 이어지고 있다는 것과 결부시키는 데 얼마간 나아갔다—그리고 그때 우리가 아는 한, 우리의 발견은 거기서 끝났다."]], [["The days passed on, the weeks passed on, and the track of the golden autumn wound its bright way visibly through the green summer of the trees.", "날이 지나고 주가 지나며, 황금빛 가을의 자취가 나무들의 푸른 여름을 뚫고 그 밝은 길을 눈에 뜨이게 굽이쳐 갔다."], ["Peaceful, fast-flowing, happy time!", "평화롭고 빠르게 흐르는 행복한 시간이여!"], ["my story glides by you now as swiftly as you once glided by me.", "한때 그대가 나를 스쳐 지나갔듯, 이제 내 이야기가 그토록 빠르게 그대를 스쳐 지나간다."], ["Of all the treasures of enjoyment that you poured so freely into my heart, how much is left me that has purpose and value enough to be written on this page?", "그대가 그토록 아낌없이 내 가슴에 쏟아부은 온갖 즐거움의 보물 가운데, 이 지면에 적을 만큼 뜻과 값어치가 있는 것이 나에게 얼마나 남아 있는가?"], ["Nothing but the saddest of all confessions that a man can make—the confession of his own folly.", "사람이 할 수 있는 모든 고백 가운데 가장 서글픈 것—제 어리석음에 대한 고백 말고는 아무것도 없다."]], [["The secret which that confession discloses should be told with little effort, for it has indirectly escaped me already.", "그 고백이 드러내는 비밀은 별 힘 들이지 않고 말할 수 있을 것이다, 이미 은연중에 새어 나와 버렸으니까."], ["The poor weak words, which have failed to describe Miss Fairlie, have succeeded in betraying the sensations she awakened in me.", "페얼리 양을 묘사하는 데 실패한 그 딱하고 힘없는 말들이, 그녀가 내 안에 일깨운 감정을 드러내는 데는 성공했다."], ["It is so with us all.", "우리 모두가 그렇다."], ["Our words are giants when they do us an injury, and dwarfs when they do us a service.", "우리의 말은 우리를 해칠 때는 거인이요, 우리를 도울 때는 난쟁이다."]], [["I loved her.", "나는 그녀를 사랑했다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["how well I know all the sadness and all the mockery that is contained in those three words.", "나는 그 세 마디에 담긴 온갖 서글픔과 온갖 조롱을 얼마나 잘 아는가."], ["I can sigh over my mournful confession with the tenderest woman who reads it and pities me.", "나는 이 애처로운 고백을 읽으며 나를 가엾이 여기는 더없이 다정한 여인과 함께 한숨지을 수 있다."], ["I can laugh at it as bitterly as the hardest man who tosses it from him in contempt. I loved her!", "나는 그것을, 그것을 업신여기며 저에게서 내던지는 더없이 냉정한 사내만큼이나 쓰라리게 비웃을 수 있다."], ["Feel for me, or despise me, I confess it with the same immovable resolution to own the truth.", "나는 그녀를 사랑했다! 나를 가엾이 여기든 나를 업신여기든, 나는 진실을 인정하려는 그 흔들림 없는 결의로 그것을 고백한다."]], [["Was there no excuse for me?", "나에게 변명거리라고는 없었던가?"], ["There was some excuse to be found, surely, in the conditions under which my term of hired service was passed at Limmeridge House.", "분명, 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 고용살이를 하던 그 형편 속에서 얼마간의 변명거리는 찾을 수 있었다."]], [["My morning hours succeeded each other calmly in the quiet and seclusion of my own room.", "내 아침 시간은 내 방의 고요와 은둔 속에서 잔잔하게 잇달아 지나갔다."], ["I had just work enough to do, in mounting my employer’s drawings, to keep my hands and eyes pleasurably employed, while my mind was left free to enjoy the dangerous luxury of its own unbridled thoughts.", "고용주의 소묘를 표구하는 데 손과 눈을 흐뭇하게 놀릴 만큼의 일이 꼭 있었고, 그러는 동안 내 마음은 제멋대로 뻗는 생각의 그 위험한 호사를 누리도록 풀려 있었다."], ["A perilous solitude, for it lasted long enough to enervate, not long enough to fortify me.", "위험한 고독이었으니, 나를 굳세게 하기에는 모자라고 나른하게 하기에는 넉넉할 만큼 오래 이어졌기 때문이다."], ["A perilous solitude, for it was followed by afternoons and evenings spent, day after day and week after week alone in the society of two women, one of whom possessed all the accomplishments of grace, wit, and high-breeding, the other all the charms of beauty, gentleness, and simple truth, that can purify and subdue the heart of man.", "위험한 고독이었으니, 그 뒤로 날마다 주마다 두 여인의 벗 속에서 홀로 보내는 오후와 저녁이 이어졌기 때문이다. 그 하나는 우아함과 재치와 고귀한 교양의 온갖 소양을 지녔고, 다른 하나는 사내의 마음을 정화하고 사로잡을 수 있는 아름다움과 부드러움과 소박한 진실의 온갖 매력을 지닌 이였다."], ["Not a day passed, in that dangerous intimacy of teacher and pupil, in which my hand was not close to Miss Fairlie’s; my cheek, as we bent together over her sketch-book, almost touching hers.", "선생과 제자의 그 위험한 친밀함 속에서, 내 손이 페얼리 양의 손 가까이 있지 않은 날, 우리가 함께 사생첩 위로 몸을 숙일 때 내 뺨이 그녀의 뺨에 거의 닿지 않은 날은 하루도 없었다."], ["The more attentively she watched every movement of my brush, the more closely I was breathing the perfume of her hair, and the warm fragrance of her breath.", "그녀가 내 붓의 움직임 하나하나를 유심히 지켜볼수록, 나는 그녀 머리칼의 향과 그 숨결의 따뜻한 향기를 더 가까이 들이마셨다."], ["It was part of my service to live in the very light of her eyes—at one time to be bending over her, so close to her bosom as to tremble at the thought of touching it; at another, to feel her bending over me, bending so close to see what I was about, that her voice sank low when she spoke to me, and her ribbons brushed my cheek in the wind before she could draw them back.", "그녀 눈의 바로 그 빛 속에서 사는 것이 내 소임의 일부였다—어느 때는 그녀에게 몸을 숙여, 그 가슴에 닿는다는 생각만으로도 떨릴 만큼 가까이 다가가고, 또 어느 때는 그녀가 내가 무얼 하는지 보려고 나에게 몸을 숙이는 것을 느꼈는데, 어찌나 가까이 숙였던지 나에게 말할 때 그 목소리가 나직이 잦아들고, 미처 거두어들이기도 전에 그 리본이 바람에 내 뺨을 스쳤다."]], [["The evenings which followed the sketching excursions of the afternoon varied, rather than checked, these innocent, these inevitable familiarities.", "오후의 사생 나들이에 뒤따르는 저녁들은, 이 순진하고 이 어쩔 수 없는 친밀함을 막기보다 오히려 다채롭게 했다."], ["My natural fondness for the music which she played with such tender feeling, such delicate womanly taste, and her natural enjoyment of giving me back, by the practice of her art, the pleasure which I had offered to her by the practice of mine, only wove another tie which drew us closer and closer to one another.", "그녀가 그토록 다정한 감정으로, 그토록 여린 여성스러운 취향으로 연주하던 음악에 대한 나의 타고난 애호와, 내가 내 예술을 부려 그녀에게 준 즐거움을 제 예술을 부려 나에게 되돌려 주는 것에 대한 그녀의 타고난 기쁨은, 우리를 서로 점점 더 가까이 끌어당기는 또 하나의 끈을 자아낼 따름이었다."], ["The accidents of conversation; the simple habits which regulated even such a little thing as the position of our places at table; the play of Miss Halcombe’s ever-ready raillery, always directed against my anxiety as teacher, while it sparkled over her enthusiasm as pupil; the harmless expression of poor Mrs. Vesey’s drowsy approval, which connected Miss Fairlie and me as two model young people who never disturbed her—every one of these trifles, and many more, combined to fold us together in the same domestic atmosphere, and to lead us both insensibly to the same hopeless end.", "우연히 오간 대화들, 식탁에서 우리 자리의 위치라는 그런 사소한 것마저 정해 놓은 소박한 습관들, 제자로서의 그녀의 열의 위로 반짝이면서도 언제나 선생으로서의 내 노심초사를 겨누던 홀콤 양의 늘 준비된 놀림의 장난, 페얼리 양과 나를 자기를 한 번도 방해하지 않는 두 모범 청년으로 짝지어 놓던 가엾은 베지 부인의 졸린 승낙의 그 해롭잖은 표현—이 자잘한 것들 하나하나가, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 것들이 어우러져, 우리를 같은 가정의 분위기 속에 함께 감싸고, 우리 둘을 저도 모르게 같은 가망 없는 결말로 이끌었다."]], [["I should have remembered my position, and have put myself secretly on my guard.", "나는 내 처지를 기억하고 남몰래 경계 태세를 갖췄어야 했다."], ["I did so, but not till it was too late.", "그렇게 하기는 했으나, 이미 너무 늦은 뒤였다."], ["All the discretion, all the experience, which had availed me with other women, and secured me against other temptations, failed me with her.", "다른 여자들에게는 통했고 다른 유혹들로부터 나를 지켜 준 온갖 조심성, 온갖 경험이, 그녀에게는 통하지 않았다."], ["It had been my profession, for years past, to be in this close contact with young girls of all ages, and of all orders of beauty.", "온갖 나이대, 온갖 부류의 아름다움을 지닌 젊은 아가씨들과 이토록 가까이 붙어 있는 것이 여러 해 동안 내 직업이었다."], ["I had accepted the position as part of my calling in life; I had trained myself to leave all the sympathies natural to my age in my employer’s outer hall, as coolly as I left my umbrella there before I went upstairs.", "나는 그 처지를 내 인생의 소명의 일부로 받아들였다. 위층으로 올라가기 전에 우산을 그 바깥 현관에 두고 가듯, 내 나이에 어울리는 온갖 감정을 고용주의 바깥 현관에 두고 가도록 태연히 나 자신을 길들여 왔다."], ["I had long since learnt to understand, composedly and as a matter of course, that my situation in life was considered a guarantee against any of my female pupils feeling more than the most ordinary interest in me, and that I was admitted among beautiful and captivating women much as a harmless domestic animal is admitted among them.", "내 인생의 처지가 여자 제자 가운데 누구도 나에게 지극히 예사로운 관심 이상을 느끼지 않으리라는 보증으로 여겨진다는 것을, 그리고 내가 아름답고 매혹적인 여인들 사이에, 해롭잖은 집짐승이 그들 사이에 받아들여지듯 받아들여진다는 것을, 나는 오래전에 태연히, 또 당연한 일로 이해하게 되었다."], ["This guardian experience I had gained early; this guardian experience had sternly and strictly guided me straight along my own poor narrow path, without once letting me stray aside, to the right hand or to the left.", "이 지켜 주는 경험을 나는 일찍이 얻었고, 이 지켜 주는 경험은 나를 오른쪽으로도 왼쪽으로도 한 번도 벗어나게 두지 않고, 내 딱하고 좁은 길을 따라 곧장 엄격하고 단단히 이끌어 왔다."], ["And now I and my trusty talisman were parted for the first time.", "그런데 이제 나와 내 믿음직한 부적이 처음으로 갈라섰다."], ["Yes, my hardly-earned self-control was as completely lost to me as if I had never possessed it; lost to me, as it is lost every day to other men, in other critical situations, where women are concerned.", "그렇다, 힘겹게 얻은 내 자제심은 애초에 지닌 적조차 없었던 양 나에게서 완전히 사라져 버렸다. 여자가 얽힌 다른 위태로운 형편에서 날마다 다른 사내들에게서 사라지듯, 나에게서도 사라졌다."], ["I know, now, that I should have questioned myself from the first.", "이제 나는 안다, 처음부터 나 자신에게 물었어야 했음을."], ["I should have asked why any room in the house was better than home to me when she entered it, and barren as a desert when she went out again—why I always noticed and remembered the little changes in her dress that I had noticed and remembered in no other woman’s before—why I saw her, heard her, and touched her (when we shook hands at night and morning) as I had never seen, heard, and touched any other woman in my life?", "그녀가 어느 방에 들어서면 그 방이 나에게 집보다 나아지고, 그녀가 다시 나가면 사막처럼 황량해지는 것은 어째서인지—다른 어떤 여자에게서도 눈여겨보거나 기억한 적 없던 그녀 옷차림의 자잘한 변화를 나는 언제나 눈여겨보고 기억하는 것은 어째서인지—(밤과 아침에 악수할 때) 내 평생 다른 어떤 여자도 그렇게 보고 듣고 만진 적 없듯 그녀를 보고 듣고 만지는 것은 어째서인지 물었어야 했다."], ["I should have looked into my own heart, and found this new growth springing up there, and plucked it out while it was young.", "나는 내 마음속을 들여다보고, 거기 이 새로운 싹이 돋아나고 있음을 알아채어, 그것이 어릴 때 뽑아냈어야 했다."], ["Why was this easiest, simplest work of self-culture always too much for me?", "이 더없이 쉽고 더없이 간단한 자기 수양의 일이 어째서 언제나 나에게는 너무 벅찼던가?"], ["The explanation has been written already in the three words that were many enough, and plain enough, for my confession.", "그 해명은 이미, 내 고백에 넉넉하고도 분명한 그 세 마디에 적혀 있다."], ["I loved her.", "나는 그녀를 사랑했다."]], [["The days passed, the weeks passed; it was approaching the third month of my stay in Cumberland.", "날이 지나고 주가 지났다. 컴벌랜드에 머문 지 어느새 석 달째로 접어들고 있었다."], ["The delicious monotony of life in our calm seclusion flowed on with me, like a smooth stream with a swimmer who glides down the current.", "우리의 잔잔한 은둔 속 그 감미로운 단조로움이, 물살을 따라 미끄러져 내려가는 헤엄치는 이를 태운 매끄러운 냇물처럼 나와 더불어 흘러갔다."], ["All memory of the past, all thought of the future, all sense of the falseness and hopelessness of my own position, lay hushed within me into deceitful rest.", "과거에 대한 온갖 기억, 미래에 대한 온갖 생각, 내 처지의 거짓됨과 가망 없음에 대한 온갖 자각이, 내 안에서 잠잠해져 속임수 같은 안식에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Lulled by the Syren-song that my own heart sung to me, with eyes shut to all sight, and ears closed to all sound of danger, I drifted nearer and nearer to the fatal rocks.", "내 심장이 스스로 나에게 불러 주는 세이렌의 노래에 홀려, 온갖 광경에 눈을 감고 온갖 위험의 소리에 귀를 닫은 채, 나는 그 치명적인 바위 쪽으로 점점 더 가까이 떠내려갔다."], ["The warning that aroused me at last, and startled me into sudden, self-accusing consciousness of my own weakness, was the plainest, the truest, the kindest of all warnings, for it came silently from HER.", "마침내 나를 흔들어 깨워, 내 자신의 나약함을 자책하는 불현듯한 자각으로 소스라치게 한 그 경고는, 온갖 경고 가운데 가장 꾸밈없고 가장 참되고 가장 다정한 것이었으니, 그것이 그녀에게서 소리 없이 왔기 때문이다."]], [["We had parted one night as usual.", "우리는 어느 날 밤 여느 때처럼 헤어졌다."], ["No word had fallen from my lips, at that time or at any time before it, that could betray me, or startle her into sudden knowledge of the truth.", "그때도, 그 전 어느 때도, 나를 드러내거나 그녀를 진실의 불현듯한 앎으로 소스라치게 할 만한 말은 내 입에서 한 마디도 떨어지지 않았다."], ["But when we met again in the morning, a change had come over her—a change that told me all.", "그러나 아침에 다시 만났을 때, 그녀에게 변화가 와 있었다—나에게 모든 것을 말해 주는 변화가."]], [["I shrank then—I shrink still—from invading the innermost sanctuary of her heart, and laying it open to others, as I have laid open my own.", "나는 그때도 움츠러들었고—지금도 움츠러든다—그녀 마음의 가장 깊은 성소를 침범해, 내 것을 열어 보였듯 그것을 남들에게 열어 보이는 일에서."], ["Let it be enough to say that the time when she first surprised my secret was, I firmly believe, the time when she first surprised her own, and the time, also, when she changed towards me in the interval of one night.", "그녀가 처음으로 내 비밀을 알아챈 그때가, 내가 굳게 믿건대, 그녀가 처음으로 제 비밀을 알아챈 그때였으며, 또한 그녀가 하룻밤 사이에 나를 향해 달라진 그때였다는 것만 말해 두면 족하리라."], ["Her nature, too truthful to deceive others, was too noble to deceive itself.", "남을 속이기에는 너무 진실한 그녀의 천성은, 제 자신을 속이기에는 너무 고귀했다."], ["When the doubt that I had hushed asleep first laid its weary weight on her heart, the true face owned all, and said, in its own frank, simple language—I am sorry for him; I am sorry for myself.", "내가 잠재워 둔 그 의문이 처음으로 그 지친 무게를 그녀 가슴에 얹었을 때, 그 진실한 얼굴은 모든 것을 인정하며, 제 솔직하고 소박한 언어로 말했다—그이가 가엾다, 나 자신이 가엾다."]], [["It said this, and more, which I could not then interpret.", "그것은 이렇게 말했고, 그때 내가 헤아릴 수 없었던 더 많은 것을 말했다."], ["I understood but too well the change in her manner, to greater kindness and quicker readiness in interpreting all my wishes, before others—to constraint and sadness, and nervous anxiety to absorb herself in the first occupation she could seize on, whenever we happened to be left together alone.", "남들 앞에서 내 온갖 바람을 더 큰 친절과 더 빠른 시원스러움으로 헤아려 주는 쪽으로—우리가 어쩌다 단둘이 남겨질 때면 언제나, 붙잡을 수 있는 첫 일거리에 자신을 파묻으려는 어색함과 서글픔과 조바심 어린 근심 쪽으로 바뀐 그녀의 태도를, 나는 너무나 잘 이해했다."], ["I understood why the sweet sensitive lips smiled so rarely and so restrainedly now, and why the clear blue eyes looked at me, sometimes with the pity of an angel, sometimes with the innocent perplexity of a child.", "그 달콤하고 예민한 입술이 이제 그토록 드물게, 그토록 삼가며 미소 짓는 것은 어째서인지, 그 맑은 파란 눈이 어느 때는 천사의 연민으로, 어느 때는 아이의 순진한 어리둥절함으로 나를 바라보는 것은 어째서인지, 나는 이해했다."], ["But the change meant more than this.", "그러나 그 변화는 이것 이상을 뜻했다."], ["There was a coldness in her hand, there was an unnatural immobility in her face, there was in all her movements the mute expression of constant fear and clinging self-reproach.", "그녀의 손에는 냉랭함이 있었고, 그 얼굴에는 부자연스러운 굳음이 있었으며, 그 온갖 몸짓에는 끊임없는 두려움과 매달리는 자책의 말 없는 표현이 있었다."], ["The sensations that I could trace to herself and to me, the unacknowledged sensations that we were feeling in common, were not these.", "그녀에게서, 또 나에게서 비롯되었다고 내가 짚어 낼 수 있는 감정, 우리가 함께 느끼면서도 인정하지 않던 그 감정은 이런 것이 아니었다."], ["There were certain elements of the change in her that were still secretly drawing us together, and others that were, as secretly, beginning to drive us apart.", "그녀 안의 그 변화에는, 아직도 남몰래 우리를 서로 끌어당기는 어떤 요소들과, 그만큼 남몰래 우리를 갈라놓기 시작하는 다른 요소들이 있었다."]], [["In my doubt and perplexity, in my vague suspicion of something hidden which I was left to find by my own unaided efforts, I examined Miss Halcombe’s looks and manner for enlightenment.", "내 의문과 어리둥절함 속에서, 오로지 나 혼자의 힘으로 찾아내도록 남겨진 무언가 감춰진 것에 대한 어렴풋한 의심 속에서, 나는 홀콤 양의 표정과 태도에서 깨달음을 얻으려 살폈다."], ["Living in such intimacy as ours, no serious alteration could take place in any one of us which did not sympathetically affect the others.", "우리 같은 친밀함 속에서 살자면, 우리 가운데 누구 하나에게 심각한 변화가 일어나면 그것이 다른 이들에게도 공명하듯 영향을 미치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The change in Miss Fairlie was reflected in her half-sister.", "페얼리 양의 변화는 그 이복언니에게 비쳐 나타났다."], ["Although not a word escaped Miss Halcombe which hinted at an altered state of feeling towards myself, her penetrating eyes had contracted a new habit of always watching me.", "나를 향한 감정이 달라졌음을 넌지시라도 비추는 말은 홀콤 양의 입에서 한 마디도 새어 나오지 않았으나, 그 꿰뚫는 눈은 언제나 나를 지켜보는 새로운 버릇이 들어 있었다."], ["Sometimes the look was like suppressed anger, sometimes like suppressed dread, sometimes like neither—like nothing, in short, which I could understand.", "어느 때는 그 눈빛이 억눌린 분노 같았고, 어느 때는 억눌린 두려움 같았으며, 어느 때는 그 어느 쪽도 아닌—한마디로 내가 이해할 수 있는 그 무엇도 아닌 것 같았다."], ["A week elapsed, leaving us all three still in this position of secret constraint towards one another.", "한 주가 지나도록 우리 셋은 여전히 서로를 향한 이 은밀한 어색함의 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["My situation, aggravated by the sense of my own miserable weakness and forgetfulness of myself, now too late awakened in me, was becoming intolerable.", "이제 너무 늦게야 내 안에 깨어난, 내 자신의 비참한 나약함과 자기 망각에 대한 자각으로 더 심해진 내 처지는 견딜 수 없어지고 있었다."], ["I felt that I must cast off the oppression under which I was living, at once and for ever—yet how to act for the best, or what to say first, was more than I could tell.", "나는 내가 놓인 이 억눌림을 당장, 그리고 영영 떨쳐 내야 함을 느꼈다—그러나 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 나은지, 무슨 말을 먼저 해야 하는지는 내가 알 수 있는 것 이상이었다."]], [["From this position of helplessness and humiliation I was rescued by Miss Halcombe.", "이 속수무책과 굴욕의 처지에서 나를 구해 준 것은 홀콤 양이었다. 그 입술이 나에게 쓰라리고, 어쩔 수 없고, 뜻밖인 진실을 말해 주었다."], ["Her lips told me the bitter, the necessary, the unexpected truth; her hearty kindness sustained me under the shock of hearing it; her sense and courage turned to its right use an event which threatened the worst that could happen, to me and to others, in Limmeridge House.", "그 진심 어린 친절이 그것을 듣는 충격 아래 나를 버텨 주었다. 그 분별과 용기가, 리머리지 하우스에서 나에게도 남들에게도 일어날 수 있는 최악을 위협하던 사태를 올바른 쓰임으로 돌려놓았다."]], [["# X", "# X"]], [["It was on a Thursday in the week, and nearly at the end of the third month of my sojourn in Cumberland.", "그 주의 어느 목요일이었고, 내가 컴벌랜드에 머문 지 석 달째가 거의 끝나 갈 무렵이었다."]], [["In the morning, when I went down into the breakfast-room at the usual hour, Miss Halcombe, for the first time since I had known her, was absent from her customary place at the table.", "아침에 여느 때와 같은 시각에 아침 식사실로 내려가니, 내가 그녀를 알게 된 이래 처음으로, 홀콤 양이 식탁의 늘 앉던 자리에 없었다."]], [["Miss Fairlie was out on the lawn.", "페얼리 양은 잔디밭에 나가 있었다."], ["She bowed to me, but did not come in.", "그녀는 나에게 고개를 숙였으나 안으로 들어오지는 않았다."], ["Not a word had dropped from my lips, or from hers, that could unsettle either of us—and yet the same unacknowledged sense of embarrassment made us shrink alike from meeting one another alone.", "우리 둘 가운데 누구를 흔들어 놓을 만한 말은 내 입에서도, 그녀의 입에서도 한 마디 떨어지지 않았다—그런데도 인정하지 않은 그 어색함이 우리로 하여금 단둘이 마주하기를 똑같이 움츠러들게 했다."], ["She waited on the lawn, and I waited in the breakfast-room, till Mrs. Vesey or Miss Halcombe came in.", "베지 부인이나 홀콤 양이 들어올 때까지, 그녀는 잔디밭에서 기다렸고 나는 아침 식사실에서 기다렸다."], ["How quickly I should have joined her: how readily we should have shaken hands, and glided into our customary talk, only a fortnight ago.", "겨우 두 주 전만 해도 나는 얼마나 얼른 그녀에게 다가갔을 것이며, 우리는 얼마나 선뜻 악수를 나누고 늘 하던 이야기 속으로 미끄러져 들어갔을 것인가."]], [["In a few minutes Miss Halcombe entered.", "몇 분 뒤 홀콤 양이 들어왔다."], ["She had a preoccupied look, and she made her apologies for being late rather absently.", "무언가에 골몰한 기색이었고, 늦은 데 대한 사과도 다소 넋 놓은 채로 했다."]], [["“I have been detained,”", "“붙들려 있었어요.”"], ["she said, “by a consultation with Mr.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Fairlie on a domestic matter which he wished to speak to me about.”", "“페얼리 씨가 저에게 이야기하고 싶어 하시는 집안일에 대해 상의하느라고요.”"]], [["Miss Fairlie came in from the garden, and the usual morning greeting passed between us.", "페얼리 양이 정원에서 들어왔고, 우리 사이에 여느 때와 같은 아침 인사가 오갔다."], ["Her hand struck colder to mine than ever.", "그녀의 손은 그 어느 때보다 차갑게 내 손에 와닿았다."], ["She did not look at me, and she was very pale.", "그녀는 나를 바라보지 않았고, 몹시 창백했다."], ["Even Mrs. Vesey noticed it when she entered the room a moment after.", "잠시 뒤 방에 들어온 베지 부인조차 그것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I suppose it is the change in the wind,”", "“바람이 바뀐 탓인가 봐요.”"], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["“The winter is coming—ah, my love, the winter is coming soon!”", "“겨울이 오고 있어요—아, 얘야, 겨울이 곧 오는구나!”"]], [["In her heart and in mine it had come already!", "그녀의 마음속에도 내 마음속에도 겨울은 이미 와 있었다!"]], [["Our morning meal—once so full of pleasant good-humoured discussion of the plans for the day—was short and silent.", "한때 그날의 계획을 유쾌하고 정겹게 의논하는 것으로 그토록 가득 차 있던 우리의 아침 식사는, 짧고 조용했다."], ["Miss Fairlie seemed to feel the oppression of the long pauses in the conversation, and looked appealingly to her sister to fill them up.", "페얼리 양은 대화의 긴 침묵의 억눌림을 느끼는 듯, 그것을 메워 달라고 언니를 애원하듯 바라보았다."], ["Miss Halcombe, after once or twice hesitating and checking herself, in a most uncharacteristic manner, spoke at last.", "홀콤 양은 한두 번 머뭇거리다 스스로를 다잡기를, 더없이 그녀답지 않은 태도로 되풀이한 끝에, 마침내 입을 열었다."]], [["“I have seen your uncle this morning, Laura,”", "“오늘 아침 네 삼촌을 뵈었단다, 로라.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“He thinks the purple room is the one that ought to be got ready, and he confirms what I told you.", "“그분은 자줏빛 방을 채비해 두는 게 좋겠다고 하시더라. 그리고 내가 너에게 말한 걸 다시 확인해 주셨어."], ["Monday is the day—not Tuesday.”", "그날은 월요일이야—화요일이 아니라.”"]], [["While these words were being spoken Miss Fairlie looked down at the table beneath her.", "이 말이 오가는 동안 페얼리 양은 제 앞의 식탁을 내려다보았다."], ["Her fingers moved nervously among the crumbs that were scattered on the cloth.", "그녀의 손가락은 식탁보에 흩어진 부스러기 사이를 초조하게 움직였다."], ["The paleness on her cheeks spread to her lips, and the lips themselves trembled visibly.", "뺨의 창백함이 입술로 번졌고, 입술 자체가 눈에 뜨이게 떨렸다."], ["I was not the only person present who noticed this.", "이것을 알아챈 것은 그 자리의 나뿐만이 아니었다."], ["Miss Halcombe saw it, too, and at once set us the example of rising from table.", "홀콤 양도 그것을 보고는, 즉시 우리에게 식탁에서 일어나는 본을 보였다."]], [["Mrs. Vesey and Miss Fairlie left the room together.", "베지 부인과 페얼리 양은 함께 방을 나섰다."], ["The kind sorrowful blue eyes looked at me, for a moment, with the prescient sadness of a coming and a long farewell.", "그 다정하고 서글픈 파란 눈이 잠시, 다가오는 긴 작별을 미리 아는 슬픔으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["I felt the answering pang in my own heart—the pang that told me I must lose her soon, and love her the more unchangeably for the loss.", "나는 내 가슴속에서 그에 응하는 아픔을 느꼈다—머잖아 그녀를 잃을 것이며, 그 잃음으로 인해 그녀를 더욱 변함없이 사랑하리라 나에게 일러 주는 그 아픔을."]], [["I turned towards the garden when the door had closed on her.", "문이 그녀 뒤로 닫히자 나는 정원 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Miss Halcombe was standing with her hat in her hand, and her shawl over her arm, by the large window that led out to the lawn, and was looking at me attentively.", "홀콤 양이 잔디밭으로 이어지는 큰 창가에, 모자를 손에 들고 숄을 팔에 걸친 채 서서 나를 유심히 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Have you any leisure time to spare,”", "“당신 방에서 일을 시작하기 전에.”"], ["she asked, “before you begin to work in your own room?”", "그녀가 물었다. “좀 내어 줄 짬이 있으세요?”"]], [["“Certainly, Miss Halcombe.", "“물론입니다, 홀콤 양."], ["I have always time at your service.”", "저에게는 언제나 당신을 위한 시간이 있지요.”"]], [["“I want to say a word to you in private, Mr. Hartright.", "“당신께 은밀히 한마디 하고 싶어요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Get your hat and come out into the garden.", "모자를 쓰고 정원으로 나오세요."], ["We are not likely to be disturbed there at this hour in the morning.”", "이 아침 시각에는 거기서 방해받을 일이 없을 거예요.”"]], [["As we stepped out on to the lawn, one of the under-gardeners—a mere lad—passed us on his way to the house, with a letter in his hand.", "우리가 잔디밭으로 나서는데, 아래 정원사 하나가—한낱 소년이었다—손에 편지를 들고 저택 쪽으로 가다가 우리 곁을 지났다."], ["Miss Halcombe stopped him.", "홀콤 양이 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Is that letter for me?”", "“그 편지, 나에게 온 거니?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Nay, miss; it’s just said to be for Miss Fairlie,”", "“아니라예, 아씨. 그저 페얼리 아씨한테 온 거라 카던데예.”"], ["answered the lad, holding out the letter as he spoke.", "소년이 말하며 편지를 내밀었다."]], [["Miss Halcombe took it from him and looked at the address.", "홀콤 양은 그에게서 그것을 받아 주소를 들여다보았다."]], [["“A strange handwriting,”", "“낯선 필체네.”"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀가 혼잣말로 말했다."], ["“Who can Laura’s correspondent be?", "“로라에게 편지 보낼 사람이 누굴까?"], ["Where did you get this?”", "이걸 어디서 받았니?”"], ["she continued, addressing the gardener.", "그녀가 정원사에게 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, miss,”", "“글쎄예, 아씨.”"], ["said the lad, “I just got it from a woman.”", "소년이 말했다. “그냥 어떤 여자한테서 받았는데예.”"]], [["“What woman?”", "“어떤 여자?”"]], [["“A woman well stricken in age.”", "“나이가 아주 지긋한 여자라예.”"]], [["“Oh, an old woman.", "“오, 노파로구나."], ["Any one you knew?”", "아는 사람이니?”"]], [["“I canna’ tak’ it on mysel’ to say that she was other than a stranger to me.”", "“그 여자가 저한테는 생판 낯선 사람 말고 딴것이었다고는 지가 감히 말 못 하겠는데예.”"]], [["“Which way did she go?”", "“어느 쪽으로 갔니?”"]], [["“That gate,”", "“저 문이라예.”"], ["said the under-gardener, turning with great deliberation towards the south, and embracing the whole of that part of England with one comprehensive sweep of his arm.", "아래 정원사가 아주 느긋하게 남쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 팔을 한 번 죽 휘둘러 잉글랜드의 그쪽 전부를 아우르듯 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Curious,”", "“이상하네.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe; “I suppose it must be a begging-letter.", "홀콤 양이 말했다. “구걸 편지인가 봐."], ["There,”", "자.”"], ["she added, handing the letter back to the lad, “take it to the house, and give it to one of the servants.", "그녀가 편지를 소년에게 도로 건네며 덧붙였다. “저택으로 가져가서 하인 아무에게나 주렴."], ["And now, Mr. Hartright, if you have no objection, let us walk this way.”", "자 그럼, 하트라이트 씨, 괜찮으시다면 이쪽으로 걸으실까요.”"]], [["She led me across the lawn, along the same path by which I had followed her on the day after my arrival at Limmeridge.", "그녀는 나를 이끌고 잔디밭을 가로질러, 내가 리머리지에 도착한 이튿날 그녀를 뒤따라 걷던 바로 그 오솔길을 따라갔다."]], [["At the little summer-house, in which Laura Fairlie and I had first seen each other, she stopped, and broke the silence which she had steadily maintained while we were walking together.", "로라 페얼리와 내가 처음 서로를 본 그 작은 정자에서, 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 함께 걷는 내내 한결같이 지켜 온 침묵을 깼다."]], [["“What I have to say to you I can say here.”", "“당신께 할 말은 여기서 할 수 있어요.”"]], [["With those words she entered the summer-house, took one of the chairs at the little round table inside, and signed to me to take the other.", "그 말과 함께 그녀는 정자로 들어가, 안의 작은 원탁 곁 의자 하나에 앉고는 나에게 다른 하나에 앉으라 손짓했다."], ["I suspected what was coming when she spoke to me in the breakfast-room; I felt certain of it now.", "아침 식사실에서 그녀가 나에게 말을 걸었을 때 나는 무슨 이야기가 나올지 어렴풋이 짐작했다. 이제 나는 그것을 확신했다."]], [["“Mr. Hartright,”", "“하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she said, “I am going to begin by making a frank avowal to you.", "그녀가 말했다. “당신께 솔직한 고백을 하는 것으로 시작하겠어요."], ["I am going to say—without phrase-making, which I detest, or paying compliments, which I heartily despise—that I have come, in the course of your residence with us, to feel a strong friendly regard for you.", "제가 몹시 싫어하는 미사여구도, 진심으로 업신여기는 겉치레 인사도 없이 말하겠어요—당신이 저희와 함께 지내시는 동안 저는 당신에게 강한 우정 어린 마음을 느끼게 되었어요."], ["I was predisposed in your favour when you first told me of your conduct towards that unhappy woman whom you met under such remarkable circumstances.", "당신이 그토록 심상찮은 형편에서 만난 그 불행한 여인을 대한 처신을 처음 저에게 말씀하셨을 때, 저는 이미 당신에게 호감을 품었지요."], ["Your management of the affair might not have been prudent, but it showed the self-control, the delicacy, and the compassion of a man who was naturally a gentleman.", "그 일을 다룬 방식이 신중하지는 못했을지 몰라도, 그것은 천성적으로 신사인 사람의 자제심과 섬세함과 연민을 보여 주었어요."], ["It made me expect good things from you, and you have not disappointed my expectations.”", "그것은 제가 당신에게 좋은 것을 기대하게 만들었고, 당신은 제 기대를 저버리지 않으셨어요.”"]], [["She paused—but held up her hand at the same time, as a sign that she awaited no answer from me before she proceeded.", "그녀는 잠시 멈추었다—그러나 동시에, 이야기를 이어 가기 전에 나에게서 대답을 기다리지 않겠다는 표시로 손을 들어 보였다."], ["When I entered the summer-house, no thought was in me of the woman in white.", "정자에 들어설 때 내 안에는 흰옷을 입은 여인에 대한 생각이 없었다."], ["But now, Miss Halcombe’s own words had put the memory of my adventure back in my mind.", "그러나 이제 홀콤 양 자신의 말이 그 겪음의 기억을 도로 내 마음에 되살려 놓았다."], ["It remained there throughout the interview—remained, and not without a result.", "그것은 이 면담 내내 거기 머물렀다—머물렀고, 결과가 없지도 않았다."]], [["“As your friend,”", "“당신의 벗으로서.” 그녀가 이어 갔다."], ["she proceeded, “I am going to tell you, at once, in my own plain, blunt, downright language, that I have discovered your secret—without help or hint, mind, from any one else.", "“저는 지금 당장, 제 나름의 꾸밈없고 무뚝뚝하고 딱 부러지는 말로 당신께 말하겠어요—저는 당신의 비밀을 알아냈어요. 명심하세요, 다른 누구의 도움이나 귀띔도 없이요."], ["Mr. Hartright, you have thoughtlessly allowed yourself to form an attachment—a serious and devoted attachment I am afraid—to my sister Laura.", "하트라이트 씨, 당신은 경솔하게도 제 동생 로라에게 정을—진지하고 헌신적인 정이 아닐까 두렵지만—붙이도록 스스로를 내버려 두셨어요."], ["I don’t put you to the pain of confessing it in so many words, because I see and know that you are too honest to deny it.", "저는 당신께 그것을 굳이 여러 말로 고백하는 아픔을 지우지 않겠어요. 당신이 그것을 부인하기엔 너무 정직한 사람임을 제가 보고 알기 때문이에요."], ["I don’t even blame you—I pity you for opening your heart to a hopeless affection.", "저는 당신을 나무라지도 않아요—가망 없는 애정에 마음을 연 당신이 가여울 뿐이지요."], ["You have not attempted to take any underhand advantage—you have not spoken to my sister in secret.", "당신은 어떤 뒷수도 쓰려 하지 않았고—은밀히 제 동생에게 말을 걸지도 않았어요."], ["You are guilty of weakness and want of attention to your own best interests, but of nothing worse.", "당신은 나약함과 자기 이익에 대한 소홀함의 죄는 있으나, 그보다 더한 죄는 없어요."], ["If you had acted, in any single respect, less delicately and less modestly, I should have told you to leave the house without an instant’s notice, or an instant’s consultation of anybody.", "만일 당신이 단 한 가지 점에서라도 덜 섬세하고 덜 겸손하게 굴었다면, 저는 당신께 한순간의 통보도, 누구와의 한순간의 상의도 없이 이 집을 떠나라고 했을 거예요."], ["As it is, I blame the misfortune of your years and your position—I don’t blame YOU.", "하지만 사정이 이러하니, 저는 당신의 나이와 처지의 불운을 나무랄 뿐—당신을 나무라진 않아요."], ["Shake hands—I have given you pain; I am going to give you more, but there is no help for it—shake hands with your friend, Marian Halcombe, first.”", "악수해요—저는 당신께 아픔을 주었어요. 더 많은 아픔을 드리려 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요—우선 당신의 벗 매리언 홀콤과 악수부터 해요.”"]], [["The sudden kindness—the warm, high-minded, fearless sympathy which met me on such mercifully equal terms, which appealed with such delicate and generous abruptness straight to my heart, my honour, and my courage, overcame me in an instant.", "그 불현듯한 친절—그토록 자비롭게 대등한 처지에서 나를 맞아 주고, 그토록 섬세하고 너그러운 느닷없음으로 곧장 내 가슴과 내 명예와 내 용기에 호소하는 그 따뜻하고 고결하고 두려움 없는 공감이, 순식간에 나를 압도했다."], ["I tried to look at her when she took my hand, but my eyes were dim.", "그녀가 내 손을 잡을 때 나는 그녀를 바라보려 했으나, 내 눈은 흐렸다."], ["I tried to thank her, but my voice failed me.", "나는 그녀에게 고마움을 표하려 했으나, 내 목소리가 나오지 않았다."]], [["“Listen to me,”", "“제 말을 들으세요.”"], ["she said, considerately avoiding all notice of my loss of self-control.", "그녀가 내 자제심의 무너짐을 짐짓 못 본 체하며 사려 깊게 말했다."], ["“Listen to me, and let us get it over at once.", "“제 말을 들으시고, 이 일을 당장 끝내 버려요."], ["It is a real true relief to me that I am not obliged, in what I have now to say, to enter into the question—the hard and cruel question as I think it—of social inequalities.", "지금 제가 할 말에서, 사회적 불평등이라는—제 생각엔 딱딱하고 매정한—그 문제로 파고들지 않아도 된다는 것이 저에게는 정말 참된 안도예요."], ["Circumstances which will try you to the quick, spare me the ungracious necessity of paining a man who has lived in friendly intimacy under the same roof with myself by any humiliating reference to matters of rank and station.", "당신을 뼛속까지 시험할 그 형편이, 저와 한 지붕 아래서 정답게 지내 온 사람에게 신분과 지위 문제를 굴욕적으로 들먹여 아픔을 주어야 하는 그 야박한 짓을 저에게서 덜어 주네요."], ["You must leave Limmeridge House, Mr. Hartright, before more harm is done.", "당신은 리머리지 하우스를 떠나셔야 해요, 하트라이트 씨, 더 큰 해가 생기기 전에요."], ["It is my duty to say that to you; and it would be equally my duty to say it, under precisely the same serious necessity, if you were the representative of the oldest and wealthiest family in England.", "당신께 그것을 말하는 것이 제 의무예요. 그리고 설령 당신이 잉글랜드에서 가장 오래되고 가장 부유한 가문의 대표라 해도, 꼭 같은 절박한 처지 아래 저는 그것을 말하는 것이 똑같이 제 의무일 거예요."], ["You must leave us, not because you are a teacher of drawing——”", "당신이 그림 선생이라서 저희를 떠나셔야 하는 게 아니에요——”"]], [["She waited a moment, turned her face full on me, and reaching across the table, laid her hand firmly on my arm.", "그녀는 잠시 멈추고, 얼굴을 온전히 나에게 돌려, 탁자 너머로 손을 뻗어 내 팔에 단단히 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Not because you are a teacher of drawing,”", "“당신이 그림 선생이라서가 아니라.”"], ["she repeated, “but because Laura Fairlie is engaged to be married.”", "그녀가 되풀이했다. “로라 페얼리가 혼인을 약속했기 때문이에요.”"]], [["The last word went like a bullet to my heart.", "그 마지막 말이 총알처럼 내 가슴을 파고들었다."], ["My arm lost all sensation of the hand that grasped it.", "내 팔은 그것을 붙든 손의 감각을 죄다 잃었다."], ["I never moved and never spoke.", "나는 움직이지도, 말하지도 않았다."], ["The sharp autumn breeze that scattered the dead leaves at our feet came as cold to me, on a sudden, as if my own mad hopes were dead leaves too, whirled away by the wind like the rest.", "우리 발치의 마른 잎을 흩날리던 매서운 가을바람이, 마치 내 미친 희망도 마른 잎이라 나머지처럼 바람에 휩쓸려 가기라도 한 양, 별안간 그토록 차갑게 나에게 와닿았다. 희망이라니!"], ["Hopes! Betrothed, or not betrothed, she was equally far from me.", "약혼했든 안 했든, 그녀는 나에게서 똑같이 멀었다."], ["Would other men have remembered that in my place?", "다른 사내들이라면 내 처지에서 그것을 기억했을까?"], ["Not if they had loved her as I did.", "나만큼 그녀를 사랑했다면 그러지 못했으리라."]], [["The pang passed, and nothing but the dull numbing pain of it remained.", "아픔은 지나갔고, 그것이 남긴 무디게 저리는 통증밖에 남지 않았다."], ["I felt Miss Halcombe’s hand again, tightening its hold on my arm—I raised my head and looked at her.", "나는 다시 홀콤 양의 손이, 내 팔을 쥔 힘을 더하는 것을 느꼈다—나는 고개를 들어 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Her large black eyes were rooted on me, watching the white change on my face, which I felt, and which she saw.", "그녀의 크고 검은 눈이 나에게 못 박힌 채, 내가 느끼고 그녀가 본, 내 얼굴의 하얗게 질리는 변화를 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“Crush it!”", "“짓눌러 버려요!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Here, where you first saw her, crush it!", "“여기, 당신이 그녀를 처음 본 이곳에서, 짓눌러 버려요!"], ["Don’t shrink under it like a woman.", "여자처럼 그 아래 움츠러들지 마세요."], ["Tear it out; trample it under foot like a man!”", "그것을 뽑아내요, 사내처럼 발밑에 짓밟아 버려요!”"]], [["The suppressed vehemence with which she spoke, the strength which her will—concentrated in the look she fixed on me, and in the hold on my arm that she had not yet relinquished—communicated to mine, steadied me.", "그녀가 말하는 그 억눌린 격렬함, 그녀의 의지의 힘이—나에게 고정한 눈빛에, 그리고 아직 놓지 않은 내 팔을 쥔 그 손에 응축되어—내 의지에 전해져 나를 다잡아 주었다."], ["We both waited for a minute in silence.", "우리는 둘 다 잠시 잠자코 기다렸다."], ["At the end of that time I had justified her generous faith in my manhood—I had, outwardly at least, recovered my self-control.", "그 시간이 끝날 무렵 나는 내 사내다움에 대한 그녀의 너그러운 믿음을 저버리지 않았다—적어도 겉으로는 자제심을 되찾았다."]], [["“Are you yourself again?”", "“이제 제정신이 드셨어요?”"]], [["“Enough myself, Miss Halcombe, to ask your pardon and hers.", "“당신과 그녀에게 용서를 청할 만큼은 제정신이 들었습니다, 홀콤 양."], ["Enough myself to be guided by your advice, and to prove my gratitude in that way, if I can prove it in no other.”", "당신의 충고에 따를 만큼, 그리고 달리 고마움을 증명할 수 없다면 그렇게라도 제 고마움을 증명할 만큼은 제정신이 들었습니다.”"]], [["“You have proved it already,”", "“당신은 그 말로 이미 그것을 증명하셨어요.”"], ["she answered, “by those words.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["Mr. Hartright, concealment is at an end between us.", "“하트라이트 씨, 우리 사이에 숨김은 끝났어요."], ["I cannot affect to hide from you what my sister has unconsciously shown to me.", "제 동생이 저도 모르게 저에게 드러내 보인 것을, 저는 당신께 숨기는 척할 수가 없어요."], ["You must leave us for her sake, as well as for your own.", "당신 자신을 위해서뿐 아니라 그 애를 위해서도 당신은 저희를 떠나셔야 해요."], ["Your presence here, your necessary intimacy with us, harmless as it has been, God knows, in all other respects, has unsteadied her and made her wretched.", "이곳에서의 당신의 존재, 저희와의 어쩔 수 없는 친밀함이, 다른 온갖 면에서 하느님도 아시다시피 아무리 해롭지 않았다 해도, 그 애를 흔들어 놓고 비참하게 만들었어요."], ["I, who love her better than my own life—I, who have learnt to believe in that pure, noble, innocent nature as I believe in my religion—know but too well the secret misery of self-reproach that she has been suffering since the first shadow of a feeling disloyal to her marriage engagement entered her heart in spite of her.", "제 목숨보다 그 애를 더 사랑하는 저, 제 신앙을 믿듯 그 순수하고 고귀하고 순진한 천성을 믿게 된 저는—혼인 약속에 어긋나는 감정의 첫 그림자가 그 애의 뜻과 달리 그 마음에 들어선 이래 그 애가 겪어 온 자책의 은밀한 괴로움을 너무나 잘 알아요."], ["I don’t say—it would be useless to attempt to say it after what has happened—that her engagement has ever had a strong hold on her affections.", "그 애의 약혼이 그 애의 애정을 강하게 붙든 적이 있었다고는—이제 벌어진 일을 두고 그렇게 말하려 해 봐야 소용없겠지만—저는 말하지 않겠어요. 그것은 사랑이 아니라 명예의 약혼이에요."], ["It is an engagement of honour, not of love; her father sanctioned it on his deathbed, two years since; she herself neither welcomed it nor shrank from it—she was content to make it.", "그 애 아버지가 두 해 전 임종 자리에서 그것을 승낙하셨어요. 그 애 자신은 그것을 반기지도 물리치지도 않았어요—그저 기꺼이 받아들였을 뿐이에요."], ["Till you came here she was in the position of hundreds of other women, who marry men without being greatly attracted to them or greatly repelled by them, and who learn to love them (when they don’t learn to hate!) after marriage, instead of before.", "당신이 이곳에 오기 전까지 그 애는, 크게 끌리지도 크게 물리지도 않는 남자와 혼인해, 혼인 전이 아니라 혼인 뒤에 (미워하기를 배우지 않는다면!) 그를 사랑하기를 배우는 수백의 다른 여자들과 같은 처지였어요."], ["I hope more earnestly than words can say—and you should have the self-sacrificing courage to hope too—that the new thoughts and feelings which have disturbed the old calmness and the old content have not taken root too deeply to be ever removed.", "저는 말로 다 할 수 없이 간절히 바라요—당신도 자기를 희생하는 용기로 함께 바라야 해요—그 옛 평온과 옛 만족을 어지럽힌 새 생각과 감정이, 두 번 다시 뽑아낼 수 없을 만큼 너무 깊이 뿌리내리지는 않았기를요."], ["Your absence (if I had less belief in your honour, and your courage, and your sense, I should not trust to them as I am trusting now) your absence will help my efforts, and time will help us all three.", "당신의 부재가 (당신의 명예와 용기와 분별을 덜 믿었다면 제가 지금처럼 그것에 기대지 않았겠지요) 제 노력을 도울 것이고, 시간이 우리 셋 모두를 도울 거예요."], ["It is something to know that my first confidence in you was not all misplaced.", "당신에 대한 제 첫 믿음이 아주 헛짚은 것은 아니었음을 아는 것만으로도 무언가 위안이 돼요."], ["It is something to know that you will not be less honest, less manly, less considerate towards the pupil whose relation to yourself you have had the misfortune to forget, than towards the stranger and the outcast whose appeal to you was not made in vain.”", "당신에게 호소해 온 그 낯설고 버림받은 이에게—그 호소가 헛되지 않았던 이에게—그러했던 만큼, 당신이 스스로와의 관계를 불운하게도 잊어버린 그 제자에게도 덜 정직하거나 덜 사내답거나 덜 사려 깊지 않으리라는 것을 아는 것만으로도 무언가 위안이 돼요.”"]], [["Again the chance reference to the woman in white!", "또다시 흰옷을 입은 여인에 대한 우연한 언급!"], ["Was there no possibility of speaking of Miss Fairlie and of me without raising the memory of Anne Catherick, and setting her between us like a fatality that it was hopeless to avoid?", "앤 캐서릭의 기억을 불러일으켜, 그녀를 피할 길 없는 숙명처럼 우리 사이에 세워 놓지 않고서는, 페얼리 양과 나에 대해 이야기할 가망이 없단 말인가?"]], [["“Tell me what apology I can make to Mr. Fairlie for breaking my engagement,”", "“제가 약속을 깨는 데 대해 페얼리 씨에게 무슨 변명을 하면 될지 말씀해 주세요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Tell me when to go after that apology is accepted.", "“그 변명이 받아들여진 뒤 언제 떠나면 될지 말씀해 주세요."], ["I promise implicit obedience to you and to your advice.”", "당신과 당신의 충고에 온전히 따르기로 약속합니다.”"]], [["“Time is every way of importance,”", "“무엇보다 시간이 중요해요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“You heard me refer this morning to Monday next, and to the necessity of setting the purple room in order.", "“오늘 아침 제가 다음 월요일과 자줏빛 방을 정돈해 두어야 할 필요를 언급한 걸 들으셨지요."], ["The visitor whom we expect on Monday——”", "월요일에 저희가 맞을 손님은——”"]], [["I could not wait for her to be more explicit.", "나는 그녀가 더 자세히 말하기를 기다릴 수 없었다."], ["Knowing what I knew now, the memory of Miss Fairlie’s look and manner at the breakfast-table told me that the expected visitor at Limmeridge House was her future husband.", "이제 내가 아는 것을 알고 나니, 아침 식탁에서의 페얼리 양의 표정과 태도의 기억이, 리머리지 하우스에서 맞을 그 손님이 그녀의 미래의 남편임을 나에게 일러 주었다."], ["I tried to force it back; but something rose within me at that moment stronger than my own will, and I interrupted Miss Halcombe.", "나는 그것을 도로 억누르려 했으나, 그 순간 내 안에서 내 의지보다 강한 무언가가 솟구쳐, 나는 홀콤 양의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“Let me go to-day,”", "“오늘 떠나게 해 주세요.”"], ["I said bitterly.", "내가 쓰라리게 말했다."], ["“The sooner the better.”", "“빠를수록 좋습니다.”"]], [["“No, not to-day,” she replied.", "“아니에요, 오늘은 안 돼요.”"], ["“The only reason you can assign to Mr.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["Fairlie for your departure, before the end of your engagement, must be that an unforeseen necessity compels you to ask his permission to return at once to London.", "“고용 기간이 끝나기 전에 떠나는 것에 대해 당신이 페얼리 씨에게 댈 수 있는 유일한 까닭은, 뜻밖의 사정으로 당장 런던으로 돌아가게 해 달라고 청할 수밖에 없다는 것뿐이에요."], ["You must wait till to-morrow to tell him that, at the time when the post comes in, because he will then understand the sudden change in your plans, by associating it with the arrival of a letter from London.", "그걸 말하는 건 내일, 우편이 오는 시각까지 기다리셔야 해요. 그래야 그분이 런던에서 온 편지의 도착과 결부시켜 당신 계획의 갑작스러운 변화를 이해하실 테니까요."], ["It is miserable and sickening to descend to deceit, even of the most harmless kind—but I know Mr.", "아무리 해롭지 않은 종류라도 속임수로 내려가는 것은 비참하고 역겨운 일이에요—하지만 저는 페얼리 씨를 알아요."], ["Fairlie, and if you once excite his suspicions that you are trifling with him, he will refuse to release you.", "당신이 자기를 가지고 논다는 의심을 한번 그분 안에 불러일으키면, 그분은 당신을 놓아주기를 거부하실 거예요."], ["Speak to him on Friday morning: occupy yourself afterwards (for the sake of your own interests with your employer) in leaving your unfinished work in as little confusion as possible, and quit this place on Saturday.", "그분께는 금요일 아침에 말씀드리세요. 그다음에는 (고용주와의 당신 자신의 이익을 위해) 끝내지 못한 일을 되도록 어수선하지 않게 남겨 두는 데 시간을 쓰시고, 토요일에 이곳을 떠나세요."], ["It will be time enough then, Mr. Hartright, for you, and for all of us.”", "그때면 당신에게도, 우리 모두에게도 충분히 이른 때일 거예요, 하트라이트 씨.”"]], [["Before I could assure her that she might depend on my acting in the strictest accordance with her wishes, we were both startled by advancing footsteps in the shrubbery.", "당신 뜻에 더없이 엄격히 따라 행동할 것을 믿으셔도 된다고 그녀에게 다짐하기도 전에, 우리는 둘 다 관목숲에서 다가오는 발소리에 소스라쳤다."], ["Some one was coming from the house to seek for us!", "누군가 우리를 찾으러 저택에서 오고 있었다!"], ["I felt the blood rush into my cheeks and then leave them again.", "나는 피가 내 뺨으로 몰렸다가 다시 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Could the third person who was fast approaching us, at such a time and under such circumstances, be Miss Fairlie?", "이런 때, 이런 형편에 우리에게 빠르게 다가오는 그 세 번째 사람이 페얼리 양일 수 있을까?"]], [["It was a relief—so sadly, so hopelessly was my position towards her changed already—it was absolutely a relief to me, when the person who had disturbed us appeared at the entrance of the summer-house, and proved to be only Miss Fairlie’s maid.", "그것은 안도였다—그녀를 향한 내 처지가 이미 그토록 서글프게, 그토록 가망 없이 바뀌어 버렸기에—우리를 방해한 그 사람이 정자 입구에 나타나 다름 아닌 페얼리 양의 하녀임이 드러났을 때, 그것은 나에게 정말이지 안도였다."]], [["“Could I speak to you for a moment, miss?”", "“잠깐 말씀 좀 드려도 될까요, 아씨?”"], ["said the girl, in rather a flurried, unsettled manner.", "소녀가 다소 허둥대고 어수선한 태도로 말했다."]], [["Miss Halcombe descended the steps into the shrubbery, and walked aside a few paces with the maid.", "홀콤 양은 계단을 내려가 관목숲으로 가더니, 하녀와 함께 몇 걸음 옆으로 물러났다."]], [["Left by myself, my mind reverted, with a sense of forlorn wretchedness which it is not in any words that I can find to describe, to my approaching return to the solitude and the despair of my lonely London home.", "혼자 남겨진 나의 마음은, 어떤 말로도 묘사할 수 없는 의지가지없는 비참함의 느낌과 함께, 곧 돌아가야 할 외로운 런던 집의 고독과 절망으로 되돌아갔다."], ["Thoughts of my kind old mother, and of my sister, who had rejoiced with her so innocently over my prospects in Cumberland—thoughts whose long banishment from my heart it was now my shame and my reproach to realise for the first time—came back to me with the loving mournfulness of old, neglected friends.", "나의 다정한 늙은 어머니와, 컴벌랜드에서의 내 앞날을 그토록 순진하게 함께 기뻐하던 누이에 대한 생각—그것들이 내 가슴에서 그토록 오래 쫓겨나 있었음을 이제 처음으로 깨닫는 것이 나의 부끄러움이자 자책인 그런 생각들이, 오래도록 소홀히 대한 옛 벗들의 정겨운 서글픔과 함께 나에게 되돌아왔다."], ["My mother and my sister, what would they feel when I returned to them from my broken engagement, with the confession of my miserable secret—they who had parted from me so hopefully on that last happy night in the Hampstead cottage!", "나의 어머니와 누이, 내가 깨진 약속에서 돌아와 내 비참한 비밀을 고백하며 그들에게 돌아갈 때 그들은 무엇을 느낄까—햄스테드 오두막에서의 그 마지막 행복한 밤에 그토록 희망에 차 나와 헤어졌던 그들이!"]], [["Anne Catherick again!", "또 앤 캐서릭이라니!"], ["Even the memory of the farewell evening with my mother and my sister could not return to me now unconnected with that other memory of the moonlight walk back to London.", "이제는 어머니와 누이와의 작별 저녁의 기억조차, 런던으로 돌아오던 그 달빛 산책의 다른 기억과 결부되지 않고서는 나에게 되돌아올 수 없었다."], ["What did it mean?", "그것은 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Were that woman and I to meet once more?", "그 여인과 내가 다시 한번 만나게 될 것인가?"], ["It was possible, at the least.", "적어도 그럴 가능성은 있었다."], ["Did she know that I lived in London?", "그녀는 내가 런던에 산다는 것을 알았을까?"], ["Yes; I had told her so, either before or after that strange question of hers, when she had asked me so distrustfully if I knew many men of the rank of Baronet.", "그렇다. 나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다—준남작 지위의 남자를 많이 아느냐고 그토록 미심쩍게 물은 그 기이한 물음의 전이든 후든."], ["Either before or after—my mind was not calm enough, then, to remember which.", "전이든 후든—그때 내 마음은 어느 쪽인지 기억할 만큼 차분하지 못했다."]], [["A few minutes elapsed before Miss Halcombe dismissed the maid and came back to me.", "몇 분이 지난 뒤 홀콤 양이 하녀를 보내고 나에게 돌아왔다."], ["She, too, looked flurried and unsettled now.", "이제 그녀도 허둥대고 어수선해 보였다."]], [["“We have arranged all that is necessary, Mr.", "“필요한 건 다 정했어요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["Hartright,” she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“We have understood each other, as friends should, and we may go back at once to the house.", "“우리는 벗이 마땅히 그러해야 하듯 서로를 이해했으니, 이제 곧바로 저택으로 돌아가도 되겠어요."], ["To tell you the truth, I am uneasy about Laura.", "사실을 말하자면, 저는 로라가 걱정돼요."], ["She has sent to say she wants to see me directly, and the maid reports that her mistress is apparently very much agitated by a letter that she has received this morning—the same letter, no doubt, which I sent on to the house before we came here.”", "그 애가 당장 저를 보고 싶다고 사람을 보냈는데, 하녀 말로는 오늘 아침 받은 편지 때문에 그 애가 몹시 동요한 듯하다는군요—틀림없이, 우리가 이곳에 오기 전에 제가 저택으로 보낸 바로 그 편지겠지요.”"]], [["We retraced our steps together hastily along the shrubbery path.", "우리는 함께 서둘러 관목숲 오솔길을 되짚어 갔다."], ["Although Miss Halcombe had ended all that she thought it necessary to say on her side, I had not ended all that I wanted to say on mine.", "홀콤 양은 제 쪽에서 할 필요가 있다고 여긴 말을 다 마쳤으나, 나는 내 쪽에서 하고 싶은 말을 다 마치지 못했다."], ["From the moment when I had discovered that the expected visitor at Limmeridge was Miss Fairlie’s future husband, I had felt a bitter curiosity, a burning envious eagerness, to know who he was.", "리머리지에서 맞을 그 손님이 페얼리 양의 미래의 남편임을 알아챈 순간부터, 나는 그가 누구인지 알고 싶은 쓰라린 호기심, 불타는 시샘 어린 조바심을 느끼고 있었다."], ["It was possible that a future opportunity of putting the question might not easily offer, so I risked asking it on our way back to the house.", "그 물음을 던질 앞으로의 기회가 쉽게 오지 않을 수도 있는지라, 나는 저택으로 돌아가는 길에 위험을 무릅쓰고 그것을 물었다."]], [["“Now that you are kind enough to tell me we have understood each other, Miss Halcombe,”", "“우리가 서로를 이해했다고 이제 친절히 말씀해 주셨으니, 홀콤 양.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “now that you are sure of my gratitude for your forbearance and my obedience to your wishes, may I venture to ask who”—(I hesitated—I had forced myself to think of him, but it was harder still to speak of him, as her promised husband)—“who the gentleman engaged to Miss Fairlie is?”", "“당신의 관용에 대한 제 고마움과 당신 뜻에 대한 제 순종을 이제 확신하시니, 감히 여쭤도 될까요—누가”—(나는 머뭇거렸다—억지로 그를 떠올리게 만들었으나, 그를 그녀의 정혼자로서 입에 올리는 것은 더더욱 힘들었다)—“페얼리 양과 약혼한 그 신사가 누구인지요?”"]], [["Her mind was evidently occupied with the message she had received from her sister.", "그녀의 마음은 동생에게서 받은 전갈에 온통 쏠려 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["She answered in a hasty, absent way—", "그녀는 서둘러, 넋 놓은 채로 대답했다—"]], [["“A gentleman of large property in Hampshire.”", "“햄프셔에 큰 재산을 가진 신사예요.”"]], [["Hampshire!", "햄프셔라니!"], ["Anne Catherick’s native place.", "앤 캐서릭의 고향."], ["Again, and yet again, the woman in white.", "또다시, 그리고 거듭 또다시, 흰옷을 입은 여인."], ["There WAS a fatality in it.", "거기에는 숙명이 있었다."]], [["“And his name?”", "“그리고 그 이름은요?”"], ["I said, as quietly and indifferently as I could.", "나는 되도록 조용하고 무심하게 말했다."]], [["“Sir Percival Glyde.”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이에요.”"]], [["SIR—Sir Percival!", "경—퍼시벌 경이라니!"], ["Anne Catherick’s question—that suspicious question about the men of the rank of Baronet whom I might happen to know—had hardly been dismissed from my mind by Miss Halcombe’s return to me in the summer-house, before it was recalled again by her own answer.", "내가 어쩌다 알지도 모를 준남작 지위의 남자에 대한 앤 캐서릭의 물음—그 미심쩍은 물음이, 정자에서 홀콤 양이 나에게 돌아옴으로써 내 마음에서 채 물러가기도 전에, 그녀 자신의 대답으로 다시 불려 나왔다."], ["I stopped suddenly, and looked at her.", "나는 별안간 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Sir Percival Glyde,”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이에요.”"], ["she repeated, imagining that I had not heard her former reply.", "그녀는 내가 앞서 한 대답을 못 들은 줄 알고 되풀이했다."]], [["“Knight, or Baronet?”", "“기사(騎士)입니까, 준남작입니까?”"], ["I asked, with an agitation that I could hide no longer.", "나는 더는 감출 수 없는 동요와 함께 물었다."]], [["She paused for a moment, and then answered, rather coldly—", "그녀는 잠시 멈추었다가, 다소 냉랭하게 대답했다—"]], [["“Baronet, of course.”", "“준남작이지요, 물론.”"]], [["# XI", "# XI"]], [["Not a word more was said, on either side, as we walked back to the house.", "저택으로 돌아오는 동안 양쪽 어느 편에서도 더는 한 마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Miss Halcombe hastened immediately to her sister’s room, and I withdrew to my studio to set in order all of Mr. Fairlie’s drawings that I had not yet mounted and restored before I resigned them to the care of other hands.", "홀콤 양은 곧장 동생의 방으로 서둘러 갔고, 나는 내 화실로 물러나 아직 배접하고 손보지 못한 페얼리 씨의 그림들을 다른 사람의 손에 넘기기 전에 모두 정리해 두려 했다."], ["Thoughts that I had hitherto restrained, thoughts that made my position harder than ever to endure, crowded on me now that I was alone.", "여태 억눌러 왔던 생각들, 내 처지를 그 어느 때보다 견디기 어렵게 만드는 생각들이, 혼자가 된 지금 나에게 몰려들었다."]], [["She was engaged to be married, and her future husband was Sir Percival Glyde.", "그녀는 약혼한 몸이었고, 그녀의 미래의 남편은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이었다."], ["A man of the rank of Baronet, and the owner of property in Hampshire.", "준남작의 지위를 지닌 사람이며, 햄프셔에 재산을 소유한 사람."]], [["There were hundreds of baronets in England, and dozens of landowners in Hampshire.", "영국에는 준남작이 수백 명 있었고, 햄프셔에는 지주가 수십 명 있었다."], ["Judging by the ordinary rules of evidence, I had not the shadow of a reason, thus far, for connecting Sir Percival Glyde with the suspicious words of inquiry that had been spoken to me by the woman in white.", "통상적인 증거의 규칙으로 판단한다면, 나에게는 여기까지로는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 흰옷을 입은 여인이 나에게 던졌던 그 수상쩍은 물음의 말들과 연결시킬 근거가 그림자만큼도 없었다."], ["And yet, I did connect him with them.", "그런데도 나는 그를 그 말들과 연결시키고 있었다."], ["Was it because he had now become associated in my mind with Miss Fairlie, Miss Fairlie being, in her turn, associated with Anne Catherick, since the night when I had discovered the ominous likeness between them?", "그것은 이제 그가 내 마음속에서 페얼리 양과 결부되어 버렸고, 페얼리 양은 또 그녀대로, 두 사람 사이의 그 불길한 닮음을 내가 발견한 그 밤 이래로 앤 캐서릭과 결부되어 있었기 때문일까?"], ["Had the events of the morning so unnerved me already that I was at the mercy of any delusion which common chances and common coincidences might suggest to my imagination?", "그날 아침의 일들이 나를 이미 그토록 무너뜨려 놓아서, 흔한 우연과 흔한 일치가 내 상상에 던져 주는 어떤 망상에도 내가 휘둘리게 된 것일까?"], ["Impossible to say.", "뭐라 말할 수 없었다."], ["I could only feel that what had passed between Miss Halcombe and myself, on our way from the summer-house, had affected me very strangely.", "다만 여름 별장에서 돌아오는 길에 홀콤 양과 나 사이에 오간 일이 나를 몹시 이상하게 뒤흔들어 놓았다는 것만은 느낄 수 있었다."], ["The foreboding of some undiscoverable danger lying hid from us all in the darkness of the future was strong on me.", "미래의 어둠 속에 숨어 우리 모두에게 보이지 않는 어떤 위험이 있다는 예감이 나를 강하게 사로잡았다."], ["The doubt whether I was not linked already to a chain of events which even my approaching departure from Cumberland would be powerless to snap asunder—the doubt whether we any of us saw the end as the end would really be—gathered more and more darkly over my mind.", "내가 이미, 컴벌랜드를 곧 떠난다 해도 끊어 낼 힘이 없는 어떤 사건의 사슬에 매여 버린 것은 아닐까 하는 의심—우리 가운데 누구도 그 끝을 실제 그대로의 끝으로 보고 있지 못한 것은 아닐까 하는 의심—이 점점 더 어둡게 내 마음 위로 몰려들었다."], ["Poignant as it was, the sense of suffering caused by the miserable end of my brief, presumptuous love seemed to be blunted and deadened by the still stronger sense of something obscurely impending, something invisibly threatening, that Time was holding over our heads.", "짧고도 주제넘었던 내 사랑의 비참한 종말이 안겨 준 고통의 감각은, 그토록 사무치는 것이었음에도, 무언가가 어렴풋이 다가오고 있다는, 무언가가 보이지 않게 위협하고 있다는, 시간이 우리 머리 위에 그것을 들고 있다는 더 강한 감각에 눌려 무디어지고 잦아드는 듯했다."]], [["I had been engaged with the drawings little more than half an hour, when there was a knock at the door.", "그림들을 붙들고 앉은 지 반 시간 남짓 되었을 때, 문 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["It opened, on my answering; and, to my surprise, Miss Halcombe entered the room.", "내가 대답하자 문이 열렸고, 놀랍게도 홀콤 양이 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["Her manner was angry and agitated.", "그녀의 태도는 노기에 차 있었고 동요해 있었다."], ["She caught up a chair for herself before I could give her one, and sat down in it, close at my side.", "내가 의자를 권하기도 전에 그녀는 스스로 의자를 끌어당겨, 내 곁에 바짝 붙여 놓고 앉았다."]], [["“Mr. Hartright,”", "“하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she said, “I had hoped that all painful subjects of conversation were exhausted between us, for to-day at least.", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 적어도 오늘 하루만큼은 우리 사이에 오갈 괴로운 이야깃거리가 다 떨어졌기를 바랐어요."], ["But it is not to be so.", "그런데 그렇게 되지가 않는군요."], ["There is some underhand villainy at work to frighten my sister about her approaching marriage.", "다가오는 혼사를 두고 제 동생을 겁주려는 어떤 음험한 흉계가 벌어지고 있어요."], ["You saw me send the gardener on to the house, with a letter addressed, in a strange handwriting, to Miss Fairlie?”", "제가 정원사를 저택으로 올려 보내는 걸 보셨지요, 낯선 필체로 페얼리 양 앞으로 주소가 적힌 편지를 들려서요?”"]], [["“Certainly.”", "“보았지요.”"]], [["“The letter is an anonymous letter—a vile attempt to injure Sir Percival Glyde in my sister’s estimation.", "“그 편지는 익명의 편지예요—퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 제 동생의 눈에 깎아내리려는 비열한 수작이지요."], ["It has so agitated and alarmed her that I have had the greatest possible difficulty in composing her spirits sufficiently to allow me to leave her room and come here.", "그것이 그 애를 어찌나 동요시키고 놀라게 했던지, 제가 그 애의 방을 나와 여기로 올 수 있을 만큼 마음을 가라앉혀 주는 데 이루 말할 수 없는 애를 먹었답니다."], ["I know this is a family matter on which I ought not to consult you, and in which you can feel no concern or interest——”", "이것이 제가 당신과 의논해서는 안 될 집안일이고, 당신으로서는 아무런 관심도 이해관계도 느낄 수 없는 일이라는 건 압니다만——”"]], [["“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe.", "“무슨 말씀을요, 홀콤 양."], ["I feel the strongest possible concern and interest in anything that affects Miss Fairlie’s happiness or yours.”", "페얼리 양의 행복이나 당신의 행복에 관계되는 일이라면 무엇에든 저는 더할 나위 없이 깊은 관심과 이해를 느낍니다.”"]], [["“I am glad to hear you say so.", "“그렇게 말씀해 주시니 기뻐요."], ["You are the only person in the house, or out of it, who can advise me. Mr.", "이 집 안에서든 밖에서든 저에게 조언해 줄 수 있는 분은 당신뿐이에요."], ["Fairlie, in his state of health and with his horror of difficulties and mysteries of all kinds, is not to be thought of.", "페얼리 씨는 그 건강 상태에다, 온갖 종류의 성가신 일과 알 수 없는 일이라면 질색을 하시니 아예 생각할 수도 없고요."], ["The clergyman is a good, weak man, who knows nothing out of the routine of his duties; and our neighbours are just the sort of comfortable, jog-trot acquaintances whom one cannot disturb in times of trouble and danger.", "목사님은 선량하지만 무른 분이라 제 직분의 판에 박힌 일 말고는 아무것도 모르시지요. 그리고 이웃들은 딱, 곤경과 위험의 때에 번거롭게 할 수 없는 그런 편안하고 느긋한 지인들이고요."], ["What I want to know is this: ought I at once to take such steps as I can to discover the writer of the letter?", "제가 알고 싶은 건 이거예요. 제가 지금 당장 이 편지를 쓴 사람을 알아낼 수 있는 조치를 취해야 할까요?"], ["or ought I to wait, and apply to Mr. Fairlie’s legal adviser to-morrow?", "아니면 기다렸다가 내일 페얼리 씨의 법률 고문에게 부탁해야 할까요?"], ["It is a question—perhaps a very important one—of gaining or losing a day.", "하루를 벌 것이냐 잃을 것이냐 하는—어쩌면 아주 중대할지도 모르는—문제예요."], ["Tell me what you think, Mr. Hartright.", "어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주세요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["If necessity had not already obliged me to take you into my confidence under very delicate circumstances, even my helpless situation would, perhaps, be no excuse for me.", "이미 필요에 몰려 아주 미묘한 사정 아래 당신을 제 속내로 끌어들이지 않았더라면, 이렇게 어찌할 바 모르는 제 처지조차도 아마 변명이 되지 못했을 거예요."], ["But as things are I cannot surely be wrong, after all that has passed between us, in forgetting that you are a friend of only three months’ standing.”", "하지만 사정이 이러하니, 우리 사이에 그 모든 일이 지나간 마당에, 당신이 겨우 석 달 사귄 벗이라는 사실을 잊는다 해도 제가 그르지는 않겠지요.”"]], [["She gave me the letter.", "그녀가 나에게 편지를 건넸다."], ["It began abruptly, without any preliminary form of address, as follows—", "편지는 아무런 서두의 인사말도 없이 불쑥 이렇게 시작되었다—"]], [["“Do you believe in dreams?", "“당신은 꿈을 믿나요?"], ["I hope, for your own sake, that you do.", "당신 자신을 위해서, 나는 당신이 믿기를 바랍니다."], ["See what Scripture says about dreams and their fulfilment (Genesis xl. 8, xli.", "성경이 꿈과 그 이루어짐에 대해 무어라 하는지 보세요(창세기 40장 8절, 41장 25절, 다니엘 4장 18절에서 25절)."], ["25; Daniel iv. 18-25), and take the warning I send you before it is too late.", "그리고 너무 늦기 전에 내가 보내는 경고를 받아들이세요."]], [["“Last night I dreamed about you, Miss Fairlie.", "“어젯밤 나는 당신 꿈을 꾸었어요, 페얼리 양."], ["I dreamed that I was standing inside the communion rails of a church—I on one side of the altar-table, and the clergyman, with his surplice and his prayer-book, on the other.", "나는 어느 교회의 성찬 난간 안쪽에 서 있는 꿈을 꾸었지요—나는 제단 탁자의 한쪽에, 그리고 성직자는 흰 예복을 걸치고 기도서를 든 채 다른 쪽에 서 있었어요."]], [["“After a time there walked towards us, down the aisle of the church, a man and a woman, coming to be married.", "“얼마 뒤 교회의 통로를 따라 한 남자와 한 여자가 혼인하러 우리 쪽으로 걸어왔습니다."], ["You were the woman.", "그 여자는 당신이었어요."], ["You looked so pretty and innocent in your beautiful white silk dress, and your long white lace veil, that my heart felt for you, and the tears came into my eyes.", "당신은 그 아름다운 흰 비단 드레스와 길고 하얀 레이스 면사포 속에서 어찌나 곱고 순결해 보였던지, 내 가슴이 당신 때문에 아팠고 눈에 눈물이 고였답니다."]], [["“They were tears of pity, young lady, that heaven blesses and instead of falling from my eyes like the everyday tears that we all of us shed, they turned into two rays of light which slanted nearer and nearer to the man standing at the altar with you, till they touched his breast.", "“그것은 연민의 눈물이었어요, 아가씨, 하늘이 축복하는 눈물이지요. 그리고 그 눈물은 우리 모두가 흘리는 여느 날의 눈물처럼 내 눈에서 떨어지는 대신, 두 줄기 빛으로 변해 당신과 함께 제단에 서 있는 그 남자에게로 점점 더 가까이 비스듬히 뻗어 가더니, 마침내 그의 가슴에 닿았습니다."], ["The two rays sprang in arches like two rainbows between me and him.", "두 줄기 빛은 나와 그 사람 사이에 두 개의 무지개처럼 아치를 그리며 솟아올랐어요."], ["I looked along them, and I saw down into his inmost heart.", "나는 그 빛을 따라 바라보았고, 그의 가장 깊은 마음속을 들여다보았습니다."]], [["“The outside of the man you were marrying was fair enough to see.", "“당신이 혼인하려던 그 남자의 겉모습은 보기에 그런대로 훤했어요."], ["He was neither tall nor short—he was a little below the middle size.", "키가 크지도 작지도 않았지요—중간 키에서 조금 못 미쳤어요."], ["A light, active, high-spirited man—about five-and-forty years old, to look at.", "몸이 가볍고 활달하고 기운이 넘치는 남자였고—보기에 마흔댓 살쯤 되었습니다."], ["He had a pale face, and was bald over the forehead, but had dark hair on the rest of his head.", "얼굴은 창백하고 이마 위쪽으로는 머리가 벗어졌으나, 나머지 머리에는 검은 머리카락이 있었어요."], ["His beard was shaven on his chin, but was let to grow, of a fine rich brown, on his cheeks and his upper lip.", "턱의 수염은 밀었지만 뺨과 윗입술에는 곱고 짙은 갈색으로 기르도록 두었더군요."], ["His eyes were brown too, and very bright; his nose straight and handsome and delicate enough to have done for a woman’s.", "눈도 갈색이었고 아주 반짝였습니다. 코는 곧고 잘생기고 여자의 것이라 해도 좋을 만큼 섬세했어요."], ["His hands the same.", "손도 그러했지요."], ["He was troubled from time to time with a dry hacking cough, and when he put up his white right hand to his mouth, he showed the red scar of an old wound across the back of it.", "그는 이따금 마른기침에 시달렸고, 흰 오른손을 입에 갖다 댈 때 손등을 가로지른 오랜 상처의 붉은 흉터가 드러났습니다. 내가 꿈에서 본 사람이 바로 그 사람인가요?"], ["Have I dreamt of the right man?", "당신이 가장 잘 알겠지요, 페얼리 양."], ["You know best, Miss Fairlie; and you can say if I was deceived or not.", "내가 속은 것인지 아닌지는 당신이 말할 수 있을 거예요."], ["Read next, what I saw beneath the outside—I entreat you, read, and profit.", "이제 내가 그 겉모습 아래에서 본 것을 읽어 보세요—간청하니, 읽고 깨우침을 얻으세요."]], [["“I looked along the two rays of light, and I saw down into his inmost heart.", "“나는 그 두 줄기 빛을 따라 바라보았고, 그의 가장 깊은 마음속을 들여다보았습니다."], ["It was black as night, and on it were written, in the red flaming letters which are the handwriting of the fallen angel, ‘Without pity and without remorse.", "그것은 밤처럼 새까맸고, 그 위에는 타락한 천사의 필적인 붉은 불꽃 글자로 이렇게 적혀 있었어요. ‘연민도 없고 뉘우침도 없다."], ["He has strewn with misery the paths of others, and he will live to strew with misery the path of this woman by his side.’ I read that, and then the rays of light shifted and pointed over his shoulder; and there, behind him, stood a fiend laughing.", "그는 다른 이들의 길에 비참함을 뿌려 왔고, 앞으로 살아가며 제 곁에 선 이 여자의 길에도 비참함을 뿌리리라.’ 나는 그것을 읽었고, 그러자 빛줄기가 옮겨 가 그의 어깨 너머를 가리켰습니다. 그리고 거기, 그의 뒤에는 악귀 하나가 웃으며 서 있었어요."], ["And the rays of light shifted once more, and pointed over your shoulder; and there behind you, stood an angel weeping.", "다시 빛줄기가 옮겨 가 당신의 어깨 너머를 가리켰습니다. 그리고 거기, 당신 뒤에는 천사 하나가 울며 서 있었어요."], ["And the rays of light shifted for the third time, and pointed straight between you and that man.", "그리고 빛줄기가 세 번째로 옮겨 가, 당신과 그 남자 사이를 곧장 가리켰습니다."], ["They widened and widened, thrusting you both asunder, one from the other.", "빛은 넓어지고 또 넓어지며 두 사람을 서로에게서 떼어 밀어냈어요."], ["And the clergyman looked for the marriage-service in vain: it was gone out of the book, and he shut up the leaves, and put it from him in despair.", "그리고 성직자는 혼인 예식문을 찾았으나 헛일이었지요. 그것은 책에서 사라져 버렸고, 그는 책장을 덮어 절망 속에 내려놓았습니다."], ["And I woke with my eyes full of tears and my heart beating—for I believe in dreams.", "그리고 나는 눈에 눈물이 가득하고 가슴이 두근거리는 채로 깨어났어요—나는 꿈을 믿으니까요."]], [["“Believe too, Miss Fairlie—I beg of you, for your own sake, believe as I do.", "“당신도 믿으세요, 페얼리 양—당신 자신을 위해 간청하니, 나처럼 믿으세요."], ["Joseph and Daniel, and others in Scripture, believed in dreams.", "요셉과 다니엘, 그리고 성경 속의 다른 이들도 꿈을 믿었습니다."], ["Inquire into the past life of that man with the scar on his hand, before you say the words that make you his miserable wife.", "그 손에 흉터가 있는 남자의 지난 삶을 캐어 보세요, 당신을 그의 가련한 아내로 만들 그 말을 입에 담기 전에 말이에요."], ["I don’t give you this warning on my account, but on yours.", "나는 이 경고를 나를 위해 하는 것이 아니라 당신을 위해 하는 것입니다."], ["I have an interest in your well-being that will live as long as I draw breath.", "나는 당신의 안녕에, 내가 숨을 쉬는 한 사그라지지 않을 관심을 두고 있어요."], ["Your mother’s daughter has a tender place in my heart—for your mother was my first, my best, my only friend.”", "당신 어머니의 딸은 내 마음속에 애틋한 자리를 차지하고 있답니다—당신 어머니는 나의 첫 번째, 나의 가장 좋은, 나의 단 하나뿐인 벗이었으니까요.”"]], [["There the extraordinary letter ended, without signature of any sort.", "그 기이한 편지는 어떤 종류의 서명도 없이 거기서 끝나 있었다."]], [["The handwriting afforded no prospect of a clue.", "필체는 실마리를 잡을 가망을 조금도 주지 않았다."], ["It was traced on ruled lines, in the cramped, conventional, copy-book character technically termed “small hand.”", "그것은 줄이 그어진 종이 위에, 전문 용어로 “작은 글씨체”라 부르는, 옹색하고 판에 박힌 습자책 글씨로 적혀 있었다."], ["It was feeble and faint, and defaced by blots, but had otherwise nothing to distinguish it.", "힘이 없고 흐릿했으며 잉크 얼룩으로 지저분해져 있었으나, 그 밖에는 이렇다 할 특징이 없었다."]], [["“That is not an illiterate letter,”", "“이건 무식한 사람의 편지가 아니에요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, “and at the same time, it is surely too incoherent to be the letter of an educated person in the higher ranks of life.", "홀콤 양이 말했다. “그러면서도 상류층의 교육받은 사람이 쓴 편지라기에는 분명 너무 두서가 없고요."], ["The reference to the bridal dress and veil, and other little expressions, seem to point to it as the production of some woman.", "혼례복과 면사포를 들먹인 대목이며 자잘한 표현들을 보면 어떤 여자의 손에서 나온 것 같아요."], ["What do you think, Mr. Hartright?”", "어떻게 생각하세요, 하트라이트 씨?”"]], [["“I think so too.", "“저도 그렇게 생각합니다."], ["It seems to me to be not only the letter of a woman, but of a woman whose mind must be——”", "제가 보기에 이건 단지 여자의 편지일 뿐 아니라, 그 정신이 필시——한 여자의 편지입니다.”"]], [["“Deranged?”", "“정신이 온전치 못한?”"], ["suggested Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말을 받았다."], ["“It struck me in that light too.”", "“저에게도 그렇게 보였어요.”"]], [["I did not answer.", "나는 대답하지 않았다."], ["While I was speaking, my eyes rested on the last sentence of the letter: “Your mother’s daughter has a tender place in my heart—for your mother was my first, my best, my only friend.”", "말을 하는 동안 내 눈은 편지의 마지막 문장에 머물러 있었다. “당신 어머니의 딸은 내 마음속에 애틋한 자리를 차지하고 있답니다—당신 어머니는 나의 첫 번째, 나의 가장 좋은, 나의 단 하나뿐인 벗이었으니까요.”"], ["Those words and the doubt which had just escaped me as to the sanity of the writer of the letter, acting together on my mind, suggested an idea, which I was literally afraid to express openly, or even to encourage secretly.", "그 말과, 편지를 쓴 사람의 제정신 여부에 대해 방금 내 입에서 새어 나온 의심이 함께 내 마음에 작용하여 하나의 생각을 떠올리게 했는데, 나는 그것을 드러내 놓고 말하기는커녕 남몰래 품는 것조차 문자 그대로 두려웠다."], ["I began to doubt whether my own faculties were not in danger of losing their balance.", "나는 내 자신의 판단력이 균형을 잃을 위험에 처한 것은 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다."], ["It seemed almost like a monomania to be tracing back everything strange that happened, everything unexpected that was said, always to the same hidden source and the same sinister influence.", "일어나는 온갖 이상한 일과 튀어나오는 온갖 뜻밖의 말을 언제나 똑같은 숨은 근원과 똑같은 불길한 영향으로 거슬러 올라가 잇는 것은 거의 편집광이나 다름없어 보였다."], ["I resolved, this time, in defence of my own courage and my own sense, to come to no decision that plain fact did not warrant, and to turn my back resolutely on everything that tempted me in the shape of surmise.", "나는 이번만큼은 나 자신의 용기와 나 자신의 분별을 지키기 위해, 명백한 사실이 뒷받침하지 않는 어떤 결론에도 이르지 않기로, 그리고 추측의 모습으로 나를 꾀는 모든 것에 단호히 등을 돌리기로 결심했다."]], [["“If we have any chance of tracing the person who has written this,”", "“이것을 쓴 사람을 추적할 가망이 조금이라도 있다면.”"], ["I said, returning the letter to Miss Halcombe, “there can be no harm in seizing our opportunity the moment it offers.", "나는 편지를 홀콤 양에게 돌려주며 말했다. “기회가 오는 그 순간에 붙잡는 편이 나쁠 것은 없겠지요."], ["I think we ought to speak to the gardener again about the elderly woman who gave him the letter, and then to continue our inquiries in the village.", "제 생각에는 정원사에게 그 편지를 준 나이 든 여자에 대해 다시 한번 물어보고, 그런 뒤에 마을에서 계속 알아보아야 할 것 같습니다."], ["But first let me ask a question.", "그런데 먼저 한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다."], ["You mentioned just now the alternative of consulting Mr. Fairlie’s legal adviser to-morrow.", "방금 내일 페얼리 씨의 법률 고문에게 부탁하는 쪽도 있다고 말씀하셨지요."], ["Is there no possibility of communicating with him earlier?", "그분과 더 일찍 연락할 방법은 없습니까?"], ["Why not to-day?”", "어째서 오늘은 안 됩니까?”"]], [["“I can only explain,”", "“설명을 하자면.”"], ["replied Miss Halcombe, “by entering into certain particulars, connected with my sister’s marriage-engagement, which I did not think it necessary or desirable to mention to you this morning.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다. “동생의 혼약과 얽힌 몇 가지 사정을 이야기해야만 하는데, 오늘 아침에는 그것을 당신에게 말할 필요도 없고 그러는 게 좋지도 않다고 생각했지요."], ["One of Sir Percival Glyde’s objects in coming here on Monday, is to fix the period of his marriage, which has hitherto been left quite unsettled.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 월요일에 이곳으로 오는 목적 가운데 하나는, 여태 전혀 정해지지 않은 채로 남아 있는 혼례 시기를 정하려는 거예요."], ["He is anxious that the event should take place before the end of the year.”", "그분은 그 일이 올해가 가기 전에 치러지기를 몹시 바라고 있어요.”"]], [["“Does Miss Fairlie know of that wish?”", "“페얼리 양이 그 바람을 알고 있습니까?”"], ["I asked eagerly.", "나는 다급히 물었다."]], [["“She has no suspicion of it, and after what has happened, I shall not take the responsibility upon myself of enlightening her.", "“그 애는 전혀 눈치채지 못하고 있고, 이런 일이 있은 마당에 제가 그 애에게 알려 주는 책임을 떠맡지는 않겠어요."], ["Sir Percival has only mentioned his views to Mr. Fairlie, who has told me himself that he is ready and anxious, as Laura’s guardian, to forward them.", "퍼시벌 경은 자기 뜻을 페얼리 씨에게만 말했고, 페얼리 씨는 로라의 후견인으로서 그 뜻을 밀어 줄 준비도 되어 있고 그러기를 바란다고 저에게 직접 말씀하셨어요."], ["He has written to London, to the family solicitor, Mr. Gilmore.", "그분은 런던에 있는 집안 변호사 길모어 씨에게 편지를 보내셨고요."], ["Mr. Gilmore happens to be away in Glasgow on business, and he has replied by proposing to stop at Limmeridge House on his way back to town.", "마침 길모어 씨는 일 때문에 글래스고에 가 있어서, 런던으로 돌아오는 길에 리머리지 하우스에 들르겠다는 답장을 보내왔어요."], ["He will arrive to-morrow, and will stay with us a few days, so as to allow Sir Percival time to plead his own cause.", "내일 도착해서 며칠 우리와 함께 머물 텐데, 퍼시벌 경이 자기 뜻을 펼 시간을 주기 위해서지요."], ["If he succeeds, Mr. Gilmore will then return to London, taking with him his instructions for my sister’s marriage-settlement.", "그분이 뜻을 이루면 길모어 씨는 그때 런던으로 돌아가면서 제 동생의 혼인 재산 계약에 대한 지시를 가지고 갈 거예요."], ["You understand now, Mr. Hartright, why I speak of waiting to take legal advice until to-morrow?", "이제 제가 어째서 법률 자문을 내일까지 기다리자고 말하는지 아시겠지요, 하트라이트 씨?"], ["Mr. Gilmore is the old and tried friend of two generations of Fairlies, and we can trust him, as we could trust no one else.”", "길모어 씨는 페얼리 가문 두 대에 걸친 오래되고 검증된 벗이고, 우리는 그분을 다른 누구에게도 그러지 못할 만큼 믿을 수 있어요.”"]], [["The marriage-settlement!", "혼인 재산 계약!"], ["The mere hearing of those two words stung me with a jealous despair that was poison to my higher and better instincts.", "그 두 마디를 듣기만 했는데도 나는 질투 어린 절망에 찔렸고, 그것은 내 안의 더 높고 더 나은 본능에 독이 되었다."], ["I began to think—it is hard to confess this, but I must suppress nothing from beginning to end of the terrible story that I now stand committed to reveal—I began to think, with a hateful eagerness of hope, of the vague charges against Sir Percival Glyde which the anonymous letter contained.", "나는 생각하기 시작했다—이것을 고백하기란 괴로운 일이나, 내가 이제 밝히기로 다짐한 이 끔찍한 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 하나도 숨겨서는 안 된다—나는 익명의 편지에 담긴 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 막연한 혐의들을, 가증스러울 만큼 간절한 희망을 품고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["What if those wild accusations rested on a foundation of truth?", "만약 그 터무니없는 고발들이 진실이라는 토대 위에 서 있는 것이라면?"], ["What if their truth could be proved before the fatal words of consent were spoken, and the marriage-settlement was drawn?", "만약 그 진실이 승낙의 치명적인 말이 오가고 혼인 재산 계약이 작성되기 전에 증명될 수 있다면?"], ["I have tried to think since, that the feeling which then animated me began and ended in pure devotion to Miss Fairlie’s interests, but I have never succeeded in deceiving myself into believing it, and I must not now attempt to deceive others.", "그 뒤로 나는 그때 나를 움직인 감정이 페얼리 양의 이익에 대한 순수한 헌신에서 시작해 거기서 끝났다고 생각해 보려 애썼으나, 나 자신을 속여 그리 믿게 하는 데 끝내 성공하지 못했고, 이제 와 남들을 속이려 들어서도 안 된다."], ["The feeling began and ended in reckless, vindictive, hopeless hatred of the man who was to marry her.", "그 감정은 그녀와 혼인할 그 남자에 대한 무모하고 앙심에 찬 가망 없는 증오에서 시작해 거기서 끝났다."]], [["“If we are to find out anything,”", "“무엇이든 알아내야 한다면.”"], ["I said, speaking under the new influence which was now directing me, “we had better not let another minute slip by us unemployed.", "나는 이제 나를 이끌고 있는 그 새로운 영향 아래에서 말했다. “단 일 분도 헛되이 흘려보내지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["I can only suggest, once more, the propriety of questioning the gardener a second time, and of inquiring in the village immediately afterwards.”", "저로서는 다시 한번, 정원사에게 두 번째로 물어보고 그 직후에 곧바로 마을에서 알아보는 것이 옳겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 수 없습니다.”"]], [["“I think I may be of help to you in both cases,”", "“두 가지 다 제가 도움이 될 수 있을 것 같아요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, rising.", "홀콤 양이 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Let us go, Mr.", "“당장 나가요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright, at once, and do the best we can together.”", "그리고 함께 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 봐요.”"]], [["I had the door in my hand to open it for her—but I stopped, on a sudden, to ask an important question before we set forth.", "나는 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주려고 손잡이를 잡고 있었으나—길을 나서기 전에 중요한 질문 하나를 하려고 문득 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“One of the paragraphs of the anonymous letter,”", "“그 익명의 편지의 한 문단에는.”"], ["I said, “contains some sentences of minute personal description.", "내가 말했다. “세세한 인물 묘사가 몇 문장 들어 있더군요."], ["Sir Percival Glyde’s name is not mentioned, I know—but does that description at all resemble him?”", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 이름은 나오지 않는다는 걸 압니다만—그 묘사가 조금이라도 그분과 닮았습니까?”"]], [["“Accurately—even in stating his age to be forty-five——”", "“정확해요—나이를 마흔다섯이라 한 것까지도——”"]], [["Forty-five; and she was not yet twenty-one!", "마흔다섯. 그런데 그녀는 아직 스물한 살도 되지 않았다!"], ["Men of his age married wives of her age every day—and experience had shown those marriages to be often the happiest ones.", "그 나이의 남자가 그 나이의 아내를 맞는 일은 날마다 있었고—경험은 그런 혼인이 흔히 가장 행복한 축에 든다는 것을 보여 주었다."], ["I knew that—and yet even the mention of his age, when I contrasted it with hers, added to my blind hatred and distrust of him.", "나도 그것을 알고 있었다—그런데도 그의 나이를 그녀의 나이와 견주어 보자, 그 나이가 언급되었다는 사실만으로도 그에 대한 나의 맹목적인 증오와 불신이 더해졌다."]], [["“Accurately,”", "“정확해요.”"], ["Miss Halcombe continued, “even to the scar on his right hand, which is the scar of a wound that he received years since when he was travelling in Italy.", "홀콤 양이 말을 이었다. “오른손의 흉터까지도요."], ["There can be no doubt that every peculiarity of his personal appearance is thoroughly well known to the writer of the letter.”", "그건 몇 해 전 이탈리아를 여행하던 때에 입은 상처의 흉터예요. 그분 외모의 온갖 특징을 편지를 쓴 사람이 속속들이 훤히 알고 있다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어요.”"]], [["“Even a cough that he is troubled with is mentioned, if I remember right?”", "“기억이 맞다면 그분이 앓는 기침까지도 나와 있지요?”"]], [["“Yes, and mentioned correctly.", "“네, 그것도 정확하게요."], ["He treats it lightly himself, though it sometimes makes his friends anxious about him.”", "본인은 대수롭지 않게 여기지만, 때로는 벗들이 그분을 걱정하게 만들지요.”"]], [["“I suppose no whispers have ever been heard against his character?”", "“그분의 인품에 대해 수군거리는 말이 들린 적은 한 번도 없겠지요?”"]], [["“Mr. Hartright!", "“하트라이트 씨!"], ["I hope you are not unjust enough to let that infamous letter influence you?”", "그 파렴치한 편지에 흔들릴 만큼 부당하신 분은 아니시겠지요?”"]], [["I felt the blood rush into my cheeks, for I knew that it HAD influenced me.", "나는 피가 뺨으로 몰리는 것을 느꼈다. 그것이 정말로 나를 흔들어 놓았다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“I hope not,”", "“그러지 않기를 바랍니다.”"], ["I answered confusedly.", "나는 당황하여 대답했다."], ["“Perhaps I had no right to ask the question.”", "“그런 질문을 할 자격이 저에게 없었는지도 모르겠군요.”"]], [["“I am not sorry you asked it,”", "“물어봐 주셔서 섭섭하지 않아요.”"], ["she said, “for it enables me to do justice to Sir Percival’s reputation.", "그녀가 말했다. “덕분에 퍼시벌 경의 평판에 정당한 대접을 해 드릴 수 있으니까요."], ["Not a whisper, Mr. Hartright, has ever reached me, or my family, against him.", "하트라이트 씨, 그분에 대한 수군거림은 단 한 마디도 저나 저희 집안에 닿은 적이 없어요."], ["He has fought successfully two contested elections, and has come out of the ordeal unscathed.", "그분은 경합 선거를 두 번이나 성공적으로 치르셨고, 그 시련에서 아무런 상처 없이 나오셨지요."], ["A man who can do that, in England, is a man whose character is established.”", "영국에서 그렇게 할 수 있는 사람이라면, 그 인품이 확립된 사람이에요.”"]], [["I opened the door for her in silence, and followed her out.", "나는 말없이 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주고 뒤따라 나갔다."], ["She had not convinced me.", "그녀는 나를 납득시키지 못했다."], ["If the recording angel had come down from heaven to confirm her, and had opened his book to my mortal eyes, the recording angel would not have convinced me.", "설령 기록의 천사가 하늘에서 내려와 그녀의 말을 뒷받침해 주고 죽을 목숨인 내 눈앞에 제 책을 펼쳐 보였다 한들, 그 기록의 천사조차 나를 납득시키지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["We found the gardener at work as usual.", "우리는 정원사가 여느 때처럼 일하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["No amount of questioning could extract a single answer of any importance from the lad’s impenetrable stupidity.", "아무리 캐물어도 그 소년의 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 우둔함에서는 조금이라도 중요한 대답 하나 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["The woman who had given him the letter was an elderly woman; she had not spoken a word to him, and she had gone away towards the south in a great hurry.", "그에게 편지를 준 여자는 나이 든 여자였다. 그 여자는 그에게 한 마디도 하지 않았고, 몹시 서두르며 남쪽을 향해 가 버렸다."], ["That was all the gardener could tell us.", "정원사가 우리에게 해 줄 수 있는 말은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["The village lay southward of the house.", "마을은 저택의 남쪽에 있었다."], ["So to the village we went next.", "그래서 우리는 다음으로 마을을 향해 갔다."]], [["# XII", "# XII"]], [["Our inquiries at Limmeridge were patiently pursued in all directions, and among all sorts and conditions of people.", "리머리지에서 우리의 탐문은 사방으로, 온갖 부류와 처지의 사람들을 상대로 끈기 있게 이어졌다."], ["But nothing came of them.", "그러나 아무 성과도 없었다."], ["Three of the villagers did certainly assure us that they had seen the woman, but as they were quite unable to describe her, and quite incapable of agreeing about the exact direction in which she was proceeding when they last saw her, these three bright exceptions to the general rule of total ignorance afforded no more real assistance to us than the mass of their unhelpful and unobservant neighbours.", "마을 사람 셋은 분명히 그 여자를 보았다고 우리에게 장담했지만, 그녀의 생김새를 도무지 설명하지 못했고 마지막으로 보았을 때 그녀가 어느 쪽으로 가고 있었는지에 대해서도 도무지 의견이 맞지 않았으므로, 전면적 무지라는 일반 법칙에 대한 이 세 사람의 빛나는 예외는 도움도 관찰력도 없는 그 이웃들의 무리보다 우리에게 조금도 더 실질적인 보탬이 되지 못했다."]], [["The course of our useless investigations brought us, in time, to the end of the village at which the schools established by Mrs. Fairlie were situated.", "쓸모없는 조사의 행로는 이윽고 우리를 페얼리 부인이 세운 학교가 자리한 마을 끝자락으로 데려갔다."], ["As we passed the side of the building appropriated to the use of the boys, I suggested the propriety of making a last inquiry of the schoolmaster, whom we might presume to be, in virtue of his office, the most intelligent man in the place.", "남학생들이 쓰는 쪽 건물 옆을 지나면서, 나는 그 직책상 이 고장에서 가장 지각 있는 사람이라고 짐작해도 좋을 교사에게 마지막으로 한 번 물어보는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 제안했다."]], [["“I am afraid the schoolmaster must have been occupied with his scholars,”", "“선생님은 아마 학생들을 가르치느라 바빴을 거예요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, “just at the time when the woman passed through the village and returned again.", "홀콤 양이 말했다. “바로 그 여자가 마을을 지나갔다가 되돌아온 그 시각에 말이죠."], ["However, we can but try.”", "그래도 해 볼 수는 있겠네요.”"]], [["We entered the playground enclosure, and walked by the schoolroom window to get round to the door, which was situated at the back of the building.", "우리는 운동장 울타리 안으로 들어가, 건물 뒤편에 있는 문으로 돌아가려고 교실 창가를 지나 걸었다."], ["I stopped for a moment at the window and looked in.", "나는 잠시 창 앞에 멈춰 서서 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["The schoolmaster was sitting at his high desk, with his back to me, apparently haranguing the pupils, who were all gathered together in front of him, with one exception.", "교사는 높은 책상 앞에 나를 등지고 앉아, 자기 앞에 죄다 모여 있는 학생들을 향해 일장 훈시를 늘어놓는 듯했는데, 한 명만은 예외였다."], ["The one exception was a sturdy white-headed boy, standing apart from all the rest on a stool in a corner—a forlorn little Crusoe, isolated in his own desert island of solitary penal disgrace.", "그 한 명의 예외는 몸이 다부지고 머리가 새하얀 소년으로, 나머지 전부와 떨어져 구석의 걸상 위에 서 있었다—외따로 떨어진 벌과 수치의 무인도에 갇힌, 가엾은 작은 크루소였다."]], [["The door, when we got round to it, was ajar, and the school-master’s voice reached us plainly, as we both stopped for a minute under the porch.", "돌아가서 보니 문은 반쯤 열려 있었고, 우리 둘이 현관 지붕 아래 잠시 멈춰 선 사이 교사의 목소리가 또렷이 들려왔다."]], [["“Now, boys,”", "“자, 얘들아.”"], ["said the voice, “mind what I tell you.", "그 목소리가 말했다. “내가 하는 말을 명심해라."], ["If I hear another word spoken about ghosts in this school, it will be the worse for all of you.", "이 학교에서 귀신 이야기가 한 마디라도 더 들리면, 너희 모두에게 좋지 않을 것이다."], ["There are no such things as ghosts, and therefore any boy who believes in ghosts believes in what can’t possibly be; and a boy who belongs to Limmeridge School, and believes in what can’t possibly be, sets up his back against reason and discipline, and must be punished accordingly.", "귀신 따위는 없다. 그러므로 귀신을 믿는 아이는 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 믿는 것이며, 리머리지 학교에 다니면서 결코 있을 수 없는 것을 믿는 아이는 이성과 규율에 등을 돌리는 것이니 그에 마땅한 벌을 받아야 한다."], ["You all see Jacob Postlethwaite standing up on the stool there in disgrace.", "너희 모두 제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 저기 걸상 위에 망신스럽게 서 있는 것을 보고 있다."], ["He has been punished, not because he said he saw a ghost last night, but because he is too impudent and too obstinate to listen to reason, and because he persists in saying he saw the ghost after I have told him that no such thing can possibly be.", "저 아이가 벌을 받는 것은 어젯밤 귀신을 보았다고 말했기 때문이 아니라, 너무 뻔뻔하고 너무 고집이 세서 이치에 귀를 기울이지 않기 때문이며, 그런 것은 결코 있을 수 없다고 내가 일러 주었는데도 귀신을 보았다고 우겨 대기 때문이다."], ["If nothing else will do, I mean to cane the ghost out of Jacob Postlethwaite, and if the thing spreads among any of the rest of you, I mean to go a step farther, and cane the ghost out of the whole school.”", "달리 방법이 없다면, 나는 제이콥 포슬스웨이트에게서 회초리로 귀신을 몰아낼 작정이다. 그리고 그 일이 너희 중 누구에게라도 번진다면, 나는 한 걸음 더 나아가 학교 전체에서 회초리로 귀신을 몰아낼 작정이다.”"]], [["“We seem to have chosen an awkward moment for our visit,”", "“방문하기에 딱한 순간을 고른 모양이네요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, pushing open the door at the end of the schoolmaster’s address, and leading the way in.", "홀콤 양이 말하며, 교사의 훈시가 끝나는 참에 문을 밀어 열고 앞장서 들어갔다."]], [["Our appearance produced a strong sensation among the boys.", "우리의 등장은 소년들 사이에 강한 파문을 일으켰다."], ["They appeared to think that we had arrived for the express purpose of seeing Jacob Postlethwaite caned.", "그들은 우리가 제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 회초리 맞는 것을 보려고 일부러 찾아왔다고 여기는 눈치였다."]], [["“Go home all of you to dinner,”", "“다들 집에 가서 점심을 먹어라.”"], ["said the schoolmaster, “except Jacob.", "교사가 말했다. “제이콥만 빼고."], ["Jacob must stop where he is; and the ghost may bring him his dinner, if the ghost pleases.”", "제이콥은 있는 자리에 그대로 있어야 한다. 그리고 귀신이 원한다면 귀신이 저 아이의 점심을 가져다주겠지.”"]], [["Jacob’s fortitude deserted him at the double disappearance of his schoolfellows and his prospect of dinner.", "학교 친구들과 점심의 전망이 한꺼번에 사라지자 제이콥의 꿋꿋함도 무너졌다."], ["He took his hands out of his pockets, looked hard at his knuckles, raised them with great deliberation to his eyes, and when they got there, ground them round and round slowly, accompanying the action by short spasms of sniffing, which followed each other at regular intervals—the nasal minute guns of juvenile distress.", "그는 주머니에서 두 손을 꺼내 제 주먹을 물끄러미 들여다보더니, 아주 신중하게 그것을 눈까지 들어 올렸고, 눈에 닿자 천천히 빙글빙글 비벼 댔는데, 그 동작에는 일정한 간격을 두고 잇따르는 짧은 훌쩍임의 발작이 곁들여졌다—어린 슬픔이 쏘아 올리는 콧소리의 조포(弔砲)였다."]], [["“We came here to ask you a question, Mr. Dempster,”", "“여쭤볼 것이 있어서 왔습니다, 뎀스터 씨.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, addressing the schoolmaster; “and we little expected to find you occupied in exorcising a ghost.", "홀콤 양이 교사에게 말을 건넸다. “선생님이 귀신을 쫓아내는 일에 몰두해 계실 줄은 미처 몰랐네요."], ["What does it all mean?", "대체 무슨 일이죠?"], ["What has really happened?”", "정말로 무슨 일이 있었던 건가요?”"]], [["“That wicked boy has been frightening the whole school, Miss Halcombe, by declaring that he saw a ghost yesterday evening,”", "“저 못된 아이가 어제 저녁에 귀신을 보았다고 떠들어 대며 학교 전체를 겁먹게 했습니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["answered the master; “and he still persists in his absurd story, in spite of all that I can say to him.”", "교사가 대답했다. “제가 아무리 타일러도 여전히 그 터무니없는 이야기를 우겨 대고 있고요.”"]], [["“Most extraordinary,”", "“참으로 놀랍군요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, “I should not have thought it possible that any of the boys had imagination enough to see a ghost.", "홀콤 양이 말했다. “저 아이들 중에 귀신을 볼 만한 상상력을 가진 아이가 있으리라고는 생각도 못 했어요."], ["This is a new accession indeed to the hard labour of forming the youthful mind at Limmeridge, and I heartily wish you well through it, Mr.", "리머리지에서 어린 정신을 길러 내는 고된 노역에 참으로 새로운 짐이 하나 더해진 셈이네요. 부디 잘 헤쳐 나가시기를 진심으로 빕니다, 뎀스터 씨."], ["Dempster. In the meantime, let me explain why you see me here, and what it is I want.”", "그건 그렇고, 제가 왜 여기 왔는지, 무엇을 알고 싶은지 말씀드리죠.”"]], [["She then put the same question to the schoolmaster which we had asked already of almost every one else in the village.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 우리가 이미 마을의 거의 모든 사람에게 물었던 것과 똑같은 질문을 교사에게 던졌다."], ["It was met by the same discouraging answer. Mr.", "돌아온 것은 똑같이 맥 빠지는 대답이었다."], ["Dempster had not set eyes on the stranger of whom we were in search.", "뎀스터 씨는 우리가 찾는 그 낯선 여자를 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“We may as well return to the house, Mr. Hartright,”", "“저택으로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠어요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe; “the information we want is evidently not to be found.”", "홀콤 양이 말했다. “우리가 원하는 정보는 아무래도 찾을 수 없는 모양이니까요.”"]], [["She had bowed to Mr. Dempster, and was about to leave the schoolroom, when the forlorn position of Jacob Postlethwaite, piteously sniffing on the stool of penitence, attracted her attention as she passed him, and made her stop good-humouredly to speak a word to the little prisoner before she opened the door.", "그녀는 뎀스터 씨에게 인사를 하고 교실을 나서려던 참이었는데, 참회의 걸상 위에서 가엾게 훌쩍이고 있는 제이콥 포슬스웨이트의 처량한 처지가 그 곁을 지나던 그녀의 눈길을 끌었고, 문을 열기 전에 그 작은 죄수에게 한마디 건네려고 너그러이 걸음을 멈추게 했다."]], [["“You foolish boy,”", "“이 어리석은 아이야.”"], ["she said, “why don’t you beg Mr.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Dempster’s pardon, and hold your tongue about the ghost?”", "“왜 뎀스터 선생님께 용서를 빌고 귀신 이야기는 입을 다물지 않는 거니?”"]], [["“Eh!—but I saw t’ ghaist,”", "“에!—그래도 지는 기신을 봤다 카이예.”"], ["persisted Jacob Postlethwaite, with a stare of terror and a burst of tears.", "제이콥 포슬스웨이트가 겁에 질린 눈으로 빤히 바라보며 울음을 터뜨린 채 우겨 댔다."]], [["“Stuff and nonsense!", "“허튼소리!"], ["You saw nothing of the kind.", "너는 그런 걸 본 적이 없어."], ["Ghost indeed!", "귀신이라니!"], ["What ghost——”", "무슨 귀신이——”"]], [["“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe,”", "“죄송합니다만, 홀콤 양.”"], ["interposed the schoolmaster a little uneasily—“but I think you had better not question the boy.", "교사가 다소 거북한 듯 끼어들었다. “저 아이에게 캐묻지 않으시는 편이 좋을 듯합니다."], ["The obstinate folly of his story is beyond all belief; and you might lead him into ignorantly——”", "저 아이 이야기의 완강한 어리석음은 도저히 믿기 어려울 지경이라, 자칫 아이가 아무것도 모르고——”"]], [["“Ignorantly what?”", "“아무것도 모르고 뭘 한다는 거죠?”"], ["inquired Miss Halcombe sharply.", "홀콤 양이 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["“Ignorantly shocking your feelings,” said Mr.", "“아무것도 모르고 아가씨의 심기를 상하게 할까 봐서요.”"], ["Dempster, looking very much discomposed.", "뎀스터 씨가 몹시 당황한 낯으로 말했다."]], [["“Upon my word, Mr. Dempster, you pay my feelings a great compliment in thinking them weak enough to be shocked by such an urchin as that!”", "“참, 뎀스터 씨, 제 심기가 저런 개구쟁이 하나에 상할 만큼 약하다고 여기시다니 저에게 대단한 찬사를 보내시는군요!”"], ["She turned with an air of satirical defiance to little Jacob, and began to question him directly.", "그녀는 빈정대는 듯 도전적인 태도로 어린 제이콥에게 돌아서서 직접 캐묻기 시작했다."], ["“Come!”", "“자!”"], ["she said, “I mean to know all about this.", "그녀가 말했다. “나는 이 일을 전부 알아야겠다."], ["You naughty boy, when did you see the ghost?”", "이 못된 아이야, 귀신을 언제 봤니?”"]], [["“Yestere’en, at the gloaming,”", "“어제 저녁예, 해거름에예.”"], ["replied Jacob.", "제이콥이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["you saw it yesterday evening, in the twilight?", "어제 저녁 땅거미 질 무렵에 봤다는 거지?"], ["And what was it like?”", "그래, 어떻게 생겼더냐?”"]], [["“Arl in white—as a ghaist should be,”", "“온통 하얗지예—기신이라믄 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로예.”"], ["answered the ghost-seer, with a confidence beyond his years.", "귀신을 보았다는 아이가 제 나이답지 않은 확신을 가지고 대답했다."]], [["“And where was it?”", "“그래서 그건 어디 있었니?”"]], [["“Away yander, in t’ kirkyard—where a ghaist ought to be.”", "“저 짝 저기, 교회 무덤가에예—기신이라믄 마땅히 있어야 할 데라예.”"]], [["“As a ‘ghaist’ should be—where a ‘ghaist’ ought to be—why, you little fool, you talk as if the manners and customs of ghosts had been familiar to you from your infancy!", "“‘기신’이라면 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로—‘기신’이라면 마땅히 있어야 할 데—아니 이 어린 바보야, 너는 마치 갓난아기 적부터 귀신의 예절과 풍습에 익숙하기라도 했던 것처럼 말하는구나!"], ["You have got your story at your fingers’ ends, at any rate.", "어쨌든 네 이야기는 손끝까지 훤히 꿰고 있는 모양이다."], ["I suppose I shall hear next that you can actually tell me whose ghost it was?”", "다음에는 그게 누구의 귀신이었는지까지 나에게 일러 줄 수 있다고 하겠구나?”"]], [["“Eh!", "“에!"], ["but I just can,”", "그거야 지가 말할 수 있지예.”"], ["replied Jacob, nodding his head with an air of gloomy triumph.", "제이콥이 음울한 승리감을 띤 태도로 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다."]], [["Mr. Dempster had already tried several times to speak while Miss Halcombe was examining his pupil, and he now interposed resolutely enough to make himself heard.", "홀콤 양이 제자를 심문하는 동안 뎀스터 씨는 이미 여러 번 말을 꺼내려 했고, 이제는 제 말이 들리도록 제법 단호하게 끼어들었다."]], [["“Excuse me, Miss Halcombe,”", "“실례합니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["he said, “if I venture to say that you are only encouraging the boy by asking him these questions.”", "그가 말했다. “감히 말씀드리자면, 그런 질문을 하시는 것은 저 아이를 부추기는 일일 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“I will merely ask one more, Mr. Dempster, and then I shall be quite satisfied.", "“하나만 더 묻고 나면 아주 만족하겠어요, 뎀스터 씨."], ["Well,”", "자.”"], ["she continued, turning to the boy, “and whose ghost was it?”", "그녀가 소년에게 돌아서며 말을 이었다. “그래, 그게 누구의 귀신이었니?”"]], [["“T’ ghaist of Mistress Fairlie,”", "“페얼리 마님의 기신이라예.”"], ["answered Jacob in a whisper.", "제이콥이 속삭이듯 대답했다."]], [["The effect which this extraordinary reply produced on Miss Halcombe fully justified the anxiety which the schoolmaster had shown to prevent her from hearing it.", "이 놀라운 대답이 홀콤 양에게 미친 효과는, 그녀가 그것을 듣지 못하게 하려고 교사가 보였던 조바심을 충분히 정당화해 주었다."], ["Her face crimsoned with indignation—she turned upon little Jacob with an angry suddenness which terrified him into a fresh burst of tears—opened her lips to speak to him—then controlled herself, and addressed the master instead of the boy.", "그녀의 얼굴은 분노로 새빨개졌다—그녀는 어린 제이콥을 향해 성난 기색으로 홱 돌아섰고, 그 서슬에 겁을 먹은 아이는 새로 울음을 터뜨렸다—그녀는 아이에게 말하려고 입술을 열었다가—이내 스스로를 다잡고, 아이 대신 교사에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“It is useless,” she said, “to hold such a child as that responsible for what he says.", "“저런 아이에게 제 말에 대한 책임을 묻는 것은.”"], ["I have little doubt that the idea has been put into his head by others.", "그녀가 말했다. “부질없는 일이죠."], ["If there are people in this village, Mr.", "그 생각을 저 아이 머릿속에 넣어 준 것은 다른 사람들이라고 저는 거의 확신해요."], ["Dempster, who have forgotten the respect and gratitude due from every soul in it to my mother’s memory, I will find them out, and if I have any influence with Mr. Fairlie, they shall suffer for it.”", "이 마을에, 뎀스터 씨, 여기 사는 모든 이가 제 어머니의 기억에 마땅히 바쳐야 할 존경과 감사를 잊어버린 사람들이 있다면, 제가 그들을 찾아낼 것이고, 저에게 페얼리 씨에 대한 영향력이 조금이라도 있다면 그들은 그 대가를 치르게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“I hope—indeed, I am sure, Miss Halcombe—that you are mistaken,”", "“아니기를 바랍니다—아니, 정말이지 확신합니다, 홀콤 양—아가씨께서 잘못 짐작하신 거라고요.”"], ["said the schoolmaster.", "교사가 말했다."], ["“The matter begins and ends with the boy’s own perversity and folly.", "“이 일은 저 아이 자신의 삐뚤어진 고집과 어리석음에서 시작해 거기서 끝납니다. 저 아이는 어제 저녁 교회 묘지를 지나다가 흰옷을 입은 여자를 보았거나, 보았다고 생각했습니다."], ["He saw, or thought he saw, a woman in white, yesterday evening, as he was passing the churchyard; and the figure, real or fancied, was standing by the marble cross, which he and every one else in Limmeridge knows to be the monument over Mrs.", "그리고 그 형체는, 실제였든 상상이었든, 대리석 십자가 옆에 서 있었지요. 저 아이도, 리머리지의 다른 모든 사람도 그것이 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위에 세워진 기념비라는 것을 압니다."], ["Fairlie’s grave. These two circumstances are surely sufficient to have suggested to the boy himself the answer which has so naturally shocked you?”", "이 두 가지 정황이면 아가씨를 그토록 당연하게 놀라게 한 그 대답을 저 아이 스스로 떠올리기에 충분하지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["Although Miss Halcombe did not seem to be convinced, she evidently felt that the schoolmaster’s statement of the case was too sensible to be openly combated.", "홀콤 양은 납득한 것 같지 않았지만, 교사의 사건 설명이 너무나 사리에 맞아 대놓고 반박할 수 없다는 것을 분명히 느꼈다."], ["She merely replied by thanking him for his attention, and by promising to see him again when her doubts were satisfied.", "그녀는 그저 신경 써 주어 고맙다는 인사와, 의혹이 풀리면 다시 찾아오겠다는 약속으로 대답을 대신했다."], ["This said, she bowed, and led the way out of the schoolroom.", "그렇게 말하고 그녀는 인사를 하고는 앞장서서 교실을 나섰다."]], [["Throughout the whole of this strange scene I had stood apart, listening attentively, and drawing my own conclusions.", "이 기이한 장면 내내 나는 한쪽에 떨어져 서서 주의 깊게 귀를 기울이며 나 나름의 결론을 끌어내고 있었다."], ["As soon as we were alone again, Miss Halcombe asked me if I had formed any opinion on what I had heard.", "다시 우리 둘만 남게 되자마자 홀콤 양은 내가 들은 것에 대해 어떤 의견이라도 세웠는지 물었다."]], [["“A very strong opinion,”", "“아주 확고한 의견이 있습니다.”"], ["I answered; “the boy’s story, as I believe, has a foundation in fact.", "내가 대답했다. “제가 믿기로는, 그 아이의 이야기에는 사실의 근거가 있습니다."], ["I confess I am anxious to see the monument over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, and to examine the ground about it.”", "고백하건대 저는 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위 기념비를 보고, 그 주변의 땅을 살펴보고 싶어 견딜 수가 없군요.”"]], [["“You shall see the grave.”", "“무덤은 보시게 해 드리죠.”"]], [["She paused after making that reply, and reflected a little as we walked on.", "그녀는 그렇게 대답한 뒤 말을 멈추고, 우리가 걸어가는 동안 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["“What has happened in the schoolroom,”", "“교실에서 있었던 일이.”"], ["she resumed, “has so completely distracted my attention from the subject of the letter, that I feel a little bewildered when I try to return to it.", "그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. “그 편지 문제에서 제 정신을 어찌나 완전히 앗아 갔는지, 다시 그리로 돌아가려 하면 조금 어리둥절해져요."], ["Must we give up all idea of making any further inquiries, and wait to place the thing in Mr. Gilmore’s hands to-morrow?”", "우리는 더 알아볼 생각을 전부 접고, 내일 이 일을 길모어 씨의 손에 맡길 때까지 기다려야 할까요?”"]], [["“By no means, Miss Halcombe.", "“결코 그렇지 않습니다, 홀콤 양."], ["What has happened in the schoolroom encourages me to persevere in the investigation.”", "교실에서 있었던 일은 오히려 제가 이 조사를 끝까지 밀고 나가도록 부추깁니다.”"]], [["“Why does it encourage you?”", "“어째서 그것이 당신을 부추기죠?”"]], [["“Because it strengthens a suspicion I felt when you gave me the letter to read.”", "“당신이 저에게 그 편지를 읽어 보라고 주셨을 때 제가 느꼈던 어떤 의심을 그것이 굳혀 주기 때문입니다.”"]], [["“I suppose you had your reasons, Mr. Hartright, for concealing that suspicion from me till this moment?”", "“지금 이 순간까지 그 의심을 저에게 숨기신 데에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있으셨겠죠, 하트라이트 씨?”"]], [["“I was afraid to encourage it in myself.", "“저 스스로 그것을 키우기가 두려웠습니다."], ["I thought it was utterly preposterous—I distrusted it as the result of some perversity in my own imagination.", "저는 그것이 전혀 터무니없다고 생각했고—제 상상 속의 어떤 비뚤어짐이 낳은 결과로 여겨 믿지 않았습니다."], ["But I can do so no longer.", "하지만 이제는 더 그럴 수가 없군요."], ["Not only the boy’s own answers to your questions, but even a chance expression that dropped from the schoolmaster’s lips in explaining his story, have forced the idea back into my mind.", "당신의 질문에 대한 그 아이 자신의 대답뿐만 아니라, 그 이야기를 설명하면서 교사의 입에서 우연히 떨어진 한 마디 표현까지, 그 생각을 다시 제 머릿속으로 밀어 넣었습니다."], ["Events may yet prove that idea to be a delusion, Miss Halcombe; but the belief is strong in me, at this moment, that the fancied ghost in the churchyard, and the writer of the anonymous letter, are one and the same person.”", "앞으로의 사건들이 그 생각을 망상으로 판명할지도 모르지요, 홀콤 양. 하지만 지금 이 순간 제 안에서는, 교회 묘지에서 보였다는 그 귀신과 익명 편지를 쓴 사람이 같은 사람이라는 믿음이 강합니다.”"]], [["She stopped, turned pale, and looked me eagerly in the face.", "그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 창백해진 얼굴로 내 얼굴을 간절히 들여다보았다."]], [["“What person?”", "“어떤 사람인데요?”"]], [["“The schoolmaster unconsciously told you.", "“교사가 자기도 모르게 당신에게 알려 주었습니다."], ["When he spoke of the figure that the boy saw in the churchyard he called it ‘a woman in white.’”", "그가 그 아이가 교회 묘지에서 본 형체를 이야기하면서 그것을 ‘흰옷을 입은 여자’라고 불렀지요.”"]], [["“Not Anne Catherick?”", "“앤 캐서릭은 아니겠죠?”"]], [["“Yes, Anne Catherick.”", "“그렇습니다, 앤 캐서릭입니다.”"]], [["She put her hand through my arm and leaned on it heavily.", "그녀는 내 팔에 손을 끼우고 무겁게 기대었다."]], [["“I don’t know why,”", "“왜인지는 모르겠어요.”"], ["she said in low tones, “but there is something in this suspicion of yours that seems to startle and unnerve me.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. “하지만 당신의 그 의심에는 저를 흠칫 놀라게 하고 기운을 앗아 가는 무언가가 있어요."], ["I feel——”", "저는 느껴져요——”"], ["She stopped, and tried to laugh it off.", "그녀는 말을 멈추고 웃어넘기려 했다."], ["“Mr. Hartright,”", "“하트라이트 씨.”"], ["she went on, “I will show you the grave, and then go back at once to the house.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “제가 무덤을 보여 드리고, 그러고는 곧장 저택으로 돌아가겠어요."], ["I had better not leave Laura too long alone.", "로라를 너무 오래 혼자 두지 않는 게 좋겠어요."], ["I had better go back and sit with her.”", "돌아가서 그 애 곁에 앉아 있는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["We were close to the churchyard when she spoke.", "그녀가 말할 때 우리는 교회 묘지 가까이에 있었다."], ["The church, a dreary building of grey stone, was situated in a little valley, so as to be sheltered from the bleak winds blowing over the moorland all round it.", "잿빛 돌로 지어진 음산한 건물인 교회는, 사방을 둘러싼 황야 위로 불어오는 매서운 바람을 피할 수 있도록 작은 골짜기 안에 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["The burial-ground advanced, from the side of the church, a little way up the slope of the hill.", "매장지는 교회 옆에서 언덕의 비탈을 따라 조금 위로 뻗어 올라가 있었다."], ["It was surrounded by a rough, low stone wall, and was bare and open to the sky, except at one extremity, where a brook trickled down the stony hill-side, and a clump of dwarf trees threw their narrow shadows over the short, meagre grass.", "그것은 거칠고 나지막한 돌담으로 둘러싸여 있었고, 한쪽 끄트머리를 빼면 하늘을 향해 휑하니 열려 있었는데, 그 한쪽 끝에서는 시내가 돌투성이 언덕 비탈을 따라 졸졸 흘러내렸고 키 작은 나무 한 무리가 짧고 메마른 풀 위로 좁은 그림자를 드리우고 있었다."], ["Just beyond the brook and the trees, and not far from one of the three stone stiles which afforded entrance, at various points, to the churchyard, rose the white marble cross that distinguished Mrs. Fairlie’s grave from the humbler monuments scattered about it.", "그 시내와 나무들 바로 너머, 교회 묘지로 여러 지점에서 들어갈 수 있게 놓인 세 개의 돌계단 가운데 하나에서 멀지 않은 곳에, 페얼리 부인의 무덤을 그 주위에 흩어진 더 초라한 기념비들과 구별해 주는 흰 대리석 십자가가 솟아 있었다."]], [["“I need go no farther with you,”", "“여기서부터는 함께 갈 필요가 없겠네요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, pointing to the grave.", "홀콤 양이 무덤을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“You will let me know if you find anything to confirm the idea you have just mentioned to me.", "“방금 저에게 말씀하신 그 생각을 뒷받침할 무언가를 찾으시거든 알려 주세요."], ["Let us meet again at the house.”", "저택에서 다시 뵙기로 해요.”"]], [["She left me.", "그녀는 나를 두고 떠났다."], ["I descended at once to the churchyard, and crossed the stile which led directly to Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.", "나는 곧장 교회 묘지로 내려가, 페얼리 부인의 무덤으로 곧바로 이어지는 돌계단을 넘었다."]], [["The grass about it was too short, and the ground too hard, to show any marks of footsteps.", "무덤 주위의 풀은 너무 짧았고 땅은 너무 단단해서 발자국의 흔적이 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Disappointed thus far, I next looked attentively at the cross, and at the square block of marble below it, on which the inscription was cut.", "거기까지에서 실망한 나는, 다음으로 십자가와 그 아래 비문이 새겨진 네모난 대리석 덩이를 주의 깊게 들여다보았다."]], [["The natural whiteness of the cross was a little clouded, here and there, by weather stains, and rather more than one half of the square block beneath it, on the side which bore the inscription, was in the same condition.", "십자가의 본래 흰빛은 여기저기 비바람의 얼룩으로 조금 흐려져 있었고, 그 아래 네모난 덩이도 비문이 새겨진 면의 절반 남짓이 같은 상태였다."], ["The other half, however, attracted my attention at once by its singular freedom from stain or impurity of any kind.", "그러나 나머지 절반은 얼룩이나 티끌이 어떤 종류도 유난히 없다는 점으로 곧바로 내 눈길을 끌었다."], ["I looked closer, and saw that it had been cleaned—recently cleaned, in a downward direction from top to bottom.", "나는 더 가까이 들여다보았고, 그것이 닦여 있다는 것을 알았다—최근에, 위에서 아래로 쓸어내리는 방향으로 닦여 있었다."], ["The boundary line between the part that had been cleaned and the part that had not was traceable wherever the inscription left a blank space of marble—sharply traceable as a line that had been produced by artificial means.", "닦인 부분과 닦이지 않은 부분 사이의 경계선은 비문이 대리석의 빈자리를 남겨 놓은 곳마다 뚜렷이 더듬을 수 있었다—인위적인 방법으로 생긴 선답게 또렷하게."], ["Who had begun the cleansing of the marble, and who had left it unfinished?", "누가 이 대리석을 닦기 시작했고, 누가 그것을 끝내지 않은 채 내버려 두었을까?"]], [["I looked about me, wondering how the question was to be solved.", "나는 그 물음을 어떻게 풀어야 할지 궁금해하며 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["No sign of a habitation could be discerned from the point at which I was standing—the burial-ground was left in the lonely possession of the dead.", "내가 서 있는 자리에서는 사람이 사는 집의 기미라고는 하나도 보이지 않았다—매장지는 죽은 이들의 쓸쓸한 차지로 남겨져 있었다."], ["I returned to the church, and walked round it till I came to the back of the building; then crossed the boundary wall beyond, by another of the stone stiles, and found myself at the head of a path leading down into a deserted stone quarry.", "나는 교회로 되돌아가 건물 뒤편에 이를 때까지 그 둘레를 걸었다. 그런 다음 그 너머의 경계 담을 또 다른 돌계단으로 넘어, 버려진 채석장으로 내려가는 오솔길의 초입에 서 있는 나를 발견했다."], ["Against one side of the quarry a little two-room cottage was built, and just outside the door an old woman was engaged in washing.", "채석장 한쪽 벽에 기대어 방 두 칸짜리 작은 오두막이 지어져 있었고, 문밖에서는 한 노파가 빨래를 하고 있었다."]], [["I walked up to her, and entered into conversation about the church and burial-ground.", "나는 그녀에게 다가가 교회와 매장지에 대해 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["She was ready enough to talk, and almost the first words she said informed me that her husband filled the two offices of clerk and sexton.", "그녀는 기꺼이 말을 받았고, 거의 첫마디부터 자기 남편이 교회 서기와 묘지기 두 가지 일을 맡고 있다고 알려 주었다."], ["I said a few words next in praise of Mrs. Fairlie’s monument.", "나는 다음으로 페얼리 부인의 기념비를 칭찬하는 말을 몇 마디 했다."], ["The old woman shook her head, and told me I had not seen it at its best.", "노파는 고개를 저으며, 내가 그것을 가장 좋은 상태로 본 것이 아니라고 말했다."], ["It was her husband’s business to look after it, but he had been so ailing and weak for months and months past, that he had hardly been able to crawl into church on Sundays to do his duty, and the monument had been neglected in consequence.", "그것을 돌보는 일은 제 남편의 소임인데, 남편이 몇 달이고 몇 달이고 앓고 기운이 없어 일요일에 제 소임을 다하러 교회까지 기어들어 가기도 힘들 지경이었고, 그래서 기념비가 방치되어 왔다는 것이었다."], ["He was getting a little better now, and in a week or ten days’ time he hoped to be strong enough to set to work and clean it.", "남편은 이제 조금 나아지고 있고, 한 주나 열흘쯤 뒤에는 일을 시작해 그것을 닦을 만큼 기운을 차리기를 바라고 있다고 했다."]], [["This information—extracted from a long rambling answer in the broadest Cumberland dialect—told me all that I most wanted to know.", "아주 짙은 컴벌랜드 사투리로 길게 두서없이 늘어놓은 대답에서 뽑아낸 이 이야기는, 내가 무엇보다 알고 싶었던 것을 전부 말해 주었다."], ["I gave the poor woman a trifle, and returned at once to Limmeridge House.", "나는 그 가난한 여인에게 얼마간 돈을 쥐여 주고 곧장 리머리지 하우스로 돌아왔다."]], [["The partial cleansing of the monument had evidently been accomplished by a strange hand.", "기념비를 반쯤 닦아 놓은 것은 분명 낯선 손의 일이었다."], ["Connecting what I had discovered, thus far, with what I had suspected after hearing the story of the ghost seen at twilight, I wanted nothing more to confirm my resolution to watch Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, in secret, that evening, returning to it at sunset, and waiting within sight of it till the night fell.", "여기까지 알아낸 것을, 땅거미 질 무렵 보였다는 귀신 이야기를 듣고 내가 품었던 의심과 이어 보니, 그날 저녁 몰래 페얼리 부인의 무덤을 지켜보리라는—해 질 녘에 그리로 돌아가 밤이 내릴 때까지 그것이 보이는 자리에서 기다리리라는—내 결심을 굳히는 데 더 필요한 것은 없었다."], ["The work of cleansing the monument had been left unfinished, and the person by whom it had been begun might return to complete it.", "기념비를 닦는 일은 끝맺지 못한 채 남겨져 있었고, 그것을 시작한 사람이 마저 끝내러 돌아올지도 몰랐다."]], [["On getting back to the house I informed Miss Halcombe of what I intended to do.", "저택으로 돌아온 나는 내가 하려는 일을 홀콤 양에게 알렸다."], ["She looked surprised and uneasy while I was explaining my purpose, but she made no positive objection to the execution of it.", "내가 뜻을 설명하는 동안 그녀는 놀라고 불안한 기색이었으나, 그 실행에 대해 딱 잘라 반대하지는 않았다."], ["She only said, “I hope it may end well.”", "그녀는 다만 “잘 끝나기를 바라요.”라고만 말했다."]], [["Just as she was leaving me again, I stopped her to inquire, as calmly as I could, after Miss Fairlie’s health.", "그녀가 다시 나에게서 떠나려는 참에, 나는 그녀를 멈춰 세우고 되도록 담담하게 페얼리 양의 안부를 물었다."], ["She was in better spirits, and Miss Halcombe hoped she might be induced to take a little walking exercise while the afternoon sun lasted.", "그녀는 기분이 좀 나아졌고, 홀콤 양은 오후 햇살이 남아 있는 동안 그녀가 조금 걷도록 권해 볼 수 있기를 바라고 있었다."]], [["I returned to my own room to resume setting the drawings in order.", "나는 내 방으로 돌아가 그림들을 정리하던 일을 다시 시작했다."], ["It was necessary to do this, and doubly necessary to keep my mind employed on anything that would help to distract my attention from myself, and from the hopeless future that lay before me.", "그 일은 해야만 하는 일이었고, 나 자신에게서, 그리고 내 앞에 놓인 가망 없는 미래에서 내 주의를 돌리는 데 도움이 될 무엇에든 마음을 붙들어 두는 일은 갑절로 필요했다."], ["From time to time I paused in my work to look out of window and watch the sky as the sun sank nearer and nearer to the horizon.", "나는 이따금 일손을 멈추고 창밖을 내다보며, 해가 점점 더 지평선 가까이 가라앉는 하늘을 바라보았다."], ["On one of those occasions I saw a figure on the broad gravel walk under my window.", "그런 때 한번은 내 창 아래 넓은 자갈길 위에 있는 한 사람의 모습을 보았다."], ["It was Miss Fairlie.", "페얼리 양이었다."]], [["I had not seen her since the morning, and I had hardly spoken to her then.", "아침 이후로 나는 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그때도 그녀와 거의 말을 나누지 못했다."], ["Another day at Limmeridge was all that remained to me, and after that day my eyes might never look on her again.", "리머리지에서 나에게 남은 것은 단 하루뿐이었고, 그날이 지나면 내 눈은 다시는 그녀를 보지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["This thought was enough to hold me at the window.", "이 생각만으로도 나를 창가에 붙들어 두기에 충분했다."], ["I had sufficient consideration for her to arrange the blind so that she might not see me if she looked up, but I had no strength to resist the temptation of letting my eyes, at least, follow her as far as they could on her walk.", "나는 그녀가 올려다보아도 나를 보지 못하도록 차양을 손보아 둘 만큼의 배려는 있었으나, 적어도 내 눈만은 그녀가 걷는 길을 눈길이 닿는 데까지 좇게 하고 싶은 유혹을 뿌리칠 힘은 없었다."]], [["She was dressed in a brown cloak, with a plain black silk gown under it.", "그녀는 갈색 망토를 걸치고, 그 안에는 수수한 검정 비단 드레스를 입고 있었다."], ["On her head was the same simple straw hat which she had worn on the morning when we first met.", "머리에는 우리가 처음 만나던 날 아침에 썼던 것과 똑같은 소박한 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["A veil was attached to it now which hid her face from me.", "지금은 거기에 베일이 달려 있어 그녀의 얼굴을 나에게서 가렸다."], ["By her side trotted a little Italian greyhound, the pet companion of all her walks, smartly dressed in a scarlet cloth wrapper, to keep the sharp air from his delicate skin.", "그녀 곁에는 산책 때마다 따라다니는 사랑스러운 동무인 작은 이탈리아 그레이하운드가 종종걸음을 쳤는데, 여린 살갗에 매서운 공기가 닿지 않도록 진홍빛 나사 덮개를 맵시 있게 두르고 있었다."], ["She did not seem to notice the dog.", "그녀는 개를 알아보지 못하는 듯했다."], ["She walked straight forward, with her head drooping a little, and her arms folded in her cloak.", "그녀는 고개를 조금 떨구고 두 팔을 망토 안에 포갠 채 곧장 앞으로 걸어갔다."], ["The dead leaves, which had whirled in the wind before me when I had heard of her marriage engagement in the morning, whirled in the wind before her, and rose and fell and scattered themselves at her feet as she walked on in the pale waning sunlight.", "그날 아침 그녀의 약혼 소식을 들었을 때 내 앞에서 바람에 소용돌이치던 마른 잎들이, 그녀 앞에서도 바람에 소용돌이쳤고, 그녀가 스러져 가는 창백한 햇빛 속을 걸어가는 동안 떠올랐다 내려앉았다 하며 그녀의 발치에 흩어졌다."], ["The dog shivered and trembled, and pressed against her dress impatiently for notice and encouragement.", "개는 오들오들 떨며 눈길과 격려를 바라고 조바심을 내며 그녀의 옷자락에 몸을 비볐다. 그러나 그녀는 끝내 그것을 알아채지 못했다."], ["But she never heeded him. She walked on, farther and farther away from me, with the dead leaves whirling about her on the path—walked on, till my aching eyes could see her no more, and I was left alone again with my own heavy heart.", "그녀는 마른 잎들이 오솔길 위에서 제 주위로 소용돌이치는 가운데 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 걸어갔다—아파 오는 내 눈이 더는 그녀를 볼 수 없을 때까지 걸어갔고, 나는 다시 나 자신의 무거운 마음과 함께 혼자 남겨졌다."]], [["In another hour’s time I had done my work, and the sunset was at hand.", "다시 한 시간이 지나자 나는 일을 끝냈고, 해 질 녘이 가까웠다."], ["I got my hat and coat in the hall, and slipped out of the house without meeting any one.", "나는 현관에서 모자와 외투를 챙겨 아무도 마주치지 않고 집을 빠져나왔다."]], [["The clouds were wild in the western heaven, and the wind blew chill from the sea.", "서쪽 하늘에는 구름이 사납게 일었고, 바다에서 불어오는 바람은 싸늘했다."], ["Far as the shore was, the sound of the surf swept over the intervening moorland, and beat drearily in my ears when I entered the churchyard.", "바닷가가 멀었는데도 파도 소리는 사이에 놓인 황야를 넘어 밀려와, 내가 교회 묘지에 들어설 때 음산하게 내 귓속을 때렸다."], ["Not a living creature was in sight.", "살아 있는 것이라고는 하나도 보이지 않았다."], ["The place looked lonelier than ever as I chose my position, and waited and watched, with my eyes on the white cross that rose over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.", "자리를 고르고 페얼리 부인의 무덤 위로 솟은 흰 십자가에 눈을 둔 채 기다리며 지켜보는 동안, 그곳은 어느 때보다도 쓸쓸해 보였다."]], [["# XIII", "# XIII"]], [["The exposed situation of the churchyard had obliged me to be cautious in choosing the position that I was to occupy.", "교회 묘지는 사방으로 훤히 트인 곳이었으므로, 나는 내가 자리 잡을 위치를 고르는 데 조심하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["The main entrance to the church was on the side next to the burial-ground, and the door was screened by a porch walled in on either side.", "교회의 정문은 매장지에 면한 쪽에 있었고, 그 문은 양옆이 벽으로 막힌 현관 지붕에 가려져 있었다."], ["After some little hesitation, caused by natural reluctance to conceal myself, indispensable as that concealment was to the object in view, I had resolved on entering the porch.", "몸을 숨긴다는 데 대한 자연스러운 거부감 때문에 잠시 망설이기는 했으나—목적을 이루기 위해서는 그 은신이 없어서는 안 될 일이었으므로—나는 결국 그 현관 안으로 들어가기로 마음을 정했다."], ["A loophole window was pierced in each of its side walls.", "양쪽 옆벽에는 총안(銃眼)처럼 좁은 창이 하나씩 뚫려 있었다."], ["Through one of these windows I could see Mrs.", "그중 한 창으로는 페얼리 부인의 무덤이 보였다."], ["Fairlie’s grave. The other looked towards the stone quarry in which the sexton’s cottage was built.", "다른 창은 무덤지기의 오두막이 지어져 있는 채석장 쪽을 향해 있었다."], ["Before me, fronting the porch entrance, was a patch of bare burial-ground, a line of low stone wall, and a strip of lonely brown hill, with the sunset clouds sailing heavily over it before the strong, steady wind.", "내 앞으로, 현관 입구 정면에는 아무것도 없는 맨 매장지 한 뙈기와 나지막한 돌담 한 줄, 그리고 쓸쓸한 갈색 언덕 한 자락이 있었고, 그 위로는 저녁놀 구름이 억세고 한결같은 바람에 밀려 무겁게 흘러가고 있었다."], ["No living creature was visible or audible—no bird flew by me, no dog barked from the sexton’s cottage.", "살아 있는 것이라고는 보이지도 들리지도 않았다—새 한 마리 곁을 날지 않았고, 무덤지기의 오두막에서 개 짖는 소리 하나 나지 않았다."], ["The pauses in the dull beating of the surf were filled up by the dreary rustling of the dwarf trees near the grave, and the cold faint bubble of the brook over its stony bed.", "둔중한 파도 소리가 멎는 사이사이를 무덤 가까이 선 왜소한 나무들의 을씨년스러운 바스락거림과, 돌바닥 위를 흐르는 개울의 차갑고 희미한 물소리가 메웠다."], ["A dreary scene and a dreary hour.", "을씨년스러운 풍경이요, 을씨년스러운 시각이었다."], ["My spirits sank fast as I counted out the minutes of the evening in my hiding-place under the church porch.", "교회 현관 아래 은신처에서 저녁의 분(分)들을 하나하나 헤아리는 동안 내 기운은 빠르게 가라앉았다."]], [["It was not twilight yet—the light of the setting sun still lingered in the heavens, and little more than the first half-hour of my solitary watch had elapsed—when I heard footsteps and a voice.", "아직 땅거미가 지지는 않았고—지는 해의 빛이 여전히 하늘에 머물러 있었으며, 홀로 지키는 시간의 첫 반 시간이 조금 지났을 뿐이었는데—그때 발소리와 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["The footsteps were approaching from the other side of the church, and the voice was a woman’s.", "발소리는 교회 반대편에서 다가오고 있었고, 목소리는 여자의 것이었다."]], [["“Don’t you fret, my dear, about the letter,”", "“편지 일은 걱정 말아요, 아가씨.”"], ["said the voice.", "그 목소리가 말했다."], ["“I gave it to the lad quite safe, and the lad he took it from me without a word.", "“내가 그 아이한테 아주 안전하게 건네줬고, 그 아이도 아무 말 없이 나한테서 받아 갔어요."], ["He went his way and I went mine, and not a living soul followed me afterwards—that I’ll warrant.”", "그 애는 제 갈 길로 가고 나는 내 갈 길로 왔지요. 그 뒤로 산 사람 하나 나를 따라오지 않았어요—그건 내가 장담해요.”"]], [["These words strung up my attention to a pitch of expectation that was almost painful.", "이 말은 내 주의를 거의 고통스러울 지경의 기대로 팽팽하게 끌어올렸다."], ["There was a pause of silence, but the footsteps still advanced.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀으나 발소리는 여전히 다가왔다."], ["In another moment two persons, both women, passed within my range of view from the porch window.", "다음 순간 두 사람이, 둘 다 여자였는데, 현관 창으로 내다보이는 내 시야 안을 지나갔다."], ["They were walking straight towards the grave; and therefore they had their backs turned towards me.", "그들은 곧장 무덤을 향해 걷고 있었고, 그래서 나에게는 등을 돌리고 있었다."]], [["One of the women was dressed in a bonnet and shawl.", "여자들 중 하나는 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르고 있었다."], ["The other wore a long travelling-cloak of a dark-blue colour, with the hood drawn over her head.", "다른 하나는 짙은 청색의 긴 여행용 망토를 걸치고 두건을 머리 위로 끌어 덮고 있었다."], ["A few inches of her gown were visible below the cloak.", "망토 아래로 가운이 몇 인치쯤 드러나 보였다."], ["My heart beat fast as I noted the colour—it was white.", "그 빛깔을 알아보는 순간 내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다—흰색이었다."]], [["After advancing about half-way between the church and the grave they stopped, and the woman in the cloak turned her head towards her companion.", "교회와 무덤 사이 절반쯤 나아갔을 때 그들은 멈춰 섰고, 망토를 걸친 여자가 동행 쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다."], ["But her side face, which a bonnet might now have allowed me to see, was hidden by the heavy, projecting edge of the hood.", "그러나 보닛을 썼더라면 이제는 내가 볼 수 있었을 그 옆얼굴이, 두툼하게 앞으로 튀어나온 두건 가장자리에 가려져 있었다."]], [["“Mind you keep that comfortable warm cloak on,”", "“그 편안하고 따뜻한 망토, 꼭 벗지 말고 있어요.”"], ["said the same voice which I had already heard—the voice of the woman in the shawl.", "내가 이미 들었던 바로 그 목소리가—숄을 두른 여자의 목소리가 말했다."], ["“Mrs. Todd is right about your looking too particular, yesterday, all in white.", "“어제 온통 흰옷을 입은 게 너무 눈에 띈다고 한 토드 부인 말이 맞아요."], ["I’ll walk about a little while you’re here, churchyards being not at all in my way, whatever they may be in yours.", "나는 아가씨가 여기 있는 동안 좀 걷고 있을게요. 교회 묘지란 데는 아가씨한테는 어떨지 몰라도 나한테는 도무지 맞지 않아서요."], ["Finish what you want to do before I come back, and let us be sure and get home again before night.”", "내가 돌아오기 전에 하고 싶은 일을 끝내요. 그리고 밤이 되기 전에 꼭 집으로 돌아가도록 합시다.”"]], [["With those words she turned about, and retracing her steps, advanced with her face towards me.", "그 말과 함께 그녀는 몸을 돌려, 왔던 길을 되짚어 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 향한 채 다가왔다."], ["It was the face of an elderly woman, brown, rugged, and healthy, with nothing dishonest or suspicious in the look of it.", "나이 지긋한 여자의 얼굴이었다. 볕에 그을고 거칠지만 건강해 보였으며, 그 표정에는 부정하거나 수상쩍은 구석이 조금도 없었다."], ["Close to the church she stopped to pull her shawl closer round her.", "교회 가까이에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 숄을 몸에 더 바싹 여몄다."]], [["“Queer,”", "“별나지.”"], ["she said to herself, “always queer, with her whims and her ways, ever since I can remember her.", "그녀가 혼잣말을 했다. “내가 저 애를 알게 된 이후로 늘 별났어, 그 변덕이며 그 하는 짓이며."], ["Harmless, though—as harmless, poor soul, as a little child.”", "그래도 해롭지는 않아—가엾은 것, 어린애만큼이나 해롭지가 않지.”"]], [["She sighed—looked about the burial-ground nervously—shook her head, as if the dreary prospect by no means pleased her, and disappeared round the corner of the church.", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬더니—불안한 눈으로 매장지를 둘러보고—이 을씨년스러운 광경이 조금도 마음에 들지 않는다는 듯 고개를 내젓고는, 교회 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다."]], [["I doubted for a moment whether I ought to follow and speak to her or not.", "나는 잠시, 그녀를 따라가 말을 걸어야 할지 말지 망설였다."], ["My intense anxiety to find myself face to face with her companion helped me to decide in the negative.", "그녀의 동행과 얼굴을 맞대고 싶은 간절한 조바심이 아니라는 쪽으로 마음을 정하게 해 주었다."], ["I could ensure seeing the woman in the shawl by waiting near the churchyard until she came back—although it seemed more than doubtful whether she could give me the information of which I was in search.", "숄을 두른 여자는 그녀가 돌아올 때까지 교회 묘지 근처에서 기다리기만 하면 반드시 만날 수 있었다—하기야 그녀가 내가 찾고 있는 정보를 줄 수 있을지는 몹시 의심스러웠지만."], ["The person who had delivered the letter was of little consequence.", "편지를 전달한 사람은 별로 중요하지 않았다."], ["The person who had written it was the one centre of interest, and the one source of information, and that person I now felt convinced was before me in the churchyard.", "그것을 쓴 사람이야말로 유일한 관심의 중심이자 유일한 정보의 원천이었고, 그 사람이 지금 이 교회 묘지 안에서 내 앞에 있다고 나는 확신하게 되었다."]], [["While these ideas were passing through my mind I saw the woman in the cloak approach close to the grave, and stand looking at it for a little while.", "이런 생각들이 마음속을 스쳐 가는 동안, 나는 망토를 걸친 여자가 무덤 가까이 다가가 잠시 그것을 바라보며 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She then glanced all round her, and taking a white linen cloth or handkerchief from under her cloak, turned aside towards the brook.", "이윽고 그녀는 사방을 빙 둘러보더니, 망토 밑에서 흰 아마포 천인지 손수건인지를 꺼내 개울 쪽으로 비켜 갔다."], ["The little stream ran into the churchyard under a tiny archway in the bottom of the wall, and ran out again, after a winding course of a few dozen yards, under a similar opening.", "그 작은 개울은 담장 밑에 뚫린 자그마한 아치를 지나 교회 묘지로 흘러들었다가, 수십 야드를 굽이돌아 흐른 뒤 비슷한 구멍을 통해 다시 흘러 나갔다."], ["She dipped the cloth in the water, and returned to the grave.", "그녀는 천을 물에 적셔 무덤으로 돌아왔다."], ["I saw her kiss the white cross, then kneel down before the inscription, and apply her wet cloth to the cleansing of it.", "나는 그녀가 흰 십자가에 입을 맞추고, 이어 비문 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 앉아 젖은 천으로 그것을 닦기 시작하는 것을 보았다."]], [["After considering how I could show myself with the least possible chance of frightening her, I resolved to cross the wall before me, to skirt round it outside, and to enter the churchyard again by the stile near the grave, in order that she might see me as I approached.", "어떻게 하면 그녀를 놀라게 할 위험을 가장 줄이면서 모습을 드러낼 수 있을까 궁리한 끝에, 나는 앞의 담을 넘어 바깥으로 돌아 나갔다가 무덤 가까이 있는 층계 디딤대를 통해 다시 교회 묘지로 들어가기로 했다. 그래야 내가 다가가는 모습을 그녀가 볼 수 있을 터였다."], ["She was so absorbed over her employment that she did not hear me coming until I had stepped over the stile.", "그녀는 자기 일에 어찌나 몰두해 있었던지 내가 디딤대를 넘어설 때까지도 다가오는 소리를 듣지 못했다."], ["Then she looked up, started to her feet with a faint cry, and stood facing me in speechless and motionless terror.", "그러다 고개를 들더니, 나직한 비명과 함께 벌떡 일어나, 말도 못 하고 꼼짝도 못 하는 공포에 사로잡힌 채 나를 마주하고 섰다."]], [["“Don’t be frightened,”", "“놀라지 마십시오.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “Surely you remember me?”", "“설마 나를 기억하지 못하는 건 아니겠지요?”"]], [["I stopped while I spoke—then advanced a few steps gently—then stopped again—and so approached by little and little till I was close to her.", "나는 말하면서 멈춰 섰다가—그러고는 몇 걸음 조용히 나아갔다가—다시 멈춰 섰고—그렇게 조금씩 조금씩 다가가 마침내 그녀 가까이에 이르렀다."], ["If there had been any doubt still left in my mind, it must have been now set at rest.", "내 마음에 아직 어떤 의심이 남아 있었다 해도 이제는 말끔히 가셨을 것이다."], ["There, speaking affrightedly for itself—there was the same face confronting me over Mrs. Fairlie’s grave which had first looked into mine on the high-road by night.", "저기, 겁에 질린 채 스스로를 증언하며—페얼리 부인의 무덤 너머로 나를 마주한 그 얼굴은, 밤중의 큰길에서 처음으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았던 바로 그 얼굴이었다."]], [["“You remember me?”", "“나를 기억하지요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“We met very late, and I helped you to find the way to London.", "“우리는 아주 늦은 시각에 만났고, 나는 당신이 런던으로 가는 길을 찾도록 도와주었습니다."], ["Surely you have not forgotten that?”", "설마 그것을 잊지는 않았겠지요?”"]], [["Her features relaxed, and she drew a heavy breath of relief.", "그녀의 이목구비가 풀리며, 그녀는 안도의 깊은 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["I saw the new life of recognition stirring slowly under the death-like stillness which fear had set on her face.", "공포가 그녀의 얼굴에 새겨 놓았던 죽음 같은 고요 아래에서, 알아봄이라는 새로운 생기가 천천히 움직이는 것이 보였다."]], [["“Don’t attempt to speak to me just yet,”", "“지금 당장은 나에게 말을 하려 애쓰지 마십시오.”"], ["I went on.", "내가 말을 이었다."], ["“Take time to recover yourself—take time to feel quite certain that I am a friend.”", "“정신을 추스를 시간을 가지세요—내가 친구라는 것을 온전히 확신할 시간을 가지세요.”"]], [["“You are very kind to me,”", "“저에게 참 친절하시군요.”"], ["she murmured.", "그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["“As kind now as you were then.”", "“그때 그러셨던 것처럼 지금도 친절하세요.”"]], [["She stopped, and I kept silence on my side.", "그녀가 말을 멈추었고, 나도 나대로 침묵을 지켰다."], ["I was not granting time for composure to her only, I was gaining time also for myself.", "나는 그녀에게만 마음을 가라앉힐 시간을 준 것이 아니라, 나 자신을 위한 시간도 벌고 있었다."], ["Under the wan wild evening light, that woman and I were met together again, a grave between us, the dead about us, the lonesome hills closing us round on every side.", "창백하고 스산한 저녁빛 아래, 그 여자와 나는 다시 마주하고 있었다. 우리 사이에는 무덤이 있었고, 우리 둘레에는 죽은 이들이 있었으며, 쓸쓸한 언덕들이 사방에서 우리를 에워싸고 있었다."], ["The time, the place, the circumstances under which we now stood face to face in the evening stillness of that dreary valley—the lifelong interests which might hang suspended on the next chance words that passed between us—the sense that, for aught I knew to the contrary, the whole future of Laura Fairlie’s life might be determined, for good or for evil, by my winning or losing the confidence of the forlorn creature who stood trembling by her mother’s grave—all threatened to shake the steadiness and the self-control on which every inch of the progress I might yet make now depended.", "그 을씨년스러운 골짜기의 저녁 정적 속에서 우리가 이렇게 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있는 시간과 장소와 정황—우리 사이에 오갈 다음 몇 마디 우연한 말에 매달려 있을지도 모르는 한평생의 이해관계—내가 달리 알지 못하는 한, 로라 페얼리의 온 미래가 제 어머니의 무덤 곁에서 떨고 서 있는 이 버림받은 존재의 신뢰를 내가 얻느냐 잃느냐에 따라 좋게든 나쁘게든 결정될지도 모른다는 생각—이 모든 것이, 내가 앞으로 나아갈 한 치 한 치가 온전히 달려 있는 그 침착함과 자제력을 뒤흔들려고 위협했다."], ["I tried hard, as I felt this, to possess myself of all my resources; I did my utmost to turn the few moments for reflection to the best account.", "그것을 느끼면서 나는 내 모든 역량을 그러모으려 애썼다. 생각할 수 있는 그 몇 순간을 최대한 살리려고 온 힘을 다했다."]], [["“Are you calmer now?”", "“이제 좀 진정되었습니까?”"], ["I said, as soon as I thought it time to speak again.", "다시 말을 걸어도 되겠다 싶었을 때 내가 말했다."], ["“Can you talk to me without feeling frightened, and without forgetting that I am a friend?”", "“겁내지 않고, 또 내가 친구라는 것도 잊지 않고, 나와 이야기할 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["“How did you come here?”", "“여기 어떻게 오셨어요?”"], ["she asked, without noticing what I had just said to her.", "그녀는 내가 방금 한 말에는 아랑곳하지 않고 물었다."]], [["“Don’t you remember my telling you, when we last met, that I was going to Cumberland?", "“지난번 만났을 때 내가 컴벌랜드로 갈 참이라고 말했던 것을 기억하지 못합니까?"], ["I have been in Cumberland ever since—I have been staying all the time at Limmeridge House.”", "그 뒤로 줄곧 컴벌랜드에 있었습니다—그동안 내내 리머리지 하우스에 머물러 왔지요.”"]], [["“At Limmeridge House!”", "“리머리지 하우스에요!”"], ["Her pale face brightened as she repeated the words, her wandering eyes fixed on me with a sudden interest.", "그 말을 되뇌는 그녀의 창백한 얼굴이 환해졌고, 헤매던 눈이 갑작스러운 관심을 담고 나에게 못 박혔다."], ["“Ah, how happy you must have been!”", "“아, 얼마나 행복하셨을까요!”"], ["she said, looking at me eagerly, without a shadow of its former distrust left in her expression.", "그녀는 이전의 불신이라고는 그림자도 남지 않은 표정으로 나를 열심히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["I took advantage of her newly-aroused confidence in me to observe her face, with an attention and a curiosity which I had hitherto restrained myself from showing, for caution’s sake.", "나는 새로이 일어난 그녀의 신뢰를 이용해, 여태껏 조심하느라 삼가 왔던 주의와 호기심을 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈다."], ["I looked at her, with my mind full of that other lovely face which had so ominously recalled her to my memory on the terrace by moonlight.", "달빛 아래 테라스에서 그토록 불길하게 그녀를 내 기억에 되살려 놓았던 그 또 다른 사랑스러운 얼굴로 마음을 가득 채운 채 나는 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["I had seen Anne Catherick’s likeness in Miss Fairlie.", "나는 페얼리 양에게서 앤 캐서릭의 닮은꼴을 보았었다."], ["I now saw Miss Fairlie’s likeness in Anne Catherick—saw it all the more clearly because the points of dissimilarity between the two were presented to me as well as the points of resemblance.", "이제는 앤 캐서릭에게서 페얼리 양의 닮은꼴을 보았다—두 사람 사이의 닮은 점만이 아니라 닮지 않은 점까지 함께 눈앞에 놓였기에 그만큼 더 또렷하게 보였다."], ["In the general outline of the countenance and general proportion of the features—in the colour of the hair and in the little nervous uncertainty about the lips—in the height and size of the figure, and the carriage of the head and body, the likeness appeared even more startling than I had ever felt it to be yet.", "얼굴의 전체적인 윤곽과 이목구비의 전체적인 균형에서—머리카락의 빛깔과 입술 언저리의 작고 예민한 흔들림에서—키와 몸집에서, 그리고 머리와 몸을 가누는 자세에서, 그 닮음은 내가 지금껏 느껴 본 어느 때보다도 더 놀랍게 보였다."], ["But there the resemblance ended, and the dissimilarity, in details, began.", "그러나 닮음은 거기서 끝나고, 세부에서의 다름이 시작되었다."], ["The delicate beauty of Miss Fairlie’s complexion, the transparent clearness of her eyes, the smooth purity of her skin, the tender bloom of colour on her lips, were all missing from the worn weary face that was now turned towards mine.", "페얼리 양의 살빛이 지닌 섬세한 아름다움, 눈의 투명한 맑음, 피부의 매끄러운 순결함, 입술에 어린 부드러운 혈색—그 모든 것이 지금 내 쪽으로 돌려진 이 지치고 여윈 얼굴에는 없었다."], ["Although I hated myself even for thinking such a thing, still, while I looked at the woman before me, the idea would force itself into my mind that one sad change, in the future, was all that was wanting to make the likeness complete, which I now saw to be so imperfect in detail.", "그런 것을 생각한다는 것만으로도 나 자신이 미웠지만, 그럼에도 눈앞의 여자를 바라보는 동안 한 가지 생각이 기어이 내 마음속으로 밀고 들어왔다. 세부에서는 그토록 불완전하다고 내가 지금 본 그 닮음을 완성하는 데 모자란 것은, 앞날에 닥칠 단 하나의 슬픈 변화뿐이라는 생각이었다."], ["If ever sorrow and suffering set their profaning marks on the youth and beauty of Miss Fairlie’s face, then, and then only, Anne Catherick and she would be the twin-sisters of chance resemblance, the living reflections of one another.", "언젠가 슬픔과 고통이 페얼리 양의 젊음과 아름다움 위에 그 더럽히는 자국을 새긴다면, 그때에야, 오직 그때에야 앤 캐서릭과 그녀는 우연한 닮음이 낳은 쌍둥이 자매가, 서로를 비추는 살아 있는 거울이 될 터였다."]], [["I shuddered at the thought.", "그 생각에 나는 몸서리쳤다."], ["There was something horrible in the blind unreasoning distrust of the future which the mere passage of it through my mind seemed to imply.", "그런 생각이 마음속을 스쳐 갔다는 사실 자체가 품고 있는 듯한, 미래에 대한 눈멀고 이치 없는 불신에는 어딘가 끔찍한 데가 있었다."], ["It was a welcome interruption to be roused by feeling Anne Catherick’s hand laid on my shoulder.", "앤 캐서릭의 손이 내 어깨에 얹히는 것을 느끼고 정신이 들었을 때, 그것은 반가운 방해였다."], ["The touch was as stealthy and as sudden as that other touch which had petrified me from head to foot on the night when we first met.", "그 손길은 우리가 처음 만난 밤에 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 굳어 버리게 했던 그 손길만큼이나 은밀하고 갑작스러웠다."]], [["“You are looking at me, and you are thinking of something,”", "“저를 보고 계시는군요. 그리고 무언가를 생각하고 계세요.”"], ["she said, with her strange breathless rapidity of utterance.", "그녀가 그 이상하리만치 숨 가쁘고 빠른 말투로 말했다."], ["“What is it?”", "“무슨 생각이에요?”"]], [["“Nothing extraordinary,”", "“별것 아닙니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“I was only wondering how you came here.”", "“그저 당신이 어떻게 여기까지 오게 되었는지 궁금했을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“I came with a friend who is very good to me.", "“저에게 아주 잘해 주는 친구와 함께 왔어요."], ["I have only been here two days.”", "여기 온 지는 이틀밖에 안 됐고요.”"]], [["“And you found your way to this place yesterday?”", "“그리고 어제 이곳을 찾아냈던 거군요?”"]], [["“How do you know that?”", "“그걸 어떻게 아세요?”"]], [["“I only guessed it.”", "“그저 짐작해 보았을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["She turned from me, and knelt down before the inscription once more.", "그녀는 나에게서 몸을 돌려 다시 한 번 비문 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“Where should I go if not here?”", "“여기가 아니면 제가 어디로 가겠어요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“The friend who was better than a mother to me is the only friend I have to visit at Limmeridge.", "“저에게 어머니보다 더 나은 분이었던 그 친구가, 리머리지에서 제가 찾아뵐 수 있는 유일한 친구인걸요."], ["Oh, it makes my heart ache to see a stain on her tomb!", "아, 그분 무덤에 얼룩이 진 것을 보면 가슴이 아파요!"], ["It ought to be kept white as snow, for her sake.", "그분을 생각하면 눈처럼 희게 지켜져야 마땅한데."], ["I was tempted to begin cleaning it yesterday, and I can’t help coming back to go on with it to-day.", "어제 이걸 닦기 시작하고 싶은 마음이 들었고, 오늘도 이어서 하러 오지 않을 수가 없었어요."], ["Is there anything wrong in that?", "그게 잘못된 일인가요?"], ["I hope not.", "아니었으면 좋겠어요."], ["Surely nothing can be wrong that I do for Mrs. Fairlie’s sake?”", "페얼리 부인을 위해 하는 일이라면 무엇도 잘못일 리 없잖아요?”"]], [["The old grateful sense of her benefactress’s kindness was evidently the ruling idea still in the poor creature’s mind—the narrow mind which had but too plainly opened to no other lasting impression since that first impression of her younger and happier days.", "은인의 친절에 대한 오래된 감사의 마음이 여전히 이 가련한 존재의 마음을 지배하는 생각임이 분명했다—더 젊고 행복하던 시절의 그 첫 인상 이후로 다른 어떤 지속적인 인상에도 너무나 분명히 열린 적이 없었던 그 좁은 마음을 말이다."], ["I saw that my best chance of winning her confidence lay in encouraging her to proceed with the artless employment which she had come into the burial-ground to pursue.", "나는 그녀의 신뢰를 얻을 최선의 길이, 그녀가 이 매장지에 하러 온 그 천진한 일을 계속하도록 북돋아 주는 데 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["She resumed it at once, on my telling her she might do so, touching the hard marble as tenderly as if it had been a sentient thing, and whispering the words of the inscription to herself, over and over again, as if the lost days of her girlhood had returned and she was patiently learning her lesson once more at Mrs. Fairlie’s knees.", "그래도 된다고 내가 말하자 그녀는 곧바로 일을 다시 시작했고, 마치 감각이 있는 것이라도 되는 양 단단한 대리석을 부드럽게 어루만졌으며, 마치 소녀 시절의 잃어버린 날들이 되돌아와 페얼리 부인의 무릎 앞에서 다시 한 번 끈기 있게 배운 것을 외우기라도 하듯, 비문의 글귀를 몇 번이고 되풀이해 혼잣말로 속삭였다."]], [["“Should you wonder very much,”", "“아주 이상하게 여기실까요.”"], ["I said, preparing the way as cautiously as I could for the questions that were to come, “if I owned that it is a satisfaction to me, as well as a surprise, to see you here?", "나는 앞으로 던질 질문들을 위해 되도록 조심스럽게 길을 닦으며 말했다. “여기서 당신을 만난 것이 나에게는 놀라움일 뿐 아니라 안심이기도 하다고 고백한다면 말입니다."], ["I felt very uneasy about you after you left me in the cab.”", "그날 밤 당신이 나를 두고 마차에서 내린 뒤로 나는 당신 일이 몹시 마음에 걸렸습니다.”"]], [["She looked up quickly and suspiciously.", "그녀는 재빨리, 그리고 의심스러운 눈길로 고개를 들었다."]], [["“Uneasy,”", "“마음에 걸렸다고요.”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되뇌었다."], ["“Why?”", "“왜요?”"]], [["“A strange thing happened after we parted that night.", "“그날 밤 우리가 헤어진 뒤에 이상한 일이 있었습니다."], ["Two men overtook me in a chaise.", "두 남자가 이륜마차를 타고 나를 앞질러 왔지요."], ["They did not see where I was standing, but they stopped near me, and spoke to a policeman on the other side of the way.”", "그들은 내가 서 있는 곳을 보지는 못했지만 내 근처에 멈춰 서서, 길 건너편에 있던 순경에게 말을 걸었습니다.”"]], [["She instantly suspended her employment.", "그녀는 즉시 하던 일을 멈추었다."], ["The hand holding the damp cloth with which she had been cleaning the inscription dropped to her side.", "비문을 닦던 젖은 천을 쥔 손이 옆으로 툭 떨어졌다."], ["The other hand grasped the marble cross at the head of the grave.", "다른 손은 무덤 머리맡의 대리석 십자가를 붙들었다."], ["Her face turned towards me slowly, with the blank look of terror set rigidly on it once more.", "그녀의 얼굴이 천천히 나를 향해 돌려졌고, 거기에는 다시 한 번 텅 빈 공포의 표정이 굳어 있었다."], ["I went on at all hazards—it was too late now to draw back.", "나는 무슨 일이 있어도 계속하기로 했다—이제 와서 물러서기에는 너무 늦었다."]], [["“The two men spoke to the policeman,”", "“그 두 남자는 순경에게,” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “and asked him if he had seen you.", "“당신을 보았느냐고 물었습니다."], ["He had not seen you; and then one of the men spoke again, and said you had escaped from his Asylum.”", "순경은 보지 못했다고 했지요. 그러자 그중 한 남자가 다시 입을 열어, 당신이 자기 정신병원에서 도망쳤다고 말했습니다.”"]], [["She sprang to her feet as if my last words had set the pursuers on her track.", "그녀는 내 마지막 말이 추격자들을 자기 뒤로 풀어놓기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Stop!", "“멈춰요!"], ["and hear the end,”", "그리고 끝까지 들으세요.”"], ["I cried.", "내가 외쳤다."], ["“Stop!", "“멈춰요!"], ["and you shall know how I befriended you.", "그러면 내가 어떻게 당신 편이 되어 주었는지 알게 될 겁니다."], ["A word from me would have told the men which way you had gone—and I never spoke that word.", "나에게서 한마디만 나왔어도 그 남자들에게 당신이 어느 쪽으로 갔는지 알려 주었을 텐데—나는 그 한마디를 끝내 하지 않았습니다."], ["I helped your escape—I made it safe and certain.", "나는 당신의 도피를 도왔습니다—그것을 안전하고 확실하게 만들어 주었어요."], ["Think, try to think.", "생각해 보세요, 생각하려고 애써 보세요."], ["Try to understand what I tell you.”", "내가 하는 말을 이해하려고 애써 보세요.”"]], [["My manner seemed to influence her more than my words.", "내 태도가 내 말보다 그녀에게 더 영향을 미치는 것 같았다."], ["She made an effort to grasp the new idea.", "그녀는 이 새로운 생각을 붙잡으려고 애를 썼다."], ["Her hands shifted the damp cloth hesitatingly from one to the other, exactly as they had shifted the little travelling-bag on the night when I first saw her.", "그녀의 두 손은 젖은 천을 이 손에서 저 손으로 머뭇머뭇 옮겼는데, 내가 그녀를 처음 본 밤에 작은 여행 가방을 옮기던 것과 똑같았다."], ["Slowly the purpose of my words seemed to force its way through the confusion and agitation of her mind.", "천천히 내 말의 뜻이 그녀 마음의 혼란과 동요를 뚫고 길을 내는 듯했다."], ["Slowly her features relaxed, and her eyes looked at me with their expression gaining in curiosity what it was fast losing in fear.", "천천히 그녀의 이목구비가 풀렸고, 그녀의 눈은 빠르게 사라져 가는 두려움만큼 호기심을 얻어 가는 표정으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“YOU don’t think I ought to be back in the Asylum, do you?”", "“당신은 제가 그 정신병원으로 돌아가야 한다고 생각하지 않으시죠, 그렇죠?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Certainly not.", "“물론 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다."], ["I am glad you escaped from it—I am glad I helped you.”", "당신이 거기서 빠져나온 것이 기쁘고—내가 당신을 도왔다는 것이 기쁩니다.”"]], [["“Yes, yes, you did help me indeed; you helped me at the hard part,”", "“네, 네, 당신은 정말로 저를 도와주셨어요. 가장 어려운 대목에서 도와주셨죠.”"], ["she went on a little vacantly.", "그녀가 조금 멍하니 말을 이었다."], ["“It was easy to escape, or I should not have got away.", "“도망쳐 나오는 건 쉬웠어요. 안 그랬으면 빠져나오지 못했을 거예요."], ["They never suspected me as they suspected the others.", "그 사람들은 다른 사람들을 의심하듯 저를 의심하지는 않았거든요."], ["I was so quiet, and so obedient, and so easily frightened.", "저는 아주 조용하고, 아주 순종적이고, 아주 잘 겁을 먹었으니까요."], ["The finding London was the hard part, and there you helped me.", "런던을 찾아가는 게 어려운 대목이었는데, 거기서 당신이 도와주셨어요."], ["Did I thank you at the time?", "제가 그때 고맙다고 인사드렸던가요?"], ["I thank you now very kindly.”", "지금 아주 고맙게 인사드려요.”"]], [["“Was the Asylum far from where you met me? Come!", "“당신이 나를 만난 곳에서 그 정신병원은 멀었습니까?"], ["show that you believe me to be your friend, and tell me where it was.”", "자, 나를 친구로 믿는다는 것을 보여 주고, 그곳이 어디였는지 말해 주세요.”"]], [["She mentioned the place—a private Asylum, as its situation informed me; a private Asylum not very far from the spot where I had seen her—and then, with evident suspicion of the use to which I might put her answer, anxiously repeated her former inquiry, “You don’t think I ought to be taken back, do you?”", "그녀는 그 장소를 말했다—그 위치로 미루어 알 수 있듯 사설 정신병원이었고, 내가 그녀를 보았던 지점에서 그리 멀지 않은 사설 정신병원이었다—그러고는 내가 그 대답을 어디에 쓸지 뻔히 의심하는 낯빛으로, 조금 전의 물음을 근심스레 되풀이했다. “저를 다시 데려가야 한다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠, 그렇죠?”"]], [["“Once again, I am glad you escaped—I am glad you prospered well after you left me,”", "“다시 말하지만, 나는 당신이 빠져나온 것이 기쁘고—나와 헤어진 뒤에 잘 지냈다는 것이 기쁩니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“You said you had a friend in London to go to.", "“런던에 찾아갈 친구가 있다고 했었지요."], ["Did you find the friend?”", "그 친구를 찾았습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["It was very late, but there was a girl up at needle-work in the house, and she helped me to rouse Mrs. Clements.", "아주 늦은 시각이었지만 그 집에 바느질을 하느라 깨어 있던 아가씨가 있어서, 그 사람이 클레멘츠 부인을 깨우는 걸 도와주었어요."], ["Mrs. Clements is my friend.", "클레멘츠 부인이 제 친구예요."], ["A good, kind woman, but not like Mrs. Fairlie.", "착하고 다정한 분이지만 페얼리 부인 같지는 않아요."], ["Ah no, nobody is like Mrs. Fairlie!”", "아, 아니에요, 페얼리 부인 같은 분은 아무도 없어요!”"]], [["“Is Mrs. Clements an old friend of yours?", "“클레멘츠 부인은 오래된 친구입니까?"], ["Have you known her a long time?”", "오래전부터 알고 지냈나요?”"]], [["“Yes, she was a neighbour of ours once, at home, in Hampshire, and liked me, and took care of me when I was a little girl.", "“네, 예전에 고향인 햄프셔에서 우리 이웃이었어요. 저를 예뻐하셨고, 제가 어린 계집아이였을 때 저를 돌봐 주셨죠."], ["Years ago, when she went away from us, she wrote down in my Prayer-book for me where she was going to live in London, and she said, ‘If you are ever in trouble, Anne, come to me.", "여러 해 전, 우리 곁을 떠나실 때 제 기도서에 런던 어디에서 살게 될지를 적어 주시면서 이렇게 말씀하셨어요. ‘앤, 언제든 어려운 일이 생기거든 나에게 오너라."], ["I have no husband alive to say me nay, and no children to look after, and I will take care of you.’ Kind words, were they not?", "나에게는 안 된다고 말릴 남편도 살아 있지 않고 돌봐야 할 자식도 없으니, 내가 너를 돌봐 주마.’ 다정한 말이지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["I suppose I remember them because they were kind.", "다정한 말이라서 제가 기억하는 것 같아요."], ["It’s little enough I remember besides—little enough, little enough!”", "그것 말고는 기억하는 게 얼마 없어요—얼마 없어요, 정말 얼마 없어요!”"]], [["“Had you no father or mother to take care of you?”", "“당신을 돌봐 줄 아버지나 어머니는 없었습니까?”"]], [["“Father?—I never saw him—I never heard mother speak of him.", "“아버지요?—저는 아버지를 본 적이 없어요—어머니가 아버지 얘기를 하는 것도 들어 본 적이 없고요."], ["Father?", "아버지요?"], ["Ah, dear!", "아, 저런!"], ["he is dead, I suppose.”", "돌아가셨겠지요.”"]], [["“And your mother?”", "“그럼 어머니는요?”"]], [["“I don’t get on well with her.", "“어머니하고는 사이가 좋지 않아요."], ["We are a trouble and a fear to each other.”", "우리는 서로에게 골칫거리이고 두려움이에요.”"]], [["A trouble and a fear to each other!", "서로에게 골칫거리이고 두려움이라니!"], ["At those words the suspicion crossed my mind, for the first time, that her mother might be the person who had placed her under restraint.", "그 말에 처음으로, 그녀를 가두어 놓은 사람이 그녀의 어머니일지도 모른다는 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다."]], [["“Don’t ask me about mother,”", "“어머니 얘기는 묻지 마세요.”"], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“I’d rather talk of Mrs.", "“저는 클레멘츠 부인 얘기가 더 좋아요."], ["Clements. Mrs.", "클레멘츠 부인은 당신과 같아요."], ["Clements is like you, she doesn’t think that I ought to be back in the Asylum, and she is as glad as you are that I escaped from it.", "제가 그 정신병원으로 돌아가야 한다고 생각하지 않으시고, 제가 거기서 빠져나온 걸 당신처럼 기뻐하세요."], ["She cried over my misfortune, and said it must be kept secret from everybody.”", "그분은 제 불행을 두고 우셨어요. 그리고 그건 모두에게 비밀로 해야 한다고 하셨고요.”"]], [["Her “misfortune.”", "그녀의 “불행”."], ["In what sense was she using that word?", "그녀는 그 말을 어떤 뜻으로 쓰고 있는 것일까?"], ["In a sense which might explain her motive in writing the anonymous letter?", "익명의 편지를 쓴 동기를 설명해 줄 만한 뜻으로?"], ["In a sense which might show it to be the too common and too customary motive that has led many a woman to interpose anonymous hindrances to the marriage of the man who has ruined her?", "자기를 파멸시킨 남자의 결혼에 익명의 훼방을 놓게 만든, 너무나 흔하고 너무나 상투적인 그 동기임을 드러내는 뜻으로?"], ["I resolved to attempt the clearing up of this doubt before more words passed between us on either side.", "나는 어느 쪽에서든 더 많은 말이 오가기 전에 이 의문을 밝혀 보기로 결심했다."]], [["“What misfortune?”", "“무슨 불행 말입니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“The misfortune of my being shut up,”", "“제가 갇혔던 불행이지요.”"], ["she answered, with every appearance of feeling surprised at my question.", "그녀는 내 질문에 놀란 기색이 역력한 채로 대답했다."], ["“What other misfortune could there be?”", "“그 말고 무슨 불행이 있을 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["I determined to persist, as delicately and forbearingly as possible.", "나는 되도록 섬세하고 참을성 있게 밀고 나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["It was of very great importance that I should be absolutely sure of every step in the investigation which I now gained in advance.", "이제부터 앞질러 얻어 내는 조사의 걸음 하나하나를 절대적으로 확신하는 것이 대단히 중요했다."]], [["“There is another misfortune,”", "“또 다른 불행이 있습니다.”"], ["I said, “to which a woman may be liable, and by which she may suffer lifelong sorrow and shame.”", "내가 말했다. “여자가 겪을 수 있는, 그리고 그로 인해 한평생의 슬픔과 수치를 당할 수도 있는 불행 말입니다.”"]], [["“What is it?”", "“그게 뭔데요?”"], ["she asked eagerly.", "그녀가 다급하게 물었다."]], [["“The misfortune of believing too innocently in her own virtue, and in the faith and honour of the man she loves,”", "“자기 자신의 정절과, 자기가 사랑하는 남자의 신의와 명예를 너무 순진하게 믿은 불행이지요.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["She looked up at me with the artless bewilderment of a child.", "그녀는 아이 같은 천진한 당혹감으로 나를 올려다보았다."], ["Not the slightest confusion or change of colour—not the faintest trace of any secret consciousness of shame struggling to the surface appeared in her face—that face which betrayed every other emotion with such transparent clearness.", "어떤 혼란도, 낯빛의 변화도—표면으로 떠오르려 애쓰는 은밀한 수치의 자각 같은 것은 아주 희미한 자취조차—그녀의 얼굴에는 나타나지 않았다. 다른 온갖 감정은 그토록 투명하게 드러내던 그 얼굴에 말이다."], ["No words that ever were spoken could have assured me, as her look and manner now assured me, that the motive which I had assigned for her writing the letter and sending it to Miss Fairlie was plainly and distinctly the wrong one.", "지금 그녀의 표정과 태도가 나를 확신시켜 준 것만큼 나를 확신시켜 줄 수 있는 말은 세상에 없었을 것이다. 그녀가 그 편지를 써서 페얼리 양에게 보낸 동기라고 내가 지목했던 것은 명백하고 분명하게 틀렸다는 확신 말이다."], ["That doubt, at any rate, was now set at rest; but the very removal of it opened a new prospect of uncertainty.", "적어도 그 의문은 이제 풀렸다. 그러나 그것이 걷히자마자 새로운 불확실의 전망이 열렸다."], ["The letter, as I knew from positive testimony, pointed at Sir Percival Glyde, though it did not name him.", "확실한 증언으로 내가 알고 있듯, 그 편지는 이름을 대지는 않았어도 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 가리키고 있었다."], ["She must have had some strong motive, originating in some deep sense of injury, for secretly denouncing him to Miss Fairlie in such terms as she had employed, and that motive was unquestionably not to be traced to the loss of her innocence and her character.", "그녀가 자신이 쓴 그런 표현으로 그를 페얼리 양에게 은밀히 고발하기까지에는, 깊은 상처의 감정에서 비롯한 어떤 강한 동기가 있었을 것이다. 그리고 그 동기가 정절과 명예의 상실에서 온 것이 아님은 의문의 여지가 없었다."], ["Whatever wrong he might have inflicted on her was not of that nature.", "그가 그녀에게 어떤 몹쓸 짓을 저질렀든 그런 종류의 것은 아니었다."], ["Of what nature could it be?", "그렇다면 어떤 종류의 것일 수 있을까?"]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.”"], ["she said, after evidently trying hard, and trying in vain, to discover the meaning of the words I had last said to her.", "그녀는 내가 마지막으로 한 말의 뜻을 알아내려고 무던히 애를 쓰다가, 그것도 헛되이 애쓴 끝에 말했다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "“괜찮습니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Let us go on with what we were talking about.", "“아까 하던 이야기를 계속하지요."], ["Tell me how long you stayed with Mrs. Clements in London, and how you came here.”", "런던에서 클레멘츠 부인과 얼마나 오래 지냈는지, 그리고 어떻게 여기까지 오게 되었는지 말해 주세요.”"]], [["“How long?”", "“얼마나 오래요?”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되뇌었다."], ["“I stayed with Mrs. Clements till we both came to this place, two days ago.”", "“이틀 전에 우리 둘이 이곳으로 올 때까지 클레멘츠 부인과 함께 지냈어요.”"]], [["“You are living in the village, then?”", "“그럼 마을에서 지내고 있군요?” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “It is strange I should not have heard of you, though you have only been here two days.”", "“여기 온 지 이틀밖에 안 됐다고는 해도, 내가 당신 소식을 못 들었다니 이상하군요.”"]], [["“No, no, not in the village.", "“아니에요, 아니에요, 마을은 아니에요."], ["Three miles away at a farm.", "3마일 떨어진 농장이에요."], ["Do you know the farm?", "그 농장을 아세요?"], ["They call it Todd’s Corner.”", "토드 코너라고 하던데요.”"]], [["I remembered the place perfectly—we had often passed by it in our drives.", "나는 그곳을 아주 잘 기억하고 있었다—마차로 나갈 때 자주 그 앞을 지나쳤다."], ["It was one of the oldest farms in the neighbourhood, situated in a solitary, sheltered spot, inland at the junction of two hills.", "그 근방에서 가장 오래된 농장 가운데 하나로, 내륙 쪽 두 언덕이 만나는 자리의 외지고 아늑한 곳에 있었다."]], [["“They are relations of Mrs. Clements at Todd’s Corner,”", "“토드 코너에 있는 분들이 클레멘츠 부인의 친척이에요.”"], ["she went on, “and they had often asked her to go and see them.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. “그분들이 자주 와서 보라고 청했다지 뭐예요."], ["She said she would go, and take me with her, for the quiet and the fresh air.", "부인은 가겠다고, 조용하고 공기 좋은 데서 지내라고 저도 데려가겠다고 하셨고요."], ["It was very kind, was it not?", "참 다정하지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["I would have gone anywhere to be quiet, and safe, and out of the way.", "저는 조용하고, 안전하고, 남의 눈에 띄지 않을 수만 있다면 어디든 갔을 거예요."], ["But when I heard that Todd’s Corner was near Limmeridge—oh!", "그런데 토드 코너가 리머리지 근처라는 말을 들었을 때는—아!"], ["I was so happy I would have walked all the way barefoot to get there, and see the schools and the village and Limmeridge House again.", "어찌나 기뻤는지 학교와 마을과 리머리지 하우스를 다시 보러 맨발로라도 그 먼 길을 걸어갔을 거예요."], ["They are very good people at Todd’s Corner.", "토드 코너 사람들은 아주 좋은 분들이에요."], ["I hope I shall stay there a long time.", "거기서 오래 지낼 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["There is only one thing I don’t like about them, and don’t like about Mrs. Clements——”", "그분들에 대해서, 그리고 클레멘츠 부인에 대해서 딱 한 가지 마음에 들지 않는 게 있는데——”"]], [["“What is it?”", "“그게 뭡니까?”"]], [["“They will tease me about dressing all in white—they say it looks so particular.", "“온통 흰옷만 입는다고 저를 놀리시는 거예요—너무 유별나 보인다고들 하시죠."], ["How do they know? Mrs.", "그분들이 뭘 아신다고요?"], ["Fairlie knew best. Mrs.", "페얼리 부인이 제일 잘 아셨어요."], ["Fairlie would never have made me wear this ugly blue cloak!", "페얼리 부인이라면 절대로 저에게 이 보기 싫은 파란 망토를 입히지 않으셨을 거예요!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["she was fond of white in her lifetime, and here is white stone about her grave—and I am making it whiter for her sake.", "그분은 살아 계실 때 흰색을 좋아하셨고, 여기 그분 무덤에도 흰 돌이 있어요—그리고 저는 그분을 위해 그것을 더 희게 만들고 있고요."], ["She often wore white herself, and she always dressed her little daughter in white.", "그분도 자주 흰옷을 입으셨고, 어린 따님에게도 늘 흰옷을 입히셨어요."], ["Is Miss Fairlie well and happy?", "페얼리 양은 잘 지내시나요, 행복하신가요?"], ["Does she wear white now, as she used when she was a girl?”", "그분도 여자아이였을 때처럼 지금도 흰옷을 입으시나요?”"]], [["Her voice sank when she put the questions about Miss Fairlie, and she turned her head farther and farther away from me.", "페얼리 양에 대해 물을 때 그녀의 목소리는 잦아들었고, 그녀는 고개를 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 돌렸다."], ["I thought I detected, in the alteration of her manner, an uneasy consciousness of the risk she had run in sending the anonymous letter, and I instantly determined so to frame my answer as to surprise her into owning it.", "나는 달라진 그녀의 태도에서, 익명의 편지를 보내며 저지른 위험을 불안하게 의식하고 있다는 낌새를 읽었다고 생각했고, 곧바로 그녀가 놀란 나머지 그것을 실토하게끔 대답을 짜기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Miss Fairlie was not very well or very happy this morning,”", "“페얼리 양은 오늘 아침 그리 건강하지도, 그리 행복하지도 않았습니다.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["She murmured a few words, but they were spoken so confusedly, and in such a low tone, that I could not even guess at what they meant.", "그녀는 몇 마디를 중얼거렸으나, 어찌나 뒤죽박죽으로, 또 어찌나 낮은 목소리로 말했던지 나는 그 뜻을 짐작조차 할 수 없었다."]], [["“Did you ask me why Miss Fairlie was neither well nor happy this morning?”", "“페얼리 양이 오늘 아침 왜 건강하지도 행복하지도 않았는지 나에게 물었습니까?”"], ["I continued.", "내가 말을 이었다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니요.”"], ["she said quickly and eagerly—“oh no, I never asked that.”", "그녀가 재빨리, 그리고 다급하게 말했다—“아, 아니에요, 그런 건 묻지 않았어요.”"]], [["“I will tell you without your asking,”", "“묻지 않아도 내가 말해 주지요.”"], ["I went on.", "내가 계속했다."], ["“Miss Fairlie has received your letter.”", "“페얼리 양은 당신의 편지를 받았습니다.”"]], [["She had been down on her knees for some little time past, carefully removing the last weather-stains left about the inscription while we were speaking together.", "그녀는 조금 전부터 무릎을 꿇은 채, 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 비문 둘레에 마지막으로 남은 비바람 얼룩을 조심스레 지우고 있었다."], ["The first sentence of the words I had just addressed to her made her pause in her occupation, and turn slowly without rising from her knees, so as to face me.", "내가 방금 그녀에게 건넨 말의 첫 문장에 그녀는 하던 일을 멈추고, 무릎을 꿇은 채로 천천히 몸을 돌려 나를 마주 보았다."], ["The second sentence literally petrified her.", "두 번째 문장은 말 그대로 그녀를 돌처럼 굳혀 놓았다."], ["The cloth she had been holding dropped from her hands—her lips fell apart—all the little colour that there was naturally in her face left it in an instant.", "쥐고 있던 천이 손에서 떨어졌고—입술이 벌어졌으며—본디 그 얼굴에 있던 얼마 안 되는 핏기마저 한순간에 가셔 버렸다."]], [["“How do you know?”", "“그걸 어떻게 아세요?”"], ["she said faintly.", "그녀가 힘없이 말했다."], ["“Who showed it to you?”", "“누가 그걸 당신에게 보여 줬나요?”"], ["The blood rushed back into her face—rushed overwhelmingly, as the sense rushed upon her mind that her own words had betrayed her.", "피가 그녀의 얼굴로 되몰렸다—자기 입에서 나온 말이 스스로를 드러내 버렸다는 자각이 마음에 밀어닥치면서, 걷잡을 수 없이 되몰렸다."], ["She struck her hands together in despair.", "그녀는 절망하여 두 손을 마주 쳤다."], ["“I never wrote it,”", "“저는 그런 걸 쓴 적 없어요.”"], ["she gasped affrightedly; “I know nothing about it!”", "그녀가 겁에 질려 헐떡였다. “저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요!”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“아니요.”"], ["I said, “you wrote it, and you know about it.", "내가 말했다. “당신이 그것을 썼고, 당신은 그것에 대해 알고 있습니다."], ["It was wrong to send such a letter, it was wrong to frighten Miss Fairlie.", "그런 편지를 보낸 것은 잘못이었고, 페얼리 양을 겁먹게 한 것도 잘못이었습니다."], ["If you had anything to say that it was right and necessary for her to hear, you should have gone yourself to Limmeridge House—you should have spoken to the young lady with your own lips.”", "그분이 들어야 마땅하고 또 반드시 들어야 할 말이 있었다면, 당신이 직접 리머리지 하우스로 갔어야 했습니다—당신 입으로 그 아가씨에게 말했어야 했습니다.”"]], [["She crouched down over the flat stone of the grave, till her face was hidden on it, and made no reply.", "그녀는 무덤의 평평한 돌 위로 몸을 웅크려 얼굴을 거기에 파묻은 채 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Miss Fairlie will be as good and kind to you as her mother was, if you mean well,”", "“당신이 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다면, 페얼리 양은 그분의 어머니가 그러셨던 것처럼 당신에게 착하고 다정하게 대해 줄 겁니다.”"], ["I went on.", "내가 말을 이었다."], ["“Miss Fairlie will keep your secret, and not let you come to any harm.", "“페얼리 양은 당신의 비밀을 지켜 줄 것이고, 당신이 어떤 해도 입지 않게 해 줄 겁니다."], ["Will you see her to-morrow at the farm?", "내일 그 농장에서 그분을 만나겠습니까?"], ["Will you meet her in the garden at Limmeridge House?”", "리머리지 하우스의 정원에서 그분을 만나겠습니까?”"]], [["“Oh, if I could die, and be hidden and at rest with YOU!”", "“아, 제가 죽어서 당신과 함께 감추어져 쉴 수만 있다면!”"], ["Her lips murmured the words close on the grave-stone, murmured them in tones of passionate endearment, to the dead remains beneath.", "그녀의 입술이 묘비에 바싹 붙어 그 말을 중얼거렸다. 아래에 누운 죽은 이를 향한 애틋한 사랑의 어조로 중얼거렸다."], ["“You know how I love your child, for your sake!", "“제가 당신을 위해 당신의 아이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 아시잖아요!"], ["Oh, Mrs. Fairlie!", "아, 페얼리 부인!"], ["Mrs. Fairlie!", "페얼리 부인!"], ["tell me how to save her.", "그분을 어떻게 구해야 할지 저에게 말해 주세요."], ["Be my darling and my mother once more, and tell me what to do for the best.”", "다시 한 번 저의 사랑하는 분, 저의 어머니가 되어 주셔서, 무엇이 가장 좋은 길인지 저에게 말해 주세요.”"]], [["I heard her lips kissing the stone—I saw her hands beating on it passionately.", "나는 그녀의 입술이 돌에 입 맞추는 소리를 들었다—그녀의 두 손이 격정에 차서 돌을 두드리는 것을 보았다."], ["The sound and the sight deeply affected me.", "그 소리와 그 광경이 나를 깊이 흔들었다."], ["I stooped down, and took the poor helpless hands tenderly in mine, and tried to soothe her.", "나는 몸을 굽혀 그 가엾고 힘없는 두 손을 내 손에 부드럽게 감싸 쥐고 그녀를 달래려 애썼다."]], [["It was useless.", "소용이 없었다."], ["She snatched her hands from me, and never moved her face from the stone.", "그녀는 내 손에서 자기 손을 낚아채고는 얼굴을 돌에서 조금도 떼지 않았다."], ["Seeing the urgent necessity of quieting her at any hazard and by any means, I appealed to the only anxiety that she appeared to feel, in connection with me and with my opinion of her—the anxiety to convince me of her fitness to be mistress of her own actions.", "무슨 수를 써서라도, 어떤 방법으로든 그녀를 진정시켜야 한다는 절박함을 깨닫고, 나는 나와 관련해서, 또 나의 평가와 관련해서 그녀가 유일하게 마음을 쓰는 듯이 보이는 그 불안에 호소했다—자기 행동의 주인이 될 자격이 있음을 나에게 납득시키고 싶어 하는 불안 말이다."]], [["“Come, come,”", "“자, 자.”"], ["I said gently.", "내가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Try to compose yourself, or you will make me alter my opinion of you.", "“마음을 가라앉히려 애써 보세요. 그러지 않으면 당신에 대한 내 생각이 바뀌게 될 겁니다."], ["Don’t let me think that the person who put you in the Asylum might have had some excuse——”", "당신을 그 정신병원에 넣은 사람에게 어떤 그럴 만한 사정이 있었을지도 모른다고 내가 생각하게 만들지 마세요——”"]], [["The next words died away on my lips.", "다음 말은 내 입술에서 스러졌다."], ["The instant I risked that chance reference to the person who had put her in the Asylum she sprang up on her knees.", "그녀를 정신병원에 넣은 사람을 무심코 입에 올리는 위험을 무릅쓴 그 순간, 그녀는 무릎을 세우며 벌떡 일어났다."], ["A most extraordinary and startling change passed over her.", "참으로 놀랍고도 섬뜩한 변화가 그녀에게 일어났다."], ["Her face, at all ordinary times so touching to look at, in its nervous sensitiveness, weakness, and uncertainty, became suddenly darkened by an expression of maniacally intense hatred and fear, which communicated a wild, unnatural force to every feature.", "평소에는 그 신경질적인 예민함과 연약함과 불안정함으로 그토록 애처롭게 보이던 그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 미친 듯이 강렬한 증오와 공포의 표정으로 어두워졌고, 그 표정이 이목구비 하나하나에 사납고 부자연스러운 힘을 불어넣었다."], ["Her eyes dilated in the dim evening light, like the eyes of a wild animal.", "그녀의 두 눈은 어스름한 저녁빛 속에서 야수의 눈처럼 크게 벌어졌다."], ["She caught up the cloth that had fallen at her side, as if it had been a living creature that she could kill, and crushed it in both her hands with such convulsive strength, that the few drops of moisture left in it trickled down on the stone beneath her.", "그녀는 옆에 떨어져 있던 천을 마치 죽여 버릴 수 있는 산 짐승이기라도 한 듯 움켜쥐고는, 두 손으로 어찌나 경련하듯 힘껏 비틀어 짰던지 그 안에 남아 있던 몇 방울 물기가 그녀 아래의 돌 위로 뚝뚝 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Talk of something else,”", "“다른 이야기를 하세요.”"], ["she said, whispering through her teeth.", "그녀가 이 사이로 속삭이듯 말했다."], ["“I shall lose myself if you talk of that.”", "“그 이야기를 하시면 저는 제정신을 잃고 말 거예요.”"]], [["Every vestige of the gentler thoughts which had filled her mind hardly a minute since seemed to be swept from it now.", "채 일 분도 되지 않은 조금 전까지 그녀의 마음을 채우고 있던 온화한 생각의 자취는 이제 죄다 쓸려 나간 듯했다."], ["It was evident that the impression left by Mrs. Fairlie’s kindness was not, as I had supposed, the only strong impression on her memory.", "페얼리 부인의 친절이 남긴 인상이, 내가 짐작했던 것처럼 그녀의 기억에 남은 유일하게 강한 인상은 아님이 분명했다."], ["With the grateful remembrance of her school-days at Limmeridge, there existed the vindictive remembrance of the wrong inflicted on her by her confinement in the Asylum.", "리머리지에서 보낸 학창 시절의 고마운 기억과 나란히, 정신병원 감금으로 그녀가 당한 부당함에 대한 원한 어린 기억이 자리 잡고 있었다. 그 부당한 짓을 저지른 사람은 누구인가?"], ["Who had done that wrong? Could it really be her mother?", "정말로 그녀의 어머니일 수 있을까?"]], [["It was hard to give up pursuing the inquiry to that final point, but I forced myself to abandon all idea of continuing it.", "그 마지막 지점까지 캐묻기를 포기하기란 힘든 일이었으나, 나는 그것을 계속하겠다는 생각을 모두 버리도록 나 자신을 다잡았다."], ["Seeing her as I saw her now, it would have been cruel to think of anything but the necessity and the humanity of restoring her composure.", "지금 내 눈앞에 있는 그녀의 모습을 보면서, 그녀의 평정을 되돌려 놓아야 한다는 필요와 인간다움 말고 다른 것을 생각한다면 그것은 잔인한 일이었을 것이다."]], [["“I will talk of nothing to distress you,”", "“당신을 괴롭히는 이야기는 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다.”"], ["I said soothingly.", "내가 달래듯 말했다."]], [["“You want something,”", "“뭔가 원하시는 게 있잖아요.”"], ["she answered sharply and suspiciously.", "그녀가 날카롭게, 의심스럽게 대꾸했다."], ["“Don’t look at me like that.", "“저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요."], ["Speak to me—tell me what you want.”", "저에게 말하세요—무엇을 원하시는지 말해 주세요.”"]], [["“I only want you to quiet yourself, and when you are calmer, to think over what I have said.”", "“나는 그저 당신이 마음을 가라앉히기를, 그리고 좀 더 진정되거든 내가 한 말을 곰곰이 생각해 보기를 바랄 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“Said?”", "“말이라고요?”"], ["She paused—twisted the cloth in her hands, backwards and forwards, and whispered to herself, “What is it he said?”", "그녀는 잠시 멈추고—손에 쥔 천을 앞뒤로 비틀며 혼잣말로 속삭였다. “저이가 무슨 말을 했더라?”"], ["She turned again towards me, and shook her head impatiently.", "그녀는 다시 나를 향해 몸을 돌리며 조바심 어린 몸짓으로 고개를 저었다."], ["“Why don’t you help me?”", "“왜 저를 도와주지 않으세요?”"], ["she asked, with angry suddenness.", "그녀가 대뜸 성난 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "“네, 네.”"], ["I said, “I will help you, and you will soon remember.", "내가 말했다. “도와드리지요."], ["I ask you to see Miss Fairlie to-morrow and to tell her the truth about the letter.”", "그러면 곧 기억이 날 겁니다. 내가 부탁하는 것은, 내일 페얼리 양을 만나 그 편지에 대한 진실을 그분께 말씀드리라는 것입니다.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["Miss Fairlie—Fairlie—Fairlie——”", "페얼리 양—페얼리—페얼리——”"]], [["The mere utterance of the loved familiar name seemed to quiet her.", "사랑스럽고 익숙한 그 이름을 입에 올리는 것만으로도 그녀는 진정되는 듯했다."], ["Her face softened and grew like itself again.", "얼굴이 부드러워지며 다시 본래의 그 얼굴로 돌아갔다."]], [["“You need have no fear of Miss Fairlie,”", "“페얼리 양을 두려워할 필요는 없습니다.”"], ["I continued, “and no fear of getting into trouble through the letter.", "내가 말을 이었다. “그 편지 때문에 곤경에 빠질까 봐 두려워할 필요도 없고요."], ["She knows so much about it already, that you will have no difficulty in telling her all.", "그분은 이미 그 일에 대해 아주 많이 알고 계셔서, 당신이 전부 털어놓는 데 아무 어려움도 없을 겁니다."], ["There can be little necessity for concealment where there is hardly anything left to conceal.", "감출 것이 거의 남지 않은 마당에 감출 필요도 거의 없지요."], ["You mention no names in the letter; but Miss Fairlie knows that the person you write of is Sir Percival Glyde——”", "당신은 편지에 아무 이름도 적지 않았지만, 페얼리 양은 당신이 쓴 그 사람이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이라는 것을 알고 있습니다——”"]], [["The instant I pronounced that name she started to her feet, and a scream burst from her that rang through the churchyard, and made my heart leap in me with the terror of it.", "내가 그 이름을 입에 올리는 순간 그녀는 벌떡 일어섰고, 교회 묘지에 쩌렁쩌렁 울리는 비명이 그녀에게서 터져 나와, 그 무서움에 내 심장이 안에서 펄쩍 뛰었다."], ["The dark deformity of the expression which had just left her face lowered on it once more, with doubled and trebled intensity.", "방금 그녀의 얼굴에서 물러갔던 그 어둡고 일그러진 표정이 두 배, 세 배 짙어진 채로 다시 얼굴에 내려앉았다."], ["The shriek at the name, the reiterated look of hatred and fear that instantly followed, told all.", "그 이름에 터진 비명과, 곧바로 뒤따른 그 되풀이된 증오와 공포의 표정이 모든 것을 말해 주었다."], ["Not even a last doubt now remained.", "이제는 마지막 의심조차 남지 않았다."], ["Her mother was guiltless of imprisoning her in the Asylum.", "그녀의 어머니는 그녀를 정신병원에 가둔 죄가 없었다."], ["A man had shut her up—and that man was Sir Percival Glyde.", "한 남자가 그녀를 가둔 것이었다—그리고 그 남자는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이었다."]], [["The scream had reached other ears than mine.", "그 비명은 내 귀에만 닿은 것이 아니었다."], ["On one side I heard the door of the sexton’s cottage open; on the other I heard the voice of her companion, the woman in the shawl, the woman whom she had spoken of as Mrs. Clements.", "한쪽에서는 무덤지기 오두막의 문이 열리는 소리가 들렸고, 다른 쪽에서는 그녀의 동행인 숄을 두른 여자, 그녀가 클레멘츠 부인이라고 말했던 그 여자의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“I’m coming!", "“가요!"], ["I’m coming!”", "지금 가요!”"], ["cried the voice from behind the clump of dwarf trees.", "왜소한 나무 덤불 뒤에서 그 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["In a moment more Mrs. Clements hurried into view.", "다음 순간 클레멘츠 부인이 허둥지둥 시야에 들어왔다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "“당신은 누구요?”"], ["she cried, facing me resolutely as she set her foot on the stile.", "그녀가 층계 디딤대에 발을 올려놓으며 단호하게 나를 마주하고 소리쳤다."], ["“How dare you frighten a poor helpless woman like that?”", "“어떻게 감히 저렇게 가엾고 힘없는 여자를 겁먹게 한단 말이오?”"]], [["She was at Anne Catherick’s side, and had put one arm around her, before I could answer.", "내가 대답도 하기 전에 그녀는 앤 캐서릭 곁에 다가가 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안았다."], ["“What is it, my dear?”", "“왜 그래요, 아가씨?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“What has he done to you?”", "“저 사람이 아가씨한테 무슨 짓을 했어요?”"]], [["“Nothing,”", "“아무것도요.”"], ["the poor creature answered.", "그 가엾은 사람이 대답했다."], ["“Nothing.", "“아무것도 안 했어요."], ["I’m only frightened.”", "그냥 제가 놀랐을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["Mrs. Clements turned on me with a fearless indignation, for which I respected her.", "클레멘츠 부인은 두려움 없는 분노로 나를 향해 돌아섰고, 나는 그 때문에 오히려 그녀를 존경하게 되었다."]], [["“I should be heartily ashamed of myself if I deserved that angry look,”", "“저 성난 눈길을 받아 마땅한 사람이라면 나는 진심으로 나 자신이 부끄러웠을 겁니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “But I do not deserve it.", "“그러나 나는 그럴 만하지 않습니다."], ["I have unfortunately startled her without intending it.", "불행히도 그럴 뜻 없이 이분을 놀라게 하고 말았습니다."], ["This is not the first time she has seen me.", "이분이 나를 본 것은 이번이 처음이 아닙니다."], ["Ask her yourself, and she will tell you that I am incapable of willingly harming her or any woman.”", "직접 물어보십시오. 내가 이분이든 다른 어느 여인이든 일부러 해칠 수 있는 사람이 아니라고 말해 줄 겁니다.”"]], [["I spoke distinctly, so that Anne Catherick might hear and understand me, and I saw that the words and their meaning had reached her.", "나는 앤 캐서릭이 듣고 이해할 수 있도록 또렷하게 말했고, 그 말과 그 뜻이 그녀에게 가닿았음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "“네, 네.”"], ["she said—“he was good to me once—he helped me——”", "그녀가 말했다—“이분은 전에 저에게 잘해 주셨어요—저를 도와주셨어요——”"], ["She whispered the rest into her friend’s ear.", "그녀는 나머지 말을 친구의 귀에 대고 속삭였다."]], [["“Strange, indeed!” said Mrs.", "“정말 신기한 일이네!”"], ["Clements, with a look of perplexity.", "클레멘츠 부인이 어리둥절한 표정으로 말했다."], ["“It makes all the difference, though.", "“하지만 그러면 사정이 완전히 달라지지요."], ["I’m sorry I spoke so rough to you, sir; but you must own that appearances looked suspicious to a stranger.", "선생님께 그렇게 거칠게 말씀드린 건 죄송합니다. 하지만 낯선 사람이 보기에 그 모양새가 수상쩍었다는 것은 인정하셔야 해요."], ["It’s more my fault than yours, for humouring her whims, and letting her be alone in such a place as this.", "저 애의 변덕을 받아 주고 이런 데에 혼자 있게 한 제 잘못이 선생님 잘못보다 크지요."], ["Come, my dear—come home now.”", "자, 아가씨—이제 집으로 갑시다.”"]], [["I thought the good woman looked a little uneasy at the prospect of the walk back, and I offered to go with them until they were both within sight of home.", "나는 이 착한 여인이 돌아가는 길을 조금 불안해하는 눈치라고 생각하고, 두 사람이 집이 보이는 곳에 이를 때까지 함께 가겠다고 청했다."], ["Mrs. Clements thanked me civilly, and declined.", "클레멘츠 부인은 정중히 고마움을 표하고는 사양했다."], ["She said they were sure to meet some of the farm-labourers as soon as they got to the moor.", "황야에 이르기만 하면 농장 일꾼들을 틀림없이 만나게 될 거라고 그녀는 말했다."]], [["“Try to forgive me,”", "“나를 용서해 주십시오.”"], ["I said, when Anne Catherick took her friend’s arm to go away.", "앤 캐서릭이 떠나려고 친구의 팔을 잡을 때 내가 말했다."], ["Innocent as I had been of any intention to terrify and agitate her, my heart smote me as I looked at the poor, pale, frightened face.", "그녀를 겁주고 동요시킬 뜻이라고는 조금도 없었건만, 그 가엾고 창백하고 겁먹은 얼굴을 바라보노라니 가슴이 아팠다."]], [["“I will try,”", "“해 볼게요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“But you know too much—I’m afraid you’ll always frighten me now.”", "“하지만 당신은 너무 많은 걸 알고 있어요—이제부터 당신은 늘 저를 겁나게 할 것만 같아요.”"]], [["Mrs. Clements glanced at me, and shook her head pityingly.", "클레멘츠 부인이 나를 흘긋 보더니 딱하다는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Good-night, sir,”", "“안녕히 계세요, 선생님.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You couldn’t help it, I know; but I wish it was me you had frightened, and not her.”", "“어쩔 수 없는 일이었다는 건 저도 압니다. 하지만 겁을 준 상대가 이 아이가 아니라 저였더라면 좋았을 텐데요.”"]], [["They moved away a few steps.", "두 사람은 몇 걸음 멀어져 갔다."], ["I thought they had left me, but Anne suddenly stopped, and separated herself from her friend.", "나는 그들이 나를 두고 떠난 줄 알았으나, 앤이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추더니 동무에게서 떨어져 나왔다."]], [["“Wait a little,”", "“잠깐만 기다려요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I must say good-bye.”", "“작별 인사를 해야 해요.”"]], [["She returned to the grave, rested both hands tenderly on the marble cross, and kissed it.", "그녀는 무덤으로 되돌아가 두 손을 대리석 십자가 위에 다정하게 얹고는 거기에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“I’m better now,”", "“이제 좀 나아졌어요.”"], ["she sighed, looking up at me quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 나를 올려다보며 한숨지었다."], ["“I forgive you.”", "“당신을 용서할게요.”"]], [["She joined her companion again, and they left the burial-ground.", "그녀는 다시 동행에게로 갔고, 두 사람은 묘지를 떠났다."], ["I saw them stop near the church and speak to the sexton’s wife, who had come from the cottage, and had waited, watching us from a distance.", "나는 그들이 교회 근처에 멈추어 서서, 오두막에서 나와 멀찍이서 우리를 지켜보며 기다리고 있던 묘지기의 아내와 이야기를 나누는 것을 보았다."], ["Then they went on again up the path that led to the moor.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 황야로 이어지는 오솔길을 따라 올라갔다."], ["I looked after Anne Catherick as she disappeared, till all trace of her had faded in the twilight—looked as anxiously and sorrowfully as if that was the last I was to see in this weary world of the woman in white.", "나는 앤 캐서릭이 사라져 가는 모습을, 그녀의 자취가 황혼 속에 온전히 스러질 때까지 바라보았다—마치 그것이 이 고단한 세상에서 흰옷을 입은 여인을 보는 마지막이기라도 한 듯, 그토록 애타고 서글프게 바라보았다."]], [["# XIV", "# XIV"]], [["Half an hour later I was back at the house, and was informing Miss Halcombe of all that had happened.", "반 시간 뒤 나는 저택으로 돌아와, 그동안 있었던 일을 하나도 빠짐없이 홀콤 양에게 알리고 있었다."]], [["She listened to me from beginning to end with a steady, silent attention, which, in a woman of her temperament and disposition, was the strongest proof that could be offered of the serious manner in which my narrative affected her.", "그녀는 처음부터 끝까지 흔들림 없는 침묵의 주의로 내 말을 들었는데, 그녀와 같은 기질과 성정을 지닌 여인에게 그것은 내 이야기가 그녀를 얼마나 심각하게 흔들어 놓았는지를 보여 줄 수 있는 무엇보다 강력한 증거였다."]], [["“My mind misgives me,”", "“마음이 놓이지 않는군요.”"], ["was all she said when I had done.", "내가 이야기를 마쳤을 때 그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."], ["“My mind misgives me sadly about the future.”", "“앞일을 생각하면 마음이 몹시도 놓이지 않아요.”"]], [["“The future may depend,”", "“앞일은.” 내가 조심스레 말했다."], ["I suggested, “on the use we make of the present.", "“우리가 지금을 어떻게 쓰느냐에 달려 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["It is not improbable that Anne Catherick may speak more readily and unreservedly to a woman than she has spoken to me.", "앤 캐서릭이 저에게 말했던 것보다 여자에게는 더 선뜻, 더 거리낌 없이 말할 가능성도 없지 않습니다."], ["If Miss Fairlie——”", "만약 페얼리 양이——”"]], [["“Not to be thought of for a moment,”", "“그건 한순간도 생각할 수 없어요.”"], ["interposed Miss Halcombe, in her most decided manner.", "홀콤 양이 더없이 단호한 태도로 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“Let me suggest, then,”", "“그렇다면 이렇게 제안드리겠습니다.”"], ["I continued, “that you should see Anne Catherick yourself, and do all you can to win her confidence.", "나는 말을 이었다. “당신이 직접 앤 캐서릭을 만나서, 그 사람의 신뢰를 얻기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 보시는 겁니다."], ["For my own part, I shrink from the idea of alarming the poor creature a second time, as I have most unhappily alarmed her already.", "저로서는 이미 그 딱한 사람을 그토록 불행하게 놀라게 해 놓은 마당에, 두 번째로 또 놀라게 한다는 생각은 견디기 어렵습니다."], ["Do you see any objection to accompanying me to the farmhouse to-morrow?”", "내일 저와 함께 그 농가까지 가시는 데 무슨 걸리는 점이라도 있습니까?”"]], [["“None whatever.", "“전혀 없어요."], ["I will go anywhere and do anything to serve Laura’s interests.", "로라를 위하는 일이라면 어디든 가고 무엇이든 하겠어요."], ["What did you say the place was called?”", "그곳 이름이 뭐라고 하셨지요?”"]], [["“You must know it well.", "“잘 아시는 곳일 겁니다."], ["It is called Todd’s Corner.”", "토드 코너라고 하더군요.”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“물론 알지요."], ["Todd’s Corner is one of Mr. Fairlie’s farms.", "토드 코너는 페얼리 씨의 농장 가운데 하나예요."], ["Our dairymaid here is the farmer’s second daughter.", "우리 집 우유 담당 하녀가 그 농부의 둘째 딸이고요."], ["She goes backwards and forwards constantly between this house and her father’s farm, and she may have heard or seen something which it may be useful to us to know.", "그 애는 이 집과 아버지의 농장 사이를 끊임없이 오가니, 우리가 알아 두면 쓸모 있을 무언가를 듣거나 보았을지도 몰라요."], ["Shall I ascertain, at once, if the girl is downstairs?”", "지금 바로 그 애가 아래층에 있는지 알아볼까요?”"]], [["She rang the bell, and sent the servant with his message.", "그녀가 종을 울려 하인에게 심부름을 보냈다."], ["He returned, and announced that the dairymaid was then at the farm.", "하인은 돌아와서, 그 하녀가 지금 농장에 가 있다고 알렸다."], ["She had not been there for the last three days, and the housekeeper had given her leave to go home for an hour or two that evening.", "지난 사흘 동안 집에 가지 못했던 터라, 가정부가 그날 저녁 한두 시간 다녀오도록 허락해 주었다는 것이었다."]], [["“I can speak to her to-morrow,”", "“그 애하고는 내일 이야기하면 되겠군요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, when the servant had left the room again.", "하인이 다시 방을 나가자 홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["“In the meantime, let me thoroughly understand the object to be gained by my interview with Anne Catherick.", "“그동안, 제가 앤 캐서릭을 만나서 얻어 내야 할 것이 무엇인지 분명히 알아 두고 싶어요."], ["Is there no doubt in your mind that the person who confined her in the Asylum was Sir Percival Glyde?”", "그 사람을 정신병원에 가둔 이가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이라는 데 대해서는 당신 마음에 아무 의심도 없나요?”"]], [["“There is not the shadow of a doubt.", "“그림자만큼의 의심도 없습니다."], ["The only mystery that remains is the mystery of his MOTIVE.", "남은 수수께끼는 오직 그의 동기라는 수수께끼뿐입니다."], ["Looking to the great difference between his station in life and hers, which seems to preclude all idea of the most distant relationship between them, it is of the last importance—even assuming that she really required to be placed under restraint—to know why HE should have been the person to assume the serious responsibility of shutting her up——”", "두 사람의 신분 차이가 워낙 커서 아무리 먼 인척 관계조차 생각할 수 없어 보이는데, 설령 그 여자가 정말로 감호를 받아야 할 처지였다고 인정한다 하더라도, 어째서 하필 그가 그 여자를 가둔다는 중대한 책임을 떠맡은 사람이어야 했는지를 아는 일이야말로 무엇보다 중요합니다——”"]], [["“In a private Asylum, I think you said?”", "“사설 정신병원이라고 말씀하셨던 것 같은데요?”"]], [["“Yes, in a private Asylum, where a sum of money, which no poor person could afford to give, must have been paid for her maintenance as a patient.”", "“그렇습니다, 사설 정신병원입니다. 가난한 사람이라면 도저히 감당할 수 없는 액수의 돈이 그 여자를 환자로 수용하는 비용으로 치러졌을 것이 분명한 곳이지요.”"]], [["“I see where the doubt lies, Mr.", "“의심이 어디에 놓여 있는지 알겠어요, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright, and I promise you that it shall be set at rest, whether Anne Catherick assists us to-morrow or not.", "그리고 내일 앤 캐서릭이 우리를 돕든 돕지 않든, 그 의심은 반드시 풀리도록 하겠다고 약속드리지요."], ["Sir Percival Glyde shall not be long in this house without satisfying Mr. Gilmore, and satisfying me.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경은 길모어 씨를 납득시키고 저를 납득시키지 않고서는 이 집에 오래 머물지 못할 거예요."], ["My sister’s future is my dearest care in life, and I have influence enough over her to give me some power, where her marriage is concerned, in the disposal of it.”", "제 동생의 앞날은 제 삶에서 가장 소중한 걱정거리이고, 저는 그 애에게 웬만큼 영향력이 있어서 혼사에 관한 한 그 앞날을 정하는 데 얼마간 힘을 쓸 수 있답니다.”"]], [["We parted for the night.", "우리는 그날 밤 인사를 나누고 헤어졌다."]], [["After breakfast the next morning, an obstacle, which the events of the evening before had put out of my memory, interposed to prevent our proceeding immediately to the farm.", "이튿날 아침 식사를 마친 뒤, 전날 저녁의 일들에 밀려 내 기억에서 사라져 있던 걸림돌 하나가 끼어들어 우리가 곧바로 농장으로 나서지 못하게 했다."], ["This was my last day at Limmeridge House, and it was necessary, as soon as the post came in, to follow Miss Halcombe’s advice, and to ask Mr. Fairlie’s permission to shorten my engagement by a month, in consideration of an unforeseen necessity for my return to London.", "그날은 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 보내는 마지막 날이었고, 우편이 들어오는 대로 홀콤 양의 조언을 따라, 뜻하지 않게 런던으로 돌아가야 할 사정이 생겼음을 들어 계약 기간을 한 달 앞당겨 끝내게 해 달라고 페얼리 씨에게 청해야 했던 것이다."]], [["Fortunately for the probability of this excuse, so far as appearances were concerned, the post brought me two letters from London friends that morning.", "겉으로 보이는 모양새만큼은 이 핑계가 그럴듯해 보이도록, 다행히도 그날 아침 우편이 런던의 벗들에게서 온 편지 두 통을 나에게 가져다주었다."], ["I took them away at once to my own room, and sent the servant with a message to Mr. Fairlie, requesting to know when I could see him on a matter of business.", "나는 그것을 곧장 내 방으로 가지고 올라간 뒤, 하인을 시켜 페얼리 씨께 사무적인 용건으로 언제 뵐 수 있겠는지 여쭙는 전갈을 보냈다."]], [["I awaited the man’s return, free from the slightest feeling of anxiety about the manner in which his master might receive my application.", "나는 하인이 돌아오기를 기다리면서, 그 주인이 내 청을 어떻게 받아들일지에 대해 털끝만 한 불안도 느끼지 않았다."], ["With Mr. Fairlie’s leave or without it, I must go.", "페얼리 씨가 허락하든 하지 않든 나는 떠나야 했다."], ["The consciousness of having now taken the first step on the dreary journey which was henceforth to separate my life from Miss Fairlie’s seemed to have blunted my sensibility to every consideration connected with myself.", "이제부터 내 삶을 페얼리 양의 삶에서 갈라놓을 그 쓸쓸한 여정의 첫걸음을 이미 내디뎠다는 자각이, 나 자신과 관련된 모든 문제에 대해 내 감각을 무디게 만들어 놓은 듯했다."], ["I had done with my poor man’s touchy pride—I had done with all my little artist vanities.", "나는 가난한 사내의 발끈하기 잘하는 자존심 따위와는 이미 결별해 있었고, 예술가로서의 자잘한 허영이란 허영과도 모두 결별해 있었다."], ["No insolence of Mr. Fairlie’s, if he chose to be insolent, could wound me now.", "페얼리 씨가 무례하게 굴기로 마음먹는다 한들, 그 어떤 무례로도 이제 나에게 상처를 낼 수는 없었다."]], [["The servant returned with a message for which I was not unprepared. Mr. Fairlie regretted that the state of his health, on that particular morning, was such as to preclude all hope of his having the pleasure of receiving me. He begged, therefore, that I would accept his apologies, and kindly communicate what I had to say in the form of a letter. Similar messages to this had reached me, at various intervals, during my three months’ residence in the house. Throughout the whole of that period Mr. Fairlie had been rejoiced to “possess” me, but had never been well enough to see me for a second time. The servant took every fresh batch of drawings that I mounted and restored back to his master with my “respects,” and returned empty-handed with Mr. Fairlie’s “kind compliments,” “best thanks,” and “sincere regrets” that the state of his health still obliged him to remain a solitary prisoner in his own room. A more satisfactory arrangement to both sides could not possibly have been adopted. It would be hard to say which of us, under the circumstances, felt the most grateful sense of obligation to Mr. Fairlie’s accommodating nerves.", "하인은 내가 어느 정도 짐작하고 있던 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다. 페얼리 씨께서는 하필 그날 아침 건강 상태가 그러하여 저를 맞이하는 기쁨을 누릴 가망이 전혀 없음을 유감으로 여기신다고 했다. 그러니 부디 사과를 받아 주시고, 하실 말씀은 편지의 형식으로 알려 주시면 고맙겠다는 것이었다. 이 집에 머문 석 달 동안 이와 비슷한 전갈이 이따금씩 나에게 전해져 왔다. 그 기간 내내 페얼리 씨는 나를 “소유”하게 되어 기뻐하셨으나, 두 번째로 나를 만날 만큼 몸이 좋았던 적은 한 번도 없었다. 내가 배접하고 손본 그림 묶음이 새로 나올 때마다 하인이 내 “문안”과 함께 그것을 주인에게 가져갔고, 페얼리 씨의 “다정한 인사”와 “깊은 감사”, 그리고 건강 상태가 여전히 자기 방에 홀로 갇힌 죄수로 남아 있기를 강요한다는 “진심 어린 유감”만을 들고 빈손으로 돌아왔다. 양쪽 모두에게 이보다 더 만족스러운 조정은 도무지 있을 수 없었을 것이다. 그런 사정 아래에서 우리 둘 가운데 누가 페얼리 씨의 그 편의를 봐주는 신경에 더 고마운 은혜를 느꼈는지는 말하기 어려울 것이다."]], [["I sat down at once to write the letter, expressing myself in it as civilly, as clearly, and as briefly as possible.", "나는 곧바로 앉아 편지를 썼는데, 되도록 정중하고 되도록 분명하고 되도록 짧게 내 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Mr. Fairlie did not hurry his reply.", "페얼리 씨는 답장을 서두르지 않았다."], ["Nearly an hour elapsed before the answer was placed in my hands.", "그 답이 내 손에 놓이기까지 거의 한 시간이 흘렀다."], ["It was written with beautiful regularity and neatness of character, in violet-coloured ink, on note-paper as smooth as ivory and almost as thick as cardboard, and it addressed me in these terms—", "그것은 상아처럼 매끄럽고 판지만큼이나 두꺼운 편지지 위에 보랏빛 잉크로, 아름다우리만치 고르고 단정한 글씨로 쓰여 있었으며, 나에게 이런 말로 건네졌다—"]], [["“Mr. Fairlie’s compliments to Mr. Hartright. Mr. Fairlie is more surprised and disappointed than he can say (in the present state of his health) by Mr. Hartright’s application. Mr. Fairlie is not a man of business, but he has consulted his steward, who is, and that person confirms Mr. Fairlie’s opinion that Mr. Hartright’s request to be allowed to break his engagement cannot be justified by any necessity whatever, excepting perhaps a case of life and death. If the highly-appreciative feeling towards Art and its professors, which it is the consolation and happiness of Mr. Fairlie’s suffering existence to cultivate, could be easily shaken, Mr. Hartright’s present proceeding would have shaken it. It has not done so—except in the instance of Mr. Hartright himself.", "“페얼리 씨가 하트라이트 씨께 인사를 전합니다. 페얼리 씨는 하트라이트 씨의 청원에 (현재의 건강 상태로는) 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라고 실망하였습니다. 페얼리 씨는 사무에 밝은 사람이 아니나, 사무에 밝은 자기 집사와 상의하였고, 그 사람은 계약을 파기하게 해 달라는 하트라이트 씨의 요청이 아마도 생사가 걸린 경우가 아니고서는 그 어떤 필요로도 정당화될 수 없다는 페얼리 씨의 견해를 확인해 주었습니다. 예술과 그 종사자들을 향한 저 지극한 흠모의 정, 곧 그것을 가꾸는 일이 고통 속에 사는 페얼리 씨의 삶에 위안이자 행복이 되어 주는 그 정이 만약 쉽사리 흔들릴 수 있는 것이었다면, 하트라이트 씨의 이번 처사는 그것을 흔들어 놓았을 것입니다. 그러나 흔들어 놓지 못하였습니다—오직 하트라이트 씨 개인의 경우를 제외하고는 말입니다."]], [["“Having stated his opinion—so far, that is to say, as acute nervous suffering will allow him to state anything—Mr. Fairlie has nothing to add but the expression of his decision, in reference to the highly irregular application that has been made to him. Perfect repose of body and mind being to the last degree important in his case, Mr. Fairlie will not suffer Mr. Hartright to disturb that repose by remaining in the house under circumstances of an essentially irritating nature to both sides. Accordingly, Mr. Fairlie waives his right of refusal, purely with a view to the preservation of his own tranquillity—and informs Mr. Hartright that he may go.”", "“자기 견해를 밝혔으니—다시 말해 격심한 신경의 고통이 그에게 무엇이든 밝히도록 허락하는 한도까지는 밝혔으니—페얼리 씨로서는 자신에게 제기된 저 지극히 이례적인 청원에 관하여 자기 결정을 표명하는 것 말고는 덧붙일 말이 없습니다. 몸과 마음의 완전한 안정이 그의 경우 더없이 중요한 만큼, 페얼리 씨는 양쪽 모두에게 본질적으로 짜증스러울 수밖에 없는 사정 아래에서 하트라이트 씨가 이 집에 머물러 그 안정을 어지럽히도록 두지 않겠습니다. 따라서 페얼리 씨는 오로지 자기 자신의 평온을 지킨다는 뜻에서 거절할 권리를 포기하며—하트라이트 씨에게 떠나도 좋다고 알립니다.”"]], [["I folded the letter up, and put it away with my other papers.", "나는 편지를 접어 다른 서류들과 함께 치워 두었다."], ["The time had been when I should have resented it as an insult—I accepted it now as a written release from my engagement.", "한때는 그것을 모욕으로 여겨 분개했을 시절도 있었으나, 나는 이제 그것을 계약에서 풀려났다는 서면 증서로 받아들였다."], ["It was off my mind, it was almost out of my memory, when I went downstairs to the breakfast-room, and informed Miss Halcombe that I was ready to walk with her to the farm.", "아래층 아침 식사실로 내려가 홀콤 양에게 함께 농장으로 걸어갈 준비가 되었다고 알렸을 때, 그것은 이미 내 마음에서 떠나 있었고 기억에서도 거의 사라져 있었다."]], [["“Has Mr. Fairlie given you a satisfactory answer?”", "“페얼리 씨가 만족스러운 답을 주셨나요?”"], ["she asked as we left the house.", "저택을 나서며 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“He has allowed me to go, Miss Halcombe.”", "“떠나도 좋다고 허락하셨습니다, 홀콤 양.”"]], [["She looked up at me quickly, and then, for the first time since I had known her, took my arm of her own accord.", "그녀는 재빨리 나를 올려다보더니, 내가 그녀를 알게 된 이래 처음으로 스스로 내 팔에 손을 얹었다."], ["No words could have expressed so delicately that she understood how the permission to leave my employment had been granted, and that she gave me her sympathy, not as my superior, but as my friend.", "고용에서 벗어나도 좋다는 그 허락이 어떤 식으로 주어졌는지 그녀가 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 윗사람으로서가 아니라 벗으로서 나에게 위로를 건네고 있다는 것을, 그 어떤 말로도 그토록 섬세하게 표현할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["I had not felt the man’s insolent letter, but I felt deeply the woman’s atoning kindness.", "나는 그 사내의 무례한 편지에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했으나, 그 여인의 속죄하는 다정함은 깊이 느꼈다."]], [["On our way to the farm we arranged that Miss Halcombe was to enter the house alone, and that I was to wait outside, within call.", "농장으로 가는 길에 우리는 홀콤 양이 혼자 집 안으로 들어가고 나는 부르면 들릴 만한 거리 밖에서 기다리기로 정했다."], ["We adopted this mode of proceeding from an apprehension that my presence, after what had happened in the churchyard the evening before, might have the effect of renewing Anne Catherick’s nervous dread, and of rendering her additionally distrustful of the advances of a lady who was a stranger to her.", "이런 방식을 택한 것은, 전날 저녁 교회 묘지에서 있었던 일이 있고 난 뒤라 내가 곁에 있으면 앤 캐서릭의 신경질적인 두려움이 되살아나, 낯선 부인이 다가오는 것에 대해 그녀가 한층 더 의심을 품게 될까 염려해서였다."], ["Miss Halcombe left me, with the intention of speaking, in the first instance, to the farmer’s wife (of whose friendly readiness to help her in any way she was well assured), while I waited for her in the near neighbourhood of the house.", "홀콤 양은 우선 농부의 아내에게—어떤 식으로든 기꺼이 자기를 도와주리라 굳게 믿고 있는 사람이었다—말을 걸어 볼 생각으로 나를 남겨 두고 갔고, 나는 그 집 가까운 언저리에서 그녀를 기다렸다."]], [["I had fully expected to be left alone for some time.", "나는 한동안 혼자 남겨지리라고 단단히 각오하고 있었다."], ["To my surprise, however, little more than five minutes had elapsed before Miss Halcombe returned.", "그런데 놀랍게도 오 분이 조금 넘었을 뿐인데 홀콤 양이 돌아왔다."]], [["“Does Anne Catherick refuse to see you?”", "“앤 캐서릭이 만나기를 거절합니까?”"], ["I asked in astonishment.", "나는 깜짝 놀라 물었다."]], [["“Anne Catherick is gone,”", "“앤 캐서릭은 가 버렸어요.”"], ["replied Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다."]], [["“Gone?”", "“가 버렸다고요?”"]], [["“Gone with Mrs. Clements.", "“클레멘츠 부인과 함께 갔어요."], ["They both left the farm at eight o’clock this morning.”", "두 사람 다 오늘 아침 여덟 시에 농장을 떠났대요.”"]], [["I could say nothing—I could only feel that our last chance of discovery had gone with them.", "나는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다—그저 진상을 밝힐 우리의 마지막 기회가 그들과 함께 사라져 버렸다는 것만을 느낄 수 있었다."]], [["“All that Mrs. Todd knows about her guests, I know,”", "“토드 부인이 그 손님들에 대해 아는 것은 저도 다 알아요.”"], ["Miss Halcombe went on, “and it leaves me, as it leaves her, in the dark.", "홀콤 양이 말을 이었다. “그런데도 그분이 그렇듯 저 역시 캄캄한 채로 남아 있지요."], ["They both came back safe last night, after they left you, and they passed the first part of the evening with Mr. Todd’s family as usual.", "두 사람은 어젯밤 당신과 헤어진 뒤 무사히 돌아왔고, 저녁의 앞부분은 여느 때처럼 토드 씨네 식구들과 함께 보냈어요."], ["Just before supper-time, however, Anne Catherick startled them all by being suddenly seized with faintness.", "그런데 저녁 식사 시간 바로 전에 앤 캐서릭이 갑자기 아득해지는 발작에 사로잡혀 모두를 놀라게 했답니다."], ["She had had a similar attack, of a less alarming kind, on the day she arrived at the farm; and Mrs.", "농장에 도착하던 날에도 그보다는 덜 놀라운 비슷한 발작이 한 번 있었는데, 그때 토드 부인은 그것을 그 사람이 마침 읽고 있던 무언가와 연결 지었어요."], ["Todd had connected it, on that occasion, with something she was reading at the time in our local newspaper, which lay on the farm table, and which she had taken up only a minute or two before.”", "농장 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 우리 고장 신문이었고, 그 사람이 그것을 집어 든 지 겨우 일이 분밖에 되지 않았을 때였지요.”"]], [["“Does Mrs. Todd know what particular passage in the newspaper affected her in that way?”", "“토드 부인은 신문의 어느 대목이 그런 식으로 그녀를 흔들어 놓았는지 알고 있습니까?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“No,” replied Miss Halcombe.", "“아니요.”"], ["“She had looked it over, and had seen nothing in it to agitate any one.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다. “그분은 신문을 훑어보았지만 누구를 동요시킬 만한 것은 아무것도 찾지 못했대요."], ["I asked leave, however, to look it over in my turn, and at the very first page I opened I found that the editor had enriched his small stock of news by drawing upon our family affairs, and had published my sister’s marriage engagement, among his other announcements, copied from the London papers, of Marriages in High Life.", "하지만 저도 제 차례로 훑어보게 해 달라고 청했지요. 그런데 펼친 바로 첫 면에서, 편집자가 얼마 되지도 않는 자기 기삿거리를 우리 집안일에서 끌어다 불려 놓았고, 런던 신문들에서 옮겨 온 상류층 혼사 소식들 사이에 제 동생의 혼약을 실어 놓은 것을 발견했어요."], ["I concluded at once that this was the paragraph which had so strangely affected Anne Catherick, and I thought I saw in it, also, the origin of the letter which she sent to our house the next day.”", "저는 곧바로 이것이 앤 캐서릭을 그토록 이상하게 흔들어 놓은 그 대목이라고 결론지었고, 그 안에서 그 사람이 이튿날 우리 집으로 보낸 편지의 기원까지도 본 듯했답니다.”"]], [["“There can be no doubt in either case.", "“두 경우 다 의심의 여지가 없겠군요."], ["But what did you hear about her second attack of faintness yesterday evening?”", "그런데 어제저녁의 두 번째 실신 발작에 대해서는 무엇을 들으셨습니까?”"]], [["“Nothing.", "“아무것도요."], ["The cause of it is a complete mystery.", "그 원인은 완전한 수수께끼예요."], ["There was no stranger in the room.", "방 안에 낯선 사람은 없었어요."], ["The only visitor was our dairymaid, who, as I told you, is one of Mr. Todd’s daughters, and the only conversation was the usual gossip about local affairs.", "손님이라고는 우리 집 우유 하녀뿐이었는데, 말씀드렸듯이 토드 씨의 딸 가운데 하나지요."], ["They heard her cry out, and saw her turn deadly pale, without the slightest apparent reason. Mrs.", "그리고 오간 이야기라고는 고장 일에 대한 여느 때의 잡담뿐이었고요."], ["Todd and Mrs. Clements took her upstairs, and Mrs.", "사람들은 그 사람이 소리치는 것을 들었고, 눈에 띄는 아무런 까닭도 없이 낯빛이 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았대요."], ["Clements remained with her. They were heard talking together until long after the usual bedtime, and early this morning Mrs.", "토드 부인과 클레멘츠 부인이 그 사람을 위층으로 데려갔고, 클레멘츠 부인이 곁에 남았지요."], ["Clements took Mrs. Todd aside, and amazed her beyond all power of expression by saying that they must go.", "두 사람이 여느 때 잠자리에 드는 시각이 한참 지나도록 이야기를 나누는 소리가 들렸고, 오늘 아침 일찍 클레멘츠 부인이 토드 부인을 한쪽으로 불러내, 이제 떠나야 한다는 말로 이루 표현할 길 없을 만큼 그분을 놀라게 했어요."], ["The only explanation Mrs. Todd could extract from her guest was, that something had happened, which was not the fault of any one at the farmhouse, but which was serious enough to make Anne Catherick resolve to leave Limmeridge immediately.", "토드 부인이 그 손님에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 유일한 설명은, 무슨 일이 있었는데 그것은 농가의 누구의 잘못도 아니지만 앤 캐서릭이 당장 리머리지를 떠나기로 마음먹을 만큼 심각한 일이라는 것뿐이었지요."], ["It was quite useless to press Mrs. Clements to be more explicit.", "클레멘츠 부인에게 더 분명히 말하라고 다그쳐 봐야 아무 소용이 없었어요."], ["She only shook her head, and said that, for Anne’s sake, she must beg and pray that no one would question her.", "그분은 그저 고개를 저으며, 앤을 생각해서라도 아무도 자기에게 캐묻지 말아 주기를 빌고 또 빈다고 했대요."], ["All she could repeat, with every appearance of being seriously agitated herself, was that Anne must go, that she must go with her, and that the destination to which they might both betake themselves must be kept a secret from everybody.", "자기 자신도 몹시 동요한 기색이 역력한 채로 되풀이할 수 있는 말이라고는, 앤이 떠나야 하고, 자기가 함께 떠나야 하며, 두 사람이 어디로 향하든 그 행선지는 누구에게도 비밀로 해야 한다는 것뿐이었고요."], ["I spare you the recital of Mrs. Todd’s hospitable remonstrances and refusals.", "토드 부인이 손님을 붙들며 만류하고 거절하던 이야기는 굳이 옮기지 않겠어요."], ["It ended in her driving them both to the nearest station, more than three hours since.", "결국 그분이 두 사람을 가장 가까운 역까지 태워다 주는 것으로 끝났는데, 벌써 세 시간도 더 전의 일이에요."], ["She tried hard on the way to get them to speak more plainly, but without success; and she set them down outside the station-door, so hurt and offended by the unceremonious abruptness of their departure and their unfriendly reluctance to place the least confidence in her, that she drove away in anger, without so much as stopping to bid them good-bye. That is exactly what has taken place.", "그분은 가는 길에 좀 더 분명히 말하게 하려고 무진 애를 썼지만 헛일이었고, 두 사람을 역사 문밖에 내려놓을 때는 그 예의 없이 갑작스러운 출발과 자기에게 티끌만 한 속내도 털어놓지 않으려는 매정한 태도에 어찌나 상하고 언짢았던지, 작별 인사를 하려고 멈추지도 않은 채 화가 나서 마차를 몰아 가 버렸답니다."], ["Search your own memory, Mr.", "벌어진 일은 정확히 이러해요."], ["Hartright, and tell me if anything happened in the burial-ground yesterday evening which can at all account for the extraordinary departure of those two women this morning.”", "하트라이트 씨, 당신의 기억을 더듬어서, 오늘 아침 그 두 여자의 그 심상찮은 출발을 조금이라도 설명해 줄 만한 일이 어제저녁 묘지에서 있었는지 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["“I should like to account first, Miss Halcombe, for the sudden change in Anne Catherick which alarmed them at the farmhouse, hours after she and I had parted, and when time enough had elapsed to quiet any violent agitation that I might have been unfortunate enough to cause.", "“홀콤 양, 저로서는 먼저 농가 사람들을 놀라게 한 앤 캐서릭의 그 갑작스러운 변화부터 설명해 보고 싶습니다. 그것은 그녀와 제가 헤어지고도 몇 시간이나 지난 뒤, 혹시라도 제가 불행히 일으켰을지 모를 어떤 격한 동요라도 가라앉기에 충분한 시간이 흐른 뒤의 일이니까요."], ["Did you inquire particularly about the gossip which was going on in the room when she turned faint?”", "그녀가 정신이 아득해졌을 때 방 안에서 오가던 잡담에 대해 특별히 물어보셨습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["But Mrs. Todd’s household affairs seem to have divided her attention that evening with the talk in the farmhouse parlour.", "그런데 그날 저녁 토드 부인은 집안일에 마음을 빼앗겨 농가 거실의 이야기에 온전히 귀를 기울이지 못했던 모양이에요."], ["She could only tell me that it was ‘just the news,’—meaning, I suppose, that they all talked as usual about each other.”", "그저 ‘그냥 소식들’이었다고밖에 말해 주지 못했는데—짐작건대 다들 여느 때처럼 서로에 대해 이야기했다는 뜻이겠지요.”"]], [["“The dairymaid’s memory may be better than her mother’s,” I said.", "“그 우유 하녀의 기억이 어머니의 기억보다 나을지도 모르겠습니다.”"], ["“It may be as well for you to speak to the girl, Miss Halcombe, as soon as we get back.”", "내가 말했다. “돌아가는 대로 그 처녀와 이야기해 보시는 편이 좋겠습니다, 홀콤 양.”"]], [["My suggestion was acted on the moment we returned to the house.", "내 제안은 우리가 저택으로 돌아온 그 즉시 실행에 옮겨졌다."], ["Miss Halcombe led me round to the servants’ offices, and we found the girl in the dairy, with her sleeves tucked up to her shoulders, cleaning a large milk-pan and singing blithely over her work.", "홀콤 양이 나를 하인들이 쓰는 구역으로 돌아 데려갔고, 우리는 그 처녀가 우유실에서 소매를 어깨까지 걷어붙인 채 커다란 우유통을 닦으며 일하는 내내 흥겹게 노래를 부르고 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["“I have brought this gentleman to see your dairy, Hannah,”", "“이 신사분을 모시고 네 우유실을 보러 왔단다, 해나.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["“It is one of the sights of the house, and it always does you credit.”", "“이 집의 볼거리 가운데 하나이고, 언제나 네 솜씨를 자랑해 주는 곳이지.”"]], [["The girl blushed and curtseyed, and said shyly that she hoped she always did her best to keep things neat and clean.", "처녀는 얼굴을 붉히고 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 늘 물건들을 단정하고 깨끗하게 간수하려고 최선을 다하고 있기를 바란다고 수줍게 말했다."]], [["“We have just come from your father’s,”", "“방금 너희 아버지 댁에 다녀오는 길이란다.”"], ["Miss Halcombe continued.", "홀콤 양이 말을 이었다."], ["“You were there yesterday evening, I hear, and you found visitors at the house?”", "“어제저녁에 거기 가 있었다고 들었는데, 집에 손님들이 와 있었다지?”"]], [["“Yes, miss.”", "“예, 아가씨.”"]], [["“One of them was taken faint and ill, I am told.", "“그중 한 분이 정신이 아득해져 몸져누우셨다고 들었다."], ["I suppose nothing was said or done to frighten her?", "그분을 놀라게 할 만한 말이나 행동은 없었겠지?"], ["You were not talking of anything very terrible, were you?”", "아주 무서운 이야기 같은 걸 하고 있지는 않았고?”"]], [["“Oh no, miss!”", "“아유, 그럼요, 아가씨!”"], ["said the girl, laughing.", "처녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“We were only talking of the news.”", "“저희는 그냥 소식들 이야기만 했는데예.”"]], [["“Your sisters told you the news at Todd’s Corner, I suppose?”", "“토드 코너에서는 언니들이 너에게 소식을 전해 주었겠구나?”"]], [["“Yes, miss.”", "“예, 아가씨.”"]], [["“And you told them the news at Limmeridge House?”", "“그리고 너는 리머리지 하우스의 소식을 그 사람들에게 전해 주었고?”"]], [["“Yes, miss.", "“예, 아가씨."], ["And I’m quite sure nothing was said to frighten the poor thing, for I was talking when she was taken ill.", "그리고 그 딱한 분을 놀라게 할 만한 말은 정말이지 아무것도 없었어예. 그분이 몸져누우실 때 마침 이야기하고 있던 게 저였거든예."], ["It gave me quite a turn, miss, to see it, never having been taken faint myself.”", "저는 여태 한 번도 정신이 아득해져 본 적이 없어서, 그걸 보고 어찌나 놀랐는지 몰라예, 아가씨.”"]], [["Before any more questions could be put to her, she was called away to receive a basket of eggs at the dairy door.", "그 애에게 더 물어볼 새도 없이, 우유실 문간에서 달걀 바구니를 받으라는 부름이 와서 그 애는 자리를 떴다."], ["As she left us I whispered to Miss Halcombe—", "그 애가 우리 곁을 떠나자 나는 홀콤 양에게 속삭였다—"]], [["“Ask her if she happened to mention, last night, that visitors were expected at Limmeridge House.”", "“어젯밤에 리머리지 하우스에 손님이 올 예정이라는 이야기를 혹시 꺼냈는지 물어봐 주십시오.”"]], [["Miss Halcombe showed me, by a look, that she understood, and put the question as soon as the dairymaid returned to us.", "홀콤 양은 눈짓으로 알아들었다는 것을 나에게 보이고는, 우유 하녀가 우리에게 돌아오자마자 그 물음을 던졌다."]], [["“Oh yes, miss, I mentioned that,”", "“아, 예, 아가씨, 그 이야기는 했어예.”"], ["said the girl simply.", "처녀가 스스럼없이 말했다."], ["“The company coming, and the accident to the brindled cow, was all the news I had to take to the farm.”", "“손님이 오신다는 것하고 얼룩소한테 생긴 사고 이야기가 제가 농장에 가져갈 소식의 전부였거든예.”"]], [["“Did you mention names?", "“이름도 말했니?"], ["Did you tell them that Sir Percival Glyde was expected on Monday?”", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 월요일에 오실 예정이라고 말했어?”"]], [["“Yes, miss—I told them Sir Percival Glyde was coming.", "“예, 아가씨—퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 오신다고 말했어예."], ["I hope there was no harm in it—I hope I didn’t do wrong.”", "그게 나쁜 일이 아니었으면 좋겠어예—제가 잘못한 게 아니었으면 좋겠어예.”"]], [["“Oh no, no harm. Come, Mr.", "“아, 아니란다, 나쁠 것 없어."], ["Hartright, Hannah will begin to think us in the way, if we interrupt her any longer over her work.”", "가시지요, 하트라이트 씨. 일하는 걸 더 붙들고 있으면 해나가 우리를 성가시게 여기기 시작할 거예요.”"]], [["We stopped and looked at one another the moment we were alone again.", "다시 단둘이 되자마자 우리는 걸음을 멈추고 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“Is there any doubt in your mind, NOW, Miss Halcombe?”", "“이제도 당신 마음에 의심이 남아 있습니까, 홀콤 양?”"]], [["“Sir Percival Glyde shall remove that doubt, Mr. Hartright—or Laura Fairlie shall never be his wife.”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 의심을 걷어 내야 할 거예요, 하트라이트 씨—그러지 못한다면 로라 페얼리는 결코 그의 아내가 되지 않아요.”"]], [["# XV", "# XV"]], [["As we walked round to the front of the house a fly from the railway approached us along the drive.", "우리가 집 앞쪽으로 돌아 걸어가는데 역에서 온 삯마차 한 대가 진입로를 따라 다가왔다."], ["Miss Halcombe waited on the door-steps until the fly drew up, and then advanced to shake hands with an old gentleman, who got out briskly the moment the steps were let down.", "홀콤 양은 마차가 멎을 때까지 현관 계단에서 기다렸다가, 발판이 내려지자마자 날렵하게 내려선 노신사에게 악수를 청하러 나아갔다."], ["Mr. Gilmore had arrived.", "길모어 씨가 도착한 것이다."]], [["I looked at him, when we were introduced to each other, with an interest and a curiosity which I could hardly conceal.", "서로 소개를 받았을 때 나는 좀처럼 감추기 어려운 관심과 호기심을 품고 그를 바라보았다. 이 노인은 내가 리머리지 하우스를 떠난 뒤에도 이곳에 남을 사람이었다."], ["This old man was to remain at Limmeridge House after I had left it, he was to hear Sir Percival Glyde’s explanation, and was to give Miss Halcombe the assistance of his experience in forming her judgment; he was to wait until the question of the marriage was set at rest; and his hand, if that question were decided in the affirmative, was to draw the settlement which bound Miss Fairlie irrevocably to her engagement.", "그는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명을 듣게 될 것이고, 홀콤 양이 판단을 내리는 데 자신의 경험으로 도움을 줄 것이었다. 그는 혼사 문제가 매듭지어질 때까지 머물 것이고, 만일 그 문제가 긍정으로 결정된다면 페얼리 양을 그 약혼에 돌이킬 수 없이 붙들어 매는 재산 계약서를 작성할 손도 바로 그의 손이었다."], ["Even then, when I knew nothing by comparison with what I know now, I looked at the family lawyer with an interest which I had never felt before in the presence of any man breathing who was a total stranger to me.", "지금 내가 아는 것에 비하면 그때는 아무것도 모르던 시절이었건만, 그럼에도 나는 그 집안의 변호사를, 생면부지의 사람을 마주하고서는 여태 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 관심으로 바라보았다."]], [["In external appearance Mr. Gilmore was the exact opposite of the conventional idea of an old lawyer.", "겉모습으로 보면 길모어 씨는 늙은 변호사라는 통념과는 정반대였다."], ["His complexion was florid—his white hair was worn rather long and kept carefully brushed—his black coat, waistcoat, and trousers fitted him with perfect neatness—his white cravat was carefully tied, and his lavender-coloured kid gloves might have adorned the hands of a fashionable clergyman, without fear and without reproach.", "안색은 불그레하고 혈색이 좋았다. 흰 머리는 다소 길게 기른 채 정성껏 빗어 넘겼고, 검은 상의와 조끼와 바지는 흠잡을 데 없이 단정하게 몸에 맞았다."], ["His manners were pleasantly marked by the formal grace and refinement of the old school of politeness, quickened by the invigorating sharpness and readiness of a man whose business in life obliges him always to keep his faculties in good working order.", "흰 넥타이는 공들여 매어져 있었으며, 연보라색 염소 가죽 장갑은 유행에 밝은 성직자의 손을 꾸민다 해도 두려울 것도 나무랄 것도 없을 만한 물건이었다."], ["A sanguine constitution and fair prospects to begin with—a long subsequent career of creditable and comfortable prosperity—a cheerful, diligent, widely-respected old age—such were the general impressions I derived from my introduction to Mr.", "그의 태도에는 옛 예법 학파의 격식 있는 우아함과 세련됨이 기분 좋게 배어 있었고, 거기에 늘 자기 능력을 최상으로 유지해야만 하는 직업을 가진 사람다운 활기찬 예리함과 민첩함이 더해져 있었다. 낙천적인 기질과 순조로운 출발, 그 뒤로 이어진 명예롭고 안락한 번영의 긴 이력, 그리고 쾌활하고 부지런하며 두루 존경받는 노년."], ["Gilmore, and it is but fair to him to add, that the knowledge I gained by later and better experience only tended to confirm them.", "길모어 씨를 처음 소개받고 내가 받은 전반적인 인상이 그러했으며, 훗날 더 오래고 더 깊은 교유로 알게 된 바가 그 인상을 확인해 주었을 뿐이라는 사실도 그를 위해 덧붙여 두는 것이 공평하겠다."]], [["I left the old gentleman and Miss Halcombe to enter the house together, and to talk of family matters undisturbed by the restraint of a stranger’s presence.", "나는 노신사와 홀콤 양이 함께 집 안으로 들어가, 낯선 사람이 있다는 거북함 없이 집안일을 이야기하도록 자리를 비켜 주었다."], ["They crossed the hall on their way to the drawing-room, and I descended the steps again to wander about the garden alone.", "두 사람이 응접실로 가느라 홀을 가로지르는 동안, 나는 다시 계단을 내려와 혼자 정원을 거닐었다."]], [["My hours were numbered at Limmeridge House—my departure the next morning was irrevocably settled—my share in the investigation which the anonymous letter had rendered necessary was at an end.", "리머리지 하우스에서 내게 남은 시간은 이제 셀 수 있을 만큼이었다. 이튿날 아침 떠나는 일은 돌이킬 수 없이 정해졌고, 익명의 편지가 불러온 그 조사에서 내가 맡은 몫도 끝이 났다."], ["No harm could be done to any one but myself if I let my heart loose again, for the little time that was left me, from the cold cruelty of restraint which necessity had forced me to inflict upon it, and took my farewell of the scenes which were associated with the brief dream-time of my happiness and my love.", "이제 남은 짧은 시간 동안, 어쩔 수 없이 내 마음에 가해야 했던 그 차갑고 잔인한 억제에서 마음을 다시 풀어놓는다 한들, 그리하여 내 행복과 내 사랑의 짧은 꿈결 같던 시절과 얽힌 풍경들에 작별을 고한다 한들, 나 자신 말고는 누구도 해를 입을 리 없었다."]], [["I turned instinctively to the walk beneath my study-window, where I had seen her the evening before with her little dog, and followed the path which her dear feet had trodden so often, till I came to the wicket gate that led into her rose garden.", "나는 본능적으로 서재 창 아래 산책길로 발을 돌렸다."], ["The winter bareness spread drearily over it now.", "전날 저녁 그녀가 작은 개를 데리고 서 있던 그곳으로."], ["The flowers that she had taught me to distinguish by their names, the flowers that I had taught her to paint from, were gone, and the tiny white paths that led between the beds were damp and green already.", "그리고 그 사랑스러운 발이 그토록 자주 밟았던 오솔길을 따라가다가 그녀의 장미원으로 통하는 쪽문에 이르렀다. 이제 그곳에는 겨울의 헐벗음이 스산하게 퍼져 있었다."], ["I went on to the avenue of trees, where we had breathed together the warm fragrance of August evenings, where we had admired together the myriad combinations of shade and sunlight that dappled the ground at our feet.", "그녀가 내게 이름으로 구별하는 법을 가르쳐 준 꽃들, 내가 그녀에게 그것을 보고 그리는 법을 가르쳐 준 그 꽃들은 사라지고, 화단 사이로 난 자그마한 흰 길들은 벌써 축축하게 푸른 이끼가 앉아 있었다. 나는 나무들이 늘어선 길로 나아갔다."], ["The leaves fell about me from the groaning branches, and the earthy decay in the atmosphere chilled me to the bones.", "팔월 저녁의 따스한 향기를 함께 들이마시던 곳, 발밑 땅에 아롱지던 그늘과 햇빛의 무수한 어우러짐을 함께 감탄하며 바라보던 곳이었다."], ["A little farther on, and I was out of the grounds, and following the lane that wound gently upward to the nearest hills.", "신음하는 가지에서 잎사귀들이 내 주위로 떨어졌고, 대기에 밴 흙냄새 나는 부패가 뼛속까지 나를 서늘하게 했다."], ["The old felled tree by the wayside, on which we had sat to rest, was sodden with rain, and the tuft of ferns and grasses which I had drawn for her, nestling under the rough stone wall in front of us, had turned to a pool of water, stagnating round an island of draggled weeds.", "조금 더 가자 나는 저택 부지를 벗어나, 가장 가까운 언덕으로 완만하게 굽어 오르는 좁은 길을 따라가고 있었다. 우리가 앉아 쉬던 길가의 베어 넘어진 늙은 나무는 비에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 우리 앞 거친 돌담 아래 포근히 자리 잡아 내가 그녀를 위해 그려 주었던 고사리와 풀 무더기는 물웅덩이로 변해, 젖어 늘어진 잡초 섬 둘레에 괴어 있었다."], ["I gained the summit of the hill, and looked at the view which we had so often admired in the happier time.", "나는 언덕 꼭대기에 올라, 더 행복하던 시절 우리가 그토록 자주 감탄하던 경치를 바라보았다."], ["It was cold and barren—it was no longer the view that I remembered.", "그것은 차갑고 황량했다. 더 이상 내가 기억하던 그 경치가 아니었다."], ["The sunshine of her presence was far from me—the charm of her voice no longer murmured in my ear.", "그녀가 곁에 있어 비추던 햇살은 내게서 멀어졌고, 그녀 목소리의 매력은 더 이상 내 귓가에 속삭이지 않았다."], ["She had talked to me, on the spot from which I now looked down, of her father, who was her last surviving parent—had told me how fond of each other they had been, and how sadly she missed him still when she entered certain rooms in the house, and when she took up forgotten occupations and amusements with which he had been associated.", "지금 내가 내려다보고 선 바로 이 자리에서 그녀는 마지막 남은 부모였던 아버지 이야기를 내게 들려주었다. 두 사람이 얼마나 서로를 아꼈는지, 집 안 어떤 방에 들어설 때, 또 아버지와 얽힌 잊혔던 일거리며 놀이를 다시 손에 잡을 때 아직도 얼마나 애달프게 그를 그리워하는지를."], ["Was the view that I had seen, while listening to those words, the view that I saw now, standing on the hill-top by myself?", "그 말을 들으며 바라보던 그 경치가, 홀로 언덕 꼭대기에 선 지금 내가 보는 이 경치와 과연 같은 것이었을까? 나는 돌아서서 그곳을 떠났다."], ["I turned and left it—I wound my way back again, over the moor, and round the sandhills, down to the beach.", "황야를 지나 모래언덕을 돌아 해변으로 내려가며 왔던 길을 되짚었다. 그곳에는 하얗게 성난 파도가 있었고, 뛰노는 물결의 무수한 영광이 있었다."], ["There was the white rage of the surf, and the multitudinous glory of the leaping waves—but where was the place on which she had once drawn idle figures with her parasol in the sand—the place where we had sat together, while she talked to me about myself and my home, while she asked me a woman’s minutely observant questions about my mother and my sister, and innocently wondered whether I should ever leave my lonely chambers and have a wife and a house of my own?", "그러나 그녀가 언젠가 양산으로 모래 위에 무심한 그림을 그리던 그 자리는 어디에 있는가. 우리가 나란히 앉아 있던 그 자리, 그녀가 나 자신과 내 집에 대해 이야기를 청하고, 내 어머니와 누이에 대해 여자다운 세심한 질문을 던지며, 내가 언젠가 그 쓸쓸한 하숙방을 떠나 아내와 내 집을 갖게 될지 순진하게 궁금해하던 그 자리는."], ["Wind and wave had long since smoothed out the trace of her which she had left in those marks on the sand.", "바람과 물결은 이미 오래전에 그녀가 모래 위 자국으로 남긴 흔적을 지워 버렸다."], ["I looked over the wide monotony of the sea-side prospect, and the place in which we two had idled away the sunny hours was as lost to me as if I had never known it, as strange to me as if I stood already on a foreign shore.", "나는 바닷가 풍경의 드넓은 단조로움을 둘러보았다. 우리 둘이 볕 좋은 시간을 한가로이 흘려보내던 그 자리는, 마치 내가 한 번도 알지 못했던 곳처럼 내게서 사라졌고, 마치 내가 이미 낯선 나라의 해변에 서 있기라도 한 듯 생소했다."]], [["The empty silence of the beach struck cold to my heart.", "텅 빈 해변의 정적이 내 가슴을 차갑게 때렸다."], ["I returned to the house and the garden, where traces were left to speak of her at every turn.", "나는 집으로, 그리고 걸음마다 그녀를 말해 주는 흔적이 남아 있는 정원으로 돌아왔다."]], [["On the west terrace walk I met Mr. Gilmore.", "서쪽 테라스 산책길에서 나는 길모어 씨와 마주쳤다."], ["He was evidently in search of me, for he quickened his pace when we caught sight of each other.", "그는 분명 나를 찾고 있었다. 서로를 알아보자마자 걸음을 빨리했기 때문이다."], ["The state of my spirits little fitted me for the society of a stranger; but the meeting was inevitable, and I resigned myself to make the best of it.", "내 마음 상태는 낯선 사람과 어울리기에 도무지 맞지 않았으나, 만남은 피할 수 없었으므로 나는 되도록 잘 넘겨 보자고 마음먹었다."]], [["“You are the very person I wanted to see,”", "“마침 뵙고 싶던 바로 그분이시군요.”"], ["said the old gentleman.", "노신사가 말했다."], ["“I had two words to say to you, my dear sir; and if you have no objection I will avail myself of the present opportunity.", "“두어 마디 드릴 말씀이 있었는데, 괜찮으시다면 지금 기회를 빌리겠습니다. 터놓고 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양과 저는 집안일을 의논하던 참이었습니다."], ["To put it plainly, Miss Halcombe and I have been talking over family affairs—affairs which are the cause of my being here—and in the course of our conversation she was naturally led to tell me of this unpleasant matter connected with the anonymous letter, and of the share which you have most creditably and properly taken in the proceedings so far.", "제가 여기 온 까닭이 되는 그 일 말입니다. 그러다 이야기 도중에 자연스럽게 홀콤 양이 익명의 편지와 얽힌 그 불쾌한 사건을 제게 들려주었고, 지금까지의 처리 과정에서 선생께서 참으로 훌륭하고도 온당하게 맡아 주신 몫도 이야기해 주었지요."], ["That share, I quite understand, gives you an interest which you might not otherwise have felt, in knowing that the future management of the investigation which you have begun will be placed in safe hands.", "그 몫이 있는 만큼, 선생께서 시작하신 조사의 앞으로의 처리가 믿을 만한 손에 맡겨지는지 알고 싶으신 마음이 여느 경우와는 달리 각별하시리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다."], ["My dear sir, make yourself quite easy on that point—it will be placed in MY hands.”", "그 점이라면 마음을 아주 놓으십시오. 그 일은 제 손에 맡겨질 것이니까요.”"]], [["“You are, in every way, Mr. Gilmore, much fitter to advise and to act in the matter than I am.", "“길모어 씨, 그 일을 조언하고 처리하시기에는 어느 모로 보나 저보다 훨씬 적임이십니다."], ["Is it an indiscretion on my part to ask if you have decided yet on a course of proceeding?”", "혹 실례가 되지 않는다면, 앞으로 어떻게 하실지 이미 정하셨는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“So far as it is possible to decide, Mr. Hartright, I have decided.", "“정할 수 있는 데까지는 정했습니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["I mean to send a copy of the letter, accompanied by a statement of the circumstances, to Sir Percival Glyde’s solicitor in London, with whom I have some acquaintance.", "저는 그 편지 사본을 사정을 적은 진술서와 함께 런던에 있는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 사무변호사에게 보낼 참입니다. 저와도 얼마간 안면이 있는 사람이지요."], ["The letter itself I shall keep here to show to Sir Percival as soon as he arrives.", "편지 원본은 여기 두었다가 퍼시벌 경이 도착하는 대로 보여 드리겠습니다. 두 여자의 행방을 좇는 일은 이미 손을 써 두었습니다."], ["The tracing of the two women I have already provided for, by sending one of Mr.", "페얼리 씨의 하인 하나를, 입이 무거운 사람으로 골라 역에 보내 알아보게 했지요."], ["Fairlie’s servants—a confidential person—to the station to make inquiries.", "그자에게는 여비와 지시를 주었으니, 실마리라도 잡히면 여자들을 뒤쫓을 것입니다."], ["The man has his money and his directions, and he will follow the women in the event of his finding any clue.", "월요일에 퍼시벌 경이 올 때까지 할 수 있는 일은 이것이 전부입니다. 저로서는 신사이자 명예를 아는 사람에게 기대할 수 있는 해명이라면 무엇이든 그분이 선선히 내놓으리라는 데 조금도 의심이 없습니다."], ["This is all that can be done until Sir Percival comes on Monday.", "퍼시벌 경으로 말하자면 대단히 높은 분이시지요."], ["I have no doubt myself that every explanation which can be expected from a gentleman and a man of honour, he will readily give.", "지위도 훌륭하고, 평판에는 의심할 구석이 없습니다. 결과에 대해서는 아주 마음이 놓입니다."], ["Sir Percival stands very high, sir—an eminent position, a reputation above suspicion—I feel quite easy about results—quite easy, I am rejoiced to assure you.", "아주 놓인다고 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다. 제 경험으로 보면 이런 종류의 일은 끊임없이 일어납니다."], ["Things of this sort happen constantly in my experience.", "익명의 편지, 불행한 여자, 개탄스러운 세태 말입니다."], ["Anonymous letters—unfortunate woman—sad state of society.", "이 사건에 유별난 얽힘이 있다는 것은 부정하지 않겠습니다."], ["I don’t deny that there are peculiar complications in this case; but the case itself is, most unhappily, common—common.”", "그러나 사건 자체는 참으로 딱하게도 흔합니다. 흔하고말고요.”"]], [["“I am afraid, Mr. Gilmore, I have the misfortune to differ from you in the view I take of the case.”", "“길모어 씨, 유감스럽게도 저는 이 사건을 보는 견해가 선생과 다른 듯합니다.”"]], [["“Just so, my dear sir—just so.", "“그러시겠지요. 그러시겠지요."], ["I am an old man, and I take the practical view. You are a young man, and you take the romantic view.", "저는 늙은이라 실제적인 견해를 취하고, 선생은 젊은이라 낭만적인 견해를 취하십니다."], ["Let us not dispute about our views.", "우리 견해를 두고 다투지는 맙시다."], ["I live professionally in an atmosphere of disputation, Mr. Hartright, and I am only too glad to escape from it, as I am escaping here.", "하트라이트 씨, 저는 직업상 언제나 논쟁의 공기 속에서 살아가는 사람이라, 지금 여기서 그러하듯 거기서 벗어나는 것이 더없이 반갑습니다."], ["We will wait for events—yes, yes, yes—we will wait for events.", "우리는 일이 어떻게 되어 가는지 기다려 보지요. 그럼요, 그럼요, 기다려 봅시다."], ["Charming place this.", "참 멋진 곳이군요."], ["Good shooting?", "사냥은 잘됩니까?"], ["Probably not, none of Mr.", "아마 아니겠지요."], ["Fairlie’s land is preserved, I think.", "페얼리 씨 땅에는 사냥터로 관리하는 데가 없지 싶습니다."], ["Charming place, though, and delightful people.", "그래도 멋진 곳이고, 사람들도 유쾌합니다."], ["You draw and paint, I hear, Mr. Hartright?", "듣자니 하트라이트 씨는 그림을 그리신다지요?"], ["Enviable accomplishment.", "부러운 재주입니다."], ["What style?”", "화풍은 어떠신지요?”"]], [["We dropped into general conversation, or rather, Mr.", "우리는 이런저런 이야기로 흘러갔다."], ["Gilmore talked and I listened.", "아니, 정확히는 길모어 씨가 말하고 나는 듣기만 했다."], ["My attention was far from him, and from the topics on which he discoursed so fluently.", "내 마음은 그에게서도, 그가 그토록 유창하게 늘어놓는 화제에서도 멀리 떠나 있었다."], ["The solitary walk of the last two hours had wrought its effect on me—it had set the idea in my mind of hastening my departure from Limmeridge House.", "지난 두 시간의 홀로 걷기가 내게 작용을 일으켜, 리머리지 하우스를 떠나는 것을 앞당기자는 생각을 마음에 심어 놓았던 것이다."], ["Why should I prolong the hard trial of saying farewell by one unnecessary minute?", "작별을 고하는 그 힘겨운 시련을 무엇 하러 단 일 분이라도 쓸데없이 늘여야 한단 말인가?"], ["What further service was required of me by any one?", "이제 누가 내게 더 무슨 소용을 바란단 말인가?"], ["There was no useful purpose to be served by my stay in Cumberland—there was no restriction of time in the permission to leave which my employer had granted to me.", "내가 컴벌랜드에 머물러서 이룰 수 있는 쓸모 있는 목적은 없었고, 고용주가 허락한 퇴거에는 시일의 제약도 없었다."], ["Why not end it there and then?", "그렇다면 지금 당장 끝내지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?"]], [["I determined to end it.", "나는 끝내기로 마음먹었다."], ["There were some hours of daylight still left—there was no reason why my journey back to London should not begin on that afternoon.", "아직 해가 남은 시간이 몇 시간 있었고, 런던으로 돌아가는 여정을 그날 오후에 시작하지 못할 이유가 없었다."], ["I made the first civil excuse that occurred to me for leaving Mr. Gilmore, and returned at once to the house.", "나는 그 자리에서 떠오른 대로 예의 바른 핑계를 하나 대어 길모어 씨에게서 물러나, 곧장 집으로 돌아왔다."]], [["On my way up to my own room I met Miss Halcombe on the stairs.", "내 방으로 올라가는 길에 계단에서 홀콤 양과 마주쳤다."], ["She saw, by the hurry of my movements and the change in my manner, that I had some new purpose in view, and asked what had happened.", "그녀는 내 서두르는 몸짓과 달라진 태도를 보고 내게 새로운 작정이 생겼음을 알아채고는 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었다."]], [["I told her the reasons which induced me to think of hastening my departure, exactly as I have told them here.", "나는 떠남을 앞당기려 생각하게 된 이유를 여기 적은 그대로 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“No, no,”", "“안 돼요, 안 돼요.”"], ["she said, earnestly and kindly, “leave us like a friend—break bread with us once more.", "그녀가 간곡하고도 다정하게 말했다. “친구처럼 우리 곁을 떠나세요."], ["Stay here and dine, stay here and help us to spend our last evening with you as happily, as like our first evenings, as we can.", "한 번만 더 우리와 빵을 나누세요. 여기 머물러 저녁을 드시고, 여기 머물러 우리가 당신과 보내는 마지막 저녁을 처음 저녁들처럼 즐겁게 보내도록 도와주세요."], ["It is my invitation—Mrs.", "제 청이에요."], ["Vesey’s invitation——”", "베지 부인의 청이기도 하고요.”"], ["she hesitated a little, and then added, “Laura’s invitation as well.”", "그녀는 잠시 망설이더니 덧붙였다. “로라의 청이기도 해요.”"]], [["I promised to remain.", "나는 남겠다고 약속했다."], ["God knows I had no wish to leave even the shadow of a sorrowful impression with any one of them.", "하느님은 아시리라, 나는 그들 중 누구에게도 슬픈 인상의 그림자조차 남기고 싶지 않았다."]], [["My own room was the best place for me till the dinner bell rang.", "저녁 식사를 알리는 종이 울릴 때까지는 내 방이 내게 가장 알맞은 자리였다."], ["I waited there till it was time to go downstairs.", "나는 아래층으로 내려갈 시간이 될 때까지 그곳에서 기다렸다."]], [["I had not spoken to Miss Fairlie—I had not even seen her—all that day.", "나는 그날 하루 종일 페얼리 양과 말을 나누지 않았다."], ["The first meeting with her, when I entered the drawing-room, was a hard trial to her self-control and to mine.", "아니, 얼굴조차 보지 못했다. 응접실에 들어섰을 때의 그 첫 대면은 그녀의 자제심에도 내 자제심에도 힘겨운 시련이었다."], ["She, too, had done her best to make our last evening renew the golden bygone time—the time that could never come again.", "그녀 역시 우리의 마지막 저녁이 지나간 황금 같은 시절을, 다시는 오지 않을 그 시절을 되살리도록 최선을 다한 터였다."], ["She had put on the dress which I used to admire more than any other that she possessed—a dark blue silk, trimmed quaintly and prettily with old-fashioned lace; she came forward to meet me with her former readiness—she gave me her hand with the frank, innocent good-will of happier days.", "그녀는 자신이 가진 옷 가운데 내가 무엇보다 예쁘다고 하던 옷을 입고 있었다. 옛스러운 레이스로 정갈하고 어여쁘게 장식한 짙푸른 비단옷이었다."], ["The cold fingers that trembled round mine—the pale cheeks with a bright red spot burning in the midst of them—the faint smile that struggled to live on her lips and died away from them while I looked at it, told me at what sacrifice of herself her outward composure was maintained.", "그녀는 예전처럼 선뜻 나를 맞으러 나왔고, 더 행복하던 날들의 그 솔직하고 순진한 호의로 내게 손을 내밀었다. 내 손안에서 떨리던 차가운 손가락, 한복판에 새빨간 점 하나가 타오르던 창백한 뺨, 입술 위에 살아남으려 애쓰다가 내가 바라보는 사이 스러져 버린 희미한 미소가 그녀의 겉으로 드러난 침착함이 어떤 자기희생 위에 지탱되고 있는지를 내게 말해 주었다."], ["My heart could take her no closer to me, or I should have loved her then as I had never loved her yet.", "내 마음은 그녀를 더 가까이 끌어당길 수 없을 만큼 이미 가득 차 있었으니, 그러지 않았다면 나는 그때 여태껏 사랑한 적 없는 방식으로 그녀를 사랑했으리라."]], [["Mr. Gilmore was a great assistance to us.", "길모어 씨는 우리에게 큰 도움이 되었다."], ["He was in high good-humour, and he led the conversation with unflagging spirit.", "그는 기분이 몹시 좋았고 지칠 줄 모르는 기운으로 이야기를 이끌었다."], ["Miss Halcombe seconded him resolutely, and I did all I could to follow her example.", "홀콤 양이 꿋꿋이 그를 거들었고, 나도 그녀를 본받으려 있는 힘을 다했다."], ["The kind blue eyes, whose slightest changes of expression I had learnt to interpret so well, looked at me appealingly when we first sat down to table.", "표정의 가장 미미한 변화까지 내가 읽을 줄 알게 된 그 다정한 푸른 눈이, 우리가 처음 식탁에 앉았을 때 애원하듯 나를 바라보았다."], ["Help my sister—the sweet anxious face seemed to say—help my sister, and you will help me.", "언니를 도와 주세요, 그 상냥하고 근심 어린 얼굴은 그렇게 말하는 듯했다. 언니를 도와 주세요, 그러면 저를 돕는 거예요."]], [["We got through the dinner, to all outward appearance at least, happily enough.", "우리는 적어도 겉보기로는 그런대로 즐겁게 저녁 식사를 마쳤다."], ["When the ladies had risen from table, and Mr. Gilmore and I were left alone in the dining-room, a new interest presented itself to occupy our attention, and to give me an opportunity of quieting myself by a few minutes of needful and welcome silence.", "부인들이 식탁에서 일어나고 길모어 씨와 나만 식당에 남았을 때, 새로운 관심거리가 나타나 우리의 주의를 끌었고, 그 덕분에 나는 꼭 필요하고 반가운 몇 분간의 침묵으로 마음을 가라앉힐 기회를 얻었다."], ["The servant who had been despatched to trace Anne Catherick and Mrs. Clements returned with his report, and was shown into the dining-room immediately.", "앤 캐서릭과 클레멘츠 부인의 자취를 좇으라고 보냈던 하인이 보고를 가지고 돌아와, 곧바로 식당으로 안내되어 들어온 것이다."]], [["“Well,” said Mr.", "“그래, 무엇을 알아냈나?”"], ["Gilmore, “what have you found out?”", "길모어 씨가 물었다."]], [["“I have found out, sir,”", "“알아낸 바로는요, 어르신,”"], ["answered the man, “that both the women took tickets at our station here for Carlisle.”", "하인이 대답했다. “두 여자가 여기 우리 역에서 칼라일까지 표를 끊었습니다.”"]], [["“You went to Carlisle, of course, when you heard that?”", "“그 말을 들었으면 당연히 칼라일로 갔겠지?”"]], [["“I did, sir, but I am sorry to say I could find no further trace of them.”", "“갔습니다, 어르신. 그런데 송구스럽게도 그 뒤로는 아무 자취도 찾지 못했습니다.”"]], [["“You inquired at the railway?”", "“철도에서 물어보았나?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“예, 어르신.”"]], [["“And at the different inns?”", "“여러 여관에서도?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“예, 어르신.”"]], [["“And you left the statement I wrote for you at the police station?”", "“내가 써 준 진술서는 경찰서에 맡기고?”"]], [["“I did, sir.”", "“그리했습니다, 어르신.”"]], [["“Well, my friend, you have done all you could, and I have done all I could, and there the matter must rest till further notice.", "“그래, 이 사람아, 자네도 할 수 있는 일은 다 했고 나도 할 수 있는 일은 다 했으니, 이 일은 다음 소식이 있을 때까지 그대로 두어야겠네.”"], ["We have played our trump cards, Mr.", "하인이 물러가자 노신사는 말을 이었다."], ["Hartright,” continued the old gentleman when the servant had withdrawn.", "“우리는 으뜸패를 다 냈습니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["“For the present, at least, the women have outmanoeuvred us, and our only resource now is to wait till Sir Percival Glyde comes here on Monday next.", "적어도 당분간은 그 여자들이 우리보다 한 수 앞섰고, 이제 우리에게 남은 방도라고는 다음 월요일에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 이곳에 올 때까지 기다리는 것뿐입니다."], ["Won’t you fill your glass again?", "잔을 다시 채우지 않으시렵니까?"], ["Good bottle of port, that—sound, substantial, old wine.", "좋은 포트와인이군요. 잡스럽지 않고 묵직한 옛 술입니다."], ["I have got better in my own cellar, though.”", "하기야 제 지하 저장고에는 이보다 나은 것이 있습니다만.”"]], [["We returned to the drawing-room—the room in which the happiest evenings of my life had been passed—the room which, after this last night, I was never to see again.", "우리는 응접실로 돌아왔다. 내 생애 가장 행복한 저녁들을 보낸 방, 이 마지막 밤이 지나면 다시는 보지 못할 그 방으로."], ["Its aspect was altered since the days had shortened and the weather had grown cold.", "해가 짧아지고 날이 추워진 뒤로 그곳의 모습도 달라져 있었다."], ["The glass doors on the terrace side were closed, and hidden by thick curtains.", "테라스 쪽 유리문은 닫힌 채 두꺼운 커튼에 가려져 있었다."], ["Instead of the soft twilight obscurity, in which we used to sit, the bright radiant glow of lamplight now dazzled my eyes.", "우리가 즐겨 앉아 있던 그 부드러운 황혼의 어스름 대신, 이제는 등불의 환하고 눈부신 빛이 내 눈을 부시게 했다. 모든 것이 달라져 있었다."], ["All was changed—indoors and out all was changed.", "안에서도 밖에서도 모든 것이 달라져 있었다."]], [["Miss Halcombe and Mr. Gilmore sat down together at the card-table—Mrs.", "홀콤 양과 길모어 씨는 카드 탁자에 나란히 앉았고, 베지 부인은 늘 앉던 의자를 차지했다."], ["Vesey took her customary chair. There was no restraint on the disposal of THEIR evening, and I felt the restraint on the disposal of mine all the more painfully from observing it.", "저들의 저녁을 어떻게 쓸지에는 아무런 구속이 없었고, 그것을 보고 있자니 내 저녁을 어떻게 쓸지에 걸린 구속이 한층 더 아프게 느껴졌다."], ["I saw Miss Fairlie lingering near the music-stand.", "페얼리 양이 악보대 근처에서 머뭇거리는 것이 보였다."], ["The time had been when I might have joined her there.", "예전 같으면 나도 그 곁으로 갔을 것이다. 나는 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다."], ["I waited irresolutely—I knew neither where to go nor what to do next.", "어디로 가야 할지도, 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지도 알 수 없었다."], ["She cast one quick glance at me, took a piece of music suddenly from the stand, and came towards me of her own accord.", "그녀가 나를 한 번 재빨리 흘긋 보더니 악보대에서 악보 한 장을 불쑥 집어 들고는, 스스로 내 쪽으로 다가왔다."]], [["“Shall I play some of those little melodies of Mozart’s which you used to like so much?”", "“당신이 그토록 좋아하시던 모차르트의 그 짧은 곡들을 좀 쳐 드릴까요?”"], ["she asked, opening the music nervously, and looking down at it while she spoke.", "그녀는 초조하게 악보를 펴며, 말하는 내내 악보를 내려다본 채 물었다."]], [["Before I could thank her she hastened to the piano.", "내가 고맙다는 말을 하기도 전에 그녀는 서둘러 피아노로 갔다."], ["The chair near it, which I had always been accustomed to occupy, stood empty.", "그 곁에 있는, 내가 늘 차지하던 의자가 비어 있었다."], ["She struck a few chords—then glanced round at me—then looked back again at her music.", "그녀는 화음을 몇 개 짚더니 나를 돌아보았고, 이내 다시 악보로 눈을 돌렸다."]], [["“Won’t you take your old place?”", "“예전 자리에 앉지 않으시겠어요?”"], ["she said, speaking very abruptly and in very low tones.", "그녀는 몹시 불쑥, 그리고 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I may take it on the last night,”", "“마지막 밤이니 앉아도 되겠지요.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["She did not reply—she kept her attention riveted on the music—music which she knew by memory, which she had played over and over again, in former times, without the book. I only knew that she had heard me, I only knew that she was aware of my being close to her, by seeing the red spot on the cheek that was nearest to me fade out, and the face grow pale all over.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그저 악보에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 외워서 알고 있는 곡, 예전에는 책 없이도 몇 번이고 되풀이해 치던 곡이었건만. 내 말을 들었다는 것을, 내가 자기 곁에 있음을 알고 있다는 것을 나는 오직 한 가지로 알 수 있었다. 내게 가까운 쪽 뺨의 붉은 점이 스러지고 얼굴 전체가 하얗게 질려 가는 것을 보고서였다."]], [["“I am very sorry you are going,”", "“떠나신다니 정말 슬퍼요.”"], ["she said, her voice almost sinking to a whisper, her eyes looking more and more intently at the music, her fingers flying over the keys of the piano with a strange feverish energy which I had never noticed in her before.", "그녀가 말했다. 목소리는 거의 속삭임으로 잦아들었고, 눈은 점점 더 뚫어지게 악보를 향했으며, 손가락은 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 기묘하고도 열에 들뜬 기운으로 건반 위를 날아다녔다."]], [["“I shall remember those kind words, Miss Fairlie, long after to-morrow has come and gone.”", "“그 다정한 말씀을, 페얼리 양, 저는 내일이 오고 또 가 버린 뒤로도 오래도록 기억하겠습니다.”"]], [["The paleness grew whiter on her face, and she turned it farther away from me.", "그녀의 얼굴은 더욱 하얗게 질렸고, 그녀는 얼굴을 나에게서 더 멀리 돌렸다."]], [["“Don’t speak of to-morrow,”", "“내일 이야기는 하지 마세요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Let the music speak to us of to-night, in a happier language than ours.”", "“오늘 밤 이야기는 음악이 대신 하게 해 주세요. 우리 말보다 더 행복한 말로요.”"]], [["Her lips trembled—a faint sigh fluttered from them, which she tried vainly to suppress.", "그녀의 입술이 떨리더니 억누르려 애썼으나 헛되이 새어 나온 희미한 한숨이 흘렀다."], ["Her fingers wavered on the piano—she struck a false note, confused herself in trying to set it right, and dropped her hands angrily on her lap. Miss Halcombe and Mr.", "손가락이 건반 위에서 흔들렸고, 그녀는 틀린 음을 짚었으며, 그것을 바로잡으려다 스스로 헝클어져 두 손을 성난 듯 무릎에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Gilmore looked up in astonishment from the card-table at which they were playing. Even Mrs.", "카드놀이를 하던 홀콤 양과 길모어 씨가 탁자에서 놀란 얼굴로 고개를 들었다."], ["Vesey, dozing in her chair, woke at the sudden cessation of the music, and inquired what had happened.", "의자에서 졸던 베지 부인조차 음악이 갑자기 그치자 잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."]], [["“You play at whist, Mr. Hartright?”", "“하트라이트 씨, 휘스트는 하실 줄 아시지요?”"], ["asked Miss Halcombe, with her eyes directed significantly at the place I occupied.", "홀콤 양이 내가 앉아 있는 자리에 의미심장하게 눈길을 던지며 물었다."]], [["I knew what she meant—I knew she was right, and I rose at once to go to the card-table.", "나는 그 뜻을 알았다. 그녀가 옳다는 것도 알았기에, 나는 곧바로 일어나 카드 탁자로 갔다."], ["As I left the piano Miss Fairlie turned a page of the music, and touched the keys again with a surer hand.", "내가 피아노를 떠날 때 페얼리 양은 악보를 한 장 넘기고 아까보다 확실해진 손길로 다시 건반을 짚었다."]], [["“I WILL play it,”", "“꼭 칠 거예요.”"], ["she said, striking the notes almost passionately.", "그녀는 거의 격정적으로 건반을 때리며 말했다."], ["“I WILL play it on the last night.”", "“마지막 밤이니 꼭 칠 거예요.”"]], [["“Come, Mrs. Vesey,”", "“베지 부인, 이리 오세요.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, “Mr.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."], ["Gilmore and I are tired of ecarte—come and be Mr.", "“길모어 씨와 저는 에카르테에 싫증이 났어요."], ["Hartright’s partner at whist.”", "오셔서 하트라이트 씨의 휘스트 짝이 되어 주세요.”"]], [["The old lawyer smiled satirically.", "늙은 변호사가 빈정거리듯 웃었다."], ["His had been the winning hand, and he had just turned up a king.", "이기고 있던 쪽은 그였고, 방금 킹까지 뒤집어 낸 참이었다."], ["He evidently attributed Miss Halcombe’s abrupt change in the card-table arrangements to a lady’s inability to play the losing game.", "그는 홀콤 양이 카드 탁자의 자리 배치를 불쑥 바꾼 것을 지고 있는 판을 견디지 못하는 여자의 성미 탓으로 돌린 것이 분명했다."]], [["The rest of the evening passed without a word or a look from her.", "그날 저녁 남은 시간은 그녀에게서 말 한마디, 눈길 한 번 받지 못한 채 흘러갔다."], ["She kept her place at the piano, and I kept mine at the card-table.", "그녀는 피아노 앞자리를 지켰고 나는 카드 탁자 앞자리를 지켰다."], ["She played unintermittingly—played as if the music was her only refuge from herself.", "그녀는 쉬지 않고 연주했다. 마치 음악만이 자기 자신에게서 벗어날 유일한 피난처인 듯이."], ["Sometimes her fingers touched the notes with a lingering fondness—a soft, plaintive, dying tenderness, unutterably beautiful and mournful to hear; sometimes they faltered and failed her, or hurried over the instrument mechanically, as if their task was a burden to them.", "어떤 때 그녀의 손가락은 머뭇거리는 애틋함으로, 부드럽고 애처롭고 스러져 가는 다정함으로 음을 어루만졌는데, 그 소리는 말로 다할 수 없이 아름답고도 구슬펐다. 또 어떤 때는 손가락이 흔들리며 그녀를 저버리거나, 그 일이 짐이라도 되는 듯 기계적으로 악기 위를 서둘러 지나갔다."], ["But still, change and waver as they might in the expression they imparted to the music, their resolution to play never faltered.", "그러나 음악에 실어 보내는 표정이 아무리 변하고 흔들려도, 연주를 계속하겠다는 결심만은 결코 흔들리지 않았다."], ["She only rose from the piano when we all rose to say good night.", "그녀가 피아노에서 일어난 것은 우리 모두가 작별 인사를 하려고 일어섰을 때뿐이었다."]], [["Mrs. Vesey was the nearest to the door, and the first to shake hands with me.", "문에 가장 가까이 있던 베지 부인이 제일 먼저 내게 악수를 청했다."]], [["“I shall not see you again, Mr. Hartright,”", "“이제 다시 뵙지 못하겠군요, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["“I am truly sorry you are going away.", "“떠나신다니 참으로 섭섭합니다."], ["You have been very kind and attentive, and an old woman like me feels kindness and attention.", "그동안 무척 친절하고 자상하셨어요. 나 같은 늙은이는 친절과 자상함을 알아본답니다."], ["I wish you happy, sir—I wish you a kind good-bye.”", "부디 행복하시기를 빕니다. 부디 다정한 작별이 되기를 빕니다.”"]], [["Mr. Gilmore came next.", "그다음은 길모어 씨였다."]], [["“I hope we shall have a future opportunity of bettering our acquaintance, Mr.", "“앞으로 더 가까이 지낼 기회가 있기를 바랍니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright. You quite understand about that little matter of business being safe in my hands?", "그 자잘한 일거리가 제 손에 안전히 맡겨졌다는 것은 잘 아시지요?"], ["Yes, yes, of course.", "그럼요, 그럼요, 물론이지요."], ["Bless me, how cold it is!", "이런, 어찌 이리 춥습니까!"], ["Don’t let me keep you at the door.", "문간에 오래 붙들어 두어서는 안 되겠군요."], ["Bon voyage, my dear sir—bon voyage, as the French say.”", "봉 보야주입니다, 선생. 프랑스 사람들 말마따나 봉 보야주.”"]], [["Miss Halcombe followed.", "이어 홀콤 양이었다."]], [["“Half-past seven to-morrow morning,”", "“내일 아침 일곱 시 반이에요.”"], ["she said—then added in a whisper, “I have heard and seen more than you think.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하더니 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. “저는 당신이 생각하시는 것보다 많이 듣고 많이 보았어요."], ["Your conduct to-night has made me your friend for life.”", "오늘 밤 당신의 처신이 저를 평생 당신의 친구로 만들었습니다.”"]], [["Miss Fairlie came last.", "페얼리 양이 마지막이었다."], ["I could not trust myself to look at her when I took her hand, and when I thought of the next morning.", "그녀의 손을 잡을 때, 그리고 이튿날 아침을 떠올릴 때, 나는 그녀를 차마 바라볼 자신이 없었다."]], [["“My departure must be a very early one,” I said.", "“제가 아주 이른 시각에 떠나야 합니다.”"], ["“I shall be gone, Miss Fairlie, before you——”", "내가 말했다. “페얼리 양, 저는 당신이 아직……”"]], [["“No, no,”", "“아니에요, 아니에요.”"], ["she interposed hastily, “not before I am out of my room.", "그녀가 서둘러 말을 가로막았다. “제가 방에서 나오기 전은 아니에요."], ["I shall be down to breakfast with Marian.", "저도 매리언과 함께 아침 식사에 내려올 거예요."], ["I am not so ungrateful, not so forgetful of the past three months——”", "저는 그렇게 은혜를 모르지도, 지난 세 달을 그렇게 쉬이 잊지도……”"]], [["Her voice failed her, her hand closed gently round mine—then dropped it suddenly.", "목소리가 끊겼고, 그녀의 손이 내 손을 부드럽게 감쌌다가 이내 툭 놓았다."], ["Before I could say “Good-night” she was gone.", "내가 “안녕히 주무세요”라고 말할 새도 없이 그녀는 사라졌다."]], [["The end comes fast to meet me—comes inevitably, as the light of the last morning came at Limmeridge House.", "끝은 빠르게 나를 향해 다가온다. 리머리지 하우스에서 맞은 마지막 아침의 빛이 그러했듯, 피할 길 없이 다가온다."]], [["It was barely half-past seven when I went downstairs, but I found them both at the breakfast-table waiting for me.", "내가 아래층으로 내려간 것은 겨우 일곱 시 반이었으나, 두 사람은 이미 아침 식탁에서 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["In the chill air, in the dim light, in the gloomy morning silence of the house, we three sat down together, and tried to eat, tried to talk.", "싸늘한 공기 속에서, 어스름한 빛 속에서, 집 안의 침울한 아침 정적 속에서 우리 셋은 함께 앉아 먹으려 애쓰고 이야기하려 애썼다."], ["The struggle to preserve appearances was hopeless and useless, and I rose to end it.", "겉모습을 지켜 내려는 싸움은 가망도 소용도 없었으므로, 나는 그것을 끝내려고 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["As I held out my hand, as Miss Halcombe, who was nearest to me, took it, Miss Fairlie turned away suddenly and hurried from the room.", "내가 손을 내밀고 가장 가까이 있던 홀콤 양이 그 손을 잡는 순간, 페얼리 양이 홱 돌아서더니 서둘러 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Better so,”", "“그편이 낫습니다.”"], ["said Miss Halcombe, when the door had closed—“better so, for you and for her.”", "문이 닫히자 홀콤 양이 말했다. “당신에게나 저 아이에게나 그편이 나아요.”"]], [["I waited a moment before I could speak—it was hard to lose her, without a parting word or a parting look.", "나는 말을 꺼내기까지 잠시 기다려야 했다. 작별의 말 한마디, 작별의 눈길 한 번 없이 그녀를 잃는 것은 견디기 힘든 일이었다."], ["I controlled myself—I tried to take leave of Miss Halcombe in fitting terms; but all the farewell words I would fain have spoken dwindled to one sentence.", "나는 스스로를 다잡았다. 홀콤 양에게 격에 맞는 말로 작별을 고하려 애썼으나, 기꺼이 하고 싶었던 그 모든 이별의 말이 한 문장으로 오그라들고 말았다."]], [["“Have I deserved that you should write to me?”", "“제가 편지를 받을 자격이 있겠습니까?”"], ["was all I could say.", "내가 할 수 있는 말이라고는 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“You have nobly deserved everything that I can do for you, as long as we both live.", "“당신은 우리 두 사람이 살아 있는 한 제가 해 드릴 수 있는 모든 것을 받을 자격을 훌륭하게 얻으셨어요."], ["Whatever the end is you shall know it.”", "끝이 어떻게 되든 반드시 알려 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["“And if I can ever be of help again, at any future time, long after the memory of my presumption and my folly is forgotten . . .”", "“그리고 언젠가 다시 제 도움이 필요할 때가 온다면, 제 주제넘음과 어리석음에 대한 기억이 잊힌 지 한참 뒤라도……”"]], [["I could add no more.", "나는 더 말을 이을 수 없었다."], ["My voice faltered, my eyes moistened in spite of me.", "목소리가 떨렸고, 나도 모르게 눈시울이 젖었다."]], [["She caught me by both hands—she pressed them with the strong, steady grasp of a man—her dark eyes glittered—her brown complexion flushed deep—the force and energy of her face glowed and grew beautiful with the pure inner light of her generosity and her pity.", "그녀가 두 손으로 내 손을 붙잡더니 남자처럼 힘 있고 굳건한 악력으로 그것을 꽉 쥐었다. 검은 눈이 반짝였고, 볕에 그은 얼굴빛이 짙게 달아올랐다. 그녀의 관대함과 연민에서 나온 맑은 내면의 빛으로 그 얼굴의 힘과 기운이 빛나며 아름다워졌다."]], [["“I will trust you—if ever the time comes I will trust you as my friend and HER friend, as my brother and HER brother.”", "“저는 당신을 믿겠어요. 언젠가 그런 때가 온다면 저는 당신을 제 친구이자 저 아이의 친구로, 제 오라비이자 저 아이의 오라비로 믿겠어요.”"], ["She stopped, drew me nearer to her—the fearless, noble creature—touched my forehead, sister-like, with her lips, and called me by my Christian name.", "그녀는 말을 멈추더니 나를 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그 두려움 없고 고결한 사람은 누이가 그러하듯 입술로 내 이마에 입을 맞추고 나를 세례명으로 불렀다."], ["“God bless you, Walter!”", "“신의 가호가 있기를, 월터!” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said.", "“여기 혼자 남아 마음을 가라앉히세요."], ["“Wait here alone and compose yourself—I had better not stay for both our sakes—I had better see you go from the balcony upstairs.”", "우리 둘 다를 위해 제가 여기 있지 않는 편이 낫겠어요. 위층 발코니에서 당신이 가는 것을 보는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["She left the room.", "그녀는 방을 나갔다. 나는 창 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["I turned away towards the window, where nothing faced me but the lonely autumn landscape—I turned away to master myself, before I too left the room in my turn, and left it for ever.", "그곳에는 쓸쓸한 가을 풍경 말고는 나를 마주할 것이 아무것도 없었다. 나 역시 내 차례가 되어 그 방을 나서기 전에, 그리하여 영영 그 방을 떠나기 전에 스스로를 다스리려고 돌아선 것이었다."]], [["A minute passed—it could hardly have been more—when I heard the door open again softly, and the rustling of a woman’s dress on the carpet moved towards me.", "일 분이 지났을까, 그보다 길지는 않았을 것이다. 문이 다시 살며시 열리는 소리가 들렸고, 카펫 위로 여자 옷자락이 스치는 소리가 내 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["My heart beat violently as I turned round.", "돌아서는 나의 심장이 사납게 뛰었다."], ["Miss Fairlie was approaching me from the farther end of the room.", "페얼리 양이 방 저쪽 끝에서 내게로 다가오고 있었다."]], [["She stopped and hesitated when our eyes met, and when she saw that we were alone.", "우리 눈이 마주쳤을 때, 그리고 우리 둘뿐임을 알았을 때 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 머뭇거렸다."], ["Then, with that courage which women lose so often in the small emergency, and so seldom in the great, she came on nearer to me, strangely pale and strangely quiet, drawing one hand after her along the table by which she walked, and holding something at her side in the other, which was hidden by the folds of her dress.", "그러더니 여자들이 사소한 위급에는 그토록 자주 잃으면서도 큰 위급에는 좀처럼 잃지 않는 그 용기로, 이상하리만치 창백하고 이상하리만치 조용한 얼굴로 내게 더 가까이 다가왔다. 한 손은 걸어가는 길에 놓인 탁자를 따라 끌듯 짚었고, 다른 손에는 무언가를 옆구리께에 들고 있었는데 그것은 옷자락 주름에 가려져 있었다."]], [["“I only went into the drawing-room,”", "“이걸 찾으러 응접실에 잠깐 다녀왔어요.”"], ["she said, “to look for this.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["It may remind you of your visit here, and of the friends you leave behind you.", "“여기 머무셨던 일과, 뒤에 남겨 두고 가시는 벗들을 떠올리게 해 줄지도 모르니까요."], ["You told me I had improved very much when I did it, and I thought you might like——”", "제가 이걸 그렸을 때 아주 많이 늘었다고 말씀해 주셨잖아요. 그래서 마음에 드실지도 모른다고 생각해서……”"]], [["She turned her head away, and offered me a little sketch, drawn throughout by her own pencil, of the summer-house in which we had first met.", "그녀는 고개를 돌리며 작은 스케치 한 장을 내밀었다. 우리가 처음 만났던 그 정자를 그녀가 처음부터 끝까지 제 손으로 그린 것이었다."], ["The paper trembled in her hand as she held it out to me—trembled in mine as I took it from her.", "그녀가 그것을 내밀 때 그 손에서 종이가 떨렸고, 내가 그것을 건네받을 때 내 손에서도 떨렸다."]], [["I was afraid to say what I felt—I only answered, “It shall never leave me—all my life long it shall be the treasure that I prize most. I am very grateful for it—very grateful to you, for not letting me go away without bidding you good-bye.”", "내가 느끼는 바를 말하기가 두려워, 나는 이렇게만 대답했다. “이것은 결코 제 곁을 떠나지 않을 겁니다. 평생토록 제가 가장 소중히 여기는 보물이 될 겁니다. 참으로 감사합니다. 작별 인사도 없이 저를 보내지 않으신 것도 참으로 감사합니다.”"]], [["“Oh!”", "“어머!”"], ["she said innocently, “how could I let you go, after we have passed so many happy days together!”", "그녀가 순진하게 말했다. “그토록 행복한 날들을 함께 보내고서, 제가 어떻게 그냥 보내 드릴 수 있겠어요!”"]], [["“Those days may never return, Miss Fairlie—my way of life and yours are very far apart.", "“그런 날들은 다시 오지 않을지도 모릅니다, 페얼리 양. 제가 사는 길과 당신이 사는 길은 아주 멀리 떨어져 있으니까요."], ["But if a time should come, when the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength will give you a moment’s happiness, or spare you a moment’s sorrow, will you try to remember the poor drawing-master who has taught you?", "그러나 만일 제 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다 바친 헌신이 당신에게 한순간의 행복을 드리거나 한순간의 슬픔을 덜어 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 당신을 가르쳤던 이 가난한 그림 선생을 기억해 주시겠습니까?"], ["Miss Halcombe has promised to trust me—will you promise too?”", "홀콤 양은 저를 믿겠노라 약속해 주셨습니다. 당신도 약속해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["The farewell sadness in the kind blue eyes shone dimly through her gathering tears.", "그 다정한 푸른 눈에 어린 작별의 슬픔이 차오르는 눈물 너머로 흐릿하게 빛났다."]], [["“I promise it,”", "“약속할게요.”"], ["she said in broken tones.", "그녀는 끊어지는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Oh, don’t look at me like that!", "“아, 저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요!"], ["I promise it with all my heart.”", "온 마음을 다해 약속해요.”"]], [["I ventured a little nearer to her, and held out my hand.", "나는 조금 더 그녀에게 다가서서 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“You have many friends who love you, Miss Fairlie.", "“페얼리 양, 당신을 사랑하는 벗이 많습니다."], ["Your happy future is the dear object of many hopes.", "당신의 행복한 앞날은 숱한 소망들이 품은 소중한 바람입니다."], ["May I say, at parting, that it is the dear object of MY hopes too?”", "헤어지면서 이 말을 드려도 될까요. 그것은 제 소망이 품은 소중한 바람이기도 하다고요.”"]], [["The tears flowed fast down her cheeks.", "눈물이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 빠르게 흘러내렸다."], ["She rested one trembling hand on the table to steady herself while she gave me the other.", "그녀는 떨리는 한 손을 탁자에 짚어 몸을 지탱하며 다른 손을 내게 내밀었다. 나는 그 손을 내 손에 받아 꼭 붙들었다."], ["I took it in mine—I held it fast.", "내 머리가 그 위로 수그러졌고, 내 눈물이 그 위에 떨어졌으며, 내 입술이 그 손을 눌렀다."], ["My head drooped over it, my tears fell on it, my lips pressed it—not in love; oh, not in love, at that last moment, but in the agony and the self-abandonment of despair.", "사랑에서가 아니었다. 아, 그 마지막 순간에 사랑에서가 아니라, 절망의 고통과 자포자기에서였다."]], [["“For God’s sake, leave me!”", "“제발 부탁이니, 저를 두고 가세요!”"], ["she said faintly.", "그녀가 힘없이 말했다."]], [["The confession of her heart’s secret burst from her in those pleading words.", "애원하는 그 말과 함께 그녀의 가슴에 숨은 비밀이 터져 나왔다."], ["I had no right to hear them, no right to answer them—they were the words that banished me, in the name of her sacred weakness, from the room.", "나에게는 그 말을 들을 권리도, 그 말에 답할 권리도 없었다. 그것은 그녀의 신성한 연약함의 이름으로 나를 그 방에서 내쫓는 말이었다."], ["It was all over.", "모든 것이 끝났다."], ["I dropped her hand, I said no more.", "나는 그녀의 손을 놓았고, 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["The blinding tears shut her out from my eyes, and I dashed them away to look at her for the last time.", "앞을 가리는 눈물이 그녀를 내 시야에서 지워 버렸으므로, 나는 마지막으로 그녀를 보려고 눈물을 훔쳐 냈다."], ["One look as she sank into a chair, as her arms fell on the table, as her fair head dropped on them wearily.", "그녀가 의자에 주저앉으며 두 팔을 탁자 위로 떨어뜨리고 그 고운 머리를 그 위에 지쳐 떨구는 모습을 한 번 보았다."], ["One farewell look, and the door had closed upon her—the great gulf of separation had opened between us—the image of Laura Fairlie was a memory of the past already.", "작별의 눈길 한 번, 그리고 문은 그녀를 가리며 닫혔다. 우리 사이에는 이별의 커다란 심연이 열렸고, 로라 페얼리의 모습은 이미 지나간 날의 기억이 되어 있었다."]], [["The End of Hartright’s Narrative.", "하트라이트의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다."]], [["(of Chancery Lane, Solicitor)", "(챈서리 레인의 사무변호사)"]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["I write these lines at the request of my friend, Mr. Walter Hartright.", "나는 벗인 월터 하트라이트 씨의 요청으로 이 글을 쓴다."], ["They are intended to convey a description of certain events which seriously affected Miss Fairlie’s interests, and which took place after the period of Mr. Hartright’s departure from Limmeridge House.", "이 글은 페얼리 양의 이해관계에 중대한 영향을 미친 어떤 사건들, 곧 하트라이트 씨가 리머리지 하우스를 떠난 뒤에 일어난 사건들을 기술하기 위한 것이다."]], [["There is no need for me to say whether my own opinion does or does not sanction the disclosure of the remarkable family story, of which my narrative forms an important component part.", "내 서술이 중요한 한 부분을 이루는 저 기이한 집안 이야기를 세상에 내놓는 일에 대해, 내 개인적 견해가 찬성인지 반대인지는 여기서 말할 필요가 없다."], ["Mr. Hartright has taken that responsibility on himself, and circumstances yet to be related will show that he has amply earned the right to do so, if he chooses to exercise it.", "하트라이트 씨가 그 책임을 스스로 떠맡았고, 앞으로 이야기될 사정들이 밝혀 주겠지만 그는 그럴 권리를 충분히 얻었으므로, 그 권리를 행사하겠다면 그것으로 족하다."], ["The plan he has adopted for presenting the story to others, in the most truthful and most vivid manner, requires that it should be told, at each successive stage in the march of events, by the persons who were directly concerned in those events at the time of their occurrence.", "이 이야기를 가장 진실하고 가장 생생한 방식으로 남에게 전하기 위해 그가 택한 방침은, 사건이 진행되는 각 단계마다 그 당시 그 사건에 직접 관계했던 사람들이 저마다 그 대목을 이야기하도록 하는 것이다."], ["My appearance here, as narrator, is the necessary consequence of this arrangement.", "내가 여기 서술자로 등장하는 것은 그 방침에서 나온 필연적인 결과다."], ["I was present during the sojourn of Sir Percival Glyde in Cumberland, and was personally concerned in one important result of his short residence under Mr. Fairlie’s roof.", "나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 컴벌랜드에 머무는 동안 그 자리에 있었고, 그가 페얼리 씨의 지붕 아래 짧게 체류한 결과 생긴 중요한 일 하나에 직접 관여했다."], ["It is my duty, therefore, to add these new links to the chain of events, and to take up the chain itself at the point where, for the present only, Mr. Hartright has dropped it.", "따라서 이 사건의 사슬에 새 고리를 몇 개 잇고, 하트라이트 씨가 일단은 놓아 둔 그 지점에서 사슬 자체를 이어받는 것이 내 소임이다."]], [["I arrived at Limmeridge House on Friday the second of November.", "나는 십일월 이일 금요일에 리머리지 하우스에 도착했다."]], [["My object was to remain at Mr. Fairlie’s until the arrival of Sir Percival Glyde.", "내 목적은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 올 때까지 페얼리 씨 댁에 머무는 것이었다."], ["If that event led to the appointment of any given day for Sir Percival’s union with Miss Fairlie, I was to take the necessary instructions back with me to London, and to occupy myself in drawing the lady’s marriage-settlement.", "그의 방문으로 퍼시벌 경과 페얼리 양의 혼인 날짜가 정해진다면, 나는 필요한 지시를 받아 런던으로 돌아가 그 아가씨의 혼인 계약서를 작성하는 일에 착수할 참이었다."]], [["On the Friday I was not favoured by Mr. Fairlie with an interview.", "금요일에는 페얼리 씨가 나를 만나 주는 호의를 베풀지 않았다."], ["He had been, or had fancied himself to be, an invalid for years past, and he was not well enough to receive me.", "그는 여러 해 전부터 병약한 몸이었거나 스스로 그렇다고 여겨 왔는데, 그날은 나를 맞을 만큼 건강하지 못했던 것이다."], ["Miss Halcombe was the first member of the family whom I saw.", "내가 처음 만난 집안사람은 홀콤 양이었다."], ["She met me at the house door, and introduced me to Mr. Hartright, who had been staying at Limmeridge for some time past.", "그녀는 현관에서 나를 맞았고, 얼마 전부터 리머리지에 머물고 있던 하트라이트 씨를 소개해 주었다."]], [["I did not see Miss Fairlie until later in the day, at dinner-time.", "페얼리 양은 그날 늦게, 만찬 자리에서야 보았다."], ["She was not looking well, and I was sorry to observe it.", "안색이 좋지 않아 보여 나는 마음이 아팠다."], ["She is a sweet lovable girl, as amiable and attentive to every one about her as her excellent mother used to be—though, personally speaking, she takes after her father.", "그녀는 상냥하고 사랑스러운 아가씨로, 훌륭했던 어머니가 그러했듯 주위 사람 모두에게 다정하고 세심하다. 다만 생김새로 말하자면 아버지를 닮았다."], ["Mrs. Fairlie had dark eyes and hair, and her elder daughter, Miss Halcombe, strongly reminds me of her.", "페얼리 부인은 눈과 머리칼이 검었는데, 그 맏딸인 홀콤 양이 어머니를 몹시 닮았다."], ["Miss Fairlie played to us in the evening—not so well as usual, I thought.", "페얼리 양은 저녁에 우리에게 연주를 들려주었으나, 내 생각에는 평소만 못했다."], ["We had a rubber at whist, a mere profanation, so far as play was concerned, of that noble game.", "우리는 휘스트를 한 판 쳤는데, 승부로 보자면 그 고귀한 놀이에 대한 모독이나 다름없었다."], ["I had been favourably impressed by Mr. Hartright on our first introduction to one another, but I soon discovered that he was not free from the social failings incidental to his age.", "나는 하트라이트 씨를 처음 소개받았을 때 좋은 인상을 받았으나, 그가 제 나이에 따르는 사교상의 결점에서 자유롭지 못하다는 것을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["There are three things that none of the young men of the present generation can do.", "요즘 세대의 젊은이들이 하지 못하는 것이 세 가지 있다."], ["They can’t sit over their wine, they can’t play at whist, and they can’t pay a lady a compliment.", "그들은 술잔을 앞에 두고 진득하게 앉아 있지 못하고, 휘스트를 칠 줄 모르며, 여자에게 인사치레 한마디 건네지 못한다."], ["Mr. Hartright was no exception to the general rule.", "하트라이트 씨도 이 일반 법칙의 예외는 아니었다."], ["Otherwise, even in those early days and on that short acquaintance, he struck me as being a modest and gentlemanlike young man.", "그 점만 빼면, 그 초기의 짧은 사귐 속에서도 그는 겸손하고 신사다운 청년으로 보였다."]], [["So the Friday passed.", "그렇게 금요일이 지나갔다."], ["I say nothing about the more serious matters which engaged my attention on that day—the anonymous letter to Miss Fairlie, the measures I thought it right to adopt when the matter was mentioned to me, and the conviction I entertained that every possible explanation of the circumstances would be readily afforded by Sir Percival Glyde, having all been fully noticed, as I understand, in the narrative which precedes this.", "그날 내 주의를 끈 더 심각한 일들, 곧 페얼리 양에게 온 익명의 편지와, 그 일을 전해 듣고 내가 취하는 것이 옳다고 판단한 조치와, 그 정황에 대해 있을 수 있는 모든 해명을 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 기꺼이 내놓으리라던 내 확신에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 내가 알기로 이 앞의 서술에서 이미 남김없이 다루어졌기 때문이다."]], [["On the Saturday Mr. Hartright had left before I got down to breakfast.", "토요일에는 내가 아침을 먹으러 내려가기 전에 하트라이트 씨가 이미 떠나고 없었다."], ["Miss Fairlie kept her room all day, and Miss Halcombe appeared to me to be out of spirits.", "페얼리 양은 온종일 방에 틀어박혀 있었고, 홀콤 양은 내가 보기에 기운이 없어 보였다."], ["The house was not what it used to be in the time of Mr. and Mrs.", "이 집은 필립 페얼리 부부가 계시던 시절의 그 집이 아니었다."], ["Philip Fairlie. I took a walk by myself in the forenoon, and looked about at some of the places which I first saw when I was staying at Limmeridge to transact family business, more than thirty years since.", "나는 오전에 혼자 산책을 나가, 삼십 년도 더 전에 집안일을 처리하러 리머리지에 머물 적에 처음 보았던 곳들을 둘러보았다."], ["They were not what they used to be either.", "그곳들 또한 예전의 그곳들이 아니었다."]], [["At two o’clock Mr. Fairlie sent to say he was well enough to see me.", "두 시에 페얼리 씨가 나를 만날 만큼 몸이 낫다고 사람을 보내왔다."], ["HE had not altered, at any rate, since I first knew him.", "적어도 그만은 내가 처음 알았을 때와 조금도 달라지지 않았다."], ["His talk was to the same purpose as usual—all about himself and his ailments, his wonderful coins, and his matchless Rembrandt etchings.", "그의 이야기는 늘 그렇듯 한결같은 것이었다. 온통 자기 자신과 자기 병, 그 훌륭하다는 주화들, 견줄 데 없다는 렘브란트 동판화 이야기뿐이었다."], ["The moment I tried to speak of the business that had brought me to his house, he shut his eyes and said I “upset” him.", "내가 이 집에 온 용건을 꺼내려 하자마자 그는 눈을 감고 내가 자기를 “심란하게 한다”고 말했다."], ["I persisted in upsetting him by returning again and again to the subject.", "나는 그 주제로 자꾸만 되돌아가며 기어이 그를 심란하게 했다."], ["All I could ascertain was that he looked on his niece’s marriage as a settled thing, that her father had sanctioned it, that he sanctioned it himself, that it was a desirable marriage, and that he should be personally rejoiced when the worry of it was over.", "내가 확인할 수 있었던 것은 이뿐이었다. 그는 조카딸의 혼인을 이미 정해진 일로 보고 있고, 그 아이 아버지가 승낙했으며, 자기도 승낙했고, 바람직한 혼사이며, 이 성가신 일이 끝나면 개인적으로 기쁘겠다는 것이었다."], ["As to the settlements, if I would consult his niece, and afterwards dive as deeply as I pleased into my own knowledge of the family affairs, and get everything ready, and limit his share in the business, as guardian, to saying Yes, at the right moment—why, of course he would meet my views, and everybody else’s views, with infinite pleasure.", "재산 설정에 관해서라면, 내가 조카딸과 상의하고, 그 다음에는 집안 사정에 대한 내 지식을 마음껏 깊이 파고들어, 모든 것을 준비해 놓은 뒤, 후견인으로서 그가 할 몫은 적당한 때에 “예”라고 말하는 것으로 한정해 준다면, 아무렴, 그는 무한한 기쁨으로 내 뜻에도 다른 누구의 뜻에도 따르겠다는 것이었다."], ["In the meantime, there I saw him, a helpless sufferer, confined to his room.", "그러는 동안 보다시피 그는 방에 갇힌 무력한 환자였다."], ["Did I think he looked as if he wanted teasing?", "내가 보기에 그가 시달림을 당해도 될 사람 같은가? 아니지."], ["No. Then why tease him?", "그렇다면 어찌하여 그를 시달리게 하는가?"]], [["I might, perhaps, have been a little astonished at this extraordinary absence of all self-assertion on Mr.", "후견인이라는 자리에 있으면서 자기 주장이라고는 이토록 놀랍도록 없는 페얼리 씨의 태도에, 나는 어쩌면 조금 놀랐을지도 모른다."], ["Fairlie’s part, in the character of guardian, if my knowledge of the family affairs had not been sufficient to remind me that he was a single man, and that he had nothing more than a life-interest in the Limmeridge property.", "집안 사정에 대한 내 지식이, 그가 독신이며 리머리지 재산에 대해 종신 수익권 이상은 가지고 있지 않다는 사실을 일깨워 주지 않았다면 말이다."], ["As matters stood, therefore, I was neither surprised nor disappointed at the result of the interview.", "사정이 그러했으므로 나는 그 면담의 결과에 놀라지도 실망하지도 않았다."], ["Mr. Fairlie had simply justified my expectations—and there was an end of it.", "페얼리 씨는 그저 내 예상을 그대로 확인해 주었을 뿐이며, 그것으로 끝이었다."]], [["Sunday was a dull day, out of doors and in.", "일요일은 바깥도 안도 따분한 날이었다."], ["A letter arrived for me from Sir Percival Glyde’s solicitor, acknowledging the receipt of my copy of the anonymous letter and my accompanying statement of the case.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 사무 변호사에게서 편지가 한 통 왔는데, 내가 보낸 익명 편지 사본과 사건 진술서를 잘 받았다는 회신이었다."], ["Miss Fairlie joined us in the afternoon, looking pale and depressed, and altogether unlike herself.", "페얼리 양은 오후에 우리와 자리를 함께했는데, 창백하고 풀이 죽어 도무지 평소의 그녀 같지 않았다."], ["I had some talk with her, and ventured on a delicate allusion to Sir Percival.", "나는 그녀와 얼마간 이야기를 나누다가 조심스럽게 퍼시벌 경 이야기를 넌지시 꺼내 보았다."], ["She listened and said nothing.", "그녀는 듣기만 할 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["All other subjects she pursued willingly, but this subject she allowed to drop.", "다른 화제는 무엇이든 기꺼이 이어 갔으나, 이 화제만은 그대로 흘려 버리게 두었다."], ["I began to doubt whether she might not be repenting of her engagement—just as young ladies often do, when repentance comes too late.", "나는 그녀가 혹시 약혼을 후회하고 있는 것은 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다. 젊은 아가씨들이 흔히 그러듯, 후회가 너무 늦게 찾아온 경우처럼 말이다."]], [["On Monday Sir Percival Glyde arrived.", "월요일에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 도착했다."]], [["I found him to be a most prepossessing man, so far as manners and appearance were concerned.", "나는 그가 태도와 용모로 보자면 대단히 호감 가는 사람임을 알았다."], ["He looked rather older than I had expected, his head being bald over the forehead, and his face somewhat marked and worn, but his movements were as active and his spirits as high as a young man’s.", "이마 위쪽이 벗어지고 얼굴에 다소 주름과 세월의 자국이 있어 내가 예상했던 것보다는 나이 들어 보였으나, 몸놀림은 젊은이처럼 활기찼고 기운도 젊은이 못지않았다."], ["His meeting with Miss Halcombe was delightfully hearty and unaffected, and his reception of me, upon my being presented to him, was so easy and pleasant that we got on together like old friends.", "홀콤 양과의 인사는 유쾌할 만큼 정답고 꾸밈이 없었으며, 내가 그에게 소개되었을 때 나를 맞는 태도도 어찌나 편안하고 기분 좋던지 우리는 오랜 친구처럼 어울렸다."], ["Miss Fairlie was not with us when he arrived, but she entered the room about ten minutes afterwards.", "그가 도착했을 때 페얼리 양은 자리에 없었으나 십 분쯤 뒤에 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Sir Percival rose and paid his compliments with perfect grace.", "퍼시벌 경은 일어서서 더없이 우아하게 인사를 건넸다."], ["His evident concern on seeing the change for the worse in the young lady’s looks was expressed with a mixture of tenderness and respect, with an unassuming delicacy of tone, voice, and manner, which did equal credit to his good breeding and his good sense.", "그 아가씨의 안색이 나빠진 것을 보고 그가 드러낸 걱정은 다정함과 존중이 뒤섞인 것이었고, 어조와 목소리와 몸가짐 어디에도 젠체하는 구석이 없이 섬세하여, 그의 좋은 가정교육과 좋은 분별력을 똑같이 잘 보여 주었다."], ["I was rather surprised, under these circumstances, to see that Miss Fairlie continued to be constrained and uneasy in his presence, and that she took the first opportunity of leaving the room again.", "이런 마당에 페얼리 양이 그의 앞에서 여전히 서먹하고 불편해하며, 기회가 오자마자 다시 방을 나가 버리는 것을 보고 나는 다소 놀랐다."], ["Sir Percival neither noticed the restraint in her reception of him, nor her sudden withdrawal from our society.", "퍼시벌 경은 그녀가 자기를 맞는 태도의 서먹함도, 우리 자리에서 갑자기 물러가는 것도 알은체하지 않았다."], ["He had not obtruded his attentions on her while she was present, and he did not embarrass Miss Halcombe by any allusion to her departure when she was gone.", "그녀가 있는 동안 그는 자기 관심을 억지로 들이밀지 않았고, 그녀가 나간 뒤에도 그 일을 입에 올려 홀콤 양을 난처하게 하지 않았다."], ["His tact and taste were never at fault on this or on any other occasion while I was in his company at Limmeridge House.", "내가 리머리지 하우스에서 그와 함께 지내는 동안, 이 일에서든 다른 어떤 일에서든 그의 요령과 안목은 한 번도 어긋나는 법이 없었다."]], [["As soon as Miss Fairlie had left the room he spared us all embarrassment on the subject of the anonymous letter, by adverting to it of his own accord.", "페얼리 양이 방을 나가자마자 그는 익명의 편지 이야기를 스스로 꺼내어, 그 문제로 우리가 겪을 곤란을 모두 덜어 주었다."], ["He had stopped in London on his way from Hampshire, had seen his solicitor, had read the documents forwarded by me, and had travelled on to Cumberland, anxious to satisfy our minds by the speediest and the fullest explanation that words could convey.", "그는 햄프셔에서 오는 길에 런던에 들러 사무 변호사를 만나고, 내가 보낸 서류를 읽었으며, 말로 전할 수 있는 가장 신속하고 가장 완전한 해명으로 우리 마음을 풀어 주고자 하는 마음에 컴벌랜드까지 곧장 왔다고 했다."], ["On hearing him express himself to this effect, I offered him the original letter, which I had kept for his inspection.", "그가 그런 뜻을 밝히는 것을 듣고 나는 그가 살펴볼 수 있도록 간직해 두었던 편지 원본을 내밀었다."], ["He thanked me, and declined to look at it, saying that he had seen the copy, and that he was quite willing to leave the original in our hands.", "그는 고맙다면서도 보기를 사양했다. 사본을 이미 보았으니 원본은 우리 손에 그대로 두어도 조금도 상관없다는 것이었다."]], [["The statement itself, on which he immediately entered, was as simple and satisfactory as I had all along anticipated it would be.", "그가 곧바로 들어간 그 진술 자체는, 내가 처음부터 그러리라고 예상했던 대로 단순하고 흡족했다."]], [["Mrs. Catherick, he informed us, had in past years laid him under some obligations for faithful services rendered to his family connections and to himself.", "그의 말에 따르면, 캐서릭 부인은 지난 세월 동안 그의 집안과 그 자신에게 충실히 봉사하여 그로 하여금 얼마간 신세를 지게 한 사람이었다."], ["She had been doubly unfortunate in being married to a husband who had deserted her, and in having an only child whose mental faculties had been in a disturbed condition from a very early age.", "그녀는 두 겹으로 불행했다. 남편이라는 자가 그녀를 버리고 떠났고, 하나뿐인 아이는 아주 어릴 적부터 정신 기능에 이상이 있었다."], ["Although her marriage had removed her to a part of Hampshire far distant from the neighbourhood in which Sir Percival’s property was situated, he had taken care not to lose sight of her—his friendly feeling towards the poor woman, in consideration of her past services, having been greatly strengthened by his admiration of the patience and courage with which she supported her calamities.", "혼인으로 인해 그녀는 퍼시벌 경의 소유지가 있는 곳에서 멀리 떨어진 햄프셔의 한 지역으로 옮겨 갔으나, 그는 그녀를 놓치지 않으려고 마음을 써 왔다. 지난날의 봉사를 생각하여 그 딱한 여인에게 품은 우호적인 마음이, 재난을 견디는 그녀의 인내와 용기에 대한 감탄으로 크게 굳어졌기 때문이다."], ["In course of time the symptoms of mental affliction in her unhappy daughter increased to such a serious extent, as to make it a matter of necessity to place her under proper medical care.", "세월이 흐르면서 그 불행한 딸의 정신 질환 증세는 심각해져, 마침내 제대로 된 의료의 보호 아래 두지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Mrs. Catherick herself recognised this necessity, but she also felt the prejudice common to persons occupying her respectable station, against allowing her child to be admitted, as a pauper, into a public Asylum.", "캐서릭 부인 자신도 그 필요를 인정했으나, 동시에 그녀와 같은 점잖은 신분의 사람들이 흔히 갖는 편견, 곧 제 아이를 극빈자로 공립 정신병원에 들여보내는 데 대한 거부감도 느꼈다."], ["Sir Percival had respected this prejudice, as he respected honest independence of feeling in any rank of life, and had resolved to mark his grateful sense of Mrs. Catherick’s early attachment to the interests of himself and his family, by defraying the expense of her daughter’s maintenance in a trustworthy private Asylum.", "퍼시벌 경은 어느 신분에서든 정직한 자존심을 존중하듯 이 편견을 존중했고, 자신과 자기 집안의 이익에 일찍이 마음을 다해 준 캐서릭 부인에게 고마움을 표하는 뜻으로, 믿을 만한 사설 요양원에 딸을 두는 비용을 자기가 부담하기로 마음먹었다."], ["To her mother’s regret, and to his own regret, the unfortunate creature had discovered the share which circumstances had induced him to take in placing her under restraint, and had conceived the most intense hatred and distrust of him in consequence.", "그런데 어머니로서도 유감이고 그 자신으로서도 유감스럽게도, 그 불행한 아이는 자기를 감호 아래 두는 데 정황상 그가 맡게 된 몫을 알아내고, 그 결과 그에 대해 더없이 강렬한 증오와 불신을 품게 되었다."], ["To that hatred and distrust—which had expressed itself in various ways in the Asylum—the anonymous letter, written after her escape, was plainly attributable. If Miss Halcombe’s or Mr.", "요양원 안에서 여러 모양으로 드러났던 그 증오와 불신에, 그녀가 탈출한 뒤에 쓴 그 익명의 편지도 분명히 돌릴 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["Gilmore’s recollection of the document did not confirm that view, or if they wished for any additional particulars about the Asylum (the address of which he mentioned, as well as the names and addresses of the two doctors on whose certificates the patient was admitted), he was ready to answer any question and to clear up any uncertainty.", "만일 홀콤 양이나 길모어 씨가 기억하는 그 문서의 내용이 이 견해와 맞지 않는다면, 혹은 그 요양원에 대해 더 자세한 사항을 알고 싶다면, 어떤 물음에도 답하고 어떤 의문도 풀어 줄 준비가 되어 있다고 했다. 그러면서 그는 요양원 주소는 물론, 환자를 입원시킬 때 소견서를 쓴 두 의사의 이름과 주소까지 일러 주었다."], ["He had done his duty to the unhappy young woman, by instructing his solicitor to spare no expense in tracing her, and in restoring her once more to medical care, and he was now only anxious to do his duty towards Miss Fairlie and towards her family, in the same plain, straightforward way.", "그는 사무 변호사에게 비용을 아끼지 말고 그녀를 찾아내어 다시 의료의 보호 아래 돌려보내라고 지시함으로써 그 불행한 젊은 여인에 대한 자기 도리를 다했으며, 이제는 페얼리 양과 그 집안에 대한 도리도 똑같이 분명하고 솔직한 방식으로 다하기만을 바랄 뿐이라고 했다."]], [["I was the first to speak in answer to this appeal.", "이 호소에 가장 먼저 대답한 사람은 나였다."], ["My own course was plain to me.", "내가 갈 길은 분명했다."], ["It is the great beauty of the Law that it can dispute any human statement, made under any circumstances, and reduced to any form.", "어떤 정황에서 어떤 형식으로 이루어진 인간의 진술이든 다툴 수 있다는 것이 법의 크나큰 아름다움이다."], ["If I had felt professionally called upon to set up a case against Sir Percival Glyde, on the strength of his own explanation, I could have done so beyond all doubt.", "만일 내가 직업상 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명 자체를 근거로 그에게 불리한 논거를 세워야 할 처지였다면, 나는 의심할 여지 없이 그렇게 할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["But my duty did not lie in this direction—my function was of the purely judicial kind.", "그러나 내 소임은 그쪽에 있지 않았다. 내 구실은 순전히 재판관의 것이었다."], ["I was to weigh the explanation we had just heard, to allow all due force to the high reputation of the gentleman who offered it, and to decide honestly whether the probabilities, on Sir Percival’s own showing, were plainly with him, or plainly against him.", "나는 방금 들은 해명을 저울에 달고, 그것을 내놓은 신사의 높은 평판에 마땅한 무게를 다 실어 준 뒤, 퍼시벌 경 자신의 진술로 볼 때 개연성이 명백히 그의 편인지 명백히 그의 반대편인지를 정직하게 판단해야 했다."], ["My own conviction was that they were plainly with him, and I accordingly declared that his explanation was, to my mind, unquestionably a satisfactory one.", "내 확신은 개연성이 명백히 그의 편이라는 것이었고, 따라서 나는 그의 해명이 내 생각에는 의문의 여지 없이 흡족하다고 선언했다."]], [["Miss Halcombe, after looking at me very earnestly, said a few words, on her side, to the same effect—with a certain hesitation of manner, however, which the circumstances did not seem to me to warrant.", "홀콤 양은 나를 아주 진지하게 바라본 뒤 자기 쪽에서도 같은 취지로 몇 마디 했다. 다만 어딘가 망설이는 기색이 있었는데, 내가 보기에 정황상 그럴 만한 까닭은 없었다."], ["I am unable to say, positively, whether Sir Percival noticed this or not.", "퍼시벌 경이 그것을 알아챘는지 아닌지는 단언할 수 없다."], ["My opinion is that he did, seeing that he pointedly resumed the subject, although he might now, with all propriety, have allowed it to drop.", "내 생각으로는 알아챈 듯한데, 이제는 예의에 조금도 어긋나지 않게 그 화제를 흘려보낼 수 있었을 텐데도 그가 일부러 그 이야기를 다시 꺼냈기 때문이다."]], [["“If my plain statement of facts had only been addressed to Mr. Gilmore,”", "“제가 사실을 있는 그대로 말씀드린 상대가 길모어 씨뿐이었다면,”"], ["he said, “I should consider any further reference to this unhappy matter as unnecessary.", "그가 말했다. “이 불행한 일에 대해 더 언급할 필요는 없다고 여기겠지요."], ["I may fairly expect Mr. Gilmore, as a gentleman, to believe me on my word, and when he has done me that justice, all discussion of the subject between us has come to an end.", "저는 길모어 씨가 신사로서 제 말을 믿어 주시리라 마땅히 기대할 수 있고, 그렇게 저를 공정히 대해 주신 이상 우리 사이에서 이 문제에 관한 논의는 끝난 것입니다."], ["But my position with a lady is not the same.", "그러나 숙녀를 대하는 제 처지는 그와 같지 않습니다."], ["I owe to her—what I would concede to no man alive—a PROOF of the truth of my assertion.", "저는 숙녀께는, 살아 있는 어떤 남자에게도 양보하지 않을 것을 빚지고 있습니다."], ["You cannot ask for that proof, Miss Halcombe, and it is therefore my duty to you, and still more to Miss Fairlie, to offer it.", "곧 제 주장이 진실이라는 증거 말입니다. 홀콤 양, 당신은 그 증거를 요구하실 수 없으니, 당신께, 그리고 더욱이 페얼리 양께 그것을 내놓는 것이 제 도리입니다."], ["May I beg that you will write at once to the mother of this unfortunate woman—to Mrs. Catherick—to ask for her testimony in support of the explanation which I have just offered to you.”", "부디 이 불행한 여인의 어머니에게, 곧 캐서릭 부인에게 지금 당장 편지를 써서, 제가 방금 드린 해명을 뒷받침할 증언을 청해 주시기를 부탁드립니다.”"]], [["I saw Miss Halcombe change colour, and look a little uneasy.", "나는 홀콤 양의 낯빛이 달라지고 다소 불편해하는 것을 보았다."], ["Sir Percival’s suggestion, politely as it was expressed, appeared to her, as it appeared to me, to point very delicately at the hesitation which her manner had betrayed a moment or two since.", "정중하게 표현되기는 했으나 퍼시벌 경의 제안은, 내게 그렇게 보였듯 그녀에게도, 조금 전 그녀의 태도에 드러난 그 망설임을 아주 은근히 겨냥한 것으로 보였던 것이다."]], [["“I hope, Sir Percival, you don’t do me the injustice to suppose that I distrust you,”", "“퍼시벌 경, 제가 경을 믿지 못한다고 여기시어 저를 그렇게 부당하게 대하시지는 않으시겠지요.”"], ["she said quickly.", "그녀가 얼른 말했다."]], [["“Certainly not, Miss Halcombe.", "“물론 그렇지 않습니다, 홀콤 양."], ["I make my proposal purely as an act of attention to YOU.", "제 제안은 순전히 당신께 마음을 쓰는 뜻에서 드리는 것입니다."], ["Will you excuse my obstinacy if I still venture to press it?”", "그래도 굳이 이 청을 밀어붙이는 제 고집을 용서해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["He walked to the writing-table as he spoke, drew a chair to it, and opened the paper case.", "그는 말하면서 필기용 탁자로 걸어가 의자를 끌어다 놓고 편지지 상자를 열었다."]], [["“Let me beg you to write the note,”", "“부디 그 쪽지를 써 주십시오.”"], ["he said, “as a favour to ME.", "그가 말했다."], ["It need not occupy you more than a few minutes.", "“저를 위한 호의로 말입니다. 몇 분이면 될 일입니다."], ["You have only to ask Mrs. Catherick two questions.", "캐서릭 부인에게 두 가지만 물으시면 됩니다."], ["First, if her daughter was placed in the Asylum with her knowledge and approval.", "첫째, 따님이 요양원에 들어간 것이 부인이 알고 승낙한 일이었는지."], ["Secondly, if the share I took in the matter was such as to merit the expression of her gratitude towards myself? Mr.", "둘째, 그 일에서 제가 맡은 몫이 부인의 감사를 받을 만한 것이었는지."], ["Gilmore’s mind is at ease on this unpleasant subject, and your mind is at ease—pray set my mind at ease also by writing the note.”", "이 불쾌한 문제에 대해 길모어 씨의 마음은 편안하고, 당신의 마음도 편안합니다. 부디 그 쪽지를 써서 제 마음도 편안하게 해 주십시오.”"]], [["“You oblige me to grant your request, Sir Percival, when I would much rather refuse it.”", "“퍼시벌 경, 저로서는 거절하고 싶은 마음이 훨씬 큰데도 경께서 이렇게 청을 들어드리지 않을 수 없게 만드시는군요.”"]], [["With those words Miss Halcombe rose from her place and went to the writing-table.", "그 말과 함께 홀콤 양은 자리에서 일어나 필기용 탁자로 갔다."], ["Sir Percival thanked her, handed her a pen, and then walked away towards the fireplace.", "퍼시벌 경은 고맙다고 하며 그녀에게 펜을 건네고는 벽난로 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["Miss Fairlie’s little Italian greyhound was lying on the rug.", "페얼리 양의 작은 이탈리아 그레이하운드가 깔개 위에 누워 있었다."], ["He held out his hand, and called to the dog good-humouredly.", "그는 손을 내밀며 기분 좋게 개를 불렀다."]], [["“Come, Nina,”", "“이리 오너라, 니나.”"], ["he said, “we remember each other, don’t we?”", "그가 말했다. “우리 서로 기억하고 있지, 그렇지 않으냐?”"]], [["The little beast, cowardly and cross-grained, as pet-dogs usually are, looked up at him sharply, shrank away from his outstretched hand, whined, shivered, and hid itself under a sofa.", "애완견들이 대개 그러하듯 겁 많고 심술궂은 그 작은 짐승은 그를 매섭게 올려다보더니, 내민 손에서 움츠러들며 낑낑거리고 부들부들 떨다가 소파 밑으로 숨어 버렸다."], ["It was scarcely possible that he could have been put out by such a trifle as a dog’s reception of him, but I observed, nevertheless, that he walked away towards the window very suddenly.", "개가 자기를 맞는 태도 따위의 사소한 일로 그가 언짢아졌을 리는 거의 없겠으나, 그럼에도 나는 그가 매우 갑작스레 창가 쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 보았다."], ["Perhaps his temper is irritable at times.", "어쩌면 그는 이따금 성미가 급해지는 사람인지도 모른다."], ["If so, I can sympathise with him.", "그렇다면 나는 그 심정을 이해할 수 있다."], ["My temper is irritable at times too.", "나 역시 이따금 성미가 급해지니까."]], [["Miss Halcombe was not long in writing the note.", "홀콤 양이 쪽지를 쓰는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["When it was done she rose from the writing-table, and handed the open sheet of paper to Sir Percival.", "다 쓰고 나자 그녀는 필기용 탁자에서 일어나 펼친 종이를 그대로 퍼시벌 경에게 건넸다."], ["He bowed, took it from her, folded it up immediately without looking at the contents, sealed it, wrote the address, and handed it back to her in silence.", "그는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 그것을 받아 들더니, 내용을 들여다보지도 않고 곧바로 접어 봉하고 겉봉에 주소를 쓴 뒤 말없이 그녀에게 돌려주었다."], ["I never saw anything more gracefully and more becomingly done in my life.", "나는 평생 그보다 더 우아하고 더 품위 있게 처신하는 것을 본 적이 없다."]], [["“You insist on my posting this letter, Sir Percival?”", "“이 편지를 꼭 제가 부쳐야 한다는 말씀이지요, 퍼시벌 경?”"], ["said Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 말했다."]], [["“I beg you will post it,”", "“부쳐 주시기를 청합니다.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“And now that it is written and sealed up, allow me to ask one or two last questions about the unhappy woman to whom it refers.", "“그리고 이제 다 써서 봉했으니, 그 편지가 언급하는 그 불행한 여인에 대해 마지막으로 한두 가지만 여쭙게 해 주십시오."], ["I have read the communication which Mr. Gilmore kindly addressed to my solicitor, describing the circumstances under which the writer of the anonymous letter was identified.", "익명 편지를 쓴 사람이 어떤 정황에서 밝혀졌는지를 적어 길모어 씨께서 친절히 제 사무 변호사에게 보내 주신 서한은 읽었습니다."], ["But there are certain points to which that statement does not refer.", "그런데 그 진술이 다루지 않은 점들이 몇 가지 있습니다."], ["Did Anne Catherick see Miss Fairlie?”", "앤 캐서릭이 페얼리 양을 보았습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly not,”", "“결코 아닙니다.”"], ["replied Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다."]], [["“Did she see you?”", "“당신은 보았습니까?”"]], [["“No.”", "“아니요.”"]], [["“She saw nobody from the house then, except a certain Mr. Hartright, who accidentally met with her in the churchyard here?”", "“그러면 그녀는 이곳 교회 묘지에서 우연히 마주쳤다는 하트라이트 씨라는 분 말고는, 이 집안 사람 누구도 보지 못했다는 말씀이군요?”"]], [["“Nobody else.”", "“아무도 보지 못했습니다.”"]], [["“Mr. Hartright was employed at Limmeridge as a drawing-master, I believe?", "“하트라이트 씨는 리머리지에서 그림 교사로 일하셨다지요?"], ["Is he a member of one of the Water-Colour Societies?”", "수채화 협회 가운데 어느 한 곳의 회원입니까?”"]], [["“I believe he is,”", "“그런 줄로 압니다.”"], ["answered Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다."]], [["He paused for a moment, as if he was thinking over the last answer, and then added—", "그는 마지막 대답을 곱씹기라도 하는 듯 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Did you find out where Anne Catherick was living, when she was in this neighbourhood?”", "“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에 있는 동안 어디서 지냈는지는 알아내셨습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["At a farm on the moor, called Todd’s Corner.”", "황야에 있는 토드 코너라는 농가입니다.”"]], [["“It is a duty we all owe to the poor creature herself to trace her,”", "“그 딱한 사람을 찾아내는 것은 우리 모두가 그 사람 자신에게 지고 있는 도리입니다.”"], ["continued Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말을 이었다."], ["“She may have said something at Todd’s Corner which may help us to find her.", "“토드 코너에서 무슨 말이든 했다면 그것이 그녀를 찾는 데 도움이 될지도 모르지요."], ["I will go there and make inquiries on the chance.", "혹시나 해서 제가 그리로 가서 알아보겠습니다."], ["In the meantime, as I cannot prevail on myself to discuss this painful subject with Miss Fairlie, may I beg, Miss Halcombe, that you will kindly undertake to give her the necessary explanation, deferring it of course until you have received the reply to that note.”", "그러는 동안, 저로서는 도저히 이 괴로운 이야기를 페얼리 양과 나눌 마음이 나지 않으니, 홀콤 양께서 부디 그분께 필요한 설명을 해 주시는 일을 맡아 주시기를 청합니다. 물론 그 쪽지의 답장을 받은 뒤로 미루시고요.”"]], [["Miss Halcombe promised to comply with his request.", "홀콤 양은 그의 청을 따르겠다고 약속했다."], ["He thanked her, nodded pleasantly, and left us, to go and establish himself in his own room.", "그는 고맙다고 하고는 기분 좋게 고갯짓을 하고, 자기 방에 자리를 잡으러 우리 곁을 떠났다."], ["As he opened the door the cross-grained greyhound poked out her sharp muzzle from under the sofa, and barked and snapped at him.", "그가 문을 여는데 그 심술궂은 그레이하운드가 소파 밑에서 뾰족한 주둥이를 내밀고 그를 향해 짖어 대며 물려고 덤볐다."]], [["“A good morning’s work, Miss Halcombe,”", "“오전 일을 훌륭히 해내셨습니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["I said, as soon as we were alone.", "우리 둘만 남자마자 내가 말했다."], ["“Here is an anxious day well ended already.”", "“걱정스러운 하루가 벌써 잘 마무리되었군요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["she answered; “no doubt.", "그녀가 대답했다. “그렇겠지요."], ["I am very glad your mind is satisfied.”", "선생님 마음이 흡족하시다니 저도 무척 기쁩니다.”"]], [["“My mind!", "“제 마음이라니요!"], ["Surely, with that note in your hand, your mind is at ease too?”", "그 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 계신데 홀콤 양 마음도 당연히 편안하시겠지요?”"]], [["“Oh yes—how can it be otherwise?", "“아, 그럼요. 어찌 안 그렇겠어요?"], ["I know the thing could not be,”", "그럴 리 없다는 것쯤은 저도 압니다.”"], ["she went on, speaking more to herself than to me; “but I almost wish Walter Hartright had stayed here long enough to be present at the explanation, and to hear the proposal to me to write this note.”", "그녀는 내게라기보다 자기 자신에게 말하듯 말을 이었다. “그래도 월터 하트라이트가 그 해명 자리에 있을 만큼, 그리고 제게 그 쪽지를 쓰라고 청하는 것을 들을 만큼 오래 여기 머물렀더라면 싶은 마음이 드는군요.”"]], [["I was a little surprised—perhaps a little piqued also—by these last words.", "나는 이 마지막 말에 조금 놀랐고, 어쩌면 조금 언짢기도 했다."]], [["“Events, it is true, connected Mr. Hartright very remarkably with the affair of the letter,”", "“사건들이 하트라이트 씨를 그 편지 일과 아주 특별하게 얽어 놓은 것은 사실입니다.”"], ["I said; “and I readily admit that he conducted himself, all things considered, with great delicacy and discretion.", "내가 말했다. “여러 사정을 감안할 때 그가 대단히 조심스럽고 분별 있게 처신했다는 것도 기꺼이 인정합니다."], ["But I am quite at a loss to understand what useful influence his presence could have exercised in relation to the effect of Sir Percival’s statement on your mind or mine.”", "그러나 퍼시벌 경의 진술이 홀콤 양이나 제 마음에 미친 영향과 관련해, 그가 그 자리에 있었다 한들 무슨 쓸모 있는 영향을 끼칠 수 있었을지는 도무지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“It was only a fancy,”", "“그저 문득 든 생각이었어요.”"], ["she said absently.", "그녀가 멍하니 말했다."], ["“There is no need to discuss it, Mr.", "“논할 것도 없는 일입니다, 길모어 씨."], ["Gilmore. Your experience ought to be, and is, the best guide I can desire.”", "선생님의 경험이야말로 제가 바랄 수 있는 가장 좋은 길잡이여야 하고, 또 그러합니다.”"]], [["I did not altogether like her thrusting the whole responsibility, in this marked manner, on my shoulders. If Mr.", "그녀가 이렇게 두드러진 태도로 책임을 통째로 내 어깨에 떠넘기는 것이 나는 그리 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["Fairlie had done it, I should not have been surprised.", "페얼리 씨가 그랬다면 나는 놀라지 않았을 것이다."], ["But resolute, clear-minded Miss Halcombe was the very last person in the world whom I should have expected to find shrinking from the expression of an opinion of her own.", "그러나 단호하고 명석한 홀콤 양이야말로, 제 의견을 밝히기를 꺼리는 모습을 보이리라고는 세상에서 가장 상상하기 어려운 사람이었다."]], [["“If any doubts still trouble you,”", "“아직도 어떤 의심이 마음에 걸리신다면,”"], ["I said, “why not mention them to me at once?", "내가 말했다. “지금 곧 제게 말씀하시는 것이 어떻겠습니까?"], ["Tell me plainly, have you any reason to distrust Sir Percival Glyde?”", "솔직히 말씀해 보십시오. 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 믿지 못할 이유라도 있으신 겁니까?”"]], [["“None whatever.”", "“전혀 없습니다.”"]], [["“Do you see anything improbable, or contradictory, in his explanation?”", "“그의 해명에서 있을 법하지 않거나 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 데라도 보이십니까?”"]], [["“How can I say I do, after the proof he has offered me of the truth of it?", "“그분이 그 해명이 진실이라는 증거까지 내놓으셨는데 제가 어떻게 그렇다고 하겠습니까?"], ["Can there be better testimony in his favour, Mr. Gilmore, than the testimony of the woman’s mother?”", "길모어 씨, 그 여인의 어머니가 하는 증언보다 그분께 더 나은 증언이 있을 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["“None better.", "“더 나은 것은 없지요."], ["If the answer to your note of inquiry proves to be satisfactory, I for one cannot see what more any friend of Sir Percival’s can possibly expect from him.”", "홀콤 양이 보내신 문의 쪽지에 대한 답이 흡족하게 나온다면, 적어도 저로서는 퍼시벌 경의 벗 된 사람이 그에게 그 이상 무엇을 바랄 수 있을지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Then we will post the note,”", "“그럼 쪽지를 부치고,”"], ["she said, rising to leave the room, “and dismiss all further reference to the subject until the answer arrives.", "그녀가 방을 나가려 일어서며 말했다. “답장이 올 때까지 이 이야기는 더 꺼내지 말기로 하지요."], ["Don’t attach any weight to my hesitation.", "제 망설임에는 아무 무게도 두지 마세요."], ["I can give no better reason for it than that I have been over-anxious about Laura lately—and anxiety, Mr.", "요즘 로라 일로 지나치게 마음을 졸였다는 것 말고는 달리 까닭을 댈 수가 없군요."], ["Gilmore, unsettles the strongest of us.”", "길모어 씨, 근심이란 우리 가운데 가장 굳센 사람도 흔들어 놓는 법이니까요.”"]], [["She left me abruptly, her naturally firm voice faltering as she spoke those last words.", "그녀는 그 마지막 말을 하면서 본디 단단하던 목소리가 떨리는 채로 불쑥 나를 두고 가 버렸다."], ["A sensitive, vehement, passionate nature—a woman of ten thousand in these trivial, superficial times.", "예민하고 격렬하고 열정적인 천성, 이 하찮고 껍데기뿐인 시대에 만에 하나 있을까 말까 한 여인이다."], ["I had known her from her earliest years—I had seen her tested, as she grew up, in more than one trying family crisis, and my long experience made me attach an importance to her hesitation under the circumstances here detailed, which I should certainly not have felt in the case of another woman.", "나는 그녀를 어릴 적부터 알아 왔고, 그녀가 자라는 동안 한두 번이 아닌 집안의 시련 속에서 시험받는 것을 보아 왔다. 그 오랜 경험 때문에 나는 여기 적은 정황에서 보인 그녀의 망설임에, 다른 여자였다면 결코 느끼지 않았을 무게를 두게 되었다."], ["I could see no cause for any uneasiness or any doubt, but she had made me a little uneasy, and a little doubtful, nevertheless.", "불안해하거나 의심할 까닭이라고는 하나도 보이지 않았건만, 그럼에도 그녀는 나를 조금 불안하게, 조금 의심스럽게 만들어 놓았다."], ["In my youth, I should have chafed and fretted under the irritation of my own unreasonable state of mind.", "젊었을 적이라면 나는 스스로도 이치에 닿지 않는 이 마음 상태에 짜증을 내며 안달했으리라."], ["In my age, I knew better, and went out philosophically to walk it off.", "나이가 든 지금은 더 잘 알기에, 달관한 마음으로 밖에 나가 걸으며 그것을 털어 버렸다."]], [["We all met again at dinner-time.", "우리 일행은 저녁 식사 시간에 다시 모두 한자리에 모였다."]], [["Sir Percival was in such boisterous high spirits that I hardly recognised him as the same man whose quiet tact, refinement, and good sense had impressed me so strongly at the interview of the morning.", "퍼시벌 경은 어찌나 떠들썩하게 들떠 있었던지, 그날 아침 면담에서 조용한 재치와 세련됨과 분별력으로 내게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던 바로 그 사람이라고는 좀처럼 알아보기 어려울 지경이었다."], ["The only trace of his former self that I could detect reappeared, every now and then, in his manner towards Miss Fairlie.", "내가 알아챌 수 있었던 예전 모습의 유일한 흔적은 페얼리 양을 대하는 태도에서 이따금 되살아났다."], ["A look or a word from her suspended his loudest laugh, checked his gayest flow of talk, and rendered him all attention to her, and to no one else at table, in an instant.", "그녀의 눈짓 한 번, 말 한마디에 그의 가장 큰 웃음소리가 멎었고 가장 흥겨운 이야기의 흐름이 끊겼으며, 그는 순식간에 식탁의 다른 누구도 아닌 오직 그녀에게만 온 정신을 쏟았다."], ["Although he never openly tried to draw her into the conversation, he never lost the slightest chance she gave him of letting her drift into it by accident, and of saying the words to her, under those favourable circumstances, which a man with less tact and delicacy would have pointedly addressed to her the moment they occurred to him.", "그는 결코 대놓고 그녀를 대화에 끌어들이려 하지 않았으나, 그녀가 무심코 대화에 흘러들 만한 아주 작은 기회조차 놓치는 법이 없었고, 그렇게 마련된 유리한 상황에서 그녀에게 말을 건넸다. 재치와 섬세함이 덜한 남자였다면 생각나는 즉시 대놓고 그녀에게 늘어놓았을 그런 말들이었다."], ["Rather to my surprise, Miss Fairlie appeared to be sensible of his attentions without being moved by them.", "다소 뜻밖이었던 것은, 페얼리 양이 그의 배려를 알아차리면서도 그것에 마음이 움직이지는 않는 듯 보였다는 점이다."], ["She was a little confused from time to time when he looked at her, or spoke to her; but she never warmed towards him.", "그가 자신을 바라보거나 말을 걸 때면 그녀는 이따금 조금 당황했으나, 결코 그를 향해 마음이 따뜻해지지는 않았다."], ["Rank, fortune, good breeding, good looks, the respect of a gentleman, and the devotion of a lover were all humbly placed at her feet, and, so far as appearances went, were all offered in vain.", "지위와 재산, 훌륭한 가문 교육과 준수한 용모, 신사의 존중과 연인의 헌신이 모두 겸손하게 그녀의 발치에 놓였건만, 겉으로 드러난 바로는 그 모두가 헛되이 바쳐지고 있었다."]], [["On the next day, the Tuesday, Sir Percival went in the morning (taking one of the servants with him as a guide) to Todd’s Corner.", "이튿날, 곧 화요일 아침에 퍼시벌 경은 (하인 하나를 길잡이로 데리고) 토드 코너로 갔다."], ["His inquiries, as I afterwards heard, led to no results.", "뒤에 들은 바로는 그의 탐문은 아무런 성과도 얻지 못했다."], ["On his return he had an interview with Mr. Fairlie, and in the afternoon he and Miss Halcombe rode out together.", "돌아와서는 페얼리 씨와 면담했고, 오후에는 홀콤 양과 함께 말을 타고 나갔다."], ["Nothing else happened worthy of record.", "그 밖에 기록할 만한 일은 일어나지 않았다."], ["The evening passed as usual.", "저녁은 여느 때처럼 지나갔다."], ["There was no change in Sir Percival, and no change in Miss Fairlie.", "퍼시벌 경에게도 변화가 없었고, 페얼리 양에게도 변화가 없었다."]], [["The Wednesday’s post brought with it an event—the reply from Mrs. Catherick.", "수요일 우편이 하나의 사건을 실어 왔다 — 캐서릭 부인의 답장이었다."], ["I took a copy of the document, which I have preserved, and which I may as well present in this place.", "나는 그 문서를 베껴 두어 지금까지 보관해 왔으니, 이 자리에 그대로 옮겨 두는 편이 좋겠다."], ["It ran as follows—", "내용은 다음과 같았다 —"]], [["“MADAM,—I beg to acknowledge the receipt of your letter, inquiring whether my daughter, Anne, was placed under medical superintendence with my knowledge and approval, and whether the share taken in the matter by Sir Percival Glyde was such as to merit the expression of my gratitude towards that gentleman.", "“부인께. 제 딸 앤이 저의 인지와 승낙 아래 의사의 관리에 맡겨졌는지, 또 그 일에서 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 맡으신 몫이 그분께 감사의 뜻을 표할 만한 것이었는지를 문의하신 귀하의 서한을 받았음을 알려 드립니다."], ["Be pleased to accept my answer in the affirmative to both those questions, and believe me to remain, your obedient servant,", "그 두 가지 물음에 모두 그렇다는 저의 대답을 받아 주시기 바라오며, 귀하의 충실한 종으로 남겠습니다."]], [["“JANE ANNE CATHERICK.”", "“제인 앤 캐서릭.”"]], [["Short, sharp, and to the point; in form rather a business-like letter for a woman to write—in substance as plain a confirmation as could be desired of Sir Percival Glyde’s statement.", "짧고 날카롭고 요점만 담긴 글이었다. 형식으로 보자면 여자가 쓴 것치고는 제법 사무적인 편지였고, 내용으로 보자면 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 진술에 대해 바랄 수 있는 한 가장 명백한 확인이었다."], ["This was my opinion, and with certain minor reservations, Miss Halcombe’s opinion also.", "이것이 내 견해였고, 몇 가지 사소한 유보를 달기는 했으나 홀콤 양의 견해이기도 했다."], ["Sir Percival, when the letter was shown to him, did not appear to be struck by the sharp, short tone of it.", "퍼시벌 경은 편지를 보여 주었을 때 그 날카롭고 짤막한 어조에 별로 놀라는 기색이 없었다."], ["He told us that Mrs. Catherick was a woman of few words, a clear-headed, straightforward, unimaginative person, who wrote briefly and plainly, just as she spoke.", "그는 캐서릭 부인이 말수가 적은 여자이며, 머리가 맑고 솔직하며 상상력이라고는 없는 사람이어서, 말하는 그대로 짧고 평범하게 쓴다고 우리에게 일러 주었다."]], [["The next duty to be accomplished, now that the answer had been received, was to acquaint Miss Fairlie with Sir Percival’s explanation.", "답장을 받은 이상 다음으로 해치워야 할 일은 페얼리 양에게 퍼시벌 경의 해명을 알리는 것이었다."], ["Miss Halcombe had undertaken to do this, and had left the room to go to her sister, when she suddenly returned again, and sat down by the easy-chair in which I was reading the newspaper.", "홀콤 양이 그 일을 맡기로 하고 동생에게 가려고 방을 나섰는데, 갑자기 다시 돌아오더니 내가 신문을 읽고 있던 안락의자 옆에 앉았다."], ["Sir Percival had gone out a minute before to look at the stables, and no one was in the room but ourselves.", "퍼시벌 경은 마구간을 보러 조금 전에 나간 뒤였고, 방에는 우리 둘밖에 없었다."]], [["“I suppose we have really and truly done all we can?”", "“우리가 정말이지 할 수 있는 일은 다 한 것이겠지요?”"], ["she said, turning and twisting Mrs. Catherick’s letter in her hand.", "그녀가 캐서릭 부인의 편지를 손안에서 이리저리 돌리고 접으며 말했다."]], [["“If we are friends of Sir Percival’s, who know him and trust him, we have done all, and more than all, that is necessary,”", "“우리가 퍼시벌 경을 알고 믿는 그의 친구라면, 필요한 일은 다 했고 그 이상까지 했습니다.”"], ["I answered, a little annoyed by this return of her hesitation.", "나는 되살아난 그녀의 망설임에 조금 짜증이 나서 대답했다."], ["“But if we are enemies who suspect him——”", "“그러나 우리가 그를 의심하는 적이라면——”"]], [["“That alternative is not even to be thought of,”", "“그런 쪽은 생각조차 할 수 없습니다.”"], ["she interposed.", "그녀가 말을 가로챘다."], ["“We are Sir Percival’s friends, and if generosity and forbearance can add to our regard for him, we ought to be Sir Percival’s admirers as well.", "“우리는 퍼시벌 경의 친구예요. 그리고 너그러움과 참을성이 그분에 대한 우리의 존중을 더해 준다면, 우리는 퍼시벌 경을 흠모하는 사람이기까지 해야 마땅하겠지요."], ["You know that he saw Mr. Fairlie yesterday, and that he afterwards went out with me.”", "그분이 어제 페얼리 씨를 만났고, 그 뒤에 저와 함께 나갔다는 건 아시지요.”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그렇습니다."], ["I saw you riding away together.”", "두 분이 함께 말을 타고 떠나는 것을 보았지요.”"]], [["“We began the ride by talking about Anne Catherick, and about the singular manner in which Mr. Hartright met with her.", "“우리는 앤 캐서릭 이야기로, 그리고 하트라이트 씨가 그 여자와 마주친 기이한 정황 이야기로 승마를 시작했어요."], ["But we soon dropped that subject, and Sir Percival spoke next, in the most unselfish terms, of his engagement with Laura.", "하지만 곧 그 화제를 접었고, 다음으로 퍼시벌 경이 로라와의 약혼에 대해 더없이 사심 없는 말투로 이야기했지요."], ["He said he had observed that she was out of spirits, and he was willing, if not informed to the contrary, to attribute to that cause the alteration in her manner towards him during his present visit.", "그분은 로라가 기운이 없어 보인다는 것을 알아차렸고, 달리 듣는 바가 없다면 이번 방문 동안 자기를 대하는 태도가 달라진 것도 그 탓으로 돌릴 생각이라고 했어요."], ["If, however, there was any more serious reason for the change, he would entreat that no constraint might be placed on her inclinations either by Mr.", "그러나 그 변화에 더 심각한 이유가 있다면, 페얼리 씨든 저든 로라의 마음에 어떤 강요도 하지 말아 달라고 간청하겠다는 겁니다."], ["Fairlie or by me. All he asked, in that case, was that she would recall to mind, for the last time, what the circumstances were under which the engagement between them was made, and what his conduct had been from the beginning of the courtship to the present time.", "그런 경우에 그분이 바라는 것이라고는, 두 사람 사이의 약혼이 어떤 정황에서 맺어졌는지, 또 구애가 시작된 때부터 지금까지 자신의 처신이 어떠했는지를 로라가 마지막으로 한 번 되새겨 달라는 것뿐이었어요."], ["If, after due reflection on those two subjects, she seriously desired that he should withdraw his pretensions to the honour of becoming her husband—and if she would tell him so plainly with her own lips—he would sacrifice himself by leaving her perfectly free to withdraw from the engagement.”", "그 두 가지를 충분히 헤아려 본 뒤에도 로라가 자기 남편이 되는 영예에 대한 권리 주장을 거두어 달라고 진심으로 바란다면 — 그리고 그 말을 로라 자신의 입으로 분명히 해 준다면 — 자기를 희생해서 로라가 약혼에서 완전히 자유로이 물러날 수 있게 하겠다고 했어요.”"]], [["“No man could say more than that, Miss Halcombe.", "“그보다 더 말할 수 있는 남자는 없습니다, 홀콤 양."], ["As to my experience, few men in his situation would have said as much.”", "제 경험에 비추어 보건대 그런 처지에 놓인 남자 가운데 그만큼 말할 사람도 몇 되지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["She paused after I had spoken those words, and looked at me with a singular expression of perplexity and distress.", "내가 그 말을 마치자 그녀는 잠시 말이 없었고, 곤혹과 괴로움이 뒤섞인 묘한 표정으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“I accuse nobody, and I suspect nothing,”", "“저는 아무도 고발하지 않고, 아무것도 의심하지 않습니다.”"], ["she broke out abruptly.", "그녀가 불쑥 입을 열었다."], ["“But I cannot and will not accept the responsibility of persuading Laura to this marriage.”", "“하지만 로라를 이 혼인으로 설득하는 책임만은 질 수 없고, 지지도 않겠어요.”"]], [["“That is exactly the course which Sir Percival Glyde has himself requested you to take,”", "“그것이야말로 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 본인이 당신께 취해 달라고 청한 태도가 아닙니까.”"], ["I replied in astonishment.", "나는 놀라서 대답했다."], ["“He has begged you not to force her inclinations.”", "“그분은 동생분의 마음을 억지로 몰아가지 말아 달라고 부탁했지요.”"]], [["“And he indirectly obliges me to force them, if I give her his message.”", "“그런데 제가 그 전언을 로라에게 전하면, 그분은 저더러 에둘러 그 마음을 억지로 몰아가게 만드는 셈이에요.”"]], [["“How can that possibly be?”", "“어떻게 그럴 수가 있습니까?”"]], [["“Consult your own knowledge of Laura, Mr. Gilmore.", "“길모어 씨, 로라에 대해 아시는 바를 되짚어 보세요."], ["If I tell her to reflect on the circumstances of her engagement, I at once appeal to two of the strongest feelings in her nature—to her love for her father’s memory, and to her strict regard for truth.", "제가 로라에게 약혼의 정황을 되새겨 보라고 말한다면, 저는 그 아이 천성 가운데 가장 강한 두 가지 감정에 대뜸 호소하는 셈이 됩니다 — 아버지에 대한 추억을 향한 사랑과, 진실을 지키려는 엄격한 마음 말이에요."], ["You know that she never broke a promise in her life—you know that she entered on this engagement at the beginning of her father’s fatal illness, and that he spoke hopefully and happily of her marriage to Sir Percival Glyde on his deathbed.”", "그 아이가 평생 약속을 어긴 적이 한 번도 없다는 걸 아시잖아요. 아버지의 병이 치명적으로 접어들던 무렵에 이 약혼을 맺었다는 것도, 그 아버지가 임종의 자리에서 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과의 혼인을 희망에 차서 기쁘게 이야기했다는 것도 아시잖아요.”"]], [["I own that I was a little shocked at this view of the case.", "고백하건대 나는 사태를 그렇게 보는 시각에 조금 충격을 받았다."]], [["“Surely,”", "“설마,”"], ["I said, “you don’t mean to infer that when Sir Percival spoke to you yesterday he speculated on such a result as you have just mentioned?”", "내가 말했다. “퍼시벌 경이 어제 당신에게 그런 말을 할 때 방금 말씀하신 그런 결과를 노리고 계산했다는 뜻은 아니겠지요?”"]], [["Her frank, fearless face answered for her before she spoke.", "그녀의 솔직하고 두려움 없는 얼굴이 입을 열기도 전에 대답을 대신했다."]], [["“Do you think I would remain an instant in the company of any man whom I suspected of such baseness as that?”", "“제가 그만한 비열함을 의심하는 남자와 한순간이라도 같이 있을 사람으로 보이세요?”"], ["she asked angrily.", "그녀가 성난 목소리로 물었다."]], [["I liked to feel her hearty indignation flash out on me in that way.", "그런 식으로 그녀의 진심 어린 분노가 나를 향해 번뜩이는 것이 나는 좋았다."], ["We see so much malice and so little indignation in my profession.", "내 직업에서는 악의는 그토록 많이 보면서 분노는 그토록 적게 보는 법이다."]], [["“In that case,”", "“그렇다면,”"], ["I said, “excuse me if I tell you, in our legal phrase, that you are travelling out of the record.", "내가 말했다. “실례를 무릅쓰고 우리 법률가의 말투로 말씀드리자면, 당신은 지금 사건 기록 밖으로 나가 계십니다."], ["Whatever the consequences may be, Sir Percival has a right to expect that your sister should carefully consider her engagement from every reasonable point of view before she claims her release from it.", "결과가 어떻게 되든, 퍼시벌 경에게는 동생분이 약혼의 파기를 요구하기 전에 그 약혼을 온갖 합리적인 관점에서 신중히 헤아려 주기를 기대할 권리가 있습니다."], ["If that unlucky letter has prejudiced her against him, go at once, and tell her that he has cleared himself in your eyes and in mine.", "그 불운한 편지가 동생분에게 그분에 대한 편견을 심어 놓았다면, 당장 가서 그분이 당신 눈에도 내 눈에도 결백을 밝혔다고 일러 주십시오."], ["What objection can she urge against him after that?", "그러고 나서 동생분이 그분에게 무슨 이의를 제기할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["What excuse can she possibly have for changing her mind about a man whom she had virtually accepted for her husband more than two years ago?”", "이 년도 더 전에 사실상 남편으로 받아들인 남자에 대해 마음을 바꾼다면, 대체 무슨 변명이 있을 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["“In the eyes of law and reason, Mr. Gilmore, no excuse, I daresay.", "“법과 이치의 눈으로 보면, 길모어 씨, 아무 변명도 없겠지요."], ["If she still hesitates, and if I still hesitate, you must attribute our strange conduct, if you like, to caprice in both cases, and we must bear the imputation as well as we can.”", "그래도 로라가 여전히 망설이고 저 또한 여전히 망설인다면, 우리의 이 이상한 처신을 좋으실 대로 양쪽 다 변덕 탓으로 돌리셔도 좋습니다. 그런 비난이야 우리가 견딜 수 있는 만큼 견뎌야겠지요.”"]], [["With those words she suddenly rose and left me.", "그 말을 남기고 그녀는 갑자기 일어나 나를 두고 나갔다."], ["When a sensible woman has a serious question put to her, and evades it by a flippant answer, it is a sure sign, in ninety-nine cases out of a hundred, that she has something to conceal.", "분별 있는 여자가 진지한 질문을 받고 가벼운 대답으로 그것을 피해 갈 때는, 백에 아흔아홉은 무언가 감추고 있다는 확실한 표시다."], ["I returned to the perusal of the newspaper, strongly suspecting that Miss Halcombe and Miss Fairlie had a secret between them which they were keeping from Sir Percival, and keeping from me.", "나는 다시 신문을 읽기 시작하면서, 홀콤 양과 페얼리 양 사이에 퍼시벌 경에게도 나에게도 숨기고 있는 비밀이 있으리라고 강하게 의심했다."], ["I thought this hard on both of us, especially on Sir Percival.", "그것은 우리 두 사람 모두에게, 특히 퍼시벌 경에게 가혹한 일이라고 나는 생각했다."]], [["My doubts—or to speak more correctly, my convictions—were confirmed by Miss Halcombe’s language and manner when I saw her again later in the day.", "나의 의심은 — 더 정확히 말하자면 나의 확신은 — 그날 늦게 홀콤 양을 다시 만났을 때 그녀의 말투와 태도로 확인되었다."], ["She was suspiciously brief and reserved in telling me the result of her interview with her sister.", "그녀는 동생과의 면담 결과를 내게 전하면서 수상쩍을 만큼 짧고 조심스러웠다."], ["Miss Fairlie, it appeared, had listened quietly while the affair of the letter was placed before her in the right point of view, but when Miss Halcombe next proceeded to say that the object of Sir Percival’s visit at Limmeridge was to prevail on her to let a day be fixed for the marriage she checked all further reference to the subject by begging for time.", "들어 보니 페얼리 양은 편지에 얽힌 일을 올바른 관점에서 설명해 주는 동안 조용히 듣고 있었으나, 홀콤 양이 이어서 퍼시벌 경이 리머리지를 찾아온 목적은 혼례 날짜를 정하도록 설득하려는 것이라고 말하자, 시간을 달라고 애원하며 그 화제에 관한 이야기를 더는 못 하게 막아 버렸다는 것이다."], ["If Sir Percival would consent to spare her for the present, she would undertake to give him his final answer before the end of the year.", "퍼시벌 경이 당분간 자기를 놓아주는 데 동의해 준다면 올해가 가기 전에 최종 답을 드리겠노라고 했다."], ["She pleaded for this delay with such anxiety and agitation, that Miss Halcombe had promised to use her influence, if necessary, to obtain it, and there, at Miss Fairlie’s earnest entreaty, all further discussion of the marriage question had ended.", "그녀가 어찌나 불안해하고 동요하며 이 유예를 간청했던지, 홀콤 양은 필요하다면 자기 영향력을 써서 그것을 얻어 주겠다고 약속했고, 페얼리 양의 간곡한 애원에 따라 혼인 문제에 관한 논의는 거기서 모두 끝나고 말았다."]], [["The purely temporary arrangement thus proposed might have been convenient enough to the young lady, but it proved somewhat embarrassing to the writer of these lines.", "이렇게 제안된 순전히 일시적인 방편은 그 젊은 아가씨에게는 꽤 편했을지 몰라도, 이 글을 쓰는 사람에게는 다소 난감한 것으로 드러났다."], ["That morning’s post had brought a letter from my partner, which obliged me to return to town the next day by the afternoon train.", "그날 아침 우편이 동업자의 편지를 가져왔는데, 그 때문에 나는 이튿날 오후 기차로 시내에 돌아가지 않으면 안 되었다."], ["It was extremely probable that I should find no second opportunity of presenting myself at Limmeridge House during the remainder of the year.", "올해가 다 가도록 리머리지 하우스를 다시 찾아올 기회는 없을 공산이 아주 컸다."], ["In that case, supposing Miss Fairlie ultimately decided on holding to her engagement, my necessary personal communication with her, before I drew her settlement, would become something like a downright impossibility, and we should be obliged to commit to writing questions which ought always to be discussed on both sides by word of mouth.", "그렇게 되면, 설령 페얼리 양이 끝내 약혼을 지키기로 마음먹는다 해도, 재산 설정 증서를 작성하기 전에 반드시 거쳐야 할 본인과의 대면 협의가 사실상 불가능에 가까워지고, 마땅히 양쪽이 말로 주고받아야 할 문제들을 글로 적어 오갈 수밖에 없게 될 터였다."], ["I said nothing about this difficulty until Sir Percival had been consulted on the subject of the desired delay.", "나는 유예 요청에 관해 퍼시벌 경의 의향을 물을 때까지는 이 난처함에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He was too gallant a gentleman not to grant the request immediately.", "그는 그런 청을 즉시 들어주지 않을 만큼 무례한 신사가 아니었다."], ["When Miss Halcombe informed me of this I told her that I must absolutely speak to her sister before I left Limmeridge, and it was, therefore, arranged that I should see Miss Fairlie in her own sitting-room the next morning.", "홀콤 양이 그 사실을 알려 주자 나는 리머리지를 떠나기 전에 반드시 동생분과 이야기를 나누어야겠다고 말했고, 그리하여 이튿날 아침 페얼리 양의 거실에서 그녀를 만나기로 정해졌다."], ["She did not come down to dinner, or join us in the evening.", "그녀는 만찬에 내려오지도, 저녁에 우리와 어울리지도 않았다."], ["Indisposition was the excuse, and I thought Sir Percival looked, as well he might, a little annoyed when he heard of it.", "몸이 편치 않다는 것이 구실이었고, 그 말을 들었을 때 퍼시벌 경은 그럴 만도 하게 조금 언짢은 얼굴이었다고 나는 생각했다."]], [["The next morning, as soon as breakfast was over, I went up to Miss Fairlie’s sitting-room.", "이튿날 아침, 조반이 끝나자마자 나는 페얼리 양의 거실로 올라갔다."], ["The poor girl looked so pale and sad, and came forward to welcome me so readily and prettily, that the resolution to lecture her on her caprice and indecision, which I had been forming all the way upstairs, failed me on the spot.", "딱한 아가씨는 어찌나 창백하고 슬퍼 보이던지, 또 어찌나 선뜻 곱게 나를 맞으러 나오던지, 계단을 올라오는 내내 다듬어 온 결심 — 그 변덕과 우유부단함을 훈계하겠다는 결심 — 이 그 자리에서 허물어지고 말았다."], ["I led her back to the chair from which she had risen, and placed myself opposite to her.", "나는 그녀를 일어섰던 의자로 도로 데려가 앉히고 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Her cross-grained pet greyhound was in the room, and I fully expected a barking and snapping reception.", "성미 까다로운 애완 그레이하운드가 방 안에 있었으므로 나는 짖고 물어뜯는 환영을 단단히 각오했다."], ["Strange to say, the whimsical little brute falsified my expectations by jumping into my lap and poking its sharp muzzle familiarly into my hand the moment I sat down.", "그런데 이상하게도 그 변덕스러운 작은 짐승은 내가 앉자마자 무릎으로 뛰어올라 뾰족한 주둥이를 내 손에 스스럼없이 들이밀며 내 예상을 배반했다."]], [["“You used often to sit on my knee when you were a child, my dear,”", "“얘야, 너는 어릴 적에 곧잘 내 무릎에 앉곤 했지.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “and now your little dog seems determined to succeed you in the vacant throne.", "“그런데 이제는 네 작은 개가 그 빈 옥좌를 물려받기로 작정한 모양이구나."], ["Is that pretty drawing your doing?”", "저 예쁜 그림은 네가 그린 것이냐?”"]], [["I pointed to a little album which lay on the table by her side and which she had evidently been looking over when I came in.", "나는 그녀 옆 탁자에 놓인 작은 화첩을 가리켰다. 내가 들어왔을 때 그녀가 들여다보고 있던 것이 분명했다."], ["The page that lay open had a small water-colour landscape very neatly mounted on it.", "펼쳐진 쪽에는 작은 수채 풍경화가 아주 정갈하게 붙어 있었다."], ["This was the drawing which had suggested my question—an idle question enough—but how could I begin to talk of business to her the moment I opened my lips?", "내 질문을 이끌어 낸 것은 바로 그 그림이었다 — 참으로 부질없는 질문이었으나, 입을 떼자마자 어찌 그 아이에게 사무 이야기부터 꺼낼 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“No,”", "“아니에요.”"], ["she said, looking away from the drawing rather confusedly, “it is not my doing.”", "그녀는 그림에서 눈을 돌리며 다소 당황한 듯 말했다. “제가 그린 게 아니에요.”"]], [["Her fingers had a restless habit, which I remembered in her as a child, of always playing with the first thing that came to hand whenever any one was talking to her.", "그녀의 손가락에는 어릴 적부터 내가 기억하는 안절부절못하는 버릇이 있었다. 누군가 자기에게 말을 걸면 언제나 손에 잡히는 첫 물건을 만지작거리는 버릇이었다."], ["On this occasion they wandered to the album, and toyed absently about the margin of the little water-colour drawing.", "이번에는 그 손가락이 화첩으로 옮겨 가더니 작은 수채화의 가장자리를 무심히 어루만졌다."], ["The expression of melancholy deepened on her face.", "얼굴에는 우울한 빛이 짙어졌다."], ["She did not look at the drawing, or look at me.", "그녀는 그림을 보지도, 나를 보지도 않았다."], ["Her eyes moved uneasily from object to object in the room, betraying plainly that she suspected what my purpose was in coming to speak to her.", "눈길이 방 안의 이 물건 저 물건으로 불안하게 옮겨 다니며, 내가 이야기하러 온 목적이 무엇인지 짐작하고 있음을 훤히 드러냈다."], ["Seeing that, I thought it best to get to the purpose with as little delay as possible.", "그것을 보고 나는 되도록 지체 없이 본론으로 들어가는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."]], [["“One of the errands, my dear, which brings me here is to bid you good-bye,”", "“얘야, 내가 여기 온 볼일 가운데 하나는 너에게 작별을 고하는 것이란다.”"], ["I began.", "내가 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“I must get back to London to-day: and, before I leave, I want to have a word with you on the subject of your own affairs.”", "“오늘 런던으로 돌아가야 해. 그리고 떠나기 전에 네 신상 문제에 관해 한마디 나누고 싶구나.”"]], [["“I am very sorry you are going, Mr.", "“가신다니 정말 서운해요, 길모어 씨.”"], ["Gilmore,” she said, looking at me kindly.", "그녀가 다정하게 나를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“It is like the happy old times to have you here.”", "“여기 계시니 행복하던 옛날 같았는데요.”"]], [["“I hope I may be able to come back and recall those pleasant memories once more,”", "“다시 와서 그 즐거운 기억들을 한 번 더 떠올릴 수 있으면 좋으련만.”"], ["I continued; “but as there is some uncertainty about the future, I must take my opportunity when I can get it, and speak to you now.", "나는 말을 이었다. “하지만 앞일이 어찌 될지 알 수 없으니, 기회가 있을 때 붙잡아 지금 이야기해야겠다."], ["I am your old lawyer and your old friend, and I may remind you, I am sure, without offence, of the possibility of your marrying Sir Percival Glyde.”", "나는 네 오랜 변호사이자 오랜 벗이니, 네가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 혼인할 가능성에 대해 일깨워 준다 해도 언짢게 여기지 않으리라 믿는다.”"]], [["She took her hand off the little album as suddenly as if it had turned hot and burnt her.", "그녀는 작은 화첩이 별안간 뜨거워져 손을 데기라도 한 듯 급히 손을 떼었다."], ["Her fingers twined together nervously in her lap, her eyes looked down again at the floor, and an expression of constraint settled on her face which looked almost like an expression of pain.", "손가락들은 무릎 위에서 초조하게 얽혔고, 눈은 다시 바닥으로 떨어졌으며, 얼굴에는 거의 고통에 가까워 보이는 굳은 표정이 내려앉았다."]], [["“Is it absolutely necessary to speak of my marriage engagement?”", "“제 혼약 이야기를 꼭 해야만 하나요?”"], ["she asked in low tones.", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“It is necessary to refer to it,”", "“언급은 해야 하지만,” 내가 대답했다."], ["I answered, “but not to dwell on it.", "“거기 오래 머물 필요는 없다."], ["Let us merely say that you may marry, or that you may not marry.", "네가 혼인할 수도 있고 하지 않을 수도 있다고만 해 두자."], ["In the first case, I must be prepared, beforehand, to draw your settlement, and I ought not to do that without, as a matter of politeness, first consulting you.", "앞의 경우라면 나는 미리 네 재산 설정 증서를 작성할 채비를 해 두어야 하고, 예의상 먼저 네 뜻을 묻지 않고 그리해서는 안 되지."], ["This may be my only chance of hearing what your wishes are.", "네 바람이 무엇인지 들을 기회는 지금뿐일지도 모른다."], ["Let us, therefore, suppose the case of your marrying, and let me inform you, in as few words as possible, what your position is now, and what you may make it, if you please, in the future.”", "그러니 네가 혼인한다고 가정해 보고, 지금 네 처지가 어떠하며 네가 원한다면 앞으로 그것을 어떻게 만들 수 있는지를 되도록 짧게 일러 주마.”"]], [["I explained to her the object of a marriage-settlement, and then told her exactly what her prospects were—in the first place, on her coming of age, and in the second place, on the decease of her uncle—marking the distinction between the property in which she had a life-interest only, and the property which was left at her own control.", "나는 그녀에게 혼인 재산 설정의 목적을 설명한 뒤, 그녀의 재산 전망이 정확히 어떠한지를 일러 주었다 — 첫째로는 성년이 되었을 때, 둘째로는 삼촌이 세상을 떠났을 때를 나누어, 종신 수익권만 갖는 재산과 그녀 자신의 처분에 맡겨진 재산의 구분을 짚어 주었다."], ["She listened attentively, with the constrained expression still on her face, and her hands still nervously clasped together in her lap.", "그녀는 여전히 굳은 표정으로, 여전히 무릎 위에서 초조하게 두 손을 맞잡은 채 주의 깊게 들었다."]], [["“And now,”", "“자, 이제,”"], ["I said in conclusion, “tell me if you can think of any condition which, in the case we have supposed, you would wish me to make for you—subject, of course, to your guardian’s approval, as you are not yet of age.”", "나는 마무리 삼아 말했다. “우리가 가정한 경우에 네가 나더러 너를 위해 넣어 달라고 바라는 조건이 있는지 말해 보아라 — 물론 너는 아직 성년이 아니니 후견인의 승낙을 받아야 하겠지만.”"]], [["She moved uneasily in her chair, then looked in my face on a sudden very earnestly.", "그녀는 의자에서 불안하게 몸을 움직이더니, 갑자기 아주 간절한 눈으로 내 얼굴을 바라보았다."]], [["“If it does happen,”", "“정말로 그렇게 된다면,”"], ["she began faintly, “if I am——”", "그녀가 가냘프게 입을 열었다. “제가 만약——”"]], [["“If you are married,”", "“혼인하게 된다면 말이지.”"], ["I added, helping her out.", "내가 말을 거들어 주었다."]], [["“Don’t let him part me from Marian,”", "“그이가 저를 매리언에게서 떼어 놓지 못하게 해 주세요.”"], ["she cried, with a sudden outbreak of energy.", "그녀가 갑자기 기운을 내어 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, Mr. Gilmore, pray make it law that Marian is to live with me!”", "“아, 길모어 씨, 매리언이 저와 함께 살도록 법으로 정해 주세요!”"]], [["Under other circumstances I might, perhaps, have been amused at this essentially feminine interpretation of my question, and of the long explanation which had preceded it.", "다른 상황이었다면 내 질문과 그에 앞선 긴 설명을 이토록 지극히 여성다운 방식으로 알아들은 데 대해 어쩌면 웃음이 났을지도 모른다."], ["But her looks and tones, when she spoke, were of a kind to make me more than serious—they distressed me.", "그러나 그 말을 할 때의 표정과 목소리는 나를 그저 진지하게 만드는 정도를 넘어 — 나를 괴롭게 했다."], ["Her words, few as they were, betrayed a desperate clinging to the past which boded ill for the future.", "몇 마디 되지 않는 그 말에는 지나간 것에 대한 필사적인 매달림이 배어 있었고, 그것은 앞날에 불길한 조짐이었다."]], [["“Your having Marian Halcombe to live with you can easily be settled by private arrangement,” I said.", "“매리언 홀콤 양이 너와 함께 사는 문제라면 사사로운 약정으로 얼마든지 정할 수 있다.”"], ["“You hardly understood my question, I think.", "내가 말했다. “내 질문을 잘못 알아들은 모양이구나."], ["It referred to your own property—to the disposal of your money.", "그것은 네 재산에 관한 이야기였다 — 네 돈을 어떻게 처분하겠느냐는 것이지."], ["Supposing you were to make a will when you come of age, who would you like the money to go to?”", "네가 성년이 되어 유언장을 쓴다고 하면, 그 돈이 누구에게 가기를 바라겠느냐?”"]], [["“Marian has been mother and sister both to me,”", "“매리언은 제게 어머니이자 언니였어요.”"], ["said the good, affectionate girl, her pretty blue eyes glistening while she spoke.", "착하고 정 많은 아가씨가 말했다. 말하는 동안 예쁜 푸른 눈이 촉촉이 반짝였다."], ["“May I leave it to Marian, Mr. Gilmore?”", "“매리언에게 남겨도 될까요, 길모어 씨?”"]], [["“Certainly, my love,”", "“물론이지, 얘야.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“But remember what a large sum it is.", "“하지만 그것이 얼마나 큰 돈인지 잊지 말아라."], ["Would you like it all to go to Miss Halcombe?”", "전부 홀콤 양에게 가기를 바라느냐?”"]], [["She hesitated; her colour came and went, and her hand stole back again to the little album.", "그녀는 망설였다. 낯빛이 붉어졌다 옅어졌다 하더니, 손이 다시 슬그머니 작은 화첩으로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Not all of it,”", "“전부는 아니에요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“There is some one else besides Marian——”", "“매리언 말고도 또 한 사람이——”"]], [["She stopped; her colour heightened, and the fingers of the hand that rested upon the album beat gently on the margin of the drawing, as if her memory had set them going mechanically with the remembrance of a favourite tune.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 낯빛이 더 붉어졌고, 화첩 위에 놓인 손의 손가락들이 그림 가장자리를 가만가만 두드렸다. 마치 기억이 좋아하던 곡조를 떠올려 그 손가락들을 저절로 움직이게 하기라도 한 것처럼."]], [["“You mean some other member of the family besides Miss Halcombe?”", "“홀콤 양 말고 집안의 다른 사람을 말하는 것이냐?”"], ["I suggested, seeing her at a loss to proceed.", "그녀가 말을 잇지 못하는 것을 보고 내가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["The heightening colour spread to her forehead and her neck, and the nervous fingers suddenly clasped themselves fast round the edge of the book.", "짙어지던 낯빛이 이마와 목까지 번졌고, 초조한 손가락들이 갑자기 책 가장자리를 꽉 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“There is some one else,”", "“또 한 사람이 있어요.”"], ["she said, not noticing my last words, though she had evidently heard them; “there is some one else who might like a little keepsake if—if I might leave it.", "그녀는 내 마지막 말을 분명히 들었으면서도 못 들은 척하며 말했다. “작은 기념품이라도 받으면 기뻐할 사람이 또 있어요 — 제가, 제가 남길 수만 있다면요."], ["There would be no harm if I should die first——”", "제가 먼저 죽는다면 해로울 것도 없잖아요——”"]], [["She paused again.", "그녀는 다시 말을 멈추었다."], ["The colour that had spread over her cheeks suddenly, as suddenly left them.", "두 뺨에 번졌던 붉은빛이 갑자기 밀려왔던 것만큼이나 갑자기 빠져나갔다."], ["The hand on the album resigned its hold, trembled a little, and moved the book away from her.", "화첩 위의 손이 힘을 풀고 조금 떨리더니 책을 저만치 밀어 놓았다."], ["She looked at me for an instant—then turned her head aside in the chair.", "그녀는 한순간 나를 바라보다가 의자에 앉은 채 고개를 옆으로 돌렸다."], ["Her handkerchief fell to the floor as she changed her position, and she hurriedly hid her face from me in her hands.", "자세를 바꾸는 사이 손수건이 바닥에 떨어졌고, 그녀는 황급히 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻어 내게서 감추었다."]], [["Sad!", "슬픈 일이었다!"], ["To remember her, as I did, the liveliest, happiest child that ever laughed the day through, and to see her now, in the flower of her age and her beauty, so broken and so brought down as this!", "내 기억 속의 그 아이는 하루 종일 웃으며 지내던 더없이 발랄하고 행복한 아이였는데, 이제 나이와 아름다움이 한창 꽃필 때에 이토록 무너지고 이토록 짓눌린 모습을 보게 되다니!"]], [["In the distress that she caused me I forgot the years that had passed, and the change they had made in our position towards one another.", "그 아이가 내게 안긴 괴로움 속에서 나는 흘러간 세월도, 그 세월이 서로의 처지에 만들어 놓은 변화도 잊어버렸다."], ["I moved my chair close to her, and picked up her handkerchief from the carpet, and drew her hands from her face gently.", "나는 의자를 그 아이 가까이 당기고 양탄자에서 손수건을 주워 들었으며, 얼굴을 가린 두 손을 부드럽게 떼어 주었다."], ["“Don’t cry, my love,”", "“울지 말아라, 얘야.”"], ["I said, and dried the tears that were gathering in her eyes with my own hand, as if she had been the little Laura Fairlie of ten long years ago.", "나는 그렇게 말하고, 마치 그 아이가 열 해 전의 그 어린 로라 페얼리이기라도 한 듯 눈에 고이는 눈물을 내 손으로 닦아 주었다."]], [["It was the best way I could have taken to compose her.", "그것이 그 아이를 진정시키는 데 내가 택할 수 있었던 가장 좋은 방법이었다."], ["She laid her head on my shoulder, and smiled faintly through her tears.", "그 아이는 내 어깨에 머리를 기대고 눈물 사이로 희미하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“I am very sorry for forgetting myself,”", "“제가 주책없이 굴어서 정말 죄송해요.”"], ["she said artlessly.", "그녀가 꾸밈없이 말했다."], ["“I have not been well—I have felt sadly weak and nervous lately, and I often cry without reason when I am alone.", "“요즘 몸이 좋지 않았어요 — 요사이 몹시 기운이 없고 신경이 예민해져서, 혼자 있으면 이유도 없이 자주 울곤 해요."], ["I am better now—I can answer you as I ought, Mr. Gilmore, I can indeed.”", "이제 괜찮아요 — 길모어 씨, 이제 제대로 대답할 수 있어요, 정말이에요.”"]], [["“No, no, my dear,”", "“아니다, 아니야, 얘야.”"], ["I replied, “we will consider the subject as done with for the present.", "내가 대답했다. “그 문제는 당분간 끝난 것으로 하자꾸나."], ["You have said enough to sanction my taking the best possible care of your interests, and we can settle details at another opportunity.", "네 이익을 최선을 다해 지켜도 좋다는 뜻은 충분히 말해 주었으니, 세세한 것은 다음 기회에 정하면 된다."], ["Let us have done with business now, and talk of something else.”", "이제 사무 이야기는 그만하고 다른 이야기를 하자.”"]], [["I led her at once into speaking on other topics.", "나는 곧바로 그 아이를 다른 화제로 이끌었다."], ["In ten minutes’ time she was in better spirits, and I rose to take my leave.", "십 분쯤 지나자 기분이 나아졌고, 나는 작별하려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Come here again,”", "“또 오세요.”"], ["she said earnestly.", "그녀가 간절하게 말했다."], ["“I will try to be worthier of your kind feeling for me and for my interests if you will only come again.”", "“다시 와 주시기만 하면, 저를 위하고 제 이익을 위해 베풀어 주시는 그 마음에 더 어울리는 사람이 되도록 애써 볼게요.”"]], [["Still clinging to the past—that past which I represented to her, in my way, as Miss Halcombe did in hers!", "여전히 지나간 것에 매달리고 있었다 — 홀콤 양이 그 나름의 방식으로 그러하듯, 나 또한 내 나름의 방식으로 그 아이에게 표상하는 그 지나간 시절에!"], ["It troubled me sorely to see her looking back, at the beginning of her career, just as I look back at the end of mine.", "인생의 초입에 선 그 아이가 뒤를 돌아보는 모습, 바로 인생의 끝자락에 선 내가 뒤를 돌아보듯 하는 그 모습이 나를 몹시 괴롭게 했다."]], [["“If I do come again, I hope I shall find you better,”", "“내가 다시 온다면, 그때는 네가 더 나아져 있기를 바란다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said; “better and happier.", "“더 건강하고 더 행복해져 있기를."], ["God bless you, my dear!”", "하느님의 축복이 있기를, 얘야!”"]], [["She only answered by putting up her cheek to me to be kissed.", "그 아이는 입 맞춰 달라고 뺨을 내미는 것으로만 대답했다."], ["Even lawyers have hearts, and mine ached a little as I took leave of her.", "변호사에게도 마음이란 것이 있어서, 그 아이와 작별하는 동안 내 마음도 조금은 아렸다."]], [["The whole interview between us had hardly lasted more than half an hour—she had not breathed a word, in my presence, to explain the mystery of her evident distress and dismay at the prospect of her marriage, and yet she had contrived to win me over to her side of the question, I neither knew how nor why.", "우리 둘 사이의 면담은 통틀어 반 시간을 겨우 넘겼을 뿐이었다. 그 아이는 내 앞에서 혼인을 앞두고 드러낸 그 뚜렷한 괴로움과 두려움의 수수께끼를 설명할 말은 한마디도 흘리지 않았건만, 그러면서도 어떻게 어째서인지 나로서는 알 수 없는 채로 나를 제 편으로 끌어들이고 말았다."], ["I had entered the room, feeling that Sir Percival Glyde had fair reason to complain of the manner in which she was treating him.", "나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 아이의 처사에 대해 불평할 만한 정당한 이유가 있다고 여기며 그 방에 들어갔다."], ["I left it, secretly hoping that matters might end in her taking him at his word and claiming her release.", "그리고 일이 그 아이가 그의 말을 그대로 받아들여 약혼의 파기를 요구하는 쪽으로 끝나기를 남몰래 바라며 그 방을 나왔다."], ["A man of my age and experience ought to have known better than to vacillate in this unreasonable manner.", "내 나이와 경험을 지닌 사람이라면 이토록 사리에 닿지 않게 흔들려서는 안 되는 법이었다."], ["I can make no excuse for myself; I can only tell the truth, and say—so it was.", "나 자신을 위해 아무 변명도 할 수 없다. 다만 사실대로 말할 수 있을 뿐이다 — 그러하였노라고."]], [["The hour for my departure was now drawing near.", "떠날 시각이 다가오고 있었다."], ["I sent to Mr. Fairlie to say that I would wait on him to take leave if he liked, but that he must excuse my being rather in a hurry.", "나는 페얼리 씨에게 사람을 보내, 원하신다면 작별 인사를 하러 들르겠으나 다소 서두르는 것은 양해해 달라고 전했다."], ["He sent a message back, written in pencil on a slip of paper: “Kind love and best wishes, dear Gilmore.", "그는 종잇조각에 연필로 적은 답신을 보내왔다. “다정한 마음과 온갖 행운을 비오, 길모어."], ["Hurry of any kind is inexpressibly injurious to me.", "어떤 종류든 서두름이란 내게는 이루 말할 수 없이 해롭다오."], ["Pray take care of yourself.", "부디 몸조심하시오."], ["Good-bye.”", "안녕히.”"]], [["Just before I left I saw Miss Halcombe for a moment alone.", "떠나기 직전에 나는 홀콤 양을 잠시 단둘이 만났다."]], [["“Have you said all you wanted to Laura?”", "“로라에게 하시려던 말씀은 다 하셨나요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“She is very weak and nervous—I am glad she has you to take care of her.”", "“동생분은 몹시 쇠약하고 신경이 곤두서 있더군요 — 돌봐 줄 당신이 곁에 있어 다행입니다.”"]], [["Miss Halcombe’s sharp eyes studied my face attentively.", "홀콤 양의 날카로운 눈이 내 얼굴을 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“You are altering your opinion about Laura,” she said.", "“로라에 대한 생각이 달라지고 계시네요.”"], ["“You are readier to make allowances for her than you were yesterday.”", "그녀가 말했다. “어제보다 그 아이를 너그럽게 헤아리려 하시는군요.”"]], [["No sensible man ever engages, unprepared, in a fencing match of words with a woman.", "분별 있는 남자라면 아무 채비 없이 여자와 말의 검술 시합에 나서는 법이 없다."], ["I only answered—", "나는 이렇게만 대답했다 —"]], [["“Let me know what happens.", "“무슨 일이 생기는지 알려 주십시오."], ["I will do nothing till I hear from you.”", "당신에게서 소식을 들을 때까지 나는 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["She still looked hard in my face.", "그녀는 여전히 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["“I wish it was all over, and well over, Mr. Gilmore—and so do you.”", "“이 일이 다 끝났으면, 그것도 잘 끝났으면 좋겠어요, 길모어 씨 — 당신도 그러시겠지요.”"], ["With those words she left me.", "그 말을 남기고 그녀는 나를 두고 갔다."]], [["Sir Percival most politely insisted on seeing me to the carriage door.", "퍼시벌 경은 더없이 정중하게 마차 문까지 나를 배웅하겠다고 우겼다."]], [["“If you are ever in my neighbourhood,”", "“혹 제 근방에 오시는 일이 있거든,”"], ["he said, “pray don’t forget that I am sincerely anxious to improve our acquaintance.", "그가 말했다. “제가 우리 사이를 더 두터이 하고 싶어 진심으로 애태우고 있다는 것을 부디 잊지 마십시오."], ["The tried and trusted old friend of this family will be always a welcome visitor in any house of mine.”", "이 집안의 오래 검증된 믿음직한 벗이라면 제 어느 집에서든 언제나 반가운 손님일 것입니다.”"]], [["A really irresistible man—courteous, considerate, delightfully free from pride—a gentleman, every inch of him.", "참으로 거부하기 어려운 사람이었다 — 예의 바르고 사려 깊으며 오만이라고는 반갑게도 찾아볼 수 없는, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 신사인 사람."], ["As I drove away to the station I felt as if I could cheerfully do anything to promote the interests of Sir Percival Glyde—anything in the world, except drawing the marriage settlement of his wife.", "역으로 마차를 달리는 동안 나는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 이익을 위해서라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 줄 수 있을 것 같았다 — 세상의 그 무엇이라도, 다만 그의 아내가 될 사람의 혼인 재산 설정 증서를 작성하는 일만은 빼고."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["A week passed, after my return to London, without the receipt of any communication from Miss Halcombe.", "런던으로 돌아온 뒤 일주일이 지나도록 홀콤 양에게서는 아무런 소식도 오지 않았다."]], [["On the eighth day a letter in her handwriting was placed among the other letters on my table.", "여드레째 되는 날, 그녀의 필체로 쓰인 편지 한 통이 내 책상 위 다른 편지들 사이에 놓여 있었다."]], [["It announced that Sir Percival Glyde had been definitely accepted, and that the marriage was to take place, as he had originally desired, before the end of the year.", "편지는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 확실히 받아들여졌다는 것, 그리고 혼례가 그가 애초에 바란 대로 연내에 치러지리라는 것을 알리고 있었다."], ["In all probability the ceremony would be performed during the last fortnight in December.", "십중팔구 예식은 십이월 마지막 보름 안에 거행될 터였다."], ["Miss Fairlie’s twenty-first birthday was late in March.", "페얼리 양의 스물한 번째 생일은 삼월 하순이었다."], ["She would, therefore, by this arrangement, become Sir Percival’s wife about three months before she was of age.", "그러므로 이 일정대로라면 그녀는 성년이 되기 석 달쯤 전에 퍼시벌 경의 아내가 되는 셈이었다."]], [["I ought not to have been surprised, I ought not to have been sorry, but I was surprised and sorry, nevertheless.", "놀랄 일도 아니었고 서글퍼할 일도 아니었건만, 그럼에도 나는 놀랐고 서글펐다."], ["Some little disappointment, caused by the unsatisfactory shortness of Miss Halcombe’s letter, mingled itself with these feelings, and contributed its share towards upsetting my serenity for the day.", "홀콤 양의 편지가 만족스럽지 못할 만큼 짧았던 데서 오는 자잘한 실망감까지 이 감정에 섞여 들어, 그날 하루 내 평온을 흐트러뜨리는 데 한몫을 했다."], ["In six lines my correspondent announced the proposed marriage—in three more, she told me that Sir Percival had left Cumberland to return to his house in Hampshire, and in two concluding sentences she informed me, first, that Laura was sadly in want of change and cheerful society; secondly, that she had resolved to try the effect of some such change forthwith, by taking her sister away with her on a visit to certain old friends in Yorkshire.", "내 서신 상대는 여섯 줄로 예정된 혼사를 알렸고, 다시 세 줄로 퍼시벌 경이 컴벌랜드를 떠나 햄프셔의 자기 저택으로 돌아갔음을 전했으며, 마지막 두 문장으로 첫째, 로라에게 변화와 유쾌한 교제가 몹시 필요하다는 것, 둘째, 그래서 자신이 그런 변화를 곧바로 시험해 보기로 마음먹고 동생을 데리고 요크셔의 오랜 친구들을 방문하러 떠나겠다는 것을 알려 왔다. 편지는 거기서 끝났다."], ["There the letter ended, without a word to explain what the circumstances were which had decided Miss Fairlie to accept Sir Percival Glyde in one short week from the time when I had last seen her.", "내가 페얼리 양을 마지막으로 본 때로부터 단 일주일 만에 그녀가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 받아들이기로 결심하게 된 정황이 무엇이었는지, 그것을 설명하는 말은 한 마디도 없었다."]], [["At a later period the cause of this sudden determination was fully explained to me.", "뒷날 이 갑작스러운 결심의 까닭은 내게 낱낱이 설명되었다."], ["It is not my business to relate it imperfectly, on hearsay evidence.", "그러나 전해 들은 이야기에 기대어 그것을 어설프게 늘어놓는 것은 내 소임이 아니다."], ["The circumstances came within the personal experience of Miss Halcombe, and when her narrative succeeds mine, she will describe them in every particular exactly as they happened.", "그 정황은 홀콤 양이 몸소 겪은 일이며, 내 기록에 이어 그녀의 기록이 나올 때 그녀가 일어난 그대로 하나하나 서술할 것이다."], ["In the meantime, the plain duty for me to perform—before I, in my turn, lay down my pen and withdraw from the story—is to relate the one remaining event connected with Miss Fairlie’s proposed marriage in which I was concerned, namely, the drawing of the settlement.", "그동안 내가 해야 할 분명한 의무는 — 내 차례를 마치고 펜을 내려놓아 이 이야기에서 물러나기 전에 — 페얼리 양의 혼사와 얽힌 일 가운데 내가 관여한 마지막 사건 하나, 곧 혼인 재산 계약서를 작성한 경위를 적어 두는 일이다."]], [["It is impossible to refer intelligibly to this document without first entering into certain particulars in relation to the bride’s pecuniary affairs.", "이 문서를 알아들을 수 있게 이야기하려면 먼저 신부의 재산 관계에 관한 몇 가지 사항을 짚지 않을 수 없다."], ["I will try to make my explanation briefly and plainly, and to keep it free from professional obscurities and technicalities.", "나는 되도록 짧고 평이하게, 법조계의 모호한 표현과 전문 용어를 걷어내고 설명해 보겠다. 이 문제는 지극히 중요하다."], ["The matter is of the utmost importance.", "이 글을 읽는 모든 분께 미리 일러 둔다."], ["I warn all readers of these lines that Miss Fairlie’s inheritance is a very serious part of Miss Fairlie’s story, and that Mr. Gilmore’s experience, in this particular, must be their experience also, if they wish to understand the narratives which are yet to come.", "페얼리 양의 상속 재산은 페얼리 양 이야기에서 매우 중대한 부분이며, 앞으로 이어질 기록들을 이해하고자 한다면 이 대목에 관한 한 길모어의 경험이 곧 독자의 경험이 되어야 한다."]], [["Miss Fairlie’s expectations, then, were of a twofold kind, comprising her possible inheritance of real property, or land, when her uncle died, and her absolute inheritance of personal property, or money, when she came of age.", "페얼리 양이 기대할 수 있는 재산은 두 가지였다. 하나는 삼촌이 세상을 떠날 때 물려받게 될지도 모르는 부동산, 곧 토지였고, 다른 하나는 성년이 되는 순간 온전히 자기 것이 되는 동산, 곧 돈이었다."]], [["Let us take the land first.", "먼저 토지부터 살펴보자."]], [["In the time of Miss Fairlie’s paternal grandfather (whom we will call Mr. Fairlie, the elder) the entailed succession to the Limmeridge estate stood thus—", "페얼리 양의 친조부(편의상 노(老) 페얼리 씨라 부르기로 하자) 시절에 리머리지 영지의 한사(限嗣) 상속 순위는 이러했다 —"]], [["Mr. Fairlie, the elder, died and left three sons, Philip, Frederick, and Arthur.", "노 페얼리 씨는 세상을 떠나면서 필립, 프레더릭, 아서 세 아들을 남겼다."], ["As eldest son, Philip succeeded to the estate, if he died without leaving a son, the property went to the second brother, Frederick; and if Frederick died also without leaving a son, the property went to the third brother, Arthur.", "맏이인 필립이 영지를 이어받되, 그가 아들 없이 죽으면 재산은 둘째 아우 프레더릭에게 넘어가고, 프레더릭 또한 아들 없이 죽으면 재산은 셋째 아우 아서에게 넘어가도록 되어 있었다."]], [["As events turned out, Mr. Philip Fairlie died leaving an only daughter, the Laura of this story, and the estate, in consequence, went, in course of law, to the second brother, Frederick, a single man.", "실제로 일이 흘러간 바를 보면, 필립 페얼리 씨는 외동딸 하나만 남기고 죽었으니 그 딸이 이 이야기의 로라이며, 그리하여 영지는 법의 절차에 따라 둘째 아우인 독신자 프레더릭에게 넘어갔다."], ["The third brother, Arthur, had died many years before the decease of Philip, leaving a son and a daughter.", "셋째 아우 아서는 필립이 죽기 여러 해 전에 이미 세상을 떠나면서 아들 하나와 딸 하나를 남겼다."], ["The son, at the age of eighteen, was drowned at Oxford.", "그 아들은 열여덟 살에 옥스퍼드에서 물에 빠져 죽었다."], ["His death left Laura, the daughter of Mr. Philip Fairlie, presumptive heiress to the estate, with every chance of succeeding to it, in the ordinary course of nature, on her uncle Frederick’s death, if the said Frederick died without leaving male issue.", "그의 죽음으로 필립 페얼리 씨의 딸 로라는 영지의 추정 상속인이 되었고, 삼촌 프레더릭이 남자 후사를 남기지 않고 죽는다면 자연의 순리에 따라 그 뒤를 이을 가망이 충분했다."]], [["Except in the event, then, of Mr. Frederick Fairlie’s marrying and leaving an heir (the two very last things in the world that he was likely to do), his niece, Laura, would have the property on his death, possessing, it must be remembered, nothing more than a life-interest in it.", "그러므로 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 혼인하여 상속인을 남기는 일이 벌어지지 않는 한 — 이 세상에서 그가 할 법하지 않은 일을 꼽자면 바로 그 두 가지였다 — 조카 로라는 그가 죽을 때 재산을 갖게 될 터였다."], ["If she died single, or died childless, the estate would revert to her cousin, Magdalen, the daughter of Mr.", "다만 그녀가 갖는 것은 종신 수익권뿐임을 잊어서는 안 된다. 그녀가 혼인하지 않은 채 죽거나 자식 없이 죽으면 영지는 사촌 매그덜런, 곧 아서 페얼리 씨의 딸에게 되돌아간다."], ["Arthur Fairlie. If she married, with a proper settlement—or, in other words, with the settlement I meant to make for her—the income from the estate (a good three thousand a year) would, during her lifetime, be at her own disposal.", "반대로 그녀가 온당한 혼인 재산 계약을 맺고 — 다시 말해 내가 그녀를 위해 맺어 주려는 그 계약을 맺고 — 혼인한다면, 영지에서 나오는 수입(넉넉히 연 삼천 파운드)은 그녀가 살아 있는 동안 그녀 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 된다."], ["If she died before her husband, he would naturally expect to be left in the enjoyment of the income, for HIS lifetime.", "그녀가 남편보다 먼저 죽으면 남편은 당연히 자기 생전에 그 수입을 계속 누리게 되기를 기대할 것이다."], ["If she had a son, that son would be the heir, to the exclusion of her cousin Magdalen.", "아들을 두면 그 아들이 상속인이 되어 사촌 매그덜런은 배제된다."], ["Thus, Sir Percival’s prospects in marrying Miss Fairlie (so far as his wife’s expectations from real property were concerned) promised him these two advantages, on Mr.", "그러니 퍼시벌 경이 페얼리 양과 혼인함으로써 얻을 전망은 (아내가 부동산에서 기대할 몫에 관한 한) 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 죽는 날 그에게 두 가지 이익을 약속하고 있었다."], ["Frederick Fairlie’s death: First, the use of three thousand a year (by his wife’s permission, while she lived, and in his own right, on her death, if he survived her); and, secondly, the inheritance of Limmeridge for his son, if he had one.", "첫째는 연 삼천 파운드를 쓰는 것(아내가 살아 있는 동안은 아내의 허락으로, 아내가 죽은 뒤에는 그가 아내보다 오래 산다면 자기 권리로), 둘째는 아들이 있다면 그 아들에게 돌아갈 리머리지의 상속이었다."]], [["So much for the landed property, and for the disposal of the income from it, on the occasion of Miss Fairlie’s marriage.", "부동산에 관해서는, 또 페얼리 양의 혼인에 즈음한 그 수입의 처분에 관해서는 이쯤 해 두자."], ["Thus far, no difficulty or difference of opinion on the lady’s settlement was at all likely to arise between Sir Percival’s lawyer and myself.", "여기까지는 신부 쪽 혼인 재산 계약을 놓고 퍼시벌 경의 변호사와 나 사이에 어떤 난관이나 견해차가 생길 여지가 거의 없었다."]], [["The personal estate, or, in other words, the money to which Miss Fairlie would become entitled on reaching the age of twenty-one years, is the next point to consider.", "다음으로 살펴볼 것은 동산, 다시 말해 페얼리 양이 스물한 살이 되는 순간 권리를 갖게 되는 돈이다."]], [["This part of her inheritance was, in itself, a comfortable little fortune.", "상속 재산의 이 부분은 그 자체로 넉넉한 자산이었다."], ["It was derived under her father’s will, and it amounted to the sum of twenty thousand pounds.", "아버지의 유언에 따라 나온 것으로 액수는 이만 파운드였다."], ["Besides this, she had a life-interest in ten thousand pounds more, which latter amount was to go, on her decease, to her aunt Eleanor, her father’s only sister.", "이 밖에 그녀는 만 파운드에 대한 종신 수익권도 가지고 있었는데, 그 만 파운드는 그녀가 죽으면 아버지의 하나뿐인 누이인 고모 엘리너에게 돌아가게 되어 있었다."], ["It will greatly assist in setting the family affairs before the reader in the clearest possible light, if I stop here for a moment, to explain why the aunt had been kept waiting for her legacy until the death of the niece.", "어째서 고모가 조카딸이 죽을 때까지 유산을 기다려야 하는 처지가 되었는지 여기서 잠시 멈추어 설명해 둔다면, 이 집안 사정을 독자 앞에 되도록 또렷하게 펼쳐 보이는 데 크게 도움이 될 것이다."]], [["Mr. Philip Fairlie had lived on excellent terms with his sister Eleanor, as long as she remained a single woman.", "필립 페얼리 씨는 누이 엘리너가 처녀로 있는 동안에는 더없이 사이좋게 지냈다."], ["But when her marriage took place, somewhat late in life, and when that marriage united her to an Italian gentleman named Fosco, or, rather, to an Italian nobleman—seeing that he rejoiced in the title of Count—Mr. Fairlie disapproved of her conduct so strongly that he ceased to hold any communication with her, and even went the length of striking her name out of his will.", "그러나 그 누이가 꽤 늦은 나이에 혼인을 하게 되었고, 그 혼인이 그녀를 포스코라는 이름의 이탈리아 신사와, 아니 정확히는 이탈리아 귀족과 — 그는 백작이라는 작위를 자랑스레 지니고 있었으므로 — 맺어 주자, 페얼리 씨는 누이의 처사를 어찌나 못마땅히 여겼던지 그녀와 왕래를 끊어 버렸고, 심지어 유언장에서 그 이름을 지워 버리기까지 했다."], ["The other members of the family all thought this serious manifestation of resentment at his sister’s marriage more or less unreasonable.", "집안의 다른 이들은 누이의 혼인에 대한 이 심각한 노여움의 표시를 다들 어느 정도 지나치다고 여겼다."], ["Count Fosco, though not a rich man, was not a penniless adventurer either.", "포스코 백작은 부유한 사람은 아니었으나 그렇다고 무일푼의 협잡꾼도 아니었다."], ["He had a small but sufficient income of his own.", "많지는 않아도 그럭저럭 살 만한 수입이 그에게는 있었다."], ["He had lived many years in England, and he held an excellent position in society.", "영국에서 여러 해를 살았고 사교계에서 훌륭한 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["These recommendations, however, availed nothing with Mr. Fairlie.", "그러나 이런 장점들도 페얼리 씨에게는 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["In many of his opinions he was an Englishman of the old school, and he hated a foreigner simply and solely because he was a foreigner.", "여러 견해에서 그는 구식 영국인이었고, 외국인이라는 단 하나의 이유만으로 외국인을 미워했다."], ["The utmost that he could be prevailed on to do, in after years—mainly at Miss Fairlie’s intercession—was to restore his sister’s name to its former place in his will, but to keep her waiting for her legacy by giving the income of the money to his daughter for life, and the money itself, if her aunt died before her, to her cousin Magdalen.", "뒷날 그가 겨우 응한 것은 — 주로 페얼리 양이 간청한 덕분이었는데 — 누이의 이름을 유언장의 본디 자리에 되돌려 놓되, 그 돈의 수입은 딸에게 종신으로 주고 원금 자체는 고모가 딸보다 먼저 죽을 경우 사촌 매그덜런에게 가도록 함으로써 누이를 계속 기다리게 만드는 것이었다. 두 여인의 나이 차를 생각하면 자연의 순리대로 고모가 만 파운드를 손에 넣을 가망은 이로써 극히 희박해졌다."], ["Considering the relative ages of the two ladies, the aunt’s chance, in the ordinary course of nature, of receiving the ten thousand pounds, was thus rendered doubtful in the extreme; and Madame Fosco resented her brother’s treatment of her as unjustly as usual in such cases, by refusing to see her niece, and declining to believe that Miss Fairlie’s intercession had ever been exerted to restore her name to Mr. Fairlie’s will.", "그리고 포스코 백작부인은 이런 경우에 흔히 그러하듯 오라비의 처사를 부당하게 원망하여, 조카딸 만나기를 거부하고 페얼리 씨의 유언장에 자기 이름이 되돌아온 것이 페얼리 양의 간청 덕분이었다는 사실도 믿으려 하지 않았다."]], [["Such was the history of the ten thousand pounds.", "만 파운드의 내력은 이러했다."], ["Here again no difficulty could arise with Sir Percival’s legal adviser.", "여기서도 퍼시벌 경의 법률 고문과 어려움이 생길 일은 없었다."], ["The income would be at the wife’s disposal, and the principal would go to her aunt or her cousin on her death.", "수입은 아내가 뜻대로 쓰고, 원금은 그녀가 죽으면 고모나 사촌에게 돌아갈 터였다."]], [["All preliminary explanations being now cleared out of the way, I come at last to the real knot of the case—to the twenty thousand pounds.", "예비 설명이 이제 모두 걷혔으니, 마침내 이 사건의 진짜 매듭에 이른다 — 곧 이만 파운드다."]], [["This sum was absolutely Miss Fairlie’s own on her completing her twenty-first year, and the whole future disposition of it depended, in the first instance, on the conditions I could obtain for her in her marriage-settlement.", "이 금액은 그녀가 스물한 살을 채우는 순간 온전히 페얼리 양 자신의 것이 되며, 그것이 앞으로 어떻게 처분되느냐는 우선 내가 혼인 재산 계약서에서 그녀를 위해 얻어 낼 수 있는 조건에 달려 있었다."], ["The other clauses contained in that document were of a formal kind, and need not be recited here.", "그 문서에 담긴 다른 조항들은 형식적인 것이어서 여기 옮길 것도 없다."], ["But the clause relating to the money is too important to be passed over.", "그러나 그 돈에 관한 조항은 그냥 지나치기에는 너무나 중요하다."], ["A few lines will be sufficient to give the necessary abstract of it.", "몇 줄이면 필요한 요지를 밝히기에 충분할 것이다."]], [["My stipulation in regard to the twenty thousand pounds was simply this: The whole amount was to be settled so as to give the income to the lady for her life—afterwards to Sir Percival for his life—and the principal to the children of the marriage.", "이만 파운드에 관한 내 요구 조건은 간단히 이러했다. 전액을 신탁으로 묶어 그 수입을 신부에게 종신으로 주고, 그 뒤에는 퍼시벌 경에게 종신으로 주며, 원금은 이 혼인에서 태어난 자녀에게 돌아가게 한다."], ["In default of issue, the principal was to be disposed of as the lady might by her will direct, for which purpose I reserved to her the right of making a will.", "자녀가 없을 경우 원금은 신부가 유언으로 지정하는 대로 처분하되, 그러기 위해 나는 그녀에게 유언을 작성할 권리를 남겨 두었다."], ["The effect of these conditions may be thus summed up.", "이 조건들의 효과는 이렇게 요약할 수 있다."], ["If Lady Glyde died without leaving children, her half-sister Miss Halcombe, and any other relatives or friends whom she might be anxious to benefit, would, on her husband’s death, divide among them such shares of her money as she desired them to have.", "글라이드 부인이 자녀를 남기지 않고 죽으면, 이복 언니인 홀콤 양과 그 밖에 그녀가 은혜를 베풀고 싶어 할 친척이나 친구들이 남편이 죽은 뒤에 그녀가 정해 준 몫대로 그 돈을 나누어 갖게 된다."], ["If, on the other hand, she died leaving children, then their interest, naturally and necessarily, superseded all other interests whatsoever.", "반대로 자녀를 남기고 죽으면, 자연히 또 필연적으로 그 자녀의 권리가 다른 모든 권리에 앞선다. 이것이 그 조항이었다."], ["This was the clause—and no one who reads it can fail, I think, to agree with me that it meted out equal justice to all parties.", "이 글을 읽는 이라면 누구든 이 조항이 모든 당사자에게 공평한 정의를 나누어 주었다는 데 동의하지 않을 수 없으리라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["We shall see how my proposals were met on the husband’s side.", "내 제안이 남편 쪽에서 어떻게 받아들여졌는지 이제 보기로 하자."]], [["At the time when Miss Halcombe’s letter reached me I was even more busily occupied than usual.", "홀콤 양의 편지가 내게 닿았을 무렵 나는 평소보다도 더 바빴다."], ["But I contrived to make leisure for the settlement.", "그러나 나는 혼인 재산 계약을 위해 짬을 냈다."], ["I had drawn it, and had sent it for approval to Sir Percival’s solicitor, in less than a week from the time when Miss Halcombe had informed me of the proposed marriage.", "홀콤 양에게서 혼사 소식을 들은 때로부터 일주일도 되기 전에 나는 계약서를 작성하여 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사에게 검토용으로 보냈다."]], [["After a lapse of two days the document was returned to me, with notes and remarks of the baronet’s lawyer.", "이틀이 지나 문서는 준남작 측 변호사의 주석과 의견이 붙은 채 내게 돌아왔다."], ["His objections, in general, proved to be of the most trifling and technical kind, until he came to the clause relating to the twenty thousand pounds.", "그의 이의는 대체로 지극히 사소하고 형식적인 것들이었는데, 이만 파운드에 관한 조항에 이르자 사정이 달라졌다."], ["Against this there were double lines drawn in red ink, and the following note was appended to them—", "그 대목에는 붉은 잉크로 두 줄이 그어져 있었고 다음과 같은 주석이 달려 있었다 —"]], [["“Not admissible.", "“받아들일 수 없음."], ["The PRINCIPAL to go to Sir Percival Glyde, in the event of his surviving Lady Glyde, and there being no issue.”", "원금은, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 글라이드 부인보다 오래 살고 자녀가 없을 경우, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 귀속할 것.”"]], [["That is to say, not one farthing of the twenty thousand pounds was to go to Miss Halcombe, or to any other relative or friend of Lady Glyde’s.", "다시 말해 이만 파운드는 한 푼도 홀콤 양에게든 글라이드 부인의 다른 어떤 친척이나 친구에게든 가지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["The whole sum, if she left no children, was to slip into the pockets of her husband.", "그녀가 자녀를 남기지 않으면 그 전액이 남편의 주머니로 슬며시 굴러 들어가게 된다는 것이었다."]], [["The answer I wrote to this audacious proposal was as short and sharp as I could make it.", "이 뻔뻔한 제안에 내가 쓴 답장은 내가 할 수 있는 한 짧고 매서웠다."], ["“My dear sir.", "“친애하는 선생."], ["Miss Fairlie’s settlement.", "페얼리 양의 혼인 재산 계약 건."], ["I maintain the clause to which you object, exactly as it stands.", "귀하가 이의를 제기하신 조항을 나는 지금 그대로 유지합니다."], ["Yours truly.”", "이만 줄입니다.”"], ["The rejoinder came back in a quarter of an hour.", "응수는 십오 분 만에 돌아왔다. “친애하는 선생."], ["“My dear sir. Miss Fairlie’s settlement.", "페얼리 양의 혼인 재산 계약 건."], ["I maintain the red ink to which you object, exactly as it stands.", "귀하가 이의를 제기하신 붉은 잉크를 나는 지금 그대로 유지합니다."], ["Yours truly.”", "이만 줄입니다.”"], ["In the detestable slang of the day, we were now both “at a deadlock,” and nothing was left for it but to refer to our clients on either side.", "요즘 통용되는 그 고약한 말투로 하자면 우리 둘은 이제 “교착 상태”에 빠진 것이었고, 남은 길이라고는 양쪽이 각자의 의뢰인에게 문의하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["As matters stood, my client—Miss Fairlie not having yet completed her twenty-first year—Mr. Frederick Fairlie, was her guardian.", "사정이 그러한 만큼 — 페얼리 양이 아직 스물한 살을 채우지 못했으므로 — 내 의뢰인은 그녀의 후견인인 프레더릭 페얼리 씨였다."], ["I wrote by that day’s post, and put the case before him exactly as it stood, not only urging every argument I could think of to induce him to maintain the clause as I had drawn it, but stating to him plainly the mercenary motive which was at the bottom of the opposition to my settlement of the twenty thousand pounds.", "나는 그날 우편으로 편지를 써서 사태를 있는 그대로 그의 앞에 내놓았고, 내가 작성한 대로 그 조항을 지키도록 그를 설득하려고 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 논거를 들이댔을 뿐 아니라, 이만 파운드에 관한 내 계약 조항에 반대하는 밑바닥에 돈 욕심이 깔려 있다는 것도 분명히 밝혔다."], ["The knowledge of Sir Percival’s affairs which I had necessarily gained when the provisions of the deed on HIS side were submitted in due course to my examination, had but too plainly informed me that the debts on his estate were enormous, and that his income, though nominally a large one, was virtually, for a man in his position, next to nothing.", "퍼시벌 경 쪽 증서 조항들이 절차에 따라 내 검토에 넘어왔을 때 나는 그의 재정 형편을 알 수밖에 없었는데, 그로써 너무나도 분명해진 것은 그의 영지에 걸린 빚이 어마어마하며, 그의 수입은 명목상으로는 크다 해도 그만한 지위의 사람에게는 사실상 없는 것이나 다름없다는 사실이었다."], ["The want of ready money was the practical necessity of Sir Percival’s existence, and his lawyer’s note on the clause in the settlement was nothing but the frankly selfish expression of it.", "당장 쓸 현금이 없다는 것이야말로 퍼시벌 경의 삶을 짓누르는 현실적 곤경이었고, 계약서의 그 조항에 붙은 그 변호사의 주석은 그것을 노골적이고 이기적으로 드러낸 것에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Fairlie’s answer reached me by return of post, and proved to be wandering and irrelevant in the extreme.", "페얼리 씨의 답장은 곧바로 다음 우편으로 도착했는데, 지극히 종잡을 수 없고 동떨어진 내용이었다."], ["Turned into plain English, it practically expressed itself to this effect: “Would dear Gilmore be so very obliging as not to worry his friend and client about such a trifle as a remote contingency?", "알아듣기 쉬운 영어로 옮기자면 사실상 이런 뜻이었다. “친애하는 길모어가, 아득히 먼 우발 사태 따위의 사소한 일로 자기 벗이자 의뢰인을 괴롭히지 않는 친절을 베풀어 줄 수는 없겠소?"], ["Was it likely that a young woman of twenty-one would die before a man of forty five, and die without children?", "스물한 살 젊은 여인이 마흔다섯 사내보다 먼저, 그것도 자식 없이 죽을 성싶기나 하오?"], ["On the other hand, in such a miserable world as this, was it possible to over-estimate the value of peace and quietness?", "한편으로 이토록 비참한 세상에서 평온과 고요의 값어치를 아무리 높이 친들 지나칠 리가 있겠소?"], ["If those two heavenly blessings were offered in exchange for such an earthly trifle as a remote chance of twenty thousand pounds, was it not a fair bargain?", "그 두 가지 천상의 축복을 이만 파운드의 아득한 가망 따위 하찮은 속세의 것과 맞바꿔 준다면, 그거야말로 공정한 거래가 아니겠소? 아무렴, 그렇고말고."], ["Surely, yes. Then why not make it?”", "그렇다면 어찌 아니 하겠소?”"]], [["I threw the letter away in disgust.", "나는 역겨워하며 편지를 내던졌다."], ["Just as it had fluttered to the ground, there was a knock at my door, and Sir Percival’s solicitor, Mr. Merriman, was shown in.", "그것이 막 바닥으로 팔랑팔랑 떨어졌을 때 문 두드리는 소리가 나더니 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사 메리먼 씨가 안내를 받아 들어왔다."], ["There are many varieties of sharp practitioners in this world, but I think the hardest of all to deal with are the men who overreach you under the disguise of inveterate good-humour.", "이 세상에는 온갖 종류의 약삭빠른 자들이 있지만, 그중에서도 가장 상대하기 어려운 것은 몸에 밴 쾌활함을 가면 삼아 사람을 속여 넘기는 부류라고 나는 생각한다."], ["A fat, well fed, smiling, friendly man of business is of all parties to a bargain the most hopeless to deal with.", "살집 좋고 배부르고 웃음 띠고 다정한 사업가야말로 흥정의 상대로는 가장 가망 없는 축이다."], ["Mr. Merriman was one of this class.", "메리먼 씨가 바로 그런 부류였다."]], [["“And how is good Mr. Gilmore?”", "“길모어 선생, 그동안 안녕하셨는지요?”"], ["he began, all in a glow with the warmth of his own amiability.", "그는 제 상냥함의 온기로 온통 달아오른 채 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Glad to see you, sir, in such excellent health.", "“이렇게 정정하신 모습을 뵈니 반갑습니다, 선생."], ["I was passing your door, and I thought I would look in in case you might have something to say to me.", "문 앞을 지나다가 혹시 제게 하실 말씀이 있으실까 하여 들러 보았지요."], ["Do—now pray do let us settle this little difference of ours by word of mouth, if we can!", "자, 부디, 부디 우리 사이의 이 자그마한 이견을 될 수만 있다면 말로 매듭지읍시다!"], ["Have you heard from your client yet?”", "의뢰인에게서 소식은 받으셨습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“받았네."], ["Have you heard from yours?”", "자네는 자네 의뢰인에게서 소식을 받았나?”"]], [["“My dear, good sir!", "“아이고, 친애하는 선생!"], ["I wish I had heard from him to any purpose—I wish, with all my heart, the responsibility was off my shoulders; but he is obstinate—or let me rather say, resolute—and he won’t take it off.", "그분에게서 쓸모 있는 소식이라도 왔더라면 좋았을 것을요. 이 책임이 제 어깨에서 벗겨지기를 진심으로 바랍니다만, 그분이 워낙 고집이 세셔서요 — 아니, 결단이 굳으시다고 해 두지요 — 도무지 벗겨 주시질 않습니다."], ["‘Merriman, I leave details to you.", "‘메리먼, 세부는 자네에게 맡기네."], ["Do what you think right for my interests, and consider me as having personally withdrawn from the business until it is all over.’ Those were Sir Percival’s words a fortnight ago, and all I can get him to do now is to repeat them.", "내 이익을 위해 옳다고 생각하는 대로 하고, 이 일이 다 끝날 때까지 나는 개인적으로 손을 뗀 사람으로 여기게.’ 보름 전에 퍼시벌 경께서 하신 말씀이 그것이고, 지금 제가 얻어 낼 수 있는 것이라고는 그 말씀의 되풀이뿐입니다."], ["I am not a hard man, Mr. Gilmore, as you know.", "길모어 선생, 아시다시피 저는 모진 사람이 아닙니다."], ["Personally and privately, I do assure you, I should like to sponge out that note of mine at this very moment.", "개인적으로, 또 사사로이 말씀드리자면 저는 지금 이 순간에도 제가 쓴 그 주석을 지워 버리고 싶습니다."], ["But if Sir Percival won’t go into the matter, if Sir Percival will blindly leave all his interests in my sole care, what course can I possibly take except the course of asserting them?", "그러나 퍼시벌 경께서 이 문제를 들여다보려 하지 않으신다면, 퍼시벌 경께서 당신의 이익 일체를 눈감고 제 손에만 맡기신다면, 제가 그 이익을 주장하는 길 말고 무슨 길을 택할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["My hands are bound—don’t you see, my dear sir?—my hands are bound.”", "제 손은 묶여 있습니다 — 아시겠지요, 친애하는 선생? — 제 손은 묶여 있어요.”"]], [["“You maintain your note on the clause, then, to the letter?”", "“그러면 자네는 그 조항에 붙인 주석을 한 글자도 물리지 않겠다는 말인가?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes—deuce take it! I have no other alternative.”", "“그렇습니다 — 빌어먹을 노릇이지요!"], ["He walked to the fireplace and warmed himself, humming the fag end of a tune in a rich convivial bass voice.", "달리 도리가 없습니다.” 그는 벽난로로 걸어가 몸을 녹이며 걸쭉하고 흥겨운 저음으로 노랫가락 끝자락을 흥얼거렸다."], ["“What does your side say?”", "“그쪽에서는 뭐라고 합니까?”"], ["he went on; “now pray tell me—what does your side say?”", "그가 말을 이었다. “자, 어서 말씀해 주십시오 — 그쪽에서는 뭐라고 합니까?”"]], [["I was ashamed to tell him.", "나는 그에게 말하기가 부끄러웠다."], ["I attempted to gain time—nay, I did worse.", "시간을 벌어 보려 했고 — 아니, 그보다 더 못한 짓을 했다."], ["My legal instincts got the better of me, and I even tried to bargain.", "법률가의 본능이 나를 이겨서, 나는 흥정까지 시도했던 것이다."]], [["“Twenty thousand pounds is rather a large sum to be given up by the lady’s friends at two days’ notice,”", "“이만 파운드는 신부 쪽 사람들이 이틀 말미에 포기하기에는 꽤 큰 액수일세.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Very true,” replied Mr.", "“참으로 옳은 말씀입니다.”"], ["Merriman, looking down thoughtfully at his boots.", "메리먼 씨가 생각에 잠긴 듯 제 구두를 내려다보며 대꾸했다."], ["“Properly put, sir—most properly put!”", "“잘 짚으셨습니다, 선생 — 참으로 잘 짚으셨어요!”"]], [["“A compromise, recognising the interests of the lady’s family as well as the interests of the husband, might not perhaps have frightened my client quite so much,”", "“남편 쪽 이익만이 아니라 신부 집안의 이익도 인정하는 절충안이었다면 아마 내 의뢰인이 그렇게까지 놀라지는 않았을 걸세.”"], ["I went on. “Come, come!", "내가 말을 이었다."], ["this contingency resolves itself into a matter of bargaining after all.", "“자, 자! 이 우발 사태라는 것도 결국은 흥정의 문제로 귀착되는군."], ["What is the least you will take?”", "자네 쪽에서 받아들일 최소는 얼마인가?”"]], [["“The least we will take,” said Mr.", "“저희가 받아들일 최소는,”"], ["Merriman, “is nineteen-thousand- nine-hundred-and-ninety-nine-pounds-nineteen-shillings-and-elevenpence- three-farthings. Ha!", "메리먼 씨가 말했다, “일만-구천-구백-구십구-파운드-십구-실링-십일-펜스-삼-파딩입니다."], ["ha! ha!", "하하하!"], ["Excuse me, Mr. Gilmore.", "실례했습니다, 길모어 선생."], ["I must have my little joke.”", "저도 농담 한마디쯤은 해야지요.”"]], [["“Little enough,”", "“참으로 자잘한 농담이군.”"], ["I remarked.", "내가 한마디 했다."], ["“The joke is just worth the odd farthing it was made for.”", "“그 농담은 딱 그것을 위해 남겨 둔 그 자투리 파딩만큼의 값어치가 있네.”"]], [["Mr. Merriman was delighted.", "메리먼 씨는 아주 기뻐했다."], ["He laughed over my retort till the room rang again.", "그는 내 응수에 방 안이 울리도록 웃어 젖혔다."], ["I was not half so good-humoured on my side; I came back to business, and closed the interview.", "내 쪽은 그 절반만큼도 유쾌하지 않았다. 나는 용건으로 돌아가 면담을 끝맺었다."]], [["“This is Friday,”", "“오늘이 금요일이군.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Give us till Tuesday next for our final answer.”", "“최종 답변은 다음 주 화요일까지 말미를 주게.”"]], [["“By all means,”", "“여부가 있겠습니까.”"], ["replied Mr. Merriman.", "메리먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["“Longer, my dear sir, if you like.”", "“원하신다면 더 오래 드리지요, 친애하는 선생.”"], ["He took up his hat to go, and then addressed me again.", "그는 가려고 모자를 집어 들었다가 다시 내게 말을 건넸다."], ["“By the way,”", "“그런데 말입니다,”"], ["he said, “your clients in Cumberland have not heard anything more of the woman who wrote the anonymous letter, have they?”", "그가 말했다, “컴벌랜드의 그쪽 의뢰인들은 익명의 편지를 쓴 그 여자에 대해 그 뒤로 아무 소식도 듣지 못했겠지요?”"]], [["“Nothing more,”", "“더는 없네.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Have you found no trace of her?”", "“자네들은 그 여자의 자취를 찾지 못했나?”"]], [["“Not yet,”", "“아직입니다.”"], ["said my legal friend.", "나의 법조계 벗이 말했다."], ["“But we don’t despair.", "“그러나 단념하지는 않습니다."], ["Sir Percival has his suspicions that Somebody is keeping her in hiding, and we are having that Somebody watched.”", "퍼시벌 경께서는 누군가가 그 여자를 숨겨 주고 있다고 의심하시고, 저희는 그 누군가를 감시하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“You mean the old woman who was with her in Cumberland,”", "“컴벌랜드에서 그 여자와 함께 있었다는 노파를 말하는 건가?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Quite another party, sir,”", "“전혀 다른 사람입니다, 선생.”"], ["answered Mr. Merriman.", "메리먼 씨가 대답했다."], ["“We don’t happen to have laid hands on the old woman yet.", "“그 노파는 아직 손에 넣지 못했지요."], ["Our Somebody is a man.", "저희가 말하는 누군가는 남자입니다."], ["We have got him close under our eye here in London, and we strongly suspect he had something to do with helping her in the first instance to escape from the Asylum.", "여기 런던에서 바로 코앞에 두고 지켜보고 있는데, 애초에 그 여자가 정신병원에서 달아나도록 도운 데 관여했으리라고 강하게 의심하고 있습니다."], ["Sir Percival wanted to question him at once, but I said, ‘No.", "퍼시벌 경께서는 당장 그를 추궁하려 하셨지만 제가 말씀드렸지요."], ["Questioning him will only put him on his guard—watch him, and wait.’ We shall see what happens.", "‘안 됩니다. 추궁해 봐야 경계심만 돋울 뿐입니다 — 지켜보면서 기다리십시오.’ 어찌 되는지 보아야지요."], ["A dangerous woman to be at large, Mr.", "저런 여자가 밖을 돌아다니는 것은 위험한 일입니다, 길모어 선생."], ["Gilmore; nobody knows what she may do next.", "다음에 무슨 짓을 할지 아무도 모르지요."], ["I wish you good-morning, sir.", "그럼 안녕히 계십시오, 선생."], ["On Tuesday next I shall hope for the pleasure of hearing from you.”", "다음 주 화요일에는 좋은 소식을 들려주시기를 기대하겠습니다.”"], ["He smiled amiably and went out.", "그는 상냥하게 웃고는 나갔다."]], [["My mind had been rather absent during the latter part of the conversation with my legal friend.", "이 법조계 벗과 나눈 대화의 뒷부분에서 내 정신은 다소 딴 데 가 있었다."], ["I was so anxious about the matter of the settlement that I had little attention to give to any other subject, and the moment I was left alone again I began to think over what my next proceeding ought to be.", "나는 혼인 재산 계약 문제가 어찌나 걱정스러웠던지 다른 어떤 일에도 마음을 쏟을 여유가 없었고, 다시 혼자 남겨진 순간 다음에 어떻게 해야 할지를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["In the case of any other client I should have acted on my instructions, however personally distasteful to me, and have given up the point about the twenty thousand pounds on the spot.", "다른 의뢰인의 일이었다면 나는 개인적으로 아무리 마뜩잖더라도 지시대로 움직여, 그 자리에서 이만 파운드에 관한 요구를 접었을 것이다."], ["But I could not act with this business-like indifference towards Miss Fairlie.", "그러나 페얼리 양을 상대로 그런 사무적인 무심함으로 처신할 수는 없었다."], ["I had an honest feeling of affection and admiration for her—I remembered gratefully that her father had been the kindest patron and friend to me that ever man had—I had felt towards her while I was drawing the settlement as I might have felt, if I had not been an old bachelor, towards a daughter of my own, and I was determined to spare no personal sacrifice in her service and where her interests were concerned.", "나는 그녀에게 정직한 애정과 감탄을 품고 있었고 — 그녀의 아버지가 사람이 가질 수 있는 가장 친절한 후원자이자 벗이었음을 고맙게 기억하고 있었으며 — 혼인 재산 계약서를 작성하는 내내 내가 늙은 독신자만 아니었더라면 제 딸에게 느꼈을 법한 마음으로 그녀를 대하고 있었다. 그러니 그녀를 위하는 일, 그녀의 이익이 걸린 일이라면 나는 어떤 개인적 희생도 아끼지 않으리라 마음먹고 있었다."], ["Writing a second time to Mr.", "페얼리 씨에게 두 번째로 편지를 쓰는 것은 생각할 것도 없었다."], ["Fairlie was not to be thought of—it would only be giving him a second opportunity of slipping through my fingers.", "그것은 그에게 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 기회를 한 번 더 주는 일일 뿐이었다."], ["Seeing him and personally remonstrating with him might possibly be of more use.", "직접 만나 몸소 따져 묻는다면 그나마 쓸모가 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["The next day was Saturday.", "이튿날은 토요일이었다."], ["I determined to take a return ticket and jolt my old bones down to Cumberland, on the chance of persuading him to adopt the just, the independent, and the honourable course.", "나는 왕복표를 끊어 이 늙은 뼈다귀를 덜컹거리며 컴벌랜드까지 내려가, 정당하고 떳떳하고 명예로운 길을 택하도록 그를 설득해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["It was a poor chance enough, no doubt, but when I had tried it my conscience would be at ease.", "물론 가망이야 형편없이 적었지만, 그렇게라도 해 보고 나면 내 양심은 편해질 터였다."], ["I should then have done all that a man in my position could do to serve the interests of my old friend’s only child.", "그러면 나는 옛 벗의 하나뿐인 아이를 위해 내 처지에서 할 수 있는 일을 다 한 셈이 될 것이었다."]], [["The weather on Saturday was beautiful, a west wind and a bright sun.", "토요일 날씨는 아름다웠다. 서풍이 불고 햇살이 밝았다."], ["Having felt latterly a return of that fulness and oppression of the head, against which my doctor warned me so seriously more than two years since, I resolved to take the opportunity of getting a little extra exercise by sending my bag on before me and walking to the terminus in Euston Square.", "요즘 들어 머리가 다시 그득하고 짓눌리는 느낌이 도져 있었는데, 두 해도 더 전에 의사가 그토록 엄중히 경고했던 바로 그 증세였으므로, 나는 이 기회에 운동을 좀 더 해 두기로 하고 가방을 먼저 부친 다음 유스턴 광장의 역까지 걸어가기로 했다."], ["As I came out into Holborn a gentleman walking by rapidly stopped and spoke to me.", "홀본으로 나서는데 빠른 걸음으로 지나가던 신사 하나가 걸음을 멈추고 내게 말을 걸었다."], ["It was Mr. Walter Hartright.", "월터 하트라이트 씨였다."]], [["If he had not been the first to greet me I should certainly have passed him.", "그가 먼저 인사를 건네지 않았더라면 나는 틀림없이 그를 지나쳤을 것이다."], ["He was so changed that I hardly knew him again.", "어찌나 달라졌던지 알아보기조차 어려웠다."], ["His face looked pale and haggard—his manner was hurried and uncertain—and his dress, which I remembered as neat and gentlemanlike when I saw him at Limmeridge, was so slovenly now that I should really have been ashamed of the appearance of it on one of my own clerks.", "얼굴은 창백하고 초췌했으며, 몸가짐은 조급하고 안정을 잃었고, 리머리지에서 보았을 때 단정하고 신사다웠던 것으로 기억하는 옷차림은 이제 어찌나 추레한지 내 사무소 서기가 그런 꼴을 하고 있었다면 정말이지 부끄러웠을 정도였다."]], [["“Have you been long back from Cumberland?”", "“컴벌랜드에서 돌아오신 지 오래되셨습니까?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I heard from Miss Halcombe lately.", "“최근에 홀콤 양에게서 소식을 들었습니다."], ["I am aware that Sir Percival Glyde’s explanation has been considered satisfactory.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 해명이 만족스러운 것으로 받아들여졌다는 것도 알고 있습니다."], ["Will the marriage take place soon?", "혼례가 곧 치러집니까?"], ["Do you happen to know, Mr. Gilmore?”", "혹시 아십니까, 길모어 선생?”"]], [["He spoke so fast, and crowded his questions together so strangely and confusedly, that I could hardly follow him.", "그는 어찌나 빨리 말하고 질문들을 어찌나 이상하고 어지럽게 몰아붙이던지 나는 따라가기조차 힘들었다."], ["However accidentally intimate he might have been with the family at Limmeridge, I could not see that he had any right to expect information on their private affairs, and I determined to drop him, as easily as might be, on the subject of Miss Fairlie’s marriage.", "그가 어쩌다 리머리지 집안과 가깝게 지냈다 한들, 그 집안의 사사로운 일을 알 권리가 그에게 있다고는 보이지 않았으므로, 나는 페얼리 양의 혼사 이야기에서 되도록 무리 없이 그를 떼어 놓기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Time will show, Mr.", "“때가 되면 알게 될 것이오, 하트라이트 씨.”"], ["Hartright,”", "내가 말했다."], ["I said—“time will show.", "“때가 되면 알게 되겠지요."], ["I dare say if we look out for the marriage in the papers we shall not be far wrong.", "신문에서 혼인 소식을 눈여겨보고 있으면 크게 어긋나지는 않을 것이오."], ["Excuse my noticing it, but I am sorry to see you not looking so well as you were when we last met.”", "이런 말을 하는 것을 언짢게 여기지 마시오만, 지난번에 만났을 때보다 안색이 좋지 않아 보여 안타깝소.”"]], [["A momentary nervous contraction quivered about his lips and eyes, and made me half reproach myself for having answered him in such a significantly guarded manner.", "순간적인 신경질적 경련이 그의 입가와 눈가에 파르르 스쳤고, 그것을 보자 나는 그토록 의미심장하게 말을 아껴 대답한 나 자신을 절반쯤 나무라게 되었다."]], [["“I had no right to ask about her marriage,”", "“그분의 혼사를 물을 권리가 제게는 없었지요.”"], ["he said bitterly.", "그가 쓰라리게 말했다."], ["“I must wait to see it in the newspapers like other people.", "“저도 다른 사람들처럼 신문에 나기를 기다려야겠습니다. 네,”"], ["Yes,”—he went on before I could make any apologies—“I have not been well lately.", "— 그는 내가 무슨 사과의 말을 꺼내기도 전에 말을 이었다 — “요즘 몸이 좋지 않았습니다."], ["I am going to another country to try a change of scene and occupation.", "다른 나라로 가서 풍경과 일을 바꿔 볼 참입니다."], ["Miss Halcombe has kindly assisted me with her influence, and my testimonials have been found satisfactory.", "홀콤 양께서 친절히 힘을 써 주셨고, 제 추천서도 만족스럽다는 평을 받았습니다."], ["It is a long distance off, but I don’t care where I go, what the climate is, or how long I am away.”", "아주 먼 곳입니다만, 저는 어디로 가든, 기후가 어떻든, 얼마나 오래 떠나 있든 상관없습니다.”"], ["He looked about him while he said this at the throng of strangers passing us by on either side, in a strange, suspicious manner, as if he thought that some of them might be watching us.", "그는 이렇게 말하는 동안 양옆으로 우리를 스쳐 지나가는 낯선 사람들의 무리를 이상하고 의심 가득한 눈길로 둘러보았다. 마치 그중 누군가가 우리를 지켜보고 있을지도 모른다고 여기는 사람처럼."]], [["“I wish you well through it, and safe back again,”", "“그 일이 잘 풀리고 무사히 돌아오시기를 바라오.”"], ["I said, and then added, so as not to keep him altogether at arm’s length on the subject of the Fairlies, “I am going down to Limmeridge to-day on business.", "나는 그렇게 말한 뒤, 페얼리 집안 이야기에서 그를 아주 밀어내 버리지는 않으려고 이렇게 덧붙였다. “나는 오늘 볼일이 있어 리머리지로 내려가는 길이오."], ["Miss Halcombe and Miss Fairlie are away just now on a visit to some friends in Yorkshire.”", "홀콤 양과 페얼리 양은 지금 요크셔의 친구들을 방문하러 떠나고 없소.”"]], [["His eyes brightened, and he seemed about to say something in answer, but the same momentary nervous spasm crossed his face again.", "그의 눈이 환해졌고 무언가 대답하려는 듯했으나, 아까와 같은 순간적인 신경의 경련이 다시 얼굴을 스쳤다. 그는 내 손을 잡아 힘껏 쥐고는 한 마디도 더 하지 않은 채 인파 속으로 사라졌다."], ["He took my hand, pressed it hard, and disappeared among the crowd without saying another word.", "그는 내게 낯선 사람이나 다름없었지만, 나는 잠시 그 자리에 서서 거의 아쉬운 마음으로 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."], ["Though he was little more than a stranger to me, I waited for a moment, looking after him almost with a feeling of regret.", "젊은이들이 그릇된 길로 접어들기 시작할 때 겉으로 어떤 조짐과 표시가 나타나는지 알 만큼은 나도 직업상 겪어 본 터였다."], ["I had gained in my profession sufficient experience of young men to know what the outward signs and tokens were of their beginning to go wrong, and when I resumed my walk to the railway I am sorry to say I felt more than doubtful about Mr. Hartright’s future.", "그리하여 다시 역을 향해 걸음을 옮기면서, 안타깝지만 나는 하트라이트 씨의 앞날에 대해 의심을 넘어선 무엇을 느꼈다고 말해 두어야겠다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["Leaving by an early train, I got to Limmeridge in time for dinner.", "이른 기차로 떠난 덕에 나는 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 리머리지에 닿았다."], ["The house was oppressively empty and dull.", "집 안은 숨이 막힐 만큼 텅 비고 적막했다."], ["I had expected that good Mrs. Vesey would have been company for me in the absence of the young ladies, but she was confined to her room by a cold.", "두 아가씨가 없는 동안 그 선량한 베지 부인이 말벗이 되어 주려니 했으나, 그녀는 감기로 방에 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["The servants were so surprised at seeing me that they hurried and bustled absurdly, and made all sorts of annoying mistakes.", "하인들은 나를 보고 어찌나 놀랐던지 우스꽝스러울 만큼 허둥지둥 부산을 떨며 온갖 성가신 실수를 저질렀다."], ["Even the butler, who was old enough to have known better, brought me a bottle of port that was chilled.", "그만한 나이면 분별이 있을 법한 집사조차 차게 식어 버린 포트와인 한 병을 내왔다."], ["The reports of Mr. Fairlie’s health were just as usual, and when I sent up a message to announce my arrival, I was told that he would be delighted to see me the next morning but that the sudden news of my appearance had prostrated him with palpitations for the rest of the evening.", "페얼리 씨의 건강에 관한 보고는 여느 때와 똑같았고, 내가 도착을 알리는 전갈을 올려 보내자 돌아온 대답인즉, 내일 아침이면 기꺼이 나를 만나겠으나 내가 나타났다는 갑작스러운 소식에 심계항진이 도져 그날 저녁 내내 몸져누웠다는 것이었다."], ["The wind howled dismally all night, and strange cracking and groaning noises sounded here, there, and everywhere in the empty house.", "밤새 바람이 음산하게 울부짖었고, 텅 빈 집 안 이곳저곳 사방에서 이상하게 갈라지고 삐걱대는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["I slept as wretchedly as possible, and got up in a mighty bad humour to breakfast by myself the next morning.", "나는 더할 나위 없이 비참하게 잤고, 이튿날 아침 몹시 언짢은 기분으로 일어나 혼자 아침을 먹었다."]], [["At ten o’clock I was conducted to Mr. Fairlie’s apartments.", "열 시에 나는 페얼리 씨의 거처로 안내되었다."], ["He was in his usual room, his usual chair, and his usual aggravating state of mind and body.", "그는 늘 있던 방, 늘 앉던 의자에, 늘 그렇듯 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 심신의 상태로 있었다."], ["When I went in, his valet was standing before him, holding up for inspection a heavy volume of etchings, as long and as broad as my office writing-desk.", "내가 들어갔을 때, 그의 시종은 주인 앞에 서서 내 사무실 책상만큼이나 길고 넓은 묵직한 동판화첩을 감상하시라고 받쳐 들고 있었다."], ["The miserable foreigner grinned in the most abject manner, and looked ready to drop with fatigue, while his master composedly turned over the etchings, and brought their hidden beauties to light with the help of a magnifying glass.", "그 가엾은 외국인은 더없이 비굴한 낯으로 히죽거리며 피곤에 겨워 곧 쓰러질 듯했고, 그동안 주인은 태연히 동판화를 한 장씩 넘기며 돋보기의 힘을 빌려 그 감춰진 아름다움을 밝혀내고 있었다."]], [["“You very best of good old friends,” said Mr.", "“더없이 좋은 옛 벗이시여,”"], ["Fairlie, leaning back lazily before he could look at me, “are you QUITE well?", "페얼리 씨가 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 나른하게 몸을 뒤로 젖히며 말했다. “아주 안녕하신가요?"], ["How nice of you to come here and see me in my solitude.", "이 외로운 처지의 나를 이렇게 찾아와 주시다니 얼마나 고마운 일인지."], ["Dear Gilmore!”", "사랑하는 길모어!”"]], [["I had expected that the valet would be dismissed when I appeared, but nothing of the sort happened.", "내가 나타나면 시종을 물릴 줄 알았는데, 그런 일은 조금도 일어나지 않았다."], ["There he stood, in front of his master’s chair, trembling under the weight of the etchings, and there Mr. Fairlie sat, serenely twirling the magnifying glass between his white fingers and thumbs.", "시종은 주인의 의자 앞에 선 채로 동판화첩의 무게에 부들부들 떨고 있었고, 페얼리 씨는 희고 가는 손가락 사이에서 돋보기를 유유히 돌리며 앉아 있었다."]], [["“I have come to speak to you on a very important matter,”", "“대단히 중요한 일로 말씀을 드리러 왔습니다.”"], ["I said, “and you will therefore excuse me, if I suggest that we had better be alone.”", "내가 말했다. “그러니 우리 둘만 있는 편이 낫겠다고 청하더라도 양해해 주시지요.”"]], [["The unfortunate valet looked at me gratefully.", "가엾은 시종이 고마운 눈길로 나를 보았다."], ["Mr. Fairlie faintly repeated my last three words, “better be alone,”", "페얼리 씨는 상상할 수 있는 최대의 경악을 얼굴에 담고서 내 마지막 말을 힘없이 되뇌었다."], ["with every appearance of the utmost possible astonishment.", "“둘만 있는 편이 낫다.”"]], [["I was in no humour for trifling, and I resolved to make him understand what I meant.", "나는 시시껄렁한 수작을 받아 줄 기분이 아니었으므로, 내 말뜻을 반드시 알아듣게 하리라 마음먹었다."]], [["“Oblige me by giving that man permission to withdraw,”", "“저 사람에게 물러가도 좋다고 허락해 주시면 고맙겠습니다.”"], ["I said, pointing to the valet.", "나는 시종을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Fairlie arched his eyebrows and pursed up his lips in sarcastic surprise.", "페얼리 씨는 눈썹을 치켜올리고 입술을 오므리며 비꼬는 듯한 놀라움을 지어 보였다."]], [["“Man?” he repeated.", "“사람이라니요?”"], ["“You provoking old Gilmore, what can you possibly mean by calling him a man?", "그가 되물었다. “속 뒤집는 길모어 노인장, 저것을 사람이라 부르시다니 대체 무슨 뜻입니까?"], ["He’s nothing of the sort.", "저건 사람 따위가 아니에요."], ["He might have been a man half an hour ago, before I wanted my etchings, and he may be a man half an hour hence, when I don’t want them any longer.", "반 시간 전, 내가 동판화를 찾기 전이라면 사람이었겠지요. 또 반 시간 뒤에, 내가 더는 동판화가 필요 없어지면 다시 사람이 될 테고요."], ["At present he is simply a portfolio stand.", "지금 저것은 그저 화첩 받침대일 뿐입니다."], ["Why object, Gilmore, to a portfolio stand?”", "길모어, 화첩 받침대를 두고 무슨 이의가 있습니까?”"]], [["“I DO object.", "“이의가 있습니다."], ["For the third time, Mr. Fairlie, I beg that we may be alone.”", "페얼리 씨, 세 번째로 청합니다만, 우리 둘만 있게 해 주십시오.”"]], [["My tone and manner left him no alternative but to comply with my request.", "내 어조와 태도에는 내 청을 들어주는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다."], ["He looked at the servant, and pointed peevishly to a chair at his side.", "그는 하인을 보더니 짜증스럽게 제 옆의 의자를 가리켰다."]], [["“Put down the etchings and go away,”", "“동판화는 내려놓고 나가라.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Don’t upset me by losing my place.", "“내가 보던 자리를 잃어버려서 나를 뒤집어 놓지는 말고."], ["Have you, or have you not, lost my place?", "내 자리를 잃어버렸느냐, 아니냐?"], ["Are you sure you have not?", "정말 잃어버리지 않았단 말이지?"], ["And have you put my hand-bell quite within my reach?", "그리고 내 손종은 손 닿는 데 잘 놓아두었느냐?"], ["Yes?", "그래?"], ["Then why the devil don’t you go?”", "그럼 대체 왜 안 나가는 게냐?”"]], [["The valet went out.", "시종이 나갔다."], ["Mr. Fairlie twisted himself round in his chair, polished the magnifying glass with his delicate cambric handkerchief, and indulged himself with a sidelong inspection of the open volume of etchings.", "페얼리 씨는 의자에 앉은 채 몸을 비틀어 돌리고는 고운 아마포 손수건으로 돋보기를 닦더니, 펼쳐진 동판화첩을 곁눈질로 살피는 즐거움에 빠져들었다."], ["It was not easy to keep my temper under these circumstances, but I did keep it.", "이런 상황에서 성미를 다스리기란 쉽지 않았으나, 나는 끝내 다스렸다."]], [["“I have come here at great personal inconvenience,”", "“저는 개인적으로 대단한 불편을 무릅쓰고 여기까지 왔습니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “to serve the interests of your niece and your family, and I think I have established some slight claim to be favoured with your attention in return.”", "“조카분과 가문의 이익을 위해서지요. 그러니 그 대가로 어른의 주의를 좀 얻을 만한 자격은 조금이나마 갖추었다고 생각합니다.”"]], [["“Don’t bully me!” exclaimed Mr.", "“나를 몰아세우지 마세요!”"], ["Fairlie, falling back helplessly in the chair, and closing his eyes.", "페얼리 씨가 의자에 힘없이 파묻히며 눈을 감고 외쳤다. “제발 나를 몰아세우지 마세요."], ["“Please don’t bully me. I’m not strong enough.”", "나는 그럴 만큼 튼튼하지 못합니다.”"]], [["I was determined not to let him provoke me, for Laura Fairlie’s sake.", "나는 로라 페얼리를 생각해서, 그의 도발에 넘어가지 않기로 작정했다."]], [["“My object,”", "“제 목적은,”"], ["I went on, “is to entreat you to reconsider your letter, and not to force me to abandon the just rights of your niece, and of all who belong to her.", "내가 말을 이었다. “보내신 편지를 다시 생각해 주십사 간청하는 것, 그리고 조카분과 그분에게 속한 모든 이의 정당한 권리를 제가 포기하도록 강요하지 말아 주십사 하는 것입니다."], ["Let me state the case to you once more, and for the last time.”", "이 사안을 한 번만 더, 이것을 마지막으로 말씀드리게 해 주십시오.”"]], [["Mr. Fairlie shook his head and sighed piteously.", "페얼리 씨는 고개를 내젓고는 애처롭게 한숨을 쉬었다."]], [["“This is heartless of you, Gilmore—very heartless,”", "“길모어, 그건 참으로 매정한 처사요—몹시 매정해.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Never mind, go on.”", "“뭐, 괜찮습니다. 계속하시지요.”"]], [["I put all the points to him carefully—I set the matter before him in every conceivable light.", "나는 요점을 하나하나 신중히 짚어 주었다—생각할 수 있는 온갖 각도에서 이 문제를 그 앞에 펼쳐 보였다."], ["He lay back in the chair the whole time I was speaking with his eyes closed.", "내가 말하는 내내 그는 눈을 감은 채 의자에 기대어 있었다."], ["When I had done he opened them indolently, took his silver smelling-bottle from the table, and sniffed at it with an air of gentle relish.", "내 말이 끝나자 그는 나른하게 눈을 뜨더니 탁자에서 은제 각성제 병을 집어 들고 부드러운 만족감을 담아 킁킁 냄새를 맡았다."]], [["“Good Gilmore!”", "“착한 길모어!”"], ["he said between the sniffs, “how very nice this is of you!", "그가 냄새를 맡는 틈틈이 말했다. “이 얼마나 고마운 일인지!"], ["How you reconcile one to human nature!”", "당신 덕에 내가 인간이라는 존재와 화해하게 되는군요!”"]], [["“Give me a plain answer to a plain question, Mr. Fairlie.", "“페얼리 씨, 분명한 물음에는 분명한 답을 주십시오."], ["I tell you again, Sir Percival Glyde has no shadow of a claim to expect more than the income of the money.", "다시 말씀드립니다만, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게는 그 돈의 수입 이상을 바랄 근거가 털끝만큼도 없습니다."], ["The money itself if your niece has no children, ought to be under her control, and to return to her family.", "조카분에게 자식이 없다면 원금 자체는 그분의 관리 아래 있어야 하고, 그분의 가문으로 되돌아와야 마땅합니다."], ["If you stand firm, Sir Percival must give way—he must give way, I tell you, or he exposes himself to the base imputation of marrying Miss Fairlie entirely from mercenary motives.”", "어른께서 뜻을 굽히지 않으시면 퍼시벌 경은 양보할 수밖에 없습니다—양보할 수밖에 없어요, 정말입니다. 아니면 순전히 돈을 노리고 페얼리 양과 혼인한다는 비열한 혐의를 스스로 뒤집어쓰게 되겠지요.”"]], [["Mr. Fairlie shook the silver smelling-bottle at me playfully.", "페얼리 씨는 은제 각성제 병을 장난스럽게 내 쪽으로 흔들어 보였다."]], [["“You dear old Gilmore, how you do hate rank and family, don’t you?", "“친애하는 길모어 노인장, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게도 지체와 가문을 싫어하십니까?"], ["How you detest Glyde because he happens to be a baronet.", "글라이드가 하필 준남작이라는 이유로 어쩌면 그렇게도 그를 미워하십니까?"], ["What a Radical you are—oh, dear me, what a Radical you are!”", "당신은 어쩌면 그리도 급진파인지—오, 이런, 어쩌면 그리도 급진파인지!”"]], [["A Radical!!!", "급진파라니!!!"], ["I could put up with a good deal of provocation, but, after holding the soundest Conservative principles all my life, I could NOT put up with being called a Radical.", "나는 웬만한 도발은 다 참아 넘길 수 있었으나, 평생 가장 건전한 보수주의 원칙을 지켜 온 사람으로서 급진파라 불리는 것만은 도저히 참을 수 없었다."], ["My blood boiled at it—I started out of my chair—I was speechless with Indignation.", "피가 끓어올랐다—나는 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다—분노로 말문이 막혔다."]], [["“Don’t shake the room!” cried Mr.", "“방을 흔들지 마세요!”"], ["Fairlie—“for Heaven’s sake don’t shake the room!", "페얼리 씨가 소리쳤다. “제발 좀, 방을 흔들지 마세요!"], ["Worthiest of all possible Gilmores, I meant no offence.", "세상 모든 길모어 가운데 가장 훌륭한 분이여, 나는 기분 상하게 할 뜻이 없었습니다."], ["My own views are so extremely liberal that I think I am a Radical myself.", "내 견해야말로 워낙 자유주의적이어서 나 자신도 급진파가 아닌가 싶은걸요. 그래요."], ["Yes. We are a pair of Radicals.", "우리는 한 쌍의 급진파입니다."], ["Please don’t be angry.", "부디 화내지 마세요."], ["I can’t quarrel—I haven’t stamina enough.", "나는 다툴 수가 없습니다—그럴 기력이 없어요."], ["Shall we drop the subject? Yes.", "이 이야기는 그만두는 게 어떻습니까?"], ["Come and look at these sweet etchings.", "그래요. 이리 와서 이 어여쁜 동판화들을 좀 보시지요."], ["Do let me teach you to understand the heavenly pearliness of these lines.", "이 선들의 천상 같은 진줏빛을 이해하는 법을 내가 가르쳐 드리게 해 주세요."], ["Do now, there’s a good Gilmore!”", "어서요, 착한 길모어!”"]], [["While he was maundering on in this way I was, fortunately for my own self-respect, returning to my senses.", "그가 이렇게 넋두리를 늘어놓는 동안, 다행히도 내 자존심을 위해서는, 나는 제정신을 되찾고 있었다."], ["When I spoke again I was composed enough to treat his impertinence with the silent contempt that it deserved.", "다시 입을 열었을 때 나는 그의 무례를 마땅히 받아야 할 침묵의 경멸로 대할 만큼 침착해져 있었다."]], [["“You are entirely wrong, sir,” I said, “in supposing that I speak from any prejudice against Sir Percival Glyde.", "“어르신, 제가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 어떤 편견에서 이런 말씀을 드린다고 여기신다면 그건 전적으로 잘못 아신 겁니다.”"], ["I may regret that he has so unreservedly resigned himself in this matter to his lawyer’s direction as to make any appeal to himself impossible, but I am not prejudiced against him.", "내가 말했다. “그가 이 문제를 제 변호사의 지시에 그토록 남김없이 내맡겨 본인에게는 어떤 호소도 할 수 없게 만든 점은 유감스러울지 모르나, 저는 그에게 편견을 품고 있지 않습니다."], ["What I have said would equally apply to any other man in his situation, high or low.", "제가 드린 말씀은 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 누구에게도, 신분이 높든 낮든 똑같이 적용될 것입니다."], ["The principle I maintain is a recognised principle.", "제가 지키는 원칙은 널리 인정된 원칙입니다."], ["If you were to apply at the nearest town here, to the first respectable solicitor you could find, he would tell you as a stranger what I tell you as a friend.", "여기서 가장 가까운 읍내에 가셔서 처음 만나는 점잖은 사무변호사에게 물어보십시오. 그는 남남의 처지에서, 제가 벗의 처지에서 드리는 말씀과 똑같은 말을 해 드릴 것입니다."], ["He would inform you that it is against all rule to abandon the lady’s money entirely to the man she marries.", "그는 신부의 재산을 그가 혼인할 남자에게 통째로 넘기는 것은 모든 원칙에 어긋난다고 일러 줄 것입니다."], ["He would decline, on grounds of common legal caution, to give the husband, under any circumstances whatever, an interest of twenty thousand pounds in his wife’s death.”", "그리고 법률가로서 마땅히 지켜야 할 조심성을 근거로, 어떤 경우에도 남편에게 아내의 죽음으로 2만 파운드를 얻는 이해관계를 안겨 주는 일은 거절할 것입니다.”"]], [["“Would he really, Gilmore?”", "“정말 그럴까요, 길모어?”"], ["said Mr. Fairlie.", "페얼리 씨가 말했다."], ["“If he said anything half so horrid, I do assure you I should tinkle my bell for Louis, and have him sent out of the house immediately.”", "“그 절반이라도 그런 끔찍한 말을 지껄인다면, 나는 당장 종을 딸랑거려 루이를 불러서 그자를 집 밖으로 내쫓아 버릴 겁니다. 정말입니다.”"]], [["“You shall not irritate me, Mr. Fairlie—for your niece’s sake and for her father’s sake, you shall not irritate me.", "“페얼리 씨, 저를 화나게 하지 못하실 겁니다—조카분을 위해서, 또 그분 아버님을 위해서, 저를 화나게 하지 못하실 겁니다."], ["You shall take the whole responsibility of this discreditable settlement on your own shoulders before I leave the room.”", "제가 이 방을 나서기 전에, 이 떳떳지 못한 재산 설정의 책임을 어른께서 온전히 어깨에 지셔야 합니다.”"]], [["“Don’t!—now please don’t!”", "“그러지 마세요!—제발 그러지 마세요!”"], ["said Mr. Fairlie.", "페얼리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Think how precious your time is, Gilmore, and don’t throw it away.", "“길모어, 당신 시간이 얼마나 귀한지 생각하셔서 그것을 헛되이 버리지 마세요."], ["I would dispute with you if I could, but I can’t—I haven’t stamina enough.", "할 수만 있다면 나도 당신과 논쟁하겠지만, 그럴 수가 없습니다—그럴 기력이 없어요."], ["You want to upset me, to upset yourself, to upset Glyde, and to upset Laura; and—oh, dear me!—all for the sake of the very last thing in the world that is likely to happen.", "당신은 나를 뒤집어 놓고, 당신 자신을 뒤집어 놓고, 글라이드를 뒤집어 놓고, 로라까지 뒤집어 놓으려 하는군요. 게다가—오, 이런!—그 모든 게 세상에서 일어날 법하기로 가장 마지막인 일 때문이라니."], ["No, dear friend—in the interests of peace and quietness, positively No!”", "아니요, 친애하는 벗이여—평화와 안온을 위하여, 단연코 아니요!”"]], [["“I am to understand, then, that you hold by the determination expressed in your letter?”", "“그렇다면 편지에 밝히신 결정을 그대로 고수하시겠다는 뜻으로 알아도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, please.", "“그래요, 부디."], ["So glad we understand each other at last.", "마침내 서로 이해하게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지."], ["Sit down again—do!”", "자, 다시 앉으시지요—어서요!”"]], [["I walked at once to the door, and Mr. Fairlie resignedly “tinkled”", "나는 곧장 문으로 걸어갔고, 페얼리 씨는 체념한 듯 손종을 “딸랑거렸다”."], ["his hand-bell. Before I left the room I turned round and addressed him for the last time.", "방을 나서기 전에 나는 몸을 돌려 그에게 마지막으로 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Whatever happens in the future, sir,”", "“어르신, 앞으로 무슨 일이 벌어지든,”"], ["I said, “remember that my plain duty of warning you has been performed.", "내가 말했다. “경고를 드려야 할 제 마땅한 의무는 다했다는 것만은 기억해 주십시오."], ["As the faithful friend and servant of your family, I tell you, at parting, that no daughter of mine should be married to any man alive under such a settlement as you are forcing me to make for Miss Fairlie.”", "이 가문의 충실한 벗이자 종복으로서, 헤어지며 말씀드립니다. 어른께서 페얼리 양을 위해 저더러 억지로 작성하게 하시는 그런 재산 설정 아래라면, 제게 딸이 있다 한들 세상 어떤 남자와도 혼인시키지 않을 것입니다.”"]], [["The door opened behind me, and the valet stood waiting on the threshold.", "등 뒤에서 문이 열리고, 시종이 문턱에서 기다리며 서 있었다."]], [["“Louis,”", "“루이,”"], ["said Mr. Fairlie, “show Mr.", "페얼리 씨가 말했다."], ["Gilmore out, and then come back and hold up my etchings for me again.", "“길모어 씨를 배웅해 드리고 나서 다시 와서 내 동판화를 받쳐 들어라."], ["Make them give you a good lunch downstairs.", "아래층에서 점심을 잘 차려 주라고 해라."], ["Do, Gilmore, make my idle beasts of servants give you a good lunch!”", "길모어, 제발 저 게으른 짐승 같은 하인들에게 점심을 잘 대접하라고 시키세요!”"]], [["I was too much disgusted to reply—I turned on my heel, and left him in silence.", "나는 너무나 역겨워 대꾸할 수도 없었다—발꿈치를 돌려 말없이 그를 남겨 두고 나왔다."], ["There was an up train at two o’clock in the afternoon, and by that train I returned to London.", "오후 두 시에 상행 열차가 있었고, 나는 그 기차로 런던에 돌아왔다."]], [["On the Tuesday I sent in the altered settlement, which practically disinherited the very persons whom Miss Fairlie’s own lips had informed me she was most anxious to benefit.", "화요일에 나는 수정된 재산 설정서를 제출했다. 그것은 페얼리 양이 제 입으로 가장 혜택을 주고 싶다고 내게 일러 준 바로 그 사람들의 상속권을 사실상 빼앗는 문서였다."], ["I had no choice.", "나로서는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었다."], ["Another lawyer would have drawn up the deed if I had refused to undertake it.", "내가 맡기를 거절했다면 다른 변호사가 그 증서를 작성했을 테니까."]], [["My task is done.", "내 소임은 끝났다."], ["My personal share in the events of the family story extends no farther than the point which I have just reached.", "이 가족 이야기의 사건들에서 내가 개인적으로 맡은 몫은 방금 이른 지점을 넘어가지 않는다."], ["Other pens than mine will describe the strange circumstances which are now shortly to follow.", "이제 곧 뒤따를 기이한 정황들은 내 것 아닌 다른 이들의 붓이 그려 낼 것이다."], ["Seriously and sorrowfully I close this brief record.", "나는 무겁고 슬픈 마음으로 이 짧은 기록을 닫는다."], ["Seriously and sorrowfully I repeat here the parting words that I spoke at Limmeridge House:—No daughter of mine should have been married to any man alive under such a settlement as I was compelled to make for Laura Fairlie.", "무겁고 슬픈 마음으로, 내가 리머리지 하우스에서 남기고 온 작별의 말을 여기 다시 적는다.—내가 로라 페얼리를 위해 어쩔 수 없이 작성해야 했던 그런 재산 설정 아래라면, 내게 딸이 있다 한들 세상 어떤 남자와도 혼인시키지 않았을 것이다."]], [["The End of Mr. Gilmore’s Narrative.", "길모어 씨의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다."]], [["(in Extracts from her Diary)", "(그녀의 일기에서 발췌한 것)"]], [["[1] The passages omitted, here and elsewhere, in Miss Halcombe’s Diary are only those which bear no reference to Miss Fairlie or to any of the persons with whom she is associated in these pages.", "[1] 여기와 그 밖의 곳에서 홀콤 양의 일기 가운데 생략한 대목들은, 페얼리 양이나 이 지면에서 그녀와 관계를 맺는 인물들과 아무런 관련이 없는 부분들뿐이다."]], [["This morning Mr. Gilmore left us.", "오늘 아침 길모어 씨가 우리를 떠났다."]], [["His interview with Laura had evidently grieved and surprised him more than he liked to confess.", "로라와 나눈 면담이 그에게 스스로 인정하고 싶은 것보다 훨씬 큰 슬픔과 놀라움을 안긴 것이 분명했다."], ["I felt afraid, from his look and manner when we parted, that she might have inadvertently betrayed to him the real secret of her depression and my anxiety.", "헤어질 때의 그 표정과 태도로 보아, 로라가 무심결에 자기 우울의 진짜 비밀과 나의 근심거리를 그에게 흘려 버린 것은 아닐까 두려웠다."], ["This doubt grew on me so, after he had gone, that I declined riding out with Sir Percival, and went up to Laura’s room instead.", "그가 떠난 뒤 이 의심이 어찌나 커지던지, 나는 퍼시벌 경과 말을 타러 나가자는 청을 거절하고 대신 로라의 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["I have been sadly distrustful of myself, in this difficult and lamentable matter, ever since I found out my own ignorance of the strength of Laura’s unhappy attachment.", "로라의 그 불행한 연정이 얼마나 깊은지 내가 전혀 모르고 있었다는 사실을 깨달은 뒤로, 나는 이 곤란하고 개탄스러운 문제에서 나 자신을 몹시도 믿지 못하게 되었다."], ["I ought to have known that the delicacy and forbearance and sense of honour which drew me to poor Hartright, and made me so sincerely admire and respect him, were just the qualities to appeal most irresistibly to Laura’s natural sensitiveness and natural generosity of nature.", "나를 가엾은 하트라이트에게 이끌었고 그를 진심으로 존경하고 흠모하게 만들었던 그 섬세함과 인내와 명예심이야말로, 로라의 타고난 감수성과 타고난 너그러움에 가장 거스를 수 없이 호소하는 자질이라는 것을 나는 진작 알았어야 했다."], ["And yet, until she opened her heart to me of her own accord, I had no suspicion that this new feeling had taken root so deeply.", "그런데도 로라가 제 발로 내게 마음을 열기 전까지, 나는 이 새로운 감정이 그토록 깊이 뿌리를 내렸으리라고는 짐작조차 못 했다."], ["I once thought time and care might remove it.", "한때 나는 시간과 보살핌이 그것을 걷어 가리라 여겼다."], ["I now fear that it will remain with her and alter her for life.", "이제는 그것이 로라에게 남아 그 아이의 평생을 바꾸어 놓지나 않을까 두렵다."], ["The discovery that I have committed such an error in judgment as this makes me hesitate about everything else.", "내가 이만한 판단 착오를 저질렀다는 발견은 다른 모든 일에 대해서도 나를 망설이게 만든다."], ["I hesitate about Sir Percival, in the face of the plainest proofs.", "나는 더없이 명백한 증거들 앞에서도 퍼시벌 경에 대해 망설인다."], ["I hesitate even in speaking to Laura.", "로라에게 말을 거는 일조차 망설인다."], ["On this very morning I doubted, with my hand on the door, whether I should ask her the questions I had come to put, or not.", "바로 오늘 아침에도 문고리에 손을 얹은 채, 물으러 온 것을 물어야 할지 말아야 할지 망설였다."]], [["When I went into her room I found her walking up and down in great impatience.", "방에 들어서니 로라는 몹시 초조한 얼굴로 서성이고 있었다."], ["She looked flushed and excited, and she came forward at once, and spoke to me before I could open my lips.", "뺨은 상기되고 흥분한 기색이었으며, 곧바로 앞으로 나와 내가 입을 열기도 전에 먼저 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I wanted you,”", "“언니를 기다렸어요.”"], ["she said.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“Come and sit down on the sofa with me.", "“이리 와서 나랑 소파에 앉아요."], ["Marian!", "매리언!"], ["I can bear this no longer—I must and will end it.”", "나 더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없어요—반드시 끝내야 하고, 끝낼 거예요.”"]], [["There was too much colour in her cheeks, too much energy in her manner, too much firmness in her voice.", "뺨에는 핏기가 지나쳤고, 태도에는 기운이 지나쳤고, 목소리에는 단호함이 지나쳤다."], ["The little book of Hartright’s drawings—the fatal book that she will dream over whenever she is alone—was in one of her hands.", "하트라이트의 그림이 담긴 작은 화첩—혼자 있을 때마다 로라가 꿈꾸듯 들여다보는 그 운명의 화첩—이 한쪽 손에 들려 있었다."], ["I began by gently and firmly taking it from her, and putting it out of sight on a side-table.", "나는 우선 부드럽되 단호하게 그것을 빼앗아 곁상 위, 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에 놓았다."]], [["“Tell me quietly, my darling, what you wish to do,”", "“얘야, 어떻게 하고 싶은지 차분히 말해 보렴.”"], ["I said. “Has Mr.", "내가 말했다."], ["Gilmore been advising you?”", "“길모어 씨가 무슨 조언이라도 하셨니?”"]], [["She shook her head.", "로라는 고개를 저었다."], ["“No, not in what I am thinking of now.", "“아니요, 지금 제가 생각하는 일에 대해서는요."], ["He was very kind and good to me, Marian, and I am ashamed to say I distressed him by crying.", "그분은 제게 아주 친절하고 좋으셨어요, 매리언. 부끄럽지만 제가 울어서 그분을 괴롭게 해 드렸죠."], ["I am miserably helpless—I can’t control myself.", "저는 비참할 만큼 무력해요—제 자신을 다스리질 못하겠어요."], ["For my own sake, and for all our sakes, I must have courage enough to end it.”", "저를 위해서도, 우리 모두를 위해서도, 이걸 끝낼 만한 용기를 내야 해요.”"]], [["“Do you mean courage enough to claim your release?”", "“파혼을 요구할 용기 말이니?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니요.”"], ["she said simply.", "로라가 담담히 말했다."], ["“Courage, dear, to tell the truth.”", "“언니, 진실을 말할 용기요.”"]], [["She put her arms round my neck, and rested her head quietly on my bosom.", "로라는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고 내 가슴에 조용히 머리를 기댔다."], ["On the opposite wall hung the miniature portrait of her father.", "맞은편 벽에는 아버지의 세밀 초상화가 걸려 있었다."], ["I bent over her, and saw that she was looking at it while her head lay on my breast.", "나는 그 아이 위로 몸을 숙이다가, 내 가슴에 머리를 얹은 채 그 초상을 바라보고 있음을 보았다."]], [["“I can never claim my release from my engagement,”", "“저는 결코 약혼에서 놓아 달라 요구할 수 없어요.”"], ["she went on.", "로라가 말을 이었다."], ["“Whatever way it ends it must end wretchedly for me.", "“어느 쪽으로 끝나든 제게는 비참하게 끝날 수밖에 없어요."], ["All I can do, Marian, is not to add the remembrance that I have broken my promise and forgotten my father’s dying words, to make that wretchedness worse.”", "매리언, 제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 그 비참함을 더 무겁게 만들 것—제가 약속을 깨뜨렸고 아버지의 임종 유언을 잊었다는 기억—만은 보태지 않는 것뿐이에요.”"]], [["“What is it you propose, then?”", "“그럼 어떻게 하겠다는 거니?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“To tell Sir Percival Glyde the truth with my own lips,”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경께 제 입으로 진실을 말씀드리는 거예요.” 로라가 대답했다."], ["she answered, “and to let him release me, if he will, not because I ask him, but because he knows all.”", "“그리고 그분이 원하신다면 저를 놓아주시도록 하는 거죠. 제가 청해서가 아니라, 모든 걸 아셨기 때문에요.”"]], [["“What do you mean, Laura, by ‘all’?", "“로라, ‘모든 걸’이라니 무슨 뜻이니?"], ["Sir Percival will know enough (he has told me so himself) if he knows that the engagement is opposed to your own wishes.”", "퍼시벌 경은 이 약혼이 네 뜻에 어긋난다는 것만 알면 충분히 안 셈이야(본인이 내게 직접 그렇게 말했단다).”"]], [["“Can I tell him that, when the engagement was made for me by my father, with my own consent?", "“아버지가 제 동의를 얻어 저를 위해 맺어 주신 약혼인데, 제가 어떻게 그런 말을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["I should have kept my promise, not happily, I am afraid, but still contentedly—”", "저는 약속을 지켰을 거예요, 행복하게는 아니었겠지만 그래도 만족하며—”"], ["she stopped, turned her face to me, and laid her cheek close against mine—“I should have kept my engagement, Marian, if another love had not grown up in my heart, which was not there when I first promised to be Sir Percival’s wife.”", "로라는 말을 멈추고 내 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리더니 제 뺨을 내 뺨에 바싹 붙였다. “매리언, 처음 퍼시벌 경의 아내가 되겠다고 약속했을 때는 없었던 또 다른 사랑이 제 마음속에 자라나지만 않았다면, 저는 그 약혼을 지켰을 거예요.”"]], [["“Laura!", "“로라!"], ["you will never lower yourself by making a confession to him?”", "그에게 고백을 해서 너 자신을 낮추는 짓은 절대로 하지 않을 거지?”"]], [["“I shall lower myself, indeed, if I gain my release by hiding from him what he has a right to know.”", "“그분이 알 권리가 있는 것을 감춘 채 놓여난다면, 그거야말로 정말 제 자신을 낮추는 일이에요.”"]], [["“He has not the shadow of a right to know it!”", "“그에게는 그걸 알 권리가 털끝만큼도 없어!”"]], [["“Wrong, Marian, wrong!", "“아니에요, 매리언, 그건 틀렸어요!"], ["I ought to deceive no one—least of all the man to whom my father gave me, and to whom I gave myself.”", "저는 누구도 속여서는 안 돼요—무엇보다도 아버지가 저를 맡기셨고 제가 제 자신을 드린 그분을 속여서는 안 되죠.”"], ["She put her lips to mine, and kissed me.", "로라는 제 입술을 내 입술에 대고 입을 맞추었다."], ["“My own love,”", "“내 사랑하는 언니,”"], ["she said softly, “you are so much too fond of me, and so much too proud of me, that you forget, in my case, what you would remember in your own.", "로라가 나직이 말했다. “언니는 나를 너무 아끼고 나를 너무 자랑스러워해서, 언니 자신의 일이라면 잊지 않았을 것을 내 일에서는 잊어버리는 거예요."], ["Better that Sir Percival should doubt my motives, and misjudge my conduct if he will, than that I should be first false to him in thought, and then mean enough to serve my own interests by hiding the falsehood.”", "퍼시벌 경이 제 동기를 의심하고 원한다면 제 행실을 그르게 판단하시더라도, 제가 먼저 마음속으로 그분을 배신하고 그다음엔 그 배신을 감춰 제 잇속을 챙길 만큼 비열해지는 것보다는 낫잖아요.”"]], [["I held her away from me in astonishment.", "나는 놀라서 그 아이를 내게서 떼어 놓고 바라보았다."], ["For the first time in our lives we had changed places—the resolution was all on her side, the hesitation all on mine.", "우리가 살아오면서 처음으로 자리가 바뀌었다—결심은 온통 저쪽에, 망설임은 온통 이쪽에 있었다."], ["I looked into the pale, quiet, resigned young face—I saw the pure, innocent heart, in the loving eyes that looked back at me—and the poor worldly cautions and objections that rose to my lips dwindled and died away in their own emptiness.", "나는 창백하고 고요하고 체념한 젊은 얼굴을 들여다보았다—나를 마주 보는 사랑 어린 눈 속에서 순결하고 티 없는 마음을 보았다—그러자 입술까지 올라왔던 초라한 세속의 조심성과 반론들이 제 스스로의 공허함 속에서 오그라들어 사그라졌다."], ["I hung my head in silence.", "나는 말없이 고개를 떨구었다."], ["In her place the despicably small pride which makes so many women deceitful would have been my pride, and would have made me deceitful too.", "그 아이의 처지였다면, 수많은 여자를 거짓되게 만드는 그 경멸스러울 만큼 하찮은 자존심이 곧 나의 자존심이었을 테고, 나 역시 거짓된 사람이 되었으리라."]], [["“Don’t be angry with me, Marian,”", "“매리언, 화내지 말아요.”"], ["she said, mistaking my silence.", "내 침묵을 오해한 로라가 말했다."]], [["I only answered by drawing her close to me again.", "나는 그저 그 아이를 다시 끌어당겨 안는 것으로 대답했다."], ["I was afraid of crying if I spoke.", "입을 열면 울음이 터질까 두려웠다."], ["My tears do not flow so easily as they ought—they come almost like men’s tears, with sobs that seem to tear me in pieces, and that frighten every one about me.", "내 눈물은 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 쉽게 흐르지 않는다—거의 남자의 눈물처럼, 몸을 갈가리 찢는 듯한 흐느낌과 함께 터져 나와 주위 사람들을 모두 놀라게 한다."]], [["“I have thought of this, love, for many days,”", "“나는 여러 날 이 일을 생각했어요, 언니.”"], ["she went on, twining and twisting my hair with that childish restlessness in her fingers, which poor Mrs.", "로라가 손가락으로 내 머리카락을 감았다 풀었다 하며 말을 이었다. 그 어린애 같은 손버릇은 가엾은 베지 부인이 지금도 그토록 참을성 있게, 그러나 그토록 헛되이 고쳐 주려 애쓰는 것이었다."], ["Vesey still tries so patiently and so vainly to cure her of—“I have thought of it very seriously, and I can be sure of my courage when my own conscience tells me I am right.", "“아주 진지하게 생각했어요. 그리고 제 양심이 옳다고 말해 줄 때는 제 용기를 믿을 수 있어요."], ["Let me speak to him to-morrow—in your presence, Marian.", "내일 그분께 말하게 해 줘요—매리언, 언니가 있는 자리에서요."], ["I will say nothing that is wrong, nothing that you or I need be ashamed of—but, oh, it will ease my heart so to end this miserable concealment!", "그르다 할 말은 하나도 하지 않을게요. 언니나 제가 부끄러워할 말도 하나도요—하지만, 아, 이 비참한 숨김을 끝내면 제 마음이 얼마나 가벼워질까요!"], ["Only let me know and feel that I have no deception to answer for on my side, and then, when he has heard what I have to say, let him act towards me as he will.”", "제 쪽에서는 책임져야 할 기만이 하나도 없다는 것만 알고 느끼게 해 주세요. 그러고 나서, 제가 드릴 말씀을 그분이 다 들으시고 나면, 저를 어떻게 대하시든 그분 뜻대로 하시게 하고요.”"]], [["She sighed, and put her head back in its old position on my bosom.", "로라는 한숨을 쉬고는 다시 내 가슴의 예전 자리에 머리를 뉘었다."], ["Sad misgivings about what the end would be weighed upon my mind, but still distrusting myself, I told her that I would do as she wished.", "끝이 어찌 될지 모른다는 슬픈 불안이 내 마음을 짓눌렀으나, 여전히 나 자신을 믿지 못하는 터라 그 아이가 바라는 대로 하겠다고 말했다."], ["She thanked me, and we passed gradually into talking of other things.", "로라는 고맙다고 했고, 우리는 차츰 다른 이야기로 넘어갔다."]], [["At dinner she joined us again, and was more easy and more herself with Sir Percival than I have seen her yet.", "저녁 식사에 로라는 다시 우리와 자리를 함께했고, 퍼시벌 경을 대하는 태도가 지금까지 내가 본 어느 때보다 편안하고 제 본모습에 가까웠다."], ["In the evening she went to the piano, choosing new music of the dexterous, tuneless, florid kind.", "저녁에는 피아노 앞에 앉아 손재주만 요란하고 가락은 없이 화려하기만 한 새 악곡들을 골랐다."], ["The lovely old melodies of Mozart, which poor Hartright was so fond of, she has never played since he left.", "가엾은 하트라이트가 그토록 좋아하던 모차르트의 그 어여쁜 옛 선율들은 그가 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 치지 않았다."], ["The book is no longer in the music-stand.", "그 악보집은 이제 보면대에 없다."], ["She took the volume away herself, so that nobody might find it out and ask her to play from it.", "누가 발견하고 그것으로 연주해 달라 청할까 봐 로라가 손수 치워 버린 것이다."]], [["I had no opportunity of discovering whether her purpose of the morning had changed or not, until she wished Sir Percival good-night—and then her own words informed me that it was unaltered.", "로라가 퍼시벌 경에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넬 때까지, 나는 아침의 그 결심이 바뀌었는지 어떤지 알아볼 기회가 없었다—그리고 그때 그 아이의 말이 결심이 그대로임을 내게 일러 주었다."], ["She said, very quietly, that she wished to speak to him after breakfast, and that he would find her in her sitting-room with me.", "로라는 아주 조용히, 아침 식사 뒤에 드릴 말씀이 있으며 자기 거실에서 나와 함께 기다리고 있겠노라고 말했다."], ["He changed colour at those words, and I felt his hand trembling a little when it came to my turn to take it.", "그 말에 퍼시벌 경은 낯빛이 변했고, 내 차례가 되어 그의 손을 잡았을 때 나는 그 손이 조금 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The event of the next morning would decide his future life, and he evidently knew it.", "이튿날 아침의 일이 그의 앞날을 결정지을 터였고, 그도 분명 그것을 알고 있었다."]], [["I went in, as usual, through the door between our two bedrooms, to bid Laura good-night before she went to sleep.", "나는 여느 때처럼 우리 두 침실 사이의 문으로 들어가, 잠들기 전의 로라에게 잘 자라는 인사를 하려 했다."], ["In stooping over her to kiss her I saw the little book of Hartright’s drawings half hidden under her pillow, just in the place where she used to hide her favourite toys when she was a child.", "입을 맞추려 몸을 숙이다가 나는 하트라이트의 그림이 담긴 작은 화첩이 베개 밑에 반쯤 숨겨져 있는 것을 보았다. 어릴 적 가장 아끼던 장난감을 감추어 두던 바로 그 자리였다."], ["I could not find it in my heart to say anything, but I pointed to the book and shook my head.", "차마 무슨 말을 할 마음이 나지 않아, 나는 그저 화첩을 가리키며 고개를 저었다."], ["She reached both hands up to my cheeks, and drew my face down to hers till our lips met.", "로라는 두 손을 뻗어 내 두 뺨을 감싸더니, 우리 입술이 닿을 때까지 내 얼굴을 제 얼굴 쪽으로 끌어내렸다."]], [["“Leave it there to-night,”", "“오늘 밤만은 거기 두게 해 줘요.”"], ["she whispered; “to-morrow may be cruel, and may make me say good-bye to it for ever.”", "로라가 속삭였다. “내일은 잔인할지도 모르고, 어쩌면 제가 이것과 영영 작별하게 만들지도 모르니까요.”"]], [["9th.—The first event of the morning was not of a kind to raise my spirits—a letter arrived for me from poor Walter Hartright.", "9일.—오늘 아침의 첫 소식은 내 기운을 북돋아 줄 만한 것이 못 되었다—가엾은 월터 하트라이트에게서 편지가 온 것이다."], ["It is the answer to mine describing the manner in which Sir Percival cleared himself of the suspicions raised by Anne Catherick’s letter.", "앤 캐서릭의 편지가 불러일으킨 의심을 퍼시벌 경이 어떻게 벗어 버렸는지 적어 보낸 내 편지에 대한 답장이었다."], ["He writes shortly and bitterly about Sir Percival’s explanations, only saying that he has no right to offer an opinion on the conduct of those who are above him.", "그는 퍼시벌 경의 해명에 대해 짧고 씁쓸하게 쓰면서, 자기보다 윗자리에 있는 이들의 처신에 의견을 낼 권리가 자기에게는 없다는 말만 할 뿐이었다."], ["This is sad, but his occasional references to himself grieve me still more.", "그것도 서글픈 일이지만, 이따금 자기 자신에 대해 언급한 대목들이 나를 더욱 아프게 한다."], ["He says that the effort to return to his old habits and pursuits grows harder instead of easier to him every day and he implores me, if I have any interest, to exert it to get him employment that will necessitate his absence from England, and take him among new scenes and new people.", "그는 옛 습관과 일로 돌아가려는 노력이 날마다 쉬워지기는커녕 더 힘들어진다고 하면서, 내게 조금이라도 영향력이 있다면 그것을 써서 영국을 떠나야만 하는 일자리, 새로운 풍경과 새로운 사람들 속으로 자신을 데려가 줄 일자리를 구해 달라고 간청한다."], ["I have been made all the readier to comply with this request by a passage at the end of his letter, which has almost alarmed me.", "편지 끝의 한 대목이 나를 거의 경악하게 만든 탓에, 나는 이 청을 한결 더 기꺼이 들어주고 싶어졌다."]], [["After mentioning that he has neither seen nor heard anything of Anne Catherick, he suddenly breaks off, and hints in the most abrupt, mysterious manner, that he has been perpetually watched and followed by strange men ever since he returned to London.", "앤 캐서릭에 대해서는 보지도 듣지도 못했다고 밝힌 뒤, 그는 문득 말을 끊더니 런던에 돌아온 뒤로 낯선 사내들에게 줄곧 감시당하고 미행당하고 있다고 더없이 불쑥, 더없이 알 수 없는 투로 넌지시 내비친다."], ["He acknowledges that he cannot prove this extraordinary suspicion by fixing on any particular persons, but he declares that the suspicion itself is present to him night and day.", "이 기이한 의심을 특정한 인물을 짚어 증명할 수는 없다고 그도 인정하지만, 그 의심 자체는 밤낮으로 자기를 떠나지 않는다고 단언한다. 이것이 나를 겁먹게 했다."], ["This has frightened me, because it looks as if his one fixed idea about Laura was becoming too much for his mind.", "로라를 향한 그 하나의 고정된 생각이 그의 정신에 감당하기 힘든 짐이 되어 가는 듯 보이기 때문이다."], ["I will write immediately to some of my mother’s influential old friends in London, and press his claims on their notice.", "나는 곧바로 런던에 있는 어머니의 유력한 옛 벗들 몇 사람에게 편지를 써서 그의 처지를 눈여겨봐 달라고 힘써 부탁하려 한다."], ["Change of scene and change of occupation may really be the salvation of him at this crisis in his life.", "풍경이 바뀌고 일이 바뀌는 것이 그의 인생의 이 고비에서 정말로 그를 구원할지도 모른다."]], [["Greatly to my relief, Sir Percival sent an apology for not joining us at breakfast.", "무척 다행스럽게도, 퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사에 함께하지 못하겠다는 사과를 보내왔다."], ["He had taken an early cup of coffee in his own room, and he was still engaged there in writing letters.", "자기 방에서 일찍 커피를 한 잔 들었고 아직 그곳에서 편지를 쓰는 중이라는 것이었다."], ["At eleven o’clock, if that hour was convenient, he would do himself the honour of waiting on Miss Fairlie and Miss Halcombe.", "열한 시가 괜찮은 시각이라면 그때 페얼리 양과 홀콤 양을 찾아뵙는 영광을 누리겠노라고 했다."]], [["My eyes were on Laura’s face while the message was being delivered.", "전갈이 전해지는 동안 내 눈은 로라의 얼굴에 머물러 있었다."], ["I had found her unaccountably quiet and composed on going into her room in the morning, and so she remained all through breakfast.", "아침에 그 방에 들어갔을 때 나는 그 아이가 영문을 알 수 없을 만큼 고요하고 침착한 것을 보았는데, 아침 식사 내내 그대로였다."], ["Even when we were sitting together on the sofa in her room, waiting for Sir Percival, she still preserved her self-control.", "심지어 퍼시벌 경을 기다리며 그 방 소파에 나란히 앉아 있을 때에도 로라는 여전히 자제력을 잃지 않았다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid of me, Marian,”", "“매리언, 나를 걱정하지 말아요.”"], ["was all she said; “I may forget myself with an old friend like Mr.", "그 아이가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."], ["Gilmore, or with a dear sister like you, but I will not forget myself with Sir Percival Glyde.”", "“길모어 씨 같은 오랜 벗 앞이나 언니 같은 사랑하는 자매 앞에서는 제가 흐트러질지 몰라도, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 앞에서는 흐트러지지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["I looked at her, and listened to her in silent surprise.", "나는 말없이 놀라며 그 아이를 바라보고 그 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["Through all the years of our close intimacy this passive force in her character had been hidden from me—hidden even from herself, till love found it, and suffering called it forth.", "그토록 가까이 지낸 그 오랜 세월 내내 그 아이의 성품 속 이 조용한 힘은 내게 감추어져 있었다—사랑이 그것을 찾아내고 고통이 그것을 불러내기 전까지는 그 아이 자신에게도 감추어져 있었다."]], [["As the clock on the mantelpiece struck eleven Sir Percival knocked at the door and came in.", "벽난로 선반 위 시계가 열한 시를 칠 때 퍼시벌 경이 문을 두드리고 들어왔다."], ["There was suppressed anxiety and agitation in every line of his face.", "얼굴의 모든 선에 억눌린 불안과 동요가 서려 있었다."], ["The dry, sharp cough, which teases him at most times, seemed to be troubling him more incessantly than ever.", "평소에도 그를 괴롭히는 그 마르고 날카로운 기침이 어느 때보다 쉴 새 없이 그를 성가시게 하는 듯했다."], ["He sat down opposite to us at the table, and Laura remained by me.", "그는 탁자를 사이에 두고 우리 맞은편에 앉았고, 로라는 내 곁에 그대로 있었다."], ["I looked attentively at them both, and he was the palest of the two.", "나는 두 사람을 유심히 살펴보았는데, 둘 중 더 창백한 쪽은 그였다."]], [["He said a few unimportant words, with a visible effort to preserve his customary ease of manner.", "그는 여느 때의 편안한 태도를 지키려 애쓰는 기색을 뚜렷이 드러내며 대수롭지 않은 말 몇 마디를 했다."], ["But his voice was not to be steadied, and the restless uneasiness in his eyes was not to be concealed.", "그러나 목소리는 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았고, 눈에 어린 안절부절못하는 불안도 감추어지지 않았다."], ["He must have felt this himself, for he stopped in the middle of a sentence, and gave up even the attempt to hide his embarrassment any longer.", "그 자신도 그것을 느꼈던 모양인지, 문장 한가운데서 말을 멈추더니 당황을 더 감추려는 시도마저 그만두었다."]], [["There was just one moment of dead silence before Laura addressed him.", "로라가 그에게 말을 건네기 전, 딱 한순간 죽은 듯한 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“I wish to speak to you, Sir Percival,”", "“퍼시벌 경, 드릴 말씀이 있습니다.”"], ["she said, “on a subject that is very important to us both.", "로라가 말했다. “우리 두 사람 모두에게 매우 중요한 일에 관해서요."], ["My sister is here, because her presence helps me and gives me confidence.", "제 언니가 여기 있는 것은 언니가 있어야 제게 힘이 되고 용기가 나기 때문이에요."], ["She has not suggested one word of what I am going to say—I speak from my own thoughts, not from hers.", "제가 이제부터 드릴 말씀은 언니가 한마디도 일러 준 것이 아닙니다—제 생각에서 나온 말이지 언니 생각에서 나온 말이 아니에요."], ["I am sure you will be kind enough to understand that before I go any farther?”", "더 나아가기 전에 그 점을 너그러이 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다.”"]], [["Sir Percival bowed.", "퍼시벌 경이 고개를 숙였다."], ["She had proceeded thus far, with perfect outward tranquillity and perfect propriety of manner.", "여기까지 로라는 겉으로 완벽하게 평온하고 완벽하게 예의 바른 태도로 말을 이어 왔다."], ["She looked at him, and he looked at her.", "로라가 그를 보았고, 그도 로라를 보았다."], ["They seemed, at the outset, at least, resolved to understand one another plainly.", "적어도 처음에는, 두 사람 다 서로를 분명히 이해하기로 작정한 듯 보였다."]], [["“I have heard from Marian,”", "“매리언에게서 들었습니다.”"], ["she went on, “that I have only to claim my release from our engagement to obtain that release from you.", "로라가 말을 이었다. “제가 약혼에서 놓아 달라 청하기만 하면 경께서 놓아주시리라고요."], ["It was forbearing and generous on your part, Sir Percival, to send me such a message.", "퍼시벌 경, 제게 그런 전갈을 보내신 것은 너그럽고 관대한 처사였습니다."], ["It is only doing you justice to say that I am grateful for the offer, and I hope and believe that it is only doing myself justice to tell you that I decline to accept it.”", "그 제안이 고맙다고 말씀드리는 것은 경께 마땅한 도리를 다하는 일일 뿐이고, 그 제안을 받아들이지 않겠다고 말씀드리는 것 또한 제게 마땅한 도리를 다하는 일일 뿐이라고 저는 믿고 또 바랍니다.”"]], [["His attentive face relaxed a little.", "주의 깊던 그의 얼굴이 조금 풀렸다."], ["But I saw one of his feet, softly, quietly, incessantly beating on the carpet under the table, and I felt that he was secretly as anxious as ever.", "그러나 나는 그의 한쪽 발이 탁자 밑 양탄자를 부드럽게, 조용히, 쉴 새 없이 두드리고 있는 것을 보았고, 그가 속으로는 여전히 초조하다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I have not forgotten,”", "“저는 잊지 않았습니다.”"], ["she said, “that you asked my father’s permission before you honoured me with a proposal of marriage.", "로라가 말했다. “경께서 제게 청혼의 영광을 베푸시기 전에 아버지의 허락을 구하셨다는 것을요."], ["Perhaps you have not forgotten either what I said when I consented to our engagement?", "제가 약혼에 동의하면서 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지도 아마 잊지 않으셨겠지요?"], ["I ventured to tell you that my father’s influence and advice had mainly decided me to give you my promise.", "저는 감히, 경께 약속을 드리기로 마음을 정한 데는 아버지의 영향과 조언이 주된 몫을 했다고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I was guided by my father, because I had always found him the truest of all advisers, the best and fondest of all protectors and friends.", "저는 아버지의 인도를 따랐습니다. 아버지야말로 언제나 모든 조언자 가운데 가장 진실하고, 모든 보호자와 벗 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 자애로운 분임을 알았기 때문이에요."], ["I have lost him now—I have only his memory to love, but my faith in that dear dead friend has never been shaken.", "이제 아버지를 잃었고—사랑할 것이라곤 그 기억뿐이지만, 세상을 떠난 그 사랑하는 벗에 대한 제 믿음은 한 번도 흔들린 적이 없습니다."], ["I believe at this moment, as truly as I ever believed, that he knew what was best, and that his hopes and wishes ought to be my hopes and wishes too.”", "저는 지금 이 순간에도, 예전에 믿었던 것과 똑같이 진실하게 믿습니다. 아버지는 무엇이 최선인지 아셨고, 아버지의 소망과 바람이 곧 제 소망과 바람이어야 한다고요.”"]], [["Her voice trembled for the first time.", "로라의 목소리가 처음으로 떨렸다."], ["Her restless fingers stole their way into my lap, and held fast by one of my hands.", "가만있지 못하는 손가락들이 슬며시 내 무릎으로 들어와 내 한 손을 꼭 붙들었다."], ["There was another moment of silence, and then Sir Percival spoke.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 퍼시벌 경이 입을 열었다."]], [["“May I ask,”", "“여쭤도 되겠습니까,”"], ["he said, “if I have ever proved myself unworthy of the trust which it has been hitherto my greatest honour and greatest happiness to possess?”", "그가 말했다. “제가 지금까지 지닌 것 가운데 가장 큰 영광이자 가장 큰 행복이었던 그 신뢰에, 제가 언제고 값하지 못한 일이 있었습니까?”"]], [["“I have found nothing in your conduct to blame,”", "“경의 처신에서 나무랄 것이라고는 하나도 찾지 못했습니다.”"], ["she answered.", "로라가 대답했다."], ["“You have always treated me with the same delicacy and the same forbearance.", "“경께서는 언제나 한결같은 섬세함과 한결같은 인내로 저를 대해 주셨습니다."], ["You have deserved my trust, and, what is of far more importance in my estimation, you have deserved my father’s trust, out of which mine grew.", "경께서는 제 신뢰를 받으실 만했고, 제 눈에 그보다 훨씬 더 중요한 일로는, 제 신뢰가 그로부터 자라난 아버지의 신뢰까지 받으실 만했습니다."], ["You have given me no excuse, even if I had wanted to find one, for asking to be released from my pledge.", "설령 제가 구실을 찾고 싶었다 한들, 서약에서 놓아 달라 청할 구실을 경께서는 조금도 주지 않으셨습니다."], ["What I have said so far has been spoken with the wish to acknowledge my whole obligation to you.", "여기까지 드린 말씀은 경께 진 빚을 남김없이 인정하고 싶은 마음에서 나온 것입니다."], ["My regard for that obligation, my regard for my father’s memory, and my regard for my own promise, all forbid me to set the example, on my side, of withdrawing from our present position.", "그 빚에 대한 마음, 아버지의 기억에 대한 마음, 제 자신의 약속에 대한 마음, 그 모두가 지금 이 처지에서 물러서는 선례를 제 쪽에서 만드는 일을 금합니다."], ["The breaking of our engagement must be entirely your wish and your act, Sir Percival—not mine.”", "우리 약혼을 깨뜨리는 일은 온전히 경의 뜻이고 경의 행위여야 합니다, 퍼시벌 경—제 것이 아니라요.”"]], [["The uneasy beating of his foot suddenly stopped, and he leaned forward eagerly across the table.", "불안하게 두드리던 그의 발이 문득 멎었고, 그는 탁자 너머로 몸을 다급하게 앞으로 기울였다."]], [["“My act?” he said.", "“제 행위라고요?”"], ["“What reason can there be on my side for withdrawing?”", "그가 말했다. “제 쪽에서 물러설 무슨 이유가 있을 수 있단 말입니까?”"]], [["I heard her breath quickening—I felt her hand growing cold.", "로라의 숨결이 빨라지는 소리가 들렸다—그 손이 차가워지는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["In spite of what she had said to me when we were alone, I began to be afraid of her.", "우리 둘만 있을 때 내게 한 말에도 불구하고, 나는 그 아이가 걱정되기 시작했다."], ["I was wrong.", "그것은 내 잘못된 걱정이었다."]], [["“A reason that it is very hard to tell you,”", "“말씀드리기 몹시 어려운 이유입니다.”"], ["she answered.", "로라가 대답했다."], ["“There is a change in me, Sir Percival—a change which is serious enough to justify you, to yourself and to me, in breaking off our engagement.”", "“퍼시벌 경, 제게 변화가 있었습니다—경께서 경 자신에 대해서도 저에 대해서도 우리 약혼을 파기할 근거로 삼기에 충분할 만큼 중대한 변화가요.”"]], [["His face turned so pale again that even his lips lost their colour.", "그의 얼굴은 다시 어찌나 창백해졌던지 입술마저 핏기를 잃었다."], ["He raised the arm which lay on the table, turned a little away in his chair, and supported his head on his hand, so that his profile only was presented to us.", "그는 탁자에 놓여 있던 팔을 들어 의자에서 몸을 조금 옆으로 돌리고는 그 손에 머리를 기대어, 우리에게는 옆얼굴만 보이게 되었다."]], [["“What change?” he asked.", "“무슨 변화입니까?”"], ["The tone in which he put the question jarred on me—there was something painfully suppressed in it.", "그가 물었다. 그 물음을 던지는 어조가 내 귀에 거슬렸다—그 안에는 고통스럽도록 억눌린 무언가가 있었다."]], [["She sighed heavily, and leaned towards me a little, so as to rest her shoulder against mine.", "로라는 무겁게 한숨을 쉬고 내 쪽으로 조금 기대어 제 어깨를 내 어깨에 기댔다."], ["I felt her trembling, and tried to spare her by speaking myself.", "그 떨림이 느껴져 내가 대신 말해 주어 그 짐을 덜어 주려 했다."], ["She stopped me by a warning pressure of her hand, and then addressed Sir Percival one more, but this time without looking at him.", "로라는 내 손을 지그시 눌러 나를 만류하고는, 다시 한 번 퍼시벌 경에게 말을 건넸다. 다만 이번에는 그를 바라보지 않은 채였다."]], [["“I have heard,”", "“저는 이렇게 들었고, 또 그렇게 믿습니다.”"], ["she said, “and I believe it, that the fondest and truest of all affections is the affection which a woman ought to bear to her husband.", "로라가 말했다. “모든 애정 가운데 가장 깊고 가장 참된 것은 아내가 남편에게 품어야 할 애정이라고요."], ["When our engagement began that affection was mine to give, if I could, and yours to win, if you could.", "우리 약혼이 시작되었을 때, 그 애정은 제가 할 수만 있다면 드려야 할 것이었고 경께서 하실 수만 있다면 얻으셔야 할 것이었습니다."], ["Will you pardon me, and spare me, Sir Percival, if I acknowledge that it is not so any longer?”", "퍼시벌 경, 이제는 그렇지 않다고 제가 고백한다면, 저를 용서하시고 저를 너그러이 봐주시겠습니까?”"]], [["A few tears gathered in her eyes, and dropped over her cheeks slowly as she paused and waited for his answer.", "로라의 눈에 눈물이 조금 고이더니, 말을 멈추고 그의 대답을 기다리는 동안 뺨을 타고 천천히 흘러내렸다."], ["He did not utter a word.", "그는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["At the beginning of her reply he had moved the hand on which his head rested, so that it hid his face.", "로라가 대답을 시작할 무렵 그는 머리를 받치고 있던 손을 옮겨 얼굴을 가려 버렸다."], ["I saw nothing but the upper part of his figure at the table.", "내게는 탁자 위로 드러난 그 몸의 윗부분밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["Not a muscle of him moved.", "근육 하나 움직이지 않았다."], ["The fingers of the hand which supported his head were dented deep in his hair.", "머리를 받친 손의 손가락들은 머리카락 속에 깊이 파묻혀 있었다."], ["They might have expressed hidden anger or hidden grief—it was hard to say which—there was no significant trembling in them.", "그것은 감춘 분노를 나타낼 수도 있었고 감춘 슬픔을 나타낼 수도 있었다—어느 쪽인지 가늠하기 어려웠다—그 손가락에는 무언가를 알려 줄 만한 떨림이 없었다."], ["There was nothing, absolutely nothing, to tell the secret of his thoughts at that moment—the moment which was the crisis of his life and the crisis of hers.", "그 순간, 그의 인생의 고비이자 로라의 인생의 고비였던 그 순간에, 그의 속마음의 비밀을 일러 줄 것은 아무것도, 정말이지 아무것도 없었다."]], [["I was determined to make him declare himself, for Laura’s sake.", "나는 로라를 위해 그가 스스로 태도를 밝히게 만들기로 작정했다."]], [["“Sir Percival!”", "“퍼시벌 경!”"], ["I interposed sharply, “have you nothing to say when my sister has said so much?", "내가 날카롭게 끼어들었다. “제 동생이 이토록 많은 말을 했는데 아무 하실 말씀이 없으신가요?"], ["More, in my opinion,”", "제가 보기에는,”"], ["I added, my unlucky temper getting the better of me, “than any man alive, in your position, has a right to hear from her.”", "나는 불운한 성미가 나를 이기게 두고 이렇게 덧붙였다. “경의 처지에 있는 세상 어떤 남자가 이 아이에게서 들을 권리가 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 말을 했습니다만.”"]], [["That last rash sentence opened a way for him by which to escape me if he chose, and he instantly took advantage of it.", "그 경솔한 마지막 문장은 그가 마음만 먹으면 나를 피해 빠져나갈 길을 열어 주었고, 그는 즉시 그것을 이용했다."]], [["“Pardon me, Miss Halcombe,”", "“용서하십시오, 홀콤 양.”"], ["he said, still keeping his hand over his face, “pardon me if I remind you that I have claimed no such right.”", "그가 여전히 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 말했다. “제가 그런 권리를 주장한 적이 없다는 점을 상기시켜 드리더라도 용서해 주십시오.”"]], [["The few plain words which would have brought him back to the point from which he had wandered were just on my lips, when Laura checked me by speaking again.", "그를 그가 벗어난 지점으로 도로 데려다 놓을 몇 마디 분명한 말이 막 내 입술에 걸려 있었는데, 로라가 다시 입을 열어 나를 제지했다."]], [["“I hope I have not made my painful acknowledgment in vain,”", "“제 괴로운 고백이 헛되지 않았기를 바랍니다.”"], ["she continued.", "로라가 말을 이었다."], ["“I hope it has secured me your entire confidence in what I have still to say?”", "“그 고백으로 제가 앞으로 드릴 말씀에 대한 경의 온전한 신뢰를 얻었기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["“Pray be assured of it.”", "“부디 그 점은 믿어 주십시오.”"], ["He made that brief reply warmly, dropping his hand on the table while he spoke, and turning towards us again.", "그는 그 짧은 대답을 따뜻하게 건네며 말하는 동안 손을 탁자 위로 내려놓고 다시 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Whatever outward change had passed over him was gone now.", "그를 스쳐 갔던 겉모습의 변화가 무엇이었든 이제는 사라지고 없었다."], ["His face was eager and expectant—it expressed nothing but the most intense anxiety to hear her next words.", "그의 얼굴은 간절하고 기대에 차 있었다—로라의 다음 말을 듣고 싶은 더없이 절박한 조바심 말고는 아무것도 담겨 있지 않았다."]], [["“I wish you to understand that I have not spoken from any selfish motive,”", "“제가 어떤 이기적인 동기에서 말씀드린 것이 아님을 알아주셨으면 합니다.”"], ["she said.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“If you leave me, Sir Percival, after what you have just heard, you do not leave me to marry another man, you only allow me to remain a single woman for the rest of my life.", "“퍼시벌 경, 방금 들으신 말을 듣고 저를 떠나신다 해도, 제가 다른 남자와 혼인하도록 떠나시는 것이 아닙니다. 남은 평생 제가 홀로 사는 여자로 남도록 허락하시는 것일 뿐이에요."], ["My fault towards you has begun and ended in my own thoughts.", "경께 지은 제 잘못은 제 생각 속에서 시작되어 제 생각 속에서 끝났습니다."], ["It can never go any farther.", "결코 그 이상으로 나아갈 수 없습니다."], ["No word has passed—”", "한마디 말도 오간 적이—”"], ["She hesitated, in doubt about the expression she should use next, hesitated in a momentary confusion which it was very sad and very painful to see.", "로라는 다음에 쓸 말을 두고 망설이며 말을 멈췄다. 잠시 스친 그 혼란은 지켜보기에 몹시 슬프고 몹시 아팠다."], ["“No word has passed,”", "“한마디 말도 오간 적이 없습니다.”"], ["she patiently and resolutely resumed, “between myself and the person to whom I am now referring for the first and last time in your presence of my feelings towards him, or of his feelings towards me—no word ever can pass—neither he nor I are likely, in this world, to meet again.", "로라가 참을성 있게, 그러나 단호하게 말을 이었다. “저와, 지금 경 앞에서 그를 향한 제 마음이나 저를 향한 그의 마음을 두고 처음이자 마지막으로 언급하는 그 사람 사이에는요—앞으로도 결코 한마디 말도 오갈 수 없습니다—그도 저도 이 세상에서 다시 만날 일은 없을 테니까요."], ["I earnestly beg you to spare me from saying any more, and to believe me, on my word, in what I have just told you.", "더 말하지 않아도 되게 해 주시기를 간절히 청하고, 지금 드린 말씀을 제 말 그대로 믿어 주시기를 청합니다."], ["It is the truth, Sir Percival—the truth which I think my promised husband has a claim to hear, at any sacrifice of my own feelings.", "그것은 진실입니다, 퍼시벌 경—제 감정을 아무리 희생해서라도 제 약혼자께서 들으실 권리가 있다고 제가 여기는 진실입니다."], ["I trust to his generosity to pardon me, and to his honour to keep my secret.”", "저를 용서해 주시리라 그 너그러움을 믿고, 제 비밀을 지켜 주시리라 그 명예를 믿습니다.”"]], [["“Both those trusts are sacred to me,”", "“그 두 가지 믿음 모두 제게는 신성합니다.”"], ["he said, “and both shall be sacredly kept.”", "그가 말했다. “그리고 두 가지 다 신성하게 지켜질 것입니다.”"]], [["After answering in those terms he paused, and looked at her as if he was waiting to hear more.", "그렇게 대답한 뒤 그는 말을 멈추고, 더 들을 말이 있기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 로라를 바라보았다."]], [["“I have said all I wish to say,”", "“저는 하고 싶은 말을 다 했습니다.”"], ["she added quietly—“I have said more than enough to justify you in withdrawing from your engagement.”", "그녀가 조용히 덧붙였다. “당신이 약혼에서 물러나시더라도 그것이 정당하다고 할 만큼은 충분히, 아니 넘치도록 말씀드렸어요.”"]], [["“You have said more than enough,”", "“충분히, 아니 넘치도록 말씀하셨습니다.”"], ["he answered, “to make it the dearest object of my life to KEEP the engagement.”", "그가 대답했다. “이 약혼을 지키는 일이 제 인생에서 가장 소중한 목표가 되게 할 만큼 말입니다.”"], ["With those words he rose from his chair, and advanced a few steps towards the place where she was sitting.", "그 말과 함께 그는 의자에서 일어나, 그녀가 앉아 있는 자리를 향해 몇 걸음 다가섰다."]], [["She started violently, and a faint cry of surprise escaped her.", "그녀는 몹시 흠칫 놀랐고, 희미한 놀라움의 외마디 소리가 새어 나왔다."], ["Every word she had spoken had innocently betrayed her purity and truth to a man who thoroughly understood the priceless value of a pure and true woman.", "그녀가 입에 담은 말 하나하나가 자신도 모르는 사이에 그녀의 순결함과 진실함을, 순결하고 진실한 여인의 값을 매길 수 없는 가치를 속속들이 아는 남자 앞에 낱낱이 드러내고 말았던 것이다."], ["Her own noble conduct had been the hidden enemy, throughout, of all the hopes she had trusted to it.", "그녀 자신의 고결한 처신이야말로 처음부터 끝까지, 그녀가 거기에 걸었던 모든 희망의 숨은 적이었다."], ["I had dreaded this from the first.", "나는 처음부터 이것을 두려워하고 있었다."], ["I would have prevented it, if she had allowed me the smallest chance of doing so.", "그녀가 내게 조금이라도 기회를 허락했더라면 나는 이 일을 막았을 것이다."], ["I even waited and watched now, when the harm was done, for a word from Sir Percival that would give me the opportunity of putting him in the wrong.", "나는 이미 일이 다 벌어진 지금조차도, 그를 그르다고 몰아붙일 빌미가 될 한마디가 퍼시벌 경의 입에서 나오기를 기다리며 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“You have left it to ME, Miss Fairlie, to resign you,”", "“당신을 단념하는 일을 저에게 맡기셨군요, 페얼리 양.”"], ["he continued.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“I am not heartless enough to resign a woman who has just shown herself to be the noblest of her sex.”", "“저는 방금 자기 성(性) 가운데 가장 고결한 사람임을 스스로 보여 준 여인을 단념할 만큼 무정한 사람이 못 됩니다.”"]], [["He spoke with such warmth and feeling, with such passionate enthusiasm, and yet with such perfect delicacy, that she raised her head, flushed up a little, and looked at him with sudden animation and spirit.", "그가 어찌나 따뜻하고 진심 어린 어조로, 어찌나 격정에 찬 열의로, 그러면서도 어찌나 완벽하게 섬세하게 말했던지, 그녀는 고개를 들고 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 갑작스러운 생기와 기백을 띤 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“No!” she said firmly.", "“아닙니다!”"], ["“The most wretched of her sex, if she must give herself in marriage when she cannot give her love.”", "그녀가 단호하게 말했다. “사랑을 줄 수 없으면서 결혼으로 자신을 내주어야 한다면, 그 여자는 자기 성 가운데 가장 비참한 사람입니다.”"]], [["“May she not give it in the future,”", "“앞으로는 그 사랑을 주실 수 없겠습니까?”"], ["he asked, “if the one object of her husband’s life is to deserve it?”", "그가 물었다. “남편 된 사람의 삶의 유일한 목표가 그 사랑을 받을 자격을 갖추는 일이라면 말입니다.”"]], [["“Never!”", "“결코 없습니다!”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“If you still persist in maintaining our engagement, I may be your true and faithful wife, Sir Percival—your loving wife, if I know my own heart, never!”", "“그래도 굳이 우리 약혼을 지키겠다고 고집하신다면, 저는 당신의 참되고 충실한 아내는 될 수 있겠지요, 퍼시벌 경. 하지만 당신을 사랑하는 아내는, 제가 제 마음을 안다면, 결코 되지 못합니다!”"]], [["She looked so irresistibly beautiful as she said those brave words that no man alive could have steeled his heart against her.", "그 용감한 말을 하는 그녀는 너무나 저항할 수 없이 아름다워서, 살아 있는 어떤 남자도 그녀 앞에서 마음을 굳게 다잡을 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["I tried hard to feel that Sir Percival was to blame, and to say so, but my womanhood would pity him, in spite of myself.", "나는 퍼시벌 경에게 잘못이 있다고 느끼려고, 또 그렇게 말하려고 무진 애를 썼지만, 내 안의 여자는 나도 모르게 그를 가엾게 여기고 있었다."]], [["“I gratefully accept your faith and truth,”", "“당신의 신의와 진실을 감사히 받겠습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The least that you can offer is more to me than the utmost that I could hope for from any other woman in the world.”", "“당신이 내줄 수 있는 가장 적은 것이, 세상의 다른 어떤 여인에게서 바랄 수 있는 가장 큰 것보다 제게는 더 소중합니다.”"]], [["Her left hand still held mine, but her right hand hung listlessly at her side.", "그녀의 왼손은 여전히 내 손을 잡고 있었지만 오른손은 옆구리에 힘없이 늘어져 있었다. 그는 그 손을 가만히 들어 입술로 가져갔다."], ["He raised it gently to his lips—touched it with them, rather than kissed it—bowed to me—and then, with perfect delicacy and discretion, silently quitted the room.", "입을 맞추었다기보다는 그저 입술을 대었을 뿐이었다. 그러고는 내게 고개를 숙여 인사하고, 더없이 섬세하고 사려 깊게, 소리 없이 방을 나갔다."]], [["She neither moved nor said a word when he was gone—she sat by me, cold and still, with her eyes fixed on the ground.", "그가 나가고 나서도 그녀는 움직이지도 한마디 하지도 않았다. 차갑고 고요하게, 눈을 바닥에 붙박은 채 내 곁에 앉아 있을 뿐이었다."], ["I saw it was hopeless and useless to speak, and I only put my arm round her, and held her to me in silence.", "말을 거는 것이 부질없고 소용없는 짓임을 알았기에 나는 그저 팔을 둘러 그녀를 말없이 내게 끌어안았다."], ["We remained together so for what seemed a long and weary time—so long and so weary, that I grew uneasy and spoke to her softly, in the hope of producing a change.", "우리는 길고 지루하게 느껴지는 시간 동안 그렇게 함께 있었다. 어찌나 길고 지루했던지 나는 불안해져서, 무언가 달라지기를 바라며 그녀에게 나직이 말을 걸었다."]], [["The sound of my voice seemed to startle her into consciousness.", "내 목소리에 그녀는 문득 정신이 든 듯 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["She suddenly drew herself away from me and rose to her feet.", "그녀는 갑자기 내게서 몸을 빼내고 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“I must submit, Marian, as well as I can,”", "“나도 견뎌 내야지, 매리언. 할 수 있는 데까지.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“My new life has its hard duties, and one of them begins to-day.”", "“내 새 삶에는 힘든 의무들이 있고, 그중 하나가 오늘부터 시작되는 거야.”"]], [["As she spoke she went to a side-table near the window, on which her sketching materials were placed, gathered them together carefully, and put them in a drawer of her cabinet.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 창가의 작은 탁자로 갔다. 거기에는 그녀의 그림 도구들이 놓여 있었다."], ["She locked the drawer and brought the key to me.", "그녀는 그것들을 조심스럽게 그러모아 장식장 서랍에 넣었다. 그러고는 서랍을 잠그고 열쇠를 내게 가져왔다."]], [["“I must part from everything that reminds me of him,”", "“그이를 떠올리게 하는 것과는 모두 헤어져야 해.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Keep the key wherever you please—I shall never want it again.”", "“열쇠는 매리언 마음대로 아무 데나 두어. 나는 다시는 그게 필요하지 않을 테니까.”"]], [["Before I could say a word she had turned away to her book-case, and had taken from it the album that contained Walter Hartright’s drawings.", "내가 한마디 하기도 전에 그녀는 책장 쪽으로 돌아서서 월터 하트라이트의 그림들이 든 화첩을 꺼냈다."], ["She hesitated for a moment, holding the little volume fondly in her hands—then lifted it to her lips and kissed it.", "그녀는 그 작은 책을 손에 애틋하게 든 채 잠시 망설이다가, 그것을 입술로 들어 올려 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Oh, Laura!", "“오, 로라! 로라!”"], ["Laura!”", "나는 말했다."], ["I said, not angrily, not reprovingly—with nothing but sorrow in my voice, and nothing but sorrow in my heart.", "화가 나서도 아니고 나무라려는 것도 아니었다. 내 목소리에도 오직 슬픔뿐이었고, 내 마음에도 오직 슬픔뿐이었다."]], [["“It is the last time, Marian,”", "“이번이 마지막이야, 매리언.”"], ["she pleaded.", "그녀가 애원하듯 말했다."], ["“I am bidding it good-bye for ever.”", "“영영 작별을 고하는 거야.”"]], [["She laid the book on the table and drew out the comb that fastened her hair.", "그녀는 책을 탁자에 내려놓고 머리를 여며 두었던 빗을 뽑았다."], ["It fell, in its matchless beauty, over her back and shoulders, and dropped round her, far below her waist.", "머리카락이 비할 데 없이 아름답게 등과 어깨 위로 흘러내려 허리 한참 아래까지 그녀를 감싸고 떨어졌다."], ["She separated one long, thin lock from the rest, cut it off, and pinned it carefully, in the form of a circle, on the first blank page of the album.", "그녀는 그중에서 가늘고 긴 한 타래를 갈라내어 잘라 낸 다음, 화첩의 첫 백지에 동그라미 모양으로 조심스럽게 꽂아 두었다."], ["The moment it was fastened she closed the volume hurriedly, and placed it in my hands.", "그것이 고정되자마자 그녀는 서둘러 책을 덮고 내 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“You write to him and he writes to you,”", "“매리언은 그이에게 편지를 쓰고, 그이도 매리언에게 편지를 쓰잖아.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“While I am alive, if he asks after me always tell him I am well, and never say I am unhappy.", "“내가 살아 있는 동안 그이가 내 안부를 묻거든 언제나 잘 지낸다고 말해 줘. 불행하다는 말은 절대 하지 말고."], ["Don’t distress him, Marian, for my sake, don’t distress him.", "그이를 괴롭히지 마, 매리언. 나를 위해서, 그이를 괴롭히지 마."], ["If I die first, promise you will give him this little book of his drawings, with my hair in it.", "만약 내가 먼저 죽거든, 그이의 그림이 든 이 작은 책을 내 머리카락과 함께 그이에게 주겠다고 약속해 줘. 내가 세상에 없을 때라면, 내가 이 손으로 직접 거기에 넣어 두었다고 말해도 해 될 게 없을 거야."], ["There can be no harm, when I am gone, in telling him that I put it there with my own hands.", "그리고 말해 줘. 오, 매리언, 그때 나를 대신해서 말해 줘."], ["And say—oh, Marian, say for me, then, what I can never say for myself—say I loved him!”", "내가 나 자신을 위해서는 결코 할 수 없는 그 말을. 내가 그이를 사랑했다고 말해 줘!”"]], [["She flung her arms round my neck, and whispered the last words in my ear with a passionate delight in uttering them which it almost broke my heart to hear.", "그녀는 두 팔로 내 목을 감고 마지막 말을 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다. 그 말을 입 밖에 낸다는 격정적인 기쁨이 어찌나 배어 있던지, 듣고 있자니 내 가슴이 찢어질 것만 같았다."], ["All the long restraint she had imposed on herself gave way in that first last outburst of tenderness.", "그토록 오랫동안 스스로에게 지워 온 억제가 그 처음이자 마지막인 애정의 폭발 속에서 모두 무너져 내렸다."], ["She broke from me with hysterical vehemence, and threw herself on the sofa in a paroxysm of sobs and tears that shook her from head to foot.", "그녀는 발작적인 격렬함으로 내게서 몸을 떼어 내더니, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 온몸을 뒤흔드는 흐느낌과 눈물의 발작 속에서 소파에 몸을 던졌다."]], [["I tried vainly to soothe her and reason with her—she was past being soothed, and past being reasoned with.", "나는 그녀를 달래고 타이르려 애썼지만 헛일이었다. 그녀는 달랠 수 있는 상태도, 타이를 수 있는 상태도 이미 지나 있었다."], ["It was the sad, sudden end for us two of this memorable day.", "그것이 우리 두 사람에게는 그 잊지 못할 하루의 슬프고 갑작스러운 끝이었다."], ["When the fit had worn itself out she was too exhausted to speak.", "발작이 제풀에 잦아들었을 때 그녀는 너무 지쳐서 말도 하지 못했다."], ["She slumbered towards the afternoon, and I put away the book of drawings so that she might not see it when she woke.", "오후 무렵 그녀는 잠이 들었고, 나는 그녀가 깨어났을 때 눈에 띄지 않도록 그림책을 치워 두었다."], ["My face was calm, whatever my heart might be, when she opened her eyes again and looked at me.", "그녀가 다시 눈을 뜨고 나를 바라보았을 때, 내 마음이야 어떻든 내 얼굴은 평온했다."], ["We said no more to each other about the distressing interview of the morning.", "우리는 그날 아침의 그 괴로운 면담에 대해 서로 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Sir Percival’s name was not mentioned.", "퍼시벌 경의 이름도 입에 오르지 않았다."], ["Walter Hartright was not alluded to again by either of us for the remainder of the day.", "그날 남은 시간 동안 우리 둘 중 누구도 월터 하트라이트를 다시 입에 담지 않았다."]], [["10th.—Finding that she was composed and like herself this morning, I returned to the painful subject of yesterday, for the sole purpose of imploring her to let me speak to Sir Percival and Mr.", "10일.—오늘 아침 그녀가 차분하고 평소의 그녀다워진 것을 보고, 나는 어제의 그 괴로운 화제로 돌아갔다."], ["Fairlie, more plainly and strongly than she could speak to either of them herself, about this lamentable marriage.", "오로지 이 통탄스러운 결혼에 대해 그녀 자신이 두 사람 어느 쪽에게도 할 수 없는 말을 내가 더 분명하고 강하게 퍼시벌 경과 페얼리 씨에게 하도록 허락해 달라고 애원하기 위해서였다."], ["She interposed, gently but firmly, in the middle of my remonstrances.", "그녀는 내 항변이 한창일 때 부드럽지만 단호하게 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“I left yesterday to decide,”", "“나는 결정을 어제에 맡겨 두었어.”"], ["she said; “and yesterday HAS decided.", "그녀가 말했다. “그리고 어제가 정말로 결정을 내렸고."], ["It is too late to go back.”", "이제 되돌리기에는 너무 늦었어.”"]], [["Sir Percival spoke to me this afternoon about what had passed in Laura’s room.", "퍼시벌 경은 오늘 오후 로라의 방에서 있었던 일에 대해 내게 이야기했다."], ["He assured me that the unparalleled trust she had placed in him had awakened such an answering conviction of her innocence and integrity in his mind, that he was guiltless of having felt even a moment’s unworthy jealousy, either at the time when he was in her presence, or afterwards when he had withdrawn from it.", "그는 그녀가 자기에게 보여 준 유례없는 신뢰가 그녀의 결백함과 성실함에 대한 확신을 그만큼 자기 마음속에 불러일으켰기에, 그녀 앞에 있던 그 순간에도 물러난 뒤에도 한순간의 치졸한 질투조차 느낀 적이 없다고 내게 확언했다."], ["Deeply as he lamented the unfortunate attachment which had hindered the progress he might otherwise have made in her esteem and regard, he firmly believed that it had remained unacknowledged in the past, and that it would remain, under all changes of circumstance which it was possible to contemplate, unacknowledged in the future.", "자기가 그녀의 존경과 애정 속에서 이루었을 진전을 가로막은 그 불행한 연정을 몹시 애석해하기는 하지만, 그는 그것이 과거에도 고백된 적 없이 남아 있었고 앞으로 상상할 수 있는 어떤 상황의 변화가 오더라도 미래에도 고백되지 않은 채로 남으리라 굳게 믿는다고 했다. 이것이 그의 절대적인 확신이었다."], ["This was his absolute conviction; and the strongest proof he could give of it was the assurance, which he now offered, that he felt no curiosity to know whether the attachment was of recent date or not, or who had been the object of it.", "그리고 그가 그 확신에 대해 내놓을 수 있는 가장 강력한 증거는, 그 연정이 최근의 것인지 아닌지, 또 그 상대가 누구였는지 알고 싶은 호기심이 조금도 없다고 지금 내놓는 바로 그 확언이라고 했다."], ["His implicit confidence in Miss Fairlie made him satisfied with what she had thought fit to say to him, and he was honestly innocent of the slightest feeling of anxiety to hear more.", "페얼리 양에 대한 무조건적인 신뢰가 있기에 그는 그녀가 자기에게 말하는 것이 옳다고 여긴 만큼으로 만족했으며, 더 듣고 싶은 조바심이라고는 티끌만큼도 없다는 점에서 정직하게 결백하다는 것이었다."]], [["He waited after saying those words and looked at me.", "그는 그 말을 마치고 기다리며 나를 바라보았다."], ["I was so conscious of my unreasonable prejudice against him—so conscious of an unworthy suspicion that he might be speculating on my impulsively answering the very questions which he had just described himself as resolved not to ask—that I evaded all reference to this part of the subject with something like a feeling of confusion on my own part.", "나는 그에 대한 내 부당한 편견을 너무나 의식하고 있었고, 방금 스스로 묻지 않겠다고 밝힌 바로 그 질문들에 내가 충동적으로 답해 주기를 그가 노리고 계산하는 것일지도 모른다는 치졸한 의심을 너무나 의식하고 있었기에, 스스로 얼마간 당혹감 비슷한 것을 느끼면서 이 대목에 대한 언급을 모조리 피해 버렸다."], ["At the same time I was resolved not to lose even the smallest opportunity of trying to plead Laura’s cause, and I told him boldly that I regretted his generosity had not carried him one step farther, and induced him to withdraw from the engagement altogether.", "그와 동시에 나는 로라를 위해 변론할 기회라면 아무리 작은 것이라도 놓치지 않겠다고 결심하고 있었으므로, 그의 아량이 한 걸음 더 나아가 그를 약혼에서 완전히 물러나게 하지는 못한 것이 유감이라고 대담하게 말했다."]], [["Here, again, he disarmed me by not attempting to defend himself.", "여기서도 그는 자신을 변호하려 들지 않음으로써 나를 무장 해제시켰다."], ["He would merely beg me to remember the difference there was between his allowing Miss Fairlie to give him up, which was a matter of submission only, and his forcing himself to give up Miss Fairlie, which was, in other words, asking him to be the suicide of his own hopes.", "그는 다만 이 차이만은 기억해 달라고 청할 뿐이라고 했다. 페얼리 양이 자기를 버리도록 허락하는 것은 그저 순순히 따르는 문제일 뿐이지만, 자기가 억지로 페얼리 양을 버리는 것은 달리 말하면 자기 희망의 자살자가 되라고 요구하는 일이라는 차이 말이다."], ["Her conduct of the day before had so strengthened the unchangeable love and admiration of two long years, that all active contention against those feelings, on his part, was henceforth entirely out of his power.", "전날 그녀의 처신은 두 해에 걸친 변함없는 사랑과 흠모를 오히려 더 굳게 만들어 놓아서, 이제 그로서는 그 감정에 맞서 능동적으로 다투는 일이 전혀 불가능해졌다는 것이었다. 내가 그를 나약하고 이기적이며 그가 그토록 떠받드는 바로 그 여인에게 무정하다고 여긴다면, 그는 되도록 체념하는 마음으로 내 의견에 머리를 숙이겠다고 했다."], ["I must think him weak, selfish, unfeeling towards the very woman whom he idolised, and he must bow to my opinion as resignedly as he could—only putting it to me, at the same time, whether her future as a single woman, pining under an unhappily placed attachment which she could never acknowledge, could be said to promise her a much brighter prospect than her future as the wife of a man who worshipped the very ground she walked on?", "다만 이것만은 함께 물어보고 싶다고 했다. 결코 인정할 수 없는 불행히 자리 잡은 연정에 시달리며 여위어 가는 독신 여자로서의 그녀의 앞날이, 그녀가 밟고 다니는 땅까지 숭배하는 남자의 아내로서의 앞날보다 훨씬 더 밝은 전망을 약속한다고 할 수 있겠느냐고."], ["In the last case there was hope from time, however slight it might be—in the first case, on her own showing, there was no hope at all.", "뒤의 경우에는 아무리 희미하더라도 세월이 가져다줄 희망이 있지만, 앞의 경우에는 그녀 자신의 말대로 희망이라고는 전혀 없지 않으냐고."]], [["I answered him—more because my tongue is a woman’s, and must answer, than because I had anything convincing to say.", "나는 그에게 대답했다. 무슨 설득력 있는 할 말이 있어서라기보다는, 내 혀가 여자의 혀여서 대답하지 않고는 못 배기기 때문이었다."], ["It was only too plain that the course Laura had adopted the day before had offered him the advantage if he chose to take it—and that he HAD chosen to take it.", "전날 로라가 취한 행로가 그에게 유리한 패를 내주었다는 것, 그가 원하기만 하면 집어 들 수 있는 패를 내주었다는 것, 그리고 그가 실제로 그것을 집어 들기로 했다는 것은 너무나 명백했다."], ["I felt this at the time, and I feel it just as strongly now, while I write these lines, in my own room.", "그때도 나는 그렇게 느꼈고, 지금 내 방에서 이 줄들을 쓰면서도 똑같이 강하게 느낀다."], ["The one hope left is that his motives really spring, as he says they do, from the irresistible strength of his attachment to Laura.", "남은 유일한 희망은 그의 동기가 정말로 그가 말하는 대로 로라를 향한 애정의 거스를 수 없는 힘에서 솟아난 것이기를 바라는 것뿐이다."]], [["Before I close my diary for to-night I must record that I wrote to-day, in poor Hartright’s interest, to two of my mother’s old friends in London—both men of influence and position.", "오늘 밤 일기를 닫기 전에 기록해 두어야 할 것이 있다. 나는 오늘 가엾은 하트라이트를 위해 런던에 있는 어머니의 옛 친구 두 분에게 편지를 썼다."], ["If they can do anything for him, I am quite sure they will.", "두 분 다 영향력과 지위가 있는 분들이다. 그를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 있다면 그분들은 반드시 해 주시리라 나는 확신한다."], ["Except Laura, I never was more anxious about any one than I am now about Walter.", "로라를 빼면, 나는 지금 월터에 대해 느끼는 만큼 누군가를 걱정해 본 적이 없다."], ["All that has happened since he left us has only increased my strong regard and sympathy for him.", "그가 우리를 떠난 뒤 일어난 모든 일은 그에 대한 나의 깊은 존경과 연민을 키워 놓기만 했다."], ["I hope I am doing right in trying to help him to employment abroad—I hope, most earnestly and anxiously, that it will end well.", "그가 외국에서 일자리를 얻도록 돕는 것이 옳은 일이기를 바란다. 그 일이 좋게 끝나기를 더없이 간절하고 애타게 바란다."]], [["11th.—Sir Percival had an interview with Mr. Fairlie, and I was sent for to join them.", "11일.—퍼시벌 경이 페얼리 씨와 면담을 가졌고, 나에게도 그 자리에 오라는 전갈이 왔다."]], [["I found Mr. Fairlie greatly relieved at the prospect of the “family worry”", "가 보니 페얼리 씨는 “집안의 골칫거리”(조카딸의 결혼을 그는 그렇게 부르기를 즐겼다)가 마침내 정리될 전망에 크게 안도하고 있었다."], ["(as he was pleased to describe his niece’s marriage) being settled at last.", "거기까지는 내 의견을 그에게 말해야겠다는 생각이 들지 않았다."], ["So far, I did not feel called on to say anything to him about my own opinion, but when he proceeded, in his most aggravatingly languid manner, to suggest that the time for the marriage had better be settled next, in accordance with Sir Percival’s wishes, I enjoyed the satisfaction of assailing Mr. Fairlie’s nerves with as strong a protest against hurrying Laura’s decision as I could put into words.", "그러나 그가 특유의 사람 속을 뒤집는 나른한 태도로, 이제는 퍼시벌 경의 뜻에 따라 결혼 시기를 정하는 편이 좋겠다고 제안하기에 이르자, 나는 로라의 결정을 재촉하는 데 대한 항의를 말로 담을 수 있는 한 가장 강하게 퍼부어 페얼리 씨의 신경을 공격하는 만족을 누렸다."], ["Sir Percival immediately assured me that he felt the force of my objection, and begged me to believe that the proposal had not been made in consequence of any interference on his part.", "퍼시벌 경은 즉시 내 반대의 무게를 느낀다고 확언하며, 그 제안이 자기 쪽의 어떤 간섭 때문에 나온 것이 아님을 믿어 달라고 청했다."], ["Mr. Fairlie leaned back in his chair, closed his eyes, said we both of us did honour to human nature, and then repeated his suggestion as coolly as if neither Sir Percival nor I had said a word in opposition to it.", "페얼리 씨는 의자에 등을 기대고 눈을 감고는, 우리 둘 다 인간 본성의 명예가 된다고 말하더니, 퍼시벌 경도 나도 반대의 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다는 듯 태연하게 그 제안을 되풀이했다."], ["It ended in my flatly declining to mention the subject to Laura, unless she first approached it of her own accord. I left the room at once after making that declaration.", "결국 나는 로라가 먼저 스스로 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않는 한 그 문제를 그녀에게 입에 올리지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 거절하는 것으로 끝을 냈다."], ["Sir Percival looked seriously embarrassed and distressed, Mr.", "그 선언을 하고 나는 곧바로 방을 나왔다."], ["Fairlie stretched out his lazy legs on his velvet footstool, and said, “Dear Marian!", "퍼시벌 경은 몹시 난처하고 괴로운 표정이었고, 페얼리 씨는 벨벳 발받침 위로 게으른 다리를 죽 뻗으며 말했다. “아, 매리언!"], ["how I envy you your robust nervous system!", "그 튼튼한 신경 조직이 얼마나 부러운지 모릅니다!"], ["Don’t bang the door!”", "문은 쾅 닫지 마세요!”"]], [["On going to Laura’s room I found that she had asked for me, and that Mrs.", "로라의 방에 가 보니 그녀가 나를 찾았고, 베지 부인이 내가 페얼리 씨와 함께 있다고 알려 주었다고 했다."], ["Vesey had informed her that I was with Mr. Fairlie.", "그녀는 곧바로 내가 무슨 일로 불려 갔었는지 물었고, 나는 있었던 일을 전부 말해 주었다."], ["She inquired at once what I had been wanted for, and I told her all that had passed, without attempting to conceal the vexation and annoyance that I really felt.", "내가 실제로 느낀 짜증과 분함을 감추려 하지도 않았다. 그녀의 대답은 이루 말할 수 없이 나를 놀라게 하고 괴롭혔다."], ["Her answer surprised and distressed me inexpressibly—it was the very last reply that I should have expected her to make.", "그것은 내가 그녀에게서 기대했을 법한 대답 중 가장 마지막의 것이었다."]], [["“My uncle is right,”", "“삼촌 말씀이 옳아.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I have caused trouble and anxiety enough to you, and to all about me.", "“나는 매리언에게도, 내 주위 모든 사람에게도 이미 충분히 말썽과 걱정을 끼쳤어."], ["Let me cause no more, Marian—let Sir Percival decide.”", "더는 끼치지 않게 해 줘, 매리언. 퍼시벌 경이 정하시게 해.”"]], [["I remonstrated warmly, but nothing that I could say moved her.", "나는 열을 내며 항의했지만, 내가 무슨 말을 해도 그녀를 움직이지 못했다."]], [["“I am held to my engagement,”", "“나는 약혼에 매인 몸이야.”"], ["she replied; “I have broken with my old life.", "그녀가 대답했다. “나는 예전의 삶과 결별했어."], ["The evil day will not come the less surely because I put it off.", "내가 미룬다고 해서 그 불길한 날이 덜 확실하게 오는 것도 아니잖아."], ["No, Marian!", "아니야, 매리언!"], ["once again my uncle is right.", "다시 한번 말하지만 삼촌 말씀이 옳아."], ["I have caused trouble enough and anxiety enough, and I will cause no more.”", "나는 이미 충분히 말썽을 끼쳤고 충분히 걱정을 끼쳤어. 더는 끼치지 않겠어.”"]], [["She used to be pliability itself, but she was now inflexibly passive in her resignation—I might almost say in her despair.", "그녀는 예전에는 유순함 그 자체였지만, 지금은 체념 속에서, 거의 절망 속에서라고 해도 좋을 만큼 꿈쩍도 하지 않는 수동성을 보이고 있었다."], ["Dearly as I love her, I should have been less pained if she had been violently agitated—it was so shockingly unlike her natural character to see her as cold and insensible as I saw her now.", "내가 그녀를 그토록 사랑하지만, 차라리 그녀가 격하게 동요했더라면 내 마음이 덜 아팠을 것이다. 지금 내가 보는 것처럼 차갑고 무감각한 그녀의 모습은 타고난 성품과 너무나 딴판이어서 충격적이었다."]], [["12th.—Sir Percival put some questions to me at breakfast about Laura, which left me no choice but to tell him what she had said.", "12일.—퍼시벌 경이 아침 식사 자리에서 로라에 대해 몇 가지를 물어 와서, 나는 그녀가 한 말을 그에게 전할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["While we were talking she herself came down and joined us.", "우리가 이야기하는 동안 그녀가 직접 내려와 우리와 자리를 함께했다."], ["She was just as unnaturally composed in Sir Percival’s presence as she had been in mine.", "그녀는 퍼시벌 경 앞에서도 내 앞에서만큼이나 부자연스러울 만큼 침착했다."], ["When breakfast was over he had an opportunity of saying a few words to her privately, in a recess of one of the windows.", "아침 식사가 끝나자 그는 창가 후미진 자리에서 그녀에게 몇 마디 사사로이 건넬 기회를 얻었다."], ["They were not more than two or three minutes together, and on their separating she left the room with Mrs. Vesey, while Sir Percival came to me.", "두 사람이 함께 있었던 것은 이삼 분에 지나지 않았고, 헤어지면서 그녀는 베지 부인과 함께 방을 나갔으며 퍼시벌 경은 내게로 왔다."], ["He said he had entreated her to favour him by maintaining her privilege of fixing the time for the marriage at her own will and pleasure.", "그는 결혼 시기를 그녀 자신의 뜻과 마음대로 정하는 특권을 부디 그대로 지녀 달라고 그녀에게 간청했노라고 말했다."], ["In reply she had merely expressed her acknowledgments, and had desired him to mention what his wishes were to Miss Halcombe.", "그에 대해 그녀는 그저 고맙다는 뜻만 표하고, 그의 바람이 무엇인지 홀콤 양에게 말해 달라고 청했다는 것이었다."]], [["I have no patience to write more.", "더 쓸 인내심이 없다."], ["In this instance, as in every other, Sir Percival has carried his point with the utmost possible credit to himself, in spite of everything that I can say or do.", "이 경우에도 다른 모든 경우와 마찬가지로, 퍼시벌 경은 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 해도 아랑곳없이 자기에게 가능한 최대한의 명예를 안은 채 자기 뜻을 관철했다."], ["His wishes are now, what they were, of course, when he first came here; and Laura having resigned herself to the one inevitable sacrifice of the marriage, remains as coldly hopeless and enduring as ever.", "그의 바람은 지금도 물론 그가 처음 여기에 왔을 때와 같다. 그리고 로라는 결혼이라는 피할 수 없는 하나의 희생에 스스로를 내맡긴 채 여전히 차갑도록 희망 없이 견디고만 있다."], ["In parting with the little occupations and relics that reminded her of Hartright, she seems to have parted with all her tenderness and all her impressibility.", "하트라이트를 떠올리게 하던 소소한 소일거리와 유품들과 헤어지면서, 그녀는 자신의 다정함과 감수성까지 모두 떠나보낸 듯하다."], ["It is only three o’clock in the afternoon while I write these lines, and Sir Percival has left us already, in the happy hurry of a bridegroom, to prepare for the bride’s reception at his house in Hampshire.", "이 줄들을 쓰는 지금은 오후 세 시밖에 되지 않았는데, 퍼시벌 경은 신랑다운 행복한 조바심으로 벌써 우리 곁을 떠나 햄프셔의 자기 집에서 신부를 맞을 준비를 하러 갔다."], ["Unless some extraordinary event happens to prevent it they will be married exactly at the time when he wished to be married—before the end of the year.", "이를 막을 무슨 특별한 일이 일어나지 않는 한, 두 사람은 그가 결혼하고 싶어 했던 바로 그 시기에, 올해가 가기 전에 결혼하게 될 것이다."], ["My very fingers burn as I write it!", "이 글을 쓰는 내 손가락이 다 타는 것 같다!"]], [["13th.—A sleepless night, through uneasiness about Laura.", "13일.—로라 걱정에 잠 못 이룬 밤이었다."], ["Towards the morning I came to a resolution to try what change of scene would do to rouse her.", "아침 무렵 나는 환경을 바꾸어 보면 그녀를 일으켜 세울 수 있을지 시도해 보기로 결심했다."], ["She cannot surely remain in her present torpor of insensibility, if I take her away from Limmeridge and surround her with the pleasant faces of old friends?", "리머리지에서 그녀를 데리고 나가 옛 친구들의 정다운 얼굴로 둘러싸 준다면, 설마 지금 같은 무감각한 마비 상태에 그대로 머물러 있지는 않겠지?"], ["After some consideration I decided on writing to the Arnolds, in Yorkshire.", "얼마간 궁리한 끝에 나는 요크셔의 아널드 댁에 편지를 쓰기로 정했다."], ["They are simple, kind-hearted, hospitable people, and she has known them from her childhood.", "그들은 소박하고 마음씨 곱고 손님을 반기는 사람들이고, 그녀는 어릴 적부터 그들을 알아 왔다."], ["When I had put the letter in the post-bag I told her what I had done.", "편지를 우편 자루에 넣고 나서 나는 그녀에게 내가 한 일을 말했다."], ["It would have been a relief to me if she had shown the spirit to resist and object.", "그녀가 반대하고 거부할 기개를 보였더라면 나는 오히려 마음이 놓였을 것이다. 그러나 그러지 않았다."], ["But no—she only said, “I will go anywhere with you, Marian.", "그녀는 그저 이렇게 말했을 뿐이다. “매리언과 함께라면 어디든 갈게."], ["I dare say you are right—I dare say the change will do me good.”", "매리언 말이 맞을 거야. 환경이 바뀌면 내게 좋겠지.”"]], [["14th.—I wrote to Mr. Gilmore, informing him that there was really a prospect of this miserable marriage taking place, and also mentioning my idea of trying what change of scene would do for Laura.", "14일.—길모어 씨에게 편지를 써서 이 비참한 결혼이 정말로 성사될 전망이라는 것을 알리고, 로라를 위해 환경을 바꿔 보려는 내 생각도 언급했다."], ["I had no heart to go into particulars.", "자세한 사정까지 쓸 마음은 나지 않았다."], ["Time enough for them when we get nearer to the end of the year.", "그런 이야기는 올해가 끝나 갈 무렵에 해도 늦지 않다."]], [["15th.—Three letters for me.", "15일.—내게 온 편지가 세 통."], ["The first, from the Arnolds, full of delight at the prospect of seeing Laura and me.", "첫 번째는 아널드 댁에서 온 것으로, 로라와 나를 만날 기대에 기쁨이 넘쳤다."], ["The second, from one of the gentlemen to whom I wrote on Walter Hartright’s behalf, informing me that he has been fortunate enough to find an opportunity of complying with my request.", "두 번째는 월터 하트라이트를 위해 내가 편지를 보냈던 신사분 가운데 한 분에게서 온 것으로, 다행히 내 부탁을 들어줄 기회를 찾아냈다고 알려 왔다."], ["The third, from Walter himself, thanking me, poor fellow, in the warmest terms, for giving him an opportunity of leaving his home, his country, and his friends.", "세 번째는 월터 본인에게서 온 것으로, 가엾게도 그는 집과 조국과 벗들을 떠날 기회를 준 데 대해 더없이 따뜻한 말로 내게 고마워하고 있었다."], ["A private expedition to make excavations among the ruined cities of Central America is, it seems, about to sail from Liverpool.", "중앙아메리카의 폐허가 된 도시들을 발굴하려는 사설 탐험대가 곧 리버풀에서 출항할 예정이라고 한다."], ["The draughtsman who had been already appointed to accompany it has lost heart, and withdrawn at the eleventh hour, and Walter is to fill his place.", "이미 동행하도록 임명되었던 제도사가 겁을 먹고 막판에 물러났는데, 월터가 그 자리를 채우게 되었다는 것이다."], ["He is to be engaged for six months certain, from the time of the landing in Honduras, and for a year afterwards, if the excavations are successful, and if the funds hold out.", "그는 온두라스에 상륙한 때부터 최소 여섯 달간 고용되며, 발굴이 성공적이고 자금이 버텨 준다면 그 뒤로 일 년 더 일하게 된다."], ["His letter ends with a promise to write me a farewell line when they are all on board ship, and when the pilot leaves them.", "그의 편지는 모두 배에 오르고 수로 안내인이 배를 떠날 때 내게 작별의 몇 줄을 쓰겠다는 약속으로 끝난다."], ["I can only hope and pray earnestly that he and I are both acting in this matter for the best.", "나는 그와 내가 이 일에서 둘 다 최선을 다해 행동하고 있기를 간절히 바라고 기도할 따름이다."], ["It seems such a serious step for him to take, that the mere contemplation of it startles me.", "그가 내딛는 걸음이 어찌나 중대해 보이는지, 그것을 떠올리기만 해도 나는 흠칫 놀란다."], ["And yet, in his unhappy position, how can I expect him or wish him to remain at home?", "그렇지만 그 불행한 처지에서, 어떻게 그가 집에 남아 있기를 기대하거나 바랄 수 있겠는가?"]], [["16th.—The carriage is at the door.", "16일.—마차가 문 앞에 와 있다."], ["Laura and I set out on our visit to the Arnolds to-day.", "로라와 나는 오늘 아널드 댁을 방문하러 길을 나선다."]], [["# POLESDEAN LODGE, YORKSHIRE.", "# POLESDEAN LODGE, YORKSHIRE."]], [["23rd.—A week in these new scenes and among these kind-hearted people has done her some good, though not so much as I had hoped.", "23일.—이 새로운 풍경 속에서, 이 마음씨 고운 사람들 틈에서 보낸 한 주가 그녀에게 얼마쯤 도움이 되었다. 내가 바랐던 만큼은 아니지만."], ["I have resolved to prolong our stay for another week at least.", "나는 적어도 한 주는 더 머물기로 마음을 정했다."], ["It is useless to go back to Limmeridge till there is an absolute necessity for our return.", "꼭 돌아가야 할 절대적인 필요가 생기기 전에는 리머리지로 돌아가 봐야 소용없다."]], [["24th.—Sad news by this morning’s post.", "24일.—오늘 아침 우편으로 슬픈 소식이 왔다."], ["The expedition to Central America sailed on the twenty-first.", "중앙아메리카 탐험대는 21일에 출항했다."], ["We have parted with a true man—we have lost a faithful friend.", "우리는 참된 사람과 헤어졌다. 우리는 충실한 벗을 잃었다."], ["Water Hartright has left England.", "월터 하트라이트는 영국을 떠났다."]], [["25th.—Sad news yesterday—ominous news to-day. Sir Percival Glyde has written to Mr.", "25일.—어제는 슬픈 소식이더니 오늘은 불길한 소식이다."], ["Fairlie, and Mr. Fairlie has written to Laura and me, to recall us to Limmeridge immediately.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 썼고, 페얼리 씨는 로라와 내게 편지를 써서 우리를 당장 리머리지로 불러들였다."]], [["What can this mean?", "이게 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["Has the day for the marriage been fixed in our absence?", "우리가 없는 사이에 결혼 날짜가 정해진 것일까?"]], [["# LIMMERIDGE HOUSE.", "# LIMMERIDGE HOUSE."]], [["November 27th.—My forebodings are realised.", "11월 27일.—나의 불길한 예감이 그대로 들어맞았다."], ["The marriage is fixed for the twenty-second of December.", "결혼식은 12월 22일로 정해졌다."]], [["The day after we left for Polesdean Lodge Sir Percival wrote, it seems, to Mr.", "우리가 폴스딘 로지로 떠난 다음 날, 퍼시벌 경은 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 보냈던 모양이다."], ["Fairlie, to say that the necessary repairs and alterations in his house in Hampshire would occupy a much longer time in completion than he had originally anticipated.", "햄프셔에 있는 자기 저택의 수리와 개조가 애초 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 오래 걸리겠다는 내용이었다."], ["The proper estimates were to be submitted to him as soon as possible, and it would greatly facilitate his entering into definite arrangements with the workpeople, if he could be informed of the exact period at which the wedding ceremony might be expected to take place.", "견적서가 되도록 빨리 자기에게 제출될 예정인데, 결혼식이 언제쯤 치러질지 정확한 시기를 알 수 있다면 인부들과 확정적인 약속을 맺는 데 큰 도움이 되리라는 것이었다."], ["He could then make all his calculations in reference to time, besides writing the necessary apologies to friends who had been engaged to visit him that winter, and who could not, of course, be received when the house was in the hands of the workmen.", "그러면 시간에 관한 모든 계산을 세울 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 그해 겨울 자기 집을 찾아오기로 되어 있던 친구들에게—집이 인부들 손에 넘어가 있는 동안에는 물론 손님을 맞을 수 없으니—필요한 사과 편지도 쓸 수 있으리라고 했다."]], [["To this letter Mr. Fairlie had replied by requesting Sir Percival himself to suggest a day for the marriage, subject to Miss Fairlie’s approval, which her guardian willingly undertook to do his best to obtain.", "이 편지에 페얼리 씨는 답장을 보내, 퍼시벌 경 본인이 결혼 날짜를 정해 제안해 달라고, 다만 페얼리 양의 승낙을 조건으로 하되 그 승낙을 얻어 내는 일에는 후견인인 자기가 기꺼이 최선을 다하겠노라고 했다."], ["Sir Percival wrote back by the next post, and proposed (in accordance with his own views and wishes from the first?) the latter part of December—perhaps the twenty-second, or twenty-fourth, or any other day that the lady and her guardian might prefer.", "퍼시벌 경은 다음 우편으로 회신하여, (처음부터 품고 있던 자기 생각과 소망에 딱 들어맞게, 라고 해야 할까?) 12월 하순을—아마도 22일이나 24일, 혹은 신부와 후견인이 더 좋아할 만한 다른 어느 날을—제안했다."], ["The lady not being at hand to speak for herself, her guardian had decided, in her absence, on the earliest day mentioned—the twenty-second of December, and had written to recall us to Limmeridge in consequence.", "정작 신부는 자기 의사를 말할 자리에 없었으므로, 후견인은 그 아이가 없는 사이에 언급된 날짜 가운데 가장 이른 날, 곧 12월 22일로 결정해 버렸고, 그에 따라 우리를 리머리지로 불러들이는 편지를 보냈던 것이다."]], [["After explaining these particulars to me at a private interview yesterday, Mr.", "어제 단둘이 만난 자리에서 이런 자초지종을 내게 설명한 뒤, 페얼리 씨는 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 태도로 오늘 내가 필요한 교섭을 시작해 주면 좋겠다고 했다."], ["Fairlie suggested, in his most amiable manner, that I should open the necessary negotiations to-day.", "로라의 뜻을 먼저 얻지 못하는 한 반대해 보아야 소용없다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 그 아이와 이야기해 보겠다고 응낙했다."], ["Feeling that resistance was useless, unless I could first obtain Laura’s authority to make it, I consented to speak to her, but declared, at the same time, that I would on no consideration undertake to gain her consent to Sir Percival’s wishes. Mr.", "다만 어떤 경우에도 퍼시벌 경의 뜻에 그 아이가 동의하도록 만드는 일은 맡지 않겠다고 그 자리에서 분명히 못 박았다."], ["Fairlie complimented me on my “excellent conscience,” much as he would have complimented me, if he had been out walking, on my “excellent constitution,”", "페얼리 씨는 나의 “훌륭한 양심”을 칭찬했는데, 그 말투란 마치 함께 산책을 나갔다가 나의 “훌륭한 체질”을 칭찬할 때와 조금도 다를 바가 없었다."], ["and seemed perfectly satisfied, so far, with having simply shifted one more family responsibility from his own shoulders to mine.", "그리고 그는 집안의 책임 하나를 자기 어깨에서 내 어깨로 옮겨 놓았다는 사실만으로 지금까지는 아주 흡족한 듯 보였다."]], [["This morning I spoke to Laura as I had promised.", "오늘 아침 나는 약속대로 로라에게 이야기했다."], ["The composure—I may almost say, the insensibility—which she has so strangely and so resolutely maintained ever since Sir Percival left us, was not proof against the shock of the news I had to tell her.", "퍼시벌 경이 떠난 이후로 그 아이가 그토록 이상하리만치, 그토록 굳세게 지켜 온 침착함—거의 무감각이라 해도 좋을 그 태도—도 내가 전해야 했던 소식의 충격 앞에서는 견디지 못했다."], ["She turned pale and trembled violently.", "그 아이는 얼굴이 창백해지더니 심하게 몸을 떨었다."]], [["“Not so soon!”", "“그렇게 빨리는 안 돼요!”"], ["she pleaded.", "로라가 애원했다."], ["“Oh, Marian, not so soon!”", "“아, 매리언, 그렇게 빨리는 안 돼요!”"]], [["The slightest hint she could give was enough for me.", "그 아이가 내비칠 수 있는 가장 희미한 기색만으로도 내게는 충분했다."], ["I rose to leave the room, and fight her battle for her at once with Mr. Fairlie.", "나는 그 자리에서 곧장 페얼리 씨를 상대로 그 아이 대신 싸움을 벌이려고 방을 나서려 일어섰다."]], [["Just as my hand was on the door, she caught fast hold of my dress and stopped me.", "내 손이 막 문에 닿았을 때, 그 아이가 내 옷자락을 꽉 붙들어 나를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Let me go!”", "“놓아 다오!”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“My tongue burns to tell your uncle that he and Sir Percival are not to have it all their own way.”", "“네 삼촌과 퍼시벌 경이 모든 것을 제 뜻대로만 할 수는 없다고 말해 주고 싶어 혀가 근질거린다.”"]], [["She sighed bitterly, and still held my dress.", "그 아이는 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬면서도 여전히 내 옷을 놓지 않았다."]], [["“No!”", "“안 돼요!”"], ["she said faintly.", "로라가 힘없이 말했다."], ["“Too late, Marian, too late!”", "“너무 늦었어요, 매리언, 너무 늦었어요!”"]], [["“Not a minute too late,”", "“단 일 분도 늦지 않았다.”"], ["I retorted.", "내가 되받았다."], ["“The question of time is OUR question—and trust me, Laura, to take a woman’s full advantage of it.”", "“시간 문제야말로 우리의 문제야. 그러니 내가 그것을 여자답게 한껏 이용하도록 나를 믿고 맡겨 다오, 로라.”"]], [["I unclasped her hand from my gown while I spoke; but she slipped both her arms round my waist at the same moment, and held me more effectually than ever.", "말하면서 나는 내 가운에서 그 아이의 손을 떼어 냈다. 그러나 그 아이는 같은 순간 두 팔을 내 허리에 둘러 감아, 전보다 더 단단히 나를 붙들었다."]], [["“It will only involve us in more trouble and more confusion,”", "“그래 봐야 우리를 더 큰 곤란과 더 큰 혼란에 빠뜨릴 뿐이에요.”"], ["she said.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“It will set you and my uncle at variance, and bring Sir Percival here again with fresh causes of complaint—”", "“매리언과 삼촌을 서로 등지게 만들고, 새로운 불평거리를 안은 퍼시벌 경을 다시 이곳으로 불러들이게 될 거예요—”"]], [["“So much the better!”", "“그러면 더욱 좋지!”"], ["I cried out passionately.", "나는 격정에 차 외쳤다."], ["“Who cares for his causes of complaint?", "“그 사람 불평거리 따위가 무슨 대수냐?"], ["Are you to break your heart to set his mind at ease?", "그 사람 마음 편하자고 네 가슴이 찢어져야 한단 말이냐?"], ["No man under heaven deserves these sacrifices from us women.", "하늘 아래 어떤 남자도 우리 여자들에게서 그런 희생을 받을 자격은 없다."], ["Men!", "남자들이란!"], ["They are the enemies of our innocence and our peace—they drag us away from our parents’ love and our sisters’ friendship—they take us body and soul to themselves, and fasten our helpless lives to theirs as they chain up a dog to his kennel.", "그들은 우리의 순결과 평화를 해치는 적이야. 부모의 사랑과 자매의 우정에서 우리를 끌어내고, 우리의 몸과 영혼을 통째로 제 것으로 가져가서는, 개를 개집에 사슬로 매어 두듯 우리의 무력한 삶을 제 삶에 붙들어 매지."], ["And what does the best of them give us in return?", "그러고서 그중 가장 나은 자가 우리에게 무엇을 돌려주더냐?"], ["Let me go, Laura—I’m mad when I think of it!”", "놓아 다오, 로라—생각만 해도 나는 미칠 것 같다!”"]], [["The tears—miserable, weak, women’s tears of vexation and rage—started to my eyes.", "눈물이—비참하고 나약한, 분함과 노여움에서 나오는 여자의 눈물이—내 눈에 솟구쳤다."], ["She smiled sadly, and put her handkerchief over my face to hide for me the betrayal of my own weakness—the weakness of all others which she knew that I most despised.", "그 아이는 슬프게 미소 지으며 내 얼굴 위에 손수건을 덮어, 내가 무엇보다 경멸한다는 것을 저도 알고 있는 그 나약함이 드러난 것을 나 대신 가려 주었다."]], [["“Oh, Marian!” she said.", "“아, 매리언!”"], ["“You crying!", "로라가 말했다."], ["Think what you would say to me, if the places were changed, and if those tears were mine.", "“매리언이 울다니! 처지가 뒤바뀌어 이 눈물이 제 것이었다면 매리언이 제게 무슨 말을 했을지 생각해 보세요."], ["All your love and courage and devotion will not alter what must happen, sooner or later.", "매리언의 사랑도 용기도 헌신도, 이르든 늦든 반드시 닥칠 일을 바꾸지는 못해요."], ["Let my uncle have his way.", "삼촌 뜻대로 하시게 두어요."], ["Let us have no more troubles and heart-burnings that any sacrifice of mine can prevent.", "제가 희생하기만 하면 막을 수 있는 괴로움과 속앓이라면 더는 만들지 말아요."], ["Say you will live with me, Marian, when I am married—and say no more.”", "제가 결혼하면 저와 함께 살아 주겠다고 말해 줘요, 매리언—그리고 더는 아무 말도 말아요.”"]], [["But I did say more.", "하지만 나는 더 말했다."], ["I forced back the contemptible tears that were no relief to ME, and that only distressed HER, and reasoned and pleaded as calmly as I could.", "나에게는 아무 위안도 되지 않고 그 아이를 괴롭히기만 하는 그 하찮은 눈물을 억지로 삼키고, 되도록 침착하게 이치를 따지고 간청했다."], ["It was of no avail.", "소용없었다."], ["She made me twice repeat the promise to live with her when she was married, and then suddenly asked a question which turned my sorrow and my sympathy for her into a new direction.", "그 아이는 제가 결혼한 뒤 함께 살겠다는 약속을 내게 두 번이나 되풀이하게 하더니, 느닷없이 한 가지 질문을 던져 그 아이를 향한 내 슬픔과 연민을 전혀 다른 방향으로 돌려놓았다."]], [["“While we were at Polesdean,”", "“우리가 폴스딘에 있는 동안에요.”"], ["she said, “you had a letter, Marian——”", "로라가 말했다. “매리언에게 편지가 한 통 왔었지요——”"]], [["Her altered tone—the abrupt manner in which she looked away from me and hid her face on my shoulder—the hesitation which silenced her before she had completed her question, all told me, but too plainly, to whom the half-expressed inquiry pointed.", "달라진 목소리, 갑자기 내게서 눈길을 돌려 내 어깨에 얼굴을 묻던 그 태도, 질문을 채 마치기도 전에 말을 멈추게 한 그 머뭇거림—이 모든 것이, 절반쯤밖에 내뱉지 못한 그 물음이 누구를 가리키는지 너무나 분명하게 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["“I thought, Laura, that you and I were never to refer to him again,”", "“로라, 우리 둘은 다시는 그 사람 이야기를 하지 않기로 하지 않았니.”"], ["I said gently.", "내가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“You had a letter from him?”", "“그 사람에게서 편지가 왔지요?”"], ["she persisted.", "그 아이는 물러서지 않았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["I replied, “if you must know it.”", "내가 대답했다. “정 알아야겠다면.”"]], [["“Do you mean to write to him again?”", "“또 편지를 쓸 생각인가요?”"]], [["I hesitated.", "나는 망설였다."], ["I had been afraid to tell her of his absence from England, or of the manner in which my exertions to serve his new hopes and projects had connected me with his departure.", "그가 영국을 떠났다는 사실도, 그의 새로운 희망과 계획을 도우려던 내 노력이 어떤 식으로 그의 출발과 얽히게 되었는지도, 나는 그 아이에게 말하기가 두려웠다."], ["What answer could I make?", "무슨 대답을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He was gone where no letters could reach him for months, perhaps for years, to come.", "그는 앞으로 몇 달, 어쩌면 몇 해 동안 어떤 편지도 닿을 수 없는 곳으로 떠나 버렸는데."]], [["“Suppose I do mean to write to him again,”", "“내가 다시 편지를 쓸 생각이라고 치자.”"], ["I said at last.", "마침내 내가 말했다."], ["“What then, Laura?”", "“그러면 어쩌겠다는 거냐, 로라?”"]], [["Her cheek grew burning hot against my neck, and her arms trembled and tightened round me.", "내 목에 닿은 그 아이의 뺨이 불덩이처럼 뜨거워졌고, 나를 감은 두 팔이 떨리며 더욱 조여들었다."]], [["“Don’t tell him about THE TWENTY-SECOND,”", "“그이에게 22일 이야기는 하지 말아요.”"], ["she whispered.", "로라가 속삭였다."], ["“Promise, Marian—pray promise you will not even mention my name to him when you write next.”", "“약속해 줘요, 매리언—다음에 편지를 쓸 때 제 이름조차 꺼내지 않겠다고 부디 약속해 줘요.”"]], [["I gave the promise.", "나는 약속했다."], ["No words can say how sorrowfully I gave it.", "얼마나 비통한 심정으로 그 약속을 했는지는 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없다."], ["She instantly took her arm from my waist, walked away to the window, and stood looking out with her back to me.", "그 아이는 곧바로 내 허리에서 팔을 풀고 창가로 걸어가, 내게 등을 돌린 채 밖을 내다보고 서 있었다."], ["After a moment she spoke once more, but without turning round, without allowing me to catch the smallest glimpse of her face.", "잠시 후 그 아이는 다시 입을 열었으나, 몸을 돌리지도 않았고 제 얼굴을 조금이라도 내가 볼 수 있게 하지도 않았다."]], [["“Are you going to my uncle’s room?”", "“삼촌 방으로 가시려는 거지요?”"], ["she asked.", "로라가 물었다."], ["“Will you say that I consent to whatever arrangement he may think best?", "“삼촌이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 무엇이든 따르겠다고 전해 주시겠어요?"], ["Never mind leaving me, Marian.", "저를 두고 가셔도 괜찮아요, 매리언."], ["I shall be better alone for a little while.”", "잠시 혼자 있는 편이 나을 거예요.”"]], [["I went out. If, as soon as I got into the passage, I could have transported Mr.", "나는 밖으로 나왔다."], ["Fairlie and Sir Percival Glyde to the uttermost ends of the earth by lifting one of my fingers, that finger would have been raised without an instant’s hesitation.", "복도로 나서자마자 손가락 하나만 들어 올려 페얼리 씨와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 땅끝으로 보내 버릴 수 있었다면, 나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그 손가락을 들었으리라."], ["For once my unhappy temper now stood my friend.", "이번만큼은 나의 그 고약한 성미가 내 편이 되어 주었다."], ["I should have broken down altogether and burst into a violent fit of crying, if my tears had not been all burnt up in the heat of my anger. As it was, I dashed into Mr.", "노여움의 열기에 눈물이 죄다 타 버리지 않았더라면, 나는 완전히 무너져 격렬하게 울음을 터뜨렸을 것이다."], ["Fairlie’s room—called to him as harshly as possible, “Laura consents to the twenty-second”—and dashed out again without waiting for a word of answer.", "하지만 실제로는 페얼리 씨의 방으로 뛰어들어 되도록 거칠게 “로라가 22일에 동의했습니다” 하고 외치고는, 대답 한마디 기다리지 않고 다시 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["I banged the door after me, and I hope I shattered Mr. Fairlie’s nervous system for the rest of the day.", "나는 문을 쾅 닫았고, 그 소리로 그날 남은 시간 내내 페얼리 씨의 신경이 산산조각 났기를 바란다."]], [["28th.—This morning I read poor Hartright’s farewell letter over again, a doubt having crossed my mind since yesterday, whether I am acting wisely in concealing the fact of his departure from Laura.", "28일.—오늘 아침 나는 가엾은 하트라이트의 작별 편지를 다시 읽었다. 그가 떠났다는 사실을 로라에게 숨기는 것이 과연 현명한 처사인지, 어제부터 마음에 의심이 들었기 때문이다."]], [["On reflection, I still think I am right.", "곰곰이 생각해 보아도 내 판단이 옳은 듯하다."], ["The allusions in his letter to the preparations made for the expedition to Central America, all show that the leaders of it know it to be dangerous.", "중앙아메리카 탐험을 위한 준비에 관해 편지에 언급된 대목들은 하나같이 그 일을 이끄는 이들조차 그것이 위험하다는 것을 알고 있음을 보여 준다."], ["If the discovery of this makes me uneasy, what would it make HER?", "이 사실을 알게 된 것이 나조차 불안하게 만든다면, 그 아이에게는 어떠하겠는가?"], ["It is bad enough to feel that his departure has deprived us of the friend of all others to whose devotion we could trust in the hour of need, if ever that hour comes and finds us helpless; but it is far worse to know that he has gone from us to face the perils of a bad climate, a wild country, and a disturbed population.", "도움이 절실한 순간이—언젠가 그런 순간이 닥쳐 우리를 무력하게 만든다면—그때 헌신을 믿고 기댈 수 있는 단 하나의 친구를 그의 출발이 앗아 갔다는 사실만으로도 이미 충분히 괴롭다. 하물며 그가 우리 곁을 떠나 나쁜 기후와 거친 땅과 소요에 휩싸인 주민들의 위험을 마주하러 갔다는 것을 안다면 그보다 훨씬 더 견디기 어려울 것이다."], ["Surely it would be a cruel candour to tell Laura this, without a pressing and a positive necessity for it?", "절박하고 분명한 필요도 없이 이런 이야기를 로라에게 하는 것은 잔인한 솔직함이 아니겠는가?"]], [["I almost doubt whether I ought not to go a step farther, and burn the letter at once, for fear of its one day falling into wrong hands.", "한 걸음 더 나아가, 언젠가 이 편지가 엉뚱한 손에 들어갈까 두려우니 당장 태워 버려야 하지 않을까 하는 생각마저 든다."], ["It not only refers to Laura in terms which ought to remain a secret for ever between the writer and me, but it reiterates his suspicion—so obstinate, so unaccountable, and so alarming—that he has been secretly watched since he left Limmeridge.", "그 편지는 쓴 사람과 나 사이에 영영 비밀로 남아야 할 표현으로 로라를 언급하고 있을 뿐 아니라, 리머리지를 떠난 뒤로 자신이 은밀히 감시당하고 있다는 그 고집스럽고 설명할 길 없고 불안하기 짝이 없는 의심을 거듭 되풀이하고 있다."], ["He declares that he saw the faces of the two strange men who followed him about the streets of London, watching him among the crowd which gathered at Liverpool to see the expedition embark, and he positively asserts that he heard the name of Anne Catherick pronounced behind him as he got into the boat.", "그는 런던 거리에서 자기를 따라다니던 낯선 두 남자의 얼굴을, 탐험대의 출항을 구경하러 리버풀에 모인 군중 속에서 자기를 지켜보고 있는 모습으로 다시 보았다고 단언한다. 그리고 배에 오를 때 등 뒤에서 앤 캐서릭이라는 이름이 불리는 것을 분명히 들었다고 잘라 말한다."], ["His own words are, “These events have a meaning, these events must lead to a result.", "그의 표현을 그대로 옮기면 이렇다. “이 사건들에는 의미가 있습니다."], ["The mystery of Anne Catherick is NOT cleared up yet.", "이 사건들은 반드시 어떤 결말로 이어질 것입니다."], ["She may never cross my path again, but if ever she crosses yours, make better use of the opportunity, Miss Halcombe, than I made of it.", "앤 캐서릭의 수수께끼는 아직 풀리지 않았습니다. 그 여자가 다시는 내 앞을 지나가지 않을지도 모르지만, 혹시라도 홀콤 양 앞을 지나가거든 내가 그 기회를 썼던 것보다 부디 더 잘 써 주십시오."], ["I speak on strong conviction—I entreat you to remember what I say.”", "나는 굳은 확신에서 말하는 것입니다. 내가 하는 말을 기억해 주시기를 간청합니다.”"], ["These are his own expressions.", "이것이 그가 쓴 그대로의 말이다."], ["There is no danger of my forgetting them—my memory is only too ready to dwell on any words of Hartright’s that refer to Anne Catherick.", "내가 그 말을 잊을 염려는 없다. 앤 캐서릭과 관련된 하트라이트의 말이라면 내 기억은 지나칠 만큼 쉽게 곱씹으니까."], ["But there is danger in my keeping the letter.", "그러나 그 편지를 간직하는 데에는 위험이 있다."], ["The merest accident might place it at the mercy of strangers.", "아주 사소한 우연만으로도 그것이 낯선 이들의 손에 넘어갈 수 있다."], ["I may fall ill—I may die.", "내가 병이 들 수도 있고—죽을 수도 있다."], ["Better to burn it at once, and have one anxiety the less.", "당장 태워 버리고 근심 하나를 더는 편이 낫다."]], [["It is burnt.", "태웠다."], ["The ashes of his farewell letter—the last he may ever write to me—lie in a few black fragments on the hearth.", "그의 작별 편지—어쩌면 그가 내게 쓰는 마지막 편지가 될지도 모를 그것—의 재가 벽난로 바닥에 검은 부스러기 몇 조각으로 남아 있다."], ["Is this the sad end to all that sad story?", "이것이 그 모든 슬픈 이야기의 슬픈 끝인가?"], ["Oh, not the end—surely, surely not the end already!", "아, 끝일 리 없다—분명히, 분명히 아직 끝은 아니다!"]], [["29th.—The preparations for the marriage have begun.", "29일.—결혼 준비가 시작되었다."], ["The dressmaker has come to receive her orders.", "재봉사가 주문을 받으러 왔다."], ["Laura is perfectly impassive, perfectly careless about the question of all others in which a woman’s personal interests are most closely bound up.", "로라는 여자의 개인적인 관심이 무엇보다도 가장 깊이 얽히는 이 문제에 대해 조금도 동요하지 않고, 조금도 마음을 쓰지 않는다."], ["She has left it all to the dressmaker and to me.", "모든 것을 재봉사와 나에게 맡겨 버렸다."], ["If poor Hartright had been the baronet, and the husband of her father’s choice, how differently she would have behaved!", "가엾은 하트라이트가 준남작이었고 그 아이 아버지가 택한 남편이었다면, 그 아이는 얼마나 다르게 굴었을까!"], ["How anxious and capricious she would have been, and what a hard task the best of dressmakers would have found it to please her!", "얼마나 조바심 내고 변덕을 부렸을 것이며, 아무리 솜씨 좋은 재봉사라도 그 아이를 만족시키기가 얼마나 힘겨웠을까!"]], [["30th.—We hear every day from Sir Percival.", "30일.—퍼시벌 경에게서 날마다 소식이 온다."], ["The last news is that the alterations in his house will occupy from four to six months before they can be properly completed.", "가장 최근 소식은 그의 저택 개조가 제대로 마무리되려면 넉 달에서 여섯 달은 걸리리라는 것이다."], ["If painters, paperhangers, and upholsterers could make happiness as well as splendour, I should be interested about their proceedings in Laura’s future home.", "페인트공과 도배공과 실내장식업자가 화려함뿐 아니라 행복까지 만들어 낼 수 있다면, 나는 로라가 앞으로 살 집에서 벌어지는 그들의 작업에 관심을 가졌을 것이다."], ["As it is, the only part of Sir Percival’s last letter which does not leave me as it found me, perfectly indifferent to all his plans and projects, is the part which refers to the wedding tour.", "그러나 실상 퍼시벌 경의 지난번 편지에서 그의 온갖 계획과 구상에 대한 나의 완전한 무관심을 그나마 흔들어 놓은 대목은 신혼여행에 관한 부분뿐이다."], ["He proposes, as Laura is delicate, and as the winter threatens to be unusually severe, to take her to Rome, and to remain in Italy until the early part of next summer.", "그는 로라가 몸이 약하고 이번 겨울이 유난히 혹독하리라 하니, 그 아이를 로마로 데려가 이듬해 초여름까지 이탈리아에 머물자고 제안한다."], ["If this plan should not be approved, he is equally ready, although he has no establishment of his own in town, to spend the season in London, in the most suitable furnished house that can be obtained for the purpose.", "만약 이 계획이 마음에 들지 않는다면, 자기는 런던 시내에 따로 거처가 없기는 하지만 그 목적에 가장 알맞은 가구 딸린 집을 구해 런던에서 사교철을 보내는 것도 마찬가지로 좋다고 한다."]], [["Putting myself and my own feelings entirely out of the question (which it is my duty to do, and which I have done), I, for one, have no doubt of the propriety of adopting the first of these proposals.", "나 자신과 내 감정은 전적으로 문제 밖에 두고—그렇게 하는 것이 내 의무이며 나는 그렇게 해 왔다—적어도 나로서는 이 두 제안 가운데 첫 번째를 받아들이는 것이 옳다는 데 조금도 의심이 없다."], ["In either case a separation between Laura and me is inevitable.", "어느 쪽이든 로라와 나의 이별은 피할 수 없다."], ["It will be a longer separation, in the event of their going abroad, than it would be in the event of their remaining in London—but we must set against this disadvantage the benefit to Laura, on the other side, of passing the winter in a mild climate, and more than that, the immense assistance in raising her spirits, and reconciling her to her new existence, which the mere wonder and excitement of travelling for the first time in her life in the most interesting country in the world, must surely afford.", "두 사람이 외국으로 나가면 런던에 머무는 경우보다 이별이 더 길어지겠지만, 그 불리함에 맞세워야 할 것은 그 아이에게 돌아올 이로움이다. 온화한 기후에서 겨울을 나는 것, 그리고 그보다 더 중요하게는, 세상에서 가장 흥미로운 나라를 난생처음 여행한다는 그 놀라움과 설렘만으로도 그 아이의 기운을 북돋우고 새로운 삶과 화해하게 하는 데 크나큰 도움이 되리라는 것이다."], ["She is not of a disposition to find resources in the conventional gaieties and excitements of London.", "그 아이는 런던의 판에 박힌 유흥과 자극에서 위안을 찾을 성품이 아니다."], ["They would only make the first oppression of this lamentable marriage fall the heavier on her.", "그런 것들은 이 개탄스러운 결혼이 처음으로 주는 압박을 그 아이에게 더욱 무겁게 얹어 놓을 뿐이리라."], ["I dread the beginning of her new life more than words can tell, but I see some hope for her if she travels—none if she remains at home.", "나는 그 아이의 새 삶이 시작되는 것이 말로 다 못 할 만큼 두렵다. 그러나 여행을 한다면 얼마간 희망이 보이고, 집에 머문다면 아무 희망도 보이지 않는다."]], [["It is strange to look back at this latest entry in my journal, and to find that I am writing of the marriage and the parting with Laura, as people write of a settled thing.", "일기의 이 마지막 대목을 돌이켜 보고, 내가 결혼과 로라와의 이별을 마치 이미 정해진 일을 적듯 쓰고 있음을 발견하니 이상한 노릇이다."], ["It seems so cold and so unfeeling to be looking at the future already in this cruelly composed way.", "벌써부터 이토록 잔인하리만치 차분한 태도로 앞일을 내다본다는 것이 참으로 차갑고 무정해 보인다."], ["But what other way is possible, now that the time is drawing so near?", "그러나 때가 이렇게 가까워진 마당에 달리 어떤 태도가 가능하겠는가?"], ["Before another month is over our heads she will be HIS Laura instead of mine!", "한 달이 채 지나기 전에 그 아이는 내 로라가 아니라 그의 로라가 될 것이다!"], ["HIS Laura!", "그의 로라라니!"], ["I am as little able to realise the idea which those two words convey—my mind feels almost as dulled and stunned by it—as if writing of her marriage were like writing of her death.", "그 두 마디가 담고 있는 뜻을 나는 도무지 실감하지 못하겠다. 그 아이의 결혼에 대해 쓰는 것이 마치 그 아이의 죽음에 대해 쓰는 것과 같기라도 한 듯, 내 정신은 그 말에 둔해지고 얼얼해질 따름이다."]], [["December 1st.—A sad, sad day—a day that I have no heart to describe at any length.", "12월 1일.—슬프고 슬픈 날, 길게 적을 마음이 도무지 나지 않는 하루였다."], ["After weakly putting it off last night, I was obliged to speak to her this morning of Sir Percival’s proposal about the wedding tour.", "어젯밤 나약하게 미루었던 일을 오늘 아침에는 하는 수 없이 해야 했다. 신혼여행에 관한 퍼시벌 경의 제안을 그 아이에게 이야기한 것이다."]], [["In the full conviction that I should be with her wherever she went, the poor child—for a child she is still in many things—was almost happy at the prospect of seeing the wonders of Florence and Rome and Naples.", "어디를 가든 내가 함께 있으리라 굳게 믿고 있던 터라, 그 가엾은 아이는—여러모로 아직 어린아이나 다름없다—피렌체와 로마와 나폴리의 경이를 보게 되리라는 기대에 거의 행복해했다."], ["It nearly broke my heart to dispel her delusion, and to bring her face to face with the hard truth.", "그 환상을 깨뜨리고 그 아이를 모진 진실 앞에 마주 세우는 일에 내 가슴은 찢어질 듯했다."], ["I was obliged to tell her that no man tolerates a rival—not even a woman rival—in his wife’s affections, when he first marries, whatever he may do afterwards.", "나는 어떤 남자든 결혼 초에는 아내의 애정을 두고 경쟁자를—그것이 여자 경쟁자라 할지라도—참지 못한다는 것을, 나중에는 어떻게 하든 간에 처음에는 그렇다는 것을 그 아이에게 말해야만 했다."], ["I was obliged to warn her that my chance of living with her permanently under her own roof, depended entirely on my not arousing Sir Percival’s jealousy and distrust by standing between them at the beginning of their marriage, in the position of the chosen depositary of his wife’s closest secrets.", "또 내가 그 아이의 지붕 아래 오래도록 함께 살 수 있을지는, 결혼 초에 두 사람 사이에 끼어들어 아내의 가장 은밀한 비밀을 맡아 두는 사람 노릇을 함으로써 퍼시벌 경의 질투와 불신을 자극하지 않느냐에 전적으로 달려 있다고 그 아이에게 일러 주어야만 했다."], ["Drop by drop I poured the profaning bitterness of this world’s wisdom into that pure heart and that innocent mind, while every higher and better feeling within me recoiled from my miserable task.", "이 세상 지혜의 그 더럽히는 쓴 즙을 나는 한 방울 한 방울 그 순결한 마음과 티 없는 정신에 부어 넣었고, 그러는 동안 내 안의 더 높고 더 나은 감정은 모두 이 비참한 소임 앞에서 움츠러들었다."], ["It is over now.", "이제 다 끝났다."], ["She has learnt her hard, her inevitable lesson.", "그 아이는 모질고도 피할 수 없는 교훈을 배웠다."], ["The simple illusions of her girlhood are gone, and my hand has stripped them off.", "소녀 시절의 단순한 환상은 사라졌고, 그것을 벗겨 낸 것은 내 손이었다."], ["Better mine than his—that is all my consolation—better mine than his.", "그의 손보다는 내 손이 낫다—그것만이 내 유일한 위안이다—그의 손보다는 내 손이 낫다."]], [["So the first proposal is the proposal accepted.", "그리하여 첫 번째 제안이 받아들여진 제안이 되었다."], ["They are to go to Italy, and I am to arrange, with Sir Percival’s permission, for meeting them and staying with them when they return to England.", "두 사람은 이탈리아로 가고, 나는 퍼시벌 경의 허락을 얻어 두 사람이 영국으로 돌아올 때 만나 함께 지내도록 준비하기로 했다."], ["In other words, I am to ask a personal favour, for the first time in my life, and to ask it of the man of all others to whom I least desire to owe a serious obligation of any kind.", "달리 말하면, 나는 태어나 처음으로 개인적인 부탁을 해야 하며, 그것도 하필 어떤 종류의 무거운 신세도 지고 싶지 않은 바로 그 사람에게 해야 한다."], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["I think I could do even more than that, for Laura’s sake.", "로라를 위해서라면 그보다 더한 일도 할 수 있으리라 생각한다."]], [["2nd.—On looking back, I find myself always referring to Sir Percival in disparaging terms.", "2일.—돌이켜 보니 나는 언제나 퍼시벌 경을 깎아내리는 말로 적고 있었다."], ["In the turn affairs have now taken, I must and will root out my prejudice against him.", "일이 이렇게 된 마당에, 나는 그에 대한 편견을 반드시 뿌리 뽑아야 하고 또 뽑을 것이다."], ["I cannot think how it first got into my mind.", "그것이 처음에 어떻게 내 마음에 들어왔는지 도무지 알 수 없다."], ["It certainly never existed in former times.", "예전에는 분명 그런 것이 없었다."]], [["Is it Laura’s reluctance to become his wife that has set me against him?", "그의 아내가 되기를 꺼리는 로라의 마음이 나를 그에게 등 돌리게 한 것일까?"], ["Have Hartright’s perfectly intelligible prejudices infected me without my suspecting their influence?", "하트라이트의, 충분히 이해할 만한 그 편견이 나도 모르는 사이에 내게 옮은 것일까?"], ["Does that letter of Anne Catherick’s still leave a lurking distrust in my mind, in spite of Sir Percival’s explanation, and of the proof in my possession of the truth of it?", "퍼시벌 경의 해명이 있었고 그 해명이 사실임을 입증하는 증거까지 내 손에 있는데도, 앤 캐서릭의 그 편지가 여전히 내 마음속에 잠복한 불신을 남겨 놓은 것일까?"], ["I cannot account for the state of my own feelings; the one thing I am certain of is, that it is my duty—doubly my duty now—not to wrong Sir Percival by unjustly distrusting him.", "내 감정이 왜 이런 상태인지 나는 설명할 수 없다. 다만 한 가지 확실한 것은, 부당하게 그를 의심함으로써 퍼시벌 경에게 잘못을 저지르지 않는 것이 내 의무—이제는 곱절로 내 의무—라는 사실이다."], ["If it has got to be a habit with me always to write of him in the same unfavourable manner, I must and will break myself of this unworthy tendency, even though the effort should force me to close the pages of my journal till the marriage is over!", "만약 그에 대해 언제나 같은 식으로 나쁘게 쓰는 것이 내 버릇이 되어 버렸다면, 나는 이 못난 습성을 반드시 고쳐야 하고 또 고칠 것이다. 설령 그러기 위해 결혼이 끝날 때까지 이 일기장을 덮어야 한다 해도 말이다!"], ["I am seriously dissatisfied with myself—I will write no more to-day.", "나는 나 자신이 몹시 못마땅하다—오늘은 더 쓰지 않겠다."]], [["December 16th.—A whole fortnight has passed, and I have not once opened these pages.", "12월 16일.—꼬박 보름이 지나도록 나는 이 일기장을 한 번도 펴지 않았다."], ["I have been long enough away from my journal to come back to it with a healthier and better mind, I hope, so far as Sir Percival is concerned.", "일기에서 충분히 오래 떨어져 있었으니, 적어도 퍼시벌 경에 관한 한 좀 더 건강하고 나은 마음으로 돌아왔기를 바란다."]], [["There is not much to record of the past two weeks.", "지난 두 주 동안 기록할 일은 그리 많지 않다."], ["The dresses are almost all finished, and the new travelling trunks have been sent here from London.", "드레스는 거의 다 완성되었고, 새 여행 가방들이 런던에서 이곳으로 도착했다."], ["Poor dear Laura hardly leaves me for a moment all day, and last night, when neither of us could sleep, she came and crept into my bed to talk to me there.", "가엾고 사랑스러운 로라는 온종일 잠시도 내 곁을 떠나지 않고, 어젯밤에는 둘 다 잠을 이루지 못하자 내 침대로 기어 들어와 거기서 이야기를 나누었다."], ["“I shall lose you so soon, Marian,”", "“이렇게 곧 매리언을 잃게 되잖아요.”"], ["she said; “I must make the most of you while I can.”", "그 아이가 말했다. “그러니 있을 수 있을 때 실컷 곁에 있어야지요.”"]], [["They are to be married at Limmeridge Church, and thank Heaven, not one of the neighbours is to be invited to the ceremony. The only visitor will be our old friend, Mr.", "두 사람은 리머리지 교회에서 결혼식을 올리기로 했고, 고맙게도 이웃 가운데 식에 초대된 사람은 하나도 없다."], ["Arnold, who is to come from Polesdean to give Laura away, her uncle being far too delicate to trust himself outside the door in such inclement weather as we now have.", "유일한 손님은 우리의 오랜 친구 아널드 씨로, 로라의 손을 잡고 신랑에게 넘겨 주기 위해 폴스딘에서 오기로 했다. 그 아이의 삼촌은 몸이 너무 약해서 지금 같은 궂은 날씨에 문밖을 나설 엄두를 내지 못하기 때문이다."], ["If I were not determined, from this day forth, to see nothing but the bright side of our prospects, the melancholy absence of any male relative of Laura’s, at the most important moment of her life, would make me very gloomy and very distrustful of the future.", "오늘부터는 우리 앞날의 밝은 면만 보기로 굳게 마음먹지 않았더라면, 인생에서 가장 중요한 순간에 로라 쪽 남자 친척이 한 사람도 없다는 그 서글픈 사실이 나를 몹시 우울하게, 또 몹시 앞날을 믿지 못하게 만들었으리라."], ["But I have done with gloom and distrust—that is to say, I have done with writing about either the one or the other in this journal.", "그러나 나는 우울과 불신과는 이제 작별했다—다시 말해, 이 일기에 그 둘 중 어느 것에 대해서도 쓰지 않기로 했다."]], [["Sir Percival is to arrive to-morrow.", "퍼시벌 경은 내일 도착한다."], ["He offered, in case we wished to treat him on terms of rigid etiquette, to write and ask our clergyman to grant him the hospitality of the rectory, during the short period of his sojourn at Limmeridge, before the marriage. Under the circumstances, neither Mr.", "그는 우리가 그를 엄격한 예법대로 대하기를 바란다면, 결혼 전 리머리지에 머무는 짧은 기간 동안 목사관에 신세를 지게 해 달라고 우리 교구 목사에게 편지를 써서 청하겠다고 했다."], ["Fairlie nor I thought it at all necessary for us to trouble ourselves about attending to trifling forms and ceremonies.", "사정이 이러하니 페얼리 씨도 나도 사소한 형식과 예절에 신경 쓸 필요가 조금도 없다고 여겼다."], ["In our wild moorland country, and in this great lonely house, we may well claim to be beyond the reach of the trivial conventionalities which hamper people in other places.", "이 거친 황야의 고장, 이 크고 외로운 저택에서라면, 다른 곳 사람들을 옭아매는 하찮은 관습 따위는 우리에게 미치지 못한다고 얼마든지 주장할 만하다."], ["I wrote to Sir Percival to thank him for his polite offer, and to beg that he would occupy his old rooms, just as usual, at Limmeridge House.", "나는 퍼시벌 경에게 편지를 써서 그 정중한 제안에 감사를 표하고, 예전처럼 리머리지 하우스의 늘 쓰던 방을 쓰시라고 청했다."]], [["17th.—He arrived to-day, looking, as I thought, a little worn and anxious, but still talking and laughing like a man in the best possible spirits.", "17일.—그가 오늘 도착했다. 내 눈에는 얼마간 지치고 근심스러워 보였으나, 그래도 더할 나위 없이 기분 좋은 사람처럼 이야기하고 웃었다."], ["He brought with him some really beautiful presents in jewellery, which Laura received with her best grace, and, outwardly at least, with perfect self-possession.", "그는 참으로 아름다운 보석 선물을 몇 가지 가져왔고, 로라는 더없이 우아하게, 적어도 겉으로는 완벽하게 침착한 태도로 그것을 받았다."], ["The only sign I can detect of the struggle it must cost her to preserve appearances at this trying time, expresses itself in a sudden unwillingness, on her part, ever to be left alone.", "이 힘겨운 시기에 겉모습을 지키느라 그 아이가 치르고 있을 싸움을 짐작하게 하는 유일한 조짐은, 혼자 남겨지는 것을 갑자기 꺼리게 되었다는 점이다."], ["Instead of retreating to her own room, as usual, she seems to dread going there.", "여느 때처럼 제 방으로 물러가기는커녕, 그 아이는 그리로 가기를 두려워하는 듯하다."], ["When I went upstairs to-day, after lunch, to put on my bonnet for a walk, she volunteered to join me, and again, before dinner, she threw the door open between our two rooms, so that we might talk to each other while we were dressing.", "오늘 점심 뒤에 산책하려고 모자를 쓰러 내가 위층에 올라갔더니 그 아이는 자청해서 따라나섰고, 저녁 식사 전에도 옷을 갈아입는 동안 서로 이야기할 수 있도록 우리 두 방 사이의 문을 활짝 열어 두었다."], ["“Keep me always doing something,”", "“늘 무언가 할 일을 주세요.”"], ["she said; “keep me always in company with somebody.", "그 아이가 말했다. “늘 누군가와 함께 있게 해 주세요."], ["Don’t let me think—that is all I ask now, Marian—don’t let me think.”", "생각하게 두지 말아요—지금 제가 바라는 건 그것뿐이에요, 매리언—생각하게 두지 말아요.”"]], [["This sad change in her only increases her attractions for Sir Percival.", "그 아이의 이 서글픈 변화는 퍼시벌 경에게는 그 아이의 매력을 더해 줄 뿐이다."], ["He interprets it, I can see, to his own advantage.", "내가 보기에 그는 그것을 자기에게 유리한 쪽으로 해석한다."], ["There is a feverish flush in her cheeks, a feverish brightness in her eyes, which he welcomes as the return of her beauty and the recovery of her spirits.", "그 아이의 뺨에는 열에 들뜬 홍조가, 눈에는 열에 들뜬 광채가 어려 있는데, 그는 그것을 미모의 회복이자 기운의 회복으로 반긴다."], ["She talked to-day at dinner with a gaiety and carelessness so false, so shockingly out of her character, that I secretly longed to silence her and take her away.", "오늘 저녁 식사 자리에서 그 아이는 어찌나 거짓되고 어찌나 제 성품에 어울리지 않게 명랑하고 무심한 태도로 이야기하던지, 나는 속으로 그 아이를 입 다물게 하고 데리고 나가고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."], ["Sir Percival’s delight and surprise appeared to be beyond all expression.", "퍼시벌 경의 기쁨과 놀라움은 이루 말할 수 없어 보였다."], ["The anxiety which I had noticed on his face when he arrived totally disappeared from it, and he looked, even to my eyes, a good ten years younger than he really is.", "그가 도착했을 때 내가 그 얼굴에서 보았던 근심은 말끔히 사라졌고, 내 눈에조차 그는 실제 나이보다 족히 열 살은 젊어 보였다."]], [["There can be no doubt—though some strange perversity prevents me from seeing it myself—there can be no doubt that Laura’s future husband is a very handsome man.", "의심할 여지가 없다—어떤 이상한 심술이 나 자신으로 하여금 그것을 보지 못하게 하지만—로라의 미래의 남편이 매우 잘생긴 남자라는 데에는 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["Regular features form a personal advantage to begin with—and he has them.", "우선 반듯한 이목구비는 그 자체로 이점인데, 그는 그것을 갖추었다."], ["Bright brown eyes, either in man or woman, are a great attraction—and he has them.", "남자든 여자든 밝은 갈색 눈은 큰 매력인데, 그는 그것도 갖추었다."], ["Even baldness, when it is only baldness over the forehead (as in his case), is rather becoming than not in a man, for it heightens the head and adds to the intelligence of the face.", "벗어진 머리조차, 그의 경우처럼 이마 위쪽만 벗어진 것이라면 남자에게 어울리지 않기는커녕 오히려 어울린다. 머리가 높아 보이고 얼굴에 지성이 더해지기 때문이다."], ["Grace and ease of movement, untiring animation of manner, ready, pliant, conversational powers—all these are unquestionable merits, and all these he certainly possesses. Surely Mr.", "우아하고 편안한 몸가짐, 지칠 줄 모르는 활기, 막힘없고 유연한 화술—이 모두가 두말할 나위 없는 장점이고, 이 모두를 그는 분명히 지니고 있다."], ["Gilmore, ignorant as he is of Laura’s secret, was not to blame for feeling surprised that she should repent of her marriage engagement?", "로라의 비밀을 모르는 길모어 씨가 그 아이가 약혼을 후회한다는 데 놀랐다 한들, 어찌 그를 탓할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Any one else in his place would have shared our good old friend’s opinion.", "그의 자리에 있었다면 누구라도 우리의 그 선량한 옛 친구와 같은 생각을 했으리라."], ["If I were asked, at this moment, to say plainly what defects I have discovered in Sir Percival, I could only point out two.", "지금 이 순간 퍼시벌 경에게서 어떤 결점을 발견했는지 솔직히 말해 보라 한다면, 나는 두 가지밖에 짚어 낼 수 없다."], ["One, his incessant restlessness and excitability—which may be caused, naturally enough, by unusual energy of character.", "하나는 그의 끊임없는 안절부절과 흥분하기 쉬운 성미인데, 이는 남달리 왕성한 기질 탓이라고 얼마든지 볼 수 있다."], ["The other, his short, sharp, ill-tempered manner of speaking to the servants—which may be only a bad habit after all.", "다른 하나는 하인들에게 말할 때의 짧고 날카롭고 성마른 말투인데, 이 역시 결국은 나쁜 버릇에 지나지 않을 수 있다."], ["No, I cannot dispute it, and I will not dispute it—Sir Percival is a very handsome and a very agreeable man.", "아니, 나는 그것을 부인할 수 없고 부인하지도 않겠다—퍼시벌 경은 매우 잘생기고 매우 유쾌한 남자다."], ["There!", "자!"], ["I have written it down at last, and I am glad it’s over.", "마침내 이렇게 적어 놓았으니, 끝나서 기쁘다."]], [["18th.—Feeling weary and depressed this morning, I left Laura with Mrs. Vesey, and went out alone for one of my brisk midday walks, which I have discontinued too much of late.", "18일.—오늘 아침 몸이 나른하고 기분이 가라앉아, 나는 로라를 베지 부인에게 맡겨 두고 요즘 너무 소홀히 했던 한낮의 활기찬 산책을 혼자 나갔다."], ["I took the dry airy road over the moor that leads to Todd’s Corner. After having been out half an hour, I was excessively surprised to see Sir Percival approaching me from the direction of the farm.", "나는 토드 코너로 이어지는, 황야를 가로지르는 마르고 바람 좋은 길을 택했다."], ["He was walking rapidly, swinging his stick, his head erect as usual, and his shooting jacket flying open in the wind.", "나선 지 반 시간쯤 되었을 때, 농장 쪽에서 퍼시벌 경이 나를 향해 걸어오는 것을 보고 나는 몹시 놀랐다."], ["When we met he did not wait for me to ask any questions—he told me at once that he had been to the farm to inquire if Mr.", "그는 지팡이를 휘두르며 여느 때처럼 고개를 꼿꼿이 들고 사냥 재킷 앞자락을 바람에 펄럭이면서 빠르게 걷고 있었다."], ["or Mrs. Todd had received any tidings, since his last visit to Limmeridge, of Anne Catherick.", "우리가 마주쳤을 때 그는 내가 무언가 묻기를 기다리지도 않고, 지난번 리머리지에 왔던 이후로 토드 씨나 토드 부인이 앤 캐서릭에 관해 무슨 소식이라도 들은 것이 있는지 알아보러 농장에 다녀오는 길이라고 대뜸 말했다."]], [["“You found, of course, that they had heard nothing?”", "“물론 아무 소식도 못 들었다고 하던가요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Nothing whatever,”", "“전혀 없다더군요.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I begin to be seriously afraid that we have lost her. Do you happen to know,”", "“저는 그 여자를 아주 놓쳐 버린 것이 아닌가 하고 심각하게 걱정되기 시작합니다."], ["he continued, looking me in the face very attentively “if the artist—Mr.", "혹시 아십니까,” 그는 내 얼굴을 아주 유심히 들여다보며 말을 이었다."], ["Hartright—is in a position to give us any further information?”", "“그 화가—하트라이트 씨—가 우리에게 무언가 더 알려 줄 만한 처지에 있는지요?”"]], [["“He has neither heard of her, nor seen her, since he left Cumberland,”", "“그분은 컴벌랜드를 떠난 뒤로 그 여자의 소식을 듣지도, 그 여자를 보지도 못했습니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["“Very sad,”", "“참으로 안타깝군요.”"], ["said Sir Percival, speaking like a man who was disappointed, and yet, oddly enough, looking at the same time like a man who was relieved.", "퍼시벌 경이 말했다. 실망한 사람 같은 말투였으나, 이상하게도 동시에 안도한 사람처럼 보였다."], ["“It is impossible to say what misfortunes may not have happened to the miserable creature.", "“그 딱한 여자에게 어떤 불행이 닥쳤을지 알 도리가 없습니다."], ["I am inexpressibly annoyed at the failure of all my efforts to restore her to the care and protection which she so urgently needs.”", "그토록 절실히 필요한 보살핌과 보호를 되찾아 주려던 제 노력이 모두 허사가 되었으니 이루 말할 수 없이 분합니다.”"]], [["This time he really looked annoyed.", "이번에는 그가 정말로 분해 보였다."], ["I said a few sympathising words, and we then talked of other subjects on our way back to the house.", "나는 몇 마디 위로의 말을 건넸고, 우리는 저택으로 돌아오는 길에 다른 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Surely my chance meeting with him on the moor has disclosed another favourable trait in his character?", "황야에서 그와 우연히 마주친 일이 그의 성품의 또 다른 좋은 면을 드러내 준 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["Surely it was singularly considerate and unselfish of him to think of Anne Catherick on the eve of his marriage, and to go all the way to Todd’s Corner to make inquiries about her, when he might have passed the time so much more agreeably in Laura’s society?", "결혼을 코앞에 두고 앤 캐서릭을 떠올리고, 로라와 함께 있었다면 훨씬 즐겁게 보낼 수 있었을 시간에 그 먼 토드 코너까지 걸어가 그 여자의 소식을 물어보다니, 참으로 남달리 사려 깊고 이기심 없는 일이 아닌가?"], ["Considering that he can only have acted from motives of pure charity, his conduct, under the circumstances, shows unusual good feeling and deserves extraordinary praise.", "순전히 자비로운 동기에서 한 행동일 수밖에 없음을 생각하면, 이런 상황에서 그의 처신은 보기 드문 선한 마음을 보여 주며 각별한 칭찬을 받아 마땅하다."], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["I give him extraordinary praise—and there’s an end of it.", "나는 그에게 각별한 칭찬을 보낸다—그것으로 끝이다."]], [["19th.—More discoveries in the inexhaustible mine of Sir Percival’s virtues.", "19일.—퍼시벌 경의 미덕이라는 마르지 않는 광맥에서 또 새로운 것들이 발견되었다."]], [["To-day I approached the subject of my proposed sojourn under his wife’s roof when he brings her back to England.", "오늘 나는 그가 아내를 데리고 영국으로 돌아왔을 때 그 지붕 아래 함께 머물고자 하는 내 뜻을 꺼내 보았다."], ["I had hardly dropped my first hint in this direction before he caught me warmly by the hand, and said I had made the very offer to him which he had been, on his side, most anxious to make to me.", "그 방향으로 첫마디를 던지기가 무섭게 그는 내 손을 따뜻하게 붙잡으며, 실은 자기 쪽에서 내게 간청하고 싶어 못 견디던 바로 그 제안을 내가 해 주었다고 말했다."], ["I was the companion of all others whom he most sincerely longed to secure for his wife, and he begged me to believe that I had conferred a lasting favour on him by making the proposal to live with Laura after her marriage, exactly as I had always lived with her before it.", "자기가 아내를 위해 진심으로 얻고 싶었던 벗은 다른 누구도 아닌 나였다면서, 결혼 전에 늘 그랬던 것과 똑같이 결혼 뒤에도 로라와 함께 살겠다고 제안해 준 것은 자기에게 두고두고 베푼 은혜이니 부디 그리 믿어 달라고 했다."]], [["When I had thanked him in her name and mine for his considerate kindness to both of us, we passed next to the subject of his wedding tour, and began to talk of the English society in Rome to which Laura was to be introduced.", "우리 두 사람 모두에게 베푼 그 사려 깊은 친절에 대해 로라와 내 이름으로 감사를 표한 뒤, 우리는 그의 신혼여행 이야기로 넘어가 로마에서 로라가 소개받게 될 영국인 사교계에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["He ran over the names of several friends whom he expected to meet abroad this winter.", "그는 이번 겨울 외국에서 만나리라 기대하는 몇몇 친구의 이름을 죽 늘어놓았다."], ["They were all English, as well as I can remember, with one exception.", "내 기억이 맞다면 한 사람만 빼고 모두 영국인이었다."], ["The one exception was Count Fosco.", "그 한 사람의 예외가 포스코 백작이었다."]], [["The mention of the Count’s name, and the discovery that he and his wife are likely to meet the bride and bridegroom on the continent, puts Laura’s marriage, for the first time, in a distinctly favourable light.", "백작의 이름이 나온 것, 그리고 그와 그의 아내가 대륙에서 신혼부부를 만날 듯하다는 사실을 알게 된 것은, 처음으로 로라의 결혼을 뚜렷하게 유리한 빛 아래 놓아 준다."], ["It is likely to be the means of healing a family feud.", "그것은 집안의 반목을 치유하는 계기가 될 법하다."], ["Hitherto Madame Fosco has chosen to forget her obligations as Laura’s aunt out of sheer spite against the late Mr. Fairlie for his conduct in the affair of the legacy.", "지금까지 포스코 백작부인은 유산 문제에서 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 보인 처사에 대한 순전한 앙심 때문에, 로라의 고모로서 지켜야 할 도리를 잊기로 작정하고 있었다."], ["Now however, she can persist in this course of conduct no longer.", "그러나 이제는 그런 태도를 더 고집할 수 없게 되었다."], ["Sir Percival and Count Fosco are old and fast friends, and their wives will have no choice but to meet on civil terms.", "퍼시벌 경과 포스코 백작은 오래되고 굳건한 친구 사이이니, 두 사람의 아내도 예의를 갖추어 만나는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없을 것이다."], ["Madame Fosco in her maiden days was one of the most impertinent women I ever met with—capricious, exacting, and vain to the last degree of absurdity.", "처녀 시절의 포스코 백작부인은 내가 만나 본 여자 가운데 가장 무례한 축에 들었다—변덕스럽고, 요구가 많고, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 허영에 차 있었다."], ["If her husband has succeeded in bringing her to her senses, he deserves the gratitude of every member of the family, and he may have mine to begin with.", "만약 그 남편이 그녀를 제정신으로 돌려놓는 데 성공했다면, 그는 이 집안 모든 사람의 감사를 받아 마땅하며, 우선 내 감사부터 받아도 좋을 것이다."]], [["I am becoming anxious to know the Count.", "나는 백작을 알고 싶어 마음이 조급해지기 시작한다."], ["He is the most intimate friend of Laura’s husband, and in that capacity he excites my strongest interest.", "그는 로라의 남편의 가장 절친한 벗이고, 그 점에서 내 관심을 가장 강하게 자극한다."], ["Neither Laura nor I have ever seen him.", "로라도 나도 그를 본 적이 없다."], ["All I know of him is that his accidental presence, years ago, on the steps of the Trinita del Monte at Rome, assisted Sir Percival’s escape from robbery and assassination at the critical moment when he was wounded in the hand, and might the next instant have been wounded in the heart.", "내가 그에 관해 아는 것이라고는, 몇 해 전 로마의 트리니타 델 몬테 계단에서 그가 우연히 그 자리에 있었던 덕분에, 퍼시벌 경이 손에 상처를 입고 다음 순간에는 가슴에 상처를 입을 뻔했던 그 위태로운 찰나에 강도와 암살에서 벗어날 수 있었다는 것뿐이다."], ["I remember also that, at the time of the late Mr. Fairlie’s absurd objections to his sister’s marriage, the Count wrote him a very temperate and sensible letter on the subject, which, I am ashamed to say, remained unanswered.", "또 기억나는 것은, 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 제 누이의 결혼에 터무니없는 반대를 하던 때에 백작이 그 문제에 관해 매우 절제되고 사리에 밝은 편지를 보냈으나, 부끄럽게도 그 편지가 답장을 받지 못한 채 남았다는 사실이다."], ["This is all I know of Sir Percival’s friend.", "퍼시벌 경의 친구에 대해 내가 아는 것은 이것이 전부다."], ["I wonder if he will ever come to England?", "그가 언젠가 영국에 오게 될까?"], ["I wonder if I shall like him?", "내가 그를 좋아하게 될까?"]], [["My pen is running away into mere speculation.", "내 펜이 한낱 억측으로 달아나고 있다."], ["Let me return to sober matter of fact.", "냉정한 사실로 돌아가자."], ["It is certain that Sir Percival’s reception of my venturesome proposal to live with his wife was more than kind, it was almost affectionate.", "그의 아내와 함께 살겠다는 나의 당돌한 제안을 퍼시벌 경이 받아들인 태도가 친절 이상이었다는 것, 거의 애정 어린 것이었다는 사실만은 분명하다."], ["I am sure Laura’s husband will have no reason to complain of me if I can only go on as I have begun.", "내가 시작한 대로만 계속해 나갈 수 있다면 로라의 남편이 나를 두고 불평할 일은 없으리라 믿는다."], ["I have already declared him to be handsome, agreeable, full of good feeling towards the unfortunate and full of affectionate kindness towards me.", "나는 이미 그가 잘생겼고, 유쾌하고, 불행한 이들에 대한 따뜻한 마음이 가득하고, 나에 대한 다정한 친절이 가득하다고 적어 놓았다."], ["Really, I hardly know myself again, in my new character of Sir Percival’s warmest friend.", "정말이지 퍼시벌 경의 가장 열렬한 친구라는 이 새로운 배역을 맡은 나 자신을 나조차 알아보기 어려울 지경이다."]], [["20th.—I hate Sir Percival!", "20일.—나는 퍼시벌 경이 밉다!"], ["I flatly deny his good looks.", "그가 잘생겼다는 것을 딱 잘라 부인한다."], ["I consider him to be eminently ill-tempered and disagreeable, and totally wanting in kindness and good feeling.", "나는 그를 지독히 성마르고 불쾌하며, 친절과 따뜻한 마음이라고는 도무지 없는 사람이라고 여긴다."], ["Last night the cards for the married couple were sent home.", "어젯밤 신혼부부의 명함이 집으로 배달되었다."], ["Laura opened the packet and saw her future name in print for the first time.", "로라가 꾸러미를 열고 제 앞날의 이름이 인쇄된 것을 처음으로 보았다."], ["Sir Percival looked over her shoulder familiarly at the new card which had already transformed Miss Fairlie into Lady Glyde—smiled with the most odious self-complacency, and whispered something in her ear.", "퍼시벌 경은 페얼리 양을 이미 글라이드 부인으로 바꾸어 놓은 그 새 명함을 그 아이의 어깨 너머로 스스럼없이 들여다보더니, 더없이 역겨운 자기만족의 미소를 띠고 그 아이의 귀에 무언가를 속삭였다."], ["I don’t know what it was—Laura has refused to tell me—but I saw her face turn to such a deadly whiteness that I thought she would have fainted.", "그것이 무슨 말이었는지 나는 모른다—로라가 말해 주기를 거부했으니까—그러나 나는 그 아이의 얼굴이 죽은 사람처럼 하얗게 질리는 것을 보았고, 기절하는 줄로만 알았다."], ["He took no notice of the change—he seemed to be barbarously unconscious that he had said anything to pain her.", "그는 그 변화를 알아채지도 못했다—자기가 그 아이를 아프게 할 말을 했다는 것을 야만스러우리만큼 의식하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["All my old feelings of hostility towards him revived on the instant, and all the hours that have passed since have done nothing to dissipate them.", "그를 향한 나의 옛 적개심이 그 순간 되살아났고, 그 뒤로 지나간 시간들도 그것을 조금도 흩어 놓지 못했다."], ["I am more unreasonable and more unjust than ever.", "나는 그 어느 때보다 더 사리에 어긋나고 더 부당하다."], ["In three words—how glibly my pen writes them!—in three words, I hate him.", "세 마디로—내 펜이 어쩌면 이토록 술술 적어 내는지!—세 마디로, 나는 그가 밉다."]], [["21st.—Have the anxieties of this anxious time shaken me a little, at last?", "21일.—이 불안한 시기의 근심들이 마침내 나를 조금 흔들어 놓은 것일까?"], ["I have been writing, for the last few days, in a tone of levity which, Heaven knows, is far enough from my heart, and which it has rather shocked me to discover on looking back at the entries in my journal.", "나는 지난 며칠 동안 하늘도 아시다시피 내 진심과는 한참 동떨어진 가벼운 어조로 글을 써 왔고, 일기의 지난 대목들을 돌이켜 보다 그것을 발견하고는 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["Perhaps I may have caught the feverish excitement of Laura’s spirits for the last week.", "지난 한 주 동안 열에 들뜬 로라의 기분이 내게 옮았는지도 모른다."], ["If so, the fit has already passed away from me, and has left me in a very strange state of mind.", "그렇다면 그 발작은 이미 내게서 지나갔고, 나를 아주 이상한 마음 상태에 남겨 두었다."], ["A persistent idea has been forcing itself on my attention, ever since last night, that something will yet happen to prevent the marriage.", "어젯밤부터 줄곧 한 가지 생각이 끈질기게 내 마음을 파고들고 있다. 결혼을 가로막을 무슨 일인가가 아직 일어나리라는 생각이다."], ["What has produced this singular fancy?", "무엇이 이런 기이한 공상을 낳았을까?"], ["Is it the indirect result of my apprehensions for Laura’s future?", "로라의 앞날에 대한 나의 염려가 간접적으로 빚어낸 것일까?"], ["Or has it been unconsciously suggested to me by the increasing restlessness and irritability which I have certainly observed in Sir Percival’s manner as the wedding-day draws nearer and nearer?", "아니면 결혼식 날이 가까워질수록 퍼시벌 경의 태도에서 분명히 눈에 띄게 심해지는 안절부절과 짜증이 나도 모르는 사이에 그런 생각을 심어 준 것일까?"], ["Impossible to say.", "알 수 없다."], ["I know that I have the idea—surely the wildest idea, under the circumstances, that ever entered a woman’s head?—but try as I may, I cannot trace it back to its source.", "내가 그런 생각을 품고 있다는 것만은 안다—이런 상황에서 여자의 머리에 떠오른 것 중 가장 터무니없는 생각이 아니겠는가?—그러나 아무리 애를 써도 그 근원을 되짚을 수가 없다."]], [["This last day has been all confusion and wretchedness.", "이 마지막 하루는 온통 혼란과 비참함이었다."], ["How can I write about it?—and yet, I must write.", "이 일을 어떻게 적을 수 있을까?—그러면서도 나는 적어야 한다."], ["Anything is better than brooding over my own gloomy thoughts.", "무엇이든 내 어두운 생각을 곱씹는 것보다는 낫다."]], [["Kind Mrs. Vesey, whom we have all too much overlooked and forgotten of late, innocently caused us a sad morning to begin with.", "요즘 우리 모두가 너무 소홀히 하고 잊고 지냈던 친절한 베지 부인이, 아무 악의 없이 이 하루의 시작을 슬프게 만들었다."], ["She has been, for months past, secretly making a warm Shetland shawl for her dear pupil—a most beautiful and surprising piece of work to be done by a woman at her age and with her habits.", "부인은 지난 몇 달 동안 사랑하는 제자를 위해 따뜻한 셰틀랜드 숄을 몰래 만들고 있었다—그 연세와 그 생활 습관을 지닌 여인이 해냈다고는 믿기 어려울 만큼 아름답고 놀라운 솜씨였다."], ["The gift was presented this morning, and poor warm-hearted Laura completely broke down when the shawl was put proudly on her shoulders by the loving old friend and guardian of her motherless childhood.", "그 선물이 오늘 아침에 전해졌고, 어머니 없이 자란 어린 시절의 다정한 옛 벗이자 보호자였던 부인이 자랑스레 그 숄을 어깨에 걸쳐 주자, 마음 여린 가엾은 로라는 그만 무너져 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["I was hardly allowed time to quiet them both, or even to dry my own eyes, when I was sent for by Mr. Fairlie, to be favoured with a long recital of his arrangements for the preservation of his own tranquillity on the wedding-day.", "두 사람을 달래거나 내 눈물을 닦을 겨를도 거의 없이, 나는 페얼리 씨에게 불려 가 결혼식 날 자신의 안정을 지키기 위한 그의 준비 사항에 대해 장황한 설명을 듣는 영광을 누려야 했다."]], [["“Dear Laura” was to receive his present—a shabby ring, with her affectionate uncle’s hair for an ornament, instead of a precious stone, and with a heartless French inscription inside, about congenial sentiments and eternal friendship—“dear Laura”", "“사랑하는 로라”는 그분의 선물을 받게 되어 있었다—보석 대신 다정한 삼촌의 머리카락을 장식으로 박은 초라한 반지, 안쪽에는 마음이 통하는 정과 영원한 우정 운운하는 무정한 프랑스어 명문이 새겨진 반지를."], ["was to receive this tender tribute from my hands immediately, so that she might have plenty of time to recover from the agitation produced by the gift before she appeared in Mr. Fairlie’s presence.", "“사랑하는 로라”는 이 애틋한 정표를 지체 없이 내 손에서 받아야 했다."], ["“Dear Laura” was to pay him a little visit that evening, and to be kind enough not to make a scene.", "페얼리 씨 앞에 나서기 전에, 선물이 불러일으킨 감동에서 넉넉히 회복할 시간을 갖도록."], ["“Dear Laura” was to pay him another little visit in her wedding-dress the next morning, and to be kind enough, again, not to make a scene.", "“사랑하는 로라”는 그날 저녁에 그분께 잠깐 문안을 드려야 했고, 부디 소란을 피우지 말아 주어야 했다."], ["“Dear Laura” was to look in once more, for the third time, before going away, but without harrowing his feelings by saying WHEN she was going away, and without tears—“in the name of pity, in the name of everything, dear Marian, that is most affectionate and most domestic, and most delightfully and charmingly self-composed, WITHOUT TEARS!”", "“사랑하는 로라”는 이튿날 아침 웨딩드레스 차림으로 다시 한 번 잠깐 문안을 드려야 했고, 이번에도 부디 소란을 피우지 말아 주어야 했다."], ["I was so exasperated by this miserable selfish trifling, at such a time, that I should certainly have shocked Mr.", "“사랑하는 로라”는 떠나기 전에 세 번째로 한 번 더 들러야 했다. 다만 언제 떠나는지를 말해 그분의 감정을 후벼 파는 일은 없어야 했고, 눈물도 없어야 했다—“제발 부탁이니, 다정하고 가정적이고 더없이 사랑스럽고 매력적으로 침착한 모든 것의 이름으로 부탁하네, 매리언, 눈물은 없이!”"], ["Fairlie by some of the hardest and rudest truths he has ever heard in his life, if the arrival of Mr. Arnold from Polesdean had not called me away to new duties downstairs.", "이런 때에 이 가엾도록 이기적인 시시덕거림을 듣고 있자니 어찌나 부아가 치밀던지, 폴스딘에서 아널드 씨가 도착한 덕에 아래층의 새 일거리로 불려 가지 않았더라면 나는 틀림없이 페얼리 씨가 평생 들어 본 적 없는 가장 모질고 무례한 진실을 몇 마디 퍼부어 그분을 기함하게 만들었을 것이다."]], [["The rest of the day is indescribable.", "그날의 나머지는 이루 말할 수가 없다."], ["I believe no one in the house really knew how it passed.", "그 하루가 어떻게 지나갔는지 이 집에서 제대로 아는 사람은 아무도 없으리라 믿는다."], ["The confusion of small events, all huddled together one on the other, bewildered everybody.", "자잘한 일들이 뒤죽박죽 겹쳐 쌓이는 혼란에 모두가 정신이 없었다."], ["There were dresses sent home that had been forgotten—there were trunks to be packed and unpacked and packed again—there were presents from friends far and near, friends high and low.", "잊고 있던 옷들이 배달되어 왔고—짐 가방은 쌌다가 풀었다가 다시 싸야 했으며—멀고 가까운 벗들, 지체 높은 벗들과 낮은 벗들에게서 선물이 들어왔다."], ["We were all needlessly hurried, all nervously expectant of the morrow.", "우리는 하나같이 공연히 서둘렀고, 하나같이 내일을 초조하게 기다렸다."], ["Sir Percival, especially, was too restless now to remain five minutes together in the same place.", "특히 퍼시벌 경은 어찌나 안절부절못하는지 한자리에 오 분도 머물지 못했다."], ["That short, sharp cough of his troubled him more than ever.", "그 짧고 날카로운 기침은 어느 때보다 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["He was in and out of doors all day long, and he seemed to grow so inquisitive on a sudden, that he questioned the very strangers who came on small errands to the house.", "그는 온종일 들락날락했고, 갑자기 어찌나 캐묻기를 좋아하게 되었는지 사소한 심부름으로 집에 온 낯선 사람들에게까지 이것저것 물어 댔다."], ["Add to all this, the one perpetual thought in Laura’s mind and mine, that we were to part the next day, and the haunting dread, unexpressed by either of us, and yet ever present to both, that this deplorable marriage might prove to be the one fatal error of her life and the one hopeless sorrow of mine.", "여기에 더해, 로라와 내 마음속에 한시도 떠나지 않던 한 가지 생각—우리가 내일이면 헤어진다는 것—과, 둘 다 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 둘 다 늘 품고 있던 그 끈질긴 두려움, 곧 이 개탄스러운 결혼이 그 애 인생의 단 하나뿐인 치명적 과오이자 내 인생의 단 하나뿐인 가망 없는 슬픔이 되고 말리라는 두려움을 더해 보라."], ["For the first time in all the years of our close and happy intercourse we almost avoided looking each other in the face, and we refrained, by common consent, from speaking together in private through the whole evening.", "우리가 가깝고 행복하게 지내 온 그 오랜 세월 동안 처음으로 우리는 서로의 얼굴을 마주 보기를 거의 피했고, 누가 먼저랄 것도 없이 저녁 내내 단둘이 이야기하는 일을 삼갔다."], ["I can dwell on it no longer.", "더는 이 대목에 머물 수가 없다."], ["Whatever future sorrows may be in store for me, I shall always look back on this twenty-first of December as the most comfortless and most miserable day of my life.", "앞으로 어떤 슬픔이 나를 기다리고 있든, 나는 언제까지나 이 십이월 이십일일을 내 생애에서 가장 위안 없고 가장 비참한 날로 돌아보게 될 것이다."]], [["I am writing these lines in the solitude of my own room, long after midnight, having just come back from a stolen look at Laura in her pretty little white bed—the bed she has occupied since the days of her girlhood.", "나는 자정이 한참 지난 시각, 내 방의 고요 속에서 이 글을 쓰고 있다. 로라가 소녀 시절부터 써 온 그 예쁘고 자그마한 흰 침대에 누운 모습을 몰래 들여다보고 막 돌아온 참이다."]], [["There she lay, unconscious that I was looking at her—quiet, more quiet than I had dared to hope, but not sleeping.", "그 애는 거기 누워 있었다. 내가 바라보고 있는 줄도 모른 채—조용히, 내가 감히 바라던 것보다 더 조용히, 그러나 잠들지는 않은 채로."], ["The glimmer of the night-light showed me that her eyes were only partially closed—the traces of tears glistened between her eyelids.", "야등의 희미한 빛에 그 애의 눈이 반쯤밖에 감기지 않은 것이 보였다—눈꺼풀 사이로 눈물 자국이 반짝였다."], ["My little keepsake—only a brooch—lay on the table at her bedside, with her prayer-book, and the miniature portrait of her father which she takes with her wherever she goes.", "내가 준 자그마한 기념품—그저 브로치 하나였지만—이 침대 곁 탁자 위에, 기도서와 그 애가 어디를 가든 지니고 다니는 아버지의 작은 초상화와 함께 놓여 있었다. 나는 잠시 베개 뒤에 서서 그 애를 내려다보았다."], ["I waited a moment, looking at her from behind her pillow, as she lay beneath me, with one arm and hand resting on the white coverlid, so still, so quietly breathing, that the frill on her night-dress never moved—I waited, looking at her, as I have seen her thousands of times, as I shall never see her again—and then stole back to my room.", "그 애는 내 아래에 누워, 한쪽 팔과 손을 흰 이불 위에 얹은 채, 어찌나 미동도 없이 고요히 숨을 쉬는지 잠옷의 주름 장식조차 흔들리지 않았다—나는 서서 그 애를 바라보았다. 수천 번을 그렇게 보아 온 것처럼, 그리고 다시는 그렇게 볼 수 없을 것처럼—그러고는 살그머니 내 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["My own love!", "나의 사랑!"], ["with all your wealth, and all your beauty, how friendless you are!", "그 많은 재산과 그 많은 아름다움을 지니고도 너는 어쩌면 이토록 벗이 없느냐!"], ["The one man who would give his heart’s life to serve you is far away, tossing, this stormy night, on the awful sea.", "너를 위해서라면 제 심장의 목숨이라도 내놓을 단 한 사람은 멀리 떠나, 이 사나운 밤에 무서운 바다 위에서 흔들리고 있다."], ["Who else is left to you?", "네게 또 누가 남아 있느냐?"], ["No father, no brother—no living creature but the helpless, useless woman who writes these sad lines, and watches by you for the morning, in sorrow that she cannot compose, in doubt that she cannot conquer.", "아버지도 없고, 오라비도 없고—이 서글픈 글을 쓰며 달랠 수 없는 슬픔과 이겨 낼 수 없는 의혹 속에서 네 곁을 지키며 아침을 기다리는, 무력하고 쓸모없는 여자 하나뿐이 아니냐."], ["Oh, what a trust is to be placed in that man’s hands to-morrow!", "아, 내일이면 그 사내의 손에 얼마나 큰 것이 맡겨지는가!"], ["If ever he forgets it—if ever he injures a hair of her head!——", "그가 언젠가 그것을 잊는다면—그가 언젠가 그 애의 머리카락 한 올이라도 상하게 한다면!——"]], [["THE TWENTY-SECOND OF DECEMBER.", "십이월 이십이일."], ["Seven o’clock.", "일곱 시."], ["A wild, unsettled morning.", "사납고 어수선한 아침이다."], ["She has just risen—better and calmer, now that the time has come, than she was yesterday.", "그 애는 방금 일어났다—막상 때가 닥치고 보니 어제보다 나아 보이고 차분하다."]], [["Ten o’clock. She is dressed.", "열 시."], ["We have kissed each other—we have promised each other not to lose courage.", "그 애는 옷을 다 갖춰 입었다. 우리는 서로 입을 맞추었고—서로 용기를 잃지 말자고 약속했다."], ["I am away for a moment in my own room.", "나는 잠시 내 방에 물러나 있다."], ["In the whirl and confusion of my thoughts, I can detect that strange fancy of some hindrance happening to stop the marriage still hanging about my mind.", "소용돌이치며 뒤엉킨 생각들 가운데서, 무언가 방해가 생겨 이 결혼을 막아 주지 않을까 하는 그 이상한 공상이 아직도 내 머릿속을 맴돌고 있음을 알아차린다."], ["Is it hanging about HIS mind too?", "그것이 그의 머릿속에도 맴돌고 있을까?"], ["I see him from the window, moving hither and thither uneasily among the carriages at the door.—How can I write such folly!", "창문으로 내다보니 그가 문 앞의 마차들 사이를 불안하게 이리저리 오가고 있다.—내가 어쩌자고 이런 어리석은 소리를 적고 있는가!"], ["The marriage is a certainty.", "결혼은 기정사실이다."], ["In less than half an hour we start for the church.", "삼십 분도 못 되어 우리는 교회로 출발한다."]], [["Eleven o’clock.", "열한 시. 다 끝났다."], ["It is all over. They are married.", "두 사람은 부부가 되었다."]], [["Three o’clock.", "세 시."], ["They are gone!", "그들이 떠났다!"], ["I am blind with crying—I can write no more——", "나는 울다 못해 앞이 보이지 않는다—더는 쓸 수가 없다——"]], [["[The First Epoch of the Story closes here.]", "[이야기의 제1기는 여기서 끝난다.]"]], [["# BLACKWATER PARK, HAMPSHIRE.", "# BLACKWATER PARK, HAMPSHIRE."]], [["June 11th, 1850.—Six months to look back on—six long, lonely months since Laura and I last saw each other!", "1850년 6월 11일.—돌이켜볼 여섯 달—로라와 내가 마지막으로 서로를 본 뒤로 길고 외로운 여섯 달이 흘렀다!"]], [["How many days have I still to wait?", "앞으로 며칠을 더 기다려야 하나?"], ["Only one!", "단 하루!"], ["To-morrow, the twelfth, the travellers return to England.", "내일, 십이일이면 여행자들이 영국으로 돌아온다."], ["I can hardly realise my own happiness—I can hardly believe that the next four-and-twenty hours will complete the last day of separation between Laura and me.", "나는 내 행복을 좀처럼 실감하지 못하겠다—앞으로 스물네 시간이면 로라와 나를 갈라놓았던 이별의 마지막 날이 끝난다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없다."]], [["She and her husband have been in Italy all the winter, and afterwards in the Tyrol.", "그녀와 그녀의 남편은 겨울 내내 이탈리아에 있었고, 그 뒤에는 티롤에 있었다."], ["They come back, accompanied by Count Fosco and his wife, who propose to settle somewhere in the neighbourhood of London, and who have engaged to stay at Blackwater Park for the summer months before deciding on a place of residence.", "두 사람은 포스코 백작 부부와 함께 돌아온다. 백작 부부는 런던 근교 어딘가에 자리를 잡을 생각이며, 거처를 정하기 전에 여름 몇 달 동안 블랙워터 파크에 머물기로 약속했다."], ["So long as Laura returns, no matter who returns with her.", "로라만 돌아온다면 누가 함께 오든 상관없다."], ["Sir Percival may fill the house from floor to ceiling, if he likes, on condition that his wife and I inhabit it together.", "퍼시벌 경이 원한다면 이 집을 바닥부터 천장까지 사람으로 채워도 좋다—그의 아내와 내가 이 집에서 함께 살 수만 있다면."]], [["Meanwhile, here I am, established at Blackwater Park, “the ancient and interesting seat” (as the county history obligingly informs me) “of Sir Percival Glyde, Bart.,”", "그러는 사이 나는 이렇게 블랙워터 파크에 자리를 잡았다."], ["and the future abiding-place (as I may now venture to add on my account) of plain Marian Halcombe, spinster, now settled in a snug little sitting-room, with a cup of tea by her side, and all her earthly possessions ranged round her in three boxes and a bag.", "(주(州) 향토지가 친절하게 알려주는 바로는) “준남작 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 유서 깊고 흥미로운 저택”이자, (이제 내 몫으로 감히 덧붙이자면) 평범한 노처녀 매리언 홀콤의 앞날의 거처가 될 이곳에. 나는 지금 아늑한 작은 거실에 자리 잡아 곁에 찻잔을 놓아두었고, 이 세상에서 내가 가진 전부는 상자 세 개와 가방 하나에 담겨 내 주위에 늘어서 있다."]], [["I left Limmeridge yesterday, having received Laura’s delightful letter from Paris the day before.", "나는 어제 리머리지를 떠났다. 그 전날 파리에서 온 로라의 반가운 편지를 받은 뒤였다."], ["I had been previously uncertain whether I was to meet them in London or in Hampshire, but this last letter informed me that Sir Percival proposed to land at Southampton, and to travel straight on to his country-house.", "그때까지 나는 두 사람을 런던에서 만나야 할지 햄프셔에서 만나야 할지 알 수 없었는데, 이 마지막 편지가 알려주기를 퍼시벌 경은 사우샘프턴에 상륙해 곧장 시골집으로 향할 작정이라고 했다."], ["He has spent so much money abroad that he has none left to defray the expenses of living in London for the remainder of the season, and he is economically resolved to pass the summer and autumn quietly at Blackwater.", "그는 외국에서 돈을 너무 많이 써서 남은 사교철 동안 런던에서 지낼 비용을 댈 돈이 하나도 없다. 그래서 여름과 가을을 블랙워터에서 조용히 보내기로 알뜰하게 결심한 것이다."], ["Laura has had more than enough of excitement and change of scene, and is pleased at the prospect of country tranquillity and retirement which her husband’s prudence provides for her.", "로라는 흥분과 낯선 풍경이라면 이미 넘치도록 겪었으므로, 남편의 신중함이 마련해준 시골의 고요와 은거를 기쁘게 여긴다."], ["As for me, I am ready to be happy anywhere in her society.", "나로 말하자면, 그녀와 함께라면 어디서든 행복할 준비가 되어 있다."], ["We are all, therefore, well contented in our various ways, to begin with.", "그러니 우선은 우리 모두 각자의 방식대로 흡족한 셈이다."]], [["Last night I slept in London, and was delayed there so long to-day by various calls and commissions, that I did not reach Blackwater this evening till after dusk.", "어젯밤에는 런던에서 잤고, 오늘은 이런저런 방문과 심부름으로 그곳에서 하도 오래 붙들려 있어서 오늘 저녁 땅거미가 진 뒤에야 블랙워터에 닿았다."]], [["Judging by my vague impressions of the place thus far, it is the exact opposite of Limmeridge.", "여태까지 받은 이곳의 어렴풋한 인상으로 판단하건대, 이곳은 리머리지와 정반대다."]], [["The house is situated on a dead flat, and seems to be shut in—almost suffocated, to my north-country notions, by trees.", "집은 아주 평평한 땅 위에 서 있고, 북쪽 지방 사람인 내 감각으로는 나무에 둘러싸여—거의 숨이 막힐 지경으로 갇혀 있는 듯하다."], ["I have seen nobody but the man-servant who opened the door to me, and the housekeeper, a very civil person, who showed me the way to my own room, and got me my tea.", "문을 열어준 남자 하인과, 내 방까지 안내해주고 차를 내어준 아주 예의 바른 가정부 말고는 아무도 보지 못했다."], ["I have a nice little boudoir and bedroom, at the end of a long passage on the first floor.", "나는 이층 긴 복도 끝에 아담한 거실과 침실을 하나씩 얻었다."], ["The servants and some of the spare rooms are on the second floor, and all the living rooms are on the ground floor.", "하인들과 남는 방 몇 개는 삼층에 있고, 생활하는 방들은 모두 일층에 있다."], ["I have not seen one of them yet, and I know nothing about the house, except that one wing of it is said to be five hundred years old, that it had a moat round it once, and that it gets its name of Blackwater from a lake in the park.", "아직 그 방들은 하나도 보지 못했고, 이 집에 대해서는 한쪽 별채가 오백 년 되었다는 것, 한때는 둘레에 해자가 있었다는 것, 그리고 블랙워터라는 이름이 정원 안의 호수에서 왔다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모른다."]], [["Eleven o’clock has just struck, in a ghostly and solemn manner, from a turret over the centre of the house, which I saw when I came in.", "방금 열한 시를 알리는 종이 집 한가운데 솟은 작은 탑에서 유령처럼 엄숙하게 울렸다. 들어올 때 본 그 탑이다."], ["A large dog has been woke, apparently by the sound of the bell, and is howling and yawning drearily, somewhere round a corner.", "큰 개 한 마리가 종소리에 잠이 깬 모양인지 어느 모퉁이 너머에서 구슬프게 짖어대며 하품을 하고 있다."], ["I hear echoing footsteps in the passages below, and the iron thumping of bolts and bars at the house door.", "아래층 복도에서 울리는 발소리와 현관문의 빗장과 자물쇠를 채우는 쇳소리가 들린다."], ["The servants are evidently going to bed.", "하인들이 자러 가는 모양이다."], ["Shall I follow their example?", "나도 그들을 따라야 할까?"]], [["No, I am not half sleepy enough.", "아니, 나는 조금도 졸리지 않다."], ["Sleepy, did I say?", "졸리다고?"], ["I feel as if I should never close my eyes again.", "내가 그런 말을 했던가? 다시는 눈을 감지 못할 것만 같다."], ["The bare anticipation of seeing that dear face, and hearing that well-known voice to-morrow, keeps me in a perpetual fever of excitement.", "내일이면 그 사랑스러운 얼굴을 보고 그 익숙한 목소리를 듣는다는 생각만으로도 나는 끊임없는 흥분의 열병에 들떠 있다."], ["If I only had the privileges of a man, I would order out Sir Percival’s best horse instantly, and tear away on a night-gallop, eastward, to meet the rising sun—a long, hard, heavy, ceaseless gallop of hours and hours, like the famous highwayman’s ride to York.", "내게 남자의 특권이 있다면 나는 당장 퍼시벌 경의 가장 좋은 말을 끌어내어 동쪽으로, 떠오르는 해를 맞으러 밤새 달렸을 것이다—몇 시간이고 쉼 없이 이어지는 길고 험하고 고된 질주를, 저 유명한 노상강도의 요크행 말달리기처럼."], ["Being, however, nothing but a woman, condemned to patience, propriety, and petticoats for life, I must respect the house-keeper’s opinions, and try to compose myself in some feeble and feminine way.", "그러나 나는 한낱 여자일 뿐이고 평생 인내와 예절과 치마에 매인 몸이니, 가정부의 의견을 존중하며 가냘프고 여자다운 방식으로 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓸 수밖에 없다."]], [["Reading is out of the question—I can’t fix my attention on books.", "책 읽기는 엄두도 못 낸다—책에 도무지 정신을 붙들어 맬 수가 없다."], ["Let me try if I can write myself into sleepiness and fatigue.", "글이라도 써서 졸음과 피로를 불러올 수 있을지 해보자."], ["My journal has been very much neglected of late.", "요즘 내 일기는 지독히 소홀했다."], ["What can I recall—standing, as I now do, on the threshold of a new life—of persons and events, of chances and changes, during the past six months—the long, weary, empty interval since Laura’s wedding-day?", "지금처럼 새 삶의 문턱에 선 채로, 지난 여섯 달 동안의 사람들과 사건들, 우연과 변화 가운데 나는 무엇을 떠올릴 수 있을까—로라의 결혼식 날 이후로 이어진 그 길고 지치고 텅 빈 시간을?"]], [["Walter Hartright is uppermost in my memory, and he passes first in the shadowy procession of my absent friends.", "월터 하트라이트가 내 기억의 맨 앞에 있고, 그는 부재중인 내 벗들의 어슴푸레한 행렬에서 가장 먼저 지나간다."], ["I received a few lines from him, after the landing of the expedition in Honduras, written more cheerfully and hopefully than he has written yet.", "탐험대가 온두라스에 상륙한 뒤 그에게서 몇 줄을 받았는데, 지금까지 그가 쓴 어떤 글보다도 밝고 희망에 찬 어조였다."], ["A month or six weeks later I saw an extract from an American newspaper, describing the departure of the adventurers on their inland journey.", "한 달인가 여섯 주쯤 뒤에 나는 어느 미국 신문의 발췌 기사를 보았다. 그 탐험가들이 내륙 여정에 올랐다는 소식이었다."], ["They were last seen entering a wild primeval forest, each man with his rifle on his shoulder and his baggage at his back.", "그들이 마지막으로 목격된 것은 저마다 어깨에 총을 메고 등에 짐을 진 채 야생의 태고림으로 들어가는 모습이었다."], ["Since that time, civilisation has lost all trace of them.", "그 뒤로 문명은 그들의 자취를 모두 잃었다."], ["Not a line more have I received from Walter, not a fragment of news from the expedition has appeared in any of the public journals.", "월터에게서 더는 한 줄도 오지 않았고, 탐험대에 관한 소식 한 조각도 어느 신문에 실린 적이 없다."]], [["The same dense, disheartening obscurity hangs over the fate and fortunes of Anne Catherick, and her companion, Mrs.", "앤 캐서릭과 그 동행인 클레멘츠 부인의 운명과 행방 위에도 똑같이 짙고 낙담스러운 어둠이 드리워 있다."], ["Clements. Nothing whatever has been heard of either of them.", "두 사람 중 누구에 대해서도 아무 소식이 없다."], ["Whether they are in the country or out of it, whether they are living or dead, no one knows.", "그들이 이 나라 안에 있는지 밖에 있는지, 살았는지 죽었는지 아무도 모른다."], ["Even Sir Percival’s solicitor has lost all hope, and has ordered the useless search after the fugitives to be finally given up.", "퍼시벌 경의 변호사조차 모든 희망을 버리고, 달아난 이들을 찾는 헛된 수색을 마침내 그만두라고 지시했다."]], [["Our good old friend Mr. Gilmore has met with a sad check in his active professional career.", "우리의 좋은 옛 벗 길모어 씨는 활발하던 직업 인생에 슬픈 제동이 걸렸다."], ["Early in the spring we were alarmed by hearing that he had been found insensible at his desk, and that the seizure was pronounced to be an apoplectic fit.", "이른 봄에 우리는 그가 책상 앞에서 의식을 잃은 채 발견되었고 그 발작이 뇌졸중으로 진단되었다는 소식에 놀랐다."], ["He had been long complaining of fulness and oppression in the head, and his doctor had warned him of the consequences that would follow his persistency in continuing to work, early and late, as if he were still a young man.", "그는 오래전부터 머리가 무겁고 짓눌리는 듯하다고 호소해왔고, 의사는 아직 젊은이인 양 새벽부터 밤까지 일을 계속하다가는 어떤 결과가 따를지 그에게 경고했었다."], ["The result now is that he has been positively ordered to keep out of his office for a year to come, at least, and to seek repose of body and relief of mind by altogether changing his usual mode of life.", "그리하여 이제 그는 적어도 앞으로 일 년 동안은 사무실에 나가지 말고, 평소의 생활방식을 통째로 바꾸어 몸의 휴식과 마음의 안정을 구하라는 엄명을 받았다."], ["The business is left, accordingly, to be carried on by his partner, and he is himself, at this moment, away in Germany, visiting some relations who are settled there in mercantile pursuits.", "따라서 업무는 동업자가 맡아 이어가게 되었고, 그 자신은 지금 이 순간 독일에 가 있다. 그곳에 자리 잡고 상업에 종사하는 친척들을 찾아간 것이다."], ["Thus another true friend and trustworthy adviser is lost to us—lost, I earnestly hope and trust, for a time only.", "이렇게 또 한 사람의 진실한 벗이자 믿음직한 조언자를 우리는 잃었다—부디 한동안만 잃은 것이기를 나는 간절히 바라고 믿는다."]], [["Poor Mrs. Vesey travelled with me as far as London.", "가엾은 베지 부인은 런던까지 나와 함께 여행했다."], ["It was impossible to abandon her to solitude at Limmeridge after Laura and I had both left the house, and we have arranged that she is to live with an unmarried younger sister of hers, who keeps a school at Clapham.", "로라와 내가 둘 다 집을 떠난 뒤 그녀를 리머리지에 홀로 버려둘 수는 없었기에, 우리는 클래펌에서 학교를 운영하는 그녀의 결혼하지 않은 여동생과 함께 살도록 주선했다."], ["She is to come here this autumn to visit her pupil—I might almost say her adopted child.", "그녀는 올가을에 이곳으로 자기 제자를—거의 자기가 기른 아이라 해도 좋을 이를—만나러 올 것이다."], ["I saw the good old lady safe to her destination, and left her in the care of her relative, quietly happy at the prospect of seeing Laura again in a few months’ time.", "나는 그 착한 노부인을 목적지까지 무사히 데려다주고 친척에게 맡기고 왔다. 그녀는 몇 달 뒤면 로라를 다시 볼 수 있다는 생각에 조용히 행복해했다."]], [["As for Mr. Fairlie, I believe I am guilty of no injustice if I describe him as being unutterably relieved by having the house clear of us women.", "페얼리 씨로 말하자면, 우리 여자들이 집에서 사라진 것에 그가 이루 말할 수 없이 안도했다고 적어도 부당한 말은 아니리라."], ["The idea of his missing his niece is simply preposterous—he used to let months pass in the old times without attempting to see her—and in my case and Mrs. Vesey’s, I take leave to consider his telling us both that he was half heart-broken at our departure, to be equivalent to a confession that he was secretly rejoiced to get rid of us.", "그가 조카딸을 그리워한다는 생각은 그야말로 터무니없다—예전에도 그는 그녀를 보려 하지도 않은 채 몇 달씩 흘려보내곤 했다—그리고 나와 베지 부인의 경우, 우리 둘의 떠남에 가슴이 반쯤 무너진다고 그가 한 말은 우리를 치워버리게 되어 남몰래 기뻐한다는 고백과 다름없다고 나는 여기련다."], ["His last caprice has led him to keep two photographers incessantly employed in producing sun-pictures of all the treasures and curiosities in his possession.", "그의 최근 변덕은 사진사 둘을 쉴 새 없이 부려 자기가 가진 온갖 보물과 진기한 물건들을 태양 그림으로 찍어내게 하는 것이다."], ["One complete copy of the collection of the photographs is to be presented to the Mechanics’ Institution of Carlisle, mounted on the finest cardboard, with ostentatious red-letter inscriptions underneath, “Madonna and Child by Raphael.", "그 사진 모음의 완전한 한 벌은 칼라일 기술학교에 기증될 예정인데, 최고급 판지에 붙이고 그 아래에는 요란한 붉은 글씨로 이렇게 새긴다. “라파엘로 작 성모자상."], ["In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire.”", "프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.”"], ["“Copper coin of the period of Tiglath Pileser.", "“티글라트 필레세르 시대의 동전."], ["In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire.”", "프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.”"], ["“Unique Rembrandt etching.", "“유일무이한 렘브란트 에칭."], ["Known all over Europe as THE SMUDGE, from a printer’s blot in the corner which exists in no other copy.", "다른 어떤 인쇄본에도 없는 모퉁이의 인쇄 얼룩 때문에 유럽 전역에 ‘얼룩’이라 알려짐."], ["Valued at three hundred guineas.", "삼백 기니로 평가됨."], ["In the possession of Frederick Fairlie, Esq.”", "프레더릭 페얼리 님 소장.”"], ["Dozens of photographs of this sort, and all inscribed in this manner, were completed before I left Cumberland, and hundreds more remain to be done.", "이런 식의 사진 수십 장이, 모두 이런 식의 문구를 달고서, 내가 컴벌랜드를 떠나기 전에 완성되었고, 앞으로 수백 장이 더 남아 있다."], ["With this new interest to occupy him, Mr. Fairlie will be a happy man for months and months to come, and the two unfortunate photographers will share the social martyrdom which he has hitherto inflicted on his valet alone.", "이 새로운 관심거리가 그를 붙들어둘 터이니 페얼리 씨는 앞으로 몇 달이고 행복한 사람일 것이며, 가엾은 두 사진사는 그가 지금까지 시종 한 사람에게만 안겨온 사회적 순교를 나누어 지게 될 것이다."]], [["So much for the persons and events which hold the foremost place in my memory.", "내 기억에서 가장 앞자리를 차지하는 사람들과 사건들에 대해서는 이쯤 해두자."], ["What next of the one person who holds the foremost place in my heart?", "그렇다면 내 마음에서 가장 앞자리를 차지하는 단 한 사람은 어떤가?"], ["Laura has been present to my thoughts all the while I have been writing these lines.", "이 줄들을 쓰는 내내 로라는 내 생각 속에 있었다."], ["What can I recall of her during the past six months, before I close my journal for the night?", "오늘 밤 일기를 덮기 전에, 지난 여섯 달 동안의 그녀에 대해 나는 무엇을 떠올릴 수 있을까?"]], [["I have only her letters to guide me, and on the most important of all the questions which our correspondence can discuss, every one of those letters leaves me in the dark.", "내게는 그녀의 편지들밖에 길잡이가 없는데, 우리 서신이 다룰 수 있는 모든 물음 가운데 가장 중요한 것에 대해서는 그 편지들이 하나같이 나를 어둠 속에 남겨둔다."]], [["Does he treat her kindly?", "그는 그녀를 다정하게 대하는가?"], ["Is she happier now than she was when I parted with her on the wedding-day?", "결혼식 날 내가 그녀와 헤어졌을 때보다 지금 그녀는 더 행복한가?"], ["All my letters have contained these two inquiries, put more or less directly, now in one form, and now in another, and all, on that point only, have remained without reply, or have been answered as if my questions merely related to the state of her health.", "내 편지에는 모두 이 두 가지 물음이, 어느 때는 이런 식으로 어느 때는 저런 식으로, 다소 직접적으로 담겨 있었다. 그런데 오직 그 대목만은 모두 답이 없거나, 마치 내가 그녀의 건강 상태만 물은 양 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["She informs me, over and over again, that she is perfectly well—that travelling agrees with her—that she is getting through the winter, for the first time in her life, without catching cold—but not a word can I find anywhere which tells me plainly that she is reconciled to her marriage, and that she can now look back to the twenty-second of December without any bitter feelings of repentance and regret.", "그녀는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 알려온다—자기는 아주 잘 지낸다고, 여행이 몸에 맞는다고, 태어나 처음으로 감기 한 번 걸리지 않고 겨울을 나고 있다고. 그러나 자기 결혼을 받아들이게 되었다고, 이제는 십이월 이십이일을 돌이켜보아도 쓰라린 후회와 아쉬움이 없다고 분명히 말해주는 구절은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없다."], ["The name of her husband is only mentioned in her letters, as she might mention the name of a friend who was travelling with them, and who had undertaken to make all the arrangements for the journey. “Sir Percival”", "편지에서 남편의 이름은 그저 함께 여행 중이며 여정의 모든 준비를 맡은 친구의 이름을 대듯 언급될 뿐이다."], ["has settled that we leave on such a day—“Sir Percival” has decided that we travel by such a road.", "“퍼시벌 경”이 우리가 며칟날 떠나기로 정했다, “퍼시벌 경”이 어느 길로 가기로 결정했다."], ["Sometimes she writes “Percival” only, but very seldom—in nine cases out of ten she gives him his title.", "이따금 “퍼시벌”이라고만 쓸 때도 있지만 아주 드물다—열에 아홉은 작위를 붙여 부른다."]], [["I cannot find that his habits and opinions have changed and coloured hers in any single particular.", "그의 습관과 견해가 그녀의 것을 조금이라도 바꾸고 물들였다는 흔적을 나는 찾을 수 없다."], ["The usual moral transformation which is insensibly wrought in a young, fresh, sensitive woman by her marriage, seems never to have taken place in Laura.", "젊고 싱그럽고 예민한 여인에게 결혼이 은연중에 일으키는 그 흔한 정신의 변화가 로라에게는 도무지 일어나지 않은 듯하다."], ["She writes of her own thoughts and impressions, amid all the wonders she has seen, exactly as she might have written to some one else, if I had been travelling with her instead of her husband.", "그녀는 눈앞의 온갖 경이 속에서도 자기 생각과 인상을 적어 보내는데, 그 문장은 남편이 아니라 내가 그녀와 함께 여행하고 있었다면 다른 누군가에게 썼을 법한 그대로다."], ["I see no betrayal anywhere of sympathy of any kind existing between them.", "두 사람 사이에 어떤 종류의 교감이 존재한다는 기색은 어디에서도 보이지 않는다."], ["Even when she wanders from the subject of her travels, and occupies herself with the prospects that await her in England, her speculations are busied with her future as my sister, and persistently neglect to notice her future as Sir Percival’s wife.", "여행 이야기에서 벗어나 영국에서 자기를 기다리는 앞날을 이야기할 때조차, 그녀의 상상은 내 동생으로서의 미래에만 골몰하고 퍼시벌 경의 아내로서의 미래는 한사코 언급하지 않는다."], ["In all this there is no undertone of complaint to warn me that she is absolutely unhappy in her married life.", "이 모든 것에도 그녀가 결혼 생활에서 정말로 불행하다고 내게 경고하는 불평의 저음은 없다."], ["The impression I have derived from our correspondence does not, thank God, lead me to any such distressing conclusion as that.", "우리 서신에서 내가 받은 인상은, 다행히도, 그런 괴로운 결론으로 나를 이끌지는 않는다."], ["I only see a sad torpor, an unchangeable indifference, when I turn my mind from her in the old character of a sister, and look at her, through the medium of her letters, in the new character of a wife.", "다만 내가 그녀를 누이라는 옛 자리에서 떼어내어 편지라는 매개를 통해 아내라는 새로운 자리에서 바라볼 때, 나는 슬픈 무기력과 변치 않는 무관심만을 볼 뿐이다."], ["In other words, it is always Laura Fairlie who has been writing to me for the last six months, and never Lady Glyde.", "다시 말해, 지난 여섯 달 동안 내게 편지를 써온 사람은 언제나 로라 페얼리였지 결코 글라이드 부인이 아니었다."]], [["The strange silence which she maintains on the subject of her husband’s character and conduct, she preserves with almost equal resolution in the few references which her later letters contain to the name of her husband’s bosom friend, Count Fosco.", "남편의 성품과 행실에 대해 그녀가 지키는 그 이상한 침묵을, 그녀는 남편의 절친한 벗 포스코 백작의 이름이 나오는 최근 편지의 몇 안 되는 대목에서도 거의 똑같은 결의로 지킨다."]], [["For some unexplained reason the Count and his wife appear to have changed their plans abruptly, at the end of last autumn, and to have gone to Vienna instead of going to Rome, at which latter place Sir Percival had expected to find them when he left England.", "무슨 까닭인지 백작 부부는 지난가을 끝 무렵에 갑자기 계획을 바꾸어, 로마 대신 빈으로 간 모양이다. 퍼시벌 경은 영국을 떠날 때 로마에서 그들을 만나리라 예상했었다."], ["They only quitted Vienna in the spring, and travelled as far as the Tyrol to meet the bride and bridegroom on their homeward journey.", "두 사람은 봄이 되어서야 빈을 떠나 티롤까지 와서, 귀국길에 오른 신혼부부를 맞았다."], ["Laura writes readily enough about the meeting with Madame Fosco, and assures me that she has found her aunt so much changed for the better—so much quieter, and so much more sensible as a wife than she was as a single woman—that I shall hardly know her again when I see her here.", "로라는 포스코 백작부인과의 만남에 대해서는 곧잘 쓴다. 고모가 훨씬 좋은 쪽으로 변해서—처녀 시절보다 아내가 된 지금이 훨씬 차분하고 훨씬 분별 있어서—여기서 다시 만나면 내가 알아보지도 못할 거라고 장담한다."], ["But on the subject of Count Fosco (who interests me infinitely more than his wife), Laura is provokingly circumspect and silent.", "그러나 포스코 백작에 대해서는 (그의 아내보다 그가 나는 한없이 더 궁금한데) 로라는 얄미울 만큼 조심스럽고 말이 없다."], ["She only says that he puzzles her, and that she will not tell me what her impression of him is until I have seen him, and formed my own opinion first.", "그저 그가 알 수 없는 사람이라고만 하고, 내가 그를 직접 보고 내 나름의 판단을 먼저 세울 때까지는 자기 인상을 말해주지 않겠다고 한다."]], [["This, to my mind, looks ill for the Count.", "내가 보기에 이것은 백작에게 좋지 않은 징조다."], ["Laura has preserved, far more perfectly than most people do in later life, the child’s subtle faculty of knowing a friend by instinct, and if I am right in assuming that her first impression of Count Fosco has not been favourable, I for one am in some danger of doubting and distrusting that illustrious foreigner before I have so much as set eyes on him.", "로라는 대개의 사람들이 나이 들며 잃어버리는, 벗을 본능으로 알아보는 아이의 섬세한 능력을 훨씬 온전하게 간직하고 있다. 그러니 포스코 백작에 대한 그녀의 첫인상이 좋지 않았다는 내 짐작이 옳다면, 나부터도 그 저명한 외국인을 눈으로 보기도 전에 의심하고 불신하게 될 위험이 적잖다."], ["But, patience, patience—this uncertainty, and many uncertainties more, cannot last much longer.", "그러나 인내, 인내다—이 불확실함도, 그 밖의 숱한 불확실함도 그리 오래가지는 못한다."], ["To-morrow will see all my doubts in a fair way of being cleared up, sooner or later.", "내일이면 내 모든 의혹이 머지않아 풀릴 길에 들어서는 것을 보게 되리라."]], [["Twelve o’clock has struck, and I have just come back to close these pages, after looking out at my open window.", "열두 시를 알리는 종이 울렸고, 열린 창밖을 내다보다가 이제야 이 지면을 덮으러 돌아왔다."]], [["It is a still, sultry, moonless night.", "고요하고 후텁지근하고 달 없는 밤이다."], ["The stars are dull and few.", "별은 흐릿하고 몇 개 되지 않는다."], ["The trees that shut out the view on all sides look dimly black and solid in the distance, like a great wall of rock.", "사방의 시야를 가로막은 나무들은 멀리서 검고 단단한 덩어리로 어렴풋이 보여, 커다란 바위벽 같다."], ["I hear the croaking of frogs, faint and far off, and the echoes of the great clock hum in the airless calm long after the strokes have ceased.", "개구리 우는 소리가 희미하게 멀리서 들리고, 큰 시계의 여운은 종소리가 그친 뒤에도 바람 한 점 없는 고요 속에 오래도록 웅웅거린다."], ["I wonder how Blackwater Park will look in the daytime?", "낮의 블랙워터 파크는 어떤 모습일까?"], ["I don’t altogether like it by night.", "밤의 이곳은 그다지 마음에 들지 않는다."]], [["12th.—A day of investigations and discoveries—a more interesting day, for many reasons, than I had ventured to anticipate.", "12일.—조사와 발견의 하루—여러 이유로, 감히 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 흥미로운 하루였다."]], [["I began my sight-seeing, of course, with the house.", "나는 물론 집부터 구경하기 시작했다."]], [["The main body of the building is of the time of that highly-overrated woman, Queen Elizabeth.", "건물의 본채는 그 지나치게 과대평가된 여인, 엘리자베스 여왕 시대의 것이다."], ["On the ground floor there are two hugely long galleries, with low ceilings lying parallel with each other, and rendered additionally dark and dismal by hideous family portraits—every one of which I should like to burn.", "일층에는 낮은 천장의 어마어마하게 긴 회랑 두 개가 나란히 뻗어 있는데, 흉측한 가족 초상화들 때문에 한층 더 어둡고 음침하다—그 그림들은 하나같이 태워버리고 싶다."], ["The rooms on the floor above the two galleries are kept in tolerable repair, but are very seldom used.", "두 회랑 위층의 방들은 그런대로 손질되어 있으나 좀처럼 쓰이지 않는다."], ["The civil housekeeper, who acted as my guide, offered to show me over them, but considerately added that she feared I should find them rather out of order.", "안내를 맡은 예의 바른 가정부가 그 방들을 보여주겠다고 했지만, 사려 깊게도 방들이 꽤 어질러져 있을 것 같다고 덧붙였다."], ["My respect for the integrity of my own petticoats and stockings infinitely exceeds my respect for all the Elizabethan bedrooms in the kingdom, so I positively declined exploring the upper regions of dust and dirt at the risk of soiling my nice clean clothes.", "내 치마와 양말의 온전함에 대한 존경심은 이 왕국의 모든 엘리자베스 시대 침실에 대한 존경심을 한없이 웃돌므로, 나는 깨끗한 새 옷을 더럽힐 위험을 무릅쓰고 먼지와 때의 상층부를 탐사하기를 딱 잘라 거절했다."], ["The housekeeper said, “I am quite of your opinion, miss,”", "가정부는 “저도 아가씨와 똑같은 생각입니다”"], ["and appeared to think me the most sensible woman she had met with for a long time past.", "하고 말했고, 나를 오랜만에 만난 가장 분별 있는 여자로 여기는 눈치였다."]], [["So much, then, for the main building.", "본채 이야기는 이쯤 해두자."], ["Two wings are added at either end of it.", "그 양쪽 끝에는 별채가 하나씩 덧붙어 있다."], ["The half-ruined wing on the left (as you approach the house) was once a place of residence standing by itself, and was built in the fourteenth century.", "(집으로 다가갈 때) 왼편의 반쯤 무너진 별채는 한때 홀로 서 있던 주거였고 십사세기에 지어졌다."], ["One of Sir Percival’s maternal ancestors—I don’t remember, and don’t care which—tacked on the main building, at right angles to it, in the aforesaid Queen Elizabeth’s time.", "퍼시벌 경의 외가 조상 가운데 누군가가—누구인지 나는 기억나지도 않고 알 바도 아니다—앞서 말한 엘리자베스 여왕 시대에 본채를 거기에 직각으로 붙여 지었다."], ["The housekeeper told me that the architecture of “the old wing,” both outside and inside, was considered remarkably fine by good judges.", "가정부는 “옛 별채”의 건축이 안팎으로 안목 있는 이들에게 대단히 훌륭하다는 평을 듣는다고 일러주었다."], ["On further investigation I discovered that good judges could only exercise their abilities on Sir Percival’s piece of antiquity by previously dismissing from their minds all fear of damp, darkness, and rats.", "좀 더 살펴본 끝에 나는 안목 있는 이들이 퍼시벌 경의 그 고색창연한 물건 앞에서 자기 안목을 발휘하려면 먼저 습기와 어둠과 쥐에 대한 두려움을 죄다 마음에서 몰아내야 한다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["Under these circumstances, I unhesitatingly acknowledged myself to be no judge at all, and suggested that we should treat “the old wing” precisely as we had previously treated the Elizabethan bedrooms.", "사정이 그러하니 나는 주저 없이 내가 안목이라고는 전혀 없는 사람임을 인정하고, “옛 별채”도 앞서 엘리자베스 시대 침실들에게 한 것과 똑같이 대하자고 제안했다."], ["Once more the housekeeper said, “I am quite of your opinion, miss,”", "가정부는 다시 한 번 “저도 아가씨와 똑같은 생각입니다”"], ["and once more she looked at me with undisguised admiration of my extraordinary common-sense.", "하고 말했고, 다시 한 번 내 비범한 상식에 대한 감탄을 숨기지 않으며 나를 바라보았다."]], [["We went next to the wing on the right, which was built, by way of completing the wonderful architectural jumble at Blackwater Park, in the time of George the Second.", "다음으로 우리는 오른편 별채로 갔다. 블랙워터 파크의 그 놀라운 건축적 뒤죽박죽을 완성하기라도 하듯, 이 별채는 조지 이세 시대에 지어졌다."]], [["This is the habitable part of the house, which has been repaired and redecorated inside on Laura’s account.", "이곳이 사람이 살 만한 부분으로, 로라를 위해 안쪽을 수리하고 새로 꾸민 곳이다."], ["My two rooms, and all the good bedrooms besides, are on the first floor, and the basement contains a drawing-room, a dining-room, a morning-room, a library, and a pretty little boudoir for Laura, all very nicely ornamented in the bright modern way, and all very elegantly furnished with the delightful modern luxuries.", "내 두 방과 그 밖의 좋은 침실들은 모두 이층에 있고, 아래층에는 응접실과 식당과 아침 거실과 서재, 그리고 로라를 위한 예쁘고 작은 규방이 있다. 모두 밝고 현대적인 방식으로 아주 곱게 꾸며졌고, 즐거운 현대의 사치품들로 아주 우아하게 갖추어져 있다."], ["None of the rooms are anything like so large and airy as our rooms at Limmeridge, but they all look pleasant to live in.", "어느 방도 리머리지의 우리 방들만큼 넓고 시원하지는 않지만 하나같이 살기에 기분 좋아 보인다."], ["I was terribly afraid, from what I had heard of Blackwater Park, of fatiguing antique chairs, and dismal stained glass, and musty, frouzy hangings, and all the barbarous lumber which people born without a sense of comfort accumulate about them, in defiance of the consideration due to the convenience of their friends.", "나는 블랙워터 파크에 대해 들은 이야기 때문에, 앉기 괴로운 골동 의자들과 침침한 색유리와 곰팡내 나는 퀴퀴한 휘장들, 그리고 안락함이라는 감각 없이 태어난 사람들이 벗들의 편의는 아랑곳하지 않고 주위에 쌓아두는 온갖 야만스러운 잡동사니를 몹시 두려워하고 있었다."], ["It is an inexpressible relief to find that the nineteenth century has invaded this strange future home of mine, and has swept the dirty “good old times” out of the way of our daily life.", "십구세기가 이 낯선 나의 새 보금자리에도 밀고 들어와 우리 일상에서 그 지저분한 “좋았던 옛 시절”을 쓸어내버렸음을 알게 되니 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓인다."]], [["I dawdled away the morning—part of the time in the rooms downstairs, and part out of doors in the great square which is formed by the three sides of the house, and by the lofty iron railings and gates which protect it in front.", "나는 오전을 빈둥거리며 흘려보냈다—얼마간은 아래층 방들에서, 얼마간은 집의 세 면과 앞쪽을 지키는 높은 쇠울타리와 대문이 이루는 커다란 사각 마당에서."], ["A large circular fish-pond with stone sides, and an allegorical leaden monster in the middle, occupies the centre of the square.", "마당 한가운데에는 돌로 두른 커다란 원형 연못이 있고 그 복판에는 우의적인 납 괴물상이 서 있다."], ["The pond itself is full of gold and silver fish, and is encircled by a broad belt of the softest turf I ever walked on.", "연못에는 금빛 은빛 물고기가 가득하고, 내가 밟아본 중 가장 부드러운 잔디의 널따란 띠가 그것을 둘러싸고 있다."], ["I loitered here on the shady side pleasantly enough till luncheon-time, and after that took my broad straw hat and wandered out alone in the warm lovely sunlight to explore the grounds.", "나는 점심때까지 그늘진 쪽에서 제법 기분 좋게 서성이다가, 그 뒤 챙 넓은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 따뜻하고 아름다운 햇빛 속으로 혼자 나가 정원을 둘러보았다."]], [["Daylight confirmed the impression which I had felt the night before, of there being too many trees at Blackwater.", "낮의 빛은 블랙워터에 나무가 너무 많다는 어젯밤의 인상을 확인해주었다."], ["The house is stifled by them.", "집은 나무에 숨이 막힌다."], ["They are, for the most part, young, and planted far too thickly.", "나무는 대개 어리고 지나치게 빽빽하게 심겨 있다."], ["I suspect there must have been a ruinous cutting down of timber all over the estate before Sir Percival’s time, and an angry anxiety on the part of the next possessor to fill up all the gaps as thickly and rapidly as possible.", "짐작건대 퍼시벌 경 이전에 영지 전체에서 재목을 결딴내다시피 베어낸 일이 있었고, 그다음 소유자가 화가 난 조바심으로 그 빈자리를 되도록 빽빽하고 빠르게 메우려 한 것이리라."], ["After looking about me in front of the house, I observed a flower-garden on my left hand, and walked towards it to see what I could discover in that direction.", "집 앞을 둘러본 뒤 나는 왼편에 화단이 있는 것을 보고, 그쪽에 무엇이 있는지 알아보려 그리로 걸어갔다."]], [["On a nearer view the garden proved to be small and poor and ill kept.", "가까이서 보니 정원은 작고 볼품없고 손질이 되어 있지 않았다."], ["I left it behind me, opened a little gate in a ring fence, and found myself in a plantation of fir-trees.", "나는 그곳을 뒤로하고 둘레 울타리의 작은 문을 열었다가 전나무 숲에 들어서 있었다."]], [["A pretty winding path, artificially made, led me on among the trees, and my north-country experience soon informed me that I was approaching sandy, heathy ground.", "사람이 손을 대어 만든 예쁘장한 굽잇길이 나무들 사이로 나를 이끌었고, 북쪽 지방에서 얻은 경험은 곧 내가 모래땅과 히스 벌판에 가까워지고 있음을 일러주었다."], ["After a walk of more than half a mile, I should think, among the firs, the path took a sharp turn—the trees abruptly ceased to appear on either side of me, and I found myself standing suddenly on the margin of a vast open space, and looking down at the Blackwater lake from which the house takes its name.", "전나무들 사이로 반 마일 남짓 걸었을까, 길이 급하게 꺾이더니 나무들이 양옆에서 홀연히 사라졌고, 나는 문득 드넓게 트인 공간의 가장자리에 서서 이 집에 이름을 준 블랙워터 호수를 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["The ground, shelving away below me, was all sand, with a few little heathy hillocks to break the monotony of it in certain places.", "발아래로 비스듬히 흘러내린 땅은 온통 모래였고, 군데군데 그 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 히스 덤불의 작은 둔덕이 몇 개 있었다."], ["The lake itself had evidently once flowed to the spot on which I stood, and had been gradually wasted and dried up to less than a third of its former size.", "호수는 분명 한때 내가 선 자리까지 차올랐다가 차츰 마르고 줄어 예전 크기의 삼분의 일에도 못 미치게 되었다."], ["I saw its still, stagnant waters, a quarter of a mile away from me in the hollow, separated into pools and ponds by twining reeds and rushes, and little knolls of earth.", "나는 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 우묵한 곳에서, 뒤엉킨 갈대와 골풀과 자잘한 흙무더기에 여러 웅덩이와 못으로 갈린 그 고요하고 썩은 물을 보았다."], ["On the farther bank from me the trees rose thickly again, and shut out the view, and cast their black shadows on the sluggish, shallow water.", "건너편 기슭에는 나무들이 다시 빽빽이 솟아 시야를 가로막고 느리고 얕은 물 위에 검은 그림자를 드리웠다."], ["As I walked down to the lake, I saw that the ground on its farther side was damp and marshy, overgrown with rank grass and dismal willows.", "호수로 내려가면서 보니 건너편 땅은 축축한 늪지로, 억센 풀과 음침한 버드나무가 무성했다."], ["The water, which was clear enough on the open sandy side, where the sun shone, looked black and poisonous opposite to me, where it lay deeper under the shade of the spongy banks, and the rank overhanging thickets and tangled trees.", "해가 비치는 트인 모래 쪽에서는 제법 맑던 물이, 내 맞은편 물컹한 둑과 무성하게 드리운 덤불과 뒤엉킨 나무의 그늘 아래 더 깊이 고인 곳에서는 검고 독기 어려 보였다."], ["The frogs were croaking, and the rats were slipping in and out of the shadowy water, like live shadows themselves, as I got nearer to the marshy side of the lake.", "늪 쪽으로 다가갈수록 개구리는 울어대고, 쥐들은 저희가 살아 있는 그림자라도 되는 양 그늘진 물속을 들락날락하며 미끄러졌다."], ["I saw here, lying half in and half out of the water, the rotten wreck of an old overturned boat, with a sickly spot of sunlight glimmering through a gap in the trees on its dry surface, and a snake basking in the midst of the spot, fantastically coiled and treacherously still.", "나는 그곳에서 물에 반쯤 잠긴 채 뒤집힌 낡은 배의 썩은 잔해를 보았다. 나무 사이 틈으로 스민 병약한 햇빛 한 조각이 그 마른 표면에 어른거렸고, 그 빛 한복판에 뱀 한 마리가 기괴하게 몸을 사린 채 음험하리만치 미동도 없이 볕을 쬐고 있었다."], ["Far and near the view suggested the same dreary impressions of solitude and decay, and the glorious brightness of the summer sky overhead seemed only to deepen and harden the gloom and barrenness of the wilderness on which it shone.", "멀리서도 가까이서도 그 풍경은 한결같이 적막과 쇠락의 을씨년스러운 인상을 자아냈고, 머리 위 여름 하늘의 눈부신 밝음은 그 빛이 내리쬐는 황무지의 음울과 메마름을 더 깊고 모질게 만들 뿐인 듯했다."], ["I turned and retraced my steps to the high heathy ground, directing them a little aside from my former path towards a shabby old wooden shed, which stood on the outer skirt of the fir plantation, and which had hitherto been too unimportant to share my notice with the wide, wild prospect of the lake.", "나는 몸을 돌려 히스가 깔린 높은 땅으로 되짚어 올라가면서, 아까의 길에서 조금 벗어나 전나무 숲 바깥자락에 서 있는 허름한 낡은 나무 헛간 쪽으로 발길을 잡았다. 그 헛간은 그때까지 호수의 넓고 거친 경치에 견주면 눈길을 나눌 만큼 대단한 것이 못 되었던 것이다."]], [["On approaching the shed I found that it had once been a boat-house, and that an attempt had apparently been made to convert it afterwards into a sort of rude arbour, by placing inside it a firwood seat, a few stools, and a table.", "헛간에 다가가서 보니 그것은 한때 보트 창고였고, 그 뒤에 안으로 전나무 의자 하나와 걸상 몇 개와 탁자를 들여 일종의 투박한 정자로 바꾸려 한 흔적이 있었다."], ["I entered the place, and sat down for a little while to rest and get my breath again.", "나는 안으로 들어가 잠시 앉아 쉬며 숨을 골랐다."]], [["I had not been in the boat-house more than a minute when it struck me that the sound of my own quick breathing was very strangely echoed by something beneath me.", "보트 창고에 들어간 지 일 분도 되지 않았을 때, 내 가쁜 숨소리가 발밑의 무언가에 아주 이상하게 되울린다는 생각이 들었다."], ["I listened intently for a moment, and heard a low, thick, sobbing breath that seemed to come from the ground under the seat which I was occupying.", "나는 한동안 귀를 곤두세워 들었고, 내가 앉은 의자 아래 땅바닥에서 나오는 듯한 낮고 탁하게 흐느끼는 숨소리를 들었다."], ["My nerves are not easily shaken by trifles, but on this occasion I started to my feet in a fright—called out—received no answer—summoned back my recreant courage, and looked under the seat.", "내 신경은 사소한 일에 쉽게 흔들리지 않지만 이때만은 놀라서 벌떡 일어섰다—소리쳐 불렀고—아무 대답도 없자—달아난 용기를 다시 불러모아 의자 밑을 들여다보았다."]], [["There, crouched up in the farthest corner, lay the forlorn cause of my terror, in the shape of a poor little dog—a black and white spaniel.", "그곳 가장 안쪽 구석에 웅크린 채, 내 공포의 가련한 원인이 누워 있었다—작고 불쌍한 개, 검고 흰 스패니얼이었다."], ["The creature moaned feebly when I looked at it and called to it, but never stirred.", "내가 들여다보며 부르자 그 짐승은 힘없이 신음했으나 몸은 조금도 움직이지 않았다."], ["I moved away the seat and looked closer.", "나는 의자를 치우고 더 가까이 들여다보았다."], ["The poor little dog’s eyes were glazing fast, and there were spots of blood on its glossy white side.", "가엾은 강아지의 눈은 빠르게 흐려지고 있었고, 윤기 나는 흰 옆구리에는 핏방울이 맺혀 있었다."], ["The misery of a weak, helpless, dumb creature is surely one of the saddest of all the mournful sights which this world can show.", "약하고 무력하고 말 못 하는 짐승의 고통이야말로 이 세상이 보여줄 수 있는 온갖 슬픈 광경 가운데 가장 슬픈 것 중 하나이리라."], ["I lifted the poor dog in my arms as gently as I could, and contrived a sort of make-shift hammock for him to lie in, by gathering up the front of my dress all round him.", "나는 그 가엾은 개를 할 수 있는 한 부드럽게 안아 올리고, 옷자락 앞섶을 그 둘레로 그러모아 임시 그물침대 같은 것을 만들어 눕혔다."], ["In this way I took the creature, as painlessly as possible, and as fast as possible, back to the house.", "그렇게 나는 그 짐승을 되도록 아프지 않게, 되도록 빠르게 집으로 데려왔다."]], [["Finding no one in the hall I went up at once to my own sitting-room, made a bed for the dog with one of my old shawls, and rang the bell.", "현관에 아무도 없기에 나는 곧장 내 거실로 올라가 낡은 숄 하나로 개의 잠자리를 만들어주고 종을 울렸다."], ["The largest and fattest of all possible house-maids answered it, in a state of cheerful stupidity which would have provoked the patience of a saint.", "세상에 있을 법한 하녀 중 가장 크고 가장 뚱뚱한 하녀가 대답하러 왔는데, 성인의 인내심마저 뒤흔들었을 법한 명랑한 아둔함에 젖어 있었다."], ["The girl’s fat, shapeless face actually stretched into a broad grin at the sight of the wounded creature on the floor.", "그 아이의 살진 흐물흐물한 얼굴은 바닥에 놓인 다친 짐승을 보자 정말로 활짝 웃음으로 벌어졌다."]], [["“What do you see there to laugh at?”", "“거기 뭐가 그렇게 우습지?”"], ["I asked, as angrily as if she had been a servant of my own.", "나는 그 애가 내 하인이라도 되는 양 화를 내며 물었다."], ["“Do you know whose dog it is?”", "“이게 누구 개인지 아니?”"]], [["“No, miss, that I certainly don’t.”", "“아뇨, 아가씨, 그건 정말 모르겠는데요.”"], ["She stooped, and looked down at the spaniel’s injured side—brightened suddenly with the irradiation of a new idea—and pointing to the wound with a chuckle of satisfaction, said, “That’s Baxter’s doings, that is.”", "그 애는 몸을 숙여 스패니얼의 다친 옆구리를 내려다보더니—새로운 생각이 떠오른 듯 갑자기 얼굴이 환해져서는—만족스럽게 킥킥거리며 상처를 가리키고 말했다. “저건 백스터가 한 짓이구먼요, 그렇고말고요.”"]], [["I was so exasperated that I could have boxed her ears.", "나는 어찌나 부아가 치미는지 그 애의 뺨이라도 갈길 뻔했다. “백스터?”"], ["“Baxter?”", "내가 말했다."], ["I said. “Who is the brute you call Baxter?”", "“네가 백스터라고 부르는 그 짐승 같은 자는 누구지?”"]], [["The girl grinned again more cheerfully than ever.", "그 애는 어느 때보다 더 신나게 다시 씩 웃었다."], ["“Bless you, miss!", "“아이고, 아가씨!"], ["Baxter’s the keeper, and when he finds strange dogs hunting about, he takes and shoots ’em.", "백스터는 사냥터지기예요. 낯선 개가 돌아다니면서 사냥질하는 걸 보면 붙잡아다 쏴버리지요."], ["It’s keeper’s dooty, miss.", "그게 사냥터지기 직분이구먼요, 아가씨."], ["I think that dog will die.", "저 개는 죽을 것 같은데요."], ["Here’s where he’s been shot, ain’t it?", "여기가 총 맞은 자리 아녜요?"], ["That’s Baxter’s doings, that is.", "저건 백스터가 한 짓이라니까요."], ["Baxter’s doings, miss, and Baxter’s dooty.”", "백스터가 한 짓이고 백스터의 직분이구먼요, 아가씨.”"]], [["I was almost wicked enough to wish that Baxter had shot the housemaid instead of the dog.", "나는 백스터가 개 대신 저 하녀를 쏘았더라면 좋았을 걸 하고 바랄 만큼 마음이 모질어질 뻔했다."], ["Seeing that it was quite useless to expect this densely impenetrable personage to give me any help in relieving the suffering creature at our feet, I told her to request the housekeeper’s attendance with my compliments.", "이 지독히도 말이 통하지 않는 인물에게 우리 발치의 고통받는 짐승을 돌보는 데 무슨 도움을 기대하기란 전혀 소용없는 일임을 알고, 나는 그 애에게 내 인사와 함께 가정부를 좀 와달라고 청하라고 일렀다."], ["She went out exactly as she had come in, grinning from ear to ear.", "그 애는 들어올 때와 똑같이 입이 귀에 걸리도록 웃으며 나갔다."], ["As the door closed on her she said to herself softly, “It’s Baxter’s doings and Baxter’s dooty—that’s what it is.”", "문이 등 뒤로 닫힐 때 그 애가 혼자 나직이 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸다. “백스터가 한 짓이고 백스터의 직분이지, 바로 그런 거라고.”"]], [["The housekeeper, a person of some education and intelligence, thoughtfully brought upstairs with her some milk and some warm water.", "가정부는 제법 교양과 지각이 있는 사람이라, 생각이 깊게도 우유와 따뜻한 물을 가지고 위층으로 올라왔다."], ["The instant she saw the dog on the floor she started and changed colour.", "바닥의 개를 보는 순간 그녀는 흠칫 놀라며 낯빛이 변했다."]], [["“Why, Lord bless me,”", "“어머, 세상에,” 가정부가 소리쳤다."], ["cried the housekeeper, “that must be Mrs. Catherick’s dog!”", "“저건 틀림없이 캐서릭 부인의 개예요!”"]], [["“Whose?”", "“누구의 개라고요?”"], ["I asked, in the utmost astonishment.", "나는 몹시 놀라 물었다."]], [["“Mrs. Catherick’s.", "“캐서릭 부인이요."], ["You seem to know Mrs. Catherick, Miss Halcombe?”", "홀콤 양께서는 캐서릭 부인을 아시는 모양이군요?”"]], [["“Not personally, but I have heard of her.", "“직접 아는 사이는 아니지만 이야기는 들었어요."], ["Does she live here?", "그분이 여기 사나요?"], ["Has she had any news of her daughter?”", "딸에 대한 소식이 있었나요?”"]], [["“No, Miss Halcombe, she came here to ask for news.”", "“아니요, 홀콤 양. 그분은 소식을 물으러 여기 오셨었지요.”"]], [["“When?”", "“언제요?”"]], [["“Only yesterday.", "“바로 어제요."], ["She said some one had reported that a stranger answering to the description of her daughter had been seen in our neighbourhood.", "그분 말씀이, 따님의 인상착의와 맞는 낯선 여자가 이 근처에서 목격되었다고 누가 알려주었다더군요."], ["No such report has reached us here, and no such report was known in the village, when I sent to make inquiries there on Mrs. Catherick’s account.", "여기서는 그런 소문이 들려온 적이 없고, 캐서릭 부인을 위해 마을에 사람을 보내 알아보게 했을 때도 마을에는 그런 소문이 알려져 있지 않았습니다."], ["She certainly brought this poor little dog with her when she came, and I saw it trot out after her when she went away.", "그분이 오실 때 이 가엾은 작은 개를 데리고 오신 건 분명하고요, 돌아가실 때 개가 종종걸음으로 뒤따라 나가는 것도 제가 봤습니다."], ["I suppose the creature strayed into the plantations, and got shot.", "아마 그 짐승이 숲으로 헤매 들어갔다가 총을 맞은 게지요."], ["Where did you find it, Miss Halcombe?”", "어디서 찾으셨어요, 홀콤 양?”"]], [["“In the old shed that looks out on the lake.”", "“호수가 내다보이는 낡은 헛간에서요.”"]], [["“Ah, yes, that is the plantation side, and the poor thing dragged itself, I suppose, to the nearest shelter, as dogs will, to die.", "“아, 그렇군요, 그쪽이 숲 쪽이지요. 가엾은 것이 개들이 흔히 그러듯 죽으려고 가장 가까운 은신처까지 제 몸을 끌고 갔나 봅니다."], ["If you can moisten its lips with the milk, Miss Halcombe, I will wash the clotted hair from the wound.", "홀콤 양께서 우유로 입술을 축여주시면 저는 상처에 엉긴 털을 씻어내겠습니다."], ["I am very much afraid it is too late to do any good. However, we can but try.”", "이제 와서 손을 써봐야 너무 늦은 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽습니다만, 그래도 해보는 수밖에요.”"]], [["Mrs. Catherick!", "캐서릭 부인!"], ["The name still rang in my ears, as if the housekeeper had only that moment surprised me by uttering it.", "그 이름은 마치 가정부가 방금 그것을 입에 올려 나를 놀라게 한 것처럼 여전히 내 귓가에 울렸다."], ["While we were attending to the dog, the words of Walter Hartright’s caution to me returned to my memory: “If ever Anne Catherick crosses your path, make better use of the opportunity, Miss Halcombe, than I made of it.”", "우리가 개를 돌보는 동안 월터 하트라이트가 내게 남긴 당부의 말이 기억에 되살아났다. “언젠가 앤 캐서릭이 당신 앞을 지나가거든, 홀콤 양, 내가 놓친 그 기회를 나보다 더 잘 쓰십시오.”"], ["The finding of the wounded spaniel had led me already to the discovery of Mrs. Catherick’s visit to Blackwater Park, and that event might lead in its turn, to something more.", "다친 스패니얼을 찾아낸 일이 벌써 캐서릭 부인이 블랙워터 파크에 다녀갔다는 발견으로 나를 이끌었으니, 그 일이 다시 무언가 더 큰 것으로 이어질지도 몰랐다."], ["I determined to make the most of the chance which was now offered to me, and to gain as much information as I could.", "나는 지금 내게 주어진 이 기회를 최대한 활용해 되도록 많은 정보를 얻어내기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Did you say that Mrs. Catherick lived anywhere in this neighbourhood?”", "“캐서릭 부인이 이 근처 어딘가에 산다고 하셨던가요?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Oh dear, no,”", "“아이고, 아니에요,”"], ["said the housekeeper.", "가정부가 말했다."], ["“She lives at Welmingham, quite at the other end of the county—five-and-twenty miles off, at least.”", "“그분은 웰밍엄에 사십니다. 이 주의 정반대 끝이지요—적어도 이십오 마일은 떨어져 있습니다.”"]], [["“I suppose you have known Mrs. Catherick for some years?”", "“캐서릭 부인을 몇 해 전부터 알고 지내셨겠군요?”"]], [["“On the contrary, Miss Halcombe, I never saw her before she came here yesterday.", "“천만에요, 홀콤 양. 저는 어제 그분이 여기 오시기 전까지는 한 번도 뵌 적이 없습니다."], ["I had heard of her, of course, because I had heard of Sir Percival’s kindness in putting her daughter under medical care.", "물론 이야기는 들었지요. 퍼시벌 경께서 친절을 베푸셔서 그분 따님을 의사에게 맡겨 돌보게 하셨다는 이야기를 들었으니까요."], ["Mrs. Catherick is rather a strange person in her manners, but extremely respectable-looking.", "캐서릭 부인은 태도가 좀 이상한 분이지만 겉보기에는 대단히 점잖으십니다."], ["She seemed sorely put out when she found that there was no foundation—none, at least, that any of us could discover—for the report of her daughter having been seen in this neighbourhood.”", "따님이 이 근처에서 목격되었다는 소문에 아무 근거가 없다는 것을—적어도 저희 중 누구도 근거를 찾아낼 수 없다는 것을—아시고는 몹시 낙심하신 듯했습니다.”"]], [["“I am rather interested about Mrs. Catherick,”", "“저는 캐서릭 부인에게 제법 관심이 있어요.”"], ["I went on, continuing the conversation as long as possible.", "나는 대화를 되도록 오래 끌며 말을 이었다."], ["“I wish I had arrived here soon enough to see her yesterday.", "“어제 그분을 뵐 수 있도록 조금만 일찍 도착했더라면 좋았을걸요."], ["Did she stay for any length of time?”", "오래 계셨나요?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네,”"], ["said the housekeeper, “she stayed for some time; and I think she would have remained longer, if I had not been called away to speak to a strange gentleman—a gentleman who came to ask when Sir Percival was expected back.", "가정부가 말했다. “한참 계셨습니다."], ["Mrs. Catherick got up and left at once, when she heard the maid tell me what the visitor’s errand was.", "제가 낯선 신사분과 이야기하러 불려가지만 않았더라면 더 오래 계셨을 겁니다—퍼시벌 경께서 언제 돌아오시는지 물으러 온 신사분이었지요."], ["She said to me, at parting, that there was no need to tell Sir Percival of her coming here.", "캐서릭 부인은 하녀가 그 손님의 용건을 제게 전하는 것을 듣자마자 자리에서 일어나 곧바로 떠나셨습니다. 헤어지면서 저에게, 여기 다녀간 일을 퍼시벌 경께 알릴 필요는 없다고 하시더군요."], ["I thought that rather an odd remark to make, especially to a person in my responsible situation.”", "저는 그게 꽤 이상한 말이라고 생각했습니다. 더구나 저처럼 책임 있는 자리에 있는 사람한테 하시는 말씀으로는요.”"]], [["I thought it an odd remark too.", "나도 그것이 이상한 말이라고 생각했다."], ["Sir Percival had certainly led me to believe, at Limmeridge, that the most perfect confidence existed between himself and Mrs. Catherick.", "퍼시벌 경은 리머리지에서 분명 자기와 캐서릭 부인 사이에 더없이 완전한 신뢰가 존재한다고 내가 믿게끔 말했었다."], ["If that was the case, why should she be anxious to have her visit at Blackwater Park kept a secret from him?", "정말 그렇다면 그녀가 무엇 때문에 블랙워터 파크에 다녀간 일을 그에게 비밀로 하려고 애쓰겠는가?"]], [["“Probably,”", "“아마도,”"], ["I said, seeing that the housekeeper expected me to give my opinion on Mrs.", "나는 가정부가 캐서릭 부인의 작별 인사에 대해 내 의견을 듣고 싶어 하는 것을 알아채고 말했다."], ["Catherick’s parting words, “probably she thought the announcement of her visit might vex Sir Percival to no purpose, by reminding him that her lost daughter was not found yet.", "“아마도 그분은 자기가 다녀갔다는 말이 잃어버린 딸을 아직 찾지 못했다는 사실을 상기시켜 퍼시벌 경을 공연히 언짢게 할까 봐 그러셨겠지요."], ["Did she talk much on that subject?”", "그 이야기를 많이 하시던가요?”"]], [["“Very little,” replied the housekeeper.", "“거의 안 하셨습니다.”"], ["“She talked principally of Sir Percival, and asked a great many questions about where he had been travelling, and what sort of lady his new wife was.", "가정부가 대답했다. “주로 퍼시벌 경 이야기를 하셨고, 그분이 어디를 여행하고 계셨는지, 새 부인은 어떤 분인지 아주 많이 물으셨어요."], ["She seemed to be more soured and put out than distressed, by failing to find any traces of her daughter in these parts.", "이 근방에서 따님의 자취를 찾지 못한 것에 대해서도 슬퍼한다기보다 마음이 상하고 언짢으신 듯했습니다."], ["‘I give her up,’ were the last words she said that I can remember; ‘I give her up, ma’am, for lost.’ And from that she passed at once to her questions about Lady Glyde, wanting to know if she was a handsome, amiable lady, comely and healthy and young——Ah, dear!", "‘그 애는 포기하겠어요.’ 그게 제가 기억하는 그분의 마지막 말씀이었습니다. ‘그 애는 잃은 셈치고 포기하겠어요, 부인.’ 그러고는 곧장 글라이드 부인에 대한 질문으로 넘어가셔서, 아름답고 상냥한 분인지, 어여쁘고 건강하고 젊은 분인지 알고 싶어 하시고——아이고, 저런!"], ["I thought how it would end.", "이렇게 될 줄 알았습니다."], ["Look, Miss Halcombe, the poor thing is out of its misery at last!”", "보세요, 홀콤 양, 가엾은 것이 드디어 고통에서 벗어났네요!”"]], [["The dog was dead.", "개는 죽어 있었다."], ["It had given a faint, sobbing cry, it had suffered an instant’s convulsion of the limbs, just as those last words, “comely and healthy and young,”", "희미하게 흐느끼는 듯한 소리를 한 번 내더니 한순간 사지에 경련이 일었다. 바로 “곱고 건강하고 젊은”이라는 마지막 말이 가정부의 입에서 떨어지던 그 순간이었다."], ["dropped from the housekeeper’s lips.", "변화는 놀랄 만큼 갑작스럽게 찾아왔다."], ["The change had happened with startling suddenness—in one moment the creature lay lifeless under our hands.", "눈 깜짝할 사이에 그 가엾은 짐승은 우리 손 밑에서 숨이 끊어져 있었다."]], [["Eight o’clock.", "여덟 시."], ["I have just returned from dining downstairs, in solitary state.", "나는 방금 아래층에서 혼자 쓸쓸히 저녁을 먹고 돌아왔다."], ["The sunset is burning redly on the wilderness of trees that I see from my window, and I am poring over my journal again, to calm my impatience for the return of the travellers.", "창문 너머로 보이는 황량한 나무숲 위로 저녁놀이 붉게 타오르고, 나는 여행자들이 돌아오기를 기다리는 초조함을 달래려고 다시 일기장을 들여다보고 있다."], ["They ought to have arrived, by my calculations, before this.", "내 계산대로라면 벌써 도착했어야 하는데."], ["How still and lonely the house is in the drowsy evening quiet!", "졸음이 밀려오는 저녁의 고요 속에서 이 집은 어찌 이리도 적막하고 외로운지!"], ["Oh me!", "아아!"], ["how many minutes more before I hear the carriage wheels and run downstairs to find myself in Laura’s arms?", "마차 바퀴 소리를 듣고 아래층으로 달려 내려가 로라의 품에 안기기까지 앞으로 몇 분이나 더 기다려야 하는 걸까?"]], [["The poor little dog!", "가엾은 작은 개!"], ["I wish my first day at Blackwater Park had not been associated with death, though it is only the death of a stray animal.", "블랙워터 파크에서 보낸 나의 첫날이 죽음과 얽히지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을. 비록 떠돌이 짐승 한 마리의 죽음일 뿐이라 해도."]], [["Welmingham—I see, on looking back through these private pages of mine, that Welmingham is the name of the place where Mrs.", "웰밍엄. 나만의 이 사사로운 기록들을 되짚어보다가 알았는데, 웰밍엄이란 캐서릭 부인이 사는 고장의 이름이다."], ["Catherick lives.", "그 여자의 짤막한 답장은 아직 내가 간직하고 있다."], ["Her note is still in my possession, the note in answer to that letter about her unhappy daughter which Sir Percival obliged me to write.", "퍼시벌 경이 억지로 쓰게 한, 그 불행한 딸에 관한 편지에 대한 답장 말이다."], ["One of these days, when I can find a safe opportunity, I will take the note with me by way of introduction, and try what I can make of Mrs. Catherick at a personal interview.", "언젠가 안전한 기회가 생기면 그 쪽지를 소개장 삼아 들고 가서, 캐서릭 부인을 직접 만나 무엇을 알아낼 수 있을지 시험해볼 작정이다."], ["I don’t understand her wishing to conceal her visit to this place from Sir Percival’s knowledge, and I don’t feel half so sure, as the housekeeper seems to do, that her daughter Anne is not in the neighbourhood after all.", "그 여자가 이곳에 다녀간 일을 퍼시벌 경이 알지 못하도록 감추고 싶어 하는 까닭을 나는 이해할 수 없다. 그리고 딸 앤이 결국 이 근방에 있지 않다는 것도, 가정부가 확신하는 것의 절반만큼도 나는 확신할 수 없다."], ["What would Walter Hartright have said in this emergency?", "이런 다급한 처지에서라면 월터 하트라이트는 뭐라고 말했을까?"], ["Poor, dear Hartright!", "가엾고 다정한 하트라이트!"], ["I am beginning to feel the want of his honest advice and his willing help already.", "나는 벌써부터 그의 정직한 조언과 기꺼운 도움이 아쉬워지기 시작한다."]], [["Surely I heard something.", "분명 무슨 소리가 들렸다."], ["Was it a bustle of footsteps below stairs?", "아래층에서 부산하게 오가는 발소리였을까?"], ["Yes!", "그래!"], ["I hear the horses’ feet—I hear the rolling wheels——", "말발굽 소리가 들린다. 굴러오는 바퀴 소리가 들린다——"]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["June 15th.—The confusion of their arrival has had time to subside.", "6월 15일.—그들이 도착하며 일었던 소동도 이제 가라앉을 만큼 시간이 흘렀다."], ["Two days have elapsed since the return of the travellers, and that interval has sufficed to put the new machinery of our lives at Blackwater Park in fair working order.", "여행자들이 돌아온 지 이틀이 지났고, 그 사이에 블랙워터 파크에서 우리 생활을 움직이는 새로운 기계 장치도 그런대로 잘 굴러가게 되었다."], ["I may now return to my journal, with some little chance of being able to continue the entries in it as collectedly as usual.", "이제 나는 평소만큼 차분하게 기록을 이어 갈 수 있으리라는 작은 희망을 품고 일기로 돌아온다."]], [["I think I must begin by putting down an odd remark which has suggested itself to me since Laura came back.", "로라가 돌아온 뒤로 문득 떠오른 묘한 생각을 하나 적어 두는 것으로 시작해야겠다."]], [["When two members of a family or two intimate friends are separated, and one goes abroad and one remains at home, the return of the relative or friend who has been travelling always seems to place the relative or friend who has been staying at home at a painful disadvantage when the two first meet.", "한 가족의 두 사람, 혹은 절친한 두 벗이 헤어져 하나는 외국으로 가고 하나는 집에 남아 있을 때, 여행하고 돌아온 쪽과 집을 지킨 쪽이 처음 다시 마주하는 순간이면 집에 남아 있던 쪽은 언제나 괴로운 열세에 놓이는 듯하다."], ["The sudden encounter of the new thoughts and new habits eagerly gained in the one case, with the old thoughts and old habits passively preserved in the other, seems at first to part the sympathies of the most loving relatives and the fondest friends, and to set a sudden strangeness, unexpected by both and uncontrollable by both, between them on either side.", "한쪽이 열심히 얻어 온 새로운 생각과 새로운 습관이, 다른 쪽이 가만히 지켜 온 옛 생각과 옛 습관과 느닷없이 부딪치면, 처음에는 세상에서 가장 다정한 혈육 사이도, 가장 애틋한 벗 사이도 마음이 갈라지고, 양쪽 다 예상하지 못했고 양쪽 다 어쩌지 못하는 어색함이 둘 사이에 불쑥 들어서는 것 같다."], ["After the first happiness of my meeting with Laura was over, after we had sat down together hand in hand to recover breath enough and calmness enough to talk, I felt this strangeness instantly, and I could see that she felt it too.", "로라와 다시 만난 첫 기쁨이 지나가고, 이야기할 만큼 숨과 침착을 되찾으려 손을 맞잡고 나란히 앉았을 때, 나는 곧바로 그 낯섦을 느꼈고 로라도 그것을 느끼고 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["It has partially worn away, now that we have fallen back into most of our old habits, and it will probably disappear before long.", "이제 우리가 옛 습관을 대부분 되찾은 터라 그 느낌은 얼마간 옅어졌고, 머지않아 아주 사라질 것이다."], ["But it has certainly had an influence over the first impressions that I have formed of her, now that we are living together again—for which reason only I have thought fit to mention it here.", "그러나 다시 함께 살게 된 지금 내가 로라에 대해 품게 된 첫인상에 그것이 영향을 끼친 것만은 분명하다—오직 그 때문에 여기 적어 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했다."]], [["She has found me unaltered, but I have found her changed.", "로라는 내가 조금도 변하지 않았다고 했지만, 나는 로라가 변했다고 느꼈다."]], [["Changed in person, and in one respect changed in character.", "모습이 변했고, 한 가지 점에서는 성격도 변했다."], ["I cannot absolutely say that she is less beautiful than she used to be—I can only say that she is less beautiful to me.", "예전보다 덜 아름다워졌다고 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없다—다만 내게는 덜 아름다워졌다고 말할 수 있을 뿐이다."]], [["Others, who do not look at her with my eyes and my recollections, would probably think her improved.", "나의 눈과 나의 기억으로 로라를 보지 않는 사람이라면 아마 더 나아졌다고 여길 것이다."], ["There is more colour and more decision and roundness of outline in her face than there used to be, and her figure seems more firmly set and more sure and easy in all its movements than it was in her maiden days.", "얼굴에는 예전보다 혈색이 돌고 윤곽이 또렷하고 둥글어졌으며, 몸매도 처녀 시절보다 한결 자리가 잡히고 모든 동작이 안정되고 편안해 보인다."], ["But I miss something when I look at her—something that once belonged to the happy, innocent life of Laura Fairlie, and that I cannot find in Lady Glyde.", "그러나 로라를 바라보노라면 무언가가 빠져 있다—한때 로라 페얼리의 행복하고 순결한 삶에 속해 있었으나 이제 글라이드 부인에게서는 찾을 수 없는 무언가가."], ["There was in the old times a freshness, a softness, an ever-varying and yet ever-remaining tenderness of beauty in her face, the charm of which it is not possible to express in words, or, as poor Hartright used often to say, in painting either.", "옛날 그 얼굴에는 싱그러움과 부드러움이, 끊임없이 변하면서도 언제나 남아 있던 아름다움의 애틋함이 있었고, 그 매력은 말로도, 가엾은 하트라이트가 늘 하던 말대로 그림으로도 옮길 수 없는 것이었다."], ["This is gone.", "그것이 사라졌다."], ["I thought I saw the faint reflection of it for a moment when she turned pale under the agitation of our sudden meeting on the evening of her return, but it has never reappeared since.", "로라가 돌아온 날 저녁, 갑작스러운 만남에 격해져 얼굴이 창백해졌을 때 나는 그 희미한 잔영을 한순간 본 듯했으나, 그 뒤로 다시는 나타나지 않았다."], ["None of her letters had prepared me for a personal change in her.", "로라의 편지 어디에도 그런 외모의 변화를 짐작하게 하는 대목은 없었다."], ["On the contrary, they had led me to expect that her marriage had left her, in appearance at least, quite unaltered.", "오히려 편지들은 결혼이 적어도 겉모습만은 로라를 조금도 바꾸어 놓지 않았으리라 기대하게 만들었다."], ["Perhaps I read her letters wrongly in the past, and am now reading her face wrongly in the present? No matter!", "어쩌면 나는 지난날 로라의 편지를 잘못 읽었고, 지금은 로라의 얼굴을 잘못 읽고 있는 것일까?"], ["Whether her beauty has gained or whether it has lost in the last six months, the separation either way has made her own dear self more precious to me than ever, and that is one good result of her marriage, at any rate!", "아무려면 어떠랴! 지난 여섯 달 사이에 로라의 아름다움이 늘었든 줄었든, 그 이별은 어느 쪽으로든 그 사랑스러운 사람 자체를 내게 그 어느 때보다 소중하게 만들어 주었으니, 어쨌든 그것만은 로라의 결혼이 낳은 하나의 좋은 결과다!"]], [["The second change, the change that I have observed in her character, has not surprised me, because I was prepared for it in this case by the tone of her letters.", "두 번째 변화, 곧 로라의 성격에서 내가 알아차린 변화는 놀랍지 않았다. 이 경우에는 편지의 어조로 이미 마음의 준비가 되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Now that she is at home again, I find her just as unwilling to enter into any details on the subject of her married life as I had previously found her all through the time of our separation, when we could only communicate with each other by writing.", "다시 집에 돌아온 지금도 로라는 결혼 생활에 관해서라면 어떤 자세한 이야기도 꺼리는데, 이는 편지로만 소식을 주고받던 헤어져 있던 내내 내가 보아 온 그대로다."], ["At the first approach I made to the forbidden topic she put her hand on my lips with a look and gesture which touchingly, almost painfully, recalled to my memory the days of her girlhood and the happy bygone time when there were no secrets between us.", "내가 그 금지된 화제에 처음 다가서려 하자 로라는 내 입술에 손을 얹었는데, 그 눈빛과 몸짓은 소녀 시절, 우리 사이에 아무 비밀도 없던 행복한 옛 시절을 뭉클하게, 거의 아프도록 떠올리게 했다."]], [["“Whenever you and I are together, Marian,”", "“매리언, 우리 둘이 함께 있을 때는 말이야,”"], ["she said, “we shall both be happier and easier with one another, if we accept my married life for what it is, and say and think as little about it as possible.", "로라가 말했다. “내 결혼 생활을 있는 그대로 받아들이고, 그 이야기는 되도록 적게 말하고 적게 생각하는 편이 서로에게 더 행복하고 편할 거야."], ["I would tell you everything, darling, about myself,”", "나에 관한 거라면 무엇이든 다 이야기해 주고 싶어, 언니,”"], ["she went on, nervously buckling and unbuckling the ribbon round my waist, “if my confidences could only end there.", "로라는 내 허리에 두른 리본을 초조하게 풀었다 조였다 하며 말을 이었다. “털어놓는 게 거기서 그칠 수만 있다면 말이야."], ["But they could not—they would lead me into confidences about my husband too; and now I am married, I think I had better avoid them, for his sake, and for your sake, and for mine.", "하지만 그럴 수가 없어—그이 이야기까지 하게 될 테니까. 이제 나는 결혼한 몸이니, 그이를 위해서도, 언니를 위해서도, 나를 위해서도 그런 이야기는 피하는 게 낫다고 생각해."], ["I don’t say that they would distress you, or distress me—I wouldn’t have you think that for the world.", "그런 이야기가 언니를 괴롭히거나 나를 괴롭힐 거라는 말은 아니야—절대로 그렇게는 생각하지 말아 줘."], ["But—I want to be so happy, now I have got you back again, and I want you to be so happy too——”", "다만—나는 이렇게 행복하고 싶어, 언니를 되찾았으니까. 그리고 언니도 그렇게 행복했으면 좋겠어——”"], ["She broke off abruptly, and looked round the room, my own sitting-room, in which we were talking.", "로라는 문득 말을 끊더니 우리가 이야기하고 있던 방, 곧 나의 거실을 둘러보았다."], ["“Ah!”", "“아!”"], ["she cried, clapping her hands with a bright smile of recognition, “another old friend found already!", "로라는 알아본 기쁨에 환하게 웃으며 손뼉을 쳤다. “벌써 옛 친구를 또 하나 찾았네!"], ["Your book-case, Marian—your dear-little-shabby-old-satin-wood book-case—how glad I am you brought it with you from Limmeridge!", "언니 책장 말이야, 매리언—그 정답고 자그마하고 낡은 새틴우드 책장—리머리지에서 가져왔다니 얼마나 반가운지!"], ["And the horrid heavy man’s umbrella, that you always would walk out with when it rained!", "그리고 비만 오면 언니가 꼭 들고 나가던 그 흉하고 묵직한 남자용 우산도!"], ["And first and foremost of all, your own dear, dark, clever, gipsy-face, looking at me just as usual!", "그리고 무엇보다도 먼저, 언제나처럼 나를 바라보고 있는 언니의 그 사랑스럽고 까무잡잡하고 총명한 집시 같은 얼굴!"], ["It is so like home again to be here.", "여기 있으니 다시 집에 돌아온 것만 같아."], ["How can we make it more like home still?", "어떻게 하면 더 집처럼 만들 수 있을까?"], ["I will put my father’s portrait in your room instead of in mine—and I will keep all my little treasures from Limmeridge here—and we will pass hours and hours every day with these four friendly walls round us.", "아버지 초상화를 내 방 말고 언니 방에 걸어야지—그리고 리머리지에서 가져온 내 자잘한 보물들도 다 여기 둘 거야—그리고 우리는 이 정다운 네 벽에 둘러싸여 날마다 몇 시간이고 함께 보내는 거야."], ["Oh, Marian!”", "오, 매리언!”"], ["she said, suddenly seating herself on a footstool at my knees, and looking up earnestly in my face, “promise you will never marry, and leave me.", "로라는 갑자기 내 무릎 앞 발받침에 앉아 내 얼굴을 간절히 올려다보며 말했다. “약속해 줘, 절대로 결혼해서 나를 떠나지 않겠다고."], ["It is selfish to say so, but you are so much better off as a single woman—unless—unless you are very fond of your husband—but you won’t be very fond of anybody but me, will you?”", "이런 말은 이기적이지만, 언니는 혼자인 편이 훨씬 나아—남편을—남편을 아주 사랑한다면 모를까—하지만 언니는 나 말고는 누구도 그렇게 사랑하지 않을 거지, 그렇지?”"], ["She stopped again, crossed my hands on my lap, and laid her face on them.", "로라는 다시 말을 멈추고, 내 두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고는 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["“Have you been writing many letters, and receiving many letters lately?”", "“요즘 편지 많이 썼어? 편지 많이 받았어?”"], ["she asked, in low, suddenly-altered tones.", "로라가 낮게, 갑자기 달라진 목소리로 물었다."], ["I understood what the question meant, but I thought it my duty not to encourage her by meeting her half way.", "나는 그 물음이 무슨 뜻인지 알았으나, 먼저 나서서 부추기지 않는 것이 도리라고 생각했다."], ["“Have you heard from him?”", "“그이한테서 소식 들었어?”"], ["she went on, coaxing me to forgive the more direct appeal on which she now ventured, by kissing my hands, upon which her face still rested.", "로라는 얼굴을 여전히 내 손에 묻은 채 그 손에 입 맞추며, 이제야 감히 꺼낸 더 직접적인 물음을 용서해 달라는 듯 나를 어르며 말을 이었다."], ["“Is he well and happy, and getting on in his profession?", "“잘 지내고 행복하대? 자기 일도 잘되어 간대?"], ["Has he recovered himself—and forgotten me?”", "마음을 추스르고—나를 잊었대?”"]], [["She should not have asked those questions.", "로라는 그런 질문을 하지 말았어야 했다."], ["She should have remembered her own resolution, on the morning when Sir Percival held her to her marriage engagement, and when she resigned the book of Hartright’s drawings into my hands for ever.", "퍼시벌 경이 약혼의 이행을 요구하던 그 아침에, 하트라이트의 그림첩을 영영 내 손에 넘겨주며 스스로 다졌던 결심을 기억했어야 했다. 그러나, 아아!"], ["But, ah me! where is the faultless human creature who can persevere in a good resolution, without sometimes failing and falling back?", "좋은 결심을 한 번도 무너뜨리거나 뒷걸음질하는 일 없이 끝까지 밀고 나가는 흠 없는 사람이 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["Where is the woman who has ever really torn from her heart the image that has been once fixed in it by a true love?", "참된 사랑으로 한번 마음에 박힌 모습을 정말로 가슴에서 뜯어낸 여자가 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["Books tell us that such unearthly creatures have existed—but what does our own experience say in answer to books?", "책은 그런 이 세상 사람 같지 않은 존재가 있었다고 말한다—그러나 우리 자신의 경험은 그 책에 무어라 답하는가?"]], [["I made no attempt to remonstrate with her: perhaps, because I sincerely appreciated the fearless candour which let me see, what other women in her position might have had reasons for concealing even from their dearest friends—perhaps, because I felt, in my own heart and conscience, that in her place I should have asked the same questions and had the same thoughts.", "나는 로라를 나무라려 하지 않았다. 어쩌면 다른 여자라면 같은 처지에서 가장 가까운 벗에게조차 숨길 이유가 있었을 것을 내게 그대로 내보이는 그 두려움 없는 솔직함이 진심으로 고마웠기 때문일 테고, 어쩌면 내가 그 자리에 있었더라도 같은 질문을 하고 같은 생각을 했으리라는 것을 내 마음과 양심으로 느꼈기 때문일 것이다."], ["All I could honestly do was to reply that I had not written to him or heard from him lately, and then to turn the conversation to less dangerous topics.", "내가 정직하게 할 수 있는 일이라고는, 요즘 그에게 편지를 쓴 적도 그에게서 소식을 받은 적도 없다고 대답하고, 이야기를 덜 위험한 화제로 돌리는 것뿐이었다."]], [["There has been much to sadden me in our interview—my first confidential interview with her since her return.", "로라가 돌아온 뒤 처음으로 나눈 그 은밀한 이야기에는 나를 슬프게 하는 것이 많았다."], ["The change which her marriage has produced in our relations towards each other, by placing a forbidden subject between us, for the first time in our lives; the melancholy conviction of the dearth of all warmth of feeling, of all close sympathy, between her husband and herself, which her own unwilling words now force on my mind; the distressing discovery that the influence of that ill-fated attachment still remains (no matter how innocently, how harmlessly) rooted as deeply as ever in her heart—all these are disclosures to sadden any woman who loves her as dearly, and feels for her as acutely, as I do.", "우리 생애 처음으로 금지된 화제가 둘 사이에 놓임으로써 로라의 결혼이 우리 사이에 빚어낸 변화, 로라 자신의 마지못한 말이 이제 내 마음에 들이미는 것—남편과 로라 사이에 따뜻한 정도, 깊은 공감도 없다는 우울한 확신, 그리고 그 불행한 애정의 힘이 아무리 순결하게, 아무리 해롭지 않게라 해도 여전히 로라의 가슴 깊이 예전 그대로 뿌리내려 있다는 괴로운 발견—이 모든 것은 나만큼 로라를 아끼고 나만큼 로라를 절실히 헤아리는 여자라면 누구든 슬프게 만들 사실들이다."]], [["There is only one consolation to set against them—a consolation that ought to comfort me, and that does comfort me.", "그에 맞설 위안은 하나뿐이다—나를 위로해야 마땅하고, 실제로 나를 위로해 주는 위안 하나."], ["All the graces and gentleness of her character—all the frank affection of her nature—all the sweet, simple, womanly charms which used to make her the darling and delight of every one who approached her, have come back to me with herself.", "로라의 성품에 깃든 모든 우아함과 온화함, 그 천성의 꾸밈없는 다정함, 다가서는 이마다 로라를 사랑하고 기뻐하게 만들던 그 달콤하고 소박하고 여성스러운 매력이 로라와 함께 내게 돌아왔다는 것."], ["Of my other impressions I am sometimes a little inclined to doubt.", "다른 인상들은 나도 이따금 조금 의심스러워진다."], ["Of this last, best, happiest of all impressions, I grow more and more certain every hour in the day.", "그러나 이 마지막, 가장 좋고 가장 행복한 인상만은 하루하루 시간이 갈수록 더욱더 확신하게 된다."]], [["Let me turn, now, from her to her travelling companions.", "이제 로라에게서 그 동행들에게로 눈을 돌려 보자."], ["Her husband must engage my attention first.", "먼저 그 남편이 내 관심을 차지해야 마땅하다."], ["What have I observed in Sir Percival, since his return, to improve my opinion of him?", "돌아온 뒤로 퍼시벌 경에게서 그에 대한 내 평가를 나아지게 할 만한 무엇을 보았던가?"]], [["I can hardly say.", "딱히 말하기 어렵다."], ["Small vexations and annoyances seem to have beset him since he came back, and no man, under those circumstances, is ever presented at his best.", "돌아온 뒤로 자잘한 짜증거리와 성가신 일들이 그를 따라다닌 듯한데, 그런 처지에서 가장 좋은 모습을 보일 수 있는 사람이 어디 있겠는가."], ["He looks, as I think, thinner than he was when he left England.", "내 눈에는 잉글랜드를 떠날 때보다 야위어 보인다."], ["His wearisome cough and his comfortless restlessness have certainly increased.", "지겨운 기침과 불편한 안절부절못함은 확실히 심해졌다."], ["His manner—at least his manner towards me—is much more abrupt than it used to be.", "태도는—적어도 나를 대하는 태도는—예전보다 훨씬 퉁명스럽다."], ["He greeted me, on the evening of his return, with little or nothing of the ceremony and civility of former times—no polite speeches of welcome—no appearance of extraordinary gratification at seeing me—nothing but a short shake of the hand, and a sharp “How-d’ye-do, Miss Halcombe—glad to see you again.”", "돌아온 날 저녁, 그는 옛날의 격식과 정중함이라고는 거의 없이 나를 맞았다—반가운 인사말도 없고—나를 보아 각별히 기뻐하는 기색도 없고—짧게 손을 한 번 잡아 흔들고는 무뚝뚝하게 “안녕하십니까, 홀콤 양—다시 뵈어 반갑습니다” 한마디뿐이었다."], ["He seemed to accept me as one of the necessary fixtures of Blackwater Park, to be satisfied at finding me established in my proper place, and then to pass me over altogether.", "그는 나를 블랙워터 파크에 딸린 필수 비품 가운데 하나쯤으로 받아들이고, 내가 제자리에 자리 잡은 것을 확인하고 만족한 뒤, 그대로 나를 완전히 지나쳐 버리는 듯했다."]], [["Most men show something of their disposition in their own houses, which they have concealed elsewhere, and Sir Percival has already displayed a mania for order and regularity, which is quite a new revelation of him, so far as my previous knowledge of his character is concerned.", "대개의 남자는 밖에서는 감추던 성정의 일단을 제 집에서 드러내는 법인데, 퍼시벌 경은 벌써 정돈과 규칙에 대한 병적인 집착을 드러냈다. 내가 여태 알던 그의 성격에 비추어 보면 아주 새로운 발견이다."], ["If I take a book from the library and leave it on the table, he follows me and puts it back again.", "내가 서재에서 책을 꺼내 탁자 위에 두면 그는 따라와서 도로 꽂아 놓는다."], ["If I rise from a chair, and let it remain where I have been sitting, he carefully restores it to its proper place against the wall.", "내가 의자에서 일어나 앉았던 자리에 그대로 두면 그는 조심스레 벽 쪽 제자리로 되돌려 놓는다."], ["He picks up stray flower-blossoms from the carpet, and mutters to himself as discontentedly as if they were hot cinders burning holes in it, and he storms at the servants if there is a crease in the tablecloth, or a knife missing from its place at the dinner-table, as fiercely as if they had personally insulted him.", "양탄자에 떨어진 꽃잎을 주우면서, 마치 그것이 양탄자에 구멍을 내는 시뻘건 잉걸불이라도 되는 양 못마땅하게 중얼거리고, 식탁보에 주름이 잡혔거나 만찬 식탁의 나이프 하나가 제자리에 없으면 마치 하인들이 자기를 몸소 모욕하기라도 한 듯 사납게 호통을 친다."]], [["I have already referred to the small annoyances which appear to have troubled him since his return.", "그가 돌아온 뒤로 그를 괴롭힌 듯한 자잘한 성가신 일들에 대해서는 앞에서도 언급했다."], ["Much of the alteration for the worse which I have noticed in him may be due to these.", "내가 알아차린 그의 나쁜 쪽 변화도 상당 부분 거기서 비롯된 것일 수 있다."], ["I try to persuade myself that it is so, because I am anxious not to be disheartened already about the future.", "나는 그렇다고 나 자신을 설득해 보려 한다. 벌써부터 앞날에 낙담하고 싶지 않기 때문이다."], ["It is certainly trying to any man’s temper to be met by a vexation the moment he sets foot in his own house again, after a long absence, and this annoying circumstance did really happen to Sir Percival in my presence.", "오랜 부재 끝에 제 집에 발을 들여놓는 바로 그 순간에 성가신 일과 마주치는 것은 누구의 성미에도 견디기 힘든 노릇이며, 실제로 그 짜증스러운 일이 내가 보는 앞에서 퍼시벌 경에게 일어났다."]], [["On the evening of their arrival the housekeeper followed me into the hall to receive her master and mistress and their guests.", "그들이 도착한 날 저녁, 가정부는 주인 내외와 손님들을 맞으려고 나를 따라 현관으로 나왔다."], ["The instant he saw her, Sir Percival asked if any one had called lately.", "그녀를 보자마자 퍼시벌 경은 최근에 찾아온 사람이 있었느냐고 물었다."], ["The housekeeper mentioned to him, in reply, what she had previously mentioned to me, the visit of the strange gentleman to make inquiries about the time of her master’s return.", "가정부는 앞서 내게 말했던 그대로, 주인의 귀가 시기를 묻고 간 낯선 신사의 방문을 이야기했다."], ["He asked immediately for the gentleman’s name.", "그는 곧바로 그 신사의 이름을 물었다."], ["No name had been left.", "이름은 남기지 않았다고 했다."], ["The gentleman’s business?", "신사의 용건은?"], ["No business had been mentioned.", "용건은 말하지 않았다고 했다."], ["What was the gentleman like?", "그 신사는 어떻게 생겼던가?"], ["The housekeeper tried to describe him, but failed to distinguish the nameless visitor by any personal peculiarity which her master could recognise.", "가정부는 그를 묘사해 보려 했으나, 주인이 알아볼 만한 어떤 개인적 특징으로도 그 이름 없는 방문객을 짚어 내지 못했다."], ["Sir Percival frowned, stamped angrily on the floor, and walked on into the house, taking no notice of anybody.", "퍼시벌 경은 눈살을 찌푸리고 성난 듯 바닥을 구르더니, 아무에게도 아랑곳하지 않고 집 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Why he should have been so discomposed by a trifle I cannot say—but he was seriously discomposed, beyond all doubt.", "그런 하찮은 일에 왜 그토록 마음이 흐트러졌는지 나는 알 수 없다—그러나 그가 심각하게 마음이 흐트러진 것만은 의심할 여지가 없다."]], [["Upon the whole, it will be best, perhaps, if I abstain from forming a decisive opinion of his manners, language, and conduct in his own house, until time has enabled him to shake off the anxieties, whatever they may be, which now evidently troubled his mind in secret.", "대체로 보아, 지금 분명 그의 마음을 남몰래 괴롭히고 있는 근심이 무엇이든 시간이 그것을 떨쳐 내게 해 줄 때까지는, 제 집에서의 그의 태도와 말과 행실에 대해 단정적인 판단을 내리는 일은 삼가는 편이 나을 것이다."], ["I will turn over to a new page, and my pen shall let Laura’s husband alone for the present.", "나는 새 장으로 넘어가겠고, 내 펜은 당분간 로라의 남편을 내버려 두겠다."]], [["The two guests—the Count and Countess Fosco—come next in my catalogue.", "두 손님—포스코 백작과 백작부인—이 내 목록의 다음 차례다."], ["I will dispose of the Countess first, so as to have done with the woman as soon as possible.", "백작부인부터 처리하기로 하자. 그 여자와는 되도록 빨리 볼일을 끝내는 편이 나으니."]], [["Laura was certainly not chargeable with any exaggeration, in writing me word that I should hardly recognise her aunt again when we met.", "다시 만나면 숙모를 알아보지도 못할 거라고 로라가 편지에 써 보낸 것은 결코 과장이 아니었다."], ["Never before have I beheld such a change produced in a woman by her marriage as has been produced in Madame Fosco.", "결혼이 여자에게 일으킨 변화 가운데 포스코 백작부인에게 일어난 것만 한 변화를 나는 여태 본 적이 없다."]], [["As Eleanor Fairlie (aged seven-and-thirty), she was always talking pretentious nonsense, and always worrying the unfortunate men with every small exaction which a vain and foolish woman can impose on long-suffering male humanity.", "엘리너 페얼리(서른일곱 살)였을 때 그녀는 늘 거드름 피우는 헛소리를 늘어놓았고, 허영심 많고 어리석은 여자가 오래 참는 남자들에게 지울 수 있는 온갖 자잘한 요구로 그 불쌍한 남자들을 볶아 댔다."], ["As Madame Fosco (aged three-and-forty), she sits for hours together without saying a word, frozen up in the strangest manner in herself.", "포스코 백작부인(마흔세 살)이 된 지금은 몇 시간이고 한마디도 없이 앉아, 더없이 기이한 방식으로 제 안에 얼어붙어 있다."], ["The hideously ridiculous love-locks which used to hang on either side of her face are now replaced by stiff little rows of very short curls, of the sort one sees in old-fashioned wigs.", "예전에 얼굴 양옆으로 늘어뜨리고 다니던 그 흉측하고 우스꽝스러운 애교머리는 이제 옛날 가발에서나 볼 법한 아주 짧은 곱슬머리의 뻣뻣한 줄로 바뀌었다."], ["A plain, matronly cap covers her head, and makes her look, for the first time in her life since I remember her, like a decent woman.", "수수한 부인용 모자가 머리를 덮고 있어, 내가 기억하는 한 그녀의 평생에 처음으로 점잖은 여자처럼 보이게 한다."], ["Nobody (putting her husband out of the question, of course) now sees in her, what everybody once saw—I mean the structure of the female skeleton, in the upper regions of the collar-bones and the shoulder-blades.", "이제는 아무도(물론 남편은 논외로 하고) 그녀에게서 예전에 누구나 보던 것을 보지 못한다—쇄골과 어깨뼈 언저리에 드러나던 여자 골격의 구조 말이다."], ["Clad in quiet black or grey gowns, made high round the throat—dresses that she would have laughed at, or screamed at, as the whim of the moment inclined her, in her maiden days—she sits speechless in corners; her dry white hands (so dry that the pores of her skin look chalky) incessantly engaged, either in monotonous embroidery work or in rolling up endless cigarettes for the Count’s own particular smoking.", "목까지 높이 올라오는 수수한 검정이나 회색 드레스—처녀 시절이라면 그때그때 기분에 따라 비웃거나 비명을 질렀을 옷을 입고, 그녀는 구석에 말없이 앉아 있다. 살결의 땀구멍까지 백묵처럼 보일 만큼 메마른 흰 두 손은 단조로운 자수를 놓거나 백작이 손수 피울 담배를 끝없이 마느라 쉴 새가 없다."], ["On the few occasions when her cold blue eyes are off her work, they are generally turned on her husband, with the look of mute submissive inquiry which we are all familiar with in the eyes of a faithful dog.", "그 차가운 푸른 눈이 이따금 일감에서 떨어질 때면 대개 남편에게로 향하는데, 그 눈빛은 충직한 개의 눈에서 우리 모두가 익히 보아 온 말 없는 순종의 물음 그것이다."], ["The only approach to an inward thaw which I have yet detected under her outer covering of icy constraint, has betrayed itself, once or twice, in the form of a suppressed tigerish jealousy of any woman in the house (the maids included) to whom the Count speaks, or on whom he looks with anything approaching to special interest or attention.", "얼음 같은 절제의 겉껍질 아래에서 내가 여태 알아챈 유일한 내면의 해빙 비슷한 것은, 백작이 말을 걸거나 특별한 관심이나 주의에 가까운 눈길을 주는 이 집안의 어떤 여자(하녀들까지 포함해서)에게든 억눌린 범 같은 질투가 한두 번 새어 나온 일뿐이다."], ["Except in this one particular, she is always, morning, noon, and night, indoors and out, fair weather or foul, as cold as a statue, and as impenetrable as the stone out of which it is cut.", "이 한 가지를 빼면 그녀는 아침이나 낮이나 밤이나, 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 날이 좋으나 궂으나, 언제나 조각상처럼 차갑고 그 조각상을 깎아 낸 돌처럼 속을 알 수 없다."], ["For the common purposes of society the extraordinary change thus produced in her is, beyond all doubt, a change for the better, seeing that it has transformed her into a civil, silent, unobtrusive woman, who is never in the way.", "세상살이의 통상적인 쓸모로 보자면 그녀에게 일어난 이 놀라운 변화는 의심할 바 없이 좋은 쪽으로의 변화다. 그 덕에 그녀는 예의 바르고 조용하고 나서지 않는, 거치적거리는 법이 없는 여자가 되었으니 말이다."], ["How far she is really reformed or deteriorated in her secret self, is another question.", "그녀의 은밀한 속마음이 정말로 나아졌는지 아니면 나빠졌는지는 또 다른 문제다."], ["I have once or twice seen sudden changes of expression on her pinched lips, and heard sudden inflexions of tone in her calm voice, which have led me to suspect that her present state of suppression may have sealed up something dangerous in her nature, which used to evaporate harmlessly in the freedom of her former life.", "나는 한두 번 그 오므린 입술 위로 표정이 홱 바뀌는 것을 보았고, 그 차분한 목소리에서 문득 억양이 달라지는 것을 들었는데, 그 때문에 지금의 억눌린 상태가 그녀의 천성 속 위험한 무언가를, 예전 자유로운 생활에서라면 해롭지 않게 증발해 버렸을 무언가를 봉인해 버린 것이 아닌가 의심하게 되었다."], ["It is quite possible that I may be altogether wrong in this idea.", "이 생각이 아주 틀렸을 가능성도 충분히 있다."], ["My own impression, however, is, that I am right.", "그러나 내 느낌으로는 내가 옳다."], ["Time will show.", "시간이 밝혀 주리라."]], [["And the magician who has wrought this wonderful transformation—the foreign husband who has tamed this once wayward English woman till her own relations hardly know her again—the Count himself?", "그러면 이 놀라운 변신을 이루어 낸 마법사—한때 제멋대로이던 이 잉글랜드 여자를 친정 식구들조차 알아보지 못할 만큼 길들여 놓은 외국인 남편—백작 그 사람은 어떤가?"], ["What of the Count?", "백작에 대해서는 무어라 해야 하나?"]], [["This in two words: He looks like a man who could tame anything.", "한마디로 말하면 이렇다. 그는 무엇이든 길들일 수 있을 것처럼 보이는 사람이다."], ["If he had married a tigress, instead of a woman, he would have tamed the tigress.", "여자가 아니라 암범과 결혼했더라도 그는 그 암범을 길들였을 것이다."], ["If he had married me, I should have made his cigarettes, as his wife does—I should have held my tongue when he looked at me, as she holds hers.", "나와 결혼했더라면 나도 그의 아내가 그러듯 그의 담배를 말고 있을 것이다—그가 나를 바라보면 그녀가 그러듯 나도 입을 다물고 있을 것이다."]], [["I am almost afraid to confess it, even to these secret pages.", "이 은밀한 종이 앞에서조차 고백하기가 거의 두렵다."], ["The man has interested me, has attracted me, has forced me to like him.", "그 남자는 나의 흥미를 끌었고, 나를 끌어당겼고, 기어이 그를 좋아하게 만들었다."], ["In two short days he has made his way straight into my favourable estimation, and how he has worked the miracle is more than I can tell.", "짧은 이틀 만에 그는 내 호의 어린 평가 속으로 곧장 걸어 들어왔는데, 어떻게 그 기적을 부렸는지는 나로서도 알 길이 없다."]], [["It absolutely startles me, now he is in my mind, to find how plainly I see him!—how much more plainly than I see Sir Percival, or Mr. Fairlie, or Walter Hartright, or any other absent person of whom I think, with the one exception of Laura herself!", "이렇게 그를 마음에 떠올려 보니, 내가 그를 얼마나 또렷하게 보고 있는지가 참으로 놀랍다!—퍼시벌 경보다도, 페얼리 씨보다도, 월터 하트라이트보다도, 로라 자신을 유일한 예외로 하면 내가 떠올리는 그 어떤 자리에 없는 사람보다도 훨씬 또렷하게!"], ["I can hear his voice, as if he was speaking at this moment.", "나는 그의 목소리를 지금 그가 말하고 있기라도 한 듯 들을 수 있다."], ["I know what his conversation was yesterday, as well as if I was hearing it now.", "어제 그가 나눈 이야기를 지금 듣고 있기라도 한 듯 알고 있다."], ["How am I to describe him?", "그를 어떻게 묘사해야 하나?"], ["There are peculiarities in his personal appearance, his habits, and his amusements, which I should blame in the boldest terms, or ridicule in the most merciless manner, if I had seen them in another man.", "그의 외모와 습관과 소일거리에는, 다른 남자에게서 보았더라면 가장 매서운 말로 나무라거나 가장 가차 없이 비웃었을 별난 구석들이 있다."], ["What is it that makes me unable to blame them, or to ridicule them in HIM?", "그런데 그에게서만은 그것을 나무라지도 비웃지도 못하게 만드는 것은 대체 무엇인가?"]], [["For example, he is immensely fat.", "이를테면 그는 엄청나게 뚱뚱하다."], ["Before this time I have always especially disliked corpulent humanity.", "나는 여태껏 살진 인간이라면 유난히 싫어해 왔다."], ["I have always maintained that the popular notion of connecting excessive grossness of size and excessive good-humour as inseparable allies was equivalent to declaring, either that no people but amiable people ever get fat, or that the accidental addition of so many pounds of flesh has a directly favourable influence over the disposition of the person on whose body they accumulate.", "지나친 비대함과 지나친 쾌활함을 떼어 놓을 수 없는 짝으로 엮는 세상의 통념은, 상냥한 사람만이 살이 찐다고 선언하거나, 아니면 우연히 몸에 붙은 그 수많은 파운드의 살이 그 살을 이고 다니는 사람의 성정에 곧바로 좋은 영향을 준다고 선언하는 것과 다름없다고 나는 늘 주장해 왔다."], ["I have invariably combated both these absurd assertions by quoting examples of fat people who were as mean, vicious, and cruel as the leanest and the worst of their neighbours.", "그리고 이웃의 그 어떤 마른 사람, 그 어떤 못된 사람 못지않게 인색하고 악랄하고 잔인한 뚱뚱한 사람들의 예를 들어 이 두 가지 터무니없는 주장을 한결같이 반박해 왔다."], ["I have asked whether Henry the Eighth was an amiable character?", "헨리 8세가 상냥한 인물이었던가?"], ["Whether Pope Alexander the Sixth was a good man? Whether Mr.", "교황 알렉산데르 6세가 선한 사람이었던가?"], ["Murderer and Mrs. Murderess Manning were not both unusually stout people?", "살인자 매닝 씨와 살인자 매닝 부인은 둘 다 유난히 뚱뚱한 사람들이 아니었던가?"], ["Whether hired nurses, proverbially as cruel a set of women as are to be found in all England, were not, for the most part, also as fat a set of women as are to be found in all England?—and so on, through dozens of other examples, modern and ancient, native and foreign, high and low.", "잉글랜드를 통틀어 가장 잔인한 여자들로 소문난 삯간호부들은 대체로 잉글랜드를 통틀어 가장 뚱뚱한 여자들이기도 하지 않던가?—이런 식으로 현대와 고대, 국내와 국외, 상류와 하류를 오가며 수십 가지 예를 들어 왔다."], ["Holding these strong opinions on the subject with might and main as I do at this moment, here, nevertheless, is Count Fosco, as fat as Henry the Eighth himself, established in my favour, at one day’s notice, without let or hindrance from his own odious corpulence.", "지금 이 순간에도 이 문제에 대해 이토록 강경한 견해를 온 힘을 다해 붙들고 있건만, 그런데도 여기 헨리 8세만큼이나 뚱뚱한 포스코 백작이 있어, 자신의 그 밉살스러운 비만이 아무 걸림돌도 되지 않은 채 단 하루 만에 내 호의 안에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Marvellous indeed!", "참으로 놀라운 일이다!"]], [["Is it his face that has recommended him?", "그의 얼굴이 그를 그렇게 추천해 준 것일까?"]], [["It may be his face.", "얼굴 때문일지도 모른다."], ["He is a most remarkable likeness, on a large scale, of the great Napoleon.", "그는 위대한 나폴레옹을 크게 확대해 놓은 듯 놀랍도록 닮았다."], ["His features have Napoleon’s magnificent regularity—his expression recalls the grandly calm, immovable power of the Great Soldier’s face.", "이목구비에는 나폴레옹의 장대한 균형이 있고, 표정은 그 위대한 군인의 얼굴에 서린 웅장하도록 고요하고 흔들림 없는 힘을 떠올리게 한다."], ["This striking resemblance certainly impressed me, to begin with; but there is something in him besides the resemblance, which has impressed me more.", "이 놀라운 닮음이 처음에 나를 사로잡은 것은 분명하다. 그러나 그에게는 그 닮음 말고도 나를 더 강하게 사로잡은 무언가가 있다."], ["I think the influence I am now trying to find is in his eyes.", "지금 내가 찾으려 하는 그 영향력은 그의 눈에 있는 것 같다."], ["They are the most unfathomable grey eyes I ever saw, and they have at times a cold, clear, beautiful, irresistible glitter in them which forces me to look at him, and yet causes me sensations, when I do look, which I would rather not feel.", "내가 여태 본 것 가운데 가장 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 잿빛 눈이며, 때때로 거기에는 차갑고 맑고 아름답고 거역할 수 없는 광채가 어리는데, 그 빛은 나로 하여금 그를 바라보지 않을 수 없게 만들면서도, 막상 바라볼 때면 차라리 느끼지 않았으면 싶은 감각을 불러일으킨다."], ["Other parts of his face and head have their strange peculiarities.", "얼굴과 머리의 다른 부분에도 이상한 특징들이 있다."], ["His complexion, for instance, has a singular sallow-fairness, so much at variance with the dark-brown colour of his hair, that I suspect the hair of being a wig, and his face, closely shaven all over, is smoother and freer from all marks and wrinkles than mine, though (according to Sir Percival’s account of him) he is close on sixty years of age.", "이를테면 안색은 유난히 누르스름하면서도 희멀건데, 짙은 갈색 머리카락과 너무나 어울리지 않아서 나는 그 머리가 가발이 아닌가 의심한다. 그리고 온통 말끔히 면도한 그 얼굴은, (퍼시벌 경의 말로는) 예순에 가까운 나이인데도 내 얼굴보다 더 매끄럽고 어떤 자국도 주름도 없다."], ["But these are not the prominent personal characteristics which distinguish him, to my mind, from all the other men I have ever seen.", "그러나 내가 보기에 여태 본 모든 남자들로부터 그를 구별해 주는 두드러진 신체적 특징은 이런 것들이 아니다."], ["The marked peculiarity which singles him out from the rank and file of humanity lies entirely, so far as I can tell at present, in the extraordinary expression and extraordinary power of his eyes.", "그를 뭇사람의 대열에서 도드라지게 하는 뚜렷한 특이함은, 적어도 지금으로서 내가 말할 수 있는 한, 오로지 그 눈의 범상치 않은 표정과 범상치 않은 힘에 있다."]], [["His manner and his command of our language may also have assisted him, in some degree, to establish himself in my good opinion.", "그의 태도와 우리말을 구사하는 솜씨도 어느 정도는 그가 내 호감을 얻는 데 한몫했을 것이다."], ["He has that quiet deference, that look of pleased, attentive interest in listening to a woman, and that secret gentleness in his voice in speaking to a woman, which, say what we may, we can none of us resist.", "그에게는 여자의 말을 들을 때의 그 조용한 공손함, 기꺼워하며 귀 기울이는 관심 어린 표정, 여자에게 말할 때 목소리에 어리는 은근한 부드러움이 있는데, 우리가 무어라 하든 우리 가운데 누구도 그것에는 저항하지 못한다."], ["Here, too, his unusual command of the English language necessarily helps him.", "여기에 더해 영어를 남달리 능란하게 구사하는 것도 당연히 그에게 도움이 된다."], ["I had often heard of the extraordinary aptitude which many Italians show in mastering our strong, hard, Northern speech; but, until I saw Count Fosco, I had never supposed it possible that any foreigner could have spoken English as he speaks it.", "많은 이탈리아 사람이 우리네 억세고 딱딱한 북방 언어를 익히는 데 놀라운 재능을 보인다는 말은 자주 들었지만, 포스코 백작을 보기 전까지 나는 어떤 외국인이 그가 하듯이 영어를 할 수 있으리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없다."], ["There are times when it is almost impossible to detect by his accent that he is not a countryman of our own, and as for fluency, there are very few born Englishmen who can talk with as few stoppages and repetitions as the Count.", "억양만으로는 그가 우리와 같은 나라 사람이 아님을 알아채기가 거의 불가능한 때가 있고, 유창함으로 말하자면 백작만큼 말이 끊기거나 되풀이되는 일 없이 이야기할 수 있는 잉글랜드 태생은 아주 드물다."], ["He may construct his sentences more or less in the foreign way, but I have never yet heard him use a wrong expression, or hesitate for a moment in his choice of a word.", "문장 구성이야 다소간 외국식일지 몰라도, 나는 그가 잘못된 표현을 쓰거나 낱말을 고르느라 한순간이라도 머뭇거리는 것을 아직 들어 본 적이 없다."]], [["All the smallest characteristics of this strange man have something strikingly original and perplexingly contradictory in them.", "이 기이한 남자의 아주 사소한 특징들에도 하나같이 놀랍도록 독창적이고 어리둥절할 만큼 모순된 데가 있다."], ["Fat as he is and old as he is, his movements are astonishingly light and easy.", "그렇게 뚱뚱하고 그렇게 나이가 들었는데도 그의 몸놀림은 놀랍도록 가볍고 부드럽다."], ["He is as noiseless in a room as any of us women, and more than that, with all his look of unmistakable mental firmness and power, he is as nervously sensitive as the weakest of us.", "방 안에서는 우리 여자들 누구 못지않게 소리가 나지 않고, 게다가 누가 보아도 분명한 정신의 굳건함과 힘을 지녔으면서도 우리 가운데 가장 약한 사람만큼이나 신경이 예민하다."], ["He starts at chance noises as inveterately as Laura herself.", "뜻하지 않은 소리에 로라 못지않게 지독하리만치 잘 놀란다."], ["He winced and shuddered yesterday, when Sir Percival beat one of the spaniels, so that I felt ashamed of my own want of tenderness and sensibility by comparison with the Count.", "어제 퍼시벌 경이 스패니얼 한 마리를 때렸을 때 그는 움찔하며 몸서리를 쳤고, 나는 백작에 견주어 내가 다정함도 감수성도 없다는 것이 부끄러울 지경이었다."]], [["The relation of this last incident reminds me of one of his most curious peculiarities, which I have not yet mentioned—his extraordinary fondness for pet animals.", "이 마지막 사건 이야기를 하다 보니 아직 언급하지 않은 그의 가장 기이한 특징 하나가 떠오른다—애완동물에 대한 유별난 애정이다."]], [["Some of these he has left on the Continent, but he has brought with him to this house a cockatoo, two canary-birds, and a whole family of white mice.", "그중 일부는 대륙에 두고 왔지만, 그는 이 집에 코카투 한 마리와 카나리아 두 마리, 그리고 흰쥐 한 가족 전부를 데려왔다."], ["He attends to all the necessities of these strange favourites himself, and he has taught the creatures to be surprisingly fond of him and familiar with him.", "이 별난 총아들에게 필요한 것은 모두 손수 챙기고, 그 짐승들이 놀랄 만큼 자기를 따르고 자기와 스스럼없이 굴도록 길들여 놓았다."], ["The cockatoo, a most vicious and treacherous bird towards every one else, absolutely seems to love him.", "다른 사람에게는 더없이 사납고 음험한 새인 코카투가 그에게만은 정말로 애정을 품은 듯하다."], ["When he lets it out of its cage, it hops on to his knee, and claws its way up his great big body, and rubs its top-knot against his sallow double chin in the most caressing manner imaginable.", "그가 새장에서 꺼내 주면 새는 그의 무릎으로 폴짝 뛰어올라 그 거대한 몸을 발톱으로 기어오르고는, 상상할 수 있는 가장 어리광 어린 몸짓으로 볏을 그의 누르스름한 이중 턱에 비벼 댄다."], ["He has only to set the doors of the canaries’ cages open, and to call them, and the pretty little cleverly trained creatures perch fearlessly on his hand, mount his fat outstretched fingers one by one, when he tells them to “go upstairs,” and sing together as if they would burst their throats with delight when they get to the top finger.", "카나리아 새장 문을 열어 놓고 부르기만 하면, 예쁘고 영리하게 길들여진 그 작은 것들은 겁 없이 그의 손에 내려앉고, 그가 “위층으로 올라가라”고 하면 통통하게 펼친 손가락을 하나씩 하나씩 밟고 올라가, 맨 꼭대기 손가락에 이르면 목이 터져라 기뻐하며 함께 노래한다."], ["His white mice live in a little pagoda of gaily-painted wirework, designed and made by himself.", "그의 흰쥐들은 그가 직접 도안하고 만든, 알록달록 칠한 철사 세공 탑 속에서 산다."], ["They are almost as tame as the canaries, and they are perpetually let out like the canaries.", "쥐들도 카나리아만큼이나 순해서 카나리아처럼 늘 밖으로 풀려 나온다."], ["They crawl all over him, popping in and out of his waistcoat, and sitting in couples, white as snow, on his capacious shoulders.", "쥐들은 그의 온몸을 기어 다니며 조끼 속을 들락날락하고, 눈처럼 흰 몸으로 짝을 지어 그 널찍한 어깨 위에 앉는다."], ["He seems to be even fonder of his mice than of his other pets, smiles at them, and kisses them, and calls them by all sorts of endearing names.", "그는 다른 애완동물보다 쥐를 더 아끼는 듯 보이는데, 쥐들을 보며 미소 짓고 입을 맞추고 온갖 애칭으로 부른다."], ["If it be possible to suppose an Englishman with any taste for such childish interests and amusements as these, that Englishman would certainly feel rather ashamed of them, and would be anxious to apologise for them, in the company of grown-up people.", "잉글랜드 사람 가운데 이런 어린애 같은 취미와 놀이를 즐기는 이가 있다고 가정할 수 있다면, 그 사람은 분명 그것을 다소 부끄러워하며 어른들 앞에서는 서둘러 변명하려 들 것이다."], ["But the Count, apparently, sees nothing ridiculous in the amazing contrast between his colossal self and his frail little pets.", "그러나 백작은 자신의 거대한 몸집과 가녀린 작은 애완동물들 사이의 놀라운 대비에서 아무런 우스움도 못 느끼는 모양이다."], ["He would blandly kiss his white mice and twitter to his canary-birds amid an assembly of English fox-hunters, and would only pity them as barbarians when they were all laughing their loudest at him.", "그는 잉글랜드 여우 사냥꾼들이 모인 자리에서도 태연히 흰쥐에게 입 맞추고 카나리아에게 재잘거릴 것이며, 그들이 아무리 크게 웃어 대도 그저 야만인이라고 딱하게 여길 뿐일 것이다."]], [["It seems hardly credible while I am writing it down, but it is certainly true, that this same man, who has all the fondness of an old maid for his cockatoo, and all the small dexterities of an organ-boy in managing his white mice, can talk, when anything happens to rouse him, with a daring independence of thought, a knowledge of books in every language, and an experience of society in half the capitals of Europe, which would make him the prominent personage of any assembly in the civilised world.", "이렇게 적어 놓고 보면 도무지 믿기지 않지만, 코카투에게 노처녀 같은 애정을 쏟고 흰쥐를 다루는 데 손풍금 소년 같은 자잘한 재주를 부리는 바로 그 사람이, 무언가 그를 자극하는 일이 생기면 대담하고 독립적인 사고와 온갖 언어로 된 책에 대한 지식과 유럽 절반의 수도에서 겪은 사교계의 경험을 담아 이야기할 수 있다는 것 또한 분명한 사실이다. 그것이면 문명 세계의 어떤 모임에서도 그를 좌중의 주인공으로 만들기에 충분하다."], ["This trainer of canary-birds, this architect of a pagoda for white mice, is (as Sir Percival himself has told me) one of the first experimental chemists living, and has discovered, among other wonderful inventions, a means of petrifying the body after death, so as to preserve it, as hard as marble, to the end of time.", "이 카나리아 조련사, 흰쥐를 위한 탑의 건축가는 (퍼시벌 경이 직접 내게 말해 준 바로는) 당대 첫손에 꼽히는 실험 화학자 가운데 하나이며, 여러 놀라운 발명 가운데 죽은 뒤의 몸을 대리석처럼 단단하게 굳혀 영원히 보존하는 방법도 발견했다고 한다."], ["This fat, indolent, elderly man, whose nerves are so finely strung that he starts at chance noises, and winces when he sees a house-spaniel get a whipping, went into the stable-yard on the morning after his arrival, and put his hand on the head of a chained bloodhound—a beast so savage that the very groom who feeds him keeps out of his reach.", "뜻하지 않은 소리에 놀라고 집 스패니얼이 매 맞는 것을 보면 움찔할 만큼 신경이 곱게 조율된 이 뚱뚱하고 굼뜬 노신사는, 도착한 이튿날 아침 마구간 뜰로 들어가 사슬에 묶인 블러드하운드—먹이를 주는 마부조차 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있으려 할 만큼 사나운 짐승—의 머리에 손을 얹었다."], ["His wife and I were present, and I shall not forget the scene that followed, short as it was.", "그의 아내와 나도 그 자리에 있었는데, 짧기는 했지만 나는 그 뒤에 벌어진 광경을 잊지 못할 것이다."]], [["“Mind that dog, sir,”", "“그 개 조심하십쇼, 나리,”"], ["said the groom; “he flies at everybody!”", "마부가 말했다. “아무한테나 달려듭니다요!”"], ["“He does that, my friend,”", "“그건 말일세, 친구,”"], ["replied the Count quietly, “because everybody is afraid of him.", "백작이 조용히 대답했다. “모두가 저를 무서워하기 때문이지."], ["Let us see if he flies at me.”", "나한테도 달려드는지 어디 보세.”"], ["And he laid his plump, yellow-white fingers, on which the canary-birds had been perching ten minutes before, upon the formidable brute’s head, and looked him straight in the eyes.", "그러고는 십 분 전까지 카나리아들이 앉아 있던 통통하고 누르스름하게 흰 손가락을 그 무시무시한 짐승의 머리에 얹고, 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다."], ["“You big dogs are all cowards,”", "“너희 덩치 큰 개들은 죄다 겁쟁이야,”"], ["he said, addressing the animal contemptuously, with his face and the dog’s within an inch of each other.", "그는 자기 얼굴과 개의 얼굴을 한 치 거리에 두고 짐승에게 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“You would kill a poor cat, you infernal coward.", "“너는 가엾은 고양이나 죽이겠지, 이 지독한 겁쟁이야."], ["You would fly at a starving beggar, you infernal coward.", "굶주린 거지에게나 달려들겠지, 이 지독한 겁쟁이야."], ["Anything that you can surprise unawares—anything that is afraid of your big body, and your wicked white teeth, and your slobbering, bloodthirsty mouth, is the thing you like to fly at.", "방심한 틈에 덮칠 수 있는 것—네 큰 몸뚱이와 못된 흰 이빨과 침 흘리는 피에 굶주린 아가리를 무서워하는 것, 그런 것한테나 달려들기를 좋아하지. 너는 지금 당장 내 목을 조를 수도 있어, 이 비열하고 가련한 골목대장아."], ["You could throttle me at this moment, you mean, miserable bully, and you daren’t so much as look me in the face, because I’m not afraid of you.", "그런데도 감히 내 얼굴을 마주 보지도 못하는군. 내가 너를 무서워하지 않으니까."], ["Will you think better of it, and try your teeth in my fat neck?", "생각을 고쳐먹고 내 살진 목덜미에 이빨을 시험해 보겠느냐?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["not you!”", "그럴 리가!”"], ["He turned away, laughing at the astonishment of the men in the yard, and the dog crept back meekly to his kennel.", "그는 뜰에 있던 사람들의 놀란 얼굴을 보고 웃으며 돌아섰고, 개는 얌전히 제 집으로 기어 들어갔다."], ["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["my nice waistcoat!”", "내 고운 조끼!”"], ["he said pathetically.", "그가 애처롭게 말했다."], ["“I am sorry I came here.", "“여기 온 것이 후회스럽군."], ["Some of that brute’s slobber has got on my pretty clean waistcoat.”", "저 짐승의 침이 내 깨끗하고 예쁜 조끼에 묻었어.”"], ["Those words express another of his incomprehensible oddities.", "이 말은 그의 이해할 수 없는 별스러움 가운데 또 하나를 잘 보여 준다."], ["He is as fond of fine clothes as the veriest fool in existence, and has appeared in four magnificent waistcoats already—all of light garish colours, and all immensely large even for him—in the two days of his residence at Blackwater Park.", "그는 세상 그 어떤 얼빠진 멋쟁이 못지않게 좋은 옷을 좋아해서, 블랙워터 파크에 머문 이틀 동안 벌써 네 벌의 화려한 조끼를 차려입고 나타났다—하나같이 밝고 요란한 색이고, 하나같이 그의 몸집에 비추어 보아도 엄청나게 큰 것들이었다."]], [["His tact and cleverness in small things are quite as noticeable as the singular inconsistencies in his character, and the childish triviality of his ordinary tastes and pursuits.", "사소한 일에서 보이는 그의 재치와 영리함은 그 성격의 기이한 모순이나 평소 취향과 소일거리의 어린애 같은 하찮음 못지않게 눈에 띈다."]], [["I can see already that he means to live on excellent terms with all of us during the period of his sojourn in this place.", "그가 이곳에 머무는 동안 우리 모두와 더없이 좋은 사이로 지낼 작정이라는 것은 이미 알 수 있다."], ["He has evidently discovered that Laura secretly dislikes him (she confessed as much to me when I pressed her on the subject)—but he has also found out that she is extravagantly fond of flowers.", "로라가 속으로 자기를 싫어한다는 것을 그는 분명 알아차렸다(내가 캐묻자 로라도 그렇다고 털어놓았다)—그러나 로라가 꽃을 유난히 좋아한다는 것도 알아냈다."], ["Whenever she wants a nosegay he has got one to give her, gathered and arranged by himself, and greatly to my amusement, he is always cunningly provided with a duplicate, composed of exactly the same flowers, grouped in exactly the same way, to appease his icily jealous wife before she can so much as think herself aggrieved. His management of the Countess (in public) is a sight to see.", "로라가 꽃다발을 갖고 싶어 할 때면 언제나 그가 손수 꺾어 다듬은 꽃다발이 준비되어 있고, 게다가 내게는 아주 재미있게도, 그는 늘 똑같은 꽃을 똑같은 방식으로 묶은 것을 하나 더 교묘하게 마련해 두어, 얼음처럼 질투 많은 아내가 자기가 홀대받았다고 생각할 겨를조차 없게 만든다."], ["He bows to her, he habitually addresses her as “my angel,”", "사람들 앞에서 백작부인을 다루는 솜씨는 볼 만한 구경거리다."], ["he carries his canaries to pay her little visits on his fingers and to sing to her, he kisses her hand when she gives him his cigarettes; he presents her with sugar-plums in return, which he puts into her mouth playfully, from a box in his pocket.", "그는 아내에게 절을 하고, 늘 “나의 천사”라고 부르고, 카나리아를 손가락에 앉혀 아내에게 문안을 드리고 노래를 불러 주게 하며, 아내가 담배를 건네면 그 손에 입을 맞춘다. 그 답례로 사탕을 주는데, 주머니에서 상자를 꺼내 장난스럽게 아내의 입에 넣어 준다."], ["The rod of iron with which he rules her never appears in company—it is a private rod, and is always kept upstairs.", "그가 아내를 다스리는 쇠막대는 사람들 앞에 결코 나타나지 않는다—그것은 은밀한 막대이고, 언제나 위층에 간직되어 있다."]], [["His method of recommending himself to me is entirely different.", "나를 자기편으로 만드는 방법은 전혀 다르다."], ["He flatters my vanity by talking to me as seriously and sensibly as if I was a man.", "그는 마치 내가 남자이기라도 한 듯 진지하고 사리에 맞게 이야기함으로써 내 허영심을 부추긴다."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["I can find him out when I am away from him—I know he flatters my vanity, when I think of him up here in my own room—and yet, when I go downstairs, and get into his company again, he will blind me again, and I shall be flattered again, just as if I had never found him out at all!", "그에게서 떨어져 있으면 나는 그 속을 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있다—여기 내 방에 올라와 그를 떠올릴 때면 그가 내 허영심을 부추긴다는 것을 안다—그런데도 아래층으로 내려가 다시 그와 자리를 함께하면, 그는 또다시 내 눈을 가릴 것이고 나는 또다시 우쭐해질 것이다. 마치 그 속을 꿰뚫어 본 적이 한 번도 없었던 것처럼!"], ["He can manage me as he manages his wife and Laura, as he managed the bloodhound in the stable-yard, as he manages Sir Percival himself, every hour in the day.", "그는 자기 아내를 다루듯, 로라를 다루듯, 마구간 뜰의 블러드하운드를 다루듯 나를 다룰 수 있고, 퍼시벌 경 그 사람 또한 하루 종일 시시각각 그렇게 다룬다."], ["“My good Percival!", "“나의 좋은 퍼시벌!"], ["how I like your rough English humour!”—“My good Percival! how I enjoy your solid English sense!”", "자네의 그 투박한 잉글랜드식 유머가 어찌나 마음에 드는지!”—“나의 좋은 퍼시벌!"], ["He puts the rudest remarks Sir Percival can make on his effeminate tastes and amusements quietly away from him in that manner—always calling the baronet by his Christian name, smiling at him with the calmest superiority, patting him on the shoulder, and bearing with him benignantly, as a good-humoured father bears with a wayward son.", "자네의 그 단단한 잉글랜드식 상식이 어찌나 즐거운지!” 퍼시벌 경이 그의 여자 같은 취향과 놀이를 두고 아무리 무례한 말을 퍼부어도 그는 그런 식으로 조용히 흘려버린다—늘 준남작을 세례명으로 부르고, 더없이 침착한 우월감으로 미소 지으며, 어깨를 두드려 주고, 사람 좋은 아버지가 제멋대로인 아들을 참아 주듯 너그럽게 참아 준다."]], [["The interest which I really cannot help feeling in this strangely original man has led me to question Sir Percival about his past life.", "이 기이하도록 독창적인 남자에게 도무지 흥미를 느끼지 않을 수 없어서, 나는 퍼시벌 경에게 그의 지난 삶에 대해 묻게 되었다."]], [["Sir Percival either knows little, or will tell me little, about it.", "퍼시벌 경도 그 일에 대해서는 아는 것이 적거나, 아니면 내게 말해줄 마음이 적은 모양이다."], ["He and the Count first met many years ago, at Rome, under the dangerous circumstances to which I have alluded elsewhere.", "그와 백작이 처음 만난 것은 여러 해 전 로마에서였고, 그때의 위험한 사정에 관해서는 내가 다른 데서 이미 언급한 바 있다."], ["Since that time they have been perpetually together in London, in Paris, and in Vienna—but never in Italy again; the Count having, oddly enough, not crossed the frontiers of his native country for years past.", "그 뒤로 두 사람은 런던에서, 파리에서, 빈에서 늘 붙어 다녔다. 그러나 이탈리아에서 다시 만난 적은 한 번도 없다."], ["Perhaps he has been made the victim of some political persecution?", "백작은 어찌 된 영문인지 지난 몇 해 동안 제 고국의 국경을 넘은 일이 없기 때문이다."], ["At all events, he seems to be patriotically anxious not to lose sight of any of his own countrymen who may happen to be in England.", "혹시 무슨 정치적 박해의 희생양이 된 것은 아닐까? 아무튼 그는 영국에 머물고 있을지 모를 제 나라 사람이라면 누구든 놓치지 않으려고 애국심에 불타 신경을 곤두세우는 듯하다."], ["On the evening of his arrival he asked how far we were from the nearest town, and whether we knew of any Italian gentlemen who might happen to be settled there.", "도착한 날 저녁에도 그는 여기서 가장 가까운 읍내가 얼마나 떨어져 있는지, 그리고 혹시 그곳에 자리 잡고 사는 이탈리아 신사를 우리가 아는지 물었다. 그가 대륙의 누군가와 편지를 주고받고 있는 것만은 분명하다."], ["He is certainly in correspondence with people on the Continent, for his letters have all sorts of odd stamps on them, and I saw one for him this morning, waiting in his place at the breakfast-table, with a huge, official-looking seal on it.", "그에게 오는 편지에는 온갖 낯선 우표가 붙어 있으니 말이다. 오늘 아침에도 아침 식탁의 그의 자리에 한 통이 놓여 기다리고 있는 것을 보았는데, 커다란 관인처럼 보이는 봉인이 찍혀 있었다."], ["Perhaps he is in correspondence with his government?", "혹시 제 나라 정부와 편지를 주고받는 것일까?"], ["And yet, that is hardly to be reconciled either with my other idea that he may be a political exile.", "하지만 그렇다면 그가 정치적 망명자일지 모른다는 나의 또 다른 짐작과는 좀처럼 아귀가 맞지 않는다."]], [["How much I seem to have written about Count Fosco! And what does it all amount to?—as poor, dear Mr.", "포스코 백작에 대해 어쩌면 이렇게나 많이 써놓았는지!"], ["Gilmore would ask, in his impenetrable business-like way I can only repeat that I do assuredly feel, even on this short acquaintance, a strange, half-willing, half-unwilling liking for the Count.", "그런데 그게 다 무엇이 되었다는 말인가?——가엾고 다정한 길모어 씨라면 그 속을 알 수 없는 사무적인 말투로 그렇게 물었으리라. 나로서는 다만 이렇게 되풀이할 수밖에 없다."], ["He seems to have established over me the same sort of ascendency which he has evidently gained over Sir Percival.", "이토록 짧은 사귐인데도 나는 분명히 백작에게 야릇한, 반은 내키고 반은 내키지 않는 호감을 느낀다고."], ["Free, and even rude, as he may occasionally be in his manner towards his fat friend, Sir Percival is nevertheless afraid, as I can plainly see, of giving any serious offence to the Count.", "그는 퍼시벌 경에게 확실히 얻어낸 것과 똑같은 종류의 우위를 내게도 세워놓은 듯하다. 그 뚱뚱한 친구를 대하는 태도가 이따금 거리낌 없고 심지어 무례하기까지 한데도, 퍼시벌 경은 그럼에도 백작의 심기를 크게 거스르지 않으려고 조심하는 것이 내 눈에는 훤히 보인다."], ["I wonder whether I am afraid too?", "나 또한 그를 두려워하고 있는 것일까?"], ["I certainly never saw a man, in all my experience, whom I should be so sorry to have for an enemy.", "지금껏 살아오면서 적으로 두기가 이토록 꺼려지는 사내를 본 적이 없는 것만은 분명하다."], ["Is this because I like him, or because I am afraid of him?", "그것이 내가 그를 좋아하기 때문일까, 아니면 그를 무서워하기 때문일까?"], ["Chi sa?—as Count Fosco might say in his own language.", "키 사?——포스코 백작이라면 제 나라 말로 그렇게 말했을 것이다."], ["Who knows?", "누가 알겠는가?"]], [["June 16th.—Something to chronicle to-day besides my own ideas and impressions.", "6월 16일.——오늘은 내 생각과 인상 말고도 적어둘 일이 생겼다."], ["A visitor has arrived—quite unknown to Laura and to me, and apparently quite unexpected by Sir Percival.", "손님이 한 사람 찾아왔다. 로라와 나로서는 전혀 모르는 사람이고, 퍼시벌 경도 전혀 예상하지 못했던 모양이다."]], [["We were all at lunch, in the room with the new French windows that open into the verandah, and the Count (who devours pastry as I have never yet seen it devoured by any human beings but girls at boarding-schools) had just amused us by asking gravely for his fourth tart—when the servant entered to announce the visitor.", "우리는 모두 점심 식사 중이었다. 베란다로 통하는 새 프랑스식 창문이 달린 그 방이었다. 백작은(기숙학교 여학생들 말고는 어떤 인간이 그렇게 먹어치우는 것을 내 눈으로 본 적이 없을 만큼 과자를 게걸스레 먹는다) 네 번째 타르트를 자못 진지한 얼굴로 청해서 우리를 웃겨놓은 참이었다. 바로 그때 하인이 들어와 손님이 왔음을 알렸다."]], [["“Mr.", "“메리먼 씨가 방금 도착하셨습니다, 퍼시벌 경."], ["Merriman has just come, Sir Percival, and wishes to see you immediately.”", "지금 바로 뵙기를 청하십니다.”"]], [["Sir Percival started, and looked at the man with an expression of angry alarm.", "퍼시벌 경은 흠칫 놀라더니 성난 듯 당황한 표정으로 하인을 바라보았다."]], [["“Mr. Merriman!”", "“메리먼 씨라고!”"], ["he repeated, as if he thought his own ears must have deceived him.", "그는 제 귀를 의심하는 사람처럼 되뇌었다."]], [["“Yes, Sir Percival—Mr.", "“예, 퍼시벌 경."], ["Merriman, from London.”", "런던에서 오신 메리먼 씨입니다.”"]], [["“Where is he?”", "“어디 있나?”"]], [["“In the library, Sir Percival.”", "“서재에 계십니다, 퍼시벌 경.”"]], [["He left the table the instant the last answer was given, and hurried out of the room without saying a word to any of us.", "마지막 대답이 떨어지자마자 그는 식탁에서 일어나, 우리 중 누구에게도 한마디 없이 서둘러 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Who is Mr. Merriman?”", "“메리먼 씨가 누구예요?”"], ["asked Laura, appealing to me.", "로라가 나를 쳐다보며 물었다."]], [["“I have not the least idea,”", "“나도 도무지 모르겠구나.”"], ["was all I could say in reply.", "내가 대답으로 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["The Count had finished his fourth tart, and had gone to a side-table to look after his vicious cockatoo.", "백작은 네 번째 타르트를 다 먹어치우고 곁상으로 가서 제 심술궂은 앵무새를 돌보고 있었다."], ["He turned round to us with the bird perched on his shoulder.", "그는 새를 어깨에 앉힌 채 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Mr. Merriman is Sir Percival’s solicitor,”", "“메리먼 씨는 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사입니다.”"], ["he said quietly.", "그가 조용히 말했다."]], [["Sir Percival’s solicitor.", "퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사."], ["It was a perfectly straightforward answer to Laura’s question, and yet, under the circumstances, it was not satisfactory.", "로라의 물음에 대한 더없이 명료한 대답이었지만, 사정이 사정인 만큼 만족스럽지는 않았다."], ["If Mr. Merriman had been specially sent for by his client, there would have been nothing very wonderful in his leaving town to obey the summons.", "만일 메리먼 씨가 의뢰인의 부름을 특별히 받고 온 것이라면, 그 부름에 따르려고 도성을 떠나온 데에 놀라울 것은 하나도 없다."], ["But when a lawyer travels from London to Hampshire without being sent for, and when his arrival at a gentleman’s house seriously startles the gentleman himself, it may be safely taken for granted that the legal visitor is the bearer of some very important and very unexpected news—news which may be either very good or very bad, but which cannot, in either case, be of the common everyday kind.", "그러나 변호사가 부름도 받지 않고 런던에서 햄프셔까지 내려왔다면, 그리고 그가 어느 신사의 집에 나타난 일이 그 신사 자신을 심각하게 놀라게 했다면, 그 법률가 손님이 매우 중대하고 매우 뜻밖의 소식을 지니고 왔다고 보아도 무방할 것이다. 그것이 아주 좋은 소식일지 아주 나쁜 소식일지는 알 수 없으나, 어느 쪽이든 흔해빠진 일상의 소식일 리는 없는 것이다."]], [["Laura and I sat silent at the table for a quarter of an hour or more, wondering uneasily what had happened, and waiting for the chance of Sir Percival’s speedy return.", "로라와 나는 십오 분 남짓 식탁에 말없이 앉아, 무슨 일이 벌어졌을까 불안하게 궁금해하며 퍼시벌 경이 곧 돌아오기를 기다렸다."], ["There were no signs of his return, and we rose to leave the room.", "그가 돌아올 낌새는 없었고, 우리는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나서려 했다."]], [["The Count, attentive as usual, advanced from the corner in which he had been feeding his cockatoo, with the bird still perched on his shoulder, and opened the door for us.", "백작은 여느 때처럼 자상하게, 앵무새에게 먹이를 주던 구석에서 새를 여전히 어깨에 앉힌 채 걸어 나와 우리를 위해 문을 열어주었다."], ["Laura and Madame Fosco went out first.", "로라와 포스코 백작부인이 먼저 나갔다."], ["Just as I was on the point of following them he made a sign with his hand, and spoke to me, before I passed him, in the oddest manner.", "내가 막 그들을 따라 나서려는데 그가 손짓을 하더니, 내가 그의 앞을 지나기 전에 참으로 야릇한 태도로 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇습니다.” 그는 마치 내가 그 자리에서 낱낱이 말로 털어놓기라도 한 것처럼, 그 순간 내 마음속에 떠올랐으나 입 밖에 내지 않은 생각에 조용히 대답했다."], ["he said, quietly answering the unexpressed idea at that moment in my mind, as if I had plainly confided it to him in so many words—“yes, Miss Halcombe, something HAS happened.”", "“그렇습니다, 홀콤 양. 무슨 일이 벌어진 것이 맞습니다.”"]], [["I was on the point of answering, “I never said so,”", "나는 “저는 그런 말 한 적 없는데요” 하고 대꾸하려던 참이었다."], ["but the vicious cockatoo ruffled his clipped wings and gave a screech that set all my nerves on edge in an instant, and made me only too glad to get out of the room.", "그러나 심술궂은 앵무새가 잘린 날개를 곤두세우며 째지는 소리로 울어대는 바람에 순식간에 온 신경이 곤두섰고, 나는 그 방을 벗어나게 된 것이 그저 고맙기만 했다."]], [["I joined Laura at the foot of the stairs.", "나는 계단 아래에서 로라와 합류했다."], ["The thought in her mind was the same as the thought in mine, which Count Fosco had surprised, and when she spoke her words were almost the echo of his.", "그 애의 마음속에 있던 생각은 내 마음속에 있던 생각, 포스코 백작이 불시에 알아맞힌 바로 그 생각과 똑같았다. 그리고 그 애가 입을 열었을 때 그 말은 거의 백작의 말을 그대로 되울린 것이었다."], ["She, too, said to me secretly that she was afraid something had happened.", "그 애 역시 무슨 일이 벌어진 것 같아 두렵다고 내게 가만히 속삭였다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["June 16th.—I have a few lines more to add to this day’s entry before I go to bed to-night.", "6월 16일.—오늘 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 이 날의 기록에 몇 줄 더 덧붙여 두어야겠다."]], [["About two hours after Sir Percival rose from the luncheon-table to receive his solicitor, Mr. Merriman, in the library, I left my room alone to take a walk in the plantations.", "퍼시벌 경이 사무 변호사 메리먼 씨를 서재에서 맞으려고 점심 식탁에서 일어난 지 두 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 나는 농원을 거닐려고 혼자 방을 나섰다."], ["Just as I was at the end of the landing the library door opened and the two gentlemen came out.", "마침 층계참 끝에 이르렀을 때 서재 문이 열리고 두 신사가 나왔다."], ["Thinking it best not to disturb them by appearing on the stairs, I resolved to defer going down till they had crossed the hall.", "계단에 모습을 드러내어 그들을 방해하지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 두 사람이 현관을 지나갈 때까지 내려가는 것을 미루기로 했다."], ["Although they spoke to each other in guarded tones, their words were pronounced with sufficient distinctness of utterance to reach my ears.", "그들은 서로 조심스러운 목소리로 이야기했지만, 그 말은 내 귀에 닿을 만큼 충분히 또렷하게 발음되었다."]], [["“Make your mind easy, Sir Percival,”", "“마음 놓으십시오, 퍼시벌 경.”"], ["I heard the lawyer say; “it all rests with Lady Glyde.”", "변호사가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. “모든 것은 글라이드 부인께 달려 있습니다.”"]], [["I had turned to go back to my own room for a minute or two, but the sound of Laura’s name on the lips of a stranger stopped me instantly.", "나는 잠깐 내 방으로 돌아가려고 몸을 돌린 참이었으나, 낯선 사람의 입에서 로라의 이름이 나오는 소리에 그 자리에 딱 멈춰 섰다."], ["I daresay it was very wrong and very discreditable to listen, but where is the woman, in the whole range of our sex, who can regulate her actions by the abstract principles of honour, when those principles point one way, and when her affections, and the interests which grow out of them, point the other?", "엿듣는 것은 매우 나쁘고 매우 떳떳지 못한 짓이었으리라. 그러나 우리 여자들 전체를 통틀어, 명예의 추상적 원칙이 한쪽을 가리키고 애정과 거기서 자라난 이해관계가 다른 쪽을 가리킬 때, 그 원칙에 따라 자기 행동을 다스릴 수 있는 여자가 대체 어디 있단 말인가?"]], [["I listened—and under similar circumstances I would listen again—yes!", "나는 엿들었다—그리고 같은 상황이라면 다시 엿들을 것이다—그렇다!"], ["with my ear at the keyhole, if I could not possibly manage it in any other way.", "달리 도리가 없다면 열쇠 구멍에 귀를 대고서라도."]], [["“You quite understand, Sir Percival,”", "“잘 알아들으셨겠지요, 퍼시벌 경.”"], ["the lawyer went on.", "변호사가 말을 이었다."], ["“Lady Glyde is to sign her name in the presence of a witness—or of two witnesses, if you wish to be particularly careful—and is then to put her finger on the seal and say, ‘I deliver this as my act and deed.’ If that is done in a week’s time the arrangement will be perfectly successful, and the anxiety will be all over.", "“글라이드 부인께서 증인 한 사람—특별히 신중을 기하고 싶으시다면 증인 두 사람—앞에서 서명을 하시고, 그런 다음 봉인에 손가락을 얹고 ‘나는 이것을 나의 행위이자 증서로서 교부합니다’라고 말씀하시면 됩니다. 그것이 일주일 안에 이루어진다면 이 조치는 완벽하게 성사될 것이고 걱정거리도 다 끝날 겁니다."], ["If not——”", "만일 그렇지 않다면——”"]], [["“What do you mean by ‘if not’?”", "“‘그렇지 않다면’이라니 무슨 소리요?”"], ["asked Sir Percival angrily.", "퍼시벌 경이 성난 목소리로 물었다."], ["“If the thing must be done it SHALL be done.", "“해야 할 일이라면 반드시 되고야 말 것이오."], ["I promise you that, Merriman.”", "내가 약속하리다, 메리먼.”"]], [["“Just so, Sir Percival—just so; but there are two alternatives in all transactions, and we lawyers like to look both of them in the face boldly.", "“옳으신 말씀입니다, 퍼시벌 경—지당하십니다. 다만 모든 거래에는 두 갈래 길이 있고, 저희 법률가들은 그 둘 다를 대담하게 정면으로 바라보기를 좋아하지요."], ["If through any extraordinary circumstance the arrangement should not be made, I think I may be able to get the parties to accept bills at three months.", "만에 하나 무슨 뜻밖의 사정으로 이 조치가 이루어지지 않는다면, 저로서는 상대편에게 삼 개월짜리 어음을 받아들이게 할 수 있을 듯합니다."], ["But how the money is to be raised when the bills fall due——”", "그러나 어음 만기가 돌아올 때 그 돈을 어떻게 마련하실지——”"]], [["“Damn the bills!", "“어음 따위 집어치우시오!"], ["The money is only to be got in one way, and in that way, I tell you again, it SHALL be got.", "돈은 오직 한 가지 방법으로만 구할 수 있고, 다시 말하지만 그 방법으로 반드시 구하고야 말 것이오."], ["Take a glass of wine, Merriman, before you go.”", "가시기 전에 포도주나 한 잔 하시오, 메리먼.”"]], [["“Much obliged, Sir Percival; I have not a moment to lose if I am to catch the up-train.", "“감사합니다만, 퍼시벌 경. 상행 열차를 타려면 한시도 지체할 수 없습니다."], ["You will let me know as soon as the arrangement is complete?", "조치가 끝나는 대로 알려 주시겠지요?"], ["and you will not forget the caution I recommended——”", "그리고 제가 권해 드린 조심할 점도 잊지 마시고——”"]], [["“Of course I won’t.", "“물론 잊지 않겠소."], ["There’s the dog-cart at the door for you.", "문 앞에 이륜마차를 대 놓았소."], ["My groom will get you to the station in no time.", "내 마부가 눈 깜짝할 새에 역까지 모셔다 줄 거요."], ["Benjamin, drive like mad!", "벤저민, 미친 듯이 몰아라!"], ["Jump in. If Mr.", "어서 타시오."], ["Merriman misses the train you lose your place.", "메리먼 씨가 기차를 놓치면 네 자리는 없는 줄 알아라."], ["Hold fast, Merriman, and if you are upset trust to the devil to save his own.”", "꽉 붙잡으시오, 메리먼. 혹시 뒤집히거든 악마가 제 편은 구해 주려니 믿으시고.”"], ["With that parting benediction the baronet turned about and walked back to the library.", "이런 작별 인사를 던지고 준남작은 몸을 돌려 서재로 돌아갔다."]], [["I had not heard much, but the little that had reached my ears was enough to make me feel uneasy. The “something”", "많이 듣지는 못했지만, 내 귀에 닿은 그 얼마 안 되는 말만으로도 나는 불안해지기에 충분했다."], ["that “had happened” was but too plainly a serious money embarrassment, and Sir Percival’s relief from it depended upon Laura.", "“무슨 일이 있었다”는 그 “무슨 일”은 너무나도 분명하게 심각한 금전 곤경이었고, 퍼시벌 경이 거기서 벗어나는 일은 로라에게 달려 있었다."], ["The prospect of seeing her involved in her husband’s secret difficulties filled me with dismay, exaggerated, no doubt, by my ignorance of business and my settled distrust of Sir Percival.", "로라가 남편의 은밀한 곤경에 휘말리는 광경을 떠올리자 나는 낙담으로 가득 찼다. 사업에 무지하고 퍼시벌 경을 뿌리 깊게 불신하는 탓에 그 낙담이 부풀려졌던 것도 틀림없으리라."], ["Instead of going out, as I proposed, I went back immediately to Laura’s room to tell her what I had heard.", "나는 나가려던 것을 그만두고 곧장 로라의 방으로 돌아가 들은 것을 이야기했다."]], [["She received my bad news so composedly as to surprise me.", "로라는 내 나쁜 소식을 어찌나 침착하게 받아들이는지 나를 놀라게 했다."], ["She evidently knows more of her husband’s character and her husband’s embarrassments than I have suspected up to this time.", "로라는 지금껏 내가 짐작해 온 것보다 남편의 성격과 남편의 곤경에 대해 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있는 것이 분명하다."]], [["“I feared as much,”", "“그럴까 봐 걱정했어.”"], ["she said, “when I heard of that strange gentleman who called, and declined to leave his name.”", "로라가 말했다. “이름 밝히기를 마다한 그 낯선 신사가 찾아왔다는 말을 들었을 때부터.”"]], [["“Who do you think the gentleman was, then?”", "“그럼 그 신사가 누구였다고 생각하니?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Some person who has heavy claims on Sir Percival,”", "“퍼시벌 경에게 무거운 채권을 가진 사람이겠지.” 로라가 대답했다."], ["she answered, “and who has been the cause of Mr. Merriman’s visit here to-day.”", "“그리고 오늘 메리먼 씨가 여기 온 것도 그 사람 때문일 거야.”"]], [["“Do you know anything about those claims?”", "“그 채권에 대해 뭔가 아는 게 있어?”"]], [["“No, I know no particulars.”", "“아니, 자세한 건 몰라.”"]], [["“You will sign nothing, Laura, without first looking at it?”", "“로라, 먼저 읽어 보지 않고는 아무것도 서명하지 않을 거지?”"]], [["“Certainly not, Marian.", "“물론이지, 매리언."], ["Whatever I can harmlessly and honestly do to help him I will do—for the sake of making your life and mine, love, as easy and as happy as possible.", "해가 없고 정직하게 그이를 도울 수 있는 일이라면 뭐든 할 거야—언니와 내 삶을 되도록 편하고 행복하게 만들기 위해서."], ["But I will do nothing ignorantly, which we might, one day, have reason to feel ashamed of.", "하지만 영문도 모르는 채로는 아무것도 하지 않을 거야. 훗날 언젠가 부끄러워할 이유가 생길지도 모르니까."], ["Let us say no more about it now.", "이제 그 얘기는 그만하자."], ["You have got your hat on—suppose we go and dream away the afternoon in the grounds?”", "언니는 벌써 모자를 쓰고 있네—우리 정원에 나가서 오후를 꿈결처럼 흘려보내면 어떨까?”"]], [["On leaving the house we directed our steps to the nearest shade.", "집을 나선 우리는 가장 가까운 그늘 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["As we passed an open space among the trees in front of the house, there was Count Fosco, slowly walking backwards and forwards on the grass, sunning himself in the full blaze of the hot June afternoon.", "집 앞 나무들 사이의 트인 공터를 지날 때, 거기 포스코 백작이 있었다. 6월 오후의 뜨거운 볕을 온몸으로 쬐며 잔디 위를 천천히 오락가락하고 있었다."], ["He had a broad straw hat on, with a violet-coloured ribbon round it.", "그는 보랏빛 리본을 두른 챙 넓은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["A blue blouse, with profuse white fancy-work over the bosom, covered his prodigious body, and was girt about the place where his waist might once have been with a broad scarlet leather belt.", "앞가슴에 흰 장식 자수가 잔뜩 놓인 파란 블라우스가 그 엄청난 몸을 덮고 있었고, 한때 허리였을 자리쯤에는 폭 넓은 주홍색 가죽 띠가 둘려 있었다."], ["Nankeen trousers, displaying more white fancy-work over the ankles, and purple morocco slippers, adorned his lower extremities.", "발목께에 또 흰 장식 자수를 드러낸 난징목면 바지와 자줏빛 모로코가죽 슬리퍼가 그의 아랫도리를 꾸미고 있었다."], ["He was singing Figaro’s famous song in the Barber of Seville, with that crisply fluent vocalisation which is never heard from any other than an Italian throat, accompanying himself on the concertina, which he played with ecstatic throwings-up of his arms, and graceful twistings and turnings of his head, like a fat St.", "그는 「세비야의 이발사」에 나오는 피가로의 그 유명한 노래를 부르고 있었는데, 이탈리아 사람의 목이 아니고서는 결코 들을 수 없는 그 또랑또랑하고 유창한 발성이었다. 그는 콘체르티나로 반주를 하면서 황홀한 듯 두 팔을 치켜올리고 우아하게 고개를 이리저리 돌리고 비틀었으니, 남장을 하고 가장무도회에 나온 뚱뚱한 성 체칠리아 같았다."], ["Cecilia masquerading in male attire.", "“피가로 콰!"], ["“Figaro qua!", "피가로 라!"], ["Figaro la!", "피가로 수!"], ["Figaro su! Figaro giu!”", "피가로 주!”"], ["sang the Count, jauntily tossing up the concertina at arm’s length, and bowing to us, on one side of the instrument, with the airy grace and elegance of Figaro himself at twenty years of age.", "백작은 이렇게 노래하며 팔을 쭉 뻗어 콘체르티나를 경쾌하게 튕겨 올리고는, 악기 한쪽 옆으로 몸을 굽혀 우리에게 인사했다. 스무 살 적 피가로 본인 같은 경쾌한 기품과 우아함이었다."]], [["“Take my word for it, Laura, that man knows something of Sir Percival’s embarrassments,”", "“내 말을 믿어, 로라. 저 사람은 퍼시벌 경의 곤경에 대해 뭔가 알고 있어.”"], ["I said, as we returned the Count’s salutation from a safe distance.", "우리가 안전한 거리에서 백작의 인사에 답례하며 내가 말했다."]], [["“What makes you think that?”", "“왜 그렇게 생각해?”"], ["she asked.", "로라가 물었다."]], [["“How should he have known, otherwise, that Mr. Merriman was Sir Percival’s solicitor?”", "“그렇지 않고서야 메리먼 씨가 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사라는 걸 어떻게 알았겠니?”"], ["I rejoined.", "내가 대꾸했다."], ["“Besides, when I followed you out of the luncheon-room, he told me, without a single word of inquiry on my part, that something had happened.", "“게다가 내가 점심 식당에서 너를 따라 나왔을 때, 내가 한마디 묻지도 않았는데 무슨 일이 생겼다고 나한테 말해 주더라. 틀림없어."], ["Depend upon it, he knows more than we do.”", "저 사람은 우리보다 많이 알고 있어.”"]], [["“Don’t ask him any questions if he does.", "“알고 있더라도 아무것도 묻지 마."], ["Don’t take him into our confidence!”", "우리 속내를 저 사람한테 털어놓지 마!”"]], [["“You seem to dislike him, Laura, in a very determined manner.", "“로라, 너 저 사람을 아주 단단히 싫어하는 것 같구나."], ["What has he said or done to justify you?”", "그이가 무슨 말이나 행동을 했기에 그러니?”"]], [["“Nothing, Marian.", "“아무것도 없어, 매리언."], ["On the contrary, he was all kindness and attention on our journey home, and he several times checked Sir Percival’s outbreaks of temper, in the most considerate manner towards me.", "오히려 돌아오는 여행 내내 더없이 친절하고 세심했고, 나를 배려해서 퍼시벌의 성마른 폭발을 여러 번 막아 주기까지 했어."], ["Perhaps I dislike him because he has so much more power over my husband than I have.", "어쩌면 그 사람이 내 남편에게 나보다 훨씬 큰 힘을 지녔기 때문에 싫은 건지도 몰라."], ["Perhaps it hurts my pride to be under any obligations to his interference.", "어쩌면 그 사람이 끼어들어 준 덕을 봐야 한다는 게 내 자존심을 상하게 하는지도 모르고."], ["All I know is, that I DO dislike him.”", "내가 아는 건 오직 하나, 나는 그 사람이 정말 싫다는 거야.”"]], [["The rest of the day and evening passed quietly enough.", "그날의 남은 시간과 저녁은 제법 조용히 지나갔다."], ["The Count and I played at chess.", "백작과 나는 체스를 두었다."], ["For the first two games he politely allowed me to conquer him, and then, when he saw that I had found him out, begged my pardon, and at the third game checkmated me in ten minutes.", "처음 두 판은 그가 정중하게 나에게 이기도록 내주었고, 내가 그 속을 알아차린 것을 눈치채자 용서를 구하더니, 세 번째 판에서는 십 분 만에 나를 외통수로 몰았다."], ["Sir Percival never once referred, all through the evening, to the lawyer’s visit.", "퍼시벌 경은 저녁 내내 변호사의 방문에 대해 단 한 번도 언급하지 않았다."], ["But either that event, or something else, had produced a singular alteration for the better in him.", "그러나 그 일 때문인지 다른 무엇 때문인지, 그에게 유별나게 좋은 쪽으로의 변화가 일어나 있었다."], ["He was as polite and agreeable to all of us, as he used to be in the days of his probation at Limmeridge, and he was so amazingly attentive and kind to his wife, that even icy Madame Fosco was roused into looking at him with a grave surprise.", "그는 리머리지에서 시험 기간을 보내던 시절처럼 우리 모두에게 정중하고 상냥했으며, 아내에게 어찌나 놀랍도록 다정하고 친절하던지, 얼음장 같은 포스코 부인마저 짐짓 놀란 얼굴로 그를 바라볼 정도였다."], ["What does this mean?", "이게 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["I think I can guess—I am afraid Laura can guess—and I am sure Count Fosco knows.", "짐작이 갈 것도 같다—로라도 짐작하고 있을까 두렵다—그리고 포스코 백작이 알고 있다는 것만은 확실하다."], ["I caught Sir Percival looking at him for approval more than once in the course of the evening.", "나는 저녁나절에 퍼시벌 경이 백작에게서 승낙을 구하듯 그를 쳐다보는 것을 한 번 이상 목격했다."]], [["June 17th.—A day of events.", "6월 17일.—사건이 많았던 하루."], ["I most fervently hope I may not have to add, a day of disasters as well.", "여기에 재난의 하루이기도 했다는 말까지 덧붙이지 않아도 되기를 간절히 바란다."]], [["Sir Percival was as silent at breakfast as he had been the evening before, on the subject of the mysterious “arrangement” (as the lawyer called it) which is hanging over our heads.", "퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사 자리에서도 어제저녁처럼, 우리 머리 위에 드리워진 그 알 수 없는 “조치”(변호사가 그렇게 불렀다)에 대해 아무 말이 없었다."], ["An hour afterwards, however, he suddenly entered the morning-room, where his wife and I were waiting, with our hats on, for Madame Fosco to join us, and inquired for the Count.", "그러나 한 시간 뒤에 그는 불쑥 아침 거실로 들어왔다. 그의 아내와 나는 모자를 쓴 채 포스코 부인이 합류하기를 기다리고 있던 참이었는데, 그는 백작을 찾았다."]], [["“We expect to see him here directly,”", "“곧 여기로 오실 거예요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "“실은 말이오.”"], ["Sir Percival went on, walking nervously about the room, “I want Fosco and his wife in the library, for a mere business formality, and I want you there, Laura, for a minute too.”", "퍼시벌 경이 초조하게 방 안을 오가며 말을 이었다. “그저 사무적인 형식을 갖출 일이 있어서 포스코와 그 부인이 서재에 있어 주었으면 하오."], ["He stopped, and appeared to notice, for the first time, that we were in our walking costume.", "그리고 로라, 당신도 잠깐 거기 있어 주었으면 좋겠소.” 그는 말을 멈추더니 그제야 처음으로 우리가 외출 차림인 것을 알아차린 듯했다."], ["“Have you just come in?”", "“방금 들어온 거요.”"], ["he asked, “or were you just going out?”", "그가 물었다. “아니면 이제 막 나가려던 참이오?”"]], [["“We were all thinking of going to the lake this morning,”", "“오늘 아침엔 다 함께 호수에 갈까 생각하던 참이었어요.”"], ["said Laura.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“But if you have any other arrangement to propose——”", "“하지만 다른 볼일을 말씀하시려는 거라면——”"]], [["“No, no,”", "“아니, 아니오.”"], ["he answered hastily.", "그가 서둘러 대답했다."], ["“My arrangement can wait.", "“내 볼일은 기다려도 되오."], ["After lunch will do as well for it as after breakfast.", "아침 식사 뒤가 아니라 점심 뒤라도 마찬가지지."], ["All going to the lake, eh?", "다들 호수에 간다고?"], ["A good idea.", "좋은 생각이오."], ["Let’s have an idle morning—I’ll be one of the party.”", "한가한 아침을 보냅시다—나도 한몫 끼겠소.”"]], [["There was no mistaking his manner, even if it had been possible to mistake the uncharacteristic readiness which his words expressed, to submit his own plans and projects to the convenience of others.", "자기 계획과 뜻을 남들의 편의에 맞추겠다는, 그답지 않은 선선함이 그 말에서 드러났으니, 설령 그 말은 잘못 알아들을 수 있었다 해도 그 태도만은 오해할 여지가 없었다."], ["He was evidently relieved at finding any excuse for delaying the business formality in the library, to which his own words had referred.", "그는 자기 입으로 말한 서재의 그 사무적인 절차를 미룰 핑곗거리가 생긴 것을 분명 안도하고 있었다."], ["My heart sank within me as I drew the inevitable inference.", "피할 수 없는 결론이 떠오르자 내 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다."]], [["The Count and his wife joined us at that moment.", "바로 그때 백작과 그 부인이 우리와 합류했다."], ["The lady had her husband’s embroidered tobacco-pouch, and her store of paper in her hand, for the manufacture of the eternal cigarettes.", "부인은 남편의 수놓은 담배쌈지와, 저 끝없는 궐련을 만들 종이 뭉치를 손에 들고 있었다."], ["The gentleman, dressed, as usual, in his blouse and straw hat, carried the gay little pagoda-cage, with his darling white mice in it, and smiled on them, and on us, with a bland amiability which it was impossible to resist.", "신사는 여느 때처럼 블라우스와 밀짚모자 차림으로 사랑스러운 흰 생쥐들이 든 화려한 작은 탑 모양 새장을 들고 있었고, 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 부드러운 상냥함으로 생쥐들에게, 그리고 우리에게 미소를 지었다."]], [["“With your kind permission,”", "“허락해 주신다면.” 백작이 말했다."], ["said the Count, “I will take my small family here—my poor-little-harmless-pretty-Mouseys, out for an airing along with us.", "“저의 작은 가족—가엾고-순하고-예쁜-생쥐들도 우리와 함께 바람을 쐬러 데려가겠습니다. 집 주변에는 개들이 있는데, 제 가련한 흰 자식들을 개들의 자비에 맡겨 두라고요?"], ["There are dogs about the house, and shall I leave my forlorn white children at the mercies of the dogs? Ah, never!”", "아, 결코 안 될 말이지요!”"]], [["He chirruped paternally at his small white children through the bars of the pagoda, and we all left the house for the lake.", "그는 탑 모양 새장의 창살 너머로 자기 작은 흰 자식들을 향해 아비처럼 짹짹거렸고, 우리는 모두 호수를 향해 집을 나섰다."]], [["In the plantation Sir Percival strayed away from us.", "농원에 들어서자 퍼시벌 경은 우리에게서 떨어져 나가 어디론가 사라졌다."], ["It seems to be part of his restless disposition always to separate himself from his companions on these occasions, and always to occupy himself when he is alone in cutting new walking-sticks for his own use.", "이런 자리에서 늘 일행에게서 저 혼자 떨어져 나가고, 혼자 있을 때면 늘 제가 쓸 새 지팡이를 깎는 데 몰두하는 것이 그 잠시도 가만있지 못하는 기질의 일부인 모양이다."], ["The mere act of cutting and lopping at hazard appears to please him.", "아무거나 되는대로 자르고 쳐 내는 그 행위 자체가 그를 즐겁게 하는 듯하다."], ["He has filled the house with walking-sticks of his own making, not one of which he ever takes up for a second time.", "그는 제 손으로 만든 지팡이로 집 안을 가득 채워 놓았지만, 그중 어느 하나도 두 번 집어 드는 법이 없다."], ["When they have been once used his interest in them is all exhausted, and he thinks of nothing but going on and making more.", "한 번 쓰고 나면 그것에 대한 흥미는 죄다 소진되고, 계속 나아가 더 많이 만드는 것밖에 생각하지 않는다."]], [["At the old boat-house he joined us again.", "낡은 보트 창고에서 그는 다시 우리와 합류했다."], ["I will put down the conversation that ensued when we were all settled in our places exactly as it passed.", "우리가 모두 자리를 잡고 앉은 뒤에 오간 대화를 오간 그대로 여기 적어 두겠다."], ["It is an important conversation, so far as I am concerned, for it has seriously disposed me to distrust the influence which Count Fosco has exercised over my thoughts and feelings, and to resist it for the future as resolutely as I can.", "그것은 나에게는 중요한 대화다. 포스코 백작이 내 생각과 감정에 행사해 온 영향력을 심각하게 불신하고, 앞으로는 있는 힘껏 단호히 물리치도록 나를 몰아붙였기 때문이다."]], [["The boat-house was large enough to hold us all, but Sir Percival remained outside trimming the last new stick with his pocket-axe.", "보트 창고는 우리 모두가 들어갈 만큼 넉넉했지만 퍼시벌 경은 밖에 남아 주머니도끼로 가장 새로 만든 지팡이를 다듬고 있었다."], ["We three women found plenty of room on the large seat.", "우리 여자 셋은 큰 걸상에 넉넉히 자리를 잡았다."], ["Laura took her work, and Madame Fosco began her cigarettes.", "로라는 바느질감을 들었고 포스코 부인은 궐련을 말기 시작했다."], ["I, as usual, had nothing to do.", "나는 여느 때처럼 할 일이 없었다."], ["My hands always were, and always will be, as awkward as a man’s.", "내 손은 늘 남자 손처럼 서툴렀고 앞으로도 그럴 것이다."], ["The Count good-humouredly took a stool many sizes too small for him, and balanced himself on it with his back against the side of the shed, which creaked and groaned under his weight.", "백작은 자기 몸에 비해 몇 치수는 작은 걸상을 선선히 차지하고, 창고 벽에 등을 기댄 채 그 위에서 몸의 균형을 잡았다. 벽은 그 무게에 삐걱거리며 신음했다."], ["He put the pagoda-cage on his lap, and let out the mice to crawl over him as usual.", "그는 탑 모양 새장을 무릎에 올려놓고 여느 때처럼 생쥐들을 풀어 제 몸 위를 기어다니게 했다."], ["They are pretty, innocent-looking little creatures, but the sight of them creeping about a man’s body is for some reason not pleasant to me.", "예쁘고 순해 보이는 작은 짐승들이지만, 그것들이 사람 몸을 기어다니는 광경은 웬일인지 나에게 유쾌하지 않다."], ["It excites a strange responsive creeping in my own nerves, and suggests hideous ideas of men dying in prison with the crawling creatures of the dungeon preying on them undisturbed.", "그것을 보면 내 신경에도 이상하게 스멀거리는 감각이 일고, 감옥에서 죽어 가는 사람들 위로 지하 감방의 기어다니는 것들이 아무 방해도 받지 않고 달려드는 끔찍한 광경이 떠오른다."]], [["The morning was windy and cloudy, and the rapid alternations of shadow and sunlight over the waste of the lake made the view look doubly wild, weird, and gloomy.", "아침 날씨는 바람이 불고 흐렸으며, 황량한 호수 위로 그늘과 햇빛이 빠르게 번갈아 스치는 통에 그 풍경은 갑절로 거칠고 기괴하고 음울해 보였다."]], [["“Some people call that picturesque,”", "“어떤 사람들은 저걸 그림 같다고들 하지.”"], ["said Sir Percival, pointing over the wide prospect with his half-finished walking-stick.", "퍼시벌 경이 다 깎지 못한 지팡이로 넓은 경치를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“I call it a blot on a gentleman’s property.", "“나는 신사의 영지에 난 얼룩이라고 부르오."], ["In my great-grandfather’s time the lake flowed to this place.", "우리 증조부 시절에는 호수가 여기까지 차 있었소."], ["Look at it now!", "지금 저 꼴을 보시오!"], ["It is not four feet deep anywhere, and it is all puddles and pools.", "어디를 재도 깊이가 넉 자가 안 되고 온통 웅덩이와 물구덩이뿐이오."], ["I wish I could afford to drain it, and plant it all over.", "여유만 있다면 저 물을 죄다 빼고 나무를 심어 버리고 싶소."], ["My bailiff (a superstitious idiot) says he is quite sure the lake has a curse on it, like the Dead Sea.", "우리 집 마름이란 자는(미신에 찌든 멍청이지) 저 호수에 사해처럼 저주가 걸려 있는 게 틀림없다고 하더군."], ["What do you think, Fosco?", "어떻게 생각하시오, 포스코?"], ["It looks just the place for a murder, doesn’t it?”", "딱 살인하기 좋은 장소 같지 않소?”"]], [["“My good Percival,”", "“여보시게 퍼시벌.”"], ["remonstrated the Count.", "백작이 나무라듯 말했다."], ["“What is your solid English sense thinking of?", "“자네의 그 견실한 영국식 분별력이 대체 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 건가?"], ["The water is too shallow to hide the body, and there is sand everywhere to print off the murderer’s footsteps.", "저 물은 너무 얕아서 시체를 감출 수 없고, 사방이 모래라 살인자의 발자국이 그대로 찍힌다네."], ["It is, upon the whole, the very worst place for a murder that I ever set my eyes on.”", "통틀어 말해서 내가 평생 눈으로 본 중에 살인하기에 가장 나쁜 장소일세.”"]], [["“Humbug!”", "“허튼소리!”"], ["said Sir Percival, cutting away fiercely at his stick.", "퍼시벌 경이 지팡이를 사납게 깎아 대며 말했다."], ["“You know what I mean.", "“내 말뜻을 알면서 그러오."], ["The dreary scenery, the lonely situation.", "저 을씨년스러운 경치, 저 외진 자리 말이오."], ["If you choose to understand me, you can—if you don’t choose, I am not going to trouble myself to explain my meaning.”", "알아들을 마음이 있으면 알아들으면 되고—그럴 마음이 없다면 내 뜻을 굳이 설명하느라 애쓰지는 않겠소.”"]], [["“And why not,”", "“왜 그러겠나?”"], ["asked the Count, “when your meaning can be explained by anybody in two words?", "백작이 물었다. “자네 뜻은 아무나 두 마디로 설명할 수 있는데 말일세."], ["If a fool was going to commit a murder, your lake is the first place he would choose for it.", "바보가 살인을 저지르려 한다면 자네 호수가 그가 첫 번째로 고를 장소일세."], ["If a wise man was going to commit a murder, your lake is the last place he would choose for it.", "현명한 자가 살인을 저지르려 한다면 자네 호수가 그가 맨 마지막으로 고를 장소고."], ["Is that your meaning?", "그게 자네 뜻인가?"], ["If it is, there is your explanation for you ready made.", "그렇다면 자, 설명은 다 만들어져 여기 있네."], ["Take it, Percival, with your good Fosco’s blessing.”", "받아 두게, 퍼시벌. 자네의 착한 포스코의 축복과 함께.”"]], [["Laura looked at the Count with her dislike for him appearing a little too plainly in her face.", "로라는 백작을 향한 혐오가 얼굴에 조금 지나치게 드러난 채로 그를 바라보았다."], ["He was so busy with his mice that he did not notice her.", "그는 생쥐들에 정신이 팔려 그것을 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“I am sorry to hear the lake-view connected with anything so horrible as the idea of murder,”", "“저 호수 경치가 살인이라는 생각처럼 끔찍한 것과 엮이는 걸 듣자니 유감이군요.” 로라가 말했다."], ["she said. “And if Count Fosco must divide murderers into classes, I think he has been very unfortunate in his choice of expressions.", "“그리고 포스코 백작께서 굳이 살인자를 부류로 나누셔야 한다면, 말을 고르시는 데 아주 잘못하신 것 같아요."], ["To describe them as fools only seems like treating them with an indulgence to which they have no claim.", "그들을 그저 바보라고 부르는 건 그들이 누릴 자격이 조금도 없는 관대함을 베푸는 것처럼 들리는걸요."], ["And to describe them as wise men sounds to me like a downright contradiction in terms.", "그리고 그들을 현명한 사람이라고 부르는 건 제게는 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 소리로 들려요."], ["I have always heard that truly wise men are truly good men, and have a horror of crime.”", "저는 참으로 현명한 사람은 참으로 선한 사람이며 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어한다고 늘 들어 왔거든요.”"]], [["“My dear lady,”", "“친애하는 부인.”"], ["said the Count, “those are admirable sentiments, and I have seen them stated at the tops of copy-books.”", "백작이 말했다. “참으로 훌륭한 생각이고, 저는 그런 말을 습자책 맨 윗줄에 적혀 있는 걸 본 적이 있지요.”"], ["He lifted one of the white mice in the palm of his hand, and spoke to it in his whimsical way.", "그는 흰 생쥐 한 마리를 손바닥에 올려놓고 그 특유의 익살맞은 투로 그것에게 말했다."], ["“My pretty little smooth white rascal,” he said, “here is a moral lesson for you.", "“나의 예쁘고 매끈한 흰 악당아, 여기 너를 위한 도덕 교훈이 있다."], ["A truly wise mouse is a truly good mouse.", "참으로 현명한 생쥐는 참으로 착한 생쥐다."], ["Mention that, if you please, to your companions, and never gnaw at the bars of your cage again as long as you live.”", "부디 그 말을 네 동무들에게 전하고, 살아 있는 동안 다시는 새장 창살을 갉지 말아라.”"]], [["“It is easy to turn everything into ridicule,”", "“뭐든지 우스개로 돌리기는 쉽지요.”"], ["said Laura resolutely; “but you will not find it quite so easy, Count Fosco, to give me an instance of a wise man who has been a great criminal.”", "로라가 단호하게 말했다. “하지만 포스코 백작님, 큰 범죄자였던 현명한 사람의 실례를 저에게 하나 대 보시는 건 그리 쉽지 않으실 거예요.”"]], [["The Count shrugged his huge shoulders, and smiled on Laura in the friendliest manner.", "백작은 그 거대한 어깨를 으쓱하고는 더없이 다정한 태도로 로라에게 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Most true!” he said.", "“지당한 말씀!”"], ["“The fool’s crime is the crime that is found out, and the wise man’s crime is the crime that is NOT found out.", "그가 말했다. “바보의 범죄란 들통난 범죄고, 현명한 자의 범죄란 들통나지 않은 범죄니까요."], ["If I could give you an instance, it would not be the instance of a wise man.", "제가 실례를 하나 댈 수 있다면, 그건 현명한 자의 실례가 아니겠지요."], ["Dear Lady Glyde, your sound English common sense has been too much for me.", "친애하는 글라이드 부인, 부인의 건전한 영국식 상식이 저에게는 너무 벅찼습니다."], ["It is checkmate for me this time, Miss Halcombe—ha?”", "이번엔 제가 외통수로군요, 홀콤 양—안 그렇습니까?”"]], [["“Stand to your guns, Laura,”", "“잘 버텨라, 로라.”"], ["sneered Sir Percival, who had been listening in his place at the door.", "문가 제자리에서 듣고 있던 퍼시벌 경이 비웃듯 말했다."], ["“Tell him next, that crimes cause their own detection.", "“다음엔 범죄란 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고 말해 주려무나."], ["There’s another bit of copy-book morality for you, Fosco.", "자, 습자책 도덕 한 자락 더 나가오, 포스코."], ["Crimes cause their own detection.", "범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다."], ["What infernal humbug!”", "이런 지긋지긋한 허튼소리가 있나!”"]], [["“I believe it to be true,”", "“저는 그게 사실이라고 믿어요.”"], ["said Laura quietly.", "로라가 조용히 말했다."]], [["Sir Percival burst out laughing, so violently, so outrageously, that he quite startled us all—the Count more than any of us.", "퍼시벌 경이 어찌나 격렬하게, 어찌나 터무니없이 웃음을 터뜨렸는지 우리 모두가 화들짝 놀랐고—누구보다 백작이 놀랐다."]], [["“I believe it too,”", "“저도 그렇게 믿어요.”"], ["I said, coming to Laura’s rescue.", "로라를 구하러 나서며 내가 말했다."]], [["Sir Percival, who had been unaccountably amused at his wife’s remark, was just as unaccountably irritated by mine.", "아내의 말에는 영문 모르게 재미있어하던 퍼시벌 경이, 내 말에는 그만큼이나 영문 모르게 짜증을 냈다."], ["He struck the new stick savagely on the sand, and walked away from us.", "그는 새 지팡이로 모래를 사납게 내리치고는 우리에게서 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“Poor dear Percival!”", "“딱하기도 하지, 우리 퍼시벌!”"], ["cried Count Fosco, looking after him gaily, “he is the victim of English spleen.", "포스코 백작이 그의 뒷모습을 유쾌하게 바라보며 외쳤다. “영국식 우울증의 희생자예요."], ["But, my dear Miss Halcombe, my dear Lady Glyde, do you really believe that crimes cause their own detection?", "그런데 친애하는 홀콤 양, 친애하는 글라이드 부인, 정말로 범죄가 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고 믿으십니까?"], ["And you, my angel,”", "그리고 당신은, 나의 천사여.”"], ["he continued, turning to his wife, who had not uttered a word yet, “do you think so too?”", "그는 아직 한마디도 하지 않은 아내를 돌아보며 말을 이었다. “당신도 그렇게 생각하오?”"]], [["“I wait to be instructed,”", "“저는 가르침을 기다립니다.”"], ["replied the Countess, in tones of freezing reproof, intended for Laura and me, “before I venture on giving my opinion in the presence of well-informed men.”", "백작부인이 로라와 나를 겨냥한 얼음장 같은 질책의 어조로 대답했다. “식견 있는 남자분들 앞에서 감히 제 의견을 내놓기 전에 말이지요.”"]], [["“Do you, indeed?”", "“아, 그러세요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I remember the time, Countess, when you advocated the Rights of Women, and freedom of female opinion was one of them.”", "“백작부인, 저는 부인께서 여성의 권리를 옹호하시던 시절을 기억합니다. 그리고 여성이 자유로이 의견을 말하는 것도 그 권리 가운데 하나였지요.”"]], [["“What is your view of the subject, Count?”", "“이 문제에 대한 당신의 견해는 어떠신가요, 백작?”"], ["asked Madame Fosco, calmly proceeding with her cigarettes, and not taking the least notice of me.", "포스코 부인이 태연히 궐련을 말면서, 나에게는 눈길 하나 주지 않고 물었다."]], [["The Count stroked one of his white mice reflectively with his chubby little finger before he answered.", "백작은 대답하기 전에 통통한 새끼손가락으로 흰 생쥐 한 마리를 생각에 잠긴 듯 쓰다듬었다."]], [["“It is truly wonderful,”", "“참으로 놀라운 일이지요.”"], ["he said, “how easily Society can console itself for the worst of its shortcomings with a little bit of clap-trap.", "그가 말했다. “사회가 제 최악의 결점들을 하찮은 상투적 문구 한 조각으로 얼마나 손쉽게 위로하는지 말입니다."], ["The machinery it has set up for the detection of crime is miserably ineffective—and yet only invent a moral epigram, saying that it works well, and you blind everybody to its blunders from that moment.", "사회가 범죄를 적발하려고 세워 놓은 기구는 한심할 만큼 무력합니다—그런데도 그것이 잘 돌아간다고 말하는 도덕적 경구 하나만 지어내 보십시오, 그 순간부터 모든 사람의 눈이 그 실책들에 대해 멀어 버립니다."], ["Crimes cause their own detection, do they?", "범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다고요?"], ["And murder will out (another moral epigram), will it?", "그리고 살인은 반드시 드러난다(이것도 또 하나의 도덕적 경구지요)고요?"], ["Ask Coroners who sit at inquests in large towns if that is true, Lady Glyde.", "그것이 사실인지 큰 도시에서 검시 심리를 주재하는 검시관들에게 물어보십시오, 글라이드 부인."], ["Ask secretaries of life-assurance companies if that is true, Miss Halcombe.", "그것이 사실인지 생명 보험 회사의 서기들에게 물어보십시오, 홀콤 양."], ["Read your own public journals.", "두 분 나라의 신문을 읽어 보십시오."], ["In the few cases that get into the newspapers, are there not instances of slain bodies found, and no murderers ever discovered?", "신문에까지 실리는 몇 안 되는 사건 중에도, 살해된 시신은 발견되었으나 살인자는 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 사례가 있지 않습니까?"], ["Multiply the cases that are reported by the cases that are NOT reported, and the bodies that are found by the bodies that are NOT found, and what conclusion do you come to?", "보도된 사건에 보도되지 않은 사건을 곱하고, 발견된 시신에 발견되지 않은 시신을 곱해 보십시오. 그러면 어떤 결론에 이르십니까?"], ["This.", "이것입니다."], ["That there are foolish criminals who are discovered, and wise criminals who escape.", "발각되는 어리석은 범죄자들이 있고, 빠져나가는 현명한 범죄자들이 있다는 것."], ["The hiding of a crime, or the detection of a crime, what is it?", "범죄를 감추는 일, 혹은 범죄를 적발하는 일, 그게 무엇입니까?"], ["A trial of skill between the police on one side, and the individual on the other.", "한쪽에는 경찰, 다른 한쪽에는 개인이 벌이는 재주 겨루기지요."], ["When the criminal is a brutal, ignorant fool, the police in nine cases out of ten win.", "범죄자가 우악스럽고 무지한 바보일 때는 열에 아홉은 경찰이 이깁니다."], ["When the criminal is a resolute, educated, highly-intelligent man, the police in nine cases out of ten lose.", "범죄자가 단호하고 교육받은, 지극히 총명한 사람일 때는 열에 아홉은 경찰이 집니다."], ["If the police win, you generally hear all about it.", "경찰이 이기면 대개 그 이야기를 죄다 듣게 되지요."], ["If the police lose, you generally hear nothing.", "경찰이 지면 대개 아무 이야기도 듣지 못하고요."], ["And on this tottering foundation you build up your comfortable moral maxim that Crime causes its own detection!", "그런데도 두 분은 이 흔들리는 토대 위에다 범죄는 제 스스로 발각을 부른다는 편안한 도덕률을 세워 올립니다!"], ["Yes—all the crime you know of.", "그렇지요—두 분이 아시는 범죄는 모두 그렇습니다."], ["And what of the rest?”", "그러면 나머지는요?”"]], [["“Devilish true, and very well put,”", "“지독히도 옳은 말씀이고, 아주 잘 표현했소.”"], ["cried a voice at the entrance of the boat-house.", "보트 창고 입구에서 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["Sir Percival had recovered his equanimity, and had come back while we were listening to the Count.", "퍼시벌 경이 평정을 되찾고, 우리가 백작의 말을 듣고 있는 사이에 돌아와 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Some of it may be true,”", "“더러는 사실이겠지요.”"], ["I said, “and all of it may be very well put.", "내가 말했다. “그리고 전부 아주 잘 표현했을 수도 있고요."], ["But I don’t see why Count Fosco should celebrate the victory of the criminal over Society with so much exultation, or why you, Sir Percival, should applaud him so loudly for doing it.”", "하지만 저는 포스코 백작께서 왜 사회에 대한 범죄자의 승리를 그토록 의기양양하게 기리셔야 하는지, 그리고 퍼시벌 경께서는 왜 그러시는 백작께 그토록 소리 높여 박수를 보내셔야 하는지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Do you hear that, Fosco?”", "“들었소, 포스코?”"], ["asked Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 물었다."], ["“Take my advice, and make your peace with your audience.", "“내 충고를 듣고 청중과 화해하시오."], ["Tell them virtue’s a fine thing—they like that, I can promise you.”", "미덕은 훌륭한 것이라고 말해 주시오—그럼 다들 좋아할 거요, 내가 장담하리다.”"]], [["The Count laughed inwardly and silently, and two of the white mice in his waistcoat, alarmed by the internal convulsion going on beneath them, darted out in a violent hurry, and scrambled into their cage again.", "백작은 소리 없이 속으로 웃었고, 그의 조끼 안에 있던 흰 생쥐 두 마리가 제 밑에서 벌어지는 내부의 진동에 놀라 허둥지둥 뛰쳐나와 다시 제 새장으로 기어들어 갔다."]], [["“The ladies, my good Percival, shall tell me about virtue,”", "“미덕에 대해서는, 여보시게 퍼시벌, 숙녀분들께서 나에게 말씀해 주실 걸세.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said.", "“숙녀분들이 나보다 나은 권위자시지."], ["“They are better authorities than I am, for they know what virtue is, and I don’t.”", "미덕이 무엇인지 아시니까 말이야. 나는 모르고.”"]], [["“You hear him?”", "“저 소리 들었소?”"], ["said Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말했다."], ["“Isn’t it awful?”", "“끔찍하지 않소?”"]], [["“It is true,”", "“사실입니다.”"], ["said the Count quietly.", "백작이 조용히 말했다."], ["“I am a citizen of the world, and I have met, in my time, with so many different sorts of virtue, that I am puzzled, in my old age, to say which is the right sort and which is the wrong.", "“저는 세계의 시민이고, 살아오는 동안 하도 여러 종류의 미덕을 만난 터라, 이 늘그막에 어느 것이 옳은 종류이고 어느 것이 그른 종류인지 말하기가 난감합니다."], ["Here, in England, there is one virtue.", "여기 영국에는 이런 미덕이 있습니다."], ["And there, in China, there is another virtue.", "그리고 저기 중국에는 저런 미덕이 있지요."], ["And John Englishman says my virtue is the genuine virtue.", "그리고 영국인 존은 내 미덕이야말로 진짜 미덕이라고 말합니다."], ["And John Chinaman says my virtue is the genuine virtue.", "그리고 중국인 존도 내 미덕이야말로 진짜 미덕이라고 말하고요."], ["And I say Yes to one, or No to the other, and am just as much bewildered about it in the case of John with the top-boots as I am in the case of John with the pigtail.", "그래서 제가 한쪽에는 예라 하고 다른 쪽에는 아니오라 합니다만, 목이 긴 장화를 신은 존의 경우나 변발을 늘어뜨린 존의 경우나 저로서는 어리둥절하기가 매한가지입니다. 아, 귀여운 생쥐야!"], ["Ah, nice little Mousey!", "이리 와서 내게 입 맞추렴."], ["come, kiss me. What is your own private notion of a virtuous man, my pret-pret-pretty?", "덕 있는 사람에 대한 너만의 은밀한 생각은 무엇이냐, 나의 예-예-예쁜 것아?"], ["A man who keeps you warm, and gives you plenty to eat.", "너를 따뜻하게 해 주고 먹을 것을 넉넉히 주는 사람이겠지."], ["And a good notion, too, for it is intelligible, at the least.”", "그것도 좋은 생각이야. 적어도 알아들을 수는 있으니까.”"]], [["“Stay a minute, Count,”", "“잠깐만요, 백작님.”"], ["I interposed.", "내가 끼어들었다."], ["“Accepting your illustration, surely we have one unquestionable virtue in England which is wanting in China.", "“백작님의 비유를 받아들인다 해도, 영국에는 중국에 없는 의심할 여지 없는 미덕이 하나 분명 있습니다."], ["The Chinese authorities kill thousands of innocent people on the most frivolous pretexts.", "중국 관헌은 지극히 하찮은 구실로 죄 없는 사람들을 수천 명씩 죽입니다."], ["We in England are free from all guilt of that kind—we commit no such dreadful crime—we abhor reckless bloodshed with all our hearts.”", "우리 영국인은 그런 죄에서 온전히 자유롭습니다—우리는 그런 무서운 범죄를 저지르지 않아요—우리는 무분별한 유혈을 진심으로 몸서리치게 싫어합니다.”"]], [["“Quite right, Marian,”", "“옳은 말이야, 매리언.”"], ["said Laura.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“Well thought of, and well expressed.”", "“잘 짚었고, 잘 표현했어.”"]], [["“Pray allow the Count to proceed,”", "“부디 백작께서 말씀을 이어 가시게 두시지요.”"], ["said Madame Fosco, with stern civility.", "포스코 부인이 엄격한 예의를 갖추어 말했다."], ["“You will find, young ladies, that HE never speaks without having excellent reasons for all that he says.”", "“젊은 숙녀분들, 이분은 하시는 모든 말씀에 훌륭한 이유가 있지 않고서는 결코 입을 여시지 않는다는 걸 알게 되실 거예요.”"]], [["“Thank you, my angel,”", "“고맙소, 나의 천사여.”"], ["replied the Count.", "백작이 대답했다."], ["“Have a bon-bon?”", "“봉봉 하나 드시겠소?”"], ["He took out of his pocket a pretty little inlaid box, and placed it open on the table.", "그는 주머니에서 예쁘장한 상감 세공 상자를 꺼내 뚜껑을 연 채 탁자에 올려놓았다."], ["“Chocolat a la Vanille,”", "“쇼콜라 아 라 바니유!”"], ["cried the impenetrable man, cheerfully rattling the sweetmeats in the box, and bowing all round.", "그 속을 알 수 없는 사내가 유쾌하게 외치며 상자 안의 사탕을 달그락거리고는 사방으로 인사를 했다."], ["“Offered by Fosco as an act of homage to the charming society.”", "“이 매혹적인 모임에 대한 경의의 표시로 포스코가 바칩니다.”"]], [["“Be good enough to go on, Count,”", "“계속 말씀하시지요, 백작.”"], ["said his wife, with a spiteful reference to myself.", "그의 아내가 나를 겨냥한 심술궂은 어조로 말했다."], ["“Oblige me by answering Miss Halcombe.”", "“홀콤 양에게 대답해 주시면 고맙겠어요.”"]], [["“Miss Halcombe is unanswerable,”", "“홀콤 양에게는 대답할 말이 없습니다.”"], ["replied the polite Italian; “that is to say, so far as she goes.", "그 예의 바른 이탈리아인이 대답했다. “다시 말해, 그분이 말씀하신 데까지는 그렇다는 거지요."], ["Yes! I agree with her.", "그렇습니다!"], ["John Bull does abhor the crimes of John Chinaman.", "저는 그분 말씀에 동의합니다."], ["He is the quickest old gentleman at finding out faults that are his neighbours’, and the slowest old gentleman at finding out the faults that are his own, who exists on the face of creation.", "존 불은 정말로 중국인 존의 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어합니다. 그는 이 세상에 존재하는 어떤 노신사보다도 이웃의 허물을 찾아내는 데는 가장 빠르고, 제 허물을 찾아내는 데는 가장 느린 노신사지요."], ["Is he so very much better in this way than the people whom he condemns in their way?", "그렇다면 그가 이런 점에서, 자기가 단죄하는 저 사람들이 저희 방식으로 그러한 것보다 그렇게나 많이 나은 사람일까요?"], ["English Society, Miss Halcombe, is as often the accomplice as it is the enemy of crime.", "홀콤 양, 영국 사회는 범죄의 적인 만큼이나 자주 범죄의 공범입니다."], ["Yes! yes!", "그렇습니다!"], ["Crime is in this country what crime is in other countries—a good friend to a man and to those about him as often as it is an enemy.", "그렇고말고요! 이 나라에서도 범죄란 다른 나라에서와 마찬가지입니다—적인 만큼이나 자주 한 사람과 그 주변 사람들에게 좋은 벗이 되어 주지요."], ["A great rascal provides for his wife and family.", "큰 악당은 제 처자식을 먹여 살립니다."], ["The worse he is the more he makes them the objects for your sympathy.", "그가 나쁘면 나쁠수록 그 처자식은 그만큼 더 여러분의 동정의 대상이 되고요."], ["He often provides also for himself.", "그는 흔히 제 자신도 먹여 살립니다."], ["A profligate spendthrift who is always borrowing money will get more from his friends than the rigidly honest man who only borrows of them once, under pressure of the direst want.", "늘 돈을 꾸러 다니는 방탕한 낭비꾼은, 더할 수 없이 절박한 궁핍에 몰려 단 한 번 돈을 꾸는 지독히 정직한 사람보다 친구들에게서 더 많이 얻어 냅니다."], ["In the one case the friends will not be at all surprised, and they will give.", "앞의 경우에 친구들은 조금도 놀라지 않고, 그래서 줍니다."], ["In the other case they will be very much surprised, and they will hesitate. Is the prison that Mr.", "뒤의 경우에 친구들은 몹시 놀라고, 그래서 망설이지요."], ["Scoundrel lives in at the end of his career a more uncomfortable place than the workhouse that Mr. Honesty lives in at the end of his career?", "악당 씨가 그 인생의 끝에 가서 사는 감옥이, 정직 씨가 그 인생의 끝에 가서 사는 구빈원보다 더 불편한 곳일까요?"], ["When John-Howard-Philanthropist wants to relieve misery he goes to find it in prisons, where crime is wretched—not in huts and hovels, where virtue is wretched too.", "자선가 존 하워드 선생은 비참을 덜어 주고 싶을 때 감옥으로 그것을 찾아갑니다. 죄악이 비참한 곳이지요—미덕 또한 비참한 오두막과 움막으로 가는 것이 아니라 말입니다."], ["Who is the English poet who has won the most universal sympathy—who makes the easiest of all subjects for pathetic writing and pathetic painting?", "가장 널리 사람들의 동정을 얻은 영국 시인은 누구입니까—애절한 글과 애절한 그림의 소재로 가장 손쉬운 이는 누구지요?"], ["That nice young person who began life with a forgery, and ended it by a suicide—your dear, romantic, interesting Chatterton.", "문서 위조로 인생을 시작해 자살로 그것을 끝낸 그 참한 젊은이, 여러분의 사랑스럽고 낭만적이고 흥미로운 채터턴이지요."], ["Which gets on best, do you think, of two poor starving dressmakers—the woman who resists temptation and is honest, or the woman who falls under temptation and steals?", "굶주리는 가난한 삯바느질 여공 둘 중에 누가 더 잘 풀린다고 생각하십니까—유혹을 물리치고 정직하게 사는 여자입니까, 유혹에 넘어가 도둑질을 하는 여자입니까?"], ["You all know that the stealing is the making of that second woman’s fortune—it advertises her from length to breadth of good-humoured, charitable England—and she is relieved, as the breaker of a commandment, when she would have been left to starve, as the keeper of it.", "그 도둑질이야말로 두 번째 여자의 팔자를 고쳐 준다는 걸 여러분 모두 아십니다—그것이 사람 좋고 자비로운 영국 방방곡곡에 그 여자를 광고해 주지요—그래서 그 여자는 계명을 지킨 자로서는 굶어 죽도록 버려졌을 터인데, 계명을 깨뜨린 자로서 구제를 받습니다."], ["Come here, my jolly little Mouse!", "이리 오너라, 나의 쾌활한 작은 생쥐야!"], ["Hey!", "자!"], ["presto!", "얍!"], ["pass!", "짠!"], ["I transform you, for the time being, into a respectable lady.", "내가 너를 잠시 동안 어엿한 숙녀로 변신시켜 주마."], ["Stop there, in the palm of my great big hand, my dear, and listen.", "거기 내 커다란 손바닥 위에 서 있거라, 얘야, 그리고 들어 보렴."], ["You marry the poor man whom you love, Mouse, and one half your friends pity, and the other half blame you.", "생쥐야, 네가 사랑하는 가난한 남자와 결혼한다면 네 친구의 절반은 너를 가엾어하고 나머지 절반은 너를 나무란다."], ["And now, on the contrary, you sell yourself for gold to a man you don’t care for, and all your friends rejoice over you, and a minister of public worship sanctions the base horror of the vilest of all human bargains, and smiles and smirks afterwards at your table, if you are polite enough to ask him to breakfast.", "그런데 이번엔 거꾸로 네가 마음에도 없는 남자에게 금을 받고 네 몸을 판다면, 네 친구들은 모두 너를 두고 기뻐하고, 공중 예배의 목사는 모든 인간 거래 중에 가장 비열한 그 천한 참극을 승인해 주며, 나중에 네가 예의 바르게 조반에 초대만 해 주면 네 식탁에서 싱글벙글 웃고 있을 게다."], ["Hey!", "자!"], ["presto!", "얍! 짠!"], ["pass! Be a mouse again, and squeak.", "다시 생쥐가 되어 찍찍 울어라."], ["If you continue to be a lady much longer, I shall have you telling me that Society abhors crime—and then, Mouse, I shall doubt if your own eyes and ears are really of any use to you.", "네가 조금만 더 오래 숙녀 노릇을 하고 있다가는 사회가 범죄를 몸서리치게 싫어한다고 나에게 말하기 시작할 테고—그러면, 생쥐야, 나는 네 눈과 귀가 정말 조금이라도 쓸모가 있는지 의심하게 될 테니까."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I am a bad man, Lady Glyde, am I not?", "저는 나쁜 사람이지요, 글라이드 부인, 그렇지 않습니까?"], ["I say what other people only think, and when all the rest of the world is in a conspiracy to accept the mask for the true face, mine is the rash hand that tears off the plump pasteboard, and shows the bare bones beneath.", "저는 남들이 생각만 하는 것을 말합니다. 그리고 온 세상이 가면을 진짜 얼굴로 받아들이자고 한통속으로 짜고 있을 때, 그 통통한 판지를 잡아 뜯어 그 아래 앙상한 뼈를 드러내 보이는 것이 제 무모한 손이지요."], ["I will get up on my big elephant’s legs, before I do myself any more harm in your amiable estimations—I will get up and take a little airy walk of my own.", "두 분의 다정한 평가 속에서 제 자신을 더 해치기 전에 이 커다란 코끼리 다리를 일으켜 세우겠습니다—일어나서 저 혼자 가벼운 산책이나 하고 오겠습니다."], ["Dear ladies, as your excellent Sheridan said, I go—and leave my character behind me.”", "친애하는 숙녀 여러분, 두 분의 훌륭한 셰리든이 말했듯이, 저는 갑니다—제 인격은 뒤에 남겨 놓고서.”"]], [["He got up, put the cage on the table, and paused for a moment to count the mice in it.", "그는 일어나 새장을 탁자에 올려놓고, 그 안의 생쥐를 세느라 잠시 멈추었다."], ["“One, two, three, four——Ha!”", "“하나, 둘, 셋, 넷——하!”"], ["he cried, with a look of horror, “where, in the name of Heaven, is the fifth—the youngest, the whitest, the most amiable of all—my Benjamin of mice!”", "그가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 외쳤다. “하느님 맙소사, 다섯 번째는 어디 있나—가장 어리고, 가장 희고, 가장 사랑스러운—내 생쥐들 중의 베냐민이!”"]], [["Neither Laura nor I were in any favorable disposition to be amused.", "로라도 나도 재미있어할 만한 마음 상태가 아니었다."], ["The Count’s glib cynicism had revealed a new aspect of his nature from which we both recoiled.", "백작의 그 매끄러운 냉소는 우리 둘 다 움츠러들 만한, 그 본성의 새로운 면모를 드러내 보였다."], ["But it was impossible to resist the comical distress of so very large a man at the loss of so very small a mouse.", "그러나 그토록 커다란 사내가 그토록 조그만 생쥐를 잃고 괴로워하는 그 우스꽝스러운 꼴만은 견뎌 낼 재간이 없었다."], ["We laughed in spite of ourselves; and when Madame Fosco rose to set the example of leaving the boat-house empty, so that her husband might search it to its remotest corners, we rose also to follow her out.", "우리는 저도 모르게 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 남편이 보트 창고를 구석구석 뒤질 수 있도록 포스코 부인이 몸소 앞장서서 창고를 비워 주려고 일어서자, 우리도 그를 따라 나가려고 일어섰다."]], [["Before we had taken three steps, the Count’s quick eye discovered the lost mouse under the seat that we had been occupying.", "우리가 세 걸음도 떼기 전에 백작의 재빠른 눈이 우리가 앉아 있던 걸상 밑에서 잃어버린 생쥐를 찾아냈다."], ["He pulled aside the bench, took the little animal up in his hand, and then suddenly stopped, on his knees, looking intently at a particular place on the ground just beneath him.", "그는 긴 의자를 옆으로 밀치고 그 작은 짐승을 손에 집어 들었으나, 그러다 문득 무릎을 꿇은 자세 그대로 멈춰 서서 바로 제 밑 땅바닥의 한 지점을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다."]], [["When he rose to his feet again, his hand shook so that he could hardly put the mouse back in the cage, and his face was of a faint livid yellow hue all over.", "다시 일어섰을 때 그의 손은 어찌나 떨리는지 생쥐를 새장에 도로 넣기도 힘들 지경이었고, 그 얼굴은 온통 희부연 납빛 누런 기색이었다."]], [["“Percival!”", "“퍼시벌!”"], ["he said, in a whisper.", "그가 속삭이듯 말했다."], ["“Percival!", "“퍼시벌!"], ["come here.”", "이리 와 보게.”"]], [["Sir Percival had paid no attention to any of us for the last ten minutes.", "퍼시벌 경은 지난 십 분 동안 우리 중 누구에게도 신경을 쓰지 않았다."], ["He had been entirely absorbed in writing figures on the sand, and then rubbing them out again with the point of his stick.", "모래에 숫자를 적었다가 지팡이 끝으로 그것을 다시 지우는 일에 온통 빠져 있었다."]], [["“What’s the matter now?”", "“이번엔 또 무슨 일이오?”"], ["he asked, lounging carelessly into the boat-house.", "그가 태평하게 보트 창고 안으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오며 물었다."]], [["“Do you see nothing there?”", "“저기 아무것도 안 보이나?”"], ["said the Count, catching him nervously by the collar with one hand, and pointing with the other to the place near which he had found the mouse.", "백작이 한 손으로 신경질적으로 그의 옷깃을 붙잡고 다른 손으로 생쥐를 찾아낸 자리 근처를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“I see plenty of dry sand,”", "“마른 모래가 잔뜩 보이는군.”"], ["answered Sir Percival, “and a spot of dirt in the middle of it.”", "퍼시벌 경이 대답했다. “그리고 그 한가운데에 흙 자국이 하나 있고.”"]], [["“Not dirt,”", "“흙이 아닐세.”"], ["whispered the Count, fastening the other hand suddenly on Sir Percival’s collar, and shaking it in his agitation.", "백작이 속삭이며 남은 손마저 갑자기 퍼시벌 경의 옷깃에 걸고 흥분해서 그것을 흔들었다."], ["“Blood.”", "“피야.”"]], [["Laura was near enough to hear the last word, softly as he whispered it.", "로라는 그가 아무리 나직하게 속삭였다 해도 그 마지막 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 있었다."], ["She turned to me with a look of terror.", "로라는 겁에 질린 얼굴로 나를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Nonsense, my dear,” I said.", "“말도 안 되는 소리야, 얘.”"], ["“There is no need to be alarmed.", "내가 말했다. “놀랄 것 없어."], ["It is only the blood of a poor little stray dog.”", "그저 가엾은 길 잃은 개의 피일 뿐이니까.”"]], [["Everybody was astonished, and everybody’s eyes were fixed on me inquiringly.", "모두가 깜짝 놀랐고, 모두의 눈이 묻는 듯이 나에게 못 박혔다."]], [["“How do you know that?”", "“그걸 어떻게 아시오?”"], ["asked Sir Percival, speaking first.", "퍼시벌 경이 먼저 입을 열어 물었다."]], [["“I found the dog here, dying, on the day when you all returned from abroad,” I replied.", "“여러분이 다들 외국에서 돌아오시던 날, 제가 여기서 죽어 가는 그 개를 발견했어요.”"], ["“The poor creature had strayed into the plantation, and had been shot by your keeper.”", "내가 대답했다. “그 가엾은 것이 농원으로 잘못 들어왔다가 사냥터지기의 총에 맞았지요.”"]], [["“Whose dog was it?”", "“누구 개였소?”"], ["inquired Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 물었다."], ["“Not one of mine?”", "“내 개는 아니었소?”"]], [["“Did you try to save the poor thing?”", "“그 가엾은 것을 살리려고 해 봤어?”"], ["asked Laura earnestly.", "로라가 간절하게 물었다."], ["“Surely you tried to save it, Marian?”", "“분명 살리려고 해 봤겠지, 매리언?”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그럼.”"], ["I said, “the housekeeper and I both did our best—but the dog was mortally wounded, and he died under our hands.”", "내가 말했다. “가정부와 내가 둘 다 최선을 다했어—하지만 그 개는 치명상을 입어서 우리 손 안에서 죽었어.”"]], [["“Whose dog was it?”", "“누구 개였소?”"], ["persisted Sir Percival, repeating his question a little irritably.", "퍼시벌 경이 조금 짜증스레 제 물음을 한 번 더 되풀이하며 물고 늘어졌다."], ["“One of mine?”", "“내 개였소?”"]], [["“No, not one of yours.”", "“아니요, 경의 개는 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“Whose then?", "“그럼 누구 개요?"], ["Did the housekeeper know?”", "가정부는 알고 있었소?”"]], [["The housekeeper’s report of Mrs. Catherick’s desire to conceal her visit to Blackwater Park from Sir Percival’s knowledge recurred to my memory the moment he put that last question, and I half doubted the discretion of answering it; but in my anxiety to quiet the general alarm, I had thoughtlessly advanced too far to draw back, except at the risk of exciting suspicion, which might only make matters worse.", "캐서릭 부인이 블랙워터 파크에 다녀간 것을 퍼시벌 경이 알지 못하도록 숨기고 싶어 했다던 가정부의 이야기가 그 마지막 질문을 받는 순간 내 기억에 되살아났고, 나는 그 질문에 답하는 것이 과연 분별 있는 일인지 반쯤 의심했다."], ["There was nothing for it but to answer at once, without reference to results.", "그러나 모두의 불안을 가라앉히려는 마음에 나는 생각 없이 이미 너무 멀리 나아가 있었으니, 이제 와 물러선다면 의심을 불러일으켜 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들 위험만 있었다. 결과는 따지지 말고 당장 대답하는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“The housekeeper knew.", "“가정부는 알고 있었습니다."], ["She told me it was Mrs. Catherick’s dog.”", "캐서릭 부인의 개라고 저에게 말해 주었어요.”"]], [["Sir Percival had hitherto remained at the inner end of the boat-house with Count Fosco, while I spoke to him from the door.", "퍼시벌 경은 그때까지 포스코 백작과 함께 보트 창고 안쪽 끝에 있었고, 나는 문간에서 그에게 말하고 있었다."], ["But the instant Mrs. Catherick’s name passed my lips he pushed by the Count roughly, and placed himself face to face with me under the open daylight.", "그러나 캐서릭 부인의 이름이 내 입술을 스치는 순간 그는 백작을 거칠게 밀치고 지나와, 훤한 대낮 아래 나와 얼굴을 맞대고 섰다."]], [["“How came the housekeeper to know it was Mrs.", "“가정부가 어떻게 그게 캐서릭 부인의 개인 줄 알았소?”"], ["Catherick’s dog?”", "그가 물었다."], ["he asked, fixing his eyes on mine with a frowning interest and attention, which half angered, half startled me.", "그는 찌푸린 관심과 주의로 내 눈에 제 눈을 못 박았는데, 그것이 나를 반쯤은 화나게 하고 반쯤은 섬뜩하게 했다."]], [["“She knew it,”", "“가정부가 그것을 안 것은.”"], ["I said quietly, “because Mrs.", "내가 조용히 말했다."], ["Catherick brought the dog with her.”", "“캐서릭 부인이 그 개를 데리고 왔기 때문입니다.”"]], [["“Brought it with her?", "“개를 데리고 왔다고?"], ["Where did she bring it with her?”", "어디로 데리고 왔단 말이오?”"]], [["“To this house.”", "“이 집으로요.”"]], [["“What the devil did Mrs. Catherick want at this house?”", "“대체 캐서릭 부인이 이 집에 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이오?”"]], [["The manner in which he put the question was even more offensive than the language in which he expressed it.", "그가 그 질문을 던진 태도는 그것을 표현한 말씨보다도 더 무례했다."], ["I marked my sense of his want of common politeness by silently turning away from him.", "나는 그의 예의 없음에 대한 내 심중을 말없이 그에게서 돌아서는 것으로 표시했다."]], [["Just as I moved the Count’s persuasive hand was laid on his shoulder, and the Count’s mellifluous voice interposed to quiet him.", "내가 몸을 움직이는 바로 그때 백작의 달래는 손이 그의 어깨에 얹혔고, 백작의 감미로운 목소리가 그를 진정시키려고 끼어들었다."]], [["“My dear Percival!—gently—gently!”", "“여보시게 퍼시벌!—차분히—차분히!”"]], [["Sir Percival looked round in his angriest manner.", "퍼시벌 경은 더없이 성난 얼굴로 돌아보았다."], ["The Count only smiled and repeated the soothing application.", "백작은 그저 미소를 지으며 그 진정제를 다시 한번 발라 주었다."]], [["“Gently, my good friend—gently!”", "“차분히, 나의 좋은 벗이여—차분히!”"]], [["Sir Percival hesitated, followed me a few steps, and, to my great surprise, offered me an apology.", "퍼시벌 경은 망설이다가 나를 몇 걸음 따라오더니, 놀랍게도 나에게 사과를 건넸다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Miss Halcombe,”", "“실례했습니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I have been out of order lately, and I am afraid I am a little irritable.", "“요즘 몸이 좋지 않아서 아무래도 신경이 좀 날카로워진 모양입니다."], ["But I should like to know what Mrs. Catherick could possibly want here.", "하지만 캐서릭 부인이 대체 여기서 무엇을 바랐는지는 알고 싶군요."], ["When did she come?", "언제 왔습니까?"], ["Was the housekeeper the only person who saw her?”", "그 여자를 본 사람이 가정부뿐이었나요?”"]], [["“The only person,”", "“가정부 한 사람뿐이었어요.”"], ["I answered, “so far as I know.”", "내가 대답했다. “제가 아는 한은 그래요.”"]], [["The Count interposed again.", "백작이 다시 끼어들었다."]], [["“In that case why not question the housekeeper?”", "“그렇다면 가정부에게 물어보면 되지 않겠나?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Why not go, Percival, to the fountain-head of information at once?”", "“퍼시벌, 어찌하여 곧장 소식의 원천으로 가지 않는 건가?”"]], [["“Quite right!”", "“옳은 말이야!”"], ["said Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말했다."], ["“Of course the housekeeper is the first person to question.", "“물론 제일 먼저 물어볼 사람은 가정부지."], ["Excessively stupid of me not to see it myself.”", "그걸 스스로 깨닫지 못하다니 나도 어지간히 어리석군.”"], ["With those words he instantly left us to return to the house.", "그 말과 함께 그는 곧바로 우리를 떠나 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["The motive of the Count’s interference, which had puzzled me at first, betrayed itself when Sir Percival’s back was turned.", "처음에는 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 백작의 참견은, 퍼시벌 경이 등을 돌리자마자 그 속셈을 스스로 드러냈다."], ["He had a host of questions to put to me about Mrs. Catherick, and the cause of her visit to Blackwater Park, which he could scarcely have asked in his friend’s presence.", "그는 캐서릭 부인에 대해, 그리고 그 여자가 블랙워터 파크를 찾아온 까닭에 대해 나에게 물어볼 것이 산더미였는데, 그런 질문들은 친구가 있는 자리에서는 도저히 꺼낼 수 없는 것들이었다."], ["I made my answers as short as I civilly could, for I had already determined to check the least approach to any exchanging of confidences between Count Fosco and myself.", "나는 예의를 잃지 않는 선에서 되도록 짧게 대답했다. 포스코 백작과 나 사이에 속내를 주고받는 일이 조금이라도 생기지 않도록 이미 마음을 굳혀 두었기 때문이다."], ["Laura, however, unconsciously helped him to extract all my information, by making inquiries herself, which left me no alternative but to reply to her, or to appear in the very unenviable and very false character of a depositary of Sir Percival’s secrets.", "그러나 로라가 자기도 모르게 그를 도와 내가 아는 것을 죄다 끌어내게 만들었다. 로라가 직접 이것저것 묻는 바람에, 나로서는 그 물음에 대답하거나 아니면 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 맡아 지키는 사람이라는 아주 달갑지 않고 아주 그릇된 배역을 떠맡거나, 둘 중 하나밖에 길이 없었던 것이다."], ["The end of it was, that, in about ten minutes’ time, the Count knew as much as I know of Mrs. Catherick, and of the events which have so strangely connected us with her daughter, Anne, from the time when Hartright met with her to this day.", "결국 십 분쯤 지났을 때 백작은 캐서릭 부인에 대해, 그리고 하트라이트가 그 딸 앤을 만난 때부터 오늘에 이르기까지 우리를 그토록 기이하게 그 여자와 얽히게 만든 사건들에 대해, 내가 아는 만큼을 알게 되었다."]], [["The effect of my information on him was, in one respect, curious enough.", "내가 알려 준 이야기가 그에게 미친 영향은 한 가지 점에서 제법 기묘했다."]], [["Intimately as he knows Sir Percival, and closely as he appears to be associated with Sir Percival’s private affairs in general, he is certainly as far as I am from knowing anything of the true story of Anne Catherick.", "그는 퍼시벌 경과 그토록 가까운 사이이고, 대체로 퍼시벌 경의 사적인 일들과도 그토록 밀접하게 얽혀 있는 듯 보이건만, 앤 캐서릭의 진짜 내력에 대해서는 분명 나만큼이나 아무것도 모르고 있다."], ["The unsolved mystery in connection with this unhappy woman is now rendered doubly suspicious, in my eyes, by the absolute conviction which I feel, that the clue to it has been hidden by Sir Percival from the most intimate friend he has in the world.", "이 가엾은 여자를 둘러싼 풀리지 않은 수수께끼는, 그 실마리를 퍼시벌 경이 이 세상에서 가장 절친한 벗에게마저 감추어 왔다는 확신이 내 안에서 굳어짐에 따라, 내 눈에는 이제 갑절로 수상쩍게 비친다."], ["It was impossible to mistake the eager curiosity of the Count’s look and manner while he drank in greedily every word that fell from my lips.", "내 입에서 떨어지는 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않고 게걸스레 들이켜는 동안 백작의 눈빛과 태도에 어린 간절한 호기심은 도무지 잘못 볼 수 없는 것이었다. 호기심에도 여러 종류가 있음을 나는 안다."], ["There are many kinds of curiosity, I know—but there is no misinterpreting the curiosity of blank surprise: if I ever saw it in my life I saw it in the Count’s face.", "그러나 아무것도 몰라 그저 놀랐을 때의 호기심만은 달리 해석할 여지가 없다. 내가 평생 그런 호기심을 본 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 백작의 얼굴에서였다."]], [["While the questions and answers were going on, we had all been strolling quietly back through the plantation.", "묻고 답하는 사이에 우리는 모두 조용히 수목원을 가로질러 돌아오고 있었다."], ["As soon as we reached the house the first object that we saw in front of it was Sir Percival’s dog-cart, with the horse put to and the groom waiting by it in his stable-jacket.", "집에 이르자마자 그 앞에서 맨 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 퍼시벌 경의 이륜마차였는데, 말이 이미 매여 있고 마부가 마구간 웃옷 차림으로 그 곁에 서서 기다리고 있었다."], ["If these unexpected appearances were to be trusted, the examination of the house-keeper had produced important results already.", "이 뜻밖의 광경을 믿어도 좋다면, 가정부를 캐물은 일이 이미 중요한 결과를 낳은 셈이었다."]], [["“A fine horse, my friend,”", "“훌륭한 말이로군, 친구.”"], ["said the Count, addressing the groom with the most engaging familiarity of manner, “You are going to drive out?”", "백작이 더할 나위 없이 살갑고 허물없는 태도로 마부에게 말을 건넸다. “마차를 몰고 나갈 참인가?”"]], [["“I am not going, sir,”", "“저는 안 갑니다, 나리.”"], ["replied the man, looking at his stable-jacket, and evidently wondering whether the foreign gentleman took it for his livery.", "사내가 자기 마구간 웃옷을 내려다보며 대답했다. 이 외국 신사가 그 옷을 제복으로 여긴 것은 아닌지 미심쩍어하는 기색이 역력했다."], ["“My master drives himself.”", "“주인 나리께서 손수 모십니다.”"]], [["“Aha!”", "“아하!”"], ["said the Count, “does he indeed?", "백작이 말했다. “정말 그런가?"], ["I wonder he gives himself the trouble when he has got you to drive for him.", "대신 몰아 줄 자네가 있는데도 굳이 수고를 자청하다니 놀랍군."], ["Is he going to fatigue that nice, shining, pretty horse by taking him very far to-day?”", "오늘 저 어여쁘고 윤기 흐르는 좋은 말을 아주 멀리까지 끌고 가서 지치게 할 셈인가?”"]], [["“I don’t know, sir,”", "“모르겠습니다, 나리.”"], ["answered the man.", "사내가 대답했다."], ["“The horse is a mare, if you please, sir.", "“그리고 저 말은 암말입죠, 나리."], ["She’s the highest-couraged thing we’ve got in the stables.", "우리 마구간을 통틀어 기운이 제일 센 놈입니다."], ["Her name’s Brown Molly, sir, and she’ll go till she drops.", "이름은 브라운 몰리인데요, 나리, 쓰러질 때까지 달리는 놈이지요."], ["Sir Percival usually takes Isaac of York for the short distances.”", "퍼시벌 경께서는 가까운 길에는 보통 요크의 아이작을 쓰십니다.”"]], [["“And your shining courageous Brown Molly for the long?”", "“그러면 먼 길에는 그 윤기 흐르고 기운찬 브라운 몰리를 쓴다는 말이지?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“그렇습니다, 나리.”"]], [["“Logical inference, Miss Halcombe,”", "“논리적 추론입니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["continued the Count, wheeling round briskly, and addressing me.", "백작이 몸을 홱 돌려 나를 향하며 말을 이었다."], ["“Sir Percival is going a long distance to-day.”", "“퍼시벌 경은 오늘 먼 길을 가는 것입니다.”"]], [["I made no reply.", "나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["I had my own inferences to draw, from what I knew through the housekeeper and from what I saw before me, and I did not choose to share them with Count Fosco.", "가정부를 통해 알게 된 것과 눈앞에 보이는 것으로부터 나에게는 나대로 끌어낼 추론이 있었고, 그것을 포스코 백작과 나눌 마음은 없었다."]], [["When Sir Percival was in Cumberland (I thought to myself), he walked away a long distance, on Anne’s account, to question the family at Todd’s Corner.", "퍼시벌 경은 컴벌랜드에 있을 때(나는 속으로 생각했다) 앤의 일로 토드 코너의 식구들을 캐물으려고 먼 길을 걸어갔었다."], ["Now he is in Hampshire, is he going to drive away a long distance, on Anne’s account again, to question Mrs. Catherick at Welmingham?", "이제 햄프셔에 와 있는 지금, 또다시 앤의 일로 웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인을 캐물으려고 먼 길을 마차로 달려가려는 것일까?"]], [["We all entered the house.", "우리는 모두 집 안으로 들어갔다."], ["As we crossed the hall Sir Percival came out from the library to meet us.", "현관 홀을 가로지르는데 퍼시벌 경이 서재에서 나와 우리를 맞았다."], ["He looked hurried and pale and anxious—but for all that, he was in his most polite mood when he spoke to us.", "그는 서두르는 기색에 얼굴이 창백하고 불안해 보였다. 그럼에도 우리에게 말을 걸 때만큼은 더없이 정중한 태도였다."]], [["“I am sorry to say I am obliged to leave you,”", "“안됐지만 여러분 곁을 떠나야겠습니다.”"], ["he began—“a long drive—a matter that I can’t very well put off.", "그가 입을 열었다. “먼 길이라—도저히 미룰 수 없는 일이 생겼어요."], ["I shall be back in good time to-morrow—but before I go I should like that little business-formality, which I spoke of this morning, to be settled.", "내일 이른 시간에는 돌아오겠습니다. 다만 떠나기 전에, 오늘 아침에 말씀드린 그 사소한 사무 절차를 매듭짓고 싶군요."], ["Laura, will you come into the library?", "로라, 서재로 들어와 주겠소?"], ["It won’t take a minute—a mere formality.", "잠깐이면 됩니다, 그저 형식일 뿐이니."], ["Countess, may I trouble you also?", "백작부인, 부인께도 폐를 좀 끼쳐도 될까요?"], ["I want you and the Countess, Fosco, to be witnesses to a signature—nothing more.", "포스코, 자네와 백작부인이 서명의 증인이 되어 주었으면 하네. 그뿐일세."], ["Come in at once and get it over.”", "어서들 들어와서 끝내 버립시다.”"]], [["He held the library door open until they had passed in, followed them, and shut it softly.", "그는 그들이 다 들어갈 때까지 서재 문을 잡고 있다가, 뒤따라 들어가서는 조용히 문을 닫았다."]], [["I remained, for a moment afterwards, standing alone in the hall, with my heart beating fast and my mind misgiving me sadly.", "나는 그 뒤로도 잠시 동안 홀에 혼자 선 채로 있었다. 가슴은 빠르게 뛰었고 마음에는 서글픈 불안이 밀려들었다."], ["Then I went on to the staircase, and ascended slowly to my own room.", "이윽고 나는 계단 쪽으로 걸어가, 천천히 내 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["June 17th.—Just as my hand was on the door of my room, I heard Sir Percival’s voice calling to me from below.", "6월 17일.—내 손이 막 방문 손잡이에 닿았을 때, 아래층에서 나를 부르는 퍼시벌 경의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“I must beg you to come downstairs again,”", "“죄송하지만 다시 아래층으로 내려와 주셔야겠습니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It is Fosco’s fault, Miss Halcombe, not mine.", "“제 탓이 아니라 포스코 탓입니다, 홀콤 양."], ["He has started some nonsensical objection to his wife being one of the witnesses, and has obliged me to ask you to join us in the library.”", "이 사람이 제 아내가 증인 가운데 하나가 되는 것을 두고 말도 안 되는 이의를 내놓는 바람에, 서재로 함께 와 주십사 청하지 않을 수 없게 되었어요.”"]], [["I entered the room immediately with Sir Percival.", "나는 퍼시벌 경과 함께 곧바로 방으로 들어갔다."], ["Laura was waiting by the writing-table, twisting and turning her garden hat uneasily in her hands.", "로라는 필기용 탁자 옆에서 정원용 모자를 불안하게 손에 쥐고 이리저리 비틀며 기다리고 있었다."], ["Madame Fosco sat near her, in an arm-chair, imperturbably admiring her husband, who stood by himself at the other end of the library, picking off the dead leaves from the flowers in the window.", "포스코 백작부인은 그 곁 안락의자에 앉아 태연한 얼굴로 제 남편을 흠모하듯 바라보고 있었고, 백작은 서재 저쪽 끝에 혼자 서서 창가 화분의 시든 잎을 따내는 중이었다."]], [["The moment I appeared the Count advanced to meet me, and to offer his explanations.", "내가 모습을 보이자마자 백작은 나를 맞으러 다가와 해명을 늘어놓았다."]], [["“A thousand pardons, Miss Halcombe,” he said.", "“천 번이고 용서를 구합니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["“You know the character which is given to my countrymen by the English?", "그가 말했다. “영국인들이 제 동포에게 어떤 평판을 붙여 주는지 아시지요?"], ["We Italians are all wily and suspicious by nature, in the estimation of the good John Bull.", "선량한 존 불의 눈에 우리 이탈리아인은 하나같이 교활하고 의심 많은 족속입니다."], ["Set me down, if you please, as being no better than the rest of my race.", "부디 저 또한 제 동족보다 나을 것 없는 인간으로 여겨 주십시오."], ["I am a wily Italian and a suspicious Italian.", "저는 교활한 이탈리아인이요, 의심 많은 이탈리아인입니다."], ["You have thought so yourself, dear lady, have you not? Well!", "당신께서도 그렇게 생각하셨겠지요, 친애하는 홀콤 양, 아니십니까?"], ["it is part of my wiliness and part of my suspicion to object to Madame Fosco being a witness to Lady Glyde’s signature, when I am also a witness myself.”", "그런데 말입니다! 제가 증인으로 나서는 마당에 포스코 부인까지 글라이드 부인의 서명에 증인이 되는 것을 반대하는 것, 바로 그것이 제 교활함의 일부이자 제 의심의 일부랍니다.”"]], [["“There is not the shadow of a reason for his objection,”", "“그가 반대할 근거라고는 그림자만큼도 없습니다.”"], ["interposed Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 끼어들었다."], ["“I have explained to him that the law of England allows Madame Fosco to witness a signature as well as her husband.”", "“영국 법에서는 포스코 부인도 그 남편과 마찬가지로 서명의 증인이 될 수 있다고 이 사람에게 설명해 주었는데도 말입니다.”"]], [["“I admit it,”", "“인정하네.”"], ["resumed the Count.", "백작이 말을 이었다."], ["“The law of England says, Yes, but the conscience of Fosco says, No.”", "“영국 법은 그렇다고 말하지만, 포스코의 양심은 아니라고 말하지.”"], ["He spread out his fat fingers on the bosom of his blouse, and bowed solemnly, as if he wished to introduce his conscience to us all, in the character of an illustrious addition to the society.", "그는 통통한 손가락을 블라우스 가슴팍에 활짝 펴 얹고는, 마치 제 양심을 이 자리에 새로 더해진 빛나는 일원으로 우리 모두에게 소개하려는 듯 엄숙하게 절을 했다. “글라이드 부인이 서명하려는 이 문서가 대체 무엇인지,”"], ["“What this document which Lady Glyde is about to sign may be,”", "그가 말을 이었다, “저는 알지도 못하고 알고 싶지도 않습니다."], ["he continued, “I neither know nor desire to know.", "제가 드리는 말씀은 이것뿐입니다."], ["I only say this, circumstances may happen in the future which may oblige Percival, or his representatives, to appeal to the two witnesses, in which case it is certainly desirable that those witnesses should represent two opinions which are perfectly independent the one of the other.", "훗날 퍼시벌이나 그의 대리인들이 두 증인에게 확인을 구해야 할 사정이 생길 수도 있지요. 그런 경우라면 두 증인이 서로 완전히 독립된 두 개의 견해를 대표하는 편이 분명 바람직합니다."], ["This cannot be if my wife signs as well as myself, because we have but one opinion between us, and that opinion is mine.", "그런데 제 아내가 저와 나란히 서명한다면 그럴 수가 없습니다. 우리 둘 사이에는 견해가 하나밖에 없고, 그 견해란 곧 제 것이니까요."], ["I will not have it cast in my teeth, at some future day, that Madame Fosco acted under my coercion, and was, in plain fact, no witness at all.", "훗날 어느 날엔가 포스코 부인이 제 강압 아래 움직였을 뿐이며 사실상 증인이랄 것도 못 되었다는 말을 얼굴에 대고 듣고 싶지는 않습니다."], ["I speak in Percival’s interest, when I propose that my name shall appear (as the nearest friend of the husband), and your name, Miss Halcombe (as the nearest friend of the wife).", "제 이름이 (남편의 가장 가까운 벗으로서) 오르고 홀콤 양 당신의 이름이 (아내의 가장 가까운 벗으로서) 오르도록 하자고 제안하는 것은 어디까지나 퍼시벌을 위해서입니다."], ["I am a Jesuit, if you please to think so—a splitter of straws—a man of trifles and crochets and scruples—but you will humour me, I hope, in merciful consideration for my suspicious Italian character, and my uneasy Italian conscience.”", "저를 예수회 수사라 여기셔도 좋습니다—지푸라기나 쪼개는 사람이라고—사소한 것과 변덕과 양심의 가책에 매달리는 사내라고—그래도 의심 많은 이탈리아인 기질과 편치 못한 이탈리아인 양심을 너그러이 헤아리시어 부디 제 뜻을 받아 주시기 바랍니다.”"], ["He bowed again, stepped back a few paces, and withdrew his conscience from our society as politely as he had introduced it.", "그는 다시 절하고 몇 걸음 물러서더니, 소개할 때만큼이나 정중하게 제 양심을 우리 자리에서 물렸다."]], [["The Count’s scruples might have been honourable and reasonable enough, but there was something in his manner of expressing them which increased my unwillingness to be concerned in the business of the signature.", "백작이 말한 그 양심의 가책이란 것은 어쩌면 명예롭고 지당한 것이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것을 표현하는 그의 태도에는 이 서명이라는 일에 관여하고 싶지 않은 내 마음을 한층 키우는 무언가가 있었다."], ["No consideration of less importance than my consideration for Laura would have induced me to consent to be a witness at all.", "로라를 생각하는 마음보다 덜 중요한 어떤 이유로도 나는 증인이 되겠다고 승낙하지 않았을 것이다."], ["One look, however, at her anxious face decided me to risk anything rather than desert her.", "그러나 근심에 찬 그녀의 얼굴을 한 번 보는 것만으로, 나는 그녀를 저버리느니 무엇이든 무릅쓰기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["“I will readily remain in the room,”", "“기꺼이 이 방에 남겠습니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “And if I find no reason for starting any small scruples on my side, you may rely on me as a witness.”", "“그리고 제 쪽에서 사소한 가책이나마 제기할 이유를 찾지 못한다면, 증인으로서 저를 믿으셔도 좋습니다.”"]], [["Sir Percival looked at me sharply, as if he was about to say something.", "퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 나를 날카롭게 쳐다보았다."], ["But at the same moment, Madame Fosco attracted his attention by rising from her chair.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 포스코 부인이 의자에서 일어서며 그의 주의를 끌었다."], ["She had caught her husband’s eye, and had evidently received her orders to leave the room.", "그녀는 남편과 눈이 마주쳤고, 방을 나가라는 지시를 받은 것이 분명했다."]], [["“You needn’t go,”", "“나가실 것 없습니다.”"], ["said Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말했다."]], [["Madame Fosco looked for her orders again, got them again, said she would prefer leaving us to our business, and resolutely walked out.", "포스코 부인은 다시 한번 남편의 지시를 구했고, 다시 한번 그것을 받았으며, 우리끼리 일을 보도록 자리를 비켜 주는 편이 좋겠다고 말하고는 단호히 걸어 나갔다."], ["The Count lit a cigarette, went back to the flowers in the window, and puffed little jets of smoke at the leaves, in a state of the deepest anxiety about killing the insects.", "백작은 담배에 불을 붙이고 창가의 꽃들에게로 돌아가, 벌레를 죽이지나 않을까 심히 걱정하는 얼굴로 잎사귀에 대고 자잘한 연기를 뿜어 댔다."]], [["Meanwhile Sir Percival unlocked a cupboard beneath one of the book-cases, and produced from it a piece of parchment, folded longwise, many times over.", "그러는 동안 퍼시벌 경은 책장 하나 아래의 벽장을 열고, 세로로 여러 번 접힌 양피지 한 장을 꺼냈다."], ["He placed it on the table, opened the last fold only, and kept his hand on the rest.", "그는 그것을 탁자 위에 놓고 맨 마지막으로 접힌 자락만 펼친 뒤 나머지에는 손을 얹은 채로 두었다."], ["The last fold displayed a strip of blank parchment with little wafers stuck on it at certain places.", "펼쳐진 마지막 자락에는 아무것도 쓰이지 않은 양피지 띠가 드러났고, 군데군데 작은 봉인 딱지가 붙어 있었다."], ["Every line of the writing was hidden in the part which he still held folded up under his hand.", "글씨가 쓰인 줄은 하나도 남김없이, 그가 여전히 접은 채 손으로 누르고 있는 부분에 감추어져 있었다."], ["Laura and I looked at each other.", "로라와 나는 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Her face was pale, but it showed no indecision and no fear.", "그녀의 얼굴은 창백했으나 망설임도 두려움도 비치지 않았다."]], [["Sir Percival dipped a pen in ink, and handed it to his wife.", "퍼시벌 경은 펜을 잉크에 적셔 아내에게 건넸다."], ["“Sign your name there,”", "“여기에 이름을 쓰시오.”"], ["he said, pointing to the place.", "그가 자리를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“You and Fosco are to sign afterwards, Miss Halcombe, opposite those two wafers.", "“홀콤 양, 당신과 포스코는 그다음에 저 두 봉인 딱지 맞은편에 서명하시면 됩니다."], ["Come here, Fosco!", "이리 오게, 포스코!"], ["witnessing a signature is not to be done by mooning out of window and smoking into the flowers.”", "서명의 증인이 되는 일은 창밖이나 멍하니 내다보며 꽃에다 연기를 뿜어서 되는 게 아닐세.”"]], [["The Count threw away his cigarette, and joined us at the table, with his hands carelessly thrust into the scarlet belt of his blouse, and his eyes steadily fixed on Sir Percival’s face.", "백작은 담배를 던져 버리고 두 손을 블라우스의 진홍색 허리띠에 아무렇게나 찔러 넣은 채, 시선은 퍼시벌 경의 얼굴에 붙박은 채로 탁자로 다가왔다."], ["Laura, who was on the other side of her husband, with the pen in her hand, looked at him too.", "남편 반대편에서 펜을 손에 든 로라 역시 그를 바라보았다."], ["He stood between them holding the folded parchment down firmly on the table, and glancing across at me, as I sat opposite to him, with such a sinister mixture of suspicion and embarrassment on his face that he looked more like a prisoner at the bar than a gentleman in his own house.", "그는 두 사람 사이에 서서 접힌 양피지를 탁자에 단단히 눌러 놓고, 맞은편에 앉은 나를 힐끗 건너다보았다. 그 얼굴에는 의심과 당혹이 어찌나 음험하게 뒤섞여 있던지, 제 집에 있는 신사라기보다 피고석에 선 죄수처럼 보였다."]], [["“Sign there,”", "“거기에 서명하시오.”"], ["he repeated, turning suddenly on Laura, and pointing once more to the place on the parchment.", "그가 불쑥 로라 쪽으로 돌아서서 양피지의 그 자리를 다시 한번 가리키며 되풀이했다."]], [["“What is it I am to sign?”", "“제가 서명해야 하는 것이 무엇인가요?”"], ["she asked quietly.", "그녀가 조용히 물었다."]], [["“I have no time to explain,”", "“설명할 시간이 없소.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“The dog-cart is at the door, and I must go directly.", "“마차가 문 앞에 와 있고 나는 곧장 떠나야 하오."], ["Besides, if I had time, you wouldn’t understand.", "게다가 시간이 있다 한들 당신은 이해하지 못할 거요."], ["It is a purely formal document, full of legal technicalities, and all that sort of thing.", "순전히 형식적인 문서요, 법률 용어니 뭐니 그런 것투성이란 말이오."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["come!", "어서!"], ["sign your name, and let us have done as soon as possible.”", "이름을 쓰고 되도록 빨리 끝냅시다.”"]], [["“I ought surely to know what I am signing, Sir Percival, before I write my name?”", "“그래도 이름을 쓰기 전에 제가 무엇에 서명하는지는 알아야 마땅하지 않을까요, 퍼시벌 경?”"]], [["“Nonsense!", "“쓸데없는 소리!"], ["What have women to do with business?", "여자가 사업에 무슨 상관이오?"], ["I tell you again, you can’t understand it.”", "다시 말하지만 당신은 이해 못 하오.”"]], [["“At any rate, let me try to understand it. Whenever Mr.", "“그래도 이해해 보려는 것쯤은 하게 해 주세요."], ["Gilmore had any business for me to do, he always explained it first, and I always understood him.”", "길모어 씨는 제가 해야 할 일이 있을 때면 언제나 먼저 설명해 주셨고, 저는 언제나 알아들었어요.”"]], [["“I dare say he did.", "“그랬겠지."], ["He was your servant, and was obliged to explain.", "그자는 당신의 고용인이었으니 설명할 의무가 있었소."], ["I am your husband, and am NOT obliged.", "나는 당신 남편이니 그럴 의무가 없소."], ["How much longer do you mean to keep me here?", "대체 언제까지 나를 여기 붙들어 둘 셈이오?"], ["I tell you again, there is no time for reading anything—the dog-cart is waiting at the door.", "다시 말하지만 무얼 읽을 시간 따위는 없소—마차가 문 앞에서 기다린단 말이오."], ["Once for all, will you sign or will you not?”", "마지막으로 묻겠소, 서명하겠소, 하지 않겠소?”"]], [["She still had the pen in her hand, but she made no approach to signing her name with it.", "그녀는 여전히 펜을 손에 쥐고 있었지만, 그것으로 이름을 쓰려는 기색은 조금도 보이지 않았다."]], [["“If my signature pledges me to anything,”", "“제 서명이 저를 무언가에 묶어 놓는 것이라면,”"], ["she said, “surely I have some claim to know what that pledge is?”", "그녀가 말했다, “그 묶임이 무엇인지 알 권리쯤은 제게 있지 않겠어요?”"]], [["He lifted up the parchment, and struck it angrily on the table.", "그는 양피지를 들어 올려 성난 손길로 탁자를 내리쳤다."]], [["“Speak out!” he said.", "“터놓고 말하시오!”"], ["“You were always famous for telling the truth.", "그가 말했다. “당신은 늘 진실을 말하기로 이름난 사람이었지."], ["Never mind Miss Halcombe, never mind Fosco—say, in plain terms, you distrust me.”", "홀콤 양도 아랑곳 말고 포스코도 아랑곳 말고—단도직입으로 말해 보시오, 나를 믿지 못하겠다고.”"]], [["The Count took one of his hands out of his belt and laid it on Sir Percival’s shoulder.", "백작이 허리띠에서 한 손을 빼내어 퍼시벌 경의 어깨에 얹었다."], ["Sir Percival shook it off irritably.", "퍼시벌 경은 짜증스럽게 그 손을 떨쳐 냈다."], ["The Count put it on again with unruffled composure.", "백작은 조금도 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 다시 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Control your unfortunate temper, Percival,”", "“그 못된 성미를 좀 다스리게, 퍼시벌.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “Lady Glyde is right.”", "“글라이드 부인의 말씀이 옳으이.”"]], [["“Right!”", "“옳다고!”"], ["cried Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다."], ["“A wife right in distrusting her husband!”", "“남편을 믿지 못하는 아내가 옳다는 건가!”"]], [["“It is unjust and cruel to accuse me of distrusting you,”", "“당신을 믿지 못한다고 저를 몰아세우시는 건 부당하고 잔인해요.”"], ["said Laura.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“Ask Marian if I am not justified in wanting to know what this writing requires of me before I sign it.”", "“이 문서가 제게 무엇을 요구하는지 서명하기 전에 알고 싶어 하는 것이 부당한 일인지, 매리언에게 물어보세요.”"]], [["“I won’t have any appeals made to Miss Halcombe,”", "“홀콤 양에게 호소하는 건 용납할 수 없소.”"], ["retorted Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 쏘아붙였다."], ["“Miss Halcombe has nothing to do with the matter.”", "“홀콤 양은 이 일과 아무 상관이 없소.”"]], [["I had not spoken hitherto, and I would much rather not have spoken now.", "나는 그때까지 한마디도 하지 않았고, 지금도 되도록 말하고 싶지 않았다."], ["But the expression of distress in Laura’s face when she turned it towards me, and the insolent injustice of her husband’s conduct, left me no other alternative than to give my opinion, for her sake, as soon as I was asked for it.", "그러나 나를 향해 돌린 로라의 얼굴에 서린 괴로움과 그 남편의 무례하고 부당한 처사 앞에서, 의견을 요청받은 이상 그녀를 위해 내 생각을 말하는 것 말고는 달리 길이 없었다."]], [["“Excuse me, Sir Percival,”", "“실례합니다만, 퍼시벌 경.”"], ["I said—“but as one of the witnesses to the signature, I venture to think that I HAVE something to do with the matter.", "내가 말했다. “서명의 증인 가운데 한 사람으로서, 저는 이 일과 상관이 있다고 감히 생각합니다."], ["Laura’s objection seems to me a perfectly fair one, and speaking for myself only, I cannot assume the responsibility of witnessing her signature, unless she first understands what the writing is which you wish her to sign.”", "로라의 이의는 제가 보기에 지극히 온당하고, 저 자신만을 두고 말씀드리자면, 서명하기를 바라시는 그 문서가 무엇인지 로라가 먼저 이해하지 못하는 한 저는 그 서명의 증인이 되는 책임을 떠맡을 수 없습니다.”"]], [["“A cool declaration, upon my soul!”", "“참으로 태연한 선언이로군!”"], ["cried Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다."], ["“The next time you invite yourself to a man’s house, Miss Halcombe, I recommend you not to repay his hospitality by taking his wife’s side against him in a matter that doesn’t concern you.”", "“다음번에 남의 집에 스스로 찾아오실 때에는, 홀콤 양, 상관도 없는 일에 그 집 아내 편을 들어 남편에게 맞서는 것으로 대접을 갚지는 마시기를 권합니다.”"]], [["I started to my feet as suddenly as if he had struck me.", "나는 그가 나를 때리기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["If I had been a man, I would have knocked him down on the threshold of his own door, and have left his house, never on any earthly consideration to enter it again.", "내가 남자였다면 나는 제 집 문지방에서 그를 때려눕히고 그 집을 떠나, 세상 어떤 사정이 있어도 두 번 다시 그 문턱을 넘지 않았을 것이다."], ["But I was only a woman—and I loved his wife so dearly!", "그러나 나는 한낱 여자였고—그리고 나는 그의 아내를 그토록 깊이 사랑했다!"]], [["Thank God, that faithful love helped me, and I sat down again without saying a word.", "고맙게도 그 변함없는 사랑이 나를 붙들어 주어, 나는 한마디도 하지 않고 다시 자리에 앉았다."], ["SHE knew what I had suffered and what I had suppressed.", "그녀는 내가 무엇을 견디었고 무엇을 억눌렀는지 알고 있었다."], ["She ran round to me, with the tears streaming from her eyes.", "그녀는 눈에서 눈물을 줄줄 흘리며 탁자를 돌아 내게로 달려왔다."], ["“Oh, Marian!”", "“오, 매리언!”"], ["she whispered softly.", "그녀가 나직이 속삭였다."], ["“If my mother had been alive, she could have done no more for me!”", "“어머니가 살아 계셨더라도 이보다 더해 주시지는 못했을 거야!”"]], [["“Come back and sign!”", "“돌아와서 서명하시오!”"], ["cried Sir Percival from the other side of the table.", "탁자 저편에서 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Shall I?”", "“서명할까?”"], ["she asked in my ear; “I will, if you tell me.”", "그녀가 내 귀에 대고 물었다. “언니가 하라고 하면 할게.”"]], [["“No,” I answered.", "“안 돼.”"], ["“The right and the truth are with you—sign nothing, unless you have read it first.”", "나는 대답했다. “옳음도 진실도 네 편이야—먼저 읽어 보지 않은 것에는 아무것도 서명하지 마.”"]], [["“Come back and sign!”", "“돌아와서 서명하란 말이오!”"], ["he reiterated, in his loudest and angriest tones.", "그가 가장 크고 가장 성난 목소리로 되풀이했다."]], [["The Count, who had watched Laura and me with a close and silent attention, interposed for the second time.", "로라와 나를 가까이서 말없이 지켜보고 있던 백작이 두 번째로 끼어들었다."]], [["“Percival!”", "“퍼시벌!”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I remember that I am in the presence of ladies.", "“나는 지금 숙녀분들 앞에 있다는 것을 잊지 않고 있네."], ["Be good enough, if you please, to remember it too.”", "부디 자네도 그것을 잊지 말아 주게.”"]], [["Sir Percival turned on him speechless with passion.", "퍼시벌 경이 분노로 말문이 막힌 채 그를 향해 홱 돌아섰다."], ["The Count’s firm hand slowly tightened its grasp on his shoulder, and the Count’s steady voice quietly repeated, “Be good enough, if you please, to remember it too.”", "백작의 굳센 손이 천천히 그의 어깨를 조여 왔고, 백작의 흔들림 없는 목소리가 조용히 되풀이했다. “부디 자네도 그것을 잊지 말아 주게.”"]], [["They both looked at each other.", "두 사람은 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["Sir Percival slowly drew his shoulder from under the Count’s hand, slowly turned his face away from the Count’s eyes, doggedly looked down for a little while at the parchment on the table, and then spoke, with the sullen submission of a tamed animal, rather than the becoming resignation of a convinced man.", "퍼시벌 경은 천천히 백작의 손 아래에서 어깨를 빼내고, 천천히 백작의 눈길에서 얼굴을 돌리고, 잠시 완강하게 탁자 위의 양피지를 내려다보다가 입을 열었다. 납득한 사람의 온당한 승복이라기보다는 길들여진 짐승의 뚱한 복종에 가까운 말투였다."]], [["“I don’t want to offend anybody,”", "“나는 누구를 모욕할 생각은 없소.”"], ["he said, “but my wife’s obstinacy is enough to try the patience of a saint.", "그가 말했다. “하지만 내 아내의 고집은 성인의 인내심이라도 시험할 만하오."], ["I have told her this is merely a formal document—and what more can she want?", "이건 그저 형식적인 문서라고 이미 말했는데 더 무엇을 바란단 말이오?"], ["You may say what you please, but it is no part of a woman’s duty to set her husband at defiance.", "뭐라고들 하시든 좋소만, 남편에게 대드는 것이 아내의 도리일 수는 없소."], ["Once more, Lady Glyde, and for the last time, will you sign or will you not?”", "글라이드 부인, 다시 한번 그리고 이번이 마지막이오—서명하겠소, 하지 않겠소?”"]], [["Laura returned to his side of the table, and took up the pen again.", "로라는 그가 있는 쪽 탁자로 돌아가 다시 펜을 들었다."]], [["“I will sign with pleasure,”", "“기꺼이 서명하겠어요.”"], ["she said, “if you will only treat me as a responsible being.", "그녀가 말했다. “저를 책임질 줄 아는 사람으로 대해 주시기만 한다면요."], ["I care little what sacrifice is required of me, if it will affect no one else, and lead to no ill results—”", "다른 누구에게도 해가 가지 않고 나쁜 결과로 이어지지만 않는다면, 제게 어떤 희생이 요구되든 저는 개의치 않아요—”"]], [["“Who talked of a sacrifice being required of you?”", "“누가 당신에게 희생을 요구한다고 했소?”"], ["he broke in, with a half-suppressed return of his former violence.", "그가 아까의 격정을 반쯤 억누른 채 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“I only meant,”", "“제 말은 다만,”"], ["she resumed, “that I would refuse no concession which I could honourably make.", "그녀가 이어 말했다, “떳떳하게 양보할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇도 거절하지 않겠다는 뜻이었어요."], ["If I have a scruple about signing my name to an engagement of which I know nothing, why should you visit it on me so severely?", "제가 무엇인지도 모르는 약정에 이름을 쓰는 일을 두고 꺼림칙해한다면, 어째서 그것을 그토록 심하게 나무라시나요?"], ["It is rather hard, I think, to treat Count Fosco’s scruples so much more indulgently than you have treated mine.”", "포스코 백작의 가책은 그토록 너그럽게 받아 주시면서 제 가책은 그렇게 대하시는 건, 제가 보기에 좀 가혹한 것 같아요.”"]], [["This unfortunate, yet most natural, reference to the Count’s extraordinary power over her husband, indirect as it was, set Sir Percival’s smouldering temper on fire again in an instant.", "에두른 것이기는 했으나 남편에 대한 백작의 남다른 영향력을 건드린 이 불행하면서도 더없이 자연스러운 언급은, 사그라들던 퍼시벌 경의 성미에 순식간에 다시 불을 붙였다."]], [["“Scruples!”", "“가책이라!”"], ["he repeated.", "그가 되풀이했다."], ["“YOUR scruples!", "“당신의 가책이라니!"], ["It is rather late in the day for you to be scrupulous.", "이제 와서 꺼림칙하다는 소리를 하기에는 좀 늦었소."], ["I should have thought you had got over all weakness of that sort, when you made a virtue of necessity by marrying me.”", "어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 삼아 나와 결혼했을 때, 그런 종류의 나약함 따위는 이미 다 넘어선 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["The instant he spoke those words, Laura threw down the pen—looked at him with an expression in her eyes which, throughout all my experience of her, I had never seen in them before, and turned her back on him in dead silence.", "그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지는 순간, 로라는 펜을 내던지고—내가 그녀를 알아 온 모든 세월 동안 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정으로 그를 바라보더니—죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 그에게 등을 돌렸다."]], [["This strong expression of the most open and the most bitter contempt was so entirely unlike herself, so utterly out of her character, that it silenced us all.", "이토록 노골적이고 이토록 신랄한 경멸의 강렬한 표현은 도무지 그녀답지 않았고 그녀의 성품과 너무나 동떨어진 것이어서, 우리 모두를 침묵하게 만들었다."], ["There was something hidden, beyond a doubt, under the mere surface-brutality of the words which her husband had just addressed to her.", "남편이 방금 그녀에게 던진 말의 그 표면적인 야비함 아래에는 분명 무언가 감추어진 것이 있었다."], ["There was some lurking insult beneath them, of which I was wholly ignorant, but which had left the mark of its profanation so plainly on her face that even a stranger might have seen it.", "그 밑에는 내가 전혀 알지 못하는 어떤 모욕이 도사리고 있었으되, 그것이 남긴 능욕의 자국은 낯선 사람이라도 알아볼 만큼 그녀의 얼굴에 뚜렷했다."]], [["The Count, who was no stranger, saw it as distinctly as I did.", "낯선 사람이 아니었던 백작은 나만큼이나 똑똑히 그것을 보았다."], ["When I left my chair to join Laura, I heard him whisper under his breath to Sir Percival, “You idiot!”", "로라에게 가려고 내가 의자에서 일어섰을 때, 나는 그가 퍼시벌 경에게 숨죽여 속삭이는 소리를 들었다. “이 바보 같으니!”"]], [["Laura walked before me to the door as I advanced, and at the same time her husband spoke to her once more.", "내가 다가가자 로라는 나보다 앞서 문 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 바로 그때 그녀의 남편이 다시 한번 그녀에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“You positively refuse, then, to give me your signature?”", "“그러니까 내게 서명해 줄 수 없다, 이 말이오?”"], ["he said, in the altered tone of a man who was conscious that he had let his own licence of language seriously injure him.", "그가 물었다. 제 입에서 나온 방자한 말이 스스로에게 심각한 손해를 입혔음을 자각한 사람의 달라진 어조였다."]], [["“After what you have just said to me,”", "“방금 제게 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니,”"], ["she replied firmly, “I refuse my signature until I have read every line in that parchment from the first word to the last.", "그녀가 단호히 대답했다, “저 양피지의 첫 글자부터 마지막 글자까지 한 줄도 빠짐없이 읽기 전에는 서명하지 않겠어요."], ["Come away, Marian, we have remained here long enough.”", "가요, 매리언, 우리는 여기 충분히 오래 있었어요.”"]], [["“One moment!”", "“잠깐만요!”"], ["interposed the Count before Sir Percival could speak again—“one moment, Lady Glyde, I implore you!”", "퍼시벌 경이 다시 입을 열기 전에 백작이 끼어들었다—“잠깐만요, 글라이드 부인, 간청드립니다!”"]], [["Laura would have left the room without noticing him, but I stopped her.", "로라는 그를 본체만체하고 방을 나가려 했으나, 내가 그녀를 붙들었다."]], [["“Don’t make an enemy of the Count!”", "“백작을 적으로 만들면 안 돼!”"], ["I whispered.", "나는 속삭였다."], ["“Whatever you do, don’t make an enemy of the Count!”", "“무슨 일이 있어도 백작을 적으로 만들지는 마!”"]], [["She yielded to me.", "그녀는 내 말에 따랐다."], ["I closed the door again, and we stood near it waiting.", "나는 다시 문을 닫았고, 우리는 그 곁에 서서 기다렸다."], ["Sir Percival sat down at the table, with his elbow on the folded parchment, and his head resting on his clenched fist.", "퍼시벌 경은 탁자에 앉아 접힌 양피지 위에 팔꿈치를 얹고 움켜쥔 주먹에 머리를 기댔다."], ["The Count stood between us—master of the dreadful position in which we were placed, as he was master of everything else.", "백작은 우리 사이에 서 있었다—다른 모든 것의 주인이듯, 우리가 놓인 이 끔찍한 처지의 주인으로서."]], [["“Lady Glyde,”", "“글라이드 부인.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, with a gentleness which seemed to address itself to our forlorn situation instead of to ourselves, “pray pardon me if I venture to offer one suggestion, and pray believe that I speak out of my profound respect and my friendly regard for the mistress of this house.”", "우리 자신이 아니라 우리의 가련한 처지를 향한 듯한 부드러움이 담긴 목소리였다. “제가 감히 한 가지 제안을 드리더라도 부디 용서해 주시고, 이 집 안주인께 품은 깊은 존경과 우정 어린 마음에서 드리는 말씀임을 믿어 주십시오.”"], ["He turned sharply towards Sir Percival.", "그는 홱 퍼시벌 경 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["“Is it absolutely necessary,”", "“묻겠네.” 그가 말했다."], ["he asked, “that this thing here, under your elbow, should be signed to-day?”", "“자네 팔꿈치 밑에 있는 저것에 반드시 오늘 서명을 받아야만 하나?”"]], [["“It is necessary to my plans and wishes,”", "“내 계획과 바람에는 그래야 하네.”"], ["returned the other sulkily.", "상대가 뚱하게 대꾸했다."], ["“But that consideration, as you may have noticed, has no influence with Lady Glyde.”", "“하지만 자네도 보았듯이 그런 사정은 글라이드 부인에게 아무 힘도 없더군.”"]], [["“Answer my plain question plainly.", "“내 단순한 물음에 단순하게 답하게."], ["Can the business of the signature be put off till to-morrow—Yes or No?”", "서명하는 일을 내일로 미룰 수 있나, 없나?”"]], [["“Yes, if you will have it so.”", "“그러라면야 그럴 수 있지.”"]], [["“Then what are you wasting your time for here?", "“그렇다면 여기서 무엇하러 시간을 허비하고 있나?"], ["Let the signature wait till to-morrow—let it wait till you come back.”", "서명은 내일까지 기다리게 하게—자네가 돌아올 때까지 기다리게 해.”"]], [["Sir Percival looked up with a frown and an oath.", "퍼시벌 경이 이맛살을 찌푸리고 욕설을 뱉으며 고개를 들었다."]], [["“You are taking a tone with me that I don’t like,”", "“자네가 내게 쓰는 그 말투는 마음에 들지 않아.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“A tone I won’t bear from any man.”", "“어떤 사내에게서도 참아 줄 수 없는 말투야.”"]], [["“I am advising you for your good,”", "“나는 자네를 위해 충고하는 걸세.”"], ["returned the Count, with a smile of quiet contempt.", "백작이 조용한 경멸의 미소를 띠고 대꾸했다."], ["“Give yourself time—give Lady Glyde time.", "“시간을 좀 두게—글라이드 부인께도 시간을 드리고."], ["Have you forgotten that your dog-cart is waiting at the door?", "마차가 문 앞에서 기다리는 걸 잊었나?"], ["My tone surprises you—ha?", "내 말투가 놀랍다고—허?"], ["I dare say it does—it is the tone of a man who can keep his temper.", "그렇겠지—이건 제 성미를 다스릴 줄 아는 사내의 말투일세."], ["How many doses of good advice have I given you in my time?", "내가 그동안 자네에게 좋은 충고를 몇 첩이나 처방해 주었던가?"], ["More than you can count.", "셀 수 없을 만큼일세."], ["Have I ever been wrong?", "내가 한 번이라도 틀린 적이 있던가?"], ["I defy you to quote me an instance of it. Go!", "한 번이라도 있거든 대 보게."], ["take your drive.", "가게! 다녀오게."], ["The matter of the signature can wait till to-morrow.", "서명 문제는 내일까지 기다려도 되네."], ["Let it wait—and renew it when you come back.”", "기다리게 두고—돌아와서 다시 꺼내게.”"]], [["Sir Percival hesitated and looked at his watch.", "퍼시벌 경은 망설이며 시계를 보았다."], ["His anxiety about the secret journey which he was to take that day, revived by the Count’s words, was now evidently disputing possession of his mind with his anxiety to obtain Laura’s signature.", "그날 떠나야 할 그 은밀한 여정에 대한 조바심이 백작의 말에 되살아나, 로라의 서명을 받아 내려는 조바심과 그의 마음을 두고 다투고 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["e considered for a little while, and then got up from his chair.", "그는 잠시 궁리하다가 의자에서 일어섰다."]], [["“It is easy to argue me down,” he said, “when I have no time to answer you.", "“내게 답할 시간이 없을 때에는 말로 나를 눌러 이기기가 쉽겠지.”"], ["I will take your advice, Fosco—not because I want it, or believe in it, but because I can’t stop here any longer.”", "그가 말했다. “자네 충고를 따르겠네, 포스코—그게 필요해서도 아니고 그걸 믿어서도 아니라, 여기 더 붙들려 있을 수 없어서야.”"], ["He paused, and looked round darkly at his wife.", "그는 말을 멈추고 침울한 눈길로 아내를 돌아보았다."], ["“If you don’t give me your signature when I come back to-morrow—!”", "“내일 돌아왔을 때 서명해 주지 않는다면—!”"], ["The rest was lost in the noise of his opening the book-case cupboard again, and locking up the parchment once more.", "그 뒤의 말은 그가 다시 책장 벽장을 열고 양피지를 도로 넣어 잠그는 소리에 묻혀 버렸다."], ["He took his hat and gloves off the table, and made for the door.", "그는 탁자에서 모자와 장갑을 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Laura and I drew back to let him pass.", "로라와 나는 그가 지나가도록 물러섰다."], ["“Remember to-morrow!”", "“내일을 잊지 마시오!”"], ["he said to his wife, and went out.", "그가 아내에게 말하고는 나가 버렸다."]], [["We waited to give him time to cross the hall and drive away.", "우리는 그가 홀을 가로질러 마차를 몰고 떠날 시간을 주려고 기다렸다."], ["The Count approached us while we were standing near the door.", "우리가 문 가까이 서 있는 동안 백작이 다가왔다."]], [["“You have just seen Percival at his worst, Miss Halcombe,” he said.", "“홀콤 양, 방금 퍼시벌의 가장 못난 모습을 보셨습니다.”"], ["“As his old friend, I am sorry for him and ashamed of him.", "그가 말했다. “오랜 벗으로서 저는 그가 딱하고 또 부끄럽습니다."], ["As his old friend, I promise you that he shall not break out to-morrow in the same disgraceful manner in which he has broken out to-day.”", "오랜 벗으로서 약속드리지요, 내일은 오늘처럼 그런 수치스러운 모습으로 폭발하는 일은 없을 것입니다.”"]], [["Laura had taken my arm while he was speaking and she pressed it significantly when he had done.", "그가 말하는 동안 로라는 내 팔을 잡았고, 말이 끝나자 의미심장하게 그 팔을 꼭 눌렀다."], ["It would have been a hard trial to any woman to stand by and see the office of apologist for her husband’s misconduct quietly assumed by his male friend in her own house—and it was a trial to HER.", "제 집에서, 남편의 잘못을 대신 변명하는 역할을 남편의 남자 친구가 태연히 떠맡는 것을 곁에 서서 지켜보는 일은 어떤 여자에게든 견디기 힘든 시련이었을 것이다—그녀에게도 그것은 시련이었다."], ["I thanked the Count civilly, and let her out.", "나는 백작에게 정중히 감사를 표하고 그녀를 데리고 나왔다."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["I thanked him: for I felt already, with a sense of inexpressible helplessness and humiliation, that it was either his interest or his caprice to make sure of my continuing to reside at Blackwater Park, and I knew after Sir Percival’s conduct to me, that without the support of the Count’s influence, I could not hope to remain there.", "나는 그에게 감사했다. 내가 블랙워터 파크에 계속 머무르도록 하는 것이 그의 이해관계이거나 그의 변덕이라는 것을, 그리고 나에 대한 퍼시벌 경의 처사를 겪은 뒤로는 백작의 영향력이 받쳐 주지 않고서는 이곳에 남기를 바랄 수도 없다는 것을, 말로 다 할 수 없는 무력감과 굴욕감과 함께 이미 느끼고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His influence, the influence of all others that I dreaded most, was actually the one tie which now held me to Laura in the hour of her utmost need!", "세상 무엇보다도 내가 가장 두려워하는 그 영향력이, 실은 로라가 가장 큰 곤경에 처한 이 시각에 나를 그녀 곁에 붙들어 두는 유일한 끈이었던 것이다!"]], [["We heard the wheels of the dog-cart crashing on the gravel of the drive as we came into the hall.", "우리가 홀로 나왔을 때 마찻바퀴가 진입로 자갈을 으스러뜨리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Sir Percival had started on his journey.", "퍼시벌 경이 길을 떠난 것이다."]], [["“Where is he going to, Marian?” Laura whispered.", "“저이가 어디로 가는 걸까, 매리언?”"], ["“Every fresh thing he does seems to terrify me about the future.", "로라가 속삭였다. “저이가 새로 하는 일마다 앞날이 무서워져."], ["Have you any suspicions?”", "짐작 가는 게 있어?”"]], [["After what she had undergone that morning, I was unwilling to tell her my suspicions.", "그날 아침 그녀가 겪은 일을 생각하니, 나는 내 짐작을 말해 주고 싶지 않았다."]], [["“How should I know his secrets?”", "“내가 저이의 비밀을 어떻게 알겠니?”"], ["I said evasively.", "나는 얼버무렸다."]], [["“I wonder if the housekeeper knows?”", "“가정부는 알고 있지 않을까?”"], ["she persisted.", "그녀가 물고 늘어졌다."]], [["“Certainly not,”", "“그럴 리 없어.”"], ["I replied.", "나는 대답했다."], ["“She must be quite as ignorant as we are.”", "“그 사람도 우리만큼이나 아무것도 모를 거야.”"]], [["Laura shook her head doubtfully.", "로라는 미심쩍은 얼굴로 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Did you not hear from the housekeeper that there was a report of Anne Catherick having been seen in this neighbourhood?", "“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에서 목격되었다는 소문이 있다고 가정부에게서 듣지 않았어?"], ["Don’t you think he may have gone away to look for her?”", "저이가 그 사람을 찾으러 간 건 아닐까?”"]], [["“I would rather compose myself, Laura, by not thinking about it at all, and after what has happened, you had better follow my example.", "“나는 그 일을 아예 생각하지 않는 편으로 마음을 가라앉히고 싶구나, 로라. 그런 일이 있었으니 너도 나를 따라 하는 게 좋겠다."], ["Come into my room, and rest and quiet yourself a little.”", "내 방으로 가서 좀 쉬면서 마음을 진정시키자꾸나.”"]], [["We sat down together close to the window, and let the fragrant summer air breathe over our faces.", "우리는 창가에 나란히 앉아, 향기로운 여름 공기가 얼굴 위로 스치도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["“I am ashamed to look at you, Marian,”", "“매리언, 나 언니 얼굴을 못 보겠어.”"], ["she said, “after what you submitted to downstairs, for my sake.", "그녀가 말했다. “나 때문에 아래층에서 그런 일을 참아 냈으니 말이야."], ["Oh, my own love, I am almost heartbroken when I think of it!", "오, 내 사랑, 그 생각만 하면 가슴이 미어질 것 같아!"], ["But I will try to make it up to you—I will indeed!”", "하지만 갚을게—꼭 갚을게, 정말이야!”"]], [["“Hush!", "“쉿!"], ["hush!”", "쉿!”"], ["I replied; “don’t talk so.", "나는 대답했다. “그런 말 하지 마."], ["What is the trifling mortification of my pride compared to the dreadful sacrifice of your happiness?”", "내 자존심이 조금 상한 것쯤이 네 행복을 그토록 끔찍하게 희생시키는 일에 비하면 무엇이겠니?”"]], [["“You heard what he said to me?”", "“저이가 내게 한 말 들었지?”"], ["she went on quickly and vehemently.", "그녀가 빠르고 격하게 말을 이었다."], ["“You heard the words—but you don’t know what they meant—you don’t know why I threw down the pen and turned my back on him.”", "“언니는 그 말을 들었지만—그게 무슨 뜻인지는 몰라—내가 왜 펜을 내던지고 등을 돌렸는지 몰라.”"], ["She rose in sudden agitation, and walked about the room.", "그녀는 갑자기 동요하여 일어서더니 방 안을 서성였다."], ["“I have kept many things from your knowledge, Marian, for fear of distressing you, and making you unhappy at the outset of our new lives.", "“언니가 괴로워할까 봐, 우리 새 삶의 첫머리에 언니를 불행하게 만들까 봐, 나는 많은 것을 언니에게 숨겨 왔어, 매리언."], ["You don’t know how he has used me.", "저이가 나를 어떻게 대해 왔는지 언니는 몰라."], ["And yet you ought to know, for you saw how he used me to-day.", "그렇지만 알아야 마땅하지, 오늘 저이가 나를 어떻게 대하는지 보았으니까."], ["You heard him sneer at my presuming to be scrupulous—you heard him say I had made a virtue of necessity in marrying him.”", "내가 감히 꺼림칙해한다고 저이가 비웃는 걸 들었잖아—어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 삼아 자기와 결혼했다고 하는 말을 들었잖아.”"], ["She sat down again, her face flushed deeply, and her hands twisted and twined together in her lap.", "그녀는 다시 앉았다. 얼굴은 붉게 달아올랐고 두 손은 무릎 위에서 뒤틀리며 얽히고 있었다."], ["“I can’t tell you about it now,”", "“지금은 그 이야기를 못 하겠어.”"], ["she said; “I shall burst out crying if I tell you now—later, Marian, when I am more sure of myself.", "그녀가 말했다. “지금 말하면 울음이 터져 버릴 거야—나중에, 매리언, 내가 좀 더 마음을 다잡았을 때 할게."], ["My poor head aches, darling—aches, aches, aches.", "머리가 아파, 언니—아파, 아파, 아파."], ["Where is your smelling-bottle?", "언니 각성제 병 어디 있어?"], ["Let me talk to you about yourself.", "언니 이야기나 하게 해 줘."], ["I wish I had given him my signature, for your sake.", "언니를 위해서라도 서명해 줄걸 그랬어. 내일은 해 줄까?"], ["Shall I give it to him to-morrow? I would rather compromise myself than compromise you.", "언니를 곤란하게 하느니 차라리 내가 나를 곤란하게 하는 편이 나아."], ["After your taking my part against him, he will lay all the blame on you if I refuse again.", "언니가 저이에게 맞서 내 편을 들었으니, 내가 또 거절하면 저이는 모든 탓을 언니에게 돌릴 거야."], ["What shall we do?", "우리 어떡하지?"], ["Oh, for a friend to help us and advise us!—a friend we could really trust!”", "오, 우리를 도와주고 조언해 줄 친구가 있었으면!—정말로 믿을 수 있는 친구가!”"]], [["She sighed bitterly.", "그녀는 쓰디쓴 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["I saw in her face that she was thinking of Hartright—saw it the more plainly because her last words set me thinking of him too.", "나는 그녀의 얼굴에서 그녀가 하트라이트를 생각하고 있음을 보았다—그녀의 마지막 말이 나까지 그를 생각하게 만들었기에 더욱 또렷이 보였다."], ["In six months only from her marriage we wanted the faithful service he had offered to us in his farewell words.", "결혼한 지 겨우 여섯 달 만에 우리는 그가 작별의 말로 내밀었던 그 성실한 도움을 필요로 하고 있었다."], ["How little I once thought that we should ever want it at all!", "언젠가 그것을 필요로 하게 되리라고, 그때의 나는 얼마나 생각지 못했던가!"]], [["“We must do what we can to help ourselves,”", "“우리 힘으로 할 수 있는 것을 해야지.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Let us try to talk it over calmly, Laura—let us do all in our power to decide for the best.”", "“차분하게 의논해 보자, 로라—최선을 정하기 위해 우리가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 해 보자.”"]], [["Putting what she knew of her husband’s embarrassments and what I had heard of his conversation with the lawyer together, we arrived necessarily at the conclusion that the parchment in the library had been drawn up for the purpose of borrowing money, and that Laura’s signature was absolutely necessary to fit it for the attainment of Sir Percival’s object.", "그녀가 알고 있는 남편의 재정적 곤경과 내가 엿들은 그와 변호사의 대화를 맞추어 보니, 우리는 서재의 그 양피지가 돈을 빌리기 위해 작성된 것이며 퍼시벌 경의 목적을 이루는 데에 로라의 서명이 반드시 필요하다는 결론에 이를 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The second question, concerning the nature of the legal contract by which the money was to be obtained, and the degree of personal responsibility to which Laura might subject herself if she signed it in the dark, involved considerations which lay far beyond any knowledge and experience that either of us possessed.", "두 번째 물음, 곧 그 돈을 얻어 내는 법적 계약이 어떤 성질의 것이며 로라가 내용을 모른 채 서명할 경우 스스로 어느 정도의 개인적 책임을 지게 되는가 하는 문제는, 우리 둘 중 누구의 지식과 경험으로도 도저히 미칠 수 없는 고려를 필요로 했다."], ["My own convictions led me to believe that the hidden contents of the parchment concealed a transaction of the meanest and the most fraudulent kind.", "나 자신의 확신으로 말하자면, 양피지의 감추어진 내용은 더없이 비열하고 더없이 기만적인 종류의 거래를 숨기고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["I had not formed this conclusion in consequence of Sir Percival’s refusal to show the writing or to explain it, for that refusal might well have proceeded from his obstinate disposition and his domineering temper alone.", "내가 이런 결론에 이른 것은 퍼시벌 경이 그 문서를 보여 주지도 설명하지도 않으려 했기 때문이 아니다. 그런 거절이야 그의 고집스러운 기질과 군림하려 드는 성미만으로도 얼마든지 나올 수 있는 것이었으니까."], ["My sole motive for distrusting his honesty sprang from the change which I had observed in his language and his manners at Blackwater Park, a change which convinced me that he had been acting a part throughout the whole period of his probation at Limmeridge House.", "그의 정직함을 의심하게 된 유일한 까닭은, 블랙워터 파크에서 그의 말투와 태도에 나타난 변화를 내가 목격했다는 데 있었다. 그 변화는 리머리지 하우스에서 시험받던 기간 내내 그가 한 편의 연기를 하고 있었음을 내게 확신시켰다."], ["His elaborate delicacy, his ceremonious politeness which harmonised so agreeably with Mr. Gilmore’s old-fashioned notions, his modesty with Laura, his candour with me, his moderation with Mr.", "그 공들인 조심성, 길모어 씨의 구식 관념과 그토록 기분 좋게 어울리던 그 예법 바른 정중함, 로라에게 보이던 겸손함, 나에게 보이던 솔직함, 페얼리 씨에게 보이던 절제—이 모두가 비열하고 교활하고 야만적인 사내의 술책이었다."], ["Fairlie—all these were the artifices of a mean, cunning, and brutal man, who had dropped his disguise when his practised duplicity had gained its end, and had openly shown himself in the library on that very day.", "그는 몸에 밴 이중성이 목적을 이루자마자 위장을 벗어 던졌고, 바로 그날 서재에서 제 본모습을 드러내 보인 것이다."], ["I say nothing of the grief which this discovery caused me on Laura’s account, for it is not to be expressed by any words of mine.", "이 깨달음이 로라 때문에 내게 안긴 슬픔에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 내 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없기 때문이다."], ["I only refer to it at all, because it decided me to oppose her signing the parchment, whatever the consequences might be, unless she was first made acquainted with the contents.", "그것을 굳이 언급하는 것은 다만, 그 슬픔이 결과야 어찌 되든 내용을 먼저 알기 전에는 그녀가 양피지에 서명하는 것을 반대하기로 나를 결심하게 했기 때문이다."]], [["Under these circumstances, the one chance for us when to-morrow came was to be provided with an objection to giving the signature, which might rest on sufficiently firm commercial or legal grounds to shake Sir Percival’s resolution, and to make him suspect that we two women understood the laws and obligations of business as well as himself.", "이런 사정에서, 내일이 왔을 때 우리에게 남은 단 하나의 방책은 서명을 거부할 근거를 미리 마련해 두는 것이었다. 퍼시벌 경의 결심을 흔들 만큼, 그리고 우리 두 여자도 사업의 법과 의무를 그 못지않게 알고 있다고 그가 의심하게 만들 만큼, 상업적으로든 법적으로든 충분히 단단한 근거 위에 선 거부 말이다."]], [["After some pondering, I determined to write to the only honest man within reach whom we could trust to help us discreetly in our forlorn situation.", "얼마간 궁리한 끝에, 나는 이 가련한 처지에서 우리를 신중하게 도와주리라 믿을 만한, 손닿는 곳의 유일한 정직한 사람에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었다."], ["That man was Mr. Gilmore’s partner, Mr.", "그 사람은 길모어 씨의 동업자인 카일 씨였다."], ["Kyrle, who conducted the business now that our old friend had been obliged to withdraw from it, and to leave London on account of his health.", "우리의 옛 벗이 건강 때문에 사무를 접고 런던을 떠나야 했던 지금은 그가 업무를 맡고 있었다."], ["I explained to Laura that I had Mr. Gilmore’s own authority for placing implicit confidence in his partner’s integrity, discretion, and accurate knowledge of all her affairs, and with her full approval I sat down at once to write the letter, I began by stating our position to Mr.", "나는 그 동업자의 성실함과 신중함, 그리고 로라의 모든 사정에 대한 정확한 지식을 전적으로 신뢰해도 좋다는 길모어 씨 본인의 보증이 내게 있음을 로라에게 설명했고, 그녀의 온전한 동의를 얻어 곧바로 앉아 편지를 썼다."], ["Kyrle exactly as it was, and then asked for his advice in return, expressed in plain, downright terms which he could comprehend without any danger of misinterpretations and mistakes.", "나는 우리의 처지를 있는 그대로 카일 씨에게 알리는 것으로 시작해서, 오해나 착오의 위험 없이 그가 알아들을 수 있도록 평이하고 단도직입적인 말로 그의 조언을 청했다."], ["My letter was as short as I could possibly make it, and was, I hope, unencumbered by needless apologies and needless details.", "편지는 내가 할 수 있는 한 짧게 썼고, 부디 쓸데없는 사과와 쓸데없는 세부로 무겁지 않기를 바랐다."]], [["Just as I was about to put the address on the envelope an obstacle was discovered by Laura, which in the effort and preoccupation of writing had escaped my mind altogether.", "봉투에 주소를 막 쓰려는 참에, 글을 쓰느라 애쓰고 골몰하는 사이 내 머릿속에서 완전히 빠져나가 있던 장애물 하나를 로라가 발견했다."]], [["“How are we to get the answer in time?”", "“답장을 어떻게 제때 받지?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["“Your letter will not be delivered in London before to-morrow morning, and the post will not bring the reply here till the morning after.”", "“언니 편지는 내일 아침에야 런던에 닿을 텐데, 우편으로는 그 답장이 그다음 날 아침에나 여기 도착할 거야.”"]], [["The only way of overcoming this difficulty was to have the answer brought to us from the lawyer’s office by a special messenger.", "이 어려움을 넘길 유일한 방법은 변호사 사무실에서 특별 심부름꾼 편으로 답장을 가져오게 하는 것이었다."], ["I wrote a postscript to that effect, begging that the messenger might be despatched with the reply by the eleven o’clock morning train, which would bring him to our station at twenty minutes past one, and so enable him to reach Blackwater Park by two o’clock at the latest.", "나는 그런 취지의 추신을 덧붙여, 심부름꾼을 답장과 함께 오전 열한 시 기차로 보내 달라고 청했다. 그러면 한 시 이십 분에 우리 역에 닿을 것이고, 늦어도 두 시까지는 블랙워터 파크에 이를 수 있을 터였다."], ["He was to be directed to ask for me, to answer no questions addressed to him by any one else, and to deliver his letter into no hands but mine.", "그는 나를 찾도록, 다른 누구의 물음에도 답하지 않도록, 그리고 편지를 내 손 외에는 어떤 손에도 건네지 않도록 지시받아야 했다."]], [["“In case Sir Percival should come back to-morrow before two o’clock,”", "“혹시 퍼시벌 경이 내일 두 시 전에 돌아온다면,”"], ["I said to Laura, “the wisest plan for you to adopt is to be out in the grounds all the morning with your book or your work, and not to appear at the house till the messenger has had time to arrive with the letter.", "나는 로라에게 말했다, “네가 취할 가장 현명한 방책은 아침 내내 책이나 바느질거리를 들고 뜰에 나가 있다가, 심부름꾼이 편지를 가지고 도착할 만한 시간이 지나기 전에는 집에 나타나지 않는 거야."], ["I will wait here for him all the morning, to guard against any misadventures or mistakes.", "나는 무슨 사고나 착오가 없도록 아침 내내 여기서 그를 기다릴게."], ["By following this arrangement I hope and believe we shall avoid being taken by surprise.", "이렇게 해 두면 불시에 당하는 일은 피할 수 있으리라 믿는다."], ["Let us go down to the drawing-room now.", "이제 응접실로 내려가자."], ["We may excite suspicion if we remain shut up together too long.”", "우리 둘이 너무 오래 틀어박혀 있으면 의심을 살지도 모르니까.”"]], [["“Suspicion?”", "“의심?”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되뇌었다."], ["“Whose suspicion can we excite, now that Sir Percival has left the house?", "“퍼시벌 경이 집을 떠났는데 누구의 의심을 산다는 거야?"], ["Do you mean Count Fosco?”", "포스코 백작 말이야?”"]], [["“Perhaps I do, Laura.”", "“그럴지도 모르지, 로라.”"]], [["“You are beginning to dislike him as much as I do, Marian.”", "“언니도 나만큼이나 그 사람이 싫어지기 시작한 거구나, 매리언.”"]], [["“No, not to dislike him.", "“아니, 싫은 건 아니야."], ["Dislike is always more or less associated with contempt—I can see nothing in the Count to despise.”", "싫음에는 늘 얼마간 경멸이 섞여 있는 법인데—나는 백작에게서 경멸할 만한 것을 하나도 찾지 못하겠어.”"]], [["“You are not afraid of him, are you?”", "“그 사람이 무섭지는 않지, 그렇지?”"]], [["“Perhaps I am—a little.”", "“어쩌면 무서운지도 모르지—조금은.”"]], [["“Afraid of him, after his interference in our favour to-day!”", "“오늘 우리 편을 들어 나서 준 사람인데 무섭다니!”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["I am more afraid of his interference than I am of Sir Percival’s violence.", "나는 퍼시벌 경의 난폭함보다 그 사람이 끼어드는 것이 더 무서워."], ["Remember what I said to you in the library.", "서재에서 내가 한 말을 기억해 두렴."], ["Whatever you do, Laura, don’t make an enemy of the Count!”", "무슨 일이 있어도, 로라, 백작을 적으로 만들지는 마!”"]], [["We went downstairs.", "우리는 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Laura entered the drawing-room, while I proceeded across the hall, with my letter in my hand, to put it into the post-bag, which hung against the wall opposite to me.", "로라는 응접실로 들어갔고, 나는 편지를 손에 든 채 홀을 가로질러, 맞은편 벽에 걸린 우편 자루에 그것을 넣으러 갔다."]], [["The house door was open, and as I crossed past it, I saw Count Fosco and his wife standing talking together on the steps outside, with their faces turned towards me.", "현관문이 열려 있었고, 그 앞을 지나가면서 나는 포스코 백작과 그의 아내가 바깥 계단에 서서 이야기를 나누며 얼굴을 이쪽으로 돌리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["The Countess came into the hall rather hastily, and asked if I had leisure enough for five minutes’ private conversation.", "백작부인이 다소 급하게 홀로 들어와, 오 분쯤 따로 이야기를 나눌 여유가 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Feeling a little surprised by such an appeal from such a person, I put my letter into the bag, and replied that I was quite at her disposal.", "그런 사람에게서 그런 청을 받으니 조금 놀랐지만, 나는 편지를 자루에 넣고 얼마든지 좋다고 대답했다."], ["She took my arm with unaccustomed friendliness and familiarity, and instead of leading me into an empty room, drew me out with her to the belt of turf which surrounded the large fish-pond.", "그녀는 평소와 달리 다정하고 스스럼없이 내 팔짱을 끼더니, 빈방으로 데려가는 대신 커다란 연못을 둘러싼 잔디밭 띠로 나를 이끌고 나갔다."]], [["As we passed the Count on the steps he bowed and smiled, and then went at once into the house, pushing the hall door to after him, but not actually closing it.", "계단에서 백작 곁을 지날 때 그는 절을 하고 미소를 지었다. 그러고는 곧장 집 안으로 들어가면서 현관문을 밀어 닫았으나, 완전히 닫지는 않았다."]], [["The Countess walked me gently round the fish-pond.", "백작부인은 나를 데리고 연못 주위를 천천히 돌았다."], ["I expected to be made the depositary of some extraordinary confidence, and I was astonished to find that Madame Fosco’s communication for my private ear was nothing more than a polite assurance of her sympathy for me, after what had happened in the library.", "나는 무슨 대단한 속내 이야기라도 맡게 되려니 했는데, 포스코 부인이 내 귀에만 들려주려던 이야기란 것이 서재에서 있었던 일에 대한 정중한 위로의 말에 지나지 않는다는 것을 알고 놀랐다."], ["Her husband had told her of all that had passed, and of the insolent manner in which Sir Percival had spoken to me.", "남편이 그 자리에서 오간 모든 일과 퍼시벌 경이 내게 얼마나 무례하게 말했는지를 그녀에게 이야기했다는 것이다."], ["This information had so shocked and distressed her, on my account and on Laura’s, that she had made up her mind, if anything of the sort happened again, to mark her sense of Sir Percival’s outrageous conduct by leaving the house.", "그 소식이 나와 로라를 생각할 때 어찌나 충격적이고 괴로웠던지, 만일 그런 일이 또 벌어진다면 이 집을 떠남으로써 퍼시벌 경의 터무니없는 처사에 대한 제 뜻을 분명히 밝히기로 마음먹었다고 했다."], ["The Count had approved of her idea, and she now hoped that I approved of it too.", "백작도 그 생각에 찬성했으니, 이제 나도 찬성해 주기를 바란다는 것이었다."]], [["I thought this a very strange proceeding on the part of such a remarkably reserved woman as Madame Fosco, especially after the interchange of sharp speeches which had passed between us during the conversation in the boat-house on that very morning.", "포스코 부인처럼 유난히 말수 없는 여자에게서 나온 행동치고는 참으로 이상한 일이라고 나는 생각했다. 더구나 바로 그날 아침 보트 창고에서 나눈 대화에서 우리 사이에 날 선 말들이 오간 뒤였으니 더욱 그랬다."], ["However, it was my plain duty to meet a polite and friendly advance on the part of one of my elders with a polite and friendly reply.", "그러나 나보다 손위인 사람이 정중하고 다정하게 다가올 때 정중하고 다정하게 응대하는 것은 분명한 내 도리였다."], ["I answered the Countess accordingly in her own tone, and then, thinking we had said all that was necessary on either side, made an attempt to get back to the house.", "그래서 나는 백작부인에게 그녀와 같은 어조로 대답했고, 이제 양쪽 다 할 말은 다 했다고 여겨 집으로 돌아가려 했다."]], [["But Madame Fosco seemed resolved not to part with me, and to my unspeakable amazement, resolved also to talk.", "그러나 포스코 부인은 나를 놓아줄 생각이 없어 보였고, 나로서는 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 이야기를 계속할 작정인 듯했다."], ["Hitherto the most silent of women, she now persecuted me with fluent conventionalities on the subject of married life, on the subject of Sir Percival and Laura, on the subject of her own happiness, on the subject of the late Mr.", "여태껏 여자들 가운데 가장 과묵했던 그녀가 이제는 결혼 생활에 대해, 퍼시벌 경과 로라에 대해, 제 자신의 행복에 대해, 유산 문제로 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 자기에게 했던 처사에 대해, 그 밖에 대여섯 가지 화제에 대해 막힘없는 상투어를 늘어놓으며 나를 들볶았다."], ["Fairlie’s conduct to her in the matter of her legacy, and on half a dozen other subjects besides, until she had detained me walking round and round the fish-pond for more than half an hour, and had quite wearied me out.", "그렇게 삼십 분이 넘도록 나를 연못 주위로 빙빙 돌게 붙들어 두어 나를 완전히 지치게 만들었다."], ["Whether she discovered this or not, I cannot say, but she stopped as abruptly as she had begun—looked towards the house door, resumed her icy manner in a moment, and dropped my arm of her own accord before I could think of an excuse for accomplishing my own release from her.", "그녀가 그것을 알아차렸는지 어떤지는 알 수 없으나, 그녀는 시작할 때만큼이나 불쑥 말을 멈추더니—현관문 쪽을 바라보고는 순식간에 그 얼음 같은 태도로 돌아가, 내가 그녀에게서 풀려날 구실을 생각해 내기도 전에 제 쪽에서 먼저 내 팔을 놓아 버렸다."]], [["As I pushed open the door and entered the hall, I found myself suddenly face to face with the Count again.", "문을 밀어 열고 홀에 들어서는 순간, 나는 또다시 백작과 정면으로 마주쳤다."], ["He was just putting a letter into the post-bag.", "그는 마침 우편 자루에 편지 한 통을 넣고 있는 참이었다."]], [["After he had dropped it in and had closed the bag, he asked me where I had left Madame Fosco.", "편지를 떨어뜨려 넣고 자루를 닫은 뒤, 그는 내게 포스코 부인을 어디에 두고 왔느냐고 물었다."], ["I told him, and he went out at the hall door immediately to join his wife.", "내가 말해 주자 그는 곧장 현관문으로 나가 아내에게 갔다."], ["His manner when he spoke to me was so unusually quiet and subdued that I turned and looked after him, wondering if he were ill or out of spirits.", "내게 말할 때의 그의 태도가 어찌나 유별나게 조용하고 가라앉아 있던지, 나는 몸이라도 편찮은 걸까 아니면 기분이 상한 걸까 궁금해하며 돌아서서 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."]], [["Why my next proceeding was to go straight up to the post-bag and take out my own letter and look at it again, with a vague distrust on me, and why the looking at it for the second time instantly suggested the idea to my mind of sealing the envelope for its greater security—are mysteries which are either too deep or too shallow for me to fathom.", "어째서 내가 그다음으로 한 일이 곧장 우편 가방으로 올라가 내 편지를 도로 꺼내어, 막연한 불신에 사로잡힌 채 그것을 다시 들여다보는 것이었는지, 그리고 어째서 두 번째로 들여다보자마자 더 안전하게 해 두려고 봉투를 봉인해야겠다는 생각이 즉시 떠올랐는지 — 그것은 내가 헤아리기에는 너무 깊거나 너무 얕은 수수께끼다."], ["Women, as everybody knows, constantly act on impulses which they cannot explain even to themselves, and I can only suppose that one of those impulses was the hidden cause of my unaccountable conduct on this occasion.", "누구나 알다시피 여자란 스스로에게조차 설명하지 못하는 충동에 따라 곧잘 행동하는 법이고, 나로서는 그런 충동 가운데 하나가 이번 일에서 보인 내 알 수 없는 처신의 숨은 원인이었으리라 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이다."]], [["Whatever influence animated me, I found cause to congratulate myself on having obeyed it as soon as I prepared to seal the letter in my own room.", "나를 움직인 것이 무엇이었든, 내 방에서 편지를 봉인하려고 준비하는 순간 나는 그 충동에 따르기를 잘했다고 자축할 만한 이유를 발견했다."], ["I had originally closed the envelope in the usual way by moistening the adhesive point and pressing it on the paper beneath, and when I now tried it with my finger, after a lapse of full three-quarters of an hour, the envelope opened on the instant, without sticking or tearing.", "나는 처음에 여느 때처럼 접착면에 침을 묻혀 아래 종이에 눌러 붙이는 식으로 봉투를 닫아 두었는데, 꼬박 사십오 분이 지난 지금 손가락으로 건드려 보니 봉투는 붙어 있지도, 찢어지지도 않은 채 그 자리에서 대뜸 열려 버렸다."], ["Perhaps I had fastened it insufficiently?", "내가 제대로 붙여 두지 않았던 걸까?"], ["Perhaps there might have been some defect in the adhesive gum?", "아니면 접착풀에 무슨 결함이라도 있었던 걸까?"]], [["Or, perhaps——No!", "아니면, 혹시—— 아니!"], ["it is quite revolting enough to feel that third conjecture stirring in my mind.", "그 세 번째 추측이 내 마음속에서 꿈틀거리는 것을 느끼는 것만으로도 충분히 넌더리가 난다."], ["I would rather not see it confronting me in plain black and white.", "그것이 흰 종이 위에 검은 글씨로 또렷이 적혀 나를 마주 보고 있는 꼴은 차라리 보고 싶지 않다."]], [["I almost dread to-morrow—so much depends on my discretion and self-control.", "내일이 거의 두렵다 — 내 분별과 자제에 너무나 많은 것이 달려 있으니."], ["There are two precautions, at all events, which I am sure not to forget.", "어쨌든 결코 잊지 않으리라 자신하는 두 가지 조심이 있다."], ["I must be careful to keep up friendly appearances with the Count, and I must be well on my guard when the messenger from the office comes here with the answer to my letter.", "나는 백작과 다정한 겉모습을 유지하도록 조심해야 하고, 사무소에서 온 심부름꾼이 내 편지에 대한 답을 가지고 이곳에 올 때 단단히 경계해야 한다."]], [["# V", "# V"]], [["June 17th.—When the dinner hour brought us together again, Count Fosco was in his usual excellent spirits.", "6월 17일.—만찬 시간이 되어 우리가 다시 모였을 때, 포스코 백작은 여느 때처럼 더없이 좋은 기분이었다."], ["He exerted himself to interest and amuse us, as if he was determined to efface from our memories all recollection of what had passed in the library that afternoon.", "그는 그날 오후 서재에서 있었던 일에 대한 기억을 우리 머릿속에서 말끔히 지워 버리기로 작정한 사람처럼, 우리의 흥미를 끌고 우리를 즐겁게 하려고 애를 썼다."], ["Lively descriptions of his adventures in travelling, amusing anecdotes of remarkable people whom he had met with abroad, quaint comparisons between the social customs of various nations, illustrated by examples drawn from men and women indiscriminately all over Europe, humorous confessions of the innocent follies of his own early life, when he ruled the fashions of a second-rate Italian town, and wrote preposterous romances on the French model for a second-rate Italian newspaper—all flowed in succession so easily and so gaily from his lips, and all addressed our various curiosities and various interests so directly and so delicately, that Laura and I listened to him with as much attention and, inconsistent as it may seem, with as much admiration also, as Madame Fosco herself.", "여행하며 겪은 모험담의 생생한 묘사, 외국에서 만난 저명한 인물들에 관한 재미있는 일화, 여러 나라의 사회 풍습에 대한 기묘한 비교—유럽 전역의 남녀를 가리지 않고 끌어온 실례로 뒷받침된—그리고 이류 이탈리아 소도시의 유행을 주름잡으며 이류 이탈리아 신문에 프랑스풍의 터무니없는 연애소설을 써 대던 젊은 시절의 순진한 어리석음에 대한 익살스러운 고백까지, 그 모든 것이 그의 입술에서 어찌나 쉽고도 흥겹게 줄줄이 흘러나오던지, 또 그 모든 것이 저마다 다른 우리의 호기심과 관심사를 어찌나 곧바로 그리고 어찌나 섬세하게 겨냥하던지, 로라와 나는 포스코 백작부인 못지않은 주의를 기울여—모순처럼 들릴지 모르나 못지않은 감탄까지 품고서—그의 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["Women can resist a man’s love, a man’s fame, a man’s personal appearance, and a man’s money, but they cannot resist a man’s tongue when he knows how to talk to them.", "여자는 남자의 사랑도, 남자의 명성도, 남자의 용모도, 남자의 돈도 뿌리칠 수 있지만, 자기에게 말을 거는 법을 아는 남자의 혀만은 뿌리치지 못한다."]], [["After dinner, while the favourable impression which he had produced on us was still vivid in our minds, the Count modestly withdrew to read in the library.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤, 그가 우리에게 남긴 호의적인 인상이 아직 마음속에 생생할 때, 백작은 겸손하게 물러나 서재로 독서를 하러 갔다."]], [["Laura proposed a stroll in the grounds to enjoy the close of the long evening.", "로라는 긴 저녁의 끝자락을 즐기자며 정원을 거닐자고 했다."], ["It was necessary in common politeness to ask Madame Fosco to join us, but this time she had apparently received her orders beforehand, and she begged we would kindly excuse her.", "예의상 포스코 백작부인에게도 같이 가자고 청하지 않을 수 없었으나, 이번에는 그녀가 미리 분부를 받아 둔 모양인지 부디 양해해 달라고 했다."], ["“The Count will probably want a fresh supply of cigarettes,”", "“백작께서 아마 담배를 새로 채워 달라고 하실 거예요.”"], ["she remarked by way of apology, “and nobody can make them to his satisfaction but myself.”", "그녀가 변명 삼아 말했다. “그리고 그이 마음에 들게 마는 건 저 말고는 아무도 못 하지요.”"], ["Her cold blue eyes almost warmed as she spoke the words—she looked actually proud of being the officiating medium through which her lord and master composed himself with tobacco-smoke!", "그 말을 하는 동안 그녀의 차가운 푸른 눈이 거의 온기를 띠었다—자기 주인이자 남편이 담배 연기로 마음을 가라앉히는 데에 쓰이는 시중꾼 노릇이 실로 자랑스럽다는 얼굴이었다!"]], [["Laura and I went out together alone.", "로라와 나는 단둘이 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["It was a misty, heavy evening.", "안개가 끼고 후텁지근한 저녁이었다."], ["There was a sense of blight in the air; the flowers were drooping in the garden, and the ground was parched and dewless.", "공기 속에는 무언가 시들어 가는 기운이 감돌았다. 정원의 꽃들은 고개를 떨구었고, 땅은 이슬 한 방울 없이 바싹 말라 있었다."], ["The western heaven, as we saw it over the quiet trees, was of a pale yellow hue, and the sun was setting faintly in a haze.", "고요한 나무들 너머로 보이는 서쪽 하늘은 옅은 노란빛이었고, 해는 아지랑이 속에서 희미하게 저물고 있었다."], ["Coming rain seemed near—it would fall probably with the fall of night.", "비가 머지않아 올 듯했다—아마도 밤이 내리면서 함께 쏟아질 터였다."]], [["“Which way shall we go?”", "“어느 쪽으로 갈까?”"], ["I asked", "내가 물었다"]], [["“Towards the lake, Marian, if you like,”", "“호수 쪽으로 가요, 매리언, 언니만 괜찮다면요.”"], ["she answered.", "로라가 대답했다."]], [["“You seem unaccountably fond, Laura, of that dismal lake.”", "“로라, 넌 어쩐지 그 음침한 호수를 유난히 좋아하는구나.”"]], [["“No, not of the lake but of the scenery about it.", "“아니에요, 호수가 아니라 그 언저리 풍경이요."], ["The sand and heath and the fir-trees are the only objects I can discover, in all this large place, to remind me of Limmeridge.", "모래밭과 히스와 전나무들만이 이 넓은 곳에서 리머리지를 떠올리게 하는 유일한 것들이거든요."], ["But we will walk in some other direction if you prefer it.”", "하지만 언니가 다른 쪽이 좋으시면 그리로 걸어요.”"]], [["“I have no favourite walks at Blackwater Park, my love.", "“나는 블랙워터 파크에 딱히 좋아하는 산책길이 없단다, 얘야."], ["One is the same as another to me.", "어디나 매한가지야."], ["Let us go to the lake—we may find it cooler in the open space than we find it here.”", "호수로 가자꾸나—여기보다는 트인 곳이 좀 시원할지도 모르지.”"]], [["We walked through the shadowy plantation in silence.", "우리는 말없이 그늘진 수림을 지나 걸었다."], ["The heaviness in the evening air oppressed us both, and when we reached the boat-house we were glad to sit down and rest inside.", "저녁 공기의 후텁지근함이 우리 둘을 짓눌렀고, 보트 창고에 이르자 우리는 반갑게 안으로 들어가 앉아 쉬었다."]], [["A white fog hung low over the lake.", "흰 안개가 호수 위에 낮게 깔려 있었다."], ["The dense brown line of the trees on the opposite bank appeared above it, like a dwarf forest floating in the sky.", "건너편 기슭의 짙은 갈색 나무 띠가 그 위로 솟아, 마치 하늘에 떠 있는 난쟁이 숲처럼 보였다."], ["The sandy ground, shelving downward from where we sat, was lost mysteriously in the outward layers of the fog.", "우리가 앉은 자리에서 비스듬히 내려가는 모래땅은 안개의 바깥 겹 속으로 알 수 없이 사라져 버렸다."], ["The silence was horrible.", "정적은 소름 끼쳤다."], ["No rustling of the leaves—no bird’s note in the wood—no cry of water-fowl from the pools of the hidden lake.", "잎사귀 스치는 소리 하나 없고—숲에서 새 우는 소리도 없고—가려진 호수의 웅덩이에서 물새가 우는 소리도 없었다."], ["Even the croaking of the frogs had ceased to-night.", "오늘 밤은 개구리 울음소리마저 그쳐 있었다."]], [["“It is very desolate and gloomy,”", "“정말 황량하고 음울하네요.”"], ["said Laura.", "로라가 말했다."], ["“But we can be more alone here than anywhere else.”", "“하지만 여기서라면 다른 어디보다 단둘이 있을 수 있어요.”"]], [["She spoke quietly and looked at the wilderness of sand and mist with steady, thoughtful eyes.", "로라는 조용히 말하면서, 흔들림 없는 사려 깊은 눈으로 모래와 안개의 황무지를 바라보았다."], ["I could see that her mind was too much occupied to feel the dreary impressions from without which had fastened themselves already on mine.", "그 애의 마음이 너무나 꽉 차 있어서, 이미 내 마음에 들러붙어 버린 바깥 풍경의 스산한 인상 따위는 느끼지 못한다는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다."]], [["“I promised, Marian, to tell you the truth about my married life, instead of leaving you any longer to guess it for yourself,”", "“매리언, 언니가 혼자 짐작하도록 더 내버려 두지 않고 제 결혼 생활의 진실을 말씀드리겠다고 약속했지요.”"], ["she began.", "로라가 말문을 열었다."], ["“That secret is the first I have ever had from you, love, and I am determined it shall be the last.", "“그 비밀은 제가 언니한테 숨긴 첫 번째 비밀이었어요. 그리고 마지막이 되게 하겠다고 마음먹었고요."], ["I was silent, as you know, for your sake—and perhaps a little for my own sake as well.", "아시다시피 제가 입을 다물었던 건 언니를 위해서였어요—어쩌면 저 자신을 위해서이기도 했겠지요."], ["It is very hard for a woman to confess that the man to whom she has given her whole life is the man of all others who cares least for the gift.", "자기 온 생을 바친 남자가 하필이면 그 선물을 세상에서 가장 하찮게 여기는 남자라고 고백하는 건 여자에게 몹시 힘든 일이에요."], ["If you were married yourself, Marian—and especially if you were happily married—you would feel for me as no single woman CAN feel, however kind and true she may be.”", "언니가 결혼한 몸이었다면—더구나 행복하게 결혼한 몸이었다면—아무리 다정하고 진실한 사람이라도 결혼하지 않은 여자로서는 결코 느낄 수 없는 마음으로 저를 헤아려 주셨을 거예요.”"]], [["What answer could I make?", "내가 무슨 대답을 할 수 있었을까?"], ["I could only take her hand and look at her with my whole heart as well as my eyes would let me.", "나는 그저 그 애의 손을 잡고, 눈으로 담아낼 수 있는 한 온 마음을 다해 그 애를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“How often,”", "“얼마나 여러 번,” 로라가 말을 이었다."], ["she went on, “I have heard you laughing over what you used to call your ‘poverty!’ how often you have made me mock-speeches of congratulation on my wealth!", "“언니가 스스로 ‘가난’이라 부르며 웃어넘기는 걸 들었는지 몰라요. 얼마나 여러 번 제 재산을 두고 축하한다며 짐짓 우스개 연설을 늘어놓으셨는지요!"], ["Oh, Marian, never laugh again.", "아, 매리언, 다시는 웃지 마세요."], ["Thank God for your poverty—it has made you your own mistress, and has saved you from the lot that has fallen on ME.”", "언니의 가난을 하느님께 감사하세요—그 덕분에 언니는 자기 자신의 주인이 되었고, 제게 떨어진 운명을 면하셨으니까요.”"]], [["A sad beginning on the lips of a young wife!—sad in its quiet plain-spoken truth.", "젊은 아내의 입에서 나온 서글픈 서두였다!—조용하고 꾸밈없는 진실이 담겨 있어 더욱 서글펐다."], ["The few days we had all passed together at Blackwater Park had been many enough to show me—to show any one—what her husband had married her for.", "우리 모두가 블랙워터 파크에서 함께 보낸 며칠은, 그 애의 남편이 무엇 때문에 그 애와 결혼했는지를 내게—아니 누구에게든—보여 주기에 충분하고도 남았다."]], [["“You shall not be distressed,” she said, “by hearing how soon my disappointments and my trials began—or even by knowing what they were.", "“제 실망과 시련이 얼마나 일찍 시작되었는지, 아니 그것이 어떤 것이었는지조차 들으시게 해서 언니를 괴롭히지는 않을게요.”"], ["It is bad enough to have them on my memory.", "그 애가 말했다. “제 기억 속에 담아 두는 것만으로도 이미 충분히 괴로우니까요."], ["If I tell you how he received the first and last attempt at remonstrance that I ever made, you will know how he has always treated me, as well as if I had described it in so many words.", "다만 제가 난생처음이자 마지막으로 항의라는 걸 해 보았을 때 그이가 그것을 어떻게 받아들였는지 말씀드리면, 그이가 늘 저를 어떻게 대해 왔는지 낱낱이 말로 설명한 것이나 다름없이 아시게 될 거예요."], ["It was one day at Rome when we had ridden out together to the tomb of Cecilia Metella.", "로마에서 어느 날 우리가 함께 말을 타고 케킬리아 메텔라의 무덤까지 나갔을 때였어요."], ["The sky was calm and lovely, and the grand old ruin looked beautiful, and the remembrance that a husband’s love had raised it in the old time to a wife’s memory, made me feel more tenderly and more anxiously towards my husband than I had ever felt yet.", "하늘은 잔잔하고 아름다웠고, 웅장한 옛 폐허는 근사해 보였어요. 그리고 옛날에 한 남편의 사랑이 아내를 기리려고 저것을 세웠다는 생각이 들자, 저는 그때껏 느껴 본 적 없이 애틋하고 간절한 마음으로 제 남편을 바라보게 되었지요."], ["‘Would you build such a tomb for ME, Percival?’ I asked him.", "‘당신도 저를 위해 저런 무덤을 지어 주시겠어요, 퍼시벌?’ 하고 물었어요."], ["‘You said you loved me dearly before we were married, and yet, since that time——’ I could get no farther.", "‘결혼 전에는 저를 끔찍이 사랑한다고 하셨잖아요. 그런데 그 뒤로는——’ 더는 말을 이을 수가 없었어요."], ["Marian!", "매리언!"], ["he was not even looking at me!", "그이는 저를 쳐다보지조차 않고 있었어요!"], ["I pulled down my veil, thinking it best not to let him see that the tears were in my eyes.", "저는 눈에 눈물이 고인 걸 그이에게 보이지 않는 게 낫겠다 싶어 베일을 내렸어요."], ["I fancied he had not paid any attention to me, but he had.", "그이가 제 말에 아무 관심도 두지 않았다고 여겼는데, 실은 아니었어요."], ["He said, ‘Come away,’ and laughed to himself as he helped me on to my horse.", "그이는 ‘가지’ 하고는 저를 말에 태워 주면서 혼자 웃었어요."], ["He mounted his own horse and laughed again as we rode away.", "자기 말에 올라타서는 우리가 말을 몰아 떠나는 동안 또 웃었고요."], ["‘If I do build you a tomb,’ he said, ‘it will be done with your own money.", "‘내가 당신 무덤을 지어 준다면,’ 그이가 말했어요. ‘그건 당신 돈으로 짓는 게 되겠지."], ["I wonder whether Cecilia Metella had a fortune and paid for hers.’ I made no reply—how could I, when I was crying behind my veil?", "케킬리아 메텔라도 재산이 있어서 제 무덤값을 치렀는지 궁금하군.’ 저는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았어요—베일 뒤에서 울고 있는데 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["‘Ah, you light-complexioned women are all sulky,’ he said.", "‘아, 당신처럼 살결 흰 여자들은 하나같이 뾰로통하지.’ 그이가 말했어요."], ["‘What do you want?", "‘뭘 원하는 거요?"], ["compliments and soft speeches?", "찬사와 달콤한 말인가?"], ["Well!", "좋소!"], ["I’m in a good humour this morning.", "오늘 아침은 내 기분이 괜찮으니."], ["Consider the compliments paid and the speeches said.’ Men little know when they say hard things to us how well we remember them, and how much harm they do us.", "찬사는 바쳐진 걸로, 그 말들은 다 해 준 걸로 치시오.’ 남자들은 우리에게 모진 말을 할 때, 우리가 그것을 얼마나 잘 기억하는지, 그것이 우리를 얼마나 해치는지 도무지 모르지요."], ["It would have been better for me if I had gone on crying, but his contempt dried up my tears and hardened my heart.", "차라리 제가 계속 울었더라면 나았을 텐데, 그이의 경멸이 제 눈물을 말려 버리고 제 마음을 굳게 만들었어요."], ["From that time, Marian, I never checked myself again in thinking of Walter Hartright.", "그때부터예요, 매리언, 제가 월터 하트라이트를 생각하는 저 자신을 다시는 억누르지 않게 된 것은요."], ["I let the memory of those happy days, when we were so fond of each other in secret, come back and comfort me.", "우리가 남몰래 서로를 그토록 아끼던 그 행복한 나날의 기억이 되돌아와 저를 위로하도록 내버려 두었어요."], ["What else had I to look to for consolation?", "그것 말고 제가 어디서 위안을 구할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["If we had been together you would have helped me to better things.", "우리가 함께 있었더라면 언니가 더 나은 길로 저를 이끌어 주셨겠지요."], ["I know it was wrong, darling, but tell me if I was wrong without any excuse.”", "잘못인 줄 알아요, 언니. 하지만 제게 변명의 여지조차 없는 잘못이었는지 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["I was obliged to turn my face from her.", "나는 그 애에게서 얼굴을 돌리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“Don’t ask me!”", "“묻지 마라!”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Have I suffered as you have suffered?", "“내가 너처럼 고통을 겪어 보기라도 했니?"], ["What right have I to decide?”", "무슨 자격으로 내가 판가름을 한단 말이냐?”"]], [["“I used to think of him,”", "“저는 그이를 생각하곤 했어요.”"], ["she pursued, dropping her voice and moving closer to me, “I used to think of him when Percival left me alone at night to go among the Opera people.", "그 애가 목소리를 낮추고 내게로 더 가까이 다가앉으며 말을 이었다. “퍼시벌이 오페라 사람들 틈에 끼려고 밤이면 저를 혼자 두고 나갈 때면 그이를 생각하곤 했어요."], ["I used to fancy what I might have been if it had pleased God to bless me with poverty, and if I had been his wife.", "하느님께서 저를 가난으로 축복해 주시고 제가 그이의 아내가 되었더라면 저는 어떤 사람이 되었을까 상상하곤 했지요."], ["I used to see myself in my neat cheap gown, sitting at home and waiting for him while he was earning our bread—sitting at home and working for him and loving him all the better because I had to work for him—seeing him come in tired and taking off his hat and coat for him, and, Marian, pleasing him with little dishes at dinner that I had learnt to make for his sake.", "값싸지만 단정한 옷을 입고 집에 앉아, 그이가 우리 두 사람의 밥벌이를 하는 동안 그이를 기다리는 저를 그려 보았어요—집에 앉아 그이를 위해 일하고, 그이를 위해 일해야 하기에 더욱더 그이를 사랑하는 저를요—그이가 지쳐 들어오면 맞이하여 모자와 외투를 받아 주고, 매리언, 그이를 위해 배워 둔 자잘한 요리로 저녁상에서 그이를 기쁘게 해 주는 저를요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["I hope he is never lonely enough and sad enough to think of me and see me as I have thought of HIM and see HIM!”", "부디 그이가 저를 떠올리고 제 모습을 그려 볼 만큼 외롭고 서글픈 처지가 되는 일은 없기를 바라요, 제가 그이를 생각하고 그이의 모습을 그려 보듯이는요!”"]], [["As she said those melancholy words, all the lost tenderness returned to her voice, and all the lost beauty trembled back into her face.", "그 애가 그 서글픈 말을 하는 동안, 잃어버렸던 다정함이 모두 그 목소리로 돌아왔고, 잃어버렸던 아름다움이 모두 그 얼굴로 떨리듯 되살아났다."], ["Her eyes rested as lovingly on the blighted, solitary, ill-omened view before us, as if they saw the friendly hills of Cumberland in the dim and threatening sky.", "그 애의 눈은 우리 앞에 펼쳐진 시들고 적막하고 불길한 풍경을, 마치 어둡고 위협적인 하늘 속에서 정겨운 컴벌랜드의 언덕을 보기라도 하는 것처럼 사랑스럽게 바라보았다."]], [["“Don’t speak of Walter any more,”", "“월터 얘기는 이제 그만하자.”"], ["I said, as soon as I could control myself.", "내가 겨우 마음을 다잡고 말했다."], ["“Oh, Laura, spare us both the wretchedness of talking of him now!”", "“아, 로라, 지금 그 사람 얘기를 해서 우리 둘 다 비참해지는 일만은 하지 말자꾸나!”"]], [["She roused herself, and looked at me tenderly.", "그 애는 정신을 차리고 다정한 눈길로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“I would rather be silent about him for ever,”", "“언니를 한순간이라도 아프게 하느니,” 그 애가 대답했다."], ["she answered, “than cause you a moment’s pain.”", "“차라리 그이에 대해 영영 입을 다물겠어요.”"]], [["“It is in your interests,”", "“너를 위해서 하는 말이야.”"], ["I pleaded; “it is for your sake that I speak.", "내가 간청하듯 말했다. “다 널 생각해서 하는 말이란다."], ["If your husband heard you——”", "네 남편이 그 말을 듣기라도 하면——”"]], [["“It would not surprise him if he did hear me.”", "“듣는다 해도 놀라지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["She made that strange reply with a weary calmness and coldness.", "그 애는 지치고 차가운 평정으로 그 이상한 대답을 했다."], ["The change in her manner, when she gave the answer, startled me almost as much as the answer itself.", "대답을 하는 순간 달라진 그 애의 태도가, 대답 그 자체 못지않게 나를 놀라게 했다."]], [["“Not surprise him!”", "“놀라지 않는다니!”"], ["I repeated.", "나는 되뇌었다."], ["“Laura!", "“로라!"], ["remember what you are saying—you frighten me!”", "네가 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 생각해 보렴—무섭구나!”"]], [["“It is true,”", "“사실인걸요.”"], ["she said; “it is what I wanted to tell you to-day, when we were talking in your room.", "그 애가 말했다. “오늘 언니 방에서 얘기할 때 말씀드리려던 게 바로 그거예요."], ["My only secret when I opened my heart to him at Limmeridge was a harmless secret, Marian—you said so yourself.", "리머리지에서 그이에게 마음을 열었을 때 제가 지녔던 유일한 비밀은 해로울 것 없는 비밀이었어요, 매리언—언니도 그렇게 말씀하셨잖아요."], ["The name was all I kept from him, and he has discovered it.”", "제가 감춘 건 이름뿐이었는데, 그이가 그걸 알아냈어요.”"]], [["I heard her, but I could say nothing.", "나는 그 말을 들었으나 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."], ["Her last words had killed the little hope that still lived in me.", "그 애의 마지막 말이 내 안에 아직 살아 있던 작은 희망마저 죽여 버렸다."]], [["“It happened at Rome,”", "“로마에서 일어난 일이에요.”"], ["she went on, as wearily calm and cold as ever.", "그 애가 여전히 지치고 차가운 평정으로 말을 이었다. “퍼시벌 경의 친구분들인 마클랜드 씨 부부가 영국인들을 위해 연 작은 모임에 갔었지요."], ["“We were at a little party given to the English by some friends of Sir Percival’s—Mr.", "마클랜드 부인은 그림을 아주 잘 그린다는 평판이 있었고, 손님들 몇몇이 청해서 그림을 보여 주게 되었어요. 우리 모두 감탄했는데, 제가 한 어떤 말이 그분의 관심을 특히 저에게로 끌었나 봐요."], ["and Mrs. Markland.", "‘부인도 분명 그림을 그리시지요?’ 하고 물으시더군요."], ["Mrs. Markland had the reputation of sketching very beautifully, and some of the guests prevailed on her to show us her drawings.", "‘예전에 조금 그렸습니다만,’ 하고 제가 대답했어요."], ["We all admired them, but something I said attracted her attention particularly to me.", "‘지금은 그만두었습니다.’ ‘한번 그려 보신 분이라면,’ 그분이 말했어요."], ["‘Surely you draw yourself?’ she asked.", "‘언젠가 다시 붙잡게 되실 거예요."], ["‘I used to draw a little once,’ I answered, ‘but I have given it up.’ ‘If you have once drawn,’ she said, ‘you may take to it again one of these days, and if you do, I wish you would let me recommend you a master.’ I said nothing—you know why, Marian—and tried to change the conversation.", "그렇게 되시거든 제가 선생님을 한 분 추천해 드리고 싶군요.’ 저는 아무 말도 하지 않고—왜인지는 언니도 아시잖아요, 매리언—화제를 돌리려 했어요."], ["But Mrs. Markland persisted.", "그런데 마클랜드 부인이 계속하시더군요."], ["‘I have had all sorts of teachers,’ she went on, ‘but the best of all, the most intelligent and the most attentive, was a Mr.", "‘저는 온갖 선생님을 다 겪어 보았지만,’ 그분이 말을 이었어요. ‘그중에서도 가장 훌륭하고 가장 총명하고 가장 자상했던 분은 하트라이트 씨였어요."], ["Hartright. If you ever take up your drawing again, do try him as a master.", "언제든 다시 그림을 붙잡으시게 되면 꼭 그분을 선생으로 삼아 보세요."], ["He is a young man—modest and gentlemanlike—I am sure you will like him.’ Think of those words being spoken to me publicly, in the presence of strangers—strangers who had been invited to meet the bride and bridegroom!", "젊은 분인데—겸손하고 신사다운 분이라—틀림없이 마음에 드실 거예요.’ 그런 말이 낯선 사람들 앞에서, 그것도 신랑 신부를 만나러 초대된 낯선 사람들 앞에서 공공연히 저에게 건네졌다고 생각해 보세요!"], ["I did all I could to control myself—I said nothing, and looked down close at the drawings.", "저는 제 자신을 다잡으려고 할 수 있는 걸 다 했어요—아무 말도 하지 않고 그림들에 바짝 고개를 숙였지요."], ["When I ventured to raise my head again, my eyes and my husband’s eyes met, and I knew, by his look, that my face had betrayed me. ‘We will see about Mr.", "겨우 다시 고개를 들었을 때 제 눈과 남편의 눈이 마주쳤고, 저는 그 눈빛에서 제 얼굴이 저를 팔아 버렸다는 걸 알았어요."], ["Hartright,’ he said, looking at me all the time, ‘when we get back to England.", "‘하트라이트 씨 일은 생각해 보기로 하지.’ 그이가 내내 저를 쳐다보며 말했어요."], ["I agree with you, Mrs.", "‘영국에 돌아가면 말이오."], ["Markland—I think Lady Glyde is sure to like him.’ He laid an emphasis on the last words which made my cheeks burn, and set my heart beating as if it would stifle me.", "마클랜드 부인, 저도 같은 생각입니다—글라이드 부인이 틀림없이 그를 마음에 들어 할 것 같군요.’ 그이가 마지막 말에 힘을 주는 바람에 제 뺨이 화끈거렸고, 숨이 막힐 듯 심장이 뛰었어요."], ["Nothing more was said.", "더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았어요."], ["We came away early.", "우리는 일찍 자리를 떴어요."], ["He was silent in the carriage driving back to the hotel.", "그이는 호텔로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 말이 없었어요."], ["He helped me out, and followed me upstairs as usual.", "저를 부축해 내려 주고 여느 때처럼 뒤따라 위층으로 올라왔지요."], ["But the moment we were in the drawing-room, he locked the door, pushed me down into a chair, and stood over me with his hands on my shoulders.", "그런데 응접실에 들어서자마자 그이는 문을 잠그고 저를 의자에 밀어 앉히더니, 제 어깨에 두 손을 얹은 채 저를 내려다보고 섰어요."], ["‘Ever since that morning when you made your audacious confession to me at Limmeridge,’ he said, ‘I have wanted to find out the man, and I found him in your face to-night.", "‘리머리지에서 당신이 그 뻔뻔스러운 고백을 하던 그날 아침부터,’ 그이가 말했어요. ‘나는 그 사내를 찾아내고 싶었소."], ["Your drawing-master was the man, and his name is Hartright.", "그런데 오늘 밤 당신 얼굴에서 찾아냈지. 당신 그림 선생이 그 사내였고, 이름은 하트라이트요."], ["You shall repent it, and he shall repent it, to the last hour of your lives.", "당신도 후회하게 될 거고, 그자도 후회하게 될 거요. 목숨이 붙어 있는 마지막 순간까지."], ["Now go to bed and dream of him if you like, with the marks of my horsewhip on his shoulders.’ Whenever he is angry with me now he refers to what I acknowledged to him in your presence with a sneer or a threat.", "이제 가서 자고, 원한다면 그자의 꿈이나 꾸시오. 그 어깨에 내 채찍 자국이 난 꼴로 말이오.’ 요즘도 저에게 화가 날 때면 그이는 언니가 계신 자리에서 제가 인정했던 그 일을 비웃음이나 협박과 함께 들먹여요."], ["I have no power to prevent him from putting his own horrible construction on the confidence I placed in him.", "제가 그이를 믿고 털어놓은 것을 그이가 제멋대로 끔찍하게 해석하는 걸 막을 힘이 저에게는 없어요."], ["I have no influence to make him believe me, or to keep him silent.", "저를 믿게 만들 힘도, 입을 다물게 할 힘도 없고요."], ["You looked surprised to-day when you heard him tell me that I had made a virtue of necessity in marrying him.", "오늘 그이가 제게 자기와 결혼한 건 어쩔 수 없는 일을 미덕인 양 꾸민 것이라고 말했을 때 언니가 놀란 얼굴을 하셨지요."], ["You will not be surprised again when you hear him repeat it, the next time he is out of temper——Oh, Marian!", "다음번에 그이가 심사가 뒤틀릴 때 그 말을 또 하는 걸 들으시면, 그때는 놀라지 않으실 거예요——아, 매리언!"], ["don’t!", "그만!"], ["don’t!", "그만요!"], ["you hurt me!”", "아파요!”"]], [["I had caught her in my arms, and the sting and torment of my remorse had closed them round her like a vice.", "나는 그 애를 두 팔로 끌어안고 있었는데, 회한의 쓰라림과 고통이 그 팔을 죔쇠처럼 그 애를 둘러 조이고 있었던 것이다. 그렇다!"], ["Yes! my remorse.", "나의 회한이었다."], ["The white despair of Walter’s face, when my cruel words struck him to the heart in the summer-house at Limmeridge, rose before me in mute, unendurable reproach.", "리머리지의 정자에서 내 모진 말이 월터의 심장을 찔렀을 때 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 창백한 절망이, 말 없고 견딜 수 없는 질책이 되어 내 앞에 되살아났다."], ["My hand had pointed the way which led the man my sister loved, step by step, far from his country and his friends.", "내 손이 길을 가리켜, 내 동생이 사랑한 남자를 한 걸음 한 걸음 제 나라와 제 벗들에게서 멀리 떠나가게 만들었다."], ["Between those two young hearts I had stood, to sunder them for ever, the one from the other, and his life and her life lay wasted before me alike in witness of the deed.", "나는 그 두 젊은 마음 사이에 서서 둘을 영영 갈라놓았고, 그의 삶과 그 애의 삶이 그 소행의 증거로 나란히 망가진 채 내 앞에 놓여 있었다."], ["I had done this, and done it for Sir Percival Glyde.", "내가 이 짓을 했다. 그것도 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 위해서."]], [["For Sir Percival Glyde.", "퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 위해서."]], [["I heard her speaking, and I knew by the tone of her voice that she was comforting me—I, who deserved nothing but the reproach of her silence!", "그 애가 말하는 소리가 들렸고, 나는 그 목소리의 어조로 그 애가 나를 위로하고 있다는 걸 알았다—침묵의 질책 말고는 받을 자격이 없는 나를!"], ["How long it was before I mastered the absorbing misery of my own thoughts, I cannot tell.", "내 생각에 사로잡힌 그 비참함을 내가 다스리기까지 얼마나 오랜 시간이 걸렸는지는 알 수 없다."], ["I was first conscious that she was kissing me, and then my eyes seemed to wake on a sudden to their sense of outward things, and I knew that I was looking mechanically straight before me at the prospect of the lake.", "나는 그 애가 내게 입을 맞추고 있다는 것을 먼저 깨달았고, 그러고 나서야 내 눈이 문득 깨어나 바깥 사물을 감각하는 듯하더니, 내가 기계적으로 눈앞의 호수 풍경을 곧장 바라보고 있다는 걸 알았다."]], [["“It is late,”", "“늦었어요.”"], ["I heard her whisper.", "그 애가 속삭이는 소리가 들렸다."], ["“It will be dark in the plantation.”", "“수림 속은 어두워질 거예요.”"], ["She shook my arm and repeated, “Marian!", "그 애는 내 팔을 흔들며 되풀이했다."], ["it will be dark in the plantation.”", "“매리언! 수림 속이 어두워질 거예요.”"]], [["“Give me a minute longer,”", "“조금만 더 있자.”"], ["I said—“a minute, to get better in.”", "내가 말했다. “잠깐만, 마음을 추스르게.”"]], [["I was afraid to trust myself to look at her yet, and I kept my eyes fixed on the view.", "나는 아직 그 애를 마주 볼 자신이 없어서, 눈을 풍경에 못 박아 둔 채로 있었다."]], [["It WAS late.", "정말로 늦은 시각이었다."], ["The dense brown line of trees in the sky had faded in the gathering darkness to the faint resemblance of a long wreath of smoke.", "하늘에 걸린 짙은 갈색 나무 띠는 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서 흐려져, 길게 늘어진 연기 자락처럼 어렴풋해져 있었다."], ["The mist over the lake below had stealthily enlarged, and advanced on us.", "아래쪽 호수 위의 안개는 슬그머니 몸을 불리며 우리 쪽으로 다가와 있었다."], ["The silence was as breathless as ever, but the horror of it had gone, and the solemn mystery of its stillness was all that remained.", "정적은 여전히 숨 막힐 듯했으나 그 소름 끼치던 기운은 가시고, 그 고요함이 지닌 엄숙한 신비만이 남아 있었다."]], [["“We are far from the house,”", "“집에서 너무 멀리 왔어요.”"], ["she whispered.", "그 애가 속삭였다."], ["“Let us go back.”", "“돌아가요.”"]], [["She stopped suddenly, and turned her face from me towards the entrance of the boat-house.", "그 애가 갑자기 멈추더니, 내게서 얼굴을 돌려 보트 창고 입구 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Marian!”", "“매리언!”"], ["she said, trembling violently.", "그 애가 심하게 떨면서 말했다."], ["“Do you see nothing?", "“아무것도 안 보이세요?"], ["Look!”", "보세요!”"]], [["“Where?”", "“어디?”"]], [["“Down there, below us.”", "“저 아래요, 우리 아래쪽에.”"]], [["She pointed.", "그 애가 손으로 가리켰다."], ["My eyes followed her hand, and I saw it too.", "내 눈이 그 손을 좇았고, 나에게도 그것이 보였다."]], [["A living figure was moving over the waste of heath in the distance.", "살아 있는 형체 하나가 저 멀리 히스 황무지 위를 지나가고 있었다."], ["It crossed our range of view from the boat-house, and passed darkly along the outer edge of the mist.", "그것은 보트 창고에서 바라보이는 우리의 시야를 가로질러, 안개의 바깥 가장자리를 따라 어둑하게 지나갔다."], ["It stopped far off, in front of us—waited—and passed on; moving slowly, with the white cloud of mist behind it and above it—slowly, slowly, till it glided by the edge of the boat-house, and we saw it no more.", "저만치 우리 앞에서 멈추어—기다리다가—다시 나아갔다. 흰 안개 구름을 뒤에 그리고 위에 두른 채 천천히 움직이더니—천천히, 천천히, 마침내 보트 창고 가장자리를 스쳐 미끄러져 가고는 더 이상 보이지 않았다."]], [["We were both unnerved by what had passed between us that evening.", "우리 둘 다 그날 저녁 우리 사이에 오간 일로 이미 넋이 나가 있었다."], ["Some minutes elapsed before Laura would venture into the plantation, and before I could make up my mind to lead her back to the house.", "로라가 감히 수림 속으로 들어서기까지, 또 내가 그 애를 집으로 데려가겠다고 마음을 정하기까지 몇 분이 지나갔다."]], [["“Was it a man or a woman?”", "“남자였을까요, 여자였을까요?”"], ["she asked in a whisper, as we moved at last into the dark dampness of the outer air.", "마침내 우리가 바깥의 어둡고 축축한 공기 속으로 걸음을 옮길 때 그 애가 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["“I am not certain.”", "“나도 모르겠구나.”"]], [["“Which do you think?”", "“어느 쪽 같으세요?”"]], [["“It looked like a woman.”", "“여자처럼 보이더구나.”"]], [["“I was afraid it was a man in a long cloak.”", "“전 긴 망토를 걸친 남자일까 봐 무서웠어요.”"]], [["“It may be a man.", "“남자일 수도 있지."], ["In this dim light it is not possible to be certain.”", "이렇게 어스름한 빛 속에서는 확실히 알 수가 없단다.”"]], [["“Wait, Marian!", "“잠깐만요, 매리언!"], ["I’m frightened—I don’t see the path.", "무서워요—길이 안 보여요."], ["Suppose the figure should follow us?”", "그 형체가 우리를 따라오면 어쩌죠?”"]], [["“Not at all likely, Laura.", "“그럴 리 없다, 로라."], ["There is really nothing to be alarmed about.", "정말이지 놀랄 만한 일은 아무것도 없어."], ["The shores of the lake are not far from the village, and they are free to any one to walk on by day or night.", "호숫가는 마을에서 멀지 않고, 낮이든 밤이든 누구나 자유로이 거닐 수 있는 곳이야."], ["It is only wonderful we have seen no living creature there before.”", "오히려 여태 그곳에서 사람 하나 못 본 게 신기할 지경이지.”"]], [["We were now in the plantation.", "우리는 이제 수림 안에 들어와 있었다."], ["It was very dark—so dark, that we found some difficulty in keeping the path.", "몹시 어두웠다—어찌나 어두운지 길을 놓치지 않기가 쉽지 않을 정도였다."], ["I gave Laura my arm, and we walked as fast as we could on our way back.", "나는 로라에게 팔을 내주었고, 우리는 돌아가는 길을 할 수 있는 한 빨리 걸었다."]], [["Before we were half-way through she stopped, and forced me to stop with her.", "절반도 채 지나지 않았을 때 그 애가 멈춰 서더니 나까지 멈추게 했다."], ["She was listening.", "그 애는 귀를 기울이고 있었다."]], [["“Hush,”", "“쉿.”"], ["she whispered.", "그 애가 속삭였다."], ["“I hear something behind us.”", "“뒤에서 무슨 소리가 나요.”"]], [["“Dead leaves,”", "“마른 잎이겠지.”"], ["I said to cheer her, “or a twig blown off the trees.”", "나는 그 애를 안심시키려고 말했다. “아니면 나무에서 떨어진 잔가지거나.”"]], [["“It is summer time, Marian, and there is not a breath of wind.", "“지금은 여름이고 바람 한 점 없잖아요, 매리언."], ["Listen!”", "들어 보세요!”"]], [["I heard the sound too—a sound like a light footstep following us.", "나에게도 그 소리가 들렸다—우리를 뒤따르는 가벼운 발소리 같은 소리가."]], [["“No matter who it is, or what it is,”", "“그게 누구든 무엇이든 상관없다.”"], ["I said, “let us walk on.", "내가 말했다. “계속 걷자."], ["In another minute, if there is anything to alarm us, we shall be near enough to the house to be heard.”", "조금만 더 가면, 놀랄 일이 생기더라도 소리쳐서 집에 들릴 만큼 가까워질 테니.”"]], [["We went on quickly—so quickly, that Laura was breathless by the time we were nearly through the plantation, and within sight of the lighted windows.", "우리는 빠르게 걸었다—어찌나 빨랐던지 수림을 거의 다 빠져나와 불 켜진 창문들이 보일 무렵에는 로라가 숨이 차 있었다."]], [["I waited a moment to give her breathing-time.", "나는 그 애가 숨을 고르도록 잠시 기다렸다."], ["Just as we were about to proceed she stopped me again, and signed to me with her hand to listen once more.", "다시 나아가려는 참에 그 애가 또 나를 붙들더니, 한 번 더 귀를 기울이라고 손짓했다."], ["We both heard distinctly a long, heavy sigh behind us, in the black depths of the trees.", "우리 둘 다 뒤편 나무들의 검은 깊숙한 곳에서 길고 무거운 한숨 소리를 또렷이 들었다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "“거기 누구요?”"], ["I called out.", "내가 소리쳐 물었다."]], [["There was no answer.", "아무 대답도 없었다."]], [["“Who’s there?”", "“거기 누구요?”"], ["I repeated.", "내가 다시 물었다."]], [["An instant of silence followed, and then we heard the light fall of the footsteps again, fainter and fainter—sinking away into the darkness—sinking, sinking, sinking—till they were lost in the silence.", "한순간 정적이 흐르더니, 이내 가벼운 발소리가 다시 들려왔다. 점점 희미해지며—어둠 속으로 잦아들며—잦아들고, 잦아들고, 잦아들어—마침내 정적 속으로 사라져 버렸다."]], [["We hurried out from the trees to the open lawn beyond, crossed it rapidly; and without another word passing between us, reached the house.", "우리는 나무들 사이를 서둘러 빠져나와 그 너머 트인 잔디밭으로 나섰고, 그곳을 빠르게 가로질러, 서로 한마디도 주고받지 않은 채 집에 이르렀다."]], [["In the light of the hall-lamp Laura looked at me, with white cheeks and startled eyes.", "현관 등불 빛 속에서 로라는 새하얗게 질린 뺨과 놀란 눈으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“I am half dead with fear,”", "“무서워서 반쯤 죽는 줄 알았어요.”"], ["she said.", "그 애가 말했다."], ["“Who could it have been?”", "“대체 누구였을까요?”"]], [["“We will try to guess to-morrow,”", "“내일 짐작이나 해 보자꾸나.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“In the meantime say nothing to any one of what we have heard and seen.”", "“그때까지는 우리가 듣고 본 것을 아무에게도 말하지 마라.”"]], [["“Why not?”", "“왜요?”"]], [["“Because silence is safe, and we have need of safety in this house.”", "“침묵은 안전하니까. 그리고 이 집에서 우리에게는 안전이 필요하니까.”"]], [["I sent Laura upstairs immediately, waited a minute to take off my hat and put my hair smooth, and then went at once to make my first investigations in the library, on pretence of searching for a book.", "나는 곧바로 로라를 위층으로 올려 보내고는, 잠시 기다려 모자를 벗고 머리를 매만진 다음, 책을 찾는 척하며 서재로 첫 조사에 나섰다."]], [["There sat the Count, filling out the largest easy-chair in the house, smoking and reading calmly, with his feet on an ottoman, his cravat across his knees, and his shirt collar wide open.", "거기 백작이 이 집에서 가장 큰 안락의자를 가득 채우고 앉아, 오토만에 발을 올리고 넥타이를 무릎에 걸쳐 놓고 셔츠 깃을 활짝 풀어 헤친 채, 담배를 피우며 태연히 책을 읽고 있었다."], ["And there sat Madame Fosco, like a quiet child, on a stool by his side, making cigarettes.", "그리고 그 옆 걸상에는 포스코 백작부인이 얌전한 아이처럼 앉아 담배를 말고 있었다."], ["Neither husband nor wife could, by any possibility, have been out late that evening, and have just got back to the house in a hurry.", "남편도 아내도 그날 저녁 늦게까지 밖에 있다가 방금 허둥지둥 집에 돌아왔을 리는 도무지 없었다."], ["I felt that my object in visiting the library was answered the moment I set eyes on them.", "나는 그들에게 눈길을 두는 순간, 서재를 찾은 내 목적이 이루어졌음을 느꼈다."]], [["Count Fosco rose in polite confusion and tied his cravat on when I entered the room.", "내가 방에 들어서자 포스코 백작은 예의 바르게 당황하며 일어나 넥타이를 맸다."]], [["“Pray don’t let me disturb you,”", "“부디 방해가 되지 않았으면 좋겠군요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I have only come here to get a book.”", "“저는 그저 책을 한 권 가지러 왔을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“All unfortunate men of my size suffer from the heat,”", "“나처럼 덩치 큰 불행한 사내들은 하나같이 더위에 시달리지요.”"], ["said the Count, refreshing himself gravely with a large green fan.", "백작이 커다란 초록색 부채로 자못 근엄하게 부채질을 하며 말했다."], ["“I wish I could change places with my excellent wife.", "“나의 훌륭한 아내와 자리를 바꿀 수만 있다면 좋으련만."], ["She is as cool at this moment as a fish in the pond outside.”", "저 사람은 지금 이 순간 바깥 연못 속의 물고기만큼이나 서늘하답니다.”"]], [["The Countess allowed herself to thaw under the influence of her husband’s quaint comparison.", "백작부인은 남편의 기묘한 비유에 힘입어 마음을 좀 누그러뜨렸다."], ["“I am never warm, Miss Halcombe,”", "“저는 결코 덥지가 않답니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["she remarked, with the modest air of a woman who was confessing to one of her own merits.", "그녀는 자기 미덕 가운데 하나를 고백하는 여자처럼 겸손한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Have you and Lady Glyde been out this evening?”", "“글라이드 부인과 함께 저녁에 밖에 나가셨던가요?”"], ["asked the Count, while I was taking a book from the shelves to preserve appearances.", "내가 겉모양을 갖추려고 선반에서 책 한 권을 꺼내는 동안 백작이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, we went out to get a little air.”", "“네, 바람을 좀 쐬러 나갔었지요.”"]], [["“May I ask in what direction?”", "“어느 쪽으로 가셨는지 여쭈어도 될까요?”"]], [["“In the direction of the lake—as far as the boat-house.”", "“호수 쪽으로요—보트 창고까지 갔습니다.”"]], [["“Aha?", "“아하?"], ["As far as the boat-house?”", "보트 창고까지 말입니까?”"]], [["Under other circumstances I might have resented his curiosity.", "다른 때 같았으면 나는 그의 호기심을 불쾌하게 여겼을지도 모른다."], ["But to-night I hailed it as another proof that neither he nor his wife were connected with the mysterious appearance at the lake.", "그러나 오늘 밤에는 그와 그의 아내 모두 호수의 그 수수께끼 같은 출현과 무관하다는 또 하나의 증거로 여기며 반겼다."]], [["“No more adventures, I suppose, this evening?”", "“오늘 저녁에는 더 이상 모험은 없으셨겠지요?”"], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“No more discoveries, like your discovery of the wounded dog?”", "“다친 개를 발견하셨던 것 같은 발견도 더는 없었고요?”"]], [["He fixed his unfathomable grey eyes on me, with that cold, clear, irresistible glitter in them which always forces me to look at him, and always makes me uneasy while I do look.", "그는 그 헤아릴 길 없는 잿빛 눈을 나에게 못 박았다. 그 눈 속에는 언제나 나로 하여금 그를 쳐다보지 않을 수 없게 만들고, 쳐다보는 동안 내내 나를 불안하게 만드는 차갑고 맑고 뿌리칠 수 없는 광채가 있다."], ["An unutterable suspicion that his mind is prying into mine overcomes me at these times, and it overcame me now.", "그럴 때면 그의 마음이 내 마음속을 캐고 있다는 형언할 수 없는 의심이 나를 짓누르는데, 지금도 그것이 나를 짓눌렀다."]], [["“No,”", "“없었습니다.”"], ["I said shortly; “no adventures—no discoveries.”", "나는 짧게 대답했다. “모험도—발견도 없었어요.”"]], [["I tried to look away from him and leave the room.", "나는 그에게서 눈을 돌려 방을 나가려 했다."], ["Strange as it seems, I hardly think I should have succeeded in the attempt if Madame Fosco had not helped me by causing him to move and look away first.", "이상하게 들리겠지만, 포스코 백작부인이 남편을 먼저 움직이고 눈길을 돌리게 만들어 나를 도와주지 않았더라면 나는 그 시도에 성공하지 못했으리라는 생각이 든다."]], [["“Count, you are keeping Miss Halcombe standing,”", "“백작, 홀콤 양을 계속 세워 두고 계시잖아요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The moment he turned round to get me a chair, I seized my opportunity—thanked him—made my excuses—and slipped out.", "그가 내게 의자를 가져다주려고 돌아서는 순간 나는 기회를 붙잡아—고맙다는 인사를 하고—양해를 구한 뒤—빠져나왔다."]], [["An hour later, when Laura’s maid happened to be in her mistress’s room, I took occasion to refer to the closeness of the night, with a view to ascertaining next how the servants had been passing their time.", "한 시간 뒤 로라의 하녀가 마침 제 마님의 방에 와 있기에, 나는 하인들이 시간을 어떻게 보냈는지 알아볼 셈으로 밤공기가 답답하다는 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Have you been suffering much from the heat downstairs?”", "“아래층에서는 더위에 많이 시달렸니?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“No, miss,”", "“아뇨, 아가씨.”"], ["said the girl, “we have not felt it to speak of.”", "하녀가 말했다. “이렇다 하게 느끼지는 않았습니다.”"]], [["“You have been out in the woods then, I suppose?”", "“그럼 숲에 나가 있었던 게로구나?”"]], [["“Some of us thought of going, miss.", "“몇몇은 나갈까 생각했지요, 아가씨."], ["But cook said she should take her chair into the cool court-yard, outside the kitchen door, and on second thoughts, all the rest of us took our chairs out there too.”", "그런데 요리사가 부엌문 밖 시원한 안뜰로 의자를 내가겠다고 하기에, 다시 생각해 보고는 나머지도 다들 의자를 거기로 내갔습니다.”"]], [["The housekeeper was now the only person who remained to be accounted for.", "이제 남은 것은 가정부의 행방뿐이었다."]], [["“Is Mrs. Michelson gone to bed yet?”", "“마이컬슨 부인은 벌써 잠자리에 들었니?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“I should think not, miss,”", "“아닐 겁니다, 아가씨.”"], ["said the girl, smiling.", "하녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Mrs. Michelson is more likely to be getting up just now than going to bed.”", "“마이컬슨 부인은 지금쯤 자리에 드는 게 아니라 일어나실 참일걸요.”"]], [["“Why?", "“어째서?"], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 말이냐?"], ["Has Mrs. Michelson been taking to her bed in the daytime?”", "마이컬슨 부인이 대낮에 잠자리에 들기라도 했다는 게냐?”"]], [["“No, miss, not exactly, but the next thing to it.", "“그런 건 아닙니다, 아가씨. 하지만 그와 다를 바 없지요."], ["She’s been asleep all the evening on the sofa in her own room.”", "저녁 내내 자기 방 소파에서 주무셨거든요.”"]], [["Putting together what I observed for myself in the library, and what I have just heard from Laura’s maid, one conclusion seems inevitable.", "서재에서 내 눈으로 본 것과 방금 로라의 하녀에게서 들은 것을 맞추어 보면 한 가지 결론이 불가피해 보인다."], ["The figure we saw at the lake was not the figure of Madame Fosco, of her husband, or of any of the servants.", "우리가 호수에서 본 형체는 포스코 백작부인의 것도, 그 남편의 것도, 하인들 가운데 누구의 것도 아니었다."], ["The footsteps we heard behind us were not the footsteps of any one belonging to the house.", "우리가 등 뒤에서 들은 발소리는 이 집에 속한 그 누구의 발소리도 아니었다."]], [["Who could it have been?", "대체 누구였을까?"]], [["It seems useless to inquire.", "물어봐야 소용없어 보인다."], ["I cannot even decide whether the figure was a man’s or a woman’s.", "나는 그 형체가 남자의 것이었는지 여자의 것이었는지조차 판단할 수가 없다."], ["I can only say that I think it was a woman’s.", "다만 여자의 것이었다고 생각한다는 말밖에는 할 수가 없다."]], [["# VI", "# VI"]], [["June 18th.—The misery of self-reproach which I suffered yesterday evening, on hearing what Laura told me in the boat-house, returned in the loneliness of the night, and kept me waking and wretched for hours.", "6월 18일.— 어제저녁 보트 창고에서 로라가 들려준 이야기를 듣고 겪었던 자책의 괴로움이 밤의 적막 속에서 되살아나, 나는 몇 시간이나 잠들지 못한 채 비참하게 뒤척였다."]], [["I lighted my candle at last, and searched through my old journals to see what my share in the fatal error of her marriage had really been, and what I might have once done to save her from it.", "결국 나는 촛불을 켜고 옛 일기장들을 뒤지며, 그 애의 불행한 결혼이라는 치명적인 잘못에 내 몫이 정말로 얼마나 있었는지, 또 그 애를 거기서 구해 내기 위해 내가 한때 무엇을 할 수 있었는지를 살펴보았다."], ["The result soothed me a little for it showed that, however blindly and ignorantly I acted, I acted for the best.", "그 결과는 나를 조금이나마 달래 주었다. 아무리 눈멀고 무지하게 행동했다 해도 나는 최선이라고 믿는 바를 행했다는 것이 드러났기 때문이다."], ["Crying generally does me harm; but it was not so last night—I think it relieved me.", "우는 일은 대개 내게 해롭지만 어젯밤만은 그렇지 않았다—오히려 마음이 가벼워진 것 같다."], ["I rose this morning with a settled resolution and a quiet mind.", "오늘 아침 나는 굳은 결심과 고요한 마음으로 자리에서 일어났다."], ["Nothing Sir Percival can say or do shall ever irritate me again, or make me forget for one moment that I am staying here in defiance of mortifications, insults, and threats, for Laura’s service and for Laura’s sake.", "이제 퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 다시는 나를 화나게 하지 못할 것이며, 내가 온갖 굴욕과 모욕과 위협을 무릅쓰고 이 집에 머무는 것은 오직 로라를 돕기 위해서, 로라를 위해서라는 사실을 한순간도 잊지 않을 것이다."]], [["The speculations in which we might have indulged this morning, on the subject of the figure at the lake and the footsteps in the plantation, have been all suspended by a trifling accident which has caused Laura great regret.", "오늘 아침이라면 호숫가의 그 그림자와 나무숲에서 들린 발소리를 두고 우리끼리 마음껏 이런저런 추측을 해 볼 수도 있었겠지만, 그 모든 것은 로라를 몹시 속상하게 만든 사소한 사고 때문에 중단되고 말았다."], ["She has lost the little brooch I gave her for a keepsake on the day before her marriage.", "그 애가 결혼 전날 내가 기념으로 준 작은 브로치를 잃어버린 것이다."], ["As she wore it when we went out yesterday evening we can only suppose that it must have dropped from her dress, either in the boat-house or on our way back.", "어제저녁 나갈 때는 분명히 달고 있었으니, 보트 창고에서든 돌아오는 길에서든 옷에서 떨어졌으리라고 짐작할 수밖에 없다."], ["The servants have been sent to search, and have returned unsuccessful.", "하인들을 보내 찾게 했지만 헛수고로 돌아왔다."], ["And now Laura herself has gone to look for it.", "그래서 이제는 로라가 직접 찾으러 나갔다."], ["Whether she finds it or not the loss will help to excuse her absence from the house, if Sir Percival returns before the letter from Mr. Gilmore’s partner is placed in my hands.", "찾든 못 찾든, 길모어 씨의 동업자에게서 오는 편지가 내 손에 들어오기 전에 퍼시벌 경이 돌아온다면, 그 애가 집에 없는 이유를 대는 데 이 분실 사건이 도움이 될 것이다."]], [["One o’clock has just struck.", "방금 한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["I am considering whether I had better wait here for the arrival of the messenger from London, or slip away quietly, and watch for him outside the lodge gate.", "나는 런던에서 오는 심부름꾼을 여기서 기다리는 편이 나을지, 아니면 조용히 빠져나가 문지기 집 대문 밖에서 그를 지켜보는 편이 나을지 궁리하는 중이다."]], [["My suspicion of everybody and everything in this house inclines me to think that the second plan may be the best.", "이 집의 모든 사람과 모든 것을 향한 내 의심은 두 번째 방법이 낫겠다는 쪽으로 기운다."], ["The Count is safe in the breakfast-room.", "백작은 아침 식사실에 안전하게 붙들려 있다."], ["I heard him, through the door, as I ran upstairs ten minutes since, exercising his canary-birds at their tricks:—“Come out on my little finger, my pret-pret-pretties!", "십 분 전 이층으로 뛰어 올라가면서 문 너머로 그가 카나리아들에게 재주를 시키는 소리를 들었다. “내 새끼손가락 위로 나오렴, 예쁘-예쁘-예쁜이들아!"], ["Come out, and hop upstairs!", "나와서 계단을 깡충깡충 올라가 보자!"], ["One, two, three—and up!", "하나, 둘, 셋—올라가!"], ["Three, two, one—and down!", "셋, 둘, 하나—내려가!"], ["One, two, three—twit-twit-twit-tweet!”", "하나, 둘, 셋—짹-짹-짹-쪽!”"], ["The birds burst into their usual ecstasy of singing, and the Count chirruped and whistled at them in return, as if he was a bird himself.", "새들은 여느 때처럼 황홀하게 노래를 터뜨렸고, 백작은 마치 자기도 새라도 된 양 짹짹거리고 휘파람을 불며 화답했다."], ["My room door is open, and I can hear the shrill singing and whistling at this very moment.", "내 방문이 열려 있어 바로 이 순간에도 그 날카로운 노랫소리와 휘파람 소리가 들려온다."], ["If I am really to slip out without being observed, now is my time.", "눈에 띄지 않고 정말로 빠져나가려면 지금이 그때다."]], [["FOUR O’CLOCK. The three hours that have passed since I made my last entry have turned the whole march of events at Blackwater Park in a new direction.", "네 시.— 마지막 기록을 한 뒤로 흘러간 세 시간이 블랙워터 파크에서 벌어지는 일들의 행로를 통째로 새로운 방향으로 돌려놓았다."], ["Whether for good or for evil, I cannot and dare not decide.", "그것이 좋은 쪽인지 나쁜 쪽인지는 나로서는 판단할 수도, 감히 판단하려 들 수도 없다."]], [["Let me get back first to the place at which I left off, or I shall lose myself in the confusion of my own thoughts.", "우선 내가 중단했던 자리로 되돌아가자. 그러지 않으면 나 자신의 뒤엉킨 생각 속에서 길을 잃고 말 테니."]], [["I went out, as I had proposed, to meet the messenger with my letter from London at the lodge gate.", "나는 마음먹은 대로, 런던에서 오는 내 편지를 지닌 심부름꾼을 문지기 집 대문에서 맞으려고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["On the stairs I saw no one.", "계단에서는 아무도 마주치지 않았다."], ["In the hall I heard the Count still exercising his birds.", "현관에서는 백작이 여전히 새들에게 재주를 시키는 소리가 들렸다."], ["But on crossing the quadrangle outside, I passed Madame Fosco, walking by herself in her favourite circle, round and round the great fish-pond.", "그러나 바깥 안뜰을 가로지르다가, 커다란 연못 둘레를 빙빙 도는 그 특유의 산책을 홀로 하고 있는 포스코 백작부인과 마주쳤다."], ["I at once slackened my pace, so as to avoid all appearance of being in a hurry, and even went the length, for caution’s sake, of inquiring if she thought of going out before lunch.", "나는 서두르는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않으려고 곧바로 걸음을 늦추었고, 조심하는 뜻에서 점심 전에 외출할 생각이 있느냐고 묻기까지 했다."], ["She smiled at me in the friendliest manner—said she preferred remaining near the house, nodded pleasantly, and re-entered the hall.", "그 여자는 더없이 다정하게 미소 지으며 집 근처에 있는 편이 좋다고 말하고는, 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕이고 현관 안으로 들어갔다."], ["I looked back, and saw that she had closed the door before I had opened the wicket by the side of the carriage gates.", "뒤를 돌아보니, 내가 마차 대문 옆의 쪽문을 열기도 전에 그 여자는 이미 문을 닫은 뒤였다."]], [["In less than a quarter of an hour I reached the lodge.", "채 십오 분도 되지 않아 나는 문지기 집에 이르렀다."]], [["The lane outside took a sudden turn to the left, ran on straight for a hundred yards or so, and then took another sharp turn to the right to join the high-road.", "바깥 오솔길은 왼쪽으로 급히 꺾였다가 백 야드쯤 곧게 뻗어 나간 뒤, 다시 오른쪽으로 급히 꺾여 큰길과 이어졌다."], ["Between these two turns, hidden from the lodge on one side, and from the way to the station on the other, I waited, walking backwards and forwards.", "한쪽으로는 문지기 집에서, 다른 쪽으로는 역으로 가는 길에서 보이지 않는 그 두 굽이 사이에서, 나는 앞뒤로 오가며 기다렸다."], ["High hedges were on either side of me, and for twenty minutes, by my watch, I neither saw nor heard anything.", "양옆으로는 높은 산울타리가 서 있었고, 내 시계로 이십 분 동안 나는 아무것도 보지 못했고 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다."], ["At the end of that time the sound of a carriage caught my ear, and I was met, as I advanced towards the second turning, by a fly from the railway.", "그 시간이 끝날 무렵 마차 소리가 귀에 들어왔고, 두 번째 굽이 쪽으로 나아가던 나는 역에서 오는 삯마차와 마주쳤다."], ["I made a sign to the driver to stop.", "나는 마부에게 세우라는 신호를 보냈다."], ["As he obeyed me a respectable-looking man put his head out of the window to see what was the matter.", "마부가 시키는 대로 하자 점잖아 보이는 남자가 무슨 일인가 하고 창밖으로 고개를 내밀었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "“실례합니다.”"], ["I said, “but am I right in supposing that you are going to Blackwater Park?”", "내가 말했다. “블랙워터 파크로 가시는 길이 맞는지요?”"]], [["“Yes, ma’am.”", "“그렇습니다, 부인.”"]], [["“With a letter for any one?”", "“누구에게 가는 편지를 가지고 계신가요?”"]], [["“With a letter for Miss Halcombe, ma’am.”", "“홀콤 양에게 가는 편지입니다, 부인.”"]], [["“You may give me the letter.", "“그 편지는 제게 주시면 됩니다."], ["I am Miss Halcombe.”", "제가 홀콤입니다.”"]], [["The man touched his hat, got out of the fly immediately, and gave me the letter.", "남자는 모자에 손을 대어 인사하고는 곧바로 삯마차에서 내려 편지를 건네주었다."]], [["I opened it at once and read these lines.", "나는 그 자리에서 편지를 뜯어 다음과 같은 글을 읽었다."], ["I copy them here, thinking it best to destroy the original for caution’s sake.", "조심하는 뜻에서 원본은 없애 버리는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 여기에 옮겨 적어 둔다."]], [["“DEAR MADAM,—Your letter received this morning has caused me very great anxiety.", "“친애하는 부인께.— 오늘 아침에 받은 부인의 편지는 저를 몹시 근심스럽게 했습니다."], ["I will reply to it as briefly and plainly as possible.", "되도록 간략하고 분명하게 답을 드리겠습니다."]], [["“My careful consideration of the statement made by yourself, and my knowledge of Lady Glyde’s position, as defined in the settlement, lead me, I regret to say, to the conclusion that a loan of the trust money to Sir Percival (or, in other words, a loan of some portion of the twenty thousand pounds of Lady Glyde’s fortune) is in contemplation, and that she is made a party to the deed, in order to secure her approval of a flagrant breach of trust, and to have her signature produced against her if she should complain hereafter.", "“부인께서 알려 주신 사정을 신중히 검토하고, 혼인 재산 계약서에 규정된 글라이드 부인의 지위에 관해 제가 아는 바를 아울러 살펴본 결과, 유감스럽게도 저는 신탁 재산을 퍼시벌 경에게 대여하는 일—달리 말하면 글라이드 부인의 재산 이만 파운드 가운데 얼마를 대여하는 일—이 계획되고 있으며, 명백한 신탁 위반에 부인의 승낙을 확보해 두고 훗날 이의를 제기할 때 그 서명을 근거로 부인에게 맞서기 위하여 글라이드 부인을 그 증서의 당사자로 삼으려 한다는 결론에 이르렀습니다."], ["It is impossible, on any other supposition, to account, situated as she is, for her execution to a deed of any kind being wanted at all.", "지금과 같은 처지에 놓인 분에게 어떤 종류의 증서든 서명이 필요할 까닭은, 이 밖의 어떤 가정으로도 설명되지 않습니다."]], [["“In the event of Lady Glyde’s signing such a document, as I am compelled to suppose the deed in question to be, her trustees would be at liberty to advance money to Sir Percival out of her twenty thousand pounds.", "“문제의 증서가 제가 짐작할 수밖에 없는 그런 문서라고 할 때, 글라이드 부인께서 거기에 서명하신다면 부인의 수탁자들은 그 이만 파운드에서 퍼시벌 경에게 돈을 융통해 줄 수 있게 됩니다."], ["If the amount so lent should not be paid back, and if Lady Glyde should have children, their fortune will then be diminished by the sum, large or small, so advanced.", "그렇게 빌려준 금액이 상환되지 않고, 또 글라이드 부인께 자녀가 생긴다면, 그 아이들의 재산은 그렇게 융통된 액수만큼—많든 적든—줄어들게 됩니다."], ["In plainer terms still, the transaction, for anything that Lady Glyde knows to the contrary, may be a fraud upon her unborn children.", "더 분명하게 말씀드리면, 글라이드 부인께서 달리 알고 계신 바가 없는 한, 이 거래는 아직 태어나지도 않은 자녀들에 대한 사기가 될 수 있습니다."]], [["“Under these serious circumstances, I would recommend Lady Glyde to assign as a reason for withholding her signature, that she wishes the deed to be first submitted to myself, as her family solicitor (in the absence of my partner, Mr. Gilmore).", "“이처럼 중대한 사정을 고려할 때, 저는 글라이드 부인께서 서명을 미루는 이유로, 그 증서를 먼저 집안의 사무 변호사인 저에게—제 동업자 길모어 씨가 자리를 비운 터이니—보여 주기를 바란다고 밝히시기를 권합니다."], ["No reasonable objection can be made to taking this course—for, if the transaction is an honourable one, there will necessarily be no difficulty in my giving my approval.", "이 방침에 대해서는 어떤 합당한 반대도 있을 수 없습니다. 그 거래가 떳떳한 것이라면 제가 승인하는 데 아무 어려움이 없을 터이기 때문입니다."]], [["“Sincerely assuring you of my readiness to afford any additional help or advice that may be wanted, I beg to remain, Madam, your faithful servant,", "“더 필요한 도움이나 조언이 있으면 언제든 기꺼이 드리겠다는 뜻을 진심으로 전하며, 이만 줄이겠습니다. 부인의 충실한 종,"]], [["“WILLIAM KYRLE.”", "“윌리엄 카일 올림.”"]], [["I read this kind and sensible letter very thankfully.", "나는 이 친절하고 사리에 밝은 편지를 몹시 고마운 마음으로 읽었다."], ["It supplied Laura with a reason for objecting to the signature which was unanswerable, and which we could both of us understand.", "이 편지는 로라에게 서명을 거부할 반박할 수 없는 이유를, 그것도 우리 둘 다 이해할 수 있는 이유를 마련해 주었다."], ["The messenger waited near me while I was reading to receive his directions when I had done.", "심부름꾼은 내가 다 읽고 나면 지시를 받으려고 곁에서 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Will you be good enough to say that I understand the letter, and that I am very much obliged?”", "“편지 잘 알겠으며 대단히 감사드린다고 전해 주시겠어요?” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “There is no other reply necessary at present.”", "“지금으로서는 달리 회답할 것이 없습니다.”"]], [["Exactly at the moment when I was speaking those words, holding the letter open in my hand, Count Fosco turned the corner of the lane from the high-road, and stood before me as if he had sprung up out of the earth.", "내가 편지를 손에 펼쳐 든 채 그 말을 하고 있던 바로 그 순간, 포스코 백작이 큰길 쪽에서 오솔길 모퉁이를 돌아 나와, 마치 땅에서 솟아나기라도 한 듯 내 앞에 섰다."]], [["The suddenness of his appearance, in the very last place under heaven in which I should have expected to see him, took me completely by surprise.", "이 세상에서 그를 만나리라고는 가장 마지막으로나 생각했을 바로 그 자리에 그가 불쑥 나타났으니, 나는 완전히 허를 찔렸다."], ["The messenger wished me good-morning, and got into the fly again.", "심부름꾼은 내게 인사를 하고 다시 삯마차에 올랐다."], ["I could not say a word to him—I was not even able to return his bow.", "나는 그에게 한마디도 하지 못했고, 그의 인사에 답례조차 하지 못했다."], ["The conviction that I was discovered—and by that man, of all others—absolutely petrified me.", "들켰다는—그것도 하필 그 남자에게 들켰다는—확신이 나를 그야말로 돌처럼 굳어 버리게 했다."]], [["“Are you going back to the house, Miss Halcombe?”", "“댁으로 돌아가시는 길입니까, 홀콤 양?”"], ["he inquired, without showing the least surprise on his side, and without even looking after the fly, which drove off while he was speaking to me.", "그는 자기 쪽에서는 조금도 놀란 기색을 보이지 않고, 자기가 말하는 동안 떠나가는 삯마차를 돌아보지도 않은 채 물었다."]], [["I collected myself sufficiently to make a sign in the affirmative.", "나는 겨우 정신을 추슬러 그렇다는 몸짓을 해 보였다."]], [["“I am going back too,” he said.", "“저도 돌아가는 길입니다.”"], ["“Pray allow me the pleasure of accompanying you.", "그가 말했다. “모시고 갈 기쁨을 부디 허락해 주십시오."], ["Will you take my arm?", "제 팔을 잡으시겠습니까?"], ["You look surprised at seeing me!”", "저를 보고 놀라신 모양이군요!”"]], [["I took his arm.", "나는 그의 팔을 잡았다."], ["The first of my scattered senses that came back was the sense that warned me to sacrifice anything rather than make an enemy of him.", "흩어졌던 감각들 가운데 가장 먼저 돌아온 것은, 무엇을 희생하더라도 이 사람을 적으로 만들지는 말라고 경고하는 감각이었다."]], [["“You look surprised at seeing me!”", "“저를 보고 놀라신 모양이군요!”"], ["he repeated in his quietly pertinacious way.", "그는 특유의 조용하면서도 집요한 태도로 되풀이했다."]], [["“I thought, Count, I heard you with your birds in the breakfast-room,”", "“백작님이 아침 식사실에서 새들과 계신 소리를 들었다고 생각했거든요.”"], ["I answered, as quietly and firmly as I could.", "나는 되도록 침착하고 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["“Surely.", "“그렇습니다."], ["But my little feathered children, dear lady, are only too like other children.", "하지만 내 작은 깃털 아이들도, 친애하는 부인, 여느 아이들과 어찌나 똑같은지요."], ["They have their days of perversity, and this morning was one of them.", "그놈들에게도 심술궂은 날이 있는데 오늘 아침이 바로 그런 날이었습니다."], ["My wife came in as I was putting them back in their cage, and said she had left you going out alone for a walk.", "내가 그놈들을 새장에 도로 넣고 있는데 아내가 들어와서는, 홀콤 양이 혼자 산책을 나가시기에 두고 왔다고 하더군요."], ["You told her so, did you not?”", "부인이 아내에게 그렇게 말씀하셨지요, 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly.”", "“그렇습니다.”"]], [["“Well, Miss Halcombe, the pleasure of accompanying you was too great a temptation for me to resist.", "“그런데 홀콤 양, 함께 걸을 수 있다는 기쁨은 제가 뿌리치기에는 너무나 큰 유혹이었습니다."], ["At my age there is no harm in confessing so much as that, is there?", "제 나이쯤 되면 그만한 고백은 해로울 것이 없지요, 그렇지 않습니까?"], ["I seized my hat, and set off to offer myself as your escort.", "저는 모자를 집어 들고 호위를 자청하러 나섰습니다."], ["Even so fat an old man as Fosco is surely better than no escort at all?", "포스코처럼 뚱뚱한 늙은이라도 호위가 아예 없는 것보다야 낫지 않겠습니까?"], ["I took the wrong path—I came back in despair, and here I am, arrived (may I say it?) at the height of my wishes.”", "길을 잘못 들어—절망한 채 되돌아왔는데—이렇게, 감히 말씀드려도 될까요, 소원의 절정에 이르러 여기 서 있습니다.”"]], [["He talked on in this complimentary strain with a fluency which left me no exertion to make beyond the effort of maintaining my composure.", "그는 이런 찬사조로 유창하게 말을 이어 갔고, 덕분에 나는 침착함을 유지하는 것 말고는 달리 애쓸 일이 없었다."], ["He never referred in the most distant manner to what he had seen in the lane, or to the letter which I still had in my hand.", "그는 오솔길에서 자기가 본 것에 대해서도, 내가 여태 손에 쥐고 있는 편지에 대해서도 아주 먼발치로조차 언급하지 않았다."], ["This ominous discretion helped to convince me that he must have surprised, by the most dishonourable means, the secret of my application in Laura’s interest to the lawyer; and that, having now assured himself of the private manner in which I had received the answer, he had discovered enough to suit his purposes, and was only bent on trying to quiet the suspicions which he knew he must have aroused in my mind.", "이 불길한 신중함은, 그가 더없이 떳떳하지 못한 방법으로 내가 로라를 위해 변호사에게 도움을 청했다는 비밀을 알아냈다는 것, 그리고 이제 내가 그 답장을 은밀히 받았다는 사실까지 확인한 이상 자기 목적에 필요한 만큼은 충분히 알아냈으므로 자기가 내 마음속에 불러일으켰을 것이 분명한 의심을 가라앉히는 데만 마음을 쏟고 있다는 것을 내게 확신시켜 주었다."], ["I was wise enough, under these circumstances, not to attempt to deceive him by plausible explanations, and woman enough, notwithstanding my dread of him, to feel as if my hand was tainted by resting on his arm.", "이런 상황에서 나는 그럴듯한 변명으로 그를 속이려 들지 않을 만큼은 지혜로웠고, 또 그를 두려워하면서도 그의 팔에 얹은 내 손이 더럽혀지는 것 같은 기분을 느낄 만큼은 여자였다."]], [["On the drive in front of the house we met the dog-cart being taken round to the stables.", "집 앞 마찻길에서 우리는 마구간으로 끌려가는 이륜마차와 마주쳤다."], ["Sir Percival had just returned.", "퍼시벌 경이 막 돌아온 것이다."], ["He came out to meet us at the house-door.", "그는 우리를 맞으러 현관으로 나왔다."], ["Whatever other results his journey might have had, it had not ended in softening his savage temper.", "그의 여행이 어떤 결과를 낳았든, 적어도 그 사나운 성미를 누그러뜨리지는 못한 모양이었다."]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["here are two of you come back,”", "둘이나 돌아왔군.”"], ["he said, with a lowering face.", "그가 험악한 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“What is the meaning of the house being deserted in this way?", "“집을 이렇게 비워 두다니 대체 어찌 된 일이오?"], ["Where is Lady Glyde?”", "글라이드 부인은 어디 있소?”"]], [["I told him of the loss of the brooch, and said that Laura had gone into the plantation to look for it.", "나는 브로치를 잃어버린 일을 이야기하고, 로라가 그것을 찾으러 나무숲에 갔다고 말했다."]], [["“Brooch or no brooch,”", "“브로치든 뭐든 간에.”"], ["he growled sulkily, “I recommend her not to forget her appointment in the library this afternoon.", "그는 뾰로통하게 으르렁거렸다. “오늘 오후 서재에서 만나기로 한 약속이나 잊지 말라고 하시오."], ["I shall expect to see her in half an hour.”", "삼십 분 안에 얼굴을 보겠소.”"]], [["I took my hand from the Count’s arm, and slowly ascended the steps.", "나는 백작의 팔에서 손을 떼고 천천히 계단을 올라갔다."], ["He honoured me with one of his magnificent bows, and then addressed himself gaily to the scowling master of the house.", "그는 특유의 장중한 절로 나를 예우한 다음, 잔뜩 인상을 쓰고 있는 이 집 주인에게 유쾌하게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Tell me, Percival,”", "“말해 보게, 퍼시벌.”"], ["he said, “have you had a pleasant drive?", "그가 말했다. “드라이브는 즐거웠는가?"], ["And has your pretty shining Brown Molly come back at all tired?”", "그 어여쁘고 윤기 흐르는 브라운 몰리는 조금이라도 지쳐서 돌아왔는가?”"]], [["“Brown Molly be hanged—and the drive too!", "“브라운 몰리고 뭐고 다 집어치우게—드라이브도!"], ["I want my lunch.”", "나는 점심이나 먹어야겠네.”"]], [["“And I want five minutes’ talk with you, Percival, first,”", "“그리고 나는 먼저 자네와 오 분만 이야기하고 싶네, 퍼시벌.”"], ["returned the Count.", "백작이 응수했다."], ["“Five minutes’ talk, my friend, here on the grass.”", "“오 분이면 되네, 친구. 여기 잔디밭에서.”"]], [["“What about?”", "“무슨 이야기인가?”"]], [["“About business that very much concerns you.”", "“자네와 아주 깊이 관련된 용건일세.”"]], [["I lingered long enough in passing through the hall-door to hear this question and answer, and to see Sir Percival thrust his hands into his pockets in sullen hesitation.", "나는 현관문을 지나가면서 이 물음과 대답을 들을 만큼, 그리고 퍼시벌 경이 뚱하게 망설이며 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 것을 볼 만큼은 오래 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“If you want to badger me with any more of your infernal scruples,”", "“그 지긋지긋한 잔소리로 또 나를 들볶을 작정이라면.”"], ["he said, “I for one won’t hear them.", "그가 말했다. “나만은 듣지 않겠네."], ["I want my lunch.”", "점심이나 먹어야겠어.”"]], [["“Come out here and speak to me,”", "“이리 나와서 내 이야기를 듣게.”"], ["repeated the Count, still perfectly uninfluenced by the rudest speech that his friend could make to him.", "백작은 친구가 퍼부을 수 있는 가장 무례한 말에도 조금도 흔들리지 않은 채 되풀이했다."]], [["Sir Percival descended the steps.", "퍼시벌 경이 계단을 내려갔다."], ["The Count took him by the arm, and walked him away gently.", "백작은 그의 팔을 잡고 부드럽게 이끌어 데려갔다."], ["The “business,” I was sure, referred to the question of the signature.", "그 “용건”이란 서명 문제를 두고 하는 말임이 틀림없었다."], ["They were speaking of Laura and of me beyond a doubt.", "두 사람이 로라와 나에 대해 이야기하고 있다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["I felt heart-sick and faint with anxiety.", "나는 불안으로 가슴이 미어지고 정신이 아뜩했다."], ["It might be of the last importance to both of us to know what they were saying to each other at that moment, and not one word of it could by any possibility reach my ears.", "바로 그 순간 두 사람이 서로 무슨 말을 주고받는지 아는 것이 우리 둘에게는 더없이 중대한 일일 수도 있었건만, 그 말은 단 한마디도 내 귀에 닿을 길이 없었다."]], [["I walked about the house, from room to room, with the lawyer’s letter in my bosom (I was afraid by this time even to trust it under lock and key), till the oppression of my suspense half maddened me.", "나는 변호사의 편지를 가슴에 품은 채—이쯤 되니 자물쇠를 채워 두는 것조차 미덥지 않았다—이 방 저 방으로 집 안을 돌아다녔고, 마침내 조바심의 압박에 반쯤 미칠 지경이 되었다."], ["There were no signs of Laura’s return, and I thought of going out to look for her.", "로라가 돌아온 기척은 없었고, 나는 그 애를 찾으러 나가 볼까 생각했다."], ["But my strength was so exhausted by the trials and anxieties of the morning that the heat of the day quite overpowered me, and after an attempt to get to the door I was obliged to return to the drawing-room and lie down on the nearest sofa to recover.", "그러나 아침나절의 시련과 근심으로 기운이 다 빠져 있던 터라 한낮의 더위가 나를 완전히 짓눌렀고, 문까지 가 보려다가 결국 응접실로 되돌아와 가장 가까운 소파에 누워 몸을 추스를 수밖에 없었다."]], [["I was just composing myself when the door opened softly and the Count looked in.", "겨우 마음을 가라앉히고 있는데 문이 살며시 열리며 백작이 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["“A thousand pardons, Miss Halcombe,”", "“천 번 용서를 구합니다, 홀콤 양.”"], ["he said; “I only venture to disturb you because I am the bearer of good news.", "그가 말했다. “좋은 소식을 전하는 사람이기에 감히 방해할 뿐입니다."], ["Percival—who is capricious in everything, as you know—has seen fit to alter his mind at the last moment, and the business of the signature is put off for the present.", "아시다시피 무슨 일에서나 변덕스러운 퍼시벌이 막판에 마음을 바꾸기로 했고, 그리하여 서명 건은 당분간 미루어졌습니다."], ["A great relief to all of us, Miss Halcombe, as I see with pleasure in your face.", "우리 모두에게 큰 안도이지요, 홀콤 양. 홀콤 양의 얼굴에서도 그것을 기쁘게 읽습니다."], ["Pray present my best respects and felicitations, when you mention this pleasant change of circumstances to Lady Glyde.”", "이 반가운 변화를 글라이드 부인께 전하실 때, 부디 제 각별한 경의와 축하의 말씀도 함께 전해 주십시오.”"]], [["He left me before I had recovered my astonishment.", "그는 내가 놀라움에서 채 벗어나기도 전에 자리를 떴다."], ["There could be no doubt that this extraordinary alteration of purpose in the matter of the signature was due to his influence, and that his discovery of my application to London yesterday, and of my having received an answer to it to-day, had offered him the means of interfering with certain success.", "서명 문제에서 이처럼 뜻밖의 방침 변경이 일어난 것이 그의 영향력 덕분이며, 어제 내가 런던에 도움을 청한 사실과 오늘 그 답장을 받은 사실을 그가 알아냈기에 확실한 성공이 보장된 개입의 수단을 손에 넣었다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["I felt these impressions, but my mind seemed to share the exhaustion of my body, and I was in no condition to dwell on them with any useful reference to the doubtful present or the threatening future.", "나는 이런 인상들을 느꼈지만, 정신도 몸의 탈진을 함께 나누어 가진 듯하여, 불확실한 현재나 위협적인 미래에 유익하게 견주어 그것들을 곱씹을 형편이 못 되었다."], ["I tried a second time to run out and find Laura, but my head was giddy and my knees trembled under me.", "나는 두 번째로 뛰쳐나가 로라를 찾으려 했으나 머리가 어질어질하고 무릎이 후들거렸다."], ["There was no choice but to give it up again and return to the sofa, sorely against my will.", "마음과는 몹시 달리, 다시 포기하고 소파로 돌아가는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The quiet in the house, and the low murmuring hum of summer insects outside the open window, soothed me.", "집 안의 고요와 열린 창 밖에서 나지막이 웅웅거리는 여름 벌레들의 소리가 나를 달래 주었다."], ["My eyes closed of themselves, and I passed gradually into a strange condition, which was not waking—for I knew nothing of what was going on about me, and not sleeping—for I was conscious of my own repose.", "눈이 저절로 감겼고, 나는 차츰 이상한 상태로 빠져들었다. 그것은 깨어 있는 것이 아니었으니 내 주위에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 전혀 알지 못했고, 잠든 것도 아니었으니 나 자신이 쉬고 있다는 것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["In this state my fevered mind broke loose from me, while my weary body was at rest, and in a trance, or day-dream of my fancy—I know not what to call it—I saw Walter Hartright.", "그 상태에서, 지친 몸이 쉬는 동안 열에 들뜬 내 정신은 나에게서 풀려났고, 무아지경이랄까 몽상이랄까—무어라 불러야 할지 모르겠으나—그 속에서 나는 월터 하트라이트를 보았다."], ["I had not thought of him since I rose that morning—Laura had not said one word to me either directly or indirectly referring to him—and yet I saw him now as plainly as if the past time had returned, and we were both together again at Limmeridge House.", "그날 아침 일어난 뒤로 나는 그를 떠올린 적이 없었고, 로라도 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그를 가리키는 말을 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그런데도 나는 지금, 지나간 시간이 되돌아와 우리 둘이 다시 리머리지 하우스에 함께 있기라도 한 듯 또렷하게 그를 보았다."]], [["He appeared to me as one among many other men, none of whose faces I could plainly discern.", "그는 여러 사람들 가운데 하나로 내게 나타났는데, 다른 이들의 얼굴은 하나도 또렷이 알아볼 수 없었다."], ["They were all lying on the steps of an immense ruined temple.", "그들은 모두 거대한 폐허가 된 신전의 계단에 누워 있었다."], ["Colossal tropical trees—with rank creepers twining endlessly about their trunks, and hideous stone idols glimmering and grinning at intervals behind leaves and stalks and branches—surrounded the temple and shut out the sky, and threw a dismal shadow over the forlorn band of men on the steps.", "줄기를 끝없이 휘감은 무성한 덩굴과, 잎사귀와 줄기와 가지 뒤에서 이따금 희끄무레 번득이며 이를 드러내고 웃는 흉측한 돌 우상들을 거느린 거대한 열대의 나무들이 신전을 에워싸고 하늘을 가려, 계단 위의 그 가련한 무리 위로 음산한 그림자를 드리웠다."], ["White exhalations twisted and curled up stealthily from the ground, approached the men in wreaths like smoke, touched them, and stretched them out dead, one by one, in the places where they lay.", "하얀 김이 땅에서 슬며시 뒤틀리며 피어올라 연기처럼 화환을 이루며 사람들에게 다가가더니, 그들을 건드려 저마다 누운 자리에서 하나씩 하나씩 시체로 뻗게 만들었다."], ["An agony of pity and fear for Walter loosened my tongue, and I implored him to escape.", "월터를 향한 연민과 두려움의 고통이 내 혀를 풀어놓아, 나는 그에게 달아나라고 애원했다."], ["“Come back, come back!”", "“돌아와요, 돌아와요!”"], ["I said.", "나는 말했다."], ["“Remember your promise to HER and to ME.", "“그 애에게, 그리고 나에게 한 약속을 기억해요."], ["Come back to us before the Pestilence reaches you and lays you dead like the rest!”", "역병이 당신에게 닿아 다른 이들처럼 당신을 죽여 눕히기 전에 우리에게 돌아와요!”"]], [["He looked at me with an unearthly quiet in his face. “Wait,”", "그는 이 세상 것 같지 않은 고요를 얼굴에 띠고 나를 바라보았다."], ["he said, “I shall come back.", "“기다리십시오.” 그가 말했다."], ["The night when I met the lost Woman on the highway was the night which set my life apart to be the instrument of a Design that is yet unseen.", "“저는 돌아갑니다. 큰길에서 그 길 잃은 여인을 만난 밤은, 아직 보이지 않는 어떤 섭리의 도구가 되도록 제 삶을 따로 떼어 놓은 밤이었습니다."], ["Here, lost in the wilderness, or there, welcomed back in the land of my birth, I am still walking on the dark road which leads me, and you, and the sister of your love and mine, to the unknown Retribution and the inevitable End.", "이곳 황야에서 길을 잃고 있든, 저곳 태어난 땅에서 환영을 받든, 저는 여전히 저와 당신과 당신과 저의 사랑을 받는 그분을 알 수 없는 응보와 피할 수 없는 종말로 이끄는 어두운 길을 걷고 있습니다."], ["Wait and look.", "기다리며 지켜보십시오."], ["The Pestilence which touches the rest will pass ME.”", "다른 이들을 건드리는 역병은 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”"]], [["I saw him again.", "나는 그를 다시 보았다."], ["He was still in the forest, and the numbers of his lost companions had dwindled to very few.", "그는 여전히 숲속에 있었고, 그와 함께 헤매던 동료들의 수는 아주 적게 줄어 있었다."], ["The temple was gone, and the idols were gone—and in their place the figures of dark, dwarfish men lurked murderously among the trees, with bows in their hands, and arrows fitted to the string.", "신전도 사라지고 우상들도 사라졌으며, 그 자리에는 손에 활을 들고 시위에 화살을 메긴 검고 왜소한 사람들의 형상이 나무들 사이에 살기를 띠고 숨어 있었다."], ["Once more I feared for Walter, and cried out to warn him.", "나는 다시 한번 월터가 걱정되어 그에게 경고하려고 소리쳤다."], ["Once more he turned to me, with the immovable quiet in his face.", "다시 한번 그는 흔들림 없는 고요를 얼굴에 띤 채 나를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Another step,”", "“어두운 길 위에서.”"], ["he said, “on the dark road.", "그가 말했다. “또 한 걸음입니다."], ["Wait and look.", "기다리며 지켜보십시오."], ["The arrows that strike the rest will spare me.”", "다른 이들을 맞히는 화살은 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”"]], [["I saw him for the third time in a wrecked ship, stranded on a wild, sandy shore.", "나는 세 번째로 그를 보았다. 거친 모래 해안에 좌초한 난파선 위였다."], ["The overloaded boats were making away from him for the land, and he alone was left to sink with the ship.", "짐이 넘치도록 실린 보트들은 그를 남겨 둔 채 뭍을 향해 멀어져 갔고, 그 혼자 배와 함께 가라앉도록 남겨져 있었다."], ["I cried to him to hail the hindmost boat, and to make a last effort for his life.", "나는 맨 뒤의 보트를 불러 세워 목숨을 위한 마지막 안간힘을 써 보라고 그에게 외쳤다."], ["The quiet face looked at me in return, and the unmoved voice gave me back the changeless reply.", "그 고요한 얼굴이 나를 돌아보았고, 흔들림 없는 목소리가 변함없는 대답을 돌려주었다."], ["“Another step on the journey.", "“여정의 또 한 걸음입니다."], ["Wait and look.", "기다리며 지켜보십시오."], ["The Sea which drowns the rest will spare me.”", "다른 이들을 빠뜨리는 바다는 저를 비껴갈 것입니다.”"]], [["I saw him for the last time.", "나는 마지막으로 그를 보았다."], ["He was kneeling by a tomb of white marble, and the shadow of a veiled woman rose out of the grave beneath and waited by his side.", "그는 흰 대리석 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고 있었고, 그 아래 무덤에서 베일을 쓴 여인의 그림자가 솟아올라 그의 곁에서 기다렸다."], ["The unearthly quiet of his face had changed to an unearthly sorrow.", "이 세상 것 같지 않던 그의 얼굴의 고요는 이 세상 것 같지 않은 슬픔으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["But the terrible certainty of his words remained the same.", "그러나 그 말의 무서운 확신만은 그대로였다."], ["“Darker and darker,”", "“점점 더 어두워지고.”"], ["he said; “farther and farther yet.", "그가 말했다. “점점 더 멀어집니다."], ["Death takes the good, the beautiful, and the young—and spares me.", "죽음은 선한 이와 아름다운 이와 젊은 이를 데려가고—저는 남겨 둡니다."], ["The Pestilence that wastes, the Arrow that strikes, the Sea that drowns, the Grave that closes over Love and Hope, are steps of my journey, and take me nearer and nearer to the End.”", "스러뜨리는 역병, 꿰뚫는 화살, 빠뜨리는 바다, 사랑과 희망 위로 닫히는 무덤, 그 모두가 제 여정의 걸음들이며, 저를 종말에 점점 더 가까이 데려갑니다.”"]], [["My heart sank under a dread beyond words, under a grief beyond tears.", "말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움에, 눈물로도 다 할 수 없는 슬픔에 내 가슴은 내려앉았다."], ["The darkness closed round the pilgrim at the marble tomb—closed round the veiled woman from the grave—closed round the dreamer who looked on them.", "어둠이 대리석 무덤 곁의 순례자를 에워쌌고—무덤에서 나온 베일 쓴 여인을 에워쌌고—그들을 바라보던 꿈꾸는 자를 에워쌌다."], ["I saw and heard no more.", "나는 더 보지도 듣지도 못했다."]], [["I was aroused by a hand laid on my shoulder.", "어깨에 놓인 손이 나를 깨웠다."], ["It was Laura’s.", "로라의 손이었다."]], [["She had dropped on her knees by the side of the sofa.", "그 애는 소파 옆에 무릎을 꿇은 채였다."], ["Her face was flushed and agitated, and her eyes met mine in a wild bewildered manner.", "얼굴은 상기되어 있고 흥분한 기색이었으며, 눈은 사납고 당황한 빛으로 내 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["I started the instant I saw her.", "나는 그 애를 보는 순간 소스라쳤다."]], [["“What has happened?”", "“무슨 일이야?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["“What has frightened you?”", "“뭐가 그렇게 무서웠어?”"]], [["She looked round at the half-open door, put her lips close to my ear, and answered in a whisper—", "그 애는 반쯤 열린 문 쪽을 돌아보고는, 내 귀에 입술을 바싹 대고 속삭이듯 대답했다—"]], [["“Marian!—the figure at the lake—the footsteps last night—I’ve just seen her!", "“매리언!—호숫가의 그 그림자—어젯밤의 그 발소리—방금 그 여자를 봤어!"], ["I’ve just spoken to her!”", "방금 그 여자와 이야기까지 했어!”"]], [["“Who, for Heaven’s sake?”", "“맙소사, 누구를?”"]], [["“Anne Catherick.”", "“앤 캐서릭.”"]], [["I was so startled by the disturbance in Laura’s face and manner, and so dismayed by the first waking impressions of my dream, that I was not fit to bear the revelation which burst upon me when that name passed her lips.", "나는 로라의 얼굴과 태도에 깃든 동요에 몹시 놀랐고, 꿈에서 막 깨어난 순간의 인상들에 몹시 심란해 있던 터라, 그 이름이 그 애의 입술을 스치는 순간 내게 터져 나온 그 계시를 감당할 형편이 못 되었다."], ["I could only stand rooted to the floor, looking at her in breathless silence.", "나는 그저 바닥에 못 박힌 듯 서서 숨죽인 채 그 애를 바라볼 뿐이었다."]], [["She was too much absorbed by what had happened to notice the effect which her reply had produced on me.", "그 애는 방금 겪은 일에 너무 사로잡혀 있어서 자기 대답이 내게 미친 영향을 알아차리지 못했다."], ["“I have seen Anne Catherick!", "“앤 캐서릭을 봤어!"], ["I have spoken to Anne Catherick!”", "앤 캐서릭과 이야기했다니까!”"], ["she repeated as if I had not heard her.", "그 애는 내가 못 들은 줄 알고 되풀이했다."], ["“Oh, Marian, I have such things to tell you!", "“아, 매리언, 언니한테 할 이야기가 얼마나 많은지!"], ["Come away—we may be interrupted here—come at once into my room.”", "이리 와—여기서는 누가 끼어들지도 몰라—당장 내 방으로 가자.”"]], [["With those eager words she caught me by the hand, and led me through the library, to the end room on the ground floor, which had been fitted up for her own especial use.", "그렇게 다급히 말하며 그 애는 내 손을 잡고 서재를 지나, 일층 끝 방으로—오로지 그 애만 쓰도록 꾸며진 방으로—나를 데려갔다."], ["No third person, except her maid, could have any excuse for surprising us here.", "그곳이라면 하녀 말고는 어느 누구도 우리를 불쑥 찾아올 구실이 없었다."], ["She pushed me in before her, locked the door, and drew the chintz curtains that hung over the inside.", "그 애는 나를 앞세워 밀어 넣고 문을 잠근 뒤, 안쪽에 드리워진 사라사 커튼을 쳤다."]], [["The strange, stunned feeling which had taken possession of me still remained.", "나를 사로잡았던 그 이상한 마비의 느낌은 여전히 남아 있었다."], ["But a growing conviction that the complications which had long threatened to gather about her, and to gather about me, had suddenly closed fast round us both, was now beginning to penetrate my mind.", "그러나 오래도록 그 애 주위에, 그리고 내 주위에 모여들려고 위협해 오던 얽히고설킨 일들이 이제 갑자기 우리 둘을 단단히 옭아매었다는 확신이 점점 커지며 내 마음을 파고들기 시작했다."], ["I could not express it in words—I could hardly even realise it dimly in my own thoughts.", "나는 그것을 말로 표현할 수 없었고, 내 생각 속에서조차 어렴풋이나마 실감하기 어려웠다."], ["“Anne Catherick!”", "“앤 캐서릭!”"], ["I whispered to myself, with useless, helpless reiteration—“Anne Catherick!”", "나는 아무 소용도 없이, 어찌할 바도 모른 채 되풀이하며 혼잣말로 속삭였다. “앤 캐서릭!”"]], [["Laura drew me to the nearest seat, an ottoman in the middle of the room.", "로라는 나를 가장 가까운 자리로, 방 한가운데의 등받이 없는 긴 의자로 이끌었다. “봐!”"], ["“Look!”", "그 애가 말했다."], ["she said, “look here!”—and pointed to the bosom of her dress.", "“여기 좀 봐!” 그러고는 자기 옷의 가슴께를 가리켰다."]], [["I saw, for the first time, that the lost brooch was pinned in its place again.", "나는 잃어버렸던 브로치가 제자리에 다시 꽂혀 있는 것을 그제야 보았다."], ["There was something real in the sight of it, something real in the touching of it afterwards, which seemed to steady the whirl and confusion in my thoughts, and to help me to compose myself.", "그것을 보는 데에도, 그러고 나서 손으로 만져 보는 데에도 무언가 실재하는 것이 있었고, 그것이 내 생각 속의 소용돌이와 혼란을 가라앉히고 마음을 다잡도록 도와주는 듯했다."]], [["“Where did you find your brooch?”", "“브로치는 어디서 찾았어?”"], ["The first words I could say to her were the words which put that trivial question at that important moment.", "그토록 중대한 순간에 내가 그 애에게 처음으로 건넬 수 있었던 말은 그런 하찮은 물음이었다."]], [["“SHE found it, Marian.”", "“그 여자가 찾아 줬어, 매리언.”"]], [["“Where?”", "“어디서?”"]], [["“On the floor of the boat-house.", "“보트 창고 바닥에서."], ["Oh, how shall I begin—how shall I tell you about it!", "아, 어디서부터 시작해야 하지—어떻게 이야기해야 하지!"], ["She talked to me so strangely—she looked so fearfully ill—she left me so suddenly!”", "그 여자는 나한테 참 이상하게 말했어—얼굴빛이 무섭도록 아파 보였어—그러다 갑자기 가 버렸어!”"]], [["Her voice rose as the tumult of her recollections pressed upon her mind.", "밀려드는 기억의 소용돌이가 그 애의 마음을 짓누르자 목소리가 높아졌다."], ["The inveterate distrust which weighs, night and day, on my spirits in this house, instantly roused me to warn her—just as the sight of the brooch had roused me to question her, the moment before.", "이 집에 있는 동안 밤낮으로 내 마음을 짓누르는 그 뿌리 깊은 불신이 그 순간 나를 일깨워 그 애에게 주의를 주게 했다. 바로 조금 전에 브로치를 본 것이 나를 일깨워 묻게 했던 것처럼."]], [["“Speak low,” I said.", "“목소리를 낮춰.”"], ["“The window is open, and the garden path runs beneath it.", "내가 말했다. “창문이 열려 있고, 그 아래로 정원 길이 지나가."], ["Begin at the beginning, Laura.", "처음부터 시작하렴, 로라."], ["Tell me, word for word, what passed between that woman and you.”", "그 여자와 네 사이에 오간 말을 한마디도 빼놓지 말고 다 이야기해 줘.”"]], [["“Shall I close the window?”", "“창문을 닫을까?”"]], [["“No, only speak low—only remember that Anne Catherick is a dangerous subject under your husband’s roof.", "“아니, 목소리만 낮춰—앤 캐서릭이 네 남편의 지붕 아래에서는 위험한 이야깃거리라는 것만 잊지 마."], ["Where did you first see her?”", "그 여자를 어디서 처음 봤어?”"]], [["“At the boat-house, Marian.", "“보트 창고에서, 매리언."], ["I went out, as you know, to find my brooch, and I walked along the path through the plantation, looking down on the ground carefully at every step.", "언니도 알다시피 나는 브로치를 찾으러 나가서 나무숲 사이 오솔길을 따라 걸으며 한 걸음 한 걸음 조심스럽게 땅을 살폈어."], ["In that way I got on, after a long time, to the boat-house, and as soon as I was inside it, I went on my knees to hunt over the floor.", "그렇게 한참 만에 보트 창고까지 갔고, 안으로 들어서자마자 무릎을 꿇고 바닥을 뒤졌지."], ["I was still searching with my back to the doorway, when I heard a soft, strange voice behind me say, ‘Miss Fairlie.’”", "문간을 등진 채 여전히 찾고 있는데, 등 뒤에서 부드럽고 낯선 목소리가 ‘페얼리 양’ 하고 부르는 소리가 들렸어.”"]], [["“Miss Fairlie!”", "“페얼리 양이라고!”"]], [["“Yes, my old name—the dear, familiar name that I thought I had parted from for ever.", "“응, 내 옛 이름—영영 떠나보낸 줄로만 알았던 그 정답고 익숙한 이름."], ["I started up—not frightened, the voice was too kind and gentle to frighten anybody—but very much surprised.", "나는 벌떡 일어났어—겁이 나서가 아니라, 그 목소리는 누구를 겁줄 만한 것이 아니라 너무나 다정하고 상냥했으니까—그저 몹시 놀라서였지."], ["There, looking at me from the doorway, stood a woman, whose face I never remembered to have seen before—”", "거기 문간에서 나를 바라보며, 전에 본 기억이라고는 도무지 없는 얼굴의 여자가 서 있었어—”"]], [["“How was she dressed?”", "“어떤 옷차림이었어?”"]], [["“She had a neat, pretty white gown on, and over it a poor worn thin dark shawl.", "“깔끔하고 예쁜 흰 드레스를 입고, 그 위에 낡고 얇은 초라한 검은 숄을 걸치고 있었어."], ["Her bonnet was of brown straw, as poor and worn as the shawl.", "보닛은 갈색 밀짚이었는데 숄만큼이나 초라하고 해져 있었지."], ["I was struck by the difference between her gown and the rest of her dress, and she saw that I noticed it.", "나는 그 드레스와 나머지 옷차림 사이의 차이에 눈길이 갔고, 내가 그걸 알아챈 걸 그 여자도 눈치챘어."], ["‘Don’t look at my bonnet and shawl,’ she said, speaking in a quick, breathless, sudden way; ‘if I mustn’t wear white, I don’t care what I wear.", "‘내 보닛이랑 숄은 보지 마세요.’ 그 여자가 빠르고 숨 가쁘고 불쑥 내뱉는 투로 말했어. ‘흰옷을 입을 수 없다면 무얼 입든 상관없어요."], ["Look at my gown as much as you please—I’m not ashamed of that.’ Very strange, was it not?", "드레스는 얼마든지 보셔도 돼요—그건 부끄럽지 않으니까요.’ 참 이상하지 않아?"], ["Before I could say anything to soothe her, she held out one of her hands, and I saw my brooch in it.", "내가 그 여자를 달랠 말을 꺼내기도 전에 한 손을 내밀었는데, 그 손에 내 브로치가 있었어."], ["I was so pleased and so grateful, that I went quite close to her to say what I really felt.", "나는 어찌나 기쁘고 고맙던지, 정말 느낀 그대로를 말하려고 아주 가까이 다가갔지."], ["‘Are you thankful enough to do me one little kindness?’ she asked.", "‘그렇게 고마우시면 작은 친절 하나만 베풀어 주시겠어요?’ 그 여자가 물었어."], ["‘Yes, indeed,’ I answered, ‘any kindness in my power I shall be glad to show you.’ ‘Then let me pin your brooch on for you, now I have found it.’ Her request was so unexpected, Marian, and she made it with such extraordinary eagerness, that I drew back a step or two, not well knowing what to do.", "‘그럼요.’ 내가 대답했어. ‘제 힘이 닿는 친절이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 해 드릴게요.’ ‘그럼 이왕 제가 찾았으니, 브로치를 제가 달아 드리게 해 주세요.’ 그 부탁이 어찌나 뜻밖이었는지, 매리언, 게다가 어찌나 유별나게 간절히 청하던지 나는 어찌할 바를 몰라 한두 걸음 물러섰어."], ["‘Ah!’ she said, ‘your mother would have let me pin on the brooch.’ There was something in her voice and her look, as well as in her mentioning my mother in that reproachful manner, which made me ashamed of my distrust.", "‘아!’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘당신 어머니라면 제가 브로치를 달게 해 주셨을 텐데.’ 그 목소리와 눈빛에도, 또 그렇게 나무라듯 우리 어머니를 들먹이는 데에도 무언가가 있어서, 나는 내가 의심한 것이 부끄러워졌어."], ["I took her hand with the brooch in it, and put it up gently on the bosom of my dress.", "나는 브로치를 쥔 그 손을 잡아 내 옷 가슴께로 살며시 가져갔지."], ["‘You knew my mother?’ I said.", "‘우리 어머니를 아셨어요?’ 내가 말했어."], ["‘Was it very long ago?", "‘아주 오래전 일인가요?"], ["have I ever seen you before?’ Her hands were busy fastening the brooch: she stopped and pressed them against my breast.", "제가 전에 당신을 본 적이 있나요?’ 그 여자의 손은 브로치를 채우느라 바빴는데, 문득 멈추더니 그 손을 내 가슴에 지그시 눌렀어."], ["‘You don’t remember a fine spring day at Limmeridge,’ she said, ‘and your mother walking down the path that led to the school, with a little girl on each side of her?", "‘리머리지의 어느 좋은 봄날, 당신 어머니가 양옆에 어린 여자아이를 하나씩 데리고 학교로 가는 길을 걸어 내려가던 걸 기억 못 하시죠.’ 그 여자가 말했어."], ["I have had nothing else to think of since, and I remember it.", "‘저는 그 뒤로 다른 건 아무것도 생각해 본 적이 없어서 그 일을 기억해요."], ["You were one of the little girls, and I was the other.", "그 어린 여자아이 하나가 당신이었고, 다른 하나가 저였어요."], ["Pretty, clever Miss Fairlie, and poor dazed Anne Catherick were nearer to each other then than they are now!’”", "예쁘고 영리한 페얼리 양과 가엾고 얼빠진 앤 캐서릭은 그때는 지금보다 서로 훨씬 가까웠지요!’”"]], [["“Did you remember her, Laura, when she told you her name?”", "“그 여자가 이름을 말했을 때 기억이 났니, 로라?”"]], [["“Yes, I remembered your asking me about Anne Catherick at Limmeridge, and your saying that she had once been considered like me.”", "“응, 리머리지에서 언니가 앤 캐서릭에 대해 내게 물었던 것도, 한때 그 여자가 나를 닮았다는 말을 들었다고 언니가 했던 것도 기억났어.”"]], [["“What reminded you of that, Laura?”", "“무엇 때문에 그 일이 떠올랐어, 로라?”"]], [["“SHE reminded me.", "“그 여자가 떠오르게 했어."], ["While I was looking at her, while she was very close to me, it came over my mind suddenly that we were like each other!", "내가 그 여자를 바라보는 동안, 그 여자가 나한테 아주 가까이 있는 동안, 우리가 서로 닮았다는 생각이 불쑥 떠올랐어!"], ["Her face was pale and thin and weary—but the sight of it startled me, as if it had been the sight of my own face in the glass after a long illness.", "그 얼굴은 창백하고 여위고 지쳐 있었지만—그걸 보는 순간 나는 오랜 병을 앓고 난 뒤 거울에 비친 내 얼굴을 보기라도 한 듯 소스라쳤어."], ["The discovery—I don’t know why—gave me such a shock, that I was perfectly incapable of speaking to her for the moment.”", "그 발견이—왜인지는 모르겠지만—어찌나 충격이었는지, 나는 그 순간 그 여자에게 도무지 한마디도 할 수가 없었어.”"]], [["“Did she seem hurt by your silence?”", "“네가 잠자코 있어서 그 여자가 상처를 받은 것 같았니?”"]], [["“I am afraid she was hurt by it.", "“상처를 받은 것 같아 마음이 아파."], ["‘You have not got your mother’s face,’ she said, ‘or your mother’s heart.", "‘당신은 어머니의 얼굴을 닮지 않았군요.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘어머니의 마음도요."], ["Your mother’s face was dark, and your mother’s heart, Miss Fairlie, was the heart of an angel.’ ‘I am sure I feel kindly towards you,’ I said, ‘though I may not be able to express it as I ought.", "당신 어머니의 얼굴은 검은빛이었고, 당신 어머니의 마음은, 페얼리 양, 천사의 마음이었어요.’ ‘저도 당신에게 다정한 마음을 품고 있는 건 분명해요.’ 내가 말했어. ‘마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 잘 표현하지는 못할지라도요."], ["Why do you call me Miss Fairlie?——’ ‘Because I love the name of Fairlie and hate the name of Glyde,’ she broke out violently.", "그런데 왜 저를 페얼리 양이라고 부르시나요——’ ‘페얼리라는 이름은 사랑하고 글라이드라는 이름은 미워하니까요.’ 그 여자가 사납게 터뜨렸어."], ["I had seen nothing like madness in her before this, but I fancied I saw it now in her eyes.", "그전까지는 그 여자에게서 미친 기색이라고는 조금도 보지 못했는데, 그때는 그 눈에서 그것을 본 것 같았어."], ["‘I only thought you might not know I was married,’ I said, remembering the wild letter she wrote to me at Limmeridge, and trying to quiet her.", "‘제가 결혼한 걸 모르실 수도 있겠다 싶었을 뿐이에요.’ 내가 말했어. 리머리지에서 그 여자가 내게 보낸 그 정신 나간 편지를 떠올리며, 진정시키려고 애쓰면서."], ["She sighed bitterly, and turned away from me.", "그 여자는 씁쓸하게 한숨을 쉬고 나에게서 몸을 돌렸어."], ["‘Not know you were married?’ she repeated.", "‘결혼한 걸 모른다고요?’ 그 여자가 되뇌었어."], ["‘I am here BECAUSE you are married.", "‘저는 당신이 결혼했기 때문에 여기 온 거예요."], ["I am here to make atonement to you, before I meet your mother in the world beyond the grave.’ She drew farther and farther away from me, till she was out of the boat-house, and then she watched and listened for a little while.", "저세상에서 당신 어머니를 만나기 전에, 당신에게 속죄하려고 온 거예요.’ 그 여자는 점점 더 나에게서 멀어져 마침내 보트 창고 밖으로 나갔고, 그러고는 잠시 동안 살피며 귀를 기울였어."], ["When she turned round to speak again, instead of coming back, she stopped where she was, looking in at me, with a hand on each side of the entrance.", "다시 말하려고 돌아섰을 때는 안으로 들어오는 대신 그 자리에 선 채로, 입구 양쪽에 한 손씩 짚고 나를 들여다보며 이렇게 말했어."], ["‘Did you see me at the lake last night?’ she said.", "‘어젯밤 호숫가에서 저를 보셨죠?"], ["‘Did you hear me following you in the wood?", "숲속에서 제가 뒤따라오는 소리를 들으셨죠?"], ["I have been waiting for days together to speak to you alone—I have left the only friend I have in the world, anxious and frightened about me—I have risked being shut up again in the mad-house—and all for your sake, Miss Fairlie, all for your sake.’ Her words alarmed me, Marian, and yet there was something in the way she spoke that made me pity her with all my heart.", "저는 당신과 단둘이 이야기하려고 며칠씩이나 기다렸어요—세상에 하나뿐인 친구를 저 때문에 애태우고 겁먹은 채로 두고 왔고요—다시 정신병원에 갇힐 위험까지 무릅썼어요—그게 다 당신을 위해서예요, 페얼리 양, 다 당신을 위해서요.’ 그 말이 나를 놀라게 했어, 매리언. 그런데도 그 말투에는 무언가가 있어서 나는 온 마음으로 그 여자가 가엾어졌어."], ["I am sure my pity must have been sincere, for it made me bold enough to ask the poor creature to come in, and sit down in the boat-house, by my side.”", "내 연민이 진심이었던 게 분명해. 그 덕분에 나는 그 가엾은 사람에게 안으로 들어와 보트 창고에 내 옆에 앉으라고 청할 만큼 대담해졌으니까.”"]], [["“Did she do so?”", "“그 여자가 그렇게 했니?”"]], [["“No.", "“아니."], ["She shook her head, and told me she must stop where she was, to watch and listen, and see that no third person surprised us.", "고개를 젓더니, 아무도 우리를 불쑥 덮치지 못하도록 살피고 귀 기울여야 하니 그 자리에 있어야 한다고 했어."], ["And from first to last, there she waited at the entrance, with a hand on each side of it, sometimes bending in suddenly to speak to me, sometimes drawing back suddenly to look about her.", "그러고는 처음부터 끝까지 입구에 서서 양쪽에 한 손씩 짚은 채, 어떤 때는 불쑥 몸을 안으로 기울여 내게 말하고, 어떤 때는 불쑥 물러나 사방을 둘러보았지. ‘어제도 여기 왔었어요.’ 그 여자가 말했어."], ["‘I was here yesterday,’ she said, ‘before it came dark, and I heard you, and the lady with you, talking together.", "‘어두워지기 전에요. 그리고 당신하고 같이 있던 부인이 이야기하는 걸 들었어요."], ["I heard you tell her about your husband.", "당신이 남편에 대해 그분에게 이야기하는 걸 들었어요."], ["I heard you say you had no influence to make him believe you, and no influence to keep him silent.", "남편이 당신 말을 믿게 할 힘도 없고 입을 다물게 할 힘도 없다고 하는 걸 들었어요."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I knew what those words meant—my conscience told me while I was listening.", "저는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 알았어요—듣고 있는 동안 제 양심이 알려 주었어요."], ["Why did I ever let you marry him!", "왜 제가 당신을 그 사람과 결혼하게 내버려 뒀을까!"], ["Oh, my fear—my mad, miserable, wicked fear!’ She covered up her face in her poor worn shawl, and moaned and murmured to herself behind it.", "아, 내 두려움—내 미치고 비참하고 사악한 두려움!’ 그 여자는 낡고 해진 숄로 얼굴을 가리고, 그 뒤에서 신음하며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸어."], ["I began to be afraid she might break out into some terrible despair which neither she nor I could master.", "나는 그 여자도 나도 감당할 수 없는 무서운 절망에 빠져 버릴까 봐 겁이 나기 시작했어."], ["‘Try to quiet yourself,’ I said; ‘try to tell me how you might have prevented my marriage.’ She took the shawl from her face, and looked at me vacantly.", "‘진정하려고 해 봐요.’ 내가 말했어. ‘제 결혼을 어떻게 막을 수 있었다는 건지 말해 봐요.’ 그 여자는 얼굴에서 숄을 걷고 멍하니 나를 바라보았어."], ["‘I ought to have had heart enough to stop at Limmeridge,’ she answered.", "‘리머리지에 머무를 만한 담력이 있었어야 했어요.’ 그 여자가 대답했어."], ["‘I ought never to have let the news of his coming there frighten me away.", "‘그 사람이 거기 온다는 소식에 겁먹고 달아나지 말았어야 했어요."], ["I ought to have warned you and saved you before it was too late.", "너무 늦기 전에 당신에게 경고하고 당신을 구했어야 했어요."], ["Why did I only have courage enough to write you that letter?", "왜 나는 그 편지 한 통 쓸 용기밖에 없었을까?"], ["Why did I only do harm, when I wanted and meant to do good?", "왜 나는 좋은 일을 하고 싶었고 하려고 했으면서 해만 끼쳤을까?"], ["Oh, my fear—my mad, miserable, wicked fear!’ She repeated those words again, and hid her face again in the end of her poor worn shawl.", "아, 내 두려움—내 미치고 비참하고 사악한 두려움!’ 그 여자는 그 말을 다시 되풀이하고, 다시 낡고 해진 숄 자락에 얼굴을 묻었어."], ["It was dreadful to see her, and dreadful to hear her.”", "그 모습을 보는 것도 그 소리를 듣는 것도 끔찍했어.”"]], [["“Surely, Laura, you asked what the fear was which she dwelt on so earnestly?”", "“분명히 물어봤겠지, 로라. 그 여자가 그토록 간절하게 되뇌던 두려움이 무엇이었는지?”"]], [["“Yes, I asked that.”", "“응, 물어봤어.”"]], [["“And what did she say?”", "“그래서 뭐라고 했어?”"]], [["“She asked me in return, if I should not be afraid of a man who had shut me up in a mad-house, and who would shut me up again, if he could?", "“그 여자는 되레 나에게 물었어. 자기를 정신병원에 가둔 남자, 할 수만 있다면 다시 가둘 남자를 두려워하지 않겠느냐고."], ["I said, ‘Are you afraid still?", "내가 말했지. ‘지금도 두려우세요?"], ["Surely you would not be here if you were afraid now?’ ‘No,’ she said, ‘I am not afraid now.’ I asked why not.", "정말 두렵다면 여기 오지도 않으셨을 텐데요.’ ‘아니요.’ 그 여자가 말했어. ‘지금은 두렵지 않아요.’ 나는 왜 그런지 물었어."], ["She suddenly bent forward into the boat-house, and said, ‘Can’t you guess why?’ I shook my head.", "그 여자는 갑자기 보트 창고 안으로 몸을 기울이며 말했어. ‘왜인지 짐작 못 하시겠어요?’ 나는 고개를 저었어."], ["‘Look at me,’ she went on.", "‘저를 보세요.’ 그 여자가 말을 이었어."], ["I told her I was grieved to see that she looked very sorrowful and very ill.", "나는 당신이 몹시 슬퍼 보이고 몹시 아파 보여서 마음이 아프다고 했어. 그 여자가 처음으로 미소를 지었어."], ["She smiled for the first time.", "‘아프다고요?’ 그 여자가 되뇌었어."], ["‘Ill?’ she repeated; ‘I’m dying.", "‘저는 죽어 가고 있어요."], ["You know why I’m not afraid of him now.", "이제 제가 왜 그 사람을 두려워하지 않는지 아시겠죠."], ["Do you think I shall meet your mother in heaven?", "제가 천국에서 당신 어머니를 만날 수 있을까요?"], ["Will she forgive me if I do?’ I was so shocked and so startled, that I could make no reply.", "만난다면 저를 용서해 주실까요?’ 나는 어찌나 충격을 받고 놀랐던지 아무 대답도 할 수 없었어."], ["‘I have been thinking of it,’ she went on, ‘all the time I have been in hiding from your husband, all the time I lay ill.", "‘줄곧 그 생각을 했어요.’ 그 여자가 말을 이었어. ‘당신 남편을 피해 숨어 지내는 동안 내내, 병들어 누워 있는 동안 내내요."], ["My thoughts have driven me here—I want to make atonement—I want to undo all I can of the harm I once did.’ I begged her as earnestly as I could to tell me what she meant.", "그 생각이 저를 여기까지 몰고 왔어요—저는 속죄하고 싶어요—제가 예전에 끼친 해를 할 수 있는 데까지 되돌리고 싶어요.’ 나는 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 되도록 간절히 물었어."], ["She still looked at me with fixed vacant eyes.", "그 여자는 여전히 초점 없는 눈으로 나를 바라보고만 있었어."], ["‘SHALL I undo the harm?’ she said to herself doubtfully.", "‘내가 그 해를 되돌릴 수 있을까?’ 그 여자는 미심쩍은 듯 혼잣말을 했어."], ["‘You have friends to take your part.", "‘당신에게는 편들어 줄 친구들이 있지요."], ["If YOU know his Secret, he will be afraid of you, he won’t dare use you as he used me.", "당신이 그 사람의 비밀을 안다면 그 사람은 당신을 두려워할 테고, 저를 다루던 대로 당신을 다루지는 못할 거예요."], ["He must treat you mercifully for his own sake, if he is afraid of you and your friends.", "당신과 당신 친구들이 두렵다면, 그 사람은 자기 자신을 위해서라도 당신을 너그럽게 대해야 할 테지요."], ["And if he treats you mercifully, and if I can say it was my doing——’ I listened eagerly for more, but she stopped at those words.”", "그리고 그 사람이 당신을 너그럽게 대하고, 그게 내가 한 일이었다고 말할 수 있다면——’ 나는 더 들으려고 귀를 곤두세웠지만, 그 여자는 거기서 말을 멈추었어.”"]], [["“You tried to make her go on?”", "“말을 더 하게 하려고 애써 봤겠지?”"]], [["“I tried, but she only drew herself away from me again, and leaned her face and arms against the side of the boat-house.", "“해 보았어. 하지만 그이는 또다시 내게서 몸을 빼더니 얼굴과 두 팔을 보트 창고 벽에 기댔어."], ["‘Oh!’ I heard her say, with a dreadful, distracted tenderness in her voice, ‘oh!", "‘아!’ 하고 그이가 말하는 소리가 들렸는데, 목소리에 무섭도록 넋 나간 애틋함이 서려 있었어."], ["if I could only be buried with your mother!", "‘아! 내가 당신 어머니 곁에 묻힐 수만 있다면!"], ["If I could only wake at her side, when the angel’s trumpet sounds, and the graves give up their dead at the resurrection!‘—Marian!", "천사의 나팔이 울리고 무덤들이 부활의 죽은 자들을 내어 놓을 때 그분 곁에서 깨어날 수만 있다면!’—매리언, 나는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었어."], ["I trembled from head to foot—it was horrible to hear her.", "그 말을 듣는 게 끔찍했어."], ["‘But there is no hope of that,’ she said, moving a little, so as to look at me again, ‘no hope for a poor stranger like me.", "‘하지만 그럴 가망은 없어요.’ 그이가 조금 몸을 움직여 다시 나를 볼 수 있게 하면서 말했어. ‘나 같은 가엾은 타관 사람에게는 아무 가망도 없지요."], ["I shall not rest under the marble cross that I washed with my own hands, and made so white and pure for her sake.", "나는 내 손으로 씻어서 그분을 위해 그토록 희고 정갈하게 만들어 놓은 그 대리석 십자가 아래에 눕지 못할 거예요. 아, 안 돼요!"], ["Oh no! oh no!", "아, 안 돼요!"], ["God’s mercy, not man’s, will take me to her, where the wicked cease from troubling and the weary are at rest.’ She spoke those words quietly and sorrowfully, with a heavy, hopeless sigh, and then waited a little.", "사람의 자비가 아니라 하느님의 자비가 나를 그분께 데려다줄 거예요. 악한 자들이 괴롭히기를 그치고 지친 자들이 안식을 얻는 그곳으로요.’ 그이는 그 말을 조용히, 슬프게, 무겁고 절망적인 한숨과 함께 내뱉고는 잠시 잠자코 있었어."], ["Her face was confused and troubled, she seemed to be thinking, or trying to think.", "그이의 얼굴은 혼란스럽고 어지러워 보였고, 무언가를 생각하는지 생각해 내려 애쓰는지 알 수 없었어."], ["‘What was it I said just now?’ she asked after a while.", "‘내가 방금 무슨 말을 했지요?’ 한참 만에 그이가 물었어."], ["‘When your mother is in my mind, everything else goes out of it.", "‘당신 어머니가 마음에 떠오르면 다른 건 전부 사라져 버려요."], ["What was I saying?", "내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었지요?"], ["what was I saying?’ I reminded the poor creature, as kindly and delicately as I could.", "무슨 말을 하고 있었지요?’ 나는 그 가엾은 사람에게 될 수 있는 대로 다정하고 조심스럽게 일러 주었어."], ["‘Ah, yes, yes,’ she said, still in a vacant, perplexed manner.", "‘아, 그래요, 그래요.’ 그이는 여전히 멍하고 어리둥절한 태도로 말했어."], ["‘You are helpless with your wicked husband. Yes.", "‘당신은 그 못된 남편 앞에서 아무 힘이 없지요."], ["And I must do what I have come to do here—I must make it up to you for having been afraid to speak out at a better time.’ ‘What IS it you have to tell me?’ I asked.", "그래요. 그러니 나는 여기 온 목적을 이뤄야 해요—더 좋은 때에 털어놓기를 두려워했던 것을 당신에게 갚아야 해요.’ ‘내게 하려는 말이 대체 뭐지요?’ 하고 내가 물었어."], ["‘The Secret that your cruel husband is afraid of,’ she answered.", "‘당신의 잔인한 남편이 두려워하는 그 비밀이지요.’ 그이가 대답했어."], ["‘I once threatened him with the Secret, and frightened him.", "‘나는 한 번 그 비밀로 그 사람을 위협해서 겁을 준 적이 있어요."], ["You shall threaten him with the Secret, and frighten him too.’ Her face darkened, and a hard, angry stare fixed itself in her eyes.", "당신도 그 비밀로 그를 위협해서 겁을 주게 될 거예요.’ 그이의 얼굴이 어두워지더니 딱딱하고 성난 응시가 두 눈에 박혔어."], ["She began waving her hand at me in a vacant, unmeaning manner.", "그이는 멍하니 뜻 모를 몸짓으로 내게 손을 흔들기 시작했어."], ["‘My mother knows the Secret,’ she said.", "‘우리 어머니가 그 비밀을 알아요.’ 그이가 말했어."], ["‘My mother has wasted under the Secret half her lifetime.", "‘우리 어머니는 그 비밀 밑에서 한평생의 절반을 시들어 버렸어요."], ["One day, when I was grown up, she said something to ME.", "내가 다 자란 어느 날, 어머니가 나에게 무슨 말을 했어요."], ["And the next day your husband——’”", "그리고 그 이튿날 당신 남편이——’”"]], [["“Yes!", "“그래!"], ["yes!", "그래!"], ["Go on.", "계속해 봐."], ["What did she tell you about your husband?”", "그 여자가 네 남편에 대해 무슨 말을 했다는 거야?”"]], [["“She stopped again, Marian, at that point——”", "“매리언, 바로 그 대목에서 그이가 또 말을 멈췄어——”"]], [["“And said no more?”", "“그러고는 더 아무 말도 안 했고?”"]], [["“And listened eagerly.", "“그러고는 열심히 귀를 기울였어."], ["‘Hush!’ she whispered, still waving her hand at me.", "‘쉿!’ 그이는 여전히 내게 손을 흔들며 속삭였어."], ["‘Hush!’ She moved aside out of the doorway, moved slowly and stealthily, step by step, till I lost her past the edge of the boat-house.”", "‘쉿!’ 그러고는 문간에서 옆으로 비켜서더니 천천히, 살금살금, 한 걸음씩 물러나서 마침내 보트 창고 모서리 너머로 내 눈에서 사라져 버렸어.”"]], [["“Surely you followed her?”", "“설마 뒤를 따라가지 않은 건 아니겠지?”"]], [["“Yes, my anxiety made me bold enough to rise and follow her.", "“따라갔어. 조바심이 나서 일어나 뒤를 밟을 만큼은 대담해졌으니까."], ["Just as I reached the entrance, she appeared again suddenly, round the side of the boat-house.", "입구에 막 다다랐을 때 그이가 보트 창고 옆쪽에서 갑자기 다시 나타났어. ‘그 비밀요.’ 나는 그이에게 속삭였어."], ["‘The Secret,’ I whispered to her—‘wait and tell me the Secret!’ She caught hold of my arm, and looked at me with wild frightened eyes.", "‘기다렸다가 그 비밀을 말해 줘요!’ 그이는 내 팔을 붙잡고 겁에 질린 사나운 눈으로 나를 보았어. ‘지금은 안 돼요.’ 그이가 말했어."], ["‘Not now,’ she said, ‘we are not alone—we are watched.", "‘우리는 둘만 있는 게 아니에요—누가 지켜보고 있어요."], ["Come here to-morrow at this time—by yourself—mind—by yourself.’ She pushed me roughly into the boat-house again, and I saw her no more.”", "내일 이 시각에 여기로 와요—혼자서—명심해요—혼자서.’ 그이는 나를 보트 창고 안으로 거칠게 밀어 넣었고, 그 뒤로는 그이를 보지 못했어.”"]], [["“Oh, Laura, Laura, another chance lost!", "“아, 로라, 로라, 또 한 번 기회를 놓쳤구나!"], ["If I had only been near you she should not have escaped us.", "내가 네 곁에만 있었더라면 그 여자가 우리를 빠져나가지는 못했을 텐데."], ["On which side did you lose sight of her?”", "어느 쪽으로 사라지는 걸 봤어?”"]], [["“On the left side, where the ground sinks and the wood is thickest.”", "“왼쪽이었어. 땅이 꺼지고 숲이 제일 우거진 쪽.”"]], [["“Did you run out again?", "“다시 뛰어나가 봤어?"], ["did you call after her?”", "뒤에다 대고 불러 봤고?”"]], [["“How could I?", "“어떻게 그럴 수 있었겠어?"], ["I was too terrified to move or speak.”", "너무 무서워서 움직이지도 말하지도 못했는걸.”"]], [["“But when you DID move—when you came out?”", "“그래도 움직이기는 움직였을 때—밖으로 나왔을 때는?”"]], [["“I ran back here, to tell you what had happened.”", "“여기로 달려왔지, 무슨 일이 있었는지 네게 말하려고.”"]], [["“Did you see any one, or hear any one, in the plantation?”", "“나무숲에서 누구를 보거나 누구 소리를 듣지는 않았어?”"]], [["“No, it seemed to be all still and quiet when I passed through it.”", "“아니, 지나올 때는 온통 고요하고 조용하기만 한 것 같았어.”"]], [["I waited for a moment to consider.", "나는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Was this third person, supposed to have been secretly present at the interview, a reality, or the creature of Anne Catherick’s excited fancy?", "그 만남 자리에 몰래 있었다는 제삼자는 실재하는 사람이었을까, 아니면 앤 캐서릭의 흥분한 상상이 지어낸 존재였을까?"], ["It was impossible to determine.", "판단할 길이 없었다."], ["The one thing certain was, that we had failed again on the very brink of discovery—failed utterly and irretrievably, unless Anne Catherick kept her appointment at the boat-house for the next day.", "확실한 것은 단 하나, 우리가 발견의 문턱에서 또 한 번 실패했다는 것—앤 캐서릭이 이튿날 보트 창고의 약속을 지키지 않는다면 철저히, 돌이킬 수 없이 실패했다는 것이었다."]], [["“Are you quite sure you have told me everything that passed?", "“네가 겪은 일을 하나도 빠짐없이 말해 준 게 확실해?"], ["Every word that was said?”", "오간 말 한마디 한마디까지?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“I think so,”", "“그런 것 같아.”"], ["she answered.", "로라가 대답했다."], ["“My powers of memory, Marian, are not like yours.", "“매리언, 내 기억력은 네 것 같지 않잖아."], ["But I was so strongly impressed, so deeply interested, that nothing of any importance can possibly have escaped me.”", "하지만 워낙 강한 인상을 받았고 워낙 깊이 마음이 쏠려 있었으니, 조금이라도 중요한 건 놓쳤을 리 없어.”"]], [["“My dear Laura, the merest trifles are of importance where Anne Catherick is concerned.", "“얘 로라, 앤 캐서릭에 관한 일이라면 아무리 하찮은 것도 다 중요해."], ["Think again.", "다시 생각해 봐."], ["Did no chance reference escape her as to the place in which she is living at the present time?”", "그 여자가 지금 어디에 묵고 있는지 지나가는 말로라도 흘리지 않았어?”"]], [["“None that I can remember.”", "“기억나는 건 없어.”"]], [["“Did she not mention a companion and friend—a woman named Mrs. Clements?”", "“동행이자 친구인 여자, 클레멘츠 부인이라는 사람 얘기는 안 했어?”"]], [["“Oh yes! yes!", "“아, 맞아!"], ["I forgot that.", "맞아!"], ["She told me Mrs.", "그걸 잊고 있었네."], ["Clements wanted sadly to go with her to the lake and take care of her, and begged and prayed that she would not venture into this neighbourhood alone.”", "클레멘츠 부인이 호수까지 같이 가서 자기를 돌봐 주고 싶어 몹시 안달했다고, 이 근방에 혼자 들어가지 말라고 사정하고 빌었다고 했어.”"]], [["“Was that all she said about Mrs. Clements?”", "“클레멘츠 부인에 대해 한 말이 그게 전부야?”"]], [["“Yes, that was all.”", "“응, 그게 전부야.”"]], [["“She told you nothing about the place in which she took refuge after leaving Todd’s Corner?”", "“토드 코너를 떠난 뒤에 어디로 몸을 피했는지는 아무 말도 안 했고?”"]], [["“Nothing—I am quite sure.”", "“아무 말도 안 했어. 확실해.”"]], [["“Nor where she has lived since?", "“그 뒤로 어디서 살았는지도?"], ["Nor what her illness had been?”", "무슨 병을 앓았는지도?”"]], [["“No, Marian, not a word.", "“아니, 매리언, 한마디도."], ["Tell me, pray tell me, what you think about it.", "말해 줘, 제발 말해 줘, 넌 이 일을 어떻게 생각하는지."], ["I don’t know what to think, or what to do next.”", "나는 어떻게 생각해야 할지, 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 모르겠어.”"]], [["“You must do this, my love: You must carefully keep the appointment at the boat-house to-morrow.", "“이렇게 해야 해, 얘. 내일 보트 창고의 약속을 꼭 지켜야 해."], ["It is impossible to say what interests may not depend on your seeing that woman again.", "그 여자를 다시 만나는 데 얼마나 큰 이해가 걸려 있을지는 아무도 모르는 일이야."], ["You shall not be left to yourself a second time.", "두 번은 너를 혼자 두지 않을 거야."], ["I will follow you at a safe distance.", "안전한 거리를 두고 뒤를 따라갈게."], ["Nobody shall see me, but I will keep within hearing of your voice, if anything happens.", "아무도 나를 보지 못하겠지만, 무슨 일이 생기면 네 목소리가 들리는 데까지는 붙어 있을 거야."], ["Anne Catherick has escaped Walter Hartright, and has escaped you.", "앤 캐서릭은 월터 하트라이트에게서 달아났고 너에게서도 달아났어."], ["Whatever happens, she shall not escape ME.”", "무슨 일이 있어도 나에게서는 달아나지 못해.”"]], [["Laura’s eyes read mine attentively.", "로라의 눈이 내 눈을 유심히 읽었다."]], [["“You believe,”", "“너는 믿는 거지?”"], ["she said, “in this secret that my husband is afraid of?", "로라가 말했다. “내 남편이 두려워한다는 그 비밀을 말이야."], ["Suppose, Marian, it should only exist after all in Anne Catherick’s fancy?", "매리언, 만약 그게 결국 앤 캐서릭의 공상 속에만 있는 거라면?"], ["Suppose she only wanted to see me and to speak to me, for the sake of old remembrances?", "만약 그 여자가 옛 기억 때문에 그저 나를 보고 나와 이야기하고 싶었을 뿐이라면?"], ["Her manner was so strange—I almost doubted her.", "태도가 어찌나 이상하던지—나는 그 여자를 거의 믿지 못할 뻔했어."], ["Would you trust her in other things?”", "넌 다른 일에서도 그 여자를 믿겠어?”"]], [["“I trust nothing, Laura, but my own observation of your husband’s conduct.", "“로라, 나는 아무것도 믿지 않아. 네 남편의 행실을 내 눈으로 지켜본 것만 믿을 뿐이야."], ["I judge Anne Catherick’s words by his actions, and I believe there is a secret.”", "나는 앤 캐서릭의 말을 그 사람의 행동으로 판단하고, 그래서 비밀이 있다고 믿는 거야.”"]], [["I said no more, and got up to leave the room.", "나는 더 말하지 않고 일어나 방을 나섰다."], ["Thoughts were troubling me which I might have told her if we had spoken together longer, and which it might have been dangerous for her to know.", "우리가 더 오래 이야기했더라면 로라에게 털어놓았을지도 모를, 그러나 그 애가 알기에는 위험했을 생각들이 나를 괴롭히고 있었다."], ["The influence of the terrible dream from which she had awakened me hung darkly and heavily over every fresh impression which the progress of her narrative produced on my mind.", "나를 깨워 놓은 그 무서운 꿈의 여운이, 그 애의 이야기가 진행되면서 내 마음에 새로 찍히는 인상마다 어둡고 무겁게 드리웠다. 나는 불길한 미래가 바짝 다가오는 것을 느꼈다."], ["I felt the ominous future coming close, chilling me with an unutterable awe, forcing on me the conviction of an unseen design in the long series of complications which had now fastened round us.", "그것은 말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움으로 나를 서늘하게 했고, 이제 우리를 옭아맨 그 길고 긴 뒤얽힘의 연쇄 속에 보이지 않는 어떤 설계가 있다는 확신을 내게 억지로 심어 주었다."], ["I thought of Hartright—as I saw him in the body when he said farewell; as I saw him in the spirit in my dream—and I too began to doubt now whether we were not advancing blindfold to an appointed and an inevitable end.", "나는 하트라이트를 떠올렸다—작별을 고하던 날 몸으로 마주 보았던 그를, 그리고 꿈속에서 혼으로 보았던 그를—그리고 나 역시 우리가 눈을 가린 채 정해진, 피할 수 없는 결말을 향해 나아가고 있는 것은 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다."]], [["Leaving Laura to go upstairs alone, I went out to look about me in the walks near the house.", "로라를 혼자 위층으로 올려 보내고, 나는 집 근처 산책로를 둘러보러 나갔다."], ["The circumstances under which Anne Catherick had parted from her had made me secretly anxious to know how Count Fosco was passing the afternoon, and had rendered me secretly distrustful of the results of that solitary journey from which Sir Percival had returned but a few hours since.", "앤 캐서릭이 그 애와 헤어진 정황 때문에 나는 포스코 백작이 오후를 어떻게 보내고 있는지 남몰래 알고 싶어졌고, 퍼시벌 경이 몇 시간 전에 돌아온 그 홀로 다녀온 여행의 결과가 남몰래 미덥지 않았다."]], [["After looking for them in every direction and discovering nothing, I returned to the house, and entered the different rooms on the ground floor one after another.", "사방을 다 찾아보았으나 아무것도 알아내지 못하고, 나는 집으로 돌아와 아래층 방들을 하나씩 차례로 들여다보았다."], ["They were all empty.", "모두 비어 있었다."], ["I came out again into the hall, and went upstairs to return to Laura.", "나는 다시 현관홀로 나와 로라에게 돌아가려고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Madame Fosco opened her door as I passed it in my way along the passage, and I stopped to see if she could inform me of the whereabouts of her husband and Sir Percival.", "복도를 지나는데 포스코 부인이 문을 열었기에, 나는 걸음을 멈추고 남편과 퍼시벌 경의 행방을 아는지 물어보았다."], ["Yes, she had seen them both from her window more than an hour since.", "그렇다, 부인은 한 시간도 더 전에 창문으로 두 사람을 다 보았다고 했다."], ["The Count had looked up with his customary kindness, and had mentioned with his habitual attention to her in the smallest trifles, that he and his friend were going out together for a long walk.", "백작은 여느 때처럼 다정하게 위를 올려다보았고, 아무리 사소한 일에도 아내를 배려하는 그 습관대로, 자기와 친구가 함께 긴 산책을 나간다고 말해 두었다는 것이었다."]], [["For a long walk!", "긴 산책이라니!"], ["They had never yet been in each other’s company with that object in my experience of them.", "내가 두 사람을 겪어 본 바로는 그런 목적으로 함께 있어 본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["Sir Percival cared for no exercise but riding, and the Count (except when he was polite enough to be my escort) cared for no exercise at all.", "퍼시벌 경은 승마 말고는 어떤 운동에도 관심이 없었고, 백작은 (친절을 베풀어 내 길동무가 되어 줄 때를 빼면) 어떤 운동에도 도무지 관심이 없었다."]], [["When I joined Laura again, I found that she had called to mind in my absence the impending question of the signature to the deed, which, in the interest of discussing her interview with Anne Catherick, we had hitherto overlooked.", "다시 로라에게 갔을 때, 나는 그 애가 내가 없는 동안 눈앞에 닥친 증서 서명 문제를 떠올렸다는 것을 알았다. 앤 캐서릭과의 만남을 이야기하는 데 정신이 팔려 우리가 여태 잊고 있던 문제였다."], ["Her first words when I saw her expressed her surprise at the absence of the expected summons to attend Sir Percival in the library.", "나를 보자마자 그 애가 꺼낸 첫마디는, 서재로 퍼시벌 경을 만나러 오라는 예상된 호출이 없다는 데 대한 놀라움이었다."]], [["“You may make your mind easy on that subject,” I said.", "“그 문제라면 마음을 놓아도 돼.”"], ["“For the present, at least, neither your resolution nor mine will be exposed to any further trial.", "내가 말했다. “적어도 당분간은 네 결심도 내 결심도 더 시험받을 일이 없을 거야."], ["Sir Percival has altered his plans—the business of the signature is put off.”", "퍼시벌 경이 계획을 바꿨어—서명 건은 미뤄졌어.”"]], [["“Put off?”", "“미뤄졌다고?”"], ["Laura repeated amazedly.", "로라가 놀라며 되뇌었다."], ["“Who told you so?”", "“누가 그래?”"]], [["“My authority is Count Fosco.", "“포스코 백작이 알려 줬어."], ["I believe it is to his interference that we are indebted for your husband’s sudden change of purpose.”", "네 남편이 갑자기 뜻을 바꾼 건 그 사람이 끼어든 덕분이라고 나는 생각해.”"]], [["“It seems impossible, Marian.", "“그럴 리가 없어, 매리언."], ["If the object of my signing was, as we suppose, to obtain money for Sir Percival that he urgently wanted, how can the matter be put off?”", "내가 서명하는 목적이 우리 짐작대로 퍼시벌 경이 급히 필요로 하는 돈을 마련하는 거라면, 어떻게 그 일을 미룰 수 있겠어?”"]], [["“I think, Laura, we have the means at hand of setting that doubt at rest.", "“로라, 그 의문을 가라앉힐 방법이 바로 가까이에 있는 것 같은데."], ["Have you forgotten the conversation that I heard between Sir Percival and the lawyer as they were crossing the hall?”", "퍼시벌 경과 그 변호사가 현관홀을 가로질러 갈 때 내가 들은 대화를 잊었어?”"]], [["“No, but I don’t remember——”", "“아니, 하지만 기억이 안 나——”"]], [["“I do. There were two alternatives proposed.", "“나는 기억해."], ["One was to obtain your signature to the parchment.", "두 가지 방안이 나왔어. 하나는 네 서명을 그 양피지 문서에 받아 내는 것."], ["The other was to gain time by giving bills at three months.", "다른 하나는 석 달 만기 어음을 끊어서 시간을 버는 것."], ["The last resource is evidently the resource now adopted, and we may fairly hope to be relieved from our share in Sir Percival’s embarrassments for some time to come.”", "마지막 수단이 지금 채택된 것이 분명해. 그러니 당분간은 퍼시벌 경의 곤경에서 우리 몫을 덜게 되리라고 기대해도 좋을 거야.”"]], [["“Oh, Marian, it sounds too good to be true!”", "“아, 매리언, 너무 좋아서 참말 같지가 않아!”"]], [["“Does it, my love?", "“그래?"], ["You complimented me on my ready memory not long since, but you seem to doubt it now.", "얘, 얼마 전에는 내 기억력이 빠르다고 칭찬해 놓고 지금은 그걸 의심하는 것 같구나."], ["I will get my journal, and you shall see if I am right or wrong.”", "내 일기를 가져올 테니, 내 말이 맞는지 틀리는지 네가 직접 봐.”"]], [["I went away and got the book at once.", "나는 곧바로 가서 일기장을 가져왔다."]], [["On looking back to the entry referring to the lawyer’s visit, we found that my recollection of the two alternatives presented was accurately correct.", "변호사의 방문에 관한 기록을 되짚어 보니, 제시된 두 방안에 대한 내 기억이 정확히 옳았음이 드러났다."], ["It was almost as great a relief to my mind as to Laura’s, to find that my memory had served me, on this occasion, as faithfully as usual.", "이번에도 내 기억이 여느 때처럼 충실하게 제 몫을 해 주었다는 사실은 로라에게 못지않게 내 마음에도 큰 위안이었다."], ["In the perilous uncertainty of our present situation, it is hard to say what future interests may not depend upon the regularity of the entries in my journal, and upon the reliability of my recollection at the time when I make them.", "지금 우리가 처한 위태로운 불확실 속에서, 앞으로 어떤 이해가 내 일기 기록의 규칙성에, 그리고 그것을 적을 당시 내 기억의 신뢰성에 달려 있게 될지 누가 알겠는가."]], [["Laura’s face and manner suggested to me that this last consideration had occurred to her as well as to myself.", "로라의 얼굴과 태도를 보니 이 마지막 생각이 나뿐 아니라 그 애에게도 떠오른 모양이었다."], ["Anyway, it is only a trifling matter, and I am almost ashamed to put it down here in writing—it seems to set the forlornness of our situation in such a miserably vivid light.", "어차피 사소한 일이고, 여기 이렇게 적어 두기가 부끄러울 지경이다—우리 처지의 막막함을 너무나도 비참하도록 또렷하게 비춰 보이는 것 같아서."], ["We must have little indeed to depend on, when the discovery that my memory can still be trusted to serve us is hailed as if it was the discovery of a new friend!", "내 기억이 아직 우리를 위해 제 몫을 하리라는 발견이 마치 새 친구를 얻은 발견인 양 반가운 판이니, 우리에게 기댈 것이 얼마나 없는 셈인가!"]], [["The first bell for dinner separated us.", "저녁 식사를 알리는 첫 종이 우리를 갈라놓았다."], ["Just as it had done ringing, Sir Percival and the Count returned from their walk.", "종소리가 막 그쳤을 때 퍼시벌 경과 백작이 산책에서 돌아왔다."], ["We heard the master of the house storming at the servants for being five minutes late, and the master’s guest interposing, as usual, in the interests of propriety, patience, and peace.", "이 집 주인이 오 분 늦었다고 하인들에게 호통치는 소리와, 주인의 손님이 여느 때처럼 예의와 인내와 평화를 위하여 끼어드는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["The evening has come and gone.", "저녁이 왔다가 갔다."], ["No extraordinary event has happened.", "별다른 일은 일어나지 않았다."], ["But I have noticed certain peculiarities in the conduct of Sir Percival and the Count, which have sent me to my bed feeling very anxious and uneasy about Anne Catherick, and about the results which to-morrow may produce.", "그러나 나는 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 행동에서 어떤 기이한 점들을 알아챘고, 그 때문에 앤 캐서릭에 대해서도 내일이 빚어낼 결과에 대해서도 몹시 불안하고 마음이 놓이지 않는 채로 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["I know enough by this time, to be sure, that the aspect of Sir Percival which is the most false, and which, therefore, means the worst, is his polite aspect.", "이제 나는 퍼시벌 경의 여러 얼굴 가운데 가장 거짓된 얼굴, 따라서 가장 나쁜 것을 뜻하는 얼굴이 바로 정중한 얼굴이라는 것을 충분히 알 만큼은 안다."], ["That long walk with his friend had ended in improving his manners, especially towards his wife.", "친구와 함께한 그 긴 산책은 그의 태도를 좋게 만들어 놓는 것으로 끝났고, 특히 아내를 대하는 태도가 그러했다."], ["To Laura’s secret surprise and to my secret alarm, he called her by her Christian name, asked if she had heard lately from her uncle, inquired when Mrs. Vesey was to receive her invitation to Blackwater, and showed her so many other little attentions that he almost recalled the days of his hateful courtship at Limmeridge House.", "로라를 남몰래 놀라게 하고 나를 남몰래 불안하게 하도록, 그는 아내를 세례명으로 불렀고, 요즘 삼촌에게서 소식을 들었는지 물었으며, 베지 부인이 블랙워터로 오라는 초대를 언제 받게 되느냐고 묻고, 그 밖에도 자잘한 배려를 어찌나 많이 보이던지 리머리지 하우스에서의 그 밉살스러운 구애 시절이 거의 되살아날 지경이었다."], ["This was a bad sign to begin with, and I thought it more ominous still that he should pretend after dinner to fall asleep in the drawing-room, and that his eyes should cunningly follow Laura and me when he thought we neither of us suspected him.", "시작부터 나쁜 징조였다. 게다가 저녁 식사 뒤에 응접실에서 잠든 척하면서, 우리 둘 다 자기를 의심하지 않는다고 여길 때면 로라와 나를 교활하게 눈으로 좇는 것을 보니 더욱 불길하게 느껴졌다."], ["I have never had any doubt that his sudden journey by himself took him to Welmingham to question Mrs. Catherick—but the experience of to-night has made me fear that the expedition was not undertaken in vain, and that he has got the information which he unquestionably left us to collect.", "그가 혼자 훌쩍 떠난 그 여행이 웰밍엄으로 캐서릭 부인을 캐물으러 간 것임을 나는 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없다—그러나 오늘 밤의 일로 나는 그 원정이 헛되지 않았을까 봐, 그가 캐내러 간 것이 분명한 그 정보를 손에 넣었을까 봐 두려워졌다."], ["If I knew where Anne Catherick was to be found, I would be up to-morrow with sunrise and warn her.", "앤 캐서릭이 어디 있는지 알기만 한다면 나는 내일 해가 뜰 때 일어나 그 여자에게 경고하러 갈 것이다."]], [["While the aspect under which Sir Percival presented himself to-night was unhappily but too familiar to me, the aspect under which the Count appeared was, on the other hand, entirely new in my experience of him.", "오늘 밤 퍼시벌 경이 내보인 얼굴은 유감스럽게도 내게 너무나 익숙한 것이었던 반면, 백작이 내보인 얼굴은 그를 겪어 온 내 경험에 비추어 전혀 새로운 것이었다."], ["He permitted me, this evening, to make his acquaintance, for the first time, in the character of a Man of Sentiment—of sentiment, as I believe, really felt, not assumed for the occasion.", "그는 오늘 저녁 처음으로 나에게 감상(感傷)의 인간이라는 모습으로 자기와 알고 지낼 기회를 주었다—그것도 내가 보기에는 이 자리를 위해 꾸며 낸 것이 아니라 정말로 느끼는 감상이었다."]], [["For instance, he was quiet and subdued—his eyes and his voice expressed a restrained sensibility.", "이를테면 그는 조용하고 차분했고, 그의 눈과 목소리는 절제된 감수성을 드러냈다."], ["He wore (as if there was some hidden connection between his showiest finery and his deepest feeling) the most magnificent waistcoat he has yet appeared in—it was made of pale sea-green silk, and delicately trimmed with fine silver braid.", "그는 (가장 화려한 옷치레와 가장 깊은 감정 사이에 무슨 숨은 연관이라도 있는 것처럼) 여태 입고 나온 것 중 가장 근사한 조끼를 입고 있었다—연한 바다빛 초록 비단으로 지어 고운 은색 끈으로 섬세하게 장식한 것이었다."], ["His voice sank into the tenderest inflections, his smile expressed a thoughtful, fatherly admiration, whenever he spoke to Laura or to me.", "로라나 나에게 말할 때면 그의 목소리는 더없이 부드러운 억양으로 내려앉았고, 그의 미소는 사려 깊고 자애로운 감탄을 드러냈다."], ["He pressed his wife’s hand under the table when she thanked him for trifling little attentions at dinner.", "그는 저녁 식탁에서 사소한 배려에 아내가 고마워하자 식탁 밑으로 그 손을 지그시 눌러 주었다."], ["He took wine with her.", "그는 아내와 잔을 나누었다."], ["“Your health and happiness, my angel!”", "“당신의 건강과 행복을 위하여, 나의 천사!”"], ["he said, with fond glistening eyes.", "그가 애정으로 반짝이는 눈을 하고 말했다."], ["He ate little or nothing, and sighed, and said “Good Percival!” when his friend laughed at him.", "그는 거의 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 한숨을 내쉬었으며, 친구가 자기를 비웃자 “착한 퍼시벌!”"], ["After dinner, he took Laura by the hand, and asked her if she would be “so sweet as to play to him.”", "하고 말했다. 식사가 끝난 뒤 그는 로라의 손을 잡고 “친절을 베풀어 자기를 위해 한 곡 쳐 줄”"], ["She complied, through sheer astonishment.", "수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He sat by the piano, with his watch-chain resting in folds, like a golden serpent, on the sea-green protuberance of his waistcoat.", "그 애는 순전히 놀란 나머지 그러겠다고 했다. 그는 피아노 옆에 앉았는데, 시곗줄이 바다빛 초록 조끼의 불룩한 배 위에 황금 뱀처럼 겹겹이 늘어져 있었다."], ["His immense head lay languidly on one side, and he gently beat time with two of his yellow-white fingers.", "그의 거대한 머리는 나른하게 한쪽으로 기울었고, 그는 누르스름하고 흰 손가락 두 개로 가만가만 박자를 맞추었다."], ["He highly approved of the music, and tenderly admired Laura’s manner of playing—not as poor Hartright used to praise it, with an innocent enjoyment of the sweet sounds, but with a clear, cultivated, practical knowledge of the merits of the composition, in the first place, and of the merits of the player’s touch in the second.", "그는 그 곡을 크게 칭찬했고 로라의 연주 솜씨를 애틋하게 상찬했다—가엾은 하트라이트가 고운 소리를 천진하게 즐기며 칭찬하던 식이 아니라, 우선은 그 곡의 됨됨이에 대해, 다음으로는 연주자의 터치가 지닌 됨됨이에 대해 또렷하고 교양 있고 실질적인 지식을 갖춘 사람의 칭찬이었다."], ["As the evening closed in, he begged that the lovely dying light might not be profaned, just yet, by the appearance of the lamps.", "저녁이 저물어 갈 무렵 그는 이 사랑스럽게 스러지는 빛이 아직은 등불 따위로 더럽혀지지 않게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He came, with his horribly silent tread, to the distant window at which I was standing, to be out of his way and to avoid the very sight of him—he came to ask me to support his protest against the lamps.", "그러고는 그 소름 끼치도록 소리 없는 걸음으로, 그를 피해 그의 모습조차 보지 않으려고 내가 서 있던 저쪽 끝 창가로 다가왔다—등불에 반대하는 자기 항의에 힘을 보태 달라고 청하려고 온 것이다."], ["If any one of them could only have burnt him up at that moment, I would have gone down to the kitchen and fetched it myself.", "그 등불 가운데 하나가 그 순간 그를 태워 없앨 수만 있었다면, 나는 부엌으로 내려가 그것을 손수 가져왔을 것이다."]], [["“Surely you like this modest, trembling English twilight?”", "“이 수줍고 떨리는 영국의 황혼이 마음에 드시지요?”"], ["he said softly. “Ah!", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["I love it.", "“아!"], ["I feel my inborn admiration of all that is noble, and great, and good, purified by the breath of heaven on an evening like this.", "나는 이것을 사랑합니다. 이런 저녁이면 고귀하고 위대하고 선한 모든 것에 대한 내 타고난 감탄이 하늘의 숨결로 정화되는 것을 느낍니다."], ["Nature has such imperishable charms, such inextinguishable tenderness for me!—I am an old, fat man—talk which would become your lips, Miss Halcombe, sounds like a derision and a mockery on mine.", "자연은 나에게 이토록 스러지지 않는 매력을, 이토록 꺼지지 않는 애정을 지니고 있지요!—나는 늙고 뚱뚱한 사내입니다—홀콤 양의 입술에서 나오면 어울렸을 이런 말이 내 입에서 나오면 조롱이나 익살처럼 들리지요."], ["It is hard to be laughed at in my moments of sentiment, as if my soul was like myself, old and overgrown.", "내 감상의 순간에 웃음거리가 되는 것은 괴로운 일입니다. 마치 내 영혼도 나처럼 늙고 살이 쪄 버렸다는 듯이 말이지요."], ["Observe, dear lady, what a light is dying on the trees!", "보십시오, 친애하는 아가씨, 저 나무들 위로 어떤 빛이 스러져 가는지!"], ["Does it penetrate your heart, as it penetrates mine?”", "저 빛이 내 가슴을 파고들듯 당신의 가슴도 파고듭니까?”"]], [["He paused, looked at me, and repeated the famous lines of Dante on the Evening-time, with a melody and tenderness which added a charm of their own to the matchless beauty of the poetry itself.", "그는 말을 멈추고 나를 바라보더니, 저녁 무렵을 노래한 단테의 그 유명한 시구를 읊었다. 그 가락과 애틋함은 견줄 데 없이 아름다운 그 시 자체에 그만의 매력을 한 겹 더해 주었다."]], [["“Bah!”", "“쳇!”"], ["he cried suddenly, as the last cadence of those noble Italian words died away on his lips; “I make an old fool of myself, and only weary you all!", "그 고귀한 이탈리아어 낱말들의 마지막 여운이 입술에서 사라지자마자 그가 불쑥 외쳤다. “내가 늙은 바보 짓을 하고 있군요."], ["Let us shut up the window in our bosoms and get back to the matter-of-fact world.", "여러분을 지루하게만 하고 있어요! 우리 가슴속의 창문은 닫아 두고 사실의 세계로 돌아갑시다."], ["Percival!", "퍼시벌!"], ["I sanction the admission of the lamps.", "등불을 들이는 것을 내가 허락하겠네."], ["Lady Glyde—Miss Halcombe—Eleanor, my good wife—which of you will indulge me with a game at dominoes?”", "글라이드 부인—홀콤 양—엘리너, 나의 좋은 아내—누가 나와 도미노 한 판 해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["He addressed us all, but he looked especially at Laura.", "그는 우리 모두에게 말을 걸었지만, 특히 로라를 바라보았다."]], [["She had learnt to feel my dread of offending him, and she accepted his proposal.", "그 애는 내가 그의 비위를 거스를까 봐 두려워한다는 것을 느끼는 법을 익혀 온 터라 그의 제안을 받아들였다."], ["It was more than I could have done at that moment.", "그 순간의 나로서는 도저히 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["I could not have sat down at the same table with him for any consideration.", "무슨 일이 있어도 나는 그와 같은 탁자에 앉을 수 없었다."], ["His eyes seemed to reach my inmost soul through the thickening obscurity of the twilight.", "그의 눈은 짙어 가는 황혼의 어둠을 뚫고 내 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳까지 닿는 것 같았다."], ["His voice trembled along every nerve in my body, and turned me hot and cold alternately.", "그의 목소리는 내 몸의 모든 신경을 따라 떨렸고 나를 번갈아 뜨겁게 또 차갑게 만들었다."], ["The mystery and terror of my dream, which had haunted me at intervals all through the evening, now oppressed my mind with an unendurable foreboding and an unutterable awe.", "저녁 내내 틈틈이 나를 따라다니던 그 꿈의 신비와 공포가 이제는 견딜 수 없는 예감과 말로 다 할 수 없는 두려움으로 내 마음을 짓눌렀다."], ["I saw the white tomb again, and the veiled woman rising out of it by Hartright’s side.", "나는 그 흰 무덤을, 그리고 하트라이트 곁에서 그 속에서 일어서던 베일 쓴 여인을 다시 보았다."], ["The thought of Laura welled up like a spring in the depths of my heart, and filled it with waters of bitterness, never, never known to it before.", "로라 생각이 내 마음 깊은 곳에서 샘물처럼 솟아올라, 이제껏 한 번도 알지 못했던 쓰디쓴 물로 내 마음을 가득 채웠다."], ["I caught her by the hand as she passed me on her way to the table, and kissed her as if that night was to part us for ever.", "그 애가 탁자로 가느라 내 곁을 지날 때 나는 그 손을 붙잡고, 그날 밤이 우리를 영영 갈라놓기라도 할 것처럼 입을 맞추었다."], ["While they were all gazing at me in astonishment, I ran out through the low window which was open before me to the ground—ran out to hide from them in the darkness, to hide even from myself.", "모두가 놀라 나를 바라보는 사이, 나는 눈앞에 땅까지 트여 있던 낮은 창문을 통해 뛰쳐나갔다—그들에게서, 나 자신에게서까지 숨으려고 어둠 속으로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["We separated that evening later than usual.", "그날 저녁 우리는 여느 때보다 늦게 헤어졌다."], ["Towards midnight the summer silence was broken by the shuddering of a low, melancholy wind among the trees.", "자정 무렵 여름의 정적이 나무들 사이로 낮고 구슬픈 바람이 몸서리치듯 지나가는 소리에 깨졌다."], ["We all felt the sudden chill in the atmosphere, but the Count was the first to notice the stealthy rising of the wind.", "우리는 모두 대기가 갑자기 서늘해진 것을 느꼈으나, 바람이 슬그머니 일어나는 것을 맨 먼저 알아챈 사람은 백작이었다."], ["He stopped while he was lighting my candle for me, and held up his hand warningly—", "그는 나를 위해 초에 불을 붙여 주다 말고 멈추더니 경고하듯 손을 들어 올렸다—"]], [["“Listen!” he said.", "“들어 보십시오!”"], ["“There will be a change to-morrow.”", "그가 말했다. “내일은 달라질 겁니다.”"]], [["# VII", "# VII"]], [["June 19th.—The events of yesterday warned me to be ready, sooner or later, to meet the worst.", "6월 19일.—어제의 사건들은 내게 머지않아 최악의 사태를 맞을 각오를 하라고 경고했다."], ["To-day is not yet at an end, and the worst has come.", "오늘은 아직 끝나지도 않았는데, 최악은 이미 닥쳐 버렸다."]], [["Judging by the closest calculation of time that Laura and I could make, we arrived at the conclusion that Anne Catherick must have appeared at the boat-house at half-past two o’clock on the afternoon of yesterday.", "로라와 내가 최대한 정밀하게 시각을 따져 본 끝에, 우리는 앤 캐서릭이 어제 오후 두 시 반에 보트 창고에 나타났음이 틀림없다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["I accordingly arranged that Laura should just show herself at the luncheon-table to-day, and should then slip out at the first opportunity, leaving me behind to preserve appearances, and to follow her as soon as I could safely do so.", "그래서 나는 오늘 로라가 점심 식탁에 잠깐 얼굴만 비쳤다가 첫 기회에 슬쩍 빠져나가고, 나는 남아서 겉모양을 지키다가 안전해지는 대로 뒤따라가기로 계획을 세웠다."], ["This mode of proceeding, if no obstacles occurred to thwart us, would enable her to be at the boat-house before half-past two, and (when I left the table, in my turn) would take me to a safe position in the plantation before three.", "이렇게 하면 우리를 가로막을 장애가 생기지 않는 한 로라는 두 시 반 전에 보트 창고에 닿을 수 있고, (내 차례로 식탁을 뜨면) 나는 세 시 전에 잡목림 안의 안전한 자리에 이를 수 있을 터였다."]], [["The change in the weather, which last night’s wind warned us to expect, came with the morning.", "어젯밤 바람이 예고했던 날씨의 변화는 아침과 함께 찾아왔다."], ["It was raining heavily when I got up, and it continued to rain until twelve o’clock—when the clouds dispersed, the blue sky appeared, and the sun shone again with the bright promise of a fine afternoon.", "내가 일어났을 때 비가 억수같이 쏟아지고 있었고, 열두 시까지 계속 내리다가—구름이 흩어지고 푸른 하늘이 드러나며 해가 다시 비쳐 맑은 오후를 환하게 약속했다."]], [["My anxiety to know how Sir Percival and the Count would occupy the early part of the day was by no means set at rest, so far as Sir Percival was concerned, by his leaving us immediately after breakfast, and going out by himself, in spite of the rain.", "퍼시벌 경과 백작이 낮의 앞부분을 어떻게 보낼지 알고 싶던 내 조바심은, 적어도 퍼시벌 경에 관한 한, 그가 아침 식사 직후 비를 무릅쓰고 혼자 나가 버리는 바람에 조금도 가라앉지 않았다."], ["He neither told us where he was going nor when we might expect him back.", "그는 어디로 가는지도, 언제 돌아올지도 우리에게 말하지 않았다."], ["We saw him pass the breakfast-room window hastily, with his high boots and his waterproof coat on—and that was all.", "우리는 그가 목이 높은 장화에 방수 외투를 걸치고 아침 식사실 창 앞을 총총히 지나가는 것을 보았을 뿐, 그것이 전부였다."]], [["The Count passed the morning quietly indoors, some part of it in the library, some part in the drawing-room, playing odds and ends of music on the piano, and humming to himself.", "백작은 오전을 실내에서 조용히 보냈는데, 얼마간은 서재에서, 얼마간은 응접실에서 피아노로 이런저런 곡조각을 치며 혼자 흥얼거렸다."], ["Judging by appearances, the sentimental side of his character was persistently inclined to betray itself still.", "겉으로 보아서는 그의 성격 중 감상적인 면이 여전히 집요하게 드러나려 하고 있었다."], ["He was silent and sensitive, and ready to sigh and languish ponderously (as only fat men CAN sigh and languish) on the smallest provocation.", "그는 말이 없고 예민했으며, 아주 사소한 자극에도 (오직 뚱뚱한 남자만이 할 수 있는 방식으로) 묵직하게 한숨짓고 나른하게 시들어 보일 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["Luncheon-time came and Sir Percival did not return.", "점심시간이 되었으나 퍼시벌 경은 돌아오지 않았다."], ["The Count took his friend’s place at the table, plaintively devoured the greater part of a fruit tart, submerged under a whole jugful of cream, and explained the full merit of the achievement to us as soon as he had done.", "백작은 식탁에서 친구의 자리를 차지하고는, 크림 한 주전자를 통째로 부어 잠기게 한 과일 타르트를 구슬픈 표정으로 태반이나 먹어 치우고, 다 먹자마자 그 위업의 온전한 가치를 우리에게 설명했다."], ["“A taste for sweets,”", "“단것을 좋아하는 취향은,”"], ["he said in his softest tones and his tenderest manner, “is the innocent taste of women and children.", "그가 더할 나위 없이 부드러운 어조와 다정한 태도로 말했다. “여자와 아이의 순진무구한 취향이지요."], ["I love to share it with them—it is another bond, dear ladies, between you and me.”", "나는 그분들과 그 취향을 함께 나누는 것이 좋습니다—그것 또한 하나의 끈이니까요, 친애하는 부인들, 여러분과 나 사이를 잇는.”"]], [["Laura left the table in ten minutes’ time.", "로라는 십 분 만에 식탁을 떴다."], ["I was sorely tempted to accompany her.", "나도 따라가고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."], ["But if we had both gone out together we must have excited suspicion, and worse still, if we allowed Anne Catherick to see Laura, accompanied by a second person who was a stranger to her, we should in all probability forfeit her confidence from that moment, never to regain it again.", "그러나 우리 둘이 함께 나갔다면 틀림없이 의심을 샀을 테고, 더 나쁘게는, 앤 캐서릭에게 낯선 제삼자를 데리고 온 로라의 모습을 보이게 했다면 우리는 십중팔구 그 순간부터 그 여자의 신뢰를 잃고 다시는 되찾지 못했을 것이다."]], [["I waited, therefore, as patiently as I could, until the servant came in to clear the table.", "그래서 나는 하인이 식탁을 치우러 들어올 때까지 되도록 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["When I quitted the room, there were no signs, in the house or out of it, of Sir Percival’s return.", "방을 나설 때 집 안에도 밖에도 퍼시벌 경이 돌아온 기색은 없었다. 나는 백작을 남겨 두고 나왔다."], ["I left the Count with a piece of sugar between his lips, and the vicious cockatoo scrambling up his waistcoat to get at it, while Madame Fosco, sitting opposite to her husband, watched the proceedings of his bird and himself as attentively as if she had never seen anything of the sort before in her life.", "그는 입술 사이에 설탕 조각을 물고 있었고, 그 심술궂은 앵무새는 그것을 차지하려고 그의 조끼를 기어오르고 있었으며, 남편 맞은편에 앉은 포스코 백작부인은 평생 그런 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없는 사람처럼 새와 남편의 거동을 골똘히 지켜보고 있었다."], ["On my way to the plantation I kept carefully beyond the range of view from the luncheon-room window.", "잡목림으로 가는 길에 나는 점심 식사실 창에서 보이는 범위를 조심스럽게 피해 다녔다."], ["Nobody saw me and nobody followed me.", "아무도 나를 보지 않았고 아무도 나를 따라오지 않았다."], ["It was then a quarter to three o’clock by my watch.", "내 시계로 그때가 세 시 십오 분 전이었다."]], [["Once among the trees I walked rapidly, until I had advanced more than half-way through the plantation.", "나무들 사이에 들어서자 나는 잡목림의 절반을 넘게 나아갈 때까지 빠르게 걸었다."], ["At that point I slackened my pace and proceeded cautiously, but I saw no one, and heard no voices.", "그 지점에서 걸음을 늦추고 조심스럽게 나아갔으나, 아무도 보이지 않았고 사람 목소리도 들리지 않았다."], ["By little and little I came within view of the back of the boat-house—stopped and listened—then went on, till I was close behind it, and must have heard any persons who were talking inside.", "조금씩 보트 창고 뒤편이 눈에 들어왔고—나는 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다—그러고는 계속 나아가 바로 그 뒤까지 다가갔으니, 안에서 이야기하는 사람이 있었다면 반드시 들렸을 것이다."], ["Still the silence was unbroken—still far and near no sign of a living creature appeared anywhere.", "그래도 침묵은 깨지지 않았고, 멀리서도 가까이서도 살아 있는 것의 기척은 어디에도 나타나지 않았다."]], [["After skirting round by the back of the building, first on one side and then on the other, and making no discoveries, I ventured in front of it, and fairly looked in.", "건물 뒤편을 이쪽저쪽으로 돌아가며 살폈지만 아무것도 발견하지 못하자, 나는 큰맘 먹고 앞으로 나가 안을 정면으로 들여다보았다."], ["The place was empty.", "안은 비어 있었다."]], [["I called, “Laura!”—at first softly, then louder and louder.", "나는 “로라!” 하고 불렀다—처음에는 나직하게, 그다음에는 점점 더 크게."], ["No one answered and no one appeared.", "아무도 대답하지 않았고 아무도 나타나지 않았다."], ["For all that I could see and hear, the only human creature in the neighbourhood of the lake and the plantation was myself.", "내가 보고 들을 수 있는 한, 호수와 잡목림 근처에 있는 유일한 사람은 나뿐이었다."]], [["My heart began to beat violently, but I kept my resolution, and searched, first the boat-house and then the ground in front of it, for any signs which might show me whether Laura had really reached the place or not.", "심장이 세차게 뛰기 시작했으나 나는 결심을 지켰고, 먼저 보트 창고 안을, 그다음 그 앞의 땅을 뒤지며 로라가 정말로 이곳에 닿았는지 알려 줄 만한 자취를 찾았다."], ["No mark of her presence appeared inside the building, but I found traces of her outside it, in footsteps on the sand.", "건물 안에는 그녀가 다녀간 표시가 없었지만, 밖에서는 모래에 찍힌 발자국으로 그 흔적을 찾아냈다."]], [["I detected the footsteps of two persons—large footsteps like a man’s, and small footsteps, which, by putting my own feet into them and testing their size in that manner, I felt certain were Laura’s.", "나는 두 사람의 발자국을 찾아냈다—남자 것처럼 큰 발자국과, 내 발을 직접 대어 크기를 재어 본 결과 로라의 것이 틀림없다고 확신하게 된 작은 발자국이었다."], ["The ground was confusedly marked in this way just before the boat-house.", "보트 창고 바로 앞의 땅이 그런 식으로 어지럽게 밟혀 있었다."], ["Close against one side of it, under shelter of the projecting roof, I discovered a little hole in the sand—a hole artificially made, beyond a doubt.", "그 한쪽 옆, 튀어나온 지붕 아래 가려진 자리에서 나는 모래에 파인 작은 구덩이를 발견했다—의심할 여지 없이 사람이 판 구덩이였다."], ["I just noticed it, and then turned away immediately to trace the footsteps as far as I could, and to follow the direction in which they might lead me.", "나는 그것을 눈여겨보기만 하고 곧바로 돌아서서, 발자국을 갈 수 있는 데까지 좇으며 그것이 나를 어디로 이끄는지 따라가 보기로 했다."]], [["They led me, starting from the left-hand side of the boat-house, along the edge of the trees, a distance, I should think, of between two and three hundred yards, and then the sandy ground showed no further trace of them.", "발자국은 보트 창고 왼편에서 시작해 나무들 가장자리를 따라, 내 짐작으로 이삼백 야드쯤 나를 데려갔고, 그 뒤로는 모래땅에 더 이상 자취가 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Feeling that the persons whose course I was tracking must necessarily have entered the plantation at this point, I entered it too.", "내가 뒤쫓던 사람들이 이 지점에서 잡목림으로 들어갔음이 틀림없다고 느낀 나도 그 안으로 들어섰다."], ["At first I could find no path, but I discovered one afterwards, just faintly traced among the trees, and followed it.", "처음에는 길을 찾을 수 없었지만 곧 나무들 사이로 희미하게 나 있는 길 하나를 찾아내어 그것을 따라갔다."], ["It took me, for some distance, in the direction of the village, until I stopped at a point where another foot-track crossed it.", "그 길은 얼마 동안 마을 쪽으로 나를 데려갔고, 나는 또 다른 발길 자국이 그 길과 엇갈리는 지점에서 멈춰 섰다."], ["The brambles grew thickly on either side of this second path.", "이 두 번째 길의 양옆으로는 가시덤불이 무성하게 자라 있었다."], ["I stood looking down it, uncertain which way to take next, and while I looked I saw on one thorny branch some fragments of fringe from a woman’s shawl.", "다음에 어느 쪽으로 가야 할지 몰라 그 길을 내려다보며 서 있는데, 가시 돋친 가지 하나에 여자 숄에서 떨어져 나온 술 조각이 걸려 있는 것이 보였다."], ["A closer examination of the fringe satisfied me that it had been torn from a shawl of Laura’s, and I instantly followed the second path.", "그 술을 더 가까이 살펴보니 로라의 숄에서 찢겨 나온 것이 분명했고, 나는 즉시 두 번째 길을 따라갔다."], ["It brought me out at last, to my great relief, at the back of the house.", "그 길은 마침내, 참으로 다행스럽게도, 저택 뒤편으로 나를 데려다주었다."], ["I say to my great relief, because I inferred that Laura must, for some unknown reason, have returned before me by this roundabout way.", "다행스럽다고 말하는 까닭은, 로라가 무슨 까닭인지는 몰라도 이 에움길로 나보다 먼저 돌아갔음이 틀림없다고 짐작했기 때문이다."], ["I went in by the court-yard and the offices.", "나는 안뜰과 부속 건물을 지나 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The first person whom I met in crossing the servants’ hall was Mrs. Michelson, the housekeeper.", "하인들이 쓰는 복도를 가로지르다 처음 마주친 사람은 가정부 마이컬슨 부인이었다."]], [["“Do you know,”", "“혹시 아세요,”"], ["I asked, “whether Lady Glyde has come in from her walk or not?”", "내가 물었다. “글라이드 부인께서 산책에서 돌아오셨는지요?”"]], [["“My lady came in a little while ago with Sir Percival,”", "“마님께서는 조금 전에 퍼시벌 경과 함께 들어오셨습니다.”"], ["answered the housekeeper.", "가정부가 대답했다."], ["“I am afraid, Miss Halcombe, something very distressing has happened.”", "“홀콤 양, 아무래도 몹시 언짢은 일이 있었던 모양입니다.”"]], [["My heart sank within me.", "가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다."], ["“You don’t mean an accident?”", "“사고가 났다는 말씀은 아니지요?”"], ["I said faintly.", "나는 힘없이 말했다."]], [["“No, no—thank God, no accident.", "“아니에요, 아닙니다—고맙게도 사고는 아닙니다."], ["But my lady ran upstairs to her own room in tears, and Sir Percival has ordered me to give Fanny warning to leave in an hour’s time.”", "하지만 마님께서는 눈물을 흘리며 당신 방으로 뛰어 올라가셨고, 퍼시벌 경께서는 저더러 패니에게 한 시간 안에 나가라고 통고하라 하셨습니다.”"]], [["Fanny was Laura’s maid—a good affectionate girl who had been with her for years—the only person in the house whose fidelity and devotion we could both depend upon.", "패니는 로라의 몸종이었다—여러 해 동안 그녀를 모셔 온 착하고 정 많은 아이로, 이 집에서 우리 둘이 그 성실함과 헌신을 믿을 수 있는 유일한 사람이었다."]], [["“Where is Fanny?”", "“패니는 어디 있나요?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“In my room, Miss Halcombe.", "“제 방에 있습니다, 홀콤 양."], ["The young woman is quite overcome, and I told her to sit down and try to recover herself.”", "그 아이가 하도 넋을 놓기에 앉아서 마음을 좀 추스르라고 일러 두었습니다.”"]], [["I went to Mrs. Michelson’s room, and found Fanny in a corner, with her box by her side, crying bitterly.", "나는 마이컬슨 부인의 방으로 갔고, 패니가 구석에 자기 상자를 옆에 둔 채 서럽게 울고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["She could give me no explanation whatever of her sudden dismissal.", "그 아이는 자기가 왜 갑자기 쫓겨나는지 아무 설명도 해 주지 못했다."], ["Sir Percival had ordered that she should have a month’s wages, in place of a month’s warning, and go.", "퍼시벌 경은 한 달 전 예고 대신 한 달 치 급료를 주어 내보내라고 명령했다."], ["No reason had been assigned—no objection had been made to her conduct.", "이유는 밝혀지지 않았고—그 아이의 처신에 무슨 트집이 잡힌 것도 아니었다."], ["She had been forbidden to appeal to her mistress, forbidden even to see her for a moment to say good-bye.", "그 아이는 여주인에게 하소연하는 것도, 작별 인사를 하려고 잠깐 얼굴을 보는 것조차 금지당했다."], ["She was to go without explanations or farewells, and to go at once.", "설명도 작별도 없이, 그것도 당장 떠나야 했다."]], [["After soothing the poor girl by a few friendly words, I asked where she proposed to sleep that night.", "다정한 몇 마디로 가엾은 아이를 달랜 뒤, 나는 그날 밤 어디서 잘 생각이냐고 물었다."], ["She replied that she thought of going to the little inn in the village, the landlady of which was a respectable woman, known to the servants at Blackwater Park.", "그 아이는 마을의 작은 여관으로 갈까 한다고, 그 집 안주인이 점잖은 여자이고 블랙워터 파크의 하인들도 아는 사람이라고 대답했다."], ["The next morning, by leaving early, she might get back to her friends in Cumberland without stopping in London, where she was a total stranger.", "이튿날 아침 일찍 떠나면, 생판 낯선 런던에 머무르지 않고도 컴벌랜드의 친지들에게 돌아갈 수 있을 터였다."]], [["I felt directly that Fanny’s departure offered us a safe means of communication with London and with Limmeridge House, of which it might be very important to avail ourselves.", "나는 패니가 떠나는 것이 우리에게 런던 및 리머리지 하우스와 통할 안전한 수단이 되어 주며, 그것을 이용하는 일이 매우 중요할지도 모른다는 것을 곧바로 느꼈다."], ["Accordingly, I told her that she might expect to hear from her mistress or from me in the course of the evening, and that she might depend on our both doing all that lay in our power to help her, under the trial of leaving us for the present.", "그래서 나는 저녁 사이에 여주인이나 나에게서 소식이 있을 것이라고, 그리고 당분간 우리를 떠나는 시련 속에서도 우리 둘이 힘닿는 데까지 그 아이를 도우리라 믿어도 좋다고 말해 주었다."], ["Those words said, I shook hands with her and went upstairs.", "그 말을 하고 나서 나는 그 아이와 악수하고 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["The door which led to Laura’s room was the door of an ante-chamber opening on to the passage.", "로라의 방으로 통하는 문은 복도로 열리는 앞방의 문이었다."], ["When I tried it, it was bolted on the inside.", "열어 보니 안에서 빗장이 걸려 있었다."]], [["I knocked, and the door was opened by the same heavy, overgrown housemaid whose lumpish insensibility had tried my patience so severely on the day when I found the wounded dog.", "내가 두드리자, 다친 개를 발견하던 날 그 굼뜬 무신경으로 내 인내심을 호되게 시험했던 바로 그 크고 미련한 하녀가 문을 열었다."]], [["I had, since that time, discovered that her name was Margaret Porcher, and that she was the most awkward, slatternly, and obstinate servant in the house.", "그 뒤로 나는 그 여자의 이름이 마거릿 포처이며, 이 집에서 가장 굼뜨고 지저분하고 고집 센 하인이라는 것을 알아냈다."]], [["On opening the door she instantly stepped out to the threshold, and stood grinning at me in stolid silence.", "문을 열자 그 여자는 곧바로 문지방으로 나서더니, 멍하니 말없이 히죽거리며 나를 마주 보고 섰다."]], [["“Why do you stand there?”", "“왜 거기 서 있는 거냐?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Don’t you see that I want to come in?”", "“내가 들어가려는 것이 보이지 않느냐?”"]], [["“Ah, but you mustn’t come in,”", "“아, 하지만 들어가시면 안 됩니다.”"], ["was the answer, with another and a broader grin still.", "그것이 대답이었고, 히죽거림은 한층 더 커졌다."]], [["“How dare you talk to me in that way?", "“네가 감히 나에게 그런 말버릇을 쓰느냐?"], ["Stand back instantly!”", "당장 비켜서라!”"]], [["She stretched out a great red hand and arm on each side of her, so as to bar the doorway, and slowly nodded her addle head at me.", "그 여자는 크고 붉은 손과 팔을 양옆으로 뻗어 문간을 가로막고는, 멍청한 머리를 천천히 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["“Master’s orders,”", "“주인 나리 분부입니다.”"], ["she said, and nodded again.", "그 여자는 그렇게 말하고 다시 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["I had need of all my self-control to warn me against contesting the matter with HER, and to remind me that the next words I had to say must be addressed to her master.", "저 여자를 상대로 이 문제를 다투어서는 안 된다고 스스로를 타이르고, 다음에 할 말은 반드시 그 여자의 주인에게 해야 한다는 것을 잊지 않으려면 내 자제력을 남김없이 끌어모아야 했다."], ["I turned my back on her, and instantly went downstairs to find him.", "나는 그 여자에게 등을 돌리고 곧장 아래층으로 그를 찾으러 내려갔다."], ["My resolution to keep my temper under all the irritations that Sir Percival could offer was, by this time, as completely forgotten—I say so to my shame—as if I had never made it.", "퍼시벌 경이 어떤 부아를 돋우더라도 성을 내지 않겠다던 내 결심은, 이때쯤에는—부끄럽지만 고백하건대—애초에 그런 결심을 한 적조차 없는 것처럼 깨끗이 잊혀 있었다."], ["It did me good, after all I had suffered and suppressed in that house—it actually did me good to feel how angry I was.", "그 집에서 그토록 참고 눌러 온 끝에, 내가 얼마나 화가 났는지를 느끼는 것은—정말이지 속이 후련했다."]], [["The drawing-room and the breakfast-room were both empty.", "응접실과 아침 식사실은 둘 다 비어 있었다."], ["I went on to the library, and there I found Sir Percival, the Count, and Madame Fosco.", "서재로 가 보니 퍼시벌 경과 백작과 포스코 백작부인이 있었다."], ["They were all three standing up, close together, and Sir Percival had a little slip of paper in his hand.", "세 사람은 모두 바싹 붙어 서 있었고, 퍼시벌 경은 손에 작은 종잇조각을 들고 있었다."], ["As I opened the door I heard the Count say to him, “No—a thousand times over, no.”", "문을 여는데 백작이 그에게 “안 되오—천 번을 물어도 안 되오.” 하고 말하는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["I walked straight up to him, and looked him full in the face.", "나는 곧장 그에게 걸어가 정면으로 얼굴을 마주 보았다."]], [["“Am I to understand, Sir Percival, that your wife’s room is a prison, and that your housemaid is the gaoler who keeps it?”", "“퍼시벌 경, 부인의 방이 감옥이고 댁의 하녀가 그 방을 지키는 간수라고 알아들으면 되겠습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, that is what you are to understand,”", "“그렇소, 그렇게 알아들으면 되겠소.”"], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Take care my gaoler hasn’t got double duty to do—take care your room is not a prison too.”", "“내 간수가 두 몫을 하게 되지 않도록 조심하시오—당신 방도 감옥이 되지 않도록 조심하란 말이오.”"]], [["“Take YOU care how you treat your wife, and how you threaten ME,”", "“당신 아내를 어떻게 대하는지, 나를 어떻게 협박하는지 당신이나 조심하시지요.”"], ["I broke out in the heat of my anger.", "나는 치미는 분노에 못 이겨 쏘아붙였다."], ["“There are laws in England to protect women from cruelty and outrage.", "“영국에는 여자를 학대와 폭력에서 지켜 주는 법이 있습니다."], ["If you hurt a hair of Laura’s head, if you dare to interfere with my freedom, come what may, to those laws I will appeal.”", "로라의 머리카락 한 올이라도 다치게 하거나, 감히 내 자유를 침해한다면, 무슨 일이 있어도 나는 그 법에 호소하겠습니다.”"]], [["Instead of answering me he turned round to the Count.", "그는 내게 대답하는 대신 백작 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“What did I tell you?”", "“내가 뭐라고 했소?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“What do you say now?”", "“이제 뭐라고 하겠소?”"]], [["“What I said before,”", "“전에 한 말 그대로요.”"], ["replied the Count—“No.”", "백작이 대답했다—“안 되오.”"]], [["Even in the vehemence of my anger I felt his calm, cold, grey eyes on my face.", "분노가 격렬한 와중에도 나는 그의 차분하고 차가운 잿빛 눈이 내 얼굴에 머무는 것을 느꼈다."], ["They turned away from me as soon as he had spoken, and looked significantly at his wife.", "그 눈은 말이 끝나자마자 나를 떠나 의미심장하게 자기 아내를 바라보았다."], ["Madame Fosco immediately moved close to my side, and in that position addressed Sir Percival before either of us could speak again.", "포스코 백작부인은 즉시 내 곁으로 바싹 다가섰고, 그 자리에서 우리 둘 중 누구도 다시 입을 열기 전에 퍼시벌 경에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Favour me with your attention for one moment,”", "“잠깐만 제 말에 귀를 기울여 주십시오.”"], ["she said, in her clear icily-suppressed tones.", "그녀는 맑고 얼음처럼 억눌린 어조로 말했다."], ["“I have to thank you, Sir Percival, for your hospitality, and to decline taking advantage of it any longer.", "“퍼시벌 경, 베풀어 주신 후대에 감사드리며, 더는 그 호의를 누리지 않겠습니다."], ["I remain in no house in which ladies are treated as your wife and Miss Halcombe have been treated here to-day!”", "오늘 이 집에서 부인과 홀콤 양이 받은 것과 같은 대우를 숙녀에게 하는 집에 저는 머무르지 않겠습니다!”"]], [["Sir Percival drew back a step, and stared at her in dead silence.", "퍼시벌 경은 한 걸음 물러서서 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에 그녀를 노려보았다."], ["The declaration he had just heard—a declaration which he well knew, as I well knew, Madame Fosco would not have ventured to make without her husband’s permission—seemed to petrify him with surprise.", "방금 들은 선언—나도 잘 알고 그도 잘 알다시피 포스코 백작부인이 남편의 허락 없이는 감히 입에 올리지 못했을 그 선언은, 그를 놀라움으로 굳어 버리게 한 듯했다."], ["The Count stood by, and looked at his wife with the most enthusiastic admiration.", "백작은 옆에 서서 더할 나위 없이 열렬한 감탄의 눈으로 아내를 바라보았다."]], [["“She is sublime!”", "“숭고하구려!”"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말처럼 말했다."], ["He approached her while he spoke, and drew her hand through his arm.", "그렇게 말하며 아내에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼웠다."], ["“I am at your service, Eleanor,”", "“나는 당신 뜻대로 하겠소, 엘리너.”"], ["he went on, with a quiet dignity that I had never noticed in him before.", "그는 내가 여태 그에게서 본 적 없는 조용한 위엄을 갖추고 말을 이었다."], ["“And at Miss Halcombe’s service, if she will honour me by accepting all the assistance I can offer her.”", "“그리고 홀콤 양의 뜻대로도 하지요, 내가 드릴 수 있는 모든 도움을 받아 주시는 영광을 베푸신다면.”"]], [["“Damn it!", "“빌어먹을!"], ["what do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 소리요?”"], ["cried Sir Percival, as the Count quietly moved away with his wife to the door.", "백작이 아내와 함께 조용히 문 쪽으로 걸어가자 퍼시벌 경이 소리쳤다."]], [["“At other times I mean what I say, but at this time I mean what my wife says,”", "“여느 때는 내 말이 곧 내 뜻이지만, 이번만은 내 아내의 말이 곧 내 뜻이오.”"], ["replied the impenetrable Italian.", "그 속을 알 수 없는 이탈리아인이 대답했다."], ["“We have changed places, Percival, for once, and Madame Fosco’s opinion is—mine.”", "“이번 한 번은 우리가 자리를 바꾸었소, 퍼시벌. 그리고 포스코 백작부인의 의견이—곧 내 의견이오.”"]], [["Sir Percival crumpled up the paper in his hand, and pushing past the Count, with another oath, stood between him and the door.", "퍼시벌 경은 손에 든 종이를 구겨 쥐고, 또 한 번 욕설을 내뱉으며 백작을 밀치고 지나가 그와 문 사이에 버티고 섰다."]], [["“Have your own way,”", "“마음대로 하시오.”"], ["he said, with baffled rage in his low, half-whispering tones.", "그는 낮게 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리에 좌절한 분노를 담아 말했다."], ["“Have your own way—and see what comes of it.”", "“마음대로 하시오—그리고 그 끝이 어찌 되는지 보시오.”"], ["With those words he left the room.", "그 말을 남기고 그는 방을 나갔다."]], [["Madame Fosco glanced inquiringly at her husband.", "포스코 백작부인이 남편을 캐묻듯 바라보았다."], ["“He has gone away very suddenly,”", "“아주 갑자기 나가 버리는군요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“What does it mean?”", "“무슨 뜻일까요?”"]], [["“It means that you and I together have brought the worst-tempered man in all England to his senses,”", "“당신과 내가 힘을 합쳐 영국에서 가장 성마른 사내를 제정신으로 돌려놓았다는 뜻이오.”"], ["answered the Count.", "백작이 대답했다."], ["“It means, Miss Halcombe, that Lady Glyde is relieved from a gross indignity, and you from the repetition of an unpardonable insult.", "“홀콤 양, 글라이드 부인은 심한 모욕에서 벗어났고, 당신은 용서할 수 없는 무례를 되풀이해 겪는 일에서 벗어났다는 뜻입니다."], ["Suffer me to express my admiration of your conduct and your courage at a very trying moment.”", "참으로 견디기 힘든 순간에 보여 주신 처신과 용기에 대한 제 감탄을 표하도록 허락해 주십시오.”"]], [["“Sincere admiration,”", "“진심 어린 감탄이지요.”"], ["suggested Madame Fosco.", "포스코 백작부인이 거들었다."]], [["“Sincere admiration,”", "“진심 어린 감탄.”"], ["echoed the Count.", "백작이 되뇌었다."]], [["I had no longer the strength of my first angry resistance to outrage and injury to support me.", "나에게는 더 이상 처음의 그 성난 저항으로 모욕과 상처를 버텨 낼 힘이 남아 있지 않았다."], ["My heart-sick anxiety to see Laura, my sense of my own helpless ignorance of what had happened at the boat-house, pressed on me with an intolerable weight.", "로라를 보고 싶은 병적인 조바심과, 보트 창고에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 아무것도 모르는 내 무력함이 견딜 수 없는 무게로 나를 짓눌렀다."], ["I tried to keep up appearances by speaking to the Count and his wife in the tone which they had chosen to adopt in speaking to me, but the words failed on my lips—my breath came short and thick—my eyes looked longingly, in silence, at the door.", "나는 백작과 그의 아내가 나에게 쓰기로 한 그 어조에 맞추어 말함으로써 겉모양을 유지하려 애썼지만, 말이 입술에서 나오지 않았다—숨이 짧고 가빠졌고—두 눈은 말없이 문 쪽을 애타게 바라보고 있었다."], ["The Count, understanding my anxiety, opened it, went out, and pulled it to after him.", "백작은 내 조바심을 알아채고 문을 열더니 밖으로 나가 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["At the same time Sir Percival’s heavy step descended the stairs.", "바로 그때 퍼시벌 경의 묵직한 발소리가 계단을 내려왔다."], ["I heard them whispering together outside, while Madame Fosco was assuring me, in her calmest and most conventional manner, that she rejoiced, for all our sakes, that Sir Percival’s conduct had not obliged her husband and herself to leave Blackwater Park.", "포스코 백작부인이 더없이 침착하고 판에 박힌 태도로, 퍼시벌 경의 처사 때문에 남편과 자기가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나지 않아도 되게 되어 우리 모두를 위해 기쁘다고 나에게 이르는 동안, 나는 두 사람이 밖에서 소곤거리는 소리를 들었다."], ["Before she had done speaking the whispering ceased, the door opened, and the Count looked in.", "그녀의 말이 끝나기도 전에 소곤거림이 그치고 문이 열리며 백작이 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Miss Halcombe,”", "“홀콤 양,”"], ["he said, “I am happy to inform you that Lady Glyde is mistress again in her own house.", "그가 말했다. “글라이드 부인께서 다시 이 댁의 안주인이 되셨음을 알려 드리게 되어 기쁩니다."], ["I thought it might be more agreeable to you to hear of this change for the better from me than from Sir Percival, and I have therefore expressly returned to mention it.”", "이 반가운 변화를 퍼시벌 경보다는 나에게서 들으시는 편이 더 마음 편하시리라 생각하여, 일부러 다시 와서 말씀드리는 것입니다.”"]], [["“Admirable delicacy!”", "“감탄스러운 배려로군요!”"], ["said Madame Fosco, paying back her husband’s tribute of admiration with the Count’s own coin, in the Count’s own manner.", "포스코 백작부인이 남편의 감탄이라는 헌사를 백작 자신의 화폐로, 백작 자신의 방식대로 되갚으며 말했다."], ["He smiled and bowed as if he had received a formal compliment from a polite stranger, and drew back to let me pass out first.", "그는 마치 예의 바른 낯선 사람에게서 격식 차린 찬사를 받은 것처럼 미소 지으며 고개를 숙였고, 내가 먼저 지나가도록 물러섰다."]], [["Sir Percival was standing in the hall.", "퍼시벌 경은 현관 홀에 서 있었다."], ["As I hurried to the stairs I heard him call impatiently to the Count to come out of the library.", "내가 서둘러 계단으로 가는데, 그가 조바심 내며 백작에게 서재에서 나오라고 부르는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“What are you waiting there for?”", "“거기서 뭘 기다리는 거요?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I want to speak to you.”", "“당신과 할 말이 있소.”"]], [["“And I want to think a little by myself,”", "“나는 혼자서 좀 생각하고 싶소.”"], ["replied the other.", "상대가 대답했다."], ["“Wait till later, Percival, wait till later.”", "“나중에 합시다, 퍼시벌, 나중에.”"]], [["Neither he nor his friend said any more.", "그도 그의 친구도 더는 말이 없었다."], ["I gained the top of the stairs and ran along the passage.", "나는 계단 꼭대기에 이르러 복도를 달렸다."], ["In my haste and my agitation I left the door of the ante-chamber open, but I closed the door of the bedroom the moment I was inside it.", "서두르고 마음이 들뜬 나머지 앞방 문은 열어 둔 채였지만, 침실 문은 들어서자마자 닫았다."]], [["Laura was sitting alone at the far end of the room, her arms resting wearily on a table, and her face hidden in her hands.", "로라는 방 저쪽 끝에 홀로 앉아 두 팔을 지친 듯 탁자에 얹고 얼굴을 손에 묻고 있었다."], ["She started up with a cry of delight when she saw me.", "그녀는 나를 보자 기쁨의 소리를 지르며 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“How did you get here?”", "“어떻게 여기 왔어?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["“Who gave you leave?", "“누가 허락해 줬어?"], ["Not Sir Percival?”", "퍼시벌 경은 아닐 테고?”"]], [["In my overpowering anxiety to hear what she had to tell me, I could not answer her—I could only put questions on my side.", "그녀가 들려줄 이야기를 듣고 싶은 조바심에 짓눌린 나는 대답할 수가 없었다—나로서는 이쪽에서 물을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Laura’s eagerness to know what had passed downstairs proved, however, too strong to be resisted.", "그러나 아래층에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 알고 싶어 하는 로라의 열의는 뿌리치기에 너무 강했다."], ["She persistently repeated her inquiries.", "그녀는 끈질기게 물음을 되풀이했다."]], [["“The Count, of course,”", "“백작이지, 누구겠니.”"], ["I answered impatiently.", "나는 조바심 내며 대답했다."], ["“Whose influence in the house——”", "“이 집에서 누구의 힘이——”"]], [["She stopped me with a gesture of disgust.", "그녀는 혐오스럽다는 몸짓으로 내 말을 막았다."]], [["“Don’t speak of him,”", "“그 사람 이야기는 하지 마.”"], ["she cried.", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“The Count is the vilest creature breathing!", "“백작은 세상에 숨 쉬는 것 중 가장 비열한 인간이야!"], ["The Count is a miserable Spy——!”", "백작은 비참한 첩자야——!”"]], [["Before we could either of us say another word we were alarmed by a soft knocking at the door of the bedroom.", "우리 둘 중 누구도 다시 한마디 하기 전에, 침실 문을 나직이 두드리는 소리에 우리는 화들짝 놀랐다."]], [["I had not yet sat down, and I went first to see who it was.", "나는 아직 앉지 않았던 터라 누구인지 보러 먼저 갔다."], ["When I opened the door Madame Fosco confronted me with my handkerchief in her hand.", "문을 여니 포스코 백작부인이 내 손수건을 손에 들고 나를 마주 보고 서 있었다."]], [["“You dropped this downstairs, Miss Halcombe,”", "“홀콤 양, 아래층에서 이걸 떨어뜨리셨더군요.”"], ["she said, “and I thought I could bring it to you, as I was passing by to my own room.”", "그녀가 말했다. “제 방으로 가는 길이라 가져다 드리면 좋겠다 싶었습니다.”"]], [["Her face, naturally pale, had turned to such a ghastly whiteness that I started at the sight of it.", "본디 창백한 그녀의 얼굴이 어찌나 소름 끼치게 하얗게 질렸던지 나는 그것을 보고 흠칫했다."], ["Her hands, so sure and steady at all other times, trembled violently, and her eyes looked wolfishly past me through the open door, and fixed on Laura.", "여느 때는 그토록 확실하고 흔들림 없던 두 손이 심하게 떨렸고, 두 눈은 나를 지나쳐 열린 문 너머 로라에게 늑대처럼 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["She had been listening before she knocked!", "그녀는 문을 두드리기 전에 엿듣고 있었던 것이다!"], ["I saw it in her white face, I saw it in her trembling hands, I saw it in her look at Laura.", "나는 그것을 그 하얀 얼굴에서 보았고, 떨리는 두 손에서 보았고, 로라를 바라보는 그 눈길에서 보았다."]], [["After waiting an instant she turned from me in silence, and slowly walked away.", "잠시 기다리더니 그녀는 말없이 나에게서 돌아서서 천천히 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["I closed the door again.", "나는 다시 문을 닫았다."], ["“Oh, Laura!", "“아, 로라!"], ["Laura!", "로라!"], ["We shall both rue the day when you called the Count a Spy!”", "네가 백작을 첩자라고 부른 오늘을 우리 둘 다 후회하게 될 거야!”"]], [["“You would have called him so yourself, Marian, if you had known what I know.", "“언니도 내가 아는 걸 알았다면 그렇게 불렀을 거야, 매리언."], ["Anne Catherick was right.", "앤 캐서릭 말이 옳았어."], ["There was a third person watching us in the plantation yesterday, and that third person—-”", "어제 잡목림에서 우리를 지켜보던 제삼자가 있었고, 그 제삼자는——”"]], [["“Are you sure it was the Count?”", "“그게 백작이었던 게 확실하니?”"]], [["“I am absolutely certain.", "“절대적으로 확실해."], ["He was Sir Percival’s spy—he was Sir Percival’s informer—he set Sir Percival watching and waiting, all the morning through, for Anne Catherick and for me.”", "그가 퍼시벌 경의 첩자였어—그가 퍼시벌 경의 밀고자였어—그가 퍼시벌 경더러 오전 내내 앤 캐서릭과 나를 지켜보며 기다리게 만든 거야.”"]], [["“Is Anne found?", "“앤은 찾았니?"], ["Did you see her at the lake?”", "호수에서 그 여자를 보았어?”"]], [["“No.", "“아니."], ["She has saved herself by keeping away from the place.", "그 사람은 그곳에 오지 않아서 스스로를 지켰어."], ["When I got to the boat-house no one was there.”", "내가 보트 창고에 갔을 때 거긴 아무도 없었어.”"]], [["“Yes?", "“그래서?"], ["Yes?”", "그래서?”"]], [["“I went in and sat waiting for a few minutes.", "“안에 들어가 몇 분 앉아서 기다렸어."], ["But my restlessness made me get up again, to walk about a little.", "그런데 안절부절못하겠어서 다시 일어나 조금 거닐었지."], ["As I passed out I saw some marks on the sand, close under the front of the boat-house.", "나가면서 보트 창고 앞쪽 바로 아래 모래에 무슨 자국이 있는 걸 봤어."], ["I stooped down to examine them, and discovered a word written in large letters on the sand.", "몸을 굽혀 살펴보니 모래 위에 큰 글씨로 한 낱말이 쓰여 있었어."], ["The word was—LOOK.”", "그 낱말은—보라, 였어.”"]], [["“And you scraped away the sand, and dug a hollow place in it?”", "“그래서 모래를 긁어내고 그 자리를 우묵하게 팠구나?”"]], [["“How do you know that, Marian?”", "“그걸 언니가 어떻게 알아, 매리언?”"]], [["“I saw the hollow place myself when I followed you to the boat-house.", "“내가 너를 따라 보트 창고에 갔을 때 그 우묵한 자리를 직접 보았단다."], ["Go on—go on!”", "계속해—계속해!”"]], [["“Yes, I scraped away the sand on the surface, and in a little while I came to a strip of paper hidden beneath, which had writing on it.", "“응, 나는 표면의 모래를 긁어냈고, 조금 지나 그 밑에 숨겨져 있던 종잇조각에 닿았는데 거기 글씨가 있었어."], ["The writing was signed with Anne Catherick’s initials.”", "그 글에는 앤 캐서릭의 머리글자가 서명되어 있었고.”"]], [["“Where is it?”", "“그건 어디 있니?”"]], [["“Sir Percival has taken it from me.”", "“퍼시벌 경이 나한테서 빼앗아 갔어.”"]], [["“Can you remember what the writing was?", "“무슨 내용이었는지 기억할 수 있겠니?"], ["Do you think you can repeat it to me?”", "나에게 그대로 되풀이해 줄 수 있을 것 같아?”"]], [["“In substance I can, Marian.", "“요지는 할 수 있어, 매리언."], ["It was very short.", "아주 짧았거든."], ["You would have remembered it, word for word.”", "언니라면 한 마디 한 마디 다 기억했을 거야.”"]], [["“Try to tell me what the substance was before we go any further.”", "“더 나아가기 전에 그 요지가 무엇이었는지 말해 보렴.”"]], [["She complied.", "그녀는 그대로 했다."], ["I write the lines down here exactly as she repeated them to me.", "나는 그녀가 나에게 되풀이해 준 그대로 그 글줄을 여기에 적어 둔다."], ["They ran thus—", "그것은 이러했다—"]], [["“I was seen with you, yesterday, by a tall, stout old man, and had to run to save myself.", "“어제 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 노인이 나와 당신이 함께 있는 것을 보았고, 나는 몸을 지키려고 달아나야 했습니다."], ["He was not quick enough on his feet to follow me, and he lost me among the trees.", "그는 발이 느려 나를 따라오지 못했고 나무들 사이에서 나를 놓쳤습니다."], ["I dare not risk coming back here to-day at the same time.", "나는 감히 오늘 같은 시각에 이곳으로 다시 올 위험을 무릅쓰지 못하겠습니다."], ["I write this, and hide it in the sand, at six in the morning, to tell you so.", "그 말씀을 드리려고 아침 여섯 시에 이 글을 써서 모래에 묻어 둡니다."], ["When we speak next of your wicked husband’s Secret we must speak safely, or not at all.", "당신의 사악한 남편의 비밀을 다음번에 이야기할 때는 안전하게 이야기해야 하며, 그러지 못한다면 아예 이야기하지 말아야 합니다."], ["Try to have patience.", "부디 참고 기다려 주십시오."], ["I promise you shall see me again and that soon.—A.", "다시, 그것도 곧 나를 만나게 되리라고 약속드립니다.—A."], ["C.”", "C.”"]], [["The reference to the “tall, stout old man” (the terms of which Laura was certain that she had repeated to me correctly) left no doubt as to who the intruder had been.", "“키가 크고 뚱뚱한 노인”이라는 대목은(로라는 그 표현을 나에게 틀림없이 그대로 옮겼다고 확신했다) 침입자가 누구였는지 의심할 여지를 남기지 않았다."], ["I called to mind that I had told Sir Percival, in the Count’s presence the day before, that Laura had gone to the boat-house to look for her brooch.", "나는 전날 백작이 있는 자리에서 퍼시벌 경에게 로라가 브로치를 찾으러 보트 창고에 갔다고 말했던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["In all probability he had followed her there, in his officious way, to relieve her mind about the matter of the signature, immediately after he had mentioned the change in Sir Percival’s plans to me in the drawing-room.", "십중팔구 그는 응접실에서 나에게 퍼시벌 경의 계획이 바뀌었다는 말을 전한 직후, 서명 문제로 그녀의 마음을 풀어 주겠다며 특유의 참견하는 방식으로 그녀를 뒤따라갔을 것이다."], ["In this case he could only have got to the neighbourhood of the boat-house at the very moment when Anne Catherick discovered him.", "그렇다면 그는 앤 캐서릭이 그를 발견한 바로 그 순간에야 보트 창고 근처에 이를 수 있었을 것이다."], ["The suspiciously hurried manner in which she parted from Laura had no doubt prompted his useless attempt to follow her.", "그 여자가 수상하리만치 서둘러 로라와 헤어진 것이 그로 하여금 헛되이 뒤쫓게 만들었을 터였다."], ["Of the conversation which had previously taken place between them he could have heard nothing.", "그 전에 두 사람 사이에 오간 대화는 그가 아무것도 듣지 못했을 것이다."], ["The distance between the house and the lake, and the time at which he left me in the drawing-room, as compared with the time at which Laura and Anne Catherick had been speaking together, proved that fact to us at any rate, beyond a doubt.", "저택과 호수 사이의 거리, 그리고 그가 응접실에서 나를 떠난 시각을 로라와 앤 캐서릭이 이야기를 나눈 시각과 견주어 보면, 적어도 우리에게는 그 사실이 의심할 여지 없이 증명되었다."]], [["Having arrived at something like a conclusion so far, my next great interest was to know what discoveries Sir Percival had made after Count Fosco had given him his information.", "여기까지 어느 정도 결론 비슷한 것에 이르자, 다음으로 내 큰 관심사는 포스코 백작이 알려 준 뒤에 퍼시벌 경이 무엇을 알아냈는가 하는 것이었다."]], [["“How came you to lose possession of the letter?” I asked.", "“어쩌다 그 편지를 빼앗기게 되었니?”"], ["“What did you do with it when you found it in the sand?”", "내가 물었다. “모래에서 그걸 찾아낸 뒤에 어떻게 했어?”"]], [["“After reading it once through,”", "“한 번 죽 읽고 나서,”"], ["she replied, “I took it into the boat-house with me to sit down and look over it a second time.", "그녀가 대답했다. “앉아서 다시 한 번 훑어보려고 보트 창고 안으로 가지고 들어갔어."], ["While I was reading a shadow fell across the paper.", "읽고 있는데 종이 위로 그림자가 드리웠어."], ["I looked up, and saw Sir Percival standing in the doorway watching me.”", "올려다보니 퍼시벌 경이 문간에 서서 나를 지켜보고 있었어.”"]], [["“Did you try to hide the letter?”", "“편지를 감추려고 해 봤니?”"]], [["“I tried, but he stopped me.", "“그러려고 했지만 그이가 막았어."], ["‘You needn’t trouble to hide that,’ he said.", "‘그걸 감추려고 애쓸 것 없소.’ 그이가 말했어."], ["‘I happen to have read it.’ I could only look at him helplessly—I could say nothing.", "‘내가 이미 읽었으니까.’ 나는 어쩔 줄 몰라 그이를 바라볼 수밖에 없었어—아무 말도 못 했어."], ["‘You understand?’ he went on; ‘I have read it.", "‘알겠소?’ 그이가 말을 이었어. ‘내가 읽었소."], ["I dug it up out of the sand two hours since, and buried it again, and wrote the word above it again, and left it ready to your hands.", "두 시간 전에 모래에서 파내어 읽고, 다시 묻고, 그 위에 그 낱말을 다시 써서, 당신 손에 들어가도록 그대로 두었소."], ["You can’t lie yourself out of the scrape now.", "이제 거짓말로 이 곤경을 빠져나갈 수는 없소."], ["You saw Anne Catherick in secret yesterday, and you have got her letter in your hand at this moment.", "당신은 어제 몰래 앤 캐서릭을 만났고, 지금 이 순간 그 여자의 편지를 손에 쥐고 있소."], ["I have not caught HER yet, but I have caught YOU.", "아직 그 여자는 잡지 못했지만 당신은 잡았소."], ["Give me the letter.’ He stepped close up to me—I was alone with him, Marian—what could I do?—I gave him the letter.”", "편지를 이리 주시오.’ 그이가 나에게 바싹 다가섰어—나는 그이와 단둘이었어, 매리언—내가 어쩌겠어?—편지를 주고 말았어.”"]], [["“What did he say when you gave it to him?”", "“네가 그걸 건네주자 그가 뭐라고 하던?”"]], [["“At first he said nothing.", "“처음에는 아무 말도 안 했어."], ["He took me by the arm, and led me out of the boat-house, and looked about him on all sides, as if he was afraid of our being seen or heard.", "내 팔을 잡고 보트 창고 밖으로 데리고 나가더니, 우리가 남의 눈에 띄거나 소리가 들릴까 두려운 사람처럼 사방을 둘러보았어."], ["Then he clasped his hand fast round my arm, and whispered to me, ‘What did Anne Catherick say to you yesterday?", "그러고는 내 팔을 손으로 꽉 움켜쥐고 나에게 속삭였어. ‘어제 앤 캐서릭이 당신한테 뭐라고 했소?"], ["I insist on hearing every word, from first to last.’”", "처음부터 끝까지 한마디도 빼놓지 말고 들어야겠소.’”"]], [["“Did you tell him?”", "“말했니?”"]], [["“I was alone with him, Marian—his cruel hand was bruising my arm—what could I do?”", "“그이와 단둘이었어, 매리언—그이의 잔인한 손이 내 팔에 멍이 들도록 죄고 있었어—내가 어쩌겠어?”"]], [["“Is the mark on your arm still?", "“팔에 그 자국이 아직 있니?"], ["Let me see it.”", "좀 보자.”"]], [["“Why do you want to see it?”", "“그건 왜 보려고 해?”"]], [["“I want to see it, Laura, because our endurance must end, and our resistance must begin to-day.", "“보고 싶은 건, 로라, 우리의 인내가 끝나야 하고 우리의 저항이 오늘부터 시작되어야 하기 때문이야."], ["That mark is a weapon to strike him with.", "그 자국은 그를 칠 무기란다."], ["Let me see it now—I may have to swear to it at some future time.”", "지금 보여 다오—언젠가 그것을 두고 선서해야 할지도 모르니까.”"]], [["“Oh, Marian, don’t look so—don’t talk so!", "“아, 매리언, 그런 얼굴 하지 마—그런 말 하지 마!"], ["It doesn’t hurt me now!”", "이제 아프지도 않아!”"]], [["“Let me see it!”", "“보여 다오!”"]], [["She showed me the marks.", "그녀는 나에게 자국을 보여 주었다."], ["I was past grieving over them, past crying over them, past shuddering over them.", "나는 그것을 슬퍼할 단계도, 눈물 흘릴 단계도, 몸서리칠 단계도 이미 지나 있었다."], ["They say we are either better than men, or worse.", "사람들은 우리 여자가 남자보다 낫거나 아니면 더 나쁘다고들 한다."], ["If the temptation that has fallen in some women’s way, and made them worse, had fallen in mine at that moment—Thank God!", "어떤 여자들에게 닥쳐 그들을 더 나쁘게 만든 그 유혹이 그 순간 나에게 닥쳤더라면—하느님, 감사합니다!"], ["my face betrayed nothing that his wife could read.", "내 얼굴은 그의 아내가 읽어 낼 만한 것을 아무것도 드러내지 않았다."], ["The gentle, innocent, affectionate creature thought I was frightened for her and sorry for her, and thought no more.", "그 온순하고 순진하고 정 많은 것은 내가 자기를 걱정하고 가엾어하는 줄로만 알았고, 그 이상은 생각하지 않았다."]], [["“Don’t think too seriously of it, Marian,”", "“너무 심각하게 여기지 마, 매리언.”"], ["she said simply, as she pulled her sleeve down again.", "그녀는 소매를 도로 내리며 담담하게 말했다."], ["“It doesn’t hurt me now.”", "“이제 아프지도 않은걸.”"]], [["“I will try to think quietly of it, my love, for your sake.—Well!", "“너를 위해서 침착하게 생각해 보도록 애쓰마, 얘야.—그래!"], ["well!", "그래!"], ["And you told him all that Anne Catherick had said to you—all that you told me?”", "그러고 나서 앤 캐서릭이 너에게 한 말을 전부 그에게 이야기했다고—나에게 이야기한 것을 전부?”"]], [["“Yes, all.", "“응, 전부."], ["He insisted on it—I was alone with him—I could conceal nothing.”", "그이가 우겨 댔고—나는 그이와 단둘이었고—아무것도 숨길 수가 없었어.”"]], [["“Did he say anything when you had done?”", "“네 말이 끝나자 그가 무슨 말을 하던?”"]], [["“He looked at me, and laughed to himself in a mocking, bitter way.", "“나를 바라보며 비웃듯 씁쓸하게 혼자 웃었어."], ["‘I mean to have the rest out of you,’ he said, ‘do you hear?—the rest.’ I declared to him solemnly that I had told him everything I knew.", "‘나머지도 당신한테서 캐낼 작정이오.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘알겠소?—나머지 말이오.’ 나는 아는 것은 다 말했다고 엄숙하게 밝혔어."], ["‘Not you,’ he answered, ‘you know more than you choose to tell.", "‘천만에.’ 그이가 대답했어. ‘당신은 말하려는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있소."], ["Won’t you tell it?", "말하지 않겠다고?"], ["You shall!", "말하게 될 거요!"], ["I’ll wring it out of you at home if I can’t wring it out of you here.’ He led me away by a strange path through the plantation—a path where there was no hope of our meeting you—and he spoke no more till we came within sight of the house.", "여기서 짜낼 수 없다면 집에 가서라도 짜내고야 말겠소.’ 그이는 잡목림을 가로지르는 이상한 길로—언니를 만날 가망이 전혀 없는 길로—나를 끌고 갔고, 저택이 보이는 데 이를 때까지 더는 말이 없었어."], ["Then he stopped again, and said, ‘Will you take a second chance, if I give it to you?", "그러다 다시 멈춰 서서 말했어. ‘두 번째 기회를 주면 받겠소?"], ["Will you think better of it, and tell me the rest?’ I could only repeat the same words I had spoken before.", "마음을 고쳐먹고 나머지를 말하겠소?’ 나는 앞서 한 말을 되풀이하는 수밖에 없었어."], ["He cursed my obstinacy, and went on, and took me with him to the house.", "그이는 내 고집에 욕설을 퍼붓고는, 계속 걸어서 나를 데리고 집으로 들어갔어."], ["‘You can’t deceive me,’ he said, ‘you know more than you choose to tell.", "‘나를 속일 수는 없소.’ 그이가 말했어. ‘당신은 말하려는 것보다 더 많이 알고 있소."], ["I’ll have your secret out of you, and I’ll have it out of that sister of yours as well.", "당신한테서 비밀을 캐내고, 당신의 그 언니한테서도 캐내고야 말겠소."], ["There shall be no more plotting and whispering between you.", "당신들 사이에 더는 모의도 속닥거림도 없을 거요."], ["Neither you nor she shall see each other again till you have confessed the truth.", "진실을 털어놓기 전에는 당신도 그 여자도 서로 얼굴을 못 볼 거요."], ["I’ll have you watched morning, noon, and night, till you confess the truth.’ He was deaf to everything I could say.", "진실을 털어놓을 때까지 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 당신을 감시하게 하겠소.’ 내가 무슨 말을 해도 그이는 귀머거리였어."], ["He took me straight upstairs into my own room.", "그이는 나를 곧장 위층 내 방으로 데려갔어."], ["Fanny was sitting there, doing some work for me, and he instantly ordered her out.", "패니가 거기 앉아 내 일감을 손질하고 있었는데, 그이는 대뜸 그 애더러 나가라고 명령했어."], ["‘I’ll take good care YOU’RE not mixed up in the conspiracy,’ he said.", "‘네가 이 음모에 끼어들지 못하게 단단히 손을 써 두겠다.’ 그이가 말했어."], ["‘You shall leave this house to-day.", "‘너는 오늘 이 집을 떠나라."], ["If your mistress wants a maid, she shall have one of my choosing.’ He pushed me into the room, and locked the door on me.", "네 여주인에게 몸종이 필요하다면 내가 고른 사람을 붙여 주지.’ 그이는 나를 방 안으로 밀어 넣고 문을 잠갔어."], ["He set that senseless woman to watch me outside, Marian!", "그 멍청한 여자를 밖에 세워 나를 감시하게 했어, 매리언!"], ["He looked and spoke like a madman.", "그이의 낯빛도 말투도 미친 사람 같았어."], ["You may hardly understand it—he did indeed.”", "언니는 잘 믿기지 않겠지만—정말 그랬어.”"]], [["“I do understand it, Laura.", "“나는 믿는단다, 로라."], ["He is mad—mad with the terrors of a guilty conscience.", "그 사람은 미쳤어—죄지은 양심의 공포로 미친 거야."], ["Every word you have said makes me positively certain that when Anne Catherick left you yesterday you were on the eve of discovering a secret which might have been your vile husband’s ruin, and he thinks you HAVE discovered it.", "네가 한 말을 하나하나 들으니, 어제 앤 캐서릭이 너를 떠났을 때 너는 그 비열한 남편을 파멸시킬 수도 있는 비밀을 알아내기 직전이었고, 그 사람은 네가 그것을 이미 알아냈다고 여기고 있다는 확신이 굳어질 뿐이야."], ["Nothing you can say or do will quiet that guilty distrust, and convince his false nature of your truth.", "네가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 해도 그 죄 많은 의심을 잠재우거나, 그 거짓된 천성에게 네가 정직하다는 것을 납득시키지는 못해."], ["I don’t say this, my love, to alarm you.", "얘야, 이런 말을 하는 건 너를 겁주려는 게 아니야."], ["I say it to open your eyes to your position, and to convince you of the urgent necessity of letting me act, as I best can, for your protection while the chance is our own.", "네가 처한 처지에 눈뜨게 하고, 기회가 우리 것일 동안 내가 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 너를 지키도록 나서야 한다는 절박한 필요를 납득시키려는 거야."], ["Count Fosco’s interference has secured me access to you to-day, but he may withdraw that interference to-morrow.", "포스코 백작이 끼어든 덕분에 오늘은 내가 너에게 올 수 있었지만, 그 사람은 내일이면 그 참견을 거두어 버릴지도 몰라."], ["Sir Percival has already dismissed Fanny because she is a quick-witted girl, and devotedly attached to you, and has chosen a woman to take her place who cares nothing for your interests, and whose dull intelligence lowers her to the level of the watch-dog in the yard.", "퍼시벌 경은 패니가 눈치 빠르고 너에게 헌신적이라는 이유로 벌써 내보냈고, 네 이익에는 아무 관심도 없으며 그 아둔한 머리가 마당의 개나 다름없는 수준인 여자를 그 자리에 앉혔어."], ["It is impossible to say what violent measures he may take next, unless we make the most of our opportunities while we have them.”", "기회가 있을 때 최대한 살리지 않는다면 그 사람이 다음에 어떤 난폭한 수를 쓸지 알 수 없어.”"]], [["“What can we do, Marian?", "“우리가 뭘 할 수 있어, 매리언?"], ["Oh, if we could only leave this house, never to see it again!”", "아, 이 집을 떠나 다시는 보지 않을 수만 있다면!”"]], [["“Listen to me, my love, and try to think that you are not quite helpless so long as I am here with you.”", "“내 말을 들으렴, 얘야. 그리고 내가 너와 함께 여기 있는 한 네가 아주 무력한 것은 아니라고 생각하도록 애써 보렴.”"]], [["“I will think so—I do think so.", "“그렇게 생각할게—정말 그렇게 생각해."], ["Don’t altogether forget poor Fanny in thinking of me.", "나를 생각하느라 가엾은 패니를 아주 잊어버리지는 마."], ["She wants help and comfort too.”", "그 애도 도움과 위로가 필요해.”"]], [["“I will not forget her.", "“잊지 않으마."], ["I saw her before I came up here, and I have arranged to communicate with her to-night.", "여기 올라오기 전에 그 아이를 보았고, 오늘 밤에 연락하기로 해 두었단다."], ["Letters are not safe in the post-bag at Blackwater Park, and I shall have two to write to-day, in your interests, which must pass through no hands but Fanny’s.”", "블랙워터 파크의 우편 자루에 넣는 편지는 안전하지 않으니, 오늘 너를 위해 두 통을 써야 하는데 그건 패니 말고 누구의 손도 거쳐서는 안 돼.”"]], [["“What letters?”", "“무슨 편지들인데?”"]], [["“I mean to write first, Laura, to Mr. Gilmore’s partner, who has offered to help us in any fresh emergency.", "“먼저, 로라, 새로 급한 일이 생기면 돕겠다고 한 길모어 씨의 동업자에게 쓰려고 해."], ["Little as I know of the law, I am certain that it can protect a woman from such treatment as that ruffian has inflicted on you to-day.", "나는 법을 아는 게 별로 없지만, 오늘 그 무뢰한이 너에게 가한 것 같은 대우에서 법이 여자를 지켜 줄 수 있다는 것만은 확신해."], ["I will go into no details about Anne Catherick, because I have no certain information to give.", "앤 캐서릭에 대해서는 확실한 정보를 줄 게 없으니 자세히 쓰지 않겠어."], ["But the lawyer shall know of those bruises on your arm, and of the violence offered to you in this room—he shall, before I rest to-night!”", "하지만 네 팔의 그 멍과 이 방에서 너에게 가해진 폭력만은 변호사가 알아야 해—오늘 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 반드시 알게 될 거야!”"]], [["“But think of the exposure, Marian!”", "“하지만 소문이 날 걸 생각해 봐, 매리언!”"]], [["“I am calculating on the exposure.", "“나는 그 소문을 계산에 넣고 있어."], ["Sir Percival has more to dread from it than you have.", "소문이 나면 너보다 퍼시벌 경이 더 두려워할 게 많아."], ["The prospect of an exposure may bring him to terms when nothing else will.”", "다른 무엇으로도 안 될 때 소문이 날지 모른다는 전망이 그를 굽히게 할 수도 있어.”"]], [["I rose as I spoke, but Laura entreated me not to leave her.", "말하면서 나는 일어섰지만, 로라가 자기를 두고 가지 말라고 애원했다."], ["“You will drive him to desperation,”", "“언니는 그이를 자포자기하게 만들 거야.”"], ["she said, “and increase our dangers tenfold.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그러면 우리의 위험은 열 배로 커질 거고.”"]], [["I felt the truth—the disheartening truth—of those words.", "나는 그 말이 진실임을—낙담스러운 진실임을 느꼈다."], ["But I could not bring myself plainly to acknowledge it to her.", "그러나 그것을 그녀에게 대놓고 인정할 수는 없었다."], ["In our dreadful position there was no help and no hope for us but in risking the worst.", "우리의 끔찍한 처지에서는 최악을 무릅쓰는 것 말고 우리에게 도움도 희망도 없었다."], ["I said so in guarded terms.", "나는 조심스러운 말로 그렇게 말했다."], ["She sighed bitterly, but did not contest the matter.", "그녀는 쓰디쓰게 한숨을 쉬었지만 그 문제로 다투지는 않았다."], ["She only asked about the second letter that I had proposed writing.", "다만 내가 쓰겠다고 한 두 번째 편지에 대해 물었다."], ["To whom was it to be addressed?", "그것은 누구에게 보내는 것이냐고."]], [["“To Mr. Fairlie,”", "“페얼리 씨에게.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Your uncle is your nearest male relative, and the head of the family.", "“네 삼촌은 너의 가장 가까운 남자 친척이고 집안의 어른이야."], ["He must and shall interfere.”", "그분이 나서야 하고, 나서게 될 거야.”"]], [["Laura shook her head sorrowfully.", "로라는 서글프게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "“그래, 그래.”"], ["I went on, “your uncle is a weak, selfish, worldly man, I know, but he is not Sir Percival Glyde, and he has no such friend about him as Count Fosco.", "내가 말을 이었다. “네 삼촌이 나약하고 이기적이고 세속적인 사람인 건 나도 알아."], ["I expect nothing from his kindness or his tenderness of feeling towards you or towards me, but he will do anything to pamper his own indolence, and to secure his own quiet.", "하지만 그분은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아니고, 곁에 포스코 백작 같은 친구도 없어. 나는 그분이 너나 나에게 품은 친절이나 다정한 마음 같은 건 조금도 기대하지 않아."], ["Let me only persuade him that his interference at this moment will save him inevitable trouble and wretchedness and responsibility hereafter, and he will bestir himself for his own sake.", "하지만 그분은 자기 게으름을 떠받들고 자기 평온을 지키기 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 사람이야. 지금 나서 주는 것이 앞으로 반드시 닥칠 성가심과 곤경과 책임을 면하게 해 준다고 설득하기만 하면, 그분은 자기를 위해서라도 몸을 움직일 거야."], ["I know how to deal with him, Laura—I have had some practice.”", "나는 그분을 다루는 법을 알아, 로라—몇 번 해 봤으니까.”"]], [["“If you could only prevail on him to let me go back to Limmeridge for a little while and stay there quietly with you, Marian, I could be almost as happy again as I was before I was married!”", "“삼촌을 설득해서 내가 잠시 리머리지로 돌아가 언니와 조용히 지낼 수 있게만 해 주신다면, 매리언, 나는 결혼하기 전만큼이나 다시 행복해질 수 있을 텐데!”"]], [["Those words set me thinking in a new direction.", "그 말은 내 생각을 새로운 방향으로 돌려놓았다."], ["Would it be possible to place Sir Percival between the two alternatives of either exposing himself to the scandal of legal interference on his wife’s behalf, or of allowing her to be quietly separated from him for a time under pretext of a visit to her uncle’s house?", "퍼시벌 경을 두 갈래 선택 앞에 세워 둘 수는 없을까? 하나는 아내를 대신한 법적 개입이 불러올 추문에 스스로를 내맡기는 길이고, 다른 하나는 삼촌 댁을 방문한다는 구실로 아내가 한동안 조용히 그의 곁을 떠나 있도록 허락하는 길이다."], ["And could he, in that case, be reckoned on as likely to accept the last resource?", "그리고 그런 처지에 몰린다면, 그가 뒤엣것을 마지막 방책으로 받아들이리라고 기대해도 좋을까?"], ["It was doubtful—more than doubtful.", "그것은 의심스러웠다—의심스럽다 못한 일이었다."], ["And yet, hopeless as the experiment seemed, surely it was worth trying.", "그럼에도 이 시도가 아무리 가망 없어 보인들, 분명 해 볼 값어치는 있었다."], ["I resolved to try it in sheer despair of knowing what better to do.", "달리 무엇을 해야 좋을지 도무지 알 수 없다는 순전한 절망 속에서, 나는 그것을 해 보기로 결심했다."]], [["“Your uncle shall know the wish you have just expressed,”", "“네가 방금 말한 바람을 삼촌께 알려 드리마.”"], ["I said, “and I will ask the lawyer’s advice on the subject as well.", "내가 말했다. “그리고 그 문제에 대해 변호사의 조언도 구해 보겠어."], ["Good may come of it—and will come of it, I hope.”", "좋은 결과가 나올 수도 있어—아니, 나오리라고 나는 믿어.”"]], [["Saying that I rose again, and again Laura tried to make me resume my seat.", "그렇게 말하면서 나는 다시 일어섰고, 로라는 또다시 나를 자리에 앉히려 했다."]], [["“Don’t leave me,”", "“가지 마.”"], ["she said uneasily.", "로라가 불안하게 말했다."], ["“My desk is on that table.", "“내 문갑이 저 탁자 위에 있어."], ["You can write here.”", "여기서 쓰면 되잖아.”"]], [["It tried me to the quick to refuse her, even in her own interests.", "그 애의 청을, 그것도 그 애 자신을 위한 일로 물리치는 것은 뼈아픈 노릇이었다."], ["But we had been too long shut up alone together already.", "그러나 우리는 이미 너무 오래 단둘이 틀어박혀 있었다."], ["Our chance of seeing each other again might entirely depend on our not exciting any fresh suspicions.", "다시 서로를 만날 수 있느냐는 새로운 의심을 사지 않는 데에 온전히 달려 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["It was full time to show myself, quietly and unconcernedly, among the wretches who were at that very moment, perhaps, thinking of us and talking of us downstairs.", "어쩌면 바로 그 순간 아래층에서 우리를 생각하고 우리 이야기를 하고 있을 그 몹쓸 자들 틈에, 조용하고 무심한 낯으로 모습을 드러낼 때가 무르익은 것이다."], ["I explained the miserable necessity to Laura, and prevailed on her to recognise it as I did.", "나는 그 비참한 필요를 로라에게 설명했고, 나와 같이 그것을 받아들이도록 그 애를 설득했다."]], [["“I will come back again, love, in an hour or less,”", "“한 시간이나 그보다 짧은 사이에 다시 올게, 얘야.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“The worst is over for to-day.", "“오늘 치 최악은 지나갔어."], ["Keep yourself quiet and fear nothing.”", "가만히 있으면서 아무것도 무서워하지 마.”"]], [["“Is the key in the door, Marian?", "“열쇠가 문에 꽂혀 있니, 매리언?"], ["Can I lock it on the inside?”", "안에서 잠글 수 있을까?”"]], [["“Yes, here is the key.", "“그래, 여기 열쇠가 있어."], ["Lock the door, and open it to nobody until I come upstairs again.”", "문을 잠그고, 내가 다시 올라올 때까지 누구에게도 열어 주지 마.”"]], [["I kissed her and left her.", "나는 그 애에게 입을 맞추고 방을 나왔다."], ["It was a relief to me as I walked away to hear the key turned in the lock, and to know that the door was at her own command.", "걸어 나오면서 자물쇠 안에서 열쇠가 돌아가는 소리를 듣고, 그 문이 그 애 뜻대로 되어 있음을 아는 것은 내게 하나의 위안이었다."]], [["# VIII", "# VIII"]], [["June 19th.—I had only got as far as the top of the stairs when the locking of Laura’s door suggested to me the precaution of also locking my own door, and keeping the key safely about me while I was out of the room.", "6월 19일.—계단 꼭대기에 이르렀을 뿐인데 로라의 방문이 잠기는 소리가 들려, 나도 내 방문을 잠그고 방을 비우는 동안 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있어야겠다는 조심성이 떠올랐다."], ["My journal was already secured with other papers in the table drawer, but my writing materials were left out.", "일기는 이미 다른 서류들과 함께 탁자 서랍에 넣어 잠가 두었지만, 필기도구는 그대로 밖에 나와 있었다."], ["These included a seal bearing the common device of two doves drinking out of the same cup, and some sheets of blotting-paper, which had the impression on them of the closing lines of my writing in these pages traced during the past night.", "그중에는 같은 잔에서 물을 마시는 두 마리 비둘기라는 흔한 도안이 새겨진 봉인 도장과, 지난밤 이 지면에 적은 마지막 줄들이 그대로 찍혀 있는 압지 몇 장이 있었다."], ["Distorted by the suspicion which had now become a part of myself, even such trifles as these looked too dangerous to be trusted without a guard—even the locked table drawer seemed to be not sufficiently protected in my absence until the means of access to it had been carefully secured as well.", "이제 내 일부가 되어 버린 의심에 뒤틀려 보인 탓인지, 이런 사소한 물건들조차 지키는 사람 없이 두기에는 너무 위험해 보였다. 잠긴 탁자 서랍마저도, 그리로 들어가는 길까지 조심스레 막아 두기 전에는 내가 없는 동안 충분히 지켜지지 못하는 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["I found no appearance of any one having entered the room while I had been talking with Laura.", "로라와 이야기하는 동안 누군가 방에 들어왔던 흔적은 보이지 않았다."], ["My writing materials (which I had given the servant instructions never to meddle with) were scattered over the table much as usual.", "(하인들에게 절대 손대지 말라고 일러 둔) 필기도구들은 여느 때와 다름없이 탁자 위에 흩어져 있었다."], ["The only circumstance in connection with them that at all struck me was that the seal lay tidily in the tray with the pencils and the wax.", "그와 관련해 조금이라도 마음에 걸린 단 한 가지는, 봉인 도장이 연필과 봉랍이 놓인 쟁반 안에 얌전히 놓여 있다는 점이었다."], ["It was not in my careless habits (I am sorry to say) to put it there, neither did I remember putting it there.", "부끄럽지만 그것을 거기에 두는 것은 덜렁대는 내 습관이 아니었고, 거기 둔 기억도 없었다."], ["But as I could not call to mind, on the other hand, where else I had thrown it down, and as I was also doubtful whether I might not for once have laid it mechanically in the right place, I abstained from adding to the perplexity with which the day’s events had filled my mind by troubling it afresh about a trifle.", "그러나 다른 한편으로 달리 어디에 던져 두었는지도 떠오르지 않았고, 어쩌다 한 번쯤 무심코 제자리에 놓았을 가능성도 없지 않았기에, 하루의 사건들로 이미 어지러운 머리를 사소한 일로 새삼 괴롭히지는 않기로 했다."], ["I locked the door, put the key in my pocket, and went downstairs.", "나는 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 주머니에 넣은 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["Madame Fosco was alone in the hall looking at the weather-glass.", "포스코 백작부인이 현관 홀에 혼자 서서 청우계를 들여다보고 있었다."]], [["“Still falling,”", "“여전히 내려가는군요.”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“I am afraid we must expect more rain.”", "“비가 더 오리라고 각오해야 할까 봐요.”"]], [["Her face was composed again to its customary expression and its customary colour.", "그 얼굴은 다시 여느 때의 표정과 여느 때의 혈색을 되찾고 있었다."], ["But the hand with which she pointed to the dial of the weather-glass still trembled.", "그러나 청우계의 눈금을 가리키는 손은 여전히 떨리고 있었다."]], [["Could she have told her husband already that she had overheard Laura reviling him, in my company, as a “spy?”", "로라가 그를 “첩자”라고 헐뜯는 말을, 그것도 내 앞에서 한 것을 엿들었다고 부인은 벌써 남편에게 말해 버렸을까?"], ["My strong suspicion that she must have told him, my irresistible dread (all the more overpowering from its very vagueness) of the consequences which might follow, my fixed conviction, derived from various little self-betrayals which women notice in each other, that Madame Fosco, in spite of her well-assumed external civility, had not forgiven her niece for innocently standing between her and the legacy of ten thousand pounds—all rushed upon my mind together, all impelled me to speak in the vain hope of using my own influence and my own powers of persuasion for the atonement of Laura’s offence.", "틀림없이 말했으리라는 강한 의심, 뒤따를 결과에 대한, 막연하기에 더욱 짓누르는 억누를 수 없는 두려움, 그리고 여자들이 서로에게서 알아채는 온갖 사소한 자기 폭로에서 얻어 낸 확신, 곧 포스코 백작부인이 겉으로는 예의를 잘 꾸며 보이지만 알지도 못한 채 자기와 만 파운드의 유산 사이를 가로막은 조카딸을 아직 용서하지 않았다는 확신, 이 모든 것이 한꺼번에 밀려들어, 내 영향력과 설득의 힘으로 로라의 잘못을 대신 속죄해 보겠다는 헛된 희망에서 입을 열게 만들었다."]], [["“May I trust to your kindness to excuse me, Madame Fosco, if I venture to speak to you on an exceedingly painful subject?”", "“포스코 백작부인, 대단히 괴로운 이야기를 감히 꺼내더라도 너그러이 용서해 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["She crossed her hands in front of her and bowed her head solemnly, without uttering a word, and without taking her eyes off mine for a moment.", "부인은 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 한마디 말도 없이, 한순간도 내 눈에서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 엄숙하게 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“When you were so good as to bring me back my handkerchief,”", "“친절하게도 제 손수건을 가져다주셨을 때 말입니다.” 나는 말을 이었다."], ["I went on, “I am very, very much afraid you must have accidentally heard Laura say something which I am unwilling to repeat, and which I will not attempt to defend.", "“로라가 하는 말을 우연히 들으셨을까 봐 몹시, 몹시 걱정스럽습니다. 저는 그 말을 되풀이하고 싶지도 않고 변호할 생각도 없습니다."], ["I will only venture to hope that you have not thought it of sufficient importance to be mentioned to the Count?”", "다만 부인께서 그 말을 백작께 전할 만큼 중요한 일로 여기지는 않으셨기를 감히 바랄 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“I think it of no importance whatever,”", "“전혀 중요하지 않다고 생각합니다.”"], ["said Madame Fosco sharply and suddenly.", "포스코 백작부인이 날카롭고 갑작스럽게 말했다."], ["“But,”", "“하지만,”"], ["she added, resuming her icy manner in a moment, “I have no secrets from my husband even in trifles.", "그러고는 곧 얼음 같은 태도를 되찾으며 덧붙였다. “저는 아무리 사소한 일이라도 남편에게 숨기는 것이 없습니다."], ["When he noticed just now that I looked distressed, it was my painful duty to tell him why I was distressed, and I frankly acknowledge to you, Miss Halcombe, that I HAVE told him.”", "아까 제가 괴로워 보인다고 남편이 눈치챘을 때, 왜 괴로운지 말하는 것이 제 괴로운 의무였지요. 그리고 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양, 저는 남편에게 말했습니다.”"]], [["I was prepared to hear it, and yet she turned me cold all over when she said those words.", "나는 그런 대답을 들을 각오가 되어 있었는데도, 그 말에 온몸이 서늘해졌다."]], [["“Let me earnestly entreat you, Madame Fosco—let me earnestly entreat the Count—to make some allowances for the sad position in which my sister is placed.", "“간절히 청합니다, 포스코 백작부인. 백작께도 간절히 청합니다."], ["She spoke while she was smarting under the insult and injustice inflicted on her by her husband, and she was not herself when she said those rash words.", "제 동생이 놓인 딱한 처지를 얼마간 헤아려 주십시오. 동생은 남편에게서 받은 모욕과 부당한 처사에 쓰라려하던 중에 그런 말을 한 것이고, 그 경솔한 말을 할 때는 제정신이 아니었습니다."], ["May I hope that they will be considerately and generously forgiven?”", "너그럽고 관대하게 용서해 주시기를 바라도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Most assuredly,”", "“물론이고말고요.”"], ["said the Count’s quiet voice behind me.", "등 뒤에서 백작의 조용한 목소리가 들렸다."], ["He had stolen on us with his noiseless tread and his book in his hand from the library.", "그는 서재에서 책을 손에 든 채 소리 없는 걸음으로 우리에게 다가와 있었다."]], [["“When Lady Glyde said those hasty words,”", "“글라이드 부인이 그 성급한 말을 하셨을 때,” 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he went on, “she did me an injustice which I lament—and forgive.", "“저를 부당하게 대하신 것이지요. 유감스럽지만, 저는 용서합니다."], ["Let us never return to the subject, Miss Halcombe; let us all comfortably combine to forget it from this moment.”", "이 이야기는 다시 꺼내지 맙시다, 홀콤 양. 우리 모두 사이좋게 힘을 모아 이 순간부터 잊어버리기로 하지요.”"]], [["“You are very kind,”", "“참으로 친절하십니다.”"], ["I said, “you relieve me inexpressibly.”", "내가 말했다. “말로 다 못 할 만큼 마음이 놓입니다.”"]], [["I tried to continue, but his eyes were on me; his deadly smile that hides everything was set, hard, and unwavering on his broad, smooth face.", "더 말을 이으려 했지만 그의 눈이 나를 향해 있었다. 모든 것을 감추는 그 치명적인 미소가 넓고 매끈한 얼굴 위에 굳은 채 흔들림 없이 걸려 있었다."], ["My distrust of his unfathomable falseness, my sense of my own degradation in stooping to conciliate his wife and himself, so disturbed and confused me, that the next words failed on my lips, and I stood there in silence.", "헤아릴 수 없는 그의 거짓됨에 대한 불신과, 그와 그 아내에게 몸을 굽혀 비위를 맞추고 있는 나 자신에 대한 굴욕감이 나를 몹시 흔들고 혼란스럽게 해서, 다음 말이 입술에서 사라져 버렸고 나는 말없이 그 자리에 서 있었다."]], [["“I beg you on my knees to say no more, Miss Halcombe—I am truly shocked that you should have thought it necessary to say so much.”", "“무릎 꿇고 청하오니 더는 말씀 마십시오, 홀콤 양. 이렇게까지 말씀하셔야 한다고 여기셨다니 참으로 마음이 아픕니다.”"], ["With that polite speech he took my hand—oh, how I despise myself!", "그 정중한 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 잡았다. 아, 나는 내 자신이 얼마나 경멸스러운가!"], ["oh, how little comfort there is even in knowing that I submitted to it for Laura’s sake!—he took my hand and put it to his poisonous lips.", "아, 로라를 위해 참았다는 것을 안들 위안이 얼마나 하찮은가! 그는 내 손을 잡아 그 독기 어린 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["Never did I know all my horror of him till then.", "그때까지 나는 그에 대한 내 공포의 크기를 온전히 알지 못했다."], ["That innocent familiarity turned my blood as if it had been the vilest insult that a man could offer me.", "그 악의 없는 친밀함이, 남자가 여자에게 던질 수 있는 가장 저열한 모욕이기라도 한 듯 내 피를 뒤집어 놓았다. 그런데도 나는 역겨움을 감추었다."], ["Yet I hid my disgust from him—I tried to smile—I, who once mercilessly despised deceit in other women, was as false as the worst of them, as false as the Judas whose lips had touched my hand.", "웃어 보이려 애썼다. 한때 다른 여자들의 위선을 가차 없이 경멸하던 내가, 그중 가장 나쁜 여자만큼이나, 내 손에 입술을 댄 저 유다만큼이나 거짓되었다."]], [["I could not have maintained my degrading self-control—it is all that redeems me in my own estimation to know that I could not—if he had still continued to keep his eyes on my face.", "그가 계속 내 얼굴에 눈을 두고 있었다면 나는 그 굴욕적인 자제를 끝까지 지키지 못했을 것이다. 지키지 못했으리라는 것을 아는 일만이 내 눈에 비친 나 자신을 그나마 구해 준다."], ["His wife’s tigerish jealousy came to my rescue and forced his attention away from me the moment he possessed himself of my hand.", "그가 내 손을 붙잡은 순간, 아내의 호랑이 같은 질투가 나를 구하러 와서 그의 주의를 내게서 억지로 떼어 놓았다."], ["Her cold blue eyes caught light, her dull white cheeks flushed into bright colour, she looked years younger than her age in an instant.", "차가운 푸른 눈에 불이 붙고, 핏기 없이 희던 뺨이 발갛게 달아올라, 그 여자는 순식간에 제 나이보다 몇 살은 젊어 보였다."]], [["“Count!”", "“백작!”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“Your foreign forms of politeness are not understood by Englishwomen.”", "“당신의 외국식 예법은 영국 여자들에게는 통하지 않아요.”"]], [["“Pardon me, my angel!", "“미안하오, 나의 천사여!"], ["The best and dearest Englishwoman in the world understands them.”", "세상에서 제일 훌륭하고 제일 사랑스러운 영국 여인은 그 예법을 알아준다오.”"], ["With those words he dropped my hand and quietly raised his wife’s hand to his lips in place of it.", "그 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 놓고, 그 대신 아내의 손을 조용히 들어 입술로 가져갔다."]], [["I ran back up the stairs to take refuge in my own room.", "나는 계단을 뛰어올라 내 방으로 피신했다."], ["If there had been time to think, my thoughts, when I was alone again, would have caused me bitter suffering.", "생각할 겨를이 있었다면, 혼자가 된 순간 그 생각들은 나를 몹시 괴롭혔을 것이다."], ["But there was no time to think.", "그러나 생각할 겨를이 없었다."], ["Happily for the preservation of my calmness and my courage there was time for nothing but action.", "내 침착함과 용기를 지키기에는 다행스럽게도, 행동할 겨를밖에 없었다."]], [["The letters to the lawyer and to Mr. Fairlie were still to be written, and I sat down at once without a moment’s hesitation to devote myself to them.", "변호사와 페얼리 씨에게 보낼 편지가 아직 남아 있었고, 나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 곧바로 앉아 그 일에 매달렸다."]], [["There was no multitude of resources to perplex me—there was absolutely no one to depend on, in the first instance, but myself.", "나를 망설이게 할 만큼 방도가 많은 것도 아니었다. 우선은 나 자신 말고 기댈 사람이 전혀 없었다."], ["Sir Percival had neither friends nor relatives in the neighbourhood whose intercession I could attempt to employ.", "퍼시벌 경에게는 중재를 부탁해 볼 만한 친구도 친척도 이 근방에 없었다."], ["He was on the coldest terms—in some cases on the worst terms—with the families of his own rank and station who lived near him.", "그는 가까이 사는 같은 신분과 지위의 집안들과 더없이 서먹한 사이였고, 몇몇과는 아주 나쁜 사이였다."], ["We two women had neither father nor brother to come to the house and take our parts.", "우리 두 여자에게는 이 집에 와서 우리 편을 들어 줄 아버지도 오라비도 없었다."], ["There was no choice but to write those two doubtful letters, or to put Laura in the wrong and myself in the wrong, and to make all peaceable negotiation in the future impossible by secretly escaping from Blackwater Park.", "그 미덥지 못한 편지 두 통을 쓰든지, 아니면 블랙워터 파크에서 몰래 달아나 로라와 나를 잘못한 쪽으로 만들고 앞으로의 평화로운 교섭을 모조리 불가능하게 만들든지, 둘 중 하나였다."], ["Nothing but the most imminent personal peril could justify our taking that second course.", "두 번째 길을 택하는 것은 신변에 가장 절박한 위험이 닥쳤을 때에만 정당화될 수 있었다."], ["The letters must be tried first, and I wrote them.", "먼저 편지를 시험해 보아야 했고, 그래서 나는 편지를 썼다."]], [["I said nothing to the lawyer about Anne Catherick, because (as I had already hinted to Laura) that topic was connected with a mystery which we could not yet explain, and which it would therefore be useless to write about to a professional man.", "변호사에게는 앤 캐서릭에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 이미 로라에게 넌지시 말했듯이 그 문제는 우리가 아직 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼와 얽혀 있어서, 직업인에게 편지로 쓴들 소용이 없을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["I left my correspondent to attribute Sir Percival’s disgraceful conduct, if he pleased, to fresh disputes about money matters, and simply consulted him on the possibility of taking legal proceedings for Laura’s protection in the event of her husband’s refusal to allow her to leave Blackwater Park for a time and return with me to Limmeridge.", "퍼시벌 경의 수치스러운 행동에 대해서는, 원한다면 돈 문제로 다시 다툰 탓으로 여기도록 상대에게 맡겨 두고, 다만 남편이 로라가 한동안 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 나와 함께 리머리지로 돌아가는 것을 허락하지 않을 경우 로라를 보호하기 위한 법적 절차를 밟을 수 있는지만 상의했다."], ["I referred him to Mr. Fairlie for the details of this last arrangement—I assured him that I wrote with Laura’s authority—and I ended by entreating him to act in her name to the utmost extent of his power and with the least possible loss of time.", "이 마지막 조처의 자세한 내용은 페얼리 씨에게 물어보라고 일러두었고, 로라의 위임을 받아 쓰는 것임을 분명히 밝혔으며, 힘닿는 데까지 되도록 시간을 허비하지 말고 로라의 이름으로 움직여 달라고 간청하며 끝맺었다."]], [["The letter to Mr. Fairlie occupied me next.", "다음은 페얼리 씨에게 보낼 편지였다."], ["I appealed to him on the terms which I had mentioned to Laura as the most likely to make him bestir himself; I enclosed a copy of my letter to the lawyer to show him how serious the case was, and I represented our removal to Limmeridge as the only compromise which would prevent the danger and distress of Laura’s present position from inevitably affecting her uncle as well as herself at no very distant time.", "나는 그를 움직이게 하기에 가장 그럴듯하다고 로라에게 말했던 그 논리로 호소했다. 사태가 얼마나 심각한지 보여 주려고 변호사에게 보낸 편지의 사본을 동봉했고, 리머리지로 옮겨 가는 것이야말로 로라의 현재 처지가 낳는 위험과 고통이 머지않아 조카딸뿐 아니라 삼촌에게까지 미치는 것을 막아 줄 유일한 절충안이라고 설명했다."]], [["When I had done, and had sealed and directed the two envelopes, I went back with the letters to Laura’s room, to show her that they were written.", "다 쓰고 봉투 두 장을 봉하고 수신인을 적은 뒤, 편지를 들고 로라의 방으로 돌아가 편지를 다 썼음을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Has anybody disturbed you?”", "“누가 방해하지는 않았니?”"], ["I asked, when she opened the door to me.", "로라가 문을 열어 주었을 때 내가 물었다."]], [["“Nobody has knocked,”", "“문을 두드린 사람은 없었어.”"], ["she replied.", "로라가 대답했다."], ["“But I heard some one in the outer room.”", "“그런데 바깥방에 누가 있는 소리가 들렸어.”"]], [["“Was it a man or a woman?”", "“남자였니, 여자였니?”"]], [["“A woman.", "“여자였어."], ["I heard the rustling of her gown.”", "옷자락 스치는 소리가 들렸거든.”"]], [["“A rustling like silk?”", "“비단 스치는 소리 같던?”"]], [["“Yes, like silk.”", "“응, 비단 같았어.”"]], [["Madame Fosco had evidently been watching outside.", "포스코 백작부인이 밖에서 지키고 있었던 게 분명했다."], ["The mischief she might do by herself was little to be feared.", "그 여자 혼자서 부릴 수 있는 해코지야 별로 두려울 게 없었다."], ["But the mischief she might do, as a willing instrument in her husband’s hands, was too formidable to be overlooked.", "그러나 남편의 손에 기꺼이 쥐인 도구로서 부릴 수 있는 해코지는 그냥 지나치기에는 너무 무서웠다."]], [["“What became of the rustling of the gown when you no longer heard it in the ante-room?”", "“바깥방에서 그 옷자락 소리가 더 들리지 않게 된 다음에는 어떻게 됐니?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."], ["“Did you hear it go past your wall, along the passage?”", "“네 방 벽을 지나 복도 쪽으로 가는 소리가 들리던?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“응."], ["I kept still and listened, and just heard it.”", "가만히 숨죽이고 귀 기울이니까 겨우 들렸어.”"]], [["“Which way did it go?”", "“어느 쪽으로 갔어?”"]], [["“Towards your room.”", "“언니 방 쪽으로.”"]], [["I considered again.", "나는 다시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The sound had not caught my ears.", "그 소리는 내 귀에 들리지 않았다."], ["But I was then deeply absorbed in my letters, and I write with a heavy hand and a quill pen, scraping and scratching noisily over the paper.", "그러나 그때 나는 편지에 깊이 빠져 있었고, 나는 손에 힘을 주어 깃펜으로 종이 위를 사각사각 긁으며 요란하게 쓴다."], ["It was more likely that Madame Fosco would hear the scraping of my pen than that I should hear the rustling of her dress.", "내가 그 여자의 옷자락 소리를 듣기보다 그 여자가 내 펜 소리를 들었을 가능성이 더 컸다."], ["Another reason (if I had wanted one) for not trusting my letters to the post-bag in the hall.", "더 필요했다면, 편지를 현관의 우편 가방에 맡기지 말아야 할 또 하나의 이유였다."]], [["Laura saw me thinking.", "로라는 내가 생각에 잠긴 것을 보았다."], ["“More difficulties!”", "“또 어려운 일이구나!”"], ["she said wearily; “more difficulties and more dangers!”", "로라가 지친 목소리로 말했다. “어려운 일도 더 생기고 위험도 더 생기는구나!”"]], [["“No dangers,”", "“위험은 없어.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Some little difficulty, perhaps.", "“조금 까다로울 뿐이지, 아마."], ["I am thinking of the safest way of putting my two letters into Fanny’s hands.”", "편지 두 통을 패니 손에 넘길 가장 안전한 방법을 생각하는 중이야.”"]], [["“You have really written them, then?", "“그럼 정말로 다 썼구나?"], ["Oh, Marian, run no risks—pray, pray run no risks!”", "아, 매리언, 위험한 짓은 하지 마. 제발, 제발 위험한 짓은 하지 마!”"]], [["“No, no—no fear.", "“아니야, 아니야."], ["Let me see—what o’clock is it now?”", "걱정 마. 어디 보자, 지금 몇 시지?”"]], [["It was a quarter to six.", "여섯 시 십오 분 전이었다."], ["There would be time for me to get to the village inn, and to come back again before dinner.", "마을 여관까지 갔다가 저녁 식사 전에 돌아올 시간은 있었다."], ["If I waited till the evening I might find no second opportunity of safely leaving the house.", "저녁까지 기다리면 무사히 집을 빠져나갈 두 번째 기회는 없을지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Keep the key turned in the lock, Laura,”", "“문은 계속 잠가 두렴, 로라.”"], ["I said, “and don’t be afraid about me.", "내가 말했다. “그리고 내 걱정은 하지 마."], ["If you hear any inquiries made, call through the door, and say that I am gone out for a walk.”", "누가 뭘 묻는 소리가 들리거든 문 너머로 내가 산책 나갔다고 대답해.”"]], [["“When shall you be back?”", "“언제 돌아올 거야?”"]], [["“Before dinner, without fail.", "“저녁 식사 전에는 꼭."], ["Courage, my love.", "힘을 내, 얘야."], ["By this time to-morrow you will have a clear-headed, trustworthy man acting for your good. Mr.", "내일 이맘때면 냉철하고 믿음직한 사람이 너를 위해 나서 줄 거야."], ["Gilmore’s partner is our next best friend to Mr. Gilmore himself.”", "길모어 씨의 동업자는 길모어 씨 다음으로 우리에게 좋은 친구지.”"]], [["A moment’s reflection, as soon as I was alone, convinced me that I had better not appear in my walking-dress until I had first discovered what was going on in the lower part of the house.", "혼자가 되자마자 잠깐 생각해 보니, 집 아래층에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 먼저 알아보기 전에는 외출복 차림으로 나서지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 확신이 들었다."], ["I had not ascertained yet whether Sir Percival was indoors or out.", "퍼시벌 경이 집 안에 있는지 밖에 있는지 아직 확인하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["The singing of the canaries in the library, and the smell of tobacco-smoke that came through the door, which was not closed, told me at once where the Count was.", "서재에서 들려오는 카나리아 노랫소리와, 닫히지 않은 문틈으로 새어 나오는 담배 연기 냄새가 백작이 어디 있는지 대번에 알려 주었다."], ["I looked over my shoulder as I passed the doorway, and saw to my surprise that he was exhibiting the docility of the birds in his most engagingly polite manner to the housekeeper.", "문간을 지나며 어깨 너머로 들여다보다가, 그가 더없이 상냥하고 정중한 태도로 가정부에게 새들의 재롱을 보여 주고 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["He must have specially invited her to see them—for she would never have thought of going into the library of her own accord.", "그가 일부러 청해서 부른 것이 틀림없었다. 가정부가 제 발로 서재에 들어갈 생각을 할 리 없으니까."], ["The man’s slightest actions had a purpose of some kind at the bottom of every one of them.", "저 사람은 아무리 사소한 행동이라도 그 밑바닥에는 어떤 목적이 깔려 있다."], ["What could be his purpose here?", "여기서 그의 목적은 무엇일까?"]], [["It was no time then to inquire into his motives.", "그때는 그의 속셈을 캘 때가 아니었다."], ["I looked about for Madame Fosco next, and found her following her favourite circle round and round the fish-pond.", "다음으로 포스코 백작부인을 찾아보았더니, 늘 그러듯 연못 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌고 있었다."]], [["I was a little doubtful how she would meet me, after the outbreak of jealousy of which I had been the cause so short a time since.", "얼마 전 내가 원인이 되어 터진 질투의 소동이 있었으니, 부인이 나를 어떻게 대할지 조금 불안했다."], ["But her husband had tamed her in the interval, and she now spoke to me with the same civility as usual.", "그러나 그사이 남편이 길들여 놓은 뒤라, 이제 부인은 여느 때와 다름없이 정중하게 말을 건넸다."], ["My only object in addressing myself to her was to ascertain if she knew what had become of Sir Percival.", "내가 말을 붙인 유일한 목적은 퍼시벌 경이 어찌 되었는지 부인이 아는지 알아보는 것이었다."], ["I contrived to refer to him indirectly, and after a little fencing on either side she at last mentioned that he had gone out.", "나는 에둘러 그를 화제에 올렸고, 서로 조금 떠본 끝에 부인은 마침내 그가 밖에 나갔다고 말했다."]], [["“Which of the horses has he taken?”", "“말은 어느 걸 타고 가셨나요?”"], ["I asked carelessly.", "내가 무심한 척 물었다."]], [["“None of them,” she replied.", "“한 마리도 안 탔어요.”"], ["“He went away two hours since on foot.", "부인이 대답했다. “두 시간 전에 걸어서 나가셨지요."], ["As I understood it, his object was to make fresh inquiries about the woman named Anne Catherick.", "제가 알기로는 앤 캐서릭이라는 여자에 대해 새로 알아보러 가신 거예요."], ["He appears to be unreasonably anxious about tracing her.", "그 여자를 찾는 데 지나칠 만큼 안달하시는 것 같더군요."], ["Do you happen to know if she is dangerously mad, Miss Halcombe?”", "혹시 그 여자가 위험할 만큼 미쳤는지 아세요, 홀콤 양?”"]], [["“I do not, Countess.”", "“모릅니다, 백작부인.”"]], [["“Are you going in?”", "“들어가시려고요?”"]], [["“Yes, I think so.", "“네, 그럴까 합니다."], ["I suppose it will soon be time to dress for dinner.”", "곧 저녁 식사 옷차림을 할 시간일 테니까요.”"]], [["We entered the house together.", "우리는 함께 집으로 들어갔다."], ["Madame Fosco strolled into the library, and closed the door.", "포스코 백작부인은 어슬렁어슬렁 서재로 들어가 문을 닫았다."], ["I went at once to fetch my hat and shawl.", "나는 곧장 모자와 숄을 가지러 갔다."], ["Every moment was of importance, if I was to get to Fanny at the inn and be back before dinner.", "여관에 있는 패니에게 갔다가 저녁 식사 전에 돌아오려면 한순간도 허투루 쓸 수 없었다."]], [["When I crossed the hall again no one was there, and the singing of the birds in the library had ceased.", "다시 현관을 가로지를 때는 아무도 없었고, 서재의 새소리도 그쳐 있었다."], ["I could not stop to make any fresh investigations.", "새로 살펴볼 겨를은 없었다."], ["I could only assure myself that the way was clear, and then leave the house with the two letters safe in my pocket.", "길이 열려 있음을 확인하고, 편지 두 통을 주머니에 안전히 넣은 채 집을 나서는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["On my way to the village I prepared myself for the possibility of meeting Sir Percival.", "마을로 가는 길에 나는 퍼시벌 경과 마주칠 가능성에 대비해 마음을 다잡았다."], ["As long as I had him to deal with alone I felt certain of not losing my presence of mind.", "그를 혼자서 상대하는 한, 내 정신을 놓치지 않으리라는 자신이 있었다."], ["Any woman who is sure of her own wits is a match at any time for a man who is not sure of his own temper.", "제 지혜를 확신하는 여자는 제 성미를 다스리지 못하는 남자쯤은 언제라도 당해 낼 수 있다."], ["I had no such fear of Sir Percival as I had of the Count.", "나는 퍼시벌 경을 백작만큼 두려워하지는 않았다."], ["Instead of fluttering, it had composed me, to hear of the errand on which he had gone out.", "그가 무슨 볼일로 나갔는지 듣고 나니 마음이 흔들리기는커녕 오히려 가라앉았다."], ["While the tracing of Anne Catherick was the great anxiety that occupied him, Laura and I might hope for some cessation of any active persecution at his hands.", "앤 캐서릭을 찾는 일이 그를 사로잡은 가장 큰 근심인 동안에는, 로라와 나는 그가 우리를 적극적으로 괴롭히는 일이 얼마간 멈추기를 바랄 수 있었다."], ["For our sakes now, as well as for Anne’s, I hoped and prayed fervently that she might still escape him.", "이제는 앤을 위해서만이 아니라 우리를 위해서도, 그 여자가 여전히 그를 피해 있기를 나는 간절히 바라고 기도했다."]], [["I walked on as briskly as the heat would let me till I reached the cross-road which led to the village, looking back from time to time to make sure that I was not followed by any one.", "나는 더위가 허락하는 한 빠른 걸음으로 걸어 마을로 이어지는 갈림길에 이르렀고, 이따금 뒤를 돌아보며 누가 따라오지 않는지 확인했다."]], [["Nothing was behind me all the way but an empty country waggon.", "오는 내내 뒤에는 빈 시골 짐마차 한 대밖에 없었다."], ["The noise made by the lumbering wheels annoyed me, and when I found that the waggon took the road to the village, as well as myself, I stopped to let it go by and pass out of hearing.", "덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리가 거슬렸고, 그 마차도 나처럼 마을로 가는 길로 접어드는 것을 보고 나는 걸음을 멈춰 마차를 먼저 보내고 소리가 멀어지기를 기다렸다."], ["As I looked toward it, more attentively than before, I thought I detected at intervals the feet of a man walking close behind it, the carter being in front, by the side of his horses.", "아까보다 더 유심히 마차 쪽을 바라보다가, 나는 마차 뒤에 바짝 붙어 걷는 남자의 발이 이따금 보이는 것 같다고 생각했다. 마부는 앞쪽에서 말 옆에 붙어 걷고 있었다."], ["The part of the cross-road which I had just passed over was so narrow that the waggon coming after me brushed the trees and thickets on either side, and I had to wait until it went by before I could test the correctness of my impression.", "내가 방금 지나온 갈림길은 워낙 좁아서 뒤따라오는 마차가 양옆의 나무와 덤불을 스칠 정도였으니, 마차가 지나갈 때까지 기다려야만 내 짐작이 맞는지 확인할 수 있었다."], ["Apparently that impression was wrong, for when the waggon had passed me the road behind it was quite clear.", "아무래도 그 짐작은 틀렸던 모양이다. 마차가 나를 지나쳐 간 뒤 그 뒤의 길은 텅 비어 있었으니까."]], [["I reached the inn without meeting Sir Percival, and without noticing anything more, and was glad to find that the landlady had received Fanny with all possible kindness.", "나는 퍼시벌 경과 마주치지도 않고 더 눈에 띄는 것도 없이 여관에 이르렀고, 여관 안주인이 패니를 더없이 친절하게 맞아 주었다는 것을 알고 기뻤다."], ["The girl had a little parlour to sit in, away from the noise of the taproom, and a clean bedchamber at the top of the house.", "그 아이는 술청의 소음에서 떨어진 작은 거실과, 집 꼭대기의 깨끗한 침실을 얻어 있었다."], ["She began crying again at the sight of me, and said, poor soul, truly enough, that it was dreadful to feel herself turned out into the world as if she had committed some unpardonable fault, when no blame could be laid at her door by anybody—not even by her master, who had sent her away.", "나를 보자 다시 울음을 터뜨리더니, 딱하게도 참으로 옳은 말을 했다. 누구도, 심지어 자기를 내보낸 주인조차 자기 탓이라 할 것이 없는데, 무슨 용서 못 할 잘못이라도 저지른 양 세상으로 내쫓긴 기분이 드는 것은 끔찍하다고."]], [["“Try to make the best of it, Fanny,” I said.", "“되도록 좋게 생각하렴, 패니.”"], ["“Your mistress and I will stand your friends, and will take care that your character shall not suffer.", "내가 말했다. “아씨와 내가 네 편이 되어 줄 테고, 네 평판에 흠이 가지 않도록 해 주마."], ["Now, listen to me.", "자, 잘 들어라."], ["I have very little time to spare, and I am going to put a great trust in your hands.", "시간이 얼마 없는데, 네게 아주 큰일을 맡기려 한다."], ["I wish you to take care of these two letters.", "이 편지 두 통을 잘 간수해 다오."], ["The one with the stamp on it you are to put into the post when you reach London to-morrow.", "우표가 붙은 것은 내일 런던에 닿거든 우체통에 넣어라."], ["The other, directed to Mr. Fairlie, you are to deliver to him yourself as soon as you get home.", "페얼리 씨 앞으로 된 다른 하나는 집에 도착하는 대로 네가 직접 그분께 전해 드려라."], ["Keep both the letters about you and give them up to no one.", "두 통 다 몸에 지니고, 누구에게도 내주지 마라."], ["They are of the last importance to your mistress’s interests.”", "아씨의 이해가 걸린 더없이 중요한 편지들이다.”"]], [["Fanny put the letters into the bosom of her dress. “There they shall stop, miss,”", "패니는 편지를 옷 앞섶에 넣었다."], ["she said, “till I have done what you tell me.”", "“아가씨가 시키신 일을 다 할 때까지 여기 넣어 두겠어요.” 그 아이가 말했다."]], [["“Mind you are at the station in good time to-morrow morning,”", "“내일 아침 역에 늦지 않게 나가야 한다.” 내가 말을 이었다."], ["I continued. “And when you see the housekeeper at Limmeridge give her my compliments, and say that you are in my service until Lady Glyde is able to take you back.", "“그리고 리머리지에서 가정부를 만나거든 내 안부를 전하고, 글라이드 부인이 너를 다시 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 네가 나를 모시고 있는 것이라고 말해라."], ["We may meet again sooner than you think.", "생각보다 빨리 다시 만나게 될지도 모르지."], ["So keep a good heart, and don’t miss the seven o’clock train.”", "그러니 마음 단단히 먹고, 일곱 시 기차를 놓치지 마라.”"]], [["“Thank you, miss—thank you kindly.", "“고맙습니다, 아가씨. 정말 고맙습니다."], ["It gives one courage to hear your voice again.", "아가씨 목소리를 다시 들으니 힘이 납니다."], ["Please to offer my duty to my lady, and say I left all the things as tidy as I could in the time.", "마님께 문안 여쭈어 주시고, 시간이 없는 대로 물건들은 최대한 정돈해 두었다고 말씀드려 주세요."], ["Oh, dear! dear!", "아이고, 저런!"], ["who will dress her for dinner to-day?", "오늘은 누가 마님 저녁 식사 옷을 입혀 드릴까요?"], ["It really breaks my heart, miss, to think of it.”", "그 생각을 하면 정말 가슴이 미어집니다, 아가씨.”"]], [["When I got back to the house I had only a quarter of an hour to spare to put myself in order for dinner, and to say two words to Laura before I went downstairs.", "집에 돌아오니 저녁 식사 채비를 하고 아래층에 내려가기 전에 로라에게 두어 마디 할 시간이 십오 분밖에 남지 않았다."]], [["“The letters are in Fanny’s hands,”", "“편지는 패니 손에 넘어갔어.”"], ["I whispered to her at the door.", "문간에서 내가 속삭였다."], ["“Do you mean to join us at dinner?”", "“저녁 식사에 함께 나올 거니?”"]], [["“Oh, no, no—not for the world.”", "“아, 아니, 아니. 무슨 일이 있어도 싫어.”"]], [["“Has anything happened?", "“무슨 일 있었니?"], ["Has any one disturbed you?”", "누가 방해했어?”"]], [["“Yes—just now—Sir Percival——”", "“응, 방금, 퍼시벌 경이——”"]], [["“Did he come in?”", "“들어왔었니?”"]], [["“No, he frightened me by a thump on the door outside.", "“아니, 밖에서 문을 쾅 쳐서 나를 놀라게 했어. 내가 ‘누구세요?’ 했더니 ‘알면서.’ 하고 대답하더구나."], ["I said, ‘Who’s there?’ ‘You know,’ he answered.", "‘마음을 고쳐먹고 나머지를 말할 텐가? 말하게 될 거다!"], ["‘Will you alter your mind, and tell me the rest?", "언젠가는 내가 기어이 짜내고 말 테니까. 당신은 앤 캐서릭이 지금 어디 있는지 알고 있어.’ ‘정말이에요, 정말이지,’ 하고 내가 말했지."], ["You shall! Sooner or later I’ll wring it out of you.", "‘저는 몰라요.’ ‘알고 있어!’ 하고 그이가 되받았어."], ["You know where Anne Catherick is at this moment.’ ‘Indeed, indeed,’ I said, ‘I don’t.’ ‘You do!’ he called back.", "‘그 고집을 꺾어 놓고 말겠다. 명심해!"], ["‘I’ll crush your obstinacy—mind that!—I’ll wring it out of you!’ He went away with those words—went away, Marian, hardly five minutes ago.”", "기어이 짜내고 말겠어!’ 그 말을 남기고 갔어. 매리언, 채 오 분도 안 됐어.”"]], [["He had not found Anne!", "그는 앤을 찾지 못한 것이다!"], ["We were safe for that night—he had not found her yet.", "그날 밤은 우리가 무사했다. 그는 아직 앤을 찾지 못했다."]], [["“You are going downstairs, Marian?", "“내려가려는 거지, 매리언?"], ["Come up again in the evening.”", "저녁에 다시 올라와 줘.”"]], [["“Yes, yes.", "“그래, 그래."], ["Don’t be uneasy if I am a little late—I must be careful not to give offence by leaving them too soon.”", "내가 좀 늦더라도 불안해하지 마. 너무 일찍 자리를 뜨는 바람에 저 사람들 심기를 건드리지 않도록 조심해야 하니까.”"]], [["The dinner-bell rang and I hastened away.", "저녁 식사 종이 울려 나는 서둘러 갔다."]], [["Sir Percival took Madame Fosco into the dining-room, and the Count gave me his arm.", "퍼시벌 경이 포스코 백작부인을 식당으로 안내했고, 백작이 내게 팔을 내밀었다."], ["He was hot and flushed, and was not dressed with his customary care and completeness.", "그는 덥고 얼굴이 붉었으며, 여느 때처럼 빈틈없이 차려입지 않았다."], ["Had he, too, been out before dinner, and been late in getting back?", "그 역시 저녁 식사 전에 밖에 나갔다가 늦게 돌아온 것일까?"], ["or was he only suffering from the heat a little more severely than usual?", "아니면 그저 여느 때보다 더위를 좀 더 심하게 타는 것뿐일까?"]], [["However this might be, he was unquestionably troubled by some secret annoyance or anxiety, which, with all his powers of deception, he was not able entirely to conceal.", "어느 쪽이든, 그는 무언가 남모를 성가심이나 근심에 시달리고 있는 것이 분명했고, 그 온갖 기만의 재주로도 그것을 온전히 감추지는 못했다."], ["Through the whole of dinner he was almost as silent as Sir Percival himself, and he, every now and then, looked at his wife with an expression of furtive uneasiness which was quite new in my experience of him.", "식사 내내 그는 퍼시벌 경 못지않게 말이 없었고, 이따금 아내를 은밀히 불안해하는 눈빛으로 바라보았는데, 내가 아는 한 그런 모습은 처음이었다."], ["The one social obligation which he seemed to be self-possessed enough to perform as carefully as ever was the obligation of being persistently civil and attentive to me.", "그가 여전히 여느 때처럼 빈틈없이 해내는 유일한 사교적 의무는, 내게 한결같이 정중하고 세심하게 대하는 일이었다."], ["What vile object he has in view I cannot still discover, but be the design what it may, invariable politeness towards myself, invariable humility towards Laura, and invariable suppression (at any cost) of Sir Percival’s clumsy violence, have been the means he has resolutely and impenetrably used to get to his end ever since he set foot in this house.", "그가 무슨 고약한 목적을 품고 있는지는 아직도 알아낼 수 없지만, 그 속셈이 무엇이든, 나에 대한 변함없는 예의, 로라에 대한 변함없는 겸손, 그리고 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 퍼시벌 경의 서투른 난폭함을 변함없이 억누르는 것, 이것이 그가 이 집에 발을 들인 뒤로 목적을 이루기 위해 단호하고 빈틈없이 써 온 수단이다."], ["I suspected it when he first interfered in our favour, on the day when the deed was produced in the library, and I feel certain of it now.", "서재에서 그 증서가 나온 날 그가 처음으로 우리 편을 들어 끼어들었을 때 나는 그것을 의심했고, 이제는 확신한다."]], [["When Madame Fosco and I rose to leave the table, the Count rose also to accompany us back to the drawing-room.", "포스코 백작부인과 내가 자리에서 일어나 식탁을 뜨자, 백작도 우리를 응접실까지 배웅하려고 일어섰다."]], [["“What are you going away for?”", "“왜 가는 거야?”"], ["asked Sir Percival—“I mean YOU, Fosco.”", "퍼시벌 경이 물었다. “자네 말이야, 포스코.”"]], [["“I am going away because I have had dinner enough, and wine enough,”", "“저녁도 충분히 먹었고 술도 충분히 마셨으니 가는 걸세.”"], ["answered the Count.", "백작이 대답했다."], ["“Be so kind, Percival, as to make allowances for my foreign habit of going out with the ladies, as well as coming in with them.”", "“여보게 퍼시벌, 숙녀들과 함께 들어오듯 함께 나가기도 하는 내 외국식 습관을 좀 봐주게나.”"]], [["“Nonsense!", "“허튼소리!"], ["Another glass of claret won’t hurt you.", "클라레 한 잔 더 한다고 해될 것 없네."], ["Sit down again like an Englishman.", "영국 사람답게 다시 앉게."], ["I want half an hour’s quiet talk with you over our wine.”", "술잔을 놓고 자네와 삼십 분쯤 조용히 이야기하고 싶으이.”"]], [["“A quiet talk, Percival, with all my heart, but not now, and not over the wine.", "“조용한 이야기라면 기꺼이 하지, 퍼시벌. 하지만 지금은 아니고, 술잔을 놓고도 아닐세."], ["Later in the evening, if you please—later in the evening.”", "괜찮다면 저녁 늦게, 저녁 늦게 하세.”"]], [["“Civil!”", "“예의도 바르시군!”"], ["said Sir Percival savagely.", "퍼시벌 경이 사납게 말했다."], ["“Civil behaviour, upon my soul, to a man in his own house!”", "“남의 집에 와서 주인에게 하는 짓치고 참으로 예의 바르구먼!”"]], [["I had more than once seen him look at the Count uneasily during dinner-time, and had observed that the Count carefully abstained from looking at him in return.", "나는 식사 중에 그가 백작을 불안한 눈으로 바라보는 것을 한 번 이상 보았고, 백작은 그 눈길을 마주 보기를 조심스레 피한다는 것도 알아챘다."], ["This circumstance, coupled with the host’s anxiety for a little quiet talk over the wine, and the guest’s obstinate resolution not to sit down again at the table, revived in my memory the request which Sir Percival had vainly addressed to his friend earlier in the day to come out of the library and speak to him.", "이 일과, 술잔을 놓고 잠깐 조용히 이야기하고 싶어 하는 주인의 조바심, 그리고 식탁에 다시 앉기를 완강히 마다하는 손님의 태도를 함께 놓고 보니, 그날 낮에 퍼시벌 경이 서재에서 나와 이야기 좀 하자고 친구에게 청했다가 헛물켠 일이 다시 떠올랐다."], ["The Count had deferred granting that private interview, when it was first asked for in the afternoon, and had again deferred granting it, when it was a second time asked for at the dinner-table.", "백작은 오후에 그 단둘의 면담을 처음 청받았을 때 미루었고, 저녁 식탁에서 두 번째로 청받았을 때도 또 미루었다."], ["Whatever the coming subject of discussion between them might be, it was clearly an important subject in Sir Percival’s estimation—and perhaps (judging from his evident reluctance to approach it) a dangerous subject as well, in the estimation of the Count.", "두 사람 사이에 오갈 이야기의 주제가 무엇이든, 그것은 퍼시벌 경이 보기에는 분명 중요한 주제였고, 그 이야기를 꺼리는 기색이 뚜렷한 것으로 미루어 보면 백작이 보기에는 아마 위험한 주제이기도 했다."]], [["These considerations occurred to me while we were passing from the dining-room to the drawing-room.", "이런 생각들이 식당에서 응접실로 옮겨 가는 동안 떠올랐다."], ["Sir Percival’s angry commentary on his friend’s desertion of him had not produced the slightest effect.", "친구가 저를 버리고 간다며 퍼시벌 경이 성을 내며 한 말은 조금도 효과가 없었다."], ["The Count obstinately accompanied us to the tea-table—waited a minute or two in the room—went out into the hall—and returned with the post-bag in his hands.", "백작은 고집스레 우리를 따라 찻상까지 왔다가, 방에서 일이 분쯤 기다리더니, 현관으로 나갔다가, 우편 가방을 손에 들고 돌아왔다."], ["It was then eight o’clock—the hour at which the letters were always despatched from Blackwater Park.", "그때가 여덟 시, 블랙워터 파크에서 늘 편지를 부치러 보내는 시각이었다."]], [["“Have you any letter for the post, Miss Halcombe?”", "“부치실 편지가 있으신가요, 홀콤 양?”"], ["he asked, approaching me with the bag.", "그가 가방을 들고 다가오며 물었다."]], [["I saw Madame Fosco, who was making the tea, pause, with the sugar-tongs in her hand, to listen for my answer.", "차를 따르던 포스코 백작부인이 설탕 집게를 손에 든 채 내 대답을 들으려고 멈추는 것이 보였다."]], [["“No, Count, thank you.", "“없습니다, 백작님, 고맙습니다."], ["No letters to-day.”", "오늘은 편지가 없어요.”"]], [["He gave the bag to the servant, who was then in the room; sat down at the piano, and played the air of the lively Neapolitan street-song, “La mia Carolina,” twice over.", "그는 그때 방에 있던 하인에게 가방을 넘기고, 피아노 앞에 앉아 흥겨운 나폴리 거리 노래 「라 미아 카롤리나」의 가락을 두 번 연달아 쳤다."], ["His wife, who was usually the most deliberate of women in all her movements, made the tea as quickly as I could have made it myself—finished her own cup in two minutes, and quietly glided out of the room.", "무슨 일에나 여자들 중에서도 가장 굼뜬 그의 아내는, 나라도 그보다 빨리는 못 했을 만큼 재빨리 차를 따라, 이 분 만에 제 잔을 비우고, 소리 없이 방을 빠져나갔다."]], [["I rose to follow her example—partly because I suspected her of attempting some treachery upstairs with Laura, partly because I was resolved not to remain alone in the same room with her husband.", "나도 그 여자를 따라 일어섰다. 한편으로는 그 여자가 위층에서 로라에게 무슨 흉계를 꾸미려는 게 아닌가 의심스러웠기 때문이고, 한편으로는 그 남편과 한방에 단둘이 남지 않기로 마음먹었기 때문이었다."]], [["Before I could get to the door the Count stopped me, by a request for a cup of tea.", "문에 이르기도 전에 백작이 차 한 잔을 청하며 나를 붙잡았다."], ["I gave him the cup of tea, and tried a second time to get away.", "나는 차를 따라 주고 두 번째로 빠져나가려 했다."], ["He stopped me again—this time by going back to the piano, and suddenly appealing to me on a musical question in which he declared that the honour of his country was concerned.", "그는 또 나를 붙잡았다. 이번에는 피아노로 돌아가더니, 조국의 명예가 걸린 문제라고 선언하며 느닷없이 음악 이야기로 내게 묻는 것이었다."]], [["I vainly pleaded my own total ignorance of music, and total want of taste in that direction.", "나는 음악에 대해 아무것도 모르고 그쪽으로는 취미도 전혀 없다고 헛되이 변명했다."], ["He only appealed to me again with a vehemence which set all further protest on my part at defiance.", "그는 내가 더 항변할 여지를 남기지 않는 열의로 다시 물어 올 뿐이었다. 영국인과 독일인은, 그가 분개하여 말하기를, 이탈리아인이 높은 차원의 음악을 가꿀 줄 모른다고 늘 헐뜯는다."], ["“The English and the Germans (he indignantly declared) were always reviling the Italians for their inability to cultivate the higher kinds of music.", "우리는 늘 오라토리오 이야기를 하고 저들은 늘 교향곡 이야기를 한다. 우리는, 또 저들은 그의 불멸의 벗이자 동포인 로시니를 잊었단 말인가?"], ["We were perpetually talking of our Oratorios, and they were perpetually talking of their Symphonies.", "「이집트의 모세」야말로 연주회장에서 차갑게 불리는 대신 무대 위에서 연기된 숭고한 오라토리오가 아니고 무엇인가? 「기욤 텔」"], ["Did we forget and did they forget his immortal friend and countryman, Rossini?", "서곡이야말로 이름만 다른 교향곡이 아니고 무엇인가? 「이집트의 모세」를 들어 본 적이 있는가?"], ["What was Moses in Egypt but a sublime oratorio, which was acted on the stage instead of being coldly sung in a concert-room?", "이것과 이것과 이것을 들어 보고, 인간의 손으로 이보다 더 숭고하고 성스럽고 웅장한 것이 지어진 적이 있는지 말해 보겠는가? 그러고는 내 동의도 반대도 기다리지 않고, 내내 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 피아노를 우레같이 두들기고 크고 드높은 열정으로 노래하기 시작했다."], ["What was the overture to Guillaume Tell but a symphony under another name?", "다만 이따금 스스로 멈추고 사나운 기세로 곡목을 알려 줄 뿐이었다. “어둠의 재앙 속 이집트인들의 합창, 홀콤 양!”"], ["Had I heard Moses in Egypt?", "“율법판을 든 모세의 레치타티보.”"], ["Would I listen to this, and this, and this, and say if anything more sublimely sacred and grand had ever been composed by mortal man?”—And without waiting for a word of assent or dissent on my part, looking me hard in the face all the time, he began thundering on the piano, and singing to it with loud and lofty enthusiasm—only interrupting himself, at intervals, to announce to me fiercely the titles of the different pieces of music: “Chorus of Egyptians in the Plague of Darkness, Miss Halcombe!”—“Recitativo of Moses with the tables of the Law.”—“Prayer of Israelites, at the passage of the Red Sea.", "“홍해를 건널 때 이스라엘 백성의 기도. 아하!"], ["Aha! Aha!", "아하!"], ["Is that sacred? is that sublime?”", "이것이 성스럽지 않소?"], ["The piano trembled under his powerful hands, and the teacups on the table rattled, as his big bass voice thundered out the notes, and his heavy foot beat time on the floor.", "이것이 숭고하지 않소?” 그 억센 손 아래 피아노가 떨었고, 굵은 저음이 음들을 우레처럼 쏟아 내고 무거운 발이 바닥을 구르며 박자를 맞추는 동안 탁자 위의 찻잔들이 달그락거렸다."]], [["There was something horrible—something fierce and devilish—in the outburst of his delight at his own singing and playing, and in the triumph with which he watched its effect upon me as I shrank nearer and nearer to the door.", "자신의 노래와 연주에 도취한 그 환희의 폭발에는, 그리고 내가 문 쪽으로 점점 더 물러나는 동안 그것이 내게 미치는 효과를 바라보는 그 득의양양함에는 무언가 끔찍한 것이, 무언가 사납고 악마 같은 것이 있었다."], ["I was released at last, not by my own efforts, but by Sir Percival’s interposition.", "마침내 나는 풀려났는데, 내 힘이 아니라 퍼시벌 경이 끼어든 덕분이었다."], ["He opened the dining-room door, and called out angrily to know what “that infernal noise” meant.", "그가 식당 문을 열고 저 “빌어먹을 소음”이 무슨 짓이냐고 성난 목소리로 외쳤던 것이다."], ["The Count instantly got up from the piano.", "백작은 즉시 피아노에서 일어섰다."], ["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["if Percival is coming,”", "퍼시벌이 온다면,”"], ["he said, “harmony and melody are both at an end.", "그가 말했다. “화성도 선율도 끝장이지요."], ["The Muse of Music, Miss Halcombe, deserts us in dismay, and I, the fat old minstrel, exhale the rest of my enthusiasm in the open air!”", "음악의 여신은 기겁하여 우리를 버리고 떠나니, 늙고 뚱뚱한 이 악사는 남은 열정을 바깥바람에 흩뿌리는 수밖에요, 홀콤 양!”"], ["He stalked out into the verandah, put his hands in his pockets, and resumed the “Recitativo of Moses”, sotto voce, in the garden.", "그는 성큼성큼 베란다로 나가 주머니에 손을 찌르고, 정원에서 나직한 소리로 「모세의 레치타티보」를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["I heard Sir Percival call after him from the dining-room window.", "퍼시벌 경이 식당 창문에서 그를 부르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["But he took no notice—he seemed determined not to hear.", "그러나 그는 아랑곳하지 않았다. 아예 들으려 하지 않기로 작정한 듯했다."], ["That long-deferred quiet talk between them was still to be put off, was still to wait for the Count’s absolute will and pleasure.", "그토록 미뤄 온 두 사람의 조용한 이야기는 여전히 미뤄지고, 여전히 백작의 뜻과 기분이 내킬 때를 기다려야 했다."]], [["He had detained me in the drawing-room nearly half an hour from the time when his wife left us.", "그는 아내가 우리를 떠난 때부터 거의 반 시간 동안 나를 응접실에 붙들어 두었다."], ["Where had she been, and what had she been doing in that interval?", "그동안 그 여자는 어디에 있었고, 무엇을 하고 있었을까?"]], [["I went upstairs to ascertain, but I made no discoveries, and when I questioned Laura, I found that she had not heard anything.", "알아보려고 위층으로 올라갔지만 아무것도 알아내지 못했고, 로라에게 물어보니 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다고 했다."], ["Nobody had disturbed her, no faint rustling of the silk dress had been audible, either in the ante-room or in the passage.", "아무도 로라를 방해하지 않았고, 바깥방에서든 복도에서든 비단 옷자락이 스치는 희미한 소리조차 들리지 않았다는 것이다."]], [["It was then twenty minutes to nine.", "그때가 아홉 시 이십 분 전이었다."], ["After going to my room to get my journal, I returned, and sat with Laura, sometimes writing, sometimes stopping to talk with her.", "내 방에 가서 일기를 가져온 다음 돌아와 로라 곁에 앉아, 때로는 쓰고 때로는 멈추어 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Nobody came near us, and nothing happened.", "아무도 우리 가까이 오지 않았고 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["We remained together till ten o’clock.", "우리는 열 시까지 함께 있었다."], ["I then rose, said my last cheering words, and wished her good-night.", "그러고 나서 나는 일어나 마지막으로 기운을 북돋우는 말을 건네고 잘 자라는 인사를 했다."], ["She locked her door again after we had arranged that I should come in and see her the first thing in the morning.", "아침에 제일 먼저 내가 들여다보러 오기로 정한 뒤, 로라는 다시 문을 잠갔다."]], [["I had a few sentences more to add to my diary before going to bed myself, and as I went down again to the drawing-room after leaving Laura for the last time that weary day, I resolved merely to show myself there, to make my excuses, and then to retire an hour earlier than usual for the night.", "잠자리에 들기 전에 일기에 몇 줄 더 보태야 했고, 그 고단한 하루의 마지막으로 로라를 두고 나와 다시 응접실로 내려가면서, 나는 그저 얼굴만 비치고 양해를 구한 뒤 여느 때보다 한 시간 일찍 물러나기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Sir Percival, and the Count and his wife, were sitting together.", "퍼시벌 경과 백작 부부가 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["Sir Percival was yawning in an easy-chair, the Count was reading, Madame Fosco was fanning herself.", "퍼시벌 경은 안락의자에서 하품을 하고, 백작은 책을 읽고, 포스코 백작부인은 부채질을 하고 있었다."], ["Strange to say, HER face was flushed now.", "이상하게도 이번에는 그 여자의 얼굴이 붉게 달아올라 있었다."], ["She, who never suffered from the heat, was most undoubtedly suffering from it to-night.", "더위를 타는 법이 없던 사람이 오늘 밤만은 틀림없이 더위에 시달리고 있었다."]], [["“I am afraid, Countess, you are not quite so well as usual?”", "“백작부인, 오늘은 여느 때만큼 편치 않으신 것 같군요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“The very remark I was about to make to you,”", "“저도 마침 그 말씀을 드리려던 참이었어요.”"], ["she replied.", "부인이 대답했다."], ["“You are looking pale, my dear.”", "“안색이 창백하시군요, 그대.”"]], [["My dear!", "그대라니!"], ["It was the first time she had ever addressed me with that familiarity!", "그 여자가 나를 그토록 허물없이 부른 것은 처음이었다!"], ["There was an insolent smile too on her face when she said the words.", "그 말을 할 때 얼굴에는 무례한 미소까지 떠올라 있었다."]], [["“I am suffering from one of my bad headaches,”", "“고약한 두통이 도져서요.”"], ["I answered coldly.", "내가 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“Ah, indeed?", "“아, 그러세요?"], ["Want of exercise, I suppose?", "운동 부족이겠지요?"], ["A walk before dinner would have been just the thing for you.”", "저녁 식사 전에 산책이라도 하셨으면 딱 좋았을 텐데요.”"], ["She referred to the “walk” with a strange emphasis.", "부인은 “산책”이라는 말을 묘하게 힘주어 말했다."], ["Had she seen me go out?", "내가 나가는 것을 보았을까?"], ["No matter if she had.", "보았다 한들 상관없다."], ["The letters were safe now in Fanny’s hands.", "편지는 이제 패니의 손에 안전히 들어가 있으니까."]], [["“Come and have a smoke, Fosco,”", "“담배나 한 대 피우러 가세, 포스코.”"], ["said Sir Percival, rising, with another uneasy look at his friend.", "퍼시벌 경이 또 한 번 불안한 눈길로 친구를 보며 일어서서 말했다."]], [["“With pleasure, Percival, when the ladies have gone to bed,”", "“기꺼이 하지, 퍼시벌. 숙녀들이 잠자리에 든 뒤에 말일세.”"], ["replied the Count.", "백작이 대답했다."]], [["“Excuse me, Countess, if I set you the example of retiring,”", "“백작부인, 제가 먼저 물러나는 본을 보이더라도 용서하십시오.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“The only remedy for such a headache as mine is going to bed.”", "“저 같은 두통에는 잠자리에 드는 것 말고는 약이 없으니까요.”"]], [["I took my leave.", "나는 작별 인사를 했다."], ["There was the same insolent smile on the woman’s face when I shook hands with her.", "악수를 할 때 그 여자의 얼굴에는 여전히 무례한 미소가 걸려 있었다."], ["Sir Percival paid no attention to me.", "퍼시벌 경은 내게 눈길도 주지 않았다."], ["He was looking impatiently at Madame Fosco, who showed no signs of leaving the room with me.", "그는 나와 함께 방을 나갈 기색이 조금도 없는 포스코 백작부인을 초조하게 바라보고 있었다."], ["The Count smiled to himself behind his book.", "백작은 책 뒤에서 혼자 미소 지었다."], ["There was yet another delay to that quiet talk with Sir Percival—and the Countess was the impediment this time.", "퍼시벌 경이 바라는 그 조용한 이야기는 또 한 번 미뤄지게 되었고, 이번에는 백작부인이 걸림돌이었다."]], [["# IX", "# IX"]], [["June 19th.—Once safely shut into my own room, I opened these pages, and prepared to go on with that part of the day’s record which was still left to write.", "6월 19일.—내 방에 무사히 들어가 문을 닫아걸자마자 나는 이 일기장을 펴고, 아직 쓰지 못한 채 남아 있던 그날의 기록을 마저 이어 쓸 채비를 했다."]], [["For ten minutes or more I sat idle, with the pen in my hand, thinking over the events of the last twelve hours.", "십 분 남짓 나는 펜을 손에 쥔 채 아무것도 쓰지 못하고 앉아, 지난 열두 시간 동안 일어난 일들을 곱씹었다."], ["When I at last addressed myself to my task, I found a difficulty in proceeding with it which I had never experienced before.", "마침내 붓을 들려 했을 때 나는 여태껏 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 곤란을 느꼈다. 눈앞의 일에 생각을 붙들어 매려고 아무리 애를 써도, 생각은 기이하리만치 집요하게 오직 한 방향으로—퍼시벌 경과 백작 쪽으로—흘러가 버렸다."], ["In spite of my efforts to fix my thoughts on the matter in hand, they wandered away with the strangest persistency in the one direction of Sir Percival and the Count, and all the interest which I tried to concentrate on my journal centred instead in that private interview between them which had been put off all through the day, and which was now to take place in the silence and solitude of the night.", "일기에 쏟으려 했던 관심은 그 대신 온통 두 사람 사이의 밀담으로 쏠렸다. 하루 종일 미뤄지다가 이제 밤의 정적과 고요 속에서 열리게 될 그 밀담으로."]], [["In this perverse state of my mind, the recollection of what had passed since the morning would not come back to me, and there was no resource but to close my journal and to get away from it for a little while.", "이런 삐딱한 심사로는 아침 이후에 있었던 일들이 도무지 떠오르지 않았고, 일기장을 덮고 잠시 그것에서 벗어나는 것 말고는 달리 방도가 없었다."]], [["I opened the door which led from my bedroom into my sitting-room, and having passed through, pulled it to again, to prevent any accident in case of draught with the candle left on the dressing-table.", "나는 침실에서 거실로 통하는 문을 열고 나간 다음, 화장대에 놓아둔 촛불이 바람에 흔들려 사고라도 날까 봐 문을 다시 닫았다."], ["My sitting-room window was wide open, and I leaned out listlessly to look at the night.", "거실 창은 활짝 열려 있었고, 나는 맥없이 몸을 내밀어 밤을 내다보았다."]], [["It was dark and quiet.", "어둡고 고요했다."], ["Neither moon nor stars were visible.", "달도 별도 보이지 않았다."], ["There was a smell like rain in the still, heavy air, and I put my hand out of window. No.", "미동도 없는 무거운 공기 속에 비 냄새 같은 것이 났고, 나는 창밖으로 손을 내밀어 보았다."], ["The rain was only threatening, it had not come yet.", "아니었다. 비는 아직 을러대기만 할 뿐 내리지는 않았다."]], [["I remained leaning on the window-sill for nearly a quarter of an hour, looking out absently into the black darkness, and hearing nothing, except now and then the voices of the servants, or the distant sound of a closing door, in the lower part of the house.", "나는 십오 분 가까이 창턱에 기대어 선 채 캄캄한 어둠 속을 멍하니 내다보았다. 이따금 아래층에서 들려오는 하인들의 목소리나 멀리서 문 닫히는 소리 말고는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다."]], [["Just as I was turning away wearily from the window to go back to the bedroom and make a second attempt to complete the unfinished entry in my journal, I smelt the odour of tobacco-smoke stealing towards me on the heavy night air.", "지친 몸을 창에서 돌려 침실로 돌아가 일기의 못다 쓴 대목을 다시 붙들어 볼 참이었는데, 바로 그때 무거운 밤공기를 타고 담배 연기 냄새가 살금살금 다가오는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["The next moment I saw a tiny red spark advancing from the farther end of the house in the pitch darkness.", "다음 순간 나는 칠흑 같은 어둠 속에서 조그맣고 붉은 불똥 하나가 집 저편 끝에서 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["I heard no footsteps, and I could see nothing but the spark.", "발소리는 들리지 않았고 보이는 것은 그 불똥뿐이었다."], ["It travelled along in the night, passed the window at which I was standing, and stopped opposite my bedroom window, inside which I had left the light burning on the dressing-table.", "그것은 밤을 가로질러 나아와 내가 서 있던 창을 지나쳐, 화장대 위에 불을 켜 둔 내 침실 창 맞은편에서 멈추었다."]], [["The spark remained stationary for a moment, then moved back again in the direction from which it had advanced.", "불똥은 잠시 그 자리에 머물러 있다가 다시 왔던 방향으로 되돌아갔다."], ["As I followed its progress I saw a second red spark, larger than the first, approaching from the distance.", "그 움직임을 눈으로 좇는데 첫 번째 것보다 큰 두 번째 붉은 불똥이 멀리서 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["The two met together in the darkness.", "두 불똥은 어둠 속에서 만났다."], ["Remembering who smoked cigarettes and who smoked cigars, I inferred immediately that the Count had come out first to look and listen under my window, and that Sir Percival had afterwards joined him.", "누가 궐련을 피우고 누가 여송연을 피우는지 떠올린 나는, 백작이 먼저 나와 내 창 아래에서 살피고 엿듣다가 뒤늦게 퍼시벌 경이 합류했음을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 둘 다 잔디밭 위를 걷고 있었던 게 틀림없었다."], ["They must both have been walking on the lawn—or I should certainly have heard Sir Percival’s heavy footfall, though the Count’s soft step might have escaped me, even on the gravel walk.", "그렇지 않았다면 퍼시벌 경의 육중한 발소리만은 분명 들렸을 테니까. 백작의 사뿐한 걸음이야 자갈길 위에서도 놓쳤을 수 있겠지만."]], [["I waited quietly at the window, certain that they could neither of them see me in the darkness of the room.", "나는 창가에서 조용히 기다렸다. 어두운 방 안에 있는 나를 둘 중 누구도 볼 수 없으리라 확신하면서."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "“무슨 일인가?”"], ["I heard Sir Percival say in a low voice.", "퍼시벌 경이 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것이 들렸다."], ["“Why don’t you come in and sit down?”", "“왜 들어와 앉지 않고?”"]], [["“I want to see the light out of that window,”", "“저 창의 불이 꺼지는 걸 보고 싶소.”"], ["replied the Count softly.", "백작이 부드럽게 대답했다."]], [["“What harm does the light do?”", "“불이 켜져 있는 게 무슨 해가 된다고?”"]], [["“It shows she is not in bed yet.", "“아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다는 뜻이지."], ["She is sharp enough to suspect something, and bold enough to come downstairs and listen, if she can get the chance.", "그 여자는 무언가 눈치챌 만큼 영민하고, 틈만 나면 아래층으로 내려와 엿들을 만큼 대담하오."], ["Patience, Percival—patience.”", "인내요, 퍼시벌—인내.”"]], [["“Humbug!", "“허튼소리!"], ["You’re always talking of patience.”", "자넨 늘 인내 타령이군.”"]], [["“I shall talk of something else presently.", "“곧 다른 얘기도 하게 될 거요."], ["My good friend, you are on the edge of your domestic precipice, and if I let you give the women one other chance, on my sacred word of honour they will push you over it!”", "이보시오 벗이여, 그대는 지금 가정이라는 낭떠러지 끝에 서 있고, 만일 내가 그 여인들에게 기회를 한 번만 더 준다면, 내 신성한 명예를 걸고 맹세하건대 그들은 그대를 그 아래로 밀어 버릴 거요!”"]], [["“What the devil do you mean?”", "“대체 무슨 소리를 하는 건가?”"]], [["“We will come to our explanations, Percival, when the light is out of that window, and when I have had one little look at the rooms on each side of the library, and a peep at the staircase as well.”", "“설명은 차차 하기로 합시다, 퍼시벌. 저 창의 불이 꺼지고, 내가 서재 양옆 방들을 한번 살펴보고 계단도 슬쩍 엿본 뒤에.”"]], [["They slowly moved away, and the rest of the conversation between them (which had been conducted throughout in the same low tones) ceased to be audible.", "두 사람은 천천히 멀어져 갔고, 그 뒤로 이어진 이야기는 (처음부터 끝까지 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 나눈 터라) 더는 들리지 않았다."], ["It was no matter.", "상관없었다."], ["I had heard enough to determine me on justifying the Count’s opinion of my sharpness and my courage.", "나의 영민함과 용기에 대한 백작의 평가가 옳았음을 입증해 보이기로 마음먹기에는 이미 충분히 들었으니까."], ["Before the red sparks were out of sight in the darkness I had made up my mind that there should be a listener when those two men sat down to their talk—and that the listener, in spite of all the Count’s precautions to the contrary, should be myself.", "붉은 불똥들이 어둠 속으로 사라지기도 전에 나는 결심을 굳혔다. 저 두 사람이 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눌 때 엿듣는 자가 반드시 있어야 하며, 백작이 아무리 조심스레 대비하더라도 그 엿듣는 자는 바로 나여야 한다고."], ["I wanted but one motive to sanction the act to my own conscience, and to give me courage enough for performing it—and that motive I had.", "그 행위를 내 양심 앞에서 정당화하고 실행에 옮길 용기를 낼 동기 하나가 필요했을 뿐인데, 그 동기라면 내게 있었다."], ["Laura’s honour, Laura’s happiness—Laura’s life itself—might depend on my quick ears and my faithful memory to-night.", "로라의 명예, 로라의 행복—아니 로라의 목숨 그 자체가 오늘 밤 내 밝은 귀와 충실한 기억력에 달려 있을지도 몰랐다."]], [["I had heard the Count say that he meant to examine the rooms on each side of the library, and the staircase as well, before he entered on any explanation with Sir Percival.", "백작이 퍼시벌 경과 어떤 설명을 시작하기 전에 서재 양옆 방들과 계단까지 살펴보겠다고 말하는 것을 나는 들었다."], ["This expression of his intentions was necessarily sufficient to inform me that the library was the room in which he proposed that the conversation should take place.", "그 말만으로도 그가 이야기를 나눌 장소로 서재를 염두에 두고 있음을 알기에 충분했다."], ["The one moment of time which was long enough to bring me to that conclusion was also the moment which showed me a means of baffling his precautions—or, in other words, of hearing what he and Sir Percival said to each other, without the risk of descending at all into the lower regions of the house.", "그 결론에 이르기까지 걸린 그 짧은 한순간은, 동시에 그의 경계를 무력화할 방법—달리 말해 집 아래층으로 내려가는 위험을 전혀 무릅쓰지 않고도 그와 퍼시벌 경이 주고받는 말을 들을 방법—을 내게 일러 준 순간이기도 했다."]], [["In speaking of the rooms on the ground floor I have mentioned incidentally the verandah outside them, on which they all opened by means of French windows, extending from the cornice to the floor.", "일층 방들을 이야기하면서 나는 그 바깥의 베란다를 지나가는 말로 언급한 적이 있다. 방들은 모두 처마 장식에서 바닥까지 내려오는 프랑스식 창을 통해 그 베란다로 열려 있었다."], ["The top of this verandah was flat, the rain-water being carried off from it by pipes into tanks which helped to supply the house.", "이 베란다의 지붕은 평평했고, 빗물은 관을 타고 집에 물을 대는 물탱크로 흘러 들어갔다."], ["On the narrow leaden roof, which ran along past the bedrooms, and which was rather less, I should think, than three feet below the sills of the window, a row of flower-pots was ranged, with wide intervals between each pot—the whole being protected from falling in high winds by an ornamental iron railing along the edge of the roof.", "침실들 앞을 따라 뻗은 그 좁다란 납 지붕은 창틀에서 채 삼 피트가 안 되게 내려온 자리에 있었는데, 그 위에는 화분들이 서로 넉넉한 간격을 두고 한 줄로 놓여 있었고, 강풍에 떨어지지 않도록 지붕 가장자리를 따라 장식용 쇠 난간이 둘러쳐져 있었다."]], [["The plan which had now occurred to me was to get out at my sitting-room window on to this roof, to creep along noiselessly till I reached that part of it which was immediately over the library window, and to crouch down between the flower-pots, with my ear against the outer railing.", "이제 내게 떠오른 계획은 거실 창으로 빠져나가 그 지붕에 올라선 뒤, 소리 없이 기어가 서재 창 바로 위에 해당하는 자리에 이르러 화분들 사이에 몸을 웅크리고 바깥 난간에 귀를 대는 것이었다."], ["If Sir Percival and the Count sat and smoked to-night, as I had seen them sitting and smoking many nights before, with their chairs close at the open window, and their feet stretched on the zinc garden seats which were placed under the verandah, every word they said to each other above a whisper (and no long conversation, as we all know by experience, can be carried on IN a whisper) must inevitably reach my ears.", "만일 오늘 밤에도 퍼시벌 경과 백작이 여러 날 밤 그래 왔듯 열린 창가에 바싹 의자를 붙여 앉아 베란다 아래 놓인 아연 정원 의자에 발을 뻗은 채 담배를 피운다면, 속삭임보다 조금이라도 큰 소리로 주고받는 말은 (그리고 우리 모두 겪어서 알듯이 긴 대화를 속삭임만으로 나눌 수는 없다) 어김없이 내 귀에 닿을 터였다."], ["If, on the other hand, they chose to-night to sit far back inside the room, then the chances were that I should hear little or nothing—and in that case, I must run the far more serious risk of trying to outwit them downstairs.", "반면 오늘 밤 그들이 방 안쪽 깊숙이 앉기로 한다면 나는 거의 아무것도 듣지 못할 공산이 컸다. 그럴 경우에는 아래층에서 그들의 눈을 속이는 훨씬 위험한 모험을 감행해야 했다."]], [["Strongly as I was fortified in my resolution by the desperate nature of our situation, I hoped most fervently that I might escape this last emergency.", "우리가 처한 절박한 사정이 내 결심을 아무리 단단히 떠받쳐 주었어도, 나는 그 마지막 사태만은 피할 수 있기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["My courage was only a woman’s courage after all, and it was very near to failing me when I thought of trusting myself on the ground floor, at the dead of night, within reach of Sir Percival and the Count.", "내 용기란 결국 여자의 용기일 뿐이었고, 한밤중에 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 손이 닿는 곳에서 일층에 내 몸을 맡긴다는 생각을 하자 그 용기는 거의 무너질 지경이었다."]], [["I went softly back to my bedroom to try the safer experiment of the verandah roof first.", "나는 우선 더 안전한 베란다 지붕 쪽을 시도해 보려고 살며시 침실로 돌아갔다."]], [["A complete change in my dress was imperatively necessary for many reasons.", "여러 이유에서 옷을 완전히 갈아입는 일이 무엇보다 시급했다."], ["I took off my silk gown to begin with, because the slightest noise from it on that still night might have betrayed me.", "우선 비단 드레스를 벗었다. 이 고요한 밤에 그 옷에서 나는 아주 작은 소리도 나를 들키게 할 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["I next removed the white and cumbersome parts of my underclothing, and replaced them by a petticoat of dark flannel.", "다음으로 희고 부피 큰 속옷들을 벗고 짙은 색 플란넬 페티코트로 갈아입었다."], ["Over this I put my black travelling cloak, and pulled the hood on to my head.", "그 위에 검은 여행용 망토를 걸치고 두건을 머리에 뒤집어썼다."], ["In my ordinary evening costume I took up the room of three men at least.", "평소의 저녁 차림으로는 적어도 남자 셋 몫의 자리를 차지했다."], ["In my present dress, when it was held close about me, no man could have passed through the narrowest spaces more easily than I.", "지금 차림으로 옷을 몸에 바싹 여미면 아무리 좁은 틈이라도 나보다 수월하게 빠져나갈 남자는 없을 터였다."], ["The little breadth left on the roof of the verandah, between the flower-pots on one side and the wall and the windows of the house on the other, made this a serious consideration.", "한쪽에는 화분들이, 다른 쪽에는 집의 벽과 창이 있는 베란다 지붕 위의 얼마 안 되는 폭을 생각하면 이는 심각하게 따져 볼 문제였다."], ["If I knocked anything down, if I made the least noise, who could say what the consequences might be?", "무엇 하나라도 넘어뜨린다면, 아주 작은 소리라도 낸다면, 그 결과가 어떠할지 누가 알겠는가?"]], [["I only waited to put the matches near the candle before I extinguished it, and groped my way back into the sitting-room.", "나는 성냥을 초 가까이에 놓아둔 뒤에야 불을 끄고 더듬더듬 거실로 돌아갔다."], ["I locked that door, as I had locked my bedroom door—then quietly got out of the window, and cautiously set my feet on the leaden roof of the verandah.", "침실 문을 잠갔던 것처럼 그 문도 잠그고—그런 다음 조용히 창을 넘어 조심스레 베란다의 납 지붕에 발을 디뎠다."]], [["My two rooms were at the inner extremity of the new wing of the house in which we all lived, and I had five windows to pass before I could reach the position it was necessary to take up immediately over the library.", "내 두 방은 우리가 모두 지내는 새 별채의 가장 안쪽 끝에 있었고, 서재 바로 위의 자리에 이르려면 창 다섯 개를 지나야 했다."], ["The first window belonged to a spare room which was empty.", "첫 번째 창은 비어 있는 손님방의 것이었다."], ["The second and third windows belonged to Laura’s room.", "두 번째와 세 번째 창은 로라의 방이었다."], ["The fourth window belonged to Sir Percival’s room.", "네 번째 창은 퍼시벌 경의 방이었다."], ["The fifth belonged to the Countess’s room.", "다섯 번째는 백작부인의 방이었다."], ["The others, by which it was not necessary for me to pass, were the windows of the Count’s dressing-room, of the bathroom, and of the second empty spare room.", "내가 지날 필요가 없는 나머지 창들은 백작의 화장방과 욕실, 그리고 두 번째 빈 손님방의 창이었다."]], [["No sound reached my ears—the black blinding darkness of the night was all round me when I first stood on the verandah, except at that part of it which Madame Fosco’s window overlooked.", "아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 처음 베란다에 올라섰을 때 사방은 눈이 멀 듯 새까만 밤의 어둠뿐이었다—포스코 부인의 창이 내려다보는 그 한 곳만 빼고."], ["There, at the very place above the library to which my course was directed—there I saw a gleam of light!", "하필 내가 가려던 그 자리, 서재 바로 위 그곳에서 나는 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다!"], ["The Countess was not yet in bed.", "백작부인은 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 것이다."]], [["It was too late to draw back—it was no time to wait.", "물러서기에는 너무 늦었고 기다릴 겨를도 없었다."], ["I determined to go on at all hazards, and trust for security to my own caution and to the darkness of the night.", "나는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓰고 나아가되 오직 나 자신의 조심성과 밤의 어둠에 안전을 맡기기로 했다."], ["“For Laura’s sake!”", "“로라를 위해서!”"], ["I thought to myself, as I took the first step forward on the roof, with one hand holding my cloak close round me, and the other groping against the wall of the house.", "한 손으로는 망토를 몸에 바싹 여미고 다른 손으로는 집의 벽을 더듬으며 지붕 위로 첫걸음을 내디딜 때 나는 속으로 그렇게 되뇌었다."], ["It was better to brush close by the wall than to risk striking my feet against the flower-pots within a few inches of me, on the other side.", "몇 인치 옆에 있는 화분에 발이 부딪히는 위험을 무릅쓰느니 벽에 바싹 붙어 스치듯 가는 편이 나았다."]], [["I passed the dark window of the spare room, trying the leaden roof at each step with my foot before I risked resting my weight on it.", "어두운 손님방 창을 지났다. 한 발 한 발 내디딜 때마다 체중을 싣기 전에 먼저 발끝으로 납 지붕을 짚어 보았다."], ["I passed the dark windows of Laura’s room (“God bless her and keep her to-night!”).", "로라의 방의 어두운 창들을 지났다(“하느님, 오늘 밤 그 아이를 지켜 주소서!”)."], ["I passed the dark window of Sir Percival’s room.", "퍼시벌 경의 방의 어두운 창을 지났다."], ["Then I waited a moment, knelt down with my hands to support me, and so crept to my position, under the protection of the low wall between the bottom of the lighted window and the verandah roof.", "그런 다음 잠시 멈추었다가 두 손으로 몸을 지탱하며 무릎을 꿇고, 불 켜진 창 아래쪽과 베란다 지붕 사이의 나지막한 벽에 몸을 숨긴 채 목표한 자리까지 기어갔다."]], [["When I ventured to look up at the window itself I found that the top of it only was open, and that the blind inside was drawn down.", "큰맘 먹고 창을 올려다보니 윗부분만 열려 있고 안쪽 블라인드는 내려져 있었다."], ["While I was looking I saw the shadow of Madame Fosco pass across the white field of the blind—then pass slowly back again.", "보고 있는 사이 포스코 부인의 그림자가 블라인드의 흰 화폭을 가로질러 지나갔다가—다시 천천히 되돌아가는 것이 보였다."], ["Thus far she could not have heard me, or the shadow would surely have stopped at the blind, even if she had wanted courage enough to open the window and look out?", "여기까지는 그녀가 내 소리를 듣지 못한 것이다. 들었다면 창을 열고 내다볼 용기까지는 없었더라도 그림자는 분명 블라인드 앞에 멈춰 섰을 테니까."]], [["I placed myself sideways against the railing of the verandah—first ascertaining, by touching them, the position of the flower-pots on either side of me.", "나는 베란다 난간에 옆으로 몸을 붙였다. 먼저 양옆 화분의 위치를 손으로 더듬어 확인한 뒤였다."], ["There was room enough for me to sit between them and no more.", "그 사이에 내가 앉을 만한 자리가 딱 그만큼 있었고 그 이상은 없었다."], ["The sweet-scented leaves of the flower on my left hand just brushed my cheek as I lightly rested my head against the railing.", "난간에 머리를 살며시 기대자 왼쪽 화분에 핀 꽃의 향긋한 잎사귀가 내 뺨을 스쳤다."]], [["The first sounds that reached me from below were caused by the opening or closing (most probably the latter) of three doors in succession—the doors, no doubt, leading into the hall and into the rooms on each side of the library, which the Count had pledged himself to examine.", "아래에서 처음 들려온 소리는 문 세 개가 잇달아 열리거나 닫히는(십중팔구 닫히는) 소리였다. 백작이 살펴보겠다고 다짐한, 현관과 서재 양옆 방으로 통하는 문들이 분명했다."], ["The first object that I saw was the red spark again travelling out into the night from under the verandah, moving away towards my window, waiting a moment, and then returning to the place from which it had set out.", "처음 눈에 들어온 것은 다시 그 붉은 불똥이었다. 베란다 아래에서 밤 속으로 나와 내 창 쪽으로 옮겨 가더니 잠시 멎었다가 출발했던 자리로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“The devil take your restlessness!", "“가만 좀 못 있겠나!"], ["When do you mean to sit down?”", "대체 언제 앉을 셈인가?”"], ["growled Sir Percival’s voice beneath me.", "발밑에서 퍼시벌 경의 목소리가 으르렁거렸다."]], [["“Ouf!", "“후!"], ["how hot it is!”", "덥기도 하지!”"], ["said the Count, sighing and puffing wearily.", "백작이 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬고 씩씩거리며 말했다."]], [["His exclamation was followed by the scraping of the garden chairs on the tiled pavement under the verandah—the welcome sound which told me they were going to sit close at the window as usual.", "그 탄식에 뒤이어 베란다 아래 타일 바닥에 정원 의자를 끄는 소리가 났다. 여느 때처럼 창가에 바싹 붙어 앉으려 한다는 것을 알려 주는 반가운 소리였다."], ["So far the chance was mine.", "여기까지는 운이 내 편이었다."], ["The clock in the turret struck the quarter to twelve as they settled themselves in their chairs.", "두 사람이 의자에 자리를 잡을 때 탑의 시계가 열두 시 십오 분 전을 알렸다."], ["I heard Madame Fosco through the open window yawning, and saw her shadow pass once more across the white field of the blind.", "열린 창 너머로 포스코 부인이 하품하는 소리가 들렸고, 그녀의 그림자가 블라인드의 흰 화폭을 또 한 번 가로지르는 것이 보였다."]], [["Meanwhile, Sir Percival and the Count began talking together below, now and then dropping their voices a little lower than usual, but never sinking them to a whisper.", "그러는 동안 퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아래에서 이야기를 시작했다. 이따금 평소보다 목소리를 조금 낮추기는 했지만 속삭임까지 떨어뜨리지는 않았다."], ["The strangeness and peril of my situation, the dread, which I could not master, of Madame Fosco’s lighted window, made it difficult, almost impossible, for me, at first, to keep my presence of mind, and to fix my attention solely on the conversation beneath.", "내가 처한 상황의 기이함과 위험, 그리고 도무지 떨쳐지지 않는 포스코 부인의 불 켜진 창에 대한 두려움 탓에, 처음에는 정신을 붙들고 아래의 대화에만 주의를 모으기가 어려웠다. 아니, 거의 불가능했다."], ["For some minutes I could only succeed in gathering the general substance of it.", "몇 분 동안은 대화의 대강만 겨우 짚어 낼 수 있었다."], ["I understood the Count to say that the one window alight was his wife’s, that the ground floor of the house was quite clear, and that they might now speak to each other without fear of accidents.", "불이 켜진 그 한 창은 자기 아내의 방이고, 집 일층은 아무도 없이 말끔하니 이제 사고를 걱정하지 않고 이야기해도 된다고 백작이 말하는 것을 나는 알아들었다."], ["Sir Percival merely answered by upbraiding his friend with having unjustifiably slighted his wishes and neglected his interests all through the day.", "퍼시벌 경은 그저 하루 종일 자기 뜻을 부당하게 무시하고 자기 이익을 소홀히 했다고 벗을 나무랄 뿐이었다."], ["The Count thereupon defended himself by declaring that he had been beset by certain troubles and anxieties which had absorbed all his attention, and that the only safe time to come to an explanation was a time when they could feel certain of being neither interrupted nor overheard.", "그러자 백작은 온 신경을 앗아 간 어떤 골칫거리와 근심에 시달렸으며, 방해받거나 엿들릴 염려가 없다고 확신할 수 있는 때라야 안심하고 설명할 수 있다고 항변했다."], ["“We are at a serious crisis in our affairs, Percival,”", "“우리 일은 심각한 고비에 와 있소, 퍼시벌.”"], ["he said, “and if we are to decide on the future at all, we must decide secretly to-night.”", "그가 말했다. “앞날을 결정하려면 오늘 밤 은밀히 결정해야 하오.”"]], [["That sentence of the Count’s was the first which my attention was ready enough to master exactly as it was spoken.", "백작의 그 문장이야말로 내 주의력이 말 그대로 온전히 붙잡아 낸 첫 문장이었다."], ["From this point, with certain breaks and interruptions, my whole interest fixed breathlessly on the conversation, and I followed it word for word.", "이 대목부터는 몇 번의 끊김과 방해가 있기는 했지만 내 온 관심이 숨죽인 채 그 대화에 붙박였고, 나는 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 따라갔다."]], [["“Crisis?”", "“고비?”"], ["repeated Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 되뇌었다."], ["“It’s a worse crisis than you think for, I can tell you.”", "“자네가 생각하는 것보다 더 고약한 고비일세, 내 말해 두지.”"]], [["“So I should suppose, from your behaviour for the last day or two,”", "“지난 하루 이틀 그대의 처신을 보니 그러리라 짐작은 했소.”"], ["returned the other coolly.", "상대가 냉랭하게 대꾸했다."], ["“But wait a little.", "“허나 잠깐."], ["Before we advance to what I do NOT know, let us be quite certain of what I DO know.", "내가 모르는 데로 나아가기 전에, 내가 아는 것부터 확실히 해 둡시다."], ["Let us first see if I am right about the time that is past, before I make any proposal to you for the time that is to come.”", "앞으로 올 시간에 대해 무언가 제안하기 전에, 지나간 시간에 대해 내 짐작이 맞는지부터 봅시다.”"]], [["“Stop till I get the brandy and water.", "“브랜디에 물 좀 가져올 동안 잠깐."], ["Have some yourself.”", "자네도 좀 들게.”"]], [["“Thank you, Percival.", "“고맙소, 퍼시벌."], ["The cold water with pleasure, a spoon, and the basin of sugar.", "찬물을 주면 기꺼이 들겠소. 숟가락 하나와 설탕 그릇도."], ["Eau sucrée, my friend—nothing more.”", "오 쉬크레, 그러니까 설탕물 말이오, 벗이여—그 이상은 아무것도.”"]], [["“Sugar-and-water for a man of your age!—There!", "“자네 나이에 설탕물이라니!—자!"], ["mix your sickly mess.", "그 느끼한 물건이나 타 드시게."], ["You foreigners are all alike.”", "외국인들이란 다 똑같군.”"]], [["“Now listen, Percival.", "“자, 들으시오, 퍼시벌."], ["I will put our position plainly before you, as I understand it, and you shall say if I am right or wrong.", "내가 이해하는 대로 우리 처지를 있는 그대로 펼쳐 놓을 테니 맞는지 틀리는지 그대가 말해 주오."], ["You and I both came back to this house from the Continent with our affairs very seriously embarrassed—”", "그대와 나는 둘 다 형편이 몹시 궁색해진 채로 대륙에서 이 집으로 돌아왔소—”"]], [["“Cut it short!", "“짧게 하게!"], ["I wanted some thousands and you some hundreds, and without the money we were both in a fair way to go to the dogs together.", "나는 수천 파운드가, 자네는 수백 파운드가 필요했고, 그 돈이 없으면 우리 둘 다 사이좋게 파멸할 판이었지."], ["There’s the situation.", "사정은 그렇네."], ["Make what you can of it.", "알아서 요리해 보게."], ["Go on.”", "계속하게.”"]], [["“Well, Percival, in your own solid English words, you wanted some thousands and I wanted some hundreds, and the only way of getting them was for you to raise the money for your own necessity (with a small margin beyond for my poor little hundreds) by the help of your wife.", "“좋소, 퍼시벌. 그대의 든든한 영국식 말투를 빌리자면, 그대는 수천 파운드가 나는 수백 파운드가 필요했고, 그것을 마련할 유일한 길은 그대가 아내의 힘을 빌려 자기 급한 사정을 메울 돈을(내 가엾은 수백 파운드 몫의 여유를 조금 얹어서) 조달하는 것이었소."], ["What did I tell you about your wife on our way to England?—and what did I tell you again when we had come here, and when I had seen for myself the sort of woman Miss Halcombe was?”", "영국으로 오는 길에 내가 그대의 아내에 대해 뭐라고 말했소?—그리고 이곳에 와서 홀콤 양이 어떤 여자인지 내 눈으로 직접 본 뒤에 다시 뭐라고 말했소?”"]], [["“How should I know?", "“내가 그걸 어떻게 아나?"], ["You talked nineteen to the dozen, I suppose, just as usual.”", "늘 그렇듯 쉴 새 없이 지껄여 댔겠지.”"]], [["“I said this: Human ingenuity, my friend, has hitherto only discovered two ways in which a man can manage a woman.", "“나는 이렇게 말했소. 벗이여, 인간의 재간은 지금껏 남자가 여자를 다루는 방법을 단 두 가지밖에 찾아내지 못했소."], ["One way is to knock her down—a method largely adopted by the brutal lower orders of the people, but utterly abhorrent to the refined and educated classes above them.", "하나는 여자를 때려눕히는 것이오. 짐승 같은 하층민들이 널리 쓰는 방법이지만, 그 위에 있는 세련되고 교양 있는 계급에게는 도무지 혐오스러운 짓이지."], ["The other way (much longer, much more difficult, but in the end not less certain) is never to accept a provocation at a woman’s hands.", "다른 하나는(훨씬 오래 걸리고 훨씬 어렵지만 결국은 그에 못지않게 확실한데) 여자가 걸어오는 도발을 결코 받아 주지 않는 것이오."], ["It holds with animals, it holds with children, and it holds with women, who are nothing but children grown up.", "이 이치는 짐승에게도 통하고 아이에게도 통하며, 다 자란 아이나 다름없는 여자에게도 통하오."], ["Quiet resolution is the one quality the animals, the children, and the women all fail in.", "짐승도 아이도 여자도 하나같이 갖추지 못한 자질이 바로 조용한 결단력이오."], ["If they can once shake this superior quality in their master, they get the better of HIM. If they can never succeed in disturbing it, he gets the better of THEM.", "저들이 주인의 그 우월한 자질을 한 번이라도 흔들 수 있다면 저들이 그를 이기는 것이고, 결코 흔들지 못한다면 그가 저들을 이기는 것이오."], ["I said to you, Remember that plain truth when you want your wife to help you to the money.", "나는 그대에게 말했소. 아내에게 돈 문제를 도와 달라 할 때 그 명백한 진리를 기억하라고."], ["I said, Remember it doubly and trebly in the presence of your wife’s sister, Miss Halcombe.", "그리고 아내의 언니인 홀콤 양 앞에서는 그것을 두 배 세 배로 기억하라고 말했소."], ["Have you remembered it?", "그대는 기억했소?"], ["Not once in all the implications that have twisted themselves about us in this house.", "이 집에서 우리를 얽어 든 온갖 사태 가운데 단 한 번도 기억하지 않았소."], ["Every provocation that your wife and her sister could offer to you, you instantly accepted from them.", "그대의 아내와 그 언니가 걸어올 수 있는 도발이란 도발은 그대가 죄다 즉시 받아 주었소."], ["Your mad temper lost the signature to the deed, lost the ready money, set Miss Halcombe writing to the lawyer for the first time.”", "그 미친 성미 탓에 증서에 서명을 받지 못했고, 당장 쓸 현금을 놓쳤고, 홀콤 양이 처음으로 변호사에게 편지를 쓰게 만들었소.”"]], [["“First time!", "“처음이라니!"], ["Has she written again?”", "또 썼단 말인가?”"]], [["“Yes, she has written again to-day.”", "“그렇소, 오늘 또 썼소.”"]], [["A chair fell on the pavement of the verandah—fell with a crash, as if it had been kicked down.", "베란다 바닥에 의자가 쓰러졌다—발로 걷어차기라도 한 듯 요란한 소리를 내며 쓰러졌다."]], [["It was well for me that the Count’s revelation roused Sir Percival’s anger as it did.", "백작의 폭로가 그렇게 퍼시벌 경의 분노를 돋운 것은 내게 다행이었다."], ["On hearing that I had been once more discovered I started so that the railing against which I leaned cracked again.", "내 일이 또다시 발각되었음을 듣는 순간 나는 어찌나 놀랐는지 기대고 있던 난간이 다시 삐걱 소리를 냈다."], ["Had he followed me to the inn?", "그가 나를 따라 여관까지 왔던 걸까?"], ["Did he infer that I must have given my letters to Fanny when I told him I had none for the post-bag?", "우편 자루에 넣을 편지가 없다고 내가 말했을 때, 편지를 패니에게 건넸으리라 짐작한 걸까?"], ["Even if it was so, how could he have examined the letters when they had gone straight from my hand to the bosom of the girl’s dress?", "설령 그렇다 해도, 편지가 내 손에서 곧장 그 아이의 옷섶으로 들어갔는데 어떻게 그것들을 살펴볼 수 있었단 말인가?"]], [["“Thank your lucky star,”", "“그대의 행운의 별에 감사하시오.”"], ["I heard the Count say next, “that you have me in the house to undo the harm as fast as you do it.", "이어서 백작이 말하는 것이 들렸다. “그대가 저지르는 해악을 저지르는 족족 되돌려 놓을 내가 이 집에 있다는 것을 말이오."], ["Thank your lucky star that I said No when you were mad enough to talk of turning the key to-day on Miss Halcombe, as you turned it in your mischievous folly on your wife.", "그대가 미친 나머지, 짓궂은 어리석음으로 아내에게 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔듯 오늘 홀콤 양에게도 그리하자고 했을 때 내가 안 된다고 말한 것을, 그 행운의 별에 감사하시오."], ["Where are your eyes?", "그대의 눈은 어디에 달렸소?"], ["Can you look at Miss Halcombe and not see that she has the foresight and the resolution of a man?", "홀콤 양을 보고도 그 여인이 남자의 선견지명과 결단력을 지녔다는 걸 모르겠소?"], ["With that woman for my friend I would snap these fingers of mine at the world.", "그 여인을 내 벗으로 둘 수 있다면 나는 이 손가락을 튕겨 온 세상을 비웃겠소."], ["With that woman for my enemy, I, with all my brains and experience—I, Fosco, cunning as the devil himself, as you have told me a hundred times—I walk, in your English phrase, upon egg-shells!", "그 여인이 내 적이라면, 이 두뇌와 경험을 다 갖춘 나, 그대가 백 번도 더 말했듯 악마만큼이나 교활한 이 포스코조차도—그대들 영국 말로—달걀 껍데기 위를 걷는 심정이오!"], ["And this grand creature—I drink her health in my sugar-and-water—this grand creature, who stands in the strength of her love and her courage, firm as a rock, between us two and that poor, flimsy, pretty blonde wife of yours—this magnificent woman, whom I admire with all my soul, though I oppose her in your interests and in mine, you drive to extremities as if she was no sharper and no bolder than the rest of her sex.", "그런데 이 대단한 여인을—나는 설탕물로 그 건강을 위해 축배를 드오—사랑과 용기의 힘으로 바위처럼 굳건히 서서 우리 두 사람과 그대의 저 가엾고 여리고 어여쁜 금발 아내 사이를 가로막고 선 이 대단한 여인을, 내가 그대의 이익과 나의 이익을 위해 맞서고는 있으나 온 영혼으로 감탄해 마지않는 이 훌륭한 여인을, 그대는 마치 여느 여자들보다 더 영민할 것도 더 대담할 것도 없다는 듯이 극단으로 몰아붙이고 있소."], ["Percival!", "퍼시벌!"], ["Percival!", "퍼시벌!"], ["you deserve to fail, and you HAVE failed.”", "그대는 실패해 마땅하고, 또 실제로 실패했소.”"]], [["There was a pause.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["I write the villain’s words about myself because I mean to remember them—because I hope yet for the day when I may speak out once for all in his presence, and cast them back one by one in his teeth.", "나에 대한 저 악당의 말을 여기 적어 두는 것은 그것을 기억하려 함이다. 언젠가 그의 면전에서 단 한 번 속 시원히 말하고, 그 말들을 하나하나 그의 얼굴에 되던져 줄 날이 오기를 아직 바라기 때문이다."]], [["Sir Percival was the first to break the silence again.", "침묵을 다시 깬 쪽은 퍼시벌 경이었다."]], [["“Yes, yes, bully and bluster as much as you like,”", "“그래, 그래, 실컷 윽박지르고 큰소리치게.”"], ["he said sulkily; “the difficulty about the money is not the only difficulty.", "그가 뾰로통하게 말했다. “돈 문제만 문제가 아닐세."], ["You would be for taking strong measures with the women yourself—if you knew as much as I do.”", "내가 아는 만큼 자네도 안다면—자네야말로 그 여인들에게 강경한 수를 쓰자고 할 걸세.”"]], [["“We will come to that second difficulty all in good time,”", "“그 두 번째 골칫거리는 때가 되면 다루기로 합시다.”"], ["rejoined the Count.", "백작이 응수했다."], ["“You may confuse yourself, Percival, as much as you please, but you shall not confuse me.", "“퍼시벌, 그대는 마음껏 스스로 갈피를 잃어도 좋으나 나까지 갈피를 잃게 하지는 못하오."], ["Let the question of the money be settled first.", "돈 문제부터 매듭짓읍시다."], ["Have I convinced your obstinacy?", "내가 그대의 고집을 꺾었소?"], ["have I shown you that your temper will not let you help yourself?—Or must I go back, and (as you put it in your dear straightforward English) bully and bluster a little more?”", "그 성미가 그대 자신을 돕지 못하게 한다는 걸 내가 보여 주었소?—아니면 다시 돌아가서 (그대의 사랑스러운 직설적 영국 말로 하자면) 좀 더 윽박지르고 큰소리쳐야겠소?”"]], [["“Pooh!", "“쳇!"], ["It’s easy enough to grumble at ME.", "나를 타박하기야 쉽지."], ["Say what is to be done—that’s a little harder.”", "어떻게 해야 하는지 말해 보게—그게 좀 더 어려운 일이니.”"]], [["“Is it?", "“그렇소?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["This is what is to be done: You give up all direction in the business from to-night—you leave it for the future in my hands only.", "해야 할 일은 이것이오. 그대는 오늘 밤부터 이 일에 대한 지휘를 전부 내려놓고, 앞으로는 오직 내 손에만 맡기시오."], ["I am talking to a Practical British man—ha?", "나는 지금 실무적인 영국 신사와 이야기하고 있소—그렇지 않소?"], ["Well, Practical, will that do for you?”", "자, 실무가 양반, 그만하면 되겠소?”"]], [["“What do you propose if I leave it all to you?”", "“내가 자네에게 전부 맡긴다면 자네는 무엇을 하겠다는 건가?”"]], [["“Answer me first.", "“먼저 대답부터 하시오."], ["Is it to be in my hands or not?”", "내 손에 맡길 거요, 말 거요?”"]], [["“Say it is in your hands—what then?”", "“맡긴다고 치세—그다음은?”"]], [["“A few questions, Percival, to begin with.", "“우선 몇 가지 물어봅시다, 퍼시벌."], ["I must wait a little yet, to let circumstances guide me, and I must know, in every possible way, what those circumstances are likely to be.", "나는 아직 조금 더 기다리며 사정이 이끄는 대로 따라야 하고, 그 사정이 어떻게 흘러갈지를 가능한 모든 방면에서 알아야 하오."], ["There is no time to lose.", "지체할 시간이 없소."], ["I have told you already that Miss Halcombe has written to the lawyer to-day for the second time.”", "홀콤 양이 오늘 두 번째로 변호사에게 편지를 썼다는 건 이미 말했소.”"]], [["“How did you find it out?", "“그걸 어떻게 알아냈나?"], ["What did she say?”", "뭐라고 썼던가?”"]], [["“If I told you, Percival, we should only come back at the end to where we are now.", "“그걸 말해 주면, 퍼시벌, 우리는 결국 지금 이 자리로 되돌아올 뿐이오."], ["Enough that I have found it out—and the finding has caused that trouble and anxiety which made me so inaccessible to you all through to-day.", "내가 알아냈다는 것으로 족하오—그리고 그것을 알아내느라 생긴 골칫거리와 근심 때문에 오늘 하루 종일 내가 그대에게 그토록 붙잡히지 않았던 거요."], ["Now, to refresh my memory about your affairs—it is some time since I talked them over with you.", "자, 그대의 사정을 되짚어 봅시다—함께 그 일을 논한 지도 꽤 되었으니."], ["The money has been raised, in the absence of your wife’s signature, by means of bills at three months—raised at a cost that makes my poverty-stricken foreign hair stand on end to think of it!", "아내의 서명이 없는 채로 돈은 석 달짜리 어음으로 마련되었소—생각만 해도 이 가난뱅이 외국인의 머리카락이 곤두설 만큼 비싼 값에!"], ["When the bills are due, is there really and truly no earthly way of paying them but by the help of your wife?”", "어음 만기가 돌아오면, 정말이지 참으로 그대 아내의 도움 말고는 그것을 갚을 길이 이 세상에 없소?”"]], [["“None.”", "“없네.”"]], [["“What!", "“무어라!"], ["You have no money at the bankers?”", "은행에 돈이 한 푼도 없단 말이오?”"]], [["“A few hundreds, when I want as many thousands.”", "“수천이 필요한 판에 수백 파운드쯤 있지.”"]], [["“Have you no other security to borrow upon?”", "“빌릴 만한 다른 담보도 없소?”"]], [["“Not a shred.”", "“실오라기 하나 없네.”"]], [["“What have you actually got with your wife at the present moment?”", "“지금 이 순간 아내에게서 실제로 받고 있는 것은 무엇이오?”"]], [["“Nothing but the interest of her twenty thousand pounds—barely enough to pay our daily expenses.”", "“이만 파운드의 이자뿐이지—하루하루 쓰기에도 빠듯하네.”"]], [["“What do you expect from your wife?”", "“아내에게서 기대하는 것은 무엇이오?”"]], [["“Three thousand a year when her uncle dies.”", "“삼촌이 죽으면 연 삼천 파운드.”"]], [["“A fine fortune, Percival.", "“훌륭한 재산이오, 퍼시벌."], ["What sort of a man is this uncle?", "그 삼촌이란 사람은 어떤 이오?"], ["Old?”", "늙었소?”"]], [["“No—neither old nor young.”", "“아니—늙지도 젊지도 않네.”"]], [["“A good-tempered, freely-living man?", "“성정이 무던하고 마음 편히 사는 사람이오?"], ["Married?", "결혼은 했소?"], ["No—I think my wife told me, not married.”", "아니지—내 아내가 결혼하지 않았다고 말해 준 것 같은데.”"]], [["“Of course not.", "“물론 안 했네."], ["If he was married, and had a son, Lady Glyde would not be next heir to the property.", "결혼해서 아들이라도 있었다면 글라이드 부인이 그 재산의 다음 상속인이 될 리 없지."], ["I’ll tell you what he is.", "그가 어떤 위인인지 말해 주지."], ["He’s a maudlin, twaddling, selfish fool, and bores everybody who comes near him about the state of his health.”", "눈물이나 짜고 시시껄렁한 소리나 늘어놓는 이기적인 바보인 데다, 곁에 오는 사람마다 붙잡고 제 건강 타령으로 진저리를 내게 하는 자일세.”"]], [["“Men of that sort, Percival, live long, and marry malevolently when you least expect it.", "“그런 부류는 오래 사오, 퍼시벌. 게다가 전혀 뜻하지 않을 때 심술궂게 장가를 들지."], ["I don’t give you much, my friend, for your chance of the three thousand a year.", "벗이여, 나는 그 연 삼천 파운드에 대한 그대의 몫을 그리 높이 쳐 주지 않겠소."], ["Is there nothing more that comes to you from your wife?”", "아내에게서 그대에게 오는 것이 그 밖에는 없소?”"]], [["“Nothing.”", "“없네.”"]], [["“Absolutely nothing?”", "“정말 아무것도?”"]], [["“Absolutely nothing—except in case of her death.”", "“정말 아무것도—아내가 죽을 경우를 빼면.”"]], [["“Aha!", "“아하!"], ["in the case of her death.”", "아내가 죽을 경우라.”"]], [["There was another pause.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["The Count moved from the verandah to the gravel walk outside.", "백작이 베란다에서 바깥 자갈길로 자리를 옮겼다."], ["I knew that he had moved by his voice.", "목소리로 그가 움직였음을 알았다."], ["“The rain has come at last,”", "“드디어 비가 오는군.”"], ["I heard him say. It had come.", "그가 말하는 것이 들렸다."], ["The state of my cloak showed that it had been falling thickly for some little time.", "정말 비가 오고 있었다. 내 망토의 상태를 보니 이미 꽤 오랫동안 굵게 내리고 있었던 모양이었다."]], [["The Count went back under the verandah—I heard the chair creak beneath his weight as he sat down in it again.", "백작이 베란다 아래로 돌아왔다—그가 다시 앉을 때 그 무게에 의자가 삐걱거리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Well, Percival,”", "“자, 퍼시벌.”"], ["he said, “and in the case of Lady Glyde’s death, what do you get then?”", "그가 말했다. “글라이드 부인이 죽을 경우에는 그대가 무엇을 얻소?”"]], [["“If she leaves no children——”", "“아내가 자식을 남기지 않는다면——”"]], [["“Which she is likely to do?”", "“그럴 법하오?”"]], [["“Which she is not in the least likely to do——”", "“그럴 법이라곤 조금도 없네——”"]], [["“Yes?”", "“그러면?”"]], [["“Why, then I get her twenty thousand pounds.”", "“그야, 아내의 이만 파운드가 내게 오지.”"]], [["“Paid down?”", "“즉시 지급으로?”"]], [["“Paid down.”", "“즉시 지급으로.”"]], [["They were silent once more.", "두 사람은 또 한 번 잠잠해졌다."], ["As their voices ceased Madame Fosco’s shadow darkened the blind again.", "목소리가 그치자 포스코 부인의 그림자가 다시 블라인드를 어둡게 물들였다."], ["Instead of passing this time, it remained, for a moment, quite still.", "이번에는 지나가는 대신 잠시 그대로 멈춰 섰다."], ["I saw her fingers steal round the corner of the blind, and draw it on one side.", "그녀의 손가락이 블라인드 모서리로 슬며시 다가와 한쪽으로 젖히는 것이 보였다."], ["The dim white outline of her face, looking out straight over me, appeared behind the window.", "곧장 나를 향해 내다보는 그녀의 희끄무레한 얼굴 윤곽이 창 뒤에 나타났다."], ["I kept still, shrouded from head to foot in my black cloak.", "나는 검은 망토로 머리부터 발끝까지 뒤집어쓴 채 꼼짝하지 않았다."], ["The rain, which was fast wetting me, dripped over the glass, blurred it, and prevented her from seeing anything.", "나를 빠르게 적셔 오던 비가 유리창에 흘러내려 뿌옇게 흐려 놓은 덕에 그녀는 아무것도 보지 못했다. “비가 또 오네!”"], ["“More rain!” I heard her say to herself.", "그녀가 혼잣말하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["She dropped the blind, and I breathed again freely.", "그녀는 블라인드를 내렸고, 나는 다시 마음 놓고 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["The talk went on below me, the Count resuming it this time.", "아래에서 이야기가 이어졌다. 이번에는 백작이 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“Percival!", "“퍼시벌!"], ["do you care about your wife?”", "그대는 아내를 아끼오?”"]], [["“Fosco!", "“포스코!"], ["that’s rather a downright question.”", "그건 좀 노골적인 질문이군.”"]], [["“I am a downright man, and I repeat it.”", "“나는 노골적인 사람이오. 그러니 다시 묻겠소.”"]], [["“Why the devil do you look at me in that way?”", "“대체 왜 나를 그런 눈으로 보는 건가?”"]], [["“You won’t answer me?", "“대답하지 않겠다?"], ["Well, then, let us say your wife dies before the summer is out——”", "좋소, 그렇다면 그대의 아내가 이 여름이 가기 전에 죽는다고 칩시다——”"]], [["“Drop it, Fosco!”", "“그만두게, 포스코!”"]], [["“Let us say your wife dies——”", "“그대의 아내가 죽는다고 칩시다——”"]], [["“Drop it, I tell you!”", "“그만두라니까!”"]], [["“In that case, you would gain twenty thousand pounds, and you would lose——”", "“그럴 경우 그대는 이만 파운드를 얻고, 잃는 것은——”"]], [["“I should lose the chance of three thousand a year.”", "“연 삼천 파운드의 가망을 잃겠지.”"]], [["“The REMOTE chance, Percival—the remote chance only.", "“그 아득한 가망이오, 퍼시벌—그저 아득한 가망일 뿐."], ["And you want money, at once.", "게다가 그대에게는 지금 당장 돈이 필요하오."], ["In your position the gain is certain—the loss doubtful.”", "그대의 처지에서 얻는 것은 확실하고—잃는 것은 불확실하오.”"]], [["“Speak for yourself as well as for me.", "“내 얘기만 하지 말고 자네 얘기도 하게."], ["Some of the money I want has been borrowed for you.", "내게 필요한 돈 가운데 일부는 자네를 위해 빌린 걸세."], ["And if you come to gain, my wife’s death would be ten thousand pounds in your wife’s pocket.", "그리고 얻는 것을 따지자면, 내 아내가 죽으면 자네 아내 주머니에 만 파운드가 들어가지."], ["Sharp as you are, you seem to have conveniently forgotten Madame Fosco’s legacy.", "그렇게 영민한 사람이 포스코 부인의 유산은 편리하게 잊어버린 모양이군."], ["Don’t look at me in that way!", "그런 눈으로 나를 보지 말게!"], ["I won’t have it!", "참을 수가 없네!"], ["What with your looks and your questions, upon my soul, you make my flesh creep!”", "자네의 그 눈초리에 그런 질문까지, 맹세코 내 살갗에 소름이 다 돋는군!”"]], [["“Your flesh?", "“그대의 살갗?"], ["Does flesh mean conscience in English?", "영국 말에서는 살갗이 곧 양심을 뜻하오?"], ["I speak of your wife’s death as I speak of a possibility. Why not?", "나는 그대 아내의 죽음을 하나의 가능성으로서 말할 뿐이오."], ["The respectable lawyers who scribble-scrabble your deeds and your wills look the deaths of living people in the face.", "어찌 못 하겠소? 그대의 증서와 유언장을 끄적거리는 점잖은 변호사들도 살아 있는 사람들의 죽음을 정면으로 마주보오."], ["Do lawyers make your flesh creep? Why should I?", "변호사들이 그대의 살갗에 소름을 돋게 하오?"], ["It is my business to-night to clear up your position beyond the possibility of mistake, and I have now done it.", "그런데 어찌 내가 그러겠소? 오늘 밤 내 할 일은 그대의 처지를 한 치의 오해도 없도록 밝히는 것이고, 이제 그 일을 마쳤소."], ["Here is your position.", "그대의 처지는 이러하오."], ["If your wife lives, you pay those bills with her signature to the parchment.", "아내가 살아 있으면 그대는 그 양피지에 아내의 서명을 받아 어음을 갚소."], ["If your wife dies, you pay them with her death.”", "아내가 죽으면 그대는 아내의 죽음으로 그것을 갚소.”"]], [["As he spoke the light in Madame Fosco’s room was extinguished, and the whole second floor of the house was now sunk in darkness.", "그가 말하는 사이 포스코 부인의 방 불이 꺼졌고, 이제 집의 이층 전체가 어둠에 잠겼다."]], [["“Talk!", "“말!"], ["talk!”", "말뿐이군!”"], ["grumbled Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 투덜거렸다."], ["“One would think, to hear you, that my wife’s signature to the deed was got already.”", "“자네 말을 듣고 있자면 내 아내의 서명을 이미 증서에 받아 낸 것 같겠어.”"]], [["“You have left the matter in my hands,”", "“이 일을 내 손에 맡겨 두지 않았소.”"], ["retorted the Count, “and I have more than two months before me to turn round in.", "백작이 되받았다."], ["Say no more about it, if you please, for the present.", "“그리고 내게는 몸을 돌릴 시간이 두 달 넘게 있소."], ["When the bills are due, you will see for yourself if my ‘talk!", "부디 지금은 그 얘기를 더 하지 맙시다."], ["talk!’ is worth something, or if it is not.", "어음 만기가 되면 나의 ‘말!"], ["And now, Percival, having done with the money matters for to-night, I can place my attention at your disposal, if you wish to consult me on that second difficulty which has mixed itself up with our little embarrassments, and which has so altered you for the worse, that I hardly know you again.", "말뿐!’이 값어치가 있는지 없는지 그대 눈으로 직접 보게 될 거요. 자, 퍼시벌, 오늘 밤 돈 문제는 마무리했으니, 우리의 자잘한 곤경에 뒤엉킨 데다 그대를 알아보기 힘들 만큼 나쁘게 바꿔 놓은 그 두 번째 골칫거리를 내게 상의하고 싶다면 이제 내 주의를 그대에게 내어 주겠소."], ["Speak, my friend—and pardon me if I shock your fiery national tastes by mixing myself a second glass of sugar-and-water.”", "말해 보시오, 벗이여—그리고 내가 설탕물을 두 잔째 타 마셔 그대의 격렬한 애국적 취향에 충격을 준다면 용서하시오.”"]], [["“It’s very well to say speak,”", "“말해 보라기야 쉽지.”"], ["replied Sir Percival, in a far more quiet and more polite tone than he had yet adopted, “but it’s not so easy to know how to begin.”", "퍼시벌 경이 여태 쓴 적 없는 훨씬 차분하고 정중한 어조로 대답했다. “허나 어디서부터 시작해야 할지 알기가 그리 쉽지 않네.”"]], [["“Shall I help you?”", "“내가 도와줄까요?”"], ["suggested the Count.", "백작이 넌지시 말했다."], ["“Shall I give this private difficulty of yours a name?", "“그대의 그 은밀한 골칫거리에 이름을 붙여 볼까요?"], ["What if I call it—Anne Catherick?”", "내가 그것을—앤 캐서릭이라 부른다면?”"]], [["“Look here, Fosco, you and I have known each other for a long time, and if you have helped me out of one or two scrapes before this, I have done the best I could to help you in return, as far as money would go.", "“이보게 포스코, 자네와 나는 서로 알고 지낸 지 오래고, 자네가 전에 한두 번 내 곤경에서 나를 건져 주었다면 나도 돈이 닿는 한 힘껏 자네를 도와 갚았네."], ["We have made as many friendly sacrifices, on both sides, as men could, but we have had our secrets from each other, of course—haven’t we?”", "사람으로서 할 수 있는 만큼 서로 우정 어린 희생을 치러 왔지만, 물론 서로에게 비밀은 있어 왔지—그렇지 않은가?”"]], [["“You have had a secret from me, Percival.", "“비밀을 지녀 온 쪽은 그대요, 퍼시벌."], ["There is a skeleton in your cupboard here at Blackwater Park that has peeped out in these last few days at other people besides yourself.”", "이 블랙워터 파크의 그대 벽장 속에는 해골이 하나 있고, 지난 며칠 사이 그것이 그대 말고 다른 사람들에게도 얼굴을 내밀었소.”"]], [["“Well, suppose it has.", "“그렇다고 치세."], ["If it doesn’t concern you, you needn’t be curious about it, need you?”", "자네와 상관없는 일이라면 궁금해할 것 없지 않은가?”"]], [["“Do I look curious about it?”", "“내가 궁금해하는 얼굴로 보이오?”"]], [["“Yes, you do.”", "“그래, 그렇게 보이네.”"]], [["“So! so!", "“그렇군!"], ["my face speaks the truth, then?", "그렇군! 그렇다면 내 얼굴은 진실을 말하고 있구려?"], ["What an immense foundation of good there must be in the nature of a man who arrives at my age, and whose face has not yet lost the habit of speaking the truth!—Come, Glyde!", "내 나이에 이르러서도 얼굴이 진실을 말하는 버릇을 아직 잃지 않은 사람이라면, 그 천성에 얼마나 어마어마한 선의 토대가 깔려 있는 것이겠소!—자, 글라이드! 서로 솔직해집시다."], ["let us be candid one with the other. This secret of yours has sought me: I have not sought it.", "그대의 이 비밀은 제가 나를 찾아온 것이지 내가 그것을 찾아간 것이 아니오."], ["Let us say I am curious—do you ask me, as your old friend, to respect your secret, and to leave it, once for all, in your own keeping?”", "내가 궁금해한다고 칩시다—그대는 옛 벗인 내게, 그대의 비밀을 존중하여 그것을 영영 그대 자신의 몫으로 남겨 두라고 청하는 거요?”"]], [["“Yes—that’s just what I do ask.”", "“그래—바로 그걸 청하는 걸세.”"]], [["“Then my curiosity is at an end.", "“그렇다면 내 호기심은 여기서 끝이오."], ["It dies in me from this moment.”", "이 순간부터 내 안에서 죽었소.”"]], [["“Do you really mean that?”", "“정말 그렇게 하겠다는 건가?”"]], [["“What makes you doubt me?”", "“무엇 때문에 나를 의심하오?”"]], [["“I have had some experience, Fosco, of your roundabout ways, and I am not so sure that you won’t worm it out of me after all.”", "“포스코, 나는 자네의 그 에두르는 수법을 겪어 봐서 아네. 결국 자네가 나에게서 그걸 캐내지 않으리라고는 그리 자신할 수가 없어.”"]], [["The chair below suddenly creaked again—I felt the trellis-work pillar under me shake from top to bottom.", "아래에서 의자가 갑자기 다시 삐걱거렸다—내 밑의 격자 기둥이 위에서 아래까지 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["The Count had started to his feet, and had struck it with his hand in indignation.", "백작이 벌떡 일어서면서 분에 못 이겨 그 기둥을 손으로 내리친 것이었다."]], [["“Percival!", "“퍼시벌!"], ["Percival!”", "퍼시벌!”"], ["he cried passionately, “do you know me no better than that?", "그가 격정에 차서 외쳤다. “그대는 나를 그것밖에 모르오?"], ["Has all your experience shown you nothing of my character yet?", "그 오랜 세월을 겪고도 내 성품에 대해 아직 아무것도 알지 못했단 말이오?"], ["I am a man of the antique type!", "나는 옛 시대의 인간이오!"], ["I am capable of the most exalted acts of virtue—when I have the chance of performing them.", "나는 가장 드높은 덕행을 능히 행할 수 있는 사람이오—그럴 기회가 주어지기만 한다면."], ["It has been the misfortune of my life that I have had few chances.", "그런 기회가 별로 없었다는 것이 내 생애의 불운이었소."], ["My conception of friendship is sublime!", "우정에 대한 내 관념은 숭고하오!"], ["Is it my fault that your skeleton has peeped out at me?", "그대의 해골이 내게 얼굴을 내민 것이 내 탓이오?"], ["Why do I confess my curiosity?", "내가 왜 내 호기심을 고백하겠소?"], ["You poor superficial Englishman, it is to magnify my own self-control.", "이 가엾고 얄팍한 영국인이여, 그것은 나 자신의 자제력을 더욱 돋보이게 하려 함이오."], ["I could draw your secret out of you, if I liked, as I draw this finger out of the palm of my hand—you know I could!", "마음만 먹으면 나는 이 손가락을 손바닥에서 빼내듯 그대에게서 비밀을 뽑아낼 수 있소—그대도 알지 않소!"], ["But you have appealed to my friendship, and the duties of friendship are sacred to me.", "허나 그대가 내 우정에 호소했고, 우정의 의무란 내게 신성한 것이오. 보시오!"], ["See! I trample my base curiosity under my feet.", "나는 내 천한 호기심을 발밑에 짓밟아 버리오."], ["My exalted sentiments lift me above it.", "나의 드높은 감정이 나를 그 위로 들어 올리는 것이오."], ["Recognise them, Percival!", "그것을 알아보시오, 퍼시벌!"], ["imitate them, Percival!", "그것을 본받으시오, 퍼시벌!"], ["Shake hands—I forgive you.”", "악수합시다—내 그대를 용서하오.”"]], [["His voice faltered over the last words—faltered, as if he were actually shedding tears!", "마지막 말에서 그의 목소리가 떨렸다. 정말로 눈물이라도 흘리는 것처럼 떨렸다!"]], [["Sir Percival confusedly attempted to excuse himself, but the Count was too magnanimous to listen to him.", "퍼시벌 경이 어리둥절해하며 변명을 해 보려 했으나, 백작은 너무나 아량이 넓어 그의 말을 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["“No!” he said.", "“아니오!”"], ["“When my friend has wounded me, I can pardon him without apologies.", "그가 말했다. “벗이 나를 상하게 했을 때, 나는 사과 없이도 그를 용서할 수 있소."], ["Tell me, in plain words, do you want my help?”", "꾸밈없는 말로 말해 보시오. 내 도움이 필요하오?”"]], [["“Yes, badly enough.”", "“그래, 절실하네.”"]], [["“And you can ask for it without compromising yourself?”", "“그러면서도 자신을 위태롭게 하지 않고 도움을 청할 수 있겠소?”"]], [["“I can try, at any rate.”", "“적어도 해 볼 수는 있네.”"]], [["“Try, then.”", "“그럼 해 보시오.”"]], [["“Well, this is how it stands:—I told you to-day that I had done my best to find Anne Catherick, and failed.”", "“좋아, 사정은 이렇네.—오늘 내가 앤 캐서릭을 찾으려고 온갖 애를 다 썼지만 실패했다고 자네에게 말했지.”"]], [["“Yes, you did.”", "“그랬소.”"]], [["“Fosco!", "“포스코!"], ["I’m a lost man if I DON’T find her.”", "그 여자를 찾지 못하면 나는 파멸한 사람일세.”"]], [["“Ha!", "“허!"], ["Is it so serious as that?”", "그 정도로 심각하오?”"]], [["A little stream of light travelled out under the verandah, and fell over the gravel-walk.", "가느다란 빛줄기가 베란다 아래로 흘러나와 자갈길 위에 떨어졌다."], ["The Count had taken the lamp from the inner part of the room to see his friend clearly by the light of it.", "백작이 방 안쪽에서 등을 가져와 그 불빛으로 벗의 얼굴을 똑똑히 보려는 것이었다."]], [["“Yes!” he said.", "“그렇군!”"], ["“Your face speaks the truth this time.", "그가 말했다. “이번에는 그대의 얼굴이 진실을 말하는구려."], ["Serious, indeed—as serious as the money matters themselves.”", "정말이지 심각하오—돈 문제만큼이나 심각하오.”"]], [["“More serious.", "“그보다 더 심각하네."], ["As true as I sit here, more serious!”", "내가 여기 앉아 있는 게 사실인 것만큼이나 확실하게, 그보다 더 심각해!”"]], [["The light disappeared again and the talk went on.", "불빛이 다시 사라졌고 이야기는 계속되었다."]], [["“I showed you the letter to my wife that Anne Catherick hid in the sand,”", "“앤 캐서릭이 모래 속에 숨겨 둔, 내 아내 앞으로 쓴 그 편지를 자네에게 보여 주지 않았나.”"], ["Sir Percival continued.", "퍼시벌 경이 말을 이었다."], ["“There’s no boasting in that letter, Fosco—she DOES know the Secret.”", "“그 편지에는 허풍 같은 건 없어, 포스코. 그 애는 정말로 그 비밀을 알고 있단 말일세.”"]], [["“Say as little as possible, Percival, in my presence, of the Secret.", "“내 앞에서는 그 비밀 이야기를 되도록 적게 하게, 퍼시벌."], ["Does she know it from you?”", "그 여자가 자네에게서 그것을 알았나?”"]], [["“No, from her mother.”", "“아니, 제 어미한테서.”"]], [["“Two women in possession of your private mind—bad, bad, bad, my friend!", "“여자 둘이 자네의 은밀한 속내를 쥐고 있다니—나쁘군, 나쁘군, 나쁘군, 이보게!"], ["One question here, before we go any farther.", "더 나아가기 전에 여기서 한 가지만 묻겠네."], ["The motive of your shutting up the daughter in the asylum is now plain enough to me, but the manner of her escape is not quite so clear.", "자네가 그 딸을 정신병원에 가둔 동기는 이제 내게 충분히 분명해졌네만, 그 여자가 빠져나온 경위는 그다지 분명하지가 않아."], ["Do you suspect the people in charge of her of closing their eyes purposely, at the instance of some enemy who could afford to make it worth their while?”", "그 여자를 맡고 있던 자들이, 값을 넉넉히 치를 만한 어떤 적의 사주를 받고 일부러 눈을 감아 주었다고 의심하나?”"]], [["“No, she was the best-behaved patient they had—and, like fools, they trusted her.", "“아니, 그 애는 거기서 제일 얌전한 환자였어—그래서 바보같이 그 애를 믿어 버린 거지."], ["She’s just mad enough to be shut up, and just sane enough to ruin me when she’s at large—if you understand that?”", "가둬 둘 만큼은 미쳤고, 풀려나 있으면 나를 망칠 만큼은 멀쩡해—무슨 말인지 알겠나?”"]], [["“I do understand it.", "“알겠네."], ["Now, Percival, come at once to the point, and then I shall know what to do.", "자, 퍼시벌, 이제 곧장 요점으로 들어가게. 그래야 내가 무엇을 해야 할지 알지."], ["Where is the danger of your position at the present moment?”", "지금 이 순간 자네 처지에서 위험은 어디에 있나?”"]], [["“Anne Catherick is in this neighbourhood, and in communication with Lady Glyde—there’s the danger, plain enough.", "“앤 캐서릭이 이 근방에 있고, 글라이드 부인과 연락을 주고받고 있어—위험은 거기 있지, 뻔하지 않나."], ["Who can read the letter she hid in the sand, and not see that my wife is in possession of the Secret, deny it as she may?”", "그 애가 모래 속에 숨긴 그 편지를 읽고서, 내 아내가 아무리 아니라고 잡아떼도 그 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 걸 알아채지 못할 사람이 어디 있겠나?”"]], [["“One moment, Percival.", "“잠깐, 퍼시벌."], ["If Lady Glyde does know the Secret, she must know also that it is a compromising secret for you.", "글라이드 부인이 정말로 그 비밀을 안다면, 그것이 자네를 옭아맬 비밀이라는 것도 알 테지."], ["As your wife, surely it is her interest to keep it?”", "아내 된 사람으로서, 그것을 덮어 두는 편이 당연히 자기에게도 이롭지 않겠나?”"]], [["“Is it?", "“그럴까?"], ["I’m coming to that.", "마침 그 이야기를 하려던 참이야."], ["It might be her interest if she cared two straws about me.", "그 여자가 나를 손톱만큼이라도 아낀다면 이로울지도 모르지."], ["But I happen to be an encumbrance in the way of another man.", "그런데 하필 내가 다른 남자에게 걸리적거리는 방해물이란 말이야."], ["She was in love with him before she married me—she’s in love with him now—an infernal vagabond of a drawing-master, named Hartright.”", "나와 혼인하기 전부터 그놈을 사랑했고—지금도 그놈을 사랑하고 있어—하트라이트라는 이름의, 그림 교사 나부랭이 떠돌이 놈이지.”"]], [["“My dear friend!", "“이보게!"], ["what is there extraordinary in that?", "그게 뭐가 그리 별스러운 일인가?"], ["They are all in love with some other man.", "여자들은 하나같이 다른 남자를 사랑하지."], ["Who gets the first of a woman’s heart?", "여자의 마음에서 첫째 자리를 차지하는 사람이 누군가?"], ["In all my experience I have never yet met with the man who was Number One.", "내 평생의 경험을 통틀어, 나는 아직 1번이었던 남자를 만나 본 적이 없네."], ["Number Two, sometimes.", "2번은 더러 있지."], ["Number Three, Four, Five, often.", "3번, 4번, 5번은 흔하고."], ["Number One, never!", "1번은 결코 없어!"], ["He exists, of course—but I have not met with him.”", "물론 그런 자가 존재하기야 하겠지—다만 나는 만나 보지 못했네.”"]], [["“Wait!", "“기다려!"], ["I haven’t done yet.", "아직 안 끝났어."], ["Who do you think helped Anne Catherick to get the start, when the people from the mad-house were after her?", "정신병원 사람들이 앤 캐서릭을 뒤쫓을 때 그 애가 한발 앞서 달아나도록 도와준 게 누구라고 생각하나?"], ["Hartright.", "하트라이트야."], ["Who do you think saw her again in Cumberland?", "컴벌랜드에서 그 애를 다시 만난 게 누구라고 생각하나?"], ["Hartright.", "하트라이트지."], ["Both times he spoke to her alone.", "두 번 다 단둘이 이야기를 나눴어."], ["Stop!", "가만!"], ["don’t interrupt me.", "말 끊지 마."], ["The scoundrel’s as sweet on my wife as she is on him.", "그 악당은 내 아내한테 홀딱 빠져 있고, 내 아내도 그놈한테 그래."], ["He knows the Secret, and she knows the Secret.", "그놈도 그 비밀을 알고, 그 여자도 그 비밀을 알아."], ["Once let them both get together again, and it’s her interest and his interest to turn their information against me.”", "그 둘이 다시 만나기만 해 봐, 자기들이 쥔 것을 나한테 들이대는 것이 여자에게도 이롭고 그놈에게도 이로운 일이 된단 말이야.”"]], [["“Gently, Percival—gently!", "“진정하게, 퍼시벌—진정해!"], ["Are you insensible to the virtue of Lady Glyde?”", "자네는 글라이드 부인의 정숙함을 조금도 믿지 않는 건가?”"]], [["“That for the virtue of Lady Glyde!", "“글라이드 부인의 정숙함 따위!"], ["I believe in nothing about her but her money.", "그 여자에 대해 내가 믿는 것은 오직 그 재산뿐이야."], ["Don’t you see how the case stands?", "사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 모르겠나?"], ["She might be harmless enough by herself; but if she and that vagabond Hartright——”", "혼자라면 별 해가 없을지도 모르지. 하지만 그 여자와 저 떠돌이 하트라이트가 손을 잡는다면——”"]], [["“Yes, yes, I see.", "“그래, 그래, 알겠네."], ["Where is Mr. Hartright?”", "하트라이트 씨는 지금 어디 있나?”"]], [["“Out of the country.", "“나라 밖에."], ["If he means to keep a whole skin on his bones, I recommend him not to come back in a hurry.”", "제 몸뚱이 가죽을 성하게 붙이고 살 생각이라면, 서둘러 돌아오지 않는 편이 좋을 걸세.”"]], [["“Are you sure he is out of the country?”", "“그자가 정말 나라 밖에 있는 것이 확실한가?”"]], [["“Certain.", "“확실해."], ["I had him watched from the time he left Cumberland to the time he sailed.", "그놈이 컴벌랜드를 떠난 때부터 배에 오를 때까지 사람을 붙여 감시했어."], ["Oh, I’ve been careful, I can tell you!", "아, 내가 얼마나 조심했는지 자네는 모를 걸세!"], ["Anne Catherick lived with some people at a farmhouse near Limmeridge.", "앤 캐서릭은 리머리지 근처 어느 농가에서 사람들과 함께 지냈어."], ["I went there myself, after she had given me the slip, and made sure that they knew nothing.", "그 애가 나를 따돌리고 달아난 뒤에 내가 직접 거기까지 가서, 그 사람들이 아무것도 모른다는 것을 확인했지."], ["I gave her mother a form of letter to write to Miss Halcombe, exonerating me from any bad motive in putting her under restraint.", "그 어미한테는 홀콤 양 앞으로 보낼 편지 문안을 주어서, 내가 그 애를 가둔 데에 나쁜 뜻이 없었다는 것을 밝히게 했어."], ["I’ve spent, I’m afraid to say how much, in trying to trace her, and in spite of it all, she turns up here and escapes me on my own property!", "그 애의 자취를 좇느라 얼마를 썼는지는 입에 담기도 두려울 지경인데, 그러고도 그 애는 여기에 나타나서 내 땅 안에서 나를 따돌린단 말이야!"], ["How do I know who else may see her, who else may speak to her?", "또 누가 그 애를 볼지, 또 누가 그 애에게 말을 걸지 내가 어찌 알겠나?"], ["That prying scoundrel, Hartright, may come back without my knowing it, and may make use of her to-morrow——”", "그 캐고 다니는 악당 하트라이트가 내가 모르는 사이에 돌아와서, 당장 내일이라도 그 애를 써먹을지도——”"]], [["“Not he, Percival!", "“그럴 리 없네, 퍼시벌!"], ["While I am on the spot, and while that woman is in the neighbourhood, I will answer for our laying hands on her before Mr. Hartright—even if he does come back.", "내가 이곳에 있고 그 여자가 이 근방에 있는 한, 하트라이트 씨보다 먼저 우리가 그 여자를 손에 넣도록 내가 책임지겠네—설령 그자가 정말 돌아온다 해도 말이야."], ["I see!", "알겠어!"], ["yes, yes, I see!", "그래, 그래, 알겠네!"], ["The finding of Anne Catherick is the first necessity—make your mind easy about the rest.", "앤 캐서릭을 찾아내는 것이 첫째로 급한 일이야—나머지는 마음을 놓게."], ["Your wife is here, under your thumb—Miss Halcombe is inseparable from her, and is, therefore, under your thumb also—and Mr. Hartright is out of the country.", "자네 아내는 여기, 자네 손아귀 안에 있네—홀콤 양은 그 부인과 떨어질 줄 모르니 역시 자네 손아귀 안에 있고—하트라이트 씨는 나라 밖에 있지."], ["This invisible Anne of yours is all we have to think of for the present.", "당장 우리가 생각할 것은 자네의 그 보이지 않는 앤뿐이야."], ["You have made your inquiries?”", "알아볼 만큼은 알아보았나?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“했지."], ["I have been to her mother, I have ransacked the village—and all to no purpose.”", "그 어미한테도 가 보았고, 마을을 샅샅이 뒤졌어—그런데 죄다 헛일이야.”"]], [["“Is her mother to be depended on?”", "“그 어미는 믿을 만한가?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“믿을 만해.”"]], [["“She has told your secret once.”", "“그 여자는 자네 비밀을 이미 한 번 발설했네.”"]], [["“She won’t tell it again.”", "“두 번은 발설하지 않을 거야.”"]], [["“Why not?", "“어째서?"], ["Are her own interests concerned in keeping it, as well as yours?”", "그것을 감추는 데에 자네뿐 아니라 그 여자 자신의 이해도 걸려 있는가?”"]], [["“Yes—deeply concerned.”", "“그래—아주 깊이 걸려 있지.”"]], [["“I am glad to hear it, Percival, for your sake.", "“그 말을 들으니 자네를 위해서 기쁘군, 퍼시벌."], ["Don’t be discouraged, my friend.", "낙심하지 말게, 이보게."], ["Our money matters, as I told you, leave me plenty of time to turn round in, and I may search for Anne Catherick to-morrow to better purpose than you.", "우리 돈 문제는 아까도 말했듯 내가 몸을 돌릴 여유를 넉넉히 남겨 주고 있으니, 내일은 내가 자네보다 더 나은 성과를 거두며 앤 캐서릭을 찾아 나설 수도 있겠지."], ["One last question before we go to bed.”", "잠자리에 들기 전에 마지막으로 한 가지만 묻겠네.”"]], [["“What is it?”", "“그게 뭔가?”"]], [["“It is this.", "“이런 것일세."], ["When I went to the boat-house to tell Lady Glyde that the little difficulty of her signature was put off, accident took me there in time to see a strange woman parting in a very suspicious manner from your wife.", "서명이라는 자잘한 문제가 미뤄졌다는 것을 글라이드 부인에게 알리러 내가 배 창고로 갔을 때, 우연이 나를 그리로 이끌어, 낯선 여자가 자네 아내와 몹시 수상쩍은 태도로 헤어지는 장면을 마침 보게 되었네."], ["But accident did not bring me near enough to see this same woman’s face plainly.", "그러나 그 우연도 나를 그 여자의 얼굴을 또렷이 볼 만큼 가까이 데려다주지는 않았어."], ["I must know how to recognise our invisible Anne.", "나는 우리의 보이지 않는 앤을 어떻게 알아볼지 알아야겠네."], ["What is she like?”", "그 여자는 어떻게 생겼나?”"]], [["“Like? Come!", "“어떻게 생겼냐고?"], ["I’ll tell you in two words.", "어디! 두 마디로 말해 주지."], ["She’s a sickly likeness of my wife.”", "병색이 도는 내 아내를 빼닮았어.”"]], [["The chair creaked, and the pillar shook once more.", "의자가 삐걱거렸고, 기둥이 다시 한 번 흔들렸다."], ["The Count was on his feet again—this time in astonishment.", "백작이 또 일어선 것이다—이번에는 놀라서."]], [["“What!!!”", "“뭐라고!!!”"], ["he exclaimed eagerly.", "그가 다급하게 소리쳤다."]], [["“Fancy my wife, after a bad illness, with a touch of something wrong in her head—and there is Anne Catherick for you,”", "“내 아내가 크게 앓고 난 모습을 떠올려 보게, 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 기색까지 얹어서—그러면 그게 바로 자네가 찾는 앤 캐서릭이야.”"], ["answered Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 대답했다."]], [["“Are they related to each other?”", "“둘이 친척인가?”"]], [["“Not a bit of it.”", "“천만의 말씀이지.”"]], [["“And yet so like?”", "“그런데도 그렇게 닮았다는 건가?”"]], [["“Yes, so like.", "“그래, 그렇게 닮았어."], ["What are you laughing about?”", "뭐가 그리 우스운가?”"]], [["There was no answer, and no sound of any kind.", "아무 대답도, 어떤 소리도 없었다."], ["The Count was laughing in his smooth silent internal way.", "백작은 그 특유의 매끄럽고 소리 없는, 속으로 삼키는 방식으로 웃고 있었다."]], [["“What are you laughing about?”", "“뭐가 그리 우습냐니까?”"], ["reiterated Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 되물었다."]], [["“Perhaps at my own fancies, my good friend.", "“아마도 나 자신의 공상이 우스운 게지, 나의 좋은 친구여."], ["Allow me my Italian humour—do I not come of the illustrious nation which invented the exhibition of Punch?", "내 이탈리아식 익살을 눈감아 주게—나는 저 펀치 인형극을 만들어 낸 영예로운 나라의 태생이 아닌가?"], ["Well, well, well, I shall know Anne Catherick when I see her—and so enough for to-night.", "자, 자, 자, 앤 캐서릭을 보면 내가 알아볼 걸세—오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐네."], ["Make your mind easy, Percival.", "마음을 편히 하게, 퍼시벌."], ["Sleep, my son, the sleep of the just, and see what I will do for you when daylight comes to help us both.", "자게, 나의 아들이여, 의인의 잠을 자게. 그리고 날이 밝아 우리 두 사람을 도와줄 때 내가 자네를 위해 무엇을 하는지 보게."], ["I have my projects and my plans here in my big head.", "내 이 커다란 머릿속에 나의 구상과 나의 계획이 들어 있네."], ["You shall pay those bills and find Anne Catherick—my sacred word of honour on it, but you shall!", "자네는 그 어음들을 갚을 것이고 앤 캐서릭도 찾아낼 걸세—내 신성한 명예를 걸고 말하네만, 자네는 반드시 그렇게 될 거야!"], ["Am I a friend to be treasured in the best corner of your heart, or am I not?", "나는 자네 마음속 가장 좋은 자리에 간직해 둘 만한 벗인가, 아닌가?"], ["Am I worth those loans of money which you so delicately reminded me of a little while since?", "조금 전 자네가 그토록 섬세하게 상기시켜 준 그 돈을 빌려줄 만한 값어치가 있는 사람인가, 아닌가?"], ["Whatever you do, never wound me in my sentiments any more.", "무슨 짓을 하든, 다시는 내 감정에 상처를 주지 말게."], ["Recognise them, Percival!", "그것을 알아보게, 퍼시벌!"], ["imitate them, Percival!", "그것을 본받게, 퍼시벌!"], ["I forgive you again—I shake hands again.", "나는 다시 자네를 용서하네—다시 손을 내미네."], ["Good-night!”", "잘 자게!”"]], [["Not another word was spoken.", "그 뒤로는 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["I heard the Count close the library door.", "백작이 서재 문을 닫는 소리가 들렸다."], ["I heard Sir Percival barring up the window-shutters.", "퍼시벌 경이 창 덧문에 빗장을 지르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["It had been raining, raining all the time.", "비가 오고 있었다, 그동안 내내 비가 오고 있었다."], ["I was cramped by my position and chilled to the bones.", "나는 웅크린 자세 탓에 몸이 굳었고 뼛속까지 얼어 있었다."], ["When I first tried to move, the effort was so painful to me that I was obliged to desist.", "처음 움직여 보려 했을 때는 그 동작이 어찌나 아프던지 그만둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["I tried a second time, and succeeded in rising to my knees on the wet roof.", "두 번째로 시도해서, 젖은 지붕 위에서 무릎을 세우고 일어나는 데까지는 성공했다."]], [["As I crept to the wall, and raised myself against it, I looked back, and saw the window of the Count’s dressing-room gleam into light.", "벽 쪽으로 기어가 그 벽에 몸을 기대어 일어서면서 나는 뒤를 돌아보았고, 백작의 옷방 창문이 환하게 밝아지는 것을 보았다."], ["My sinking courage flickered up in me again, and kept my eyes fixed on his window, as I stole my way back, step by step, past the wall of the house.", "가라앉던 내 용기가 다시 안에서 파닥이며 살아났고, 나는 집의 벽을 따라 한 걸음 한 걸음 몰래 돌아오는 동안 그 창문에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["The clock struck the quarter after one, when I laid my hands on the window-sill of my own room.", "내가 내 방 창턱에 손을 얹었을 때 시계가 한 시 십오 분을 알렸다."], ["I had seen nothing and heard nothing which could lead me to suppose that my retreat had been discovered.", "내 퇴각이 발각되었으리라고 짐작할 만한 것은 아무것도 보지 못했고 아무것도 듣지 못했다."]], [["# X", "# X"]], [["June 20th.—Eight o’clock.", "6월 20일.—여덟 시."], ["The sun is shining in a clear sky.", "해가 맑은 하늘에서 빛나고 있다."], ["I have not been near my bed—I have not once closed my weary wakeful eyes.", "나는 침대 근처에도 가지 않았다—지친 채 말똥말똥한 눈을 한 번도 감지 못했다."], ["From the same window at which I looked out into the darkness of last night, I look out now at the bright stillness of the morning.", "어젯밤 어둠 속을 내다보던 바로 그 창가에서, 나는 지금 아침의 환한 고요를 내다본다."]], [["I count the hours that have passed since I escaped to the shelter of this room by my own sensations—and those hours seem like weeks.", "이 방의 은신처로 달아난 뒤로 몇 시간이 흘렀는지를 나는 내 몸의 느낌만으로 헤아린다—그리고 그 몇 시간이 몇 주처럼 여겨진다."]], [["How short a time, and yet how long to ME—since I sank down in the darkness, here, on the floor—drenched to the skin, cramped in every limb, cold to the bones, a useless, helpless, panic-stricken creature.", "얼마나 짧은 시간인가, 그런데도 나에게는 얼마나 긴가—여기 이 바닥에, 어둠 속에 주저앉았던 그때부터. 살갗까지 흠뻑 젖고, 사지가 하나같이 뒤틀리고, 뼛속까지 차갑고, 쓸모없고 무력하고 공포에 질린 한 생물이 되어."]], [["I hardly know when I roused myself.", "언제 정신을 차렸는지 나는 잘 모르겠다."], ["I hardly know when I groped my way back to the bedroom, and lighted the candle, and searched (with a strange ignorance, at first, of where to look for them) for dry clothes to warm me.", "언제 더듬더듬 침실로 돌아가 촛불을 켜고, (처음에는 어디를 뒤져야 할지도 이상하리만치 알지 못한 채) 몸을 덥힐 마른 옷을 찾았는지도 잘 모르겠다."], ["The doing of these things is in my mind, but not the time when they were done.", "그 일들을 했다는 것은 머릿속에 있으나, 그것을 한 시각은 없다."]], [["Can I even remember when the chilled, cramped feeling left me, and the throbbing heat came in its place?", "얼어붙어 뒤틀리던 느낌이 사라지고 그 자리에 두근거리는 열이 들어선 때를, 나는 기억이나 할 수 있을까?"]], [["Surely it was before the sun rose?", "분명 해가 뜨기 전이었을 것이다."], ["Yes, I heard the clock strike three.", "그래, 시계가 세 번 치는 소리를 들었다."], ["I remember the time by the sudden brightness and clearness, the feverish strain and excitement of all my faculties which came with it.", "그 시각을 기억하는 것은, 그와 함께 찾아온 갑작스러운 밝음과 또렷함, 온 감각의 열에 들뜬 팽팽함과 흥분 때문이다."], ["I remember my resolution to control myself, to wait patiently hour after hour, till the chance offered of removing Laura from this horrible place, without the danger of immediate discovery and pursuit.", "나 자신을 다스리자고, 즉시 발각되어 추격당할 위험 없이 로라를 이 끔찍한 곳에서 데리고 나갈 기회가 올 때까지 한 시간 또 한 시간 참을성 있게 기다리자고 결심했던 것을 기억한다."], ["I remember the persuasion settling itself in my mind that the words those two men had said to each other would furnish us, not only with our justification for leaving the house, but with our weapons of defence against them as well.", "그 두 사람이 서로 주고받은 말들이 우리에게 이 집을 떠날 명분뿐 아니라 그들에 맞설 방어의 무기까지 되어 주리라는 확신이 마음속에 자리 잡던 것을 기억한다."], ["I recall the impulse that awakened in me to preserve those words in writing, exactly as they were spoken, while the time was my own, and while my memory vividly retained them.", "시간이 아직 내 것이고 기억이 그 말들을 생생히 붙들고 있는 동안에, 들은 그대로 한 마디도 틀리지 않게 글로 남겨 두어야겠다는 충동이 내 안에서 깨어나던 것을 나는 되새긴다."], ["All this I remember plainly: there is no confusion in my head yet.", "이 모두를 나는 또렷이 기억한다. 아직 내 머릿속에는 아무 혼란도 없다."], ["The coming in here from the bedroom, with my pen and ink and paper, before sunrise—the sitting down at the widely-opened window to get all the air I could to cool me—the ceaseless writing, faster and faster, hotter and hotter, driving on more and more wakefully, all through the dreadful interval before the house was astir again—how clearly I recall it, from the beginning by candle-light, to the end on the page before this, in the sunshine of the new day!", "해가 뜨기 전에 펜과 잉크와 종이를 들고 침실에서 이리로 건너온 일—몸을 식힐 바람을 조금이라도 더 받으려고 활짝 열어젖힌 창가에 앉은 일—집안이 다시 깨어나기 전의 그 무서운 동안 내내, 더 빠르게 더 빠르게, 더 뜨겁게 더 뜨겁게, 점점 더 말똥말똥해지며 쉬지 않고 써 내려간 일—촛불로 시작해 새날의 햇빛 속에서 앞 장의 끝에 이르기까지, 나는 그것을 얼마나 또렷이 되새기는가!"]], [["Why do I sit here still?", "왜 나는 아직도 여기 앉아 있는가?"], ["Why do I weary my hot eyes and my burning head by writing more?", "왜 더 씀으로써 뜨거운 눈과 타는 머리를 지치게 하는가?"], ["Why not lie down and rest myself, and try to quench the fever that consumes me, in sleep?", "왜 누워서 쉬며, 나를 태우는 열을 잠으로 꺼 보려 하지 않는가?"]], [["I dare not attempt it.", "감히 그럴 엄두가 나지 않는다."], ["A fear beyond all other fears has got possession of me.", "다른 어떤 두려움보다 큰 두려움이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["I am afraid of this heat that parches my skin.", "내 살갗을 바싹 말리는 이 열이 두렵다."], ["I am afraid of the creeping and throbbing that I feel in my head.", "머릿속에서 기어 다니며 두근거리는 것이 두렵다."], ["If I lie down now, how do I know that I may have the sense and the strength to rise again?", "지금 누우면, 다시 일어날 정신과 힘이 내게 남아 있으리라고 무엇으로 알겠는가?"]], [["Oh, the rain, the rain—the cruel rain that chilled me last night!", "아, 그 비, 그 비—어젯밤 나를 얼어붙게 한 그 모진 비!"]], [["Nine o’clock.", "아홉 시."], ["Was it nine struck, or eight?", "아홉 번이 울린 것인가, 여덟 번인가?"], ["Nine, surely?", "분명 아홉 번이겠지?"], ["I am shivering again—shivering, from head to foot, in the summer air.", "나는 또 떨고 있다—여름 공기 속에서, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있다."], ["Have I been sitting here asleep?", "내가 여기 앉은 채로 잠들었던가?"], ["I don’t know what I have been doing.", "내가 무엇을 하고 있었는지 모르겠다."]], [["Oh, my God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["am I going to be ill?", "나는 병이 나려는 것인가?"]], [["Ill, at such a time as this!", "하필 이런 때에 병이라니!"]], [["My head—I am sadly afraid of my head.", "머리가—내 머리가 몹시 두렵다."], ["I can write, but the lines all run together.", "쓸 수는 있는데 줄들이 죄다 뒤엉킨다. 글자가 보인다."], ["I see the words. Laura—I can write Laura, and see I write it.", "로라—나는 로라라고 쓸 수 있고, 내가 그렇게 쓰는 것이 보인다."], ["Eight or nine—which was it?", "여덟 번인가 아홉 번인가—어느 쪽이었지?"]], [["So cold, so cold—oh, that rain last night!—and the strokes of the clock, the strokes I can’t count, keep striking in my head——", "너무 춥다, 너무 추워—아, 어젯밤 그 비!—그리고 시계 소리가, 셀 수 없는 그 소리가, 자꾸 머릿속에서 울려——"]], [["Note [At this place the entry in the Diary ceases to be legible.", "주(註) [이 대목에서 일기의 기록은 알아볼 수 없게 된다."], ["The two or three lines which follow contain fragments of words only, mingled with blots and scratches of the pen.", "뒤따르는 두세 줄에는 낱말의 조각들만이 잉크 얼룩과 펜으로 긁힌 자국에 뒤섞여 남아 있다."], ["The last marks on the paper bear some resemblance to the first two letters (L and A) of the name of Lady Glyde.", "종이에 남은 마지막 자국은 글라이드 부인의 이름 첫 두 글자(L과 A)와 얼마간 닮아 있다."]], [["On the next page of the Diary, another entry appears.", "일기의 다음 장에는 또 하나의 기록이 나타난다."], ["It is in a man’s handwriting, large, bold, and firmly regular, and the date is “June the 21st.”", "그것은 크고 굵으며 흔들림 없이 고른 남자의 필적이고, 날짜는 “6월 21일”이다."], ["It contains these lines—]", "거기에는 이런 글이 적혀 있다—]"]], [["# POSTSCRIPT BY A SINCERE FRIEND", "# POSTSCRIPT BY A SINCERE FRIEND"]], [["The illness of our excellent Miss Halcombe has afforded me the opportunity of enjoying an unexpected intellectual pleasure.", "우리의 훌륭하신 홀콤 양의 병환은 나에게 뜻밖의 지적 쾌락을 누릴 기회를 베풀어 주었다."]], [["I refer to the perusal (which I have just completed) of this interesting Diary.", "내가 말하는 것은 이 흥미로운 일기의 통독이다(방금 막 끝마쳤다)."]], [["There are many hundred pages here.", "여기에는 수백 쪽이 있다."], ["I can lay my hand on my heart, and declare that every page has charmed, refreshed, delighted me.", "나는 가슴에 손을 얹고 단언할 수 있으니, 그 한 쪽 한 쪽이 나를 매혹하였고, 상쾌하게 하였고, 기쁘게 하였다."]], [["To a man of my sentiments it is unspeakably gratifying to be able to say this.", "나와 같은 감성을 지닌 사내에게 이렇게 말할 수 있다는 것은 이루 말할 수 없이 흐뭇한 일이다."]], [["Admirable woman!", "감탄스러운 여인이여!"]], [["I allude to Miss Halcombe.", "내가 가리키는 것은 홀콤 양이다."]], [["Stupendous effort!", "경이로운 역작이여!"]], [["I refer to the Diary.", "내가 가리키는 것은 이 일기다."]], [["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["these pages are amazing.", "이 지면들은 놀랍다."], ["The tact which I find here, the discretion, the rare courage, the wonderful power of memory, the accurate observation of character, the easy grace of style, the charming outbursts of womanly feeling, have all inexpressibly increased my admiration of this sublime creature, of this magnificent Marian.", "여기서 내가 발견하는 기지, 그 신중함, 그 보기 드문 담대함, 그 경탄스러운 기억력, 인물에 대한 정확한 관찰, 유려한 문체의 기품, 여인다운 감정이 매혹적으로 터져 나오는 대목들—이 모든 것이 이 숭고한 존재, 이 장엄한 매리언을 향한 나의 감복을 형언할 수 없이 드높였다."], ["The presentation of my own character is masterly in the extreme.", "내 자신의 인물됨을 그려 낸 솜씨는 극도로 대가답다."], ["I certify, with my whole heart, to the fidelity of the portrait.", "나는 온 마음을 다하여 그 초상의 충실함을 증언한다."], ["I feel how vivid an impression I must have produced to have been painted in such strong, such rich, such massive colours as these.", "이토록 강렬하고, 이토록 풍부하고, 이토록 육중한 빛깔로 그려질 만큼 내가 얼마나 생생한 인상을 남겼을 것인지 나는 절감한다."], ["I lament afresh the cruel necessity which sets our interests at variance, and opposes us to each other.", "우리의 이해를 서로 어긋나게 하고 우리를 맞세우는 저 잔인한 필연을 나는 새삼 한탄한다."], ["Under happier circumstances how worthy I should have been of Miss Halcombe—how worthy Miss Halcombe would have been of ME.", "더 행복한 사정 아래였다면 나는 홀콤 양에게 얼마나 어울리는 사내였을 것이며—홀콤 양은 나에게 얼마나 어울리는 여인이었을 것인가."]], [["The sentiments which animate my heart assure me that the lines I have just written express a Profound Truth.", "내 가슴을 뛰게 하는 감정들이 나에게 확신시켜 주노니, 내가 방금 적은 줄들은 하나의 심오한 진실을 말한 것이다."]], [["Those sentiments exalt me above all merely personal considerations.", "그 감정들은 나를 한낱 사사로운 이해타산 위로 드높여 준다."], ["I bear witness, in the most disinterested manner, to the excellence of the stratagem by which this unparalleled woman surprised the private interview between Percival and myself—also to the marvellous accuracy of her report of the whole conversation from its beginning to its end.", "나는 지극히 사심 없는 태도로 증언하노라—이 견줄 데 없는 여인이 퍼시벌과 나 사이의 은밀한 밀담을 불시에 알아낸 그 계략의 탁월함을, 또한 그 대화를 처음부터 끝까지 옮겨 적은 그 놀라운 정확함을."]], [["Those sentiments have induced me to offer to the unimpressionable doctor who attends on her my vast knowledge of chemistry, and my luminous experience of the more subtle resources which medical and magnetic science have placed at the disposal of mankind.", "그 감정들에 이끌려 나는 그녀를 돌보는 저 감동할 줄 모르는 의사에게 나의 방대한 화학 지식과, 의학과 자기(磁氣) 과학이 인류의 손에 쥐여 준 더 미묘한 방편들에 대한 나의 빛나는 경험을 내어놓았다."], ["He has hitherto declined to avail himself of my assistance.", "그는 지금껏 내 도움을 받기를 마다해 왔다."], ["Miserable man!", "가련한 사내여!"]], [["Finally, those sentiments dictate the lines—grateful, sympathetic, paternal lines—which appear in this place.", "끝으로, 그 감정들이 이 자리에 나타나는 이 줄들을—감사에 차고, 연민에 차고, 아버지와 같은 이 줄들을—받아쓰게 한다."], ["I close the book.", "나는 책을 덮는다."], ["My strict sense of propriety restores it (by the hands of my wife) to its place on the writer’s table.", "나의 엄정한 예법 감각이 이것을 (내 아내의 손을 빌려) 글쓴이의 탁자 위 제자리에 돌려놓는다."], ["Events are hurrying me away.", "사건들이 나를 재촉하여 몰아간다."], ["Circumstances are guiding me to serious issues.", "정황이 나를 중대한 국면으로 이끌어 간다."], ["Vast perspectives of success unroll themselves before my eyes.", "성공의 광대한 전망이 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진다."], ["I accomplish my destiny with a calmness which is terrible to myself.", "나는 나 자신에게조차 두려울 만큼의 침착함으로 내 운명을 이룬다."], ["Nothing but the homage of my admiration is my own.", "오직 나의 감복이 바치는 경의만이 내 것이다."], ["I deposit it with respectful tenderness at the feet of Miss Halcombe.", "나는 그것을 공손한 애틋함으로 홀콤 양의 발치에 놓아 둔다."]], [["I breathe my wishes for her recovery.", "나는 그녀의 쾌유를 비는 마음을 숨결에 실어 보낸다."]], [["I condole with her on the inevitable failure of every plan that she has formed for her sister’s benefit.", "나는 그녀가 동생을 위해 세운 모든 계획이 필연코 실패하고 말리라는 데 대하여 그녀에게 애도를 표한다."], ["At the same time, I entreat her to believe that the information which I have derived from her Diary will in no respect help me to contribute to that failure.", "동시에 나는 그녀에게 간청하노니, 내가 그녀의 일기에서 얻은 정보가 그 실패를 앞당기는 데에 어떤 식으로도 보탬이 되지 않으리라는 것을 믿어 주기 바란다."], ["It simply confirms the plan of conduct which I had previously arranged.", "그것은 다만 내가 앞서 마련해 둔 행동 계획을 확인해 주었을 뿐이다."], ["I have to thank these pages for awakening the finest sensibilities in my nature—nothing more.", "이 지면들에 대하여 나는 내 천성 속의 가장 고운 감수성을 일깨워 준 것을 고마워해야 하니—그 이상은 아무것도 없다."]], [["To a person of similar sensibility this simple assertion will explain and excuse everything.", "비슷한 감수성을 지닌 사람에게라면 이 단순한 단언이 모든 것을 설명하고 또 용서해 줄 것이다."]], [["Miss Halcombe is a person of similar sensibility.", "홀콤 양은 비슷한 감수성을 지닌 사람이다."]], [["In that persuasion I sign myself, Fosco.", "그렇게 믿으며 나는 이렇게 서명한다, 포스코."]], [["THE STORY CONTINUED BY FREDERICK FAIRLIE, ESQ., OF LIMMERIDGE HOUSE[2]", "리머리지 하우스의 프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 이어 가는 이야기[2]"]], [["[2] The manner in which Mr. Fairlie’s Narrative, and other Narratives that are shortly to follow it, were originally obtained, forms the subject of an explanation which will appear at a later period.", "[2] 페얼리 씨의 서술과, 곧 뒤따를 다른 서술들이 애초에 어떤 경위로 입수되었는지는 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 설명의 주제가 된다."]], [["It is the grand misfortune of my life that nobody will let me alone.", "아무도 나를 가만히 내버려 두지 않는다는 것, 그것이 내 인생의 크나큰 불행입니다."]], [["Why—I ask everybody—why worry ME?", "어째서—나는 모두에게 묻습니다—어째서 하필 나를 못살게 구는 겁니까?"], ["Nobody answers that question, and nobody lets me alone.", "아무도 그 물음에 답하지 않고, 아무도 나를 가만두지 않습니다."], ["Relatives, friends, and strangers all combine to annoy me.", "친척이며 친구며 낯선 사람이며 하나같이 합심해서 나를 성가시게 합니다."], ["What have I done?", "내가 무슨 짓을 했다고?"], ["I ask myself, I ask my servant, Louis, fifty times a day—what have I done?", "나는 나 자신에게 묻고, 내 하인 루이에게 하루에 쉰 번은 묻습니다—내가 대체 무슨 짓을 했느냐고."], ["Neither of us can tell.", "우리 둘 중 누구도 답하지 못합니다."], ["Most extraordinary!", "참으로 기이한 일이지요!"]], [["The last annoyance that has assailed me is the annoyance of being called upon to write this Narrative.", "가장 최근에 나를 덮친 성가심은 이 서술을 쓰라는 요구를 받은 성가심입니다."], ["Is a man in my state of nervous wretchedness capable of writing narratives?", "나처럼 신경이 이 딱한 상태에 있는 사람이 서술 따위를 쓸 수 있겠습니까?"], ["When I put this extremely reasonable objection, I am told that certain very serious events relating to my niece have happened within my experience, and that I am the fit person to describe them on that account.", "내가 이 지극히 온당한 이의를 제기하면, 내 조카딸에 관한 매우 중대한 사건들이 내가 겪은 범위 안에서 일어났으니 그런 까닭에 그것을 적어 내기에 내가 적임자라는 대답이 돌아옵니다."], ["I am threatened if I fail to exert myself in the manner required, with consequences which I cannot so much as think of without perfect prostration.", "요구받은 대로 애써 힘쓰지 않으면 생각만 해도 완전히 기진해 버릴 결과가 따르리라는 위협까지 받습니다."], ["There is really no need to threaten me.", "나를 위협할 필요는 정말이지 없습니다."], ["Shattered by my miserable health and my family troubles, I am incapable of resistance.", "비참한 건강과 집안의 우환에 무너져 버린 나에게는 저항할 힘이 없으니까요."], ["If you insist, you take your unjust advantage of me, and I give way immediately.", "굳이 우기신다면 그것은 나에게 부당한 이점을 취하는 것이고, 나는 즉시 굴복합니다."], ["I will endeavour to remember what I can (under protest), and to write what I can (also under protest), and what I can’t remember and can’t write, Louis must remember and write for me.", "나는 (항의하는 마음으로) 기억나는 것을 애써 기억해 보고, (역시 항의하는 마음으로) 쓸 수 있는 것을 애써 써 보겠습니다. 그리고 기억나지 않고 쓸 수 없는 것은 루이가 나를 대신해 기억하고 써야 합니다."], ["He is an ass, and I am an invalid, and we are likely to make all sorts of mistakes between us.", "그는 얼간이고 나는 병자이니, 우리 둘 사이에서 온갖 오류가 나올 법합니다."], ["How humiliating!", "이 얼마나 굴욕적인지요!"]], [["I am told to remember dates.", "날짜를 기억하라는 말을 듣습니다."], ["Good heavens!", "저런 세상에!"], ["I never did such a thing in my life—how am I to begin now?", "나는 평생 그런 짓을 해 본 적이 없는데—이제 와서 어떻게 시작하라는 겁니까?"]], [["I have asked Louis.", "루이에게 물어보았습니다."], ["He is not quite such an ass as I have hitherto supposed.", "그는 내가 여태 짐작했던 만큼 얼간이는 아니더군요."], ["He remembers the date of the event, within a week or two—and I remember the name of the person.", "그는 그 일이 있었던 날짜를 한두 주 오차 안에서 기억하고—나는 그 사람의 이름을 기억합니다."], ["The date was towards the end of June, or the beginning of July, and the name (in my opinion a remarkably vulgar one) was Fanny.", "날짜는 6월 말이나 7월 초였고, 이름은 (내 생각에는 유난히 상스러운 이름인데) 패니였습니다."]], [["At the end of June, or the beginning of July, then, I was reclining in my customary state, surrounded by the various objects of Art which I have collected about me to improve the taste of the barbarous people in my neighbourhood.", "그러니까 6월 말이나 7월 초에, 나는 여느 때와 같은 자세로 비스듬히 누워, 이웃의 야만스러운 사람들의 안목을 개선하고자 내 주위에 모아 둔 온갖 미술품에 둘러싸여 있었습니다."], ["That is to say, I had the photographs of my pictures, and prints, and coins, and so forth, all about me, which I intend, one of these days, to present (the photographs, I mean, if the clumsy English language will let me mean anything) to present to the institution at Carlisle (horrid place!), with a view to improving the tastes of the members (Goths and Vandals to a man).", "그러니까 내 그림들의 사진이며 판화며 주화 따위가 죄다 내 주위에 있었다는 말이지요. 나는 언젠가 그것들을 (사진들 말입니다, 이 서투른 영어라는 언어가 나로 하여금 무언가를 뜻할 수 있게 허락해 준다면) 칼라일(끔찍한 곳!)의 협회에 기증하여 그 회원들(하나같이 고트족이요 반달족인 자들)의 안목을 개선할 작정입니다."], ["It might be supposed that a gentleman who was in course of conferring a great national benefit on his countrymen was the last gentleman in the world to be unfeelingly worried about private difficulties and family affairs.", "온 국민에게 크나큰 국가적 은덕을 베푸는 중인 신사라면 사사로운 곤란과 집안일로 매정하게 시달릴 일이 세상에서 가장 없는 신사이리라고 여길 만도 하지요."], ["Quite a mistake, I assure you, in my case.", "내 경우엔 완전한 착각입니다, 정말입니다."]], [["However, there I was, reclining, with my art-treasures about me, and wanting a quiet morning.", "어쨌든 나는 거기 그렇게, 미술의 보물들을 곁에 두고 비스듬히 누운 채 조용한 오전을 바라고 있었습니다."], ["Because I wanted a quiet morning, of course Louis came in.", "내가 조용한 오전을 바랐으니 당연히 루이가 들어왔지요."], ["It was perfectly natural that I should inquire what the deuce he meant by making his appearance when I had not rung my bell.", "내가 종을 울리지도 않았는데 대체 무슨 심산으로 나타났느냐고 묻는 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었습니다."], ["I seldom swear—it is such an ungentlemanlike habit—but when Louis answered by a grin, I think it was also perfectly natural that I should damn him for grinning.", "나는 좀처럼 욕을 하지 않습니다—그렇게 신사답지 못한 버릇이 또 없으니까요—그러나 루이가 히죽 웃는 것으로 대답했을 때, 히죽거린다고 그에게 욕을 퍼부은 것 또한 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다고 생각합니다."], ["At any rate, I did.", "아무튼 나는 그렇게 했습니다."]], [["This rigorous mode of treatment, I have observed, invariably brings persons in the lower class of life to their senses.", "이 엄한 처방은, 내가 관찰한 바로는, 하층 계급 사람들을 어김없이 제정신으로 돌아오게 합니다."], ["It brought Louis to HIS senses.", "그것은 루이를 그의 제정신으로 돌아오게 했습니다."], ["He was so obliging as to leave off grinning, and inform me that a Young Person was outside wanting to see me.", "그는 친절하게도 히죽거리기를 그만두고, 밖에 어떤 젊은 여자가 나를 만나고 싶어 한다고 알렸습니다."], ["He added (with the odious talkativeness of servants), that her name was Fanny.", "그는 (하인들 특유의 그 밉살스러운 수다스러움으로) 그 여자의 이름이 패니라고 덧붙였습니다."]], [["“Who is Fanny?”", "“패니가 누구냐?”"]], [["“Lady Glyde’s maid, sir.”", "“글라이드 부인의 하녀입니다, 나리.”"]], [["“What does Lady Glyde’s maid want with me?”", "“글라이드 부인의 하녀가 나한테 무슨 볼일이냐?”"]], [["“A letter, sir——”", "“편지가 한 통 있다고——”"]], [["“Take it.”", "“받아 두어라.”"]], [["“She refuses to give it to anybody but you, sir.”", "“나리 말고는 아무에게도 드릴 수 없다고 합니다.”"]], [["“Who sends the letter?”", "“누가 보낸 편지냐?”"]], [["“Miss Halcombe, sir.”", "“홀콤 양입니다, 나리.”"]], [["The moment I heard Miss Halcombe’s name I gave up.", "홀콤 양의 이름을 듣는 순간 나는 포기했습니다."], ["It is a habit of mine always to give up to Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양에게는 언제나 지고 마는 것이 내 버릇입니다."], ["I find, by experience, that it saves noise.", "경험으로 보건대 그편이 소란을 덜어 주더군요."], ["I gave up on this occasion.", "이번에도 나는 포기했습니다."], ["Dear Marian!", "사랑스러운 매리언!"]], [["“Let Lady Glyde’s maid come in, Louis.", "“글라이드 부인의 하녀를 들여보내라, 루이. 잠깐!"], ["Stop! Do her shoes creak?”", "그 여자 신발에서 삐걱 소리가 나느냐?”"]], [["I was obliged to ask the question.", "그 질문은 하지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["Creaking shoes invariably upset me for the day.", "삐걱거리는 신발은 어김없이 하루 종일 나를 망쳐 놓으니까요."], ["I was resigned to see the Young Person, but I was NOT resigned to let the Young Person’s shoes upset me.", "나는 그 젊은 여자를 만나는 데까지는 체념했지만, 그 젊은 여자의 신발이 나를 망쳐 놓게 두는 데까지 체념한 것은 아니었습니다."], ["There is a limit even to my endurance.", "내 인내에도 한계라는 것이 있습니다."]], [["Louis affirmed distinctly that her shoes were to be depended upon.", "루이는 그 여자의 신발은 믿어도 좋다고 분명히 단언했습니다."], ["I waved my hand.", "나는 손을 저었습니다."], ["He introduced her.", "그가 여자를 들여보냈습니다."], ["Is it necessary to say that she expressed her sense of embarrassment by shutting up her mouth and breathing through her nose?", "그 여자가 입을 꾹 다물고 코로 숨을 쉬는 것으로 자기가 거북하다는 뜻을 나타냈다는 말을 굳이 해야 할까요?"], ["To the student of female human nature in the lower orders, surely not.", "하층 계급 여성 인간 본성의 연구자에게라면 분명 그럴 필요가 없겠지요."]], [["Let me do the girl justice.", "그 아이에게 공정하게 굴기로 합시다."], ["Her shoes did NOT creak.", "그 아이의 신발은 삐걱거리지 않았습니다."], ["But why do Young Persons in service all perspire at the hands?", "그런데 어째서 남의집살이하는 젊은 여자들은 하나같이 손에 땀이 나는 걸까요?"], ["Why have they all got fat noses and hard cheeks?", "어째서 하나같이 코는 뭉툭하고 뺨은 딱딱한 걸까요?"], ["And why are their faces so sadly unfinished, especially about the corners of the eyelids?", "그리고 어째서 그 얼굴들은, 특히 눈꺼풀 언저리가, 그토록 딱하게도 마무리가 덜 된 것일까요?"], ["I am not strong enough to think deeply myself on any subject, but I appeal to professional men, who are.", "나는 어떤 주제든 스스로 깊이 생각할 만큼 튼튼하지 못하니, 그럴 만한 전문가들에게 여쭙습니다."], ["Why have we no variety in our breed of Young Persons?", "어째서 우리네 젊은 여자라는 품종에는 변화가 없는 것입니까?"]], [["“You have a letter for me, from Miss Halcombe?", "“홀콤 양에게서 온 편지를 나에게 가져왔다지?"], ["Put it down on the table, please, and don’t upset anything.", "탁자 위에 내려놓아라, 무엇 하나 넘어뜨리지 말고."], ["How is Miss Halcombe?”", "홀콤 양은 안녕하시냐?”"]], [["“Very well, thank you, sir.”", "“네, 안녕하십니다, 나리. 감사합니다.”"]], [["“And Lady Glyde?”", "“그러면 글라이드 부인은?”"]], [["I received no answer.", "나는 아무 대답도 받지 못했습니다."], ["The Young Person’s face became more unfinished than ever, and I think she began to cry.", "그 젊은 여자의 얼굴은 어느 때보다 더 마무리가 덜 된 꼴이 되었고, 내 생각에는 울기 시작한 것 같았습니다."], ["I certainly saw something moist about her eyes.", "눈가에 무언가 축축한 것이 있는 것만은 분명히 보았습니다."], ["Tears or perspiration?", "눈물일까요, 땀일까요?"], ["Louis (whom I have just consulted) is inclined to think, tears.", "(방금 물어본) 루이는 눈물 쪽으로 기웁니다."], ["He is in her class of life, and he ought to know best.", "그는 그 아이와 같은 계급이니 가장 잘 알겠지요."], ["Let us say, tears.", "눈물이라고 해 둡시다."]], [["Except when the refining process of Art judiciously removes from them all resemblance to Nature, I distinctly object to tears.", "예술의 정련 과정이 슬기롭게도 눈물에서 자연을 닮은 구석을 모조리 걷어 낸 경우가 아니라면, 나는 눈물이라는 것에 딱 잘라 반대합니다."], ["Tears are scientifically described as a Secretion.", "눈물은 과학적으로 분비물이라고 서술됩니다."], ["I can understand that a secretion may be healthy or unhealthy, but I cannot see the interest of a secretion from a sentimental point of view.", "분비물이 건강할 수도 있고 건강하지 않을 수도 있다는 것은 이해합니다만, 감상적인 관점에서 분비물이 무슨 흥미가 있는지는 알 수가 없습니다."], ["Perhaps my own secretions being all wrong together, I am a little prejudiced on the subject. No matter.", "아마도 내 자신의 분비물이 죄다 잘못되어 있는 탓에 이 문제에 대해 내가 조금 편견을 갖고 있는지도 모르지요."], ["I behaved, on this occasion, with all possible propriety and feeling.", "아무래도 좋습니다. 나는 이번 경우에 있을 수 있는 온갖 예의와 정으로 처신했습니다."], ["I closed my eyes and said to Louis—", "나는 눈을 감고 루이에게 말했습니다—"]], [["“Endeavour to ascertain what she means.”", "“저 아이가 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지 알아내도록 애써 보아라.”"]], [["Louis endeavoured, and the Young Person endeavoured.", "루이가 애를 썼고, 그 젊은 여자도 애를 썼습니다."], ["They succeeded in confusing each other to such an extent that I am bound in common gratitude to say, they really amused me.", "두 사람은 서로를 어찌나 헷갈리게 만드는 데 성공했는지, 나는 그저 고마운 마음에서 말하지 않을 수 없습니다—그들은 정말로 나를 즐겁게 해 주었습니다."], ["I think I shall send for them again when I am in low spirits.", "기분이 가라앉을 때면 그 둘을 다시 불러야겠다 싶습니다."], ["I have just mentioned this idea to Louis.", "방금 이 생각을 루이에게 말했습니다."], ["Strange to say, it seems to make him uncomfortable.", "이상하게도 그는 그 말에 불편해하는 것 같더군요."], ["Poor devil!", "딱한 녀석!"]], [["Surely I am not expected to repeat my niece’s maid’s explanation of her tears, interpreted in the English of my Swiss valet?", "설마 내 조카딸의 하녀가 자기 눈물을 설명한 것을, 그것도 내 스위스인 시종의 영어로 옮겨진 것을, 내가 그대로 되풀이하리라고 기대하시는 건 아니겠지요?"], ["The thing is manifestly impossible.", "그건 명백히 불가능한 일입니다."], ["I can give my own impressions and feelings perhaps.", "내 자신의 인상과 감정이라면 아마 전해 드릴 수 있겠습니다."], ["Will that do as well?", "그것으로도 괜찮을까요?"], ["Please say, Yes.", "그렇다고 말씀해 주십시오."]], [["My idea is that she began by telling me (through Louis) that her master had dismissed her from her mistress’s service.", "내 짐작으로 그 아이는 (루이를 통해) 자기 주인 나리가 자기를 마님 시중에서 내보냈다는 말로 시작했습니다."], ["(Observe, throughout, the strange irrelevancy of the Young Person.", "(내내 이 젊은 여자의 이야기가 얼마나 엉뚱하게 겉도는지 눈여겨보십시오."], ["Was it my fault that she had lost her place?) On her dismissal, she had gone to the inn to sleep.", "그 아이가 일자리를 잃은 것이 내 탓입니까?) 쫓겨난 뒤 그 아이는 여관에 가서 자기로 했다더군요."], ["(I don’t keep the inn—why mention it to ME?) Between six o’clock and seven Miss Halcombe had come to say good-bye, and had given her two letters, one for me, and one for a gentleman in London.", "(여관은 내가 하는 것이 아닌데—어째서 나에게 그 이야기를 합니까?) 여섯 시에서 일곱 시 사이에 홀콤 양이 작별 인사를 하러 와서 편지 두 통을 주었는데, 하나는 나에게, 하나는 런던의 어떤 신사에게 가는 것이었습니다."], ["(I am not a gentleman in London—hang the gentleman in London!) She had carefully put the two letters into her bosom (what have I to do with her bosom?); she had been very unhappy, when Miss Halcombe had gone away again; she had not had the heart to put bit or drop between her lips till it was near bedtime, and then, when it was close on nine o’clock, she had thought she should like a cup of tea.", "(나는 런던의 신사가 아닙니다—런던의 그 신사 따위 될 대로 되라지요!) 그 아이는 두 통의 편지를 조심스레 품속에 넣어 두었고 (그 아이의 품이 나와 무슨 상관입니까?), 홀콤 양이 다시 가 버리자 몹시 침울해졌고, 잠자리에 들 무렵이 다 되도록 입에 한 술 한 모금 넣을 마음이 나지 않다가, 아홉 시가 다 되어서야 차를 한 잔 마시고 싶어졌다고 합니다."], ["(Am I responsible for any of these vulgar fluctuations, which begin with unhappiness and end with tea?) Just as she was WARMING THE POT (I give the words on the authority of Louis, who says he knows what they mean, and wishes to explain, but I snub him on principle)—just as she was warming the pot the door opened, and she was STRUCK OF A HEAP (her own words again, and perfectly unintelligible this time to Louis, as well as to myself) by the appearance in the inn parlour of her ladyship the Countess.", "(침울함으로 시작해 차로 끝나는 이런 상스러운 감정의 기복에 내가 무슨 책임이 있습니까?) 마침 그 아이가 주전자를 데우고 있을 때 (이 말은 루이의 권위를 빌려 옮깁니다. 그는 그 뜻을 안다면서 설명하고 싶어 했지만 나는 원칙적으로 그를 면박했습니다)—마침 주전자를 데우고 있을 때 문이 열렸고, 그 아이는 여관 응접실에 백작부인 마님이 나타나신 것을 보고 억이 막혔다고 합니다 (역시 그 아이 자신의 말인데, 이번에는 루이에게도 나에게도 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 말이었습니다)."], ["I give my niece’s maid’s description of my sister’s title with a sense of the highest relish.", "내 조카딸의 하녀가 내 누이의 작위를 일컫는 것을 나는 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 기분으로 옮겨 적습니다."], ["My poor dear sister is a tiresome woman who married a foreigner.", "내 가엾은 누이는 외국인과 결혼한 성가신 여자입니다."], ["To resume: the door opened, her ladyship the Countess appeared in the parlour, and the Young Person was struck of a heap.", "다시 이어 가자면, 문이 열렸고, 백작부인 마님이 응접실에 나타나셨고, 그 젊은 여자는 억이 막혔습니다."], ["Most remarkable!", "참으로 놀랍기도 하지요!"]], [["I must really rest a little before I can get on any farther.", "더 나아가기 전에 정말이지 조금 쉬어야겠습니다."], ["When I have reclined for a few minutes, with my eyes closed, and when Louis has refreshed my poor aching temples with a little eau-de-Cologne, I may be able to proceed.", "몇 분쯤 눈을 감고 비스듬히 누워 있고, 루이가 내 딱하게 지끈거리는 관자놀이를 오드콜로뉴로 좀 식혀 주고 나면, 계속할 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다."]], [["Her ladyship the Countess——", "백작부인 마님께서는——"]], [["No.", "아니오."], ["I am able to proceed, but not to sit up.", "계속할 수는 있겠으나 일어나 앉아 있을 수는 없습니다."], ["I will recline and dictate.", "비스듬히 누운 채 받아쓰게 하겠습니다."], ["Louis has a horrid accent, but he knows the language, and can write.", "루이는 억양이 끔찍하지만 말은 알아듣고 글도 쓸 줄 압니다."], ["How very convenient!", "참으로 편리하지요!"]], [["Her ladyship, the Countess, explained her unexpected appearance at the inn by telling Fanny that she had come to bring one or two little messages which Miss Halcombe in her hurry had forgotten.", "백작부인 마님께서는 여관에 뜻밖에 나타나신 까닭을, 홀콤 양이 서두르는 통에 잊어버린 자잘한 전갈 한두 가지를 전하러 왔노라고 패니에게 말씀하시는 것으로 설명하셨습니다."], ["The Young Person thereupon waited anxiously to hear what the messages were, but the Countess seemed disinclined to mention them (so like my sister’s tiresome way!) until Fanny had had her tea.", "그러자 그 젊은 여자는 그 전갈이 무엇인지 애가 타서 들으려 했으나, 백작부인은 패니가 차를 다 마실 때까지는 그것을 말할 마음이 없어 보이셨다더군요 (내 누이의 그 성가신 방식 그대로지요!)."], ["Her ladyship was surprisingly kind and thoughtful about it (extremely unlike my sister), and said, “I am sure, my poor girl, you must want your tea.", "마님은 그 일에 놀라우리만치 다정하고 사려 깊으셨고 (내 누이와는 극히 딴판입니다), 이렇게 말씀하셨답니다. “가엾은 아이야, 네가 차를 마시고 싶은 게 틀림없구나."], ["We can let the messages wait till afterwards.", "전갈은 그다음으로 미뤄도 된단다."], ["Come, come, if nothing else will put you at your ease, I’ll make the tea and have a cup with you.”", "자, 자, 달리 네 마음을 편하게 할 것이 없다면, 내가 차를 끓여 너와 한 잔 같이 마시마.”"], ["I think those were the words, as reported excitably, in my presence, by the Young Person.", "내 앞에서 그 젊은 여자가 흥분해서 전한 바로는 그 말이 그러했던 것 같습니다."], ["At any rate, the Countess insisted on making the tea, and carried her ridiculous ostentation of humility so far as to take one cup herself, and to insist on the girl’s taking the other.", "아무튼 백작부인은 기어이 차를 끓이겠다고 우기셨고, 겸손을 내세우는 그 우스꽝스러운 과시를 어찌나 밀고 나가셨는지 손수 한 잔을 드시고는 다른 한 잔을 그 아이에게 기어이 마시게 하셨습니다."], ["The girl drank the tea, and according to her own account, solemnised the extraordinary occasion five minutes afterwards by fainting dead away for the first time in her life.", "그 아이는 차를 마셨고, 자기 말로는 오 분 뒤에 난생처음 까무러쳐 기절함으로써 그 예사롭지 않은 자리를 엄숙히 기렸다고 합니다."], ["Here again I use her own words.", "여기서도 역시 그 아이 자신의 말을 씁니다."], ["Louis thinks they were accompanied by an increased secretion of tears.", "루이는 그 말에 분비물의 증가가 곁들여졌다고 생각합니다."], ["I can’t say myself.", "나로서는 뭐라 말할 수 없습니다."], ["The effort of listening being quite as much as I could manage, my eyes were closed.", "듣는 노력만으로도 내가 감당할 수 있는 전부였던지라 내 눈은 감겨 있었으니까요."]], [["Where did I leave off?", "어디까지 이야기했더라?"], ["Ah, yes—she fainted after drinking a cup of tea with the Countess—a proceeding which might have interested me if I had been her medical man, but being nothing of the sort I felt bored by hearing of it, nothing more.", "아, 그렇지—백작부인과 차를 한 잔 마신 뒤에 기절했다고 했지요. 내가 그 아이의 주치의였다면 흥미가 갔을 법한 사건이지만, 나는 그런 것이 전혀 아니므로 그 이야기를 듣는 것이 지루했을 뿐 그 이상은 아무것도 아니었습니다."], ["When she came to herself in half an hour’s time she was on the sofa, and nobody was with her but the landlady.", "삼십 분쯤 뒤에 정신을 차렸을 때 그 아이는 소파에 누워 있었고, 곁에는 여관 안주인 말고는 아무도 없었습니다."], ["The Countess, finding it too late to remain any longer at the inn, had gone away as soon as the girl showed signs of recovering, and the landlady had been good enough to help her upstairs to bed.", "백작부인은 여관에 더 머물기에는 너무 늦었다고 보시고 그 아이가 깨어날 낌새를 보이자마자 떠나셨고, 여관 안주인이 친절하게도 그 아이를 부축해 위층 잠자리로 데려다주었습니다."]], [["Left by herself, she had felt in her bosom (I regret the necessity of referring to this part of the subject a second time), and had found the two letters there quite safe, but strangely crumpled.", "혼자 남겨진 뒤 그 아이는 품속을 더듬어 보고 (이 부분을 두 번째로 언급해야 하는 것이 유감입니다), 편지 두 통이 무사히 거기 있으나 이상하게 구겨져 있는 것을 발견했습니다."], ["She had been giddy in the night, but had got up well enough to travel in the morning.", "그 아이는 밤새 어지러웠지만 아침에는 길을 떠날 만큼은 추슬러 일어났습니다."], ["She had put the letter addressed to that obtrusive stranger, the gentleman in London into the post, and had now delivered the other letter into my hands as she was told.", "그 아이는 저 주제넘은 낯선 사람, 런던의 그 신사 앞으로 된 편지를 우체통에 넣었고, 이제 다른 편지를 시킨 대로 내 손에 건넸습니다."], ["This was the plain truth, and though she could not blame herself for any intentional neglect, she was sadly troubled in her mind, and sadly in want of a word of advice.", "이것이 있는 그대로의 사실이며, 일부러 소홀히 한 것으로 자신을 탓할 수는 없다 해도 그 아이는 마음이 몹시 괴로웠고 한마디 조언이 몹시 아쉬웠다는 것이었습니다."], ["At this point Louis thinks the secretions appeared again.", "이 대목에서 루이는 분비물이 다시 나타났다고 생각합니다."], ["Perhaps they did, but it is of infinitely greater importance to mention that at this point also I lost my patience, opened my eyes, and interfered.", "아마 그랬을 테지만, 그보다 무한히 더 중요한 것은 바로 이 대목에서 나 또한 인내심을 잃고 눈을 뜨고 끼어들었다는 사실입니다."]], [["“What is the purport of all this?”", "“이 모든 이야기의 요지가 무엇이냐?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["My niece’s irrelevant maid stared, and stood speechless.", "엉뚱하게 겉도는 내 조카딸의 하녀는 나를 빤히 쳐다보며 말없이 서 있었습니다."]], [["“Endeavour to explain,”", "“설명하도록 애써 보아라.”"], ["I said to my servant.", "나는 하인에게 말했습니다."], ["“Translate me, Louis.”", "“나를 통역해라, 루이.”"]], [["Louis endeavoured and translated.", "루이가 애를 썼고 통역을 했습니다."], ["In other words, he descended immediately into a bottomless pit of confusion, and the Young Person followed him down.", "다시 말해 그는 즉시 바닥없는 혼란의 구렁으로 굴러떨어졌고, 그 젊은 여자도 뒤따라 굴러떨어졌습니다."], ["I really don’t know when I have been so amused.", "내가 언제 이토록 즐거웠는지 정말 모르겠습니다."], ["I left them at the bottom of the pit as long as they diverted me.", "나는 그들이 나를 즐겁게 해 주는 동안에는 구렁 바닥에 그대로 내버려 두었습니다."], ["When they ceased to divert me, I exerted my intelligence, and pulled them up again.", "그들이 더는 즐겁게 해 주지 못하게 되자 나는 지력을 발휘하여 그들을 다시 끌어올려 주었습니다."]], [["It is unnecessary to say that my interference enabled me, in due course of time, to ascertain the purport of the Young Person’s remarks.", "내가 끼어든 덕분에 때가 이르러 그 젊은 여자가 하는 말의 요지를 알아낼 수 있었다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지요."]], [["I discovered that she was uneasy in her mind, because the train of events that she had just described to me had prevented her from receiving those supplementary messages which Miss Halcombe had intrusted to the Countess to deliver.", "내가 알아낸 바는 이러합니다. 그 아이는 방금 나에게 늘어놓은 그 일련의 사건들 때문에, 홀콤 양이 백작부인에게 전해 달라고 맡긴 그 나머지 전갈을 받지 못하게 되어 마음이 편치 않다는 것이었습니다."], ["She was afraid the messages might have been of great importance to her mistress’s interests.", "그 전갈이 자기 마님의 이해에 대단히 중요한 것이었을까 봐 두려웠던 겁니다."], ["Her dread of Sir Percival had deterred her from going to Blackwater Park late at night to inquire about them, and Miss Halcombe’s own directions to her, on no account to miss the train in the morning, had prevented her from waiting at the inn the next day.", "퍼시벌 경이 무서워서 밤늦게 블랙워터 파크로 그것을 물으러 가지 못했고, 아침 기차를 절대 놓치지 말라는 홀콤 양 자신의 당부 때문에 이튿날 여관에서 기다릴 수도 없었다고 합니다."], ["She was most anxious that the misfortune of her fainting-fit should not lead to the second misfortune of making her mistress think her neglectful, and she would humbly beg to ask me whether I would advise her to write her explanations and excuses to Miss Halcombe, requesting to receive the messages by letter, if it was not too late.", "그 아이는 자기가 기절한 불행이 마님으로 하여금 자기를 소홀한 아이로 여기게 만드는 두 번째 불행으로 이어지지 않기를 몹시 애태우며 바랐고, 감히 여쭙건대 자기가 홀콤 양께 사정과 변명을 적어 보내고 아직 너무 늦지 않았다면 그 전갈을 편지로 받게 해 달라고 청하는 편이 좋겠는지 조언해 주십사 하는 것이었습니다."], ["I make no apologies for this extremely prosy paragraph.", "나는 이 지극히 따분한 문단에 대해 아무 변명도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["I have been ordered to write it.", "이것을 쓰라는 명을 받았으니까요."], ["There are people, unaccountable as it may appear, who actually take more interest in what my niece’s maid said to me on this occasion than in what I said to my niece’s maid.", "도무지 이해할 수 없는 노릇이지만, 이 자리에서 내가 조카딸의 하녀에게 한 말보다 조카딸의 하녀가 나에게 한 말에 실제로 더 관심을 갖는 사람들이 있습니다."], ["Amusing perversity!", "재미있는 심사의 뒤틀림이지요!"]], [["“I should feel very much obliged to you, sir, if you would kindly tell me what I had better do,”", "“나리, 제가 어떻게 하는 게 좋을지 일러 주시면 참으로 감사하겠습니다.”"], ["remarked the Young Person.", "그 젊은 여자가 말했습니다."]], [["“Let things stop as they are,”", "“일이란 있는 그대로 두는 법이다.”"], ["I said, adapting my language to my listener.", "나는 듣는 이에게 말을 맞추어 대답했습니다."], ["“I invariably let things stop as they are.", "“나는 언제나 일을 있는 그대로 둔다."], ["Yes.", "그래."], ["Is that all?”", "그게 전부냐?”"]], [["“If you think it would be a liberty in me, sir, to write, of course I wouldn’t venture to do so.", "“나리, 제가 편지를 쓰는 것이 주제넘은 짓이라 여기신다면 물론 감히 그러지 않겠습니다."], ["But I am so very anxious to do all I can to serve my mistress faithfully——”", "다만 마님을 충실히 모시려고 제가 할 수 있는 일은 다 하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절한지——”"]], [["People in the lower class of life never know when or how to go out of a room.", "하층 계급 사람들은 언제 어떻게 방에서 물러나야 하는지 도무지 알지 못합니다."], ["They invariably require to be helped out by their betters.", "그들은 어김없이 윗사람이 물러나도록 도와주기를 바라지요."], ["I thought it high time to help the Young Person out.", "나는 그 젊은 여자를 물러나게 도와줄 때가 무르익었다고 보았습니다."], ["I did it with two judicious words—", "나는 슬기로운 두 마디로 그 일을 해냈습니다—"]], [["“Good-morning.”", "“그만 가 보아라.”"]], [["Something outside or inside this singular girl suddenly creaked.", "이 별난 아이의 몸 밖에서인지 안에서인지 갑자기 삐걱 소리가 났습니다."], ["Louis, who was looking at her (which I was not), says she creaked when she curtseyed.", "그 아이를 보고 있던 (나는 보고 있지 않았습니다) 루이는, 그 아이가 무릎을 굽혀 인사할 때 삐걱거렸다고 합니다."], ["Curious.", "희한한 일이지요."], ["Was it her shoes, her stays, or her bones?", "신발이었을까요, 코르셋이었을까요, 아니면 뼈였을까요?"], ["Louis thinks it was her stays.", "루이는 코르셋이었으리라고 생각합니다."], ["Most extraordinary!", "참으로 기이한 일입니다!"]], [["As soon as I was left by myself I had a little nap—I really wanted it.", "혼자 남게 되자마자 나는 잠깐 눈을 붙였습니다—정말이지 그럴 필요가 있었지요."], ["When I awoke again I noticed dear Marian’s letter.", "다시 깨어났을 때 나는 사랑스러운 매리언의 편지를 알아보았습니다."], ["If I had had the least idea of what it contained I should certainly not have attempted to open it.", "그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 조금이라도 짐작했더라면 나는 분명 그것을 열어 볼 생각조차 하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["Being, unfortunately for myself, quite innocent of all suspicion, I read the letter.", "불행히도 아무런 의심 없이 순진하기만 했던 나는 그 편지를 읽었습니다."], ["It immediately upset me for the day.", "그것은 즉시 그날 하루를 망쳐 놓았습니다."]], [["I am, by nature, one of the most easy-tempered creatures that ever lived—I make allowances for everybody, and I take offence at nothing.", "나는 천성이 이제껏 살았던 누구보다도 성품이 무던한 사람입니다—누구에게나 너그럽고, 무엇에도 화를 내지 않지요."], ["But as I have before remarked, there are limits to my endurance.", "그러나 앞서도 말했듯이 내 인내에도 한계가 있습니다."], ["I laid down Marian’s letter, and felt myself—justly felt myself—an injured man.", "나는 매리언의 편지를 내려놓았고, 스스로를—당연히 스스로를—피해 입은 사람으로 느꼈습니다."]], [["I am about to make a remark.", "한마디 하려 합니다."], ["It is, of course, applicable to the very serious matter now under notice, or I should not allow it to appear in this place.", "물론 지금 다루고 있는 매우 중대한 사안에 해당되는 말이니, 그렇지 않다면 이 자리에 적히도록 허락하지 않았을 겁니다."]], [["Nothing, in my opinion, sets the odious selfishness of mankind in such a repulsively vivid light as the treatment, in all classes of society, which the Single people receive at the hands of the Married people.", "내 생각으로는, 모든 계층의 사회에서 결혼한 사람들의 손에 독신인 사람들이 받는 대접만큼 인류의 밉살스러운 이기심을 역겨우리만치 뚜렷하게 드러내 보이는 것도 없습니다."], ["When you have once shown yourself too considerate and self-denying to add a family of your own to an already overcrowded population, you are vindictively marked out by your married friends, who have no similar consideration and no similar self-denial, as the recipient of half their conjugal troubles, and the born friend of all their children.", "이미 넘칠 만큼 붐비는 인구에 제 가족까지 보태지 않을 만큼 사려 깊고 자기를 억제하는 사람임을 한 번 보여 놓으면, 그런 사려도 그런 자기 억제도 없는 결혼한 친구들이 앙심이라도 품은 듯 당신을 찍어 두고는, 자기네 부부 우환의 절반을 받아 주는 사람이자 자기네 자식들 모두의 타고난 벗으로 삼습니다."], ["Husbands and wives TALK of the cares of matrimony, and bachelors and spinsters BEAR them.", "남편과 아내들은 결혼 생활의 근심을 말로 하고, 총각과 노처녀들은 그것을 짊어집니다."], ["Take my own case.", "내 경우를 보십시오."], ["I considerately remain single, and my poor dear brother Philip inconsiderately marries.", "나는 사려 깊게도 독신으로 남고, 내 가엾은 형 필립은 사려 없이 결혼합니다."], ["What does he do when he dies?", "그가 죽을 때 무슨 짓을 합니까?"], ["He leaves his daughter to ME.", "딸을 나에게 남깁니다."], ["She is a sweet girl—she is also a dreadful responsibility.", "그 아이는 사랑스러운 아이입니다—또한 무시무시한 책임이기도 합니다."], ["Why lay her on my shoulders?", "어째서 그것을 내 어깨에 지웁니까?"], ["Because I am bound, in the harmless character of a single man, to relieve my married connections of all their own troubles.", "내가 독신 남자라는 무해한 신분에 매여, 결혼한 인척들의 우환을 죄다 덜어 줄 의무가 있기 때문이지요."], ["I do my best with my brother’s responsibility—I marry my niece, with infinite fuss and difficulty, to the man her father wanted her to marry.", "나는 형이 남긴 책임을 최선을 다해 감당합니다—무한한 소란과 어려움을 무릅쓰고, 그 아비가 원했던 사내에게 조카딸을 시집보냅니다."], ["She and her husband disagree, and unpleasant consequences follow.", "그 아이와 남편이 사이가 틀어지고, 달갑지 않은 결과가 따릅니다."], ["What does she do with those consequences?", "그 아이는 그 결과를 어떻게 합니까?"], ["She transfers them to ME.", "그것을 나에게 떠넘깁니다."], ["Why transfer them to ME?", "어째서 나에게 떠넘깁니까?"], ["Because I am bound, in the harmless character of a single man, to relieve my married connections of all their own troubles.", "내가 독신 남자라는 무해한 신분에 매여, 결혼한 인척들의 우환을 죄다 덜어 줄 의무가 있기 때문입니다."], ["Poor single people!", "가엾은 독신자들이여!"], ["Poor human nature!", "가엾은 인간 본성이여!"]], [["It is quite unnecessary to say that Marian’s letter threatened me.", "매리언의 편지가 나를 위협했다는 것은 굳이 말할 필요도 없습니다."], ["Everybody threatens me.", "누구나 나를 위협하니까요."], ["All sorts of horrors were to fall on my devoted head if I hesitated to turn Limmeridge House into an asylum for my niece and her misfortunes.", "리머리지 하우스를 조카딸과 그 아이의 불운을 위한 피난처로 내주기를 내가 망설이기라도 하면 온갖 끔찍한 일이 이 헌신적인 내 머리 위로 떨어질 참이었습니다."], ["I did hesitate, nevertheless.", "그럼에도 나는 망설였습니다."]], [["I have mentioned that my usual course, hitherto, had been to submit to dear Marian, and save noise.", "지금껏 내 통상적인 방침은 사랑스러운 매리언에게 굴복하여 소란을 더는 것이었다고 앞서 말한 바 있습니다."], ["But on this occasion, the consequences involved in her extremely inconsiderate proposal were of a nature to make me pause.", "그러나 이번만은 그 아이의 지극히 사려 없는 제안에 딸린 결과들이 나를 멈칫하게 할 성질의 것이었습니다."], ["If I opened Limmeridge House as an asylum to Lady Glyde, what security had I against Sir Percival Glyde’s following her here in a state of violent resentment against ME for harbouring his wife?", "내가 리머리지 하우스를 글라이드 부인의 피난처로 열어 준다면, 아내를 숨겨 주었다고 나에게 격렬한 원한을 품은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 그 뒤를 따라 여기까지 오는 일에 대해 내게 무슨 안전 보장이 있겠습니까?"], ["I saw such a perfect labyrinth of troubles involved in this proceeding that I determined to feel my ground, as it were.", "나는 이 일에 얽힌 우환의 미로가 어찌나 완벽한지 알아보고, 말하자면 땅을 짚어 보기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["I wrote, therefore, to dear Marian to beg (as she had no husband to lay claim to her) that she would come here by herself, first, and talk the matter over with me.", "그리하여 나는 사랑스러운 매리언에게 편지를 써서 (그 아이에게는 권리를 주장할 남편이 없으니) 먼저 그 아이 혼자 이리로 와서 그 문제를 나와 의논해 달라고 청했습니다."], ["If she could answer my objections to my own perfect satisfaction, then I assured her that I would receive our sweet Laura with the greatest pleasure, but not otherwise.", "그 아이가 내 이의에 나 자신이 아주 흡족할 만큼 답해 준다면 그때는 우리의 사랑스러운 로라를 더없는 기쁨으로 맞이하겠으나 그렇지 않으면 안 된다고 다짐해 두었습니다."]], [["I felt, of course, at the time, that this temporising on my part would probably end in bringing Marian here in a state of virtuous indignation, banging doors.", "물론 그때 나는 이렇게 시간을 버는 내 처사가 아마도 매리언을 의로운 분노에 차서 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 여기로 오게 만드는 것으로 끝나리라고 느꼈습니다."], ["But then, the other course of proceeding might end in bringing Sir Percival here in a state of virtuous indignation, banging doors also, and of the two indignations and bangings I preferred Marian’s, because I was used to her.", "그러나 다른 쪽 방침은 퍼시벌 경을 의로운 분노에 차서 역시 문을 쾅쾅 닫으며 여기로 오게 만드는 것으로 끝날 수도 있었고, 두 가지 분노와 두 가지 쾅쾅거림 가운데 나는 매리언 쪽을 골랐습니다. 그 아이 쪽에는 익숙했으니까요."], ["Accordingly I despatched the letter by return of post.", "그리하여 나는 그 편지를 회신 우편으로 부쳤습니다."], ["It gained me time, at all events—and, oh dear me!", "어쨌든 그것은 나에게 시간을 벌어 주었습니다—그리고, 아이고 저런!"], ["what a point that was to begin with.", "그것만 해도 시작으로는 얼마나 대단한 것이었는지요."]], [["When I am totally prostrated (did I mention that I was totally prostrated by Marian’s letter?) it always takes me three days to get up again.", "완전히 기진해 버리면 (매리언의 편지로 내가 완전히 기진해 버렸다는 말을 했던가요?) 나는 언제나 다시 일어나는 데 사흘이 걸립니다."], ["I was very unreasonable—I expected three days of quiet.", "나는 무척 억지스러운 사람이었지요—사흘의 평온을 기대했으니까요."], ["Of course I didn’t get them.", "물론 그것을 얻지 못했습니다."]], [["The third day’s post brought me a most impertinent letter from a person with whom I was totally unacquainted.", "사흘째 되는 날 우편으로 전혀 알지 못하는 사람에게서 지극히 무례한 편지가 왔습니다."], ["He described himself as the acting partner of our man of business—our dear, pig-headed old Gilmore—and he informed me that he had lately received, by the post, a letter addressed to him in Miss Halcombe’s handwriting.", "그는 자기를 우리 집안 사무를 보아 주는 사람—우리의 사랑스럽고 고집불통인 늙은 길모어—의 업무 집행 동업자라고 밝히고는, 최근 우편으로 홀콤 양의 필적으로 자기 앞으로 온 편지를 한 통 받았다고 알려 왔습니다."], ["On opening the envelope, he had discovered, to his astonishment, that it contained nothing but a blank sheet of note-paper.", "봉투를 열어 보니 놀랍게도 그 안에는 백지 편지지 한 장밖에 들어 있지 않더라는 것이었습니다."], ["This circumstance appeared to him so suspicious (as suggesting to his restless legal mind that the letter had been tampered with) that he had at once written to Miss Halcombe, and had received no answer by return of post.", "이 정황이 그에게는 어찌나 수상쩍어 보였던지 (가만있지 못하는 그 법률가의 머리에 편지가 손을 탔다는 생각이 떠올랐던 거지요) 그는 즉시 홀콤 양에게 편지를 썼으나 회신 우편으로 답을 받지 못했다고 합니다."], ["In this difficulty, instead of acting like a sensible man and letting things take their proper course, his next absurd proceeding, on his own showing, was to pester me by writing to inquire if I knew anything about it.", "이 곤경에서 분별 있는 사람답게 처신하여 일이 제 갈 길을 가도록 두는 대신, 그의 다음번 어처구니없는 처사는 자기 입으로 밝힌 바에 따르면 나에게 편지를 써서 내가 그 일에 대해 무언가 아는 것이 있는지 묻는 것으로 나를 성가시게 구는 일이었습니다. 대체 내가 그 일에 대해 무엇을 안다는 말입니까?"], ["What the deuce should I know about it?", "어째서 저 혼자 놀라는 데 그치지 않고 나까지 놀라게 합니까?"], ["Why alarm me as well as himself?", "나는 그런 뜻으로 답장을 썼습니다."], ["I wrote back to that effect. It was one of my keenest letters.", "내가 쓴 편지 중에서도 손꼽히게 날 선 편지였지요."], ["I have produced nothing with a sharper epistolary edge to it since I tendered his dismissal in writing to that extremely troublesome person, Mr. Walter Hartright.", "저 지극히 성가신 사람, 월터 하트라이트 씨에게 서면으로 해고를 통고한 이래로 그보다 날카로운 서한의 날을 세워 본 적이 없습니다."]], [["My letter produced its effect.", "내 편지는 효과가 있었습니다."], ["I heard nothing more from the lawyer.", "그 변호사에게서는 더 이상 아무 소식도 없었습니다."]], [["This perhaps was not altogether surprising.", "이것은 어쩌면 그리 놀랄 일이 아니었을 겁니다."], ["But it was certainly a remarkable circumstance that no second letter reached me from Marian, and that no warning signs appeared of her arrival.", "그러나 매리언에게서 두 번째 편지가 오지 않았고 그 아이가 도착할 조짐도 보이지 않았다는 것은 분명 주목할 만한 정황이었습니다. 그 아이가 뜻밖에도 나타나지 않은 것은 나에게 놀랍도록 좋은 일을 해 주었습니다."], ["Her unexpected absence did me amazing good. It was so very soothing and pleasant to infer (as I did of course) that my married connections had made it up again.", "내 결혼한 인척들이 다시 화해했다고 (물론 나는 그렇게 추론했지요) 미루어 짐작하는 것은 어찌나 마음을 달래 주고 흐뭇한 일이던지요."], ["Five days of undisturbed tranquillity, of delicious single blessedness, quite restored me.", "방해받지 않은 닷새의 고요, 감미로운 독신의 축복이 나를 아주 회복시켜 주었습니다."], ["On the sixth day I felt strong enough to send for my photographer, and to set him at work again on the presentation copies of my art-treasures, with a view, as I have already mentioned, to the improvement of taste in this barbarous neighbourhood.", "엿새째 되는 날 나는 사진사를 부르러 보내, 앞서 말씀드린 대로 이 야만스러운 이웃의 안목을 개선할 목적으로 내 미술 보물들의 기증용 복제본 작업을 그에게 다시 맡길 만큼 기운을 차렸습니다."], ["I had just dismissed him to his workshop, and had just begun coquetting with my coins, when Louis suddenly made his appearance with a card in his hand.", "막 그를 작업실로 물려 보내고 막 내 주화들과 시시덕거리기 시작했을 때, 루이가 명함 한 장을 손에 들고 불쑥 나타났습니다."]], [["“Another Young Person?” I said.", "“또 젊은 여자냐?”"], ["“I won’t see her.", "내가 말했습니다."], ["In my state of health Young Persons disagree with me.", "“만나지 않겠다. 내 건강 상태로는 젊은 여자들이 몸에 맞지 않아."], ["Not at home.”", "집에 없다고 해라.”"]], [["“It is a gentleman this time, sir.”", "“이번에는 신사분입니다, 나리.”"]], [["A gentleman of course made a difference.", "신사라면 물론 이야기가 달라집니다."], ["I looked at the card.", "나는 명함을 들여다보았습니다."]], [["Gracious Heaven!", "이런 세상에!"], ["my tiresome sister’s foreign husband, Count Fosco.", "내 성가신 누이의 외국인 남편, 포스코 백작이었습니다."]], [["Is it necessary to say what my first impression was when I looked at my visitor’s card?", "손님의 명함을 보았을 때 내 첫인상이 어떠했는지 굳이 말해야 할까요? 분명 그럴 필요 없겠지요!"], ["Surely not! My sister having married a foreigner, there was but one impression that any man in his senses could possibly feel.", "누이가 외국인과 결혼한 마당이니, 제정신인 사람이라면 도무지 하나의 인상밖에 가질 수 없었습니다."], ["Of course the Count had come to borrow money of me.", "물론 백작은 나에게 돈을 꾸러 온 것이었지요."]], [["“Louis,”", "“루이,”"], ["I said, “do you think he would go away if you gave him five shillings?”", "내가 말했습니다. “네가 오 실링을 주면 저 사람이 돌아갈 것 같으냐?”"]], [["Louis looked quite shocked.", "루이는 몹시 충격을 받은 표정이었습니다."], ["He surprised me inexpressibly by declaring that my sister’s foreign husband was dressed superbly, and looked the picture of prosperity.", "그는 내 누이의 외국인 남편이 훌륭하게 차려입었고 부유함의 표본으로 보인다고 말해서 나를 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했습니다."], ["Under these circumstances my first impression altered to a certain extent.", "이런 사정이고 보니 내 첫인상은 어느 정도 바뀌었습니다."], ["I now took it for granted that the Count had matrimonial difficulties of his own to contend with, and that he had come, like the rest of the family, to cast them all on my shoulders.", "나는 이제 백작이 감당해야 할 자기 나름의 부부 문제가 있어서, 집안의 나머지 사람들처럼 그것을 죄다 내 어깨에 지우러 왔으리라고 당연시하게 되었습니다."]], [["“Did he mention his business?”", "“무슨 용건인지 말하더냐?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었습니다."]], [["“Count Fosco said he had come here, sir, because Miss Halcombe was unable to leave Blackwater Park.”", "“포스코 백작께서는 홀콤 양이 블랙워터 파크를 떠날 수 없어서 이리로 오셨다고 하십니다, 나리.”"]], [["Fresh troubles, apparently.", "또 새로운 우환이로군요."], ["Not exactly his own, as I had supposed, but dear Marian’s.", "짐작했던 것처럼 딱히 그 사람 자신의 것은 아니고 사랑스러운 매리언의 것이었습니다."], ["Troubles, anyway.", "어쨌거나 우환입니다."], ["Oh dear!", "아이고 저런!"]], [["“Show him in,”", "“들여보내라.”"], ["I said resignedly.", "나는 체념하여 말했습니다."]], [["The Count’s first appearance really startled me.", "백작이 처음 나타났을 때 저는 정말이지 깜짝 놀랐습니다."], ["He was such an alarmingly large person that I quite trembled.", "어찌나 겁이 날 만큼 덩치가 큰 사람이던지 저는 그만 몸이 떨렸습니다."], ["I felt certain that he would shake the floor and knock down my art-treasures.", "저 사람이 마루를 흔들어 제 미술품들을 넘어뜨리고 말리라 확신했지요."], ["He did neither the one nor the other.", "그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 하지 않았습니다."], ["He was refreshingly dressed in summer costume—his manner was delightfully self-possessed and quiet—he had a charming smile.", "여름옷 차림은 보기만 해도 시원했고, 태도는 더없이 침착하고 조용했으며, 미소는 매력적이었습니다."], ["My first impression of him was highly favourable.", "그에 대한 저의 첫인상은 대단히 호의적이었습니다."], ["It is not creditable to my penetration—as the sequel will show—to acknowledge this, but I am a naturally candid man, and I DO acknowledge it notwithstanding.", "뒤에 이어질 일이 보여주듯 이렇게 인정하는 것은 제 안목에 그리 명예로운 일이 아닙니다만, 저는 천성이 솔직한 사람이라 그럼에도 불구하고 인정합니다."]], [["“Allow me to present myself, Mr.", "“제 소개를 하도록 허락해 주십시오, 페얼리 씨.”"], ["Fairlie,” he said.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["“I come from Blackwater Park, and I have the honour and the happiness of being Madame Fosco’s husband.", "“저는 블랙워터 파크에서 왔고, 포스코 백작부인의 남편이 되는 영광과 행복을 누리고 있는 사람입니다."], ["Let me take my first and last advantage of that circumstance by entreating you not to make a stranger of me.", "그 인연을 처음이자 마지막으로 이용해, 저를 낯선 사람으로 대하지 말아 주십사 간청드리겠습니다."], ["I beg you will not disturb yourself—I beg you will not move.”", "부디 몸을 수고롭게 하지 마십시오. 부디 움직이지 마십시오.”"]], [["“You are very good,”", "“참으로 친절하십니다.”"], ["I replied.", "제가 대답했습니다."], ["“I wish I was strong enough to get up.", "“일어설 만큼 기운이 있으면 좋으련만."], ["Charmed to see you at Limmeridge.", "리머리지에서 뵙게 되어 참으로 기쁩니다."], ["Please take a chair.”", "부디 의자에 앉으시지요.”"]], [["“I am afraid you are suffering to-day,”", "“오늘은 편찮으신 모양이군요.”"], ["said the Count.", "백작이 말했습니다."]], [["“As usual,” I said.", "“늘 그렇습니다.”"], ["“I am nothing but a bundle of nerves dressed up to look like a man.”", "제가 말했습니다. “저는 사람처럼 보이게 차려입은 신경 뭉치에 지나지 않습니다.”"]], [["“I have studied many subjects in my time,”", "“저는 지금까지 여러 분야를 공부해 왔습니다.”"], ["remarked this sympathetic person.", "이 인정 많은 인물이 말했습니다."], ["“Among others the inexhaustible subject of nerves.", "“그중에는 끝을 알 수 없는 신경이라는 주제도 있었지요."], ["May I make a suggestion, at once the simplest and the most profound?", "지극히 간단하면서도 지극히 깊은 제안을 하나 드려도 되겠습니까?"], ["Will you let me alter the light in your room?”", "이 방의 빛을 제가 좀 손보아도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Certainly—if you will be so very kind as not to let any of it in on me.”", "“그러시지요—그 빛이 조금이라도 제게 들이치지 않게만 해 주신다면 말입니다.”"]], [["He walked to the window.", "그는 창가로 걸어갔습니다."], ["Such a contrast to dear Marian!", "사랑스러운 매리언과는 어찌나 대조적인지!"], ["so extremely considerate in all his movements!", "몸짓 하나하나가 그토록 사려 깊다니!"]], [["“Light,”", "“빛이란,”"], ["he said, in that delightfully confidential tone which is so soothing to an invalid, “is the first essential.", "그가 병자에게 그토록 위안이 되는 그 유쾌하고도 은밀한 어조로 말했습니다. “무엇보다 먼저 필요한 것입니다."], ["Light stimulates, nourishes, preserves.", "빛은 자극하고, 기르고, 보존합니다."], ["You can no more do without it, Mr. Fairlie, than if you were a flower.", "페얼리 씨, 당신이 한 송이 꽃이라 해도 빛 없이는 살 수 없을 것입니다."], ["Observe.", "보십시오."], ["Here, where you sit, I close the shutters to compose you.", "여기, 당신이 앉아 계신 쪽은 마음을 가라앉히시도록 덧문을 닫습니다."], ["There, where you do NOT sit, I draw up the blind and let in the invigorating sun.", "저기, 당신이 앉아 계시지 않은 쪽은 발을 걷어 올려 기운을 돋우는 햇빛을 들입니다."], ["Admit the light into your room if you cannot bear it on yourself.", "몸에 직접 받으실 수 없다면 방 안에라도 빛을 들이십시오."], ["Light, sir, is the grand decree of Providence.", "선생, 빛은 섭리가 내린 위대한 명령입니다."], ["You accept Providence with your own restrictions.", "당신은 스스로 정한 제한을 두고 섭리를 받아들이시지요."], ["Accept light on the same terms.”", "빛도 같은 조건으로 받아들이십시오.”"]], [["I thought this very convincing and attentive.", "저는 이것이 무척 설득력 있고 세심하다고 생각했습니다."], ["He had taken me in up to that point about the light, he had certainly taken me in.", "빛에 관한 그 대목까지 그는 저를 감쪽같이 속였습니다. 분명히 속였지요."]], [["“You see me confused,”", "“제가 당황한 것이 보이시지요.”"], ["he said, returning to his place—“on my word of honour, Mr.", "그가 자기 자리로 돌아오며 말했습니다."], ["Fairlie, you see me confused in your presence.”", "“명예를 걸고 말씀드립니다만, 페얼리 씨, 저는 당신 앞에서 당황하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Shocked to hear it, I am sure.", "“그런 말씀을 들으니 참으로 놀랍습니다."], ["May I inquire why?”", "이유를 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Sir, can I enter this room (where you sit a sufferer), and see you surrounded by these admirable objects of Art, without discovering that you are a man whose feelings are acutely impressionable, whose sympathies are perpetually alive?", "“선생, 제가 이 방에—고통받는 분이 앉아 계신 이 방에—들어와, 당신이 이 감탄스러운 예술품들에 둘러싸여 계신 것을 보고서, 당신이 감정이 몹시 예민하고 공감이 끊임없이 살아 있는 분이라는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Tell me, can I do this?”", "말씀해 보십시오, 제가 그럴 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["If I had been strong enough to sit up in my chair I should, of course, have bowed.", "의자에 똑바로 앉을 만한 기운이 있었다면 저는 물론 고개를 숙여 보였을 것입니다."], ["Not being strong enough, I smiled my acknowledgments instead.", "그럴 기운이 없었으므로 대신 미소로 사의를 표했습니다."], ["It did just as well, we both understood one another.", "그것으로도 충분했고, 우리는 서로를 이해했습니다."]], [["“Pray follow my train of thought,”", "“부디 제 생각의 흐름을 따라와 주십시오.”"], ["continued the Count.", "백작이 말을 이었습니다."], ["“I sit here, a man of refined sympathies myself, in the presence of another man of refined sympathies also.", "“저 역시 섬세한 공감을 지닌 사람으로서, 마찬가지로 섬세한 공감을 지닌 또 한 사람 앞에 앉아 있습니다."], ["I am conscious of a terrible necessity for lacerating those sympathies by referring to domestic events of a very melancholy kind.", "그런데 저는 몹시 우울한 종류의 집안일을 꺼냄으로써 그 공감을 갈가리 찢어 놓아야 할 끔찍한 필요를 느끼고 있습니다."], ["What is the inevitable consequence?", "그 필연적인 결과가 무엇이겠습니까?"], ["I have done myself the honour of pointing it out to you already.", "이미 영광스럽게도 당신께 말씀드린 그대로입니다."], ["I sit confused.”", "저는 당황한 채 앉아 있습니다.”"]], [["Was it at this point that I began to suspect he was going to bore me?", "그가 나를 지루하게 만들려 한다고 제가 의심하기 시작한 것이 바로 이 대목이었을까요?"], ["I rather think it was.", "아마 그랬던 것 같습니다."]], [["“Is it absolutely necessary to refer to these unpleasant matters?”", "“그 불쾌한 일들을 꼭 꺼내야만 합니까?” 제가 물었습니다."], ["I inquired. “In our homely English phrase, Count Fosco, won’t they keep?”", "“우리 영국의 소박한 표현을 빌리자면, 포스코 백작, 그건 좀 묵혀 두면 안 되겠습니까?”"]], [["The Count, with the most alarming solemnity, sighed and shook his head.", "백작은 더없이 겁나는 엄숙함으로 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 저었습니다."]], [["“Must I really hear them?”", "“정말로 제가 그것을 들어야만 합니까?”"]], [["He shrugged his shoulders (it was the first foreign thing he had done since he had been in the room), and looked at me in an unpleasantly penetrating manner.", "그는 어깨를 으쓱했고(그가 이 방에 들어온 이래 처음으로 보인 외국식 몸짓이었습니다), 불쾌하리만치 꿰뚫어 보는 태도로 저를 바라보았습니다."], ["My instincts told me that I had better close my eyes.", "눈을 감는 편이 낫겠다고 제 본능이 일러 주었습니다."], ["I obeyed my instincts.", "저는 본능에 따랐습니다."]], [["“Please break it gently,”", "“제발 부드럽게 말씀해 주십시오.”"], ["I pleaded.", "제가 애원했습니다."], ["“Anybody dead?”", "“누가 죽었습니까?”"]], [["“Dead!”", "“죽다니요!”"], ["cried the Count, with unnecessary foreign fierceness.", "백작이 쓸데없이 외국식으로 격렬하게 외쳤습니다."], ["“Mr. Fairlie, your national composure terrifies me.", "“페얼리 씨, 당신네 국민 특유의 그 침착함이 저를 겁나게 합니다."], ["In the name of Heaven, what have I said or done to make you think me the messenger of death?”", "맙소사, 제가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 했기에 저를 죽음의 전령으로 여기시는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Pray accept my apologies,”", "“부디 제 사과를 받아 주십시오.”"], ["I answered.", "제가 대답했습니다."], ["“You have said and done nothing.", "“당신은 아무 말도, 아무 짓도 하지 않았습니다."], ["I make it a rule in these distressing cases always to anticipate the worst.", "저는 이런 괴로운 경우에 언제나 최악을 먼저 예상하는 것을 원칙으로 삼고 있습니다."], ["It breaks the blow by meeting it half-way, and so on.", "반쯤 마중 나가 맞으면 충격이 덜하니까요. 뭐 그런 것입니다."], ["Inexpressibly relieved, I am sure, to hear that nobody is dead.", "아무도 죽지 않았다니 이루 말할 수 없이 마음이 놓입니다."], ["Anybody ill?”", "누가 아픕니까?”"]], [["I opened my eyes and looked at him.", "저는 눈을 뜨고 그를 바라보았습니다."], ["Was he very yellow when he came in, or had he turned very yellow in the last minute or two?", "그는 들어올 때부터 그렇게 누렇던가요, 아니면 지난 한두 분 사이에 그렇게 누레진 것인가요?"], ["I really can’t say, and I can’t ask Louis, because he was not in the room at the time.", "저는 정말 알 수 없고, 루이에게 물어볼 수도 없습니다. 그때 루이는 방에 없었으니까요."]], [["“Anybody ill?”", "“누가 아픕니까?”"], ["I repeated, observing that my national composure still appeared to affect him.", "제가 되풀이했습니다. 제 국민 특유의 그 침착함이 여전히 그에게 영향을 미치는 듯 보였기 때문입니다."]], [["“That is part of my bad news, Mr.", "“그것이 제가 가져온 나쁜 소식의 일부입니다, 페얼리 씨."], ["Fairlie.", "그렇습니다."], ["Yes. Somebody is ill.”", "누군가 아픕니다.”"]], [["“Grieved, I am sure.", "“참으로 안타깝습니다."], ["Which of them is it?”", "그중 누구입니까?”"]], [["“To my profound sorrow, Miss Halcombe.", "“더없이 깊은 슬픔과 함께 말씀드리자면, 홀콤 양입니다."], ["Perhaps you were in some degree prepared to hear this?", "어쩌면 이 소식을 들으실 마음의 준비가 어느 정도는 되어 계셨는지도 모르겠군요?"], ["Perhaps when you found that Miss Halcombe did not come here by herself, as you proposed, and did not write a second time, your affectionate anxiety may have made you fear that she was ill?”", "제안하신 대로 홀콤 양이 혼자 이곳으로 오지도 않고 두 번째 편지도 쓰지 않은 것을 아셨을 때, 애정 어린 염려 때문에 혹 그분이 아픈 것은 아닐까 걱정하시지 않았습니까?”"]], [["I have no doubt my affectionate anxiety had led to that melancholy apprehension at some time or other, but at the moment my wretched memory entirely failed to remind me of the circumstance.", "제 애정 어린 염려가 언젠가 그런 우울한 걱정으로 이어졌으리라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없습니다만, 그 순간 제 형편없는 기억력은 그런 사정을 도무지 떠올려 주지 못했습니다."], ["However, I said yes, in justice to myself.", "그래도 저는 제 자신의 명예를 위해 그렇다고 말했습니다."], ["I was much shocked.", "저는 몹시 충격을 받았습니다."], ["It was so very uncharacteristic of such a robust person as dear Marian to be ill, that I could only suppose she had met with an accident.", "사랑스러운 매리언처럼 튼튼한 사람이 앓는다는 것은 너무나 그답지 않은 일이어서, 저는 그가 사고를 당했으리라고밖에 생각할 수 없었습니다."], ["A horse, or a false step on the stairs, or something of that sort.", "말이라든가, 계단에서 발을 헛디뎠다든가, 그런 종류의 일 말입니다."]], [["“Is it serious?”", "“심각합니까?”"], ["I asked.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Serious—beyond a doubt,” he replied.", "“심각합니다—의심할 여지 없이요.”"], ["“Dangerous—I hope and trust not.", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["Miss Halcombe unhappily exposed herself to be wetted through by a heavy rain.", "“위험하냐 하면—그렇지 않기를 바라고 또 믿습니다. 홀콤 양은 불행히도 억수 같은 비를 그대로 맞아 흠뻑 젖었습니다."], ["The cold that followed was of an aggravated kind, and it has now brought with it the worst consequence—fever.”", "그 뒤에 온 감기가 고약한 종류였고, 이제 그것이 최악의 결과를 불러왔습니다—열병입니다.”"]], [["When I heard the word fever, and when I remembered at the same moment that the unscrupulous person who was now addressing me had just come from Blackwater Park, I thought I should have fainted on the spot.", "열병이라는 말을 듣고, 동시에 지금 제게 말을 걸고 있는 이 파렴치한 인물이 방금 블랙워터 파크에서 왔다는 사실을 떠올렸을 때, 저는 그 자리에서 기절하는 줄 알았습니다."]], [["“Good God!” I said.", "“맙소사!”"], ["“Is it infectious?”", "제가 말했습니다. “전염됩니까?”"]], [["“Not at present,”", "“지금은 아닙니다.”"], ["he answered, with detestable composure.", "그가 밉살스러울 만큼 침착하게 대답했습니다."], ["“It may turn to infection—but no such deplorable complication had taken place when I left Blackwater Park.", "“전염성으로 바뀔 수도 있습니다만, 제가 블랙워터 파크를 떠날 때까지는 그런 개탄스러운 사태는 일어나지 않았습니다."], ["I have felt the deepest interest in the case, Mr. Fairlie—I have endeavoured to assist the regular medical attendant in watching it—accept my personal assurances of the uninfectious nature of the fever when I last saw it.”", "저는 이 병에 깊은 관심을 기울여 왔습니다, 페얼리 씨—담당 의사를 도와 병세를 지켜보려고 애써 왔습니다—제가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그 열병에 전염성이 없었다는 저의 개인적인 보증을 받아 주십시오.”"]], [["Accept his assurances!", "그의 보증을 받아 달라니!"], ["I never was farther from accepting anything in my life.", "제 평생 무언가를 받아들이는 것과 그토록 거리가 멀었던 적은 없었습니다."], ["I would not have believed him on his oath.", "그가 맹세를 했더라도 저는 믿지 않았을 것입니다."], ["He was too yellow to be believed.", "그는 믿기에는 너무 누랬습니다."], ["He looked like a walking-West-Indian-epidemic.", "걸어 다니는 서인도제도 전염병 같았습니다."], ["He was big enough to carry typhus by the ton, and to dye the very carpet he walked on with scarlet fever.", "발진티푸스를 톤 단위로 나르고, 밟고 다니는 양탄자마다 성홍열로 물들일 만큼 덩치가 컸습니다."], ["In certain emergencies my mind is remarkably soon made up.", "어떤 위급한 상황에서는 제 마음이 놀랄 만큼 빨리 정해집니다."], ["I instantly determined to get rid of him.", "저는 즉시 그를 쫓아내기로 결심했습니다."]], [["“You will kindly excuse an invalid,”", "“병자의 사정을 너그러이 헤아려 주십시오.”"], ["I said—“but long conferences of any kind invariably upset me.", "제가 말했습니다. “어떤 종류든 긴 이야기는 반드시 저를 무너뜨립니다."], ["May I beg to know exactly what the object is to which I am indebted for the honour of your visit?”", "이렇게 찾아와 주신 영광이 정확히 어떤 용건에서 비롯된 것인지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["I fervently hoped that this remarkably broad hint would throw him off his balance—confuse him—reduce him to polite apologies—in short, get him out of the room.", "저는 이 유난히 노골적인 암시가 그의 균형을 무너뜨리고—당황하게 만들고—정중한 사과로 몰아넣어—요컨대 그를 방에서 내보내 주기를 간절히 바랐습니다."], ["On the contrary, it only settled him in his chair.", "그런데 정반대로, 그것은 그를 의자에 더 눌러앉혔을 뿐이었습니다."], ["He became additionally solemn, and dignified, and confidential.", "그는 한층 더 엄숙해지고, 위엄 있어지고, 은밀해졌습니다."], ["He held up two of his horrid fingers and gave me another of his unpleasantly penetrating looks.", "그는 그 끔찍한 손가락 두 개를 들어 보이더니 또 한 번 불쾌하리만치 꿰뚫는 눈길을 던졌습니다."], ["What was I to do?", "제가 어쩌겠습니까?"], ["I was not strong enough to quarrel with him.", "저는 그와 다툴 만한 기운이 없었습니다."], ["Conceive my situation, if you please.", "부디 제 처지를 헤아려 보십시오."], ["Is language adequate to describe it?", "말로 그것을 다 표현할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I think not.", "저는 없다고 봅니다."]], [["“The objects of my visit,”", "“제 방문의 용건은,”"], ["he went on, quite irrepressibly, “are numbered on my fingers.", "그가 도무지 걷잡을 수 없이 말을 이었습니다. “이 손가락에 헤아려 두었습니다."], ["They are two.", "두 가지입니다."], ["First, I come to bear my testimony, with profound sorrow, to the lamentable disagreements between Sir Percival and Lady Glyde.", "첫째, 저는 퍼시벌 경과 글라이드 부인 사이의 통탄스러운 불화에 대해 깊은 슬픔을 안고 증언하러 왔습니다."], ["I am Sir Percival’s oldest friend—I am related to Lady Glyde by marriage—I am an eye-witness of all that has happened at Blackwater Park.", "저는 퍼시벌 경의 가장 오랜 친구이고, 혼인으로 글라이드 부인과 인척이 된 사람이며, 블랙워터 파크에서 벌어진 모든 일을 직접 본 목격자입니다."], ["In those three capacities I speak with authority, with confidence, with honourable regret.", "그 세 가지 자격으로 저는 권위를 가지고, 확신을 가지고, 명예로운 유감을 담아 말씀드립니다."], ["Sir, I inform you, as the head of Lady Glyde’s family, that Miss Halcombe has exaggerated nothing in the letter which she wrote to your address.", "선생, 글라이드 부인 집안의 가장이신 당신께 알려 드립니다. 홀콤 양이 당신께 보낸 편지에서 과장한 것은 하나도 없습니다."], ["I affirm that the remedy which that admirable lady has proposed is the only remedy that will spare you the horrors of public scandal.", "저는 그 훌륭한 부인이 제안한 방책이야말로 당신을 세상의 추문이라는 끔찍한 일에서 구해 줄 유일한 방책이라고 단언합니다."], ["A temporary separation between husband and wife is the one peaceable solution of this difficulty.", "남편과 아내를 한동안 떼어 놓는 것이 이 난국의 유일하고도 평화로운 해결책입니다."], ["Part them for the present, and when all causes of irritation are removed, I, who have now the honour of addressing you—I will undertake to bring Sir Percival to reason.", "지금은 두 사람을 갈라놓으십시오. 그리고 갈등의 원인이 모두 사라졌을 때, 지금 당신께 말씀드리는 영광을 누리고 있는 제가—바로 제가 퍼시벌 경을 이치에 따르게 만들겠습니다."], ["Lady Glyde is innocent, Lady Glyde is injured, but—follow my thought here!—she is, on that very account (I say it with shame), the cause of irritation while she remains under her husband’s roof.", "글라이드 부인은 죄가 없고, 글라이드 부인은 상처받은 쪽입니다. 그러나—여기서 제 생각을 따라와 주십시오!—바로 그 때문에, 부인이 남편의 지붕 아래 머무는 한 부인은 (부끄러운 마음으로 말씀드립니다만) 갈등의 원인이 됩니다."], ["No other house can receive her with propriety but yours.", "부인을 온당하게 맞아들일 수 있는 집은 당신의 집밖에 없습니다."], ["I invite you to open it.”", "그 문을 열어 주시기를 청합니다.”"]], [["Cool.", "참으로 태연하더군요."], ["Here was a matrimonial hailstorm pouring in the South of England, and I was invited, by a man with fever in every fold of his coat, to come out from the North of England and take my share of the pelting.", "잉글랜드 남부에서 부부 싸움의 우박이 쏟아지고 있는데, 저더러 옷 주름마다 열병을 묻히고 온 사내의 초대를 받아 잉글랜드 북부에서 나가 그 우박을 나눠 맞으라는 것이었습니다."], ["I tried to put the point forcibly, just as I have put it here.", "저는 여기 적은 그대로 그 점을 힘주어 말해 보려 했습니다."], ["The Count deliberately lowered one of his horrid fingers, kept the other up, and went on—rode over me, as it were, without even the common coachmanlike attention of crying “Hi!”", "백작은 그 끔찍한 손가락 하나를 천천히 내리고 다른 하나는 세워 둔 채 말을 이어 갔습니다. 말하자면 저를 밟고 지나간 셈인데, 사람을 치기 전에 “이랴!”"], ["before he knocked me down.", "하고 외쳐 주는 마부의 흔한 배려조차 없었습니다."]], [["“Follow my thought once more, if you please,”", "“한 번만 더 제 생각을 따라와 주십시오.”"], ["he resumed.", "그가 다시 말했습니다."], ["“My first object you have heard.", "“첫 번째 용건은 들으셨습니다."], ["My second object in coming to this house is to do what Miss Halcombe’s illness has prevented her from doing for herself.", "제가 이 댁에 온 두 번째 용건은, 홀콤 양이 병 때문에 스스로 할 수 없게 된 일을 대신하는 것입니다."], ["My large experience is consulted on all difficult matters at Blackwater Park, and my friendly advice was requested on the interesting subject of your letter to Miss Halcombe.", "블랙워터 파크에서는 어려운 일이 있을 때마다 제 풍부한 경험에 자문을 구하는데, 홀콤 양께 보내신 편지라는 흥미로운 문제에 대해서도 제 우정 어린 조언을 청해 왔습니다."], ["I understood at once—for my sympathies are your sympathies—why you wished to see her here before you pledged yourself to inviting Lady Glyde.", "저는 즉시 알아들었습니다—제 공감은 곧 당신의 공감이니까요—글라이드 부인을 초대하겠다고 약속하기 전에 어째서 홀콤 양을 먼저 이곳에서 만나 보고 싶어 하셨는지를 말입니다."], ["You are most right, sir, in hesitating to receive the wife until you are quite certain that the husband will not exert his authority to reclaim her. I agree to that.", "선생, 남편이 아내를 되찾겠다고 권위를 휘두르지는 않으리라는 확신이 설 때까지 아내를 받아들이기를 망설이신 것은 지극히 옳습니다."], ["I also agree that such delicate explanations as this difficulty involves are not explanations which can be properly disposed of by writing only.", "저는 그 점에 동의합니다. 또한 이런 난국에 따르는 미묘한 설명은 편지만으로 온당하게 처리될 수 있는 성질의 것이 아니라는 데도 동의합니다."], ["My presence here (to my own great inconvenience) is the proof that I speak sincerely.", "제가 (제게는 대단히 불편한 일임에도) 여기 와 있다는 것이 제 진심의 증거입니다."], ["As for the explanations themselves, I—Fosco—I, who know Sir Percival much better than Miss Halcombe knows him, affirm to you, on my honour and my word, that he will not come near this house, or attempt to communicate with this house, while his wife is living in it.", "설명 그 자체로 말하자면, 저는—포스코는—홀콤 양보다 퍼시벌 경을 훨씬 잘 아는 이 사람은, 명예와 말을 걸고 당신께 단언합니다. 아내가 이 집에 사는 동안 그는 이 집 가까이 오지도, 이 집에 연락을 취하려 하지도 않을 것입니다."], ["His affairs are embarrassed.", "그의 재정 형편은 곤란한 상태입니다."], ["Offer him his freedom by means of the absence of Lady Glyde.", "글라이드 부인이 자리를 비움으로써 그에게 자유를 주십시오."], ["I promise you he will take his freedom, and go back to the Continent at the earliest moment when he can get away.", "장담하건대 그는 그 자유를 받아들이고, 빠져나갈 수 있는 가장 이른 순간에 대륙으로 돌아갈 것입니다. 이것이 수정처럼 분명하십니까?"], ["Is this clear to you as crystal? Yes, it is.", "그렇습니다, 분명합니다."], ["Have you questions to address to me?", "제게 하실 질문이 있으십니까?"], ["Be it so, I am here to answer. Ask, Mr.", "그러시다면 저는 답하려고 여기 있습니다."], ["Fairlie—oblige me by asking to your heart’s content.”", "물으십시오, 페얼리 씨—마음껏 물어 주시면 고맙겠습니다.”"]], [["He had said so much already in spite of me, and he looked so dreadfully capable of saying a great deal more also in spite of me, that I declined his amiable invitation in pure self-defence.", "제가 말리는데도 그는 이미 그토록 많은 말을 했고, 역시 제가 말리는데도 앞으로 훨씬 더 많은 말을 할 수 있어 보이는 것이 어찌나 무시무시하던지, 저는 순전히 자기 방어를 위해 그의 상냥한 제안을 사양했습니다."]], [["“Many thanks,”", "“천 번 감사합니다.”"], ["I replied.", "제가 대답했습니다."], ["“I am sinking fast.", "“저는 빠르게 가라앉고 있습니다."], ["In my state of health I must take things for granted.", "이런 건강 상태에서는 무엇이든 그러려니 하고 받아들일 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Allow me to do so on this occasion.", "이번에도 그렇게 하도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["We quite understand each other. Yes.", "우리는 서로를 아주 잘 이해하고 있습니다."], ["Much obliged, I am sure, for your kind interference.", "그래요. 친절히 나서 주신 데 대해 참으로 감사드립니다."], ["If I ever get better, and ever have a second opportunity of improving our acquaintance—”", "혹시라도 제가 나아져서 다시 한번 친분을 두터이 할 기회가 온다면—”"]], [["He got up.", "그가 일어섰습니다."], ["I thought he was going.", "저는 그가 가려는 줄 알았습니다."], ["No.", "아니었습니다."], ["More talk, more time for the development of infectious influences—in my room, too—remember that, in my room!", "이야기가 더 이어졌고, 전염의 기운이 퍼질 시간도 더 이어졌습니다—그것도 제 방에서 말입니다. 그 점을 기억해 주십시오, 제 방에서요!"]], [["“One moment yet,”", "“한 순간만 더.”"], ["he said, “one moment before I take my leave.", "그가 말했습니다. “물러가기 전에 한 순간만 더 주십시오."], ["I ask permission at parting to impress on you an urgent necessity.", "헤어지면서 한 가지 절박한 필요를 당신께 새겨 드릴 것을 허락해 주십시오."], ["It is this, sir.", "이런 것입니다, 선생."], ["You must not think of waiting till Miss Halcombe recovers before you receive Lady Glyde.", "홀콤 양이 회복될 때까지 기다렸다가 글라이드 부인을 맞으실 생각은 하지 마십시오."], ["Miss Halcombe has the attendance of the doctor, of the housekeeper at Blackwater Park, and of an experienced nurse as well—three persons for whose capacity and devotion I answer with my life.", "홀콤 양에게는 의사와 블랙워터 파크의 가정부와 경험 많은 간호사가 붙어 있습니다—그 능력과 헌신에 대해서는 제 목숨을 걸고 보증할 수 있는 세 사람입니다."], ["I tell you that.", "그 점을 말씀드립니다."], ["I tell you, also, that the anxiety and alarm of her sister’s illness has already affected the health and spirits of Lady Glyde, and has made her totally unfit to be of use in the sick-room.", "또한 이 점도 말씀드립니다. 언니의 병으로 인한 불안과 놀람이 이미 글라이드 부인의 건강과 기력을 해쳐, 부인은 병실에서 아무 도움도 될 수 없는 상태입니다."], ["Her position with her husband grows more and more deplorable and dangerous every day.", "남편과의 관계는 날마다 더 개탄스럽고 더 위태로워지고 있습니다."], ["If you leave her any longer at Blackwater Park, you do nothing whatever to hasten her sister’s recovery, and at the same time, you risk the public scandal, which you and I, and all of us, are bound in the sacred interests of the family to avoid.", "부인을 블랙워터 파크에 더 오래 두신다 해도 언니의 회복을 앞당기는 데는 아무 보탬이 되지 않으며, 동시에 당신과 저, 그리고 우리 모두가 집안의 신성한 이익을 위해 반드시 피해야 할 세상의 추문을 무릅쓰게 됩니다."], ["With all my soul, I advise you to remove the serious responsibility of delay from your own shoulders by writing to Lady Glyde to come here at once.", "온 마음을 다해 권합니다. 글라이드 부인께 당장 이리로 오라는 편지를 쓰셔서, 미루는 데 따르는 무거운 책임을 당신의 어깨에서 덜어 내십시오."], ["Do your affectionate, your honourable, your inevitable duty, and whatever happens in the future, no one can lay the blame on you.", "애정 어린 의무, 명예로운 의무, 피할 수 없는 의무를 다하십시오. 그러면 앞으로 무슨 일이 일어나든 아무도 당신을 탓할 수 없습니다."], ["I speak from my large experience—I offer my friendly advice.", "저는 저의 풍부한 경험에서 말씀드리는 것이며, 우정 어린 조언을 드리는 것입니다."], ["Is it accepted—Yes, or No?”", "받아들이시겠습니까—예입니까, 아니요입니까?”"]], [["I looked at him—merely looked at him—with my sense of his amazing assurance, and my dawning resolution to ring for Louis and have him shown out of the room expressed in every line of my face.", "저는 그를 바라보았습니다—그저 바라보기만 했습니다—그의 놀라운 뻔뻔함에 대한 제 심정과, 루이를 불러 그를 방 밖으로 내보내게 하겠다는 막 싹튼 결심을 얼굴의 선 하나하나에 담아서 말입니다."], ["It is perfectly incredible, but quite true, that my face did not appear to produce the slightest impression on him.", "도무지 믿기지 않는 일입니다만 엄연한 사실입니다. 제 얼굴은 그에게 티끌만 한 인상도 주지 못한 듯했습니다."], ["Born without nerves—evidently born without nerves.", "신경 없이 태어난 사람—분명히 신경 없이 태어난 사람이었습니다."]], [["“You hesitate?”", "“망설이십니까?”"], ["he said. “Mr.", "그가 말했습니다."], ["Fairlie!", "“페얼리 씨!"], ["I understand that hesitation.", "저는 그 망설임을 이해합니다."], ["You object—see, sir, how my sympathies look straight down into your thoughts!—you object that Lady Glyde is not in health and not in spirits to take the long journey, from Hampshire to this place, by herself.", "당신은 이렇게 반대하시는 것이지요—보십시오, 선생, 제 공감이 당신의 생각 속을 얼마나 곧장 들여다보는지!—글라이드 부인은 햄프셔에서 이곳까지 그 먼 길을 혼자 갈 만한 건강도 기력도 없다고 말입니다."], ["Her own maid is removed from her, as you know, and of other servants fit to travel with her, from one end of England to another, there are none at Blackwater Park.", "아시다시피 부인의 하녀는 부인에게서 떼어졌고, 잉글랜드의 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 부인과 동행할 만한 다른 하인은 블랙워터 파크에 하나도 없습니다. 또 이렇게도 반대하시겠지요."], ["You object, again, that she cannot comfortably stop and rest in London, on her way here, because she cannot comfortably go alone to a public hotel where she is a total stranger.", "부인은 오는 길에 런던에서 편안히 머물며 쉴 수도 없다고요. 전혀 낯선 여관에 혼자 들어가는 것이 부인으로서는 편안할 리 없으니까요."], ["In one breath, I grant both objections—in another breath, I remove them.", "한마디로 저는 두 가지 반대를 모두 인정합니다—그리고 또 한마디로 두 가지를 모두 없애 드리겠습니다."], ["Follow me, if you please, for the last time.", "마지막으로 한 번만 저를 따라와 주십시오."], ["It was my intention, when I returned to England with Sir Percival, to settle myself in the neighbourhood of London.", "저는 퍼시벌 경과 함께 잉글랜드로 돌아올 때부터 런던 근처에 자리를 잡을 생각이었습니다."], ["That purpose has just been happily accomplished.", "그 계획이 마침 다행히도 이루어졌습니다."], ["I have taken, for six months, a little furnished house in the quarter called St. John’s Wood.", "저는 세인트존스우드라는 동네에 가구가 딸린 작은 집을 여섯 달 기한으로 얻었습니다."], ["Be so obliging as to keep this fact in your mind, and observe the programme I now propose.", "부디 이 사실을 마음에 새겨 두시고, 제가 지금 제안하는 순서를 살펴 주십시오."], ["Lady Glyde travels to London (a short journey)—I myself meet her at the station—I take her to rest and sleep at my house, which is also the house of her aunt—when she is restored I escort her to the station again—she travels to this place, and her own maid (who is now under your roof) receives her at the carriage-door.", "글라이드 부인이 런던까지 여행합니다(짧은 여정입니다)—제가 직접 역으로 마중을 나갑니다—부인을 제 집으로 모셔 쉬고 주무시게 합니다. 그 집은 부인의 고모의 집이기도 하지요—기력을 되찾으시면 제가 다시 역까지 모셔다 드립니다—부인은 이곳으로 여행하고, 지금 당신의 지붕 아래 있는 부인의 하녀가 마차 문 앞에서 부인을 맞습니다."], ["Here is comfort consulted—here are the interests of propriety consulted—here is your own duty—duty of hospitality, sympathy, protection, to an unhappy lady in need of all three—smoothed and made easy, from the beginning to the end.", "여기에 편안함이 고려되어 있고, 여기에 예법의 이익이 고려되어 있으며, 여기에 당신의 의무가—그 셋을 모두 필요로 하는 불행한 부인에 대한 환대와 공감과 보호의 의무가—처음부터 끝까지 매끄럽고 수월하게 마련되어 있습니다."], ["I cordially invite you, sir, to second my efforts in the sacred interests of the family.", "선생, 집안의 신성한 이익을 위한 제 노력에 힘을 보태 주시기를 진심으로 청합니다."], ["I seriously advise you to write, by my hands, offering the hospitality of your house (and heart), and the hospitality of my house (and heart), to that injured and unfortunate lady whose cause I plead to-day.”", "오늘 제가 그 사정을 대신 아뢰고 있는 그 상처받고 불행한 부인에게, 당신의 집(과 마음)의 환대와 제 집(과 마음)의 환대를 내어 주겠다는 편지를 제 손을 통해 보내시기를 진지하게 권합니다.”"]], [["He waved his horrid hand at me—he struck his infectious breast—he addressed me oratorically, as if I was laid up in the House of Commons.", "그는 그 끔찍한 손을 제게 흔들어 보였고, 병균이 우글거릴 자기 가슴을 쳤으며, 마치 제가 하원 의사당에 드러누워 있기라도 한 듯 웅변조로 제게 연설했습니다."], ["It was high time to take a desperate course of some sort.", "무슨 수를 쓰든 필사적인 방책을 택해야 할 때였습니다."], ["It was also high time to send for Louis, and adopt the precaution of fumigating the room.", "또한 루이를 불러 방을 훈증 소독하는 예방책을 취해야 할 때이기도 했습니다."]], [["In this trying emergency an idea occurred to me—an inestimable idea which, so to speak, killed two intrusive birds with one stone.", "이 괴로운 위기 속에서 제게 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐습니다—말하자면 돌 하나로 성가신 새 두 마리를 잡는, 값을 매길 수 없는 생각이었습니다."], ["I determined to get rid of the Count’s tiresome eloquence, and of Lady Glyde’s tiresome troubles, by complying with this odious foreigner’s request, and writing the letter at once.", "저는 이 혐오스러운 외국인의 청을 들어주어 당장 편지를 씀으로써, 백작의 지겨운 웅변과 글라이드 부인의 지겨운 곤경을 한꺼번에 떨쳐 버리기로 결심했습니다. 그 초대가 받아들여질 위험은 조금도 없었습니다."], ["There was not the least danger of the invitation being accepted, for there was not the least chance that Laura would consent to leave Blackwater Park while Marian was lying there ill.", "매리언이 거기 앓아누워 있는 동안 로라가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나는 데 동의할 가능성은 조금도 없었으니까요. 이토록 기막히게 편리한 걸림돌이 어떻게 백작의 그 참견 잘하는 통찰을 피해 갈 수 있었는지는 도무지 짐작할 수 없습니다만, 어쨌든 그것은 그를 피해 갔습니다."], ["How this charmingly convenient obstacle could have escaped the officious penetration of the Count, it was impossible to conceive—but it HAD escaped him.", "생각할 시간을 더 주면 그가 그것을 알아차릴지도 모른다는 두려움에 저는 놀라울 정도로 기운이 솟아, 안간힘을 써서 몸을 일으켜 앉고는 옆에 있던 필기도구를 집어 들었습니다."], ["My dread that he might yet discover it, if I allowed him any more time to think, stimulated me to such an amazing degree, that I struggled into a sitting position—seized, really seized, the writing materials by my side, and produced the letter as rapidly as if I had been a common clerk in an office.", "정말로 집어 들었지요. 그러고는 사무실의 평범한 서기라도 된 듯 빠르게 편지를 써냈습니다."], ["“Dearest Laura, Please come, whenever you like.", "“사랑하는 로라, 네가 좋을 때 언제든 오너라."], ["Break the journey by sleeping in London at your aunt’s house.", "런던 고모 댁에서 하룻밤 묵어 여정을 나누도록 하고."], ["Grieved to hear of dear Marian’s illness.", "사랑하는 매리언이 아프다니 마음이 아프구나."], ["Ever affectionately yours.”", "언제나 너를 아끼는 사람으로부터.”"], ["I handed these lines, at arm’s length, to the Count—I sank back in my chair—I said, “Excuse me—I am entirely prostrated—I can do no more.", "저는 팔을 뻗어 그 몇 줄을 백작에게 건네고 의자에 도로 파묻히며 말했습니다. “실례합니다—저는 완전히 기진맥진했습니다—더는 아무것도 못 하겠습니다."], ["Will you rest and lunch downstairs?", "아래층에서 쉬시며 점심이라도 드시겠습니까?"], ["Love to all, and sympathy, and so on.", "모두에게 안부와 위로를 전해 주시고요."], ["Good-morning.”", "그럼 안녕히 가십시오.”"]], [["He made another speech—the man was absolutely inexhaustible.", "그는 또 한바탕 연설을 했습니다—이 사람은 정말이지 마르지 않는 샘이었습니다."], ["I closed my eyes—I endeavoured to hear as little as possible.", "저는 눈을 감고 되도록 적게 들으려고 애썼습니다."], ["In spite of my endeavours I was obliged to hear a great deal.", "그런 노력에도 불구하고 저는 아주 많은 것을 들어야만 했습니다."], ["My sister’s endless husband congratulated himself, and congratulated me, on the result of our interview—he mentioned a great deal more about his sympathies and mine—he deplored my miserable health—he offered to write me a prescription—he impressed on me the necessity of not forgetting what he had said about the importance of light—he accepted my obliging invitation to rest and lunch—he recommended me to expect Lady Glyde in two or three days’ time—he begged my permission to look forward to our next meeting, instead of paining himself and paining me, by saying farewell—he added a great deal more, which, I rejoice to think, I did not attend to at the time, and do not remember now.", "제 누이의 그 끝도 없는 남편은 우리 면담의 결과를 두고 스스로를 축하하고 저를 축하했습니다—자기의 공감과 저의 공감에 대해 훨씬 더 많은 이야기를 늘어놓았고—제 비참한 건강을 개탄했고—처방전을 써 주겠다고 했고—빛의 중요성에 대해 자기가 한 말을 잊지 말아야 한다고 못 박았고—쉬면서 점심을 들라는 제 너그러운 초대를 받아들였고—이삼일 안에 글라이드 부인이 도착할 것으로 예상하라고 권했고—자신과 저를 함께 아프게 하는 작별 인사를 하는 대신 다음 만남을 기대할 수 있도록 허락해 달라고 청했습니다—그 밖에도 훨씬 더 많은 말을 덧붙였는데, 그때도 귀담아듣지 않았고 지금도 기억나지 않는다는 것이 기쁠 따름입니다."], ["I heard his sympathetic voice travelling away from me by degrees—but, large as he was, I never heard him.", "저는 그 인정 많은 목소리가 조금씩 멀어져 가는 것을 들었습니다만, 그렇게 덩치가 큰데도 그가 걷는 소리는 한 번도 듣지 못했습니다."], ["He had the negative merit of being absolutely noiseless.", "그에게는 완벽하게 소리를 내지 않는다는 소극적인 미덕이 있었습니다."], ["I don’t know when he opened the door, or when he shut it.", "그가 언제 문을 열었는지, 언제 닫았는지 저는 모릅니다."], ["I ventured to make use of my eyes again, after an interval of silence—and he was gone.", "한동안 침묵이 흐른 뒤 저는 다시 눈을 써 볼 용기를 냈고, 그는 가고 없었습니다."]], [["I rang for Louis, and retired to my bathroom.", "저는 루이를 부르고 욕실로 물러났습니다."], ["Tepid water, strengthened with aromatic vinegar, for myself, and copious fumigation for my study, were the obvious precautions to take, and of course I adopted them.", "향이 나는 식초를 탄 미지근한 물로 제 몸을 씻고 서재를 넉넉히 훈증하는 것이 당연히 취해야 할 예방책이었고, 물론 저는 그렇게 했습니다. 그것이 효과가 있었다고 말할 수 있어 기쁩니다."], ["I rejoice to say they proved successful.", "저는 늘 하던 대로 낮잠을 즐겼습니다."], ["I enjoyed my customary siesta. I awoke moist and cool.", "촉촉하고 서늘한 기분으로 깨어났지요."]], [["My first inquiries were for the Count.", "제가 맨 먼저 물은 것은 백작에 관한 것이었습니다."], ["Had we really got rid of him?", "우리가 정말로 그를 떨쳐 냈는가?"], ["Yes—he had gone away by the afternoon train.", "그렇습니다—그는 오후 기차로 떠났습니다."], ["Had he lunched, and if so, upon what?", "점심은 들었는가, 들었다면 무엇을 들었는가?"], ["Entirely upon fruit-tart and cream.", "오로지 과일 타르트와 크림뿐이었습니다."], ["What a man!", "어떤 인간인지!"], ["What a digestion!", "어떤 소화력인지!"]], [["Am I expected to say anything more?", "제가 더 무슨 말을 하기를 기대하십니까?"], ["I believe not.", "저는 아니라고 봅니다."], ["I believe I have reached the limits assigned to me.", "제게 주어진 몫은 다 채웠다고 봅니다."], ["The shocking circumstances which happened at a later period did not, I am thankful to say, happen in my presence.", "뒷날 벌어진 그 충격적인 일들은, 다행히도 제가 있는 자리에서 일어나지 않았습니다."], ["I do beg and entreat that nobody will be so very unfeeling as to lay any part of the blame of those circumstances on me.", "부디 간청드리건대, 그 일들의 책임 가운데 한 조각이라도 제게 지우는 그런 몰인정한 사람은 아무도 없기를 바랍니다."], ["I did everything for the best.", "저는 최선을 다해 모든 것을 했습니다."], ["I am not answerable for a deplorable calamity, which it was quite impossible to foresee.", "도저히 내다볼 수 없었던 개탄스러운 재앙에 대해 저는 책임이 없습니다."], ["I am shattered by it—I have suffered under it, as nobody else has suffered.", "저는 그 일로 산산이 부서졌고, 그 누구도 겪지 않은 만큼 그 아래에서 고통받았습니다."], ["My servant, Louis (who is really attached to me in his unintelligent way), thinks I shall never get over it.", "제 하인 루이는(제 나름의 아둔한 방식으로나마 정말로 저를 따르는 사람입니다) 제가 결코 이 일을 이겨 내지 못하리라고 생각합니다."], ["He sees me dictating at this moment, with my handkerchief to my eyes.", "그는 제가 지금 이 순간 손수건을 눈에 대고 구술하는 모습을 보고 있습니다."], ["I wish to mention, in justice to myself, that it was not my fault, and that I am quite exhausted and heartbroken.", "제 자신의 명예를 위해 밝혀 두고 싶은 것은, 그것이 제 잘못이 아니었다는 것과 제가 완전히 기진맥진하고 가슴이 찢어졌다는 것입니다."], ["Need I say more?", "더 말할 필요가 있겠습니까?"]], [["(Housekeeper at Blackwater Park)", "(블랙워터 파크의 가정부)"]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["I am asked to state plainly what I know of the progress of Miss Halcombe’s illness and of the circumstances under which Lady Glyde left Blackwater Park for London.", "저는 홀콤 양의 병세가 어떻게 진행되었는지, 그리고 글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 가게 된 정황이 어떠했는지, 제가 아는 바를 있는 그대로 진술해 달라는 요청을 받았습니다."]], [["The reason given for making this demand on me is, that my testimony is wanted in the interests of truth.", "이런 요구를 제게 하는 이유는, 진실을 밝히는 데 제 증언이 필요하다는 것입니다."], ["As the widow of a clergyman of the Church of England (reduced by misfortune to the necessity of accepting a situation), I have been taught to place the claims of truth above all other considerations.", "영국 국교회 목사의 미망인으로서(불운으로 형편이 기울어 남의 집에 들어가 일하는 처지가 되었습니다만), 저는 진실의 요구를 다른 무엇보다 앞세우도록 배웠습니다."], ["I therefore comply with a request which I might otherwise, through reluctance to connect myself with distressing family affairs, have hesitated to grant.", "그러므로 저는, 괴로운 집안일에 제 이름을 얽어 넣기가 내키지 않아 그렇지 않았더라면 망설였을 이 청을 받아들입니다."]], [["I made no memorandum at the time, and I cannot therefore be sure to a day of the date, but I believe I am correct in stating that Miss Halcombe’s serious illness began during the last fortnight or ten days in June.", "당시에 따로 적어 둔 것이 없어 날짜를 하루까지 정확히 말씀드릴 수는 없습니다만, 홀콤 양의 중병이 시작된 것은 6월의 마지막 두 주, 혹은 열흘 사이였다고 말씀드려도 틀리지 않으리라 믿습니다."], ["The breakfast hour was late at Blackwater Park—sometimes as late as ten, never earlier than half-past nine.", "블랙워터 파크의 아침 식사 시간은 늦어서, 때로는 열 시가 되기도 했고 아무리 일러도 아홉 시 반은 넘겼습니다."], ["On the morning to which I am now referring, Miss Halcombe (who was usually the first to come down) did not make her appearance at the table.", "지금 말씀드리는 그날 아침, 늘 제일 먼저 내려오시던 홀콤 양이 식탁에 나타나지 않으셨습니다."], ["After the family had waited a quarter of an hour, the upper housemaid was sent to see after her, and came running out of the room dreadfully frightened.", "집안 식구들이 십오 분쯤 기다린 뒤에 수석 하녀를 올려보내 살펴보게 했더니, 그 아이가 몹시 겁에 질려 방에서 뛰어나왔습니다."], ["I met the servant on the stairs, and went at once to Miss Halcombe to see what was the matter.", "저는 계단에서 그 하인을 만나 곧바로 홀콤 양에게 무슨 일인지 보러 갔습니다."], ["The poor lady was incapable of telling me.", "가엾은 그분은 제게 아무 말도 하실 수 없는 상태였습니다."], ["She was walking about her room with a pen in her hand, quite light-headed, in a state of burning fever.", "손에 펜을 쥔 채 방 안을 이리저리 걸어 다니고 계셨는데, 정신은 온전치 못했고 열이 불덩이처럼 오른 상태였습니다."]], [["Lady Glyde (being no longer in Sir Percival’s service, I may, without impropriety, mention my former mistress by her name, instead of calling her my lady) was the first to come in from her own bedroom.", "글라이드 부인은(이제 저는 퍼시벌 경 댁에서 일하는 몸이 아니니, 예전 안주인을 마님이라 부르는 대신 이름으로 말씀드려도 결례는 아니겠지요) 자기 침실에서 제일 먼저 건너오셨습니다."], ["She was so dreadfully alarmed and distressed that she was quite useless.", "그런데 어찌나 놀라고 상심하셨던지 아무 도움이 되지 못하셨습니다."], ["The Count Fosco, and his lady, who came upstairs immediately afterwards, were both most serviceable and kind.", "곧이어 올라오신 포스코 백작과 그 부인은 두 분 다 더없이 요긴하고 친절하셨습니다."], ["Her ladyship assisted me to get Miss Halcombe to her bed.", "백작부인은 홀콤 양을 침대에 눕히는 일을 저와 함께해 주셨습니다."], ["His lordship the Count remained in the sitting-room, and having sent for my medicine-chest, made a mixture for Miss Halcombe, and a cooling lotion to be applied to her head, so as to lose no time before the doctor came.", "백작 각하는 거실에 남으셔서, 제 약상자를 가져오게 하시더니 홀콤 양에게 드릴 물약과 머리에 얹을 냉찜질 물을 만드셨습니다. 의사가 오기 전에 조금이라도 시간을 허비하지 않으시려는 것이었지요."], ["We applied the lotion, but we could not get her to take the mixture.", "저희는 냉찜질은 해 드렸지만 물약은 도무지 넘기게 할 수가 없었습니다."], ["Sir Percival undertook to send for the doctor.", "퍼시벌 경은 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내겠다고 하셨습니다."], ["He despatched a groom, on horseback, for the nearest medical man, Mr. Dawson, of Oak Lodge.", "마부 하나를 말에 태워 가장 가까운 의사인 오크 로지의 도슨 씨에게 보내셨습니다."]], [["Mr. Dawson arrived in less than an hour’s time.", "도슨 씨는 한 시간도 안 되어 도착했습니다."], ["He was a respectable elderly man, well known all round the country, and we were much alarmed when we found that he considered the case to be a very serious one.", "그는 이 근방에서 널리 알려진 점잖은 노인이었는데, 그가 이 병을 매우 위중한 것으로 본다는 사실을 알고 저희는 크게 놀랐습니다."]], [["His lordship the Count affably entered into conversation with Mr. Dawson, and gave his opinions with a judicious freedom.", "백작 각하는 도슨 씨와 상냥하게 대화를 나누시며 사려 깊고 거침없이 소견을 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Mr. Dawson, not over-courteously, inquired if his lordship’s advice was the advice of a doctor, and being informed that it was the advice of one who had studied medicine unprofessionally, replied that he was not accustomed to consult with amateur physicians.", "도슨 씨는 그리 정중하지 못한 태도로, 각하의 조언이 의사로서의 조언이냐고 물었고, 정식으로 개업하지는 않았으나 의학을 공부한 사람의 조언이라는 대답을 듣자, 자기는 아마추어 의사와 상의하는 습관이 없다고 응수했습니다."], ["The Count, with truly Christian meekness of temper, smiled and left the room.", "백작은 참으로 그리스도인다운 온유함으로 미소만 지으시고 방을 나가셨습니다."], ["Before he went out he told me that he might be found, in case he was wanted in the course of the day, at the boat-house on the banks of the lake.", "나가시기 전에 그분은, 그날 중에 혹시 찾을 일이 있거든 호숫가의 보트 창고에 있을 것이라고 제게 일러 주셨습니다."], ["Why he should have gone there, I cannot say.", "어째서 거기로 가셨는지는 저도 알 수 없습니다."], ["But he did go, remaining away the whole day till seven o’clock, which was dinner-time.", "그러나 실제로 가셔서, 저녁 식사 시간인 일곱 시까지 온종일 돌아오지 않으셨습니다."], ["Perhaps he wished to set the example of keeping the house as quiet as possible.", "아마도 집안을 되도록 조용히 두는 본을 보이려 하셨던 것이겠지요."], ["It was entirely in his character to do so.", "그것이야말로 그분의 성품에 꼭 맞는 일이었습니다."], ["He was a most considerate nobleman.", "참으로 사려 깊은 귀족이셨습니다."]], [["Miss Halcombe passed a very bad night, the fever coming and going, and getting worse towards the morning instead of better.", "홀콤 양은 몹시 나쁜 밤을 보내셨습니다. 열이 올랐다 내렸다 하다가 아침이 가까워질수록 나아지기는커녕 더 심해졌습니다."], ["No nurse fit to wait on her being at hand in the neighbourhood, her ladyship the Countess and myself undertook the duty, relieving each other.", "근처에는 그분을 돌볼 만한 간호인이 없었으므로, 백작부인과 제가 번갈아 가며 그 일을 맡았습니다."], ["Lady Glyde, most unwisely, insisted on sitting up with us.", "글라이드 부인은 참으로 지각없게도 저희와 함께 밤을 새우겠다고 고집하셨습니다."], ["She was much too nervous and too delicate in health to bear the anxiety of Miss Halcombe’s illness calmly.", "그분은 신경이 너무 예민하고 몸이 너무 약해서 홀콤 양의 병으로 인한 불안을 차분히 견뎌 내지 못하셨습니다."], ["She only did herself harm, without being of the least real assistance.", "실질적인 도움은 조금도 못 되면서 당신 몸만 상하게 하셨을 뿐입니다."], ["A more gentle and affectionate lady never lived—but she cried, and she was frightened, two weaknesses which made her entirely unfit to be present in a sick-room.", "그보다 더 온화하고 다정한 부인은 세상에 없었습니다만, 우시는 데다 겁을 내셨으니 이 두 가지 약점 때문에 병실에 계시기에는 전혀 적합하지 않으셨습니다."]], [["Sir Percival and the Count came in the morning to make their inquiries.", "퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아침에 병세를 물으러 오셨습니다."]], [["Sir Percival (from distress, I presume, at his lady’s affliction and at Miss Halcombe’s illness) appeared much confused and unsettled in his mind.", "퍼시벌 경은(부인의 상심과 홀콤 양의 병 때문이었으리라 짐작합니다만) 몹시 혼란스럽고 안정을 잃은 듯 보이셨습니다."], ["His lordship testified, on the contrary, a becoming composure and interest.", "반대로 백작 각하는 어울리는 침착함과 관심을 보이셨습니다."], ["He had his straw hat in one hand, and his book in the other, and he mentioned to Sir Percival in my hearing that he would go out again and study at the lake.", "한 손에는 밀짚모자를, 다른 손에는 책을 드시고, 제가 듣는 자리에서 퍼시벌 경에게 다시 나가 호수에서 공부하겠노라고 말씀하셨습니다. “집안을 조용히 둡시다.”"], ["“Let us keep the house quiet,”", "하고 그분이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["he said. “Let us not smoke indoors, my friend, now Miss Halcombe is ill.", "“여보게, 홀콤 양이 앓고 있으니 실내에서는 담배를 피우지 마세."], ["You go your way, and I will go mine.", "자네는 자네 길로 가고 나는 내 길로 가겠네."], ["When I study I like to be alone.", "나는 공부할 때 혼자 있는 것을 좋아하니까."], ["Good-morning, Mrs. Michelson.”", "안녕히 계십시오, 마이컬슨 부인.”"]], [["Sir Percival was not civil enough—perhaps I ought in justice to say, not composed enough—to take leave of me with the same polite attention.", "퍼시벌 경은 저에게 같은 정중한 예의로 작별을 표할 만큼 예의 바르지, 공평하게 말하자면 마음이 가라앉아 있지 못하셨습니다."], ["The only person in the house, indeed, who treated me, at that time or at any other, on the footing of a lady in distressed circumstances, was the Count.", "사실 그 무렵이든 다른 어느 때든, 이 집에서 저를 형편이 기운 숙녀로 대접해 준 유일한 분은 백작이었습니다."], ["He had the manners of a true nobleman—he was considerate towards every one.", "그분은 참된 귀족의 몸가짐을 지니셨고, 누구에게나 사려 깊으셨습니다."], ["Even the young person (Fanny by name) who attended on Lady Glyde was not beneath his notice.", "글라이드 부인의 시중을 들던 어린 아이(패니라는 이름이었습니다)까지도 그분의 눈길 밖에 있지 않았습니다."], ["When she was sent away by Sir Percival, his lordship (showing me his sweet little birds at the time) was most kindly anxious to know what had become of her, where she was to go the day she left Blackwater Park, and so on.", "그 아이가 퍼시벌 경에게 쫓겨났을 때, 각하는(마침 제게 그 귀여운 새들을 보여 주고 계셨는데) 그 아이가 어찌 되었는지, 블랙워터 파크를 떠나는 날 어디로 갈 것인지 등을 참으로 친절하게 걱정하며 물으셨습니다."], ["It is in such little delicate attentions that the advantages of aristocratic birth always show themselves.", "귀족으로 태어난 이의 장점은 언제나 이런 사소하고 섬세한 배려에서 드러나는 법입니다."], ["I make no apology for introducing these particulars—they are brought forward in justice to his lordship, whose character, I have reason to know, is viewed rather harshly in certain quarters.", "이런 자잘한 이야기를 늘어놓는 데 대해 저는 변명하지 않겠습니다. 이것은 각하를 위한 공정함에서 내놓는 것이며, 제가 알기로 그분의 인품은 어떤 자리에서는 꽤 가혹하게 평가되고 있으니까요."], ["A nobleman who can respect a lady in distressed circumstances, and can take a fatherly interest in the fortunes of an humble servant girl, shows principles and feelings of too high an order to be lightly called in question.", "형편이 기운 숙녀를 존중할 줄 알고, 미천한 하녀 아이의 앞날에 아버지 같은 관심을 가질 줄 아는 귀족이라면, 함부로 의심받기에는 너무나 높은 차원의 원칙과 심성을 보여 주는 것입니다. 저는 의견을 내세우지 않습니다."], ["I advance no opinions—I offer facts only.", "사실만 내놓을 뿐입니다."], ["My endeavour through life is to judge not that I be not judged.", "평생 제가 힘써 온 바는, 심판받지 않으려거든 심판하지 말라는 것입니다."], ["One of my beloved husband’s finest sermons was on that text.", "사랑하는 제 남편의 가장 훌륭한 설교 가운데 하나가 그 구절을 본문으로 삼은 것이었습니다. 저는 그것을 늘 읽습니다."], ["I read it constantly—in my own copy of the edition printed by subscription, in the first days of my widowhood—and at every fresh perusal I derive an increase of spiritual benefit and edification.", "과부가 된 첫 무렵에 예약 구독으로 인쇄한 판본 가운데 제 몫으로 가진 책으로 말입니다. 그리고 읽을 때마다 영적인 유익과 교훈을 더욱 얻습니다."]], [["There was no improvement in Miss Halcombe, and the second night was even worse than the first.", "홀콤 양은 조금도 차도가 없었고, 둘째 날 밤은 첫날 밤보다도 더 나빴습니다."], ["Mr. Dawson was constant in his attendance.", "도슨 씨는 한결같이 곁을 지켰습니다."], ["The practical duties of nursing were still divided between the Countess and myself, Lady Glyde persisting in sitting up with us, though we both entreated her to take some rest.", "실제 간병은 여전히 백작부인과 제가 나누어 맡았고, 글라이드 부인은 저희 둘이 제발 좀 쉬시라고 간청했는데도 함께 밤을 새우겠다고 버티셨습니다."], ["“My place is by Marian’s bedside,” was her only answer.", "“내 자리는 매리언의 머리맡이에요.”가 그분의 유일한 대답이었습니다."], ["“Whether I am ill, or well, nothing will induce me to lose sight of her.”", "“내가 아프든 성하든, 무슨 일이 있어도 언니에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠어요.”"]], [["Towards midday I went downstairs to attend to some of my regular duties.", "정오 무렵 저는 제가 늘 하던 일을 보러 아래층으로 내려갔습니다."], ["An hour afterwards, on my way back to the sick-room, I saw the Count (who had gone out again early, for the third time) entering the hall, to all appearance in the highest good spirits.", "한 시간 뒤 병실로 돌아가는 길에, 그날 아침 벌써 세 번째로 나가셨던 백작이 보기에도 몹시 기분이 좋으신 모습으로 현관에 들어서시는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Sir Percival, at the same moment, put his head out of the library door, and addressed his noble friend, with extreme eagerness, in these words—", "바로 그때 퍼시벌 경이 서재 문으로 고개를 내밀고, 몹시 다급한 목소리로 그 고귀한 벗에게 이렇게 말을 건네셨습니다."]], [["“Have you found her?”", "“그 여자를 찾았나?”"]], [["His lordship’s large face became dimpled all over with placid smiles, but he made no reply in words.", "각하의 커다란 얼굴에 잔잔한 미소가 온통 보조개처럼 번졌지만, 말로는 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["At the same time Sir Percival turned his head, observed that I was approaching the stairs, and looked at me in the most rudely angry manner possible.", "그와 동시에 퍼시벌 경은 고개를 돌려 제가 계단으로 다가오는 것을 보시고는, 더할 나위 없이 무례하고 성난 눈길로 저를 노려보셨습니다."]], [["“Come in here and tell me about it,”", "“여기 들어와서 얘기하게.”"], ["he said to the Count.", "그분이 백작에게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“Whenever there are women in a house they’re always sure to be going up or down stairs.”", "“집 안에 여자들이 있으면 꼭 계단을 오르내리고 있단 말이야.”"]], [["“My dear Percival,”", "“여보게 퍼시벌.”"], ["observed his lordship kindly, “Mrs.", "각하가 부드럽게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Michelson has duties.", "“마이컬슨 부인께는 맡은 일이 있네."], ["Pray recognise her admirable performance of them as sincerely as I do!", "부인이 그 일을 훌륭히 해내고 계신 것을 나만큼 진심으로 인정해 드리게!"], ["How is the sufferer, Mrs. Michelson?”", "환자분은 어떠십니까, 마이컬슨 부인?”"]], [["“No better, my lord, I regret to say.”", "“나아지지 않으셨습니다, 각하. 말씀드리기 송구합니다.”"]], [["“Sad—most sad!” remarked the Count.", "“안타깝군요, 참으로 안타깝습니다!”"], ["“You look fatigued, Mrs.", "백작이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Michelson. It is certainly time you and my wife had some help in nursing.", "“부인, 몹시 지쳐 보이십니다."], ["I think I may be the means of offering you that help.", "부인과 제 아내가 간병에 도움을 받을 때가 확실히 되었지요."], ["Circumstances have happened which will oblige Madame Fosco to travel to London either to-morrow or the day after.", "제가 그 도움을 마련해 드릴 수 있을 듯합니다. 사정이 생겨서 포스코 부인이 내일이나 모레쯤 런던에 다녀와야 하게 되었습니다."], ["She will go away in the morning and return at night, and she will bring back with her, to relieve you, a nurse of excellent conduct and capacity, who is now disengaged.", "아침에 떠나 밤에 돌아올 텐데, 부인의 짐을 덜어 드릴 간호인을 하나 데려올 겁니다. 품행과 능력이 훌륭하고 마침 일이 비어 있는 사람이지요."], ["The woman is known to my wife as a person to be trusted.", "제 아내가 믿을 만한 사람으로 알고 있습니다."], ["Before she comes here say nothing about her, if you please, to the doctor, because he will look with an evil eye on any nurse of my providing.", "그 여자가 오기 전에는 부디 의사에게 아무 말도 마십시오. 제가 주선한 간호인이라면 무엇이든 곱지 않은 눈으로 볼 테니까요."], ["When she appears in this house she will speak for herself, and Mr. Dawson will be obliged to acknowledge that there is no excuse for not employing her.", "이 집에 나타나면 그 여자가 스스로 자기를 증명할 것이고, 도슨 씨도 그 사람을 쓰지 않을 구실이 없다고 인정할 수밖에 없을 겁니다."], ["Lady Glyde will say the same.", "글라이드 부인도 같은 말씀을 하시겠지요."], ["Pray present my best respects and sympathies to Lady Glyde.”", "부디 글라이드 부인께 제 깊은 경의와 위로를 전해 주십시오.”"]], [["I expressed my grateful acknowledgments for his lordship’s kind consideration.", "저는 각하의 친절한 배려에 감사의 뜻을 표했습니다."], ["Sir Percival cut them short by calling to his noble friend (using, I regret to say, a profane expression) to come into the library, and not to keep him waiting there any longer.", "퍼시벌 경이 그 말을 도중에 끊고, 그 고귀한 벗을 불러(말씀드리기 민망합니다만 불경한 말을 섞어) 서재로 들어오라고, 더 이상 기다리게 하지 말라고 소리치셨습니다."]], [["I proceeded upstairs.", "저는 위층으로 올라갔습니다."], ["We are poor erring creatures, and however well established a woman’s principles may be she cannot always keep on her guard against the temptation to exercise an idle curiosity.", "사람은 가엾게도 허물 많은 존재라, 여자의 신조가 아무리 굳게 서 있다 해도 부질없는 호기심의 유혹에 언제나 자신을 지키지는 못합니다."], ["I am ashamed to say that an idle curiosity, on this occasion, got the better of my principles, and made me unduly inquisitive about the question which Sir Percival had addressed to his noble friend at the library door.", "부끄럽게도 그때 저는 부질없는 호기심에 제 신조가 지고 말아, 퍼시벌 경이 서재 문 앞에서 그 고귀한 벗에게 던진 물음을 지나치게 궁금해하고 말았습니다. 백작이 블랙워터 파크에서 학구적인 아침 산책을 하시는 동안 만나기로 되어 있던 사람은 누구였을까요?"], ["Who was the Count expected to find in the course of his studious morning rambles at Blackwater Park?", "퍼시벌 경의 물음투로 보아 여자였으리라 짐작되었습니다."], ["A woman, it was to be presumed, from the terms of Sir Percival’s inquiry.", "저는 백작에게 부적절한 구석이 있으리라고는 의심하지 않았습니다."], ["I did not suspect the Count of any impropriety—I knew his moral character too well.", "그분의 도덕적 인품을 너무 잘 알았으니까요. 제가 스스로에게 던진 물음은 오직 하나였습니다."], ["The only question I asked myself was—Had he found her?", "그분은 그 여자를 찾으셨을까?"]], [["To resume.", "다시 이야기를 이어 가겠습니다."], ["The night passed as usual without producing any change for the better in Miss Halcombe.", "그날 밤도 여느 때와 다름없이 지나갔고 홀콤 양에게 나아진 기미는 없었습니다."], ["The next day she seemed to improve a little.", "이튿날은 조금 나아 보이셨습니다."], ["The day after that her ladyship the Countess, without mentioning the object of her journey to any one in my hearing, proceeded by the morning train to London—her noble husband, with his customary attention, accompanying her to the station.", "그다음 날 백작부인은, 제가 듣는 자리에서는 누구에게도 그 여행의 목적을 말하지 않으신 채 아침 기차로 런던을 향해 떠나셨습니다. 그 고귀한 남편은 늘 하시던 대로 정성껏 역까지 배웅하셨습니다."]], [["I was now left in sole charge of Miss Halcombe, with every apparent chance, in consequence of her sister’s resolution not to leave the bedside, of having Lady Glyde herself to nurse next.", "이제 홀콤 양을 돌보는 일은 오롯이 제 몫이 되었고, 동생분이 머리맡을 떠나지 않겠다고 고집하시는 바람에 다음번에는 글라이드 부인마저 제가 간호하게 되리라는 것이 눈에 훤히 보였습니다."]], [["The only circumstance of any importance that happened in the course of the day was the occurrence of another unpleasant meeting between the doctor and the Count.", "그날 하루 동안 일어난 일 가운데 그나마 중요한 것이라고는, 의사와 백작 사이에 또 한 번 불쾌한 마주침이 있었던 것뿐입니다."]], [["His lordship, on returning from the station, stepped up into Miss Halcombe’s sitting-room to make his inquiries.", "각하는 역에서 돌아오시자 병세를 물으러 홀콤 양의 거실로 올라오셨습니다."], ["I went out from the bedroom to speak to him, Mr. Dawson and Lady Glyde being both with the patient at the time.", "마침 도슨 씨와 글라이드 부인이 함께 환자 곁에 계셨으므로, 제가 침실에서 나가 그분과 이야기를 나누었습니다."], ["The Count asked me many questions about the treatment and the symptoms.", "백작은 치료와 증상에 대해 여러 가지를 물으셨습니다."], ["I informed him that the treatment was of the kind described as “saline,”", "저는 치료가 이른바 “염류”"], ["and that the symptoms, between the attacks of fever, were certainly those of increasing weakness and exhaustion.", "요법이라는 것과, 열이 물러난 사이의 증상은 분명 쇠약과 탈진이 심해지는 것이라고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["Just as I was mentioning these last particulars, Mr. Dawson came out from the bedroom.", "이 마지막 대목을 말씀드리는 참에 도슨 씨가 침실에서 나왔습니다."]], [["“Good-morning, sir,”", "“안녕하십니까, 선생.”"], ["said his lordship, stepping forward in the most urbane manner, and stopping the doctor, with a high-bred resolution impossible to resist, “I greatly fear you find no improvement in the symptoms to-day?”", "각하는 더없이 정중한 태도로 앞으로 나서며, 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 귀티 나는 결연함으로 의사를 붙들고 말씀하셨습니다. “오늘도 증상에 차도가 없는 것은 아닌지 몹시 염려됩니다만?”"]], [["“I find decided improvement,”", "“분명한 차도가 있습니다.”"], ["answered Mr. Dawson.", "도슨 씨가 대답했습니다."]], [["“You still persist in your lowering treatment of this case of fever?”", "“이 열병에 여전히 그 쇠약시키는 요법을 고집하시는 겁니까?”"], ["continued his lordship.", "각하가 이어 물으셨습니다."]], [["“I persist in the treatment which is justified by my own professional experience,”", "“저는 제 직업적 경험이 뒷받침하는 치료를 고수합니다.”"], ["said Mr. Dawson.", "도슨 씨가 말했습니다."]], [["“Permit me to put one question to you on the vast subject of professional experience,”", "“직업적 경험이라는 저 광대한 주제에 대해 한 가지만 여쭙게 해 주십시오.”"], ["observed the Count.", "백작이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“I presume to offer no more advice—I only presume to make an inquiry.", "“저는 더는 감히 조언하지 않겠습니다. 그저 감히 여쭙기만 하겠습니다."], ["You live at some distance, sir, from the gigantic centres of scientific activity—London and Paris.", "선생은 과학 활동의 거대한 중심지인 런던과 파리에서 다소 떨어진 곳에 사시지요."], ["Have you ever heard of the wasting effects of fever being reasonably and intelligibly repaired by fortifying the exhausted patient with brandy, wine, ammonia, and quinine?", "열병이 몸을 축내는 작용을, 지친 환자에게 브랜디와 포도주, 암모니아, 키니네로 기운을 북돋아 합리적이고 납득할 만하게 회복시킨다는 이야기를 들어 보신 적이 있습니까?"], ["Has that new heresy of the highest medical authorities ever reached your ears—Yes or No?”", "최고 의학 권위자들의 그 새로운 이단설이 선생의 귀에 닿은 적이 있습니까, 예입니까 아니오입니까?”"]], [["“When a professional man puts that question to me I shall be glad to answer him,”", "“그 질문을 의사가 제게 한다면 기꺼이 대답하겠습니다.”"], ["said the doctor, opening the door to go out.", "의사는 나가려고 문을 열며 말했습니다."], ["“You are not a professional man, and I beg to decline answering you.”", "“선생은 의사가 아니시니, 대답을 사양하겠습니다.”"]], [["Buffeted in this inexcusably uncivil way on one cheek, the Count, like a practical Christian, immediately turned the other, and said, in the sweetest manner, “Good-morning, Mr.", "이렇게 변명의 여지 없이 무례한 방식으로 한쪽 뺨을 얻어맞고도, 백작은 실천하는 그리스도인답게 즉시 다른 뺨을 내밀며 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Dawson.”", "“안녕히 가십시오, 도슨 씨.”"]], [["If my late beloved husband had been so fortunate as to know his lordship, how highly he and the Count would have esteemed each other!", "세상을 떠난 제 사랑하는 남편이 각하를 알 만한 복을 누렸더라면, 그이와 백작은 서로를 얼마나 높이 존경했을까요!"]], [["Her ladyship the Countess returned by the last train that night, and brought with her the nurse from London.", "백작부인은 그날 밤 마지막 기차로 돌아오시면서 런던에서 간호인을 데려오셨습니다."], ["I was instructed that this person’s name was Mrs. Rubelle.", "저는 그 사람의 이름이 루벨 부인이라고 전해 들었습니다."], ["Her personal appearance, and her imperfect English when she spoke, informed me that she was a foreigner.", "생김새와, 입을 열었을 때의 서투른 영어로 보아 외국인임을 알 수 있었습니다."]], [["I have always cultivated a feeling of humane indulgence for foreigners.", "저는 외국인에 대해 늘 너그러운 마음을 기르려고 애써 왔습니다."], ["They do not possess our blessings and advantages, and they are, for the most part, brought up in the blind errors of Popery.", "그들은 우리가 누리는 축복과 이점을 갖지 못했고, 대개는 교황교의 눈먼 오류 속에서 자랐으니까요."], ["It has also always been my precept and practice, as it was my dear husband’s precept and practice before me (see Sermon XXIX. in the Collection by the late Rev.", "또한 남에게 대접받고 싶은 대로 남을 대접하라는 것이, 제 앞서 사랑하는 남편의 가르침이자 실천이었듯(고故 새뮤얼 마이컬슨 목사 문학사의 설교집 제29편을 보십시오) 제 가르침이자 실천이기도 했습니다."], ["Samuel Michelson, M.A.), to do as I would be done by. On both these accounts I will not say that Mrs.", "이 두 가지 이유에서, 루벨 부인이 쉰 살 안팎의 몸집 작고 깡마르고 음흉한 사람으로, 낯빛은 짙은 갈색이거나 크리올 사람 같고 눈은 옅은 회색으로 사방을 살피더라는 말은 하지 않겠습니다."], ["Rubelle struck me as being a small, wiry, sly person, of fifty or thereabouts, with a dark brown or Creole complexion and watchful light grey eyes. Nor will I mention, for the reasons just alleged, that I thought her dress, though it was of the plainest black silk, inappropriately costly in texture and unnecessarily refined in trimming and finish, for a person in her position in life.", "방금 든 이유에서, 그 여자의 옷이 아주 수수한 검은 비단이기는 했으나 그런 처지의 사람에게는 어울리지 않게 감이 값지고 장식과 마무리가 쓸데없이 고급스러워 보이더라는 말도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["I should not like these things to be said of me, and therefore it is my duty not to say them of Mrs. Rubelle.", "저 자신이 그런 말을 듣기는 싫으니, 루벨 부인에 대해서도 그런 말을 하지 않는 것이 제 도리입니다."], ["I will merely mention that her manners were, not perhaps unpleasantly reserved, but only remarkably quiet and retiring—that she looked about her a great deal, and said very little, which might have arisen quite as much from her own modesty as from distrust of her position at Blackwater Park; and that she declined to partake of supper (which was curious perhaps, but surely not suspicious?), although I myself politely invited her to that meal in my own room.", "다만 이 정도만 말씀드리겠습니다. 그 여자의 태도는 불쾌할 만큼 쌀쌀맞다기보다 다만 유난히 조용하고 물러서 있는 편이었다는 것, 주위를 아주 많이 둘러보면서 말은 거의 하지 않았다는 것, 이는 블랙워터 파크에서 자기 처지를 미덥지 않게 여긴 탓일 수도 있으나 그만큼 겸손한 성품 탓일 수도 있다는 것, 그리고 제가 제 방으로 정중히 청했는데도 저녁 식사를 함께 들기를 사양했다는 것(이는 어쩌면 이상한 일이었을지 몰라도 결코 수상한 일은 아니지 않겠습니까?) 정도입니다."]], [["At the Count’s particular suggestion (so like his lordship’s forgiving kindness!), it was arranged that Mrs. Rubelle should not enter on her duties until she had been seen and approved by the doctor the next morning.", "백작의 각별한 제안으로(참으로 각하다운 너그러운 친절이지요!), 루벨 부인은 이튿날 아침 의사가 보고 승낙하기 전에는 일을 시작하지 않기로 정해졌습니다."], ["I sat up that night.", "그날 밤은 제가 새웠습니다."], ["Lady Glyde appeared to be very unwilling that the new nurse should be employed to attend on Miss Halcombe.", "글라이드 부인은 새 간호인을 홀콤 양에게 붙이는 것을 몹시 꺼리시는 듯했습니다."], ["Such want of liberality towards a foreigner on the part of a lady of her education and refinement surprised me.", "교양과 품위를 갖추신 부인께서 외국인에게 그토록 너그럽지 못하시다니 저는 놀랐습니다. 저는 조심스레 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I ventured to say, “My lady, we must all remember not to be hasty in our judgments on our inferiors—especially when they come from foreign parts.”", "“마님, 우리보다 못한 사람들을 성급히 판단하지 않도록 다들 유념해야지요. 더구나 외국에서 온 사람이라면요.”"], ["Lady Glyde did not appear to attend to me.", "글라이드 부인은 제 말을 듣고 계신 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["She only sighed, and kissed Miss Halcombe’s hand as it lay on the counterpane.", "그저 한숨을 쉬시고는, 이불 위에 놓인 홀콤 양의 손에 입을 맞추셨습니다."], ["Scarcely a judicious proceeding in a sick-room, with a patient whom it was highly desirable not to excite.", "흥분시키지 않는 것이 무엇보다 중요한 환자가 있는 병실에서 결코 분별 있는 행동이라 할 수는 없었지요."], ["But poor Lady Glyde knew nothing of nursing—nothing whatever, I am sorry to say.", "그러나 가엾은 글라이드 부인은 간병에 대해 아무것도 모르셨습니다. 애석하게도 정말 아무것도 모르셨습니다."]], [["The next morning Mrs. Rubelle was sent to the sitting-room, to be approved by the doctor on his way through to the bedroom.", "이튿날 아침 루벨 부인은 의사가 침실로 가는 길에 보고 승낙하도록 거실로 보내졌습니다."]], [["I left Lady Glyde with Miss Halcombe, who was slumbering at the time, and joined Mrs. Rubelle, with the object of kindly preventing her from feeling strange and nervous in consequence of the uncertainty of her situation.", "저는 마침 잠들어 계신 홀콤 양 곁에 글라이드 부인을 남겨 두고, 자기 처지가 불확실한 탓에 그 여자가 낯설고 불안하게 느끼지 않도록 다정히 살펴 줄 생각으로 루벨 부인에게 갔습니다."], ["She did not appear to see it in that light.", "그런데 그 여자는 그렇게 여기는 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["She seemed to be quite satisfied, beforehand, that Mr. Dawson would approve of her, and she sat calmly looking out of window, with every appearance of enjoying the country air.", "도슨 씨가 자기를 승낙하리라고 미리부터 아주 흡족하게 확신하는 듯, 창밖을 내다보며 시골 공기를 즐기는 기색이 역력한 채로 태연히 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Some people might have thought such conduct suggestive of brazen assurance.", "어떤 사람들은 그런 태도를 뻔뻔한 배짱이라 여겼을지도 모릅니다."], ["I beg to say that I more liberally set it down to extraordinary strength of mind.", "저는 좀 더 너그럽게 그것을 남다른 담력으로 여겼다고 말씀드리고 싶습니다."]], [["Instead of the doctor coming up to us, I was sent for to see the doctor.", "의사가 저희에게 올라오는 대신, 제가 의사를 보러 오라는 부름을 받았습니다."], ["I thought this change of affairs rather odd, but Mrs. Rubelle did not appear to be affected by it in any way.", "저는 이렇게 일이 뒤바뀐 것이 좀 이상하다고 생각했지만, 루벨 부인은 그 일에 조금도 동요하는 기색이 없었습니다."], ["I left her still calmly looking out of the window, and still silently enjoying the country air.", "저는 그 여자가 여전히 태연히 창밖을 내다보며 여전히 말없이 시골 공기를 즐기도록 두고 나왔습니다."]], [["Mr. Dawson was waiting for me by himself in the breakfast-room.", "도슨 씨는 아침 식사 방에서 혼자 저를 기다리고 있었습니다."]], [["“About this new nurse, Mrs. Michelson,”", "“이번에 온 간호인 말입니다, 마이컬슨 부인.”"], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했습니다."]], [["“Yes, sir?”", "“네, 선생님?”"]], [["“I find that she has been brought here from London by the wife of that fat old foreigner, who is always trying to interfere with me.", "“그 여자는 늘 내 일에 참견하려 드는 저 뚱뚱한 늙은 외국인의 아내가 런던에서 데려온 사람이더군요."], ["Mrs. Michelson, the fat old foreigner is a quack.”", "마이컬슨 부인, 그 뚱뚱한 늙은 외국인은 돌팔이입니다.”"]], [["This was very rude.", "이것은 매우 무례한 말이었습니다."], ["I was naturally shocked at it.", "저는 당연히 충격을 받았습니다."]], [["“Are you aware, sir,”", "“선생님.” 제가 말했습니다."], ["I said, “that you are talking of a nobleman?”", "“지금 귀족을 두고 하시는 말씀인 줄은 아십니까?”"]], [["“Pooh!", "“흥!"], ["He isn’t the first quack with a handle to his name.", "이름 앞에 작위 붙인 돌팔이가 그자가 처음도 아니지요."], ["They’re all Counts—hang ’em!”", "하나같이 백작입디다, 빌어먹을!”"]], [["“He would not be a friend of Sir Percival Glyde’s, sir, if he was not a member of the highest aristocracy—excepting the English aristocracy, of course.”", "“최고의 귀족 가문 사람이 아니었다면 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 벗이 되지 못하셨겠지요, 선생님. 물론 영국 귀족은 별개입니다만.”"]], [["“Very well, Mrs.", "“좋습니다, 마이컬슨 부인."], ["Michelson, call him what you like, and let us get back to the nurse.", "그자를 뭐라 부르든 마음대로 하시고, 간호인 이야기로 돌아갑시다."], ["I have been objecting to her already.”", "나는 이미 그 여자를 반대했습니다.”"]], [["“Without having seen her, sir?”", "“보지도 않으시고요, 선생님?”"]], [["“Yes, without having seen her.", "“그렇습니다, 보지도 않고요."], ["She may be the best nurse in existence, but she is not a nurse of my providing.", "세상에 둘도 없는 간호인일지도 모르지만, 내가 구한 간호인은 아니니까요."], ["I have put that objection to Sir Percival, as the master of the house.", "나는 이 집 주인인 퍼시벌 경께 그 반대 의견을 말씀드렸습니다."], ["He doesn’t support me.", "경은 내 편을 들지 않으시더군요."], ["He says a nurse of my providing would have been a stranger from London also, and he thinks the woman ought to have a trial, after his wife’s aunt has taken the trouble to fetch her from London.", "내가 구했더라도 런던에서 온 낯선 사람이기는 마찬가지였을 테고, 부인의 고모께서 수고스럽게 런던까지 가서 데려온 사람이니 한번 써 보아야 한다고 하십니다."], ["There is some justice in that, and I can’t decently say No.", "일리가 없지 않아서 나도 대놓고 안 된다고는 못 하겠더군요."], ["But I have made it a condition that she is to go at once, if I find reason to complain of her.", "그러나 나는 그 여자에게 흠잡을 일이 생기면 즉시 내보낸다는 조건을 달았습니다."], ["This proposal being one which I have some right to make, as medical attendant, Sir Percival has consented to it. Now, Mrs.", "이 정도 제안은 담당 의사로서 할 권리가 있는 것이라, 퍼시벌 경도 승낙하셨습니다."], ["Michelson, I know I can depend on you, and I want you to keep a sharp eye on the nurse for the first day or two, and to see that she gives Miss Halcombe no medicines but mine.", "자, 마이컬슨 부인, 나는 부인을 믿을 수 있다는 걸 압니다. 처음 하루 이틀은 그 간호인을 눈여겨봐 주시고, 홀콤 양께 내 약 말고 다른 약은 절대 드리지 못하게 해 주십시오."], ["This foreign nobleman of yours is dying to try his quack remedies (mesmerism included) on my patient, and a nurse who is brought here by his wife may be a little too willing to help him.", "부인이 그리 떠받드는 저 외국 귀족은 내 환자에게 자기 돌팔이 처방을(최면술까지 포함해서) 시험해 보고 싶어 안달이 났는데, 그 사람 아내가 데려온 간호인이라면 그를 돕는 데 지나치게 기꺼울지도 모릅니다."], ["You understand?", "아시겠지요?"], ["Very well, then, we may go upstairs.", "좋습니다, 그럼 위층으로 올라가지요."], ["Is the nurse there?", "간호인이 거기 있습니까?"], ["I’ll say a word to her before she goes into the sick-room.”", "병실에 들어가기 전에 한마디 해 두어야겠습니다.”"]], [["We found Mrs. Rubelle still enjoying herself at the window.", "저희가 가 보니 루벨 부인은 여전히 창가에서 흡족해하고 있었습니다."], ["When I introduced her to Mr. Dawson, neither the doctor’s doubtful looks nor the doctor’s searching questions appeared to confuse her in the least.", "제가 그 여자를 도슨 씨에게 소개했을 때, 의사의 미심쩍은 눈길도 캐묻는 질문도 그 여자를 조금도 당황시키지 못하는 듯했습니다."], ["She answered him quietly in her broken English, and though he tried hard to puzzle her, she never betrayed the least ignorance, so far, about any part of her duties.", "그 여자는 서투른 영어로 조용히 대답했고, 의사가 애써 말문을 막으려 했는데도 자기 일에 관해서는 어느 대목에서도 무지를 드러내는 법이 없었습니다."], ["This was doubtless the result of strength of mind, as I said before, and not of brazen assurance, by any means.", "이는 앞서 말씀드린 대로 결코 뻔뻔한 배짱이 아니라 분명 담력에서 나온 것이었습니다."]], [["We all went into the bedroom.", "저희는 다 함께 침실로 들어갔습니다."]], [["Mrs. Rubelle looked very attentively at the patient, curtseyed to Lady Glyde, set one or two little things right in the room, and sat down quietly in a corner to wait until she was wanted.", "루벨 부인은 환자를 아주 유심히 살펴보고, 글라이드 부인에게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고, 방 안의 자잘한 것 한두 가지를 바로잡아 놓고는, 부름을 받을 때까지 기다리려고 구석에 조용히 앉았습니다."], ["Her ladyship seemed startled and annoyed by the appearance of the strange nurse.", "글라이드 부인은 낯선 간호인이 나타난 것에 놀라고 언짢아하시는 듯했습니다."], ["No one said anything, for fear of rousing Miss Halcombe, who was still slumbering, except the doctor, who whispered a question about the night.", "아직 잠들어 계신 홀콤 양을 깨울까 봐 아무도 입을 열지 않았는데, 다만 의사가 밤새 어떠했는지 속삭여 물었습니다."], ["I softly answered, “Much as usual,” and then Mr.", "저는 “여느 때와 비슷했습니다.”라고 나직이 대답했고, 그러고 나서 도슨 씨는 나갔습니다."], ["Dawson went out. Lady Glyde followed him, I suppose to speak about Mrs.", "글라이드 부인이 그를 따라 나가셨는데, 루벨 부인 이야기를 하시려는 것이었겠지요."], ["Rubelle. For my own part, I had made up my mind already that this quiet foreign person would keep her situation.", "저로서는 이 조용한 외국 여자가 자리를 지키게 되리라고 이미 마음속으로 결론을 내리고 있었습니다."], ["She had all her wits about her, and she certainly understood her business.", "그 여자는 정신을 온전히 차리고 있었고, 자기 일을 확실히 알고 있었습니다."], ["So far, I could hardly have done much better by the bedside myself.", "적어도 거기까지는 저라도 머리맡에서 그보다 훨씬 잘하지는 못했을 것입니다."]], [["Remembering Mr. Dawson’s caution to me, I subjected Mrs.", "도슨 씨의 당부를 잊지 않고, 저는 그 뒤 사나흘 동안 이따금 루벨 부인을 매섭게 살폈습니다."], ["Rubelle to a severe scrutiny at certain intervals for the next three or four days.", "몇 번이고 소리 없이 불쑥 방에 들어가 보았지만, 수상한 짓을 하는 것을 한 번도 잡아내지 못했습니다."], ["I over and over again entered the room softly and suddenly, but I never found her out in any suspicious action.", "저만큼이나 유심히 그 여자를 지켜보시던 글라이드 부인도 아무것도 발견하지 못하셨습니다."], ["Lady Glyde, who watched her as attentively as I did, discovered nothing either. I never detected a sign of the medicine bottles being tampered with, I never saw Mrs.", "저는 약병에 손을 댄 흔적을 조금도 찾지 못했고, 루벨 부인이 백작에게 한마디 하는 것도, 백작이 그 여자에게 한마디 하는 것도 본 적이 없습니다."], ["Rubelle say a word to the Count, or the Count to her. She managed Miss Halcombe with unquestionable care and discretion.", "그 여자는 나무랄 데 없는 정성과 분별로 홀콤 양을 돌보았습니다."], ["The poor lady wavered backwards and forwards between a sort of sleepy exhaustion, which was half faintness and half slumbering, and attacks of fever which brought with them more or less of wandering in her mind.", "가엾은 그분은 반쯤은 실신이고 반쯤은 선잠인 나른한 탈진 상태와, 정신이 어느 정도 오락가락하는 열의 발작 사이를 오갔습니다. 루벨 부인은 앞의 상태일 때는 결코 그분을 흔들어 깨우지 않았고, 뒤의 상태일 때는 낯선 얼굴로 머리맡에 불쑥 나타나 그분을 놀라게 하는 일이 결코 없었습니다."], ["Mrs. Rubelle never disturbed her in the first case, and never startled her in the second, by appearing too suddenly at the bedside in the character of a stranger.", "공을 세운 이에게는 공을 돌려야 하는 법이니(외국인이든 영국인이든 말입니다) 저는 그 권리를 루벨 부인에게도 공평하게 드립니다."], ["Honour to whom honour is due (whether foreign or English)—and I give her privilege impartially to Mrs. Rubelle.", "그 여자는 자기 이야기를 유난히 하지 않았고, 병실 일을 아는 경험 많은 사람의 조언이라면 무엇이든 너무 조용히 아랑곳하지 않았습니다."], ["She was remarkably uncommunicative about herself, and she was too quietly independent of all advice from experienced persons who understood the duties of a sick-room—but with these drawbacks, she was a good nurse, and she never gave either Lady Glyde or Mr. Dawson the shadow of a reason for complaining of her.", "그러나 그런 흠을 빼면 좋은 간호인이었고, 글라이드 부인이나 도슨 씨에게 불평할 만한 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없습니다."]], [["The next circumstance of importance that occurred in the house was the temporary absence of the Count, occasioned by business which took him to London.", "그다음으로 이 집에 일어난 중요한 일은, 볼일이 생겨 런던에 가시게 된 백작이 잠시 집을 비우신 것이었습니다."], ["He went away (I think) on the morning of the fourth day after the arrival of Mrs. Rubelle, and at parting he spoke to Lady Glyde very seriously, in my presence, on the subject of Miss Halcombe.", "제 기억으로 그분은 루벨 부인이 온 지 나흘째 되는 날 아침에 떠나셨는데, 떠나시면서 제가 있는 자리에서 글라이드 부인께 홀콤 양의 일을 두고 아주 진지하게 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“Trust Mr. Dawson,”", "“며칠만 더 도슨 씨를 믿어 보십시오.”"], ["he said, “for a few days more, if you please.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["But if there is not some change for the better in that time, send for advice from London, which this mule of a doctor must accept in spite of himself.", "“그러나 그동안 차도가 없거든 런던에서 의견을 구하십시오. 저 노새 같은 의사도 싫든 좋든 받아들일 수밖에 없을 겁니다."], ["Offend Mr. Dawson, and save Miss Halcombe.", "도슨 씨의 비위를 거스르고, 홀콤 양을 구하십시오."], ["I say this seriously, on my word of honour and from the bottom of my heart.”", "저는 명예를 걸고, 진심을 다해 진지하게 이 말씀을 드립니다.”"]], [["His lordship spoke with extreme feeling and kindness.", "각하는 지극한 정과 친절을 담아 말씀하셨습니다."], ["But poor Lady Glyde’s nerves were so completely broken down that she seemed quite frightened at him.", "그러나 가엾은 글라이드 부인은 신경이 완전히 무너져 내려서 그분을 몹시 두려워하시는 것 같았습니다."], ["She trembled from head to foot, and allowed him to take his leave without uttering a word on her side.", "머리끝부터 발끝까지 떠시면서, 그분이 작별을 고하시는데도 한마디도 하지 못하셨습니다."], ["She turned to me when he had gone, and said, “Oh, Mrs.", "그분이 가신 뒤에 부인은 제게 돌아서서 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Michelson, I am heartbroken about my sister, and I have no friend to advise me!", "“아, 마이컬슨 부인, 언니 때문에 가슴이 미어지는데 의논할 벗이 하나도 없어요!"], ["Do you think Mr. Dawson is wrong?", "도슨 씨가 틀렸다고 생각하세요?"], ["He told me himself this morning that there was no fear, and no need to send for another doctor.”", "오늘 아침에 그이가 직접 저에게 걱정할 것도 없고 다른 의사를 부를 필요도 없다고 말했는데요.”"]], [["“With all respect to Mr. Dawson,”", "“도슨 씨께는 죄송한 말씀입니다만,”"], ["I answered, “in your ladyship’s place I should remember the Count’s advice.”", "제가 대답했습니다. “제가 마님이라면 백작님의 조언을 잊지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["Lady Glyde turned away from me suddenly, with an appearance of despair, for which I was quite unable to account.", "글라이드 부인은 갑자기 제게서 돌아서셨는데, 그 절망하는 듯한 모습을 저로서는 도무지 설명할 수 없었습니다."]], [["“HIS advice!”", "“그 사람의 조언이라니!”"], ["she said to herself.", "부인이 혼잣말을 하셨습니다."], ["“God help us—HIS advice!”", "“하느님, 저희를 도우소서. 그 사람의 조언이라니!”"]], [["The Count was away from Blackwater Park, as nearly as I remember, a week.", "백작이 블랙워터 파크를 비우신 것은 제 기억으로 거의 일주일이었습니다."]], [["Sir Percival seemed to feel the loss of his lordship in various ways, and appeared also, I thought, much depressed and altered by the sickness and sorrow in the house.", "퍼시벌 경은 각하가 안 계신 것을 여러모로 아쉬워하시는 듯했고, 제가 보기에는 집안의 병과 근심 때문에 몹시 침울해지고 달라지신 듯도 했습니다."], ["Occasionally he was so very restless that I could not help noticing it, coming and going, and wandering here and there and everywhere in the grounds.", "이따금 어찌나 안절부절못하시는지 제 눈에 띄지 않을 수 없었습니다. 오락가락하시고, 저택 뜰 여기저기 아무 데나 헤매고 다니셨지요."], ["His inquiries about Miss Halcombe, and about his lady (whose failing health seemed to cause him sincere anxiety), were most attentive.", "홀콤 양에 대해서, 또 건강이 나빠지시는 것이 그분께 진심으로 근심을 안기는 듯한 부인에 대해서 물으시는 것은 더없이 자상하셨습니다."], ["I think his heart was much softened.", "저는 그분의 마음이 많이 누그러졌다고 생각합니다."], ["If some kind clerical friend—some such friend as he might have found in my late excellent husband—had been near him at this time, cheering moral progress might have been made with Sir Percival.", "만일 어느 친절한 목사 벗, 세상을 떠난 제 훌륭한 남편 같은 그런 벗이 그때 곁에 있었더라면, 퍼시벌 경에게서 기쁜 도덕적 진전을 이끌어 낼 수도 있었을 것입니다."], ["I seldom find myself mistaken on a point of this sort, having had experience to guide me in my happy married days.", "저는 이런 문제에 대해서는 좀처럼 잘못 보는 일이 없습니다. 행복했던 결혼 시절의 경험이 저를 이끌어 주니까요."]], [["Her ladyship the Countess, who was now the only company for Sir Percival downstairs, rather neglected him, as I considered—or, perhaps, it might have been that he neglected her.", "이제 아래층에서 퍼시벌 경의 유일한 말벗이 된 백작부인은 제가 보기에 그분을 오히려 소홀히 대하셨습니다. 어쩌면 그분이 부인을 소홀히 대하신 것인지도 모르지요."], ["A stranger might almost have supposed that they were bent, now they were left together alone, on actually avoiding one another.", "낯선 이라면 두 분이 이제 단둘이 남게 되자 서로를 아예 피하기로 작정하신 것이라고 생각했을지도 모릅니다."], ["This, of course, could not be.", "물론 그럴 리야 없겠지요."], ["But it did so happen, nevertheless, that the Countess made her dinner at luncheon-time, and that she always came upstairs towards evening, although Mrs. Rubelle had taken the nursing duties entirely off her hands.", "그러나 어쨌든 백작부인은 점심시간에 저녁 식사를 대신 드셨고, 루벨 부인이 간병을 전부 떠맡아 손을 놓게 되셨는데도 저녁 무렵이면 늘 위층으로 올라오셨습니다."], ["Sir Percival dined by himself, and William (the man out of livery) made the remark, in my hearing, that his master had put himself on half rations of food and on a double allowance of drink.", "퍼시벌 경은 혼자서 저녁을 드셨는데, 제복을 입지 않는 하인 윌리엄이 제가 듣는 데서 주인 어른이 음식은 절반으로 줄이고 술은 곱절로 늘리셨다고 말했습니다."], ["I attach no importance to such an insolent observation as this on the part of a servant.", "하인이 이런 건방진 소리를 하는 데에 저는 아무런 무게도 두지 않습니다."], ["I reprobated it at the time, and I wish to be understood as reprobating it once more on this occasion.", "그때도 그를 나무랐고, 지금 이 자리에서도 다시 한 번 나무라는 것으로 알아주시기 바랍니다."]], [["In the course of the next few days Miss Halcombe did certainly seem to all of us to be mending a little.", "그 뒤 며칠 동안 홀콤 양은 저희 모두가 보기에 분명 조금씩 나아지시는 듯했습니다."], ["Our faith in Mr. Dawson revived.", "도슨 씨에 대한 저희의 믿음도 되살아났습니다."], ["He appeared to be very confident about the case, and he assured Lady Glyde, when she spoke to him on the subject, that he would himself propose to send for a physician the moment he felt so much as the shadow of a doubt crossing his own mind.", "그는 이 병에 대해 매우 자신 있어 보였고, 글라이드 부인이 그 문제로 말을 꺼내시자 조금이라도 의심의 그림자가 마음을 스치는 순간 자기 쪽에서 먼저 전문의를 부르자고 하겠노라고 장담했습니다."]], [["The only person among us who did not appear to be relieved by these words was the Countess.", "저희 중에 이 말을 듣고도 마음이 놓이지 않는 듯한 유일한 분은 백작부인이었습니다."], ["She said to me privately, that she could not feel easy about Miss Halcombe on Mr. Dawson’s authority, and that she should wait anxiously for her husband’s opinion on his return.", "그분은 제게만 따로, 도슨 씨의 말만 믿고는 홀콤 양의 일을 마음 놓을 수 없으며 남편이 돌아와 의견을 말해 주기를 애타게 기다리겠다고 하셨습니다."], ["That return, his letters informed her, would take place in three days’ time.", "편지로 알려 온 바에 따르면 그 귀환은 사흘 뒤가 될 것이라 했습니다."], ["The Count and Countess corresponded regularly every morning during his lordship’s absence.", "백작 내외분은 각하가 집을 비우신 동안 아침마다 어김없이 편지를 주고받으셨습니다."], ["They were in that respect, as in all others, a pattern to married people.", "그 점에서도, 다른 모든 점에서와 마찬가지로 두 분은 부부의 본보기셨습니다."]], [["On the evening of the third day I noticed a change in Miss Halcombe, which caused me serious apprehension.", "사흘째 되는 날 저녁, 저는 홀콤 양에게서 심각한 걱정을 자아내는 변화를 알아차렸습니다."], ["Mrs. Rubelle noticed it too.", "루벨 부인도 그것을 알아차렸습니다."], ["We said nothing on the subject to Lady Glyde, who was then lying asleep, completely overpowered by exhaustion, on the sofa in the sitting-room.", "저희는 그 일에 대해 글라이드 부인께 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그분은 그때 탈진에 완전히 짓눌려 거실 소파에 누워 잠들어 계셨습니다."]], [["Mr. Dawson did not pay his evening visit till later than usual.", "도슨 씨는 여느 때보다 늦게야 저녁 왕진을 왔습니다."], ["As soon as he set eyes on his patient I saw his face alter.", "환자를 보자마자 그의 얼굴빛이 달라지는 것이 제 눈에 보였습니다."], ["He tried to hide it, but he looked both confused and alarmed.", "그는 감추려 했지만, 당황하고 놀란 기색이 역력했습니다."], ["A messenger was sent to his residence for his medicine-chest, disinfecting preparations were used in the room, and a bed was made up for him in the house by his own directions.", "사람을 그의 집으로 보내 약상자를 가져오게 했고, 방 안에 소독약을 썼으며, 그 자신의 지시로 이 집 안에 그가 잘 침상이 마련되었습니다. “열이 전염병으로 돌아섰습니까?”"], ["“Has the fever turned to infection?”", "하고 제가 그에게 속삭였습니다."], ["I whispered to him.", "“그런 것 같습니다.”"], ["“I am afraid it has,”", "그가 대답했습니다."], ["he answered; “we shall know better to-morrow morning.”", "“내일 아침이면 더 확실히 알겠지요.”"]], [["By Mr. Dawson’s own directions Lady Glyde was kept in ignorance of this change for the worse.", "도슨 씨 본인의 지시에 따라 글라이드 부인께는 이 악화 사실을 알리지 않았습니다."], ["He himself absolutely forbade her, on account of her health, to join us in the bedroom that night.", "그는 부인의 건강을 이유로 그날 밤 침실에서 저희와 함께 계시는 것을 딱 잘라 금했습니다."], ["She tried to resist—there was a sad scene—but he had his medical authority to support him, and he carried his point.", "부인은 버티려 하셨고, 가슴 아픈 장면이 있었습니다. 그러나 그에게는 의사로서의 권위가 있었으므로 그의 뜻이 관철되었습니다."]], [["The next morning one of the men-servants was sent to London at eleven o’clock, with a letter to a physician in town, and with orders to bring the new doctor back with him by the earliest possible train.", "이튿날 아침 열한 시에 남자 하인 하나가 런던의 전문의에게 보내는 편지를 지니고, 되도록 가장 이른 기차로 그 새 의사를 모셔 오라는 분부를 받고 런던으로 떠났습니다."], ["Half an hour after the messenger had gone the Count returned to Blackwater Park.", "심부름꾼이 떠난 지 반 시간 뒤에 백작이 블랙워터 파크로 돌아오셨습니다."]], [["The Countess, on her own responsibility, immediately brought him in to see the patient.", "백작부인은 자기 판단으로 곧장 그분을 환자에게 데려가셨습니다."], ["There was no impropriety that I could discover in her taking this course.", "그렇게 하신 데에 제가 보기에 부적절한 구석은 없었습니다."], ["His lordship was a married man, he was old enough to be Miss Halcombe’s father, and he saw her in the presence of a female relative, Lady Glyde’s aunt. Mr.", "각하는 아내가 있는 분이었고, 홀콤 양의 아버지뻘 되는 나이였으며, 여자 친척인 글라이드 부인의 고모가 함께 있는 자리에서 그분을 보셨으니까요."], ["Dawson nevertheless protested against his presence in the room, but I could plainly remark the doctor was too much alarmed to make any serious resistance on this occasion.", "그런데도 도슨 씨는 그분이 방에 있는 것에 항의했지만, 그때 의사는 너무 놀란 나머지 이 일로 진지하게 버티지는 못하는 것이 제 눈에 분명히 보였습니다."]], [["The poor suffering lady was past knowing any one about her.", "병으로 고통받는 가엾은 그분은 이미 곁에 있는 사람을 알아보지 못하는 지경이었습니다."], ["She seemed to take her friends for enemies.", "벗을 적으로 여기시는 듯했습니다."], ["When the Count approached her bedside her eyes, which had been wandering incessantly round and round the room before, settled on his face with a dreadful stare of terror, which I shall remember to my dying day.", "백작이 머리맡으로 다가가시자, 그때까지 쉴 새 없이 방 안을 이리저리 헤매던 그분의 눈이 그분의 얼굴에 멎으며 무서운 공포의 응시를 보냈는데, 저는 그것을 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 것입니다."], ["The Count sat down by her, felt her pulse and her temples, looked at her very attentively, and then turned round upon the doctor with such an expression of indignation and contempt in his face, that the words failed on Mr. Dawson’s lips, and he stood for a moment, pale with anger and alarm—pale and perfectly speechless.", "백작은 그분 곁에 앉아 맥과 관자놀이를 짚어 보시고, 아주 유심히 살펴보신 다음, 의사를 향해 홱 돌아서시는데 그 얼굴에 어린 분노와 경멸의 빛이 어찌나 대단하던지 도슨 씨의 입술에서 말이 얼어붙었고, 그는 잠시 노여움과 놀람으로 창백해진 채, 창백하게 그리고 아주 벙어리가 되어 서 있었습니다."]], [["His lordship looked next at me.", "각하는 다음으로 저를 보셨습니다."]], [["“When did the change happen?”", "“변화가 언제 일어났습니까?”"], ["he asked.", "그분이 물으셨습니다."]], [["I told him the time.", "저는 그 시각을 말씀드렸습니다."]], [["“Has Lady Glyde been in the room since?”", "“그 뒤로 글라이드 부인이 이 방에 들어온 적이 있습니까?”"]], [["I replied that she had not.", "저는 없다고 대답했습니다."], ["The doctor had absolutely forbidden her to come into the room on the evening before, and had repeated the order again in the morning.", "의사가 어제저녁에 부인이 방에 들어오시는 것을 딱 잘라 금했고, 아침에 그 분부를 다시 되풀이했다고 말씀드렸습니다."]], [["“Have you and Mrs. Rubelle been made aware of the full extent of the mischief?”", "“부인과 루벨 부인은 이 사태가 얼마나 심각한지 온전히 알고 계십니까?”"], ["was his next question.", "하는 것이 그분의 다음 물음이었습니다."]], [["We were aware, I answered, that the malady was considered infectious.", "저는 이 병이 전염된다고 여겨진다는 것은 알고 있다고 대답했습니다."], ["He stopped me before I could add anything more.", "제가 더 덧붙이기 전에 그분이 말을 막으셨습니다."]], [["“It is typhus fever,”", "“발진티푸스입니다.”"], ["he said.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["In the minute that passed, while these questions and answers were going on, Mr. Dawson recovered himself, and addressed the Count with his customary firmness.", "이런 문답이 오가는 동안 흐른 그 일 분 사이에 도슨 씨는 정신을 추슬러, 여느 때의 단호함으로 백작에게 말했습니다."]], [["“It is NOT typhus fever,”", "“발진티푸스가 아닙니다.”"], ["he remarked sharply.", "그가 날카롭게 말했습니다."], ["“I protest against this intrusion, sir.", "“선생, 나는 이 무단 개입에 항의합니다."], ["No one has a right to put questions here but me.", "여기서 질문할 권리는 나 말고 누구에게도 없습니다."], ["I have done my duty to the best of my ability—”", "나는 내 능력이 미치는 한 최선을 다해 내 소임을—”"]], [["The Count interrupted him—not by words, but only by pointing to the bed. Mr.", "백작이 그의 말을 끊으셨는데, 말로가 아니라 다만 침대를 가리켜 보이는 것으로였습니다."], ["Dawson seemed to feel that silent contradiction to his assertion of his own ability, and to grow only the more angry under it.", "도슨 씨는 자기 능력에 대한 주장에 그 말 없는 반박이 가해지는 것을 느끼는 듯했고, 그럴수록 더욱 화가 치미는 모양이었습니다."]], [["“I say I have done my duty,”", "“나는 내 소임을 다했다고 말했습니다.”"], ["he reiterated.", "그가 되풀이했습니다."], ["“A physician has been sent for from London.", "“런던으로 전문의를 부르러 사람을 보냈습니다."], ["I will consult on the nature of the fever with him, and with no one else.", "이 열병의 성질에 대해서는 그와 상의할 것이고, 다른 누구와도 상의하지 않겠습니다."], ["I insist on your leaving the room.”", "이 방에서 나가 주시기를 요구합니다.”"]], [["“I entered this room, sir, in the sacred interests of humanity,”", "“선생, 나는 인류애라는 신성한 뜻에서 이 방에 들어왔습니다.”"], ["said the Count.", "백작이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“And in the same interests, if the coming of the physician is delayed, I will enter it again.", "“그리고 같은 뜻에서, 전문의가 오는 것이 늦어진다면 나는 다시 이 방에 들어올 것입니다."], ["I warn you once more that the fever has turned to typhus, and that your treatment is responsible for this lamentable change.", "다시 한 번 경고하건대 이 열병은 발진티푸스로 돌아섰고, 이 통탄할 변화의 책임은 당신의 치료에 있습니다."], ["If that unhappy lady dies, I will give my testimony in a court of justice that your ignorance and obstinacy have been the cause of her death.”", "저 불행한 부인이 세상을 떠난다면, 나는 법정에서 당신의 무지와 고집이 그분의 죽음의 원인이었다고 증언하겠습니다.”"]], [["Before Mr. Dawson could answer, before the Count could leave us, the door was opened from the sitting-room, and we saw Lady Glyde on the threshold.", "도슨 씨가 대답하기도 전에, 백작이 저희 곁을 떠나기도 전에, 거실 쪽에서 문이 열리며 문지방에 서 계신 글라이드 부인이 저희 눈에 들어왔습니다."]], [["“I MUST and WILL come in,”", "“나는 들어가야겠어요, 반드시 들어가겠어요.”"], ["she said, with extraordinary firmness.", "부인이 놀라운 단호함으로 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["Instead of stopping her, the Count moved into the sitting-room, and made way for her to go in.", "백작은 부인을 막는 대신 거실로 물러서서 부인이 들어오시도록 길을 내주셨습니다."], ["On all other occasions he was the last man in the world to forget anything, but in the surprise of the moment he apparently forgot the danger of infection from typhus, and the urgent necessity of forcing Lady Glyde to take proper care of herself.", "다른 어떤 경우에도 그분은 세상에서 무엇 하나 잊는 일이 없는 분이었으나, 그 순간의 놀라움 속에서 발진티푸스가 옮을 위험도, 글라이드 부인이 자신을 제대로 돌보시도록 강권해야 할 절박한 필요도 잊어버리신 모양이었습니다."]], [["To my astonishment Mr. Dawson showed more presence of mind.", "놀랍게도 도슨 씨가 더 침착한 판단력을 보였습니다."], ["He stopped her ladyship at the first step she took towards the bedside.", "그는 부인이 침대 쪽으로 첫걸음을 내딛는 순간 그 앞을 막아섰습니다."], ["“I am sincerely sorry, I am sincerely grieved,”", "“진심으로 송구합니다, 진심으로 안타깝습니다.” 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said. “The fever may, I fear, be infectious.", "“이 열병은 아무래도 전염성이 있을지 모릅니다."], ["Until I am certain that it is not, I entreat you to keep out of the room.”", "그렇지 않다는 확신이 설 때까지는, 부디 이 방에 들어오지 마시기를 간청드립니다.”"]], [["She struggled for a moment, then suddenly dropped her arms and sank forward.", "부인은 잠시 몸부림치다가 문득 두 팔을 늘어뜨리고 앞으로 쓰러졌습니다."], ["She had fainted.", "정신을 잃은 것이었습니다."], ["The Countess and I took her from the doctor and carried her into her own room.", "백작부인과 저는 부인을 의사에게서 넘겨받아 부인의 방으로 옮겼습니다."], ["The Count preceded us, and waited in the passage till I came out and told him that we had recovered her from the swoon.", "백작이 앞서 걸어갔고, 제가 밖으로 나와 부인이 혼절에서 깨어났다고 알려드릴 때까지 복도에서 기다리고 있었습니다."]], [["I went back to the doctor to tell him, by Lady Glyde’s desire, that she insisted on speaking to him immediately.", "저는 다시 의사에게로 가서, 글라이드 부인의 분부대로 부인이 지금 당장 의사와 이야기하기를 고집하신다고 전했습니다."], ["He withdrew at once to quiet her ladyship’s agitation, and to assure her of the physician’s arrival in the course of a few hours.", "그는 곧바로 물러가 부인의 격동을 가라앉히고, 몇 시간 안에 전문의가 도착하리라는 것을 부인께 확언해 드렸습니다."], ["Those hours passed very slowly.", "그 몇 시간이 무척이나 더디게 흘렀습니다."], ["Sir Percival and the Count were together downstairs, and sent up from time to time to make their inquiries.", "퍼시벌 경과 백작은 아래층에 함께 있으면서 이따금 사람을 올려 보내 병세를 물었습니다."], ["At last, between five and six o’clock, to our great relief, the physician came.", "마침내 다섯 시와 여섯 시 사이에, 우리 모두가 크게 안도하게도 전문의가 왔습니다."]], [["He was a younger man than Mr. Dawson, very serious and very decided.", "그는 도슨 씨보다 젊은 사람이었고, 매우 진지하며 매우 단호했습니다."], ["What he thought of the previous treatment I cannot say, but it struck me as curious that he put many more questions to myself and to Mrs. Rubelle than he put to the doctor, and that he did not appear to listen with much interest to what Mr.", "그가 그때까지의 치료를 어떻게 생각했는지는 제가 알 수 없으나, 그가 의사보다 저와 루벨 부인에게 훨씬 더 많은 질문을 던지는 것, 그리고 도슨 씨의 환자를 진찰하는 동안 도슨 씨의 말을 그다지 관심 있게 듣지 않는 듯 보이는 것이 저에게는 이상하게 여겨졌습니다."], ["Dawson said, while he was examining Mr. Dawson’s patient.", "이런 식으로 제가 관찰한 바로 미루어, 저는 백작이 이 병에 대해 처음부터 끝까지 옳았던 것이 아닌가 의심하기 시작했습니다."], ["I began to suspect, from what I observed in this way, that the Count had been right about the illness all the way through, and I was naturally confirmed in that idea when Mr. Dawson, after some little delay, asked the one important question which the London doctor had been sent for to set at rest.", "그리고 도슨 씨가 얼마쯤 뜸을 들인 뒤, 런던의 전문의를 부른 목적이었던 바로 그 중대한 질문을 던졌을 때 저는 자연히 그 생각을 굳히게 되었습니다."]], [["“What is your opinion of the fever?”", "“열병에 대한 선생의 소견은 어떠십니까?”"], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었습니다."]], [["“Typhus,”", "“발진티푸스입니다.”"], ["replied the physician.", "전문의가 대답했습니다."], ["“Typhus fever beyond all doubt.”", "“의심할 여지 없는 발진티푸스 열병입니다.”"]], [["That quiet foreign person, Mrs. Rubelle, crossed her thin brown hands in front of her, and looked at me with a very significant smile.", "그 조용한 외국인 루벨 부인은 야위고 거무스름한 두 손을 앞으로 모아 포개고서, 자못 의미심장한 미소를 띤 채 저를 바라보았습니다."], ["The Count himself could hardly have appeared more gratified if he had been present in the room and had heard the confirmation of his own opinion.", "백작 자신이 그 방에 있으면서 제 소견이 확인되는 것을 직접 들었다 한들, 그보다 더 흡족한 표정을 지을 수는 없었을 것입니다."]], [["After giving us some useful directions about the management of the patient, and mentioning that he would come again in five days’ time, the physician withdrew to consult in private with Mr. Dawson.", "전문의는 환자를 돌보는 방법에 관해 유익한 지시를 몇 가지 내리고, 닷새 뒤에 다시 오겠다고 말한 다음, 도슨 씨와 따로 상의하려고 물러갔습니다."], ["He would offer no opinion on Miss Halcombe’s chances of recovery—he said it was impossible at that stage of the illness to pronounce one way or the other.", "홀콤 양의 회복 가능성에 대해서는 아무런 소견도 밝히려 하지 않았습니다. 병이 이 단계에 있는 이상 이렇다 저렇다 단언하기는 불가능하다는 것이었습니다."]], [["The five days passed anxiously.", "그 닷새는 애태우며 지나갔습니다."]], [["Countess Fosco and myself took it by turns to relieve Mrs. Rubelle, Miss Halcombe’s condition growing worse and worse, and requiring our utmost care and attention.", "포스코 백작부인과 저는 번갈아 가며 루벨 부인과 교대했는데, 홀콤 양의 병세가 점점 더 나빠져 우리의 온 정성과 주의를 요구했기 때문입니다."], ["It was a terribly trying time.", "참으로 견디기 힘든 시간이었습니다."], ["Lady Glyde (supported, as Mr. Dawson said, by the constant strain of her suspense on her sister’s account) rallied in the most extraordinary manner, and showed a firmness and determination for which I should myself never have given her credit.", "글라이드 부인은 (도슨 씨의 말로는 언니 걱정에 조마조마한 마음이 끊임없이 팽팽하게 지탱해 준 덕분에) 참으로 놀라운 방식으로 기운을 되찾았고, 제가 여태 부인에게 있으리라고는 결코 생각지 못했던 굳셈과 결단을 보여 주었습니다."], ["She insisted on coming into the sick-room two or three times every day, to look at Miss Halcombe with her own eyes, promising not to go too close to the bed, if the doctor would consent to her wishes so far. Mr.", "부인은 하루에도 두세 번씩 병실에 들어와 자기 눈으로 직접 홀콤 양을 보아야겠다고 고집했고, 의사가 그만큼이라도 뜻을 들어준다면 침대에 너무 가까이 가지는 않겠다고 약속했습니다."], ["Dawson very unwillingly made the concession required of him—I think he saw that it was hopeless to dispute with her.", "도슨 씨는 몹시 마지못해 요구받은 양보를 했습니다. 제 생각에 그는 부인과 다투어 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알아차렸던 것 같습니다."], ["She came in every day, and she self-denyingly kept her promise.", "부인은 날마다 들어왔고, 스스로를 억누르며 약속을 지켰습니다."], ["I felt it personally so distressing (as reminding me of my own affliction during my husband’s last illness) to see how she suffered under these circumstances, that I must beg not to dwell on this part of the subject any longer.", "그런 처지에서 부인이 얼마나 괴로워하는지를 지켜보는 일이 (제 남편이 마지막 병을 앓던 때의 제 슬픔을 떠올리게 하여) 저 개인적으로도 어찌나 마음 아팠는지, 이 대목에 대해서는 더 이상 늘어놓지 않기를 부디 양해해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["It is more agreeable to me to mention that no fresh disputes took place between Mr. Dawson and the Count.", "그보다는 도슨 씨와 백작 사이에 새로운 다툼이 일어나지는 않았다는 말씀을 드리는 편이 저로서는 더 마음 편합니다."], ["His lordship made all his inquiries by deputy, and remained continually in company with Sir Percival downstairs.", "백작 각하는 모든 문의를 사람을 시켜서 했고, 아래층에서 줄곧 퍼시벌 경과 함께 지냈습니다."]], [["On the fifth day the physician came again and gave us a little hope.", "닷샛날 전문의가 다시 와서 우리에게 약간의 희망을 주었습니다."], ["He said the tenth day from the first appearance of the typhus would probably decide the result of the illness, and he arranged for his third visit to take place on that date.", "그는 발진티푸스 증상이 처음 나타난 날로부터 열흘째가 되는 날이 아마도 병의 결말을 판가름하리라고 말했고, 세 번째 왕진을 그날로 잡았습니다."], ["The interval passed as before—except that the Count went to London again one morning and returned at night.", "그 사이의 기간은 전과 다름없이 흘렀습니다. 다만 백작이 어느 날 아침 다시 런던에 갔다가 밤에 돌아온 일만은 달랐습니다."]], [["On the tenth day it pleased a merciful Providence to relieve our household from all further anxiety and alarm.", "열흘째 되는 날, 자비로우신 섭리께서 우리 집안을 더 이상의 근심과 놀람에서 놓아 주시기를 기뻐하셨습니다."], ["The physician positively assured us that Miss Halcombe was out of danger.", "전문의는 홀콤 양이 고비를 넘겼다고 단호히 확언했습니다. “이제 이분께는 의사가 필요 없습니다."], ["“She wants no doctor now—all she requires is careful watching and nursing for some time to come, and that I see she has.”", "앞으로 얼마 동안 세심하게 지켜보며 간호하는 것, 그것만이 필요할 뿐인데 그것은 이미 갖추어져 있는 것으로 보입니다.”"], ["Those were his own words.", "그것이 그의 말 그대로였습니다."], ["That evening I read my husband’s touching sermon on Recovery from Sickness, with more happiness and advantage (in a spiritual point of view) than I ever remember to have derived from it before.", "그날 저녁 저는 남편이 쓴 「병에서의 회복」이라는 감동적인 설교를 읽었는데, 예전에 그것에서 얻었던 그 어떤 때보다도 (영적인 관점에서) 더 큰 행복과 유익을 느꼈습니다."]], [["The effect of the good news on poor Lady Glyde was, I grieve to say, quite overpowering.", "그 반가운 소식이 가엾은 글라이드 부인에게 미친 영향은, 참으로 애석하게도 감당하기 어려운 것이었습니다. 부인은 너무 쇠약해서 그 격렬한 반동을 견디지 못했고, 하루 이틀 뒤에는 쇠약과 우울에 빠져 방에만 머물러야 했습니다."], ["She was too weak to bear the violent reaction, and in another day or two she sank into a state of debility and depression which obliged her to keep her room.", "안정과 평온, 그리고 그 뒤의 요양지 이동이 도슨 씨가 부인을 위해 내놓을 수 있는 최선의 처방이었습니다. 사정이 그 이상 나빠지지 않은 것은 다행이었습니다."], ["Rest and quiet, and change of air afterwards, were the best remedies which Mr. Dawson could suggest for her benefit.", "왜냐하면 부인이 방에 틀어박힌 바로 그 이튿날, 백작과 의사 사이에 또 한 번 의견 충돌이 있었기 때문입니다."], ["It was fortunate that matters were no worse, for, on the very day after she took to her room, the Count and the doctor had another disagreement—and this time the dispute between them was of so serious a nature that Mr. Dawson left the house.", "그런데 이번 다툼은 성질이 어찌나 심각했던지 도슨 씨가 집을 떠나 버리고 말았습니다."]], [["I was not present at the time, but I understood that the subject of dispute was the amount of nourishment which it was necessary to give to assist Miss Halcombe’s convalescence after the exhaustion of the fever.", "저는 그 자리에 있지 않았으나, 다툼의 주제는 열병으로 기진한 뒤 홀콤 양의 회복을 돕기 위해 얼마만큼의 영양을 공급해야 하는가 하는 문제였다고 들었습니다."], ["Mr. Dawson, now that his patient was safe, was less inclined than ever to submit to unprofessional interference, and the Count (I cannot imagine why) lost all the self-control which he had so judiciously preserved on former occasions, and taunted the doctor, over and over again, with his mistake about the fever when it changed to typhus.", "도슨 씨는 이제 환자가 안전해진 만큼 문외한의 간섭에 굽히고 들어갈 마음이 그 어느 때보다도 적었고, 백작은 (저로서는 까닭을 짐작할 수 없으나) 이전에 그토록 사려 깊게 지켜 오던 자제심을 모조리 잃고서, 열병이 발진티푸스로 바뀌었을 때 의사가 저지른 오진을 두고 몇 번이고 되풀이해 조롱했습니다."], ["The unfortunate affair ended in Mr. Dawson’s appealing to Sir Percival, and threatening (now that he could leave without absolute danger to Miss Halcombe) to withdraw from his attendance at Blackwater Park if the Count’s interference was not peremptorily suppressed from that moment.", "이 딱한 일은 도슨 씨가 퍼시벌 경에게 호소하며, (이제는 홀콤 양에게 절대적인 위험을 끼치지 않고도 떠날 수 있게 된 만큼) 백작의 간섭을 그 순간부터 단호히 막아 주지 않는다면 블랙워터 파크에서의 왕진을 그만두겠다고 으름장을 놓는 것으로 끝났습니다."], ["Sir Percival’s reply (though not designedly uncivil) had only resulted in making matters worse, and Mr. Dawson had thereupon withdrawn from the house in a state of extreme indignation at Count Fosco’s usage of him, and had sent in his bill the next morning.", "퍼시벌 경의 대답은 (일부러 무례하게 굴려던 것은 아니었겠으나) 사태를 더 악화시키는 결과만 낳았고, 그리하여 도슨 씨는 포스코 백작이 자기를 대한 방식에 극도로 분개하며 이 집을 떠났고, 이튿날 아침 청구서를 보내왔습니다."]], [["We were now, therefore, left without the attendance of a medical man.", "그리하여 우리는 이제 의사의 왕진 없이 남겨졌습니다."], ["Although there was no actual necessity for another doctor—nursing and watching being, as the physician had observed, all that Miss Halcombe required—I should still, if my authority had been consulted, have obtained professional assistance from some other quarter, for form’s sake.", "실제로 다른 의사가 꼭 필요한 것은 아니었지만, 즉 전문의가 말한 대로 홀콤 양에게 필요한 것은 간호와 보살핌뿐이었지만, 그래도 만약 제 권한을 물어 주었더라면 저는 형식을 갖추기 위해서라도 다른 곳에서 전문가의 도움을 구했을 것입니다."]], [["The matter did not seem to strike Sir Percival in that light.", "퍼시벌 경에게는 이 일이 그런 식으로 여겨지지 않는 모양이었습니다."], ["He said it would be time enough to send for another doctor if Miss Halcombe showed any signs of a relapse.", "그는 홀콤 양이 재발 기미를 보이면 그때 다른 의사를 부르러 보내도 늦지 않다고 말했습니다."], ["In the meanwhile we had the Count to consult in any minor difficulty, and we need not unnecessarily disturb our patient in her present weak and nervous condition by the presence of a stranger at her bedside.", "그동안에는 사소한 어려움이 생길 때마다 백작에게 상의할 수 있으니, 지금처럼 쇠약하고 예민한 상태에 있는 환자를 낯선 사람이 머리맡에 있게 하여 공연히 어지럽힐 필요가 없다는 것이었습니다."], ["There was much that was reasonable, no doubt, in these considerations, but they left me a little anxious nevertheless.", "이런 고려에는 물론 지당한 점이 많았습니다만, 그럼에도 저는 다소 마음이 놓이지 않았습니다."], ["Nor was I quite satisfied in my own mind of the propriety of our concealing the doctor’s absence as we did from Lady Glyde.", "또한 우리가 그렇게 의사의 부재를 글라이드 부인에게 숨기는 것이 과연 온당한 일인지도 제 마음속으로는 썩 개운치가 않았습니다."], ["It was a merciful deception, I admit—for she was in no state to bear any fresh anxieties.", "그것이 자비로운 속임이었다는 점은 인정합니다. 부인은 새로운 근심을 감당할 만한 상태가 전혀 아니었으니까요."], ["But still it was a deception, and, as such, to a person of my principles, at best a doubtful proceeding.", "그러나 그래도 그것은 속임이었고, 그러한 이상 저와 같은 신조를 지닌 사람에게는 아무리 좋게 보아도 미심쩍은 처사였습니다."]], [["A second perplexing circumstance which happened on the same day, and which took me completely by surprise, added greatly to the sense of uneasiness that was now weighing on my mind.", "같은 날 일어난 두 번째 곤혹스러운 일은 저를 완전히 허를 찔린 상태로 몰아넣었고, 그때 제 마음을 짓누르고 있던 불안감을 크게 더해 주었습니다."]], [["I was sent for to see Sir Percival in the library.", "저는 서재로 퍼시벌 경을 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았습니다."], ["The Count, who was with him when I went in, immediately rose and left us alone together.", "제가 들어갔을 때 함께 있던 백작은 곧바로 일어나 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔습니다."], ["Sir Percival civilly asked me to take a seat, and then, to my great astonishment, addressed me in these terms—", "퍼시벌 경은 정중하게 저에게 앉으라고 청하더니, 참으로 놀랍게도 저에게 이런 말을 꺼냈습니다."]], [["“I want to speak to you, Mrs. Michelson, about a matter which I decided on some time ago, and which I should have mentioned before, but for the sickness and trouble in the house.", "“마이컬슨 부인, 얼마 전에 내가 마음을 정한 일에 대해 이야기하고 싶소."], ["In plain words, I have reasons for wishing to break up my establishment immediately at this place—leaving you in charge, of course, as usual.", "진작 말했어야 했는데 집안에 병이 나고 소란이 있어서 미뤄 두었소. 단도직입적으로 말하자면, 나는 지금 당장 이곳의 살림을 정리해야 할 이유가 있소."], ["As soon as Lady Glyde and Miss Halcombe can travel they must both have change of air.", "물론 관리는 여느 때처럼 당신에게 맡기고 말이오. 글라이드 부인과 홀콤 양이 여행할 수 있게 되는 대로 두 사람 다 요양지를 옮겨야 하오."], ["My friends, Count Fosco and the Countess, will leave us before that time to live in the neighbourhood of London, and I have reasons for not opening the house to any more company, with a view to economising as carefully as I can.", "내 벗인 포스코 백작 부부는 그 전에 우리를 떠나 런던 근교에서 살 것이오. 그리고 나는 될 수 있는 대로 아껴 쓸 생각이라, 이 집에 손님을 더 들이지 않을 이유가 있소."], ["I don’t blame you, but my expenses here are a great deal too heavy.", "당신을 탓하는 건 아니지만, 여기서 나가는 내 비용이 너무나 과하오."], ["In short, I shall sell the horses, and get rid of all the servants at once.", "요컨대 나는 말들을 팔아 치우고, 하인들도 당장 전부 내보낼 작정이오."], ["I never do things by halves, as you know, and I mean to have the house clear of a pack of useless people by this time to-morrow.”", "알다시피 나는 무슨 일이든 어중간하게 하는 법이 없소. 내일 이맘때까지는 쓸모없는 것들을 이 집에서 싹 치워 버릴 생각이오.”"]], [["I listened to him, perfectly aghast with astonishment.", "저는 놀라움에 완전히 아연실색한 채로 그 말을 들었습니다."]], [["“Do you mean, Sir Percival, that I am to dismiss the indoor servants under my charge without the usual month’s warning?”", "“퍼시벌 경, 제가 관리하는 집안 하인들을 통상적인 한 달 전 예고도 없이 내보내라는 말씀이십니까?”"], ["I asked.", "제가 물었습니다."]], [["“Certainly I do.", "“그렇소, 물론이오."], ["We may all be out of the house before another month, and I am not going to leave the servants here in idleness, with no master to wait on.”", "우리 모두가 한 달도 되기 전에 이 집을 떠날지도 모르는데, 시중들 주인도 없는 곳에 하인들을 빈둥거리게 놔둘 생각은 없소.”"]], [["“Who is to do the cooking, Sir Percival, while you are still staying here?”", "“퍼시벌 경께서 아직 여기 머무시는 동안 요리는 누가 합니까?”"]], [["“Margaret Porcher can roast and boil—keep her.", "“마거릿 포처가 굽고 삶을 줄은 알지 않소."], ["What do I want with a cook if I don’t mean to give any dinner-parties?”", "그 애는 남겨 두시오. 만찬회를 열 생각도 없는데 요리사가 무슨 소용이오?”"]], [["“The servant you have mentioned is the most unintelligent servant in the house, Sir Percival.”", "“말씀하신 그 하인은 이 집에서 가장 아둔한 하인입니다, 퍼시벌 경.”"]], [["“Keep her, I tell you, and have a woman in from the village to do the cleaning and go away again.", "“남겨 두라고 하지 않았소. 그리고 마을에서 여자를 하나 불러다 청소만 시키고 돌려보내시오."], ["My weekly expenses must and shall be lowered immediately.", "내 주간 비용은 당장 줄여야 하고, 또 줄일 것이오."], ["I don’t send for you to make objections, Mrs.", "나는 반대 의견을 들으려고 당신을 부른 게 아니오, 마이컬슨 부인."], ["Michelson—I send for you to carry out my plans of economy.", "내 절약 계획을 실행하라고 부른 것이오."], ["Dismiss the whole lazy pack of indoor servants to-morrow, except Porcher.", "포처만 빼고 게으른 집안 하인들을 내일 몽땅 내보내시오."], ["She is as strong as a horse—and we’ll make her work like a horse.”", "그 애는 말처럼 튼튼하니, 말처럼 부려 먹으면 될 것이오.”"]], [["“You will excuse me for reminding you, Sir Percival, that if the servants go to-morrow they must have a month’s wages in lieu of a month’s warning.”", "“외람되오나 한 말씀 여쭙겠습니다, 퍼시벌 경. 하인들이 내일 나간다면 한 달 전 예고를 대신하여 한 달 치 급료를 주어야 합니다.”"]], [["“Let them!", "“주라지!"], ["A month’s wages saves a month’s waste and gluttony in the servants’ hall.”", "한 달 치 급료면 하인방에서 한 달 동안 축내고 먹어 치울 것을 아끼는 셈이오.”"]], [["This last remark conveyed an aspersion of the most offensive kind on my management.", "이 마지막 말은 제 살림 관리에 대한 더없이 모욕적인 종류의 비방을 담고 있었습니다."], ["I had too much self-respect to defend myself under so gross an imputation.", "저는 그토록 무례한 누명 아래에서 자신을 변호하기에는 자존심이 너무 강했습니다."], ["Christian consideration for the helpless position of Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde, and for the serious inconvenience which my sudden absence might inflict on them, alone prevented me from resigning my situation on the spot.", "홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인의 무력한 처지에 대한, 그리고 제가 갑작스레 자리를 비움으로써 두 분께 끼칠지도 모를 심각한 불편에 대한 그리스도인다운 배려, 오직 그것만이 제가 그 자리에서 일을 그만두는 것을 막아 주었습니다."], ["I rose immediately.", "저는 곧바로 일어섰습니다."], ["It would have lowered me in my own estimation to have permitted the interview to continue a moment longer.", "그 면담을 한순간이라도 더 이어 가도록 내버려 두었다면 제 스스로에 대한 평가가 깎였을 것입니다."]], [["“After that last remark, Sir Percival, I have nothing more to say.", "“퍼시벌 경, 그 마지막 말씀을 들은 이상 저는 더 드릴 말씀이 없습니다."], ["Your directions shall be attended to.”", "분부하신 대로 처리하겠습니다.”"], ["Pronouncing those words, I bowed my head with the most distant respect, and went out of the room.", "그 말을 하고서 저는 더없이 서먹한 예의를 갖추어 고개를 숙이고는 방을 나왔습니다."]], [["The next day the servants left in a body.", "이튿날 하인들은 한꺼번에 떠났습니다."], ["Sir Percival himself dismissed the grooms and stablemen, sending them, with all the horses but one, to London.", "퍼시벌 경은 마부들과 마구간 일꾼들을 직접 해고하여, 말 한 필만 남기고 모두 런던으로 보냈습니다."], ["Of the whole domestic establishment, indoors and out, there now remained only myself, Margaret Porcher, and the gardener—this last living in his own cottage, and being wanted to take care of the one horse that remained in the stables.", "안팎의 모든 살림꾼 가운데 이제 남은 사람은 저와 마거릿 포처, 그리고 정원사뿐이었습니다. 이 마지막 사람은 자기 오두막에서 지냈고, 마구간에 남은 한 필의 말을 돌보기 위해 필요했습니다."]], [["With the house left in this strange and lonely condition—with the mistress of it ill in her room—with Miss Halcombe still as helpless as a child—and with the doctor’s attendance withdrawn from us in enmity—it was surely not unnatural that my spirits should sink, and my customary composure be very hard to maintain.", "집이 이렇게 낯설고 쓸쓸한 상태로 남겨지고, 그 안주인은 방에서 앓아누워 있고, 홀콤 양은 여전히 어린아이처럼 무력하고, 의사는 적의를 품고 왕진을 끊어 버린 마당이니, 제 기운이 가라앉고 늘 지녀 오던 평정을 지키기가 몹시 어려워진 것도 분명 이상한 일은 아니었습니다."], ["My mind was ill at ease.", "제 마음은 편치 않았습니다."], ["I wished the poor ladies both well again, and I wished myself away from Blackwater Park.", "저는 가엾은 두 부인이 모두 다시 건강해지기를 바랐고, 그리고 저 자신은 블랙워터 파크에서 멀리 떠나 있기를 바랐습니다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["The next event that occurred was of so singular a nature that it might have caused me a feeling of superstitious surprise, if my mind had not been fortified by principle against any pagan weakness of that sort.", "다음으로 일어난 일은 어찌나 기이한 성질의 것이었던지, 제 마음이 원칙으로 단단히 무장되어 그런 종류의 이교도적 나약함을 물리치지 못했더라면 저는 미신적인 놀라움을 느꼈을지도 모릅니다."], ["The uneasy sense of something wrong in the family which had made me wish myself away from Blackwater Park, was actually followed, strange to say, by my departure from the house.", "이 집안에 무언가 잘못되어 가고 있다는 불안한 예감 때문에 저는 블랙워터 파크를 떠났으면 싶다고 바랐었는데, 이상하게도 그 뒤를 이어 실제로 제가 이 집을 떠나는 일이 벌어졌던 것입니다."], ["It is true that my absence was for a temporary period only, but the coincidence was, in my opinion, not the less remarkable on that account.", "물론 제가 자리를 비운 것은 한동안뿐이었습니다만, 그렇다고 해서 그 우연의 일치가 덜 놀라운 것은 아니었다고 저는 생각합니다."]], [["My departure took place under the following circumstances—", "제가 떠나게 된 경위는 다음과 같습니다—"]], [["A day or two after the servants all left I was again sent for to see Sir Percival.", "하인들이 모두 나가고 하루나 이틀 뒤에 저는 다시 퍼시벌 경께 불려 갔습니다."], ["The undeserved slur which he had cast on my management of the household did not, I am happy to say, prevent me from returning good for evil to the best of my ability, by complying with his request as readily and respectfully as ever.", "그분이 제 살림 관리에 씌우신 부당한 오명도, 다행히 말씀드리자면, 제가 그분의 청에 여느 때와 다름없이 선뜻 공손히 응함으로써 악을 선으로 갚는 것을 막지는 못했습니다."], ["It cost me a struggle with that fallen nature, which we all share in common, before I could suppress my feelings.", "제 감정을 억누르기까지는, 우리 모두가 함께 지니고 있는 저 타락한 본성과 한바탕 싸움을 치러야 했습니다."], ["Being accustomed to self-discipline, I accomplished the sacrifice.", "자기 절제에 익숙한 터라 저는 그 희생을 치러 냈습니다."]], [["I found Sir Percival and Count Fosco sitting together again.", "가 보니 퍼시벌 경과 포스코 백작이 또다시 함께 앉아 계셨습니다."], ["On this occasion his lordship remained present at the interview, and assisted in the development of Sir Percival’s views.", "이번에는 백작께서 면담 자리에 그대로 남으시어 퍼시벌 경의 뜻을 펼쳐 보이는 일을 거드셨습니다."]], [["The subject to which they now requested my attention related to the healthy change of air by which we all hoped that Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde might soon be enabled to profit.", "두 분이 이번에 제 주의를 청하신 문제는, 홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인이 곧 그 덕을 볼 수 있으리라고 우리 모두가 바라던 요양차의 전지(轉地)에 관한 것이었습니다."], ["Sir Percival mentioned that both the ladies would probably pass the autumn (by invitation of Frederick Fairlie, Esquire) at Limmeridge House, Cumberland.", "퍼시벌 경은 두 분이 아마도 (프레더릭 페얼리 씨의 초대로) 컴벌랜드의 리머리지 하우스에서 가을을 보내게 되리라고 말씀하셨습니다."], ["But before they went there, it was his opinion, confirmed by Count Fosco (who here took up the conversation and continued it to the end), that they would benefit by a short residence first in the genial climate of Torquay.", "그러나 그리로 가기 전에 먼저 토키의 온화한 기후에서 잠시 지내는 편이 두 분께 이로우리라는 것이 퍼시벌 경의 의견이었고, 포스코 백작(여기서부터 대화를 이어받아 끝까지 이끌어 가셨습니다)께서도 이를 확인해 주셨습니다."], ["The great object, therefore, was to engage lodgings at that place, affording all the comforts and advantages of which they stood in need, and the great difficulty was to find an experienced person capable of choosing the sort of residence which they wanted.", "그러니 중요한 것은 그곳에 두 분께 필요한 온갖 편의와 이점을 갖춘 숙소를 얻는 일이었고, 큰 어려움은 그런 거처를 고를 만한 경험 있는 사람을 찾는 일이었습니다."], ["In this emergency the Count begged to inquire, on Sir Percival’s behalf, whether I would object to give the ladies the benefit of my assistance, by proceeding myself to Torquay in their interests.", "이 딱한 사정에서 백작께서는 퍼시벌 경을 대신하여, 제가 몸소 토키로 가서 두 분을 위해 힘을 보태 드리는 데 이의가 없겠는지 여쭙고자 한다고 하셨습니다."]], [["It was impossible for a person in my situation to meet any proposal, made in these terms, with a positive objection.", "저 같은 처지의 사람이 그런 식으로 내놓은 제안을 딱 잘라 거절할 수는 없는 노릇이었습니다."]], [["I could only venture to represent the serious inconvenience of my leaving Blackwater Park in the extraordinary absence of all the indoor servants, with the one exception of Margaret Porcher.", "저는 다만 마거릿 포처 하나를 빼고는 집 안 하인이 모조리 없는 이 유별난 형편에 제가 블랙워터 파크를 비우는 것이 여간 곤란한 일이 아니라는 점을 감히 말씀드릴 수 있었을 뿐입니다."], ["But Sir Percival and his lordship declared that they were both willing to put up with inconvenience for the sake of the invalids.", "그러나 퍼시벌 경과 백작께서는 병자들을 위해서라면 두 분 다 기꺼이 불편을 감수하겠다고 하셨습니다."], ["I next respectfully suggested writing to an agent at Torquay, but I was met here by being reminded of the imprudence of taking lodgings without first seeing them.", "저는 다음으로 토키의 중개인에게 편지를 보내는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 조심스레 여쭈었으나, 숙소를 직접 보지도 않고 얻는 것이 얼마나 경솔한 일인지 일깨움을 받았을 뿐입니다."], ["I was also informed that the Countess (who would otherwise have gone to Devonshire herself) could not, in Lady Glyde’s present condition, leave her niece, and that Sir Percival and the Count had business to transact together which would oblige them to remain at Blackwater Park.", "또 백작부인께서는 (그렇지 않았다면 몸소 데번셔로 가셨을 테지만) 글라이드 부인이 지금 같은 상태에서는 조카딸 곁을 떠날 수 없으시고, 퍼시벌 경과 백작께서는 함께 처리해야 할 일이 있어 블랙워터 파크에 남아 계셔야 한다는 말씀도 들었습니다."], ["In short, it was clearly shown me that if I did not undertake the errand, no one else could be trusted with it.", "요컨대, 제가 그 심부름을 맡지 않으면 달리 그 일을 맡길 만한 사람이 아무도 없다는 것이 분명히 드러났습니다."], ["Under these circumstances, I could only inform Sir Percival that my services were at the disposal of Miss Halcombe and Lady Glyde.", "이런 사정이니 저로서는 홀콤 양과 글라이드 부인을 위해 제 힘을 쓰겠노라고 퍼시벌 경께 아뢸 수밖에 없었습니다."]], [["It was thereupon arranged that I should leave the next morning, that I should occupy one or two days in examining all the most convenient houses in Torquay, and that I should return with my report as soon as I conveniently could.", "그리하여 제가 이튿날 아침에 떠나 토키에서 가장 편리한 집들을 하루 이틀 동안 두루 살펴보고, 되도록 빨리 결과를 보고하러 돌아오는 것으로 정해졌습니다."], ["A memorandum was written for me by his lordship, stating the requisites which the place I was sent to take must be found to possess, and a note of the pecuniary limit assigned to me was added by Sir Percival.", "제가 얻어야 할 곳이 반드시 갖추어야 할 조건들을 적은 각서를 백작께서 써 주셨고, 퍼시벌 경은 거기에 제게 허락된 금액의 한도를 덧붙여 적으셨습니다."]], [["My own idea on reading over these instructions was, that no such residence as I saw described could be found at any watering-place in England, and that, even if it could by chance be discovered, it would certainly not be parted with for any period on such terms as I was permitted to offer.", "그 지시서를 읽어 보며 제가 든 생각은, 거기 적힌 것과 같은 거처는 영국의 어느 해변 요양지에서도 찾을 수 없으리라는 것, 설령 요행히 찾아낸다 한들 제게 허락된 조건으로는 결코 얼마 동안이라도 빌려주지 않으리라는 것이었습니다."], ["I hinted at these difficulties to both the gentlemen, but Sir Percival (who undertook to answer me) did not appear to feel them.", "저는 두 분께 이런 어려움을 넌지시 말씀드렸습니다만, 퍼시벌 경은(대답을 맡으셨습니다) 그것을 어려움으로 여기시는 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["It was not for me to dispute the question.", "제가 따질 일은 아니었습니다."], ["I said no more, but I felt a very strong conviction that the business on which I was sent away was so beset by difficulties that my errand was almost hopeless at starting.", "저는 더 말하지 않았습니다만, 제가 떠맡아 가는 그 일이 어찌나 난관투성이인지 출발부터 거의 가망이 없는 심부름이라는 확신이 아주 강하게 들었습니다."]], [["Before I left I took care to satisfy myself that Miss Halcombe was going on favourably.", "떠나기 전에 저는 홀콤 양의 차도가 좋은지 반드시 제 눈으로 확인해 두었습니다."]], [["There was a painful expression of anxiety in her face which made me fear that her mind, on first recovering itself, was not at ease.", "그분의 얼굴에는 괴로운 근심의 빛이 어려 있어서, 정신을 처음 되찾고 나서 마음이 편치 않으신 게 아닌가 하는 걱정이 들었습니다."], ["But she was certainly strengthening more rapidly than I could have ventured to anticipate, and she was able to send kind messages to Lady Glyde, saying that she was fast getting well, and entreating her ladyship not to exert herself again too soon.", "그러나 몸은 분명 제가 감히 바랐던 것보다 빠르게 회복되고 계셨고, 글라이드 부인께 다정한 전갈—자신은 빠르게 낫고 있으니 부디 다시 무리하지 마시라는 전갈을 보내실 만큼 기운도 있으셨습니다."], ["I left her in charge of Mrs. Rubelle, who was still as quietly independent of every one else in the house as ever.", "저는 여전히 집안의 누구에게도 조용히 아랑곳하지 않는 루벨 부인에게 그분을 맡기고 떠났습니다."], ["When I knocked at Lady Glyde’s door before going away, I was told that she was still sadly weak and depressed, my informant being the Countess, who was then keeping her company in her room.", "떠나기 전 글라이드 부인의 방문을 두드렸을 때는 그분이 아직 몹시 쇠약하고 침울하시다는 대답을 들었는데, 그렇게 알려 주신 이는 그때 방에서 마님과 함께 계시던 백작부인이었습니다."], ["Sir Percival and the Count were walking on the road to the lodge as I was driven by in the chaise.", "제가 마차를 타고 지나갈 때 퍼시벌 경과 백작은 문간채로 난 길을 걷고 계셨습니다."], ["I bowed to them and quitted the house, with not a living soul left in the servants’ offices but Margaret Porcher.", "저는 두 분께 인사를 드리고 집을 떠났으니, 하인들 처소에는 마거릿 포처 말고는 살아 있는 사람이 하나도 남지 않았습니다."]], [["Every one must feel what I have felt myself since that time, that these circumstances were more than unusual—they were almost suspicious.", "누구라도 그때 이후로 제가 느껴 온 바를 똑같이 느끼실 것입니다. 이런 정황들은 예사롭지 않은 정도를 넘어—거의 수상쩍기까지 했다는 것을 말입니다."], ["Let me, however, say again that it was impossible for me, in my dependent position, to act otherwise than I did.", "그러나 다시 한번 말씀드리건대, 남에게 매인 처지의 제가 달리 처신할 도리는 없었습니다."]], [["The result of my errand at Torquay was exactly what I had foreseen.", "토키에서 제 심부름의 결과는 제가 내다본 그대로였습니다."], ["No such lodgings as I was instructed to take could be found in the whole place, and the terms I was permitted to give were much too low for the purpose, even if I had been able to discover what I wanted.", "지시받은 것과 같은 숙소는 그 고장 어디에도 없었고, 제게 허락된 조건은 설령 원하는 곳을 찾아냈다 한들 턱없이 낮았습니다."], ["I accordingly returned to Blackwater Park, and informed Sir Percival, who met me at the door, that my journey had been taken in vain.", "그리하여 저는 블랙워터 파크로 돌아와, 문간에서 저를 맞으신 퍼시벌 경께 헛걸음을 했다고 아뢰었습니다."], ["He seemed too much occupied with some other subject to care about the failure of my errand, and his first words informed me that even in the short time of my absence another remarkable change had taken place in the house.", "그분은 무언가 다른 일에 골몰해 계신 듯 제 심부름이 실패한 것 따위에는 아랑곳하지 않으셨고, 첫마디부터 제가 잠시 자리를 비운 그 짧은 사이에 집안에 또 한 번 놀라운 변화가 있었음을 알려 주셨습니다."]], [["The Count and Countess Fosco had left Blackwater Park for their new residence in St. John’s Wood.", "포스코 백작 내외분이 세인트존스우드의 새 거처로 떠나 블랙워터 파크를 비웠다는 것이었습니다."]], [["I was not made aware of the motive for this sudden departure—I was only told that the Count had been very particular in leaving his kind compliments to me.", "그 갑작스러운 출발의 까닭은 제게 알려 주지 않았습니다—다만 백작께서 제게 각별히 안부를 남기고 가셨다는 말만 들었습니다."], ["When I ventured on asking Sir Percival whether Lady Glyde had any one to attend to her comforts in the absence of the Countess, he replied that she had Margaret Porcher to wait on her, and he added that a woman from the village had been sent for to do the work downstairs.", "백작부인이 안 계신 동안 글라이드 부인의 시중을 들어 드릴 사람이 있느냐고 감히 퍼시벌 경께 여쭈었더니, 마거릿 포처가 시중을 든다고 대답하시고는 아래층 일을 할 여자를 마을에서 부르러 보냈다고 덧붙이셨습니다."]], [["The answer really shocked me—there was such a glaring impropriety in permitting an under-housemaid to fill the place of confidential attendant on Lady Glyde.", "그 대답에 저는 정말이지 충격을 받았습니다—아래 하녀에게 글라이드 부인의 몸종 노릇을 맡기다니 예의에 어긋나도 너무나 어긋난 일이었습니다."], ["I went upstairs at once, and met Margaret on the bedroom landing.", "저는 곧바로 위층으로 올라가 침실 층계참에서 마거릿과 마주쳤습니다."], ["Her services had not been required (naturally enough), her mistress having sufficiently recovered that morning to be able to leave her bed.", "(당연한 일이지만) 그 애의 시중은 필요치 않았으니, 마님께서 그날 아침 자리에서 일어나실 만큼 회복되셨기 때문입니다."], ["I asked next after Miss Halcombe, but I was answered in a slouching, sulky way, which left me no wiser than I was before.", "다음으로 홀콤 양의 안부를 물었으나, 늘어진 데다 뚱한 대답이 돌아와 저는 전보다 나을 것이 하나도 없었습니다."]], [["I did not choose to repeat the question, and perhaps provoke an impertinent reply.", "저는 그 물음을 되풀이하여 버릇없는 대꾸를 자아내고 싶지는 않았습니다."], ["It was in every respect more becoming to a person in my position to present myself immediately in Lady Glyde’s room.", "제 처지의 사람으로서는 곧장 글라이드 부인의 방으로 가 뵙는 편이 모든 면에서 온당했습니다."]], [["I found that her ladyship had certainly gained in health during the last few days.", "마님께서는 지난 며칠 사이 확실히 건강이 나아지신 것을 알 수 있었습니다."], ["Although still sadly weak and nervous, she was able to get up without assistance, and to walk slowly about her room, feeling no worse effect from the exertion than a slight sensation of fatigue.", "아직 몹시 쇠약하고 신경이 예민하시긴 했으나 남의 부축 없이 일어나실 수 있었고, 방 안을 천천히 거니셔도 약간 피로한 느낌 말고는 나빠지는 기색이 없으셨습니다."], ["She had been made a little anxious that morning about Miss Halcombe, through having received no news of her from any one.", "그날 아침 마님께서는 아무에게서도 홀콤 양의 소식을 듣지 못하신 탓에 조금 불안해하고 계셨습니다."], ["I thought this seemed to imply a blamable want of attention on the part of Mrs. Rubelle, but I said nothing, and remained with Lady Glyde to assist her to dress.", "저는 이것이 루벨 부인 쪽의 나무랄 만한 무성의를 뜻하는 듯싶었으나 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마님 곁에 남아 옷 입으시는 것을 도와 드렸습니다."], ["When she was ready we both left the room together to go to Miss Halcombe.", "채비가 끝나자 우리는 홀콤 양에게 가려고 함께 방을 나섰습니다."]], [["We were stopped in the passage by the appearance of Sir Percival.", "복도에서 우리는 퍼시벌 경이 나타나시는 바람에 걸음을 멈추었습니다."], ["He looked as if he had been purposely waiting there to see us.", "그분은 마치 우리를 보려고 일부러 거기서 기다리고 계셨던 것 같은 모습이었습니다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "“어디 가는 길이오?”"], ["he said to Lady Glyde.", "그분이 글라이드 부인께 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“To Marian’s room,”", "“매리언의 방에요.”"], ["she answered.", "마님께서 대답하셨습니다."]], [["“It may spare you a disappointment,”", "“실망을 덜어 드리자면,”"], ["remarked Sir Percival, “if I tell you at once that you will not find her there.”", "퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다. “거기 가도 그 사람은 없다고 지금 일러 두는 편이 낫겠군.”"]], [["“Not find her there!”", "“거기 없다니요!”"]], [["“No.", "“그렇소."], ["She left the house yesterday morning with Fosco and his wife.”", "어제 아침에 포스코 내외와 함께 이 집을 떠났소.”"]], [["Lady Glyde was not strong enough to bear the surprise of this extraordinary statement.", "글라이드 부인은 이 엄청난 말이 안겨 준 충격을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 못하셨습니다."], ["She turned fearfully pale, and leaned back against the wall, looking at her husband in dead silence.", "얼굴이 무섭도록 창백해지며 벽에 등을 기대신 채, 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 남편을 바라보셨습니다."]], [["I was so astonished myself that I hardly knew what to say.", "저 자신도 어찌나 놀랐는지 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐습니다."], ["I asked Sir Percival if he really meant that Miss Halcombe had left Blackwater Park.", "저는 퍼시벌 경께 홀콤 양이 정말로 블랙워터 파크를 떠났다는 말씀이시냐고 여쭈었습니다."]], [["“I certainly mean it,”", "“물론 그렇다는 말이오.”"], ["he answered.", "그분이 대답하셨습니다."]], [["“In her state, Sir Percival!", "“그런 몸으로요, 퍼시벌 경!"], ["Without mentioning her intentions to Lady Glyde!”", "글라이드 부인께 뜻을 말씀도 드리지 않고요!”"]], [["Before he could reply her ladyship recovered herself a little and spoke.", "그분이 대답하시기도 전에 마님께서 조금 정신을 차리시고 입을 여셨습니다."]], [["“Impossible!”", "“그럴 리가 없어요!”"], ["she cried out in a loud, frightened manner, taking a step or two forward from the wall.", "마님께서 벽에서 한두 걸음 앞으로 나서시며 크고 겁에 질린 목소리로 외치셨습니다."], ["“Where was the doctor?", "“의사 선생님은 어디 계셨나요?"], ["where was Mr. Dawson when Marian went away?”", "매리언이 떠날 때 도슨 선생님은 어디 계셨어요?”"]], [["“Mr. Dawson wasn’t wanted, and wasn’t here,”", "“도슨 씨는 필요하지도 않았고, 여기 있지도 않았소.”"], ["said Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“He left of his own accord, which is enough of itself to show that she was strong enough to travel.", "“제 발로 그만두고 갔지. 그것만 봐도 그 사람이 여행할 만큼 튼튼했다는 게 드러나는 셈이오."], ["How you stare!", "뭘 그렇게 빤히 쳐다보시오!"], ["If you don’t believe she has gone, look for yourself.", "갔다는 걸 못 믿겠거든 직접 가서 보구려."], ["Open her room door, and all the other room doors if you like.”", "그 방 문을 열어 보고, 원한다면 다른 방문도 죄다 열어 보시오.”"]], [["She took him at his word, and I followed her.", "마님은 그 말대로 하셨고 저도 뒤따랐습니다."], ["There was no one in Miss Halcombe’s room but Margaret Porcher, who was busy setting it to rights.", "홀콤 양의 방에는 방을 치우느라 바쁜 마거릿 포처 말고는 아무도 없었습니다."], ["There was no one in the spare rooms or the dressing-rooms when we looked into them afterwards.", "이어 들여다본 빈방들과 화장방에도 아무도 없었습니다."], ["Sir Percival still waited for us in the passage.", "퍼시벌 경은 여전히 복도에서 우리를 기다리고 계셨습니다."], ["As we were leaving the last room that we had examined Lady Glyde whispered, “Don’t go, Mrs.", "마지막으로 살펴본 방을 나서면서 글라이드 부인께서 속삭이셨습니다. “가지 마세요, 마이컬슨 부인!"], ["Michelson! don’t leave me, for God’s sake!”", "제발이지 저를 혼자 두지 마세요!”"], ["Before I could say anything in return she was out again in the passage, speaking to her husband.", "제가 무어라 대답하기도 전에 마님은 다시 복도로 나가 남편에게 말을 걸고 계셨습니다."]], [["“What does it mean, Sir Percival?", "“이게 무슨 뜻이죠, 퍼시벌 경?"], ["I insist—I beg and pray you will tell me what it means.”", "말씀해 주세요—간청드려요, 제발 무슨 뜻인지 말씀해 주세요.”"]], [["“It means,”", "“뜻인즉,”"], ["he answered, “that Miss Halcombe was strong enough yesterday morning to sit up and be dressed, and that she insisted on taking advantage of Fosco’s going to London to go there too.”", "그분이 대답하셨습니다. “홀콤 양이 어제 아침에 일어나 앉아 옷을 입을 만큼 튼튼했고, 포스코가 런던에 가는 길을 이용해 자기도 같이 가겠다고 고집을 부렸다는 것이오.”"]], [["“To London!”", "“런던으로요!”"]], [["“Yes—on her way to Limmeridge.”", "“그렇소—리머리지로 가는 길에 말이오.”"]], [["Lady Glyde turned and appealed to me.", "글라이드 부인께서 돌아서서 제게 호소하셨습니다."]], [["“You saw Miss Halcombe last,” she said.", "“홀콤 양을 마지막으로 본 건 부인이었죠.”"], ["“Tell me plainly, Mrs.", "마님께서 말씀하셨습니다."], ["Michelson, did you think she looked fit to travel?”", "“솔직히 말해 주세요, 마이컬슨 부인. 그이가 여행할 만해 보이던가요?”"]], [["“Not in MY opinion, your ladyship.”", "“제 눈에는 그렇지 않았습니다, 마님.”"]], [["Sir Percival, on his side, instantly turned and appealed to me also.", "이번에는 퍼시벌 경께서 곧바로 저를 향해 돌아서서 역시 제게 물으셨습니다."]], [["“Before you went away,”", "“떠나기 전에,”"], ["he said, “did you, or did you not, tell the nurse that Miss Halcombe looked much stronger and better?”", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “홀콤 양이 훨씬 튼튼하고 좋아 보인다고 간병인에게 말했소, 안 했소?”"]], [["“I certainly made the remark, Sir Percival.”", "“그런 말씀을 드린 것은 사실입니다, 퍼시벌 경.”"]], [["He addressed her ladyship again the moment I offered that reply.", "제가 그렇게 대답하자마자 그분은 다시 마님을 향해 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“Set one of Mrs. Michelson’s opinions fairly against the other,”", "“마이컬슨 부인의 두 가지 의견을 공평하게 견주어 보고,”"], ["he said, “and try to be reasonable about a perfectly plain matter.", "그분이 말씀하셨습니다. “이 뻔한 일에 대해 이치에 맞게 생각해 보시오."], ["If she had not been well enough to be moved do you think we should any of us have risked letting her go?", "옮겨도 될 만큼 몸이 낫지 않았다면 우리 중 누구라도 그 사람을 보내는 모험을 했겠소?"], ["She has got three competent people to look after her—Fosco and your aunt, and Mrs. Rubelle, who went away with them expressly for that purpose.", "돌봐 줄 사람이 셋이나 유능하게 붙어 있소—포스코와 당신 고모, 그리고 바로 그 일을 위해 일부러 함께 떠난 루벨 부인 말이오."], ["They took a whole carriage yesterday, and made a bed for her on the seat in case she felt tired.", "어제 객차 한 칸을 통째로 잡아서, 피곤해할 때를 대비해 좌석에 잠자리까지 만들어 주었소."], ["To-day, Fosco and Mrs. Rubelle go on with her themselves to Cumberland.”", "오늘은 포스코와 루벨 부인이 그 사람을 데리고 컴벌랜드까지 갈 거요.”"]], [["“Why does Marian go to Limmeridge and leave me here by myself?”", "“매리언은 왜 나를 여기 혼자 두고 리머리지로 가는 거죠?”"], ["said her ladyship, interrupting Sir Percival.", "마님께서 퍼시벌 경의 말을 끊고 물으셨습니다."]], [["“Because your uncle won’t receive you till he has seen your sister first,” he replied.", "“당신 삼촌이 당신 언니부터 만나 보기 전에는 당신을 받아들이지 않겠다니까 그렇지.”"], ["“Have you forgotten the letter he wrote to her at the beginning of her illness?", "그분이 대답하셨습니다. “그 사람이 병나던 무렵에 삼촌이 보낸 편지를 잊었소?"], ["It was shown to you, you read it yourself, and you ought to remember it.”", "당신한테도 보여 줘서 직접 읽었으니 기억해야 마땅할 텐데.”"]], [["“I do remember it.”", "“기억해요.”"]], [["“If you do, why should you be surprised at her leaving you?", "“기억한다면서 왜 그 사람이 당신을 두고 간 걸 놀라워하시오?"], ["You want to be back at Limmeridge, and she has gone there to get your uncle’s leave for you on his own terms.”", "당신은 리머리지로 돌아가고 싶어 하고, 그 사람은 삼촌이 내건 조건대로 당신 허락을 받아 내려고 거기 간 거요.”"]], [["Poor Lady Glyde’s eyes filled with tears.", "가엾은 글라이드 부인의 눈에 눈물이 고였습니다."]], [["“Marian never left me before,”", "“매리언은 한 번도 나한테 작별 인사 없이 떠난 적이 없어요.”"], ["she said, “without bidding me good-bye.”", "마님께서 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“She would have bid you good-bye this time,”", "“이번에도 작별 인사를 했을 거요.”"], ["returned Sir Percival, “if she had not been afraid of herself and of you.", "퍼시벌 경이 대꾸하셨습니다. “자기 자신과 당신이 두렵지만 않았다면 말이오."], ["She knew you would try to stop her, she knew you would distress her by crying. Do you want to make any more objections?", "당신이 붙잡으려 들 것을 알았고, 당신이 울어서 자기를 괴롭게 하리라는 것도 알았던 거지."], ["If you do, you must come downstairs and ask questions in the dining-room.", "더 따질 게 있소? 있거든 아래층으로 내려와서 식당에서 묻구려."], ["These worries upset me.", "이런 성가신 일은 사람 진을 빼는군."], ["I want a glass of wine.”", "포도주나 한 잔 해야겠소.”"]], [["He left us suddenly.", "그분은 갑자기 우리를 두고 가 버리셨습니다."]], [["His manner all through this strange conversation had been very unlike what it usually was.", "이 이상한 대화가 오가는 내내 그분의 태도는 평소와 아주 딴판이었습니다."], ["He seemed to be almost as nervous and fluttered, every now and then, as his lady herself.", "이따금 그분은 마님 못지않게 신경이 곤두서고 안절부절못하시는 듯이 보였습니다."], ["I should never have supposed that his health had been so delicate, or his composure so easy to upset.", "저는 그분의 건강이 그토록 여리다거나 침착함이 그토록 쉽게 흐트러지리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했습니다."]], [["I tried to prevail on Lady Glyde to go back to her room, but it was useless.", "저는 글라이드 부인을 방으로 돌아가시게 하려 애썼습니다만 소용없었습니다."], ["She stopped in the passage, with the look of a woman whose mind was panic-stricken.", "마님은 공포에 사로잡힌 여인의 얼굴로 복도에 멈춰 서 계셨습니다."]], [["“Something has happened to my sister!”", "“언니에게 무슨 일이 생긴 거예요!”"], ["she said.", "마님께서 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["“Remember, my lady, what surprising energy there is in Miss Halcombe,”", "“마님, 홀콤 양께 얼마나 놀라운 기력이 있는지 헤아려 보십시오.”"], ["I suggested.", "제가 여쭈었습니다."], ["“She might well make an effort which other ladies in her situation would be unfit for.", "“같은 처지의 다른 부인들이라면 엄두도 못 낼 일을 그분은 능히 해내실 수 있습니다."], ["I hope and believe there is nothing wrong—I do indeed.”", "잘못된 일은 아무것도 없기를 바라고, 또 그렇게 믿습니다—정말입니다.”"]], [["“I must follow Marian,”", "“매리언을 따라가야겠어요.”"], ["said her ladyship, with the same panic-stricken look.", "마님께서 여전히 공포에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“I must go where she has gone, I must see that she is alive and well with my own eyes.", "“언니가 간 곳으로 가야 해요. 언니가 살아서 무사한지 이 눈으로 봐야겠어요."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["come down with me to Sir Percival.”", "저와 함께 퍼시벌 경께 내려가 주세요.”"]], [["I hesitated, fearing that my presence might be considered an intrusion.", "제 존재가 주제넘은 참견으로 여겨질까 저어되어 망설였습니다."], ["I attempted to represent this to her ladyship, but she was deaf to me.", "마님께 그 점을 말씀드리려 하였으나 마님은 제 말을 들으려 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["She held my arm fast enough to force me to go downstairs with her, and she still clung to me with all the little strength she had at the moment when I opened the dining-room door.", "마님은 제 팔을 어찌나 단단히 붙드셨는지 저는 함께 아래층으로 내려갈 수밖에 없었고, 제가 식당 문을 여는 순간에도 마님은 그때 남아 있던 얼마 안 되는 힘을 다해 제게 매달려 계셨습니다."]], [["Sir Percival was sitting at the table with a decanter of wine before him.", "퍼시벌 경은 포도주 병을 앞에 놓고 탁자에 앉아 계셨습니다."], ["He raised the glass to his lips as we went in and drained it at a draught.", "우리가 들어서자 그분은 잔을 입으로 가져가 단숨에 비우셨습니다."], ["Seeing that he looked at me angrily when he put it down again, I attempted to make some apology for my accidental presence in the room.", "잔을 내려놓으시며 저를 성난 눈으로 보시기에, 저는 얼떨결에 이 방에 들어온 것에 대해 무어라 변명을 하려 했습니다."]], [["“Do you suppose there are any secrets going on here?”", "“여기 무슨 비밀이라도 있는 줄 아나?”"], ["he broke out suddenly; “there are none—there is nothing underhand, nothing kept from you or from any one.”", "그분이 별안간 터뜨리듯 말씀하셨습니다. “그런 건 없어—뒤가 구린 것도 없고, 당신이나 그 누구에게도 숨기는 것 따위 없단 말이야.”"], ["After speaking those strange words loudly and sternly, he filled himself another glass of wine and asked Lady Glyde what she wanted of him.", "그 이상한 말을 큰 소리로 매섭게 내뱉고 나서, 그분은 포도주를 한 잔 더 채우고는 글라이드 부인께 무슨 볼일이냐고 물으셨습니다."]], [["“If my sister is fit to travel I am fit to travel”", "“언니가 여행할 만하다면 저도 여행할 만합니다.”"], ["said her ladyship, with more firmness than she had yet shown.", "마님께서 여태껏 보이신 적 없는 단호함으로 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“I come to beg you will make allowances for my anxiety about Marian, and let me follow her at once by the afternoon train.”", "“매리언에 대한 제 걱정을 헤아려 주시고, 오늘 오후 기차로 곧장 뒤따라가게 해 주십사 부탁드리러 왔어요.”"]], [["“You must wait till to-morrow,”", "“내일까지 기다려야 하오.”"], ["replied Sir Percival, “and then if you don’t hear to the contrary you can go.", "퍼시벌 경이 대답하셨습니다. “그때 가서 반대되는 소식이 없거든 가시오."], ["I don’t suppose you are at all likely to hear to the contrary, so I shall write to Fosco by to-night’s post.”", "반대되는 소식이 올 것 같지는 않으니, 오늘 밤 우편으로 포스코에게 편지를 써 두겠소.”"]], [["He said those last words holding his glass up to the light, and looking at the wine in it instead of at Lady Glyde.", "그 마지막 말씀을 하실 때 그분은 잔을 불빛에 비추어 들고, 글라이드 부인이 아니라 그 안의 포도주를 들여다보고 계셨습니다."], ["Indeed he never once looked at her throughout the conversation.", "실은 그 대화 내내 마님을 단 한 번도 쳐다보지 않으셨습니다."], ["Such a singular want of good breeding in a gentleman of his rank impressed me, I own, very painfully.", "그만한 지체의 신사에게서 그토록 유별난 예법의 결여를 보는 것은, 고백하건대, 제게 몹시 마음 아픈 일이었습니다."]], [["“Why should you write to Count Fosco?”", "“어째서 포스코 백작께 편지를 쓰시나요?”"], ["she asked, in extreme surprise.", "마님께서 몹시 놀라 물으셨습니다."]], [["“To tell him to expect you by the midday train,”", "“당신이 낮 기차로 갈 테니 기다리라고 일러 두려는 거요.”"], ["said Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“He will meet you at the station when you get to London, and take you on to sleep at your aunt’s in St. John’s Wood.”", "“당신이 런던에 닿으면 역으로 마중 나와서, 세인트존스우드에 있는 당신 고모 집으로 데려가 재워 줄 거요.”"]], [["Lady Glyde’s hand began to tremble violently round my arm—why I could not imagine.", "글라이드 부인의 손이 제 팔을 감은 채 심하게 떨리기 시작했습니다—어째서인지 저로서는 짐작할 수 없었습니다."]], [["“There is no necessity for Count Fosco to meet me,”", "“포스코 백작이 저를 마중 나올 필요는 없어요.” 마님께서 말씀하셨습니다."], ["she said. “I would rather not stay in London to sleep.”", "“런던에서 자고 가지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“You must.", "“그래야 하오."], ["You can’t take the whole journey to Cumberland in one day.", "컴벌랜드까지 하루 만에 갈 수는 없소."], ["You must rest a night in London—and I don’t choose you to go by yourself to an hotel.", "런던에서 하룻밤 쉬어야 하는데—당신 혼자 여관에 가는 건 내가 원치 않소."], ["Fosco made the offer to your uncle to give you house-room on the way down, and your uncle has accepted it.", "포스코가 당신 삼촌에게, 내려가는 길에 재워 주겠다고 제안했고 삼촌이 그것을 받아들였소."], ["Here!", "자!"], ["here is a letter from him addressed to yourself.", "여기 삼촌이 당신 앞으로 쓴 편지가 있소."], ["I ought to have sent it up this morning, but I forgot.", "오늘 아침에 올려 보냈어야 했는데 잊었군."], ["Read it and see what Mr. Fairlie himself says to you.”", "읽어 보고 페얼리 씨가 직접 뭐라고 하는지 보시오.”"]], [["Lady Glyde looked at the letter for a moment and then placed it in my hands.", "글라이드 부인은 잠시 그 편지를 바라보시더니 제 손에 쥐여 주셨습니다."]], [["“Read it,”", "“읽어 주세요.”"], ["she said faintly.", "마님께서 힘없이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“I don’t know what is the matter with me.", "“제가 왜 이러는지 모르겠어요."], ["I can’t read it myself.”", "도무지 혼자서는 읽을 수가 없네요.”"]], [["It was a note of only four lines—so short and so careless that it quite struck me.", "그것은 겨우 네 줄짜리 쪽지였는데, 어찌나 짧고 무심한지 저로서는 적잖이 놀랐습니다."], ["If I remember correctly it contained no more than these words—", "제 기억이 옳다면 그 안에는 이런 말밖에 없었습니다—"]], [["“Dearest Laura, Please come whenever you like.", "“사랑하는 로라, 아무 때나 오고 싶을 때 오려무나."], ["Break the journey by sleeping at your aunt’s house.", "오는 길에 네 고모 댁에서 하룻밤 묵어 가고."], ["Grieved to hear of dear Marian’s illness.", "매리언이 앓는다니 마음이 아프구나."], ["Affectionately yours, Frederick Fairlie.”", "사랑을 담아, 프레더릭 페얼리.”"]], [["“I would rather not go there—I would rather not stay a night in London,”", "“거기는 가고 싶지 않아요—런던에서 하룻밤 묵고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["said her ladyship, breaking out eagerly with those words before I had quite done reading the note, short as it was.", "마님께서 제가 그 짧은 쪽지를 미처 다 읽기도 전에 다급히 그 말을 터뜨리셨습니다."], ["“Don’t write to Count Fosco!", "“포스코 백작께 편지하지 마세요!"], ["Pray, pray don’t write to him!”", "제발, 제발 그이에게 편지하지 마세요!”"]], [["Sir Percival filled another glass from the decanter so awkwardly that he upset it and spilt all the wine over the table.", "퍼시벌 경은 술병에서 또 한 잔을 따르시다가 어찌나 서투르게 하셨는지 잔을 넘어뜨려 포도주를 죄다 탁자에 쏟으셨습니다."], ["“My sight seems to be failing me,”", "“눈이 어두워지는 모양이군.”"], ["he muttered to himself, in an odd, muffled voice.", "그분이 이상하게 먹먹한 목소리로 혼잣말을 중얼거리셨습니다."], ["He slowly set the glass up again, refilled it, and drained it once more at a draught.", "그러고는 천천히 잔을 다시 세우고 채워서 또다시 단숨에 들이켜셨습니다."], ["I began to fear, from his look and manner, that the wine was getting into his head.", "저는 그 표정과 태도로 보아 술기운이 오르는 게 아닌가 걱정되기 시작했습니다."]], [["“Pray don’t write to Count Fosco,”", "“제발 포스코 백작께 편지하지 마세요.”"], ["persisted Lady Glyde, more earnestly than ever.", "글라이드 부인께서 어느 때보다 간절하게 매달리셨습니다."]], [["“Why not, I should like to know?”", "“왜 안 된다는 거요, 어디 들어나 봅시다!”"], ["cried Sir Percival, with a sudden burst of anger that startled us both.", "퍼시벌 경이 갑자기 노기를 터뜨리며 소리치시는 바람에 우리 둘 다 깜짝 놀랐습니다."], ["“Where can you stay more properly in London than at the place your uncle himself chooses for you—at your aunt’s house?", "“런던에서 당신 삼촌이 손수 정해 준 곳보다—당신 고모 집보다 더 마땅히 묵을 데가 어디 있소?"], ["Ask Mrs. Michelson.”", "마이컬슨 부인에게 물어보시오.”"]], [["The arrangement proposed was so unquestionably the right and the proper one, that I could make no possible objection to it.", "제안된 그 조처는 의심할 여지 없이 옳고 온당한 것이어서 저로서는 도무지 반대할 도리가 없었습니다."], ["Much as I sympathised with Lady Glyde in other respects, I could not sympathise with her in her unjust prejudices against Count Fosco.", "다른 일에서는 글라이드 부인께 그토록 마음이 쓰였습니다만, 포스코 백작에 대한 마님의 부당한 편견에는 공감할 수 없었습니다."], ["I never before met with any lady of her rank and station who was so lamentably narrow-minded on the subject of foreigners.", "저는 그런 지체와 신분의 부인이 외국인에 관해서 그토록 딱하리만치 편협한 것을 이제껏 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["Neither her uncle’s note nor Sir Percival’s increasing impatience seemed to have the least effect on her.", "삼촌의 쪽지도, 점점 더해 가는 퍼시벌 경의 조바심도 마님께는 조금도 먹혀들지 않는 듯했습니다."], ["She still objected to staying a night in London, she still implored her husband not to write to the Count.", "마님은 여전히 런던에서 하룻밤 묵는 것을 마다하셨고, 여전히 백작께 편지하지 말아 달라고 남편께 애원하셨습니다."]], [["“Drop it!”", "“그만두시오!”"], ["said Sir Percival, rudely turning his back on us.", "퍼시벌 경이 무례하게 우리에게 등을 돌리며 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“If you haven’t sense enough to know what is best for yourself other people must know it for you.", "“자기한테 뭐가 제일 좋은지 분간할 머리가 없으면 남이 대신 알아줘야 하는 법이오."], ["The arrangement is made and there is an end of it.", "이미 정해진 일이고, 그것으로 끝이오."], ["You are only wanted to do what Miss Halcombe has done before you—-”", "당신은 그저 홀콤 양이 먼저 한 대로 하기만 하면 되는데——”"]], [["“Marian?”", "“매리언이요?”"], ["repeated her Ladyship, in a bewildered manner; “Marian sleeping in Count Fosco’s house!”", "마님께서 어리둥절한 태도로 되뇌셨습니다. “매리언이 포스코 백작 집에서 잤다고요!”"]], [["“Yes, in Count Fosco’s house.", "“그렇소, 포스코 백작 집에서."], ["She slept there last night to break the journey, and you are to follow her example, and do what your uncle tells you.", "어젯밤 여정을 나누느라 거기서 잤소. 당신도 그 본을 따라 삼촌이 시키는 대로 하시오."], ["You are to sleep at Fosco’s to-morrow night, as your sister did, to break the journey.", "당신 언니가 그랬듯이 내일 밤 포스코 집에서 자고 여정을 나누란 말이오."], ["Don’t throw too many obstacles in my way!", "내 앞길에 걸림돌을 너무 많이 놓지 마시오!"], ["don’t make me repent of letting you go at all!”", "아예 보내 준 걸 후회하게 만들지 말란 말이오!”"]], [["He started to his feet, and suddenly walked out into the verandah through the open glass doors.", "그분은 벌떡 일어나시더니, 열려 있던 유리문을 지나 불쑥 베란다로 나가 버리셨습니다."]], [["“Will your ladyship excuse me,”", "“마님, 외람됩니다만,” 제가 속삭였습니다."], ["I whispered, “if I suggest that we had better not wait here till Sir Percival comes back?", "“퍼시벌 경께서 돌아오실 때까지 여기서 기다리지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 여쭈어도 될까요?"], ["I am very much afraid he is over-excited with wine.”", "아무래도 술기운이 지나치신 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽습니다.”"]], [["She consented to leave the room in a weary, absent manner.", "마님은 지친 듯 넋 나간 태도로 방을 나서는 데 응하셨습니다."]], [["As soon as we were safe upstairs again, I did all I could to compose her ladyship’s spirits.", "무사히 위층으로 올라오자마자 저는 마님의 마음을 가라앉혀 드리려고 할 수 있는 것을 다 했습니다."], ["I reminded her that Mr. Fairlie’s letters to Miss Halcombe and to herself did certainly sanction, and even render necessary, sooner or later, the course that had been taken.", "저는 페얼리 씨가 홀콤 양과 마님께 보낸 편지들이 이번에 취해진 방침을 분명히 승인하고 있으며, 조만간 그리하도록 만들기까지 한다는 점을 일깨워 드렸습니다."], ["She agreed to this, and even admitted, of her own accord, that both letters were strictly in character with her uncle’s peculiar disposition—but her fears about Miss Halcombe, and her unaccountable dread of sleeping at the Count’s house in London, still remained unshaken in spite of every consideration that I could urge.", "마님도 이에 동의하셨고, 두 편지 모두 삼촌의 별난 성정과 꼭 들어맞는다는 것을 스스로 인정까지 하셨습니다—그러나 홀콤 양에 대한 두려움과 런던의 백작 댁에서 자는 것에 대한 까닭 모를 공포는, 제가 아무리 이런저런 이유를 들어도 여전히 흔들리지 않았습니다."], ["I thought it my duty to protest against Lady Glyde’s unfavourable opinion of his lordship, and I did so, with becoming forbearance and respect.", "저는 글라이드 부인이 백작을 나쁘게 보시는 데 대해 이의를 여쭙는 것이 제 도리라고 여겨, 분수에 맞는 조심스러움과 공경을 갖추어 그리 하였습니다."]], [["“Your ladyship will pardon my freedom,”", "“마님, 제 주제넘음을 용서해 주십시오.” 저는 마무리 삼아 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I remarked, in conclusion, “but it is said, ‘by their fruits ye shall know them.’ I am sure the Count’s constant kindness and constant attention, from the very beginning of Miss Halcombe’s illness, merit our best confidence and esteem.", "“허나 ‘그 열매로 그들을 알리라’ 하지 않았습니까. 홀콤 양께서 앓기 시작하신 바로 그때부터 백작께서 한결같이 베푸신 친절과 한결같은 보살핌은 마땅히 우리의 두터운 믿음과 존경을 받을 만하다고 저는 믿습니다."], ["Even his lordship’s serious misunderstanding with Mr. Dawson was entirely attributable to his anxiety on Miss Halcombe’s account.”", "백작께서 도슨 씨와 크게 틀어지신 것조차 오로지 홀콤 양을 염려하신 탓이었습니다.”"]], [["“What misunderstanding?”", "“틀어지다니, 무슨 일인가요?”"], ["inquired her ladyship, with a look of sudden interest.", "마님께서 갑자기 관심을 보이는 얼굴로 물으셨습니다."]], [["I related the unhappy circumstances under which Mr. Dawson had withdrawn his attendance—mentioning them all the more readily because I disapproved of Sir Percival’s continuing to conceal what had happened (as he had done in my presence) from the knowledge of Lady Glyde.", "저는 도슨 씨가 왕진을 그만두게 된 딱한 사정을 말씀드렸습니다—퍼시벌 경께서 (제가 보는 앞에서 그러셨듯이) 그 일을 글라이드 부인께 계속 숨기시는 것을 저는 못마땅하게 여겼던 터라 더더욱 선뜻 말씀드렸습니다."]], [["Her ladyship started up, with every appearance of being additionally agitated and alarmed by what I had told her.", "마님께서는 제가 말씀드린 것에 한층 더 동요하고 겁먹은 기색이 역력한 채 벌떡 일어나셨습니다."]], [["“Worse!", "“더 나빠요!"], ["worse than I thought!”", "생각했던 것보다 더 나빠요!”"], ["she said, walking about the room, in a bewildered manner.", "마님께서 어리둥절한 걸음으로 방 안을 오가며 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“The Count knew Mr. Dawson would never consent to Marian’s taking a journey—he purposely insulted the doctor to get him out of the house.”", "“백작은 도슨 선생님이 매리언의 여행을 결코 허락하지 않으리라는 걸 알았던 거예요—그이를 집에서 내보내려고 일부러 의사 선생님을 모욕한 거라고요.”"]], [["“Oh, my lady!", "“오, 마님!"], ["my lady!”", "마님!”"], ["I remonstrated.", "제가 말렸습니다."]], [["“Mrs. Michelson!”", "“마이컬슨 부인!”"], ["she went on vehemently, “no words that ever were spoken will persuade me that my sister is in that man’s power and in that man’s house with her own consent.", "마님께서 격하게 말을 이으셨습니다. “세상의 그 어떤 말로도, 제 언니가 스스로 원해서 그 사람의 손아귀에, 그 사람의 집에 있다고 저를 설득하지는 못할 거예요."], ["My horror of him is such, that nothing Sir Percival could say and no letters my uncle could write, would induce me, if I had only my own feelings to consult, to eat, drink, or sleep under his roof.", "저는 그이가 어찌나 무서운지, 퍼시벌 경이 무슨 말을 하든 삼촌이 무슨 편지를 쓰든, 제 마음만 따르면 되는 일이라면 그 지붕 아래서 먹지도 마시지도 자지도 않을 거예요."], ["But my misery of suspense about Marian gives me the courage to follow her anywhere, to follow her even into Count Fosco’s house.”", "하지만 매리언 걱정에 애가 타는 이 괴로움이, 언니를 어디로든—포스코 백작의 집까지도—따라갈 용기를 줍니다.”"]], [["I thought it right, at this point, to mention that Miss Halcombe had already gone on to Cumberland, according to Sir Percival’s account of the matter.", "저는 이 대목에서, 퍼시벌 경의 말씀에 따르면 홀콤 양은 이미 컴벌랜드로 떠나셨다는 점을 말씀드리는 것이 옳다고 여겼습니다."]], [["“I am afraid to believe it!”", "“그 말을 믿기가 두려워요!”"], ["answered her ladyship.", "마님께서 대답하셨습니다."], ["“I am afraid she is still in that man’s house.", "“언니가 아직 그 사람 집에 있을까 봐 두려워요."], ["If I am wrong, if she has really gone on to Limmeridge, I am resolved I will not sleep to-morrow night under Count Fosco’s roof.", "제 생각이 틀렸다면, 언니가 정말로 리머리지로 갔다면, 저는 내일 밤 포스코 백작의 지붕 아래서 자지 않기로 마음먹었어요."], ["My dearest friend in the world, next to my sister, lives near London.", "언니 다음으로 이 세상에서 제일 소중한 벗이 런던 근처에 살아요."], ["You have heard me, you have heard Miss Halcombe, speak of Mrs.", "저한테서도, 홀콤 양한테서도 베지 부인 이야기를 들으셨죠?"], ["Vesey? I mean to write, and propose to sleep at her house.", "그분께 편지를 써서 그 댁에서 자게 해 달라고 청할 참이에요."], ["I don’t know how I shall get there—I don’t know how I shall avoid the Count—but to that refuge I will escape in some way, if my sister has gone to Cumberland.", "어떻게 거기까지 갈지도, 어떻게 백작을 피할지도 모르겠지만, 언니가 컴벌랜드로 갔다면 저는 어떻게든 그 피난처로 달아날 거예요."], ["All I ask of you to do, is to see yourself that my letter to Mrs. Vesey goes to London to-night, as certainly as Sir Percival’s letter goes to Count Fosco.", "부인께 부탁드리는 건 오직 하나, 퍼시벌 경의 편지가 포스코 백작께 가는 것만큼이나 확실하게 베지 부인께 보내는 제 편지도 오늘 밤 런던으로 가도록 손수 확인해 주시는 일이에요."], ["I have reasons for not trusting the post-bag downstairs.", "아래층 우편 가방을 믿지 못할 까닭이 제게는 있어요."], ["Will you keep my secret, and help me in this?", "제 비밀을 지켜 주시고 이 일을 도와주시겠어요?"], ["it is the last favour, perhaps, that I shall ever ask of you.”", "아마 이것이 제가 부인께 청하는 마지막 부탁이 될 거예요.”"]], [["I hesitated, I thought it all very strange, I almost feared that her ladyship’s mind had been a little affected by recent anxiety and suffering.", "저는 망설였고, 이 모든 것이 매우 이상하다고 여겼으며, 마님의 정신이 최근의 근심과 고통으로 조금 상하신 게 아닌가 하는 걱정마저 들었습니다."], ["At my own risk, however, I ended by giving my consent.", "그러나 저는 결국 제 책임을 무릅쓰고 승낙하고 말았습니다."], ["If the letter had been addressed to a stranger, or to any one but a lady so well known to me by report as Mrs. Vesey, I might have refused.", "그 편지가 낯선 사람 앞으로 된 것이었거나, 소문으로나마 제가 익히 아는 베지 부인 같은 분이 아닌 다른 누구 앞으로 된 것이었다면 저는 거절했을지도 모릅니다."], ["I thank God—looking to what happened afterwards—I thank God I never thwarted that wish, or any other, which Lady Glyde expressed to me, on the last day of her residence at Blackwater Park.", "하느님께 감사드립니다—나중에 벌어진 일을 생각하면—글라이드 부인께서 블랙워터 파크에 머무신 마지막 날에 제게 말씀하신 그 소원을, 아니 그 어떤 소원도 제가 가로막지 않았음을 하느님께 감사드립니다."]], [["The letter was written and given into my hands.", "편지는 다 쓰여 제 손에 건네졌습니다."], ["I myself put it into the post-box in the village that evening.", "저는 그날 저녁 그 편지를 손수 마을 우체통에 넣었습니다."]], [["We saw nothing more of Sir Percival for the rest of the day.", "그날 남은 시간 동안 우리는 퍼시벌 경을 더는 뵙지 못했습니다."]], [["I slept, by Lady Glyde’s own desire, in the next room to hers, with the door open between us.", "저는 글라이드 부인께서 손수 청하신 대로, 사이의 문을 열어 둔 채 마님 방 옆방에서 잤습니다."], ["There was something so strange and dreadful in the loneliness and emptiness of the house, that I was glad, on my side, to have a companion near me.", "텅 비어 적막한 이 집에는 어찌나 이상하고 무서운 데가 있는지, 저로서도 곁에 사람이 있는 것이 다행이었습니다."], ["Her ladyship sat up late, reading letters and burning them, and emptying her drawers and cabinets of little things she prized, as if she never expected to return to Blackwater Park.", "마님께서는 밤늦도록 앉아 편지들을 읽고는 태우셨고, 서랍과 장에서 아끼시던 자잘한 물건들을 꺼내어 비우셨는데, 마치 다시는 블랙워터 파크로 돌아오지 않으실 작정인 듯했습니다."], ["Her sleep was sadly disturbed when she at last went to bed—she cried out in it several times, once so loud that she woke herself.", "마침내 잠자리에 드신 뒤에도 마님의 잠은 몹시 뒤숭숭했습니다—몇 번이나 잠결에 소리를 지르셨고, 한 번은 어찌나 크게 지르셨는지 스스로 놀라 깨어나셨습니다."], ["Whatever her dreams were, she did not think fit to communicate them to me.", "어떤 꿈을 꾸셨든 마님은 그것을 제게 알려 주는 것이 마땅하다고 여기지 않으셨습니다."], ["Perhaps, in my situation, I had no right to expect that she should do so.", "어쩌면 제 처지에 그것을 기대할 권리는 없었겠지요. 이제 와 대수로운 일은 아닙니다."], ["It matters little now.", "저는 마님이 가여웠습니다."], ["I was sorry for her, I was indeed heartily sorry for her all the same.", "그래도 정말이지 진심으로 가여웠습니다."]], [["The next day was fine and sunny.", "이튿날은 맑고 화창했습니다."], ["Sir Percival came up, after breakfast, to tell us that the chaise would be at the door at a quarter to twelve—the train to London stopping at our station at twenty minutes after.", "퍼시벌 경은 아침 식사 뒤에 올라오시어, 마차가 열두 시 십오 분 전에 문 앞에 대기할 것이며—런던행 기차가 우리 역에 열두 시 이십 분에 선다고 일러 주셨습니다."], ["He informed Lady Glyde that he was obliged to go out, but added that he hoped to be back before she left.", "그분은 글라이드 부인께 자신이 외출해야 한다고 알리시면서도, 마님이 떠나시기 전에 돌아오기를 바란다고 덧붙이셨습니다."], ["If any unforeseen accident delayed him, I was to accompany her to the station, and to take special care that she was in time for the train.", "뜻밖의 일로 늦어지거든 제가 마님을 모시고 역까지 가서 기차 시간에 늦지 않도록 각별히 살피라고 하셨습니다."], ["Sir Percival communicated these directions very hastily—walking here and there about the room all the time.", "퍼시벌 경은 이 지시를 아주 서둘러 전하셨는데—그러는 내내 방 안 이리저리를 서성이셨습니다."], ["Her ladyship looked attentively after him wherever he went.", "마님께서는 그분이 어디로 가시든 눈여겨 그 뒤를 좇으셨습니다."], ["He never once looked at her in return.", "그분은 한 번도 마님을 마주 보지 않으셨습니다."]], [["She only spoke when he had done, and then she stopped him as he approached the door, by holding out her hand.", "마님은 그분의 말씀이 다 끝난 뒤에야 입을 여셨고, 그러고는 문 쪽으로 다가가시는 그분을 손을 내밀어 붙드셨습니다."]], [["“I shall see you no more,”", "“이제 다시는 뵙지 못하겠지요.”"], ["she said, in a very marked manner.", "마님께서 아주 힘주어 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“This is our parting—our parting, it may be for ever.", "“이것이 우리의 이별이에요—영영 이별일지도 모르는 이별."], ["Will you try to forgive me, Percival, as heartily as I forgive YOU?”", "제가 당신을 진심으로 용서하듯이, 당신도 저를 용서해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["His face turned of an awful whiteness all over, and great beads of perspiration broke out on his bald forehead.", "그분의 얼굴은 온통 소름 끼치도록 하얗게 질렸고, 벗어진 이마에는 굵은 땀방울이 돋았습니다."], ["“I shall come back,”", "“돌아오겠소.”"], ["he said, and made for the door, as hastily as if his wife’s farewell words had frightened him out of the room.", "그분은 그렇게 말씀하시고, 마치 아내의 작별 인사에 놀라 방에서 쫓겨 나가기라도 하는 듯 황급히 문으로 향하셨습니다."]], [["I had never liked Sir Percival, but the manner in which he left Lady Glyde made me feel ashamed of having eaten his bread and lived in his service.", "저는 퍼시벌 경을 좋아한 적이 한 번도 없었습니다만, 그분이 글라이드 부인을 두고 떠나신 그 태도는 그분의 밥을 얻어먹고 그분을 섬기며 살아온 것이 부끄럽게 느껴질 정도였습니다."], ["I thought of saying a few comforting and Christian words to the poor lady, but there was something in her face, as she looked after her husband when the door closed on him, that made me alter my mind and keep silence.", "저는 가엾은 마님께 위로가 될 그리스도인다운 말씀을 몇 마디 드릴까 하였으나, 문이 남편의 뒤로 닫힌 뒤 그 뒤를 바라보시는 마님의 얼굴에는 무언가가 있어 저는 마음을 바꾸어 잠자코 있었습니다."]], [["At the time named the chaise drew up at the gates.", "정해진 시각에 마차가 대문 앞에 와서 섰습니다."], ["Her ladyship was right—Sir Percival never came back.", "마님의 말씀이 옳았습니다—퍼시벌 경은 끝내 돌아오지 않으셨습니다."], ["I waited for him till the last moment, and waited in vain.", "저는 마지막 순간까지 그분을 기다렸으나 헛일이었습니다."]], [["No positive responsibility lay on my shoulders, and yet I did not feel easy in my mind.", "제 어깨에 뚜렷한 책임이 지워진 것은 아니었습니다만, 그래도 마음이 편치 않았습니다."], ["“It is of your own free will,”", "“마님께서 런던으로 가시는 것은,”"], ["I said, as the chaise drove through the lodge-gates, “that your ladyship goes to London?”", "마차가 문간채 대문을 지날 때 제가 여쭈었습니다. “마님의 자유로운 뜻에서 하시는 일이지요?”"]], [["“I will go anywhere,”", "“저는 어디든 갈 거예요.”"], ["she answered, “to end the dreadful suspense that I am suffering at this moment.”", "마님께서 대답하셨습니다. “지금 이 순간 제가 겪고 있는 이 끔찍한 애타는 마음을 끝낼 수만 있다면요.”"]], [["She had made me feel almost as anxious and as uncertain about Miss Halcombe as she felt herself.", "마님 때문에 저까지 홀콤 양에 대해 거의 마님만큼이나 걱정스럽고 불안해졌습니다."], ["I presumed to ask her to write me a line, if all went well in London.", "저는 런던에서 모든 일이 잘되거든 한 줄 적어 보내 주십사고 감히 청을 드렸습니다."], ["She answered, “Most willingly, Mrs.", "마님은 “기꺼이 그러지요, 마이컬슨 부인”"], ["Michelson.”", "하고 대답하셨습니다."]], [["“We all have our crosses to bear, my lady,”", "“우리 모두에게는 저마다 져야 할 십자가가 있는 법입니다, 마님.”"], ["I said, seeing her silent and thoughtful, after she had promised to write.", "편지를 쓰겠노라 약속하신 뒤 마님이 잠자코 생각에 잠기신 것을 보고 제가 말씀드렸습니다."]], [["She made no reply—she seemed to be too much wrapped up in her own thoughts to attend to me.", "마님은 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다—당신 생각에 너무 깊이 잠기시어 제 말에 마음 쓸 겨를이 없으신 듯했습니다."]], [["“I fear your ladyship rested badly last night,”", "“마님께서 간밤에 편히 쉬지 못하신 듯하여 염려됩니다.”"], ["I remarked, after waiting a little.", "잠시 기다렸다가 제가 여쭈었습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["she said, “I was terribly disturbed by dreams.”", "마님께서 말씀하셨습니다. “꿈 때문에 몹시 시달렸어요.”"]], [["“Indeed, my lady?”", "“저런, 마님?”"], ["I thought she was going to tell me her dreams, but no, when she spoke next it was only to ask a question.", "저는 마님이 꿈 이야기를 하시려나 보다 생각했습니다만 그렇지 않았고, 다음에 입을 여셨을 때는 그저 질문을 하나 하셨을 뿐입니다."]], [["“You posted the letter to Mrs. Vesey with your own hands?”", "“베지 부인께 보내는 편지를 손수 부치셨죠?”"]], [["“Yes, my lady.”", "“네, 마님.”"]], [["“Did Sir Percival say, yesterday, that Count Fosco was to meet me at the terminus in London?”", "“퍼시벌 경이 어제, 포스코 백작이 런던 종착역으로 저를 마중 나온다고 하셨던가요?”"]], [["“He did, my lady.”", "“그리 말씀하셨습니다, 마님.”"]], [["She sighed heavily when I answered that last question, and said no more.", "그 마지막 물음에 제가 대답하자 마님은 무겁게 한숨을 쉬시고는 더 말씀이 없으셨습니다."]], [["We arrived at the station, with hardly two minutes to spare.", "우리는 겨우 이 분 남짓 남기고 역에 닿았습니다."], ["The gardener (who had driven us) managed about the luggage, while I took the ticket.", "(마차를 몰아 준) 정원사가 짐을 맡았고 저는 표를 샀습니다."], ["The whistle of the train was sounding when I joined her ladyship on the platform.", "승강장에서 마님과 합류했을 때는 기차의 기적 소리가 울리고 있었습니다."], ["She looked very strangely, and pressed her hand over her heart, as if some sudden pain or fright had overcome her at that moment.", "마님은 아주 이상한 얼굴빛으로, 그 순간 갑작스러운 통증이나 두려움에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯 손으로 가슴을 누르고 계셨습니다."]], [["“I wish you were going with me!”", "“부인이 저와 함께 가 주시면 좋겠어요!”"], ["she said, catching eagerly at my arm when I gave her the ticket.", "제가 표를 건네 드리자 마님께서 제 팔을 다급히 붙드시며 말씀하셨습니다."]], [["If there had been time, if I had felt the day before as I felt then, I would have made my arrangements to accompany her, even though the doing so had obliged me to give Sir Percival warning on the spot.", "시간이 있었더라면, 그 전날 제가 그때와 같은 심정이었더라면, 저는 그 자리에서 퍼시벌 경께 일을 그만두겠다고 통고하는 한이 있더라도 마님을 모시고 갈 채비를 했을 것입니다."], ["As it was, her wishes, expressed at the last moment only, were expressed too late for me to comply with them.", "그러나 사정이 그러하여, 마지막 순간에야 밝히신 마님의 바람은 제가 따르기에는 너무 늦게 밝혀진 것이었습니다."], ["She seemed to understand this herself before I could explain it, and did not repeat her desire to have me for a travelling companion.", "마님은 제가 설명해 드리기도 전에 스스로 그것을 헤아리신 듯, 저를 길동무로 삼고 싶다는 말씀을 되풀이하지 않으셨습니다."], ["The train drew up at the platform.", "기차가 승강장에 와서 섰습니다."], ["She gave the gardener a present for his children, and took my hand, in her simple hearty manner, before she got into the carriage.", "마님은 정원사에게 그 아이들에게 줄 선물을 건네시고, 객차에 오르시기 전에 늘 그러시듯 소탈하고 다정한 태도로 제 손을 잡으셨습니다."]], [["“You have been very kind to me and to my sister,”", "“부인은 저와 제 언니에게 참으로 친절하셨어요.”"], ["she said—“kind when we were both friendless.", "마님께서 말씀하셨습니다—“우리 둘 다 의지할 데 없을 때 베풀어 주신 친절이었지요."], ["I shall remember you gratefully, as long as I live to remember any one.", "제가 살아서 누군가를 기억하는 한, 부인을 고마운 마음으로 기억할 거예요."], ["Good-bye—and God bless you!”", "안녕히 계세요—하느님의 축복이 있기를!”"]], [["She spoke those words with a tone and a look which brought the tears into my eyes—she spoke them as if she was bidding me farewell for ever.", "마님께서 그 말씀을 하신 어조와 눈빛에 제 눈에는 눈물이 고였습니다—마치 제게 영영 작별을 고하시는 듯한 어조요 눈빛이었습니다."]], [["“Good-bye, my lady,”", "“안녕히 가십시오, 마님.” 저는 마님을 객차에 모셔 태우고 기운을 북돋아 드리려 애쓰며 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I said, putting her into the carriage, and trying to cheer her; “good-bye, for the present only; good-bye, with my best and kindest wishes for happier times.”", "“안녕히 가십시오, 다만 잠시 동안의 작별입니다. 더 행복한 날들이 오기를 바라는 저의 가장 크고 따뜻한 마음과 함께, 안녕히 가십시오.”"]], [["She shook her head, and shuddered as she settled herself in the carriage.", "마님은 고개를 저으시고, 객차 안에 자리를 잡으시며 몸을 떠셨습니다."], ["The guard closed the door.", "차장이 문을 닫았습니다."], ["“Do you believe in dreams?”", "“꿈을 믿으세요?”"], ["she whispered to me at the window.", "마님이 창가에서 제게 속삭이셨습니다."], ["“My dreams, last night, were dreams I have never had before.", "“간밤에 꾼 꿈은 여태 한 번도 꾼 적 없는 꿈이었어요."], ["The terror of them is hanging over me still.”", "그 무서움이 아직도 제 위에 드리워 있어요.”"], ["The whistle sounded before I could answer, and the train moved.", "제가 대답하기도 전에 기적이 울리고 기차가 움직였습니다."], ["Her pale quiet face looked at me for the last time—looked sorrowfully and solemnly from the window.", "창백하고 고요한 마님의 얼굴이 마지막으로 저를 바라보았습니다—창문 너머로 슬프고도 엄숙하게 바라보았습니다."], ["She waved her hand, and I saw her no more.", "마님이 손을 흔드셨고, 저는 그것으로 다시는 마님을 뵙지 못했습니다."]], [["Towards five o’clock on the afternoon of that same day, having a little time to myself in the midst of the household duties which now pressed upon me, I sat down alone in my own room, to try and compose my mind with the volume of my husband’s Sermons.", "바로 그날 오후 다섯 시 무렵, 이제 제게 몰려든 집안일 가운데서 잠시 짬이 나기에 저는 제 방에 홀로 앉아 남편의 설교집으로 마음을 가라앉혀 보려 하였습니다."], ["For the first time in my life I found my attention wandering over those pious and cheering words.", "난생처음으로 저는 그 경건하고 힘이 되는 말씀들 위에서 정신이 딴 데로 흩어지는 것을 느꼈습니다."], ["Concluding that Lady Glyde’s departure must have disturbed me far more seriously than I had myself supposed, I put the book aside, and went out to take a turn in the garden.", "글라이드 부인의 출발이 제가 스스로 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심하게 저를 어지럽혔던 모양이라고 여기며, 저는 책을 밀어 두고 정원을 한 바퀴 거닐러 나갔습니다."], ["Sir Percival had not yet returned, to my knowledge, so I could feel no hesitation about showing myself in the grounds.", "제가 아는 한 퍼시벌 경은 아직 돌아오지 않으셨으므로 뜰에 모습을 드러내는 데 거리낄 것이 없었습니다."]], [["On turning the corner of the house, and gaining a view of the garden, I was startled by seeing a stranger walking in it.", "집 모퉁이를 돌아 정원이 눈에 들어왔을 때, 나는 낯선 사람이 그 안을 거닐고 있는 것을 보고 흠칫 놀랐습니다."], ["The stranger was a woman—she was lounging along the path with her back to me, and was gathering the flowers.", "낯선 사람은 여자였습니다. 내게 등을 돌린 채 오솔길을 따라 어슬렁거리며 꽃을 꺾고 있었습니다."]], [["As I approached she heard me, and turned round.", "내가 다가가자 그 여자는 인기척을 듣고 몸을 돌렸습니다."]], [["My blood curdled in my veins.", "피가 얼어붙는 것 같았습니다."], ["The strange woman in the garden was Mrs. Rubelle!", "정원에 있던 그 낯선 여자는 루벨 부인이었습니다!"]], [["I could neither move nor speak.", "나는 움직일 수도 말할 수도 없었습니다."], ["She came up to me, as composedly as ever, with her flowers in her hand.", "그 여자는 손에 꽃을 든 채 여느 때와 다름없이 태연한 걸음으로 내게 다가왔습니다."]], [["“What is the matter, ma’am?”", "“무슨 일이신가요, 마님?”"], ["she said quietly.", "그 여자가 조용히 말했습니다."]], [["“You here!”", "“당신이 여기에!”"], ["I gasped out.", "나는 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했습니다."], ["“Not gone to London!", "“런던에 가지 않았군요!"], ["Not gone to Cumberland!”", "컴벌랜드에도 가지 않았고요!”"]], [["Mrs. Rubelle smelt at her flowers with a smile of malicious pity.", "루벨 부인은 짓궂은 연민의 미소를 띤 채 손에 든 꽃 냄새를 맡았습니다."]], [["“Certainly not,”", "“물론 가지 않았지요.”"], ["she said.", "그 여자가 말했습니다."], ["“I have never left Blackwater Park.”", "“저는 블랙워터 파크를 한 번도 떠난 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["I summoned breath enough and courage enough for another question.", "나는 또 하나의 질문을 던질 만한 숨과 용기를 겨우 끌어모았습니다."]], [["“Where is Miss Halcombe?”", "“홀콤 양은 어디 계십니까?”"]], [["Mrs. Rubelle fairly laughed at me this time, and replied in these words—", "이번에는 루벨 부인이 대놓고 나를 비웃으며 이런 말로 대답했습니다—"]], [["“Miss Halcombe, ma’am, has not left Blackwater Park either.”", "“홀콤 양도 블랙워터 파크를 떠나지 않으셨습니다, 마님.”"]], [["When I heard that astounding answer, all my thoughts were startled back on the instant to my parting with Lady Glyde.", "그 어처구니없는 대답을 들은 순간, 내 모든 생각은 글라이드 부인과 헤어지던 장면으로 화들짝 되돌아갔습니다."], ["I can hardly say I reproached myself, but at that moment I think I would have given many a year’s hard savings to have known four hours earlier what I knew now.", "스스로를 나무랐다고까지는 말하기 어렵지만, 그 순간 나는 지금 알게 된 것을 네 시간만 일찍 알 수 있었더라면 여러 해 애써 모은 돈이라도 기꺼이 내놓았으리라고 생각합니다."]], [["Mrs. Rubelle waited, quietly arranging her nosegay, as if she expected me to say something.", "루벨 부인은 내가 무슨 말이라도 하기를 기다리는 듯, 조용히 꽃다발을 매만지며 서 있었습니다."]], [["I could say nothing.", "나는 아무 말도 할 수 없었습니다."], ["I thought of Lady Glyde’s worn-out energies and weakly health, and I trembled for the time when the shock of the discovery that I had made would fall on her.", "글라이드 부인의 소진된 기력과 쇠약한 건강을 떠올리며, 내가 알아낸 이 사실의 충격이 그분에게 닥칠 때를 생각하니 몸이 떨렸습니다."], ["For a minute or more my fears for the poor ladies silenced me. At the end of that time Mrs.", "한참 동안 그 딱한 두 분에 대한 걱정이 내 입을 막았습니다."], ["Rubelle looked up sideways from her flowers, and said, “Here is Sir Percival, ma’am, returned from his ride.”", "그러다 루벨 부인이 꽃에서 눈을 들어 곁눈질하며 말했습니다. “퍼시벌 경께서 승마를 마치고 돌아오시는군요, 마님.”"]], [["I saw him as soon as she did.", "나도 그 여자와 동시에 그분을 보았습니다."], ["He came towards us, slashing viciously at the flowers with his riding-whip.", "그분은 승마용 채찍으로 꽃들을 사납게 후려치면서 우리 쪽으로 다가왔습니다."], ["When he was near enough to see my face he stopped, struck at his boot with the whip, and burst out laughing, so harshly and so violently that the birds flew away, startled, from the tree by which he stood.", "내 얼굴이 보일 만큼 가까워지자 그분은 걸음을 멈추고 채찍으로 자기 장화를 친 다음, 어찌나 거칠고 요란하게 웃음을 터뜨렸던지 곁에 선 나무에서 새들이 놀라 날아가 버렸습니다."]], [["“Well, Mrs. Michelson,”", "“허, 마이컬슨 부인,”"], ["he said, “you have found it out at last, have you?”", "그분이 말했습니다. “드디어 알아냈군그래, 그렇지?”"]], [["I made no reply.", "나는 대답하지 않았습니다."], ["He turned to Mrs. Rubelle.", "그분은 루벨 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰습니다."]], [["“When did you show yourself in the garden?”", "“언제부터 정원에 모습을 드러냈나?”"]], [["“I showed myself about half an hour ago, sir.", "“반 시간쯤 전부터입니다, 나리."], ["You said I might take my liberty again as soon as Lady Glyde had gone away to London.”", "글라이드 부인께서 런던으로 떠나시는 대로 다시 자유롭게 다녀도 좋다고 하셨지요.”"]], [["“Quite right.", "“옳은 말이야."], ["I don’t blame you—I only asked the question.”", "탓하는 게 아니라 그저 물어본 것뿐이니.”"], ["He waited a moment, and then addressed himself once more to me.", "그분은 잠시 뜸을 들이더니 다시 내게 말을 걸었습니다."], ["“You can’t believe it, can you?”", "“못 믿겠지, 안 그런가?”"], ["he said mockingly.", "그분이 비웃듯 말했습니다."], ["“Here!", "“자!"], ["come along and see for yourself.”", "따라와서 직접 눈으로 보게.”"]], [["He led the way round to the front of the house.", "그분은 앞장서서 집 정면 쪽으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["I followed him, and Mrs. Rubelle followed me.", "나는 그분을 따라갔고, 루벨 부인이 내 뒤를 따랐습니다."], ["After passing through the iron gates he stopped, and pointed with his whip to the disused middle wing of the building.", "쇠문을 지나자 그분은 걸음을 멈추고 채찍으로 건물 가운데의 폐쇄된 별채를 가리켰습니다."]], [["“There!” he said.", "“저기!”"], ["“Look up at the first floor.", "그분이 말했습니다. “이층을 올려다봐."], ["You know the old Elizabethan bedrooms?", "그 옛 엘리자베스 시대 침실들을 알지?"], ["Miss Halcombe is snug and safe in one of the best of them at this moment. Take her in, Mrs.", "홀콤 양은 지금 그중에서도 제일 좋은 방에 편안하고 안전하게 있어."], ["Rubelle (you have got your key?); take Mrs.", "데리고 들어가게, 루벨 부인. 열쇠는 가지고 있지?"], ["Michelson in, and let her own eyes satisfy her that there is no deception this time.”", "마이컬슨 부인을 안으로 데려가서, 이번만은 속임수가 없다는 걸 제 눈으로 확인하게 해 주라고.”"]], [["The tone in which he spoke to me, and the minute or two that had passed since we left the garden, helped me to recover my spirits a little.", "그분이 내게 말한 말투와, 정원을 떠난 뒤 흐른 일이 분의 시간이 내 정신을 조금이나마 되찾게 해 주었습니다."], ["What I might have done at this critical moment, if all my life had been passed in service, I cannot say.", "내 평생이 남의집살이로만 채워졌더라면 이 중대한 순간에 내가 어떻게 처신했을지 알 수 없습니다."], ["As it was, possessing the feelings, the principles, and the bringing up of a lady, I could not hesitate about the right course to pursue.", "그러나 나는 숙녀로서의 감정과 신조와 교양을 지닌 사람이었기에, 마땅히 취해야 할 길을 두고 망설일 수 없었습니다."], ["My duty to myself, and my duty to Lady Glyde, alike forbade me to remain in the employment of a man who had shamefully deceived us both by a series of atrocious falsehoods.", "나 자신에 대한 의무도, 글라이드 부인에 대한 의무도, 흉악한 거짓말을 거듭하여 우리 두 사람을 부끄럽게도 속인 사람 밑에 더 머무는 것을 한결같이 금하고 있었습니다."]], [["“I must beg permission, Sir Percival, to speak a few words to you in private,” I said.", "“퍼시벌 경, 잠시 은밀히 몇 말씀 여쭐 것을 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.”"], ["“Having done so, I shall be ready to proceed with this person to Miss Halcombe’s room.”", "내가 말했습니다. “말씀을 드리고 나면 이 사람과 함께 홀콤 양의 방으로 갈 준비가 되겠습니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Rubelle, whom I had indicated by a slight turn of my head, insolently sniffed at her nosegay and walked away, with great deliberation, towards the house door.", "내가 고개를 살짝 돌려 가리킨 루벨 부인은 무례하게 꽃다발 냄새를 킁 맡더니, 아주 느긋한 걸음으로 현관 쪽으로 걸어갔습니다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그래,”"], ["said Sir Percival sharply, “what is it now?”", "퍼시벌 경이 퉁명스럽게 말했습니다. “이번엔 또 무슨 일인가?”"]], [["“I wish to mention, sir, that I am desirous of resigning the situation I now hold at Blackwater Park.”", "“나리, 제가 지금 블랙워터 파크에서 맡고 있는 자리를 그만두고자 한다는 말씀을 드리려 합니다.”"], ["That was literally how I put it.", "나는 글자 그대로 이렇게 말했습니다."], ["I was resolved that the first words spoken in his presence should be words which expressed my intention to leave his service.", "그분 앞에서 꺼내는 첫마디는 그분의 일자리를 떠나겠다는 뜻을 담은 말이어야 한다고 나는 마음을 굳히고 있었습니다."]], [["He eyed me with one of his blackest looks, and thrust his hands savagely into the pockets of his riding-coat.", "그분은 더없이 험악한 눈빛으로 나를 노려보며 승마복 주머니에 두 손을 거칠게 찔러 넣었습니다."]], [["“Why?”", "“어째서?”"], ["he said; “why, I should like to know?”", "그분이 말했습니다. “어째서인지 좀 알아야겠는데?”"]], [["“It is not for me, Sir Percival, to express an opinion on what has taken place in this house.", "“퍼시벌 경, 이 집에서 일어난 일에 대해 제가 의견을 말씀드릴 처지는 아닙니다."], ["I desire to give no offence.", "저는 심기를 거스를 뜻이 없습니다."], ["I merely wish to say that I do not feel it consistent with my duty to Lady Glyde and to myself to remain any longer in your service.”", "다만 글라이드 부인과 저 자신에 대한 의무에 비추어, 더 이상 나리를 모시는 것이 옳지 않다고 여겨진다는 말씀을 드리고 싶을 뿐입니다.”"]], [["“Is it consistent with your duty to me to stand there, casting suspicion on me to my face?”", "“거기 서서 내 면전에 의심을 던지는 것은 나에 대한 자네 의무에 맞는 일인가?”"], ["he broke out in his most violent manner.", "그분은 더없이 사나운 태도로 쏘아붙였습니다."], ["“I see what you’re driving at.", "“자네 속셈이 뭔지 알겠어."], ["You have taken your own mean, underhand view of an innocent deception practised on Lady Glyde for her own good.", "글라이드 부인 자신을 위해 베푼 죄 없는 속임수를 두고 자네 혼자 비열하고 음흉한 해석을 내린 게지."], ["It was essential to her health that she should have a change of air immediately, and you know as well as I do she would never have gone away if she had been told Miss Halcombe was still left here.", "그분 건강에는 당장 공기를 바꾸는 것이 무엇보다 필요했고, 홀콤 양이 아직 여기 남아 있다는 말을 들었다면 그분이 결코 떠나지 않았으리라는 건 자네도 나만큼 잘 알잖나."], ["She has been deceived in her own interests—and I don’t care who knows it.", "그분은 그분 자신의 이익을 위해 속은 거야. 누가 알든 나는 상관없어."], ["Go, if you like—there are plenty of housekeepers as good as you to be had for the asking.", "가고 싶으면 가게. 자네만 한 가정부야 부르기만 하면 얼마든지 구할 수 있으니."], ["Go when you please—but take care how you spread scandals about me and my affairs when you’re out of my service.", "언제든 마음대로 가라고. 다만 내 밑을 떠난 뒤에 나와 내 일에 대해 소문을 퍼뜨릴 때는 조심하는 게 좋을 거야."], ["Tell the truth, and nothing but the truth, or it will be the worse for you!", "진실을, 오직 진실만을 말하게. 안 그러면 자네에게 더 나쁠 테니!"], ["See Miss Halcombe for yourself—see if she hasn’t been as well taken care of in one part of the house as in the other.", "홀콤 양을 직접 보게. 집의 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 똑같이 잘 보살핌을 받았는지 아닌지 말이야."], ["Remember the doctor’s own orders that Lady Glyde was to have a change of air at the earliest possible opportunity.", "글라이드 부인은 되도록 빨리 공기를 바꾸어야 한다던 의사 자신의 지시도 잊지 말고."], ["Bear all that well in mind, and then say anything against me and my proceedings if you dare!”", "그것들을 잘 새겨 두고, 그러고도 배짱이 있거든 나와 내 처사에 대해 무슨 말이든 해 보라고!”"]], [["He poured out these words fiercely, all in a breath, walking backwards and forwards, and striking about him in the air with his whip.", "그분은 이 말들을 사납게, 단숨에 쏟아 내면서 앞뒤로 오가며 허공에 채찍을 휘둘러 댔습니다."]], [["Nothing that he said or did shook my opinion of the disgraceful series of falsehoods that he had told in my presence the day before, or of the cruel deception by which he had separated Lady Glyde from her sister, and had sent her uselessly to London, when she was half distracted with anxiety on Miss Halcombe’s account.", "그분이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든, 그 전날 내 앞에서 늘어놓은 부끄러운 거짓말들에 대한, 또 글라이드 부인을 언니와 떼어 놓고 홀콤 양 걱정으로 반쯤 넋이 나간 그분을 부질없이 런던으로 보낸 그 잔인한 속임수에 대한 내 판단은 조금도 흔들리지 않았습니다."], ["I naturally kept these thoughts to myself, and said nothing more to irritate him; but I was not the less resolved to persist in my purpose.", "물론 나는 이런 생각을 마음에 담아 두고, 그분을 자극할 말은 더 하지 않았습니다. 그러나 내 뜻을 끝까지 밀고 나가겠다는 결심은 그만큼 더 굳었습니다."], ["A soft answer turneth away wrath, and I suppressed my own feelings accordingly when it was my turn to reply.", "부드러운 대답은 노여움을 물리치는 법이니, 내가 대답할 차례가 되었을 때 나는 그에 따라 내 감정을 눌렀습니다."]], [["“While I am in your service, Sir Percival,”", "“퍼시벌 경, 제가 나리를 모시는 동안에는,”"], ["I said, “I hope I know my duty well enough not to inquire into your motives.", "내가 말했습니다. “나리의 뜻을 캐묻지 않을 만큼의 도리는 안다고 생각합니다."], ["When I am out of your service, I hope I know my own place well enough not to speak of matters which don’t concern me—”", "나리를 모시지 않게 된 뒤에는, 저와 상관없는 일을 입에 올리지 않을 만큼 제 분수는 안다고 생각합니다—”"]], [["“When do you want to go?”", "“언제 나가고 싶은가?”"], ["he asked, interrupting me without ceremony.", "그분이 예의도 없이 내 말을 끊으며 물었습니다."], ["“Don’t suppose I am anxious to keep you—don’t suppose I care about your leaving the house.", "“내가 자네를 붙들고 싶어 한다고는 생각지 말게. 자네가 이 집을 떠나든 말든 내가 마음 쓴다고도 생각지 말고."], ["I am perfectly fair and open in this matter, from first to last.", "나는 이 일에 처음부터 끝까지 아주 공정하고 떳떳하니까."], ["When do you want to go?”", "언제 나가고 싶으냐고?”"]], [["“I should wish to leave at your earliest convenience, Sir Percival.”", "“나리께서 편하신 대로 되도록 이른 때에 떠났으면 합니다, 퍼시벌 경.”"]], [["“My convenience has nothing to do with it.", "“내가 편하고 말고는 이 일과 상관없어."], ["I shall be out of the house for good and all to-morrow morning, and I can settle your accounts to-night.", "나는 내일 아침이면 이 집을 영영 떠날 테고, 자네 셈은 오늘 밤에 치러 줄 수 있어."], ["If you want to study anybody’s convenience, it had better be Miss Halcombe’s.", "누구 사정을 헤아리겠다면 차라리 홀콤 양 사정을 헤아리는 게 낫겠지."], ["Mrs. Rubelle’s time is up to-day, and she has reasons for wishing to be in London to-night.", "루벨 부인은 오늘로 기한이 다 됐고, 오늘 밤 안에 런던에 가 있어야 할 사정이 있다더군."], ["If you go at once, Miss Halcombe won’t have a soul left here to look after her.”", "자네가 당장 나가 버리면 홀콤 양을 돌볼 사람이 이 집에 하나도 남지 않을 거야.”"]], [["I hope it is unnecessary for me to say that I was quite incapable of deserting Miss Halcombe in such an emergency as had now befallen Lady Glyde and herself. After first distinctly ascertaining from Sir Percival that Mrs.", "글라이드 부인과 홀콤 양에게 닥친 이런 위급한 상황에서 내가 홀콤 양을 저버릴 수는 도저히 없었다는 말은 굳이 하지 않아도 되리라 생각합니다."], ["Rubelle was certain to leave at once if I took her place, and after also obtaining permission to arrange for Mr. Dawson’s resuming his attendance on his patient, I willingly consented to remain at Blackwater Park until Miss Halcombe no longer required my services.", "내가 그 자리를 대신 맡으면 루벨 부인이 틀림없이 곧바로 떠난다는 것을 퍼시벌 경에게서 분명히 확인하고, 또 도슨 의사가 환자를 다시 진료하도록 주선해도 좋다는 허락까지 받은 뒤에, 나는 홀콤 양이 더는 내 도움을 필요로 하지 않을 때까지 블랙워터 파크에 남기로 기꺼이 승낙했습니다."], ["It was settled that I should give Sir Percival’s solicitor a week’s notice before I left, and that he was to undertake the necessary arrangements for appointing my successor.", "떠나기 일주일 전에 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사에게 통고하고, 후임을 정하는 데 필요한 절차는 그 변호사가 맡기로 정해졌습니다."], ["The matter was discussed in very few words.", "이야기는 아주 짧은 말로 끝났습니다."], ["At its conclusion Sir Percival abruptly turned on his heel, and left me free to join Mrs. Rubelle.", "이야기가 끝나자 퍼시벌 경은 불쑥 발길을 돌렸고, 나는 루벨 부인에게로 갈 수 있게 되었습니다."], ["That singular foreign person had been sitting composedly on the door-step all this time, waiting till I could follow her to Miss Halcombe’s room.", "그 유별난 외국 여자는 그동안 내내 현관 층계에 태연히 앉아, 내가 자기를 따라 홀콤 양의 방으로 갈 수 있게 되기를 기다리고 있었습니다."]], [["I had hardly walked half-way towards the house when Sir Percival, who had withdrawn in the opposite direction, suddenly stopped and called me back.", "내가 집 쪽으로 절반도 채 걸어가지 못했을 때, 반대 방향으로 물러가던 퍼시벌 경이 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 나를 다시 불렀습니다."]], [["“Why are you leaving my service?”", "“자네는 왜 내 밑을 떠나는 건가?”"], ["he asked.", "그분이 물었습니다."]], [["The question was so extraordinary, after what had just passed between us, that I hardly knew what to say in answer to it.", "방금 우리 사이에 오간 일을 생각하면 그 질문은 어찌나 뜻밖이었던지, 나는 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없었습니다."]], [["“Mind!", "“알아 두게!"], ["I don’t know why you are going,” he went on.", "나는 자네가 왜 나가는지 모르네.”"], ["“You must give a reason for leaving me, I suppose, when you get another situation.", "그분이 말을 이었습니다. “다른 자리를 구하려면 나를 떠나는 이유를 대야 할 것 아닌가."], ["What reason?", "무슨 이유로?"], ["The breaking up of the family?", "이 집안이 흩어지게 되어서?"], ["Is that it?”", "그거면 되겠나?”"]], [["“There can be no positive objection, Sir Percival, to that reason——”", "“퍼시벌 경, 그 이유라면 딱히 반대할 까닭이 없겠습니다——”"]], [["“Very well!", "“좋아!"], ["That’s all I want to know.", "내가 알고 싶은 건 그것뿐이야."], ["If people apply for your character, that’s your reason, stated by yourself.", "사람들이 자네 신원을 물어 오면 그게 자네가 스스로 밝힌 이유가 되는 거야."], ["You go in consequence of the breaking up of the family.”", "자네는 집안이 흩어지게 되어 나가는 거지.”"]], [["He turned away again before I could say another word, and walked out rapidly into the grounds.", "그분은 내가 한마디 더 하기도 전에 다시 몸을 돌려 빠른 걸음으로 뜰 쪽으로 나가 버렸습니다."], ["His manner was as strange as his language.", "그분의 태도는 그 말만큼이나 이상했습니다."], ["I acknowledge he alarmed me.", "솔직히 말해 나는 겁이 났습니다."]], [["Even the patience of Mrs. Rubelle was getting exhausted, when I joined her at the house door.", "내가 현관에서 루벨 부인과 만났을 때, 그 여자는 참을성마저 바닥이 난 모양이었습니다."]], [["“At last!”", "“이제야 오셨군요!”"], ["she said, with a shrug of her lean foreign shoulders.", "그 여자가 마르고 외국인다운 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했습니다."], ["She led the way into the inhabited side of the house, ascended the stairs, and opened with her key the door at the end of the passage, which communicated with the old Elizabethan rooms—a door never previously used, in my time, at Blackwater Park.", "그러고는 앞장서서 사람이 사는 쪽 집으로 들어가 층계를 올라간 다음, 복도 끝에서 옛 엘리자베스 시대 방들과 통하는 문을 자기 열쇠로 열었습니다. 내가 이 집에 있는 동안 한 번도 쓰인 적이 없는 문이었습니다."], ["The rooms themselves I knew well, having entered them myself on various occasions from the other side of the house.", "방들 자체는 나도 잘 알고 있었습니다. 집의 반대쪽에서 여러 차례 들어가 본 적이 있었으니까요."], ["Mrs. Rubelle stopped at the third door along the old gallery, handed me the key of it, with the key of the door of communication, and told me I should find Miss Halcombe in that room.", "루벨 부인은 그 낡은 회랑을 따라 세 번째 문 앞에서 걸음을 멈추더니, 그 방 열쇠와 통용문 열쇠를 내게 건네주며 홀콤 양이 그 방에 계실 것이라고 말했습니다."], ["Before I went in I thought it desirable to make her understand that her attendance had ceased.", "나는 안으로 들어가기 전에, 그 여자의 간병이 끝났다는 것을 분명히 알려 두는 편이 좋겠다고 생각했습니다."], ["Accordingly, I told her in plain words that the charge of the sick lady henceforth devolved entirely on myself.", "그래서 이제부터는 병드신 부인을 돌보는 일이 온전히 내 몫이 되었다고 딱 잘라 말했습니다."]], [["“I am glad to hear it, ma’am,”", "“그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다, 마님.”"], ["said Mrs. Rubelle.", "루벨 부인이 말했습니다."], ["“I want to go very much.”", "“저는 몹시 가고 싶었거든요.”"]], [["“Do you leave to-day?”", "“오늘 떠나는 겁니까?”"], ["I asked, to make sure of her.", "나는 확실히 해 두려고 물었습니다."]], [["“Now that you have taken charge, ma’am, I leave in half an hour’s time.", "“마님께서 맡으셨으니 저는 반 시간 뒤에 떠납니다."], ["Sir Percival has kindly placed at my disposition the gardener, and the chaise, whenever I want them.", "퍼시벌 경께서 친절하게도 필요할 때면 언제든 정원사와 이륜마차를 쓰도록 해 주셨지요."], ["I shall want them in half an hour’s time to go to the station.", "반 시간 뒤에 역으로 가는 데 쓸 참입니다."], ["I am packed up in anticipation already.", "짐은 미리 다 꾸려 두었습니다."], ["I wish you good-day, ma’am.”", "안녕히 계십시오, 마님.”"]], [["She dropped a brisk curtsey, and walked back along the gallery, humming a little tune, and keeping time to it cheerfully with the nosegay in her hand.", "그 여자는 날렵하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는, 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 손에 든 꽃다발로 흥겹게 박자를 맞추면서 회랑을 되짚어 걸어갔습니다."], ["I am sincerely thankful to say that was the last I saw of Mrs. Rubelle.", "그것이 내가 루벨 부인을 본 마지막이었다는 것을 진심으로 감사히 여깁니다."]], [["When I went into the room Miss Halcombe was asleep.", "내가 방에 들어갔을 때 홀콤 양은 잠들어 있었습니다."], ["I looked at her anxiously, as she lay in the dismal, high, old-fashioned bed.", "나는 음침하고 높다란 구식 침대에 누워 있는 그분을 걱정스레 바라보았습니다."], ["She was certainly not in any respect altered for the worse since I had seen her last.", "지난번에 뵈었을 때보다 어느 모로도 나빠지신 것 같지는 않았습니다."], ["She had not been neglected, I am bound to admit, in any way that I could perceive.", "내가 알아볼 수 있는 한 어떤 식으로도 방치되지는 않았다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["The room was dreary, and dusty, and dark, but the window (looking on a solitary court-yard at the back of the house) was opened to let in the fresh air, and all that could be done to make the place comfortable had been done.", "방은 스산하고 먼지가 앉았으며 어두웠지만, 집 뒤편의 인적 없는 안뜰을 향한 창문은 신선한 공기가 들어오도록 열려 있었고, 그곳을 편하게 만들기 위해 할 수 있는 일은 다 되어 있었습니다."], ["The whole cruelty of Sir Percival’s deception had fallen on poor Lady Glyde. The only ill-usage which either he or Mrs.", "퍼시벌 경의 속임수가 지닌 잔인함은 온전히 가엾은 글라이드 부인에게만 떨어졌던 것입니다."], ["Rubelle had inflicted on Miss Halcombe consisted, so far as I could see, in the first offence of hiding her away.", "그분이나 루벨 부인이 홀콤 양에게 저지른 유일한 학대는, 내가 보기에는 그분을 숨겨 놓았다는 처음의 잘못뿐이었습니다."]], [["I stole back, leaving the sick lady still peacefully asleep, to give the gardener instructions about bringing the doctor. I begged the man, after he had taken Mrs.", "나는 병드신 분이 여전히 평온하게 잠들어 있는 것을 두고 살며시 물러 나와, 정원사에게 의사를 모셔 오는 일에 대해 일렀습니다."], ["Rubelle to the station, to drive round by Mr. Dawson’s, and leave a message in my name, asking him to call and see me.", "나는 그 사람에게, 루벨 부인을 역에 데려다준 뒤 도슨 의사 댁에 들러 내 이름으로 말을 전하여 와서 나를 만나 달라고 청해 달라고 부탁했습니다."], ["I knew he would come on my account, and I knew he would remain when he found Count Fosco had left the house.", "나를 봐서라도 그분이 와 주시리라는 것을, 또 포스코 백작이 이 집을 떠났다는 것을 알면 계속 머물러 주시리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었습니다."]], [["In due course of time the gardener returned, and said that he had driven round by Mr. Dawson’s residence, after leaving Mrs.", "이윽고 정원사가 돌아와서, 루벨 부인을 역에 내려 준 뒤 도슨 의사 댁에 들렀다고 말했습니다."], ["Rubelle at the station. The doctor sent me word that he was poorly in health himself, but that he would call, if possible, the next morning.", "의사는 자기도 몸이 좋지 않지만 될 수 있으면 이튿날 아침에 들르겠다는 전갈을 보내 왔습니다."]], [["Having delivered his message the gardener was about to withdraw, but I stopped him to request that he would come back before dark, and sit up that night, in one of the empty bedrooms, so as to be within call in case I wanted him.", "전갈을 전한 정원사가 물러가려 하자 나는 그를 붙들고, 어두워지기 전에 돌아와 그날 밤은 빈 침실 하나에서 지새우며 내가 부르면 들릴 곳에 있어 달라고 청했습니다."], ["He understood readily enough my unwillingness to be left alone all night in the most desolate part of that desolate house, and we arranged that he should come in between eight and nine.", "그 황량한 집에서도 가장 황량한 곳에 밤새 홀로 남기가 꺼려진다는 내 마음을 그는 어렵지 않게 알아들었고, 우리는 그가 여덟 시에서 아홉 시 사이에 오기로 정했습니다."]], [["He came punctually, and I found cause to be thankful that I had adopted the precaution of calling him in.", "그는 시간을 어기지 않고 왔고, 나는 그를 부르는 조심을 해 둔 것을 다행으로 여길 까닭이 있었습니다."], ["Before midnight Sir Percival’s strange temper broke out in the most violent and most alarming manner, and if the gardener had not been on the spot to pacify him on the instant, I am afraid to think what might have happened.", "자정이 되기 전에 퍼시벌 경의 이상한 성미가 더없이 사납고 놀라운 모습으로 터져 나왔으니, 만일 정원사가 그 자리에서 곧바로 그분을 달래지 못했더라면 무슨 일이 일어났을지 생각하기도 두렵습니다."]], [["Almost all the afternoon and evening he had been walking about the house and grounds in an unsettled, excitable manner, having, in all probability, as I thought, taken an excessive quantity of wine at his solitary dinner.", "그날 오후와 저녁 내내 그분은 안절부절못하고 흥분한 모습으로 집 안과 뜰을 돌아다녔는데, 아마도 혼자 드신 저녁 식사에서 술을 지나치게 많이 드신 탓이었으리라고 나는 생각했습니다."], ["However that may be, I heard his voice calling loudly and angrily in the new wing of the house, as I was taking a turn backwards and forwards along the gallery the last thing at night.", "어쨌든 나는 밤늦게 회랑을 앞뒤로 오가고 있다가, 새 별채 쪽에서 그분이 크고 성난 목소리로 고함치는 것을 들었습니다."], ["The gardener immediately ran down to him, and I closed the door of communication, to keep the alarm, if possible, from reaching Miss Halcombe’s ears.", "정원사가 곧장 아래로 달려 내려갔고, 나는 그 소란이 홀콤 양의 귀에 들어가지 않도록 통용문을 닫았습니다."], ["It was full half an hour before the gardener came back.", "정원사가 돌아오기까지는 꼬박 반 시간이 걸렸습니다."], ["He declared that his master was quite out of his senses—not through the excitement of drink, as I had supposed, but through a kind of panic or frenzy of mind, for which it was impossible to account.", "그는 주인 나리가 완전히 제정신이 아니라고 했습니다. 내가 짐작한 대로 술기운 때문이 아니라, 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 일종의 공황이랄까 광란 때문이라는 것이었습니다."], ["He had found Sir Percival walking backwards and forwards by himself in the hall, swearing, with every appearance of the most violent passion, that he would not stop another minute alone in such a dungeon as his own house, and that he would take the first stage of his journey immediately in the middle of the night.", "그가 갔을 때 퍼시벌 경은 홀 안을 혼자 앞뒤로 오가며, 제 집 같은 감옥에 단 일 분도 더 혼자 머물지 않겠다고, 한밤중이라도 당장 여정의 첫 구간을 떠나겠다고 더없이 격렬한 기색으로 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었습니다."], ["The gardener, on approaching him, had been hunted out, with oaths and threats, to get the horse and chaise ready instantly.", "정원사가 다가가자 그분은 욕설과 위협으로 그를 몰아세워 당장 말과 이륜마차를 준비하게 했습니다."], ["In a quarter of an hour Sir Percival had joined him in the yard, had jumped into the chaise, and, lashing the horse into a gallop, had driven himself away, with his face as pale as ashes in the moonlight.", "십오 분 만에 퍼시벌 경은 마당으로 나와 마차에 뛰어올랐고, 말에 채찍질을 하여 질주시키며 달빛 아래 잿빛처럼 창백한 얼굴로 손수 마차를 몰아 떠나갔습니다."], ["The gardener had heard him shouting and cursing at the lodge-keeper to get up and open the gate—had heard the wheels roll furiously on again in the still night, when the gate was unlocked—and knew no more.", "정원사는 그분이 문지기에게 일어나 문을 열라고 고함치며 저주를 퍼붓는 소리를 들었고, 문이 열리자 고요한 밤 속으로 바퀴 소리가 다시 미친 듯이 굴러가는 것을 들었을 뿐, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 알지 못했습니다."]], [["The next day, or a day or two after, I forget which, the chaise was brought back from Knowlesbury, our nearest town, by the ostler at the old inn.", "이튿날인지 하루 이틀 뒤인지 기억이 나지 않지만, 가장 가까운 읍인 놀즈베리에서 그곳 오래된 여관의 마부가 이륜마차를 돌려보내 왔습니다."], ["Sir Percival had stopped there, and had afterwards left by the train—for what destination the man could not tell.", "퍼시벌 경은 거기에 들렀다가 기차로 떠났다는데, 어디로 갔는지는 그 사람도 알지 못했습니다."], ["I never received any further information, either from himself or from any one else, of Sir Percival’s proceedings, and I am not even aware, at this moment, whether he is in England or out of it.", "그 뒤로 나는 퍼시벌 경의 행적에 대해 그분에게서든 다른 누구에게서든 아무런 소식도 듣지 못했고, 지금 이 순간에도 그분이 영국에 계신지 아닌지조차 알지 못합니다."], ["He and I have not met since he drove away like an escaped criminal from his own house, and it is my fervent hope and prayer that we may never meet again.", "그분이 마치 달아나는 죄인처럼 제 집에서 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤로 우리는 만난 적이 없으며, 다시는 만나는 일이 없기를 나는 간절히 바라고 기도합니다."]], [["My own part of this sad family story is now drawing to an end.", "이 슬픈 집안 이야기에서 내가 맡은 몫도 이제 끝에 다다르고 있습니다."]], [["I have been informed that the particulars of Miss Halcombe’s waking, and of what passed between us when she found me sitting by her bedside, are not material to the purpose which is to be answered by the present narrative.", "홀콤 양이 깨어난 정황이며, 내가 침대 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 그분이 보았을 때 우리 사이에 오간 이야기는 이 기록이 이루려는 목적에 그리 중요하지 않다는 말을 나는 들었습니다."], ["It will be sufficient for me to say in this place, that she was not herself conscious of the means adopted to remove her from the inhabited to the uninhabited part of the house.", "여기서는 다만 이 말만 해 두면 충분하겠습니다. 그분 자신은 사람이 사는 쪽에서 사람이 살지 않는 쪽으로 옮겨진 방법을 의식하지 못했습니다."], ["She was in a deep sleep at the time, whether naturally or artificially produced she could not say.", "그때 그분은 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 그것이 저절로 든 잠이었는지 약으로 만들어진 잠이었는지는 말할 수 없다고 했습니다."], ["In my absence at Torquay, and in the absence of all the resident servants except Margaret Porcher (who was perpetually eating, drinking, or sleeping, when she was not at work), the secret transfer of Miss Halcombe from one part of the house to the other was no doubt easily performed.", "내가 토키에 가 있고, 마거릿 포처를 뺀 하인들이 모두 자리를 비운 사이에 홀콤 양을 집의 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 몰래 옮기는 일은 틀림없이 손쉬웠을 것입니다. 그 마거릿 포처라는 여자는 일하지 않을 때면 늘 먹거나 마시거나 자고 있는 사람이었지요."], ["Mrs. Rubelle (as I discovered for myself, in looking about the room) had provisions, and all other necessaries, together with the means of heating water, broth, and so on, without kindling a fire, placed at her disposal during the few days of her imprisonment with the sick lady.", "루벨 부인에게는, 내가 그 방을 둘러보다가 스스로 알아낸 바로는, 병드신 분과 함께 갇혀 지낸 며칠 동안 먹을 것과 그 밖의 온갖 필요한 물건이며, 불을 피우지 않고도 물이나 고깃국 따위를 데울 수 있는 도구까지 마련되어 있었습니다."], ["She had declined to answer the questions which Miss Halcombe naturally put, but had not, in other respects, treated her with unkindness or neglect.", "그 여자는 홀콤 양이 당연히 던졌을 질문에는 대답하기를 거부했지만, 다른 점에서는 그분을 매정하게 대하거나 소홀히 하지 않았습니다."], ["The disgrace of lending herself to a vile deception is the only disgrace with which I can conscientiously charge Mrs. Rubelle.", "비열한 속임수에 몸을 빌려주었다는 것, 그것만이 내가 양심에 따라 루벨 부인에게 물을 수 있는 유일한 허물입니다."]], [["I need write no particulars (and I am relieved to know it) of the effect produced on Miss Halcombe by the news of Lady Glyde’s departure, or by the far more melancholy tidings which reached us only too soon afterwards at Blackwater Park.", "글라이드 부인이 떠났다는 소식이, 또 그 뒤 너무나 빨리 블랙워터 파크에 닿은 훨씬 더 슬픈 기별이 홀콤 양에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 자세히 적지 않아도 된다고 하니, 그 사실을 알게 되어 마음이 놓입니다."], ["In both cases I prepared her mind beforehand as gently and as carefully as possible, having the doctor’s advice to guide me, in the last case only, through Mr.", "두 경우 모두 나는 될 수 있는 대로 부드럽고 조심스럽게 그분의 마음을 미리 준비시켰는데, 의사의 조언을 받아 그리한 것은 나중 경우뿐이었습니다."], ["Dawson’s being too unwell to come to the house for some days after I had sent for him.", "내가 사람을 보낸 뒤로도 도슨 의사가 며칠 동안 몸이 좋지 않아 이 집에 오지 못했기 때문입니다."], ["It was a sad time, a time which it afflicts me to think of or to write of now.", "슬픈 시절이었고, 지금 떠올리거나 적기만 해도 마음이 아픈 시절이었습니다."], ["The precious blessings of religious consolation which I endeavoured to convey were long in reaching Miss Halcombe’s heart, but I hope and believe they came home to her at last.", "내가 전하려 애쓴 신앙의 귀한 위로가 홀콤 양의 마음에 닿기까지는 오래 걸렸지만, 마침내는 그분의 마음에 가닿았으리라고 나는 바라고 또 믿습니다."], ["I never left her till her strength was restored.", "나는 그분의 기력이 회복될 때까지 곁을 떠나지 않았습니다."], ["The train which took me away from that miserable house was the train which took her away also.", "나를 그 비참한 집에서 데려다준 기차는 그분을 데려다준 기차이기도 했습니다."], ["We parted very mournfully in London.", "우리는 런던에서 몹시 서글프게 헤어졌습니다."], ["I remained with a relative at Islington, and she went on to Mr. Fairlie’s house in Cumberland.", "나는 이즐링턴에 있는 친척 집에 머물렀고, 그분은 컴벌랜드의 페얼리 씨 댁으로 갔습니다."]], [["I have only a few lines more to write before I close this painful statement.", "이 괴로운 진술을 마치기까지 몇 줄만 더 쓰면 됩니다."], ["They are dictated by a sense of duty.", "그것은 의무감에서 나온 말입니다."]], [["In the first place, I wish to record my own personal conviction that no blame whatever, in connection with the events which I have now related, attaches to Count Fosco.", "첫째로, 지금까지 이야기한 일들과 관련하여 포스코 백작에게는 어떤 허물도 없다는 것이 내 개인적인 확신임을 기록해 두고 싶습니다."], ["I am informed that a dreadful suspicion has been raised, and that some very serious constructions are placed upon his lordship’s conduct.", "무서운 의심이 일고 있으며 백작의 처신을 두고 매우 심각한 해석들이 나오고 있다는 말을 나는 들었습니다."], ["My persuasion of the Count’s innocence remains, however, quite unshaken.", "그러나 백작의 결백에 대한 내 믿음은 조금도 흔들리지 않습니다."], ["If he assisted Sir Percival in sending me to Torquay, he assisted under a delusion, for which, as a foreigner and a stranger, he was not to blame. If he was concerned in bringing Mrs.", "그분이 나를 토키로 보내는 데 퍼시벌 경을 도왔다 하더라도, 그것은 그릇된 믿음 아래에서 도운 것이며, 외국인이자 손님인 그분에게 그 책임을 물을 수는 없습니다."], ["Rubelle to Blackwater Park, it was his misfortune and not his fault, when that foreign person was base enough to assist a deception planned and carried out by the master of the house.", "그분이 루벨 부인을 블랙워터 파크에 데려오는 일에 관계했다 하더라도, 그 외국 여자가 이 집 주인이 꾸미고 저지른 속임수를 도울 만큼 비열했던 것은 그분의 잘못이 아니라 불운이었습니다."], ["I protest, in the interests of morality, against blame being gratuitously and wantonly attached to the proceedings of the Count.", "나는 도덕을 위하여, 백작의 처신에 까닭 없이 함부로 허물이 씌워지는 데 항의합니다."]], [["In the second place, I desire to express my regret at my own inability to remember the precise day on which Lady Glyde left Blackwater Park for London.", "둘째로, 글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 향한 정확한 날짜를 기억해 내지 못하는 내 무능을 유감스럽게 여긴다는 뜻을 밝히고 싶습니다."], ["I am told that it is of the last importance to ascertain the exact date of that lamentable journey, and I have anxiously taxed my memory to recall it.", "그 애달픈 여행의 정확한 날짜를 확인하는 일이 무엇보다 중요하다는 말을 듣고, 나는 그것을 떠올리려고 애타게 기억을 쥐어짰습니다."], ["The effort has been in vain.", "그러나 헛수고였습니다."], ["I can only remember now that it was towards the latter part of July.", "지금 기억나는 것이라고는 그것이 칠월 하순 무렵이었다는 것뿐입니다."], ["We all know the difficulty, after a lapse of time, of fixing precisely on a past date unless it has been previously written down.", "미리 적어 두지 않은 지난 날짜를 세월이 흐른 뒤에 정확히 짚어 내기가 얼마나 어려운지는 우리 모두 알고 있습니다."], ["That difficulty is greatly increased in my case by the alarming and confusing events which took place about the period of Lady Glyde’s departure.", "게다가 내 경우에는 글라이드 부인이 떠날 무렵에 일어난 놀랍고 어지러운 일들 때문에 그 어려움이 한층 더 커졌습니다."], ["I heartily wish I had made a memorandum at the time.", "그때 적어 두지 못한 것이 진심으로 아쉽습니다."], ["I heartily wish my memory of the date was as vivid as my memory of that poor lady’s face, when it looked at me sorrowfully for the last time from the carriage window.", "마차 창문에서 마지막으로 나를 서글피 바라보던 그 가엾은 분의 얼굴이 내 기억에 남은 것만큼, 그 날짜에 대한 기억도 또렷했더라면 하고 진심으로 바랍니다."]], [["# THE STORY CONTINUED IN SEVERAL NARRATIVES", "# THE STORY CONTINUED IN SEVERAL NARRATIVES"]], [["1.", "1."], ["THE NARRATIVE OF HESTER PINHORN, COOK IN THE SERVICE OF COUNT FOSCO", "포스코 백작 댁에서 요리사로 일한 헤스터 핀혼의 진술"]], [["[Taken down from her own statement]", "[본인의 진술을 그대로 받아 적음]"]], [["I am sorry to say that I have never learnt to read or write.", "죄송한 말씀입니다만 저는 글을 읽을 줄도 쓸 줄도 배우지 못했습니다."], ["I have been a hard-working woman all my life, and have kept a good character.", "평생 몸으로 일해 온 여자이고, 품행만은 나무랄 데 없이 지켜 왔습니다."], ["I know that it is a sin and wickedness to say the thing which is not, and I will truly beware of doing so on this occasion.", "없는 일을 있다고 말하는 것은 죄요 사악한 짓이라는 걸 알고 있으니, 이번 일에서도 참말이지 그런 짓은 삼가겠습니다."], ["All that I know I will tell, and I humbly beg the gentleman who takes this down to put my language right as he goes on, and to make allowances for my being no scholar.", "제가 아는 것은 다 말씀드리겠습니다. 그리고 이걸 받아 적으시는 나리께서 적어 나가시면서 제 말투를 바로잡아 주시고, 제가 배운 것 없는 사람인 걸 너그러이 봐주시기를 삼가 부탁드립니다."]], [["In this last summer I happened to be out of place (through no fault of my own), and I heard of a situation as plain cook, at Number Five, Forest Road, St.", "지난여름에 저는 제 잘못이라고는 없이 마침 일자리가 없었는데, 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지에 보통 요리사 자리가 있다는 말을 들었습니다."], ["John’s Wood. I took the place on trial.", "저는 시험 삼아 그 자리에 들어갔습니다."], ["My master’s name was Fosco.", "주인 나리 성함은 포스코였습니다."], ["My mistress was an English lady.", "마님은 영국 부인이셨고요."], ["He was Count and she was Countess.", "나리는 백작이고 마님은 백작부인이었습니다."], ["There was a girl to do housemaid’s work when I got there.", "제가 갔을 때 하녀 일을 하는 아이가 하나 있었습니다."], ["She was not over-clean or tidy, but there was no harm in her.", "그리 깔끔하거나 야무지진 않았지만 나쁜 아이는 아니었습니다."], ["I and she were the only servants in the house.", "그 집 하인이라고는 저와 그 아이뿐이었습니다."]], [["Our master and mistress came after we got in; and as soon as they did come we were told, downstairs, that company was expected from the country.", "나리와 마님은 저희가 들어간 뒤에 오셨습니다. 그리고 오시자마자 시골에서 손님이 오실 거라는 말이 아래층에 전해졌습니다."]], [["The company was my mistress’s niece, and the back bedroom on the first floor was got ready for her.", "손님은 마님의 조카딸이었고, 이층 뒤쪽 침실이 그분을 위해 준비되었습니다."], ["My mistress mentioned to me that Lady Glyde (that was her name) was in poor health, and that I must be particular in my cooking accordingly.", "마님은 저에게 글라이드 부인, 그게 그분 성함이었습니다만, 그분 몸이 편찮으시니 그에 맞게 음식에 각별히 신경을 쓰라고 이르셨습니다."], ["She was to come that day, as well as I can remember—but whatever you do, don’t trust my memory in the matter.", "제 기억으로는 그분이 그날 오셨습니다만, 무슨 일이 있어도 날짜에 관해서는 제 기억을 믿지 마십시오."], ["I am sorry to say it’s no use asking me about days of the month, and such-like.", "죄송하지만 저한테 며칟날이니 하는 걸 물어 보셔야 소용없습니다."], ["Except Sundays, half my time I take no heed of them, being a hard-working woman and no scholar.", "주일날 말고는 저는 태반 날짜에 마음을 두지 않고 삽니다. 몸으로 일하는 여자인 데다 배운 게 없어서요."], ["All I know is Lady Glyde came, and when she did come, a fine fright she gave us all surely.", "제가 아는 것은 글라이드 부인이 오셨다는 것, 그리고 오셨을 때 저희 모두를 참말이지 몹시 놀라게 하셨다는 것뿐입니다."], ["I don’t know how master brought her to the house, being hard at work at the time.", "그때 저는 한창 일하는 중이어서 나리가 그분을 어떻게 집에 모셔 왔는지는 모릅니다."], ["But he did bring her in the afternoon, I think, and the housemaid opened the door to them, and showed them into the parlour.", "하지만 오후에 모셔 온 건 맞을 겁니다. 하녀 아이가 문을 열어 드리고 응접실로 안내했지요."], ["Before she had been long down in the kitchen again with me, we heard a hurry-skurry upstairs, and the parlour bell ringing like mad, and my mistress’s voice calling out for help.", "그 아이가 부엌으로 내려와 저와 함께 있은 지 얼마 되지도 않아, 위층에서 우당탕거리는 소리와 응접실 종이 미친 듯이 울리는 소리, 그리고 마님이 사람을 부르는 소리가 들렸습니다."]], [["We both ran up, and there we saw the lady laid on the sofa, with her face ghastly white, and her hands fast clenched, and her head drawn down to one side.", "저희 둘은 위로 뛰어올라갔고, 거기서 그 부인이 소파에 누워 있는 것을 보았습니다. 얼굴은 소름 끼치게 하얗고, 두 손은 꽉 움켜쥐었으며, 고개는 한쪽으로 꺾여 있었습니다."], ["She had been taken with a sudden fright, my mistress said, and master he told us she was in a fit of convulsions.", "갑자기 놀라셔서 그리 되었다고 마님은 말씀하셨고, 나리는 저희에게 그분이 경련을 일으켰다고 하셨습니다."], ["I ran out, knowing the neighbourhood a little better than the rest of them, to fetch the nearest doctor’s help.", "저는 다른 사람들보다 그 동네를 조금 더 잘 알았기에, 가장 가까운 의사를 부르러 뛰어나갔습니다."], ["The nearest help was at Goodricke’s and Garth’s, who worked together as partners, and had a good name and connection, as I have heard, all round St. John’s Wood.", "가장 가까운 데가 구드리크 앤드 가스였는데, 동업으로 함께 일하는 이들이고 제가 듣기로 세인트존스우드 일대에서 평판도 좋고 손님도 많았습니다."], ["Mr. Goodricke was in, and he came back with me directly.", "구드리크 선생이 계셔서 곧바로 저와 함께 와 주셨습니다."]], [["It was some time before he could make himself of much use.", "선생이 큰 도움이 되기까지는 한참이 걸렸습니다."], ["The poor unfortunate lady fell out of one fit into another, and went on so till she was quite wearied out, and as helpless as a new-born babe.", "그 가엾은 부인은 발작이 끝나기 무섭게 또 발작을 일으켰고, 그렇게 계속하다가 아주 기진맥진해져서 갓난아기처럼 힘없이 늘어지셨습니다."], ["We then got her to bed. Mr.", "그제야 저희는 그분을 침대에 뉘었습니다."], ["Goodricke went away to his house for medicine, and came back again in a quarter of an hour or less.", "구드리크 선생은 약을 가지러 댁에 갔다가 십오 분도 안 되어 돌아오셨습니다."], ["Besides the medicine he brought a bit of hollow mahogany wood with him, shaped like a kind of trumpet, and after waiting a little while, he put one end over the lady’s heart and the other to his ear, and listened carefully.", "약 말고도 나팔 비슷하게 생긴 속 빈 마호가니 나무토막을 하나 가져오셨는데, 잠시 기다렸다가 한쪽 끝을 부인의 심장에 대고 다른 쪽 끝을 자기 귀에 대고는 주의 깊게 들으셨습니다."]], [["When he had done he says to my mistress, who was in the room, “This is a very serious case,”", "다 듣고 나서 선생은 방에 있던 마님께 이렇게 말했습니다. “이건 아주 심각한 경우입니다."], ["he says, “I recommend you to write to Lady Glyde’s friends directly.”", "글라이드 부인의 가족에게 곧바로 편지를 쓰시기를 권합니다.”"], ["My mistress says to him, “Is it heart-disease?”", "마님이 선생께 “심장병인가요?” 하고 물으니 선생이 “그렇습니다."], ["And he says, “Yes, heart-disease of a most dangerous kind.”", "아주 위험한 종류의 심장병입니다.” 하고 대답했습니다."], ["He told her exactly what he thought was the matter, which I was not clever enough to understand.", "선생은 자기가 보기에 무엇이 문제인지 소상히 말씀하셨지만, 저는 알아들을 만큼 똑똑하지 못했습니다."], ["But I know this, he ended by saying that he was afraid neither his help nor any other doctor’s help was likely to be of much service.", "다만 이것만은 압니다. 선생은 자기 도움이든 다른 어떤 의사의 도움이든 그리 큰 소용이 없을까 두렵다는 말로 이야기를 맺었습니다."]], [["My mistress took this ill news more quietly than my master.", "마님은 이 나쁜 소식을 나리보다 차분하게 받아들이셨습니다."], ["He was a big, fat, odd sort of elderly man, who kept birds and white mice, and spoke to them as if they were so many Christian children.", "나리는 덩치가 크고 뚱뚱한 별난 노인으로, 새와 흰 쥐를 기르면서 마치 사람의 자식이라도 되는 양 그것들에게 말을 걸곤 하셨습니다."], ["He seemed terribly cut up by what had happened.", "나리는 그 일로 몹시 상심하신 듯했습니다. “아!"], ["“Ah! poor Lady Glyde!", "가엾은 글라이드 부인!"], ["poor dear Lady Glyde!”", "가엾고 가여운 글라이드 부인!”"], ["he says, and went stalking about, wringing his fat hands more like a play-actor than a gentleman.", "하고 말하면서, 뚱뚱한 두 손을 비틀며 신사라기보다 연극배우처럼 성큼성큼 돌아다니셨습니다."], ["For one question my mistress asked the doctor about the lady’s chances of getting round, he asked a good fifty at least.", "마님이 그 부인이 회복될 가망을 두고 의사에게 한 가지를 물으면 나리는 적어도 쉰 가지는 물으셨습니다."], ["I declare he quite tormented us all, and when he was quiet at last, out he went into the bit of back garden, picking trumpery little nosegays, and asking me to take them upstairs and make the sick-room look pretty with them.", "참말이지 저희 모두를 어지간히 볶아 대셨는데, 겨우 잠잠해지시더니 뒤뜰 자락으로 나가서 볼품없는 작은 꽃다발들을 만들어서는, 그걸 위층으로 가져가 병실을 예쁘게 꾸며 달라고 저에게 부탁하셨습니다."], ["As if THAT did any good.", "그런다고 무슨 소용이 있다고요."], ["I think he must have been, at times, a little soft in his head.", "저는 나리 머리가 이따금 좀 모자라신 게 아닌가 싶습니다."], ["But he was not a bad master—he had a monstrous civil tongue of his own, and a jolly, easy, coaxing way with him.", "그래도 나쁜 주인은 아니셨습니다. 입은 놀랍도록 공손하셨고, 유쾌하고 느긋하고 사람을 살살 구슬리는 데가 있으셨지요."], ["I liked him a deal better than my mistress.", "저는 마님보다 나리가 훨씬 좋았습니다."], ["She was a hard one, if ever there was a hard one yet.", "마님은 모진 분이었습니다. 세상에 모진 사람이 있다면 바로 그런 분이었지요."]], [["Towards night-time the lady roused up a little.", "밤이 가까워지자 부인이 조금 정신을 차렸습니다."], ["She had been so wearied out, before that, by the convulsions, that she never stirred hand or foot, or spoke a word to anybody.", "그 전에는 경련으로 어찌나 지치셨던지 손발 하나 까딱하지 않고 누구에게 말 한마디 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["She moved in the bed now, and stared about her at the room and us in it.", "이제 침대에서 몸을 움직이며 방과 방 안에 있는 저희를 둘러보셨습니다."], ["She must have been a nice-looking lady when well, with light hair, and blue eyes and all that.", "성하실 때는 고운 분이셨을 겁니다. 머리는 밝은 빛이고 눈은 파랗고 그랬으니까요."], ["Her rest was troubled at night—at least so I heard from my mistress, who sat up alone with her.", "밤에는 잠자리가 편치 않으셨습니다. 적어도 홀로 곁을 지키신 마님께 그렇게 들었습니다."], ["I only went in once before going to bed to see if I could be of any use, and then she was talking to herself in a confused, rambling manner.", "저는 잠자리에 들기 전에 도울 일이 있을까 하고 한 번 들어가 봤을 뿐인데, 그때 그분은 두서없이 횡설수설하며 혼잣말을 하고 계셨습니다."], ["She seemed to want sadly to speak to somebody who was absent from her somewhere.", "어딘가 곁에 없는 누군가에게 몹시도 말을 걸고 싶어 하시는 것 같았습니다."], ["I couldn’t catch the name the first time, and the second time master knocked at the door, with his regular mouthful of questions, and another of his trumpery nosegays.", "처음에는 그 이름을 알아듣지 못했고, 두 번째에는 나리가 늘 하시는 질문 보따리와 볼품없는 꽃다발을 또 하나 들고 문을 두드리셨습니다."]], [["When I went in early the next morning, the lady was clean worn out again, and lay in a kind of faint sleep. Mr.", "이튿날 이른 아침에 들어가 보니 부인은 또 완전히 기진하여 까무룩 잠든 것 같은 상태로 누워 계셨습니다."], ["Goodricke brought his partner, Mr. Garth, with him to advise.", "구드리크 선생이 상의하려고 동업자인 가스 선생을 데리고 오셨습니다."], ["They said she must not be disturbed out of her rest on any account.", "두 분은 무슨 일이 있어도 부인의 잠을 깨워서는 안 된다고 했습니다."], ["They asked my mistress many questions, at the other end of the room, about what the lady’s health had been in past times, and who had attended her, and whether she had ever suffered much and long together under distress of mind.", "두 분은 방 저쪽 끝에서 마님께 여러 가지를 물었습니다. 부인의 건강이 예전에는 어떠했는지, 누가 진료했는지, 마음의 괴로움을 오래도록 심하게 겪은 일이 있는지 하는 것들이었습니다."], ["I remember my mistress said “Yes” to that last question.", "마지막 질문에 마님이 “네”"], ["And Mr. Goodricke looked at Mr.", "하고 대답하신 것이 기억납니다."], ["Garth, and shook his head; and Mr. Garth looked at Mr.", "그러자 구드리크 선생이 가스 선생을 보며 고개를 저었고, 가스 선생이 구드리크 선생을 보며 고개를 저었습니다."], ["Goodricke, and shook his head. They seemed to think that the distress might have something to do with the mischief at the lady’s heart.", "두 분은 그 괴로움이 부인의 심장 탈과 무슨 관계가 있으리라고 여기시는 듯했습니다."], ["She was but a frail thing to look at, poor creature!", "가엾게도 부인은 보기에도 여린 분이었습니다!"], ["Very little strength at any time, I should say—very little strength.", "언제나 기운이 아주 없으셨을 겁니다. 참으로 기운이 없으셨지요."]], [["Later on the same morning, when she woke, the lady took a sudden turn, and got seemingly a great deal better.", "같은 날 조금 늦게 잠에서 깨어난 부인은 갑자기 상태가 달라져서 겉보기에는 훨씬 좋아지셨습니다."], ["I was not let in again to see her, no more was the housemaid, for the reason that she was not to be disturbed by strangers.", "저는 다시 들어가 뵙지 못했고 하녀 아이도 마찬가지였습니다. 낯선 사람 때문에 방해를 받아서는 안 된다는 까닭에서였지요."], ["What I heard of her being better was through my master.", "그분이 좋아지셨다는 이야기는 나리를 통해 들었습니다."], ["He was in wonderful good spirits about the change, and looked in at the kitchen window from the garden, with his great big curly-brimmed white hat on, to go out.", "나리는 그 변화를 두고 기분이 아주 좋으셔서, 챙이 크게 말려 올라간 커다란 흰 모자를 쓰고 나가시는 길에 뜰에서 부엌 창문 안을 들여다보셨습니다."]], [["“Good Mrs. Cook,”", "“착한 요리사 아주머니,”"], ["says he, “Lady Glyde is better.", "나리가 말씀하셨습니다. “글라이드 부인이 좋아지셨소."], ["My mind is more easy than it was, and I am going out to stretch my big legs with a sunny little summer walk.", "내 마음도 한결 놓여서, 볕 좋은 여름 산책으로 이 큰 다리를 좀 뻗으러 나가는 길이오. 뭘 좀 시켜다 줄까, 장을 봐다 줄까, 요리사 아주머니?"], ["Shall I order for you, shall I market for you, Mrs.", "거기서 무얼 만들고 있소? 저녁에 낼 맛있는 타르트인가?"], ["Cook? What are you making there?", "껍질은 넉넉하게, 부디 넉넉하게 해 주시오."], ["A nice tart for dinner?", "바삭바삭한 껍질 말이오, 아주머니."], ["Much crust, if you please—much crisp crust, my dear, that melts and crumbles delicious in the mouth.”", "입안에서 사르르 녹아 부서지는 그런 것으로.”"], ["That was his way.", "나리는 늘 그런 식이셨습니다."], ["He was past sixty, and fond of pastry.", "예순이 넘은 분이 과자를 좋아하셨지요."], ["Just think of that!", "생각 좀 해 보십시오!"]], [["The doctor came again in the forenoon, and saw for himself that Lady Glyde had woke up better.", "의사는 오전에 다시 와서 글라이드 부인이 깨어나 좋아지신 것을 직접 보셨습니다."], ["He forbid us to talk to her, or to let her talk to us, in case she was that way disposed, saying she must be kept quiet before all things, and encouraged to sleep as much as possible.", "선생은 저희에게 부인에게 말을 걸지도 말고, 부인이 말하고 싶어 하시더라도 말을 하시게 두지도 말라고 이르며, 무엇보다 조용히 계시게 하고 되도록 많이 주무시도록 해야 한다고 하셨습니다. 제가 뵐 때마다 부인은 말하고 싶어 하시는 것 같지 않았습니다."], ["She did not seem to want to talk whenever I saw her, except overnight, when I couldn’t make out what she was saying—she seemed too much worn down.", "무슨 말씀인지 알아들을 수 없었던 전날 밤만 빼고요. 너무 지치신 듯했습니다."], ["Mr. Goodricke was not nearly in such good spirits about her as master.", "구드리크 선생은 나리만큼 부인의 상태를 낙관하지 않으셨습니다."], ["He said nothing when he came downstairs, except that he would call again at five o’clock.", "아래층에 내려와서는 다섯 시에 다시 들르겠다는 말 외에는 아무 말씀도 없으셨습니다."]], [["About that time (which was before master came home again) the bell rang hard from the bedroom, and my mistress ran out into the landing, and called to me to go for Mr. Goodricke, and tell him the lady had fainted.", "그 무렵, 나리가 아직 돌아오시기 전이었습니다만, 침실에서 종이 세차게 울렸고, 마님이 층계참으로 뛰어나와 저를 부르시며 구드리크 선생께 가서 부인이 기절하셨다고 전하라고 하셨습니다."], ["I got on my bonnet and shawl, when, as good luck would have it, the doctor himself came to the house for his promised visit.", "제가 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두르는데, 다행히도 마침 선생이 약속한 왕진으로 집에 오셨습니다."]], [["I let him in, and went upstairs along with him.", "저는 선생을 들여보내고 함께 위층으로 올라갔습니다."], ["“Lady Glyde was just as usual,”", "“글라이드 부인은 여느 때와 같았어요.”"], ["says my mistress to him at the door; “she was awake, and looking about her in a strange, forlorn manner, when I heard her give a sort of half cry, and she fainted in a moment.”", "마님이 문간에서 선생께 말했습니다. “깨어나서 이상하게도 쓸쓸한 모습으로 주위를 둘러보고 있었는데, 반쯤 비명 같은 소리를 내는가 싶더니 순식간에 기절해 버렸어요.”"], ["The doctor went up to the bed, and stooped down over the sick lady.", "선생은 침대로 다가가 병든 부인 위로 몸을 굽혔습니다."], ["He looked very serious, all on a sudden, at the sight of her, and put his hand on her heart.", "그분을 보는 순간 갑자기 몹시 심각한 얼굴이 되더니, 부인의 심장에 손을 얹었습니다."]], [["My mistress stared hard in Mr.", "마님이 구드리크 선생의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 쳐다보았습니다."], ["Goodricke’s face. “Not dead!”", "“돌아가신 건 아니겠죠!”"], ["says she, whispering, and turning all of a tremble from head to foot.", "마님이 속삭이듯 말하며 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨었습니다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["says the doctor, very quiet and grave. “Dead.", "선생이 아주 조용하고 무겁게 말했습니다."], ["I was afraid it would happen suddenly when I examined her heart yesterday.”", "“돌아가셨습니다. 어제 심장을 진찰했을 때 갑자기 이렇게 되지 않을까 두려웠습니다.”"], ["My mistress stepped back from the bedside while he was speaking, and trembled and trembled again. “Dead!”", "선생이 말하는 동안 마님은 침대 곁에서 물러서며 떨고 또 떨었습니다."], ["she whispers to herself; “dead so suddenly!", "“돌아가셨어!” 마님이 혼잣말로 속삭였습니다."], ["dead so soon!", "“이렇게 갑자기 돌아가시다니!"], ["What will the Count say?” Mr.", "이렇게 빨리 돌아가시다니!"], ["Goodricke advised her to go downstairs, and quiet herself a little.", "백작이 뭐라고 할까?” 구드리크 선생은 마님께 아래층에 내려가 좀 진정하시라고 권했습니다."], ["“You have been sitting up all night,”", "“밤새 앉아 지새우셨으니,”"], ["says he, “and your nerves are shaken.", "선생이 말했습니다. “신경이 흔들리셨을 겁니다."], ["This person,”", "이 사람이,”"], ["says he, meaning me, “this person will stay in the room till I can send for the necessary assistance.”", "하고 선생은 저를 가리키며 말했습니다. “이 사람이 필요한 도움을 부르러 보낼 수 있을 때까지 방에 남아 있을 겁니다.”"], ["My mistress did as he told her.", "마님은 선생이 이르는 대로 하셨습니다."], ["“I must prepare the Count,”", "“백작에게 미리 알려야 해요.”"], ["she says.", "마님이 말했습니다."], ["“I must carefully prepare the Count.”", "“조심스럽게 미리 알려 드려야 해요.”"], ["And so she left us, shaking from head to foot, and went out.", "그러고는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서 저희를 두고 나가셨습니다."]], [["“Your master is a foreigner,”", "“댁의 주인 양반은 외국 사람이지요.”"], ["says Mr. Goodricke, when my mistress had left us.", "마님이 우리 곁을 떠난 뒤에 구드리크 선생님이 이렇게 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“Does he understand about registering the death?”", "“사망 신고하는 일은 알고 계신가요?” “저야 잘 모르겠습니다요, 선생님.”"], ["“I can’t rightly tell, sir,”", "하고 제가 말했지요. “하지만 모르실 것 같습니다요.”"], ["says I, “but I should think not.”", "선생님은 잠시 생각하시더니 이렇게 말씀하시더군요. “내가 보통 이런 일까지 하지는 않네만,”"], ["The doctor considered a minute, and then says he, “I don’t usually do such things,”", "하고 말씀하시길, “이번 경우에는 내가 직접 사망 신고를 해 두면 그 댁의 수고를 덜어 줄 수 있겠군. 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 관할 등록 사무소 앞을 지나갈 테니 들르는 거야 어려울 것 없지."], ["says he, “but it may save the family trouble in this case if I register the death myself.", "미안하지만 내가 그렇게 하겠다고 전해 주게.” “예, 선생님.”"], ["I shall pass the district office in half an hour’s time, and I can easily look in.", "하고 제가 말했지요. “그렇게까지 생각해 주시니 참말 고맙습니다요.”"], ["Mention, if you please, that I will do so.”", "“적당한 사람을 보내 줄 때까지 자네가 여기 남아 있어도 괜찮겠나?” 하고 선생님이 말씀하셨습니다."], ["“Yes, sir,”", "“예, 선생님.”"], ["says I, “with thanks, I’m sure, for your kindness in thinking of it.”", "하고 제가 말했지요. “그때까지 이 딱한 마님 곁을 지키고 있겠습니다요."], ["“You don’t mind staying here till I can send you the proper person?”", "여쭙습니다만, 해 드린 것 말고 더 해 드릴 수 있는 일은 없었을까요, 선생님?”"], ["says he.", "하고 제가 말했지요."], ["“No, sir,”", "“없네.”"], ["says I; “I’ll stay with the poor lady till then.", "하고 선생님이 말씀하셨습니다. “아무것도 없었어."], ["I suppose nothing more could be done, sir, than was done?”", "내가 보기 훨씬 전부터 이 부인은 몹시 괴로워했을 걸세. 나를 불렀을 때는 이미 가망이 없었지.”"], ["says I. “No,”", "“아이고, 저런!"], ["says he, “nothing; she must have suffered sadly before ever I saw her—the case was hopeless when I was called in.”", "우리 모두가 이르든 늦든 언젠가는 그리 되고 마는 거지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?”"], ["“Ah, dear me!", "하고 제가 말했지요."], ["we all come to it, sooner or later, don’t we, sir?”", "선생님은 거기에는 아무 대답도 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["says I. He gave no answer to that—he didn’t seem to care about talking.", "이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 없어 보이셨지요."], ["He said, “Good-day,”", "선생님은 “그럼 이만.”"], ["and went out.", "하고는 나가셨습니다."]], [["I stopped by the bedside from that time till the time when Mr. Goodricke sent the person in, as he had promised.", "그때부터 구드리크 선생님이 약속하신 대로 사람을 들여보내 주실 때까지 저는 침대 곁을 지켰습니다."], ["She was, by name, Jane Gould.", "그 여자는 이름이 제인 굴드였습니다."], ["I considered her to be a respectable-looking woman.", "제가 보기에는 점잖아 보이는 여자였지요."], ["She made no remark, except to say that she understood what was wanted of her, and that she had winded a many of them in her time.", "그 여자는 자기가 무슨 일을 하러 왔는지 알고 있다는 말과, 그동안 그런 시신을 여럿 거두어 보았다는 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 않았습니다."]], [["How master bore the news, when he first heard it, is more than I can tell, not having been present.", "주인 나리께서 그 소식을 처음 들으셨을 때 어떻게 견디셨는지는, 제가 그 자리에 없었으니 제 입으로 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다."], ["When I did see him he looked awfully overcome by it, to be sure.", "제가 나리를 뵈었을 때는 정말이지 몹시 상심하신 얼굴이셨습니다."], ["He sat quiet in a corner, with his fat hands hanging over his thick knees, and his head down, and his eyes looking at nothing.", "나리는 구석에 조용히 앉아 계셨는데, 살진 두 손을 굵은 무릎 위로 늘어뜨리고 고개를 떨군 채 아무것도 아닌 데를 바라보고 계셨지요."], ["He seemed not so much sorry, as scared and dazed like, by what had happened.", "슬퍼하신다기보다는 벌어진 일에 겁을 먹고 얼이 빠지신 것처럼 보이셨습니다."], ["My mistress managed all that was to be done about the funeral.", "장례에 관한 일은 모두 마님이 처리하셨습니다."], ["It must have cost a sight of money—the coffin, in particular, being most beautiful.", "돈이 어마어마하게 들었을 겁니다. 특히 관이 더할 나위 없이 훌륭했으니까요."], ["The dead lady’s husband was away, as we heard, in foreign parts.", "돌아가신 마님의 남편 되시는 분은 우리가 듣기로 외국에 나가 계시다고 했습니다."], ["But my mistress (being her aunt) settled it with her friends in the country (Cumberland, I think) that she should be buried there, in the same grave along with her mother.", "하지만 마님께서는 (돌아가신 분의 고모님이 되시니) 시골에 있는 그분 친척들과 (컴벌랜드였던 것 같습니다) 의논하셔서, 그곳에 어머니와 같은 무덤에 묻히도록 정하셨습니다."], ["Everything was done handsomely, in respect of the funeral, I say again, and master went down to attend the burying in the country himself.", "다시 말씀드리지만 장례에 관해서는 모든 것이 훌륭하게 치러졌고, 나리께서도 몸소 시골로 내려가 매장에 참석하셨습니다."], ["He looked grand in his deep mourning, with his big solemn face, and his slow walk, and his broad hatband—that he did!", "짙은 상복을 입으신 데다 큼직하고 엄숙한 얼굴에 느릿한 걸음걸이, 넓은 모자 띠까지 두르신 모습이 참으로 위풍당당하셨습니다. 정말 그러셨지요!"]], [["In conclusion.", "끝으로."], ["I have to say, in answer to questions put to me—", "제게 주어진 물음에 답하여 다음과 같이 말씀드립니다—"]], [["(1) That neither I nor my fellow-servant ever saw my master give Lady Glyde any medicine himself.", "(1) 저나 저와 함께 일하던 하인이나, 주인 나리께서 글라이드 부인께 손수 약을 드리는 것은 한 번도 본 적이 없습니다."]], [["(2) That he was never, to my knowledge and belief, left alone in the room with Lady Glyde.", "(2) 제가 알고 믿는 한, 나리께서 글라이드 부인과 방 안에 단둘이 남아 계신 일은 한 번도 없었습니다."]], [["(3) That I am not able to say what caused the sudden fright, which my mistress informed me had seized the lady on her first coming into the house.", "(3) 그 부인이 이 집에 처음 들어오셨을 때 갑작스러운 공포에 사로잡히셨다고 마님께서 제게 말씀하셨습니다만, 그 공포의 원인이 무엇이었는지는 저로서는 말씀드릴 수 없습니다."], ["The cause was never explained, either to me or to my fellow-servant.", "그 까닭은 저에게도, 저와 함께 일하던 하인에게도 끝내 설명되지 않았습니다."]], [["The above statement has been read over in my presence.", "위의 진술은 제가 있는 자리에서 낭독되었습니다."], ["I have nothing to add to it, or to take away from it.", "저는 여기에 더할 것도 뺄 것도 없습니다."], ["I say, on my oath as a Christian woman, this is the truth.", "기독교인 여자로서 맹세코 말씀드리건대, 이것은 사실입니다."]], [["(Signed) HESTER PINHORN, Her + Mark.", "(서명) 헤스터 핀혼, 본인의 + 표시."]], [["# 2. THE NARRATIVE OF THE DOCTOR", "# 2. THE NARRATIVE OF THE DOCTOR"]], [["To the Registrar of the Sub-District in which the undermentioned death took place.—I hereby certify that I attended Lady Glyde, aged Twenty-One last Birthday; that I last saw her on Thursday the 25th July 1850; that she died on the same day at No.", "아래에 기재된 사망이 발생한 관할 구역의 등록관 앞. — 본인은 이에 다음과 같이 증명합니다."], ["5 Forest Road, St. John’s Wood, and that the cause of her death was Aneurism.", "본인은 지난 생일로 스물한 살이 된 글라이드 부인을 진료하였으며, 1850년 7월 25일 목요일에 마지막으로 그를 진찰하였고, 그는 같은 날 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지에서 사망하였으며, 사인은 동맥류였습니다."], ["Duration of disease not known.", "발병 기간은 알 수 없습니다."]], [["(Signed) Alfred Goodricke.", "(서명) 앨프리드 구드리크."]], [["Prof. Title.", "직함."], ["M.R.C.S. Eng., L.S.A.", "영국 왕립외과의사회 회원, 약제사협회 면허."], ["Address, 12 Croydon Gardens St. John's Wood.", "주소, 세인트존스우드 크로이던 가든스 12번지."]], [["# 3. THE NARRATIVE OF JANE GOULD", "# 3. THE NARRATIVE OF JANE GOULD"]], [["I was the person sent in by Mr. Goodricke to do what was right and needful by the remains of a lady who had died at the house named in the certificate which precedes this.", "저는 이 서류 앞에 놓인 증명서에 적힌 집에서 사망한 어느 부인의 유해에 대하여 마땅하고 필요한 일을 하도록 구드리크 선생께서 보내신 사람입니다."], ["I found the body in charge of the servant, Hester Pinhorn.", "저는 그 시신이 하녀 헤스터 핀혼의 손에 맡겨져 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["I remained with it, and prepared it at the proper time for the grave.", "저는 시신 곁에 남아 때가 되었을 때 매장을 위한 준비를 했습니다."], ["It was laid in the coffin in my presence, and I afterwards saw the coffin screwed down previous to its removal.", "시신은 제가 보는 앞에서 관에 뉘어졌고, 그 뒤에 저는 관이 옮겨지기에 앞서 나사로 잠기는 것을 보았습니다."], ["When that had been done, and not before, I received what was due to me and left the house.", "그 일이 끝난 뒤에야, 그전이 아니라, 저는 제 몫을 받고 그 집을 떠났습니다."], ["I refer persons who may wish to investigate my character to Mr. Goodricke.", "제 사람됨을 알아보고자 하시는 분은 구드리크 선생께 물어보시기 바랍니다."], ["He will bear witness that I can be trusted to tell the truth.", "제가 진실을 말할 만한 사람이라는 것을 그분이 증언해 주실 것입니다."]], [["(Signed) JANE GOULD", "(서명) 제인 굴드"]], [["# 4. THE NARRATIVE OF THE TOMBSTONE", "# 4. THE NARRATIVE OF THE TOMBSTONE"]], [["Sacred to the Memory of Laura, Lady Glyde, wife of Sir Percival Glyde, Bart., of Blackwater Park, Hampshire, and daughter of the late Philip Fairlie, Esq., of Limmeridge House, in this parish.", "햄프셔 블랙워터 파크의 준남작 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 아내이며, 이 교구 리머리지 하우스의 고(故) 필립 페얼리 님의 딸인 로라 글라이드 부인을 기리며."], ["Born March 27th, 1829; married December 22nd, 1849; died July 25th, 1850.", "1829년 3월 27일 출생, 1849년 12월 22일 혼인, 1850년 7월 25일 사망."]], [["# 5. THE NARRATIVE OF WALTER HARTRIGHT", "# 5. THE NARRATIVE OF WALTER HARTRIGHT"]], [["Early in the summer of 1850 I and my surviving companions left the wilds and forests of Central America for home.", "1850년 초여름, 나와 살아남은 동료들은 중앙아메리카의 황야와 밀림을 떠나 고향으로 향했다."], ["Arrived at the coast, we took ship there for England.", "해안에 이르러 우리는 그곳에서 영국행 배를 탔다."], ["The vessel was wrecked in the Gulf of Mexico—I was among the few saved from the sea.", "배는 멕시코 만에서 난파했고, 나는 바다에서 구조된 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was my third escape from peril of death.", "죽음의 위험에서 벗어난 것이 그것으로 세 번째였다."], ["Death by disease, death by the Indians, death by drowning—all three had approached me; all three had passed me by.", "병으로 인한 죽음, 인디언의 손에 죽는 죽음, 물에 빠져 죽는 죽음 — 그 셋 모두가 내게 다가왔고, 셋 모두가 나를 비켜 갔다."]], [["The survivors of the wreck were rescued by an American vessel bound for Liverpool.", "난파선의 생존자들은 리버풀로 향하던 미국 배에 구조되었다."], ["The ship reached her port on the thirteenth day of October 1850.", "그 배가 항구에 닿은 것은 1850년 10월 13일이었다."], ["We landed late in the afternoon, and I arrived in London the same night.", "우리는 늦은 오후에 상륙했고, 나는 그날 밤 런던에 도착했다."]], [["These pages are not the record of my wanderings and my dangers away from home.", "이 글은 고향을 떠나 있는 동안 내가 겪은 방랑과 위험의 기록이 아니다."], ["The motives which led me from my country and my friends to a new world of adventure and peril are known.", "나를 조국과 벗들에게서 떼어 내어 모험과 위험의 새로운 세계로 이끈 동기는 이미 알려져 있다."], ["From that self-imposed exile I came back, as I had hoped, prayed, believed I should come back—a changed man.", "스스로에게 지운 그 유배에서 나는, 내가 바라고 기도하고 또 그러하리라 믿었던 대로 — 달라진 사람이 되어 돌아왔다."], ["In the waters of a new life I had tempered my nature afresh.", "새로운 삶의 물속에서 나는 내 천성을 다시 담금질했다."], ["In the stern school of extremity and danger my will had learnt to be strong, my heart to be resolute, my mind to rely on itself.", "극한과 위험이라는 혹독한 학교에서 내 의지는 강해지는 법을, 내 마음은 굳건해지는 법을, 내 정신은 스스로에게 기대는 법을 배웠다."], ["I had gone out to fly from my own future.", "나는 나 자신의 미래로부터 달아나려고 떠났다."], ["I came back to face it, as a man should.", "나는 사내라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 그 미래와 맞서기 위해 돌아왔다."]], [["To face it with that inevitable suppression of myself which I knew it would demand from me.", "그 미래가 내게 요구하리라는 것을 내가 알고 있던, 저 피할 수 없는 자기 억제와 더불어 맞서기 위해서."], ["I had parted with the worst bitterness of the past, but not with my heart’s remembrance of the sorrow and the tenderness of that memorable time.", "나는 지난날의 가장 쓰라린 아픔과는 작별했으나, 그 잊지 못할 시절의 슬픔과 애틋함에 대한 마음속 기억과는 작별하지 못했다."], ["I had not ceased to feel the one irreparable disappointment of my life—I had only learnt to bear it.", "내 삶의 그 돌이킬 수 없는 단 하나의 좌절을 나는 여전히 느끼고 있었다. 다만 그것을 견디는 법을 배웠을 뿐이었다."], ["Laura Fairlie was in all my thoughts when the ship bore me away, and I looked my last at England.", "배가 나를 실어 가고 내가 영국을 마지막으로 바라보았을 때, 내 모든 생각 속에는 로라 페얼리가 있었다."], ["Laura Fairlie was in all my thoughts when the ship brought me back, and the morning light showed the friendly shore in view.", "배가 나를 데리고 돌아오고 아침 햇살이 반가운 해안을 눈앞에 드러내 보였을 때에도, 내 모든 생각 속에는 로라 페얼리가 있었다."]], [["My pen traces the old letters as my heart goes back to the old love.", "내 마음이 옛사랑으로 돌아가니 내 펜도 옛 이름의 글자를 그린다."], ["I write of her as Laura Fairlie still.", "나는 그녀를 여전히 로라 페얼리라고 쓴다."], ["It is hard to think of her, it is hard to speak of her, by her husband’s name.", "그녀를 남편의 성으로 떠올리는 것도, 남편의 성으로 부르는 것도 내게는 힘든 일이다."]], [["There are no more words of explanation to add on my appearance for the second time in these pages.", "이 기록에 내가 두 번째로 등장하는 데 대해 덧붙일 설명은 더 없다."], ["This narrative, if I have the strength and the courage to write it, may now go on.", "이 이야기는, 내게 그것을 써 나갈 힘과 용기가 있다면, 이제 계속될 수 있으리라."]], [["My first anxieties and first hopes when the morning came centred in my mother and my sister.", "아침이 왔을 때 나의 첫 근심과 첫 희망은 어머니와 누이에게 쏠려 있었다."], ["I felt the necessity of preparing them for the joy and surprise of my return, after an absence during which it had been impossible for them to receive any tidings of me for months past.", "여러 달 동안 두 사람이 내 소식이라고는 한 자도 받아 볼 수 없었던 그 부재의 시간 뒤였으니, 돌아온 기쁨과 놀라움을 감당할 수 있도록 두 사람을 미리 준비시켜야겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Early in the morning I sent a letter to the Hampstead Cottage, and followed it myself in an hour’s time.", "이른 아침에 나는 햄스테드의 오두막으로 편지를 보냈고, 한 시간쯤 뒤에 그 편지를 뒤따라 내가 갔다."]], [["When the first meeting was over, when our quiet and composure of other days began gradually to return to us, I saw something in my mother’s face which told me that a secret oppression lay heavy on her heart.", "첫 만남이 지나가고 지난날의 고요와 침착이 우리에게 조금씩 되돌아오기 시작했을 때, 나는 어머니의 얼굴에서 어떤 무거운 비밀이 그 마음을 짓누르고 있음을 알려 주는 무언가를 보았다."], ["There was more than love—there was sorrow in the anxious eyes that looked on me so tenderly—there was pity in the kind hand that slowly and fondly strengthened its hold on mine.", "나를 그토록 다정하게 바라보는 그 근심 어린 눈에는 사랑만이 아니라 슬픔이 있었고, 천천히 애틋하게 내 손을 더 힘주어 쥐는 그 인자한 손에는 연민이 있었다."], ["We had no concealments from each other.", "우리 사이에는 감추는 것이 없었다."], ["She knew how the hope of my life had been wrecked—she knew why I had left her.", "어머니는 내 인생의 희망이 어떻게 부서졌는지 알고 계셨고, 내가 왜 어머니 곁을 떠났는지도 알고 계셨다."], ["It was on my lips to ask as composedly as I could if any letter had come for me from Miss Halcombe, if there was any news of her sister that I might hear.", "홀콤 양에게서 내게 온 편지가 있었는지, 그 동생에 관해 내가 들을 만한 소식이 있는지 되도록 담담하게 물어보려고 나는 입을 열려 했다."], ["But when I looked in my mother’s face I lost courage to put the question even in that guarded form.", "그러나 어머니의 얼굴을 들여다보는 순간, 그렇게 조심스러운 형태로조차 그 물음을 꺼낼 용기를 잃고 말았다."], ["I could only say, doubtingly and restrainedly—", "나는 다만 머뭇거리며, 애써 자제하며 이렇게 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["“You have something to tell me.”", "“제게 하실 말씀이 있으시군요.”"]], [["My sister, who had been sitting opposite to us, rose suddenly without a word of explanation—rose and left the room.", "우리 맞은편에 앉아 있던 누이가 아무런 설명도 없이 갑자기 일어섰다. 일어서서 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["My mother moved closer to me on the sofa and put her arms round my neck.", "어머니는 소파 위에서 내 곁으로 더 가까이 다가와 두 팔로 내 목을 감싸 안으셨다."], ["Those fond arms trembled—the tears flowed fast over the faithful loving face.", "그 다정한 두 팔이 떨렸고, 신실하고 사랑 어린 얼굴 위로 눈물이 빠르게 흘러내렸다."]], [["“Walter!”", "“월터야!”"], ["she whispered, “my own darling!", "어머니가 속삭이셨다. “내 귀한 아들아!"], ["my heart is heavy for you.", "네 일을 생각하면 어미 가슴이 미어진다."], ["Oh, my son!", "오, 내 아들아!"], ["my son!", "내 아들아!"], ["try to remember that I am still left!”", "그래도 어미가 아직 네 곁에 남아 있다는 걸 잊지 말아 다오!”"]], [["My head sank on her bosom.", "내 머리가 어머니의 가슴에 묻혔다."], ["She had said all in saying those words.", "그 말씀으로 어머니는 이미 모든 것을 말한 셈이었다."]], [["It was the morning of the third day since my return—the morning of the sixteenth of October.", "돌아온 지 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 10월 16일 아침이었다."]], [["I had remained with them at the cottage—I had tried hard not to embitter the happiness of my return to THEM as it was embittered to ME.", "나는 오두막에서 두 사람과 함께 지냈다. 나에게 그러했듯 두 사람에게까지 내 귀향의 기쁨이 쓰디쓴 것이 되지 않도록 무던히도 애썼다."], ["I had done all man could to rise after the shock, and accept my life resignedly—to let my great sorrow come in tenderness to my heart, and not in despair.", "충격을 딛고 일어서서 내 삶을 체념으로 받아들이려고, 내 크나큰 슬픔이 절망이 아니라 애틋함으로 내 마음에 찾아들게 하려고, 사람으로서 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보았다."], ["It was useless and hopeless.", "소용없고 가망 없는 일이었다."], ["No tears soothed my aching eyes, no relief came to me from my sister’s sympathy or my mother’s love.", "어떤 눈물도 쑤시는 내 눈을 달래 주지 못했고, 누이의 위로에서도 어머니의 사랑에서도 내게 위안은 오지 않았다."]], [["On that third morning I opened my heart to them.", "그 사흘째 되는 날 아침, 나는 두 사람에게 마음을 열었다."], ["At last the words passed my lips which I had longed to speak on the day when my mother told me of her death.", "어머니에게서 그녀의 죽음을 전해 들은 날부터 입 밖에 내고 싶어 애태우던 말이 마침내 내 입술을 넘어 나왔다."]], [["“Let me go away alone for a little while,” I said.", "“잠시만 저 혼자 다녀오게 해 주세요.”"], ["“I shall bear it better when I have looked once more at the place where I first saw her—when I have knelt and prayed by the grave where they have laid her to rest.”", "내가 말했다. “제가 그분을 처음 본 그곳을 한 번만 더 바라보고 나면 — 그분을 뉘어 놓은 무덤 곁에 무릎 꿇고 기도하고 나면, 이 일을 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 것 같습니다.”"]], [["I departed on my journey—my journey to the grave of Laura Fairlie.", "나는 길을 떠났다. 로라 페얼리의 무덤으로 가는 길이었다."]], [["It was a quiet autumn afternoon when I stopped at the solitary station, and set forth alone on foot by the well-remembered road.", "내가 그 외딴 역에 내려 눈에 익은 길을 홀로 걸어 나선 것은 고요한 가을 오후였다."], ["The waning sun was shining faintly through thin white clouds—the air was warm and still—the peacefulness of the lonely country was overshadowed and saddened by the influence of the falling year.", "기우는 해가 엷은 흰 구름 사이로 희미하게 비치고 있었고, 공기는 따스하고 고요했다. 인적 없는 시골의 평온함 위로는 저물어 가는 한 해의 기운이 그늘을 드리워 서글픔을 더하고 있었다."]], [["I reached the moor—I stood again on the brow of the hill—I looked on along the path—and there were the familiar garden trees in the distance, the clear sweeping semicircle of the drive, the high white walls of Limmeridge House.", "황야에 이르러 나는 다시 언덕 마루에 섰다. 길을 따라 눈길을 던지니, 저 멀리 낯익은 정원의 나무들과 시원스레 휘어 도는 진입로의 반원, 리머리지 하우스의 높다란 흰 벽이 있었다."], ["The chances and changes, the wanderings and dangers of months and months past, all shrank and shrivelled to nothing in my mind.", "지나간 여러 달 동안의 온갖 우여곡절과 방랑과 위험이 내 마음속에서 모조리 오그라들어 아무것도 아닌 것이 되어 버렸다."], ["It was like yesterday since my feet had last trodden the fragrant heathy ground.", "내 발이 향기로운 히스 땅을 마지막으로 밟은 것이 어제 일만 같았다."], ["I thought I should see her coming to meet me, with her little straw hat shading her face, her simple dress fluttering in the air, and her well-filled sketch-book ready in her hand.", "작은 밀짚모자로 얼굴을 가리고 수수한 옷자락을 바람에 나부끼며, 잔뜩 채워진 스케치북을 손에 들고 그녀가 나를 맞으러 오는 모습이 보일 것만 같았다."]], [["Oh death, thou hast thy sting!", "오 죽음이여, 그대에게는 쏘는 것이 있구나!"], ["oh, grave, thou hast thy victory!", "오 무덤이여, 그대에게는 이기는 것이 있구나!"]], [["I turned aside, and there below me in the glen was the lonesome grey church, the porch where I had waited for the coming of the woman in white, the hills encircling the quiet burial-ground, the brook bubbling cold over its stony bed.", "나는 옆으로 돌아섰다. 그러자 발아래 골짜기에는 쓸쓸한 잿빛 교회가, 흰옷을 입은 여인이 오기를 내가 기다렸던 그 현관이, 고요한 묘지를 둘러싼 언덕들이, 돌 바닥 위로 차갑게 재잘거리며 흐르는 시내가 있었다."], ["There was the marble cross, fair and white, at the head of the tomb—the tomb that now rose over mother and daughter alike.", "무덤 머리맡에는 곱고 흰 대리석 십자가가 서 있었다. 이제는 어머니와 딸을 함께 덮고 솟아오른 무덤이었다."]], [["I approached the grave.", "나는 무덤으로 다가갔다."], ["I crossed once more the low stone stile, and bared my head as I touched the sacred ground.", "낮은 돌 층계를 다시 한번 넘어, 그 성스러운 땅을 밟으며 모자를 벗었다."], ["Sacred to gentleness and goodness, sacred to reverence and grief.", "온유함과 선함에 바쳐진 땅, 경외와 슬픔에 바쳐진 땅이었다."]], [["I stopped before the pedestal from which the cross rose.", "나는 십자가가 솟아 있는 대좌 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["On one side of it, on the side nearest to me, the newly-cut inscription met my eyes—the hard, clear, cruel black letters which told the story of her life and death.", "그 한쪽 면, 내게 가장 가까운 쪽 면에 새로 새긴 비문이 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀의 삶과 죽음을 이야기하는 딱딱하고 또렷하고 잔인한 검은 글자들이었다."], ["I tried to read them.", "나는 그것을 읽으려 했다."], ["I did read as far as the name.", "이름까지는 정말로 읽었다."], ["“Sacred to the Memory of Laura——”", "“로라를 기리며——”"], ["The kind blue eyes dim with tears—the fair head drooping wearily—the innocent parting words which implored me to leave her—oh, for a happier last memory of her than this; the memory I took away with me, the memory I bring back with me to her grave!", "눈물로 흐려진 그 다정한 푸른 눈, 지쳐 떨구어진 그 고운 머리, 자기를 두고 떠나 달라고 애원하던 그 순결한 이별의 말 — 아, 이보다 더 행복한 마지막 기억을 그녀에 대해 지닐 수 있었더라면. 내가 지니고 떠났던 기억, 이제 그녀의 무덤으로 다시 가지고 온 기억이 하필 이것이라니!"]], [["A second time I tried to read the inscription.", "두 번째로 나는 비문을 읽으려 했다."], ["I saw at the end the date of her death, and above it——", "끝머리에 있는 그녀의 사망 날짜가 눈에 들어왔고, 그 위에는——"]], [["Above it there were lines on the marble—there was a name among them which disturbed my thoughts of her.", "그 위에는 대리석에 새겨진 몇 줄의 글이 있었고, 그 가운데에는 그녀를 향한 내 생각을 어지럽히는 이름이 하나 있었다."], ["I went round to the other side of the grave, where there was nothing to read, nothing of earthly vileness to force its way between her spirit and mine.", "나는 무덤 반대편으로 돌아갔다. 그쪽에는 읽을 것이 아무것도 없었고, 그녀의 영혼과 내 영혼 사이를 비집고 들어올 세속의 더러움도 아무것도 없었다."]], [["I knelt down by the tomb.", "나는 무덤 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["I laid my hands, I laid my head on the broad white stone, and closed my weary eyes on the earth around, on the light above.", "넓고 흰 돌 위에 두 손을 얹고 머리를 얹은 채, 둘레의 땅에 대해서도 위의 빛에 대해서도 지친 두 눈을 감았다."], ["I let her come back to me.", "나는 그녀가 내게 돌아오도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Oh, my love!", "오, 내 사랑!"], ["my love!", "내 사랑!"], ["my heart may speak to you NOW!", "이제야 내 마음이 그대에게 말할 수 있구려!"], ["It is yesterday again since we parted—yesterday, since your dear hand lay in mine—yesterday, since my eyes looked their last on you.", "우리가 헤어진 것이 다시 어제 일이 되는구려 — 그대의 사랑스러운 손이 내 손 안에 놓여 있던 것도 어제, 내 두 눈이 그대를 마지막으로 바라본 것도 어제가 되는구려."], ["My love!", "내 사랑!"], ["my love!", "내 사랑!"]], [["Time had flowed on, and silence had fallen like thick night over its course.", "시간은 흘러갔고, 그 흐름 위로 짙은 밤처럼 침묵이 내려앉아 있었다."]], [["The first sound that came after the heavenly peace rustled faintly like a passing breath of air over the grass of the burial-ground.", "그 천상의 평화 뒤에 찾아온 첫 소리는 묘지의 풀 위를 스치는 한 줄기 숨결처럼 희미하게 바스락거렸다."], ["I heard it nearing me slowly, until it came changed to my ear—came like footsteps moving onward—then stopped.", "나는 그것이 천천히 내게 가까워지는 것을 들었고, 이윽고 그 소리는 내 귀에 달리 들려왔다. 앞으로 옮겨 오는 발소리처럼 들려오다가, 멈추었다."]], [["I looked up.", "나는 고개를 들었다."]], [["The sunset was near at hand.", "해 질 녘이 가까웠다."], ["The clouds had parted—the slanting light fell mellow over the hills.", "구름이 갈라져 비스듬한 빛이 언덕 위로 부드럽게 내려앉고 있었다."], ["The last of the day was cold and clear and still in the quiet valley of the dead.", "죽은 이들의 고요한 골짜기에 남은 하루의 마지막은 차갑고 맑고 고요했다."]], [["Beyond me, in the burial-ground, standing together in the cold clearness of the lower light, I saw two women.", "저만치 묘지 안, 낮게 깔린 빛의 차가운 맑음 속에 나란히 서 있는 두 여자가 보였다."], ["They were looking towards the tomb, looking towards me.", "그들은 무덤 쪽을, 나를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Two.", "둘이었다."]], [["They came a little on, and stopped again.", "그들은 조금 앞으로 나오다가 다시 멈추었다."], ["Their veils were down, and hid their faces from me.", "베일을 내리고 있어 얼굴은 내게 가려져 있었다."], ["When they stopped, one of them raised her veil.", "멈춰 섰을 때 그중 한 사람이 베일을 걷어 올렸다."], ["In the still evening light I saw the face of Marian Halcombe.", "고요한 저녁 빛 속에서 나는 매리언 홀콤의 얼굴을 보았다."]], [["Changed, changed as if years had passed over it!", "달라져 있었다. 그 위로 몇 해가 지나가 버리기라도 한 듯 달라져 있었다!"], ["The eyes large and wild, and looking at me with a strange terror in them.", "두 눈은 커다랗고 사나웠으며, 그 안에 낯선 공포를 담은 채 나를 바라보고 있었다."], ["The face worn and wasted piteously.", "얼굴은 애처로울 만큼 상하고 여위어 있었다."], ["Pain and fear and grief written on her as with a brand.", "고통과 두려움과 슬픔이 낙인처럼 그녀에게 찍혀 있었다."]], [["I took one step towards her from the grave.", "나는 무덤에서 그녀에게로 한 걸음 내디뎠다."], ["She never moved—she never spoke.", "그녀는 움직이지도, 말하지도 않았다."], ["The veiled woman with her cried out faintly.", "그녀와 함께 있던 베일 쓴 여자가 희미하게 소리를 질렀다."], ["I stopped.", "나는 멈춰 섰다."], ["The springs of my life fell low, and the shuddering of an unutterable dread crept over me from head to foot.", "내 생명의 샘이 낮게 잦아들었고, 말로 다 할 수 없는 공포의 전율이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 나를 타고 기어올랐다."]], [["The woman with the veiled face moved away from her companion, and came towards me slowly.", "베일로 얼굴을 가린 여자가 일행에게서 떨어져 나와 천천히 내게로 다가왔다."], ["Left by herself, standing by herself, Marian Halcombe spoke.", "홀로 남겨진 채, 홀로 선 채 매리언 홀콤이 입을 열었다."], ["It was the voice that I remembered—the voice not changed, like the frightened eyes and the wasted face.", "내가 기억하는 바로 그 목소리였다. 겁에 질린 눈이나 여윈 얼굴과는 달리 변하지 않은 목소리였다."]], [["“My dream!", "“내 꿈!"], ["my dream!”", "내 꿈이!”"], ["I heard her say those words softly in the awful silence.", "그 무서운 침묵 속에서 그녀가 나직이 그렇게 말하는 것을 나는 들었다."], ["She sank on her knees, and raised her clasped hands to heaven.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 맞잡은 두 손을 하늘로 들어 올렸다."], ["“Father!", "“아버지!"], ["strengthen him.", "저 사람을 강하게 하소서."], ["Father!", "아버지!"], ["help him in his hour of need.”", "저 사람이 절실히 도움을 필요로 하는 이 순간에 도우소서.”"]], [["The woman came on, slowly and silently came on.", "여자는 다가왔다. 천천히, 소리 없이 다가왔다."], ["I looked at her—at her, and at none other, from that moment.", "나는 그녀를 바라보았다. 그 순간부터 오직 그녀만을, 다른 누구도 아닌 그녀만을 바라보았다."]], [["The voice that was praying for me faltered and sank low—then rose on a sudden, and called affrightedly, called despairingly to me to come away.", "나를 위해 기도하던 목소리가 흔들리며 낮게 잦아들더니, 별안간 높아져 겁에 질린 듯, 절망한 듯 내게 물러나라고 외쳤다."]], [["But the veiled woman had possession of me, body and soul.", "그러나 베일 쓴 여자가 내 몸과 영혼을 온통 사로잡고 있었다."], ["She stopped on one side of the grave.", "그녀는 무덤 한쪽에 멈춰 섰다."], ["We stood face to face with the tombstone between us.", "우리는 묘비를 사이에 두고 얼굴을 마주하고 섰다."], ["She was close to the inscription on the side of the pedestal.", "그녀는 대좌 면에 새겨진 비문 바로 곁에 있었다."], ["Her gown touched the black letters.", "그녀의 옷자락이 그 검은 글자들에 닿았다."]], [["The voice came nearer, and rose and rose more passionately still.", "목소리가 더 가까이 다가오며 점점 더, 점점 더 격정적으로 높아졌다."], ["“Hide your face!", "“얼굴을 가려요!"], ["don’t look at her!", "저 여자를 보지 말아요!"], ["Oh, for God’s sake, spare him——”", "오, 하느님, 부디 저 사람을 살려 주——”"]], [["The woman lifted her veil.", "여자가 베일을 걷어 올렸다."]], [["“Sacred to the Memory of Laura, Lady Glyde——”", "“로라 글라이드 부인을 기리며——”"]], [["Laura, Lady Glyde, was standing by the inscription, and was looking at me over the grave.", "로라 글라이드 부인이 그 비문 곁에 서서, 무덤 너머로 나를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["[The Second Epoch of the Story closes here.]", "[이야기의 제2기는 여기에서 끝난다.]"]], [["I open a new page.", "나는 여기서 새 페이지를 연다."], ["I advance my narrative by one week.", "내 이야기를 일주일 앞으로 옮겨 놓는다."]], [["The history of the interval which I thus pass over must remain unrecorded.", "이렇게 건너뛰는 그 사이의 내력은 기록되지 않은 채로 남아야 한다."], ["My heart turns faint, my mind sinks in darkness and confusion when I think of it.", "그 일을 떠올리면 가슴이 아득해지고 정신은 어둠과 혼란 속으로 가라앉는다."], ["This must not be, if I who write am to guide, as I ought, you who read.", "글을 쓰는 내가 읽는 당신을 마땅히 이끌어야 한다면, 그래서는 안 된다."], ["This must not be, if the clue that leads through the windings of the story is to remain from end to end untangled in my hands.", "이 이야기의 굽이진 길을 헤쳐 나가는 실마리가 처음부터 끝까지 내 손안에서 엉키지 않은 채 남아 있어야 한다면, 그래서는 안 된다."]], [["A life suddenly changed—its whole purpose created afresh, its hopes and fears, its struggles, its interests, and its sacrifices all turned at once and for ever into a new direction—this is the prospect which now opens before me, like the burst of view from a mountain’s top.", "갑작스레 달라진 삶 — 그 목적 전부가 새로이 세워지고, 희망과 두려움, 투쟁과 관심사와 희생이 모두 한꺼번에 그리고 영원히 새로운 방향으로 돌아선 삶 — 이것이 지금 내 앞에 열리는 전망이다. 산꼭대기에 올라섰을 때 별안간 터져 나오는 시야처럼."], ["I left my narrative in the quiet shadow of Limmeridge church—I resume it, one week later, in the stir and turmoil of a London street.", "나는 리머리지 교회의 고요한 그늘 속에 내 이야기를 놓아두었다. 그리고 일주일 뒤, 런던 어느 거리의 소란과 혼잡 속에서 그것을 다시 잇는다."]], [["The street is in a populous and a poor neighbourhood.", "그 거리는 사람이 붐비는 가난한 동네에 있다."], ["The ground floor of one of the houses in it is occupied by a small newsvendor’s shop, and the first floor and the second are let as furnished lodgings of the humblest kind.", "거리에 늘어선 집들 가운데 하나는 1층을 작은 신문 가게가 차지하고 있고, 2층과 3층은 더없이 초라한 가구 딸린 셋방으로 내놓았다."]], [["I have taken those two floors in an assumed name.", "나는 가명으로 그 두 층을 얻었다."], ["On the upper floor I live, with a room to work in, a room to sleep in.", "위층에서 내가 사는데, 일하는 방 하나와 잠자는 방 하나를 쓴다."], ["On the lower floor, under the same assumed name, two women live, who are described as my sisters.", "아래층에는 같은 가명으로 두 여자가 사는데, 내 누이들이라고 되어 있다."], ["I get my bread by drawing and engraving on wood for the cheap periodicals.", "나는 싸구려 정기간행물에 실을 그림을 그리고 목판에 새겨 밥벌이를 한다."], ["My sisters are supposed to help me by taking in a little needle-work.", "내 누이들은 삯바느질을 조금 맡아 나를 거드는 것으로 되어 있다."], ["Our poor place of abode, our humble calling, our assumed relationship, and our assumed name, are all used alike as a means of hiding us in the house-forest of London.", "우리의 초라한 거처, 보잘것없는 생업, 꾸며 낸 관계, 꾸며 낸 이름 — 이 모두가 한결같이 런던이라는 집들의 숲 속에 우리를 감추는 수단으로 쓰인다."], ["We are numbered no longer with the people whose lives are open and known.", "우리는 더 이상 삶이 훤히 드러나고 알려진 사람들 축에 들지 않는다."], ["I am an obscure, unnoticed man, without patron or friend to help me.", "나는 후원자도 도와줄 벗도 없는, 이름 없고 눈에 띄지 않는 사내다."], ["Marian Halcombe is nothing now but my eldest sister, who provides for our household wants by the toil of her own hands.", "매리언 홀콤은 이제 제 손으로 일해 살림을 꾸려 가는 내 맏누이일 뿐이다."], ["We two, in the estimation of others, are at once the dupes and the agents of a daring imposture.", "남들이 보기에 우리 둘은 대담한 사기극에 속아 넘어간 자인 동시에 그 앞잡이다."], ["We are supposed to be the accomplices of mad Anne Catherick, who claims the name, the place, and the living personality of dead Lady Glyde.", "우리는 죽은 글라이드 부인의 이름과 지위와 살아 있는 인격을 제 것이라 주장하는 미친 앤 캐서릭의 공범으로 여겨지는 것이다."]], [["That is our situation.", "그것이 우리의 처지다."], ["That is the changed aspect in which we three must appear, henceforth, in this narrative, for many and many a page to come.", "그것이 앞으로 이 이야기에서 여러 페이지에 걸쳐 우리 세 사람이 지니고 나타나야 할, 달라진 모습이다."]], [["In the eye of reason and of law, in the estimation of relatives and friends, according to every received formality of civilised society, “Laura, Lady Glyde,” lay buried with her mother in Limmeridge churchyard.", "이성과 법의 눈으로 보나, 친척과 벗들의 판단으로 보나, 문명사회의 온갖 공인된 격식에 따르나, “로라, 글라이드 부인”은 리머리지 교회 묘지에 제 어머니와 나란히 묻혀 있었다."], ["Torn in her own lifetime from the list of the living, the daughter of Philip Fairlie and the wife of Percival Glyde might still exist for her sister, might still exist for me, but to all the world besides she was dead.", "살아 있는 동안에 산 자의 명부에서 찢겨 나간 필립 페얼리의 딸이자 퍼시벌 글라이드의 아내는 제 언니에게는 아직 살아 있을 수 있었고 나에게도 아직 살아 있을 수 있었으나, 그 밖의 온 세상에게 그녀는 죽은 사람이었다."], ["Dead to her uncle, who had renounced her; dead to the servants of the house, who had failed to recognise her; dead to the persons in authority, who had transmitted her fortune to her husband and her aunt; dead to my mother and my sister, who believed me to be the dupe of an adventuress and the victim of a fraud; socially, morally, legally—dead.", "그녀를 내친 삼촌에게 죽었고, 그녀를 알아보지 못한 그 집 하인들에게 죽었고, 그녀의 재산을 남편과 고모에게 넘겨준 관계 당국의 사람들에게 죽었고, 내가 여자 사기꾼에게 홀린 얼간이요 협잡의 희생자라고 믿는 내 어머니와 누이에게 죽었다. 사회적으로도, 도덕적으로도, 법적으로도 — 죽은 사람이었다."]], [["And yet alive!", "그런데도 살아 있었다!"], ["Alive in poverty and in hiding.", "가난 속에서, 숨어 지내며 살아 있었다."], ["Alive, with the poor drawing-master to fight her battle, and to win the way back for her to her place in the world of living beings.", "그녀를 대신해 싸우고 살아 있는 사람들의 세계에서 그녀의 자리를 되찾아 줄 가난한 그림 교사를 곁에 두고 살아 있었다."]], [["Did no suspicion, excited by my own knowledge of Anne Catherick’s resemblance to her, cross my mind, when her face was first revealed to me?", "앤 캐서릭이 그녀를 닮았다는 것을 내가 익히 알고 있었으니, 그녀의 얼굴이 처음 내 앞에 드러났을 때 어떤 의심이 내 마음을 스치지는 않았던가?"], ["Not the shadow of a suspicion, from the moment when she lifted her veil by the side of the inscription which recorded her death.", "그녀가 제 죽음을 새긴 비문 곁에서 베일을 걷어 올린 그 순간부터, 의심의 그림자조차 없었다."]], [["Before the sun of that day had set, before the last glimpse of the home which was closed against her had passed from our view, the farewell words I spoke, when we parted at Limmeridge House, had been recalled by both of us—repeated by me, recognised by her.", "그날의 해가 지기 전에, 그녀를 향해 닫혀 버린 그 집의 마지막 모습이 우리 시야에서 사라지기 전에, 우리가 리머리지 하우스에서 헤어질 때 내가 했던 작별의 말이 우리 둘 모두에게 되살아났다 — 내가 그 말을 되풀이했고, 그녀가 그 말을 알아보았다."], ["“If ever the time comes, when the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength will give you a moment’s happiness, or spare you a moment’s sorrow, will you try to remember the poor drawing-master who has taught you?”", "“언젠가 제 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다한 헌신이 아가씨께 한순간의 행복을 드리거나 한순간의 슬픔을 덜어 드릴 수 있는 때가 온다면, 아가씨를 가르쳤던 가난한 그림 교사를 기억해 주시겠습니까?”"], ["She, who now remembered so little of the trouble and terror of a later time, remembered those words, and laid her poor head innocently and trustingly on the bosom of the man who had spoken them.", "뒷날의 괴로움과 공포는 이제 거의 기억하지 못하는 그녀가 그 말만은 기억했고, 그 말을 했던 사내의 가슴에 천진하고도 믿음 가득하게 제 가엾은 머리를 기댔다. 그녀가 내 이름을 부르며 “저 사람들이 저더러 모든 걸 잊게 하려고 했어요, 월터."], ["In that moment, when she called me by my name, when she said, “They have tried to make me forget everything, Walter; but I remember Marian, and I remember YOU”—in that moment, I, who had long since given her my love, gave her my life, and thanked God that it was mine to bestow on her.", "하지만 저는 매리언을 기억하고, 당신을 기억해요” 하고 말하던 그 순간에, 오래전에 이미 그녀에게 내 사랑을 바쳤던 나는 그녀에게 내 목숨을 바쳤고, 그것이 그녀에게 내어 줄 수 있는 내 것임을 하느님께 감사드렸다."], ["Yes!", "그렇다!"], ["the time had come.", "그때가 온 것이다."], ["From thousands on thousands of miles away—through forest and wilderness, where companions stronger than I had fallen by my side, through peril of death thrice renewed, and thrice escaped, the Hand that leads men on the dark road to the future had led me to meet that time.", "수천수만 마일 떨어진 곳에서부터 — 나보다 강한 동료들이 내 곁에서 쓰러져 간 숲과 황야를 지나, 세 번 거듭 닥쳤다가 세 번 벗어난 죽음의 위험을 지나, 사람들을 미래로 향한 어두운 길 위로 이끄는 그 손이 나를 그때와 만나도록 이끌어 온 것이다."], ["Forlorn and disowned, sorely tried and sadly changed—her beauty faded, her mind clouded—robbed of her station in the world, of her place among living creatures—the devotion I had promised, the devotion of my whole heart and soul and strength, might be laid blamelessly now at those dear feet.", "버림받고 부인당하고, 모질게 시달리고 슬프도록 달라진 그녀 — 아름다움은 시들고 정신은 흐려졌으며, 세상에서의 지위도 살아 있는 사람들 사이의 자리도 빼앗긴 그녀에게, 내가 약속했던 헌신을, 내 온 마음과 영혼과 힘을 다한 그 헌신을 이제는 떳떳이 그 사랑스러운 발치에 바칠 수 있었다."], ["In the right of her calamity, in the right of her friendlessness, she was mine at last!", "그녀의 재난이 준 권리로, 그녀의 외로움이 준 권리로, 그녀는 마침내 내 사람이 되었다!"], ["Mine to support, to protect, to cherish, to restore.", "부양하고 지키고 아끼고 되살려 낼 내 사람."], ["Mine to love and honour as father and brother both.", "아버지이자 오라비로서 사랑하고 존중할 내 사람."], ["Mine to vindicate through all risks and all sacrifices—through the hopeless struggle against Rank and Power, through the long fight with armed deceit and fortified Success, through the waste of my reputation, through the loss of my friends, through the hazard of my life.", "온갖 위험과 온갖 희생을 무릅쓰고 — 신분과 권력에 맞선 가망 없는 싸움을 뚫고, 무장한 기만과 요새를 두른 성공과의 긴 싸움을 뚫고, 내 명성이 소진되는 일을 뚫고, 벗들을 잃는 일을 뚫고, 내 목숨이 위태로워지는 일을 뚫고 — 그 결백을 밝혀 주어야 할 내 사람이었다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["My position is defined—my motives are acknowledged.", "내 처지는 분명해졌고, 내 동기도 밝혀졌다."], ["The story of Marian and the story of Laura must come next.", "다음은 매리언의 이야기와 로라의 이야기가 올 차례다."]], [["I shall relate both narratives, not in the words (often interrupted, often inevitably confused) of the speakers themselves, but in the words of the brief, plain, studiously simple abstract which I committed to writing for my own guidance, and for the guidance of my legal adviser.", "나는 두 사람의 이야기를 그들 자신이 한 말 — 자주 끊기고 또 어쩔 수 없이 자주 뒤엉킨 그 말 — 그대로 옮기지 않고, 나 자신의 지침으로 삼고 또 내 법률 고문의 지침으로 삼기 위해 적어 두었던, 짧고 담백하며 애써 단순하게 다듬은 요약문의 말로 옮기려 한다."], ["So the tangled web will be most speedily and most intelligibly unrolled.", "그래야 뒤얽힌 실타래가 가장 빠르고 가장 알아보기 쉽게 풀릴 것이다."]], [["The story of Marian begins where the narrative of the housekeeper at Blackwater Park left off.", "매리언의 이야기는 블랙워터 파크 가정부의 서술이 끝난 지점에서 시작된다."]], [["On Lady Glyde’s departure from her husband’s house, the fact of that departure, and the necessary statement of the circumstances under which it had taken place, were communicated to Miss Halcombe by the housekeeper.", "글라이드 부인이 남편의 집을 떠났을 때, 그 떠남이라는 사실과 그것이 이루어진 정황에 대한 필요한 설명은 가정부를 통해 홀콤 양에게 전해졌다."], ["It was not till some days afterwards (how many days exactly, Mrs. Michelson, in the absence of any written memorandum on the subject, could not undertake to say) that a letter arrived from Madame Fosco announcing Lady Glyde’s sudden death in Count Fosco’s house.", "포스코 백작의 집에서 글라이드 부인이 갑작스레 세상을 떠났다는 것을 알리는 편지가 포스코 백작부인에게서 온 것은 그로부터 며칠이 지난 뒤였다(정확히 며칠 뒤였는지는, 그 문제에 관해 적어 둔 기록이 없는 터라 마이컬슨 부인도 단언하지 못했다)."], ["The letter avoided mentioning dates, and left it to Mrs. Michelson’s discretion to break the news at once to Miss Halcombe, or to defer doing so until that lady’s health should be more firmly established.", "편지는 날짜를 밝히기를 피했고, 그 소식을 곧바로 홀콤 양에게 알릴지 아니면 그분의 건강이 좀 더 확실히 회복될 때까지 미룰지는 마이컬슨 부인의 판단에 맡겼다."]], [["Having consulted Mr. Dawson (who had been himself delayed, by ill health, in resuming his attendance at Blackwater Park), Mrs.", "마이컬슨 부인은 도슨 의사와 상의한 뒤 — 그 역시 병 때문에 블랙워터 파크로 다시 왕진 나오는 일이 늦어지고 있었다 — 의사의 조언에 따라, 그리고 의사가 있는 자리에서 그 소식을 전했다."], ["Michelson, by the doctor’s advice, and in the doctor’s presence, communicated the news, either on the day when the letter was received, or on the day after.", "편지가 온 그날이었거나 그 이튿날이었다."], ["It is not necessary to dwell here upon the effect which the intelligence of Lady Glyde’s sudden death produced on her sister.", "글라이드 부인의 급작스러운 죽음이라는 소식이 그 언니에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 여기서 길게 이야기할 필요가 없다."], ["It is only useful to the present purpose to say that she was not able to travel for more than three weeks afterwards.", "지금의 목적에 소용되는 것은 그 뒤로 삼 주가 넘도록 그녀가 여행을 할 수 없었다는 사실뿐이다."], ["At the end of that time she proceeded to London accompanied by the housekeeper.", "그 기간이 끝나자 그녀는 가정부와 함께 런던으로 향했다."], ["They parted there—Mrs. Michelson previously informing Miss Halcombe of her address, in case they might wish to communicate at a future period.", "두 사람은 거기서 헤어졌는데, 그에 앞서 마이컬슨 부인은 훗날 서로 연락할 일이 있을지 모른다며 자기 주소를 홀콤 양에게 알려 주었다."]], [["On parting with the housekeeper Miss Halcombe went at once to the office of Messrs. Gilmore & Kyrle to consult with the latter gentleman in Mr.", "가정부와 헤어진 뒤 홀콤 양은 곧바로 길모어 앤드 카일 사무소로 가서, 길모어 씨가 자리에 없는 터라 카일 씨와 상의했다."], ["Gilmore’s absence. She mentioned to Mr.", "그녀는 다른 누구에게도 — 마이컬슨 부인에게조차 — 감추는 편이 낫다고 여겼던 것을 카일 씨에게는 털어놓았다."], ["Kyrle what she had thought it desirable to conceal from every one else (Mrs. Michelson included)—her suspicion of the circumstances under which Lady Glyde was said to have met her death.", "곧 글라이드 부인이 죽음을 맞았다고 하는 그 정황에 대한 의심이었다."], ["Mr. Kyrle, who had previously given friendly proof of his anxiety to serve Miss Halcombe, at once undertook to make such inquiries as the delicate and dangerous nature of the investigation proposed to him would permit.", "전에도 홀콤 양을 돕고자 하는 마음을 우정 어린 태도로 보여 준 바 있는 카일 씨는, 자기에게 맡겨진 그 조사의 미묘하고 위험한 성격이 허락하는 한도 안에서 알아보겠노라고 곧바로 나섰다."]], [["To exhaust this part of the subject before going farther, it may be mentioned that Count Fosco offered every facility to Mr. Kyrle, on that gentleman’s stating that he was sent by Miss Halcombe to collect such particulars as had not yet reached her of Lady Glyde’s decease.", "더 나아가기 전에 이 대목을 마저 정리해 두자면, 카일 씨가 글라이드 부인의 별세에 관해 아직 홀콤 양에게 전해지지 않은 자세한 사정을 알아 오라는 부탁을 받고 왔다고 밝히자 포스코 백작이 온갖 편의를 다 제공했다는 사실은 언급해 둘 만하다."], ["Mr. Kyrle was placed in communication with the medical man, Mr.", "카일 씨는 의사인 구드리크 선생, 그리고 두 하인과 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다."], ["Goodricke, and with the two servants. In the absence of any means of ascertaining the exact date of Lady Glyde’s departure from Blackwater Park, the result of the doctor’s and the servants’ evidence, and of the volunteered statements of Count Fosco and his wife, was conclusive to the mind of Mr.", "글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 정확한 날짜를 확인할 방도가 전혀 없는 상황에서, 의사와 하인들의 증언, 그리고 포스코 백작 부부가 자진해서 내놓은 진술이 낳은 결과는 카일 씨의 마음속에서 결정적이었다."], ["Kyrle. He could only assume that the intensity of Miss Halcombe’s suffering, under the loss of her sister, had misled her judgment in a most deplorable manner, and he wrote her word that the shocking suspicion to which she had alluded in his presence was, in his opinion, destitute of the smallest fragment of foundation in truth.", "그는 동생을 잃은 슬픔이 너무도 극심한 나머지 홀콤 양의 판단이 참으로 딱한 방향으로 흐려진 것이라고밖에 볼 수 없었고, 자기 앞에서 그녀가 내비쳤던 그 끔찍한 의심에는 자기 소견으로는 진실에 근거한 구석이 티끌만큼도 없다고 편지에 적어 보냈다."], ["Thus the investigation by Mr. Gilmore’s partner began and ended.", "이렇게 해서 길모어 씨 동업자의 조사는 시작되자마자 끝나 버렸다."]], [["Meanwhile, Miss Halcombe had returned to Limmeridge House, and had there collected all the additional information which she was able to obtain.", "그동안 홀콤 양은 리머리지 하우스로 돌아가, 거기서 얻어 낼 수 있는 모든 추가 정보를 모아 두었다."]], [["Mr. Fairlie had received his first intimation of his niece’s death from his sister, Madame Fosco, this letter also not containing any exact reference to dates.", "페얼리 씨는 조카딸의 죽음에 대한 첫 기별을 누이인 포스코 백작부인에게서 받았는데, 이 편지에도 날짜에 대한 정확한 언급은 없었다."], ["He had sanctioned his sister’s proposal that the deceased lady should be laid in her mother’s grave in Limmeridge churchyard.", "그는 세상을 떠난 부인을 리머리지 교회 묘지의 어머니 무덤에 함께 모시자는 누이의 제안을 승낙했다."], ["Count Fosco had accompanied the remains to Cumberland, and had attended the funeral at Limmeridge, which took place on the 30th of July.", "포스코 백작은 유해를 모시고 컴벌랜드까지 따라와, 7월 30일에 리머리지에서 치러진 장례에 참석했다."], ["It was followed, as a mark of respect, by all the inhabitants of the village and the neighbourhood.", "그 뒤로는 마을과 인근의 주민 전부가 예를 표하며 뒤를 따랐다."], ["On the next day the inscription (originally drawn out, it was said, by the aunt of the deceased lady, and submitted for approval to her brother, Mr. Fairlie) was engraved on one side of the monument over the tomb.", "이튿날에는 비문이 — 처음에는 고인의 고모가 지어서 그 오라비인 페얼리 씨에게 승낙을 받으려고 보냈다고 한다 — 무덤 위 기념비의 한 면에 새겨졌다."]], [["On the day of the funeral, and for one day after it, Count Fosco had been received as a guest at Limmeridge House, but no interview had taken place between Mr. Fairlie and himself, by the former gentleman’s desire.", "장례가 있던 날과 그 다음 하루 동안 포스코 백작은 리머리지 하우스에 손님으로 머물렀으나, 페얼리 씨의 뜻에 따라 두 사람이 얼굴을 맞대는 일은 없었다."], ["They had communicated by writing, and through this medium Count Fosco had made Mr. Fairlie acquainted with the details of his niece’s last illness and death.", "두 사람은 글로 연락을 주고받았고, 그 방법으로 포스코 백작은 페얼리 씨에게 조카딸의 마지막 병세와 죽음에 대한 자세한 사정을 알렸다."], ["The letter presenting this information added no new facts to the facts already known, but one very remarkable paragraph was contained in the postscript.", "이 사연을 전한 편지는 이미 알려진 사실에 새로운 사실을 보태지 않았지만, 추신에는 매우 주목할 만한 한 대목이 들어 있었다."], ["It referred to Anne Catherick.", "앤 캐서릭에 관한 것이었다."]], [["The substance of the paragraph in question was as follows—", "문제의 그 대목이 담고 있는 내용은 다음과 같았다—"]], [["It first informed Mr. Fairlie that Anne Catherick (of whom he might hear full particulars from Miss Halcombe when she reached Limmeridge) had been traced and recovered in the neighbourhood of Blackwater Park, and had been for the second time placed under the charge of the medical man from whose custody she had once escaped.", "그것은 먼저 페얼리 씨에게, 앤 캐서릭이 — 그에 관한 자세한 사정은 홀콤 양이 리머리지에 닿으면 그녀에게서 들을 수 있을 것이라며 — 블랙워터 파크 인근에서 행방이 밝혀져 붙잡혔고, 한때 그 손에서 달아났던 그 의사의 관리 아래 두 번째로 맡겨졌다는 것을 알렸다."]], [["This was the first part of the postscript.", "이것이 추신의 첫 부분이었다."], ["The second part warned Mr. Fairlie that Anne Catherick’s mental malady had been aggravated by her long freedom from control, and that the insane hatred and distrust of Sir Percival Glyde, which had been one of her most marked delusions in former times, still existed under a newly-acquired form.", "두 번째 부분은 페얼리 씨에게, 앤 캐서릭의 정신 질환이 오랫동안 통제 없이 지낸 탓에 악화되었으며, 예전부터 그 여자의 가장 두드러진 망상 가운데 하나였던 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대한 광적인 증오와 불신이 새로 얻은 형태로 여전히 남아 있음을 일러 주었다."], ["The unfortunate woman’s last idea in connection with Sir Percival was the idea of annoying and distressing him, and of elevating herself, as she supposed, in the estimation of the patients and nurses, by assuming the character of his deceased wife, the scheme of this personation having evidently occurred to her after a stolen interview which she had succeeded in obtaining with Lady Glyde, and at which she had observed the extraordinary accidental likeness between the deceased lady and herself.", "그 불행한 여자가 퍼시벌 경과 관련해 마지막으로 품은 생각은, 그를 성가시게 하고 괴롭히며 아울러 그의 죽은 아내 행세를 함으로써 환자들과 간호인들 사이에서 제 격이 올라간다고 여기는 것이었다. 이렇게 남의 신분을 사칭하려는 계획은 그 여자가 용케 성사시킨 글라이드 부인과의 은밀한 만남 뒤에 떠오른 것이 분명한데, 그때 그 여자는 세상을 떠난 부인과 자신이 우연히도 놀랍게 닮았다는 것을 알아차렸던 것이다."], ["It was to the last degree improbable that she would succeed a second time in escaping from the Asylum, but it was just possible she might find some means of annoying the late Lady Glyde’s relatives with letters, and in that case Mr. Fairlie was warned beforehand how to receive them.", "그 여자가 두 번째로 정신병원에서 달아나는 데 성공할 가능성은 지극히 희박하지만, 어떻게든 방도를 찾아 고 글라이드 부인의 친척들을 편지로 성가시게 굴지도 모르니, 그런 경우 어떻게 받아들여야 할지를 페얼리 씨에게 미리 일러 둔다는 것이었다."]], [["The postscript, expressed in these terms, was shown to Miss Halcombe when she arrived at Limmeridge.", "이런 말로 적힌 추신은 홀콤 양이 리머리지에 도착했을 때 그녀에게 보여졌다."], ["There were also placed in her possession the clothes Lady Glyde had worn, and the other effects she had brought with her to her aunt’s house.", "아울러 글라이드 부인이 입고 있던 옷과, 고모의 집에 가지고 갔던 그 밖의 소지품도 그녀 손에 넘겨졌다."], ["They had been carefully collected and sent to Cumberland by Madame Fosco.", "그것들은 포스코 백작부인이 정성껏 챙겨서 컴벌랜드로 보낸 것이었다."]], [["Such was the posture of affairs when Miss Halcombe reached Limmeridge in the early part of September.", "홀콤 양이 9월 초에 리머리지에 닿았을 때 사정은 이러했다."]], [["Shortly afterwards she was confined to her room by a relapse, her weakened physical energies giving way under the severe mental affliction from which she was now suffering.", "그 직후 그녀는 병이 도져 방에 갇혀 지냈다. 지금 겪고 있는 심한 마음의 고통 아래 약해진 몸의 기력이 무너진 것이었다."], ["On getting stronger again, in a month’s time, her suspicion of the circumstances described as attending her sister’s death still remained unshaken.", "한 달 만에 다시 기운을 차렸을 때에도, 동생의 죽음에 따랐다고 하는 그 정황에 대한 의심은 여전히 흔들림이 없었다."], ["She had heard nothing in the interim of Sir Percival Glyde, but letters had reached her from Madame Fosco, making the most affectionate inquiries on the part of her husband and herself.", "그사이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 대해서는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했으나, 포스코 백작부인에게서는 남편과 자신을 대신해 더없이 다정하게 안부를 묻는 편지가 여러 통 왔다."], ["Instead of answering these letters, Miss Halcombe caused the house in St. John’s Wood, and the proceedings of its inmates, to be privately watched.", "홀콤 양은 그 편지들에 답하는 대신, 세인트존스우드의 그 집과 거기 사는 사람들의 동태를 남몰래 살피게 했다."]], [["Nothing doubtful was discovered.", "의심스러운 것은 아무것도 나오지 않았다."], ["The same result attended the next investigations, which were secretly instituted on the subject of Mrs. Rubelle.", "뒤이어 루벨 부인에 관해 몰래 진행한 조사도 같은 결과였다."], ["She had arrived in London about six months before with her husband.", "그 여자는 여섯 달쯤 전에 남편과 함께 런던에 왔다."], ["They had come from Lyons, and they had taken a house in the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, to be fitted up as a boarding-house for foreigners, who were expected to visit England in large numbers to see the Exhibition of 1851.", "두 사람은 리옹에서 왔고, 1851년 박람회를 보러 영국에 많이 몰려올 것으로 예상되는 외국인들을 위한 하숙집으로 꾸미려고 레스터 광장 근처에 집을 한 채 얻었다."], ["Nothing was known against husband or wife in the neighbourhood.", "동네에서 남편이나 아내에 대해 나쁘게 알려진 것은 없었다."], ["They were quiet people, and they had paid their way honestly up to the present time.", "조용한 사람들이었고, 지금까지 셈은 정직하게 치러 왔다."], ["The final inquiries related to Sir Percival Glyde.", "마지막 조사는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에 관한 것이었다."], ["He was settled in Paris, and living there quietly in a small circle of English and French friends.", "그는 파리에 자리를 잡고 영국인과 프랑스인 친구 몇몇의 좁은 무리 안에서 조용히 지내고 있었다."]], [["Foiled at all points, but still not able to rest, Miss Halcombe next determined to visit the Asylum in which she then supposed Anne Catherick to be for the second time confined.", "가는 곳마다 막혔으나 그래도 가만있을 수는 없었기에, 홀콤 양은 다음으로 앤 캐서릭이 두 번째로 갇혀 있으리라고 그때 생각하고 있던 정신병원을 찾아가 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["She had felt a strong curiosity about the woman in former days, and she was now doubly interested—first, in ascertaining whether the report of Anne Catherick’s attempted personation of Lady Glyde was true, and secondly (if it proved to be true), in discovering for herself what the poor creature’s real motives were for attempting the deceit.", "그녀는 예전부터 그 여자에 대해 강한 호기심을 느껴 왔고, 이제는 그 관심이 갑절이 되었다. 첫째로는 앤 캐서릭이 글라이드 부인 행세를 하려 했다는 보고가 사실인지 확인하기 위해서였고, 둘째로는 (그것이 사실로 드러난다면) 그 가엾은 것이 그런 속임수를 꾀한 진짜 동기가 무엇인지 제 눈으로 알아내기 위해서였다."]], [["Although Count Fosco’s letter to Mr. Fairlie did not mention the address of the Asylum, that important omission cast no difficulties in Miss Halcombe’s way. When Mr. Hartright had met Anne Catherick at Limmeridge, she had informed him of the locality in which the house was situated, and Miss Halcombe had noted down the direction in her diary, with all the other particulars of the interview exactly as she heard them from Mr. Hartright’s own lips. Accordingly she looked back at the entry and extracted the address—furnished herself with the Count’s letter to Mr. Fairlie as a species of credential which might be useful to her, and started by herself for the Asylum on the eleventh of October.", "포스코 백작이 페얼리 씨에게 보낸 편지에 정신병원 주소가 적혀 있지는 않았지만, 그 중요한 누락도 홀콤 양에게는 아무 걸림돌이 되지 못했다. 하트라이트 씨가 리머리지에서 앤 캐서릭을 만났을 때 그 여자가 그 집이 있는 고장을 그에게 일러 주었고, 홀콤 양은 그 만남의 다른 모든 세부 사항과 함께 하트라이트 씨의 입에서 나온 그대로 그 방향을 일기에 적어 두었던 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 기록을 다시 들춰 주소를 찾아내고, 쓸모가 있을지 모를 일종의 증빙으로 백작이 페얼리 씨에게 보낸 편지를 챙긴 다음, 10월 11일에 혼자서 정신병원을 향해 출발했다."]], [["She passed the night of the eleventh in London.", "그녀는 11일 밤을 런던에서 보냈다."], ["It had been her intention to sleep at the house inhabited by Lady Glyde’s old governess, but Mrs. Vesey’s agitation at the sight of her lost pupil’s nearest and dearest friend was so distressing that Miss Halcombe considerately refrained from remaining in her presence, and removed to a respectable boarding-house in the neighbourhood, recommended by Mrs.", "애초에는 글라이드 부인의 옛 가정교사가 사는 집에서 묵을 작정이었으나, 잃어버린 제자의 가장 가깝고 가장 소중한 벗을 본 베지 부인이 어찌나 마음을 못 가누던지 보기가 딱해서, 홀콤 양은 그 앞에 더 머물기를 삼가고 베지 부인의 결혼한 여동생이 일러 준 근처의 점잖은 하숙집으로 옮겼다."], ["Vesey’s married sister. The next day she proceeded to the Asylum, which was situated not far from London on the northern side of the metropolis.", "이튿날 그녀는 정신병원으로 향했는데, 그곳은 런던에서 그리 멀지 않은 이 대도시의 북쪽에 있었다."]], [["She was immediately admitted to see the proprietor.", "그녀는 곧바로 원장을 만날 수 있게 안내되었다."]], [["At first he appeared to be decidedly unwilling to let her communicate with his patient.", "처음에 그는 자기 환자와 그녀가 이야기를 나누게 하는 것을 딱 잘라 꺼리는 눈치였다."], ["But on her showing him the postscript to Count Fosco’s letter—on her reminding him that she was the “Miss Halcombe” there referred to—that she was a near relative of the deceased Lady Glyde—and that she was therefore naturally interested, for family reasons, in observing for herself the extent of Anne Catherick’s delusion in relation to her late sister—the tone and manner of the owner of the Asylum altered, and he withdrew his objections.", "그러나 그녀가 포스코 백작 편지의 추신을 보이고 — 거기 언급된 “홀콤 양”이 바로 자기라는 것, 자기가 세상을 떠난 글라이드 부인의 가까운 친척이라는 것, 그러니 집안 사정상 죽은 동생과 관련한 앤 캐서릭의 망상이 어느 정도인지 직접 살펴보고 싶은 것이 당연하다는 것을 일깨우자 — 정신병원 주인의 어조와 태도가 달라지면서 그는 반대를 거두었다."], ["He probably felt that a continued refusal, under these circumstances, would not only be an act of discourtesy in itself, but would also imply that the proceedings in his establishment were not of a nature to bear investigation by respectable strangers.", "아마 그는 이런 상황에서 계속 거절하는 것이 그 자체로 무례한 짓일 뿐 아니라, 제 시설에서 벌어지는 일이 점잖은 외부인의 조사를 견딜 만한 성질이 못 된다는 뜻으로 비칠 수 있음을 느꼈을 것이다."]], [["Miss Halcombe’s own impression was that the owner of the Asylum had not been received into the confidence of Sir Percival and the Count.", "홀콤 양 자신의 인상으로는, 정신병원 주인은 퍼시벌 경과 백작에게서 속내를 나눠 받은 사람이 아니었다."], ["His consenting at all to let her visit his patient seemed to afford one proof of this, and his readiness in making admissions which could scarcely have escaped the lips of an accomplice, certainly appeared to furnish another.", "자기 환자를 만나게 해 주기로 승낙한 것 자체가 그 한 가지 증거인 듯했고, 공범이라면 좀처럼 입 밖에 낼 수 없었을 이야기를 선선히 털어놓은 것도 분명 또 하나의 증거가 되는 듯했다."]], [["For example, in the course of the introductory conversation which took place, he informed Miss Halcombe that Anne Catherick had been brought back to him with the necessary order and certificates by Count Fosco on the twenty-seventh of July—the Count also producing a letter of explanations and instructions signed by Sir Percival Glyde.", "이를테면 처음에 나눈 대화 도중에 그는 홀콤 양에게, 앤 캐서릭이 7월 27일 포스코 백작의 손에 필요한 지시서와 증명서와 함께 자기에게 도로 맡겨졌다고 알려 주었다. 백작은 아울러 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 서명한 설명과 지시의 편지도 내놓았다는 것이었다."], ["On receiving his inmate again, the proprietor of the Asylum acknowledged that he had observed some curious personal changes in her.", "그 수용자를 다시 받았을 때 정신병원 주인은 그 여자에게서 몇 가지 묘한 신체의 변화를 알아차렸다고 인정했다."], ["Such changes no doubt were not without precedent in his experience of persons mentally afflicted.", "물론 그런 변화가 정신을 앓는 사람들을 겪어 온 그의 경험에서 전례 없는 일은 아니었다."], ["Insane people were often at one time, outwardly as well as inwardly, unlike what they were at another—the change from better to worse, or from worse to better, in the madness having a necessary tendency to produce alterations of appearance externally.", "미친 사람들은 흔히 겉으로 보나 속으로 보나 때마다 딴사람 같기 마련이니, 광기가 나아지든 더해지든 그 변화는 겉모습에도 달라짐을 낳게 되어 있었다."], ["He allowed for these, and he allowed also for the modification in the form of Anne Catherick’s delusion, which was reflected no doubt in her manner and expression.", "그는 그런 점을 감안했고, 앤 캐서릭의 망상이 형태를 바꾼 것도 감안했는데, 그 역시 그 여자의 태도와 표정에 나타났을 터였다."], ["But he was still perplexed at times by certain differences between his patient before she had escaped and his patient since she had been brought back.", "그럼에도 그는 달아나기 전의 환자와 되돌아온 뒤의 환자 사이에 있는 어떤 차이 때문에 이따금 여전히 어리둥절해했다."], ["Those differences were too minute to be described.", "그 차이란 말로 설명하기에는 너무 미세한 것이었다."], ["He could not say of course that she was absolutely altered in height or shape or complexion, or in the colour of her hair and eyes, or in the general form of her face—the change was something that he felt more than something that he saw.", "물론 그 여자의 키나 몸매나 안색이, 또는 머리카락과 눈의 빛깔이, 또는 얼굴의 전체적인 생김이 아주 달라졌다고는 말할 수 없었다. 그 변화는 눈에 보이는 무엇이라기보다 느껴지는 무엇이었다."], ["In short, the case had been a puzzle from the first, and one more perplexity was added to it now.", "요컨대 이 환자는 처음부터 수수께끼였는데, 이제 거기 수수께끼가 하나 더 보태진 셈이었다."]], [["It cannot be said that this conversation led to the result of even partially preparing Miss Halcombe’s mind for what was to come.", "이 대화가 앞으로 닥칠 일에 홀콤 양의 마음을 조금이나마 대비시켜 주는 결과를 낳았다고는 할 수 없다."], ["But it produced, nevertheless, a very serious effect upon her.", "그러나 그럼에도 그것은 그녀에게 아주 심각한 영향을 미쳤다."], ["She was so completely unnerved by it, that some little time elapsed before she could summon composure enough to follow the proprietor of the Asylum to that part of the house in which the inmates were confined.", "그 때문에 어찌나 마음이 흐트러졌던지, 수용자들이 갇혀 있는 구역으로 정신병원 주인을 따라나설 만큼 침착을 되찾기까지 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다."]], [["On inquiry, it turned out that the supposed Anne Catherick was then taking exercise in the grounds attached to the establishment.", "물어보니 앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자는 그때 시설에 딸린 뜰에서 산책을 하고 있다고 했다."], ["One of the nurses volunteered to conduct Miss Halcombe to the place, the proprietor of the Asylum remaining in the house for a few minutes to attend to a case which required his services, and then engaging to join his visitor in the grounds.", "간호인 하나가 홀콤 양을 그리로 데려다주겠다고 나섰고, 정신병원 주인은 손이 필요한 환자를 보러 잠깐 집 안에 남았다가 뜰에서 손님과 합류하겠다고 했다."]], [["The nurse led Miss Halcombe to a distant part of the property, which was prettily laid out, and after looking about her a little, turned into a turf walk, shaded by a shrubbery on either side.", "간호인은 홀콤 양을 부지의 멀찍한 곳으로 데려갔다. 예쁘게 꾸며 놓은 자리였는데, 잠시 둘레를 살피더니 양옆으로 관목이 그늘을 드리운 잔디 길로 접어들었다."], ["About half-way down this walk two women were slowly approaching.", "그 길 중간쯤에서 여자 둘이 천천히 다가오고 있었다. 간호인이 그들을 가리키며 말했다."], ["The nurse pointed to them and said, “There is Anne Catherick, ma’am, with the attendant who waits on her.", "“저기 앤 캐서릭이 있습니다, 부인. 옆에 있는 사람은 그 여자를 돌보는 사람이고요."], ["The attendant will answer any questions you wish to put.”", "물어보실 것이 있으면 그 사람이 답해 드릴 겁니다.”"], ["With those words the nurse left her to return to the duties of the house.", "그 말을 남기고 간호인은 집안일로 돌아가려고 그녀를 두고 갔다."]], [["Miss Halcombe advanced on her side, and the women advanced on theirs.", "홀콤 양은 그쪽으로 나아갔고, 두 여자는 이쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["When they were within a dozen paces of each other, one of the women stopped for an instant, looked eagerly at the strange lady, shook off the nurse’s grasp on her, and the next moment rushed into Miss Halcombe’s arms.", "서로 열두어 걸음 안까지 가까워졌을 때, 여자 하나가 문득 걸음을 멈추고 낯선 부인을 뚫어져라 바라보더니, 저를 붙든 간호인의 손을 뿌리치고 다음 순간 홀콤 양의 품으로 달려들었다."], ["In that moment Miss Halcombe recognised her sister—recognised the dead-alive.", "그 순간 홀콤 양은 제 동생을 알아보았다. 죽었다던 산 사람을 알아본 것이다."]], [["Fortunately for the success of the measures taken subsequently, no one was present at that moment but the nurse.", "그 뒤에 취한 조치들이 성공한 데는 다행스럽게도, 그 순간 그 자리에는 간호인 말고 아무도 없었다."], ["She was a young woman, and she was so startled that she was at first quite incapable of interfering.", "젊은 여자였던 그 간호인은 어찌나 놀랐던지 처음에는 도무지 손을 쓰지 못했다."], ["When she was able to do so her whole services were required by Miss Halcombe, who had for the moment sunk altogether in the effort to keep her own senses under the shock of the discovery.", "겨우 손을 쓸 수 있게 되었을 때는 그 시중이 온통 홀콤 양에게 필요했으니, 이 발견의 충격 아래 제정신을 붙들려고 애쓰다 그만 잠시 폭삭 주저앉아 버렸던 것이다."], ["After waiting a few minutes in the fresh air and the cool shade, her natural energy and courage helped her a little, and she became sufficiently mistress of herself to feel the necessity of recalling her presence of mind for her unfortunate sister’s sake.", "맑은 공기와 서늘한 그늘에서 몇 분을 보내자 타고난 기력과 용기가 그녀를 조금 일으켜 세웠고, 그녀는 불행한 동생을 위해서라도 침착함을 되찾아야 한다는 것을 느낄 만큼 자신을 추스를 수 있었다."]], [["She obtained permission to speak alone with the patient, on condition that they both remained well within the nurse’s view.", "그녀는 두 사람 다 간호인의 시야 안에 확실히 머문다는 조건으로 환자와 단둘이 이야기할 허락을 얻었다."], ["There was no time for questions—there was only time for Miss Halcombe to impress on the unhappy lady the necessity of controlling herself, and to assure her of immediate help and rescue if she did so.", "이것저것 물어볼 겨를은 없었다. 다만 그 가엾은 부인에게 스스로를 다잡아야 한다는 것을 새겨 주고, 그렇게만 하면 곧 도움과 구출이 있으리라고 다짐해 줄 겨를만 있었다."], ["The prospect of escaping from the Asylum by obedience to her sister’s directions was sufficient to quiet Lady Glyde, and to make her understand what was required of her.", "언니가 시키는 대로 하면 이 정신병원에서 벗어날 수 있다는 전망은 글라이드 부인을 진정시키고 제게 무엇이 요구되는지 알아듣게 하기에 충분했다."], ["Miss Halcombe next returned to the nurse, placed all the gold she then had in her pocket (three sovereigns) in the nurse’s hands, and asked when and where she could speak to her alone.", "이어 홀콤 양은 간호인에게로 돌아가, 그때 주머니에 있던 금화를 몽땅(소브린 금화 세 닢이었다) 간호인의 손에 쥐여 주고는 언제 어디서 단둘이 이야기할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["The woman was at first surprised and distrustful.", "그 여자는 처음에 놀라며 미심쩍어했다."], ["But on Miss Halcombe’s declaring that she only wanted to put some questions which she was too much agitated to ask at that moment, and that she had no intention of misleading the nurse into any dereliction of duty, the woman took the money, and proposed three o’clock on the next day as the time for the interview.", "그러나 홀콤 양이, 그저 지금은 너무 마음이 흔들려 물어보지 못한 몇 가지를 묻고 싶을 뿐이며 간호인을 꾀어 직분을 저버리게 할 뜻은 조금도 없다고 밝히자, 여자는 돈을 받고 이튿날 세 시를 만날 시각으로 내놓았다."], ["She might then slip out for half an hour, after the patients had dined, and she would meet the lady in a retired place, outside the high north wall which screened the grounds of the house.", "그때라면 환자들 식사가 끝난 뒤라 반 시간쯤 빠져나올 수 있으니, 이 집의 뜰을 가리고 있는 북쪽 높은 담 바깥의 한갓진 자리에서 만나 뵙겠다는 것이었다."], ["Miss Halcombe had only time to assent, and to whisper to her sister that she should hear from her on the next day, when the proprietor of the Asylum joined them.", "홀콤 양은 그러겠노라고 답하고 동생에게 내일 소식을 듣게 될 것이라고 속삭일 겨를밖에 없었는데, 그때 정신병원 주인이 그들에게 다가왔다."], ["He noticed his visitor’s agitation, which Miss Halcombe accounted for by saying that her interview with Anne Catherick had a little startled her at first.", "그는 손님이 흔들려 있는 것을 알아챘고, 홀콤 양은 앤 캐서릭과 만난 것이 처음에는 좀 놀라웠던 탓이라고 둘러댔다."], ["She took her leave as soon after as possible—that is to say, as soon as she could summon courage to force herself from the presence of her unfortunate sister.", "그녀는 되도록 곧 자리를 떴다. 다시 말해 불행한 동생 앞에서 억지로 몸을 떼어 낼 용기를 낼 수 있게 되자마자 그렇게 했다."]], [["A very little reflection, when the capacity to reflect returned, convinced her that any attempt to identify Lady Glyde and to rescue her by legal means, would, even if successful, involve a delay that might be fatal to her sister’s intellects, which were shaken already by the horror of the situation to which she had been consigned.", "생각할 힘이 돌아오자, 조금만 헤아려 보아도 분명해졌다. 글라이드 부인의 신원을 밝히고 법의 힘으로 그녀를 구해 내려는 어떤 시도든, 설령 성공한다 해도 시간을 끌 수밖에 없을 터인데, 그 지체는 이미 제가 내던져진 처지의 참담함에 흔들려 버린 동생의 정신에 치명적일 수 있었다."], ["By the time Miss Halcombe had got back to London, she had determined to effect Lady Glyde’s escape privately, by means of the nurse.", "홀콤 양은 런던으로 돌아왔을 무렵 그 간호인을 통해 남몰래 글라이드 부인을 빼내기로 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["She went at once to her stockbroker, and sold out of the funds all the little property she possessed, amounting to rather less than seven hundred pounds.", "그녀는 곧장 증권 중개인에게 가서 자기가 가진 얼마 안 되는 재산을 공채에서 몽땅 팔아 치웠는데, 칠백 파운드가 채 안 되는 돈이었다."], ["Determined, if necessary, to pay the price of her sister’s liberty with every farthing she had in the world, she repaired the next day, having the whole sum about her in bank-notes, to her appointment outside the Asylum wall.", "필요하다면 이 세상에 가진 마지막 한 푼까지 동생의 자유를 사는 값으로 치르겠다고 작정한 그녀는, 이튿날 그 돈 전부를 지폐로 몸에 지니고 정신병원 담 바깥의 약속 자리로 갔다."]], [["The nurse was there.", "간호인은 와 있었다."], ["Miss Halcombe approached the subject cautiously by many preliminary questions.", "홀콤 양은 여러 가지 사전 질문으로 조심스럽게 본론에 다가갔다."], ["She discovered, among other particulars, that the nurse who had in former times attended on the true Anne Catherick had been held responsible (although she was not to blame for it) for the patient’s escape, and had lost her place in consequence.", "그녀는 다른 여러 사정과 함께, 예전에 진짜 앤 캐서릭을 맡았던 간호인이 그 환자의 탈출에 대해 (잘못이 없었는데도) 책임을 지고 그 자리를 잃었다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["The same penalty, it was added, would attach to the person then speaking to her, if the supposed Anne Catherick was missing a second time; and, moreover, the nurse in this case had an especial interest in keeping her place.", "앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자가 두 번째로 없어진다면 지금 자기와 이야기하고 있는 사람에게도 같은 벌이 떨어지리라는 말이 뒤따랐다. 게다가 이 간호인에게는 자리를 지켜야 할 각별한 사정이 있었다."], ["She was engaged to be married, and she and her future husband were waiting till they could save, together, between two and three hundred pounds to start in business.", "그녀는 약혼한 몸이었고, 장래의 남편과 둘이서 이삼백 파운드를 모아 장사를 시작하려고 기다리는 중이었다."], ["The nurse’s wages were good, and she might succeed, by strict economy, in contributing her small share towards the sum required in two years’ time.", "간호인의 급료는 넉넉했고, 알뜰히만 하면 이태 안에 필요한 돈에서 제 몫을 보탤 수 있을 성싶었다."]], [["On this hint Miss Halcombe spoke.", "이 실마리를 붙들고 홀콤 양은 입을 열었다."], ["She declared that the supposed Anne Catherick was nearly related to her, that she had been placed in the Asylum under a fatal mistake, and that the nurse would be doing a good and a Christian action in being the means of restoring them to one another.", "그녀는 앤 캐서릭이라는 그 여자가 자기와 가까운 친척이며, 돌이킬 수 없는 착오로 정신병원에 들어와 있는 것이니, 두 사람을 서로에게 돌려주는 매개가 되어 준다면 간호인은 착하고 그리스도인다운 일을 하는 셈이라고 밝혔다."], ["Before there was time to start a single objection, Miss Halcombe took four bank-notes of a hundred pounds each from her pocket-book, and offered them to the woman, as a compensation for the risk she was to run, and for the loss of her place.", "반대할 말이 한마디라도 나올 겨를도 없이, 홀콤 양은 지갑에서 백 파운드짜리 지폐 넉 장을 꺼내, 감수할 위험과 잃게 될 자리에 대한 보상으로 그 여자에게 내밀었다."]], [["The nurse hesitated, through sheer incredulity and surprise.", "간호인은 도무지 믿기지 않고 놀라워서 망설였다."], ["Miss Halcombe pressed the point on her firmly.", "홀콤 양은 그 점을 굳게 밀어붙였다."]], [["“You will be doing a good action,”", "“착한 일을 하시는 겁니다.”"], ["she repeated; “you will be helping the most injured and unhappy woman alive.", "그녀가 거듭 말했다. “세상에서 가장 심하게 짓밟히고 가장 불행한 여자를 돕는 일이에요."], ["There is your marriage portion for a reward.", "그 삯으로 결혼 밑천이 여기 있고요."], ["Bring her safely to me here, and I will put these four bank-notes into your hand before I claim her.”", "그 사람을 무사히 이리로 데려와 주시면, 제가 그 사람을 넘겨받기 전에 이 지폐 넉 장을 손에 쥐여 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["“Will you give me a letter saying those words, which I can show to my sweetheart when he asks how I got the money?”", "“그 돈을 어떻게 얻었느냐고 정혼한 사람이 물으면 보여 줄 수 있게, 그 말씀을 적은 편지를 주시겠어요?”"], ["inquired the woman.", "여자가 물었다."]], [["“I will bring the letter with me, ready written and signed,”", "“편지는 미리 써서 서명해 가지고 오겠습니다.”"], ["answered Miss Halcombe.", "홀콤 양이 대답했다."]], [["“Then I’ll risk it,”", "“그럼 한번 해 보겠어요.”"], ["said the nurse.", "간호인이 말했다."]], [["“When?”", "“언제요?”"]], [["“To-morrow.”", "“내일요.”"]], [["It was hastily agreed between them that Miss Halcombe should return early the next morning and wait out of sight among the trees—always, however, keeping near the quiet spot of ground under the north wall.", "두 사람은 홀콤 양이 이튿날 아침 일찍 다시 와서 나무들 사이에 몸을 숨기고 기다리되, 언제나 북쪽 담 아래의 그 한적한 자리 가까이에 머물러 있기로 서둘러 합의했다."], ["The nurse could fix no time for her appearance, caution requiring that she should wait and be guided by circumstances.", "간호인은 자기가 나타날 시각은 정하지 못했는데, 조심하자면 형편을 보아 가며 기다려야 했기 때문이다."], ["On that understanding they separated.", "그렇게 이야기를 맞추고 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["Miss Halcombe was at her place, with the promised letter and the promised bank-notes, before ten the next morning.", "홀콤 양은 이튿날 아침 열 시가 되기 전에 약속한 편지와 약속한 지폐를 가지고 그 자리에 나가 있었다."], ["She waited more than an hour and a half.", "그녀는 한 시간 반이 넘도록 기다렸다."], ["At the end of that time the nurse came quickly round the corner of the wall holding Lady Glyde by the arm.", "그 시간이 다 갈 무렵 간호인이 글라이드 부인의 팔을 붙든 채 담 모퉁이를 돌아 빠르게 나왔다."], ["The moment they met Miss Halcombe put the bank-notes and the letter into her hand, and the sisters were united again.", "만나는 순간 홀콤 양은 지폐와 편지를 그 손에 넘겨주었고, 자매는 다시 하나가 되었다."]], [["The nurse had dressed Lady Glyde, with excellent forethought, in a bonnet, veil, and shawl of her own.", "간호인은 매우 사려 깊게도 제 보닛과 베일과 숄을 글라이드 부인에게 입혀 놓았다."], ["Miss Halcombe only detained her to suggest a means of turning the pursuit in a false direction, when the escape was discovered at the Asylum.", "홀콤 양은 정신병원에서 탈출이 발각되었을 때 추적을 엉뚱한 방향으로 돌릴 방책을 일러 주려고 잠깐만 그녀를 붙들었다."], ["She was to go back to the house, to mention in the hearing of the other nurses that Anne Catherick had been inquiring latterly about the distance from London to Hampshire, to wait till the last moment, before discovery was inevitable, and then to give the alarm that Anne was missing.", "간호인은 집으로 돌아가 다른 간호인들이 듣는 데서 앤 캐서릭이 요즘 런던에서 햄프셔까지 얼마나 되느냐고 묻곤 했다는 말을 흘리고, 발각이 불가피해지는 마지막 순간까지 기다렸다가 앤이 없어졌다고 알려야 했다."], ["The supposed inquiries about Hampshire, when communicated to the owner of the Asylum, would lead him to imagine that his patient had returned to Blackwater Park, under the influence of the delusion which made her persist in asserting herself to be Lady Glyde, and the first pursuit would, in all probability, be turned in that direction.", "햄프셔에 관해 물었다고 하는 그 말이 정신병원 주인에게 전해지면, 그는 자기 환자가 스스로 글라이드 부인이라고 우기게 만드는 그 망상에 이끌려 블랙워터 파크로 돌아갔으리라고 짐작할 터이고, 첫 추적은 십중팔구 그쪽으로 향할 것이었다."]], [["The nurse consented to follow these suggestions, the more readily as they offered her the means of securing herself against any worse consequences than the loss of her place, by remaining in the Asylum, and so maintaining the appearance of innocence, at least.", "간호인은 이 방책을 따르기로 선선히 응했는데, 정신병원에 그대로 남아 적어도 겉으로는 아무것도 모르는 척함으로써 자리를 잃는 것보다 더 나쁜 결과는 면할 방도가 되어 주었기 때문이다."], ["She at once returned to the house, and Miss Halcombe lost no time in taking her sister back with her to London.", "그녀는 곧바로 집으로 돌아갔고, 홀콤 양은 지체 없이 동생을 데리고 런던으로 향했다."], ["They caught the afternoon train to Carlisle the same afternoon, and arrived at Limmeridge, without accident or difficulty of any kind, that night.", "두 사람은 그날 오후 칼라일행 오후 기차를 탔고, 그날 밤 아무런 사고나 어려움도 없이 리머리지에 닿았다."]], [["During the latter part of their journey they were alone in the carriage, and Miss Halcombe was able to collect such remembrances of the past as her sister’s confused and weakened memory was able to recall.", "여정의 뒷부분에서 두 사람은 객실에 단둘이 있었고, 홀콤 양은 동생의 어지럽고 약해진 기억이 되살릴 수 있는 만큼의 지난 일들을 그러모을 수 있었다."], ["The terrible story of the conspiracy so obtained was presented in fragments, sadly incoherent in themselves, and widely detached from each other.", "그렇게 얻어 낸 그 무서운 음모의 이야기는 조각조각 흩어진 채로, 그 자체로도 딱하리만치 앞뒤가 닿지 않고 서로 멀찍이 떨어진 모양으로 나타났다."], ["Imperfect as the revelation was, it must nevertheless be recorded here before this explanatory narrative closes with the events of the next day at Limmeridge House.", "드러난 것이 아무리 성기다 해도, 이 해명의 서술이 이튿날 리머리지 하우스에서 벌어진 일로 끝맺기 전에 여기 적어 두어야만 한다."]], [["Lady Glyde’s recollection of the events which followed her departure from Blackwater Park began with her arrival at the London terminus of the South Western Railway.", "글라이드 부인이 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 뒤에 일어난 일들에 대한 기억은 사우스웨스턴 철도의 런던 종착역에 닿은 데서부터 시작되었다."], ["She had omitted to make a memorandum beforehand of the day on which she took the journey.", "그녀는 그 여행을 떠난 날짜를 미리 적어 두지 못했다."], ["All hope of fixing that important date by any evidence of hers, or of Mrs. Michelson’s, must be given up for lost.", "그 중요한 날짜를 그녀의 증언으로든 마이컬슨 부인의 증언으로든 확정하겠다는 희망은 모두 접어야 했다."]], [["On the arrival of the train at the platform Lady Glyde found Count Fosco waiting for her.", "기차가 승강장에 닿자 글라이드 부인은 포스코 백작이 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He was at the carriage door as soon as the porter could open it.", "짐꾼이 문을 열자마자 그는 객실 문 앞에 서 있었다."], ["The train was unusually crowded, and there was great confusion in getting the luggage.", "기차는 유난히 붐볐고 짐을 찾느라 몹시 혼잡했다."], ["Some person whom Count Fosco brought with him procured the luggage which belonged to Lady Glyde.", "포스코 백작이 데려온 어떤 사람이 글라이드 부인의 짐을 찾아왔다."], ["It was marked with her name.", "짐에는 그녀의 이름이 붙어 있었다."], ["She drove away alone with the Count in a vehicle which she did not particularly notice at the time.", "그녀는 그때 딱히 눈여겨보지 않은 마차를 타고 백작과 단둘이 떠났다."]], [["Her first question, on leaving the terminus, referred to Miss Halcombe.", "종착역을 떠나며 그녀가 맨 먼저 물은 것은 홀콤 양에 관한 것이었다."], ["The Count informed her that Miss Halcombe had not yet gone to Cumberland, after-consideration having caused him to doubt the prudence of her taking so long a journey without some days’ previous rest.", "백작은 홀콤 양이 아직 컴벌랜드로 가지 않았다면서, 다시 생각해 보니 며칠 미리 쉬지도 않고 그토록 긴 여행을 하는 것이 현명한지 의심스러웠기 때문이라고 알려 주었다."]], [["Lady Glyde next inquired whether her sister was then staying in the Count’s house.", "글라이드 부인은 이어서 언니가 그때 백작의 집에 머물고 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Her recollection of the answer was confused, her only distinct impression in relation to it being that the Count declared he was then taking her to see Miss Halcombe.", "그 대답에 대한 기억은 어지러웠고, 그와 관련해 또렷하게 남은 인상이라고는 지금 홀콤 양을 만나러 데려가는 길이라고 백작이 말했다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Lady Glyde’s experience of London was so limited that she could not tell, at the time, through what streets they were driving.", "글라이드 부인은 런던을 아는 것이 워낙 적어서 그때 어느 거리를 지나고 있는지 알 수 없었다."], ["But they never left the streets, and they never passed any gardens or trees.", "그러나 그들은 내내 거리를 벗어나지 않았고, 정원이나 나무 곁을 지나는 일도 없었다."], ["When the carriage stopped, it stopped in a small street behind a square—a square in which there were shops, and public buildings, and many people.", "마차가 멈춰 선 곳은 어느 광장 뒤편의 작은 거리였는데, 그 광장에는 가게와 공공건물이 있고 사람이 많았다."], ["From these recollections (of which Lady Glyde was certain) it seems quite clear that Count Fosco did not take her to his own residence in the suburb of St. John’s Wood.", "이런 기억들로 미루어 (그리고 이 점에 대해서는 글라이드 부인도 확신하고 있었으므로) 포스코 백작이 그녀를 교외 세인트 존스 우드의 제 집으로 데려간 것이 아님은 아주 분명해 보인다."]], [["They entered the house, and went upstairs to a back room, either on the first or second floor.", "두 사람은 그 집에 들어가 2층인지 3층인지의 뒤쪽 방으로 올라갔다."], ["The luggage was carefully brought in.", "짐도 조심스럽게 안으로 들여왔다."], ["A female servant opened the door, and a man with a dark beard, apparently a foreigner, met them in the hall, and with great politeness showed them the way upstairs.", "하녀 하나가 문을 열어 주었고, 검은 수염을 기른, 보아하니 외국인인 남자가 현관에서 그들을 맞아 아주 공손하게 위층으로 길을 안내했다."], ["In answer to Lady Glyde’s inquiries, the Count assured her that Miss Halcombe was in the house, and that she should be immediately informed of her sister’s arrival.", "글라이드 부인이 묻자 백작은 홀콤 양이 이 집 안에 있으며 동생이 왔다는 것을 곧 알리겠다고 다짐했다."], ["He and the foreigner then went away and left her by herself in the room.", "그러고 나서 그와 그 외국인은 그녀만 방에 남겨 두고 나가 버렸다."], ["It was poorly furnished as a sitting-room, and it looked out on the backs of houses.", "방은 응접실치고는 세간이 초라했고, 창은 다른 집들의 뒤편을 향하고 있었다."]], [["The place was remarkably quiet—no footsteps went up or down the stairs—she only heard in the room beneath her a dull, rumbling sound of men’s voices talking.", "그곳은 유난히 조용했다. 계단을 오르내리는 발소리도 없었고, 아래층 방에서 남자들의 목소리가 웅얼웅얼 낮게 울리는 것만 들릴 뿐이었다."], ["Before she had been long left alone the Count returned, to explain that Miss Halcombe was then taking rest, and could not be disturbed for a little while.", "혼자 남겨진 지 얼마 되지 않아 백작이 돌아와서는, 홀콤 양이 지금 쉬고 있어서 당분간 방해할 수 없다고 설명했다."], ["He was accompanied into the room by a gentleman (an Englishman), whom he begged to present as a friend of his.", "그는 어떤 신사(영국인이었다)와 함께 방에 들어왔는데, 자기 친구라며 소개하겠다고 청했다."]], [["After this singular introduction—in the course of which no names, to the best of Lady Glyde’s recollection, had been mentioned—she was left alone with the stranger.", "이 묘한 소개가 있고 나서 — 글라이드 부인의 기억으로는 그동안 이름은 하나도 나오지 않았다 — 그녀는 그 낯선 사람과 단둘이 남겨졌다."], ["He was perfectly civil, but he startled and confused her by some odd questions about herself, and by looking at her, while he asked them, in a strange manner.", "그는 더없이 정중했지만, 그녀 자신에 관한 이상한 질문을 몇 가지 던지고 그것을 물으면서 야릇한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보아 그녀를 놀라게 하고 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["After remaining a short time he went out, and a minute or two afterwards a second stranger—also an Englishman—came in.", "잠시 있다가 그가 나가더니, 일이 분 뒤에 또 다른 낯선 사람이 — 이 사람도 영국인이었다 — 들어왔다."], ["This person introduced himself as another friend of Count Fosco’s, and he, in his turn, looked at her very oddly, and asked some curious questions—never, as well as she could remember, addressing her by name, and going out again, after a little while, like the first man.", "이 사람 역시 포스코 백작의 또 다른 친구라고 자기를 소개하고는, 그도 제 나름대로 아주 야릇하게 그녀를 바라보며 이상한 질문을 몇 가지 던졌는데, 그녀가 기억하는 한 한 번도 이름을 불러 그녀를 부르지는 않았고, 앞사람과 마찬가지로 조금 있다가 다시 나갔다."], ["By this time she was so frightened about herself, and so uneasy about her sister, that she had thoughts of venturing downstairs again, and claiming the protection and assistance of the only woman she had seen in the house—the servant who answered the door.", "이쯤 되자 그녀는 제 신변이 몹시 무서워지고 언니의 일이 몹시 불안해져서, 다시 아래층으로 내려가 이 집에서 본 유일한 여자 — 문을 열어 준 그 하녀 — 에게 보호와 도움을 청해 볼까 하는 생각까지 했다."]], [["Just as she had risen from her chair, the Count came back into the room.", "그녀가 막 의자에서 일어섰을 때 백작이 다시 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["The moment he appeared she asked anxiously how long the meeting between her sister and herself was to be still delayed.", "그가 나타나자마자 그녀는 언니와 자기가 만나는 일이 얼마나 더 미뤄져야 하느냐고 초조하게 물었다."], ["At first he returned an evasive answer, but on being pressed, he acknowledged, with great apparent reluctance, that Miss Halcombe was by no means so well as he had hitherto represented her to be.", "처음에 그는 얼버무리는 대답을 했으나, 다그치자 몹시 내키지 않아 하는 기색으로 홀콤 양의 상태가 지금껏 자기가 전한 것만큼 결코 좋지 않다고 털어놓았다."], ["His tone and manner, in making this reply, so alarmed Lady Glyde, or rather so painfully increased the uneasiness which she had felt in the company of the two strangers, that a sudden faintness overcame her, and she was obliged to ask for a glass of water.", "이 대답을 하는 그의 어조와 태도가 어찌나 글라이드 부인을 놀라게 했는지, 아니 그보다도 두 낯선 사람과 함께 있으면서 느꼈던 불안을 어찌나 괴롭게 부풀려 놓았는지, 별안간 아뜩함이 그녀를 덮쳐 물 한 잔을 청할 수밖에 없었다."], ["The Count called from the door for water, and for a bottle of smelling-salts.", "백작은 문간에서 물과 각성제 병을 가져오라고 소리쳤다."], ["Both were brought in by the foreign-looking man with the beard.", "둘 다 수염을 기른 그 외국인 같은 남자가 들여왔다."], ["The water, when Lady Glyde attempted to drink it, had so strange a taste that it increased her faintness, and she hastily took the bottle of salts from Count Fosco, and smelt at it.", "글라이드 부인이 마시려 하니 물에서 어찌나 이상한 맛이 나던지 아뜩함이 더해졌고, 그녀는 서둘러 포스코 백작에게서 각성제 병을 받아 냄새를 맡았다."], ["Her head became giddy on the instant.", "그 순간 머리가 어질했다."], ["The Count caught the bottle as it dropped out of her hand, and the last impression of which she was conscious was that he held it to her nostrils again.", "손에서 떨어지는 병을 백작이 받아 들었고, 그녀가 의식한 마지막 인상은 그가 그것을 다시 그녀의 콧구멍에 갖다 댔다는 것이었다."]], [["From this point her recollections were found to be confused, fragmentary, and difficult to reconcile with any reasonable probability.", "이 지점부터 그녀의 기억은 어지럽고 조각나 있어서 어떤 그럴듯한 앞뒤와도 맞춰 보기가 어려웠다."]], [["Her own impression was that she recovered her senses later in the evening, that she then left the house, that she went (as she had previously arranged to go, at Blackwater Park) to Mrs. Vesey’s—that she drank tea there, and that she passed the night under Mrs. Vesey’s roof. She was totally unable to say how, or when, or in what company she left the house to which Count Fosco had brought her. But she persisted in asserting that she had been to Mrs. Vesey’s, and still more extraordinary, that she had been helped to undress and get to bed by Mrs. Rubelle! She could not remember what the conversation was at Mrs. Vesey’s or whom she saw there besides that lady, or why Mrs. Rubelle should have been present in the house to help her.", "그녀 자신의 인상으로는, 그날 저녁 늦게 정신이 들어 그 집을 나섰고, (블랙워터 파크에서 미리 정해 두었던 대로) 베지 부인 댁으로 갔으며, 거기서 차를 마시고 베지 부인의 지붕 아래에서 밤을 보냈다는 것이었다. 포스코 백작이 데려간 그 집을 어떻게, 언제, 누구와 함께 나섰는지는 도무지 말하지 못했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 베지 부인 댁에 갔었다고 우겼고, 더욱 놀랍게도 루벨 부인이 제 옷을 벗기고 잠자리에 들도록 거들어 주었다고 우겼다! 베지 부인 댁에서 무슨 이야기를 나누었는지, 그 부인 말고 누구를 보았는지, 루벨 부인이 왜 그 집에 있으면서 자기를 거들었는지는 기억하지 못했다."]], [["Her recollection of what happened to her the next morning was still more vague and unreliable.", "이튿날 아침 제게 무슨 일이 있었는지에 대한 기억은 더욱 흐릿하고 미덥지 못했다."]], [["She had some dim idea of driving out (at what hour she could not say) with Count Fosco, and with Mrs. Rubelle again for a female attendant.", "그녀에게는 포스코 백작과 함께, 그리고 또다시 시중드는 여자로 루벨 부인을 데리고 (몇 시였는지는 말하지 못했다) 마차를 타고 나갔다는 어렴풋한 생각이 있었다."], ["But when, and why, she left Mrs. Vesey she could not tell; neither did she know what direction the carriage drove in, or where it set her down, or whether the Count and Mrs.", "그러나 언제, 왜 베지 부인 댁을 떠났는지는 말하지 못했고, 마차가 어느 쪽으로 갔는지, 어디서 자기를 내려 주었는지, 나가 있는 내내 백작과 루벨 부인이 곁에 있었는지 아닌지도 알지 못했다."], ["Rubelle did or did not remain with her all the time she was out.", "그 슬픈 이야기의 이 대목에는 온통 공백이 있었다."], ["At this point in her sad story there was a total blank.", "그녀는 조금이나마 전할 수 있는 인상이 하나도 없었다."], ["She had no impressions of the faintest kind to communicate—no idea whether one day, or more than one day, had passed—until she came to herself suddenly in a strange place, surrounded by women who were all unknown to her.", "하루가 지났는지 하루보다 더 지났는지조차 알지 못했다 — 낯선 곳에서, 온통 알지 못하는 여자들에게 둘러싸인 채 문득 정신이 든 그때까지는."]], [["This was the Asylum.", "그곳이 바로 그 정신병원이었다."], ["Here she first heard herself called by Anne Catherick’s name, and here, as a last remarkable circumstance in the story of the conspiracy, her own eyes informed her that she had Anne Catherick’s clothes on.", "그녀는 그곳에서 처음으로 자신이 앤 캐서릭이라는 이름으로 불리는 것을 들었고, 또 그곳에서, 이 음모의 이야기에서 마지막으로 주목할 만한 정황으로서, 자신이 앤 캐서릭의 옷을 입고 있다는 사실을 제 눈으로 확인했다."], ["The nurse, on the first night in the Asylum, had shown her the marks on each article of her underclothing as it was taken off, and had said, not at all irritably or unkindly, “Look at your own name on your own clothes, and don’t worry us all any more about being Lady Glyde.", "정신병원에서 맞은 첫날 밤, 간호사는 벗겨 내는 속옷 한 점 한 점에 새겨진 표시를 그녀에게 보여 주면서, 조금도 짜증스럽거나 매정하지 않은 어조로 이렇게 말했다. “당신 옷에 당신 이름이 있잖아요, 그것 좀 보시고, 글라이드 부인이니 뭐니 하면서 더는 우리 모두를 성가시게 하지 마세요."], ["She’s dead and buried, and you’re alive and hearty.", "그분은 죽어서 묻혔고, 당신은 이렇게 살아서 멀쩡하잖아요."], ["Do look at your clothes now!", "자, 옷을 좀 보시라니까요!"], ["There it is, in good marking ink, and there you will find it on all your old things, which we have kept in the house—Anne Catherick, as plain as print!”", "여기 이렇게 좋은 표시용 잉크로 적혀 있고, 우리가 집에 보관해 둔 당신의 옛 물건에도 죄다 이 이름이 있을 거예요—앤 캐서릭, 인쇄한 것처럼 또렷하게요!”"], ["And there it was, when Miss Halcombe examined the linen her sister wore, on the night of their arrival at Limmeridge House.", "그리고 실제로 그 이름은 거기 있었다. 두 사람이 리머리지 하우스에 도착한 날 밤, 홀콤 양이 동생이 입고 있던 속옷을 살펴보았을 때에도 그대로."]], [["These were the only recollections—all of them uncertain, and some of them contradictory—which could be extracted from Lady Glyde by careful questioning on the journey to Cumberland.", "컴벌랜드로 가는 여정에서 조심스러운 질문으로 글라이드 부인에게서 끌어낼 수 있었던 기억은 이것이 전부였다—모두가 불확실했고, 그중 몇몇은 서로 어긋나기까지 했다."], ["Miss Halcombe abstained from pressing her with any inquiries relating to events in the Asylum—her mind being but too evidently unfit to bear the trial of reverting to them.", "홀콤 양은 정신병원에서 일어난 일에 관한 물음으로는 그녀를 몰아붙이지 않았다. 그 일을 다시 떠올리는 시련을 견디기에는 그녀의 정신이 너무나 뚜렷하게 부실했기 때문이다."], ["It was known, by the voluntary admission of the owner of the mad-house, that she was received there on the twenty-seventh of July.", "그 미치광이 수용소 주인이 스스로 인정한 바에 따라, 그녀가 그곳에 받아들여진 것이 7월 27일이라는 사실은 알려져 있었다."], ["From that date until the fifteenth of October (the day of her rescue) she had been under restraint, her identity with Anne Catherick systematically asserted, and her sanity, from first to last, practically denied.", "그날부터 10월 15일(그녀가 구출된 날)까지 그녀는 감금되어 있었고, 앤 캐서릭과 동일인이라는 주장이 조직적으로 되풀이되었으며, 그녀의 온전한 정신은 처음부터 끝까지 사실상 부정당했다."], ["Faculties less delicately balanced, constitutions less tenderly organised, must have suffered under such an ordeal as this.", "그보다 덜 섬세하게 균형 잡힌 정신, 그보다 덜 여리게 짜인 체질이었더라도 이런 시련 아래에서는 상하고야 말았을 것이다."], ["No man could have gone through it and come out of it unchanged.", "그 누구도 그 일을 겪고서 조금도 달라지지 않은 채 빠져나올 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["Arriving at Limmeridge late on the evening of the fifteenth, Miss Halcombe wisely resolved not to attempt the assertion of Lady Glyde’s identity until the next day.", "15일 저녁 늦게 리머리지에 닿은 홀콤 양은 글라이드 부인의 신원을 주장하는 일을 이튿날까지 미루기로 현명하게 결심했다."]], [["The first thing in the morning she went to Mr. Fairlie’s room, and using all possible cautions and preparations beforehand, at last told him in so many words what had happened.", "아침이 밝자마자 그녀는 페얼리 씨의 방으로 찾아가, 미리 할 수 있는 모든 조심과 준비를 다한 끝에, 마침내 무슨 일이 있었는지를 낱낱이 말했다."], ["As soon as his first astonishment and alarm had subsided, he angrily declared that Miss Halcombe had allowed herself to be duped by Anne Catherick.", "처음의 경악과 놀람이 가라앉자마자 그는 홀콤 양이 앤 캐서릭에게 감쪽같이 속아 넘어간 것이라고 성난 어조로 단언했다."], ["He referred her to Count Fosco’s letter, and to what she had herself told him of the personal resemblance between Anne and his deceased niece, and he positively declined to admit to his presence, even for one minute only, a madwoman, whom it was an insult and an outrage to have brought into his house at all.", "그는 포스코 백작의 편지를 들먹였고, 앤과 세상을 떠난 자기 조카딸이 얼마나 닮았는지에 대해 그녀 자신이 그에게 들려주었던 이야기까지 들먹이면서, 애초에 자기 집으로 데려온 것 자체가 모욕이요 폭거인 미친 여자를 단 1분이라도 제 앞에 들이는 일은 결단코 못 하겠다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["Miss Halcombe left the room—waited till the first heat of her indignation had passed away—decided on reflection that Mr. Fairlie should see his niece in the interests of common humanity before he closed his doors on her as a stranger—and thereupon, without a word of previous warning, took Lady Glyde with her to his room.", "홀콤 양은 방을 나왔다—분노의 첫 열기가 가라앉기를 기다렸다—곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 페얼리 씨가 조카딸을 낯선 사람 취급하며 문을 닫아걸기 전에 최소한의 인간된 도리로라도 한 번은 그 얼굴을 보아야 한다고 결론지었다—그러고는 미리 한마디 예고도 없이 글라이드 부인을 데리고 그의 방으로 갔다."], ["The servant was posted at the door to prevent their entrance, but Miss Halcombe insisted on passing him, and made her way into Mr. Fairlie’s presence, leading her sister by the hand.", "하인이 문 앞을 지키고 서서 두 사람이 들어가지 못하게 막았으나, 홀콤 양은 기어이 그를 밀치고 지나가, 동생의 손을 잡고 이끈 채 페얼리 씨 앞으로 들어섰다."]], [["The scene that followed, though it only lasted for a few minutes, was too painful to be described—Miss Halcombe herself shrank from referring to it.", "뒤이어 벌어진 장면은 몇 분밖에 이어지지 않았으나 차마 옮겨 적을 수 없을 만큼 고통스러웠다—홀콤 양 자신도 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 꺼렸다."], ["Let it be enough to say that Mr. Fairlie declared, in the most positive terms, that he did not recognise the woman who had been brought into his room—that he saw nothing in her face and manner to make him doubt for a moment that his niece lay buried in Limmeridge churchyard, and that he would call on the law to protect him if before the day was over she was not removed from the house.", "페얼리 씨가 더할 나위 없이 단호한 말로, 자기 방에 들어온 여자를 알아보지 못하겠다고, 조카딸이 리머리지 교회 묘지에 묻혀 있다는 사실을 단 한순간이라도 의심하게 만들 만한 것은 그 얼굴에도 몸가짐에도 없다고, 그리고 해가 저물기 전에 저 여자를 집에서 내보내지 않으면 법에 호소해 자신을 보호받겠다고 선언했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하리라."]], [["Taking the very worst view of Mr. Fairlie’s selfishness, indolence, and habitual want of feeling, it was manifestly impossible to suppose that he was capable of such infamy as secretly recognising and openly disowning his brother’s child.", "페얼리 씨의 이기심과 나태와 습관처럼 굳어진 무정함을 아무리 나쁘게 본다 해도, 형의 딸을 속으로는 알아보면서 겉으로는 모른 체하는 그런 파렴치한 짓을 그가 저지를 수 있으리라고는 도저히 생각할 수 없었다."], ["Miss Halcombe humanely and sensibly allowed all due force to the influence of prejudice and alarm in preventing him from fairly exercising his perceptions, and accounted for what had happened in that way.", "홀콤 양은 인정 있고 분별 있게, 편견과 놀람이 그로 하여금 제 지각을 공정하게 발휘하지 못하도록 막았으리라는 점을 충분히 인정하고, 벌어진 일을 그렇게 헤아렸다."], ["But when she next put the servants to the test, and found that they too were, in every case, uncertain, to say the least of it, whether the lady presented to them was their young mistress or Anne Catherick, of whose resemblance to her they had all heard, the sad conclusion was inevitable that the change produced in Lady Glyde’s face and manner by her imprisonment in the Asylum was far more serious than Miss Halcombe had at first supposed.", "그러나 그다음으로 그녀가 하인들을 시험해 보았을 때, 그들 역시 하나같이—아무리 좋게 말해도—눈앞에 세워진 부인이 자기네 젊은 여주인인지, 아니면 그녀와 닮았다는 이야기를 다들 익히 들어 온 앤 캐서릭인지 확신하지 못한다는 사실을 알게 되자, 슬픈 결론을 피할 길이 없었다. 정신병원에 갇혀 지낸 일이 글라이드 부인의 얼굴과 몸가짐에 일으킨 변화는 홀콤 양이 처음에 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심각했던 것이다."], ["The vile deception which had asserted her death defied exposure even in the house where she was born, and among the people with whom she had lived.", "그녀가 죽었다고 주장한 그 사악한 기만은, 그녀가 태어난 집 안에서도, 그녀와 함께 살아온 사람들 사이에서도 끝내 들통나지 않았다."]], [["In a less critical situation the effort need not have been given up as hopeless even yet.", "사정이 이토록 급박하지만 않았다면, 그 노력을 아직 가망 없다고 포기할 필요까지는 없었을 것이다."]], [["For example, the maid, Fanny, who happened to be then absent from Limmeridge, was expected back in two days, and there would be a chance of gaining her recognition to start with, seeing that she had been in much more constant communication with her mistress, and had been much more heartily attached to her than the other servants.", "이를테면 마침 그때 리머리지를 비우고 있던 하녀 패니가 이틀 뒤면 돌아올 예정이었으니, 우선 그녀가 알아보아 주기를 기대해 볼 여지가 있었다. 패니는 다른 하인들보다 훨씬 더 자주 여주인과 마음을 나누어 왔고, 훨씬 더 진심으로 그녀를 따랐기 때문이다."], ["Again, Lady Glyde might have been privately kept in the house or in the village to wait until her health was a little recovered and her mind was a little steadied again.", "또한 글라이드 부인을 저택이나 마을에 남몰래 머물게 하면서 건강이 조금 회복되고 정신이 조금 가라앉기를 기다릴 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["When her memory could be once more trusted to serve her, she would naturally refer to persons and events in the past with a certainty and a familiarity which no impostor could simulate, and so the fact of her identity, which her own appearance had failed to establish, might subsequently be proved, with time to help her, by the surer test of her own words.", "그녀의 기억을 다시 믿을 수 있게 되면, 그녀는 자연히 지난날의 사람과 사건을 어떤 사칭꾼도 흉내 낼 수 없는 확신과 친숙함으로 이야기할 터였고, 그러면 겉모습으로는 세우지 못한 신원의 사실이 시간의 도움을 받아 그녀 자신의 말이라는 더 확실한 시험으로 나중에라도 증명될 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["But the circumstances under which she had regained her freedom rendered all recourse to such means as these simply impracticable.", "그러나 그녀가 자유를 되찾은 정황은 이런 수단에 기대는 일을 아예 실행 불가능하게 만들었다."], ["The pursuit from the Asylum, diverted to Hampshire for the time only, would infallibly next take the direction of Cumberland.", "정신병원 쪽의 추적은 잠시 햄프셔로 방향이 틀어졌을 뿐, 다음에는 어김없이 컴벌랜드 쪽으로 향할 것이었다."], ["The persons appointed to seek the fugitive might arrive at Limmeridge House at a few hours’ notice, and in Mr. Fairlie’s present temper of mind they might count on the immediate exertion of his local influence and authority to assist them.", "도망친 여자를 찾으라고 지명된 자들이 몇 시간 전 통보만으로 리머리지 하우스에 들이닥칠 수도 있었고, 지금의 페얼리 씨 심기로 보아 그들은 그가 이 고장에서 지닌 영향력과 권위를 즉각 발휘해 자기들을 도우리라고 기대할 만했다."], ["The commonest consideration for Lady Glyde’s safety forced on Miss Halcombe the necessity of resigning the struggle to do her justice, and of removing her at once from the place of all others that was now most dangerous to her—the neighbourhood of her own home.", "글라이드 부인의 안전을 조금이라도 헤아린다면, 홀콤 양으로서는 그녀의 정당한 권리를 되찾으려는 싸움을 접고, 이제는 세상 어느 곳보다 그녀에게 가장 위험해진 장소—곧 그녀 자신의 집 근처—에서 그녀를 당장 데리고 나오는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["An immediate return to London was the first and wisest measure of security which suggested itself.", "곧바로 런던으로 돌아가는 것이 가장 먼저, 그리고 가장 현명하게 떠오른 안전책이었다."], ["In the great city all traces of them might be most speedily and most surely effaced.", "그 거대한 도시에서라면 두 사람의 자취를 가장 빠르고 가장 확실하게 지울 수 있을 터였다."], ["There were no preparations to make—no farewell words of kindness to exchange with any one.", "챙길 준비랄 것도 없었고—다정한 작별 인사를 나눌 사람도 하나 없었다."], ["On the afternoon of that memorable day of the sixteenth Miss Halcombe roused her sister to a last exertion of courage, and without a living soul to wish them well at parting, the two took their way into the world alone, and turned their backs for ever on Limmeridge House.", "그 잊지 못할 16일 오후, 홀콤 양은 동생을 다잡아 마지막 용기를 짜내게 했고, 떠나는 길에 잘 가라고 빌어 줄 사람 하나 없이, 두 여자는 단둘이 세상 속으로 길을 나서며 리머리지 하우스에 영영 등을 돌렸다."]], [["They had passed the hill above the churchyard, when Lady Glyde insisted on turning back to look her last at her mother’s grave.", "교회 묘지 위쪽 언덕을 지났을 때, 글라이드 부인이 어머니의 무덤을 마지막으로 한 번 보아야겠다며 되돌아가자고 고집했다."], ["Miss Halcombe tried to shake her resolution, but, in this one instance, tried in vain.", "홀콤 양은 그 결심을 꺾어 보려 했으나, 오직 이 한 가지 일에서만은 애를 써도 소용이 없었다."], ["She was immovable.", "그녀는 요지부동이었다."], ["Her dim eyes lit with a sudden fire, and flashed through the veil that hung over them—her wasted fingers strengthened moment by moment round the friendly arm by which they had held so listlessly till this time.", "흐릿하던 두 눈에 갑작스러운 불이 붙어, 그 위에 드리운 장막을 뚫고 번쩍였다—그때까지 그토록 맥없이 매달려 있던 다정한 팔을 붙든 야윈 손가락에 순간순간 힘이 실렸다. 나는 영혼 깊이 믿는다."], ["I believe in my soul that the hand of God was pointing their way back to them, and that the most innocent and the most afflicted of His creatures was chosen in that dread moment to see it.", "그 순간 하느님의 손이 두 사람에게 돌아갈 길을 가리켜 보이셨고, 그분이 지으신 것들 가운데 가장 죄 없고 가장 시달린 사람이 그 두려운 순간에 그것을 볼 자로 택함받았음을."]], [["They retraced their steps to the burial-ground, and by that act sealed the future of our three lives.", "두 사람은 왔던 길을 되짚어 묘지로 돌아갔고, 그 한 가지 행동으로 우리 세 사람의 앞날이 결정되었다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["This was the story of the past—the story so far as we knew it then.", "이것이 지난 일의 전말이었다—적어도 그때까지 우리가 알고 있던 한에서의 이야기였다."]], [["Two obvious conclusions presented themselves to my mind after hearing it.", "그 이야기를 듣고 난 뒤 두 가지 결론이 저절로 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["In the first place, I saw darkly what the nature of the conspiracy had been, how chances had been watched, and how circumstances had been handled to ensure impunity to a daring and an intricate crime.", "첫째로, 나는 그 음모의 본질이 어떤 것이었는지, 어떻게 기회를 노렸고 어떻게 정황을 주물러서 대담하고도 복잡한 범죄에 아무런 처벌이 미치지 않도록 만들었는지를 어렴풋하게나마 알아보았다."], ["While all details were still a mystery to me, the vile manner in which the personal resemblance between the woman in white and Lady Glyde had been turned to account was clear beyond a doubt.", "세부 사정은 여전히 내게 수수께끼였지만, 흰옷을 입은 여인과 글라이드 부인 사이의 용모의 닮음이 얼마나 비열하게 이용되었는지는 의심할 여지 없이 분명했다."], ["It was plain that Anne Catherick had been introduced into Count Fosco’s house as Lady Glyde—it was plain that Lady Glyde had taken the dead woman’s place in the Asylum—the substitution having been so managed as to make innocent people (the doctor and the two servants certainly, and the owner of the mad-house in all probability) accomplices in the crime.", "앤 캐서릭이 글라이드 부인 행세로 포스코 백작의 집에 들여보내졌다는 것도 분명했고, 글라이드 부인이 죽은 여자의 자리를 대신해 정신병원에 갇혔다는 것도 분명했다. 그리고 그 뒤바꿈은 아무 죄 없는 사람들—의사와 두 하인은 확실히, 그리고 십중팔구 정신병원의 소유주까지—을 범죄의 공범으로 만들도록 꾸며져 있었다."]], [["The second conclusion came as the necessary consequence of the first.", "두 번째 결론은 첫 번째 결론에서 필연적으로 따라 나왔다."], ["We three had no mercy to expect from Count Fosco and Sir Percival Glyde.", "우리 세 사람은 포스코 백작과 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게서 어떠한 자비도 기대할 수 없었다."], ["The success of the conspiracy had brought with it a clear gain to those two men of thirty thousand pounds—twenty thousand to one, ten thousand to the other through his wife.", "음모의 성공은 그 두 사람에게 삼만 파운드라는 뚜렷한 이득을 안겨 주었다—한 사람에게 이만 파운드, 다른 한 사람에게는 아내를 통해 만 파운드였다."], ["They had that interest, as well as other interests, in ensuring their impunity from exposure, and they would leave no stone unturned, no sacrifice unattempted, no treachery untried, to discover the place in which their victim was concealed, and to part her from the only friends she had in the world—Marian Halcombe and myself.", "그들에게는 그 이득이 있었고, 그 밖의 이해관계도 있었으므로, 자기들의 죄가 폭로되지 않게 하려고 무슨 짓이든 할 것이었다. 그들은 온갖 수단을 다 뒤져 보고, 어떤 희생도 마다하지 않고, 어떤 배신도 서슴지 않으면서, 자기들의 희생자가 숨어 있는 곳을 찾아내어 그녀를 이 세상에 남은 단 두 사람의 벗—매리언 홀콤과 나에게서 떼어 놓으려 할 것이었다."]], [["The sense of this serious peril—a peril which every day and every hour might bring nearer and nearer to us—was the one influence that guided me in fixing the place of our retreat.", "이 엄중한 위험—날마다 시시각각 우리에게 점점 더 가까이 다가올지 모르는 위험—에 대한 인식이야말로 우리가 숨어 지낼 곳을 정할 때 나를 이끈 유일한 기준이었다."], ["I chose it in the far east of London, where there were fewest idle people to lounge and look about them in the streets.", "나는 런던의 저 동쪽 끝, 거리에서 빈둥거리며 이리저리 둘러보는 한가한 사람이 가장 적은 곳을 골랐다."], ["I chose it in a poor and a populous neighbourhood—because the harder the struggle for existence among the men and women about us, the less the risk of their having the time or taking the pains to notice chance strangers who came among them.", "나는 가난하고 사람이 빽빽한 동네를 골랐다—우리 주위의 남녀들이 먹고살기 위해 힘겹게 싸울수록, 그들 틈에 끼어든 낯선 사람을 눈여겨볼 시간이나 수고를 들일 위험이 적어지기 때문이었다."], ["These were the great advantages I looked to, but our locality was a gain to us also in another and a hardly less important respect.", "내가 노린 것은 그런 커다란 이점들이었지만, 우리가 자리 잡은 곳은 그에 못지않게 중요한 또 다른 점에서도 우리에게 이로웠다."], ["We could live cheaply by the daily work of my hands, and could save every farthing we possessed to forward the purpose, the righteous purpose, of redressing an infamous wrong—which, from first to last, I now kept steadily in view.", "우리는 내 손으로 하는 나날의 일감으로 값싸게 살아갈 수 있었고, 가진 돈은 한 푼도 남김없이 아껴 두었다가 그 목적, 저 정당한 목적—파렴치한 악행을 바로잡는 일—에 쓸 수 있었다. 나는 그때부터 처음부터 끝까지 그 목적만을 한결같이 눈앞에 두고 있었다."]], [["In a week’s time Marian Halcombe and I had settled how the course of our new lives should be directed.", "일주일 만에 매리언 홀콤과 나는 우리의 새로운 삶을 어떤 방향으로 이끌어 갈지 정해 놓았다."]], [["There were no other lodgers in the house, and we had the means of going in and out without passing through the shop.", "그 집에는 다른 세입자가 없었고, 우리는 가게를 지나지 않고도 드나들 수 있는 길이 있었다."], ["I arranged, for the present at least, that neither Marian nor Laura should stir outside the door without my being with them, and that in my absence from home they should let no one into their rooms on any pretence whatever.", "나는 적어도 당분간은 매리언도 로라도 내가 함께 있지 않으면 문밖으로 한 걸음도 나서지 않도록, 그리고 내가 집을 비운 사이에는 어떤 구실로도 방 안에 아무도 들이지 않도록 정해 두었다."], ["This rule established, I went to a friend whom I had known in former days—a wood engraver in large practice—to seek for employment, telling him, at the same time, that I had reasons for wishing to remain unknown.", "이 규칙을 세워 놓고 나서 나는 예전에 알고 지내던 친구—큰 일감을 맡아 하는 목판 조각가—를 찾아가 일자리를 부탁하면서, 동시에 내 이름을 드러내지 않고 지내야 할 사정이 있다고 말했다."]], [["He at once concluded that I was in debt, expressed his regret in the usual forms, and then promised to do what he could to assist me.", "그는 대뜸 내가 빚을 졌다고 단정하고는 으레 하는 말투로 안됐다는 뜻을 표하더니, 힘닿는 데까지 나를 돕겠다고 약속했다."], ["I left his false impression undisturbed, and accepted the work he had to give.", "나는 그의 잘못된 짐작을 굳이 바로잡지 않고, 그가 주는 일을 받았다."], ["He knew that he could trust my experience and my industry.", "그는 내 솜씨와 내 성실함을 믿을 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["I had what he wanted, steadiness and facility, and though my earnings were but small, they sufficed for our necessities.", "나에게는 그가 원하는 것, 곧 꾸준함과 손놀림의 능숙함이 있었고, 벌이는 얼마 되지 않았지만 우리가 살아가는 데는 넉넉했다."], ["As soon as we could feel certain of this, Marian Halcombe and I put together what we possessed.", "그 점을 확실히 믿을 수 있게 되자마자 매리언 홀콤과 나는 각자 가진 것을 한데 모았다."], ["She had between two and three hundred pounds left of her own property, and I had nearly as much remaining from the purchase-money obtained by the sale of my drawing-master’s practice before I left England.", "그녀에게는 자기 재산에서 남은 이삼백 파운드가 있었고, 나에게는 영국을 떠나기 전에 그림 교습 자리를 넘기고 받은 돈에서 남은 것이 거의 그만큼 있었다. 둘이 합치니 사백 파운드가 넘었다."], ["Together we made up between us more than four hundred pounds.", "나는 이 얼마 안 되는 재산을 은행에 맡겨 두었다."], ["I deposited this little fortune in a bank, to be kept for the expense of those secret inquiries and investigations which I was determined to set on foot, and to carry on by myself if I could find no one to help me.", "내가 기어이 시작하기로 마음먹은, 그리고 도와줄 사람을 아무도 찾지 못하면 나 혼자서라도 밀고 나갈 저 은밀한 조사와 탐문에 드는 비용으로 쓰기 위해서였다."], ["We calculated our weekly expenditure to the last farthing, and we never touched our little fund except in Laura’s interests and for Laura’s sake.", "우리는 주간 지출을 마지막 한 푼까지 따져 가며 계산했고, 로라를 위한 일이 아니면, 로라를 위해서가 아니면 그 작은 자금에는 결코 손을 대지 않았다."]], [["The house-work, which, if we had dared trust a stranger near us, would have been done by a servant, was taken on the first day, taken as her own right, by Marian Halcombe.", "집안일은, 우리가 낯선 사람을 곁에 두어도 좋다고 여겼더라면 하녀가 맡았을 그 일은, 첫날부터 매리언 홀콤이 제 몫이라는 듯 떠맡았다."], ["“What a woman’s hands ARE fit for,”", "“여자의 손으로 할 수 있는 일이라면,”"], ["she said, “early and late, these hands of mine shall do.”", "그녀가 말했다. “아침부터 밤까지 이 손으로 다 하겠어요.”"], ["They trembled as she held them out.", "그녀가 내밀어 보인 두 손은 떨리고 있었다."], ["The wasted arms told their sad story of the past, as she turned up the sleeves of the poor plain dress that she wore for safety’s sake; but the unquenchable spirit of the woman burnt bright in her even yet.", "몸을 지키려고 걸친 초라하고 수수한 옷의 소매를 걷어 올리자, 여윈 팔이 지나간 나날의 서글픈 사연을 그대로 말해 주었다. 그러나 이 여인의 꺼지지 않는 기상은 아직도 그 안에서 환하게 타오르고 있었다."], ["I saw the big tears rise thick in her eyes, and fall slowly over her cheeks as she looked at me.", "그녀가 나를 바라볼 때 커다란 눈물방울이 눈에 그렁그렁 차올라 천천히 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것이 보였다."], ["She dashed them away with a touch of her old energy, and smiled with a faint reflection of her old good spirits.", "그녀는 예전의 그 기운을 살짝 되살려 눈물을 훔쳐 내고는, 옛날의 밝은 기분을 어렴풋이 되비추는 미소를 지었다."], ["“Don’t doubt my courage, Walter,”", "“내 용기를 의심하지 말아요, 월터.”"], ["she pleaded, “it’s my weakness that cries, not ME.", "그녀가 호소하듯 말했다. “우는 건 내 약한 구석이지 내가 아니에요."], ["The house-work shall conquer it if I can’t.”", "내가 못 이기면 집안일이 그 약한 구석을 이겨 줄 거예요.”"], ["And she kept her word—the victory was won when we met in the evening, and she sat down to rest.", "그리고 그녀는 그 말을 지켰다. 저녁에 우리가 마주 앉고 그녀가 자리에 앉아 쉴 무렵에는 이미 승리를 거둔 뒤였다."], ["Her large steady black eyes looked at me with a flash of their bright firmness of bygone days.", "그녀의 크고 흔들림 없는 검은 눈이 지난날의 그 빛나는 결연함을 번뜩이며 나를 바라보았다."], ["“I am not quite broken down yet,” she said.", "“아직 완전히 무너지지는 않았어요.”"], ["“I am worth trusting with my share of the work.”", "그녀가 말했다. “내 몫의 일을 맡길 만은 하다고요.”"], ["Before I could answer, she added in a whisper, “And worth trusting with my share in the risk and the danger too.", "내가 대답하기도 전에 그녀는 속삭이듯 덧붙였다. “그리고 내 몫의 위험과 위기를 맡길 만도 하고요."], ["Remember that, if the time comes!”", "때가 오거든 그걸 잊지 말아요!”"]], [["I did remember it when the time came.", "때가 왔을 때 나는 그 말을 잊지 않았다."]], [["As early as the end of October the daily course of our lives had assumed its settled direction, and we three were as completely isolated in our place of concealment as if the house we lived in had been a desert island, and the great network of streets and the thousands of our fellow-creatures all round us the waters of an illimitable sea.", "시월이 끝나 갈 무렵에는 벌써 우리의 나날이 정해진 방향을 잡았고, 우리 세 사람은 숨어 지내는 곳에서 완전히 고립되어 있었다. 마치 우리가 사는 집이 무인도이고, 사방으로 뻗은 거대한 거리의 그물망과 우리를 둘러싼 수천의 동포들이 끝없는 바다의 물결이라도 되는 것 같았다."], ["I could now reckon on some leisure time for considering what my future plan of action should be, and how I might arm myself most securely at the outset for the coming struggle with Sir Percival and the Count.", "이제 나는 앞으로 어떤 계획으로 움직여야 할지, 그리고 퍼시벌 경과 백작을 상대로 다가올 싸움에 대비해 처음부터 어떻게 하면 가장 든든하게 무장할 수 있을지를 궁리할 여유를 조금은 낼 수 있었다."]], [["I gave up all hope of appealing to my recognition of Laura, or to Marian’s recognition of her, in proof of her identity.", "나는 로라의 신원을 증명하는 데 내가 그녀를 알아본다는 사실이나 매리언이 그녀를 알아본다는 사실에 기대려던 희망을 모두 버렸다."], ["If we had loved her less dearly, if the instinct implanted in us by that love had not been far more certain than any exercise of reasoning, far keener than any process of observation, even we might have hesitated on first seeing her.", "우리가 그녀를 그토록 애틋하게 사랑하지 않았더라면, 그 사랑이 우리 안에 심어 놓은 직감이 어떤 추론보다도 훨씬 확실하고 어떤 관찰보다도 훨씬 예리하지 않았더라면, 우리조차 그녀를 처음 보았을 때 망설였을지도 모른다."]], [["The outward changes wrought by the suffering and the terror of the past had fearfully, almost hopelessly, strengthened the fatal resemblance between Anne Catherick and herself.", "지나간 고통과 공포가 겉모습에 남긴 변화는, 앤 캐서릭과 그녀 사이의 그 치명적인 닮음을 무섭도록, 거의 절망적일 만큼 짙게 만들어 놓았다."], ["In my narrative of events at the time of my residence in Limmeridge House, I have recorded, from my own observation of the two, how the likeness, striking as it was when viewed generally, failed in many important points of similarity when tested in detail.", "리머리지 하우스에 머물던 무렵의 일을 적은 내 기록에서, 나는 두 사람을 직접 살펴본 바에 따라, 그 닮음이 전체적으로 볼 때는 그토록 놀라웠으면서도 하나하나 뜯어보면 여러 중요한 대목에서 어긋난다는 것을 적어 두었다."], ["In those former days, if they had both been seen together side by side, no person could for a moment have mistaken them one for the other—as has happened often in the instances of twins.", "그 시절이었다면 두 사람을 나란히 세워 놓고 보았을 때 어느 누구도 쌍둥이를 두고 흔히 그러듯 한 사람을 다른 사람으로 잠시라도 잘못 볼 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["I could not say this now.", "이제는 그렇게 말할 수 없었다."], ["The sorrow and suffering which I had once blamed myself for associating even by a passing thought with the future of Laura Fairlie, HAD set their profaning marks on the youth and beauty of her face; and the fatal resemblance which I had once seen and shuddered at seeing, in idea only, was now a real and living resemblance which asserted itself before my own eyes.", "한때 나는 지나가는 생각으로나마 로라 페얼리의 앞날에 슬픔과 고통을 결부시켰다는 것만으로도 스스로를 나무랐었는데, 그 슬픔과 고통이 이제 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움 위에 더러운 자국을 남겨 놓았다. 그리고 한때 관념으로만 보고도 몸서리쳤던 그 치명적인 닮음이, 이제는 내 두 눈앞에서 제 존재를 주장하는 실제의 살아 있는 닮음이 되어 있었다."], ["Strangers, acquaintances, friends even who could not look at her as we looked, if she had been shown to them in the first days of her rescue from the Asylum, might have doubted if she were the Laura Fairlie they had once seen, and doubted without blame.", "우리처럼 그녀를 바라볼 수 없는 낯선 사람들이나 지인들, 심지어 벗들까지도, 정신병원에서 구해 낸 첫 무렵에 그녀를 보았더라면 그녀가 예전에 보았던 그 로라 페얼리인지 의심했을 것이고, 그 의심을 나무랄 수도 없었을 것이다."]], [["The one remaining chance, which I had at first thought might be trusted to serve us—the chance of appealing to her recollection of persons and events with which no impostor could be familiar, was proved, by the sad test of our later experience, to be hopeless.", "처음에는 우리에게 도움이 되리라 믿었던 마지막 남은 가능성—어떤 사기꾼도 알 리 없는 사람들과 사건들에 대한 그녀의 기억에 호소하는 방법—은, 이후의 서글픈 경험이 시험해 본 끝에 가망 없는 것으로 드러났다."], ["Every little caution that Marian and I practised towards her—every little remedy we tried, to strengthen and steady slowly the weakened, shaken faculties, was a fresh protest in itself against the risk of turning her mind back on the troubled and the terrible past.", "매리언과 내가 그녀를 대하며 기울인 자잘한 조심 하나하나, 약해지고 흔들린 정신의 힘을 천천히 북돋우고 다잡으려고 시도한 자잘한 처방 하나하나가, 그 자체로 그녀의 마음을 저 어지럽고 끔찍한 과거로 되돌리는 위험에 대한 새로운 항변이었다."]], [["The only events of former days which we ventured on encouraging her to recall were the little trivial domestic events of that happy time at Limmeridge, when I first went there and taught her to draw.", "우리가 감히 그녀에게 떠올려 보라고 권할 수 있었던 지난날의 일이라고는, 내가 처음 리머리지에 가서 그녀에게 그림을 가르치던 그 행복한 시절의 사소하고 소소한 집안일들뿐이었다."], ["The day when I roused those remembrances by showing her the sketch of the summer-house which she had given me on the morning of our farewell, and which had never been separated from me since, was the birthday of our first hope.", "이별하던 날 아침에 그녀가 나에게 주었고 그 뒤로 한 번도 내 몸에서 떨어져 본 적 없던 정자 그림을 보여 주며 그 기억들을 일깨운 날은, 우리의 첫 희망이 태어난 날이었다."], ["Tenderly and gradually, the memory of the old walks and drives dawned upon her, and the poor weary pining eyes looked at Marian and at me with a new interest, with a faltering thoughtfulness in them, which from that moment we cherished and kept alive.", "다정하게, 그리고 조금씩, 옛날의 산책과 마차 나들이에 대한 기억이 그녀 안에서 밝아 왔고, 지치고 시들어 가던 가엾은 두 눈이 새로운 관심을 담고, 머뭇거리는 사려를 담고 매리언과 나를 바라보았다. 그 순간부터 우리는 그것을 소중히 여기며 꺼지지 않게 지켰다."], ["I bought her a little box of colours, and a sketch-book like the old sketch-book which I had seen in her hands on the morning that we first met.", "나는 그녀에게 작은 물감 상자 하나와, 우리가 처음 만난 날 아침에 그녀의 손에 들려 있던 옛 사생첩과 같은 사생첩 하나를 사다 주었다."], ["Once again—oh me, once again!—at spare hours saved from my work, in the dull London light, in the poor London room, I sat by her side to guide the faltering touch, to help the feeble hand.", "다시 한 번—아, 나에게 다시 한 번!—일에서 아껴 낸 짬을 내어, 흐릿한 런던의 빛 아래, 초라한 런던의 방에서, 나는 그녀 곁에 앉아 머뭇거리는 붓끝을 이끌고 힘없는 손을 거들었다. 나날이 나는 그 새로운 관심을 북돋우고 또 북돋워, 마침내 그것이 그녀의 텅 빈 삶 속에서 제자리를 잡게 했다."], ["Day by day I raised and raised the new interest till its place in the blank of her existence was at last assured—till she could think of her drawing and talk of it, and patiently practise it by herself, with some faint reflection of the innocent pleasure in my encouragement, the growing enjoyment in her own progress, which belonged to the lost life and the lost happiness of past days.", "마침내 그녀는 자기 그림을 생각하고 그 이야기를 하고, 혼자서 참을성 있게 연습할 수 있게 되었다. 내가 격려할 때의 천진한 기쁨, 자기 실력이 느는 데서 오는 커져 가는 즐거움—잃어버린 삶과 잃어버린 행복에 속해 있던 그것들이 어렴풋이 되비치기 시작했다."]], [["We helped her mind slowly by this simple means, we took her out between us to walk on fine days, in a quiet old City square near at hand, where there was nothing to confuse or alarm her—we spared a few pounds from the fund at the banker’s to get her wine, and the delicate strengthening food that she required—we amused her in the evenings with children’s games at cards, with scrap-books full of prints which I borrowed from the engraver who employed me—by these, and other trifling attentions like them, we composed her and steadied her, and hoped all things, as cheerfully as we could from time and care, and love that never neglected and never despaired of her. But to take her mercilessly from seclusion and repose—to confront her with strangers, or with acquaintances who were little better than strangers—to rouse the painful impressions of her past life which we had so carefully hushed to rest—this, even in her own interests, we dared not do. Whatever sacrifices it cost, whatever long, weary, heartbreaking delays it involved, the wrong that had been inflicted on her, if mortal means could grapple it, must be redressed without her knowledge and without her help.", "우리는 이런 소박한 방법으로 그녀의 마음을 천천히 도왔다. 날씨 좋은 날이면 우리 둘이 그녀를 사이에 두고 가까운 곳의 조용하고 오래된 시내 광장으로 산책을 나갔다. 그녀를 어리둥절하게 하거나 놀라게 할 것이 아무것도 없는 곳이었다. 우리는 은행에 맡긴 자금에서 몇 파운드를 덜어 내어 그녀에게 필요한 포도주와 몸을 돋우는 좋은 음식을 마련해 주었다. 저녁이면 아이들이 하는 카드놀이로, 나를 고용한 조각가에게서 빌려 온 판화가 가득한 그림책으로 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주었다. 이런 자잘한 배려며 그와 비슷한 자잘한 일들로 우리는 그녀를 가라앉히고 다잡아 주었고, 세월과 보살핌, 그리고 그녀를 결코 소홀히 하지도 단념하지도 않는 사랑에 힘입어 될 수 있는 한 밝게 온갖 것을 바랐다. 그러나 그녀를 은둔과 안식에서 매정하게 끌어내는 일—낯선 사람들이나, 낯선 사람이나 다름없는 지인들 앞에 그녀를 세우는 일—우리가 그토록 조심스레 잠재워 놓은 지난 삶의 괴로운 기억들을 다시 흔들어 깨우는 일, 그것만은 그녀 자신을 위한 일이라 해도 감히 할 수 없었다. 어떤 희생을 치러야 하든, 얼마나 길고 지치고 가슴 미어지는 지연을 겪어야 하든, 그녀에게 가해진 그 악행은, 사람의 힘으로 붙들어 볼 수 있는 것이라면, 그녀가 모르는 채로 그녀의 도움 없이 바로잡혀야 했다."]], [["This resolution settled, it was next necessary to decide how the first risk should be ventured, and what the first proceedings should be.", "이렇게 마음을 정하고 나니, 다음으로는 첫 모험을 어떻게 감행할 것인지, 첫 조치를 무엇으로 삼을 것인지 결정해야 했다."]], [["After consulting with Marian, I resolved to begin by gathering together as many facts as could be collected—then to ask the advice of Mr. Kyrle (whom we knew we could trust), and to ascertain from him, in the first instance, if the legal remedy lay fairly within our reach.", "매리언과 의논한 끝에, 나는 우선 모을 수 있는 한 많은 사실을 그러모으고, 그런 다음 카일 씨(우리가 믿을 수 있다고 알고 있는 사람이었다)에게 조언을 구하여, 무엇보다 먼저 법적인 구제가 우리 손이 닿는 범위 안에 정말로 있는지를 그에게서 확인하기로 했다."], ["I owed it to Laura’s interests not to stake her whole future on my own unaided exertions, so long as there was the faintest prospect of strengthening our position by obtaining reliable assistance of any kind.", "어떤 종류든 믿을 만한 도움을 얻어 우리 처지를 튼튼히 할 가망이 조금이라도 남아 있는 한, 나 혼자만의 힘에 그녀의 앞날 전부를 거는 것은 로라의 이익을 위해서 할 일이 아니었다."]], [["The first source of information to which I applied was the journal kept at Blackwater Park by Marian Halcombe.", "내가 맨 처음 손을 댄 정보의 원천은 매리언 홀콤이 블랙워터 파크에서 쓴 일기였다."], ["There were passages in this diary relating to myself which she thought it best that I should not see.", "이 일기에는 나에 관한 대목들이 있었는데, 그녀는 내가 그것을 보지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다."], ["Accordingly, she read to me from the manuscript, and I took the notes I wanted as she went on.", "그래서 그녀가 원고를 나에게 읽어 주었고, 나는 그녀가 읽어 나가는 동안 필요한 것을 받아 적었다."], ["We could only find time to pursue this occupation by sitting up late at night.", "이 일에 쓸 수 있는 시간이라고는 밤늦게까지 자지 않고 앉아 있는 때뿐이었다."], ["Three nights were devoted to the purpose, and were enough to put me in possession of all that Marian could tell.", "사흘 밤을 이 일에 바쳤고, 그것으로 매리언이 나에게 들려줄 수 있는 모든 것을 손에 넣기에 충분했다."]], [["My next proceeding was to gain as much additional evidence as I could procure from other people without exciting suspicion.", "다음으로 내가 한 일은, 의심을 사지 않으면서 다른 사람들에게서 얻어 낼 수 있는 만큼 증거를 더 모으는 것이었다."], ["I went myself to Mrs. Vesey to ascertain if Laura’s impression of having slept there was correct or not.", "나는 로라가 그곳에서 잤다고 하는 인상이 맞는지 아닌지 확인하러 직접 베지 부인을 찾아갔다."], ["In this case, from consideration for Mrs. Vesey’s age and infirmity, and in all subsequent cases of the same kind from considerations of caution, I kept our real position a secret, and was always careful to speak of Laura as “the late Lady Glyde.”", "이 경우에는 베지 부인의 나이와 병약함을 헤아려서, 그리고 그 뒤 같은 종류의 모든 경우에는 조심스러움을 위해서, 나는 우리의 실제 처지를 비밀에 부쳤고 로라를 언제나 “고인이 된 글라이드 부인”이라고 부르도록 신경 썼다."]], [["Mrs. Vesey’s answer to my inquiries only confirmed the apprehensions which I had previously felt.", "내 물음에 대한 베지 부인의 대답은 내가 전부터 품고 있던 우려를 확인해 줄 뿐이었다."], ["Laura had certainly written to say she would pass the night under the roof of her old friend—but she had never been near the house.", "로라가 옛 벗의 지붕 아래에서 하룻밤을 묵겠다고 편지를 쓴 것은 분명했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 집 근처에도 온 적이 없었다."]], [["Her mind in this instance, and, as I feared, in other instances besides, confusedly presented to her something which she had only intended to do in the false light of something which she had really done.", "이 경우에, 그리고 내가 우려하기로는 이 밖의 다른 경우들에서도, 그녀의 마음은 하려고 마음먹었을 뿐인 일을 실제로 한 일인 양 잘못된 빛깔로 어지럽게 떠올리고 있었다."], ["The unconscious contradiction of herself was easy to account for in this way—but it was likely to lead to serious results.", "그녀가 저도 모르게 자기 말을 뒤집는 것은 이렇게 설명하면 쉽게 납득할 수 있었지만, 그것은 심각한 결과를 낳을 만한 일이었다."], ["It was a stumble on the threshold at starting—it was a flaw in the evidence which told fatally against us.", "그것은 출발선의 문턱에서 발을 헛디딘 것이었고, 우리에게 치명적으로 불리하게 작용하는 증거의 흠이었다."]], [["When I next asked for the letter which Laura had written to Mrs. Vesey from Blackwater Park, it was given to me without the envelope, which had been thrown into the wastepaper basket, and long since destroyed.", "이어서 내가 로라가 블랙워터 파크에서 베지 부인에게 쓴 편지를 달라고 하자, 편지는 봉투 없이 건네졌다."], ["In the letter itself no date was mentioned—not even the day of the week.", "봉투는 휴지통에 던져졌다가 오래전에 없어져 버렸던 것이다."], ["It only contained these lines:—“Dearest Mrs.", "편지 자체에는 날짜가 적혀 있지 않았다."], ["Vesey, I am in sad distress and anxiety, and I may come to your house to-morrow night, and ask for a bed.", "요일조차 없었다. 거기에는 이런 몇 줄만이 담겨 있었다.—“가장 그리운 베지 부인, 저는 지금 몹시 괴롭고 불안합니다."], ["I can’t tell you what is the matter in this letter—I write it in such fear of being found out that I can fix my mind on nothing.", "내일 밤 부인 댁으로 찾아가 하룻밤 재워 달라고 부탁드리게 될지도 모르겠어요. 무슨 일인지는 이 편지에 적을 수가 없습니다—들킬까 봐 어찌나 두려운지 아무 데도 마음을 붙일 수가 없어요."], ["Pray be at home to see me.", "부디 집에 계셔서 저를 맞아 주세요."], ["I will give you a thousand kisses, and tell you everything.", "부인께 천 번의 입맞춤을 드리고, 모든 것을 다 말씀드리겠습니다. 부인을 사랑하는 로라 올림.”"], ["Your affectionate Laura.”", "이 몇 줄에 무슨 도움이 있었겠는가?"], ["What help was there in those lines? None.", "아무것도 없었다."]], [["On returning from Mrs. Vesey’s, I instructed Marian to write (observing the same caution which I practised myself) to Mrs.", "베지 부인 댁에서 돌아온 뒤, 나는 매리언에게 마이컬슨 부인 앞으로 편지를 쓰라고 일렀다(내가 지키던 것과 같은 조심을 지키면서 말이다)."], ["Michelson. She was to express, if she pleased, some general suspicion of Count Fosco’s conduct, and she was to ask the housekeeper to supply us with a plain statement of events, in the interests of truth.", "원한다면 포스코 백작의 처신에 대한 대략의 의혹을 내비쳐도 좋고, 진실을 위해서 그동안의 일을 있는 그대로 적어 보내 달라고 그 가정부에게 부탁하라고 했다."], ["While we were waiting for the answer, which reached us in a week’s time, I went to the doctor in St.", "일주일 만에 도착한 답장을 기다리는 동안, 나는 세인트존스우드의 의사를 찾아가, 카일 씨가 미처 알아볼 시간이 없었던 홀콤 양 동생의 마지막 병세에 대해 되도록 자세한 사정을 알아 오라고 홀콤 양이 보내서 왔노라고 나를 소개했다."], ["John’s Wood, introducing myself as sent by Miss Halcombe to collect, if possible, more particulars of her sister’s last illness than Mr. Kyrle had found the time to procure.", "구드리크 선생의 도움으로 나는 사망 진단서 사본 한 통과, 시신을 염하도록 고용되었던 여자(제인 굴드)와의 면담을 얻어 냈다."], ["By Mr. Goodricke’s assistance, I obtained a copy of the certificate of death, and an interview with the woman (Jane Gould) who had been employed to prepare the body for the grave.", "이 사람을 통해 나는 하녀 헤스터 핀혼과 연락할 방도도 알아냈다."], ["Through this person I also discovered a means of communicating with the servant, Hester Pinhorn. She had recently left her place in consequence of a disagreement with her mistress, and she was lodging with some people in the neighbourhood whom Mrs.", "그녀는 얼마 전 주인마님과 다투는 바람에 일자리를 그만두고, 굴드 부인이 아는 근처 사람들 집에 머물고 있었다."], ["Gould knew. In the manner here indicated I obtained the Narratives of the housekeeper, of the doctor, of Jane Gould, and of Hester Pinhorn, exactly as they are presented in these pages.", "이런 식으로 나는 가정부의 이야기, 의사의 이야기, 제인 굴드의 이야기, 헤스터 핀혼의 이야기를 여기 이 지면에 실린 그대로 손에 넣었다."]], [["Furnished with such additional evidence as these documents afforded, I considered myself to be sufficiently prepared for a consultation with Mr. Kyrle, and Marian wrote accordingly to mention my name to him, and to specify the day and hour at which I requested to see him on private business.", "이 문서들이 제공한 추가 증거로 무장하고 나니 카일 씨와 상의할 준비가 충분히 되었다고 생각되어, 매리언은 그에 따라 편지를 써서 그에게 내 이름을 알리고 내가 사사로운 용무로 그를 만나 뵙고 싶은 날짜와 시각을 밝혔다."]], [["There was time enough in the morning for me to take Laura out for her walk as usual, and to see her quietly settled at her drawing afterwards.", "그날 아침에는 여느 때처럼 로라를 데리고 산책을 나갔다가 돌아와 그녀가 조용히 그림 앞에 자리 잡는 것을 지켜볼 만한 시간이 넉넉했다."], ["She looked up at me with a new anxiety in her face as I rose to leave the room, and her fingers began to toy doubtfully, in the old way, with the brushes and pencils on the table.", "내가 방을 나서려고 일어서자 그녀는 얼굴에 새로운 불안을 띤 채 나를 올려다보았고, 그 손가락은 예전 버릇대로 탁자 위의 붓과 연필을 미덥지 못한 듯 만지작거리기 시작했다."]], [["“You are not tired of me yet?” she said.", "“아직 제가 싫증 나신 건 아니죠?”"], ["“You are not going away because you are tired of me?", "그녀가 말했다. “제가 싫증 나서 나가시는 건 아니죠?"], ["I will try to do better—I will try to get well.", "더 잘하도록 애써 볼게요—얼른 나으려고 애써 볼게요."], ["Are you as fond of me, Walter, as you used to be, now I am so pale and thin, and so slow in learning to draw?”", "월터, 이렇게 창백하고 여위고 그림 배우는 것도 이렇게 굼뜬 저를, 예전만큼 좋아하시나요?”"]], [["She spoke as a child might have spoken, she showed me her thoughts as a child might have shown them.", "그녀는 아이가 말하듯이 말했고, 아이가 제 속을 내보이듯이 나에게 제 생각을 내보였다."], ["I waited a few minutes longer—waited to tell her that she was dearer to me now than she had ever been in the past times.", "나는 몇 분을 더 머물렀다—그녀가 지금 나에게는 지난 어느 때보다도 더 소중하다는 말을 해 주려고 머물렀다."], ["“Try to get well again,”", "“다시 건강해지도록 애써 봐요.”"], ["I said, encouraging the new hope in the future which I saw dawning in her mind, “try to get well again, for Marian’s sake and for mine.”", "나는 그녀의 마음속에서 밝아 오는 것이 보이는 그 새로운 앞날의 희망을 북돋우며 말했다. “다시 건강해지도록 애써 봐요, 매리언을 위해서 그리고 나를 위해서.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["she said to herself, returning to her drawing.", "그녀가 그림으로 돌아가면서 혼잣말처럼 말했다."], ["“I must try, because they are both so fond of me.”", "“애써야지, 두 사람 다 나를 이렇게 좋아해 주니까.”"], ["She suddenly looked up again.", "그러다 그녀는 문득 다시 고개를 들었다."], ["“Don’t be gone long!", "“오래 나가 계시면 안 돼요!"], ["I can’t get on with my drawing, Walter, when you are not here to help me.”", "월터, 당신이 곁에서 도와주지 않으면 저는 그림을 못 그리는걸요.”"]], [["“I shall soon be back, my darling—soon be back to see how you are getting on.”", "“곧 돌아올게요, 그대여—곧 돌아와서 얼마나 잘 그렸나 볼게요.”"]], [["My voice faltered a little in spite of me.", "내 목소리는 나도 모르게 조금 떨렸다."], ["I forced myself from the room.", "나는 억지로 몸을 떼어 방을 나섰다."], ["It was no time, then, for parting with the self-control which might yet serve me in my need before the day was out.", "그때는 자제심을 놓아 버릴 때가 아니었다. 그 자제심이 그날이 저물기 전에 내가 절실히 필요로 할 때 아직 나를 도와줄지도 몰랐으니까."]], [["As I opened the door, I beckoned to Marian to follow me to the stairs.", "문을 열면서 나는 매리언에게 계단까지 따라 나오라고 손짓했다."], ["It was necessary to prepare her for a result which I felt might sooner or later follow my showing myself openly in the streets.", "내가 거리에 드러내 놓고 모습을 보인 결과로 언젠가는 벌어질 수 있겠다 싶은 일에 대비하도록 그녀를 미리 일러 둘 필요가 있었다."]], [["“I shall, in all probability, be back in a few hours,”", "“아마도 몇 시간이면 돌아올 거예요.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “and you will take care, as usual, to let no one inside the doors in my absence.", "“그리고 늘 그랬듯이 내가 없는 동안 문 안으로 아무도 들이지 않도록 조심해 주고요."], ["But if anything happens——”", "하지만 만에 하나 무슨 일이 생기면——”"]], [["“What can happen?”", "“무슨 일이 생길 수 있는데요?”"], ["she interposed quickly.", "그녀가 얼른 말을 가로챘다."], ["“Tell me plainly, Walter, if there is any danger, and I shall know how to meet it.”", "“월터, 위험이 있다면 솔직히 말해 줘요. 그러면 내가 어떻게 맞설지 알 테니까요.”"]], [["“The only danger,”", "“유일한 위험은,”"], ["I replied, “is that Sir Percival Glyde may have been recalled to London by the news of Laura’s escape.", "내가 대답했다. “퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 로라가 빠져나왔다는 소식을 듣고 런던으로 불려 왔을지도 모른다는 것이죠."], ["You are aware that he had me watched before I left England, and that he probably knows me by sight, although I don’t know him?”", "내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 그가 나를 감시하게 했다는 것, 그래서 나는 그를 몰라도 그는 아마 내 얼굴을 알고 있으리라는 것은 당신도 아시잖아요?”"]], [["She laid her hand on my shoulder and looked at me in anxious silence.", "그녀는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 걱정스러운 얼굴로 말없이 나를 바라보았다."], ["I saw she understood the serious risk that threatened us.", "나는 그녀가 우리를 위협하는 그 심각한 위험을 알아차렸음을 알았다."]], [["“It is not likely,” I said, “that I shall be seen in London again so soon, either by Sir Percival himself or by the persons in his employ.", "“퍼시벌 경 본인이든 그가 부리는 사람들이든, 이렇게 빨리 런던에서 다시 나를 볼 것 같지는 않아요.”"], ["But it is barely possible that an accident may happen.", "내가 말했다. “하지만 뜻밖의 일이 생길 가능성이 아주 없지는 않죠."], ["In that case, you will not be alarmed if I fail to return to-night, and you will satisfy any inquiry of Laura’s with the best excuse that you can make for me?", "그런 경우라도 내가 오늘 밤 돌아오지 못하더라도 놀라지 말고, 로라가 뭐라고 묻거든 당신이 지어낼 수 있는 가장 그럴듯한 핑계로 답해 주겠어요?"], ["If I find the least reason to suspect that I am watched, I will take good care that no spy follows me back to this house.", "감시받고 있다고 의심할 만한 낌새가 조금이라도 있으면, 어떤 첩자도 나를 따라 이 집까지 오지 못하도록 단단히 조심하겠어요."], ["Don’t doubt my return, Marian, however it may be delayed—and fear nothing.”", "아무리 늦어지더라도 내가 돌아오리라는 걸 의심하지 말아요, 매리언—그리고 아무것도 두려워하지 말고요.”"]], [["“Nothing!”", "“아무것도요!”"], ["she answered firmly.", "그녀가 단단하게 대답했다."], ["“You shall not regret, Walter, that you have only a woman to help you.”", "“월터, 도와줄 사람이 여자 하나뿐이라는 걸 후회하게 만들지는 않겠어요.”"], ["She paused, and detained me for a moment longer.", "그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 나를 조금 더 붙들었다."], ["“Take care!”", "“조심해요!”"], ["she said, pressing my hand anxiously—“take care!”", "그녀는 걱정스럽게 내 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. “조심해요!”"]], [["I left her, and set forth to pave the way for discovery—the dark and doubtful way, which began at the lawyer’s door.", "나는 그녀를 남겨 두고, 진상을 밝힐 길을 닦으러 나섰다—변호사의 문간에서 시작되는, 어둡고도 앞을 알 수 없는 그 길을."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["No circumstance of the slightest importance happened on my way to the offices of Messrs. Gilmore & Kyrle, in Chancery Lane.", "챈서리 레인에 있는 길모어 앤드 카일 법률 사무소로 가는 길에는 조금이라도 중요하달 만한 일이 하나도 일어나지 않았다."]], [["While my card was being taken in to Mr. Kyrle, a consideration occurred to me which I deeply regretted not having thought of before.", "내 명함이 카일 씨에게 들어가는 동안, 진작 떠올리지 못한 것이 몹시 후회스러운 한 가지 생각이 문득 머리를 스쳤다."], ["The information derived from Marian’s diary made it a matter of certainty that Count Fosco had opened her first letter from Blackwater Park to Mr. Kyrle, and had, by means of his wife, intercepted the second.", "매리언의 일기에서 얻은 정보로 미루어 볼 때, 포스코 백작이 블랙워터 파크에서 카일 씨에게 보낸 그녀의 첫 번째 편지를 뜯어보았고 아내를 시켜 두 번째 편지를 가로챘다는 것은 확실한 사실이었다."], ["He was therefore well aware of the address of the office, and he would naturally infer that if Marian wanted advice and assistance, after Laura’s escape from the Asylum, she would apply once more to the experience of Mr. Kyrle.", "그러니 그는 이 사무소의 주소를 훤히 알고 있었을 터이고, 로라가 정신병원에서 빠져나온 뒤 매리언이 조언과 도움을 구하려 든다면 다시 한 번 카일 씨의 경험에 기대리라고 자연스레 짐작했을 것이다."], ["In this case the office in Chancery Lane was the very first place which he and Sir Percival would cause to be watched, and if the same persons were chosen for the purpose who had been employed to follow me, before my departure from England, the fact of my return would in all probability be ascertained on that very day.", "그렇다면 챈서리 레인의 이 사무소야말로 그와 퍼시벌 경이 가장 먼저 감시를 붙일 곳이었고, 내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 나를 미행하던 바로 그자들이 그 일에 뽑혔다면 내가 돌아왔다는 사실은 십중팔구 바로 그날로 드러날 것이었다."], ["I had thought, generally, of the chances of my being recognised in the streets, but the special risk connected with the office had never occurred to me until the present moment.", "거리에서 얼굴이 알려질 가능성은 대략 헤아려 보았으나, 이 사무소에 얽힌 특별한 위험은 이 순간까지 한 번도 생각나지 않았다."], ["It was too late now to repair this unfortunate error in judgment—too late to wish that I had made arrangements for meeting the lawyer in some place privately appointed beforehand.", "이 잘못된 판단을 돌이키기에는 이미 늦었다. 미리 은밀히 정해 둔 어딘가에서 변호사를 만나도록 손을 써 두었더라면 하고 바라기에도 늦었다."], ["I could only resolve to be cautious on leaving Chancery Lane, and not to go straight home again under any circumstances whatever.", "나는 다만 챈서리 레인을 나설 때 조심하기로, 그리고 어떤 일이 있어도 곧장 집으로 돌아가지는 않기로 마음먹는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["After waiting a few minutes I was shown into Mr. Kyrle’s private room.", "몇 분을 기다린 뒤 나는 카일 씨의 집무실로 안내되었다."], ["He was a pale, thin, quiet, self-possessed man, with a very attentive eye, a very low voice, and a very undemonstrative manner—not (as I judged) ready with his sympathy where strangers were concerned, and not at all easy to disturb in his professional composure.", "그는 창백하고 여윈, 조용하고 침착한 사람으로, 눈매가 몹시 주의 깊고 목소리가 아주 낮았으며 태도에는 감정을 드러내는 구석이 조금도 없었다. 내가 보기에 낯선 사람에게 선뜻 동정을 내주는 인물도 아니었고, 직업적인 평정을 흐트러뜨리기가 결코 쉬운 사람도 아니었다."], ["A better man for my purpose could hardly have been found.", "내 목적에 이보다 더 알맞은 사람은 찾기 어려웠을 것이다."], ["If he committed himself to a decision at all, and if the decision was favourable, the strength of our case was as good as proved from that moment.", "그가 어떤 판단을 내리기로 하고 그 판단이 우리에게 유리하다면, 우리 주장의 힘은 그 순간부터 증명된 것이나 다름없었다."]], [["“Before I enter on the business which brings me here,”", "“저를 이리로 오게 한 용건에 들어가기 전에,” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “I ought to warn you, Mr.", "“미리 말씀드려 둘 것이 있습니다, 카일 씨."], ["Kyrle, that the shortest statement I can make of it may occupy some little time.”", "아무리 짧게 말씀드려도 시간이 조금 걸릴지 모릅니다.”"]], [["“My time is at Miss Halcombe’s disposal,”", "“제 시간은 홀콤 양이 쓰시라고 있는 것입니다.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Where any interests of hers are concerned, I represent my partner personally, as well as professionally.", "“그분의 이해가 걸린 일이라면 저는 제 동업자를 직무상으로만이 아니라 개인적으로도 대리합니다."], ["It was his request that I should do so, when he ceased to take an active part in business.”", "그분이 실무에서 손을 뗄 때 그렇게 해 달라고 부탁하셨으니까요.”"]], [["“May I inquire whether Mr. Gilmore is in England?”", "“길모어 씨는 지금 영국에 계신지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“He is not, he is living with his relatives in Germany.", "“아닙니다, 독일에서 친척들과 지내고 계십니다."], ["His health has improved, but the period of his return is still uncertain.”", "건강은 좋아지셨지만 돌아오실 시기는 아직 정해지지 않았습니다.”"]], [["While we were exchanging these few preliminary words, he had been searching among the papers before him, and he now produced from them a sealed letter.", "이런 몇 마디 인사말을 주고받는 동안 그는 앞에 놓인 서류들을 뒤적이고 있었고, 이제 그 속에서 봉인된 편지 한 통을 꺼냈다."], ["I thought he was about to hand the letter to me, but, apparently changing his mind, he placed it by itself on the table, settled himself in his chair, and silently waited to hear what I had to say.", "나는 그가 그 편지를 내게 건네려는 줄 알았으나, 마음을 바꾼 듯 그것을 따로 탁자 위에 놓고는 의자에 자세를 고쳐 앉아, 내가 할 말을 잠자코 기다렸다."]], [["Without wasting a moment in prefatory words of any sort, I entered on my narrative, and put him in full possession of the events which have already been related in these pages.", "나는 서두를 꾸미느라 한순간도 허비하지 않고 곧바로 이야기에 들어가, 이 지면에 이미 적어 온 사건들을 그에게 남김없이 알렸다."]], [["Lawyer as he was to the very marrow of his bones, I startled him out of his professional composure.", "뼛속까지 변호사인 사람이었건만 나는 그의 직업적인 평정을 흔들어 놓았다."], ["Expressions of incredulity and surprise, which he could not repress, interrupted me several times before I had done.", "미처 억누르지 못한 불신과 놀람의 말이 내 이야기가 끝나기도 전에 몇 번이나 나를 가로막았다."], ["I persevered, however, to the end, and as soon as I reached it, boldly asked the one important question—", "그래도 나는 끝까지 밀고 나갔고, 이야기를 마치자마자 대담하게 그 중대한 물음 하나를 던졌다."]], [["“What is your opinion, Mr. Kyrle?”", "“어떻게 보십니까, 카일 씨?”"]], [["He was too cautious to commit himself to an answer without taking time to recover his self-possession first.", "그는 우선 평정을 되찾을 시간을 갖지도 않고 어떤 대답에 자신을 묶어 두기에는 지나치게 신중한 사람이었다."]], [["“Before I give my opinion,”", "“제 견해를 말씀드리기 전에,”"], ["he said, “I must beg permission to clear the ground by a few questions.”", "그가 말했다. “몇 가지 질문으로 바닥을 정리하도록 허락해 주셔야겠습니다.”"]], [["He put the questions—sharp, suspicious, unbelieving questions, which clearly showed me, as they proceeded, that he thought I was the victim of a delusion, and that he might even have doubted, but for my introduction to him by Miss Halcombe, whether I was not attempting the perpetration of a cunningly-designed fraud.", "그는 질문을 던졌다. 날카롭고 미심쩍어하며 믿지 않는 질문들이었고, 그것이 이어지는 동안 나는 그가 나를 망상에 빠진 사람으로 여기고 있다는 것을, 그리고 홀콤 양의 소개가 없었더라면 내가 교묘하게 꾸민 사기를 저지르려는 것은 아닌지 의심했을지도 모른다는 것을 똑똑히 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Do you believe that I have spoken the truth, Mr.", "“제가 진실을 말했다고 믿으십니까, 카일 씨?”"], ["Kyrle?” I asked, when he had done examining me.", "그가 나를 다 캐묻고 났을 때 내가 물었다."]], [["“So far as your own convictions are concerned, I am certain you have spoken the truth,”", "“선생 자신의 확신에 관한 한, 진실을 말씀하셨다고 확신합니다.”"], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I have the highest esteem for Miss Halcombe, and I have therefore every reason to respect a gentleman whose mediation she trusts in a matter of this kind.", "“저는 홀콤 양을 대단히 존경하며, 따라서 그분이 이런 일에 중재를 맡길 만큼 믿으시는 신사분을 존중할 온갖 이유가 있습니다."], ["I will even go farther, if you like, and admit, for courtesy’s sake and for argument’s sake, that the identity of Lady Glyde as a living person is a proved fact to Miss Halcombe and yourself.", "원하신다면 한 걸음 더 나아가, 예의를 위해서 그리고 논의를 위해서, 글라이드 부인이 살아 있는 사람으로 그 자리에 있다는 것이 홀콤 양과 선생께는 증명된 사실이라고 인정하겠습니다."], ["But you come to me for a legal opinion.", "그러나 선생께서는 법률적인 견해를 들으러 저를 찾아오셨습니다."], ["As a lawyer, and as a lawyer only, it is my duty to tell you, Mr.", "변호사로서, 오직 변호사로서 말씀드리는 것이 제 의무이니 말씀드립니다."], ["Hartright, that you have not the shadow of a case.”", "하트라이트 씨, 선생께는 소송이 될 만한 것이 그림자만큼도 없습니다.”"]], [["“You put it strongly, Mr. Kyrle.”", "“심하게 말씀하시는군요, 카일 씨.”"]], [["“I will try to put it plainly as well.", "“분명하게 말씀드리려고도 애써 보겠습니다."], ["The evidence of Lady Glyde’s death is, on the face of it, clear and satisfactory.", "글라이드 부인이 사망했다는 증거는 겉으로 보기에 명확하고 흠잡을 데가 없습니다."], ["There is her aunt’s testimony to prove that she came to Count Fosco’s house, that she fell ill, and that she died.", "그분이 포스코 백작의 집에 왔고, 병이 들었고, 죽었다는 것을 증명하는 고모의 증언이 있습니다."], ["There is the testimony of the medical certificate to prove the death, and to show that it took place under natural circumstances.", "사망을 증명하고 그것이 자연스러운 정황에서 일어났음을 보여 주는 의사 진단서의 증언이 있습니다."], ["There is the fact of the funeral at Limmeridge, and there is the assertion of the inscription on the tomb.", "리머리지에서 장례가 치러졌다는 사실이 있고, 무덤의 비문이 그렇게 단언하고 있습니다."], ["That is the case you want to overthrow.", "이것이 선생께서 뒤엎으려는 사건입니다."], ["What evidence have you to support the declaration on your side that the person who died and was buried was not Lady Glyde?", "죽어서 묻힌 사람이 글라이드 부인이 아니라는 선생 쪽의 주장을 뒷받침할 증거로는 무엇이 있습니까?"], ["Let us run through the main points of your statement and see what they are worth.", "선생 진술의 요점들을 하나하나 훑어보며 그것들이 얼마나 값이 나가는지 봅시다."], ["Miss Halcombe goes to a certain private Asylum, and there sees a certain female patient.", "홀콤 양이 어느 사립 정신병원에 가서 그곳의 어떤 여자 환자를 봅니다."], ["It is known that a woman named Anne Catherick, and bearing an extraordinary personal resemblance to Lady Glyde, escaped from the Asylum; it is known that the person received there last July was received as Anne Catherick brought back; it is known that the gentleman who brought her back warned Mr.", "앤 캐서릭이라는 이름의, 글라이드 부인과 놀랍도록 닮은 여자가 그 병원에서 달아났다는 것은 알려진 사실입니다. 지난 7월에 그곳에 들어온 사람은 되잡혀 온 앤 캐서릭으로서 받아들여졌다는 것도 알려진 사실입니다."], ["Fairlie that it was part of her insanity to be bent on personating his dead niece; and it is known that she did repeatedly declare herself in the Asylum (where no one believed her) to be Lady Glyde.", "그 여자를 데려온 신사가 죽은 조카를 사칭하려 드는 것이 그 여자 광증의 일부라고 페얼리 씨에게 일러 주었다는 것도 알려진 사실이고, 그 여자가 병원 안에서 자기가 글라이드 부인이라고 거듭 주장했다는 것도, 그리고 그곳에서 아무도 그 말을 믿지 않았다는 것도 알려진 사실입니다."], ["These are all facts.", "이것이 모두 사실입니다."], ["What have you to set against them?", "여기에 맞세울 것으로 선생께는 무엇이 있습니까?"], ["Miss Halcombe’s recognition of the woman, which recognition after-events invalidate or contradict.", "홀콤 양이 그 여자를 알아보았다는 것뿐인데, 그 뒤에 일어난 일들이 그 확인을 무효로 만들거나 뒤집고 있습니다."], ["Does Miss Halcombe assert her supposed sister’s identity to the owner of the Asylum, and take legal means for rescuing her?", "홀콤 양은 병원 주인에게 자기 동생이 맞다고 주장하고 법적인 절차를 밟아 그 여자를 구해 냈습니까?"], ["No, she secretly bribes a nurse to let her escape.", "아닙니다, 몰래 간호사를 매수해 달아나게 했습니다."], ["When the patient has been released in this doubtful manner, and is taken to Mr.", "환자가 그런 미심쩍은 방식으로 풀려나 페얼리 씨에게 데려가졌을 때 그가 그 여자를 알아보았습니까?"], ["Fairlie, does he recognise her?", "조카가 죽었다는 그의 믿음이 한순간이라도 흔들렸습니까?"], ["Is he staggered for one instant in his belief of his niece’s death? No.", "아닙니다."], ["Do the servants recognise her?", "하인들은 알아보았습니까? 아닙니다."], ["No. Is she kept in the neighbourhood to assert her own identity, and to stand the test of further proceedings?", "그 여자를 그 근처에 머무르게 하여 스스로 신원을 주장하게 하고 뒤이은 절차의 시험을 견디게 했습니까?"], ["No, she is privately taken to London.", "아닙니다, 남몰래 런던으로 데려갔습니다."], ["In the meantime you have recognised her also, but you are not a relative—you are not even an old friend of the family.", "그동안 선생 또한 그 여자를 알아보셨지만, 선생은 친척이 아닙니다. 집안의 오랜 친구조차 아닙니다."], ["The servants contradict you, and Mr. Fairlie contradicts Miss Halcombe, and the supposed Lady Glyde contradicts herself.", "하인들은 선생의 말을 부인하고, 페얼리 씨는 홀콤 양의 말을 부인하며, 글라이드 부인이라는 그 사람은 스스로 제 말을 뒤집습니다."], ["She declares she passed the night in London at a certain house.", "그 사람은 런던의 어느 집에서 밤을 지냈다고 말합니다."], ["Your own evidence shows that she has never been near that house, and your own admission is that her condition of mind prevents you from producing her anywhere to submit to investigation, and to speak for herself.", "선생 자신의 증언은 그 사람이 그 집 근처에도 간 적이 없음을 보여 주고, 선생 자신이 인정하시기를 그 사람의 정신 상태 때문에 어디에도 내세워 조사를 받게 하거나 스스로 말하게 할 수 없다고 하십니다."], ["I pass over minor points of evidence on both sides to save time, and I ask you, if this case were to go now into a court of law—to go before a jury, bound to take facts as they reasonably appear—where are your proofs?”", "시간을 아끼려 양쪽의 자잘한 증거들은 넘어가고 여쭙겠습니다. 이 사건이 지금 법정으로, 사실을 상식이 보이는 대로 받아들일 수밖에 없는 배심 앞으로 간다면, 선생의 증거는 어디에 있습니까?”"]], [["I was obliged to wait and collect myself before I could answer him.", "나는 그에게 대답하기 전에 잠시 기다리며 마음을 가다듬어야 했다."], ["It was the first time the story of Laura and the story of Marian had been presented to me from a stranger’s point of view—the first time the terrible obstacles that lay across our path had been made to show themselves in their true character.", "로라의 이야기와 매리언의 이야기가 남의 눈을 통해 내 앞에 펼쳐진 것은 그때가 처음이었고, 우리 앞길을 가로막은 무서운 장애물들이 제 본모습을 드러낸 것도 그때가 처음이었다."]], [["“There can be no doubt,”", "“의심할 여지가 없습니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “that the facts, as you have stated them, appear to tell against us, but——”", "“말씀하신 대로의 사실들이 우리에게 불리하게 보인다는 것은요. 하지만——”"]], [["“But you think those facts can be explained away,”", "“하지만 그 사실들은 해명해서 없앨 수 있다고 생각하시는 거지요.”"], ["interposed Mr. Kyrle.", "카일 씨가 말을 받았다."], ["“Let me tell you the result of my experience on that point.", "“그 점에 관해 제 경험이 알려 준 바를 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["When an English jury has to choose between a plain fact ON the surface and a long explanation UNDER the surface, it always takes the fact in preference to the explanation.", "영국의 배심은 겉으로 드러난 단순한 사실과 그 밑에 깔린 긴 설명 가운데 하나를 골라야 할 때면 언제나 설명보다 사실을 택합니다."], ["For example, Lady Glyde (I call the lady you represent by that name for argument’s sake) declares she has slept at a certain house, and it is proved that she has not slept at that house.", "이를테면 글라이드 부인이, 논의를 위해 선생께서 대리하시는 그분을 그렇게 부르겠습니다만, 어느 집에서 잤다고 말하는데 그 집에서 자지 않았다는 것이 증명됩니다."], ["You explain this circumstance by entering into the state of her mind, and deducing from it a metaphysical conclusion.", "선생께서는 그분의 정신 상태를 파고들어 거기서 형이상학적인 결론을 끌어내어 이 사정을 설명하십니다."], ["I don’t say the conclusion is wrong—I only say that the jury will take the fact of her contradicting herself in preference to any reason for the contradiction that you can offer.”", "저는 그 결론이 틀렸다고 말하는 것이 아닙니다. 다만 배심은 선생께서 내놓으실 수 있는 그 어떤 모순의 이유보다도 그분이 스스로 제 말을 뒤집었다는 사실 쪽을 택하리라는 말씀입니다.”"]], [["“But is it not possible,”", "“하지만 인내와 노력을 다한다면,”"], ["I urged, “by dint of patience and exertion, to discover additional evidence?", "내가 힘주어 말했다. “증거를 더 찾아내는 것이 가능하지 않겠습니까?"], ["Miss Halcombe and I have a few hundred pounds——”", "홀콤 양과 저에게는 수백 파운드가——”"]], [["He looked at me with a half-suppressed pity, and shook his head.", "그는 반쯤 억누른 연민의 눈으로 나를 보며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Consider the subject, Mr. Hartright, from your own point of view,”", "“하트라이트 씨, 이 일을 선생 자신의 관점에서 한번 생각해 보십시오.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“If you are right about Sir Percival Glyde and Count Fosco (which I don’t admit, mind), every imaginable difficulty would be thrown in the way of your getting fresh evidence.", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 포스코 백작에 대한 선생의 판단이 옳다면, 저는 인정하지 않습니다만 유념해 두십시오, 새 증거를 얻으려는 선생 앞에는 상상할 수 있는 온갖 어려움이 놓일 것입니다."], ["Every obstacle of litigation would be raised—every point in the case would be systematically contested—and by the time we had spent our thousands instead of our hundreds, the final result would, in all probability, be against us.", "소송에서 나올 수 있는 방해란 방해는 다 나올 테고, 사건의 모든 쟁점이 조직적으로 다투어질 것이며, 우리가 수백 파운드가 아니라 수천 파운드를 쓰고 났을 즈음이면 최종 결과는 십중팔구 우리에게 불리할 것입니다."], ["Questions of identity, where instances of personal resemblance are concerned, are, in themselves, the hardest of all questions to settle—the hardest, even when they are free from the complications which beset the case we are now discussing.", "사람이 닮았다는 사정이 얽힌 신원 문제는 그 자체로 가장 판가름하기 어려운 문제입니다. 지금 우리가 이야기하고 있는 이 사건을 둘러싼 복잡한 사정이 없을 때조차 그렇습니다."], ["I really see no prospect of throwing any light whatever on this extraordinary affair.", "저로서는 이 기이한 일에 조금이라도 빛을 비출 가망이 도무지 보이지 않습니다."], ["Even if the person buried in Limmeridge churchyard be not Lady Glyde, she was, in life, on your own showing, so like her, that we should gain nothing, if we applied for the necessary authority to have the body exhumed.", "설령 리머리지 교회 묘지에 묻힌 사람이 글라이드 부인이 아니라 해도, 선생 말씀대로 그 사람은 살아 있을 때 그분과 너무나 닮았으니, 시신을 파낼 권한을 얻어 낸다 한들 우리가 얻을 것은 없습니다."], ["In short, there is no case, Mr.", "요컨대 소송이 될 만한 것이 없습니다, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Hartright—there is really no case.”", "정말로 없습니다.”"]], [["I was determined to believe that there WAS a case, and in that determination shifted my ground, and appealed to him once more.", "나는 소송이 될 만한 것이 있다고 기어이 믿기로 작정했고, 그 작정에 따라 발판을 옮겨 다시 한 번 그에게 호소했다."]], [["“Are there not other proofs that we might produce besides the proof of identity?”", "“신원을 증명하는 것 말고 우리가 내놓을 수 있는 다른 증거는 없겠습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Not as you are situated,” he replied.", "“선생께서 처하신 형편에서는 없습니다.”"], ["“The simplest and surest of all proofs, the proof by comparison of dates, is, as I understand, altogether out of your reach.", "그가 대답했다. “모든 증거 가운데 가장 단순하고 가장 확실한 것, 곧 날짜를 맞대어 보는 증명은 제가 알기로 선생의 손이 전혀 닿지 않는 곳에 있습니다."], ["If you could show a discrepancy between the date of the doctor’s certificate and the date of Lady Glyde’s journey to London, the matter would wear a totally different aspect, and I should be the first to say, Let us go on.”", "의사 진단서의 날짜와 글라이드 부인이 런던으로 간 날짜 사이의 어긋남을 보여 주실 수 있다면 이 일은 아주 다른 모습을 띠게 될 테고, 저부터 나서서 시작합시다 하고 말씀드릴 것입니다.”"]], [["“That date may yet be recovered, Mr. Kyrle.”", "“그 날짜는 아직 찾아낼 수 있습니다, 카일 씨.”"]], [["“On the day when it is recovered, Mr. Hartright, you will have a case.", "“그 날짜를 찾아내는 날, 하트라이트 씨, 선생께는 소송이 생깁니다."], ["If you have any prospect, at this moment, of getting at it—tell me, and we shall see if I can advise you.”", "지금 이 순간 그것에 다가갈 가망이 조금이라도 있다면 말씀해 주십시오. 제가 조언을 드릴 수 있을지 보겠습니다.”"]], [["I considered.", "나는 생각해 보았다."], ["The housekeeper could not help us—Laura could not help us—Marian could not help us.", "가정부는 우리를 도울 수 없었다. 로라도 도울 수 없었다."], ["In all probability, the only persons in existence who knew the date were Sir Percival and the Count.", "매리언도 도울 수 없었다. 그 날짜를 아는 사람은 이 세상에 십중팔구 퍼시벌 경과 백작뿐이었다."]], [["“I can think of no means of ascertaining the date at present,”", "“지금으로서는 그 날짜를 알아낼 방법이 떠오르지 않습니다.”"], ["I said, “because I can think of no persons who are sure to know it, but Count Fosco and Sir Percival Glyde.”", "내가 말했다. “그것을 틀림없이 알고 있을 사람으로는 포스코 백작과 퍼시벌 글라이드 경밖에 떠오르지 않기 때문입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Kyrle’s calmly attentive face relaxed, for the first time, into a smile.", "카일 씨의 차분하고 주의 깊은 얼굴이 처음으로 풀어지며 미소가 되었다."]], [["“With your opinion of the conduct of those two gentlemen,”", "“그 두 신사의 행실에 대해 그런 견해를 갖고 계시니,”"], ["he said, “you don’t expect help in that quarter, I presume?", "그가 말했다. “그쪽에서 도움을 기대하지는 않으시겠지요?"], ["If they have combined to gain large sums of money by a conspiracy, they are not likely to confess it, at any rate.”", "두 사람이 공모하여 큰돈을 얻은 것이라면 어쨌든 그것을 자백할 리는 없을 테니까요.”"]], [["“They may be forced to confess it, Mr. Kyrle.”", "“자백하도록 몰릴 수는 있습니다, 카일 씨.”"]], [["“By whom?”", "“누구에게 말입니까?”"]], [["“By me.”", "“저에게 말입니다.”"]], [["We both rose.", "우리는 함께 일어섰다."], ["He looked me attentively in the face with more appearance of interest than he had shown yet.", "그는 지금까지 보인 것보다 더 관심이 어린 눈으로 내 얼굴을 찬찬히 들여다보았다."], ["I could see that I had perplexed him a little.", "내가 그를 조금 어리둥절하게 만들었다는 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“You are very determined,” he said.", "“대단히 굳게 마음먹으셨군요.”"], ["“You have, no doubt, a personal motive for proceeding, into which it is not my business to inquire.", "그가 말했다. “일을 밀고 나가시는 데에 개인적인 동기가 있으시겠지요."], ["If a case can be produced in the future, I can only say, my best assistance is at your service.", "그것은 제가 물을 바가 아닙니다. 앞으로 소송이 될 만한 것이 갖추어진다면 제 힘껏 도와 드리겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 것이 없습니다."], ["At the same time I must warn you, as the money question always enters into the law question, that I see little hope, even if you ultimately established the fact of Lady Glyde’s being alive, of recovering her fortune.", "다만 돈 문제는 언제나 법 문제에 따라붙는 법이니 미리 일러 두겠습니다. 설령 끝내 글라이드 부인이 살아 계시다는 사실을 확정하신다 해도 그분의 재산을 되찾을 가망은 거의 없어 보입니다."], ["The foreigner would probably leave the country before proceedings were commenced, and Sir Percival’s embarrassments are numerous enough and pressing enough to transfer almost any sum of money he may possess from himself to his creditors.", "그 외국인은 절차가 시작되기 전에 이 나라를 떠날 테고, 퍼시벌 경의 빚은 그가 가진 돈이 얼마든 거의 다 채권자들에게 넘어갈 만큼 많고도 급합니다."], ["You are of course aware——”", "물론 알고 계시겠지만——”"]], [["I stopped him at that point.", "나는 거기서 그를 막았다."]], [["“Let me beg that we may not discuss Lady Glyde’s affairs,”", "“글라이드 부인의 재산 이야기는 하지 않았으면 합니다.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I have never known anything about them in former times, and I know nothing of them now—except that her fortune is lost.", "“저는 예전에도 그 일에 대해 아무것도 알지 못했고 지금도 아무것도 모릅니다. 다만 그분의 재산이 없어졌다는 것만 알 뿐입니다."], ["You are right in assuming that I have personal motives for stirring in this matter.", "제가 이 일에 나서는 데에 개인적인 동기가 있다고 보신 것은 옳습니다."], ["I wish those motives to be always as disinterested as they are at the present moment——”", "저는 그 동기가 지금 이 순간처럼 언제까지나 사심 없는 것이기를 바랍니다——”"]], [["He tried to interpose and explain.", "그는 말을 끼워 넣어 해명하려 했다."], ["I was a little heated, I suppose, by feeling that he had doubted me, and I went on bluntly, without waiting to hear him.", "그가 나를 의심했다는 느낌에 조금 달아올랐던 모양으로, 나는 그의 말을 기다리지 않고 무뚝뚝하게 이어 나갔다."]], [["“There shall be no money motive,”", "“돈이 동기가 되는 일은 없을 것입니다.”"], ["I said, “no idea of personal advantage in the service I mean to render to Lady Glyde.", "내가 말했다. “제가 글라이드 부인께 해 드리려는 일에 제 이익을 셈하는 마음은 없을 것입니다."], ["She has been cast out as a stranger from the house in which she was born—a lie which records her death has been written on her mother’s tomb—and there are two men, alive and unpunished, who are responsible for it.", "그분은 자기가 태어난 집에서 남처럼 쫓겨났습니다. 그분의 죽음을 기록한 거짓말이 어머니의 무덤에 새겨졌습니다."], ["That house shall open again to receive her in the presence of every soul who followed the false funeral to the grave—that lie shall be publicly erased from the tombstone by the authority of the head of the family, and those two men shall answer for their crime to ME, though the justice that sits in tribunals is powerless to pursue them.", "그리고 그 일에 책임이 있는 두 사내가 살아서 벌도 받지 않고 있습니다. 그 거짓 장례를 무덤까지 따라간 사람들이 하나도 빠짐없이 보는 앞에서 그 집은 다시 열려 그분을 맞아들일 것이고, 그 거짓말은 집안 어른의 권한으로 묘비에서 공공연히 지워질 것이며, 법정에 앉은 정의가 그자들을 뒤쫓을 힘이 없다 해도 그 두 사내는 제게 그 죄를 답해야 할 것입니다."], ["I have given my life to that purpose, and, alone as I stand, if God spares me, I will accomplish it.”", "저는 그 일에 제 목숨을 바쳤고, 이렇게 홀로 서 있으나 하느님이 저를 살려 두시는 한 기어이 그것을 이루겠습니다.”"]], [["He drew back towards his table, and said nothing.", "그는 탁자 쪽으로 물러서며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["His face showed plainly that he thought my delusion had got the better of my reason, and that he considered it totally useless to give me any more advice.", "내 망상이 이성을 이겨 버렸다고 그가 생각한다는 것, 그리고 내게 더 조언해 보아야 아무 소용없다고 여긴다는 것이 그의 얼굴에 뚜렷이 드러나 있었다."]], [["“We each keep our opinion, Mr. Kyrle,”", "“우리는 저마다 제 생각을 지키기로 하지요, 카일 씨.”"], ["I said, “and we must wait till the events of the future decide between us.", "내가 말했다. “그리고 앞으로 일어날 일들이 우리 사이를 판가름해 줄 때까지 기다려야겠지요."], ["In the meantime, I am much obliged to you for the attention you have given to my statement.", "그동안 제 진술을 귀 기울여 들어 주신 데 깊이 감사드립니다."], ["You have shown me that the legal remedy lies, in every sense of the word, beyond our means.", "선생께서는 법이라는 구제책이 온갖 뜻에서 우리 힘 밖에 있음을 보여 주셨습니다."], ["We cannot produce the law proof, and we are not rich enough to pay the law expenses.", "우리는 법이 요구하는 증명을 내놓을 수 없고, 소송 비용을 댈 만큼 넉넉하지도 못합니다."], ["It is something gained to know that.”", "그것을 알게 된 것만도 얻은 바가 있습니다.”"]], [["I bowed and walked to the door.", "나는 고개를 숙여 인사하고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["He called me back and gave me the letter which I had seen him place on the table by itself at the beginning of our interview.", "그는 나를 불러 세워, 면담이 시작될 때 탁자 위에 따로 놓아두는 것을 내가 보았던 그 편지를 건네주었다."]], [["“This came by post a few days ago,”", "“며칠 전에 우편으로 온 것입니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Perhaps you will not mind delivering it?", "“번거로우시겠지만 전해 주시겠습니까?"], ["Pray tell Miss Halcombe, at the same time, that I sincerely regret being, thus far, unable to help her, except by advice, which will not be more welcome, I am afraid, to her than to you.”", "아울러 홀콤 양께 이렇게 전해 주십시오. 지금까지는 조언 말고 달리 도와 드릴 길이 없어 진심으로 유감이라고, 그 조언이라는 것도 선생께 그랬듯 그분께도 반갑지는 않을 듯하여 송구하다고요.”"]], [["I looked at the letter while he was speaking.", "그가 말하는 동안 나는 그 편지를 들여다보았다."], ["It was addressed to “Miss Halcombe. Care of Messrs.", "수신인은 “챈서리 레인, 길모어 앤드 카일 법률 사무소 전교, 홀콤 양”이라고 적혀 있었다."], ["Gilmore & Kyrle, Chancery Lane.” The handwriting was quite unknown to me.", "필적은 내가 전혀 모르는 것이었다."]], [["On leaving the room I asked one last question.", "방을 나서면서 나는 마지막으로 한 가지를 물었다."]], [["“Do you happen to know,”", "“혹시 아십니까,” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “if Sir Percival Glyde is still in Paris?”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아직 파리에 있는지요?”"]], [["“He has returned to London,”", "“런던으로 돌아왔습니다.”"], ["replied Mr. Kyrle.", "카일 씨가 대답했다."], ["“At least I heard so from his solicitor, whom I met yesterday.”", "“적어도 어제 만난 그의 사무 변호사에게서 그렇게 들었습니다.”"]], [["After that answer I went out.", "그 대답을 듣고 나는 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["On leaving the office the first precaution to be observed was to abstain from attracting attention by stopping to look about me.", "사무소를 나서면서 지켜야 할 첫 번째 조심은 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보아 남의 눈길을 끄는 짓을 삼가는 것이었다."], ["I walked towards one of the quietest of the large squares on the north of Holborn, then suddenly stopped and turned round at a place where a long stretch of pavement was left behind me.", "나는 홀본 북쪽의 큰 광장들 가운데 가장 한적한 곳을 향해 걸었고, 뒤로 긴 보도가 훤히 남는 자리에 이르렀을 때 문득 멈추어 서서 뒤를 돌아보았다."]], [["There were two men at the corner of the square who had stopped also, and who were standing talking together.", "광장 모퉁이에 사내 둘이 있었는데 그들도 걸음을 멈추고 서서 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["After a moment’s reflection I turned back so as to pass them.", "잠시 생각한 끝에 나는 그들 곁을 지나가도록 되돌아섰다."], ["One moved as I came near, and turned the corner leading from the square into the street.", "내가 가까이 가자 한 사람이 몸을 움직여 광장에서 거리로 이어지는 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다."], ["The other remained stationary.", "다른 하나는 그 자리에 그대로 있었다."], ["I looked at him as I passed and instantly recognised one of the men who had watched me before I left England.", "나는 지나가면서 그를 보았고, 내가 영국을 떠나기 전에 나를 감시하던 자들 가운데 하나임을 대번에 알아보았다."]], [["If I had been free to follow my own instincts, I should probably have begun by speaking to the man, and have ended by knocking him down.", "내 본능이 시키는 대로 할 수 있었다면 나는 아마 그 사내에게 말을 거는 것으로 시작해 그를 때려눕히는 것으로 끝냈을 것이다."], ["But I was bound to consider consequences.", "그러나 나는 뒷일을 헤아리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["If I once placed myself publicly in the wrong, I put the weapons at once into Sir Percival’s hands.", "한 번이라도 남들이 보는 앞에서 내가 잘못한 처지가 된다면, 그것은 곧바로 퍼시벌 경의 손에 무기를 쥐여 주는 일이었다."], ["There was no choice but to oppose cunning by cunning.", "꾀에는 꾀로 맞서는 수밖에 없었다."], ["I turned into the street down which the second man had disappeared, and passed him, waiting in a doorway.", "나는 두 번째 사내가 사라진 거리로 접어들어, 어느 문간에서 기다리고 있던 그를 지나쳤다."], ["He was a stranger to me, and I was glad to make sure of his personal appearance in case of future annoyance.", "그는 내가 모르는 얼굴이었고, 앞으로 성가신 일이 생길 때를 대비해 그 생김새를 똑똑히 익혀 둔 것이 다행이었다."], ["Having done this, I again walked northward till I reached the New Road.", "그러고 나서 나는 다시 북쪽으로 걸어 뉴 로드에 이르렀다."], ["There I turned aside to the west (having the men behind me all the time), and waited at a point where I knew myself to be at some distance from a cab-stand, until a fast two-wheel cab, empty, should happen to pass me.", "거기서 서쪽으로 방향을 틀어, 그동안 내내 사내들을 뒤에 달고서, 마차 승강장에서 어지간히 떨어졌다 싶은 지점에 이르러 빈 이륜 쾌속 마차가 지나가기를 기다렸다."], ["One passed in a few minutes.", "몇 분 만에 한 대가 지나갔다."], ["I jumped in and told the man to drive rapidly towards Hyde Park.", "나는 뛰어올라 마부에게 하이드 파크 쪽으로 빨리 몰라고 일렀다."], ["There was no second fast cab for the spies behind me.", "뒤에 있던 첩자들에게는 뒤따를 빠른 마차가 없었다."], ["I saw them dart across to the other side of the road, to follow me by running, until a cab or a cab-stand came in their way.", "그들이 길 건너편으로 내달아, 마차든 승강장이든 눈에 띌 때까지 달려서 나를 쫓으려는 것이 보였다."], ["But I had the start of them, and when I stopped the driver and got out, they were nowhere in sight.", "그러나 나는 그들보다 앞서 있었고, 마부를 세우고 내렸을 때 그들은 어디에도 보이지 않았다."], ["I crossed Hyde Park and made sure, on the open ground, that I was free.", "나는 하이드 파크를 가로지르며 탁 트인 땅에서 내가 자유로워졌음을 확인했다."], ["When I at last turned my steps homewards, it was not till many hours later—not till after dark.", "마침내 집으로 발길을 돌린 것은 그로부터 여러 시간이 지난 뒤, 날이 어두워지고 나서였다."]], [["I found Marian waiting for me alone in the little sitting-room.", "매리언은 작은 거실에서 혼자 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She had persuaded Laura to go to rest, after first promising to show me her drawing the moment I came in.", "그녀는 내가 들어오는 대로 그림을 보여 주겠다고 약속한 끝에 로라를 겨우 재우러 보낸 참이었다."], ["The poor little dim faint sketch—so trifling in itself, so touching in its associations—was propped up carefully on the table with two books, and was placed where the faint light of the one candle we allowed ourselves might fall on it to the best advantage.", "그 가엾고 흐릿하고 희미한 소묘는, 그 자체로는 하잘것없으나 거기 얽힌 사연으로는 그토록 가슴을 치는 것이었는데, 책 두 권에 조심스레 받쳐 탁자 위에 세워져 있었고, 우리가 스스로에게 허락한 단 하나의 촛불이 뿜는 희미한 빛이 가장 잘 비치는 자리에 놓여 있었다."], ["I sat down to look at the drawing, and to tell Marian, in whispers, what had happened.", "나는 그 그림을 들여다보며 매리언에게 그동안 있었던 일을 속삭여 들려주려고 자리에 앉았다."], ["The partition which divided us from the next room was so thin that we could almost hear Laura’s breathing, and we might have disturbed her if we had spoken aloud.", "옆방과 우리를 가르는 칸막이가 어찌나 얇은지 로라의 숨소리가 들릴 지경이어서, 소리 내어 말했다가는 그녀를 깨울 수도 있었다."]], [["Marian preserved her composure while I described my interview with Mr. Kyrle.", "매리언은 내가 카일 씨와의 면담을 이야기하는 동안 평정을 지켰다."], ["But her face became troubled when I spoke next of the men who had followed me from the lawyer’s office, and when I told her of the discovery of Sir Percival’s return.", "그러나 그다음으로 변호사 사무소에서부터 나를 미행한 사내들 이야기를 하고 퍼시벌 경이 돌아왔다는 사실을 알렸을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 근심으로 흐려졌다."]], [["“Bad news, Walter,”", "“나쁜 소식이군요, 월터.”"], ["she said, “the worst news you could bring.", "그녀가 말했다. “가져올 수 있는 소식 가운데 가장 나쁜 소식이에요."], ["Have you nothing more to tell me?”", "더 해 줄 이야기는 없나요?”"]], [["“I have something to give you,”", "“드릴 것이 하나 있어요.”"], ["I replied, handing her the note which Mr. Kyrle had confided to my care.", "내가 대답하며, 카일 씨가 내 손에 맡긴 쪽지를 그녀에게 건넸다."]], [["She looked at the address and recognised the handwriting instantly.", "그녀는 수신인을 보고 대번에 그 필적을 알아보았다."]], [["“You know your correspondent?”", "“보낸 사람을 아시겠어요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Too well,”", "“너무나 잘 알지요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“My correspondent is Count Fosco.”", "“내게 편지를 보낸 사람은 포스코 백작이에요.”"]], [["With that reply she opened the note.", "그렇게 대답하고 그녀는 쪽지를 펼쳤다."], ["Her face flushed deeply while she read it—her eyes brightened with anger as she handed it to me to read in my turn.", "읽어 내려가는 동안 그녀의 얼굴이 짙게 달아올랐고, 이번에는 나더러 읽으라며 그것을 건넬 때 그녀의 두 눈은 분노로 번쩍였다."]], [["The note contained these lines—", "쪽지에는 이런 글이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Impelled by honourable admiration—honourable to myself, honourable to you—I write, magnificent Marian, in the interests of your tranquillity, to say two consoling words—", "“떳떳한 흠모에 이끌려, 나 자신에게도 떳떳하고 그대에게도 떳떳한 그 흠모에 이끌려 나는 씁니다, 장엄한 매리언이여. 그대의 평온을 위하여, 위로가 될 두 마디를 전하고자."]], [["“Fear nothing!", "“아무것도 두려워하지 마시오!"]], [["“Exercise your fine natural sense and remain in retirement.", "“타고난 그 훌륭한 분별을 발휘하여 물러나 계시오."], ["Dear and admirable woman, invite no dangerous publicity.", "사랑스럽고 감탄스러운 여인이여, 위험한 세상의 이목을 부르지 마시오."], ["Resignation is sublime—adopt it.", "체념은 숭고하니 그것을 취하시오."], ["The modest repose of home is eternally fresh—enjoy it.", "가정의 조촐한 안식은 영원히 새로우니 그것을 누리시오."], ["The storms of life pass harmless over the valley of Seclusion—dwell, dear lady, in the valley.", "인생의 폭풍은 은둔의 골짜기 위를 해를 끼치지 않고 지나가는 법이니, 부인이여, 그 골짜기에 머무시오."]], [["“Do this and I authorise you to fear nothing.", "“그리하면 나는 그대에게 아무것도 두려워하지 말라고 이를 권한을 얻습니다."], ["No new calamity shall lacerate your sensibilities—sensibilities precious to me as my own.", "어떤 새로운 재앙도 그대의 감수성을, 내 것만큼이나 내게 소중한 그 감수성을 찢어 놓지 않을 것이오."], ["You shall not be molested, the fair companion of your retreat shall not be pursued.", "그대는 시달리지 않을 것이며, 그대가 은거하는 곳의 아리따운 동반자도 쫓기지 않을 것이오."], ["She has found a new asylum in your heart.", "그 여인은 그대의 마음속에서 새 안식처를 찾았소."], ["Priceless asylum!—I envy her and leave her there.", "값을 매길 수 없는 안식처여! 나는 그 여인을 부러워하며 거기 그대로 두겠소."]], [["“One last word of affectionate warning, of paternal caution, and I tear myself from the charm of addressing you—I close these fervent lines.", "“애정 어린 경고, 아비 같은 당부의 말 한마디를 마지막으로 남기고 나는 그대에게 말을 건네는 이 매혹에서 나를 떼어 놓겠소. 이 뜨거운 몇 줄을 이만 맺겠소."]], [["“Advance no farther than you have gone already, compromise no serious interests, threaten nobody.", "“이미 나아간 데서 한 걸음도 더 나아가지 말고, 중대한 이해를 건드리지 말며, 누구도 위협하지 마시오. 부디 간청하건대 나를 행동으로 몰지 마시오."], ["Do not, I implore you, force me into action—ME, the Man of Action—when it is the cherished object of my ambition to be passive, to restrict the vast reach of my energies and my combinations for your sake.", "행동의 사람인 나를 말이오. 그대를 위하여 가만히 있는 것, 내 정력과 계략이 미치는 그 광대한 범위를 스스로 묶어 두는 것이야말로 내 야심이 소중히 여기는 목적일진대."], ["If you have rash friends, moderate their deplorable ardour.", "그대에게 무모한 벗이 있거든 그 딱한 열의를 누그러뜨리시오."], ["If Mr. Hartright returns to England, hold no communication with him.", "하트라이트 씨가 영국으로 돌아오거든 그와 아무 왕래도 하지 마시오."], ["I walk on a path of my own, and Percival follows at my heels.", "나는 나만의 길을 걷고, 퍼시벌은 내 뒤꿈치를 따라옵니다."], ["On the day when Mr. Hartright crosses that path, he is a lost man.”", "하트라이트 씨가 그 길을 가로지르는 날, 그는 끝장난 사람이오.”"]], [["The only signature to these lines was the initial letter F, surrounded by a circle of intricate flourishes.", "이 글에 붙은 서명이라고는 복잡한 장식 곡선의 동그라미에 둘러싸인 첫 글자 F 하나뿐이었다."], ["I threw the letter on the table with all the contempt that I felt for it.", "나는 그 편지에 대해 느낀 경멸을 그대로 담아 그것을 탁자 위에 내던졌다."]], [["“He is trying to frighten you—a sure sign that he is frightened himself,”", "“당신을 겁주려는 겁니다. 자기가 겁먹었다는 확실한 표시지요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["She was too genuine a woman to treat the letter as I treated it.", "그녀는 나처럼 그 편지를 대하기에는 너무나 진솔한 여자였다."], ["The insolent familiarity of the language was too much for her self-control.", "그 무례하도록 허물없는 말투는 그녀의 자제심으로 감당하기에 벅찼다."], ["As she looked at me across the table, her hands clenched themselves in her lap, and the old quick fiery temper flamed out again brightly in her cheeks and her eyes.", "탁자 너머로 나를 바라보는 그녀의 두 손은 무릎 위에서 꼭 움켜쥐어졌고, 예전의 그 빠르고 불같은 성미가 다시 그녀의 뺨과 두 눈에서 환하게 타올랐다."]], [["“Walter!”", "“월터!”"], ["she said, “if ever those two men are at your mercy, and if you are obliged to spare one of them, don’t let it be the Count.”", "그녀가 말했다. “언젠가 그 두 사람이 당신 손아귀에 들어오고, 그중 하나를 살려 주어야만 한다면, 그게 백작이 되게는 하지 말아요.”"]], [["“I will keep this letter, Marian, to help my memory when the time comes.”", "“이 편지는 간직해 두겠어요, 매리언. 때가 왔을 때 잊지 않도록요.”"]], [["She looked at me attentively as I put the letter away in my pocket-book.", "내가 편지를 수첩에 넣는 동안 그녀는 나를 유심히 바라보았다."]], [["“When the time comes?” she repeated.", "“때가 왔을 때라니요?”"], ["“Can you speak of the future as if you were certain of it?—certain after what you have heard in Mr.", "그녀가 되뇌었다. “앞일을 확신하는 사람처럼 말할 수 있나요?"], ["Kyrle’s office, after what has happened to you to-day?”", "카일 씨 사무소에서 들은 이야기를 겪고도, 오늘 당신에게 일어난 일을 겪고도요?”"]], [["“I don’t count the time from to-day, Marian.", "“나는 오늘부터 날을 세지 않아요, 매리언."], ["All I have done to-day is to ask another man to act for me.", "오늘 내가 한 일이라고는 남에게 나를 대신해 나서 달라고 부탁한 것뿐이에요."], ["I count from to-morrow——”", "나는 내일부터 셉니다——”"]], [["“Why from to-morrow?”", "“어째서 내일부터죠?”"]], [["“Because to-morrow I mean to act for myself.”", "“내일부터는 내가 나 자신을 위해 나설 작정이니까요.”"]], [["“How?”", "“어떻게요?”"]], [["“I shall go to Blackwater by the first train, and return, I hope, at night.”", "“첫차로 블랙워터에 갔다가, 바라건대 밤에는 돌아올 생각이에요.”"]], [["“To Blackwater!”", "“블랙워터라니요!”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래요."], ["I have had time to think since I left Mr.", "카일 씨와 헤어진 뒤로 생각할 시간이 있었어요."], ["Kyrle. His opinion on one point confirms my own.", "한 가지 점에서 그의 견해는 내 생각을 확실히 뒷받침해 줍니다."], ["We must persist to the last in hunting down the date of Laura’s journey.", "우리는 로라가 런던으로 간 날짜를 끝까지 캐내야 해요."], ["The one weak point in the conspiracy, and probably the one chance of proving that she is a living woman, centre in the discovery of that date.”", "이 음모의 단 하나뿐인 약점, 그리고 아마도 그이가 살아 있는 사람임을 증명할 단 하나뿐인 기회가 그 날짜를 밝혀내는 데에 모여 있어요.”"]], [["“You mean,”", "“그러니까,”"], ["said Marian, “the discovery that Laura did not leave Blackwater Park till after the date of her death on the doctor’s certificate?”", "매리언이 말했다. “로라가 블랙워터 파크를 떠난 것이 의사 진단서에 적힌 사망 날짜보다 뒤였다는 걸 밝혀내자는 말이군요?”"]], [["“Certainly.”", "“그래요.”"]], [["“What makes you think it might have been AFTER?", "“왜 그게 뒤였을 거라고 생각하죠?"], ["Laura can tell us nothing of the time she was in London.”", "로라는 런던에 있던 동안의 일은 아무것도 말해 주지 못하는데요.”"]], [["“But the owner of the Asylum told you that she was received there on the twenty-seventh of July.", "“하지만 병원 주인이 당신에게 그이를 7월 27일에 받았다고 말했잖아요."], ["I doubt Count Fosco’s ability to keep her in London, and to keep her insensible to all that was passing around her, more than one night.", "나는 포스코 백작이 그이를 런던에 붙들어 두고 주위에서 벌어지는 일을 조금도 알아채지 못하게 만드는 일을 하룻밤 넘게 해낼 수 있었을지 의심스러워요."], ["In that case, she must have started on the twenty-sixth, and must have come to London one day after the date of her own death on the doctor’s certificate.", "그렇다면 그이는 26일에 떠났을 것이고, 의사 진단서에 적힌 자기 사망 날짜보다 하루 뒤에 런던에 닿았을 겁니다."], ["If we can prove that date, we prove our case against Sir Percival and the Count.”", "그 날짜를 증명할 수 있다면 우리는 퍼시벌 경과 백작을 상대로 우리 주장을 증명하는 셈이에요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes—I see!", "“그래요, 그래요, 알겠어요!"], ["But how is the proof to be obtained?”", "그런데 그 증명을 어떻게 얻지요?”"]], [["“Mrs. Michelson’s narrative has suggested to me two ways of trying to obtain it.", "“마이컬슨 부인의 기록을 보고 그것을 얻어 볼 두 가지 길이 떠올랐어요."], ["One of them is to question the doctor, Mr.", "하나는 의사인 도슨 씨에게 물어보는 것입니다."], ["Dawson, who must know when he resumed his attendance at Blackwater Park after Laura left the house.", "로라가 집을 떠난 뒤 언제부터 블랙워터 파크 왕진을 다시 시작했는지 그는 알고 있을 테니까요."], ["The other is to make inquiries at the inn to which Sir Percival drove away by himself at night.", "다른 하나는 퍼시벌 경이 그날 밤 혼자 마차를 몰고 간 여관에 가서 알아보는 것이에요."], ["We know that his departure followed Laura’s after the lapse of a few hours, and we may get at the date in that way.", "그가 떠난 것이 로라가 떠난 지 몇 시간 뒤였다는 것은 우리가 알고 있으니, 그 길로 날짜에 이를 수 있을지도 몰라요."], ["The attempt is at least worth making, and to-morrow I am determined it shall be made.”", "적어도 해 볼 만한 일이고, 내일은 기어이 해 보고야 말겠습니다.”"]], [["“And suppose it fails—I look at the worst now, Walter; but I will look at the best if disappointments come to try us—suppose no one can help you at Blackwater?”", "“그런데 그게 실패하면요? 월터, 지금은 최악을 보고 있지만 실망스러운 일이 우리를 시험하러 오면 그때는 최선을 보겠어요. 블랙워터에서 아무도 당신을 도와줄 수 없다면 어쩌죠?”"]], [["“There are two men who can help me, and shall help me in London—Sir Percival and the Count.", "“런던에는 나를 도울 수 있고 또 반드시 돕게 될 두 사람이 있어요. 퍼시벌 경과 백작이지요."], ["Innocent people may well forget the date—but THEY are guilty, and THEY know it.", "죄 없는 사람이야 날짜를 잊을 수도 있겠지만 그자들은 죄가 있고 스스로 그것을 압니다."], ["If I fail everywhere else, I mean to force a confession out of one or both of them on my own terms.”", "다른 데서 다 실패한다면 나는 내 방식대로 둘 가운데 하나, 혹은 둘 다에게서 자백을 짜낼 작정이에요.”"]], [["All the woman flushed up in Marian’s face as I spoke.", "내가 그렇게 말하자 매리언의 얼굴에 여자다운 것이 모조리 확 달아올랐다."]], [["“Begin with the Count,”", "“백작부터 시작해요.”"], ["she whispered eagerly.", "그녀가 다급하게 속삭였다."], ["“For my sake, begin with the Count.”", "“나를 위해서, 백작부터 시작해요.”"]], [["“We must begin, for Laura’s sake, where there is the best chance of success,”", "“로라를 위해서, 성공할 가망이 가장 큰 쪽부터 시작해야 해요.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["The colour faded from her face again, and she shook her head sadly.", "그녀의 얼굴에서 다시 핏기가 가셨고, 그녀는 슬프게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래요.”"], ["she said, “you are right—it was mean and miserable of me to say that.", "그녀가 말했다. “당신 말이 옳아요."], ["I try to be patient, Walter, and succeed better now than I did in happier times.", "그런 말을 한 건 내가 옹졸하고 못난 탓이에요. 나도 참으려고 애쓰고 있어요, 월터."], ["But I have a little of my old temper still left, and it will get the better of me when I think of the Count!”", "행복하던 시절보다 지금이 더 잘되기도 하고요. 하지만 예전의 그 성미가 아직 조금 남아 있어서, 백작 생각만 하면 그것이 나를 이겨 버려요!”"]], [["“His turn will come,” I said.", "“그자의 차례도 올 겁니다.”"], ["“But, remember, there is no weak place in his life that we know of yet.”", "내가 말했다. “하지만 잊지 말아요."], ["I waited a little to let her recover her self-possession, and then spoke the decisive words—", "우리가 아는 한 그의 삶에는 아직 약한 데가 없어요.” 나는 그녀가 평정을 되찾도록 잠시 기다렸다가 결정적인 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Marian!", "“매리언!"], ["There is a weak place we both know of in Sir Percival’s life——”", "퍼시벌 경의 삶에는 우리 둘 다 알고 있는 약한 데가 있어요——”"]], [["“You mean the Secret!”", "“비밀 말이군요!”"]], [["“Yes: the Secret.", "“그래요, 그 비밀입니다."], ["It is our only sure hold on him.", "그것이 우리가 그를 붙들 유일하고도 확실한 손잡이예요."], ["I can force him from his position of security, I can drag him and his villainy into the face of day, by no other means.", "다른 어떤 수단으로도 나는 그를 안전한 자리에서 끌어내릴 수 없고, 그와 그의 악행을 환한 대낮으로 끌어낼 수 없어요."], ["Whatever the Count may have done, Sir Percival has consented to the conspiracy against Laura from another motive besides the motive of gain.", "백작이 무슨 짓을 했든, 퍼시벌 경이 로라를 해치는 음모에 동의한 데에는 돈이라는 동기 말고 다른 동기가 있었어요."], ["You heard him tell the Count that he believed his wife knew enough to ruin him?", "그가 백작에게, 아내가 자기를 파멸시키기에 넉넉할 만큼 알고 있다고 믿는다 말하는 걸 들으셨지요?"], ["You heard him say that he was a lost man if the secret of Anne Catherick was known?”", "앤 캐서릭의 비밀이 알려지면 자기는 끝장이라고 말하는 걸 들으셨지요?”"]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["yes!", "그래요!"], ["I did.”", "들었어요.”"]], [["“Well, Marian, when our other resources have failed us, I mean to know the Secret.", "“그렇다면 매리언, 다른 방도가 모두 우리를 저버렸을 때 나는 그 비밀을 알아낼 작정이에요."], ["My old superstition clings to me, even yet.", "옛날의 그 미신이 아직도 내게서 떠나지 않습니다."], ["I say again the woman in white is a living influence in our three lives.", "다시 말하지만 흰옷을 입은 여인은 우리 세 사람의 삶에 살아 있는 힘이에요."], ["The End is appointed—the End is drawing us on—and Anne Catherick, dead in her grave, points the way to it still!”", "끝은 정해져 있고, 그 끝이 우리를 끌어당기고 있으며, 무덤 속에서 죽어 있는 앤 캐서릭이 아직도 그리로 가는 길을 가리키고 있어요!”"]], [["The story of my first inquiries in Hampshire is soon told.", "햄프셔에서 벌인 나의 첫 조사에 관한 이야기는 짧게 끝맺을 수 있다."]], [["My early departure from London enabled me to reach Mr. Dawson’s house in the forenoon.", "런던을 이른 시각에 떠난 덕분에 나는 오전 중에 도슨 의사의 집에 닿을 수 있었다."], ["Our interview, so far as the object of my visit was concerned, led to no satisfactory result.", "그러나 내 방문의 목적에 관한 한, 우리의 면담은 아무런 만족스러운 결과도 낳지 못했다."]], [["Mr. Dawson’s books certainly showed when he had resumed his attendance on Miss Halcombe at Blackwater Park, but it was not possible to calculate back from this date with any exactness, without such help from Mrs.", "도슨 의사의 진료 기록부에는 그가 블랙워터 파크에서 홀콤 양의 진료를 다시 시작한 날짜가 분명히 적혀 있었다."], ["Michelson as I knew she was unable to afford. She could not say from memory (who, in similar cases, ever can?) how many days had elapsed between the renewal of the doctor’s attendance on his patient and the previous departure of Lady Glyde.", "그러나 그 날짜에서 거슬러 올라가 조금이라도 정확하게 셈을 하려면 마이컬슨 부인의 도움이 있어야 했고, 나는 그녀가 그런 도움을 줄 수 없다는 것을 이미 알고 있었다."], ["She was almost certain of having mentioned the circumstance of the departure to Miss Halcombe, on the day after it happened—but then she was no more able to fix the date of the day on which this disclosure took place, than to fix the date of the day before, when Lady Glyde had left for London.", "그녀는 의사가 환자의 진료를 다시 시작하기까지, 그에 앞서 글라이드 부인이 떠난 날로부터 며칠이 흘렀는지를 기억만으로는 말하지 못했다(이런 경우에 그것을 말할 수 있는 사람이 대체 어디 있겠는가?). 그녀는 부인이 떠난 사정을 그 이튿날 홀콤 양에게 이야기했다는 것만은 거의 확신했으나, 그 이야기를 한 날의 날짜를 짚어내지 못하기는 글라이드 부인이 런던으로 떠난 그 전날의 날짜를 짚어내지 못하는 것과 다를 바가 없었다."], ["Neither could she calculate, with any nearer approach to exactness, the time that had passed from the departure of her mistress, to the period when the undated letter from Madame Fosco arrived.", "여주인이 떠난 때로부터 날짜 없는 포스코 백작부인의 편지가 도착한 때까지 얼마나 지났는지도 그녀는 조금이라도 더 정확하게 헤아리지 못했다."], ["Lastly, as if to complete the series of difficulties, the doctor himself, having been ill at the time, had omitted to make his usual entry of the day of the week and month when the gardener from Blackwater Park had called on him to deliver Mrs. Michelson’s message.", "마지막으로, 난관의 목록을 완성하기라도 하려는 듯, 의사 자신도 그 무렵 앓고 있던 터라 블랙워터 파크의 정원사가 마이컬슨 부인의 전갈을 들고 찾아왔던 요일과 날짜를 늘 하던 대로 기입해 두지 않았다."]], [["Hopeless of obtaining assistance from Mr. Dawson, I resolved to try next if I could establish the date of Sir Percival’s arrival at Knowlesbury.", "도슨 의사에게서 도움을 얻기는 글렀으므로, 나는 다음으로 퍼시벌 경이 놀즈베리에 도착한 날짜를 확정할 수 있는지 알아보기로 했다."]], [["It seemed like a fatality!", "마치 운명의 장난 같았다!"], ["When I reached Knowlesbury the inn was shut up, and bills were posted on the walls.", "놀즈베리에 닿았을 때 그 여관은 문을 닫았고 벽에는 공고문이 붙어 있었다."], ["The speculation had been a bad one, as I was informed, ever since the time of the railway.", "내가 들은 바로는 철도가 놓인 뒤로 그 장사는 줄곧 신통치 않았다고 한다."], ["The new hotel at the station had gradually absorbed the business, and the old inn (which we knew to be the inn at which Sir Percival had put up), had been closed about two months since.", "역에 새로 생긴 호텔이 손님을 차츰 다 빨아들였고, 우리가 퍼시벌 경이 묵었던 곳으로 알고 있는 그 낡은 여관은 두 달쯤 전에 문을 닫았다는 것이다."], ["The proprietor had left the town with all his goods and chattels, and where he had gone I could not positively ascertain from any one.", "주인은 가재도구를 죄다 챙겨 마을을 떠났는데, 어디로 갔는지는 누구에게 물어도 확실히 알아낼 수 없었다."], ["The four people of whom I inquired gave me four different accounts of his plans and projects when he left Knowlesbury.", "내가 물어본 네 사람은 그가 놀즈베리를 떠날 때 품었던 계획과 속셈에 대해 저마다 다른 네 가지 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["There were still some hours to spare before the last train left for London, and I drove back again in a fly from the Knowlesbury station to Blackwater Park, with the purpose of questioning the gardener and the person who kept the lodge.", "런던행 막차가 떠나기까지는 아직 몇 시간이 남아 있었다. 그래서 나는 놀즈베리 역에서 삯마차를 얻어 타고 다시 블랙워터 파크로 향했다."], ["If they, too, proved unable to assist me, my resources for the present were at an end, and I might return to town.", "정원사와 문간채를 지키는 사람을 만나 캐물을 작정이었다. 그들마저 나를 도울 수 없다면 당장 쓸 수 있는 수단은 다 떨어진 셈이니, 그때는 런던으로 돌아가면 될 일이었다."]], [["I dismissed the fly a mile distant from the park, and getting my directions from the driver, proceeded by myself to the house.", "나는 파크에서 1마일쯤 떨어진 곳에서 삯마차를 돌려보내고, 마부에게 길을 물어 둔 다음 혼자서 저택을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["As I turned into the lane from the high-road, I saw a man, with a carpet-bag, walking before me rapidly on the way to the lodge.", "큰길에서 좁은 길로 접어들 때, 나는 융단 천으로 만든 여행 가방을 든 사내가 문간채 쪽으로 빠르게 앞서 걸어가는 것을 보았다."], ["He was a little man, dressed in shabby black, and wearing a remarkably large hat.", "몸집이 작은 사내였고, 낡아빠진 검은 옷을 입었으며, 유난히 큰 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["I set him down (as well as it was possible to judge) for a lawyer’s clerk, and stopped at once to widen the distance between us.", "겉으로 짐작할 수 있는 한도에서 나는 그를 변호사 사무실의 서기쯤으로 보았고, 곧바로 걸음을 멈추어 사이를 벌렸다."], ["He had not heard me, and he walked on out of sight, without looking back.", "그는 내 기척을 듣지 못했는지 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 걸어가 시야에서 사라졌다."], ["When I passed through the gates myself, a little while afterwards, he was not visible—he had evidently gone on to the house.", "조금 뒤에 내가 직접 대문을 지날 때 그는 보이지 않았다. 저택 쪽으로 계속 올라간 것이 분명했다."]], [["There were two women in the lodge.", "문간채에는 여자가 둘 있었다."], ["One of them was old, the other I knew at once, by Marian’s description of her, to be Margaret Porcher.", "한 사람은 노파였고, 다른 한 사람은 매리언에게서 들은 인상착의로 보아 곧바로 마거릿 포처임을 알 수 있었다."]], [["I asked first if Sir Percival was at the Park, and receiving a reply in the negative, inquired next when he had left it.", "나는 먼저 퍼시벌 경이 파크에 계시냐고 물었고, 아니라는 대답을 듣고는 언제 떠났느냐고 다시 물었다."], ["Neither of the women could tell me more than that he had gone away in the summer.", "두 여자 모두 그가 여름에 떠났다는 것 말고는 아무것도 말해 주지 못했다."], ["I could extract nothing from Margaret Porcher but vacant smiles and shakings of the head.", "마거릿 포처에게서는 멍한 웃음과 고개를 젓는 시늉 말고는 아무것도 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["The old woman was a little more intelligent, and I managed to lead her into speaking of the manner of Sir Percival’s departure, and of the alarm that it caused her.", "노파는 그보다 조금 나아서, 나는 그녀가 퍼시벌 경이 떠나던 정황과 그때 자기가 받은 놀라움에 대해 이야기하도록 이끌 수 있었다. 그녀는 주인이 자기를 잠자리에서 불러냈던 일과, 욕설을 퍼부어 자기를 겁먹게 했던 일을 기억하고 있었다."], ["She remembered her master calling her out of bed, and remembered his frightening her by swearing—but the date at which the occurrence happened was, as she honestly acknowledged, “quite beyond her.”", "그러나 그 일이 있었던 날짜는, 그녀 자신이 솔직히 인정했듯이 “도무지 알 수 없는” 것이었다."]], [["On leaving the lodge I saw the gardener at work not far off.", "문간채를 나서다가 나는 멀지 않은 곳에서 일하고 있는 정원사를 보았다."], ["When I first addressed him, he looked at me rather distrustfully, but on my using Mrs. Michelson’s name, with a civil reference to himself, he entered into conversation readily enough.", "처음 말을 걸었을 때 그는 자못 미심쩍은 눈으로 나를 보았으나, 내가 마이컬슨 부인의 이름을 대며 그에 대해서도 정중하게 언급하자 선선히 대화에 응했다."], ["There is no need to describe what passed between us—it ended, as all my other attempts to discover the date had ended.", "우리 사이에 오간 말을 옮길 필요는 없다. 날짜를 알아내려던 다른 모든 시도가 그랬듯 이번에도 같은 결말로 끝났으니까."], ["The gardener knew that his master had driven away, at night, “some time in July, the last fortnight or the last ten days in the month”—and knew no more.", "정원사는 주인이 밤에 마차를 타고 떠났다는 것, 그것이 “7월 어느 무렵, 그달 마지막 두 주나 마지막 열흘 사이”라는 것만 알 뿐 그 이상은 알지 못했다."]], [["While we were speaking together I saw the man in black, with the large hat, come out from the house, and stand at some little distance observing us.", "우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안, 나는 큰 모자를 쓴 그 검은 옷의 사내가 저택에서 나와 조금 떨어진 곳에 서서 우리를 지켜보는 것을 보았다."]], [["Certain suspicions of his errand at Blackwater Park had already crossed my mind.", "그가 블랙워터 파크에 온 용건에 대해서는 이미 어떤 의심이 내 머리를 스치고 지나간 터였다."], ["They were now increased by the gardener’s inability (or unwillingness) to tell me who the man was, and I determined to clear the way before me, if possible, by speaking to him.", "그 사내가 누구인지 말해 주지 못하는(혹은 말해 주지 않으려는) 정원사의 태도가 그 의심을 한층 키웠고, 나는 가능하다면 그에게 직접 말을 붙여 앞길을 트기로 마음먹었다."], ["The plainest question I could put as a stranger would be to inquire if the house was allowed to be shown to visitors.", "낯선 사람으로서 던질 수 있는 가장 무난한 질문은 이 저택을 구경할 수 있게 해 주느냐고 묻는 것이었다."], ["I walked up to the man at once, and accosted him in those words.", "나는 곧장 그 사내에게 걸어가 그 말로 말을 붙였다."]], [["His look and manner unmistakably betrayed that he knew who I was, and that he wanted to irritate me into quarrelling with him.", "그의 눈빛과 태도는 그가 내가 누구인지 알고 있으며 나를 자극해 시비를 걸게 만들고 싶어 한다는 것을 뚜렷이 드러냈다."], ["His reply was insolent enough to have answered the purpose, if I had been less determined to control myself.", "그의 대답은, 내가 스스로를 억누르려는 결심을 덜 굳게 했더라면 충분히 그 목적을 이루었을 만큼 무례했다."], ["As it was, I met him with the most resolute politeness, apologised for my involuntary intrusion (which he called a “trespass,”) and left the grounds.", "그러나 나는 더없이 단호한 예의로 그를 맞았고, 본의 아닌 침입을(그는 그것을 “무단 침입”이라 불렀다) 사과한 뒤 그 부지를 떠났다."], ["It was exactly as I suspected.", "내가 의심한 그대로였다."], ["The recognition of me when I left Mr. Kyrle’s office had been evidently communicated to Sir Percival Glyde, and the man in black had been sent to the Park in anticipation of my making inquiries at the house or in the neighbourhood.", "카일 씨의 사무실을 나설 때 누군가 나를 알아본 사실이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 전해진 것이 분명했고, 내가 저택이나 그 근방에서 캐고 다닐 것을 예상해 그 검은 옷의 사내를 파크로 보내 둔 것이었다."], ["If I had given him the least chance of lodging any sort of legal complaint against me, the interference of the local magistrate would no doubt have been turned to account as a clog on my proceedings, and a means of separating me from Marian and Laura for some days at least.", "만일 내가 그에게 나를 상대로 어떤 법적 고발을 제기할 빌미라도 조금 주었더라면, 지방 치안판사의 개입이 틀림없이 내 일을 가로막는 족쇄로, 그리고 적어도 며칠 동안은 나를 매리언과 로라에게서 떼어놓는 수단으로 이용되었을 것이다."]], [["I was prepared to be watched on the way from Blackwater Park to the station, exactly as I had been watched in London the day before.", "나는 전날 런던에서 감시당했던 것과 똑같이 블랙워터 파크에서 역까지 가는 길에도 감시당하리라 각오하고 있었다."], ["But I could not discover at the time, whether I was really followed on this occasion or not.", "그러나 그때는 내가 정말로 미행을 당하고 있는지 아닌지 알아낼 수 없었다."], ["The man in black might have had means of tracking me at his disposal of which I was not aware, but I certainly saw nothing of him, in his own person, either on the way to the station, or afterwards on my arrival at the London terminus in the evening.", "그 검은 옷의 사내에게는 내가 알지 못하는 추적 수단이 마련되어 있었을지도 모르지만, 역으로 가는 길에서도, 그 뒤 저녁에 런던 종착역에 내렸을 때도 그의 모습만은 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["I reached home on foot, taking the precaution, before I approached our own door, of walking round by the loneliest street in the neighbourhood, and there stopping and looking back more than once over the open space behind me.", "나는 걸어서 집으로 향했고, 우리 집 문에 다다르기 전에 그 일대에서 가장 인적 없는 거리를 빙 둘러 걷고 거기서 걸음을 멈춰 등 뒤의 트인 공간을 몇 번씩 돌아보는 조심을 잊지 않았다."], ["I had first learnt to use this stratagem against suspected treachery in the wilds of Central America—and now I was practising it again, with the same purpose and with even greater caution, in the heart of civilised London!", "배신이 의심될 때 쓰는 이 술책을 나는 중앙아메리카의 황야에서 처음 익혔다. 그런데 이제 나는 문명의 심장인 런던 한복판에서, 같은 목적으로, 그때보다 한층 더 조심스럽게 그것을 다시 쓰고 있었다!"]], [["Nothing had happened to alarm Marian during my absence.", "내가 없는 동안 매리언을 놀라게 할 만한 일은 아무것도 없었다."], ["She asked eagerly what success I had met with.", "그녀는 성과가 어떠했느냐고 다급히 물었다."], ["When I told her she could not conceal her surprise at the indifference with which I spoke of the failure of my investigations thus far.", "내가 이야기해 주자, 그녀는 지금까지의 조사가 실패했다는 것을 내가 무심하게 말하는 데 놀라움을 감추지 못했다."]], [["The truth was, that the ill-success of my inquiries had in no sense daunted me.", "사실인즉, 조사가 신통치 않았다고 해서 내가 조금이라도 기가 꺾인 것은 아니었다."], ["I had pursued them as a matter of duty, and I had expected nothing from them.", "나는 그 일을 의무로 여겨 밀고 나갔을 뿐, 거기서 무엇을 기대한 적은 없었다."], ["In the state of my mind at that time, it was almost a relief to me to know that the struggle was now narrowed to a trial of strength between myself and Sir Percival Glyde.", "그 무렵 내 마음 상태로는, 이제 싸움이 나와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 사이의 힘겨루기로 좁혀졌다는 것을 아는 편이 오히려 마음이 놓이기까지 했다."], ["The vindictive motive had mingled itself all along with my other and better motives, and I confess it was a satisfaction to me to feel that the surest way, the only way left, of serving Laura’s cause, was to fasten my hold firmly on the villain who had married her.", "복수의 동기는 처음부터 줄곧 내 다른, 더 나은 동기들에 섞여 들어 있었다. 그리고 로라의 일을 위하는 가장 확실한 길, 이제 남은 유일한 길이 그녀와 결혼한 그 악당의 목덜미를 단단히 움켜쥐는 것이라는 사실이 나에게 만족스러웠음을 나는 고백한다."]], [["While I acknowledge that I was not strong enough to keep my motives above the reach of this instinct of revenge, I can honestly say something in my own favour on the other side.", "내 동기를 이 복수의 본능이 미치지 못하는 곳에 붙들어 둘 만큼 내가 강하지 못했음을 인정하면서도, 다른 한편으로 나 자신을 위해 정직하게 해 둘 말이 있다."], ["No base speculation on the future relations of Laura and myself, and on the private and personal concessions which I might force from Sir Percival if I once had him at my mercy, ever entered my mind.", "로라와 나의 앞날의 관계에 대해서나, 퍼시벌 경을 한번 내 손아귀에 넣기만 하면 그에게서 억지로 받아낼 수 있을 사사롭고 개인적인 양보에 대해서나, 나는 단 한 번도 비열한 계산을 품은 적이 없다."], ["I never said to myself, “If I do succeed, it shall be one result of my success that I put it out of her husband’s power to take her from me again.”", "“내가 정말로 성공한다면, 그 성공의 한 결과로 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 다시 내게서 빼앗아 갈 힘을 잃게 되리라” 하고 스스로에게 말한 적이 없다."], ["I could not look at her and think of the future with such thoughts as those.", "그녀를 바라보면서 그런 생각으로 앞날을 그릴 수는 없었다."], ["The sad sight of the change in her from her former self, made the one interest of my love an interest of tenderness and compassion which her father or her brother might have felt, and which I felt, God knows, in my inmost heart.", "예전의 그녀와 달라져 버린 그 서글픈 모습은, 내 사랑의 유일한 관심을 그녀의 아버지나 오라비가 품었을 법한 다정함과 연민의 관심으로 바꾸어 놓았고, 하느님은 아시겠지만 나는 그것을 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에서 느끼고 있었다."], ["All my hopes looked no farther on now than to the day of her recovery.", "이제 내 모든 희망은 그녀가 회복될 날 너머를 내다보지 않았다."], ["There, till she was strong again and happy again—there, till she could look at me as she had once looked, and speak to me as she had once spoken—the future of my happiest thoughts and my dearest wishes ended.", "거기까지, 그녀가 다시 건강해지고 다시 행복해질 때까지, 거기까지, 그녀가 예전처럼 나를 바라보고 예전처럼 나에게 말을 걸 수 있게 될 때까지 — 내 가장 행복한 생각과 가장 간절한 소망의 앞날은 거기서 끝났다."]], [["These words are written under no prompting of idle self-contemplation.", "이 말들은 부질없는 자기 성찰에 이끌려 쓰는 것이 아니다."], ["Passages in this narrative are soon to come which will set the minds of others in judgment on my conduct.", "이 이야기에는 머지않아 남들이 내 행동을 두고 판단을 내리게 될 대목이 나온다."], ["It is right that the best and the worst of me should be fairly balanced before that time.", "그때가 오기 전에 내 가장 나은 면과 가장 못난 면이 공평하게 저울에 놓이는 것이 옳다."]], [["On the morning after my return from Hampshire I took Marian upstairs into my working-room, and there laid before her the plan that I had matured thus far, for mastering the one assailable point in the life of Sir Percival Glyde.", "햄프셔에서 돌아온 이튿날 아침, 나는 매리언을 위층 내 작업실로 데리고 올라가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 생애에서 유일하게 파고들 만한 지점을 손에 넣기 위해 지금까지 다듬어 온 계획을 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다."]], [["The way to the Secret lay through the mystery, hitherto impenetrable to all of us, of the woman in white.", "그 비밀에 이르는 길은 지금껏 우리 누구도 뚫지 못한 수수께끼, 곧 흰옷을 입은 여인이라는 수수께끼를 지나고 있었다."], ["The approach to that in its turn might be gained by obtaining the assistance of Anne Catherick’s mother, and the only ascertainable means of prevailing on Mrs. Catherick to act or to speak in the matter depended on the chance of my discovering local particulars and family particulars first of all from Mrs.", "그리고 그 수수께끼에 다가가는 길은 다시 앤 캐서릭의 어머니에게서 도움을 얻는 데 있었으며, 캐서릭 부인이 이 일에 나서거나 입을 열도록 설득할 유일하게 확실한 방법은 클레멘츠 부인에게서 그 고장의 사정과 집안의 내력을 먼저 알아낼 수 있느냐에 달려 있었다."], ["Clements. After thinking the subject over carefully, I felt certain that I could only begin the new inquiries by placing myself in communication with the faithful friend and protectress of Anne Catherick.", "이 문제를 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에 나는 앤 캐서릭의 충실한 벗이자 보호자였던 그 여인과 연락이 닿는 것으로만 새 조사를 시작할 수 있으리라고 확신하게 되었다."]], [["The first difficulty then was to find Mrs. Clements.", "그러니 첫 번째 난관은 클레멘츠 부인을 찾아내는 일이었다."]], [["I was indebted to Marian’s quick perception for meeting this necessity at once by the best and simplest means. She proposed to write to the farm near Limmeridge (Todd’s Corner), to inquire whether Mrs. Clements had communicated with Mrs. Todd during the past few months. How Mrs. Clements had been separated from Anne it was impossible for us to say, but that separation once effected, it would certainly occur to Mrs. Clements to inquire after the missing woman in the neighbourhood of all others to which she was known to be most attached—the neighbourhood of Limmeridge. I saw directly that Marian’s proposal offered us a prospect of success, and she wrote to Mrs. Todd accordingly by that day’s post.", "이 필요를 가장 좋고 가장 간단한 방법으로 그 자리에서 해결한 것은 매리언의 빠른 눈치 덕분이었다. 그녀는 리머리지 근처의 농장, 곧 토드 코너에 편지를 보내 지난 몇 달 사이에 클레멘츠 부인이 토드 부인에게 연락한 일이 있었는지 물어보자고 했다. 클레멘츠 부인이 어쩌다 앤과 헤어지게 되었는지는 우리로서는 알 도리가 없었지만, 일단 헤어지고 나면 클레멘츠 부인이 사라진 그 여인의 행방을 그녀가 가장 애착을 가졌다고 알려진 바로 그 고장, 리머리지 근방에 수소문해 보리라는 것은 틀림없었다. 나는 매리언의 제안이 우리에게 성공의 가망을 열어 준다는 것을 곧바로 알아보았고, 그녀는 그날 우편으로 토드 부인에게 편지를 부쳤다."]], [["While we were waiting for the reply, I made myself master of all the information Marian could afford on the subject of Sir Percival’s family, and of his early life.", "답장을 기다리는 동안 나는 퍼시벌 경의 집안과 그의 젊은 시절에 대해 매리언이 알려 줄 수 있는 것을 모조리 내 것으로 만들었다."], ["She could only speak on these topics from hearsay, but she was reasonably certain of the truth of what little she had to tell.", "그 일에 관해 그녀가 말할 수 있는 것은 전해 들은 이야기뿐이었으나, 그 얼마 안 되는 이야기가 사실이라는 것만은 그런대로 확신하고 있었다."]], [["Sir Percival was an only child.", "퍼시벌 경은 외아들이었다."], ["His father, Sir Felix Glyde, had suffered from his birth under a painful and incurable deformity, and had shunned all society from his earliest years.", "그의 아버지 펠릭스 글라이드 경은 태어날 때부터 고통스럽고 고칠 수 없는 기형을 안고 있었으며, 아주 어릴 적부터 사람들과의 교제를 모두 피했다."], ["His sole happiness was in the enjoyment of music, and he had married a lady with tastes similar to his own, who was said to be a most accomplished musician.", "그의 유일한 낙은 음악을 즐기는 것이었고, 그래서 자기와 취향이 비슷한 여인, 아주 뛰어난 연주가라는 평을 듣던 여인과 결혼했다."], ["He inherited the Blackwater property while still a young man.", "그는 아직 젊은 나이에 블랙워터의 재산을 물려받았다."], ["Neither he nor his wife after taking possession, made advances of any sort towards the society of the neighbourhood, and no one endeavoured to tempt them into abandoning their reserve, with the one disastrous exception of the rector of the parish.", "그도 그의 아내도 그것을 손에 넣은 뒤 인근 사교계에 다가서려는 시늉조차 하지 않았고, 이들을 그 은둔에서 끌어내 보려고 애쓴 사람도 아무도 없었다. 다만 그 교구의 목사 한 사람이 불행한 예외였다."]], [["The rector was the worst of all innocent mischief-makers—an over-zealous man.", "그 목사는 악의 없이 말썽을 일으키는 부류 가운데서도 가장 고약한 축, 곧 지나치게 열심인 사람이었다."], ["He had heard that Sir Felix had left College with the character of being little better than a revolutionist in politics and an infidel in religion, and he arrived conscientiously at the conclusion that it was his bounden duty to summon the lord of the manor to hear sound views enunciated in the parish church.", "그는 펠릭스 경이 정치에서는 혁명가나 다름없고 종교에서는 불신자라는 평판을 안고 대학을 나왔다는 말을 들은 터라, 그 영지의 주인을 교구 교회로 불러내 올바른 가르침을 듣게 하는 것이 자기의 마땅한 의무라는 결론에 양심적으로 이르렀다."], ["Sir Felix fiercely resented the clergyman’s well-meant but ill-directed interference, insulting him so grossly and so publicly, that the families in the neighbourhood sent letters of indignant remonstrance to the Park, and even the tenants of the Blackwater property expressed their opinion as strongly as they dared.", "펠릭스 경은 선의에서 나왔으나 방향을 잘못 잡은 그 성직자의 참견에 사납게 발끈했고, 어찌나 심하게, 또 어찌나 공공연하게 그를 모욕했던지 인근의 여러 집안이 파크로 분노에 찬 항의 편지를 보냈고 블랙워터 영지의 소작인들까지 감히 할 수 있는 한도까지 세게 제 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["The baronet, who had no country tastes of any kind, and no attachment to the estate or to any one living on it, declared that society at Blackwater should never have a second chance of annoying him, and left the place from that moment.", "시골 취향이라고는 조금도 없고 영지에도, 거기 사는 어느 누구에게도 애착이 없던 그 준남작은 블랙워터의 사교계가 자기를 성가시게 할 두 번째 기회는 결코 없을 것이라고 선언하고는 그 길로 그곳을 떠났다."]], [["After a short residence in London he and his wife departed for the Continent, and never returned to England again.", "런던에서 잠시 지낸 뒤 그와 그의 아내는 대륙으로 떠났고, 다시는 영국으로 돌아오지 않았다."], ["They lived part of the time in France and part in Germany—always keeping themselves in the strict retirement which the morbid sense of his own personal deformity had made a necessity to Sir Felix.", "그들은 얼마간은 프랑스에서, 얼마간은 독일에서 살았는데, 자기 몸의 기형에 대한 병적인 자의식 때문에 펠릭스 경에게 필수가 되어 버린 그 엄격한 은둔만은 언제나 지켰다."], ["Their son, Percival, had been born abroad, and had been educated there by private tutors.", "아들 퍼시벌은 외국에서 태어났고 거기서 가정교사들에게 교육을 받았다."], ["His mother was the first of his parents whom he lost.", "부모 가운데 먼저 여읜 쪽은 어머니였다."], ["His father had died a few years after her, either in 1825 or 1826.", "아버지는 그로부터 몇 해 뒤인 1825년이나 1826년에 세상을 떠났다."], ["Sir Percival had been in England, as a young man, once or twice before that period, but his acquaintance with the late Mr. Fairlie did not begin till after the time of his father’s death.", "퍼시벌 경은 그전에도 젊은 나이로 한두 번 영국에 온 일이 있었으나, 고인이 된 페얼리 씨와의 교분은 아버지가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 시작되었다."], ["They soon became very intimate, although Sir Percival was seldom, or never, at Limmeridge House in those days. Mr.", "두 사람은 곧 아주 가까운 사이가 되었지만, 그 시절 퍼시벌 경이 리머리지 하우스에 온 일은 드물었거나 아예 없었다."], ["Frederick Fairlie might have met him once or twice in Mr. Philip Fairlie’s company, but he could have known little of him at that or at any other time.", "프레더릭 페얼리 씨는 필립 페얼리 씨와 함께 있는 자리에서 그를 한두 번 만났을지 모르나, 그때든 다른 어느 때든 그에 대해 아는 것은 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["Sir Percival’s only intimate friend in the Fairlie family had been Laura’s father.", "페얼리 집안에서 퍼시벌 경과 가깝게 지낸 유일한 사람은 로라의 아버지였다."]], [["These were all the particulars that I could gain from Marian.", "매리언에게서 얻어낼 수 있는 자세한 사정은 이것이 전부였다."], ["They suggested nothing which was useful to my present purpose, but I noted them down carefully, in the event of their proving to be of importance at any future period.", "지금의 내 목적에 쓸모 있는 것은 아무것도 없었지만, 나는 훗날 어느 때고 그것들이 중요한 것으로 드러날 경우에 대비해 꼼꼼히 적어 두었다."]], [["Mrs. Todd’s reply (addressed, by our own wish, to a post-office at some distance from us) had arrived at its destination when I went to apply for it.", "토드 부인의 답장은, 우리 쪽 부탁대로 우리 집에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 우체국 앞으로 부쳐졌는데, 내가 찾으러 갔을 때 이미 그곳에 도착해 있었다."], ["The chances, which had been all against us hitherto, turned from this moment in our favour. Mrs.", "그때까지 줄곧 우리에게 불리하기만 하던 운이 이 순간부터 우리 편으로 돌아섰다."], ["Todd’s letter contained the first item of information of which we were in search.", "토드 부인의 편지에는 우리가 찾고 있던 첫 번째 소식이 담겨 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Clements, it appeared, had (as we had conjectured) written to Todd’s Corner, asking pardon in the first place for the abrupt manner in which she and Anne had left their friends at the farm-house (on the morning after I had met the woman in white in Limmeridge churchyard), and then informing Mrs.", "알고 보니 클레멘츠 부인은 우리가 짐작한 대로 토드 코너에 편지를 보냈다."], ["Todd of Anne’s disappearance, and entreating that she would cause inquiries to be made in the neighbourhood, on the chance that the lost woman might have strayed back to Limmeridge. In making this request, Mrs.", "먼저 자기와 앤이 그 농가의 친구들 곁을 갑작스레 떠난 일을(내가 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 흰옷을 입은 여인을 만난 그 이튿날 아침이었다) 용서해 달라고 청한 다음, 앤이 사라졌다는 사실을 토드 부인에게 알리고, 잃어버린 그 여자가 리머리지로 되돌아왔을지도 모르니 그 근방을 수소문해 달라고 간곡히 부탁하는 내용이었다."], ["Clements had been careful to add to it the address at which she might always be heard of, and that address Mrs. Todd now transmitted to Marian.", "그렇게 부탁하면서 클레멘츠 부인은 언제든 자기 소식을 들을 수 있는 주소를 잊지 않고 덧붙여 두었고, 이제 토드 부인이 그 주소를 매리언에게 전해 준 것이었다."], ["It was in London, and within half an hour’s walk of our own lodging.", "그것은 런던 안, 우리 하숙에서 걸어서 삼십 분도 안 되는 곳이었다."]], [["In the words of the proverb, I was resolved not to let the grass grow under my feet.", "속담에 있는 말대로, 나는 발밑에 풀이 자라도록 내버려 두지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["The next morning I set forth to seek an interview with Mrs. Clements.", "이튿날 아침 나는 클레멘츠 부인을 만나 보러 길을 나섰다."], ["This was my first step forward in the investigation.", "이것이 이 조사에서 내가 내디딘 첫걸음이었다."], ["The story of the desperate attempt to which I now stood committed begins here.", "이제 내가 몸을 던지기로 한 그 필사의 시도에 관한 이야기는 여기서 시작된다."]], [["# VI", "# VI"]], [["The address communicated by Mrs. Todd took me to a lodging-house situated in a respectable street near the Gray’s Inn Road.", "토드 부인이 알려 준 주소를 따라가자, 그레이스 인 로드 가까운 곳의 점잖은 거리에 자리 잡은 하숙집이 나왔다."]], [["When I knocked the door was opened by Mrs. Clements herself.", "내가 문을 두드리자 클레멘츠 부인이 손수 문을 열었다."], ["She did not appear to remember me, and asked what my business was.", "그녀는 나를 기억하지 못하는 눈치로, 무슨 일로 왔느냐고 물었다."], ["I recalled to her our meeting in Limmeridge churchyard at the close of my interview there with the woman in white, taking special care to remind her that I was the person who assisted Anne Catherick (as Anne had herself declared) to escape the pursuit from the Asylum.", "나는 리머리지 묘지에서 흰옷을 입은 여인과 이야기를 마친 뒤 그녀를 만났던 일을 상기시키면서, 앤이 스스로 밝힌 대로 내가 앤 캐서릭을 도와 정신병원의 추적에서 벗어나게 해 준 사람이라는 점을 각별히 힘주어 일깨웠다."], ["This was my only claim to the confidence of Mrs. Clements.", "그것이 클레멘츠 부인의 신뢰를 얻을 나의 유일한 근거였다."], ["She remembered the circumstance the moment I spoke of it, and asked me into the parlour, in the greatest anxiety to know if I had brought her any news of Anne.", "내가 그 이야기를 꺼내자마자 그녀는 그때 일을 떠올렸고, 앤의 소식을 가져왔는지 몹시 애타게 알고 싶어 하며 나를 응접실로 청해 들였다."]], [["It was impossible for me to tell her the whole truth without, at the same time, entering into particulars on the subject of the conspiracy, which it would have been dangerous to confide to a stranger.", "음모의 자세한 내막까지 들어가지 않고서 그녀에게 진실을 온전히 말해 주기란 불가능한 일이었고, 그 내막은 낯선 사람에게 털어놓기에는 위험한 것이었다."], ["I could only abstain most carefully from raising any false hopes, and then explain that the object of my visit was to discover the persons who were really responsible for Anne’s disappearance.", "나는 다만 헛된 희망을 부추기는 일만은 극도로 삼가면서, 내가 찾아온 목적은 앤의 실종에 실제로 책임이 있는 자들을 밝혀내는 데 있다고 설명할 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["I even added, so as to exonerate myself from any after-reproach of my own conscience, that I entertained not the least hope of being able to trace her—that I believed we should never see her alive again—and that my main interest in the affair was to bring to punishment two men whom I suspected to be concerned in luring her away, and at whose hands I and some dear friends of mine had suffered a grievous wrong.", "게다가 나중에 내 양심의 가책을 받는 일이 없도록 이런 말까지 덧붙였다. 그녀를 찾아낼 수 있으리라는 희망은 조금도 품고 있지 않다는 것, 우리가 그녀를 다시 살아서 보는 일은 없으리라 믿는다는 것, 그리고 이 일에서 내 주된 관심사는 그녀를 꾀어내는 데 관련되었으리라 의심되는 두 사내—나와 나의 소중한 벗들이 그 손에 참혹한 해를 입은 두 사내—를 벌받게 하는 것이라는 이야기였다."], ["With this explanation I left it to Mrs. Clements to say whether our interest in the matter (whatever difference there might be in the motives which actuated us) was not the same, and whether she felt any reluctance to forward my object by giving me such information on the subject of my inquiries as she happened to possess.", "이렇게 설명한 뒤, 이 일에 대한 우리의 관심이 (우리를 움직이는 동기에는 차이가 있을지언정) 결국 같은 것이 아닌지, 그리고 내가 묻는 문제에 관해 그녀가 알고 있는 바를 알려 주어 내 목적을 도와주는 데 꺼리는 마음이 있는지를 클레멘츠 부인 스스로 말하도록 맡겼다."]], [["The poor woman was at first too much confused and agitated to understand thoroughly what I said to her.", "가엾은 부인은 처음에는 너무 혼란스럽고 동요한 나머지 내 말을 온전히 알아듣지 못했다."], ["She could only reply that I was welcome to anything she could tell me in return for the kindness I had shown to Anne; but as she was not very quick and ready, at the best of times, in talking to strangers, she would beg me to put her in the right way, and to say where I wished her to begin.", "그녀는 다만, 내가 앤에게 베푼 친절에 보답하는 뜻에서 자기가 아는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 말해 주겠다고 대답할 뿐이었다. 다만 자기는 평소에도 낯선 이와 이야기할 때 머리가 빠르게 돌아가는 편이 못 되니, 부디 갈피를 잡아 주고 어디서부터 시작하면 좋을지 일러 달라고 청했다."]], [["Knowing by experience that the plainest narrative attainable from persons who are not accustomed to arrange their ideas, is the narrative which goes far enough back at the beginning to avoid all impediments of retrospection in its course, I asked Mrs.", "제 생각을 정리하는 데 익숙하지 않은 사람에게서 얻어낼 수 있는 가장 명료한 이야기란, 도중에 되짚어 올라가야 하는 번거로움이 없도록 처음부터 충분히 멀리 거슬러 시작하는 이야기라는 것을 나는 경험으로 알고 있었다."], ["Clements to tell me first what had happened after she had left Limmeridge, and so, by watchful questioning, carried her on from point to point, till we reached the period of Anne’s disappearance.", "그래서 나는 클레멘츠 부인에게 먼저 리머리지를 떠난 뒤에 있었던 일부터 말해 달라고 청했고, 그런 다음 조심스레 질문을 던져 가며 한 대목에서 다음 대목으로 그녀를 이끌어, 마침내 앤이 사라진 시기에까지 이르렀다."]], [["The substance of the information which I thus obtained was as follows:—", "내가 그렇게 하여 얻어낸 이야기의 요지는 다음과 같았다.—"]], [["On leaving the farm at Todd’s Corner, Mrs. Clements and Anne had travelled that day as far as Derby, and had remained there a week on Anne’s account.", "토드 코너의 농가를 떠난 그날 클레멘츠 부인과 앤은 더비까지 갔고, 앤의 몸 때문에 그곳에서 일주일을 머물렀다."], ["They had then gone on to London, and had lived in the lodging occupied by Mrs. Clements at that time for a month or more, when circumstances connected with the house and the landlord had obliged them to change their quarters.", "그다음 두 사람은 런던으로 올라가, 당시 클레멘츠 부인이 얻어 살던 하숙에서 한 달 남짓 지냈는데, 그 집과 집주인에 얽힌 사정 때문에 거처를 옮기지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Anne’s terror of being discovered in London or its neighbourhood, whenever they ventured to walk out, had gradually communicated itself to Mrs. Clements, and she had determined on removing to one of the most out-of-the-way places in England—to the town of Grimsby in Lincolnshire, where her deceased husband had passed all his early life.", "밖으로 나설 때마다 런던이나 그 근방에서 발각될까 두려워하는 앤의 공포가 차츰 클레멘츠 부인에게도 옮아, 그녀는 잉글랜드에서 가장 외진 곳 가운데 하나로—링컨셔의 그림즈비라는 고을로 옮겨 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["His relatives were respectable people settled in the town—they had always treated Mrs.", "세상을 떠난 그녀의 남편이 젊은 시절을 온통 그곳에서 보냈던 것이다."], ["Clements with great kindness, and she thought it impossible to do better than go there and take the advice of her husband’s friends.", "남편의 친척들은 그 고을에 자리 잡은 점잖은 사람들이었고 늘 클레멘츠 부인을 크게 다정히 대해 주었으니, 그리로 가서 남편 친구들의 조언을 듣는 것보다 나은 길은 없다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["Anne would not hear of returning to her mother at Welmingham, because she had been removed to the Asylum from that place, and because Sir Percival would be certain to go back there and find her again.", "앤은 웰밍엄의 어머니에게로 돌아가는 일은 들으려고도 하지 않았다. 자기가 바로 그곳에서 정신병원으로 끌려갔으니, 퍼시벌 경이 반드시 그리로 되돌아와 자기를 다시 찾아내리라는 것이었다."], ["There was serious weight in this objection, and Mrs. Clements felt that it was not to be easily removed.", "그 반대에는 무겁게 새겨들을 만한 이유가 있었고, 클레멘츠 부인도 쉽사리 물리칠 수 있는 이유가 아니라고 느꼈다."]], [["At Grimsby the first serious symptoms of illness had shown themselves in Anne.", "그림즈비에서 앤에게 병의 첫 심각한 징후가 나타났다."], ["They appeared soon after the news of Lady Glyde’s marriage had been made public in the newspapers, and had reached her through that medium.", "그 징후는 글라이드 부인의 결혼 소식이 신문에 공표되고 그 지면을 통해 그녀에게까지 전해진 직후에 나타난 것이었다."]], [["The medical man who was sent for to attend the sick woman discovered at once that she was suffering from a serious affection of the heart.", "앓는 여인을 보러 불려 온 의사는 그녀가 심장에 심각한 병을 앓고 있음을 대번에 알아보았다."], ["The illness lasted long, left her very weak, and returned at intervals, though with mitigated severity, again and again.", "병은 오래 이어져 그녀를 몹시 쇠약하게 만들었고, 정도는 다소 누그러졌으나 이따금씩 거듭 되살아났다."], ["They remained at Grimsby, in consequence, during the first half of the new year, and there they might probably have stayed much longer, but for the sudden resolution which Anne took at this time to venture back to Hampshire, for the purpose of obtaining a private interview with Lady Glyde.", "그런 까닭에 두 사람은 새해의 전반기 내내 그림즈비에 머물렀고, 아마 훨씬 더 오래 머물렀을 터였다. 다만 이 무렵 앤이 글라이드 부인과 은밀히 만나 보겠다는 목적으로 햄프셔로 되돌아가겠다는 갑작스러운 결심을 하지만 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["Mrs. Clements did all in her power to oppose the execution of this hazardous and unaccountable project.", "클레멘츠 부인은 이 위험하고도 영문 모를 계획이 실행에 옮겨지지 못하도록 온 힘을 다해 막았다."], ["No explanation of her motives was offered by Anne, except that she believed the day of her death was not far off, and that she had something on her mind which must be communicated to Lady Glyde, at any risk, in secret.", "앤은 자기 동기에 대해 아무런 설명도 내놓지 않았다. 다만 자기가 죽을 날이 머지않았다고 믿는다는 것, 그리고 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 은밀히 글라이드 부인에게 전해야 할 마음속의 일이 있다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Her resolution to accomplish this purpose was so firmly settled that she declared her intention of going to Hampshire by herself if Mrs. Clements felt any unwillingness to go with her.", "그 뜻을 이루겠다는 결심이 어찌나 굳었던지, 클레멘츠 부인이 함께 가기를 꺼린다면 자기 혼자서라도 햄프셔로 가겠노라고 그녀는 잘라 말했다."], ["The doctor, on being consulted, was of opinion that serious opposition to her wishes would, in all probability, produce another and perhaps a fatal fit of illness, and Mrs.", "의견을 물으니 의사는, 그녀의 뜻을 심하게 거스르면 십중팔구 또 한 차례, 어쩌면 목숨을 앗아 갈 발작을 일으키게 되리라는 견해였다."], ["Clements, under this advice, yielded to necessity, and once more, with sad forebodings of trouble and danger to come, allowed Anne Catherick to have her own way.", "이 조언을 듣고 클레멘츠 부인은 어쩔 수 없이 뜻을 굽혔고, 앞으로 닥칠 곤경과 위험에 대한 서글픈 예감을 안은 채 또 한 번 앤 캐서릭이 제 뜻대로 하도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["On the journey from London to Hampshire Mrs. Clements discovered that one of their fellow-passengers was well acquainted with the neighbourhood of Blackwater, and could give her all the information she needed on the subject of localities.", "런던에서 햄프셔로 가는 길에 클레멘츠 부인은 같은 칸에 탄 승객 하나가 블랙워터 근방을 훤히 알고 있어 지리에 관해 필요한 것을 모두 일러 줄 수 있다는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["In this way she found out that the only place they could go to, which was not dangerously near to Sir Percival’s residence, was a large village called Sandon.", "그리하여 그녀는 퍼시벌 경의 저택에 위험할 만큼 가깝지 않으면서 두 사람이 갈 수 있는 유일한 곳이 샌던이라는 큰 마을임을 알아냈다."], ["The distance here from Blackwater Park was between three and four miles—and that distance, and back again, Anne had walked on each occasion when she had appeared in the neighbourhood of the lake.", "그곳에서 블랙워터 파크까지의 거리는 삼사 마일쯤 되었는데, 앤은 호숫가 근처에 모습을 드러낼 때마다 그 거리를 걸어서 오갔던 것이다."]], [["For the few days during which they were at Sandon without being discovered they had lived a little away from the village, in the cottage of a decent widow-woman who had a bedroom to let, and whose discreet silence Mrs. Clements had done her best to secure, for the first week at least.", "샌던에서 발각되지 않고 지낸 며칠 동안 두 사람은 마을에서 조금 떨어진 곳, 방 하나를 세놓던 점잖은 과부의 오두막에서 지냈고, 클레멘츠 부인은 적어도 첫 주만이라도 그 여인이 입을 다물어 주도록 힘껏 애를 썼다."], ["She had also tried hard to induce Anne to be content with writing to Lady Glyde, in the first instance; but the failure of the warning contained in the anonymous letter sent to Limmeridge had made Anne resolute to speak this time, and obstinate in the determination to go on her errand alone.", "또한 우선은 글라이드 부인에게 편지를 쓰는 것으로 만족하라고 앤을 설득하려 무던히도 애를 썼다. 그러나 리머리지로 보낸 익명의 편지에 담긴 경고가 아무 소용이 없었던 터라, 앤은 이번에는 직접 말을 하겠다고 마음을 굳혔고 혼자서 그 일을 하러 가겠다고 고집을 부렸다."]], [["Mrs. Clements, nevertheless, followed her privately on each occasion when she went to the lake, without, however, venturing near enough to the boat-house to be witness of what took place there.", "그럼에도 클레멘츠 부인은 앤이 호수로 갈 때마다 몰래 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["When Anne returned for the last time from the dangerous neighbourhood, the fatigue of walking, day after day, distances which were far too great for her strength, added to the exhausting effect of the agitation from which she had suffered, produced the result which Mrs.", "다만 보트 창고에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 지켜볼 만큼 가까이 다가갈 엄두는 내지 못했다. 앤이 그 위험한 근방에서 마지막으로 돌아왔을 때, 제 기력에 견주어 턱없이 먼 거리를 날마다 걸어 다닌 피로에다 그녀가 겪은 격한 동요의 소모까지 겹쳐, 클레멘츠 부인이 내내 두려워하던 결과가 나타났다."], ["Clements had dreaded all along. The old pain over the heart and the other symptoms of the illness at Grimsby returned, and Anne was confined to her bed in the cottage.", "심장 위쪽의 그 해묵은 통증과 그림즈비에서 앓던 병의 다른 증세들이 되살아났고, 앤은 오두막의 침상에 몸져눕고 말았다."]], [["In this emergency the first necessity, as Mrs. Clements knew by experience, was to endeavour to quiet Anne’s anxiety of mind, and for this purpose the good woman went herself the next day to the lake, to try if she could find Lady Glyde (who would be sure, as Anne said, to take her daily walk to the boat-house), and prevail on her to come back privately to the cottage near Sandon.", "이 위급한 상황에서 무엇보다 먼저 해야 할 일은, 클레멘츠 부인이 경험으로 알고 있듯이, 앤의 불안한 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰는 것이었다."], ["On reaching the outskirts of the plantation Mrs.", "그래서 이 착한 부인은 이튿날 손수 호수로 갔다."], ["Clements encountered, not Lady Glyde, but a tall, stout, elderly gentleman, with a book in his hand—in other words, Count Fosco.", "앤의 말대로라면 글라이드 부인이 틀림없이 날마다 보트 창고까지 산책을 나올 터이니, 그분을 만나 샌던 근처의 오두막으로 남몰래 와 달라고 청해 볼 수 있을까 싶어서였다. 수풀 가장자리에 이르렀을 때 클레멘츠 부인이 마주친 것은 글라이드 부인이 아니라, 손에 책을 든 키 크고 살진 노신사—다시 말해 포스코 백작이었다."]], [["The Count, after looking at her very attentively for a moment, asked if she expected to see any one in that place, and added, before she could reply, that he was waiting there with a message from Lady Glyde, but that he was not quite certain whether the person then before him answered the description of the person with whom he was desired to communicate.", "백작은 잠시 그녀를 아주 유심히 살펴본 뒤, 그곳에서 누군가를 만나기로 되어 있느냐고 물었다. 그러고는 그녀가 대답하기도 전에, 자기는 글라이드 부인의 전갈을 가지고 여기서 기다리는 중인데 지금 눈앞에 있는 사람이 자기가 말을 전하라고 부탁받은 그 사람의 인상착의와 맞는지 썩 확신이 서지 않는다고 덧붙였다."]], [["Upon this Mrs. Clements at once confided her errand to him, and entreated that he would help to allay Anne’s anxiety by trusting his message to her.", "이 말에 클레멘츠 부인은 곧바로 자기가 온 용건을 그에게 털어놓고, 그 전갈을 자기에게 맡겨 앤의 불안을 가라앉히는 데 도움을 달라고 간청했다."], ["The Count most readily and kindly complied with her request.", "백작은 아주 선선히, 그리고 친절하게 그 청을 들어주었다."], ["The message, he said, was a very important one.", "그가 말하기를, 전갈은 매우 중요한 것이었다."], ["Lady Glyde entreated Anne and her good friend to return immediately to London, as she felt certain that Sir Percival would discover them if they remained any longer in the neighbourhood of Blackwater.", "글라이드 부인은 앤과 그녀의 착한 벗에게 곧바로 런던으로 돌아가 달라고 간곡히 청한다는 것이었다. 두 사람이 블랙워터 근방에 더 오래 머문다면 퍼시벌 경이 반드시 찾아내리라 확신한다는 것이었다."], ["She was herself going to London in a short time, and if Mrs. Clements and Anne would go there first, and would let her know what their address was, they should hear from her and see her in a fortnight or less.", "부인 자신도 머지않아 런던으로 갈 터이니, 클레멘츠 부인과 앤이 먼저 그리로 가서 주소를 알려 준다면 보름이나 그 안에 소식을 듣고 만나게 되리라 했다."], ["The Count added that he had already attempted to give a friendly warning to Anne herself, but that she had been too much startled by seeing that he was a stranger to let him approach and speak to her.", "백작은 자기도 앤에게 직접 다정하게 경고를 해 주려 해 보았으나, 그녀가 낯선 사람인 자기를 보고 너무 놀란 나머지 가까이 가서 말을 붙이도록 놓아두지 않았다고 덧붙였다."]], [["To this Mrs. Clements replied, in the greatest alarm and distress, that she asked nothing better than to take Anne safely to London, but that there was no present hope of removing her from the dangerous neighbourhood, as she lay ill in her bed at that moment.", "이에 클레멘츠 부인은 몹시 놀라고 애가 타서, 앤을 무사히 런던으로 데려가는 것이야말로 자기가 무엇보다 바라는 바이지만 지금은 그 아이가 병상에 누워 있으니 그 위험한 근방에서 데리고 나갈 가망이 당장은 없다고 대답했다."], ["The Count inquired if Mrs. Clements had sent for medical advice, and hearing that she had hitherto hesitated to do so, from the fear of making their position publicly known in the village, informed her that he was himself a medical man, and that he would go back with her if she pleased, and see what could be done for Anne.", "백작은 클레멘츠 부인이 의사를 부른 적이 있는지 물었고, 마을에 자기들 처지가 알려질까 두려워 여태 부르기를 망설였다는 말을 듣자, 자기가 바로 의사이니 원한다면 함께 돌아가 앤을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠다고 알려 주었다."], ["Mrs. Clements (feeling a natural confidence in the Count, as a person trusted with a secret message from Lady Glyde) gratefully accepted the offer, and they went back together to the cottage.", "클레멘츠 부인은 (글라이드 부인의 은밀한 전갈을 맡은 사람인 만큼 백작을 자연스레 믿었으므로) 고맙게 그 제안을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 함께 오두막으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Anne was asleep when they got there.", "그들이 도착했을 때 앤은 잠들어 있었다."], ["The Count started at the sight of her (evidently from astonishment at her resemblance to Lady Glyde).", "백작은 그녀를 보는 순간 흠칫 놀랐다(글라이드 부인과 닮은 모습에 놀란 것이 분명했다)."], ["Poor Mrs. Clements supposed that he was only shocked to see how ill she was.", "가엾은 클레멘츠 부인은 그가 그저 앤의 병세가 얼마나 심한지를 보고 충격을 받았으려니 여겼다."], ["He would not allow her to be awakened—he was contented with putting questions to Mrs.", "그는 앤을 깨우지 못하게 했다."], ["Clements about her symptoms, with looking at her, and with lightly touching her pulse.", "클레멘츠 부인에게 증세를 물어보고, 그녀를 들여다보고, 맥을 가볍게 짚어 보는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Sandon was a large enough place to have a grocer’s and druggist’s shop in it, and thither the Count went to write his prescription and to get the medicine made up.", "샌던은 식료품점 겸 약재상이 있을 만큼은 큰 곳이었으므로, 백작은 그리로 가서 처방을 적고 약을 지어 오게 했다."], ["He brought it back himself, and told Mrs. Clements that the medicine was a powerful stimulant, and that it would certainly give Anne strength to get up and bear the fatigue of a journey to London of only a few hours.", "그는 손수 약을 가져와서는, 그것이 강력한 흥분제이니 앤이 자리에서 일어나 몇 시간밖에 걸리지 않는 런던행 여정의 고단함을 견딜 만한 기운을 틀림없이 얻게 되리라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 일러 주었다."], ["The remedy was to be administered at stated times on that day and on the day after.", "약은 그날과 이튿날에 정해진 시각마다 먹여야 했다."], ["On the third day she would be well enough to travel, and he arranged to meet Mrs. Clements at the Blackwater station, and to see them off by the midday train.", "사흘째가 되면 앤이 길을 떠날 만큼 나아질 터였고, 그는 블랙워터 역에서 클레멘츠 부인과 만나 정오 기차로 두 사람을 떠나보내기로 약속을 잡았다."], ["If they did not appear he would assume that Anne was worse, and would proceed at once to the cottage.", "만일 두 사람이 나타나지 않으면 앤의 병이 더 나빠진 것으로 여기고 곧장 오두막으로 가겠다고 했다."]], [["As events turned out, no such emergency as this occurred.", "그러나 일이 되어 간 형편으로는 그런 위급한 사태는 벌어지지 않았다."]], [["This medicine had an extraordinary effect on Anne, and the good results of it were helped by the assurance Mrs. Clements could now give her that she would soon see Lady Glyde in London.", "이 약은 앤에게 놀라운 효험을 냈고, 이제 런던에서 곧 글라이드 부인을 만나게 되리라고 클레멘츠 부인이 장담해 줄 수 있었던 것이 그 좋은 결과를 거들었다."], ["At the appointed day and time (when they had not been quite so long as a week in Hampshire altogether), they arrived at the station.", "약속한 날 약속한 시각에 (햄프셔에 머문 기간을 다 합쳐도 채 일주일이 되지 않았을 무렵) 두 사람은 역에 닿았다."], ["The Count was waiting there for them, and was talking to an elderly lady, who appeared to be going to travel by the train to London also.", "백작이 거기서 그들을 기다리고 있었는데, 역시 기차로 런던까지 가는 듯한 어느 나이 지긋한 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["He most kindly assisted them, and put them into the carriage himself, begging Mrs. Clements not to forget to send her address to Lady Glyde.", "그는 아주 친절하게 두 사람을 거들어 손수 객차에 태워 주면서, 글라이드 부인에게 주소 보내는 것을 잊지 말라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 당부했다."], ["The elderly lady did not travel in the same compartment, and they did not notice what became of her on reaching the London terminus. Mrs.", "그 나이 지긋한 부인은 같은 칸에 타지 않았고, 두 사람은 런던 종착역에 닿았을 때 그녀가 어디로 갔는지 알아차리지 못했다."], ["Clements secured respectable lodgings in a quiet neighbourhood, and then wrote, as she had engaged to do, to inform Lady Glyde of the address.", "클레멘츠 부인은 조용한 동네에 번듯한 하숙을 얻은 다음, 약속한 대로 글라이드 부인에게 편지를 써서 주소를 알렸다."]], [["A little more than a fortnight passed, and no answer came.", "보름이 조금 넘게 지났으나 아무런 답장도 오지 않았다."]], [["At the end of that time a lady (the same elderly lady whom they had seen at the station) called in a cab, and said that she came from Lady Glyde, who was then at an hotel in London, and who wished to see Mrs.", "그 무렵이 지났을 때 한 부인이(역에서 보았던 바로 그 나이 지긋한 부인이었다) 삯마차를 타고 찾아와, 자기는 글라이드 부인의 심부름으로 왔노라고 말했다."], ["Clements, for the purpose of arranging a future interview with Anne. Mrs.", "글라이드 부인은 지금 런던의 어느 호텔에 머물고 있는데, 앞으로 앤과 만날 일을 의논하려고 클레멘츠 부인을 만나고 싶어 한다는 것이었다."], ["Clements expressed her willingness (Anne being present at the time, and entreating her to do so) to forward the object in view, especially as she was not required to be away from the house for more than half an hour at the most.", "클레멘츠 부인은 (그때 자리에 있던 앤이 꼭 그렇게 해 달라고 간청하기도 했으므로) 그 일을 돕는 데 기꺼이 응하겠노라 밝혔다. 더구나 집을 비우는 시간이 길어야 반 시간을 넘지 않아도 된다니 더욱 그러했다."], ["She and the elderly lady (clearly Madame Fosco) then left in the cab.", "그리하여 그녀와 그 나이 지긋한 부인은(포스코 백작부인이 분명했다) 삯마차를 타고 떠났다."], ["The lady stopped the cab, after it had driven some distance, at a shop before they got to the hotel, and begged Mrs. Clements to wait for her for a few minutes while she made a purchase that had been forgotten.", "마차가 얼마쯤 달린 뒤, 그 부인은 호텔에 닿기 전 어느 가게 앞에서 마차를 세우고는, 깜빡 잊고 사지 못한 물건을 사 올 동안 몇 분만 기다려 달라고 클레멘츠 부인에게 청했다."], ["She never appeared again.", "그러고는 다시 나타나지 않았다."]], [["After waiting some time Mrs. Clements became alarmed, and ordered the cabman to drive back to her lodgings.", "한참을 기다리던 클레멘츠 부인은 덜컥 겁이 나서 마부에게 하숙집으로 되돌아가 달라고 일렀다."], ["When she got there, after an absence of rather more than half an hour, Anne was gone.", "반 시간 남짓 집을 비운 끝에 그곳에 닿았을 때, 앤은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["The only information to be obtained from the people of the house was derived from the servant who waited on the lodgers.", "그 집 사람들에게서 얻을 수 있는 정보라고는 하숙인들의 시중을 드는 하녀에게서 나온 것이 전부였다."], ["She had opened the door to a boy from the street, who had left a letter for “the young woman who lived on the second floor” (the part of the house which Mrs.", "하녀는 거리에서 온 사내아이에게 문을 열어 주었는데, 그 아이가 “이층에 사는 젊은 여자분”(클레멘츠 부인이 쓰던 층이었다) 앞으로 온 편지를 두고 갔다는 것이었다."], ["Clements occupied). The servant had delivered the letter, had then gone downstairs, and five minutes afterwards had observed Anne open the front door and go out, dressed in her bonnet and shawl.", "하녀는 편지를 전해 준 뒤 아래층으로 내려갔고, 오 분쯤 지나 앤이 보닛에 숄을 두른 차림으로 현관문을 열고 나가는 것을 보았다."], ["She had probably taken the letter with her, for it was not to be found, and it was therefore impossible to tell what inducement had been offered to make her leave the house.", "앤은 그 편지를 지니고 갔던 모양이다. 편지를 찾을 수 없었으니, 그녀를 집에서 나가게 만든 유인이 무엇이었는지는 알 길이 없었다."], ["It must have been a strong one, for she would never stir out alone in London of her own accord.", "그것은 필시 대단히 강한 것이었을 터다. 그녀는 결코 제 발로 런던 거리에 혼자 나서는 법이 없었으니까."], ["If Mrs. Clements had not known this by experience nothing would have induced her to go away in the cab, even for so short a time as half an hour only.", "클레멘츠 부인이 그것을 경험으로 알고 있지 않았더라면, 아무리 반 시간뿐인 짧은 동안이라 해도 삯마차를 타고 집을 비우는 일은 결코 하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["As soon as she could collect her thoughts, the first idea that naturally occurred to Mrs. Clements was to go and make inquiries at the Asylum, to which she dreaded that Anne had been taken back.", "정신을 겨우 추스르자마자 클레멘츠 부인에게 자연히 떠오른 첫 생각은, 앤이 도로 끌려갔을까 봐 두려워하던 그 정신병원에 가서 알아보는 것이었다."]], [["She went there the next day, having been informed of the locality in which the house was situated by Anne herself.", "그녀는 이튿날 그리로 갔다. 그 병원이 어디에 있는지는 앤에게서 직접 들어 알고 있었다."], ["The answer she received (her application having in all probability been made a day or two before the false Anne Catherick had really been consigned to safe keeping in the Asylum) was, that no such person had been brought back there.", "그녀가 받은 대답은 (그녀가 문의한 것은 십중팔구 가짜 앤 캐서릭이 실제로 그 병원에 갇히기 하루 이틀 전이었을 것이다) 그런 사람이 되돌아온 적은 없다는 것이었다."], ["She had then written to Mrs. Catherick at Welmingham to know if she had seen or heard anything of her daughter, and had received an answer in the negative.", "그래서 그녀는 웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인에게 편지를 써서 딸을 보거나 딸의 소식을 들은 적이 있는지 물었으나, 없다는 답장을 받았다."], ["After that reply had reached her, she was at the end of her resources, and perfectly ignorant where else to inquire or what else to do.", "그 답장이 온 뒤로 그녀는 방도가 다했고, 달리 어디에 물어야 할지 무엇을 해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["From that time to this she had remained in total ignorance of the cause of Anne’s disappearance and of the end of Anne’s story.", "그때부터 지금까지 그녀는 앤이 사라진 까닭도, 앤의 이야기가 어떻게 끝났는지도 전혀 알지 못한 채 지내 왔다."]], [["# VII", "# VII"]], [["Thus far the information which I had received from Mrs. Clements—though it established facts of which I had not previously been aware—was of a preliminary character only.", "여기까지 클레멘츠 부인에게서 들은 이야기는—내가 그때까지 알지 못했던 사실들을 밝혀 주기는 했으나—어디까지나 예비적인 성격의 것에 지나지 않았다."]], [["It was clear that the series of deceptions which had removed Anne Catherick to London, and separated her from Mrs.", "앤 캐서릭을 런던으로 옮겨 놓고 클레멘츠 부인과 떼어 놓은 그 일련의 기만이 오로지 포스코 백작과 백작부인의 손으로 이루어졌다는 것은 분명했다."], ["Clements, had been accomplished solely by Count Fosco and the Countess, and the question whether any part of the conduct of husband or wife had been of a kind to place either of them within reach of the law might be well worthy of future consideration.", "그 부부의 행위 가운데 어느 부분이 법의 손이 닿을 만한 것이었는지는 훗날 충분히 따져 볼 만한 문제였다."], ["But the purpose I had now in view led me in another direction than this.", "그러나 내가 지금 눈앞에 두고 있는 목적은 나를 그와는 다른 방향으로 이끌었다."], ["The immediate object of my visit to Mrs. Clements was to make some approach at least to the discovery of Sir Percival’s secret, and she had said nothing as yet which advanced me on my way to that important end.", "클레멘츠 부인을 찾아온 당면한 목적은 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 밝혀내는 일에 적어도 한 걸음이라도 다가서는 것이었는데, 부인은 그 중대한 목표로 나를 나아가게 할 만한 이야기는 아직 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["I felt the necessity of trying to awaken her recollections of other times, persons, and events than those on which her memory had hitherto been employed, and when I next spoke I spoke with that object indirectly in view.", "나는 그때까지 부인의 기억이 머물러 있던 시절과 사람들과 사건들이 아닌, 그 이전의 것들에 대한 회상을 일깨워 볼 필요를 느꼈고, 다음에 입을 열었을 때는 그 목적을 간접적으로 염두에 두고 말했다."]], [["“I wish I could be of any help to you in this sad calamity,”", "“이 슬픈 재난에 제가 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있으면 좋으련만.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “All I can do is to feel heartily for your distress.", "“제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 부인의 비통함에 진심으로 함께하는 것뿐입니다."], ["If Anne had been your own child, Mrs. Clements, you could have shown her no truer kindness—you could have made no readier sacrifices for her sake.”", "앤이 부인의 친자식이었다 해도, 클레멘츠 부인, 그보다 더 참된 사랑을 보여 주실 수는 없었을 겁니다—그 아이를 위해 그보다 더 선뜻 희생하실 수도 없었을 거예요.”"]], [["“There’s no great merit in that, sir,”", "“그건 대단할 것도 없는 일입니다, 선생님.”"], ["said Mrs. Clements simply.", "클레멘츠 부인이 담담하게 말했다."], ["“The poor thing was as good as my own child to me.", "“그 가엾은 것은 저에게 친자식이나 다름없었어요."], ["I nursed her from a baby, sir, bringing her up by hand—and a hard job it was to rear her.", "젖먹이 때부터 제가 젖도 없이 손으로 먹여 키웠지요, 선생님—키우기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었답니다."], ["It wouldn’t go to my heart so to lose her if I hadn’t made her first short clothes and taught her to walk.", "제가 그 아이 첫 배내옷을 지어 주지 않았더라면, 걸음마를 가르치지 않았더라면 잃은 게 이렇게까지 가슴에 사무치지는 않았을 거예요."], ["I always said she was sent to console me for never having chick or child of my own.", "저는 늘 그 아이가 제게 자식 하나 없는 걸 위로해 주라고 보내진 아이라고 말했지요."], ["And now she’s lost the old times keep coming back to my mind, and even at my age I can’t help crying about her—I can’t indeed, sir!”", "그런데 이제 그 아이를 잃고 나니 옛날 일들이 자꾸만 떠올라서, 이 나이에도 그 아이 생각에 우는 걸 어쩔 수가 없습니다—정말 어쩔 수가 없어요, 선생님!”"]], [["I waited a little to give Mrs. Clements time to compose herself.", "나는 클레멘츠 부인이 마음을 가라앉힐 시간을 주려고 잠시 기다렸다."], ["Was the light that I had been looking for so long glimmering on me—far off, as yet—in the good woman’s recollections of Anne’s early life?", "내가 그토록 오래 찾아 헤매던 그 빛이—아직은 아득히 멀리서나마—이 선량한 부인이 간직한 앤의 어린 시절 기억 속에서 희미하게 비쳐 오고 있는 것일까?"]], [["“Did you know Mrs. Catherick before Anne was born?”", "“앤이 태어나기 전부터 캐서릭 부인을 아셨습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Not very long, sir—not above four months.", "“그리 오래는 아니고요, 선생님—넉 달이 채 안 되었지요."], ["We saw a great deal of each other in that time, but we were never very friendly together.”", "그동안 서로 자주 만나기는 했지만, 사이가 그리 살가웠던 적은 없었습니다.”"]], [["Her voice was steadier as she made that reply.", "그 대답을 할 때 부인의 목소리는 한결 안정되어 있었다."], ["Painful as many of her recollections might be, I observed that it was unconsciously a relief to her mind to revert to the dimly-seen troubles of the past, after dwelling so long on the vivid sorrows of the present.", "그 회상들 가운데 많은 것이 아무리 괴로운 것이라 해도, 눈앞의 생생한 슬픔에 그토록 오래 잠겨 있다가 희미하게 보이는 옛 시절의 고통으로 되돌아가는 것이 부인의 마음에는 자기도 모르게 위안이 되고 있음을 나는 알아차렸다."]], [["“Were you and Mrs. Catherick neighbours?”", "“부인과 캐서릭 부인은 이웃이었습니까?”"], ["I inquired, leading her memory on as encouragingly as I could.", "나는 될 수 있는 대로 부인의 기억을 북돋아 이끌며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir—neighbours at Old Welmingham.”", "“네, 선생님—올드 웰밍엄에서 이웃이었지요.”"]], [["“OLD Welmingham?", "“올드 웰밍엄이라고요?"], ["There are two places of that name, then, in Hampshire?”", "그러면 햄프셔에 그 이름을 가진 곳이 두 군데 있는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Well, sir, there used to be in those days—better than three-and-twenty years ago.", "“그게요, 선생님, 그 시절에는 그랬습니다—스물세 해도 더 전 일이지요."], ["They built a new town about two miles off, convenient to the river—and Old Welmingham, which was never much more than a village, got in time to be deserted.", "강에 가깝게 두 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 새 읍을 세웠고, 원래도 마을 정도밖에 안 되던 올드 웰밍엄은 차츰 사람이 다 빠져나가 버렸어요."], ["The new town is the place they call Welmingham now—but the old parish church is the parish church still.", "지금 웰밍엄이라고들 부르는 곳은 새 읍입니다—하지만 옛 교구 교회는 여전히 그대로 교구 교회지요."], ["It stands by itself, with the houses pulled down or gone to ruin all round it.", "주위의 집들은 헐리거나 무너져 내리고 그 교회만 홀로 서 있답니다."], ["I’ve lived to see sad changes.", "저도 참 서글픈 변화들을 다 보고 살았네요."], ["It was a pleasant, pretty place in my time.”", "제가 살던 시절에는 정답고 어여쁜 곳이었는데요.”"]], [["“Did you live there before your marriage, Mrs. Clements?”", "“결혼하시기 전부터 그곳에 사셨습니까, 클레멘츠 부인?”"]], [["“No, sir—I’m a Norfolk woman.", "“아니요, 선생님—저는 노퍽 사람입니다."], ["It wasn’t the place my husband belonged to either.", "제 남편이 원래 살던 고장도 아니었고요."], ["He was from Grimsby, as I told you, and he served his apprenticeship there.", "말씀드렸다시피 남편은 그림즈비 출신이라 거기서 도제 생활을 했습니다."], ["But having friends down south, and hearing of an opening, he got into business at Southampton.", "그런데 남쪽에 아는 이들이 있었고 자리가 났다는 말을 듣고서 사우샘프턴에서 장사를 시작했지요."], ["It was in a small way, but he made enough for a plain man to retire on, and settled at Old Welmingham.", "규모는 작았지만 평범한 사람 하나 물러앉기에 넉넉할 만큼은 벌어서 올드 웰밍엄에 자리를 잡았습니다."], ["I went there with him when he married me.", "저는 남편이 저와 혼인하면서 그이를 따라 그리로 갔지요."], ["We were neither of us young, but we lived very happy together—happier than our neighbour, Mr. Catherick, lived along with his wife when they came to Old Welmingham a year or two afterwards.”", "우리 둘 다 젊은 나이는 아니었지만 아주 화목하게 살았습니다—한두 해 뒤에 올드 웰밍엄으로 온 이웃 캐서릭 씨가 그 부인과 살던 것보다야 훨씬 화목했지요.”"]], [["“Was your husband acquainted with them before that?”", "“그전부터 바깥어른께서 그 부부를 아셨던 겁니까?”"]], [["“With Catherick, sir—not with his wife.", "“캐서릭은 알았지요, 선생님—부인은 몰랐고요."], ["She was a stranger to both of us.", "그 여자는 우리 둘 다에게 낯선 사람이었습니다."], ["Some gentlemen had made interest for Catherick, and he got the situation of clerk at Welmingham church, which was the reason of his coming to settle in our neighbourhood.", "어떤 신사 분들이 캐서릭을 위해 힘을 써 주어서 웰밍엄 교회의 서기 자리를 얻었는데, 그래서 우리 동네에 와 살게 된 것이었어요."], ["He brought his newly-married wife along with him, and we heard in course of time she had been lady’s-maid in a family that lived at Varneck Hall, near Southampton.", "그이는 갓 혼인한 아내를 데리고 왔는데, 얼마 뒤에 그 여자가 사우샘프턴 근처 바넥 홀에 사는 집안에서 시녀로 있었다는 말을 들었습니다."], ["Catherick had found it a hard matter to get her to marry him, in consequence of her holding herself uncommonly high.", "캐서릭은 그 여자가 어찌나 도도하게 굴었던지 혼인 승낙을 받아 내느라 애를 먹었다더군요."], ["He had asked and asked, and given the thing up at last, seeing she was so contrary about it.", "청하고 또 청하다가, 그 여자가 하도 어깃장을 놓으니 결국 단념했답니다."], ["When he HAD given it up she turned contrary just the other way, and came to him of her own accord, without rhyme or reason seemingly.", "그런데 막상 단념하고 나니까 이번엔 정반대로 어깃장을 놓아, 아무 까닭도 이유도 없어 보이는데 제 발로 그이에게 왔다는 거예요."], ["My poor husband always said that was the time to have given her a lesson.", "제 가엾은 남편은 늘 그때가 그 여자 버릇을 고쳐 놓았어야 할 때였다고 말했습니다."], ["But Catherick was too fond of her to do anything of the sort—he never checked her either before they were married or after.", "그렇지만 캐서릭은 그 여자를 너무 사랑해서 그런 짓은 할 사람이 못 되었지요—혼인 전에도 후에도 한 번 나무란 적이 없었어요."], ["He was a quick man in his feelings, letting them carry him a deal too far, now in one way and now in another, and he would have spoilt a better wife than Mrs. Catherick if a better had married him.", "감정이 앞서는 사람이라 이쪽으로든 저쪽으로든 그 감정에 너무 멀리 끌려다녔으니, 캐서릭 부인보다 나은 여자를 얻었더라도 그 사람은 아내를 버려 놓았을 겁니다."], ["I don’t like to speak ill of any one, sir, but she was a heartless woman, with a terrible will of her own—fond of foolish admiration and fine clothes, and not caring to show so much as decent outward respect to Catherick, kindly as he always treated her.", "저는 남의 험담을 하고 싶지 않습니다만, 선생님, 그 여자는 무정한 여자였어요. 무서운 고집을 지닌 데다—어리석은 칭찬과 고운 옷가지를 좋아하고, 캐서릭이 늘 다정하게 대해 주는데도 겉으로나마 마땅한 예의조차 차릴 줄 몰랐지요."], ["My husband said he thought things would turn out badly when they first came to live near us, and his words proved true.", "제 남편은 그 부부가 우리 옆에 살러 왔을 때부터 일이 좋지 않게 될 것 같다고 했는데, 그 말이 그대로 들어맞았습니다."], ["Before they had been quite four months in our neighbourhood there was a dreadful scandal and a miserable break-up in their household.", "우리 동네에 온 지 넉 달이 채 되기도 전에 끔찍한 추문이 나고 그 집은 비참하게 깨어지고 말았어요."], ["Both of them were in fault—I am afraid both of them were equally in fault.”", "둘 다 잘못이었지요—안타깝지만 둘 다 똑같이 잘못이었습니다.”"]], [["“You mean both husband and wife?”", "“부부 둘 다를 말씀하시는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Oh, no, sir!", "“아이고, 아니에요, 선생님!"], ["I don’t mean Catherick—he was only to be pitied.", "캐서릭을 말하는 게 아닙니다—그이는 그저 딱할 뿐이었지요."], ["I meant his wife and the person—”", "제가 말한 건 그 아내와 그 사람이—”"]], [["“And the person who caused the scandal?”", "“그러면 그 추문을 일으킨 사람 말씀이군요?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.", "“그렇습니다, 선생님."], ["A gentleman born and brought up, who ought to have set a better example.", "태생도 가정 교육도 훌륭한 신사였으니 마땅히 본을 보였어야 할 사람이지요."], ["You know him, sir—and my poor dear Anne knew him only too well.”", "선생님도 아시는 분입니다—그리고 제 가엾은 앤도 그이를 너무나 잘 알고 있었고요.”"]], [["“Sir Percival Glyde?”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니까?”"]], [["“Yes, Sir Percival Glyde.”", "“네, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니다.”"]], [["My heart beat fast—I thought I had my hand on the clue.", "내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다—나는 마침내 실마리를 손에 쥐었다고 생각했다."], ["How little I knew then of the windings of the labyrinths which were still to mislead me!", "앞으로도 나를 헤매게 할 미로의 굽이굽이를 그때의 나는 얼마나 몰랐던가!"]], [["“Did Sir Percival live in your neighbourhood at that time?”", "“그 무렵 퍼시벌 경이 부인 동네에 살고 있었습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“No, sir.", "“아닙니다, 선생님."], ["He came among us as a stranger.", "그이는 낯선 사람으로 우리 사이에 나타났어요."], ["His father had died not long before in foreign parts.", "그이 부친이 얼마 전에 외국에서 세상을 떠난 참이었지요."], ["I remember he was in mourning.", "상복 차림이었던 게 기억납니다."], ["He put up at the little inn on the river (they have pulled it down since that time), where gentlemen used to go to fish.", "강가의 작은 여관에 묵었는데—그 여관은 그 뒤에 헐렸습니다—신사분들이 낚시하러 오면 묵던 곳이었지요."], ["He wasn’t much noticed when he first came—it was a common thing enough for gentlemen to travel from all parts of England to fish in our river.”", "처음 왔을 때는 별로 눈에 띄지도 않았습니다—잉글랜드 각지에서 신사들이 우리 강으로 낚시하러 오는 일은 흔했으니까요.”"]], [["“Did he make his appearance in the village before Anne was born?”", "“앤이 태어나기 전에 그가 마을에 나타났습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.", "“네, 선생님."], ["Anne was born in the June month of eighteen hundred and twenty-seven—and I think he came at the end of April or the beginning of May.”", "앤은 1827년 6월에 태어났고요—그이는 4월 말이나 5월 초에 왔던 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“Came as a stranger to all of you? A stranger to Mrs.", "“여러분 모두에게 낯선 사람으로 왔다는 말씀이지요?"], ["Catherick as well as to the rest of the neighbours?”", "다른 이웃들에게도 그랬듯이 캐서릭 부인에게도 낯선 사람이었나요?”"]], [["“So we thought at first, sir.", "“처음에는 우리도 그렇게 생각했지요, 선생님."], ["But when the scandal broke out, nobody believed they were strangers.", "그런데 추문이 터지고 나니 아무도 그 둘이 초면이었다고 믿지 않았습니다."], ["I remember how it happened as well as if it was yesterday.", "그 일이 어떻게 벌어졌는지 저는 어제 일처럼 또렷이 기억해요."], ["Catherick came into our garden one night, and woke us by throwing up a handful of gravel from the walk at our window.", "어느 날 밤 캐서릭이 우리 집 마당에 들어와서는 오솔길의 자갈을 한 줌 집어 창문에 던져 우리를 깨웠습니다."], ["I heard him beg my husband, for the Lord’s sake, to come down and speak to him.", "그이가 제 남편더러 제발 좀 내려와서 이야기를 들어 달라고 사정하는 소리가 들렸어요."], ["They were a long time together talking in the porch.", "둘이 현관에서 한참을 이야기했지요."], ["When my husband came back upstairs he was all of a tremble.", "남편이 이층으로 돌아왔을 때는 온몸을 떨고 있었습니다."], ["He sat down on the side of the bed and he says to me, ‘Lizzie!", "침대 가장자리에 앉더니 저에게 이러더군요. ‘리지!"], ["I always told you that woman was a bad one—I always said she would end ill, and I’m afraid in my own mind that the end has come already.", "내가 늘 그 여자는 못된 여자라고 하지 않았소—끝이 좋지 않을 거라고 늘 말했는데, 아무래도 그 끝이 벌써 온 것 같아 겁이 나오."], ["Catherick has found a lot of lace handkerchiefs, and two fine rings, and a new gold watch and chain, hid away in his wife’s drawer—things that nobody but a born lady ought ever to have—and his wife won’t say how she came by them.’ ‘Does he think she stole them?’ says I.", "캐서릭이 제 아내 서랍 속에 감춰 둔 레이스 손수건 여러 장하고 좋은 반지 두 개, 새 금시계와 줄까지 찾아냈다는군—태생이 귀한 부인네나 가질 법한 물건들인데, 아내는 그것들이 어디서 났는지 말하지를 않는다지.’ ‘그이는 아내가 훔쳤다고 생각하는 건가요?’ 제가 물었습니다."], ["‘No,’ says he, ‘stealing would be bad enough.", "‘아니오.’ 남편이 말했어요."], ["But it’s worse than that, she’s had no chance of stealing such things as those, and she’s not a woman to take them if she had.", "‘훔쳤다면 그것도 나쁜 일이지만, 이건 그보다 더 나쁘오. 그 여자가 그런 물건을 훔칠 기회도 없었거니와, 기회가 있었대도 훔칠 여자가 아니오."], ["They’re gifts, Lizzie—there’s her own initials engraved inside the watch—and Catherick has seen her talking privately, and carrying on as no married woman should, with that gentleman in mourning, Sir Percival Glyde.", "그건 선물이오, 리지—시계 안쪽에 그 여자 이름 첫 글자가 새겨져 있다는군—게다가 캐서릭은 아내가 그 상복 입은 신사, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경과 남몰래 이야기하고 유부녀답지 않게 굴어 대는 걸 보았다오."], ["Don’t you say anything about it—I’ve quieted Catherick for to-night.", "당신은 이 일을 입 밖에 내지 마오—오늘 밤은 내가 캐서릭을 달래 놓았소."], ["I’ve told him to keep his tongue to himself, and his eyes and his ears open, and to wait a day or two, till he can be quite certain.’ ‘I believe you are both of you wrong,’ says I.", "입은 다물고 눈과 귀는 열어 두고서, 확실해질 때까지 하루 이틀만 기다리라고 일러두었소.’ ‘저는 두 분 다 잘못 보신 것 같아요.’ 제가 말했습니다."], ["‘It’s not in nature, comfortable and respectable as she is here, that Mrs. Catherick should take up with a chance stranger like Sir Percival Glyde.’ ‘Ay, but is he a stranger to her?’ says my husband.", "‘여기서 저만큼 편안하고 남부럽지 않게 사는 캐서릭 부인이 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 같은 뜨내기 낯선 사람과 놀아난다는 건 사람 이치에 맞지 않아요.’ ‘그렇지, 하지만 그 사람이 그 여자한테도 낯선 사람일까?’ 남편이 말했지요."], ["‘You forget how Catherick’s wife came to marry him.", "‘캐서릭의 아내가 어쩌다 그이와 혼인하게 되었는지 잊었소."], ["She went to him of her own accord, after saying No over and over again when he asked her.", "청할 때는 몇 번이고 싫다더니 제 발로 그이를 찾아갔지 않소."], ["There have been wicked women before her time, Lizzie, who have used honest men who loved them as a means of saving their characters, and I’m sorely afraid this Mrs.", "리지, 세상에는 예로부터 자기 몸을 사랑해 준 정직한 사내를 제 이름을 지키는 방편으로 써먹은 못된 여자들이 있었소. 아무래도 이 캐서릭 부인도 그중에 가장 못된 여자만큼이나 못된 것 같아 몹시 걱정이오."], ["Catherick is as wicked as the worst of them.", "두고 보면 알겠지.’ 남편이 말했습니다."], ["We shall see,’ says my husband, ‘we shall soon see.’ And only two days afterwards we did see.”", "‘머잖아 알게 될 거요.’ 그리고 꼭 이틀 뒤에 우리는 알게 되었지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Clements waited for a moment before she went on.", "클레멘츠 부인은 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이야기를 이어 갔다."], ["Even in that moment, I began to doubt whether the clue that I thought I had found was really leading me to the central mystery of the labyrinth after all.", "그 잠깐 사이에도 나는 내가 찾았다고 여긴 실마리가 정말로 이 미로의 한복판에 있는 수수께끼로 나를 이끌어 가고 있는 것인지 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["Was this common, too common, story of a man’s treachery and a woman’s frailty the key to a secret which had been the lifelong terror of Sir Percival Glyde?", "남자의 배신과 여자의 부정이라는, 흔하디흔한 이 이야기가 과연 퍼시벌 글라이드 경을 평생토록 떨게 한 그 비밀의 열쇠일 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["“Well, sir, Catherick took my husband’s advice and waited,”", "“그래서요, 선생님, 캐서릭은 제 남편 말대로 기다렸습니다.”"], ["Mrs. Clements continued.", "클레멘츠 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["“And as I told you, he hadn’t long to wait.", "“그리고 말씀드렸다시피 오래 기다릴 것도 없었지요."], ["On the second day he found his wife and Sir Percival whispering together quite familiar, close under the vestry of the church.", "이틀째 되는 날 그이는 제 아내와 퍼시벌 경이 교회 성구실 바로 아래에서 아주 스스럼없이 소곤거리고 있는 걸 보았습니다."], ["I suppose they thought the neighbourhood of the vestry was the last place in the world where anybody would think of looking after them, but, however that may be, there they were.", "아마 두 사람은 성구실 근처야말로 세상에서 아무도 자기들을 찾을 생각을 않을 곳이라고 여겼겠지요. 하여간 어찌 되었든 두 사람은 거기 있었어요."], ["Sir Percival, being seemingly surprised and confounded, defended himself in such a guilty way that poor Catherick (whose quick temper I have told you of already) fell into a kind of frenzy at his own disgrace, and struck Sir Percival.", "퍼시벌 경은 놀라고 당황한 듯 보였는데, 어찌나 켕기는 태도로 변명을 하던지 가엾은 캐서릭은—성미가 급한 사람이라고 이미 말씀드렸지요—제 치욕에 미친 사람처럼 되어 퍼시벌 경을 때렸습니다."], ["He was no match (and I am sorry to say it) for the man who had wronged him, and he was beaten in the cruelest manner, before the neighbours, who had come to the place on hearing the disturbance, could run in to part them.", "자기를 욕보인 그 사내의 적수가 되지 못했지요, 안타깝게도요. 소란을 듣고 달려온 이웃들이 두 사람을 뜯어말리기도 전에 그이는 참혹하게 얻어맞았습니다."], ["All this happened towards evening, and before nightfall, when my husband went to Catherick’s house, he was gone, nobody knew where.", "이 모든 일이 저녁 무렵에 벌어졌는데, 날이 저물기도 전에 제 남편이 캐서릭의 집에 가 보니 그이는 이미 떠나고 없었어요. 아무도 어디로 갔는지 몰랐지요."], ["No living soul in the village ever saw him again.", "마을에서 그이를 다시 본 사람은 하나도 없습니다."], ["He knew too well, by that time, what his wife’s vile reason had been for marrying him, and he felt his misery and disgrace, especially after what had happened to him with Sir Percival, too keenly.", "그때쯤엔 아내가 자기와 혼인한 더러운 속셈이 무엇이었는지 너무나 잘 알게 되었고, 더구나 퍼시벌 경에게 그런 일까지 당하고 나니 제 비참함과 치욕을 견디기 어려웠던 거예요."], ["The clergyman of the parish put an advertisement in the paper begging him to come back, and saying that he should not lose his situation or his friends.", "교구 목사님이 신문에 광고를 내어 돌아오라고, 자리도 벗들도 잃지 않을 거라고 사정했습니다."], ["But Catherick had too much pride and spirit, as some people said—too much feeling, as I think, sir—to face his neighbours again, and try to live down the memory of his disgrace.", "그렇지만 캐서릭은 자존심과 기개가 너무 강해서—제가 보기엔 마음이 너무 여려서였지만요, 선생님—다시 이웃들 앞에 서서 제 치욕의 기억을 이겨 내며 살아갈 수가 없었던 겁니다."], ["My husband heard from him when he had left England, and heard a second time, when he was settled and doing well in America.", "제 남편은 그이가 잉글랜드를 떠난 뒤에 소식을 들었고, 그이가 아메리카에 자리 잡고 잘 지낸다는 소식을 한 번 더 들었어요."], ["He is alive there now, as far as I know, but none of us in the old country—his wicked wife least of all—are ever likely to set eyes on him again.”", "제가 아는 한 그이는 지금도 거기서 살아 있습니다만, 이 옛 고향의 누구도—무엇보다 그 못된 아내는—다시는 그이를 볼 일이 없을 겁니다.”"]], [["“What became of Sir Percival?”", "“퍼시벌 경은 어떻게 되었습니까?”"], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."], ["“Did he stay in the neighbourhood?”", "“그 동네에 그대로 머물렀나요?”"]], [["“Not he, sir.", "“천만에요, 선생님."], ["The place was too hot to hold him.", "그 자리에 붙어 있기엔 너무 뜨거웠지요."], ["He was heard at high words with Mrs. Catherick the same night when the scandal broke out, and the next morning he took himself off.”", "추문이 터진 바로 그날 밤 캐서릭 부인과 언성을 높여 다투는 소리가 들렸고, 이튿날 아침에 훌쩍 떠나 버렸습니다.”"]], [["“And Mrs. Catherick?", "“그러면 캐서릭 부인은요?"], ["Surely she never remained in the village among the people who knew of her disgrace?”", "설마 제 치욕을 다 아는 사람들 틈에 그대로 마을에 남지는 않았겠지요?”"]], [["“She did, sir.", "“남았습니다, 선생님."], ["She was hard enough and heartless enough to set the opinions of all her neighbours at flat defiance.", "이웃들의 눈총 따위는 대놓고 무시할 만큼 모질고 무정한 여자였으니까요."], ["She declared to everybody, from the clergyman downwards, that she was the victim of a dreadful mistake, and that all the scandal-mongers in the place should not drive her out of it, as if she was a guilty woman.", "그 여자는 목사님을 비롯해 만나는 사람마다 자기는 끔찍한 오해의 피해자라고, 이 동네의 그 어떤 입방아꾼도 자기를 죄지은 여자처럼 몰아내지는 못한다고 큰소리쳤습니다."], ["All through my time she lived at Old Welmingham, and after my time, when the new town was building, and the respectable neighbours began moving to it, she moved too, as if she was determined to live among them and scandalise them to the very last.", "제가 거기 사는 동안 내내 그 여자는 올드 웰밍엄에 살았고, 제가 떠난 뒤에도 새 읍이 세워지면서 점잖은 이웃들이 그리로 옮겨 가자 자기도 옮겨 갔습니다. 마치 끝까지 그 사람들 틈에 살면서 그들에게 눈꼴사나운 꼴을 보이겠다고 작정한 사람처럼요."], ["There she is now, and there she will stop, in defiance of the best of them, to her dying day.”", "지금도 거기 있고, 죽는 날까지 아무리 훌륭한 이들이 뭐라 해도 거기 눌러 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“But how has she lived through all these years?”", "“그런데 그 오랜 세월을 어떻게 살아왔을까요?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["“Was her husband able and willing to help her?”", "“남편이 도울 형편도 되고 도울 마음도 있었습니까?”"]], [["“Both able and willing, sir,”", "“형편도 되고 마음도 있었지요, 선생님.”"], ["said Mrs. Clements.", "클레멘츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“In the second letter he wrote to my good man, he said she had borne his name, and lived in his home, and, wicked as she was, she must not starve like a beggar in the street.", "“그이가 제 남편에게 보낸 두 번째 편지에 이렇게 썼습니다. 그래도 그 여자가 제 이름을 지녔고 제 집에서 살았으니, 아무리 못됐다 해도 길거리 거지처럼 굶겨서는 안 된다고요."], ["He could afford to make her some small allowance, and she might draw for it quarterly at a place in London.”", "얼마간 생활비를 대 줄 형편은 되니 런던의 어느 곳에서 석 달에 한 번씩 찾아가면 된다고 했지요.”"]], [["“Did she accept the allowance?”", "“그 여자가 그 생활비를 받았습니까?”"]], [["“Not a farthing of it, sir.", "“한 푼도 안 받았어요, 선생님."], ["She said she would never be beholden to Catherick for bit or drop, if she lived to be a hundred.", "백 살까지 산다 해도 캐서릭에게 한 입 한 모금도 신세 지지 않겠다고 했답니다."], ["And she has kept her word ever since.", "그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 말을 지켰지요."], ["When my poor dear husband died, and left all to me, Catherick’s letter was put in my possession with the other things, and I told her to let me know if she was ever in want.", "제 가엾은 남편이 죽으면서 모든 걸 저에게 남겼을 때 캐서릭의 편지도 다른 물건들과 함께 제 손에 들어왔고, 저는 그 여자에게 궁하거든 언제든 알리라고 했습니다."], ["‘I’ll let all England know I’m in want,’ she said, ‘before I tell Catherick, or any friend of Catherick’s.", "‘내가 캐서릭이나 캐서릭의 친구 나부랭이한테 궁하다고 말하느니,’ 그 여자가 말하더군요. ‘온 잉글랜드에 내가 궁하다고 알리겠소."], ["Take that for your answer, and give it to HIM for an answer, if he ever writes again.’”", "그걸 답으로 삼으시오. 그 사람이 또 편지하거든 그대로 답으로 전하시오.’”"]], [["“Do you suppose that she had money of her own?”", "“그 여자에게 제 재산이 있었으리라고 보십니까?”"]], [["“Very little, if any, sir.", "“있어야 아주 조금이었을 겁니다, 선생님."], ["It was said, and said truly, I am afraid, that her means of living came privately from Sir Percival Glyde.”", "그 여자의 생활비가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게서 몰래 나온다는 말이 돌았는데, 아무래도 그건 사실이었던 것 같아요.”"]], [["After that last reply I waited a little, to reconsider what I had heard.", "그 마지막 대답을 듣고 나서 나는 들은 이야기를 다시 헤아려 보려고 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["If I unreservedly accepted the story so far, it was now plain that no approach, direct or indirect, to the Secret had yet been revealed to me, and that the pursuit of my object had ended again in leaving me face to face with the most palpable and the most disheartening failure.", "여기까지의 이야기를 남김없이 그대로 받아들인다면, 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그 비밀에 다가서는 길은 아직 내게 하나도 드러나지 않았다는 것, 그리고 내 목적의 추구는 또다시 가장 명백하고 가장 낙담스러운 실패와 마주하는 것으로 끝났다는 것이 이제 분명했다."]], [["But there was one point in the narrative which made me doubt the propriety of accepting it unreservedly, and which suggested the idea of something hidden below the surface.", "그러나 그 이야기에는 그것을 남김없이 그대로 받아들여도 좋을지 의심하게 만드는 지점이 하나 있었고, 그 지점은 표면 아래 무언가 감추어져 있다는 생각을 불러일으켰다."]], [["I could not account to myself for the circumstance of the clerk’s guilty wife voluntarily living out all her after-existence on the scene of her disgrace.", "교회 서기의 죄지은 아내가 남은 평생을 제 치욕의 무대에서 자진해 살아간다는 사정을 나는 스스로 납득할 수가 없었다."], ["The woman’s own reported statement that she had taken this strange course as a practical assertion of her innocence did not satisfy me.", "자신의 결백을 몸으로 주장하기 위해 그 기이한 길을 택했다는 그 여자 자신의 말은 나를 만족시키지 못했다."], ["It seemed, to my mind, more natural and more probable to assume that she was not so completely a free agent in this matter as she had herself asserted.", "그보다는 그 여자가 이 일에서 스스로 주장하는 것만큼 자유로운 처지가 아니었다고 보는 편이 더 자연스럽고 더 그럴듯해 보였다."], ["In that case, who was the likeliest person to possess the power of compelling her to remain at Welmingham?", "그렇다면 그 여자를 웰밍엄에 붙들어 둘 힘을 지녔을 사람은 누구겠는가?"], ["The person unquestionably from whom she derived the means of living.", "그 여자에게 살아갈 방편을 대 주는 바로 그 사람일 수밖에 없었다."], ["She had refused assistance from her husband, she had no adequate resources of her own, she was a friendless, degraded woman—from what source should she derive help but from the source at which report pointed—Sir Percival Glyde?", "그 여자는 남편의 도움을 거절했고, 제 힘으로 살 만한 재산도 없었으며, 벗도 없이 몰락한 여자였다—소문이 가리키는 그 출처, 곧 퍼시벌 글라이드 경 말고 어디서 도움을 받을 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Reasoning on these assumptions, and always bearing in mind the one certain fact to guide me, that Mrs.", "이런 가정 위에서 따져 보되, 나를 이끌어 줄 단 하나의 확실한 사실—캐서릭 부인이 그 비밀을 쥐고 있다는 사실—을 늘 염두에 두자, 나는 그 여자를 웰밍엄에 붙들어 두는 것이 퍼시벌 경에게 이롭다는 것을 어렵지 않게 이해할 수 있었다."], ["Catherick was in possession of the Secret, I easily understood that it was Sir Percival’s interest to keep her at Welmingham, because her character in that place was certain to isolate her from all communication with female neighbours, and to allow her no opportunities of talking incautiously in moments of free intercourse with inquisitive bosom friends. But what was the mystery to be concealed?", "그곳에서의 그 여자의 평판은 이웃 여자들과의 왕래를 반드시 끊어 놓을 것이고, 캐묻기 좋아하는 벗과 허물없이 어울리다 무심코 입을 놀릴 기회를 아예 주지 않을 터였기 때문이다."], ["Not Sir Percival’s infamous connection with Mrs.", "그런데 감추어야 할 그 수수께끼란 대체 무엇인가?"], ["Catherick’s disgrace, for the neighbours were the very people who knew of it—not the suspicion that he was Anne’s father, for Welmingham was the place in which that suspicion must inevitably exist.", "캐서릭 부인의 몰락에 퍼시벌 경이 부끄럽게 얽혀 있다는 것은 아니었다. 이웃 사람들이야말로 그것을 다 아는 이들이었으니까."], ["If I accepted the guilty appearances described to me as unreservedly as others had accepted them, if I drew from them the same superficial conclusion which Mr.", "그가 앤의 아버지라는 의심도 아니었다. 웰밍엄이야말로 그 의심이 필연적으로 존재할 수밖에 없는 곳이었으니까."], ["Catherick and all his neighbours had drawn, where was the suggestion, in all that I had heard, of a dangerous secret between Sir Percival and Mrs. Catherick, which had been kept hidden from that time to this?", "내가 전해 들은 그 죄스러운 정황들을 남들이 받아들인 것처럼 나도 남김없이 받아들이고, 거기서 캐서릭 씨와 그 이웃들이 모두 끌어낸 것과 똑같은 피상적인 결론을 끌어낸다면, 내가 들은 그 모든 이야기 가운데 어디에 퍼시벌 경과 캐서릭 부인 사이에 그때부터 지금까지 감춰져 온 위험한 비밀이 있다는 암시가 있단 말인가?"]], [["And yet, in those stolen meetings, in those familiar whisperings between the clerk’s wife and “the gentleman in mourning,”", "그런데도 그 몰래 만나던 자리, 서기의 아내와 “상복 입은 신사”"], ["the clue to discovery existed beyond a doubt.", "사이의 그 허물없는 속삭임 속에 발견의 실마리가 있다는 것만은 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["Was it possible that appearances in this case had pointed one way while the truth lay all the while unsuspected in another direction? Could Mrs.", "이 경우에 겉으로 드러난 정황은 한쪽을 가리키는데 진실은 내내 아무도 의심하지 않는 다른 쪽에 놓여 있었을 가능성은 없을까?"], ["Catherick’s assertion, that she was the victim of a dreadful mistake, by any possibility be true?", "자신이 끔찍한 오해의 피해자라는 캐서릭 부인의 주장이 어쩌면 사실일 수는 없을까?"], ["Or, assuming it to be false, could the conclusion which associated Sir Percival with her guilt have been founded in some inconceivable error?", "혹은 그것이 거짓이라 치더라도, 퍼시벌 경을 그 여자의 죄와 결부시킨 그 결론이 무언가 상상도 못 할 착오 위에 세워진 것일 수는 없을까?"], ["Had Sir Percival, by any chance, courted the suspicion that was wrong for the sake of diverting from himself some other suspicion that was right?", "퍼시벌 경이 혹시라도 옳은 다른 의심을 자기에게서 돌려놓기 위해 일부러 그 그릇된 의심을 자초한 것은 아닐까?"], ["Here—if I could find it—here was the approach to the Secret, hidden deep under the surface of the apparently unpromising story which I had just heard.", "여기에—내가 찾아낼 수만 있다면—여기에, 방금 들은 별 볼 일 없어 보이는 이야기의 표면 밑 깊숙한 곳에 그 비밀로 다가서는 길이 있었다."]], [["My next questions were now directed to the one object of ascertaining whether Mr. Catherick had or had not arrived truly at the conviction of his wife’s misconduct.", "이제 내 다음 질문들은 캐서릭 씨가 제 아내의 부정을 진실로 확신하기에 이르렀는가 아닌가를 확인하는 한 가지 목적을 향했다."], ["The answers I received from Mrs. Clements left me in no doubt whatever on that point.", "클레멘츠 부인에게서 받은 대답들은 그 점에 관해 내게 한 점의 의심도 남기지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Catherick had, on the clearest evidence, compromised her reputation, while a single woman, with some person unknown, and had married to save her character.", "캐서릭 부인은 더없이 분명한 증거로 미루어, 처녀 시절에 이름 모를 누군가와의 일로 제 평판을 더럽혔고, 제 체면을 지키려고 혼인한 것이었다."], ["It had been positively ascertained, by calculations of time and place into which I need not enter particularly, that the daughter who bore her husband’s name was not her husband’s child.", "굳이 자세히 들어갈 필요 없는 시간과 장소의 계산을 통해, 그 남편의 성을 지닌 딸이 그 남편의 자식이 아니라는 것은 확실하게 밝혀져 있었다."]], [["The next object of inquiry, whether it was equally certain that Sir Percival must have been the father of Anne, was beset by far greater difficulties.", "다음 탐구의 대상, 곧 퍼시벌 경이 앤의 아버지임이 그와 똑같이 확실한가 하는 문제에는 훨씬 큰 어려움이 따랐다."], ["I was in no position to try the probabilities on one side or on the other in this instance by any better test than the test of personal resemblance.", "이 경우 나는 어느 쪽 개연성을 따져 보든 용모의 닮음이라는 시금석보다 나은 시금석을 쓸 처지가 못 되었다."]], [["“I suppose you often saw Sir Percival when he was in your village?”", "“퍼시벌 경이 마을에 있는 동안 자주 보셨겠지요?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir, very often,”", "“네, 선생님, 아주 자주 보았지요.”"], ["replied Mrs. Clements.", "클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다."]], [["“Did you ever observe that Anne was like him?”", "“앤이 그를 닮았다고 느끼신 적이 있습니까?”"]], [["“She was not at all like him, sir.”", "“그 아이는 그이를 조금도 닮지 않았습니다, 선생님.”"]], [["“Was she like her mother, then?”", "“그럼 어머니는 닮았습니까?”"]], [["“Not like her mother either, sir.", "“어머니도 닮지 않았어요, 선생님."], ["Mrs. Catherick was dark, and full in the face.”", "캐서릭 부인은 살빛이 검고 얼굴이 통통했지요.”"]], [["Not like her mother and not like her (supposed) father.", "어머니도 닮지 않고, 아버지라고들 하는 사람도 닮지 않았다."], ["I knew that the test by personal resemblance was not to be implicitly trusted, but, on the other hand, it was not to be altogether rejected on that account.", "용모의 닮음이라는 시금석을 곧이곧대로 믿을 수 없다는 것은 나도 알고 있었지만, 그렇다고 그 때문에 그것을 통째로 내버릴 것도 아니었다."], ["Was it possible to strengthen the evidence by discovering any conclusive facts in relation to the lives of Mrs. Catherick and Sir Percival before they either of them appeared at Old Welmingham?", "캐서릭 부인과 퍼시벌 경이 올드 웰밍엄에 나타나기 전의 삶에 관하여 결정적인 사실을 찾아냄으로써 이 증거를 보강할 수는 없을까?"], ["When I asked my next questions I put them with this view.", "다음 질문들은 그런 생각을 가지고 던진 것이었다."]], [["“When Sir Percival first arrived in your neighbourhood,”", "“퍼시벌 경이 처음 그 동네에 왔을 때,” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “did you hear where he had come from last?”", "“그가 직전에 어디에서 왔는지 들으셨습니까?”"]], [["“No, sir.", "“아니요, 선생님."], ["Some said from Blackwater Park, and some said from Scotland—but nobody knew.”", "어떤 이들은 블랙워터 파크에서 왔다 하고 어떤 이들은 스코틀랜드에서 왔다고 했지만—아무도 몰랐습니다.”"]], [["“Was Mrs. Catherick living in service at Varneck Hall immediately before her marriage?”", "“캐서릭 부인은 혼인하기 바로 전까지 바넥 홀에서 일하고 있었습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "“네, 선생님.”"]], [["“And had she been long in her place?”", "“그 자리에 오래 있었습니까?”"]], [["“Three or four years, sir; I am not quite certain which.”", "“서너 해쯤요, 선생님. 몇 해인지는 확실치 않습니다.”"]], [["“Did you ever hear the name of the gentleman to whom Varneck Hall belonged at that time?”", "“그 무렵 바넥 홀의 주인이 누구였는지 이름을 들으신 적 있습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.", "“네, 선생님."], ["His name was Major Donthorne.”", "돈손 소령이라는 분이었습니다.”"]], [["“Did Mr. Catherick, or did any one else you knew, ever hear that Sir Percival was a friend of Major Donthorne’s, or ever see Sir Percival in the neighbourhood of Varneck Hall?”", "“캐서릭 씨나 부인이 아시는 다른 누구라도, 퍼시벌 경이 돈손 소령의 친구라는 말을 들었거나 퍼시벌 경이 바넥 홀 근처에 있는 것을 본 적이 있습니까?”"]], [["“Catherick never did, sir, that I can remember—nor any one else either, that I know of.”", "“캐서릭은 그런 적 없었습니다, 선생님, 제가 기억하는 한은요—제가 아는 한 다른 누구도 없었고요.”"]], [["I noted down Major Donthorne’s name and address, on the chance that he might still be alive, and that it might be useful at some future time to apply to him.", "나는 돈손 소령이 아직 살아 있을지도 모르고 훗날 그에게 문의하는 것이 쓸모 있을지도 모른다는 생각에서 그의 이름과 주소를 적어 두었다."], ["Meanwhile, the impression on my mind was now decidedly adverse to the opinion that Sir Percival was Anne’s father, and decidedly favourable to the conclusion that the secret of his stolen interviews with Mrs. Catherick was entirely unconnected with the disgrace which the woman had inflicted on her husband’s good name.", "한편 내 마음에 남은 인상은 이제 퍼시벌 경이 앤의 아버지라는 견해에는 확실히 부정적인 쪽으로, 그리고 그가 캐서릭 부인과 몰래 만난 그 비밀은 그 여자가 제 남편의 명예에 안긴 치욕과는 전혀 무관하다는 결론에는 확실히 우호적인 쪽으로 기울어 있었다."], ["I could think of no further inquiries which I might make to strengthen this impression—I could only encourage Mrs. Clements to speak next of Anne’s early days, and watch for any chance-suggestion which might in this way offer itself to me.", "이 인상을 굳혀 줄 만한 추가 질문은 더 떠오르지 않았다—나로서는 클레멘츠 부인이 이제 앤의 어린 시절을 이야기하도록 이끌면서, 그런 식으로 우연히 떠오를지 모를 어떤 실마리를 지켜보는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“I have not heard yet,”", "“아직 못 들은 이야기가 있습니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “how the poor child, born in all this sin and misery, came to be trusted, Mrs. Clements, to your care.”", "“그 죄와 비참함 속에 태어난 가엾은 아이가 어떻게 부인의 손에 맡겨지게 되었는지 말입니다, 클레멘츠 부인.”"]], [["“There was nobody else, sir, to take the little helpless creature in hand,”", "“그 가엾고 힘없는 것을 거두어 줄 사람이 달리 아무도 없었거든요, 선생님.”"], ["replied Mrs. Clements.", "클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다."], ["“The wicked mother seemed to hate it—as if the poor baby was in fault!—from the day it was born.", "“그 못된 어미는 태어난 날부터 그 아이를 미워하는 것 같았어요—가엾은 갓난것이 무슨 잘못이라도 한 것처럼요!"], ["My heart was heavy for the child, and I made the offer to bring it up as tenderly as if it was my own.”", "저는 그 아이가 안쓰러워 견딜 수가 없어서 제 자식처럼 애지중지 키우겠다고 나섰지요.”"]], [["“Did Anne remain entirely under your care from that time?”", "“앤은 그때부터 줄곧 부인의 손에서 자랐습니까?”"]], [["“Not quite entirely, sir. Mrs.", "“아주 줄곧은 아니었어요, 선생님."], ["Catherick had her whims and fancies about it at times, and used now and then to lay claim to the child, as if she wanted to spite me for bringing it up.", "캐서릭 부인은 이따금 변덕이 나서, 제가 키우는 게 심술이 났는지 가끔씩 아이를 제 것이라며 데려가곤 했습니다."], ["But these fits of hers never lasted for long.", "그렇지만 그런 발작은 오래가는 법이 없었지요."], ["Poor little Anne was always returned to me, and was always glad to get back—though she led but a gloomy life in my house, having no playmates, like other children, to brighten her up.", "가엾은 어린 앤은 늘 저에게 돌아왔고, 늘 돌아오는 걸 기뻐했습니다—다른 아이들처럼 같이 놀 동무도 없이 우리 집에서 쓸쓸하게 지냈는데도 말이에요."], ["Our longest separation was when her mother took her to Limmeridge.", "가장 오래 떨어져 있었던 건 그 어미가 아이를 리머리지로 데려갔을 때였습니다."], ["Just at that time I lost my husband, and I felt it was as well, in that miserable affliction, that Anne should not be in the house.", "마침 그때 제가 남편을 잃었는데, 그 참담한 슬픔 가운데 앤이 집에 없었던 게 차라리 다행이다 싶었지요."], ["She was between ten and eleven years old then, slow at her lessons, poor soul, and not so cheerful as other children—but as pretty a little girl to look at as you would wish to see.", "그때 그 아이는 열 살에서 열한 살 사이였는데, 딱하게도 배우는 게 더디고 다른 아이들처럼 밝지도 않았지만—보기에는 더할 나위 없이 어여쁜 계집아이였습니다."], ["I waited at home till her mother brought her back, and then I made the offer to take her with me to London—the truth being, sir, that I could not find it in my heart to stop at Old Welmingham after my husband’s death, the place was so changed and so dismal to me.”", "저는 그 어미가 아이를 데려올 때까지 집에서 기다렸다가, 그 아이를 런던으로 데려가겠다고 청했어요—사실은요, 선생님, 남편이 죽은 뒤로는 도무지 올드 웰밍엄에 머무를 마음이 나지 않았습니다. 그곳이 너무 달라지고 너무 쓸쓸해져서요.”"]], [["“And did Mrs. Catherick consent to your proposal?”", "“캐서릭 부인이 그 제안을 받아들였습니까?”"]], [["“No, sir.", "“아니요, 선생님."], ["She came back from the north harder and bitterer than ever.", "그 여자는 북쪽에서 그 어느 때보다 모질고 독해져서 돌아왔습니다."], ["Folks did say that she had been obliged to ask Sir Percival’s leave to go, to begin with; and that she only went to nurse her dying sister at Limmeridge because the poor woman was reported to have saved money—the truth being that she hardly left enough to bury her.", "사람들 말로는 애초에 떠나려면 퍼시벌 경의 허락부터 받아야 했다고 하더군요. 그리고 리머리지로 죽어 가는 제 언니를 간호하러 간 것도 그 가엾은 이가 돈을 모아 두었다는 소문 때문이었는데—실은 장례 치를 만큼도 남기지 못했다지요."], ["These things may have soured Mrs. Catherick likely enough, but however that may be, she wouldn’t hear of my taking the child away.", "그런 일들이 캐서릭 부인의 심사를 뒤틀어 놓았을 법도 합니다만, 어찌 되었든 그 여자는 제가 아이를 데려가겠다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았습니다."], ["She seemed to like distressing us both by parting us.", "우리 둘을 갈라놓아 둘 다 괴롭히는 걸 즐기는 것 같았어요."], ["All I could do was to give Anne my direction, and to tell her privately, if she was ever in trouble, to come to me.", "제가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 앤에게 제 주소를 일러 주고, 혹시라도 어려운 일이 생기면 저를 찾아오라고 몰래 말해 두는 것뿐이었지요."], ["But years passed before she was free to come.", "그렇지만 그 아이가 자유로이 올 수 있게 되기까지는 몇 해가 걸렸습니다."], ["I never saw her again, poor soul, till the night she escaped from the mad-house.”", "그 아이가 정신병원에서 도망쳐 나온 그날 밤까지, 가엾은 것을 저는 다시 보지 못했어요.”"]], [["“You know, Mrs. Clements, why Sir Percival Glyde shut her up?”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 왜 그 아이를 가두었는지 아십니까, 클레멘츠 부인?”"]], [["“I only know what Anne herself told me, sir.", "“저는 앤이 저에게 해 준 말밖에는 모릅니다, 선생님."], ["The poor thing used to ramble and wander about it sadly.", "그 가엾은 것은 그 이야기가 나오면 딱하게도 횡설수설 종잡을 수 없게 되곤 했어요."], ["She said her mother had got some secret of Sir Percival’s to keep, and had let it out to her long after I left Hampshire—and when Sir Percival found she knew it, he shut her up.", "제 어미가 퍼시벌 경의 무슨 비밀을 지키고 있었는데, 제가 햄프셔를 떠난 지 한참 뒤에 그걸 그 아이에게 흘렸다고 하더군요—그리고 퍼시벌 경이 그 아이가 그걸 안다는 걸 알고서 가두었다고요."], ["But she never could say what it was when I asked her.", "하지만 제가 물어도 그게 무엇인지는 끝내 말하지 못했습니다."], ["All she could tell me was, that her mother might be the ruin and destruction of Sir Percival if she chose. Mrs.", "그 아이가 해 줄 수 있는 말이라고는, 제 어미가 마음만 먹으면 퍼시벌 경을 파멸시켜 망하게 할 수 있다는 것뿐이었어요."], ["Catherick may have let out just as much as that, and no more.", "캐서릭 부인이 흘린 것도 딱 그만큼이지 그 이상은 아니었을 겁니다."], ["I’m next to certain I should have heard the whole truth from Anne, if she had really known it as she pretended to do, and as she very likely fancied she did, poor soul.”", "앤이 정말로 제가 안다고 내세운 만큼 알고 있었다면—또 가엾게도 스스로 안다고 여겼던 만큼 알고 있었다면—저에게 사실을 다 털어놓았을 게 거의 틀림없거든요.”"]], [["This idea had more than once occurred to my own mind.", "이런 생각은 내 마음에도 한 번 이상 떠올랐던 것이었다."], ["I had already told Marian that I doubted whether Laura was really on the point of making any important discovery when she and Anne Catherick were disturbed by Count Fosco at the boat-house.", "로라와 앤 캐서릭이 배 창고에서 포스코 백작에게 방해받았을 때 로라가 정말로 무슨 중대한 발견을 앞두고 있었는지 의심스럽다고 나는 이미 매리언에게 말한 바 있었다."], ["It was perfectly in character with Anne’s mental affliction that she should assume an absolute knowledge of the secret on no better grounds than vague suspicion, derived from hints which her mother had incautiously let drop in her presence.", "어머니가 제 앞에서 무심코 흘린 암시에서 나온 막연한 의심 말고는 아무 근거도 없이 그 비밀을 온전히 안다고 여기는 것은 앤의 정신 질환에 꼭 들어맞는 일이었다."], ["Sir Percival’s guilty distrust would, in that case, infallibly inspire him with the false idea that Anne knew all from her mother, just as it had afterwards fixed in his mind the equally false suspicion that his wife knew all from Anne.", "그렇다면 퍼시벌 경의 켕기는 의심은 앤이 제 어미에게서 다 들어 알고 있다는 그릇된 생각을 그에게 어김없이 불어넣었을 것이고, 그것은 나중에 제 아내가 앤에게서 다 들어 알고 있다는 똑같이 그릇된 의심을 그의 마음에 박아 넣은 것과 다르지 않았다."]], [["The time was passing, the morning was wearing away.", "시간은 흘러 아침나절이 저물어 가고 있었다."], ["It was doubtful, if I stayed longer, whether I should hear anything more from Mrs. Clements that would be at all useful to my purpose.", "더 머문다 한들 클레멘츠 부인에게서 내 목적에 조금이라도 쓸모 있는 이야기를 더 들을 수 있을지는 의심스러웠다."], ["I had already discovered those local and family particulars, in relation to Mrs. Catherick, of which I had been in search, and I had arrived at certain conclusions, entirely new to me, which might immensely assist in directing the course of my future proceedings.", "나는 캐서릭 부인에 관하여 찾고 있던 그 고장과 집안의 자세한 사정들을 이미 알아냈고, 앞으로의 행보를 정하는 데 대단히 큰 도움이 될 만한, 나로서는 전혀 새로운 몇 가지 결론에 이르러 있었다."], ["I rose to take my leave, and to thank Mrs. Clements for the friendly readiness she had shown in affording me information.", "나는 작별을 고하고, 기꺼이 정보를 나누어 준 클레멘츠 부인의 다정한 호의에 감사를 표하려고 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“I am afraid you must have thought me very inquisitive,” I said.", "“제가 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는다고 여기셨을 것 같아 송구합니다.”"], ["“I have troubled you with more questions than many people would have cared to answer.”", "내가 말했다. “웬만한 사람이라면 대답하고 싶지 않았을 만큼 많은 질문으로 폐를 끼쳤습니다.”"]], [["“You are heartily welcome, sir, to anything I can tell you,”", "“제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 드리겠습니다, 선생님.”"], ["answered Mrs. Clements.", "클레멘츠 부인이 대답했다."], ["She stopped and looked at me wistfully.", "부인은 말을 멈추고 애타는 눈으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["“But I do wish,”", "“하지만 저는요.”"], ["said the poor woman, “you could have told me a little more about Anne, sir.", "그 가엾은 부인이 말했다. “선생님께서 앤에 대해 조금만 더 말씀해 주셨으면 싶습니다."], ["I thought I saw something in your face when you came in which looked as if you could.", "들어오실 때 선생님 얼굴에서 무언가 말씀해 주실 수 있을 것 같은 기색을 본 듯했거든요."], ["You can’t think how hard it is not even to know whether she is living or dead.", "그 아이가 살았는지 죽었는지조차 모른다는 게 얼마나 견디기 힘든지 선생님은 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["I could bear it better if I was only certain.", "확실하기만 하다면 차라리 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 텐데요."], ["You said you never expected we should see her alive again.", "선생님은 그 아이를 다시 살아서 보리라고는 기대하지 않는다고 하셨지요."], ["Do you know, sir—do you know for truth—that it has pleased God to take her?”", "아십니까, 선생님—정말로 아십니까—하느님께서 그 아이를 데려가셨는지요?”"]], [["I was not proof against this appeal; it would have been unspeakably mean and cruel of me if I had resisted it.", "나는 이 호소를 견뎌 낼 수 없었다. 이것을 뿌리쳤다면 그것은 말할 수 없이 비열하고 잔인한 짓이었을 것이다."]], [["“I am afraid there is no doubt of the truth,”", "“안타깝지만 사실인 것을 의심할 여지가 없습니다.”"], ["I answered gently; “I have the certainty in my own mind that her troubles in this world are over.”", "나는 부드럽게 대답했다. “그 아이가 이 세상에서 겪던 고통이 끝났다는 것을 저는 마음속으로 확신합니다.”"]], [["The poor woman dropped into her chair and hid her face from me.", "가엾은 부인은 의자에 털썩 주저앉아 내게서 얼굴을 감추었다. “아이고, 선생님.”"], ["“Oh, sir,”", "부인이 말했다."], ["she said, “how do you know it?", "“그걸 어떻게 아십니까?"], ["Who can have told you?”", "누가 그런 말을 했단 말입니까?”"]], [["“No one has told me, Mrs. Clements.", "“누가 말해 준 것이 아닙니다, 클레멘츠 부인."], ["But I have reasons for feeling sure of it—reasons which I promise you shall know as soon as I can safely explain them.", "그러나 제게는 그것을 확신할 만한 근거가 있습니다—안전하게 설명할 수 있게 되는 대로 반드시 알려 드리겠다고 약속하는 그런 근거 말입니다."], ["I am certain she was not neglected in her last moments—I am certain the heart complaint from which she suffered so sadly was the true cause of her death.", "저는 그 아이가 마지막 순간에 돌봄을 받지 못한 것이 아니었음을 확신합니다—그토록 딱하게 앓던 심장병이 그 아이의 참된 사인이었다는 것도 확신합니다."], ["You shall feel as sure of this as I do, soon—you shall know, before long, that she is buried in a quiet country churchyard—in a pretty, peaceful place, which you might have chosen for her yourself.”", "머잖아 부인께서도 저만큼 이것을 확신하시게 될 겁니다—그 아이가 조용한 시골 교회 묘지에, 부인께서 손수 골라 주셨을 법한 어여쁘고 평화로운 자리에 묻혀 있다는 것도 오래지 않아 아시게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Dead!” said Mrs.", "“죽었다니!”"], ["Clements, “dead so young, and I am left to hear it!", "클레멘츠 부인이 말했다. “그 어린 나이에 죽고, 나는 남아서 그 소식을 듣는구나!"], ["I made her first short frocks.", "내가 그 아이 첫 배내옷을 지었는데."], ["I taught her to walk.", "내가 걸음마를 가르쳤는데."], ["The first time she ever said Mother she said it to me—and now I am left and Anne is taken! Did you say, sir,”", "그 아이가 난생처음 엄마라고 부른 것도 나한테였는데—그런데 이제 나는 남고 앤은 가 버렸다니!”"], ["said the poor woman, removing the handkerchief from her face, and looking up at me for the first time, “did you say that she had been nicely buried?", "가엾은 부인은 얼굴에서 손수건을 떼고 처음으로 나를 올려다보며 말했다. “선생님, 방금 그 아이가 곱게 묻혔다고 하셨습니까?"], ["Was it the sort of funeral she might have had if she had really been my own child?”", "정말로 제 친자식이었다면 치러 주었을 그런 장례였나요?”"]], [["I assured her that it was.", "나는 그렇다고 부인을 안심시켰다."], ["She seemed to take an inexplicable pride in my answer—to find a comfort in it which no other and higher considerations could afford.", "부인은 내 대답에 알 수 없는 자부심을 느끼는 듯했고—더 높고 다른 어떤 위로도 줄 수 없는 위안을 거기서 찾는 듯했다. “앤이 곱게 묻히지 못했다면 제 가슴이 미어졌을 겁니다.”"], ["“It would have broken my heart,”", "부인이 담담하게 말했다."], ["she said simply, “if Anne had not been nicely buried—but how do you know it, sir?", "“그런데 선생님은 그걸 어떻게 아십니까? 누가 말해 주었나요?”"], ["who told you?” I once more entreated her to wait until I could speak to her unreservedly.", "나는 다시 한번 모든 것을 숨김없이 말할 수 있을 때까지 기다려 달라고 간청했다."], ["“You are sure to see me again,”", "“저를 다시 보시게 될 겁니다.”"], ["I said, “for I have a favour to ask when you are a little more composed—perhaps in a day or two.”", "내가 말했다. “마음이 조금 가라앉으시거든—아마 하루 이틀 안에—청할 부탁이 있으니까요.”"]], [["“Don’t keep it waiting, sir, on my account,”", "“저 때문에 미루지 마십시오, 선생님.”"], ["said Mrs. Clements.", "클레멘츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“Never mind my crying if I can be of use.", "“제가 쓸모가 될 수 있다면 제가 우는 건 아랑곳 마세요."], ["If you have anything on your mind to say to me, sir, please to say it now.”", "저에게 하실 말씀이 마음에 있으시거든, 선생님, 지금 해 주십시오.”"]], [["“I only wish to ask you one last question,” I said.", "“마지막으로 한 가지만 여쭙고자 합니다.”"], ["“I only want to know Mrs.", "내가 말했다."], ["Catherick’s address at Welmingham.”", "“웰밍엄의 캐서릭 부인 주소만 알고 싶습니다.”"]], [["My request so startled Mrs. Clements, that, for the moment, even the tidings of Anne’s death seemed to be driven from her mind.", "내 청에 클레멘츠 부인은 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 순간만은 앤의 죽음이라는 소식조차 머릿속에서 밀려난 듯했다."], ["Her tears suddenly ceased to flow, and she sat looking at me in blank amazement.", "흐르던 눈물이 갑자기 멎었고, 부인은 어리둥절한 얼굴로 나를 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["“For the Lord’s sake, sir!”", "“맙소사, 선생님!” 부인이 말했다."], ["she said, “what do you want with Mrs. Catherick!”", "“캐서릭 부인에게 무슨 볼일이 있으시다고요!”"]], [["“I want this, Mrs. Clements,”", "“이런 볼일입니다, 클레멘츠 부인.”"], ["I replied, “I want to know the secret of those private meetings of hers with Sir Percival Glyde.", "내가 대답했다. “저는 그 여자와 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 몰래 만나던 그 일의 비밀을 알고 싶습니다."], ["There is something more in what you have told me of that woman’s past conduct, and of that man’s past relations with her, than you or any of your neighbours ever suspected.", "부인께서 들려주신 그 여자의 지난 행실과 그 남자가 그 여자와 맺었던 지난 관계에는, 부인도 이웃 누구도 짐작조차 못 한 무언가가 더 있습니다."], ["There is a secret we none of us know between those two, and I am going to Mrs. Catherick with the resolution to find it out.”", "그 둘 사이에는 우리 중 누구도 모르는 비밀이 있고, 저는 그것을 기어이 밝혀내겠다는 결심으로 캐서릭 부인을 찾아가려는 겁니다.”"]], [["“Think twice about it, sir!” said Mrs.", "“다시 한번 생각해 보십시오, 선생님!”"], ["Clements, rising in her earnestness and laying her hand on my arm.", "클레멘츠 부인이 간절한 마음에 자리에서 일어나 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["“She’s an awful woman—you don’t know her as I do.", "“그 여자는 무서운 여자예요—선생님은 제가 아는 만큼 그 여자를 모르십니다."], ["Think twice about it.”", "다시 한번 생각해 보세요.”"]], [["“I am sure your warning is kindly meant, Mrs. Clements.", "“부인의 경고가 호의에서 나온 것임을 잘 압니다, 클레멘츠 부인."], ["But I am determined to see the woman, whatever comes of it.”", "그러나 무슨 일이 있더라도 저는 그 여자를 만나기로 마음먹었습니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Clements looked me anxiously in the face.", "클레멘츠 부인은 근심스러운 눈으로 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“I see your mind is made up, sir,”", "“마음을 굳히셨군요, 선생님.”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“I will give you the address.”", "“주소를 알려 드리겠습니다.”"]], [["I wrote it down in my pocket-book and then took her hand to say farewell.", "나는 그것을 수첩에 적고 나서 작별 인사를 하려고 부인의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“You shall hear from me soon,”", "“곧 소식 전해 드리겠습니다.”"], ["I said; “you shall know all that I have promised to tell you.”", "내가 말했다. “말씀드리기로 약속한 것을 모두 아시게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["Mrs. Clements sighed and shook her head doubtfully.", "클레멘츠 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 미덥지 않다는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["“An old woman’s advice is sometimes worth taking, sir,”", "“늙은이의 충고가 때로는 새겨들을 만한 법입니다, 선생님.”"], ["she said.", "부인이 말했다."], ["“Think twice before you go to Welmingham.”", "“웰밍엄에 가시기 전에 다시 한번 생각해 보세요.”"]], [["# VIII", "# VIII"]], [["When I reached home again after my interview with Mrs. Clements, I was struck by the appearance of a change in Laura.", "클레멘츠 부인과의 면담을 마치고 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때, 나는 로라에게 어떤 변화가 나타난 것을 보고 가슴이 철렁했다."]], [["The unvarying gentleness and patience which long misfortune had tried so cruelly and had never conquered yet, seemed now to have suddenly failed her.", "오랜 불행이 그토록 모질게 시험하고도 끝내 꺾지 못했던 그 한결같은 온화함과 인내가, 이제 갑자기 그녀를 저버린 듯했다."], ["Insensible to all Marian’s attempts to soothe and amuse her, she sat, with her neglected drawing pushed away on the table, her eyes resolutely cast down, her fingers twining and untwining themselves restlessly in her lap.", "달래고 기분을 풀어 주려는 매리언의 온갖 노력에도 아랑곳없이, 그녀는 손도 대지 않은 그림을 탁자 저편으로 밀어 둔 채, 눈을 완강히 내리깔고, 무릎 위에서 손가락을 쉼 없이 얽었다 풀었다 하며 앉아 있었다."], ["Marian rose when I came in, with a silent distress in her face, waited for a moment to see if Laura would look up at my approach, whispered to me, “Try if you can rouse her,”", "내가 들어서자 매리언은 얼굴에 말 없는 근심을 띠고 일어섰고, 내가 다가가면 로라가 고개를 들까 싶어 잠시 지켜보더니, 나에게 “당신이 기운을 북돋아 줄 수 있는지 해 봐요”"], ["and left the room.", "하고 속삭이고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["I sat down in the vacant chair—gently unclasped the poor, worn, restless fingers, and took both her hands in mine.", "나는 비어 있는 의자에 앉아—그 가엾고 여위고 쉴 줄 모르는 손가락을 부드럽게 풀어 주고, 그녀의 두 손을 내 손으로 감쌌다."]], [["“What are you thinking of, Laura?", "“무슨 생각을 하고 있어요, 로라?"], ["Tell me, my darling—try and tell me what it is.”", "말해 봐요, 내 사랑—그게 무엇인지 말해 보려고 애써 봐요.”"]], [["She struggled with herself, and raised her eyes to mine.", "그녀는 스스로와 싸우다가 눈을 들어 내 눈을 마주 보았다."], ["“I can’t feel happy,”", "“행복하다고 느낄 수가 없어요.”"], ["she said, “I can’t help thinking——”", "그녀가 말했다. “자꾸만 생각이 나서——”"], ["She stopped, bent forward a little, and laid her head on my shoulder, with a terrible mute helplessness that struck me to the heart.", "그녀는 말을 멈추고 몸을 조금 앞으로 숙이더니 내 어깨에 머리를 기댔다. 그 무섭도록 말 없는 무력함이 내 가슴을 찔렀다."]], [["“Try to tell me,”", "“말해 보려고 해 봐요.”"], ["I repeated gently; “try to tell me why you are not happy.”", "나는 부드럽게 되풀이했다. “왜 행복하지 않은지 말해 보려고 해 봐요.”"]], [["“I am so useless—I am such a burden on both of you,”", "“나는 너무 쓸모가 없어요—두 사람한테 짐만 되고요.”"], ["she answered, with a weary, hopeless sigh.", "그녀가 지치고 희망 없는 한숨과 함께 대답했다."], ["“You work and get money, Walter, and Marian helps you.", "“당신은 일해서 돈을 벌고, 매리언은 당신을 돕잖아요."], ["Why is there nothing I can do?", "왜 나는 할 수 있는 게 하나도 없죠?"], ["You will end in liking Marian better than you like me—you will, because I am so helpless!", "결국 당신은 나보다 매리언을 더 좋아하게 될 거예요—그렇게 될 거예요, 내가 이렇게 아무것도 못 하니까!"], ["Oh, don’t, don’t, don’t treat me like a child!”", "아, 제발, 제발, 제발 나를 어린애처럼 대하지 말아요!”"]], [["I raised her head, and smoothed away the tangled hair that fell over her face, and kissed her—my poor, faded flower!", "나는 그녀의 머리를 들어 올리고, 얼굴 위로 흘러내린 헝클어진 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 입을 맞추었다—나의 가엾고 빛바랜 꽃이여!"], ["my lost, afflicted sister!", "잃어버린, 상처 입은 나의 누이여!"], ["“You shall help us, Laura,”", "“당신은 우리를 도울 거예요, 로라.”"], ["I said, “you shall begin, my darling, to-day.”", "내가 말했다. “오늘부터 시작하는 거예요, 내 사랑.”"]], [["She looked at me with a feverish eagerness, with a breathless interest, that made me tremble for the new life of hope which I had called into being by those few words.", "그녀는 열에 들뜬 간절함으로, 숨 막힐 듯한 관심으로 나를 바라보았다. 그 몇 마디로 내가 불러낸 희망의 새 생명이 도리어 나를 떨리게 할 만큼."]], [["I rose, and set her drawing materials in order, and placed them near her again.", "나는 일어나 그녀의 화구를 정돈해서 다시 그녀 곁에 놓아 주었다."]], [["“You know that I work and get money by drawing,” I said.", "“내가 그림을 그려 일하고 돈을 버는 건 알고 있죠.”"], ["“Now you have taken such pains, now you are so much improved, you shall begin to work and get money too.", "내가 말했다. “이제 당신도 그토록 공을 들였고 그토록 솜씨가 늘었으니, 당신도 일해서 돈을 벌기 시작하는 거예요."], ["Try to finish this little sketch as nicely and prettily as you can.", "이 작은 스케치를 할 수 있는 한 곱고 예쁘게 마무리해 봐요."], ["When it is done I will take it away with me, and the same person will buy it who buys all that I do.", "다 되면 내가 가지고 나가서, 내 그림을 전부 사 주는 바로 그 사람에게 팔아 줄게요."], ["You shall keep your own earnings in your own purse, and Marian shall come to you to help us, as often as she comes to me.", "당신이 번 돈은 당신 지갑에 넣어 두는 거예요. 그리고 매리언이 나를 도우러 오는 만큼 자주 당신한테도 우리를 도우러 올 거고요."], ["Think how useful you are going to make yourself to both of us, and you will soon be as happy, Laura, as the day is long.”", "당신이 우리 두 사람에게 얼마나 쓸모 있는 사람이 될지 생각해 봐요. 그러면 곧 하루가 긴 만큼 내내 행복해질 거예요, 로라.”"]], [["Her face grew eager, and brightened into a smile.", "그녀의 얼굴이 간절해지더니 미소로 환해졌다."], ["In the moment while it lasted, in the moment when she again took up the pencils that had been laid aside, she almost looked like the Laura of past days.", "그 미소가 머무는 동안, 밀쳐 두었던 연필을 다시 집어 드는 그 순간, 그녀는 거의 지난날의 로라처럼 보였다."]], [["I had rightly interpreted the first signs of a new growth and strength in her mind, unconsciously expressing themselves in the notice she had taken of the occupations which filled her sister’s life and mine.", "나는 그녀의 마음속에서 새로운 성장과 힘이 처음 내비친 신호를 옳게 읽어 냈던 것이다. 그 신호는 언니와 나의 삶을 채우고 있는 일에 그녀가 관심을 보인 데서 저도 모르게 드러나 있었다."], ["Marian (when I told her what had passed) saw, as I saw, that she was longing to assume her own little position of importance, to raise herself in her own estimation and in ours—and, from that day, we tenderly helped the new ambition which gave promise of the hopeful, happier future, that might now not be far off.", "매리언은 (내가 그동안의 일을 이야기하자) 나와 마찬가지로, 로라가 자기 나름의 작은 소임을 맡아 자기 눈에도 우리 눈에도 스스로를 높이고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아차렸다—그리고 그날부터 우리는 이제 멀지 않을지도 모를 희망차고 더 행복한 미래를 약속해 주는 그 새로운 야심을 정성껏 거들었다."], ["Her drawings, as she finished them, or tried to finish them, were placed in my hands.", "그녀가 완성한, 혹은 완성하려 애쓴 그림들은 내 손에 맡겨졌다."], ["Marian took them from me and hid them carefully, and I set aside a little weekly tribute from my earnings, to be offered to her as the price paid by strangers for the poor, faint, valueless sketches, of which I was the only purchaser.", "매리언은 그것을 나에게서 받아 조심스레 감추어 두었고, 나는 내 벌이에서 매주 얼마씩을 따로 떼어, 그 가엾고 희미하고 값어치 없는—내가 유일한 구매자였던—스케치의 값으로 낯선 사람들이 치른 돈인 양 그녀에게 건넸다."], ["It was hard sometimes to maintain our innocent deception, when she proudly brought out her purse to contribute her share towards the expenses, and wondered with serious interest, whether I or she had earned the most that week.", "그녀가 자랑스럽게 지갑을 꺼내 살림에 제 몫을 보태면서, 그 주에 나와 자기 가운데 누가 더 많이 벌었는지 자못 진지하게 궁금해할 때면, 우리의 그 죄 없는 속임수를 지켜 내기가 때때로 힘들었다."], ["I have all those hidden drawings in my possession still—they are my treasures beyond price—the dear remembrances that I love to keep alive—the friends in past adversity that my heart will never part from, my tenderness never forget.", "감춰 둔 그 그림들을 나는 지금도 모두 간직하고 있다—값을 매길 수 없는 나의 보물이요—내가 살려 두기를 좋아하는 소중한 기억이요—내 마음이 결코 놓지 않을, 내 애틋함이 결코 잊지 않을, 지난 역경 속의 벗들이다."]], [["Am I trifling, here, with the necessities of my task?", "나는 지금 내 임무의 절박함을 두고 한가한 소리를 하고 있는 것인가?"], ["am I looking forward to the happier time which my narrative has not yet reached? Yes.", "내 이야기가 아직 이르지 못한 더 행복한 시절을 앞질러 바라보고 있는 것인가?"], ["Back again—back to the days of doubt and dread, when the spirit within me struggled hard for its life, in the icy stillness of perpetual suspense.", "그렇다. 다시 돌아가자—의혹과 두려움의 나날로, 끝없는 불안의 얼음 같은 정적 속에서 내 안의 정신이 살아남으려 안간힘을 쓰던 그 시절로."], ["I have paused and rested for a while on my forward course.", "나는 앞으로 나아가던 길에서 잠시 멈추어 쉬었다."], ["It is not, perhaps, time wasted, if the friends who read these pages have paused and rested too.", "이 글을 읽는 벗들도 함께 멈추어 쉬었다면, 어쩌면 헛되이 보낸 시간은 아니리라."]], [["I took the first opportunity I could find of speaking to Marian in private, and of communicating to her the result of the inquiries which I had made that morning.", "나는 매리언과 단둘이 이야기할 기회를 되도록 빨리 잡아, 그날 아침 내가 벌인 조사의 결과를 그녀에게 전했다."], ["She seemed to share the opinion on the subject of my proposed journey to Welmingham, which Mrs. Clements had already expressed to me.", "웰밍엄으로 가겠다는 내 계획에 대해 그녀는 이미 클레멘츠 부인이 나에게 밝혔던 것과 같은 의견을 품고 있는 듯했다."]], [["“Surely, Walter,”", "“월터, 정말이지,”"], ["she said, “you hardly know enough yet to give you any hope of claiming Mrs. Catherick’s confidence?", "그녀가 말했다, “캐서릭 부인의 신뢰를 얻으리라 기대할 만큼 아직 충분히 알고 있는 건 아니잖아요?"], ["Is it wise to proceed to these extremities, before you have really exhausted all safer and simpler means of attaining your object?", "목적을 이룰 더 안전하고 간단한 수단을 정말로 다 써 보기도 전에 그런 극단으로 나아가는 게 현명할까요?"], ["When you told me that Sir Percival and the Count were the only two people in existence who knew the exact date of Laura’s journey, you forgot, and I forgot, that there was a third person who must surely know it—I mean Mrs.", "로라가 여행한 정확한 날짜를 아는 사람이 세상에 퍼시벌 경과 백작 둘뿐이라고 당신이 말했을 때, 당신도 나도 그 날짜를 반드시 알고 있을 세 번째 사람을 잊고 있었어요—루벨 부인 말이에요."], ["Rubelle. Would it not be far easier, and far less dangerous, to insist on a confession from her, than to force it from Sir Percival?”", "퍼시벌 경에게서 억지로 자백을 받아 내는 것보다 그 여자에게서 받아 내는 편이 훨씬 쉽고 훨씬 덜 위험하지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“It might be easier,”", "“쉬울지도 모르지요.”"], ["I replied, “but we are not aware of the full extent of Mrs.", "내가 대답했다."], ["Rubelle’s connivance and interest in the conspiracy, and we are therefore not certain that the date has been impressed on her mind, as it has been assuredly impressed on the minds of Sir Percival and the Count.", "“하지만 루벨 부인이 그 음모에 어느 정도까지 가담했고 어떤 이해관계를 가졌는지 우리는 전부 알지 못하고, 따라서 퍼시벌 경과 백작의 머릿속에 그 날짜가 분명히 새겨져 있는 것처럼 그 여자의 머릿속에도 새겨져 있으리라 확신할 수 없습니다."], ["It is too late, now, to waste the time on Mrs. Rubelle, which may be all-important to the discovery of the one assailable point in Sir Percival’s life.", "퍼시벌 경의 생애에서 공략할 수 있는 단 하나의 지점을 찾아내는 데 무엇보다 소중할 시간을, 이제 와서 루벨 부인에게 허비하기에는 너무 늦었어요."], ["Are you thinking a little too seriously, Marian, of the risk I may run in returning to Hampshire?", "매리언, 내가 햄프셔로 돌아갔을 때 무릅쓸 위험을 너무 심각하게 생각하는 것 아닙니까?"], ["Are you beginning to doubt whether Sir Percival Glyde may not in the end be more than a match for me?”", "결국 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 나보다 한 수 위가 아닐까 의심하기 시작한 건가요?”"]], [["“He will not be more than your match,”", "“그 사람은 당신보다 한 수 위가 되지 못해요.”"], ["she replied decidedly, “because he will not be helped in resisting you by the impenetrable wickedness of the Count.”", "그녀가 단호하게 대답했다. “당신에게 맞서는 데 백작의 그 헤아릴 수 없는 악의 도움을 받지 못할 테니까요.”"]], [["“What has led you to that conclusion?”", "“무엇을 보고 그런 결론을 내렸습니까?”"], ["I replied, in some surprise.", "나는 다소 놀라며 되물었다."]], [["“My own knowledge of Sir Percival’s obstinacy and impatience of the Count’s control,”", "“퍼시벌 경의 고집과, 백작의 통제를 견디지 못하는 그 성미를 내가 잘 아니까요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I believe he will insist on meeting you single-handed—just as he insisted at first on acting for himself at Blackwater Park.", "“그 사람은 혼자서 당신을 상대하겠다고 우길 거예요—블랙워터 파크에서 처음에 제 힘으로 일을 처리하겠다고 우겼던 것처럼요."], ["The time for suspecting the Count’s interference will be the time when you have Sir Percival at your mercy.", "백작이 끼어들지 의심해야 할 때는, 당신이 퍼시벌 경을 손아귀에 넣었을 때예요."], ["His own interests will then be directly threatened, and he will act, Walter, to terrible purpose in his own defence.”", "그때는 백작 자신의 이익이 곧바로 위협받을 테고, 그러면 그는 자기를 지키기 위해 무서운 목적으로 움직일 거예요, 월터.”"]], [["“We may deprive him of his weapons beforehand,” I said.", "“미리 그의 무기를 빼앗아 둘 수도 있지요.”"], ["“Some of the particulars I have heard from Mrs.", "내가 말했다."], ["Clements may yet be turned to account against him, and other means of strengthening the case may be at our disposal. There are passages in Mrs.", "“클레멘츠 부인에게서 들은 이야기 가운데 몇 가지는 아직 그에게 불리하게 쓸 수 있을지 모르고, 사건을 더 굳건히 다질 다른 수단도 우리에게 있을 겁니다."], ["Michelson’s narrative which show that the Count found it necessary to place himself in communication with Mr. Fairlie, and there may be circumstances which compromise him in that proceeding.", "마이컬슨 부인의 진술에는 백작이 페얼리 씨와 연락을 취할 필요가 있었음을 보여 주는 대목이 있고, 그 과정에서 그를 옭아맬 정황이 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["While I am away, Marian, write to Mr. Fairlie and say that you want an answer describing exactly what passed between the Count and himself, and informing you also of any particulars that may have come to his knowledge at the same time in connection with his niece.", "매리언, 내가 떠나 있는 동안 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 써서, 백작과 그 사이에 오간 일을 정확히 적어 보내 달라고, 아울러 같은 시기에 조카에 관해 그가 알게 된 사정이 있으면 그것도 알려 달라고 답장을 요구하세요."], ["Tell him that the statement you request will, sooner or later, be insisted on, if he shows any reluctance to furnish you with it of his own accord.”", "그가 자진해서 그 진술을 내놓기를 꺼린다면 언젠가는 반드시 요구받게 될 것이라고 일러 두고요.”"]], [["“The letter shall be written, Walter.", "“편지는 쓸게요, 월터."], ["But are you really determined to go to Welmingham?”", "그런데 정말로 웰밍엄에 갈 작정인가요?”"]], [["“Absolutely determined.", "“확고히 작정했습니다."], ["I will devote the next two days to earning what we want for the week to come, and on the third day I go to Hampshire.”", "다음 이틀은 다가올 한 주에 필요한 돈을 버는 데 쓰고, 사흘째 되는 날 햄프셔로 가겠습니다.”"]], [["When the third day came I was ready for my journey.", "사흘째 날이 오자 나는 떠날 채비를 마쳤다."]], [["As it was possible that I might be absent for some little time, I arranged with Marian that we were to correspond every day—of course addressing each other by assumed names, for caution’s sake.", "얼마간 집을 비우게 될지도 몰랐으므로, 나는 매리언과 날마다 서로 편지를 주고받기로 정해 두었다—물론 조심하느라 서로 가명으로 부르기로 했다."], ["As long as I heard from her regularly, I should assume that nothing was wrong.", "그녀에게서 규칙적으로 소식이 오는 한 나는 아무 일도 없다고 여길 참이었다."], ["But if the morning came and brought me no letter, my return to London would take place, as a matter of course, by the first train.", "그러나 아침이 되어도 편지가 오지 않는다면, 나는 두말없이 첫 기차로 런던에 돌아올 것이었다."], ["I contrived to reconcile Laura to my departure by telling her that I was going to the country to find new purchasers for her drawings and for mine, and I left her occupied and happy.", "나는 로라에게 그녀의 그림과 내 그림을 사 줄 새 구매자를 찾으러 시골에 간다고 말해 내 출발을 납득시켰고, 그녀를 일거리에 몰두한 행복한 모습으로 남겨 두고 나왔다."], ["Marian followed me downstairs to the street door.", "매리언이 아래층 현관문까지 나를 따라 내려왔다."]], [["“Remember what anxious hearts you leave here,”", "“여기 얼마나 애타는 마음들을 두고 가는지 잊지 말아요.”"], ["she whispered, as we stood together in the passage.", "복도에 함께 서 있을 때 그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“Remember all the hopes that hang on your safe return.", "“당신이 무사히 돌아오기를 바라는 온갖 희망도요."], ["If strange things happen to you on this journey—if you and Sir Percival meet——”", "이번 여행에서 이상한 일이 생기거든—당신과 퍼시벌 경이 마주치거든——”"]], [["“What makes you think we shall meet?”", "“우리가 마주치리라고 생각하는 까닭이 뭡니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know—I have fears and fancies that I cannot account for.", "“모르겠어요—까닭을 알 수 없는 두려움과 망상이 자꾸 들어요."], ["Laugh at them, Walter, if you like—but, for God’s sake, keep your temper if you come in contact with that man!”", "웃어도 좋아요, 월터—하지만 제발, 그 사람과 맞닥뜨리거든 부디 화를 참아요!”"]], [["“Never fear, Marian!", "“걱정 말아요, 매리언!"], ["I answer for my self-control.”", "내 자제력은 내가 책임집니다.”"]], [["With those words we parted.", "그 말을 끝으로 우리는 헤어졌다."]], [["I walked briskly to the station.", "나는 힘차게 역으로 걸어갔다."], ["There was a glow of hope in me.", "내 안에 희망의 불빛이 있었다."], ["There was a growing conviction in my mind that my journey this time would not be taken in vain.", "이번 여행은 헛되지 않으리라는 확신이 마음속에서 자라고 있었다."], ["It was a fine, clear, cold morning.", "맑고 서늘한 좋은 아침이었다."], ["My nerves were firmly strung, and I felt all the strength of my resolution stirring in me vigorously from head to foot.", "신경은 팽팽히 조여져 있었고, 결의의 힘이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 씩씩하게 요동치는 것이 느껴졌다."]], [["As I crossed the railway platform, and looked right and left among the people congregated on it, to search for any faces among them that I knew, the doubt occurred to me whether it might not have been to my advantage if I had adopted a disguise before setting out for Hampshire.", "승강장을 가로지르며 아는 얼굴이 있는지 모여 선 사람들 사이를 좌우로 둘러보는 동안, 햄프셔로 떠나기 전에 변장을 했더라면 유리하지 않았을까 하는 의문이 떠올랐다."], ["But there was something so repellent to me in the idea—something so meanly like the common herd of spies and informers in the mere act of adopting a disguise—that I dismissed the question from consideration almost as soon as it had risen in my mind.", "그러나 그 생각에는 나를 밀어내는 무엇이—변장을 한다는 그 행위 자체에 흔해빠진 밀정과 밀고자 무리처럼 비열한 무엇이—있었기에, 나는 그 의문이 떠오르자마자 거의 곧바로 고려 대상에서 지워 버렸다."], ["Even as a mere matter of expediency the proceeding was doubtful in the extreme.", "순전히 편의의 문제로 따져도 그 방책은 극히 미심쩍었다."], ["If I tried the experiment at home the landlord of the house would sooner or later discover me, and would have his suspicions aroused immediately.", "집에서 그런 시도를 한다면 집주인이 언젠가는 나를 알아보고 즉시 의심을 품을 터였다."], ["If I tried it away from home the same persons might see me, by the commonest accident, with the disguise and without it, and I should in that way be inviting the notice and distrust which it was my most pressing interest to avoid.", "집 밖에서 시도한다면 같은 사람들이 지극히 사소한 우연으로 변장한 나와 변장하지 않은 나를 다 보게 될 수 있고, 그러면 나는 무엇보다 피해야 할 주목과 불신을 스스로 불러들이는 셈이었다."], ["In my own character I had acted thus far—and in my own character I was resolved to continue to the end.", "나는 여기까지 내 본모습으로 행동해 왔다—그리고 끝까지 내 본모습으로 밀고 나가기로 결심했다."]], [["The train left me at Welmingham early in the afternoon.", "기차는 이른 오후에 나를 웰밍엄에 내려놓았다."]], [["Is there any wilderness of sand in the deserts of Arabia, is there any prospect of desolation among the ruins of Palestine, which can rival the repelling effect on the eye, and the depressing influence on the mind, of an English country town in the first stage of its existence, and in the transition state of its prosperity?", "아라비아 사막의 어느 모래 황무지가, 팔레스타인 폐허의 어느 황량한 풍경이, 존재의 첫 단계에 있으면서 번영의 과도기에 놓인 영국 시골 소읍이 눈에 주는 거부감과 마음에 끼치는 침울함에 견줄 수 있으랴?"], ["I asked myself that question as I passed through the clean desolation, the neat ugliness, the prim torpor of the streets of Welmingham.", "웰밍엄 거리의 말끔한 황량함, 단정한 추함, 얌전한 무기력 속을 지나며 나는 스스로에게 그렇게 물었다."], ["And the tradesmen who stared after me from their lonely shops—the trees that drooped helpless in their arid exile of unfinished crescents and squares—the dead house-carcasses that waited in vain for the vivifying human element to animate them with the breath of life—every creature that I saw, every object that I passed, seemed to answer with one accord: The deserts of Arabia are innocent of our civilised desolation—the ruins of Palestine are incapable of our modern gloom!", "그리고 쓸쓸한 가게에서 나를 빤히 내다보던 상인들도—미완성인 초승달꼴 거리와 광장의 메마른 유배지에서 힘없이 늘어진 나무들도—생명의 숨결로 저희를 살려 낼 사람이라는 요소를 헛되이 기다리는 죽은 집의 시체들도—내가 본 모든 생물, 내가 지나친 모든 사물이 한목소리로 이렇게 대답하는 것 같았다. 아라비아의 사막은 우리네 문명화된 황량함을 알지 못하고—팔레스타인의 폐허는 우리네 현대의 음울함을 감당하지 못한다고!"]], [["I inquired my way to the quarter of the town in which Mrs. Catherick lived, and on reaching it found myself in a square of small houses, one story high.", "나는 캐서릭 부인이 사는 동네를 물어 찾아갔고, 그곳에 이르러 보니 단층 소형 주택들이 늘어선 광장 안이었다."], ["There was a bare little plot of grass in the middle, protected by a cheap wire fence.", "한가운데에는 값싼 철망 울타리로 둘러친 휑한 잔디밭이 조금 있었다."], ["An elderly nursemaid and two children were standing in a corner of the enclosure, looking at a lean goat tethered to the grass.", "나이 지긋한 유모 하나와 아이 둘이 울타리 한구석에 서서, 잔디에 매어 놓은 여윈 염소를 바라보고 있었다."], ["Two foot-passengers were talking together on one side of the pavement before the houses, and an idle little boy was leading an idle little dog along by a string on the other.", "집들 앞 보도 한쪽에서는 행인 둘이 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 다른 쪽에서는 한가한 사내아이가 한가한 강아지를 끈에 매어 끌고 가고 있었다."], ["I heard the dull tinkling of a piano at a distance, accompanied by the intermittent knocking of a hammer nearer at hand.", "멀리서 피아노의 둔탁한 소리가 들리고, 그보다 가까운 곳에서 간간이 망치 두드리는 소리가 섞여 들었다."], ["These were all the sights and sounds of life that encountered me when I entered the square.", "내가 그 광장에 들어섰을 때 나를 맞이한 삶의 광경과 소리는 그것이 전부였다."]], [["I walked at once to the door of Number Thirteen—the number of Mrs. Catherick’s house—and knocked, without waiting to consider beforehand how I might best present myself when I got in.", "나는 곧장 13번지—캐서릭 부인 집의 번지—문 앞으로 걸어가, 안에 들어가서 어떻게 나를 소개하는 것이 가장 좋을지 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고 문을 두드렸다."], ["The first necessity was to see Mrs. Catherick.", "우선 급한 것은 캐서릭 부인을 만나는 일이었다."], ["I could then judge, from my own observation, of the safest and easiest manner of approaching the object of my visit.", "그런 다음에야 내가 직접 관찰한 바에 따라, 내 방문의 목적에 다가가는 가장 안전하고 손쉬운 방법을 판단할 수 있을 터였다."]], [["The door was opened by a melancholy middle-aged woman servant.", "문을 연 사람은 우울한 얼굴의 중년 하녀였다."], ["I gave her my card, and asked if I could see Mrs. Catherick.", "나는 명함을 건네며 캐서릭 부인을 뵐 수 있는지 물었다."], ["The card was taken into the front parlour, and the servant returned with a message requesting me to mention what my business was.", "명함이 앞쪽 응접실로 들어갔고, 하녀는 무슨 용건인지 밝혀 달라는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다."]], [["“Say, if you please, that my business relates to Mrs. Catherick’s daughter,”", "“미안하지만, 캐서릭 부인의 따님에 관한 용건이라고 전해 주십시오.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["This was the best pretext I could think of, on the spur of the moment, to account for my visit.", "그 자리에서 얼른 떠올릴 수 있는, 내 방문을 설명할 가장 그럴듯한 구실이었다."]], [["The servant again retired to the parlour, again returned, and this time begged me, with a look of gloomy amazement, to walk in.", "하녀는 다시 응접실로 물러갔다가 다시 돌아왔고, 이번에는 침울한 놀라움이 서린 얼굴로 들어오시라고 청했다."]], [["I entered a little room, with a flaring paper of the largest pattern on the walls.", "나는 벽에 가장 큰 무늬의 요란한 벽지를 바른 작은 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Chairs, tables, cheffonier, and sofa, all gleamed with the glutinous brightness of cheap upholstery.", "의자며 탁자며 찬장이며 소파가 모두 값싼 가구의 끈적한 광택으로 번들거렸다."], ["On the largest table, in the middle of the room, stood a smart Bible, placed exactly in the centre on a red and yellow woollen mat and at the side of the table nearest to the window, with a little knitting-basket on her lap, and a wheezing, blear-eyed old spaniel crouched at her feet, there sat an elderly woman, wearing a black net cap and a black silk gown, and having slate-coloured mittens on her hands.", "방 한가운데 가장 큰 탁자 위에는 빨갛고 노란 모직 깔개 정중앙에 정확히 놓인 말쑥한 성경책이 있었고, 창에 가장 가까운 탁자 옆에는—무릎에 작은 뜨개질 바구니를 얹고, 발치에 숨을 쌕쌕대는 눈곱 낀 늙은 스패니얼을 웅크린 채로—검은 망사 모자에 검은 비단 가운을 입고 두 손에 슬레이트빛 반장갑을 낀 나이 든 여인이 앉아 있었다."], ["Her iron-grey hair hung in heavy bands on either side of her face—her dark eyes looked straight forward, with a hard, defiant, implacable stare.", "희끗희끗한 잿빛 머리는 얼굴 양옆으로 묵직하게 갈라져 내려와 있었고—검은 눈은 굳세고 도전적이고 무자비한 응시로 똑바로 앞을 향하고 있었다."], ["She had full square cheeks, a long, firm chin, and thick, sensual, colourless lips.", "볼은 도톰하고 각졌으며, 턱은 길고 단단했고, 입술은 두툼하고 관능적이면서 핏기가 없었다."], ["Her figure was stout and sturdy, and her manner aggressively self-possessed.", "몸집은 다부지고 튼튼했으며, 태도는 공격적일 만큼 침착했다."], ["This was Mrs. Catherick.", "이 사람이 캐서릭 부인이었다."]], [["“You have come to speak to me about my daughter,”", "“제 딸 이야기를 하러 오셨군요.”"], ["she said, before I could utter a word on my side.", "내가 미처 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Be so good as to mention what you have to say.”", "“하실 말씀이 무엇인지 말씀해 보시지요.”"]], [["The tone of her voice was as hard, as defiant, as implacable as the expression of her eyes.", "목소리의 어조는 눈빛만큼이나 굳세고 도전적이고 무자비했다."], ["She pointed to a chair, and looked me all over attentively, from head to foot, as I sat down in it.", "그녀는 의자를 가리켰고, 내가 거기 앉는 동안 머리끝에서 발끝까지 나를 유심히 훑어보았다."], ["I saw that my only chance with this woman was to speak to her in her own tone, and to meet her, at the outset of our interview, on her own ground.", "이 여자를 상대할 유일한 기회는 그녀 자신의 어조로 말하는 것, 면담이 시작되는 순간부터 그녀 자신의 땅에서 그녀를 맞상대하는 것뿐임을 나는 알아차렸다."]], [["“You are aware,”", "“따님이 행방불명된 것은 알고 계시겠지요?”"], ["I said, “that your daughter has been lost?”", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I am perfectly aware of it.”", "“물론 잘 알고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Have you felt any apprehension that the misfortune of her loss might be followed by the misfortune of her death?”", "“행방불명이라는 불행에 뒤이어 사망이라는 불행이 닥쳤을지도 모른다는 염려를 해 보신 적은 없습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“있지요."], ["Have you come here to tell me she is dead?”", "그 애가 죽었다는 말을 하러 오셨나요?”"]], [["“I have.”", "“그렇습니다.”"]], [["“Why?”", "“왜죠?”"]], [["She put that extraordinary question without the slightest change in her voice, her face, or her manner.", "그녀는 목소리도 얼굴도 태도도 조금도 바꾸지 않은 채 그 기이한 질문을 던졌다."], ["She could not have appeared more perfectly unconcerned if I had told her of the death of the goat in the enclosure outside.", "바깥 울타리 안의 염소가 죽었다는 이야기를 했더라도 그보다 더 완벽하게 무심해 보일 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Why?” I repeated.", "“왜냐고요?”"], ["“Do you ask why I come here to tell you of your daughter’s death?”", "나는 되물었다. “따님의 죽음을 알려 드리러 제가 여기 온 까닭을 물으시는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래요."], ["What interest have you in me, or in her?", "나에게든 그 애에게든 무슨 관심이 있으시죠?"], ["How do you come to know anything about my daughter?”", "내 딸에 대해 대체 어떻게 아시게 됐나요?”"]], [["“In this way.", "“이렇게 되었습니다."], ["I met her on the night when she escaped from the Asylum, and I assisted her in reaching a place of safety.”", "저는 따님이 정신병원에서 도망친 날 밤에 그분을 만났고, 안전한 곳에 이르도록 도왔습니다.”"]], [["“You did very wrong.”", "“아주 잘못하신 겁니다.”"]], [["“I am sorry to hear her mother say so.”", "“그 애 어머니께 그런 말을 들으니 유감입니다.”"]], [["“Her mother does say so.", "“그 애 어머니가 하는 말이 그래요."], ["How do you know she is dead?”", "그 애가 죽은 걸 어떻게 아시죠?”"]], [["“I am not at liberty to say how I know it—but I DO know it.”", "“어떻게 아는지는 말씀드릴 수 없습니다—그러나 저는 분명히 알고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Are you at liberty to say how you found out my address?”", "“제 주소를 어떻게 알아내셨는지는 말씀하실 수 있나요?”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“물론입니다."], ["I got your address from Mrs. Clements.”", "클레멘츠 부인에게서 주소를 얻었습니다.”"]], [["“Mrs. Clements is a foolish woman.", "“클레멘츠 부인은 어리석은 여자예요."], ["Did she tell you to come here?”", "그 여자가 여기 오라고 하던가요?”"]], [["“She did not.”", "“아닙니다.”"]], [["“Then, I ask you again, why did you come?”", "“그러면 다시 묻겠습니다. 왜 오셨죠?”"]], [["As she was determined to have her answer, I gave it to her in the plainest possible form.", "그녀가 기어이 대답을 들으려 했으므로, 나는 더없이 단순한 형태로 대답을 주었다."]], [["“I came,”", "“제가 온 것은,”"], ["I said, “because I thought Anne Catherick’s mother might have some natural interest in knowing whether she was alive or dead.”", "내가 말했다, “앤 캐서릭의 어머니라면 딸이 살았는지 죽었는지 아는 데 자연스러운 관심을 가지실 거라고 생각했기 때문입니다.”"]], [["“Just so,” said Mrs.", "“그렇겠지요.”"], ["Catherick, with additional self-possession.", "캐서릭 부인이 한층 더 침착해져서 말했다."], ["“Had you no other motive?”", "“다른 동기는 없으셨나요?”"]], [["I hesitated.", "나는 망설였다."], ["The right answer to that question was not easy to find at a moment’s notice.", "그 질문에 대한 옳은 대답은 순간에 찾아내기 쉽지 않았다."]], [["“If you have no other motive,”", "“다른 동기가 없으시다면,”"], ["she went on, deliberately taking off her slate-coloured mittens, and rolling them up, “I have only to thank you for your visit, and to say that I will not detain you here any longer.", "그녀는 슬레이트빛 반장갑을 천천히 벗어 둘둘 말면서 말을 이었다, “방문해 주신 데 감사드리고 더는 붙들지 않겠다는 말씀밖에 드릴 게 없군요."], ["Your information would be more satisfactory if you were willing to explain how you became possessed of it.", "그 소식을 어떻게 알게 되셨는지 기꺼이 설명해 주신다면 더 만족스러울 텐데요."], ["However, it justifies me, I suppose, in going into mourning.", "어쨌든 이만하면 제가 상복을 입어도 되겠지요."], ["There is not much alteration necessary in my dress, as you see.", "보시다시피 제 옷은 크게 고칠 것도 없습니다."], ["When I have changed my mittens, I shall be all in black.”", "반장갑만 바꿔 끼면 온통 검은색이 되니까요.”"]], [["She searched in the pocket of her gown, drew out a pair of black lace mittens, put them on with the stoniest and steadiest composure, and then quietly crossed her hands in her lap.", "그녀는 가운 주머니를 뒤져 검은 레이스 반장갑 한 켤레를 꺼내더니, 돌처럼 흔들림 없는 침착함으로 그것을 끼고는 조용히 두 손을 무릎 위에 포갰다."]], [["“I wish you good morning,”", "“그럼 안녕히 가십시오.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The cool contempt of her manner irritated me into directly avowing that the purpose of my visit had not been answered yet.", "그 태도의 서늘한 경멸에 부아가 치민 나는, 아직 내 방문의 목적이 이루어지지 않았다고 곧바로 털어놓고 말았다."]], [["“I HAVE another motive in coming here,”", "“저는 여기 온 다른 동기가 있습니다.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I thought so,”", "그럴 줄 알았지요.”"], ["remarked Mrs. Catherick.", "캐서릭 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Your daughter’s death——”", "“따님의 죽음이——”"]], [["“What did she die of?”", "“무슨 병으로 죽었나요?”"]], [["“Of disease of the heart.”", "“심장병입니다.”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그렇군요."], ["Go on.”", "계속하시죠.”"]], [["“Your daughter’s death has been made the pretext for inflicting serious injury on a person who is very dear to me.", "“따님의 죽음은 저에게 몹시 소중한 사람에게 심각한 해를 입히는 구실로 이용되었습니다."], ["Two men have been concerned, to my certain knowledge, in doing that wrong.", "제가 확실히 아는 바로는 두 사람이 그 못된 짓에 관여했습니다."], ["One of them is Sir Percival Glyde.”", "그중 하나가 퍼시벌 글라이드 경입니다.”"]], [["“Indeed!”", "“저런!”"]], [["I looked attentively to see if she flinched at the sudden mention of that name.", "나는 그 이름이 갑자기 나왔을 때 그녀가 움찔하는지 눈여겨보았다."], ["Not a muscle of her stirred—the hard, defiant, implacable stare in her eyes never wavered for an instant.", "근육 하나 까딱하지 않았다—굳세고 도전적이고 무자비한 눈의 응시는 한순간도 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["“You may wonder,”", "“따님의 죽음이라는 사건이 어떻게 다른 사람에게 해를 입히는 수단이 될 수 있었는지 의아하실 겁니다.”"], ["I went on, “how the event of your daughter’s death can have been made the means of inflicting injury on another person.”", "내가 말을 이었다."]], [["“No,” said Mrs.", "“아니요.”"], ["Catherick; “I don’t wonder at all.", "캐서릭 부인이 말했다. “조금도 의아하지 않습니다."], ["This appears to be your affair.", "그건 선생 일인 듯하군요."], ["You are interested in my affairs. I am not interested in yours.”", "선생은 내 일에 관심이 있고, 나는 선생 일에 관심이 없습니다.”"]], [["“You may ask, then,”", "“그러면 왜 제가 부인 앞에서 이 문제를 꺼내는지 물으시겠지요.”"], ["I persisted, “why I mention the matter in your presence.”", "나는 물러서지 않았다."]], [["“Yes, I DO ask that.”", "“그래요, 그건 정말로 묻고 싶군요.”"]], [["“I mention it because I am determined to bring Sir Percival Glyde to account for the wickedness he has committed.”", "“제가 이 말씀을 드리는 것은, 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 저지른 악행에 대해 반드시 책임을 묻겠다고 결심했기 때문입니다.”"]], [["“What have I to do with your determination?”", "“선생 결심이 나와 무슨 상관이죠?”"]], [["“You shall hear.", "“들어 보시면 압니다."], ["There are certain events in Sir Percival’s past life which it is necessary for my purpose to be fully acquainted with.", "퍼시벌 경의 지난 생애에는 제 목적을 위해 반드시 소상히 알아야 할 어떤 사건들이 있습니다."], ["YOU know them—and for that reason I come to YOU.”", "부인께서 그것을 알고 계십니다—그래서 저는 부인을 찾아온 것입니다.”"]], [["“What events do you mean?”", "“무슨 사건 말인가요?”"]], [["“Events that occurred at Old Welmingham when your husband was parish-clerk at that place, and before the time when your daughter was born.”", "“부인의 남편이 올드 웰밍엄의 교회 서기로 있던 시절, 따님이 태어나기 전에 그곳에서 일어난 사건들입니다.”"]], [["I had reached the woman at last through the barrier of impenetrable reserve that she had tried to set up between us.", "마침내 나는 그녀가 우리 사이에 세우려 했던 뚫을 수 없는 과묵의 장벽을 뚫고 그 여자에게 가닿았다."], ["I saw her temper smouldering in her eyes—as plainly as I saw her hands grow restless, then unclasp themselves, and begin mechanically smoothing her dress over her knees.", "그녀의 눈에서 노기가 이글거리는 것이 보였다—두 손이 다시 안절부절못하다가 깍지를 풀고 무릎 위 옷자락을 기계적으로 쓸어내리기 시작하는 것이 보인 만큼이나 또렷하게."]], [["“What do you know of those events?”", "“그 사건들에 대해 무엇을 아시죠?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“All that Mrs. Clements could tell me,”", "“클레멘츠 부인이 저에게 말해 줄 수 있는 것은 전부.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["There was a momentary flush on her firm square face, a momentary stillness in her restless hands, which seemed to betoken a coming outburst of anger that might throw her off her guard.", "그 단단하고 각진 얼굴에 순간 홍조가 스쳤고, 쉼 없던 두 손이 순간 멎었다. 경계를 무너뜨릴 만한 노여움의 폭발이 다가온다는 조짐 같았다."], ["But no—she mastered the rising irritation, leaned back in her chair, crossed her arms on her broad bosom, and with a smile of grim sarcasm on her thick lips, looked at me as steadily as ever.", "그러나 아니었다—그녀는 치미는 분을 억누르고 의자에 등을 기대더니, 넓은 가슴 위에 팔짱을 끼고, 두툼한 입술에 험상궂은 빈정거림의 미소를 띤 채 여전히 흔들림 없이 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I begin to understand it all now,”", "이제야 다 알겠군요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, her tamed and disciplined anger only expressing itself in the elaborate mockery of her tone and manner.", "길들여지고 다스려진 그 노여움은 오직 어조와 태도의 공들인 조롱으로만 드러났다."], ["“You have got a grudge of your own against Sir Percival Glyde, and I must help you to wreak it.", "“선생은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게 사사로운 원한이 있고, 나는 선생이 그 원한을 푸는 걸 도와야 한다는 거군요."], ["I must tell you this, that, and the other about Sir Percival and myself, must I?", "퍼시벌 경과 나에 대해 이런저런 이야기를 다 해 드려야 한다, 그거죠?"], ["Yes, indeed?", "정말 그런가요?"], ["You have been prying into my private affairs.", "선생은 내 사생활을 캐고 다녔군요."], ["You think you have found a lost woman to deal with, who lives here on sufferance, and who will do anything you ask for fear you may injure her in the opinions of the town’s-people.", "남의 눈치를 보며 여기 얹혀사는 타락한 여자를 상대하게 됐다고, 읍내 사람들 앞에서 평판을 깎아 버릴까 두려워 무엇이든 시키는 대로 할 여자를 찾아냈다고 생각하는 거죠."], ["I see through you and your precious speculation—I do!", "나는 선생과 선생의 그 갸륵한 속셈을 훤히 꿰뚫어 봅니다—그렇고말고요!"], ["and it amuses me.", "그리고 그게 우습군요."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!”", "하!”"]], [["She stopped for a moment, her arms tightened over her bosom, and she laughed to herself—a hard, harsh, angry laugh.", "그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추고 가슴 위로 팔을 더 죄더니 혼자 웃었다—굳고 거칠고 성난 웃음이었다."]], [["“You don’t know how I have lived in this place, and what I have done in this place, Mr.", "“선생, 이름이 뭐라고 하셨죠, 선생은 내가 이곳에서 어떻게 살아왔는지, 이곳에서 무엇을 해냈는지 모릅니다.”"], ["What’s-your-name,”", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she went on. “I’ll tell you, before I ring the bell and have you shown out.", "“종을 울려 선생을 내보내기 전에 말해 드리지요."], ["I came here a wronged woman—I came here robbed of my character and determined to claim it back.", "나는 억울한 일을 당한 여자로 이곳에 왔습니다—평판을 빼앗긴 채로 와서, 그것을 되찾겠다고 마음먹었지요."], ["I’ve been years and years about it—and I HAVE claimed it back.", "몇 해고 몇 해고 그 일에 매달렸고—나는 그것을 되찾았습니다."], ["I have matched the respectable people fairly and openly on their own ground.", "나는 점잖은 사람들과 그들의 땅에서 정정당당하게 겨루어 대등해졌어요."], ["If they say anything against me now they must say it in secret—they can’t say it, they daren’t say it, openly.", "이제 그들이 나를 두고 무슨 말을 하려면 몰래 해야 합니다—대놓고는 못 해요, 감히 못 합니다."], ["I stand high enough in this town to be out of your reach.", "나는 이 읍내에서 선생 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 높이 서 있습니다."], ["THE CLERGYMAN BOWS TO ME.", "목사님이 나에게 인사를 합니다."], ["Aha!", "아하!"], ["you didn’t bargain for that when you came here.", "여기 올 때 그건 미처 셈에 넣지 못하셨겠지요."], ["Go to the church and inquire about me—you will find Mrs. Catherick has her sitting like the rest of them, and pays the rent on the day it’s due.", "교회에 가서 나에 대해 물어보세요—캐서릭 부인도 남들처럼 제 좌석을 가지고 있고 자릿세를 기한에 꼬박꼬박 낸다는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["Go to the town-hall.", "읍사무소에도 가 보세요."], ["There’s a petition lying there—a petition of the respectable inhabitants against allowing a circus to come and perform here and corrupt our morals—yes!", "거기 청원서가 하나 놓여 있어요—서커스단이 들어와 공연을 하며 우리 풍속을 어지럽히지 못하게 해 달라는 점잖은 주민들의 청원서 말입니다—그래요, 우리 풍속이오."], ["OUR morals. I signed that petition this morning.", "나는 오늘 아침에 그 청원서에 서명했습니다."], ["Go to the bookseller’s shop.", "서점에도 가 보세요."], ["The clergyman’s Wednesday evening Lectures on Justification by Faith are publishing there by subscription—I’m down on the list.", "목사님의 수요일 저녁 강론집 ‘믿음으로 말미암은 칭의’가 예약 구독으로 출간되는 중인데—내 이름이 명단에 올라 있습니다."], ["The doctor’s wife only put a shilling in the plate at our last charity sermon—I put half-a-crown.", "지난번 자선 설교 때 의사 부인은 헌금 접시에 겨우 1실링을 넣었지만—나는 반 크라운을 넣었어요."], ["Mr. Churchwarden Soward held the plate, and bowed to me.", "접시를 받쳐 든 교회 위원 소워드 씨가 나에게 인사를 했지요."], ["Ten years ago he told Pigrum the chemist I ought to be whipped out of the town at the cart’s tail.", "십 년 전에 그 사람은 약제사 피그럼에게 나 같은 여자는 수레 꽁무니에 매달아 채찍질하며 읍에서 쫓아내야 한다고 말했습니다."], ["Is your mother alive?", "선생 어머니는 살아 계신가요?"], ["Has she got a better Bible on her table than I have got on mine?", "그분 탁자 위에 내 것보다 더 좋은 성경책이 놓여 있나요?"], ["Does she stand better with her trades-people than I do with mine?", "그분은 나만큼 단골 상인들에게 신용을 얻고 계신가요?"], ["Has she always lived within her income?", "그분은 늘 수입에 맞게 살아오셨나요?"], ["I have always lived within mine.", "나는 늘 내 수입에 맞게 살았습니다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["there IS the clergyman coming along the square.", "저기 목사님이 광장을 지나오시는군요."], ["Look, Mr. What’s-your-name—look, if you please!”", "보세요, 선생, 이름이 뭐라고 하셨죠—어디 좀 보시라니까요!”"]], [["She started up with the activity of a young woman, went to the window, waited till the clergyman passed, and bowed to him solemnly.", "그녀는 젊은 여자처럼 날래게 일어나 창가로 가서, 목사가 지나가기를 기다렸다가 정중하게 인사를 했다."], ["The clergyman ceremoniously raised his hat, and walked on.", "목사는 격식을 차려 모자를 들어 올리고는 걸어갔다."], ["Mrs. Catherick returned to her chair, and looked at me with a grimmer sarcasm than ever.", "캐서릭 부인은 의자로 돌아와, 그 어느 때보다 험상궂은 빈정거림을 띠고 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“There!” she said.", "“보셨죠!”"], ["“What do you think of that for a woman with a lost character?", "그녀가 말했다. “평판 잃은 여자치고 저만하면 어떻습니까?"], ["How does your speculation look now?”", "이제 선생의 그 속셈은 어때 보이나요?”"]], [["The singular manner in which she had chosen to assert herself, the extraordinary practical vindication of her position in the town which she had just offered, had so perplexed me that I listened to her in silent surprise.", "그녀가 스스로를 내세우려고 택한 그 별난 방식, 읍내에서의 제 위치를 방금 눈앞에 실증해 보인 그 유별난 방법에 나는 어리둥절해져서, 말없이 놀란 채 그녀의 말을 듣고만 있었다."], ["I was not the less resolved, however, to make another effort to throw her off her guard.", "그러나 그녀의 경계를 무너뜨리려는 노력을 한 번 더 해 보겠다는 결심만은 조금도 흔들리지 않았다."], ["If the woman’s fierce temper once got beyond her control, and once flamed out on me, she might yet say the words which would put the clue in my hands.", "이 여자의 사나운 성미가 한 번이라도 통제를 벗어나 나에게 불길처럼 터져 나온다면, 실마리를 내 손에 쥐여 줄 말이 아직 튀어나올지도 몰랐다."]], [["“How does your speculation look now?”", "“선생의 그 속셈은 이제 어때 보이나요?”"], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되풀이했다."]], [["“Exactly as it looked when I first came in,”", "“처음 들어왔을 때와 똑같아 보입니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“I don’t doubt the position you have gained in the town, and I don’t wish to assail it even if I could.", "“부인이 이 읍내에서 얻으신 지위를 저는 의심하지 않고, 설령 할 수 있다 해도 그것을 헐뜯을 생각이 없습니다."], ["I came here because Sir Percival Glyde is, to my certain knowledge, your enemy, as well as mine.", "제가 여기 온 것은 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이, 제가 확실히 아는 바로는, 저에게만이 아니라 부인께도 원수이기 때문입니다."], ["If I have a grudge against him, you have a grudge against him too.", "제가 그에게 원한이 있다면 부인께도 그에게 원한이 있습니다."], ["You may deny it if you like, you may distrust me as much as you please, you may be as angry as you will—but, of all the women in England, you, if you have any sense of injury, are the woman who ought to help me to crush that man.”", "부인하셔도 좋고, 저를 마음껏 불신하셔도 좋고, 얼마든지 노여워하셔도 좋습니다—그러나 잉글랜드의 모든 여자 가운데, 부인이야말로 억울함을 느낄 줄 아신다면, 그자를 짓밟는 일을 저와 함께해야 할 분입니다.”"]], [["“Crush him for yourself,”", "“혼자서 짓밟으세요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said; “then come back here, and see what I say to you.”", "“그런 다음에 여기 다시 오시면, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지 들으실 겁니다.”"]], [["She spoke those words as she had not spoken yet, quickly, fiercely, vindictively.", "그녀는 지금껏 하지 않던 투로 그 말을 했다. 빠르고, 사납고, 앙심에 차서."], ["I had stirred in its lair the serpent-hatred of years, but only for a moment.", "나는 여러 해 묵은 뱀 같은 증오를 그 굴에서 건드려 놓았던 것이다."], ["Like a lurking reptile it leaped up at me as she eagerly bent forward towards the place in which I was sitting.", "그러나 그것도 한순간이었다. 그녀가 내가 앉은 자리 쪽으로 열렬히 몸을 숙일 때, 숨어 있던 파충류처럼 그것이 나에게 달려들었다."], ["Like a lurking reptile it dropped out of sight again as she instantly resumed her former position in the chair.", "그리고 그녀가 곧바로 의자에서 아까의 자세로 돌아가자, 숨어 있던 파충류처럼 그것은 다시 자취를 감추었다."]], [["“You won’t trust me?”", "“저를 믿지 못하시겠습니까?”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“No.”", "“못 믿습니다.”"]], [["“You are afraid?”", "“두려우신 겁니까?”"]], [["“Do I look as if I was?”", "“내가 두려워하는 사람으로 보이나요?”"]], [["“You are afraid of Sir Percival Glyde?”", "“퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 두려우신 겁니까?”"]], [["“Am I?”", "“내가요?”"]], [["Her colour was rising, and her hands were at work again smoothing her gown.", "그녀의 낯빛이 붉어지고 있었고, 두 손은 다시 가운을 쓸어내리며 움직이고 있었다."], ["I pressed the point farther and farther home, I went on without allowing her a moment of delay.", "나는 그 지점을 점점 더 깊이 파고들었고, 그녀에게 한순간의 여유도 주지 않고 밀어붙였다."]], [["“Sir Percival has a high position in the world,”", "“퍼시벌 경은 세상에서 높은 지위에 있습니다.”"], ["I said; “it would be no wonder if you were afraid of him.", "내가 말했다. “부인이 그를 두려워한다 해도 이상할 것이 없지요."], ["Sir Percival is a powerful man, a baronet, the possessor of a fine estate, the descendant of a great family——”", "퍼시벌 경은 힘 있는 사람이고, 준남작이고, 훌륭한 영지의 소유자이며, 대단한 가문의 후손이니까요——”"]], [["She amazed me beyond expression by suddenly bursting out laughing.", "그녀는 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨려 나를 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래요.”"], ["she repeated, in tones of the bitterest, steadiest contempt.", "그녀가 더없이 신랄하고 흔들림 없는 경멸의 어조로 되뇌었다."], ["“A baronet, the possessor of a fine estate, the descendant of a great family.", "“준남작이고, 훌륭한 영지의 소유자이고, 대단한 가문의 후손이지요."], ["Yes, indeed!", "정말 그렇고말고요!"], ["A great family—especially by the mother’s side.”", "대단한 가문—특히 어머니 쪽으로요.”"]], [["There was no time to reflect on the words that had just escaped her, there was only time to feel that they were well worth thinking over the moment I left the house.", "방금 그녀의 입에서 새어 나온 말을 곱씹을 겨를은 없었다. 다만 그 집을 나서는 순간 곰곰이 생각해 볼 값어치가 충분한 말이라는 느낌만이 스쳤을 뿐이다."]], [["“I am not here to dispute with you about family questions,”", "“저는 부인과 가문 문제로 다투러 온 것이 아닙니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “I know nothing of Sir Percival’s mother——”", "“저는 퍼시벌 경의 어머니에 대해서는 아무것도 모릅니다——”"]], [["“And you know as little of Sir Percival himself,”", "“퍼시벌 경 본인에 대해서도 아는 게 그만큼 없지요.”"], ["she interposed sharply.", "그녀가 날카롭게 끼어들었다."]], [["“I advise you not to be too sure of that,”", "“그렇게 자신하지 않으시는 게 좋을 겁니다.”"], ["I rejoined.", "내가 받아쳤다."], ["“I know some things about him, and I suspect many more.”", "“저는 그에 대해 몇 가지를 알고 있고, 그보다 훨씬 많은 것을 의심하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“What do you suspect?”", "“무엇을 의심하시죠?”"]], [["“I’ll tell you what I DON’T suspect.", "“제가 의심하지 않는 것을 말씀드리지요."], ["I DON’T suspect him of being Anne’s father.”", "저는 그가 앤의 아버지라고 의심하지 않습니다.”"]], [["She started to her feet, and came close up to me with a look of fury.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 격노한 얼굴로 내 앞에 바싹 다가섰다."]], [["“How dare you talk to me about Anne’s father!", "“감히 나에게 앤의 아버지 이야기를 하다니!"], ["How dare you say who was her father, or who wasn’t!”", "감히 누가 그 애 아비였고 누가 아니었는지를 말하다니!”"], ["she broke out, her face quivering, her voice trembling with passion.", "그녀가 얼굴을 떨고 격정에 목소리를 떨며 소리쳤다."]], [["“The secret between you and Sir Percival is not THAT secret,”", "“부인과 퍼시벌 경 사이의 비밀은 그런 비밀이 아닙니다.”"], ["I persisted.", "나는 물러서지 않았다."], ["“The mystery which darkens Sir Percival’s life was not born with your daughter’s birth, and has not died with your daughter’s death.”", "“퍼시벌 경의 생애를 어둡게 하는 그 수수께끼는 따님의 출생과 함께 태어나지 않았고, 따님의 죽음과 함께 죽지도 않았습니다.”"]], [["She drew back a step. “Go!”", "그녀는 한 걸음 물러섰다."], ["she said, and pointed sternly to the door.", "“나가세요!” 그녀가 말하며 준엄하게 문을 가리켰다."]], [["“There was no thought of the child in your heart or in his,”", "“부인의 마음에도 그의 마음에도 그 아이에 대한 생각은 없었습니다.”"], ["I went on, determined to press her back to her last defences.", "나는 그녀를 마지막 방어선까지 몰아붙이기로 작정하고 말을 이었다."], ["“There was no bond of guilty love between you and him when you held those stolen meetings, when your husband found you whispering together under the vestry of the church.”", "“남편분이 두 분이 소곤거리는 것을 발견했던 그때, 교회 성구실 아래에서 남몰래 만나던 그 자리에는, 부인과 그 사람 사이의 죄스러운 사랑 같은 것은 없었습니다.”"]], [["Her pointing hand instantly dropped to her side, and the deep flush of anger faded from her face while I spoke.", "가리키던 손이 즉시 옆으로 떨어졌고, 내가 말하는 동안 노여움의 짙은 홍조가 얼굴에서 걷혔다."], ["I saw the change pass over her—I saw that hard, firm, fearless, self-possessed woman quail under a terror which her utmost resolution was not strong enough to resist when I said those five last words, “the vestry of the church.”", "나는 그 변화가 그녀를 스쳐 지나가는 것을 보았다—“교회 성구실”이라는 그 마지막 말을 내가 했을 때, 굳세고 단단하고 겁 없고 침착하던 그 여자가 자기의 온 결의로도 버텨 낼 수 없는 공포에 움츠러드는 것을 보았다."]], [["For a minute or more we stood looking at each other in silence.", "우리는 일 분 남짓 말없이 서로를 마주 보고 서 있었다."], ["I spoke first.", "먼저 입을 연 것은 나였다."]], [["“Do you still refuse to trust me?”", "“아직도 저를 믿지 못하시겠습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["She could not call the colour that had left it back to her face, but she had steadied her voice, she had recovered the defiant self-possession of her manner when she answered me.", "얼굴에서 가신 핏기를 그녀는 되불러오지 못했지만, 나에게 대답할 때는 목소리를 가다듬고 그 도전적이고 침착한 태도를 되찾은 뒤였다."]], [["“I do refuse,”", "“못 믿습니다.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Do you still tell me to go?”", "“아직도 나가라고 하시겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래요."], ["Go—and never come back.”", "나가세요—그리고 두 번 다시 오지 마세요.”"]], [["I walked to the door, waited a moment before I opened it, and turned round to look at her again.", "나는 문 쪽으로 걸어가, 문을 열기 전에 잠시 멈추어 서서, 몸을 돌려 그녀를 다시 바라보았다."]], [["“I may have news to bring you of Sir Percival which you don’t expect,”", "“퍼시벌 경에 관해 부인이 예상하지 못한 소식을 가져오게 될지도 모릅니다.”"], ["I said, “and in that case I shall come back.”", "내가 말했다. “그렇게 되면 저는 다시 오겠습니다.”"]], [["“There is no news of Sir Percival that I don’t expect, except——”", "“퍼시벌 경에 관해 내가 예상하지 못할 소식은 없습니다. 다만——”"]], [["She stopped, her pale face darkened, and she stole back with a quiet, stealthy, cat-like step to her chair.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었고, 창백한 얼굴이 어두워졌으며, 조용하고 은밀한 고양이 같은 걸음으로 슬며시 의자로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Except the news of his death,”", "“그가 죽었다는 소식만 빼고요.”"], ["she said, sitting down again, with the mockery of a smile just hovering on her cruel lips, and the furtive light of hatred lurking deep in her steady eyes.", "그녀는 다시 앉으며 말했다. 잔인한 입술에는 조롱의 미소가 어렴풋이 감돌았고, 흔들림 없는 눈 속 깊은 곳에는 증오의 은밀한 빛이 도사리고 있었다."]], [["As I opened the door of the room to go out, she looked round at me quickly.", "방을 나가려고 문을 열었을 때, 그녀가 홱 나를 돌아보았다."], ["The cruel smile slowly widened her lips—she eyed me, with a strange stealthy interest, from head to foot—an unutterable expectation showed itself wickedly all over her face.", "잔인한 미소가 천천히 입술을 벌려 갔다—그녀는 야릇하고 은밀한 관심으로 나를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 훑어보았다—이루 말할 수 없는 기대가 얼굴 전체에 사악하게 떠올랐다."], ["Was she speculating, in the secrecy of her own heart, on my youth and strength, on the force of my sense of injury and the limits of my self-control, and was she considering the lengths to which they might carry me, if Sir Percival and I ever chanced to meet?", "그녀는 제 마음속 은밀한 곳에서 내 젊음과 힘을, 내가 느끼는 억울함의 크기와 내 자제력의 한계를 가늠하고 있었던 것일까? 그리고 퍼시벌 경과 내가 언젠가 마주치게 된다면 그것들이 나를 어디까지 몰고 갈지 헤아리고 있었던 것일까?"], ["The bare doubt that it might be so drove me from her presence, and silenced even the common forms of farewell on my lips.", "그럴지도 모른다는 의심만으로도 나는 그 앞을 떠나야 했고, 흔한 작별 인사조차 입에서 나오지 않았다."], ["Without a word more, on my side or on hers, I left the room.", "내 쪽에서도 그녀 쪽에서도 더는 한마디 없이, 나는 방을 나섰다."]], [["As I opened the outer door, I saw the same clergyman who had already passed the house once, about to pass it again, on his way back through the square.", "바깥문을 열었을 때, 이미 한 번 이 집 앞을 지나갔던 그 목사가 광장을 돌아 돌아가는 길에 다시 이 집 앞을 지나려는 참이었다."], ["I waited on the door-step to let him go by, and looked round, as I did so, at the parlour window.", "나는 그가 지나가도록 문간에 서서 기다렸고, 그러면서 응접실 창을 돌아보았다."]], [["Mrs. Catherick had heard his footsteps approaching, in the silence of that lonely place, and she was on her feet at the window again, waiting for him.", "캐서릭 부인은 그 적막한 곳의 정적 속에서 다가오는 발소리를 듣고, 다시 창가에 일어서서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Not all the strength of all the terrible passions I had roused in that woman’s heart, could loosen her desperate hold on the one fragment of social consideration which years of resolute effort had just dragged within her grasp.", "내가 그 여자의 마음속에 불러일으킨 온갖 무서운 격정의 힘을 다 합쳐도, 여러 해의 굳센 노력이 이제 막 손안에 끌어다 놓은 그 한 조각 사회적 인정에 대한 그녀의 필사적인 집착을 떼어 놓을 수는 없었다."], ["There she was again, not a minute after I had left her, placed purposely in a position which made it a matter of common courtesy on the part of the clergyman to bow to her for a second time.", "내가 그 곁을 떠난 지 채 일 분도 되지 않아 그녀는 다시 그 자리에, 목사가 두 번째로 인사를 하는 것이 예사로운 예의가 되도록 일부러 잡은 위치에 서 있었다."], ["He raised his hat once more.", "그는 다시 한번 모자를 들어 올렸다."], ["I saw the hard ghastly face behind the window soften, and light up with gratified pride—I saw the head with the grim black cap bend ceremoniously in return.", "나는 창 너머 그 굳고 소름 끼치는 얼굴이 누그러지며 흡족한 자부심으로 환해지는 것을 보았다—험상궂은 검은 모자를 쓴 머리가 답례로 격식 차려 숙여지는 것을 보았다."], ["The clergyman had bowed to her, and in my presence, twice in one day!", "목사가 그녀에게 인사를 한 것이다, 그것도 내가 보는 앞에서, 하루에 두 번씩이나!"]], [["# IX", "# IX"]], [["I left the house, feeling that Mrs. Catherick had helped me a step forward, in spite of herself.", "나는 그 집을 나서면서, 캐서릭 부인이 본인의 뜻과는 상관없이 나를 한 걸음 앞으로 나아가게 해 주었다고 느꼈다."], ["Before I had reached the turning which led out of the square, my attention was suddenly aroused by the sound of a closing door behind me.", "광장을 벗어나는 모퉁이에 이르기도 전에, 등 뒤에서 문 닫히는 소리가 들려 나는 문득 주의를 빼앗겼다."]], [["I looked round, and saw an undersized man in black on the door-step of a house, which, as well as I could judge, stood next to Mrs.", "돌아보니, 검은 옷을 입은 자그마한 사내가 어느 집 현관 계단에 서 있었다."], ["Catherick’s place of abode—next to it, on the side nearest to me.", "내가 짐작하기로 그 집은 캐서릭 부인이 사는 집 바로 옆, 그중에서도 내 쪽에 가까운 편의 이웃집이었다."], ["The man did not hesitate a moment about the direction he should take.", "사내는 어느 쪽으로 갈지 조금도 망설이지 않았다."], ["He advanced rapidly towards the turning at which I had stopped.", "그는 내가 걸음을 멈춘 그 모퉁이를 향해 빠르게 다가왔다."], ["I recognised him as the lawyer’s clerk, who had preceded me in my visit to Blackwater Park, and who had tried to pick a quarrel with me, when I asked him if I could see the house.", "나는 그가 블랙워터 파크로 나보다 먼저 다녀갔던 그 변호사의 서기임을, 그리고 내가 저택을 볼 수 있겠느냐고 물었을 때 나에게 시비를 걸려 했던 자임을 알아보았다."]], [["I waited where I was, to ascertain whether his object was to come to close quarters and speak on this occasion.", "나는 그 자리에 선 채로, 이번에는 그가 바짝 다가와 말을 걸 작정인지 아닌지를 확인해 보려 했다."], ["To my surprise he passed on rapidly, without saying a word, without even looking up in my face as he went by.", "그런데 놀랍게도 그는 한마디도 하지 않고, 지나치면서 내 얼굴을 쳐다보지조차 않은 채 빠른 걸음으로 지나가 버렸다."], ["This was such a complete inversion of the course of proceeding which I had every reason to expect on his part, that my curiosity, or rather my suspicion, was aroused, and I determined on my side to keep him cautiously in view, and to discover what the business might be in which he was now employed.", "그것은 내가 마땅히 예상했던 그의 행동과는 완전히 정반대였으므로, 내 호기심이, 아니 오히려 내 의심이 일깨워졌다. 나 또한 그를 조심스럽게 시야에 두고, 그가 지금 무슨 일에 부려지고 있는지 알아내기로 마음먹었다."], ["Without caring whether he saw me or not, I walked after him.", "그가 나를 보든 말든 개의치 않고 나는 그의 뒤를 따라 걸었다."], ["He never looked back, and he led me straight through the streets to the railway station.", "그는 한 번도 뒤를 돌아보지 않았고, 거리를 지나 곧장 철도역까지 나를 이끌었다."]], [["The train was on the point of starting, and two or three passengers who were late were clustering round the small opening through which the tickets were issued.", "기차는 막 출발하려는 참이었고, 늦은 승객 두어 사람이 표를 파는 작은 창구 앞에 몰려 있었다."], ["I joined them, and distinctly heard the lawyer’s clerk demand a ticket for the Blackwater station.", "나는 그들 틈에 끼어들어, 변호사의 서기가 블랙워터 역까지 가는 표를 달라고 하는 소리를 똑똑히 들었다."], ["I satisfied myself that he had actually left by the train before I came away.", "나는 그가 실제로 그 기차를 타고 떠나는 것을 내 눈으로 확인한 뒤에야 그 자리를 떠났다."]], [["There was only one interpretation that I could place on what I had just seen and heard.", "방금 보고 들은 것에 대해 내가 내릴 수 있는 해석은 하나뿐이었다."], ["I had unquestionably observed the man leaving a house which closely adjoined Mrs. Catherick’s residence.", "나는 그 사내가 캐서릭 부인의 집과 바짝 붙어 있는 집에서 나오는 것을 분명히 보았다."], ["He had been probably placed there, by Sir Percival’s directions, as a lodger, in anticipation of my inquiries leading me, sooner or later, to communicate with Mrs. Catherick.", "그는 아마도 퍼시벌 경의 지시로, 내 조사가 조만간 캐서릭 부인과의 접촉으로 이어지리라 예상하고, 하숙인으로 그 집에 자리를 잡고 있었을 것이다."], ["He had doubtless seen me go in and come out, and he had hurried away by the first train to make his report at Blackwater Park, to which place Sir Percival would naturally betake himself (knowing what he evidently knew of my movements), in order to be ready on the spot, if I returned to Hampshire.", "그는 틀림없이 내가 드나드는 것을 보았고, 첫 기차로 서둘러 블랙워터 파크로 가서 보고하려는 것이었다. 퍼시벌 경은 (내 행적에 관해 그가 분명히 알고 있는 바로 미루어) 내가 햄프셔로 돌아올 경우 그 자리에서 곧바로 대응하기 위해 자연히 그곳에 가 있을 터였다."], ["Before many days were over, there seemed every likelihood now that he and I might meet.", "이제 며칠 안에 그와 내가 마주치게 될 가능성이 매우 커 보였다."]], [["Whatever result events might be destined to produce, I resolved to pursue my own course, straight to the end in view, without stopping or turning aside for Sir Percival or for any one.", "앞으로 어떤 결과가 벌어지도록 정해져 있든, 나는 퍼시벌 경을 위해서든 그 누구를 위해서든 멈추거나 옆길로 새지 않고, 내가 정한 길을 목표한 끝까지 곧장 밀고 나가기로 결심했다."], ["The great responsibility which weighed on me heavily in London—the responsibility of so guiding my slightest actions as to prevent them from leading accidentally to the discovery of Laura’s place of refuge—was removed, now that I was in Hampshire.", "런던에서 나를 무겁게 짓누르던 그 큰 책임—내 사소한 행동 하나까지도 뜻하지 않게 로라의 은신처를 드러내는 일로 이어지지 않도록 다스려야 한다는 책임—은 내가 햄프셔에 와 있는 지금은 사라졌다."], ["I could go and come as I pleased at Welmingham, and if I chanced to fail in observing any necessary precautions, the immediate results, at least, would affect no one but myself.", "웰밍엄에서라면 나는 마음대로 오갈 수 있었고, 혹시 필요한 조심을 지키지 못하는 일이 생기더라도 적어도 그 즉각적인 결과는 나 자신 말고는 아무에게도 미치지 않을 터였다."]], [["When I left the station the winter evening was beginning to close in.", "역을 나섰을 때 겨울 저녁이 어둑어둑 내려앉기 시작하고 있었다."], ["There was little hope of continuing my inquiries after dark to any useful purpose in a neighbourhood that was strange to me.", "낯선 고장에서 어두워진 뒤에 조사를 이어 간들 쓸 만한 성과를 얻으리라는 희망은 거의 없었다."], ["Accordingly, I made my way to the nearest hotel, and ordered my dinner and my bed.", "그래서 나는 가장 가까운 여관으로 찾아가 저녁 식사와 잠자리를 청했다."], ["This done, I wrote to Marian, to tell her that I was safe and well, and that I had fair prospects of success.", "그러고 나서 매리언에게 편지를 써서, 나는 무사하고 건강하며 성공의 전망도 괜찮다고 알렸다."], ["I had directed her, on leaving home, to address the first letter she wrote to me (the letter I expected to receive the next morning) to “The Post-Office, Welmingham,” and I now begged her to send her second day’s letter to the same address.", "집을 떠나올 때 나는 그녀에게 첫 편지(다음 날 아침에 받아 보리라 기대하던 편지)를 “웰밍엄 우체국”으로 부치라고 일러 두었는데, 이제 이틀째 편지도 같은 주소로 보내 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["I could easily receive it by writing to the postmaster if I happened to be away from the town when it arrived.", "편지가 도착할 무렵 내가 마침 이 도시를 떠나 있게 되더라도, 우체국장에게 편지를 써 두면 어렵지 않게 받아 볼 수 있을 터였다."]], [["The coffee-room of the hotel, as it grew late in the evening, became a perfect solitude.", "여관의 다실은 밤이 깊어 가면서 완전히 텅 비었다."], ["I was left to reflect on what I had accomplished that afternoon as uninterruptedly as if the house had been my own.", "나는 마치 그 집이 내 집이기라도 한 듯 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 그날 오후에 이룬 일을 곱씹을 수 있었다."], ["Before I retired to rest I had attentively thought over my extraordinary interview with Mrs. Catherick from beginning to end, and had verified at my leisure the conclusions which I had hastily drawn in the earlier part of the day.", "잠자리에 들기 전에 나는 캐서릭 부인과의 그 기이한 면담을 처음부터 끝까지 찬찬히 되짚어 보았고, 낮에 성급하게 내렸던 결론들을 느긋하게 검토해 확인했다."]], [["The vestry of Old Welmingham church was the starting-point from which my mind slowly worked its way back through all that I had heard Mrs. Catherick say, and through all I had seen Mrs. Catherick do.", "구(舊)웰밍엄 교회의 성구실은 내 생각이 캐서릭 부인이 하는 말로 들은 모든 것, 캐서릭 부인이 하는 것으로 본 모든 것을 천천히 되짚어 나가는 출발점이었다."]], [["At the time when the neighbourhood of the vestry was first referred to in my presence by Mrs. Clements, I had thought it the strangest and most unaccountable of all places for Sir Percival to select for a clandestine meeting with the clerk’s wife.", "클레멘츠 부인이 내 앞에서 성구실 근처를 처음 입에 올렸을 때, 나는 퍼시벌 경이 교회 서기의 아내와 은밀히 만나기로 고른 장소치고 그보다 더 이상하고 더 이해할 수 없는 곳은 없다고 생각했다."], ["Influenced by this impression, and by no other, I had mentioned “the vestry of the church” before Mrs.", "다른 이유가 아니라 바로 그 인상에 이끌려, 나는 캐서릭 부인 앞에서 순전히 넘겨짚어 “교회의 성구실”이라는 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Catherick on pure speculation—it represented one of the minor peculiarities of the story which occurred to me while I was speaking.", "그것은 말하는 도중에 문득 떠오른, 그 이야기의 사소한 특이점 가운데 하나였을 뿐이다."], ["I was prepared for her answering me confusedly or angrily, but the blank terror that seized her when I said the words took me completely by surprise.", "나는 그녀가 당황해서, 혹은 화를 내며 대답하리라 각오하고 있었지만, 그 말을 했을 때 그녀를 사로잡은 그 새하얀 공포는 나를 완전히 허를 찌르듯 놀라게 했다."], ["I had long before associated Sir Percival’s Secret with the concealment of a serious crime which Mrs. Catherick knew of, but I had gone no further than this.", "나는 오래전부터 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을, 캐서릭 부인이 알고 있는 어떤 중대한 범죄를 감추는 일과 연결 지어 생각해 왔지만, 그 이상은 나아가지 못하고 있었다."], ["Now the woman’s paroxysm of terror associated the crime, either directly or indirectly, with the vestry, and convinced me that she had been more than the mere witness of it—she was also the accomplice, beyond a doubt.", "그런데 이제 그 여자의 발작적인 공포가 그 범죄를 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 성구실과 결부시켰고, 그녀가 단순한 목격자 이상이었음을—의심할 여지 없이 공범이기도 했음을 나에게 확신시켰다."]], [["What had been the nature of the crime?", "그 범죄의 성격은 어떤 것이었을까?"], ["Surely there was a contemptible side to it, as well as a dangerous side, or Mrs.", "거기에는 위험한 면뿐 아니라 경멸스러운 면도 분명히 있었을 것이다."], ["Catherick would not have repeated my own words, referring to Sir Percival’s rank and power, with such marked disdain as she had certainly displayed.", "그렇지 않고서야 캐서릭 부인이 퍼시벌 경의 지위와 권세를 두고 내가 한 말을, 그토록 뚜렷한 멸시를 담아 되풀이했을 리가 없다."], ["It was a contemptible crime then and a dangerous crime, and she had shared in it, and it was associated with the vestry of the church.", "그러니까 그것은 경멸스러운 범죄인 동시에 위험한 범죄였고, 그녀도 거기에 한몫 끼었으며, 교회의 성구실과 얽혀 있었다."]], [["The next consideration to be disposed of led me a step farther from this point.", "다음으로 정리해야 할 문제는 나를 이 지점에서 한 걸음 더 나아가게 했다."]], [["Mrs. Catherick’s undisguised contempt for Sir Percival plainly extended to his mother as well.", "퍼시벌 경에 대한 캐서릭 부인의 노골적인 경멸은 명백히 그의 어머니에게까지 미치고 있었다."], ["She had referred with the bitterest sarcasm to the great family he had descended from—“especially by the mother’s side.”", "그녀는 그가 물려받은 그 대단한 가문을 두고 “특히 어머니 쪽으로 말이지요”라며 더없이 신랄한 빈정거림을 던졌다."], ["What did this mean?", "그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까?"]], [["There appeared to be only two explanations of it.", "거기에는 두 가지 설명밖에 없어 보였다."], ["Either his mother’s birth had been low, or his mother’s reputation was damaged by some hidden flaw with which Mrs. Catherick and Sir Percival were both privately acquainted?", "그의 어머니의 출신이 미천했거나, 아니면 캐서릭 부인과 퍼시벌 경이 둘 다 남몰래 알고 있는 어떤 숨은 흠 때문에 그의 어머니의 평판이 훼손되어 있거나 둘 중 하나였다."], ["I could only put the first explanation to the test by looking at the register of her marriage, and so ascertaining her maiden name and her parentage as a preliminary to further inquiries.", "첫 번째 설명은 그녀의 혼인 등록부를 살펴보아 처녀 적 이름과 친정 내력을 확인하는 것으로만 검증할 수 있었고, 그것이 더 깊은 조사로 나아가는 첫걸음이 될 터였다."]], [["On the other hand, if the second case supposed were the true one, what had been the flaw in her reputation?", "한편, 만일 두 번째로 가정한 경우가 참이라면, 그녀의 평판에 있던 흠이란 무엇이었을까?"], ["Remembering the account which Marian had given me of Sir Percival’s father and mother, and of the suspiciously unsocial secluded life they had both led, I now asked myself whether it might not be possible that his mother had never been married at all.", "매리언이 나에게 들려주었던 퍼시벌 경의 아버지와 어머니 이야기, 그리고 두 사람이 함께 지냈다는 그 수상쩍게 비사교적이고 폐쇄적인 생활을 떠올리며, 나는 이제 그의 어머니가 애초에 혼인을 한 적이 아예 없었을 가능성은 없는지 스스로에게 물었다."], ["Here again the register might, by offering written evidence of the marriage, prove to me, at any rate, that this doubt had no foundation in truth.", "여기서도 등록부는 혼인의 문서 증거를 보여 줌으로써, 적어도 이 의혹이 사실에 아무 근거가 없음을 나에게 증명해 줄 수 있을 터였다."], ["But where was the register to be found?", "그런데 그 등록부는 어디에 있을까?"], ["At this point I took up the conclusions which I had previously formed, and the same mental process which had discovered the locality of the concealed crime, now lodged the register also in the vestry of Old Welmingham church.", "이 대목에서 나는 앞서 세워 둔 결론들을 다시 집어 들었고, 감추어진 범죄의 장소를 찾아냈던 것과 똑같은 사고의 과정이 이제 등록부 역시 구웰밍엄 교회의 성구실에 놓아두었다."]], [["These were the results of my interview with Mrs.", "이것이 캐서릭 부인과의 면담에서 얻은 결과였다."], ["Catherick—these were the various considerations, all steadily converging to one point, which decided the course of my proceedings on the next day.", "그리고 이것이, 모두가 한 지점으로 꾸준히 모여들며 이튿날 내가 나아갈 길을 결정지은 여러 고찰이었다."]], [["The morning was cloudy and lowering, but no rain fell.", "아침은 구름이 끼고 잔뜩 찌푸렸으나 비는 내리지 않았다."], ["I left my bag at the hotel to wait there till I called for it, and, after inquiring the way, set forth on foot for Old Welmingham church.", "나는 가방을 여관에 맡겨 두고 찾으러 올 때까지 보관해 달라 이른 뒤, 길을 물어 걸어서 구웰밍엄 교회로 나섰다."]], [["It was a walk of rather more than two miles, the ground rising slowly all the way.", "두 마일이 조금 넘는 길이었고, 지면은 내내 완만하게 높아졌다."]], [["On the highest point stood the church—an ancient, weather-beaten building, with heavy buttresses at its sides, and a clumsy square tower in front.", "가장 높은 지점에 교회가 서 있었다. 옆구리에 육중한 버팀벽을 두르고 앞쪽에는 투박한 사각 종탑을 세운, 오래되어 비바람에 시달린 건물이었다."], ["The vestry at the back was built out from the church, and seemed to be of the same age.", "뒤편의 성구실은 교회에서 내밀어 지은 것으로, 같은 시대의 것으로 보였다."], ["Round the building at intervals appeared the remains of the village which Mrs.", "건물 둘레 여기저기에는 클레멘츠 부인이 예전에 자기 남편이 살던 곳이라고 나에게 말해 준 그 마을의 잔해가 남아 있었다."], ["Clements had described to me as her husband’s place of abode in former years, and which the principal inhabitants had long since deserted for the new town.", "주요 주민들은 오래전에 그곳을 버리고 새 도시로 옮겨 간 뒤였다."], ["Some of the empty houses had been dismantled to their outer walls, some had been left to decay with time, and some were still inhabited by persons evidently of the poorest class.", "빈집들 가운데 더러는 바깥벽만 남기고 헐렸고, 더러는 세월에 그대로 삭아 가도록 버려졌으며, 더러는 아직도 누가 보아도 가장 가난한 계층의 사람들이 살고 있었다."], ["It was a dreary scene, and yet, in the worst aspect of its ruin, not so dreary as the modern town that I had just left.", "을씨년스러운 풍경이었으나, 그 폐허의 가장 흉한 모습조차도 내가 방금 떠나온 그 현대적인 도시만큼 을씨년스럽지는 않았다."], ["Here there was the brown, breezy sweep of surrounding fields for the eye to repose on—here the trees, leafless as they were, still varied the monotony of the prospect, and helped the mind to look forward to summer-time and shade.", "여기에는 눈을 쉬게 해 줄, 갈색으로 바람이 쓸고 가는 들판이 사방으로 펼쳐져 있었다. 여기에는 잎을 다 떨군 채로나마 단조로운 경치에 변화를 주고, 여름날과 그늘을 내다보게 마음을 도와주는 나무들이 있었다."]], [["As I moved away from the back of the church, and passed some of the dismantled cottages in search of a person who might direct me to the clerk, I saw two men saunter out after me from behind a wall.", "교회 뒤편에서 물러나 나와서, 서기가 있는 곳을 일러 줄 만한 사람을 찾아 헐린 오두막들 사이를 지나가는데, 두 사내가 담장 뒤에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나를 뒤따라 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["The tallest of the two—a stout muscular man in the dress of a gamekeeper—was a stranger to me.", "둘 가운데 키가 큰 쪽—사냥터지기 차림의 다부지고 근육질인 사내—은 나에게 낯선 자였다."], ["The other was one of the men who had followed me in London on the day when I left Mr. Kyrle’s office.", "다른 하나는 내가 카일 씨의 사무실을 나서던 날 런던에서 나를 미행했던 사내들 가운데 하나였다."], ["I had taken particular notice of him at the time; and I felt sure that I was not mistaken in identifying the fellow on this occasion.", "나는 그때 그를 특히 눈여겨 두었기에, 이번에도 그 작자를 알아본 것이 틀림없다고 확신했다."]], [["Neither he nor his companion attempted to speak to me, and both kept themselves at a respectful distance, but the motive of their presence in the neighbourhood of the church was plainly apparent.", "그도 그의 동행도 나에게 말을 걸려 하지 않았고, 둘 다 조심스레 거리를 두었지만, 그들이 교회 근처에 나타난 까닭은 뻔히 드러나 있었다. 내가 짐작한 그대로였다."], ["It was exactly as I had supposed—Sir Percival was already prepared for me.", "퍼시벌 경은 이미 나를 맞을 준비를 갖추고 있었던 것이다."], ["My visit to Mrs. Catherick had been reported to him the evening before, and those two men had been placed on the look-out near the church in anticipation of my appearance at Old Welmingham.", "내가 캐서릭 부인을 찾아간 일은 전날 저녁에 이미 그에게 보고되었고, 그 두 사내는 내가 구웰밍엄에 나타날 것을 예상하고 교회 부근에 파수꾼으로 배치되어 있었다."], ["If I had wanted any further proof that my investigations had taken the right direction at last, the plan now adopted for watching me would have supplied it.", "내 조사가 마침내 옳은 방향을 잡았다는 증거가 더 필요했다면, 나를 감시하려고 지금 동원된 이 계략이 바로 그것을 대 주었을 것이다."]], [["I walked on away from the church till I reached one of the inhabited houses, with a patch of kitchen garden attached to it on which a labourer was at work.", "나는 교회를 벗어나 계속 걸어, 사람이 사는 집 하나에 이르렀다. 딸린 텃밭 한 뙈기에서 일꾼 하나가 일을 하고 있었다."], ["He directed me to the clerk’s abode, a cottage at some little distance off, standing by itself on the outskirts of the forsaken village.", "그는 서기가 사는 곳을 일러 주었는데, 조금 떨어진 곳, 버려진 마을 변두리에 홀로 서 있는 오두막이었다."], ["The clerk was indoors, and was just putting on his greatcoat.", "서기는 집 안에 있었고, 마침 외투를 걸치던 참이었다."], ["He was a cheerful, familiar, loudly-talkative old man, with a very poor opinion (as I soon discovered) of the place in which he lived, and a happy sense of superiority to his neighbours in virtue of the great personal distinction of having once been in London.", "그는 쾌활하고 스스럼없으며 목청 좋게 말이 많은 노인으로, (내가 곧 알게 되었듯이) 자기가 사는 고장을 아주 하찮게 여기면서, 한때 런던에 가 보았다는 대단한 개인적 영예 덕분에 이웃들보다 우월하다는 흐뭇한 자부심을 품고 있었다."]], [["“It’s well you came so early, sir,”", "“일찍 오셔서 다행이올시다, 선생님.”"], ["said the old man, when I had mentioned the object of my visit.", "내가 찾아온 용건을 말하자 노인이 말했다."], ["“I should have been away in ten minutes more.", "“십 분만 늦었어도 저는 나가고 없었을 겝니다."], ["Parish business, sir, and a goodish long trot before it’s all done for a man at my age.", "교구 일이지요, 선생님. 제 나이에는 다 마치자면 꽤나 오래 종종걸음을 쳐야 하는 일입니다."], ["But, bless you, I’m strong on my legs still!", "하지만요, 저는 아직 다리 하나는 튼튼하답니다!"], ["As long as a man don’t give at his legs, there’s a deal of work left in him.", "사람이란 다리만 안 무너지면 아직 할 일이 잔뜩 남아 있는 법이지요."], ["Don’t you think so yourself, sir?”", "선생님도 그리 생각지 않으십니까?”"]], [["He took his keys down while he was talking from a hook behind the fireplace, and locked his cottage door behind us.", "그는 이야기하는 동안 벽난로 뒤 고리에서 열쇠를 내려 들더니, 우리가 나온 뒤 오두막 문을 잠갔다."]], [["“Nobody at home to keep house for me,”", "“집을 봐 줄 사람이 하나도 없어놔서요.”"], ["said the clerk, with a cheerful sense of perfect freedom from all family encumbrances.", "서기가 온갖 식솔의 짐에서 완전히 벗어난 자의 쾌활한 홀가분함으로 말했다."], ["“My wife’s in the churchyard there, and my children are all married.", "“제 안사람은 저기 교회 묘지에 누웠고, 자식들은 죄다 짝을 지어 나갔지요."], ["A wretched place this, isn’t it, sir?", "참 형편없는 데지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?"], ["But the parish is a large one—every man couldn’t get through the business as I do.", "하지만 교구는 넓습니다. 아무나 저처럼 일을 해낼 수 있는 게 아니에요."], ["It’s learning does it, and I’ve had my share, and a little more.", "그게 다 배움 덕이지요. 저는 배울 만큼 배웠고, 조금 더 배웠습죠."], ["I can talk the Queen’s English (God bless the Queen!), and that’s more than most of the people about here can do.", "저는 여왕님의 영어를 할 줄 압니다(여왕님께 축복이 있기를!). 이 근방 사람들 태반은 못 하는 일이지요."], ["You’re from London, I suppose, sir?", "선생님은 런던서 오셨지요, 아마?"], ["I’ve been in London a matter of five-and-twenty year ago.", "저도 런던에 가 봤습니다, 한 이십오 년쯤 전에요."], ["What’s the news there now, if you please?”", "요새 거기 소식은 어떻습니까, 여쭈어도 될는지요?”"]], [["Chattering on in this way, he led me back to the vestry.", "이런 식으로 쉴 새 없이 지껄이며 그는 나를 성구실로 데려갔다."], ["I looked about to see if the two spies were still in sight.", "나는 그 두 밀정이 아직 보이는지 둘러보았다."], ["They were not visible anywhere.", "어디에도 보이지 않았다."], ["After having discovered my application to the clerk, they had probably concealed themselves where they could watch my next proceedings in perfect freedom.", "내가 서기를 찾아간 것을 알아낸 뒤로, 그들은 아마 내 다음 행동을 마음 놓고 지켜볼 수 있는 곳에 몸을 숨겼을 것이다."]], [["The vestry door was of stout old oak, studded with strong nails, and the clerk put his large heavy key into the lock with the air of a man who knew that he had a difficulty to encounter, and who was not quite certain of creditably conquering it.", "성구실 문은 튼튼한 못을 촘촘히 박은 두꺼운 참나무 문이었다. 서기는 크고 묵직한 열쇠를 자물쇠에 꽂았는데, 만만찮은 상대를 만나리라는 것을 알면서도 이번에도 무사히 이겨 낼지는 썩 자신이 없는 사람의 태도였다."]], [["“I’m obliged to bring you this way, sir,”", "“이쪽으로 모실 수밖에 없습니다, 선생님.”"], ["he said, “because the door from the vestry to the church is bolted on the vestry side.", "그가 말했다. “성구실에서 예배당으로 통하는 문은 성구실 쪽에서 빗장이 걸려 있어서요."], ["We might have got in through the church otherwise.", "아니면 예배당을 통해 들어갔을 겝니다."], ["This is a perverse lock, if ever there was one yet.", "이건 세상에 둘도 없이 심술궂은 자물쇠지요."], ["It’s big enough for a prison-door—it’s been hampered over and over again, and it ought to be changed for a new one.", "감옥 문에나 어울릴 만큼 커다랗고, 하도 여러 번 성가시게 굴어 왔으니 새것으로 바꿔야 마땅합니다."], ["I’ve mentioned that to the churchwarden fifty times over at least—he’s always saying, ‘I’ll see about it’—and he never does see.", "제가 교구 위원한테 그 말을 적어도 쉰 번은 했지요. 그이는 늘 ‘내가 알아보지’ 하고는, 도무지 알아보는 법이 없어요."], ["Ah, it’s a sort of lost corner, this place.", "아이고, 여기는 세상에서 잊힌 구석이올시다."], ["Not like London—is it, sir?", "런던하고는 다르지요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?"], ["Bless you, we are all asleep here!", "참말이지, 여기서는 다들 잠들어 있습니다!"], ["We don’t march with the times.”", "우리는 시대를 따라 걷지를 않아요.”"]], [["After some twisting and turning of the key, the heavy lock yielded, and he opened the door.", "열쇠를 이리저리 비틀고 돌린 끝에 그 육중한 자물쇠가 풀렸고, 그는 문을 열었다."]], [["The vestry was larger than I should have supposed it to be, judging from the outside only.", "성구실은 바깥에서만 보고 짐작했던 것보다 넓었다."], ["It was a dim, mouldy, melancholy old room, with a low, raftered ceiling.", "서까래가 드러난 낮은 천장을 인, 어둑하고 곰팡내 나는 음울한 옛 방이었다."], ["Round two sides of it, the sides nearest to the interior of the church, ran heavy wooden presses, worm-eaten and gaping with age.", "예배당 안쪽에 가까운 두 면에는 세월에 좀먹고 틈이 벌어진 묵직한 나무 궤장이 죽 늘어서 있었다."], ["Hooked to the inner corner of one of these presses hung several surplices, all bulging out at their lower ends in an irreverent-looking bundle of limp drapery.", "그중 한 궤장의 안쪽 모서리에 걸린 고리에는 성직자 예복 여러 벌이 걸려 있었는데, 하나같이 아랫자락이 불룩하게 부풀어 올라 축 늘어진 천 뭉치처럼 불경스러워 보였다."], ["Below the surplices, on the floor, stood three packing-cases, with the lids half off, half on, and the straw profusely bursting out of their cracks and crevices in every direction.", "예복 아래 바닥에는 포장 상자 세 개가 뚜껑을 반은 덮고 반은 벗긴 채 놓여 있었고, 갈라진 틈이란 틈마다 짚이 사방으로 잔뜩 삐져나와 있었다."], ["Behind them, in a corner, was a litter of dusty papers, some large and rolled up like architects’ plans, some loosely strung together on files like bills or letters.", "그 뒤 구석에는 먼지 앉은 서류 뭉치가 어지러이 쌓여 있었는데, 더러는 커다랗게 건축가의 도면처럼 말려 있었고, 더러는 청구서나 편지처럼 서류철에 헐겁게 꿰여 있었다."], ["The room had once been lighted by a small side window, but this had been bricked up, and a lantern skylight was now substituted for it.", "이 방은 한때 작은 옆창으로 빛을 들였으나 그 창은 벽돌로 막혀 버렸고, 지금은 그 대신 채광창이 나 있었다."], ["The atmosphere of the place was heavy and mouldy, being rendered additionally oppressive by the closing of the door which led into the church.", "방 안의 공기는 무겁고 곰팡내가 났으며, 예배당으로 통하는 문이 닫혀 있어 답답함이 한층 더했다."], ["This door also was composed of solid oak, and was bolted at the top and bottom on the vestry side.", "그 문 역시 단단한 참나무로 짜였고, 성구실 쪽에서 위아래로 빗장이 걸려 있었다."]], [["“We might be tidier, mightn’t we, sir?”", "“좀 더 말끔했으면 좋았을 텐데요, 안 그렇습니까, 선생님?”"], ["said the cheerful clerk; “but when you’re in a lost corner of a place like this, what are you to do?", "쾌활한 서기가 말했다. “하지만 이런 잊힌 구석에 처박혀 있으면 무슨 수가 있겠습니까?"], ["Why, look here now, just look at these packing-cases.", "아, 여기 좀 보십시오, 이 포장 상자들 좀 보시라고요."], ["There they’ve been, for a year or more, ready to go down to London—there they are, littering the place, and there they’ll stop as long as the nails hold them together.", "저것들이 저기 저러고 있은 지가 일 년이 넘습니다, 런던으로 내려보낼 채비를 다 갖춘 채로요. 저기 저렇게 자리만 어지럽히고 있지요."], ["I’ll tell you what, sir, as I said before, this is not London.", "못이 저것들을 붙들고 있는 한 저기 그대로 있을 겝니다. 제가 말씀드리지 않았습니까, 선생님, 여기는 런던이 아니라고요."], ["We are all asleep here.", "여기서는 다들 잠들어 있습니다."], ["Bless you, WE don’t march with the times!”", "참말이지, 우리는 시대를 따라 걷지를 않아요!”"]], [["“What is there in the packing-cases?”", "“그 상자들 안에는 무엇이 들었습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Bits of old wood carvings from the pulpit, and panels from the chancel, and images from the organ-loft,”", "“설교단에서 떼어 낸 낡은 목조 조각들이랑, 성단소에서 뜯어낸 판벽이랑, 오르간 다락에서 내린 조각상들입지요.”"], ["said the clerk.", "서기가 말했다."], ["“Portraits of the twelve apostles in wood, and not a whole nose among ’em.", "“나무로 새긴 열두 사도의 상인데, 그중에 코가 성한 놈이 하나도 없습니다."], ["All broken, and worm-eaten, and crumbling to dust at the edges.", "죄다 부서지고 좀먹어서 가장자리는 바스러져 가루가 되고 있지요."], ["As brittle as crockery, sir, and as old as the church, if not older.”", "사기그릇처럼 잘 깨지는 데다, 교회만큼이나 오래된 것들입니다. 어쩌면 교회보다 더 오래됐을지도요.”"]], [["“And why were they going to London?", "“그것들이 런던으로 갈 일은 왜 생긴 겁니까?"], ["To be repaired?”", "수리하려고요?”"]], [["“That’s it, sir, to be repaired, and where they were past repair, to be copied in sound wood.", "“그렇습지요, 선생님, 수리하려고요. 그리고 손댈 수 없을 만큼 상한 것은 성한 나무로 본떠 새로 만들려고요."], ["But, bless you, the money fell short, and there they are, waiting for new subscriptions, and nobody to subscribe.", "그런데 어쩌겠습니까, 돈이 모자라서 저것들이 저기 저렇게 새 기부금을 기다리고 있는데, 기부하는 사람이 없습니다."], ["It was all done a year ago, sir.", "다 일 년 전 일이지요, 선생님."], ["Six gentlemen dined together about it, at the hotel in the new town.", "신사분 여섯이 그 일로 새 도시의 여관에서 함께 만찬을 드셨습니다. 연설을 하고, 결의를 통과시키고, 이름을 적어 넣고, 취지서를 수천 장 찍어 냈지요."], ["They made speeches, and passed resolutions, and put their names down, and printed off thousands of prospectuses.", "아주 훌륭한 취지서였습니다, 선생님. 붉은 잉크로 고딕 문양을 잔뜩 둘러 놓고, 교회를 복원하지 않고 그 이름난 조각들을 손보지 않는 것은 수치라는 둥 하는 말들을 적어 놓았지요."], ["Beautiful prospectuses, sir, all flourished over with Gothic devices in red ink, saying it was a disgrace not to restore the church and repair the famous carvings, and so on.", "그때 다 나눠 주지 못한 취지서들이랑, 건축가의 도면과 견적서랑, 모두를 서로 으르렁대게 만들어 결국 다툼으로 끝나 버린 편지 뭉치가 몽땅 저기 저 구석, 포장 상자들 뒤에 처박혀 있습니다. 처음에는 돈이 조금씩 들어왔지요."], ["There are the prospectuses that couldn’t be distributed, and the architect’s plans and estimates, and the whole correspondence which set everybody at loggerheads and ended in a dispute, all down together in that corner, behind the packing-cases.", "하지만 런던 바깥에서 무얼 기대할 수 있겠습니까? 부서진 조각들을 포장하고, 견적을 뽑고, 인쇄소 값을 치를 만큼만 겨우 됐고, 그러고 나니 반 페니도 남지 않았습니다."], ["The money dribbled in a little at first—but what CAN you expect out of London?", "그래서 그것들이 저기 저러고 있는 겝니다, 아까 말씀드린 대로요."], ["There was just enough, you know, to pack the broken carvings, and get the estimates, and pay the printer’s bill, and after that there wasn’t a halfpenny left.", "달리 둘 데가 없어요. 새 도시에서는 아무도 우리를 거들어 줄 마음이 없고, 우리는 잊힌 구석에 있고, 그러니 성구실이 어수선한 겝니다."], ["There the things are, as I said before.", "누구더러 어쩌란 말입니까?"], ["We have nowhere else to put them—nobody in the new town cares about accommodating us—we’re in a lost corner—and this is an untidy vestry—and who’s to help it?—that’s what I want to know.”", "저야말로 그게 알고 싶습니다.”"]], [["My anxiety to examine the register did not dispose me to offer much encouragement to the old man’s talkativeness.", "등록부를 살펴보고 싶은 조바심 탓에 나는 이 노인의 수다에 별로 장단을 맞춰 줄 기분이 아니었다."], ["I agreed with him that nobody could help the untidiness of the vestry, and then suggested that we should proceed to our business without more delay.", "나는 성구실이 어수선한 것을 누구도 어쩔 수 없다는 데 동의한 뒤, 더 지체하지 말고 우리 용건으로 넘어가자고 제안했다."]], [["“Ay, ay, the marriage-register, to be sure,”", "“암요, 암요, 혼인 등록부지요, 물론입니다.”"], ["said the clerk, taking a little bunch of keys from his pocket.", "서기가 주머니에서 작은 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["“How far do you want to look back, sir?”", "“얼마나 옛날까지 거슬러 보시려는 겝니까, 선생님?”"]], [["Marian had informed me of Sir Percival’s age at the time when we had spoken together of his marriage engagement with Laura.", "매리언은 로라와 퍼시벌 경의 약혼에 대해 나와 이야기를 나누던 때에 그의 나이를 알려 준 적이 있었다."], ["She had then described him as being forty-five years old.", "그때 그녀는 그를 마흔다섯 살이라고 했다."], ["Calculating back from this, and making due allowance for the year that had passed since I had gained my information, I found that he must have been born in eighteen hundred and four, and that I might safely start on my search through the register from that date.", "거기서 거꾸로 셈해 보고, 내가 그 이야기를 들은 뒤로 흘러간 한 해를 마땅히 감안하니, 그는 1804년에 태어났음이 틀림없었고 나는 그 해부터 등록부를 뒤지기 시작하면 안전하겠다는 결론이 나왔다."]], [["“I want to begin with the year eighteen hundred and four,”", "“1804년부터 시작하고 싶습니다.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Which way after that, sir?”", "“그다음은 어느 쪽으로 갑니까, 선생님?”"], ["asked the clerk.", "서기가 물었다."], ["“Forwards to our time or backwards away from us?”", "“우리 시대 쪽으로 내려옵니까, 아니면 거꾸로 거슬러 올라갑니까?”"]], [["“Backwards from eighteen hundred and four.”", "“1804년에서 거꾸로 거슬러 갑니다.”"]], [["He opened the door of one of the presses—the press from the side of which the surplices were hanging—and produced a large volume bound in greasy brown leather.", "그는 궤장 하나의 문을 열었다. 옆면에 성직자 예복이 걸려 있던 바로 그 궤장이었다."], ["I was struck by the insecurity of the place in which the register was kept.", "그러고는 기름때 낀 갈색 가죽으로 장정한 커다란 책 한 권을 꺼냈다."], ["The door of the press was warped and cracked with age, and the lock was of the smallest and commonest kind.", "나는 등록부가 보관된 자리가 허술한 데 놀랐다. 궤장 문은 세월에 뒤틀리고 갈라져 있었고, 자물쇠는 가장 작고 가장 흔한 종류였다."], ["I could have forced it easily with the walking-stick I carried in my hand.", "내가 손에 들고 있던 지팡이로도 쉽게 부술 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["“Is that considered a sufficiently secure place for the register?”", "“이곳이 등록부를 두기에 충분히 안전한 자리로 여겨집니까?”"], ["I inquired.", "나는 물었다."], ["“Surely a book of such importance as this ought to be protected by a better lock, and kept carefully in an iron safe?”", "“이만큼 중요한 책이라면 더 좋은 자물쇠로 지키고, 쇠금고에 조심스레 넣어 두어야 하지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["“Well, now, that’s curious!”", "“아니, 이런, 그거 참 신통한 말씀이십니다!”"], ["said the clerk, shutting up the book again, just after he had opened it, and smacking his hand cheerfully on the cover.", "서기가 책을 펼치자마자 도로 덮고는 표지를 쾌활하게 탁 치며 말했다."], ["“Those were the very words my old master was always saying years and years ago, when I was a lad.", "“여러 해 전에, 제가 아직 애송이였을 적에 제 옛 주인 어르신이 늘 하시던 말씀이 바로 그것이었지요."], ["‘Why isn’t the register’ (meaning this register here, under my hand)—‘why isn’t it kept in an iron safe?’ If I’ve heard him say that once, I’ve heard him say it a hundred times.", "‘어째서 이 등록부를’(제 손 밑에 있는 바로 이 등록부 말입니다) ‘어째서 이걸 쇠금고에 넣어 두지 않는 게냐?’ 제가 그 말씀을 한 번 들었으면 백 번은 들었습니다."], ["He was the solicitor in those days, sir, who had the appointment of vestry-clerk to this church.", "그 어른은 당시 이 교회의 성구실 서기 자리를 맡고 계시던 사무 변호사셨습니다."], ["A fine hearty old gentleman, and the most particular man breathing.", "훌륭하고 정정한 노신사이자, 세상에 둘도 없이 꼼꼼한 분이셨지요."], ["As long as he lived he kept a copy of this book in his office at Knowlesbury, and had it posted up regular, from time to time, to correspond with the fresh entries here.", "살아 계신 동안 내내 그 어른은 놀즈베리의 사무실에 이 책의 사본을 두고, 여기 새로 오르는 기재 사항과 맞도록 때때로 어김없이 적어 넣게 하셨습니다."], ["You would hardly think it, but he had his own appointed days, once or twice in every quarter, for riding over to this church on his old white pony, to check the copy, by the register, with his own eyes and hands.", "믿기 어려우시겠지만, 그 어른은 분기마다 한두 번씩 날을 따로 정해 두고, 늙은 백마 조랑말을 타고 이 교회까지 오셔서 당신 눈과 당신 손으로 등록부와 사본을 대조하셨지요."], ["‘How do I know?’ (he used to say) ‘how do I know that the register in this vestry may not be stolen or destroyed?", "‘내가 어찌 알겠느냐?’ 하고 늘 말씀하셨습니다. ‘이 성구실의 등록부가 도둑맞거나 없어지지 않으리라고 내가 어찌 알겠느냐?"], ["Why isn’t it kept in an iron safe?", "어째서 이걸 쇠금고에 넣지 않는 게냐?"], ["Why can’t I make other people as careful as I am myself?", "어째서 나는 남들을 나만큼 조심스럽게 만들 수가 없단 말이냐? 언젠가는 사고가 나고 말 게다."], ["Some of these days there will be an accident happen, and when the register’s lost, then the parish will find out the value of my copy.’ He used to take his pinch of snuff after that, and look about him as bold as a lord.", "등록부를 잃고 나서야 이 교구는 내 사본의 값어치를 알게 될 게야.’ 그러고 나면 어른은 코담배를 한 줌 집어 들고, 영주님처럼 당당하게 사방을 둘러보시곤 했지요. 아이고!"], ["Ah! the like of him for doing business isn’t easy to find now.", "일 처리에서 그 어른 같은 분은 요새 쉬 찾아볼 수 없습니다."], ["You may go to London and not match him, even THERE.", "런던에 가신들 그 어른만 한 분은 못 만나실 겁니다, 거기서도 말이지요."], ["Which year did you say, sir?", "몇 년이라고 하셨습니까, 선생님?"], ["Eighteen hundred and what?”", "1800 몇 년입니까?”"]], [["“Eighteen hundred and four,”", "“1804년입니다.”"], ["I replied, mentally resolving to give the old man no more opportunities of talking, until my examination of the register was over.", "나는 대답하면서, 등록부 조사를 마칠 때까지는 이 노인에게 더 이상 이야기할 기회를 주지 않으리라 속으로 다짐했다."]], [["The clerk put on his spectacles, and turned over the leaves of the register, carefully wetting his finger and thumb at every third page.", "서기는 안경을 쓰고, 세 장마다 손가락과 엄지에 조심스레 침을 발라 가며 등록부를 넘겼다. “여기 있습니다, 선생님.”"], ["“There it is, sir,” said he, with another cheerful smack on the open volume.", "그가 펼쳐진 책을 또 한 번 쾌활하게 탁 치며 말했다."], ["“There’s the year you want.”", "“찾으시는 해가 여기 있습니다.”"]], [["As I was ignorant of the month in which Sir Percival was born, I began my backward search with the early part of the year.", "퍼시벌 경이 몇 월에 태어났는지 몰랐으므로, 나는 그해 초부터 거슬러 올라가는 조사를 시작했다."], ["The register-book was of the old-fashioned kind, the entries being all made on blank pages in manuscript, and the divisions which separated them being indicated by ink lines drawn across the page at the close of each entry.", "등록부 책은 옛날식이어서, 기재 사항이 모두 빈 종이에 손으로 적혀 있었고, 각 항목이 끝나는 자리에 잉크로 가로줄을 그어 서로를 구분해 놓았다."]], [["I reached the beginning of the year eighteen hundred and four without encountering the marriage, and then travelled back through December eighteen hundred and three—through November and October—through——", "나는 그 혼인 기록을 만나지 못한 채 1804년의 첫머리에 이르렀고, 그다음에는 1803년 12월을 지나 거슬러 올라갔다. 11월과 10월을 지나고, 다시—"]], [["No!", "아니!"], ["not through September also.", "9월까지 지나가지는 않았다."], ["Under the heading of that month in the year I found the marriage.", "그해 그달의 표제 아래에서 나는 그 혼인 기록을 찾아냈다."]], [["I looked carefully at the entry.", "나는 그 항목을 찬찬히 들여다보았다."], ["It was at the bottom of a page, and was for want of room compressed into a smaller space than that occupied by the marriages above.", "그것은 한 면의 맨 아래에 있었고, 자리가 모자란 탓에 위쪽 혼인들이 차지한 것보다 좁은 공간에 눌러 적혀 있었다."], ["The marriage immediately before it was impressed on my attention by the circumstance of the bridegroom’s Christian name being the same as my own.", "바로 앞의 혼인 항목은 신랑의 이름이 내 이름과 같다는 사정 때문에 내 주의에 남았다."], ["The entry immediately following it (on the top of the next page) was noticeable in another way from the large space it occupied, the record in this case registering the marriages of two brothers at the same time.", "바로 뒤에 이어지는 항목은(다음 면의 맨 위에 있었다) 차지한 자리가 유난히 넓다는 점에서 또 다르게 눈에 띄었는데, 그 기록은 형제 두 사람의 혼인을 한꺼번에 등록한 것이었다."], ["The register of the marriage of Sir Felix Glyde was in no respect remarkable except for the narrowness of the space into which it was compressed at the bottom of the page.", "펠릭스 글라이드 경의 혼인 기록 자체는 그 면 아래쪽 좁은 자리에 눌러 적혔다는 점 말고는 어느 모로도 특별할 것이 없었다."], ["The information about his wife was the usual information given in such cases.", "그의 아내에 관한 사항도 그런 경우에 흔히 적히는 통상적인 내용이었다."], ["She was described as “Cecilia Jane Elster, of Park-View Cottages, Knowlesbury, only daughter of the late Patrick Elster, Esq., formerly of Bath.”", "그녀는 “놀즈베리 파크뷰 코티지 거주, 전에 바스에 살던 고(故) 패트릭 엘스터 님의 외동딸 세실리아 제인 엘스터”라고 적혀 있었다."]], [["I noted down these particulars in my pocket-book, feeling as I did so both doubtful and disheartened about my next proceedings.", "나는 이 사항들을 수첩에 적어 넣었는데, 그러면서 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 막막하고 맥이 빠지는 심정이었다."], ["The Secret which I had believed until this moment to be within my grasp seemed now farther from my reach than ever.", "이 순간까지 손에 넣었다고 믿었던 그 비밀이 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 멀리 달아나 버린 듯했다."]], [["What suggestions of any mystery unexplained had arisen out of my visit to the vestry?", "성구실 방문에서 풀리지 않은 수수께끼의 실마리라 할 만한 것이 무엇이 나왔는가?"], ["I saw no suggestions anywhere.", "나는 아무런 실마리도 보지 못했다."], ["What progress had I made towards discovering the suspected stain on the reputation of Sir Percival’s mother?", "퍼시벌 경의 어머니의 평판에 있으리라 짐작했던 흠을 밝히는 일에서 나는 얼마나 나아갔는가?"], ["The one fact I had ascertained vindicated her reputation.", "내가 확인한 단 하나의 사실은 오히려 그녀의 평판을 옹호해 주었다."], ["Fresh doubts, fresh difficulties, fresh delays began to open before me in interminable prospect.", "새로운 의혹, 새로운 난관, 새로운 지체가 끝없이 이어질 전망으로 내 앞에 열리기 시작했다."], ["What was I to do next?", "다음으로 나는 무엇을 해야 하는가?"], ["The one immediate resource left to me appeared to be this.", "당장 내게 남은 방도는 하나뿐인 듯했다."], ["I might institute inquiries about “Miss Elster of Knowlesbury,”", "나는 “놀즈베리의 엘스터 양”에 대해 수소문해 볼 수 있었다."], ["on the chance of advancing towards the main object of my investigation, by first discovering the secret of Mrs. Catherick’s contempt for Sir Percival’s mother.", "퍼시벌 경의 어머니를 향한 캐서릭 부인의 경멸이 어디서 비롯되었는지를 먼저 알아냄으로써, 내 조사의 본래 목표에 다가서게 될지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["“Have you found what you wanted, sir?”", "“원하시던 것을 찾으셨습니까, 선생님?”"], ["said the clerk, as I closed the register-book.", "내가 등록부 책을 덮자 서기가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["I replied, “but I have some inquiries still to make.", "나는 대답했다. “하지만 아직 알아보아야 할 것이 몇 가지 있습니다."], ["I suppose the clergyman who officiated here in the year eighteen hundred and three is no longer alive?”", "1803년에 이곳에서 예식을 주관한 목사님은 이제 생존해 계시지 않겠지요?”"]], [["“No, no, sir, he was dead three or four years before I came here, and that was as long ago as the year twenty-seven.", "“그럼요, 그럼요, 선생님. 그분은 제가 여기 오기 서너 해 전에 돌아가셨습니다."], ["I got this place, sir,”", "제가 온 게 벌써 27년의 일이니까요. 저는 이 자리를 말입지요, 선생님,”"], ["persisted my talkative old friend, “through the clerk before me leaving it.", "수다스러운 내 늙은 벗이 기어이 말을 이었다. “제 앞의 서기가 그만두는 바람에 얻었습니다."], ["They say he was driven out of house and home by his wife—and she’s living still down in the new town there.", "사람들 말로는 그이가 마누라한테 시달려 집이며 살림이며 다 내던지고 쫓겨났다더군요. 그 여편네는 아직도 저 아래 새 도시에 살아 있습니다."], ["I don’t know the rights of the story myself—all I know is I got the place. Mr.", "저야 그 얘기의 옳고 그름은 모르지요."], ["Wansborough got it for me—the son of my old master that I was telling you of.", "제가 아는 건 제가 이 자리를 얻었다는 것뿐입니다. 완즈버러 씨가 저를 위해 얻어 주셨지요."], ["He’s a free, pleasant gentleman as ever lived—rides to the hounds, keeps his pointers and all that.", "아까 말씀드린 제 옛 주인 어른의 아드님 말입니다. 세상에 다시 없이 소탈하고 유쾌한 신사분이시지요."], ["He’s vestry-clerk here now as his father was before him.”", "사냥개를 따라 말을 달리시고, 사냥개도 여러 마리 두시고 뭐 그러십니다. 지금은 그분이 아버님이 그러셨듯이 여기 성구실 서기이십니다.”"]], [["“Did you not tell me your former master lived at Knowlesbury?”", "“전 주인이 놀즈베리에 사셨다고 하지 않으셨습니까?”"], ["I asked, calling to mind the long story about the precise gentleman of the old school with which my talkative friend had wearied me before he opened the register-book.", "나는, 등록부 책을 펼치기 전에 이 수다스러운 벗이 나를 지치게 하며 늘어놓았던, 그 옛 시대의 꼼꼼한 신사에 관한 긴 이야기를 떠올리며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, to be sure, sir,”", "“그럼요, 물론입지요, 선생님.”"], ["replied the clerk. “Old Mr.", "서기가 대답했다."], ["Wansborough lived at Knowlesbury, and young Mr. Wansborough lives there too.”", "“완즈버러 노인 어르신은 놀즈베리에 사셨고, 젊은 완즈버러 씨도 거기 사십니다.”"]], [["“You said just now he was vestry-clerk, like his father before him.", "“방금 그분이 아버지가 그랬듯이 성구실 서기라고 하셨지요."], ["I am not quite sure that I know what a vestry-clerk is.”", "성구실 서기가 무엇인지를 제가 잘 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Don’t you indeed, sir?—and you come from London too!", "“정말 모르십니까, 선생님?"], ["Every parish church, you know, has a vestry-clerk and a parish-clerk.", "게다가 런던서 오신 분이!—아시다시피 교구 교회에는 어디나 성구실 서기와 교구 서기가 있습니다."], ["The parish-clerk is a man like me (except that I’ve got a deal more learning than most of them—though I don’t boast of it).", "교구 서기는 저 같은 사람이지요(다만 저는 그 사람들 태반보다 배움이 훨씬 많습니다만, 뭐 자랑삼는 건 아닙니다)."], ["The vestry-clerk is a sort of an appointment that the lawyers get, and if there’s any business to be done for the vestry, why there they are to do it.", "성구실 서기라는 건 변호사들이 얻는 일종의 임명직이라서, 성구실을 위해 처리할 일이 생기면 그이들이 있어 그 일을 하는 겝니다."], ["It’s just the same in London.", "런던도 꼭 마찬가지입지요."], ["Every parish church there has got its vestry-clerk—and you may take my word for it he’s sure to be a lawyer.”", "거기도 교구 교회마다 성구실 서기가 있고, 제 말을 믿으셔도 좋습니다만, 그이는 틀림없이 변호사입니다.”"]], [["“Then young Mr. Wansborough is a lawyer, I suppose?”", "“그러면 젊은 완즈버러 씨도 변호사겠군요?”"]], [["“Of course he is, sir!", "“당연하지요, 선생님!"], ["A lawyer in High Street, Knowlesbury—the old offices that his father had before him.", "놀즈베리 하이 스트리트의 변호사입니다. 아버님이 쓰시던 그 옛 사무실을 그대로 쓰지요."], ["The number of times I’ve swept those offices out, and seen the old gentleman come trotting in to business on his white pony, looking right and left all down the street and nodding to everybody!", "제가 그 사무실을 쓸어 낸 게 몇 번인지 모릅니다. 노신사께서 백마 조랑말을 타고 일하러 총총 들어오시면서 거리 좌우를 죽 둘러보고 사람마다 고개를 끄덕여 주시던 걸 얼마나 보았는지요!"], ["Bless you, he was a popular character!—he’d have done in London!”", "참말이지, 그 어른은 인기 있는 분이셨습니다! 런던에서도 통하셨을 겝니다!”"]], [["“How far is it to Knowlesbury from this place?”", "“여기서 놀즈베리까지는 얼마나 됩니까?”"]], [["“A long stretch, sir,”", "“한참 가셔야지요, 선생님.”"], ["said the clerk, with that exaggerated idea of distances, and that vivid perception of difficulties in getting from place to place, which is peculiar to all country people.", "서기가, 거리를 부풀려 재고 이곳에서 저곳으로 가는 어려움을 유난히 실감 나게 여기는, 시골 사람 특유의 그 버릇대로 말했다."], ["“Nigh on five mile, I can tell you!”", "“오 마일은 족히 될걸요!”"]], [["It was still early in the forenoon.", "아직 오전 이른 시각이었다."], ["There was plenty of time for a walk to Knowlesbury and back again to Welmingham; and there was no person probably in the town who was fitter to assist my inquiries about the character and position of Sir Percival’s mother before her marriage than the local solicitor.", "놀즈베리까지 걸어갔다가 웰밍엄으로 되돌아올 시간은 넉넉했다. 그리고 퍼시벌 경의 어머니가 혼인하기 전의 성품과 처지에 관해 내 조사를 도와줄 사람으로, 그 도시에서 그곳 사무 변호사보다 더 적합한 이는 아마 없을 터였다."], ["Resolving to go at once to Knowlesbury on foot, I led the way out of the vestry.", "당장 걸어서 놀즈베리로 가기로 결심하고, 나는 앞장서서 성구실을 나섰다."]], [["“Thank you kindly, sir,”", "“고맙습니다, 선생님.”"], ["said the clerk, as I slipped my little present into his hand.", "내가 작은 사례를 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주자 서기가 말했다."], ["“Are you really going to walk all the way to Knowlesbury and back?", "“정말로 놀즈베리까지 내내 걸어갔다 오실 참입니까?"], ["Well!", "저런!"], ["you’re strong on your legs, too—and what a blessing that is, isn’t it?", "선생님도 다리가 튼튼하시구먼요. 그게 얼마나 큰 복입니까, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["There’s the road, you can’t miss it.", "길은 저기입니다, 놓치실 리 없지요."], ["I wish I was going your way—it’s pleasant to meet with gentlemen from London in a lost corner like this.", "저도 같이 갔으면 좋겠습니다만. 이런 잊힌 구석에서 런던 신사분을 뵙는 건 즐거운 일이지요."], ["One hears the news.", "소식도 좀 듣고요."], ["Wish you good-morning, sir, and thank you kindly once more.”", "좋은 아침 되십시오, 선생님. 다시 한번 고맙습니다.”"]], [["We parted.", "우리는 헤어졌다."], ["As I left the church behind me I looked back, and there were the two men again on the road below, with a third in their company, that third person being the short man in black whom I had traced to the railway the evening before.", "교회를 뒤로하고 걸어 나오다가 돌아보니, 아래쪽 길에 그 두 사내가 다시 나와 있었고, 세 번째 사람이 그들과 함께 있었다. 그 세 번째 인물은 전날 저녁에 내가 철도역까지 뒤쫓았던 검은 옷의 자그마한 사내였다."]], [["The three stood talking together for a little while, then separated.", "셋은 잠시 함께 서서 이야기를 나누더니 이내 갈라섰다."], ["The man in black went away by himself towards Welmingham—the other two remained together, evidently waiting to follow me as soon as I walked on.", "검은 옷의 사내는 혼자서 웰밍엄 쪽으로 떠났고, 나머지 둘은 함께 남아 내가 걸음을 옮기는 대로 곧 뒤를 밟으려고 기다리는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["I proceeded on my way without letting the fellows see that I took any special notice of them.", "나는 그자들에게 내가 그들을 특별히 눈여겨보고 있다는 티를 내지 않은 채 가던 길을 계속했다."], ["They caused me no conscious irritation of feeling at that moment—on the contrary, they rather revived my sinking hopes.", "그 순간 그들은 나에게 아무런 짜증도 불러일으키지 않았다. 오히려 가라앉던 내 희망을 되살려 주는 편이었다."], ["In the surprise of discovering the evidence of the marriage, I had forgotten the inference I had drawn on first perceiving the men in the neighbourhood of the vestry.", "혼인의 증거를 발견하고 놀란 나머지, 나는 성구실 근처에서 그 사내들을 처음 보았을 때 이끌어 냈던 추론을 잊고 있었다."], ["Their reappearance reminded me that Sir Percival had anticipated my visit to Old Welmingham church as the next result of my interview with Mrs.", "그들이 다시 나타난 것은, 캐서릭 부인과의 면담 다음에 내가 구웰밍엄 교회를 찾아갈 것을 퍼시벌 경이 미리 내다보고 있었음을 나에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["Catherick—otherwise he would never have placed his spies there to wait for me.", "그렇지 않고서야 나를 기다리라고 그곳에 밀정을 배치했을 리가 없다."], ["Smoothly and fairly as appearances looked in the vestry, there was something wrong beneath them—there was something in the register-book, for aught I knew, that I had not discovered yet.", "성구실에서 겉으로는 매끄럽고 그럴듯해 보였지만, 그 아래에는 뭔가 잘못된 것이 있었다. 내가 아직 알아내지 못한 무언가가, 어쩌면 그 등록부 책 안에 있었다."]], [["# X", "# X"]], [["Once out of sight of the church, I pressed forward briskly on my way to Knowlesbury.", "교회가 시야에서 사라지자마자, 나는 놀즈베리를 향한 길을 부지런히 재촉했다."]], [["The road was, for the most part, straight and level.", "길은 대부분 곧고 평탄했다."], ["Whenever I looked back over it I saw the two spies steadily following me.", "뒤를 돌아볼 때마다 두 첩자가 꾸준히 나를 뒤따라오는 것이 보였다."], ["For the greater part of the way they kept at a safe distance behind.", "길의 대부분에서 그들은 안전한 거리를 두고 뒤에 처져 있었다."], ["But once or twice they quickened their pace, as if with the purpose of overtaking me, then stopped, consulted together, and fell back again to their former position.", "그러나 한두 번은 나를 따라잡으려는 듯 걸음을 재촉했다가, 이내 멈춰 서서 서로 의논한 뒤 다시 본래 자리로 물러났다."], ["They had some special object evidently in view, and they seemed to be hesitating or differing about the best means of accomplishing it.", "그들에게 무언가 특별한 목적이 있는 것은 분명했고, 그것을 이루는 최선의 방법을 두고 망설이거나 의견이 갈리는 듯했다."], ["I could not guess exactly what their design might be, but I felt serious doubts of reaching Knowlesbury without some mischance happening to me on the way.", "그들의 계략이 정확히 무엇인지는 짐작할 수 없었으나, 도중에 무슨 사달이 나지 않고 놀즈베리에 닿을 수 있을지 나는 심각하게 의심스러웠다."], ["These doubts were realised.", "그 의심은 현실이 되었다."]], [["I had just entered on a lonely part of the road, with a sharp turn at some distance ahead, and had just concluded (calculating by time) that I must be getting near to the town, when I suddenly heard the steps of the men close behind me.", "인적 없는 구간에 막 들어선 참이었다. 얼마 앞쪽에 급하게 꺾이는 모퉁이가 있었고, 시간을 헤아려 보건대 이제 읍내에 거의 다다랐겠거니 하고 막 생각을 끝낸 참에, 나는 문득 등 뒤 바로 가까이에서 두 사내의 발소리를 들었다."]], [["Before I could look round, one of them (the man by whom I had been followed in London) passed rapidly on my left side and hustled me with his shoulder.", "미처 돌아보기도 전에 그중 하나가 — 런던에서 나를 미행했던 그 사내가 — 잰걸음으로 내 왼쪽을 스쳐 지나며 어깨로 나를 떠밀었다."], ["I had been more irritated by the manner in which he and his companion had dogged my steps all the way from Old Welmingham than I was myself aware of, and I unfortunately pushed the fellow away smartly with my open hand.", "올드 웰밍엄에서부터 줄곧 그와 그의 동료가 내 뒤를 밟아 온 그 태도에 나는 스스로 자각한 것보다 훨씬 더 화가 나 있었고, 불행히도 손바닥으로 그 사내를 세게 밀쳐 냈다."], ["He instantly shouted for help.", "그는 즉시 도와달라고 소리쳤다."], ["His companion, the tall man in the gamekeeper’s clothes, sprang to my right side, and the next moment the two scoundrels held me pinioned between them in the middle of the road.", "그의 동료, 사냥터지기 옷차림의 키 큰 사내가 내 오른쪽으로 달려들었고, 다음 순간 두 악당은 길 한복판에서 나를 사이에 끼워 두 팔을 붙들어 꼼짝 못 하게 만들었다."]], [["The conviction that a trap had been laid for me, and the vexation of knowing that I had fallen into it, fortunately restrained me from making my position still worse by an unavailing struggle with two men, one of whom would, in all probability, have been more than a match for me single-handed.", "나를 노린 덫이 놓여 있었다는 확신과, 거기에 걸려들었음을 아는 데서 오는 분함이 다행히도 나를 붙들어, 두 사내와 부질없는 몸싸움을 벌여 처지를 더 나쁘게 만드는 일만은 면하게 했다. 둘 중 하나는 아마 일대일로도 나보다 한 수 위였을 것이다."], ["I repressed the first natural movement by which I had attempted to shake them off, and looked about to see if there was any person near to whom I could appeal.", "나는 그들을 뿌리치려던 첫 본능적인 몸짓을 억누르고, 도움을 청할 만한 사람이 근처에 있는지 둘러보았다."]], [["A labourer was at work in an adjoining field who must have witnessed all that had passed.", "옆 밭에서 일꾼 하나가 일하고 있었는데, 지금까지 벌어진 일을 다 보았을 것이 틀림없었다."], ["I called to him to follow us to the town.", "나는 그에게 읍내까지 우리를 따라와 달라고 외쳤다."], ["He shook his head with stolid obstinacy, and walked away in the direction of a cottage which stood back from the high-road.", "그는 무뚝뚝하고 고집스럽게 고개를 저으며 큰길에서 물러앉은 오두막 쪽으로 걸어가 버렸다."], ["At the same time the men who held me between them declared their intention of charging me with an assault.", "그와 동시에 나를 사이에 끼고 붙든 사내들은 나를 폭행죄로 고발하겠다고 선언했다."], ["I was cool enough and wise enough now to make no opposition.", "나는 이제 반항하지 않을 만큼은 냉정했고 또 현명했다."], ["“Drop your hold of my arms,”", "“내 팔을 놓으시오.”"], ["I said, “and I will go with you to the town.”", "내가 말했다. “그러면 함께 읍내로 가겠소.”"], ["The man in the gamekeeper’s dress roughly refused.", "사냥터지기 옷차림의 사내는 거칠게 거절했다."], ["But the shorter man was sharp enough to look to consequences, and not to let his companion commit himself by unnecessary violence.", "그러나 키가 작은 쪽은 뒷일을 헤아릴 만큼 약았고, 동료가 쓸데없는 폭력으로 스스로 발목을 잡히게 두지 않았다."], ["He made a sign to the other, and I walked on between them with my arms free.", "그가 다른 쪽에게 신호를 보냈고, 나는 두 팔이 자유로운 채로 그들 사이에 끼어 걸어갔다."]], [["We reached the turning in the road, and there, close before us, were the suburbs of Knowlesbury.", "우리는 길의 모퉁이에 이르렀고, 바로 눈앞에 놀즈베리의 변두리가 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["One of the local policemen was walking along the path by the roadside.", "지역 순경 하나가 길가 인도를 따라 걷고 있었다."], ["The men at once appealed to him.", "사내들은 곧바로 그에게 호소했다."], ["He replied that the magistrate was then sitting at the town-hall, and recommended that we should appear before him immediately.", "순경은 지금 시각이면 치안판사가 읍청사에 나와 있을 테니 즉시 그 앞에 출두하는 게 좋겠다고 대답했다."]], [["We went on to the town-hall.", "우리는 읍청사로 갔다."], ["The clerk made out a formal summons, and the charge was preferred against me, with the customary exaggeration and the customary perversion of the truth on such occasions.", "서기가 정식 소환장을 작성했고, 이런 자리에 으레 따르는 과장과 으레 따르는 사실 왜곡을 곁들여 내게 죄가 제기되었다."], ["The magistrate (an ill-tempered man, with a sour enjoyment in the exercise of his own power) inquired if any one on or near the road had witnessed the assault, and, greatly to my surprise, the complainant admitted the presence of the labourer in the field.", "치안판사는 — 성마른 사람으로, 제 권한을 휘두르는 데서 시큰둥한 재미를 느끼는 부류였다 — 길 위나 그 근처에서 그 폭행을 목격한 사람이 있는지 물었고, 놀랍게도 고발인은 밭에 일꾼이 있었음을 인정했다."], ["I was enlightened, however, as to the object of the admission by the magistrate’s next words.", "그러나 그 인정의 속셈은 치안판사의 다음 말로 곧 밝혀졌다."], ["He remanded me at once for the production of the witness, expressing, at the same time, his willingness to take bail for my reappearance if I could produce one responsible surety to offer it.", "그는 증인을 불러 세우기 위해 나를 즉시 구류하겠다고 하면서, 다만 책임질 만한 보증인 한 사람을 세울 수 있다면 재출두를 조건으로 보석을 받아들이겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["If I had been known in the town he would have liberated me on my own recognisances, but as I was a total stranger it was necessary that I should find responsible bail.", "내가 이 읍내에 알려진 사람이었다면 그는 내 서약만으로 풀어주었을 테지만, 나는 완전한 타관 사람이었으므로 책임 있는 보증인을 구해야만 했다."]], [["The whole object of the stratagem was now disclosed to me.", "이제 그 술책의 전모가 내게 드러났다."], ["It had been so managed as to make a remand necessary in a town where I was a perfect stranger, and where I could not hope to get my liberty on bail.", "내가 아무 연고도 없어 보석으로 풀려날 가망이 없는 읍내에서 구류가 불가피하도록 꾸며진 일이었다."], ["The remand merely extended over three days, until the next sitting of the magistrate.", "구류는 다음 치안판사 개정일까지 사흘에 지나지 않았다."], ["But in that time, while I was in confinement, Sir Percival might use any means he pleased to embarrass my future proceedings—perhaps to screen himself from detection altogether—without the slightest fear of any hindrance on my part.", "그러나 내가 갇혀 있는 그 사흘 동안 퍼시벌 경은 내 쪽에서 방해할 걱정이라고는 조금도 없이, 앞으로의 내 행보를 가로막을 — 어쩌면 발각을 아예 모면할 — 온갖 수단을 마음대로 쓸 수 있었다."], ["At the end of the three days the charge would, no doubt, be withdrawn, and the attendance of the witness would be perfectly useless.", "사흘이 지나면 고발은 틀림없이 취하될 것이고, 증인의 출석 따위는 아무 쓸모도 없어질 터였다."]], [["My indignation, I may almost say, my despair, at this mischievous check to all further progress—so base and trifling in itself, and yet so disheartening and so serious in its probable results—quite unfitted me at first to reflect on the best means of extricating myself from the dilemma in which I now stood.", "이 심술궂은 발목잡기에 대한 나의 분노는 — 아니, 거의 절망이라 해도 좋으리라 — 그 자체로는 그토록 비열하고 하찮으면서도 초래될 결과는 그토록 낙담스럽고 심각한 것이었기에, 처음에는 내가 처한 곤경에서 벗어날 최선의 방책을 궁리할 여력조차 앗아 갔다."], ["I had the folly to call for writing materials, and to think of privately communicating my real position to the magistrate.", "나는 어리석게도 필기도구를 청해, 내 진짜 처지를 치안판사에게 은밀히 알릴 생각을 했다."], ["The hopelessness and the imprudence of this proceeding failed to strike me before I had actually written the opening lines of the letter.", "그 짓이 얼마나 가망 없고 경솔한지는 실제로 편지의 첫머리를 쓰고 난 뒤에야 비로소 떠올랐다."], ["It was not till I had pushed the paper away—not till, I am ashamed to say, I had almost allowed the vexation of my helpless position to conquer me—that a course of action suddenly occurred to my mind, which Sir Percival had probably not anticipated, and which might set me free again in a few hours.", "종이를 밀쳐 내고서야 — 부끄러운 말이지만, 무력한 처지의 분함에 거의 굴복하려던 순간에야 — 문득 한 가지 방책이 머리에 떠올랐다. 퍼시벌 경도 미처 예상하지 못했을 방책이었고, 몇 시간 안에 나를 다시 자유롭게 해 줄지도 모르는 방책이었다."], ["I determined to communicate the situation in which I was placed to Mr. Dawson, of Oak Lodge.", "나는 내가 놓인 사정을 오크 로지의 도슨 의사에게 알리기로 마음먹었다."]], [["I had visited this gentleman’s house, it may be remembered, at the time of my first inquiries in the Blackwater Park neighbourhood, and I had presented to him a letter of introduction from Miss Halcombe, in which she recommended me to his friendly attention in the strongest terms.", "기억하시겠지만, 나는 블랙워터 파크 근방에서 처음 탐문을 벌이던 무렵 이 신사의 집을 찾아간 적이 있었고, 그때 홀콤 양이 나를 최대한 호의로 대해 달라고 강력히 부탁하는 소개 편지를 그에게 건넸었다."], ["I now wrote, referring to this letter, and to what I had previously told Mr. Dawson of the delicate and dangerous nature of my inquiries.", "나는 이제 그 편지와, 앞서 도슨 의사에게 내 조사가 얼마나 미묘하고 위험한 성격의 것인지 말해 두었던 바를 상기시키며 글을 썼다."], ["I had not revealed to him the truth about Laura, having merely described my errand as being of the utmost importance to private family interests with which Miss Halcombe was concerned.", "로라에 관한 진실은 그에게 밝히지 않았고, 다만 내 볼일이 홀콤 양이 관련된 사사로운 집안의 이해에 지극히 중대한 것이라고만 설명해 두었었다."], ["Using the same caution still, I now accounted for my presence at Knowlesbury in the same manner, and I put it to the doctor to say whether the trust reposed in me by a lady whom he well knew, and the hospitality I had myself received in his house, justified me or not in asking him to come to my assistance in a place where I was quite friendless.", "지금도 같은 신중함을 지켜, 놀즈베리에 와 있는 까닭도 같은 식으로 둘러댔다. 그리고 그가 잘 아는 어느 부인이 내게 맡긴 신뢰와, 그의 집에서 내가 받은 환대에 비추어, 아는 이 하나 없는 이곳에서 도움을 청하는 것이 온당한지 아닌지 의사 자신이 판단해 달라고 적었다."]], [["I obtained permission to hire a messenger to drive away at once with my letter in a conveyance which might be used to bring the doctor back immediately.", "나는 심부름꾼을 사서 내 편지를 지니고 당장 마차로 달려가되, 그 마차로 곧바로 의사를 모셔 올 수 있도록 하는 허락을 얻었다."], ["Oak Lodge was on the Knowlesbury side of Blackwater.", "오크 로지는 블랙워터에서 놀즈베리 쪽에 있었다."], ["The man declared he could drive there in forty minutes, and could bring Mr. Dawson back in forty more.", "심부름꾼은 사십 분이면 그곳에 닿고 다시 사십 분이면 도슨 의사를 모셔 올 수 있다고 장담했다."], ["I directed him to follow the doctor wherever he might happen to be, if he was not at home, and then sat down to wait for the result with all the patience and all the hope that I could summon to help me.", "나는 의사가 집에 없거든 어디에 있든 찾아가라고 일러 두고는, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 인내와 온갖 희망을 짜내어 결과를 기다리며 앉아 있었다."]], [["It was not quite half-past one when the messenger departed.", "심부름꾼이 떠난 것은 한 시 반이 채 되기 전이었다."], ["Before half-past three he returned, and brought the doctor with him.", "세 시 반이 되기 전에 그는 의사를 모시고 돌아왔다."], ["Mr. Dawson’s kindness, and the delicacy with which he treated his prompt assistance quite as a matter of course, almost overpowered me.", "도슨 의사의 친절함과, 그 즉각적인 도움을 지극히 당연한 일처럼 대하는 그 조심스러운 태도에 나는 거의 압도되었다."], ["The bail required was offered, and accepted immediately.", "요구된 보석이 제시되었고 곧바로 받아들여졌다."], ["Before four o’clock, on that afternoon, I was shaking hands warmly with the good old doctor—a free man again—in the streets of Knowlesbury.", "그날 오후 네 시가 되기 전에 나는 놀즈베리 거리에서 — 다시 자유로운 몸이 되어 — 그 선량한 노의사와 뜨겁게 악수하고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Dawson hospitably invited me to go back with him to Oak Lodge, and take up my quarters there for the night.", "도슨 의사는 함께 오크 로지로 돌아가 그날 밤 묵어 가라고 후하게 청했다."], ["I could only reply that my time was not my own, and I could only ask him to let me pay my visit in a few days, when I might repeat my thanks, and offer to him all the explanations which I felt to be only his due, but which I was not then in a position to make.", "나는 다만 내 시간이 내 것이 아니라고 대답할 수밖에 없었고, 며칠 뒤에 찾아뵙게 해 달라고, 그때 다시 감사를 드리고 마땅히 그분이 들으실 자격이 있으나 지금은 도저히 드릴 수 없는 모든 설명을 올리겠다고 청할 수밖에 없었다."], ["We parted with friendly assurances on both sides, and I turned my steps at once to Mr. Wansborough’s office in the High Street.", "우리는 서로 다정한 인사를 나누고 헤어졌고, 나는 곧장 하이 스트리트에 있는 완즈버러 씨의 사무실로 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["Time was now of the last importance.", "이제 시간이 무엇보다 중요했다."]], [["The news of my being free on bail would reach Sir Percival, to an absolute certainty, before night.", "내가 보석으로 풀려났다는 소식은 밤이 되기 전에 반드시 퍼시벌 경의 귀에 들어갈 것이었다."], ["If the next few hours did not put me in a position to justify his worst fears, and to hold him helpless at my mercy, I might lose every inch of the ground I had gained, never to recover it again.", "앞으로 몇 시간 안에 그의 최악의 두려움이 옳았음을 입증하고 그를 내 손아귀에 무력하게 붙들어 둘 처지에 서지 못한다면, 나는 애써 얻은 발판을 한 뼘도 남김없이 잃고 영영 되찾지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["The unscrupulous nature of the man, the local influence he possessed, the desperate peril of exposure with which my blindfold inquiries threatened him—all warned me to press on to positive discovery, without the useless waste of a single minute.", "그 사내의 파렴치한 본성, 그가 이 고장에서 가진 영향력, 앞을 더듬는 내 조사가 그를 위협하는 폭로의 절박한 위험 — 이 모두가 단 일 분도 헛되이 버리지 말고 확실한 발견을 향해 밀고 나가라고 나를 채근했다."], ["I had found time to think while I was waiting for Mr. Dawson’s arrival, and I had well employed it.", "도슨 의사가 오기를 기다리는 동안 나는 생각할 시간을 얻었고, 그 시간을 잘 썼다."], ["Certain portions of the conversation of the talkative old clerk, which had wearied me at the time, now recurred to my memory with a new significance, and a suspicion crossed my mind darkly which had not occurred to me while I was in the vestry.", "그 수다스러운 늙은 서기와 나눈 대화의 어떤 대목들은 당시에는 나를 지루하게 했으나 이제 새로운 의미를 띠고 기억에 되살아났고, 성구실에 있을 때는 떠오르지 않았던 어두운 의심 하나가 마음을 스쳤다."], ["On my way to Knowlesbury, I had only proposed to apply to Mr. Wansborough for information on the subject of Sir Percival’s mother.", "놀즈베리로 오는 길에 나는 퍼시벌 경의 어머니에 관한 정보를 얻으려고 완즈버러 씨를 찾을 작정이었을 뿐이다."], ["My object now was to examine the duplicate register of Old Welmingham Church.", "그러나 이제 내 목적은 올드 웰밍엄 교회 대장의 사본을 살펴보는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Wansborough was in his office when I inquired for him.", "완즈버러 씨는 내가 찾았을 때 사무실에 있었다."]], [["He was a jovial, red-faced, easy-looking man—more like a country squire than a lawyer—and he seemed to be both surprised and amused by my application.", "그는 쾌활하고 얼굴이 불그레하며 느긋해 보이는 사람으로 — 변호사라기보다 시골 지주에 가까웠다 — 내 청을 듣고 놀라면서도 재미있어하는 눈치였다."], ["He had heard of his father’s copy of the register, but had not even seen it himself.", "그는 아버지가 남긴 대장 사본에 대해 들어는 보았으나 자기 눈으로 본 적조차 없었다."], ["It had never been inquired after, and it was no doubt in the strong room among other papers that had not been disturbed since his father’s death.", "그것을 찾는 사람이 여태 한 명도 없었으니, 아버지가 세상을 떠난 뒤로 손대지 않은 다른 서류들과 함께 틀림없이 금고방에 있을 것이라고 했다."], ["It was a pity (Mr. Wansborough said) that the old gentleman was not alive to hear his precious copy asked for at last.", "노인장이 살아 계셔서 그 귀한 사본을 마침내 찾는 소리를 들으셨다면 좋았을 것을, 하고 완즈버러 씨는 아쉬워했다."], ["He would have ridden his favourite hobby harder than ever now.", "그랬다면 아버지는 그 애지중지하던 취미에 어느 때보다 더 열을 올리셨으리라는 것이다."], ["How had I come to hear of the copy?", "그런데 나는 어떻게 그 사본 이야기를 들었는가?"], ["was it through anybody in the town?", "읍내의 누군가에게서 들었는가?"]], [["I parried the question as well as I could.", "나는 되도록 그 질문을 슬쩍 피했다."], ["It was impossible at this stage of the investigation to be too cautious, and it was just as well not to let Mr. Wansborough know prematurely that I had already examined the original register.", "조사가 이 단계에 이른 이상 아무리 조심해도 지나치지 않았고, 이미 원본 대장을 살펴보았다는 사실을 완즈버러 씨에게 섣불리 알리지 않는 편이 나았다."], ["I described myself, therefore, as pursuing a family inquiry, to the object of which every possible saving of time was of great importance.", "그래서 나는 집안일을 알아보는 중인데 그 목적상 시간을 조금이라도 아끼는 것이 매우 중요하다고 나 자신을 설명했다."], ["I was anxious to send certain particulars to London by that day’s post, and one look at the duplicate register (paying, of course, the necessary fees) might supply what I required, and save me a further journey to Old Welmingham.", "그날 우편으로 어떤 세부 사항을 런던에 부치고 싶은데, 사본 대장을 한 번 들여다보면 — 물론 필요한 수수료는 내고서 — 내가 원하는 것을 얻어 올드 웰밍엄까지 다시 다녀오는 수고를 덜 수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["I added that, in the event of my subsequently requiring a copy of the original register, I should make application to Mr. Wansborough’s office to furnish me with the document.", "나는 나중에 원본 대장의 등본이 필요해질 경우에는 완즈버러 씨의 사무소에 그 서류의 발급을 신청하겠다는 말도 덧붙였다."]], [["After this explanation no objection was made to producing the copy.", "이 설명이 있고 나자 사본을 내주는 데 아무 이의도 없었다."], ["A clerk was sent to the strong room, and after some delay returned with the volume.", "서기 하나가 금고방으로 보내졌고, 얼마쯤 지체된 뒤 그 책을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["It was of exactly the same size as the volume in the vestry, the only difference being that the copy was more smartly bound.", "그것은 성구실에 있던 책과 크기가 꼭 같았고, 다만 사본 쪽이 더 말끔하게 제본되어 있다는 점만 달랐다."], ["I took it with me to an unoccupied desk.", "나는 그것을 들고 비어 있는 책상으로 갔다."], ["My hands were trembling—my head was burning hot—I felt the necessity of concealing my agitation as well as I could from the persons about me in the room, before I ventured on opening the book.", "손이 떨리고 머리가 불덩이처럼 달아올랐다. 책을 펴기 전에 나는 방 안 사람들에게 내 동요를 되도록 감출 필요를 느꼈다."]], [["On the blank page at the beginning, to which I first turned, were traced some lines in faded ink.", "맨 앞의 백지에 — 내가 제일 먼저 펼친 곳이었다 — 빛바랜 잉크로 몇 줄이 적혀 있었다."], ["They contained these words—", "거기에는 이런 말이 담겨 있었다 —"]], [["“Copy of the Marriage Register of Welmingham Parish Church.", "“웰밍엄 교구 교회 혼인 대장의 사본."], ["Executed under my orders, and afterwards compared, entry by entry, with the original, by myself.", "나의 지시로 작성되었고, 그 뒤 내가 직접 원본과 한 항목씩 대조하였음."], ["(Signed) Robert Wansborough, vestry-clerk.”", "(서명) 로버트 완즈버러, 성구실 서기.”"], ["Below this note there was a line added, in another handwriting, as follows: “Extending from the first of January, 1800, to the thirtieth of June, 1815.”", "이 기록 아래에는 다른 필체로 한 줄이 덧붙어 있었는데, 다음과 같았다. “1800년 1월 1일부터 1815년 6월 30일까지에 해당함.”"]], [["I turned to the month of September, eighteen hundred and three.", "나는 1803년 9월 항목을 펼쳤다."], ["I found the marriage of the man whose Christian name was the same as my own.", "내 세례명과 같은 이름을 가진 사내의 혼인 기록을 찾아냈다."], ["I found the double register of the marriages of the two brothers.", "두 형제의 혼인이 나란히 적힌 이중 기록도 찾아냈다."], ["And between these entries, at the bottom of the page?", "그런데 그 두 기록 사이, 페이지 아래쪽에는?"]], [["Nothing!", "아무것도 없었다!"], ["Not a vestige of the entry which recorded the marriage of Sir Felix Glyde and Cecilia Jane Elster in the register of the church!", "교회 대장에 적혀 있던 펠릭스 글라이드 경과 세실리아 제인 엘스터의 혼인 기록은 흔적조차 없었다!"]], [["My heart gave a great bound, and throbbed as if it would stifle me.", "심장이 크게 뛰어올랐고, 숨이 막힐 듯 두근거렸다."], ["I looked again—I was afraid to believe the evidence of my own eyes. No!", "나는 다시 들여다보았다 — 내 눈이 본 증거를 차마 믿기가 두려웠다."], ["not a doubt.", "아니!"], ["The marriage was not there.", "의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["The entries on the copy occupied exactly the same places on the page as the entries in the original.", "그 혼인은 거기 없었다. 사본의 기록들은 원본의 기록들과 페이지 위에서 정확히 같은 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["The last entry on one page recorded the marriage of the man with my Christian name.", "한쪽 페이지의 마지막 기록은 나와 같은 세례명을 가진 사내의 혼인이었다."], ["Below it there was a blank space—a space evidently left because it was too narrow to contain the entry of the marriages of the two brothers, which in the copy, as in the original, occupied the top of the next page.", "그 아래에는 빈자리가 있었다 — 두 형제의 혼인 기록을 담기에는 너무 좁아서 비워 둔 것이 분명한 자리였고, 그 기록은 사본에서도 원본에서와 마찬가지로 다음 페이지 맨 위를 차지하고 있었다."], ["That space told the whole story!", "그 빈자리가 모든 사연을 말해 주었다!"], ["There it must have remained in the church register from eighteen hundred and three (when the marriages had been solemnised and the copy had been made) to eighteen hundred and twenty-seven, when Sir Percival appeared at Old Welmingham.", "교회 대장의 그 자리는 혼인이 거행되고 사본이 만들어진 1803년부터 퍼시벌 경이 올드 웰밍엄에 나타난 1827년까지 그대로 비어 있었음이 틀림없다."], ["Here, at Knowlesbury, was the chance of committing the forgery shown to me in the copy, and there, at Old Welmingham, was the forgery committed in the register of the church.", "여기 놀즈베리에서는 위조를 저지를 기회가 사본을 통해 내 눈앞에 드러났고, 저기 올드 웰밍엄에서는 그 위조가 교회 대장에 실제로 저질러져 있었던 것이다."]], [["My head turned giddy—I held by the desk to keep myself from falling.", "머리가 어지러워져 나는 쓰러지지 않으려고 책상을 붙들었다."], ["Of all the suspicions which had struck me in relation to that desperate man, not one had been near the truth.", "그 필사적인 사내를 두고 내게 떠올랐던 온갖 의심 가운데 진실 근처에라도 간 것은 하나도 없었다."]], [["The idea that he was not Sir Percival Glyde at all, that he had no more claim to the baronetcy and to Blackwater Park than the poorest labourer who worked on the estate, had never once occurred to my mind.", "그가 애초에 퍼시벌 글라이드 경이 아니라는 것, 준남작 작위와 블랙워터 파크에 대해 그 영지에서 일하는 가장 가난한 일꾼만큼의 권리도 없다는 것 — 그런 생각은 단 한 번도 내 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["At one time I had thought he might be Anne Catherick’s father—at another time I had thought he might have been Anne Catherick’s husband—the offence of which he was really guilty had been, from first to last, beyond the widest reach of my imagination.", "한때 나는 그가 앤 캐서릭의 아버지가 아닐까 여겼고, 또 한때는 앤 캐서릭의 남편이었던 것이 아닐까 여겼다. 그가 실제로 저지른 죄는 처음부터 끝까지 내 상상이 미칠 수 있는 가장 먼 데보다도 훨씬 멀리 있었다."]], [["The paltry means by which the fraud had been effected, the magnitude and daring of the crime that it represented, the horror of the consequences involved in its discovery, overwhelmed me.", "그 사기가 이루어진 하찮은 수단, 그것이 뜻하는 범죄의 크기와 대담함, 발각될 경우 뒤따를 결과의 끔찍함이 나를 압도했다."], ["Who could wonder now at the brute-restlessness of the wretch’s life—at his desperate alternations between abject duplicity and reckless violence—at the madness of guilty distrust which had made him imprison Anne Catherick in the Asylum, and had given him over to the vile conspiracy against his wife, on the bare suspicion that the one and the other knew his terrible secret?", "그 가련한 자의 짐승 같은 안절부절, 비굴한 이중성과 무모한 폭력 사이를 오가던 그 필사적인 진폭, 앤 캐서릭을 정신병원에 가둔 짓과 제 아내에게 저지른 비열한 음모에 몸을 던지게 만든 죄지은 자의 미치광이 같은 불신 — 그 두 여자가 자기의 무서운 비밀을 안다는 막연한 의심 하나만으로 그리한 것을 이제 누가 이상하게 여기겠는가?"], ["The disclosure of that secret might, in past years, have hanged him—might now transport him for life.", "그 비밀이 드러났다면 지난 세월에는 그를 교수대에 올렸을 것이고, 지금이라면 종신 유형에 처할 것이었다."], ["The disclosure of that secret, even if the sufferers by his deception spared him the penalties of the law, would deprive him at one blow of the name, the rank, the estate, the whole social existence that he had usurped.", "설령 그의 속임수에 당한 이들이 법의 처벌만은 면하게 해 준다 해도, 그 비밀이 드러나면 그는 자기가 가로챈 이름과 지위와 영지와 사회적 존재 전부를 단번에 잃을 터였다."], ["This was the Secret, and it was mine!", "이것이 바로 그 비밀이었고, 그것은 내 손에 있었다!"], ["A word from me, and house, lands, baronetcy, were gone from him for ever—a word from me, and he was driven out into the world, a nameless, penniless, friendless outcast!", "내가 한마디만 하면 저택도 땅도 준남작 작위도 그에게서 영영 사라진다 — 내가 한마디만 하면 그는 이름도 돈도 벗도 없는 부랑자가 되어 세상 밖으로 내쫓긴다!"], ["The man’s whole future hung on my lips—and he knew it by this time as certainly as I did!", "그 사내의 앞날 전체가 내 입술에 매달려 있었고 — 이제 그도 나만큼이나 그것을 확실히 알고 있었다!"]], [["That last thought steadied me.", "그 마지막 생각이 나를 다잡았다."], ["Interests far more precious than my own depended on the caution which must now guide my slightest actions.", "이제 내 사소한 행동 하나하나까지 이끌어야 할 신중함에, 내 것보다 훨씬 더 귀한 이해가 걸려 있었다."], ["There was no possible treachery which Sir Percival might not attempt against me.", "퍼시벌 경이 내게 저지르지 못할 배신이란 없었다."], ["In the danger and desperation of his position he would be staggered by no risks, he would recoil at no crime—he would literally hesitate at nothing to save himself.", "그 위태롭고 절박한 처지에서 그는 어떤 위험 앞에서도 물러서지 않을 것이고 어떤 범죄 앞에서도 움츠러들지 않을 것이었다. 제 몸을 구하기 위해서라면 그는 말 그대로 무슨 짓이든 서슴지 않을 사람이었다."]], [["I considered for a minute.", "나는 잠시 궁리했다."], ["My first necessity was to secure positive evidence in writing of the discovery that I had just made, and in the event of any personal misadventure happening to me, to place that evidence beyond Sir Percival’s reach.", "무엇보다 먼저 할 일은 방금 내가 해낸 발견을 문서로 확실히 남기는 것, 그리고 내 신상에 무슨 변고가 생길 경우에 대비해 그 증거를 퍼시벌 경의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 두는 것이었다."], ["The copy of the register was sure to be safe in Mr. Wansborough’s strong room.", "대장 사본은 완즈버러 씨의 금고방에 있으니 안전할 것이 분명했다."], ["But the position of the original in the vestry was, as I had seen with my own eyes, anything but secure.", "그러나 성구실에 있는 원본의 처지는, 내 눈으로 직접 보았듯이 안전과는 거리가 멀었다."]], [["In this emergency I resolved to return to the church, to apply again to the clerk, and to take the necessary extract from the register before I slept that night.", "이 다급한 사정에 나는 교회로 되돌아가 다시 서기에게 부탁해서, 그날 밤 잠들기 전에 대장에서 필요한 부분을 베껴 두기로 결심했다."], ["I was not then aware that a legally-certified copy was necessary, and that no document merely drawn out by myself could claim the proper importance as a proof.", "그때 나는 법적으로 인증된 등본이라야 하며 나 혼자 작성한 서류는 증거로서 마땅한 무게를 지닐 수 없다는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["I was not aware of this, and my determination to keep my present proceedings a secret prevented me from asking any questions which might have procured the necessary information.", "나는 그것을 몰랐고, 지금 하는 일을 비밀로 지키겠다는 결심 때문에 그 정보를 얻을 수도 있었을 질문을 아무것도 하지 못했다."], ["My one anxiety was the anxiety to get back to Old Welmingham.", "내 유일한 조바심은 올드 웰밍엄으로 돌아가려는 조바심이었다."], ["I made the best excuses I could for the discomposure in my face and manner which Mr. Wansborough had already noticed, laid the necessary fee on his table, arranged that I should write to him in a day or two, and left the office, with my head in a whirl and my blood throbbing through my veins at fever heat.", "나는 완즈버러 씨가 이미 눈치챈 내 얼굴과 태도의 흐트러짐에 대해 되도록 그럴듯한 변명을 둘러대고, 필요한 수수료를 그의 탁자에 올려놓고, 하루이틀 안에 편지를 드리겠다고 약속한 뒤, 머릿속이 어지럽고 열병처럼 피가 혈관을 두드리는 채로 사무실을 나섰다."]], [["It was just getting dark.", "막 어두워지려는 참이었다."], ["The idea occurred to me that I might be followed again and attacked on the high-road.", "큰길에서 다시 미행당하고 습격당할지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다."]], [["My walking-stick was a light one, of little or no use for purposes of defence.", "내 지팡이는 가벼운 것이라 방어에는 거의, 아니 전혀 쓸모가 없었다."], ["I stopped before leaving Knowlesbury and bought a stout country cudgel, short, and heavy at the head.", "나는 놀즈베리를 떠나기 전에 걸음을 멈추고 튼튼한 시골 몽둥이를 하나 샀다. 짧고 머리가 묵직한 것이었다."], ["With this homely weapon, if any one man tried to stop me I was a match for him.", "이 투박한 무기만 있으면 한 사람이 나를 막아서는 정도야 맞설 만했다."], ["If more than one attacked me I could trust to my heels.", "여럿이 덤빈다면 두 다리를 믿으면 되었다."], ["In my school-days I had been a noted runner, and I had not wanted for practice since in the later time of my experience in Central America.", "학창 시절 나는 이름난 달리기 선수였고, 그 뒤 중앙아메리카에서 겪은 시절에도 연습이 모자란 적은 없었다."]], [["I started from the town at a brisk pace, and kept the middle of the road.", "나는 빠른 걸음으로 읍내를 나서서 길 한복판으로만 걸었다."]], [["A small misty rain was falling, and it was impossible for the first half of the way to make sure whether I was followed or not.", "안개 같은 가랑비가 내리고 있어서, 길의 전반부 동안에는 미행이 붙었는지 아닌지 확인할 수 없었다."], ["But at the last half of my journey, when I supposed myself to be about two miles from the church, I saw a man run by me in the rain, and then heard the gate of a field by the roadside shut to sharply.", "그러나 후반부에 들어 교회에서 이 마일쯤 떨어졌겠거니 짐작하던 참에, 한 사내가 빗속에서 나를 앞질러 달려가는 것이 보였고, 이어 길가 밭의 문이 탁 닫히는 소리가 들렸다."], ["I kept straight on, with my cudgel ready in my hand, my ears on the alert, and my eyes straining to see through the mist and the darkness.", "나는 몽둥이를 손에 쥐고 귀를 곤두세우고 안개와 어둠 너머를 뚫어 보려 눈을 부릅뜬 채 곧장 걸어 나갔다."], ["Before I had advanced a hundred yards there was a rustling in the hedge on my right, and three men sprang out into the road.", "백 야드도 채 나아가기 전에 오른쪽 산울타리에서 부스럭 소리가 나더니 사내 셋이 길로 뛰어나왔다."]], [["I drew aside on the instant to the footpath.", "나는 순간적으로 옆의 보도로 비켜섰다."], ["The two foremost men were carried beyond me before they could check themselves.", "앞선 두 사내는 미처 몸을 멈추지 못하고 나를 지나쳐 갔다."], ["The third was as quick as lightning.", "세 번째는 번개처럼 빨랐다."], ["He stopped, half turned, and struck at me with his stick.", "그는 멈춰 서서 반쯤 돌아서더니 막대기로 나를 후려쳤다."], ["The blow was aimed at hazard, and was not a severe one.", "아무렇게나 겨눈 일격이라 심하지는 않았다."], ["It fell on my left shoulder.", "그것은 내 왼쪽 어깨에 떨어졌다."], ["I returned it heavily on his head.", "나는 그의 머리에 묵직하게 되갚아 주었다."], ["He staggered back and jostled his two companions just as they were both rushing at me.", "그는 비틀거리며 물러나다가, 마침 나에게 달려들던 두 동료와 부딪쳤다."], ["This circumstance gave me a moment’s start.", "그 덕에 나는 한순간의 여유를 얻었다."], ["I slipped by them, and took to the middle of the road again at the top of my speed.", "나는 그들 곁을 빠져나가 다시 길 한복판으로 나서서 전속력으로 달렸다."]], [["The two unhurt men pursued me.", "다치지 않은 두 사내가 나를 뒤쫓았다."], ["They were both good runners—the road was smooth and level, and for the first five minutes or more I was conscious that I did not gain on them.", "둘 다 잘 달렸다. 길은 매끄럽고 평탄했으며, 처음 오 분 남짓 동안 나는 그들과의 거리를 벌리지 못하고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["It was perilous work to run for long in the darkness.", "어둠 속에서 오래 달리는 것은 위험한 일이었다."], ["I could barely see the dim black line of the hedges on either side, and any chance obstacle in the road would have thrown me down to a certainty.", "양옆 산울타리의 희미한 검은 선이 겨우 보일 뿐이어서 길에 무엇이라도 걸리면 영락없이 나동그라질 판이었다."], ["Ere long I felt the ground changing—it descended from the level at a turn, and then rose again beyond.", "이윽고 땅이 달라지는 것이 느껴졌다. 굽이에서 평지가 내리막으로 떨어졌다가 그 너머에서 다시 솟아올랐다."], ["Downhill the men rather gained on me, but uphill I began to distance them.", "내리막에서는 사내들이 오히려 나를 따라붙었으나, 오르막에서는 내가 거리를 벌리기 시작했다."], ["The rapid, regular thump of their feet grew fainter on my ear, and I calculated by the sound that I was far enough in advance to take to the fields with a good chance of their passing me in the darkness.", "규칙적으로 빠르게 울리던 그들의 발소리가 귀에서 점점 희미해졌고, 나는 그 소리로 미루어 어둠 속에서 그들이 나를 지나쳐 갈 만큼 충분히 앞섰다고 셈하고 들판으로 들어가기로 했다."], ["Diverging to the footpath, I made for the first break that I could guess at, rather than see, in the hedge.", "보도 쪽으로 벗어나, 보인다기보다 짐작으로 산울타리의 첫 번째 틈을 향해 나아갔다."], ["It proved to be a closed gate.", "그것은 닫힌 문이었다."], ["I vaulted over, and finding myself in a field, kept across it steadily with my back to the road.", "나는 뛰어넘었고, 밭 안에 들어선 것을 알고는 길을 등진 채 꾸준히 밭을 가로질렀다."], ["I heard the men pass the gate, still running, then in a minute more heard one of them call to the other to come back.", "사내들이 여전히 달리며 그 문을 지나치는 소리가 들렸고, 일 분쯤 뒤에는 하나가 다른 하나에게 돌아오라고 외치는 소리가 들렸다."], ["It was no matter what they did now, I was out of their sight and out of their hearing.", "이제 그들이 무엇을 하든 상관없었다. 나는 그들의 눈에서도 귀에서도 벗어나 있었다."], ["I kept straight across the field, and when I had reached the farther extremity of it, waited there for a minute to recover my breath.", "나는 밭을 곧장 가로질러 끝까지 간 뒤, 거기서 잠시 숨을 돌리며 기다렸다."]], [["It was impossible to venture back to the road, but I was determined nevertheless to get to Old Welmingham that evening.", "큰길로 되돌아가는 모험은 할 수 없었으나, 그럼에도 나는 그날 저녁 안에 올드 웰밍엄에 닿고야 말겠다고 마음먹었다."]], [["Neither moon nor stars appeared to guide me.", "길잡이가 되어 줄 달도 별도 없었다."], ["I only knew that I had kept the wind and rain at my back on leaving Knowlesbury, and if I now kept them at my back still, I might at least be certain of not advancing altogether in the wrong direction.", "다만 놀즈베리를 떠날 때 바람과 비를 등지고 걸었다는 것만은 알았으므로, 지금도 그것들을 등지고 간다면 적어도 아주 엉뚱한 방향으로 나아가지는 않으리라 확신할 수 있었다."]], [["Proceeding on this plan, I crossed the country—meeting with no worse obstacles than hedges, ditches, and thickets, which every now and then obliged me to alter my course for a little while—until I found myself on a hill-side, with the ground sloping away steeply before me.", "이 방침대로 나는 들판을 가로질렀다. 이따금 잠시 진로를 바꾸게 만드는 산울타리와 도랑과 덤불 말고는 더 나쁜 장애를 만나지 않은 채 나아가다가, 어느 비탈에 이르러 앞으로 땅이 가파르게 내려가는 것을 보았다."], ["I descended to the bottom of the hollow, squeezed my way through a hedge, and got out into a lane.", "나는 골짜기 바닥까지 내려가 산울타리를 비집고 빠져나와 좁은 길로 나섰다."], ["Having turned to the right on leaving the road, I now turned to the left, on the chance of regaining the line from which I had wandered.", "큰길을 벗어날 때 오른쪽으로 돌았으므로, 이번에는 내가 벗어난 선으로 되돌아가 볼 요량으로 왼쪽으로 돌았다."], ["After following the muddy windings of the lane for ten minutes or more, I saw a cottage with a light in one of the windows.", "십 분 남짓 진창인 좁은 길의 굽이를 따라간 끝에, 창 하나에 불이 켜진 오두막이 보였다."], ["The garden gate was open to the lane, and I went in at once to inquire my way.", "정원 문이 길 쪽으로 열려 있어서 나는 길을 물으려고 곧장 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["Before I could knock at the door it was suddenly opened, and a man came running out with a lighted lantern in his hand.", "문을 두드리기도 전에 문이 갑자기 열리더니 한 사내가 불 켜진 등을 손에 들고 뛰어나왔다."], ["He stopped and held it up at the sight of me.", "그는 나를 보고 멈춰 서서 등을 치켜들었다."], ["We both started as we saw each other.", "우리는 서로를 알아보고 둘 다 흠칫했다."], ["My wanderings had led me round the outskirts of the village, and had brought me out at the lower end of it.", "헤매던 걸음이 나를 마을 변두리로 빙 둘러 데려와 아래쪽 끝으로 내놓았던 것이다."], ["I was back at Old Welmingham, and the man with the lantern was no other than my acquaintance of the morning, the parish clerk.", "나는 올드 웰밍엄에 돌아와 있었고, 등을 든 사내는 다름 아닌 그날 아침에 만난 그 교구 서기였다."]], [["His manner appeared to have altered strangely in the interval since I had last seen him.", "그의 태도는 내가 마지막으로 본 뒤로 그사이에 이상하게 달라져 있었다."], ["He looked suspicious and confused—his ruddy cheeks were deeply flushed—and his first words, when he spoke, were quite unintelligible to me.", "그는 의심스럽고 얼떨떨한 낯빛이었고, 불그레하던 뺨은 시뻘겋게 달아올라 있었다. 그리고 그가 입을 열어 처음 한 말은 내게 도무지 알아들을 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["“Where are the keys?”", "“열쇠는 어디 있습니까?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Have you taken them?”", "“선생이 가져가셨소?”"]], [["“What keys?”", "“무슨 열쇠 말이오?”"], ["I repeated.", "내가 되물었다."], ["“I have this moment come from Knowlesbury.", "“나는 방금 놀즈베리에서 오는 길이오."], ["What keys do you mean?”", "대체 무슨 열쇠를 말하는 겁니까?”"]], [["“The keys of the vestry.", "“성구실 열쇠 말이오."], ["Lord save us and help us!", "주여, 우리를 구하시고 도우소서!"], ["what shall I do?", "어떡하면 좋단 말인가?"], ["The keys are gone!", "열쇠가 없어졌소!"], ["Do you hear?”", "들립니까?”"], ["cried the old man, shaking the lantern at me in his agitation, “the keys are gone!”", "노인은 흥분해서 등을 내 앞에 흔들어 대며 소리쳤다. “열쇠가 없어졌단 말이오!”"]], [["“How? When?", "“어떻게 말이오?"], ["Who can have taken them?”", "언제? 대체 누가 가져갔단 말입니까?”"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“모르겠소.”"], ["said the clerk, staring about him wildly in the darkness.", "서기는 어둠 속에서 이리저리 미친 듯이 두리번거리며 말했다."], ["“I’ve only just got back.", "“나도 방금 막 돌아왔소."], ["I told you I had a long day’s work this morning—I locked the door and shut the window down—it’s open now, the window’s open.", "아침에 하루 종일 일이 많다고 말씀드렸지요 — 문을 잠그고 창문도 내려 닫았단 말입니다 — 그런데 지금은 열려 있어요, 창문이 열려 있단 말이오."], ["Look!", "보시오!"], ["somebody has got in there and taken the keys.”", "누가 저기 들어가서 열쇠를 가져갔어요.”"]], [["He turned to the casement window to show me that it was wide open.", "그는 여닫이창 쪽으로 돌아서서 그것이 활짝 열려 있음을 내게 보여 주었다."], ["The door of the lantern came loose from its fastening as he swayed it round, and the wind blew the candle out instantly.", "그가 등을 휘두르는 바람에 등피 문이 걸쇠에서 풀렸고, 바람이 순식간에 촛불을 꺼 버렸다."]], [["“Get another light,”", "“불을 다시 켜 오시오.”"], ["I said, “and let us both go to the vestry together.", "내가 말했다. “그리고 둘이 함께 성구실로 갑시다."], ["Quick!", "어서!"], ["quick!”", "어서요!”"]], [["I hurried him into the house.", "나는 그를 재촉해 집 안으로 들여보냈다."], ["The treachery that I had every reason to expect, the treachery that might deprive me of every advantage I had gained, was at that moment, perhaps, in process of accomplishment.", "내가 예상할 만한 온갖 이유가 있었던 그 배신, 내가 얻어 낸 모든 이점을 앗아 갈지도 모르는 그 배신이 어쩌면 바로 그 순간에 진행되고 있을 터였다."], ["My impatience to reach the church was so great that I could not remain inactive in the cottage while the clerk lit the lantern again.", "교회에 닿고 싶은 조바심이 어찌나 컸던지 서기가 등에 다시 불을 붙이는 동안 오두막 안에 가만히 있을 수가 없었다."], ["I walked out, down the garden path, into the lane.", "나는 밖으로 나가 정원 길을 따라 내려가 좁은 길로 들어섰다."]], [["Before I had advanced ten paces a man approached me from the direction leading to the church.", "열 걸음도 채 나아가기 전에 교회 쪽에서 오는 사내 하나가 내게 다가왔다."], ["He spoke respectfully as we met.", "우리가 마주쳤을 때 그는 공손하게 말을 건넸다."], ["I could not see his face, but judging by his voice only, he was a perfect stranger to me.", "얼굴은 보이지 않았으나 목소리로만 판단하건대 나로서는 전혀 모르는 사람이었다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Sir Percival——”", "“실례합니다, 퍼시벌 경——”"], ["he began.", "그가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["I stopped him before he could say more.", "나는 그가 더 말하기 전에 그를 막았다."]], [["“The darkness misleads you,”", "“어두워서 잘못 보신 거요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I am not Sir Percival.”", "“나는 퍼시벌 경이 아니오.”"]], [["The man drew back directly.", "사내는 즉시 물러섰다."]], [["“I thought it was my master,”", "“저희 주인님이신 줄 알았습니다.”"], ["he muttered, in a confused, doubtful way.", "그는 얼떨떨하고 미심쩍은 투로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“You expected to meet your master here?”", "“여기서 주인을 만나기로 되어 있었소?”"]], [["“I was told to wait in the lane.”", "“이 길에서 기다리라는 분부를 받았습니다.”"]], [["With that answer he retraced his steps.", "그 대답을 남기고 그는 왔던 길을 되짚어 갔다."], ["I looked back at the cottage and saw the clerk coming out, with the lantern lighted once more.", "오두막 쪽을 돌아보니 서기가 다시 불을 붙인 등을 들고 나오는 것이 보였다."], ["I took the old man’s arm to help him on the more quickly.", "나는 노인의 팔을 붙들어 걸음을 더 재촉하게 했다."], ["We hastened along the lane, and passed the person who had accosted me.", "우리는 좁은 길을 서둘러 지나가며 아까 내게 말을 건 사람 옆을 스쳤다."], ["As well as I could see by the light of the lantern, he was a servant out of livery.", "등불 빛에 보이는 대로 판단하건대 그는 제복을 입지 않은 하인이었다."]], [["“Who’s that?”", "“저 사람은 누굽니까?”"], ["whispered the clerk.", "서기가 속삭였다."], ["“Does he know anything about the keys?”", "“열쇠에 대해 뭘 알고 있는 걸까요?”"]], [["“We won’t wait to ask him,” I replied.", "“물어보느라 지체할 것 없소.”"], ["“We will go on to the vestry first.”", "내가 대답했다. “먼저 성구실로 갑시다.”"]], [["The church was not visible, even by daytime, until the end of the lane was reached.", "교회는 낮에도 좁은 길 끝에 이르기 전에는 보이지 않았다."], ["As we mounted the rising ground which led to the building from that point, one of the village children—a boy—came close up to us, attracted by the light we carried, and recognised the clerk.", "그 지점에서 건물로 이어지는 오르막을 오르는데, 우리가 든 불빛에 이끌린 마을 아이 하나가 — 사내아이였다 — 바짝 다가와 서기를 알아보았다."]], [["“I say, measter,”", "“저기예, 아저씨.”"], ["said the boy, pulling officiously at the clerk’s coat, “there be summun up yander in the church.", "아이가 서기의 웃옷을 참견하듯 잡아당기며 말했다. “교회 저 우에 누가 있어예."], ["I heerd un lock the door on hisself—I heerd un strike a loight wi’ a match.”", "안에서 지가 문을 잠그는 소리를 내가 들었다카이 — 성냥으로 불 켜는 소리도 들었다카이.”"]], [["The clerk trembled and leaned against me heavily.", "서기는 부들부들 떨며 내게 무겁게 기대었다."]], [["“Come!", "“자!"], ["come!”", "자!”"], ["I said encouragingly.", "나는 기운을 북돋우며 말했다."], ["“We are not too late.", "“아직 늦지 않았소."], ["We will catch the man, whoever he is.", "누구든 그자를 붙잡읍시다."], ["Keep the lantern, and follow me as fast as you can.”", "등을 들고 되도록 빨리 나를 따라오시오.”"]], [["I mounted the hill rapidly.", "나는 서둘러 언덕을 올랐다."], ["The dark mass of the church-tower was the first object I discerned dimly against the night sky.", "밤하늘을 배경으로 어렴풋이 제일 먼저 알아본 것은 교회 탑의 검은 덩어리였다."], ["As I turned aside to get round to the vestry, I heard heavy footsteps close to me.", "성구실 쪽으로 돌아가려고 옆으로 비켜서는데 바로 곁에서 묵직한 발소리가 들렸다."], ["The servant had ascended to the church after us.", "그 하인이 우리 뒤를 따라 교회까지 올라온 것이었다."], ["“I don’t mean any harm,”", "“해치려는 게 아닙니다.”"], ["he said, when I turned round on him, “I’m only looking for my master.”", "내가 돌아서서 노려보자 그가 말했다. “저는 그저 주인님을 찾고 있을 뿐입니다.”"], ["The tones in which he spoke betrayed unmistakable fear.", "그 말투에는 틀림없는 두려움이 배어 있었다."], ["I took no notice of him and went on.", "나는 그를 아랑곳하지 않고 계속 나아갔다."]], [["The instant I turned the corner and came in view of the vestry, I saw the lantern-skylight on the roof brilliantly lit up from within.", "모퉁이를 돌아 성구실이 눈에 들어온 순간, 나는 지붕의 채광창이 안에서부터 환하게 밝혀져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["It shone out with dazzling brightness against the murky, starless sky.", "그것은 별 하나 없는 컴컴한 하늘을 배경으로 눈부시게 빛나고 있었다."]], [["I hurried through the churchyard to the door.", "나는 교회 묘지를 가로질러 문으로 달려갔다."]], [["As I got near there was a strange smell stealing out on the damp night air.", "가까이 다가가자 축축한 밤공기 속으로 이상한 냄새가 새어 나오고 있었다."], ["I heard a snapping noise inside—I saw the light above grow brighter and brighter—a pane of the glass cracked—I ran to the door and put my hand on it.", "안에서 무언가 탁탁거리는 소리가 들렸다 — 위쪽의 불빛이 점점 더 밝아지는 것이 보였다 — 유리 한 장이 쩍 갈라졌다 — 나는 문으로 달려가 손을 얹었다."], ["The vestry was on fire!", "성구실에 불이 난 것이었다!"]], [["Before I could move, before I could draw my breath after that discovery, I was horror-struck by a heavy thump against the door from the inside.", "미처 움직이기도 전에, 그것을 알고 숨을 돌리기도 전에, 나는 안쪽에서 문에 부딪히는 묵직한 소리에 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["I heard the key worked violently in the lock—I heard a man’s voice behind the door, raised to a dreadful shrillness, screaming for help.", "열쇠가 자물쇠 안에서 거칠게 돌아가는 소리가 들렸다 — 문 뒤에서 무섭도록 새된 소리로 높아진 한 사내의 목소리가 살려 달라고 비명을 지르는 것이 들렸다."]], [["The servant who had followed me staggered back shuddering, and dropped to his knees.", "나를 뒤따라온 하인이 몸서리치며 비틀비틀 물러나더니 무릎을 꿇었다. “오, 하느님!”"], ["“Oh, my God!”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “it’s Sir Percival!”", "“퍼시벌 경이십니다!”"]], [["As the words passed his lips the clerk joined us, and at the same moment there was another and a last grating turn of the key in the lock.", "그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지는 순간 서기가 우리에게 합류했고, 동시에 자물쇠 속에서 열쇠가 갈리는 소리를 내며 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아갔다."]], [["“The Lord have mercy on his soul!”", "“주여, 저 영혼에 자비를 베푸소서!”"], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."], ["“He is doomed and dead.", "“저분은 이제 끝장이오, 죽은 목숨이오."], ["He has hampered the lock.”", "자물쇠를 망가뜨려 버렸소.”"]], [["I rushed to the door.", "나는 문으로 달려들었다."], ["The one absorbing purpose that had filled all my thoughts, that had controlled all my actions, for weeks and weeks past, vanished in an instant from my mind.", "몇 주 동안이나 내 모든 생각을 채우고 내 모든 행동을 지배해 온 그 한 가지 골똘한 목적이 순식간에 마음에서 사라졌다."], ["All remembrance of the heartless injury the man’s crimes had inflicted—of the love, the innocence, the happiness he had pitilessly laid waste—of the oath I had sworn in my own heart to summon him to the terrible reckoning that he deserved—passed from my memory like a dream.", "그 사내의 죄가 안긴 무정한 상처에 대한 기억도, 그가 무자비하게 짓밟은 사랑과 순결과 행복에 대한 기억도, 그에게 마땅한 무서운 심판을 반드시 치르게 하겠노라고 마음속에 세운 맹세에 대한 기억도 모두 꿈처럼 내 기억에서 지워졌다."], ["I remembered nothing but the horror of his situation.", "나는 그가 처한 상황의 끔찍함 외에는 아무것도 기억하지 못했다."], ["I felt nothing but the natural human impulse to save him from a frightful death.", "그를 무서운 죽음에서 구해 내려는 자연스러운 인간의 충동 외에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했다."]], [["“Try the other door!”", "“다른 문을 열어 보시오!”"], ["I shouted.", "나는 소리쳤다."], ["“Try the door into the church!", "“교회로 통하는 문 말이오!"], ["The lock’s hampered.", "자물쇠가 망가졌소."], ["You’re a dead man if you waste another moment on it.”", "거기에 한순간이라도 더 지체하면 당신은 죽은 목숨이오.”"]], [["There had been no renewed cry for help when the key was turned for the last time.", "열쇠가 마지막으로 돌아간 뒤로는 살려 달라는 외침이 다시 들리지 않았다."], ["There was no sound now of any kind, to give token that he was still alive.", "그가 아직 살아 있음을 알려 줄 어떤 소리도 이제는 나지 않았다."], ["I heard nothing but the quickening crackle of the flames, and the sharp snap of the glass in the skylight above.", "들리는 것이라고는 점점 빨라지는 불길의 탁탁거림과 위쪽 채광창 유리가 날카롭게 튀는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["I looked round at my two companions.", "나는 두 동행을 둘러보았다."], ["The servant had risen to his feet—he had taken the lantern, and was holding it up vacantly at the door.", "하인은 일어서서 등을 들고는 멍하니 문 쪽으로 치켜들고 있었다."], ["Terror seemed to have struck him with downright idiocy—he waited at my heels, he followed me about when I moved like a dog.", "공포가 그를 완전한 백치로 만들어 버린 듯했다. 그는 내 발치에서 기다리다가 내가 움직이면 개처럼 나를 따라다녔다."], ["The clerk sat crouched up on one of the tombstones, shivering, and moaning to himself.", "서기는 묘비 하나에 웅크리고 앉아 벌벌 떨며 혼자 신음하고 있었다."], ["The one moment in which I looked at them was enough to show me that they were both helpless.", "그들을 바라본 그 한순간만으로도 둘 다 아무 쓸모가 없다는 것을 알기에 충분했다."]], [["Hardly knowing what I did, acting desperately on the first impulse that occurred to me, I seized the servant and pushed him against the vestry wall.", "내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 모른 채, 문득 떠오른 첫 충동에 따라 필사적으로 움직여, 나는 하인을 붙들어 성구실 벽에 밀어붙였다. “엎드리시오!”"], ["“Stoop!”", "내가 말했다."], ["I said, “and hold by the stones.", "“그리고 돌을 붙잡고 버티시오."], ["I am going to climb over you to the roof—I am going to break the skylight, and give him some air!”", "당신을 밟고 지붕으로 올라가겠소 — 채광창을 깨뜨려서 저 사람에게 공기를 넣어 주겠소!”"]], [["The man trembled from head to foot, but he held firm.", "사내는 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨었으나 굳게 버텨 주었다."], ["I got on his back, with my cudgel in my mouth, seized the parapet with both hands, and was instantly on the roof.", "나는 몽둥이를 입에 물고 그의 등에 올라서서 두 손으로 난간을 붙잡았고, 순식간에 지붕 위에 올라섰다."], ["In the frantic hurry and agitation of the moment, it never struck me that I might let out the flame instead of letting in the air.", "그 미친 듯한 다급함과 동요 속에서, 공기를 들여보내는 대신 불길을 내보내게 될지도 모른다는 생각은 조금도 들지 않았다."], ["I struck at the skylight, and battered in the cracked, loosened glass at a blow.", "나는 채광창을 내리쳐 금이 가고 헐거워진 유리를 단번에 두들겨 부수었다."], ["The fire leaped out like a wild beast from its lair.", "불길이 굴에서 뛰쳐나오는 들짐승처럼 솟구쳤다."], ["If the wind had not chanced, in the position I occupied, to set it away from me, my exertions might have ended then and there.", "내가 선 자리에서 마침 바람이 불길을 내게서 밀어내 주지 않았더라면, 내 안간힘은 그 자리에서 끝나 버렸을 것이다."], ["I crouched on the roof as the smoke poured out above me with the flame.", "나는 연기가 불길과 함께 머리 위로 쏟아져 나오는 동안 지붕에 웅크렸다."], ["The gleams and flashes of the light showed me the servant’s face staring up vacantly under the wall—the clerk risen to his feet on the tombstone, wringing his hands in despair—and the scanty population of the village, haggard men and terrified women, clustered beyond in the churchyard—all appearing and disappearing, in the red of the dreadful glare, in the black of the choking smoke.", "어른거리며 번쩍이는 불빛에 벽 아래에서 멍하니 올려다보는 하인의 얼굴이 보였고, 묘비 위에 일어서서 절망에 겨워 두 손을 비틀어 대는 서기가 보였고, 그 너머 교회 묘지에 몰려든 마을의 몇 안 되는 사람들, 초췌한 사내들과 겁에 질린 여인들이 보였다. 모두가 그 무시무시한 붉은 불빛 속에, 숨 막히는 검은 연기 속에 나타났다 사라졌다 했다."], ["And the man beneath my feet!—the man, suffocating, burning, dying so near us all, so utterly beyond our reach!", "그리고 내 발밑의 그 사내! — 우리 모두와 그토록 가까이에서 질식하고 불타고 죽어 가면서도, 우리 손이 도무지 닿지 않는 그 사내!"]], [["The thought half maddened me.", "그 생각에 나는 반쯤 미칠 지경이었다."], ["I lowered myself from the roof, by my hands, and dropped to the ground.", "나는 두 손으로 지붕에 매달려 몸을 내린 뒤 땅으로 뛰어내렸다."]], [["“The key of the church!”", "“교회 열쇠 말이오!”"], ["I shouted to the clerk.", "나는 서기에게 소리쳤다."], ["“We must try it that way—we may save him yet if we can burst open the inner door.”", "“그쪽으로 해 봐야 하오 — 안쪽 문을 부수고 열 수만 있다면 아직 저 사람을 구할 수 있을지 모르오.”"]], [["“No, no, no!” cried the old man.", "“안 되오, 안 돼요, 안 됩니다!”"], ["“No hope!", "노인이 외쳤다."], ["the church key and the vestry key are on the same ring—both inside there!", "“가망 없소! 교회 열쇠와 성구실 열쇠는 같은 고리에 꿰여 있소 — 둘 다 저 안에 있단 말이오!"], ["Oh, sir, he’s past saving—he’s dust and ashes by this time!”", "아, 선생, 저분은 이미 구할 수 없소 — 지금쯤은 재와 티끌이 되었을 거요!”"]], [["“They’ll see the fire from the town,”", "“읍내에서 불을 볼 거라예.”"], ["said a voice from among the men behind me.", "내 뒤의 사람들 틈에서 한 목소리가 말했다."], ["“There’s a ingine in the town.", "“읍내에 소방 펌푸가 있으니께."], ["They’ll save the church.”", "교회는 살릴 거라예.”"]], [["I called to that man—HE had his wits about him—I called to him to come and speak to me.", "나는 그 남자를 불렀다—그는 정신을 놓지 않고 있었다—나는 그에게 이리 와서 나와 이야기하자고 소리쳤다."], ["It would be a quarter of an hour at least before the town engine could reach us.", "마을의 소방 펌프가 여기까지 닿으려면 적어도 십오 분은 걸릴 터였다."], ["The horror of remaining inactive all that time was more than I could face.", "그 시간 내내 아무것도 하지 못한 채 있어야 한다는 공포는 내가 견딜 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["In defiance of my own reason I persuaded myself that the doomed and lost wretch in the vestry might still be lying senseless on the floor, might not be dead yet.", "나는 내 이성을 거스르면서까지, 성구실 안에서 파멸을 맞은 그 가엾은 자가 아직 바닥에 정신을 잃고 누워 있을 뿐 죽지는 않았을지도 모른다고 스스로를 설득했다."], ["If we broke open the door, might we save him?", "문을 부수고 들어간다면 그를 구할 수 있지 않을까?"], ["I knew the strength of the heavy lock—I knew the thickness of the nailed oak—I knew the hopelessness of assailing the one and the other by ordinary means.", "나는 그 육중한 자물쇠의 힘을 알고 있었다—못을 박은 참나무 문의 두께를 알고 있었다—평범한 방법으로 그 둘에 맞서 보아야 가망이 없다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["But surely there were beams still left in the dismantled cottages near the church?", "그러나 교회 근처에 헐어 놓은 오두막들에는 아직 들보가 남아 있지 않겠는가?"], ["What if we got one, and used it as a battering-ram against the door?", "그중 하나를 가져다 문을 쳐부술 공성추로 쓴다면 어떨까?"]], [["The thought leaped through me like the fire leaping out of the shattered skylight.", "그 생각은 부서진 채광창으로 치솟는 불길처럼 내 몸을 꿰뚫고 지나갔다."], ["I appealed to the man who had spoken first of the fire-engine in the town.", "나는 마을의 소방 펌프를 처음 입에 올린 남자에게 말을 걸었다."], ["“Have you got your pickaxes handy?”", "“곡괭이는 가까이 있소?”"], ["Yes, they had.", "있다고 했다."], ["“And a hatchet, and a saw, and a bit of rope?”", "“손도끼와 톱과 밧줄 한 가닥도?”"], ["Yes! yes!", "있다, 있다, 있다!"], ["yes! I ran down among the villagers, with the lantern in my hand.", "나는 등불을 손에 든 채 마을 사람들 사이로 뛰어 내려갔다."], ["“Five shillings apiece to every man who helps me!”", "“나를 돕는 사람에게는 한 사람당 오 실링씩 주겠소!”"], ["They started into life at the words.", "그 말에 사람들이 벌떡 살아났다."], ["That ravenous second hunger of poverty—the hunger for money—roused them into tumult and activity in a moment.", "가난이 품은 그 두 번째 게걸스러운 굶주림—돈에 대한 굶주림—이 순식간에 그들을 소란과 활기 속으로 몰아넣었다."], ["“Two of you for more lanterns, if you have them!", "“둘은 등불을 더 가져오시오, 있거든!"], ["Two of you for the pickaxes and the tools!", "둘은 곡괭이와 연장을 가져오고!"], ["The rest after me to find the beam!”", "나머지는 나를 따라와 들보를 찾읍시다!”"], ["They cheered—with shrill starveling voices they cheered.", "그들이 환호했다—굶주려 새된 목소리로 환호했다."], ["The women and the children fled back on either side.", "여자들과 아이들이 양옆으로 물러났다."], ["We rushed in a body down the churchyard path to the first empty cottage.", "우리는 한 덩어리가 되어 교회 묘지 길을 따라 첫 번째 빈 오두막으로 달려 내려갔다."], ["Not a man was left behind but the clerk—the poor old clerk standing on the flat tombstone sobbing and wailing over the church.", "뒤에 남은 사람은 서기 하나뿐이었다—그 딱한 늙은 서기는 평평한 묘석 위에 서서 교회를 두고 흐느끼며 통곡하고 있었다."], ["The servant was still at my heels—his white, helpless, panic-stricken face was close over my shoulder as we pushed into the cottage.", "하인은 여전히 내 뒤꿈치를 바짝 따라왔다—우리가 오두막 안으로 밀고 들어갈 때 그의 하얗고 무력하고 공포에 질린 얼굴이 내 어깨 너머에 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["There were rafters from the torn-down floor above, lying loose on the ground—but they were too light.", "위층에서 뜯겨 나온 서까래들이 땅바닥에 흩어져 있었으나 너무 가벼웠다."], ["A beam ran across over our heads, but not out of reach of our arms and our pickaxes—a beam fast at each end in the ruined wall, with ceiling and flooring all ripped away, and a great gap in the roof above, open to the sky.", "우리 머리 위로 들보 하나가 가로질러 있었는데, 팔과 곡괭이가 닿지 못할 높이는 아니었다—무너진 벽에 양끝이 단단히 박힌 들보였고, 천장과 마루는 죄다 뜯겨 나갔으며, 위쪽 지붕에는 하늘이 훤히 보이는 커다란 구멍이 뚫려 있었다."], ["We attacked the beam at both ends at once.", "우리는 들보의 양끝을 한꺼번에 공격했다."], ["God! how it held—how the brick and mortar of the wall resisted us!", "아, 어찌나 단단히 붙어 있던지—벽의 벽돌과 회반죽이 어찌나 우리에게 버티던지!"], ["We struck, and tugged, and tore.", "우리는 치고, 당기고, 뜯어냈다."], ["The beam gave at one end—it came down with a lump of brickwork after it.", "들보가 한쪽 끝에서 빠졌다—벽돌 덩이 하나를 달고 내려왔다."], ["There was a scream from the women all huddled in the doorway to look at us—a shout from the men—two of them down but not hurt.", "문간에 옹기종기 모여 우리를 보고 있던 여자들에게서 비명이 터졌고—남자들에게서는 고함이 터졌다—둘이 넘어졌으나 다치지는 않았다."], ["Another tug all together—and the beam was loose at both ends.", "다 함께 한 번 더 당기자—들보가 양끝에서 모두 풀려났다."], ["We raised it, and gave the word to clear the doorway.", "우리는 그것을 들어 올리고 문간을 비우라고 외쳤다."], ["Now for the work!", "자, 이제 일이다!"], ["now for the rush at the door!", "이제 문으로 돌진이다!"], ["There is the fire streaming into the sky, streaming brighter than ever to light us!", "저기 불길이 하늘로 뻗어 오른다, 그 어느 때보다 밝게 뻗어 올라 우리를 비춘다!"], ["Steady along the churchyard path—steady with the beam for a rush at the door.", "묘지 길을 따라 침착하게—들보를 들고 침착하게, 문을 향해 돌진할 준비."], ["One, two, three—and off.", "하나, 둘, 셋—간다."], ["Out rings the cheering again, irrepressibly.", "다시 환호가 터져 나온다, 걷잡을 수 없이."], ["We have shaken it already, the hinges must give if the lock won’t.", "벌써 문이 흔들렸다, 자물쇠가 버틴다면 돌쩌귀라도 나갈 것이다."], ["Another run with the beam!", "들보를 들고 한 번 더!"], ["One, two, three—and off.", "하나, 둘, 셋—간다."], ["It’s loose!", "헐거워졌다!"], ["the stealthy fire darts at us through the crevice all round it.", "그 틈으로 사방에서 불길이 슬금슬금 우리를 향해 혀를 내민다."], ["Another, and a last rush!", "한 번 더, 마지막 돌진!"], ["The door falls in with a crash.", "문이 요란한 소리를 내며 안으로 무너진다."], ["A great hush of awe, a stillness of breathless expectation, possesses every living soul of us.", "커다란 경외의 침묵이, 숨죽인 기다림의 정적이 우리 모두의 살아 있는 넋을 사로잡는다."], ["We look for the body.", "우리는 시신을 찾는다."], ["The scorching heat on our faces drives us back: we see nothing—above, below, all through the room, we see nothing but a sheet of living fire.", "얼굴을 태우는 열기가 우리를 뒤로 밀어낸다. 아무것도 보이지 않는다—위에도, 아래에도, 방 안 어디에도, 살아 움직이는 불의 장막 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다."]], [["“Where is he?”", "“그분은 어디 있습니까?”"], ["whispered the servant, staring vacantly at the flames.", "하인이 불길을 멍하니 응시하며 속삭였다."]], [["“He’s dust and ashes,”", "“재가 되고 먼지가 되었지요.”"], ["said the clerk.", "서기가 말했다."], ["“And the books are dust and ashes—and oh, sirs!", "“장부들도 재가 되고 먼지가 되었고—아아, 여러분!"], ["the church will be dust and ashes soon.”", "교회도 곧 재가 되고 먼지가 될 겁니다.”"]], [["Those were the only two who spoke.", "입을 연 사람은 그 둘뿐이었다."], ["When they were silent again, nothing stirred in the stillness but the bubble and the crackle of the flames.", "그들이 다시 잠잠해지자, 그 정적 속에서 움직이는 것이라고는 불길이 부글거리고 탁탁 튀는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["Hark!", "들어 보라!"]], [["A harsh rattling sound in the distance—then the hollow beat of horses’ hoofs at full gallop—then the low roar, the all-predominant tumult of hundreds of human voices clamouring and shouting together.", "멀리서 거칠게 덜컹거리는 소리—이어 전속력으로 달리는 말발굽의 텅 빈 울림—이어 낮은 포효, 수백 명이 한꺼번에 외치고 고함치는, 모든 것을 압도하는 소란."], ["The engine at last.", "마침내 소방 펌프가 온 것이었다."]], [["The people about me all turned from the fire, and ran eagerly to the brow of the hill.", "내 주위의 사람들이 모두 불에서 등을 돌려 언덕마루로 앞다투어 달려갔다."], ["The old clerk tried to go with the rest, but his strength was exhausted.", "늙은 서기도 나머지 사람들을 따라가려 했으나 기운이 다해 있었다."], ["I saw him holding by one of the tombstones.", "나는 그가 묘석 하나를 붙잡고 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["“Save the church!”", "“교회를 구해 주시오!”"], ["he cried out faintly, as if the firemen could hear him already.", "그는 마치 소방대원들이 벌써 자기 말을 들을 수 있기라도 한 듯 가냘프게 외쳤다."]], [["“Save the church!”", "“교회를 구해 주시오!”"]], [["The only man who never moved was the servant.", "끝내 움직이지 않은 단 한 사람은 그 하인이었다."], ["There he stood, his eyes still fastened on the flames in a changeless, vacant stare.", "그는 그 자리에 선 채, 변함없이 텅 빈 눈길로 불길에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["I spoke to him, I shook him by the arm.", "나는 그에게 말을 걸었고, 그의 팔을 흔들었다."], ["He was past rousing.", "그는 깨울 수 있는 상태가 아니었다."], ["He only whispered once more, “Where is he?”", "그는 다만 한 번 더 속삭였다. “그분은 어디 있습니까?”"]], [["In ten minutes the engine was in position, the well at the back of the church was feeding it, and the hose was carried to the doorway of the vestry.", "십 분 만에 소방 펌프가 자리를 잡았고, 교회 뒤편 우물이 물을 대었으며, 호스가 성구실 문간까지 끌려갔다."], ["If help had been wanted from me I could not have afforded it now.", "이제 와서 나에게 도움을 청했더라도 나는 아무것도 해 줄 수 없었을 것이다."], ["My energy of will was gone—my strength was exhausted—the turmoil of my thoughts was fearfully and suddenly stilled, now I knew that he was dead.", "내 의지의 기력은 사라졌고—힘은 다했으며—그가 죽었다는 것을 알게 된 지금, 소용돌이치던 내 생각은 무섭도록 갑작스럽게 잠잠해져 있었다."]], [["I stood useless and helpless—looking, looking, looking into the burning room.", "나는 쓸모없이 무력하게 서서, 불타는 방 안을 보고, 보고, 또 보았다."]], [["I saw the fire slowly conquered.", "나는 불이 서서히 제압되는 것을 보았다."], ["The brightness of the glare faded—the steam rose in white clouds, and the smouldering heaps of embers showed red and black through it on the floor.", "이글거리던 빛이 사그라들고—수증기가 흰 구름처럼 피어올랐으며, 그 사이로 바닥에 쌓인 잉걸불 더미가 붉고 검게 드러났다."], ["There was a pause—then an advance all together of the firemen and the police which blocked up the doorway—then a consultation in low voices—and then two men were detached from the rest, and sent out of the churchyard through the crowd.", "잠시 멈춤이 있었고—이어 소방대원들과 경찰이 다 함께 앞으로 나아가 문간을 막았고—이어 낮은 목소리로 의논이 오갔고—그런 다음 두 사람이 무리에서 떨어져 나와 군중을 헤치고 교회 묘지 밖으로 보내졌다."], ["The crowd drew back on either side in dead silence to let them pass.", "군중은 죽은 듯 조용히 양옆으로 물러나 그들을 지나가게 했다."]], [["After a while a great shudder ran through the people, and the living lane widened slowly.", "얼마 뒤 사람들 사이로 커다란 전율이 훑고 지나갔고, 살아 있는 사람들로 이루어진 그 길이 천천히 넓어졌다."], ["The men came back along it with a door from one of the empty houses.", "두 사람은 빈집에서 떼어 온 문짝 하나를 들고 그 길을 따라 돌아왔다."], ["They carried it to the vestry and went in.", "그들은 그것을 성구실로 옮겨 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The police closed again round the doorway, and men stole out from among the crowd by twos and threes and stood behind them to be the first to see.", "경찰이 다시 문간을 에워쌌고, 군중 속에서 남자들이 둘씩 셋씩 슬그머니 빠져나와 먼저 보려고 그들 뒤에 섰다."], ["Others waited near to be the first to hear.", "다른 이들은 먼저 들으려고 가까이서 기다렸다."], ["Women and children were among these last.", "여자들과 아이들이 그 나중 무리에 섞여 있었다."]], [["The tidings from the vestry began to flow out among the crowd—they dropped slowly from mouth to mouth till they reached the place where I was standing.", "성구실에서 나온 소식이 군중 사이로 퍼져 나가기 시작했다—그것은 입에서 입으로 천천히 흘러 내가 서 있는 자리까지 이르렀다."], ["I heard the questions and answers repeated again and again in low, eager tones all round me.", "나는 낮고 다급한 어조로 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 질문과 대답을 사방에서 들었다."]], [["“Have they found him?”", "“찾았대?”"], ["“Yes.”—“Where?”", "“찾았대.”—“어디서?”"], ["“Against the door, on his face.”—“Which door?”", "“문 앞에서, 엎드린 채로.”—“어느 문?”"], ["“The door that goes into the church.", "“교회로 통하는 문."], ["His head was against it—he was down on his face.”—“Is his face burnt?”", "머리가 그 문에 닿아 있었고—엎어져 있었대.”—“얼굴도 탔대?” “아니.”"], ["“No.” “Yes, it is.”", "“탔다니까.”"], ["“No, scorched, not burnt—he lay on his face, I tell you.”—“Who was he?", "“아니야, 그을렸을 뿐 타지는 않았어—엎드려 있었다고 하잖아.”—“누구래?"], ["A lord, they say.”", "귀족이라던데.”"], ["“No, not a lord.", "“아니야, 귀족은 아니고."], ["SIR Something; Sir means Knight.”", "‘서’ 뭐라던데, ‘서’는 기사라는 뜻이야.”"], ["“And Baronight, too.” “No.”", "“‘준남작’인가 하는 것도 되고.”"], ["“Yes, it does.”—“What did he want in there?”", "“아니야.” “된다니까.”—“거기서 뭘 하려던 거래?”"], ["“No good, you may depend on it.”—“Did he do it on purpose?”—“Burn himself on purpose!”—“I don’t mean himself, I mean the vestry.”—“Is he dreadful to look at?”", "“좋은 일은 아니었겠지, 그건 틀림없어.”—“일부러 그런 걸까?”—“일부러 제 몸을 태워?”—“제 몸 말고, 성구실 말이야.”—“보기에 끔찍해?”"], ["“Dreadful!”—“Not about the face, though?”", "“끔찍하지!”—“그래도 얼굴은 아니지?”"], ["“No, no, not so much about the face.", "“응, 응, 얼굴은 그렇게까지는 아니래."], ["Don’t anybody know him?”", "아는 사람 없나?”"], ["“There’s a man says he does.”—“Who?”", "“안다는 사람이 하나 있다던데.”—“누구?”"], ["“A servant, they say.", "“하인이라나 봐."], ["But he’s struck stupid-like, and the police don’t believe him.”—“Don’t anybody else know who it is?”", "그런데 넋이 나가 버려서 경찰이 그 말을 안 믿는대.”—“그 사람 말고 누군지 아는 이는 없어?”"], ["“Hush——!”", "“쉿——!”"]], [["The loud, clear voice of a man in authority silenced the low hum of talking all round me in an instant.", "권위 있는 어떤 사람의 크고 또렷한 목소리가 내 주위에서 낮게 웅웅거리던 말소리를 순식간에 잠재웠다."]], [["“Where is the gentleman who tried to save him?”", "“그를 구하려 했던 신사분은 어디 계십니까?”"], ["said the voice.", "그 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Here, sir—here he is!”", "“여기요, 나리—여기 계십니다!”"], ["Dozens of eager faces pressed about me—dozens of eager arms parted the crowd.", "열띤 얼굴 수십이 내 주위로 몰려들었고—열띤 팔 수십이 군중을 갈랐다."], ["The man in authority came up to me with a lantern in his hand.", "권위 있는 그 남자가 등불을 손에 들고 내게 다가왔다."]], [["“This way, sir, if you please,”", "“이쪽으로 오시지요, 선생.”"], ["he said quietly.", "그가 조용히 말했다."]], [["I was unable to speak to him, I was unable to resist him when he took my arm.", "나는 그에게 말을 할 수도 없었고, 그가 내 팔을 잡았을 때 거스를 수도 없었다."], ["I tried to say that I had never seen the dead man in his lifetime—that there was no hope of identifying him by means of a stranger like me.", "나는 죽은 그 사람을 살아생전에 본 적이 없다고—나 같은 낯선 사람을 통해 그의 신원을 밝힐 가망은 없다고 말하려 했다."], ["But the words failed on my lips.", "그러나 말이 입술에서 힘을 잃었다."], ["I was faint, and silent, and helpless.", "나는 아득하고, 말이 없었고, 무력했다."]], [["“Do you know him, sir?”", "“이 사람을 아십니까, 선생?”"]], [["I was standing inside a circle of men.", "나는 사람들이 둘러선 원 안에 서 있었다."], ["Three of them opposite to me were holding lanterns low down to the ground.", "내 맞은편에 선 세 사람이 등불을 땅바닥 가까이 낮게 들고 있었다."], ["Their eyes, and the eyes of all the rest, were fixed silently and expectantly on my face.", "그들의 눈은, 그리고 나머지 모두의 눈은, 말없이 무언가를 기다리며 내 얼굴에 고정되어 있었다."], ["I knew what was at my feet—I knew why they were holding the lanterns so low to the ground.", "나는 내 발치에 무엇이 있는지 알았다—그들이 왜 등불을 그토록 땅바닥 가까이 들고 있는지 알았다."]], [["“Can you identify him, sir?”", "“신원을 확인해 주시겠습니까, 선생?”"]], [["My eyes dropped slowly.", "내 눈이 천천히 내려갔다."], ["At first I saw nothing under them but a coarse canvas cloth.", "처음에는 그 아래로 거친 범포 천 한 장밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["The dripping of the rain on it was audible in the dreadful silence.", "그 위로 빗방울이 떨어지는 소리가 그 무서운 정적 속에서 또렷이 들렸다."], ["I looked up, along the cloth, and there at the end, stark and grim and black, in the yellow light—there was his dead face.", "나는 천을 따라 위쪽으로 눈길을 옮겼고, 그 끝에서, 누런 불빛 속에 뻣뻣하고 험상궂고 시커멓게—거기 그의 죽은 얼굴이 있었다."]], [["So, for the first and last time, I saw him.", "그리하여 처음이자 마지막으로 나는 그를 보았다."], ["So the Visitation of God ruled it that he and I should meet.", "그리하여 하느님의 징벌은 그와 내가 그렇게 만나도록 정하셨다."]], [["# XI", "# XI"]], [["The inquest was hurried for certain local reasons which weighed with the coroner and the town authorities.", "검시는 검시관과 읍 당국자들에게 무겁게 작용한 몇 가지 지역 사정 때문에 서둘러 진행되었다."], ["It was held on the afternoon of the next day.", "그것은 이튿날 오후에 열렸다."], ["I was necessarily one among the witnesses summoned to assist the objects of the investigation.", "나는 당연하게도 그 조사의 목적을 돕기 위해 소환된 증인들 가운데 한 사람이었다."]], [["My first proceeding in the morning was to go to the post-office, and inquire for the letter which I expected from Marian.", "그날 아침 내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 우체국에 가서 매리언에게서 오기로 되어 있는 편지를 묻는 것이었다."], ["No change of circumstances, however extraordinary, could affect the one great anxiety which weighed on my mind while I was away from London.", "아무리 이례적인 상황 변화가 있다 한들, 런던을 떠나 있는 동안 내 마음을 짓누르던 그 하나의 큰 불안만은 어쩌지 못했다."], ["The morning’s letter, which was the only assurance I could receive that no misfortune had happened in my absence, was still the absorbing interest with which my day began.", "내가 자리를 비운 사이에 아무 불행도 일어나지 않았음을 확인시켜 줄 유일한 증거인 아침 편지는, 여전히 내 하루를 열어 주는 온 마음을 사로잡는 관심사였다."]], [["To my relief, the letter from Marian was at the office waiting for me.", "다행히도 매리언의 편지는 우체국에서 나를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Nothing had happened—they were both as safe and as well as when I had left them.", "아무 일도 없었다—두 사람 다 내가 떠나올 때와 똑같이 무사하고 잘 지내고 있었다."], ["Laura sent her love, and begged that I would let her know of my return a day beforehand.", "로라는 사랑을 전하며, 내가 돌아오는 날을 하루 앞서 미리 알려 달라고 청했다."], ["Her sister added, in explanation of this message, that she had saved “nearly a sovereign”", "그 언니는 이 부탁에 설명을 덧붙여, 자기 개인 지갑에서 “1소버린 가까이”"], ["out of her own private purse, and that she had claimed the privilege of ordering the dinner and giving the dinner which was to celebrate the day of my return.", "모아 두었으며, 내가 돌아오는 날을 축하할 만찬을 주문하고 차려 낼 특권을 자기가 갖겠노라 주장했다고 적었다."], ["I read these little domestic confidences in the bright morning with the terrible recollection of what had happened the evening before vivid in my memory.", "나는 그 자잘한 집안 이야기들을, 바로 전날 저녁에 벌어진 일의 끔찍한 기억이 아직 생생한 채로, 밝은 아침 햇빛 속에서 읽었다."], ["The necessity of sparing Laura any sudden knowledge of the truth was the first consideration which the letter suggested to me.", "로라가 그 진상을 갑작스레 알게 되는 일만은 막아야 한다는 것이 그 편지가 내게 일깨운 첫 번째 고려 사항이었다."], ["I wrote at once to Marian to tell her what I have told in these pages—presenting the tidings as gradually and gently as I could, and warning her not to let any such thing as a newspaper fall in Laura’s way while I was absent.", "나는 곧바로 매리언에게 편지를 써서 이 지면에 적어 온 것을 그대로 알렸다—되도록 서서히, 되도록 부드럽게 소식을 전하고, 내가 없는 동안 신문 따위가 로라의 눈에 띄지 않게 하라고 당부했다."], ["In the case of any other woman, less courageous and less reliable, I might have hesitated before I ventured on unreservedly disclosing the whole truth.", "용기와 미더움이 덜한 다른 여자였다면, 나는 진실을 남김없이 털어놓기 전에 망설였을지도 모른다."], ["But I owed it to Marian to be faithful to my past experience of her, and to trust her as I trusted myself.", "그러나 나는 매리언에 대해 지금껏 겪어 온 바에 충실해야 했고, 나 자신을 믿듯 그녀를 믿어야 했다."]], [["My letter was necessarily a long one.", "내 편지는 어쩔 수 없이 긴 것이 되었다."], ["It occupied me until the time came for proceeding to the inquest.", "검시가 열리는 곳으로 갈 시간이 될 때까지 나는 줄곧 그 편지에 매달려 있었다."]], [["The objects of the legal inquiry were necessarily beset by peculiar complications and difficulties.", "법적 심리의 목적들은 필연적으로 남다른 복잡함과 어려움에 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["Besides the investigation into the manner in which the deceased had met his death, there were serious questions to be settled relating to the cause of the fire, to the abstraction of the keys, and to the presence of a stranger in the vestry at the time when the flames broke out.", "고인이 어떻게 죽음에 이르렀는가를 조사하는 일 말고도, 화재의 원인과 열쇠의 절취, 그리고 불길이 치솟을 무렵 성구실에 낯선 자가 있었다는 사실에 관하여 밝혀야 할 중대한 문제들이 있었다."], ["Even the identification of the dead man had not yet been accomplished.", "죽은 자의 신원 확인조차 아직 끝나지 않은 터였다."], ["The helpless condition of the servant had made the police distrustful of his asserted recognition of his master.", "하인의 정신이 온전치 못한 상태였기에 경찰은 그가 주인을 알아보았다고 주장한 것을 미덥지 않게 여겼다."], ["They had sent to Knowlesbury overnight to secure the attendance of witnesses who were well acquainted with the personal appearance of Sir Percival Glyde, and they had communicated, the first thing in the morning, with Blackwater Park.", "그들은 간밤에 놀즈베리로 사람을 보내어 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 용모를 잘 아는 증인들의 출석을 확보해 두었고, 아침이 되자마자 블랙워터 파크에 연락을 취했다."], ["These precautions enabled the coroner and jury to settle the question of identity, and to confirm the correctness of the servant’s assertion; the evidence offered by competent witnesses, and by the discovery of certain facts, being subsequently strengthened by an examination of the dead man’s watch.", "이런 조처 덕분에 검시관과 배심은 신원 문제를 매듭짓고 하인의 진술이 옳았음을 확인할 수 있었다. 유능한 증인들이 내놓은 증언과 몇 가지 사실의 발견은, 뒤이어 죽은 자의 시계를 살펴봄으로써 한층 더 뒷받침되었다."], ["The crest and the name of Sir Percival Glyde were engraved inside it.", "시계 안쪽에는 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 문장과 이름이 새겨져 있었다."]], [["The next inquiries related to the fire.", "다음 심문은 화재에 관한 것이었다."]], [["The servant and I, and the boy who had heard the light struck in the vestry, were the first witnesses called.", "하인과 나, 그리고 성구실에서 불 켜는 소리를 들었다는 소년이 맨 먼저 불려 나온 증인이었다."], ["The boy gave his evidence clearly enough, but the servant’s mind had not yet recovered the shock inflicted on it—he was plainly incapable of assisting the objects of the inquiry, and he was desired to stand down.", "소년은 제법 또렷하게 증언했으나, 하인의 정신은 그가 받은 충격에서 아직 회복되지 못한 상태였다—그가 조사의 목적에 도움이 될 수 없다는 것은 명백했고, 그래서 그는 내려가라는 말을 들었다."]], [["To my own relief, my examination was not a long one.", "다행히도 나에 대한 신문은 길지 않았다."], ["I had not known the deceased—I had never seen him—I was not aware of his presence at Old Welmingham—and I had not been in the vestry at the finding of the body.", "나는 고인을 알지 못했고—그를 본 적이 한 번도 없었으며—그가 올드 웰밍엄에 와 있다는 것도 알지 못했고—시신이 발견되었을 때 성구실 안에 있지도 않았다."], ["All I could prove was that I had stopped at the clerk’s cottage to ask my way—that I had heard from him of the loss of the keys—that I had accompanied him to the church to render what help I could—that I had seen the fire—that I had heard some person unknown, inside the vestry, trying vainly to unlock the door—and that I had done what I could, from motives of humanity, to save the man.", "내가 입증할 수 있는 것이라고는, 길을 묻느라 서기의 오두막에 들렀다는 것—그에게서 열쇠를 잃어버렸다는 말을 들었다는 것—힘닿는 데까지 돕고자 그와 함께 교회로 갔다는 것—불을 보았다는 것—성구실 안에서 누군지 모를 사람이 헛되이 문을 열려고 애쓰는 소리를 들었다는 것—그리고 인간된 도리로 그 사람을 구하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Other witnesses, who had been acquainted with the deceased, were asked if they could explain the mystery of his presumed abstraction of the keys, and his presence in the burning room.", "고인을 알고 지내던 다른 증인들은, 그가 열쇠를 몰래 가져갔으리라는 점과 불타는 방 안에 있었던 까닭의 수수께끼를 설명할 수 있겠느냐는 물음을 받았다."], ["But the coroner seemed to take it for granted, naturally enough, that I, as a total stranger in the neighbourhood, and a total stranger to Sir Percival Glyde, could not be in a position to offer any evidence on these two points.", "그러나 검시관은, 그럴 만도 하게, 이 고장에 전혀 낯선 사람이자 퍼시벌 글라이드 경에게도 전혀 낯선 사람인 내가 그 두 가지 점에 관하여 무슨 증언을 내놓을 처지가 못 된다고 당연하게 여기는 듯했다."]], [["The course that I was myself bound to take, when my formal examination had closed, seemed clear to me.", "정식 신문이 끝났을 때 나 자신이 취해야 할 길은 분명해 보였다. 나는 내 사사로운 확신을 자진하여 진술할 까닭이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["I did not feel called on to volunteer any statement of my own private convictions; in the first place, because my doing so could serve no practical purpose, now that all proof in support of any surmises of mine was burnt with the burnt register; in the second place, because I could not have intelligibly stated my opinion—my unsupported opinion—without disclosing the whole story of the conspiracy, and producing beyond a doubt the same unsatisfactory effect an the minds of the coroner and the jury, which I had already produced on the mind of Mr. Kyrle.", "첫째로는, 내 추측을 뒷받침할 모든 증거가 불타 버린 등기부와 함께 재가 된 마당에 그렇게 해 봐야 아무 실질적 소용이 없기 때문이었고, 둘째로는, 음모의 전말을 다 드러내지 않고서는 내 의견을—아무 뒷받침도 없는 내 의견을—알아듣게 진술할 수 없었을 뿐 아니라, 그렇게 하면 이미 카일 씨의 마음에 남긴 것과 똑같이 미덥지 못한 인상을 검시관과 배심의 마음에도 틀림없이 남기게 될 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["In these pages, however, and after the time that has now elapsed, no such cautions and restraints as are here described need fetter the free expression of my opinion.", "그러나 이 지면에서는, 그리고 그때로부터 이만한 세월이 흐른 지금에는, 여기 적어 온 그런 조심이나 삼감이 내 의견을 자유로이 밝히는 것을 얽맬 까닭이 없다."], ["I will state briefly, before my pen occupies itself with other events, how my own convictions lead me to account for the abstraction of the keys, for the outbreak of the fire, and for the death of the man.", "내 펜이 다른 사건들을 다루기 전에, 열쇠의 절취와 화재의 발생과 그 사람의 죽음을 내 확신이 어떻게 설명하는지를 간략히 적어 두겠다."]], [["The news of my being free on bail drove Sir Percival, as I believe, to his last resources.", "내가 보석으로 풀려났다는 소식은, 내가 믿기로, 퍼시벌 경을 마지막 수단으로 몰아갔다."], ["The attempted attack on the road was one of those resources, and the suppression of all practical proof of his crime, by destroying the page of the register on which the forgery had been committed, was the other, and the surest of the two.", "길에서의 습격 시도가 그 수단 가운데 하나였고, 위조가 저질러진 등기부의 그 쪽을 없애 버림으로써 자기 범죄의 실질적 증거를 모조리 지워 버리는 것이 다른 하나이자 둘 중 더 확실한 쪽이었다."], ["If I could produce no extract from the original book to compare with the certified copy at Knowlesbury, I could produce no positive evidence, and could threaten him with no fatal exposure.", "놀즈베리에 있는 인증 사본과 대조할 원본의 발췌를 내가 내놓지 못한다면, 나는 확실한 증거를 내놓을 수 없고 치명적인 폭로로 그를 위협할 수도 없었다."], ["All that was necessary to the attainment of his end was, that he should get into the vestry unperceived, that he should tear out the page in the register, and that he should leave the vestry again as privately as he had entered it.", "그가 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 것이라고는, 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 성구실에 들어가서, 등기부의 그 쪽을 뜯어내고, 들어갈 때처럼 남몰래 성구실을 빠져나오는 것뿐이었다."]], [["On this supposition, it is easy to understand why he waited until nightfall before he made the attempt, and why he took advantage of the clerk’s absence to possess himself of the keys.", "이렇게 가정하면, 그가 왜 밤이 되기를 기다렸다가 일을 벌였는지, 왜 서기가 자리를 비운 틈을 타서 열쇠를 손에 넣었는지 쉽게 이해가 된다."], ["Necessity would oblige him to strike a light to find his way to the right register, and common caution would suggest his locking the door on the inside in case of intrusion on the part of any inquisitive stranger, or on my part, if I happened to be in the neighbourhood at the time.", "필요한 등기부를 찾으려면 불을 켤 수밖에 없었을 것이고, 웬만한 조심성이라면 캐묻기 좋아하는 낯선 자가 끼어들 경우에 대비하여, 혹은 마침 내가 그 근처에 있을 경우에 대비하여 안에서 문을 잠그라고 일러 주었을 것이다."]], [["I cannot believe that it was any part of his intention to make the destruction of the register appear to be the result of accident, by purposely setting the vestry on fire.", "나는 그가 일부러 성구실에 불을 질러 등기부의 소실을 사고인 것처럼 꾸미려 했다고는 믿을 수 없다."], ["The bare chance that prompt assistance might arrive, and that the books might, by the remotest possibility, be saved, would have been enough, on a moment’s consideration, to dismiss any idea of this sort from his mind.", "도움의 손길이 재빨리 닿을지도 모른다는 실낱같은 가능성, 그래서 아무리 희박하나마 장부들이 구조될지도 모른다는 가능성만으로도, 잠깐만 생각해 보면 그런 종류의 발상은 그의 머리에서 지워 버리기에 충분했을 것이다."], ["Remembering the quantity of combustible objects in the vestry—the straw, the papers, the packing-cases, the dry wood, the old worm-eaten presses—all the probabilities, in my estimation, point to the fire as the result of an accident with his matches or his light.", "성구실 안에 있던 불붙기 쉬운 물건들의 양—짚이며 서류며 포장 상자며 마른 나무며 벌레 먹은 낡은 장들—을 떠올려 보면, 내 판단으로는 모든 개연성이 그 불을 그의 성냥이나 등불로 인한 사고의 결과로 가리키고 있다."]], [["His first impulse, under these circumstances, was doubtless to try to extinguish the flames, and failing in that, his second impulse (ignorant as he was of the state of the lock) had been to attempt to escape by the door which had given him entrance.", "이런 정황에서 그가 처음 보인 충동은 틀림없이 불길을 끄려는 것이었을 테고, 그것이 안 되자 두 번째 충동은 (자물쇠가 어떤 상태인지 몰랐으므로) 자기가 들어온 그 문으로 빠져나가려는 것이었으리라."], ["When I had called to him, the flames must have reached across the door leading into the church, on either side of which the presses extended, and close to which the other combustible objects were placed.", "내가 그를 불렀을 때, 불길은 이미 교회로 통하는 문 앞을 가로질러 번져 있었을 것이다. 그 문 양옆으로 장들이 늘어서 있었고, 바로 그 곁에 불붙기 쉬운 다른 물건들이 놓여 있었으니 말이다."], ["In all probability, the smoke and flame (confined as they were to the room) had been too much for him when he tried to escape by the inner door.", "십중팔구 그가 안쪽 문으로 빠져나가려 했을 때, (그 방 안에 갇혀 있던) 연기와 불길이 그에게 너무 벅찼을 것이다."], ["He must have dropped in his death-swoon—he must have sunk in the place where he was found—just as I got on the roof to break the skylight window.", "그는 죽음의 혼절 속에 쓰러졌을 것이다—시신이 발견된 그 자리에 주저앉았을 것이다—내가 채광창을 깨려고 지붕에 막 올라섰을 바로 그때에."], ["Even if we had been able, afterwards, to get into the church, and to burst open the door from that side, the delay must have been fatal.", "설령 우리가 그 뒤에 교회 안으로 들어가서 그쪽에서 문을 부수어 열 수 있었다 하더라도, 그 지체는 치명적이었을 것이다."], ["He would have been past saving, long past saving, by that time.", "그때쯤이면 그는 이미 구할 수 없는, 진작에 구할 수 없는 사람이 되어 있었으리라."], ["We should only have given the flames free ingress into the church—the church, which was now preserved, but which, in that event, would have shared the fate of the vestry.", "우리는 다만 불길이 교회 안으로 거침없이 들어오게 했을 뿐일 테고—지금은 무사히 남은 그 교회도 그랬더라면 성구실과 같은 운명을 겪었을 것이다."], ["There is no doubt in my mind, there can be no doubt in the mind of any one, that he was a dead man before ever we got to the empty cottage, and worked with might and main to tear down the beam.", "우리가 그 빈 오두막에 이르러 젖 먹던 힘까지 다해 들보를 뜯어내기 전에 그는 이미 죽은 사람이었다는 것을, 나는 조금도 의심하지 않으며 누구라도 의심할 수 없을 것이다."]], [["This is the nearest approach that any theory of mine can make towards accounting for a result which was visible matter of fact.", "이것이 눈에 보이는 사실로 드러난 결과를 두고 내가 세울 수 있는 어떤 추론이든 가장 가깝게 다가갈 수 있는 설명이다."], ["As I have described them, so events passed to us outside.", "내가 적어 온 그대로, 밖에 있던 우리에게 일들은 그렇게 지나갔다."], ["As I have related it, so his body was found.", "내가 이야기한 그대로, 그의 시신은 그렇게 발견되었다."]], [["The inquest was adjourned over one day—no explanation that the eye of the law could recognise having been discovered thus far to account for the mysterious circumstances of the case.", "검시는 하루 연기되었다—이 사건의 알 수 없는 정황들을 설명해 줄, 법의 눈이 인정할 만한 해명이 아직 아무것도 발견되지 않았기 때문이었다."]], [["It was arranged that more witnesses should be summoned, and that the London solicitor of the deceased should be invited to attend.", "증인을 더 소환하고, 고인의 런던 사무 변호사에게 출석을 요청하기로 정해졌다."], ["A medical man was also charged with the duty of reporting on the mental condition of the servant, which appeared at present to debar him from giving any evidence of the least importance.", "또한 의사 한 사람에게는 하인의 정신 상태를 보고할 임무가 맡겨졌는데, 그 상태로는 당장 조금이라도 중요한 증언을 하기 어려워 보였기 때문이다."], ["He could only declare, in a dazed way, that he had been ordered, on the night of the fire, to wait in the lane, and that he knew nothing else, except that the deceased was certainly his master.", "그는 다만 얼떨떨한 투로, 불이 난 밤에 좁은 길에서 기다리라는 명을 받았다는 것과, 고인이 자기 주인인 것만은 틀림없다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모른다고 되뇔 뿐이었다."]], [["My own impression was, that he had been first used (without any guilty knowledge on his own part) to ascertain the fact of the clerk’s absence from home on the previous day, and that he had been afterwards ordered to wait near the church (but out of sight of the vestry) to assist his master, in the event of my escaping the attack on the road, and of a collision occurring between Sir Percival and myself.", "내 나름의 짐작은 이러했다. 그는 먼저 (본인은 아무 죄의식 없이) 전날 서기가 집을 비운다는 사실을 알아내는 데 쓰였고, 그다음에는 내가 길에서의 습격을 벗어나 퍼시벌 경과 나 사이에 충돌이 벌어질 경우 주인을 돕도록 교회 가까이에 (다만 성구실에서는 보이지 않는 곳에) 기다리라는 명을 받았으리라는 것이다."], ["It is necessary to add, that the man’s own testimony was never obtained to confirm this view.", "덧붙여 두어야 할 것은, 이 견해를 확인해 줄 증언을 그 사내에게서는 끝내 얻지 못했다는 사실이다."], ["The medical report of him declared that what little mental faculty he possessed was seriously shaken; nothing satisfactory was extracted from him at the adjourned inquest, and for aught I know to the contrary, he may never have recovered to this day.", "그에 대한 의사의 소견서는 그가 지녔던 얼마 안 되는 정신 능력마저 심하게 흔들렸다고 밝혔다. 속개된 검시에서도 그에게서 이렇다 할 것은 아무것도 끌어내지 못했으며, 내가 아는 한 그는 오늘까지도 회복하지 못했을지 모른다."]], [["I returned to the hotel at Welmingham so jaded in body and mind, so weakened and depressed by all that I had gone through, as to be quite unfit to endure the local gossip about the inquest, and to answer the trivial questions that the talkers addressed to me in the coffee-room.", "나는 웰밍엄의 여관으로 돌아왔는데, 몸도 마음도 어찌나 지쳤는지, 겪어 낸 일들로 어찌나 쇠약해지고 가라앉았는지, 검시를 둘러싼 이 고장 사람들의 수군거림을 견디거나 식당에서 떠들어 대는 이들이 내게 던지는 하찮은 물음에 대답할 형편이 도무지 못 되었다."], ["I withdrew from my scanty dinner to my cheap garret-chamber to secure myself a little quiet, and to think undisturbed of Laura and Marian.", "나는 변변찮은 저녁 식사를 물리고 값싼 다락방으로 물러나, 잠깐이나마 고요를 얻고 방해받지 않고 로라와 매리언을 생각하기로 했다."]], [["If I had been a richer man I would have gone back to London, and would have comforted myself with a sight of the two dear faces again that night.", "내가 더 넉넉한 처지였다면 런던으로 돌아가서 그날 밤 다시 그 그리운 두 얼굴을 보는 것으로 스스로를 위로했을 것이다."], ["But I was bound to appear, if called on, at the adjourned inquest, and doubly bound to answer my bail before the magistrate at Knowlesbury.", "그러나 나는 부름을 받으면 속개된 검시에 출석해야 했고, 놀즈베리의 치안 판사 앞에 보석에 답해야 할 의무는 더더욱 무거웠다."], ["Our slender resources had suffered already, and the doubtful future—more doubtful than ever now—made me dread decreasing our means unnecessarily by allowing myself an indulgence even at the small cost of a double railway journey in the carriages of the second class.", "우리의 얄팍한 재원은 이미 축이 나 있었고, 어느 때보다도 더 불투명해진 앞날은, 이등칸 기차를 두 번 타는 적은 비용일망정 나 자신에게 그런 호사를 허락하여 살림을 공연히 줄이는 일을 두렵게 만들었다."]], [["The next day—the day immediately following the inquest—was left at my own disposal.", "이튿날은—검시 바로 다음 날은—내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 남겨졌다."], ["I began the morning by again applying at the post-office for my regular report from Marian.", "나는 아침에 다시 우체국에 들러 매리언에게서 오는 정기 보고를 찾는 것으로 하루를 시작했다."], ["It was waiting for me as before, and it was written throughout in good spirits.", "편지는 여느 때처럼 나를 기다리고 있었고, 처음부터 끝까지 밝은 기분으로 쓰여 있었다."], ["I read the letter thankfully, and then set forth with my mind at ease for the day to go to Old Welmingham, and to view the scene of the fire by the morning light.", "나는 고마운 마음으로 그것을 읽고, 하루 동안 마음을 놓은 채 올드 웰밍엄으로 가서 아침 햇빛 속에 화재의 현장을 보려고 길을 나섰다."]], [["What changes met me when I got there!", "그곳에 이르렀을 때 나를 맞은 것은 얼마나 달라진 광경이었던가!"]], [["Through all the ways of our unintelligible world the trivial and the terrible walk hand in hand together.", "우리가 알 수 없는 이 세상의 온갖 길목에서 사소한 것과 끔찍한 것은 손을 맞잡고 나란히 걷는다."], ["The irony of circumstances holds no mortal catastrophe in respect.", "정황의 아이러니는 죽음의 어떤 참사도 존중하지 않는다."], ["When I reached the church, the trampled condition of the burial-ground was the only serious trace left to tell of the fire and the death.", "내가 교회에 이르렀을 때, 불과 죽음을 말해 주는 진지한 흔적이라고는 짓밟힌 묘지의 꼴뿐이었다."], ["A rough hoarding of boards had been knocked up before the vestry doorway.", "성구실 문간 앞에는 널빤지로 얼기설기 가림막이 세워져 있었다."], ["Rude caricatures were scrawled on it already, and the village children were fighting and shouting for the possession of the best peep-hole to see through.", "그 위에는 벌써 서투른 낙서 그림들이 그려져 있었고, 마을 아이들은 안을 들여다보기 좋은 구멍을 차지하겠다고 다투며 소리를 질러 댔다."], ["On the spot where I had heard the cry for help from the burning room, on the spot where the panic-stricken servant had dropped on his knees, a fussy flock of poultry was now scrambling for the first choice of worms after the rain; and on the ground at my feet, where the door and its dreadful burden had been laid, a workman’s dinner was waiting for him, tied up in a yellow basin, and his faithful cur in charge was yelping at me for coming near the food.", "불타는 방에서 살려 달라는 외침이 들려오던 그 자리에서, 겁에 질린 하인이 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉던 그 자리에서, 이제는 닭들이 부산스레 몰려다니며 비 온 뒤에 나온 지렁이를 먼저 차지하려고 아우성이었다. 그리고 문짝과 그 무시무시한 짐이 놓여 있던 내 발치의 땅바닥에는, 노란 사발에 싸맨 인부의 점심이 주인을 기다리고 있었고, 그것을 지키는 충직한 잡종개가 음식에 가까이 왔다고 나를 향해 짖어 댔다."], ["The old clerk, looking idly at the slow commencement of the repairs, had only one interest that he could talk about now—the interest of escaping all blame for his own part on account of the accident that had happened.", "늙은 서기는 더디게 시작된 수리 공사를 하릴없이 바라보며, 이제 입에 올릴 수 있는 관심사라고는 오직 하나, 이번 사고에 대해 제 몫의 책임을 면하는 일뿐이었다."], ["One of the village women, whose white wild face I remembered the picture of terror when we pulled down the beam, was giggling with another woman, the picture of inanity, over an old washing-tub. There is nothing serious in mortality!", "우리가 들보를 뜯어낼 때 공포의 초상 같던 새하얗게 질린 얼굴이 아직 내 기억에 남아 있는 마을 여자 하나는, 얼빠짐의 초상 같은 다른 여자와 낡은 빨래통을 사이에 두고 킬킬거리고 있었다."], ["Solomon in all his glory was Solomon with the elements of the contemptible lurking in every fold of his robes and in every corner of his palace.", "죽어야 할 목숨에 진지한 것이란 아무것도 없구나! 온갖 영화를 누린 솔로몬도, 옷자락 주름마다 궁전 구석마다 하찮음의 씨앗을 숨긴 솔로몬이었다."]], [["As I left the place, my thoughts turned, not for the first time, to the complete overthrow that all present hope of establishing Laura’s identity had now suffered through Sir Percival’s death.", "그곳을 떠나면서 내 생각은, 이번이 처음은 아니었지만, 퍼시벌 경의 죽음으로 로라의 신원을 밝히려던 지금의 모든 희망이 온통 무너져 버렸다는 데로 향했다."], ["He was gone—and with him the chance was gone which had been the one object of all my labours and all my hopes.", "그는 갔다—그리고 그와 함께, 내 모든 수고와 모든 희망의 유일한 목표였던 그 기회도 사라졌다."]], [["Could I look at my failure from no truer point of view than this?", "나는 내 실패를 이보다 더 참된 눈으로 볼 수는 없었던가?"]], [["Suppose he had lived, would that change of circumstance have altered the result?", "그가 살아 있었다고 치자, 그 정황의 변화가 결과를 바꾸어 놓았겠는가?"], ["Could I have made my discovery a marketable commodity, even for Laura’s sake, after I had found out that robbery of the rights of others was the essence of Sir Percival’s crime?", "남의 권리를 빼앗는 것이 퍼시벌 경이 저지른 범죄의 본질임을 알아낸 뒤에, 아무리 로라를 위해서라 한들 내가 그 발견을 흥정거리로 삼을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Could I have offered the price of MY silence for HIS confession of the conspiracy, when the effect of that silence must have been to keep the right heir from the estates, and the right owner from the name?", "내 침묵의 대가로 그의 음모 자백을 얻어 낼 수 있었겠는가—그 침묵의 결과가 정당한 상속인에게서 영지를, 정당한 주인에게서 이름을 계속 빼앗아 두는 것일 수밖에 없는데? 될 수 없는 일이다!"], ["Impossible! If Sir Percival had lived, the discovery, from which (in my ignorance of the true nature of the Secret) I had hoped so much, could not have been mine to suppress or to make public, as I thought best, for the vindication of Laura’s rights.", "퍼시벌 경이 살아 있었다면, (그 비밀의 참된 성질을 몰랐기에) 내가 그토록 큰 기대를 걸었던 그 발견은, 로라의 권리를 되찾기 위해 내 좋을 대로 덮어 두거나 세상에 알릴 수 있는 내 것이 될 수 없었을 것이다."], ["In common honesty and common honour I must have gone at once to the stranger whose birthright had been usurped—I must have renounced the victory at the moment when it was mine by placing my discovery unreservedly in that stranger’s hands—and I must have faced afresh all the difficulties which stood between me and the one object of my life, exactly as I was resolved in my heart of hearts to face them now!", "여느 정직함과 여느 명예심으로 보아도 나는 당장 그 타고난 권리를 빼앗긴 낯선 이에게 갔어야 했고—내 발견을 남김없이 그 낯선 이의 손에 넘겨줌으로써 승리가 내 것이 된 바로 그 순간에 승리를 포기했어야 했으며—나와 내 삶의 유일한 목표 사이를 가로막은 온갖 어려움을, 지금 내가 마음속 깊이 맞서기로 다짐한 그대로, 다시금 마주해야 했을 것이다!"]], [["I returned to Welmingham with my mind composed, feeling more sure of myself and my resolution than I had felt yet.", "나는 마음이 가라앉은 채로, 지금껏 느껴 본 어느 때보다도 나 자신과 내 결의에 확신을 품고 웰밍엄으로 돌아왔다."]], [["On my way to the hotel I passed the end of the square in which Mrs. Catherick lived.", "여관으로 가는 길에 나는 캐서릭 부인이 사는 광장의 끄트머리를 지났다."], ["Should I go back to the house, and make another attempt to see her?", "그 집으로 돌아가 그녀를 만나려고 다시 한번 애써 볼까? 아니다."], ["No. That news of Sir Percival’s death, which was the last news she ever expected to hear, must have reached her hours since.", "그녀가 들으리라고는 끝끝내 생각지 못했을 퍼시벌 경의 죽음 소식은 벌써 몇 시간 전에 그녀에게 닿았을 것이다."], ["All the proceedings at the inquest had been reported in the local paper that morning—there was nothing I could tell her which she did not know already.", "검시에서 오간 모든 절차가 그날 아침 지역 신문에 실렸으니—그녀가 아직 모르는 것으로서 내가 일러 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["My interest in making her speak had slackened.", "그녀의 입을 열게 하는 데 대한 내 관심도 시들해져 있었다."], ["I remembered the furtive hatred in her face when she said, “There is no news of Sir Percival that I don’t expect—except the news of his death.”", "나는 그녀가 “퍼시벌 경에 관해 내가 예상하지 못할 소식은 없습니다. 다만 그가 죽었다는 소식만 빼고요.”"], ["I remembered the stealthy interest in her eyes when they settled on me at parting, after she had spoken those words.", "하고 말할 때 얼굴에 어리던 숨은 증오를 떠올렸다. 그 말을 하고 나서 헤어질 때 나를 향해 멎던 그 눈길의 은밀한 관심을 떠올렸다."], ["Some instinct, deep in my heart, which I felt to be a true one, made the prospect of again entering her presence repulsive to me—I turned away from the square, and went straight back to the hotel.", "내 마음 깊은 곳의 어떤 본능이—나는 그것이 참되다고 느꼈다—그녀 앞에 다시 나선다는 생각을 역겹게 만들었다. 나는 광장에서 발길을 돌려 곧장 여관으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Some hours later, while I was resting in the coffee-room, a letter was placed in my hands by the waiter.", "몇 시간 뒤, 식당에서 쉬고 있는데 급사가 편지 한 통을 내 손에 건네주었다."], ["It was addressed to me by name, and I found on inquiry that it had been left at the bar by a woman just as it was near dusk, and just before the gas was lighted.", "내 이름 앞으로 온 것이었고, 물어보니 땅거미가 질 무렵, 가스등을 켜기 바로 전에 어떤 여자가 계산대에 두고 갔다는 것이었다."], ["She had said nothing, and she had gone away again before there was time to speak to her, or even to notice who she was.", "그 여자는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 말을 붙이거나 누구인지 눈여겨볼 겨를도 없이 이미 가 버린 뒤였다."]], [["I opened the letter.", "나는 편지를 열었다."], ["It was neither dated nor signed, and the handwriting was palpably disguised.", "날짜도 서명도 없었고, 필체는 누가 보아도 일부러 꾸민 것이었다."], ["Before I had read the first sentence, however, I knew who my correspondent was—Mrs. Catherick.", "그러나 첫 문장을 다 읽기도 전에 나는 이 편지를 보낸 이가 누구인지 알았다—캐서릭 부인이었다."]], [["The letter ran as follows—I copy it exactly, word for word:—", "편지의 내용은 다음과 같다—나는 한 마디 한 마디 그대로 옮긴다:—"]], [["# THE STORY CONTINUED BY MRS. CATHERICK", "# THE STORY CONTINUED BY MRS. CATHERICK"]], [["SIR,—You have not come back, as you said you would.", "선생께,—말씀하신 대로 돌아오지 않으셨더군요."], ["No matter—I know the news, and I write to tell you so.", "상관없습니다—저는 그 소식을 알고 있고, 그것을 안다고 알려 드리려 이렇게 씁니다."], ["Did you see anything particular in my face when you left me?", "저를 떠나실 때 제 얼굴에서 무언가 남다른 것을 보셨는지요?"], ["I was wondering, in my own mind, whether the day of his downfall had come at last, and whether you were the chosen instrument for working it.", "저는 속으로 궁금해하고 있었답니다. 그자가 무너지는 날이 마침내 온 것인지, 그리고 그 일을 이루도록 뽑힌 도구가 바로 당신인지를요."], ["You were, and you HAVE worked it.", "과연 그러했고, 당신은 기어이 그 일을 이루어 내셨습니다."]], [["You were weak enough, as I have heard, to try and save his life.", "듣자 하니 당신은 그자의 목숨을 구해 보려 할 만큼 무르셨더군요."], ["If you had succeeded, I should have looked upon you as my enemy.", "만약 성공하셨더라면 저는 당신을 제 원수로 여겼을 것입니다."], ["Now you have failed, I hold you as my friend.", "이제 실패하셨으니 저는 당신을 제 벗으로 삼겠습니다."], ["Your inquiries frightened him into the vestry by night—your inquiries, without your privity and against your will, have served the hatred and wreaked the vengeance of three-and-twenty years.", "당신의 캐물음이 그자를 겁주어 밤중에 성구실로 몰아넣었고—당신의 캐물음이, 당신도 모르는 사이에 당신 뜻과는 어긋나게, 스물세 해에 걸친 증오를 섬기고 그 복수를 이루어 주었습니다. 고맙습니다, 선생."], ["Thank you, sir, in spite of yourself.", "당신 뜻과는 상관없이 말이지요."]], [["I owe something to the man who has done this.", "저는 이 일을 해 주신 분께 빚을 졌습니다."], ["How can I pay my debt?", "그 빚을 어떻게 갚을까요?"], ["If I was a young woman still I might say, “Come, put your arm round my waist, and kiss me, if you like.”", "제가 아직 젊은 여자였다면 이렇게 말했을지도 모르지요. “자, 제 허리에 팔을 두르고, 좋으시다면 입을 맞추세요.”"], ["I should have been fond enough of you even to go that length, and you would have accepted my invitation—you would, sir, twenty years ago!", "저는 그만큼은 당신이 마음에 들었을 테고, 당신도 그 청을 받아들이셨을 것입니다—받아들이고말고요, 선생, 스무 해 전이었다면요! 그러나 이제 저는 늙은 여자입니다."], ["But I am an old woman now.", "그러니 이렇게 하지요!"], ["Well! I can satisfy your curiosity, and pay my debt in that way.", "저는 당신의 호기심을 채워 드리는 것으로 빚을 갚을 수 있습니다."], ["You HAD a great curiosity to know certain private affairs of mine when you came to see me—private affairs which all your sharpness could not look into without my help—private affairs which you have not discovered, even now.", "저를 만나러 오셨을 때 당신은 제 사사로운 일들을 알고 싶어 몹시도 궁금해하셨지요—당신의 그 온갖 명민함으로도 제 도움 없이는 들여다볼 수 없었던 사사로운 일들, 지금 이 순간까지도 당신이 알아내지 못한 사사로운 일들 말입니다."], ["You SHALL discover them—your curiosity shall be satisfied.", "이제 그것들을 알게 되실 겁니다—당신의 호기심은 채워질 것입니다."], ["I will take any trouble to please you, my estimable young friend!", "저는 당신을 기쁘게 해 드리기 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않겠습니다, 나의 훌륭하신 젊은 벗이여!"]], [["You were a little boy, I suppose, in the year twenty-seven?", "1827년이면 당신은 아직 어린 사내아이였겠지요?"], ["I was a handsome young woman at that time, living at Old Welmingham.", "저는 그때 올드 웰밍엄에 살던, 얼굴이 반반한 젊은 여자였습니다."], ["I had a contemptible fool for a husband.", "남편이라고 있는 것은 하찮은 바보였고요."], ["I had also the honour of being acquainted (never mind how) with a certain gentleman (never mind whom).", "또한 저는 어떤 신사분과 (어떻게 그리되었는지는 아무래도 좋습니다) 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸습니다 (누구였는지도 아무래도 좋습니다)."], ["I shall not call him by his name.", "그자의 이름을 부르지는 않겠습니다. 그럴 까닭이 어디 있나요?"], ["Why should I? It was not his own.", "그것은 그자의 이름이 아니었으니까요."], ["He never had a name: you know that, by this time, as well as I do.", "그자에게는 애초에 이름이란 것이 없었지요. 이제쯤은 당신도 저만큼이나 그 사실을 알고 계실 테고요."]], [["It will be more to the purpose to tell you how he worked himself into my good graces.", "그자가 어떻게 제 환심을 샀는지 말씀드리는 편이 더 요긴하겠지요."], ["I was born with the tastes of a lady, and he gratified them—in other words, he admired me, and he made me presents.", "저는 귀부인의 취향을 타고났고, 그자는 그것을 채워 주었습니다—달리 말하면 저를 찬탄했고, 제게 선물을 했지요."], ["No woman can resist admiration and presents—especially presents, provided they happen to be just the thing she wants.", "찬탄과 선물을 물리칠 수 있는 여자는 없습니다—더구나 그 선물이 마침 갖고 싶던 바로 그것일 때는요."], ["He was sharp enough to know that—most men are.", "그자는 그쯤은 알 만큼 약았습니다—사내들이란 대개 그렇지요."], ["Naturally he wanted something in return—all men do.", "당연히 그자는 답례로 무언가를 바랐습니다—사내들이란 다 그러니까요."], ["And what do you think was the something?", "그런데 그 무언가가 무엇이었을 것 같습니까?"], ["The merest trifle.", "그야말로 하찮은 것이었습니다."], ["Nothing but the key of the vestry, and the key of the press inside it, when my husband’s back was turned.", "남편이 등을 돌린 사이에 성구실 열쇠와 그 안에 있는 장의 열쇠를 얻어다 주는 것, 그뿐이었지요."], ["Of course he lied when I asked him why he wished me to get him the keys in that private way.", "왜 그런 은밀한 방식으로 열쇠를 구해 달라고 하느냐고 물었을 때 그자는 물론 거짓말을 했습니다."], ["He might have saved himself the trouble—I didn’t believe him.", "그런 수고는 접어 두어도 좋았을 텐데요—저는 그 말을 믿지 않았으니까요."], ["But I liked my presents, and I wanted more.", "그러나 저는 선물이 마음에 들었고, 더 갖고 싶었습니다."], ["So I got him the keys, without my husband’s knowledge, and I watched him, without his own knowledge.", "그래서 남편 몰래 열쇠를 얻어다 주었고, 그자 몰래 그자를 지켜보았습니다."], ["Once, twice, four times I watched him, and the fourth time I found him out.", "한 번, 두 번, 네 번을 지켜보았고, 네 번째에 그자의 속셈을 알아냈습니다."]], [["I was never over-scrupulous where other people’s affairs were concerned, and I was not over-scrupulous about his adding one to the marriages in the register on his own account.", "저는 남의 일에 관해서라면 그리 까다롭게 군 적이 없었고, 그자가 제 잇속을 위해 등기부의 혼인 기록에 하나를 보태는 일에 대해서도 까다롭게 굴지 않았습니다."]], [["Of course I knew it was wrong, but it did no harm to me, which was one good reason for not making a fuss about it.", "물론 그것이 나쁜 짓인 줄은 알았습니다만, 제게 해가 될 것은 없었고, 그것이 소란을 피우지 않을 한 가지 그럴듯한 이유였습니다."], ["And I had not got a gold watch and chain, which was another, still better—and he had promised me one from London only the day before, which was a third, best of all.", "게다가 저는 금시계와 금줄이 없었는데, 그것이 두 번째 이유로 앞의 것보다 나았고—그자가 바로 전날 런던에서 하나 구해다 주겠노라 약속했으니, 그것이 세 번째 이유로 무엇보다도 나았지요."], ["If I had known what the law considered the crime to be, and how the law punished it, I should have taken proper care of myself, and have exposed him then and there.", "법이 그 범죄를 어떻게 보고 어떻게 벌하는지 제가 알았더라면, 저는 제 몸을 제대로 사려서 그 자리에서 그자를 들추어냈을 것입니다."], ["But I knew nothing, and I longed for the gold watch.", "그러나 저는 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 금시계가 몹시도 갖고 싶었습니다."], ["All the conditions I insisted on were that he should take me into his confidence and tell me everything.", "제가 고집한 조건이라고는 그자가 저를 믿고 모든 것을 털어놓아야 한다는 것뿐이었습니다."], ["I was as curious about his affairs then as you are about mine now.", "그때 저는 그자의 일을 궁금해했는데, 지금 당신이 제 일을 궁금해하는 것과 꼭 같았지요."], ["He granted my conditions—why, you will see presently.", "그자는 제 조건을 들어주었습니다—왜 그랬는지는 곧 아시게 될 겁니다."]], [["This, put in short, is what I heard from him.", "그자에게서 들은 이야기를 간추리면 이렇습니다."], ["He did not willingly tell me all that I tell you here.", "여기 적는 것을 그자가 다 순순히 말해 준 것은 아닙니다."], ["I drew some of it from him by persuasion and some of it by questions.", "얼마쯤은 구슬려서, 얼마쯤은 캐물어서 끌어냈지요."], ["I was determined to have all the truth, and I believe I got it.", "저는 진실을 남김없이 알아내겠다고 작정했고, 그리했다고 믿습니다."]], [["He knew no more than any one else of what the state of things really was between his father and mother till after his mother’s death.", "그자는 자기 아버지와 어머니 사이가 실제로 어떤 형편이었는지를 어머니가 죽은 뒤에야 비로소 알았고, 그전까지는 남들이나 다를 바 없이 몰랐습니다."], ["Then his father confessed it, and promised to do what he could for his son.", "그때 아버지가 그것을 털어놓고 아들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠노라 약속했습니다."], ["He died having done nothing—not having even made a will.", "그러나 그는 아무것도 하지 못한 채—유언장조차 남기지 않은 채 죽었습니다."], ["The son (who can blame him?) wisely provided for himself.", "아들은 (누가 그를 나무라겠습니까?) 슬기롭게 제 앞가림을 했습니다."], ["He came to England at once, and took possession of the property.", "곧바로 잉글랜드로 건너와 재산을 차지했지요."], ["There was no one to suspect him, and no one to say him nay.", "그를 의심할 사람도 없었고, 안 된다고 할 사람도 없었습니다."], ["His father and mother had always lived as man and wife—none of the few people who were acquainted with them ever supposed them to be anything else.", "그의 아버지와 어머니는 늘 부부로 살아왔으니까요—그들을 알던 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데 누구도 그 밖의 무엇이라고는 생각해 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["The right person to claim the property (if the truth had been known) was a distant relation, who had no idea of ever getting it, and who was away at sea when his father died.", "(진실이 알려졌더라면) 그 재산을 요구할 정당한 사람은 먼 친척이었는데, 그는 그것을 얻으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했고 아버지가 죽었을 때 바다에 나가 있었습니다."], ["He had no difficulty so far—he took possession, as a matter of course.", "여기까지는 그자에게 아무 어려움이 없었습니다—그는 당연한 일인 양 재산을 차지했지요."], ["But he could not borrow money on the property as a matter of course.", "그러나 그 재산을 담보로 돈을 빌리는 일만은 당연한 일인 양 되지 않았습니다."], ["There were two things wanted of him before he could do this.", "그러려면 두 가지가 필요했습니다."], ["One was a certificate of his birth, and the other was a certificate of his parents’ marriage.", "하나는 자기 출생 증명서였고, 다른 하나는 부모의 혼인 증명서였지요."], ["The certificate of his birth was easily got—he was born abroad, and the certificate was there in due form.", "출생 증명서는 쉽게 구했습니다—그는 외국에서 태어났고, 증명서가 격식대로 갖추어져 있었으니까요."], ["The other matter was a difficulty, and that difficulty brought him to Old Welmingham.", "다른 하나가 문제였고, 그 문제가 그자를 올드 웰밍엄으로 데려왔습니다."]], [["But for one consideration he might have gone to Knowlesbury instead.", "한 가지 사정만 아니었다면 그자는 그 대신 놀즈베리로 갔을지도 모릅니다."]], [["His mother had been living there just before she met with his father—living under her maiden name, the truth being that she was really a married woman, married in Ireland, where her husband had ill-used her, and had afterwards gone off with some other person.", "그자의 어머니는 아버지를 만나기 바로 전까지 거기서 살았습니다—처녀 적 이름을 쓰고 살았지요. 실은 이미 혼인한 몸이었는데, 아일랜드에서 결혼했고 남편이 그 여자를 학대하다가 나중에 다른 사람과 달아나 버렸다는 것이 진상이었습니다."], ["I give you this fact on good authority—Sir Felix mentioned it to his son as the reason why he had not married.", "이 사실은 확실한 데서 나온 것이니 믿으셔도 좋습니다—펠릭스 경이 자기가 왜 혼인하지 않았는지 그 까닭으로 아들에게 이야기했으니까요."], ["You may wonder why the son, knowing that his parents had met each other at Knowlesbury, did not play his first tricks with the register of that church, where it might have been fairly presumed his father and mother were married.", "부모가 놀즈베리에서 만난 것을 알면서도 아들이 왜 그 교회의 등기부부터 손대지 않았는지 의아하실 겁니다. 아버지와 어머니가 거기서 결혼했다고 보는 것이 그럴듯했을 텐데 말이지요."], ["The reason was that the clergyman who did duty at Knowlesbury church, in the year eighteen hundred and three (when, according to his birth certificate, his father and mother OUGHT to have been married), was alive still when he took possession of the property in the New Year of eighteen hundred and twenty-seven.", "그 까닭은, (출생 증명서에 따르면 아버지와 어머니가 마땅히 혼인했어야 할 해인) 1803년에 놀즈베리 교회에서 직무를 맡았던 목사가, 그자가 1827년 새해에 재산을 차지할 때까지도 아직 살아 있었기 때문입니다."], ["This awkward circumstance forced him to extend his inquiries to our neighbourhood.", "이 껄끄러운 사정 때문에 그자는 조사를 우리 동네로까지 넓힐 수밖에 없었습니다."], ["There no such danger existed, the former clergyman at our church having been dead for some years.", "여기에는 그런 위험이 없었지요. 우리 교회의 전임 목사는 몇 해 전에 이미 죽고 없었으니까요."]], [["Old Welmingham suited his purpose as well as Knowlesbury.", "올드 웰밍엄은 놀즈베리 못지않게 그자의 목적에 들어맞았습니다."], ["His father had removed his mother from Knowlesbury, and had lived with her at a cottage on the river, a little distance from our village.", "그 아버지는 어머니를 놀즈베리에서 데려와, 우리 마을에서 조금 떨어진 강가의 오두막에서 함께 살았습니다."], ["People who had known his solitary ways when he was single did not wonder at his solitary ways when he was supposed to be married.", "그가 홀몸일 적의 은둔벽을 알던 사람들은 혼인한 것으로 여겨진 뒤의 은둔벽도 이상하게 여기지 않았지요."], ["If he had not been a hideous creature to look at, his retired life with the lady might have raised suspicions; but, as things were, his hiding his ugliness and his deformity in the strictest privacy surprised nobody.", "그가 보기에 흉측한 몰골이 아니었다면 그 부인과의 은거 생활이 의심을 샀을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 사정이 그러했으니, 그가 자기 추한 얼굴과 뒤틀린 몸을 더없이 철저한 은둔 속에 감추는 것을 아무도 이상히 여기지 않았습니다."], ["He lived in our neighbourhood till he came in possession of the Park.", "그는 파크를 차지하게 될 때까지 우리 동네에서 살았습니다."], ["After three or four and twenty years had passed, who was to say (the clergyman being dead) that his marriage had not been as private as the rest of his life, and that it had not taken place at Old Welmingham church?", "스물서너 해가 지난 뒤에, (목사도 죽고 없는 마당에) 그의 혼인 역시 그의 다른 삶처럼 남몰래 치러졌으며 그것이 올드 웰밍엄 교회에서 있었던 일이 아니라고 누가 말할 수 있었겠습니까?"]], [["So, as I told you, the son found our neighbourhood the surest place he could choose to set things right secretly in his own interests.", "그러니 말씀드린 대로, 아들은 제 잇속을 위해 몰래 일을 바로잡기에 우리 동네가 고를 수 있는 가장 확실한 곳임을 알았습니다."], ["It may surprise you to hear that what he really did to the marriage register was done on the spur of the moment—done on second thoughts.", "그자가 혼인 등기부에 실제로 저지른 짓이 그 자리에서 즉흥으로—고쳐 생각한 끝에 나온 것이었다는 말을 들으면 놀라실지도 모르겠습니다."]], [["His first notion was only to tear the leaf out (in the right year and month), to destroy it privately, to go back to London, and to tell the lawyers to get him the necessary certificate of his father’s marriage, innocently referring them of course to the date on the leaf that was gone.", "그자의 애초 생각은 그저 (해당하는 해와 달의) 그 장을 뜯어내어 남몰래 없애 버리고, 런던으로 돌아가 변호사들에게 아버지의 혼인 증명서를 떼어 오라고 시키면서, 물론 시치미를 뚝 떼고 사라진 그 장의 날짜를 일러 주는 것이었습니다."], ["Nobody could say his father and mother had NOT been married after that, and whether, under the circumstances, they would stretch a point or not about lending him the money (he thought they would), he had his answer ready at all events, if a question was ever raised about his right to the name and the estate.", "그다음에는 그의 아버지와 어머니가 혼인하지 않았다고 아무도 말할 수 없을 테고, 그런 사정에서 변호사들이 돈을 빌려주는 일에 규정을 조금 눅여 줄지 어떨지는 몰라도 (그는 그러리라 여겼습니다) 어쨌든 자기 이름과 영지에 대한 권리에 의문이 제기되는 날에는 대답을 마련해 둔 셈이었지요."]], [["But when he came to look privately at the register for himself, he found at the bottom of one of the pages for the year eighteen hundred and three a blank space left, seemingly through there being no room to make a long entry there, which was made instead at the top of the next page.", "그런데 등기부를 몰래 제 눈으로 들여다보다가, 그자는 1803년 부분의 어느 쪽 맨 아래에 빈자리가 남아 있는 것을 발견했습니다. 긴 기록을 거기 적을 자리가 없어서 대신 다음 쪽 맨 위에 적은 모양이었지요."], ["The sight of this chance altered all his plans.", "이 우연을 보자 그자의 계획은 온통 바뀌었습니다."], ["It was an opportunity he had never hoped for, or thought of—and he took it—you know how.", "그것은 그가 바라지도, 생각해 보지도 못한 기회였고—그자는 그것을 잡았습니다—어떻게 잡았는지는 당신도 아시지요."], ["The blank space, to have exactly tallied with his birth certificate, ought to have occurred in the July part of the register.", "그 빈자리가 출생 증명서와 정확히 들어맞으려면 등기부의 7월 부분에 있어야 했습니다."], ["It occurred in the September part instead.", "그런데 그것은 9월 부분에 있었지요."], ["However, in this case, if suspicious questions were asked, the answer was not hard to find.", "그러나 이 경우에도 의심스러운 물음이 나온다면 대답을 찾기는 어렵지 않았습니다."], ["He had only to describe himself as a seven months’ child.", "자기가 일곱 달 만에 난 아이라고 말하기만 하면 되었으니까요."]], [["I was fool enough, when he told me his story, to feel some interest and some pity for him—which was just what he calculated on, as you will see. I thought him hardly used.", "그자가 제게 그 이야기를 들려주었을 때, 저는 어리석게도 그자에게 얼마쯤 흥미와 얼마쯤 연민을 느꼈습니다—그자가 노린 것이 바로 그것이었음을 곧 아시게 될 겁니다."], ["It was not his fault that his father and mother were not married, and it was not his father’s and mother’s fault either.", "저는 그자가 몹쓸 대접을 받았다고 생각했습니다. 아버지와 어머니가 혼인하지 않은 것이 그의 잘못은 아니었고, 아버지와 어머니의 잘못도 아니었으니까요."], ["A more scrupulous woman than I was—a woman who had not set her heart on a gold watch and chain—would have found some excuses for him.", "저보다 더 까다로운 여자였다면—금시계와 금줄에 마음을 빼앗기지 않은 여자였다면—그자에게 무슨 변명거리를 찾아 주었을지도 모르지요."], ["At all events, I held my tongue, and helped to screen what he was about.", "어쨌든 저는 입을 다물었고, 그자가 하는 일을 감춰 주는 데 손을 보탰습니다."]], [["He was some time getting the ink the right colour (mixing it over and over again in pots and bottles of mine), and some time afterwards in practising the handwriting.", "그자는 잉크 빛깔을 제대로 내느라 한참을 (제 단지며 병에다 몇 번이고 되풀이해 섞어 가며) 애썼고, 그 뒤로도 한참을 필체를 익히느라 애썼습니다."], ["But he succeeded in the end, and made an honest woman of his mother after she was dead in her grave!", "그러나 끝내 해냈고, 무덤 속에 든 뒤의 제 어머니를 떳떳한 여자로 만들어 주었지요!"], ["So far, I don’t deny that he behaved honourably enough to myself.", "거기까지는 그자가 제게도 제법 명예롭게 처신했다는 것을 부인하지 않겠습니다."], ["He gave me my watch and chain, and spared no expense in buying them; both were of superior workmanship, and very expensive.", "제게 시계와 줄을 주었고, 그것들을 사는 데 돈을 아끼지 않았습니다. 둘 다 솜씨가 뛰어난 물건이었고, 아주 값진 것이었지요."], ["I have got them still—the watch goes beautifully.", "저는 지금도 그것들을 갖고 있습니다—그 시계는 참 잘도 갑니다."]], [["You said the other day that Mrs. Clements had told you everything she knew.", "지난번에 당신은 클레멘츠 부인이 아는 것을 다 이야기해 주었다고 하셨지요."], ["In that case there is no need for me to write about the trumpery scandal by which I was the sufferer—the innocent sufferer, I positively assert.", "그렇다면 제가 그 하찮은 추문에 대해 쓸 필요는 없겠습니다—제가 그 피해자였고, 죄 없는 피해자였다고 저는 분명히 말해 둡니다."], ["You must know as well as I do what the notion was which my husband took into his head when he found me and my fine-gentleman acquaintance meeting each other privately and talking secrets together.", "저와 그 훌륭하신 신사 나리가 남몰래 만나서 비밀을 주고받는 것을 보았을 때 제 남편의 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박혔는지는 당신도 저만큼이나 아실 테지요."], ["But what you don’t know is how it ended between that same gentleman and myself.", "그러나 당신이 모르는 것은 그 신사와 저 사이가 어떻게 끝났느냐 하는 것입니다."], ["You shall read and see how he behaved to me.", "그자가 저를 어떻게 대했는지 읽어 보고 아시기 바랍니다."]], [["The first words I said to him, when I saw the turn things had taken, were, “Do me justice—clear my character of a stain on it which you know I don’t deserve.", "일이 그렇게 돌아가는 것을 보고 제가 그자에게 맨 처음 한 말은 이러했습니다. “제게 정당한 몫을 해 주세요—제가 받을 까닭이 없다는 걸 당신도 아시는 그 오명을 씻어 주세요."], ["I don’t want you to make a clean breast of it to my husband—only tell him, on your word of honour as a gentleman, that he is wrong, and that I am not to blame in the way he thinks I am.", "제 남편에게 다 털어놓으라는 게 아닙니다—다만 신사로서 명예를 걸고, 그가 잘못 알았으며 제가 그이가 생각하는 그런 식으로 나무랄 짓을 하지 않았다고만 말해 주세요."], ["Do me that justice, at least, after all I have done for you.” He flatly refused, in so many words.", "제가 당신을 위해 해 드린 그 모든 것을 생각하면, 그 정도 정당함은 베풀어 주셔야지요.”"], ["He told me plainly that it was his interest to let my husband and all my neighbours believe the falsehood—because, as long as they did so they were quite certain never to suspect the truth.", "그자는 딱 잘라 거절했습니다. 그러고는 제 남편과 이웃들이 그 거짓을 계속 믿게 두는 편이 자기에게 이롭다고 대놓고 말했습니다—그들이 그렇게 믿는 한 진실만은 결코 의심하지 않을 것이 확실하니까요."], ["I had a spirit of my own, and I told him they should know the truth from my lips.", "저에게도 제 기개가 있어서, 제 입으로 그들에게 진실을 알리겠다고 했습니다."], ["His reply was short, and to the point.", "그자의 대답은 짧고도 핵심을 찔렀습니다."], ["If I spoke, I was a lost woman, as certainly as he was a lost man.", "제가 입을 연다면, 그가 파멸한 사내가 되는 것만큼이나 저 또한 확실히 파멸한 여자가 되리라는 것이었지요."]], [["Yes! it had come to that.", "그렇습니다!"], ["He had deceived me about the risk I ran in helping him.", "일이 거기까지 온 것입니다. 그자는 제가 자기를 돕느라 무릅쓴 위험에 대해 저를 속였습니다."], ["He had practised on my ignorance, he had tempted me with his gifts, he had interested me with his story—and the result of it was that he made me his accomplice.", "제 무지를 이용했고, 선물로 저를 꾀었고, 제 관심을 자기 이야기에 붙들어 두었습니다—그리고 그 결과 저를 자기 공범으로 만들어 놓았습니다."], ["He owned this coolly, and he ended by telling me, for the first time, what the frightful punishment really was for his offence, and for any one who helped him to commit it.", "그자는 이것을 태연히 인정했고, 끝으로 그 죄에 대한, 그리고 그 죄를 돕는 자에 대한 형벌이 실제로 얼마나 무서운 것인지를 그제야 처음으로 일러 주었습니다."], ["In those days the law was not so tender-hearted as I hear it is now.", "그 시절의 법은 요즘 듣기로 그렇다는 것만큼 인정이 많지 않았습니다."], ["Murderers were not the only people liable to be hanged, and women convicts were not treated like ladies in undeserved distress.", "교수형에 처해질 수 있는 것이 살인자만은 아니었고, 여자 죄수라고 해서 억울한 곤경에 빠진 귀부인처럼 대접받지도 않았지요."], ["I confess he frightened me—the mean impostor!", "고백하건대 그자는 저를 겁먹게 만들었습니다—그 비열한 사기꾼!"], ["the cowardly blackguard!", "그 겁쟁이 악당!"], ["Do you understand now how I hated him?", "이제 제가 그자를 얼마나 미워했는지 아시겠습니까?"], ["Do you understand why I am taking all this trouble—thankfully taking it—to gratify the curiosity of the meritorious young gentleman who hunted him down?", "제가 왜 이 모든 수고를—기꺼운 마음으로 이 수고를—무릅쓰고, 그자를 사냥해 잡은 갸륵하신 젊은 신사분의 호기심을 채워 드리려 하는지 아시겠습니까?"]], [["Well, to go on.", "자, 이야기를 이어 가지요."], ["He was hardly fool enough to drive me to downright desperation.", "그자는 저를 아주 절망으로까지 몰아넣을 만큼 어리석지는 않았습니다."], ["I was not the sort of woman whom it was quite safe to hunt into a corner—he knew that, and wisely quieted me with proposals for the future.", "저는 궁지로 몰아넣어도 마냥 안전한 그런 여자가 아니었지요—그자도 그것을 알았기에, 앞날에 대한 제안으로 슬기롭게 저를 달랬습니다."]], [["I deserved some reward (he was kind enough to say) for the service I had done him, and some compensation (he was so obliging as to add) for what I had suffered.", "제가 그자에게 해 준 일에 대해서는 얼마간 보답을 받을 만하고 (친절하게도 그렇게 말해 주더군요), 제가 겪은 일에 대해서도 얼마간 보상을 받을 만하다는 것이었습니다 (황송하게도 그렇게 덧붙이더군요)."], ["He was quite willing—generous scoundrel!—to make me a handsome yearly allowance, payable quarterly, on two conditions.", "그자는—이 너그러우신 악당께서는—두 가지 조건 아래 제게 넉넉한 연금을 분기마다 나누어 지급할 뜻이 아주 흔쾌히 있었습니다."], ["First, I was to hold my tongue—in my own interests as well as in his.", "첫째, 저는 그의 이익을 위해서만이 아니라 제 이익을 위해서도 입을 다물어야 한다는 것."], ["Secondly, I was not to stir away from Welmingham without first letting him know, and waiting till I had obtained his permission.", "둘째, 저는 먼저 그에게 알리고 허락을 얻을 때까지 기다리지 않고서는 웰밍엄을 떠나 움직여서는 안 된다는 것이었습니다."], ["In my own neighbourhood, no virtuous female friends would tempt me into dangerous gossiping at the tea-table. In my own neighbourhood, he would always know where to find me.", "제 동네에 있으면 정숙한 여자 친구들이 저를 꾀어 다과상 앞에서 위험한 수다를 떨게 할 일이 없을 테고, 제 동네에 있으면 그는 언제든 저를 어디서 찾을지 알 테니까요."], ["A hard condition, that second one—but I accepted it.", "그 두 번째 조건은 가혹한 것이었습니다—그러나 저는 받아들였습니다."]], [["What else was I to do?", "달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["I was left helpless, with the prospect of a coming incumbrance in the shape of a child.", "저는 아무 의지할 데 없이 버려진 처지였고, 게다가 아이라는 짐이 다가오고 있었습니다."], ["What else was I to do?", "달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["Cast myself on the mercy of my runaway idiot of a husband who had raised the scandal against me?", "저에게 그 추문을 뒤집어씌워 놓고 달아난 그 얼간이 남편의 자비에 몸을 맡길까요?"], ["I would have died first.", "차라리 죽는 편이 나았습니다."], ["Besides, the allowance WAS a handsome one.", "게다가 그 수당은 실로 후한 것이었습니다."], ["I had a better income, a better house over my head, better carpets on my floors, than half the women who turned up the whites of their eyes at the sight of me.", "저를 보기만 하면 흰자위를 뒤집던 그 여자들의 절반보다도 저는 수입이 나았고, 머리 위의 집이 나았고, 바닥에 깔린 양탄자가 나았습니다. 우리 고장에서 미덕의 옷은 무명 날염이었지요."], ["The dress of Virtue, in our parts, was cotton print. I had silk.", "저는 비단을 입었습니다."]], [["So I accepted the conditions he offered me, and made the best of them, and fought my battle with my respectable neighbours on their own ground, and won it in course of time—as you saw yourself.", "그리하여 저는 그가 내놓은 조건을 받아들였고, 그 조건에서 뽑아낼 수 있는 것은 모두 뽑아냈으며, 점잖은 이웃들과 그들의 땅에서 싸움을 벌였고, 시간이 흐르는 동안 그 싸움에서 이겼습니다—당신 눈으로 직접 보신 그대로입니다."], ["How I kept his Secret (and mine) through all the years that have passed from that time to this, and whether my late daughter, Anne, ever really crept into my confidence, and got the keeping of the Secret too—are questions, I dare say, to which you are curious to find an answer.", "그때부터 지금까지 흘러간 그 모든 세월 동안 제가 어떻게 그의 비밀(그리고 저의 비밀)을 지켜 왔는지, 그리고 죽은 제 딸 앤이 정말로 제 속내에 파고들어 그 비밀까지 함께 간직하게 되었는지—이런 것들이 아마 당신이 답을 알고 싶어 하는 물음이겠지요. 좋습니다!"], ["Well! my gratitude refuses you nothing.", "저의 감사는 당신에게 아무것도 거절하지 않습니다."], ["I will turn to a fresh page and give you the answer immediately.", "새 장을 넘겨 곧바로 답을 드리지요."], ["But you must excuse one thing—you must excuse my beginning, Mr. Hartright, with an expression of surprise at the interest which you appear to have felt in my late daughter.", "다만 한 가지는 양해해 주셔야겠습니다—하트라이트 씨, 죽은 제 딸에게 당신이 품은 듯한 그 관심에 대해 놀라움을 표하는 것으로 말문을 여는 것을 양해해 주십시오."], ["It is quite unaccountable to me.", "저로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 일입니다."], ["If that interest makes you anxious for any particulars of her early life, I must refer you to Mrs. Clements, who knows more of the subject than I do.", "그 관심 때문에 그 애의 어린 시절에 관한 자세한 사정이 궁금하시다면, 저보다 그 문제를 더 잘 아는 클레멘츠 부인에게 물어보시라고 하는 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Pray understand that I do not profess to have been at all overfond of my late daughter.", "그리고 제가 죽은 딸을 조금이라도 끔찍이 아꼈던 척할 생각은 없다는 점을 부디 이해해 주십시오."], ["She was a worry to me from first to last, with the additional disadvantage of being always weak in the head.", "그 애는 처음부터 끝까지 제게 근심거리였고, 게다가 늘 머리가 모자란다는 결점까지 있었습니다."], ["You like candour, and I hope this satisfies you.", "당신은 솔직함을 좋아하시니, 이만하면 만족하시리라 믿습니다."]], [["There is no need to trouble you with many personal particulars relating to those past times.", "지난 시절과 관련된 개인적인 사정을 시시콜콜 늘어놓아 당신을 성가시게 할 필요는 없겠지요."], ["It will be enough to say that I observed the terms of the bargain on my side, and that I enjoyed my comfortable income in return, paid quarterly.", "제 쪽에서는 그 거래의 조건을 지켰고, 그 대가로 분기마다 지급되는 넉넉한 수입을 누렸다고만 말해 두면 충분할 것입니다."]], [["Now and then I got away and changed the scene for a short time, always asking leave of my lord and master first, and generally getting it.", "이따금 저는 잠시 집을 떠나 다른 곳에서 바람을 쐬고 왔는데, 언제나 제 주인이자 상전에게 먼저 허락을 청했고 대개는 허락을 받아냈습니다."], ["He was not, as I have already told you, fool enough to drive me too hard, and he could reasonably rely on my holding my tongue for my own sake, if not for his.", "이미 말씀드렸다시피 그는 저를 지나치게 몰아붙일 만큼 어리석지는 않았고, 그를 위해서는 아니더라도 제 자신을 위해서라도 제가 입을 다물리라고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었습니다."], ["One of my longest trips away from home was the trip I took to Limmeridge to nurse a half-sister there, who was dying.", "집을 떠나 있었던 가장 긴 여행 가운데 하나가 리머리지로 가서 죽어 가는 이복 자매를 간호한 일이었습니다."], ["She was reported to have saved money, and I thought it as well (in case any accident happened to stop my allowance) to look after my own interests in that direction.", "그 여자가 돈을 모아 두었다는 소문이 있었고, 저는 (혹시라도 무슨 일이 생겨 수당이 끊길 경우를 대비해) 그쪽으로도 제 몫을 챙겨 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했지요."], ["As things turned out, however, my pains were all thrown away, and I got nothing, because nothing was to be had.", "그러나 일이 돌아간 결과 제 수고는 모두 헛되었고, 저는 아무것도 얻지 못했습니다. 애초에 얻을 것이 없었으니까요."]], [["I had taken Anne to the north with me, having my whims and fancies, occasionally, about my child, and getting, at such times, jealous of Mrs.", "저는 앤을 데리고 북쪽으로 갔습니다. 제 아이에 대해 이따금 변덕과 애착이 일었고, 그럴 때면 클레멘츠 부인이 그 애에게 미치는 영향력에 샘이 났기 때문입니다."], ["Clements’ influence over her. I never liked Mrs.", "저는 클레멘츠 부인을 좋아한 적이 없습니다."], ["Clements. She was a poor, empty-headed, spiritless woman—what you call a born drudge—and I was now and then not averse to plaguing her by taking Anne away.", "가엾고 머리가 텅 비고 기개라고는 없는 여자였지요—당신들 말로 하자면 천생 부림당할 팔자인 사람 말입니다—그래서 저는 이따금 앤을 데려가 그 여자를 애태우는 것도 싫지 않았습니다."], ["Not knowing what else to do with my girl while I was nursing in Cumberland, I put her to school at Limmeridge.", "컴벌랜드에서 간병을 하는 동안 제 딸을 달리 어찌할 방도가 없어서, 저는 그 애를 리머리지의 학교에 넣었습니다."], ["The lady of the manor, Mrs. Fairlie (a remarkably plain-looking woman, who had entrapped one of the handsomest men in England into marrying her), amused me wonderfully by taking a violent fancy to my girl.", "그 고장 지주의 안주인인 페얼리 부인(놀랄 만큼 못생긴 여자였는데도 영국에서 가장 잘생긴 남자 중 하나를 홀려 결혼에 끌어들인 사람이지요)이 제 딸에게 갑작스레 푹 빠져 버려서 저를 몹시 재미나게 했습니다."], ["The consequence was, she learnt nothing at school, and was petted and spoilt at Limmeridge House.", "그 결과 그 애는 학교에서 아무것도 배우지 못했고, 리머리지 하우스에서 응석받이가 되어 버릇만 나빠졌습니다."], ["Among other whims and fancies which they taught her there, they put some nonsense into her head about always wearing white.", "거기서 그 애에게 가르쳐 준 온갖 변덕과 공상 가운데는, 늘 흰옷만 입어야 한다는 터무니없는 생각을 머릿속에 심어 준 것도 있었습니다."], ["Hating white and liking colours myself, I determined to take the nonsense out of her head as soon as we got home again.", "저는 흰색을 싫어하고 색깔 있는 옷을 좋아하는 사람이라, 집에 돌아가는 대로 그 어리석은 생각을 그 애 머리에서 뽑아내야겠다고 마음먹었습니다."]], [["Strange to say, my daughter resolutely resisted me.", "이상하게도 제 딸은 완강하게 저에게 저항했습니다."], ["When she HAD got a notion once fixed in her mind she was, like other half-witted people, as obstinate as a mule in keeping it.", "한번 머릿속에 어떤 생각이 박히면 그 애는 다른 얼빠진 사람들이 다 그렇듯 노새처럼 고집스럽게 그것을 붙들었습니다."], ["We quarrelled finely, and Mrs. Clements, not liking to see it, I suppose, offered to take Anne away to live in London with her.", "우리는 실컷 다투었고, 클레멘츠 부인은 그 꼴을 보기가 딱했던지 앤을 데려가 런던에서 함께 살겠다고 나섰습니다."], ["I should have said Yes, if Mrs. Clements had not sided with my daughter about her dressing herself in white.", "클레멘츠 부인이 흰옷 입는 문제에서 제 딸 편을 들지만 않았다면 저는 그러라고 했을 것입니다."], ["But being determined she should NOT dress herself in white, and disliking Mrs. Clements more than ever for taking part against me, I said No, and meant No, and stuck to No.", "그러나 그 애에게 흰옷을 입히지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 데다, 저를 거스르고 편을 든 그 여자가 그 어느 때보다 미워졌기에, 저는 안 된다고 말했고, 안 된다는 뜻이었으며, 안 된다는 데서 물러서지 않았습니다."], ["The consequence was, my daughter remained with me, and the consequence of that, in its turn, was the first serious quarrel that happened about the Secret.", "그 결과 제 딸은 저와 함께 남았고, 그 결과가 다시 낳은 것이 바로 그 비밀을 둘러싼 첫 번째 심각한 다툼이었습니다."]], [["The circumstance took place long after the time I have just been writing of.", "그 일은 방금 제가 적은 시기보다 한참 뒤에 일어났습니다."], ["I had been settled for years in the new town, and was steadily living down my bad character and slowly gaining ground among the respectable inhabitants.", "저는 새 읍내에 자리를 잡고 여러 해를 지내며 나쁜 평판을 꾸준히 씻어 내고 점잖은 주민들 사이에서 천천히 발판을 넓혀 가고 있었습니다."], ["It helped me forward greatly towards this object to have my daughter with me.", "딸을 데리고 있는 것이 이 목적에 크게 도움이 되었습니다."], ["Her harmlessness and her fancy for dressing in white excited a certain amount of sympathy.", "그 애의 무해함과 흰옷을 입으려는 별난 취향이 얼마간의 동정을 불러일으켰거든요."], ["I left off opposing her favourite whim on that account, because some of the sympathy was sure, in course of time, to fall to my share.", "그래서 저는 그 애가 좋아하는 그 변덕에 반대하기를 그만두었습니다. 그 동정의 얼마쯤은 시간이 지나면 반드시 제 몫으로도 떨어질 것이 분명했기 때문입니다."], ["Some of it did fall.", "실제로 얼마쯤은 떨어졌습니다."], ["I date my getting a choice of the two best sittings to let in the church from that time, and I date the clergyman’s first bow from my getting the sittings.", "교회에서 세를 놓는 가장 좋은 자리 두 개를 골라 가질 수 있게 된 것도 그때부터였고, 목사님이 처음으로 저에게 인사를 건넨 것도 그 자리를 얻은 뒤부터였습니다."]], [["Well, being settled in this way, I received a letter one morning from that highly born gentleman (now deceased) in answer to one of mine, warning him, according to agreement, of my wishing to leave the town for a little change of air and scene.", "자, 이렇게 자리를 잡고 지내던 어느 날 아침, 저는 그 지체 높으신 신사(지금은 고인이 되었지요)에게서 편지 한 통을 받았습니다. 약정에 따라, 잠시 바람도 쐬고 경치도 바꿀 겸 읍내를 떠나고 싶다고 미리 알린 제 편지에 대한 답장이었습니다."]], [["The ruffianly side of him must have been uppermost, I suppose, when he got my letter, for he wrote back, refusing me in such abominably insolent language, that I lost all command over myself, and abused him, in my daughter’s presence, as “a low impostor whom I could ruin for life if I chose to open my lips and let out his Secret.”", "제 편지를 받았을 때 그의 불한당 같은 면이 고개를 쳐들었던 모양입니다. 그는 지독히도 무례한 말투로 거절하는 답장을 보내왔고, 저는 그만 자제력을 완전히 잃고 제 딸이 보는 앞에서 그를 두고 “내가 입을 열어 그 비밀을 터뜨리기로 마음만 먹으면 평생을 망가뜨릴 수 있는 천한 사기꾼”이라고 욕했습니다."], ["I said no more about him than that, being brought to my senses as soon as those words had escaped me by the sight of my daughter’s face looking eagerly and curiously at mine.", "그에 대해서는 그 이상 말하지 않았습니다. 그 말이 입에서 튀어나오자마자 제 얼굴을 열심히, 그리고 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보는 딸아이의 얼굴이 눈에 들어와 정신이 번쩍 들었기 때문입니다."], ["I instantly ordered her out of the room until I had composed myself again.", "저는 마음을 다시 가라앉힐 때까지 그 애를 즉시 방에서 내보냈습니다."]], [["My sensations were not pleasant, I can tell you, when I came to reflect on my own folly.", "제 어리석음을 곱씹게 되었을 때의 기분이 유쾌하지 않았다는 것만은 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["Anne had been more than usually crazy and queer that year, and when I thought of the chance there might be of her repeating my words in the town, and mentioning HIS name in connection with them, if inquisitive people got hold of her, I was finely terrified at the possible consequences.", "그해에 앤은 여느 때보다도 더 미친 듯하고 이상했는데, 만약 캐묻기 좋아하는 사람들이 그 애를 붙들기라도 하면 그 애가 읍내에서 제 말을 되풀이하고 그 말에 그의 이름까지 갖다 붙일 가능성이 있다는 데 생각이 미치자, 저는 그로 인해 벌어질지 모를 결과에 몹시 겁이 났습니다."], ["My worst fears for myself, my worst dread of what he might do, led me no farther than this.", "제 자신에 대한 최악의 두려움도, 그가 저지를지 모를 일에 대한 최악의 공포도 거기까지밖에 미치지 못했습니다."], ["I was quite unprepared for what really did happen only the next day.", "바로 그다음 날 실제로 벌어진 일에 대해서는 저는 전혀 대비가 되어 있지 않았습니다."]], [["On that next day, without any warning to me to expect him, he came to the house.", "바로 그다음 날, 온다는 예고 한마디 없이 그가 집으로 찾아왔습니다."]], [["His first words, and the tone in which he spoke them, surly as it was, showed me plainly enough that he had repented already of his insolent answer to my application, and that he had come in a mighty bad temper to try and set matters right again before it was too late.", "그의 첫마디와 그것을 내뱉는 어조는, 무뚝뚝하기는 했어도, 그가 제 청원에 대한 자신의 무례한 답장을 이미 후회하고 있으며 너무 늦기 전에 일을 다시 바로잡아 보려고 잔뜩 뒤틀린 기분으로 찾아왔다는 것을 저에게 충분히 분명하게 보여 주었습니다."], ["Seeing my daughter in the room with me (I had been afraid to let her out of my sight after what had happened the day before) he ordered her away.", "방 안에 딸이 저와 함께 있는 것을 보자(전날 일이 있은 뒤로 저는 그 애를 제 눈에서 떼어 놓기가 겁이 났습니다) 그는 그 애더러 나가라고 명령했습니다."], ["They neither of them liked each other, and he vented the ill-temper on HER which he was afraid to show to ME.", "그 둘은 서로를 좋아하지 않았고, 그는 저에게는 감히 드러내지 못하는 심술을 그 애에게 쏟아부었습니다."]], [["“Leave us,”", "“자리를 비켜라.”"], ["he said, looking at her over his shoulder.", "어깨 너머로 그 애를 보며 그가 말했습니다."], ["She looked back over HER shoulder and waited as if she didn’t care to go.", "그 애도 제 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 갈 마음이 없다는 듯 버티고 서 있었습니다."], ["“Do you hear?”", "“안 들리느냐?”"], ["he roared out, “leave the room.”", "그가 고함을 질렀습니다. “방에서 나가라니까.”"], ["“Speak to me civilly,”", "“말씀을 곱게 하세요.”"], ["says she, getting red in the face.", "얼굴이 붉어지며 그 애가 말했습니다."], ["“Turn the idiot out,”", "“저 얼간이를 내보내시오.”"], ["says he, looking my way.", "제 쪽을 보며 그가 말했습니다."], ["She had always had crazy notions of her own about her dignity, and that word “idiot” upset her in a moment.", "그 애는 자기 위신에 관해서는 늘 얼빠진 사람다운 고집을 품고 있던 터라, 그 “얼간이”라는 말에 대번에 발끈했습니다."], ["Before I could interfere she stepped up to him in a fine passion.", "제가 끼어들 새도 없이 그 애는 대단한 기세로 그에게 다가섰습니다."], ["“Beg my pardon, directly,”", "“당장 사과하세요.” 그 애가 말했습니다."], ["says she, “or I’ll make it the worse for you.", "“안 그러면 더 험한 꼴을 보게 해 드리죠."], ["I’ll let out your Secret.", "당신 비밀을 터뜨려 버릴 테니까요."], ["I can ruin you for life if I choose to open my lips.”", "내가 입을 열기로 마음만 먹으면 당신 평생을 망가뜨릴 수 있어요.”"], ["My own words!—repeated exactly from what I had said the day before—repeated, in his presence, as if they had come from herself.", "제가 했던 바로 그 말이었습니다!—전날 제가 한 말을 고스란히 되풀이한 것이었지요—그것도 그가 있는 앞에서, 마치 그 애 자신에게서 나온 말인 양 되풀이한 것입니다."], ["He sat speechless, as white as the paper I am writing on, while I pushed her out of the room.", "제가 그 애를 방 밖으로 밀어내는 동안 그는 지금 제가 글을 쓰고 있는 이 종이만큼이나 하얘진 얼굴로 말 한마디 못 하고 앉아 있었습니다."], ["When he recovered himself——", "그러다 정신을 차리고 나서는——"]], [["No!", "아니요!"], ["I am too respectable a woman to mention what he said when he recovered himself.", "저는 그가 정신을 차린 뒤에 무슨 말을 했는지 입에 올리기에는 너무 점잖은 여자입니다."], ["My pen is the pen of a member of the rector’s congregation, and a subscriber to the “Wednesday Lectures on Justification by Faith”—how can you expect me to employ it in writing bad language?", "제 펜은 목사님 회중의 일원이자 “믿음으로 말미암은 의(義)에 관한 수요 강좌”의 후원자인 사람의 펜입니다—그런 펜으로 상스러운 말을 받아쓰기를 어찌 기대하실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Suppose, for yourself, the raging, swearing frenzy of the lowest ruffian in England, and let us get on together, as fast as may be, to the way in which it all ended.", "영국에서 가장 천한 불한당이 미쳐 날뛰며 욕설을 퍼붓는 광경을 알아서 상상하시고, 우리는 되도록 빨리 그 모든 일이 어떻게 끝났는지로 함께 나아가기로 하지요."]], [["It ended, as you probably guess by this time, in his insisting on securing his own safety by shutting her up.", "그 일은, 이쯤이면 당신도 짐작하시겠지만, 그가 자기 안전을 지키겠다며 그 애를 가두어야 한다고 우기는 것으로 끝났습니다."]], [["I tried to set things right.", "저는 일을 바로잡아 보려 했습니다."], ["I told him that she had merely repeated, like a parrot, the words she had heard me say and that she knew no particulars whatever, because I had mentioned none.", "그 애는 그저 앵무새처럼 제가 하는 말을 듣고 되풀이했을 뿐이며, 제가 아무것도 말해 주지 않았으니 자세한 내막은 조금도 알지 못한다고 그에게 말했습니다."], ["I explained that she had affected, out of crazy spite against him, to know what she really did NOT know—that she only wanted to threaten him and aggravate him for speaking to her as he had just spoken—and that my unlucky words gave her just the chance of doing mischief of which she was in search.", "그 애는 그를 향한 미친 앙심에서 실제로는 알지 못하는 것을 아는 척했을 뿐이고—방금 그가 자기에게 그런 식으로 말한 데 대해 그를 위협하고 약을 올리고 싶었을 뿐이며—운 나쁘게 튀어나온 제 말이 마침 그 애가 찾고 있던 심술 부릴 기회를 주었을 뿐이라고 설명했습니다."], ["I referred him to other queer ways of hers, and to his own experience of the vagaries of half-witted people—it was all to no purpose—he would not believe me on my oath—he was absolutely certain I had betrayed the whole Secret.", "그 애의 다른 별난 행동들과, 얼빠진 사람들의 변덕에 대한 그 자신의 경험까지 들먹였지만—아무 소용이 없었습니다—그는 제가 맹세를 해도 믿지 않았습니다—그는 제가 비밀을 통째로 발설했다고 완전히 확신하고 있었습니다."], ["In short, he would hear of nothing but shutting her up.", "요컨대 그는 그 애를 가두는 것 말고는 어떤 말도 들으려 하지 않았습니다."]], [["Under these circumstances, I did my duty as a mother.", "이런 상황에서 저는 어머니로서의 도리를 다했습니다. “빈민 정신병원은 안 됩니다.”"], ["“No pauper Asylum,”", "저는 말했습니다."], ["I said, “I won’t have her put in a pauper Asylum.", "“그 애를 빈민 정신병원에 넣게 두지는 않겠어요."], ["A Private Establishment, if you please.", "굳이 하시겠다면 사립 시설로 하시지요."], ["I have my feelings as a mother, and my character to preserve in the town, and I will submit to nothing but a Private Establishment, of the sort which my genteel neighbours would choose for afflicted relatives of their own.”", "저에게도 어머니로서의 심정이 있고 읍내에서 지켜야 할 체면이 있으니, 저희 점잖은 이웃들이 자기네 병든 친척을 위해 고를 법한 그런 사립 시설이 아니면 어떤 것에도 응하지 않겠습니다.”"], ["Those were my words.", "그것이 제가 한 말이었습니다."], ["It is gratifying to me to reflect that I did my duty.", "제가 도리를 다했다는 것을 돌이켜 보면 흡족합니다."], ["Though never overfond of my late daughter, I had a proper pride about her.", "죽은 딸을 끔찍이 아낀 적은 없었어도 저는 그 애에 대해 마땅한 자존심을 지니고 있었습니다."], ["No pauper stain—thanks to my firmness and resolution—ever rested on MY child.", "제 단호함과 결단 덕분에 빈민이라는 오점은 제 아이에게 결코 묻지 않았습니다."]], [["Having carried my point (which I did the more easily, in consequence of the facilities offered by private Asylums), I could not refuse to admit that there were certain advantages gained by shutting her up.", "제 주장을 관철하고 나자(사립 정신병원이 제공하는 편의 덕분에 그것은 한결 수월했습니다) 저는 그 애를 가둠으로써 얻어지는 이점이 분명히 있다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["In the first place, she was taken excellent care of—being treated (as I took care to mention in the town) on the footing of a lady.", "우선 그 애는 극진한 보살핌을 받았습니다—제가 읍내에 빠짐없이 알린 대로, 숙녀 대접을 받으면서 말입니다."], ["In the second place, she was kept away from Welmingham, where she might have set people suspecting and inquiring, by repeating my own incautious words.", "다음으로 그 애는 웰밍엄에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 되었는데, 그곳에 그대로 있었더라면 제가 경솔하게 내뱉은 말을 되풀이해 사람들이 의심하고 캐묻게 만들었을지도 모를 일이었습니다."]], [["The only drawback of putting her under restraint was a very slight one.", "그 애를 가두어 두는 데 따르는 단점은 하나뿐이었고 그나마도 아주 사소했습니다."], ["We merely turned her empty boast about knowing the Secret into a fixed delusion.", "우리는 비밀을 안다는 그 애의 헛된 허풍을 고정된 망상으로 바꿔 놓았을 뿐이니까요."], ["Having first spoken in sheer crazy spitefulness against the man who had offended her, she was cunning enough to see that she had seriously frightened him, and sharp enough afterwards to discover that HE was concerned in shutting her up.", "처음에는 자기를 모욕한 남자에게 순전히 미친 앙심으로 내뱉은 말이었지만, 그 애는 자기가 그를 단단히 겁먹게 했다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 교활했고, 나중에는 자기를 가둔 일에 그가 관련되어 있다는 것을 알아낼 만큼은 영리했습니다."], ["The consequence was she flamed out into a perfect frenzy of passion against him, going to the Asylum, and the first words she said to the nurses, after they had quieted her, were, that she was put in confinement for knowing his Secret, and that she meant to open her lips and ruin him, when the right time came.", "그 결과 그 애는 그를 향한 완전한 광란의 분노로 타올랐고, 그 상태로 정신병원에 들어갔습니다. 간호인들이 그 애를 진정시킨 뒤 그 애가 처음으로 한 말은, 자기는 그의 비밀을 알기 때문에 갇힌 것이며 때가 되면 입을 열어 그를 파멸시킬 작정이라는 것이었습니다."]], [["She may have said the same thing to you, when you thoughtlessly assisted her escape.", "당신이 생각 없이 그 애의 탈출을 도왔을 때에도 그 애가 당신에게 같은 말을 했을지 모르겠군요."], ["She certainly said it (as I heard last summer) to the unfortunate woman who married our sweet-tempered, nameless gentleman lately deceased.", "지난여름에 들은 바로는, 최근에 세상을 떠난 그 상냥한 성품의 이름 없는 신사와 결혼한 불행한 여인에게도 그 애가 분명 그 말을 했다더군요."], ["If either you, or that unlucky lady, had questioned my daughter closely, and had insisted on her explaining what she really meant, you would have found her lose all her self-importance suddenly, and get vacant, and restless, and confused—you would have discovered that I am writing nothing here but the plain truth.", "당신이든 그 딱한 부인이든 제 딸을 바짝 캐물어 정말로 무슨 뜻인지 설명해 보라고 다그쳤더라면, 그 애가 대번에 그 잘난 척을 다 잃고 멍해지고 안절부절못하고 혼란스러워지는 것을 보셨을 테고—제가 여기에 적는 것이 순전한 사실뿐임을 알아차리셨을 것입니다."], ["She knew that there was a Secret—she knew who was connected with it—she knew who would suffer by its being known—and beyond that, whatever airs of importance she may have given herself, whatever crazy boasting she may have indulged in with strangers, she never to her dying day knew more.", "그 애는 비밀이 있다는 것을 알았고—누가 거기에 얽혀 있는지 알았고—그것이 알려지면 누가 곤경에 빠지는지 알았습니다. 그리고 그 너머로는, 스스로 아무리 대단한 척을 했든 낯선 사람들 앞에서 아무리 미친 허풍을 늘어놓았든, 죽는 날까지 그 이상은 알지 못했습니다."]], [["Have I satisfied your curiosity?", "당신의 호기심을 만족시켜 드렸는지요?"], ["I have taken pains enough to satisfy it at any rate.", "어쨌든 그것을 만족시켜 드리려고 어지간히 공을 들이기는 했습니다."], ["There is really nothing else I have to tell you about myself or my daughter.", "저 자신이나 제 딸에 관해 당신에게 더 들려드릴 이야기는 정말이지 없습니다."], ["My worst responsibilities, so far as she was concerned, were all over when she was secured in the Asylum.", "그 애와 관련해 제가 짊어졌던 최악의 책임은 그 애가 정신병원에 안전히 들어가면서 모두 끝났습니다."], ["I had a form of letter relating to the circumstances under which she was shut up, given me to write, in answer to one Miss Halcombe, who was curious in the matter, and who must have heard plenty of lies about me from a certain tongue well accustomed to the telling of the same.", "그 애가 갇히게 된 정황에 관해 홀콤 양이라는 분이 궁금해하며 물어 왔을 때에는, 답장에 쓸 편지의 문안을 받아서 그대로 옮겨 적었습니다. 그분은 그런 이야기를 옮기는 데 아주 익숙한 어떤 혀에게서 저에 관한 거짓말을 잔뜩 들었을 것이 틀림없습니다."], ["And I did what I could afterwards to trace my runaway daughter, and prevent her from doing mischief by making inquiries myself in the neighbourhood where she was falsely reported to have been seen.", "그리고 그 뒤에는 달아난 딸의 행방을 좇고 그 애가 말썽을 부리지 못하게 하려고 제가 할 수 있는 일을 했는데, 그 애가 목격되었다는 헛소문이 도는 동네에서 직접 사람들에게 묻고 다니기도 했습니다."], ["But these, and other trifles like them, are of little or no interest to you after what you have heard already.", "그러나 이런 일이나 그와 비슷한 자잘한 일들은, 당신이 이미 들으신 이야기에 비하면 흥미랄 것이 거의 또는 전혀 없겠지요."]], [["So far, I have written in the friendliest possible spirit.", "여기까지 저는 더할 나위 없이 다정한 마음으로 썼습니다."], ["But I cannot close this letter without adding a word here of serious remonstrance and reproof, addressed to yourself.", "그러나 이 편지를 닫기 전에 당신을 향한 진지한 항의와 책망의 말을 한마디 덧붙이지 않을 수 없습니다."]], [["In the course of your personal interview with me, you audaciously referred to my late daughter’s parentage on the father’s side, as if that parentage was a matter of doubt.", "저를 직접 만나 이야기하는 자리에서 당신은 죽은 제 딸의 아비 쪽 혈통을, 마치 그것이 의심스러운 문제라도 되는 양 뻔뻔스럽게 입에 올렸습니다."], ["This was highly improper and very ungentlemanlike on your part!", "그것은 당신으로서 대단히 온당치 못하고 신사답지 못한 짓이었습니다!"], ["If we see each other again, remember, if you please, that I will allow no liberties to be taken with my reputation, and that the moral atmosphere of Welmingham (to use a favourite expression of my friend the rector’s) must not be tainted by loose conversation of any kind.", "우리가 다시 만나게 되거든 부디 기억해 주십시오. 저는 제 평판을 함부로 다루는 일을 결코 용납하지 않을 것이며, 웰밍엄의 도덕적 공기는(제 친구인 목사님이 즐겨 쓰는 표현을 빌리자면) 그 어떤 종류의 방정치 못한 대화로도 더럽혀져서는 안 됩니다."], ["If you allow yourself to doubt that my husband was Anne’s father, you personally insult me in the grossest manner.", "당신이 스스로 제 남편이 앤의 아버지라는 사실을 의심한다면, 그것은 당신이 저를 가장 저열한 방식으로 모욕하는 것입니다."], ["If you have felt, and if you still continue to feel, an unhallowed curiosity on this subject, I recommend you, in your own interests, to check it at once, and for ever.", "이 문제에 대해 부정한 호기심을 느꼈다면, 그리고 지금도 여전히 느끼고 있다면, 저는 당신 자신을 위해서 그것을 당장, 그리고 영원히 접으시라고 권합니다."], ["On this side of the grave, Mr. Hartright, whatever may happen on the other, THAT curiosity will never be gratified.", "하트라이트 씨, 무덤 저편에서는 어찌 될지 몰라도 무덤 이편에서는 그 호기심이 결코 채워지지 않을 것입니다."]], [["Perhaps, after what I have just said, you will see the necessity of writing me an apology.", "방금 제가 한 말을 듣고 나면 아마 당신도 저에게 사과 편지를 써야 할 필요를 느끼시겠지요."], ["Do so, and I will willingly receive it.", "그렇게 하십시오. 그러면 기꺼이 받아들이겠습니다."], ["I will, afterwards, if your wishes point to a second interview with me, go a step farther, and receive you.", "그런 다음, 당신이 저와 두 번째로 만나 뵙기를 원하신다면 한 걸음 더 나아가 당신을 맞아들이겠습니다."], ["My circumstances only enable me to invite you to tea—not that they are at all altered for the worse by what has happened.", "제 형편으로는 차 한잔 대접하는 것밖에 청할 수가 없습니다—그렇다고 이번 일로 형편이 조금이라도 나빠진 것은 아닙니다."], ["I have always lived, as I think I told you, well within my income, and I have saved enough, in the last twenty years, to make me quite comfortable for the rest of my life.", "말씀드린 것 같습니다만 저는 늘 수입 안에서 넉넉히 살아왔고, 지난 이십 년 동안 남은 평생을 아주 편안히 지낼 만큼은 모아 두었습니다."], ["It is not my intention to leave Welmingham.", "웰밍엄을 떠날 생각은 없습니다."], ["There are one or two little advantages which I have still to gain in the town.", "이 읍내에서 아직 얻어야 할 자잘한 이점이 한두 가지 남아 있으니까요."], ["The clergyman bows to me—as you saw.", "목사님은 저에게 인사를 합니다—당신도 보셨듯이 말이지요."], ["He is married, and his wife is not quite so civil.", "그분에게는 부인이 있는데 그 부인은 그만큼 예의 바르지가 않습니다."], ["I propose to join the Dorcas Society, and I mean to make the clergyman’s wife bow to me next.", "저는 도르카스 회에 들어갈 작정이고, 다음에는 그 목사님 부인이 저에게 인사하도록 만들 생각입니다."]], [["If you favour me with your company, pray understand that the conversation must be entirely on general subjects.", "당신이 저를 찾아 주는 영광을 베푸신다면, 대화는 전적으로 일반적인 화제로만 이루어져야 한다는 점을 부디 이해해 주십시오."], ["Any attempted reference to this letter will be quite useless—I am determined not to acknowledge having written it.", "이 편지를 조금이라도 언급하려는 시도는 아무 소용이 없을 것입니다—저는 이것을 썼다는 사실을 인정하지 않기로 마음먹었으니까요."], ["The evidence has been destroyed in the fire, I know, but I think it desirable to err on the side of caution, nevertheless.", "증거는 불에 타 없어졌다는 것을 알고 있습니다만, 그래도 조심하는 쪽으로 치우쳐 두는 편이 바람직하다고 생각합니다."]], [["On this account no names are mentioned here, nor is any signature attached to these lines: the handwriting is disguised throughout, and I mean to deliver the letter myself, under circumstances which will prevent all fear of its being traced to my house.", "그런 까닭에 여기에는 아무 이름도 적지 않았고, 이 글에는 서명도 붙이지 않았습니다. 글씨체는 처음부터 끝까지 위장했으며, 편지는 제가 직접, 저희 집으로 추적당할 염려가 전혀 없을 만한 정황에서 전달할 작정입니다."], ["You can have no possible cause to complain of these precautions, seeing that they do not affect the information I here communicate, in consideration of the special indulgence which you have deserved at my hands.", "이런 조심 때문에 당신이 불평할 이유는 조금도 없을 것입니다. 그것이 제가 여기에 전하는 정보에는 아무 영향도 주지 않으니까요."], ["My hour for tea is half-past five, and my buttered toast waits for nobody.", "이 정보는 당신이 제게서 받아 마땅한 특별한 호의를 헤아려 전해 드리는 것입니다. 제가 차를 마시는 시간은 다섯 시 반이고, 제 버터 바른 토스트는 누구도 기다려 주지 않습니다."]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["My first impulse, after reading Mrs. Catherick’s extraordinary narrative, was to destroy it.", "캐서릭 부인의 그 기이한 수기를 다 읽고 난 뒤 내게 처음 일어난 충동은 그것을 없애 버리자는 것이었다."], ["The hardened shameless depravity of the whole composition, from beginning to end—the atrocious perversity of mind which persistently associated me with a calamity for which I was in no sense answerable, and with a death which I had risked my life in trying to avert—so disgusted me, that I was on the point of tearing the letter, when a consideration suggested itself which warned me to wait a little before I destroyed it.", "처음부터 끝까지 그 글 전체에 굳어 붙어 있는 뻔뻔한 타락, 내게는 아무 책임도 없는 재난에, 그리고 막아 보려고 내가 목숨까지 걸었던 죽음에 나를 끝끝내 결부시키는 그 악랄하게 뒤틀린 심사가 어찌나 역겨웠던지, 나는 그 편지를 찢어 버리기 직전까지 갔다. 그때 한 가지 생각이 떠올라, 없애기 전에 조금만 기다리라고 나를 일깨웠다."]], [["This consideration was entirely unconnected with Sir Percival.", "이 생각은 퍼시벌 경과는 전혀 상관이 없었다."], ["The information communicated to me, so far as it concerned him, did little more than confirm the conclusions at which I had already arrived.", "그에 관한 한, 내게 전해진 정보는 이미 내가 도달해 있던 결론을 확인해 준 것 이상은 되지 못했다."]], [["He had committed his offence, as I had supposed him to have committed it, and the absence of all reference, on Mrs.", "그는 내가 짐작한 그대로 죄를 저질렀다."], ["Catherick’s part, to the duplicate register at Knowlesbury, strengthened my previous conviction that the existence of the book, and the risk of detection which it implied, must have been necessarily unknown to Sir Percival.", "그리고 캐서릭 부인이 놀즈베리에 있는 사본 등록부에 대해 한마디도 비치지 않았다는 사실은, 그 책의 존재와 그것이 안고 있는 발각의 위험을 퍼시벌 경이 끝내 몰랐으리라는 내 이전의 확신을 한층 굳혀 주었다."], ["My interest in the question of the forgery was now at an end, and my only object in keeping the letter was to make it of some future service in clearing up the last mystery that still remained to baffle me—the parentage of Anne Catherick on the father’s side.", "위조 문제에 대한 내 관심은 이제 끝났고, 그 편지를 간직해 두는 유일한 목적은 아직도 나를 가로막고 선 마지막 수수께끼, 곧 아버지 쪽으로 본 앤 캐서릭의 혈통을 밝히는 데 훗날 얼마간 쓸모가 있게 하려는 것이었다."], ["There were one or two sentences dropped in her mother’s narrative, which it might be useful to refer to again, when matters of more immediate importance allowed me leisure to search for the missing evidence.", "그 어머니의 수기에는 한두 문장 흘리듯 떨어뜨려 놓은 대목이 있었는데, 더 급한 일들이 정리되어 사라진 증거를 찾아 나설 여유가 생겼을 때 다시 들춰 보면 요긴할지도 몰랐다. 나는 그 증거를 언젠가 찾아내리라는 희망을 버리지 않았고, 그것을 밝혀내려는 조바심 또한 조금도 잃지 않았다."], ["I did not despair of still finding that evidence, and I had lost none of my anxiety to discover it, for I had lost none of my interest in tracing the father of the poor creature who now lay at rest in Mrs. Fairlie’s grave.", "이제 페얼리 부인의 무덤에 잠들어 있는 그 가엾은 사람의 아버지를 좇는 일에 대한 내 관심이 조금도 식지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Accordingly, I sealed up the letter and put it away carefully in my pocket-book, to be referred to again when the time came.", "그리하여 나는 그 편지를 봉하여 수첩 속에 조심스레 간수해 두고, 때가 오면 다시 꺼내 보기로 했다."]], [["The next day was my last in Hampshire.", "다음 날은 내가 햄프셔에서 보내는 마지막 날이었다."], ["When I had appeared again before the magistrate at Knowlesbury, and when I had attended at the adjourned inquest, I should be free to return to London by the afternoon or the evening train.", "놀즈베리에서 치안 판사 앞에 다시 출두하고 연기되었던 검시 심문에도 참석하고 나면, 그다음에는 오후나 저녁 기차로 자유로이 런던에 돌아갈 수 있을 터였다."]], [["My first errand in the morning was, as usual, to the post-office.", "그날 아침 내가 맨 먼저 볼일을 본 곳은 여느 때처럼 우체국이었다."], ["The letter from Marian was there, but I thought when it was handed to me that it felt unusually light.", "매리언의 편지가 와 있었으나, 손에 건네받는 순간 나는 그것이 유난히 가볍다고 느꼈다."], ["I anxiously opened the envelope.", "나는 조바심을 내며 봉투를 뜯었다."], ["There was nothing inside but a small strip of paper folded in two.", "안에는 두 번 접은 작은 종잇조각 하나밖에 없었다."], ["The few blotted hurriedly-written lines which were traced on it contained these words:", "거기에 급히 갈겨써서 얼룩진 몇 줄에는 이런 말이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Come back as soon as you can.", "“할 수 있는 대로 빨리 돌아와요."], ["I have been obliged to move.", "어쩔 수 없이 거처를 옮겨야 했어요."], ["Come to Gower’s Walk, Fulham (number five).", "풀럼의 가워스 워크 오 번지로 와요."], ["I will be on the look-out for you.", "내가 나와서 당신을 지켜보고 있을게요."], ["Don’t be alarmed about us, we are both safe and well.", "우리 걱정은 하지 말아요, 우리 둘 다 무사하고 잘 있어요."], ["But come back.—Marian.”", "그래도 어서 돌아와요.—매리언.”"]], [["The news which those lines contained—news which I instantly associated with some attempted treachery on the part of Count Fosco—fairly overwhelmed me.", "그 몇 줄이 담고 있는 소식은, 나는 그것을 대번에 포스코 백작 쪽의 무슨 배신 기도와 결부시켰거니와, 나를 완전히 압도해 버렸다."], ["I stood breathless with the paper crumpled up in my hand.", "나는 그 종이를 손안에 구겨 쥔 채 숨도 못 쉬고 서 있었다."], ["What had happened?", "무슨 일이 벌어진 것인가?"], ["What subtle wickedness had the Count planned and executed in my absence?", "내가 없는 사이에 백작은 어떤 교묘한 악행을 꾸미고 실행에 옮겼단 말인가? 매리언이 그 쪽지를 쓴 뒤로 하룻밤이 지나 있었다."], ["A night had passed since Marian’s note was written—hours must elapse still before I could get back to them—some new disaster might have happened already of which I was ignorant.", "내가 그들에게 돌아가기까지는 아직도 몇 시간이 더 흘러야 했다. 내가 모르는 사이에 벌써 또 다른 재앙이 닥쳤을지도 모를 일이었다."], ["And here, miles and miles away from them, here I must remain—held, doubly held, at the disposal of the law!", "그런데 나는 여기, 그들에게서 몇 마일이고 떨어진 이곳에 머물러 있어야 했다. 법의 처분에 묶인 채로, 그것도 이중으로 묶인 채로!"]], [["I hardly know to what forgetfulness of my obligations anxiety and alarm might not have tempted me, but for the quieting influence of my faith in Marian.", "불안과 경악이 나를 어떤 의무 망각으로까지 꾀어냈을지, 매리언에 대한 내 믿음이 마음을 가라앉혀 주지 않았더라면 나로서도 알 수 없는 일이다."], ["My absolute reliance on her was the one earthly consideration which helped me to restrain myself, and gave me courage to wait.", "그녀에 대한 절대적인 신뢰야말로 나를 자제하게 하고 기다릴 용기를 준 이 세상의 유일한 버팀목이었다."], ["The inquest was the first of the impediments in the way of my freedom of action.", "검시 심문은 내 행동의 자유를 가로막는 첫 번째 걸림돌이었다."], ["I attended it at the appointed time, the legal formalities requiring my presence in the room, but as it turned out, not calling on me to repeat my evidence.", "나는 정해진 시각에 출석했다. 법의 절차가 내가 그 방에 있을 것을 요구했기 때문인데, 막상 열어 보니 내 증언을 되풀이하라고 나를 부르지는 않았다."], ["This useless delay was a hard trial, although I did my best to quiet my impatience by following the course of the proceedings as closely as I could.", "이 쓸데없는 지체는 견디기 힘든 시련이었으나, 나는 심리의 진행을 될 수 있는 대로 꼼꼼히 좇으며 초조함을 달래려고 애썼다."]], [["The London solicitor of the deceased (Mr. Merriman) was among the persons present.", "고인의 런던 사무 변호사(메리먼 씨)도 참석한 사람들 가운데 있었다."], ["But he was quite unable to assist the objects of the inquiry.", "그러나 그는 이 조사의 목적에 도움이 될 만한 것을 조금도 내놓지 못했다."], ["He could only say that he was inexpressibly shocked and astonished, and that he could throw no light whatever on the mysterious circumstances of the case.", "그는 다만 이루 말할 수 없이 충격을 받고 놀랐다는 것, 그리고 이 사건의 불가사의한 정황에 대해서는 아무런 빛도 던져 줄 수 없다는 것만을 말할 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["At intervals during the adjourned investigation, he suggested questions which the Coroner put, but which led to no results.", "연기되었던 심리가 진행되는 동안 그는 이따금 검시관이 대신 물어 줄 질문을 내놓았으나, 어느 것도 아무런 결과로 이어지지 않았다."], ["After a patient inquiry, which lasted nearly three hours, and which exhausted every available source of information, the jury pronounced the customary verdict in cases of sudden death by accident.", "거의 세 시간 가까이 이어지며 얻을 수 있는 정보의 원천을 남김없이 다 써 버린 끈기 있는 조사 끝에, 배심은 사고로 인한 급사 사건에 으레 내려지는 평결을 선고했다."], ["They added to the formal decision a statement, that there had been no evidence to show how the keys had been abstracted, how the fire had been caused, or what the purpose was for which the deceased had entered the vestry.", "그들은 그 형식적인 결정에 한 가지 진술을 덧붙였다. 열쇠가 어떻게 빼돌려졌는지, 불이 어떻게 났는지, 고인이 무슨 목적으로 성구실에 들어갔는지를 밝혀 줄 증거는 하나도 없었다는 것이었다."], ["This act closed the proceedings.", "이로써 절차는 끝났다."], ["The legal representative of the dead man was left to provide for the necessities of the interment, and the witnesses were free to retire.", "죽은 사람의 법정 대리인에게는 매장에 필요한 일들을 처리할 몫이 남겨졌고, 증인들은 물러가도 좋았다."]], [["Resolved not to lose a minute in getting to Knowlesbury, I paid my bill at the hotel, and hired a fly to take me to the town.", "놀즈베리로 가는 데 단 일 분도 허비하지 않기로 마음먹은 나는 여관에서 셈을 치르고 삯마차를 한 대 세내어 그 읍으로 향했다."], ["A gentleman who heard me give the order, and who saw that I was going alone, informed me that he lived in the neighbourhood of Knowlesbury, and asked if I would have any objection to his getting home by sharing the fly with me.", "내가 마차를 부르는 소리를 듣고 또 내가 혼자 가는 것을 본 어떤 신사가, 자기는 놀즈베리 근처에 사는 사람인데 마차를 같이 타고 집으로 돌아가도 괜찮겠느냐고 물었다."], ["I accepted his proposal as a matter of course.", "나는 두말없이 그 청을 받아들였다."]], [["Our conversation during the drive was naturally occupied by the one absorbing subject of local interest.", "마차를 타고 가는 동안 우리의 이야기는 자연히 그 고장의 관심을 온통 사로잡고 있는 그 한 가지 화제로 채워졌다."]], [["My new acquaintance had some knowledge of the late Sir Percival’s solicitor, and he and Mr. Merriman had been discussing the state of the deceased gentleman’s affairs and the succession to the property.", "새로 알게 된 그 사람은 고인이 된 퍼시벌 경의 사무 변호사와 어느 정도 아는 사이여서, 그와 메리먼 씨는 고인의 재정 형편과 재산 상속 문제를 이야기하던 참이었다."], ["Sir Percival’s embarrassments were so well known all over the county that his solicitor could only make a virtue of necessity and plainly acknowledge them.", "퍼시벌 경이 진 빚은 주 안에 워낙 널리 알려져 있어서, 그의 사무 변호사로서도 어쩔 수 없는 노릇을 차라리 미덕으로 삼아 그것을 솔직히 인정하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["He had died without leaving a will, and he had no personal property to bequeath, even if he had made one, the whole fortune which he had derived from his wife having been swallowed up by his creditors.", "그는 유언장을 남기지 않고 죽었고, 설령 유언장을 만들어 두었다 한들 물려줄 개인 재산조차 없었다. 아내에게서 얻은 재산은 통째로 채권자들에게 삼켜져 버렸기 때문이다."], ["The heir to the estate (Sir Percival having left no issue) was a son of Sir Felix Glyde’s first cousin, an officer in command of an East Indiaman.", "퍼시벌 경에게 자식이 없었으므로 그 영지의 상속인은 펠릭스 글라이드 경의 사촌의 아들, 곧 동인도 무역선의 지휘를 맡고 있는 사관이었다."], ["He would find his unexpected inheritance sadly encumbered, but the property would recover with time, and, if “the captain” was careful, he might be a rich man yet before he died.", "그는 뜻하지 않게 굴러든 그 유산이 딱하게도 빚에 잔뜩 짓눌려 있음을 알게 되겠지만, 세월이 지나면 재산은 되살아날 터이고, “그 선장”이 조심스럽게만 처신한다면 죽기 전에 부자가 될 수도 있으리라는 것이었다."]], [["Absorbed as I was in the one idea of getting to London, this information (which events proved to be perfectly correct) had an interest of its own to attract my attention.", "런던으로 가야 한다는 그 한 가지 생각에 온통 사로잡혀 있었으면서도, 이 이야기는 그 나름의 흥미로 내 주의를 끌었다. 뒷날의 일들은 이것이 조금도 틀림없는 사실임을 증명해 주었다."], ["I thought it justified me in keeping secret my discovery of Sir Percival’s fraud.", "나는 이 이야기 덕분에 퍼시벌 경의 사기를 밝혀낸 사실을 비밀에 부쳐도 좋다는 명분이 선다고 생각했다."], ["The heir, whose rights he had usurped, was the heir who would now have the estate.", "그가 권리를 가로챘던 그 상속인이 바로 이제 영지를 차지하게 될 상속인이었다."], ["The income from it, for the last three-and-twenty years, which should properly have been his, and which the dead man had squandered to the last farthing, was gone beyond recall.", "지난 스물세 해 동안 마땅히 그의 것이었어야 했고 죽은 사람이 마지막 한 푼까지 탕진해 버린 그 수입은 이제 되찾을 길이 없었다."], ["If I spoke, my speaking would confer advantage on no one.", "내가 입을 연다 한들 그 말은 누구에게도 이득을 주지 못할 것이었다."], ["If I kept the secret, my silence concealed the character of the man who had cheated Laura into marrying him.", "내가 비밀을 지키면, 내 침묵은 로라를 속여 자기와 결혼하게 만든 사내의 정체를 덮어 주게 된다."], ["For her sake, I wished to conceal it—for her sake, still, I tell this story under feigned names.", "로라를 위하여 나는 그것을 덮어 두고 싶었다. 지금도 로라를 위하여 나는 이 이야기를 지어낸 이름들로 적고 있다."]], [["I parted with my chance companion at Knowlesbury, and went at once to the town-hall.", "나는 놀즈베리에서 우연히 만난 동행과 헤어져 곧바로 읍사무소로 갔다."], ["As I had anticipated, no one was present to prosecute the case against me—the necessary formalities were observed, and I was discharged.", "예상했던 대로 나를 걸어 소송을 이어 갈 사람은 아무도 나와 있지 않았다. 필요한 절차가 지켜졌고, 나는 방면되었다."], ["On leaving the court a letter from Mr. Dawson was put into my hand.", "법정을 나서는데 도슨 의사가 보낸 편지 한 통이 내 손에 쥐어졌다."], ["It informed me that he was absent on professional duty, and it reiterated the offer I had already received from him of any assistance which I might require at his hands.", "그가 왕진 때문에 자리를 비웠다는 것과, 내게 필요한 도움이 있다면 무엇이든 주겠다고 이미 해 두었던 제안을 다시 한번 되풀이한다는 내용이었다."], ["I wrote back, warmly acknowledging my obligations to his kindness, and apologising for not expressing my thanks personally, in consequence of my immediate recall on pressing business to town.", "나는 그의 호의에 큰 신세를 졌음을 따뜻이 인정하고, 급한 볼일로 곧장 런던으로 불려 가게 되어 직접 찾아뵙고 감사를 표하지 못하는 것을 사과하는 답장을 써 보냈다."]], [["Half an hour later I was speeding back to London by the express train.", "삼십 분 뒤 나는 급행열차를 타고 런던으로 되돌아가고 있었다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["It was between nine and ten o’clock before I reached Fulham, and found my way to Gower’s Walk.", "풀럼에 이르러 가워스 워크로 가는 길을 찾아냈을 때는 아홉 시와 열 시 사이였다."]], [["Both Laura and Marian came to the door to let me in.", "로라와 매리언이 함께 문간까지 나와 나를 맞아들였다."], ["I think we had hardly known how close the tie was which bound us three together, until the evening came which united us again.", "우리 셋을 묶고 있는 유대가 얼마나 깊은지, 우리는 다시 하나로 모인 그 저녁이 오기 전까지는 미처 알지 못했던 것 같다."], ["We met as if we had been parted for months instead of for a few days only.", "우리는 며칠이 아니라 몇 달을 떨어져 있었던 사람들처럼 만났다."], ["Marian’s face was sadly worn and anxious.", "매리언의 얼굴은 애처로울 만큼 수척하고 근심에 잠겨 있었다."], ["I saw who had known all the danger and borne all the trouble in my absence the moment I looked at her.", "내가 없는 동안 모든 위험을 혼자 알고 모든 고생을 혼자 짊어진 사람이 누구였는지, 나는 그녀를 보는 순간 알아차렸다."], ["Laura’s brighter looks and better spirits told me how carefully she had been spared all knowledge of the dreadful death at Welmingham, and of the true reason of our change of abode.", "로라의 밝아진 낯빛과 나아진 기분은, 웰밍엄에서 벌어진 그 끔찍한 죽음과 우리가 거처를 옮긴 진짜 이유를 그녀가 얼마나 세심하게 모른 채 지낼 수 있었는지를 말해 주었다."]], [["The stir of the removal seemed to have cheered and interested her.", "이사의 부산함이 오히려 그녀의 기운을 북돋우고 흥미를 돋운 모양이었다."], ["She only spoke of it as a happy thought of Marian’s to surprise me on my return with a change from the close, noisy street to the pleasant neighbourhood of trees and fields and the river.", "로라는 그 일을 두고, 답답하고 시끄러운 거리에서 나무와 들판과 강이 있는 쾌적한 동네로 옮겨 와 돌아오는 나를 놀래 주려 한 매리언의 멋진 착상이라고만 말했다."], ["She was full of projects for the future—of the drawings she was to finish—of the purchasers I had found in the country who were to buy them—of the shillings and sixpences she had saved, till her purse was so heavy that she proudly asked me to weigh it in my own hand.", "그녀는 앞날의 계획으로 가득 차 있었다—마저 끝내야 할 그림 이야기, 그 그림을 사 주기로 한 시골의 구매자들 이야기, 자기가 모아 둔 실링과 육펜스 동전 이야기까지. 지갑이 어찌나 묵직해졌던지 그녀는 자랑스럽게 내 손으로 무게를 가늠해 보라고 내밀었다."], ["The change for the better which had been wrought in her during the few days of my absence was a surprise to me for which I was quite unprepared—and for all the unspeakable happiness of seeing it, I was indebted to Marian’s courage and to Marian’s love.", "내가 없던 며칠 사이에 그녀에게 일어난 이 좋은 변화는 전혀 예상하지 못한 놀라움이었다. 그리고 그것을 보는 형언할 수 없는 행복은 모두 매리언의 용기와 매리언의 사랑 덕분이었다."]], [["When Laura had left us, and when we could speak to one another without restraint, I tried to give some expression to the gratitude and the admiration which filled my heart.", "로라가 자리를 뜨고 우리 둘이 거리낌 없이 이야기할 수 있게 되었을 때, 나는 가슴에 가득 찬 감사와 경탄을 어떻게든 말로 옮겨 보려 했다."], ["But the generous creature would not wait to hear me.", "그러나 그 너그러운 사람은 내 말을 끝까지 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["That sublime self-forgetfulness of women, which yields so much and asks so little, turned all her thoughts from herself to me.", "그토록 많이 내어 주고 그토록 적게 바라는 여자의 그 숭고한 자기망각이, 그녀의 모든 생각을 자기 자신에게서 나에게로 돌려놓았다."]], [["“I had only a moment left before post-time,”", "“우편 시간까지 잠깐밖에 여유가 없었어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “or I should have written less abruptly.", "“그러지 않았다면 그렇게 불쑥 쓰지는 않았을 거예요."], ["You look worn and weary, Walter.", "지치고 고단해 보이는군요, 월터."], ["I am afraid my letter must have seriously alarmed you?”", "내 편지가 당신을 몹시 놀라게 한 건 아닌지 걱정이에요.”"]], [["“Only at first,”", "“처음에만 그랬어요.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“My mind was quieted, Marian, by my trust in you.", "“당신을 믿는 마음으로 이내 진정이 되었지요, 매리언."], ["Was I right in attributing this sudden change of place to some threatened annoyance on the part of Count Fosco?”", "이 갑작스러운 거처 이동을 포스코 백작 쪽에서 무슨 위협이 있었던 탓으로 짐작했는데, 맞나요?”"]], [["“Perfectly right,”", "“정확해요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I saw him yesterday, and worse than that, Walter—I spoke to him.”", "“어제 그 사람을 봤어요. 그보다 더한 일도 있지요, 월터—내가 그 사람과 말을 했어요.”"]], [["“Spoke to him?", "“말을 했다고요?"], ["Did he know where we lived?", "그자가 우리 사는 곳을 알던가요?"], ["Did he come to the house?”", "집까지 왔던가요?”"]], [["“He did.", "“왔어요."], ["To the house—but not upstairs.", "집까지—하지만 위층으로는 오지 않았어요."], ["Laura never saw him—Laura suspects nothing.", "로라는 그 사람을 보지 못했어요—로라는 아무것도 눈치채지 못했고요."], ["I will tell you how it happened: the danger, I believe and hope, is over now.", "어떻게 된 일인지 말해 줄게요. 위험은, 나는 그렇게 믿고 또 그러기를 바라는데, 이제 지나갔어요."], ["Yesterday, I was in the sitting-room, at our old lodgings.", "어제 나는 예전 하숙집 거실에 있었어요."], ["Laura was drawing at the table, and I was walking about and setting things to rights.", "로라는 탁자에서 그림을 그리고 있었고, 나는 이리저리 걸어 다니며 물건을 정리하고 있었지요."], ["I passed the window, and as I passed it, looked out into the street.", "창가를 지나다가 무심코 거리를 내다보았어요."], ["There, on the opposite side of the way, I saw the Count, with a man talking to him——”", "그런데 길 건너편에 백작이 서 있고, 웬 남자가 그와 이야기를 나누고 있지 뭐예요—”"]], [["“Did he notice you at the window?”", "“그자가 창가의 당신을 알아채던가요?”"]], [["“No—at least, I thought not.", "“아니요—적어도 못 봤다고 생각해요."], ["I was too violently startled to be quite sure.”", "너무 심하게 놀라서 확실하다고는 못 하겠지만요.”"]], [["“Who was the other man?", "“다른 남자는 누구였어요?"], ["A stranger?”", "낯선 사람이던가요?”"]], [["“Not a stranger, Walter.", "“낯선 사람이 아니었어요, 월터."], ["As soon as I could draw my breath again, I recognised him.", "겨우 숨을 돌리고 나서 나는 그 사람을 알아보았어요."], ["He was the owner of the Lunatic Asylum.”", "그 정신병원의 원장이었어요.”"]], [["“Was the Count pointing out the house to him?”", "“백작이 그 사람에게 집을 가리켜 보이던가요?”"]], [["“No, they were talking together as if they had accidentally met in the street.", "“아니요, 두 사람은 거리에서 우연히 마주친 것처럼 이야기를 나누고 있었어요."], ["I remained at the window looking at them from behind the curtain.", "나는 커튼 뒤에서 그들을 지켜보며 창가에 그대로 서 있었어요."], ["If I had turned round, and if Laura had seen my face at that moment——Thank God, she was absorbed over her drawing!", "만약 내가 돌아섰더라면, 그리고 로라가 그 순간 내 얼굴을 보았더라면—하느님 감사하게도 로라는 그림에 푹 빠져 있었어요!"], ["They soon parted.", "두 사람은 곧 헤어졌어요."], ["The man from the Asylum went one way, and the Count the other.", "병원에서 온 남자는 이쪽으로, 백작은 저쪽으로 갔지요."], ["I began to hope they were in the street by chance, till I saw the Count come back, stop opposite to us again, take out his card-case and pencil, write something, and then cross the road to the shop below us.", "나는 두 사람이 우연히 그 거리에 있었을 뿐이라고 생각하기 시작했는데, 그때 백작이 되돌아와 다시 우리 집 맞은편에 멈춰 서더니 명함집과 연필을 꺼내 무언가를 적고는 길을 건너 우리 아래층 가게로 들어가는 것이 보였어요."], ["I ran past Laura before she could see me, and said I had forgotten something upstairs.", "나는 로라가 보기 전에 그 앞을 지나 달려 나가며 위층에 뭘 두고 왔다고 말했어요."], ["As soon as I was out of the room I went down to the first landing and waited—I was determined to stop him if he tried to come upstairs.", "방을 나서자마자 나는 첫 번째 층계참까지 내려가 기다렸어요—그 사람이 올라오려 하면 기어이 막을 작정이었지요."], ["He made no such attempt.", "그는 그러려 하지 않았어요."], ["The girl from the shop came through the door into the passage, with his card in her hand—a large gilt card with his name, and a coronet above it, and these lines underneath in pencil: ‘Dear lady’ (yes!", "가게 아가씨가 그의 명함을 들고 문을 통해 복도로 나왔어요—이름 위에 관(冠) 문장이 박힌 커다란 금박 명함이었고, 그 아래에 연필로 이런 글이 적혀 있었어요. ‘친애하는 부인’(그래요!"], ["the villain could address me in that way still)—‘dear lady, one word, I implore you, on a matter serious to us both.’ If one can think at all, in serious difficulties, one thinks quick.", "그 악당은 아직도 나를 그렇게 부를 수 있더군요)—‘친애하는 부인, 우리 두 사람 모두에게 중대한 일로 한마디만 청합니다.’ 위급한 처지에서도 생각이란 걸 할 수 있다면, 그때는 생각이 빨라지는 법이에요."], ["I felt directly that it might be a fatal mistake to leave myself and to leave you in the dark, where such a man as the Count was concerned.", "나는 백작 같은 사람이 얽힌 일에서 나 자신과 당신을 어둠 속에 내버려 두는 것이 치명적인 실수가 될 수 있다는 걸 대번에 느꼈어요."], ["I felt that the doubt of what he might do, in your absence, would be ten times more trying to me if I declined to see him than if I consented.", "그를 만나기를 거절한다면, 당신이 없는 사이 그가 무슨 짓을 할지 모른다는 의심이 만나는 쪽보다 열 배는 더 나를 괴롭히리라는 것도 느꼈고요."], ["‘Ask the gentleman to wait in the shop,’ I said.", "‘그분께 가게에서 기다려 달라고 전해 주세요.’ 내가 말했어요."], ["‘I will be with him in a moment.’ I ran upstairs for my bonnet, being determined not to let him speak to me indoors.", "‘곧 내려가겠다고요.’ 나는 보닛을 가지러 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔어요. 집 안에서는 절대 그와 말을 섞지 않을 작정이었으니까요."], ["I knew his deep ringing voice, and I was afraid Laura might hear it, even in the shop.", "나는 그의 깊고 울리는 목소리를 알고 있었고, 가게에서라도 로라가 그 소리를 들을까 겁이 났어요."], ["In less than a minute I was down again in the passage, and had opened the door into the street.", "일 분도 안 되어 나는 다시 복도로 내려와 거리로 나가는 문을 열었어요."], ["He came round to meet me from the shop.", "그가 가게에서 돌아 나와 나를 맞았어요."], ["There he was in deep mourning, with his smooth bow and his deadly smile, and some idle boys and women near him, staring at his great size, his fine black clothes, and his large cane with the gold knob to it.", "짙은 상복 차림에, 그 매끄러운 목례와 그 소름 끼치는 미소를 띠고 서 있었지요. 그 옆에는 한가한 아이들과 여자들이 몇 모여 그의 거대한 몸집과 훌륭한 검은 옷과 금 손잡이가 달린 큼직한 지팡이를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었어요."], ["All the horrible time at Blackwater came back to me the moment I set eyes on him.", "그를 보는 순간 블랙워터에서의 그 끔찍한 시간이 모두 되살아났어요."], ["All the old loathing crept and crawled through me, when he took off his hat with a flourish and spoke to me, as if we had parted on the friendliest terms hardly a day since.”", "그가 과장된 몸짓으로 모자를 벗고, 마치 우리가 바로 어제까지 더없이 다정한 사이로 헤어지기라도 한 듯 내게 말을 걸었을 때, 예전의 그 혐오감이 온몸을 기어 다녔어요.”"]], [["“You remember what he said?”", "“그가 뭐라고 했는지 기억나요?”"]], [["“I can’t repeat it, Walter.", "“그 말을 옮길 수는 없어요, 월터."], ["You shall know directly what he said about you—-but I can’t repeat what he said to me.", "당신에 대해 뭐라고 했는지는 곧 알려 드리겠지만—내게 한 말은 옮길 수가 없어요."], ["It was worse than the polite insolence of his letter.", "그 편지에 담겼던 정중한 무례보다 더 지독했어요."], ["My hands tingled to strike him, as if I had been a man!", "내가 남자였다면 그를 후려치고 싶어 손이 근질거릴 지경이었어요!"], ["I only kept them quiet by tearing his card to pieces under my shawl.", "숄 밑에서 그의 명함을 갈기갈기 찢는 것으로 겨우 손을 가만두었지요."], ["Without saying a word on my side, I walked away from the house (for fear of Laura seeing us), and he followed, protesting softly all the way.", "나는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 (로라가 우리를 볼까 봐) 집에서 멀어지는 쪽으로 걸었고, 그는 내내 부드럽게 항변하며 따라왔어요."], ["In the first by-street I turned, and asked him what he wanted with me.", "첫 골목에 들어서서 나는 돌아서서 내게 무슨 볼일이냐고 물었어요."], ["He wanted two things.", "그가 원하는 것은 두 가지였어요."], ["First, if I had no objection, to express his sentiments.", "첫째는, 내가 싫다고 하지 않는다면 자기 심정을 털어놓는 것이었어요."], ["I declined to hear them.", "나는 듣기를 거절했지요."], ["Secondly, to repeat the warning in his letter.", "둘째는 편지에 쓴 경고를 되풀이하는 것이었어요."], ["I asked, what occasion there was for repeating it.", "나는 그걸 되풀이할 무슨 까닭이 있느냐고 물었어요."], ["He bowed and smiled, and said he would explain.", "그는 절하고 미소 지으며 설명하겠다고 했어요."], ["The explanation exactly confirmed the fears I expressed before you left us.", "그 설명은 당신이 우리 곁을 떠나기 전에 내가 말했던 우려를 그대로 확인해 주었어요."], ["I told you, if you remember, that Sir Percival would be too headstrong to take his friend’s advice where you were concerned, and that there was no danger to be dreaded from the Count till his own interests were threatened, and he was roused into acting for himself?”", "기억하겠지만, 나는 당신 문제에 관해서라면 퍼시벌 경이 너무 고집이 세서 친구의 충고를 듣지 않을 것이고, 백작 쪽에서는 자기 이해가 위협받아 스스로 나서게 되기 전까지는 두려워할 위험이 없다고 말했잖아요?”"]], [["“I recollect, Marian.”", "“기억해요, 매리언.”"]], [["“Well, so it has really turned out.", "“그런데 정말로 그렇게 되었어요."], ["The Count offered his advice, but it was refused.", "백작은 충고를 했지만 거절당했지요."], ["Sir Percival would only take counsel of his own violence, his own obstinacy, and his own hatred of you.", "퍼시벌 경은 자기 자신의 난폭함, 자기 자신의 고집, 그리고 당신에 대한 자기 자신의 증오만을 따랐어요."], ["The Count let him have his way, first privately ascertaining, in case of his own interests being threatened next, where we lived.", "백작은 그가 하는 대로 내버려 두었어요. 다만 다음 차례로 자기 이해가 위협받을 경우에 대비해 우리가 어디 사는지를 남몰래 알아 두었지요."], ["You were followed, Walter, on returning here, after your first journey to Hampshire, by the lawyer’s men for some distance from the railway, and by the Count himself to the door of the house.", "월터, 당신이 햄프셔로 처음 다녀온 뒤 이곳으로 돌아올 때 당신은 미행당했어요. 역에서부터 한동안은 변호사가 붙인 사내들이, 그다음에는 집 문간까지 백작이 직접 따라붙었지요."], ["How he contrived to escape being seen by you he did not tell me, but he found us out on that occasion, and in that way.", "어떻게 당신 눈에 띄지 않았는지는 말해 주지 않았지만, 아무튼 그는 그때 그런 식으로 우리를 찾아냈어요."], ["Having made the discovery, he took no advantage of it till the news reached him of Sir Percival’s death, and then, as I told you, he acted for himself, because he believed you would next proceed against the dead man’s partner in the conspiracy.", "알아내고도 그는 퍼시벌 경의 사망 소식이 들려오기 전까지는 그것을 이용하지 않았어요. 그러다가, 아까 말했듯이, 당신이 다음 차례로 그 죽은 자의 음모 동료를 치리라고 믿었기에 스스로 나선 거예요."], ["He at once made his arrangements to meet the owner of the Asylum in London, and to take him to the place where his runaway patient was hidden, believing that the results, whichever way they ended, would be to involve you in interminable legal disputes and difficulties, and to tie your hands for all purposes of offence, so far as he was concerned.", "그는 즉시 런던에서 그 정신병원 원장을 만나 달아난 환자가 숨어 있는 곳으로 데려갈 준비를 했어요. 그 결과가 어느 쪽으로 끝나든 당신을 끝없는 법적 분쟁과 곤경에 얽어매어, 적어도 자기에 관한 한 당신이 공격에 나설 손발을 묶어 놓게 되리라 믿었으니까요."], ["That was his purpose, on his own confession to me.", "그것이 그의 목적이었어요. 내게 스스로 실토한 바로는요."], ["The only consideration which made him hesitate, at the last moment——”", "마지막 순간에 그를 망설이게 한 단 하나의 고려는—”"]], [["“Yes?”", "“뭐였어요?”"]], [["“It is hard to acknowledge it, Walter, and yet I must.", "“인정하기 괴롭지만, 월터, 그래도 말해야겠지요."], ["I was the only consideration.", "그 단 하나의 고려가 나였어요."], ["No words can say how degraded I feel in my own estimation when I think of it, but the one weak point in that man’s iron character is the horrible admiration he feels for me.", "그 생각을 하면 내가 스스로 얼마나 천하게 여겨지는지 말로 다 할 수 없어요. 하지만 그 쇠 같은 성격에 난 단 하나의 약점이 나에 대한 그 끔찍한 흠모예요."], ["I have tried, for the sake of my own self-respect, to disbelieve it as long as I could; but his looks, his actions, force on me the shameful conviction of the truth.", "나는 내 자존심을 위해서라도 될 수 있는 한 오래 그것을 믿지 않으려 애썼어요. 그러나 그의 눈빛과 행동이 그 부끄러운 사실을 기어이 내게 확신시켜요."], ["The eyes of that monster of wickedness moistened while he was speaking to me—they did, Walter!", "나와 말하는 동안 그 사악한 괴물의 두 눈이 젖어들었어요—정말이에요, 월터!"], ["He declared that at the moment of pointing out the house to the doctor, he thought of my misery if I was separated from Laura, of my responsibility if I was called on to answer for effecting her escape, and he risked the worst that you could do to him, the second time, for my sake.", "그는 의사에게 그 집을 가리켜 보이려던 순간, 내가 로라와 떨어지면 얼마나 비참할지, 그녀를 도망시킨 일에 대해 책임을 추궁당하면 어찌 될지를 생각했노라고, 그래서 나를 위해 두 번째로 당신이 자기에게 할 수 있는 최악의 일을 무릅쓴 것이라고 했어요."], ["All he asked was that I would remember the sacrifice, and restrain your rashness, in my own interests—interests which he might never be able to consult again.", "그가 청한 것이라고는 내가 그 희생을 기억해 주고, 나 자신의 이익을 위해—그가 다시는 돌보아 줄 수 없을지도 모를 그 이익을 위해—당신의 성급함을 눌러 달라는 것뿐이었어요."], ["I made no such bargain with him—I would have died first.", "나는 그런 거래를 하지 않았어요—차라리 죽었을 거예요."], ["But believe him or not, whether it is true or false that he sent the doctor away with an excuse, one thing is certain, I saw the man leave him without so much as a glance at our window, or even at our side of the way.”", "하지만 그를 믿든 안 믿든, 그가 핑계를 대어 의사를 돌려보냈다는 게 사실이든 거짓이든, 한 가지는 분명해요. 나는 그 남자가 우리 창문 쪽은커녕 우리 쪽 길조차 한 번 쳐다보지 않고 그와 헤어지는 것을 보았어요.”"]], [["“I believe it, Marian.", "“나도 믿어요, 매리언."], ["The best men are not consistent in good—why should the worst men be consistent in evil?", "가장 선한 사람도 선함에 한결같지 못한데, 가장 악한 사람이라고 악함에 한결같으란 법이 있겠어요?"], ["At the same time, I suspect him of merely attempting to frighten you, by threatening what he cannot really do.", "그와 동시에 나는 그가 실제로는 할 수 없는 일을 위협하며 당신을 겁주려 했을 뿐이라고 의심해요."], ["I doubt his power of annoying us, by means of the owner of the Asylum, now that Sir Percival is dead, and Mrs. Catherick is free from all control.", "퍼시벌 경이 죽고 캐서릭 부인도 모든 속박에서 풀려난 지금, 그가 그 정신병원 원장을 이용해 우리를 괴롭힐 힘이 있을지 의심스러워요."], ["But let me hear more.", "그건 그렇고, 더 들려줘요."], ["What did the Count say of me?”", "백작이 나에 대해서는 뭐라고 했나요?”"]], [["“He spoke last of you.", "“당신 이야기는 맨 나중에 했어요."], ["His eyes brightened and hardened, and his manner changed to what I remember it in past times—to that mixture of pitiless resolution and mountebank mockery which makes it so impossible to fathom him.", "그의 눈이 번쩍이며 굳어졌고, 태도는 내가 예전에 기억하던 그것으로—그를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없게 만드는 그 무자비한 결의와 광대 같은 조롱이 뒤섞인 태도로 바뀌었어요."], ["‘Warn Mr. Hartright!’ he said in his loftiest manner.", "‘하트라이트 씨에게 경고하시오!’ 그가 더없이 거만한 투로 말했어요."], ["‘He has a man of brains to deal with, a man who snaps his big fingers at the laws and conventions of society, when he measures himself with ME.", "‘그는 두뇌를 가진 사람을 상대하고 있소. 나와 겨루려 들 때 사회의 법과 관습 따위는 굵은 손가락 하나로 튕겨 버리는 사람 말이오."], ["If my lamented friend had taken my advice, the business of the inquest would have been with the body of Mr. Hartright.", "애도하는 내 벗이 내 충고를 들었더라면, 검시 심문이 다루어야 할 것은 하트라이트 씨의 시신이었을 것이오."], ["But my lamented friend was obstinate.", "그러나 애도하는 내 벗은 고집이 셌소. 보시오!"], ["See! I mourn his loss—inwardly in my soul, outwardly on my hat.", "나는 그의 죽음을 애도하고 있소—속으로는 내 영혼으로, 겉으로는 내 모자로."], ["This trivial crape expresses sensibilities which I summon Mr. Hartright to respect.", "이 하찮은 검은 띠가 나타내는 감정을 존중하라고 나는 하트라이트 씨에게 요구하는 바이오."], ["They may be transformed to immeasurable enmities if he ventures to disturb them.", "그가 감히 그것을 건드린다면 그 감정은 가늠할 수 없는 적의로 변할 것이오."], ["Let him be content with what he has got—with what I leave unmolested, for your sake, to him and to you.", "그가 얻은 것으로 만족하게 하시오—그대를 위하여 내가 손대지 않고 그와 그대에게 남겨 두는 것으로 말이오."], ["Say to him (with my compliments), if he stirs me, he has Fosco to deal with.", "그에게 (내 인사와 함께) 이렇게 전하시오. 나를 건드리면 그는 포스코를 상대하게 되리라고."], ["In the English of the Popular Tongue, I inform him—Fosco sticks at nothing.", "세간의 말투로 옮기자면, 이렇게 알려 두겠소—포스코는 못 할 짓이 없다고."], ["Dear lady, good morning.’ His cold grey eyes settled on my face—he took off his hat solemnly—bowed, bare-headed—and left me.”", "친애하는 부인, 안녕히 계시오.’ 그 차가운 잿빛 두 눈이 내 얼굴에 머물렀어요—그는 엄숙하게 모자를 벗고—맨머리로 절하고—떠났어요.”"]], [["“Without returning?", "“다시 돌아오지도 않고요?"], ["without saying more last words?”", "마지막 말을 더 하지도 않고요?”"]], [["“He turned at the corner of the street, and waved his hand, and then struck it theatrically on his breast.", "“거리 모퉁이에서 돌아서더니 손을 흔들고는, 그 손을 연극배우처럼 자기 가슴에 탁 얹었어요."], ["I lost sight of him after that.", "그 뒤로는 그를 보지 못했어요."], ["He disappeared in the opposite direction to our house, and I ran back to Laura.", "그는 우리 집과 반대 방향으로 사라졌고, 나는 로라에게 달려갔지요."], ["Before I was indoors again, I had made up my mind that we must go.", "집 안으로 들어서기도 전에 나는 우리가 떠나야 한다고 마음을 굳혔어요."], ["The house (especially in your absence) was a place of danger instead of a place of safety, now that the Count had discovered it.", "백작이 그곳을 알아낸 이상, (특히 당신이 없는 동안에는) 그 집은 안전한 곳이 아니라 위험한 곳이었으니까요."], ["If I could have felt certain of your return, I should have risked waiting till you came back.", "당신이 돌아올 날을 확실히 알 수 있었다면 돌아올 때까지 기다리는 위험을 감수했을 거예요."], ["But I was certain of nothing, and I acted at once on my own impulse.", "하지만 나는 아무것도 확신할 수 없었고, 그래서 곧바로 내 판단대로 움직였어요."], ["You had spoken, before leaving us, of moving into a quieter neighbourhood and purer air, for the sake of Laura’s health.", "당신은 떠나기 전에 로라의 건강을 위해 더 조용한 동네, 더 맑은 공기를 찾아 옮기자는 이야기를 한 적이 있지요."], ["I had only to remind her of that, and to suggest surprising you and saving you trouble by managing the move in your absence, to make her quite as anxious for the change as I was.", "나는 로라에게 그 말을 상기시키고, 당신이 없는 동안 이사를 해치워서 당신을 놀래 주고 수고를 덜어 주자고 제안하기만 하면 되었어요. 그러자 로라도 나만큼이나 그 변화를 바라게 되었지요."], ["She helped me to pack up your things, and she has arranged them all for you in your new working-room here.”", "로라가 당신 물건 꾸리는 걸 도왔고, 여기 당신의 새 작업실에 그것을 다 정리해 두었답니다.”"]], [["“What made you think of coming to this place?”", "“여기로 올 생각은 어떻게 했어요?”"]], [["“My ignorance of other localities in the neighbourhood of London.", "“런던 근교의 다른 동네를 몰라서요."], ["I felt the necessity of getting as far away as possible from our old lodgings, and I knew something of Fulham, because I had once been at school there.", "예전 하숙집에서 되도록 멀리 떨어져야 한다고 느꼈는데, 풀럼은 조금 알고 있었어요. 한때 이곳 학교에 다닌 적이 있거든요."], ["I despatched a messenger with a note, on the chance that the school might still be in existence.", "그 학교가 아직 남아 있을까 하는 요행을 걸고 쪽지를 들려 심부름꾼을 보냈어요."], ["It was in existence—the daughters of my old mistress were carrying it on for her, and they engaged this place from the instructions I had sent.", "학교는 남아 있었어요—옛 여선생님의 딸들이 어머니를 대신해 학교를 꾸려 가고 있었고, 내가 보낸 부탁대로 이 집을 얻어 주었지요."], ["It was just post-time when the messenger returned to me with the address of the house.", "심부름꾼이 집 주소를 가지고 돌아왔을 때가 마침 우편 시간이었어요."], ["We moved after dark—we came here quite unobserved.", "우리는 어두워진 뒤에 옮겼어요—아무 눈에도 띄지 않고 여기로 왔지요."], ["Have I done right, Walter?", "내가 잘한 건가요, 월터?"], ["Have I justified your trust in me?”", "나를 믿어 준 당신 마음에 부끄럽지 않게 한 건가요?”"]], [["I answered her warmly and gratefully, as I really felt.", "나는 정말로 느낀 그대로, 따뜻하고 고마운 마음을 담아 대답했다."], ["But the anxious look still remained on her face while I was speaking, and the first question she asked, when I had done, related to Count Fosco.", "그러나 내가 말하는 동안에도 근심 어린 표정은 여전히 그녀의 얼굴에 남아 있었고, 내 말이 끝나자 그녀가 맨 처음 던진 물음은 포스코 백작에 관한 것이었다."]], [["I saw that she was thinking of him now with a changed mind.", "나는 그녀가 이제 달라진 마음으로 그를 생각하고 있음을 알았다."], ["No fresh outbreak of anger against him, no new appeal to me to hasten the day of reckoning escaped her.", "그를 향한 새로운 분노의 폭발도, 셈을 치를 날을 앞당기라는 새로운 호소도 그녀의 입에서 나오지 않았다."], ["Her conviction that the man’s hateful admiration of herself was really sincere, seemed to have increased a hundredfold her distrust of his unfathomable cunning, her inborn dread of the wicked energy and vigilance of all his faculties.", "자신을 향한 그 사내의 가증스러운 흠모가 실은 진심이라는 확신은, 헤아릴 수 없는 그의 교활함에 대한 불신과 그의 모든 능력에 깃든 사악한 정력과 경계심에 대한 타고난 두려움을 백 배로 키워 놓은 듯했다."], ["Her voice fell low, her manner was hesitating, her eyes searched into mine with an eager fear when she asked me what I thought of his message, and what I meant to do next after hearing it.", "그의 전언을 어떻게 생각하는지, 그것을 들은 다음 내가 무엇을 할 작정인지 물을 때 그녀의 목소리는 낮게 잠겼고, 태도는 머뭇거렸으며, 두 눈은 애타는 두려움을 담고 내 눈을 파고들었다."]], [["“Not many weeks have passed, Marian,”", "“카일 씨를 만난 지 몇 주 되지 않았어요, 매리언.” 내가 대답했다."], ["I answered, “since my interview with Mr. Kyrle.", "“그와 헤어질 때 로라에 대해 내가 마지막으로 한 말은 이랬어요."], ["When he and I parted, the last words I said to him about Laura were these: ‘Her uncle’s house shall open to receive her, in the presence of every soul who followed the false funeral to the grave; the lie that records her death shall be publicly erased from the tombstone by the authority of the head of the family, and the two men who have wronged her shall answer for their crime to ME, though the justice that sits in tribunals is powerless to pursue them.’ One of those men is beyond mortal reach.", "‘그 가짜 장례 행렬을 따라 무덤까지 갔던 모든 사람이 보는 앞에서 그녀의 삼촌 집 문이 열려 그녀를 맞아들일 것이오. 그녀가 죽었다고 기록한 그 거짓말은 가문 어른의 권위로 묘비에서 공개적으로 지워질 것이오."], ["The other remains, and my resolution remains.”", "그리고 그녀를 짓밟은 두 사내는, 법정에 앉은 정의가 그들을 쫓을 힘이 없다 해도, 나에게 그 죄를 치를 것이오.’ 그 두 사람 가운데 하나는 이제 인간의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 갔어요. 다른 하나는 남아 있고, 내 결심도 남아 있어요.”"]], [["Her eyes lit up—her colour rose.", "그녀의 두 눈이 빛났다—뺨에 혈색이 올랐다."], ["She said nothing, but I saw all her sympathies gathering to mine in her face.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 나는 그녀의 온 마음이 내 편으로 모여드는 것을 그 얼굴에서 다 보았다."]], [["“I don’t disguise from myself, or from you,”", "“나 자신에게도, 당신에게도 감추지 않겠어요.”"], ["I went on, “that the prospect before us is more than doubtful.", "나는 말을 이었다. “우리 앞에 놓인 전망은 의심스럽다는 말로도 부족해요."], ["The risks we have run already are, it may be, trifles compared with the risks that threaten us in the future, but the venture shall be tried, Marian, for all that.", "우리가 이미 무릅쓴 위험은 앞으로 우리를 위협할 위험에 비하면 어쩌면 사소한 것일지도 몰라요. 그래도 매리언, 이 모험은 기어이 해 볼 겁니다."], ["I am not rash enough to measure myself against such a man as the Count before I am well prepared for him.", "나는 백작 같은 사람을 상대로 충분히 준비되기도 전에 덤벼들 만큼 무모하지는 않아요."], ["I have learnt patience—I can wait my time.", "나는 참는 법을 배웠어요—내 때를 기다릴 수 있어요."], ["Let him believe that his message has produced its effect—let him know nothing of us, and hear nothing of us—let us give him full time to feel secure—his own boastful nature, unless I seriously mistake him, will hasten that result.", "그의 전언이 효과를 냈다고 믿게 둡시다—우리에 대해 아무것도 알지 못하고 아무 소식도 듣지 못하게 둡시다—마음 놓을 시간을 넉넉히 줍시다. 내가 그를 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 스스로 뽐내기 좋아하는 그 천성이 그 결과를 앞당겨 줄 거예요."], ["This is one reason for waiting, but there is another more important still.", "이것이 기다리는 한 가지 이유인데, 그보다 더 중요한 이유가 또 하나 있어요."], ["My position, Marian, towards you and towards Laura ought to be a stronger one than it is now before I try our last chance.”", "매리언, 마지막 승부를 걸기 전에 당신과 로라에 대한 내 위치가 지금보다 더 든든해져야 해요.”"]], [["She leaned near to me, with a look of surprise.", "그녀는 놀란 얼굴로 내 쪽에 몸을 기울였다."]], [["“How can it be stronger?”", "“어떻게 더 든든해질 수 있다는 거죠?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I will tell you,”", "“때가 오면 말해 줄게요.”"], ["I replied, “when the time comes.", "내가 대답했다."], ["It has not come yet—it may never come at all.", "“아직 그때가 오지 않았어요—어쩌면 영영 오지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["I may be silent about it to Laura for ever—I must be silent now, even to YOU, till I see for myself that I can harmlessly and honourably speak.", "나는 로라에게 그 일을 영원히 말하지 못할 수도 있어요—지금은 당신에게조차 침묵해야 해요. 해를 끼치지 않고 떳떳하게 말할 수 있다고 스스로 확인하기 전까지는요."], ["Let us leave that subject.", "그 이야기는 그만둡시다."], ["There is another which has more pressing claims on our attention.", "더 급히 우리 마음을 써야 할 일이 또 있어요."], ["You have kept Laura, mercifully kept her, in ignorance of her husband’s death——”", "당신은 로라에게, 자비롭게도 로라에게 남편의 죽음을 알리지 않고 지켜 왔지요—”"]], [["“Oh, Walter, surely it must be long yet before we tell her of it?”", "“아, 월터, 그 이야기를 그 애에게 하기까지는 아직 한참 있어야 하지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“No, Marian.", "“아니에요, 매리언."], ["Better that you should reveal it to her now, than that accident, which no one can guard against, should reveal it to her at some future time.", "누구도 막을 수 없는 우연이 언젠가 그 사실을 알려 주게 되느니, 지금 당신이 알려 주는 편이 나아요."], ["Spare her all the details—break it to her very tenderly, but tell her that he is dead.”", "자세한 이야기는 다 덜어 내고—아주 부드럽게 말을 꺼내되, 그가 죽었다는 것만은 알려 줘요.”"]], [["“You have a reason, Walter, for wishing her to know of her husband’s death besides the reason you have just mentioned?”", "“월터, 방금 말한 이유 말고도 그 애가 남편의 죽음을 알기 바라는 다른 이유가 있는 거죠?”"]], [["“I have.”", "“있어요.”"]], [["“A reason connected with that subject which must not be mentioned between us yet?—which may never be mentioned to Laura at all?”", "“아직 우리 사이에서 말해서는 안 되는 그 이야기와—어쩌면 로라에게는 영영 말하지 못할지도 모르는 그 이야기와 관련된 이유인가요?”"]], [["She dwelt on the last words meaningly.", "그녀는 마지막 말에 뜻을 담아 힘주어 머물렀다."], ["When I answered her in the affirmative, I dwelt on them too.", "내가 그렇다고 대답할 때, 나 역시 그 말에 힘주어 머물렀다."]], [["Her face grew pale.", "그녀의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."], ["For a while she looked at me with a sad, hesitating interest.", "한동안 그녀는 슬프고 머뭇거리는 관심을 담아 나를 바라보았다."], ["An unaccustomed tenderness trembled in her dark eyes and softened her firm lips, as she glanced aside at the empty chair in which the dear companion of all our joys and sorrows had been sitting.", "우리의 모든 기쁨과 슬픔을 함께한 그 사랑스러운 동무가 앉아 있던 빈 의자를 곁눈으로 흘긋 보았을 때, 낯선 다정함이 그녀의 검은 눈에 떨렸고 굳게 다문 입술을 부드럽게 풀어 놓았다."]], [["“I think I understand,”", "“알 것 같아요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “I think I owe it to her and to you, Walter, to tell her of her husband’s death.”", "“그 애를 위해서도, 당신을 위해서도, 내가 그 애에게 남편의 죽음을 알리는 것이 도리라는 생각이 들어요, 월터.”"]], [["She sighed, and held my hand fast for a moment—then dropped it abruptly, and left the room.", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬고 잠시 내 손을 꼭 잡았다—그러더니 불쑥 그 손을 놓고 방을 나갔다."], ["On the next day Laura knew that his death had released her, and that the error and the calamity of her life lay buried in his tomb.", "이튿날 로라는 그의 죽음이 자신을 풀어 주었다는 것을, 그리고 자기 삶의 그 잘못과 재난이 그의 무덤에 함께 묻혔다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["His name was mentioned among us no more.", "그 뒤로 우리 사이에서 그의 이름이 오르내리는 일은 없었다."], ["Thenceforward, we shrank from the slightest approach to the subject of his death, and in the same scrupulous manner, Marian and I avoided all further reference to that other subject, which, by her consent and mine, was not to be mentioned between us yet.", "그날 이후 우리는 그의 죽음이라는 화제에 조금이라도 다가가는 것을 피했고, 꼭 그만큼 조심스럽게 매리언과 나는 그녀와 나의 합의로 아직은 우리 사이에서 입에 올리지 않기로 한 그 다른 이야기에 대해서도 더는 언급하지 않았다."], ["It was not the less present in our minds—it was rather kept alive in them by the restraint which we had imposed on ourselves.", "그렇다고 그것이 우리 마음에서 사라진 것은 아니었다—오히려 우리가 스스로에게 지운 그 절제 때문에 마음속에서 더욱 생생하게 살아 있었다."], ["We both watched Laura more anxiously than ever, sometimes waiting and hoping, sometimes waiting and fearing, till the time came.", "우리 둘은 그 어느 때보다 애타는 마음으로 로라를 지켜보았다. 어떤 때는 기다리며 바라고, 어떤 때는 기다리며 두려워하면서, 그 때가 올 때까지."]], [["By degrees we returned to our accustomed way of life.", "우리는 차츰 예전의 생활로 돌아갔다."], ["I resumed the daily work, which had been suspended during my absence in Hampshire.", "나는 햄프셔에 가 있는 동안 중단되었던 매일의 일을 다시 시작했다."], ["Our new lodgings cost us more than the smaller and less convenient rooms which we had left, and the claim thus implied on my increased exertions was strengthened by the doubtfulness of our future prospects.", "새 하숙은 우리가 떠나온 더 작고 불편한 방들보다 비용이 더 들었고, 그만큼 내가 더 힘써 벌어야 한다는 요구는 앞날이 불투명하다는 사실 때문에 한층 무거워졌다."], ["Emergencies might yet happen which would exhaust our little fund at the banker’s, and the work of my hands might be, ultimately, all we had to look to for support.", "은행에 넣어 둔 얼마 안 되는 돈을 바닥낼 위급한 일이 아직 닥칠 수도 있었고, 그렇게 되면 결국 우리를 먹여 살릴 것은 내 두 손의 일뿐일 터였다."], ["More permanent and more lucrative employment than had yet been offered to me was a necessity of our position—a necessity for which I now diligently set myself to provide.", "지금까지 내게 주어진 것보다 더 안정적이고 더 벌이가 나은 일자리는 우리 처지에 반드시 필요한 것이었다—나는 이제 그것을 마련하려고 부지런히 나섰다."]], [["It must not be supposed that the interval of rest and seclusion of which I am now writing, entirely suspended, on my part, all pursuit of the one absorbing purpose with which my thoughts and actions are associated in these pages.", "내가 지금 쓰고 있는 이 휴식과 은둔의 기간 동안, 이 기록에 담긴 내 모든 생각과 행동이 매여 있는 그 하나의 절실한 목적을 내가 완전히 접어 두었으리라고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["That purpose was, for months and months yet, never to relax its claims on me.", "그 목적은 앞으로도 여러 달 동안 나를 놓아주지 않을 것이었다."], ["The slow ripening of it still left me a measure of precaution to take, an obligation of gratitude to perform, and a doubtful question to solve.", "그것이 더디게 익어 가는 동안에도 내게는 취해야 할 예방 조치 하나와, 이행해야 할 감사의 의무 하나와, 풀어야 할 의심스러운 물음 하나가 남아 있었다."]], [["The measure of precaution related, necessarily, to the Count.", "예방 조치는 당연히 백작과 관련된 것이었다."], ["It was of the last importance to ascertain, if possible, whether his plans committed him to remaining in England—or, in other words, to remaining within my reach.", "그의 계획이 그를 영국에 머무르게 하는지—달리 말해 내 손이 닿는 범위 안에 머무르게 하는지를 될 수 있으면 확인해 두는 일이 무엇보다 중요했다."], ["I contrived to set this doubt at rest by very simple means.", "나는 아주 간단한 방법으로 이 의심을 가라앉혔다."], ["His address in St. John’s Wood being known to me, I inquired in the neighbourhood, and having found out the agent who had the disposal of the furnished house in which he lived, I asked if number five, Forest Road, was likely to be let within a reasonable time.", "세인트존스우드에 있는 그의 주소를 알고 있었으므로 나는 그 동네에서 수소문하여 그가 사는 가구 딸린 집을 관리하는 중개인을 찾아냈고, 포리스트 로드 5번지가 머지않아 세를 놓게 되겠느냐고 물었다."], ["The reply was in the negative.", "대답은 아니라는 것이었다."], ["I was informed that the foreign gentleman then residing in the house had renewed his term of occupation for another six months, and would remain in possession until the end of June in the following year.", "지금 그 집에 사는 외국인 신사가 앞으로 여섯 달 더 임차 기간을 갱신했으며 이듬해 유월 말까지 그 집을 쓸 것이라는 이야기였다."], ["We were then at the beginning of December only.", "그때는 아직 십이월 초순에 지나지 않았다."], ["I left the agent with my mind relieved from all present fear of the Count’s escaping me.", "나는 백작이 내게서 달아날지 모른다는 당장의 걱정을 덜고 중개인과 헤어졌다."]], [["The obligation I had to perform took me once more into the presence of Mrs. Clements.", "내가 이행해야 할 의무는 나를 다시 한번 클레멘츠 부인 앞으로 데려갔다."], ["I had promised to return, and to confide to her those particulars relating to the death and burial of Anne Catherick which I had been obliged to withhold at our first interview.", "나는 다시 찾아가겠다고, 그리고 처음 만났을 때는 어쩔 수 없이 감추어야 했던 앤 캐서릭의 죽음과 매장에 관한 자세한 사정을 털어놓겠다고 약속했었다."], ["Changed as circumstances now were, there was no hindrance to my trusting the good woman with as much of the story of the conspiracy as it was necessary to tell.", "사정이 달라진 지금은 그 음모의 내막 가운데 이야기해야 할 만큼을 그 선량한 부인에게 믿고 알리는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었다."], ["I had every reason that sympathy and friendly feeling could suggest to urge on me the speedy performance of my promise, and I did conscientiously and carefully perform it.", "연민과 우정이 일깨울 수 있는 온갖 이유가 그 약속을 어서 지키라고 나를 재촉했고, 나는 양심적으로 또 정성껏 그것을 지켰다."], ["There is no need to burden these pages with any statement of what passed at the interview.", "그 자리에서 오간 이야기를 이 기록에 굳이 담을 필요는 없다."], ["It will be more to the purpose to say, that the interview itself necessarily brought to my mind the one doubtful question still remaining to be solved—the question of Anne Catherick’s parentage on the father’s side.", "그보다는 그 만남 자체가 아직 풀리지 않고 남아 있던 그 의심스러운 물음—앤 캐서릭의 아버지 쪽 혈통에 관한 물음—을 자연히 내 머리에 떠올렸다는 사실을 말해 두는 편이 나을 것이다."]], [["A multitude of small considerations in connection with this subject—trifling enough in themselves, but strikingly important when massed together—had latterly led my mind to a conclusion which I resolved to verify.", "이 문제와 얽힌 자잘한 정황들이—하나하나는 사소하지만 한데 모아 놓으면 놀랄 만큼 중요한 것들이—근래 들어 나를 어떤 결론으로 이끌었고, 나는 그것을 확인해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["I obtained Marian’s permission to write to Major Donthorne, of Varneck Hall (where Mrs. Catherick had lived in service for some years previous to her marriage), to ask him certain questions.", "나는 매리언의 허락을 얻어 바넥 홀의 돈손 소령에게—캐서릭 부인이 결혼하기 전 몇 해 동안 하인으로 일했던 곳이다—몇 가지를 묻는 편지를 썼다."], ["I made the inquiries in Marian’s name, and described them as relating to matters of personal history in her family, which might explain and excuse my application.", "나는 매리언의 이름으로 물었고, 그 문의가 그녀 집안의 내력에 관한 일이라고 밝혀 내가 편지를 보내는 까닭을 설명하고 양해를 구했다."], ["When I wrote the letter I had no certain knowledge that Major Donthorne was still alive—I despatched it on the chance that he might be living, and able and willing to reply.", "편지를 쓸 때 나는 돈손 소령이 아직 살아 있는지조차 확실히 알지 못했다—그가 살아 있고, 답할 수 있고 또 답해 줄 마음이 있기를 요행에 걸고 부친 것이었다."]], [["After a lapse of two days proof came, in the shape of a letter, that the Major was living, and that he was ready to help us.", "이틀이 지나자 소령이 살아 있으며 우리를 기꺼이 도우려 한다는 증거가 편지의 형태로 도착했다."]], [["The idea in my mind when I wrote to him, and the nature of my inquiries will be easily inferred from his reply.", "내가 그에게 편지를 쓸 때 품고 있던 생각과 내가 물은 것들의 성격은 그의 답장에서 쉽게 미루어 알 수 있을 것이다."], ["His letter answered my questions by communicating these important facts—", "그의 편지는 다음과 같은 중요한 사실들을 알려 줌으로써 내 물음에 답해 주었다—"]], [["In the first place, “the late Sir Percival Glyde, of Blackwater Park,” had never set foot in Varneck Hall.", "첫째로, “블랙워터 파크의 고(故) 퍼시벌 글라이드 경”은 바넥 홀에 한 번도 발을 들인 적이 없었다."], ["The deceased gentleman was a total stranger to Major Donthorne, and to all his family.", "세상을 떠난 그 신사는 돈손 소령에게도, 그의 온 집안에게도 전혀 낯선 사람이었다."]], [["In the second place, “the late Mr. Philip Fairlie, of Limmeridge House,”", "둘째로, “리머리지 하우스의 고(故) 필립 페얼리 씨”는 젊은 시절 돈손 소령의 절친한 벗이자 늘 찾아오는 손님이었다."], ["had been, in his younger days, the intimate friend and constant guest of Major Donthorne. Having refreshed his memory by looking back to old letters and other papers, the Major was in a position to say positively that Mr.", "소령은 옛 편지와 여러 서류를 들춰 기억을 되살린 끝에, 필립 페얼리 씨가 1826년 팔월에 바넥 홀에 머물렀고, 이어 구월과 시월 얼마간을 사냥철 동안 그곳에서 지냈다고 단언할 수 있었다."], ["Philip Fairlie was staying at Varneck Hall in the month of August, eighteen hundred and twenty-six, and that he remained there for the shooting during the month of September and part of October following. He then left, to the best of the Major’s belief, for Scotland, and did not return to Varneck Hall till after a lapse of time, when he reappeared in the character of a newly-married man.", "그 뒤 그는 소령이 알기로는 스코틀랜드로 떠났고, 한동안 세월이 흐른 뒤에야 바넥 홀에 다시 나타났는데, 그때는 갓 결혼한 사람의 신분이었다."]], [["Taken by itself, this statement was, perhaps, of little positive value, but taken in connection with certain facts, every one of which either Marian or I knew to be true, it suggested one plain conclusion that was, to our minds, irresistible.", "이 진술만 따로 놓고 보면 아마 확실한 값어치는 별로 없었을 것이다. 그러나 매리언이나 내가 사실로 알고 있는 몇 가지 정황과 이어 놓고 보면, 그것은 우리 마음에 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 하나의 명백한 결론을 가리켰다."]], [["Knowing, now, that Mr. Philip Fairlie had been at Varneck Hall in the autumn of eighteen hundred and twenty-six, and that Mrs.", "이제 필립 페얼리 씨가 1826년 가을에 바넥 홀에 있었다는 것과 캐서릭 부인이 같은 시기에 그곳에서 하인으로 살고 있었다는 것을 알게 된 우리는, 그 밖에도 이런 것들을 알고 있었다."], ["Catherick had been living there in service at the same time, we knew also—first, that Anne had been born in June, eighteen hundred and twenty-seven; secondly, that she had always presented an extraordinary personal resemblance to Laura; and, thirdly, that Laura herself was strikingly like her father.", "첫째, 앤이 1827년 유월에 태어났다는 것. 둘째, 앤이 언제나 로라와 놀랄 만큼 닮은 모습이었다는 것."], ["Mr. Philip Fairlie had been one of the notoriously handsome men of his time.", "셋째, 로라 자신이 아버지를 몹시 닮았다는 것."], ["In disposition entirely unlike his brother Frederick, he was the spoilt darling of society, especially of the women—an easy, light-hearted, impulsive, affectionate man—generous to a fault—constitutionally lax in his principles, and notoriously thoughtless of moral obligations where women were concerned.", "필립 페얼리 씨는 당대에 미남으로 이름난 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 성격은 동생 프레더릭과 전혀 달라서, 사교계의, 특히 여자들의 응석받이였다—태평하고 쾌활하고 충동적이고 다정한 사람, 지나칠 만큼 너그러운 사람, 천성적으로 품행이 헤펐고 여자 문제에서는 도덕적 의무를 대수롭지 않게 여기기로 소문난 사람이었다."], ["Such were the facts we knew—such was the character of the man.", "우리가 아는 사실이 이러했고, 그 사람의 됨됨이가 이러했다."], ["Surely the plain inference that follows needs no pointing out?", "여기서 따라 나오는 뻔한 추론을 굳이 짚어 줄 필요가 있겠는가?"]], [["Read by the new light which had now broken upon me, even Mrs. Catherick’s letter, in despite of herself, rendered its mite of assistance towards strengthening the conclusion at which I had arrived.", "이제 내게 밝아 온 새로운 빛으로 읽으니, 캐서릭 부인의 편지조차 본인의 뜻과는 달리 내가 다다른 결론을 뒷받침하는 데 제 몫의 도움을 보태 주었다."], ["She had described Mrs. Fairlie (in writing to me) as “plain-looking,”", "그녀는 (내게 보낸 글에서) 페얼리 부인을 “인물이 변변찮은”"], ["and as having “entrapped the handsomest man in England into marrying her.”", "여자로, 그리고 “영국에서 가장 잘생긴 남자를 옭아매어 결혼한” 여자로 그렸다."], ["Both assertions were gratuitously made, and both were false. Jealous dislike (which, in such a woman as Mrs.", "두 가지 주장 모두 굳이 할 필요가 없는 말이었고, 두 가지 모두 거짓이었다."], ["Catherick, would express itself in petty malice rather than not express itself at all) appeared to me to be the only assignable cause for the peculiar insolence of her reference to Mrs. Fairlie, under circumstances which did not necessitate any reference at all.", "아무 언급도 할 까닭이 없는 자리에서 페얼리 부인을 그토록 유별나게 무례하게 언급한 데에는, 질투 섞인 미움—캐서릭 부인 같은 여자에게서라면 아예 드러나지 않느니 좀스러운 악의로 드러날 그런 미움—말고는 달리 짚을 만한 원인이 없어 보였다."]], [["The mention here of Mrs. Fairlie’s name naturally suggests one other question.", "여기서 페얼리 부인의 이름이 나온 김에 또 하나의 물음이 자연스레 떠오른다."], ["Did she ever suspect whose child the little girl brought to her at Limmeridge might be?", "그녀는 리머리지로 데려와진 그 어린 소녀가 누구의 아이일지 한 번이라도 의심한 적이 있었을까?"]], [["Marian’s testimony was positive on this point. Mrs.", "이 점에 대해서는 매리언의 증언이 분명했다."], ["Fairlie’s letter to her husband, which had been read to me in former days—the letter describing Anne’s resemblance to Laura, and acknowledging her affectionate interest in the little stranger—had been written, beyond all question, in perfect innocence of heart.", "예전에 내게 읽어 주었던, 페얼리 부인이 남편에게 보낸 편지—앤이 로라와 닮았다고 쓰고 그 낯선 어린아이에게 애정 어린 관심을 품었노라 털어놓은 그 편지—는 의심할 나위 없이 조금도 티 없는 마음으로 쓰인 것이었다."], ["It even seemed doubtful, on consideration, whether Mr. Philip Fairlie himself had been nearer than his wife to any suspicion of the truth.", "곰곰 생각해 보면 필립 페얼리 씨 자신이 그 아내보다 진실에 조금이라도 더 가까이 다가가 의심한 적이 있었는지조차 미덥지 않았다."], ["The disgracefully deceitful circumstances under which Mrs. Catherick had married, the purpose of concealment which the marriage was intended to answer, might well keep her silent for caution’s sake, perhaps for her own pride’s sake also, even assuming that she had the means, in his absence, of communicating with the father of her unborn child.", "캐서릭 부인이 결혼하게 된 그 낯 뜨겁도록 기만적인 정황과 그 결혼으로 감추려 한 목적을 생각하면, 설령 그가 없는 동안 뱃속 아이의 아비에게 연락할 방도가 그녀에게 있었다 해도, 조심하느라, 어쩌면 제 자존심 때문에도 그녀는 얼마든지 입을 다물고 있었을 만했다."]], [["As this surmise floated through my mind, there rose on my memory the remembrance of the Scripture denunciation which we have all thought of in our time with wonder and with awe: “The sins of the fathers shall be visited on the children.”", "이런 추측이 머릿속을 스칠 때, 우리 모두가 살아오며 한 번쯤 놀라움과 두려움으로 떠올려 보았을 성경의 그 준엄한 말씀이 내 기억에 떠올랐다. “아비의 죄가 자식에게 미치리라.”"], ["But for the fatal resemblance between the two daughters of one father, the conspiracy of which Anne had been the innocent instrument and Laura the innocent victim could never have been planned.", "한 아버지의 두 딸이 그토록 치명적으로 닮지만 않았더라면, 앤이 죄 없는 도구가 되고 로라가 죄 없는 희생자가 된 그 음모는 애초에 꾸며질 수도 없었을 것이다."], ["With what unerring and terrible directness the long chain of circumstances led down from the thoughtless wrong committed by the father to the heartless injury inflicted on the child!", "아버지가 저지른 경솔한 잘못에서 그 자식에게 가해진 무정한 상해에 이르기까지, 그 기나긴 정황의 사슬은 얼마나 어김없고 얼마나 무섭도록 곧바르게 이어져 내려왔던가!"]], [["These thoughts came to me, and others with them, which drew my mind away to the little Cumberland churchyard where Anne Catherick now lay buried.", "이런 생각들이, 그리고 그와 함께 다른 생각들이 내게 찾아와, 내 마음을 앤 캐서릭이 이제 묻혀 있는 컴벌랜드의 그 작은 교회 묘지로 데려갔다."], ["I thought of the bygone days when I had met her by Mrs. Fairlie’s grave, and met her for the last time.", "나는 페얼리 부인의 무덤가에서 그녀를 만났던 지난날을, 그리고 마지막으로 그녀를 만났던 때를 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of her poor helpless hands beating on the tombstone, and her weary, yearning words, murmured to the dead remains of her protectress and her friend: “Oh, if I could die, and be hidden and at rest with YOU!”", "나는 묘비를 두드리던 그 가엾고 무력한 두 손과, 자신을 지켜 준 보호자이자 벗의 유해를 향해 중얼거리던 그 지치고 애타는 말을 떠올렸다. “아, 내가 죽어서 당신과 함께 감추어져 쉴 수 있다면!”"], ["Little more than a year had passed since she breathed that wish; and how inscrutably, how awfully, it had been fulfilled!", "그녀가 그 소원을 내뱉은 지 겨우 일 년 남짓 지났을 뿐인데, 그것이 얼마나 헤아릴 수 없이, 얼마나 무섭게 이루어졌는가!"], ["The words she had spoken to Laura by the shores of the lake, the very words had now come true.", "호숫가에서 로라에게 했던 그 말이, 바로 그 말이 이제 그대로 이루어진 것이다."], ["“Oh, if I could only be buried with your mother!", "“아, 당신 어머니 곁에 묻힐 수만 있다면!"], ["If I could only wake at her side when the angel’s trumpet sounds and the graves give up their dead at the resurrection!”", "천사의 나팔이 울리고 무덤들이 죽은 자를 내어놓는 부활의 날에, 그분 곁에서 깨어날 수만 있다면!”"], ["Through what mortal crime and horror, through what darkest windings of the way down to death—the lost creature had wandered in God’s leading to the last home that, living, she never hoped to reach!", "인간의 어떤 죄와 참혹을 지나, 죽음으로 내려가는 길의 어떤 캄캄한 굽이를 지나—그 길 잃은 사람은 하느님의 인도를 받아, 살아서는 결코 다다르기를 바랄 수 없었던 마지막 집에 이르도록 헤매었던가!"], ["In that sacred rest I leave her—in that dread companionship let her remain undisturbed.", "그 거룩한 안식 속에 나는 그녀를 남겨 둔다—그 두려운 동행 속에서 그녀가 방해받지 않고 머물기를."]], [["So the ghostly figure which has haunted these pages, as it haunted my life, goes down into the impenetrable gloom.", "그리하여 내 삶에 붙어 다녔듯 이 기록에도 붙어 다니던 그 유령 같은 모습은 뚫을 수 없는 어둠 속으로 내려간다."], ["Like a shadow she first came to me in the loneliness of the night.", "그림자처럼 그녀는 밤의 외로움 속에서 처음 내게 왔다."], ["Like a shadow she passes away in the loneliness of the dead.", "그림자처럼 그녀는 죽은 자의 외로움 속으로 사라진다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["Four months elapsed.", "넉 달이 흘렀다."], ["April came—the month of spring—the month of change.", "4월이 왔다—봄의 달이자, 변화의 달이었다."]], [["The course of time had flowed through the interval since the winter peacefully and happily in our new home.", "겨울이 지난 뒤의 그 기간 동안, 시간은 우리의 새 보금자리에서 평온하고 행복하게 흘러갔다."], ["I had turned my long leisure to good account, had largely increased my sources of employment, and had placed our means of subsistence on surer grounds.", "나는 오래 남아돌던 여가를 잘 활용하여 일거리의 원천을 크게 늘렸고, 우리의 생계 기반을 한결 든든한 자리에 올려놓았다."], ["Freed from the suspense and the anxiety which had tried her so sorely and hung over her so long, Marian’s spirits rallied, and her natural energy of character began to assert itself again, with something, if not all, of the freedom and the vigour of former times.", "그토록 모질게 그녀를 시험하며 그토록 오래 그녀 위에 드리워 있던 불안과 초조에서 벗어나자, 매리언의 기운은 되살아났고, 타고난 기개도 다시 제 모습을 드러내기 시작했다. 예전만큼은 아닐지언정, 그 자유로움과 활력의 일부가 돌아온 것이다."]], [["More pliable under change than her sister, Laura showed more plainly the progress made by the healing influences of her new life.", "언니보다 변화에 더 유연했던 로라는 새로운 삶이 베푸는 치유의 힘이 이루어낸 진전을 한층 뚜렷하게 보여주었다."], ["The worn and wasted look which had prematurely aged her face was fast leaving it, and the expression which had been the first of its charms in past days was the first of its beauties that now returned.", "그 얼굴을 나이보다 늙어 보이게 만들었던 초췌하고 야윈 기색은 빠르게 걷혀 갔고, 지난날 그 얼굴의 첫째가는 매력이었던 그 표정이 이제 되돌아오는 아름다움의 첫째가 되었다."], ["My closest observations of her detected but one serious result of the conspiracy which had once threatened her reason and her life.", "아무리 가까이서 지켜보아도, 한때 그녀의 이성과 목숨을 위협했던 그 음모가 남긴 심각한 자취는 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["Her memory of events, from the period of her leaving Blackwater Park to the period of our meeting in the burial-ground of Limmeridge Church, was lost beyond all hope of recovery.", "블랙워터 파크를 떠나던 때부터 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 우리가 다시 만나던 때까지의 기억은 되찾을 가망 없이 사라져 버렸다."], ["At the slightest reference to that time she changed and trembled still, her words became confused, her memory wandered and lost itself as helplessly as ever.", "그 시기를 조금이라도 언급하면 그녀는 여전히 낯빛이 변하고 몸을 떨었으며, 말이 뒤엉키고, 기억은 예전처럼 속절없이 헤매다 길을 잃었다."], ["Here, and here only, the traces of the past lay deep—too deep to be effaced.", "여기, 오직 여기에만 과거의 흔적이 깊이 박혀 있었다—지워내기에는 너무도 깊이."]], [["In all else she was now so far on the way to recovery that, on her best and brightest days, she sometimes looked and spoke like the Laura of old times.", "그 밖의 모든 면에서 그녀는 회복의 길을 아주 멀리까지 걸어와서, 가장 좋고 밝은 날이면 이따금 옛날의 로라처럼 보이고 옛날의 로라처럼 말했다."], ["The happy change wrought its natural result in us both.", "이 행복한 변화는 우리 두 사람 안에서 자연스러운 결과를 낳았다."], ["From their long slumber, on her side and on mine, those imperishable memories of our past life in Cumberland now awoke, which were one and all alike, the memories of our love.", "그녀 쪽에서도 내 쪽에서도, 컴벌랜드에서 보낸 지난날의 그 스러지지 않는 기억들이 긴 잠에서 깨어났다. 그것들은 하나같이 모두 우리 사랑의 기억이었다."]], [["Gradually and insensibly our daily relations towards each other became constrained.", "서서히, 그리고 알지 못하는 사이에, 서로를 대하는 우리의 일상은 조심스러워졌다."], ["The fond words which I had spoken to her so naturally, in the days of her sorrow and her suffering, faltered strangely on my lips.", "그녀가 슬픔과 고통에 잠겨 있던 시절 그토록 자연스럽게 건네던 다정한 말들이 이상하게도 내 입술에서 머뭇거렸다."], ["In the time when my dread of losing her was most present to my mind, I had always kissed her when she left me at night and when she met me in the morning.", "그녀를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움이 내 마음을 가장 크게 짓누르던 때에는, 밤에 그녀가 나를 두고 물러갈 때도 아침에 나를 맞이할 때도 나는 늘 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["The kiss seemed now to have dropped between us—to be lost out of our lives.", "그런데 이제 그 입맞춤은 우리 사이에서 툭 떨어져 버린 듯했다—우리 삶에서 사라져 버린 듯했다."], ["Our hands began to tremble again when they met.", "손이 닿으면 우리의 손은 다시 떨리기 시작했다."], ["We hardly ever looked long at one another out of Marian’s presence.", "매리언이 없는 자리에서는 서로를 오래 바라보는 일이 좀처럼 없었다."], ["The talk often flagged between us when we were alone.", "단둘이 있으면 대화가 자주 끊겼다."], ["When I touched her by accident I felt my heart beating fast, as it used to beat at Limmeridge House—I saw the lovely answering flush glowing again in her cheeks, as if we were back among the Cumberland Hills in our past characters of master and pupil once more.", "뜻하지 않게 그녀에게 손이 닿으면 심장이 빠르게 뛰는 것이 느껴졌다. 리머리지 하우스에서 그러했듯이."], ["She had long intervals of silence and thoughtfulness, and denied she had been thinking when Marian asked her the question.", "그러면 그 사랑스러운 홍조가 화답하듯 그녀의 뺨에 다시 피어오르는 것이 보였다. 마치 우리가 컴벌랜드의 언덕으로 돌아가 다시 선생과 제자라는 옛 자리에 서 있기라도 한 것처럼."], ["I surprised myself one day neglecting my work to dream over the little water-colour portrait of her which I had taken in the summer-house where we first met—just as I used to neglect Mr.", "그녀는 오래도록 말없이 생각에 잠기곤 했고, 매리언이 무슨 생각을 하느냐고 물으면 아무 생각도 하지 않았다고 부인했다. 어느 날 나는 일을 내팽개친 채, 우리가 처음 만난 여름 별채에서 그려두었던 그녀의 작은 수채 초상화를 바라보며 몽상에 잠긴 나 자신에 스스로 놀랐다."], ["Fairlie’s drawings to dream over the same likeness when it was newly finished in the bygone time.", "지나간 시절 그 초상이 갓 완성되었을 때 페얼리 씨의 그림들을 내팽개치고 같은 초상을 바라보며 몽상에 잠기던 것과 꼭 같았다."], ["Changed as all the circumstances now were, our position towards each other in the golden days of our first companionship seemed to be revived with the revival of our love.", "모든 정황이 이토록 달라졌는데도, 우리가 처음으로 함께하던 그 황금 같은 날들의 서로에 대한 자리가 우리 사랑의 되살아남과 더불어 되살아나는 듯했다."], ["It was as if Time had drifted us back on the wreck of our early hopes to the old familiar shore!", "마치 시간이 우리를 젊은 날 희망의 난파선에 실어 옛적 낯익은 물가로 도로 떠밀어다 놓은 것만 같았다!"]], [["To any other woman I could have spoken the decisive words which I still hesitated to speak to HER.", "다른 여자에게라면 나는 그 결정적인 말을 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그런데 그녀에게만은 여전히 그 말을 하기가 망설여졌다."], ["The utter helplessness of her position—her friendless dependence on all the forbearing gentleness that I could show her—my fear of touching too soon some secret sensitiveness in her which my instinct as a man might not have been fine enough to discover—these considerations, and others like them, kept me self-distrustfully silent.", "그녀가 처한 처지의 그 지독한 무력함—내가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 너그러운 다정함에 오로지 기대어 있는, 달리 의지할 데 없는 그 신세—사내인 내 직감으로는 미처 알아차릴 만큼 섬세하지 못했을지도 모를 어떤 은밀한 예민함을 너무 이르게 건드리지나 않을까 하는 두려움—이런 생각들, 그리고 그와 비슷한 다른 생각들이 나를 스스로 미덥지 못하게 만들어 입을 다물게 했다."], ["And yet I knew that the restraint on both sides must be ended, that the relations in which we stood towards one another must be altered in some settled manner for the future, and that it rested with me, in the first instance, to recognise the necessity for a change.", "그러면서도 나는 알고 있었다. 양쪽 모두의 이 조심스러움은 끝나야 한다는 것을, 우리가 서로에 대해 서 있는 자리는 앞날을 위해 어떤 확실한 방식으로 바뀌어야 한다는 것을, 그리고 그 변화의 필요를 먼저 인정해야 할 사람은 다름 아닌 나라는 것을."]], [["The more I thought of our position, the harder the attempt to alter it appeared, while the domestic conditions on which we three had been living together since the winter remained undisturbed.", "우리의 처지를 생각하면 할수록, 겨울 이래로 우리 세 사람이 함께 지내온 살림의 조건이 그대로 남아 있는 한 그것을 바꿔보려는 시도는 더 어렵게만 보였다. 이런 마음이 어디서 비롯된 것인지, 그 변덕스러운 심사를 나로서는 설명할 길이 없다."], ["I cannot account for the capricious state of mind in which this feeling originated, but the idea nevertheless possessed me that some previous change of place and circumstances, some sudden break in the quiet monotony of our lives, so managed as to vary the home aspect under which we had been accustomed to see each other, might prepare the way for me to speak, and might make it easier and less embarrassing for Laura and Marian to hear.", "그러나 어쨌든 한 가지 생각이 나를 사로잡았다. 먼저 장소와 정황을 좀 바꾸어보는 것, 우리 삶의 조용한 단조로움에 갑작스러운 틈을 하나 내는 것, 그것도 우리가 서로를 늘 보아온 그 집안의 얼굴이 달라지도록 잘 꾸며서 그리한다면, 내가 말을 꺼낼 길이 닦일 수도 있고 로라와 매리언이 그 말을 듣기도 한결 쉽고 덜 거북해질 수 있으리라는 생각이었다."]], [["With this purpose in view, I said, one morning, that I thought we had all earned a little holiday and a change of scene.", "그런 뜻을 품고서 나는 어느 날 아침, 우리 모두 짧은 휴가와 자리를 바꿔볼 자격은 벌어놓은 것 같다고 말했다."], ["After some consideration, it was decided that we should go for a fortnight to the sea-side.", "얼마쯤 의논한 끝에 우리는 보름 동안 바닷가로 가기로 정했다."]], [["On the next day we left Fulham for a quiet town on the south coast.", "이튿날 우리는 풀럼을 떠나 남쪽 해안의 조용한 소읍으로 갔다."], ["At that early season of the year we were the only visitors in the place.", "한 해의 그 이른 철에 그곳을 찾은 사람은 우리뿐이었다."], ["The cliffs, the beach, and the walks inland were all in the solitary condition which was most welcome to us.", "벼랑도, 모래밭도, 내륙으로 난 산책길도 모두 우리에게 더없이 반가운 그 호젓한 상태였다."], ["The air was mild—the prospects over hill and wood and down were beautifully varied by the shifting April light and shade, and the restless sea leapt under our windows, as if it felt, like the land, the glow and freshness of spring.", "공기는 부드러웠다—언덕과 숲과 구릉 너머로 펼쳐진 풍경은 4월의 빛과 그늘이 옮겨 다니는 데 따라 아름답게 변화했고, 쉼 없는 바다는 우리 창 아래에서 뛰놀았다. 마치 뭍과 마찬가지로 봄의 열기와 싱그러움을 느끼기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["I owed it to Marian to consult her before I spoke to Laura, and to be guided afterwards by her advice.", "로라에게 말하기 전에 매리언과 먼저 의논하고, 그런 다음 그녀의 조언을 따르는 것이 마땅히 내가 매리언에게 해야 할 도리였다."]], [["On the third day from our arrival I found a fit opportunity of speaking to her alone.", "도착한 지 사흘째 되던 날, 나는 그녀와 단둘이 이야기할 알맞은 기회를 얻었다."], ["The moment we looked at one another, her quick instinct detected the thought in my mind before I could give it expression.", "우리가 서로를 바라본 그 순간, 그녀의 빠른 직감은 내가 입 밖에 내기도 전에 내 마음속 생각을 알아차렸다."], ["With her customary energy and directness she spoke at once, and spoke first.", "늘 그렇듯 기운차고 단도직입적으로 그녀는 곧바로, 그리고 먼저 말했다."]], [["“You are thinking of that subject which was mentioned between us on the evening of your return from Hampshire,”", "“햄프셔에서 돌아온 날 저녁에 우리 사이에 나왔던 그 이야기를 생각하고 있는 거죠.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “I have been expecting you to allude to it for some time past.", "“한동안 당신이 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 기다리고 있었어요."], ["There must be a change in our little household, Walter, we cannot go on much longer as we are now.", "우리 이 작은 살림에는 변화가 있어야 해요, 월터. 지금 이대로는 그리 오래 갈 수 없어요."], ["I see it as plainly as you do—as plainly as Laura sees it, though she says nothing.", "당신 눈에 보이는 만큼 내 눈에도 훤히 보여요—로라는 아무 말도 하지 않지만, 그 애 눈에 보이는 만큼이나요."], ["How strangely the old times in Cumberland seem to have come back!", "컴벌랜드의 그 옛날이 어쩌면 이렇게 고스란히 돌아온 것 같을까요!"], ["You and I are together again, and the one subject of interest between us is Laura once more.", "당신과 나는 다시 함께 있고, 우리 사이에 걸린 단 하나의 관심사는 또다시 로라예요."], ["I could almost fancy that this room is the summer-house at Limmeridge, and that those waves beyond us are beating on our sea-shore.”", "이 방이 리머리지의 여름 별채이고, 저 너머 저 물결이 우리 바닷가에 부딪는 파도라고 해도 곧이들을 것만 같아요.”"]], [["“I was guided by your advice in those past days,”", "“지나간 그 시절에도 나는 당신의 조언을 따랐지요.”"], ["I said, “and now, Marian, with reliance tenfold greater I will be guided by it again.”", "내가 말했다. “그리고 이제, 매리언, 그때보다 열 배는 더 큰 믿음으로 다시 그 조언을 따르겠습니다.”"]], [["She answered by pressing my hand.", "그녀는 내 손을 꼭 쥐는 것으로 대답했다."], ["I saw that she was deeply touched by my reference to the past.", "지난날에 대한 내 언급에 그녀가 깊이 마음이 움직였음을 나는 알아보았다."], ["We sat together near the window, and while I spoke and she listened, we looked at the glory of the sunlight shining on the majesty of the sea.", "우리는 창가에 나란히 앉았고, 내가 말하고 그녀가 듣는 동안, 우리는 장엄한 바다 위에 쏟아지는 눈부신 햇빛을 바라보았다."]], [["“Whatever comes of this confidence between us,”", "“우리 사이의 이 흉금 없는 이야기에서 무엇이 나오든,”"], ["I said, “whether it ends happily or sorrowfully for ME, Laura’s interests will still be the interests of my life.", "내가 말했다. “그것이 나에게 행복하게 끝나든 슬프게 끝나든, 로라의 이익은 앞으로도 내 삶의 이익일 겁니다."], ["When we leave this place, on whatever terms we leave it, my determination to wrest from Count Fosco the confession which I failed to obtain from his accomplice, goes back with me to London, as certainly as I go back myself.", "우리가 이곳을 떠날 때, 어떤 사정으로 떠나게 되든, 그자의 공범에게서 얻어내지 못한 그 자백을 포스코 백작에게서 기어이 빼앗아내겠다는 내 결심은 나와 함께 런던으로 돌아갈 겁니다. 내가 돌아가는 것만큼이나 확실하게요."], ["Neither you nor I can tell how that man may turn on me, if I bring him to bay; we only know, by his own words and actions, that he is capable of striking at me through Laura, without a moment’s hesitation, or a moment’s remorse.", "그자를 궁지에 몰아넣었을 때 그자가 나에게 어떻게 덤벼들지는 당신도 나도 알 수 없습니다. 다만 그자가 제 입으로 한 말과 제 손으로 한 짓으로 미루어, 그자가 잠시도 망설이지 않고 한 조각 뉘우침도 없이 로라를 통해 나를 칠 수 있는 인간이라는 것만은 압니다."], ["In our present position I have no claim on her which society sanctions, which the law allows, to strengthen me in resisting him, and in protecting HER.", "지금 우리 처지로는, 그자에게 맞서고 그녀를 지키는 데 나를 굳게 받쳐줄, 세상이 인정하고 법이 허락하는 그런 권리가 나에게는 그녀에 대해 하나도 없습니다."], ["This places me at a serious disadvantage.", "이것이 나를 심각하게 불리한 자리에 세웁니다."], ["If I am to fight our cause with the Count, strong in the consciousness of Laura’s safety, I must fight it for my Wife.", "로라가 안전하다는 확신 위에 굳건히 서서 백작과 우리의 싸움을 치러야 한다면, 나는 그 싸움을 내 아내를 위해 치러야 합니다."], ["Do you agree to that, Marian, so far?”", "여기까지, 매리언, 동의하십니까?”"]], [["“To every word of it,”", "“한 마디 한 마디 다 동의해요.”"], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“I will not plead out of my own heart,”", "“내 마음속을 근거로 호소하지는 않겠습니다.” 나는 말을 이었다."], ["I went on; “I will not appeal to the love which has survived all changes and all shocks—I will rest my only vindication of myself for thinking of her, and speaking of her as my wife, on what I have just said.", "“온갖 변화와 온갖 충격을 다 견디고 살아남은 그 사랑에 기대지도 않겠습니다. 그녀를 마음에 두고, 그녀를 내 아내라 일컫는 나 자신에 대한 유일한 변명을 나는 방금 말한 그것 위에만 두겠습니다."], ["If the chance of forcing a confession from the Count is, as I believe it to be, the last chance left of publicly establishing the fact of Laura’s existence, the least selfish reason that I can advance for our marriage is recognised by us both.", "백작에게서 자백을 강제로 얻어낼 그 기회가, 내가 믿는 대로, 로라가 살아 있다는 사실을 세상 앞에 세울 마지막 기회라면, 우리의 혼인을 위해 내가 내세울 수 있는 가장 이기적이지 않은 이유는 우리 두 사람이 함께 인정하는 것이 됩니다."], ["But I may be wrong in my conviction—other means of achieving our purpose may be in our power, which are less uncertain and less dangerous.", "그러나 내 확신이 틀렸을 수도 있습니다—우리 목적을 이룰 다른 수단, 덜 불확실하고 덜 위험한 수단이 우리 손안에 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["I have searched anxiously, in my own mind, for those means, and I have not found them.", "나는 내 머릿속에서 애타게 그런 수단을 찾아보았지만, 찾지 못했습니다."], ["Have you?”", "당신은 찾았습니까?”"]], [["“No.", "“아니요."], ["I have thought about it too, and thought in vain.”", "나도 그 생각을 해봤지만, 헛일이었어요.”"]], [["“In all likelihood,”", "“십중팔구,”"], ["I continued, “the same questions have occurred to you, in considering this difficult subject, which have occurred to me.", "내가 이어 말했다. “이 어려운 문제를 생각하면서 나에게 떠올랐던 것과 똑같은 물음들이 당신에게도 떠올랐을 겁니다."], ["Ought we to return with her to Limmeridge, now that she is like herself again, and trust to the recognition of her by the people of the village, or by the children at the school?", "그녀가 다시 제 모습을 되찾은 지금, 그녀와 함께 리머리지로 돌아가서 마을 사람들이나 학교 아이들이 그녀를 알아보아 주기를 믿어보아야 할까요?"], ["Ought we to appeal to the practical test of her handwriting?", "그녀의 필적이라는 실질적인 증거에 호소해야 할까요?"], ["Suppose we did so.", "그렇게 했다고 칩시다."], ["Suppose the recognition of her obtained, and the identity of the handwriting established.", "사람들이 그녀를 알아보았고 필적이 같다는 것도 입증되었다고 칩시다."], ["Would success in both those cases do more than supply an excellent foundation for a trial in a court of law? Would the recognition and the handwriting prove her identity to Mr.", "그 두 가지 모두 성공한들, 법정에서 재판을 벌일 훌륭한 토대를 마련하는 것 이상의 무엇이 되겠습니까?"], ["Fairlie and take her back to Limmeridge House, against the evidence of her aunt, against the evidence of the medical certificate, against the fact of the funeral and the fact of the inscription on the tomb?", "그 알아봄과 그 필적이, 그녀 고모의 증언에 맞서서, 의사 진단서의 증언에 맞서서, 장례를 치렀다는 사실과 무덤에 새겨진 비문의 사실에 맞서서, 페얼리 씨에게 그녀가 그녀임을 증명하고 그녀를 리머리지 하우스로 되돌려놓을 수 있겠습니까? 없습니다!"], ["No! We could only hope to succeed in throwing a serious doubt on the assertion of her death, a doubt which nothing short of a legal inquiry can settle.", "우리는 기껏해야 그녀가 죽었다는 주장에 심각한 의심을 던지는 데 성공하기를 바랄 수 있을 뿐이고, 그 의심은 법적인 심리가 아니고서는 결코 결말이 나지 않습니다."], ["I will assume that we possess (what we have certainly not got) money enough to carry this inquiry on through all its stages. I will assume that Mr.", "이 심리를 모든 단계에 걸쳐 끌고 갈 만한 돈이 우리에게 있다고 쳐봅시다(사실 우리에게는 분명 없지만 말입니다)."], ["Fairlie’s prejudices might be reasoned away—that the false testimony of the Count and his wife, and all the rest of the false testimony, might be confuted—that the recognition could not possibly be ascribed to a mistake between Laura and Anne Catherick, or the handwriting be declared by our enemies to be a clever fraud—all these are assumptions which, more or less, set plain probabilities at defiance; but let them pass—and let us ask ourselves what would be the first consequence or the first questions put to Laura herself on the subject of the conspiracy.", "페얼리 씨의 편견을 이치로 풀어낼 수 있다고 쳐봅시다—백작 부부의 거짓 증언과 그 밖의 온갖 거짓 증언을 모조리 뒤엎을 수 있다고, 사람들이 그녀를 알아본 것을 로라와 앤 캐서릭을 혼동한 착오로 돌릴 수 없다고, 우리 적들이 그 필적을 교묘한 위조라고 우길 수 없다고 쳐봅시다—이 모든 가정은 크든 작든 뻔한 개연성을 거스르는 것입니다. 그러나 그것도 다 그렇다 치고, 이렇게 자문해봅시다."], ["We know only too well what the consequence would be, for we know that she has never recovered her memory of what happened to her in London.", "그 음모라는 주제를 두고 로라 자신에게 던져질 첫 번째 결과, 혹은 첫 번째 물음은 무엇이겠는가 하고 말입니다. 그 결과가 어떠할지 우리는 너무나 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["Examine her privately, or examine her publicly, she is utterly incapable of assisting the assertion of her own case.", "그녀가 런던에서 자기에게 일어난 일에 대한 기억을 끝내 되찾지 못했다는 것을 알기 때문입니다. 사사로이 캐묻든 공개적으로 캐묻든, 그녀는 자기 사건의 주장을 거들 능력이 전혀 없습니다."], ["If you don’t see this, Marian, as plainly as I see it, we will go to Limmeridge and try the experiment to-morrow.”", "매리언, 이것이 내가 보는 만큼 훤히 당신 눈에 보이지 않는다면, 우리는 내일 당장 리머리지로 가서 그 시험을 해봅시다.”"]], [["“I DO see it, Walter.", "“나도 훤히 보여요, 월터."], ["Even if we had the means of paying all the law expenses, even if we succeeded in the end, the delays would be unendurable, the perpetual suspense, after what we have suffered already, would be heartbreaking.", "설령 그 모든 소송 비용을 댈 방도가 있다 해도, 설령 끝내 이긴다 해도, 그 지체는 견딜 수 없을 거예요. 이미 겪을 만큼 겪은 뒤에 끝없이 이어질 그 애태움은 가슴이 찢어지는 일일 거고요."], ["You are right about the hopelessness of going to Limmeridge.", "리머리지로 가는 것이 가망 없다는 당신 말이 옳아요."], ["I wish I could feel sure that you are right also in determining to try that last chance with the Count.", "백작을 상대로 그 마지막 기회를 걸어보겠다는 결심에서도 당신이 옳다는 확신이 들면 좋겠는데."], ["IS it a chance at all?”", "그게 대체 기회이기는 한가요?”"]], [["“Beyond a doubt, Yes.", "“의심할 여지 없이, 그렇습니다."], ["It is the chance of recovering the lost date of Laura’s journey to London.", "그것은 로라가 런던으로 간 날짜, 그 잃어버린 날짜를 되찾을 기회입니다."], ["Without returning to the reasons I gave you some time since, I am still as firmly persuaded as ever that there is a discrepancy between the date of that journey and the date on the certificate of death.", "얼마 전에 당신에게 말했던 근거들을 다시 늘어놓지 않더라도, 나는 그 여정의 날짜와 사망 진단서에 적힌 날짜 사이에 어긋남이 있다고 여전히 처음처럼 굳게 믿습니다."], ["There lies the weak point of the whole conspiracy—it crumbles to pieces if we attack it in that way, and the means of attacking it are in possession of the Count.", "음모 전체의 약한 고리가 바로 거기에 있습니다—그쪽으로 치고 들어가면 그것은 산산이 무너집니다. 그리고 그것을 칠 수단은 백작이 쥐고 있습니다."], ["If I succeed in wresting them from him, the object of your life and mine is fulfilled.", "내가 그자에게서 그것을 빼앗아내는 데 성공한다면, 당신과 내 삶의 목적은 이루어집니다."], ["If I fail, the wrong that Laura has suffered will, in this world, never be redressed.”", "내가 실패한다면, 로라가 당한 그 부당함은 이 세상에서 결코 바로잡히지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Do you fear failure yourself, Walter?”", "“당신 자신은 실패가 두렵나요, 월터?”"]], [["“I dare not anticipate success, and for that very reason, Marian, I speak openly and plainly as I have spoken now.", "“나는 감히 성공을 미리 점치지 못합니다. 그리고 바로 그 때문에, 매리언, 지금처럼 이렇게 터놓고 꾸밈없이 말하는 겁니다."], ["In my heart and my conscience I can say it, Laura’s hopes for the future are at their lowest ebb.", "내 마음으로도 내 양심으로도 이렇게 말할 수 있습니다. 앞날에 대한 로라의 희망은 지금 가장 밑바닥까지 내려가 있습니다."], ["I know that her fortune is gone—I know that the last chance of restoring her to her place in the world lies at the mercy of her worst enemy, of a man who is now absolutely unassailable, and who may remain unassailable to the end.", "그녀의 재산이 사라졌다는 것을 나는 압니다—그녀를 세상에서 제자리로 되돌려놓을 마지막 기회가 그녀의 가장 악랄한 원수의 손에, 지금으로서는 도무지 건드릴 수 없고 어쩌면 끝까지 건드릴 수 없을지도 모를 한 사내의 손에 놓여 있다는 것을 압니다."], ["With every worldly advantage gone from her, with all prospect of recovering her rank and station more than doubtful, with no clearer future before her than the future which her husband can provide, the poor drawing-master may harmlessly open his heart at last.", "세속의 온갖 이점이 그녀에게서 다 사라지고, 지위와 신분을 되찾을 가망이라고는 몹시도 미덥지 못하고, 남편이 마련해줄 수 있는 앞날 말고는 더 밝은 앞날이랄 것이 없는 지금이라면, 가난한 그림 선생도 마침내 아무 해될 것 없이 제 마음을 열어 보여도 되겠지요."], ["In the days of her prosperity, Marian, I was only the teacher who guided her hand—I ask for it, in her adversity, as the hand of my wife!”", "그녀가 부유하던 시절, 매리언, 나는 그저 그녀의 손을 이끌어주던 선생일 뿐이었습니다—이제 그녀가 곤궁해진 지금, 나는 그 손을 내 아내의 손으로 달라고 청하는 겁니다!”"]], [["Marian’s eyes met mine affectionately—I could say no more.", "매리언의 눈이 다정하게 내 눈과 마주쳤다—나는 더 말할 수 없었다."], ["My heart was full, my lips were trembling.", "가슴이 벅찼고 입술이 떨렸다."], ["In spite of myself I was in danger of appealing to her pity.", "나도 모르게 그녀의 연민에 호소할 지경에 이르러 있었다."], ["I got up to leave the room.", "나는 방을 나가려고 일어섰다."], ["She rose at the same moment, laid her hand gently on my shoulder, and stopped me.", "그녀도 그 순간 함께 일어나 내 어깨에 가만히 손을 얹고 나를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Walter!”", "“월터!”"], ["she said, “I once parted you both, for your good and for hers.", "그녀가 말했다. “나는 한때 당신을 위해서, 또 그 애를 위해서 두 사람을 갈라놓았어요."], ["Wait here, my brother!—wait, my dearest, best friend, till Laura comes, and tells you what I have done now!”", "여기서 기다려요, 내 동생!—기다려요, 나의 더없이 소중하고 가장 좋은 벗이여, 로라가 와서 내가 이번에 무슨 일을 했는지 당신에게 말해줄 때까지.”"]], [["For the first time since the farewell morning at Limmeridge she touched my forehead with her lips.", "리머리지에서 작별하던 그 아침 이후 처음으로 그녀는 내 이마에 입술을 대었다."], ["A tear dropped on my face as she kissed me.", "그녀가 입 맞출 때 눈물 한 방울이 내 얼굴에 떨어졌다."], ["She turned quickly, pointed to the chair from which I had risen, and left the room.", "그녀는 얼른 몸을 돌려 내가 일어섰던 의자를 가리키고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["I sat down alone at the window to wait through the crisis of my life.", "나는 내 인생의 고비를 홀로 견디려고 창가에 앉았다."], ["My mind in that breathless interval felt like a total blank.", "숨죽인 그 시간 동안 내 머릿속은 온통 텅 빈 것 같았다."], ["I was conscious of nothing but a painful intensity of all familiar perceptions.", "익숙한 감각이란 감각이 모두 고통스러울 만큼 예민해졌다는 것 말고는 아무것도 의식하지 못했다."], ["The sun grew blinding bright, the white sea birds chasing each other far beyond me seemed to be flitting before my face, the mellow murmur of the waves on the beach was like thunder in my ears.", "햇빛은 눈이 멀 만큼 밝아졌고, 저 멀리 서로를 쫓아다니는 흰 바닷새들은 내 얼굴 앞에서 파닥이는 듯했으며, 모래밭에 부딪는 파도의 부드러운 속삭임은 귓속에서 천둥처럼 울렸다."]], [["The door opened, and Laura came in alone.", "문이 열리고 로라가 혼자 들어왔다."], ["So she had entered the breakfast-room at Limmeridge House on the morning when we parted.", "우리가 헤어지던 그 아침에도 그녀는 리머리지 하우스의 아침 식사실로 그렇게 들어왔었다."], ["Slowly and falteringly, in sorrow and in hesitation, she had once approached me.", "그때는 슬픔과 망설임 속에서 느릿느릿 더듬거리며 나에게 다가왔다."], ["Now she came with the haste of happiness in her feet, with the light of happiness radiant in her face.", "이제 그녀는 행복으로 서두르는 걸음으로, 얼굴에 행복의 빛을 환히 담고서 왔다."], ["Of their own accord those dear arms clasped themselves round me, of their own accord the sweet lips came to meet mine.", "그 사랑스러운 두 팔이 저절로 나를 감싸 안았고, 그 다정한 입술이 저절로 내 입술을 찾아왔다. “내 사랑,”"], ["“My darling!”", "그녀가 속삭였다."], ["she whispered, “we may own we love each other now?”", "“우리 이제 서로 사랑한다고 말해도 되는 거죠?”"], ["Her head nestled with a tender contentedness on my bosom.", "그녀의 머리가 부드러운 만족감에 잠겨 내 가슴에 파묻혔다."], ["“Oh,”", "“아,”"], ["she said innocently, “I am so happy at last!”", "그녀가 천진하게 말했다. “나 이제야 정말 행복해요!”"]], [["Ten days later we were happier still.", "열흘 뒤 우리는 한층 더 행복했다."], ["We were married.", "우리는 부부가 되었다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["The course of this narrative, steadily flowing on, bears me away from the morning-time of our married life, and carries me forward to the end.", "이 이야기의 흐름은 꾸준히 앞으로 나아가며, 나를 우리 결혼 생활의 아침으로부터 데리고 나와 그 끝을 향해 실어 간다."]], [["In a fortnight more we three were back in London, and the shadow was stealing over us of the struggle to come.", "다시 두 주가 지나 우리 세 사람은 런던으로 돌아와 있었고, 다가올 싸움의 그림자가 우리 위로 조용히 덮여 오고 있었다."]], [["Marian and I were careful to keep Laura in ignorance of the cause that had hurried us back—the necessity of making sure of the Count.", "매리언과 나는 우리를 서둘러 돌아오게 만든 이유를 — 백작의 소재를 확실히 해 두어야 한다는 필요를 — 로라가 모르도록 조심했다."], ["It was now the beginning of May, and his term of occupation at the house in Forest Road expired in June.", "때는 5월 초였고, 포리스트 로드의 그 집에 대한 그의 거주 기한은 6월에 만료될 예정이었다."], ["If he renewed it (and I had reasons, shortly to be mentioned, for anticipating that he would), I might be certain of his not escaping me.", "그가 기한을 갱신한다면(그리고 나는 곧 이야기할 몇 가지 이유로 그러리라 예상하고 있었다), 그가 내 손에서 빠져나가지 못하리라 확신할 수 있었다."], ["But if by any chance he disappointed my expectations and left the country, then I had no time to lose in arming myself to meet him as I best might.", "그러나 혹시라도 그가 내 예상을 저버리고 이 나라를 떠난다면, 나는 그를 맞아 싸울 무장을 갖추는 데 한시도 허비할 수 없었다."]], [["In the first fulness of my new happiness, there had been moments when my resolution faltered—moments when I was tempted to be safely content, now that the dearest aspiration of my life was fulfilled in the possession of Laura’s love.", "새로운 행복이 처음으로 벅차게 차오르던 무렵, 내 결심이 흔들리던 순간들이 있었다 — 내 생애의 가장 간절한 소망이 로라의 사랑을 얻음으로써 이루어졌으니 이제 안전하게 만족해 버리자는 유혹에 빠지던 순간들이."], ["For the first time I thought faint-heartedly of the greatness of the risk, of the adverse chances arrayed against me, of the fair promise of our new life, and of the peril in which I might place the happiness which we had so hardly earned. Yes!", "나는 난생처음으로 그 위험의 크기를, 나를 상대로 늘어선 불리한 승산을, 우리 새 삶의 아름다운 약속을, 그리고 그토록 어렵게 얻어 낸 행복을 내가 몰아넣을지도 모를 위태로움을 겁먹은 마음으로 헤아렸다."], ["let me own it honestly.", "그렇다! 정직하게 인정하자."], ["For a brief time I wandered, in the sweet guiding of love, far from the purpose to which I had been true under sterner discipline and in darker days.", "짧은 동안 나는 사랑의 달콤한 인도에 이끌려, 더 혹독한 단련 아래 더 어두운 나날 속에서 지켜 왔던 그 목적으로부터 멀리 헤매었다."], ["Innocently Laura had tempted me aside from the hard path—innocently she was destined to lead me back again.", "로라는 순진하게도 나를 그 험한 길에서 비켜서게 했고 — 순진하게도 그녀는 나를 다시 그 길로 데려다 놓을 운명이었다."]], [["At times, dreams of the terrible past still disconnectedly recalled to her, in the mystery of sleep, the events of which her waking memory had lost all trace.", "때때로 끔찍한 과거의 꿈들이 잠의 신비 속에서, 깨어 있는 기억으로는 흔적조차 잃어버린 사건들을 그녀에게 두서없이 되살려 놓곤 했다."], ["One night (barely two weeks after our marriage), when I was watching her at rest, I saw the tears come slowly through her closed eyelids, I heard the faint murmuring words escape her which told me that her spirit was back again on the fatal journey from Blackwater Park.", "어느 밤(결혼한 지 겨우 두 주밖에 지나지 않았을 때), 잠든 그녀를 지켜보고 있던 나는 감긴 눈꺼풀 사이로 눈물이 천천히 배어 나오는 것을 보았고, 그녀의 넋이 다시 블랙워터 파크에서 시작된 그 운명의 여정으로 돌아가 있음을 알려 주는 희미한 중얼거림을 들었다."], ["That unconscious appeal, so touching and so awful in the sacredness of her sleep, ran through me like fire.", "잠의 신성함 속에서 그토록 애처롭고 그토록 두렵던 그 무의식의 호소가 불길처럼 나를 관통했다."], ["The next day was the day we came back to London—the day when my resolution returned to me with tenfold strength.", "그 이튿날이 우리가 런던으로 돌아온 날이었다 — 내 결심이 열 배의 힘으로 내게 돌아온 날이었다."]], [["The first necessity was to know something of the man.", "가장 먼저 필요한 일은 그 사내에 관해 무엇이든 알아내는 것이었다."], ["Thus far, the true story of his life was an impenetrable mystery to me.", "그때까지 그의 생애의 참된 내력은 내게 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 수수께끼였다."]], [["I began with such scanty sources of information as were at my own disposal.", "나는 내 손안에 있는 빈약한 정보원들부터 시작했다."], ["The important narrative written by Mr. Frederick Fairlie (which Marian had obtained by following the directions I had given to her in the winter) proved to be of no service to the special object with which I now looked at it.", "프레더릭 페얼리 씨가 쓴 그 중요한 기록은(매리언이 지난겨울 내가 일러 준 지시를 따라 얻어 낸 것이었다) 지금 내가 그것을 들여다보는 특별한 목적에는 아무 도움도 되지 않는 것으로 드러났다."], ["While reading it I reconsidered the disclosure revealed to me by Mrs. Clements of the series of deceptions which had brought Anne Catherick to London, and which had there devoted her to the interests of the conspiracy.", "그것을 읽으면서 나는 클레멘츠 부인이 내게 밝혀 준 사실을 — 앤 캐서릭을 런던으로 데려왔고 거기서 그녀를 음모의 이해에 바쳐 버린 일련의 기만들을 — 다시 헤아려 보았다."], ["Here, again, the Count had not openly committed himself—here, again, he was, to all practical purpose, out of my reach.", "여기서도 역시 백작은 드러내 놓고 자신을 얽어매지 않았다 — 여기서도 역시 그는 실질적으로 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었다."]], [["I next returned to Marian’s journal at Blackwater Park.", "다음으로 나는 블랙워터 파크에서 쓰인 매리언의 일기로 돌아갔다."], ["At my request she read to me again a passage which referred to her past curiosity about the Count, and to the few particulars which she had discovered relating to him.", "내 청에 따라 그녀는 백작에 대한 지난날의 호기심과 그에 관해 알아낸 몇 가지 세부를 적어 둔 대목을 내게 다시 읽어 주었다."]], [["The passage to which I allude occurs in that part of her journal which delineates his character and his personal appearance.", "내가 말하는 그 대목은 그의 성격과 외모를 그려 낸 부분에 나온다."], ["She describes him as “not having crossed the frontiers of his native country for years past”—as “anxious to know if any Italian gentlemen were settled in the nearest town to Blackwater Park”—as “receiving letters with all sorts of odd stamps on them, and one with a large official-looking seal on it.”", "그녀는 그를 이렇게 적고 있다 — “여러 해 동안 고국의 국경을 넘어 본 일이 없다”고, “블랙워터 파크에서 가장 가까운 읍내에 정착해 사는 이탈리아 신사가 있는지 몹시 알고 싶어 한다”고, “온갖 기묘한 소인이 찍힌 편지들을 받는데, 그중 하나에는 크고 관청 것처럼 보이는 봉인이 찍혀 있었다”고."], ["She is inclined to consider that his long absence from his native country may be accounted for by assuming that he is a political exile.", "그녀는 그가 고국을 그토록 오래 떠나 있는 까닭을 그가 정치적 망명자라고 가정하면 설명할 수 있으리라는 쪽으로 기운다."], ["But she is, on the other hand, unable to reconcile this idea with the reception of the letter from abroad bearing “the large official-looking seal”—letters from the Continent addressed to political exiles being usually the last to court attention from foreign post-offices in that way.", "그러나 다른 한편으로 그녀는 그 생각을, “크고 관청 것처럼 보이는 봉인”이 찍힌 그 외국 편지를 받았다는 사실과는 도무지 조화시키지 못한다 — 대륙에서 정치적 망명자 앞으로 보내는 편지야말로 그런 식으로 외국 우체국의 주목을 사려 들지 않는 법이기 때문이다."]], [["The considerations thus presented to me in the diary, joined to certain surmises of my own that grew out of them, suggested a conclusion which I wondered I had not arrived at before.", "일기가 이렇게 내 앞에 내놓은 정황들에, 거기서 자라난 내 나름의 몇 가지 추측이 더해지자, 어째서 진작 이르지 못했을까 싶은 결론 하나가 떠올랐다."], ["I now said to myself—what Laura had once said to Marian at Blackwater Park, what Madame Fosco had overheard by listening at the door—the Count is a spy!", "이제 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다 — 언젠가 로라가 블랙워터 파크에서 매리언에게 했던 그 말, 포스코 백작부인이 문에 귀를 대고 엿들었던 그 말을. 백작은 첩자다!"]], [["Laura had applied the word to him at hazard, in natural anger at his proceedings towards herself.", "로라는 자신에게 저지른 그의 처사에 대한 당연한 분노에서 되는대로 그 말을 그에게 붙였을 뿐이다."], ["I applied it to him with the deliberate conviction that his vocation in life was the vocation of a spy.", "나는 그의 생업이 첩자의 생업이라는 확고한 신념을 가지고 그 말을 그에게 붙였다."], ["On this assumption, the reason for his extraordinary stay in England so long after the objects of the conspiracy had been gained, became, to my mind, quite intelligible.", "이 가정 위에 서고 보니, 음모의 목적이 이미 달성된 뒤에도 그가 그토록 오래 영국에 머무는 그 기이한 일의 이유가 내게는 아주 이해할 만한 것이 되었다."]], [["The year of which I am now writing was the year of the famous Crystal Palace Exhibition in Hyde Park.", "내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그해는 하이드 파크에서 저 유명한 수정궁 박람회가 열린 해였다."], ["Foreigners in unusually large numbers had arrived already, and were still arriving in England.", "외국인들이 이미 유례없이 많이 들어와 있었고, 지금도 계속 영국으로 들어오는 중이었다."], ["Men were among us by hundreds whom the ceaseless distrustfulness of their governments had followed privately, by means of appointed agents, to our shores.", "제 나라 정부의 그칠 줄 모르는 의심이 지정된 요원들을 통해 은밀히 뒤를 밟아 우리 해안까지 따라온 사람들이 수백 명 단위로 우리 가운데 있었다."], ["My surmises did not for a moment class a man of the Count’s abilities and social position with the ordinary rank and file of foreign spies.", "내 추측은 백작만 한 능력과 사회적 지위를 지닌 인물을 여느 외국인 첩자들의 하급 무리와 한 부류로 묶은 것이 결코 아니었다."], ["I suspected him of holding a position of authority, of being entrusted by the government which he secretly served with the organisation and management of agents specially employed in this country, both men and women, and I believed Mrs. Rubelle, who had been so opportunely found to act as nurse at Blackwater Park, to be, in all probability, one of the number.", "나는 그가 권한 있는 자리를 차지하고 있으리라고, 그가 은밀히 섬기는 정부로부터 이 나라에서 특별히 부리는 남녀 요원들을 조직하고 관리하는 일을 맡고 있으리라고 의심했으며, 블랙워터 파크에서 간호인 노릇을 하도록 그토록 때맞춰 구해졌던 루벨 부인도 십중팔구 그 무리 가운데 하나이리라 믿었다."]], [["Assuming that this idea of mine had a foundation in truth, the position of the Count might prove to be more assailable than I had hitherto ventured to hope.", "이 생각이 사실에 근거를 두고 있다고 가정한다면, 백작의 처지는 내가 지금껏 감히 바라던 것보다 더 공략하기 쉬운 것으로 드러날지도 몰랐다."], ["To whom could I apply to know something more of the man’s history and of the man himself than I knew now?", "그 사내의 내력과 그 사내 자신에 대해 지금 내가 아는 것보다 더 많이 알려면 나는 누구를 찾아가야 하는가?"]], [["In this emergency it naturally occurred to my mind that a countryman of his own, on whom I could rely, might be the fittest person to help me.", "이 다급한 처지에서 자연히 내 머리에 떠오른 것은, 내가 믿을 수 있는 그의 동포야말로 나를 도울 가장 알맞은 사람이리라는 생각이었다."], ["The first man whom I thought of under these circumstances was also the only Italian with whom I was intimately acquainted—my quaint little friend, Professor Pesca.", "이런 사정에서 내가 맨 먼저 떠올린 사람은 또한 내가 친밀하게 알고 지내는 단 한 사람의 이탈리아인이었다 — 나의 별난 작은 친구, 페스카 교수였다."]], [["The professor has been so long absent from these pages that he has run some risk of being forgotten altogether.", "교수는 이 지면에서 너무도 오래 자리를 비운 탓에, 아예 잊혀 버릴 위험까지 무릅쓴 셈이 되었다."]], [["It is the necessary law of such a story as mine that the persons concerned in it only appear when the course of events takes them up—they come and go, not by favour of my personal partiality, but by right of their direct connection with the circumstances to be detailed.", "내 이야기 같은 것에는 어쩔 수 없는 법칙이 있으니, 거기 관련된 사람들은 사건의 흐름이 그들을 집어 올릴 때에만 나타난다는 것이다 — 그들은 내 개인적 편애의 은혜로가 아니라, 낱낱이 적어야 할 정황과 곧바로 이어져 있다는 권리에 따라 오고 간다."], ["For this reason, not Pesca alone, but my mother and sister as well, have been left far in the background of the narrative.", "그런 까닭에 페스카뿐 아니라 내 어머니와 누이도 이 이야기의 훨씬 뒤편에 남겨져 왔다."], ["My visits to the Hampstead cottage, my mother’s belief in the denial of Laura’s identity which the conspiracy had accomplished, my vain efforts to overcome the prejudice on her part and on my sister’s to which, in their jealous affection for me, they both continued to adhere, the painful necessity which that prejudice imposed on me of concealing my marriage from them till they had learnt to do justice to my wife—all these little domestic occurrences have been left unrecorded because they were not essential to the main interest of the story.", "햄스테드 오두막을 찾아가던 일, 음모가 이루어 낸 로라의 신원 부인을 어머니가 그대로 믿고 있던 일, 나를 아끼는 질투 어린 애정에서 어머니와 누이가 한결같이 붙들고 놓지 않던 그 편견을 깨뜨리려던 내 헛된 노력, 그리고 그 편견 탓에 두 사람이 내 아내를 정당하게 대할 줄 알게 되기까지 내 결혼을 숨겨야만 했던 괴로운 처지 — 이 모든 자잘한 집안일들은 이야기의 주된 관심에 꼭 필요하지 않았기에 적히지 않은 채로 남았다."], ["It is nothing that they added to my anxieties and embittered my disappointments—the steady march of events has inexorably passed them by.", "그것들이 내 근심을 더하고 내 실망을 쓰라리게 했다 한들 아무 상관이 없다 — 사건의 꾸준한 행진은 가차 없이 그것들을 지나쳐 갔다."]], [["For the same reason I have said nothing here of the consolation that I found in Pesca’s brotherly affection for me, when I saw him again after the sudden cessation of my residence at Limmeridge House.", "같은 이유로 나는 리머리지 하우스에서의 체류가 갑작스레 끝난 뒤 페스카를 다시 만났을 때 그의 형제 같은 애정에서 얻은 위안에 대해서도 여기 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["I have not recorded the fidelity with which my warm-hearted little friend followed me to the place of embarkation when I sailed for Central America, or the noisy transport of joy with which he received me when we next met in London.", "내가 중앙아메리카로 떠날 때 그 다정한 작은 친구가 얼마나 한결같이 승선지까지 나를 따라왔는지도, 다음번 런던에서 다시 만났을 때 그가 얼마나 요란한 기쁨으로 나를 맞았는지도 적지 않았다."], ["If I had felt justified in accepting the offers of service which he made to me on my return, he would have appeared again long ere this.", "내가 돌아왔을 때 그가 내놓은 도움의 제안을 받아들여도 좋다고 여겼더라면, 그는 진작에 다시 이 지면에 등장했을 것이다."], ["But, though I knew that his honour and his courage were to be implicitly relied on, I was not so sure that his discretion was to be trusted, and, for that reason only, I followed the course of all my inquiries alone.", "그러나 그의 명예심과 용기는 온전히 기댈 만하다는 것을 알면서도, 그의 신중함까지 믿어도 좋을지는 그만큼 확신이 서지 않았고, 오직 그 이유 하나 때문에 나는 모든 조사를 혼자서 밀고 나갔다."], ["It will now be sufficiently understood that Pesca was not separated from all connection with me and my interests, although he has hitherto been separated from all connection with the progress of this narrative.", "페스카가 지금까지 이 이야기의 진행과는 아무 연결도 없이 떨어져 있었을 뿐, 나와 내 일과의 연결에서까지 떨어져 있었던 것은 아님을 이제 충분히 이해할 것이다."], ["He was as true and as ready a friend of mine still as ever he had been in his life.", "그는 여전히, 평생 그러했던 그대로 진실하고 선뜻 나서는 내 친구였다."]], [["Before I summoned Pesca to my assistance it was necessary to see for myself what sort of man I had to deal with.", "페스카를 불러 도움을 청하기 전에, 내가 상대해야 할 자가 어떤 인간인지 내 눈으로 직접 확인할 필요가 있었다."], ["Up to this time I had never once set eyes on Count Fosco.", "그때까지 나는 포스코 백작을 단 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Three days after my return with Laura and Marian to London, I set forth alone for Forest Road, St. John’s Wood, between ten and eleven o’clock in the morning.", "로라와 매리언과 함께 런던으로 돌아온 지 사흘째 되던 날, 나는 아침 열 시와 열한 시 사이에 혼자 세인트존스우드의 포리스트 로드로 나섰다."], ["It was a fine day—I had some hours to spare—and I thought it likely, if I waited a little for him, that the Count might be tempted out.", "날은 화창했고 — 내게는 몇 시간의 여유가 있었으며 — 조금 기다리다 보면 백작이 밖으로 나오도록 이끌릴 법도 하다고 생각했다."], ["I had no great reason to fear the chance of his recognising me in the daytime, for the only occasion when I had been seen by him was the occasion on which he had followed me home at night.", "낮에 그가 나를 알아볼 가능성은 그다지 두려워할 이유가 없었으니, 그가 나를 본 유일한 경우는 그가 밤중에 내 집까지 나를 뒤따라왔던 그때뿐이었기 때문이다."]], [["No one appeared at the windows in the front of the house.", "집 앞쪽 창문에는 아무도 나타나지 않았다."], ["I walked down a turning which ran past the side of it, and looked over the low garden wall.", "나는 그 집 옆을 따라 지나는 골목으로 걸어 내려가 낮은 정원 담 너머를 넘겨다보았다."], ["One of the back windows on the lower floor was thrown up and a net was stretched across the opening.", "아래층 뒤쪽 창문 하나가 밀어 올려져 있었고, 그 열린 곳에는 그물이 쳐져 있었다."], ["I saw nobody, but I heard, in the room, first a shrill whistling and singing of birds, then the deep ringing voice which Marian’s description had made familiar to me.", "아무도 보이지 않았으나, 방 안에서 처음에는 새들의 날카로운 휘파람 소리와 노랫소리가, 이어서 매리언의 묘사로 내게 익숙해진 그 깊고 울리는 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["“Come out on my little finger, my pret-pret-pretties!”", "“내 새끼손가락 위로 나오너라, 나의 예-예-예쁜이들아!”"], ["cried the voice.", "그 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["“Come out and hop upstairs!", "“나와서 위로 깡충 뛰어라!"], ["One, two, three—and up!", "하나, 둘, 셋 — 올라가!"], ["Three, two, one—and down!", "셋, 둘, 하나 — 내려와!"], ["One, two, three—twit-twit-twit-tweet!”", "하나, 둘, 셋 — 짹-짹-짹-찌잇!”"], ["The Count was exercising his canaries as he used to exercise them in Marian’s time at Blackwater Park.", "백작은 매리언이 블랙워터 파크에 있던 시절에 그러했듯 카나리아들을 훈련시키고 있었다."]], [["I waited a little while, and the singing and the whistling ceased.", "나는 잠시 기다렸고, 노랫소리와 휘파람 소리가 그쳤다."], ["“Come, kiss me, my pretties!” said the deep voice.", "“자, 내게 입 맞추어라, 나의 예쁜이들아!”"], ["There was a responsive twittering and chirping—a low, oily laugh—a silence of a minute or so, and then I heard the opening of the house door.", "그 깊은 목소리가 말했다. 이에 답하는 지저귐과 짹짹거림이 있었고 — 낮고 기름진 웃음소리가 나더니 — 한 일 분쯤의 침묵이 이어졌으며, 그러고 나서 나는 현관문이 열리는 소리를 들었다."], ["I turned and retraced my steps.", "나는 몸을 돌려 왔던 길을 되짚었다."], ["The magnificent melody of the Prayer in Rossini’s Moses, sung in a sonorous bass voice, rose grandly through the suburban silence of the place.", "로시니의 「모세」에 나오는 저 장엄한 기도의 선율이 우렁찬 저음으로 불려, 그 교외의 고요를 뚫고 웅장하게 솟아올랐다."], ["The front garden gate opened and closed.", "앞뜰 문이 열렸다가 닫혔다."], ["The Count had come out.", "백작이 나온 것이다."]], [["He crossed the road and walked towards the western boundary of the Regent’s Park.", "그는 길을 건너 리젠트 파크의 서쪽 경계를 향해 걸어갔다."], ["I kept on my own side of the way, a little behind him, and walked in that direction also.", "나는 내 쪽 길가에 붙어 그보다 조금 뒤처진 채, 나 역시 같은 방향으로 걸었다."]], [["Marian had prepared me for his high stature, his monstrous corpulence, and his ostentatious mourning garments, but not for the horrible freshness and cheerfulness and vitality of the man.", "매리언은 그의 큰 키와 괴물 같은 비대함과 요란한 상복 차림에 대해서는 나를 준비시켜 주었으나, 그 사내의 소름 끼치도록 싱싱하고 쾌활하고 활기 넘치는 모습에 대해서는 그러지 못했다."], ["He carried his sixty years as if they had been fewer than forty.", "그는 예순 해의 나이를 마흔 해도 채 안 되는 것처럼 지고 다녔다."], ["He sauntered along, wearing his hat a little on one side, with a light jaunty step, swinging his big stick, humming to himself, looking up from time to time at the houses and gardens on either side of him with superb, smiling patronage.", "그는 모자를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울여 쓰고 가볍고 경쾌한 걸음으로 큰 지팡이를 흔들며 혼자 흥얼거리면서, 이따금 양옆의 집들과 정원들을 당당하고 미소 띤 시혜의 눈길로 올려다보며 어슬렁어슬렁 걸었다."], ["If a stranger had been told that the whole neighbourhood belonged to him, that stranger would not have been surprised to hear it.", "어느 낯선 이에게 이 동네 전체가 저 사람 것이라고 일러 준다 해도, 그 낯선 이는 그 말을 듣고 놀라지 않았을 것이다."], ["He never looked back, he paid no apparent attention to me, no apparent attention to any one who passed him on his own side of the road, except now and then, when he smiled and smirked, with an easy paternal good humour, at the nursery-maids and the children whom he met.", "그는 결코 뒤를 돌아보지 않았고, 내게도 겉으로는 아무 주의를 기울이지 않았으며, 제 쪽 길에서 그를 스쳐 지나가는 어느 누구에게도 겉으로는 아무 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 다만 이따금 마주치는 보모들과 아이들을 향해 넉넉한 아버지 같은 좋은 기분으로 웃음 짓고 히죽거릴 때만은 예외였다."], ["In this way he led me on, till we reached a colony of shops outside the western terraces of the Park.", "이런 식으로 그는 나를 이끌고 나아갔고, 마침내 우리는 공원 서쪽 연립 주택가 바깥에 늘어선 상점 무리에 이르렀다."]], [["Here he stopped at a pastrycook’s, went in (probably to give an order), and came out again immediately with a tart in his hand.", "여기서 그는 어느 제과점 앞에 멈추어 안으로 들어갔다가(아마 주문을 하려는 것이었으리라), 곧바로 타르트 하나를 손에 들고 다시 나왔다."], ["An Italian was grinding an organ before the shop, and a miserable little shrivelled monkey was sitting on the instrument.", "이탈리아인 하나가 가게 앞에서 손풍금을 돌리고 있었고, 비참하게 쪼그라든 작은 원숭이 한 마리가 그 악기 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["The Count stopped, bit a piece for himself out of the tart, and gravely handed the rest to the monkey.", "백작은 걸음을 멈추고 타르트를 제 몫으로 한 입 베어 문 뒤, 남은 것을 엄숙하게 원숭이에게 건넸다."], ["“My poor little man!”", "“가엾은 나의 작은 친구여!”"], ["he said, with grotesque tenderness, “you look hungry.", "그가 기괴한 다정함으로 말했다. “배가 고파 보이는구나."], ["In the sacred name of humanity, I offer you some lunch!”", "인류애의 거룩한 이름으로, 내 그대에게 점심을 좀 대접하노라!”"], ["The organ-grinder piteously put in his claim to a penny from the benevolent stranger.", "손풍금장이는 애처로운 몸짓으로 이 자비로운 낯선 이에게 한 푼만 달라고 청했다."], ["The Count shrugged his shoulders contemptuously, and passed on.", "백작은 경멸하듯 어깨를 으쓱하고는 그대로 지나쳐 갔다."]], [["We reached the streets and the better class of shops between the New Road and Oxford Street.", "우리는 뉴 로드와 옥스퍼드 스트리트 사이의 거리들과 한결 나은 부류의 상점들에 이르렀다."], ["The Count stopped again and entered a small optician’s shop, with an inscription in the window announcing that repairs were neatly executed inside.", "백작은 다시 걸음을 멈추고 자그마한 안경점으로 들어갔는데, 그 진열창에는 수리를 정갈하게 해 드린다는 문구가 붙어 있었다."], ["He came out again with an opera-glass in his hand, walked a few paces on, and stopped to look at a bill of the opera placed outside a music-seller’s shop.", "그는 오페라글라스를 손에 들고 다시 나와 몇 걸음 걷다가, 어느 악보상 앞에 내걸린 오페라 광고를 보려고 멈추어 섰다."], ["He read the bill attentively, considered a moment, and then hailed an empty cab as it passed him.", "그는 그 광고를 꼼꼼히 읽고 잠시 생각하더니, 마침 곁을 지나가는 빈 마차를 불러 세웠다."], ["“Opera Box-office,”", "“오페라 매표소로.”"], ["he said to the man, and was driven away.", "그가 마부에게 말하고는 그대로 실려 갔다."]], [["I crossed the road, and looked at the bill in my turn.", "나는 길을 건너 이번에는 내가 그 광고를 들여다보았다."], ["The performance announced was Lucrezia Borgia, and it was to take place that evening.", "예고된 공연은 「루크레치아 보르자」였고, 바로 그날 저녁에 열릴 예정이었다."], ["The opera-glass in the Count’s hand, his careful reading of the bill, and his direction to the cabman, all suggested that he proposed making one of the audience.", "백작의 손에 들린 오페라글라스와, 그가 광고를 꼼꼼히 읽던 모습과, 마부에게 이른 행선지가 모두 그가 그 관객 가운데 한 사람이 될 작정임을 일러 주고 있었다."], ["I had the means of getting an admission for myself and a friend to the pit by applying to one of the scene-painters attached to the theatre, with whom I had been well acquainted in past times.", "나는 예전에 잘 알고 지내던, 그 극장에 딸린 무대 화가 한 사람에게 부탁하면 나와 친구 한 사람 몫의 일층 뒷자리 입장권을 구할 방도가 있었다."], ["There was a chance at least that the Count might be easily visible among the audience to me and to any one with me, and in this case I had the means of ascertaining whether Pesca knew his countryman or not that very night.", "적어도 관객 가운데 있는 백작이 나에게도, 나와 함께 있는 사람에게도 쉽게 눈에 띌 기회는 있는 셈이었고, 그렇다면 바로 그날 밤에 페스카가 그 동포를 아는지 모르는지 확인할 방도가 내게 있었다."]], [["This consideration at once decided the disposal of my evening.", "이 생각으로 내 저녁 일정은 곧바로 정해졌다."], ["I procured the tickets, leaving a note at the Professor’s lodgings on the way.", "나는 표를 구했고, 가는 길에 교수의 하숙집에 쪽지를 남겼다."], ["At a quarter to eight I called to take him with me to the theatre.", "여덟 시 십오 분 전에 나는 그를 극장으로 데려가려고 그를 찾아갔다."], ["My little friend was in a state of the highest excitement, with a festive flower in his button-hole, and the largest opera-glass I ever saw hugged up under his arm.", "나의 작은 친구는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 상태였으며, 단춧구멍에 축제용 꽃을 꽂고 내가 여태 본 것 중 가장 큰 오페라글라스를 겨드랑이에 꼭 끼고 있었다."]], [["“Are you ready?”", "“준비되셨습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Right-all-right,”", "“좋아-다-좋아!”"], ["said Pesca.", "페스카가 말했다."]], [["We started for the theatre.", "우리는 극장을 향해 출발했다."]], [["# V", "# V"]], [["The last notes of the introduction to the opera were being played, and the seats in the pit were all filled, when Pesca and I reached the theatre.", "우리가 극장에 닿았을 때는 오페라 서곡의 마지막 음들이 연주되는 중이었고, 일층 평석의 자리는 이미 빈틈없이 들어차 있었다."]], [["There was plenty of room, however, in the passage that ran round the pit—precisely the position best calculated to answer the purpose for which I was attending the performance.", "그러나 평석을 빙 둘러 나 있는 통로에는 자리가 넉넉했다. 내가 그날 공연에 온 목적을 이루기에는 바로 그 자리가 더할 나위 없이 알맞았다."], ["I went first to the barrier separating us from the stalls, and looked for the Count in that part of the theatre.", "나는 먼저 특등석과 우리를 갈라놓는 난간으로 가서 극장의 그쪽 구역에 백작이 있는지 살폈다."], ["He was not there.", "그는 거기 없었다."], ["Returning along the passage, on the left-hand side from the stage, and looking about me attentively, I discovered him in the pit.", "무대를 기준으로 왼편 통로를 따라 되돌아오며 주위를 주의 깊게 둘러본 끝에, 나는 평석에서 그를 발견했다. 그는 아주 좋은 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["He occupied an excellent place, some twelve or fourteen seats from the end of a bench, within three rows of the stalls.", "긴 의자의 끝에서 열두어 자리 혹은 열네 자리쯤 안쪽, 특등석에서 세 줄도 떨어지지 않은 곳이었다."], ["I placed myself exactly on a line with him; Pesca standing by my side.", "나는 그와 정확히 일직선이 되는 위치에 섰고, 페스카는 내 곁에 섰다."], ["The Professor was not yet aware of the purpose for which I had brought him to the theatre, and he was rather surprised that we did not move nearer to the stage.", "교수는 내가 무슨 목적으로 자기를 극장에 데려왔는지 아직 알지 못했으므로, 우리가 무대 쪽으로 더 다가가지 않는 것을 오히려 의아해했다."]], [["The curtain rose, and the opera began.", "막이 오르고 오페라가 시작되었다."]], [["Throughout the whole of the first act we remained in our position—the Count, absorbed by the orchestra and the stage, never casting so much as a chance glance at us.", "1막 내내 우리는 그 자리를 지켰다. 백작은 관현악과 무대에 온통 마음을 빼앗겨 우리 쪽으로는 눈길 한 번 흘리지 않았다."], ["Not a note of Donizetti’s delicious music was lost on him.", "도니제티의 감미로운 음악은 한 음도 그를 그냥 지나치지 않았다."], ["There he sat, high above his neighbours, smiling, and nodding his great head enjoyingly from time to time.", "그는 이웃한 사람들보다 머리 하나쯤 높이 솟은 채 앉아 미소를 짓고, 이따금 그 커다란 머리를 흡족한 듯 끄덕였다."], ["When the people near him applauded the close of an air (as an English audience in such circumstances always WILL applaud), without the least consideration for the orchestral movement which immediately followed it, he looked round at them with an expression of compassionate remonstrance, and held up one hand with a gesture of polite entreaty.", "곁의 사람들이 아리아가 끝나자마자—영국 관객이 그런 자리에서 늘 그러하듯—뒤이어 곧바로 흘러나오는 관현악 악절은 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 박수를 쳐대면, 그는 가엾다는 듯 나무라는 표정으로 그들을 돌아보며 정중히 청하는 몸짓으로 한 손을 들어 보였다."], ["At the more refined passages of the singing, at the more delicate phases of the music, which passed unapplauded by others, his fat hands, adorned with perfectly-fitting black kid gloves, softly patted each other, in token of the cultivated appreciation of a musical man.", "노래의 한결 세련된 대목, 남들은 박수도 보내지 않고 지나치는 음악의 한결 섬세한 굽이에 이르면, 꼭 맞는 검은 염소가죽 장갑을 낀 그의 통통한 두 손이 음악을 아는 사람다운 교양 있는 감상의 표시로 서로를 부드럽게 두드렸다."], ["At such times, his oily murmur of approval, “Bravo!", "그럴 때면 “브라보! 브라아아아!”"], ["Bra-a-a-a!” hummed through the silence, like the purring of a great cat.", "하는 그의 기름진 찬탄이 정적을 뚫고, 커다란 고양이가 그르렁대는 소리처럼 낮게 울렸다."], ["His immediate neighbours on either side—hearty, ruddy-faced people from the country, basking amazedly in the sunshine of fashionable London—seeing and hearing him, began to follow his lead.", "양옆에 앉은 사람들—혈색 좋고 서글서글한 시골 사람들로, 유행의 도시 런던의 햇살을 얼떨떨하게 쬐고 있던—은 그를 보고 듣더니 그가 하는 대로 따라 하기 시작했다."], ["Many a burst of applause from the pit that night started from the soft, comfortable patting of the black-gloved hands.", "그날 밤 평석에서 터져 나온 갈채는 여러 차례, 그 검은 장갑 낀 손의 부드럽고 편안한 두드림에서 시작되었다."], ["The man’s voracious vanity devoured this implied tribute to his local and critical supremacy with an appearance of the highest relish.", "그 사내의 게걸스러운 허영은 자기가 그 자리의 우두머리이자 비평의 권위라는 이 은근한 찬사를, 더할 나위 없이 흡족해하는 낯빛으로 삼켜버렸다."], ["Smiles rippled continuously over his fat face.", "미소가 그 살진 얼굴 위로 끊임없이 물결쳤다."], ["He looked about him, at the pauses in the music, serenely satisfied with himself and his fellow-creatures.", "음악이 멎는 사이사이 그는 자기 자신과 동료 인간들에게 두루 만족한 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보았다. “그래!"], ["“Yes!", "그래!"], ["yes! these barbarous English people are learning something from ME.", "이 야만스러운 영국 사람들이 나에게서 무언가를 배우고 있구나."], ["Here, there, and everywhere, I—Fosco—am an influence that is felt, a man who sits supreme!”", "여기서나 저기서나 어디서나, 나 포스코는 느껴지는 영향력이요, 지존의 자리에 앉은 사람이다!”"], ["If ever face spoke, his face spoke then, and that was its language.", "얼굴이 말을 할 수 있는 것이라면 그때 그의 얼굴이 말을 하고 있었으니, 그 말이 바로 그것이었다."]], [["The curtain fell on the first act, and the audience rose to look about them.", "1막이 끝나며 막이 내렸고, 관객들은 주위를 둘러보려 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["This was the time I had waited for—the time to try if Pesca knew him.", "내가 기다려온 순간이었다. 페스카가 그를 아는지 시험해볼 때가 온 것이다."]], [["He rose with the rest, and surveyed the occupants of the boxes grandly with his opera-glass.", "그도 다른 사람들과 함께 일어나, 오페라글라스로 특별석의 사람들을 거만하게 훑어보았다."], ["At first his back was towards us, but he turned round in time, to our side of the theatre, and looked at the boxes above us, using his glass for a few minutes—then removing it, but still continuing to look up.", "처음에는 등을 우리 쪽으로 두고 있었으나, 이윽고 우리가 있는 쪽으로 몸을 돌려 우리 머리 위의 특별석을 몇 분 동안 안경으로 바라보았다. 그러고는 안경을 내렸으나 여전히 위쪽을 계속 올려다보고 있었다."], ["This was the moment I chose, when his full face was in view, for directing Pesca’s attention to him.", "그의 얼굴이 온전히 드러난 이때야말로 페스카의 주의를 그에게 돌리기 위해 내가 고른 순간이었다."]], [["“Do you know that man?”", "“저 사람 아십니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Which man, my friend?”", "“어느 사람 말인가, 나의 친구?”"]], [["“The tall, fat man, standing there, with his face towards us.”", "“저기 서서 우리 쪽으로 얼굴을 두고 있는, 키 크고 살진 사람 말입니다.”"]], [["Pesca raised himself on tiptoe, and looked at the Count.", "페스카가 발끝으로 몸을 돋우어 백작을 바라보았다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니.”"], ["said the Professor.", "교수가 말했다."], ["“The big fat man is a stranger to me.", "“저 덩치 큰 뚱뚱한 사람은 내게 낯선 사람일세."], ["Is he famous?", "유명한 사람인가?"], ["Why do you point him out?”", "왜 저 사람을 가리키는 겐가?”"]], [["“Because I have particular reasons for wishing to know something of him.", "“저 사람에 대해 뭐라도 알고 싶은 각별한 사정이 있어서요."], ["He is a countryman of yours—his name is Count Fosco.", "교수님과 같은 나라 사람입니다. 이름은 포스코 백작이고요."], ["Do you know that name?”", "그 이름을 아십니까?”"]], [["“Not I, Walter.", "“모르네, 월터."], ["Neither the name nor the man is known to me.”", "이름도 사람도 내가 아는 바 없네.”"]], [["“Are you quite sure you don’t recognise him?", "“정말 못 알아보시겠습니까?"], ["Look again—look carefully.", "다시 한번 보세요, 찬찬히 보세요."], ["I will tell you why I am so anxious about it when we leave the theatre.", "제가 왜 이렇게 애가 타는지는 극장을 나가서 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Stop!", "잠깐만요!"], ["let me help you up here, where you can see him better.”", "여기 올라서시게 제가 부축해드리죠. 여기서라면 더 잘 보이실 겁니다.”"]], [["I helped the little man to perch himself on the edge of the raised dais upon which the pit-seats were all placed.", "나는 그 작은 사람이 평석 의자들이 놓인 높은 단의 가장자리에 올라서도록 부축했다."], ["His small stature was no hindrance to him—here he could see over the heads of the ladies who were seated near the outermost part of the bench.", "작은 키는 그에게 아무런 방해도 되지 않았다. 여기서라면 긴 의자의 바깥쪽 끝에 앉은 부인들의 머리 너머로 내다볼 수 있었다."]], [["A slim, light-haired man standing by us, whom I had not noticed before—a man with a scar on his left cheek—looked attentively at Pesca as I helped him up, and then looked still more attentively, following the direction of Pesca’s eyes, at the Count.", "우리 곁에 서 있던, 그때까지 내가 눈여겨보지 못했던 호리호리한 금발 남자—왼쪽 뺨에 흉터가 있는 남자—가 내가 페스카를 올려주는 동안 페스카를 유심히 바라보았고, 이어 페스카의 시선이 향하는 쪽을 따라 백작을 한층 더 유심히 바라보았다."], ["Our conversation might have reached his ears, and might, as it struck me, have roused his curiosity.", "우리 대화가 그의 귀에 닿았을 수도 있었고, 내 짐작으로는 그 대화가 그의 호기심을 자극했을 수도 있었다."]], [["Meanwhile, Pesca fixed his eyes earnestly on the broad, full, smiling face turned a little upward, exactly opposite to him.", "그러는 동안 페스카는 자기 바로 맞은편에서 조금 위로 향한, 넓고 살진 웃는 얼굴에 눈길을 진지하게 붙박고 있었다."]], [["“No,”", "“아닐세.”"], ["he said, “I have never set my two eyes on that big fat man before in all my life.”", "그가 말했다. “평생 저 덩치 큰 뚱뚱한 사람을 이 두 눈으로 본 적이 없네.”"]], [["As he spoke the Count looked downwards towards the boxes behind us on the pit tier.", "그가 그렇게 말하는 동안 백작은 우리 뒤쪽, 평석 층의 특별석 쪽으로 눈길을 내렸다."]], [["The eyes of the two Italians met.", "두 이탈리아 사람의 눈이 마주쳤다."]], [["The instant before I had been perfectly satisfied, from his own reiterated assertion, that Pesca did not know the Count.", "바로 조금 전까지만 해도 나는 그가 거듭 밝힌 말에 따라 페스카가 백작을 모른다고 완전히 믿고 있었다."], ["The instant afterwards I was equally certain that the Count knew Pesca!", "그런데 바로 그다음 순간, 나는 백작이 페스카를 안다는 것을 그만큼이나 확실하게 알아차렸다!"]], [["Knew him, and—more surprising still—FEARED him as well!", "알아보았을 뿐 아니라—더욱 놀랍게도—두려워하기까지 했다!"], ["There was no mistaking the change that passed over the villain’s face.", "그 악당의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나간 변화는 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다."], ["The leaden hue that altered his yellow complexion in a moment, the sudden rigidity of all his features, the furtive scrutiny of his cold grey eyes, the motionless stillness of him from head to foot told their own tale.", "누렇던 안색을 한순간에 납빛으로 바꿔놓은 그 빛깔, 이목구비 전체가 문득 굳어버린 것, 차가운 잿빛 눈의 은밀한 살핌, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 미동도 없는 그 정지 상태가 저마다 사연을 말해주고 있었다."], ["A mortal dread had mastered him body and soul—and his own recognition of Pesca was the cause of it!", "죽음 같은 공포가 그의 몸과 마음을 사로잡았으며, 그 까닭은 그가 페스카를 알아보았다는 사실 바로 그것이었다!"]], [["The slim man with the scar on his cheek was still close by us.", "뺨에 흉터가 있는 호리호리한 남자는 여전히 우리 가까이에 있었다."], ["He had apparently drawn his inference from the effect produced on the Count by the sight of Pesca as I had drawn mine.", "그도 페스카를 본 백작에게 일어난 변화에서 내가 끌어낸 것과 같은 추론을 끌어낸 모양이었다."], ["He was a mild, gentlemanlike man, looking like a foreigner, and his interest in our proceedings was not expressed in anything approaching to an offensive manner.", "그는 온순하고 신사다운 사람으로 외국인처럼 보였으며, 우리 일에 대한 그의 관심은 조금도 무례하다 할 만한 방식으로 드러나지는 않았다."]], [["For my own part I was so startled by the change in the Count’s face, so astounded at the entirely unexpected turn which events had taken, that I knew neither what to say or do next.", "나로 말하자면, 백작의 얼굴에 일어난 변화에 너무나 놀랐고 사태가 전혀 뜻밖의 방향으로 돌아간 데 너무나 어리둥절해서, 다음에 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Pesca roused me by stepping back to his former place at my side and speaking first.", "페스카가 원래 있던 내 곁자리로 물러서서 먼저 말을 걸어옴으로써 나를 정신 들게 했다."]], [["“How the fat man stares!”", "“저 뚱뚱한 사람이 어찌나 빤히 쳐다보는지!”"], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“Is it at ME?", "“나를 보는 건가?"], ["Am I famous?", "내가 유명 인사인가?"], ["How can he know me when I don’t know him?”", "내가 저 사람을 모르는데 저 사람이 어떻게 나를 알겠나?”"]], [["I kept my eye still on the Count.", "나는 여전히 백작에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["I saw him move for the first time when Pesca moved, so as not to lose sight of the little man in the lower position in which he now stood.", "페스카가 움직이자 그도 처음으로 몸을 움직이는 것이 보였다. 이제 낮은 자리로 내려선 그 작은 사람을 시야에서 놓치지 않으려는 것이었다."], ["I was curious to see what would happen if Pesca’s attention under these circumstances was withdrawn from him, and I accordingly asked the Professor if he recognised any of his pupils that evening among the ladies in the boxes.", "나는 이런 상황에서 페스카의 주의가 그에게서 거두어지면 어떤 일이 벌어질지 궁금했고, 그래서 교수에게 오늘 저녁 특별석의 부인들 가운데 가르치는 제자가 보이느냐고 물었다."], ["Pesca immediately raised the large opera-glass to his eyes, and moved it slowly all round the upper part of the theatre, searching for his pupils with the most conscientious scrutiny.", "페스카는 곧바로 커다란 오페라글라스를 눈에 갖다 대고, 더없이 성실한 눈길로 제자들을 찾으며 극장 위층을 천천히 두루 살폈다."]], [["The moment he showed himself to be thus engaged the Count turned round, slipped past the persons who occupied seats on the farther side of him from where we stood, and disappeared in the middle passage down the centre of the pit.", "그가 그 일에 몰두한 것을 보이자마자 백작은 몸을 돌려, 우리가 선 쪽에서 먼 편에 앉은 사람들 곁을 슬쩍 빠져나가 평석 한가운데로 난 중앙 통로로 사라졌다."], ["I caught Pesca by the arm, and to his inexpressible astonishment, hurried him round with me to the back of the pit to intercept the Count before he could get to the door.", "나는 페스카의 팔을 붙들고, 그가 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라거나 말거나, 백작이 문에 이르기 전에 가로막으려고 그를 데리고 평석 뒤편으로 서둘러 돌아갔다."], ["Somewhat to my surprise, the slim man hastened out before us, avoiding a stoppage caused by some people on our side of the pit leaving their places, by which Pesca and myself were delayed.", "다소 놀랍게도 그 호리호리한 남자가 우리보다 먼저 서둘러 빠져나갔다. 우리 쪽 평석의 몇몇 사람이 자리를 뜨는 바람에 길이 막혀 페스카와 내가 지체하는 사이에 그는 그 정체를 피해 간 것이다."], ["When we reached the lobby the Count had disappeared, and the foreigner with the scar was gone too.", "우리가 로비에 이르렀을 때 백작은 사라지고 없었고, 흉터 있는 그 외국인도 가고 없었다."]], [["“Come home,”", "“집으로 갑시다.”"], ["I said; “come home, Pesca, to your lodgings.", "내가 말했다. “교수님 숙소로 가시죠, 페스카."], ["I must speak to you in private—I must speak directly.”", "단둘이 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 지금 곧 말씀드려야 합니다.”"]], [["“My-soul-bless-my-soul!”", "“오-내-영혼에-축복이!”"], ["cried the Professor, in a state of the extremest bewilderment.", "교수가 극도의 어리둥절함에 빠져 외쳤다."], ["“What on earth is the matter?”", "“대체 무슨 일인가?”"]], [["I walked on rapidly without answering.", "나는 대답 없이 빠른 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["The circumstances under which the Count had left the theatre suggested to me that his extraordinary anxiety to escape Pesca might carry him to further extremities still.", "백작이 극장을 떠난 정황으로 미루어, 페스카를 피하려는 그의 유별난 조바심이 그를 더한 극단으로 몰고 갈지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["He might escape me, too, by leaving London.", "그는 런던을 떠남으로써 나에게서도 달아날 수 있었다."], ["I doubted the future if I allowed him so much as a day’s freedom to act as he pleased.", "그에게 단 하루라도 제 마음대로 움직일 자유를 허락한다면 앞일이 어찌 될지 알 수 없었다."], ["And I doubted that foreign stranger, who had got the start of us, and whom I suspected of intentionally following him out.", "그리고 우리보다 앞서 나간 그 낯선 외국인도 미덥지 않았다. 나는 그가 일부러 백작을 뒤따라 나갔으리라 의심했다."]], [["With this double distrust in my mind, I was not long in making Pesca understand what I wanted.", "이 두 겹의 불신을 품은 채, 나는 오래지 않아 페스카에게 내가 바라는 바를 알아듣게 했다."], ["As soon as we two were alone in his room, I increased his confusion and amazement a hundredfold by telling him what my purpose was as plainly and unreservedly as I have acknowledged it here.", "그의 방에 단둘이 남자마자, 나는 여기 적어놓은 것만큼이나 분명하고 숨김없이 내 목적을 이야기함으로써 그의 혼란과 경악을 백 배로 키워놓았다."]], [["“My friend, what can I do?”", "“나의 친구, 내가 무얼 할 수 있단 말인가?”"], ["cried the Professor, piteously appealing to me with both hands.", "교수가 두 손을 내밀어 애처롭게 호소하며 외쳤다."], ["“Deuce-what-the-deuce!", "“제기랄-이런-제기랄!"], ["how can I help you, Walter, when I don’t know the man?”", "내가 그 사람을 모르는데 어떻게 자네를 돕겠나, 월터?”"]], [["“HE knows YOU—he is afraid of you—he has left the theatre to escape you.", "“그가 교수님을 압니다. 그는 교수님을 두려워합니다."], ["Pesca! there must be a reason for this.", "교수님을 피하려고 극장을 떠났고요."], ["Look back into your own life before you came to England.", "페스카! 여기에는 반드시 까닭이 있습니다."], ["You left Italy, as you have told me yourself, for political reasons.", "영국에 오시기 전 교수님의 삶을 돌이켜보세요. 교수님은 정치적인 이유로 이탈리아를 떠났다고 제게 직접 말씀하셨습니다."], ["You have never mentioned those reasons to me, and I don’t inquire into them now.", "그 이유를 제게 말씀하신 적은 한 번도 없고, 지금도 저는 그것을 캐묻지 않겠습니다."], ["I only ask you to consult your own recollections, and to say if they suggest no past cause for the terror which the first sight of you produced in that man.”", "다만 교수님의 기억을 더듬어보시고, 교수님을 처음 본 순간 그 사람이 보인 공포를 설명해줄 만한 지난날의 원인이 정말 짚이지 않는지만 말씀해주십시오.”"]], [["To my unutterable surprise, these words, harmless as they appeared to ME, produced the same astounding effect on Pesca which the sight of Pesca had produced on the Count.", "이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도, 나에게는 아무 해도 없어 보이던 이 말이 페스카에게, 페스카를 본 것이 백작에게 일으켰던 것과 똑같이 아연한 효과를 일으켰다."], ["The rosy face of my little friend whitened in an instant, and he drew back from me slowly, trembling from head to foot.", "내 작은 친구의 발그레하던 얼굴이 한순간에 하얗게 질렸고, 그는 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨면서 천천히 내게서 물러섰다."]], [["“Walter!”", "“월터!”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You don’t know what you ask.”", "“자네는 자네가 무엇을 청하는지 모르네.”"]], [["He spoke in a whisper—he looked at me as if I had suddenly revealed to him some hidden danger to both of us.", "그는 속삭이듯 말했다. 마치 내가 우리 둘 모두에게 닥친 어떤 숨은 위험을 불쑥 알려주기라도 한 듯한 눈으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["In less than one minute of time he was so altered from the easy, lively, quaint little man of all my past experience, that if I had met him in the street, changed as I saw him now, I should most certainly not have known him again.", "채 일 분도 지나지 않아 그는 내가 여태 알아온 그 소탈하고 활기차고 별난 작은 사람과 너무나 달라져서, 지금 보는 그 모습으로 길에서 마주쳤다면 나는 틀림없이 그를 알아보지 못했을 것이다."]], [["“Forgive me, if I have unintentionally pained and shocked you,”", "“뜻하지 않게 마음을 아프게 하고 놀라게 해드렸다면 용서하십시오.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Remember the cruel wrong my wife has suffered at Count Fosco’s hands.", "“제 아내가 포스코 백작의 손에 겪은 잔인한 부당함을 생각해주십시오."], ["Remember that the wrong can never be redressed, unless the means are in my power of forcing him to do her justice.", "그가 아내에게 정당한 대접을 하도록 제가 강제할 수단을 손에 넣지 못하는 한, 그 부당함은 결코 바로잡힐 수 없다는 것을 생각해주십시오. 저는 아내를 위해서 말씀드린 겁니다, 페스카."], ["I spoke in HER interests, Pesca—I ask you again to forgive me—I can say no more.”", "다시 한번 용서를 구합니다. 더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.”"]], [["I rose to go.", "나는 가려고 일어섰다."], ["He stopped me before I reached the door.", "그가 내가 문에 이르기 전에 나를 붙들었다."]], [["“Wait,”", "“기다리게.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You have shaken me from head to foot.", "“자네가 나를 머리끝에서 발끝까지 뒤흔들어놓았네."], ["You don’t know how I left my country, and why I left my country.", "내가 어떻게 조국을 떠났는지, 왜 떠났는지 자네는 모르지. 마음을 좀 가라앉히게 해주게."], ["Let me compose myself, let me think, if I can.”", "생각을 할 수 있다면, 생각을 좀 하게 해주게.”"]], [["I returned to my chair.", "나는 다시 의자로 돌아갔다."], ["He walked up and down the room, talking to himself incoherently in his own language.", "그는 방 안을 오락가락하며 제 나라 말로 두서없이 혼잣말을 했다."], ["After several turns backwards and forwards, he suddenly came up to me, and laid his little hands with a strange tenderness and solemnity on my breast.", "몇 번을 왔다 갔다 하더니 그는 문득 내게 다가와, 그 작은 두 손을 이상하리만치 다정하고 엄숙하게 내 가슴에 얹었다."]], [["“On your heart and soul, Walter,”", "“자네 마음과 영혼을 걸고 말해주게, 월터.”"], ["he said, “is there no other way to get to that man but the chance-way through ME?”", "그가 말했다. “그 사람에게 이르는 길이, 어쩌다 나를 거치게 된 이 길밖에는 정말 없는 겐가?”"]], [["“There is no other way,”", "“다른 길은 없습니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["He left me again, opened the door of the room and looked out cautiously into the passage, closed it once more, and came back.", "그는 다시 나를 두고 물러나, 방문을 열고 복도를 조심스레 내다보더니 문을 도로 닫고 돌아왔다."]], [["“You won your right over me, Walter,”", "“자네는 나에 대한 권리를 얻었네, 월터.”"], ["he said, “on the day when you saved my life.", "그가 말했다. “내 목숨을 구해준 그날에 말일세."], ["It was yours from that moment, when you pleased to take it.", "그 순간부터 내 목숨은 자네 것이었으니, 자네가 원할 때 가져가면 되네."], ["Take it now.", "지금 가져가게."], ["Yes!", "그래!"], ["I mean what I say.", "나는 진심으로 하는 말일세."], ["My next words, as true as the good God is above us, will put my life into your hands.”", "우리 위에 계신 선하신 하느님이 참되신 것만큼이나 참되게, 이제부터 내가 할 말은 내 목숨을 자네 손에 쥐여주는 것이네.”"]], [["The trembling earnestness with which he uttered this extraordinary warning, carried with it, to my mind, the conviction that he spoke the truth.", "그가 이 예사롭지 않은 경고를 내뱉을 때의 떨리는 진지함은, 그가 진실을 말하고 있다는 확신을 내 마음에 심어주었다."]], [["“Mind this!”", "“이것만은 명심하게!”"], ["he went on, shaking his hands at me in the vehemence of his agitation.", "그는 격한 동요에 못 이겨 두 손을 내게 흔들어 보이며 말을 이었다."], ["“I hold no thread, in my own mind, between that man Fosco, and the past time which I call back to me for your sake.", "“내 마음속에는 저 포스코라는 사람과, 자네를 위해 되불러오는 그 지난 시절 사이를 잇는 실이 한 오라기도 없네. 만일 자네가 그 실을 찾거든 자네 혼자만 알고 있게."], ["If you find the thread, keep it to yourself—tell me nothing—on my knees I beg and pray, let me be ignorant, let me be innocent, let me be blind to all the future as I am now!”", "내게는 아무 말도 하지 말게. 무릎 꿇고 빌고 애원하네, 나를 모르는 채로 있게 해주게, 죄 없는 채로 있게 해주게, 지금처럼 앞일에 대해서는 눈먼 채로 있게 해주게!”"]], [["He said a few words more, hesitatingly and disconnectedly, then stopped again.", "그는 몇 마디를 더, 머뭇거리며 앞뒤 없이 말하다가 다시 멈추었다."]], [["I saw that the effort of expressing himself in English, on an occasion too serious to permit him the use of the quaint turns and phrases of his ordinary vocabulary, was painfully increasing the difficulty he had felt from the first in speaking to me at all.", "나는 그가 평소 쓰는 어휘의 그 별스러운 말투와 표현을 허락하지 않을 만큼 심각한 자리에서 영어로 자기를 표현하려 애쓰는 일이, 애초부터 내게 말을 꺼내는 것 자체에 느껴온 어려움을 고통스럽게 키우고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Having learnt to read and understand his native language (though not to speak it), in the earlier days of our intimate companionship, I now suggested to him that he should express himself in Italian, while I used English in putting any questions which might be necessary to my enlightenment.", "우리가 가깝게 지내던 초기에 나는 그의 모국어를 (말하지는 못해도) 읽고 이해할 만큼은 익혀두었으므로, 이제 그에게 이탈리아어로 이야기하라고 권하고, 내가 알아야 할 것을 묻는 데는 내가 영어를 쓰기로 했다."], ["He accepted the proposal.", "그가 이 제안을 받아들였다."], ["In his smooth-flowing language, spoken with a vehement agitation which betrayed itself in the perpetual working of his features, in the wildness and the suddenness of his foreign gesticulations, but never in the raising of his voice, I now heard the words which armed me to meet the last struggle, that is left for this story to record.[3]", "유창하게 흐르는 그의 말로, 끊임없이 씰룩이는 표정과 느닷없고 격렬한 외국식 몸짓에는 드러나되 결코 목소리를 높이는 데는 드러나지 않는 격한 동요 속에서, 나는 이제 이 이야기가 기록해야 할 마지막 싸움에 맞설 무기를 내게 쥐여준 그 말들을 들었다.[3]"]], [["[3] It is only right to mention here, that I repeat Pesco’s statement to me with the careful suppressions and alterations which the serious nature of the subject and my own sense of duty to my friend demand.", "[3] 여기서 밝혀두는 것이 옳겠다. 나는 페스카가 내게 한 이야기를, 이 문제의 심각한 성격과 친구에 대한 나 자신의 의무감이 요구하는 대로 조심스럽게 삭제하고 고쳐서 옮긴다."], ["My first and last concealments from the reader are those which caution renders absolutely necessary in this portion of the narrative.", "내가 독자에게 감추는 것은 이것이 처음이자 마지막이며, 이 대목에서는 신중을 기하자면 그렇게 하지 않을 수 없다."]], [["“You know nothing of my motive for leaving Italy,” he began, “except that it was for political reasons.", "“자네는 내가 이탈리아를 떠난 동기에 대해서, 그것이 정치적인 이유였다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르네.”"], ["If I had been driven to this country by the persecution of my government, I should not have kept those reasons a secret from you or from any one.", "그가 말을 시작했다. “만일 내가 정부의 박해에 떠밀려 이 나라에 왔다면, 나는 그 이유를 자네에게도 다른 누구에게도 숨기지 않았을 걸세."], ["I have concealed them because no government authority has pronounced the sentence of my exile.", "내가 그것을 숨긴 것은 어떤 정부 당국도 내게 추방을 선고한 적이 없기 때문이네."], ["You have heard, Walter, of the political societies that are hidden in every great city on the continent of Europe?", "월터, 유럽 대륙의 큰 도시마다 숨어 있는 정치 결사에 대해 들어본 적이 있는가?"], ["To one of those societies I belonged in Italy—and belong still in England.", "나는 이탈리아에서 그런 결사 가운데 하나에 속해 있었고, 영국에서도 여전히 속해 있네."], ["When I came to this country, I came by the direction of my chief.", "내가 이 나라에 온 것은 우리 우두머리의 지시에 따른 것이었네."], ["I was over-zealous in my younger time—I ran the risk of compromising myself and others.", "젊은 시절 나는 지나치게 열성이었네. 나 자신과 다른 이들을 위태롭게 할 위험을 무릅썼지."], ["For those reasons I was ordered to emigrate to England and to wait.", "그래서 나는 영국으로 이주해 기다리라는 명을 받았네."], ["I emigrated—I have waited—I wait still.", "나는 이주했고, 기다려왔고, 지금도 기다리고 있네."], ["To-morrow I may be called away—ten years hence I may be called away.", "내일 부름을 받을 수도 있고, 십 년 뒤에 부름을 받을 수도 있지."], ["It is all one to me—I am here, I support myself by teaching, and I wait.", "내게는 매한가지네. 나는 여기 있고, 가르치는 일로 먹고살며, 기다리네."], ["I violate no oath (you shall hear why presently) in making my confidence complete by telling you the name of the society to which I belong.", "내가 속한 결사의 이름을 자네에게 말해줌으로써 내 고백을 온전한 것으로 만든다 해도 나는 아무 맹세도 어기는 것이 아닐세(그 까닭은 곧 듣게 될 걸세)."], ["All I do is to put my life in your hands.", "내가 하는 일이란 내 목숨을 자네 손에 맡기는 것뿐이지."], ["If what I say to you now is ever known by others to have passed my lips, as certainly as we two sit here, I am a dead man.”", "지금 자네에게 하는 이 말이 내 입에서 나왔다는 사실이 언젠가 다른 이들에게 알려진다면, 우리 둘이 여기 앉아 있는 것이 확실한 만큼이나 확실하게 나는 죽은 목숨이네.”"]], [["He whispered the next words in my ear.", "그는 다음 말을 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다."], ["I keep the secret which he thus communicated.", "그렇게 전해 들은 그 비밀을 나는 지킨다."], ["The society to which he belonged will be sufficiently individualised for the purpose of these pages, if I call it “The Brotherhood,” on the few occasions when any reference to the subject will be needed in this place.", "그가 속한 결사는, 여기서 그 문제를 언급할 필요가 있는 몇 안 되는 자리에서 내가 그것을 “형제단”이라고만 불러도 이 지면의 목적에는 충분히 특정될 것이다."]], [["“The object of the Brotherhood,”", "“형제단의 목적은,” 페스카가 말을 이었다."], ["Pesca went on, “is, briefly, the object of other political societies of the same sort—the destruction of tyranny and the assertion of the rights of the people.", "“간단히 말해 같은 종류의 다른 정치 결사들의 목적과 같네. 폭정을 무너뜨리고 인민의 권리를 세우는 것이지."], ["The principles of the Brotherhood are two.", "형제단의 원칙은 두 가지네."], ["So long as a man’s life is useful, or even harmless only, he has the right to enjoy it.", "한 사람의 삶이 이롭거나, 하다못해 해롭지만 않은 동안에는 그에게 그 삶을 누릴 권리가 있네."], ["But, if his life inflicts injury on the well-being of his fellow-men, from that moment he forfeits the right, and it is not only no crime, but a positive merit, to deprive him of it.", "그러나 그의 삶이 동료 인간들의 안녕을 해치는 순간부터 그는 그 권리를 잃으며, 그에게서 그 삶을 빼앗는 것은 죄가 아닐 뿐 아니라 분명한 공덕이 되네."], ["It is not for me to say in what frightful circumstances of oppression and suffering this society took its rise.", "이 결사가 얼마나 끔찍한 억압과 고통의 정황 속에서 생겨났는지는 내가 말할 바가 아닐세."], ["It is not for you to say—you Englishmen, who have conquered your freedom so long ago, that you have conveniently forgotten what blood you shed, and what extremities you proceeded to in the conquering—it is not for you to say how far the worst of all exasperations may, or may not, carry the maddened men of an enslaved nation.", "자네들이 말할 바도 아니지. 자유를 쟁취한 지 하도 오래되어 그 쟁취 과정에서 자기들이 어떤 피를 흘렸고 어떤 극단으로 치달았는지 편리하게 잊어버린 자네들 영국 사람이, 노예가 된 나라의 미쳐버린 사람들을 세상에서 가장 참혹한 분노가 어디까지 몰아갈 수 있고 없는지를 말할 자격은 없네."], ["The iron that has entered into our souls has gone too deep for you to find it.", "우리 영혼에 박힌 쇠는 자네들이 찾아낼 수 없을 만큼 깊이 들어가 있네."], ["Leave the refugee alone!", "망명자를 내버려두게!"], ["Laugh at him, distrust him, open your eyes in wonder at that secret self which smoulders in him, sometimes under the everyday respectability and tranquillity of a man like me—sometimes under the grinding poverty, the fierce squalor, of men less lucky, less pliable, less patient than I am—but judge us not!", "그를 비웃고, 그를 의심하고, 그의 안에서 연기를 피우는 그 은밀한 자아—때로는 나 같은 사람의 일상적인 점잖음과 평온함 아래, 때로는 나만큼 운 좋지도 유순하지도 참을성 있지도 못한 사람들의 짓누르는 가난과 사나운 누추함 아래 도사린 그 자아—에 놀라 눈을 크게 뜨게. 그러나 우리를 심판하지는 말게!"], ["In the time of your first Charles you might have done us justice—the long luxury of your own freedom has made you incapable of doing us justice now.”", "자네들의 첫 번째 찰스 왕 시절이었다면 자네들도 우리를 공정하게 대할 수 있었을 걸세. 자네들 자신의 자유를 오래도록 누려온 안락함이 이제 자네들에게서 우리를 공정하게 대할 능력을 앗아가 버렸네.”"]], [["All the deepest feelings of his nature seemed to force themselves to the surface in those words—all his heart was poured out to me for the first time in our lives—but still his voice never rose, still his dread of the terrible revelation he was making to me never left him.", "그 말에는 그의 천성 깊은 곳의 온갖 감정이 죄다 표면으로 밀려 올라오는 듯했다. 평생 처음으로 그의 마음이 통째로 내게 쏟아져 나왔다. 그럼에도 그의 목소리는 여전히 높아지지 않았고, 자기가 내게 하고 있는 그 무서운 폭로에 대한 두려움도 여전히 그를 떠나지 않았다."]], [["“So far,”", "“여기까지는,”"], ["he resumed, “you think the society like other societies.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다. “자네는 이 결사가 다른 결사들과 비슷하다고 생각하겠지."], ["Its object (in your English opinion) is anarchy and revolution.", "그 목적이란 (자네들 영국식 견해로는) 무정부와 혁명이라고 말이야."], ["It takes the life of a bad king or a bad minister, as if the one and the other were dangerous wild beasts to be shot at the first opportunity.", "악한 왕이나 악한 대신의 목숨을 빼앗기를, 마치 그 둘이 기회가 닿는 대로 쏘아 죽여야 할 위험한 맹수라도 되는 양 한다고."], ["I grant you this.", "그건 인정하겠네."], ["But the laws of the Brotherhood are the laws of no other political society on the face of the earth.", "그러나 형제단의 규율은 이 지상의 어떤 다른 정치 결사의 규율과도 같지 않네."], ["The members are not known to one another.", "단원들은 서로를 알지 못하네."], ["There is a president in Italy; there are presidents abroad.", "이탈리아에 회장이 한 사람 있고, 국외에도 회장들이 있네."], ["Each of these has his secretary.", "그들 각자에게는 서기가 있지."], ["The presidents and the secretaries know the members, but the members, among themselves, are all strangers, until their chiefs see fit, in the political necessity of the time, or in the private necessity of the society, to make them known to each other.", "회장과 서기는 단원들을 알지만, 단원들끼리는 모두가 서로에게 낯선 사람일세. 시대의 정치적 필요나 결사의 내부적 필요에 따라 우두머리들이 서로를 알게 하는 것이 마땅하다고 볼 때까지는 말이네."], ["With such a safeguard as this there is no oath among us on admittance.", "이런 안전장치가 있으므로 우리에게는 가입할 때 하는 맹세가 없네."], ["We are identified with the Brotherhood by a secret mark, which we all bear, which lasts while our lives last.", "우리는 목숨이 다할 때까지 지워지지 않는, 우리 모두가 지니는 비밀 표식으로 형제단의 일원임이 확인되네."], ["We are told to go about our ordinary business, and to report ourselves to the president, or the secretary, four times a year, in the event of our services being required.", "우리는 각자 평범한 생업에 종사하되, 우리의 봉사가 필요할 경우에 대비해 일 년에 네 번 회장이나 서기에게 자신을 알리라는 지시를 받네."], ["We are warned, if we betray the Brotherhood, or if we injure it by serving other interests, that we die by the principles of the Brotherhood—die by the hand of a stranger who may be sent from the other end of the world to strike the blow—or by the hand of our own bosom-friend, who may have been a member unknown to us through all the years of our intimacy.", "우리가 형제단을 배신하거나 다른 이익에 봉사하여 형제단을 해칠 경우에는 형제단의 원칙에 따라 죽는다는 경고를 받네. 세상 반대편 끝에서 그 일격을 가하러 보내진 낯선 사람의 손에 죽거나, 아니면 서로 가깝게 지낸 그 모든 세월 내내 우리가 모르는 단원이었을 수도 있는 절친한 벗의 손에 죽는 것이지."], ["Sometimes the death is delayed—sometimes it follows close on the treachery.", "때로는 죽음이 늦춰지고, 때로는 배신 직후에 곧바로 닥치네."], ["It is our first business to know how to wait—our second business to know how to obey when the word is spoken.", "우리의 첫째 임무는 기다릴 줄 아는 것이고, 둘째 임무는 명령이 떨어졌을 때 복종할 줄 아는 것이네."], ["Some of us may wait our lives through, and may not be wanted.", "우리 가운데 어떤 이는 평생을 기다리고도 필요해지지 않을 수 있네."], ["Some of us may be called to the work, or to the preparation for the work, the very day of our admission.", "어떤 이는 가입한 바로 그날에 일에, 혹은 일의 준비에 불려 나갈 수도 있지."], ["I myself—the little, easy, cheerful man you know, who, of his own accord, would hardly lift up his handkerchief to strike down the fly that buzzes about his face—I, in my younger time, under provocation so dreadful that I will not tell you of it, entered the Brotherhood by an impulse, as I might have killed myself by an impulse.", "나 자신은—자네가 아는 그 자그맣고 소탈하고 쾌활한 사람, 제 얼굴 둘레를 윙윙대는 파리를 잡겠다고 손수건 한 장 들어 올리는 일조차 좀처럼 하지 않을 이 사람은—젊은 시절에, 자네에게 이야기하지 않으려는 그토록 끔찍한 도발을 겪고서, 충동적으로 자살했을지도 모를 그런 충동으로 형제단에 들어갔네. 이제 나는 그 안에 남아 있어야 하네."], ["I must remain in it now—it has got me, whatever I may think of it in my better circumstances and my cooler manhood, to my dying day.", "형편이 나아지고 장년의 냉정을 얻은 지금 내가 그것을 어떻게 생각하든, 그것은 죽는 날까지 나를 붙들고 있네."], ["While I was still in Italy I was chosen secretary, and all the members of that time, who were brought face to face with my president, were brought face to face also with me.”", "아직 이탈리아에 있을 때 나는 서기로 뽑혔고, 그 시절의 단원들 가운데 우리 회장과 얼굴을 맞댄 사람은 모두 나와도 얼굴을 맞대었네.”"]], [["I began to understand him—I saw the end towards which his extraordinary disclosure was now tending.", "나는 그의 말을 알아듣기 시작했다. 그의 예사롭지 않은 고백이 이제 어디를 향해 가고 있는지 보였던 것이다."], ["He waited a moment, watching me earnestly—watching till he had evidently guessed what was passing in my mind before he resumed.", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추고 나를 간절하게 지켜보았다. 내 마음속에 무엇이 지나가고 있는지 분명히 알아챘다 싶을 때까지 지켜보고 나서야 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“You have drawn your own conclusion already,”", "“자네는 이미 자네 나름의 결론을 내렸군.” 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “I see it in your face.", "“자네 얼굴에 그렇게 쓰여 있네."], ["Tell me nothing—keep me out of the secret of your thoughts.", "내게는 아무 말도 말게. 자네 생각의 비밀에서 나를 빼놓아주게."], ["Let me make my one last sacrifice of myself, for your sake, and then have done with this subject, never to return to it again.”", "자네를 위해 나 자신을 바치는 마지막 희생을 한 번만 더 하게 해주고, 그런 다음 이 이야기는 끝내고 다시는 여기로 돌아오지 마세.”"]], [["He signed to me not to answer him—rose—removed his coat—and rolled up the shirt-sleeve on his left arm.", "그는 대답하지 말라는 손짓을 하고, 일어서서 외투를 벗고 왼팔의 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올렸다."]], [["“I promised you that this confidence should be complete,”", "“이 고백을 온전히 하겠다고 자네에게 약속했지.”"], ["he whispered, speaking close at my ear, with his eyes looking watchfully at the door.", "그는 문 쪽을 경계하듯 바라보며 내 귀 가까이에서 속삭였다."], ["“Whatever comes of it you shall not reproach me with having hidden anything from you which it was necessary to your interests to know.", "“이 일이 어떻게 되든, 자네의 이해에 알아야 할 필요가 있는 것을 내가 감추었다고 나를 나무라지는 못할 걸세."], ["I have said that the Brotherhood identifies its members by a mark that lasts for life.", "형제단은 평생 지워지지 않는 표식으로 단원을 확인한다고 내가 말했지."], ["See the place, and the mark on it for yourself.”", "그 자리와 거기 새겨진 표식을 자네 눈으로 직접 보게.”"]], [["He raised his bare arm, and showed me, high on the upper part of it and in the inner side, a brand deeply burnt in the flesh and stained of a bright blood-red colour.", "그는 맨팔을 들어 올려, 팔의 윗부분 높직한 곳 안쪽에 살 속 깊이 지져 새기고 선명한 핏빛으로 물들인 낙인을 보여주었다."], ["I abstain from describing the device which the brand represented.", "그 낙인이 나타내는 문양을 묘사하는 일은 삼가겠다."], ["It will be sufficient to say that it was circular in form, and so small that it would have been completely covered by a shilling coin.", "그것이 둥근 모양이었고 실링 은화 하나로 완전히 덮일 만큼 작았다는 말이면 충분할 것이다."]], [["“A man who has this mark, branded in this place,”", "“이 자리에 이 표식이 지져진 사람은,”"], ["he said, covering his arm again, “is a member of the Brotherhood.", "그가 팔을 다시 덮으며 말했다. “형제단의 일원이네."], ["A man who has been false to the Brotherhood is discovered sooner or later by the chiefs who know him—presidents or secretaries, as the case may be.", "형제단을 배신한 사람은 그를 아는 우두머리들—회장이든 서기든—에게 조만간 발각되네. 그리고 우두머리들에게 발각된 사람은 죽은 목숨이네."], ["And a man discovered by the chiefs is dead.", "어떤 인간의 법도 그를 지켜주지 못하네."], ["NO HUMAN LAWS CAN PROTECT HIM.", "자네가 보고 들은 것을 기억하게."], ["Remember what you have seen and heard—draw what conclusions YOU like—act as you please.", "자네 좋을 대로 결론을 내리고, 자네 좋을 대로 행동하게."], ["But, in the name of God, whatever you discover, whatever you do, tell me nothing!", "그러나 하느님의 이름으로 부탁하건대, 자네가 무엇을 알아내든 무엇을 하든 내게는 아무 말도 말게!"], ["Let me remain free from a responsibility which it horrifies me to think of—which I know, in my conscience, is not my responsibility now.", "생각만 해도 소름 끼치는 그 책임에서 나를 자유롭게 놔두게. 양심에 비추어 그것이 지금 내 책임이 아님을 나는 아네."], ["For the last time I say it—on my honour as a gentleman, on my oath as a Christian, if the man you pointed out at the Opera knows ME, he is so altered, or so disguised, that I do not know him.", "마지막으로 한 번 더 말하네. 신사로서의 명예를 걸고, 그리스도인으로서의 맹세를 걸고 말하건대, 만일 자네가 오페라 극장에서 가리킨 그 사람이 나를 안다면, 그는 내가 알아보지 못할 만큼 달라졌거나 그만큼 감쪽같이 변장한 것이네."], ["I am ignorant of his proceedings or his purposes in England.", "나는 그가 영국에서 무슨 일을 하는지 무슨 속셈인지 아무것도 모르네."], ["I never saw him, I never heard the name he goes by, to my knowledge, before to-night.", "내가 아는 한 나는 오늘 밤 이전에 그를 본 적도, 그가 쓰는 이름을 들은 적도 없네."], ["I say no more.", "더는 말하지 않겠네."], ["Leave me a little, Walter.", "나를 잠시 혼자 있게 해주게, 월터."], ["I am overpowered by what has happened—I am shaken by what I have said.", "나는 일어난 일에 압도되어 있고, 내가 한 말에 뒤흔들려 있네."], ["Let me try to be like myself again when we meet next.”", "다음에 만날 때는 다시 예전의 나로 돌아가 있도록 애써보겠네.”"]], [["He dropped into a chair, and turning away from me, hid his face in his hands.", "그는 의자에 털썩 주저앉더니 내게서 몸을 돌리고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["I gently opened the door so as not to disturb him, and spoke my few parting words in low tones, which he might hear or not, as he pleased.", "나는 그를 방해하지 않으려고 문을 조용히 열고, 그가 듣든 말든 그의 뜻에 맡긴 채 나직한 목소리로 몇 마디 작별 인사를 했다."]], [["“I will keep the memory of to-night in my heart of hearts,”", "“오늘 밤의 일을 제 마음 깊은 곳에 간직하겠습니다.” 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “You shall never repent the trust you have reposed in me.", "“저에게 베풀어주신 그 믿음을 결코 후회하시게 하지 않겠습니다."], ["May I come to you to-morrow?", "내일 찾아뵈어도 되겠습니까?"], ["May I come as early as nine o’clock?”", "아홉 시쯤 일찍 와도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, Walter,”", "“그러게, 월터.”"], ["he replied, looking up at me kindly, and speaking in English once more, as if his one anxiety now was to get back to our former relations towards each other.", "그는 다정하게 나를 올려다보며, 이제는 오로지 우리 사이의 예전 관계로 되돌아가는 것만이 그의 걱정이라는 듯 다시 영어로 말했다."], ["“Come to my little bit of breakfast before I go my ways among the pupils that I teach.”", "“내가 가르치는 제자들에게 가기 전에, 나의 소박한 아침식사 자리로 오게나.”"]], [["“Good-night, Pesca.”", "“안녕히 주무세요, 페스카.”"]], [["“Good-night, my friend.”", "“잘 자게, 나의 친구.”"]], [["# VI", "# VI"]], [["My first conviction as soon as I found myself outside the house, was that no alternative was left me but to act at once on the information I had received—to make sure of the Count that night, or to risk the loss, if I only delayed till the morning, of Laura’s last chance.", "그 집 밖으로 나서자마자 내게 처음으로 확고해진 생각은, 방금 들은 정보에 따라 지체 없이 움직이는 것 말고는 다른 길이 없다는 것이었다. 그날 밤 안에 백작을 붙들어야 한다, 아침까지 미루기라도 하면 로라의 마지막 기회를 잃을 위험을 감수해야 한다."], ["I looked at my watch—it was ten o’clock.", "나는 시계를 보았다. 열 시였다."]], [["Not the shadow of a doubt crossed my mind of the purpose for which the Count had left the theatre.", "백작이 극장을 떠난 목적에 대해서는 한 점의 의심도 내 마음을 스치지 않았다. 그날 저녁 그가 우리에게서 달아난 것은, 두말할 나위 없이 런던에서 달아나기 위한 준비 단계일 뿐이었다."], ["His escape from us, that evening, was beyond all question the preliminary only to his escape from London.", "형제단의 표식이 그의 팔에 있었다. 그가 직접 낙인을 보여 준 것이나 다름없이 나는 확신했다."], ["The mark of the Brotherhood was on his arm—I felt as certain of it as if he had shown me the brand; and the betrayal of the Brotherhood was on his conscience—I had seen it in his recognition of Pesca.", "그리고 형제단에 대한 배신이 그의 양심 위에 얹혀 있었다. 나는 그것을 그가 페스카를 알아보는 눈빛에서 보았다."]], [["It was easy to understand why that recognition had not been mutual.", "어째서 그 알아봄이 서로 오가지 않았는지는 쉽게 이해할 수 있었다."], ["A man of the Count’s character would never risk the terrible consequences of turning spy without looking to his personal security quite as carefully as he looked to his golden reward.", "백작 같은 성격의 사내라면 밀고자가 되는 무서운 결과를 무릅쓰면서, 황금 같은 보수를 챙기는 만큼이나 제 신변의 안전도 세심하게 챙기지 않았을 리 없다. 내가 오페라 극장에서 가리켜 보인 그 말끔히 면도한 얼굴은 페스카가 알던 시절에는 수염에 덮여 있었을지도 모른다."], ["The shaven face, which I had pointed out at the Opera, might have been covered by a beard in Pesca’s time—his dark brown hair might be a wig—his name was evidently a false one.", "짙은 갈색 머리카락은 가발일 수도 있다. 이름은 두말할 것 없이 가명이었다."], ["The accident of time might have helped him as well—his immense corpulence might have come with his later years.", "세월이라는 우연도 그를 도왔을 것이다. 그 어마어마한 비대함은 나중에 나이가 들면서 생긴 것일 수도 있었다."], ["There was every reason why Pesca should not have known him again—every reason also why he should have known Pesca, whose singular personal appearance made a marked man of him, go where he might.", "페스카가 그를 다시 알아보지 못한 데에는 온갖 이유가 있었고, 반대로 그가 페스카를 알아보았을 데에도 온갖 이유가 있었다. 페스카의 유별난 생김새는 어디를 가든 그를 눈에 띄는 사람으로 만들어 놓았으니까."]], [["I have said that I felt certain of the purpose in the Count’s mind when he escaped us at the theatre.", "극장에서 우리를 따돌렸을 때 백작의 마음속에 있던 목적을 나는 확신했다고 앞서 말했다."], ["How could I doubt it, when I saw, with my own eyes, that he believed himself, in spite of the change in his appearance, to have been recognised by Pesca, and to be therefore in danger of his life?", "어찌 의심할 수 있었겠는가. 그가 겉모습을 바꾸었음에도 페스카에게 정체를 들켰다고 스스로 믿고 있으며, 그래서 제 목숨이 위태롭다고 여기고 있음을 내 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보았는데."], ["If I could get speech of him that night, if I could show him that I, too knew of the mortal peril in which he stood, what result would follow?", "만일 내가 그날 밤 그와 말을 나눌 수 있다면, 그가 처한 치명적인 위험을 나 또한 알고 있음을 그에게 내보일 수 있다면, 어떤 결과가 따라올 것인가."], ["Plainly this.", "뻔한 일이다."], ["One of us must be master of the situation—one of us must inevitably be at the mercy of the other.", "우리 둘 가운데 하나가 상황의 주인이 되고, 우리 둘 가운데 하나는 어쩔 수 없이 상대의 처분에 맡겨진다."]], [["I owed it to myself to consider the chances against me before I confronted them.", "맞서기 전에 내게 불리한 승산부터 헤아려 보는 것이 나 자신에 대한 도리였다."], ["I owed it to my wife to do all that lay in my power to lessen the risk.", "위험을 줄이기 위해 내 힘이 미치는 모든 일을 하는 것이 아내에 대한 도리였다."]], [["The chances against me wanted no reckoning up—they were all merged in one.", "내게 불리한 승산은 굳이 셈해 볼 것도 없었다. 모두가 한 가지로 모아졌다."], ["If the Count discovered, by my own avowal, that the direct way to his safety lay through my life, he was probably the last man in existence who would shrink from throwing me off my guard and taking that way, when he had me alone within his reach.", "만일 백작이 내 입으로 하는 고백을 통해 제가 안전해지는 가장 곧은 길이 내 목숨을 지나간다는 사실을 알아차린다면, 나를 혼자 제 손이 닿는 곳에 두고서 내 방심을 틈타 그 길로 나아가기를 주저할 사람은 이 세상에 그가 마지막일 것이다."], ["The only means of defence against him on which I could at all rely to lessen the risk, presented themselves, after a little careful thinking, clearly enough.", "위험을 줄이는 데 조금이라도 기댈 만한 방어 수단은, 잠시 신중히 궁리해 보니 제법 또렷하게 떠올랐다."], ["Before I made any personal acknowledgment of my discovery in his presence, I must place the discovery itself where it would be ready for instant use against him, and safe from any attempt at suppression on his part.", "그의 면전에서 내가 알아낸 것을 스스로 인정하기 전에, 그 알아낸 내용 자체를 그에게 즉시 쓸 수 있도록 준비해 두되 그가 어떻게든 뭉개려 해도 안전한 곳에 놓아두어야 한다."], ["If I laid the mine under his feet before I approached him, and if I left instructions with a third person to fire it on the expiration of a certain time, unless directions to the contrary were previously received under my own hand, or from my own lips—in that event the Count’s security was absolutely dependent upon mine, and I might hold the vantage ground over him securely, even in his own house.", "그에게 다가가기 전에 그의 발밑에 지뢰를 묻어 두고, 정해진 시각이 지나기 전까지 내 손으로 쓴 글이나 내 입으로 하는 말로 취소 지시가 오지 않으면 그것을 터뜨리라고 제삼자에게 일러 둔다면, 그때 백작의 안전은 전적으로 내 안전에 달리게 되고, 나는 그의 집 안에서도 든든하게 우위를 쥘 수 있을 것이다."]], [["This idea occurred to me when I was close to the new lodgings which we had taken on returning from the sea-side.", "이 생각이 떠오른 것은 바닷가에서 돌아온 뒤 우리가 얻은 새 하숙집 가까이에 이르렀을 때였다."], ["I went in without disturbing any one, by the help of my key.", "나는 열쇠를 써서 아무도 깨우지 않고 안으로 들어갔다."], ["A light was in the hall, and I stole up with it to my workroom to make my preparations, and absolutely to commit myself to an interview with the Count, before either Laura or Marian could have the slightest suspicion of what I intended to do.", "현관에 등불이 하나 켜져 있기에 그것을 들고 살그머니 내 작업실로 올라가, 준비를 갖추고 백작과의 면담에 돌이킬 수 없이 나 자신을 묶어 두기로 했다. 로라도 매리언도 내가 무엇을 하려는지 조금도 눈치채지 못하게 하려면 그래야 했다."]], [["A letter addressed to Pesca represented the surest measure of precaution which it was now possible for me to take.", "페스카 앞으로 쓰는 편지가 그때 내가 취할 수 있는 가장 확실한 예방책이었다."], ["I wrote as follows—", "나는 이렇게 썼다."]], [["“The man whom I pointed out to you at the Opera is a member of the Brotherhood, and has been false to his trust.", "“내가 오페라 극장에서 자네에게 가리켜 보인 사내는 형제단의 일원이며, 자신이 맡은 바를 배신했다."], ["Put both these assertions to the test instantly.", "이 두 가지 주장을 지금 당장 시험해 보게."], ["You know the name he goes by in England.", "그가 영국에서 쓰는 이름은 자네도 안다."], ["His address is No. 5 Forest Road, St.", "주소는 세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드 5번지."], ["John’s Wood. On the love you once bore me, use the power entrusted to you without mercy and without delay against that man.", "자네가 한때 내게 품었던 우정을 걸고, 자네에게 맡겨진 권한을 그 사내에게 자비 없이, 지체 없이 행사해 주게."], ["I have risked all and lost all—and the forfeit of my failure has been paid with my life.”", "나는 모든 것을 걸었고 모든 것을 잃었다. 그리고 내 실패의 대가는 내 목숨으로 치렀다.”"]], [["I signed and dated these lines, enclosed them in an envelope, and sealed it up.", "나는 이 글에 서명하고 날짜를 적어 봉투에 넣고 봉인했다."], ["On the outside I wrote this direction: “Keep the enclosure unopened until nine o’clock to-morrow morning.", "겉면에는 이렇게 지시를 써 두었다. “내일 아침 아홉 시까지 안의 편지를 뜯지 말 것."], ["If you do not hear from me, or see me, before that time, break the seal when the clock strikes, and read the contents.”", "그 시각 전에 내 소식을 듣거나 나를 보지 못하거든, 시계가 아홉 시를 칠 때 봉인을 뜯고 내용을 읽을 것.”"], ["I added my initials, and protected the whole by enclosing it in a second sealed envelope, addressed to Pesca at his lodgings.", "거기에 내 머리글자를 덧붙이고, 그 전부를 다시 봉인한 두 번째 봉투에 넣어 페스카의 하숙집 주소로 앞면을 적어 안전하게 만들었다."]], [["Nothing remained to be done after this but to find the means of sending my letter to its destination immediately.", "이제 남은 일은 편지를 즉시 목적지로 보낼 방법을 찾는 것뿐이었다."], ["I should then have accomplished all that lay in my power.", "그러고 나면 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 일은 다 한 셈이었다."], ["If anything happened to me in the Count’s house, I had now provided for his answering it with his life.", "백작의 집에서 내게 무슨 일이 생긴다면, 그 값을 그가 제 목숨으로 치르도록 나는 이미 마련해 둔 것이다."]], [["That the means of preventing his escape, under any circumstances whatever, were at Pesca’s disposal, if he chose to exert them, I did not for an instant doubt.", "어떤 사정에서든 그의 도주를 막을 수단이 페스카의 손안에 있다는 것, 그가 마음만 먹으면 그 수단을 쓸 수 있다는 것을 나는 한순간도 의심하지 않았다."], ["The extraordinary anxiety which he had expressed to remain unenlightened as to the Count’s identity—or, in other words, to be left uncertain enough about facts to justify him to his own conscience in remaining passive—betrayed plainly that the means of exercising the terrible justice of the Brotherhood were ready to his hand, although, as a naturally humane man, he had shrunk from plainly saying as much in my presence.", "백작의 정체에 대해서는 끝까지 모르는 채로 있고 싶다고, 다시 말해 가만히 있는 자신을 제 양심 앞에서 정당화할 만큼은 사실을 모르는 채로 남겨 달라고 그가 그토록 애타게 청했던 것은, 형제단의 무서운 심판을 집행할 수단이 그의 손안에 갖추어져 있음을 뚜렷이 드러내는 일이었다. 다만 천성이 인정 많은 사람인지라 내 앞에서 그것을 대놓고 말하기를 꺼렸을 뿐이다."], ["The deadly certainty with which the vengeance of foreign political societies can hunt down a traitor to the cause, hide himself where he may, had been too often exemplified, even in my superficial experience, to allow of any doubt.", "외국의 정치 결사들이 대의를 배신한 자를 어디에 숨든 기어이 찾아내어 응징하는 그 죽음처럼 확실한 솜씨는, 내 얕은 경험 안에서도 너무 여러 번 실례가 있었기에 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["Considering the subject only as a reader of newspapers, cases recurred to my memory, both in London and in Paris, of foreigners found stabbed in the streets, whose assassins could never be traced—of bodies and parts of bodies thrown into the Thames and the Seine, by hands that could never be discovered—of deaths by secret violence which could only be accounted for in one way.", "그저 신문을 읽는 사람으로서 이 문제를 떠올려 보아도, 런던에서든 파리에서든 거리에서 칼에 찔린 채 발견되었으나 그 자객은 끝내 밝혀지지 않은 외국인들의 사건, 누구의 손인지 결코 알아낼 수 없는 손에 의해 템스강과 센강에 던져진 시신과 시신 조각들, 오직 한 가지로밖에는 설명할 길이 없는 은밀한 폭력에 의한 죽음들이 기억에 되살아났다."], ["I have disguised nothing relating to myself in these pages, and I do not disguise here that I believed I had written Count Fosco’s death-warrant, if the fatal emergency happened which authorised Pesca to open my enclosure.", "나는 이 기록에서 나 자신에 관한 어떤 것도 감추지 않았으며, 여기서도 감추지 않겠다. 페스카가 내 봉투를 뜯을 권한을 갖게 되는 그 치명적인 사태가 벌어진다면, 나는 방금 포스코 백작의 사형 집행 영장을 써 놓은 것이라고 나는 믿고 있었다."]], [["I left my room to go down to the ground floor of the house, and speak to the landlord about finding me a messenger.", "나는 방을 나와 아래층으로 내려갔다. 집주인에게 심부름꾼을 하나 구해 달라고 부탁하기 위해서였다."], ["He happened to be ascending the stairs at the time, and we met on the landing.", "마침 그가 계단을 올라오던 참이어서 우리는 층계참에서 마주쳤다."], ["His son, a quick lad, was the messenger he proposed to me on hearing what I wanted.", "내 용건을 듣고 그가 내놓은 심부름꾼은 제 아들이었는데, 눈치 빠른 아이였다."], ["We had the boy upstairs, and I gave him his directions.", "우리는 소년을 위층으로 불러올렸고 나는 그에게 지시를 일러 주었다."], ["He was to take the letter in a cab, to put it into Professor Pesca’s own hands, and to bring me back a line of acknowledgment from that gentleman—returning in the cab, and keeping it at the door for my use.", "삯마차를 타고 편지를 가져가 페스카 교수의 손에 직접 건네고, 그분에게서 받았다는 한 줄을 받아 오라고, 그리고 그 마차를 타고 돌아와 내가 쓸 수 있도록 문 앞에 세워 두라고 했다."], ["It was then nearly half-past ten.", "그때가 열 시 반이 다 되어 가는 시각이었다."], ["I calculated that the boy might be back in twenty minutes, and that I might drive to St. John’s Wood, on his return, in twenty minutes more.", "나는 소년이 이십 분이면 돌아올 수 있고, 그가 돌아오면 다시 이십 분이면 세인트존스우드까지 갈 수 있으리라고 셈했다."]], [["When the lad had departed on his errand I returned to my own room for a little while, to put certain papers in order, so that they might be easily found in case of the worst.", "소년이 심부름을 떠난 뒤, 나는 잠시 내 방으로 돌아가 서류 몇 가지를 정리해 두었다. 최악의 경우에 쉽게 찾을 수 있게 하기 위해서였다."], ["The key of the old-fashioned bureau in which the papers were kept I sealed up, and left it on my table, with Marian’s name written on the outside of the little packet.", "서류를 넣어 둔 구식 문갑의 열쇠는 봉해서 탁자 위에 두었고, 그 작은 꾸러미 겉면에는 매리언의 이름을 적어 두었다."], ["This done, I went downstairs to the sitting-room, in which I expected to find Laura and Marian awaiting my return from the Opera.", "그러고 나서 나는 아래층 거실로 내려갔다. 로라와 매리언이 오페라에서 돌아올 나를 기다리고 있으리라 여긴 방이었다."], ["I felt my hand trembling for the first time when I laid it on the lock of the door.", "문의 손잡이에 손을 얹는 순간, 나는 처음으로 손이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["No one was in the room but Marian.", "방에는 매리언밖에 없었다."], ["She was reading, and she looked at her watch, in surprise, when I came in.", "그녀는 책을 읽고 있다가, 내가 들어서자 놀라서 시계를 보았다."]], [["“How early you are back!”", "“어쩜 이렇게 일찍 오셨어요!”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You must have come away before the Opera was over.”", "“오페라가 끝나기도 전에 나오신 게 분명하군요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["I replied, “neither Pesca nor I waited for the end.", "내가 대답했다. “페스카도 나도 끝까지 기다리지 않았어."], ["Where is Laura?”", "로라는 어디 있지?”"]], [["“She had one of her bad headaches this evening, and I advised her to go to bed when we had done tea.”", "“오늘 저녁에 그 지독한 두통이 또 도져서, 차를 마시고 나서 자리에 눕는 게 좋겠다고 제가 권했어요.”"]], [["I left the room again on the pretext of wishing to see whether Laura was asleep.", "나는 로라가 잠들었는지 보고 싶다는 핑계를 대고 다시 방을 나섰다."], ["Marian’s quick eyes were beginning to look inquiringly at my face—Marian’s quick instinct was beginning to discover that I had something weighing on my mind.", "매리언의 재빠른 눈이 내 얼굴을 캐묻듯 바라보기 시작했고, 매리언의 재빠른 직감이 내 마음에 무언가 얹혀 있음을 알아채기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["When I entered the bedchamber, and softly approached the bedside by the dim flicker of the night-lamp, my wife was asleep.", "침실에 들어가 희미하게 일렁이는 취침등 불빛을 의지해 살며시 침대 곁으로 다가갔을 때, 아내는 잠들어 있었다."]], [["We had not been married quite a month yet.", "우리가 결혼한 지 아직 한 달도 채 되지 않았다."], ["If my heart was heavy, if my resolution for a moment faltered again, when I looked at her face turned faithfully to my pillow in her sleep—when I saw her hand resting open on the coverlid, as if it was waiting unconsciously for mine—surely there was some excuse for me?", "잠결에도 내 베개 쪽으로 미더이 돌린 그 얼굴을 바라보았을 때, 마치 무의식중에 내 손을 기다리기라도 하는 듯 이불 위에 펼쳐 놓인 그 손을 보았을 때, 내 가슴이 무겁고 내 결심이 한순간 다시 흔들렸다 한들 그만한 변명은 있지 않겠는가."], ["I only allowed myself a few minutes to kneel down at the bedside, and to look close at her—so close that her breath, as it came and went, fluttered on my face.", "나는 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀를 가까이 들여다보는 데 몇 분만을 허락했다. 오가는 숨결이 내 얼굴에 나부낄 만큼 가까이서."], ["I only touched her hand and her cheek with my lips at parting.", "헤어지며 그녀의 손과 뺨에 입술을 댔을 뿐이다."], ["She stirred in her sleep and murmured my name, but without waking.", "그녀는 잠결에 뒤척이며 내 이름을 중얼거렸으나 깨지는 않았다."], ["I lingered for an instant at the door to look at her again.", "나는 문가에서 잠시 머뭇거리며 그녀를 다시 한번 바라보았다."], ["“God bless and keep you, my darling!”", "“하느님이 그대를 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를, 나의 사랑.”"], ["I whispered, and left her.", "나는 속삭이고 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["Marian was at the stairhead waiting for me.", "매리언이 계단 위에서 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She had a folded slip of paper in her hand.", "그녀의 손에는 접힌 종잇조각이 들려 있었다."]], [["“The landlord’s son has brought this for you,”", "“집주인 아들이 이걸 가져왔어요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“He has got a cab at the door—he says you ordered him to keep it at your disposal.”", "“문 앞에 삯마차를 세워 두었대요. 오라버니가 대기시켜 두라고 하셨다더군요.”"]], [["“Quite right, Marian.", "“잘했군, 매리언."], ["I want the cab—I am going out again.”", "그 마차가 필요해. 나는 다시 나가야 해.”"]], [["I descended the stairs as I spoke, and looked into the sitting-room to read the slip of paper by the light on the table.", "말하면서 나는 계단을 내려갔고, 탁자 위의 불빛으로 그 종잇조각을 읽으려고 거실을 들여다보았다."], ["It contained these two sentences in Pesca’s handwriting—", "페스카의 필체로 두 문장이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Your letter is received.", "“자네 편지 받았네."], ["If I don’t see you before the time you mention, I will break the seal when the clock strikes.”", "자네가 말한 시각까지 자네를 보지 못하면, 시계가 그 시각을 칠 때 봉인을 뜯겠네.”"]], [["I placed the paper in my pocket-book, and made for the door.", "나는 그 종이를 수첩에 끼워 넣고 문 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Marian met me on the threshold, and pushed me back into the room, where the candle-light fell full on my face.", "매리언이 문지방에서 나를 맞아 방 안으로 밀어 넣었고, 촛불이 내 얼굴을 환히 비추었다."], ["She held me by both hands, and her eyes fastened searchingly on mine.", "그녀는 두 손으로 나를 붙잡고, 그 눈으로 내 눈을 파고들듯 응시했다."]], [["“I see!”", "“알겠어요!”"], ["she said, in a low eager whisper.", "그녀가 낮고 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다."], ["“You are trying the last chance to-night.”", "“오늘 밤 마지막 기회를 걸어 보시려는 거군요.”"]], [["“Yes, the last chance and the best,”", "“그래, 마지막 기회이자 가장 좋은 기회지.”"], ["I whispered back.", "나도 속삭여 대답했다."]], [["“Not alone!", "“혼자는 안 돼요!"], ["Oh, Walter, for God’s sake, not alone!", "오, 월터, 제발 혼자는 안 돼요!"], ["Let me go with you.", "저도 함께 가게 해 주세요."], ["Don’t refuse me because I’m only a woman.", "제가 한낱 여자라는 이유로 거절하지 마세요."], ["I must go!", "저는 가야 해요!"], ["I will go!", "저는 가겠어요!"], ["I’ll wait outside in the cab!”", "밖에서 마차 안에 앉아 기다릴게요!”"]], [["It was my turn now to hold HER.", "이번에는 내가 그녀를 붙들 차례였다."], ["She tried to break away from me and get down first to the door.", "그녀는 내게서 벗어나 먼저 문으로 내려가려 했다."]], [["“If you want to help me,”", "“나를 돕고 싶거든,”"], ["I said, “stop here and sleep in my wife’s room to-night.", "내가 말했다. “여기 남아서 오늘 밤 아내 방에서 함께 자 줘."], ["Only let me go away with my mind easy about Laura, and I answer for everything else.", "로라 걱정만 놓고 떠날 수 있게 해 주면, 나머지는 다 내가 책임지마."], ["Come, Marian, give me a kiss, and show that you have the courage to wait till I come back.”", "자, 매리언, 입 맞춰 주고, 내가 돌아올 때까지 기다릴 용기가 있다는 걸 보여 다오.”"]], [["I dared not allow her time to say a word more.", "나는 그녀에게 한마디라도 더 할 틈을 감히 주지 않았다."], ["She tried to hold me again.", "그녀가 다시 나를 붙들려 했다."], ["I unclasped her hands, and was out of the room in a moment.", "나는 그 손을 풀고 순식간에 방을 빠져나왔다."], ["The boy below heard me on the stairs, and opened the hall-door.", "아래층의 소년이 계단에서 나는 소리를 듣고 현관문을 열어 주었다."], ["I jumped into the cab before the driver could get off the box.", "마부가 마부석에서 내리기도 전에 나는 마차에 뛰어올랐다."], ["“Forest Road, St. John’s Wood,”", "“세인트존스우드 포리스트 로드로.”"], ["I called to him through the front window.", "나는 앞창 너머로 그에게 외쳤다."], ["“Double fare if you get there in a quarter of an hour.”", "“십오 분 안에 닿으면 삯을 두 배로 주겠소.”"], ["“I’ll do it, sir.”", "“해 보입지요, 나리.”"], ["I looked at my watch.", "나는 시계를 보았다."], ["Eleven o’clock.", "열한 시."], ["Not a minute to lose.", "일 분도 허비할 수 없었다."]], [["The rapid motion of the cab, the sense that every instant now was bringing me nearer to the Count, the conviction that I was embarked at last, without let or hindrance, on my hazardous enterprise, heated me into such a fever of excitement that I shouted to the man to go faster and faster.", "마차의 빠른 움직임, 이제 매 순간이 나를 백작에게 가까이 데려가고 있다는 느낌, 마침내 아무 방해도 걸림도 없이 이 위태로운 일에 몸을 실었다는 확신이 나를 열병 같은 흥분으로 달구어, 나는 마부에게 더 빨리, 더 빨리 달리라고 소리쳤다."], ["As we left the streets, and crossed St. John’s Wood Road, my impatience so completely overpowered me that I stood up in the cab and stretched my head out of the window, to see the end of the journey before we reached it.", "시가지를 벗어나 세인트존스우드 로드를 가로지를 무렵에는 조바심이 나를 완전히 짓눌러, 나는 마차 안에 일어서서 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 아직 닿지도 않은 여정의 끝을 미리 보려 했다."], ["Just as a church clock in the distance struck the quarter past, we turned into the Forest Road.", "멀리서 교회 시계가 십오 분을 알리는 그때, 우리는 포리스트 로드로 접어들었다."], ["I stopped the driver a little away from the Count’s house, paid and dismissed him, and walked on to the door.", "나는 백작의 집에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 마차를 세우게 하고, 삯을 치러 돌려보낸 뒤 그 집 문까지 걸어갔다."]], [["As I approached the garden gate, I saw another person advancing towards it also from the direction opposite to mine.", "정원 문에 다가가는데, 나와 반대쪽 방향에서 또 한 사람이 그리로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["We met under the gas lamp in the road, and looked at each other.", "우리는 길가의 가스등 아래에서 마주쳐 서로를 쳐다보았다."], ["I instantly recognised the light-haired foreigner with the scar on his cheek, and I thought he recognised me.", "나는 뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 금발의 외국인을 대번에 알아보았고, 그도 나를 알아보았다고 생각했다."], ["He said nothing, and instead of stopping at the house, as I did, he slowly walked on.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나처럼 그 집 앞에 멈추는 대신 천천히 걸어 지나갔다."], ["Was he in the Forest Road by accident?", "그가 포리스트 로드에 있는 것은 우연이었을까?"], ["Or had he followed the Count home from the Opera?", "아니면 오페라 극장에서부터 백작을 뒤따라 집까지 온 것일까?"]], [["I did not pursue those questions.", "나는 그 의문들을 좇지 않았다."], ["After waiting a little till the foreigner had slowly passed out of sight, I rang the gate bell.", "그 외국인이 천천히 걸어 시야에서 사라질 때까지 잠시 기다린 뒤, 나는 문의 초인종을 울렸다."], ["It was then twenty minutes past eleven—late enough to make it quite easy for the Count to get rid of me by the excuse that he was in bed.", "그때가 열한 시 이십 분이었다. 백작이 잠자리에 들었다는 핑계로 나를 손쉽게 물리칠 수 있을 만큼 늦은 시각이었다."]], [["The only way of providing against this contingency was to send in my name without asking any preliminary questions, and to let him know, at the same time, that I had a serious motive for wishing to see him at that late hour.", "그런 사태에 대비하는 유일한 길은, 미리 아무것도 묻지 않고 내 이름부터 들여보내면서, 동시에 이렇게 늦은 시각에 그를 만나려는 데에는 심상찮은 이유가 있음을 알리는 것이었다."], ["Accordingly, while I was waiting, I took out my card and wrote under my name “On important business.”", "그래서 기다리는 동안 나는 명함을 꺼내 이름 아래에 “긴한 용무로”라고 적었다."], ["The maid-servant answered the door while I was writing the last word in pencil, and asked me distrustfully what I “pleased to want.”", "마지막 글자를 연필로 쓰고 있을 때 하녀가 문을 열더니, 미심쩍은 얼굴로 “무슨 볼일이시냐”고 물었다."]], [["“Be so good as to take that to your master,”", "“수고스럽겠지만 이것을 주인어른께 갖다 드리게.”"], ["I replied, giving her the card.", "나는 명함을 건네며 대답했다."]], [["I saw, by the girl’s hesitation of manner, that if I had asked for the Count in the first instance she would only have followed her instructions by telling me he was not at home.", "그 아가씨가 머뭇거리는 태도로 보아, 내가 처음부터 백작을 뵙자고 청했더라면 그녀는 지시받은 대로 주인이 댁에 안 계신다고 말했으리라는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["She was staggered by the confidence with which I gave her the card.", "그녀는 내가 명함을 건네는 그 자신만만한 태도에 흔들렸다."], ["After staring at me, in great perturbation, she went back into the house with my message, closing the door, and leaving me to wait in the garden.", "몹시 당황한 얼굴로 나를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그녀는 내 전갈을 들고 집 안으로 들어가면서 문을 닫아 버렸고, 나는 정원에 남아 기다리게 되었다."]], [["In a minute or so she reappeared.", "한 일 분쯤 지나 그녀가 다시 나타났다."], ["“Her master’s compliments, and would I be so obliging as to say what my business was?”", "“주인어른께서 인사 전하시며, 무슨 용무이신지 말씀해 주시면 고맙겠다고 하십니다.”"], ["“Take my compliments back,”", "“내 인사도 전해 드리고,”"], ["I replied, “and say that the business cannot be mentioned to any one but your master.”", "내가 대답했다. “주인어른 말고 다른 어느 누구에게도 말할 수 없는 용무라고 여쭈게.”"], ["She left me again, again returned, and this time asked me to walk in.", "그녀는 다시 나를 두고 갔다가 다시 돌아왔고, 이번에는 안으로 드시라고 청했다."]], [["I followed her at once.", "나는 곧바로 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["In another moment I was inside the Count’s house.", "다음 순간 나는 백작의 집 안에 들어와 있었다."]], [["# VII", "# VII"]], [["There was no lamp in the hall, but by the dim light of the kitchen candle, which the girl had brought upstairs with her, I saw an elderly lady steal noiselessly out of a back room on the ground floor.", "현관에는 등불이 없었으나, 하녀가 들고 올라온 부엌 촛불의 희미한 빛으로 나는 나이 지긋한 부인 하나가 아래층 안쪽 방에서 소리 없이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다."], ["She cast one viperish look at me as I entered the hall, but said nothing, and went slowly upstairs without returning my bow.", "내가 현관에 들어서자 그 부인은 독사 같은 눈길을 한 번 던졌을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 내 인사에 답하지도 않은 채 천천히 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["My familiarity with Marian’s journal sufficiently assured me that the elderly lady was Madame Fosco.", "매리언의 일기를 속속들이 알고 있던 나로서는 그 나이 든 부인이 포스코 백작부인이라는 것을 알아보기에 충분했다."]], [["The servant led me to the room which the Countess had just left.", "하녀는 백작부인이 방금 나온 방으로 나를 안내했다."], ["I entered it, and found myself face to face with the Count.", "나는 그 방에 들어섰고, 백작과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다."]], [["He was still in his evening dress, except his coat, which he had thrown across a chair.", "그는 아직 야회복 차림이었는데, 상의만은 벗어서 의자에 걸쳐 두고 있었다."], ["His shirt-sleeves were turned up at the wrists, but no higher.", "셔츠 소매는 손목께까지 걷어 올렸을 뿐 그 위로는 올리지 않았다."], ["A carpet-bag was on one side of him, and a box on the other.", "한쪽 옆에는 융단 여행 가방이, 다른 쪽에는 궤짝이 놓여 있었다."], ["Books, papers, and articles of wearing apparel were scattered about the room.", "책과 서류와 옷가지가 방 안에 어질러져 있었다."], ["On a table, at one side of the door, stood the cage, so well known to me by description, which contained his white mice.", "문 한쪽 탁자 위에는 이야기로만 익히 들어 알고 있던 그 새장이 놓여 있었고, 그 안에는 그의 흰 쥐들이 들어 있었다."], ["The canaries and the cockatoo were probably in some other room.", "카나리아와 코카투는 아마 다른 방에 있는 모양이었다."], ["He was seated before the box, packing it, when I went in, and rose with some papers in his hand to receive me.", "내가 들어갔을 때 그는 궤짝 앞에 앉아 짐을 꾸리는 중이었는데, 서류 몇 장을 손에 든 채 일어서서 나를 맞았다."], ["His face still betrayed plain traces of the shock that had overwhelmed him at the Opera.", "그의 얼굴에는 오페라 극장에서 그를 덮쳤던 충격의 흔적이 아직도 뚜렷이 남아 있었다."], ["His fat cheeks hung loose, his cold grey eyes were furtively vigilant, his voice, look, and manner were all sharply suspicious alike, as he advanced a step to meet me, and requested, with distant civility, that I would take a chair.", "살진 두 볼은 축 늘어졌고, 차가운 잿빛 눈은 남몰래 경계하고 있었으며, 목소리도 표정도 태도도 하나같이 날카롭게 의심에 차 있었다. 그는 나를 맞으러 한 걸음 나서더니, 서먹한 예의를 갖추어 앉으라고 권했다."]], [["“You come here on business, sir?”", "“업무차 오셨습니까, 선생?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I am at a loss to know what that business can possibly be.”", "“대체 무슨 업무인지 나로서는 도무지 짐작이 가지 않는군요.”"]], [["The unconcealed curiosity, with which he looked hard in my face while he spoke, convinced me that I had passed unnoticed by him at the Opera.", "그가 말하면서 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보는 그 감추지 않은 호기심으로 미루어, 나는 오페라 극장에서 그가 나를 알아보지 못하고 지나쳤음을 확신했다."], ["He had seen Pesca first, and from that moment till he left the theatre he had evidently seen nothing else.", "그는 페스카를 먼저 보았고, 그 순간부터 극장을 떠날 때까지 다른 것은 아무것도 눈에 들어오지 않았던 것이 분명했다."], ["My name would necessarily suggest to him that I had not come into his house with other than a hostile purpose towards himself, but he appeared to be utterly ignorant thus far of the real nature of my errand.", "내 이름을 듣고 나면 내가 그에게 적의를 품고 이 집에 들어왔다는 것쯤은 당연히 짐작하겠지만, 그때까지 그는 내가 찾아온 진짜 용건이 무엇인지는 전혀 모르는 눈치였다."]], [["“I am fortunate in finding you here to-night,” I said.", "“오늘 밤 여기서 백작을 만나게 되어 다행입니다.”"], ["“You seem to be on the point of taking a journey?”", "내가 말했다. “막 여행을 떠나려던 참인 것 같군요?”"]], [["“Is your business connected with my journey?”", "“당신 용건이 내 여행과 관계있는 일이오?”"]], [["“In some degree.”", "“어느 정도는 그렇습니다.”"]], [["“In what degree?", "“어느 정도라니?"], ["Do you know where I am going to?”", "내가 어디로 가는지 아시오?”"]], [["“No.", "“모릅니다."], ["I only know why you are leaving London.”", "다만 당신이 왜 런던을 떠나는지는 압니다.”"]], [["He slipped by me with the quickness of thought, locked the door, and put the key in his pocket.", "그는 생각처럼 빠르게 내 옆을 스쳐 지나가 문을 잠그고, 열쇠를 자기 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["“You and I, Mr. Hartright, are excellently well acquainted with one another by reputation,”", "“하트라이트 씨, 당신과 나는 서로에 대해 소문으로 아주 잘 알고 있지요.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Did it, by any chance, occur to you when you came to this house that I was not the sort of man you could trifle with?”", "“혹시 이 집에 들어올 때, 내가 함부로 놀려 볼 만한 사람이 아니라는 생각은 해 보셨소?”"]], [["“It did occur to me,”", "“해 봤습니다.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“And I have not come to trifle with you.", "“그리고 나는 당신을 놀리러 온 것이 아닙니다."], ["I am here on a matter of life and death, and if that door which you have locked was open at this moment, nothing you could say or do would induce me to pass through it.”", "나는 생사가 걸린 일로 여기 왔고, 당신이 잠근 저 문이 지금 이 순간 열려 있다 해도, 당신이 무슨 말을 하든 무슨 짓을 하든 나를 저 문 밖으로 내보내지는 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["I walked farther into the room, and stood opposite to him on the rug before the fireplace.", "나는 방 안쪽으로 더 걸어 들어가, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에 그와 마주 보고 섰다."], ["He drew a chair in front of the door, and sat down on it, with his left arm resting on the table.", "그는 문 앞으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 거기에 앉아 왼팔을 탁자에 얹었다."], ["The cage with the white mice was close to him, and the little creatures scampered out of their sleeping-place as his heavy arm shook the table, and peered at him through the gaps in the smartly painted wires.", "흰 쥐가 든 새장이 그의 바로 곁에 있었는데, 그의 묵직한 팔이 탁자를 흔들자 작은 것들이 잠자리에서 뛰쳐나와 산뜻하게 칠한 철사 틈으로 그를 내다보았다."]], [["“On a matter of life and death,”", "“생사가 걸린 일이라.”"], ["he repeated to himself.", "그가 혼잣말처럼 되뇌었다."], ["“Those words are more serious, perhaps, than you think.", "“그 말은 아마 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 무거운 말일 거요."], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 뜻이오?”"]], [["“What I say.”", "“말 그대로입니다.”"]], [["The perspiration broke out thickly on his broad forehead.", "그의 넓은 이마에 굵은 땀방울이 배어 나왔다."], ["His left hand stole over the edge of the table.", "왼손이 탁자 가장자리로 슬며시 옮겨 갔다."], ["There was a drawer in it, with a lock, and the key was in the lock.", "거기에는 자물쇠 달린 서랍이 있었고, 열쇠가 그 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있었다."], ["His finger and thumb closed over the key, but did not turn it.", "그의 엄지와 검지가 열쇠를 감쌌지만, 돌리지는 않았다."]], [["“So you know why I am leaving London?” he went on.", "“그러니까 내가 왜 런던을 떠나는지 안다는 말이지요?”"], ["“Tell me the reason, if you please.”", "그가 말을 이었다. “그 이유를 말해 보시오.”"], ["He turned the key, and unlocked the drawer as he spoke.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 열쇠를 돌려 서랍을 열었다."]], [["“I can do better than that,”", "“말보다 더 나은 것을 할 수 있습니다.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“I can SHOW you the reason, if you like.”", "“원하신다면 그 이유를 보여 드리지요.”"]], [["“How can you show it?”", "“어떻게 보여 준다는 거요?”"]], [["“You have got your coat off,”", "“상의를 벗고 계시는군요.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Roll up the shirt-sleeve on your left arm, and you will see it there.”", "“왼팔의 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올려 보십시오. 거기 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["The same livid leaden change passed over his face which I had seen pass over it at the theatre.", "극장에서 그의 얼굴에 스치는 것을 보았던 그 납빛의 창백한 변화가 다시 한번 지나갔다."], ["The deadly glitter in his eyes shone steady and straight into mine. He said nothing.", "그의 두 눈에 어린 살기 어린 광채가 흔들림 없이 곧장 내 눈을 찔러 왔다."], ["But his left hand slowly opened the table-drawer, and softly slipped into it.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 왼손이 천천히 탁자 서랍을 열고 소리 없이 그 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다."], ["The harsh grating noise of something heavy that he was moving unseen to me sounded for a moment, then ceased.", "내 눈에 보이지 않는 무거운 무언가를 움직이는 거친 마찰음이 잠깐 들리다가 그쳤다."], ["The silence that followed was so intense that the faint ticking nibble of the white mice at their wires was distinctly audible where I stood.", "뒤이은 정적이 어찌나 깊던지, 흰 쥐들이 철사를 갉는 희미한 소리가 내가 선 자리에서도 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["My life hung by a thread, and I knew it.", "내 목숨은 실오라기 하나에 걸려 있었고, 나도 그것을 알고 있었다."], ["At that final moment I thought with HIS mind, I felt with HIS fingers—I was as certain as if I had seen it of what he kept hidden from me in the drawer.", "그 마지막 순간에 나는 그의 머리로 생각했고 그의 손가락으로 느꼈다. 그가 서랍 속에 감추어 두고 내게 보이지 않는 것이 무엇인지, 나는 직접 눈으로 본 것이나 다름없이 확신했다."]], [["“Wait a little,”", "“잠깐 기다리십시오.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“You have got the door locked—you see I don’t move—you see my hands are empty.", "“문은 당신이 잠갔습니다—보시다시피 나는 움직이지 않습니다—보시다시피 내 손은 비어 있습니다."], ["Wait a little.", "잠깐만 기다리십시오."], ["I have something more to say.”", "할 말이 더 있습니다.”"]], [["“You have said enough,”", "“할 말은 충분히 하셨소.”"], ["he replied, with a sudden composure so unnatural and so ghastly that it tried my nerves as no outbreak of violence could have tried them.", "그가 대답했다. 그 갑작스러운 침착함은 어찌나 부자연스럽고 소름 끼치던지, 어떤 격렬한 폭발도 그만큼 내 신경을 시험하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["“I want one moment for my own thoughts, if you please.", "“잠깐 내 생각을 정리할 시간을 주시오."], ["Do you guess what I am thinking about?”", "내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 짐작이 가시오?”"]], [["“Perhaps I do.”", "“아마도 갑니다.”"]], [["“I am thinking,”", "“나는 지금,”"], ["he remarked quietly, “whether I shall add to the disorder in this room by scattering your brains about the fireplace.”", "그가 조용히 말했다. “당신 골을 벽난로 앞에 흩뿌려서 이 방의 어지러움을 조금 더 보태 줄까 생각하는 중이오.”"]], [["If I had moved at that moment, I saw in his face that he would have done it.", "그 순간 내가 조금이라도 움직였다면 그는 정말로 그렇게 했으리라는 것을, 나는 그의 얼굴에서 읽었다."]], [["“I advise you to read two lines of writing which I have about me,”", "“그 문제를 최종적으로 결정하시기 전에,” 내가 받아 말했다."], ["I rejoined, “before you finally decide that question.”", "“내가 지니고 있는 두 줄짜리 글을 읽어 보시기를 권합니다.”"]], [["The proposal appeared to excite his curiosity.", "그 제안은 그의 호기심을 자극한 듯했다."], ["He nodded his head.", "그가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["I took Pesca’s acknowledgment of the receipt of my letter out of my pocket-book, handed it to him at arm’s length, and returned to my former position in front of the fireplace.", "나는 내 편지를 받았다는 페스카의 회신을 수첩에서 꺼내 팔을 뻗어 그에게 건네고, 벽난로 앞의 원래 자리로 돌아왔다."]], [["He read the lines aloud: “Your letter is received.", "그는 그 두 줄을 소리 내어 읽었다."], ["If I don’t hear from you before the time you mention, I will break the seal when the clock strikes.”", "“편지 받았소. 당신이 말한 시각까지 당신에게서 소식이 없으면, 시계가 칠 때 봉인을 뜯겠소.”"]], [["Another man in his position would have needed some explanation of those words—the Count felt no such necessity.", "그의 처지에 있는 다른 사람이라면 그 말에 대해 얼마간 설명을 요구했을 것이다—백작은 그럴 필요를 느끼지 않았다."], ["One reading of the note showed him the precaution that I had taken as plainly as if he had been present at the time when I adopted it.", "그 쪽지를 한 번 읽는 것만으로, 그는 내가 취해 둔 대비책을 마치 내가 그 대비를 하던 자리에 함께 있었기라도 한 듯 환히 알아차렸다."], ["The expression of his face changed on the instant, and his hand came out of the drawer empty.", "그의 얼굴빛이 그 즉시 달라졌고, 서랍에서 나온 손은 비어 있었다."]], [["“I don’t lock up my drawer, Mr.", "“하트라이트 씨, 나는 내 서랍을 잠그지 않겠소.”"], ["Hartright,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “and I don’t say that I may not scatter your brains about the fireplace yet.", "“그리고 당신 골을 벽난로 앞에 흩뿌리는 일이 아직 없으리라고 말하지도 않겠소."], ["But I am a just man even to my enemy, and I will acknowledge beforehand that they are cleverer brains than I thought them.", "하지만 나는 적에게도 공정한 사람이오. 그러니 미리 인정해 두지요—당신 머리는 내가 생각했던 것보다 영리한 머리요."], ["Come to the point, sir!", "본론으로 들어갑시다, 선생!"], ["You want something of me?”", "내게서 무언가 원하는 게 있소?”"]], [["“I do, and I mean to have it.”", "“있습니다. 그리고 반드시 얻어 낼 작정입니다.”"]], [["“On conditions?”", "“조건부로?”"]], [["“On no conditions.”", "“아무 조건 없이.”"]], [["His hand dropped into the drawer again.", "그의 손이 다시 서랍 속으로 떨어졌다."]], [["“Bah!", "“쳇!"], ["we are travelling in a circle,”", "우리는 제자리를 맴돌고 있군.”"], ["he said, “and those clever brains of yours are in danger again.", "그가 말했다. “그리고 당신의 그 영리한 머리는 또다시 위험에 처했소."], ["Your tone is deplorably imprudent, sir—moderate it on the spot!", "당신 말투는 한심할 만큼 경솔하오, 선생—당장 누그러뜨리시오!"], ["The risk of shooting you on the place where you stand is less to me than the risk of letting you out of this house, except on conditions that I dictate and approve.", "내가 지시하고 승낙한 조건 없이 당신을 이 집에서 내보내는 위험에 비하면, 당신이 서 있는 그 자리에서 당신을 쏘아 버리는 위험쯤은 내게 아무것도 아니오."], ["You have not got my lamented friend to deal with now—you are face to face with Fosco! If the lives of twenty Mr.", "지금 당신이 상대하는 것은 애도해 마지않는 내 친구가 아니오—당신은 포스코와 얼굴을 마주하고 있소!"], ["Hartrights were the stepping-stones to my safety, over all those stones I would go, sustained by my sublime indifference, self-balanced by my impenetrable calm.", "하트라이트 씨 스무 명의 목숨이 내 안전으로 가는 징검돌이라면, 나는 그 돌을 모조리 밟고 건너갈 것이오. 나의 숭고한 무심함에 떠받쳐지고, 나의 뚫을 수 없는 평정으로 균형을 잡은 채 말이오."], ["Respect me, if you love your own life!", "당신 목숨이 아깝거든 나를 존중하시오!"], ["I summon you to answer three questions before you open your lips again.", "다시 입을 열기 전에 세 가지 질문에 답할 것을 요구하오."], ["Hear them—they are necessary to this interview.", "들으시오—이 면담에 필요한 질문들이오."], ["Answer them—they are necessary to ME.”", "답하시오—내게 필요한 것들이오.”"], ["He held up one finger of his right hand.", "그는 오른손 손가락 하나를 세웠다."], ["“First question!”", "“첫 번째 질문!”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You come here possessed of information which may be true or may be false—where did you get it?”", "“당신은 참일 수도 있고 거짓일 수도 있는 정보를 가지고 여기 왔소—그것을 어디서 얻었소?”"]], [["“I decline to tell you.”", "“말하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["“No matter—I shall find out.", "“상관없소—내가 알아낼 테니."], ["If that information is true—mind I say, with the whole force of my resolution, if—you are making your market of it here by treachery of your own or by treachery of some other man.", "만일 그 정보가 참이라면—내 결심의 온 힘을 다해 말하건대, 만일이오—당신은 당신 자신의 배신이든 다른 누군가의 배신이든 그것을 밑천 삼아 여기서 값을 흥정하고 있는 것이오. 나는 그 정황을 뒷날 쓰기 위해 내 기억에 새겨 두겠소."], ["I note that circumstance for future use in my memory, which forgets nothing, and proceed.”", "내 기억은 무엇 하나 잊는 법이 없소. 그러면 다음으로 넘어가지요.”"], ["He held up another finger.", "그는 손가락을 하나 더 세웠다."], ["“Second question!", "“두 번째 질문!"], ["Those lines you invited me to read are without signature.", "당신이 읽어 보라고 준 그 두 줄에는 서명이 없소."], ["Who wrote them?”", "누가 썼소?”"]], [["“A man whom I have every reason to depend on, and whom you have every reason to fear.”", "“내가 믿을 만한 온갖 이유가 있는 사람, 그리고 당신이 두려워할 만한 온갖 이유가 있는 사람입니다.”"]], [["My answer reached him to some purpose.", "내 대답은 얼마간 효과가 있었다."], ["His left hand trembled audibly in the drawer.", "서랍 속에서 그의 왼손이 소리가 들릴 만큼 떨렸다."]], [["“How long do you give me,”", "“시계가 치고 봉인이 뜯길 때까지,”"], ["he asked, putting his third question in a quieter tone, “before the clock strikes and the seal is broken?”", "그가 세 번째 질문을 한결 낮은 어조로 던지며 물었다. “내게 얼마나 시간을 주겠소?”"]], [["“Time enough for you to come to my terms,”", "“당신이 내 조건을 받아들이기에 충분한 시간입니다.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["“Give me a plainer answer, Mr. Hartright.", "“더 분명히 대답하시오, 하트라이트 씨."], ["What hour is the clock to strike?”", "그 시계가 몇 시를 치오?”"]], [["“Nine, to-morrow morning.”", "“내일 아침 9시입니다.”"]], [["“Nine, to-morrow morning?", "“내일 아침 9시라?"], ["Yes, yes—your trap is laid for me before I can get my passport regulated and leave London.", "그렇지, 그렇지—내가 여권 수속을 마치고 런던을 떠나기도 전에 덫을 놓아 두었군."], ["It is not earlier, I suppose?", "그보다 이르지는 않겠지요?"], ["We will see about that presently—I can keep you hostage here, and bargain with you to send for your letter before I let you go.", "그건 곧 알아보기로 합시다—나는 당신을 여기 인질로 잡아 둘 수도 있고, 당신을 놓아주기 전에 사람을 보내 그 편지를 가져오게 하라고 흥정할 수도 있으니까."], ["In the meantime, be so good next as to mention your terms.”", "그건 그렇고, 이제 당신 조건을 말해 보시오.”"]], [["“You shall hear them.", "“말씀드리지요."], ["They are simple, and soon stated.", "간단하고, 곧 끝납니다."], ["You know whose interests I represent in coming here?”", "내가 여기 오면서 누구의 이익을 대변하고 있는지 아십니까?”"]], [["He smiled with the most supreme composure, and carelessly waved his right hand.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 침착한 미소를 지으며 오른손을 무심히 저었다."]], [["“I consent to hazard a guess,”", "“한번 맞혀 보기로 하지요.”"], ["he said jeeringly.", "그가 비웃듯 말했다."], ["“A lady’s interests, of course!”", "“물론 어느 부인의 이익이겠지!”"]], [["“My Wife’s interests.”", "“내 아내의 이익입니다.”"]], [["He looked at me with the first honest expression that had crossed his face in my presence—an expression of blank amazement.", "그는 내 앞에서 처음으로 꾸밈없는 표정을 지으며 나를 바라보았다—그것은 완전한 경악의 표정이었다."], ["I could see that I sank in his estimation as a dangerous man from that moment.", "그 순간부터 위험한 인물로서의 내 값이 그의 눈에서 뚝 떨어지는 것이 보였다."], ["He shut up the drawer at once, folded his arms over his breast, and listened to me with a smile of satirical attention.", "그는 즉시 서랍을 닫고, 두 팔을 가슴 위로 포개고, 빈정거리는 주의를 담은 미소를 띤 채 내 말에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“You are well enough aware,”", "“지난 여러 달 동안 내 조사가 어떤 길을 밟아 왔는지 당신도 충분히 알고 있을 테니,”"], ["I went on, “of the course which my inquiries have taken for many months past, to know that any attempted denial of plain facts will be quite useless in my presence.", "내가 말을 이었다. “내 앞에서 명백한 사실을 부인해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다는 것도 알 겁니다."], ["You are guilty of an infamous conspiracy!", "당신은 파렴치한 음모를 저지른 죄인입니다!"], ["And the gain of a fortune of ten thousand pounds was your motive for it.”", "그리고 만 파운드의 재산을 손에 넣는 것이 그 동기였습니다.”"]], [["He said nothing.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["But his face became overclouded suddenly by a lowering anxiety.", "그러나 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 침울한 불안으로 어두워졌다."]], [["“Keep your gain,” I said.", "“그 돈은 당신이 가지십시오.”"], ["(His face lightened again immediately, and his eyes opened on me in wider and wider astonishment.) “I am not here to disgrace myself by bargaining for money which has passed through your hands, and which has been the price of a vile crime——”", "내가 말했다. (그의 얼굴이 즉시 다시 밝아졌고, 두 눈이 점점 더 크게 놀라며 나를 향해 열렸다.) “나는 당신 손을 거쳐 간 돈, 비열한 범죄의 값이었던 돈을 흥정해서 스스로를 욕되게 하려고 여기 온 것이 아닙니다——”"]], [["“Gently, Mr. Hartright.", "“진정하시오, 하트라이트 씨."], ["Your moral clap-traps have an excellent effect in England—keep them for yourself and your own countrymen, if you please.", "당신네 도덕적 상투어는 영국에서라면 아주 훌륭한 효과를 내지—부디 당신 자신과 당신 동포들에게나 쓰시오."], ["The ten thousand pounds was a legacy left to my excellent wife by the late Mr. Fairlie.", "그 만 파운드는 고인이 된 페얼리 씨가 나의 훌륭한 아내에게 남긴 유산이오."], ["Place the affair on those grounds, and I will discuss it if you like.", "그 문제를 그런 토대 위에 올려놓는다면 원하는 대로 논해 보겠소."], ["To a man of my sentiments, however, the subject is deplorably sordid.", "그러나 나 같은 감정을 지닌 사람에게 그 화제는 한심할 만큼 천박하오."], ["I prefer to pass it over.", "그냥 넘어가는 편이 좋겠소."], ["I invite you to resume the discussion of your terms.", "당신 조건 이야기로 돌아갑시다."], ["What do you demand?”", "무엇을 요구하시오?”"]], [["“In the first place, I demand a full confession of the conspiracy, written and signed in my presence by yourself.”", "“첫째, 나는 그 음모에 관한 완전한 자백을 요구합니다. 내가 보는 앞에서 당신 손으로 쓰고 서명한 것이어야 합니다.”"]], [["He raised his finger again.", "그는 다시 손가락을 세웠다."], ["“One!”", "“하나!”"], ["he said, checking me off with the steady attention of a practical man.", "그가 말하며 실무가다운 차분한 주의력으로 내 요구를 하나씩 헤아렸다."]], [["“In the second place, I demand a plain proof, which does not depend on your personal asseveration, of the date at which my wife left Blackwater Park and travelled to London.”", "“둘째, 나는 당신의 개인적인 단언에 기대지 않는 명백한 증거를 요구합니다. 내 아내가 블랙워터 파크를 떠나 런던으로 간 날짜에 관한 증거 말입니다.”"]], [["“So! so!", "“호오!"], ["you can lay your finger, I see, on the weak place,”", "호오! 당신은 약한 지점에 손가락을 짚을 줄 아는군.”"], ["he remarked composedly.", "그가 태연하게 말했다."], ["“Any more?”", "“더 있소?”"]], [["“At present, no more.”", "“지금으로서는 더 없습니다.”"]], [["“Good!", "“좋소!"], ["you have mentioned your terms, now listen to mine.", "당신 조건은 들었으니, 이제 내 조건을 들으시오."], ["The responsibility to myself of admitting what you are pleased to call the ‘conspiracy’ is less, perhaps, upon the whole, than the responsibility of laying you dead on that hearthrug.", "당신이 굳이 ‘음모’라 부르는 것을 인정함으로써 내가 지게 될 책임은, 아마도 전체적으로 보아 당신을 저 난롯가 깔개 위에 시체로 눕히는 책임보다는 가벼울 거요."], ["Let us say that I meet your proposal—on my own conditions.", "그러니 내가 당신 제안을 받아들이겠다고 해 둡시다—내 조건 아래서 말이오."], ["The statement you demand of me shall be written, and the plain proof shall be produced.", "당신이 요구하는 진술서는 작성될 것이고, 명백한 증거도 제시될 거요."], ["You call a letter from my late lamented friend informing me of the day and hour of his wife’s arrival in London, written, signed, and dated by himself, a proof, I suppose?", "애석하게도 고인이 된 내 친구가 자기 아내가 런던에 도착하는 날짜와 시각을 알려 온 편지, 그가 손수 쓰고 서명하고 날짜를 적은 그 편지라면 증거가 되겠지요?"], ["I can give you this.", "그건 내가 줄 수 있소."], ["I can also send you to the man of whom I hired the carriage to fetch my visitor from the railway, on the day when she arrived—his order-book may help you to your date, even if his coachman who drove me proves to be of no use.", "또 그 여인이 도착하던 날 철도역에서 내 손님을 데려오도록 내가 마차를 빌렸던 사람에게 당신을 보낼 수도 있소—그 사람의 예약 장부가 날짜를 찾는 데 도움이 될 거요. 설령 나를 태우고 몰았던 마부가 아무 쓸모 없는 것으로 드러나더라도 말이오."], ["These things I can do, and will do, on conditions.", "이런 일들을 나는 할 수 있고, 또 하겠소—조건부로."], ["I recite them. First condition!", "그 조건을 읊어 보겠소."], ["Madame Fosco and I leave this house when and how we please, without interference of any kind on your part.", "첫째 조건! 포스코 부인과 나는 우리가 원하는 때에 원하는 방식으로 이 집을 떠나며, 당신은 어떤 식으로도 방해하지 않는다."], ["Second condition!", "둘째 조건!"], ["You wait here, in company with me, to see my agent, who is coming at seven o’clock in the morning to regulate my affairs.", "당신은 나와 함께 여기서 기다려, 아침 7시에 내 일을 정리하러 오는 내 대리인을 만난다."], ["You give my agent a written order to the man who has got your sealed letter to resign his possession of it.", "당신은 그 대리인에게, 당신의 봉인된 편지를 가지고 있는 사람 앞으로 그 편지를 내놓으라는 서면 지시를 써 준다."], ["You wait here till my agent places that letter unopened in my hands, and you then allow me one clear half-hour to leave the house—after which you resume your own freedom of action and go where you please.", "당신은 내 대리인이 그 편지를 뜯지 않은 채로 내 손에 놓을 때까지 여기서 기다리고, 그런 뒤 내가 이 집을 떠날 수 있도록 꼬박 30분을 준다—그다음에는 당신의 행동의 자유를 되찾아 어디로든 가면 되오."], ["Third condition!", "셋째 조건!"], ["You give me the satisfaction of a gentleman for your intrusion into my private affairs, and for the language you have allowed yourself to use to me at this conference.", "당신은 내 사사로운 일에 끼어든 것과, 이 자리에서 감히 내게 쓴 언사에 대하여 신사로서의 결투를 내게 허락한다."], ["The time and place, abroad, to be fixed in a letter from my hand when I am safe on the Continent, and that letter to contain a strip of paper measuring accurately the length of my sword.", "시간과 장소는 국외로 하되, 내가 대륙에서 안전해졌을 때 내 손으로 쓴 편지로 정해 알리겠소. 그리고 그 편지에는 내 검의 길이를 정확히 잰 종잇조각이 들어 있을 거요."], ["Those are my terms.", "이것이 내 조건이오."], ["Inform me if you accept them—Yes or No.”", "받아들이는지 아닌지 알려 주시오—예스요, 노요.”"]], [["The extraordinary mixture of prompt decision, far-sighted cunning, and mountebank bravado in this speech, staggered me for a moment—and only for a moment.", "이 말 속에 뒤섞인 즉각적인 결단과 멀리 내다보는 교활함과 협잡꾼 같은 허세의 그 기묘한 배합은 나를 한순간 휘청이게 했다—그러나 한순간뿐이었다."], ["The one question to consider was, whether I was justified or not in possessing myself of the means of establishing Laura’s identity at the cost of allowing the scoundrel who had robbed her of it to escape me with impunity.", "고려할 문제는 오직 하나였다. 로라의 신원을 밝힐 수단을 손에 넣기 위하여, 그 신원을 그녀에게서 빼앗은 악당이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 내 손을 벗어나도록 놓아주는 것이 과연 정당한가 하는 것이었다."], ["I knew that the motive of securing the just recognition of my wife in the birthplace from which she had been driven out as an impostor, and of publicly erasing the lie that still profaned her mother’s tombstone, was far purer, in its freedom from all taint of evil passion, than the vindictive motive which had mingled itself with my purpose from the first.", "사기꾼으로 몰려 쫓겨났던 고향 땅에서 내 아내가 정당한 인정을 되찾게 하고, 아직도 그 어머니의 묘비를 더럽히고 있는 거짓말을 세상 앞에서 지우려는 동기가, 처음부터 내 목적에 섞여 들어 있던 복수의 동기보다 훨씬 순수하며 악한 정념의 얼룩이 없다는 것을 나는 알고 있었다."], ["And yet I cannot honestly say that my own moral convictions were strong enough to decide the struggle in me by themselves.", "그러면서도 솔직히 말해, 내 안의 싸움을 그 도덕적 확신만으로 판가름 낼 만큼 그것이 강했다고는 말할 수 없다."], ["They were helped by my remembrance of Sir Percival’s death.", "거기에는 퍼시벌 경의 죽음에 대한 기억이 힘을 보탰다."], ["How awfully, at the last moment, had the working of the retribution THERE been snatched from my feeble hands!", "그 마지막 순간에, 그곳에서 응보의 손길은 얼마나 무섭게도 내 힘없는 손에서 낚아채였던가!"], ["What right had I to decide, in my poor mortal ignorance of the future, that this man, too, must escape with impunity because he escaped ME?", "미래에 대해 아무것도 모르는 가련한 인간의 무지 속에서, 이 사람 역시 내게서 벗어났으니 아무 벌도 받지 않고 빠져나가리라고 단정할 권리가 내게 무엇이 있단 말인가?"], ["I thought of these things—perhaps with the superstition inherent in my nature, perhaps with a sense worthier of me than superstition.", "나는 그런 것들을 생각했다—어쩌면 내 천성에 배어 있는 미신으로, 어쩌면 미신보다는 나에게 더 어울리는 감각으로."], ["It was hard, when I had fastened my hold on him at last, to loosen it again of my own accord—but I forced myself to make the sacrifice.", "마침내 그를 붙잡았는데 내 발로 그 손을 다시 푸는 것은 괴로운 일이었다—그러나 나는 그 희생을 치르도록 나 자신을 몰아세웠다."], ["In plainer words, I determined to be guided by the one higher motive of which I was certain, the motive of serving the cause of Laura and the cause of Truth.", "더 쉽게 말하자면, 나는 내가 확신하는 단 하나의 더 높은 동기, 곧 로라의 대의와 진실의 대의에 봉사한다는 동기를 따르기로 결심했다."]], [["“I accept your conditions,”", "“당신 조건을 받아들이겠습니다.”"], ["I said.", "내가 말했다."], ["“With one reservation on my part.”", "“다만 내 쪽에서 한 가지 유보를 두겠습니다.”"]], [["“What reservation may that be?”", "“그 유보란 무엇이오?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“It refers to the sealed letter,”", "“봉인된 편지에 관한 것입니다.”"], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“I require you to destroy it unopened in my presence as soon as it is placed in your hands.”", "“그 편지가 당신 손에 들어오는 즉시, 내가 보는 앞에서 뜯지 않은 채로 없애기를 요구합니다.”"]], [["My object in making this stipulation was simply to prevent him from carrying away written evidence of the nature of my communication with Pesca.", "내가 이 조항을 내건 목적은 단지, 내가 페스카와 주고받은 연락의 내용을 적은 증거를 그가 들고 가지 못하게 하려는 데 있었다."], ["The fact of my communication he would necessarily discover, when I gave the address to his agent in the morning.", "내가 연락을 취했다는 사실 자체는 아침에 내가 그의 대리인에게 주소를 적어 주는 순간 그가 알아차릴 수밖에 없었다."], ["But he could make no use of it on his own unsupported testimony—even if he really ventured to try the experiment—which need excite in me the slightest apprehension on Pesca’s account.", "그러나 뒷받침할 것 없는 그 자신의 증언만으로는—설령 그가 정말로 그 모험을 해 볼 마음을 먹는다 해도—페스카를 두고 내가 조금이라도 불안해할 만한 쓸모를 얻어 낼 수는 없을 터였다."]], [["“I grant your reservation,”", "“당신 유보를 받아들이겠소.”"], ["he replied, after considering the question gravely for a minute or two.", "그는 그 문제를 일이 분 남짓 진지하게 헤아려 본 뒤에 대답했다."], ["“It is not worth dispute—the letter shall be destroyed when it comes into my hands.”", "“다툴 값어치가 없는 일이오—그 편지는 내 손에 들어오는 대로 없애겠소.”"]], [["He rose, as he spoke, from the chair in which he had been sitting opposite to me up to this time.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 그때까지 나와 마주 보고 앉아 있던 의자에서 일어났다."], ["With one effort he appeared to free his mind from the whole pressure on it of the interview between us thus far.", "그는 단 한 번의 몸짓으로, 여기까지의 면담이 그의 마음에 지운 짐 전체를 훌훌 털어 버린 듯했다."], ["“Ouf!”", "“우프!”"], ["he cried, stretching his arms luxuriously, “the skirmish was hot while it lasted.", "그가 두 팔을 흐드러지게 뻗으며 외쳤다. “접전이 벌어지는 동안은 제법 뜨거웠소."], ["Take a seat, Mr. Hartright.", "앉으시오, 하트라이트 씨."], ["We meet as mortal enemies hereafter—let us, like gallant gentlemen, exchange polite attentions in the meantime.", "앞으로 우리는 불구대천의 원수로 만나게 되겠지만—그때까지는 의젓한 신사답게 정중한 예를 주고받읍시다."], ["Permit me to take the liberty of calling for my wife.”", "실례를 무릅쓰고 내 아내를 부르겠소.”"]], [["He unlocked and opened the door.", "그는 잠긴 문을 열고 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["“Eleanor!”", "“엘리너!”"], ["he called out in his deep voice.", "그가 굵은 목소리로 불렀다."], ["The lady of the viperish face came in.", "독사 같은 얼굴의 부인이 들어왔다."], ["“Madame Fosco—Mr. Hartright,”", "“포스코 부인—하트라이트 씨요.”"], ["said the Count, introducing us with easy dignity.", "백작이 여유로운 위엄을 갖추어 우리를 서로에게 소개했다."], ["“My angel,”", "“나의 천사,”"], ["he went on, addressing his wife, “will your labours of packing up allow you time to make me some nice strong coffee?", "그가 아내를 향해 말을 이었다. “짐 꾸리는 일 중에도 나를 위해 진하고 좋은 커피를 좀 끓여 줄 짬이 나겠소?"], ["I have writing business to transact with Mr. Hartright—and I require the full possession of my intelligence to do justice to myself.”", "하트라이트 씨와 처리할 글 쓰는 일이 있는데—나 자신에게 부끄럽지 않으려면 내 지력을 온전히 지녀야 하거든.”"]], [["Madame Fosco bowed her head twice—once sternly to me, once submissively to her husband, and glided out of the room.", "포스코 부인은 두 번 고개를 숙였다—한 번은 나를 향해 엄하게, 한 번은 남편을 향해 순종적으로—그러고는 미끄러지듯 방을 나갔다."]], [["The Count walked to a writing-table near the window, opened his desk, and took from it several quires of paper and a bundle of quill pens.", "백작은 창가의 필기 탁자로 걸어가 책상을 열고, 종이 여러 첩과 깃펜 한 다발을 꺼냈다."], ["He scattered the pens about the table, so that they might lie ready in all directions to be taken up when wanted, and then cut the paper into a heap of narrow slips, of the form used by professional writers for the press.", "그는 펜을 탁자 위에 흩어 놓아 어느 방향에서든 필요할 때 곧바로 집을 수 있게 해 두고, 그런 다음 종이를 잘라 신문사에 글을 대는 문필가들이 쓰는 형태의 좁고 긴 쪽지 더미를 만들었다."], ["“I shall make this a remarkable document,”", "“이것을 주목할 만한 문서로 만들어 보이겠소.”"], ["he said, looking at me over his shoulder.", "그가 어깨 너머로 나를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“Habits of literary composition are perfectly familiar to me.", "“글을 짓는 습관이라면 나는 아주 익숙하오."], ["One of the rarest of all the intellectual accomplishments that a man can possess is the grand faculty of arranging his ideas.", "사람이 지닐 수 있는 온갖 지적 재능 가운데 가장 드문 것 하나가 자기 생각을 정연하게 배열하는 위대한 능력이오. 엄청난 특권이지!"], ["Immense privilege!", "나는 그것을 가지고 있소."], ["I possess it. Do you?”", "당신은 어떻소?”"]], [["He marched backwards and forwards in the room, until the coffee appeared, humming to himself, and marking the places at which obstacles occurred in the arrangement of his ideas, by striking his forehead from time to time with the palm of his hand.", "그는 커피가 나올 때까지 방 안을 왔다 갔다 행진하며 콧노래를 흥얼거렸고, 생각을 배열하는 데 걸림돌이 나타나는 대목마다 이따금 손바닥으로 자기 이마를 쳐서 표를 해 두었다."], ["The enormous audacity with which he seized on the situation in which I placed him, and made it the pedestal on which his vanity mounted for the one cherished purpose of self-display, mastered my astonishment by main force.", "내가 그를 몰아넣은 처지를 오히려 움켜쥐고서, 그것을 자기 과시라는 그 소중한 목적을 위해 허영심이 올라설 받침대로 삼는 그 엄청난 대담함은 힘으로 밀어붙이듯 내 놀라움을 압도했다."], ["Sincerely as I loathed the man, the prodigious strength of his character, even in its most trivial aspects, impressed me in spite of myself.", "나는 그를 진심으로 혐오했지만, 그 성격의 가장 사소한 면모에서까지 드러나는 어마어마한 힘은 나도 모르게 나를 감탄시켰다."]], [["The coffee was brought in by Madame Fosco.", "커피는 포스코 부인이 들여왔다."], ["He kissed her hand in grateful acknowledgment, and escorted her to the door; returned, poured out a cup of coffee for himself, and took it to the writing-table.", "그는 고마움을 담아 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 문까지 배웅한 뒤, 돌아와서 자기 몫의 커피를 한 잔 따라 필기 탁자로 가져갔다."]], [["“May I offer you some coffee, Mr. Hartright?”", "“하트라이트 씨, 커피 좀 드시겠소?”"], ["he said, before he sat down.", "그가 앉기 전에 물었다."]], [["I declined.", "나는 사양했다."]], [["“What!", "“아니!"], ["you think I shall poison you?”", "내가 당신을 독살할 거라고 생각하시오?”"], ["he said gaily.", "그가 유쾌하게 말했다."], ["“The English intellect is sound, so far as it goes,”", "“영국인의 지성은 미치는 데까지는 건전하오.”"], ["he continued, seating himself at the table; “but it has one grave defect—it is always cautious in the wrong place.”", "그가 탁자 앞에 앉으며 말을 이었다. “하지만 한 가지 중대한 결함이 있소—언제나 엉뚱한 데서 조심을 한단 말이오.”"]], [["He dipped his pen in the ink, placed the first slip of paper before him with a thump of his hand on the desk, cleared his throat, and began.", "그는 펜을 잉크에 적시고, 첫 번째 쪽지를 앞에 놓고, 책상을 손바닥으로 탁 치고, 목을 가다듬고, 시작했다."], ["He wrote with great noise and rapidity, in so large and bold a hand, and with such wide spaces between the lines, that he reached the bottom of the slip in not more than two minutes certainly from the time when he started at the top.", "그는 요란하고 빠르게, 아주 크고 굵은 글씨로 행간을 넓게 벌려 가며 써 내려갔으므로, 쪽지 맨 위에서 시작해 맨 아래에 이르기까지 확실히 2분이 넘게 걸리지 않았다."], ["Each slip as he finished it was paged, and tossed over his shoulder out of his way on the floor.", "한 장을 끝낼 때마다 쪽수를 매기고 어깨 너머 바닥으로 던져 치웠다."], ["When his first pen was worn out, THAT went over his shoulder too, and he pounced on a second from the supply scattered about the table.", "첫 번째 펜이 다 닳자 그것 역시 어깨 너머로 날아갔고, 그는 탁자에 흩어 놓은 것들 가운데서 두 번째 펜을 낚아챘다."], ["Slip after slip, by dozens, by fifties, by hundreds, flew over his shoulders on either side of him till he had snowed himself up in paper all round his chair.", "쪽지가 한 장 한 장, 열 장씩, 쉰 장씩, 백 장씩 그의 양쪽 어깨 너머로 날아가, 마침내 의자 둘레에 종이 눈이 쌓여 그를 파묻어 버렸다."], ["Hour after hour passed—and there I sat watching, there he sat writing.", "몇 시간이 흐르고 또 흘렀다—나는 그 자리에 앉아 보고 있었고, 그는 그 자리에 앉아 쓰고 있었다."], ["He never stopped, except to sip his coffee, and when that was exhausted, to smack his forehead from time to time.", "그는 커피를 홀짝일 때 말고는, 그리고 커피가 다 떨어진 뒤로 이따금 이마를 탁 칠 때 말고는 멈추는 법이 없었다."], ["One o’clock struck, two, three, four—and still the slips flew about all round him; still the untiring pen scraped its way ceaselessly from top to bottom of the page, still the white chaos of paper rose higher and higher all round his chair.", "1시를 알리는 종이 울리고, 2시, 3시, 4시—그래도 여전히 쪽지는 그의 사방으로 날아다녔고, 여전히 지칠 줄 모르는 펜은 쉬지 않고 종이 위에서 아래로 사각거리며 나아갔으며, 여전히 하얀 종이의 혼돈은 그의 의자 둘레에서 점점 더 높이 쌓여 갔다."], ["At four o’clock I heard a sudden splutter of the pen, indicative of the flourish with which he signed his name.", "4시가 되었을 때 나는 펜이 갑자기 튀는 소리를 들었다. 그가 자기 이름에 장식 획을 그으며 서명하는 소리였다."], ["“Bravo!”", "“브라보!”"], ["he cried, springing to his feet with the activity of a young man, and looking me straight in the face with a smile of superb triumph.", "그가 젊은이 같은 활기로 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 더없이 의기양양한 미소를 띠고 내 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["“Done, Mr. Hartright!”", "“다 됐소, 하트라이트 씨!”"], ["he announced with a self-renovating thump of his fist on his broad breast.", "그가 자신을 되살리듯 넓은 가슴을 주먹으로 쿵 치며 알렸다."], ["“Done, to my own profound satisfaction—to YOUR profound astonishment, when you read what I have written.", "“다 됐소—나 자신은 깊이 만족스럽게, 당신은 내가 쓴 것을 읽으면 깊이 경악하게 될 만큼!"], ["The subject is exhausted: the man—Fosco—is not.", "주제는 소진되었으나 사람은—포스코라는 사람은 소진되지 않았소."], ["I proceed to the arrangement of my slips—to the revision of my slips—to the reading of my slips—addressed emphatically to your private ear.", "이제 나는 내 쪽지들을 배열하는 일로, 내 쪽지들을 손보는 일로, 내 쪽지들을 낭독하는 일로 나아가겠소—오직 당신 한 사람의 귀를 향하여 힘주어 읽어 드리지."], ["Four o’clock has just struck.", "방금 4시를 쳤소."], ["Good!", "좋소!"], ["Arrangement, revision, reading, from four to five.", "배열과 수정과 낭독, 4시부터 5시까지."], ["Short snooze of restoration for myself from five to six.", "원기를 회복할 짧은 선잠, 5시부터 6시까지."], ["Final preparations from six to seven.", "마지막 준비, 6시부터 7시까지."], ["Affair of agent and sealed letter from seven to eight.", "대리인과 봉인된 편지 건, 7시부터 8시까지."], ["At eight, en route.", "8시에 출발."], ["Behold the programme!”", "보시오, 이것이 일정표요!”"]], [["He sat down cross-legged on the floor among his papers, strung them together with a bodkin and a piece of string—revised them, wrote all the titles and honours by which he was personally distinguished at the head of the first page, and then read the manuscript to me with loud theatrical emphasis and profuse theatrical gesticulation.", "그는 종이 더미 속에 책상다리를 하고 바닥에 앉아, 송곳과 노끈으로 쪽지들을 꿰어 묶고—손을 보고—자신에게 딸린 온갖 칭호와 훈위를 첫 장 머리에 죄다 써넣은 다음, 우렁찬 연극조의 억양과 넘쳐 나는 연극조의 몸짓을 곁들여 그 원고를 내게 읽어 주었다."], ["The reader will have an opportunity, ere long, of forming his own opinion of the document.", "독자도 머지않아 그 문서에 대해 스스로 판단할 기회를 얻게 될 것이다."], ["It will be sufficient to mention here that it answered my purpose.", "여기서는 그것이 내 목적에 부합했다는 사실만 말해 두면 충분하겠다."]], [["He next wrote me the address of the person from whom he had hired the fly, and handed me Sir Percival’s letter.", "그는 이어서 자기가 삯마차를 빌렸던 사람의 주소를 적어 주고, 퍼시벌 경의 편지를 내게 건넸다."], ["It was dated from Hampshire on the 25th of July, and it announced the journey of “Lady Glyde” to London on the 26th.", "그 편지는 7월 25일에 햄프셔에서 쓴 것이었고, “글라이드 부인”이 26일에 런던으로 간다는 소식을 알리고 있었다."], ["Thus, on the very day (the 25th) when the doctor’s certificate declared that she had died in St. John’s Wood, she was alive, by Sir Percival’s own showing, at Blackwater—and, on the day after, she was to take a journey!", "그러니까 의사의 진단서가 그녀가 세인트존스우드에서 죽었다고 적은 바로 그날(25일)에, 퍼시벌 경 자신의 진술에 따르면 그녀는 블랙워터에 살아 있었고—그 이튿날에야 여행길에 오를 예정이었던 것이다!"], ["When the proof of that journey was obtained from the flyman, the evidence would be complete.", "그 여행의 증거를 삯마차꾼에게서 얻어 내기만 하면 증거는 완결될 것이었다."]], [["“A quarter-past five,”", "“5시 15분.”"], ["said the Count, looking at his watch.", "백작이 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["“Time for my restorative snooze.", "“원기를 되찾을 선잠 시간이오."], ["I personally resemble Napoleon the Great, as you may have remarked, Mr. Hartright—I also resemble that immortal man in my power of commanding sleep at will.", "하트라이트 씨, 당신도 눈치챘을지 모르나 나는 개인적으로 위대한 나폴레옹을 닮았소—뜻대로 잠을 불러올 수 있다는 점에서도 나는 그 불멸의 인물을 닮았지."], ["Excuse me one moment.", "잠깐만 실례하겠소."], ["I will summon Madame Fosco, to keep you from feeling dull.”", "당신이 심심하지 않도록 포스코 부인을 부르겠소.”"]], [["Knowing as well as he did, that he was summoning Madame Fosco to ensure my not leaving the house while he was asleep, I made no reply, and occupied myself in tying up the papers which he had placed in my possession.", "그가 포스코 부인을 부르는 것은 자기가 자는 동안 내가 집을 떠나지 못하게 하려는 것임을 나 역시 그 못지않게 잘 알고 있었으므로, 나는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 그가 내 손에 넘긴 서류들을 묶는 일에 몰두했다."]], [["The lady came in, cool, pale, and venomous as ever.", "부인이 들어왔다. 여전히 서늘하고 창백하고 독기 어린 얼굴이었다."], ["“Amuse Mr. Hartright, my angel,”", "“나의 천사, 하트라이트 씨를 심심찮게 해 드리시오.”"], ["said the Count.", "백작이 말했다."], ["He placed a chair for her, kissed her hand for the second time, withdrew to a sofa, and, in three minutes, was as peacefully and happily asleep as the most virtuous man in existence.", "그는 아내에게 의자를 놓아 주고, 두 번째로 그 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤, 소파로 물러가더니 3분 만에 세상에서 가장 덕망 높은 사람처럼 평온하고 행복하게 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["Madame Fosco took a book from the table, sat down, and looked at me, with the steady vindictive malice of a woman who never forgot and never forgave.", "포스코 부인은 탁자에서 책을 한 권 집어 들고 자리에 앉아, 결코 잊지 않고 결코 용서하지 않는 여자의 흔들림 없는 앙심을 담아 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“I have been listening to your conversation with my husband,”", "“남편과 나누시는 이야기를 듣고 있었습니다.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “If I had been in HIS place—I would have laid you dead on the hearthrug.”", "“내가 그이 자리에 있었다면—당신을 저 난롯가 깔개 위에 시체로 눕혔을 겁니다.”"]], [["With those words she opened her book, and never looked at me or spoke to me from that time till the time when her husband woke.", "그 말과 함께 그녀는 책을 펼쳤고, 그때부터 남편이 깨어날 때까지 나를 쳐다보지도 말을 걸지도 않았다."]], [["He opened his eyes and rose from the sofa, accurately to an hour from the time when he had gone to sleep.", "그는 잠든 시각으로부터 정확히 한 시간 만에 눈을 뜨고 소파에서 일어났다."]], [["“I feel infinitely refreshed,”", "“한없이 개운하군.”"], ["he remarked.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Eleanor, my good wife, are you all ready upstairs?", "“엘리너, 착한 내 아내, 위층은 다 준비되었소?"], ["That is well.", "잘했소."], ["My little packing here can be completed in ten minutes—my travelling-dress assumed in ten minutes more.", "여기 내 자잘한 짐은 10분이면 마무리할 수 있고—여행복으로 갈아입는 데 다시 10분이면 되겠지."], ["What remains before the agent comes?”", "대리인이 오기 전에 남은 일이 무엇이 있소?”"], ["He looked about the room, and noticed the cage with his white mice in it.", "그는 방 안을 둘러보다가 흰 쥐가 든 새장을 발견했다."], ["“Ah!”", "“아!”"], ["he cried piteously, “a last laceration of my sympathies still remains.", "그가 애처롭게 외쳤다. “내 정을 찢는 마지막 일이 아직 남아 있구나."], ["My innocent pets!", "죄 없는 내 귀염둥이들!"], ["my little cherished children!", "사랑스러운 내 어린것들!"], ["what am I to do with them?", "너희를 어찌한단 말이냐?"], ["For the present we are settled nowhere; for the present we travel incessantly—the less baggage we carry the better for ourselves.", "당분간 우리는 어디에도 자리 잡지 못하고, 당분간 쉬지 않고 떠돌아야 하는데—짐이 적을수록 우리에게 좋으니."], ["My cockatoo, my canaries, and my little mice—who will cherish them when their good Papa is gone?”", "내 코카투, 내 카나리아들, 내 작은 쥐들—너희들의 착한 아빠가 떠나고 나면 누가 너희를 아껴 준단 말이냐?”"]], [["He walked about the room deep in thought.", "그는 깊은 생각에 잠겨 방 안을 서성였다."], ["He had not been at all troubled about writing his confession, but he was visibly perplexed and distressed about the far more important question of the disposal of his pets.", "자백서를 쓰는 일로는 조금도 괴로워하지 않던 그가, 애완동물을 어디에 맡기느냐 하는 훨씬 더 중대한 문제 앞에서는 눈에 띄게 당혹스러워하고 괴로워했다."], ["After long consideration he suddenly sat down again at the writing-table.", "오랜 궁리 끝에 그는 갑자기 다시 필기 탁자 앞에 앉았다."]], [["“An idea!” he exclaimed.", "“좋은 생각이 났다!”"], ["“I will offer my canaries and my cockatoo to this vast Metropolis—my agent shall present them in my name to the Zoological Gardens of London.", "그가 외쳤다. “내 카나리아들과 코카투를 이 거대한 수도에 바치겠다—내 대리인이 내 이름으로 그것들을 런던 동물원에 헌정하도록 하지."], ["The Document that describes them shall be drawn out on the spot.”", "그것들을 소개하는 문서는 이 자리에서 작성하겠다.”"]], [["He began to write, repeating the words as they flowed from his pen.", "그는 펜에서 흘러나오는 말을 소리 내어 되뇌며 쓰기 시작했다."]], [["“Number one.", "“일번."], ["Cockatoo of transcendent plumage: attraction, of himself, to all visitors of taste.", "더할 나위 없이 빼어난 깃털을 지닌 코카투. 그 자체로 안목 있는 모든 관람객을 끌어당기는 매력물임."], ["Number two.", "이번."], ["Canaries of unrivalled vivacity and intelligence: worthy of the garden of Eden, worthy also of the garden in the Regent’s Park.", "견줄 데 없는 활기와 지성을 지닌 카나리아들. 에덴동산에 어울리며, 리젠트 파크의 동산에도 어울림."], ["Homage to British Zoology.", "영국 동물학에 바치는 경의."], ["Offered by Fosco.”", "포스코 증정.”"]], [["The pen spluttered again, and the flourish was attached to his signature.", "펜이 다시 튀는 소리를 냈고, 그의 서명에는 장식 획이 덧붙었다."]], [["“Count!", "“백작!"], ["you have not included the mice,”", "쥐들은 넣지 않으셨군요.”"], ["said Madame Fosco.", "포스코 부인이 말했다."]], [["He left the table, took her hand, and placed it on his heart.", "그는 탁자를 떠나 아내의 손을 잡고 자기 가슴에 얹었다."]], [["“All human resolution, Eleanor,”", "“엘리너, 인간의 모든 결심에는,”"], ["he said solemnly, “has its limits.", "그가 엄숙하게 말했다. “한계가 있는 법이오."], ["MY limits are inscribed on that Document.", "나의 한계는 저 문서에 새겨져 있소."], ["I cannot part with my white mice.", "나는 내 흰 쥐들과 헤어질 수 없소."], ["Bear with me, my angel, and remove them to their travelling cage upstairs.”", "나를 이해해 주시오, 나의 천사. 그리고 저 아이들을 위층의 여행용 새장으로 옮겨 주시오.”"]], [["“Admirable tenderness!”", "“참으로 감탄스러운 다정함이에요!”"], ["said Madame Fosco, admiring her husband, with a last viperish look in my direction.", "포스코 부인이 남편을 우러러보며 말하고는, 마지막으로 독사 같은 눈길을 내 쪽에 던졌다."], ["She took up the cage carefully, and left the room.", "그녀는 새장을 조심스럽게 들고 방을 나갔다."]], [["The Count looked at his watch.", "백작은 시계를 보았다."], ["In spite of his resolute assumption of composure, he was getting anxious for the agent’s arrival.", "태연한 척 굳게 마음먹고 있었음에도, 그는 대리인이 오기를 초조하게 기다리고 있었다."], ["The candles had long since been extinguished, and the sunlight of the new morning poured into the room.", "촛불은 이미 오래전에 꺼졌고, 새 아침의 햇살이 방 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔다."], ["It was not till five minutes past seven that the gate bell rang, and the agent made his appearance.", "대문 초인종이 울리고 대리인이 모습을 드러낸 것은 7시 5분이 되어서였다."], ["He was a foreigner with a dark beard.", "그는 검은 수염을 기른 외국인이었다."]], [["“Mr. Hartright—Monsieur Rubelle,”", "“하트라이트 씨—루벨 씨요.”"], ["said the Count, introducing us.", "백작이 우리를 소개하며 말했다."], ["He took the agent (a foreign spy, in every line of his face, if ever there was one yet) into a corner of the room, whispered some directions to him, and then left us together. “Monsieur Rubelle,”", "그는 그 대리인을(세상에 외국인 첩자라는 것이 있다면 얼굴 어느 한 줄까지도 영락없는 첩자였다) 방 한구석으로 데려가 몇 가지 지시를 속삭이고는, 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔다."], ["as soon as we were alone, suggested with great politeness that I should favour him with his instructions.", "단둘이 되자마자 “루벨 씨”는 더없이 정중하게, 자기에게 지시서를 써 주십사 청했다."], ["I wrote two lines to Pesca, authorising him to deliver my sealed letter “to the bearer,” directed the note, and handed it to Monsieur Rubelle.", "나는 페스카에게 두 줄을 써서 봉인된 편지를 “지참인”에게 넘겨 달라고 위임하고, 겉봉에 수신인을 적어 루벨 씨에게 건넸다."]], [["The agent waited with me till his employer returned, equipped in travelling costume.", "대리인은 고용주가 여행복 차림으로 돌아올 때까지 나와 함께 기다렸다."], ["The Count examined the address of my letter before he dismissed the agent.", "백작은 대리인을 내보내기 전에 내 편지의 수신인을 살펴보았다."], ["“I thought so!”", "“그럴 줄 알았소!”"], ["he said, turning on me with a dark look, and altering again in his manner from that moment.", "그가 어두운 눈빛으로 나를 돌아보며 말했고, 그 순간부터 태도가 다시 달라졌다."]], [["He completed his packing, and then sat consulting a travelling map, making entries in his pocket-book, and looking every now and then impatiently at his watch.", "그는 짐 꾸리기를 마치고, 그런 다음 앉아서 여행 지도를 들여다보며 수첩에 무언가를 적고, 이따금 초조하게 시계를 보았다."], ["Not another word, addressed to myself, passed his lips.", "내게 건네는 말은 그의 입에서 단 한마디도 더 나오지 않았다."], ["The near approach of the hour for his departure, and the proof he had seen of the communication established between Pesca and myself, had plainly recalled his whole attention to the measures that were necessary for securing his escape.", "출발 시각이 가까워진 것과, 페스카와 나 사이에 연락이 닿아 있다는 증거를 눈으로 확인한 것이, 분명 그의 온 신경을 도피를 확실히 하는 데 필요한 조치들로 되돌려 놓은 것이었다."]], [["A little before eight o’clock, Monsieur Rubelle came back with my unopened letter in his hand.", "8시가 조금 못 되어 루벨 씨가 뜯지 않은 내 편지를 손에 들고 돌아왔다."], ["The Count looked carefully at the superscription and the seal, lit a candle, and burnt the letter.", "백작은 겉봉의 글씨와 봉인을 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤, 촛불을 켜고 그 편지를 태웠다. “나는 내 약속을 지키오.”"], ["“I perform my promise,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but this matter, Mr. Hartright, shall not end here.”", "“그러나 이 일은, 하트라이트 씨, 여기서 끝나지 않을 거요.”"]], [["The agent had kept at the door the cab in which he had returned.", "대리인은 자기가 타고 온 마차를 문 앞에 세워 두고 있었다."], ["He and the maid-servant now busied themselves in removing the luggage.", "그와 하녀가 이제 짐을 내가는 일에 분주해졌다."], ["Madame Fosco came downstairs, thickly veiled, with the travelling cage of the white mice in her hand.", "포스코 부인이 두꺼운 베일을 쓰고, 흰 쥐들의 여행용 새장을 손에 든 채 아래층으로 내려왔다."], ["She neither spoke to me nor looked towards me.", "그녀는 내게 말을 걸지도, 내 쪽을 쳐다보지도 않았다."], ["Her husband escorted her to the cab.", "남편이 아내를 마차까지 배웅했다."], ["“Follow me as far as the passage,”", "“복도까지 나를 따라오시오.”"], ["he whispered in my ear; “I may want to speak to you at the last moment.”", "그가 내 귀에 대고 속삭였다. “마지막 순간에 당신에게 할 말이 있을지도 모르니.”"]], [["I went out to the door, the agent standing below me in the front garden.", "나는 문간으로 나갔고, 대리인은 내 아래쪽 앞뜰에 서 있었다."], ["The Count came back alone, and drew me a few steps inside the passage.", "백작이 혼자 돌아와 나를 복도 안쪽으로 몇 걸음 끌어당겼다."]], [["“Remember the Third condition!”", "“셋째 조건을 잊지 마시오!”"], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."], ["“You shall hear from me, Mr. Hartright—I may claim from you the satisfaction of a gentleman sooner than you think for.”", "“내게서 소식이 갈 거요, 하트라이트 씨—당신이 생각하는 것보다 이르게 신사로서의 결투를 청할지도 모르오.”"], ["He caught my hand before I was aware of him, and wrung it hard—then turned to the door, stopped, and came back to me again.", "그는 내가 알아차리기도 전에 내 손을 붙잡아 힘껏 움켜쥐었다—그러고는 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다가 멈추어 서더니, 다시 내게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“One word more,”", "“한마디만 더 하겠소.”"], ["he said confidentially.", "그가 은밀하게 말했다."], ["“When I last saw Miss Halcombe, she looked thin and ill.", "“내가 마지막으로 홀콤 양을 뵈었을 때, 그분은 야위고 병약해 보였소."], ["I am anxious about that admirable woman.", "나는 그 훌륭한 여인이 걱정스럽소."], ["Take care of her, sir!", "그분을 잘 보살펴 주시오, 선생!"], ["With my hand on my heart, I solemnly implore you, take care of Miss Halcombe!”", "가슴에 손을 얹고 엄숙히 간청하오, 홀콤 양을 잘 보살펴 주시오!”"]], [["Those were the last words he said to me before he squeezed his huge body into the cab and drove off.", "그것이 그가 거대한 몸을 마차 안으로 밀어 넣고 떠나기 전에 내게 남긴 마지막 말이었다."]], [["The agent and I waited at the door a few moments looking after him.", "대리인과 나는 문간에서 잠시 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["While we were standing together, a second cab appeared from a turning a little way down the road.", "우리가 그렇게 함께 서 있는 동안, 길 조금 아래쪽 모퉁이에서 두 번째 마차가 나타났다."], ["It followed the direction previously taken by the Count’s cab, and as it passed the house and the open garden gate, a person inside looked at us out of the window.", "그 마차는 앞서 백작의 마차가 간 방향을 그대로 따라갔고, 집 앞과 열린 정원 문을 지나칠 때 안에 탄 사람이 창밖으로 우리를 내다보았다."], ["The stranger at the Opera again!—the foreigner with a scar on his left cheek.", "오페라 극장에서 보았던 그 낯선 사내였다!—왼뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 외국인이었다."]], [["“You wait here with me, sir, for half an hour more!”", "“선생, 여기서 나와 함께 30분만 더 기다려 주시지요!”"], ["said Monsieur Rubelle.", "루벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I do.”", "“그러겠습니다.”"]], [["We returned to the sitting-room.", "우리는 거실로 돌아갔다."], ["I was in no humour to speak to the agent, or to allow him to speak to me.", "나는 그 대리인에게 말을 걸 기분도, 그가 내게 말을 걸도록 내버려 둘 기분도 아니었다."], ["I took out the papers which the Count had placed in my hands, and read the terrible story of the conspiracy told by the man who had planned and perpetrated it.", "나는 백작이 내 손에 넘긴 서류를 꺼내, 그 음모를 계획하고 실행한 자가 직접 들려주는 그 끔찍한 이야기를 읽었다."]], [["# THE STORY CONTINUED BY ISIDOR, OTTAVIO, BALDASSARE FOSCO", "# THE STORY CONTINUED BY ISIDOR, OTTAVIO, BALDASSARE FOSCO"]], [["(Count of the Holy Roman Empire, Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Brazen Crown, Perpetual Arch-Master of the Rosicrucian Masons of Mesopotamia; Attached (in Honorary Capacities) to Societies Musical, Societies Medical, Societies Philosophical, and Societies General Benevolent, throughout Europe; etc. etc. etc.)", "(신성 로마 제국의 백작, 청동 왕관 훈장 대십자 기사, 메소포타미아 장미십자 프리메이슨단 종신 대사부(大師父); 유럽 전역의 음악 협회, 의학 협회, 철학 협회, 일반 자선 협회의 (명예직) 회원 등등 등등)"]], [["# THE COUNT’S NARRATIVE", "# THE COUNT’S NARRATIVE"]], [["In the summer of eighteen hundred and fifty I arrived in England, charged with a delicate political mission from abroad.", "1850년 여름, 나는 국외로부터 미묘한 정치적 임무를 띠고 영국에 도착했다."], ["Confidential persons were semi-officially connected with me, whose exertions I was authorised to direct, Monsieur and Madame Rubelle being among the number.", "반쯤은 공적으로 나와 연결된 믿을 만한 사람들이 있었고 나는 그들의 활동을 지휘할 권한을 위임받았는데, 루벨 씨 부부도 그 가운데 들어 있었다."], ["Some weeks of spare time were at my disposal, before I entered on my functions by establishing myself in the suburbs of London.", "런던 교외에 거처를 정하고 내 직무에 들어가기까지, 나에게는 몇 주간의 여유가 주어져 있었다."], ["Curiosity may stop here to ask for some explanation of those functions on my part.", "호기심이 여기서 걸음을 멈추고 그 직무가 대체 무엇이었는지 나에게 설명을 요구할지도 모르겠다."], ["I entirely sympathise with the request.", "나는 그 요구에 전적으로 공감한다."], ["I also regret that diplomatic reserve forbids me to comply with it.", "그리고 외교적 신중함이 그에 응하는 것을 금하고 있음을 또한 유감으로 여긴다."]], [["I arranged to pass the preliminary period of repose, to which I have just referred, in the superb mansion of my late lamented friend, Sir Percival Glyde.", "나는 앞서 말한 그 예비적 휴식기를, 애석하게 세상을 떠난 나의 벗 퍼시벌 글라이드 경의 장려한 저택에서 보내기로 정해 두었다."], ["HE arrived from the Continent with his wife.", "그는 아내를 데리고 대륙에서 건너왔다."], ["I arrived from the Continent with MINE.", "나 또한 내 아내를 데리고 대륙에서 건너왔다."], ["England is the land of domestic happiness—how appropriately we entered it under these domestic circumstances!", "영국은 가정적 행복의 나라다—그토록 가정적인 정황 속에서 우리가 그 땅에 발을 들였으니, 이 얼마나 어울리는 입국인가!"]], [["The bond of friendship which united Percival and myself was strengthened, on this occasion, by a touching similarity in the pecuniary position on his side and on mine.", "퍼시벌과 나를 묶어 준 우정의 끈은 이 시기에, 그의 형편과 내 형편이 금전 문제에서 눈물겹도록 닮아 있었던 덕분에 한층 단단해졌다."], ["We both wanted money.", "우리 둘 다 돈이 필요했다."], ["Immense necessity!", "막대한 궁핍!"], ["Universal want!", "만인의 결핍!"], ["Is there a civilised human being who does not feel for us?", "문명인이라면 우리를 딱하게 여기지 않을 자가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["How insensible must that man be!", "그런 자가 있다면 얼마나 무감각한 인간이겠는가!"], ["Or how rich!", "아니면 얼마나 부자이겠는가!"]], [["I enter into no sordid particulars, in discussing this part of the subject.", "이 대목을 이야기하면서 나는 구차한 세목에는 들어가지 않겠다."], ["My mind recoils from them.", "내 정신은 그런 것들 앞에서 움츠러든다."], ["With a Roman austerity, I show my empty purse and Percival’s to the shrinking public gaze.", "나는 로마인의 준엄함으로, 텅 빈 내 지갑과 퍼시벌의 지갑을 움찔하는 대중의 눈앞에 내보인다."], ["Let us allow the deplorable fact to assert itself, once for all, in that manner, and pass on.", "이 개탄스러운 사실은 그런 방식으로 단 한 번에 스스로를 밝히도록 두고, 우리는 그냥 지나가기로 하자."]], [["We were received at the mansion by the magnificent creature who is inscribed on my heart as “Marian,” who is known in the colder atmosphere of society as “Miss Halcombe.”", "저택에서 우리를 맞아 준 이는 내 가슴에 “매리언”이라 새겨져 있는, 그리고 사교계의 차가운 공기 속에서는 “홀콤 양”이라 불리는 저 장엄한 존재였다."]], [["Just Heaven!", "정의로운 하늘이여!"], ["with what inconceivable rapidity I learnt to adore that woman.", "나는 얼마나 상상도 못 할 속도로 그 여인을 흠모하게 되었던가."], ["At sixty, I worshipped her with the volcanic ardour of eighteen.", "예순의 나이에 나는 열여덟의 화산 같은 열정으로 그녀를 숭배했다."], ["All the gold of my rich nature was poured hopelessly at her feet.", "내 풍요로운 천성의 황금은 모조리 가망 없이 그녀의 발치에 쏟아부어졌다."], ["My wife—poor angel!—my wife, who adores me, got nothing but the shillings and the pennies.", "내 아내는—가엾은 천사여!—나를 숭배하는 내 아내는 실링과 페니 몇 닢밖에 얻지 못했다."], ["Such is the World, such Man, such Love.", "세상이란 그런 것이고, 인간이란 그런 것이며, 사랑이란 그런 것이다."], ["What are we (I ask) but puppets in a show-box?", "우리가 (묻거니와) 인형 상자 속의 꼭두각시가 아니면 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["Oh, omnipotent Destiny, pull our strings gently!", "오, 전능한 운명이여, 우리의 실을 부드럽게 당겨 다오!"], ["Dance us mercifully off our miserable little stage!", "이 비참하고 조그만 무대에서 우리를 자비롭게 춤추듯 퇴장시켜 다오!"]], [["The preceding lines, rightly understood, express an entire system of philosophy.", "앞의 몇 줄은 제대로 이해하기만 하면 하나의 완결된 철학 체계를 담고 있다."], ["It is mine.", "그것이 곧 나의 철학이다."]], [["I resume.", "다시 이어 가겠다."]], [["The domestic position at the commencement of our residence at Blackwater Park has been drawn with amazing accuracy, with profound mental insight, by the hand of Marian herself.", "블랙워터 파크에서 우리가 머물기 시작하던 무렵의 그 집안 형편은, 매리언 본인의 손으로 놀라우리만치 정확하게, 심오한 정신적 통찰과 더불어 그려져 있다."], ["(Pass me the intoxicating familiarity of mentioning this sublime creature by her Christian name.) Accurate knowledge of the contents of her journal—to which I obtained access by clandestine means, unspeakably precious to me in the remembrance—warns my eager pen from topics which this essentially exhaustive woman has already made her own.", "(이 숭고한 존재를 세례명으로 부르는 도취적인 허물없음을 부디 눈감아 주기 바란다.) 그녀의 일기 내용을 정확히 알고 있다는 사실은—나는 은밀한 수단으로 그것을 손에 넣었고, 그 기억은 내게 이루 말할 수 없이 소중하다—본질적으로 남김이 없는 이 여인이 이미 제 것으로 만들어 놓은 화제들로부터 내 성급한 펜을 물러서게 한다."]], [["The interests—interests, breathless and immense!—with which I am here concerned, begin with the deplorable calamity of Marian’s illness.", "내가 여기서 다루려는 이해관계는—숨 막히고 막대한 이해관계다!—매리언이 병으로 쓰러진 저 개탄스러운 재앙에서 시작된다."]], [["The situation at this period was emphatically a serious one.", "이 시기의 상황은 단연코 심각한 것이었다."], ["Large sums of money, due at a certain time, were wanted by Percival (I say nothing of the modicum equally necessary to myself), and the one source to look to for supplying them was the fortune of his wife, of which not one farthing was at his disposal until her death.", "정해진 기일에 갚아야 할 거액의 돈이 퍼시벌에게 필요했고(나 자신에게도 그에 못지않게 필요했던 소액에 대해서는 말하지 않겠다), 그것을 마련할 유일한 원천은 그의 아내의 재산이었는데, 그 재산은 그녀가 죽기 전에는 단 한 푼도 그의 뜻대로 쓸 수 없었다."], ["Bad so far, and worse still farther on.", "여기까지도 나쁘지만 그 앞은 더 나빴다."], ["My lamented friend had private troubles of his own, into which the delicacy of my disinterested attachment to him forbade me from inquiring too curiously.", "애석한 내 벗에게는 그만의 은밀한 고민거리가 있었고, 사심 없는 나의 우정이 지닌 섬세함은 그 일을 지나치게 캐묻는 것을 금했다."], ["I knew nothing but that a woman, named Anne Catherick, was hidden in the neighbourhood, that she was in communication with Lady Glyde, and that the disclosure of a secret, which would be the certain ruin of Percival, might be the result.", "내가 아는 것이라고는 앤 캐서릭이라는 여자가 인근에 숨어 있다는 것, 그녀가 글라이드 부인과 연락을 주고받고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 결과로 퍼시벌을 확실히 파멸시킬 비밀이 드러날지도 모른다는 것뿐이었다."], ["He had told me himself that he was a lost man, unless his wife was silenced, and unless Anne Catherick was found.", "그는 아내의 입을 막지 못하고 앤 캐서릭을 찾아내지 못하면 자신은 끝장난 사람이라고 내게 직접 말했다."], ["If he was a lost man, what would become of our pecuniary interests?", "그가 끝장난다면 우리의 금전적 이해관계는 어찌 되겠는가?"], ["Courageous as I am by nature, I absolutely trembled at the idea!", "천성이 담대한 나조차도 그 생각에 그야말로 몸이 떨렸다!"]], [["The whole force of my intelligence was now directed to the finding of Anne Catherick.", "이제 내 지성의 온 힘은 앤 캐서릭을 찾아내는 데로 향했다."], ["Our money affairs, important as they were, admitted of delay—but the necessity of discovering the woman admitted of none.", "돈 문제는 아무리 중대하다 해도 미룰 여지가 있었지만, 그 여자를 찾아내야 할 필요는 조금도 미룰 수 없었다."], ["I only knew her by description, as presenting an extraordinary personal resemblance to Lady Glyde.", "나는 그녀를 오직 전해 들은 인상착의로만 알고 있었는데, 글라이드 부인과 놀라울 만큼 닮았다는 것이었다."], ["The statement of this curious fact—intended merely to assist me in identifying the person of whom we were in search—when coupled with the additional information that Anne Catherick had escaped from a mad-house, started the first immense conception in my mind, which subsequently led to such amazing results.", "이 기이한 사실은—본래 우리가 찾는 인물을 알아보는 데 도움을 주려는 목적으로만 전해진 것이었지만—앤 캐서릭이 정신병원에서 달아났다는 추가 정보와 맞물리면서, 훗날 그토록 놀라운 결과로 이어진 최초의 거대한 구상을 내 머릿속에 촉발했다."], ["That conception involved nothing less than the complete transformation of two separate identities.", "그 구상은 다름 아닌 두 개의 서로 다른 신원을 통째로 뒤바꾸는 일이었다."], ["Lady Glyde and Anne Catherick were to change names, places, and destinies, the one with the other—the prodigious consequences contemplated by the change being the gain of thirty thousand pounds, and the eternal preservation of Sir Percival’s secret.", "글라이드 부인과 앤 캐서릭이 서로 이름과 자리와 운명을 맞바꾸는 것—그 교환으로 노린 어마어마한 결과는 삼만 파운드의 획득과 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 영구히 지켜 내는 일이었다."]], [["My instincts (which seldom err) suggested to me, on reviewing the circumstances, that our invisible Anne would, sooner or later, return to the boat-house at the Blackwater lake.", "정황을 되짚어 보던 내게, 좀처럼 빗나가지 않는 내 직감은 우리의 보이지 않는 앤이 조만간 블랙워터 호수의 보트 창고로 돌아오리라고 일러 주었다."], ["There I posted myself, previously mentioning to Mrs. Michelson, the housekeeper, that I might be found when wanted, immersed in study, in that solitary place.", "나는 그곳에 자리를 잡았고, 미리 가정부인 마이컬슨 부인에게 볼일이 있거든 그 외딴 곳에서 연구에 몰두해 있는 나를 찾으면 된다고 일러 두었다."], ["It is my rule never to make unnecessary mysteries, and never to set people suspecting me for want of a little seasonable candour on my part.", "불필요한 비밀을 만들지 않는 것, 그리고 때맞춘 약간의 솔직함이 모자라 사람들이 나를 의심하게 만들지 않는 것이 내 원칙이다."], ["Mrs. Michelson believed in me from first to last.", "마이컬슨 부인은 처음부터 끝까지 나를 믿었다."], ["This ladylike person (widow of a Protestant priest) overflowed with faith.", "이 기품 있는 부인은(신교 목사의 미망인이다) 믿음이 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["Touched by such superfluity of simple confidence in a woman of her mature years, I opened the ample reservoirs of my nature and absorbed it all.", "그만한 연배의 여인에게 그처럼 소박한 신뢰가 넘쳐나는 데 감동하여, 나는 내 천성의 너른 저수지를 열어 그것을 남김없이 빨아들였다."]], [["I was rewarded for posting myself sentinel at the lake by the appearance—not of Anne Catherick herself, but of the person in charge of her.", "호숫가에 보초로 자리를 잡은 보람은 앤 캐서릭 본인이 아니라 그녀를 돌보는 사람의 등장으로 돌아왔다."], ["This individual also overflowed with simple faith, which I absorbed in myself, as in the case already mentioned.", "이 인물 역시 소박한 믿음이 넘쳐흘렀고, 앞서 말한 경우와 마찬가지로 나는 그것을 내 안으로 빨아들였다."], ["I leave her to describe the circumstances (if she has not done so already) under which she introduced me to the object of her maternal care.", "그녀가 어떤 정황에서 자신이 어머니처럼 돌보던 그 사람에게 나를 소개했는지는 (아직 하지 않았다면) 그녀 자신이 이야기하도록 남겨 두겠다."], ["When I first saw Anne Catherick she was asleep.", "내가 앤 캐서릭을 처음 보았을 때 그녀는 잠들어 있었다."], ["I was electrified by the likeness between this unhappy woman and Lady Glyde.", "나는 이 불행한 여자와 글라이드 부인의 닮은꼴에 전기를 맞은 듯 충격을 받았다."], ["The details of the grand scheme which had suggested themselves in outline only, up to that period, occurred to me, in all their masterly combination, at the sight of the sleeping face.", "그때까지 윤곽으로만 떠올라 있던 저 원대한 계획의 세부가, 잠든 얼굴을 바라보는 순간 완벽하게 짜맞춰진 모습으로 내게 떠올랐다."], ["At the same time, my heart, always accessible to tender influences, dissolved in tears at the spectacle of suffering before me.", "동시에 언제나 부드러운 감화에 열려 있는 내 심장은 눈앞의 고통스러운 광경 앞에서 눈물로 녹아내렸다."], ["I instantly set myself to impart relief.", "나는 즉시 그 고통을 덜어 주는 일에 착수했다."], ["In other words, I provided the necessary stimulant for strengthening Anne Catherick to perform the journey to London.", "다시 말해, 앤 캐서릭이 런던까지 갈 수 있도록 기운을 북돋울 강장제를 마련해 주었다."]], [["The best years of my life have been passed in the ardent study of medical and chemical science.", "내 인생의 가장 좋은 시절은 의학과 화학을 열렬히 연구하는 데 바쳐졌다."], ["Chemistry especially has always had irresistible attractions for me from the enormous, the illimitable power which the knowledge of it confers.", "특히 화학은 그 지식이 부여하는 어마어마하고 무한한 힘 때문에 언제나 내게 거부할 수 없는 매력이었다."], ["Chemists—I assert it emphatically—might sway, if they pleased, the destinies of humanity.", "화학자는—나는 힘주어 단언한다—마음만 먹으면 인류의 운명을 좌지우지할 수 있다."], ["Let me explain this before I go further.", "더 나아가기 전에 이 말을 설명해 두겠다."]], [["Mind, they say, rules the world.", "사람들은 정신이 세계를 지배한다고 말한다."], ["But what rules the mind?", "그러면 정신은 무엇이 지배하는가?"], ["The body (follow me closely here) lies at the mercy of the most omnipotent of all potentates—the Chemist.", "육체다(여기서는 나를 바짝 따라오라). 그리고 육체는 모든 군주 가운데 가장 전능한 자, 곧 화학자의 처분 아래 놓여 있다."], ["Give me—Fosco—chemistry; and when Shakespeare has conceived Hamlet, and sits down to execute the conception—with a few grains of powder dropped into his daily food, I will reduce his mind, by the action of his body, till his pen pours out the most abject drivel that has ever degraded paper.", "나에게—이 포스코에게—화학을 다오. 그러면 셰익스피어가 햄릿을 구상하고 그 구상을 실행하려 자리에 앉을 때, 그의 일용할 음식에 가루 몇 알갱이만 떨어뜨려도 나는 그의 육체를 통해 그의 정신을 무너뜨려, 그 펜이 종이를 더럽힌 것 중 가장 비천한 헛소리를 쏟아 내게 만들 것이다."], ["Under similar circumstances, revive me the illustrious Newton.", "같은 조건에서 저 저명한 뉴턴을 되살려 보라."], ["I guarantee that when he sees the apple fall he shall EAT IT, instead of discovering the principle of gravitation.", "나는 장담하거니와 그는 사과가 떨어지는 것을 보고 만유인력의 원리를 발견하는 대신 그것을 먹어 치울 것이다."], ["Nero’s dinner shall transform Nero into the mildest of men before he has done digesting it, and the morning draught of Alexander the Great shall make Alexander run for his life at the first sight of the enemy the same afternoon.", "네로의 저녁 식사는 그 소화가 끝나기도 전에 네로를 세상에서 가장 온순한 인간으로 바꿔 놓을 것이고, 알렉산드로스 대왕이 아침에 마신 물약은 그날 오후 적을 보자마자 알렉산드로스가 목숨을 걸고 달아나게 만들 것이다."], ["On my sacred word of honour it is lucky for Society that modern chemists are, by incomprehensible good fortune, the most harmless of mankind.", "내 신성한 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 근대의 화학자들이 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 행운 덕분에 인류 가운데 가장 무해한 자들이라는 사실은 사회를 위해 다행한 일이다."], ["The mass are worthy fathers of families, who keep shops.", "그 다수는 가게를 꾸리는 훌륭한 가장들이다."], ["The few are philosophers besotted with admiration for the sound of their own lecturing voices, visionaries who waste their lives on fantastic impossibilities, or quacks whose ambition soars no higher than our corns.", "소수는 제 강연 목소리에 취해 감탄에 빠진 철학자들이거나, 허황된 불가능에 인생을 허비하는 몽상가들이거나, 야심이 우리 발의 티눈보다 높이 날지 못하는 돌팔이들이다."], ["Thus Society escapes, and the illimitable power of Chemistry remains the slave of the most superficial and the most insignificant ends.", "그리하여 사회는 무사하고, 화학의 무한한 힘은 가장 피상적이고 가장 하찮은 목적의 노예로 남아 있다."]], [["Why this outburst?", "어찌하여 이런 격발인가?"], ["Why this withering eloquence?", "어찌하여 이런 통렬한 웅변인가?"]], [["Because my conduct has been misrepresented, because my motives have been misunderstood.", "내 행동이 곡해되었기 때문이고, 내 동기가 오해되었기 때문이다."], ["It has been assumed that I used my vast chemical resources against Anne Catherick, and that I would have used them if I could against the magnificent Marian herself.", "사람들은 내가 앤 캐서릭에게 나의 방대한 화학적 수단을 썼으리라고, 그리고 할 수만 있었다면 저 장엄한 매리언에게조차 그것을 썼으리라고 넘겨짚었다."], ["Odious insinuations both!", "둘 다 가증스러운 억측이다!"], ["All my interests were concerned (as will be seen presently) in the preservation of Anne Catherick’s life.", "내 모든 이해관계는 (곧 드러나겠지만) 앤 캐서릭을 살려 두는 데 걸려 있었다."], ["All my anxieties were concentrated on Marian’s rescue from the hands of the licensed imbecile who attended her, and who found my advice confirmed from first to last by the physician from London.", "내 모든 근심은 그녀를 돌보던 저 면허 가진 얼간이의 손아귀에서 매리언을 구해 내는 데 집중되어 있었고, 내 조언은 런던에서 온 의사에 의해 처음부터 끝까지 옳았음이 확인되었다."], ["On two occasions only—both equally harmless to the individual on whom I practised—did I summon to myself the assistance of chemical knowledge.", "단 두 번만—그것도 내가 시술한 당사자에게는 똑같이 무해했던 두 번만—나는 화학 지식의 도움을 불러들였다."], ["On the first of the two, after following Marian to the inn at Blackwater (studying, behind a convenient waggon which hid me from her, the poetry of motion, as embodied in her walk), I availed myself of the services of my invaluable wife, to copy one and to intercept the other of two letters which my adored enemy had entrusted to a discarded maid.", "그 첫 번째는 블랙워터의 여관까지 매리언을 뒤따라간 뒤였다(나를 가려 준 마침맞은 짐수레 뒤에서, 그녀의 걸음걸이에 구현된 운동의 시를 연구하면서 말이다). 그때 나는 더없이 소중한 내 아내의 수고를 빌려, 내가 흠모하는 저 적수가 쫓겨난 하녀에게 맡긴 두 통의 편지 가운데 하나는 베끼고 하나는 가로챘다."], ["In this case, the letters being in the bosom of the girl’s dress, Madame Fosco could only open them, read them, perform her instructions, seal them, and put them back again by scientific assistance—which assistance I rendered in a half-ounce bottle.", "이 경우 편지들이 그 처녀의 옷가슴 속에 있었으므로, 포스코 부인은 과학의 도움을 빌려야만 그것을 열고 읽고 지시받은 일을 행하고 다시 봉해 제자리에 돌려놓을 수 있었다—그 도움은 반 온스들이 작은 병에 담아 내가 제공했다."], ["The second occasion, when the same means were employed, was the occasion (to which I shall soon refer) of Lady Glyde’s arrival in London.", "같은 수단을 쓴 두 번째는 글라이드 부인이 런던에 도착하던 때였다(이 일은 곧 이야기하겠다)."], ["Never at any other time was I indebted to my Art as distinguished from myself.", "그 밖의 어느 때에도 나는 나 자신과 구별되는 내 기술에 신세를 진 적이 없다."], ["To all other emergencies and complications my natural capacity for grappling, single-handed, with circumstances, was invariably equal.", "다른 모든 위급과 얽힘에 대해서는, 홀몸으로 정황과 맞붙는 내 타고난 역량이 언제나 넉넉했다."], ["I affirm the all-pervading intelligence of that capacity.", "나는 그 역량이 모든 것에 두루 미치는 지성임을 단언한다."], ["At the expense of the Chemist I vindicate the Man.", "화학자를 희생시켜 나는 인간의 명예를 옹호한다."]], [["Respect this outburst of generous indignation.", "이 너그러운 분노의 격발을 존중해 주기 바란다."], ["It has inexpressibly relieved me.", "덕분에 나는 이루 말할 수 없이 후련해졌다."], ["En route!", "자, 출발!"], ["Let us proceed.", "계속 나아가자."]], [["Having suggested to Mrs. Clement (or Clements, I am not sure which) that the best method of keeping Anne out of Percival’s reach was to remove her to London—having found that my proposal was eagerly received, and having appointed a day to meet the travellers at the station and to see them leave it, I was at liberty to return to the house and to confront the difficulties which still remained to be met.", "클레멘트 부인에게(아니면 클레멘츠 부인인가, 어느 쪽인지 나는 확실히 모르겠다) 앤을 퍼시벌의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 두는 최선의 방법은 그녀를 런던으로 옮기는 것이라고 일러 주고—내 제안이 반색하며 받아들여지는 것을 확인하고—역에서 그 여행자들을 만나 떠나보낼 날까지 정해 둔 뒤, 나는 마음 놓고 저택으로 돌아가 아직 남아 있는 난제들과 마주할 수 있었다."]], [["My first proceeding was to avail myself of the sublime devotion of my wife.", "내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 아내의 숭고한 헌신을 활용하는 것이었다."], ["I had arranged with Mrs. Clements that she should communicate her London address, in Anne’s interests, to Lady Glyde.", "나는 클레멘츠 부인과, 앤을 위해서 런던의 주소를 글라이드 부인에게 알려 주기로 약속을 해 두었다."], ["But this was not enough.", "그러나 그것만으로는 충분치 않았다."], ["Designing persons in my absence might shake the simple confidence of Mrs. Clements, and she might not write after all.", "내가 없는 사이에 흉계를 꾸미는 자들이 클레멘츠 부인의 순박한 신뢰를 흔들어 놓아, 결국 그녀가 편지를 쓰지 않게 될 수도 있었다."], ["Who could I find capable of travelling to London by the train she travelled by, and of privately seeing her home?", "그녀가 타는 바로 그 기차로 런던까지 가서 은밀히 그녀의 집까지 따라가 확인해 줄 사람을 어디서 찾는단 말인가?"], ["I asked myself this question.", "나는 스스로에게 이 물음을 던졌다."], ["The conjugal part of me immediately answered—Madame Fosco.", "내 안의 남편 된 부분이 즉각 대답했다—포스코 부인이라고."]], [["After deciding on my wife’s mission to London, I arranged that the journey should serve a double purpose.", "아내를 런던으로 보내기로 정한 뒤, 나는 그 여행이 두 가지 목적에 쓰이도록 조처했다."], ["A nurse for the suffering Marian, equally devoted to the patient and to myself, was a necessity of my position.", "앓고 있는 매리언을 돌볼 간호인, 그것도 환자에게만큼이나 나에게도 충실한 간호인은 내 처지에 반드시 필요한 존재였다."], ["One of the most eminently confidential and capable women in existence was by good fortune at my disposal.", "다행히도 세상에서 가장 입이 무겁고 유능한 여인 가운데 하나를 나는 마음대로 쓸 수 있었다."], ["I refer to that respectable matron, Madame Rubelle, to whom I addressed a letter, at her residence in London, by the hands of my wife.", "저 존경할 만한 부인, 루벨 부인 말이다. 나는 아내의 손에 편지를 들려 런던의 그녀 거처로 보냈다."]], [["On the appointed day Mrs. Clements and Anne Catherick met me at the station.", "정한 날, 클레멘츠 부인과 앤 캐서릭이 역에서 나를 만났다."], ["I politely saw them off, I politely saw Madame Fosco off by the same train.", "나는 정중히 그들을 배웅했고, 같은 기차로 떠나는 포스코 부인도 정중히 배웅했다."], ["The last thing at night my wife returned to Blackwater, having followed her instructions with the most unimpeachable accuracy.", "그날 밤늦게 아내는 블랙워터로 돌아왔는데, 내 지시를 나무랄 데 없이 정확하게 따른 뒤였다."], ["She was accompanied by Madame Rubelle, and she brought me the London address of Mrs.", "그녀는 루벨 부인과 함께였고, 클레멘츠 부인의 런던 주소를 가지고 왔다."], ["Clements. After-events proved this last precaution to have been unnecessary.", "나중에 벌어진 일들로 보아 이 마지막 대비책은 불필요했음이 드러났다."], ["Mrs. Clements punctually informed Lady Glyde of her place of abode.", "클레멘츠 부인은 어김없이 글라이드 부인에게 자기 거처를 알렸던 것이다."], ["With a wary eye on future emergencies, I kept the letter.", "나는 앞날의 위급에 대비하는 조심스러운 눈으로 그 편지를 간직해 두었다."]], [["The same day I had a brief interview with the doctor, at which I protested, in the sacred interests of humanity, against his treatment of Marian’s case.", "같은 날 나는 의사와 짧게 면담을 했고, 그 자리에서 인류의 신성한 이익을 위해 매리언의 병증에 대한 그의 처치에 항의했다."], ["He was insolent, as all ignorant people are.", "그는 무례했다. 무지한 자들이 다 그렇듯이."], ["I showed no resentment, I deferred quarrelling with him till it was necessary to quarrel to some purpose.", "나는 노여움을 드러내지 않았다. 실효를 거둘 만한 때에 다투기로 하고 그와의 싸움은 미뤄 두었다."], ["My next proceeding was to leave Blackwater myself.", "다음으로 내가 한 일은 나 자신이 블랙워터를 떠나는 것이었다."], ["I had my London residence to take in anticipation of coming events.", "앞으로 벌어질 일들을 내다보고 런던의 거처를 얻어 두어야 했다."], ["I had also a little business of the domestic sort to transact with Mr. Frederick Fairlie.", "또한 프레더릭 페얼리 씨와 처리할 소소한 집안일도 있었다."], ["I found the house I wanted in St. John’s Wood.", "나는 세인트존스우드에서 원하던 집을 찾아냈다."], ["I found Mr. Fairlie at Limmeridge, Cumberland.", "그리고 컴벌랜드의 리머리지에서 페얼리 씨를 찾아냈다."]], [["My own private familiarity with the nature of Marian’s correspondence had previously informed me that she had written to Mr. Fairlie, proposing, as a relief to Lady Glyde’s matrimonial embarrassments, to take her on a visit to her uncle in Cumberland.", "매리언의 서신 왕래가 어떤 성격인지 내밀히 알고 있던 터라, 나는 그녀가 페얼리 씨에게 편지를 써서 글라이드 부인의 결혼 생활의 곤경을 덜어 주고자 그녀를 컴벌랜드의 삼촌 댁으로 데려가겠다고 제안했음을 이미 알고 있었다."], ["This letter I had wisely allowed to reach its destination, feeling at the time that it could do no harm, and might do good.", "이 편지는 당장은 해가 될 것이 없고 오히려 득이 될 수도 있겠다 싶어, 내가 현명하게도 목적지에 그대로 닿게 두었던 것이다."], ["I now presented myself before Mr. Fairlie to support Marian’s own proposal—with certain modifications which, happily for the success of my plans, were rendered really inevitable by her illness.", "이제 나는 매리언 자신의 제안을 뒷받침하고자 페얼리 씨 앞에 나섰다—다만 몇 군데를 손보아서였는데, 내 계획의 성공을 위해서는 다행스럽게도 그녀의 병 덕분에 그 수정이 정말로 불가피해져 있었다."], ["It was necessary that Lady Glyde should leave Blackwater alone, by her uncle’s invitation, and that she should rest a night on the journey at her aunt’s house (the house I had in St. John’s Wood) by her uncle’s express advice.", "글라이드 부인은 삼촌의 초대를 받아 홀로 블랙워터를 떠나야 했고, 삼촌의 명시적인 권유에 따라 여정 중 하룻밤을 고모 집에서(내가 세인트존스우드에 얻어 둔 그 집에서) 묵어야 했다."], ["To achieve these results, and to secure a note of invitation which could be shown to Lady Glyde, were the objects of my visit to Mr. Fairlie.", "이 결과를 얻어 내는 것, 그리고 글라이드 부인에게 보여 줄 수 있는 초대 편지를 확보하는 것이 페얼리 씨를 찾아간 목적이었다."], ["When I have mentioned that this gentleman was equally feeble in mind and body, and that I let loose the whole force of my character on him, I have said enough.", "이 신사가 정신도 육체도 똑같이 허약했으며 내가 내 성격의 온 힘을 그에게 풀어놓았다고만 말해 두면, 그것으로 충분할 것이다."], ["I came, saw, and conquered Fairlie.", "나는 왔고, 보았고, 페얼리를 정복했다."]], [["On my return to Blackwater Park (with the letter of invitation) I found that the doctor’s imbecile treatment of Marian’s case had led to the most alarming results.", "초대 편지를 손에 넣고 블랙워터 파크로 돌아와 보니, 매리언의 병에 대한 의사의 얼빠진 처치가 더없이 우려스러운 결과로 이어져 있었다."], ["The fever had turned to typhus.", "열병이 발진티푸스로 번진 것이다."], ["Lady Glyde, on the day of my return, tried to force herself into the room to nurse her sister.", "내가 돌아온 날 글라이드 부인은 언니를 간호하겠다며 억지로 방에 들어가려 했다."], ["She and I had no affinities of sympathy—she had committed the unpardonable outrage on my sensibilities of calling me a spy—she was a stumbling-block in my way and in Percival’s—but, for all that, my magnanimity forbade me to put her in danger of infection with my own hand.", "그녀와 나 사이에는 마음이 통하는 구석이 없었고—그녀는 나를 첩자라 부르는, 내 감수성에 대한 용서받지 못할 폭거를 저질렀으며—내 앞길과 퍼시벌의 앞길을 가로막는 걸림돌이었다. 그럼에도 불구하고 내 아량은 내 손으로 그녀를 전염의 위험에 빠뜨리는 일을 금했다."], ["At the same time I offered no hindrance to her putting herself in danger.", "동시에 그녀가 스스로 위험에 뛰어드는 것을 나는 조금도 막지 않았다."], ["If she had succeeded in doing so, the intricate knot which I was slowly and patiently operating on might perhaps have been cut by circumstances.", "만약 그녀가 그렇게 하는 데 성공했더라면, 내가 더디고 참을성 있게 풀어 나가던 그 얽히고설킨 매듭이 어쩌면 정황의 힘으로 단칼에 끊겼을지도 모른다."], ["As it was, the doctor interfered and she was kept out of the room.", "실제로는 의사가 끼어들었고, 그녀는 그 방에 들어가지 못했다."]], [["I had myself previously recommended sending for advice to London.", "런던에 자문을 청하자고 진작 권한 사람은 나 자신이었다."], ["This course had been now taken.", "이제 그 절차가 밟혔다."], ["The physician, on his arrival, confirmed my view of the case.", "도착한 의사는 내 진단을 확인해 주었다."], ["The crisis was serious.", "고비는 심각했다."], ["But we had hope of our charming patient on the fifth day from the appearance of the typhus. I was only once absent from Blackwater at this time—when I went to London by the morning train to make the final arrangements at my house in St.", "그러나 발진티푸스가 나타난 날로부터 닷새째에 우리는 이 매력적인 환자에게 희망을 걸 수 있게 되었다."], ["John’s Wood, to assure myself by private inquiry that Mrs. Clements had not moved, and to settle one or two little preliminary matters with the husband of Madame Rubelle.", "이 무렵 내가 블랙워터를 비운 것은 단 한 번뿐이었다—아침 기차로 런던에 가서 세인트존스우드 집의 마지막 준비를 마치고, 클레멘츠 부인이 거처를 옮기지 않았음을 은밀한 탐문으로 확인하고, 루벨 부인의 남편과 사소한 예비 문제 한두 가지를 매듭짓던 날이었다."], ["I returned at night.", "나는 밤에 돌아왔다."], ["Five days afterwards the physician pronounced our interesting Marian to be out of all danger, and to be in need of nothing but careful nursing.", "닷새 뒤 의사는 우리의 흥미로운 매리언이 모든 위험에서 벗어났으며 이제 세심한 간병 말고는 아무것도 필요치 않다고 선언했다."], ["This was the time I had waited for.", "이때가 바로 내가 기다려 온 순간이었다."], ["Now that medical attendance was no longer indispensable, I played the first move in the game by asserting myself against the doctor.", "의사의 시중이 더는 없어서는 안 될 것이 아니게 되자, 나는 그 의사에게 나를 내세움으로써 이 승부의 첫 수를 두었다."], ["He was one among many witnesses in my way whom it was necessary to remove.", "그는 내 앞길에 놓인 여러 목격자 가운데 하나였고, 치워야 할 인물이었다."], ["A lively altercation between us (in which Percival, previously instructed by me, refused to interfere) served the purpose in view.", "우리 둘 사이의 격렬한 언쟁이(퍼시벌은 내가 미리 일러 둔 대로 끼어들기를 거부했다) 그 목적에 부합했다."], ["I descended on the miserable man in an irresistible avalanche of indignation, and swept him from the house.", "나는 걷잡을 수 없는 분노의 눈사태가 되어 그 가련한 인간을 덮쳤고, 그를 집 밖으로 쓸어냈다."]], [["The servants were the next encumbrances to get rid of.", "다음으로 치워야 할 방해물은 하인들이었다."], ["Again I instructed Percival (whose moral courage required perpetual stimulants), and Mrs. Michelson was amazed, one day, by hearing from her master that the establishment was to be broken up.", "나는 다시 퍼시벌을 움직였고(그의 도덕적 용기는 끊임없는 자극제를 필요로 했다), 마이컬슨 부인은 어느 날 주인에게서 이 집 살림을 해산한다는 말을 듣고 경악했다."], ["We cleared the house of all the servants but one, who was kept for domestic purposes, and whose lumpish stupidity we could trust to make no embarrassing discoveries.", "우리는 집안일을 위해 한 사람만 남기고 하인을 모두 내보냈는데, 남긴 아이는 그 굼뜬 우둔함 덕분에 곤란한 것을 알아챌 리 없다고 믿을 만했다."], ["When they were gone, nothing remained but to relieve ourselves of Mrs. Michelson—a result which was easily achieved by sending this amiable lady to find lodgings for her mistress at the sea-side.", "그들이 떠나고 나자 이제 마이컬슨 부인을 떼어 놓는 일만 남았는데, 이 상냥한 부인을 바닷가로 보내 여주인이 묵을 숙소를 알아보게 하는 것으로 손쉽게 이루어졌다."]], [["The circumstances were now exactly what they were required to be.", "이제 정황은 우리가 바라던 그대로였다."], ["Lady Glyde was confined to her room by nervous illness, and the lumpish housemaid (I forget her name) was shut up there at night in attendance on her mistress.", "글라이드 부인은 신경성 질환으로 방에 갇혀 있었고, 그 굼뜬 하녀는(이름은 잊었다) 밤이면 여주인의 시중을 들러 그 방에 함께 들어갔다."], ["Marian, though fast recovering, still kept her bed, with Mrs. Rubelle for nurse.", "매리언은 빠르게 회복되고 있었으나 아직 자리보전을 하고 있었고, 루벨 부인이 간호를 맡았다."], ["No other living creatures but my wife, myself, and Percival were in the house.", "그 집에는 내 아내와 나, 그리고 퍼시벌 말고는 살아 있는 사람이 없었다."], ["With all the chances thus in our favour I confronted the next emergency, and played the second move in the game.", "이렇게 모든 기회가 우리에게 유리해진 가운데 나는 다음 고비와 맞섰고, 이 승부의 두 번째 수를 두었다."]], [["The object of the second move was to induce Lady Glyde to leave Blackwater unaccompanied by her sister.", "두 번째 수의 목적은 글라이드 부인이 언니 없이 홀로 블랙워터를 떠나도록 유도하는 것이었다."], ["Unless we could persuade her that Marian had gone on to Cumberland first, there was no chance of removing her, of her own free will, from the house.", "매리언이 먼저 컴벌랜드로 갔다고 그녀를 납득시키지 못한다면, 그녀 스스로의 뜻으로 그 집을 떠나게 할 가망은 없었다."], ["To produce this necessary operation in her mind, we concealed our interesting invalid in one of the uninhabited bedrooms at Blackwater.", "그녀의 마음에 그 필요한 작용을 일으키기 위해, 우리는 우리의 흥미로운 환자를 블랙워터의 빈 침실 중 하나에 숨겼다."], ["At the dead of night Madame Fosco, Madame Rubelle, and myself (Percival not being cool enough to be trusted) accomplished the concealment.", "깊은 밤에 포스코 부인과 루벨 부인과 내가(퍼시벌은 침착하지 못해 믿고 맡길 수 없었다) 그 은닉을 해냈다."], ["The scene was picturesque, mysterious, dramatic in the highest degree.", "그 광경은 그림 같고 신비롭고 더없이 극적이었다."], ["By my directions the bed had been made, in the morning, on a strong movable framework of wood.", "내 지시에 따라 침상은 아침에 튼튼하고 옮길 수 있는 나무틀 위에 마련되어 있었다."], ["We had only to lift the framework gently at the head and foot, and to transport our patient where we pleased, without disturbing herself or her bed.", "우리는 그 틀의 머리와 발을 가만히 들어 올리기만 하면, 환자도 침상도 흐트러뜨리지 않고 원하는 곳으로 옮길 수 있었다."], ["No chemical assistance was needed or used in this case.", "이 경우에는 화학의 도움이 필요하지도 않았고 쓰이지도 않았다."], ["Our interesting Marian lay in the deep repose of convalescence.", "우리의 흥미로운 매리언은 회복기의 깊은 잠에 빠져 있었다."], ["We placed the candles and opened the doors beforehand.", "우리는 미리 촛불을 놓아두고 문을 열어 두었다."], ["I, in right of my great personal strength, took the head of the framework—my wife and Madame Rubelle took the foot.", "나는 대단한 완력을 지닌 자격으로 틀의 머리 쪽을 들었고, 아내와 루벨 부인이 발 쪽을 들었다."], ["I bore my share of that inestimably precious burden with a manly tenderness, with a fatherly care.", "나는 그 값을 매길 수 없이 소중한 짐의 내 몫을 사내다운 다정함으로, 아버지 같은 조심스러움으로 감당했다."], ["Where is the modern Rembrandt who could depict our midnight procession?", "우리의 한밤중 행렬을 그려 낼 근대의 렘브란트는 어디에 있는가?"], ["Alas for the Arts!", "아, 예술이여!"], ["alas for this most pictorial of subjects!", "아, 이토록 그림 같은 소재여!"], ["The modern Rembrandt is nowhere to be found.", "근대의 렘브란트는 어디에도 없다."]], [["The next morning my wife and I started for London, leaving Marian secluded, in the uninhabited middle of the house, under care of Madame Rubelle, who kindly consented to imprison herself with her patient for two or three days.", "이튿날 아침 아내와 나는 런던으로 떠났고, 매리언은 사람이 살지 않는 집 한가운데에 격리되어 루벨 부인의 보살핌에 맡겨졌다. 루벨 부인은 이삼 일 동안 환자와 함께 스스로를 가두는 데 흔쾌히 동의해 주었다."], ["Before taking our departure I gave Percival Mr. Fairlie’s letter of invitation to his niece (instructing her to sleep on the journey to Cumberland at her aunt’s house), with directions to show it to Lady Glyde on hearing from me.", "떠나기 전에 나는 퍼시벌에게 페얼리 씨가 조카딸에게 보낸 초대 편지를(컴벌랜드로 가는 도중 고모 집에서 하룻밤 묵으라는 당부가 담긴 편지를) 건네주며, 내 연락을 받는 대로 글라이드 부인에게 보이라고 일러 두었다."], ["I also obtained from him the address of the Asylum in which Anne Catherick had been confined, and a letter to the proprietor, announcing to that gentleman the return of his runaway patient to medical care.", "또한 그에게서 앤 캐서릭이 갇혀 있던 정신병원의 주소와, 달아난 환자가 다시 의료의 보호 아래로 돌아온다는 사실을 원장에게 알리는 편지를 받아 두었다."]], [["I had arranged, at my last visit to the metropolis, to have our modest domestic establishment ready to receive us when we arrived in London by the early train.", "지난번 수도를 다녀올 때 나는 우리가 이른 기차로 런던에 도착하는 대로 곧바로 맞을 수 있도록 조촐한 살림을 준비해 두라고 조처해 두었다."], ["In consequence of this wise precaution, we were enabled that same day to play the third move in the game—the getting possession of Anne Catherick.", "이 현명한 대비 덕분에 우리는 바로 그날 이 승부의 세 번째 수를 둘 수 있었다—앤 캐서릭을 손에 넣는 수였다."]], [["Dates are of importance here.", "여기서는 날짜가 중요하다."], ["I combine in myself the opposite characteristics of a Man of Sentiment and a Man of Business.", "나는 감성의 인간과 사무의 인간이라는 상반된 특성을 한 몸에 겸비하고 있다."], ["I have all the dates at my fingers’ ends.", "날짜란 날짜는 모두 내 손끝에 있다."]], [["On Wednesday, the 24th of July 1850, I sent my wife in a cab to clear Mrs. Clements out of the way, in the first place.", "1850년 7월 24일 수요일, 나는 우선 클레멘츠 부인을 치워 놓기 위해 아내를 삯마차에 태워 보냈다."], ["A supposed message from Lady Glyde in London was sufficient to obtain this result. Mrs.", "런던에 있는 글라이드 부인이 보냈다는 전갈 하나면 그 목적에는 충분했다."], ["Clements was taken away in the cab, and was left in the cab, while my wife (on pretence of purchasing something at a shop) gave her the slip, and returned to receive her expected visitor at our house in St.", "클레멘츠 부인은 마차에 실려 나갔고 마차에 남겨졌다. 그사이 내 아내는(가게에서 무엇을 산다는 핑계로) 그녀를 따돌리고 세인트존스우드의 우리 집으로 돌아가, 오기로 되어 있는 손님을 맞았다."], ["John’s Wood. It is hardly necessary to add that the visitor had been described to the servants as “Lady Glyde.”", "그 손님이 하인들에게 “글라이드 부인”이라고 소개되어 있었음은 굳이 덧붙일 것도 없다."]], [["In the meanwhile I had followed in another cab, with a note for Anne Catherick, merely mentioning that Lady Glyde intended to keep Mrs.", "그동안 나는 다른 마차를 타고 뒤따라가 있었는데, 앤 캐서릭에게 보낼 쪽지를 지니고 있었다."], ["Clements to spend the day with her, and that she was to join them under care of the good gentleman waiting outside, who had already saved her from discovery in Hampshire by Sir Percival.", "쪽지에는 그저 글라이드 부인이 클레멘츠 부인을 붙들어 하루를 함께 보낼 작정이니, 밖에서 기다리는 친절한 신사분—햄프셔에서 퍼시벌 경에게 발각되는 것을 이미 막아 준 그분—의 보살핌을 받아 두 사람에게 합류하라는 말만 적혀 있었다."], ["The “good gentleman” sent in this note by a street boy, and paused for results a door or two farther on.", "그 “친절한 신사”는 거리의 아이를 시켜 이 쪽지를 들여보내고는 한두 집 건너에서 결과를 기다렸다."], ["At the moment when Anne appeared at the house door and closed it this excellent man had the cab door open ready for her, absorbed her into the vehicle, and drove off.", "앤이 문간에 나타나 문을 닫는 순간, 이 훌륭한 사내는 그녀를 위해 마차 문을 열어 놓고 있다가 그녀를 차 안으로 빨아들이고 그대로 달려갔다."]], [["(Pass me, here, one exclamation in parenthesis.", "(여기서 괄호 속 감탄 한마디를 눈감아 주기 바란다."], ["How interesting this is!)", "이 얼마나 흥미로운가!)"]], [["On the way to Forest Road my companion showed no fear.", "포리스트 로드로 가는 길에 내 동승자는 아무런 두려움도 보이지 않았다."], ["I can be paternal—no man more so—when I please, and I was intensely paternal on this occasion.", "나는 마음만 먹으면 아버지처럼 굴 수 있다—그 점에서 나를 능가할 자는 없다—그리고 그때 나는 지극히 아버지 같았다."], ["What titles I had to her confidence!", "그녀의 신뢰를 받을 자격이 내게 얼마나 많았던가!"], ["I had compounded the medicine which had done her good—I had warned her of her danger from Sir Percival.", "그녀에게 이로웠던 약을 조제한 것도 나였고, 퍼시벌 경에게서 오는 위험을 그녀에게 일러 준 것도 나였다."], ["Perhaps I trusted too implicitly to these titles—perhaps I underrated the keenness of the lower instincts in persons of weak intellect—it is certain that I neglected to prepare her sufficiently for a disappointment on entering my house.", "어쩌면 나는 그 자격을 너무 굳게 믿었는지도 모른다—어쩌면 지력이 약한 사람들에게 있는 낮은 본능의 예민함을 얕보았는지도 모른다—어쨌든 내 집에 들어설 때의 실망에 대해 그녀를 충분히 대비시키지 못한 것만은 분명하다."], ["When I took her into the drawing-room—when she saw no one present but Madame Fosco, who was a stranger to her—she exhibited the most violent agitation; if she had scented danger in the air, as a dog scents the presence of some creature unseen, her alarm could not have displayed itself more suddenly and more causelessly.", "내가 그녀를 응접실로 데리고 들어갔을 때—그녀에게 낯선 사람인 포스코 부인 말고는 아무도 없는 것을 그녀가 보았을 때—그녀는 더없이 격렬한 동요를 드러냈다. 개가 보이지 않는 무언가의 기척을 맡듯 그녀가 공기 중에서 위험의 냄새를 맡았다 한들, 그보다 더 갑작스럽고 더 까닭 없이 경보를 드러낼 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["I interposed in vain.", "내가 끼어들어 보았으나 소용없었다."], ["The fear from which she was suffering I might have soothed, but the serious heart-disease, under which she laboured, was beyond the reach of all moral palliatives.", "그녀가 겪고 있던 그 두려움이라면 내가 달래 줄 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀가 앓고 있던 중한 심장병은 어떤 정신적 완화책도 미치지 못하는 것이었다."], ["To my unspeakable horror she was seized with convulsions—a shock to the system, in her condition, which might have laid her dead at any moment at our feet.", "이루 말할 수 없이 소름 끼치게도 그녀는 경련에 사로잡혔다—그 몸 상태에서 그런 충격은 언제라도 그녀를 우리 발치에 시체로 눕혀 놓을 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["The nearest doctor was sent for, and was told that “Lady Glyde” required his immediate services.", "가장 가까운 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈고, “글라이드 부인”이 당장 진료를 받아야 한다고 전했다."], ["To my infinite relief, he was a capable man.", "더없이 다행스럽게도 그는 유능한 사람이었다."], ["I represented my visitor to him as a person of weak intellect, and subject to delusions, and I arranged that no nurse but my wife should watch in the sick-room.", "나는 내 손님을 지력이 약하고 망상에 시달리는 사람이라고 그에게 소개했으며, 병실에는 내 아내 말고는 어떤 간호인도 지키지 못하도록 조처했다."], ["The unhappy woman was too ill, however, to cause any anxiety about what she might say.", "그러나 그 불행한 여인은 무슨 말을 할지 걱정할 것도 없을 만큼 위중했다."], ["The one dread which now oppressed me was the dread that the false Lady Glyde might die before the true Lady Glyde arrived in London.", "이제 나를 짓누르는 유일한 두려움은, 진짜 글라이드 부인이 런던에 도착하기 전에 가짜 글라이드 부인이 죽어 버리지나 않을까 하는 것이었다."]], [["I had written a note in the morning to Madame Rubelle, telling her to join me at her husband’s house on the evening of Friday the 26th, with another note to Percival, warning him to show his wife her uncle’s letter of invitation, to assert that Marian had gone on before her, and to despatch her to town by the midday train, on the 26th, also.", "나는 그날 아침 루벨 부인에게 26일 금요일 저녁에 남편의 집에서 나와 합류하라는 쪽지를 써 보냈고, 퍼시벌에게도 아내에게 삼촌의 초대 편지를 보여 주고 매리언이 먼저 떠났다고 못 박은 뒤 역시 26일 정오 기차로 그녀를 런던으로 보내라고 당부하는 쪽지를 써 보냈다."], ["On reflection I had felt the necessity, in Anne Catherick’s state of health, of precipitating events, and of having Lady Glyde at my disposal earlier than I had originally contemplated.", "앤 캐서릭의 건강 상태를 헤아려 보고 나는 일을 앞당겨야 할 필요를, 그리고 애초 예상보다 이르게 글라이드 부인을 내 뜻대로 둘 필요를 절감했던 것이다."], ["What fresh directions, in the terrible uncertainty of my position, could I now issue?", "내 처지의 그 끔찍한 불확실 속에서 이제 무슨 새 지시를 내릴 수 있었겠는가?"], ["I could do nothing but trust to chance and the doctor.", "나는 운과 의사에게 맡기는 것 외에 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["My emotions expressed themselves in pathetic apostrophes, which I was just self-possessed enough to couple, in the hearing of other people, with the name of “Lady Glyde.”", "내 감정은 애처로운 탄식으로 터져 나왔고, 다른 사람들이 듣는 데서는 그 탄식에 “글라이드 부인”이라는 이름을 붙일 만큼의 자제력만은 겨우 남아 있었다."], ["In all other respects Fosco, on that memorable day, was Fosco shrouded in total eclipse.", "그 밖의 모든 면에서, 그 잊지 못할 날의 포스코는 온통 개기일식에 뒤덮인 포스코였다."]], [["She passed a bad night, she awoke worn out, but later in the day she revived amazingly.", "그녀는 밤을 험하게 넘겼고 기진한 채 깨어났으나, 그날 늦게 놀랄 만큼 기운을 되찾았다."], ["My elastic spirits revived with her.", "탄력 좋은 내 기분도 그녀와 함께 되살아났다."], ["I could receive no answers from Percival and Madame Rubelle till the morning of the next day, the 26th.", "퍼시벌과 루벨 부인의 답은 이튿날인 26일 아침에나 받을 수 있었다."], ["In anticipation of their following my directions, which, accident apart, I knew they would do, I went to secure a fly to fetch Lady Glyde from the railway, directing it to be at my house on the 26th, at two o’clock.", "사고만 없다면 그들이 내 지시를 따르리라는 것을 알고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 미리 내다보고 글라이드 부인을 역에서 데려올 삯마차를 잡으러 나가, 26일 두 시에 우리 집으로 오도록 일러 두었다."], ["After seeing the order entered in the book, I went on to arrange matters with Monsieur Rubelle.", "주문이 장부에 적히는 것을 확인한 뒤, 나는 루벨 씨와 일을 매듭지으러 갔다."], ["I also procured the services of two gentlemen who could furnish me with the necessary certificates of lunacy.", "또한 필요한 정신이상 진단서를 떼어 줄 수 있는 신사 두 사람의 도움도 확보했다."], ["One of them I knew personally—the other was known to Monsieur Rubelle.", "그중 한 사람은 내가 개인적으로 아는 사이였고, 다른 한 사람은 루벨 씨가 아는 사이였다."], ["Both were men whose vigorous minds soared superior to narrow scruples—both were labouring under temporary embarrassments—both believed in ME.", "둘 다 그 활달한 정신이 옹졸한 양심의 가책 따위는 훌쩍 뛰어넘는 사람들이었고, 둘 다 일시적인 궁핍에 시달리고 있었으며, 둘 다 이 몸 포스코를 믿었다."]], [["It was past five o’clock in the afternoon before I returned from the performance of these duties.", "이 일들을 마치고 돌아온 것은 오후 다섯 시가 넘어서였다. 돌아와 보니 앤 캐서릭은 죽어 있었다."], ["When I got back Anne Catherick was dead.", "25일에 죽은 것이다."], ["Dead on the 25th, and Lady Glyde was not to arrive in London till the 26th!", "그런데 글라이드 부인은 26일에야 런던에 도착할 참이었다!"]], [["I was stunned.", "나는 망연자실했다."], ["Meditate on that.", "이것을 곱씹어 보라."], ["Fosco stunned!", "포스코가 망연자실했다니!"]], [["It was too late to retrace our steps.", "되돌리기에는 너무 늦었다."], ["Before my return the doctor had officiously undertaken to save me all trouble by registering the death, on the date when it happened, with his own hand.", "내가 돌아오기 전에 그 의사가 주제넘게도 내 수고를 덜어 준답시고 사망한 날짜 그대로 제 손으로 사망 신고를 해 버린 것이다."], ["My grand scheme, unassailable hitherto, had its weak place now—no efforts on my part could alter the fatal event of the 25th.", "그때까지 빈틈없던 내 원대한 계획에 이제 약한 곳이 생겼다—25일이라는 그 치명적인 사실은 내가 어떤 노력을 해도 바꿀 수 없었다."], ["I turned manfully to the future.", "나는 사내답게 앞날 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Percival’s interests and mine being still at stake, nothing was left but to play the game through to the end.", "퍼시벌의 이해와 나의 이해가 여전히 걸려 있는 이상, 남은 것은 이 승부를 끝까지 두는 일뿐이었다."], ["I recalled my impenetrable calm—and played it.", "나는 뚫을 수 없는 평정을 되찾았다—그리고 승부를 두었다."]], [["On the morning of the 26th Percival’s letter reached me, announcing his wife’s arrival by the midday train.", "26일 아침 퍼시벌의 편지가 도착해 아내가 정오 기차로 떠난다고 알려 왔다."], ["Madame Rubelle also wrote to say she would follow in the evening.", "루벨 부인도 저녁에 뒤따르겠다고 편지를 보내왔다."], ["I started in the fly, leaving the false Lady Glyde dead in the house, to receive the true Lady Glyde on her arrival by the railway at three o’clock.", "나는 가짜 글라이드 부인을 죽은 채로 집에 남겨 둔 채 삯마차를 타고 나서, 세 시에 기차로 도착하는 진짜 글라이드 부인을 맞으러 갔다."], ["Hidden under the seat of the carriage, I carried with me all the clothes Anne Catherick had worn on coming into my house—they were destined to assist the resurrection of the woman who was dead in the person of the woman who was living.", "마차 좌석 밑에 감추어 나는 앤 캐서릭이 내 집에 들어올 때 입고 있던 옷을 모두 싣고 갔다—그 옷들은 죽은 여자가 산 여자의 몸으로 부활하는 일을 거들 운명이었다. 이 얼마나 대단한 상황인가!"], ["What a situation! I suggest it to the rising romance writers of England.", "나는 이것을 영국의 신진 소설가들에게 권한다."], ["I offer it, as totally new, to the worn-out dramatists of France.", "이것을 완전히 새로운 것으로서, 진이 빠진 프랑스의 극작가들에게 바친다."]], [["Lady Glyde was at the station.", "글라이드 부인은 역에 나와 있었다."], ["There was great crowding and confusion, and more delay than I liked (in case any of her friends had happened to be on the spot), in reclaiming her luggage.", "사람이 크게 붐비고 어수선했으며, 짐을 찾는 데 내 마음에 들지 않을 만큼 시간이 지체되었다(혹시라도 그녀의 친지 가운데 누가 그 자리에 있었을까 봐 하는 말이다)."], ["Her first questions, as we drove off, implored me to tell her news of her sister.", "마차가 떠나자 그녀는 첫마디로 언니의 소식을 알려 달라고 애원했다."], ["I invented news of the most pacifying kind, assuring her that she was about to see her sister at my house.", "나는 더없이 마음을 놓게 할 소식을 지어내어, 곧 내 집에서 언니를 보게 될 것이라고 그녀를 안심시켰다."], ["My house, on this occasion only, was in the neighbourhood of Leicester Square, and was in the occupation of Monsieur Rubelle, who received us in the hall.", "이번만은 내 집이 레스터 스퀘어 근처에 있었고 루벨 씨가 거처하고 있었는데, 그가 현관에서 우리를 맞았다."]], [["I took my visitor upstairs into a back room, the two medical gentlemen being there in waiting on the floor beneath to see the patient, and to give me their certificates.", "나는 손님을 데리고 위층 뒷방으로 올라갔고, 두 의료계 신사는 환자를 진찰하고 진단서를 써 주려고 아래층에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["After quieting Lady Glyde by the necessary assurances about her sister, I introduced my friends separately to her presence.", "언니에 대해 필요한 만큼 안심시켜 글라이드 부인을 진정시킨 뒤, 나는 두 벗을 따로따로 그녀 앞에 들여보냈다."], ["They performed the formalities of the occasion briefly, intelligently, conscientiously.", "그들은 이 자리에 필요한 절차를 짧고 명민하고 성실하게 수행했다."], ["I entered the room again as soon as they had left it, and at once precipitated events by a reference of the alarming kind to “Miss Halcombe’s” state of health.", "그들이 방을 나서자마자 나는 다시 방에 들어가, “홀콤 양”의 건강 상태에 대해 놀랄 만한 이야기를 꺼냄으로써 곧바로 사태를 재촉했다."]], [["Results followed as I had anticipated.", "결과는 내가 예상한 대로 따라왔다."], ["Lady Glyde became frightened, and turned faint.", "글라이드 부인은 겁에 질렸고 정신이 아득해졌다."], ["For the second time, and the last, I called Science to my assistance.", "두 번째이자 마지막으로 나는 과학을 도움으로 불러들였다."], ["A medicated glass of water and a medicated bottle of smelling-salts relieved her of all further embarrassment and alarm.", "약을 탄 물 한 잔과 약을 처리한 각성제 병 하나가 그녀에게서 그 이상의 곤혹과 경계를 모두 걷어 갔다."], ["Additional applications later in the evening procured her the inestimable blessing of a good night’s rest.", "저녁 늦게 몇 차례 더 쓴 처치는 그녀에게 값을 매길 수 없는 축복인 하룻밤의 단잠을 마련해 주었다."], ["Madame Rubelle arrived in time to preside at Lady Glyde’s toilet.", "루벨 부인은 글라이드 부인의 몸단장을 맡을 시간에 맞춰 도착했다."], ["Her own clothes were taken away from her at night, and Anne Catherick’s were put on her in the— morning, with the strictest regard to propriety, by the matronly hands of the good Rubelle.", "밤에 그녀 자신의 옷을 벗겨 두었고, 아침에 앤 캐서릭의 옷을 입혔는데, 예법을 가장 엄격히 지켜 가며 저 훌륭한 루벨 부인의 원숙한 손이 그 일을 했다."], ["Throughout the day I kept our patient in a state of partially-suspended consciousness, until the dexterous assistance of my medical friends enabled me to procure the necessary order rather earlier than I had ventured to hope.", "나는 그날 내내 우리 환자를 의식이 반쯤 정지된 상태로 두었고, 마침내 내 의료계 벗들의 능란한 도움 덕분에 감히 바랐던 것보다 이르게 필요한 명령서를 손에 넣을 수 있었다."], ["That evening (the evening of the 27th) Madame Rubelle and I took our revived “Anne Catherick” to the Asylum.", "그날 저녁(27일 저녁) 루벨 부인과 나는 되살아난 우리의 “앤 캐서릭”을 정신병원으로 데려갔다."], ["She was received with great surprise, but without suspicion, thanks to the order and certificates, to Percival’s letter, to the likeness, to the clothes, and to the patient’s own confused mental condition at the time.", "그녀는 큰 놀라움 속에 인계되었으나 아무런 의심도 사지 않았다. 명령서와 진단서 덕분에, 퍼시벌의 편지 덕분에, 그 닮은꼴 덕분에, 그 옷 덕분에, 그리고 그때 환자 자신의 혼미한 정신 상태 덕분에."], ["I returned at once to assist Madame Fosco in the preparations for the burial of the False “Lady Glyde,” having the clothes and luggage of the true “Lady Glyde”", "나는 곧바로 돌아와 가짜 “글라이드 부인”의 장례 준비를 하는 포스코 부인을 도왔고, 진짜 “글라이드 부인”의 옷과 짐은 내가 가지고 있었다."], ["in my possession. They were afterwards sent to Cumberland by the conveyance which was used for the funeral.", "그것들은 나중에 장례에 쓰인 운송편으로 컴벌랜드에 보내졌다."], ["I attended the funeral, with becoming dignity, attired in the deepest mourning.", "나는 가장 깊은 상복을 갖춰 입고 합당한 위엄을 지키며 장례에 참석했다."]], [["My narrative of these remarkable events, written under equally remarkable circumstances, closes here.", "이 놀라운 사건들에 대한, 그에 못지않게 놀라운 정황 속에서 쓰인 나의 기록은 여기서 끝난다."], ["The minor precautions which I observed in communicating with Limmeridge House are already known—so is the magnificent success of my enterprise—so are the solid pecuniary results which followed it.", "리머리지 하우스와 연락을 주고받으며 내가 지켰던 사소한 조심들은 이미 알려져 있다—내 사업의 장대한 성공도, 그 뒤를 따른 든든한 금전적 결실도 마찬가지다."], ["I have to assert, with the whole force of my conviction, that the one weak place in my scheme would never have been found out if the one weak place in my heart had not been discovered first.", "나는 내 확신의 온 힘을 실어 단언해야겠다. 내 계획의 단 하나 약한 곳은, 내 심장의 단 하나 약한 곳이 먼저 발견되지 않았더라면 결코 드러나지 않았으리라고."], ["Nothing but my fatal admiration for Marian restrained me from stepping in to my own rescue when she effected her sister’s escape.", "매리언이 동생을 탈출시켰을 때 내가 나서서 나 자신을 구하지 못하도록 막은 것은 오직 그녀를 향한 치명적인 흠모뿐이었다."], ["I ran the risk, and trusted in the complete destruction of Lady Glyde’s identity. If either Marian or Mr.", "나는 그 위험을 무릅썼고, 글라이드 부인의 신원이 완전히 파괴되었음을 믿었다."], ["Hartright attempted to assert that identity, they would publicly expose themselves to the imputation of sustaining a rank deception, they would be distrusted and discredited accordingly, and they would therefore be powerless to place my interests or Percival’s secret in jeopardy.", "매리언이나 하트라이트 씨가 그 신원을 주장하려 든다면, 그들은 세상 앞에 지독한 사기를 떠받든다는 혐의를 스스로 뒤집어쓸 것이고, 그에 따라 불신받고 신용을 잃을 것이며, 따라서 내 이익이나 퍼시벌의 비밀을 위태롭게 할 힘이 없으리라 여겼다."], ["I committed one error in trusting myself to such a blindfold calculation of chances as this.", "나는 이런 눈 가린 확률 계산에 나를 맡긴 데서 한 가지 잘못을 저질렀다."], ["I committed another when Percival had paid the penalty of his own obstinacy and violence, by granting Lady Glyde a second reprieve from the mad-house, and allowing Mr. Hartright a second chance of escaping me.", "또 한 가지 잘못은, 퍼시벌이 제 고집과 난폭함의 대가를 치른 뒤에 글라이드 부인에게 정신병원으로부터 두 번째 사면을 베풀고 하트라이트 씨에게 나를 벗어날 두 번째 기회를 허락한 것이다."], ["In brief, Fosco, at this serious crisis, was untrue to himself.", "요컨대 이 중대한 고비에서 포스코는 자기 자신에게 충실하지 못했다."], ["Deplorable and uncharacteristic fault!", "개탄스럽고 그답지 않은 과실이여!"], ["Behold the cause, in my heart—behold, in the image of Marian Halcombe, the first and last weakness of Fosco’s life!", "그 원인을 내 심장에서 보라—매리언 홀콤이라는 모습에서, 포스코의 생애에서 처음이자 마지막인 약점을 보라!"]], [["At the ripe age of sixty, I make this unparalleled confession.", "예순의 원숙한 나이에 나는 이 유례없는 고백을 한다. 젊은이들이여!"], ["Youths! I invoke your sympathy.", "그대들의 공감을 청한다."], ["Maidens!", "처녀들이여!"], ["I claim your tears.", "그대들의 눈물을 요구한다."]], [["A word more, and the attention of the reader (concentrated breathlessly on myself) shall be released.", "한마디만 더 하면, 숨죽인 채 나에게 집중되어 있는 독자의 주의를 놓아주겠다."]], [["My own mental insight informs me that three inevitable questions will be asked here by persons of inquiring minds.", "나 자신의 정신적 통찰이 일러 주는 바, 탐구심 있는 이들은 여기서 필연적인 세 가지 질문을 던질 것이다."], ["They shall be stated—they shall be answered.", "그 질문들을 내가 제시하겠다—그리고 내가 답하겠다."]], [["First question.", "첫 번째 질문."], ["What is the secret of Madame Fosco’s unhesitating devotion of herself to the fulfilment of my boldest wishes, to the furtherance of my deepest plans?", "포스코 백작부인이 내 가장 대담한 소망의 성취와 내 가장 깊은 계획의 추진에 스스로를 주저 없이 바친 비밀은 무엇인가? 나는 그저 내 성품을 가리키는 것만으로 이에 답할 수도 있으리라."], ["I might answer this by simply referring to my own character, and by asking, in my turn, Where, in the history of the world, has a man of my order ever been found without a woman in the background self-immolated on the altar of his life?", "그리고 이번에는 내 쪽에서 되물을 수도 있으리라. 세계의 역사를 통틀어, 나 같은 부류의 사내에게 그 배후에서 그의 생애의 제단 위에 스스로를 제물로 바친 여인이 없었던 예가 대체 어디 있더냐고."], ["But I remember that I am writing in England, I remember that I was married in England, and I ask if a woman’s marriage obligations in this country provide for her private opinion of her husband’s principles?", "그러나 나는 내가 영국에서 이 글을 쓰고 있음을 기억하고, 내가 영국에서 혼인하였음을 기억한다. 그리하여 나는 묻는다."], ["No! They charge her unreservedly to love, honour, and obey him.", "이 나라에서 아내의 혼인 의무가 남편의 신조에 대한 그녀 개인의 견해라는 것까지 마련해 주던가?"], ["That is exactly what my wife has done.", "아니다! 그 의무는 아내에게 아무 유보 없이 남편을 사랑하고 존경하고 순종하라 명한다."], ["I stand here on a supreme moral elevation, and I loftily assert her accurate performance of her conjugal duties.", "내 아내가 행한 것이 바로 그것이다. 나는 지고한 도덕적 높이에 서서, 그녀가 아내의 본분을 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 수행하였음을 드높이 단언하는 바이다."], ["Silence, Calumny!", "침묵하라, 중상이여!"], ["Your sympathy, Wives of England, for Madame Fosco!", "잉글랜드의 아내들이여, 포스코 백작부인에게 그대들의 공감을!"]], [["Second question.", "두 번째 질문."], ["If Anne Catherick had not died when she did, what should I have done?", "만일 앤 캐서릭이 그때 죽지 않았더라면 나는 어찌하였겠는가?"], ["I should, in that case, have assisted worn-out Nature in finding permanent repose.", "그 경우 나는 기진한 자연이 영구한 안식을 찾도록 거들었을 것이다."], ["I should have opened the doors of the Prison of Life, and have extended to the captive (incurably afflicted in mind and body both) a happy release.", "나는 생명이라는 감옥의 문을 열어, 그 수인에게—정신도 육체도 아울러 불치의 병에 시달리던 그 수인에게—행복한 해방을 베풀었을 것이다."]], [["Third question.", "세 번째 질문."], ["On a calm revision of all the circumstances—Is my conduct worthy of any serious blame?", "모든 정황을 차분히 되짚어 볼 때—내 행위는 진지한 비난을 받을 만한가?"], ["Most emphatically, No!", "참으로 단호하게, 아니다!"], ["Have I not carefully avoided exposing myself to the odium of committing unnecessary crime?", "나는 불필요한 범죄를 저질러 스스로를 증오의 표적으로 내놓는 일을 조심스레 피하지 않았던가?"], ["With my vast resources in chemistry, I might have taken Lady Glyde’s life.", "화학에 관한 나의 방대한 자산을 쓰자면 나는 글라이드 부인의 목숨을 앗을 수도 있었다."], ["At immense personal sacrifice I followed the dictates of my own ingenuity, my own humanity, my own caution, and took her identity instead.", "나는 막대한 개인적 희생을 치르면서 내 자신의 독창성이, 내 자신의 인간애가, 내 자신의 신중함이 명하는 바를 따랐고, 목숨 대신 그녀의 신분을 취하였다."], ["Judge me by what I might have done. How comparatively innocent!", "내가 할 수도 있었던 일에 견주어 나를 판단하라."], ["how indirectly virtuous I appear in what I really did!", "내가 실제로 행한 바는 얼마나 비교적 결백한가! 얼마나 간접적으로 유덕해 보이는가!"]], [["I announced on beginning it that this narrative would be a remarkable document.", "이 글을 시작하며 나는 이 수기가 주목할 만한 문서가 되리라고 공언하였다."], ["It has entirely answered my expectations.", "그것은 내 기대에 온전히 부응하였다."], ["Receive these fervid lines—my last legacy to the country I leave for ever.", "이 뜨거운 글줄들을 받으라—내가 영원히 떠나는 나라에 남기는 나의 마지막 유산이다."], ["They are worthy of the occasion, and worthy of FOSCO.", "이 글줄들은 이 자리에 값하며, 또한 다음 이름에 값한다. 포스코."]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["When I closed the last leaf of the Count’s manuscript the half-hour during which I had engaged to remain at Forest Road had expired.", "백작의 원고 마지막 장을 덮었을 때, 내가 포리스트 로드에 머물기로 약속했던 삼십 분은 이미 다 지나 있었다."], ["Monsieur Rubelle looked at his watch and bowed.", "루벨 씨는 시계를 들여다보고 고개를 숙였다."], ["I rose immediately, and left the agent in possession of the empty house.", "나는 곧바로 일어나, 그 빈집을 대리인의 손에 맡긴 채 그곳을 떠났다."], ["I never saw him again—I never heard more of him or of his wife.", "나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했다—그에 대해서도, 그의 아내에 대해서도 더는 아무 소식을 듣지 못했다."], ["Out of the dark byways of villainy and deceit they had crawled across our path—into the same byways they crawled back secretly and were lost.", "악행과 기만의 어두운 뒷골목에서 기어 나와 우리 앞길을 가로질렀던 그들은, 바로 그 뒷골목 속으로 남몰래 도로 기어들어가 자취를 감추었다."]], [["In a quarter of an hour after leaving Forest Road I was at home again.", "포리스트 로드를 떠난 지 십오 분 만에 나는 다시 집에 돌아와 있었다."]], [["But few words sufficed to tell Laura and Marian how my desperate venture had ended, and what the next event in our lives was likely to be.", "나의 필사적인 모험이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그리고 우리 삶에 다음으로 닥칠 일이 무엇일지를 로라와 매리언에게 알리는 데에는 몇 마디면 충분했다."], ["I left all details to be described later in the day, and hastened back to St.", "자세한 이야기는 그날 늦게 들려주기로 미뤄 두고, 나는 서둘러 세인트존스우드로 되돌아갔다."], ["John’s Wood, to see the person of whom Count Fosco had ordered the fly, when he went to meet Laura at the station.", "포스코 백작이 로라를 맞으러 역으로 나갈 때 삯마차를 주문했던 그 사람을 만나기 위해서였다."]], [["The address in my possession led me to some “livery stables,” about a quarter of a mile distant from Forest Road.", "내가 지니고 있던 주소는 포리스트 로드에서 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 “대여 마구간”으로 나를 이끌었다."], ["The proprietor proved to be a civil and respectable man.", "주인은 예의 바르고 점잖은 사람이었다."], ["When I explained that an important family matter obliged me to ask him to refer to his books for the purpose of ascertaining a date with which the record of his business transactions might supply me, he offered no objection to granting my request.", "중요한 집안일 때문에 부득이 장부를 들춰 보아야겠다고, 그 영업 기록에서 확인하고 싶은 날짜가 하나 있다고 설명하자, 그는 내 청을 들어주는 데 아무런 이의도 달지 않았다."], ["The book was produced, and there, under the date of “July 26th, 1850,” the order was entered in these words—", "장부를 가져오게 했더니, “1850년 7월 26일”이라는 날짜 아래 그 주문이 이런 말로 적혀 있었다—"]], [["“Brougham to Count Fosco, 5 Forest Road.", "“포스코 백작, 포리스트 로드 5번지로 브로엄 마차."], ["Two o’clock.", "두 시."], ["(John Owen).”", "(존 오언).”"]], [["I found on inquiry that the name of “John Owen,” attached to the entry, referred to the man who had been employed to drive the fly.", "물어보니 그 항목에 붙은 “존 오언”이라는 이름은 그 삯마차를 몰았던 사람을 가리키는 것이었다."], ["He was then at work in the stable-yard, and was sent for to see me at my request.", "그는 마침 마구간 뜰에서 일하고 있었고, 내 청에 따라 나를 만나러 불려 왔다."]], [["“Do you remember driving a gentleman, in the month of July last, from Number Five Forest Road to the Waterloo Bridge station?”", "“작년 7월에 어떤 신사분을 포리스트 로드 5번지에서 워털루 브리지 역까지 태워다 드린 일을 기억하십니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Well, sir,”", "“글쎄올시다, 나리,”"], ["said the man, “I can’t exactly say I do.”", "사내가 말했다. “딱히 기억이 난다고는 못 하겠는뎁쇼.”"]], [["“Perhaps you remember the gentleman himself?", "“그러면 그 신사분 자체는 기억나실지도 모르겠군요."], ["Can you call to mind driving a foreigner last summer—a tall gentleman and remarkably fat?”", "지난여름에 외국인 한 분을 태우신 일을 떠올려 보실 수 있겠습니까—키가 크고 유난히 뚱뚱한 신사분 말입니다.”"]], [["The man’s face brightened directly.", "사내의 얼굴이 대번에 환해졌다."], ["“I remember him, sir!", "“그분이라면 기억납니다요, 나리!"], ["The fattest gentleman as ever I see, and the heaviest customer as ever I drove.", "제가 여태껏 본 중에 제일 뚱뚱한 신사분이었고, 여태껏 태워 본 중에 제일 무거운 손님이었습죠."], ["Yes, yes—I call him to mind, sir!", "그럼요, 그럼요—그분이라면 떠오릅니다, 나리!"], ["We DID go to the station, and it WAS from Forest Road.", "우리가 역까지 간 게 맞고, 떠난 데가 포리스트 로드가 맞습니다."], ["There was a parrot, or summat like it, screeching in the window.", "창가에서 앵무새인지 뭔지가 꽥꽥거리고 있었습죠."], ["The gentleman was in a mortal hurry about the lady’s luggage, and he gave me a handsome present for looking sharp and getting the boxes.”", "그 신사분은 부인의 짐 때문에 죽을 듯이 서두르셨고, 제가 눈치 빠르게 굴어 상자들을 챙겨 드렸더니 후하게 사례를 주셨습니다요.”"]], [["Getting the boxes!", "상자들을 챙겨 주었다니!"], ["I recollected immediately that Laura’s own account of herself on her arrival in London described her luggage as being collected for her by some person whom Count Fosco brought with him to the station.", "나는 곧바로 떠올렸다. 로라가 런던에 도착했을 때의 일을 스스로 이야기하면서, 자기 짐은 포스코 백작이 역에 데리고 온 어떤 사람이 대신 챙겨 주었다고 했던 것을."], ["This was the man.", "바로 이 사내였다."]], [["“Did you see the lady?”", "“그 부인은 보셨습니까?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["“What did she look like?", "“어떻게 생긴 분이었지요?"], ["Was she young or old?”", "젊었습니까, 나이가 들었습니까?”"]], [["“Well, sir, what with the hurry and the crowd of people pushing about, I can’t rightly say what the lady looked like.", "“글쎄올시다, 나리, 서두르는 통에다 사람들이 밀치락달치락하는 통에, 그 부인이 어떻게 생겼는지는 제대로 말씀드리기가 어렵습죠."], ["I can’t call nothing to mind about her that I know of excepting her name.”", "이름 말고는 그분에 대해 떠오르는 게 아무것도 없습니다요.”"]], [["“You remember her name?”", "“이름은 기억하신다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["“Yes, sir.", "“예, 나리."], ["Her name was Lady Glyde.”", "그 부인 이름은 글라이드 부인이었습니다.”"]], [["“How do you come to remember that, when you have forgotten what she looked like?”", "“어떻게 생겼는지는 잊어버리고서, 어떻게 이름은 기억하십니까?”"]], [["The man smiled, and shifted his feet in some little embarrassment.", "사내는 웃으면서, 조금 겸연쩍은 듯 발을 옮겨 디뎠다."]], [["“Why, to tell you the truth, sir,”", "“그게, 사실대로 말씀드리자면 말입니다, 나리,”"], ["he said, “I hadn’t been long married at that time, and my wife’s name, before she changed it for mine, was the same as the lady’s—meaning the name of Glyde, sir.", "그가 말했다. “그때 제가 결혼한 지 얼마 안 됐었는데, 제 아내가 제 성으로 바꾸기 전 이름이 그 부인 이름하고 똑같았습죠—그러니까 글라이드라는 이름 말입니다요, 나리."], ["The lady mentioned it herself.", "그 부인이 직접 그 이름을 말씀하셨습니다."], ["‘Is your name on your boxes, ma’am?’ says I.", "‘상자에 성함이 적혀 있습니까, 마님?’ 제가 그랬죠."], ["‘Yes,’ says she, ‘my name is on my luggage—it is Lady Glyde.’ ‘Come!’ I says to myself, ‘I’ve a bad head for gentlefolks’ names in general—but THIS one comes like an old friend, at any rate.’ I can’t say nothing about the time, sir, it might be nigh on a year ago, or it mightn’t.", "‘그래요,’ 하시더군요, ‘내 이름이 짐에 적혀 있어요—글라이드 부인이에요.’ ‘옳거니!’ 제가 속으로 그랬습죠, ‘내가 대체로 지체 높은 분들 성함에는 머리가 나쁜 편이다만—이 이름만은 어쨌든 옛 친구처럼 반갑게 다가오는구나.’ 시기는 뭐라 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다, 나리. 한 해쯤 됐을 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있고요."], ["But I can swear to the stout gentleman, and swear to the lady’s name.”", "하지만 그 뚱뚱한 신사분에 대해서는 맹세할 수 있고, 그 부인 이름에 대해서도 맹세할 수 있습니다요.”"]], [["There was no need that he should remember the time—the date was positively established by his master’s order-book.", "그가 시기까지 기억할 필요는 없었다—날짜는 그의 주인이 지닌 주문 장부로 확실하게 입증되어 있었으니까."], ["I felt at once that the means were now in my power of striking down the whole conspiracy at a blow with the irresistible weapon of plain fact.", "나는 명백한 사실이라는 저항할 수 없는 무기로 음모 전체를 단칼에 쳐서 무너뜨릴 수단이 이제 내 손안에 들어왔음을 단번에 느꼈다."], ["Without a moment’s hesitation, I took the proprietor of the livery stables aside and told him what the real importance was of the evidence of his order-book and the evidence of his driver.", "나는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 대여 마구간 주인을 한쪽으로 데려가, 그의 주문 장부가 지닌 증거와 그의 마부가 지닌 증언이 실제로 얼마나 중대한 것인지를 이야기했다."], ["An arrangement to compensate him for the temporary loss of the man’s services was easily made, and a copy of the entry in the book was taken by myself, and certified as true by the master’s own signature.", "그 사람의 일손을 한동안 잃게 되는 데 대한 보상 문제는 쉽게 합의되었고, 장부의 해당 항목은 내가 직접 베껴 적은 뒤 주인의 친필 서명으로 사실임을 증명받았다."], ["I left the livery stables, having settled that John Owen was to hold himself at my disposal for the next three days, or for a longer period if necessity required it.", "나는 존 오언이 앞으로 사흘 동안, 필요하다면 그보다 더 오래 내 뜻에 따라 대기하기로 정해 놓고 대여 마구간을 나섰다."]], [["I now had in my possession all the papers that I wanted—the district registrar’s own copy of the certificate of death, and Sir Percival’s dated letter to the Count, being safe in my pocket-book.", "이제 내가 원하던 서류는 모두 내 손안에 있었다—관할 구역 등록관이 직접 떼어 준 사망 증명서 사본과, 날짜가 적힌 퍼시벌 경의 편지가 내 수첩 속에 안전하게 들어 있었다."]], [["With this written evidence about me, and with the coachman’s answers fresh in my memory, I next turned my steps, for the first time since the beginning of all my inquiries, in the direction of Mr. Kyrle’s office.", "이 문서 증거를 몸에 지니고, 마부의 대답을 기억 속에 생생히 담은 채, 나는 온갖 조사를 시작한 이래 처음으로 카일 씨의 사무실 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["One of my objects in paying him this second visit was, necessarily, to tell him what I had done.", "그를 두 번째로 찾아가는 목적 하나는 당연히 내가 해낸 일을 알리는 것이었다."], ["The other was to warn him of my resolution to take my wife to Limmeridge the next morning, and to have her publicly received and recognised in her uncle’s house. I left it to Mr.", "다른 하나는, 내일 아침 아내를 리머리지로 데려가 그녀의 삼촌 집에서 공개적으로 맞아들여지고 그 신분을 인정받게 하겠다는 나의 결심을 미리 알려 두는 것이었다."], ["Kyrle to decide under these circumstances, and in Mr. Gilmore’s absence, whether he was or was not bound, as the family solicitor, to be present on that occasion in the family interests.", "이런 상황에서, 그리고 길모어 씨가 자리를 비운 터에, 집안의 사무 변호사로서 집안의 이익을 위해 그 자리에 참석할 의무가 있는지 없는지는 카일 씨의 판단에 맡겼다."]], [["I will say nothing of Mr. Kyrle’s amazement, or of the terms in which he expressed his opinion of my conduct from the first stage of the investigation to the last.", "카일 씨가 얼마나 놀랐는지, 또 조사의 첫 단계부터 마지막까지 내가 한 처신에 대해 그가 어떤 말로 자기 의견을 표현했는지는 아무것도 적지 않겠다."], ["It is only necessary to mention that he at once decided on accompanying us to Cumberland.", "다만 그가 그 자리에서 곧바로 우리와 함께 컴벌랜드로 가겠다고 결정했다는 것만 밝혀 두면 충분하겠다."]], [["We started the next morning by the early train. Laura, Marian, Mr.", "우리는 이튿날 아침 이른 기차로 출발했다."], ["Kyrle, and myself in one carriage, and John Owen, with a clerk from Mr. Kyrle’s office, occupying places in another.", "로라와 매리언과 카일 씨와 나는 한 객실에 탔고, 존 오언은 카일 씨 사무실의 서기 한 사람과 함께 다른 객실에 자리를 잡았다."], ["On reaching the Limmeridge station we went first to the farmhouse at Todd’s Corner.", "리머리지 역에 닿자 우리는 먼저 토드 코너의 농가로 갔다."], ["It was my firm determination that Laura should not enter her uncle’s house till she appeared there publicly recognised as his niece. I left Marian to settle the question of accommodation with Mrs.", "로라가 삼촌의 조카딸로 공개적으로 인정받은 모습으로 그곳에 나타나기 전에는 그 집에 발을 들이지 않게 하겠다는 것이 나의 확고한 결심이었다."], ["Todd, as soon as the good woman had recovered from the bewilderment of hearing what our errand was in Cumberland, and I arranged with her husband that John Owen was to be committed to the ready hospitality of the farm-servants.", "나는 착한 토드 부인이 우리가 컴벌랜드에 온 용건을 듣고 받은 어리둥절함에서 정신을 차리자마자 매리언에게 그녀와 숙소 문제를 정하도록 맡기고, 그녀의 남편과는 존 오언을 농장 일꾼들의 선선한 대접에 맡기기로 이야기해 두었다."], ["These preliminaries completed, Mr. Kyrle and I set forth together for Limmeridge House.", "이런 준비를 마치고 나서 카일 씨와 나는 함께 리머리지 하우스로 향했다."]], [["I cannot write at any length of our interview with Mr.", "페얼리 씨와의 면담에 대해서는 길게 쓸 수가 없다."], ["Fairlie, for I cannot recall it to mind without feelings of impatience and contempt, which make the scene, even in remembrance only, utterly repulsive to me.", "그 일은 떠올리기만 해도 조바심과 경멸의 감정이 함께 치밀어 올라, 그저 기억 속에서일 뿐인데도 그 장면이 도무지 견딜 수 없이 역겨워지기 때문이다."], ["I prefer to record simply that I carried my point.", "나는 그저 내 뜻을 관철했다고만 적어 두고 싶다."], ["Mr. Fairlie attempted to treat us on his customary plan.", "페얼리 씨는 늘 하던 방식대로 우리를 상대하려 들었다."], ["We passed without notice his polite insolence at the outset of the interview.", "면담 첫머리의 정중한 무례는 우리가 못 들은 척 넘겼다."], ["We heard without sympathy the protestations with which he tried next to persuade us that the disclosure of the conspiracy had overwhelmed him.", "이어서 그가 음모의 폭로에 자기가 압도당했노라고 우리를 설득하려 늘어놓은 항변들도 우리는 아무 동정 없이 들어 넘겼다."], ["He absolutely whined and whimpered at last like a fretful child.", "마침내 그는 영락없이 보채는 아이처럼 칭얼거리고 훌쩍거렸다."], ["“How was he to know that his niece was alive when he was told that she was dead?", "“조카가 죽었다는 말을 들었는데 살아 있는 줄을 그가 어떻게 알았겠는가?"], ["He would welcome dear Laura with pleasure, if we would only allow him time to recover.", "회복할 시간만 좀 준다면 사랑스러운 로라를 기꺼이 맞아들이겠다."], ["Did we think he looked as if he wanted hurrying into his grave? No.", "자기가 무덤 속으로 서둘러 떠밀려 들어가고 싶어 하는 사람처럼 보이는가?"], ["Then, why hurry him?”", "아니잖은가."], ["He reiterated these remonstrances at every available opportunity, until I checked them once for all, by placing him firmly between two inevitable alternatives.", "그런데 어째서 사람을 재촉하는가?” 그는 틈만 나면 이런 하소연을 되풀이했고, 나는 결국 그를 피할 수 없는 두 가지 선택지 사이에 단단히 세워 놓음으로써 그것을 단번에 끝내 버렸다."], ["I gave him his choice between doing his niece justice on my terms, or facing the consequence of a public assertion of her existence in a court of law.", "나는 그에게 내가 제시한 조건대로 조카에게 정당한 대우를 해 주든지, 아니면 법정에서 그녀의 생존을 공개적으로 주장했을 때 따라올 결과를 감당하든지 택하라고 했다."], ["Mr. Kyrle, to whom he turned for help, told him plainly that he must decide the question then and there.", "그가 도움을 청하며 돌아본 카일 씨는, 지금 이 자리에서 그 문제를 결정하셔야 한다고 딱 잘라 말했다."], ["Characteristically choosing the alternative which promised soonest to release him from all personal anxiety, he announced with a sudden outburst of energy, that he was not strong enough to bear any more bullying, and that we might do as we pleased.", "그는 자기 개인의 근심에서 가장 빨리 벗어나게 해 줄 쪽을 고르는, 그다운 선택을 하고는, 느닷없이 기운을 짜내어 자기는 더 이상 이런 윽박지름을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 않으니 우리 마음대로 하라고 선언했다."]], [["Mr. Kyrle and I at once went downstairs, and agreed upon a form of letter which was to be sent round to the tenants who had attended the false funeral, summoning them, in Mr.", "카일 씨와 나는 곧바로 아래층으로 내려가, 가짜 장례식에 참석했던 소작인들에게 돌릴 편지의 서식을 함께 정했다."], ["Fairlie’s name, to assemble in Limmeridge House on the next day but one. An order referring to the same date was also written, directing a statuary in Carlisle to send a man to Limmeridge churchyard for the purpose of erasing an inscription—Mr.", "페얼리 씨의 이름으로, 모레 리머리지 하우스에 모여 달라고 부르는 내용이었다."], ["Kyrle, who had arranged to sleep in the house, undertaking that Mr. Fairlie should hear these letters read to him, and should sign them with his own hand.", "같은 날짜를 가리키는 지시서도 한 통 작성해, 칼라일의 석공에게 사람을 하나 리머리지 교회 묘지로 보내 비문 하나를 지워 달라고 요청했다—그 집에서 자기로 되어 있던 카일 씨는, 페얼리 씨가 이 편지들을 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 자기 손으로 직접 서명하게끔 하는 일을 맡았다."]], [["I occupied the interval day at the farm in writing a plain narrative of the conspiracy, and in adding to it a statement of the practical contradiction which facts offered to the assertion of Laura’s death.", "그 사이의 하루를 나는 농가에서 음모의 경위를 담백하게 적은 이야기를 쓰고, 거기에 로라가 죽었다는 주장에 사실들이 실제로 어떻게 어긋나는지를 정리한 진술을 덧붙이는 데 썼다."], ["This I submitted to Mr. Kyrle before I read it the next day to the assembled tenants.", "나는 이것을 이튿날 모인 소작인들 앞에서 읽기 전에 먼저 카일 씨에게 보였다."], ["We also arranged the form in which the evidence should be presented at the close of the reading.", "우리는 낭독이 끝날 때 증거를 어떤 형식으로 제시할지도 함께 정했다."], ["After these matters were settled, Mr. Kyrle endeavoured to turn the conversation next to Laura’s affairs.", "이런 문제들이 정리되자 카일 씨는 이번에는 화제를 로라의 재산 문제로 돌리려 했다."], ["Knowing, and desiring to know nothing of those affairs, and doubting whether he would approve, as a man of business, of my conduct in relation to my wife’s life-interest in the legacy left to Madame Fosco, I begged Mr. Kyrle to excuse me if I abstained from discussing the subject.", "나는 그 문제에 대해 아는 바가 없었고 알고 싶지도 않았으며, 포스코 백작부인에게 남겨진 유산에 대한 아내의 종신 수익권과 관련해 내가 취한 처신을 사업하는 사람의 눈으로 그가 좋게 볼지 어떨지도 미심쩍었으므로, 그 이야기를 삼가더라도 양해해 달라고 카일 씨에게 청했다."], ["It was connected, as I could truly tell him, with those sorrows and troubles of the past which we never referred to among ourselves, and which we instinctively shrank from discussing with others.", "그 문제는, 내가 그에게 사실대로 말할 수 있었듯이, 우리끼리도 결코 입에 올리지 않고 남에게 이야기하기는 본능적으로 꺼리게 되는 지난날의 슬픔과 고통에 얽혀 있었다."]], [["My last labour, as the evening approached, was to obtain “The Narrative of the Tombstone,” by taking a copy of the false inscription on the grave before it was erased.", "저녁이 다가올 무렵 내가 한 마지막 일은, 무덤 위의 그 거짓 비문이 지워지기 전에 그것을 베껴 적어 “묘비의 이야기”를 얻어 두는 것이었다."]], [["The day came—the day when Laura once more entered the familiar breakfast-room at Limmeridge House.", "그날이 왔다—로라가 리머리지 하우스의 낯익은 아침 식사실에 다시 한번 들어서는 날이."], ["All the persons assembled rose from their seats as Marian and I led her in.", "매리언과 내가 그녀를 이끌고 들어서자 모여 있던 사람들이 모두 자리에서 일어났다."], ["A perceptible shock of surprise, an audible murmur of interest ran through them, at the sight of her face. Mr.", "그녀의 얼굴을 본 순간, 눈에 보이는 놀라움의 전율과 귀에 들리는 관심의 웅성거림이 그들 사이로 번져 갔다."], ["Fairlie was present (by my express stipulation), with Mr. Kyrle by his side.", "페얼리 씨도 (내가 분명히 못 박아 둔 대로) 그 자리에 있었고, 그 곁에는 카일 씨가 앉아 있었다."], ["His valet stood behind him with a smelling-bottle ready in one hand, and a white handkerchief, saturated with eau-de-Cologne, in the other.", "그의 시종은 한 손에는 각성제 병을, 다른 손에는 오드콜로뉴를 흠뻑 적신 하얀 손수건을 들고 그의 뒤에 서 있었다."]], [["I opened the proceedings by publicly appealing to Mr.", "나는 페얼리 씨에게, 내가 그의 권한과 명시적인 승낙 아래 이 자리에 나선 것인지를 공개적으로 밝혀 달라고 청하는 것으로 절차를 시작했다."], ["Fairlie to say whether I appeared there with his authority and under his express sanction. He extended an arm, on either side, to Mr.", "그는 양옆으로 한 팔씩을 뻗어 한쪽은 카일 씨에게, 한쪽은 시종에게 내밀었고—두 사람의 부축을 받아 제 다리로 일어서더니, 이렇게 말했다."], ["Kyrle and to his valet—was by them assisted to stand on his legs, and then expressed himself in these terms: “Allow me to present Mr.", "“하트라이트 씨를 소개하게 해 주십시오."], ["Hartright. I am as great an invalid as ever, and he is so very obliging as to speak for me.", "저는 여전히 더할 나위 없는 병자여서, 이분이 참으로 친절하게도 저를 대신해 말씀해 주십니다."], ["The subject is dreadfully embarrassing.", "이 문제는 끔찍하게 난처한 것입니다."], ["Please hear him, and don’t make a noise!”", "부디 이분의 말씀을 들어 주시고, 소란은 피우지 말아 주십시오!”"], ["With those words he slowly sank back again into the chair, and took refuge in his scented pocket-handkerchief.", "그 말을 하고 나서 그는 천천히 다시 의자 속으로 파묻히며, 향내 나는 손수건 속으로 피신했다."]], [["The disclosure of the conspiracy followed, after I had offered my preliminary explanation, first of all, in the fewest and the plainest words.", "내가 먼저 가장 짧고 가장 평이한 말로 예비 설명을 마친 뒤, 음모의 폭로가 이어졌다."], ["I was there present (I informed my hearers) to declare, first, that my wife, then sitting by me, was the daughter of the late Mr. Philip Fairlie; secondly, to prove by positive facts, that the funeral which they had attended in Limmeridge churchyard was the funeral of another woman; thirdly, to give them a plain account of how it had all happened.", "나는 (청중에게 이렇게 알렸다) 첫째로 지금 내 곁에 앉아 있는 내 아내가 고 필립 페얼리 씨의 딸임을 선언하기 위해, 둘째로 그들이 리머리지 교회 묘지에서 참석했던 그 장례식이 다른 여자의 장례식이었음을 확실한 사실들로 입증하기 위해, 셋째로 이 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지를 그들에게 담백하게 설명하기 위해 이 자리에 나왔노라고 했다."], ["Without further preface, I at once read the narrative of the conspiracy, describing it in clear outline, and dwelling only upon the pecuniary motive for it, in order to avoid complicating my statement by unnecessary reference to Sir Percival’s secret.", "더 이상의 서두 없이 나는 곧바로 음모의 경위를 적은 글을 읽어, 그 윤곽을 또렷하게 그려 보이되 퍼시벌 경의 비밀을 불필요하게 끌어들여 내 진술을 복잡하게 만들지 않으려고 금전적 동기에만 초점을 맞추었다."], ["This done, I reminded my audience of the date on the inscription in the churchyard (the 25th), and confirmed its correctness by producing the certificate of death.", "그것이 끝나자 나는 청중에게 교회 묘지 비문에 새겨진 날짜(25일)를 상기시키고, 사망 증명서를 내보여 그 날짜가 옳음을 확인해 주었다."], ["I then read them Sir Percival’s letter of the 25th, announcing his wife’s intended journey from Hampshire to London on the 26th.", "이어서 나는 25일자로 된 퍼시벌 경의 편지를 읽어 주었다. 그의 아내가 26일에 햄프셔에서 런던으로 떠날 예정이라고 알린 편지였다."], ["I next showed that she had taken that journey, by the personal testimony of the driver of the fly, and I proved that she had performed it on the appointed day, by the order-book at the livery stables.", "다음으로 나는 삯마차 마부의 직접 증언으로 그녀가 실제로 그 여행을 했음을 보였고, 대여 마구간의 주문 장부로 그녀가 그 정해진 날에 그 여행을 했음을 입증했다."], ["Marian then added her own statement of the meeting between Laura and herself at the mad-house, and of her sister’s escape.", "그러자 매리언이 정신병원에서 로라와 만난 일과 동생이 그곳을 빠져나온 일에 대한 자기 진술을 덧붙였다."], ["After which I closed the proceedings by informing the persons present of Sir Percival’s death and of my marriage.", "그런 다음 나는 퍼시벌 경의 죽음과 나의 결혼을 그 자리에 모인 사람들에게 알리는 것으로 절차를 마쳤다."]], [["Mr. Kyrle rose when I resumed my seat, and declared, as the legal adviser of the family, that my case was proved by the plainest evidence he had ever heard in his life.", "내가 다시 자리에 앉자 카일 씨가 일어나, 집안의 법률 고문으로서, 내 주장은 자기가 평생 들어 본 가장 명백한 증거로 입증되었다고 선언했다."], ["As he spoke those words, I put my arm round Laura, and raised her so that she was plainly visible to every one in the room.", "그가 그 말을 하는 동안 나는 로라를 팔로 감싸 일으켜 세워, 방 안의 모든 사람에게 또렷이 보이도록 했다."], ["“Are you all of the same opinion?”", "“여러분도 모두 같은 생각이십니까?”"], ["I asked, advancing towards them a few steps, and pointing to my wife.", "나는 그들 쪽으로 몇 걸음 나아가 아내를 가리키며 물었다."]], [["The effect of the question was electrical.", "그 물음이 일으킨 효과는 전기와도 같았다."], ["Far down at the lower end of the room one of the oldest tenants on the estate started to his feet, and led the rest with him in an instant.", "방의 저 아래쪽 끝에서 이 영지의 가장 나이 많은 소작인 가운데 한 사람이 벌떡 일어나더니, 순식간에 나머지 사람들을 이끌었다."], ["I see the man now, with his honest brown face and his iron-grey hair, mounted on the window-seat, waving his heavy riding-whip over his head, and leading the cheers.", "나는 지금도 그 사람이 눈에 선하다. 정직해 보이는 볕에 그은 얼굴과 희끗한 잿빛 머리를 하고서 창가 자리에 올라서서, 묵직한 승마 채찍을 머리 위로 휘두르며 환호를 이끌던 그 모습이."], ["“There she is, alive and hearty—God bless her!", "“저기 아씨가 살아서 정정하게 계신다—하느님의 축복이 있기를!"], ["Gi’ it tongue, lads!", "목청껏 질러라, 얘들아!"], ["Gi’ it tongue!”", "목청껏 질러!”"], ["The shout that answered him, reiterated again and again, was the sweetest music I ever heard.", "그에게 화답해 몇 번이고 거듭 터져 나온 그 함성은 내가 평생 들어 본 가장 아름다운 음악이었다."], ["The labourers in the village and the boys from the school, assembled on the lawn, caught up the cheering and echoed it back on us.", "잔디밭에 모여 있던 마을의 일꾼들과 학교 아이들이 그 환호를 받아 우리에게 되울려 주었다."], ["The farmers’ wives clustered round Laura, and struggled which should be first to shake hands with her, and to implore her, with the tears pouring over their own cheeks, to bear up bravely and not to cry.", "농부의 아내들은 로라를 에워싸고 서로 먼저 그녀와 악수하려고, 제 볼 위로 눈물을 쏟으면서 부디 씩씩하게 견디고 울지 말라고 애원하려고 다투었다."], ["She was so completely overwhelmed, that I was obliged to take her from them, and carry her to the door.", "그녀가 너무나 벅차 어쩔 줄을 몰라 해서, 나는 그녀를 그들에게서 떼어 내 문간까지 안고 가야 했다."], ["There I gave her into Marian’s care—Marian, who had never failed us yet, whose courageous self-control did not fail us now.", "거기서 나는 그녀를 매리언에게 맡겼다—여태 한 번도 우리를 저버린 적 없는 매리언, 그 용감한 자제력이 이번에도 우리를 저버리지 않은 매리언에게."], ["Left by myself at the door, I invited all the persons present (after thanking them in Laura’s name and mine) to follow me to the churchyard, and see the false inscription struck off the tombstone with their own eyes.", "문간에 혼자 남은 나는 (로라와 나의 이름으로 감사를 전한 뒤) 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람에게, 나를 따라 교회 묘지로 가서 저 거짓 비문이 묘비에서 깎여 나가는 것을 제 눈으로 보아 달라고 청했다."]], [["They all left the house, and all joined the throng of villagers collected round the grave, where the statuary’s man was waiting for us.", "그들은 모두 집을 나섰고, 무덤 둘레에 모여 있던 마을 사람들의 무리에 모두 합류했다. 그곳에서는 석공의 일꾼이 우리를 기다리고 있었다."], ["In a breathless silence, the first sharp stroke of the steel sounded on the marble.", "숨을 죽인 정적 속에서 강철 정의 첫 날카로운 타격이 대리석 위에 울렸다."], ["Not a voice was heard—not a soul moved, till those three words, “Laura, Lady Glyde,” had vanished from sight.", "“로라, 글라이드 부인”이라는 그 세 마디가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 아무 소리도 들리지 않았고—아무도 움직이지 않았다."], ["Then there was a great heave of relief among the crowd, as if they felt that the last fetters of the conspiracy had been struck off Laura herself, and the assembly slowly withdrew.", "그러고 나자 마치 음모의 마지막 족쇄가 로라 자신에게서 떨어져 나간 것을 느끼기라도 한 듯 군중 사이에서 커다란 안도의 물결이 일었고, 모였던 사람들은 천천히 흩어졌다."], ["It was late in the day before the whole inscription was erased.", "비문 전체가 다 지워진 것은 그날 늦은 시각이었다."], ["One line only was afterwards engraved in its place: “Anne Catherick, July 25th, 1850.”", "그 자리에는 나중에 단 한 줄만이 새겨졌다. “앤 캐서릭, 1850년 7월 25일.”"]], [["I returned to Limmeridge House early enough in the evening to take leave of Mr. Kyrle.", "나는 카일 씨에게 작별 인사를 하려고 저녁 이른 시각에 리머리지 하우스로 돌아갔다."], ["He and his clerk, and the driver of the fly, went back to London by the night train. On their departure an insolent message was delivered to me from Mr.", "그와 그의 서기, 그리고 삯마차 마부는 밤 기차로 런던에 돌아갔다."], ["Fairlie—who had been carried from the room in a shattered condition, when the first outbreak of cheering answered my appeal to the tenantry. The message conveyed to us “Mr.", "그들이 떠나자 페얼리 씨에게서 무례한 전갈이 하나 내게 전해졌다—소작인들에게 던진 나의 물음에 환호가 처음 터져 나왔을 때 넋이 나간 몰골로 방에서 실려 나갔던 그 사람에게서 말이다."], ["Fairlie’s best congratulations,” and requested to know whether “we contemplated stopping in the house.”", "전갈은 우리에게 “페얼리 씨의 진심 어린 축하”를 전하면서, “우리가 이 집에 머물 생각인지”를 알고 싶다고 했다."], ["I sent back word that the only object for which we had entered his doors was accomplished—that I contemplated stopping in no man’s house but my own—and that Mr. Fairlie need not entertain the slightest apprehension of ever seeing us or hearing from us again.", "나는 우리가 그 집 문을 들어선 유일한 목적은 이미 이루어졌다고, 나는 내 집이 아닌 어느 누구의 집에도 머물 생각이 없다고, 그리고 페얼리 씨는 우리를 다시 보거나 우리에게서 소식을 듣게 될 걱정은 손톱만큼도 하실 필요가 없다고 답을 보냈다."], ["We went back to our friends at the farm to rest that night, and the next morning—escorted to the station, with the heartiest enthusiasm and good will, by the whole village and by all the farmers in the neighbourhood—we returned to London.", "우리는 그날 밤 농장의 벗들에게 돌아가 쉬었고, 이튿날 아침—온 마을 사람들과 이웃의 농부들 모두가 더없이 뜨거운 열의와 호의로 역까지 배웅해 주는 가운데—런던으로 돌아왔다."]], [["As our view of the Cumberland hills faded in the distance, I thought of the first disheartening circumstances under which the long struggle that was now past and over had been pursued.", "컴벌랜드의 언덕들이 멀리 시야에서 흐려져 갈 때, 나는 이제 다 지나가 버린 그 긴 싸움을 시작할 무렵의, 낙담스럽기만 하던 처지를 떠올렸다. 이제 와 돌이켜보면 참으로 이상한 일이었다."], ["It was strange to look back and to see, now, that the poverty which had denied us all hope of assistance had been the indirect means of our success, by forcing me to act for myself.", "우리에게서 남의 도움을 바랄 여지를 모두 앗아 갔던 그 가난이, 나로 하여금 스스로 나서서 움직이게 만듦으로써 도리어 우리 성공의 간접적인 수단이 되었다는 것이."], ["If we had been rich enough to find legal help, what would have been the result?", "우리가 법률적인 도움을 살 만큼 부유했더라면 결과가 어떠했을까?"], ["The gain (on Mr. Kyrle’s own showing) would have been more than doubtful—the loss, judging by the plain test of events as they had really happened, certain.", "(카일 씨 자신의 설명대로라면) 얻는 것은 극히 미심쩍었을 테고—실제로 일어난 사건들이라는 명백한 시험대에 비추어 판단하건대, 잃는 것은 확실했을 것이다."], ["The law would never have obtained me my interview with Mrs. Catherick.", "법은 결코 내게 캐서릭 부인과의 면담을 얻어다 주지 못했을 것이다."], ["The law would never have made Pesca the means of forcing a confession from the Count.", "법은 결코 페스카를 백작에게서 자백을 받아 내는 수단으로 만들어 주지 못했을 것이다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["Two more events remain to be added to the chain before it reaches fairly from the outset of the story to the close.", "이 사슬이 이야기의 첫머리에서 끝까지 온전히 이어지려면, 아직 두 가지 사건을 더 이어 붙여야 한다."]], [["While our new sense of freedom from the long oppression of the past was still strange to us, I was sent for by the friend who had given me my first employment in wood engraving, to receive from him a fresh testimony of his regard for my welfare.", "오랜 세월 우리를 짓눌러 온 과거에서 벗어났다는 그 새로운 자유의 감각이 아직 우리에게 낯설던 무렵, 나는 목판 조각 일의 첫 일자리를 내게 주었던 그 친구에게서 부름을 받았고, 나의 앞날을 염려하는 그의 마음을 다시 한 번 새롭게 확인하게 되었다."], ["He had been commissioned by his employers to go to Paris, and to examine for them a fresh discovery in the practical application of his Art, the merits of which they were anxious to ascertain.", "그는 고용주들로부터 파리에 가서 자기 기술의 실제 응용에 관한 새로운 발명 하나를 그들을 대신하여 살펴보고 오라는 위임을 받은 참이었다. 고용주들은 그 발명이 과연 값어치가 있는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 했다."], ["His own engagements had not allowed him leisure time to undertake the errand, and he had most kindly suggested that it should be transferred to me.", "그러나 그 자신은 다른 일에 매여 그 심부름을 떠맡을 겨를이 없었고, 그리하여 참으로 친절하게도 그 일을 내게 넘기자고 제안한 것이었다."], ["I could have no hesitation in thankfully accepting the offer, for if I acquitted myself of my commission as I hoped I should, the result would be a permanent engagement on the illustrated newspaper, to which I was now only occasionally attached.", "나로서는 그 제안을 감사히 받아들이는 데 조금도 망설일 이유가 없었다. 내가 바라는 대로 맡은 소임을 잘 해내기만 한다면, 그 결과로 지금은 이따금씩만 관계를 맺고 있는 그 화보 신문에 정식으로 자리를 얻게 될 터였기 때문이다."]], [["I received my instructions and packed up for the journey the next day.", "나는 필요한 지시를 받고 이튿날 여행 짐을 꾸렸다."], ["On leaving Laura once more (under what changed circumstances!) in her sister’s care, a serious consideration recurred to me, which had more than once crossed my wife’s mind, as well as my own, already—I mean the consideration of Marian’s future.", "로라를 다시 한 번 그 언니의 보살핌에 맡기고 떠나면서—아, 지난번과는 얼마나 달라진 사정 속에서였던가!—전에도 여러 차례 아내의 마음에도 내 마음에도 떠올랐던 진지한 생각 하나가 되살아났다. 매리언의 앞날에 관한 생각이었다."], ["Had we any right to let our selfish affection accept the devotion of all that generous life?", "우리의 이기적인 애정이 그 너그러운 생애의 헌신을 통째로 받아 챙길 권리가 우리에게 대체 있는가?"], ["Was it not our duty, our best expression of gratitude, to forget ourselves, and to think only of HER?", "우리 자신을 잊고 오직 그녀만을 생각하는 것이야말로 우리의 도리이자, 우리가 감사를 표하는 가장 좋은 길이 아니겠는가?"], ["I tried to say this when we were alone for a moment, before I went away.", "나는 떠나기 전 잠시 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 이 말을 꺼내 보려 했다."], ["She took my hand, and silenced me at the first words.", "그녀는 내 손을 잡았고, 첫마디에서 나를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“After all that we three have suffered together,”", "“우리 셋이 함께 그 모든 일을 겪어 낸 마당에,”"], ["she said, “there can be no parting between us till the last parting of all.", "그녀가 말했다, “마지막의 마지막 이별이 오기 전까지 우리 사이에 헤어짐이란 있을 수 없어요."], ["My heart and my happiness, Walter, are with Laura and you.", "내 마음도 내 행복도, 월터, 로라와 당신 곁에 있어요."], ["Wait a little till there are children’s voices at your fireside.", "조금만 기다려 봐요, 당신들 난롯가에서 아이들 목소리가 들려올 때까지."], ["I will teach them to speak for me in THEIR language, and the first lesson they say to their father and mother shall be—We can’t spare our aunt!”", "나는 그 아이들에게 아이들의 말로 나를 대신해 말하는 법을 가르치겠어요. 그리고 그 아이들이 제 아버지와 어머니 앞에서 맨 처음 외우게 될 말은 이것이 될 거예요—우리 이모는 절대로 내드릴 수 없어요!”"]], [["My journey to Paris was not undertaken alone.", "파리로 가는 길에 나 혼자만 오른 것은 아니었다."], ["At the eleventh hour Pesca decided that he would accompany me.", "막판에 이르러 페스카가 나와 동행하겠다고 마음을 정했다."], ["He had not recovered his customary cheerfulness since the night at the Opera, and he determined to try what a week’s holiday would do to raise his spirits.", "그는 오페라 극장에서의 그 밤 이후로 평소의 쾌활함을 되찾지 못하고 있었고, 일주일의 휴가가 기분을 돋우는 데 얼마나 도움이 될지 시험해 보기로 결심한 것이었다."]], [["I performed the errand entrusted to me, and drew out the necessary report, on the fourth day from our arrival in Paris.", "파리에 도착한 지 나흘째 되는 날, 나는 내게 맡겨진 소임을 마치고 필요한 보고서를 작성했다."], ["The fifth day I arranged to devote to sight-seeing and amusements in Pesca’s company.", "닷새째 되는 날은 페스카와 함께 구경을 다니고 즐기는 데 쓰기로 정해 두었다."]], [["Our hotel had been too full to accommodate us both on the same floor.", "우리가 묵은 호텔은 손님이 너무 많아 우리 둘을 같은 층에 재울 수 없었다."], ["My room was on the second story, and Pesca’s was above me, on the third.", "내 방은 삼층에 있었고 페스카의 방은 그 위 사층에 있었다."], ["On the morning of the fifth day I went upstairs to see if the Professor was ready to go out.", "닷새째 되는 날 아침, 나는 교수가 나갈 채비를 마쳤는지 보려고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Just before I reached the landing I saw his door opened from the inside—a long, delicate, nervous hand (not my friend’s hand certainly) held it ajar.", "층계참에 채 이르기 직전, 나는 그의 방문이 안쪽에서 열리는 것을 보았다—길고 가녀리고 신경질적인 손 하나가(분명 내 친구의 손은 아니었다) 문을 조금 열어 붙들고 있었다."], ["At the same time I heard Pesca’s voice saying eagerly, in low tones, and in his own language—“I remember the name, but I don’t know the man.", "그와 동시에 페스카의 목소리가 들렸다. 낮은 소리로, 다급하게, 제 나라 말로 이렇게 말하고 있었다—“이름은 기억하지만 그 사람은 모릅니다."], ["You saw at the Opera he was so changed that I could not recognise him.", "오페라 극장에서 보셨듯이 그자는 너무나 달라져서 나는 알아보지 못했습니다."], ["I will forward the report—I can do no more.”", "보고는 올리겠습니다—그 이상은 할 수 없습니다.”"], ["“No more need be done,”", "“그 이상은 할 필요도 없소.”"], ["answered the second voice.", "두 번째 목소리가 대답했다."], ["The door opened wide, and the light-haired man with the scar on his cheek—the man I had seen following Count Fosco’s cab a week before—came out.", "문이 활짝 열리더니, 뺨에 흉터가 있는 그 금발 사내—일주일 전에 포스코 백작의 마차를 뒤쫓아 가던 것을 내가 보았던 바로 그 사내가 걸어 나왔다."], ["He bowed as I drew aside to let him pass—his face was fearfully pale—and he held fast by the banisters as he descended the stairs.", "내가 길을 비켜 주자 그는 고개를 숙여 인사했다—그의 얼굴은 무섭도록 창백했다—그리고 계단을 내려가는 동안 난간을 꽉 붙들고 있었다."]], [["I pushed open the door and entered Pesca’s room.", "나는 문을 밀고 페스카의 방으로 들어섰다."], ["He was crouched up, in the strangest manner, in a corner of the sofa.", "그는 소파 한구석에 참으로 기이한 모양새로 몸을 웅크린 채 있었다."], ["He seemed to shrink from me when I approached him.", "내가 다가가자 그는 나를 피해 움츠러드는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“Am I disturbing you?”", "“내가 방해가 되나?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["“I did not know you had a friend with you till I saw him come out.”", "“저 사람이 나오는 걸 보고서야 자네에게 손님이 있는 줄 알았네.”"]], [["“No friend,”", "“손님이 아닐세.”"], ["said Pesca eagerly.", "페스카가 다급히 말했다."], ["“I see him to-day for the first time and the last.”", "“저 사람을 오늘 처음 보았고, 오늘이 마지막일세.”"]], [["“I am afraid he has brought you bad news?”", "“안 좋은 소식을 가져온 모양이지?”"]], [["“Horrible news, Walter!", "“끔찍한 소식이야, 월터!"], ["Let us go back to London—I don’t want to stop here—I am sorry I ever came.", "우리 런던으로 돌아가세—나는 여기 더 머물고 싶지 않네—여기 온 게 후회스러워."], ["The misfortunes of my youth are very hard upon me,”", "젊은 시절의 그 불행들이 나를 몹시도 무겁게 짓누르는군.”"], ["he said, turning his face to the wall, “very hard upon me in my later time.", "그는 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다. “나이 든 뒤에까지 이토록 무겁게 짓누른단 말일세."], ["I try to forget them—and they will not forget ME!”", "나는 그것들을 잊으려 애쓰는데—그것들은 나를 잊어 주지 않아!”"]], [["“We can’t return, I am afraid, before the afternoon,”", "“오후가 되기 전에는 떠날 수 없을 것 같은데.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Would you like to come out with me in the meantime?”", "“그동안 나와 함께 나가 보지 않겠나?”"]], [["“No, my friend, I will wait here.", "“아닐세, 친구. 나는 여기서 기다리겠네."], ["But let us go back to-day—pray let us go back.”", "하지만 오늘 돌아가세—제발 돌아가세.”"]], [["I left him with the assurance that he should leave Paris that afternoon.", "나는 그가 그날 오후에 파리를 떠나게 될 것이라고 다짐해 주고 그를 남겨 둔 채 나왔다."], ["We had arranged the evening before to ascend the Cathedral of Notre Dame, with Victor Hugo’s noble romance for our guide.", "우리는 전날 저녁에 빅토르 위고의 고매한 소설을 길잡이 삼아 노트르담 대성당에 올라가 보기로 약속해 두었던 터였다."], ["There was nothing in the French capital that I was more anxious to see, and I departed by myself for the church.", "프랑스의 수도에서 내가 그보다 더 보고 싶었던 것은 없었으므로, 나는 혼자서 그 성당을 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["Approaching Notre Dame by the river-side, I passed on my way the terrible dead-house of Paris—the Morgue.", "강가를 따라 노트르담으로 다가가던 길에 나는 파리의 그 무시무시한 시체 안치소—모르그를 지나게 되었다."], ["A great crowd clamoured and heaved round the door.", "커다란 군중이 문 앞에 몰려 왁자하게 떠들며 밀치락달치락하고 있었다."], ["There was evidently something inside which excited the popular curiosity, and fed the popular appetite for horror.", "안에 무언가 뭇사람의 호기심을 자극하고 뭇사람의 공포에 대한 갈증을 채워 주는 것이 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["I should have walked on to the church if the conversation of two men and a woman on the outskirts of the crowd had not caught my ear.", "군중의 언저리에 있던 두 남자와 한 여자의 이야기가 내 귀에 들어오지 않았더라면 나는 그대로 성당을 향해 걸어갔을 것이다."], ["They had just come out from seeing the sight in the Morgue, and the account they were giving of the dead body to their neighbours described it as the corpse of a man—a man of immense size, with a strange mark on his left arm.", "그들은 모르그 안의 구경거리를 보고 방금 나온 참이었는데, 곁에 선 사람들에게 그 시신에 대해 들려주는 이야기인즉, 그것은 한 사내의 주검이며—엄청나게 몸집이 큰 사내로, 왼팔에 이상한 표시가 있더라는 것이었다."]], [["The moment those words reached me I stopped and took my place with the crowd going in.", "그 말이 내 귀에 닿는 순간 나는 걸음을 멈추고 안으로 들어가는 군중 틈에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Some dim foreshadowing of the truth had crossed my mind when I heard Pesca’s voice through the open door, and when I saw the stranger’s face as he passed me on the stairs of the hotel.", "열린 문틈으로 페스카의 목소리를 들었을 때, 그리고 호텔 계단에서 나를 스쳐 지나가는 그 낯선 사내의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 진실의 어렴풋한 그림자가 이미 내 마음을 스쳐 갔었다."], ["Now the truth itself was revealed to me—revealed in the chance words that had just reached my ears.", "이제 방금 내 귀에 닿은 우연한 몇 마디 말로 그 진실 자체가 내게 드러난 것이었다."], ["Other vengeance than mine had followed that fated man from the theatre to his own door—from his own door to his refuge in Paris.", "나 아닌 다른 이의 복수가 그 운명 지어진 사내를 극장에서 그의 집 문간까지, 그의 집 문간에서 파리의 은신처까지 뒤쫓았던 것이다."], ["Other vengeance than mine had called him to the day of reckoning, and had exacted from him the penalty of his life.", "나 아닌 다른 이의 복수가 그를 셈을 치르는 날로 불러내어 그에게서 목숨이라는 대가를 받아 낸 것이었다."], ["The moment when I had pointed him out to Pesca at the theatre in the hearing of that stranger by our side, who was looking for him too—was the moment that sealed his doom.", "극장에서, 그자를 함께 찾고 있던 그 낯선 사내가 우리 곁에서 듣고 있는 가운데, 내가 페스카에게 그자를 가리켜 보인 바로 그 순간이—그의 운명을 확정 지은 순간이었다."], ["I remembered the struggle in my own heart, when he and I stood face to face—the struggle before I could let him escape me—and shuddered as I recalled it.", "나는 그와 내가 얼굴을 맞대고 섰을 때 내 마음속에서 벌어졌던 그 싸움을—그를 놓아 보내 주기까지 내가 치러야 했던 그 싸움을 떠올렸고, 그것을 되새기며 몸서리쳤다."]], [["Slowly, inch by inch, I pressed in with the crowd, moving nearer and nearer to the great glass screen that parts the dead from the living at the Morgue—nearer and nearer, till I was close behind the front row of spectators, and could look in.", "나는 천천히, 한 치 한 치 군중과 더불어 안으로 밀려 들어갔고, 모르그에서 죽은 자와 산 자를 갈라놓는 커다란 유리 칸막이에 점점 더 가까이, 점점 더 가까이 다가갔다. 마침내 나는 맨 앞줄 구경꾼들의 바로 등 뒤에 이르러 안을 들여다볼 수 있게 되었다."]], [["There he lay, unowned, unknown, exposed to the flippant curiosity of a French mob!", "거기에 그가 누워 있었다. 거두는 이도 없이, 아는 이도 없이, 프랑스 군중의 경박한 호기심 앞에 내걸린 채로!"], ["There was the dreadful end of that long life of degraded ability and heartless crime!", "능력을 타락시켜 쓰고 무정한 죄악으로 채웠던 그 기나긴 생애의 끔찍한 결말이 바로 이것이었다!"], ["Hushed in the sublime repose of death, the broad, firm, massive face and head fronted us so grandly that the chattering Frenchwomen about me lifted their hands in admiration, and cried in shrill chorus, “Ah, what a handsome man!”", "죽음의 숭고한 안식 속에 잠잠해진 그 넓고 굳세고 우람한 얼굴과 머리는 어찌나 당당하게 우리를 마주 보고 있었던지, 내 주위의 재잘거리는 프랑스 여인들이 감탄하여 두 손을 치켜들고 새된 소리로 입을 모아 외쳤다. “아, 이 얼마나 잘생긴 사내인가!”"], ["The wound that had killed him had been struck with a knife or dagger exactly over his heart.", "그를 죽인 상처는 칼이나 단검으로 심장 바로 위를 정확히 찔러 낸 것이었다."], ["No other traces of violence appeared about the body except on the left arm, and there, exactly in the place where I had seen the brand on Pesca’s arm, were two deep cuts in the shape of the letter T, which entirely obliterated the mark of the Brotherhood.", "왼팔을 제외하면 시신 어디에도 다른 폭력의 흔적은 없었다. 그리고 그 왼팔에는, 페스카의 팔에서 내가 낙인을 보았던 바로 그 자리에, T 자 모양으로 깊이 벤 두 줄기 칼자국이 있어 형제단의 표식을 온전히 지워 없애고 있었다."], ["His clothes, hung above him, showed that he had been himself conscious of his danger—they were clothes that had disguised him as a French artisan.", "그의 머리 위에 걸린 옷가지들은 그 자신이 제 위험을 알고 있었음을 말해 주었다—그것은 그를 프랑스 직공으로 꾸며 주던 옷이었다."], ["For a few moments, but not for longer, I forced myself to see these things through the glass screen.", "잠시 동안, 그러나 그 이상은 아니게, 나는 유리 칸막이 너머로 이 광경들을 억지로 바라보았다."], ["I can write of them at no greater length, for I saw no more.", "그 이상 길게 적을 수는 없다. 나는 더는 보지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["The few facts in connection with his death which I subsequently ascertained (partly from Pesca and partly from other sources), may be stated here before the subject is dismissed from these pages.", "그의 죽음과 관련하여 내가 뒤에 알아낸 몇 안 되는 사실들은(일부는 페스카에게서, 일부는 다른 경로로 얻은 것이다) 이 주제를 이 지면에서 물리기 전에 여기에 적어 두어도 좋으리라."]], [["His body was taken out of the Seine in the disguise which I have described, nothing being found on him which revealed his name, his rank, or his place of abode.", "그의 시신은 내가 앞서 묘사한 그 변장 차림 그대로 센 강에서 건져 올려졌고, 그의 이름도 신분도 거처도 알려 줄 만한 것은 몸에서 아무것도 발견되지 않았다."], ["The hand that struck him was never traced, and the circumstances under which he was killed were never discovered.", "그를 찌른 손은 끝내 밝혀지지 않았고, 그가 살해된 정황도 끝내 드러나지 않았다."], ["I leave others to draw their own conclusions in reference to the secret of the assassination as I have drawn mine.", "나는 이 암살의 비밀에 관하여, 내가 나대로 결론을 내렸듯 다른 이들도 저마다 결론을 내리도록 맡겨 두겠다."], ["When I have intimated that the foreigner with the scar was a member of the Brotherhood (admitted in Italy after Pesca’s departure from his native country), and when I have further added that the two cuts, in the form of a T, on the left arm of the dead man, signified the Italian word “Traditore,” and showed that justice had been done by the Brotherhood on a traitor, I have contributed all that I know towards elucidating the mystery of Count Fosco’s death.", "흉터가 있는 그 외국인이 형제단의 일원이었다는 것(페스카가 조국을 떠난 뒤 이탈리아에서 가입한 것이다)을 내가 넌지시 밝혔고, 나아가 죽은 사내의 왼팔에 새겨진 T 자 모양의 두 칼자국이 이탈리아 말 “트라디토레”, 곧 배신자를 뜻하며 형제단이 배신자에게 정의를 집행하였음을 보여 주는 것이라고 덧붙였으니, 포스코 백작의 죽음에 얽힌 수수께끼를 밝히는 데 내가 아는 바는 모두 내놓은 셈이다."]], [["The body was identified the day after I had seen it by means of an anonymous letter addressed to his wife.", "시신은 내가 그것을 본 이튿날, 그의 아내에게 부쳐진 익명의 편지로 신원이 확인되었다."], ["He was buried by Madame Fosco in the cemetery of Père la Chaise.", "그는 포스코 백작부인의 손으로 페르 라셰즈 묘지에 묻혔다."], ["Fresh funeral wreaths continue to this day to be hung on the ornamental bronze railings round the tomb by the Countess’s own hand.", "오늘까지도 새 조화가 백작부인 자신의 손에 의해 무덤을 두른 장식 청동 난간에 계속 걸리고 있다."], ["She lives in the strictest retirement at Versailles.", "그녀는 베르사유에서 극히 은둔한 생활을 하고 있다."], ["Not long since she published a biography of her deceased husband.", "얼마 전 그녀는 세상을 떠난 남편의 전기를 펴냈다."], ["The work throws no light whatever on the name that was really his own or on the secret history of his life—it is almost entirely devoted to the praise of his domestic virtues, the assertion of his rare abilities, and the enumeration of the honours conferred on him.", "그 책은 그의 진짜 이름이 무엇이었는지에 대해서도, 그의 생애의 은밀한 내력에 대해서도 아무런 빛을 던지지 못한다—그 책은 거의 전부가 그의 가정적 미덕에 대한 찬사와 그의 보기 드문 재능에 대한 단언, 그리고 그에게 수여된 영예들의 열거에 바쳐져 있다."], ["The circumstances attending his death are very briefly noticed, and are summed up on the last page in this sentence—“His life was one long assertion of the rights of the aristocracy and the sacred principles of Order, and he died a martyr to his cause.”", "그의 죽음에 얽힌 정황은 아주 간략하게만 언급되며, 마지막 쪽에서 이런 문장으로 정리된다—“그의 생애는 귀족의 권리와 신성한 질서의 원리를 향한 하나의 기나긴 선언이었으며, 그는 자신의 대의를 위해 순교하였다.”"]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["The summer and autumn passed after my return from Paris, and brought no changes with them which need be noticed here.", "파리에서 돌아온 뒤로 여름과 가을이 지나갔으나, 그 계절들은 여기에 적어 둘 만한 어떤 변화도 데려오지 않았다."], ["We lived so simply and quietly that the income which I was now steadily earning sufficed for all our wants.", "우리는 더없이 소박하고 조용하게 살았으므로, 내가 이제 꾸준히 벌어들이게 된 수입만으로도 우리에게 필요한 모든 것이 넉넉히 채워졌다."]], [["In the February of the new year our first child was born—a son.", "이듬해 2월에 우리의 첫아이가 태어났다—아들이었다."], ["My mother and sister and Mrs. Vesey were our guests at the little christening party, and Mrs.", "조촐한 세례 잔치에는 어머니와 누이와 베지 부인이 손님으로 오셨고, 클레멘츠 부인도 같은 자리에서 아내를 거들려고 참석했다."], ["Clements was present to assist my wife on the same occasion. Marian was our boy’s godmother, and Pesca and Mr.", "매리언이 우리 아이의 대모가 되었고, 페스카와 길모어 씨(뒤의 사람은 대리인을 세워서)가 대부가 되었다."], ["Gilmore (the latter acting by proxy) were his godfathers. I may add here that when Mr.", "여기에 덧붙여 두자면, 길모어 씨는 한 해 뒤 우리에게 돌아왔을 때 내 청을 받아들여 이 지면의 구상을 도와주었으니, 이 이야기의 앞부분에 그의 이름으로 실린 서술이 바로 그가 써 준 글이다."], ["Gilmore returned to us a year later he assisted the design of these pages, at my request, by writing the Narrative which appears early in the story under his name, and which, though first in order of precedence, was thus, in order of time, the last that I received.", "그 글은 배열의 순서로는 앞자리를 차지하고 있으나, 시간의 순서로는 이렇듯 내가 가장 나중에 받은 글이었다."]], [["The only event in our lives which now remains to be recorded, occurred when our little Walter was six months old.", "이제 우리 삶에서 기록해 두어야 할 사건은 단 하나 남았는데, 그것은 우리의 어린 월터가 태어난 지 여섯 달이 되었을 무렵에 일어났다."]], [["At that time I was sent to Ireland to make sketches for certain forthcoming illustrations in the newspaper to which I was attached.", "그 무렵 나는 내가 몸담고 있던 신문에 곧 실릴 삽화의 밑그림을 그리기 위해 아일랜드로 파견되었다."], ["I was away for nearly a fortnight, corresponding regularly with my wife and Marian, except during the last three days of my absence, when my movements were too uncertain to enable me to receive letters.", "나는 거의 두 주일 동안 집을 비웠고, 그동안 아내와 매리언과 꼬박꼬박 편지를 주고받았다. 다만 마지막 사흘만은 내 이동이 너무 불확실해 편지를 받을 수가 없었다."], ["I performed the latter part of my journey back at night, and when I reached home in the morning, to my utter astonishment there was no one to receive me.", "돌아오는 여정의 뒷부분은 밤에 지나왔고, 아침에 집에 닿아 보니 참으로 놀랍게도 나를 맞아 줄 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["Laura and Marian and the child had left the house on the day before my return.", "로라와 매리언과 아이는 내가 돌아오기 전날 집을 떠났던 것이다."]], [["A note from my wife, which was given to me by the servant, only increased my surprise, by informing me that they had gone to Limmeridge House.", "하인이 건네준 아내의 쪽지는 그들이 리머리지 하우스로 갔다는 것을 알려 주어 내 놀라움을 키우기만 했다."], ["Marian had prohibited any attempt at written explanations—I was entreated to follow them the moment I came back—complete enlightenment awaited me on my arrival in Cumberland—and I was forbidden to feel the slightest anxiety in the meantime.", "매리언이 글로 설명하려는 시도를 일절 금했다는 것, 돌아오는 즉시 뒤따라와 달라는 간청, 컴벌랜드에 도착하면 모든 것이 환히 밝혀지리라는 것, 그리고 그동안 조금이라도 불안해하는 일은 금한다는 것."], ["There the note ended.", "쪽지는 거기서 끝나 있었다."], ["It was still early enough to catch the morning train.", "아직은 아침 기차를 잡을 만큼 이른 시각이었다."], ["I reached Limmeridge House the same afternoon.", "나는 그날 오후에 리머리지 하우스에 닿았다."]], [["My wife and Marian were both upstairs.", "아내와 매리언은 둘 다 위층에 있었다."], ["They had established themselves (by way of completing my amazement) in the little room which had been once assigned to me for a studio, when I was employed on Mr. Fairlie’s drawings.", "그들은 (내 경악을 완성이라도 하려는 듯) 예전에 페얼리 씨의 그림을 손보는 일을 하던 시절 내게 화실로 내주었던 그 작은 방에 자리를 잡고 있었다."], ["On the very chair which I used to occupy when I was at work Marian was sitting now, with the child industriously sucking his coral upon her lap—while Laura was standing by the well-remembered drawing-table which I had so often used, with the little album that I had filled for her in past times open under her hand.", "내가 일할 때면 늘 차지하고 앉던 바로 그 의자에 이제 매리언이 앉아 있었고, 아이는 그 무릎 위에서 산호를 열심히 빨고 있었다. 그러는 동안 로라는 내가 그토록 자주 쓰던, 잊을 수 없는 그 제도용 탁자 곁에 서서, 지난날 내가 그녀를 위해 채워 주었던 작은 화첩을 손 밑에 펼쳐 놓고 있었다."]], [["“What in the name of heaven has brought you here?”", "“도대체 무슨 일로 여기까지 온 거요?”"], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["“Does Mr. Fairlie know——?”", "“페얼리 씨는 알고 있——?”"]], [["Marian suspended the question on my lips by telling me that Mr. Fairlie was dead.", "매리언은 페얼리 씨가 세상을 떠났다고 알려 주어 내 입술 위의 물음을 멎게 했다."], ["He had been struck by paralysis, and had never rallied after the shock. Mr.", "그는 중풍으로 쓰러졌고, 그 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못했다는 것이었다."], ["Kyrle had informed them of his death, and had advised them to proceed immediately to Limmeridge House.", "카일 씨가 그들에게 그의 죽음을 알리고, 지체 없이 리머리지 하우스로 가라고 권했다고 했다."]], [["Some dim perception of a great change dawned on my mind.", "거대한 변화가 일어났다는 어렴풋한 느낌이 내 마음에 어슴푸레 밝아 왔다."], ["Laura spoke before I had quite realised it.", "내가 그것을 채 다 깨닫기도 전에 로라가 입을 열었다."], ["She stole close to me to enjoy the surprise which was still expressed in my face.", "그녀는 아직 내 얼굴에 남아 있는 놀라움을 즐기려고 슬며시 내게 다가왔다."]], [["“My darling Walter,”", "“사랑하는 월터,”"], ["she said, “must we really account for our boldness in coming here?", "그녀가 말했다. “우리가 이렇게 대담하게 여기 온 것을 정말 해명해야 하나요?"], ["I am afraid, love, I can only explain it by breaking through our rule, and referring to the past.”", "여보, 아무래도 저는 우리의 규칙을 깨고 지난날을 들추지 않고서는 설명할 길이 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“There is not the least necessity for doing anything of the kind,”", "“그런 짓을 할 필요는 조금도 없어요.”"], ["said Marian.", "매리언이 말했다."], ["“We can be just as explicit, and much more interesting, by referring to the future.”", "“미래를 이야기하면 똑같이 분명하면서도 훨씬 더 흥미롭게 설명할 수 있으니까요.”"], ["She rose and held up the child kicking and crowing in her arms.", "그녀는 일어서서, 팔에 안긴 채 발을 차며 옹알거리는 아이를 들어 올렸다."], ["“Do you know who this is, Walter?”", "“이 아이가 누군지 아세요, 월터?”"], ["she asked, with bright tears of happiness gathering in her eyes.", "그녀가 물었다. 행복에 겨운 눈물이 그 눈에 환하게 차오르고 있었다."]], [["“Even MY bewilderment has its limits,”", "“나의 얼떨떨함에도 한계는 있어요.”"], ["I replied.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“I think I can still answer for knowing my own child.”", "“내 아이쯤은 아직 알아볼 수 있다고 자신하는데요.”"]], [["“Child!”", "“아이라뇨!”"], ["she exclaimed, with all her easy gaiety of old times.", "그녀가 옛 시절의 그 거침없는 쾌활함을 고스란히 되찾아 외쳤다."], ["“Do you talk in that familiar manner of one of the landed gentry of England?", "“잉글랜드 지주 계급의 어느 분을 두고 그렇게 허물없이 말해도 되나요?"], ["Are you aware, when I present this illustrious baby to your notice, in whose presence you stand?", "이 고귀하신 아기를 당신 앞에 소개하는 이 순간, 당신이 누구 앞에 서 있는지 알기나 하세요?"], ["Evidently not!", "분명 모르시는군요!"], ["Let me make two eminent personages known to one another: Mr.", "두 분 저명하신 분을 서로 소개해 드리겠습니다."], ["Walter Hartright—THE HEIR OF LIMMERIDGE.”", "월터 하트라이트 씨—리머리지의 상속인이십니다.”"]], [["So she spoke.", "그녀는 그렇게 말했다."], ["In writing those last words, I have written all.", "그 마지막 말을 적음으로써 나는 적어야 할 모든 것을 다 적었다."], ["The pen falters in my hand.", "펜이 내 손안에서 머뭇거린다."], ["The long, happy labour of many months is over.", "여러 달에 걸친 길고도 행복한 노고가 끝났다."], ["Marian was the good angel of our lives—let Marian end our Story.", "매리언은 우리 삶의 선한 천사였다—우리의 이야기를 맺는 일은 매리언에게 맡기자."]]];
